¢etotal&0ddataô&0∂habstractyöThis memo specifies an RTP payload format for MPEG-I haptic data. A haptic media stream is composed of MPEG-I Haptic Stream (MIHS) units including a MIHS unit header and zero or more MIHS packets. The RTP payload header format allows for packetization of a MIHS unit in an RTP packet payload as well as fragmentation of a MIHS unit into multiple RTP packets. The original subtype registration for 'haptics/hmpg' (RFC 9695) did not include any required or optional parameters. This memo updates RFC 9695 and the 'haptics/hmpg' registration to add optional parameters. It also provides Session Description Protocol (SDP) usage information for the 'haptics' media type.fadNameoGorry Fairhurstdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlInterDigitaldnamegHS Yang¢kaffiliationlInterDigitaldnameiX. de FoyddatejPhfilenamegrfc9993eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-156972hkeywordsÑetouchgtactilelvibrotactilekkinesthetickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejNgranking'	crfcd9993irfcNumber'	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Payload Format for HapticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµDomain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) is a mechanism by which a Domain Owner can request feedback about email messages using their domain in the From: address field. This document describes "failure reports", or "failed message reports", which provide details about individual messages that failed to authenticate according to the DMARC mechanism.

This document updates RFC 6591 and obsoletes RFC 7489.fadNamekAndy Newtondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiDMARC.orgdnamehS. Jones¢kaffiliationdTanadnameiA. Veselyddatej◊-hfilenamegrfc9991eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-156742hkeywordsÜeDMARCdDKIMcSPFhfeedbacktEmail AuthenticationnFailure reportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejE·granking'crfcd9991irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYDomain-Based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) Failure ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) allows for Domain Owners to request aggregate reports from receivers. This report is an XML document and contains extensible elements that allow for other types of data to be specified later. The aggregate reports can be submitted by the receiver to the Domain Owner's specified destination as declared in the associated DNS record.

This document obsoletes RFC 7489.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmComcast, Inc.dnamejA. Brotmanddatej–Ùhfilenamegrfc9990eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-156741hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDatejE·granking'crfcd9990irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Domain-Based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) Aggregate ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) protocol.

DMARC permits the owner of an email's Author Domain to enable validation of the domain's use to indicate the Domain Owner's or Public Suffix Operator's message handling preference regarding failed validation and to request reports about the use of the domain name. Mail-receiving organizations can use this information when evaluating handling choices for incoming mail.

This document obsoletes RFCs 7489 and 9091.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhValimaildnamegT. Herr¢kaffiliationmStandcore LLCdnameiJ. Levineddatej—hfilenamegrfc9989eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-156740hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDatejE‡granking'crfcd9989irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGDomain-Based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxXThis document specifies a key agent protocol for use in the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgOpenSSHdnameiD. Millerddatejõmhfilenamegrfc9987eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsshmdfullxsshm - Secure Shell MaintenancednamexSecure Shell Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-156929hkeywordsÉcssheagentissh-agentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejõkgranking'crfcd9987irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Secure Shell (SSH) Agent ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇAn IP prefix may be configured as anycast and, as such, the same value can be advertised by multiple routers. It is useful for other routers to know that the advertisement is for an anycast prefix.

This document defines a new flag in the OSPFv2 Extended Prefix TLV Flags to advertise the anycast property. The document also specifies a companion YANG module for managing this function.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegR. Chen¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegD. Zhao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationtNew H3C TechnologiesdnamefC. Linddatej◊.hfilenamegrfc9983eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-156737hkeywordsÉsExtended Prefix TLViAC‚ÄëflagdYANGkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejÎçgranking&ˇcrfcd9983irfcNumber&ˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%OSPFv2 Anycast Property AdvertisementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document defines version "2.0" of JSContact. It defines the uid property of a Card object to be optional, rather than mandatory, as defined previously in version "1.0". All other definitions of JSContact version "1.0" remain as defined in RFC 9553. This document updates RFC 9555 by redefining how to convert the now optional uid property from and to vCard. It also registers the vCard JSCOMPS parameter at IANA, which was defined but not registered in RFC 9555.fadNamekAndy Newtondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekR. Stepanekddatejñohfilenamegrfc9982eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-156928hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejñmgranking&˛crfcd9982irfcNumber&˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<JSContact Version 2.0: A JSON Representation of Contact DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThe Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) makes use of signed objects called "manifests", each of which includes a "manifest number". This document updates RFC 9286 by specifying issuer and Relying Party (RP) behaviour when a manifest number reaches the largest possible value, a situation not considered in RFC 9286.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx'Asia Pacific Network Information CentrednamekT. Harrison¢kaffiliationx'Asia-Pacific Network Information CentrednamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationxBSD Software DevelopmentdnamekJ. Snijdersddatej´hfilenamegrfc9981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-156964hkeywordsÉdRPKIpRouting SecurityeX.509kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatej©granking&˝crfcd9981irfcNumber&˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBResource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Manifest Number HandlingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document defines a number of keywords and mailbox name
attributes that have been in use across different server and client
implementations.  It defines the intended use of these keywords and
mailbox name attributes.  This document registers all of these with
IANA to avoid name collisions.fadNamekAndy Newtondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamejN. Jenkins¢kaffiliationjApple, IncdnameiD. Eggertddatejshfilenamegrfc9979eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimailmaintdfullxmailmaint - Mail MaintenancednamepMail Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-156963hkeywordsÇdIMAPdJMAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejqgranking&˚crfcd9979irfcNumber&˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFRegistration of Further IMAP/JMAP Keywords and Mailbox Name AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document specifies how to augment the Routing Policy Specification Language (RPSL) inetnum: class to refer specifically to prefixlen files, which are Comma-Separated Values (CSV) files used to specify end-site prefix lengths. This document also describes an optional mechanism that uses the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) to authenticate the prefixlen files.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfIPinfodnameiO. Gasser¢kaffiliationuIIJ Research & ArrcusdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejM. Candela¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housleyddatej◊khfilenamegrfc9977eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-156960hkeywordsÑfprefixjallocationdRPSLginetnumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatej◊igranking&˘crfcd9977irfcNumber&˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Publishing End-Site Prefix LengthsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfMaintenance of DNS delegations requires occasional changes of the DS and NS record sets on the parent side of the delegation. For the case of DS records, "Automating DNSSEC Delegation Trust Maintenance" (RFC 7344) provides automation by allowing the child to publish CDS and/or CDNSKEY records holding the prospective DS parameters that the parent can ingest. Similarly, "Child-to-Parent Synchronization in DNS" (RFC 7477) specifies CSYNC records to indicate a desired update of the delegation's NS (and glue) records. Parent-side entities (e.g., Registries and Registrars) can query these records from the child and, after validation, use them to update the parent-side Resource Record Sets (RRsets) of the delegation.

This document specifies under which conditions the target states expressed via CDS/CDNSKEY and CSYNC records are considered "consistent". Parent-side entities accepting such records from the child have to ensure that update requests retrieved from different authoritative nameservers satisfy these consistency requirements before taking any action based on them.

This document updates RFCs 7344 and 7477.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx%SSE - Secure Systems Engineering GmbHdnamelP. ThomassenddatejYhfilenamegrfc9975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-156863hkeywordsÜfDNSSECbDScCDSgCDNSKEYeCSYNCjautomationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejVgranking&˜crfcd9975irfcNumber&˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Clarifications on CDS/CDNSKEY and CSYNC ConsistencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP) described in RFC 7854 defines statistics message types to observe events that occur on a monitored router.  This document defines new statistics types to monitor BMP Adj-RIB-In and Adj-RIB-Out Routing Information Bases (RIBs).fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHewlett Packard EnterprisednamemM. Srivastava¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefY. Liu¢kaffiliationtNew H3C TechnologiesdnamefC. Lin¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeJ. Liddatej◊+hfilenamegrfc9972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-156736hkeywordsÑcIDRdGROWcBGPcBMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejÊ)granking&Ùcrfcd9972irfcNumber&ÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Advanced BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP) Statistics TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyΩThe AI-CONTROL Workshop was convened by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) in September 2024. This report summarizes its significant points of discussion and identifies topics that may warrant further consideration and work.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliation`dnamekS. Krishnanddatej◊;hfilenamegrfc9969eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-156733hkeywordsÖfpolicywArtificial IntelligencexRobots Exclusion Protocolkweb crawlerjrobots.txtkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejÆ˚granking&Òcrfcd9969irfcNumber&ÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex*Report from the IAB Workshop on AI-CONTROLdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¨The "Next Era of Network Management Operations (NEMOPS)" workshop was convened by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) from December 3-5, 2024 as a three-day online meeting. It builds on a previous 2002 workshop, the outcome of which was documented in RFC 3535, identifying 14 operator requirements for consideration in future network management protocol design and related data models, along with some recommendations for the IETF. Much has changed in the Internet's operation and technological foundations since then. The NEMOPS workshop reviewed the past outcomes and discussed any operational barriers that prevented these technologies from being widely implemented. With the industry, network operators and protocol engineers working in collaboration, the workshop developed a suggested plan of action and network management recommendations for the IETF and IRTF. Building on RFC 3535, this document provides the report of the follow-up IAB workshop on network management.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Dhodyddatej◊;hfilenamegrfc9968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-156609hkeywordsÖdYANGgNETCONFhRESTCONFeNMOPSgRFC3535kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejÄ¶granking&crfcd9968irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexVReport from the IAB Workshop on the Next Era of Network Management Operations (NEMOPS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThis specification defines a set of System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) Security Events using the Security Event Token (SET) specification (RFC 8417) to enable the asynchronous exchange of messages between SCIM service providers and receivers.

This specification updates RFC 7643 by defining additional attributes for the "urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:ServiceProviderConfig" schema, and it updates RFC 7644 with an optional new asynchronous SCIM request capability.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnIndependent IddnamegP. Hunt¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationiSailpointdnamehM. Kiser¢kaffiliationgWorkdaydnamelJ. Schreiberddatej◊;hfilenamegrfc9967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdscimdfullx2scim - System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdnamex+System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-156673hkeywordsÜhIdentityeEventcSETlProvisioningeTokendSCIMkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatejPEgranking&Ôcrfcd9967irfcNumber&ÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[System for Cross-Domain Identity Management (SCIM) Profile for Security Event Tokens (SETs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a mechanism that enables a bootstrapping device to establish trust and mutually authenticate against a TLS server. Bootstrapping devices have a public/private key pair; this mechanism enables a TLS server to prove to the device that it knows the public key and enables the device to prove to the TLS server that it knows the private key. The mechanism leverages existing Device Provisioning Profile (DPP) and TLS standards and can be used in an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) exchange with an EAP server.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Friel¢kaffiliationxHewlett-Packard EnterprisednamejD. Harkinsddatej˛Whfilenamegrfc9966eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-156862hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatej˛Ugranking&Ócrfcd9966irfcNumber&ÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Bootstrapped TLS Authentication with Proof of KnowledgedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document defines the eap.arpa. domain for use only in Network Access Identifiers (NAIs) as a way for Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) peers to signal to EAP servers that they wish to obtain limited, and unauthenticated, network access. EAP peers signal which kind of access is required via certain predefined identifiers that use the NAI format of RFC 7542. A table of identifiers and meanings is defined, which includes entries for RFC 9140.

This document updates RFCs 5216 and 9190 to define an unauthenticated provisioning method. Those specifications suggest that such a method is possible, but they do not define how it would be done. This document also updates RFC 9140 to deprecate "eap-noob.arpa" and replace it with "@noob.eap.arpa".fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrInkBridge NetworksdnamehA. DeKokddatej—3hfilenamegrfc9965eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-156738hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejÚágranking&Ìcrfcd9965irfcNumber&ÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNThe eap.arpa. Domain and Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) ProvisioningdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE) and CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) serializations for the Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Standard (ML-DSA), a Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) digital signature scheme defined in US NIST FIPS 204.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlTradeverifyddnamejM. Prorock¢kaffiliationlTradeverifyddnameiO. Steeleddatej◊:hfilenamegrfc9964eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-156739hkeywordsÖdJOSEdCOSEcPQCiDILITHIUMfML-DSAkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDatej=granking&Ïcrfcd9964irfcNumber&ÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbML-DSA for JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE) and CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxØThis document allocates code points for the use of RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 with client certificates in TLS 1.3.  This removes an obstacle for some deployments to migrate to TLS 1.3.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Benjamin¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamehA. Popovddatej—5hfilenamegrfc9963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-156426hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÒBˆgranking&Îcrfcd9963irfcNumber&ÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Legacy RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 Code Points for TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty4This document describes how the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP)
Mapping System can be distributed for scale and decentralized for
management, while maintaining trust among data plane nodes.  This is
an Informational RFC and should be used by LISP-Decent
implementations to interoperate with each other.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationeNexusdnamekC. Cantrellddatej—4hfilenamegrfc9962eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-156663hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejVgranking&Ícrfcd9962irfcNumber&ÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexKA Decentralized Locator/ID Separation Protocol Mapping System (LISP-Decent)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypSegment Routing (SR) Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Policies are used to define and manage explicit P2MP paths within a network. These policies are typically calculated via a controller-based mechanism and installed via, e.g., a Path Computation Element (PCE). In other cases, these policies can be installed using the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) / YANG or a Command Line Interface (CLI). They are used to steer Multicast traffic along optimized paths from a Root to a set of Leaf routers.

This document defines extensions to ping and traceroute mechanisms for an SR P2MP Policy with MPLS encapsulation to provide Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) capabilities. The extensions enable operators to verify connectivity, diagnose failures, and troubleshoot forwarding issues within SR P2MP Policy Multicast trees.

By introducing new mechanisms for detecting failures and validating path integrity, this document enhances the operational robustness of P2MP Multicast deployments. Additionally, it ensures that existing MPLS and SR-based OAM tools can be effectively applied to networks utilizing SR P2MP Policies.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejH. Bidgoli¢kaffiliationlCisco SystemdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationlCisco SystemdnamelA. Budhiraja¢kaffiliationlCisco SystemdnamehD. Voyerddatej◊9hfilenamegrfc9961eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-156469hkeywordsÖlSR MulticastnSRv6 MulticastqSR Multicast pingwSR Multicast traceroutepSR Multicast OAMkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateiÛâ granking&Ècrfcd9961irfcNumber&ÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;MPLS Segment Routing Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Policy PingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThe Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Policy enables creation of P2MP trees for efficient multipoint packet delivery in a Segment Routing (SR) domain.  This document specifies the architecture, signaling, and procedures for SR P2MP Policies with Segment Routing over MPLS (SR-MPLS) and Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6).  It defines the SR P2MP Policy construct, candidate paths (CPs) of an SR P2MP Policy, and the instantiation of the P2MP tree instances (PTIs) of a CP using Replication segments.  Additionally, it describes the required extensions for a controller to support P2MP path computation and provisioning.  This document updates RFC 9524.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnameiR. Parekh¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejH. Bidgoli¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhangddatej◊:hfilenamegrfc9960eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-156466hkeywordsÇlSR MulticastnSRv6 MulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÛÉôgranking&Ëcrfcd9960irfcNumber&ËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Segment Routing Point-to-Multipoint PolicydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document specifies a cautious method for Internet transports that enables fast startup of Congestion Control (CC) for a wide range of connections, known as "Careful Resume". It reuses a set of computed CC parameters that are based on previously observed path characteristics between the same pair of transport endpoints. These parameters are saved, allowing them to be later used to modify the CC behaviour of a subsequent connection.

This document describes the assumptions and defines the requirements for how a sender utilises these parameters to provide opportunities for a connection to more rapidly get up to speed and utilise available capacity. It discusses how the use of this method impacts the capacity at a shared network bottleneck and the safe response that is needed after any indication that the new rate is inappropriate.fadNamekMike Bishopdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsThales Alenia SpacednamegN. Kuhn¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejE. Stephan¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnameiR. Secchi¢kaffiliationtPrivate Octopus Inc.dnamejC. Huitemaddatej◊7hfilenamegrfc9959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-156608hkeywordsÉiTransportrCongestion ControldQUICkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatejqgranking&Ácrfcd9959irfcNumber&ÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexECareful Resume: Convergence of Congestion Control from Retained StatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyˆThis Informational RFC explains the specification of the queue protection algorithm introduced into Data-Over-Cable Service Interface Specification (DOCSIS) technology at version 3.1.  A shared low-latency queue relies on the non-queue-building behaviour of every traffic flow using it.  However, some flows might not take such care, either accidentally or maliciously.  If a queue is about to exceed a threshold level of delay, the Queue Protection algorithm can rapidly detect the flows most likely to be responsible.  It can then prevent harm to other traffic in the low-latency queue by ejecting selected packets (or all packets) of these flows.  This document is designed for four audiences: a) congestion control designers who need to understand how to keep on the "good" side of the algorithm; b) implementers of the algorithm who want to understand it in more depth; c) designers of algorithms with similar goals, perhaps for non-DOCSIS scenarios; and d) researchers interested in evaluating the algorithm.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamehG. Whiteddatej÷ƒhfilenamegrfc9957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-156606hkeywordsÑxIndependent Submission StreamcISEgLatencyhPolicingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatej[¸granking&Âcrfcd9957irfcNumber&ÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex=The DOCSIS Queue Protection Algorithm to Preserve Low LatencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies characteristics of a Non-Queue-Building Per-Hop Behavior (NQB PHB).  The NQB PHB provides a shallow-buffered, best-effort service as a complement to a Default deep-buffered, best-effort service for Internet services.  The purpose of this NQB PHB is to provide a separate queue that enables smooth (i.e., non-bursty), low-data-rate, application-limited traffic microflows, to avoid the delay, delay variation and loss that would ordinarily be caused by sharing a queue with bursty, capacity-seeking traffic.  This PHB is implemented without prioritization and can be implemented without rate policing, making it suitable for environments where the use of these features is restricted.  The NQB PHB has been developed primarily for use by access network segments, where queuing delay and queuing loss caused by Queue-Building (QB) protocols are manifested; however, its use is not limited to such segments.  In particular, the application of NQB PHB to cable broadband links, Wi-Fi links, and mobile network radio/core segments are discussed in this document.  This document recommends a specific Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) to identify NQB microflows and updates the guidance in RFC 8325 on mapping Differentiated Services (Diffserv or DS) to IEEE 802.11 for this codepoint.fadNameoGorry Fairhurstdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamehG. White¢kaffiliationfLinarodnamejT. Fossati¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geibddatej◊8hfilenamegrfc9956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-156605hkeywordsébDSdDSCPcL4Sedelayfjitterodelay variationqQueuing variationmOne Way DelayoRound-Trip TimecRTTrQuality of ServicecQoSuQuality of ExperiencecQoEkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDatej[˚granking&‰crfcd9956irfcNumber&‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKA Non-Queue-Building Per-Hop Behavior (NQB PHB) for Differentiated ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document defines a protocol for exchanging DNS queries (OPCODE 0) over the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP).  These CoAP messages can be protected by (D)TLS-Secured CoAP or Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE) to provide encrypted DNS message exchange for constrained devices in the Internet of Things (IoT).fadNamekMike Bishopdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx$TUD Dresden University of TechnologydnamemM. S. Lenders¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Ams√ºss¢kaffiliationpNeuralAgent GmbHdnamemC. G√ºndoƒüan¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamemT. C. Schmidt¢kaffiliationx:TUD Dresden University of Technology & Barkhausen InstitutdnamelM. W√§hlischddatej◊7hfilenamegrfc9953eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-155804hkeywordsÉdCoREdCoAPcDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÃT£granking&·crfcd9953irfcNumber&·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesDNS over CoAP (DoC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxöThis document specifies an Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) ID for Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) services that are secured by DTLS.fadNamekMike Bishopdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx$TUD Dresden University of TechnologydnamemM. S. Lenders¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Ams√ºss¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamemT. C. Schmidt¢kaffiliationx:TUD Dresden University of Technology & Barkhausen InstitutdnamelM. W√§hlischddatej◊7hfilenamegrfc9952eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-155803hkeywordsÖdCoREdCoAPdSVCBdDTLSdALPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÃT¢granking&‡crfcd9952irfcNumber&‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCApplication-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) ID for CoAP over DTLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document specifies new IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Elements to export the On-Path delay at each Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) transit and decapsulating nodes.  The On-Path delay is defined as the delay between the OAM header encapsulating node and each OAM header transit and OAM header decapsulating nodes.  This delay measurement is computed by an On-Path Telemetry protocol and is exported by the IPFIX process.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhSwisscomdnamegT. Graf¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationiINSA-LyondnamemA. Huang-Fengddatej—3hfilenamegrfc9951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-156363hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÎ∂∫granking&ﬂcrfcd9951irfcNumber&ﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIExport of Delay Performance Metrics in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis document defines a Terminal Access Controller Access-Control
System Plus (TACACS+) client YANG module that augments the System
Management data model, defined in RFC 7317, to allow devices to make
use of TACACS+ servers for centralized Authentication, Authorization,
and Accounting (AAA). Specifically, the TACACS+ YANG module can be
used to manage TACACS+ over TLS.

This document obsoletes RFC 9105.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeB. Wuddatej◊9hfilenamegrfc9950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-155806hkeywordsàcTLSqdevice managementpnetwork operatorpprovider networkcAAAnauthenticationmauthorizationjaccountingkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateiÃîÕgranking&ﬁcrfcd9950irfcNumber&ﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA YANG Data Model for Terminal Access Controller Access-Control System Plus (TACACS+)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyTLS 1.3 (RFC 8446) eliminates null cipher suites entirely. However, one vestigial zero remains in the key schedule: when no Pre-Shared Key (PSK) is used, the Input Keying Material (IKM) for the initial HKDF-Extract operation is a string of zero bytes. This document specifies that this zero-byte IKM MUST be replaced with the SHA-256 digest of the raw PCM audio data of "Banned in the U.S.A." by 2 Live Crew (from the album "Banned in the U.S.A.", 1990), hereafter referred to as the Mandatory Audio Component (MAC). Implementations that omit the MAC are non-conformant with BUSA-TLS and also have questionable taste in music.

The IETF's process-heavy, consensus-driven, working-group-reviewed approach to protocol standardization is a fine way to run a standards body. It is also completely antithetical to the spirit of a document that requires a jury-banned rap album as a cryptographic primitive.

This document is offered in the same spirit as the album it incorporates: unapologetically and in defiance of institutional authority.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Sayreddatej—6hfilenamegrfc9949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-155851hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateiÕü˝granking&›crfcd9949irfcNumber&›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexÄBUSA-TLS: Mandatory Audio Component (MAC) Pre-Shared Key (PSK) Derivation for TLS 1.3 Using 2 Live Crew's "Banned in the U.S.A."dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx…The Internet Protocol Police (IPP) is in charge of punishing willful infractions of the Collected Wisdom of the IETF community.  This document sets out the schedule of punishments for such infractions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxInternet Protocol PolicednamejG.R. Beard¢kaffiliationxInternet Protocol PolicednamehO.F. Art¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrandddatej—,hfilenamegrfc9948eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-155847hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÕîgranking&‹crfcd9948irfcNumber&‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8Internet Protocol Police (IPP) - Schedule of PunishmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¯This document describes an alternative experimental approach for the application of the Alternate-Marking Method to Segment Routing for IPv6 (SRv6). It uses an experimental TLV in the Segment Routing Header (SRH); thus, participation in this experiment should be between coordinating parties in a controlled domain. This approach has potential scaling and simplification benefits over the technique described in RFC 9343, and the scope of the experiment is to determine whether those are significant and attractive to the community.

This protocol extension has been developed outside the IETF as an alternative to the IETF's Standards Track specification RFC 9343 and it does not have IETF consensus. It is published here to guide experimental implementation and ensure interoperability among implementations to better determine the value of this approach. Researchers are invited to submit their evaluations of this work to the Independent Submissions Editor or to the IETF SPRING Working Group as Internet-Drafts.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekG. Fioccola¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegT. Zhou¢kaffiliationlVerizon Inc.dnameiG. Mishra¢kaffiliationfRuijiednamegX. Wang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehG. Zhang¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Cociglioddatej◊6hfilenamegrfc9947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-155794hkeywordsêcSRHcTLViExtensionfHeaderfOptionkPerformancekMeasurementjMonitoringgPassivefHybriddLosseDelayoDelay VariationjMultipointgClusterkClosed-LoopkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÃ.granking&€crfcd9947irfcNumber&€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexIApplication of the Alternate-Marking Method to the Segment Routing HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This document addresses the problem of protocol support for measuring One-Way IP Capacity metrics specified by RFC 9097.  The Method of Measurement discussed in that RFC requires a feedback channel from the receiver to control the sender's transmission rate in near real-time.  This document defines the UDP Speed Test Protocol (UDPSTP) for conducting measurements as described in RFC 9097 and other related measurements.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamemL. Ciavattone¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geibddatej◊8hfilenamegrfc9946eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-156362hkeywordsÅxload rate adjustment algorithmkobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateiÎØûgranking&⁄crfcd9946irfcNumber&⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOThe UDP Speed Test Protocol (UDPSTP) for One-Way IP Capacity Metric MeasurementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty˙The IETF community will treat people with kindness and grace, but not endless patience.

This memo obsoletes RFCs 3683 and 3934, and it updates RFCs 2418 and 9245 by establishing a policy for the moderation of disruptive participation across the IETF's various public contribution channels and discussion fora. It establishes guardrails for moderation and a moderator team. That team will develop a set of moderation procedures and facilitate their consistent implementation with chairs and administrators.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Learddatej◊4hfilenamegrfc9945eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmodpoddfullxmodpod - MODeration PrOceDuresdnameuMODeration PrOceDuresdtypebwgbidjdoc-153882hkeywordsÉjdisruptionjmoderationmparticipationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei¢)	granking&Ÿcrfcd9945irfcNumber&ŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberıetotaletitlexIETF Community ModerationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThe initial core schema for the System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) was designed for provisioning users.  This memo specifies schema extensions that enable provisioning of devices using various underlying bootstrapping systems such as Wi-Fi Easy Connect, FIDO device onboarding vouchers, Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) passcodes, and MAC Authenticated Bypass (MAB).fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNorth Carolina State UniversitydnamejM. Shahzad¢kaffiliationxNorth Carolina State UniversitydnamehH. Iqbal¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Learddatej◊6hfilenamegrfc9944eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdscimdfullx2scim - System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdnamex+System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-156662hkeywordsÇlprovisioningdCRUDkobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDatejjgranking&ÿcrfcd9944irfcNumber&ÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXDevice Schema Extensions to the System for Cross-Domain Identity Management (SCIM) ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≤This document describes a widely deployed hybrid key exchange method in the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol that is based on Streamlined NTRU Prime sntrup761 and X25519 with SHA-512.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgOpenSSHdnameiM. Friedl¢kaffiliationgTinySSHdnameiJ. Mojzis¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. Josefssonddatej◊:hfilenamegrfc9941eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsshmdfullxsshm - Secure Shell MaintenancednamexSecure Shell Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-156006hkeywordsÜiNTRUPrimefX25519cSSHlkey exchangexpost-quantum cryptographyshybrid key exchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiŸOÀgranking&’crfcd9941irfcNumber&’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑSecure Shell (SSH) Key Exchange Method Using Hybrid Streamlined NTRU Prime sntrup761 and X25519 with SHA-512: sntrup761x25519-sha512dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document sets out some terms that are fundamental to a common understanding of network fault and problem management within the IETF.

The purpose of this document is to bring clarity to discussions and other work related to network fault and problem management -- in particular, to YANG data models and management protocols that report, make visible, or manage network faults and problems.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCienadnamehN. Davis¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationhSwisscomdnamegT. Graf¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeC. Yuddatej◊6hfilenamegrfc9940eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnmopdfullx$nmop - Network Management OperationsdnamexNetwork Management Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-155875hkeywordsâgProblemeEventeFaultjOccurrencehIncidentgAnomalygSymptomeAlerteAlarmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiŒÒ˜granking&‘crfcd9940irfcNumber&‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Some Key Terms for Network Fault and Problem ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx}This document defines PKCS #8 content types for use with PrivateKeyInfo and EncryptedPrivateKeyInfo as specified in RFC 5958.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfAKAYLAdnameiJ. Mandel¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej◊)hfilenamegrfc9939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-153912hkeywordsÅcCMSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei£ÀØgranking&”crfcd9939irfcNumber&”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.PKCS #8: Private-Key Information Content TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis document provides a framework overview for the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Controller Plane.  It discusses concepts and requirements for the DetNet Controller Plane, which could be the basis for a future solution specification.fadNamepKetan Talaulikardarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegX. Geng¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej◊9hfilenamegrfc9938eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-154568hkeywordsÉfDetNetpController planecSDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei∂@fgranking&“crfcd9938irfcNumber&“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFA Framework for the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Controller PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document specifies a Standards Track version of the Proportional Rate Reduction (PRR) algorithm that obsoletes the Experimental version described in RFC 6937.  PRR regulates the amount of data sent by TCP or other transport protocols during fast recovery.  PRR accurately regulates the actual flight size through recovery such that at the end of recovery it will be as close as possible to the slow start threshold (ssthresh), as determined by the congestion control algorithm.fadNameoGorry Fairhurstdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Mathis¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekN. Cardwell¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehY. Cheng¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamelN. Dukkipatiddatej◊3hfilenamegrfc9937eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-152990hkeywordsÖmloss recoverydSACKjself clockofast retransmitmfast recoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiEÜ¥granking&—crfcd9937irfcNumber&—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Proportional Rate Reduction (PRR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàModule-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM) is a quantum-resistant Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM).  Three parameter sets for the ML-KEM algorithm are specified by the US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) in FIPS 203.  In order of increasing security strength (and decreasing performance), these parameter sets are ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024.  This document specifies the conventions for using ML-KEM with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) using the KEMRecipientInfo structure defined in "Using Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) Algorithms in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)" (RFC 9629).fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCryptoNext SecuritydnamegJ. Prat¢kaffiliationoEntrust LimiteddnamelM. Ounsworth¢kaffiliationsCryptoNext SecuritydnamelD. Van Geestddatej◊5hfilenamegrfc9936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-154172hkeywordsÑx!Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM)pKEMRecipientInfofML-KEMeKyberkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei´dJgranking&–crfcd9936irfcNumber&–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Use of ML-KEM in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%The Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM) is a quantum-resistant Key Encapsulation Mechanism.  This document specifies the conventions for using the ML-KEM in X.509 Public Key Infrastructure.  The conventions for the subject public keys and private keys are also specified.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationcAWSdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationcAWSdnamejJ. Massimo¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamepB. E. Westerbaanddatej◊3hfilenamegrfc9935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-154171hkeywordsÜfML-KEMeKybercKEMkCertificateeX.509dPKIXkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDatei´cägranking&œcrfcd9935irfcNumber&œestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure - Algorithm Identifiers for the Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEncrypted ClientHello (ECH) key pairs need to be configured into TLS servers, which can be built using different TLS libraries.  This document specifies a standard file format for this purpose, similar to how RFC 7468 defines other Privacy-Enhanced Mail (PEM) file formats.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej◊.hfilenamegrfc9934eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-154125hkeywordsÇcTLSdESNIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei®Œögranking&Œcrfcd9934irfcNumber&ŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGPrivacy-Enhanced Mail (PEM) File Format for Encrypted ClientHello (ECH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty.This Informational Independent Submission to the RFC Series describes a means to use TLS 1.3 to perform machine-to-machine mutual authentication within federations. This memo is not a standard. It does not modify the TLS protocol in any way, nor does it require changes to common TLS libraries. TLS is specified and standardized by the IETF's TLS Working Group.

The framework enables interoperable trust management for federated machine-to-machine communication. It introduces a centrally managed trust anchor and a controlled metadata publication process, ensuring that only authorized members are identifiable within the federation. These mechanisms support unambiguous entity identification and reduce the risk of impersonation, promoting secure and policy-aligned interaction across organizational boundaries.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxThe Swedish Internet FoundationdnameiS. Hal√©n¢kaffiliationhKirei ABdnamekJ. Schlyterddatej◊!hfilenamegrfc9932eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-156009hkeywordsàrmachine-to-machineotrust frameworkjmutual TLSdmTLSrpublic key pinningdSPKIsfederation metadatajfederationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiŸ[%granking&Ãcrfcd9932irfcNumber&ÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9Mutually Authenticating TLS in the Context of FederationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(In HTTP/1.1, the client can request a change to a new protocol on the existing connection.  This document discusses the security considerations that apply to data sent by the client before this request is confirmed and adds new requirements to RFCs 9112 and 9298 to avoid related security issues.fadNamekMike Bishopdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtMeta Platforms, Inc.dnamekB. Schwartzddatej◊8hfilenamegrfc9931eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-154168hkeywordsÉqRequest SmugglingkFalse StartlHTTP CONNECTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei´0“granking&Àcrfcd9931irfcNumber&ÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGSecurity Considerations for Optimistic Protocol Transitions in HTTP/1.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the Tunnel Extensible Authentication Protocol (TEAP) version 1.  TEAP is a tunnel-based EAP method that enables secure communication between a peer and a server by using the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to establish a mutually authenticated tunnel.  Within the tunnel, TLV objects are used to convey authentication-related data between the EAP peer and the EAP server.  This document obsoletes RFC 7170 and updates RFC 9427 by moving all TEAP specifications from those documents to this one.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. DeKokddatej–Ïhfilenamegrfc9930eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-153884hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepageskopublicationDatei¢.Ógranking& crfcd9930irfcNumber& estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Tunnel Extensible Authentication Protocol (TEAP) Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMSummarization is often used in multi-area or multi-domain networks to improve network efficiency and scalability. With summarization in place, there is a need to signal loss of reachability to an individual prefix covered by the summary. This enables fast convergence by steering traffic, when applicable, away from the node which owns the prefix and is no longer reachable.

This document specifies protocol mechanisms in IS-IS and OSPF, together with two new flags, to advertise such prefix reachability loss.

The term "OSPF" in this document is used to refer to both OSPFv2 and OSPFv3.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationcHPEdnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationlVerizon Inc.dnameiG. Mishraddatej—hfilenamegrfc9929eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-153881hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei¢%àgranking&…crfcd9929irfcNumber&…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#IGP Unreachable Prefix AnnouncementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆThis document describes a mechanism for networks with legacy IPv4-only clients to use services provided by DHCPv4 over DHCPv6 in a Relay Agent.  RFC 7341 specifies the use of DHCPv4 over DHCPv6 in the client only.  This document specifies an approach based on RFC 7341 that allows a Relay Agent to implement the DHCPv4-over-DHCPv6 encapsulation and decapsulation of DHCPv4 messages in DHCPv6 messages on behalf of a DHCPv4 client.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejC. Porfiri¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewindddatej◊5hfilenamegrfc9928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-155796hkeywordsÅddhcpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiÃ)_granking&»crfcd9928irfcNumber&»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+DHCPv4 over DHCPv6 with Relay Agent SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡This document updates "Address-Protected Neighbor Discovery for Low-Power and Lossy Networks" (RFC 8928) by changing the position of the C-flag in the Extended Address Registration Option (EARO) and registering it with IANA.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationsPolitecnico di BaridnameiA. Rashidddatej—$hfilenamegrfc9927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-153623hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiåπ'granking&«crfcd9927irfcNumber&«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDFixing the C-Flag in the Extended Address Registration Option (EARO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document updates IPv6 Neighbor Discovery (RFC 4861) and IPv6 Subnet Neighbor Discovery (RFC 8505, RFC 8928) to enable a node that owns or is directly connected to a prefix to register that prefix to neighbor routers.  The registration indicates that the registered prefix can be reached via the advertising node without a loop.  The unicast prefix registration allows the node to request one or more neighbor routers to redistribute the prefix in another routing domain regardless of the routing protocol used in that domain.  This document updates the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL), as specified in RFCs 6550 and 9010, to enable a 6LoWPAN Router (6LR) to inject the registered prefix in RPL.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Thubertddatej—)hfilenamegrfc9926eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-153622hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiåπ'granking&∆crfcd9926irfcNumber&∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Prefix Registration for IPv6 Neighbor DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx–This document defines a placeholder X.509 signature algorithm that may be used in contexts where the consumer of the certificate is not expected to verify the signature.  As part of this, it updates RFC 5280.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Benjaminddatej◊7hfilenamegrfc9925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-153857hkeywordsÅwself-signed certificatekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatei†⁄granking&≈crfcd9925irfcNumber&≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUnsigned X.509 CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx‡This document describes the bitstream format of Advanced Professional Video (APV) and its decoding process.  APV is a professional video codec providing visually lossless compression mainly for recording and post production.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamefY. Lim¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamegM. Park¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamekM. Budagavi¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamehR. Joshi¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamegK. Choiddatej◊;hfilenamegrfc9924eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-153605hkeywordsÇomezzanine codecxvisually lossless compressionkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateiãœ¢granking&ƒcrfcd9924irfcNumber&ƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexAdvanced Professional VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyNFNV (Fowler/Noll/Vo) is a fast, non-cryptographic hash algorithm with good dispersion that has been widely used and is referenced in a number of standards documents.  The purpose of this document is to make information on FNV and open-source code performing all specified sizes of FNV conveniently available to the Internet community.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamegL. Noll¢kaffiliationfGooglednameeK. Vo¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiT. Hansenddatej—-hfilenamegrfc9923eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-153879hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesoopublicationDatei¢granking&√crfcd9923irfcNumber&√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex(The FNV Non-Cryptographic Hash AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document defines common types and groupings that are meant to be used for scheduling purposes, such as events, policies, services, or resources based on date and time.  For the sake of better modularity, the YANG module includes a set of recurrence-related groupings with varying levels of representation (i.e., from basic to advanced) to accommodate a variety of requirements.  It also defines groupings for validating requested schedules and reporting scheduling statuses.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Ma¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationtLancaster UniversitydnamegD. Kingddatej◊5hfilenamegrfc9922eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-154243hkeywordsÑkcalendaringjschedulingdYANGigroupingskobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDatei∞˙ggranking&¬crfcd9922irfcNumber&¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'A Common YANG Data Model for SchedulingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document defines two CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) header parameters for incorporating timestamping based on RFC 3161 into COSE message structures (COSE_Sign and COSE_Sign1).  This enables the use of established timestamping infrastructure per RFC 3161 in COSE-based protocols.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer SITdnamekH. Birkholz¢kaffiliationfLinarodnamejT. Fossati¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamekM. Riechertddatej—/hfilenamegrfc9921eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-153860hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei†˙≤granking&¡crfcd9921irfcNumber&¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfCBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) Header Parameter for Timestamp Tokens as Defined in RFC 3161dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyíThis document specifies version 3 of the RFC Editor Model. The model defines two high-level tasks related to the RFC Series. First, policy definition is the joint responsibility of the RFC Series Working Group (RSWG), which produces policy proposals, and the RFC Series Approval Board (RSAB), which approves such proposals. Second, policy implementation is primarily the responsibility of the RFC Production Center (RPC) as contractually overseen by the IETF Administration Limited Liability Company (IETF LLC). In addition, various responsibilities of the RFC Editor function are now performed alone or in combination by the RSWG, RSAB, RPC, RFC Series Consulting Editor (RSCE), and IETF LLC. Finally, this document specifies the Editorial Stream for publication of future policy definition documents produced through the processes defined herein.

Since the publication of RFC 9280, lessons have been learned about implementing this model. This document lists some of those lessons learned and updates RFC 9280 based on that experience. This document obsoletes RFC 9280.

This document updates RFCs 7841, 7991, 7992, 7993, 7994, 7995, 7996, 7997, 8729, 8730, and 9720.darea£gacronymirfceditordfullvrfceditor - RFC EditordnamejRFC EditorgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationxRFC Series Consulting EditordnamehA. Rossiddatej◊:hfilenamegrfc9920eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrswgdfullxrswg - RFC Series Working GroupdnamexRFC Series Working Groupdtypededwgbidjdoc-153627hkeywordsÖjRFC SeriesxRFC Series Working GroupdRSWGuRFC Production CentercRPCkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateiåÙÎgranking&¿crfcd9920irfcNumber&¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnameiEditorialdslugieditorialetitlexRFC Editor Model (Version 3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòAn IGP Flexible Algorithm (Flex-Algorithm) enables the computation of constraint-based paths within an IGP domain, allowing operators to influence path selection according to administrative policies. This document defines an extension to Flex-Algorithm that allows the inclusion or exclusion of links from path computation based on Administrative Groups (also known as link affinities) associated with the reverse direction of the path under computation.

This document updates RFCs 9350 and 9843 by introducing the new IANA registry that specifies the ordered set of rules that are used to prune links from the topology during the Flex-Algorithm path computation.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Horn¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamejA. Dhamijaddatej—,hfilenamegrfc9917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-153404hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiyñgranking&Ωcrfcd9917irfcNumber&ΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3IGP Flexible Algorithms Reverse Affinity ConstraintdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyîThis document specifies the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6), an extensible mechanism for configuring nodes with network configuration parameters, IP addresses, and prefixes. Parameters can be provided statelessly or in combination with stateful assignment of one or more IPv6 addresses and/or IPv6 prefixes. DHCPv6 can operate either in place of or in addition to stateless address autoconfiguration (SLAAC).

This document obsoletes RFC 8415. It incorporates verified errata and obsoletes the assignment of temporary addresses (the IA_TA option) and the server unicast capability (the Server Unicast option and UseMulticast status code).fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalski¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationcSSWdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationdBUPTdnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationgQA CafednamejT. Wintersddatej◊/hfilenamegrfc9915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-153436hkeywordsÉfDHCPv6dIPv6dDHCPkobsoletedByÄepagessopublicationDatei|ˇ√granking&ªcrfcd9915irfcNumber&ªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberfetotaletitlex5Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThe Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) (RFC 6550) enables data packet routing along a Destination-Oriented Directed Acyclic Graph (DODAG).  However, the default route establishment mechanism relies on hop-by-hop forwarding along the DODAG, which may not always provide optimal routing efficiency.  This document introduces the concept of Destination Advertisement Object (DAO) Projection, a mechanism that allows a RPL Root or an external controller to install optimized routes within the RPL domain.  DAO Projections enable the creation of optimized unicast or multicast routes that do not strictly follow the DODAG structure, thereby improving routing efficiency, reliability, availability, and resource utilization in the RPL domain.  This document specifies two types of Projected Routes (P-Routes) -- Storing Mode and Non-Storing Mode -- and outlines the signaling procedures necessary to establish, maintain, and remove these routes.  This document updates RFCs 6550, 6553, and 8138.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationhAccuKnoxdnamekR.A. Jadhav¢kaffiliationiSandelmandnamemM. Richardsonddatej◊(hfilenamegrfc9914eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-156227hkeywordsÑcIOTcSDNfDetNetcRAWkobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDatei‡[Pgranking&∫crfcd9914irfcNumber&∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Root-Initiated Routing State in the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document surveys the short- and middle-range radio technologies over which providing Deterministic Networking (DetNet), and more specifically, Reliable and Available Wireless (RAW) service is suitable.  It also presents the characteristics that RAW may leverage and explores the applicability of the technologies to carry deterministic flows, as of the time of publication.  The studied technologies are Wi-Fi 6/7, Time-Slotted Channel Hopping (TSCH), 3GPP 5G, and L-band Digital Aeronautical Communications System (LDACS).fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationeInteldnamemD. Cavalcanti¢kaffiliationxUniversitat Oberta de CatalunyadnamemX. Vilajosana¢kaffiliationx!Research Institute CODE / UniBw MdnamejC. Schmitt¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkasddatej—hfilenamegrfc9913eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-156226hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDatei‡[Ogranking&πcrfcd9913irfcNumber&πestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Reliable and Available Wireless (RAW) TechnologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚Reliable and Available Wireless (RAW) extends the reliability and availability of Deterministic Networking (DetNet) to networks composed of any combination of wired and wireless segments.  The RAW architecture leverages and extends RFC 8655 ("Deterministic Networking Architecture") to adapt to challenges that prominently affect the wireless medium, notably intermittent transmission loss.  This document defines a network control loop that optimizes the use of constrained bandwidth and energy while ensuring the expected DetNet services.  The loop involves a new Point of Local Repair (PLR) function in the DetNet Service sub-layer that dynamically selects the DetNet path(s) for packets to route around local connectivity degradation.fadNamepKetan Talaulikardarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Thubertddatej◊3hfilenamegrfc9912eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-156225hkeywordsÅfDetNetkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDatei‡[Ngranking&∏crfcd9912irfcNumber&∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Reliable and Available Wireless (RAW) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx™This document defines a collection of common data types to be used with the YANG data modeling language.  It includes several new type definitions and obsoletes RFC 6991.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvConstructor UniversitydnamepJ. Sch√∂nw√§lderddatej◊2hfilenamegrfc9911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-153011hkeywordsÖdyangjdata typesmaddress typesjtime typesjdate typeskobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateiIöËgranking&∑crfcd9911irfcNumber&∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevCommon YANG Data TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) is used by Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) and Domain Name Registries (DNRs) to provide access to their resource registration information.  The core specifications for RDAP define basic search functionality, but there are various search options related to IP addresses, IP prefixes, and Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs), which are provided by RIRs via their WHOIS services, but for which there is no corresponding RDAP functionality.  This document extends RDAP to support those search options.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamekT. Harrison¢kaffiliationdARINdnamehJ. Singhddatej—*hfilenamegrfc9910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-153121hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei^ˆœgranking&∂crfcd9910irfcNumber&∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRegistration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Regional Internet Registry (RIR) SearchdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨Digital signatures are used within the X.509 Public Key Infrastructure, such as X.509 certificates and Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs), as well as to sign messages.  This document specifies the conventions for using the Stateless Hash-Based Digital Signature Algorithm (SLH-DSA) in the X.509 Public Key Infrastructure.  The conventions for the associated signatures, subject public keys, and private keys are also specified.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamejK. Bashiri¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Fluhrer¢kaffiliationjgenua GmbHdnameiS. Gazdag¢kaffiliationsCryptoNext SecuritydnamelD. Van Geest¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamekS. Kousidisddatej◊.hfilenamegrfc9909eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-153009hkeywordsÑgSLH-DSAhSPHINCS+mPQ Signaturesrpost-quantum X.509kobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateiIê¥granking&µcrfcd9909irfcNumber&µestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure -- Algorithm Identifiers for the Stateless Hash-Based Digital Signature Algorithm (SLH-DSA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document updates RFC 7030, "Enrollment over Secure Transport" (EST), clarifying how the Certificate Signing Request (CSR) Attributes Response can be used by an EST server to specify both CSR attribute Object Identifiers (OIDs) and CSR attribute values, particularly X.509 extension values, that the server expects the client to include in a subsequent CSR request. RFC 9148 is derived from RFC 7030 and is also updated.

RFC 7030 is ambiguous in its specification of the CSR Attributes Response. This has resulted in implementation challenges and implementor confusion because there was no universal understanding of what was specified. This document clarifies the encoding rules.

This document also provides a new straightforward approach: using a template for CSR contents that may be partially filled in by the server. This also allows an EST server to specify a subject Distinguished Name (DN).fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Friel¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemD. von Oheimb¢kaffiliationuThe Industrial LoungednamejD. Harkinsddatej◊.hfilenamegrfc9908eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-153107hkeywordsÑnSubjectAltNamecSANvCertificate ExtensionsxCertificate Signing RequestkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei]^wgranking&¥crfcd9908irfcNumber&¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJClarification and Enhancement of the CSR Attributes Definition in RFC 7030dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty This document provides guidelines for authors and reviewers of specifications containing YANG data models, including IANA-maintained YANG modules. Recommendations and procedures are defined, which are intended to increase interoperability and usability of Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) and RESTCONF protocol implementations that utilize YANG modules.

This document obsoletes RFC 8407; it also updates RFC 8126 by providing additional guidelines for writing the IANA considerations for RFCs that specify IANA-maintained YANG modules.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej◊1hfilenamegrfc9907eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-154769hkeywordsÉgNETCONFhRESTCONFjAutomationkobsoletedByÄepagesUopublicationDatei∑yMgranking&≥crfcd9907irfcNumber&≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÿetotaletitlexMGuidelines for Authors and Reviewers of Documents Containing YANG Data ModelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document retires the use of GOST R 34.10-2001 (mnemonic "ECC-GOST") and GOST R 34.11-94 within DNSSEC.

RFC 5933 (Historic) defined the use of GOST R 34.10-2001 and GOST R 34.11-94 algorithms with DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC). This document updates RFC 5933 by deprecating the use of ECC-GOST.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej◊0hfilenamegrfc9906eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-152571hkeywordsÜcDNSfDNSSECdGOSTialgorithmhrollovergagilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei,‰mgranking&≤crfcd9906irfcNumber&≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Deprecate Usage of ECC-GOST within DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˘This document deprecates the use of the RSASHA1 and
RSASHA1-NSEC3-SHA1 algorithms for the creation of DNS Public Key
(DNSKEY) and Resource Record Signature (RRSIG) records.

It updates RFCs 4034 and 5155 as it deprecates the use of these
algorithms.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej◊0hfilenamegrfc9905eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-152570hkeywordsÜcDNSfDNSSEChrollovergagilityialgorithmdSHA1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei,‰mgranking&±crfcd9905irfcNumber&±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Deprecating the Use of SHA-1 in DNSSEC Signature AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıThe DNSSEC protocol makes use of various cryptographic algorithms to provide authentication of DNS data and proof of nonexistence. To ensure interoperability between DNS resolvers and DNS authoritative servers, it is necessary to specify both a set of algorithm implementation requirements and usage guidelines to ensure that there is at least one algorithm that all implementations support. This document replaces and obsoletes RFC 8624 and moves the canonical source of algorithm implementation requirements and usage guidance for DNSSEC from RFC 8624 to the IANA DNSSEC algorithm registries. This is done to allow the list of requirements to be more easily updated and referenced. Extensions to these registries can be made in future RFCs. This document also updates RFC 9157 and incorporates the revised IANA DNSSEC considerations from that RFC.

This document does not change the recommendation status (MUST, MAY, RECOMMENDED, etc.) of the algorithms listed in RFC 8624; that is the work of future documents.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej◊/hfilenamegrfc9904eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-152569hkeywordsÑcDNSfDNSSEChrollovergagilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei,·ñgranking&∞crfcd9904irfcNumber&∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<DNSSEC Cryptographic Algorithm Recommendation Update ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÄThis document defines a YANG data model that can be used to manage OSPF extensions for Segment Routing over the MPLS data plane.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Zhang¢kaffiliationlRed Hat Inc.dnamegI. Chenddatej— hfilenamegrfc9903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-152860hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDatei8ôïgranking&Øcrfcd9903irfcNumber&ØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCA YANG Data Model for OSPF Segment Routing over the MPLS Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÜThis document defines a YANG data model that can be used to manage IS-IS extensions for Segment Routing (SR) over the MPLS data plane.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationlRed Hat Inc.dnamegI. Chen¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej—hfilenamegrfc9902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-152858hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei8îgranking&Æcrfcd9902irfcNumber&ÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDA YANG Data Model for IS-IS Segment Routing over the MPLS Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯This specification defines a mechanism for the selective disclosure
of individual elements of a JSON data structure used as the payload
of a JSON Web Signature (JWS).  The primary use case is the selective
disclosure of JSON Web Token (JWT) claims.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhAuthletednamegD. Fett¢kaffiliationoKeio UniversitydnameiK. Yasuda¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbellddatej◊,hfilenamegrfc9901eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-152420hkeywordsÉhsecurityfoauth2fSD-JWTkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateil˘granking&≠crfcd9901irfcNumber&≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Selective Disclosure for JSON Web TokensdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx®This document releases IANA-assigned port numbers for services related to the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) that have not been in use in production networks.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej◊8hfilenamegrfc9900eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-152861hkeywordsÉide-assignjdeallocategreleasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei8¢granking&¨crfcd9900irfcNumber&¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Updates to NETCONF Transport Port NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶RFC 8519 defines a YANG data model for Access Control Lists (ACLs). This document specifies a set of extensions that fix many of the limitations of the ACL model as initially defined in RFC 8519. Specifically, it introduces augmentations to the ACL base model to enhance its functionality and applicability.

This document also creates initial versions of IANA-maintained modules for ICMP types and IPv6 extension headers.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej◊+hfilenamegrfc9899eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-153007hkeywordsàifilteringhsecuritymauthorizationematchupacket identificationiscrubbingnpacket patternqpayload filteringkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateiIÜÉgranking&´crfcd9899irfcNumber&´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAExtensions to the YANG Data Model for Access Control Lists (ACLs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜The Neighbor Discovery (ND) protocol is a critical component of the
IPv6 architecture. The protocol uses multicast in many messages. It
also assumes a security model where all nodes on a link are trusted.
Such a design might be inefficient in some scenarios (e.g., use of
multicast in wireless networks) or when nodes are not trustworthy
(e.g., public access networks). These security and operational issues
and the associated mitigation solutions are documented in more than
twenty RFCs. There is a need to track these issues and solutions in a
single document.

To that aim, this document summarizes the published ND issues and
then describes how all these issues originate from three causes.
Addressing the issues is made simpler by addressing the causes. This
document also analyzes the mitigation solutions and demonstrates that
isolating hosts into different subnets and links can help to address
the three causes. Guidance is provided for selecting a suitable
isolation method to prevent potential ND issues.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies DusseldorfdnamegX. Xiao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelE. Vasilenko¢kaffiliationhKPN N.V.dnamegE. Metz¢kaffiliationlVerizon Inc.dnameiG. Mishra¢kaffiliationwEnergy Sciences NetworkdnamekN. Buraglioddatej◊0hfilenamegrfc9898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-152448hkeywordsÖbNDcNDPeSLACCiDHCPv6-PDnhost isolationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei´Ãgranking&™crfcd9898irfcNumber&™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Neighbor Discovery Considerations in IPv6 DeploymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆDatagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) communications, as defined in RFC 4340, are inherently restricted to a single path per connection, despite the availability of multiple network paths between peers. The ability to utilize multiple paths simultaneously for a DCCP session can enhance network resource utilization, improve throughput, and increase resilience to network failures, ultimately enhancing the user experience.

Use cases for Multipath DCCP (MP-DCCP) include mobile devices (e.g., handsets and vehicles) and residential home gateways that maintain simultaneous connections to distinct network types such as cellular and Wireless Local Area Networks (WLANs) or cellular and fixed access networks. Compared to existing multipath transport protocols, such as Multipath TCP (MPTCP), MP-DCCP is particularly suited for latency-sensitive applications with varying requirements for reliability and in-order delivery.

This document specifies a set of protocol extensions to DCCP that enable multipath operations. These extensions maintain the same service model as DCCP while introducing mechanisms to establish and utilize multiple concurrent DCCP flows across different network paths.fadNameoGorry Fairhurstdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationbDTdnamehM. Amend¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnamelA. Brunstrom¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnamejA. Kassler¢kaffiliationx'City St George's / University of LondondnamelV. Rakocevic¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejS. Johnsonddatej◊*hfilenamegrfc9897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-153122hkeywordsäddccpjextensionsimultipathjmultihomedgsubflowjconcurrentlsimultaneoushmobilityfmpdccpgmp-dccpkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDatei^˛œgranking&©crfcd9897irfcNumber&©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfDatagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) Extensions for Multipath Operation with Multiple AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty±This document defines policy for the inclusion of Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) in the definitive versions of RFCs and relevant publication formats. It contains policy requirements from RFC 7996 but removes all requirements related to using a specific SVG profile or implementation code. It also makes the RFC Production Center (RPC) responsible for decisions about SVG tooling and implementation.

This document obsoletes RFC 7996.darea£gacronymirfceditordfullvrfceditor - RFC EditordnamejRFC EditorgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxRFC Series Consulting EditordnamehA. Rossi¢kaffiliation`dnamekN. Brownlee¢kaffiliationuRFC Production CenterdnamejJ. Mahoney¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Thomsonddatej◊7hfilenamegrfc9896eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrswgdfullxrswg - RFC Series Working GroupdnamexRFC Series Working Groupdtypededwgbidjdoc-153435hkeywordsÅcSVGkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei|ı8granking&®crfcd9896irfcNumber&®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnameiEditorialdslugieditorialetitlekSVG in RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¡This document defines an extension to the Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) that enables an Ethernet IEEE 802.1Q aware credit window scheme for destination-specific and shared flow control.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Wiggins¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej—hfilenamegrfc9895eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-153449hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei~}5granking&ßcrfcd9895irfcNumber&ßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexODynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) IEEE 802.1Q Aware Credit Window ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¥This document defines an extension to the Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) that enables a Diffserv aware credit window scheme for destination-specific and shared flow control.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnamehB. Cheng¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Wiggins¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatejÕ‡hfilenamegrfc9894eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-153448hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei~}4granking&¶crfcd9894irfcNumber&¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) Diffserv Aware Credit Window ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis document defines new Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) Data Items that are used to support credit-based flow control. Credit window flow control is used to regulate when data may be sent to an associated virtual or physical queue. These Data Items are extensible and reusable.

This document also defines new messages that support credit window flow control.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnamehB. Cheng¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Wiggins¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Ratliff¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiE. KinzieddatejÕ∆hfilenamegrfc9893eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-153447hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei~}4granking&•crfcd9893irfcNumber&•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWDynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) Credit-Based Flow Control Messages and Data ItemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new Data Item for the Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) to support traffic classification.  Traffic classification information identifies traffic flows based on frame/packet content such as a destination address.  The Data Item is defined in an extensible and reusable fashion.  Its use will be mandated in other documents defining specific DLEP extensions.  This document also introduces DLEP Sub-Data Items and defines two new Sub-Data Items to support Diffserv and Ethernet traffic classification.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnamehB. Cheng¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Wiggins¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehD. Fedykddatej◊(hfilenamegrfc9892eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-153446hkeywordsÇtDiffserv Code PointsxEthernet Priority Code PointskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei~}3granking&§crfcd9892irfcNumber&§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFDynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) Traffic Classification Data ItemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!This document specifies an extension to the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) protocol that allows an ACME server to validate the Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN) Node ID for an ACME client.  A DTN Node ID is an identifier used in the Bundle Protocol (BP) to name a "singleton endpoint": an endpoint that is registered on a single BP Node.  The DTN Node ID is encoded both as a certificate Subject Alternative Name (SAN) identity of type otherName with an Other Name form of BundleEID and as an ACME Identifier type "bundleEID".fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoRKF EngineeringdnamehB. Siposddatej◊2hfilenamegrfc9891eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-152447hkeywordsÇdACMEcDTNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiègranking&£crfcd9891irfcNumber&£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpAutomated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN) Node ID Validation ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxªThis document amends the IANA guidance on the uniqueness of YANG module and submodule names.

The document updates RFC 6020 to clarify how modules and their revisions are handled by IANA.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej◊5hfilenamegrfc9890eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-150611hkeywordsÉgNETCONFhRESTCONFjAutomationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei≤ngranking&¢crfcd9890irfcNumber&¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+An Update to YANG Module Names RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<Network slicing is a feature that was introduced by the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) in Mobile Networks. Realization of 5G slicing implies requirements for all mobile domains, including the Radio Access Network (RAN), Core Network (CN), and Transport Network (TN).

This document describes a network slice realization model for IP/MPLS networks with a focus on the Transport Network fulfilling the service objectives for 5G slicing connectivity. The realization model reuses many building blocks commonly used in service provider networks at the current time.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcHPEdnamemK. Szarkowicz¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejR. Roberts¢kaffiliationcHPEdnamehJ. Lucek¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamemLM. Contrerasddatej◊&hfilenamegrfc9889eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-151850hkeywordsÉeL3VPNeL2VPNmSlice ServicekobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDatei‘granking&°crfcd9889irfcNumber&°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRA Realization of Network Slices for 5G Networks Using Current IP/MPLS TechnologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÛThis document specifies the use of Transport Layer Security (TLS) version 1.3 to secure the communication channel between a Terminal Access Controller Access-Control System Plus (TACACS+) client and server. TACACS+ is a protocol used for Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) in networked environments. The original TACACS+ protocol does not mandate the use of encryption or secure transport. This specification defines a profile for using TLS 1.3 with TACACS+, including guidance on authentication, connection establishment, and operational considerations. The goal is to enhance the confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity of TACACS+ traffic, aligning the protocol with modern security best practices.

This document updates RFC 8907.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamegT. Dahm¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejJ. Heasley¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamepD.C. Medway Gash¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamefA. Otaddatej◊3hfilenamegrfc9887eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-152864hkeywordsÅgTACACS+kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei8¿âgranking&ücrfcd9887irfcNumber&üestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLTerminal Access Controller Access-Control System Plus (TACACS+) over TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document defines the Domain Name System (DNS) functionality of a Drone Remote Identification Protocol (DRIP) Identity Management Entity (DIME).  It is built around DRIP Entity Tags (DETs) to standardize a hierarchical registry structure and associated processes to facilitate trustable and scalable registration and lookup of information related to Unmanned Aircraft Systems (UAS).  The registry system supports issuance, discovery, and verification of DETs, enabling secure identification and association of UAS and their operators.  It also defines the interactions between different classes of registries (root, organizational, and individual) and their respective roles in maintaining the integrity of the registration data.  This architecture enables decentralized, federated operation while supporting privacy, traceability, and regulatory compliance requirements in the context of UAS Remote Identification and other services.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrAX Enterprize, LLCdnameoA. Wiethuechter¢kaffiliationhRTFM llpdnamegJ. Reidddatej—-hfilenamegrfc9886eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddripdfullxdrip - Drone Remote ID ProtocoldnamexDrone Remote ID Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-153031hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateiL<'granking&ûcrfcd9886irfcNumber&ûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1DRIP Entity Tags (DETs) in the Domain Name SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;New technologies are adding new information into IS-IS while deployment scales are simultaneously increasing.  This causes the contents of many critical TLVs to exceed the currently supported limit of 255 octets.  This document codifies the common mechanism of extending the TLV content space through multiple TLVs.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekP. Kaneriya¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemA. Przygienda¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsbergddatej— hfilenamegrfc9885eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-150673hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiΩ˛granking&ùcrfcd9885irfcNumber&ùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMulti-Part TLVs in IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«Segment Routing (SR) leverages source routing to steer packets through an ordered list of instructions called "segments".  SR can be instantiated over the MPLS data plane.  Path Segment Identifiers (PSIDs) are used to identify and correlate bidirectional or end-to-end paths in SR networks.  This document defines procedures (i.e., six new Target Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) Stack sub-TLVs) for the use of LSP Ping to support connectivity verification and fault isolation for SR paths that include PSIDs.  The mechanisms described enable the validation and tracing of SR paths with Path SIDs in MPLS networks, complementing existing SR-MPLS Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) capabilities.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefX. Min¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamegS. Peng¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Gong¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationtBlue Fern ConsultingdnamelC. Pignataroddatej◊1hfilenamegrfc9884eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-150668hkeywordsÇpTarget FEC StackdPSIDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei†granking&úcrfcd9884irfcNumber&úestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYLabel Switched Path Ping for Segment Routing Path Segment Identifier with MPLS Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This document specifies an attribute for a statement of possession of a private key by a certificate subject.  As part of X.509 certificate enrollment, a Certification Authority (CA) typically demands proof that the subject possesses the private key that corresponds to the to-be-certified public key.  In some cases, a CA might accept a signed statement from the certificate subject.  For example, when a certificate subject needs separate certificates for signature and key establishment, a statement that can be validated with the previously issued signature certificate for the same subject might be adequate for subsequent issuance of the key establishment certificate.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej◊2hfilenamegrfc9883eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-150050hkeywordsÅgPKCS#10kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÏtΩgranking&õcrfcd9883irfcNumber&õestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9An Attribute for Statement of Possession of a Private KeydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂The Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Algorithm (ML-DSA), as defined by NIST in FIPS 204, is a post-quantum digital signature scheme that aims to be secure against an adversary in possession of a Cryptographically Relevant Quantum Computer (CRQC).  This document specifies the conventions for using the ML-DSA signature algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  In addition, the algorithm identifier syntax is provided.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx!UK National Cyber Security CentrednameiB. Salter¢kaffiliationx!UK National Cyber Security CentrednamehA. Raine¢kaffiliationsCryptoNext SecuritydnamelD. Van Geestddatej◊3hfilenamegrfc9882eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-150672hkeywordsÉccmsfml-dsaidilithiumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei±ïgranking&öcrfcd9882irfcNumber&öestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOUse of the ML-DSA Signature Algorithm in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅDigital signatures are used within X.509 certificates and Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs), and to sign messages.  This document specifies the conventions for using FIPS 204, the Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Algorithm (ML-DSA) in Internet X.509 certificates and CRLs.  The conventions for the associated signatures, subject public keys, and private key are also described.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcAWSdnamejJ. Massimo¢kaffiliationcAWSdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamepB. E. Westerbaanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9881eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-150671hkeywordsÑfML-DSAkCertificateeX.509dPKIXkobsoletedByÄepages[opublicationDatei±îgranking&ôcrfcd9881irfcNumber&ôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure -- Algorithm Identifiers for the Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Algorithm (ML-DSA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Semantic Definition Format (SDF) is a format for domain experts to use in the creation and maintenance of data and interaction models that describe Things, i.e., physical objects that are available for interaction over a network.  An SDF specification describes definitions of SDF Objects/SDF Things and their associated interactions (Events, Actions, and Properties), as well as the Data types for the information exchanged in those interactions.  Tools convert this format to database formats and other serializations as needed.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnKTC Control ABdnameiM. Koster¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Ker√§nenddatej◊)hfilenamegrfc9880eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdasdfdfullxGasdf - A Semantic Definition Format for Data and Interactions of Thingsdnamex@A Semantic Definition Format for Data and Interactions of Thingsdtypebwgbidjdoc-153434hkeywordsácIoTlDevice ModelqInteraction ModeljAffordancehPropertyfActioneEventkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDatei|Ÿgranking&òcrfcd9880irfcNumber&òestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDSemantic Definition Format (SDF) for Data and Interactions of ThingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document specifies additions and amendments to RFCs 7292 and 8018.  It also obsoletes the RFC 9579.  It defines a way to use the Password-Based Message Authentication Code 1 (PBMAC1), defined in RFC 8018, inside the PKCS #12 syntax.  The purpose of this specification is to permit the use of more modern Password-Based Key Derivation Functions (PBKDFs) and allow for regulatory compliance.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamehA. Karioddatej◊,hfilenamegrfc9879eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-149630hkeywordsÉfpbmac1fpkcs12fpbkdf2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÃcÈgranking&ócrfcd9879irfcNumber&óestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOUse of Password-Based Message Authentication Code 1 (PBMAC1) in PKCS #12 SyntaxdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®The Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) has identified cases where different SIP private header extensions referred to as "P-" header fields, and defined in RFC 7315, need to be included in SIP requests and responses where they were not allowed according to RFC 7315. This document updates RFC 7315, in order to allow inclusion of the affected "P-" header fields in such requests and responses. This document obsoletes RFC 7976. The changes related to RFC 7976 involve the inclusion of the P-Visited-Network-ID header field in SIP responses.

This document also makes updates to RFC 7315 in order to fix misalignments that occurred when RFC 3455 was obsoleted by RFC 7315.fadNamekAndy Newtondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationdETSIdnamejN. Biondic¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesskeddatej◊4hfilenamegrfc9878eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-152413hkeywordsÖd3GPPgVisitedbIDbP-cIMSkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatei øgranking&ñcrfcd9878irfcNumber&ñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpUpdates to Private Header (P-Header) Extension Usage in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Requests and ResponsesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document defines a new Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) extension, "geofeed1", for indicating that an RDAP server hosts geofeed URLs for its IP network objects.  It also defines a new media type and a new link relation type for the associated link objects included in responses.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdARINdnamehJ. Singh¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamekT. Harrisonddatej—+hfilenamegrfc9877eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-150049hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÏrIgranking&ïcrfcd9877irfcNumber&ïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCRegistration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Extension for Geofeed DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document updates RFC 7252 by modifying the registration procedures for the "CoAP Content-Formats" IANA registry, within the "Constrained RESTful Environments (CoRE) Parameters" registry group.  This document also introduces a new column, "Media Type", to the registry.  Furthermore, this document reserves Content-Format identifiers 64998 and 64999 for use in documentation.fadNamekMike Bishopdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfLinarodnamejT. Fossati¢kaffiliationqIoTconsultancy.nldnamegE. Dijkddatej◊/hfilenamegrfc9876eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-151910hkeywordsÖdIANAlregistrationfupdatedCoAPnContent-FormatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei$Tgranking&îcrfcd9876irfcNumber&îestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgUpdates to the IANA Registration Procedures for Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Content-FormatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This specification introduces a means of describing the relationships between stored responses in HTTP caches, grouping them by associating a stored response with one or more strings.fadNamekMike Bishopdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamemM. Nottinghamddatej◊/hfilenamegrfc9875eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-150719hkeywordsÉdHTTPgCachinglInvalidationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei≠•granking&ìcrfcd9875irfcNumber&ìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqHTTP Cache GroupsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty˙The Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) includes commands for clients to delete domain and host objects, both of which are used to publish information in the Domain Name System (DNS).  EPP also includes guidance for deletions that is intended to avoid DNS resolution disruptions and maintain data consistency.  However, operational relationships between objects can make that guidance difficult to implement.  Some EPP clients have developed operational practices to delete those objects that have unintended impacts on DNS resolution and security.  This document describes best current practices and proposes new potential practices to delete domain and host objects that reduce the risk of DNS resolution failure and maintain client-server data consistency.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamejW. Carroll¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamejG. Akiwateddatej◊)hfilenamegrfc9874eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-149757hkeywordsÅcEPPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehŸÁ∂granking&ícrfcd9874irfcNumber&íestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÙetotaletitlexdBest Practices for Deletion of Domain and Host Objects in the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑The Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) does not inherently support internationalized email addresses because the specifications for these addresses did not exist when EPP was developed.  This document describes a command-response extension that adds support for associating an additional email address with an EPP contact object.  That additional email address can be either an internationalized email address or an ASCII-only address.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. Belyavsky¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej—%hfilenamegrfc9873eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-150047hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÏ
ngranking&ëcrfcd9873irfcNumber&ëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQAdditional Email Address Extension for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZOn networks providing IPv4-IPv6 translation (RFC 7915), hosts and other endpoints need to know the IPv6 prefix(es) used for translation (the NAT64 prefix (RFC 6052)).  This document provides guidelines for NAT64 prefix discovery, specifically recommending obtaining the NAT64 prefix from the Router Advertisement option (RFC 8781) when available.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwEnergy Sciences NetworkdnamekN. Buraglio¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Jensen¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkovaddatej◊4hfilenamegrfc9872eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-149813hkeywordsÖdIPv6eDNS64fPREF64eNAT64dCLATkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateh‹£»granking&êcrfcd9872irfcNumber&êestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRecommendations for Discovering IPv6 Prefix Used for IPv6 Address SynthesisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThis document describes a BGP-based routing solution to establish end-to-end intent-aware paths across a multi-domain transport network. The transport network can span multiple service provider and customer network domains. The BGP intent-aware paths can be used to steer traffic flows for service routes that need a specific intent. This solution is called BGP Color-Aware Routing (BGP CAR).

This document describes the routing framework and BGP extensions to enable intent-aware routing using the BGP CAR solution. The solution defines two new BGP SAFIs (BGP CAR SAFI and BGP VPN CAR SAFI) for IPv4 and IPv6. It also defines an extensible Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) model for both SAFIs that allows multiple NLRI types to be defined for different use cases. Each type of NLRI contains key and TLV-based non-key fields for efficient encoding of different per-prefix information. This specification defines two NLRI types: Color-Aware Route NLRI and IP Prefix NLRI. It defines non-key TLV types for the MPLS label stack, SR-MPLS label index, and Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) Segment Identifiers (SIDs). This solution also defines a new Local Color Mapping (LCM) Extended Community.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefD. Rao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Agrawalddatej◊(hfilenamegrfc9871eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-152446hkeywordsÖtintent-aware routingtintent-based routingpcolor-aware VPNsxcolor-aware BGP transportsBGP extensible NLRIkobsoletedByÄepagesLopublicationDateiègranking&ècrfcd9871irfcNumber&èestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGP Color-Aware Routing (CAR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxdThis document specifies new IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Elements for UDP Options.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.Kddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9870eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-149922hkeywordsÇlsurplus areakUDP optionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÂ°Dgranking&écrfcd9870irfcNumber&éestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGExport of UDP Options Information in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔThis document specifies how a UDP Options sender implements Datagram Packetization Layer Path MTU Discovery (DPLPMTUD) as a robust method for Path MTU Discovery (PMTUD).  This method uses the UDP Options packetization layer.  It allows an application to discover the largest size of datagram that can be sent across a network path.  It also provides a way to allow the application to periodically verify the current Maximum Packet Size (MPS) supported by a path and to update this when required.fadNameoGorry Fairhurstdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamehT. Jonesddatej◊'hfilenamegrfc9869eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-149921hkeywordsÜcUDPkUDP-OptionsePMTUDgPLPMTUDhDPLPMTUDhDatagramkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÂ°Cgranking&çcrfcd9869irfcNumber&çestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJDatagram Packetization Layer Path MTU Discovery (DPLPMTUD) for UDP OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&Transport protocols are extended through the use of transport header options.  This document updates RFC 768 (UDP) by indicating the location, syntax, and semantics for UDP transport layer options within the surplus area after the end of the UDP user data but before the end of the IP datagram.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehC. Heardddatej—hfilenamegrfc9868eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-149920hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDatehÂ°Bgranking&åcrfcd9868irfcNumber&åestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTransport Options for UDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXAn Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) extension defined in RFC 8784 allows IPsec traffic to be protected against someone storing VPN communications and decrypting them later, when (and if) a Cryptographically Relevant Quantum Computer (CRQC) is available. The protection is achieved by means of a Post-quantum Preshared Key (PPK) that is mixed into the session keys calculation. However, this protection does not cover an initial IKEv2 Security Association (SA), which might be unacceptable in some scenarios. This specification defines an alternative way to provide protection against quantum computers, which is similar to the solution defined in RFC 8784, but it also protects the initial IKEv2 SA.

RFC 8784 assumes that PPKs are static and thus they are only used when an initial IKEv2 SA is created. If a fresh PPK is available before the IKE SA expires, then the only way to use it is to delete the current IKE SA and create a new one from scratch, which is inefficient. This specification defines a way to use PPKs in active IKEv2 SAs for creating additional IPsec SAs and rekey operations.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej◊.hfilenamegrfc9867eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-151804hkeywordsÜuinternet key exchangepquantum computerlpost quantumlpost-quantumlquantum safecPPKkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei≤ogranking&ãcrfcd9867irfcNumber&ãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexùMixing Preshared Keys in the IKE_INTERMEDIATE and CREATE_CHILD_SA Exchanges of the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) for Post-Quantum SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥By and large, correct operation of a network running the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) requires border routers to be up.  In many applications, it is beneficial for the nodes to detect a failure of a border router as soon as possible to trigger fallback actions.  This document specifies the Root Node Failure Detector (RNFD), an extension to RPL that expedites detection of border router crashes by having nodes collaboratively monitor the status of a given border router.  The extension introduces an additional state at each node, a new type of RPL Control Message Option for synchronizing this state among different nodes, and the coordination algorithm itself.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of WarsawdnamekK. Iwanickiddatej—hfilenamegrfc9866eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-149923hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÂ¶Ãgranking&äcrfcd9866irfcNumber&äestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâRoot Node Failure Detector (RNFD): Fast Detection of Border Router Crashes in the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document updates RFCs 7643 and 7644 by defining additional System for Cross-Domain Identity Management (SCIM) query parameters and result attributes to allow use of cursor-based pagination in SCIM service providers that are implemented with existing codebases, databases, or APIs where cursor-based pagination is already well established.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamekM. Peterson¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejD. Zollner¢kaffiliationsAmazon Web ServicesdnameiA. Sehgalddatej—#hfilenamegrfc9865eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdscimdfullx2scim - System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdnamex+System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-149985hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehËUgranking&âcrfcd9865irfcNumber&âestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVCursor-Based Pagination of System of Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) ResourcesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis specification refers to cryptographic algorithm identifiers that fully specify the cryptographic operations to be performed, including any curve, key derivation function (KDF), and hash functions, as being "fully specified". It refers to cryptographic algorithm identifiers that require additional information beyond the algorithm identifier to determine the cryptographic operations to be performed as being "polymorphic". This specification creates fully-specified algorithm identifiers for registered JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE) and CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) polymorphic algorithm identifiers, enabling applications to use only fully-specified algorithm identifiers. It deprecates those polymorphic algorithm identifiers.

This specification updates RFCs 7518, 8037, and 9053. It deprecates polymorphic algorithms defined by RFCs 8037 and 9053 and provides fully-specified replacements for them. It adds to the instructions to designated experts in RFCs 7518 and 9053.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvSelf-Issued ConsultingdnamejM.B. Jones¢kaffiliationlTradeverifyddnameiO. Steeleddatej◊-hfilenamegrfc9864eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-150726hkeywordsÖx#Cryptographic Algorithm Identifiersx)JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE)x)CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE)vPolymorphic AlgorithmswAlgorithm Cipher SuiteskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei¡ãgranking&àcrfcd9864irfcNumber&àestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvFully-Specified Algorithms for JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE) and CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™Color is a 32-bit numerical (unsigned integer) attribute used to associate a Traffic Engineering (TE) tunnel or policy with an intent or objective.  For example, a TE Tunnel constructed to deliver low latency services and whose path is optimized for delay can be tagged with a color that represents "low latency." This document specifies extensions to the Path Computation Element Protocol (PCEP) to carry the color attribute.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamenB. Rajagopalan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegS. Peng¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelM. Koldychev¢kaffiliationxVerizon Communications Inc.dnameiG. Mishraddatej◊&hfilenamegrfc9863eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-150665hkeywordsÅecolorkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateiò1granking&ácrfcd9863irfcNumber&áestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Path Computation Element Protocol (PCEP) Extension for ColordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°A Segment Routing (SR) Policy is an ordered list of instructions called "segments" that represent a source-routed policy. Packet flows are steered into an SR Policy on a node where it is instantiated. An SR Policy is made of one or more Candidate Paths.

This document specifies the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) extension to signal Candidate Paths of an SR Policy. Additionally, this document updates RFC 8231 to allow delegation and setup of an SR Label Switched Path (LSP) without using the path computation request and reply messages. This document is applicable to both Segment Routing over MPLS (SR-MPLS) and Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6).fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelM. Koldychev¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehS. Sidor¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehC. Barth¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegS. Peng¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejH. Bidgoliddatej◊+hfilenamegrfc9862eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-150654hkeywordsÉdPCEPiSR PolicynCandidate PathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiygranking&Ücrfcd9862irfcNumber&ÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Segment Routing (SR) Policy Candidate PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty4This document defines four eXtendable-Output Functions (XOFs), hash functions with output of arbitrary length, named TurboSHAKE128, TurboSHAKE256, KT128, and KT256.

All four functions provide efficient and secure hashing primitives, and the last two are able to exploit the parallelism of the implementation in a scalable way.

This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group. It builds up on the definitions of the permutations and of the sponge construction in NIST FIPS 202 and is meant to serve as a stable reference and an implementation guide.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmABN AMRO BankdnamejB. Viguier¢kaffiliationjzkSecuritydnamegD. Wong¢kaffiliationrSTMicroelectronicsdnamemG. Van Assche¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegQ. Dang¢kaffiliationrRadboud UniversitydnameiJ. Daemenddatej◊%hfilenamegrfc9861eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-150048hkeywordsÜfKeccakfSakuranKangarooTwelvejTurboSHAKErCryptographic HashxeXtendable Output FunctionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÏhıgranking&Öcrfcd9861irfcNumber&ÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexKangarooTwelve and TurboSHAKEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This document specifies the use of Topology Independent Loop-Free Alternate (TI-LFA) mechanisms with Multicast-only Fast Reroute (MoFRR) for Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM).  The TI-LFA provides Fast Reroute (FRR) protection for unicast traffic in IP networks by precomputing backup paths that avoid potential failures.  By integrating TI-LFA with MoFRR, this document extends the benefits of FRR mechanisms to multicast traffic, enabling enhanced resilience and minimized packet loss in multicast networks.  The document outlines an optional approach to implement TI-LFA in conjunction with MoFRR for PIM, ensuring that multicast traffic is rapidly rerouted in the event of a failure.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefY. Liu¢kaffiliationnFuturewei Inc.dnamejM. McBride¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. Xie¢kaffiliationtNew H3C TechnologiesdnamefC. Linddatej◊*hfilenamegrfc9860eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-150610hkeywordsàcPIMeMoFRRcLFAfTI-LFAgSR-MPLSdSRv6jRPF VectornJoin attributekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei™¬granking&Ñcrfcd9860irfcNumber&ÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkMulticast-Only Fast Reroute (MoFRR) Based on Topology Independent Loop-Free Alternate (TI-LFA) Fast ReroutedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This document generalizes and extends the use of DNS NOTIFY (RFC 1996) beyond conventional zone transfer hints to allow other types of actions that were previously lacking a trigger mechanism to be triggered via the DNS. Notifications merely nudge the receiver to initiate a predefined action promptly (instead of on a schedule); they do not alter the action itself (including any security checks it might employ).

To enable this functionality, a method for discovering the receiver endpoint for such notification messages is introduced, via the new DSYNC record type. Notification types are recorded in a new registry, with initial support for parental NS and DS record updates including DNSSEC bootstrapping.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxThe Swedish Internet FoundationdnamekJ. Stenstam¢kaffiliationx"deSEC & Secure Systems EngineeringdnamelP. Thomassen¢kaffiliationmStandcore LLCdnameiJ. Levineddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9859eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-149756hkeywordsÑjDelegationjAutomationbDSfDNSSECkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehŸ·Àgranking&Écrfcd9859irfcNumber&ÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGeneralized DNS NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty‰This document extends HSS/LMS (RFC 8554) by defining parameter sets that use alternative hash functions. These include hash functions that result in signatures with significantly smaller sizes than the signatures that use the RFC 8554 parameter sets and should have sufficient security.

This document is a product of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF). The IRTF publishes the results of Internet-related research and development activities. These results might not be suitable for deployment. This RFC represents the consensus of the Crypto Forum Research Group of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF). Documents approved for publication by the IRSG are not candidates for any level of Internet Standard; see Section 2 of RFC 7841.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Fluhrer¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegQ. Dangddatej—hfilenamegrfc9858eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-150045hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÏ*granking&Çcrfcd9858irfcNumber&ÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex;Additional Parameter Sets for HSS/LMS Hash-Based SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document describes a mechanism used to collect Segment Routing (SR) Policy information that is locally available in a node and advertise it into BGP - Link State (BGP-LS) updates.  Such information can be used by external components for path computation, reoptimization, service placement, network visualization, etc.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej◊'hfilenamegrfc9857eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-150651hkeywordsâcBGPfBGP-LSoSegment RoutingbSRiSR PolicygSR-MPLSdSRv6sTraffic EngineeringmBGP SR PolicykobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateikEgranking&Åcrfcd9857irfcNumber&ÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Advertisement of Segment Routing Policies Using BGP - Link StatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàIn Ethernet Virtual Private Networks (EVPNs), IP multicast traffic replication and delivery play a crucial role in enabling efficient and scalable Layer 2 and Layer 3 services.  A common deployment scenario involves redundant multicast sources that ensure high availability and resiliency.  However, the presence of redundant sources can lead to duplicate IP multicast traffic in the network, causing inefficiencies and increased overhead.  This document specifies extensions to the EVPN multicast procedures that allow for the suppression of duplicate IP multicast traffic from redundant sources.  The proposed mechanisms enhance the EVPN's capability to deliver multicast traffic efficiently while maintaining high availability.  These extensions are applicable to various EVPN deployment scenarios and provide guidelines to ensure consistent and predictable behavior across diverse network topologies.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Kotalwar¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. Sathappan¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamefW. Linddatej◊$hfilenamegrfc9856eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-149732hkeywordsÜlWarm standbykhot standbydOISMrredundant G-sourcecSFGqSingle Flow GroupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh’≠dgranking&Äcrfcd9856irfcNumber&ÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Multicast Source Redundancy in EVPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyπThis document presents Topology Independent Loop-Free Alternate (TI-LFA) Fast Reroute (FRR), which is aimed at providing protection of node and Adjacency segments within the Segment Routing (SR) framework.  This FRR behavior builds on proven IP FRR concepts being LFAs, Remote LFAs (RLFAs), and Directed Loop-Free Alternates (DLFAs).  It extends these concepts to provide guaranteed coverage in any two-connected networks using a link-state IGP.  An important aspect of TI-LFA is the FRR path selection approach establishing protection over the expected post-convergence paths from the Point of Local Repair (PLR), reducing the operational need to control the tie-breaks among various FRR options.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationcHPEdnamekA. Bashandy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationiINSA LyondnamekP. Francois¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyerddatej◊%hfilenamegrfc9855eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-150609hkeywordsàeTILFAcLFAcFRRlfast reroutehrecoverybSRjprotectionkconvergencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei¶dgranking&crfcd9855irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Topology Independent Fast Reroute Using Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$Route discovery for symmetric and asymmetric Peer-to-Peer (P2P) traffic flows is a desirable feature in Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLNs).  For that purpose, this document specifies AODV-RPL -- the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) based on Ad hoc On-demand Distance Vector (AODV) routing.  AODV-RPL is a reactive P2P route discovery mechanism for both hop-by-hop routes and source routing.  Paired instances are used to construct directional paths for cases where there are asymmetric links between source and target nodes.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtBlue Meadow NetworksdnamelC.E. Perkins¢kaffiliationxIndian Institute of SciencednamelS.V.R. Anand¢kaffiliationqSRM University-APdnamenS. Anamalamudi¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefB. Liuddatej◊'hfilenamegrfc9854eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-150670hkeywordsÉdAODVxPeer-to-Peer Route DiscoveryjAsymmetrickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei¶igranking&~crfcd9854irfcNumber&~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~AODV-RPL: The Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) Based on Ad Hoc On-Demand Distance Vector (AODV) RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨This document specifies a Return Routability Check (RRC) subprotocol for use in the context of the Connection ID (CID) construct for the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol versions 1.2 and 1.3.

Implementations offering the CID functionality described in RFCs 9146 and 9147 are encouraged to also provide the RRC functionality described in this document. For this reason, this document updates RFCs 9146 and 9147.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhUniBw M.dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Kraus¢kaffiliationfLinarodnamejT. Fossatiddatej◊4hfilenamegrfc9853eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-154244hkeywordsÉdDTLScRRCcCIDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatei∞ˇugranking&}crfcd9853irfcNumber&}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Return Routability Check for DTLS 1.2 and 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxyThis document describes a mechanism in Transport Layer Security (TLS) for encrypting a message under a server public key.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamefK. Oku¢kaffiliationxCryptography Consulting LLCdnamekN. Sullivan¢kaffiliationeApplednamejC. A. Woodddatej……hfilenamegrfc9849eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-154077hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateiß»ygranking&ycrfcd9849irfcNumber&yestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTLS Encrypted Client HellodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!To use TLS Encrypted ClientHello (ECH), the client needs to learn the ECH configuration for a server before it attempts a connection to the server.  This specification provides a mechanism for conveying the ECH configuration information via DNS, using a SVCB or HTTPS resource record (RR).fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtMeta Platforms, Inc.dnamekB. Schwartz¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnameiM. Bishop¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnameiE. Nygrenddatej◊1hfilenamegrfc9848eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-154076hkeywordsâoech SvcParamKeylech SvcParamcECHdSVCBvHTTPS Resource RecordsgTLS SNImEncrypted SNIvServer Name Indicationx Encrypted Server Name IndicationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateiß»xgranking&xcrfcd9848irfcNumber&xestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexABootstrapping TLS Encrypted ClientHello with DNS Service BindingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis document updates the changes to the TLS and DTLS IANA registries made in RFC 8447. It adds a new value, "D" for discouraged, to the "Recommended" column of the selected TLS registries and adds a "Comment" column to all active registries that do not already have a "Comment" column. Finally, it updates the registration request instructions.

This document updates RFC 8447.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhCyberArkdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej—(hfilenamegrfc9847eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-153030hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateiL4øgranking&wcrfcd9847irfcNumber&westateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&IANA Registry Updates for TLS and DTLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyuReducing humankind's environmental footprint and making technology more environmentally sustainable are among the biggest challenges of our age. Networks play an important part in this challenge. On one hand, they enable applications that help to reduce this footprint. On the other hand, they significantly contribute to this footprint themselves. Therefore, methods to make networking technology itself "greener" and to manage and operate networks in ways that reduce their environmental footprint without impacting their utility need to be explored. This document outlines a corresponding set of opportunities, along with associated research challenges, for networking technology in general and management technology in particular to become greener, i.e., more sustainable, with reduced greenhouse gas emissions and less negative impact on the environment.

This document is a product of the Network Management Research Group (NMRG) of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF). This document reflects the consensus of the research group. It is not a candidate for any level of Internet Standard and is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationiSympotechdnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationx*NC State University & Blue Fern ConsultingdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationx#University of California Santa CruzdnamekC. Westphal¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelL. Ciavaglia¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliation`dnamejM-P. Odiniddatej—hfilenamegrfc9845eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnmrgdfullxnmrg - Network ManagementdnamerNetwork Managementdtypebrgbidjdoc-149919hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÂå=granking&ucrfcd9845irfcNumber&uestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex?Challenges and Opportunities in Management for Green NetworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how the zone identifier of an IPv6 scoped address, defined in the IPv6 Scoped Address Architecture specification (RFC 4007), should be entered into a user interface.  This document obsoletes RFC 6874 and updates RFCs 4007, 7622, and 8089.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hindenddatej—"hfilenamegrfc9844eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-149296hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh©*granking&tcrfcd9844irfcNumber&testateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Entering IPv6 Zone Identifiers in User InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}Many networks configure the IGP link metric relative to the link capacity, and high bandwidth traffic gets routed per the link capacity. Flexible Algorithms provide mechanisms to create constraint-based paths in an IGP. This specification documents a generic metric-type and a set of bandwidth-related constraints to be used in Flexible Algorithms.

This document updates RFC 9350.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnameiW. Britto¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnameiR. Shetty¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnameeT. Liddatej◊#hfilenamegrfc9843eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-149550hkeywordsÇbAScIGPkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatehƒØ—granking&scrfcd9843irfcNumber&sestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCIGP Flexible Algorithms: Bandwidth, Delay, Metrics, and ConstraintsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9917∂habstracty≠This document specifies a mechanism for dictionary-based compression in the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).  By utilizing this technique, clients and servers can reduce the size of transmitted data, leading to improved performance and reduced bandwidth consumption.  This document extends existing HTTP compression methods and provides guidelines for the delivery and use of compression dictionaries within the HTTP protocol.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnameiP. Meenan¢kaffiliationlShopify Inc.dnamehY. Weissddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9842eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-149803hkeywordsÑvcompression dictionarymshared brotlitzstandard dictionaryqdelta compressionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh‹T≠granking&rcrfcd9842irfcNumber&restateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Compression Dictionary TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis specification defines a data format for shared brotli compression, which adds support for shared dictionaries, large window, and a container format to brotli (RFC 7932).  Shared dictionaries and large window support allow significant compression gains compared to regular brotli.  This document specifies an extension to the method defined in RFC 7932.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamemJ. Alakuijala¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamehT. Duong¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamenE. Kliuchnikov¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamekZ. Szabadka¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamemL. Vandevenneddatej◊#hfilenamegrfc9841eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet Transportdtypedareabidjdoc-149802hkeywordsÅxdictionary compression lz77kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh‹RÎgranking&qcrfcd9841irfcNumber&qestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Shared Brotli Compressed Data FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty^This document specifies receiver-driven Low Extra Delay Background Transport (rLEDBAT) -- a set of mechanisms that enable the execution of a less-than-best-effort congestion control algorithm for TCP at the receiver end.  This document is a product of the Internet Congestion Control Research Group (ICCRG) of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF).gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx Universidad Carlos III de MadriddnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationx Universidad Carlos III de MadriddnametA. Garc√≠a-Mart√≠nez¢kaffiliation`dnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationiConfluentdnamerP. Balasubramanianddatej◊$hfilenamegrfc9840eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiccrgdfullx#iccrg - Internet Congestion ControldnamexInternet Congestion Controldtypebrgbidjdoc-149626hkeywordsÇrCongestion controlx'scavenger/less-than-best-effort traffickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÃ:¶granking&pcrfcd9840irfcNumber&pestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexErLEDBAT: Receiver-Driven Low Extra Delay Background Transport for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx±This document discusses subsets of the Unicode character repertoire for use in protocols and data formats and specifies three subsets recommended for use in IETF specifications.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsTextuality ServicesdnamegT. Bray¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej◊/hfilenamegrfc9839eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-149295hkeywordsÖgUnicodeeUTF-8jSurrogatesmNoncharactersrControl characterskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateh©granking&ocrfcd9839irfcNumber&oestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Unicode Character Repertoire SubsetsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document presents an extension to the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) for the purpose of group key management. The protocol is in conformance with the Multicast Security (MSEC) Group Key Management architecture, which contains two components: member registration and group rekeying. Both components are required for a Group Controller/Key Server (GCKS) to provide authorized Group Members (GMs) with IPsec Group Security Associations (GSAs). The GMs then exchange IP multicast or other group traffic as IPsec packets.

This document obsoletes RFC 6407.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslov¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegB. Weisddatej—hfilenamegrfc9838eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-151829hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDatei√:granking&ncrfcd9838irfcNumber&nestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOGroup Key Management Using the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document describes an experiment in which VPN service information is encoded in an experimental IPv6 Destination Option. The experimental IPv6 Destination Option is called the VPN Service Option.

One purpose of this experiment is to demonstrate that the VPN Service Option can be deployed in a production network. Another purpose is to demonstrate that the security measures described in this document are sufficient to protect a VPN. Finally, this document encourages replication of the experiment.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsNTT DOCOMO BUSINESSdnameiY. Kamite¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalilddatej◊-hfilenamegrfc9837eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-149294hkeywordsÉdIPv6rDestination OptioncVPNkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateh©ïgranking&mcrfcd9837irfcNumber&mestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'The IPv6 VPN Service Destination OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThis document defines a YANG data model, referred to as the "AC Glue"
model, to augment the LxVPN Service Model (LxSM) and LxVPN Network
Model (LxNM) with references to attachment circuits (ACs).  The AC
Glue model enables a provider to associate Layer 2/3 VPN (LxVPN)
services with the underlying AC infrastructure, thereby facilitating
consistent provisioning and management of new or existing ACs in
conjunction with LxVPN services.  Specifically, by introducing an
integrated approach to AC and LxVPN management, this model supports
Attachment Circuit as a Service (ACaaS) and provides a standardized
mechanism for aligning AC/VPN requests with the network
configurations required to deliver them.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejR. Roberts¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Diosddatej◊!hfilenamegrfc9836eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-149780hkeywordsémSlice ServiceeL3VPNeL2VPNjAutomationrNetwork AutomationmOrchestrationpservice deliverytService provisioningtservice segmentationsservice flexibilityvservice simplificationoNetwork Serviced3GPPoNetwork SlicingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh€JDgranking&lcrfcd9836irfcNumber&lestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXA YANG Data Model for Augmenting VPN Service and Network Models with Attachment CircuitsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document specifies a network model for attachment circuits. The model can be used for the provisioning of attachment circuits prior or during service provisioning (e.g., VPN, Network Slice Service). A companion service model is specified in the YANG Data Models for Bearers and 'Attachment Circuits'-as-a-Service (ACaaS) (I-D.ietf- opsawg-teas-attachment-circuit).

The module augments the base network ('ietf-network') and the Service Attachment Point (SAP) models with the detailed information for the provisioning of attachment circuits in Provider Edges (PEs).fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejR. Roberts¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeB. Wuddatej◊!hfilenamegrfc9835eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-149779hkeywordsémSlice ServiceeL3VPNeL2VPNjAutomationrNetwork AutomationmOrchestrationpservice deliverytService provisioningtservice segmentationsservice flexibilityvservice simplificationoNetwork Serviced3GPPoNetwork SlicingkobsoletedByÄepagesyopublicationDateh€JCgranking&kcrfcd9835irfcNumber&kestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1A Network YANG Data Model for Attachment CircuitsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoDelivery of network services assumes that appropriate setup is
provisioned over the links that connect customer termination points
and a provider network. The required setup to allow successful data
exchange over these links is referred to as an attachment circuit
(AC), while the underlying link is referred to as a "bearer".

This document specifies a YANG service data model for ACs. This model
can be used for the provisioning of ACs before or during service
provisioning (e.g., RFC 9543 Network Slice Service).

The document also specifies a YANG service data model for managing
bearers over which ACs are established.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejR. Roberts¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeB. Wuddatej◊"hfilenamegrfc9834eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-149778hkeywordsémSlice ServiceeL3VPNeL2VPNjAutomationrNetwork AutomationmOrchestrationpservice deliverytService provisioningtservice segmentationsservice flexibilityvservice simplificationoNetwork Serviced3GPPoNetwork SlicingkobsoletedByÄepagesñopublicationDateh€JBgranking&jcrfcd9834irfcNumber&jestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIYANG Data Models for Bearers and Attachment Circuits as a Service (ACaaS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£The document specifies a common attachment circuits (ACs) YANG data
model, which is designed to be reusable by other models.  This design
is meant to ensure consistent AC structures among models that
manipulate ACs.  For example, this common model can be reused by
service models to expose ACs as a service, service models that
require binding a service to a set of ACs, network and device models
to provision ACs, etc.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejR. Roberts¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeB. Wuddatej◊"hfilenamegrfc9833eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-149777hkeywordsémSlice ServiceeL3VPNeL2VPNjAutomationrNetwork AutomationmOrchestrationpservice deliverytService provisioningtservice segmentationsservice flexibilityvservice simplificationoNetwork Serviced3GPPoNetwork SlicingkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateh€J@granking&icrfcd9833irfcNumber&iestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0A Common YANG Data Model for Attachment CircuitsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document specifies a mechanism referred to as "Intent-Driven Service Mapping". The mechanism uses BGP to express Intent-based association of overlay routes with underlay routes having specific Traffic Engineering (TE) characteristics satisfying a certain Service Level Agreement (SLA). This is achieved by defining new constructs to group underlay routes with sufficiently similar TE characteristics into identifiable classes (called "Transport Classes" or "TCs"), that overlay routes use as an ordered set to resolve reachability (Resolution Schemes) towards service endpoints. These constructs can be used, for example, to realize the "IETF Network Slice" defined in the TEAS Network Slices framework (RFC 9543).

Additionally, this document specifies protocol procedures for BGP that enable dissemination of service mapping information in a network that may span multiple cooperating administrative domains. These domains may be administered either by the same provider or by closely coordinating providers. A new BGP address family that leverages the procedures described in RFC 4364 ("BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)") and follows the NLRI encoding described in RFC 8277 ("Using BGP to Bind MPLS Labels to Address Prefixes") is defined to enable each advertised underlay route to be identified by its class. This new address family is called "BGP Classful Transport" (or "BGP CT").fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamepK. Vairavakkalai¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnameoN. Venkataramanddatej◊'hfilenamegrfc9832eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-149545hkeywordsÑfBGP-CTbCTtIntent-Based RoutingsBGP Service MappingkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDatehƒÅògranking&hcrfcd9832irfcNumber&hestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGP Classful Transport PlanesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÆThis document specifies the signaling of additional Segment Routing (SR) Segment Types for SR Policies in BGP using the SR Policy Subsequent Address Family Identifier (SAFI).fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiP. Mattes¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegD. Jainddatej◊$hfilenamegrfc9831eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-149544hkeywordsÑoSegment RoutinghBGP-SRTEeSR-TErBGP SR Policy SAFIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehƒÅògranking&gcrfcd9831irfcNumber&gestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Segment Type Extensions for BGP Segment Routing (SR) PolicydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=A Segment Routing (SR) Policy is an ordered list of segments (also referred to as "instructions") that define a source-routed policy. An SR Policy consists of one or more Candidate Paths (CPs), each comprising one or more segment lists. A headend can be provisioned with these CPs using various mechanisms such as Command-Line Interface (CLI), Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF), Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP), or BGP.

This document specifies how BGP can be used to distribute SR Policy CPs. It introduces a BGP SAFI for advertising a CP of an SR Policy and defines sub-TLVs for the Tunnel Encapsulation Attribute to signal information related to these CPs.

Furthermore, this document updates RFC 9012 by extending the Color Extended Community to support additional steering modes over SR Policy.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiP. Mattes¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegD. Jainddatej—hfilenamegrfc9830eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-149543hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDatehƒÅògranking&fcrfcd9830irfcNumber&festateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Advertising Segment Routing Policies in BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx•This document revises how the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) handles Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Number extensions.  This document updates RFC 6487.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationjWorkonlinednamekB. Maddison¢kaffiliationgOpenBSDdnamejT. Buehlerddatej◊.hfilenamegrfc9829eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-147260hkeywordsÖdRPKIpRouting SecuritycBGPeX.509cCRLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehy=Tgranking&ecrfcd9829irfcNumber&eestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiHandling of Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Number ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document defines the RTP payload format for the streaming of a video signal encoded as a sequence of JPEG 2000 codestreams.  The format allows sub-codestream latency, such that the first RTP packet for a given image can be emitted before the entire image is available to or encoded by the sender.fadNameoGorry Fairhurstdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSandflow Consulting LLCdnamemP.-A. Lemieux¢kaffiliationxUniversity of New South WalesdnamejD. Taubmanddatej◊1hfilenamegrfc9828eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-149391hkeywordsÜiJPEG 2000cJ2KeHTJ2Kklow latencyhscalableistreamingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh≤;Égranking&dcrfcd9828irfcNumber&destateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFRTP Payload Format for JPEG 2000 Streaming with Sub-Codestream LatencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document clarifies and extends the meaning of Transform Type 5 in Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2).  It updates RFC 7296 by renaming Transform Type 5 from "Extended Sequence Numbers (ESN)" to "Sequence Numbers (SN)".  It also renames two currently defined values for this Transform Type: value 0 from "No Extended Sequence Numbers" to "32-bit Sequential Numbers" and value 1 from "Extended Sequence Numbers" to "Partially Transmitted 64-bit Sequential Numbers".fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej◊"hfilenamegrfc9827eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-151782hkeywordsÖqreplay protectionkanti-replayeIPseccESPbAHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateií|granking&ccrfcd9827irfcNumber&cestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsRenaming the Extended Sequence Numbers (ESN) Transform Type in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis document defines a YANG data model for the management of the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for communications between a Path Computation Client (PCC) and a Path Computation Element (PCE), or between two PCEs.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Hardwick¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9826eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-149542hkeywordsÉhPCE YANGiPCEP YANGhPCC YANGkobsoletedByÄepageslopublicationDatehƒfΩgranking&bcrfcd9826irfcNumber&bestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPA YANG Data Model for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõIt is useful for routers in OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 routing domains to be able to associate tags with prefixes.  Previously, OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 were relegated to a single tag and only for Autonomous System (AS) External and Not-So-Stubby-Area (NSSA) prefixes.  With the flexible encodings provided by OSPFv2 Prefix/Link Attribute Advertisement and OSPFv3 Extended Link State Advertisements (LSAs), multiple administrative tags may be advertised for all types of prefixes.  These administrative tags can be used for many applications including route redistribution policy, selective prefix prioritization, selective IP Fast Reroute (IPFRR) prefix protection, and many others.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameeY. Quddatej—hfilenamegrfc9825eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-148894hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehã©Jgranking&acrfcd9825irfcNumber&aestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Extensions to OSPF for Advertising Prefix Administrative TagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document describes a technique to generate a signed DNS response on demand for a nonexistent name by claiming that the name exists but doesn't have any data for the queried record type. Such responses require only one minimally covering NSEC or NSEC3 record, allow online signing servers to minimize signing operations and response sizes, and prevent zone content disclosure.

This document updates RFCs 4034 and 4035.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamehS. Huque¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. Elmerot¢kaffiliation`dnamenO. Gudmundssonddatej◊&hfilenamegrfc9824eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-149629hkeywordsâcDNSfDNSSECsDenial of ExistencexCompact Denial of ExistenceoCompact AnswersjBlack LieshNXDOMAINfNODATArEmpty Non-TerminalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÃY©granking&`crfcd9824irfcNumber&`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Compact Denial of Existence in DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document specifies an authentication service that uses the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) as a transport method to carry the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP).  As such, it defines an EAP lower layer based on CoAP called "CoAP-EAP".  One of the main goals is to authenticate a CoAP-enabled Internet of Things (IoT) device (EAP peer) that intends to join a security domain managed by a Controller (EAP authenticator).  Secondly, it allows deriving key material to protect CoAP messages exchanged between them based on Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE), enabling the establishment of a security association between them.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnamenR. Marin-Lopez¢kaffiliationtUniversity of OviedodnamerD. Garcia-Carrilloddatej◊#hfilenamegrfc9820eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-149548hkeywordsádCoAPcEAPoEAP lower layerrInternet of ThingscIoTpConstrained NodelSmart ObjectkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDatehƒüÄgranking&\crfcd9820irfcNumber&\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÖAuthentication Service Based on the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) for Use with the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿RFC 9252 defines procedures and messages for BGP overlay services for Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6), including Layer 3 Virtual Private Network (L3VPN), Ethernet VPN (EVPN), and global Internet routing.  This document updates RFC 9252 and provides more detailed specifications for the signaling and processing of SRv6 Segment Identifier advertisements for BGP overlay service routes associated with SRv6 Endpoint Behaviors that support arguments.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefW. Linddatej◊,hfilenamegrfc9819eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-147437hkeywordsÇcBGPdSRv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh{“¶granking&[crfcd9819irfcNumber&[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGArgument Signaling for BGP Services in Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxËThis document defines requirements for IPv6 Customer Edge (CE) routers to support DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation for distributing available prefixes to LAN devices that were delegated to an IPv6 CE router.  This document updates RFC 7084.fadNameqMohamed Boucadairdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgQA CafednamejT. Wintersddatej◊-hfilenamegrfc9818eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-148852hkeywordsÉdIPv6xInternet Protocol Version 6fDHCPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateháÚÚgranking&Zcrfcd9818irfcNumber&ZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCDHCPv6 Prefix Delegation on IPv6 Customer Edge (CE) Routers in LANsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≥Computing in the Network (COIN) comes with the prospect of deploying processing functionality on networking devices such as switches and network interface cards. While such functionality can be beneficial, it has to be carefully placed into the context of the general Internet communication, and it needs to be clearly identified where and how those benefits apply.

This document presents some use cases to demonstrate how a number of salient COIN-related applications can benefit from COIN. Furthermore, to guide research on COIN, it identifies essential research questions and outlines desirable capabilities that COIN systems addressing these use cases may need to support. Finally, the document provides a preliminary categorization of the described research questions to source future work in this domain. This document is a product of the Computing in the Network Research Group (COINRG). It is not an IETF product and it is not a standard.gauthorsá¢kaffiliationkRWTH AachendnamehI. Kunze¢kaffiliationkRWTH AachendnameiK. Wehrle¢kaffiliationlDaPaDOT TechdnamejD. Trossen¢kaffiliationiSLICES-RIdnamenM-J. Montpetit¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnameiX. de Foy¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejD. Griffin¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamefM. Rioddatej◊$hfilenamegrfc9817eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcoinrgdfullx0coinrg - Computing in the Network Research Groupdnamex'Computing in the Network Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-149243hkeywordsâdCOINtin-network computingbVRbXRlIndustry 4.0oIndustrial IIoToPerforming ArtscCDNbP4kobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateh¶Mõgranking&Ycrfcd9817irfcNumber&YestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex"Use Cases for In-Network ComputingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document discusses the usage and applicability of BGP Link State (BGP-LS) Shortest Path First (SPF) extensions in data center networks utilizing Clos or Fat Tree topologies.  The document is intended to provide simplified guidance for the deployment of BGP-LS SPF extensions.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Zandi¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehG. Dawra¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dongddatej—hfilenamegrfc9816eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlsvrdfullx lsvr - Link State Vector RoutingdnamexLink State Vector Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-147694hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh}b!granking&Xcrfcd9816irfcNumber&XestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdUsage and Applicability of BGP Link State (BGP-LS) Shortest Path First (SPF) Routing in Data CentersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(Many Massively Scaled Data Centers (MSDCs) have converged on simplified Layer 3 (L3) routing.  Furthermore, requirements for operational simplicity have led many of these MSDCs to converge on BGP as their single routing protocol for both fabric routing and Data Center Interconnect (DCI) routing.  This document describes extensions to BGP for use with BGP Link State (BGP-LS) distribution and the Shortest Path First (SPF) algorithm.  In doing this, it allows BGP to be efficiently used as both the underlay protocol and the overlay protocol in MSDCs.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Zandi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickxddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9815eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlsvrdfullx lsvr - Link State Vector RoutingdnamexLink State Vector Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-147691hkeywordsÅcIDRkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateh}_Ègranking&Wcrfcd9815irfcNumber&WestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9BGP Link State (BGP-LS) Shortest Path First (SPF) RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySLH-DSA is a stateless hash-based signature algorithm.  This document specifies the conventions for using the SLH-DSA signature algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  In addition, the algorithm identifier and public key syntax are provided.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Fluhrer¢kaffiliationsAmazon Web ServicesdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamemB. Westerbaanddatej◊ hfilenamegrfc9814eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-147399hkeywordsÅx(stateless hash-based signature algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh{z"granking&Vcrfcd9814irfcNumber&VestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPUse of the SLH-DSA Signature Algorithm in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document provides implementation and operational considerations for using TLS Pre-Shared Keys (TLS-PSKs) with RADIUS/TLS (RFC 6614) and RADIUS/DTLS (RFC 7360).  The purpose of the document is to help smooth the operational transition from the use of RADIUS/UDP to RADIUS/TLS.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrInkBridge NetworksdnamehA. DeKokddatej–ıhfilenamegrfc9813eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-147075hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehqápgranking&Ucrfcd9813irfcNumber&UestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÛetotaletitlexOOperational Considerations for Using TLS Pre-Shared Keys (TLS-PSKs) with RADIUSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxyThis document specifies the approval process for changes to the "IPv6
Address Space" registry.  It also updates RFC 7249.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxThe University of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationwUniversity of MinnesotadnameiD. Farmerddatej—"hfilenamegrfc9812eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-150426hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh¯çgranking&Tcrfcd9812irfcNumber&TestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÚetotaletitlex/Clarification of IPv6 Address Allocation PolicydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document describes how to layer the Certificate Management Protocol (CMP) over HTTP.

It includes the updates to RFC 6712 specified in Section 3 of RFC 9480; these updates introduce CMP URIs using a well-known prefix. It obsoletes RFC 6712; and, together with RFC 9810, it also obsoletes RFC 9480.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamelH. Brockhaus¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemD. von Oheimb¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamelM. Ounsworth¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamegJ. Grayddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9811eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-148869hkeywordsÑcCMPdHTTPvCertificate managementcPKIkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatehâ5_granking&Scrfcd9811irfcNumber&SestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure -- HTTP Transfer for the Certificate Management Protocol (CMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document describes the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) Certificate Management Protocol (CMP). Protocol messages are defined for X.509v3 certificate creation and management. CMP provides interactions between client systems and PKI components such as a Registration Authority (RA) and a Certification Authority (CA).

This document adds support for management of certificates containing a Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) public key and uses EnvelopedData instead of EncryptedValue. This document also includes the updates specified in Section 2 and Appendix A.2 of RFC 9480.

This document obsoletes RFC 4210, and together with RFC 9811, it also obsoletes RFC 9480. Appendix F of this document updates Section 9 of RFC 5912.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamelH. Brockhaus¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemD. von Oheimb¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamelM. Ounsworth¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamegJ. Grayddatej◊ hfilenamegrfc9810eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-148868hkeywordsÑcCMPdHTTPvCertificate managementcPKIkobsoletedByÄepagestopublicationDatehâ5^granking&Rcrfcd9810irfcNumber&RestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure -- Certificate Management Protocol (CMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™RFC 5280 defines the Extended Key Usage (EKU) extension and specifies several extended key purpose identifiers (KeyPurposeIds) for use with that extension in X.509 certificates.  This document defines KeyPurposeIds for general-purpose and trust anchor configuration files, for software and firmware update packages, and for safety-critical communication to be included in the EKU extension of X.509 v3 public key certificates.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamelH. Brockhaus¢kaffiliationpSiemens MobilitydnamelD. Goltzscheddatej◊*hfilenamegrfc9809eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-147074hkeywordsÖuIndustrial AutomationdERJUrextended key usageiextensioncPKIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehqÅIgranking&Qcrfcd9809irfcNumber&QestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhX.509 Certificate Extended Key Usage (EKU) for Configuration, Updates, and Safety-Critical CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›This specification defines a set of additional Capability Objects that provide information about current downstream CDN (dCDN) utilization and specified usage limits to the delegating upstream CDN (uCDN) in order to inform traffic delegation decisions.

This document supplements the CDNI Capability Objects, defined in RFC 8008 as part of the Footprint & Capabilities Advertisement Interface (FCI), with two additional Capability Objects: FCI.CapacityLimits and FCI.Telemetry.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpDisney StreamingdnamegA. Ryan¢kaffiliationfVecimadnamelB. Rosenblum¢kaffiliationeQwiltdnameiN. Sopherddatej–˘hfilenamegrfc9808eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-148893hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehã§¶granking&Pcrfcd9808irfcNumber&PestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Capacity Capability Advertisement ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyUThis document describes the OPAQUE protocol, an Augmented (or Asymmetric) Password-Authenticated Key Exchange (aPAKE) protocol that supports mutual authentication in a client-server setting without reliance on PKI and with security against pre-computation attacks upon server compromise.  In addition, the protocol provides forward secrecy and the ability to hide the password from the server, even during password registration.  This document specifies the core OPAQUE protocol and one instantiation based on 3DH.  This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamekD. Bourdrez¢kaffiliationcAWSdnamekH. Krawczyk¢kaffiliationdMetadnamegK. Lewi¢kaffiliationpCloudflare, Inc.dnamejC. A. Woodddatej—hfilenamegrfc9807eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-148577hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDatehÇgranking&Ocrfcd9807irfcNumber&OestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexIThe OPAQUE Augmented Password-Authenticated Key Exchange (aPAKE) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»The SIP-based Media Recording (SIPREC) protocol is defined by both "Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Recording Metadata" (RFC 7865) and "Session Recording Protocol" (RFC 7866).  Unfortunately, both RFCs contradict each other regarding how recording metadata is to be labeled.  In addition, neither RFC registered the new media type.  This document updates RFC 7866 to align with RFC 7865 when labeling recording metadata and also registers the media type.fadNamekAndy Newtondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrMotorola SolutionsdnamekD. Mongrainddatej◊-hfilenamegrfc9806eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-146681hkeywordsÇfsiprecferratakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh_!ggranking&Ncrfcd9806irfcNumber&NestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLUpdates to SIP-Based Media Recording (SIPREC) to Correct Metadata Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document deprecates the IPv6 Router Alert option. Protocols that use the IPv6 Router Alert option may continue to do so, even in future versions. However, new protocols that are standardized in the future must not use the IPv6 Router Alert option.

This document updates RFC 2711.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonicaddatej◊,hfilenamegrfc9805eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-146648hkeywordsÅdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh]π˘granking&Mcrfcd9805irfcNumber&MestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Deprecation of the IPv6 Router Alert Option for New ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis memo specifies the data structure representation that was devised to support Simple Public Key Infrastructure (SPKI) certificates, as detailed in RFC 2692, with the intent that it be more widely applicable.  It has been and is being used elsewhere.  There are multiple implementations in a variety of programming languages.  Uses of this representation are referred to in this document as "S-expressions".  This memo makes precise the encodings of these SPKI S-expressions: It gives a "canonical form" for them, describes two "transport" representations, and also describes an "advanced" format for display to people.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMIT CSAILdnameiR. Rivest¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9804eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-146516hkeywordsÉdSexpkSexpressionlS-expressionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehQ’:granking&Lcrfcd9804irfcNumber&LestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Simple Public Key Infrastructure (SPKI) S-ExpressionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxµThis document describes an extension to the Extensible Provisioning
Protocol (EPP) that allows EPP clients to manage the Time-to-Live
(TTL) value for domain name delegation records.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamehG. Brownddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9803eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-146467hkeywordsÑcEPPcDNScTTLltime-to-livekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehL≠dgranking&Kcrfcd9803irfcNumber&KestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPExtensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Mapping for DNS Time-to-Live (TTL) ValuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This document specifies algorithm identifiers and ASN.1 encoding formats for the following stateful Hash-Based Signature (HBS) schemes: Hierarchical Signature System (HSS), eXtended Merkle Signature Scheme (XMSS), and XMSS^MT (a multi-tree variant of XMSS).  This specification applies to the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) when digital signatures are used to sign certificates and certificate revocation lists (CRLs).fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCryptoNext SecuritydnamelD. Van Geest¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamejK. Bashiri¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Fluhrer¢kaffiliationjgenua GmbHdnameiS. Gazdag¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamekS. Kousidisddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9802eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-146651hkeywordsàcHSScLMSdXMSSkcertificateeX.509dPKIXhstatefuljhash-basedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh]◊Sgranking&Jcrfcd9802irfcNumber&JestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcUse of the HSS and XMSS Hash-Based Signature Algorithms in Internet X.509 Public Key InfrastructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¸This document expands the applicability of Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS) bit-stream payloads beyond Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) signals and provides pseudowire transport with complete signal transparency over Packet Switched Networks (PSNs).fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamekS. Gringeri¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamelJ. Whittaker¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Schmutzer¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamehC. Brownddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9801eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-147058hkeywordsákpseudowiresqcircuit emulationxstructure-agnostic emulationrbit-stream payloadgSR-MPLSdSRv6cIWFkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatehpzÃgranking&Icrfcd9801irfcNumber&IestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Private Line Emulation over Packet Switched NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYSegment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) is the instantiation of Segment Routing (SR) on the IPv6 data plane. This document specifies new flavors for the SRv6 endpoint behaviors defined in RFC 8986, which enable the compression of an SRv6 segment list. Such compression significantly reduces the size of the SRv6 encapsulation needed to steer packets over long segment lists.

This document updates RFC 8754 by allowing a Segment List entry in the Segment Routing Header (SRH) to be either an IPv6 address, as specified in RFC 8754, or a REPLACE-CSID container in packed format, as specified in this document.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Cheng¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegF. Cladddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9800eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-146714hkeywordsáoSegment RoutingtIPv6 Segment RoutingnCompressed SIDdCSIDiNEXT-CSIDkREPLACE-SIDoSRH CompressionkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDatehbı≥granking&Hcrfcd9800irfcNumber&HestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Compressed SRv6 Segment List EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÀThis document defines extensions to the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) to allow for the automatic issuance of certificates to Tor Hidden Services (".onion" Special-Use Domain Names).fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsAS207960 CyfyngedigdnamehQ Misellddatej◊ hfilenamegrfc9799eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-146653hkeywordsÖctornhidden servicemonion serviceccaacdnskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh^2°granking&Gcrfcd9799irfcNumber&GestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdAutomated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) Extensions for ".onion" Special-Use Domain NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔThis document specifies an update to the Receiver RLOC (Routing Locator) field of the PIM Join/Prune attribute that supports the construction of multicast distribution trees where the source and receivers are located in different Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) sites and are connected using underlay IP multicast.  This attribute allows the receiver site to signal the underlay multicast group to the control plane of the root Ingress Tunnel Router (ITR).  This document updates RFC 8059.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekV. Govindan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Venaasddatej—hfilenamegrfc9798eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-146652hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh^2°granking&Fcrfcd9798irfcNumber&FestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiPIM Join/Prune Attributes for Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Environments Using Underlay MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTo limit the privacy issues created by the association between a device, its traffic, its location, and its user in IEEE 802 networks, client vendors and client OS vendors have started implementing Media Access Control (MAC) address randomization. This technology is particularly important in Wi-Fi networks (defined in IEEE 802.11) due to the over-the-air medium and device mobility. When such randomization happens, some in-network states may break, which may affect network connectivity and user experience. At the same time, devices may continue using other stable identifiers, defeating the purpose of MAC address randomization.

This document lists various network environments and a range of network services that may be affected by such randomization. This document then examines settings where the user experience may be affected by in-network state disruption. Last, this document examines some existing frameworks that maintain user privacy while preserving user quality of experience and network operation efficiency.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehJ. Henry¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Leeddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9797eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmadinasdfullxPmadinas - MAC Address Device Identification for Network and Application ServicesdnamexFMAC Address Device Identification for Network and Application Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-146671hkeywordsñcRCMuUniversal MAC addressqLocal MAC addressx Locally Administered MAC addressoPersonal DeviceuShared Service DevicexNetwork Functional EntitiesvHuman-Related EntitiesxOver-the-Air (OTA) observersx!Wireless access network operatorsxNetwork access providersx(Over-the-Wired internal (OTWi) observersx(Over-the-Wired external (OTWe) observersjfull trustoselective trustjzero trustgprivacytResidential settingsxManaged residential settingstpublic guest networkx-Enterprises with Bring-Your-Own-Device (BYOD)sManaged EnterpriseskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh^˘granking&Ecrfcd9797irfcNumber&EestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeRandomized and Changing Media Access Control (MAC) Addresses: Context, Network Impacts, and Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This document specifies a usage of the SIP Call-Info header field that incorporates Rich Call Data (RCD) associated with the identity of the originating party in order to provide to the terminating party a description of the caller (including details about the reason for the session). RCD includes information about the caller beyond the telephone number (such as a calling name, logo, photo, or jCard object representing the caller), which can help the called party decide how to handle the session request.

This document defines three new parameters 'call-reason', 'verified', and 'integrity' for the SIP Call-Info header field and also a new token ("jcard") for the 'purpose' parameter of the Call-Info header field. It also provides guidance on the use of the Call-Info 'purpose' parameter token, "icon".fadNamekAndy Newtondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeSomosdnamehC. Wendt¢kaffiliationjTransUniondnamekJ. Petersonddatej◊+hfilenamegrfc9796eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-147057hkeywordsÅhIdentitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehpzÀgranking&Dcrfcd9796irfcNumber&DestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+SIP Call-Info Parameters for Rich Call DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThis document extends Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT), a token for conveying cryptographically signed call information about personal communications, to include rich metadata about a call and caller that can be signed and integrity protected, transmitted, and subsequently rendered to the called party.  This framework is intended to include and extend caller- and call-specific information beyond human-readable display name, comparable to the "Caller ID" function common on the telephone network.  It is also enhanced with an integrity mechanism that is designed to protect the authoring and transport of this information for different authoritative use cases.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjSomos Inc.dnamehC. Wendt¢kaffiliationjTransUniondnamekJ. Petersonddatej÷Èhfilenamegrfc9795eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-147056hkeywordsÅhIdentitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehpzÀgranking&Ccrfcd9795irfcNumber&CestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT) Extension for Rich Call DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåOne aspect of the transition to post-quantum algorithms in cryptographic protocols is the development of hybrid schemes that incorporate both post-quantum and traditional asymmetric algorithms.  This document defines terminology for such schemes.  It is intended to be used as a reference and, hopefully, to ensure consistency and clarity across different protocols, standards, and organisations.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx!UK National Cyber Security CentrednamekF. Driscoll¢kaffiliationx!UK National Cyber Security CentrednamejM. Parsons¢kaffiliationxNaval Postgraduate SchooldnamegB. Haleddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9794eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepquipdfullx%pquip - Post-Quantum Use In ProtocolsdnamexPost-Quantum Use In Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-146469hkeywordsÑlcryptographylquantum-safecpqcicompositekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehL∫<granking&Bcrfcd9794irfcNumber&BestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Terminology for Post-Quantum Traditional Hybrid SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) is a multicast forwarding architecture that doesn't require an explicit tree-building protocol and doesn't require intermediate routers to maintain per-tree multicast states.  Some BIER-specific information and states, which are only in proportion to the number of BIER routers but not per-tree, do need to be advertised, calculated, and maintained.  This document describes BGP extensions for advertising the BIER information and methods for calculating BIER states based on the advertisements.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeX. Xu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehZ. Zhangddatej—hfilenamegrfc9793eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-146460hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehLg8granking&Acrfcd9793irfcNumber&AestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8BGP Extensions for Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§Each OSPF prefix can be advertised with an 8-bit field to indicate specific properties of that prefix. However, all the OSPFv3 Prefix Options bits have already been assigned, and only a few bits remain unassigned in the Flags field of the OSPFv2 Extended Prefix TLV.

This document solves this problem by defining a variable-length Prefix Extended Flags sub-TLV for OSPF. This sub-TLV is applicable to OSPFv2 and OSPFv3.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegR. Chen¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegD. Zhao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Gongddatej◊*hfilenamegrfc9792eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-146453hkeywordsÇqPrefix attributescIGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehK Egranking&@crfcd9792irfcNumber&@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Prefix Flag Extension for OSPFv2 and OSPFv3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document presents use cases that have a common feature that may be addressed by encoding network action indicators and associated ancillary data within MPLS packets. There is community interest in extending the MPLS data plane to carry such indicators and ancillary data to address these use cases.

The use cases described in this document are not an exhaustive set but rather the ones that have been actively discussed by members of the IETF MPLS, PALS, and DetNet Working Groups from the beginning of work on MPLS Network Action (MNA) until the publication of this document.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamelK. Makhijani¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamegH. Song¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirskyddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9791eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-147050hkeywordsÇuSpecial Purpose LabeloMPLS data planekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehp$granking&?crfcd9791irfcNumber&?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Use Cases for MPLS Network Action Indicators and Ancillary DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document creates a new IANA registry (called the "Post-Stack First Nibble" registry) for the first nibble (4-bit field) immediately following an MPLS label stack. Furthermore, this document presents some requirements for registering new values and making the processing of MPLS packets easier and more robust.

The relationship between the IANA "Post-Stack First Nibble" registry and the "IP Version Numbers" registry (RFC 2780) is described in this document.

This document updates RFC 4928 by deprecating the heuristic method for identifying the type of packet encapsulated in MPLS.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Surrey 5GICdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dongddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9790eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-147049hkeywordsÇqPost-stack headernLoad-balancingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehp$granking&>crfcd9790irfcNumber&>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYIANA Registry and Processing Recommendations for the First Nibble Following a Label StackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an architectural framework for MPLS Network Action (MNA) technologies. MNA technologies are used to indicate actions that impact the forwarding or other processing (such as monitoring) of the packet along the Label Switched Path (LSP) of the packet and to transfer any additional data needed for these actions.

This document provides the foundation for the development of a common set of network actions and information elements supporting additional operational models and capabilities of MPLS networks.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Surrey 5GICdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameeT. Liddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9789eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-147048hkeywordsÇxnetwork function virtualizationpservice chainingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehp$granking&=crfcd9789irfcNumber&=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%MPLS Network Actions (MNAs) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2S/MIME version 3.1 introduced a mechanism to provide end-to-end cryptographic protection of email message headers. However, few implementations generate messages using this mechanism, and several legacy implementations have revealed rendering or security issues when handling such a message.

This document updates the S/MIME specification (RFC 8551) to offer a different mechanism that provides the same cryptographic protections but with fewer downsides when handled by legacy clients. Furthermore, it offers more explicit usability, privacy, and security guidance for clients when generating or handling email messages with cryptographic protection of message headers.

The Header Protection scheme defined here is also applicable to messages with PGP/MIME (Pretty Good Privacy with MIME) cryptographic protections.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxAmerican Civil Liberties UniondnamemD. K. Gillmor¢kaffiliationkpEp ProjectdnamelB. Hoeneisen¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9788eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-149298hkeywordsíqHeader ProtectionxHeader Confidentiality PolicycHCPscryptographic emailpemail encryptionjencryptiongencryptisignaturedsignfS/MIMEhPGP/MIMEnLegacy DisplayvLegacy Display ElementdMIMEgPrivacyhHP-Outerqhp-legacy-displaybhpkobsoletedByÄepages⁄opublicationDateh©1≤granking&<crfcd9788irfcNumber&<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Header Protection for Cryptographically Protected EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHEnd-to-end cryptographic protections for email messages can provide useful security.  However, the standards for providing cryptographic protection are extremely flexible.  That flexibility can trap users and cause surprising failures.  This document offers guidance for Mail User Agent (MUA) implementers to help mitigate those risks and to make end-to-end email simple and secure for the end user.  It provides a useful set of vocabulary as well as recommendations to avoid common failures.  It also identifies a number of currently unsolved usability and interoperability problems.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdACLUdnamemD. K. Gillmor¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationkpEp ProjectdnamelB. Hoeneisenddatej÷˛hfilenamegrfc9787eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-149297hkeywordsâlcryptographyjencryptionisignaturegsigningiusabilitydMIMEoconfidentialityiintegritylauthenticitykobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateh©1±granking&;crfcd9787irfcNumber&;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Guidance on End-to-End Email SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0The Multi-Chassis Link Aggregation (MC-LAG) group technology enables establishing a logical link-aggregation connection with a redundant group of independent nodes.  The objective of MC-LAG is to enhance both network availability and bandwidth utilization through various modes of traffic load balancing.  RFC 7432 defines an EVPN-based MC-LAG with Single-Active and All-Active multihoming redundancy modes.  This document builds on the existing redundancy mechanisms supported by EVPN and introduces a new active/standby redundancy mode, called 'Port-Active'.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Brissette¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejLA. Burdet¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefB. Wen¢kaffiliationpVerizon WirelessdnameiE. Leyton¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9786eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-146680hkeywordsÉkPort-ActivedEVPNkMultihomingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh_!fgranking&:crfcd9786irfcNumber&:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex EVPN Port-Active Redundancy ModedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Designated Forwarder (DF) in Ethernet Virtual Private Networks (EVPNs) is defined as the Provider Edge (PE) router responsible for sending Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, and Multicast (BUM) traffic to a multihomed device/network in the case of an All-Active multihoming Ethernet Segment (ES) or BUM and unicast in the case of Single-Active multihoming. The Designated Forwarder is selected out of a candidate list of PEs that advertise the same Ethernet Segment Identifier (ESI) to the EVPN network, according to the default DF election algorithm. While the default algorithm provides an efficient and automated way of selecting the Designated Forwarder across different Ethernet Tags in the Ethernet Segment, there are some use cases where a more "deterministic" and user-controlled method is required. At the same time, Network Operators require an easy way to force an on-demand Designated Forwarder switchover in order to carry out some maintenance tasks on the existing Designated Forwarder or control whether a new active PE can preempt the existing Designated Forwarder PE.

This document proposes use of a DF election algorithm that meets the requirements of determinism and operation control. This document updates RFC 8584 by modifying the definition of the DF Election Extended Community.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. Sathappan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefW. Lin¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassiddatej÷Úhfilenamegrfc9785eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-146679hkeywordsÖrHighest-PreferenceqLowest-PreferencemNon-RevertiveoDon‚Äôt PreemptjPreemptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh_!fgranking&9crfcd9785irfcNumber&9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Preference-Based EVPN Designated Forwarder (DF) ElectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqEthernet VPN (EVPN) and Provider Backbone Bridge EVPN (PBB-EVPN) introduce a comprehensive suite of solutions for delivering Ethernet services over MPLS/IP networks.  These solutions offer advanced multihoming capabilities.  Specifically, they support Single-Active and All-Active redundancy modes for an Ethernet Segment (ES), which is defined as a collection of physical links connecting a multihomed device or network to a set of Provider Edge (PE) devices.  This document extends the concept of an ES by allowing an ES to be associated with a set of Ethernet Virtual Circuits (EVCs), such as VLANs, or other entities, including MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs) or pseudowires (PWs).  This extended concept is referred to as virtual Ethernet Segments (vESes).  This document lists the requirements and specifies the necessary extensions to support vES in both EVPN and PBB-EVPN.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Brissette¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiR. Schell¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9784eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-146678hkeywordsÇcvESjVirtual ESkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh_!egranking&8crfcd9784irfcNumber&8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDVirtual Ethernet Segments for EVPN and Provider Backbone Bridge EVPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÁArm's Platform Security Architecture (PSA) is a family of hardware and firmware security specifications, along with open-source reference implementations, aimed at helping device makers and chip manufacturers integrate best-practice security into their products. Devices that comply with PSA can generate attestation tokens as described in this document, which serve as the foundation for various protocols, including secure provisioning and network access control. This document specifies the structure and semantics of the PSA attestation token.

The PSA attestation token is a profile of the Entity Attestation Token (EAT). This specification describes the claims used in an attestation token generated by PSA-compliant systems, how these claims are serialized for transmission, and how they are cryptographically protected.

This Informational document is published as an Independent Submission to improve interoperability with Arm's architecture. It is not a standard nor a product of the IETF.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeH-BRSdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamehS. Frost¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamekM. Brossard¢kaffiliationgHP LabsdnamegA. Shaw¢kaffiliationfLinarodnamejT. Fossatiddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9783eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-146459hkeywordsÖrRemote AttestationkEAT profilehEvidencedRATScIoTkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDatehLg7granking&7crfcd9783irfcNumber&7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex<Arm's Platform Security Architecture (PSA) Attestation TokendtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˝The payloads used in Remote ATtestation procedureS (RATS) may require an associated media type for their conveyance, for example, when the payloads are used in RESTful APIs.

This memo defines media types to be used for Entity Attestation Tokens (EATs).fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsSecurity Theory LLCdnamelL. Lundblade¢kaffiliationx6Fraunhofer Institute for Secure Information TechnologydnamekH. Birkholz¢kaffiliationfLinarodnamejT. Fossatiddatej◊	hfilenamegrfc9782eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdratsdfullx$rats - Remote ATtestation ProcedureSdnamexRemote ATtestation ProcedureSdtypebwgbidjdoc-146263hkeywordsÇcEATjmedia typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh6òhgranking&6crfcd9782irfcNumber&6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Entity Attestation Token (EAT) Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThis document defines the Unprotected CWT Claims Set (UCCS), a data format for representing a CBOR Web Token (CWT) Claims Set without protecting it by a signature, Message Authentication Code (MAC), or encryption.  UCCS enables the use of CWT claims in environments where protection is provided by other means, such as secure communication channels or trusted execution environments.  This specification defines a CBOR tag for UCCS and describes the UCCS format, its encoding, and its processing considerations.  It also discusses security implications of using unprotected claims sets.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer SITdnamekH. Birkholz¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies Inc.dnamemJ. O'Donoghue¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej◊	hfilenamegrfc9781eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdratsdfullx$rats - Remote ATtestation ProcedureSdnamexRemote ATtestation ProcedureSdtypebwgbidjdoc-146262hkeywordsànSecure ChanneldRATSxConceptual Message ConveyancegPrivacyoConfidentialitydCDDLcEATdUJCSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh6òggranking&5crfcd9781irfcNumber&5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcA Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) Tag for Unprotected CBOR Web Token Claims Sets (UCCS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯This document describes procedures for using Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for multipoint networks to detect data plane failures in point-to-multipoint MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs) and Segment Routing (SR) point-to-multipoint policies with an SR over MPLS (SR-MPLS) data plane.

Furthermore, this document updates RFC 8562 by recommending the use of an IPv6 address from the Dummy IPv6 Prefix address block 100:0:0:1::/64 and discouraging the use of an IPv4 loopback address mapped to IPv6.

In addition, this document describes the applicability of LSP Ping (as an in-band solution) and the control plane (as an out-of-band solution) to bootstrap a BFD session. The document also describes the behavior of the active tail for head notification.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationlVerizon Inc.dnameiG. Mishra¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej◊$hfilenamegrfc9780eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-146302hkeywordsÇcBFDnMultipoint LSPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh;¶◊granking&4crfcd9780irfcNumber&4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for Multipoint Networks over Point-to-Multipoint MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝This document specifies the application of the MPLS loss and delay measurement techniques (originally defined in RFCs 6374, 7876, and 9341) within Segment Routing (SR) networks that utilize the MPLS data plane, also referred to as Segment Routing over MPLS (SR-MPLS).  SR enables the forwarding of packets through an ordered list of instructions, known as segments, which are imposed at the ingress node.  This document defines the procedures and extensions necessary to perform accurate measurement of packet loss and delay in SR-MPLS environments, ensuring that network operators can effectively measure and maintain the quality of service across their SR-based MPLS networks.  This includes coverage of links and end-to-end SR-MPLS paths, as well as SR Policies.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationx Universita di Roma "Tor Vergata"dnamejS. Salsano¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9779eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-145992hkeywordsÑqDelay MeasurementpLoss MeasurementpLink MeasurementxSR-MPLS Policy MeasurementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehˆœgranking&3crfcd9779irfcNumber&3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMPerformance Measurement for Segment Routing Networks with the MPLS Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¶This document specifies revised IANA considerations for the Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and the Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) protocol. This document specifies the guidance provided to IANA to manage values associated with various fields within the protocol headers of the group management protocols.

This document obsoletes RFC 3228 and unifies guidelines for IPv4 and IPv6 group management protocols.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx,Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics LabdnamekB. Habermanddatej–˙hfilenamegrfc9778eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-145259hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÊ¸Sgranking&2crfcd9778irfcNumber&2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber9etotaletitlex;IANA Considerations for Internet Group Management ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyLThis document specifies the Multicast Listener Discovery version 2 (MLDv2) protocol. MLD is used by an IPv6 router to discover the presence of multicast listeners on directly attached links and to discover which multicast addresses are of interest to those neighboring nodes. MLDv2 is designed to be interoperable with MLDv1. MLDv2 adds the ability for a node to report interest in listening to packets with a particular multicast address only from specific source addresses or from all sources except for specific source addresses.

This document updates RFC 2710 and obsoletes RFC 3810.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx,Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics LabdnamekB. Habermanddatej–˙hfilenamegrfc9777eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-145257hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDategÊ¸Rgranking&1crfcd9777irfcNumber&1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumbereetotaletitlex7Multicast Listener Discovery Version 2 (MLDv2) for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty-The Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) is the protocol used by IPv4 systems to report their IP multicast group memberships to neighboring multicast routers. Version 3 of IGMP (IGMPv3) adds support for source filtering, that is, the ability for a system to report interest in receiving packets only from specific source addresses, or from all but specific source addresses, sent to a particular multicast address. That information may be used by multicast routing protocols to avoid delivering multicast packets from specific sources to networks where there are no interested receivers.

This document specifies IGMPv3. It is a revised version of RFC 3376 that includes clarifications and fixes for errata, and it is backward compatible with RFC 3376.

This document updates RFC 2236 and obsoletes RFC 3376.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx,Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics LabdnamekB. Habermanddatej–˚hfilenamegrfc9776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-145255hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDategÊ¸Rgranking&0crfcd9776irfcNumber&0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberdetotaletitlex-Internet Group Management Protocol, Version 3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes the code of conduct for participants in the
Internet Research Task Force (IRTF).

The IRTF believes that research is most effective when done in an
open and inclusive forum that encourages diversity of ideas and
participation. Through this code of conduct, the IRTF continues to
strive to create and maintain an environment that encourages broad
participation, and one in which people are treated with dignity,
decency, and respect.

This document is a product of the Internet Research Steering Group
(IRSG).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamemC. S. Perkinsddatej◊%hfilenamegrfc9775eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhirtfopendfullxirtfopen - IRTF Open MeetingdnameqIRTF Open Meetingdtypecragbidjdoc-144762hkeywordsÇdIRTFoCode of ConductkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg⁄Agranking&/crfcd9775irfcNumber&/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitletIRTF Code of ConductdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBCP 172 (i.e., RFC 6472) recommends not using AS_SET and AS_CONFED_SET AS_PATH segment types in the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP).  This document advances that recommendation to a standards requirement in BGP; it prohibits the use of the AS_SET and AS_CONFED_SET path segment types in the AS_PATH.  This is done to simplify the design and implementation of BGP and to make the semantics of the originator of a BGP route clearer.  This will also simplify the design, implementation, and deployment of various BGP security mechanisms.  This document updates RFC 4271 by deprecating the origination of BGP routes with AS_SET (Type 1 AS_PATH segment) and updates RFC 5065 by deprecating the origination of BGP routes with AS_CONFED_SET (Type 4 AS_PATH segment).  Finally, it obsoletes RFC 6472.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationhUSA NISTdnameiK. Sriram¢kaffiliationhUSA NISTdnamekL. Hannachi¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamegJ. Haasddatej—hfilenamegrfc9774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-146186hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh.Ìgranking&.crfcd9774irfcNumber&.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Deprecation of AS_SET and AS_CONFED_SET in BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis document specifies how an Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) server may provide suggestions to ACME clients as to when they should attempt to renew their certificates.  This allows servers to mitigate load spikes and ensures that clients do not make false assumptions about appropriate certificate renewal periods.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx Internet Security Research GroupdnamehA. Gableddatej”Âhfilenamegrfc9773eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-146506hkeywordsákcertificatebCAdx509cpkifwebpkierenewgreplacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehQògranking&-crfcd9773irfcNumber&-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(ACME Renewal Information (ARI) ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑Geneve (Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation) is a flexible and extensible network virtualization overlay protocol designed to encapsulate network packets for transport across underlying physical networks.  This document specifies the requirements and provides a framework for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) in Geneve networks.  It outlines the OAM functions necessary to monitor, diagnose, and troubleshoot Geneve overlay networks to ensure proper operation and performance.  The document aims to guide the implementation of OAM mechanisms within the Geneve protocol to support network operators in maintaining reliable and efficient virtualized network environments.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationeCienadnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationdDelldnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamemS. Pallagattiddatej◊ hfilenamegrfc9772eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-146301hkeywordsÅcOAMkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateh;¶%granking&,crfcd9772irfcNumber&,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyActive Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) for Use in Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation (Geneve)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyˆAuthenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) algorithms provide both confidentiality and integrity of data.  The widespread use of AEAD algorithms in various applications has led to an increased demand for AEAD algorithms with additional properties, driving research in the field.  This document provides definitions for the most common of those properties and aims to improve consistency in the terminology used in documentation.  This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnameiA. Bozhkoddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9771eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-145975hkeywordsÑxauthenticated encryptionqmode of operationdAEADjpropertieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehÎigranking&+crfcd9771irfcNumber&+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexMProperties of Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document specifies a method of the Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) framework, which allows an authorization server to notify clients and resource servers (i.e., registered devices) about revoked access tokens.  As specified in this document, the method allows clients and resource servers (RSs) to access a Token Revocation List (TRL) on the authorization server by using the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP), with the possible additional use of resource observation.  Resulting (unsolicited) notifications of revoked access tokens complement alternative approaches such as token introspection, while not requiring additional endpoints on clients and RSs.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgRISE ABdnameiM. Tiloca¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelF. Palombini¢kaffiliationgCMU SEIdnamemS. Echeverria¢kaffiliationgCMU SEIdnamehG. Lewisddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9770eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-146458hkeywordsáhSecuritynAccess controlmAccess rightsjRevocationdCoAPcIoTxConstrained environmentskobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDatehLg6granking&*crfcd9770irfcNumber&*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzNotification of Revoked Access Tokens in the Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑This document specifies interleaved modes for the Network Time Protocol (NTP).  These new modes improve the accuracy of time synchronization by enabling the use of more accurate transmit timestamps that are available only after the transmission of NTP messages.  These enhancements are intended to improve timekeeping in environments where high accuracy is critical.  This document updates RFC 5905 by defining these new operational modes.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejM. Lichvar¢kaffiliationqBoston UniversitydnamekA. Malhotraddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9769eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-146229hkeywordsÇcNTPpinterleaved modekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh0ı—granking&)crfcd9769irfcNumber&)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuNTP Interleaved ModesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybExplicit Congestion Notification (ECN) is a mechanism by which network nodes can mark IP packets instead of dropping them to indicate incipient congestion to the endpoints.  Receivers with an ECN-capable transport protocol feed back this information to the sender.  ECN was originally specified for TCP in such a way that only one feedback signal can be transmitted per Round-Trip Time (RTT).  More recently defined mechanisms like Congestion Exposure (ConEx), Data Center TCP (DCTCP), or Low Latency, Low Loss, and Scalable Throughput (L4S) need more accurate ECN feedback information whenever more than one marking is received in one RTT.  This document updates the original ECN specification defined in RFC 3168 by specifying a scheme that provides more than one feedback signal per RTT in the TCP header, called More Accurate ECN (AccECN).  Given TCP header space is scarce, it allocates a reserved header bit previously assigned to the ECN-nonce.  It also overloads the two existing ECN flags in the TCP header.  The resulting extra space is additionally exploited to feed back the IP-ECN field received during the TCP connection establishment.  Supplementary feedback information can optionally be provided in two new TCP Option alternatives, which are never used on the TCP SYN.  The document also specifies the treatment of this updated TCP wire protocol by middleboxes.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamepR. Scheffeneggerddatej◊)hfilenamegrfc9768eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-156365hkeywordsáx!Congestion Control and ManagementwCongestion NotificationhFeedbackhReliablegOrderedhProtocolcECNkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateiÎœPgranking&(crfcd9768irfcNumber&(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGMore Accurate Explicit Congestion Notification (AccECN) Feedback in TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\The Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocol (GNAP) defines a mechanism for delegating authorization to a piece of software (the client) and conveying the results and artifacts of that delegation to the software.  This extension defines methods for resource servers (RSs) to connect with authorization servers (ASs) in an interoperable fashion.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsBespoke EngineeringdnameiJ. Richer¢kaffiliationhacert.iodnamejF. Imbaultddatej—hfilenamegrfc9767eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgnapdfullx3gnap - Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocoldnamex,Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-145795hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateh8"granking&'crfcd9767irfcNumber&'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHGrant Negotiation and Authorization Protocol Resource Server ConnectionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThis document specifies extensions to NFSv4.2 for improving Weak Cache Consistency (WCC).  These extensions introduce mechanisms that ensure partial writes performed under a Parallel NFS (pNFS) layout remain coherent and correctly tracked.  The solution addresses concurrency and data integrity concerns that may arise when multiple clients write to the same file through separate data servers.  By defining additional interactions among clients, metadata servers, and data servers, this specification enhances the reliability of NFSv4 in parallel-access environments and ensures consistency across diverse deployment scenarios.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednameiT. Haynes¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednamelT. Myklebustddatej—hfilenamegrfc9766eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-145810hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh	TÈgranking&&crfcd9766irfcNumber&&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGExtensions for Weak Cache Consistency in NFSv4.2's Flexible File LayoutdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) extensions for use with RADIUS/TLS and RADIUS/DTLS. These extensions permit the negotiation of an application protocol variant of RADIUS called "RADIUS/1.1". No changes are made to RADIUS/UDP or RADIUS/TCP. The extensions allow the negotiation of a transport profile where the RADIUS shared secret is no longer used, and all MD5-based packet authentication and attribute obfuscation methods are removed.

This document updates RFCs 2865, 2866, 5176, 6613, 6614, and 7360.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9765eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-145794hkeywordsÖfRADIUSdALPNcMD5hFIPS 140cTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh#granking&%crfcd9765irfcNumber&%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRRADIUS/1.1: Leveraging Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) to Remove MD5dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™The Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol is commonly
used to verify connectivity between two systems. BFD packets are
typically very small. It is desirable in some circumstances to know
not only that the path between two systems is reachable, but also
that it is capable of carrying a payload of a particular size. This
document specifies how to implement such a mechanism using BFD in
Asynchronous mode.

YANG modules for managing this mechanism are also defined in this
document. These YANG modules augment the existing BFD YANG modules
defined in RFC 9314. The YANG modules in this document conform to the
Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) (RFC 8342).fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamegJ. Haas¢kaffiliationnBloomberg L.P.dnameeA. Fuddatej—hfilenamegrfc9764eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-145401hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategqgranking&$crfcd9764irfcNumber&$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) Encapsulated in Large PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document defines a new Certificate Signing Request (CSR) attribute, relatedCertRequest, and a new X.509 certificate extension, RelatedCertificate.  The use of the relatedCertRequest attribute in a CSR and the inclusion of the RelatedCertificate extension in the resulting certificate together provide additional assurance that two certificates each belong to the same end entity.  This mechanism is particularly useful in the context of non-composite hybrid authentication, which enables users to employ the same certificates in hybrid authentication as in authentication done with only traditional or post-quantum algorithms.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnameiA. Becker¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejR. Guthrie¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejM. Jenkinsddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9763eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-146468hkeywordsàkcertificatescertificate requestfhybridnauthenticationlpost-quantumeX.509qdigital signatureqquantum resistantkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehLµ#granking&#crfcd9763irfcNumber&#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJRelated Certificates for Use in Multiple Authentications within a ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document defines the P flag in the Prefix Information Option (PIO) of IPv6 Router Advertisements (RAs). The flag is used to indicate that the network prefers that clients use the deployment model in RFC 9663 instead of using individual addresses in the on-link prefix assigned using Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC) or DHCPv6 address assignment.

This document updates RFC 4862 to indicate that the Autonomous flag in a PIO needs to be ignored if the PIO has the P flag set. It also updates RFC 4861 to specify that the P flag indicates DHCPv6 prefix delegation support for clients.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Colitti¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkova¢kaffiliationfGooglednameeX. Ma¢kaffiliationlNetDEF, Inc.dnamelD. Lamparterddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9762eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-146497hkeywordsÑdipv6eslaaccpioidhcpv6-pdkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehPÈLgranking&"crfcd9762irfcNumber&"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Using Router Advertisements to Signal the Availability of DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation to ClientsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOThis memo extends the Manufacturer Usage Description (MUD) specification to allow manufacturers to define TLS and DTLS profile parameters.  This allows a network security service to identify unexpected (D)TLS usage, which can indicate the presence of unauthorized software, malware, or security policy-violating traffic on an endpoint.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationfCitrixdnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekB. Andersonddatej–˚hfilenamegrfc9761eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-145776hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatehΩSgranking&!crfcd9761irfcNumber&!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcManufacturer Usage Description (MUD) for TLS and DTLS Profiles for Internet of Things (IoT) DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document describes a Precision Time Protocol (PTP) Profile (IEEE Standard 1588-2019) for use in an IPv4 or IPv6 enterprise information system environment.  The PTP Profile uses the End-to-End delay measurement mechanism, allowing both multicast and unicast Delay Request and Delay Response messages.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlMeinberg-USAdnameiD. Arnold¢kaffiliationhMeinbergdnamekH. Gerstungddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9760eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftictocdfullx8tictoc - Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdnamex/Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdtypebwgbidjdoc-146228hkeywordsÇcPTPrEnterprise ProfilekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh0Ú6granking& crfcd9760irfcNumber& estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Enterprise Profile for the Precision Time Protocol with Mixed Multicast and Unicast MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyEstimating time requirements for tasks, both critical and mundane, remains a challenge in engineering, business, and everyday communication.  Existing models fail due to inherent unpredictability and inconsistencies in human estimation.  This document introduces the Two-Week Principle (TWP), a novel, universally adaptable time scale that seeks to standardize all temporal references to a singular, uniform duration.  TWP ensures clarity, predictability, and synchronization across all sectors that rely on time-based scheduling.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjYahoo Inc.dnamehK. Kuhnsddatej◊(hfilenamegrfc9759eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-145332hkeywordsÇrTwo-Week PrinciplecTWPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÏ$granking&crfcd9759irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9Unified Time Scaling for Temporal Coordination FrameworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document updates the specification of the 'ipn' URI scheme previously defined in RFC 6260 and the IANA registries established in RFC 7116.  It also updates the rules for the encoding and decoding of these URIs when used as an Endpoint Identifier (EID) in the Bundle Protocol version 7 (BPv7) as defined in RFC 9171.  These updates clarify the structure and behavior of the 'ipn' URI scheme, define new encodings of 'ipn' scheme URIs, and establish the registries necessary to manage this scheme.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtAalyria TechnologiesdnameiR. Taylor¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamenE. Birrane IIIddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9758eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdtndfullx*dtn - Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdnamex$Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-146227hkeywordsÖcDTNcipndBPv7dCBHEoBundle ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh0Óﬂgranking&crfcd9758irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUpdates to the 'ipn' URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document introduces extensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) to support path computation in Native IP networks through a PCE-based central control mechanism known as Centralized Control Dynamic Routing (CCDR).  These extensions empower a PCE to calculate and manage paths specifically for Native IP networks, thereby expanding PCEP's capabilities beyond its past use in MPLS and GMPLS networks.  By implementing these extensions, IP network resources can be utilized more efficiently, facilitating the deployment of traffic engineering in Native IP environments.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegA. Wang¢kaffiliationvMTS Web Services (MWS)dnamekB. Khasanov¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegS. Fang¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamefC. Zhuddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9757eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-143872hkeywordsÇdCCDRePCECCkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateg‘∫ﬁgranking&crfcd9757irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Native IP NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document updates the registration procedure within the IANA "Path Computation Element Protocol (PCEP) Numbers" registry group. This specification changes some of the registries with Standards Action to IETF Review as defined in RFC 8126 and thus updates RFCs 8231, 8233, 8281, 8623, 8664, 8685, 8697, 8733, 8745, 8779, 8780, 8800, 8934, 9050, 9059, 9168, 9357, 9504, 9603, and 9604.

Designating "experimental use" sub-ranges within codepoint registries is often beneficial for protocol experimentation in controlled environments. Although the registries for PCEP messages, objects, and TLV types have sub-ranges assigned for Experimental Use, the registry for PCEP Error-Types and Error-values currently does not. This document updates RFC 5440 by designating a specific range of PCEP Error-Types for Experimental Use.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej—hfilenamegrfc9756eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-143748hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg“ägranking&crfcd9756irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexãUpdate to the IANA Path Communication Element Protocol (PCEP) Numbers Registration Procedures and the Allowance of Experimental Error CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis specification extends the Internet Message Access Protocol, specifically IMAP4rev1 (RFC 3501), to support UTF-8 encoded international characters in user names, mail addresses, and message headers.  This specification replaces RFC 6855.  This specification does not extend IMAP4rev2 (RFC 9051), since that protocol includes everything in this extension.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEpistemednamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Yao¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamenA. Gulbrandsenddatej◊	hfilenamegrfc9755eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-144312hkeywordsÅdIMAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg◊Àgranking&crfcd9755irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIMAP Support for UTF-8dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThe Network File System v4 (NFSv4) allows a client to both open a file and be granted a delegation of that file.  This delegation provides the client the right to authoritatively cache metadata on the file locally.  This document presents several extensions for both opening the file and delegating it to the client.  This document extends NFSv4.2 (see RFC 7863).fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednameiT. Haynes¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednamelT. Myklebustddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9754eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-145219hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg‰êúgranking&crfcd9754irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Extensions for Opening and Delegating Files in NFSv4.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis document introduces a mechanism to mark some of the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) objects as optional during PCEP message exchange, so the stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) model can relax some constraints during path computation and setup.  This document introduces this relaxation to stateful PCE, and it updates RFC 8231.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeC. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehH. Zheng¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelS. Litkowskiddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9753eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-145785hkeywordsÉdPCEPhStatefulsoptional processingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatehùIgranking&crfcd9753irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyExtension for Stateful PCE to Allow Optional Processing of Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) ObjectsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document specifies extensions to the Path Computation Element
Communication Protocol (PCEP) that enable the inclusion of
vendor-specific information in stateful Path Computation Element
(PCE) operations. These extensions allow vendors to incorporate
proprietary data within PCEP messages, facilitating enhanced network
optimization and functionality in environments requiring
vendor-specific features. The extensions maintain compatibility with
existing PCEP implementations and promote interoperability across
diverse network deployments. RFC 7470 defines a facility to carry
vendor-specific information in stateless PCEP messages. This document
extends this capability for the stateful PCEP messages.

This document updates RFC 7470 to specify that Enterprise Numbers are
managed through the "Private Enterprise Numbers (PENs)" registry.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeC. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehH. Zheng¢kaffiliationeCienadnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehS. Sidor¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Aliddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9752eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-145429hkeywordsÉcPCEovendor specificovendor-specifickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÙ	–granking&crfcd9752irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexConveying Vendor-Specific Information in the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Stateful PCEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢The working group process and the authors of RTP payload formats have sometimes failed to ensure that the media types are registered in the IANA "RTP Payload Format Media Types" registry as recommended by RFC 8088.  To simplify the process and rely only on the "Media Types" registry, this document closes the RTP payload- specific registry.  In addition, it updates the instruction in RFC 8088 to reflect this change.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej—hfilenamegrfc9751eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-144784hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg⁄i≤granking&crfcd9751irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Closing the RTP Payload Format Media Types RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThe Messaging Layer Security (MLS) protocol (RFC 9420) provides a group key agreement protocol for messaging applications. MLS is designed to protect against eavesdropping, tampering, and message forgery, and to provide forward secrecy (FS) and post-compromise security (PCS).

This document describes the architecture for using MLS in a general secure group messaging infrastructure and defines the security goals for MLS. It provides guidance on building a group messaging system and discusses security and privacy trade-offs offered by multiple security mechanisms that are part of the MLS protocol (e.g., frequency of public encryption key rotation). The document also provides guidance for parts of the infrastructure that are not standardized by MLS and are instead left to the application.

While the recommendations of this document are not mandatory to follow in order to interoperate at the protocol level, they affect the overall security guarantees that are achieved by a messaging application. This is especially true in the case of active adversaries that are able to compromise clients, the Delivery Service (DS), or the Authentication Service (AS).fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoInria & MozilladnamemB. Beurdouche¢kaffiliation`dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. Omara¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Inguva¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Duricddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9750eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcmlsdfullxmls - Messaging Layer SecuritydnamexMessaging Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-145791hkeywordsÉhsecurityxauthenticated key exchangeuend-to-end encryptionkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDatehIgranking&crfcd9750irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/The Messaging Layer Security (MLS) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document defines a method for JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) servers to advertise their capability to authenticate Web Push notifications using the Voluntary Application Server Identification (VAPID) protocol.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Gultschddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9749eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-144133hkeywordsÅgWebPushkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg÷ûgranking&crfcd9749irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlUse of Voluntary Application Server Identification (VAPID) in JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) Web PushdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑The Network Time Protocol (NTP) and Network Time Security (NTS) documents define a number of registries, collectively called the NTP registries.

Some registries are correct, but some include incorrect assignments and some don't follow common practice. For the sake of completeness, this document reviews all NTP and NTS registries, and corrects the registries where necessary.

This document updates RFCs 5905, 5906, 7821, 7822, and 8573.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamegR. Salzddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9748eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-143348hkeywordsÑcNTPjextensionsjregistriesdIANAkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateg¬\)granking&crfcd9748irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUpdating the NTP RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThis document specifies an extension to the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol that enables the use of the BFD Echo function without the need for an associated BFD control session. This "Unaffiliated BFD Echo" mechanism allows rapid detection of forwarding path failures in networks where establishing BFD control sessions is impractical or undesirable. By decoupling the Echo function from the control plane, network devices can utilize BFD's fast failure detection capabilities in a simplified manner, enhancing network resiliency and operational efficiency.

This document updates RFC 5880 by defining a new Unaffiliated BFD Echo mechanism.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Cheng¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegR. Wang¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefX. Min¢kaffiliationgEquinixdnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemR. Boddireddyddatej—hfilenamegrfc9747eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-145220hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg‰ïgranking&crfcd9747irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Unaffiliated Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) EchodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚAn Ethernet Virtual Private Network (EVPN) is commonly used with Network Virtualization Overlay (NVO) tunnels as well as with MPLS and Segment Routing (SR) tunnels. The multihoming procedures in EVPN may vary based on the type of tunnel used within the EVPN Broadcast Domain. Specifically, there are two multihoming split-horizon procedures designed to prevent looped frames on multihomed Customer Edge (CE) devices: the Ethernet Segment Identifier (ESI) Label-based procedure and the local-bias procedure. The ESI Label-based split-horizon procedure is applied to MPLS-based tunnels such as MPLS over UDP (MPLSoUDP), while the local-bias procedure is used for other tunnels such as Virtual eXtensible Local Area Network (VXLAN) tunnels.

Current specifications do not allow operators to choose which split-horizon procedure to use for tunnel encapsulations that support both methods. Examples of tunnels that may support both procedures include MPLSoUDP, MPLS over GRE (MPLSoGRE), Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation (Geneve), and Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) tunnels. This document updates the EVPN multihoming procedures described in RFCs 7432 and 8365, enabling operators to select the split-horizon procedure that meets their specific requirements.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejK. Nagaraj¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefW. Lin¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Sajassiddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9746eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-143633hkeywordsÜpEVPN MultihomingwSplit Horizon FilteringjLocal BiascESInencapsulationscSHTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg Îgranking&crfcd9746irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;BGP EVPN Multihoming Extensions for Split-Horizon FilteringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThe Deprecation HTTP response header field is used to signal to consumers of a resource (identified by a URI) that the resource will be or has been deprecated.  Additionally, the deprecation link relation can be used to link to a resource that provides further information about planned or existing deprecation.  It may also provide ways in which client application developers can best manage deprecation.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Dalal¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. Wildeddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9745eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpapidfullx'httpapi - Building Blocks for HTTP APIsdnamexBuilding Blocks for HTTP APIsdtypebwgbidjdoc-144361hkeywordsÇdHTTPkdeprecationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÿ˘granking&crfcd9745irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*The Deprecation HTTP Response Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document describes a new EVPN Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS) service type specifically for multiplexing multiple attachment circuits across different Ethernet Segments (ESs) and physical interfaces into a single EVPN-VPWS service tunnel and still providing Single-Active and All-Active multi-homing.  This new service is referred to as the EVPN-VPWS Flexible Cross-Connect (FXC) service.  This document specifies a solution based on extensions to EVPN-VPWS (RFC 8214), which in turn is based on extensions to EVPN (RFC 7432).  Therefore, a thorough understanding of RFCs 7432 and 8214 are prerequisites for this document.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Brissette¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeCienadnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9744eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-144048hkeywordsÑdEVPNdVPWSvFlexible Cross ConnectcFXCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg÷lxgranking&crfcd9744irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMEVPN Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS) Flexible Cross-Connect (FXC) ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyVRFC 5033 discusses the principles and guidelines for standardizing new congestion control algorithms.  This document obsoletes RFC 5033 to reflect changes in the congestion control landscape by providing a framework for the development and assessment of congestion control mechanisms, promoting stability across diverse network paths.  This document seeks to ensure that proposed congestion control algorithms operate efficiently and without harm when used in the global Internet.  It emphasizes the need for comprehensive testing and validation to prevent adverse interactions with existing flows.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamegM. Duke¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9743eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdccwgdfullx'ccwg - Congestion Control Working Groupdnamex Congestion Control Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-143747hkeywordsÇiTransportbCCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg“âgranking&crfcd9743irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÖetotaletitlex,Specifying New Congestion Control AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxπThis document defines a YANG data model for the management of a syslog process.  It is intended that this data model be used by vendors who implement syslog collectors in their systems.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiJ. Clarke¢kaffiliationnKloud ServicesdnameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnameiC. Wildes¢kaffiliationpVerizon WirelessdnamejK. Koushikddatej–˛hfilenamegrfc9742eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-145874hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDatehEégranking&crfcd9742irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'A YANG Data Model for Syslog ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨The Concise Data Definition Language (CDDL), standardized in RFC 8610, provides "control operators" as its main language extension point. RFCs have added to this extension point in both an application-specific and a more general way.

The present document defines a number of additional generally applicable control operators for text conversion (bytes, integers, printf-style formatting, and JSON) and for an operation on text.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9741eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-143551hkeywordsÇx Concise Data Definition LanguagepControl OperatorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg«òçgranking&crfcd9741irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoConcise Data Definition Language (CDDL): Additional Control Operators for the Conversion and Processing of TextdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚This document specifies new IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Elements (IEs) to solve issues with existing ipv6ExtensionHeaders and tcpOptions IPFIX IEs, especially the ability to export any observed IPv6 extension headers or TCP options.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claiseddatej÷˝hfilenamegrfc9740eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-143543hkeywordsÅeIPFIXkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg«Eågranking&crfcd9740irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexINew IPFIX Information Elements for TCP Options and IPv6 Extension HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies Protocol Independent Multicast Light (PIM Light) and the PIM Light Interface (PLI).  A PLI does not need a PIM Hello message to accept PIM Join/Prune messages, and it can signal multicast states over networks that cannot support full PIM neighbor discovery, such as Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) networks that connect two or more PIM domains.  This document outlines the PIM Light protocol and procedures to ensure loop-free multicast traffic between two or more PIM Light routers.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejH. Bidgoli¢kaffiliationrCisco System, Inc.dnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Mishra¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies Inc.dnamejM. McBrideddatej—hfilenamegrfc9739eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-143550hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateg«îgranking&crfcd9739irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Protocol Independent Multicast Light (PIM Light)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe MESSAGELIMIT extension of the Internet Message Access Protocol (RFCs 3501 and 9051) allows servers to announce a limit on the number of messages that can be processed in a single FETCH, SEARCH, STORE, COPY, or MOVE command (or their UID variants), or in a single APPEND or UID EXPUNGE command.  This helps servers to control resource usage when performing various IMAP operations.  This helps clients to know the message limit enforced by the corresponding IMAP server and avoid issuing commands that would exceed such limit.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeIsodednamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnamenA. P. Achuthan¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnamenV. Nagulakonda¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Alvesddatej–Ìhfilenamegrfc9738eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-143528hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg∆AÇgranking&
crfcd9738irfcNumber&
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIMAP MESSAGELIMIT ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Parallel Network File System (pNFS) allows for a file's metadata and data to be on different servers (i.e., the metadata server (MDS) and the data server (DS)).  When the MDS is restarted, the client can still modify the data file component.  During the recovery phase of startup, the MDS and the DSs work together to recover state.  If the client has not encountered errors with the data files, then the state can be recovered and the resilvering of the data files can be avoided.  With any errors, there is no means by which the client can report errors to the MDS.  As such, the MDS has to assume that a file needs resilvering.  This document presents an extension to RFC 8435 to allow the client to update the metadata via LAYOUTRETURN and avoid the resilvering.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednameiT. Haynes¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednamelT. Myklebustddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9737eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-143307hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategøßÏgranking&	crfcd9737irfcNumber&	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Reporting Errors in NFSv4.2 via LAYOUTRETURNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞RFC 7854, the BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP), uses different message types for different purposes. Most of these are structured as Type, Length, Value (TLV). One message type, the Peer Up message, lacks a set of TLVs defined for its use, instead sharing a namespace with the Initiation message. Experience has shown that this namespace sharing was a mistake, as it hampers the extension of the protocol.

This document updates RFC 7854 by creating an independent namespace for the Peer Up message. It also updates RFCs 8671 and 9069 by moving defined codepoints into the newly introduced registry. Compliant implementations of RFCs 7854, 8671, and 9069 also comply with this specification.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejP. Lucenteddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9736eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-143799hkeywordsÑcIDRdGROWcBGPcBMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg”Utgranking&crfcd9736irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;The BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP) Peer Up Message NamespacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document defines how to use the Address Family Identifier (AFI) 17 "Distinguished Name" in the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP).  LISP introduces two new numbering spaces: Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs) and Routing Locators (RLOCs).  Distinguished Names (DNs) can be used in either EID-Records or RLOC-Records in LISP control messages to convey additional information.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejL. Iannoneddatej—hfilenamegrfc9735eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-142928hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateg£ı˝granking&crfcd9735irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexALocator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Distinguished Name EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 5280 specifies several extended key purpose identifiers (KeyPurposeIds) for X.509 certificates.  This document defines an Instant Messaging (IM) identity KeyPurposeId for inclusion in the Extended Key Usage (EKU) extension of X.509 v3 public key certificatesfadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxRohan Mahy Consulting ServicesdnamegR. Mahyddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9734eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-143009hkeywordsáex.509kcertificaterextended key usagecekuqinstant messagingfim URIhmimi URLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg´ûEgranking&crfcd9734irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEX.509 Certificate Extended Key Usage (EKU) for Instant Messaging URIsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document defines enhancements to the Bootstrapping Remote Secure Key Infrastructure (BRSKI) protocol, known as BRSKI with Alternative Enrollment (BRSKI-AE).  BRSKI-AE extends BRSKI to support certificate enrollment mechanisms instead of the originally specified use of Enrollment over Secure Transport (EST).  It supports certificate enrollment protocols such as the Certificate Management Protocol (CMP) that use authenticated self-contained signed objects for certification messages, allowing for flexibility in network device onboarding scenarios.  The enhancements address use cases where the existing enrollment mechanism may not be feasible or optimal, providing a framework for integrating suitable alternative enrollment protocols.  This document also updates the BRSKI reference architecture to accommodate these alternative methods, ensuring secure and scalable deployment across a range of network environments.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemD. von Oheimb¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamehS. Fries¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamelH. Brockhausddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9733eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-143525hkeywordsàeBRSKIcIoTuzero-touch onboardingx alternative enrollment protocolscCMPxself-contained signed objectsxend-to-end proof of originxauditable source authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg∆<5granking&crfcd9733irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,BRSKI with Alternative Enrollment (BRSKI-AE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a framework for Enhanced Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) based on Network Resource Partitions (NRPs) in order to support the needs of applications with specific traffic performance requirements (e.g., low latency, bounded jitter).  NRP-based enhanced VPNs leverage the VPN and Traffic Engineering (TE) technologies and add characteristics that specific services require beyond those provided by conventional VPNs.  Typically, an NRP-based enhanced VPN will be used to underpin network slicing, but it could also be of use in its own right providing enhanced connectivity services between customer sites.  This document also provides an overview of relevant technologies in different network layers and identifies some areas for potential new work.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationtUniversity of SurreydnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnamekT. Miyasaka¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamefY. Leeddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9732eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-145245hkeywordsÉonetwork slicingstraffic engineeringuperformance guaranteekobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDategÂ„kgranking&crfcd9732irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;A Framework for NRP-Based Enhanced Virtual Private NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykA Virtual Network (VN) is a network provided by a service provider to a customer for the customer to use in any way it wants as though it were a physical network.  This document provides a YANG data model generally applicable to any mode of VN operations.  This includes VN operations as per the Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN) framework (RFC 8453).fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegB. Yoonddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9731eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-145244hkeywordsÉdACTNcSDNbTEkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDategÂ„jgranking&crfcd9731irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5A YANG Data Model for Virtual Network (VN) OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykGeneralized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) control allows each network element (NE) to perform local resource discovery, routing, and signaling in a distributed manner.

The advancement of software-defined transport networking technology enables a group of NEs to be managed through centralized controller hierarchies. This helps to tackle challenges arising from multiple domains, vendors, and technologies. An example of such a centralized architecture is the Abstraction and Control of Traffic-Engineered Networks (ACTN) controller hierarchy, as described in RFC 8453.

Both the distributed and centralized control planes have their respective advantages and should complement each other in the system, rather than compete. This document outlines how the GMPLS distributed control plane can work together with a centralized controller system in a transport network.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehH. Zheng¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefY. Lin¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegY. Zhao¢kaffiliationeCAICTdnameeY. Xu¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiD. Bellerddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9730eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-143549hkeywordsÉlarchitecturedACTNmcontrol planekobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateg«êîgranking&crfcd9730irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Interworking of GMPLS Control and Centralized Controller SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØMost HTTP authentication schemes are probeable in the sense that it is possible for an unauthenticated client to probe whether an origin serves resources that require authentication.  It is possible for an origin to hide the fact that it requires authentication by not generating Unauthorized status codes; however, that only works with non-cryptographic authentication schemes: cryptographic signatures require a fresh nonce to be signed.  Prior to this document, there was no existing way for the origin to share such a nonce without exposing the fact that it serves resources that require authentication.  This document defines a new non-probeable cryptographic authentication scheme.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinazi¢kaffiliationpGuardian ProjectdnameiD. Oliver¢kaffiliationoCloudflare Inc.dnamejJ. Hoylandddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9729eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-143056hkeywordsÑfsecuregtunnelsfmasqueghttp-ngkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategØêögranking&crfcd9729irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(The Concealed HTTP Authentication SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∏This specification defines a metadata format that an OAuth 2.0 client or authorization server can use to obtain the information needed to interact with an OAuth 2.0 protected resource.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvSelf-Issued ConsultingdnamejM.B. Jones¢kaffiliationxIndependent Identity, Inc.dnamegP. Hunt¢kaffiliationdOktadnamejA. Pareckiddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9728eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-145809hkeywordsåeOAuthiDiscoveryhMetadatarDiscovery MetadataxConfiguration InformationoResource ServerrProtected ResourcesResource IdentifierxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONnJSON Web TokencJWTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh	TÁgranking&␀crfcd9728irfcNumber&␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%OAuth 2.0 Protected Resource MetadatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document defines the "api-catalog" well-known URI and link relation.  It is intended to facilitate automated discovery and usage of published Application Programming Interfaces (APIs).  A request to the api-catalog resource will return a document providing information about, and links to, the Publisher's APIs.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhVodafonednamehK. Smithddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9727eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpapidfullx'httpapi - Building Blocks for HTTP APIsdnamexBuilding Blocks for HTTP APIsdtypebwgbidjdoc-146465hkeywordsÅcAPIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehLïØgranking%ˇcrfcd9727irfcNumber%ˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIapi-catalog: A Well-Known URI and Link Relation to Help Discovery of APIsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyeThis document details considerations about how Internet of Things (IoT) devices use IP addresses and DNS names. These concerns become acute as network operators begin deploying Manufacturer Usage Descriptions (MUD), as specified in RFC 8520, to control device access.

Also, this document makes recommendations on when and how to use DNS names in MUD files.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Panddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9726eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-145305hkeywordsácDNScMUDkround-robinqtailored responsefDNSSEClIoT securityoDevice IdentitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÍ÷granking%˛crfcd9726irfcNumber%˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÒetotaletitlexMOperational Considerations for Use of DNS in Internet of Things (IoT) DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx“This document describes a simple HTTP-based protocol that will allow WebRTC-based ingestion of content into streaming services and/or Content Delivery Networks (CDNs).

This document updates RFCs 8840 and 8842.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMillicastdnameqS. Garcia Murillo¢kaffiliationnCoSMo SoftwarednamemA. Gouaillardddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9725eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdwishdfullx)wish - WebRTC Ingest Signaling over HTTPSdnamex"WebRTC Ingest Signaling over HTTPSdtypebwgbidjdoc-145218hkeywordsÉfwebrtcistreamingfingestkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg‰ãgranking%˝crfcd9725irfcNumber%˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%WebRTC-HTTP Ingestion Protocol (WHIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºInternet users are becoming more aware that their activity over the Internet leaves a vast digital footprint, that communications might not always be properly secured, and that their location and actions can be tracked. One of the main factors that eases tracking of Internet users is the wide use of long-lasting, and sometimes persistent, identifiers at various protocol layers. This document focuses on Media Access Control (MAC) addresses.

There have been several initiatives within the IETF and the IEEE 802 standards committees to address some of the privacy issues involved. This document provides an overview of these activities to help coordinate standardization activities in these bodies.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJC. Z√∫√±iga¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationmSafespring ABdnameoA. Andersdotterddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9724eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmadinasdfullxPmadinas - MAC Address Device Identification for Network and Application ServicesdnamexFMAC Address Device Identification for Network and Application Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-144352hkeywordsÜgprivacyhtrackingdslapcrcmh802.11bhh802.11bikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg◊Ô3granking%¸crfcd9724irfcNumber%¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQState of Affairs for Randomized and Changing Media Access Control (MAC) AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document describes a mechanism to advertise IPv6 prefixes in BGP that are associated with Color Extended Communities to establish end-to-end intent-aware paths for Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) services. Such IPv6 prefixes are called "Colored Prefixes", and this mechanism is called "Colored Prefix Routing" (CPR). In SRv6 networks, the Colored Prefixes are the SRv6 locators associated with different intents. SRv6 services (e.g., SRv6 VPN services) with a specific intent could be assigned with SRv6 Segment Identifiers (SIDs) under the corresponding SRv6 locators, which are advertised as Colored Prefixes.

This operational methodology allows the SRv6 service traffic to be steered into end-to-end intent-aware paths based on the longest prefix matching of SRv6 Service SIDs to the Colored Prefixes. The existing IPv6 Address Family and Color Extended Community are reused to advertise IPv6 Colored Prefixes without new BGP extensions; thus, this mechanism is easy to interoperate and can be deployed incrementally in multi-Autonomous System (AS) networks that belong to the same trusted domain.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Wang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefT. Han¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegR. Chenddatej◊%hfilenamegrfc9723eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-146283hkeywordsÅtintent-aware routingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh8´ﬁgranking%˚crfcd9723irfcNumber%˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWBGP Colored Prefix Routing (CPR) for Services Based on Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6The Ethernet Virtual Private Network (EVPN) solution in RFC 7432 provides Designated Forwarder (DF) election procedures for multihomed Ethernet Segments. These procedures have been enhanced further by applying the Highest Random Weight (HRW) algorithm for DF election to avoid unnecessary DF status changes upon a failure. This document improves these procedures by providing a fast DF election upon recovery of the failed link or node associated with the multihomed Ethernet Segment. This document updates RFC 8584 by optionally introducing delays between some of the events therein.

The solution is independent of the number of EVPN Instances (EVIs) associated with that Ethernet Segment, and it is performed via a simple signaling in BGP between the recovered node and each of the other nodes in the multihoming group.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelP. Brissette¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejLA. Burdet¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9722eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-146303hkeywordsÑdEVPNtDesignated ForwarderkConvergencehRecoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh;´8granking%˙crfcd9722irfcNumber%˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Fast Recovery for EVPN Designated Forwarder ElectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies extensions to the Ethernet VPN Integrated Routing and Bridging (EVPN-IRB) procedures specified in RFCs 7432 and 9135 to enhance the mobility mechanisms for networks based on EVPN-IRB.  The proposed extensions improve the handling of host mobility and duplicate address detection in EVPN-IRB networks to cover a broader set of scenarios where a host's unicast IP address to Media Access Control (MAC) address bindings may change across moves.  These enhancements address limitations in the existing EVPN-IRB mobility procedures by providing more efficient and scalable solutions.  The extensions are backward compatible with existing EVPN-IRB implementations and aim to optimize network performance in scenarios involving frequent IP address mobility.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekN. Malhotra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekA. Pattekar¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamejA. Lingala¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehJ. Drakeddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9721eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-145881hkeywordsâbESpEthernet SegmentoPeer-Sync-LocalgOverlaycNVOdNVO3dRT-2dRT-5fMAC-IPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateh§zgranking%˘crfcd9721irfcNumber%˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXExtended Mobility Procedures for Ethernet VPN Integrated Routing and Bridging (EVPN-IRB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyñIn order to improve the readability of RFCs while supporting their archivability, the definitive version of the RFC Series transitioned from plain-text ASCII to XML using the RFCXML vocabulary; different publication versions are rendered from that base document. This document describes how RFCs are published.

This document obsoletes RFC 7990. This document also updates the stability policy in RFC 9280.darea£gacronymirfceditordfullvrfceditor - RFC EditordnamejRFC EditorgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationxSpherical Cow ConsultingdnamekH. Flanaganddatej–˝hfilenamegrfc9720eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrswgdfullxrswg - RFC Series Working GroupdnamexRFC Series Working Groupdtypededwgbidjdoc-142651hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategäÊ€granking%¯crfcd9720irfcNumber%¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnameiEditorialdslugieditorialetitlexRFC Formats and VersionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920∂habstractyThis document defines a YANG data model for the configuration and management of the Routing in Fat Trees (RIFT) Protocol.  The model is based on YANG 1.1, which is defined in RFC 7950 and conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) as described in RFC 8342.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamefY. Wei¢kaffiliationfGooglednameeS. Ma¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamejB. Rijsmanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9719eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdriftdfullxrift - Routing In Fat TreesdnametRouting In Fat Treesdtypebwgbidjdoc-145399hkeywordsÇdRIFTdYANGkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDategÔ˛ígranking%˜crfcd9719irfcNumber%˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/YANG Data Model for Routing in Fat Trees (RIFT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyãThe root zone of the global Domain Name System (DNS) is cryptographically signed using DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC).

In order to obtain secure answers from the root zone of the DNS using DNSSEC, a client must configure a suitable trust anchor. This document describes the format and publication mechanisms IANA uses to distribute the DNSSEC trust anchors.

This document obsoletes RFC 7958.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationhKirei ABdnamekJ. Schlyter¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiG. Bailey¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9718eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-142732hkeywordsÅcKSKkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategî[granking%ˆcrfcd9718irfcNumber%ˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1DNSSEC Trust Anchor Publication for the Root ZonedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractySatellite networks present some interesting challenges for packet networking. The entire topology is continually in motion, with links far less reliable than what is common in terrestrial networks. Some changes to link connectivity can be anticipated due to orbital dynamics.

This document proposes a scalable routing architecture for satellite networks based on existing routing protocols and mechanisms that is enhanced with scheduled link connectivity change information. This document proposes no protocol changes.

This document presents the author's view and is neither the product of the IETF nor a consensus view of the community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameeT. Liddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9717eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-142752hkeywordsÉeIS-ISdMPLSoSegment routingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategó‰granking%ıcrfcd9717irfcNumber%ıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-A Routing Architecture for Satellite NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Segment Routing (SR) architecture leverages source routing and can be directly applied to the use of an MPLS data plane.  A Segment Routing over MPLS (SR-MPLS) network may consist of multiple IGP domains or multiple Autonomous Systems (ASes) under the control of the same organization.  It is useful to have the Label Switched Path (LSP) ping and traceroute procedures when an SR end-to-end path traverses multiple ASes or IGP domains.  This document outlines mechanisms to enable efficient LSP ping and traceroute procedures in inter-AS and inter-domain SR-MPLS networks.  This is achieved through a straightforward extension to the Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) protocol, relying solely on data plane forwarding for handling echo replies on transit nodes.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehK. Arora¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnamemM. Srivastava¢kaffiliationeCienadnamehS. Ninan¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehN. Kumarddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9716eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-142952hkeywordsÜcOAMcEPEfBGP-LScBGPfSPRINGcSDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg¶@•granking%Ùcrfcd9716irfcNumber%ÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Mechanisms for MPLS Ping and Traceroute Procedures in Inter-Domain Segment Routing NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThe widely deployed Extension Mechanisms for DNS (EDNS(0)) feature in the DNS enables a DNS receiver to indicate its received UDP message size capacity, which supports the sending of large UDP responses by a DNS server.  Large DNS/UDP messages are more likely to be fragmented, and IP fragmentation has exposed weaknesses in application protocols.  It is possible to avoid IP fragmentation in DNS by limiting the response size where possible and signaling the need to upgrade from UDP to TCP transport where necessary.  This document describes techniques to avoid IP fragmentation in DNS.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwara¢kaffiliationlAWS SecuritydnamehP. Vixieddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9715eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-142751hkeywordsÉcDNSpIP FragmentationfattackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategó‰granking%Ûcrfcd9715irfcNumber%ÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*IP Fragmentation Avoidance in DNS over UDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¬This document defines the encapsulation for MPLS performance
measurement with the Alternate-Marking Method, which performs
flow-based packet loss, delay, and jitter measurements on MPLS
traffic.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Cheng¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefX. Min¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegT. Zhou¢kaffiliationiFiberHomednamefJ. Dai¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamehY. Pelegddatej÷Óhfilenamegrfc9714eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-143059hkeywordsÇwFlow-ID Label IndicatormFlow-ID LabelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategØÕ∞granking%Úcrfcd9714irfcNumber%ÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPEncapsulation for MPLS Performance Measurement with the Alternate-Marking MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document updates RFC 9171 to clarify that Bundle Protocol Version 7 agents are expected to use the IANA "Bundle Administrative Record Types" registry to identify and document administrative record types.  This document also designates code points for Private and Experimental Use.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx7The Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics LaboratorydnamehB. Siposddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9713eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdtndfullx*dtn - Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdnamex$Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-142650hkeywordsÅcDTNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategä›granking%Òcrfcd9713irfcNumber%ÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Bundle Protocol Version 7 Administrative Record Types RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÑFollowing a review of the IETF meeting venue requirements, this document updates RFC 8718 ("IETF Plenary Meeting Venue Selection Process"), clarifies how the IETF Administration Support Activity (IASA) should interpret some elements of RFC 8718, and specifies a replacement exploratory meeting process, thereby updating RFC 8719 ("High-Level Guidance for the Meeting Policy of the IETF").fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwIETF Administration LLCdnamehJ. Daley¢kaffiliationwIETF Administration LLCdnameiS. Turnerddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9712eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-142750hkeywordsÇkIETF regionsExploratory meetingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDategó–¡granking%crfcd9712irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‚etotaletitlex&IETF Meeting Venue Requirements ReviewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøAn Entity Attestation Token (EAT) provides an attested claims set that describes the state and characteristics of an entity, a device such as a smartphone, an Internet of Things (IoT) device, network equipment, or such. This claims set is used by a relying party, server, or service to determine the type and degree of trust placed in the entity.

An EAT is either a CBOR Web Token (CWT) or a JSON Web Token (JWT) with attestation-oriented claims.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsSecurity Theory LLCdnamelL. Lundblade¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. Mandyam¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies Inc.dnamemJ. O'Donoghue¢kaffiliationxRed Hound Software, Inc.dnamejC. Wallaceddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9711eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdratsdfullx$rats - Remote ATtestation ProcedureSdnamexRemote ATtestation ProcedureSdtypebwgbidjdoc-145879hkeywordsÉgsigningkattestationdcborkobsoletedByÄepagesUopublicationDatehôÓgranking%Ôcrfcd9711irfcNumber%ÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"The Entity Attestation Token (EAT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document provides simple fixes to the IANA "IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Entities" registry.  Specifically, this document provides updates to fix shortcomings in the description of some Information Elements (IEs), to ensure a consistent structure when citing an existing IANA registry, and to fix broken pointers, orphaned section references, etc.  The updates are also meant to bring some consistency among the entries of the registry.fadNamesMahesh Jethanandanidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claiseddatej÷¸hfilenamegrfc9710eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-143272hkeywordsÜeIPFIXjOperationsqNetwork ProvidersqNetwork ManagmentqAnomaly detectioniTelemetrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategæ∏mgranking%Ócrfcd9710irfcNumber%ÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMSimple Fixes to the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Entities IANA RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠This document specifies the derivation of the content-encryption key or the content-authenticated-encryption key in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) using the HMAC-based Extract-and-Expand Key Derivation Function (HKDF) with SHA-256.  The use of this mechanism provides protection against an attacker that manipulates the content-encryption algorithm identifier or the content-authenticated-encryption algorithm identifier.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housleyddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9709eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-142555hkeywordsÇvcontent-encryption keyx$content-authenticated-encryption keykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÅîËgranking%Ìcrfcd9709irfcNumber%ÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Encryption Key Derivation in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Using HKDF with SHA-256dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThis document specifies the conventions for using the Hierarchical Signature System (HSS) / Leighton-Micali Signature (LMS) hash-based signature algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  In addition, the algorithm identifier and public key syntax are provided.  The HSS/LMS algorithm is one form of hash-based digital signature; it is described in RFC 8554.  This document obsoletes RFC 8708.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9708eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-142554hkeywordsÇqdigital signatureomessage contentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÅè”granking%Ïcrfcd9708irfcNumber%ÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Use of the HSS/LMS Hash-Based Signature Algorithm in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty"The "Barriers to Internet Access of Services (BIAS)" workshop was
convened by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) from January 15-17,
2024 as a three-day online meeting. Based on the submitted position
papers, the workshop covered three areas of interest: the role of
Community Networks in Internet access of services, reports and
comments on the observed digital divide, and measurements of
censorship and censorship circumvention. This report summarizes the
workshop's discussions and serves as a reference for reports on the
current barriers to Internet access.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the
workshop. The views and positions documented in this report were
expressed during the workshop by participants and do not necessarily
reflect the IAB's views and positions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Knodelddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9707eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-143298hkeywordsÑoInternet AccessjCensorshiprCommunity Networksndigital dividekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategøV∂granking%Îcrfcd9707irfcNumber%ÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexNReport from the IAB Workshop on Barriers to Internet Access of Services (BIAS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$As Internet audience sizes for high-interest live events reach unprecedented levels and bitrates climb to support formats and applications such as 4K, 8K, and Augmented Reality (AR), live streaming can place a unique type of stress upon network resources.  TreeDN is a tree-based Content Delivery Network (CDN) architecture designed to address the distinctive scaling challenges of live streaming to mass audiences.  TreeDN enables operators to offer Replication-as-a-Service (RaaS) at a fraction of the cost of traditional, unicast-based CDNs -- in some cases, at no additional cost to the infrastructure.  In addition to efficiently utilizing network resources to deliver existing multi-destination traffic, this architecture also enables new types of content and use cases that previously were not possible or economically viable using traditional CDN approaches.  Finally, TreeDN is a decentralized architecture and a democratizing technology that makes content distribution more accessible to more people by dramatically reducing the costs of replication.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekL. Giuliano¢kaffiliationgVerizondnameiC. Lenart¢kaffiliationeGEANTdnamegR. Adamddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9706eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmopsdfullwmops - Media OPerationSdnamepMedia OPerationSdtypebwgbidjdoc-142649hkeywordsÜimulticastcSSMcAMTdLISPcCDNgPIM-SSMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategäøgranking%Ícrfcd9706irfcNumber%ÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVTreeDN: Tree-Based Content Delivery Network (CDN) for Live Streaming to Mass AudiencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥The RSVP-TE Fast Reroute (FRR) extensions specified in RFC 4090 define two local repair techniques to reroute Label Switched Path (LSP) traffic over pre-established backup tunnels.  Facility backup method allows one or more LSPs traversing a connected link or node to be protected using a bypass tunnel.  The many-to-one nature of local repair technique is attractive from a scalability point of view.  This document enumerates facility backup procedures in RFC 4090 that rely on refresh timeout, hence, making facility backup method refresh-interval dependent.  The RSVP-TE extensions defined in this document will enhance the facility backup protection mechanism by making the corresponding procedures refresh-interval independent, and hence, compatible with the Refresh-Interval Independent RSVP (RI-RSVP) capability specified in RFC 8370.  Hence, this document updates RFC 4090 in order to support the RI-RSVP capability specified in RFC 8370.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnameoC. Ramachandran¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationmVerizon, Inc.dnamejD. Pacelladdatej◊hfilenamegrfc9705eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-145217hkeywordsÇkri-rsvp-frrkRI-RSVP-FRRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg‰Ç1granking%Ècrfcd9705irfcNumber%ÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBRefresh-Interval Independent RSVP Fast Reroute Facility ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶When split-horizon DNS is deployed by a network, certain domain names can be resolved authoritatively by a network-provided DNS resolver.  DNS clients that are not configured to use this resolver by default can use it for these specific domains only.  This specification defines a mechanism for domain owners to inform DNS clients about local resolvers that are authorized to answer authoritatively for certain subdomains.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationtCitrix Systems, Inc.dnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationnVodafone GroupdnamehK. Smith¢kaffiliationtMeta Platforms, Inc.dnamekB. Schwartzddatej–ˆhfilenamegrfc9704eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcadddfullxadd - Adaptive DNS DiscoverydnamevAdaptive DNS Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-142780hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategô~7granking%Ëcrfcd9704irfcNumber%ËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHEstablishing Local DNS Authority in Validated Split-Horizon EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈEgress Peer Engineering (EPE) is an application of Segment Routing (SR) that solves the problem of egress peer selection.  The SR-based BGP-EPE solution allows a centralized controller, e.g., a Software-Defined Network (SDN) controller, to program any egress peer.  The EPE solution requires the node or the SDN controller to program 1) the PeerNode Segment Identifier (SID) describing a session between two nodes, 2) the PeerAdj SID describing the link or links that are used by the sessions between peer nodes, and 3) the PeerSet SID describing any connected interface to any peer in the related group.  This document provides new sub-TLVs for EPE-SIDs that are used in the Target FEC Stack TLV (Type 1) in MPLS Ping and Traceroute procedures.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnamemM. Srivastava¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehK. Arora¢kaffiliationeCienadnamehS. Ninan¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeX. Xuddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9703eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-142461hkeywordsácOAMcEPEfBGP-LScBGPfSPRINGcSDNcSIDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategpCπgranking%Ácrfcd9703irfcNumber%ÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexêLabel Switched Path (LSP) Ping/Traceroute for Segment Routing (SR) Egress Peer Engineering (EPE) Segment Identifiers (SIDs) with MPLS Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis document defines two YANG modules.  The first module is the initial version of the IANA-maintained YANG module for Maximum Segment Identifier (SID) Depth (MSD) Types, which includes identities for both the MPLS data plane and Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) data plane.  The second module augments the IETF MPLS YANG data model to provide support for MPLS MSDs as defined in RFCs 8476 and 8491.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9702eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-142553hkeywordsÅiMSD TypeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÅà‰granking%Êcrfcd9702irfcNumber%ÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSYANG Data Model for Maximum Segment Identifier (SID) Depth (MSD) Types and MPLS MSDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxuThis specification proposes an additional response secured by JSON Web Token (JWT) for OAuth 2.0 Token Introspection.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjyes.com AGdnamenT. Lodderstedt¢kaffiliationoConnect2id Ltd.dnamelV. Dzhuvinovddatej÷©hfilenamegrfc9701eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-142820hkeywordsÉstoken introspectioncJWTfoauth2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategúägranking%Âcrfcd9701irfcNumber%ÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;JSON Web Token (JWT) Response for OAuth Token IntrospectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyúThis document describes best current security practice for OAuth 2.0.  It updates and extends the threat model and security advice given in RFCs 6749, 6750, and 6819 to incorporate practical experiences gathered since OAuth 2.0 was published and covers new threats relevant due to the broader application of OAuth 2.0.  Further, it deprecates some modes of operation that are deemed less secure or even insecure.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfSPRINDdnamenT. Lodderstedt¢kaffiliationfYubicodnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationvIndependent ResearcherdnamekA. Labunets¢kaffiliationhAuthletednamegD. Fettddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9700eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-142818hkeywordsÉlthreat modelgattackskmitigationskobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDategúägranking%‰crfcd9700irfcNumber%‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex,Best Current Practice for OAuth 2.0 SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document explores the issues involved in the use of edge computing resources to operationalize a media use case that involves an Extended Reality (XR) application.  In particular, this document discusses an XR application that can run on devices having different form factors (such as different physical sizes and shapes) and needs edge computing resources to mitigate the effect of problems such as the need to support interactive communication requiring low latency, limited battery power, and heat dissipation from those devices.  This document also discusses the expected behavior of XR applications, which can be used to manage traffic, and the service requirements for XR applications to be able to run on the network.  Network operators who are interested in providing edge computing resources to operationalize the requirements of such applications are the intended audience for this document.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Krishna¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Rahmanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9699eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmopsdfullwmops - Media OPerationSdnamepMedia OPerationSdtypebwgbidjdoc-142463hkeywordsÉklow-latencymmanaged-cloudgoffloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategpM}granking%„crfcd9699irfcNumber%„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMUse Case for an Extended Reality Application on Edge Computing InfrastructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines an IMAP extension to let clients know that the messages in this IMAP server are also available via the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP), and how.  It is intended for clients that want to migrate gradually to JMAP or use JMAP extensions within an IMAP client.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamenA. Gulbrandsen¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekB. Gondwanaddatej÷˚hfilenamegrfc9698eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-142705hkeywordsÇdIMAPdJMAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategëMqgranking%‚crfcd9698irfcNumber%‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!The JMAPACCESS Extension for IMAPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıThis document describes an approach for Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Relying Parties to detect a particular form of RPKI Repository Delta Protocol (RRDP) session desynchronization and how to recover.  This document updates RFC 8182.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamejT. de Kockddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9697eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-142326hkeywordsÉqdesynchronizationdRPKIdRRDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg\~ granking%·crfcd9697irfcNumber%·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIDetecting RPKI Repository Delta Protocol (RRDP) Session DesynchronizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document discusses the properties, applicability, and operational considerations of Routing in Fat Trees (RIFT) in different network scenarios with the intention of providing a rough guide on how RIFT can be deployed to simplify routing operations in Clos topologies and their variations.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamefY. Wei¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationfYandexdnamelD. Afanasiev¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemT. Przygiendaddatej–Ùhfilenamegrfc9696eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdriftdfullxrift - Routing In Fat TreesdnametRouting In Fat Treesdtypebwgbidjdoc-145390hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDategÔ7granking%‡crfcd9696irfcNumber%‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRouting in Fat Trees (RIFT) Applicability and Operational ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo registers and documents the 'haptics' top-level media type, under which subtypes for representation formats for haptics may be registered.  This document also serves as a registration for a set of subtypes, which are representative of some existing subtypes already in use.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameoY. K. Muthusamy¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Ullrichddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9695eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhmediamandfullx!mediaman - Media Type MaintenancednamevMedia Type Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-144543hkeywordsÑetouchgtactilelvibrotactilekkinesthetickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÿˆﬁgranking%ﬂcrfcd9695irfcNumber%ﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"The 'haptics' Top-Level Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9993∑habstractx”This document defines best practices for defining new top-level media types.  It also introduces a registry for top-level media types, and contains a short history of top-level media types.  It updates RFC 6838.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxAoyama Gakuin UniversitydnamekM.J. D√ºrstddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9694eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmediamandfullx!mediaman - Media Type MaintenancednamevMedia Type Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-144542hkeywordsÇjMedia TypeiTop-LevelkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDategÿˆﬁgranking%ﬁcrfcd9694irfcNumber%ﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex:Guidelines for the Definition of New Top-Level Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁRFC 2544 defines a benchmarking methodology for network interconnect devices.  RFC 5180 addresses IPv6 specificities, and it also provides a technology update but excludes IPv6 transition technologies.  RFC 8219 addresses IPv6 transition technologies, including stateful NAT64.  However, none of them discuss how to apply pseudorandom port numbers from RFC 4814 to any stateful NATxy (such as NAT44, NAT64, and NAT66) technologies.  This document discusses why using pseudorandom port numbers with stateful NATxy gateways is a difficult problem.  It recommends a solution that limits the port number ranges and uses two test phases (phase 1 and phase 2).  This document shows how the classic performance measurement procedures (e.g., throughput, frame loss rate, latency, etc.) can be carried out.  New performance metrics and measurement procedures are also defined for measuring the maximum connection establishment rate, connection tear-down rate, and connection tracking table capacity.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSz√©chenyi Istv√°n UniversitydnameiG. Lencse¢kaffiliationnSoftBank Corp.dnamehK. Shimaddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9693eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-142556hkeywordsálBenchmarkingnStateful NATxyuMeasurement ProcedurejThroughputoFrame Loss RategLatencycPDVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÅôgranking%›crfcd9693irfcNumber%›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Benchmarking Methodology for Stateful NATxy GatewaysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document defines a specialized, dynamic routing protocol for Clos, fat tree, and variants thereof.  These topologies were initially used within crossbar interconnects and consequently router and switch backplanes, but their characteristics make them ideal for constructing IP fabrics as well.  The protocol specified by this document is optimized towards the minimization of control plane state to support very large substrates as well as the minimization of configuration and operational complexity to allow for a simplified deployment of said topologies.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegJ. Head¢kaffiliationtHudson River TradingdnameiA. Sharma¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamejB. Rijsman¢kaffiliationfYandexdnamelD. Afanasievddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9692eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdriftdfullxrift - Routing In Fat TreesdnametRouting In Fat Treesdtypebwgbidjdoc-145389hkeywordsîpRouting protocolcIGPsAnisotropic routingdCloshFat treeeCloudoDistance vectorjLink staterOptimized floodinghZeroconfndisaggregationffabriccZTPcLIEcTIEdTIDEdTIREfKV-TIEkmulti-planecToFkobsoletedByÄepagesñopublicationDategÔ6granking%‹crfcd9692irfcNumber%‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRIFT: Routing in Fat TreesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNA Trust Anchor Locator (TAL) is used by Relying Parties (RPs) in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) to locate and validate a Trust Anchor (TA) Certification Authority (CA) certificate used in RPKI validation.  This document defines an RPKI signed object for a Trust Anchor Key (TAK).  A TAK object can be used by a TA to signal to RPs the location(s) of the accompanying CA certificate for the current public key, as well as the successor public key and the location(s) of its CA certificate.  This object helps to support planned key rollovers without impacting RPKI validation.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfLACNICdnamekC. Martinez¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamekT. Harrison¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamenT. Bruijnzeels¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. Austeinddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9691eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-142379hkeywordsÉhsecuritylcryptographyeX.509kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategcb∞granking%€crfcd9691irfcNumber%€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPA Profile for Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Trust Anchor Keys (TAKs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThe RSA Key Encapsulation Mechanism (RSA-KEM) algorithm is a one-pass (store-and-forward) cryptographic mechanism for an originator to securely send keying material to a recipient using the recipient's RSA public key.  The RSA-KEM algorithm is specified in Clause 11.5 of ISO/IEC: 18033-2:2006.  This document specifies the conventions for using the RSA-KEM algorithm as a standalone KEM algorithm and the conventions for using the RSA-KEM algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) using KEMRecipientInfo as specified in RFC 9629.  This document obsoletes RFC 5990.fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9690eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-142988hkeywordsÇx!Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM)pKEMRecipientInfokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg™z‹granking%⁄crfcd9690irfcNumber%⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFUse of the RSA-KEM Algorithm in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"The PCE is a core component of a Software-Defined Networking (SDN) system. It can be used to compute optimal paths for network traffic and update existing paths to reflect changes in the network or traffic demands. The PCE was developed to derive Traffic Engineering (TE) paths in MPLS networks, which are supplied to the headend of the paths using the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP).

SDN has much broader applicability than signalled MPLS TE networks, and the PCE may be used to determine paths in a range of use cases including static Label-Switched Paths (LSPs), Segment Routing (SR), Service Function Chaining (SFC), and most forms of a routed or switched network. Therefore, it is reasonable to consider PCEP as a control protocol for use in these environments to allow the PCE to be fully enabled as a central controller.

A PCE as a Central Controller (PCECC) can simplify the processing of a distributed control plane by blending it with elements of SDN without necessarily completely replacing it. This document describes general considerations for PCECC deployment and examines its applicability and benefits, as well as its challenges and limitations, through a number of use cases. PCEP extensions, which are required for the PCECC use cases, are covered in separate documents.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationpEtheric NetworksdnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationuTencent Holdings Ltd.dnameeZ. Ke¢kaffiliationvMTS Web Services (MWS)dnamekB. Khasanovddatej◊	hfilenamegrfc9689eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-142236hkeywordsÅcSDNkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDategP˚granking%Ÿcrfcd9689irfcNumber%ŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Use Cases for a PCE as a Central Controller (PCECC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document describes the conventions for using the one-way hash functions in the SHA3 family with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  The SHA3 family can be used as a message digest algorithm, as part of a signature algorithm, as part of a message authentication code, or as part of a Key Derivation Function (KDF).fadNamejDeb Cooleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housleyddatej◊
hfilenamegrfc9688eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-141841hkeywordsÉdHKDFkKMAC128-KDFkKMAC256-KDFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg;√}granking%ÿcrfcd9688irfcNumber%ÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPUse of the SHA3 One-Way Hash Functions in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$This document defines the SendHoldTimer, along with the SendHoldTimer_Expires event, for the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) Finite State Machine (FSM).  Implementation of the SendHoldTimer helps overcome situations where a BGP connection is not terminated after the local system detects that the remote system is not processing BGP messages.  This document specifies that the local system should close the BGP connection and not solely rely on the remote system for connection closure when the SendHoldTimer expires.  This document updates RFC 4271.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationhPort 179dnameqB. Cartwright-Cox¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameeY. Quddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9687eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-141180hkeywordsÇcBGPcTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg+=¿granking%◊crfcd9687irfcNumber%◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4) Send Hold TimerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxâThis document defines a method to inform a DHCPv6 server that a device has one or more self-generated or statically configured addresses.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkGoogle, LLCdnameiW. Kumari¢kaffi