grity measurements from a device, following the operational context defined in RFC 9683 "TPM-based Network Device Remote Integrity Verification".  Complementary measurement logs originating from one or more Roots of Trust for Measurement (RTMs) are also provided by the YANG RPCs.  The defined module requires the inclusion of the following in the device components of the composite device on which the YANG server is running: at least one Trusted Platform Module (TPM) of either version 1.2 or 2.0 as well as a corresponding TPM Software Stack (TSS), or an equivalent hardware implementation that includes the protected capabilities as provided by TPMs as well as a corresponding software stack.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationwFraunhofer SIT | ATHENEdnamekH. Birkholz¢kaffiliationwFraunhofer SIT | ATHENEdnamehM. Eckel¢kaffiliationkThoughtSpotdnamekS. Bhandari¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegE. Voit¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiB. Sulzen¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefL. Xia¢kaffiliationcHPEdnameiT. Laffey¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamenG. C. Fedorkowddatej–êhfilenamegrfc9684eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdratsdfullx$rats - Remote ATtestation ProcedureSdnamexRemote ATtestation ProcedureSdtypebwgbidjdoc-142259hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDategS:ßgranking%‘crfcd9684irfcNumber%‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{A YANG Data Model for Challenge-Response-Based Remote Attestation (CHARRA) Procedures Using Trusted Platform Modules (TPMs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis document describes a workflow for remote attestation of the integrity of firmware and software installed on network devices that contain Trusted Platform Modules (TPMs), as defined by the Trusted Computing Group (TCG), or equivalent hardware implementations that include the protected capabilities, as provided by TPMs.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamenG. C. Fedorkow¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegE. Voit¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamesJ. Fitzgerald-McKayddatej÷¿hfilenamegrfc9683eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdratsdfullx$rats - Remote ATtestation ProcedureSdnamexRemote ATtestation ProcedureSdtypebwgbidjdoc-142258hkeywordsÇkAttestationcTPMkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDategS:ßgranking%”crfcd9683irfcNumber%”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTRemote Integrity Verification of Network Devices Containing Trusted Platform ModulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThe Concise Data Definition Language (CDDL), as defined in RFCs 8610 and 9165, provides an easy and unambiguous way to express structures for protocol messages and data formats that are represented in Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) or JSON.

This document updates RFC 8610 by addressing related errata reports and making other small fixes for the ABNF grammar defined for CDDL.fadNamekOrie Steeledarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9682eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-141842hkeywordsÅx Concise Data Definition LanguagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg;’Ãgranking%“crfcd9682irfcNumber%“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Updates to the Concise Data Definition Language (CDDL) GrammardtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóCurrent Link State PDU flooding rates are much slower than what modern networks can support.  The use of IS-IS at larger scale requires faster flooding rates to achieve desired convergence goals.  This document discusses the need for faster flooding, the issues around faster flooding, and some example approaches to achieve faster flooding.  It also defines protocol extensions relevant to faster flooding.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliation`dnamekG. Solignac¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Karasek¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoG. Van de Velde¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamemT. Przygiendaddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9681eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-141960hkeywordsácLSPjcongestionlflow controlescalekperformancedIS-ShfloodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategE␀ìgranking%—crfcd9681irfcNumber%—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesIS-IS Fast FloodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx´This document provides education and guidance for IETF participants on compliance with antitrust laws and how to reduce antitrust risks in connection with IETF activities.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationwIETF Administration LLCdnamehJ. Daleyddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9680eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-140167hkeywordsÖoanticompetitiveocompetition lawfpolicyjcomplianceorecommendationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg"££granking%–crfcd9680irfcNumber%–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Antitrust Guidelines for IETF ParticipantsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This specification defines a method for computing a hash value over a CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) Key.  It specifies which fields within the COSE Key structure are included in the cryptographic hash computation, the process for creating a canonical representation of these fields, and how to hash the resulting byte sequence.  The resulting hash value, referred to as a "thumbprint", can be used to identify or select the corresponding key.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlSECOM CO LTDdnamehK. Isobe¢kaffiliationx.University of Applied Sciences Bonn-Rhein-SiegdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationiTransmutednameiO. Steeleddatej–˝hfilenamegrfc9679eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-142438hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategeÏ>granking%œcrfcd9679irfcNumber%œestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) Key ThumbprintdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis document updates RFC 9048, "Improved Extensible Authentication Protocol Method for 3GPP Mobile Network Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA')", and its predecessor RFC 5448 with an optional extension providing ephemeral key exchange.  The extension EAP-AKA' Forward Secrecy (EAP-AKA' FS), when negotiated, provides forward secrecy for the session keys generated as a part of the authentication run in EAP-AKA'.  This prevents an attacker who has gained access to the long-term key from obtaining session keys established in the past.  In addition, EAP-AKA' FS mitigates passive attacks (e.g., large-scale pervasive monitoring) against future sessions.  This forces attackers to use active attacks instead.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejK. Norrman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Preu√ü Mattssonddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9678eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-143548hkeywordsÜcEAPcAKAdAKA'hEAP-AKA'kEAP-AKA' FSd3GPPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg«êëgranking%Œcrfcd9678irfcNumber%ŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÜForward Secrecy Extension to the Improved Extensible Authentication Protocol Method for Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA' FS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`The delivery of content over HTTPS involving multiple Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) raises credential management issues.  This document defines metadata in the Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Control and Metadata interface to set up HTTPS delegation using delegated credentials from an upstream CDN (uCDN) to a downstream CDN (dCDN).fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehF. Fieau¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejE. Stephan¢kaffiliationiBroadpeakdnameiG. Bichot¢kaffiliationiBroadpeakdnamejC. Neumannddatej–¸hfilenamegrfc9677eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-140259hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg#ˆIgranking%Õcrfcd9677irfcNumber%ÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRContent Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Metadata for Delegated CredentialsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÕThis document describes LEX, a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace identifier that identifies, names, assigns, and manages persistent resources in the legal domain. This specification allows adoption of a common convention by multiple jurisdictions to facilitate ease of reference and access to resources in the legal domain.

This specification is an Independent Submission to the RFC Series. It is not a standard and does not have the consensus of the IETF.fadNamepMurray KucherawygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Spinosa¢kaffiliationx(National Research Council of Italy (CNR)dnamenE. Francesconi¢kaffiliation`dnamegC. Lupoddatej◊
hfilenamegrfc9676eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-146169hkeywordsÑxidentifier for legal documentsklegislationhcase lawsadministrative actskobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateh-¬granking%Ãcrfcd9676irfcNumber%ÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex?LEX: A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for Sources of LawdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThe Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN) architecture describes a type of challenged network in which communications may be significantly affected by long signal propagation delays, frequent link disruptions, or both. The unique characteristics of this environment require a unique approach to network management that supports asynchronous transport, autonomous local control, and a small footprint (in both resources and dependencies) so as to deploy on constrained devices.

This document describes a DTN Management Architecture (DTNMA) suitable for managing devices in any challenged environment but, in particular, those communicating using the DTN Bundle Protocol (BP). Operating over BP requires an architecture that neither presumes synchronized transport behavior nor relies on query-response mechanisms. Implementations compliant with this DTNMA should expect to successfully operate in extremely challenging conditions, such as over unidirectional links and other places where BP is the preferred transport.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnameoE. Birrane, III¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnameiS. Heiner¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamehE. Annisddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9675eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdtndfullx*dtn - Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdnamex$Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-141427hkeywordsÇcDTNrNetwork ManagementkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateg,Àugranking%Àcrfcd9675irfcNumber%ÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Delay-Tolerant Networking Management Architecture (DTNMA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document describes a Same-Origin Policy (SOP) requirement for Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Repository Delta Protocol (RRDP) servers and clients.  Application of a SOP in RRDP client/server communication isolates resources such as Delta and Snapshot files from different Repository Servers, reducing possible attack vectors.  This document updates RFC 8182.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamekJ. Snijdersddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9674eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-142234hkeywordsÉksame-origindRPKIdRRDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategPógranking% crfcd9674irfcNumber% estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Same-Origin Policy for the RPKI Repository Delta Protocol (RRDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document specifies procedures for processing IPv6 Hop-by-Hop options in IPv6 routers and hosts.  It modifies the procedures specified in the IPv6 Protocol Specification (RFC 8200) to make processing of the IPv6 Hop-by-Hop Options header practical with the goal of making IPv6 Hop-by-Hop options useful to deploy and use at IPv6 routers and hosts.  This document updates RFC 8200.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej–Úhfilenamegrfc9673eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-140251hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg#€≠granking%…crfcd9673irfcNumber%…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-IPv6 Hop-by-Hop Options Processing ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõRFC 8110 describes Opportunistic Wireless Encryption (OWE), a mode that allows unauthenticated clients to connect to a network using encrypted traffic. This document transfers the ongoing maintenance and further development of the protocol to the IEEE 802.11 Working Group.

This document updates RFC 8110 by noting that future work on the protocol described therein will occur in the IEEE 802.11 Working Group.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkGoogle, LLCdnameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationxHewlett-Packard EnterprisednamejD. Harkinsddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9672eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-142462hkeywordsÑdIEEEcOWEx!Opportunistic Wireless EncryptiongRFC8110kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategpJ¿granking%»crfcd9672irfcNumber%»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOTransferring Opportunistic Wireless Encryption to the IEEE 802.11 Working GroupdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the "processcalendar" extension to the Sieve email filtering language.  The "processcalendar" extension gives Sieve the ability to process machine-readable calendar data that is encapsulated in an email message using Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME).fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamelK. Murchison¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnameiR. Signes¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekM. Horsfallddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9671eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-139688hkeywordsÅeSievekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg	´çgranking%«crfcd9671irfcNumber%«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDSieve Email Filtering: Extension for Processing Calendar AttachmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÂThis document specifies a data model for sharing data between users using the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP).  Future documents can reference this document when defining data types to support a consistent model of sharing.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamejN. Jenkinsddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9670eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-141864hkeywordsÉdJMAPdJSONgsharingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg=Igranking%∆crfcd9670irfcNumber%∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) SharingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&eBPF (which is no longer an acronym for anything), also commonly referred to as BPF, is a technology with origins in the Linux kernel that can run untrusted programs in a privileged context such as an operating system kernel.  This document specifies the BPF instruction set architecture (ISA).fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Thalerddatej÷˙hfilenamegrfc9669eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbpfdfullnbpf - BPF/eBPFdnamehBPF/eBPFdtypebwgbidjdoc-140263hkeywordsÉdeBPFovirtual machinemextensibilitykobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateg$ƒgranking%≈crfcd9669irfcNumber%≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&BPF Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠The lightweight authenticated key exchange protocol Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman Over COSE (EDHOC) can be run over the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) and used by two peers to establish a Security Context for the security protocol Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE).  This document details this use of the EDHOC protocol by specifying a number of additional and optional mechanisms, including an optimization approach for combining the execution of EDHOC with the first OSCORE transaction.  This combination reduces the number of round trips required to set up an OSCORE Security Context and to complete an OSCORE transaction using that Security Context.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelF. Palombini¢kaffiliationgRISE ABdnameiM. Tiloca¢kaffiliationgRISE ABdnamekR. H√∂glund¢kaffiliationgEripticdnamelS. Hristozov¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Selanderddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9668eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-141840hkeywordsáhsecuritymkey agreementtsecure communicationxconstrained environmentscAKEx&lightweight authenticated key exchangelIoT securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg;√}granking%ƒcrfcd9668irfcNumber%ƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex£Using Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman Over COSE (EDHOC) with the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) and Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîRouting with link-state protocols in dense network topologies can result in suboptimal convergence times due to the overhead associated with flooding. This can be addressed by decreasing the flooding topology so that it is less dense.

This document discusses the problem in some depth and an architectural solution. Specific protocol changes for IS-IS, OSPFv2, and OSPFv3 are described in this document.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamegH. Chen¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalil¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamejS. Dontuladdatej◊hfilenamegrfc9667eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-140262hkeywordsájdatacentercIGPgroutinghfloodingedenseegraphhtopologykobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateg$granking%√crfcd9667irfcNumber%√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Dynamic Flooding on Dense GraphsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLink-state routing protocols have hierarchical abstraction already built into them. However, when lower levels are used for transit, they must expose their internal topologies to each other, thereby leading to scaling issues.

To avoid such issues, this document discusses extensions to the IS-IS routing protocol that allow Level 1 (L1) areas to provide transit but only inject an abstraction of the Level 1 topology into Level 2 (L2). Each Level 1 area is represented as a single Level 2 node, thereby enabling a greater scale.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnamegS. Chen¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnamelV. Ilangovan¢kaffiliationlVerizon Inc.dnameiG. Mishraddatej÷˘hfilenamegrfc9666eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-140261hkeywordsàjdatacentercIGPgroutinghtopologyelevelkabstractioneIS-ISeproxykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg$granking%¬crfcd9666irfcNumber%¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletArea Proxy for IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Service Registration Protocol (SRP) for DNS-based Service Discovery (DNS-SD) uses the standard DNS Update mechanism to enable DNS-SD using only unicast packets.  This makes it possible to deploy DNS-SD without multicast, which greatly improves scalability and improves performance on networks where multicast service is not an optimal choice, particularly IEEE 802.11 (Wi-Fi) and IEEE 802.15.4 networks.  DNS-SD Service registration uses public keys and SIG(0) to allow services to defend their registrations.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshireddatej◊␀hfilenamegrfc9665eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednssddfullx5dnssd - Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydnamex-Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-146442hkeywordsÑmMulticast DNSxDNS-Based Service DiscoveryjDNS UpdatefSIG(0)kobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDatehJ,¯granking%¡crfcd9665irfcNumber%¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Service Registration Protocol for DNS-Based Service DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document describes an EDNS(0) option that can be used between DNS Update Requesters and authoritative DNS servers to include a lifetime (lease duration) in a DNS Update or DNS Update Response, allowing a server to garbage collect stale Resource Records that have been added by DNS Updates if they are not renewed.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Lemonddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9664eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednssddfullx5dnssd - Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydnamex-Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-146441hkeywordsÅjDNS UpdatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatehJ,˜granking%¿crfcd9664irfcNumber%¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4An EDNS(0) Option to Negotiate Leases on DNS UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document discusses an IPv6 deployment scenario when individual nodes connected to large broadcast networks (such as enterprise networks or public Wi-Fi networks) are allocated unique prefixes via DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation (DHCPv6-PD), as specified in RFC 8415.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkGoogle, LLCdnamejL. Colitti¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkova¢kaffiliationfGooglednameeX. Maddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9663eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-139599hkeywordsÉdIPv6eSLAACiDHCPv6-PDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg)dgranking%øcrfcd9663irfcNumber%øestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrUsing DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation (DHCPv6-PD) to Allocate Unique IPv6 Prefixes per Client in Large Broadcast NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=RFCs 5425 and 6012 describe using TLS and DTLS to securely transport syslog messages.  This document updates the cipher suites required by RFC 5245 (TLS Transport Mapping for Syslog) and RFC 6012 (DTLS Transport Mapping for Syslog).  It also updates the protocol recommended by RFC 6012 for secure datagram transport.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Lonvick¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationfVenafidnamejJ. Saloweyddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9662eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-139598hkeywordsáfsyslogmsecure syslogxTLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHAx%TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256dDTLScTLSlcipher suitekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg)dgranking%æcrfcd9662irfcNumber%æestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Updates to the Cipher Suites in Secure SyslogdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡This document specifies a data model for managing Sieve scripts on a server using the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP).  Clients can use this protocol to efficiently search, access, organize, and validate Sieve scripts.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamelK. Murchisonddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9661eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-139446hkeywordsÉdJMAPdJSONeSievekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefˆ—§granking%Ωcrfcd9661irfcNumber%ΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;The JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) for Sieve ScriptsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe DNS ZONEVERSION option is a way for DNS clients to request, and for authoritative DNS servers to provide, information regarding the version of the zone from which a response is generated. The SERIAL field from the Start of Authority (SOA) resource record (RR) is a good example of a zone's version, and it is the only one defined by this specification. Additional version types may be defined by future specifications.

Including zone version data in a response simplifies and improves the quality of debugging and diagnostics since the version and the DNS answer are provided atomically. This can be especially useful for zones and DNS providers that leverage IP anycast or multiple backend systems. It functions similarly to the DNS Name Server Identifier (NSID) option described in RFC 5001.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiNIC ChilednamejH. Salgado¢kaffiliationlDigitalOceandnamejM. Vergara¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejD. Wesselsddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9660eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-139687hkeywordsÅkzoneversionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg	¶˘granking%ºcrfcd9660irfcNumber%ºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The DNS Zone Version (ZONEVERSION) OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoDeployments of Zstandard, or "zstd", can use different window sizes to limit memory usage during compression and decompression.  Some browsers and user agents limit window sizes to mitigate memory usage concerns, thereby causing interoperability issues.  This document updates the window size limit in RFC 8878 from a recommendation to a requirement in HTTP contexts.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegN. Jaju¢kaffiliationtMeta Platforms, Inc.dnamelW. F. Handteddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9659eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-139450hkeywordsÜdzstdizstandardkcompressionpcontent encodingncontent codingpapplication/zstdkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefˆ›ûgranking%ªcrfcd9659irfcNumber%ªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Window Sizing for Zstandard Content EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMulti-Topology Routing (MTR) is a technology that enables service differentiation within an IP network.  The Flexible Algorithm (FA) is another mechanism for creating a sub-topology within a topology using defined topology constraints and computation algorithms.  In order to deploy Multipoint LDP (mLDP) in a network that supports MTR, FA, or other methods of signaling non-default IGP Algorithms (IPAs), mLDP is required to become topology and algorithm aware.  This document specifies extensions to mLDP to support the use of MTR/IPAs such that, when building multipoint Label Switched Paths (LSPs), the LSPs can follow a particular topology and algorithm.  This document updates RFC 7307 by allocating eight bits from a previously reserved field to be used as the "IPA" field.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Mishra¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationlEdward JonesdnamehA. Gulkoddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9658eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-140117hkeywordsÉdMPLSdmLDPnMulti-topologykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg Xgranking%∫crfcd9658irfcNumber%∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Multipoint LDP Extensions for Multi-Topology RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document introduces use cases where Time-Variant Routing (TVR) computations (i.e., routing computations that take into consideration time-based or scheduled changes to a network) could improve routing protocol convergence and/or network performance.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnameoE. Birrane, III¢kaffiliationsThales Alenia SpacednamegN. Kuhn¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationtAalyria TechnologiesdnameiR. Taylor¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehL. Zhangddatej÷Ûhfilenamegrfc9657eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctvrdfullxtvr - Time-Variant RoutingdnametTime-Variant Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-139626hkeywordsÖlTime-variantgRoutinghMobilityoGreen computingsResource managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg¨	granking%πcrfcd9657irfcNumber%πestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Time-Variant Routing (TVR) Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxtThis document defines a YANG data model to describe microwave and millimeter-wave radio links in a network topology.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlEricsson IncdnamelS. Mansfield¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamejJ. Ahlberg¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeM. Ye¢kaffiliationwNEC Laboratories EuropednameeX. Li¢kaffiliationjNokia - ITdnamelD. Spreaficoddatej◊␀hfilenamegrfc9656eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-139489hkeywordsÇimicrowavehtopologykobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDatef˚6granking%∏crfcd9656irfcNumber%∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A YANG Data Model for Microwave TopologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThe MPLS ping and traceroute mechanisms described in RFC 8029 and the related extensions for Segment Routing (SR) defined in RFC 8287 are highly valuable for validating control plane and data plane synchronization. In certain environments, only some intermediate or transit nodes may have been upgraded to support these validation procedures. A straightforward MPLS ping and traceroute mechanism allows traversal of any path without validation of the control plane state. RFC 8029 supports this mechanism with the Nil Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC). The procedures outlined in RFC 8029 are primarily applicable when the Nil FEC is used as an intermediate FEC in the FEC stack. However, challenges arise when all labels in the label stack are represented using the Nil FEC.

This document introduces a new Type-Length-Value (TLV) as an extension to the existing Nil FEC. It describes MPLS ping and traceroute procedures using the Nil FEC with this extension to address and overcome these challenges.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehD. Rathi¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehK. Arora¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiN. Nainarddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9655eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-141865hkeywordsÖcFECcOAMdOSPFeIS-ISfSPRINGkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateg=¶granking%∑crfcd9655irfcNumber%∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGEgress Validation in Label Switched Path Ping and Traceroute MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûRFC 8954 imposed size constraints on the optional Nonce extension for the Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP). OCSP is used to check the status of a certificate, and the Nonce extension is used to cryptographically bind an OCSP response message to a particular OCSP request message.

Some environments use cryptographic algorithms that generate a Nonce value that is longer than 32 octets. This document also modifies the "Nonce" section of RFC 6960 to clearly define and differentiate the encoding format and values for easier implementation and understanding. This document obsoletes RFC 8954, which includes updated ASN.1 modules for OCSP, and updates RFC 6960.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlNetskope IncdnameiH. Sharmaddatej–Ôhfilenamegrfc9654eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-139048hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefÕY‡granking%∂crfcd9654irfcNumber%∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) Nonce ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThe Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) uses a 32-bit checksum in the common header of each packet to provide some level of data integrity. If another method used by SCTP already provides the same or a higher level of data integrity, computing this checksum does not provide any additional protection but does consume computing resources.

This document provides a simple extension allowing SCTP to save these computing resources by using zero as the checksum in a backwards-compatible way. It also defines how this feature can be used when SCTP packets are encapsulated in Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) packets.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx M√ºnster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. T√ºxen¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiV. Boivie¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekF. Castelli¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamehR. Jesupddatej–Íhfilenamegrfc9653eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-139447hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefˆ’çgranking%µcrfcd9653irfcNumber%µestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Zero Checksum for the Stream Control Transmission ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≤This specification defines the Link-Template HTTP header field, providing a means for describing the structure of a link between two resources so that new links can be generated.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9652eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpapidfullx'httpapi - Building Blocks for HTTP APIsdnamexBuilding Blocks for HTTP APIsdtypebwgbidjdoc-139488hkeywordsÅmlink relationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef˚.granking%¥crfcd9652irfcNumber%¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#The Link-Template HTTP Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document describes a set of data types and associated algorithms that are intended to make it easier and safer to define and handle HTTP header and trailer fields, known as "Structured Fields", "Structured Headers", or "Structured Trailers". It is intended for use by specifications of new HTTP fields.

