e FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈThis document specifies a minimal YANG data model for TCP on devices that are configured and managed by network management protocols.  The YANG data model defines a container for all TCP connections and groupings of authentication parameters that can be imported and used in TCP implementations or by other models that need to configure TCP parameters.  The model also includes basic TCP statistics.  The model is compliant with Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) (RFC 8342).fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtHochschule EsslingendnameiM. Scharf¢kaffiliationnKloud ServicesdnameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationhF5, Inc.dnameiV. Murgaiddatej÷œhfilenamegrfc9648eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-139670hkeywordsÅdYANGkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategqògranking%∞crfcd9648irfcNumber%∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewYANG Data Model for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÑThis document defines a data model for the Babel routing protocol.  The data model is defined using the YANG data modeling language.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnKloud ServicesdnameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Starkddatej÷´hfilenamegrfc9647eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-139669hkeywordsÇebabeldYANGkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDategqògranking%Øcrfcd9647irfcNumber%ØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA YANG Data Model for BabeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis document extends the input to the "get-bootstrapping-data" RPC defined in RFC 8572 to include an optional certificate signing request (CSR), enabling a bootstrapping device to additionally obtain an identity certificate (e.g., a Local Device Identifier (LDevID) from IEEE 802.1AR) as part of the "onboarding information" response provided in the RPC-reply.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷ªhfilenamegrfc9646eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139668hkeywordsäizerotouchibootstrapdsztpcztpccsrgpkcs#10cp10ep10crccmcccmpkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDategqògranking%Æcrfcd9646irfcNumber%ÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnConveying a Certificate Signing Request (CSR) in a Secure Zero-Touch Provisioning (SZTP) Bootstrapping RequestdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document presents four YANG 1.1 modules -- three IETF modules and one supporting IANA module.

The three IETF modules are "ietf-tls-common", "ietf-tls-client", and "ietf-tls-server". The "ietf-tls-client" and "ietf-tls-server" modules are the primary productions of this work, supporting the configuration and monitoring of TLS clients and servers.

The IANA module is "iana-tls-cipher-suite-algs". This module defines YANG enumerations that provide support for an IANA-maintained algorithm registry.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej÷hfilenamegrfc9645eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139667hkeywordsÅdIETFkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDategqógranking%≠crfcd9645irfcNumber%≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.YANG Groupings for TLS Clients and TLS ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document presents three IETF-defined YANG modules and a script used to create four supporting IANA modules.

The three IETF modules are ietf-ssh-common, ietf-ssh-client, and ietf-ssh-server. The "ietf-ssh-client" and "ietf-ssh-server" modules are the primary productions of this work, supporting the configuration and monitoring of Secure Shell (SSH) clients and servers.

The four IANA modules are iana-ssh-encryption-algs, iana-ssh-key-exchange-algs, iana-ssh-mac-algs, and iana-ssh-public-key-algs. These modules each define YANG enumerations providing support for an IANA-maintained algorithm registry.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej–‹hfilenamegrfc9644eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139675hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateg{™granking%¨crfcd9644irfcNumber%¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.YANG Groupings for SSH Clients and SSH ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document presents three YANG 1.1 modules to support the configuration of TCP clients and TCP servers.  The modules include basic parameters of a TCP connection relevant for client or server applications, as well as client configuration required for traversing proxies.  The data models defined by these modules may be used directly (e.g., to define a specific TCP client or TCP server) or in conjunction with the configuration defined for higher level protocols that depend on TCP (e.g., SSH, TLS, etc.).  Examples of higher level protocol configuration designed to be used in conjunction with this configuration are in RFCs 9644 and 9645.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationtHochschule EsslingendnameiM. Scharfddatej÷˛hfilenamegrfc9643eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139674hkeywordsÅdIETFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg{™granking%´crfcd9643irfcNumber%´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.YANG Groupings for TCP Clients and TCP ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This document presents a YANG module called "ietf-keystore" that enables centralized configuration of both symmetric and asymmetric keys.  The secret value for both key types may be encrypted or hidden.  Asymmetric keys may be associated with certificates.  Notifications are sent when certificates are about to expire.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej÷Ûhfilenamegrfc9642eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139673hkeywordsÅdIETFkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateg{™granking%™crfcd9642irfcNumber%™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex A YANG Data Model for a KeystoredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx◊This document presents a YANG module for configuring bags of certificates and bags of public keys that can be referenced by other data models for trust.  Notifications are sent when certificates are about to expire.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej÷ˇhfilenamegrfc9641eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139672hkeywordsÅdIETFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategx†granking%©crfcd9641irfcNumber%©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"A YANG Data Model for a TruststoredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÜThis document presents a YANG 1.1 (RFC 7950) module defining identities, typedefs, and groupings useful to cryptographic applications.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej–·hfilenamegrfc9640eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139671hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDategxügranking%®crfcd9640irfcNumber%®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.YANG Data Types and Groupings for CryptographydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThis document defines the Free Lossless Audio Codec (FLAC) format and its streamable subset.  FLAC is designed to reduce the amount of computer storage space needed to store digital audio signals.  It does this losslessly, i.e., it does so without losing information.  FLAC is free in the sense that its specification is open and its reference implementation is open source.  Compared to other lossless audio coding formats, FLAC is a format with low complexity and can be encoded and decoded with little computing resources.  Decoding of FLAC has been implemented independently for many different platforms, and both encoding and decoding can be implemented without needing floating-point arithmetic.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamerM.Q.C. van Beurden¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Weaverddatej÷˚hfilenamegrfc9639eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcellardfullxHcellar - Codec Encoding for LossLess Archiving and Realtime transmissiondnamex?Codec Encoding for LossLess Archiving and Realtime transmissiondtypebwgbidjdoc-142381hkeywordsçdfreehlosslesseaudioecodecgencodergdecoderkcompressionjcompressorharchivalgarchiveiarchivingfbackupemusickobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDategcs+granking%ßcrfcd9639irfcNumber%ßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Free Lossless Audio Codec (FLAC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThe IETF Network Virtualization Overlays (NVO3) Working Group developed considerations for a common encapsulation that addresses various network virtualization overlay technical concerns. This document provides a record, for the benefit of the IETF community, of the considerations arrived at by the NVO3 Working Group starting from the output of the NVO3 encapsulation Design Team. These considerations may be helpful with future deliberations by working groups over the choice of encapsulation formats.

There are implications of having different encapsulations in real environments consisting of both software and hardware implementations and within and spanning multiple data centers. For example, Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) functions such as path MTU discovery become challenging with multiple encapsulations along the data path.

Based on these considerations, the NVO3 Working Group determined that Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation (Geneve) with a few modifications is the common encapsulation. This document provides more details, particularly in Section 7.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej÷ˇhfilenamegrfc9638eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-139424hkeywordsÖmEncapsulationcNVEfGenevecGUEiVXLAN-GPEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefÙX^granking%¶crfcd9638irfcNumber%¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGNetwork Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3) Encapsulation ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThe document describes the reservation of an additional IPv6 address prefix for use in documentation.  This update to RFC 3849 expands on the existing 2001:db8::/32 address block with the reservation of an additional, larger prefix.  The addition of a /20 prefix allows documented examples to more closely reflect a broader range of realistic, current deployment scenarios and more closely aligns with contemporary allocation models for large networks.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationwEnergy Sciences NetworkdnamekN. Buraglioddatej–˜hfilenamegrfc9637eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-139064hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefŒHÚgranking%•crfcd9637irfcNumber%•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Expanding the IPv6 Documentation SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the Time Zone Information Format (TZif) for representing and exchanging time zone information, independent of any particular service or protocol. Two media types for this format are also defined.

This document replaces and obsoletes RFC 8536.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Olson¢kaffiliationx$University of California Los AngelesdnameiP. Eggert¢kaffiliationoFastmail US LLCdnamelK. Murchisonddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9636eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-140250hkeywordsÉitime zoneftzdatadtzifkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateg#€¨granking%§crfcd9636irfcNumber%§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'The Time Zone Information Format (TZif)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@The Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocol (GNAP) defines a mechanism for delegating authorization to a piece of software and conveying the results and artifacts of that delegation to the software.  This delegation can include access to a set of APIs as well as subject information passed directly to the software.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsBespoke EngineeringdnameiJ. Richer¢kaffiliationhacert.iodnamejF. Imbaultddatej÷˛hfilenamegrfc9635eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgnapdfullx3gnap - Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocoldnamex,Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-139624hkeywordsÉnauthenticationjdelegationhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesªopublicationDateg°8granking%£crfcd9635irfcNumber%£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocol (GNAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¥This document discusses the use of existing IP Operations, Administration, and Maintenance protocols and mechanisms in Deterministic Networking networks that use the IP data plane.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamehD. Blackddatej÷˛hfilenamegrfc9634eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-140114hkeywordsÇfDetNetcOAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateg˚—granking%¢crfcd9634irfcNumber%¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnOperations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) for Deterministic Networking (DetNet) with the IP Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document contains the specification for the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) YANG data model for configuration and operational data for DetNet flows. The model allows the provisioning of an end-to-end DetNet service on devices along the path without depending on any signaling protocol. It also specifies operational status for flows.

The YANG module defined in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegX. Geng¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegY. Ryoo¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationgEquinixdnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Liddatej–ﬂhfilenamegrfc9633eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-140110hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages~opublicationDategÌPgranking%°crfcd9633irfcNumber%°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Deterministic Networking (DetNet) YANG Data ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document specifies how to augment the Routing Policy Specification Language (RPSL) inetnum: class to refer specifically to geofeed comma-separated values (CSV) data files and describes an optional scheme that uses the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) to authenticate the geofeed data files.  This document obsoletes RFC 9092.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuIIJ Research & ArrcusdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejM. Candela¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housleyddatej÷˙hfilenamegrfc9632eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-138680hkeywordsÑkgeolocationlgeo-locationdRPSLginetnumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef≥¯˛granking%†crfcd9632irfcNumber%†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFinding and Using Geofeed DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$This document describes an experiment in which two new IPv6 Routing headers are implemented and deployed. Collectively, they are called the Compact Routing Header (CRH). Individually, they are called CRH-16 and CRH-32.

One purpose of this experiment is to demonstrate that the CRH can be implemented and deployed in a production network. Another purpose is to demonstrate that the security considerations described in this document can be addressed with Access Control Lists (ACLs). Finally, this document encourages replication of the experiment.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnameiY. Kamite¢kaffiliationoAlston NetworksdnameiA. Alston¢kaffiliationnLiquid TelecomdnamelD. Henriques¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalilddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9631eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-139050hkeywordsÇdIPv6nRouting headerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefÕa¬granking%ücrfcd9631irfcNumber%üestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%The IPv6 Compact Routing Header (CRH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document specifies two solutions to meet the requirements of on-path telemetry for multicast traffic using IOAM.  While IOAM is advantageous for multicast traffic telemetry, some unique challenges are present.  This document provides the solutions based on the IOAM trace option and direct export option to support the telemetry data correlation and the multicast tree reconstruction without incurring data redundancy.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamegH. Song¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamejM. McBride¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationlVerizon Inc.dnameiG. Mishra¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameiH. Asaeda¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegT. Zhouddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9630eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-139057hkeywordsÇiMulticastiTelemetrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefŒgranking%ûcrfcd9630irfcNumber%ûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Multicast On-Path Telemetry Using In Situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèThe Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) supports key transport and key agreement algorithms.  In recent years, cryptographers have been specifying Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) algorithms, including quantum-secure KEM algorithms.  This document defines conventions for the use of KEM algorithms by the originator and recipients to encrypt and decrypt CMS content.  This document updates RFC 5652.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamegJ. Gray¢kaffiliationxPenguin Securities Pte. Ltd.dnamehT. Okuboddatej÷˝hfilenamegrfc9629eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-138679hkeywordsÇekemripKEMRecipientInfokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef≥¯˛granking%ùcrfcd9629irfcNumber%ùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Using Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) Algorithms in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification describes an RTP payload format for the VP9 video codec.  The payload format has wide applicability as it supports applications from low bitrate peer-to-peer usage to high bitrate video conferences.  It includes provisions for temporal and spatial scalability.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Holmer¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejM. Flodman¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamegD. Hong¢kaffiliationq8x8, Inc. / JitsidnameiJ. Lennoxddatej÷ùhfilenamegrfc9628eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-145248hkeywordsÉcRTPcVP9dWebMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÊ/Ngranking%úcrfcd9628irfcNumber%úestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex RTP Payload Format for VP9 VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo describes the RTCP Payload-Specific Feedback Message Layer Refresh Request (LRR), which can be used to request a state refresh of one or more substreams of a layered media stream.  This document also defines its use with several RTP payloads for scalable media formats.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationq8x8, Inc. / JitsidnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamegD. Hong¢kaffiliationfOpenAIdnameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Holmer¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejM. Flodmanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc9627eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-145247hkeywordsÅcRTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÊ/Mgranking%õcrfcd9627irfcNumber%õestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5The Layer Refresh Request (LRR) RTCP Feedback MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document describes a Video Frame Marking RTP header extension used to convey information about video frames that is critical for error recovery and packet forwarding in RTP middleboxes or network nodes.  It is most useful when media is encrypted and essential when the middlebox or node has no access to the media decryption keys.  It is also useful for codec-agnostic processing of encrypted or unencrypted media, while it also supports extensions for codec-specific information.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Zanaty¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiE. Berger¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Nandakumarddatej÷˝hfilenamegrfc9626eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-145246hkeywordsÖnScalable videoeH.264eH.265cVP9mlayered videokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÊ/Lgranking%öcrfcd9626irfcNumber%öestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Video Frame Marking RTP Header ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEthernet VPN (EVPN) provides a service that allows a single Local Area Network (LAN), comprising a single IP subnet, to be divided into multiple segments.  Each segment may be located at a different site, and the segments are interconnected by an IP or MPLS backbone.  Intra-subnet traffic (either unicast or multicast) always appears to the end users to be bridged, even when it is actually carried over the IP or MPLS backbone.  When a single tenant owns multiple such LANs, EVPN also allows IP unicast traffic to be routed between those LANs.  This document specifies new procedures that allow inter-subnet IP multicast traffic to be routed among the LANs of a given tenant while still making intra-subnet IP multicast traffic appear to be bridged.  These procedures can provide optimal routing of the inter-subnet multicast traffic and do not require any such traffic to egress a given router and then ingress that same router.  These procedures also accommodate IP multicast traffic that originates or is destined to be external to the EVPN domain.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamefW. Lin¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassiddatej÷ˇhfilenamegrfc9625eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-139121hkeywordsÑdOISMcPEGcMEGcSBDkobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDatef”u&granking%ôcrfcd9625irfcNumber%ôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7EVPN Optimized Inter-Subnet Multicast (OISM) ForwardingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxºThis document specifies protocols and procedures for forwarding Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, or Multicast (BUM) traffic of Ethernet VPNs (EVPNs) using Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER).fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej–⁄hfilenamegrfc9624eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139120hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef”m2granking%òcrfcd9624irfcNumber%òestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_EVPN Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, or Multicast (BUM) Using Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThe Transport Services System enables applications to use transport protocols flexibly for network communication and defines a protocol-independent Transport Services Application Programming Interface (API) that is based on an asynchronous, event-driven interaction pattern.  This document serves as a guide to implementing such a system.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnamelA. Brunstrom¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamekR. Enghardt¢kaffiliationfSAP SEdnamekP.S. Tiesel¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzlddatej–Èhfilenamegrfc9623eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-142711hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDategë|¯granking%ócrfcd9623irfcNumber%óestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Implementing Interfaces to Transport ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an abstract Application Programming Interface (API) to the transport layer that enables the selection of transport protocols and network paths dynamically at runtime.  This API enables faster deployment of new protocols and protocol features without requiring changes to the applications.  The specified API follows the Transport Services Architecture by providing asynchronous, atomic transmission of Messages.  It is intended to replace the BSD Socket API as the common interface to the transport layer, in an environment where endpoints could select from multiple network paths and potential transport protocols.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet Transportgauthorsà¢kaffiliationwGoogle Switzerland GmbHdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamekR. Enghardt¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamemC. S. Perkins¢kaffiliationfSAP SEdnamekP.S. Tiesel¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Paulyddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9622eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-142710hkeywordsÇnHappy EyeballssTransport SelectionkobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDategë|¯granking%ñcrfcd9622irfcNumber%ñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJAn Abstract Application Programming Interface (API) for Transport ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document describes an architecture that exposes transport protocol features to applications for network communication.  The Transport Services Application Programming Interface (API) is based on an asynchronous, event-driven interaction pattern.  This API uses Messages for representing data transfer to applications and describes how a Transport Services Implementation can use multiple IP addresses, multiple protocols, and multiple paths and can provide multiple application streams.  This document provides the architecture and requirements.  It defines common terminology and concepts to be used in definitions of a Transport Services API and a Transport Services Implementation.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationwGoogle Switzerland GmbHdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnamelA. Brunstrom¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamemC. S. Perkinsddatej–Ëhfilenamegrfc9621eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-142709hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategë|˜granking%ïcrfcd9621irfcNumber%ïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Architecture and Requirements for Transport ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyíThis document sets guidelines for human rights considerations for developers working on network protocols and architectures, similar to the work done on the guidelines for privacy considerations (RFC 6973). This is an updated version of the guidelines for human rights considerations in RFC 8280.

This document is a product of the Human Right Protocol Considerations (HRPC) Research Group in the IRTF.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiG. Grover¢kaffiliationwUniversity of AmsterdamdnamelN. ten Oeverddatej÷Ìhfilenamegrfc9620eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdhrpcdfullx+hrpc - Human Rights Protocol Considerationsdnamex$Human Rights Protocol Considerationsdtypebrgbidjdoc-139292hkeywordsÇxInternational human rightsoProtocol designkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef„Qfgranking%îcrfcd9620irfcNumber%îestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexDGuidelines for Human Rights Protocol and Architecture ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document updates RFC 1035 by constraining the allowed value of the QDCOUNT parameter in DNS messages with OPCODE = 0 (QUERY) to a maximum of one, and it specifies the required behavior when values that are not allowed are encountered.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiR. Bellis¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamehJ. Ableyddatej–ˆhfilenamegrfc9619eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-138061hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef°Døgranking%ìcrfcd9619irfcNumber%ìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$In the DNS, QDCOUNT Is (Usually) OnedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document updates RFC 5280 to replace the algorithm for X.509 policy validation with an equivalent, more efficient algorithm.  The original algorithm built a structure that scaled exponentially in the worst case, leaving implementations vulnerable to denial-of-service attacks.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Benjaminddatej–⁄hfilenamegrfc9618eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-139112hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef“Ogranking%ícrfcd9618irfcNumber%íestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Updates to X.509 Policy ValidationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòIn situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM) is an example of an on-path hybrid measurement method.  IOAM defines a method for producing operational and telemetry information that may be exported using the in-band or out-of-band method.  RFCs 9197 and 9326 discuss the data fields and associated data types for IOAM.  This document defines a YANG module for the configuration of IOAM functions.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegT. Zhou¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamekJ. Guichard¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Brockners¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. Raghavanddatej÷˚hfilenamegrfc9617eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-139056hkeywordsÇcOAMmConfigurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefÕ˙xgranking%ëcrfcd9617irfcNumber%ëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPA YANG Data Model for In Situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∆This document defines an extension to the Babel routing protocol that measures the round-trip time (RTT) between routers and makes it possible to prefer lower-latency links over higher-latency ones.fadNamesGunter Van de Veldedarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhENS LyondnamejB. Jonglez¢kaffiliationxIRIF Universit√© Paris Cit√©dnamemJ. Chroboczekddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9616eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-139304hkeywordsÜiTimestampeclockcNTPeMillseHellohFuzzballkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef‰èFgranking%êcrfcd9616irfcNumber%êestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Delay-Based Metric Extension for the Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document introduces an in-band method for DNS operators to publish arbitrary information about the zones for which they are authoritative, in an authenticated fashion and on a per-zone basis. The mechanism allows managed DNS operators to securely announce DNSSEC key parameters for zones under their management, including for zones that are not currently securely delegated.

Whenever DS records are absent for a zone's delegation, this signal enables the parent's registry or registrar to cryptographically validate the CDS/CDNSKEY records found at the child's apex. The parent can then provision DS records for the delegation without resorting to out-of-band validation or weaker types of cross-checks such as "Accept after Delay".

This document establishes the DS enrollment method described in Section 4 of this document as the preferred method over those from Section 3 of RFC 8078. It also updates RFC 7344.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx deSEC Secure Systems EngineeringdnamelP. Thomassen¢kaffiliationx$deSEC Technische Universit√§t BerlindnameiN. Wisiolddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9615eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-137109hkeywordsÖfDNSSECmbootstrappingbDScCDSgCDNSKEYkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefñˇgranking%ècrfcd9615irfcNumber%èestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSAutomatic DNSSEC Bootstrapping Using Authenticated Signals from the Zone's OperatordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyüThis document describes the principle of privacy partitioning, which selectively spreads data and communication across multiple parties as a means to improve privacy by separating user identity from user data.  This document describes emerging patterns in protocols to partition what data and metadata is revealed through protocol interactions, provides common terminology, and discusses how to analyze such models.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliation`dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. A. Woodddatej÷˘hfilenamegrfc9614eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-138579hkeywordsÑfMASQUElPrivacy PassnOblivious HTTPeProxykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef™ÜBgranking%écrfcd9614irfcNumber%éestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex+Partitioning as an Architecture for PrivacydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìThis document specifies requirements for the development of MPLS Network Actions (MNAs) that affect the forwarding or other processing of MPLS packets.  These requirements are informed by a number of proposals for additions to the MPLS information in the labeled packet to allow such actions to be performed, either by a transit or terminating Label Switching Router (i.e., the Label Edge Router - LER).fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Surrey ICSdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehJ. Drakeddatej–Òhfilenamegrfc9613eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-139049hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatefÕ]˛granking%çcrfcd9613irfcNumber%çestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCRequirements for Solutions that Support MPLS Network Actions (MNAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) is expected to be able to monitor a wide variety of encapsulations of paths between systems.  When a BFD session monitors an explicitly routed unidirectional path, there may be a need to direct the egress BFD peer to use a specific path for the reverse direction of the BFD session.  This document describes an extension to the MPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) echo request that allows a BFD system to request that the remote BFD peer transmit BFD control packets over the specified LSP.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationfNVIDIAdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemI. Varlashkin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9612eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-137954hkeywordsÇhLSP PingcBFDkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatef†UÆgranking%åcrfcd9612irfcNumber%åestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) Reverse Path for MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.In order to increase the bandwidth of IPsec traffic between peers, this document defines one Notify Message Status Types and one Notify Message Error Types payload for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) to support the negotiation of multiple Child Security Associations (SAs) with the same Traffic Selectors used on different resources, such as CPUs.

The SA_RESOURCE_INFO notification is used to convey information that the negotiated Child SA and subsequent new Child SAs with the same Traffic Selectors are a logical group of Child SAs where most or all of the Child SAs are bound to a specific resource, such as a specific CPU. The TS_MAX_QUEUE notify conveys that the peer is unwilling to create more additional Child SAs for this particular negotiated Traffic Selector combination.

Using multiple Child SAs with the same Traffic Selectors has the benefit that each resource holding the Child SA has its own Sequence Number Counter, ensuring that CPUs don't have to synchronize their cryptographic state or disable their packet replay protection.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgsecunetdnameiA. Antony¢kaffiliationhcodelabsdnamejT. Brunner¢kaffiliationgsecunetdnamekS. Klassert¢kaffiliationeAivendnamejP. Woutersddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9611eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-137266hkeywordsÇeIKEv2eIPseckobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatefö¢∞granking%ãcrfcd9611irfcNumber%ãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkInternet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Support for Per-Resource Child Security Associations (SAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÇThis document specifies a data model for synchronising contact data with a server using the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP).fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamejN. Jenkinsddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9610eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-142380hkeywordsÉdJMAPdJSONhcontactskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategclúgranking%äcrfcd9610irfcNumber%äestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) for ContactsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty)This document describes the queries that a DNS resolver should emit to initialize its cache. The result is that the resolver gets both a current NS resource record set (RRset) for the root zone and the necessary address information for reaching the root servers.

This document obsoletes RFC 8109.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhDENIC eGdnamegP. Koch¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Larson¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9609eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-143008hkeywordsÇkDNS cachingiroot zonekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateg´ñgranking%âcrfcd9609irfcNumber%âestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber—etotaletitlex0Initializing a DNS Resolver with Priming QueriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-X.509v3 public key certificates are profiled in RFC 5280.  Short-lived certificates are seeing greater use in the Internet.  The Certification Authority (CA) that issues these short-lived certificates do not publish revocation information because the certificate lifespan that is shorter than the time needed to detect, report, and distribute revocation information.  Some long-lived X.509v3 public key certificates never expire, and they are never revoked.  This specification defines the noRevAvail certificate extension so that a relying party can readily determine that the CA does not publish revocation information for the certificate, and it updates the certification path validation algorithm defined in RFC 5280 so that revocation checking is skipped when the noRevAvail certificate extension is present.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationhDigiCertdnamehT. Okubo¢kaffiliationlAKAYLA, Inc.dnameiJ. Mandelddatej–‰hfilenamegrfc9608eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-136706hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatef]granking%àcrfcd9608irfcNumber%àestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9No Revocation Available for X.509 Public Key CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document describes the RTP payload format of the Secure Communication Interoperability Protocol (SCIP).  SCIP is an application-layer protocol that provides end-to-end session establishment, payload encryption, packetization and de-packetization of media, and reliable transport.  This document provides a globally available reference that can be used for the development of network equipment and procurement of services that support SCIP traffic.  The intended audience is network security policymakers; network administrators, architects, and original equipment manufacturers (OEMs); procurement personnel; and government agency and commercial industry representatives.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx&General Dynamics Mission Systems, Inc.dnameiD. Hanson¢kaffiliationx&General Dynamics Mission Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Faller¢kaffiliationx&General Dynamics Mission Systems, Inc.dnamehK. Maverddatej–›hfilenamegrfc9607eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-138052hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef°:5granking%ácrfcd9607irfcNumber%áestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVRTP Payload Format for the Secure Communication Interoperability Protocol (SCIP) CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a method for DNS resolvers to publish information about themselves.  DNS clients can use the resolver information to identify the capabilities of DNS resolvers.  How DNS clients use such information is beyond the scope of this document.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9606eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcadddfullxadd - Adaptive DNS DiscoverydnamevAdaptive DNS Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-136705hkeywordsÉlTransparencyoUser ExperiencetDNS server selectionkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatef␀Ógranking%Ücrfcd9606irfcNumber%ÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNS Resolver InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document describes the Secure Frame (SFrame) end-to-end encryption and authentication mechanism for media frames in a multiparty conference call, in which central media servers (Selective Forwarding Units or SFUs) can access the media metadata needed to make forwarding decisions without having access to the actual media.

This mechanism differs from the Secure Real-Time Protocol (SRTP) in that it is independent of RTP (thus compatible with non-RTP media transport) and can be applied to whole media frames in order to be more bandwidth efficient.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehE. Omara¢kaffiliationhFixie.aidnameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationnCoSMo SoftwarednamemS. G. Murillo¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationeApplednameiY. Fabletddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9605eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsframedfullxsframe - Secure Media FramesdnamesSecure Media Framesdtypebwgbidjdoc-139071hkeywordsÉhsecurityxreal-time media encryptionuend-to-end encryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDatefŒWIgranking%Öcrfcd9605irfcNumber%ÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSecure Frame (SFrame): Lightweight Authenticated Encryption for Real-Time MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn order to provide greater scalability, network confidentiality, and service independence, Segment Routing (SR) utilizes a Binding Segment Identifier (BSID), as described in RFC 8402.  It is possible to associate a BSID to an RSVP-TE-signaled Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Path (LSP) or an SR TE path.  The BSID can be used by an upstream node for steering traffic into the appropriate TE path to enforce SR policies.  This document specifies the concept of binding value, which can be either an MPLS label or a Segment Identifier (SID).  It further specifies an extension to Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for reporting the binding value by a Path Computation Client (PCC) to the Path Computation Element (PCE) to support PCE-based TE policies.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeC. Liddatej–Ühfilenamegrfc9604eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-138779hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefø…4granking%Ñcrfcd9604irfcNumber%ÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Carrying Binding Label/SID in PCE-Based NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractyñSegment Routing (SR) can be used to steer packets through a network using the IPv6 or MPLS data plane, employing the source routing paradigm.

An SR Path can be derived from a variety of mechanisms, including an IGP Shortest Path Tree (SPT), explicit configuration, or a Path Computation Element (PCE).

Since SR can be applied to both MPLS and IPv6 data planes, a PCE should be able to compute an SR Path for both MPLS and IPv6 data planes. The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) extension and mechanisms to support SR-MPLS have been defined. This document outlines the necessary extensions to support SR for the IPv6 data plane within PCEP.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeC. Li¢kaffiliationkRtBrick IncdnamemP. Kaladharan¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelM. Koldychev¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefY. Zhuddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9603eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-138509hkeywordsÉdPCEPdSRv6dIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef®Ígranking%Écrfcd9603irfcNumber%ÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for IPv6 Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractyƒSegment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) uses IPv6 as the underlying data plane.  Thus, Segment Identifiers (SIDs) used by SRv6 can resemble IPv6 addresses and behave like them while exhibiting slightly different behaviors in some situations.  This document explores the characteristics of SRv6 SIDs and focuses on the relationship of SRv6 SIDs to the IPv6 Addressing Architecture.  This document allocates and makes a dedicated prefix available for SRv6 SIDs.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekS. Krishnanddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9602eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-139952hkeywordsÅqprefix allocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDategÙ+granking%Çcrfcd9602irfcNumber%ÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXSegment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) Segment Identifiers in the IPv6 Addressing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLRFC 6040 on "Tunnelling of Explicit Congestion Notification" made the rules for propagation of Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) consistent for all forms of IP-in-IP tunnel.  This specification updates RFC 6040 to clarify that its scope includes tunnels where two IP headers are separated by at least one shim header that is not sufficient on its own for wide-area packet forwarding.  It surveys widely deployed IP tunnelling protocols that use such shim headers and updates the specifications of those that do not mention ECN propagation (including RFCs 2661, 3931, 2784, 4380 and 7450, which specify L2TPv2, L2TPv3, Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE), Teredo, and Automatic Multicast Tunneling (AMT), respectively).  This specification also updates RFC 6040 with configuration requirements needed to make any legacy tunnel ingress safe.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejB. Briscoeddatej÷Ùhfilenamegrfc9601eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-139118hkeywordsàx!Congestion Control and ManagementwCongestion NotificationtInformation SecurityjTunnellingxEncapsulation & DecapsulationhProtocolcECNhLayeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef“ï@granking%Åcrfcd9601irfcNumber%ÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYPropagating Explicit Congestion Notification across IP Tunnel Headers Separated by a ShimdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂExplicit Congestion Notification (ECN) allows a forwarding element to notify downstream devices, including the destination, of the onset of congestion without having to drop packets.  This can improve network efficiency through better congestion control without packet drops.  This document extends ECN to TRansparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) switches, including integration with IP ECN, and provides for ECN marking in the TRILL header extension flags word (RFC 7179).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejB. Briscoeddatej÷!hfilenamegrfc9600eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-139117hkeywordsÉjCongestioncL4SgRBridgekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef“ï@granking%Äcrfcd9600irfcNumber%ÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdTRansparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyAThe purpose of this document is to guide the design of congestion notification in any lower-layer or tunnelling protocol that encapsulates IP.  The aim is for explicit congestion signals to propagate consistently from lower-layer protocols into IP.  Then, the IP internetwork layer can act as a portability layer to carry congestion notification from non-IP-aware congested nodes up to the transport layer (L4).  Specifications that follow these guidelines, whether produced by the IETF or other standards bodies, should assure interworking among IP-layer and lower-layer congestion notification mechanisms.  This document is included in BCP 89 and updates the single paragraph of advice to subnetwork designers about Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) in Section 13 of RFC 3819 by replacing it with a reference to this document.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameqJ. Kaippallimalilddatej÷ıhfilenamegrfc9599eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-139116hkeywordsâx!Congestion Control and ManagementwCongestion NotificationtInformation SecurityjTunnellingmEncapsulationmDecapsulationhProtocolcECNhLayeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef“ë–granking%crfcd9599irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberYetotaletitlexNGuidelines for Adding Congestion Notification to Protocols that Encapsulate IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document defines a new name form for inclusion in the otherName field of an X.509 Subject Alternative Name and Issuer Alternative Name extension that allows a certificate subject to be associated with an internationalized email address.

This document updates RFC 5280 and obsoletes RFC 8398.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiW. Chuang¢kaffiliationhDigiCertdnamejC. Bonnellddatej÷˙hfilenamegrfc9598eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-136204hkeywordsÉcEAIdPKIXmemail addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefO≤¸granking%~crfcd9598irfcNumber%~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Internationalized Email Addresses in X.509 CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This document describes how to include CBOR Web Token (CWT) claims in the header parameters of any CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) structure.  This functionality helps to facilitate applications that wish to make use of CWT claims in encrypted COSE structures and/or COSE structures featuring detached signatures, while having some of those claims be available before decryption and/or without inspecting the detached payload.  Another use case is using CWT claims with payloads that are not CWT Claims Sets, including payloads that are not CBOR at all.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeMattrdnameiT. Looker¢kaffiliationvSelf-Issued ConsultingdnamejM.B. Jonesddatej÷¯hfilenamegrfc9597eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-136630hkeywordsÇdCOSEdJOSEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefzägranking%}crfcd9597irfcNumber%}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+CBOR Web Token (CWT) Claims in COSE HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶This specification adds the equivalent of the JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE) "typ" (type) header parameter to CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE).  This enables the benefits of explicit typing (as defined in RFC 8725, "JSON Web Token Best Current Practices") to be brought to COSE objects.  The syntax of the COSE type header parameter value is the same as the existing COSE content type header parameter.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvSelf-Issued ConsultingdnamejM.B. Jones¢kaffiliationiTransmutednameiO. Steeleddatej÷¸hfilenamegrfc9596eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-136467hkeywordsÅoExplicit TypingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefh∆Sgranking%|crfcd9596irfcNumber%|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGCBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) "typ" (type) Header ParameterdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYANG Schema Item iDentifiers (YANG SIDs) are globally unique 63-bit unsigned integers used to identify YANG items.  SIDs provide a more compact method for identifying those YANG items that can be used efficiently, notably in constrained environments (RFC 7228).  This document defines the semantics, registration processes, and assignment processes for YANG SIDs for IETF-managed YANG modules.  To enable the implementation of these processes, this document also defines a file format used to persist and publish assigned YANG SIDs.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwTrilliant Networks Inc.dnamelM. Veillette¢kaffiliationnIMT AtlantiquednamehA. Pelov¢kaffiliationwGoogle Switzerland GmbHdnameiI. Petrov¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardsonddatej÷¯hfilenamegrfc9595eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-138585hkeywordsÅdCBORkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDatef™æägranking%{crfcd9595irfcNumber%{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&YANG Schema Item iDentifier (YANG SID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂This document defines how to use the Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) framework to distribute keying material and configuration parameters for secure group communication.  Candidate group members that act as Clients and are authorized to join a group can do so by interacting with a Key Distribution Center (KDC) acting as the Resource Server, from which they obtain the keying material to communicate with other group members.  While defining general message formats as well as the interface and operations available at the KDC, this document supports different approaches and protocols for secure group communication.  Therefore, details are delegated to separate application profiles of this document as specialized instances that target a particular group communication approach and define how communications in the group are protected.  Compliance requirements for such application profiles are also specified.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelF. Palombini¢kaffiliationgRISE ABdnameiM. Tilocaddatej÷˜hfilenamegrfc9594eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-139307hkeywordsÑnKey ManagementnAccess Controlx'Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)xSecure group communicationkobsoletedByÄepagesaopublicationDatef‰‡granking%zcrfcd9594irfcNumber%zestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrKey Provisioning for Group Communication Using Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis specification defines a mechanism that allows implementations of the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) to indicate the list of supported authentication methods to their peers while establishing IKEv2 Security Associations (SAs).  This mechanism improves interoperability when IKEv2 partners are configured with multiple credentials of different types for authenticating each other.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9593eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-137865hkeywordsÑisignatureqdigital signaturekcredentialsuintermediate exchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefüÏ4granking%ycrfcd9593irfcNumber%yestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcAnnouncing Supported Authentication Methods in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document retires and obsoletes the Tao of the IETF as an IETF-maintained document.  This document also obsoletes RFC 6722, which describes the publication process of the Tao.  Furthermore, this document describes the rationale for the retirement of the Tao.  For archival purposes, the last version of the Tao is included in the appendix.  Information that new participants need to engage in the work of the IETF will continue to be provided through the IETF website in a more timely and accessible manner.  This is the way.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of AmsterdamdnamelN. ten Oever¢kaffiliationwIETF Administration LLCdnamegG. Woodddatej÷˝hfilenamegrfc9592eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-136608hkeywordsÖcTaogarchivegretiredhinactivehtutorialkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDatefu∏¢granking%xcrfcd9592irfcNumber%xestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRetiring the Tao of the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty=This document specifies the Flexible Round-Optimized Schnorr Threshold (FROST) signing protocol.  FROST signatures can be issued after a threshold number of entities cooperate to compute a signature, allowing for improved distribution of trust and redundancy with respect to a secret key.  FROST depends only on a prime-order group and cryptographic hash function.  This document specifies a number of ciphersuites to instantiate FROST using different prime-order groups and hash functions.  This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpZcash FoundationdnamekD. Connolly¢kaffiliationx'University of Waterloo Zcash FoundationdnamehC. Komlo¢kaffiliationvUniversity of WaterloodnamekI. Goldberg¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej÷ˆhfilenamegrfc9591eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-136707hkeywordsÖsThreshold signaturevthreshold cryptographynsecret sharingoEdDSA signatureqSchnorr signaturekobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDatef$@granking%wcrfcd9591irfcNumber%westateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex`The Flexible Round-Optimized Schnorr Threshold (FROST) Protocol for Two-Round Schnorr SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÂThis document defines an extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) LIST command that allows the client to request mailbox annotations (metadata), along with other information typically returned by the LIST command.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamelK. Murchison¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekB. Gondwanaddatej÷¸hfilenamegrfc9590eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-136323hkeywordsÉeIMAP4dLISThMETADATAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefZ1›granking%vcrfcd9590irfcNumber%vestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=IMAP Extension for Returning Mailbox METADATA in Extended LISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,In the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), Signed Objects are defined as Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) protected content types.  A Signed Object contains a signing-time attribute, representing the purported time at which the object was signed by its issuer.  RPKI repositories are accessible using the rsync and RPKI Repository Delta protocols, allowing Relying Parties (RPs) to synchronize a local copy of the RPKI repository used for validation with the remote repositories.  This document describes how the CMS signing-time attribute can be used to avoid needless retransfers of data when switching between different synchronization protocols.  This document updates RFC 6488 by mandating the presence of the CMS signing-time attribute and disallowing the use of the binary-signing-time attribute.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamekT. Harrisonddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9589eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-136207hkeywordsÇcCMSlsigning-timekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatefO√ygranking%ucrfcd9589irfcNumber%uestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexáOn the Use of the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Signing-Time Attribute in Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Signed ObjectsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÂThis document defines a new pre-authentication mechanism for the Kerberos protocol.  The mechanism uses a password-authenticated key exchange (PAKE) to prevent brute-force password attacks, and it may incorporate a second factor.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamekN. McCallum¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamehS. Sorce¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamejR. Harwood¢kaffiliationcMITdnameiG. Hudsonddatej–€hfilenamegrfc9588eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139061hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatefŒChgranking%tcrfcd9588irfcNumber%testateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNKerberos Simple Password-Authenticated Key Exchange (SPAKE) Pre-authenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This document defines a YANG data model augmenting the IETF OSPF YANG data model (RFC 9129) to provide support for OSPFv3 Link State Advertisement (LSA) Extensibility as defined in RFC 8362.  OSPFv3 Extended LSAs provide extensible TLV-based LSAs for the base LSA types defined in RFC 5340.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiS. Palani¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameeY. Quddatej–€hfilenamegrfc9587eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-136422hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefcígranking%scrfcd9587irfcNumber%sestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDYANG Data Model for OSPFv3 Extended Link State Advertisements (LSAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThe UIDONLY extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol (RFCs 3501 and 9051) allows clients to enable a mode in which information about mailbox changes is returned using only Unique Identifiers (UIDs). Message numbers are not returned in responses and cannot be used in requests once this extension is enabled. This helps both clients and servers to reduce resource usage required to maintain a map between message numbers and UIDs.

This document defines an experimental IMAP extension.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnamenA. P. Achuthan¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnamenV. Nagulakonda¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnamehA. Singh¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Alvesddatej÷˘hfilenamegrfc9586eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-136309hkeywordsÑdIMAPgUIDONLYiIMAP4rev1iIMAP4rev2kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatefXÆXgranking%rcrfcd9586irfcNumber%restateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEIMAP Extension for Using and Returning Unique Identifiers (UIDs) OnlydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxöThis document defines a new IMAP untagged response code, "INPROGRESS", that provides progress notifications regarding the status of long-running commands.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoOpen-Xchange OydnamejM. Bettiniddatej◊hfilenamegrfc9585eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-136319hkeywordsÇdIMAPmresponse codekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefZ‹granking%qcrfcd9585irfcNumber%qestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5IMAP Response Code for Command Progress NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an RTP payload format for the Essential Video Coding (EVC) standard, published as ISO/IEC International Standard 23094-1.  EVC was developed by the MPEG.  The RTP payload format allows for the packetization of one or more Network Abstraction Layer (NAL) units in each RTP packet payload and the fragmentation of a NAL unit into multiple RTP packets.  The payload format has broad applicability in videoconferencing, Internet video streaming, and high-bitrate entertainment-quality video, among other applications.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamegS. Zhao¢kaffiliationgTencentdnameiS. Wenger¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamefY. Limddatej÷˙hfilenamegrfc9584eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-136470hkeywordsÉcEVCoISO/IEC 23094-1vEssential Video CodingkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDatefi‚granking%pcrfcd9584irfcNumber%pestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3RTP Payload Format for Essential Video Coding (EVC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyRThe Quantum Internet has the potential to improve application functionality by incorporating quantum information technology into the infrastructure of the overall Internet.  This document provides an overview of some applications expected to be used on the Quantum Internet and categorizes them.  Some general requirements for the Quantum Internet are also discussed.  The intent of this document is to describe a framework for applications and to describe a few selected application scenarios for the Quantum Internet.  This document is a product of the Quantum Internet Research Group (QIRG).gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnamegC. Wang¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Rahman¢kaffiliationsKanazawa UniversitydnameeR. Li¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejM. Aelmans¢kaffiliationxThe University of EdinburghdnamenK. Chakrabortyddatej÷˜hfilenamegrfc9583eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdqirgdfullx&qirg - Quantum Internet Research GroupdnamexQuantum Internet Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-136610hkeywordsÖxQuantum Key DistributionwBlind Quantum ComputingxDistributed Quantum ComputingxEntanglement DistributionxQuantum Internet RequirementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefu 4granking%ocrfcd9583irfcNumber%oestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex.Application Scenarios for the Quantum InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document defines a standard profile for Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs).  A ROA is a digitally signed object that provides a means of verifying that an IP address block holder has authorized an Autonomous System (AS) to originate routes to one or more prefixes within the address block.  This document obsoletes RFC 6482.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationjWorkonlinednamekB. Maddison¢kaffiliationpCarleton CollegednamekM. Lepinski¢kaffiliationhRaytheondnamegD. Kong¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegS. Kentddatej÷ˆhfilenamegrfc9582eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-136202hkeywordsÉdRPKIpRouting SecuritycBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefO¶qgranking%ncrfcd9582irfcNumber%nestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0A Profile for Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚The Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR, RFC 8949) is a data format whose design goals include the possibility of extremely small code size, fairly small message size, and extensibility without the need for version negotiation.

In CBOR, one point of extensibility is the definition of CBOR tags. RFC 8949 defines two tags for time: CBOR tag 0 (RFC 3339 time as a string) and tag 1 (POSIX time as int or float). Since then, additional requirements have become known. The present document defines a CBOR tag for time that allows a more elaborate representation of time, as well as related CBOR tags for duration and time period. This document is intended as the reference document for the IANA registration of the CBOR tags defined.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationjWell-TypeddnameiB. Gamari¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer SITdnamekH. Birkholzddatej÷Ôhfilenamegrfc9581eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-138778hkeywordsãmPoint in timehDurationnPeriod in timenClock accuracyiTimescalecTAIcUTCcNTPuNetwork Time ProtocolcPTPwPrecision Time ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateføπ÷granking%mcrfcd9581irfcNumber%mestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOConcise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) Tags for Time, Duration, and PerioddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document specifies the message formats used in OpenPGP. OpenPGP provides encryption with public key or symmetric cryptographic algorithms, digital signatures, compression, and key management.

This document is maintained in order to publish all necessary information needed to develop interoperable applications based on the OpenPGP format. It is not a step-by-step cookbook for writing an application. It describes only the format and methods needed to read, check, generate, and write conforming packets crossing any network. It does not deal with storage and implementation questions. It does, however, discuss implementation issues necessary to avoid security flaws.

This document obsoletes RFCs 4880 ("OpenPGP Message Format"), 5581 ("The Camellia Cipher in OpenPGP"), and 6637 ("Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) in OpenPGP").fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeAivendnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationiProton AGdnamejD. Huigens¢kaffiliationkSequoia PGPdnameiJ. Winter¢kaffiliationdFSIJdnamehY. Niibeddatej÷˜hfilenamegrfc9580eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgopenpgpdfullx4openpgp - Open Specification for Pretty Good Privacydnamex*Open Specification for Pretty Good Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-138586hkeywordsÑjencryptionrdigital signaturesnkey managementcpgpkobsoletedByÄepages¶opublicationDatef™¬granking%lcrfcd9580irfcNumber%lestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlegOpenPGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis document specifies additions and amendments to RFCs 7292 and 8018.  It defines a way to use the Password-Based Message Authentication Code 1 (PBMAC1), defined in RFC 8018, inside the PKCS #12 syntax.  The purpose of this specification is to permit the use of more modern Password-Based Key Derivation Functions (PBKDFs) and allow for regulatory compliance.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamehH. Karioddatej÷˚hfilenamegrfc9579eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-136318hkeywordsÉfpbmac1fpkcs12fpbkdf2kobsoletedByÅd9879epagesopublicationDatefZÏgranking%kcrfcd9579irfcNumber%kestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOUse of Password-Based Message Authentication Code 1 (PBMAC1) in PKCS #12 SyntaxdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬This document specifies two variants of the two-message issuance protocol for Privacy Pass tokens: one that produces tokens that are privately verifiable using the Issuer Private Key and one that produces tokens that are publicly verifiable using the Issuer Public Key.  Instances of "issuance protocol" and "issuance protocols" in the text of this document are used interchangeably to refer to the two variants of the Privacy Pass issuance protocol.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnBrave SoftwarednamegS. Celi¢kaffiliationnBrave SoftwarednamekA. Davidson¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnameiS. Valdez¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej–Õhfilenamegrfc9578eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymkprivacypassdfullxprivacypass - Privacy PassdnamelPrivacy Passdtypebwgbidjdoc-136512hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDatefkvgranking%jcrfcd9578irfcNumber%jestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPrivacy Pass Issuance ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This document defines an HTTP authentication scheme for Privacy Pass, a privacy-preserving authentication mechanism used for authorization.  The authentication scheme specified in this document can be used by Clients to redeem Privacy Pass tokens with an Origin.  It can also be used by Origins to challenge Clients to present Privacy Pass tokens.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnameiS. Valdez¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej÷Ûhfilenamegrfc9577eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymkprivacypassdfullxprivacypass - Privacy PassdnamelPrivacy Passdtypebwgbidjdoc-136511hkeywordsÉianonymousmauthorizationfcryptokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefkv~granking%icrfcd9577irfcNumber%iestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+The Privacy Pass HTTP Authentication SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆThis document specifies the Privacy Pass architecture and requirements for its constituent protocols used for authorization based on privacy-preserving authentication mechanisms.  It describes the conceptual model of Privacy Pass and its protocols, its security and privacy goals, practical deployment models, and recommendations for each deployment model, to help ensure that the desired security and privacy goals are fulfilled.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx'NOVA LINCS, Universidade NOVA de LisboadnamekA. Davidson¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamejJ. Iyengar¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej–“hfilenamegrfc9576eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymkprivacypassdfullxprivacypass - Privacy PassdnamelPrivacy Passdtypebwgbidjdoc-136510hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefkv~granking%hcrfcd9576irfcNumber%hestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Privacy Pass ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$The Drone Remote Identification Protocol (DRIP), plus trust policies and periodic access to registries, augments Unmanned Aircraft System (UAS) Remote Identification (RID), enabling local real-time assessment of trustworthiness of received RID messages and observed UAS, even by Observers lacking Internet access.  This document defines DRIP message types and formats to be sent in Broadcast RID Authentication Messages to verify that attached and recently detached messages were signed by the registered owner of the DRIP Entity Tag (DET) claimed.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrAX Enterprize, LLCdnameoA. Wiethuechter¢kaffiliationrAX Enterprize, LLCdnamegS. Card¢kaffiliationnHTT ConsultingdnamelR. Moskowitzddatej÷˙hfilenamegrfc9575eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddripdfullxdrip - Drone Remote ID ProtocoldnamexDrone Remote ID Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-136677hkeywordsÖedronecUASmBroadcast RIDx!trustworthy remote identificationmanti-spoofingkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDatef|cñgranking%gcrfcd9575irfcNumber%gestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdDRIP Entity Tag (DET) Authentication Formats and Protocols for Broadcast Remote Identification (RID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNetwork Virtualization Overlay (NVO) networks using Ethernet VPNs (EVPNs) as their control plane may use trees based on ingress replication or Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) to convey the overlay Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, or Multicast (BUM) traffic.  PIM provides an efficient solution that prevents sending multiple copies of the same packet over the same physical link; however, it may not always be deployed in the NVO network core.  Ingress replication avoids the dependency on PIM in the NVO network core.  While ingress replication provides a simple multicast transport, some NVO networks with demanding multicast applications require a more efficient solution without PIM in the core.  This document describes a solution to optimize the efficiency of ingress replication trees.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. Sathappan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefW. Lin¢kaffiliationnVersa NetworksdnamejM. Katiyar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassiddatej÷¥hfilenamegrfc9574eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-136224hkeywordsátAssisted ReplicationbARmAR-ReplicatordRNVEqPruned Flood ListcPFLtPruned Flooding ListkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefP–|granking%fcrfcd9574irfcNumber%festateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Optimized Ingress Replication Solution for Ethernet VPNs (EVPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThe Multicast VPN (MVPN) specifications allow a single Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) tunnel to carry traffic of multiple IP VPNs (referred to as VPNs in this document).  The EVPN specifications allow a single P2MP tunnel to carry traffic of multiple Broadcast Domains (BDs).  These features require the ingress router of the P2MP tunnel to allocate an upstream-assigned MPLS label for each VPN or for each BD.  A packet sent on a P2MP tunnel then carries the label that is mapped to its VPN or BD (in some cases, a distinct upstream-assigned label is needed for each flow.) Since each ingress router allocates labels independently, with no coordination among the ingress routers, the egress routers may need to keep track of a large number of labels.  The number of labels may need to be as large as, or larger than, the product of the number of ingress routers times the number of VPNs or BDs.  However, the number of labels can be greatly reduced if the association between a label and a VPN or BD is made by provisioning, so that all ingress routers assign the same label to a particular VPN or BD.  New procedures are needed in order to take advantage of such provisioned labels.  These new procedures also apply to Multipoint-to-Multipoint (MP2MP) tunnels.  This document updates RFCs 6514, 7432, and 7582 by specifying the necessary procedures.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefW. Lin¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamelIJ. Wijnandsddatej÷hfilenamegrfc9573eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-136223hkeywordsÇcDCBrTunnel AggregationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefP–|granking%ecrfcd9573irfcNumber%eestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/MVPN/EVPN Tunnel Aggregation with Common LabelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÌThis document specifies updated procedures for handling Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, or Multicast (BUM) traffic in Ethernet VPNs (EVPNs), including selective multicast and segmentation of provider tunnels.  This document updates RFC 7432.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefW. Lin¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassiddatej÷±hfilenamegrfc9572eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-136222hkeywordsÖqper-region I-PMSIxselective multicast forwardingstunnel segmentationuinter-AS segmentationxinter-region segmentationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefP–{granking%dcrfcd9572irfcNumber%destateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIUpdates to EVPN Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, or Multicast (BUM) ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySRFC 6374 describes methods of making loss and delay measurements on Label Switched Paths (LSPs) primarily as they are used in MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) networks.  This document describes a method of extending the performance measurements (specified in RFC 6374) from flows carried over MPLS-TP to flows carried over generic MPLS LSPs.  In particular, it extends the technique to allow loss and delay measurements to be made on multipoint-to-point LSPs and introduces some additional techniques to allow more sophisticated measurements to be made in both MPLS-TP and generic MPLS networks.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of SurreydnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamekG. Fioccola¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirskyddatej–Rhfilenamegrfc9571eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-136296hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefWùsgranking%ccrfcd9571irfcNumber%cestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPExtension of RFC 6374-Based Performance Measurement Using Synonymous Flow LabelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿThe MPLS echo request and MPLS echo response messages, defined in RFC 8029, "Detecting Multiprotocol Label Switched (MPLS) Data-Plane Failures" (usually referred to as LSP ping), are encapsulated in IP packets with headers that include a Router Alert Option (RAO). In actual deployments, the RAO was neither required nor used. Furthermore, RFC 6398 identifies security vulnerabilities associated with the RAO in non-controlled environments, e.g., the case of using the MPLS echo request/reply as inter-area Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM), and recommends against its use outside of controlled environments.

Therefore, this document retires the RAO for MPLS OAM and updates RFC 8029 to remove the RAO from LSP ping message encapsulations. Furthermore, this document explains why RFC 7506 has been reclassified as Historic.

Also, this document recommends the use of an IPv6 loopback address (::1/128) as the IPv6 destination address for an MPLS echo request message.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirskyddatej◊␀hfilenamegrfc9570eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-136172hkeywordsÇhLSP pinglrouter alertkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatefNågranking%bcrfcd9570irfcNumber%bestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Deprecating the Use of Router Alert in LSP PingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄ"Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Protocol" (RFC 7285) leverages HTTP/1.1 and is designed for the simple, sequential request-reply use case, in which an ALTO client requests a sequence of information resources and the server responds with the complete content of each resource, one at a time.

RFC 8895, which describes ALTO incremental updates using Server-Sent Events (SSE), defines a multiplexing protocol on top of HTTP/1.x, so that an ALTO server can incrementally push resource updates to clients whenever monitored network information resources change, allowing the clients to monitor multiple resources at the same time. However, HTTP/2 and later versions already support concurrent, non-blocking transport of multiple streams in the same HTTP connection.

To take advantage of newer HTTP features, this document introduces the ALTO Transport Information Publication Service (TIPS). TIPS uses an incremental RESTful design to give an ALTO client the new capability to explicitly and concurrently (in a non-blocking manner) request (or pull) specific incremental updates using HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, while still functioning for HTTP/1.1.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrSichuan UniversitydnamefK. Gao¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Schott¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamejY. R. Yang¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamekL. Delwiche¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnameiL. Kellerddatej÷˜hfilenamegrfc9569eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-139268hkeywordsÅrincremental updatekobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDatef·*Ègranking%acrfcd9569irfcNumber%aestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbThe Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Transport Information Publication Service (TIPS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines version 3 of the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) for IPv4 and IPv6.  It obsoletes RFC 5798, which previously specified VRRP (version 3).  RFC 5798 obsoleted RFC 3768, which specified VRRP (version 2) for IPv4.  VRRP specifies an election protocol that dynamically assigns responsibility for a Virtual Router to one of the VRRP Routers on a LAN.  The VRRP Router controlling the IPv4 or IPv6 address(es) associated with a Virtual Router is called the Active Router, and it forwards packets routed to these IPv4 or IPv6 addresses.  Active Routers are configured with virtual IPv4 or IPv6 addresses, and Backup Routers infer the address family of the virtual addresses being advertised based on the IP protocol version.  Within a VRRP Router, the Virtual Routers in each of the IPv4 and IPv6 address families are independent of one another and always treated as separate Virtual Router instances.  The election process provides dynamic failover in the forwarding responsibility should the Active Router become unavailable.  For IPv4, the advantage gained from using VRRP is a higher-availability default path without requiring configuration of dynamic routing or router discovery protocols on every end-host.  For IPv6, the advantage gained from using VRRP for IPv6 is a quicker switchover to Backup Routers than can be obtained with standard IPv6 Neighbor Discovery mechanisms.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Dograddatej–◊hfilenamegrfc9568eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-135915hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDatef1∆granking%`crfcd9568irfcNumber%`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEVirtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) Version 3 for IPv4 and IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDNS error reporting is a lightweight reporting mechanism that provides the operator of an authoritative server with reports on DNS resource records that fail to resolve or validate. A domain owner or DNS hosting organization can use these reports to improve domain hosting. The reports are based on extended DNS errors as described in RFC 8914.

When a domain name fails to resolve or validate due to a misconfiguration or an attack, the operator of the authoritative server may be unaware of this. To mitigate this lack of feedback, this document describes a method for a validating resolver to automatically signal an error to a monitoring agent specified by the authoritative server. The error is encoded in the QNAME; thus, the very act of sending the query is to report the error.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiR. Arends¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Larsonddatej÷˝hfilenamegrfc9567eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135858hkeywordsÑjmonitoringeagentgRFC8914d8914kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef+˝ıgranking%_crfcd9567irfcNumber%_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesDNS Error ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how the DetNet IP data plane can support the Packet Replication, Elimination, and Ordering Functions (PREOF) built on the existing MPLS PREOF solution defined for the DetNet MPLS data plane and the mechanisms defined by MPLS-over-UDP technology.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationpMalis ConsultingdnamehA. Malisddatej÷˚hfilenamegrfc9566eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-135854hkeywordsÖmIP Data PlaneqService sub-layerkReplicationkEliminationhOrderingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatef+ÁXgranking%^crfcd9566irfcNumber%^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvDeterministic Networking (DetNet) Packet Replication, Elimination, and Ordering Functions (PREOF) via MPLS over UDP/IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 7125 revised the tcpControlBits IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Element that was originally defined in RFC 5102 to reflect changes to the TCP header control bits since RFC 793. However, that update is still problematic for interoperability because some flag values have subsequently been deprecated.

This document removes stale information from the IANA "IPFIX Information Elements" registry and avoids future conflicts with the authoritative IANA "TCP Header Flags" registry.

This document obsoletes RFC 7125.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷Èhfilenamegrfc9565eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-134088hkeywordsÖeIPFIXcTCPkMeasurementfExportmObservabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÔ‡çgranking%]crfcd9565irfcNumber%]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVAn Update to the tcpControlBits IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information ElementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyøThe recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) such as large language models enable the design of the Faster than LIght speed Protocol (FLIP) for Internet.  FLIP provides a way to avoid congestion, enhance security, and deliver faster packets on the Internet by using AI to predict future packets at the receiving peer before they arrive.  This document describes the protocol, its various encapsulations, and some operational considerations.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej◊␀hfilenamegrfc9564eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-135424hkeywordsÇcLLMbIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef
