This document is an Independent Submission to the RFC series and does not have consensus of the IETF community.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamehC. Zhang¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefY. Liu¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegF. Leng¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnameeZ. Heddatej–⁄hfilenamegrfc9563eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-142235hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDategP˚granking%[crfcd9563irfcNumber%[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex*SM2 Digital Signature Algorithm for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€This specification defines UUIDs (Universally Unique IDentifiers) --
also known as GUIDs (Globally Unique IDentifiers) -- and a Uniform
Resource Name namespace for UUIDs. A UUID is 128 bits long and is
intended to guarantee uniqueness across space and time. UUIDs were
originally used in the Apollo Network Computing System (NCS), later
in the Open Software Foundation's (OSF's) Distributed Computing
Environment (DCE), and then in Microsoft Windows platforms.

This specification is derived from the OSF DCE specification with the
kind permission of the OSF (now known as "The Open Group"). Information from earlier versions of the OSF DCE specification have
been incorporated into this document. This document obsoletes RFC
4122.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Davis¢kaffiliationgUnclouddnamejB. Peabody¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamehP. Leachddatej÷Úhfilenamegrfc9562eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymguuidrevdfullx:uuidrev - Revise Universally Unique Identifier Definitionsdnamex0Revise Universally Unique Identifier Definitionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135990hkeywordsÅduuidkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDatef:xpgranking%Zcrfcd9562irfcNumber%ZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Universally Unique IDentifiers (UUIDs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx’This document specifies how to use the Parallel Network File System (pNFS) Small Computer System Interface (SCSI) Layout Type to access storage devices using the Non-Volatile Memory Express (NVMe) protocol family.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Hellwig¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamehC. Lever¢kaffiliationnOpendrives.comdnamejS. Faibish¢kaffiliationqDell TechnologiesdnamehD. Blackddatej÷Ïhfilenamegrfc9561eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-135462hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatef∆Êgranking%Ycrfcd9561irfcNumber%YestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfUsing the Parallel NFS (pNFS) SCSI Layout to Access Non-Volatile Memory Express (NVMe) Storage DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)The Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) provides Representational State Transfer (RESTful) web services to retrieve registration metadata from domain name and regional internet registries.  RDAP allows a server to make access control decisions based on client identity, and as such, it includes support for client identification features provided by the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).  Identification methods that require clients to obtain and manage credentials from every RDAP server operator present management challenges for both clients and servers, whereas a federated authentication system would make it easier to operate and use RDAP without the need to maintain server-specific client credentials.  This document describes a federated authentication system for RDAP based on OpenID Connect.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej÷Úhfilenamegrfc9560eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135856hkeywordsÉdRDAPiFederatednAuthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDatef+Úπgranking%Xcrfcd9560irfcNumber%XestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Federated Authentication for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Using OpenID ConnectdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This document defines the Matroska audiovisual data container structure, including definitions of its structural elements, terminology, vocabulary, and application.

This document updates RFC 8794 to permit the use of a previously reserved Extensible Binary Meta Language (EBML) Element ID.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Lhomme¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Bunkus¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Riceddatej÷Ìhfilenamegrfc9559eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcellardfullxHcellar - Codec Encoding for LossLess Archiving and Realtime transmissiondnamex?Codec Encoding for LossLess Archiving and Realtime transmissiondtypebwgbidjdoc-139708hkeywordsÖfbinarygstoragehmatroskadebmldwebmkobsoletedByÄepagesùopublicationDategØ7granking%Wcrfcd9559irfcNumber%WestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Matroska Media Container Format SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxıThis document describes how to produce digital signatures and hash functions using the GOST R 34.10-2012 and GOST R 34.11-2012 algorithms for DNSKEY, RRSIG, and DS resource records, for use in the Domain Name System Security Extensions (DNSSEC).gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx%The Technical center of Internet, LLCdnamelB. Makarenko¢kaffiliationtJSC "NPK Kryptonite"dnamekV. Dolmatovddatej–«hfilenamegrfc9558eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-135571hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatefgranking%Vcrfcd9558irfcNumber%VestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexUUse of GOST 2012 Signature Algorithms in DNSKEY and RRSIG Resource Records for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis document defines an extension to the timestamp format defined in RFC 3339 for representing additional information, including a time zone.

It updates RFC 3339 in the specific interpretation of the local offset Z, which is no longer understood to "imply that UTC is the preferred reference point for the specified time".fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIgalia S.L.dnameiU. Sharma¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Òhfilenamegrfc9557eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsedatedfullx9sedate - Serialising Extended Data About Times and Eventsdnamex0Serialising Extended Data About Times and Eventsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135853hkeywordsârGregorian calendarrCalendar awarenessiTime zonehISO 8601hTemporalhRFC 3339iExtensionx"Internet Extended Date/Time FormateIXDTFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef+ÁXgranking%Ucrfcd9557irfcNumber%UestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEDate and Time on the Internet: Timestamps with Additional InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyüMany Internet of Things (IoT) applications have requirements that cannot be satisfied by centralized cloud-based systems (i.e., cloud computing).  These include time sensitivity, data volume, connectivity cost, operation in the face of intermittent services, privacy, and security.  As a result, IoT is driving the Internet toward edge computing.  This document outlines the requirements of the emerging IoT edge and its challenges.  It presents a general model and major components of the IoT edge to provide a common basis for future discussions in the Thing-to-Thing Research Group (T2TRG) and other IRTF and IETF groups.  This document is a product of the IRTF T2TRG.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Hong¢kaffiliationrDaejeon UniversitydnameiY-G. Hong¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnameiX. de Foy¢kaffiliationx$Huawei Technologies Duesseldorf GmbHdnamekM. Kovatsch¢kaffiliationtUniversity of OxforddnamekE. Schooler¢kaffiliationiHKUST(GZ)dnamekD. Kutscherddatej÷hfilenamegrfc9556eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymet2trgdfullvt2trg - Thing-to-ThingdnamenThing-to-Thingdtypebrgbidjdoc-135572hkeywordsÉtin-network computingrin-network cachingrin-network storagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefgranking%Tcrfcd9556irfcNumber%TestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex6Internet of Things (IoT) Edge Challenges and FunctionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document defines how to convert contact information between the JSContact and vCard data formats.  It defines conversion rules for every JSContact and vCard element registered at IANA at the time of publication.  It also defines new JSContact properties as well as vCard properties and parameters, to support converting arbitrary or unknown JSContact and vCard elements.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsIIT-CNR/Registro.itdnamekM. Loffredo¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekR. Stepanekddatej÷Êhfilenamegrfc9555eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135989hkeywordsÑdJSONhcontactsevCardejCardkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDatef:xpgranking%Scrfcd9555irfcNumber%SestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'JSContact: Converting from and to vCarddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9982∂habstractyNThis document defines a set of new properties for vCard and extends the use of existing ones.  Their primary purpose is to align the same set of features between the JSContact and vCard formats, but the new definitions also aim to be useful within just the vCard format.  This document updates RFC 6350 ("vCard Format Specification").fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekR. Stepanek¢kaffiliationgIIT-CNRdnamekM. Loffredoddatej÷Êhfilenamegrfc9554eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135988hkeywordsÖkaddressbookhcontactsecardsevCardiJSContactkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef:xpgranking%Rcrfcd9554irfcNumber%RestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%vCard Format Extensions for JSContactdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This specification defines a data model and JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) representation of contact card information that can be used for data storage and exchange in address book or directory applications.  It aims to be an alternative to the vCard data format and to be unambiguous, extendable, and simple to process.  In contrast to the JSON-based jCard format, it is not a direct mapping from the vCard data model and expands semantics where appropriate.  Two additional specifications define new vCard elements and how to convert between JSContact and vCard.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekR. Stepanek¢kaffiliationgIIT-CNRdnamekM. Loffredoddatej÷Òhfilenamegrfc9553eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135987hkeywordsÖdJSONkaddressbookhcontactsecardseVCARDkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDatef:xogranking%Qcrfcd9553irfcNumber%QestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0JSContact: A JSON Representation of Contact DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAIn many environments, a component external to a network is called upon to perform computations based on the network topology and the current state of the connections within the network, including Traffic Engineering (TE) information. This is information typically distributed by IGP routing protocols within the network.

This document describes a mechanism by which link-state and TE information can be collected from networks and shared with external components using the BGP routing protocol. This is achieved using a BGP Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) encoding format. The mechanism applies to physical and virtual (e.g., tunnel) IGP links. The mechanism described is subject to policy control.

Applications of this technique include Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) servers and Path Computation Elements (PCEs).

This document obsoletes RFC 7752 by completely replacing that document. It makes some small changes and clarifications to the previous specification. This document also obsoletes RFC 9029 by incorporating the updates that it made to RFC 7752.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikarddatej÷›hfilenamegrfc9552eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131755hkeywordsÜhresearchkmeasurementnidentificationgprobingkout-of-bandgin-bandkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateeÖˆégranking%Pcrfcd9552irfcNumber%PestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHDistribution of Link-State and Traffic Engineering Information Using BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*Deterministic Networking (DetNet), as defined in RFC 8655, aims to provide bounded end-to-end latency on top of the network infrastructure, comprising both Layer 2 bridged and Layer 3 routed segments.  This document's primary purpose is to detail the specific requirements of the Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) recommended to maintain a deterministic network.  The document will be used in future work that defines the applicability of and extension of OAM protocols for a deterministic network.  With the implementation of the OAM framework in DetNet, an operator will have a real-time view of the network infrastructure regarding the network's ability to respect the Service Level Objective (SLO), such as packet delay, delay variation, and packet-loss ratio, assigned to each DetNet flow.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationdCNRSdnamelF. Theoleyre¢kaffiliationnIMT AtlantiquednameoG. Papadopoulos¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkasddatej÷¯hfilenamegrfc9551eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-134087hkeywordsÇgMetricsgTracingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÔ››granking%Ocrfcd9551irfcNumber%OestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdFramework of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) for Deterministic Networking (DetNet)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe replication and elimination functions of the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) architecture can result in out-of-order packets, which is not acceptable for some time-sensitive applications.  The Packet Ordering Function (POF) algorithms described in this document enable restoration of the correct packet order when the replication and elimination functions are used in DetNet networks.  The POF only provides ordering within the latency bound of a DetNet flow; it does not provide any additional reliability.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationfBeldendnameiS. Kehrer¢kaffiliationfBeldendnamegT. Heerddatej–Ÿhfilenamegrfc9550eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-135146hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee˚´]granking%Ncrfcd9550irfcNumber%NestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Deterministic Networking (DetNet): Packet Ordering FunctiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶The updates to RFC 5280 described in this document provide alignment with the 2008 specification for Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs) and includes support for internationalized email addresses in X.509 certificates.  The updates ensure that name constraints for email addresses that contain only ASCII characters and internationalized email addresses are handled in the same manner.  This document obsoletes RFC 8399.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housleyddatej÷˘hfilenamegrfc9549eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-134089hkeywordsÅxInternationalized Email AddresskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateeÔ‰granking%Mcrfcd9549irfcNumber%MestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Internationalization Updates to RFC 5280dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document specifies how to use "PKCS #12: Personal Information Exchange Syntax v1.1" (RFC 7292) to transport key containers (PFX) for storing keys and certificates in conjunction with the Russian national standard GOST algorithms.

This specification has been developed outside the IETF. The purpose of publication is to facilitate interoperable implementations that wish to support the GOST algorithms. This document does not imply IETF endorsement of the cryptographic algorithms used here.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhInfoTeCSdnamekE. Karelinaddatej÷ıhfilenamegrfc9548eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-136294hkeywordsÜxtransport key containerslcertificatesoGOST algorithmsfpkcs12dgostcPFXkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefWïYgranking%Lcrfcd9548irfcNumber%LestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexCGenerating Transport Key Containers (PFX) Using the GOST AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyßInternet communications and applications have both environmental costs and benefits. The IAB ran an online workshop in December 2022 to explore and understand these impacts.

The role of the workshop was to discuss the impacts and the evolving industry needs, and to identify areas for improvements and future work. A key goal of the workshop was to call further attention to the topic and bring together a diverse stakeholder community to discuss these issues.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliation`dnamemC. S. Perkins¢kaffiliation`dnamekS. Krishnanddatej÷ıhfilenamegrfc9547eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-132272hkeywordsÑkenvironmentfenergypInternet impactsnsustainabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÃ∏£granking%Kcrfcd9547irfcNumber%KestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex_Report from the IAB Workshop on Environmental Impact of Internet Applications and Systems, 2022dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document defines format and usage principles of the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) service Associated Channel over a DetNet network with the MPLS data plane.  The DetNet service Associated Channel can be used to carry test packets of active Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) protocols that are used to detect DetNet failures and measure performance metrics.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Vargaddatej÷¯hfilenamegrfc9546eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-133773hkeywordsÇfDetNetcOAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeﬂÈugranking%Jcrfcd9546irfcNumber%JestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpOperations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) for Deterministic Networking (DetNet) with the MPLS Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±A Segment Routing (SR) path is identified by an SR segment list. A subset of segments from the segment list cannot be leveraged to distinguish one SR path from another as they may be partially congruent. SR path identification is a prerequisite for various use cases such as performance measurement and end-to-end 1+1 path protection.

In an SR over MPLS (SR-MPLS) data plane, an egress node cannot determine on which SR path a packet traversed the network from the label stack because the segment identifiers are removed from the label stack as the packet transits the network.

This document defines a Path Segment Identifier (PSID) to identify an SR path on the egress node of the path.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Cheng¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeH. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeC. Li¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnameiR. Ziglerddatej÷Ùhfilenamegrfc9545eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-132427hkeywordsÖwPerformance MeasurementuBidirectional SR PathxEnd-to-End Path ProtectionoSR Path SegmentlPath SegmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee◊≈Ägranking%Icrfcd9545irfcNumber%IestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Path Segment Identifier in MPLS-Based Segment Routing NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document defines a set of metrics for networking services with
performance requirements expressed as Service Level Objectives
(SLOs).  These metrics, referred to as "Precision Availability Metrics
(PAMs)", are useful for defining and monitoring SLOs.  For example,
PAMs can be used by providers and/or customers of an RFC 9543 Network
Slice Service to assess whether the service is provided in compliance
with its defined SLOs.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefX. Min¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamelJ. Strassner¢kaffiliationx"Inria and University of LuxembourgdnamelJ. Fran√ßoisddatej÷Ùhfilenamegrfc9544eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-135156hkeywordsÇdIPPMwPerformance MeasurementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee˚¡£granking%Hcrfcd9544irfcNumber%HestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Precision Availability Metrics (PAMs) for Services Governed by Service Level Objectives (SLOs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document describes network slicing in the context of networks built from IETF technologies. It defines the term "IETF Network Slice" to describe this type of network slice and establishes the general principles of network slicing in the IETF context.

The document discusses the general framework for requesting and operating IETF Network Slices, the characteristics of an IETF Network Slice, the necessary system components and interfaces, and the mapping of abstract requests to more specific technologies. The document also discusses related considerations with monitoring and security.

This document also provides definitions of related terms to enable consistent usage in other IETF documents that describe or use aspects of IETF Network Slices.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeCienadnamehR. Rokui¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehS. Homma¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamelK. Makhijani¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamelL. Contreras¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej÷Òhfilenamegrfc9543eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-134085hkeywordsÅoNetwork SlicingkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateeÔƒÛgranking%Gcrfcd9543irfcNumber%GestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGA Framework for Network Slices in Networks Built from IETF TechnologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyrSome IETF protocols make use of Ethernet frame formats and IEEE 802 parameters.  This document discusses several aspects of such parameters and their use in IETF protocols, specifies IANA considerations for assignment of points under the IANA Organizationally Unique Identifier (OUI), and provides some values for use in documentation.  This document obsoletes RFC 7042.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Liddatej÷Úhfilenamegrfc9542eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-135575hkeywordsåhEthernetiEtherTypec802cOUIcEUIdLSAPcAFNdCBORcLLCdSLAPdSNAPcCIDkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDatefM∂granking%Fcrfcd9542irfcNumber%FestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberçetotaletitlexUIANA Considerations and IETF Protocol and Documentation Usage for IEEE 802 ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞Provider Backbone Bridging (PBB) can be combined with Ethernet Virtual Private Networks (EVPNs) to deploy Ethernet Local Area Network (E-LAN) services in large Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) networks.  That combination is what we refer to as "PBB-EVPN." Single-Active multihoming and per Service Instance Identifier (I-SID) load-balancing can be provided to access devices and aggregation networks.  In order to speed up the network convergence in case of failures on Single-Active multihomed Ethernet Segments (ESs), PBB-EVPN defines a flush mechanism for Customer MACs (C-MACs) called "C-MAC flush" that works for different Ethernet Segment Backbone MAC (B-MAC) address allocation models.  This document complements those C-MAC flush procedures for cases in which no PBB-EVPN ESs are defined (i.e., the attachment circuit is associated with a zero Ethernet Segment Identifier (ESI)) and the C-MAC flush requires I-SID-level granularity.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. Sathappan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejK. Nagaraj¢kaffiliationhSoftbankdnameiM. Miyake¢kaffiliationhSoftbankdnamejT. Matsudaddatej–œhfilenamegrfc9541eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-135139hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee˚ù˚granking%Ecrfcd9541irfcNumber%EestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÖFlush Mechanism for Customer MAC Addresses Based on Service Instance Identifier (I-SID) in Provider Backbone Bridging EVPN (PBB-EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document defines a parameter that can be included in Service Binding (SVCB) and HTTPS DNS resource records to denote that a service is accessible using Oblivious HTTP, by offering an Oblivious Gateway Resource through which to access the target.  This document also defines a mechanism for learning the key configuration of the discovered Oblivious Gateway Resource.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.Kddatej÷Òhfilenamegrfc9540eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdohaidfullx0ohai - Oblivious HTTP Application Intermediationdnamex)Oblivious HTTP Application Intermediationdtypebwgbidjdoc-132413hkeywordsÉcDNSnOblivious HTTPdSVCBkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatee÷≤8granking%Dcrfcd9540irfcNumber%DestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Discovery of Oblivious Services via Service Binding RecordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆThis document sets out steps that DNS servers (recursive resolvers and authoritative servers) can take unilaterally (without any coordination with other peers) to defend DNS query privacy against a passive network monitor. The protections provided by the guidance in this document can be defeated by an active attacker, but they should be simpler and less risky to deploy than more powerful defenses.

The goal of this document is to simplify and speed up deployment of opportunistic encrypted transport in the recursive-to-authoritative hop of the DNS ecosystem. Wider easy deployment of the underlying encrypted transport on an opportunistic basis may facilitate the future specification of stronger cryptographic protections against more-powerful attacks.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdACLUdnamemD. K. Gillmor¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Salazar¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc9539eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-133772hkeywordsâlDNS over TLSmDNS over QUICcToTcDoQjencryptionjunilateralirecursivemauthoritativecDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeﬂÃñgranking%Ccrfcd9539irfcNumber%CestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOUnilateral Opportunistic Deployment of Encrypted Recursive-to-Authoritative DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document defines metadata to support delegating the delivery of HTTPS content between two or more interconnected Content Delivery Networks (CDNs).  Specifically, this document defines a Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Metadata interface object to enable delegation of X.509 certificates leveraging delegation schemes defined in RFC 9115.  Per RFC 9115, delegating entities can remain in full control of the delegation and can revoke it at any time.  This avoids the need to share private cryptographic key material between the involved entities.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehF. Fieau¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejE. Stephan¢kaffiliationgVerizondnameiS. Mishraddatej÷Ûhfilenamegrfc9538eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-132425hkeywordsÑeHTTPSjdelegationdACMEdSTARkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatee◊µ‚granking%Bcrfcd9538irfcNumber%BestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqContent Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Delegation Using the Automated Certificate Management EnvironmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document describes a Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) extension for specifying methods of redaction of RDAP responses and explicitly identifying redacted RDAP response fields, using JSONPath as the default expression language.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehD. Smith¢kaffiliationlGoDaddy Inc.dnameiJ. Kolker¢kaffiliationlGoDaddy Inc.dnameiR. Carneyddatej÷Ùhfilenamegrfc9537eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135394hkeywordsÑhRedactediRedactioniRedactinghJSONPathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefÚ\granking%Acrfcd9537irfcNumber%AestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRedacted Fields in the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) ResponsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThe Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) does not include query capabilities for finding the list of domains related to a set of entities matching a given search pattern.  Considering that an RDAP entity can be associated with any defined object class and other relationships between RDAP object classes exist, a reverse search can be applied to other use cases besides the classic domain-entity scenario.  This document describes an RDAP extension that allows servers to provide a reverse search feature based on the relationship defined in RDAP between an object class for search and any related object class.  The reverse search based on the domain-entity relationship is treated as a particular case.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsIIT-CNR/Registro.itdnamekM. Loffredo¢kaffiliationsIIT-CNR/Registro.itdnamemM. Martinelliddatej÷‰hfilenamegrfc9536eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135570hkeywordsÇdRDAPnReverse searchkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef
Úgranking%@crfcd9536irfcNumber%@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Reverse SearchdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxtJSONPath defines a string syntax for selecting and extracting JSON (RFC 8259) values from within a given JSON value.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwFachhochschule DortmunddnamekS. G√∂ssner¢kaffiliation`dnamemG. Normington¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Ôhfilenamegrfc9535eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhjsonpathdfulltjsonpath - JSON PathdnameiJSON Pathdtypebwgbidjdoc-132416hkeywordsÅdJSONkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDatee÷‘Ëgranking%?crfcd9535irfcNumber%?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$JSONPath: Query Expressions for JSONdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document extends Simple Two-way Active Measurement Protocol (STAMP) to implement performance measurement on every member link of a Link Aggregation Group (LAG).  Knowing the measured metrics of each member link of a LAG enables operators to enforce a performance-based traffic steering policy across the member links.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegT. Zhou¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefJ. Guo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhiddatej÷ˆhfilenamegrfc9534eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-132110hkeywordsÑeSTAMPwPerformance MeasurementcLAGmMicro SessionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee∫Á≥granking%>crfcd9534irfcNumber%>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmSimple Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol Extensions for Performance Measurement on a Link Aggregation GroupdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document defines extensions to the One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) and the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP) to implement performance measurement on every member link of a Link Aggregation Group (LAG).  Knowing the measured metrics of each member link of a LAG enables operators to enforce the performance-based traffic steering policy across the member links.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegT. Zhou¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefJ. Guo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhiddatej÷ˆhfilenamegrfc9533eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-132109hkeywordsÖeTWAMPeOWAMPwPerformance MeasurementcLAGmMicro-sessionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee∫Ê$granking%=crfcd9533irfcNumber%=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrOne-Way and Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol Extensions for Performance Measurement on a Link Aggregation GroupdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÏThis document defines the next-hop-aliases HTTP Proxy-Status Parameter.  This parameter carries the list of aliases and canonical names an intermediary received during DNS resolution as part of establishing a connection to the next hop.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamehT. Paulyddatej÷ˆhfilenamegrfc9532eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-132107hkeywordsÅlproxy statuskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee∫¬Ægranking%<crfcd9532irfcNumber%<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0HTTP Proxy-Status Parameter for Next-Hop AliasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty§Path steering is a mechanism to discover paths to the producers of Information-Centric Networking (ICN) Content Objects and steer subsequent Interest messages along a previously discovered path. It has various uses, including the operation of state-of-the-art multi-path congestion control algorithms and for network measurement and management. This specification derives directly from the design published in "Path Switching in Content Centric and Named Data Networks" (4th ACM Conference on Information-Centric Networking) and, therefore, does not recapitulate the design motivations, implementation details, or evaluation of the scheme. However, some technical details are different, and where there are differences, the design documented here is to be considered definitive.

This document is a product of the IRTF Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG). It is not an IETF product and is not an Internet Standard.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamelI. Moiseenko¢kaffiliationx#Network Systems Research and DesigndnamegD. Oranddatej÷Ôhfilenamegrfc9531eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-134082hkeywordsÅcICNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÔÑ˛granking%;crfcd9531irfcNumber%;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexRPath Steering in Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) and Named Data Networking (NDN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document defines HTTP fields that support integrity digests. The Content-Digest field can be used for the integrity of HTTP message content. The Repr-Digest field can be used for the integrity of HTTP representations. Want-Content-Digest and Want-Repr-Digest can be used to indicate a sender's interest and preferences for receiving the respective Integrity fields.

This document obsoletes RFC 3230 and the Digest and Want-Digest HTTP fields.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx Team Digitale Italian GovernmentdnamehR. Polli¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednameiL. Pardueddatej÷Íhfilenamegrfc9530eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-132277hkeywordsÅfDigestkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateeÕ£granking%:crfcd9530irfcNumber%:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlemDigest FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxTThis document contains example traces of Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman Over COSE (EDHOC).fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Preu√ü Mattsson¢kaffiliationjASSA ABLOYdnamejM. Serafin¢kaffiliationgRISE ABdnameiM. Tiloca¢kaffiliationeInriadnamelM. Vuƒçiniƒáddatej÷Óhfilenamegrfc9529eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlakedfullx-lake - Lightweight Authenticated Key Exchangednamex&Lightweight Authenticated Key Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-135130hkeywordsÖktest vectorklightweightxauthenticated key exchangedLAKEcAKEkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDatee˚î/granking%9crfcd9529irfcNumber%9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Traces of Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman Over COSE (EDHOC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document specifies Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman Over COSE (EDHOC), a very compact and lightweight authenticated Diffie-Hellman key exchange with ephemeral keys.  EDHOC provides mutual authentication, forward secrecy, and identity protection.  EDHOC is intended for usage in constrained scenarios, and a main use case is to establish an Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE) security context.  By reusing CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) for cryptography, Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) for encoding, and Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) for transport, the additional code size can be kept very low.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Preu√ü Mattsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelF. Palombiniddatej÷Ïhfilenamegrfc9528eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlakedfullx-lake - Lightweight Authenticated Key Exchangednamex&Lightweight Authenticated Key Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-135129hkeywordsácAKEdLAKEdCOSEfOSCOREx&lightweight authenticated key exchangexconstrained node networkslIoT securitykobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDatee˚î/granking%8crfcd9528irfcNumber%8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman Over COSE (EDHOC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines DHCPv6 options so that a Homenet Naming Authority (HNA) can automatically set the appropriate configuration and outsource the authoritative naming service for the home network.  In most cases, the outsourcing mechanism is transparent for the end user.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migault¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamehR. Weber¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalskiddatej÷€hfilenamegrfc9527eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-132051hkeywordsÅcDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee∑9úgranking%7crfcd9527irfcNumber%7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/DHCPv6 Options for the Homenet Naming AuthoritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4Home network owners may have devices or services hosted on their home network that they wish to access from the Internet (i.e., from a network outside of the home network). Home networks are increasingly numbered using IPv6 addresses, which in principle makes this access simpler, but accessing home networks from the Internet requires the names and IP addresses of these devices and services to be made available in the public DNS.

This document describes how a Home Naming Authority (NHA) instructs the outsourced infrastructure to publish these pieces of information in the public DNS. The names and IP addresses of the home network are set in the Public Homenet Zone by the Homenet Naming Authority (HNA), which in turn instructs an outsourced infrastructure to publish the zone on behalf of the home network owner.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migault¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamehR. Weber¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationtGlobis Consulting BVdnameiR. Hunterddatej÷⁄hfilenamegrfc9526eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-132050hkeywordsÖcDNSgHomenetkoutsourcingjmanagementghostingkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDatee∑9úgranking%6crfcd9526irfcNumber%6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Simple Provisioning of Public Names for Residential NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybMany application technologies enable secure communication between two entities by means of Transport Layer Security (TLS) with Internet Public Key Infrastructure using X.509 (PKIX) certificates. This document specifies procedures for representing and verifying the identity of application services in such interactions.

This document obsoletes RFC 6125.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamegR. Salzddatej–ƒhfilenamegrfc9525eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131295hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeKÇ·granking%5crfcd9525irfcNumber%5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewService Identity in TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≈This document describes the Segment Routing Replication segment for multipoint service delivery.  A Replication segment allows a packet to be replicated from a replication node to downstream nodes.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Parekh¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejH. Bidgoli¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhangddatej–…hfilenamegrfc9524eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-132426hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee◊ºgranking%4crfcd9524irfcNumber%4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Segment Routing Replication for Multipoint Service DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9960∂habstractyThe Network Time Protocol version 4 (NTPv4), as defined in RFC 5905, is the mechanism used by NTP clients to synchronize with NTP servers across the Internet.  This document describes a companion application to the NTPv4 client, named "Khronos", that is used as a "watchdog" alongside NTPv4 and that provides improved security against time-shifting attacks.  Khronos involves changes to the NTP client's system process only.  Since it does not affect the wire protocol, the Khronos mechanism is applicable to current and future time protocols.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHebrew University of JerusalemdnameoN. Rozen-Schiff¢kaffiliationxHebrew University of JerusalemdnamehD. Dolev¢kaffiliationx Huawei Network.IO Innovation LabdnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationxHebrew University of JerusalemdnamekM. Schapiraddatej÷„hfilenamegrfc9523eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-132412hkeywordsÇcNTPeNTPv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee÷áUgranking%3crfcd9523irfcNumber%3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA Secure Selection and Filtering Mechanism for the Network Time Protocol with KhronosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the principles of traffic engineering (TE) in the Internet. The document is intended to promote better understanding of the issues surrounding traffic engineering in IP networks and the networks that support IP networking and to provide a common basis for the development of traffic-engineering capabilities for the Internet. The principles, architectures, and methodologies for performance evaluation and performance optimization of operational networks are also discussed.

This work was first published as RFC 3272 in May 2002. This document obsoletes RFC 3272 by making a complete update to bring the text in line with best current practices for Internet traffic engineering and to include references to the latest relevant work in the IETF.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej–«hfilenamegrfc9522eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-132039hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDatee∂◊granking%2crfcd9522irfcNumber%2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Overview and Principles of Internet Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‘This document describes the use of the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol in point-to-point Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation (Geneve) unicast tunnels used to make up an overlay network.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefX. Min¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamemS. Pallagatti¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Aldrinddatej÷‹hfilenamegrfc9521eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-131922hkeywordsÉcNVEcVAPcVNIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee¶ÒÔgranking%1crfcd9521irfcNumber%1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for  Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation (Geneve)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$In the DNS, resolvers employ caching to reduce both latency for end users and load on authoritative name servers. The process of resolution may result in one of three types of responses: (1) a response containing the requested data, (2) a response indicating the requested data does not exist, or (3) a non-response due to a resolution failure in which the resolver does not receive any useful information regarding the data's existence. This document concerns itself only with the third type.

RFC 2308 specifies requirements for DNS negative caching. There, caching of TYPE 2 responses is mandatory and caching of TYPE 3 responses is optional. This document updates RFC 2308 to require negative caching for DNS resolution failures.

RFC 4035 allows DNSSEC validation failure caching. This document updates RFC 4035 to require caching for DNSSEC validation failures.

RFC 4697 prohibits aggressive requerying for NS records at a failed zone's parent zone. This document updates RFC 4697 to expand this requirement to all query types and to all ancestor zones.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejD. Wessels¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejW. Carroll¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnameiM. Thomasddatej÷Óhfilenamegrfc9520eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131754hkeywordsÉcDNShNegativegCachingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÖ„÷granking%0crfcd9520irfcNumber%0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Negative Caching of DNS Resolution FailuresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠This specification updates the registration policies for adding new entries to registries within the IANA "Secure Shell (SSH) Protocol Parameters" group of registries.  Previously, the registration policy was generally IETF Review, as defined in RFC 8126, although a few registries require Standards Action.  This specification changes it from IETF Review to Expert Review.  This document updates RFCs 4250, 4716, 4819, and 8308.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfAKAYLAdnamefP. Yeeddatej÷“hfilenamegrfc9519eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-132011hkeywordsÉcsshdianahregistrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee±Æ»granking%/crfcd9519irfcNumber%/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Update to the IANA SSH Protocol Parameters Registry RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document discusses aspects of centralization that relate to Internet standards efforts.  It argues that, while standards bodies have a limited ability to prevent many forms of centralization, they can still make contributions that assist in the decentralization of the Internet.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷‹hfilenamegrfc9518eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131707hkeywordsÅjgovernancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÅ€granking%.crfcd9518irfcNumber%.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8Centralization, Decentralization, and Internet StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyüThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) "ddi" for Uniform Resource Names (URNs) used to identify resources that conform to the standards published by the Data Documentation Initiative (DDI) Alliance.

The DDI Alliance is not affiliated with the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) or Internet Society (ISOC). This Independent Submission is not a standard nor does it have IETF community consensus.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlDDI AlliancednamekJ. Wackerowddatej–&hfilenamegrfc9517eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131925hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeß…granking%-crfcd9517irfcNumber%-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex;A URN Namespace for the Data Documentation Initiative (DDI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-A set of requirements for active Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) for Service Function Chaining (SFC) in a network is presented in this document.  Based on these requirements, an encapsulation of active OAM messages in SFC and a mechanism to detect and localize defects are described.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegW. Meng¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeT. Ao¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamemB. Khasnabish¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationlVerizon Inc.dnameiG. Mishraddatej–√hfilenamegrfc9516eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131294hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee\L6granking%,crfcd9516irfcNumber%,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Active Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) for Service Function Chaining (SFC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document updates RFC 7854, "BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP)", by changing the registration procedures for several registries.  Specifically, any BMP registry with a range of 32768-65530 designated "Specification Required" has that range redesignated as "First Come First Served".fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej÷Ìhfilenamegrfc9515eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131293hkeywordsÅcIDRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateesáhgranking%+crfcd9515irfcNumber%+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Revision to Registration Procedures for Multiple BMP RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHSegment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) allows for a flexible definition of end-to-end paths within various topologies by encoding paths as sequences of topological or functional sub-paths called "segments". These segments are advertised by various protocols such as BGP, IS-IS, and OSPFv3.

This document defines extensions to BGP - Link State (BGP-LS) to advertise SRv6 segments along with their behaviors and other attributes via BGP. The BGP-LS address-family solution for SRv6 described in this document is similar to BGP-LS for SR for the MPLS data plane, which is defined in RFC 9085.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamehG. Dawra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamejD. Bernier¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraeneddatej÷∫hfilenamegrfc9514eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131292hkeywordsÑcBGPfBGP-LSoSegment RoutingdSRv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeo¬Ngranking%*crfcd9514irfcNumber%*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) Extensions for Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7The Segment Routing (SR) architecture allows a flexible definition of the end-to-end path by encoding it as a sequence of topological elements called segments.  It can be implemented over an MPLS or IPv6 data plane.  This document describes the OSPFv3 extensions required to support SR over the IPv6 data plane.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Hu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenakddatej÷Ëhfilenamegrfc9513eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131291hkeywordsÑdOSPFfOSPFv3oSegment RoutingdSRv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeo¬Hgranking%)crfcd9513irfcNumber%)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6OSPFv3 Extensions for Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≈This document registers the application/yaml media type and the +yaml structured syntax suffix with IANA.  Both identify document components that are serialized according to the YAML specification.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwDTD, Italian GovernmentdnamehR. Polli¢kaffiliationeAxwaydnamehE. Wilde¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamefE. Aroddatej÷Âhfilenamegrfc9512eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpapidfullx'httpapi - Building Blocks for HTTP APIsdnamexBuilding Blocks for HTTP APIsdtypebwgbidjdoc-132279hkeywordsÅwdata interchange formatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÕ;ugranking%(crfcd9512irfcNumber%(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleoYAML Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhActive measurements over the public Internet can target either collaborating parties or non-collaborating ones. Sometimes these measurements, also called "probes", are viewed as unwelcome or aggressive.

This document suggests some simple techniques for a source to identify its probes. This allows any party or organization to understand what an unsolicited probe packet is, what its purpose is, and, most importantly, who to contact. The technique relies on offline analysis of the probe; therefore, it does not require any change in the data or control plane. It has been designed mainly for layer 3 measurements.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamej√â. Vyncke¢kaffiliationuUniversit√© de Li√®gednameiB. Donnet¢kaffiliationuUniversit√© de Li√®gednameiJ. Iurmanddatej–…hfilenamegrfc9511eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-131290hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateefù—granking%'crfcd9511irfcNumber%'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAttribution of Internet ProbesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractylContent-Centric Networking (CCNx) utilizes delta time for a number of functions. When using CCNx in environments with constrained nodes or bandwidth constrained networks, it is valuable to have a compressed representation of delta time. In order to do so, either accuracy or dynamic range has to be sacrificed. Since the current uses of delta time do not require both simultaneously, one can consider a logarithmic encoding. This document updates RFC 8609 ("CCNx messages in TLV Format") to specify this alternative encoding.

This document is a product of the IRTF Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamemC. G√ºndoƒüan¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationx#Network Systems Research and DesigndnamegD. Oran¢kaffiliationjTU DresdendnamelM. W√§hlischddatej÷Ùhfilenamegrfc9510eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-132429hkeywordsÑdCCNxtconstrained networksucompressed delta timecIoTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee◊⁄¢granking%&crfcd9510irfcNumber%&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexmAlternative Delta Time Encoding for Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) Using Compact Floating-Point ArithmeticdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáRFC 5280 specifies several extended key purpose identifiers (KeyPurposeIds) for X.509 certificates.  This document defines encrypting JSON objects in HTTP messages, using JSON Web Tokens (JWTs), and signing the OAuth 2.0 access tokens KeyPurposeIds for inclusion in the Extended Key Usage (EKU) extension of X.509 v3 public key certificates used by Network Functions (NFs) for the 5G System.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehJ. Ekman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migaultddatej÷Óhfilenamegrfc9509eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-135125hkeywordsÅd3GPPkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatee˚â\granking%%crfcd9509irfcNumber%%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCX.509 Certificate Extended Key Usage (EKU) for 5G Network FunctionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document presents the design of an Information-Centric Networking (ICN) Ping protocol. It includes the operations of both the client and the forwarder.

This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG) of the IRTF.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Notre DamednamemS. Mastorakis¢kaffiliationx#Network Systems Research and DesigndnamegD. Oran¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiJ. Gibson¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamelI. Moiseenko¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehR. Dromsddatej÷Îhfilenamegrfc9508eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-134081hkeywordsÇcICNrNetwork ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÔÑ˝granking%$crfcd9508irfcNumber%$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex@Information-Centric Networking (ICN) Ping Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty	This document presents the design of an Information-Centric Networking (ICN) Traceroute protocol. This includes the operation of both the client and the forwarder.

This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG) of the IRTF.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Notre DamednamemS. Mastorakis¢kaffiliationx#Network Systems Research and DesigndnamegD. Oran¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamelI. Moiseenko¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiJ. Gibson¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehR. Dromsddatej÷Îhfilenamegrfc9507eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-134080hkeywordsÇcICNrNetwork ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÔÑ˝granking%#crfcd9507irfcNumber%#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexFInformation-Centric Networking (ICN) Traceroute Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This document describes protocol-independent methods called Explicit Host-to-Network Flow Measurement Techniques that can be applicable to transport-layer protocols between the client and server.  These methods employ just a few marking bits inside the header of each packet for performance measurements and require the client and server to collaborate.  Both endpoints cooperate by marking packets and, possibly, mirroring the markings on the round-trip connection.  The techniques are especially valuable when applied to protocols that encrypt transport headers since they enable loss and delay measurements by passive, on-path network devices.  This document describes several methods that can be used separately or jointly depending of the availability of marking bits, desired measurements, and properties of the protocol to which the methods are applied.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationtTelecom Italia - TIMdnamekM. Cociglio¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamekA. Ferrieux¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamekG. Fioccola¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamekI. Lubashev¢kaffiliationtTelecom Italia - TIMdnamemF. Bulgarella¢kaffiliationtTelecom Italia - TIMdnamegM. Nilo¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamelI. Hamchaoui¢kaffiliationuPolitecnico di TorinodnamehR. Sistoddatej÷Îhfilenamegrfc9506eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-131289hkeywordsìkPerformancejMonitoringgPassivefHybriddLosseDelayfClientfServergOn-pathhObservereProbeiAlternategMarkingeRounddTripgLatencyiEncryptedhProtocoldBitskobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateeAÅµgranking%"crfcd9506irfcNumber%"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Explicit Host-to-Network Flow Measurements TechniquesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty2This document describes technical mechanisms employed in network censorship that regimes around the world use for blocking or impairing Internet traffic.  It aims to make designers, implementers, and users of Internet protocols aware of the properties exploited and mechanisms used for censoring end-user access to information.  This document makes no suggestions on individual protocol considerations, and is purely informational, intended as a reference.  This document is a product of the Privacy Enhancement and Assessment Research Group (PEARG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamejJ. L. Hall¢kaffiliationjCU BoulderdnamekM. D. Aaron¢kaffiliation`dnameoA. Andersdotter¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Jones¢kaffiliationiU ChicagodnamekN. Feamster¢kaffiliationx!Center for Democracy & TechnologydnameiM. Knodelddatej÷Íhfilenamegrfc9505eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepeargdfullx;pearg - Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Groupdnamex3Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-131288hkeywordsÖrnetwork censorshippnetwork blockingrnetwork throttlingrtraffic impairmentxcensorship circumventionkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDatee\TÍgranking%!crfcd9505irfcNumber%!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex+A Survey of Worldwide Censorship TechniquesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) has been extended to support stateful PCE functions where the stateful PCE maintains information about paths and resource usage within a network; however, these extensions do not cover all requirements for GMPLS networks.

This document provides the extensions required for PCEP so as to enable the usage of a stateful PCE capability in GMPLS-controlled networks.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehH. Zheng¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehV. Lopez¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefZ. Aliddatej÷Ïhfilenamegrfc9504eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-131287hkeywordsÉlStateful PCEeGMPLSqPCE-initiated LSPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeqÊgranking% crfcd9504irfcNumber% estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Stateful PCE Usage in GMPLS-Controlled NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractyèSegment Routing (SR) leverages the source routing paradigm.  SR is applicable to both Multiprotocol Label Switching (SR-MPLS) and IPv6 (SRv6) forwarding planes.  This document specifies Simple Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (STAMP) extensions (as described in RFC 8762) for SR networks, for both the SR-MPLS and SRv6 forwarding planes, by augmenting the optional extensions defined in RFC 8972.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationdColtdnamekB. Janssens¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Footeddatej–≈hfilenamegrfc9503eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-131286hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeAﬁOgranking%crfcd9503irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZSimple Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (STAMP) Extensions for Segment Routing NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxjThis document extends IGP Flexible Algorithm so that it can be used with regular IPv4 and IPv6 forwarding.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiW. Britto¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekP. Kaneriya¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Shetty¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenakddatej÷Ëhfilenamegrfc9502eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131285hkeywordsÅeIS-ISkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee^ígranking%crfcd9502irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%IGP Flexible Algorithm in IP NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx˘This document outlines a principle for open participation that
extends the open process principle defined in RFC 3935 by stating
that there must be a free option for online participation to IETF
meetings and, if possible, related IETF-hosted events.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamegJ. Reed¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamegR. Salzddatej÷Ïhfilenamegrfc9501eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeshmoodfullx*shmoo - Stay Home Meet Occasionally Onlinednamex"Stay Home Meet Occasionally Onlinedtypebwgbidjdoc-131683hkeywordsÑgno-costhmeetingsxremote participation feefremotekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee|∏granking%crfcd9501irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÔetotaletitlex>Open Participation Principle regarding Remote Registration FeedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides a set of standard Public Key Cryptography (PKC) test keys that may be used wherever pre-generated keys and associated operations like digital signatures are required.  Like the European Institute for Computer Antivirus Research (EICAR) virus test and the Generic Test for Unsolicited Bulk Email (GTUBE) spam test files, these publicly known test keys can be detected and recognised by applications consuming them as being purely for testing purposes without assigning any security properties to them.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamejP. Gutmann¢kaffiliationhDigiCertdnamejC. Bonnellddatej–ohfilenamegrfc9500eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-131284hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateesÅ§granking%crfcd9500irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Standard Public Key Cryptography (PKC) Test KeysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyIThe Domain Name System (DNS) is defined in literally dozens of different RFCs. The terminology used by implementers and developers of DNS protocols, and by operators of DNS systems, has changed in the decades since the DNS was first defined. This document gives current definitions for many of the terms used in the DNS in a single document.

This document updates RFC 2308 by clarifying the definitions of "forwarder" and "QNAME". It obsoletes RFC 8499 by adding multiple terms and clarifications. Comprehensive lists of changed and new definitions can be found in Appendices A and B.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwaraddatej÷Ôhfilenamegrfc9499eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135200hkeywordsÇjvocabularyrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDatee˚„∂granking%crfcd9499irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber€etotaletitleoDNS TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty∆This document provides the GNU Name System (GNS) technical specification. GNS is a decentralized and censorship-resistant domain name resolution protocol that provides a privacy-enhancing alternative to the Domain Name System (DNS) protocols.

This document defines the normative wire format of resource records, resolution processes, cryptographic routines, and security and privacy considerations for use by implementers.

This specification was developed outside the IETF and does not have IETF consensus. It is published here to inform readers about the function of GNS, guide future GNS implementations, and ensure interoperability among implementations (for example, pre-existing GNUnet implementations).gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer AISECdnameoM. Schanzenbach¢kaffiliationuBerner FachhochschulednamekC. Grothoff¢kaffiliationkGNUnet e.V.dnamefB. Fixddatej÷Èhfilenamegrfc9498eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131283hkeywordsÅlname systemskobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDatee\¡granking%crfcd9498irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlesThe GNU Name SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≥An Oblivious Pseudorandom Function (OPRF) is a two-party protocol between a client and a server for computing the output of a Pseudorandom Function (PRF).  The server provides the PRF private key, and the client provides the PRF input.  At the end of the protocol, the client learns the PRF output without learning anything about the PRF private key, and the server learns neither the PRF input nor output.  An OPRF can also satisfy a notion of 'verifiability', called a VOPRF.  A VOPRF ensures clients can verify that the server used a specific private key during the execution of the protocol.  A VOPRF can also be partially oblivious, called a POPRF.  A POPRF allows clients and servers to provide public input to the PRF computation.  This document specifies an OPRF, VOPRF, and POPRF instantiated within standard prime-order groups, including elliptic curves.  This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnBrave SoftwarednamekA. Davidson¢kaffiliationpCloudflare, Inc.dnamepA. Faz-Hernandez¢kaffiliationpCloudflare, Inc.dnamekN. Sullivan¢kaffiliationpCloudflare, Inc.dnamejC. A. Woodddatej–µhfilenamegrfc9497eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-131753hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateeÖŒºgranking%crfcd9497irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexAOblivious Pseudorandom Functions (OPRFs) Using Prime-Order GroupsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty—This memo specifies two prime-order groups, ristretto255 and decaf448, suitable for safely implementing higher-level and complex cryptographic protocols. The ristretto255 group can be implemented using Curve25519, allowing existing Curve25519 implementations to be reused and extended to provide a prime-order group. Likewise, the decaf448 group can be implemented using edwards448.

This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. de Valence¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Grigg¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Hamburg¢kaffiliation`dnamelI. Lovecruft¢kaffiliation`dnamemG. Tankersley¢kaffiliation`dnamekF. Valsordaddatej÷Áhfilenamegrfc9496eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-131752hkeywordsäscryptographic grouplcryptographyjCurve25519ceccjedwards448nelliptic curvexelliptic curve cryptographyvnonmalleable encodingskprime-orderkprime orderkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÖŒºgranking%crfcd9496irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex$The ristretto255 and decaf448 GroupsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœThe Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) DNS resource record (RR) provides a mechanism for domains to express the allowed set of Certification Authorities that are authorized to issue certificates for the domain.  RFC 8659 contains the core CAA specification, where Property Tags that restrict the issuance of certificates that certify domain names are defined.  This specification defines a Property Tag that grants authorization to Certification Authorities to issue certificates that contain the id-kp-emailProtection key purpose in the extendedKeyUsage extension and at least one rfc822Name value or otherName value of type id-on-SmtpUTF8Mailbox that includes the domain name in the subjectAltName extension.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnDigiCert, Inc.dnamejC. Bonnellddatej÷Îhfilenamegrfc9495eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-131282hkeywordsÉccaax%certification authority authorizationmemail addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee)À˛granking%crfcd9495irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJCertification Authority Authorization (CAA) Processing for Email AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document introduces a BGP capability called the "Long-Lived Graceful Restart Capability" (or "LLGR Capability"). The benefit of this capability is that stale routes can be retained for a longer time upon session failure than is provided for by BGP Graceful Restart (as described in RFC 4724). A well-known BGP community called "LLGR_STALE" is introduced for marking stale routes retained for a longer time. A second well-known BGP community called "NO_LLGR" is introduced for marking routes for which these procedures should not be applied. We also specify that such long-lived stale routes be treated as the least preferred and that their advertisements be limited to BGP speakers that have advertised the capability. Use of this extension is not advisable in all cases, and we provide guidelines to help determine if it is.

This memo updates RFC 6368 by specifying that the LLGR_STALE community must be propagated into, or out of, the path attributes exchanged between the Provider Edge (PE) and Customer Edge (CE) routers.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwIndependent ContributordnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationrPalo Alto NetworksdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej–ƒhfilenamegrfc9494eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131281hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateegƒ6granking%crfcd9494irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Long-Lived Graceful Restart for BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌSecurity events communicated within Security Event Tokens may support a variety of identifiers to identify subjects related to the event.  This specification formalizes the notion of Subject Identifiers as structured information that describes a subject and named formats that define the syntax and semantics for encoding Subject Identifiers as JSON objects.  It also establishes a registry for defining and allocating names for such formats as well as the JSON Web Token (JWT) "sub_id" Claim.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfAmazondnamejA. Backman¢kaffiliationhCoinbasednamelM. Scurtescu¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamegP. Jainddatej÷Ëhfilenamegrfc9493eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhseceventdfullxsecevent - Security EventsdnameoSecurity Eventsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131280hkeywordsÅcjwtkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeq‡granking%crfcd9493irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Subject Identifiers for Security Event TokensdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸Existing traffic-engineering-related link attribute advertisements have been defined and are used in RSVP-TE deployments. Since the original RSVP-TE use case was defined, additional applications such as Segment Routing (SR) Policy and Loop-Free Alternates (LFAs) that also make use of the link attribute advertisements have been defined. In cases where multiple applications wish to make use of these link attributes, the current advertisements do not support application-specific values for a given attribute, nor do they support indication of which applications are using the advertised value for a given link. This document introduces link attribute advertisements in OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 that address both of these shortcomings.

This document obsoletes RFC 8920.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drakeddatej–Ωhfilenamegrfc9492eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131279hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee%‡Igranking%crfcd9492irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)OSPF Application-Specific Link AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|This document describes the integration of the Network Service Header (NSH) and Segment Routing (SR), as well as encapsulation details, to efficiently support Service Function Chaining (SFC) while maintaining separation of the service and transport planes as originally intended by the SFC architecture.

Combining these technologies allows SR to be used for steering packets between Service Function Forwarders (SFFs) along a given Service Function Path (SFP), whereas the NSH is responsible for maintaining the integrity of the service plane, the SFC instance context, and any associated metadata.

This integration demonstrates that the NSH and SR can work cooperatively and provide a network operator with the flexibility to use whichever transport technology makes sense in specific areas of their network infrastructure while still maintaining an end-to-end service plane using the NSH.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamekJ. Guichard¢kaffiliationfNvidiadnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej÷Êhfilenamegrfc9491eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131278hkeywordsÖcNSHbSRgMPLS-SRdSRv6cSFCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeJT granking%crfcd9491irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgIntegration of the Network Service Header (NSH) and Segment Routing for Service Function Chaining (SFC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty The "Management Techniques in Encrypted Networks (M-TEN)" workshop was convened by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) from 17 October 2022 to 19 October 2022 as a three-day online meeting. The workshop was organized in three parts to discuss ways to improve network management techniques in support of even broader adoption of encryption on the Internet. This report summarizes the workshop's discussion and identifies topics that warrant future work and consideration.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the expressed during the workshop by participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Knodel¢kaffiliation`dnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliation`dnamehT. Paulyddatej÷Èhfilenamegrfc9490eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-131924hkeywordsÇjencryptionrnetwork managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee¶ˇ˛granking%crfcd9490irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexSReport from the IAB Workshop on Management Techniques in Encrypted Networks (M-TEN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping is a widely deployed Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) mechanism in MPLS networks.  This document describes mechanisms for detecting data plane failures using LSP Ping in MPLS-based Ethernet VPN (EVPN) and Provider Backbone Bridging EVPN (PBB-EVPN) networks.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegP. Jain¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationeCienadnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirskyddatej÷Âhfilenamegrfc9489eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-131277hkeywordsÉdEVPNhLSP PinghMPLS OAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeLü∆granking%crfcd9489irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaLabel Switched Path (LSP) Ping Mechanisms for EVPN and Provider Backbone Bridging EVPN (PBB-EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÛThis document updates RFC 5440 to clarify usage of the Local Protection Desired bit signaled in the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP).  This document also introduces a new flag for signaling protection enforcement in PCEP.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehA. Stone¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Aissaoui¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehS. Sidor¢kaffiliationrCiena CoroporationdnamelS. Sivabalanddatej÷Ëhfilenamegrfc9488eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-131276hkeywordsÖosegment routingmadjacency sidcslalfast reroutefti-lfakobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatee0'¡granking%crfcd9488irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZLocal Protection Enforcement in the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document introduces new IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Elements (IEs) to identify a set of information related to Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) such as data contained in a Segment Routing Header (SRH), the SRv6 control plane, and the SRv6 Endpoint behavior that traffic is being forwarded with.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhSwisscomdnamegT. Graf¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationiINSA-LyondnamekP. Francoisddatej÷Êhfilenamegrfc9487eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131275hkeywordsÉdSRv6bSRkFlow RecordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeKm…granking%crfcd9487irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUExport of Segment Routing over IPv6 Information in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM) records operational and telemetry information in the packet while the packet traverses a path between two points in the network.  This document outlines how IOAM Data-Fields are encapsulated in IPv6.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkThoughtspotdnamekS. Bhandari¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Brocknersddatej–ºhfilenamegrfc9486eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-131274hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatee(!granking%crfcd9486irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKIPv6 Options for In Situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≠This document specifies I-Regexp, a flavor of regular expression that is limited in scope with the goal of interoperation across many different regular expression libraries.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationjTextualitydnamegT. Brayddatej÷Íhfilenamegrfc9485eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhjsonpathdfulltjsonpath - JSON PathdnameiJSON Pathdtypebwgbidjdoc-131273hkeywordsÇfRegexpeRegexkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatee)≈pgranking%crfcd9485irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4I-Regexp: An Interoperable Regular Expression FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThis document describes how to proxy IP packets in HTTP.  This protocol is similar to UDP proxying in HTTP but allows transmitting arbitrary IP packets.  More specifically, this document defines a protocol that allows an HTTP client to create an IP tunnel through an HTTP server that acts as an IP proxy.  This document updates RFC 9298.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinazi¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnameqA. Chernyakhovsky¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej÷„hfilenamegrfc9484eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmasquedfullx?masque - Multiplexed Application Substrate over QUIC Encryptiondnamex6Multiplexed Application Substrate over QUIC Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-131272hkeywordsädquicdhttphdatagramcVPNeproxygtunnelsxquic in udp in IP in quicxturtles all the way downfmasqueghttp-ngkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDatee1çÙgranking%crfcd9484irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesProxying IP in HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThis document aims at simple, interoperable, and automated PKI management operations covering typical use cases of industrial and Internet of Things (IoT) scenarios.  This is achieved by profiling the Certificate Management Protocol (CMP), the related Certificate Request Message Format (CRMF), and transfer based on HTTP or Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) in a succinct but sufficiently detailed and self-contained way.  To make secure certificate management for simple scenarios and constrained devices as lightweight as possible, only the most crucial types of operations and options are specified as mandatory.  More specialized or complex use cases are supported with optional features.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamelH. Brockhaus¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemD. von Oheimb¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamehS. Friesddatej–∞hfilenamegrfc9483eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-131271hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesSopublicationDateeG∏2granking%crfcd9483irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Lightweight Certificate Management Protocol (CMP) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis document specifies the use of the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) as a transfer mechanism for the Certificate Management Protocol (CMP).  CMP defines the interaction between various PKI entities for the purpose of certificate creation and management.  CoAP is an HTTP-like client-server protocol used by various constrained devices in the Internet of Things space.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrPalo Alto NetworksdnamehM. Sahni¢kaffiliationrPalo Alto NetworksdnamekS. Tripathiddatej–Ωhfilenamegrfc9482eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131270hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateeG∏1granking%
crfcd9482irfcNumber%
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXConstrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Transfer for the Certificate Management ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document describes the conventions for using several
cryptographic algorithms with the Certificate Management Protocol
(CMP).  CMP is used to enroll and further manage the lifecycle of
X.509 certificates.  This document also updates the algorithm use
profile from Appendix D.2 of RFC 4210.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamelH. Brockhaus¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamekH. Aschauer¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamelM. Ounsworth¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamegJ. Grayddatej–ìhfilenamegrfc9481eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-131269hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeG∏1granking%	crfcd9481irfcNumber%	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Certificate Management Protocol (CMP) AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∏This document contains a set of updates to the syntax of Certificate Management Protocol (CMP) version 2 and its HTTP transfer mechanism. This document updates RFCs 4210, 5912, and 6712.

The aspects of CMP updated in this document are using EnvelopedData instead of EncryptedValue, clarifying the handling of p10cr messages, improving the crypto agility, as well as adding new general message types, extended key usages to identify certificates for use with CMP, and well-known URI path segments.

CMP version 3 is introduced to enable signaling support of EnvelopedData instead of EncryptedValue and signal the use of an explicit hash AlgorithmIdentifier in certConf messages, as far as needed.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamelH. Brockhaus¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemD. von Oheimb¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnamegJ. Grayddatej÷Àhfilenamegrfc9480eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-131268hkeywordsÅcCMPkobsoletedByÇd9810d9811epages7opublicationDateeG∏,granking%crfcd9480irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Certificate Management Protocol (CMP) UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁExisting traffic-engineering-related link attribute advertisements have been defined and are used in RSVP-TE deployments. Since the original RSVP-TE use case was defined, additional applications (e.g., Segment Routing Policy and Loop-Free Alternates) that also make use of the link attribute advertisements have been defined. In cases where multiple applications wish to make use of these link attributes, the current advertisements do not support application-specific values for a given attribute, nor do they support an indication of which applications are using the advertised value for a given link. This document introduces link attribute advertisements that address both of these shortcomings.

This document obsoletes RFC 8919.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drakeddatej–Ωhfilenamegrfc9479eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131267hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee%ﬁågranking%crfcd9479irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*IS-IS Application-Specific Link AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document defines a new Traffic Selector Type (TS Type) for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) to add support for negotiating Mandatory Access Control (MAC) security labels as a Traffic Selector of the Security Policy Database (SPD).  Security Labels for IPsec are also known as "Labeled IPsec".  The new TS Type, TS_SECLABEL, consists of a variable length opaque field that specifies the security label.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAivendnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnameiS. Prasadddatej÷‰hfilenamegrfc9478eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131266hkeywordsÉeIKEv2cSPDcSADkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee%Ægranking%crfcd9478irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Labeled IPsec Traffic Selector Support for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¸This document describes a method that allows a Message Originator to specify a Complaint Feedback Loop (CFBL) address as a message header field. It also defines the rules for processing and forwarding such a complaint. The motivation for this arises out of the absence of a standardized and automated way to provide Mailbox Providers with an address for a CFBL. Currently, providing and maintaining such an address is a manual and time-consuming process for Message Originators and Mailbox Providers.

The mechanism specified in this document is being published as an experiment to gather feedback and gauge the interest of implementers and deployers. This document is produced through the Independent RFC Stream and was not subject to the IETF's approval process.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCleverReach GmbH & Co. KGdnamejJ. Beneckeddatej–ºhfilenamegrfc9477eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131265hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee·granking%crfcd9477irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&Complaint Feedback Loop Address HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∞This document reserves a Top-Level Domain (TLD) label "alt" to be used in non-DNS contexts.  It also provides advice and guidance to developers creating alternative namespaces.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej–∫hfilenamegrfc9476eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131264hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeÜÓgranking%crfcd9476irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%The .alt Special-Use Top-Level DomaindtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytSecure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) provides a means of attesting the identity of a telephone caller via a signed token in order to prevent impersonation of a calling party number, which is a key enabler for illegal robocalling.  Similar impersonation is sometimes leveraged by bad actors in the text and multimedia messaging space.  This document explores the applicability of STIR's Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT) and certificate issuance framework to text and multimedia messaging use cases, including support for both messages carried as a payload in SIP requests and messages sent in sessions negotiated by SIP.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationeSomosdnamehC. Wendtddatej÷Íhfilenamegrfc9475eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-131734hkeywordsÅcSIPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateeÇQøgranking%crfcd9475irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQMessaging Use Cases and Extensions for Secure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty6This document specifies an RSA-based blind signature protocol. RSA blind signatures were first introduced by Chaum for untraceable payments. A signature that is output from this protocol can be verified as an RSA-PSS signature.

This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkFastly Inc.dnamehF. Denis¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnameiF. Jacobs¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej÷Áhfilenamegrfc9474eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-131263hkeywordsÉgRSABSSAcRSAoblind signaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee%ÎÚgranking%crfcd9474irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitletRSA Blind SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty$Path properties express information about paths across a network and the services provided via such paths.  In a path-aware network, path properties may be fully or partially available to entities such as endpoints.  This document defines and categorizes path properties.  Furthermore, the document identifies several path properties that might be useful to endpoints or other entities, e.g., for selecting between paths or for invoking some of the provided services.  This document is a product of the Path Aware Networking Research Group (PANRG).gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamekR. Enghardt¢kaffiliationkETH Z√ºrichdnameoC. Kr√§henb√ºhlddatej÷‰hfilenamegrfc9473eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepanrgdfullx panrg - Path Aware Networking RGdnamexPath Aware Networking RGdtypebrgbidjdoc-131262hkeywordsÑcPANrpath-aware networkmpath propertynpath selectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee¸ granking%crfcd9473irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexA Vocabulary of Path PropertiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïTo improve cybersecurity posture, automation is necessary to locate the software a device is using, whether that software has known vulnerabilities, and what, if any, recommendations suppliers may have.  This memo extends the Manufacturer User Description (MUD) YANG schema to provide the locations of software bills of materials (SBOMs) and vulnerability information by introducing a transparency schema.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. Roseddatej–∫hfilenamegrfc9472eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131261hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee%îªgranking%␀crfcd9472irfcNumber%␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaA YANG Data Model for Reporting Software Bills of Materials (SBOMs) and Vulnerability InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThe DNS uses glue records to allow iterative clients to find the addresses of name servers that are contained within a delegated zone.  Authoritative servers are expected to return all available glue records for in-domain name servers in a referral response.  If message size constraints prevent the inclusion of all glue records for in-domain name servers, the server must set the TC (Truncated) flag to inform the client that the response is incomplete and that the client should use another transport to retrieve the full response.  This document updates RFC 1034 to clarify correct server behavior.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejM. Andrews¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamehS. Huque¢kaffiliationeAivendnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejD. Wesselsddatej÷Êhfilenamegrfc9471eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131260hkeywordsÉkGlue RecorduIn-Domain Name ServerxSibling Domain Name ServerkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateeDÜgranking$ˇcrfcd9471irfcNumber$ˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+DNS Glue Requirements in Referral ResponsesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQIt is not uncommon for resource servers to require different authentication strengths or recentness according to the characteristics of a request.  This document introduces a mechanism that resource servers can use to signal to a client that the authentication event associated with the access token of the current request does not meet its authentication requirements and, further, how to meet them.  This document also codifies a mechanism for a client to request that an authorization server achieve a specific authentication strength or recentness when processing an authorization request.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjAuth0/OktadnamekV. Bertocci¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbellddatej÷Ëhfilenamegrfc9470eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131259hkeywordsÖhsecurityfoauth2nopenid connecteoauthgstep-upkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated˚O{granking$˛crfcd9470irfcNumber$˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3OAuth 2.0 Step Up Authentication Challenge ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇAn Ethernet Virtual Private Network (EVPN) provides a unified control plane that solves the issues of Network Virtualization Edge (NVE) auto-discovery, tenant Media Access Control (MAC) / IP dissemination, and advanced features in a scablable way as required by Network Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3) networks.  EVPN is a scalable solution for NVO3 networks and keeps the independence of the underlay IP Fabric, i.e., there is no need to enable Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) in the underlay network and maintain multicast states for tenant Broadcast Domains.  This document describes the use of EVPN for NVO3 networks and discusses its applicability to basic Layer 2 and Layer 3 connectivity requirements and to advanced features such as MAC Mobility, MAC Protection and Loop Protection, multihoming, Data Center Interconnect (DCI), and much more.  No new EVPN procedures are introduced.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationeCienadnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassiddatej÷‚hfilenamegrfc9469eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-131258hkeywordsÉdEVPNmApplicabilitydNVO3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeéúgranking$˝crfcd9469irfcNumber$˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoApplicability of Ethernet Virtual Private Network (EVPN) to Network Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòFor operational simplification of "sessionless" applications using Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD), in this document, we present procedures for "unsolicited BFD" that allow a BFD session to be initiated by only one side and established without explicit per-session configuration or registration by the other side (subject to certain per-interface or global policies).

We also introduce a new YANG module to configure and manage "unsolicited BFD". The YANG module in this document is based on YANG 1.1, as defined in RFC 7950, and conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA), as described in RFC 8342. This document augments RFC 9314.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrPalo Alto NetworksdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationfZededadnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationgEquinixdnameiR. Rahmanddatej–πhfilenamegrfc9468eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-131257hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÒjugranking$¸crfcd9468irfcNumber$¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQUnsolicited Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for Sessionless ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis document relaxes the packet verification rules defined in "MAC Authentication for the Babel Routing Protocol" (RFC 8967) in order to make the protocol more robust in the presence of packet reordering.  This document updates RFC 8967.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxIRIF University of Paris-Cit√©dnamemJ. Chroboczek¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamevT. H√∏iland-J√∏rgensenddatej÷Áhfilenamegrfc9467eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131923hkeywordsÜimulticastewi-fihsecuritynauthenticationqpacket reorderingdwifikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee¶ıTgranking$˚crfcd9467irfcNumber$˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Relaxed Packet Counter Verification for Babel MAC AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥When PIM Sparse Mode (PIM-SM), including PIM Source-Specific Multicast (PIM-SSM), is used in shared LAN networks, there is often more than one upstream router. This can lead to duplicate IP multicast packets being forwarded by these PIM routers. PIM Assert messages are used to elect a single forwarder for each IP multicast traffic flow between these routers.

This document defines a mechanism to send and receive information for multiple IP multicast flows in a single PackedAssert message. This optimization reduces the total number of PIM packets on the LAN and can therefore speed up the election of the single forwarder, reducing the number of duplicate IP multicast packets incurred.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefY. Liu¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamejM. McBride¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamehZ. Zhangddatej–¥hfilenamegrfc9466eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-131256hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee)—Ágranking$˙crfcd9466irfcNumber$˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPIM Assert Message PackingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóIn PIM Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) networks, PIM Null-Register messages are sent by the Designated Router (DR) to the Rendezvous Point (RP) to signal the presence of multicast sources in the network. There are periodic PIM Null-Registers sent from the DR to the RP to keep the state alive at the RP as long as the source is active. The PIM Null-Register message carries information about a single multicast source and group.

This document defines a standard to send information about multiple multicast sources and groups in a single PIM message. This document refers to the new messages as the "PIM Packed Null-Register message" and "PIM Packed Register-Stop message".fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfVMwarednameiV. Kamath¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamexR. Chokkanathapuram Sundaram¢kaffiliationfApstradnamejR. Banthia¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehA. Gopalddatej÷ﬂhfilenamegrfc9465eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-131255hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatee(granking$˘crfcd9465irfcNumber$˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPIM Null-Register PackingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚This document specifies new Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Configuration Payload Attribute Types to assign DNS resolvers that support encrypted DNS protocols, such as DNS over HTTPS (DoH), DNS over TLS (DoT), and DNS over QUIC (DoQ).fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationtCloud Software GroupdnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej÷„hfilenamegrfc9464eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131254hkeywordsêhsecurityoname resolutionjencryptionqservice discoveryfnaminghresolvermstub-resolvercCPExCustomer premise equipmentcVPNpSecure discoverycDoTcDoHcDoQfTunnellconnectivitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeIa˝granking$¯crfcd9464irfcNumber$¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPInternet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Configuration for Encrypted DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document specifies new DHCP and IPv6 Router Advertisement options to discover encrypted DNS resolvers (e.g., DNS over HTTPS, DNS over TLS, and DNS over QUIC).  Particularly, it allows a host to learn an Authentication Domain Name together with a list of IP addresses and a set of service parameters to reach such encrypted DNS resolvers.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationtCloud Software GroupdnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationlOpen-XchangednamegN. Cook¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiT. Jensenddatej÷·hfilenamegrfc9463eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcadddfullxadd - Adaptive DNS DiscoverydnamevAdaptive DNS Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-131253hkeywordsäcDNSrservice resolutionjencryptionqservice discoverypservice providerpnetwork providerjautomationcDoHcDoTcDoQkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeIa¸granking$˜crfcd9463irfcNumber$˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]DHCP and Router Advertisement Options for the Discovery of Network-designated Resolvers (DNR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document defines Discovery of Designated Resolvers (DDR), a set of mechanisms for DNS clients to use DNS records to discover a resolver's encrypted DNS configuration.  An Encrypted DNS Resolver discovered in this manner is referred to as a "Designated Resolver".  These mechanisms can be used to move from unencrypted DNS to encrypted DNS when only the IP address of a resolver is known.  These mechanisms are designed to be limited to cases where Unencrypted DNS Resolvers and their Designated Resolvers are operated by the same entity or cooperating entities.  It can also be used to discover support for encrypted DNS protocols when the name of an Encrypted DNS Resolver is known.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamejE. Kinnear¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Wood¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamejP. McManus¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiT. Jensenddatej÷Õhfilenamegrfc9462eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcadddfullxadd - Adaptive DNS DiscoverydnamevAdaptive DNS Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-131252hkeywordsÑcDNScDoHcDoTcDoQkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeIa¸granking$ˆcrfcd9462irfcNumber$ˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Discovery of Designated ResolversdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThe SVCB DNS resource record type expresses a bound collection of endpoint metadata, for use when establishing a connection to a named service.  DNS itself can be such a service, when the server is identified by a domain name.  This document provides the SVCB mapping for named DNS servers, allowing them to indicate support for encrypted transport protocols.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtMeta Platforms, Inc.dnamekB. Schwartzddatej÷·hfilenamegrfc9461eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcadddfullxadd - Adaptive DNS DiscoverydnamevAdaptive DNS Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-131251hkeywordsÖcDoHcDoTcDoQcDDRcDNRkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateeIa¸granking$ıcrfcd9461irfcNumber$ıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Service Binding Mapping for DNS ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the "SVCB" ("Service Binding") and "HTTPS" DNS resource record (RR) types to facilitate the lookup of information needed to make connections to network services, such as for HTTP origins.  SVCB records allow a service to be provided from multiple alternative endpoints, each with associated parameters (such as transport protocol configuration), and are extensible to support future uses (such as keys for encrypting the TLS ClientHello).  They also enable aliasing of apex domains, which is not possible with CNAME.  The HTTPS RR is a variation of SVCB for use with HTTP (see RFC 9110, "HTTP Semantics").  By providing more information to the client before it attempts to establish a connection, these records offer potential benefits to both performance and privacy.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtMeta Platforms, Inc.dnamekB. Schwartz¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnameiM. Bishop¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnameiE. Nygrenddatej÷ƒhfilenamegrfc9460eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131250hkeywordsãimulti-CDNdHSTScECHeCNAMEdapexdALPNfHTTP/3ealiashSvcParamiAliasModekServiceModekobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateeIaˆgranking$Ùcrfcd9460irfcNumber$ÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYService Binding and Parameter Specification via the DNS (SVCB and HTTPS Resource Records)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThe Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) data format is designed for small code size and small message size.  CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) is specified in RFC 9052 to provide basic security services using the CBOR data format.  This document specifies the conventions for using AES-CTR and AES-CBC as content encryption algorithms with COSE.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej÷Âhfilenamegrfc9459eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-131249hkeywordsÇoCOSE EncryptionsFirmware encryptionkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateeÄgranking$Ûcrfcd9459irfcNumber$ÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE): AES-CTR and AES-CBCdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis document describes Oblivious HTTP, a protocol for forwarding encrypted HTTP messages.  Oblivious HTTP allows a client to make multiple requests to an origin server without that server being able to link those requests to the client or to identify the requests as having come from the same client, while placing only limited trust in the nodes used to forward the messages.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej÷ﬁhfilenamegrfc9458eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdohaidfullx0ohai - Oblivious HTTP Application Intermediationdnamex)Oblivious HTTP Application Intermediationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131887hkeywordsÑgprivacyeproxylpartitioningftunnelkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDatee°‹µgranking$Úcrfcd9458irfcNumber$ÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlenOblivious HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx÷This document defines a "problem detail" to carry machine-readable details of errors in HTTP response content to avoid the need to define new error response formats for HTTP APIs.

This document obsoletes RFC 7807.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. Wilde¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Dalalddatej÷‚hfilenamegrfc9457eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpapidfullx'httpapi - Building Blocks for HTTP APIsdnamexBuilding Blocks for HTTP APIsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131248hkeywordsáfstatusdHTTPeerrorgproblemcAPIdJSONcXMLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated»å,granking$Òcrfcd9457irfcNumber$ÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexProblem Details for HTTP APIsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙This document updates RFC 6353 ("Transport Layer Security (TLS) Transport Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)") to reflect changes necessary to support Transport Layer Security version 1.3 (TLS 1.3) and Datagram Transport Layer Security version 1.3 (DTLS 1.3), which are jointly known as "(D)TLS 1.3". This document is compatible with (D)TLS 1.2 and is intended to be compatible with future versions of SNMP and (D)TLS.

This document updates the SNMP-TLS-TM-MIB as defined in RFC 6353.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlTrevilon LLCdnameiK. Vaughnddatej÷·hfilenamegrfc9456eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131247hkeywordsÖeTLSTMdDTLShsecurityfSNMPv3cMIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeKf!granking$crfcd9456irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Updates to the TLS Transport Model for SNMPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyâWhen using the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), address space holders need to issue Route Origin Authorization (ROA) object(s) to authorize one or more Autonomous Systems (ASes) to originate BGP routes to IP address prefix(es).  This memo discusses operational problems that may arise from ROAs containing multiple IP prefixes and recommends that each ROA contain a single IP prefix.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefZ. Yan¢kaffiliationxIIJ Research Lab & Arrcus, Inc.dnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationpJinan UniversitydnamegG. Geng¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamejT. de Kock¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Yaoddatej÷„hfilenamegrfc9455eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131246hkeywordsÇcROAxRoute Origin AuthorizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÊæÊgranking$Ôcrfcd9455irfcNumber$ÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÓetotaletitlexKAvoiding Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs) Containing Multiple IP PrefixesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document updates some OSPF terminology to be in line with inclusive language used in the industry. The IETF has designated "Guidance for NIST Staff on Using Inclusive Language in Documentary Standards" by the US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) for its inclusive language guidelines. It is intended that all future OSPF documents use this revised terminology even when they reference the RFCs updated by this document.

This document updates RFCs 2328, 4222, 4811, 5243, 5340, 5614, and 5838.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamefM. Fox¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies, Inc.dnameiA. Retanaddatej÷„hfilenamegrfc9454eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131245hkeywordsÅpOSPF terminologykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated›?Ìgranking$Ócrfcd9454irfcNumber$ÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUpdate to OSPF TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the applicability of IPv6 over constrained-node networks (6lo) and provides practical deployment examples.  In addition to IEEE Std 802.15.4, various link-layer technologies are used as examples, such as ITU-T G.9959 (Z-Wave), Bluetooth Low Energy (Bluetooth LE), Digital Enhanced Cordless Telecommunications - Ultra Low Energy (DECT-ULE), Master-Slave/Token Passing (MS/TP), Near Field Communication (NFC), and Power Line Communication (PLC).  This document targets an audience who would like to understand and evaluate running end-to-end IPv6 over the constrained-node networks for local or Internet connectivity.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrDaejeon UniversitydnamegY. Hong¢kaffiliationcUPCdnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegY. Choi¢kaffiliationwWenzhou-Kean UniversitydnamehA. Sangi¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamenS. Chakrabartiddatej–∂hfilenamegrfc9453eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-131244hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeû@granking$Ìcrfcd9453irfcNumber$ÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVApplicability and Use Cases for IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodes (6lo)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/In situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM) is used for recording and collecting operational and telemetry information while the packet traverses a path between two points in the network.  This document outlines how IOAM-Data-Fields are encapsulated with the Network Service Header (NSH).fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Brockners¢kaffiliationkThoughtspotdnamekS. Bhandariddatej÷„hfilenamegrfc9452eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131243hkeywordsÑfinbandgIn-situiTelemetrygTracingkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatedÊΩ8granking$Ïcrfcd9452irfcNumber$ÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFNetwork Service Header (NSH) Encapsulation for In Situ OAM (IOAM) DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document clarifies an ambiguity in the Network Service Header (NSH) specification related to the handling of O bit. In particular, this document clarifies the meaning of "OAM packet".

This document updates RFC 8300.fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷·hfilenamegrfc9451eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131242hkeywordsájDiagnosticoTroubelshootingxService Function ChainingjAutomationcSDNuProgrammable NetworkswService DifferentiationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated÷‡¨granking$Îcrfcd9451irfcNumber$ÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiOperations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Packet and Behavior in the Network Service Header (NSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(The wireless medium presents significant specific challenges to achieve properties similar to those of wired deterministic networks.  At the same time, a number of use cases cannot be solved with wires and justify the extra effort of going wireless.  This document presents wireless use cases (such as aeronautical communications, amusement parks, industrial applications, pro audio and video, gaming, Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) and vehicle-to-vehicle (V2V) control, edge robotics, and emergency vehicles), demanding reliable and available behavior.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationnIMT AtlantiquednameoG. Papadopoulos¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationdCNRSdnamelF. Theoleyreddatej÷·hfilenamegrfc9450eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrawdfullx%raw - Reliable and Available WirelessdnamexReliable and Available Wirelessdtypebwgbidjdoc-131241hkeywordsÑkdeterminismlavailabilitygroutingdpathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÊ∑|granking$Ícrfcd9450irfcNumber$ÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Reliable and Available Wireless (RAW) Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÂThis document describes a mechanism for sender-constraining OAuth 2.0 tokens via a proof-of-possession mechanism on the application level.  This mechanism allows for the detection of replay attacks with access and refresh tokens.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhAuthletednamegD. Fett¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationfYubicodnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationgTuconicdnamenT. Lodderstedt¢kaffiliationvSelf-Issued ConsultingdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamehD. Waiteddatej÷‚hfilenamegrfc9449eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131240hkeywordsÇhsecurityfoauth2kobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDated˙_,granking$Ècrfcd9449irfcNumber$ÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2OAuth 2.0 Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document defines a profile of the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) Authority Token for the automated and authorized creation of certificates for Voice over IP (VoIP) telephone providers to support Secure Telephone Identity (STI) using the TNAuthList defined by STI certificates.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjSomos Inc.dnamehC. Wendt¢kaffiliationjSomos Inc.dnamejD. Hancock¢kaffiliationlNeustar Inc.dnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationlNeustar Inc.dnamekJ. Petersonddatej÷‹hfilenamegrfc9448eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-131239hkeywordsÅdSTIRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee9ügranking$Ëcrfcd9448irfcNumber$ËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYTNAuthList Profile of Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) Authority TokendtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§Some proposed extensions to the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) rely on proving eligibility for certificates through consulting an external authority that issues a token according to a particular policy.  This document specifies a generic Authority Token Challenge for ACME that supports subtype claims for different identifiers or namespaces that can be defined separately for specific applications.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationgNeustardnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationeSomosdnamejD. Hancock¢kaffiliationeSomosdnamehC. Wendtddatej÷πhfilenamegrfc9447eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-131238hkeywordsÅdACMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee9ögranking$Ácrfcd9447irfcNumber$ÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWAutomated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) Challenges Using an Authority TokendtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÎThis memo contains the thoughts and recountings of events that transpired during and after the release of information about the United States National Security Agency (NSA) by Edward Snowden in 2013.  There are four perspectives: that of someone who was involved with sifting through the information to responsibly inform the public, that of a security area director of the IETF, that of a human rights expert, and that of a computer science and affiliate law professor.  The purpose of this memo is to provide some historical perspective, while at the same time offering a view as to what security and privacy challenges the technical community should consider.  These essays do not represent a consensus view, but that of the individual authors.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationnDigital MedusadnamehF. Badii¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamekB. Schneier¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenS. M. Bellovinddatej÷Áhfilenamegrfc9446eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131237hkeywordsÉtpervasive monitoringgprivacyhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedπØ#granking$Êcrfcd9446irfcNumber$ÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex5Reflections on Ten Years Past the Snowden RevelationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis document specifies two new Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) attributes that carry DHCP options. The specification is generic and can be applicable to any service that relies upon DHCP. Both DHCPv4- and DHCPv6-configured services are covered.

Also, this document updates RFC 4014 by relaxing a constraint on permitted RADIUS attributes in the RADIUS Attributes DHCP suboption.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatej÷ﬂhfilenamegrfc9445eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131236hkeywordsékredirectionssubscriber policiesvdifferentiated servicecDNScDoHcDoTcDoQdQUICjEncryptionpService deliverytService provisioningrservice activationhpolicieslconnectivitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated›BNgranking$Âcrfcd9445irfcNumber$ÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.RADIUS Extensions for DHCP-Configured ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„This document specifies how Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) can be used by a client to obtain a certificate for a subdomain identifier from a certification authority.  Additionally, this document specifies how a client can fulfill a challenge against an ancestor domain but may not need to fulfill a challenge against the explicit subdomain if certification authority policy allows issuance of the subdomain certificate without explicit subdomain ownership proof.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Friel¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationhDigiCertdnamelT. Hollebeek¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardsonddatej–≥hfilenamegrfc9444eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-131235hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÊØ(granking$‰crfcd9444irfcNumber$‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBAutomated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) for SubdomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËRFC 7983 defines a scheme for a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) receiver to demultiplex Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS), Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN), Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) / Secure Real-time Transport Control Protocol (SRTCP), ZRTP, and Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) channel packets arriving on a single port.  This document updates RFC 7983 and RFC 5764 to also allow QUIC packets to be multiplexed on a single receiving socket.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej÷‡hfilenamegrfc9443eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-131234hkeywordsÖcRTPdZRTPdSTUNdTURNdDTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÆÏcgranking$„crfcd9443irfcNumber$„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Multiplexing Scheme Updates for QUICdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThe Static Context Header Compression (SCHC) and fragmentation specification (RFC 8724) describes a generic framework for application header compression and fragmentation modes designed for Low-Power Wide Area Network (LPWAN) technologies.  This document defines a profile of SCHC over Sigfox LPWAN and provides optimal parameter values and modes of operation.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Z√∫√±iga¢kaffiliationx%Universitat Polit√®cnica de CatalunyadnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationx%Universitat Polit√®cnica de CatalunyadnamejS. Aguilar¢kaffiliationnIMT-AtlantiquednamejL. Toutain¢kaffiliationtConcordia UniversitydnamelS. C√©spedes¢kaffiliationxNIC Labs / Universidad de ChilednamejD. Wistuba¢kaffiliationoUnabiz (Sigfox)dnamehJ. Boiteddatej÷‹hfilenamegrfc9442eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelpwandfullx.lpwan - IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdnamex&IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-131233hkeywordsâcIoTfSigfoxdSCHCeLPWANmfragmentationqReliable DeliverytLink Layer ProtocolsuCross-Layer ProtocolspAdaptation LayerkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDated¡«àgranking$‚crfcd9442irfcNumber$‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXStatic Context Header Compression (SCHC) over Sigfox Low-Power Wide Area Network (LPWAN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹This document updates the Static Context Header Compression (SCHC) and fragmentation protocol (RFC 8724) and the corresponding YANG module (RFC 9363). It defines a SCHC Compound Acknowledgement (ACK) message format and procedure, which are intended to reduce the number of response transmissions (i.e., SCHC ACKs) in the ACK-on-Error Mode, by accumulating bitmaps of several windows in a single SCHC message (i.e., the SCHC Compound ACK).

Both the message format and procedure are generic, so they can be used, for instance, by any of the four Low-Power Wide Area Network (LPWAN) technologies defined in RFC 8376, which are Sigfox, Long Range Wide Area Network (LoRaWAN), Narrowband Internet of Things (NB-IoT), and IEEE 802.15.4w.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekJ. Z√∫√±iga¢kaffiliationx%Universitat Polit√®cnica de CatalunyadnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationx%Universitat Polit√®cnica de CatalunyadnamejS. Aguilar¢kaffiliationnIMT-AtlantiquednamejL. Toutain¢kaffiliationtConcordia UniversitydnamelS. C√©spedes¢kaffiliationxNIC Labs / Universidad de ChilednamejD. Wistubaddatej÷ﬂhfilenamegrfc9441eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelpwandfullx.lpwan - IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdnamex&IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-131232hkeywordsçdSCHCeLPWANcIoTmFragmentationqReliable DeliverytLink Layer ProtocolsuCross-Layer ProtocolspAdaptation LayernAcknowledgmentfSigfoxgLoRaWANfNB-IoTlCompound ACKkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated¡«Égranking$·crfcd9441irfcNumber$·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGStatic Context Header Compression (SCHC) Compound Acknowledgement (ACK)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˜This document describes HTTP extension header fields that allow a TLS terminating reverse proxy (TTRP) to convey the client certificate information of a mutually authenticated TLS connection to the origin server in a common and predictable manner.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnameiM. Bishopddatej÷ﬂhfilenamegrfc9440eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131231hkeywordsÇdhttprclient certificatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedªÄ“granking$‡crfcd9440irfcNumber$‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexClient-Cert HTTP Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸The cost metric is a basic concept in Application-Layer Traffic
Optimization (ALTO), and different applications may use different
types of cost metrics. Since the ALTO base protocol (RFC 7285)
defines only a single cost metric (namely, the generic "routingcost"
metric), if an application wants to issue a cost map or an endpoint
cost request in order to identify a resource provider that offers
better performance metrics (e.g., lower delay or loss rate), the base
protocol does not define the cost metric to be used.

This document addresses this issue by extending the specification to
provide a variety of network performance metrics, including network
delay, delay variation (a.k.a. jitter), packet loss rate, hop count,
and bandwidth.

There are multiple sources (e.g., estimations based on measurements
or a Service Level Agreement) available for deriving a performance
metric. This document introduces an additional "cost-context" field
to the ALTO "cost-type" field to convey the source of a performance
metric.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationuNokia Networks FrancednamenS. Randriamasy¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamelL. Contrerasddatej÷Ωhfilenamegrfc9439eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131230hkeywordsÇxJavaScript Object Notationx&Application-Layer Traffic OptimizationkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDated◊granking$ﬂcrfcd9439irfcNumber$ﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFApplication-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Performance Cost MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕCUBIC is a standard TCP congestion control algorithm that uses a cubic function instead of a linear congestion window increase function to improve scalability and stability over fast and long-distance networks. CUBIC has been adopted as the default TCP congestion control algorithm by the Linux, Windows, and Apple stacks.

This document updates the specification of CUBIC to include algorithmic improvements based on these implementations and recent academic work. Based on the extensive deployment experience with CUBIC, this document also moves the specification to the Standards Track and obsoletes RFC 8312. This document also updates RFC 5681, to allow for CUBIC's occasionally more aggressive sending behavior.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcUNLdnameeL. Xu¢kaffiliationhColoradodnameeS. Ha¢kaffiliationfBowerydnamegI. Rhee¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamegV. Goel¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggertddatej÷⁄hfilenamegrfc9438eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131229hkeywordsÑrCongestion controlilarge BDPrwindow scalabilitylRTT fairnesskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated◊`granking$ﬁcrfcd9438irfcNumber$ﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)CUBIC for Fast and Long-Distance NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxèThis document specifies an extension to the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) control plane to enable Publish/Subscribe (PubSub) operation.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamerA. Rodriguez-Natal¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejV. Ermagan¢kaffiliationqUPC/BarcelonaTechdnamekA. Cabellos¢kaffiliationeNexardnameiS. Barkai¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷ﬁhfilenamegrfc9437eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131228hkeywordsÜdlispgpublishisubscribelsubscriptioncsdncnfvkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÕwpgranking$›crfcd9437irfcNumber$›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMPublish/Subscribe Functionality for the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe PIM version 2 messages share a common message header format. The common header definition contains eight reserved bits. This document specifies how these bits may be used by individual message types and extends the PIM type space.

This document updates RFCs 7761 and 3973 by defining the use of the Reserved field in the PIM common header. This document further updates RFCs 7761 and 3973, along with RFCs 5015, 5059, 6754, and 8364, by specifying the use of the bits for each PIM message.

This document obsoletes RFC 8736.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies, Inc.dnameiA. Retanaddatej÷Âhfilenamegrfc9436eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-131227hkeywordsÅmMulticast PIMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated˚Cgranking$‹crfcd9436irfcNumber$‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2PIM Message Type Space Extension and Reserved BitsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOThis document discusses considerations for assigning a new recommended Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) for a standard Per-Hop Behavior (PHB).  It considers the common observed re-marking behaviors that the Diffserv field might be subjected to along an Internet path.  It also notes some implications of using a specific DSCP.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamejA. Custura¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnameiR. Secchiddatej÷øhfilenamegrfc9435eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131226hkeywordsÇdDSCPsDiffServ CodepointskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedπºÖgranking$€crfcd9435irfcNumber$€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXConsiderations for Assigning a New Recommended Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis document describes an architecture for protocols and services to support Unmanned Aircraft System Remote Identification and tracking (UAS RID), plus UAS-RID-related communications.  This architecture adheres to the requirements listed in the Drone Remote Identification Protocol (DRIP) Requirements document (RFC 9153).fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmAX EnterprizednamegS. Card¢kaffiliationmAX EnterprizednameoA. Wiethuechter¢kaffiliationnHTT ConsultingdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationeInteldnamegS. Zhao¢kaffiliationuLink√∂ping UniversitydnameiA. Gurtovddatej÷“hfilenamegrfc9434eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddripdfullxdrip - Drone Remote ID ProtocoldnamexDrone Remote ID Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131225hkeywordsÑgUAS RIDeF3411dDRIPedronekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated∂$Egranking$⁄crfcd9434irfcNumber$⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Drone Remote Identification Protocol (DRIP) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis document discusses the applicability of Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) to the user plane of mobile networks. The network programming nature of SRv6 accomplishes mobile user-plane functions in a simple manner. The statelessness of SRv6 and its ability to control both service layer path and underlying transport can be beneficial to the mobile user plane, providing flexibility, end-to-end network slicing, and Service Level Agreement (SLA) control for various applications.

This document discusses how SRv6 could be used as the user plane of mobile networks. This document also specifies the SRv6 Endpoint Behaviors required for mobility use cases.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhSoftBankdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Kohno¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelP. Camarillo¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyerddatej÷›hfilenamegrfc9433eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-131224hkeywordsÖmSRv6 mobilitycMUPdCMUPisrv6 3gppcGTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedª¥granking$Ÿcrfcd9433irfcNumber$ŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Segment Routing over IPv6 for the Mobile User PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx÷This document describes a method for automatic DNS zone provisioning among DNS primary and secondary name servers by storing and transferring the catalog of zones to be provisioned as one or more regular DNS zones.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationhPowerDNSdnamekP. van Dijk¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnameiL. Peltan¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamehO. Sur√Ω¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnameiW. Toorop¢kaffiliation`dnameoC.R. Monshouwer¢kaffiliationx(deSEC / SSE - Secure Systems EngineeringdnamelP. Thomassen¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamekA. Sargsyanddatej–±hfilenamegrfc9432eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131223hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedßQgranking$ÿcrfcd9432irfcNumber$ÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqDNS Catalog ZonesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑This document specifies a profile for the Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) framework to enable authorization in a publish-subscribe messaging system based on Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT).  Proof-of-Possession keys, bound to OAuth 2.0 access tokens, are used to authenticate and authorize MQTT Clients.  The protocol relies on TLS for confidentiality and MQTT server (Broker) authentication.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqBrunel UniversitydnameiC. Sengul¢kaffiliationhOxboticadnamehA. Kirbyddatej÷¿hfilenamegrfc9431eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131222hkeywordsÇqpublish-subscribex authorization information formatkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatedÆ¬granking$◊crfcd9431irfcNumber$◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex¶Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) and Transport Layer Security (TLS) Profile of Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx⁄This document updates "Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Profile for Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE)" (RFC 9202) by specifying that the profile applies to TLS as well as DTLS.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcTZIdnamekO. Bergmann¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Preu√ü Mattsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Selanderddatej÷ﬁhfilenamegrfc9430eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131221hkeywordsÖxInternet of Things (IoT)rInternet of ThingscIOTeOAuthlAccess TokenkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÆΩgranking$÷crfcd9430irfcNumber$÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex´Extension of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Profile for Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) to Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document describes the mechanisms for allowing a JavaScript application to control the signaling plane of a multimedia session via the interface specified in the W3C RTCPeerConnection API and discusses how this relates to existing signaling protocols.

This specification obsoletes RFC 8829.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationwWindy Hill Systems, LLCdnamekE. Rescorladdatej÷ÿhfilenamegrfc9429eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-135573hkeywordsäfwebrtccsdpknegotiationisignalingnpeerconnectioncapicicecrtpeofferfanswerkobsoletedByÄepagesVopublicationDatef:Ggranking$’crfcd9429irfcNumber$’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0JavaScript Session Establishment Protocol (JSEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈNear Field Communication (NFC) is a set of standards for smartphones and portable devices to establish radio communication with each other by touching them together or bringing them into proximity, usually no more than 10 cm apart.  NFC standards cover communication protocols and data exchange formats and are based on existing Radio Frequency Identification (RFID) standards, including ISO/IEC 14443 and FeliCa.  The standards include ISO/IEC 18092 and those defined by the NFC Forum.  The NFC technology has been widely implemented and available in mobile phones, laptop computers, and many other devices.  This document describes how IPv6 is transmitted over NFC using IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) techniques.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegY. Choi¢kaffiliationkDaejon UnivdnameiY-G. Hong¢kaffiliationlDongeui UnivdnameiJ-S. Younddatej÷ﬁhfilenamegrfc9428eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-131220hkeywordsáxNear Field CommunicationcNFCg6LowPANdIPv6pAdaptation LayercIoTrInternet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedπ√ﬁgranking$‘crfcd9428irfcNumber$‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Transmission of IPv6 Packets over Near Field CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThe Extensible Authentication Protocol-TLS (EAP-TLS) (RFC 5216) has been updated for TLS 1.3 in RFC 9190.  Many other EAP Types also depend on TLS, such as EAP-Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling (EAP-FAST) (RFC 4851), EAP-Tunneled TLS (EAP-TTLS) (RFC 5281), the Tunnel Extensible Authentication Protocol (TEAP) (RFC 7170).  It is possible that many vendor-specific EAP methods, such as the Protected Extensible Authentication Protocol (PEAP), depend on TLS as well.  This document updates those methods in order to use the new key derivation methods available in TLS 1.3.  Additional changes necessitated by TLS 1.3 are also discussed.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatej÷›hfilenamegrfc9427eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-131219hkeywordsÉdTTLSjPEAP. FASTdTEAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedõõ=granking$”crfcd9427irfcNumber$”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMTLS-Based Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Types for Use with TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9930¥habstractyvIn general, linear network coding can improve the network communication performance in terms of throughput, latency, and reliability.  BATched Sparse (BATS) code is a class of efficient linear network coding scheme with a matrix generalization of fountain codes as the outer code and batch-based linear network coding as the inner code.  This document describes a baseline BATS coding scheme for communication through multi-hop networks and discusses the related research issues towards a more sophisticated BATS coding scheme.  This document is a product of the Coding for Efficient Network Communications Research Group (NWCRG).gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxCUHK(SZ) & n-hop technologiesdnamegS. Yang¢kaffiliationdCUHKdnamehX. Huang¢kaffiliationxCUHK & n-hop technologiesdnamehR. Yeung¢kaffiliationdCUHKdnamefJ. Zaoddatej÷›hfilenamegrfc9426eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenwcrgdfullxBnwcrg - Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdnamex:Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-131218hkeywordsÇiBATS codeimulti-hopkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedª{␀granking$“crfcd9426irfcNumber$“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex@BATched Sparse (BATS) Coding Scheme for Multi-hop Data TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÉThis document specifies a data model for handling quotas on accounts with a server using the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP).fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpLinagora VietnamdnamejR. Cordierddatej÷‹hfilenamegrfc9425eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131217hkeywordsÑdJMAPdJSONeemailfquotaskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedíë|granking$—crfcd9425irfcNumber$—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) for QuotasdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCyber defenders frequently rely on Indicators of Compromise (IoCs) to identify, trace, and block malicious activity in networks or on endpoints.  This document reviews the fundamentals, opportunities, operational limitations, and recommendations for IoC use.  It highlights the need for IoCs to be detectable in implementations of Internet protocols, tools, and technologies -- both for the IoCs' initial discovery and their use in detection -- and provides a foundation for approaches to operational challenges in network security.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkSplunk Inc.dnamehK. Paine¢kaffiliationnBinary FireflydnamemO. Whitehouse¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Sellwood¢kaffiliationx!UK National Cyber Security CentrednamegA. Shawddatej÷€hfilenamegrfc9424eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-131216hkeywordsÇcIOCnAttack DefencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated◊πgranking$–crfcd9424irfcNumber$–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Indicators of Compromise (IoCs) and Their Role in Attack DefencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Constrained RESTful Environments (CoRE) specifications apply web technologies to constrained environments. One such important technology is Web Linking (RFC 8288), which CoRE specifications use as the basis for a number of discovery protocols, such as the Link Format (RFC 6690) in the Constrained Application Protocol's (CoAP's) resource discovery process (Section 7.2 of RFC 7252) and the Resource Directory (RD) (RFC 9176).

Web Links can have target attributes, the names of which are not generally coordinated by the Web Linking specification (Section 2.2 of RFC 8288). This document introduces an IANA registry for coordinating names of target attributes when used in CoRE. It updates the "RD Parameters" IANA registry created by RFC 9176 to coordinate with this registry.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Íhfilenamegrfc9423eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-135576hkeywordsÉdCoAPkWeb LinkingrResource DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatefVfgranking$œcrfcd9423irfcNumber$œestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBConstrained RESTful Environments (CoRE) Target Attributes RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘This document defines a LIMITS extension for the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP), including submission, as well as the Local Mail Transfer Protocol (LMTP).  It also defines an associated limit registry.  The extension provides the means for an SMTP, submission, or LMTP server to inform the client of limits the server intends to apply to the protocol during the current session.  The client is then able to adapt its behavior in order to conform to those limits.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamehN. Freed¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej÷Úhfilenamegrfc9422eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-132192hkeywordsÖdSMTPdLMTPrMessage SubmissioneESMTPiExtensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateeƒ*granking$Œcrfcd9422irfcNumber$ŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!The LIMITS SMTP Service ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThis document describes a mechanism for creating, encoding, and verifying digital signatures or message authentication codes over components of an HTTP message.  This mechanism supports use cases where the full HTTP message may not be known to the signer and where the message may be transformed (e.g., by intermediaries) before reaching the verifier.  This document also describes a means for requesting that a signature be applied to a subsequent HTTP message in an ongoing HTTP exchange.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfAmazondnamejA. Backman¢kaffiliationsBespoke EngineeringdnameiJ. Richer¢kaffiliationnDigital BazaardnameiM. Spornyddatej÷Îhfilenamegrfc9421eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-132276hkeywordsÅcPKIkobsoletedByÄepages_opublicationDateeÕ†granking$Õcrfcd9421irfcNumber$ÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewHTTP Message SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytMessaging applications are increasingly making use of end-to-end security mechanisms to ensure that messages are only accessible to the communicating endpoints, and not to any servers involved in delivering messages.  Establishing keys to provide such protections is challenging for group chat settings, in which more than two clients need to agree on a key but may not be online at the same time.  In this document, we specify a key establishment protocol that provides efficient asynchronous group key establishment with forward secrecy (FS) and post-compromise security (PCS) for groups in size ranging from two to thousands.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationoInria & MozilladnamemB. Beurdouche¢kaffiliationkPhoenix R&DdnameiR. Robert¢kaffiliationnMeta PlatformsdnamekJ. Millican¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. Omara¢kaffiliationtUniversity of OxforddnamenK. Cohn-Gordonddatej–¥hfilenamegrfc9420eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcmlsdfullxmls - Messaging Layer SecuritydnamexMessaging Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-131215hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesÑopublicationDated∂è¬granking$Ãcrfcd9420irfcNumber$ÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+The Messaging Layer Security (MLS) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyßThis document discusses principles for designing mechanisms that use or provide path signals and calls for standards action in specific valuable cases.  RFC 8558 describes path signals as messages to or from on-path elements and points out that visible information will be used whether or not it is intended as a signal.  The principles in this document are intended as guidance for the design of explicit path signals, which are encouraged to be authenticated and include a minimal set of parties to minimize information sharing.  These principles can be achieved through mechanisms like encryption of information and establishing trust relationships between entities on a path.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiT. Hardie¢kaffiliationeApplednamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewindddatej–§hfilenamegrfc9419eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-131214hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedßI“granking$Àcrfcd9419irfcNumber$ÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexHConsiderations on Application - Network Collaboration Using Path SignalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis document specifies YANG modules for representing assurance graphs. These graphs represent the assurance of a given service by decomposing it into atomic assurance elements called subservices. The companion document, "Service Assurance for Intent-Based Networking Architecture" (RFC 9417), presents an architecture for implementing the assurance of such services.

The YANG data models in this document conform to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) defined in RFC 8342.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekJ. Quilbeuf¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejP. Lucente¢kaffiliationiTIM S.p.AdnameiP. Fasano¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekT. Arumugamddatej÷Ÿhfilenamegrfc9418eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131213hkeywordsÖfhealthdSAINjsubservicegsymptomitelemetrykobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDatedÆJ'granking$ crfcd9418irfcNumber$ estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'A YANG Data Model for Service AssurancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document describes an architecture that provides some assurance that service instances are running as expected.  As services rely upon multiple subservices provided by a variety of elements, including the underlying network devices and functions, getting the assurance of a healthy service is only possible with a holistic view of all involved elements.  This architecture not only helps to correlate the service degradation with symptoms of a specific network component but, it also lists the services impacted by the failure or degradation of a specific network component.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekJ. Quilbeuf¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekT. Arumugamddatej–´hfilenamegrfc9417eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131212hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedÆJ"granking$…crfcd9417irfcNumber$…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Service Assurance for Intent-Based Networking ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty~Poor selection of transient numerical identifiers in protocols such as the TCP/IP suite has historically led to a number of attacks on implementations, ranging from Denial of Service (DoS) or data injection to information leakages that can be exploited by pervasive monitoring.  Due diligence in the specification of transient numeric identifiers is required even when cryptographic techniques are employed, since these techniques might not mitigate all the associated issues.  This document formally updates RFC 3552, incorporating requirements for transient numeric identifiers, to prevent flaws in future protocols and implementations.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationiQuarkslabdnamegI. Arceddatej÷€hfilenamegrfc9416eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-131211hkeywordsÖhsecuritymvulnerabilityialgorithmfattacknfingerprintingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatedª1,granking$»crfcd9416irfcNumber$»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberHetotaletitlexWSecurity Considerations for Transient Numeric Identifiers Employed in Network ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractywThis document performs an analysis of the security and privacy implications of different types of "transient numeric identifiers" used in IETF protocols and tries to categorize them based on their interoperability requirements and their associated failure severity when such requirements are not met.  Subsequently, it provides advice on possible algorithms that could be employed to satisfy the interoperability requirements of each identifier category while minimizing the negative security and privacy implications, thus providing guidance to protocol designers and protocol implementers.  Finally, it describes a number of algorithms that have been employed in real implementations to generate transient numeric identifiers and analyzes their security and privacy properties.  This document is a product of the Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Group (PEARG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationiQuarkslabdnamegI. Arceddatej÷◊hfilenamegrfc9415eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepeargdfullx;pearg - Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Groupdnamex3Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-131210hkeywordsÖhsecuritymvulnerabilityialgorithmfattacknfingerprintingkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDatedª2granking$«crfcd9415irfcNumber$«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex2On the Generation of Transient Numeric IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyØThis document analyzes the timeline of the specification and implementation of different types of "transient numeric identifiers" used in IETF protocols and how the security and privacy properties of such protocols have been affected as a result of it.  It provides empirical evidence that advice in this area is warranted.  This document is a product of the Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Group (PEARG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationiQuarkslabdnamegI. Arceddatej÷ÿhfilenamegrfc9414eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepeargdfullx;pearg - Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Groupdnamex3Privacy Enhancements and Assessments Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-131209hkeywordsÖhsecuritymvulnerabilityialgorithmfattacknfingerprintingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedª-©granking$∆crfcd9414irfcNumber$∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex4Unfortunate History of Transient Numeric IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThe main goal of the networking standards process is to enable the long-term interoperability of protocols. This document describes active protocol maintenance, a means to accomplish that goal. By evolving specifications and implementations, it is possible to reduce ambiguity over time and create a healthy ecosystem.

The robustness principle, often phrased as "be conservative in what you send, and liberal in what you accept", has long guided the design and implementation of Internet protocols. However, it has been interpreted in a variety of ways. While some interpretations help ensure the health of the Internet, others can negatively affect interoperability over time. When a protocol is actively maintained, protocol designers and implementers can avoid these pitfalls.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliation`dnamekD. Schinaziddatej÷€hfilenamegrfc9413eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-131208hkeywordsÜfpostelclawjrobustnessiprincipleewrongitolerancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedõlHgranking$≈crfcd9413irfcNumber$≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexMaintaining Robust ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxúThe ORIGIN frame for HTTP/2 is equally applicable to HTTP/3, but it needs to be separately registered.  This document describes the ORIGIN frame for HTTP/3.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnameiM. Bishopddatej–≤hfilenamegrfc9412eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131207hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedáaÕgranking$ƒcrfcd9412irfcNumber$ƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe ORIGIN Extension in HTTP/3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis document provides benchmarking terminology and methodology for next-generation network security devices, including next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) and next-generation intrusion prevention systems (NGIPSs).  The main areas covered in this document are test terminology, test configuration parameters, and benchmarking methodology for NGFWs and NGIPSs. (It is assumed that readers have a working knowledge of these devices and the security functionality they contain.) This document aims to improve the applicability, reproducibility, and transparency of benchmarks and to align the test methodology with today's increasingly complex layer 7 security-centric network application use cases.  As a result, this document makes RFC 3511 obsolete.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamelB. Balarajah¢kaffiliationhEANTC AGdnameoC. Rossenhoevel¢kaffiliationjNetSecOPENdnamejB. Monkmanddatej÷’hfilenamegrfc9411eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-131206hkeywordsÖdNGFWeNGIPSlbenchmarkingsperformance testingpsecurity testingkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDated
–ıgranking$√crfcd9411irfcNumber$√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Benchmarking Methodology for Network Security Device PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document extends the current error-handling procedures for mapping of verification failure reasons to 4xx codes for Secure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) and the Authenticated Identity Management in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  It extends the ability to use the Reason header field as an option for conveying an error associated with an Identity header field to the upstream authentication service when local policy dictates that the call should continue in the presence of a verification failure.  This document also defines procedures that enable a failure reason to be mapped to a specific Identity header field for scenarios that use multiple Identity header fields, where some may have errors and others may not.  The handling of those situations is also defined.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjSomos Inc.dnamehC. Wendtddatej÷‡hfilenamegrfc9410eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-131205hkeywordsÑhIdentityomultiple errorshpassportsreason header fieldkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated»éJgranking$¬crfcd9410irfcNumber$¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQHandling of Identity Header Errors for Secure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This Informational document defines the 'sip-trunking-capability'
link relation type that may be used by an enterprise telephony
Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) network to retrieve a SIP trunking
capability set document, which contains the capabilities and
configuration requirements of an Internet Telephony Service Provider
(ITSP).  These technical requirements allow for seamless peering
between SIP-based enterprise telephony networks and the ITSP.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamejK. Inamdar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelS. Narayanan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Engi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiroddatej÷chfilenamegrfc9409eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdasapdfullx)asap - Automatic SIP trunking And Peeringdnamex"Automatic SIP trunking And Peeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-131204hkeywordsÖcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolqautomatic peeringiWebFingerncapability setkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated±€Êgranking$¡crfcd9409irfcNumber$¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0The 'sip-trunking-capability' Link Relation TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document defines a YANG data model for representing an abstract view of the provider network topology that contains the points from which its services can be attached (e.g., basic connectivity, VPN, network slices). Also, the model can be used to retrieve the points where the services are actually being delivered to customers (including peer networks).

This document augments the 'ietf-network' data model defined in RFC 8345 by adding the concept of Service Attachment Points (SAPs). The SAPs are the network reference points to which network services, such as Layer 3 Virtual Private Network (L3VPN) or Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN), can be attached. One or multiple services can be bound to the same SAP. Both User-to-Network Interface (UNI) and Network-to-Network Interface (NNI) are supported in the SAP data model.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehV. Lopezddatej÷Ÿhfilenamegrfc9408eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131203hkeywordsåvService InfrastructurevUser Network InterfacecUNIcNNIxNetwork-to-Network InterfacelInter-AS VPNbCEbPErAttachment CircuitvService Delivery PointjAutomationpService DeliverykobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDatedíä{granking$¿crfcd9408irfcNumber$¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A YANG Network Data Model for Service Attachment Points (SAPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty?This document describes Tetrys, which is an on-the-fly network coding protocol that can be used to transport delay-sensitive and loss-sensitive data over a lossy network. Tetrys may recover from erasures within an RTT-independent delay thanks to the transmission of coded packets. This document is a record of the experience gained by the authors while developing and testing the Tetrys protocol in real conditions.

This document is a product of the Coding for Efficient NetWork Communications Research Group (NWCRG). It conforms to the NWCRG taxonomy described in RFC 8406.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlISAE-SUPAEROdnamekJ. Detchart¢kaffiliationdENACdnameiE. Lochin¢kaffiliationlISAE-SUPAEROdnamehJ. Lacan¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Rocaddatej÷ÿhfilenamegrfc9407eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenwcrgdfullxBnwcrg - Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdnamex:Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-131202hkeywordsÅnNetwork CodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedáU2granking$øcrfcd9407irfcNumber$øestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex-Tetrys: An On-the-Fly Network Coding ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†This document describes HyStart++, a simple modification to the slow start phase of congestion control algorithms.  Slow start can overshoot the ideal send rate in many cases, causing high packet loss and poor performance.  HyStart++ uses increase in round-trip delay as a heuristic to find an exit point before possible overshoot.  It also adds a mitigation to prevent jitter from causing premature slow start exit.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiConfluentdnamerP. Balasubramanian¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehY. Huang¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Olsonddatej÷ﬁhfilenamegrfc9406eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131201hkeywordsÇcTCPrcongestion controlkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatedbÑÖgranking$æcrfcd9406irfcNumber$æestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&HyStart++: Modified Slow Start for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty∞This RFC proposes a framework for detecting sarcasm in AI systems and provides guidelines for using sarcasm without causing offense.  By training AI systems to identify linguistic patterns that indicate sarcasm, we can improve their understanding of human communication.  The guidelines offer a lighthearted approach to using sarcasm in a way that is both effective and respectful, without crossing the line into offensive language.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOpenAIdnamefC. GPT¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelR. L. Barnesddatej÷·hfilenamegrfc9405eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-116296hkeywordsÖwArtificial intelligencegChatGPTuLarge Language ModelsgSarcasmrSentiment analysiskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated({†granking$Ωcrfcd9405irfcNumber$ΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9AI Sarcasm Detection: Insult Your AI without Offending ItdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty…The JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) base protocol (RFC 8620) provides the ability to upload and download arbitrary binary data via HTTP POST and GET on a defined endpoint. This binary data is called a "blob".

This extension adds additional ways to create and access blobs by making inline method calls within a standard JMAP request.

This extension also adds a reverse lookup mechanism to discover where blobs are referenced within other data types.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekB. Gondwanaddatej÷_hfilenamegrfc9404eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131200hkeywordsÅdjmapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated›8„granking$ºcrfcd9404irfcNumber$ºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) Blob Management ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1A Routing Information Base (RIB) is a list of routes and their corresponding administrative data and operational state.

RFC 8349 defines the basic building blocks for the RIB data model, and this model augments it to support multiple next hops (aka paths) for each route as well as additional attributes.fadNamelJim Guicharddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameeY. Quddatej÷Âhfilenamegrfc9403eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131199hkeywordsámconfigurationxIPv6 Router AdvertisementsgNETCONFhRESTCONFdYANGgRoutingcRIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatee\'3granking$ªcrfcd9403irfcNumber$ªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$A YANG Data Model for RIB ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx¶This document defines the Concat notation: a text-based language used to describe pictures and videos whose subject includes cats, containers, and their interactions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Basaglia¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Bernards¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Maasddatej÷‡hfilenamegrfc9402eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-116295hkeywordsÇccaticontainerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDated({†granking$∫crfcd9402irfcNumber$∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitleoConcat NotationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxøThis memo specifies the incorporation of Death (DTH) flag to TCP, including DTH's use of one bit in the TCP header.  The flag is designed to make TCP session narratives smooth and attractive.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamekS. Toyosawaddatej÷‡hfilenamegrfc9401eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-116294hkeywordsÉcTCPlControl bit