ecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document specifies and extends the "Robots Exclusion Protocol" method originally defined by Martijn Koster in 1994 for service owners to control how content served by their services may be accessed, if at all, by automatic clients known as crawlers.  Specifically, it adds definition language for the protocol, instructions for handling errors, and instructions for caching.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpNone (in header)dnameiM. Koster¢kaffiliationkGoogle LLC.dnameiG. Illyes¢kaffiliationkGoogle LLC.dnameiH. Zeller¢kaffiliationkGoogle LLC.dnamejL. Sassmanddatej÷«hfilenamegrfc9309eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-131108hkeywordsÉerobotgcrawlerjrobots.txtkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec·§granking$]crfcd9309irfcNumber$]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRobots Exclusion ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document discusses the applicability of the QUIC transport protocol, focusing on caveats impacting application protocol development and deployment over QUIC.  Its intended audience is designers of application protocol mappings to QUIC and implementors of these application protocols.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliationwGoogle Switzerland GmbHdnamekB. Trammellddatej÷Àhfilenamegrfc9308eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdquicdfullkquic - QUICdnamedQUICdtypebwgbidjdoc-131107hkeywordsÇxapplication protocol mappingjdeploymentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec3é(granking$\crfcd9308irfcNumber$\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Applicability of the QUIC Transport ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≈The "Show me the numbers: Workshop on Analyzing IETF Data (AID)" workshop was convened by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) from November 29 to December 2, 2021 and hosted by the IN-SIGHT.it project at the University of Amsterdam; however, it was converted to an online-only event. The workshop was organized into two discussion parts with a hackathon activity in between. This report summarizes the workshop's discussion and identifies topics that warrant future work and consideration.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of AmsterdamdnamelN. ten Oever¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamegC. Cath¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamemC. S. Perkinsddatej÷…hfilenamegrfc9307eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-131106hkeywordsÇldata sciencemdata analysiskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec2ã¨granking$[crfcd9307irfcNumber$[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex>Report from the IAB Workshop on Analyzing IETF Data (AID) 2021dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document describes a new Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Canonical Address Format (LCAF), the Vendor-Specific LCAF.  This LCAF enables organizations to have implementation-specific encodings for LCAF addresses.  This document updates RFC 8060.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamerA. Rodriguez-Natal¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejV. Ermagan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Smirnov¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemV. Ashtaputre¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacciddatej÷…hfilenamegrfc9306eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131105hkeywordsÜdlispdlcafhinternalfdomainlorganizationgprivatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecQ∫ngranking$Zcrfcd9306irfcNumber$ZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Vendor-Specific LISP Canonical Address Format (LCAF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx€This document describes extensions to the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) data plane, via changes to the LISP header, to support multiprotocol encapsulation and allow the introduction of new protocol capabilities.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehF. Maino¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Lemon¢kaffiliationhInnoviumdnamejP. Agarwal¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Lewis¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Smithddatej÷Ähfilenamegrfc9305eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131104hkeywordsÇhsecurityfpolicykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecQ∫ngranking$Ycrfcd9305irfcNumber$YestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Generic Protocol ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÛThis document specifies a Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) shared message type for defining future extensions and conducting experiments without consuming a LISP Packet Type codepoint for each extension.

This document obsoletes RFC 8113.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenetddatej’:hfilenamegrfc9304eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131103hkeywordsävShared Experiment CodeoLISP codepointsuExperiment IdentifiermExperiment IDxLISP Experimental RegistrynLISP ExtensionnExtending LISPsExhauted LISP typesiLISP IANAdIANAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecQ∫ngranking$Xcrfcd9304irfcNumber$XestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmLocator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP): Shared Extension Message and IANA Registry for Packet Type AllocationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis memo specifies Locator/ID Separation Protocol Security (LISP-SEC), a set of security mechanisms that provides origin authentication, integrity, and anti-replay protection to the LISP's Endpoint-ID-to-Routing-Locator (EID-to-RLOC) mapping data conveyed via the mapping lookup process.  LISP-SEC also enables verification of authorization on EID-Prefix claims in Map-Reply messages.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Maino¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejV. Ermagan¢kaffiliationx$Universitat Politecnica de CatalunyadnamekA. Cabellos¢kaffiliationeInriadnameiD. Saucezddatej‘Òhfilenamegrfc9303eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131102hkeywordsÇdLISPjdeploymentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecQ∫mgranking$Wcrfcd9303irfcNumber$WestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Locator/ID Separation Protocol Security (LISP-SEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThis document describes the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Map-Versioning mechanism, which provides in-packet information about Endpoint-ID-to-Routing-Locator (EID-to-RLOC) mappings used to encapsulate LISP data packets. This approach is based on associating a version number to EID-to-RLOC mappings and transporting such a version number in the LISP-specific header of LISP-encapsulated packets. LISP Map-Versioning is particularly useful to inform communicating Ingress Tunnel Routers (ITRs) and Egress Tunnel Routers (ETRs) about modifications of the mappings used to encapsulate packets. The mechanism is optional and transparent to implementations not supporting this feature, since in the LISP-specific header and in the Map Records, bits used for Map-Versioning can be safely ignored by ITRs and ETRs that do not support or do not want to use the mechanism.

This document obsoletes RFC 6834, which is the initial experimental specifications of the mechanisms updated by this document.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies FrancednamejL. Iannone¢kaffiliationeInriadnameiD. Saucez¢kaffiliationx Universite catholique de LouvaindnamenO. Bonaventureddatej–ñhfilenamegrfc9302eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131101hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecQ∫mgranking$Vcrfcd9302irfcNumber$VestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Map-VersioningdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document describes the control plane and Mapping Service for the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP), implemented by two types of LISP-speaking devices -- the LISP Map-Resolver and LISP Map-Server -- that provide a simplified "front end" for one or more Endpoint IDs (EIDs) to Routing Locator mapping databases.

By using this control plane service interface and communicating with Map-Resolvers and Map-Servers, LISP Ingress Tunnel Routers (ITRs) and Egress Tunnel Routers (ETRs) are not dependent on the details of mapping database systems; this behavior facilitates modularity with different database designs. Since these devices implement the "edge" of the LISP control plane infrastructure, connecting EID addressable nodes of a LISP site, the implementation and operational complexity of the overall cost and effort of deploying LISP is reduced.

This document obsoletes RFCs 6830 and 6833.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Maino¢kaffiliationxvaf.net Internet ConsultingdnameiV. Fuller¢kaffiliationx$Universitat Politecnica de CatalunyadnamekA. Cabellosddatej–Khfilenamegrfc9301eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131100hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDatecQ∫mgranking$Ucrfcd9301irfcNumber$UestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Control PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document describes the data plane protocol for the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP). LISP defines two namespaces: Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs), which identify end hosts; and Routing Locators (RLOCs), which identify network attachment points. With this, LISP effectively separates control from data and allows routers to create overlay networks. LISP-capable routers exchange encapsulated packets according to EID-to-RLOC mappings stored in a local Map-Cache.

LISP requires no change to either host protocol stacks or underlay routers and offers Traffic Engineering (TE), multihoming, and mobility, among other features.

This document obsoletes RFC 6830.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationxvaf.net Internet ConsultingdnameiV. Fuller¢kaffiliationi1-4-5.netdnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Lewis¢kaffiliationx$Universitat Politecnica de CatalunyadnamekA. Cabellosddatej÷ähfilenamegrfc9300eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131099hkeywordsÅoLISP data planekobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatecQ∫lgranking$Tcrfcd9300irfcNumber$TestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes the architecture of the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP), making it easier to read the rest of the LISP specifications and providing a basis for discussion about the details of the LISP protocols.  This document is used for introductory purposes; more details can be found in the protocol specifications, RFCs 9300 and 9301.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx$Universitat Politecnica de CatalunyadnamekA. Cabellos¢kaffiliationeInriadnameiD. Saucezddatej’õhfilenamegrfc9299eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131098hkeywordsÇdLISPlArchitecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecQ∫hgranking$Scrfcd9299irfcNumber$SestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJAn Architectural Introduction to the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how to proxy UDP in HTTP, similar to how the HTTP CONNECT method allows proxying TCP in HTTP.  More specifically, this document defines a protocol that allows an HTTP client to create a tunnel for UDP communications through an HTTP server that acts as a proxy.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinaziddatej÷»hfilenamegrfc9298eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmasquedfullx?masque - Multiplexed Application Substrate over QUIC Encryptiondnamex6Multiplexed Application Substrate over QUIC Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-131097hkeywordsädquicdhttphdatagramcudpeproxygtunnelslquic in quicxturtles all the way downfmasqueghttp-ngkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecù¡granking$Rcrfcd9298irfcNumber$RestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletProxying UDP in HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9484d9931∂habstractyThis document describes HTTP Datagrams, a convention for conveying multiplexed, potentially unreliable datagrams inside an HTTP connection.

In HTTP/3, HTTP Datagrams can be sent unreliably using the QUIC DATAGRAM extension. When the QUIC DATAGRAM frame is unavailable or undesirable, HTTP Datagrams can be sent using the Capsule Protocol, which is a more general convention for conveying data in HTTP connections.

HTTP Datagrams and the Capsule Protocol are intended for use by HTTP extensions, not applications.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinazi¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednameiL. Pardueddatej÷…hfilenamegrfc9297eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmasquedfullx?masque - Multiplexed Application Substrate over QUIC Encryptiondnamex6Multiplexed Application Substrate over QUIC Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-131096hkeywordsàdquicdhttphdatagramdfastgtunnelsxturtles all the way downfmasqueghttp-ngkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecp5granking$Qcrfcd9297irfcNumber$QestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'HTTP Datagrams and the Capsule ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyΩRFC 5309 defines the Point-to-Point (P2P) circuit type, one of the two circuit types used in the link-state routing protocols, and highlights that it is important to identify the correct circuit type when forming adjacencies, flooding link-state database packets, and monitoring the link state.

This document provides advice about the ifStack for the P2P interface over a LAN Type to facilitate operational control, maintenance, and statistics.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamefD. Liu¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehC. Zhangddatej÷≈hfilenamegrfc9296eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131095hkeywordsÉfifTypegifStacknpoint-to-pointkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatec[◊granking$Pcrfcd9296irfcNumber$PestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex\ifStackTable for the Point-to-Point (P2P) Interface over a LAN Type: Definition and ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx„This document updates RFC 8410 to clarify existing semantics, and specify missing semantics, for key usage bits when used in certificates that support the Ed25519, Ed448, X25519, and X448 Elliptic Curve Cryptography algorithms.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationkSquare Inc.dnamekD. McCarney¢kaffiliationnSECOM CO. LTD.dnamefT. Itoddatej”Vhfilenamegrfc9295eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-131094hkeywordsÑdPKIXeX.509hkeyUsagexElliptic Curve CryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec!mÛgranking$Ocrfcd9295irfcNumber$OestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIClarifications for Ed25519, Ed448, X25519, and X448 Algorithm IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõExtensions have been defined for link-state routing protocols that enable distribution of application-specific link attributes for existing as well as newer applications such as Segment Routing (SR).  This document defines extensions to the Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) to enable the advertisement of these application-specific attributes as a part of the topology information from the network.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkArrcus Inc.dnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej÷ hfilenamegrfc9294eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131093hkeywordsÖfBGP-LSoSegment RoutingeIS-ISdOSPFfOSPFv3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec␀p_granking$Ncrfcd9294irfcNumber$NestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjApplication-Specific Link Attributes Advertisement Using the Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty®This document specifies the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).  TCP is an important transport-layer protocol in the Internet protocol stack, and it has continuously evolved over decades of use and growth of the Internet.  Over this time, a number of changes have been made to TCP as it was specified in RFC 793, though these have only been documented in a piecemeal fashion.  This document collects and brings those changes together with the protocol specification from RFC 793.  This document obsoletes RFC 793, as well as RFCs 879, 2873, 6093, 6429, 6528, and 6691 that updated parts of RFC 793.  It updates RFCs 1011 and 1122, and it should be considered as a replacement for the portions of those documents dealing with TCP requirements.  It also updates RFC 5961 by adding a small clarification in reset handling while in the SYN-RECEIVED state.  The TCP header control bits from RFC 793 have also been updated based on RFC 3168.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkMTI SystemsdnamegW. Eddyddatej–ãhfilenamegrfc9293eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131092hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesbopublicationDateb˛>Îgranking$Mcrfcd9293irfcNumber$MestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlex#Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxEThis document defines a binary format for representing HTTP messages.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej–ñhfilenamegrfc9292eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131091hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecÆ—granking$Lcrfcd9292irfcNumber$LestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Binary Representation of HTTP MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty…This document defines an L2VPN Network Model (L2NM) that can be used to manage the provisioning of Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN) services within a network (e.g., a service provider network). The L2NM complements the L2VPN Service Model (L2SM) by providing a network-centric view of the service that is internal to a service provider. The L2NM is particularly meant to be used by a network controller to derive the configuration information that will be sent to relevant network devices.

Also, this document defines a YANG module to manage Ethernet segments and the initial versions of two IANA-maintained modules that include a set of identities of BGP Layer 2 encapsulation types and pseudowire types.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationhVodafonednamehL. Munozddatej÷«hfilenamegrfc9291eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131090hkeywordsÜjautomationmnetwork modelpservice provideruservice provisionningrnetwork automationpservice deliverykobsoletedByÄepagesãopublicationDatec,∑Ãgranking$Kcrfcd9291irfcNumber$KestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A YANG Network Data Model for Layer 2 VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document defines a concise "problem detail" as a way to carry machine-readable details of errors in a Representational State Transfer (REST) response to avoid the need to define new error response formats for REST APIs for constrained environments.  The format is inspired by, but intended to be more concise than, the problem details for HTTP APIs defined in RFC 7807.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamejT. Fossati¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷ hfilenamegrfc9290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131089hkeywordsÜdCoAPcAPIoProblem DetailshCBOR TaglLanguage TagdBidikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecPZÀgranking$Jcrfcd9290irfcNumber$JestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHConcise Problem Details for Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) APIsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document describes a mechanism that, through the use of opportunistic Transport Layer Security (TLS), enables encryption of Remote Procedure Call (RPC) transactions while they are in transit.  The proposed mechanism interoperates with Open Network Computing (ONC) RPC implementations that do not support it.  This document updates RFC 5531.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoHammerspace IncdnamelT. Myklebust¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehC. Leverddatej÷áhfilenamegrfc9289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-131088hkeywordsÑsnetwork file systemuremote procedure callxtransport layer securityeX.509kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec!w∆granking$Icrfcd9289irfcNumber$IestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Towards Remote Procedure Call Encryption by DefaultdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√This document analyzes the security implications of IPv6 Extension Headers and associated IPv6 options.  Additionally, it discusses the operational and interoperability implications of discarding packets based on the IPv6 Extension Headers and IPv6 options they contain.  Finally, it provides advice on the filtering of such IPv6 packets at transit routers for traffic not directed to them, for those cases where such filtering is deemed as necessary.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgEdgeUnodnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liuddatej÷≈hfilenamegrfc9288eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-131087hkeywordsÜqDenial of ServicexDistributed Denial of ServicecDoSdDDoScACLpfiltering policykobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateb˛…§granking$Hcrfcd9288irfcNumber$HestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeRecommendations on the Filtering of IPv6 Packets Containing IPv6 Extension Headers at Transit RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxåThis document describes a method for negotiating the ability to send an arbitrary value for the second-most significant bit in QUIC packets.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej”bhfilenamegrfc9287eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdquicdfullkquic - QUICdnamedQUICdtypebwgbidjdoc-131086hkeywordsÇfHeaderkPath signalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec`ngranking$Gcrfcd9287irfcNumber$GestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuGreasing the QUIC BitdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»This document defines a "manifest" for use in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI).  A manifest is a signed object (file) that contains a listing of all the signed objects (files) in the repository publication point (directory) associated with an authority responsible for publishing in the repository.  For each certificate, Certificate Revocation List (CRL), or other type of signed objects issued by the authority that are published at this repository publication point, the manifest contains both the name of the file containing the object and a hash of the file content.  Manifests are intended to enable a relying party (RP) to detect certain forms of attacks against a repository.  Specifically, if an RP checks a manifest's contents against the signed objects retrieved from a repository publication point, then the RP can detect replay attacks, and unauthorized in-flight modification or deletion of signed objects.  This document obsoletes RFC 6486.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationsNew College FloridadnamekM. Lepinskiddatej–åhfilenamegrfc9286eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131085hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebæ`granking$Fcrfcd9286irfcNumber$FestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Manifests for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9981∂habstractxxThis document describes the Base45 encoding scheme, which is built upon the Base64, Base32, and Base16 encoding schemes.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNetnoddnamenP. F√§ltstr√∂m¢kaffiliationeKireidnamelF. Ljunggren¢kaffiliationjWebweavingdnamenD.W. van Gulikddatej÷»hfilenamegrfc9285eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131084hkeywordsÅfBASE45kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebÚ‡granking$Ecrfcd9285irfcNumber$EestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Base45 Data EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¡This document discusses multihoming considerations for DDoS Open Threat Signaling (DOTS).  The goal is to provide some guidance for DOTS clients and client-domain DOTS gateways when multihomed.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Panddatej–ëhfilenamegrfc9284eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131083hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecÁ=granking$Dcrfcd9284irfcNumber$DestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKMultihoming Deployment Considerations for DDoS Open Threat Signaling (DOTS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxtThis document updates the IAB Charter (RFC 2850) to be consistent with version 3 of the RFC Editor Model (RFC 9280).fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenterddatej–åhfilenamegrfc9283eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131082hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebΩÒágranking$Ccrfcd9283irfcNumber$CestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber'etotaletitlex'IAB Charter Update for RFC Editor ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyêIn RFC 9280, responsibility for the RFC Series moved to the RFC Series Working Group and the RFC Series Approval Board.  It is no longer the responsibility of the RFC Editor, and the role of the IAB in the RFC Series is altered.  Accordingly, in Section 2.1 of RFC 2026, the sentence "RFC publication is the direct responsibility of the RFC Editor, under the general direction of the IAB" is deleted.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Rosenddatej÷¬hfilenamegrfc9282eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131081hkeywordsÑiProtocolsjcopyrightslintellectualhpropertykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebΩÒágranking$Bcrfcd9282irfcNumber$BestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber	etotaletitlex(Responsibility Change for the RFC SeriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyüThis document describes the individuals and organizations involved in the IETF standards process, as described in BCP 9. It includes brief descriptions of the entities involved and the role they play in the standards process.

The IETF and its structure have undergone many changes since RFC 2028 was published in 1996. This document reflects the changed organizational structure of the IETF and obsoletes RFC 2028.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamegR. Salzddatej÷¬hfilenamegrfc9281eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131080hkeywordsádIESGjRFC EditordIRTFhIETF LLCdISOCjregistriesdIANAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebΩÒágranking$Acrfcd9281irfcNumber$AestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex/Entities Involved in the IETF Standards ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¶This document specifies version 3 of the RFC Editor Model. The model defines two high-level tasks related to the RFC Series. First, policy definition is the joint responsibility of the RFC Series Working Group (RSWG), which produces policy proposals, and the RFC Series Approval Board (RSAB), which approves such proposals. Second, policy implementation is primarily the responsibility of the RFC Production Center (RPC) as contractually overseen by the IETF Administration Limited Liability Company (IETF LLC). In addition, various responsibilities of the RFC Editor function are now performed alone or in combination by the RSWG, RSAB, RPC, RFC Series Consulting Editor (RSCE), and IETF LLC. Finally, this document establishes the Editorial Stream for publication of future policy definition documents produced through the processes defined herein.

This document obsoletes RFC 8728. This document updates RFCs 7841, 8729, and 8730.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej÷¬hfilenamegrfc9280eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-131079hkeywordsÑieditorialkpublicationfseriesgstreamskobsoletedByÅd9920epagesopublicationDatebΩÒÜgranking$@crfcd9280irfcNumber$@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexRFC Editor Model (Version 3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9720∂habstractx¢This document specifies a generic mechanism to extend IGMPv3 and Multicast Listener Discovery Version 2 (MLDv2) by using a list of TLVs (Type, Length, and Value).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelM. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameiH. Asaedaddatej÷«hfilenamegrfc9279eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-131078hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebÈº®granking$?crfcd9279irfcNumber$?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzInternet Group Management Protocol Version 3 (IGMPv3) and Multicast Listener Discovery Version 2 (MLDv2) Message ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÚThis specification registers a kind of URI that represents a JSON Web Key (JWK) Thumbprint value.  JWK Thumbprints are defined in RFC 7638.  This enables JWK Thumbprints to be used, for instance, as key identifiers in contexts requiring URIs.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiK. Yasudaddatej÷»hfilenamegrfc9278eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsol