rdaker¢kaffiliationnBloomberg L.P.dnamekV. Dukhovniddatej÷∆hfilenamegrfc9276eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131075hkeywordsÖfDNSSECcDNSeNSEC3dNSECsDenial of ExistencekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatebı'∞granking$<crfcd9276irfcNumber$<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÏetotaletitlex%Guidance for NSEC3 Parameter SettingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document is an extension to the base Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) protocol.  It extends the ALTO cost map and ALTO property map services so that an application can decide to which endpoint(s) to connect based not only on numerical/ordinal cost values but also on fine-grained abstract information regarding the paths.  This is useful for applications whose performance is impacted by specific components of a network on the end-to-end paths, e.g., they may infer that several paths share common links and prevent traffic bottlenecks by avoiding such paths.  This extension introduces a new abstraction called the "Abstract Network Element" (ANE) to represent these components and encodes a network path as a vector of ANEs.  Thus, it provides a more complete but still abstract graph representation of the underlying network(s) for informed traffic optimization among endpoints.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrSichuan UniversitydnamefK. Gao¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamenS. Randriamasy¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationqTongji UniversitydnamehJ. Zhangddatej÷æhfilenamegrfc9275eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131074hkeywordsÉrnetwork visibilityxabstract network elementqshared bottleneckkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDatec.ågranking$;crfcd9275irfcNumber$;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKAn Extension for Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO): Path VectordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document creates a new IANA registry for tracking cost modes supported by the Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Protocol. Also, this document relaxes a constraint that was imposed by the ALTO specification on allowed cost mode values.

This document updates RFC 7285.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej÷∑hfilenamegrfc9274eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131073hkeywordsàlOptimizationsservice performancekcost metricgroutingkcomputationhnetworksxservice-network interactionsnetwork programmingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb‡Køgranking$:crfcd9274irfcNumber$:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSA Cost Mode Registry for the Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyòThis document describes the current research outcomes in Network Coding (NC) for Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) / Named Data Networking (NDN) and clarifies the technical considerations and potential challenges for applying NC in CCNx/NDN.  This document is the product of the Coding for Efficient Network Communications Research Group (NWCRG) and the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdNICTdnamelK. Matsuzono¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameiH. Asaeda¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekC. Westphalddatej÷∆hfilenamegrfc9273eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenwcrgdfullxBnwcrg - Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdnamex:Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-131072hkeywordsÖcICNdCCNxcNDNnnetwork codingpin-network cachekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb¸Î∞granking$9crfcd9273irfcNumber$9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexdNetwork Coding for Content-Centric Networking / Named Data Networking: Considerations and ChallengesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis document specifies general rules for the interaction between the BIER Algorithm (BAR) and the IGP Algorithm (IPA) used for underlay path calculation within the Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) architecture.  The semantics defined in this document update RFC 8401 and RFC 8444.  This document also updates the "BIER Algorithm" registry established in RFC 8401.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamekA. Dolganow¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejH. Bidgoli¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationxEdward Jones Wealth ManagementdnamehA. Gulkoddatej–ãhfilenamegrfc9272eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131071hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec”ìgranking$8crfcd9272irfcNumber$8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Underlay Path Calculation Algorithm and Constraints for Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes the command/response protocol currently used in the management of Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) units and other power devices often deployed in small offices and in IT installations subject to an erratic public power supply.  The UPS units typically interface to an Attachment Daemon in the system they protect.  This daemon is in turn polled by a Management Daemon that notifies users and system administrators of power supply incidents and automates system shutdown decisions.  The commands and responses described by this document are exchanged between the UPS Attachment Daemon and the Management Daemon.  The practice current when this protocol was first developed risks weak security, and this is addressed in the Security Considerations sections of this document.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxNetwork UPS Tools ProjectdnamehR. Priceddatej÷≈hfilenamegrfc9271eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131070hkeywordsÖoUninterruptibleePowerfSupplycUPScNUTkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDatebÚÔ?granking$7crfcd9271irfcNumber$7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPUninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) Management Protocol -- Commands and ResponsesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòITU-T Recommendation G.808.3 defines the generic aspects of a Shared Mesh Protection (SMP) mechanism, where the difference between SMP and Shared Mesh Restoration (SMR) is also identified. ITU-T Recommendation G.873.3 defines the protection switching operation and associated protocol for SMP at the Optical Data Unit (ODU) layer. RFC 7412 provides requirements for any mechanism that would be used to implement SMP in a Multi-Protocol Label Switching - Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) network.

This document updates RFCs 4872 and 4873 to provide extensions for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) signaling to support the control of the SMP mechanism.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeJ. He¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegI. Busi¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Ryoo¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegB. Yoon¢kaffiliationbKTdnamegP. Parkddatej÷∆hfilenamegrfc9270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131069hkeywordsÇeGMPLSvShared mesh protectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebı¯granking$6crfcd9270irfcNumber$6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5GMPLS Signaling Extensions for Shared Mesh ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty“A 4G mobile network uses IP-based transport for the control plane to establish the data session at the user plane for the actual data delivery.  In the existing architecture, IP-based unicast is used for the delivery of multimedia content to a mobile terminal, where each user is receiving a separate stream from the server.  From a bandwidth and routing perspective, this approach is inefficient.  Evolved multimedia broadcast and multicast service (eMBMS) provides capabilities for delivering contents to multiple users simultaneously, but its deployment is very limited or at an experimental stage due to numerous challenges.  The focus of this document is to list the options for using Information-Centric Networking (ICN) in 4G mobile networks and elaborate the experimental setups for its further evaluation.  The experimental setups discussed provide guidance for using ICN either natively or with an existing mobility protocol stack.  With further investigations based on the listed experiments, ICN with its inherent capabilities such as network-layer multicast, anchorless mobility, security, and optimized data delivery using local caching at the edge may provide a viable alternative to IP transport in 4G mobile networks.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnameiP. Suthar¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnameiM. Stolic¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnameiA. Jangam¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejD. Trossen¢kaffiliationkF5 NetworksdnamelR. Ravindranddatej÷√hfilenamegrfc9269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-131068hkeywordsácLTEcICNfICN-4GfICNoIPfIPoICNiHybridICNoDual TrannsportkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatecligranking$5crfcd9269irfcNumber$5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex`Experimental Scenarios of Information-Centric Networking (ICN) Integration in 4G Mobile NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a new IPv6 Hop-by-Hop Option that is used to record the Minimum Path MTU (PMTU) along the forward path between a source host to a destination host.  The recorded value can then be communicated back to the source using the return Path MTU field in the Option.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej÷ƒhfilenamegrfc9268eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-131067hkeywordsÇhDPLPMTUDePMTUDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecïgranking$4crfcd9268irfcNumber$4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IPv6 Minimum Path MTU Hop-by-Hop OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyWThis memo describes common vulnerabilities related to Domain Name System (DNS) resource record (RR) processing as seen in several DNS client implementations.  These vulnerabilities may lead to successful Denial-of-Service and Remote Code Execution attacks against the affected software.  Where applicable, violations of RFC 1035 are mentioned.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvForescout TechnologiesdnamemS. Dashevskyi¢kaffiliationvForescout TechnologiesdnamemD. dos Santos¢kaffiliationvForescout TechnologiesdnamejJ. Wetzels¢kaffiliationvForescout TechnologiesdnamegA. Amriddatej÷≈hfilenamegrfc9267eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131066hkeywordsÇovulnerabilitiesmvulnerabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb‚ó9granking$3crfcd9267irfcNumber$3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexgCommon Implementation Anti-Patterns Related to Domain Name System (DNS) Resource Record (RR) ProcessingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document defines a channel binding type, tls-exporter, that is compatible with TLS 1.3 in accordance with RFC 5056, "On the Use of Channel Bindings to Secure Channels".  Furthermore, it updates the default channel binding to the new binding for versions of TLS greater than 1.2.  This document updates RFCs 5801, 5802, 5929, and 7677.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Whitedddatej–éhfilenamegrfc9266eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131065hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb‡H¢granking$2crfcd9266irfcNumber$2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexChannel Bindings for TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty4Forward Erasure Correction (FEC) is a reliability mechanism that is distinct and separate from the retransmission logic in reliable transfer protocols such as TCP. FEC coding can help deal with losses at the end of transfers or with networks having non-congestion losses. However, FEC coding mechanisms should not hide congestion signals. This memo offers a discussion of how FEC coding and congestion control can coexist. Another objective is to encourage the research community to also consider congestion control aspects when proposing and comparing FEC coding solutions in communication systems.

This document is the product of the Coding for Efficient Network Communications Research Group (NWCRG). The scope of the document is end-to-end communications; FEC coding for tunnels is out of the scope of the document.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdCNESdnamegN. Kuhn¢kaffiliationdENACdnameiE. Lochin¢kaffiliationiUCLouvaindnameiF. Michel¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzlddatej÷∆hfilenamegrfc9265eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenwcrgdfullxBnwcrg - Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdnamex:Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-131064hkeywordsÇfcodingjcongestionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb‡ígranking$1crfcd9265irfcNumber$1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexKForward Erasure Correction (FEC) Coding and Congestion Control in TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This specification defines two formats and associated media types for representing sets of links as standalone documents.  One format is based on JSON, and the other is aligned with the format for representing links in the HTTP "Link" header field.  This specification also introduces a link relation type to support the discovery of sets of links.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAxwaydnamehE. Wilde¢kaffiliationx%Data Archiving and Networked ServicesdnamepH. Van de Sompelddatej÷√hfilenamegrfc9264eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpapidfullx'httpapi - Building Blocks for HTTP APIsdnamexBuilding Blocks for HTTP APIsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131063hkeywordsÑkWeb linkingkTyped linksdJSONdHTTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb‡çgranking$0crfcd9264irfcNumber$0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Linkset: Media Types and a Link Relation Type for Link SetsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfService Function Chaining (SFC) uses the Network Service Header (NSH) (RFC 8300) to steer and provide context metadata (MD) with each packet.  Such metadata can be of various types, including MD Type 2, consisting of Variable-Length Context Headers.  This document specifies several such Context Headers that can be used within a Service Function Path (SFP).fadNamemAndrew Alstondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamefY. Wei¢kaffiliationeInteldnamehU. Elzur¢kaffiliationvIndividual contributordnamehS. Majee¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej÷√hfilenamegrfc9263eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131062hkeywordsÅlSFC metadatakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebÚÙ`granking$/crfcd9263irfcNumber$/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLNetwork Service Header (NSH) Metadata Type 2 Variable-Length Context HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This memo describes per-packet stateless strict and loose path steered replication and forwarding for "Bit Index Explicit Replication" (BIER) packets (RFC 8279); it is called "Tree Engineering for Bit Index Explicit Replication" (BIER-TE) and is intended to be used as the path steering mechanism for Traffic Engineering with BIER.

BIER-TE introduces a new semantic for "bit positions" (BPs). These BPs indicate adjacencies of the network topology, as opposed to (non-TE) BIER in which BPs indicate "Bit-Forwarding Egress Routers" (BFERs). A BIER-TE "packets BitString" therefore indicates the edges of the (loop-free) tree across which the packets are forwarded by BIER-TE. BIER-TE can leverage BIER forwarding engines with little changes. Co-existence of BIER and BIER-TE forwarding in the same domain is possible -- for example, by using separate BIER "subdomains" (SDs). Except for the optional routed adjacencies, BIER-TE does not require a BIER routing underlay and can therefore operate without depending on a routing protocol such as the "Interior Gateway Protocol" (IGP).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies Inc.dnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TuebingendnamehM. Menth¢kaffiliationfKOEVOOdnamejG. Cauchieddatej÷√hfilenamegrfc9262eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131061hkeywordsãdBIERgBIER-TEjcontrollerdECMPjforwardingstraffic-engineeringimulticastjpseudocodegroutingptraffic-steeringmtree-steeringkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDatecJD‚granking$.crfcd9262irfcNumber$.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Tree Engineering for Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document describes a mechanism that builds on Transport Layer Security (TLS) or Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) and enables peers to provide proof of ownership of an identity, such as an X.509 certificate.  This proof can be exported by one peer, transmitted out of band to the other peer, and verified by the receiving peer.fadNamelPaul Woutersdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCloudflare Inc.dnamekN. Sullivanddatej–èhfilenamegrfc9261eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-131060hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebœMægranking$-crfcd9261irfcNumber$-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexExported Authenticators in TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document describes the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) and obsoletes RFC 4960. It incorporates the specification of the chunk flags registry from RFC 6096 and the specification of the I bit of DATA chunks from RFC 7053. Therefore, RFCs 6096 and 7053 are also obsoleted by this document. In addition, RFCs 4460 and 8540, which describe errata for SCTP, are obsoleted by this document.

SCTP was originally designed to transport Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) signaling messages over IP networks. It is also suited to be used for other applications, for example, WebRTC.

SCTP is a reliable transport protocol operating on top of a connectionless packet network, such as IP. It offers the following services to its users:

The design of SCTP includes appropriate congestion avoidance behavior and resistance to flooding and masquerade attacks.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNetflix, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationx'M√ºnster University of Applied SciencesdnameiM. T√ºxen¢kaffiliationlKamstrup A/SdnamejK. Nielsenddatej÷∂hfilenamegrfc9260eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131059hkeywordsÜdSCTPbIPhinternetitransportfpacketgnetworkkobsoletedByÄepagesÖopublicationDatebõò±granking$,crfcd9260irfcNumber$,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Stream Control Transmission ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This document describes how the existing IPv6 mechanisms for ping and traceroute can be used in a Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) network.  The document also specifies the OAM flag (O-flag) in the Segment Routing Header (SRH) for performing controllable and predictable flow sampling from segment endpoints.  In addition, the document describes how a centralized monitoring system performs a path continuity check between any nodes within an SRv6 domain.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationhSoftbankdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatej÷¿hfilenamegrfc9259eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-131058hkeywordsÉdSRv6oSegment RoutingcOAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb¥∂îgranking$+crfcd9259irfcNumber$+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUOperations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) in Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx`This document describes an interface for importing external Pre-Shared Keys (PSKs) into TLS 1.3.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, LLC.dnamekD. Benjamin¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej–çhfilenamegrfc9258eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-131057hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebﬁ˘granking$*crfcd9258irfcNumber$*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Importing External Pre-Shared Keys (PSKs) for TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!This document provides usage guidance for external Pre-Shared Keys (PSKs) in Transport Layer Security (TLS) 1.3 as defined in RFC 8446.  It lists TLS security properties provided by PSKs under certain assumptions, then it demonstrates how violations of these assumptions lead to attacks.  Advice for applications to help meet these assumptions is provided.  This document also discusses PSK use cases and provisioning processes.  Finally, it lists the privacy and security properties that are not provided by TLS 1.3 when external PSKs are used.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationoCloudflare Ltd.dnamejJ. Hoyland¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamehM. Sethi¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamejC. A. Woodddatej–{hfilenamegrfc9257eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-131056hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebﬁ˘granking$)crfcd9257irfcNumber$)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Guidance for External Pre-Shared Key (PSK) Usage in TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)Segment Routing (SR) allows a node to steer a packet flow along any path. Intermediate per-path states are eliminated thanks to source routing. SR Policy is an ordered list of segments (i.e., instructions) that represent a source-routed policy. Packet flows are steered into an SR Policy on a node where it is instantiated called a headend node. The packets steered into an SR Policy carry an ordered list of segments associated with that SR Policy.

This document updates RFC 8402 as it details the concepts of SR Policy and steering into an SR Policy.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationoBritish TelecomdnamekA. Bogdanov¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiP. Mattesddatej÷¡hfilenamegrfc9256eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131055hkeywordsÖoSegment RoutingiSR PolicygSR-MPLSdSRv6sTraffic EngineeringkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateb›∂Ágranking$(crfcd9256irfcNumber$(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Segment Routing Policy ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹There is a false notion that Internet Number Resources (INRs) in the RPKI can be associated with the real-world identity of the 'holder' of an INR.  This document specifies that RPKI does not associate to the INR holder.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuArrcus & IIJ ResearchdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷¬hfilenamegrfc9255eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131054hkeywordsÅx"Resource Public Key InfrastructurekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb°}/granking$'crfcd9255irfcNumber$'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+The 'I' in RPKI Does Not Stand for IdentitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'YANG (RFC 7950) is a data modeling language used to model configuration data, state data, parameters and results of Remote Procedure Call (RPC) operations or actions, and notifications.

