Ethernet VPN (EVPN), and Internet services.  It builds on "BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)" (RFC 4364) and "BGP MPLS-Based Ethernet VPN" (RFC 7432).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamehG. Dawra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationwNTT Network InnovationsdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiS. Zhuang¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej÷¡hfilenamegrfc9252eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-131051hkeywordsÇcBGPdSRv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb’¶granking$$crfcd9252irfcNumber$$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>BGP Overlay Services Based on Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9819∂habstracty This document describes how to support endpoints running the Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) or Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) efficiently for the multicast services over an Ethernet VPN (EVPN) network by incorporating IGMP/MLD Proxy procedures on EVPN Provider Edges (PEs).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Thoria¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Mishra¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefW. Linddatej–âhfilenamegrfc9251eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-131050hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb≥#granking$#crfcd9251irfcNumber$#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpInternet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) Proxies for Ethernet VPN (EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìThis document describes the use of QUIC to provide transport confidentiality for DNS.  The encryption provided by QUIC has similar properties to those provided by TLS, while QUIC transport eliminates the head-of-line blocking issues inherent with TCP and provides more efficient packet-loss recovery than UDP.  DNS over QUIC (DoQ) has privacy properties similar to DNS over TLS (DoT) specified in RFC 7858, and latency characteristics similar to classic DNS over UDP.  This specification describes the use of DoQ as a general-purpose transport for DNS and includes the use of DoQ for stub to recursive, recursive to authoritative, and zone transfer scenarios.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtPrivate Octopus Inc.dnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationjSinodun ITdnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednameiA. Mankinddatej÷ªhfilenamegrfc9250eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-131049hkeywordsÖcDNSdQUICmDNS over QUICmEncrypted DNScDoQkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb|W&granking$"crfcd9250irfcNumber$"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#DNS over Dedicated QUIC ConnectionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document defines a YANG data model that can be used to configure and manage Network Time Protocol (NTP) version 4.  It can also be used to configure and manage version 3.  The data model includes configuration data and state data.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeN. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamehA. Sinha¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamelA. Kumar S N¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegY. Zhaoddatej÷ªhfilenamegrfc9249eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131048hkeywordsÑcNTPdYANGeNTPv4eNTPv3kobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDatebƒÌ,granking$!crfcd9249irfcNumber$!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA YANG Data Model for NTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘Video Relay Service (VRS) is a term used to describe a method by which a hearing person can communicate with a sign language speaker who is deaf, deafblind, or hard of hearing (HoH) or has a speech disability using an interpreter (i.e., a Communications Assistant (CA)) connected via a videophone to the sign language speaker and an audio telephone call to the hearing user.  The CA interprets using sign language on the videophone link and voice on the telephone link.  Often the interpreters may be employed by a company or agency, termed a "provider" in this document.  The provider also provides a video service that allows users to connect video devices to their service and subsequently to CAs and other sign language speakers.  It is desirable that the videophones used by the sign language speaker conform to a standard so that any device may be used with any provider and that direct video calls between sign language speakers work.  This document describes the interface between a videophone and a provider.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Rosenddatej÷∫hfilenamegrfc9248eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrumdfullxrum - Relay User MachinednamerRelay User Machinedtypebwgbidjdoc-131047hkeywordsÇcruetrelay user equipmentkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateb¢7:granking$ crfcd9248irfcNumber$ estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Interoperability Profile for Relay User EquipmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD) defines a simplified mechanism to use Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) with large portions of negotiation aspects eliminated, thus providing benefits such as quick provisioning as well as improved control and flexibility to network nodes initiating the path monitoring. The link-state routing protocols (IS-IS and OSPF) have been extended to advertise the S-BFD Discriminators.

This document defines extensions to the BGP - Link State (BGP-LS) address family to carry the S-BFD Discriminators' information via BGP.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Zhuang¢kaffiliationjArrcus IncdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationjGoogle IncdnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirskyddatej÷√hfilenamegrfc9247eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131046hkeywordsÖfBGP-LScBFDeIS-ISdOSPFfOSPFv3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb¥∞granking$crfcd9247irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\BGP - Link State (BGP-LS) Extensions for Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how the concept of URI Signing supports the content access control requirements of Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) and proposes a URI Signing method as a JSON Web Token (JWT) profile.

The proposed URI Signing method specifies the information needed to be included in the URI to transmit the signed JWT, as well as the claims needed by the signed JWT to authorize a User Agent (UA). The mechanism described can be used both in CDNI and single Content Delivery Network (CDN) scenarios.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjTiledmediadnamerR. van Brandenburg¢kaffiliation`dnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiP. Sorberddatej–ähfilenamegrfc9246eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-131045hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateb©:granking$crfcd9246irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?URI Signing for Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyDThe Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) discussion mailing list furthers the development and specification of Internet technology through the general discussion of technical, procedural, operational, and other topics for which no dedicated mailing lists exist. As this is the most general IETF mailing list, considerable latitude in terms of topics is allowed, but there are posts and topics that are unsuitable for this mailing list. This document defines the charter for the IETF discussion list and explains its scope.

This document obsoletes RFC 3005 and updates RFC 3683.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Harrisddatej–Ähfilenamegrfc9245eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131044hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb≤Hfgranking$crfcd9245irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber-etotaletitlexIETF Discussion List CharterdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9945∂habstractykThis document aims to enrich the Distributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) signal channel protocol with various telemetry attributes, allowing for optimal Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attack mitigation. It specifies the normal traffic baseline and attack traffic telemetry attributes a DOTS client can convey to its DOTS server in the mitigation request, the mitigation status telemetry attributes a DOTS server can communicate to a DOTS client, and the mitigation efficacy telemetry attributes a DOTS client can communicate to a DOTS server. The telemetry attributes can assist the mitigator in choosing the DDoS mitigation techniques and performing optimal DDoS attack mitigation.

This document specifies two YANG modules: one for representing DOTS telemetry message types and one for sharing the attack mapping details over the DOTS data channel.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationlRadware Ltd.dnamehE. Doron¢kaffiliationdCMCCdnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Shallowddatej÷¿hfilenamegrfc9244eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131043hkeywordsàjautomationmcybersecuritydDDoSjResiliencelIntelligencepService deliveryjRobustnessmCollaborativekobsoletedByÄepageslopublicationDateb∞≥Cgranking$crfcd9244irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDDistributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) TelemetrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxØThis document describes YANG data models for the configuration and management of Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) (RFC 8415) servers, relays, and clients.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrerddatej÷æhfilenamegrfc9243eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131042hkeywordsådYANGgNETCONFdRESTjdata modelfDHCPv6dIPv6mconfigurationjmanagementeleaseqprefix delegationladdress poolkprefix poolkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDateb≤Dˆgranking$crfcd9243irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A YANG Data Model for DHCPv6 ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document defines a new exchange, called "Intermediate Exchange", for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2).  This exchange can be used for transferring large amounts of data in the process of IKEv2 Security Association (SA) establishment.  An example of the need to do this is using key exchange methods resistant to Quantum Computers (QCs) for IKE SA establishment.  The Intermediate Exchange makes it possible to use the existing IKE fragmentation mechanism (which cannot be used in the initial IKEv2 exchange), helping to avoid IP fragmentation of large IKE messages if they need to be sent before IKEv2 SA is established.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej÷ºhfilenamegrfc9242eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131041hkeywordsÉpIKE_INTERMEDIATEx.Quantum Computer resistant key exchange methodlPost-quantumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebë^égranking$crfcd9242irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMIntermediate Exchange in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThe Content Delivery Networks Interconnection (CDNI) framework in RFC 6707 defines a set of protocols to interconnect CDNs to achieve multiple goals, including extending the reach of a given CDN.  A CDNI Request Routing Footprint & Capabilities Advertisement interface (FCI) is needed to achieve the goals of a CDNI.  RFC 8008 defines the FCI semantics and provides guidelines on the FCI protocol, but the exact protocol is not specified.  This document defines a new Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) service, called "CDNI Advertisement Service", that provides an implementation of the FCI, following the guidelines defined in RFC 8008.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmHFT StuttgartdnamejJ. Seedorf¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameeK. Ma¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationqTongji UniversitydnamehJ. Zhangddatej÷πhfilenamegrfc9241eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131040hkeywordsÅdALTOkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateb–I#granking$crfcd9241irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexåContent Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Footprint and Capabilities Advertisement Using Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document specifies an extension to the base Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Protocol that generalizes the concept of "endpoint properties", which have been tied to IP addresses so far, to entities defined by a wide set of objects.  Further, these properties are presented as maps, similar to the network and cost maps in the base ALTO Protocol.  While supporting the endpoints and related Endpoint Property Service defined in RFC 7285, the ALTO Protocol is extended in two major directions.  First, from endpoints restricted to IP addresses to entities covering a wider and extensible set of objects; second, from properties for specific endpoints to entire entity property maps.  These extensions introduce additional features that allow entities and property values to be specific to a given information resource.  This is made possible by a generic and flexible design of entity and property types.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamehW. Roome¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamenS. Randriamasy¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationqTongji UniversitydnamehJ. Zhang¢kaffiliationrSichuan UniversitydnamefK. Gaoddatej÷∫hfilenamegrfc9240eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-131039hkeywordsÅdALTOkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateb–Igranking$crfcd9240irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTAn Extension for Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO): Entity Property MapsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis document describes the registration of media types for the ECMAScript and JavaScript programming languages and conformance requirements for implementations of these types.  This document obsoletes RFC 4329 ("Scripting Media Types)", replacing the previous registrations with information and requirements aligned with common usage and implementation experiences.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Miller¢kaffiliationfGitHubdnameiM. Borins¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiM. Bynens¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Fariasddatej÷ªhfilenamegrfc9239eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdispatchdfullsdispatch - DispatchdnamehDispatchdtypebwgbidjdoc-131038hkeywordsájJavaScriptjECMAScriptiMIME typelContent-TypegmodulesdTC39cESMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebuwgranking$crfcd9239irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Updates to ECMAScript Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyRThis informational document details a protocol to load Manufacturer Usage Description (MUD) definitions from RFC 8520 for devices that do not have them integrated.

This document is published to inform the Internet community of this mechanism to allow interoperability and to serve as a basis of other standards work if there is interest.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationiCIRA LabsdnameiJ. Latour¢kaffiliationkUNSW SydneydnameuH. Habibi Gharakheiliddatej÷æhfilenamegrfc9238eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131037hkeywordsÉcRLAlISOIEC189004mANSI MH10.8.2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb}9[granking$crfcd9238irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex?Loading Manufacturer Usage Description (MUD) URLs from QR CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeInformation about which entities are authorized to perform what operations on which constituents of other entities is a crucial component of producing an overall system that is secure. Conveying precise authorization information is especially critical in highly automated systems with large numbers of entities, such as the Internet of Things.

This specification provides a generic information model and format for representing such authorization information, as well as two variants of a specific instantiation of that format for use with Representational State Transfer (REST) resources identified by URI path.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷ºhfilenamegrfc9237eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131036hkeywordsÑeOAuthcIoTrInternet of ThingsnAccess ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec—˛granking$crfcd9237irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqAn Authorization Information Format (AIF) for Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyzThis document describes architectural considerations and implications related to the use of a Name Resolution Service (NRS) in Information-Centric Networking (ICN).  It explains how the ICN architecture can change when an NRS is utilized and how its use influences the ICN routing system.  This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Hong¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamefT. You¢kaffiliationdNICTdnamehV. Kafleddatej÷πhfilenamegrfc9236eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-131035hkeywordsÉxInformation-Centric NetworkingwName Resolution ServicexName to location mappingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateba€˚granking$crfcd9236irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexdArchitectural Considerations of Information-Centric Networking (ICN) Using a Name Resolution ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis document provides test vectors to validate implementations of the two mandatory authentication algorithms specified for the TCP Authentication Option over both IPv4 and IPv6.  This includes validation of the key derivation function (KDF) based on a set of test connection parameters as well as validation of the message authentication code (MAC).  Vectors are provided for both currently required pairs of KDF and MAC algorithms: KDF_HMAC_SHA1 and HMAC- SHA-1-96, and KDF_AES_128_CMAC and AES-128-CMAC-96.  The vectors also validate both whole TCP segments as well as segments whose options are excluded for middlebox traversal.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamemJ. Kuusisaariddatej÷∏hfilenamegrfc9235eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131034hkeywordsÑcTCPnauthenticationfoptionktest vectorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb}∞granking$crfcd9235irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/TCP Authentication Option (TCP-AO) Test VectorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%Route leaks are the propagation of BGP prefixes that violate assumptions of BGP topology relationships, e.g., announcing a route learned from one transit provider to another transit provider or a lateral (i.e., non-transit) peer or announcing a route learned from one lateral peer to another lateral peer or a transit provider.  These are usually the result of misconfigured or absent BGP route filtering or lack of coordination between autonomous systems (ASes).  Existing approaches to leak prevention rely on marking routes by operator configuration, with no check that the configuration corresponds to that of the External BGP (eBGP) neighbor, or enforcement of the two eBGP speakers agreeing on the peering relationship.  This document enhances the BGP OPEN message to establish an agreement of the peering relationship on each eBGP session between autonomous systems in order to enforce appropriate configuration on both sides.  Propagated routes are then marked according to the agreed relationship, allowing both prevention and detection of route leaks.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtQrator Labs & YandexdnameiA. Azimov¢kaffiliationkQrator LabsdnamelE. Bogomazov¢kaffiliationlIIJ & ArrcusdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationhUSA NISTdnameiK. Sriramddatej÷ºhfilenamegrfc9234eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131033hkeywordsÉcBGPjRoute leakhBGP RolekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebu{úgranking$crfcd9234irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRoute Leak Prevention and Detection Using Roles in UPDATE and OPEN MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document describes the changes between Unicode 6.0.0 and Unicode 12.0.0 in the context of the current version of Internationalized Domain Names for Applications 2008 (IDNA2008). Some additions and changes have been made in the Unicode Standard that affect the values produced by the algorithm IDNA2008 specifies. IDNA2008 allows adding exceptions to the algorithm for backward compatibility; however, this document does not add any such exceptions. This document provides the necessary tables to IANA to make its database consistent with Unicode 12.0.0.

To improve understanding, this document describes systems that are being used as alternatives to those that conform to IDNA2008.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNetnoddnamenP. F√§ltstr√∂mddatej÷∏hfilenamegrfc9233eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-131032hkeywordsÉcIDNdIDNAhIDNA2008kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebFNögranking$crfcd9233irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRInternationalized Domain Names for Applications 2008 (IDNA2008) and Unicode 12.0.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”Network telemetry is a technology for gaining network insight and facilitating efficient and automated network management.  It encompasses various techniques for remote data generation, collection, correlation, and consumption.  This document describes an architectural framework for network telemetry, motivated by challenges that are encountered as part of the operation of networks and by the requirements that ensue.  This document clarifies the terminology and classifies the modules and components of a network telemetry system from different perspectives.  The framework and taxonomy help to set a common ground for the collection of related work and provide guidance for related technique and standard developments.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamegH. Song¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefF. Qin¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameqP. Martinez-Julia¢kaffiliationnRakuten MobilednamelL. Ciavaglia¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegA. Wangddatej÷∏hfilenamegrfc9232eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-131031hkeywordsÇiTelemetrycOAMkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDatebë8˘granking$crfcd9232irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNetwork Telemetry FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document updates and corrects the IANA "XML Security URIs" registry that lists URIs intended for use with XML digital signatures, encryption, canonicalization, and key management.  These URIs identify algorithms and types of information.  This document also obsoletes and corrects three errata against RFC 6931.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies, Inc.dnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej÷ºhfilenamegrfc9231eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131030hkeywordsâfXMLSECgXMLDSIGfXMLENClDigestMethodnSigntureMethodpEncryptionMethodoAgreementMethodsKeyDerivationMethodgKeyInfokobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDatebƒ’±granking$crfcd9231irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Additional XML Security Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes a protocol that allows clients to hide their IP addresses from DNS resolvers via proxying encrypted DNS over HTTPS (DoH) messages. This improves privacy of DNS operations by not allowing any one server entity to be aware of both the client IP address and the content of DNS queries and answers.

This experimental protocol has been developed outside the IETF and is published here to guide implementation, ensure interoperability among implementations, and enable wide-scale experimentation.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamejE. Kinnear¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamejP. McManus¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamehT. Verma¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednameiC.A. Woodddatej÷∏hfilenamegrfc9230eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131029hkeywordsÖgPrivacykDNS PrivacycDoHdODoHdHPKEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb°;Pgranking$crfcd9230irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexOblivious DNS over HTTPSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯This document defines an extension to the Babel routing protocol that allows announcing routes to an IPv4 prefix with an IPv6 next hop, which makes it possible for IPv4 traffic to flow through interfaces that have not been assigned an IPv4 address.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxIRIF University of ParisdnamemJ. Chroboczekddatej÷ªhfilenamegrfc9229eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131028hkeywordsÜgroutingjtransitionoIPv6 transitionldouble-stackjdual-stackxglorious IPv6-only futurekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatebuwgranking$crfcd9229irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?IPv4 Routes with an IPv6 Next Hop in the Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≤The address to which email is delivered might be different than any of the addresses shown in any of the content header fields that were created by the email's author. For example, the address used by the email transport service is provided separately, such as through SMTP's "RCPT TO" command, and might not match any address in the To: or cc: fields. In addition, before final delivery, handling can entail a sequence of submission/delivery events, using a sequence of different destination addresses that (eventually) lead to the recipient. As well, a receiving system's delivery process can produce local address transformations.

It can be helpful for a message to have a common way to record each delivery in such a sequence, noting each address used in the sequence to that recipient, such as for (1) analyzing the path a message has taken, (2) loop detection, or (3) formulating the author's address in a reply message. This document defines a header field for this information.

Email handling information discloses details about the email infrastructure, as well as about a particular recipient; this can raise privacy concerns.

A header field such as this is not automatically assured of widespread use. Therefore, this document is being published as an Experimental RFC, looking for constituency and for operational utility. This document was produced through the Independent Submission Stream and was not subject to the IETF's approval process.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej÷±hfilenamegrfc9228eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131027hkeywordsähhandlingeemailcmhsirecipientitransporthdeliverycmdacmtaerelayfheaderkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatebWπÛgranking$crfcd9228irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexDelivered-To Email Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty`This document defines a set of encryption transforms for use in the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and in the Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocols, which are parts of the IP Security (IPsec) protocol suite. The transforms are based on the GOST R 34.12-2015 block ciphers (which are named "Magma" and "Kuznyechik") in Multilinear Galois Mode (MGM) and the external rekeying approach.

This specification was developed to facilitate implementations that wish to support the GOST algorithms. This document does not imply IETF endorsement of the cryptographic algorithms used in this document.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej÷∑hfilenamegrfc9227eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131026hkeywordsÇdAEADcMGMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebFNògranking$crfcd9227irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlextUsing GOST Ciphers in the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty@The prevailing hexadecimal system was chosen for congruence with groups of four binary digits, but its design exhibits an indifference to cognitive factors.  An alternative is introduced that is designed to reduce brain cycles in cases where a hexadecimal number should be readily convertible to binary by a human being.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjmbreen.comdnamehM. Breenddatej–ãhfilenamegrfc9226eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-107848hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebGˇ◊granking$
