estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexBioctal: Hexadecimal 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty*This document discourages the practice of introducing software defects in general and in network protocol implementations specifically.  Software defects are one of the largest cost drivers for the networking industry.  This document is intended to clarify the best current practice in this regard.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiC. Morrow¢kaffiliationhAsteroiddnamekR. van Mookddatej÷∑hfilenamegrfc9225eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-107847hkeywordsÅdbugskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebGˇ÷granking$	crfcd9225irfcNumber$	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex#Software Defects Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx˝This document specifies a method to find which Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) server is authoritative to answer queries for a requested scope, such as domain names, IP addresses, or Autonomous System numbers.  This document obsoletes RFC 7484.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej÷µhfilenamegrfc9224eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131025hkeywordsâewhoisibootstrapfdomaincIDNcDNSbASdIPv4dIPv6dJSONkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebFNógranking$crfcd9224irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber_etotaletitlexJFinding the Authoritative Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≠The Real-time Transport Object delivery over Unidirectional Transport (ROUTE) protocol is specified for robust delivery of Application Objects, including Application Objects with real-time delivery constraints, to receivers over a unidirectional transport. Application Objects consist of data that has meaning to applications that use the ROUTE protocol for delivery of data to receivers; for example, it can be a file, a Dynamic Adaptive Streaming over HTTP (DASH) or HTTP Live Streaming (HLS) segment, a WAV audio clip, etc. The ROUTE protocol also supports low-latency streaming applications.

The ROUTE protocol is suitable for unicast, broadcast, and multicast transport. Therefore, it can be run over UDP/IP, including multicast IP. The ROUTE protocol can leverage the features of the underlying protocol layer, e.g., to provide security, it can leverage IP security protocols such as IPsec.

This document specifies the ROUTE protocol such that it could be used by a variety of services for delivery of Application Objects by specifying their own profiles of this protocol (e.g., by adding or constraining some features).

This is not an IETF specification and does not have IETF consensus.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxQualcomm CDMA Technologies GmbHdnamefW. Zia¢kaffiliationxQualcomm CDMA Technologies GmbHdnamenT. Stockhammer¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies Inc.dnamenL. Chaponniere¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies Inc.dnamejG. Mandyam¢kaffiliationoBitRipple, Inc.dnamegM. Lubyddatej÷µhfilenamegrfc9223eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131024hkeywordsÜiMulticastiBroadcastcFECdDASHcHLSeFLUTEkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDatebiëùgranking$crfcd9223irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexIReal-Time Transport Object Delivery over Unidirectional Transport (ROUTE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=This document proposes guidelines for the design of Autonomic Service Agents for autonomic networks.  Autonomic Service Agents, together with the Autonomic Network Infrastructure, the Autonomic Control Plane, and the GeneRic Autonomic Signaling Protocol, constitute base elements of an autonomic networking ecosystem.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationnRakuten MobilednamelL. Ciavaglia¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Pelosoddatej÷∂hfilenamegrfc9222eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-131023hkeywordsÑeGRASPjautonomousrautonomic functionoself-managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebFNïgranking$crfcd9222irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Guidelines for Autonomic Service AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxóThis document defines an extension to the QUIC transport protocol to add support for sending and receiving unreliable datagrams over a QUIC connection.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamejE. Kinnear¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinaziddatej–|hfilenamegrfc9221eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdquicdfullkquic - QUICdnamedQUICdtypebwgbidjdoc-131022hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatebFNwgranking$crfcd9221irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(An Unreliable Datagram Extension to QUICdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¸The mechanism for running the WebSocket Protocol over a single stream of an HTTP/2 connection is equally applicable to HTTP/3, but the HTTP-version-specific details need to be specified.  This document describes how the mechanism is adapted for HTTP/3.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekR. Hamiltonddatej÷∑hfilenamegrfc9220eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131021hkeywordsÇpextended connectb42kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb°3#granking$crfcd9220irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Bootstrapping WebSockets with HTTP/3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxüThis document specifies an extension to "The JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) for Mail" (RFC 8621) for returning the S/MIME signature verification status.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej÷¥hfilenamegrfc9219eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131020hkeywordsÇdJMAPfS/MIMEkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatebLíkgranking$crfcd9219irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTS/MIME Signature Verification Extension to the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document describes a scheme that allows an HTTP client to communicate its preferences for how the upstream server prioritizes responses to its requests, and also allows a server to hint to a downstream intermediary how its responses should be prioritized when they are forwarded.  This document defines the Priority header field for communicating the initial priority in an HTTP version-independent manner, as well as HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 frames for reprioritizing responses.  These share a common format structure that is designed to provide future extensibility.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamefK. Oku¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednameiL. Pardueddatej÷≥hfilenamegrfc9218eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131019hkeywordsÑqResponse prioritysStream multiplexingpReprioritizationqServer schedulingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebû^÷granking$crfcd9218irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Extensible Prioritization Scheme for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyíIn contrast to the present Internet architecture, a path-aware internetworking architecture has two important properties: it exposes the properties of available Internet paths to endpoints, and it provides for endpoints and applications to use these properties to select paths through the Internet for their traffic. While this property of "path awareness" already exists in many Internet-connected networks within single domains and via administrative interfaces to the network layer, a fully path-aware internetwork expands these concepts across layers and across the Internet.

This document poses questions in path-aware networking, open as of 2021, that must be answered in the design, development, and deployment of path-aware internetworks. It was originally written to frame discussions in the Path Aware Networking Research Group (PANRG), and has been published to snapshot current thinking in this space.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwGoogle Switzerland GmbHdnamekB. Trammellddatej–yhfilenamegrfc9217eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepanrgdfullx panrg - Path Aware Networking RGdnamexPath Aware Networking RGdtypebrgbidjdoc-131018hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateb2_granking$crfcd9217irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex/Current Open Questions in Path-Aware NetworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxËThe S/MIME development community benefits from sharing samples of signed or encrypted data.  This document facilitates such collaboration by defining a small set of X.509v3 certificates and keys for use when generating such samples.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxAmerican Civil Liberties UniondnamemD. K. Gillmorddatej÷∂hfilenamegrfc9216eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-131017hkeywordsédpkixjencryptionhsecuritynauthenticationfS/MIMEesmimeeemaildmailoconfidentialitykcertificateepkcs8fpkcs12dx509m"test vector"kobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateba¨Ωgranking$␀crfcd9216irfcNumber$␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$S/MIME Example Keys and CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyîThis document describes encoding formats, identifiers, and parameter formats for the GOST R 34.10-2012 and GOST R 34.11-2012 algorithms for use in the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI).

This specification is developed to facilitate implementations that wish to support the GOST algorithms. This document does not imply IETF endorsement of the cryptographic algorithms used in this document.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkLinaro Ltd.dnamelD. Baryshkov¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnamekV. Nikolaev¢kaffiliationlInfoTeCS JSCdnamelA. Chelpanovddatej÷¥hfilenamegrfc9215eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131016hkeywordsÇdGOSTcPKIkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateb8(Ωgranking#ˇcrfcd9215irfcNumber#ˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexjUsing GOST R 34.10-2012 and GOST R 34.11-2012 Algorithms with the Internet X.509 Public Key InfrastructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙIANA has created "Protocol in the Segment ID Sub-TLV" and "Protocol in Label Stack Sub-TLV of Downstream Detailed Mapping TLV" registries under the "Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) Ping Parameters" registry. RFC 8287 defines the code points for Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) and Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocols.

This document specifies the code point to be used in the Segment ID sub-TLV and Downstream Detailed Mapping (DDMAP) TLV when the Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) is OSPFv3. This document also updates RFC 8287 by clarifying that the existing "OSPF" code point is to be used only to indicate OSPFv2 and by defining the behavior when the Segment ID sub-TLV indicates the use of IPv6.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiN. Nainar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Aissaouiddatej÷µhfilenamegrfc9214eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-131015hkeywordsÅdMPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebWπµgranking#˛crfcd9214irfcNumber#˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#OSPFv3 Code Point for MPLS LSP PingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This specification defines a convention for HTTP response header fields that allow cache directives to be targeted at specific caches or classes of caches.  It also defines one such header field, the CDN-Cache-Control response header field, which is targeted at content delivery network (CDN) caches.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamehS. Ludin¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednameeY. Wuddatej÷¥hfilenamegrfc9213eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131014hkeywordsÉcCDNxContent Delivery NetworkgCachingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb°-Ügranking#˝crfcd9213irfcNumber#˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTargeted HTTP Cache ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty?The United States Government has published the National Security Agency (NSA) Commercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite, which defines cryptographic algorithm policy for national security applications.  This document specifies the conventions for using the United States National Security Agency's CNSA Suite algorithms with the Secure Shell Transport Layer Protocol and the Secure Shell Authentication Protocol.  It applies to the capabilities, configuration, and operation of all components of US National Security Systems (described in NIST Special Publication 800-59) that employ Secure Shell (SSH).  This document is also appropriate for all other US Government systems that process high-value information.  It is made publicly available for use by developers and operators of these and any other system deployments.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamelN. Gajcowski¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejM. Jenkinsddatej÷≥hfilenamegrfc9212eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131013hkeywordsÇcNSSuremote administrationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateb/Ò4granking#¸crfcd9212irfcNumber#¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexWCommercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite Cryptography for Secure Shell (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÈTo aid debugging, HTTP caches often append header fields to a response, explaining how they handled the request in an ad hoc manner.  This specification defines a standard mechanism to do so that is aligned with HTTP's caching model.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷®hfilenamegrfc9211eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131012hkeywordsÑdhttpecacheidebugginggx-cachekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateb†Tgranking#˚crfcd9211irfcNumber#˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+The Cache-Status HTTP Response Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty'This document updates RFCs 1123 and 1536.  This document requires the operational practice of permitting DNS messages to be carried over TCP on the Internet as a Best Current Practice.  This operational requirement is aligned with the implementation requirements in RFC 7766.  The use of TCP includes both DNS over unencrypted TCP as well as over an encrypted TLS session.  The document also considers the consequences of this form of DNS communication and the potential operational issues that can arise when this Best Current Practice is not upheld.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmDataPlane.orgdnamekJ. Kristoff¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejD. Wesselsddatej÷≥hfilenamegrfc9210eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131011hkeywordsÇcDNScTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb:kCgranking#˙crfcd9210irfcNumber#˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÎetotaletitlex1DNS Transport over TCP - Operational RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxîThis document defines the Proxy-Status HTTP response field to convey the details of an intermediary's response handling, including generated errors.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiP. Sikoraddatej÷Øhfilenamegrfc9209eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131010hkeywordsÉeproxylintermediarymreverse proxykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb°(Ògranking#˘crfcd9209irfcNumber#˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+The Proxy-Status HTTP Response Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This document defines a QUOTA extension of the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) (see RFCs 3501 and 9051) that permits administrative limits on resource usage (quotas) to be manipulated through the IMAP protocol.

This document obsoletes RFC 2087 but attempts to remain backwards compatible whenever possible.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeIsodednamekA. Melnikovddatej–shfilenamegrfc9208eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-131009hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebFNvgranking#¯crfcd9208irfcNumber#¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletIMAP QUOTA ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a new parameter called iss.  This parameter is used to explicitly include the issuer identifier of the authorization server in the authorization response of an OAuth authorization flow.  The iss parameter serves as an effective countermeasure to "mix-up attacks".fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiHackmanitdnameuK. Meyer zu Selhausen¢kaffiliationgyes.comdnamegD. Fettddatej÷≤hfilenamegrfc9207eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-131008hkeywordsÇhsecurityfoauth2kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateb5hgranking#˜crfcd9207irfcNumber#˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4OAuth 2.0 Authorization Server Issuer IdentificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¯The United States Government has published the National Security Agency's Commercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite, which defines cryptographic algorithm policy for national security applications.  This document specifies the conventions for using the United States National Security Agency's CNSA Suite algorithms in Internet Protocol Security (IPsec).  It applies to the capabilities, configuration, and operation of all components of US National Security Systems (described in NIST Special Publication 800-59) that employ IPsec.  This document is also appropriate for all other US Government systems that process high-value information.  It is made publicly available for use by developers and operators of these and any other system deployments.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamekL. Corcoran¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejM. Jenkinsddatej÷≥hfilenamegrfc9206eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131007hkeywordsÉlpost quantumqquantum resistantcNSAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebIgranking#ˆcrfcd9206irfcNumber#ˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexgCommercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite Cryptography for Internet Protocol Security (IPsec)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÇApplications often use HTTP as a substrate to create HTTP-based APIs. This document specifies best practices for writing specifications that use HTTP to define new application protocols. It is written primarily to guide IETF efforts to define application protocols using HTTP for deployment on the Internet but might be applicable in other situations.

This document obsoletes RFC 3205.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷¶hfilenamegrfc9205eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-131006hkeywordsÅhHTTP APIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb†ﬂ˛granking#ıcrfcd9205irfcNumber#ıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber8etotaletitlexBuilding Protocols with HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx”This specification defines QPACK: a compression format for efficiently representing HTTP fields that is to be used in HTTP/3.  This is a variation of HPACK compression that seeks to reduce head-of-line blocking.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiC. Krasic¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnameiM. Bishop¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnamekA. Frindellddatej÷óhfilenamegrfc9204eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdquicdfullkquic - QUICdnamedQUICdtypebwgbidjdoc-131005hkeywordsàiTransportdQUICkcompressionfHTTP/3eHPACKfheaderefieldgtrailerkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDatebû^÷granking#Ùcrfcd9204irfcNumber#ÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#QPACK: Field Compression for HTTP/3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document specifies a profile for the Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) framework.  It utilizes Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE) to provide communication security and proof-of-possession for a key owned by the client and bound to an OAuth 2.0 access token.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelF. Palombini¢kaffiliationiCombitechdnamehL. Seitz¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliationdRISEdnamemM. Gunnarssonddatej’"hfilenamegrfc9203eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131004hkeywordsÖdCoAPiOAuth 2.0nAccess ControlmAuthorizationrInternet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec—˝granking#Ûcrfcd9203irfcNumber#ÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexûThe Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE) Profile of the Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5This specification defines a profile of the Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE) framework that allows constrained servers to delegate client authentication and authorization.  The protocol relies on DTLS version 1.2 or later for communication security between entities in a constrained network using either raw public keys or pre-shared keys.  A resource-constrained server can use this protocol to delegate management of authorization information to a trusted host with less-severe limitations regarding processing power and memory.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnameiS. Gerdes¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamekO. Bergmann¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliationiCombitechdnamehL. Seitzddatej÷§hfilenamegrfc9202eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131003hkeywordsÖxInternet of Thinks (IoT)rInternet of ThingscIOTeOAuthlAccess TokenkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec—˝granking#Úcrfcd9202irfcNumber#ÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxDatagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Profile for Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9430∂habstracty®This specification defines new parameters and encodings for the OAuth 2.0 token and introspection endpoints when used with the framework for Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE).  These are used to express the proof-of-possession (PoP) key the client wishes to use, the PoP key that the authorization server has selected, and the PoP key the resource server uses to authenticate to the client.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCombitechdnamehL. Seitzddatej÷§hfilenamegrfc9201eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131002hkeywordsÖdCoAPiOAuth 2.0nAccess ControlmAuthorizationrInternet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatec—¸granking#Òcrfcd9201irfcNumber#ÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcAdditional OAuth Parameters for Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments (ACE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThis specification defines a framework for authentication and authorization in Internet of Things (IoT) environments called ACE-OAuth.  The framework is based on a set of building blocks including OAuth 2.0 and the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP), thus transforming a well-known and widely used authorization solution into a form suitable for IoT devices.  Existing specifications are used where possible, but extensions are added and profiles are defined to better serve the IoT use cases.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiCombitechdnamehL. Seitz¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliation`dnamemE. Wahlstroem¢kaffiliationjSpotify ABdnamejS. Erdtman¢kaffiliationhArm Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej÷§hfilenamegrfc9200eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-131001hkeywordsÖdCoAPiOAuth 2.0nAccess ControlmAuthorizationrInternet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepagesHopublicationDatec—¯granking#crfcd9200irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgAuthentication and Authorization for Constrained Environments Using the OAuth 2.0 Framework (ACE-OAuth)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyπRecent research work has explored the deployment characteristics and configuration of the Domain Name System (DNS). This document summarizes the conclusions from these research efforts and offers specific, tangible considerations or advice to authoritative DNS server operators. Authoritative server operators may wish to follow these considerations to improve their DNS services.

It is possible that the results presented in this document could be applicable in a wider context than just the DNS protocol, as some of the results may generically apply to any stateless/short-duration anycasted service.

This document is not an IETF consensus document: it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrSIDN Labs/TU DelftdnamehG. Moura¢kaffiliationx"USC/Information Sciences InstitutednamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliationx"USC/Information Sciences InstitutednamelJ. Heidemann¢kaffiliationiSIDN LabsdnameiM. Davidsddatej÷≤hfilenamegrfc9199eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-131000hkeywordságRoutingcDNSgAnycastfDomaindNamefSystemcBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb0&Dgranking#Ôcrfcd9199irfcNumber#ÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex;Considerations for Large Authoritative DNS Server OperatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©This memo introduces an advanced unidirectional route assessment (AURA) metric and associated measurement methodology based on the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) framework (RFC 2330).  This memo updates RFC 2330 in the areas of path-related terminology and path description, primarily to include the possibility of parallel subpaths between a given Source and Destination pair, owing to the presence of multipath technologies.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxUniversidad de Buenos AiresdnamerJ. Alvarez-Hamelin¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationgTU WiendnameiJ. Fabini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geibddatej÷Åhfilenamegrfc9198eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130999hkeywordsÖkPerformancegMetricsdIPPMdpathnparallel pathskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebãﬂŒgranking#Ócrfcd9198irfcNumber#ÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Advanced Unidirectional Route Assessment (AURA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(In situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM) collects operational and telemetry information in the packet while the packet traverses a path between two points in the network.  This document discusses the data fields and associated data types for IOAM.  IOAM-Data-Fields can be encapsulated into a variety of protocols, such as Network Service Header (NSH), Segment Routing, Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation (Geneve), or IPv6.  IOAM can be used to complement OAM mechanisms based on, e.g., ICMP or other types of probe packets.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Brockners¢kaffiliationkThoughtspotdnamekS. Bhandari¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejT. Mizrahiddatej÷±hfilenamegrfc9197eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130998hkeywordsÉfinbandiTelemetrygTracingkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDatebãŸågranking#Ìcrfcd9197irfcNumber#ÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJData Fields for In Situ Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (IOAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document defines two YANG modules, "ietf-system-capabilities" and "ietf-notification-capabilities".

The module "ietf-system-capabilities" provides a placeholder structure that can be used to discover YANG-related system capabilities for servers. The module can be used to report capability information from the server at runtime or at implementation time by making use of the YANG instance data file format.

The module "ietf-notification-capabilities" augments "ietf-system-capabilities" to specify capabilities related to "Subscription to YANG Notifications for Datastore Updates" (RFC 8641).fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejB. Lengyel¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claiseddatej÷Æhfilenamegrfc9196eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130997hkeywordsÉdNMDAgNETCONFhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebø(granking#Ïcrfcd9196irfcNumber#ÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSYANG Modules Describing Capabilities for Systems and Datastore Update NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)There is a need to document data defined in YANG models at design time, implementation time, or when a live server is unavailable.  This document specifies a standard file format for YANG instance data, which follows the syntax and semantics of existing YANG models and annotates it with metadata.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejB. Lengyel¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claiseddatej–lhfilenamegrfc9195eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130996hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb∂ógranking#Îcrfcd9195irfcNumber#ÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$A File Format for YANG Instance DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxªThis document defines a YANG module for managing the reverse metric extension to the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) intra-domain routing information exchange protocol.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehC. Hoppsddatej–uhfilenamegrfc9194eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130995hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatecPúfgranking#Ícrfcd9194irfcNumber#ÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&A YANG Module for IS-IS Reverse MetricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThe Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML) media types support multiple types of values, from numbers to text strings and arbitrary binary Data Values.  In order to facilitate processing of binary Data Values, this document specifies a pair of new SenML fields for indicating the content format of those binary Data Values, i.e., their Internet media type, including parameters as well as any content codings applied.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Ker√§nen¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Øhfilenamegrfc9193eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130994hkeywordsÖxInternet of Things (IoT)rInternet of ThingscIOTjdata modeljmedia typekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatebüËIgranking#Ècrfcd9193irfcNumber#ÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPSensor Measurement Lists (SenML) Fields for Indicating Data Value Content-FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty∏The Network Service Header (NSH) specification defines two possible methods of including metadata (MD): MD Type 0x1 and MD Type 0x2. MD Type 0x1 uses a fixed-length Context Header. The allocation of this Context Header, i.e., its structure and semantics, has not been standardized. This memo defines the Timestamp Context Header, which is an NSH fixed-length Context Header that incorporates the packet's timestamp, a sequence number, and a source interface identifier.

Although the definition of the Context Header presented in this document has not been standardized by the IETF, it has been implemented in silicon by several manufacturers and is published here to facilitate interoperability.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamemI. Yerushalmi¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnameiD. Melman¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiR. Browneddatej÷±hfilenamegrfc9192eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130993hkeywordsÉcNSHnContext HeaderitimestampkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateb
˛granking#Ëcrfcd9192irfcNumber#ËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexCNetwork Service Header (NSH) Fixed-Length Context Header AllocationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝The Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), defined in RFC 3748, provides a standard mechanism for support of multiple authentication methods.  EAP-TLS and other TLS-based EAP methods are widely deployed and used for network access authentication.  Large certificates and long certificate chains combined with authenticators that drop an EAP session after only 40 - 50 round trips is a major deployment problem.  This document looks at this problem in detail and describes the potential solutions available.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehM. Sethi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Preu√ü Mattsson¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷çhfilenamegrfc9191eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130992hkeywordsÜgEAP-TLSeX.509cEAPmauthenticatorxMaximum Transmission Unite(MTU)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebe2granking#Ácrfcd9191irfcNumber#ÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPHandling Large Certificates and Long Certificate Chains in TLS-Based EAP MethodsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), defined in RFC 3748, provides a standard mechanism for support of multiple authentication methods.  This document specifies the use of EAP-TLS with TLS 1.3 while remaining backwards compatible with existing implementations of EAP-TLS.  TLS 1.3 provides significantly improved security and privacy, and reduced latency when compared to earlier versions of TLS.  EAP-TLS with TLS 1.3 (EAP-TLS 1.3) further improves security and privacy by always providing forward secrecy, never disclosing the peer identity, and by mandating use of revocation checking when compared to EAP-TLS with earlier versions of TLS.  This document also provides guidance on authentication, authorization, and resumption for EAP-TLS in general (regardless of the underlying TLS version used).  This document updates RFC 5216.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Preu√ü Mattsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehM. Sethiddatej–qhfilenamegrfc9190eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130991hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebegranking#Êcrfcd9190irfcNumber#ÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFEAP-TLS 1.3: Using the Extensible Authentication Protocol with TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9965¥habstractywThis document specifies three new cipher suites, two new signature algorithms, seven new supported groups, and two new certificate types for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol version 1.2 to support the Russian cryptographic standard algorithms (called "GOST" algorithms). This document specifies a profile of TLS 1.2 with GOST algorithms so that implementers can produce interoperable implementations.

This specification facilitates implementations that aim to support the GOST algorithms. This document does not imply IETF endorsement of the cipher suites, signature algorithms, supported groups, and certificate types.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnamemS. Smyshlyaev¢kaffiliationiCryptocomdnamelD. Belyavsky¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnamekE. Alekseevddatej÷∞hfilenamegrfc9189eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130990hkeywordsÉdGOSTlcipher suitecTLSkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDateb/ê8granking#Âcrfcd9189irfcNumber#ÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexJGOST Cipher Suites for Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractycA device can be simultaneously connected to multiple networks, e.g., Wi-Fi, LTE, 5G, and DSL.  It is desirable to seamlessly combine the connectivity over these networks below the transport layer (L4) to improve the quality of experience for applications that do not have built-in multi-path capabilities.  Such optimization requires additional control information, e.g., a sequence number, in each packet.  This document presents a new lightweight and flexible encapsulation protocol for this need.  The solution has been developed by the authors based on their experiences in multiple standards bodies including the IETF and 3GPP.  However, this document is not an Internet Standard and does not represent the consensus opinion of the IETF.  This document will enable other developers to build interoperable implementations in order to experiment with the protocol.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamefJ. Zhu¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekS. Kanugoviddatej–shfilenamegrfc9188eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130989hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb2granking#‰crfcd9188irfcNumber#‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex1Generic Multi-Access (GMA) Encapsulation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyISliding window protocols use finite sequence numbers to determine segment placement and order.  These sequence number spaces wrap around and are reused during the operation of such protocols.  This document describes a way to extend the size of these sequence numbers at the endpoints to avoid the impact of that wrap and reuse without transmitting additional information in the packet header.  The resulting extended sequence numbers can be used at the endpoints in encryption and authentication algorithms to ensure input bit patterns do not repeat over the lifetime of a connection.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvIndependent consultantdnamehJ. Touchddatej–shfilenamegrfc9187eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130988hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatea¯ﬁ”granking#„crfcd9187irfcNumber#„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0Sequence Number Extension for Windowed ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis document specifies how Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for multipoint networks can provide sub-second failover for routers that participate in Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM).  An extension to the PIM Hello message used to bootstrap a point-to-multipoint BFD session is also defined in this document.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnameeX. Jiddatej÷®hfilenamegrfc9186eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130987hkeywordsÇfPIM-SMcBFDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaÚ“granking#‚crfcd9186irfcNumber#‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexçFast Failover in Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) Using Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for Multipoint NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€This document defines a protocol for tunneling DTLS traffic in multimedia conferences that enables a Media Distributor to facilitate key exchange between an endpoint in a conference and the Key Distributor.  The protocol is designed to ensure that the keying material used for hop-by-hop encryption and authentication is accessible to the Media Distributor, while the keying material used for end-to-end encryption and authentication is inaccessible to the Media Distributor.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationtPrinceton UniversitydnamemP. Ellenbogen¢kaffiliationi8x8, Inc.dnamekN. Ohlmeierddatej÷∞hfilenamegrfc9185eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpercdfullx(perc - Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdnamex!Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130986hkeywordsàdPERCdSRTPcRTPdDTLSiDTLS-SRTPkDTLS tunnellconferencinghsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebgLBgranking#·crfcd9185irfcNumber#·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVDTLS Tunnel between a Media Distributor and Key Distributor to Facilitate Key ExchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document updates several BGP Extended Community registries in order to replace the "Experimental Use" registration procedure in some entries, since their use is clearly not experimental and is thus misleading.

This document updates RFCs 7153 and 8955.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwnext layer Telekom GmbHdnamehC. Loiblddatej÷±hfilenamegrfc9184eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130985hkeywordsÅdIANAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaÈ¢–granking#‡crfcd9184irfcNumber#‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(BGP Extended Community Registries UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA major issue in multilevel TRILL is how to manage RBridge nicknames.  In this document, area border RBridges use a single nickname in both Level 1 and Level 2.  RBridges in Level 2 must obtain unique nicknames but RBridges in different Level 1 areas may have the same nicknames.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnameiM. Cullen¢kaffiliationcJITdnamegH. Zhaiddatej÷∞hfilenamegrfc9183eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130984hkeywordsÅjaggregatedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebdgranking#ﬂcrfcd9183irfcNumber#ﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmSingle Nickname for an Area Border RBridge in Multilevel Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇAs a complement to the Layer 3 Virtual Private Network Service Model (L3SM), which is used for communication between customers and service providers, this document defines an L3VPN Network Model (L3NM) that can be used for the provisioning of Layer 3 Virtual Private Network (L3VPN) services within a service provider network. The model provides a network-centric view of L3VPN services.

The L3NM is meant to be used by a network controller to derive the configuration information that will be sent to relevant network devices. The model can also facilitate communication between a service orchestrator and a network controller/orchestrator.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationhVodafonednamehL. Munoz¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiA. Aguadoddatej÷≠hfilenamegrfc9182eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130983hkeywordsäel3vpnjAutomationtService ProvisioningrNetwork AutomationuService OrchestrationpService DeliverygNETCONFhRESTCONFfSlicesonetwork slicingkobsoletedByÄepagesÅopublicationDatebuùgranking#ﬁcrfcd9182irfcNumber#ﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A YANG Network Data Model for Layer 3 VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxòThis document defines a common YANG module that is meant to be reused by various VPN-related modules such as Layer 3 VPN and Layer 2 VPN network models.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamejS. Barguil¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej÷≠hfilenamegrfc9181eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130982hkeywordsârservice automationrnetwork automationpservice deliverytservice provisioningeSliceonetwork slicinglvitalisationjAutomationnNetwork ModelskobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDatebuÄgranking#›crfcd9181irfcNumber#›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5A Common YANG Data Model for Layer 2 and Layer 3 VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyâThis document describes a scheme for hybrid public key encryption (HPKE). This scheme provides a variant of public key encryption of arbitrary-sized plaintexts for a recipient public key. It also includes three authenticated variants, including one that authenticates possession of a pre-shared key and two optional ones that authenticate possession of a key encapsulation mechanism (KEM) private key. HPKE works for any combination of an asymmetric KEM, key derivation function (KDF), and authenticated encryption with additional data (AEAD) encryption function. Some authenticated variants may not be supported by all KEMs. We provide instantiations of the scheme using widely used and efficient primitives, such as Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) key agreement, HMAC-based key derivation function (HKDF), and SHA2.

This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationeInriadnamelK. Bhargavan¢kaffiliationeInriadnamegB. Lipp¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamegC. Woodddatej÷∞hfilenamegrfc9180eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130981hkeywordsÉupublic-key encryptionqkey encapsulationx"post-quantum public-key encryptionkobsoletedByÄepageskopublicationDatebägranking#‹crfcd9180irfcNumber#‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexHybrid Public Key EncryptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document defines a generic geographical location YANG grouping.  The geographical location grouping is intended to be used in YANG data models for specifying a location on or in reference to Earth or any other astronomical object.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehC. Hoppsddatej÷Øhfilenamegrfc9179eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130980hkeywordsÅkgeolocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebŒwgranking#€crfcd9179irfcNumber#€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A YANG Grouping for Geographic LocationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis memo discusses the use of the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) in building sensors and other devices that employ cellular networks as a communications medium.  Building communicating devices that employ these networks is obviously well known, but this memo focuses specifically on techniques necessary to minimize power consumption.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamekA. Eriksson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Ker√§nenddatej‘≈hfilenamegrfc9178eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlwigdfullx+lwig - Light-Weight Implementation Guidancednamex$Light-Weight Implementation Guidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130979hkeywordsÇdCoAPqcellular networkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebë8ıgranking#⁄crfcd9178irfcNumber#⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Building Power-Efficient Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Devices for Cellular NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document specifies alternative Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) block-wise transfer options: Q-Block1 and Q-Block2.

These options are similar to, but distinct from, the CoAP Block1 and Block2 options defined in RFC 7959. The Q-Block1 and Q-Block2 options are not intended to replace the Block1 and Block2 options but rather have the goal of supporting Non-confirmable (NON) messages for large amounts of data with fewer packet interchanges. Also, the Q-Block1 and Q-Block2 options support faster recovery should any of the blocks get lost in transmission.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Shallowddatej÷¢hfilenamegrfc9177eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130978hkeywordsçkQuick-BlocklRobust-BlockgR-BlockkTough-BlockoResilient-BlockjFast-BlockjResilienceiFilteringsFaster transmissionuLarge amounts of datawLess packet interchangemFast recoverydDOTSkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateb:k5granking#Ÿcrfcd9177irfcNumber#ŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbConstrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Block-Wise Transfer Options Supporting Robust TransmissiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıIn many Internet of Things (IoT) applications, direct discovery of resources is not practical due to sleeping nodes or networks where multicast traffic is inefficient.  These problems can be solved by employing an entity called a Resource Directory (RD), which contains information about resources held on other servers, allowing lookups to be performed for those resources.  The input to an RD is composed of links, and the output is composed of links constructed from the information stored in the RD.  This document specifies the web interfaces that an RD supports for web servers to discover the RD and to register, maintain, look up, and remove information on resources.  Furthermore, new target attributes useful in conjunction with an RD are defined.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Ams√ºss¢kaffiliationlEdge ImpulsednameiZ. Shelby¢kaffiliationlPassiveLogicdnameiM. Koster¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationvvanderstok consultancydnameoP. van der Stokddatej÷ôhfilenamegrfc9176eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130977hkeywordsÑdCoREkWeb LinkingrResource DiscoveryrResource DirectorykobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDatebfÏgranking#ÿcrfcd9176irfcNumber#ÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Constrained RESTful Environments (CoRE) Resource DirectorydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—This document specifies enhancements to the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) that mitigate security issues in particular use cases.  The Echo option enables a CoAP server to verify the freshness of a request or to force a client to demonstrate reachability at its claimed network address.  The Request-Tag option allows the CoAP server to match block-wise message fragments belonging to the same request.  This document updates RFC 7252 with respect to the following: processing requirements for client Tokens, forbidding non-secure reuse of Tokens to ensure response-to-request binding when CoAP is used with a security protocol, and amplification mitigation (where the use of the Echo option is now recommended).fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Ams√ºss¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamerJ. Preu√ü Mattsson¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamekG. Selanderddatej÷Æhfilenamegrfc9175eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130976hkeywordsãfOSCOREjblock-wisedDTLSifreshnessedelayqdenial-of-servicemamplificationvMessage Body IntegrityuConcurrent Block-WisexRequest-Response BindingkToken ReusekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb˛Êgranking#◊crfcd9175irfcNumber#◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPConstrained Application Protocol (CoAP): Echo, Request-Tag, and Token ProcessingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes a TCP convergence layer (TCPCL) for Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN).  This version of the TCPCL protocol resolves implementation issues in the earlier TCPCL version 3 as defined in RFC 7242 and provides updates to the Bundle Protocol (BP) contents, encodings, and convergence-layer requirements in BP version 7 (BPv7).  Specifically, TCPCLv4 uses BPv7 bundles encoded by the Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) as its service data unit being transported and provides a reliable transport of such bundles.  This TCPCL version also includes security and extensibility mechanisms.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoRKF EngineeringdnamehB. Sipos¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Demmer¢kaffiliationxTechnical University of MunichdnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationgLogMeIndnamelS. Perreaultddatej–Lhfilenamegrfc9174eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdtndfullx*dtn - Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdnamex$Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130975hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDatea¯€âgranking#÷crfcd9174irfcNumber#÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDelay-Tolerant Networking TCP Convergence-Layer Protocol Version 4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis document defines default integrity and confidentiality security contexts that can be used with Bundle Protocol Security (BPSec) implementations.  These security contexts are intended to be used both for testing the interoperability of BPSec implementations and for providing basic security operations when no other security contexts are defined or otherwise required for a network.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnameoE. Birrane, III¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamehA. White¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnameiS. Heinerddatej÷ßhfilenamegrfc9173eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdtndfullx*dtn - Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdnamex$Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130974hkeywordsÑhsecurityfbundleiintegrityoconfidentialitykobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDatea¯€àgranking#’crfcd9173irfcNumber#’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Default Security Contexts for Bundle Protocol Security (BPSec)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx}This document defines a security protocol providing data integrity and confidentiality services for the Bundle Protocol (BP).fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnameoE. Birrane, III¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamekK. McKeeverddatej÷ñhfilenamegrfc9172eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdtndfullx*dtn - Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdnamex$Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130973hkeywordsÑhsecurityfbundleiintegrityoconfidentialitykobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDatea¯€ágranking#‘crfcd9172irfcNumber#‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Bundle Protocol Security (BPSec)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıThis document presents a specification for the Bundle Protocol, adapted from the experimental Bundle Protocol specification developed by the Delay-Tolerant Networking Research Group of the Internet Research Task Force and documented in RFC 5050.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhIPNGROUPdnamekS. Burleigh¢kaffiliationxRoland Computing ServicesdnamegK. Fall¢kaffiliationxAPL Johns Hopkins UniversitydnameoE. Birrane, IIIddatej÷ñhfilenamegrfc9171eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdtndfullx*dtn - Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdnamex$Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130972hkeywordsÇxDelay-tolerant networkingxdisruption-tolerant networkingkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDatea¯€^granking#”crfcd9171irfcNumber#”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBundle Protocol Version 7dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9713d9758¥habstractyVThe ability to change protocols depends on exercising the extension and version-negotiation mechanisms that support change.  This document explores how regular use of new protocol features can ensure that it remains possible to deploy changes to a protocol.  Examples are given where lack of use caused changes to be more difficult or costly.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliation`dnamehT. Paulyddatej÷¨hfilenamegrfc9170eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130971hkeywordsÉjExtensionshversionsfgreasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaŒægranking#“crfcd9170irfcNumber#“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex4Long-Term Viability of Protocol Extension MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThe Evidence Record Syntax (ERS) and the conventions for including these evidence records in the Server-based Certificate Validation Protocol (SCVP) are expressed using ASN.1.  This document offers alternative ASN.1 modules that conform to the 2002 version of ASN.1 and employ the conventions adopted in RFCs 5911, 5912, and 6268.  There are no bits-on-the-wire changes to any of the formats; this is simply a change to the ASN.1 syntax.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationrRed Hound SoftwarednamejC. Wallaceddatej÷Øhfilenamegrfc9169eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130970hkeywordsÇeLTANSqlong-term archivekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaŒΩgranking#—crfcd9169irfcNumber#—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6New ASN.1 Modules for the Evidence Record Syntax (ERS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThe Path Computation Element (PCE) is a functional component capable of selecting paths through a traffic engineering (TE) network. These paths may be supplied in response to requests for computation or may be unsolicited requests issued by the PCE to network elements. Both approaches use the PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) to convey the details of the computed path.

Traffic flows may be categorized and described using "Flow Specifications". RFC 8955 defines the Flow Specification and describes how Flow Specification components are used to describe traffic flows. RFC 8955 also defines how Flow Specifications may be distributed in BGP to allow specific traffic flows to be associated with routes.

This document specifies a set of extensions to PCEP to support dissemination of Flow Specifications. This allows a PCE to indicate what traffic should be placed on each path that it is aware of.

The extensions defined in this document include the creation, update, and withdrawal of Flow Specifications via PCEP and can be applied to tunnels initiated by the PCE or to tunnels where control is delegated to the PCE by the Path Computation Client (PCC). Furthermore, a PCC requesting a new path can include Flow Specifications in the request to indicate the purpose of the tunnel allowing the PCE to factor this into the path computation.fadNamelJohn Scudderdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Liddatej÷áhfilenamegrfc9168eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130969hkeywordsÉcPCEhFlowSpeciFlow SpeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea·‘€granking#–crfcd9168irfcNumber#–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extension for Flow SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractxÛThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension called "Registry Maintenance Notification", which is used by EPP servers to notify EPP clients and allow EPP clients to query EPP servers regarding maintenance events.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejT. Sattler¢kaffiliationlGoDaddy Inc.dnameiR. Carney¢kaffiliationlGoDaddy Inc.dnameiJ. Kolkerddatej–lhfilenamegrfc9167eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130968hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea≈Ó@granking#œcrfcd9167irfcNumber#œestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRegistry Maintenance Notification for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a YANG data model that can be used to configure and manage Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) snooping devices.  The YANG module in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegH. Zhao¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefY. Liu¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamehA. Peter¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelM. Sivakumarddatej÷Åhfilenamegrfc9166eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130967hkeywordsàdYANGmIGMP SnoopinglMLD Snoopingimulticastjdata modelvietf-igmp-mld-snoopingrnetwork managementgroutingkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDatea˙granking#Œcrfcd9166irfcNumber#ŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoA YANG Data Model for Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) SnoopingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’The Concise Data Definition Language (CDDL), standardized in RFC 8610, provides "control operators" as its main language extension point.

The present document defines a number of control operators that were not yet ready at the time RFC 8610 was completed: .plus, .cat, and .det for the construction of constants; .abnf/.abnfb for including ABNF (RFC 5234 and RFC 7405) in CDDL specifications; and .feature for indicating the use of a non-basic feature in an instance.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Øhfilenamegrfc9165eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130966hkeywordsàmbinary formatwdata interchange formattdescription languageoschema languageltree grammardABNFmAugmented BNFrfeature indicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea≈„granking#Õcrfcd9165irfcNumber#ÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLAdditional Control Operators for the Concise Data Definition Language (CDDL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÇThis specification defines two Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) tags for use with IPv6 and IPv4 addresses and prefixes.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Øhfilenamegrfc9164eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130965hkeywordsÑmbinary formatwdata interchange formatqinterface addressozone identifierkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatea∑Ÿgranking#Ãcrfcd9164irfcNumber#ÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYConcise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) Tags for IPv4 and IPv6 Addresses and PrefixesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new HTTP header field named "Expect-CT", which allows web host operators to instruct user agents (UAs) to expect valid Signed Certificate Timestamps (SCTs) to be served on connections to these hosts.  Expect-CT allows web host operators to discover misconfigurations in their Certificate Transparency (CT) deployments.  Further, web host operators can use Expect-CT to ensure that if a UA that supports Expect-CT accepts a misissued certificate, that certificate will be discoverable in Certificate Transparency logs.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehE. Starkddatej÷?hfilenamegrfc9163eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130964hkeywordsÇxCertificate TransparencyiExpect-CTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebü‰}granking#Àcrfcd9163irfcNumber#ÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexExpect-CT Extension for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document describes version 2.0 of the Certificate Transparency (CT) protocol for publicly logging the existence of Transport Layer Security (TLS) server certificates as they are issued or observed, in a manner that allows anyone to audit certification authority (CA) activity and notice the issuance of suspect certificates as well as to audit the certificate logs themselves. The intent is that eventually clients would refuse to honor certificates that do not appear in a log, effectively forcing CAs to add all issued certificates to the logs.

This document obsoletes RFC 6962. It also specifies a new TLS extension that is used to send various CT log artifacts.

Logs are network services that implement the protocol operations for submissions and queries that are defined in this document.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiB. Laurie¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejE. Messeri¢kaffiliationgSectigodnamelR. Stradlingddatej÷ßhfilenamegrfc9162eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetransdfullx"trans - Public Notary TransparencydnamexPublic Notary Transparencydtypebwgbidjdoc-130963hkeywordsÜlcertificatesdpkixctlsgwebsitefwebpkihbrowserskobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDatea≥#granking# crfcd9162irfcNumber# estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Certificate Transparency Version 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis document describes the Ethernet Virtual Private Network (EVPN) Proxy ARP/ND function augmented by the capability of the ARP/ND Extended Community.  From that perspective, this document updates the EVPN specification to provide more comprehensive documentation of the operation of the Proxy ARP/ND function.  The EVPN Proxy ARP/ND function and the ARP/ND Extended Community help operators of Internet Exchange Points, Data Centers, and other networks deal with IPv4 and IPv6 address resolution issues associated with large Broadcast Domains by reducing and even suppressing the flooding produced by address resolution in the EVPN network.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. Sathappan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejK. Nagaraj¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejG. Hankins¢kaffiliationfDE-CIXdnamegT. Kingddatej–mhfilenamegrfc9161eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130962hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea·4granking#…crfcd9161irfcNumber#…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHOperational Aspects of Proxy ARP/ND in Ethernet Virtual Private NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThis document introduces new IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) code points to identify which traffic is being forwarded based on which MPLS control plane protocol is used within a Segment Routing domain.  In particular, this document defines five code points for the IPFIX mplsTopLabelType Information Element for Path Computation Element (PCE), IS-IS, OSPFv2, OSPFv3, and BGP MPLS Segment Routing extensions.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhSwisscomdnamegT. Grafddatej÷¨hfilenamegrfc9160eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130961hkeywordsàwcontrol plane migrationrtraffic monitoringrtraffic accountingdOSPFeIS-ISnBGP Prefix-SIDcPCEgPCEP SRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaºMSgranking#»crfcd9160irfcNumber#»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Export of MPLS Segment Routing Label Type Information in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@RFC 7668 describes the adaptation of IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) techniques to enable IPv6 over Bluetooth Low Energy (Bluetooth LE) networks that follow the star topology.  However, recent Bluetooth specifications allow the formation of extended topologies as well.  This document specifies mechanisms that are needed to enable IPv6 mesh over Bluetooth LE links established by using the Bluetooth Internet Protocol Support Profile (IPSP).  This document does not specify the routing protocol to be used in an IPv6 mesh over Bluetooth LE links.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx$Universitat Politecnica de CatalunyadnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationx$Universitat Politecnica de CatalunyadnamemS.M. Darroudi¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamemT. Savolainen¢kaffiliationxGraz University of TechnologydnameiM. Spoerkddatej÷™hfilenamegrfc9159eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130960hkeywordsátBluetooth Low Energymmesh networksg6lowpandIPv6iLow powercIoTrInternet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea∑á&granking#«crfcd9159irfcNumber#«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYIPv6 Mesh over BLUETOOTH(R) Low Energy Using the Internet Protocol Support Profile (IPSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRRFC 7299 describes the object identifiers that were assigned by the Public Key Infrastructure using X.509 (PKIX) Working Group in an arc that was allocated by IANA (1.3.6.1.5.5.7).  A small number of object identifiers that were assigned in RFC 4212 are omitted from RFC 7299, and this document updates RFC 7299 to correct that oversight.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷Æhfilenamegrfc9158eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130959hkeywordsÑx"Certificate Request Message FormatdCRMFxCRMF Registration ControlsxAlternate Certificate FormatskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea¶Ø…granking#∆crfcd9158irfcNumber#∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCUpdate to the Object Identifier Registry for the PKIX Working GroupdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document changes the review requirements needed to get DNSSEC algorithms and resource records added to IANA registries.  It updates RFC 6014 to include hash algorithms for Delegation Signer (DS) records and NextSECure version 3 (NSEC3) parameters (for Hashed Authenticated Denial of Existence).  It also updates RFCs 5155 and 6014, which have requirements for DNSSEC algorithms, and updates RFC 8624 to clarify the implementation recommendation related to the algorithms described in RFCs that are not on the standards track.  The rationale for these changes is to bring the requirements for DS records and hash algorithms used in NSEC3 in line with the requirements for all other DNSSEC algorithms.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej–lhfilenamegrfc9157eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130958hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea®8granking#≈crfcd9157irfcNumber#≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Revised IANA Considerations for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9904∂habstractxThis document describes a technique called "QNAME minimisation" to improve DNS privacy, where the DNS resolver no longer always sends the full original QNAME and original QTYPE to the upstream name server.  This document obsoletes RFC 7816.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAFNICdnamemS. Bortzmeyer¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejR. Dolmans¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej÷©hfilenamegrfc9156eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130957hkeywordsÅeQNAMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaóCzgranking#ƒcrfcd9156irfcNumber#ƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.DNS Query Name Minimisation to Improve PrivacydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>The MD5 and SHA-1 hashing algorithms are increasingly vulnerable to attack, and this document deprecates their use in TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2 digital signatures.  However, this document does not deprecate SHA-1 with Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC), as used in record protection.  This document updates RFC 5246.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmcyberstorm.mudnamemL. Velvindron¢kaffiliationcCISdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationoCloudflare Inc.dnamejA. Ghediniddatej÷¨hfilenamegrfc9155eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130956hkeywordsÅctlskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea¿ºDgranking#√crfcd9155irfcNumber#√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDeprecating MD5 and SHA-1 Signature Hashes in TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸The Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) (RFC 5730) defines the use of authorization information to authorize a transfer of an EPP object, such as a domain name, between clients that are referred to as "registrars".  Object-specific, password-based authorization information (see RFCs 5731 and 5733) is commonly used but raises issues related to the security, complexity, storage, and lifetime of authentication information.  This document defines an operational practice, using the EPP RFCs, that leverages the use of strong random authorization information values that are short lived, not stored by the client, and stored by the server using a cryptographic hash that provides for secure authorization information that can safely be used for object transfers.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamejR. Wilhelmddatej÷¨hfilenamegrfc9154eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130955hkeywordsácEPPhauthinfofrandomkshort-livedfstronggstoringhsecurelykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaŒ≤granking#¬crfcd9154irfcNumber#¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTExtensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Secure Authorization Information for TransferdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This document defines terminology and requirements for solutions produced by the Drone Remote Identification Protocol (DRIP) Working Group.  These solutions will support Unmanned Aircraft System Remote Identification and tracking (UAS RID) for security, safety, and other purposes (e.g., initiation of identity-based network sessions supporting UAS applications).  DRIP will facilitate use of existing Internet resources to support RID and to enable enhanced related services, and it will enable online and offline verification that RID information is trustworthy.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmAX EnterprizednamegS. Card¢kaffiliationmAX EnterprizednameoA. Wiethuechter¢kaffiliationnHTT ConsultingdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationuLink√∂ping UniversitydnameiA. Gurtovddatej÷©hfilenamegrfc9153eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddripdfullxdrip - Drone Remote ID ProtocoldnamexDrone Remote ID Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130954hkeywordsÅdDRIPkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateb¥@granking#¡crfcd9153irfcNumber#¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHDrone Remote Identification Protocol (DRIP) Requirements and TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÍThis document specifies protocol interfaces profiled by the United States National Security Agency (NSA) for National Security System (NSS) servers that provide public key certificates, Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs), and symmetric keys to NSS clients. Servers that support these interfaces are referred to as Secure Object Delivery Protocol (SODP) servers. The intended audience for this profile comprises developers of client devices that will obtain key management services from NSA-operated SODP servers. Interfaces supported by SODP servers include Enrollment over Secure Transport (EST) and its extensions as well as Certificate Management over CMS (CMC).

This profile applies to the capabilities, configuration, and operation of all components of US National Security Systems (SP 800-59). It is also appropriate for other US Government systems that process high-value information. It is made publicly available for use by developers and operators of these and any other system deployments.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejM. Jenkins¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷åhfilenamegrfc9152eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130953hkeywordsÇdCNSAcNSSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebhv⁄granking#¿crfcd9152irfcNumber#¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex®Secure Object Delivery Protocol (SODP) Server Interfaces: NSA's Profile for Delivery of Certificates, Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs), and Symmetric Keys to ClientsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty,This document defines a base profile for TLS protocol versions 1.2 and 1.3 as well as DTLS protocol versions 1.2 and 1.3 for use with the US Commercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite.

The profile applies to the capabilities, configuration, and operation of all components of US National Security Systems that use TLS or DTLS. It is also appropriate for all other US Government systems that process high-value information.

The profile is made publicly available here for use by developers and operators of these and any other system deployments.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnameiD. Cooleyddatej–Lhfilenamegrfc9151eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130952hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebg7âgranking#øcrfcd9151irfcNumber#øestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexXCommercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite Profile for TLS and DTLS 1.2 and 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyèThis document defines the use of cipher suites for TLS 1.3 based on Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC).  Using these cipher suites provides server and, optionally, mutual authentication and data authenticity, but not data confidentiality.  Cipher suites with these properties are not of general applicability, but there are use cases, specifically in Internet of Things (IoT) and constrained environments, that do not require confidentiality of exchanged messages while still requiring integrity protection, server authentication, and optional client authentication.  This document gives examples of such use cases, with the caveat that prior to using these integrity-only cipher suites, a threat model for the situation at hand is needed, and a threat analysis must be performed within that model to determine whether the use of integrity-only cipher suites is appropriate.  The approach described in this document is not endorsed by the IETF and does not have IETF consensus, but it is presented here to enable interoperable implementation of a reduced-security mechanism that provides authentication and message integrity without supporting confidentiality.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationdODVAdnameiJ. Visokyddatej÷£hfilenamegrfc9150eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130951hkeywordsÉdHMACcIoTsconstrained deviceskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatebaﬂõgranking#æcrfcd9150irfcNumber#æestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex7TLS 1.3 Authentication and Integrity-Only Cipher SuitesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjTLS session tickets enable stateless connection resumption for clients without server-side, per-client state.  Servers vend an arbitrary number of session tickets to clients, at their discretion, upon connection establishment.  Clients store and use tickets when resuming future connections.  This document describes a mechanism by which clients can specify the desired number of tickets needed for future connections.  This extension aims to provide a means for servers to determine the number of tickets to generate in order to reduce ticket waste while simultaneously priming clients for future connection attempts.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinazi¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednameiC.A. Woodddatej÷éhfilenamegrfc9149eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130950hkeywordsÇcTLSgTicketskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebg2\granking#Ωcrfcd9149irfcNumber#ΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesTLS Ticket RequestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖEnrollment over Secure Transport (EST) is used as a certificate provisioning protocol over HTTPS.  Low-resource devices often use the lightweight Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) for message exchanges.  This document defines how to transport EST payloads over secure CoAP (EST-coaps), which allows constrained devices to use existing EST functionality for provisioning certificates.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameoP. van der Stok¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationcSSWdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationx"RISE Research Institutes of SwedendnamegS. Razaddatej–hfilenamegrfc9148eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130949hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDatebg·granking#ºcrfcd9148irfcNumber#ºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\EST-coaps: Enrollment over Secure Transport with the Secure Constrained Application ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9908∂habstracty.This document specifies version 1.3 of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol. DTLS 1.3 allows client/server applications to communicate over the Internet in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, and message forgery.

The DTLS 1.3 protocol is based on the Transport Layer Security (TLS) 1.3 protocol and provides equivalent security guarantees with the exception of order protection / non-replayability. Datagram semantics of the underlying transport are preserved by the DTLS protocol.

This document obsoletes RFC 6347.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekN. Modaduguddatej÷ühfilenamegrfc9147eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130948hkeywordsÅvCommunication SecuritykobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateba€Ïgranking#ªcrfcd9147irfcNumber#ªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAThe Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Protocol Version 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9853∂habstracty€This document specifies the Connection ID (CID) construct for the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol version 1.2.

A CID is an identifier carried in the record layer header that gives the recipient additional information for selecting the appropriate security association. In "classical" DTLS, selecting a security association of an incoming DTLS record is accomplished with the help of the 5-tuple. If the source IP address and/or source port changes during the lifetime of an ongoing DTLS session, then the receiver will be unable to locate the correct security context.

The new ciphertext record format with the CID also provides content type encryption and record layer padding.

This document updates RFC 6347.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamejT. Fossati¢kaffiliationmBosch.IO GmbHdnamehA. Krausddatej÷•hfilenamegrfc9146eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130947hkeywordsÅmNAT rebindingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateb4˘◊granking#∫crfcd9146irfcNumber#∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Connection Identifier for DTLS 1.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9853∂habstractyThis specification presents an optional method to add integrity protection directly to the Network Service Header (NSH) used for Service Function Chaining (SFC).  Also, this specification allows for the encryption of sensitive metadata (MD) that is carried in the NSH.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationfCitrixdnamegD. Wingddatej÷¨hfilenamegrfc9145eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130946hkeywordsàhSecurityjResiliencejAutomationpService deliveryiProviderswDifferentiated servicessTraffic EngineeringqAttack mitigationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea∑ágranking#πcrfcd9145irfcNumber#πestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeIntegrity Protection for the Network Service Header (NSH) and Encryption of Sensitive Context HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx•This document defines a Remote Procedure Call (RPC) operation to compare management datastores that comply with the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Biermanddatej÷™hfilenamegrfc9144eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130945hkeywordsÉoTroubleshootinghYANG RPCoYANG Data ModelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea≥˘Úgranking#∏crfcd9144irfcNumber#∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIComparison of Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) DatastoresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThis specification defines a new Session Description Protocol (SDP) Grouping Framework extension called 'BUNDLE'. The extension can be used with the SDP offer/answer mechanism to negotiate the usage of a single transport (5-tuple) for sending and receiving media described by multiple SDP media descriptions ("m=" sections). Such transport is referred to as a "BUNDLE transport", and the media is referred to as "bundled media". The "m=" sections that use the BUNDLE transport form a BUNDLE group.

This specification defines a new RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Source Description (SDES) item and a new RTP header extension.

This specification updates RFCs 3264, 5888, and 7941.

This specification obsoletes RFC 8843.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrand¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jenningsddatej÷´hfilenamegrfc9143eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130944hkeywordsäcRTPcSDPfBundlelMultiplexingfRTCWEBdCLUEfMMUSICcAVTcWEBgBrowserkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDatebé‰granking#∑crfcd9143irfcNumber#∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKNegotiating Media Multiplexing Using the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx«This document updates the recommended set of key exchange methods for use in the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol to meet evolving needs for stronger security.  It updates RFCs 4250, 4253, 4432, and 4462.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Baushkeddatej÷®hfilenamegrfc9142eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130943hkeywordsò!d3DEScAESxAdvanced Encryption StandardjCurve25519hCurve448bDHnDiffie-HellmanxDigital Encryption StandardcECCdECDHxElliptic Curve CryptographyxElliptic Curve Diffie-HellmancFFCxFinite Field CryptographycIFCx"Integer Factorization CryptographydMODPcMTIvMandatory to ImplementsModular ExponentialsPublic Key ExchangecRSAeSHA-2xSecure Shell Key ExchangelSecure ShellqSecurity StrengthjTriple-DESdsha1fsha256fsha384fsha512eSHA-1vModular ExponentiationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea·2granking#∂crfcd9142irfcNumber#∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLKey Exchange (KEX) Method Updates and Recommendations for Secure Shell (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThe IETF FTP service running at ftp.ietf.org, ops.ietf.org, and ietf.org will be retired.  A number of published RFCs in the IETF and IAB streams include URIs that reference this FTP service.  To ensure that the materials referenced using the IETF FTP service can still be found, this document updates the FTP-based references in these affected documents with HTTPS URIs.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxSoftware Engineering InstitutednamejR. Danyliwddatej–phfilenamegrfc9141eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130942hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaóO÷granking#µcrfcd9141irfcNumber#µestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Updating References to the IETF FTP ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) provides support for multiple authentication methods.  This document defines the EAP-NOOB authentication method for nimble out-of-band (OOB) authentication and key derivation.  The EAP method is intended for bootstrapping all kinds of Internet-of-Things (IoT) devices that have no preconfigured authentication credentials.  The method makes use of a user-assisted, one-directional, out-of-band (OOB) message between the peer device and authentication server to authenticate the in-band key exchange.  The device must have a nonnetwork input or output interface, such as a display, microphone, speaker, or blinking light, that can send or receive dynamically generated messages of tens of bytes in length.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamegT. Aura¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehM. Sethi¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamekA. Peltonenddatej÷©hfilenamegrfc9140eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130941hkeywordsÖlIoT securitymcybersecurityxnetwork access authorizationx"Extensible Authentication Protocollkey exchangekobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateaŒ˙granking#¥crfcd9140irfcNumber#¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Nimble Out-of-Band Authentication for EAP (EAP-NOOB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9965¥habstractyåThis document defines a convergence layer for Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) and Named Data Networking (NDN) over IEEE 802.15.4 Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (LoWPANs). A new frame format is specified to adapt CCNx and NDN packets to the small MTU size of IEEE 802.15.4. For that, syntactic and semantic changes to the TLV-based header formats are described. To support compatibility with other LoWPAN technologies that may coexist on a wireless medium, the dispatching scheme provided by IPv6 over LoWPAN (6LoWPAN) is extended to include new dispatch types for CCNx and NDN. Additionally, the fragmentation component of the 6LoWPAN dispatching framework is applied to Information-Centric Network (ICN) chunks. In its second part, the document defines stateless and stateful compression schemes to improve efficiency on constrained links. Stateless compression reduces TLV expressions to static header fields for common use cases. Stateful compression schemes elide states local to the LoWPAN and replace names in Data packets by short local identifiers.

This document is a product of the IRTF Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamemC. G√ºndoƒüan¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationtlink-lab & FU BerlindnamelM. W√§hlisch¢kaffiliationsUniversity of BaseldnameiC. Scherb¢kaffiliationsUniversity of BaseldnameiC. Marxer¢kaffiliationsUniversity of BaseldnamekC. Tschudinddatej÷|hfilenamegrfc9139eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130940hkeywordsÜx!Content-Centric Networking (CCNx)xNamed Data Networking (NDN)rheader compressionmfragmentationg6LoWPANxInternet of Things (IoT)kobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDatea¶ΩZgranking#≥crfcd9139irfcNumber#≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexfInformation-Centric Networking (ICN) Adaptation to Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (LoWPANs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyìThis document provides the functionalities and design considerations for a Name Resolution Service (NRS) in Information-Centric Networking (ICN).  The purpose of an NRS in ICN is to translate an object name into some other information such as a locator, another name, etc.  in order to forward the object request.  This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Hong¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamefT. You¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies Inc.dnamegL. Dong¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies Inc.dnamekC. Westphal¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Ohlmanddatej–ghfilenamegrfc9138eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130939hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea®œgranking#≤crfcd9138irfcNumber#≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexYDesign Considerations for Name Resolution Service in Information-Centric Networking (ICN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyæThe IETF ordinarily holds three in-person meetings per year to discuss issues and advance the Internet.  However, various events can make a planned in-person meeting infeasible.  This document provides criteria to aid the IETF Administration LLC (IETF LLC), the Internet Engineering Steering Group (IESG), and the Chair of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF) in deciding to relocate, virtualize, postpone, or cancel an in-person IETF meeting.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqF5 Networks, Inc.dnamegM. Dukeddatej÷®hfilenamegrfc9137eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeshmoodfullx*shmoo - Stay Home Meet Occasionally Onlinednamex"Stay Home Meet Occasionally Onlinedtypebwgbidjdoc-130938hkeywordsÉjvirtualizehpostponedmovekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateae;ˆgranking#±crfcd9137irfcNumber#±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‚etotaletitlex0Considerations for Cancellation of IETF MeetingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThe BGP MPLS-based Ethernet VPN (EVPN) (RFC 7432) mechanism provides a flexible control plane that allows intra-subnet connectivity in an MPLS and/or Network Virtualization Overlay (NVO) (RFC 7365) network.  In some networks, there is also a need for dynamic and efficient inter-subnet connectivity across Tenant Systems and end devices that can be physical or virtual and do not necessarily participate in dynamic routing protocols.  This document defines a new EVPN route type for the advertisement of IP prefixes and explains some use-case examples where this new route type is used.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamefW. Lin¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassiddatej÷%hfilenamegrfc9136eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130937hkeywordsÖcRT5dRT-5fType-5nInterface-lessmInterface-fulkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateagBUgranking#∞crfcd9136irfcNumber#∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.IP Prefix Advertisement in Ethernet VPN (EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEthernet VPN (EVPN) provides an extensible and flexible multihoming VPN solution over an MPLS/IP network for intra-subnet connectivity among Tenant Systems and end devices that can be physical or virtual.  However, there are scenarios for which there is a need for a dynamic and efficient inter-subnet connectivity among these Tenant Systems and end devices while maintaining the multihoming capabilities of EVPN.  This document describes an Integrated Routing and Bridging (IRB) solution based on EVPN to address such requirements.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Thoria¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej÷¶hfilenamegrfc9135eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130936hkeywordsÖcIRBwinter-subnet-forwardingisymmetricjasymmetrichmobilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateagB␀granking#Øcrfcd9135irfcNumber#ØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Integrated Routing and Bridging in Ethernet VPN (EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThis document specifies a Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format to be used for transporting video encoded with JPEG XS (ISO/IEC 21122).  JPEG XS is a low-latency, lightweight image coding system.  Compared to an uncompressed video use case, it allows higher resolutions and video frame rates while offering visually lossless quality, reduced power consumption, and encoding-decoding latency confined to a fraction of a video frame.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgintoPIXdnamelT. Bruylants¢kaffiliationiUCLouvaindnamekA. Descampe¢kaffiliationgintoPIXdnameiC. Damman¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer IISdnamejT. Richterddatej÷¶hfilenamegrfc9134eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130935hkeywordsâevideoitransporthprotocolejointlphotographicgexpertsegroupireal-timefstreamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea{4granking#Æcrfcd9134irfcNumber#ÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.RTP Payload Format for ISO/IEC 21122 (JPEG XS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document specifies an extension to the Distributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) signal channel protocol so that DOTS clients can control their filtering rules when an attack mitigation is active.

Particularly, this extension allows a DOTS client to activate or deactivate existing filtering rules during a Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attack. The characterization of these filtering rules is conveyed by a DOTS client during an 'idle' time (i.e., no mitigation is active) by means of the DOTS data channel protocol.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamelK. Nishizuka¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationgLepidumdnameiT. Nagataddatej÷|hfilenamegrfc9133eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130934hkeywordsäjMitigationjAutomationiFilteringuProtective NetworkingrProtected NetworkshSecurityiAnti-DDoShReactivexCollaborative NetworkingvCollaborative SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea1VÎgranking#≠crfcd9133irfcNumber#≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkControlling Filtering Rules Using Distributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) Signal ChanneldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€This document specifies the Distributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) signal channel, a protocol for signaling the need for protection against Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attacks to a server capable of enabling network traffic mitigation on behalf of the requesting client.

A companion document defines the DOTS data channel, a separate reliable communication layer for DOTS management and configuration purposes.

This document obsoletes RFC 8782.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Shallow¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamejT. Reddy.Kddatej÷ìhfilenamegrfc9132eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130933hkeywordsêhsecurityjmitigationpservice deliverylconnectivityianti-DDoSjautomationkcooperationjresilienceifilteringosecurity centerimitigatoriscrubbingxdynamic service protectionrdynamic mitigationvcooperative networkinguprotective networkingkobsoletedByÄepageskopublicationDatea1VŸgranking#¨crfcd9132irfcNumber#¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWDistributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) Signal Channel SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFNeighbor Discovery (RFC 4861) is used by IPv6 nodes to determine the link-layer addresses of neighboring nodes as well as to discover and maintain reachability information.  This document updates RFC 4861 to allow routers to proactively create a Neighbor Cache entry when a new IPv6 address is assigned to a node.  It also updates RFC 4861 and recommends that nodes send unsolicited Neighbor Advertisements upon assigning a new IPv6 address.  These changes will minimize the delay and packet loss when a node initiates connections to an off-link destination from a new IPv6 address.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkovaddatej÷•hfilenamegrfc9131eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130932hkeywordsÑdIPv6eSLAACx#stateless address autoconfigurationvneighbor advertisementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaWpgranking#´crfcd9131irfcNumber#´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqPr