sed to configure and manage devices supporting Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM).  The model covers the PIM protocol configuration, operational state, and event notifications data.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationsMetaswitch NetworksdnamemP. McAllister¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamehA. Peter¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelM. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefY. Liu¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnameeF. Huddatej÷"hfilenamegrfc9128eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130929hkeywordsÉrNetwork ManagementcPIMdYANGkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDatecPú`granking#®crfcd9128irfcNumber#®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8YANG Data Model for Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÈThis document defines a YANG data model that can be used to configure and manage Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD).

The YANG modules in this document conform to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) (RFC 8342).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehL. Zheng¢kaffiliationsXoriant CorporationdnameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamemS. Pallagatti¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirskyddatej“≥hfilenamegrfc9127eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130928hkeywordsÜpLiveliness checkcBGPdOSPFeIS-ISfTCP-AOcMD5kobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDatearXgranking#ßcrfcd9127irfcNumber#ßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<YANG Data Model for Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9314∂habstractyEThis document defines the pushed authorization request (PAR) endpoint, which allows clients to push the payload of an OAuth 2.0 authorization request to the authorization server via a direct request and provides them with a request URI that is used as reference to the data in a subsequent call to the authorization endpoint.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgyes.comdnamenT. Lodderstedt¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationnNAT.ConsultingdnamekN. Sakimura¢kaffiliationxMoneyhub Financial TechnologydnamehD. Tonge¢kaffiliationeAuth0dnameiF. Skokanddatej÷ßhfilenamegrfc9126eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130927hkeywordsÇhsecurityfoauth2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaBHégranking#¶crfcd9126irfcNumber#¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'OAuth 2.0 Pushed Authorization RequestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘Data centers are attached to the Internet or a backbone network by gateway routers. One data center typically has more than one gateway for commercial, load-balancing, and resiliency reasons. Other sites, such as access networks, also need to be connected across backbone networks through gateways.

This document defines a mechanism using the BGP Tunnel Encapsulation attribute to allow data center gateway routers to advertise routes to the prefixes reachable in the site, including advertising them on behalf of other gateways at the same site. This allows segment routing to be used to identify multiple paths across the Internet or backbone network between different gateways. The paths can be selected for load-balancing, resilience, and quality purposes.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalilddatej÷¶hfilenamegrfc9125eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130926hkeywordsÉbSRbGWcBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea*n€granking#•crfcd9125irfcNumber#•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Gateway Auto-Discovery and Route Advertisement for Site Interconnection Using Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrVulnerabilities with Internet of Things (IoT) devices have raised the need for a reliable and secure firmware update mechanism that is also suitable for constrained devices. Ensuring that devices function and remain secure over their service lifetime requires such an update mechanism to fix vulnerabilities, update configuration settings, and add new functionality.

One component of such a firmware update is a concise and machine-processable metadata document, or manifest, that describes the firmware image(s) and offers appropriate protection. This document describes the information that must be present in the manifest.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamehB. Moran¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer SITdnamekH. Birkholzddatej÷•hfilenamegrfc9124eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsuitdfullx.suit - Software Updates for Internet of Thingsdnamex'Software Updates for Internet of Thingsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130925hkeywordsÇqcomputer securitymsmart objectskobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDatea·‘–granking#§crfcd9124irfcNumber#§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA Manifest Information Model for Firmware Updates in Internet of Things (IoT) DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Sieve Email Filtering Language (RFC 5228) is a popular email filtering language used upon final mail delivery.  This document creates a registry for Sieve actions to help developers and Sieve extension writers track interactions between different extensions.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamelK. Murchisonddatej÷‡hfilenamegrfc9122eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130924hkeywordsÅeSievekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatedõÄïgranking#¢crfcd9122irfcNumber#¢estateÉcRFCjI-D ExistsiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIANA Registry for Sieve ActionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document deprecates the use of any "int" domain names that were designated for infrastructure purposes by the IETF, and it identifies them for removal from the "int" top-level domain.  Any implementations that involve these domains are now deprecated.  This document also changes the status of RFC 1528 and RFC 1706 to Historic.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIANAdnameiK. Davies¢kaffiliationdIANAdnamehA. Baberddatej–áhfilenamegrfc9121eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130923hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatedL7fgranking#°crfcd9121irfcNumber#°estateÉcRFCjI-D ExistsiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Deprecating Infrastructure "int" DomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx∏This document describes revisions to operational practices to separate the function of the "arpa" top-level domain in the DNS from its historical operation alongside the DNS root zone.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiK. Davies¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Arkkoddatej”Thfilenamegrfc9120eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130922hkeywordsÜiroot zonedIANAptop-level domainproot nameserverscDNSdARPAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea|B∑granking#†crfcd9120irfcNumber#†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexFNameservers for the Address and Routing Parameter Area ("arpa") DomaindtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEWell-known issues with multicast have prevented the deployment of multicast in 802.11 (Wi-Fi) and other local-area wireless environments.  This document describes the known limitations of wireless (primarily 802.11) Layer 2 multicast.  Also described are certain multicast enhancement features that have been specified by the IETF and by IEEE 802 for wireless media, as well as some operational choices that can be made to improve the performance of the network.  Finally, some recommendations are provided about the usage and combination of these features and operational choices.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkLupin LodgednamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamejM. McBride¢kaffiliationcHPEdnamejD. Stanley¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationfSIGFOXdnamelJC. Z√∫√±igaddatej÷ßhfilenamegrfc9119eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130921hkeywordsÜiMulticastxIEEE 802 Wireless MulticastiBroadcastcBUMdwifihwirelesskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea`»Ûgranking#ücrfcd9119irfcNumber#üestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Multicast Considerations over IEEE 802 Wireless MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 8226 specifies the use of certificates for Secure Telephone Identity Credentials; these certificates are often called "Secure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) Certificates".  RFC 8226 provides a certificate extension to constrain the JSON Web Token (JWT) claims that can be included in the Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT), as defined in RFC 8225.  If the PASSporT signer includes a JWT claim outside the constraint boundaries, then the PASSporT recipient will reject the entire PASSporT.  This document updates RFC 8226; it provides all of the capabilities available in the original certificate extension as well as an additional way to constrain the allowable JWT claims.  The enhanced extension can also provide a list of claims that are not allowed to be included in the PASSporT.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷•hfilenamegrfc9118eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130920hkeywordsÅxX.509 Certificate ExtensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea$ägranking#ûcrfcd9118irfcNumber#ûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkEnhanced JSON Web Token (JWT) Claim Constraints for Secure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This document describes a modification to the validation procedure defined for the dissemination of BGP Flow Specifications. The dissemination of BGP Flow Specifications as specified in RFC 8955 requires that the originator of the Flow Specification match the originator of the best-match unicast route for the destination prefix embedded in the Flow Specification. For an Internal Border Gateway Protocol (iBGP) received route, the originator is typically a border router within the same autonomous system (AS). The objective is to allow only BGP speakers within the data forwarding path to originate BGP Flow Specifications. Sometimes it is desirable to originate the BGP Flow Specification from any place within the autonomous system itself, for example, from a centralized BGP route controller. However, the validation procedure described in RFC 8955 will fail in this scenario. The modification proposed herein relaxes the validation rule to enable Flow Specifications to be originated within the same autonomous system as the BGP speaker performing the validation. Additionally, this document revises the AS_PATH validation rules so Flow Specifications received from an External Border Gateway Protocol (eBGP) peer can be validated when such a peer is a BGP route server.

This document updates the validation procedure in RFC 8955.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejJ. Alcaide¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Smith¢kaffiliationpSproute NetworksdnamelP. Mohapatraddatej÷•hfilenamegrfc9117eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130919hkeywordsÅlBGP flowspeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea$âÌgranking#ùcrfcd9117irfcNumber#ùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Revised Validation Procedure for BGP Flow SpecificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfWhen security vulnerabilities are discovered by researchers, proper reporting channels are often lacking.  As a result, vulnerabilities may be left unreported.  This document defines a machine-parsable format ("security.txt") to help organizations describe their vulnerability disclosure practices to make it easier for researchers to report vulnerabilities.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiE. Foudil¢kaffiliationxNightwatch CybersecuritydnameoY. Shafranovichddatej–bhfilenamegrfc9116eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130918hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebiöÕgranking#úcrfcd9116irfcNumber#úestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9A File Format to Aid in Security Vulnerability DisclosuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛This document defines a profile of the Automatic Certificate Management Environment (ACME) protocol by which the holder of an identifier (e.g., a domain name) can allow a third party to obtain an X.509 certificate such that the certificate subject is the delegated identifier while the certified public key corresponds to a private key controlled by the third party.  A primary use case is that of a Content Delivery Network (CDN), the third party, terminating TLS sessions on behalf of a content provider (the holder of a domain name).  The presented mechanism allows the holder of the identifier to retain control over the delegation and revoke it at any time.  Importantly, this mechanism does not require any modification to the deployed TLS clients and servers.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfIntuitdnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnameiD. L√≥pez¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnameqA. Pastor Perales¢kaffiliationcARMdnamejT. Fossatiddatej÷£hfilenamegrfc9115eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130917hkeywordsÇxContent Delivery NetworkcCDNkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateaI$Êgranking#õcrfcd9115irfcNumber#õestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdAn Automatic Certificate Management Environment (ACME) Profile for Generating Delegated CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThe QUIC transport protocol has several features that are desirable in a transport for HTTP, such as stream multiplexing, per-stream flow control, and low-latency connection establishment.  This document describes a mapping of HTTP semantics over QUIC.  This document also identifies HTTP/2 features that are subsumed by QUIC and describes how HTTP/2 extensions can be ported to HTTP/3.fadNamesZaheduzzaman Sarkerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnameiM. Bishopddatej÷ñhfilenamegrfc9114eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdquicdfullkquic - QUICdnamedQUICdtypebwgbidjdoc-130916hkeywordsÜiTransportdQUICfHTTP/2eHPACKeQPACKcWebkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDatebû^’granking#öcrfcd9114irfcNumber#öestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlefHTTP/3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis specification describes an optimized expression of the semantics of the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP), referred to as HTTP version 2 (HTTP/2). HTTP/2 enables a more efficient use of network resources and a reduced latency by introducing field compression and allowing multiple concurrent exchanges on the same connection.

This document obsoletes RFCs 7540 and 8740.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekC. Benfieldddatej÷µhfilenamegrfc9113eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130915hkeywordsÉdHTTPdSPDYcWebkobsoletedByÄepagesNopublicationDatebû^‘granking#ôcrfcd9113irfcNumber#ôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlefHTTP/2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty:The Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) is a stateless application-level protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypertext information systems. This document specifies the HTTP/1.1 message syntax, message parsing, connection management, and related security concerns.

This document obsoletes portions of RFC 7230.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej÷™hfilenamegrfc9112eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130914hkeywordsÉxHypertext Transfer ProtocoldHTTPsHTTP message formatkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDatebû^‘granking#òcrfcd9112irfcNumber#òestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumbercetotaletitlehHTTP/1.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9931∑habstracty:The Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) is a stateless application-level protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypertext information systems. This document defines HTTP caches and the associated header fields that control cache behavior or indicate cacheable response messages.

This document obsoletes RFC 7234.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej÷™hfilenamegrfc9111eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130913hkeywordsÉxHypertext Transfer ProtocoldHTTPlHTTP CachingkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDatebû^‘granking#ócrfcd9111irfcNumber#óestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberbetotaletitlelHTTP CachingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty6The Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) is a stateless application-level protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypertext information systems. This document describes the overall architecture of HTTP, establishes common terminology, and defines aspects of the protocol that are shared by all versions. In this definition are core protocol elements, extensibility mechanisms, and the "http" and "https" Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) schemes.

This document updates RFC 3864 and obsoletes RFCs 2818, 7231, 7232, 7233, 7235, 7538, 7615, 7694, and portions of 7230.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej÷´hfilenamegrfc9110eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130912hkeywordsÜxHypertext Transfer ProtocoldHTTPnHTTP semanticslHTTP contentkHTTP methodpHTTP status codekobsoletedByÄepages¬opublicationDatebû^œgranking#ñcrfcd9110irfcNumber#ñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberaetotaletitlenHTTP SemanticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThe Network Time Protocol (NTP) can operate in several modes.  Some of these modes are based on the receipt of unsolicited packets and therefore require the use of a well-known port as the local port.  However, in the case of NTP modes where the use of a well-known port is not required, employing such a well-known port unnecessarily facilitates the ability of attackers to perform blind/off-path attacks.  This document formally updates RFC 5905, recommending the use of transport-protocol ephemeral port randomization for those modes where use of the NTP well-known port is not required.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegG. Gont¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejM. Lichvarddatej÷£hfilenamegrfc9109eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130911hkeywordsÇhsecuritystransport protocolskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatea$uÂgranking#ïcrfcd9109irfcNumber#ïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Network Time Protocol Version 4: Port RandomizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document introduces the YANG module "iana-dns-class-rr-type", which contains derived types reflecting two IANA registries: DNS CLASSes and Resource Record (RR) TYPEs.  These YANG types are intended as the minimum basis for future data modeling work.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnameiL. Lhotka¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamekP. ≈†paƒçekddatej÷öhfilenamegrfc9108eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130910hkeywordsÇmIANA registrynDNS ParameterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea9/‚granking#îcrfcd9108irfcNumber#îestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4YANG Types for DNS Classes and Resource Record TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúThis document defines an extension to BGP route reflectors. On route reflectors, BGP route selection is modified in order to choose the best route from the standpoint of their clients, rather than from the standpoint of the route reflectors themselves. Depending on the scaling and precision requirements, route selection can be specific for one client, common for a set of clients, or common for all clients of a route reflector. This solution is particularly applicable in deployments using centralized route reflectors, where choosing the best route based on the route reflector's IGP location is suboptimal. This facilitates, for example, a "best exit point" policy ("hot potato routing").

The solution relies upon all route reflectors learning all paths that are eligible for consideration. BGP route selection is performed in the route reflectors based on the IGP cost from configured locations in the link-state IGP.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwNTT Network InnovationsdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliation`dnameiC. Cassar¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. √Öman¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegK. Wangddatej÷£hfilenamegrfc9107eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130909hkeywordsÅcIDRkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatea$~mgranking#ìcrfcd9107irfcNumber#ìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&BGP Optimal Route Reflection (BGP ORR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyIThis document describes the Argon2 memory-hard function for password hashing and proof-of-work applications.  We provide an implementer-oriented description with test vectors.  The purpose is to simplify adoption of Argon2 for Internet protocols.  This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of LuxembourgdnamekA. Biryukov¢kaffiliationxUniversity of LuxembourgdnamegD. Dinu¢kaffiliationoABDK ConsultingdnameoD. Khovratovich¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefssonddatej÷ûhfilenamegrfc9106eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130908hkeywordságArgon2dgArgon2ihArgon2idcKDFnCryptocurrencyxTime-Space Trade-Off AttackshSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea8Ygranking#ícrfcd9106irfcNumber#íestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexOArgon2 Memory-Hard Function for Password Hashing and Proof-of-Work ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis document defines a Terminal Access Controller Access-Control System Plus (TACACS+) client YANG module that augments the System Management data model, defined in RFC 7317, to allow devices to make use of TACACS+ servers for centralized Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA). Though being a standard module, this module does not endorse the security mechanisms of the TACACS+ protocol (RFC 8907), and TACACS+ be used within a secure deployment.

The YANG module in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) defined in RFC 8342.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeB. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehG. Zheng¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Wangddatej–Shfilenamegrfc9105eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130907hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9950epagesopublicationDateaZLgranking#ëcrfcd9105irfcNumber#ëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA YANG Data Model for Terminal Access Controller Access-Control System Plus (TACACS+)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹Administrative groups are link attributes used for traffic engineering.  This document defines an extension to the Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) for advertisement of extended administrative groups (EAGs).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegZ. Wang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikarddatej÷öhfilenamegrfc9104eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130906hkeywordsÅtInter-Domain RoutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaegranking#êcrfcd9104irfcNumber#êestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzDistribution of Traffic Engineering Extended Administrative Groups Using the Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒDNS zone transfers are transmitted in cleartext, which gives attackers the opportunity to collect the content of a zone by eavesdropping on network connections.  The DNS Transaction Signature (TSIG) mechanism is specified to restrict direct zone transfer to authorized clients only, but it does not add confidentiality.  This document specifies the use of TLS, rather than cleartext, to prevent zone content collection via passive monitoring of zone transfers: XFR over TLS (XoT).  Additionally, this specification updates RFC 1995 and RFC 5936 with respect to efficient use of TCP connections and RFC 7766 with respect to the recommended number of connections between a client and server for each transport.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnameiW. Toorop¢kaffiliationjSinodun ITdnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationnBrave SoftwarednamehS. Sahib¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamegP. Aras¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednameiA. Mankinddatej÷ühfilenamegrfc9103eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130905hkeywordsÉcDNSjoperationsgprivacykobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDatea$u‘granking#ècrfcd9103irfcNumber#èestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNS Zone Transfer over TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyeThis document describes an experimental TLS extension for the in-band transport of the complete set of records that can be validated by DNSSEC and that are needed to perform DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) of a TLS server. This extension obviates the need to perform separate, out-of-band DNS lookups. When the requisite DNS records do not exist, the extension conveys a denial-of-existence proof that can be validated.

This experimental extension is developed outside the IETF and is published here to guide implementation of the extension and to ensure interoperability among implementations.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiTwo SigmadnamekV. Dukhovni¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamehS. Huque¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnameiW. Toorop¢kaffiliationeAivendnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamehM. Shoreddatej–_hfilenamegrfc9102eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130904hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateadÙgranking#écrfcd9102irfcNumber#éestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexTLS DNSSEC Chain ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡The authorization request in OAuth 2.0 described in RFC 6749 utilizes query parameter serialization, which means that authorization request parameters are encoded in the URI of the request and sent through user agents such as web browsers. While it is easy to implement, it means that a) the communication through the user agents is not integrity protected and thus, the parameters can be tainted, b) the source of the communication is not authenticated, and c) the communication through the user agents can be monitored. Because of these weaknesses, several attacks to the protocol have now been put forward.

This document introduces the ability to send request parameters in a JSON Web Token (JWT) instead, which allows the request to be signed with JSON Web Signature (JWS) and encrypted with JSON Web Encryption (JWE) so that the integrity, source authentication, and confidentiality properties of the authorization request are attained. The request can be sent by value or by reference.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnNAT.ConsultingdnamekN. Sakimura¢kaffiliationfYubicodnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej÷†hfilenamegrfc9101eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130903hkeywordsåiAssertioneClaimnSecurity TokeneOAuthxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONnJSON Web TokencJWTrJSON Web SignaturecJWSsJSON Web EncryptioncJWEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea ≠granking#çcrfcd9101irfcNumber#çestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNThe OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: JWT-Secured Authorization Request (JAR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This short document updates RFC 8428, "Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML)", by specifying the use of independently selectable "SenML Features" and mapping them to SenML version numbers.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej”1hfilenamegrfc9100eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130902hkeywordsÑxInternet of Things (IoT)rInternet of ThingscIOTjdata modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea·ágranking#åcrfcd9100irfcNumber#åestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML) Features and VersionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—Knowledge and experience on how to operate IPv4 networks securely is available, whether the operator is an Internet Service Provider (ISP) or an enterprise internal network. However, IPv6 presents some new security challenges. RFC 4942 describes security issues in the protocol, but network managers also need a more practical, operations-minded document to enumerate advantages and/or disadvantages of certain choices.

This document analyzes the operational security issues associated with several types of networks and proposes technical and procedural mitigation techniques. This document is only applicable to managed networks, such as enterprise networks, service provider networks, or managed residential networks.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamej√â. Vyncke¢kaffiliation`dnameoK. Chittimaneni¢kaffiliationtDouble Shot SecuritydnamegM. Kaeo¢kaffiliationdERNWdnamefE. Reyddatej÷°hfilenamegrfc9099eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-130901hkeywordsÉdIPv6hSecuritytOperational SecuritykobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateav€granking#ãcrfcd9099irfcNumber#ãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Operational Security Considerations for IPv6 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˘This document summarizes the operational implications of IPv6 extension headers specified in the IPv6 protocol specification (RFC 8200) and attempts to analyze reasons why packets with IPv6 extension headers are often dropped in the public Internet.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationdINEXdnamekN. Hilliard¢kaffiliationkSpaceNet AGdnamejG. Doering¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liuddatej…€hfilenamegrfc9098eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130900hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaBH~granking#äcrfcd9098irfcNumber#äestateÅiPublishedfs