DMARC distinguishes the portion of a name that is a Public Suffix Domain (PSD), below which Organizational Domain names are created. The basic DMARC capability allows Organizational Domains to specify policies that apply to their subdomains, but it does not give that capability to PSDs. This document describes an extension to DMARC to fully enable DMARC functionality for PSDs.

Some implementations of DMARC consider a PSD to be ineligible for DMARC enforcement. This specification addresses that case.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvfTLD Registry ServicesdnamelS. Kitterman¢kaffiliation`dnamekT. Wicinskiddatej÷¢hfilenamegrfc9091eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130893hkeywordsÉeDMARCtemail authenticationcTLDkobsoletedByÅd9989epagesopublicationDate`ˇ∂=granking#Écrfcd9091irfcNumber#ÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxExperimental Domain-Based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) Extension for Public Suffix DomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThe Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR), defined in RFC 8949, is a data format whose design goals include the possibility of extremely small code size, fairly small message size, and extensibility without the need for version negotiation.

This document defines CBOR tags for object identifiers (OIDs) and is the reference document for the IANA registration of the CBOR tags so defined.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷ühfilenamegrfc9090eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130892hkeywordsÖmbinary formatwdata interchange formateASN.1cOIDqObject IdentifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`»granking#Çcrfcd9090irfcNumber#ÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGConcise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) Tags for Object IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) has defined a mechanism to load-balance traffic flows using Entropy Labels (EL).  An ingress Label Switching Router (LSR) cannot insert ELs for packets going into a given Label Switched Path (LSP) unless an egress LSR has indicated via signaling that it has the capability to process ELs, referred to as the Entropy Label Capability (ELC), on that LSP.  In addition, it would be useful for ingress LSRs to know each LSR's capability for reading the maximum label stack depth and performing EL-based load-balancing, referred to as Entropy Readable Label Depth (ERLD).  This document defines a mechanism to signal these two capabilities using OSPFv2 and OSPFv3, and Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCapitalonlinednameeX. Xu¢kaffiliation`dnamegS. Kini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehM. Bocciddatej–'hfilenamegrfc9089eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130891hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea«Égranking#Åcrfcd9089irfcNumber#ÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNSignaling Entropy Label Capability and Entropy Readable Label Depth Using OSPFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) has defined a mechanism to load-balance traffic flows using Entropy Labels (EL).  An ingress Label Switching Router (LSR) cannot insert ELs for packets going into a given Label Switched Path (LSP) unless an egress LSR has indicated via signaling that it has the capability to process ELs, referred to as the Entropy Label Capability (ELC), on that LSP.  In addition, it would be useful for ingress LSRs to know each LSR's capability for reading the maximum label stack depth and performing EL-based load-balancing, referred to as Entropy Readable Label Depth (ERLD).  This document defines a mechanism to signal these two capabilities using IS-IS and Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCapitalonlinednameeX. Xu¢kaffiliation`dnamegS. Kini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehM. Bocciddatej–hfilenamegrfc9088eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130890hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea«Çgranking#Äcrfcd9088irfcNumber#ÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSignaling Entropy Label Capability and Entropy Readable Label Depth Using IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜSegment Routing (SR) leverages source routing. A node steers a packet through a controlled set of instructions, called segments, by prepending the packet with an SR header. A segment can represent any instruction, topological or service based. SR allows for the enforcement of a flow through any topological path while maintaining per-flow state only at the ingress node of the SR domain.

The Segment Routing architecture can be directly applied to the MPLS data plane with no change on the forwarding plane. It requires a minor extension to the existing link-state routing protocols.

This document illustrates the application of Segment Routing to solve the BGP Egress Peer Engineering (BGP-EPE) requirement. The SR-based BGP-EPE solution allows a centralized (Software-Defined Networking, or SDN) controller to program any egress peer policy at ingress border routers or at hosts within the domain.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehG. Dawra¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehE. Aries¢kaffiliationfYandexdnamelD. AfanasievddatejŒãhfilenamegrfc9087eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130889hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea«Ägranking#crfcd9087irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Segment Routing Centralized BGP Egress Peer EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûA node steers a packet through a controlled set of instructions, called segments, by prepending the packet with a list of segment identifiers (SIDs). A segment can represent any instruction, topological or service based. SR segments allow steering a flow through any topological path and service chain while maintaining per-flow state only at the ingress node of the SR domain.

This document describes an extension to Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) for advertisement of BGP Peering Segments along with their BGP peering node information so that efficient BGP Egress Peer Engineering (EPE) policies and strategies can be computed based on Segment Routing.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamefS. Ray¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dongddatej÷Nhfilenamegrfc9086eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130888hkeywordsÉcBGPfBGP-LSoSegment RoutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea«qgranking#~crfcd9086irfcNumber#~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhBorder Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) Extensions for Segment Routing BGP Egress Peer EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµSegment Routing (SR) allows for a flexible definition of end-to-end paths by encoding paths as sequences of topological subpaths, called "segments". These segments are advertised by routing protocols, e.g., by the link-state routing protocols (IS-IS, OSPFv2, and OSPFv3) within IGP topologies.

This document defines extensions to the Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) address family in order to carry SR information via BGP.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Chenddatej÷Vhfilenamegrfc9085eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130887hkeywordsàfBGP-LSoSegment RoutingcSIDdMPLSsLabel advertisementeIS-ISdOSPFfOSPFv3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea≈Œgranking#}crfcd9085irfcNumber#}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLBorder Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) Extensions for Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9356∂habstractyKThis document defines OSPF extensions to include information associated with the node originating a prefix along with the prefix advertisement.  These extensions do not change the core OSPF route computation functionality but provide useful information for network analysis, troubleshooting, and use cases like traffic engineering.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegA. Wang¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikarddatej÷õhfilenamegrfc9084eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130886hkeywordsÅdOSPFkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatea≈Ωgranking#|crfcd9084irfcNumber#|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!OSPF Prefix Originator ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty,This document describes JSON data structures representing registration information maintained by Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) and Domain Name Registries (DNRs).  These data structures are used to form Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) query responses.  This document obsoletes RFC 7483.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationcAWSdnameiA. Newtonddatej–Phfilenamegrfc9083eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130885hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesQopublicationDate` @Ïgranking#{crfcd9083irfcNumber#{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber_etotaletitlex?JSON Responses for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÜThis document describes uniform patterns to construct HTTP URLs that may be used to retrieve registration information from registries (including both Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) and Domain Name Registries (DNRs)) using "RESTful" web access patterns.  These uniform patterns define the query syntax for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP).  This document obsoletes RFC 7482.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationcAWSdnameiA. Newtonddatej–Phfilenamegrfc9082eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130884hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate` @‡granking#zcrfcd9082irfcNumber#zestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber_etotaletitlex5Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Query FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜This document specifies the procedures for interoperation between Multicast Virtual Private Network (MVPN) Source-Active (SA) routes and customer Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP) SA routes, which is useful for MVPN provider networks offering services to customers with an existing MSDP infrastructure.  Without the procedures described in this document, VPN-specific MSDP sessions are required among the Provider Edge (PE) routers that are customer MSDP peers.  This document updates RFC 6514.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekL. Giulianoddatej–]hfilenamegrfc9081eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130883hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˙pÉgranking#ycrfcd9081irfcNumber#yestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉInteroperation between Multicast Virtual Private Network (MVPN) and Multicast Source Directory Protocol (MSDP) Source-Active RoutesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˜This document defines the exact subset of the Babel routing protocol and its extensions that is required by an implementation of the Homenet protocol suite, as well as the interactions between the Home Networking Control Protocol (HNCP) and Babel.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx"IRIF / University of Paris-DiderotdnamemJ. Chroboczekddatejœ¿hfilenamegrfc9080eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130882hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea(Sgranking#xcrfcd9080irfcNumber#xestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Homenet Profile of the Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBSource-specific routing, also known as Source Address Dependent Routing (SADR), is an extension to traditional next-hop routing where packets are forwarded according to both their destination address and their source address.  This document describes an extension for source-specific routing to the Babel routing protocol.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxIRIF / University of ParisdnamejM. Boutier¢kaffiliationxIRIF / University of ParisdnamemJ. Chroboczekddatej÷ûhfilenamegrfc9079eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130881hkeywordsãdSADRx source address-dependent routingnsource addresskmultihomingx#multihoming with multiple addressesrmultiple addressesxsource address selectionomultiple routesimultipathodisjoint routesoroute diversitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea(Cgranking#wcrfcd9079irfcNumber#westateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Source-Specific Routing in the Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÊThe popularity of social media has led to user comfort with easily signaling basic reactions to an author's posting, such as with a 'thumbs up' or 'smiley' graphic.  This specification permits a similar facility for Internet Mail.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnameiR. Signes¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehN. Freedddatej÷úhfilenamegrfc9078eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130880hkeywordsãhreactioneemojiqsocial networkingeemailfaffectimessaginghemoticongsmileysdlikedmimeereplykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatea!Lgranking#vcrfcd9078irfcNumber#vestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Reaction: Indicating Summary Reaction to a MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKDue to a combination of unfortunate wording in earlier documents, aggressive use of NSEC and NSEC3 records may deny the existence of names far beyond the intended lifetime of a denial.  This document changes the definition of the NSEC and NSEC3 TTL to correct that situation.  This document updates RFCs 4034, 4035, 5155, and 8198.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhPowerDNSdnamekP. van Dijkddatej÷°hfilenamegrfc9077eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130879hkeywordsÉfDNSSECnnegative cachesDenial of ExistencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˙pÇgranking#ucrfcd9077irfcNumber#uestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'NSEC and NSEC3: TTLs and Aggressive UsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document describes the privacy issues associated with the use of the DNS by Internet users.  It provides general observations about typical current privacy practices.  It is intended to be an analysis of the present situation and does not prescribe solutions.  This document obsoletes RFC 7626.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekT. Wicinskiddatej÷öhfilenamegrfc9076eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130878hkeywordsÅcDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˙pagranking#tcrfcd9076irfcNumber#testateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNS Privacy ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty:The Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) pandemic caused changes in Internet user behavior, particularly during the introduction of initial quarantine and work-from-home arrangements. These behavior changes drove changes in Internet traffic.

The Internet Architecture Board (IAB) held a workshop to discuss network impacts of the pandemic on November 9-13, 2020. The workshop was held to convene interested researchers, network operators, network management experts, and Internet technologists to share their experiences. The meeting was held online given the ongoing travel and contact restrictions at that time.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. K√ºhlewind¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Perkinsddatej–[hfilenamegrfc9075eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130877hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˙6ˆgranking#scrfcd9075irfcNumber#sestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex:Report from the IAB COVID-19 Network Impacts Workshop 2020dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≈This document defines a set of extensions to the iCalendar "VALARM" component to enhance the use of alarms and improve interoperability between clients and servers.

This document updates RFC 5545.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamelK. Murchisonddatej÷ïhfilenamegrfc9074eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130876hkeywordsÑfalarmskcalendaringiiCalendarfCalDAVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateagranking#rcrfcd9074irfcNumber#restateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!"VALARM" Extensions for iCalendardtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis specification updates RFC 5545 by introducing a number of new iCalendar properties and components that are of particular use for event publishers and in social networking.

This specification also defines a new "STRUCTURED-DATA" property for iCalendar (RFC 5545) to allow for data that is directly pertinent to an event or task to be included with the calendar data.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhBedeworkdnamekM. Douglassddatej÷àhfilenamegrfc9073eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130875hkeywordsÇiiCalendarjpropertieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaygranking#qcrfcd9073irfcNumber#qestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Event Publishing Extensions to iCalendardtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘The Optional Parameters in the BGP OPEN message as defined in the base BGP specification are limited to 255 octets due to a one-octet length field. BGP capabilities are carried in this field and may foreseeably exceed 255 octets in the future, leading to concerns about this limitation.

This document updates RFC 4271 by extending, in a backward-compatible manner, the length of the Optional Parameters in a BGP OPEN message. The Parameter Length field of individual Optional Parameters is also extended.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrPalo Alto NetworksdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej÷ûhfilenamegrfc9072eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130874hkeywordsÇcIDRcBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`«˝granking#pcrfcd9072irfcNumber#pestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Extended Optional Parameters Length for BGP OPEN MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThis document provides enhancements of real-time text (as specified in RFC 4103) suitable for mixing in a centralized conference model, enabling source identification and rapidly interleaved transmission of text from different sources. The intended use is for real-time text mixers and participant endpoints capable of providing an efficient presentation or other treatment of a multiparty real-time text session. The specified mechanism builds on the standard use of the Contributing Source (CSRC) list in the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) packet for source identification. The method makes use of the same "text/t140" and "text/red" formats as for two-party sessions.

Solutions using multiple RTP streams in the same RTP session are briefly mentioned, as they could have some benefits over the RTP-mixer model. The RTP-mixer model was selected to be used for the fully specified solution in this document because it can be applied to a wide range of existing RTP implementations.

A capability exchange is specified so that it can be verified that a mixer and a participant can handle the multiparty-coded real-time text stream using the RTP-mixer method. The capability is indicated by the use of a Session Description Protocol (SDP) (RFC 8866) media attribute, "rtt-mixer".

This document updates RFC 4103 ("RTP Payload for Text Conversation").

A specification for how a mixer can format text for the case when the endpoint is not multiparty aware is also provided.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhGHAccessdnamemG. Hellstr√∂mddatej÷¢hfilenamegrfc9071eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130873hkeywordsÉjconferencefbridgecSIPkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate`‰Î´granking#ocrfcd9071irfcNumber#oestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1RTP-Mixer Formatting of Multiparty Real-Time TextdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document describes a YANG data model for the Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Distribution Protocol (LDP). The model also serves as the base model to define the Multipoint LDP (mLDP) model.

The YANG modules in this document conform to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Asati¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehS. Esale¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegX. Chen¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamegH. Shahddatej÷rhfilenamegrfc9070eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130872hkeywordsÉdMPLScLDPdYANGkobsoletedByÄepagesOopublicationDateb/≈¸granking#ncrfcd9070irfcNumber#nestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYANG Data Model for MPLS LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThe BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP) defines access to local Routing Information Bases (RIBs).  This document updates BMP (RFC 7854) by adding access to the Local Routing Information Base (Loc-RIB), as defined in RFC 4271.  The Loc-RIB contains the routes that have been selected by the local BGP speaker's Decision Process.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Evens¢kaffiliationnMenlo SecuritydnamelS. Bayraktar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Bhardwaj¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamejP. Lucenteddatej÷•hfilenamegrfc9069eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130871hkeywordsÑcBGPcBMPilocal-ribgloc-ribkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatebôgranking#mcrfcd9069irfcNumber#mestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Support for Local RIB in the BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9736∂habstractyThis specification defines a profile for issuing OAuth 2.0 access tokens in JSON Web Token (JWT) format.  Authorization servers and resource servers from different vendors can leverage this profile to issue and consume access tokens in an interoperable manner.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAuth0dnamekV. Bertocciddatej÷©hfilenamegrfc9068eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130870hkeywordsÑeOAuthhResourcelAccess tokencJWTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaq˚•granking#lcrfcd9068irfcNumber#lestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8JSON Web Token (JWT) Profile for OAuth 2.0 Access TokensdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˝This document defines a YANG data model for configuring and managing routing policies in a vendor-neutral way.  The model provides a generic routing policy framework that can be extended for specific routing protocols using the YANG 'augment' mechanism.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liuddatej–chfilenamegrfc9067eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130869hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateaeugranking#kcrfcd9067irfcNumber#kestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$A YANG Data Model for Routing PolicydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document specifies the Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) signal channel Call Home, which enables a Call Home DOTS server to initiate a secure connection to a Call Home DOTS client and to receive attack traffic information from the Call Home DOTS client. The Call Home DOTS server in turn uses the attack traffic information to identify compromised devices launching outgoing DDoS attacks and take appropriate mitigation action(s).

The DOTS signal channel Call Home is not specific to home networks; the solution targets any deployment in which it is required to block DDoS attack traffic closer to the source(s) of a DDoS attack.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Shallowddatej÷®hfilenamegrfc9066eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130868hkeywordsájAutomationtAnti-DDoS AutomationoDDoS MitigationxCollaborative NetworkinguProtective NetworkinghSecurityiScrubbingkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDatea≤ˆ≥granking#jcrfcd9066irfcNumber#jestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSDistributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) Signal Channel Call HomedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTo protect user data and privacy, Internet transport protocols have supported payload encryption and authentication for some time. Such encryption and authentication are now also starting to be applied to the transport protocol headers. This helps avoid transport protocol ossification by middleboxes, mitigate attacks against the transport protocol, and protect metadata about the communication. Current operational practice in some networks inspect transport header information within the network, but this is no longer possible when those transport headers are encrypted.

This document discusses the possible impact when network traffic uses a protocol with an encrypted transport header. It suggests issues to consider when designing new transport protocols or features.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej÷†hfilenamegrfc9065eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130867hkeywordsÇptransport designxoperations and managementkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDate`ÔŸsgranking#icrfcd9065irfcNumber#iestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Considerations around Transport Header Confidentiality, Network Operations, and the Evolution of Internet Transport ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty2This is a position paper. It documents the author's personal views on how Quality of Service (QoS) capabilities ought to be accommodated in Information-Centric Networking (ICN) protocols like Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) or Named Data Networking (NDN), which employ flow-balanced Interest/Data exchanges and hop-by-hop forwarding state as their fundamental machinery. It argues that such protocols demand a substantially different approach to QoS from that taken in TCP/IP and proposes specific design patterns to achieve both classification and differentiated QoS treatment on both a flow and aggregate basis. It also considers the effect of caches in addition to memory, CPU, and link bandwidth as resources that should be subject to explicitly unfair resource allocation. The proposed methods are intended to operate purely at the network layer, providing the primitives needed to achieve transport- and higher-layer QoS objectives. It explicitly excludes any discussion of Quality of Experience (QoE), which can only be assessed and controlled at the application layer or above.

This document is not a product of the IRTF Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG) but has been through formal Last Call and has the support of the participants in the research group for publication as an individual submission.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx#Network Systems Research and DesigndnamegD. Oranddatej÷úhfilenamegrfc9064eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130866hkeywordsÑcICNcQoSrcongestion controlqadmission controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`‹¨ægranking#hcrfcd9064irfcNumber#hestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexnConsiderations in the Development of a QoS Architecture for CCNx-Like Information-Centric Networking ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThis memo describes the Host Identity (HI) namespace, which provides a cryptographic namespace to applications, and the associated protocol layer, the Host Identity Protocol, located between the internetworking and transport layers, that supports end-host mobility, multihoming, and NAT traversal. Herein are presented the basics of the current namespaces, their strengths and weaknesses, and how a HI namespace will add completeness to them. The roles of the HI namespace in the protocols are defined.

This document obsoletes RFC 4423 and addresses the concerns raised by the IESG, particularly that of crypto agility. The Security Considerations section also describes measures against flooding attacks, usage of identities in access control lists, weaker types of identifiers, and trust on first use. This document incorporates lessons learned from the implementations of RFC 7401 and goes further to explain how HIP works as a secure signaling channel.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnHTT ConsultingdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegM. Komuddatej÷Hhfilenamegrfc9063eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130865hkeywordsévcryptographic identitywcryptographic namespacexidentifier-locator splithmobilitykmultihomingmNAT traversaleIPseccESPdIPv6send-to-end securitywend-to-end connectivityqendpoint identitymleap of faithjrendezvouskobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate`êÀgranking#gcrfcd9063irfcNumber#gestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Host Identity Protocol ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬This document specifies the requirements and reference framework for Ethernet VPN (EVPN) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM).  The requirements cover the OAM aspects of EVPN and Provider Backbone Bridge EVPN (PBB-EVPN).  The framework defines the layered OAM model encompassing the EVPN service layer, network layer, underlying Packet Switched Network (PSN) transport layer, and link layer but focuses on the service and network layers.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej÷ùhfilenamegrfc9062eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130864hkeywordsÉhPBB-EVPNpfault managementvperformance managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`›)¢granking#fcrfcd9062irfcNumber#festateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdFramework and Requirements for Ethernet VPN (EVPN) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document describes how to provide IPsec-based flow protection (integrity and confidentiality) by means of an Interface to Network Security Function (I2NSF) Controller. It considers two main well-known scenarios in IPsec: gateway-to-gateway and host-to-host. The service described in this document allows the configuration and monitoring of IPsec Security Associations (IPsec SAs) from an I2NSF Controller to one or several flow-based Network Security Functions (NSFs) that rely on IPsec to protect data traffic.

This document focuses on the I2NSF NSF-Facing Interface by providing YANG data models for configuring the IPsec databases, namely Security Policy Database (SPD), Security Association Database (SAD), Peer Authorization Database (PAD), and Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2). This allows IPsec SA establishment with minimal intervention by the network administrator. This document defines three YANG modules, but it does not define any new protocol.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnamenR. Marin-Lopez¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnameoG. Lopez-Millan¢kaffiliationxUniversity Defense CenterdnametF. Pereniguez-Garciaddatej÷úhfilenamegrfc9061eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymei2nsfdfullx/i2nsf - Interface to Network Security Functionsdnamex'Interface to Network Security Functionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130863hkeywordsÉcNSFcSDNeIPseckobsoletedByÄepagesZopublicationDate`ÔŸqgranking#ecrfcd9061irfcNumber#eestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVA YANG Data Model for IPsec Flow Protection Based on Software-Defined Networking (SDN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThe Secure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) certificate profile provides a way to attest authority over telephone numbers and related identifiers for the purpose of preventing telephone number spoofing.  This specification details how that authority can be delegated from a parent certificate to a subordinate certificate.  This supports a number of use cases, including those where service providers grant credentials to enterprises or other customers capable of signing calls with STIR.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Petersonddatej÷ûhfilenamegrfc9060eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130862hkeywordsÜcSIPxSecure Origin IdentificationvCommunication SecuritylCertificatesxPublic Key InfrastructurewReal-Time CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateaEñ•granking#dcrfcd9060irfcNumber#destateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASecure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) Certificate DelegationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This document defines Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) extensions for grouping two unidirectional MPLS-TE Label Switched Paths (LSPs), one in each direction in the network, into an associated bidirectional LSP.  These PCEP extensions can be applied either using a stateful PCE for both PCE-initiated and PCC-initiated LSPs or using a stateless PCE.  The PCEP procedures defined are applicable to the LSPs using RSVP-TE for signaling.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehC. Barth¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefB. Wenddatej÷òhfilenamegrfc9059eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130861hkeywordsÉkRSVP-TE LSPmCo-routed LSPkReverse LSPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`”5—granking#ccrfcd9059irfcNumber#cestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Associated Bidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756¥habstracty˜Multilinear Galois Mode (MGM) is an Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) block cipher mode based on the Encrypt-then-MAC (EtM) principle. MGM is defined for use with 64-bit and 128-bit block ciphers.

MGM has been standardized in Russia. It is used as an AEAD mode for the GOST block cipher algorithms in many protocols, e.g., TLS 1.3 and IPsec. This document provides a reference for MGM to enable review of the mechanisms in use and to make MGM available for use with any block cipher.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnamemS. Smyshlyaev¢kaffiliationeTC 26dnamelV. Nozdrunov¢kaffiliationeTC 26dnamekV. Shishkin¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnamemE. Griboedovaddatej÷ûhfilenamegrfc9058eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130860hkeywordsÉxauthenticated encryptionqmode of operationdAEADkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`›¡granking#bcrfcd9058irfcNumber#bestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexMultilinear Galois Mode (MGM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyõInternet mail defines the From: header field to indicate the author of the message's content and the Sender: field to indicate who initially handled the message on the author's behalf. The Sender: field is optional if it has the same information as the From: field. This was not a problem until development of stringent protections on use of the From: field. It has prompted Mediators, such as mailing lists, to modify the From: field to circumvent mail rejection caused by those protections. In effect, the From: field has become dominated by its role as a handling identifier.

The current specification augments the altered use of the From: field by specifying the Author: field, which ensures identification of the original author of the message and is not subject to modification by Mediators. This document is published as an Experimental RFC to assess community interest, functional efficacy, and technical adequacy.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej÷ûhfilenamegrfc9057eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130859hkeywordséfdomaineemailhsecurityimessagingddkimcspfnauthenticationireportingkconformancefauthorkoriginationhoriginaldfromfsenderkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`“‚Âgranking#acrfcd9057irfcNumber#aestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexEmail Author Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx{This document specifies the Deterministic Networking data plane when encapsulating IP over an MPLS packet-switched network.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Korhonenddatej÷Öhfilenamegrfc9056eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130858hkeywordsÇksub-networkjsubnetworkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea[S—granking#`crfcd9056irfcNumber#`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Data Plane: IP over MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA DetNet (deterministic network) provides specific performance guarantees to its data flows, such as extremely low data loss rates and bounded latency (including bounded latency variation, i.e., "jitter"). As a result, securing a DetNet requires that in addition to the best practice security measures taken for any mission-critical network, additional security measures may be needed to secure the intended operation of these novel service properties.

This document addresses DetNet-specific security considerations from the perspectives of both the DetNet system-level designer and component designer. System considerations include a taxonomy of relevant threats and attacks, and associations of threats versus use cases and service properties. Component-level considerations include ingress filtering and packet arrival-time violation detection.

This document also addresses security considerations specific to the IP and MPLS data plane technologies, thereby complementing the Security Considerations sections of those documents.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeDOLBYdnamekE. Grossman¢kaffiliationfHUAWEIdnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationgTHOUGHTdnameiA. Hackerddatej÷òhfilenamegrfc9055eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130857hkeywordsÇfDetNethsecuritykobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDate`÷i›granking#_crfcd9055irfcNumber#_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Security ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆The CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) syntax (see RFC 9052) does not define any direct methods for using hash algorithms.  There are, however, circumstances where hash algorithms are used, such as indirect signatures, where the hash of one or more contents are signed, and identification of an X.509 certificate or other object by the use of a fingerprint.  This document defines hash algorithms that are identified by COSE algorithm identifiers.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaadddatej÷Éhfilenamegrfc9054eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130856hkeywordsÉtSHA-1 Hash AlgorithmtSHA-2 Hash AlgorithmoSHAKE AlgorithmkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatecù'granking#^crfcd9054irfcNumber#^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE): Hash AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄConcise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) is a data format designed for small code size and small message size. There is a need to be able to define basic security services for this data format. This document defines a set of algorithms that can be used with the CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) protocol (RFC 9052).

This document, along with RFC 9052, obsoletes RFC 8152.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaadddatej÷~hfilenamegrfc9053eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130855hkeywordsèoObject SecuritydCOSEsConstrained DevicescAESgAES-GCMgAES-CCMeECDSAeEdDSAcECCgHSS-LMSfAES-KWdECDHdHMACdCMAClCryptographykobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDatecù'granking#]crfcd9053irfcNumber#]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE): Initial AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9864∑habstracty3Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) is a data format designed for small code size and small message size. There is a need to be able to define basic security services for this data format. This document defines the CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) protocol. This specification describes how to create and process signatures, message authentication codes, and encryption using CBOR for serialization. This specification additionally describes how to represent cryptographic keys using CBOR.

This document, along with RFC 9053, obsoletes RFC 8152.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaadddatej÷íhfilenamegrfc9052eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130854hkeywordsÉoObject SecuritydCOSEsConstrained DeviceskobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDatecù#granking#\crfcd9052irfcNumber#\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber`etotaletitlexACBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE): Structures and ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9338∂habstracty§The Internet Message Access Protocol Version 4rev2 (IMAP4rev2) allows a client to access and manipulate electronic mail messages on a server. IMAP4rev2 permits manipulation of mailboxes (remote message folders) in a way that is functionally equivalent to local folders. IMAP4rev2 also provides the capability for an offline client to resynchronize with the server.

IMAP4rev2 includes operations for creating, deleting, and renaming mailboxes; checking for new messages; removing messages permanently; setting and clearing flags; parsing per RFCs 5322, 2045, and 2231; searching; and selective fetching of message attributes, texts, and portions thereof. Messages in IMAP4rev2 are accessed by the use of numbers. These numbers are either message sequence numbers or unique identifiers.

IMAP4rev2 does not specify a means of posting mail; this function is handled by a mail submission protocol such as the one specified in RFC 6409.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej÷ñhfilenamegrfc9051eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130853hkeywordsÇiIMAP4rev2dimapkobsoletedByÄepages£opublicationDatea$4Sgranking#[crfcd9051irfcNumber#[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - Version 4rev2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘The Path Computation Element (PCE) is a core component of Software-Defined Networking (SDN) systems.

A PCE as a Central Controller (PCECC) can simplify the processing of a distributed control plane by blending it with elements of SDN and without necessarily completely replacing it. Thus, the Label Switched Path (LSP) can be calculated/set up/initiated and the label-forwarding entries can also be downloaded through a centralized PCE server to each network device along the path while leveraging the existing PCE technologies as much as possible.

This document specifies the procedures and Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) extensions for using the PCE as the central controller for provisioning labels along the path of the static LSP.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegS. Peng¢kaffiliationkRtBrick IncdnamegM. Negi¢kaffiliationpEtheric NetworksdnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationcHPEdnamegC. Zhouddatej÷ôhfilenamegrfc9050eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130852hkeywordsÉcSDNcCCIoCentral ControlkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate`ê…granking#Zcrfcd9050irfcNumber#ZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Procedures and Extensions for Using the PCE as a Central Controller (PCECC) of LSPsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756¥habstracty°This document is a product of the Path Aware Networking Research Group (PANRG). At the first meeting of the PANRG, the Research Group agreed to catalog and analyze past efforts to develop and deploy Path Aware techniques, most of which were unsuccessful or at most partially successful, in order to extract insights and lessons for Path Aware networking researchers.

This document contains that catalog and analysis.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoTencent AmericadnamejS. Dawkinsddatej÷ôhfilenamegrfc9049eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepanrgdfullx panrg - Path Aware Networking RGdnamexPath Aware Networking RGdtypebrgbidjdoc-130851hkeywordsÅcPANkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDate`÷i”granking#Ycrfcd9049irfcNumber#YestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexNPath Aware Networking: Obstacles to Deployment (A Bestiary of Roads Not Taken)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*The 3GPP mobile network Authentication and Key Agreement (AKA) is an authentication mechanism for devices wishing to access mobile networks. RFC 4187 (EAP-AKA) made the use of this mechanism possible within the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) framework. RFC 5448 (EAP-AKA') was an improved version of EAP-AKA.

This document is the most recent specification of EAP-AKA', including, for instance, details about and references related to operating EAP-AKA' in 5G networks.

EAP-AKA' differs from EAP-AKA by providing a key derivation function that binds the keys derived within the method to the name of the access network. The key derivation function has been defined in the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP). EAP-AKA' allows its use in EAP in an interoperable manner. EAP-AKA' also updates the algorithm used in hash functions, as it employs SHA-256 / HMAC-SHA-256 instead of SHA-1 / HMAC-SHA-1, which is used in EAP-AKA.

This version of the EAP-AKA' specification defines the protocol behavior for both 4G and 5G deployments, whereas the previous version defined protocol behavior for 4G deployments only. While EAP-AKA' as defined in RFC 5448 is not obsolete, this document defines the most recent and fully backwards-compatible specification of EAP-AKA'. This document updates both RFCs 4187 and 5448.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemV. Lehtovirta¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekV. Torvinen¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiP. Eronenddatej÷°hfilenamegrfc9048eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130850hkeywordsÑcEAPcAKAdAKA'd3GPPkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDatea|$ˇgranking#Xcrfcd9048irfcNumber#XestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvImproved Extensible Authentication Protocol Method for 3GPP Mobile Network Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA')dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9678∂habstracty#This document defines an Extended Community that is advertised along with an Ethernet Virtual Private Network (EVPN) Media Access Control (MAC) / IP Advertisement route and carries information relevant to the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) / Neighbor Discovery (ND) resolution so that an EVPN Provider Edge (PE) implementing a proxy-ARP/ND function in broadcast domains (BDs) or an ARP/ND function on Integrated Routing and Bridging (IRB) interfaces can reply to ARP Requests or Neighbor Solicitation (NS) messages with the correct information.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. Sathappan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejK. Nagaraj¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamefW. Linddatej÷õhfilenamegrfc9047eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§g