ges3opublicationDatea$4Agranking#Scrfcd9043irfcNumber#SestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-FFV1 Video Coding Format Versions 0, 1, and 3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9The OBJECTID capability of IMAP (RFC 8474) allows clients to identify mailboxes by a unique identifier that survives renaming.

This document extends the Sieve email filtering language (RFC 5228) to allow using that same unique identifier as a target for fileinto rules and for testing the existence of mailboxes.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekB. Gondwanaddatej÷õhfilenamegrfc9042eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130844hkeywordsÇesieveeemailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`›)‹granking#Rcrfcd9042irfcNumber#RestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Sieve Email Filtering: Delivery by MAILBOXIDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document updates RFCs 8029 and 8611, both of which define IANA registries for MPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping. In particular, the registration procedure "Private Use" (previously known as "Vendor Private Use") has been changed to "First Come First Served" for the TLV and sub-TLV registries.

It also updates the description of the procedures for the responses sent when an unknown or erroneous code point is found. The updates are to clarify and align this namespace with recent developments, e.g., aligning terminology with RFC 8126 instead of the now obsoleted RFC 5226 (both titled "Guidelines for Writing an IANA Considerations Section in RFCs").fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxBronze Dragon ConsultingdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegT. Saadddatej–Mhfilenamegrfc9041eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130843hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ˇ
|granking#Qcrfcd9041irfcNumber#QestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKUpdating the MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs) Ping Parameters IANA RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis memo provides guidance to TCP implementers that is intended to help improve connection convergence to steady-state operation without affecting interoperability.  It updates and replaces RFC 2140's description of sharing TCP state, as typically represented in TCP Control Blocks, among similar concurrent or consecutive connections.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehS. Islamddatej–Xhfilenamegrfc9040eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130842hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatea␀ˆIgranking#Pcrfcd9040irfcNumber#PestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!TCP Control Block InterdependencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document describes a new Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for hardware device identifiers.  A general representation of device identity can be useful in many applications, such as in sensor data streams and storage or in equipment inventories.  A URN-based representation can be passed along in applications that need the information.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationlEdge ImpulsednameiZ. Shelbyddatej÷òhfilenamegrfc9039eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130841hkeywordsácURNqdevice identifierdIMEIf1-WirekMAC addressfEUI-48fEUI-64kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`‹ÿgranking#Ocrfcd9039irfcNumber#OestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Uniform Resource Names for Device IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThe Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP), as defined in RFC 5730, includes a method for the client and server to determine the objects to be managed during a session and the object extensions to be used during a session.  The services are identified using namespace URIs, and an "unhandled namespace" is one that is associated with a service not supported by the client.  This document defines an operational practice that enables the server to return information associated with unhandled namespace URIs and that maintains compliance with the negotiated services defined in RFC 5730.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationfSWITCHdnamekM. Casanovaddatej÷óhfilenamegrfc9038eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130840hkeywordsãeloginhgreetingcURIinamespacehresponseggeneraldpolllobject-levelpcommand-responsefsignalisignalingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`≥#ßgranking#Ncrfcd9038irfcNumber#NestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Unhandled NamespacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis document specifies the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) MPLS data plane when operating over an IEEE 802.1 Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN) sub-network.  This document does not define new procedures or processes.  Whenever this document makes statements or recommendations, they are taken from normative text in the referenced RFCs.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationpMalis ConsultingdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameiS. Bryantddatej÷óhfilenamegrfc9037eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130839hkeywordsÇksub-networklflow mappingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`∏´granking#Mcrfcd9037irfcNumber#MestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbDeterministic Networking (DetNet) Data Plane: MPLS over IEEE 802.1 Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document changes the policy of the "Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Location Profiles" IANA registry established by RFC 5222 from Standards Action to Specification Required.  This allows standards development organizations (SDOs) other than the IETF to add new values.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCore Technology ConsultingdnamejR. Gellensddatej–Thfilenamegrfc9036eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130838hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`πÀ%granking#Lcrfcd9036irfcNumber#LestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUChanging the Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Location Profiles Registry PolicydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document updates RFC 8138 by defining a bit in the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) Destination-Oriented Directed Acyclic Graph (DODAG) Configuration option to indicate whether compression is used within the RPL Instance and to specify the behavior of nodes compliant with RFC 8138 when the bit is set and unset.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegL. Zhaoddatej÷íhfilenamegrfc9035eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130837hkeywordsÖcIoTrHeader CompressionuSource Routing HeaderqHop-by-Hop HeadermRPL artifactskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate`åÚ*granking#Kcrfcd9035irfcNumber#KestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex°A Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) Destination-Oriented Directed Acyclic Graph (DODAG) Configuration Option for the 6LoWPAN Routing HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈This document specifies a new type for the 6LoWPAN routing header containing the deadline time for data packets, designed for use over constrained networks.  The deadline time enables forwarding and scheduling decisions for time-critical machine-to-machine (M2M) applications running on Internet-enabled devices that operate within time-synchronized networks.  This document also specifies a representation for the deadline time values in such networks.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeC-DACdnameiL. Thomas¢kaffiliationqSRM University-APdnamenS. Anamalamudi¢kaffiliationxIndian Institute of SciencednamelS.V.R. Anand¢kaffiliationxIndian Institute of SciencednamehM. Hegde¢kaffiliationkLupin LodgednamejC. Perkinsddatej÷7hfilenamegrfc9034eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130836hkeywordsÇnRouting headeriTimestampkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`¬jgranking#Jcrfcd9034irfcNumber#JestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvPacket Delivery Deadline Time in the Routing Header for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis specification defines the "IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4" (6TiSCH) Minimal Scheduling Function (MSF).  This Scheduling Function describes both the behavior of a node when joining the network and how the communication schedule is managed in a distributed fashion.  MSF is built upon the 6TiSCH Operation Sublayer Protocol (6P) and the minimal security framework for 6TiSCH.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeInriadnamehT. Chang¢kaffiliationeInriadnamelM. Vuƒçiniƒá¢kaffiliationxUniversitat Oberta de CatalunyadnamemX. Vilajosana¢kaffiliationiRISE SICSdnamelS. Duquennoy¢kaffiliationxUniversidad Diego PortalesdnamejD. Dujovneddatej÷zhfilenamegrfc9033eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6tischdfullx26tisch - IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4ednamex)IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4edtypebwgbidjdoc-130835hkeywordsÉdTSCHvcommunication scheduleb6PkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`≥Ægranking#Icrfcd9033irfcNumber#IestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(6TiSCH Minimal Scheduling Function (MSF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœIn the Time-Slotted Channel Hopping (TSCH) mode of IEEE Std 802.15.4, opportunities for broadcasts are limited to specific times and specific channels.  Routers in a TSCH network transmit Enhanced Beacon (EB) frames to announce the presence of the network.  This document provides a mechanism by which additional information critical for new nodes (pledges) and long-sleeping nodes may be carried within the EB in order to conserve use of broadcast opportunities.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversidad Diego PortalesdnamejD. Dujovne¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardsonddatej÷phfilenamegrfc9032eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6tischdfullx26tisch - IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4ednamex)IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4edtypebwgbidjdoc-130834hkeywordsÖeBRSKIfenrolljzero-touchoDODAG balancingmLLN balancingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate`≥Ægranking#Hcrfcd9032irfcNumber#HestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Encapsulation of 6TiSCH Join and Enrollment Information ElementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document describes the minimal framework required for a new device, called a "pledge", to securely join a 6TiSCH (IPv6 over the Time-Slotted Channel Hopping mode of IEEE 802.15.4) network.  The framework requires that the pledge and the JRC (Join Registrar/Coordinator, a central entity), share a symmetric key.  How this key is provisioned is out of scope of this document.  Through a single CoAP (Constrained Application Protocol) request-response exchange secured by OSCORE (Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments), the pledge requests admission into the network, and the JRC configures it with link-layer keying material and other parameters.  The JRC may at any time update the parameters through another request-response exchange secured by OSCORE.  This specification defines the Constrained Join Protocol and its CBOR (Concise Binary Object Representation) data structures, and it describes how to configure the rest of the 6TiSCH communication stack for this join process to occur in a secure manner.  Additional security mechanisms may be added on top of this minimal framework.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeInriadnamelM. Vuƒçiniƒá¢kaffiliationnAnalog DevicesdnamehJ. Simon¢kaffiliationx!University of California BerkeleydnameiK. Pister¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardsonddatej÷fhfilenamegrfc9031eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6tischdfullx26tisch - IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4ednamex)IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4edtypebwgbidjdoc-130833hkeywordsÉmbootstrappingjonboardingfoscorekobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate`≥≠granking#Gcrfcd9031irfcNumber#GestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Constrained Join Protocol (CoJP) for 6TiSCHdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document describes a network architecture that provides low-latency, low-jitter, and high-reliability packet delivery.  It combines a high-speed powered backbone and subnetworks using IEEE 802.15.4 time-slotted channel hopping (TSCH) to meet the requirements of low-power wireless deterministic applications.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubertddatej÷bhfilenamegrfc9030eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6tischdfullx26tisch - IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4ednamex)IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4edtypebwgbidjdoc-130832hkeywordsÉvdeterministic wirelesseradiodmeshkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDate`≥¨granking#Fcrfcd9030irfcNumber#FestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]An Architecture for IPv6 over the Time-Slotted Channel Hopping Mode of IEEE 802.15.4 (6TiSCH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚRFC 7752 defines the Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS). IANA created a registry consistent with that document called "Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) Parameters" with a number of subregistries. The allocation policy applied by IANA for those registries is "Specification Required", as defined in RFC 8126.

This document updates RFC 7752 by changing the allocation policy for all of the registries to "Expert Review" and by updating the guidance to the designated experts.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej÷öhfilenamegrfc9029eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130831hkeywordsÇfBGP-LSdIANAkobsoletedByÅd9552epagesopublicationDate`øıÛgranking#Ecrfcd9029irfcNumber#EestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlUpdates to the Allocation Policy for the Border Gateway Protocol - Link State (BGP-LS) Parameters RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document specifies a new Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal mode for the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  The new mode is based on the Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) methodology and UDP encapsulation of data and signaling traffic.  The main difference from the previously specified modes is the use of HIP messages instead of ICE for all NAT traversal procedures due to the kernel-space dependencies of HIP.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Ker√§nen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Mel√©n¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegM. Komuddatej÷hfilenamegrfc9028eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130830hkeywordsÉcHIPcNATmNAT traversalkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDate`êΩgranking#Dcrfcd9028irfcNumber#DestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Native NAT Traversal Mode for the Host Identity ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document adds new assertion values for a Resource Priority Header ("rph") claim and a new SIP Priority Header ("sph") claim for protection of the "psap-callback" value as part of the "rph" Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT) extension in support of the security of emergency services networks for emergency call origination and callback.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehM. Dolly¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamehC. Wendtddatej÷öhfilenamegrfc9027eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130829hkeywordsÉcrphhPASSporteesnetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`π¬ granking#Ccrfcd9027irfcNumber#CestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvAssertion Values for Resource Priority Header and SIP Priority Header Claims in Support of Emergency Services NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document defines Multicast Virtual Private Network (VPN) extensions and procedures that allow fast failover for upstream failures by allowing downstream Provider Edges (PEs) to consider the status of Provider-Tunnels (P-tunnels) when selecting the Upstream PE for a VPN multicast flow.  The fast failover is enabled by using "Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for Multipoint Networks" (RFC 8562) and the new BGP Attribute, BFD Discriminator.  Also, this document introduces a new BGP Community, Standby PE, extending BGP Multicast VPN (MVPN) routing so that a C-multicast route can be advertised toward a Standby Upstream PE.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehT. Morin¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Kebler¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirskyddatej÷ìhfilenamegrfc9026eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-13