opublicationDate`ø€Úgranking#?crfcd9023irfcNumber#?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Data Plane: IP over IEEE 802.1 Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxèThis document specifies the format, contents, and semantics of Domain Name Registration Data (DNRD) escrow deposits for a domain name registry.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiG. Lozano¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamenC. Thippeswamyddatej÷êhfilenamegrfc9022eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130824hkeywordsÑkdata escrowhregistrykdomain namexdomain name registration datakobsoletedByÄepages©opublicationDate`±4granking#>crfcd9022irfcNumber#>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Domain Name Registration Data (DNRD) Objects MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyiThis document specifies the conventions for using the Walnut Digital Signature Algorithm (WalnutDSA) for digital signatures with the CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) syntax. WalnutDSA is a lightweight, quantum-resistant signature scheme based on Group Theoretic Cryptography with implementation and computational efficiency of signature verification in constrained environments, even on 8- and 16-bit platforms.

The goal of this publication is to document a way to use the lightweight, quantum-resistant WalnutDSA signature algorithm in COSE in a way that would allow multiple developers to build compatible implementations. As of this publication, the security properties of WalnutDSA have not been evaluated by the IETF and its use has not been endorsed by the IETF.

WalnutDSA and the Walnut Digital Signature Algorithm are trademarks of Veridify Security Inc.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqVeridify SecuritydnameiD. Atkinsddatej÷ïhfilenamegrfc9021eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130823hkeywordsÇdCOSEiWalnutDSAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`∞bgranking#=crfcd9021irfcNumber#=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex\Use of the Walnut Digital Signature Algorithm with CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document defines three YANG data models.  The first is for Segment Routing (SR) configuration and operation, which is to be augmented by different Segment Routing data planes.  The next is a YANG data model that defines a collection of generic types and groupings for SR.  The third module defines the configuration and operational states for the Segment Routing MPLS data plane.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationkVMware, IncdnameiP. Sarkar¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej÷ìhfilenamegrfc9020eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130822hkeywordsÅdmplskobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate`Øfgranking#<crfcd9020irfcNumber#<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#YANG Data Model for Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygVulnerabilities in Internet of Things (IoT) devices have raised the need for a reliable and secure firmware update mechanism suitable for devices with resource constraints. Incorporating such an update mechanism is a fundamental requirement for fixing vulnerabilities, but it also enables other important capabilities such as updating configuration settings and adding new functionality.

In addition to the definition of terminology and an architecture, this document provides the motivation for the standardization of a manifest format as a transport-agnostic means for describing and protecting firmware updates.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamehB. Moran¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationfLinarodnamehD. Brown¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiM. Meriacddatej÷êhfilenamegrfc9019eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsuitdfullx.suit - Software Updates for Internet of Thingsdnamex'Software Updates for Internet of Thingsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130821hkeywordsácIoTfupdatehsoftwarehfirmwarekconstrainedfSecuredBootkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`åÚgranking#;crfcd9019irfcNumber#;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5A Firmware Update Architecture for Internet of ThingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9DNS Cookies, as specified in RFC 7873, are a lightweight DNS transaction security mechanism that provide limited protection to DNS servers and clients against a variety of denial-of-service amplification, forgery, or cache-poisoning attacks by off-path attackers.

This document updates RFC 7873 with precise directions for creating Server Cookies so that an anycast server set including diverse implementations will interoperate with standard clients, with suggestions for constructing Client Cookies in a privacy-preserving fashion, and with suggestions on how to update a Server Secret. An IANA registry listing the methods and associated pseudorandom function suitable for creating DNS Server Cookies has been created with the method described in this document as the first and, as of the time of publication, only entry.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamegO. Sury¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnameiW. Toorop¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamejM. Andrewsddatej÷ëhfilenamegrfc9018eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130820hkeywordsÇfClientdHashkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`kıgranking#:crfcd9018irfcNumber#:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Interoperable Domain Name System (DNS) Server CookiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document discusses and recommends terminology that may be used when MPLS Special-Purpose Labels (SPLs) are specified and documented.

This document applies that terminology change to the relevant IANA registry and also clarifies the use of the Entropy Label Indicator (7) when immediately preceded by the Extension Label (15).

This document updates RFCs 3032 and 7274.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxBronze Dragon ConsultingdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej÷éhfilenamegrfc9017eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130819hkeywordsÖdMPLSxExtended Special-Purpose LabelxBase Special-Purpose LabelnReserved LabelwEntropy Label IndicatorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`p€Àgranking#9crfcd9017irfcNumber#9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Special-Purpose Label TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx£This document describes the flow and service information model for Deterministic Networking (DetNet).  These models are defined for IP and MPLS DetNet data planes.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationtNational InstrumentsdnamekR. Cummings¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehY. Jiang¢kaffiliationoLabN ConsultingdnamehD. Fedykddatej÷ëhfilenamegrfc9016eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130818hkeywordsÇfDetNetx"Flow and Service Information ModelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`cº"granking#8crfcd9016irfcNumber#8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHFlow and Service Information Model for Deterministic Networking (DetNet)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document describes the use of BGP as a control plane for networks that support service function chaining. The document introduces a new BGP address family called the "Service Function Chain (SFC) Address Family Identifier / Subsequent Address Family Identifier" (SFC AFI/SAFI) with two Route Types. One Route Type is originated by a node to advertise that it hosts a particular instance of a specified service function. This Route Type also provides "instructions" on how to send a packet to the hosting node in a way that indicates that the service function has to be applied to the packet. The other Route Type is used by a controller to advertise the paths of "chains" of service functions and give a unique designator to each such path so that they can be used in conjunction with the Network Service Header (NSH) defined in RFC 8300.

This document adopts the service function chaining architecture described in RFC 7665.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalilddatej÷Ähfilenamegrfc9015eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130817hkeywordsãxService Function ChainingvService Function ChainvNetwork Service HeaderpService FunctionxService Function ForwarderuService Function PathxService Function Path RoutexService Function InstancexService Function Instance RouteuService Function TypemControl PlanekobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDate`ø¯	granking#7crfcd9015irfcNumber#7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMBGP Control Plane for the Network Service Header in Service Function ChainingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛This document describes how Network Virtualization Overlays (NVOs) can be connected to a Wide Area Network (WAN) in order to extend the Layer 2 connectivity required for some tenants.  The solution analyzes the interaction between NVO networks running Ethernet Virtual Private Networks (EVPNs) and other Layer 2 VPN (L2VPN) technologies used in the WAN, such as Virtual Private LAN Services (VPLSs), VPLS extensions for Provider Backbone Bridging (PBB-VPLS), EVPN, or PBB-EVPN.  It also describes how the existing technical specifications apply to the interconnection and extends the EVPN procedures needed in some cases.  In particular, this document describes how EVPN routes are processed on Gateways (GWs) that interconnect EVPN-Overlay and EVPN-MPLS networks, as well as the Interconnect Ethernet Segment (I-ES), to provide multihoming.  This document also describes the use of the Unknown MAC Route (UMR) to avoid issues of a Media Access Control (MAC) scale on Data Center Network Virtualization Edge (NVE) devices.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. Sathappan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drakeddatej÷ hfilenamegrfc9014eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130816hkeywordsÖcDCIcUMRqUnknown MAC RoutedI-ESeI-ESIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ÆÙ‚granking#6crfcd9014irfcNumber#6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Interconnect Solution for Ethernet VPN (EVPN) Overlay NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉNetworks use tunnels for a variety of reasons.  A large variety of tunnel types are defined, and the tunnel encapsulator router needs to select a type of tunnel that is supported by the tunnel decapsulator router.  This document defines how to advertise, in OSPF Router Information Link State Advertisements (LSAs), the list of tunnel encapsulations supported by the tunnel decapsulator.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCapitalonlinednameeX. Xu¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationwNTT Network InnovationsdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamelL. Contreras¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalilddatej÷hfilenamegrfc9013eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130815hkeywordsÅcBGPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate`àãTgranking#5crfcd9013irfcNumber#5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+OSPF Advertisement of Tunnel EncapsulationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document defines a BGP path attribute known as the "Tunnel Encapsulation attribute", which can be used with BGP UPDATEs of various Subsequent Address Family Identifiers (SAFIs) to provide information needed to create tunnels and their corresponding encapsulation headers. It provides encodings for a number of tunnel types, along with procedures for choosing between alternate tunnels and routing packets into tunnels.

This document obsoletes RFC 5512, which provided an earlier definition of the Tunnel Encapsulation attribute. RFC 5512 was never deployed in production. Since RFC 5566 relies on RFC 5512, it is likewise obsoleted. This document updates RFC 5640 by indicating that the Load-Balancing Block sub-TLV may be included in any Tunnel Encapsulation attribute where load balancing is desired.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkArrcus, IncdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoG. Van de Velde¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiS. Sangli¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej÷éhfilenamegrfc9012eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130814hkeywordsÅcBGPkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate`àãKgranking#4crfcd9012irfcNumber#4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&The BGP Tunnel Encapsulation AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9830∂habstractyËThe Static Context Header Compression and fragmentation (SCHC) specification (RFC 8724) describes generic header compression and fragmentation techniques for Low-Power Wide Area Network (LPWAN) technologies. SCHC is a generic mechanism designed for great flexibility so that it can be adapted for any of the LPWAN technologies.

This document defines a profile of SCHC (RFC 8724) for use in LoRaWAN networks and provides elements such as efficient parameterization and modes of operation.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgSemtechdnamejO. Gimenez¢kaffiliationfAckliodnameiI. Petrovddatej÷êhfilenamegrfc9011eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelpwandfullx.lpwan - IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdnamex&IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130813hkeywordsórheader compressionkcompressionmfragmentationnstatic contextjrule-basedeLPWANfLPWANsilow powerilow-powerdLoRagLoRaWANcIoTrInternet of Thingspadaptation layercUDPdIPv6nsensor networkwwireless sensor networkh802.15.4sconstrained networkpconstrained nodexconstrained-node networkdSCHCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`Å’±granking#3crfcd9011irfcNumber#3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGStatic Context Header Compression and Fragmentation (SCHC) over LoRaWANdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis specification provides a mechanism for a host that implements a routing-agnostic interface based on IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) Neighbor Discovery to obtain reachability services across a network that leverages RFC 6550 for its routing operations.  It updates RFCs 6550, 6775, and 8505.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationiSandelmandnamemM. Richardsonddatej÷íhfilenamegrfc9010eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130812hkeywordsÉdIPv6bNDnRedistributionkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDate`p€ granking#2crfcd9010irfcNumber#2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJRouting for RPL (Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks) LeavesdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9685d9926∂habstracty£This document explains the problems associated with the use of No-Path Destination Advertisement Object (NPDAO) messaging in RFC 6550 and also discusses the requirements for an optimized route invalidation messaging scheme.  Further, this document specifies a new proactive route invalidation message called the "Destination Cleanup Object" (DCO), which fulfills requirements for optimized route invalidation messaging.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekR.A. Jadhav¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejR.N. Sahoo¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefZ. Caoddatej÷Whfilenamegrfc9009eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130811hkeywordsÖeNPDAOcDCOgno-patheroutegcleanupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`p€…granking#1crfcd9009irfcNumber#1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEfficient Route InvalidationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document looks at different data flows through Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLN) where RPL (IPv6 Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks) is used to establish routing.  The document enumerates the cases where RPL Packet Information (RPI) Option Type (RFC 6553), RPL Source Route Header (RFC 6554), and IPv6-in-IPv6 encapsulation are required in the data plane.  This analysis provides the basis upon which to design efficient compression of these headers.  This document updates RFC 6553 by adding a change to the RPI Option Type.  Additionally, this document updates RFC 6550 by defining a flag in the DODAG Information Object (DIO) Configuration option to indicate this change and updates RFC 8138 as well to consider the new Option Type when the RPL Option is decompressed.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmUTN-FRM/AaltodnamekM.I. Robles¢kaffiliationcSSWdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubertddatej÷ëhfilenamegrfc9008eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130810hkeywordsÉjRPL Optiong6LoWPANhRFC 6553kobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDate`p€ægranking#0crfcd9008irfcNumber#0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmUsing RPI Option Type, Routing Header for Source Routes, and IPv6-in-IPv6 Encapsulation in the RPL Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∞This document specifies a data model for handling Message Disposition Notifications (MDNs) (see RFC 8098) in the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) (see RFCs 8620 and 8621).fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhLinagoradnamejR. Ouazanaddatej÷íhfilenamegrfc9007eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130809hkeywordsÑdJMAPdJSONeemailcMDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`Yugranking#/crfcd9007irfcNumber#/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXHandling Message Disposition Notification with the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document provides guidance on how to implement and use the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) in Constrained-Node Networks (CNNs), which are a characteristic of the Internet of Things (IoT).  Such environments require a lightweight TCP implementation and may not make use of optional functionality.  This document explains a number of known and deployed techniques to simplify a TCP stack as well as corresponding trade-offs.  The objective is to help embedded developers with decisions on which TCP features to use.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcUPCdnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamelJ. Crowcroft¢kaffiliationtHochschule EsslingendnameiM. Scharfddatej÷ëhfilenamegrfc9006eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlwigdfullx+lwig - Light-Weight Implementation Guidancednamex$Light-Weight Implementation Guidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130808hkeywordsãxconstrained node networksdCNNsdHTTPdCoAPdMQTTg6LoWPANc6LomIEEE 802.15.4tBluetooth Low EnergygContikicuIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`_ƒtgranking#.crfcd9006irfcNumber#.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2TCP Usage Guidance in the Internet of Things (IoT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document introduces a simple mechanism to associate policies with a group of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) via an extension to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP).  The extension allows a PCEP speaker to advertise to a PCEP peer that a particular LSP belongs to a particular Policy Association Group (PAG).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationeCienadnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationsMetaswitch NetworksdnamekJ. Hardwick¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeC. Liddatej÷åhfilenamegrfc9005eflags