group§gacronymdquicdfullkquic - QUICdnamedQUICdtypebwgbidjdoc-130802hkeywordsäimultipathpnext generationshprotocolfsctp++fsecureesmartetcp/2etcpngitransportltransport-ngkobsoletedByÄepagesóopublicationDate`∞wgranking#(crfcd9000irfcNumber#(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2QUIC: A UDP-Based Multiplexed and Secure TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxtThis document defines the properties of the QUIC transport protocol that are common to all versions of the protocol.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej÷ïhfilenamegrfc8999eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdquicdfullkquic - QUICdnamedQUICdtypebwgbidjdoc-130801hkeywordsáfcryptoonext generationhprotocolfsecureitransportcUDPjinvariantskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate`∞lgranking#'crfcd8999irfcNumber#'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Version-Independent Properties of QUICdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9368¥habstracty∑This document specifies how to use the ShangMi (SM) cryptographic algorithms with Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol version 1.3.

The use of these algorithms with TLS 1.3 is not endorsed by the IETF. The SM algorithms are becoming mandatory in China, so this document provides a description of how to use the SM algorithms with TLS 1.3 and specifies a profile of TLS 1.3 so that implementers can produce interworking implementations.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAnt GroupdnamegP. Yangddatej÷éhfilenamegrfc8998eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130800hkeywordsÑlcryptographyjencryptionnauthenticationpnetwork securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`I,∞granking#&crfcd8998irfcNumber#&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&ShangMi (SM) Cipher Suites for TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This specification updates the current recommendation for the use of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to provide confidentiality of email between a Mail User Agent (MUA) and a Mail Submission Server or Mail Access Server.  This document updates RFC 8314.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmcyberstorm.mudnamemL. Velvindron¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej–hfilenamegrfc8997eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130799hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`Z,·granking#%crfcd8997irfcNumber#%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Deprecation of TLS 1.1 for Email Submission and AccessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyZThis document formally deprecates Transport Layer Security (TLS) versions 1.0 (RFC 2246) and 1.1 (RFC 4346). Accordingly, those documents have been moved to Historic status. These versions lack support for current and recommended cryptographic algorithms and mechanisms, and various government and industry profiles of applications using TLS now mandate avoiding these old TLS versions. TLS version 1.2 became the recommended version for IETF protocols in 2008 (subsequently being obsoleted by TLS version 1.3 in 2018), providing sufficient time to transition away from older versions. Removing support for older versions from implementations reduces the attack surface, reduces opportunity for misconfiguration, and streamlines library and product maintenance.

This document also deprecates Datagram TLS (DTLS) version 1.0 (RFC 4347) but not DTLS version 1.2, and there is no DTLS version 1.1.

This document updates many RFCs that normatively refer to TLS version 1.0 or TLS version 1.1, as described herein. This document also updates the best practices for TLS usage in RFC 7525; hence, it is part of BCP 195.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcCISdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej÷éhfilenamegrfc8996eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130798hkeywordsÑcTLSideprecategTLSv1.0gTLSv1.1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`Z,÷granking#$crfcd8996irfcNumber#$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber√etotaletitlexDeprecating TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document specifies automated bootstrapping of an Autonomic Control Plane.  To do this, a Secure Key Infrastructure is bootstrapped.  This is done using manufacturer-installed X.509 certificates, in combination with a manufacturer's authorizing service, both online and offline.  We call this process the Bootstrapping Remote Secure Key Infrastructure (BRSKI) protocol.  Bootstrapping a new device can occur when using a routable address and a cloud service, only link-local connectivity, or limited/disconnected networks.  Support for deployment models with less stringent security requirements is included.  Bootstrapping is complete when the cryptographic identity of the new key infrastructure is successfully deployed to the device.  The established secure connection can be used to deploy a locally issued certificate to the device as well.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekM. Pritikin¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationmFuturewei USAdnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Behringer¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej÷uhfilenamegrfc8995eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130797hkeywordsãtAutonomic NetworkingtAutonomous OperationoSelf-Managementovoucher-requestjonboardingjzero-touchgvoucheroRFC8366 vouchernIoT-onboardingnIoT-zero-touchlnetwork-joinkobsoletedByÄepagestopublicationDate`®úgranking##crfcd8995irfcNumber##estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Bootstrapping Remote Secure Key Infrastructure (BRSKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨Autonomic functions need a control plane to communicate, which depends on some addressing and routing.  This Autonomic Control Plane should ideally be self-managing and be as independent as possible of configuration.  This document defines such a plane and calls it the "Autonomic Control Plane", with the primary use as a control plane for autonomic functions.  It also serves as a "virtual out-of-band channel" for Operations, Administration, and Management (OAM) communications over a network that provides automatically configured, hop-by-hop authenticated and encrypted communications via automatically configured IPv6 even when the network is not configured or is misconfigured.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmFuturewei USAdnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Behringer¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelS. Bjarnasonddatej÷Ühfilenamegrfc8994eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130796hkeywordsòqaddressing-schemecANItautonomic networkingtautonomous operationeBRSKIkcertificatejData-PlanefdomaindDTLSdDULLcESTeGRASPfIDevIDfinbandeIPsecdIPv6fLDevIDrloopback-interfacecNOCcOAMkout-of-bandiregistrargrenewalcRPLfsecureoself-managementcULAcVPNcVRFkobsoletedByÄepagesÄopublicationDate`®úgranking#"crfcd8994irfcNumber#"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex An Autonomic Control Plane (ACP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document describes a reference model for Autonomic Networking for managed networks.  It defines the behavior of an autonomic node, how the various elements in an autonomic context work together, and how autonomic services can use the infrastructure.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Behringer¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationmFuturewei USAdnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelL. Ciavaglia¢kaffiliationeUFRGSdnamehJ. Nobreddatej÷6hfilenamegrfc8993eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130795hkeywordsÜtautonomic networkingtautonomous operationoself-managementninfrastructurefintentwautonomic control planekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`®ú	granking#!crfcd8993irfcNumber#!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A Reference Model for Autonomic NetworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This document defines two autonomic technical objectives for IPv6 prefix management at the edge of large-scale ISP networks, with an extension to support IPv4 prefixes.  An important purpose of this document is to use it for validation of the design of various components of the Autonomic Networking Infrastructure.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Du¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefQ. Sunddatej’Hhfilenamegrfc8992eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130794hkeywordsÇtAutonomic NetworkingqPrefix ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`®úgranking# crfcd8992irfcNumber# estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Autonomic IPv6 Edge Prefix Management in Large-Scale NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document is a conceptual outline of an Application Programming Interface (API) for the GeneRic Autonomic Signaling Protocol (GRASP).  Such an API is needed for Autonomic Service Agents (ASAs) calling the GRASP protocol module to exchange Autonomic Network messages with other ASAs.  Since GRASP is designed to support asynchronous operations, the API will need to be adapted according to the support for asynchronicity in various programming languages and operating systems.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefB. Liu¢kaffiliationoBUPT UniversitydnamegW. Wang¢kaffiliationoBUPT UniversitydnamegX. Gongddatej÷ãhfilenamegrfc8991eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130793hkeywordsÉtAutonomic NetworkingtAutonomous OperationoSelf-ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`®úgranking#crfcd8991irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNGeneRic Autonomic Signaling Protocol Application Program Interface (GRASP API)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document specifies the GeneRic Autonomic Signaling Protocol (GRASP), which enables autonomic nodes and Autonomic Service Agents to dynamically discover peers, to synchronize state with each other, and to negotiate parameter settings with each other.  GRASP depends on an external security environment that is described elsewhere.  The technical objectives and parameters for specific application scenarios are to be described in separate documents.  Appendices briefly discuss requirements for the protocol and existing protocols with comparable features.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., LtddnamefB. Liuddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8990eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130792hkeywordsÉtautonomic networkingtautonomous operationoself-managementkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDate`®õ–granking#crfcd8990irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,GeneRic Autonomic Signaling Protocol (GRASP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document defines a process experiment under RFC 3933 that temporarily updates the criteria for qualifying volunteers to participate in the IETF Nominating Committee.  It therefore also updates the criteria for qualifying signatories to a community recall petition.  The purpose is to make the criteria more flexible in view of increasing remote participation in the IETF and a reduction in face-to-face meetings.  The experiment is of fixed duration and will apply to one, or at most two, consecutive Nominating Committee cycles, starting in 2021.  This document temporarily varies the rules in RFC 8713.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej–Bhfilenamegrfc8989eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130791hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9389epages
opublicationDate`>?granking#crfcd8989irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Additional Criteria for Nominating Committee EligibilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document describes operational problems that are known to occur when using DHCPv6 relays with prefix delegation. These problems can prevent successful delegation and result in routing failures. To address these problems, this document provides necessary functional requirements for operating DHCPv6 relays with prefix delegation.

It is recommended that any network operator using DHCPv6 prefix delegation with relays ensure that these requirements are followed on their networks.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationmBenu NetworksdnamemN. Kottapalli¢kaffiliationxTechnical University of LiberecdnamehM. Hunek¢kaffiliationjSky UK LtddnamelR. Pattersonddatej÷åhfilenamegrfc8987eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130790hkeywordsÖqPrefix DelegationlDHCPv6 relayqDelegating routerqRequesting routerpDelegating relaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`<9lgranking#crfcd8987irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+DHCPv6 Prefix Delegating Relay RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) Network Programming framework enables a network operator or an application to specify a packet processing program by encoding a sequence of instructions in the IPv6 packet header.

Each instruction is implemented on one or several nodes in the network and identified by an SRv6 Segment Identifier in the packet.

This document defines the SRv6 Network Programming concept and specifies the base set of SRv6 behaviors that enables the creation of interoperable overlays with underlay optimization.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelP. Camarillo¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamehJ. Leddy¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyer¢kaffiliationhSoftBankdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Liddatej÷Çhfilenamegrfc8986eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130789hkeywordsÉdSRv6oSegment RoutingtIPv6 Segment RoutingkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate`3˜pgranking#crfcd8986irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) Network ProgrammingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThis document presents the RACK-TLP loss detection algorithm for TCP.  RACK-TLP uses per-segment transmit timestamps and selective acknowledgments (SACKs) and has two parts.  Recent Acknowledgment (RACK) starts fast recovery quickly using time-based inferences derived from acknowledgment (ACK) feedback, and Tail Loss Probe (TLP) leverages RACK and sends a probe packet to trigger ACK feedback to avoid retransmission timeout (RTO) events.  Compared to the widely used duplicate acknowledgment (DupAck) threshold approach, RACK-TLP detects losses more efficiently when there are application-limited flights of data, lost retransmissions, or data packet reordering events.  It is intended to be an alternative to the DupAck threshold approach.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehY. Cheng¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekN. Cardwell¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamelN. Dukkipati¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamefP. Jhaddatej÷çhfilenamegrfc8985eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130788hkeywordsÉcTCPmLoss RecoveryjReorderingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`1√granking#crfcd8985irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-The RACK-TLP Loss Detection Algorithm for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification defines a data model and JSON representation of calendar data that can be used for storage and data exchange in a calendaring and scheduling environment.  It aims to be an alternative and, over time, successor to the widely deployed iCalendar data format.  It also aims to be unambiguous, extendable, and simple to process.  In contrast to the jCal format, which is also based on JSON, JSCalendar is not a direct mapping from iCalendar but defines the data model independently and expands semantics where appropriate.fadNamesFrancesca Palombinidarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamejN. Jenkins¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamekR. Stepanekddatej÷ähfilenamegrfc8984eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130787hkeywordsÜdJSONiiCalendarhcalendarfeventsddatedtimekobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDate`Ò◊°granking#crfcd8984irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2JSCalendar: A JSON Representation of Calendar DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies new Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) notification status types to better manage IPv4 and IPv6 coexistence by allowing the responder to signal to the initiator which address families are allowed.

This document updates RFC 7296.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷çhfilenamegrfc8983eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130786hkeywordsâwIPv4 service continuityeVoLTEhHandoverrService continuityd3GPPoIPv6 transitionhTS.24302kPDP contexthPDP typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`'|Dgranking#crfcd8983irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdInternet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Notification Status Types for IPv4/IPv6 CoexistencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) does not include capabilities to request partial responses.  Servers will only return full responses that include all of the information that a client is authorized to receive.  A partial response capability that limits the amount of information returned, especially in the case of search queries, could bring benefits to both clients and servers.  This document describes an RDAP query extension that allows clients to specify their preference for obtaining a partial response.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsIIT-CNR/Registro.itdnamekM. Loffredo¢kaffiliationsIIT-CNR/Registro.itdnamemM. Martinelliddatej÷çhfilenamegrfc8982eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130785hkeywordsÇdRDAPpPartial responsekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`#áHgranking#crfcd8982irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Partial ResponsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This document describes an extension to IPv6 Stateless Address Autoconfiguration that causes hosts to generate temporary addresses with randomized interface identifiers for each prefix advertised with autoconfiguration enabled.  Changing addresses over time limits the window of time during which eavesdroppers and other information collectors may trivially perform address-based network-activity correlation when the same address is employed for multiple transactions by the same host.  Additionally, it reduces the window of exposure of a host as being accessible via an address that becomes revealed as a result of active communication.  This document obsoletes RFC 4941.fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationfKaloomdnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnameiR. Dravesddatej÷âhfilenamegrfc8981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130784hkeywordsÑgprivacyianonymitymunlinkabilityxcrypto-based address changingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`<Zgranking#crfcd8981irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLTemporary Address Extensions for Stateless Address Autoconfiguration in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÚThe Design Expectations vs. Deployment Reality in Protocol Development Workshop was convened by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) in June 2019. This report summarizes the workshop's significant points of discussion and identifies topics that may warrant further consideration.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Hardieddatej–@hfilenamegrfc8980eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130783hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`0eÊgranking#crfcd8980irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexbReport from the IAB Workshop on Design Expectations vs. Deployment Reality in Protocol DevelopmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊This document defines the Subscriber and Performance Policy Identifier Context Headers.  These Variable-Length Context Headers can be carried in the Network Service Header (NSH) and are used to inform Service Functions (SFs) of subscriber- and performance-related information for the sake of policy enforcement and appropriate Service Function Chaining (SFC) operations.  The structure of each Context Header and their use and processing by NSH-aware nodes are described.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamekD. von Hugo¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷çhfilenamegrfc8979eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130782hkeywordsèqsubscriber policyrpolicy enforcementjsubscriberfpolicyequotanidentificationwimplicit identificationmservice chainvservice function chaincsfccSFPuservice function pathnclassificationb5Gptraffic steeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`>8granking#crfcd8979irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Subscriber and Performance Policy Identifier Context Headers in the Network Service Header (NSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylIn scenarios where network configuration information related to IPv6 prefixes becomes invalid without any explicit and reliable signaling of that condition (such as when a Customer Edge router crashes and reboots without knowledge of the previously employed prefixes), hosts on the local network may continue using stale prefixes for an unacceptably long time (on the order of several days), thus resulting in connectivity problems.  This document describes this issue and discusses operational workarounds that may help to improve network robustness.  Additionally, it highlights areas where further work may be needed.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationh6connectdnameiJ. ≈Ωor≈æ¢kaffiliationfSky UKdnamelR. Pattersonddatej–?hfilenamegrfc8978eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130781hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`I,•granking#crfcd8978irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXReaction of IPv6 Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC) to Flash-Renumbering EventsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) does not include core functionality for clients to provide sorting and paging parameters for control of large result sets.  This omission can lead to unpredictable server processing of queries and client processing of responses.  This unpredictability can be greatly reduced if clients can provide servers with their preferences for managing large responses.  This document describes RDAP query extensions that allow clients to specify their preferences for sorting and paging result sets.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsIIT-CNR/Registro.itdnamekM. Loffredo¢kaffiliationsIIT-CNR/Registro.itdnamemM. Martinelli¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej÷ãhfilenamegrfc8977eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130780hkeywordsÉdRDAPgSortingfPagingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`oˇgranking#crfcd8977irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWRegistration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Query Parameters for Result Sorting and PagingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis document describes a protocol and new DNS Resource Record that provides a cryptographic message digest over DNS zone data at rest. The ZONEMD Resource Record conveys the digest data in the zone itself. When used in combination with DNSSEC, ZONEMD allows recipients to verify the zone contents for data integrity and origin authenticity. This provides assurance that received zone data matches published data, regardless of how the zone data has been transmitted and received. When used without DNSSEC, ZONEMD functions as a checksum, guarding only against unintentional changes.

ZONEMD does not replace DNSSEC: DNSSEC protects individual RRsets (DNS data with fine granularity), whereas ZONEMD protects a zone's data as a whole, whether consumed by authoritative name servers, recursive name servers, or any other applications.

As specified herein, ZONEMD is impractical for large, dynamic zones due to the time and resources required for digest calculation. However, the ZONEMD record is extensible so that new digest schemes may be added in the future to support large, dynamic zones.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejD. Wessels¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnameiP. Barber¢kaffiliationfAmazondnamekM. Weinberg¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekW. Hardakerddatej÷âhfilenamegrfc8976eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130779hkeywordsÖcDNSfDNSSEChChecksumdHashmZone TransferkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`#Å(granking#crfcd8976irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMessage Digest for DNS ZonesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≈This document is a product of the Coding for Efficient Network Communications Research Group (NWCRG). It conforms to the directions found in the NWCRG taxonomy (RFC 8406).

The objective is to contribute to a larger deployment of Network Coding techniques in and above the network layer in satellite communication systems. This document also identifies open research issues related to the deployment of Network Coding in satellite communication systems.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdCNESdnamegN. Kuhn¢kaffiliationdENACdnameiE. Lochinddatej÷ãhfilenamegrfc8975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenwcrgdfullxBnwcrg - Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdnamex:Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-130778hkeywordsÇfSATCOMqcoding techniqueskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`
^granking#crfcd8975irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex$Network Coding for Satellite SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document provides considerations for alleviating Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) clients and intermediaries of keeping per-request state. To facilitate this, this document additionally introduces a new, optional CoAP protocol extension for extended token lengths.

This document updates RFCs 7252 and 8323 with an extended definition of the "TKL" field in the CoAP message header.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiK. Hartke¢kaffiliationiSandelmandnamemM. Richardsonddatej÷âhfilenamegrfc8974eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130777hkeywordsÇf6tischpminimal-securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_¯æÛgranking#crfcd8974irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTExtended Tokens and Stateless Clients in the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document specifies mechanisms to configure DDoS Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) clients with their DOTS servers.  The discovery procedure also covers the DOTS signal channel Call Home.  It can be useful to know the appropriate DOTS server for a given location in order to engage mitigation actions.  This is true even in cases where the DOTS client cannot localize the attack: cases where it only knows that some resources are under attack and that help is needed.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamejT. Reddy.Kddatej÷àhfilenamegrfc8973eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130776hkeywordsàjAutomationlProvisioningmConfigurationhLocationjDeploymentkMultihomingdDDoShSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_˛òãgranking#crfcd8973irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1DDoS Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) Agent DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÁThis document describes optional extensions to Simple Two-way Active Measurement Protocol (STAMP) that enable measurement of performance metrics.  The document also defines a STAMP Test Session Identifier and thus updates RFC 8762.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefX. Min¢kaffiliationqAccedian NetworksdnameiH. Nydell¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Foote¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekA. Masputra¢kaffiliationfOutSysdnamejE. Ruffiniddatej÷mhfilenamegrfc8972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130775hkeywordsÇdIPPMwPerformance MeasurementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`
