estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+IMAP4 Extension: Message Preview GenerationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøData models provide a programmatic approach to represent services and networks. Concretely, they can be used to derive configuration information for network and service components, and state information that will be monitored and tracked. Data models can be used during the service and network management life cycle (e.g., service instantiation, service provisioning, service optimization, service monitoring, service diagnosing, and service assurance). Data models are also instrumental in the automation of network management, and they can provide closed-loop control for adaptive and deterministic service creation, delivery, and maintenance.

This document describes a framework for service and network management automation that takes advantage of YANG modeling technologies. This framework is drawn from a network operator perspective irrespective of the origin of a data model; thus, it can accommodate YANG modules that are developed outside the IETF.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefC. Xie¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Gengddatej÷Ñhfilenamegrfc8969eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130772hkeywordsÜlModel DrivenoYANG Data Modeljautomationpservice deliverylnotificationcSDNkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate`o¯granking#	crfcd8969irfcNumber#	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCA Framework for Automating Service and Network Management with YANGdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Babel Routing Protocol does not contain any means to authenticate neighbours or provide integrity or confidentiality for messages sent between them.  This document specifies a mechanism to ensure these properties using Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx IRIF University of Paris-DiderotdnamejA. D√©cimo¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinazi¢kaffiliationx IRIF University of Paris-DiderotdnamemJ. Chroboczekddatej–5hfilenamegrfc8968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130771hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate_¸∑Àgranking#crfcd8968irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Babel Routing Protocol over Datagram Transport Layer SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¨This document describes a cryptographic authentication mechanism for the Babel routing protocol that has provisions for replay avoidance.  This document obsoletes RFC 7298.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx IRIF University of Paris-DiderotdnamefC. D√¥¢kaffiliationx IRIF University of Paris-DiderotdnamenW. Kolodziejak¢kaffiliationx IRIF University of Paris-DiderotdnamemJ. Chroboczekddatej÷Éhfilenamegrfc8967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130770hkeywordsÑprouting protocolnauthenticationfreplayqreplay protectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_¸∑ granking#crfcd8967irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1MAC Authentication for the Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9467∂habstractxÔBabel is a loop-avoiding, distance-vector routing protocol that is robust and efficient both in ordinary wired networks and in wireless mesh networks.  This document describes the Babel routing protocol and obsoletes RFC 6126 and RFC 7557.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx IRIF University of Paris-DiderotdnamemJ. Chroboczek¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinaziddatej÷Çhfilenamegrfc8966eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130769hkeywordsÑlBellman-FordcIGPnloop-avoidancelmesh networkkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDate_¸∑…granking#crfcd8966irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#Babel is a routing protocol based on the distance-vector algorithm augmented with mechanisms for loop avoidance and starvation avoidance.  This document describes a number of niches where Babel has been found to be useful and that are arguably not adequately served by more mature protocols.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx IRIF University of Paris-DiderotdnamemJ. Chroboczekddatej÷Çhfilenamegrfc8965eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymebabeldfullxbabel - Babel routing protocoldnamevBabel routing protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130768hkeywordsâodistance-vectordloopjstarvationlBellman-Fordgroutingprouting protocolhwirelesslmesh networkcIGPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate_¸∑√granking#crfcd8965irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Applicability of the Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis document specifies the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) data plane when operating over an MPLS Packet Switched Network.  It leverages existing pseudowire (PW) encapsulations and MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) encapsulations and mechanisms.  This document builds on the DetNet architecture and data plane framework.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationpMalis ConsultingdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Korhonenddatej÷Öhfilenamegrfc8964eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130767hkeywordsãfdetnetdmplsepreofdprefdpeofcprfcpefcpofjprotectionkreplicationkeliminationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`o˜granking#crfcd8964irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Data Plane: MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty*This document presents the author's effort to understand the delays involved in publishing an idea in the IETF or through the Independent Stream, from the first individual draft to the publication of the RFC. We analyze a set of randomly chosen RFCs approved in 2018, looking for history and delays. We also use two randomly chosen sets of RFCs published in 2008 and 1998 for comparing delays seen in 2018 to those observed 10 or 20 years ago. The average RFC in the 2018 sample was produced in 3 years and 4 months, of which 2 years and 10 months were spent in the working group, 3 to 4 months for IETF consensus and IESG review, and 3 to 4 months in RFC production. The main variation in RFC production delays comes from the AUTH48 phase.

We also measure the number of citations of the chosen RFC using Semantic Scholar, and compare citation counts with what we know about deployment. We show that citation counts indicate academic interest, but correlate only loosely with deployment or usage of the specifications. Counting web references could complement that.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtPrivate Octopus Inc.dnamejC. Huitemaddatej–/hfilenamegrfc8963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130766hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate`&vgranking#crfcd8963irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex/Evaluation of a Sample of RFCs Produced in 2018dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractypOne mantra of the IETF is, "We are not the Protocol Police." However, to ensure that protocols are implemented and deployed in full compliance with the IETF's standards, it is important to set up a body that is responsible for assessing and enforcing correct protocol behavior.

This document formally establishes the Protocol Police. It defines the body and sets out what aspects of IETF protocols they will police. This document acts as a point of reference for networking engineers, law enforcement officials, government representatives, and others. It also provides advice on how to report issues to the Protocol Police.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiG. Grover¢kaffiliation`dnamelN. ten Oever¢kaffiliation`dnamegC. Cath¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Sahibddatej–Uhfilenamegrfc8962eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-101955hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`fÂgranking#crfcd8962irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex Establishing the Protocol PolicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyMany protocols must detect packet loss for various reasons (e.g., to ensure reliability using retransmissions or to understand the level of congestion along a network path).  While many mechanisms have been designed to detect loss, ultimately, protocols can only count on the passage of time without delivery confirmation to declare a packet "lost".  Each implementation of a time-based loss detection mechanism represents a balance between correctness and timeliness; therefore, no implementation suits all situations.  This document provides high-level requirements for time-based loss detectors appropriate for general use in unicast communication across the Internet.  Within the requirements, implementations have latitude to define particulars that best address each situation.fadNamekMartin Dukedarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdICSIdnameiM. Allmanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8961eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130765hkeywordsÑvretransmission timeoutkpacket lossnloss detectionlrequirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ºa6granking#crfcd8961irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÈetotaletitlex*Requirements for Time-Based Loss DetectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document contains a specification of the MPLS base YANG data model.  The MPLS base YANG data model serves as a base framework for configuring and managing an MPLS switching subsystem on an MPLS-enabled router.  It is expected that other MPLS YANG data models (e.g., MPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) static, LDP, or RSVP-TE YANG data models) will augment the MPLS base YANG data model.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeramddatej÷Öhfilenamegrfc8960eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130764hkeywordsÑtMPLS YANG Data ModeljMPLS ModelhMPLS RIBxMPLS Routing Information BasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_›5Õgranking#␀crfcd8960irfcNumber#␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA YANG Data Model for MPLS BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxlThis document registers the "secret-token" URI scheme to aid in the identification of authentication tokens.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷Ñhfilenamegrfc8959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130763hkeywordsÇlbearer tokenntoken scanningkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`q5granking"ˇcrfcd8959irfcNumber"ˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe "secret-token" URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxüThis document updates RFC 3405 by removing references to the IETF tree from the procedures for requesting that a URI scheme be inserted into the URI.ARPA zone.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Hardieddatej–7hfilenamegrfc8958eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130762hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_»|˚granking"˛crfcd8958irfcNumber"˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberAetotaletitlex'Updated Registration Rules for URI.ARPAdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty RFC 8372 ("MPLS Flow Identification Considerations") describes the requirement for introducing flow identities within the MPLS architecture.  This document describes a method of accomplishing this by using a technique called "Synonymous Flow Labels" in which labels that mimic the behavior of other labels provide the identification service.  These identifiers can be used to trigger per-flow operations on the packet at the receiving label switching router.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies IncdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationxSouthend Technical CenterdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirskyddatej÷Ühfilenamegrfc8957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130761hkeywordsÉdMPLSdFloweLabelkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate`
Xgranking"˝crfcd8957irfcNumber"˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSynonymous Flow Label FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyn"Dissemination of Flow Specification Rules" (RFC 8955) provides a Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) extension for the propagation of traffic flow information for the purpose of rate limiting or filtering IPv4 protocol data packets.

This document extends RFC 8955 with IPv6 functionality. It also updates RFC 8955 by changing the IANA Flow Spec Component Types registry.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwnext layer Telekom GmbHdnamehC. Loibl¢kaffiliationwNTT Network InnovationsdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Haresddatej÷ãhfilenamegrfc8956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130760hkeywordsÇvBGP Flow SpecificationbV6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ÓH granking"¸crfcd8956irfcNumber"¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Dissemination of Flow Specification Rules for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document defines a Border Gateway Protocol Network Layer Reachability Information (BGP NLRI) encoding format that can be used to distribute (intra-domain and inter-domain) traffic Flow Specifications for IPv4 unicast and IPv4 BGP/MPLS VPN services. This allows the routing system to propagate information regarding more specific components of the traffic aggregate defined by an IP destination prefix.

It also specifies BGP Extended Community encoding formats, which can be used to propagate Traffic Filtering Actions along with the Flow Specification NLRI. Those Traffic Filtering Actions encode actions a routing system can take if the packet matches the Flow Specification.

This document obsoletes both RFC 5575 and RFC 7674.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwnext layer Telekom GmbHdnamehC. Loibl¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationwNTT Network InnovationsdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationpT-Mobile AustriadnameiM. Bacherddatej–'hfilenamegrfc8955eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130759hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDate_ÓHƒgranking"˚crfcd8955irfcNumber"˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Dissemination of Flow Specification RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8956d9184d9117∂habstracty_This document specifies the updated format of the Nonce extension in the Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) request and response messages.  OCSP is used to check the status of a certificate, and the Nonce extension is used to cryptographically bind an OCSP response message to a particular OCSP request message.  This document updates RFC 6960.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrPalo Alto NetworksdnamehM. Sahniddatej÷Åhfilenamegrfc8954eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130758hkeywordsÇqOCSP Nonce LengthuOCSP Nonce RandomnesskobsoletedByÅd9654epagesopublicationDate_∑Fgranking"˙crfcd8954irfcNumber"˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) Nonce ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty–The Coordinating Attack Response at Internet Scale (CARIS) 2 workshop, sponsored by the Internet Society, took place on 28 February and 1 March 2019 in Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA.  Participants spanned regional, national, international, and enterprise Computer Security Incident Response Teams (CSIRTs), operators, service providers, network and security operators, transport operators and researchers, incident response researchers, vendors, and participants from standards communities.  This workshop continued the work started at the first CARIS workshop, with a focus on scaling incident prevention and detection as the Internet industry moves to a stronger and a more ubiquitous deployment of session encryption.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCenter for Internet SecuritydnamekK. Moriartyddatej÷Éhfilenamegrfc8953eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130757hkeywordsÑrNetwork ManagementoAttack ResponseeCARIShIncidentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_⁄Ùpgranking"˘crfcd8953irfcNumber"˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexICoordinating Attack Response at Internet Scale 2 (CARIS2) Workshop ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx’This document describes a captive portal architecture.  Network provisioning protocols such as DHCP or Router Advertisements (RAs), an optional signaling protocol, and an HTTP API are used to provide the solution.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhAgilicusdnameiK. Larose¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Dolson¢kaffiliationfGooglednamefH. Liuddatej÷shfilenamegrfc8952eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgcapportdfullx$capport - Captive Portal InteractiondnamexCaptive Portal Interactiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130756hkeywordsànCaptive PortallArchitecturedWifieWi-FihWirelessgRoamingfMobilecAPIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_≈∞granking"¯crfcd8952irfcNumber"¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCaptive Portal ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document updates RFC 7030: Enrollment over Secure Transport to resolve some errata that were reported and that have proven to cause interoperability issues when RFC 7030 was extended.

This document deprecates the specification of "Content-Transfer-Encoding" headers for Enrollment over Secure Transport (EST) endpoints. This document fixes some syntactical errors in ASN.1 that were present.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnameiT. Werner¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Panddatej÷Ähfilenamegrfc8951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130755hkeywordsÉmCSRattributeseBRSKIgRFC7030kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_∑?granking"˜crfcd8951irfcNumber"˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUClarification of Enrollment over Secure Transport (EST): Transfer Encodings and ASN.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝Multiprotocol BGP (MP-BGP) specifies that the set of usable next-hop address families is determined by the Address Family Identifier (AFI) and the Subsequent Address Family Identifier (SAFI). The AFI/SAFI definitions for the IPv4 address family only have provisions for advertising a next-hop address that belongs to the IPv4 protocol when advertising IPv4 Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) or VPN-IPv4 NLRI.

This document specifies the extensions necessary to allow the advertising of IPv4 NLRI or VPN-IPv4 NLRI with a next-hop address that belongs to the IPv6 protocol. This comprises an extension of the AFI/SAFI definitions to allow the address of the next hop for IPv4 NLRI or VPN-IPv4 NLRI to also belong to the IPv6 protocol, the encoding of the next hop to determine which of the protocols the address actually belongs to, and a BGP Capability allowing MP-BGP peers to dynamically discover whether they can exchange IPv4 NLRI and VPN-IPv4 NLRI with an IPv6 next hop. This document obsoletes RFC 5549.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Agrawal¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamepK. Ananthamurthy¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patelddatej÷Çhfilenamegrfc8950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130754hkeywordsÑcbgpdmvpnevpnv4evpnv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_∂ˆ?granking"ˆcrfcd8950irfcNumber"ˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTAdvertising IPv4 Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) with an IPv6 Next HopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty$The Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) is a data format whose design goals include the possibility of extremely small code size, fairly small message size, and extensibility without the need for version negotiation. These design goals make it different from earlier binary serializations such as ASN.1 and MessagePack.

This document obsoletes RFC 7049, providing editorial improvements, new details, and errata fixes while keeping full compatibility with the interchange format of RFC 7049. It does not create a new version of the format.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130753hkeywordsÜfparsergdecodergencodermbinary formatwdata interchange formatdJSONkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDate_ ◊Lgranking"ıcrfcd8949irfcNumber"ıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber^etotaletitlex+Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThe IEEE originally structured the 48-bit Media Access Control (MAC) address space in such a way that half of it was reserved for local use. In 2017, the IEEE published a new standard (IEEE Std 802c) with a new optional Structured Local Address Plan (SLAP). It specifies different assignment approaches in four specified regions of the local MAC address space.

The IEEE is developing protocols to assign addresses (IEEE P802.1CQ). There is also work in the IETF on specifying a new mechanism that extends DHCPv6 operation to handle the local MAC address assignments.

This document proposes extensions to DHCPv6 protocols to enable a DHCPv6 client or a DHCPv6 relay to indicate a preferred SLAP quadrant to the server so that the server may allocate MAC addresses in the quadrant requested by the relay or client. A new DHCPv6 option (QUAD) is defined for this purpose.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationlInterDigitaldnameiA. Mouradddatej÷Éhfilenamegrfc8948eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130752hkeywordsÜiIEEE 802ccELIcSAIcAAIkMAC addressfLLADDRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_«ˇgranking"Ùcrfcd8948irfcNumber"ÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIStructured Local Address Plan (SLAP) Quadrant Selection Option for DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]In certain environments, e.g., large-scale virtualization deployments, new devices are created in an automated manner.  Such devices may have their link-layer addresses assigned in an automated fashion.  With sufficient scale, the likelihood of a collision using random assignment without duplication detection is not acceptable.  Therefore, an allocation mechanism is required.  This document proposes an extension to DHCPv6 that allows a scalable approach to link-layer address assignments where preassigned link-layer address assignments (such as by a manufacturer) are not possible or are unnecessary.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalski¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej–6hfilenamegrfc8947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130751hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_«¯granking"Ûcrfcd8947irfcNumber"ÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Link-Layer Address Assignment Mechanism for DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThe Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT) is specified in RFC 8225 to convey cryptographically signed information about the people involved in personal communications. This document extends PASSporT to include an indication that a call has been diverted from its original destination to a new one. This information can greatly improve the decisions made by verification services in call forwarding scenarios. Also specified here is an encapsulation mechanism for nesting a PASSporT within another PASSporT that assists relying parties in some diversion scenarios.

This document updates RFC 8224.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Petersonddatej÷~hfilenamegrfc8946eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130750hkeywordsÉcSIPdSTIRhIdentitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`'Åígranking"Úcrfcd8946irfcNumber"ÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT) Extension for Diverted CallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document describes a protocol for transaction-level authentication using shared secrets and one-way hashing. It can be used to authenticate dynamic updates to a DNS zone as coming from an approved client or to authenticate responses as coming from an approved name server.

No recommendation is made here for distributing the shared secrets; it is expected that a network administrator will statically configure name servers and clients using some out-of-band mechanism.

This document obsoletes RFCs 2845 and 4635.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiF. Dupont¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiS. Morris¢kaffiliationhFarsightdnamehP. Vixie¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamenO. Gudmundsson¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamemB. Wellingtonddatej–-hfilenamegrfc8945eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130749hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_≈~Agranking"Òcrfcd8945irfcNumber"ÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber]etotaletitlex4Secret Key Transaction Authentication for DNS (TSIG)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹This document defines a YANG data model for Layer 2 network topologies.  In particular, this data model augments the generic network and network topology data models with topology attributes that are specific to Layer 2.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefX. Wei¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfTecentdnamefA. Liuddatej–1hfilenamegrfc8944eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-130748hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate_≥â—granking"crfcd8944irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0A YANG Data Model for Layer 2 Network TopologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶The Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR), as specified in RFC 7049, is a data format whose design goals include the possibility of extremely small code size, fairly small message size, and extensibility without the need for version negotiation.

In CBOR, one point of extensibility is the definition of CBOR tags. RFC 7049 defines two tags for time: CBOR tag 0 (date/time string as per RFC 3339) and tag 1 (POSIX "seconds since the epoch"). Since then, additional requirements have become known. This specification defines a CBOR tag for a date text string (as per RFC 3339) for applications needing a textual date representation within the Gregorian calendar without a time. It also defines a CBOR tag for days since the date 1970-01-01 in the Gregorian calendar for applications needing a numeric date representation without a time. This specification is the reference document for IANA registration of the CBOR tags defined.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejA. Nadalin¢kaffiliationxpdv Financial Software GmbHdnamejJ. Richterddatej÷Éhfilenamegrfc8943eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130747hkeywordsÑx$Compact Binary Object RepresentationdCBORcTagdDatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_∏UÛgranking"Ôcrfcd8943irfcNumber"ÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) Tags for DatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytHTTP defines proactive content negotiation to allow servers to select the appropriate response for a given request, based upon the user agent's characteristics, as expressed in request headers. In practice, user agents are often unwilling to send those request headers, because it is not clear whether they will be used, and sending them impacts both performance and privacy.

This document defines an Accept-CH response header that servers can use to advertise their use of request headers for proactive content negotiation, along with a set of guidelines for the creation of such headers, colloquially known as "Client Hints."fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekI. Grigorik¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehY. Weissddatej÷}hfilenamegrfc8942eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130746hkeywordsÅsContent NegotiationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate`!Àügranking"Ócrfcd8942irfcNumber"ÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqHTTP Client HintsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document describes a set of data types and associated algorithms that are intended to make it easier and safer to define and handle HTTP header and trailer fields, known as "Structured Fields", "Structured Headers", or "Structured Trailers".  It is intended for use by specifications of new HTTP fields that wish to use a common syntax that is more restrictive than traditional HTTP field values.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationxThe Varnish Cache ProjectdnameiP-H. Kampddatej÷}hfilenamegrfc8941eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130745hkeywordsÇgtrailerfheaderkobsoletedByÅd9651epagesopublicationDate`!Àügranking"Ìcrfcd8941irfcNumber"ÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Structured Field Values for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLRFC 5247 is updated to define and clarify EAP Session-Id derivation for multiple Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) methods.  The derivation of Session-Id was not given for EAP Subscriber Identity Module (EAP-SIM) or EAP Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA) when using the fast reconnect exchange instead of full authentication.  The derivation of Session-Id for full authentication is clarified for both EAP-SIM and EAP-AKA.  The derivation of Session-Id for Protected EAP (PEAP) is also given.  The definition for PEAP follows the definition for other TLS-based EAP methods.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatej÷Çhfilenamegrfc8940eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130744hkeywordsâcEAPdPEAPgEAP-AKAgEAP-SIMcERPdFILSjSession-IDnfast reconnectcTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ì[∫granking"Ïcrfcd8940irfcNumber"ÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexµExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Session-Id Derivation for EAP Subscriber Identity Module (EAP-SIM), EAP Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA), and Protected EAP (PEAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document specifies the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) data plane operation for IP hosts and routers that provide DetNet service to IP-encapsulated data.  No DetNet-specific encapsulation is defined to support IP flows; instead, the existing IP-layer and higher-layer protocol header information is used to support flow identification and DetNet service delivery.  This document builds on the DetNet architecture (RFC 8655) and data plane framework (RFC 8938).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameiS. Bryantddatej÷}hfilenamegrfc8939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130743hkeywordsÑkApplicationhEndpointqService Sub-layertForwarding Sub-layerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_≈nègranking"Îcrfcd8939irfcNumber"ÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Data Plane: IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This document provides an overall framework for the Deterministic Networking (DetNet) data plane.  It covers concepts and considerations that are generally common to any DetNet data plane specification.  It describes related Controller Plane considerations as well.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationpMalis ConsultingdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameiS. Bryantddatej–(hfilenamegrfc8938eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130742hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_≈nogranking"Ícrfcd8938irfcNumber"ÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Deterministic Networking (DetNet) Data Plane FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyíRandomness is a crucial ingredient for Transport Layer Security (TLS) and related security protocols. Weak or predictable "cryptographically secure" pseudorandom number generators (CSPRNGs) can be abused or exploited for malicious purposes. An initial entropy source that seeds a CSPRNG might be weak or broken as well, which can also lead to critical and systemic security problems. This document describes a way for security protocol implementations to augment their CSPRNGs using long-term private keys. This improves randomness from broken or otherwise subverted CSPRNGs.

This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCISPAdnamejC. Cremers¢kaffiliationlCisco MerakidnamejL. Garratt¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnamemS. Smyshlyaev¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamekN. Sullivan¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamegC. Woodddatej÷Ähfilenamegrfc8937eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130741hkeywordsÉhSecuritylCryptographycTLSkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate_äÈ£granking"Ècrfcd8937irfcNumber"ÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex.Randomness Improvements for Security ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This specification defines how a series of Security Event Tokens (SETs) can be delivered to an intended recipient using HTTP POST over TLS initiated as a poll by the recipient.  The specification also defines how delivery can be assured, subject to the SET Recipient's need for assurance.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfAmazondnamejA. Backman¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationhCoinbasednamelM. Scurtescu¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiM. Ansari¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejA. Nadalinddatej÷{hfilenamegrfc8936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhseceventdfullxsecevent - Security EventsdnameoSecurity Eventsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130740hkeywordsánJSON Web TokencJWTtSecurity Event TokencSEThDeliveryxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_≈íWgranking"Ëcrfcd8936irfcNumber"ËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Poll-Based Security Event Token (SET) Delivery Using HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This specification defines how a Security Event Token (SET) can be delivered to an intended recipient using HTTP POST over TLS.  The SET is transmitted in the body of an HTTP POST request to an endpoint operated by the recipient, and the recipient indicates successful or failed transmission via the HTTP response.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfAmazondnamejA. Backman¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationhCoinbasednamelM. Scurtescu¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiM. Ansari¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejA. Nadalinddatej÷{hfilenamegrfc8935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhseceventdfullxsecevent - Security EventsdnameoSecurity Eventsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130739hkeywordsánJSON Web TokencJWTtSecurity Event TokencSEThDeliveryxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_≈í+granking"Ácrfcd8935irfcNumber"ÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Push-Based Security Event Token (SET) Delivery Using HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document defines a set of extensions to the stateful PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) to enable Label Switched Path (LSP) path computation, activation, setup, and deletion based on scheduled time intervals for the LSP and the actual network resource usage in a centralized network environment, as stated in RFC 8413.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamegH. Chen¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiY. Zhuang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelliddatej÷Åhfilenamegrfc8934eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130738hkeywordsÅxPath Computation ElementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ùæŸgranking"Êcrfcd8934irfcNumber"ÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgPCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Label Switched Path (LSP) Scheduling with Stateful PCEdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractx≈This document updates the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) specified in RFC 5652 to ensure that algorithm identifiers in signed-data and authenticated-data content types are adequately protected.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷rhfilenamegrfc8933eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130737hkeywordsÉndigitally signlauthenticatexalgorithm identifier integritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Ä‰Fgranking"Âcrfcd8933irfcNumber"ÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTUpdate to the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) for Algorithm Identifier ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyBThis document presents operational, policy, and security considerations for DNS recursive resolver operators who choose to offer DNS privacy services. With these recommendations, the operator can make deliberate decisions regarding which services to provide, as well as understanding how those decisions and the alternatives impact the privacy of users.

This document also presents a non-normative framework to assist writers of a Recursive operator Privacy Statement, analogous to DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) Policies and DNSSEC Practice Statements described in RFC 6841.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjSinodun ITdnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamemB. Overeinder¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnametR. van Rijswijk-Deij¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednameiA. Mankinddatej÷}hfilenamegrfc8932eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130736hkeywordsÅcDNSkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate_ì[àgranking"‰crfcd8932irfcNumber"‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberËetotaletitlex1Recommendations for DNS Privacy Service OperatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≈This document updates RFC 4944 with a protocol that forwards individual fragments across a route-over mesh and recovers them end to end, with congestion control capabilities to protect the network.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubertddatej÷thfilenamegrfc8931eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130735hkeywordsÑcLLNjRoute-OverdmeshcIoTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_º:granking"„crfcd8931irfcNumber"„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXIPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) Selective Fragment RecoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document provides generic rules to enable the forwarding of an IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) fragment over a route-over network.  Forwarding fragments can improve both end-to-end latency and reliability as well as reduce the buffer requirements in intermediate nodes; it may be implemented using RFC 4944 and Virtual Reassembly Buffers (VRBs).fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAnalog DevicesdnamekT. Watteyne¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷shfilenamegrfc8930eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130734hkeywordsÇg6LoWPANhFragmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_º:granking"‚crfcd8930irfcNumber"‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=On Forwarding 6LoWPAN Fragments over a Multi-Hop IPv6 NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This document updates RFCs 6775 and 8505 in order to enable proxy services for IPv6 Neighbor Discovery by Routing Registrars called "Backbone Routers".  Backbone Routers are placed along the wireless edge of a backbone and federate multiple wireless links to form a single Multi-Link Subnet (MLSN).fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationvBlue Meadow NetworkingdnamelC.E. Perkins¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamepE. Levy-Abegnoliddatej÷rhfilenamegrfc8929eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130733hkeywordsÖhND ProxymRouting ProxynBridging Proxyhproxy NDhproxy-NDkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate_º9granking"·crfcd8929irfcNumber"·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletIPv6 Backbone RouterdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document updates the IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) Neighbor Discovery (ND) protocol defined in RFCs 6775 and 8505.  The new extension is called Address-Protected Neighbor Discovery (AP-ND), and it protects the owner of an address against address theft and impersonation attacks in a Low-Power and Lossy Network (LLN).  Nodes supporting this extension compute a cryptographic identifier (Crypto-ID), and use it with one or more of their Registered Addresses.  The Crypto-ID identifies the owner of the Registered Address and can be used to provide proof of ownership of the Registered Addresses.  Once an address is registered with the Crypto-ID and a proof of ownership is provided, only the owner of that address can modify the registration information, thereby enforcing Source Address Validation.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliation`dnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehM. Sethi¢kaffiliationxStruik Security ConsultancydnameiR. Struikddatej÷phfilenamegrfc8928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130732hkeywordsÉtAddress registrationoNetwork Overlayxhost to router interfacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_º0granking"‡crfcd8928irfcNumber"‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEAddress-Protected Neighbor Discovery for Low-Power and Lossy NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9926d9927¥habstractyÜThis document proposes a format, called JSON Type Definition (JTD), for describing the shape of JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) messages. Its main goals are to enable code generation from schemas as well as portable validation with standardized error indicators. To this end, JTD is intentionally limited to be no more expressive than the type systems of mainstream programming languages. This intentional limitation, as well as the decision to make JTD schemas be JSON documents, makes tooling atop of JTD easier to build.

This document does not have IETF consensus and is presented here to facilitate experimentation with the concept of JTD.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgSegmentdnameiU. Carionddatej÷{hfilenamegrfc8927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130731hkeywordsÑwdata interchange formattdescription languageoschema languageltree grammarkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDate_•›'granking"ﬂcrfcd8927irfcNumber"ﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitletJSON Type DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydNetwork virtualization involves the cooperation of devices with a wide variety of capabilities such as software and hardware tunnel endpoints, transit fabrics, and centralized control clusters.  As a result of their role in tying together different elements of the system, the requirements on tunnels are influenced by all of these components.  Therefore, flexibility is the most important aspect of a tunneling protocol if it is to keep pace with the evolution of technology.  This document describes Geneve, an encapsulation protocol designed to recognize and accommodate these changing capabilities and needs.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Gross¢kaffiliationeInteldnamehI. Ganga¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamejT. Sridharddatej÷xhfilenamegrfc8926eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-130730hkeywordsàgoverlayftunneljextensiblehvariablehmetadatagoptionshendpointgtransitkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate_•›granking"ﬁcrfcd8926irfcNumber"ﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Geneve: Generic Network Virtualization EncapsulationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|This document specifies a DHCPv4 option to indicate that a host supports an IPv6-only mode and is willing to forgo obtaining an IPv4 address if the network provides IPv6 connectivity.  It also updates RFC 2563 to specify DHCPv4 server behavior when the server receives a DHCPDISCOVER not containing the Auto-Configure option but containing the new option defined in this document.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Colitti¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkova¢kaffiliationiSandelmandnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalskiddatej–2hfilenamegrfc8925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130729hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_äÈ¢granking"›crfcd8925irfcNumber"›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%IPv6-Only Preferred Option for DHCPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxáThis document provides a reference framework for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) for Service Function Chaining (SFC).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehN. Kumar¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamekR. Krishnan¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwaniddatej÷|hfilenamegrfc8924eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130728hkeywordsÉcSFCcOAMiFrameworkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Äˆ—granking"‹crfcd8924irfcNumber"‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Service Function Chaining (SFC) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx€This document recommends a minimal set of Transport Services offered by end systems and gives guidance on choosing among the available mechanisms and protocols.  It is based on the set of transport features in RFC 8303.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamekS. Gjessingddatej÷2hfilenamegrfc8923eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130727hkeywordsÇdtapsrtransport serviceskobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDate_ê¡øgranking"€crfcd8923irfcNumber"€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3A Minimal Set of Transport Services for End SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document provides a survey of commonly used or notable network security protocols, with a focus on how they interact and integrate with applications and transport protocols.  Its goal is to supplement efforts to define and catalog Transport Services by describing the interfaces required to add security protocols.  This survey is not limited to protocols developed within the scope or context of the IETF, and those included represent a superset of features a Transport Services system may need to support.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiTU BerlindnamekT. Enghardt¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationxAkamai Technologies, Inc.dnamegK. Rose¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamegC. Woodddatej÷qhfilenamegrfc8922eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130726hkeywordsÇsTransport ProtocolsrTransport SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ê¡ªgranking"⁄crfcd8922irfcNumber"⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMA Survey of the Interaction between Security Protocols and Transport ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractywThis document defines the Connectivity Provisioning Negotiation Protocol (CPNP), which is designed to facilitate the dynamic negotiation of service parameters.

CPNP is a generic protocol that can be used for various negotiation purposes that include (but are not necessarily limited to) connectivity provisioning services, storage facilities, Content Delivery Networks, etc.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenet¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Zhang¢kaffiliationeCERTHdnamemP. Georgatsosddatej÷Zhfilenamegrfc8921eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130725hkeywordsàcSDNvOrder Request HandlingjAutomationtDynamic ProvisioningcCDNoInterconnectionpService DeliveryrService ActivationkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDate_ê¡∂granking"Ÿcrfcd8921irfcNumber"ŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexVDynamic Service Negotiation: The Connectivity Provisioning Negotiation Protocol (CPNP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘Existing traffic-engineering-related link attribute advertisements have been defined and are used in RSVP-TE deployments.  Since the original RSVP-TE use case was defined, additional applications (e.g., Segment Routing Policy and Loop-Free Alternates) that also make use of the link attribute advertisements have been defined.  In cases where multiple applications wish to make use of these link attributes, the current advertisements do not support application-specific values for a given attribute, nor do they support indication of which applications are using the advertised value for a given link.  This document introduces new link attribute advertisements in OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 that address both of these shortcomings.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationfApstradnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drakeddatej–+hfilenamegrfc8920eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130724hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9492epagesopublicationDate_íœgranking"ÿcrfcd8920irfcNumber"ÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)OSPF Application-Specific Link AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøExisting traffic-engineering-related link attribute advertisements have been defined and are used in RSVP-TE deployments.  Since the original RSVP-TE use case was defined, additional applications (e.g., Segment Routing Policy and Loop-Free Alternates) that also make use of the link attribute advertisements have been defined.  In cases where multiple applications wish to make use of these link attributes, the current advertisements do not support application-specific values for a given attribute, nor do they support indication of which applications are using the advertised value for a given link.  This document introduces new link attribute advertisements that address both of these shortcomings.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drakeddatej–+hfilenamegrfc8919eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130723hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9479epagesopublicationDate_íÀgranking"◊crfcd8919irfcNumber"◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*IS-IS Application-Specific Link AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThe key to the extensibility of the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol has been the handling of unsupported and/or invalid Type-Length-Value (TLV) tuples. Although there are explicit statements in existing specifications, deployment experience has shown that there are inconsistencies in the behavior when a TLV that is disallowed in a particular Protocol Data Unit (PDU) is received.

This document discusses such cases and makes the correct behavior explicit in order to ensure that interoperability is maximized.

This document updates RFCs 5305 and 6232.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Wells¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehS. Hegdeddatej÷~hfilenamegrfc8918eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130722hkeywordsÇcTLVeIS-ISkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_mògranking"÷crfcd8918irfcNumber"÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInvalid TLV Handling in IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document adds the 'LoST-Validation' service tag to the Straightforward-Naming Authority PoinTeR (S-NAPTR) Application Service Tag IANA registry.  This tag can appear in a Naming Authority Pointer (NAPTR) Domain Name System (DNS) record to assist clients of the Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Protocol in identifying LoST servers designated for location validation.  This tag and the information about its use update RFC 5222, which enables the explicit discovery of a server that supports location validation.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxCore Technology ConsultingdnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Rosenddatej÷}hfilenamegrfc8917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130721hkeywordsáhlocationdLoSTiemergencyremergency servicesdecrfclvfbi3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_íægranking"’crfcd8917irfcNumber"’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^The LoST-Validation Straightforward-Naming Authority PoinTeR (S-NAPTR) Application Service TagdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxèThis document defines a YANG data model for the configuration and management of Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP) protocol operations.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationvIndividual contributordnamehA. Peter¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelM. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefF. Guo¢kaffiliationsMetaswitch NetworksdnamemP. McAllisterddatej÷whfilenamegrfc8916eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130720hkeywordsÇdMSDPdYANGkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDate_ùæ¥granking"‘crfcd8916irfcNumber"‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDA YANG Data Model for the Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis memo specifies Network Time Security (NTS), a mechanism for using Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) to provide cryptographic security for the client-server mode of the Network Time Protocol (NTP).

NTS is structured as a suite of two loosely coupled sub-protocols. The first (NTS Key Establishment (NTS-KE)) handles initial authentication and key establishment over TLS. The second (NTS Extension Fields for NTPv4) handles encryption and authentication during NTP time synchronization via extension fields in the NTP packets, and holds all required state only on the client via opaque cookies.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnameiD. Franke¢kaffiliationcPTBdnameiD. Sibold¢kaffiliationcPTBdnamejK. Teichel¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Dansarie¢kaffiliationfNetnoddnamekR. Sundbladddatej÷uhfilenamegrfc8915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130719hkeywordsÑiIntegritynAuthenticationcNTPhSecuritykobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate_umdgranking"”crfcd8915irfcNumber"”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Network Time Security for the Network Time ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document defines an extensible method to return additional information about the cause of DNS errors.  Though created primarily to extend SERVFAIL to provide additional information about the cause of DNS and DNSSEC failures, the Extended DNS Errors option defined in this document allows all response types to contain extended error information.  Extended DNS Error information does not change the processing of RCODEs.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationcISCdnamegE. Hunt¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiR. Arends¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamekD. Lawrenceddatej–%hfilenamegrfc8914eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130718hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ìHgranking"“crfcd8914irfcNumber"“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesExtended DNS ErrorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document specifies a data model for client and server
implementations of the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP).
This document defines the TWAMP data model through Unified Modeling
Language (UML) class diagrams and formally specifies it using the
YANG data modeling language (RFC 7950).  The data model is compliant
with the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamehR. Civil¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationsXoriant CorporationdnameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationgDetecondnamenK. Pentikousisddatejœπhfilenamegrfc8913eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130717hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateaï˜≥granking"—crfcd8913irfcNumber"—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP) YANG Data ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis memo defines the set of initial entries for the IANA Registry of
