Loss, Packet Delay Variation, DNS Response Latency and Loss, UDP
Poisson One-Way Delay and Loss, UDP Periodic One-Way Delay and Loss,
ICMP Round-Trip Latency and Loss, and TCP Round-Trip Delay and Loss.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamejK. D'Souzaddatej–hfilenamegrfc8912eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130716hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateaï˜≤granking"–crfcd8912irfcNumber"–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Initial Performance Metrics Registry EntriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∏This document defines the format for the IANA Registry of Performance
Metrics.  This document also gives a set of guidelines for Registered
Performance Metric requesters and reviewers.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiA. Akhterddatej÷nhfilenamegrfc8911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130715hkeywordsÉdIPPMdLosseDelaykobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateaï˜®granking"œcrfcd8911irfcNumber"œestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Registry for Performance MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVIn many environments offering short-term or temporary Internet access (such as coffee shops), it is common to start new connections in a captive portal mode. This highly restricts what the user can do until the user has satisfied the captive portal conditions.

This document describes a DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 option and a Router Advertisement (RA) option to inform clients that they are behind some sort of captive portal enforcement device, and that they will need to satisfy the Captive Portal conditions to get Internet access. It is not a full solution to address all of the issues that clients may have with captive portals; it is designed to be one component of a standardized approach for hosts to interact with such portals. While this document defines how the network operator may convey the captive portal API endpoint to hosts, the specific methods of satisfying and interacting with the captive portal are out of scope of this document.

This document replaces RFC 7710, which used DHCP code point 160. Due to a conflict, this document specifies 114. Consequently, this document also updates RFC 3679.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationdLoondnamehE. Klineddatej÷|hfilenamegrfc8910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgcapportdfullx$capport - Captive Portal InteractiondnamexCaptive Portal Interactiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130714hkeywordsÑnCaptive PortalmWalled GardenkCoffee-shopeHotelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_iwﬁgranking"Œcrfcd8910irfcNumber"ŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexECaptive-Portal Identification in DHCP and Router Advertisements (RAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document specifies the format and contents of data escrow deposits targeted primarily for domain name registries.  The specification is designed to be independent of the underlying objects that are being escrowed, and therefore it could also be used for purposes other than domain name registries.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiG. Lozanoddatej÷~hfilenamegrfc8909eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130713hkeywordsÇkdata escrowhregistrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_≥âœgranking"Õcrfcd8909irfcNumber"ÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Registry Data Escrow SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx◊This document describes an HTTP API that allows clients to interact with a Captive Portal system.  With this API, clients can discover how to get out of captivity and fetch state about their Captive Portal sessions.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamejD. Thakoreddatej–+hfilenamegrfc8908eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgcapportdfullx$capport - Captive Portal InteractiondnamexCaptive Portal Interactiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130712hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_iwÿgranking"Ãcrfcd8908irfcNumber"ÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlerCaptive Portal APIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Terminal Access Controller Access-Control System Plus (TACACS+) protocol, which is widely deployed today to provide Device Administration for routers, network access servers, and other networked computing devices via one or more centralized servers.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamegT. Dahm¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamefA. Ota¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamepD.C. Medway Gash¢kaffiliationnIPsec ResearchdnameiD. Carrel¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Grantddatej÷qhfilenamegrfc8907eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130711hkeywordsÇgTACACS+hProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate_u\granking"Àcrfcd8907irfcNumber"ÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLThe Terminal Access Controller Access-Control System Plus (TACACS+) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9887∑habstractyThe DNS is a query/response protocol. Failing to respond to queries, or responding incorrectly, causes both immediate operational problems and long-term problems with protocol development.

This document identifies a number of common kinds of queries to which some servers either fail to respond or respond incorrectly. This document also suggests procedures for zone operators to apply to identify and remediate the problem.

The document does not look at the DNS data itself, just the structure of the responses.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejM. Andrews¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiR. Bellisddatej÷vhfilenamegrfc8906eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130710hkeywordsÇkconformancejcompliancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_j9granking" crfcd8906irfcNumber" estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÁetotaletitlexCA Common Operational Problem in DNS Servers: Failure to CommunicatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyBThis document defines the 'payto' Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme for designating targets for payments.

A unified URI scheme for all payment target types allows applications to offer user interactions with URIs that represent payment targets, simplifying the introduction of new payment systems and applications.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpTaler Systems SAdnamegF. Dold¢kaffiliationx#Bern University of Applied SciencesdnamekC. Grothoffddatej÷yhfilenamegrfc8905eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130709hkeywordsÅhpaymentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_îìgranking"…crfcd8905irfcNumber"…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex#The 'payto' URI Scheme for PaymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyWThis document describes an email authentication method compliant with RFC 8601. The method consists of looking up the sender's IP address in a DNS whitelist. This document provides information in case the method is seen in the field, suggests a useful practice, and registers the relevant keywords.

This document does not consider blacklists.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Veselyddatej÷xhfilenamegrfc8904eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130708hkeywordsÉeDNSWLeEMAILvAuthentication-ResultskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_cÓºgranking"»crfcd8904irfcNumber"»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex;DNS Whitelist (DNSWL) Email Authentication Method ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThe DDoS Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) effort is intended to provide protocols to facilitate interoperability across disparate DDoS Mitigation solutions.  This document presents sample use cases that describe the interactions expected between the DOTS components as well as DOTS messaging exchanges.  These use cases are meant to identify the interacting DOTS components, how they collaborate, and what the typical information to be exchanged is.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnNetscout, Inc.dnamejR. Dobbins¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migault¢kaffiliationnHTT ConsultingdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationxIron Mountain Data CentersdnameiN. Teague¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefL. Xia¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamelK. Nishizukaddatej–.hfilenamegrfc8903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130707hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`∞ûgranking"«crfcd8903irfcNumber"«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Use Cases for DDoS Open Threat SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxºThe IEEE and ETSI have specified a type of end-entity certificate.  This document defines an experimental change to TLS to support IEEE/ETSI certificate types to authenticate TLS entities.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmTelecom ParisdnameiM. Msahli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamehW. Whyte¢kaffiliationmTelecom ParisdnamenA. Serhrouchni¢kaffiliationmTelecom ParisdnameiH. Labiodddatej÷lhfilenamegrfc8902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130706hkeywordsÑcTLSx/Intelligent Transport System (ITS) CertificatesdIEEEdETSIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_t“granking"∆crfcd8902irfcNumber"∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHTLS Authentication Using Intelligent Transport System (ITS) CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆Many enterprises today employ the service of multiple DNS providers to distribute their authoritative DNS service.  Deploying DNSSEC in such an environment may present some challenges, depending on the configuration and feature set in use.  In particular, when each DNS provider independently signs zone data with their own keys, additional key-management mechanisms are necessary.  This document presents deployment models that accommodate this scenario and describes these key-management requirements.  These models do not require any changes to the behavior of validating resolvers, nor do they impose the new key-management requirements on authoritative servers not involved in multi-signer configurations.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamehS. Huque¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamegP. Aras¢kaffiliationjSinodun ITdnamelJ. Dickinson¢kaffiliationcNS1dnameiJ. Vcelak¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnameiD. Blackaddatej÷whfilenamegrfc8901eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130705hkeywordsÖfDNSSEChMultiplehProviderfSignerfModelskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_m2granking"≈crfcd8901irfcNumber"≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMulti-Signer DNSSEC ModelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxÌThis document describes IP fragmentation and explains how it introduces fragility to Internet communication.

This document also proposes alternatives to IP fragmentation and provides recommendations for developers and network operators.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationlSI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gontddatej÷ahfilenamegrfc8900eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130704hkeywordsÇdIPv6mFragmentationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_\ˆgranking"ƒcrfcd8900irfcNumber"ƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÊetotaletitlex#IP Fragmentation Considered FragiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThis document specifies Datagram Packetization Layer Path MTU Discovery (DPLPMTUD). This is a robust method for Path MTU Discovery (PMTUD) for datagram Packetization Layers (PLs). It allows a PL, or a datagram application that uses a PL, to discover whether a network path can support the current size of datagram. This can be used to detect and reduce the message size when a sender encounters a packet black hole. It can also probe a network path to discover whether the maximum packet size can be increased. This provides functionality for datagram transports that is equivalent to the PLPMTUD specification for TCP, specified in RFC 4821, which it updates. It also updates the UDP Usage Guidelines to refer to this method for use with UDP datagrams and updates SCTP.

The document provides implementation notes for incorporating Datagram PMTUD into IETF datagram transports or applications that use datagram transports.

This specification updates RFC 4960, RFC 4821, RFC 6951, RFC 8085, and RFC 8261.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamehT. Jones¢kaffiliationx'M√ºnster University of Applied SciencesdnameiM. T√ºxen¢kaffiliationx'M√ºnster University of Applied SciencesdnamelI. R√ºngeler¢kaffiliationx'M√ºnster University of Applied SciencesdnamejT. V√∂lkerddatej÷yhfilenamegrfc8899eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130703hkeywordsÖcUDPdSCTPiTransportePMTUDgPLPMTUDkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate_\€granking"√crfcd8899irfcNumber"√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Packetization Layer Path MTU Discovery for Datagram TransportsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document defines the "Bearer" authentication scheme for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and a mechanism by which user authentication and SIP registration authorization is delegated to a third party, using the OAuth 2.0 framework and OpenID Connect Core 1.0.  This document updates RFC 3261 to provide guidance on how a SIP User Agent Client (UAC) responds to a SIP 401/407 response that contains multiple WWW-Authenticate/Proxy-Authenticate header fields.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAuth0dnamenR. Shekh-Yusef¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejV. Pascualddatej÷yhfilenamegrfc8898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130702hkeywordsÉiSIP OAuthx3rd party authenticationxThird party authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Zn`granking"¬crfcd8898irfcNumber"¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Third-Party Token-Based Authentication and Authorization for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document provides a single reference point for requirements for Relying Party (RP) software for use in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI).  It cites requirements that appear in several RPKI RFCs, making it easier for implementers to become aware of these requirements.  Over time, this RFC will be updated to reflect changes to the requirements and guidance specified in the RFCs discussed herein.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdZDNSdnameeD. Ma¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegS. Kentddatej–#hfilenamegrfc8897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130701hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Qçgranking"¡crfcd8897irfcNumber"¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJRequirements for Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Relying PartiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document is an extension to the base Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) protocol.  It extends the ALTO cost information service so that applications decide not only 'where' to connect but also 'when'.  This is useful for applications that need to perform bulk data transfer and would like to schedule these transfers during an off-peak hour, for example.  This extension introduces the ALTO Cost Calendar with which an ALTO Server exposes ALTO cost values in JSON arrays where each value corresponds to a given time interval.  The time intervals, as well as other Calendar attributes, are specified in the Information Resources Directory and ALTO Server responses.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamenS. Randriamasy¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegR. Yang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Deng¢kaffiliationrThales DeutschlanddnameiN. Schwanddatej–hfilenamegrfc8896eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130700hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate_•Ø[granking"¿crfcd8896irfcNumber"¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Cost CalendardtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThe Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) protocol (RFC 7285) provides network-related information, called network information resources, to client applications so that clients can make informed decisions in utilizing network resources.  This document presents a mechanism to allow an ALTO server to push updates to ALTO clients to achieve two benefits: (1) updates can be incremental, in that if only a small section of an information resource changes, the ALTO server can send just the changes and (2) updates can be immediate, in that the ALTO server can send updates as soon as they are available.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamehW. Roome¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yangddatej÷fhfilenamegrfc8895eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130699hkeywordsÅdALTOkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDate_•ØRgranking"øcrfcd8895irfcNumber"øestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Incremental Updates Using Server-Sent Events (SSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis document specifies the Simple Certificate Enrolment Protocol (SCEP), a PKI protocol that leverages existing technology by using Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS, formerly known as PKCS #7) and PKCS #10 over HTTP.  SCEP is the evolution of the enrolment protocol sponsored by Cisco Systems, which enjoys wide support in both client and server implementations, as well as being relied upon by numerous other industry standards that work with certificates.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamejP. Gutmannddatej–hfilenamegrfc8894eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130698hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate_Zf∂granking"æcrfcd8894irfcNumber"æestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Simple Certificate Enrolment ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)A BGP speaker may perform Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) origin validation not only on routes received from BGP neighbors and routes that are redistributed from other routing protocols, but also on routes it sends to BGP neighbors.  For egress policy, it is important that the classification use the 'effective origin AS' of the processed route, which may specifically be altered by the commonly available knobs, such as removing private ASes, confederation handling, and other modifications of the origin AS.  This document updates RFC 6811.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx"Internet Initiative Japan & ArrcusdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliation`dnamegR. Volk¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehJ. Heitzddatej÷whfilenamegrfc8893eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130697hkeywordsÉgroutinghsecuritydRPKIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_nÅégranking"Ωcrfcd8893irfcNumber"ΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJResource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Origin Validation for BGP ExportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document provides guidelines and procedures for those who are
defining, registering, or evaluating definitions of new interface
types ("ifType" values) and tunnel types.  The original definition of
the IANA interface type registry predated the use of IANA
Considerations sections and YANG modules, so some confusion arose
over time.  Tunnel types were added later, with the same requirements
and allocation policy as interface types.  This document updates RFC
2863 and provides updated guidance for these registries.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamelD. Romascanuddatej÷uhfilenamegrfc8892eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130696hkeywordsÉfifTypejtunnelTypesTransmission NumberkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Iî1granking"ºcrfcd8892irfcNumber"ºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKGuidelines and Registration Procedures for Interface Types and Tunnel TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyˇIn addition to a new cipher with a block length of n=128 bits (referred to as "Kuznyechik" and described in RFC 7801), Russian Federal standard GOST R 34.12-2015 includes an updated version of the block cipher with a block length of n=64 bits and key length of k=256 bits, which is also referred to as "Magma".  The algorithm is an updated version of an older block cipher with a block length of n=64 bits described in GOST 28147-89 (RFC 5830).  This document is intended to be a source of information about the updated version of the 64-bit cipher.  It may facilitate the use of the block cipher in Internet applications by providing information for developers and users of the GOST 64-bit cipher with the revised version of the cipher for encryption and decryption.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtJSC "NPK Kryptonite"dnamekV. Dolmatov¢kaffiliationlAuriga, Inc.dnamelD. Baryshkovddatej÷rhfilenamegrfc8891eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130695hkeywordsÇeMagmalBlock CipherkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_`Regranking"ªcrfcd8891irfcNumber"ªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex'GOST R 34.12-2015: Block Cipher "Magma"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx˘This document explains why the IAB believes that, when there is a conflict between the interests of end users of the Internet and other parties, IETF decisions should favor end users.  It also explores how the IETF can more effectively achieve this.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷thfilenamegrfc8890eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130694hkeywordsÅkstakeholderkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate_HJ*granking"∫crfcd8890irfcNumber"∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexThe Internet is for End UsersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Alternate-Marking method, as presented in RFC 8321, can only be applied to point-to-point flows, because it assumes that all the packets of the flow measured on one node are measured again by a single second node.  This document generalizes and expands this methodology to measure any kind of unicast flow whose packets can follow several different paths in the network -- in wider terms, a multipoint-to-multipoint network.  For this reason, the technique here described is called "Multipoint Alternate Marking".fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamekG. Fioccola¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekM. Cociglio¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamehA. Sapio¢kaffiliationuPolitecnico di TorinodnamehR. Sistoddatej÷dhfilenamegrfc8889eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130693hkeywordsÑxClustered Alternate MarkingxMultipoint Marking MethodxMultipoint Coloring TechniquerNetwork ClusteringkobsoletedByÅd9342epagesopublicationDate_Gsgranking"πcrfcd8889irfcNumber"πestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQMultipoint Alternate-Marking Method for Passive and Hybrid Performance MonitoringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåAn effective RTP congestion control algorithm requires more fine-grained feedback on packet loss, timing, and Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) marks than is provided by the standard RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Sender Report (SR) and Receiver Report (RR) packets.  This document describes an RTCP feedback message intended to enable congestion control for interactive real-time traffic using RTP.  The feedback message is designed for use with a sender-based congestion control algorithm, in which the receiver of an RTP flow sends back to the sender RTCP feedback packets containing the information the sender needs to perform congestion control.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnameiZ. Sarker¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationlcallstats.iodnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationmAcousticCommsdnamejM. Ramalhoddatej÷àhfilenamegrfc8888eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130692hkeywordsÑrCongestion controlpfeedback messagecRTPdRTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`}granking"∏crfcd8888irfcNumber"∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Feedback for Congestion ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx”This document defines a binding for the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) over a WebSocket transport layer.  The WebSocket binding for JMAP provides higher performance than the current HTTP binding for JMAP.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastmaildnamelK. Murchisonddatej–hfilenamegrfc8887eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130691hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_HJ&granking"∑crfcd8887irfcNumber"∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAA JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) Subprotocol for WebSocketdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybDeploying a new network device in a location where the operator has no staff of its own often requires that an employee physically travel to the location to perform the initial install and configuration, even in shared facilities with "remote-hands" (or similar) support. In many cases, this could be avoided if there were an easy way to transfer the initial configuration to a new device while still maintaining confidentiality of the configuration.

This document extends existing vendor proprietary auto-install to provide limited confidentiality to initial configuration during bootstrapping of the device.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehC. Doyleddatej÷{hfilenamegrfc8886eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130690hkeywordsÜhautobootiauto-bootkautoinstalldtftpginstallebunnykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_k◊‰granking"∂crfcd8886irfcNumber"∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuSecure Device InstalldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈Distributed Mobility Management solutions allow networks to be set up in such a way that traffic is distributed optimally and centrally deployed anchors are not relied upon to provide IP mobility support.

There are many different approaches to address Distributed Mobility Management -- for example, extending network-based mobility protocols (like Proxy Mobile IPv6) or client-based mobility protocols (like Mobile IPv6), among others. This document follows the former approach and proposes a solution based on Proxy Mobile IPv6, in which mobility sessions are anchored at the last IP hop router (called the mobility anchor and access router). The mobility anchor and access router is an enhanced access router that is also able to operate as a local mobility anchor or mobility access gateway on a per-prefix basis. The document focuses on the required extensions to effectively support the simultaneous anchoring several flows at different distributed gateways.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamenA. de la Oliva¢kaffiliationgAthonetdnamehF. Giust¢kaffiliationfSIGFOXdnamelJC. Z√∫√±iga¢kaffiliationlInterDigitaldnameiA. Mouradddatej÷shfilenamegrfc8885eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130689hkeywordsáfPMIPv6fanchorrsession continuitytaddress reachabilitycHNPcCMDdMAARkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Ä‰>granking"µcrfcd8885irfcNumber"µestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Proxy Mobile IPv6 Extensions for Distributed Mobility ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyôInformation-Centric Networking (ICN) is a new paradigm where the network provides users with named content instead of communication channels between hosts.  This document outlines some research directions for ICN with respect to applying ICN approaches for coping with natural or human-generated, large-scale disasters.  This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx)HFT Stuttgart - Univ. of Applied SciencesdnamejJ. Seedorf¢kaffiliationxUniversity of G√∂ttingendnameoM. Arumaithurai¢kaffiliationrKDDI Research Inc.dnameiA. Tagami¢kaffiliationxUniversity of CaliforniadnameoK. Ramakrishnan¢kaffiliationvUniversity Tor VergatadnamerN. Blefari-Melazziddatej÷nhfilenamegrfc8884eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130688hkeywordsÅcICNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ì%°granking"¥crfcd8884irfcNumber"¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexXResearch Directions for Using Information-Centric Networking (ICN) in Disaster ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWNetwork nodes may discard packets if they are unable to process protocol headers of packets due to processing constraints or limits.  When such packets are dropped, the sender receives no indication, so it cannot take action to address the cause of discarded packets.  This specification defines several new ICMPv6 errors that can be sent by a node that discards packets because it is unable to process the protocol headers.  A node that receives such an ICMPv6 error may use the information to diagnose packet loss and may modify what it sends in future packets to avoid subsequent packet discards.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejT. Herbertddatej÷rhfilenamegrfc8883eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130687hkeywordsÉqextension Headerssdestination OptionsrHop-by-Hop OptionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_m$sgranking"≥crfcd8883irfcNumber"≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=ICMPv6 Errors for Discarding Packets Due to Processing LimitsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫DNS-SD (DNS-based Service Discovery) normally discloses information about devices offering and requesting services.  This information includes hostnames, network parameters, and possibly a further description of the corresponding service instance.  Especially when mobile devices engage in DNS-based Service Discovery at a public hotspot, serious privacy problems arise.  We analyze the requirements of a privacy-respecting discovery service.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtPrivate Octopus Inc.dnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationxUniversity of LuxembourgdnameiD. Kaiserddatej‘·hfilenamegrfc8882eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednssddfullx5dnssd - Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydnamex-Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-130686hkeywordsÇmMulticast DNSdmDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Zfôgranking"≤crfcd8882irfcNumber"≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFDNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD) Privacy and Security RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This document describes the Network File System (NFS) version 4 minor version 1, including features retained from the base protocol (NFS version 4 minor version 0, which is specified in RFC 7530) and protocol extensions made subsequently. The later minor version has no dependencies on NFS version 4 minor version 0, and is considered a separate protocol.

This document obsoletes RFC 5661. It substantially revises the treatment of features relating to multi-server namespace, superseding the description of those features appearing in RFC 5661.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiD. Noveck¢kaffiliationfORACLEdnamehC. Leverddatej–hfilenamegrfc8881eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130685hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDate_;`ggranking"±crfcd8881irfcNumber"±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Network File System (NFS) Version 4 Minor Version 1 ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´NAT64 (Network Address and Protocol Translation from IPv6 Clients to IPv4 Servers) allows client devices using IPv6 to communicate with servers that have only IPv4 connectivity.

The specification for how a client discovers its local network's NAT64 prefix (RFC 7050) defines the special name 'ipv4only.arpa' for this purpose. However, in its Domain Name Reservation Considerations section (Section 8.1), that specification (RFC 7050) indicates that the name actually has no particularly special properties that would require special handling.

Consequently, despite the well-articulated special purpose of the name, 'ipv4only.arpa' was not recorded in the Special-Use Domain Names registry as a name with special properties.

This document updates RFC 7050. It describes the special treatment required and formally declares the special properties of the name. It also adds similar declarations for the corresponding reverse mapping names.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinaziddatej÷qhfilenamegrfc8880eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130684hkeywordsÉdIPv6eNAT64eDNS64kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_Mz§granking"∞crfcd8880irfcNumber"∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Special Use Domain Name 'ipv4only.arpa'dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx·In TLS handshakes, certificate chains often take up the majority of the bytes transmitted.

This document describes how certificate chains can be compressed to reduce the amount of data transmitted and avoid some round trips.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpCloudflare, Inc.dnamejA. Ghedini¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekV. Vasilievddatej÷ihfilenamegrfc8879eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130683hkeywordsÉdzlibfbrotlidzstdkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_«Cjgranking"Øcrfcd8879irfcNumber"ØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTLS Certificate CompressiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZstandard, or "zstd" (pronounced "zee standard"), is a lossless data compression mechanism. This document describes the mechanism and registers a media type, content encoding, and a structured syntax suffix to be used when transporting zstd-compressed content via MIME.

Despite use of the word "standard" as part of Zstandard, readers are advised that this document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is being published for informational purposes only.

This document replaces and obsoletes RFC 8478.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnameiY. Collet¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej÷mhfilenamegrfc8878eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130682hkeywordsÅkcompressionkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDate`#Å%granking"Æcrfcd8878irfcNumber"ÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Zstandard Compression and the 'application/zstd' Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9659∂habstractyÏVarious network protocols make use of binary-encoded timestamps that are incorporated in the protocol packet format, referred to as "packet timestamps" for short.  This document specifies guidelines for defining packet timestamp formats in networking protocols at various layers.  It also presents three recommended timestamp formats.  The target audience of this document includes network protocol designers.  It is expected that a new network protocol that requires a packet timestamp will, in most cases, use one of the recommended timestamp formats.  If none of the recommended formats fits the protocol requirements, the new protocol specification should specify the format of the packet timestamp according to the guidelines in this document.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationgTU WiendnameiJ. Fabini¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej÷ohfilenamegrfc8877eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130681hkeywordsÅjTimestampskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_kñ•granking"≠crfcd8877irfcNumber"≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Guidelines for Defining Packet TimestampsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGUse of the Internet for emergency calling is described in RFC 6443, 'Framework for Emergency Calling Using Internet Multimedia'.  In some cases of emergency calls, the transmission of application data is all that is needed, and no interactive media channel is established: a situation referred to as 'non-interactive emergency calls', where, unlike most emergency calls, there is no two-way interactive media such as voice or video or text.  This document describes use of a SIP MESSAGE transaction that includes a container for the data based on the Common Alerting Protocol (CAP).  That type of emergency request does not establish a session, distinguishing it from SIP INVITE, which does.  Any device that needs to initiate a request for emergency services without an interactive media channel would use the mechanisms in this document.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Rosen¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationxCore Technology ConsultingdnamejR. Gellensddatej÷nhfilenamegrfc8876eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130680hkeywordsÉcCAPxCommon Alerting ProtocolxNon-Interactive Emergency callskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_j9Ìgranking"¨crfcd8876irfcNumber"¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNon-interactive Emergency CallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;The use of GitHub in IETF working group processes is increasing.  This document describes uses and conventions for working groups that are considering starting to use GitHub.  It does not mandate any processes and does not require changes to the processes used by current and future working groups not using GitHub.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Cooper¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej–"hfilenamegrfc8875eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgitdfullx$git - GitHub Integration and ToolingdnamexGitHub Integration and Toolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130679hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_HYÑgranking"´crfcd8875irfcNumber"´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Working Group GitHub AdministrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxgThis document provides a set of guidelines for working groups that choose to use GitHub for their work.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Starkddatej÷rhfilenamegrfc8874eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgitdfullx$git - GitHub Integration and ToolingdnamexGitHub Integration and Toolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130678hkeywordsÖcgitoversion controlmworking grouphdocumentgeditingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_HYbgranking"™crfcd8874irfcNumber"™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Working Group GitHub Usage GuidancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document specifies how a Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC) data channel can be used as a transport mechanism for the Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) and how the Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer/answer mechanism can be used to negotiate such a data channel, referred to as an MSRP data channel.  Two network configurations are supported: the connection of two MSRP data channel endpoints; and a gateway configuration, which connects an MSRP data channel endpoint with an MSRP endpoint that uses either TCP or TLS.  This document updates RFC 4975.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiJM. Recio¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej÷Ähfilenamegrfc8873eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130677hkeywordsÅfwebrtckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,›granking"©crfcd8873irfcNumber"©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) over Data ChannelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is a flexible protocol that can be used in a wide range of applications, networks, and system topologies.  That flexibility makes for wide applicability but can complicate the application design process.  One particular design question that has received much attention is how to support multiple media streams in RTP.  This memo discusses the available options and design trade-offs, and provides guidelines on how to use the multiplexing features of RTP to support multiple media streams.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Burman¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrand¢kaffiliation`dnamegR. Evenddatej÷zhfilenamegrfc8872eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130676hkeywordsÅiSimulcastkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDate`,‹granking"®crfcd8872irfcNumber"®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWGuidelines for Using the Multiplexing Features of RTP to Support Multiple Media StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document describes a solution framework for ensuring that media confidentiality and integrity are maintained end to end within the context of a switched conferencing environment where Media Distributors are not trusted with the end-to-end media encryption keys.  The solution builds upon existing security mechanisms defined for the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP).fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiD. Benham¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiC. Grovesddatej÷Rhfilenamegrfc8871eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpercdfullx(perc - Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdnamex!Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130675hkeywordsÉdPERCwPrivate Media FrameworklconferencingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,‹granking"ßcrfcd8871irfcNumber"ßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRA Solution Framework for Private Media in Privacy-Enhanced RTP Conferencing (PERC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyEncrypted Key Transport (EKT) is an extension to DTLS (Datagram Transport Layer Security) and the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) that provides for the secure transport of SRTP master keys, rollover counters, and other information within SRTP.  This facility enables SRTP for decentralized conferences by distributing a common key to all of the conference endpoints.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamekJ. Mattsson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationfCitrixdnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej÷mhfilenamegrfc8870eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpercdfullx(perc - Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdnamex!Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130674hkeywordsÖdPERCdSRTPcRTPlconferencingjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,€granking"¶crfcd8870irfcNumber"¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Encrypted Key Transport for DTLS and Secure RTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is a common transport choice for interactive multimedia communication applications.  The performance of these applications typically depends on a well-functioning congestion control algorithm.  To ensure a seamless and robust user experience, a well-designed RTP-based congestion control algorithm should work well across all access network types.  This document describes test cases for evaluating performances of candidate congestion control algorithms over cellular and Wi-Fi networks.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnameiZ. Sarker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefX. Zhu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameeJ. Fuddatej÷ohfilenamegrfc8869eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130673hkeywordsÑpCellular NetworkmWi-Fi NetworkrCongestion ControlcRTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,⁄granking"•crfcd8869irfcNumber"•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLEvaluation Test Cases for Interactive Real-Time Media over Wireless NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯The Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) is used to transmit media in telephony and video conferencing applications.  This document describes the guidelines to evaluate new congestion control algorithms for interactive point-to-point real-time media.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlcallstats.iodnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationxTechnical University of MunichdnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Holmerddatej÷thfilenamegrfc8868eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130672hkeywordsÉcRTPdRTCPrCongestion ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`xgranking"§crfcd8868irfcNumber"§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Evaluating Congestion Control for Interactive Real-Time MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThe Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is used to transmit media in multimedia telephony applications.  These applications are typically required to implement congestion control.  This document describes the test cases to be used in the performance evaluation of such congestion control algorithms in a controlled environment.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnameiZ. Sarker¢kaffiliationlcallstats.iodnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefX. Zhu¢kaffiliationmAcousticCommsdnamejM. Ramalhoddatej÷Phfilenamegrfc8867eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130671hkeywordsÉjMultimediajTest casesrCongestion ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`xgranking"£crfcd8867irfcNumber"£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLTest Cases for Evaluating Congestion Control for Interactive Real-Time MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This memo defines the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  SDP is intended for describing multimedia sessions for the purposes of session announcement, session invitation, and other forms of multimedia session initiation.  This document obsoletes RFC 4566.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNetworked MediadnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Kyzivat¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handleyddatej÷Yhfilenamegrfc8866eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130670hkeywordsãjMultimedialconferencingmsession setupisignalingemediacSIPdRTSPdvoipeaudioevideoistreamingkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDate`xgranking"¢crfcd8866irfcNumber"¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!SDP: Session Description ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—This document specifies how a Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC) data channel can be used as a transport mechanism for real-time text using the ITU-T Protocol for multimedia application text conversation (Recommendation ITU-T T.140) and how the Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer/answer mechanism can be used to negotiate such a data channel, referred to as a T.140 data channel.  This document updates RFC 8373 to specify its use with WebRTC data channels.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationx*Gunnar Hellstr√∂m Accessible CommunicationdnamemG. Hellstr√∂mddatej÷vhfilenamegrfc8865eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130669hkeywordsÜcSDPeITU-TeT.140lData ChannelfWebRTCnreal-time textkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`xgranking"°crfcd8865irfcNumber"°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;T.140 Real-Time Text Conversation over WebRTC Data ChannelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?Data channel setup can be done using either the in-band Data Channel Establishment Protocol (DCEP) or some out-of-band non-DCEP protocol.  This document specifies how the SDP (Session Description Protocol) offer/answer exchange can be used to achieve an out-of-band non-DCEP negotiation for establishing a data channel.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehK. Drage¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekM. Makaraju¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehR. Ejzak¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiJ. Marcon¢kaffiliation`dnamegR. EvenddatejœÁhfilenamegrfc8864eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130668hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`xgranking"†crfcd8864irfcNumber"†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFNegotiation Data Channels Using the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykDuring the process of establishing peer-to-peer connectivity, Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) agents can encounter situations where they have no candidate pairs to check, and, as a result, conclude that ICE processing has failed. However, because additional candidate pairs can be discovered during ICE processing, declaring failure at this point may be premature. This document discusses when these situations can occur.

This document updates RFCs 8445 and 8838 by requiring that an ICE agent wait a minimum amount of time before declaring ICE failure, even if there are no candidate pairs left to check.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Ubertiddatej÷whfilenamegrfc8863eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcicedfullx,ice - Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdnamex&Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130667hkeywordsÉcICEcPACiCandidatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,⁄granking"ücrfcd8863irfcNumber"üestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPInteractive Connectivity Establishment Patiently Awaiting Connectivity (ICE PAC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyXAlthough the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) includes a suite of security services that has been expanded by numerous specifications over the years, there is no single place that explains how to use SIP to establish confidential media sessions.  Additionally, existing mechanisms have some feature gaps that need to be identified and resolved in order for them to address the pervasive monitoring threat model.  This specification describes best practices for negotiating confidential media with SIP, including a comprehensive protection solution that binds the media layer to SIP layer identities.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷Nhfilenamegrfc8862eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymisipbrandydfullxPsipbrandy - SIP Best-practice Recommendations Against Network Dangers to privacYdnamexDSIP Best-practice Recommendations Against Network Dangers to privacYdtypebwgbidjdoc-130666hkeywordsÉcSIPcRTPhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,Ÿgranking"ûcrfcd8862irfcNumber"ûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‰etotaletitlex7Best Practices for Securing RTP Media Signaled with SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^RTP allows multiple RTP streams to be sent in a single session but requires each Synchronization Source (SSRC) to send RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) reception quality reports for every other SSRC visible in the session.  This causes the number of RTCP reception reports to grow with the number of SSRCs, rather than the number of endpoints.  In many cases, most of these RTCP reception reports are unnecessary, since all SSRCs of an endpoint are normally co-located and see the same reception quality.  This memo defines a Reporting Group extension to RTCP to reduce the reporting overhead in such scenarios.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationk8x8 / JitsidnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej’¬hfilenamegrfc8861eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130665hkeywordsÖdRGRPdSDESbXRiReportingeGroupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,ÿgranking"ùcrfcd8861irfcNumber"ùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇSending Multiple RTP Streams in a Single RTP Session: Grouping RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Reception Statistics and Other FeedbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document specifies how an RTP session can contain RTP streams with media from multiple media types such as audio, video, and text.  This has been restricted by the RTP specifications (RFCs 3550 and 3551), and thus this document updates RFCs 3550 and 3551 to enable this behaviour for applications that satisfy the applicability for using multiple media types in a single RTP session.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationk8x8 / JitsidnameiJ. Lennoxddatej’øhfilenamegrfc8860eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130664hkeywordsÉiReal-timelMultiplexingfBundlekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`,◊granking"úcrfcd8860irfcNumber"úestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Sending Multiple Types of Media in a Single RTP SessiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyéThe purpose of this specification is to provide a framework for analyzing the multiplexing characteristics of Session Description Protocol (SDP) attributes when SDP is used to negotiate the usage of a single 5-tuple for sending and receiving media associated with multiple media descriptions.

This specification also categorizes the existing SDP attributes based on the framework described herein.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. NandakumarddatejœHhfilenamegrfc8859eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130663hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDate`,‘granking"õcrfcd8859irfcNumber"õestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOA Framework for Session Description Protocol (SDP) Attributes When MultiplexingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document defines a new Session Description Protocol (SDP) media-level attribute, 'rtcp-mux-only', that can be used by an endpoint to indicate exclusive support of RTP and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) multiplexing.  The document also updates RFC 5761 by clarifying that an offerer can use a mechanism to indicate that it is not able to send and receive RTCP on separate ports.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej’›hfilenamegrfc8858eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130662hkeywordsäcRTPdRTCPcSDPeOFFERfANSWERcMUXiMULTIPLEXfRTCWEBfWebRTCdJSEPkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate`,Œgranking"öcrfcd8858irfcNumber"öestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Indicating Exclusive Support of RTP and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Multiplexing Using the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe WebSocket protocol enables two-way real-time communication between clients and servers.  This document specifies the use of Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP) as a new WebSocket subprotocol enabling a reliable transport mechanism between BFCP entities in new scenarios.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejV. Pascual¢kaffiliationfQuobisdnameiA. Rom√°n¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejS. Cazeaux¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoR. Ravindranathddatej’˚hfilenamegrfc8857eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgbfcpbisdfullx+bfcpbis - Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdnamex!Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdtypebwgbidjdoc-130661hkeywordsÇdBFCPiWebSocketkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`c¯granking"ôcrfcd8857irfcNumber"ôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRThe WebSocket Protocol as a Transport for the Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∆This document defines the Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer/answer procedures for negotiating and establishing Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP) streams.

This document obsoletes RFC 4583.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationfJotrondnamemT. Kristensen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej”ôhfilenamegrfc8856eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgbfcpbisdfullx+bfcpbis - Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdnamex!Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdtypebwgbidjdoc-130660hkeywordsÉmfloor controldBFCPcSDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`c˜granking"òcrfcd8856irfcNumber"òestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZSession Description Protocol (SDP) Format for Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP) StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)Floor control is a means to manage joint or exclusive access to shared resources in a (multiparty) conferencing environment. Thereby, floor control complements other functions -- such as conference and media session setup, conference policy manipulation, and media control -- that are realized by other protocols.

This document specifies the Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP). BFCP is used between floor participants and floor control servers, and between floor chairs (i.e., moderators) and floor control servers.

This document obsoletes RFC 4582.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliation`dnamehK. Drage¢kaffiliationfJotrondnamemT. Kristensen¢kaffiliationxTechnical University MunichdnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehC. Eckelddatej’ªhfilenamegrfc8855eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgbfcpbisdfullx+bfcpbis - Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdnamex!Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdtypebwgbidjdoc-130659hkeywordsÇmfloor controljconferencekobsoletedByÄepagesWopublicationDate`cˆgranking"ócrfcd8855irfcNumber"óestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(The Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx|This document provides information and requirements for the use of Forward Error Correction (FEC) by WebRTC implementations.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Ubertiddatej÷Xhfilenamegrfc8854eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130658hkeywordsÇcRTPcFECkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate`cıgranking"ñcrfcd8854irfcNumber"ñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,WebRTC Forward Error Correction RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢In some application scenarios, it may be desirable to send multiple differently encoded versions of the same media source in different RTP streams.  This is called simulcast.  This document describes how to accomplish simulcast in RTP and how to signal it in the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  The described solution uses an RTP/RTCP identification method to identify RTP streams belonging to the same media source and makes an extension to SDP to indicate that those RTP streams are different simulcast formats of that media source.  The SDP extension consists of a new media-level SDP attribute that expresses capability to send and/or receive simulcast RTP streams.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Burman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Nandakumar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiM. Zanatyddatejœπhfilenamegrfc8853eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130657hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`cÙgranking"ïcrfcd8853irfcNumber"ïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFUsing Simulcast in Session Description Protocol (SDP) and RTP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document defines and registers two new Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP) Stream Identifier Source Description (SDES) items.  One, named RtpStreamId, is used for unique identification of RTP streams.  The other, RepairedRtpStreamId, can be used to identify which stream is to be repaired using a redundancy RTP stream.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejA.B. Roach¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Nandakumar¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekP. ThatcherddatejœRhfilenamegrfc8852eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130656hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`cÙgranking"îcrfcd8852irfcNumber"îestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/RTP Stream Identifier Source Description (SDES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?In this specification, we define a framework for specifying restrictions on RTP streams in the Session Description Protocol (SDP). This framework defines a new "rid" ("restriction identifier") SDP attribute to unambiguously identify the RTP streams within an RTP session and restrict the streams' payload format parameters in a codec-agnostic way beyond what is provided with the regular payload types.

This specification updates RFC 4855 to give additional guidance on choice of Format Parameter (fmtp) names and their relation to the restrictions defined by this document.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejA.B. Roachddatejœ≠hfilenamegrfc8851eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130655hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`cÌgranking"ìcrfcd8851irfcNumber"ìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Payload Format RestrictionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÙThis document defines how to use the WebRTC data channel mechanism to realize a data channel, referred to as a Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE) data channel, for transporting CLUE protocol messages between two CLUE entities.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej÷Jhfilenamegrfc8850eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-130654hkeywordsçcSIPcSDPdDTLSdSCTPdDATAgCHANNELdDCEPqDATA_CHANNEL_OPENpDATA_CHANNEL_ACKdPPIDlTELEPRESENCEfRTCWEBfWEBRTCkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate`˛fgranking"ícrfcd8850irfcNumber"íestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJControlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE) Protocol Data ChanneldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis document describes how the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is used in the context of the Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE) protocol.  It also describes the mechanisms and recommended practice for mapping RTP media streams, as defined in the Session Description Protocol (SDP), to CLUE Media Captures and defines a new RTP header extension (CaptureID).fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationk8x8 / JitsidnameiJ. Lennoxddatejœxhfilenamegrfc8849eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-130653hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˛egranking"ëcrfcd8849irfcNumber"ëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZMapping RTP Streams to Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE) Media CapturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document is about Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE) signaling.  It specifies how the CLUE protocol and the CLUE data channel are used in conjunction with each other and with existing signaling mechanisms, such as SIP and the Session Description Protocol (SDP), to produce a telepresence call.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Hanton¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Kyzivat¢kaffiliationxBeijing ChuangshiyouliandnamegL. Xiao¢kaffiliation`dnameiC. Grovesddatej–hfilenamegrfc8848eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-130652hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˛dgranking"êcrfcd8848irfcNumber"êestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSession Signaling for Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThe Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE) protocol is an application protocol conceived for the description and negotiation of a telepresence session.  The design of the CLUE protocol takes into account the requirements and the framework defined within the IETF CLUE Working Group.  A companion document, RFC 8848, delves into CLUE signaling details as well as the SIP / Session Description Protocol (SDP) session establishment phase.  CLUE messages flow over the CLUE data channel, based on reliable and ordered SCTP-over-DTLS transport. ("SCTP" stands for "Stream Control Transmission Protocol".) Message details, together with the behavior of CLUE Participants acting as Media Providers and/or Media Consumers, are herein discussed.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnameiR. Presta¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnamekS P. Romanoddatej÷ehfilenamegrfc8847eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-130651hkeywordsÉlTelepresencehProtocoliFrameworkkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDate`˛dgranking"ècrfcd8847irfcNumber"èestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAProtocol for Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis document provides an XML schema file for the definition of CLUE data model types.  The term "CLUE" stands for "Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence" and is the name of the IETF working group in which this document, as well as other companion documents, has been developed.  The document defines a coherent structure for information associated with the description of a telepresence scenario.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnameiR. Presta¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnamekS P. Romanoddatej’ﬁhfilenamegrfc8846eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-130650hkeywordsÑdCLUElTelepresencejData ModeliFrameworkkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDate`˛cgranking"écrfcd8846irfcNumber"éestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUAn XML Schema for the Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE) Data ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’This document defines a framework for a protocol to enable devices in a telepresence conference to interoperate.  The protocol enables communication of information about multiple media streams so a sending system and receiving system can make reasonable decisions about transmitting, selecting, and rendering the media streams.  This protocol is used in addition to SIP signaling and Session Description Protocol (SDP) negotiation for setting up a telepresence session.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Duckworth¢kaffiliationeAcanodnamelA. Pepperell¢kaffiliationgTencentdnameiS. Wengerddatej’¿hfilenamegrfc8845eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-130649hkeywordsÑlTelepresencelConferencingrVideo-ConferencingcMCUkobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDate`˛bgranking"çcrfcd8845irfcNumber"çestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Framework for Telepresence Multi-StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes unknown key-share attacks on the use of Datagram Transport Layer Security for the Secure Real-Time Transport Protocol (DTLS-SRTP).  Similar attacks are described on the use of DTLS-SRTP with the identity bindings used in Web Real-Time Communications (WebRTC) and SIP identity.  These attacks are difficult to mount, but they cause a victim to be misled about the identity of a communicating peer.  This document defines mitigation techniques that implementations of RFC 8122 are encouraged to deploy.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorladdatej÷Uhfilenamegrfc8844eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130648hkeywordsÖxUnknown Key-Share AttackcSDPiDTLS-SRTPfWebRTClSIP identitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˛agranking"åcrfcd8844irfcNumber"åestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTUnknown Key-Share Attacks on Uses of TLS with the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°This specification defines a new Session Description Protocol (SDP) Grouping Framework extension called 'BUNDLE'. The extension can be used with the SDP offer/answer mechanism to negotiate the usage of a single transport (5-tuple) for sending and receiving media described by multiple SDP media descriptions ("m=" sections). Such transport is referred to as a BUNDLE transport, and the media is referred to as bundled media. The "m=" sections that use the BUNDLE transport form a BUNDLE group.

This specification defines a new RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Source Description (SDES) item and a new RTP header extension.

This specification updates RFCs 3264, 5888, and 7941.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrand¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jenningsddatej÷'hfilenamegrfc8843eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130647hkeywordsäcRTPcSDPfBundlelMultiplexingfRTCWEBdCLUEfMMUSICcAVTcWEBgBrowserkobsoletedByÅd9143epages2opublicationDate`Àögranking"ãcrfcd8843irfcNumber"ãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKNegotiating Media Multiplexing Using the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThis document defines the Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer/answer procedures for negotiating and establishing a Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) association. The document also defines the criteria for when a new DTLS association must be established. The document updates RFCs 5763 and 7345 by replacing common SDP offer/answer procedures with a reference to this specification.

This document defines a new SDP media-level attribute, "tls-id".

This document also defines how the "tls-id" attribute can be used for negotiating and establishing a Transport Layer Security (TLS) connection, in conjunction with the procedures in RFCs 4145 and 8122.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationkTurboBridgednamejR. Shpountddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8842eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130646hkeywordsÉcSDPdDTLSftls-idkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˛`granking"äcrfcd8842irfcNumber"äestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexéSession Description Protocol (SDP) Offer/Answer Considerations for Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) and Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9725∂habstracty<The Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) is a transport protocol used to establish associations between two endpoints. RFC 8261 specifies how SCTP can be used on top of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol, which is referred to as SCTP-over-DTLS.

This specification defines the following new Session Description Protocol (SDP) protocol identifiers (proto values): "UDP/DTLS/SCTP" and "TCP/DTLS/SCTP". This specification also specifies how to use the new proto values with the SDP offer/answer mechanism for negotiating SCTP-over-DTLS associations.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationkTurboBridgednamejR. Shpount¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loreto¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8841eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130645hkeywordsÉdSCTPcSDPdDTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˛`granking"âcrfcd8841irfcNumber"âestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex¢Session Description Protocol (SDP) Offer/Answer Procedures for Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) over Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<The Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) protocol describes a Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal mechanism for UDP-based multimedia sessions established with the Offer/Answer model. The ICE extension for Incremental Provisioning of Candidates (Trickle ICE) defines a mechanism that allows ICE Agents to shorten session establishment delays by making the candidate gathering and connectivity checking phases of ICE non-blocking and by executing them in parallel.

This document defines usage semantics for Trickle ICE with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP). The document also defines a new SIP Info Package to support this usage together with the corresponding media type. Additionally, a new Session Description Protocol (SDP) "end-of-candidates" attribute and a new SIP option tag "trickle-ice" are defined.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeJitsidnamegE. Ivov¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehT. Stach¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejE. Marocco¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej÷*hfilenamegrfc8840eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130644hkeywordsÖcICEgtricklecSIPcSDPcNATkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate`˛_granking"àcrfcd8840irfcNumber"àestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexëA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Usage for Incremental Provisioning of Candidates for the Interactive Connectivity Establishment (Trickle ICE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9725∂habstractx—This document describes Session Description Protocol (SDP) Offer/Answer procedures for carrying out Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) between the agents.

This document obsoletes RFCs 5245 and 6336.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrImpedance MismatchdnameqM. Petit-Huguenin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Nandakumar¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Ker√§nen¢kaffiliationkTurboBridgednamejR. ShpountddatejœÒhfilenamegrfc8839eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130643hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDate`˛Ygranking"ácrfcd8839irfcNumber"áestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkSession Description Protocol (SDP) Offer/Answer Procedures for Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document describes "Trickle ICE", an extension to the Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) protocol that enables ICE agents to begin connectivity checks while they are still gathering candidates, by incrementally exchanging candidates over time instead of all at once.  This method can considerably accelerate the process of establishing a communication session.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationq8x8, Inc. / JitsidnamegE. Ivov¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamenP. Saint-Andreddatejœ±hfilenamegrfc8838eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcicedfullx,ice - Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdnamex&Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130642hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`˛Xgranking"Ücrfcd8838irfcNumber"ÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqTrickle ICE: Incremental Provisioning of Candidates for the Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8863∂habstracty*Networks can provide different forwarding treatments for individual packets based on Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) values on a per-hop basis.  This document provides the recommended DSCP values for web browsers to use for various classes of Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC) traffic.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamekS. Dhesikan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehD. Drutaddatej’‡hfilenamegrfc8837eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130641hkeywordsÇhDiffservfrtcwebkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate`ógranking"Öcrfcd8837irfcNumber"ÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHDifferentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) Packet Markings for WebRTC QoSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvCongestion control is needed for all data transported across the Internet, in order to promote fair usage and prevent congestion collapse. The requirements for interactive, point-to-point real-time multimedia, which needs low-delay, semi-reliable data delivery, are different from the requirements for bulk transfer like FTP or bursty transfers like web pages. Due to an increasing amount of RTP-based real-time media traffic on the Internet (e.g., with the introduction of the Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC)), it is especially important to ensure that this kind of traffic is congestion controlled.

This document describes a set of requirements that can be used to evaluate other congestion control mechanisms in order to figure out their fitness for this purpose, and in particular to provide a set of possible requirements for a real-time media congestion avoidance technique.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Jesup¢kaffiliation`dnameiZ. Sarkerddatej’Ähfilenamegrfc8836eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130640hkeywordsÑvInteractive multimediafwebrtcsvideo communicationhRTP/RTCPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate`ñgranking"Ñcrfcd8836irfcNumber"ÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Congestion Control Requirements for Interactive Real-Time MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx—This document describes the data transport protocols used by Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC), including the protocols used for interaction with intermediate boxes such as firewalls, relays, and NAT boxes.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. AlvestrandddatejœXhfilenamegrfc8835eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130639hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ïgranking"Écrfcd8835irfcNumber"ÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuTransports for WebRTCdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«The framework for Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC) provides support for direct interactive rich communication using audio, video, text, collaboration, games, etc.  between two peers' web browsers.  This memo describes the media transport aspects of the WebRTC framework.  It specifies how the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is used in the WebRTC context and gives requirements for which RTP features, profiles, and extensions need to be supported.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationxTechnical University MunichdnamefJ. OttddatejŒÛhfilenamegrfc8834eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130638hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate`ìgranking"Çcrfcd8834irfcNumber"ÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Media Transport and Use of RTP in WebRTCdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies two Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) labels for use with Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC).  The "webrtc" label identifies regular WebRTC: a DTLS session that is used to establish keys for the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) or to establish data channels using the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) over DTLS.  The "c-webrtc" label describes the same protocol, but the peers also agree to maintain the confidentiality of the media by not sharing it with other applications.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej’–hfilenamegrfc8833eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130637hkeywordsÉdALPNhProtocoljIdentifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ìgranking"Åcrfcd8833irfcNumber"ÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) for WebRTCdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThe WebRTC framework specifies protocol support for direct interactive rich communication using audio, video, and data between two peers' web browsers.  This document specifies a simple protocol for establishing symmetric data channels between the peers.  It uses a two-way handshake and allows sending of user data without waiting for the handshake to complete.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamehR. Jesup¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loreto¢kaffiliationx M√ºnster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. T√ºxenddatejŒ¡hfilenamegrfc8832eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130636hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ígranking"Äcrfcd8832irfcNumber"ÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*WebRTC Data Channel Establishment ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–The WebRTC framework specifies protocol support for direct, interactive, rich communication using audio, video, and data between two peers' web browsers.  This document specifies the non-media data transport aspects of the WebRTC framework.  It provides an architectural overview of how the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) is used in the WebRTC context as a generic transport service that allows web browsers to exchange generic data from peer to peer.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamehR. Jesup¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loreto¢kaffiliationx M√ºnster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. T√ºxenddatejŒ¡hfilenamegrfc8831eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130635hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ëgranking"crfcd8831irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletWebRTC Data ChannelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document specifies a Session Description Protocol (SDP) grouping mechanism for RTP media streams that can be used to specify relations between media streams.

This mechanism is used to signal the association between the SDP concept of "media description" and the Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC) concept of MediaStream/MediaStreamTrack using SDP signaling.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrandddatej’⁄hfilenamegrfc8830eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-130634hkeywordsÅpMediaStreamTrackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`êgranking"~crfcd8830irfcNumber"~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEWebRTC MediaStream Identification in the Session Description ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document describes the mechanisms for allowing a JavaScript application to control the signaling plane of a multimedia session via the interface specified in the W3C RTCPeerConnection API and discusses how this relates to existing signaling protocols.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorladdatej’Íhfilenamegrfc8829eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130633hkeywordsäfwebrtccsdpknegotiationisignalingnpeerconnectioncapicicecrtpeofferfanswerkobsoletedByÅd9429epages_opublicationDate`Àìgranking"}crfcd8829irfcNumber"}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0JavaScript Session Establishment Protocol (JSEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxìThis document provides information and requirements for how IP addresses should be handled by Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC) implementations.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliation`dnamehG. Shiehddatej÷Whfilenamegrfc8828eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130632hkeywordsàfWebRTCgprivacygprivatejIP addressgroutingeproxydpeerdmodekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate`ägranking"|crfcd8828irfcNumber"|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'WebRTC IP Address Handling RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx√This document defines the security architecture for WebRTC, a protocol suite intended for use with real-time applications that can be deployed in browsers -- "real-time communication on the Web".fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorladdatejœ˘hfilenamegrfc8827eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130631hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate` |granking"{crfcd8827irfcNumber"{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWebRTC Security ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÚWebRTC is a protocol suite for use with real-time applications that can be deployed in browsers -- "real-time communication on the Web".  This document defines the WebRTC threat model and analyzes the security threats of WebRTC in that model.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. RescorladdatejœÛhfilenamegrfc8826eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130630hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`␀9granking"zcrfcd8826irfcNumber"zestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Security Considerations for WebRTCdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This document gives an overview and context of a protocol suite intended for use with real-time applications that can be deployed in browsers -- "real-time communication on the Web".

It intends to serve as a starting and coordination point to make sure that (1) all the parts that are needed to achieve this goal are findable and (2) the parts that belong in the Internet protocol suite are fully specified and on the right publication track.

This document is an applicability statement -- it does not itself specify any protocol, but it specifies which other specifications implementations are supposed to follow to be compliant with Web Real-Time Communication (WebRTC).fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrandddatejœ•hfilenamegrfc8825eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130629hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`␀4granking"ycrfcd8825irfcNumber"yestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Overview: Real-Time Protocols for Browser-Based ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document defines how to compress Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) headers using the Static Context Header Compression and fragmentation (SCHC) framework.  SCHC defines a header compression mechanism adapted for Constrained Devices.  SCHC uses a static description of the header to reduce the header's redundancy and size.  While RFC 8724 describes the SCHC compression and fragmentation framework, and its application for IPv6/UDP headers, this document applies SCHC to CoAP headers.  The CoAP header structure differs from IPv6 and UDP, since CoAP uses a flexible header with a variable number of options, themselves of variable length.  The CoAP message format is asymmetric: the request messages have a header format different from the format in the response messages.  This specification gives guidance on applying SCHC to flexible headers and how to leverage the asymmetry for more efficient compression Rules.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfAckliodnamekA. Minaburo¢kaffiliationnIMT AtlantiquednamejL. Toutain¢kaffiliationxUniversidad de Buenos AiresdnamelR. Andreasenddatej÷öhfilenamegrfc8824eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelpwandfullx.lpwan - IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdnamex&IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130628hkeywordsärheader compressionmfragmentationcIoTtconstrained networkseLPWANnsensor networkpconstrained nodewwireless sensor networkdcorefOSCOREkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`‹granking"xcrfcd8824irfcNumber"xestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXStatic Context Header Compression (SCHC) for the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx√This document specifies identifiers and challenges required to enable the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) to issue certificates for use by email users that want to use S/MIME.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej÷ïhfilenamegrfc8823eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130627hkeywordsÇdACMEfS/MIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ÄeÂgranking"wcrfcd8823irfcNumber"westateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Extensions to Automatic Certificate Management Environment for End-User S/MIME CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖAs part of providing wireline access to the 5G Core (5GC), deployed wireline networks carry user data between 5G residential gateways and the 5G Access Gateway Function (AGF).  The encapsulation method specified in this document supports the multiplexing of traffic for multiple PDU sessions within a VLAN-delineated access circuit, permits legacy equipment in the data path to inspect certain packet fields, carries 5G QoS information associated with the packet data, and provides efficient encoding.  It achieves this by specific points of similarity with the Point-to-Point Protocol over Ethernet (PPPoE) data packet encapsulation (RFC 2516).fadNamejErik Klinedarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehD. Allan¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamekD. Eastlake¢kaffiliationsTelstra CorporationdnamejD. Woolleyddatej÷óhfilenamegrfc8822eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130626hkeywordsÖePPPoEeW-AGFcQFIcRQIcWWCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`{;›granking"vcrfcd8822irfcNumber"vestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?5G Wireless Wireline Convergence User Plane Encapsulation (5WE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThis document defines an architecture for providing traffic engineering in a native IP network using multiple BGP sessions and a Path Computation Element (PCE)-based central control mechanism.  It defines the Centralized Control Dynamic Routing (CCDR) procedures and identifies needed extensions for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegA. Wang¢kaffiliationjYandex LLCdnamekB. Khasanov¢kaffiliationpEtheric NetworksdnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamegH. Chenddatej÷ïhfilenamegrfc8821eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130625hkeywordsÇx#Centralized Control Dynamic RoutingdCCDRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`kıgranking"ucrfcd8821irfcNumber"uestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8PCE-Based Traffic Engineering (TE) in Native IP NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyfSection 1.1.1 of RFC 3986 defines URI syntax as "a federated and extensible naming system wherein each scheme's specification may further restrict the syntax and semantics of identifiers using that scheme." In other words, the structure of a URI is defined by its scheme. While it is common for schemes to further delegate their substructure to the URI's owner, publishing independent standards that mandate particular forms of substructure in URIs is often problematic.

This document provides guidance on the specification of URI substructure in standards.

This document obsoletes RFC 7320 and updates RFC 3986.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷khfilenamegrfc8820eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130624hkeywordsÅmURI structurekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^˚µgranking"tcrfcd8820irfcNumber"testateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberæetotaletitlexURI Design and OwnershipdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThis document provides for the association of tags with YANG modules.  The expectation is for such tags to be used to help classify and organize modules.  A method for defining, reading, and writing modules tags is provided.  Tags may be registered and assigned during module definition, assigned by implementations, or dynamically defined and set by users.  This document also provides guidance to future model writers; as such, this document updates RFC 8407.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehC. Hopps¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamemD. Bogdanovicddatej÷ohfilenamegrfc8819eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130623hkeywordsÇdYANGdtagskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ÛüUgranking"scrfcd8819irfcNumber"sestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlepYANG Module TagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This document defines distributed mobility anchoring in terms of the different configurations and functions to provide IP mobility support.  A network may be configured with distributed mobility anchoring functions for both network-based or host-based mobility support, depending on the network's needs.  In a distributed mobility anchoring environment, multiple anchors are available for mid-session switching of an IP prefix anchor.  To start a new flow or to handle a flow not requiring IP session continuity as a mobile node moves to a new network, the flow can be started or restarted using an IP address configured from the new IP prefix anchored to the new network.  If the flow needs to survive the change of network, there are solutions that can be used to enable IP address mobility.  This document describes different anchoring approaches, depending on the IP mobility needs, and how this IP address mobility is handled by the network.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdCIHEdnamegH. Chan¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefX. Wei¢kaffiliationqSejong UniversitydnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationwSungkyunkwan UniversitydnamegS. Jeon¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej÷phfilenamegrfc8818eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130622hkeywordsÜfanchorraddress continuitylreachabilityjcontinuityfPMIPv6eMIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_çÌºgranking"rcrfcd8818irfcNumber"restateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDistributed Mobility AnchoringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document describes the RTP payload format for the Tactical Secure Voice Cryptographic Interoperability Specification (TSVCIS) speech coder.  TSVCIS is a scalable narrowband voice coder supporting varying encoder data rates and fallbacks.  It is implemented as an augmentation to the Mixed Excitation Linear Prediction Enhanced (MELPe) speech coder by conveying additional speech coder parameters to enhance voice quality.  TSVCIS augmented speech data is processed in conjunction with its temporally matched Mixed Excitation Linear Prediction (MELP) 2400 speech data.  The RTP packetization of TSVCIS and MELPe speech coder data is described in detail.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxVOCAL Technologies, Ltd.dnamemV. Demjanenko¢kaffiliationxVOCAL Technologies, Ltd.dnameiJ. Punaro¢kaffiliationxVOCAL Technologies, Ltd.dnamelD. Satterleeddatej÷uhfilenamegrfc8817eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130621hkeywordsådMELPeMELPefTSVCISfNRLVDRxNaval Research LaboratorycNRLdNATOeTSVWGuDepartment of DefensecDoDcNSAgMIL-STDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_;•granking"qcrfcd8817irfcNumber"qestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhRTP Payload Format for Tactical Secure Voice Cryptographic Interoperability Specification (TSVCIS) CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)The Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT) format defines a token that can be carried by signaling protocols, including SIP, to cryptographically attest the identity of callers.  However, not all telephone calls use Internet signaling protocols, and some calls use them for only part of their signaling path, while some cannot reliably deliver SIP header fields end-to-end.  This document describes use cases that require the delivery of PASSporT objects outside of the signaling path, and defines architectures and semantics to provide this functionality.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Petersonddatej÷nhfilenamegrfc8816eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130620hkeywordsÅcSIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`%äígranking"pcrfcd8816irfcNumber"pestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQSecure Telephone Identity Revisited (STIR) Out-of-Band Architecture and Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty˘This document recommends deprecation of the use of Any-Source Multicast (ASM) for interdomain multicast.  It recommends the use of Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) for interdomain multicast applications and recommends that hosts and routers in these deployments fully support SSM.  The recommendations in this document do not preclude the continued use of ASM within a single organization or domain and are especially easy to adopt in existing deployments of intradomain ASM using PIM Sparse Mode (PIM-SM).fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM. Abrahamsson¢kaffiliationdJiscdnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamekL. Giuliano¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies Inc.dnameiT. Eckertddatej÷ohfilenamegrfc8815eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130619hkeywordsãcASMiDeprecatekDeprecationkInterdomainkIntradomainfPIM-SMgPIM-SSMcSSMdMSDPeMBONEiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_HIˆgranking"ocrfcd8815irfcNumber"oestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÂetotaletitlex@Deprecating Any-Source Multicast (ASM) for Interdomain MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis document defines a way for a Border Gateway Protocol - Link
State (BGP-LS) speaker to advertise multiple types of supported
Maximum SID Depths (MSDs) at node and/or link granularity.

Such advertisements allow entities (e.g., centralized controllers) to
determine whether a particular Segment Identifier (SID) stack can be
supported in a given network.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamekU. Chunduri¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationsAmazon Web ServicesdnamepN. Triantafillisddatej÷yhfilenamegrfc8814eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130618hkeywordsÑfBGP-LScSIDcMSDbSRkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate_4»qgranking"ncrfcd8814irfcNumber"nestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPSignaling Maximum SID Depth (MSD) Using the Border Gateway Protocol - Link StatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≥This document updates RFC 5480 to specify semantics for the keyEncipherment and dataEncipherment key usage bits when used in certificates that support Elliptic Curve Cryptography.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnSECOM CO. LTD.dnamefT. Ito¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷xhfilenamegrfc8813eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130617hkeywordsÉdPKIXeX.509cECCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_4»\granking"mcrfcd8813irfcNumber"mestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMClarifications for Elliptic Curve Cryptography Subject Public Key InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrThe W3C Web Authentication (WebAuthn) specification and the FIDO Alliance FIDO2 Client to Authenticator Protocol (CTAP) specification use CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) algorithm identifiers.  This specification registers the following algorithms (which are used by WebAuthn and CTAP implementations) in the IANA "COSE Algorithms" registry: RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 using SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512, and SHA-1; and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) using the secp256k1 curve and SHA-256.  It registers the secp256k1 elliptic curve in the IANA "COSE Elliptic Curves" registry.  Also, for use with JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE), it registers the algorithm ECDSA using the secp256k1 curve and SHA-256 in the IANA "JSON Web Signature and Encryption Algorithms" registry and the secp256k1 elliptic curve in the IANA "JSON Web Key Elliptic Curve" registry.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej÷zhfilenamegrfc8812eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130616hkeywordsålCryptographyqDigital SignaturejEncryptioncW3CxWorld Wide Web ConsortiumhWebAuthnrWeb AuthenticationmFIDO AlliancedFIDOeFIDO2dCTAPeCTAP2kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate_7 Ôgranking"lcrfcd8812irfcNumber"lestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexíCBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) and JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE) Registrations for Web Authentication (WebAuthn) AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document describes an architecture for establishing and maintaining Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) within and between domains.  The document does not specify protocols or protocol extensions, instead focusing on defining architectural relationships, components, and concepts used in a DOTS deployment.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjForcepointdnamelA. Mortensen¢kaffiliationlMcAfee, Inc.dnamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Andreasen¢kaffiliationmIron MountaindnameiN. Teague¢kaffiliationgCharterdnamejR. Comptonddatej–hfilenamegrfc8811eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130615hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_;f granking"kcrfcd8811irfcNumber"kestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.DDoS Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document updates RFC 5492 by making a change to the registration procedures for BGP Capability Codes.  Specifically, the range formerly designated "Private Use" is divided into three new ranges: "First Come First Served", "Experimental Use", and "Reserved".fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej÷yhfilenamegrfc8810eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130614hkeywordsÅcIDRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_;ùgranking"jcrfcd8810irfcNumber"jestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Revision to Capability Codes Registration ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxîThis specification defines IANA registries for W3C Web Authentication (WebAuthn) attestation statement format identifiers and extension identifiers.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Hodges¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies Inc.dnamejG. Mandyam¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej÷xhfilenamegrfc8809eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130613hkeywordsÑhwebauthnkattestationjextensionshregistrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_-û¶granking"icrfcd8809irfcNumber"iestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Registries for Web Authentication (WebAuthn)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document defines a YANG data model with the "factory-reset" RPC to allow clients to reset a server back to its factory default condition. It also defines an optional "factory-default" datastore to allow clients to read the factory default configuration for the device.

The YANG data model in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) defined in RFC 8342.fadNamemRobert Wiltondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationpEricsson HungarydnamejB. Lengyel¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Niuddatej–%hfilenamegrfc8808eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130612hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate_MuLgranking"hcrfcd8808irfcNumber"hestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A YANG Data Model for Factory Default SettingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) includes a client authentication scheme that is based on a user identifier and password.  The structure of the password field is defined by an XML Schema data type that specifies minimum and maximum password length values, but there are no other provisions for password management other than changing the password.  This document describes an EPP extension that allows longer passwords to be created and adds additional security features to the EPP login command and response.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehM. Pozunddatej–hfilenamegrfc8807eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130611hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_.!ågranking"gcrfcd8807irfcNumber"gestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGLogin Security Extension for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySome DNS recursive resolvers have longer-than-desired round-trip times to the closest DNS root server; those resolvers may have difficulty getting responses from the root servers, such as during a network attack. Some DNS recursive resolver operators want to prevent snooping by third parties of requests sent to DNS root servers. In both cases, resolvers can greatly decrease the round-trip time and prevent observation of requests by serving a copy of the full root zone on the same server, such as on a loopback address or in the resolver software. This document shows how to start and maintain such a copy of the root zone that does not cause problems for other users of the DNS, at the cost of adding some operational fragility for the operator.

This document obsoletes RFC 7706.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej÷ohfilenamegrfc8806eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130610hkeywordsÇcDNSjlocal-rootkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Î’Ÿgranking"fcrfcd8806irfcNumber"festateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Running a Root Server Local to a ResolverdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document records a format whereby a network operator can publish a mapping of IP address prefixes to simplified geolocation information, colloquially termed a "geolocation feed". Interested parties can poll and parse these feeds to update or merge with other geolocation data sources and procedures. This format intentionally only allows specifying coarse-level location.

Some technical organizations operating networks that move from one conference location to the next have already experimentally published small geolocation feeds.

This document describes a currently deployed format. At least one consumer (Google) has incorporated these feeds into a geolocation data pipeline, and a significant number of ISPs are using it to inform them where their prefixes should be geolocated.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhLoon LLCdnamehE. Kline¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiK. Duleba¢kaffiliationwGoogle Switzerland GmbHdnamekZ. Szamonek¢kaffiliationwGoogle Switzerland GmbHdnamehS. Moser¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej÷phfilenamegrfc8805eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130609hkeywordsÉlgeo-locationkgeolocationiaddresseskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_,î>granking"ecrfcd8805irfcNumber"eestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0A Format for Self-Published IP Geolocation FeedsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓOpen Caching architecture is a use case of Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) in which the commercial Content Delivery Network (CDN) is the upstream CDN (uCDN) and the ISP caching layer serves as the downstream CDN (dCDN).  This document defines extensions to the CDNI Metadata Interface (MI) and the Footprint & Capabilities Advertisement interface (FCI).  These extensions are derived from requirements raised by Open Caching but are also applicable to CDNI use cases in general.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeQwiltdnamelO. Finkelman¢kaffiliationgVerizondnameiS. Mishraddatej–hfilenamegrfc8804eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130608hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_kñçgranking"dcrfcd8804irfcNumber"destateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJContent Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Request Routing ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies an application proxy, called Transport Converter, to assist the deployment of TCP extensions such as Multipath TCP. A Transport Converter may provide conversion service for one or more TCP extensions. The conversion service is provided by means of the 0-RTT TCP Convert Protocol (Convert).

This protocol provides 0-RTT (Zero Round-Trip Time) conversion service since no extra delay is induced by the protocol compared to connections that are not proxied. Also, the Convert Protocol does not require any encapsulation (no tunnels whatsoever).

This specification assumes an explicit model, where the Transport Converter is explicitly configured on hosts. As a sample applicability use case, this document specifies how the Convert Protocol applies for Multipath TCP.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhTessaresdnamenO. Bonaventure¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationmKorea TelecomdnamefS. Seo¢kaffiliationhTessaresdnamejB. Hesmansddatej÷thfilenamegrfc8803eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130607hkeywordsàmHybrid accesskaggregationstransport evolutionofuture internetiextensionoTrafic SteeringeATSSSmMultipath TCPkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDate_ ≥Lgranking"ccrfcd8803irfcNumber"cestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0-RTT TCP Convert ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty…This memo describes an application-level protocol for the communication of end-to-end QoS compliance information based on the HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) and the Session Description Protocol (SDP). The Quality for Service (Q4S) protocol provides a mechanism to negotiate and monitor latency, jitter, bandwidth, and packet loss, and to alert whenever one of the negotiated conditions is violated.

Implementation details on the actions to be triggered upon reception/detection of QoS alerts exchanged by the protocol are out of scope of this document; it is either application dependent (e.g., act to increase quality or reduce bit-rate) or network dependent (e.g., change connection's quality profile).

This protocol specification is the product of research conducted over a number of years; it is presented here as a permanent record and to offer a foundation for future similar work. It does not represent a standard protocol and does not have IETF consensus.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ.J. Aranda¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiM. Cortes¢kaffiliationxUniv. Politecnica de MadriddnamemJ. Salvach√∫a¢kaffiliationhTecnaliadnamekM. Narganes¢kaffiliationlOptiva MediadnamevI. Mart√≠nez-Sarrieguiddatej÷Xhfilenamegrfc8802eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130606hkeywordsÜsquality measurementtmeasurement protocolglatencyfjitteribandwidthkpacket-losskobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDate_!-granking"bcrfcd8802irfcNumber"bestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&The Quality for Service (Q4S) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìProvisioning Domains (PvDs) are defined as consistent sets of network configuration information. PvDs allows hosts to manage connections to multiple networks and interfaces simultaneously, such as when a home router provides connectivity through both a broadband and cellular network provider.

This document defines a mechanism for explicitly identifying PvDs through a Router Advertisement (RA) option. This RA option announces a PvD identifier, which hosts can compare to differentiate between PvDs. The option can directly carry some information about a PvD and can optionally point to PvD Additional Information that can be retrieved using HTTP over TLS.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Pfister¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamej√â. Vyncke¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Schinazi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegW. Shaoddatej÷lhfilenamegrfc8801eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130605hkeywordsÑdIPv6lProvisioningdDHCPcPvDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_!o␀granking"acrfcd8801irfcNumber"aestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Discovering Provisioning Domain Names and DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document introduces a simple mechanism to associate a group of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) via an extension to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) with the purpose of computing diverse (disjointed) paths for those LSPs.  The proposed extension allows a Path Computation Client (PCC) to advertise to a Path Computation Element (PCE) that a particular LSP belongs to a particular Disjoint Association Group; thus, the PCE knows that the LSPs in the same group need to be disjoint from each other.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehC. Barth¢kaffiliationmRtBrick IndiadnamegM. Negiddatej÷lhfilenamegrfc8800eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130604hkeywordsÉhDisjointldisjointnesskassociationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ ∞granking"`crfcd8800irfcNumber"`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extension for Label Switched Path (LSP) Diversity Constraint SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756¥habstracty¢There is a noticeable trend towards network behaviors and semantics that are specific to a particular set of requirements applied within a limited region of the Internet. Policies, default parameters, the options supported, the style of network management, and security requirements may vary between such limited regions. This document reviews examples of such limited domains (also known as controlled environments), notes emerging solutions, and includes a related taxonomy. It then briefly discusses the standardization of protocols for limited domains. Finally, it shows the need for a precise definition of "limited domain membership" and for mechanisms to allow nodes to join a domain securely and to find other members, including boundary nodes.

This document is the product of the research of the authors. It has been produced through discussions and consultation within the IETF but is not the product of IETF consensus.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefB. Liuddatej–hfilenamegrfc8799eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130603hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_5«granking"_crfcd8799irfcNumber"_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&Limited Domains and Internet ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThe Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML) media type supports the indication of units for a quantity represented.  This short document registers a number of additional unit names in the IANA registry for units in SenML.  It also defines a registry for secondary units that cannot be in SenML's main registry, as they are derived by linear transformation from units already in that registry.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷qhfilenamegrfc8798eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130602hkeywordsÖxInternet of Things (IoT)jdata modeltquantities and unitsx"International System of Units (SI)x(International System of Quantities (ISQ)kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate^ÙÍØgranking"^crfcd8798irfcNumber"^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Additional Units for Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|This document specifies the format of Remote Direct Memory Access - Connection Manager (RDMA-CM) Private Data exchanged between RPC-over-RDMA version 1 peers as part of establishing a connection.  The addition of the Private Data payload specified in this document is an optional extension that does not alter the RPC-over-RDMA version 1 protocol.  This document updates RFC 8166.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehC. Leverddatej÷mhfilenamegrfc8797eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130601hkeywordsÅmNFS-over-RDMAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^ÙÍ†granking"]crfcd8797irfcNumber"]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcRemote Direct Memory Access - Connection Manager (RDMA-CM) Private Data for RPC-over-RDMA Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸThis document updates RFC 4090 for the Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Traffic Engineering (TE) procedures defined for facility backup protection.  The updates include extensions that reduce the amount of signaling and processing that occurs during Fast Reroute (FRR); as a result, scalability when undergoing FRR convergence after a link or node failure is improved.  These extensions allow the RSVP message exchange between the Point of Local Repair (PLR) and the Merge Point (MP) nodes to be independent of the number of protected Label Switched Paths (LSPs) traversing between them when facility bypass FRR protection is used.  The signaling extensions are fully backwards compatible with nodes that do not support them.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejM. Taillon¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekA. Deshmukh¢kaffiliationn128 TechnologydnamegM. Jork¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeramddatej–hfilenamegrfc8796eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130600hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_>’granking"\crfcd8796irfcNumber"\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMRSVP-TE Summary Fast Reroute Extensions for Label Switched Path (LSP) TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹This document defines a YANG data model for representing, retrieving, and manipulating Traffic Engineering (TE) Topologies.  The model serves as a base model that other technology-specific TE topology models can augment.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies, Inc.dnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationeCienadnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Diosddatej÷Xhfilenamegrfc8795eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130599hkeywordsákTE topologyvTE topology YANG modeltAbstract TE topologyrNative TE topologyvCustomized TE topologytUnderlay TE topologysOverlay TE topologykobsoletedByÄepages™opublicationDate_,ãjgranking"[crfcd8795irfcNumber"[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7YANG Data Model for Traffic Engineering (TE) TopologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document defines the Extensible Binary Meta Language (EBML) format as a binary container format designed for audio/video storage.  EBML is designed as a binary equivalent to XML and uses a storage-efficient approach to build nested Elements with identifiers, lengths, and values.  Similar to how an XML Schema defines the structure and semantics of an XML Document, this document defines how EBML Schemas are created to convey the semantics of an EBML Document.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Lhomme¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Rice¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Bunkusddatej÷khfilenamegrfc8794eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfcellardfullxHcellar - Codec Encoding for LossLess Archiving and Realtime transmissiondnamex?Codec Encoding for LossLess Archiving and Realtime transmissiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130598hkeywordsáfcellarfbinarygstoragecxmlhmatroskadwebmdebmlkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDate_ÅÍgranking"Zcrfcd8794irfcNumber"ZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexExtensible Binary Meta LanguagedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9559¥habstractyôInformation-Centric Networking (ICN) is a novel paradigm where network communications are accomplished by requesting named content instead of sending packets to destination addresses.  Named Data Networking (NDN) and Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) are two prominent ICN architectures.  This document provides an overview of the terminology and definitions that have been used in describing concepts in these two implementations of ICN.  While there are other ICN architectures, they are not part of the NDN and CCNx concepts and as such are out of scope for this document.  This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamekB. Wissingh¢kaffiliationxUniversity of California IrvinednamegC. Wood¢kaffiliationx Florida International UniversitydnamelA. Afanasyev¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehL. Zhang¢kaffiliationx!Network Systems Research & DesigndnamegD. Oran¢kaffiliationsUniversity of BaseldnamekC. Tschudinddatej÷jhfilenamegrfc8793eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130597hkeywordsÑocontent routingocontent cachingxcontent distribution networksudata-centric securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Í¿granking"Ycrfcd8793irfcNumber"YestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexsInformation-Centric Networking (ICN): Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) and Named Data Networking (NDN) TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœThis document defines two strategies for handling long lines in width-bounded text content.  One strategy, called the "single backslash" strategy, is based on the historical use of a single backslash ('\') character to indicate where line-folding has occurred, with the continuation occurring with the first character that is not a space character (' ') on the next line.  The second strategy, called the "double backslash" strategy, extends the first strategy by adding a second backslash character to identify where the continuation begins and is thereby able to handle cases not supported by the first strategy.  Both strategies use a self-describing header enabling automated reconstitution of the original content.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamelE. Auerswald¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeQ. Wuddatej÷khfilenamegrfc8792eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130596hkeywordsÇjsourcecodegartworkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^˙ mgranking"Xcrfcd8792irfcNumber"XestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Handling Long Lines in Content of Internet-Drafts and RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxXThis document describes YANG mechanisms for defining abstract data structures with YANG.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemM. Bj√∂rklund¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej–hfilenamegrfc8791eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130595hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Í¿
granking"Wcrfcd8791irfcNumber"WestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYANG Data Structure ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€The Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML) media type and data model can be used to send collections of resources, such as batches of sensor data or configuration parameters.  The Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) FETCH, PATCH, and iPATCH methods enable accessing and updating parts of a resource or multiple resources with one request.  This document defines new media types for the CoAP FETCH, PATCH, and iPATCH methods for resources represented using the SenML data model.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Ker√§nen¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Mohajerddatej÷nhfilenamegrfc8790eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130594hkeywordsÉdCoAPcIoTjdata modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^˚µÔgranking"Vcrfcd8790irfcNumber"VestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5FETCH and PATCH with Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx}This document requires that the IETF never publish any IETF Stream RFCs without IETF rough consensus.  This updates RFC 2026.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorladdatej÷mhfilenamegrfc8789eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130593hkeywordsÇgprocesskpublicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Í∆¸granking"Ucrfcd8789irfcNumber"UestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber	etotaletitlex2IETF Stream Documents Require IETF Rough ConsensusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1The 2020-2021 Nominating Committee (NomCom) is to be formed between the IETF 107 and IETF 108 meetings, and the issue of eligibility of who can serve on that NomCom needs clarification.  This document provides a one-time interpretation of the eligibility rules that is required for the exceptional situation of the cancellation of the in-person IETF 107 meeting.  This document only affects the seating of the 2020-2021 NomCom and any rules or processes that relate to NomCom eligibility before IETF 108; it does not set a precedent to be applied in the future.fadNamepMurray Kucherawydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvFutureWei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej÷yhfilenamegrfc8788eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130592hkeywordsÅfnomcomkobsoletedByÅd9389epagesopublicationDate^Œ	Xgranking"Tcrfcd8788irfcNumber"TestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Eligibility for the 2020-2021 Nominating CommitteedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThere are some circumstances where a Geolocation header field may contain more than one locationValue.  Knowing the identity of the node adding the locationValue allows the recipient more freedom in selecting the value to look at first rather than relying solely on the order of the locationValues.  This document defines the "loc-src" parameter so that the entity adding the locationValue to the Geolocation header field can identify itself using its hostname.  This document updates RFC 6442.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxWinterb Consulting ServicesdnameoJ. Winterbottom¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesske¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamejB. Chatras¢kaffiliationdAtosdnameiA. Huttonddatej÷lhfilenamegrfc8787eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130591hkeywordsÉiEmergencydCallhLocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^‘„granking"Scrfcd8787irfcNumber"SestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Location Source Parameter for the SIP Geolocation Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄExtensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) to support stateful Path Computation Elements (PCEs) are defined in RFC 8231. One of the extensions is the Stateful PCE Request Parameters (SRP) object. That object includes a Flags field that is a set of 32 bit flags, and RFC 8281 defines an IANA registry for tracking assigned flags. However, RFC 8231 does not explain how an implementation should set unassigned flags in transmitted messages, nor how an implementation should process unassigned, unknown, or unsupported flags in received messages.

This document updates RFC 8231 by defining the correct behaviors.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej÷khfilenamegrfc8786eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130590hkeywordsÑdPCEPxPath Computation ElementlStateful PCEeFlagskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^”˛ granking"Rcrfcd8786irfcNumber"RestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBUpdated Rules for Processing Stateful PCE Request Parameters FlagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty*Cryptographic operations like hashing and signing need the data to be expressed in an invariant format so that the operations are reliably repeatable. One way to address this is to create a canonical representation of the data. Canonicalization also permits data to be exchanged in its original form on the "wire" while cryptographic operations performed on the canonicalized counterpart of the data in the producer and consumer endpoints generate consistent results.

This document describes the JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS). This specification defines how to create a canonical representation of JSON data by building on the strict serialization methods for JSON primitives defined by ECMAScript, constraining JSON data to the Internet JSON (I-JSON) subset, and by using deterministic property sorting.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamekA. Rundgren¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnameiB. Jordan¢kaffiliationjSpotify ABdnamejS. Erdtmanddatej÷jhfilenamegrfc8785eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130589hkeywordsÖdJSONjECMAScriptjSignatureslCryptographypCanonicalizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^˙ ggranking"Qcrfcd8785irfcNumber"QestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex"JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThe possibility of quantum computers poses a serious challenge to cryptographic algorithms deployed widely today.  The Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) is one example of a cryptosystem that could be broken; someone storing VPN communications today could decrypt them at a later time when a quantum computer is available.  It is anticipated that IKEv2 will be extended to support quantum-secure key exchange algorithms; however, that is not likely to happen in the near term.  To address this problem before then, this document describes an extension of IKEv2 to allow it to be resistant to a quantum computer by using preshared keys.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Fluhrer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej÷jhfilenamegrfc8784eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130588hkeywordsáuinternet key exchangepquantum computerlpost quantumlpost-quantumlquantum safenquantum secureqquantum resistantkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^˚µ‰granking"Pcrfcd8784irfcNumber"PestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgMixing Preshared Keys in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) for Post-quantum SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThe document specifies a Distributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) data channel used for bulk exchange of data that cannot easily or appropriately communicated through the DOTS signal channel under attack conditions.

This is a companion document to "Distributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) Signal Channel Specification" (RFC 8782).fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamejT. Reddy.Kddatej÷Uhfilenamegrfc8783eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130587hkeywordsãdDOTSjAutomationhSecurityjMitigationiScrubbingiAnti-DDoSiMitigatoroSecurity CenteriFilteringjResiliencehRESTCONFkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDate^”Kggranking"Ocrfcd8783irfcNumber"OestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUDistributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) Data Channel SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∏This document specifies the Distributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) signal channel, a protocol for signaling the need for protection against Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attacks to a server capable of enabling network traffic mitigation on behalf of the requesting client.

A companion document defines the DOTS data channel, a separate reliable communication layer for DOTS management and configuration purposes.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Patil¢kaffiliationtArbor Networks, Inc.dnamelA. Mortensen¢kaffiliationxIron Mountain Data CentersdnameiN. Teagueddatej÷ihfilenamegrfc8782eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130586hkeywordsêhsecurityjmitigationpservice deliverylconnectivityianti-DDoSjautomationkcooperationjresilienceifilteringosecurity centerimitigatoriscrubbingxdynamic service protectionrdynamic mitigationvcooperative networkinguprotective networkingkobsoletedByÅd9132epagesdopublicationDate^”KXgranking"Ncrfcd8782irfcNumber"NestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWDistributed Denial-of-Service Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) Signal Channel SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx’This document specifies a Neighbor Discovery option to be used in Router Advertisements (RAs) to communicate prefixes of Network Address and Protocol Translation from IPv6 clients to IPv4 servers (NAT64) to hosts.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Colitti¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkovaddatej–hfilenamegrfc8781eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130585hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate^£2(granking"Mcrfcd8781irfcNumber"MestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Discovering PREF64 in Router AdvertisementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document provides Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) extensions for the support of Routing and Wavelength Assignment (RWA) in Wavelength Switched Optical Networks (WSONs).  Path provisioning in WSONs requires an RWA process.  From a path computation perspective, wavelength assignment is the process of determining which wavelength can be used on each hop of a path and forms an additional routing constraint to optical path computation.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnamekR. Casellasddatej÷Ghfilenamegrfc8780eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130584hkeywordsÉuWavelength AllocationxTransparent Optical NetworksoFixed DWDM GridkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_·granking"Lcrfcd8780irfcNumber"LestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexõThe Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extension for Wavelength Switched Optical Network (WSON) Routing and Wavelength Assignment (RWA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractyyA Path Computation Element (PCE) provides path computation functions for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks. Additional requirements for GMPLS are identified in RFC 7025.

This memo provides extensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for the support of the GMPLS control plane to address those requirements.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamekC. Margaria¢kaffiliationx%Telefonica Investigacion y DesarrollodnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Zhangddatej÷hhfilenamegrfc8779eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130583hkeywordsÉgRSVP-TEeGMPLScPCEkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDate_Ÿgranking"Kcrfcd8779irfcNumber"KestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for GMPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstracty6This document specifies the conventions for using the Hierarchical Signature System (HSS) / Leighton-Micali Signature (LMS) hash-based signature algorithm with the CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) syntax.  The HSS/LMS algorithm is one form of hash-based digital signature; it is described in RFC 8554.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷ihfilenamegrfc8778eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130582hkeywordsÇqdigital signaturex&HSS/LMS Hash-based Signature AlgorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^î’Ógranking"Jcrfcd8778irfcNumber"JestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Use of the HSS/LMS Hash-Based Signature Algorithm with CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!This document updates RFC 7450, "Automatic Multicast Tunneling" (or AMT), by modifying the relay discovery process.  A new DNS resource record named AMTRELAY is defined for publishing AMT relays for source-specific multicast channels.  The reverse IP DNS zone for a multicast sender's IP address is configured to use AMTRELAY resource records to advertise a set of AMT relays that can receive and forward multicast traffic from that sender over an AMT tunnel.  Other extensions and clarifications to the relay discovery process are also defined.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxAkamai Technologies, Inc.dnamejJ. Hollandddatej÷ihfilenamegrfc8777eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130581hkeywordséeDRIADeDRYADcAMTfIGMPv3eMLDv2cSSMkamt gatewayiamt relayimulticastumulticast replicationwmulticast encapsulationsamt relay discoverymamt discoveryhAMTRELAYkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate^£0granking"Icrfcd8777irfcNumber"IestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<DNS Reverse IP Automatic Multicast Tunneling (AMT) DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document defines a collection of common data types and groupings in YANG data modeling language.  These derived common types and groupings are intended to be imported by modules that model Traffic Engineering (TE) configuration and state capabilities.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies, Inc.dnamejI. Bryskinddatej÷ghfilenamegrfc8776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130580hkeywordsáiTE TunnelhTE ModelhTE TypesgTE YANGkTE TopologymTE InterfaceslTE LSP ModelkobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDate^·{Vgranking"Hcrfcd8776irfcNumber"HestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Common YANG Data Types for Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„On a multi-access network, one of the PIM-SM (PIM Sparse Mode) routers is elected as a Designated Router.  One of the responsibilities of the Designated Router is to track local multicast listeners and forward data to these listeners if the group is operating in PIM-SM.  This document specifies a modification to the PIM-SM protocol that allows more than one of the PIM-SM routers to take on this responsibility so that the forwarding load can be distributed among multiple routers.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmAlibaba GroupdnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationmAlibaba GroupdnameeH. Ou¢kaffiliation`dnamemS. Vallepalli¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Mishra¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationoBritish TelecomdnamehA. Greenddatej÷hhfilenamegrfc8775eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130579hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^†ıñgranking"Gcrfcd8775irfcNumber"GestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$PIM Designated Router Load BalancingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThe age of quantum networking is upon us, and with it comes "entanglement": a procedure in which a state (i.e., a bit) can be transferred instantly, with no measurable delay between peers.  This will lead to a perceived round-trip time of zero seconds on some Internet paths, a capability which was not predicted and so not included as a possibility in many protocol specifications.  Worse than the millennium bug, this unexpected value is bound to cause serious Internet failures unless the specifications are fixed in time.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzlddatej÷uhfilenamegrfc8774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-95877hkeywordsÉmTeleportationlEntanglemente0-RTTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Ñ¿4granking"Fcrfcd8774irfcNumber"FestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitleoThe Quantum BugdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxöThis document specifies a TLS 1.3 extension that allows a server to authenticate with a combination of a certificate and an external pre-shared key (PSK).fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej–hfilenamegrfc8773eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130578hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Å‘granking"Ecrfcd8773irfcNumber"EestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVTLS 1.3 Extension for Certificate-Based Authentication with an External Pre-Shared KeydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyA Broadband Network Gateway (BNG) in a fixed wireline access network is an Ethernet-centric IP edge router and the aggregation point for subscriber traffic. Control and User Plane Separation (CUPS) for such a BNG improves flexibility and scalability but requires various communication between the User Plane (UP) and the Control Plane (CP). China Mobile, Huawei Technologies, and ZTE have developed a simple CUPS control channel protocol to support such communication: the Simple Control and User Plane Separation Protocol (S-CUSP). S-CUSP is defined in this document.

This document is not an IETF standard and does not have IETF consensus. S-CUSP is presented here to make its specification conveniently available to the Internet community to enable diagnosis and interoperability.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeS. Hu¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamekD. Eastlake¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefF. Qin¢kaffiliationxSingapore TelecommunicationsdnamegT. Chua¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamehD. Huangddatej÷ghfilenamegrfc8772eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130577hkeywordsÑdCUPSdCUSPdBRASeBBRASkobsoletedByÄepages|opublicationDate^πÈ„granking"Dcrfcd8772irfcNumber"DestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex|The China Mobile, Huawei, and ZTE Broadband Network Gateway (BNG) Simple Control and User Plane Separation Protocol (S-CUSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty[Domain Names were designed for humans, IP addresses were not.  But more than 30 years after the introduction of the DNS, a minority of mankind persists in invading the realm of machine-to-machine communication by reading, writing, misspelling, memorizing, permuting, and confusing IP addresses.  This memo describes the Internationalized Deliberately Unreadable Network NOtation ("I-DUNNO"), a notation designed to replace current textual representations of IP addresses with something that is not only more concise but will also discourage this small, but obviously important, subset of human activity.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationknic.at GmbHdnamelA. Mayrhofer¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamehJ. Hagueddatejœ‚hfilenamegrfc8771eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-95876hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate^Ñ¿4granking"Ccrfcd8771irfcNumber"CestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHThe Internationalized Deliberately Unreadable Network NOtation (I-DUNNO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9The Open Shortest Path First Version 2 (OSPFv2) protocol does not have a mechanism for a node to repel transit traffic if it is on the shortest path.  This document defines a bit called the Host-bit (H-bit).  This bit enables a router to advertise that it is a non-transit router.  This document also describes the changes needed to support the H-bit in the domain.  In addition, this document updates RFC 6987 to advertise Type 2 External and Not-So-Stubby Area (NSSA) Link State Advertisements (LSAs) (RFC 3101) with a high cost in order to repel traffic effectively.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationnPPE ConsultingdnameqP. Pillay-Esnault¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Bhardwaj¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Bayraktarddatej÷ihfilenamegrfc8770eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130576hkeywordsÅknon-transitkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^èYÜgranking"Bcrfcd8770irfcNumber"BestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHost Router Support for OSPFv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyConcise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) is becoming a widely used method of doing content encoding.  The Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) is still a widely used method of doing message-based security.  This document defines a set of content types for CMS that hold CBOR content.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaadddatej–hfilenamegrfc8769eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130575hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^uñögranking"Acrfcd8769irfcNumber"AestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Content Types for Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx“The presence of Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) proxies may lead to infinite forwarding loops, which is undesirable.  To prevent and detect such loops, this document specifies the Hop-Limit CoAP option.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamejT. Reddy.K¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Shallowddatej÷ghfilenamegrfc8768eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130574hkeywordséhsecurityjmitigationpservice deliverylconnectivityianti-DDoSjautomationkcooperationjResilienceiFilteringoSecurity CenteriMitigatoriScrubbingxdynamic service protectionrdynamic mitigationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^uñógranking"@crfcd8768irfcNumber"@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Hop-Limit OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document defines a method (serve-stale) for recursive resolvers to use stale DNS data to avoid outages when authoritative nameservers cannot be reached to refresh expired data.  One of the motivations for serve-stale is to make the DNS more resilient to DoS attacks and thereby make them less attractive as an attack vector.  This document updates the definitions of TTL from RFCs 1034 and 1035 so that data can be kept in the cache beyond the TTL expiry; it also updates RFC 2181 by interpreting values with the high-order bit set as being positive, rather than 0, and suggests a cap of 7 days.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekD. Lawrence¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegP. Soodddatej÷hhfilenamegrfc8767eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130573hkeywordsÖcDNSdDDoSjResiliencyqDenial-of-ServicegExpiredkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^É≈Çgranking"?crfcd8767irfcNumber"?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Serving Stale Data to Improve DNS ResiliencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx“This document specifies a network proxy that uses Multicast DNS to automatically populate the wide-area unicast Domain Name System namespace with records describing devices and services found on the local link.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshireddatej÷,hfilenamegrfc8766eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednssddfullx5dnssd - Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydnamex-Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-130572hkeywordsÇmMulticast DNSxDNS-Based Service DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate^ÒÖÇgranking">crfcd8766irfcNumber">estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Discovery Proxy for Multicast DNS-Based Service DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝The Domain Name System (DNS) was designed to return matching records efficiently for queries for data that are relatively static.  When those records change frequently, DNS is still efficient at returning the updated results when polled, as long as the polling rate is not too high.  But, there exists no mechanism for a client to be asynchronously notified when these changes occur.  This document defines a mechanism for a client to be notified of such changes to DNS records, called DNS Push Notifications.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekT. Pusateri¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshireddatej÷fhfilenamegrfc8765eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednssddfullx5dnssd - Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydnamex-Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-130571hkeywordsÑcdnsfupdatedpushlnotificationkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate^ÒÖÅgranking"=crfcd8765irfcNumber"=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevDNS Push NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty"Apple's DNS Long-Lived Queries (LLQ) is a mechanism for extending the DNS protocol to support change notification, thus allowing clients to learn about changes to DNS data without polling the server. From 2005 onwards, LLQ was implemented in Apple products including Mac OS X, Bonjour for Windows, and AirPort wireless base stations. In 2020, the LLQ protocol was superseded by the IETF Standards Track RFC 8765, "DNS Push Notifications", which builds on experience gained with the LLQ protocol to create a superior replacement.

The existing LLQ protocol deployed and used from 2005 to 2020 is documented here to give background regarding the operational experience that informed the development of DNS Push Notifications, and to help facilitate a smooth transition from LLQ to DNS Push Notifications.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekM. Krochmalddatej÷Zhfilenamegrfc8764eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130570hkeywordsÑeAsynclAsynchronoussChange NotificationqPush NotificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^ÒÖÄgranking"<crfcd8764irfcNumber"<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex'Apple's DNS Long-Lived Queries ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty4Information-Centric Networking (ICN) is now reaching technological maturity after many years of fundamental research and experimentation.  This document provides a number of deployment considerations in the interest of helping the ICN community move forward to the next step of live deployments.  First, the major deployment configurations for ICN are described, including the key overlay and underlay approaches.  Then, proposed deployment migration paths are outlined to address major practical issues, such as network and application migration.  Next, selected ICN trial experiences are summarized.  Finally, protocol areas that require further standardization are identified to facilitate future interoperable ICN deployments.  This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnameiA. Rahman¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejD. Trossen¢kaffiliationpEmden UniversitydnamekD. Kutscher¢kaffiliationuSterlite TechnologiesdnamelR. Ravindranddatej÷ghfilenamegrfc8763eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130569hkeywordsâgroutingxContent Delivery Network (CDN)goverlayhunderlaygvirtualnvirtualizationfnamingcNFVcSDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^ôﬁgranking";crfcd8763irfcNumber";estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexBDeployment Considerations for Information-Centric Networking (ICN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxœThis document describes the Simple Two-way Active Measurement Protocol (STAMP), which enables the measurement of both one-way and round-trip performance metrics, like delay, delay variation, and packet loss.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnamefG. Jun¢kaffiliationqAccedian NetworksdnameiH. Nydell¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Footeddatej–hfilenamegrfc8762eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130568hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^sÎÚgranking":crfcd8762irfcNumber":estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Simple Two-Way Active Measurement ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8972∂habstractyThis document provides requirements for a video codec designed mainly for use over the Internet.  In addition, this document describes an evaluation methodology for measuring the compression efficiency to determine whether or not the stated requirements have been fulfilled.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamekA. Filippov¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnameiA. Norkin¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelJ.R. Alvarezddatej÷ghfilenamegrfc8761eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenetvcdfullxnetv