al Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite published by the United States Government.

The profile applies to the capabilities, configuration, and operation of all components of US National Security Systems that manage X.509 public key certificates over CMS. It is also appropriate for all other US Government systems that process high-value information.

The profile is made publicly available here for use by developers and operators of these and any other system deployments.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejM. Jenkins¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejL. Zieglarddatej÷Rhfilenamegrfc8756eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130562hkeywordsÖcNSAdCNSAcNSSkcertificatejenrollmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^~Fgranking"4crfcd8756irfcNumber"4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex^Commercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite Profile of Certificate Management over CMSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyIThe United States Government has published the National Security Agency (NSA) Commercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite, which defines cryptographic algorithm policy for national security applications.  This document specifies the conventions for using the United States National Security Agency's CNSA Suite algorithms in Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) as specified in RFC 8551.  It applies to the capabilities, configuration, and operation of all components of US National Security Systems that employ S/MIME messaging.  US National Security Systems are described in NIST Special Publication 800-59.  It is also appropriate for all other US Government systems that process high-value information.  It is made publicly available for use by developers and operators of these and any other system deployments.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejM. Jenkinsddatej÷fhfilenamegrfc8755eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130561hkeywordsÑcNSAdCNSAcNSSesmimekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^s∞agranking"3crfcd8755irfcNumber"3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexmUsing Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite Algorithms in Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔSegment Routing can be applied to the IPv6 data plane using a new type of Routing Extension Header called the Segment Routing Header (SRH).  This document describes the SRH and how it is used by nodes that are Segment Routing (SR) capable.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehD. Dukes¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamehJ. Leddy¢kaffiliationhSoftBankdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamehD. Voyerddatej÷bhfilenamegrfc8754eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130560hkeywordsÉdSRv6nsource-routingsnetwork-programmingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^m-ßgranking"2crfcd8754irfcNumber"2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!IPv6 Segment Routing Header (SRH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9800∂habstractyThe standards for Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) require a review of each new version of Unicode to determine whether incompatibilities with prior versions or other issues exist and, where appropriate, to allow the IETF to decide on the trade-offs between compatibility with prior IDNA versions and compatibility with Unicode going forward.  That requirement, and its relationship to tables maintained by IANA, has caused significant confusion in the past.  This document makes adjustments to the review procedure based on experience and updates IDNA, specifically RFC 5892, to reflect those changes and to clarify the various relationships involved.  It also makes other minor adjustments to align that document with experience.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationfNetnoddnamenP. F√§ltstr√∂mddatej÷chfilenamegrfc8753eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130559hkeywordsÖhIDNA2008cIDNxUnicode Algorithmic ReviewxUnicode Code Point ReviewvIDNA Designated ExpertkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^ó˙Ägranking"1crfcd8753irfcNumber"1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVInternationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA) Review for New Unicode VersionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty˜The Exploring Synergy between Content Aggregation and the Publisher Ecosystem (ESCAPE) Workshop was convened by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) in July 2019. This report summarizes its significant points of discussion and identifies topics that may warrant further consideration.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷ehfilenamegrfc8752eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130558hkeywordsÖcwebhsecurityforiginipackagingfbundlekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^iy(granking"0crfcd8752irfcNumber"0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexrReport from the IAB Workshop on Exploring Synergy between Content Aggregation and the Publisher Ecosystem (ESCAPE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›A stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) maintains information on the current network state received from the Path Computation Clients (PCCs), including computed Label Switched Paths (LSPs), reserved resources within the network, and pending path computation requests. This information may then be considered when computing the path for a new traffic-engineered LSP or for any associated/dependent LSPs. The path-computation response from a PCE helps the PCC to gracefully establish the computed LSP.

The Hierarchical Path Computation Element (H-PCE) architecture allows the optimum sequence of interconnected domains to be selected and network policy to be applied if applicable, via the use of a hierarchical relationship between PCEs.

Combining the capabilities of stateful PCE and the hierarchical PCE would be advantageous. This document describes general considerations and use cases for the deployment of stateful, but not stateless, PCEs using the hierarchical PCE architecture.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationjSK TelecomdnamegJ. Shin¢kaffiliationtLancaster UniversitydnamegD. Kingddatej–hfilenamegrfc8751eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130557hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Éçggranking"/crfcd8751irfcNumber"/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Hierarchical Stateful Path Computation Element (PCE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊEncapsulating Security Payload (ESP) sends an initialization vector (IV) in each packet.  The size of the IV depends on the applied transform and is usually 8 or 16 octets for the transforms defined at the time this document was written.  When used with IPsec, some algorithms, such as AES-GCM, AES-CCM, and ChaCha20-Poly1305, take the IV to generate a nonce that is used as an input parameter for encrypting and decrypting.  This IV must be unique but can be predictable.  As a result, the value provided in the ESP Sequence Number (SN) can be used instead to generate the nonce.  This avoids sending the IV itself and saves 8 octets per packet in the case of AES-GCM, AES-CCM, and ChaCha20-Poly1305.  This document describes how to do this.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migault¢kaffiliationjLMU MunichdnamekT. Guggemos¢kaffiliationqDell TechnologiesdnamefY. Nirddatej÷ehfilenamegrfc8750eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130556hkeywordsÖcIKEeIPseccGCMcCCMhChaCha20kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^ilágranking".crfcd8750irfcNumber".estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeImplicit Initialization Vector (IV) for Counter-Based Ciphers in Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This document retires DNSSEC Lookaside Validation (DLV) and reclassifies RFCs 4431 and 5074 as Historic.  Furthermore, this document updates RFC 6698 by excluding the DLV resource record from certificates and updates RFC 6840 by excluding the DLV registries from the trust anchor selection.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejW. Mekking¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejD. Mahoneyddatej÷dhfilenamegrfc8749eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130555hkeywordsÉcDNSfDNSSECcDLVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^~Fgranking"-crfcd8749irfcNumber"-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Moving DNSSEC Lookaside Validation (DLV) to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòGiven the expansion of the DNS namespace and the proliferation of novel business models, it is desirable to provide a method for Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) clients to query EPP servers for the fees and credits associated with various billable transactions and provide expected fees and credits for certain commands and objects.  This document describes an EPP extension mapping for registry fees.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlGoDaddy Inc.dnameiR. Carney¢kaffiliationtCentralNic Group plcdnamehG. Brown¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Frakesddatej–hfilenamegrfc8748eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130554hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^m-•granking",crfcd8748irfcNumber",estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexERegistry Fee Extension for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification describes how to declare in a CBOR Web Token (CWT) (which is defined by RFC 8392) that the presenter of the CWT possesses a particular proof-of-possession key.  Being able to prove possession of a key is also sometimes described as being the holder-of-key.  This specification provides equivalent functionality to "Proof-of-Possession Key Semantics for JSON Web Tokens (JWTs)" (RFC 7800) but using Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) and CWTs rather than JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) and JSON Web Tokens (JWTs).fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationiCombitechdnamehL. Seitz¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliationgSpotifydnamejS. Erdtman¢kaffiliationhArm Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej÷dhfilenamegrfc8747eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130553hkeywordsÑnCBOR Web TokencCWTsProof-of-PossessionmHolder-of-KeykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^g¿granking"+crfcd8747irfcNumber"+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Proof-of-Possession Key Semantics for CBOR Web Tokens (CWTs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR), as defined in RFC 7049, is a data format whose design goals include the possibility of extremely small code size, fairly small message size, and extensibility without the need for version negotiation.

This document makes use of this extensibility to define a number of CBOR tags for typed arrays of numeric data, as well as additional tags for multi-dimensional and homogeneous arrays. It is intended as the reference document for the IANA registration of the CBOR tags defined.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Hhfilenamegrfc8746eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130552hkeywordsÉmbinary formatwdata interchange formatdJSONkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Y¨çgranking"*crfcd8746irfcNumber"*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAConcise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) Tags for Typed ArraysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆An active stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) is capable of computing as well as controlling via Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Multiprotocol Label Switching Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  Furthermore, it is also possible for an active stateful PCE to create, maintain, and delete LSPs.  This document defines the PCEP extension to associate two or more LSPs to provide end-to-end path protection.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamerH. Ananthakrishnan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehC. Barth¢kaffiliationjGoogle IncdnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Negiddatej÷ahfilenamegrfc8745eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130551hkeywordsÅdPCEPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Å“granking")crfcd8745irfcNumber")estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexñPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Associating Working and Protection Label Switched Paths (LSPs) with Stateful PCEdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstracty,This document describes the general problem of encrypting the Server Name Identification (SNI) TLS parameter. The proposed solutions hide a hidden service behind a fronting service, only disclosing the SNI of the fronting service to external observers. This document lists known attacks against SNI encryption, discusses the current "HTTP co-tenancy" solution, and presents requirements for future TLS-layer solutions.

In practice, it may well be that no solution can meet every requirement and that practical solutions will have to make some compromises.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtPrivate Octopus Inc.dnamejC. Huitemaddatej–hfilenamegrfc8744eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130550hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_ üãgranking"(crfcd8744irfcNumber"(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNIssues and Requirements for Server Name Identification (SNI) Encryption in TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyåIn multiconnectivity scenarios, the clients can simultaneously connect to multiple networks based on different access technologies and network architectures like Wi-Fi, LTE, and DSL. Both the quality of experience of the users and the overall network utilization and efficiency may be improved through the smart selection and combination of access and core network paths that can dynamically adapt to changing network conditions.

This document presents a unified problem statement and introduces a solution for managing multiconnectivity. The solution has been developed by the authors based on their experiences in multiple standards bodies, including the IETF and the 3GPP. However, this document is not an Internet Standards Track specification, and it does not represent the consensus opinion of the IETF.

This document describes requirements, solution principles, and the architecture of the Multi-Access Management Services (MAMS) framework. The MAMS framework aims to provide best performance while being easy to implement in a wide variety of multiconnectivity deployments. It specifies the protocol for (1) flexibly selecting the best combination of access and core network paths for the uplink and downlink, and (2) determining the user-plane treatment (e.g., tunneling, encryption) and traffic distribution over the selected links, to ensure network efficiency and the best possible application performance.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamekS. Kanugovi¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamekF. Baboescu¢kaffiliationeInteldnamefJ. Zhu¢kaffiliationmKorea TelecomdnamefS. Seoddatej÷Qhfilenamegrfc8743eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130549hkeywordsåkIntegrationkAggregationiSwitchingeMPTCPfMPQUICcGMAb5GcLTEeWi-FihEthernetdEdgeeProxykobsoletedByÄepagesèopublicationDate^{¨Ogranking"'crfcd8743irfcNumber"'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexKMultiple Access Management Services Multi-Access Management Services (MAMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) Sequence format and associated media type "application/cbor-seq". A CBOR Sequence consists of any number of encoded CBOR data items, simply concatenated in sequence.

Structured syntax suffixes for media types allow other media types to build on them and make it explicit that they are built on an existing media type as their foundation. This specification defines and registers "+cbor-seq" as a structured syntax suffix for CBOR Sequences.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversit√§t Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷ahfilenamegrfc8742eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130548hkeywordsÉmbinary formatwdata interchange formatdJSONkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate^MΩ¥granking"&crfcd8742irfcNumber"&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) SequencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘A stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) retains information about the placement of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs). When a PCE has stateful control over LSPs, it may send indications to LSP head-ends to modify the attributes (especially the paths) of the LSPs. A Path Computation Client (PCC) that has set up LSPs under local configuration may delegate control of those LSPs to a stateful PCE.

There are use cases in which a stateful PCE may wish to obtain control of locally configured LSPs that it is aware of but have not been delegated to the PCE.

This document describes an extension to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) to enable a PCE to make requests for such control.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekA. Raghuram¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamejA. Goddard¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejJ. Karthik¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Negiddatej–hfilenamegrfc8741eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130547hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^gºgranking"%crfcd8741irfcNumber"%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrAbility for a Stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) to Request and Obtain Control of a Label Switched Path (LSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxìThis document updates RFC 7540 by forbidding TLS 1.3 post-handshake authentication, as an analog to the existing TLS 1.2 renegotiation restriction.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Benjaminddatej÷Ehfilenamegrfc8740eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130546hkeywordsÉdHTTPmrenegotiationx$post-handshake client authenticationkobsoletedByÅd9113epagesopublicationDate^R6—granking"$crfcd8740irfcNumber"$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUsing TLS 1.3 with HTTP/2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPublic key certificates need to be revoked when they are compromised, that is, when the associated private key is exposed to an unauthorized entity.  However, the revocation process is often unreliable.  An alternative to revocation is issuing a sequence of certificates, each with a short validity period, and terminating the sequence upon compromise.  This memo proposes an Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) extension to enable the issuance of Short-Term, Automatically Renewed (STAR) X.509 certificates.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfIntuitdnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnameqA. Pastor Perales¢kaffiliationcARMdnamejT. Fossatiddatej÷bhfilenamegrfc8739eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130545hkeywordsÉdOCSPcCRLjrevocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^ilÜgranking"#crfcd8739irfcNumber"#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|Support for Short-Term, Automatically Renewed (STAR) Certificates in the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx•This document specifies identifiers and challenges required to enable the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) to issue certificates for IP addresses.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx Internet Security Research GroupdnamenR.B. Shoemakerddatejœ“hfilenamegrfc8738eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130544hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Z ãgranking""crfcd8738irfcNumber""estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVAutomated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) IP Identifier Validation ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx°This document specifies a new challenge for the Automated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) protocol that allows for domain control validation using TLS.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx Internet Security Research GroupdnamenR.B. Shoemakerddatej÷Ehfilenamegrfc8737eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130543hkeywordsÇdacmecpkikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Z ägranking"!crfcd8737irfcNumber"!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyAutomated Certificate Management Environment (ACME) TLS Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) Challenge ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe PIM version 2 messages share a common message header format. The common header definition contains eight reserved bits. This document specifies how these bits may be used by individual message types and creates a registry containing the per-message-type usage. This document also extends the PIM type space by defining three new message types. For each of the new types, four of the previously reserved bits are used to form an extended type range.

This document updates RFCs 7761 and 3973 by defining the use of the currently Reserved field in the PIM common header. This document further updates RFCs 7761 and 3973, along with RFCs 5015, 5059, 6754, and 8364, by specifying the use of the currently reserved bits for each PIM message.

This document obsoletes RFC 6166.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies, Inc.dnameiA. Retanaddatej÷`hfilenamegrfc8736eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130542hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÅd9436epagesopublicationDate^Z âgranking" crfcd8736irfcNumber" estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2PIM Message Type Space Extension and Reserved BitsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüRequirements for providing the End-to-End (E2E) performance assurance are emerging within the service provider networks. While there are various technology solutions, there is no single solution that can fulfill these requirements for a native IP network. In particular, there is a need for a universal E2E solution that can cover both intra- and inter-domain scenarios.

One feasible E2E traffic-engineering solution is the addition of central control in a native IP network. This document describes various complex scenarios and simulation results when applying the Path Computation Element (PCE) in a native IP network. This solution, referred to as Centralized Control Dynamic Routing (CCDR), integrates the advantage of using distributed protocols and the power of a centralized control technology, providing traffic engineering for native IP networks in a manner that applies equally to intra- and inter-domain scenarios.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegA. Wang¢kaffiliationdBUPTdnamehX. Huang¢kaffiliationdBUPTdnamefC. Kou¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeP. Middatej÷dhfilenamegrfc8735eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130541hkeywordsÇdCCDRsTraffic EngineeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Y¨àgranking"crfcd8735irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Scenarios and Simulation Results of PCE in a Native IP NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty.Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Brainpool curves were an option for authentication and key exchange in the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol version 1.2 but were deprecated by the IETF for use with TLS version 1.3 because they had little usage. However, these curves have not been shown to have significant cryptographical weaknesses, and there is some interest in using several of these curves in TLS 1.3.

This document provides the necessary protocol mechanisms for using ECC Brainpool curves in TLS 1.3. This approach is not endorsed by the IETF.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxsecunet Security NetworksdnamekL. Bruckert¢kaffiliationxsecunet Security NetworksdnameiJ. Merkle¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamejM. Lochterddatej÷`hfilenamegrfc8734eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130540hkeywordsÇcTLSxElliptic Curve CryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^PÀgranking"crfcd8734irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexaElliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Brainpool Curves for Transport Layer Security (TLS) Version 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThe Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) provides mechanisms for Path Computation Elements (PCEs) to perform path computations in response to Path Computation Client (PCC) requests. Stateful PCE extensions allow stateful control of MPLS-TE Label Switched Paths (LSPs) using PCEP.

The auto-bandwidth feature allows automatic and dynamic adjustment of the TE LSP bandwidth reservation based on the volume of traffic flowing through the LSP. This document describes PCEP extensions for auto-bandwidth adjustment when employing an active stateful PCE for both PCE-initiated and PCC-initiated LSPs.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehU. Palle¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehR. Singh¢kaffiliationsExpedia Group, Inc.dnamegL. Fangddatej÷_hfilenamegrfc8733eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130539hkeywordsÉvBandwidth optimizationnPCEP OverwhelmsLSP re-optimizationkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate^U√granking"crfcd8733irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexìPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for MPLS-TE Label Switched Path (LSP) Auto-Bandwidth Adjustment with Stateful PCEdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstracty7This document specifies additions and amendments to RFC 4462.  It defines a new key exchange method that uses SHA-2 for integrity and deprecates weak Diffie-Hellman (DH) groups.  The purpose of this specification is to modernize the cryptographic primitives used by Generic Security Service (GSS) key exchanges.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamehS. Sorce¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamehH. Karioddatej÷Shfilenamegrfc8732eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130538hkeywordsÅcSSHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Yö∂granking"crfcd8732irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXGeneric Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Key Exchange with SHA-2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÑThis document describes the specification for using Curve25519 and Curve448 key exchange methods in the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationflibsshdnameoA. Adamantiadis¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejM. Baushkeddatej÷_hfilenamegrfc8731eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130537hkeywordsÖhEllipticeCurvefDiffiegHellmandECDHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Yöµgranking"crfcd8731irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDSecure Shell (SSH) Key Exchange Method Using Curve25519 and Curve448dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÔThis document describes the function and responsibilities of the RFC Independent Submission Editor (ISE). The Independent Submission stream is one of the stream producers that create draft RFCs, with the ISE as its stream approver. The ISE is overall responsible for activities within the Independent Submission stream, working with draft editors and reviewers, and interacts with the RFC Production Center and Publisher, and the RFC Series Editor (RSE). The ISE is appointed by the IAB, and also interacts with the IETF Administration Limited Liability Company (LLC).

This version obsoletes RFC 6548 to replace all references to the Internet Administrative Support Activity (IASA) and related structures with those defined by the IASA 2.0 structure.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxThe University of AucklanddnamekN. Brownlee¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hindenddatej÷chfilenamegrfc8730eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130536hkeywordsÖcISEcRSEcLLCcIABdIASAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X¨granking"crfcd8730irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex#Independent Submission Editor ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9920d9280¥habstractyXThis document describes the framework for an RFC Series and an RFC Editor function that incorporate the principles of organized community involvement and accountability that has become necessary as the Internet technical community has grown, thereby enabling the RFC Series to continue to fulfill its mandate.  This document obsoletes RFC 4844.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliation`dnameiL. Daigleddatej÷chfilenamegrfc8729eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130535hkeywordsÉdIASAeIASA2stechnical publisherkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X´granking"crfcd8729irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexThe RFC Series and RFC EditordtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9920d9280¥habstractyñThe RFC Editor model described in this document divides the responsibilities for the RFC Series into three functions: the RFC Series Editor, the RFC Production Center, and the RFC Publisher.  Internet Architecture Board (IAB) oversight via the RFC Series Oversight Committee (RSOC) is described, as is the relationship between the IETF Administration Limited Liability Company and the RSOC.  This document reflects the experience gained with "RFC Editor Model (Version 1)", documented in RFC 5620; and obsoletes RFC 6635 to replace all references to the IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA) and related structures with those defined by the IASA 2.0 Model.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hindenddatej÷chfilenamegrfc8728eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130534hkeywordsÖcIABdIASAdRSOCcRSEeIASA2kobsoletedByÅd9280epagesopublicationDate^X™granking"crfcd8728irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexRFC Editor Model (Version 2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§The Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) defined in RFC 7970 provides an information model and a corresponding XML data model for exchanging incident and indicator information.  This document gives implementers and operators an alternative format to exchange the same information by defining an alternative data model implementation in JSON and its encoding in Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR).fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdNICTdnamelT. Takahashi¢kaffiliationdCERTdnamejR. Danyliw¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameiM. Suzukiddatej÷mhfilenamegrfc8727eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130533hkeywordsÉdCBORdJSONeIODEFkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDate_+EMgranking"crfcd8727irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?JSON Binding of the Incident Object Description Exchange FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¨The Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) maintains registries to track code points used by protocols such as those defined by the IETF and documented in RFCs developed on the IETF Stream.

The Independent Submission Stream is another source of documents that can be published as RFCs. This stream is under the care of the Independent Submissions Editor (ISE).

This document complements RFC 4846 by providing a description of how the ISE currently handles documents in the Independent Submission Stream that request actions from IANA. Nothing in this document changes existing IANA registries or their allocation policies, nor does it change any previously documented processes.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxIndependent Submissions EditordnameiA. Farrelddatej÷`hfilenamegrfc8726eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130532hkeywordsÉdIANAxIndependent Submissions StreamcISEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate_∂Í§granking"crfcd8726irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexFHow Requests for IANA Action Will Be Handled on the Independent StreamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty’JSON Web Tokens, also known as JWTs, are URL-safe JSON-based security tokens that contain a set of claims that can be signed and/or encrypted.  JWTs are being widely used and deployed as a simple security token format in numerous protocols and applications, both in the area of digital identity and in other application areas.  This Best Current Practices document updates RFC 7519 to provide actionable guidance leading to secure implementation and deployment of JWTs.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfIntuitdnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Hardt¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej÷Mhfilenamegrfc8725eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130531hkeywordsånJSON Web TokencJWTx"JSON Object Signing and EncryptiondJOSErJSON Web SignaturecJWSsJSON Web EncryptioncJWEgattacksfClaimshSecuritylCryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^MΩ•granking"crfcd8725irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber·etotaletitlex%JSON Web Token Best Current PracticesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document defines the Static Context Header Compression and fragmentation (SCHC) framework, which provides both a header compression mechanism and an optional fragmentation mechanism. SCHC has been designed with Low-Power Wide Area Networks (LPWANs) in mind.

SCHC compression is based on a common static context stored both in the LPWAN device and in the network infrastructure side. This document defines a generic header compression mechanism and its application to compress IPv6/UDP headers.

This document also specifies an optional fragmentation and reassembly mechanism. It can be used to support the IPv6 MTU requirement over the LPWAN technologies. Fragmentation is needed for IPv6 datagrams that, after SCHC compression or when such compression was not possible, still exceed the Layer 2 maximum payload size.

The SCHC header compression and fragmentation mechanisms are independent of the specific LPWAN technology over which they are used. This document defines generic functionalities and offers flexibility with regard to parameter settings and mechanism choices. This document standardizes the exchange over the LPWAN between two SCHC entities. Settings and choices specific to a technology or a product are expected to be grouped into profiles, which are specified in other documents. Data models for the context and profiles are out of scope.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfAckliodnamekA. Minaburo¢kaffiliationnIMT-AtlantiquednamejL. Toutain¢kaffiliationx%Universitat Polit√®cnica de CatalunyadnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamejD. Barthel¢kaffiliationfSIGFOXdnamejJC. Zunigaddatej÷hhfilenamegrfc8724eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymelpwandfullx.lpwan - IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdnamex&IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130530hkeywordsò"rheader compressionkcompressionmfragmentationnstatic contextjrule-basedeLPWANfLPWANsilow powerilow-powerg6LoWPANc6lofLoWPANgLoWPANscLLNdLLNscLTNcLTEeLTE-MfSigfoxgLoRaWANfNB-IOTb5GcIoTrInternet of Thingspadaptation layercUDPdIPv6cWSNnSensor networkwwireless sensor networkh802.15.4rcontrained networkpconstrained nodexconstrained-node networkkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDate^ó≈∞granking"crfcd8724irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSCHC: Generic Framework for Static Context Header Compression and FragmentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9441∂habstractyÑIn some conferencing scenarios, it is desirable for an intermediary to be able to manipulate some parameters in Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) packets, while still providing strong end-to-end security guarantees.  This document defines a cryptographic transform for the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) that uses two separate but related cryptographic operations to provide hop-by-hop and end-to-end security guarantees.  Both the end-to-end and hop-by-hop cryptographic algorithms can utilize an authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD) algorithm or take advantage of future SRTP transforms with different properties.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejA.B. Roachddatej÷^hfilenamegrfc8723eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpercdfullx(perc - Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdnamex!Privacy Enhanced RTP Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130529hkeywordsÖdPERCdSRTPcRTPlconferencingjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^´%Ωgranking"crfcd8723irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexODouble Encryption Procedures for the Secure Real-Time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty]Many Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) protocols make use of commonly defined values that are passed in messages or packets.  To ensure consistent interpretation of these values between independent implementations, there is a need to ensure that the values and associated semantic intent are uniquely defined.  The IETF uses registry functions to record assigned protocol parameter values and their associated semantic intentions.  For each IETF protocol parameter, it is current practice for the IETF to delegate the role of Protocol Parameter Registry Operator to a nominated entity.  This document provides a description of, and the requirements for, these delegated functions.  This document obsoletes RFC 6220 to replace all references to the IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA) and related structures with those defined by the IASA 2.0 Model.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej÷chfilenamegrfc8722eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130528hkeywordsÇdIANAjGovernancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X™granking"crfcd8722irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexLDefining the Role and Function of IETF Protocol Parameter Registry OperatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÛContributors grant intellectual property rights to the IETF.  The IETF Trust holds and manages those rights on behalf of the IETF.  The Trustees of the IETF Trust are responsible for that management.  This management includes granting the licenses to copy, implement, and otherwise use IETF Contributions, among them Internet-Drafts and RFCs.  The Trustees of the IETF Trust accept direction from the IETF regarding the rights to be granted.  This document describes the desires of the IETF regarding outbound rights to be granted in IETF Contributions.  This document obsoletes RFC 5377 solely for the purpose of removing references to the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC), which was part of the IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA).fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpernddatej÷^hfilenamegrfc8721eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130527hkeywordsÇdIASAeTrustkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X©granking"crfcd8721irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRAdvice to the Trustees of the IETF Trust on Rights to Be Granted in IETF DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxmThis document provides principles for the operation of Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) registries.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamejO. Kolkmanddatej÷Zhfilenamegrfc8720eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130526hkeywordsÅdIASAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X®granking"crfcd8720irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexQPrinciples for Operation of Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx|This document describes a meeting location policy for the IETF and the various stakeholders required to realize this policy.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfKaloomdnamekS. Krishnanddatej’Yhfilenamegrfc8719eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhmtgvenuedfullxmtgvenue - Meeting VenuednamemMeeting Venuedtypebwgbidjdoc-130525hkeywordsÇx geographic distribution locationdIASAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X®granking"crfcd8719irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‚etotaletitlex6High-Level Guidance for the Meeting Policy of the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9712∑habstractyrThe IETF Administration Support Activity (IASA) is responsible for arranging the selection and operation of the IETF plenary meeting venue.  This memo specifies IETF community requirements for meeting venues, including hotels and meeting space.  It also directs the IASA to make available additional process documents that describe the current meeting selection process.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Learddatej÷'hfilenamegrfc8718eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhmtgvenuedfullxmtgvenue - Meeting VenuednamemMeeting Venuedtypebwgbidjdoc-130524hkeywordsÉnMeeting VenuesxMeeting selection processdIASAkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate^Xßgranking"crfcd8718irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‚etotaletitlex,IETF Plenary Meeting Venue Selection ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9712∑habstractyeIn 2018, the IETF began the transition to a new administrative structure and updated its IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA) to a new "IASA 2.0" structure.  In addition to more substantive changes that are described in other documents, the transition to the 2018 IETF Administrative Support structure changes several position titles and organizational relationships that are referenced elsewhere.  Rather than reissue those referencing documents individually, this specification provides updates to them and deprecates some now-obsolete documents to ensure that there is no confusion due to these changes.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej÷Jhfilenamegrfc8717eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130523hkeywordsÇdIASAeIASA2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Xßgranking"crfcd8717irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbereetotaletitlexaIETF Administrative Support Activity 2.0: Consolidated Updates to IETF Administrative TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty∆The IETF Anti-Harassment Procedures are described in RFC 7776.

The IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) has been replaced by the IETF Administration LLC, and the IETF Administrative Director has been replaced by the IETF LLC Executive Director. This document updates RFC 7776 to amend these terms.

RFC 7776 contained updates to RFC 7437. RFC 8713 has incorporated those updates, so this document also updates RFC 7776 to remove those updates.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx"Episteme Technology Consulting LLCdnamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej÷_hfilenamegrfc8716eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130522hkeywordsÑjHarassmentjOmbudsteamdIAOCwIETF Administration LLCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X¶granking"crfcd8716irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlexòUpdate to the IETF Anti-Harassment Procedures for the Replacement of the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) with the IETF Administration LLCdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document captures the rationale for the changes introduced in RFC 8714, "Update to the Process for Selection of Trustees for the IETF Trust".

At the time RFC 8714 was published, the changes to the IETF Administrative Support Activity, Version 2.0 (IASA 2.0) had an impact on the IETF Trust because members of the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC), which was being phased out, had served as Trustees of the IETF Trust. This document provides background on the past IETF Trust arrangements, explains the effect of the rules in the founding documents during the transition to the new arrangement, and provides a rationale for the update.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej÷5hfilenamegrfc8715eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130521hkeywordsÑsIETF administrationuintellectual propertytleadership selectiondIASAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X•granking"crfcd8715irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlIETF Administrative Support Activity 2.0: Update to the Process for Selection of Trustees for the IETF TrustdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¸This memo updates the process for selection of Trustees for the IETF Trust. Previously, the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) members also acted as Trustees, but the IAOC has been eliminated as part of an update to the structure of the IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA). This memo specifies that the Trustees shall be selected separately.

This memo obsoletes RFC 4371. The changes relate only to the selection of Trustees. All other aspects of the IETF Trust remain as they are today.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Hardieddatejœ«hfilenamegrfc8714eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130520hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X•granking"
crfcd8714irfcNumber"
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbereetotaletitlexBUpdate to the Process for Selection of Trustees for the IETF TrustdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÍThe process by which the members of the IAB and IESG, some Trustees of the IETF Trust, and some Directors of the IETF Administration LLC (IETF LLC) are selected, confirmed, and recalled is specified in this document. This document is based on RFC 7437. Only those updates required to reflect the changes introduced by IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA) 2.0 have been included. Any other changes will be addressed in future documents.

This document obsoletes RFC 7437 and RFC 8318.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawy¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingoodddatej÷Xhfilenamegrfc8713eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130519hkeywordsÉdIASAhIASA 2.0eIASA2kobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate^X§granking"	crfcd8713irfcNumber"	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber
etotaletitlexáIAB, IESG, IETF Trust, and IETF LLC Selection, Confirmation, and Recall Process: Operation of the IETF Nominating and Recall CommitteesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9389∂habstractyÂThis document summarizes the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) - Internet Society (ISOC) relationship, following a major revision to the structure of the IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA) in 2018.  The IASA was revised under a new "IASA 2.0" structure by the IASA2 Working Group, which changed the IETF's administrative, legal, and financial structure.  As a result, it also changed the relationship between the IETF and ISOC, which made it necessary to revise RFC 2031.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingoodddatej÷]hfilenamegrfc8712eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130518hkeywordsÅdIASAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X§granking"crfcd8712irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe IETF-ISOC RelationshipdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty§The IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA) was originally established in 2005. In the years since then, the needs of the IETF evolved in ways that required changes to its administrative structure. The purpose of this RFC is to document and describe the IETF Administrative Support Activity, version 2.0 (IASA 2.0). It defines the roles and responsibilities of the IETF Administration LLC Board (IETF LLC Board), the IETF Executive Director, and the Internet Society in the fiscal and administrative support of the IETF standards process. It also defines the membership and selection rules for the IETF LLC Board.

This document obsoletes RFC 4071, RFC 4333, and RFC 7691.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxJohns Hopkins UniversitydnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamegJ. Hall¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingoodddatej÷Jhfilenamegrfc8711eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiasa2dfullx.iasa2 - IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dnamex&IETF Administrative Support Activity 2dtypebwgbidjdoc-130517hkeywordsÇdIASAeIASA2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^X¢granking"crfcd8711irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbereetotaletitlexBStructure of the IETF Administrative Support Activity, Version 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2This memo defines application/multipart-core, an application-independent media type that can be used to combine representations of zero or more different media types (each with a Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Content-Format identifier) into a single representation, with minimal framing overhead.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcARMdnamejT. Fossati¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiK. Hartke¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷`hfilenamegrfc8710eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130516hkeywordsÇdCoAPxMultipart Content-FormatkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate^Yö≤granking"crfcd8710irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMultipart Content-Format for the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¢This document describes the use of the Ed25519 and Ed448 digital signature algorithms in the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.  Accordingly, this RFC updates RFC 4253.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Harris¢kaffiliationmcyberstorm.mudnamemL. Velvindronddatejœˇhfilenamegrfc8709eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130515hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^U√granking"crfcd8709irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKEd25519 and Ed448 Public Key Algorithms for the Secure Shell (SSH) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document specifies the conventions for using the Hierarchical Signature System (HSS) / Leighton-Micali Signature (LMS) hash-based signature algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  In addition, the algorithm identifier and public key syntax are provided.  The HSS/LMS algorithm is one form of hash-based digital signature; it is described in RFC 8554.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷`hfilenamegrfc8708eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130514hkeywordsÇqdigital signatureomessage contentkobsoletedByÅd9708epagesopublicationDate^DÖOgranking"crfcd8708irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Use of the HSS/LMS Hash-Based Signature Algorithm in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document specifies an extension to the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework defining request parameters that enable a client to explicitly signal to an authorization server about the identity of the protected resource(s) to which it is requesting access.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationfYubicodnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationkArm LimiteddnamemH. Tschofenigddatej÷Lhfilenamegrfc8707eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130513hkeywordsÉeOAuthhResourcehAudiencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Y¨Ügranking"crfcd8707irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Resource Indicators for OAuth 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡This document describes a mechanism for a restarting router to signal to its neighbors that it is restarting, allowing them to reestablish their adjacencies without cycling through the DOWN state while still correctly initiating database synchronization.

This document additionally describes a mechanism for a router to signal its neighbors that it is preparing to initiate a restart while maintaining forwarding-plane state. This allows the neighbors to maintain their adjacencies until the router has restarted but also allows the neighbors to bring the adjacencies down in the event of other topology changes.

This document additionally describes a mechanism for a restarting router to determine when it has achieved Link State Protocol Data Unit (LSP) database synchronization with its neighbors and a mechanism to optimize LSP database synchronization while minimizing transient routing disruption when a router starts.

This document obsoletes RFC 5306.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Wellsddatej÷`hfilenamegrfc8706eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130512hkeywordsÇcIGPpgraceful restartkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^R6–granking"crfcd8706irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRestart Signaling for IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document describes OAuth client authentication and certificate-bound access and refresh tokens using mutual Transport Layer Security (TLS) authentication with X.509 certificates.  OAuth clients are provided a mechanism for authentication to the authorization server using mutual TLS, based on either self-signed certificates or public key infrastructure (PKI).  OAuth authorization servers are provided a mechanism for binding access tokens to a client's mutual-TLS certificate, and OAuth protected resources are provided a method for ensuring that such an access token presented to it was issued to the client presenting the token.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationfYubicodnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationxNomura Research InstitutednamekN. Sakimura¢kaffiliationjYES.com AGdnamenT. Lodderstedtddatej÷_hfilenamegrfc8705eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130511hkeywordsénJSON Web TokencJWTdMTLSjMutual TLSsproof-of-possessionx proof-of-possession access tokenxkey confirmed access tokenxcertificate-bound access tokenrclient certificatex'X.509 Client Certificate Authenticationpkey confirmationsconfirmation methodmholder-of-keyeOAuthkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Y¨Ögranking"crfcd8705irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNOAuth 2.0 Mutual-TLS Client Authentication and Certificate-Bound Access TokensdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÛThis document identifies a need for and proposes improvement of the unicast Reverse Path Forwarding (uRPF) techniques (see RFC 3704) for detection and mitigation of source address spoofing (see BCP 38).  Strict uRPF is inflexible about directionality, the loose uRPF is oblivious to directionality, and the current feasible-path uRPF attempts to strike a balance between the two (see RFC 3704).  However, as shown in this document, the existing feasible-path uRPF still has shortcomings.  This document describes enhanced feasible-path uRPF (EFP-uRPF) techniques that are more flexible (in a meaningful way) about directionality than the feasible-path uRPF (RFC 3704).  The proposed EFP-uRPF methods aim to significantly reduce false positives regarding invalid detection in source address validation (SAV).  Hence, they can potentially alleviate ISPs' concerns about the possibility of disrupting service for their customers and encourage greater deployment of uRPF techniques.  This document updates RFC 3704.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhUSA NISTdnameiK. Sriram¢kaffiliationhUSA NISTdnamemD. Montgomery¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamegJ. Haasddatej÷Lhfilenamegrfc8704eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-130510hkeywordsãcBGPwsource address spoofingxsource address validationcSAVwReverse Path ForwardingcRPFkunicast RPFduRPFoDDoS mitigationfBCP 38fBCP 84kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^=¶Wgranking"␀crfcd8704irfcNumber"␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberTetotaletitlex6Enhanced Feasible-Path Unicast Reverse Path ForwardingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıThe Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) is a protocol for modems to advertise the status of wireless links between reachable destinations to attached routers. The core specification of the protocol (RFC 8175) assumes that every modem in the radio network has an attached DLEP router and requires that the Media Access Control (MAC) address of the DLEP interface on the attached router be used to identify the destination in the network, for purposes of reporting the state and quality of the link to that destination.

This document describes a DLEP extension that allows modems that do not meet the strict requirement above to use DLEP to describe link availability and quality to one or more destinations reachable beyond a device on the Layer 2 domain.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvAirbus Defence & SpacednameiR. Taylor¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Ratliffddatej÷_hfilenamegrfc8703eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130509hkeywordsÑdDLEPeMANETjLink-AwarekRadio-AwarekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate^Cè4granking!ˇcrfcd8703irfcNumber!ˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) Link Identifier ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†This document updates the "Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Algorithms" (RFC 3370) and describes the conventions for using the SHAKE family of hash functions in the Cryptographic Message Syntax as one-way hash functions with the RSA Probabilistic Signature Scheme (RSASSA-PSS) and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA).  The conventions for the associated signer public keys in CMS are also described.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegQ. Dangddatej÷Mhfilenamegrfc8702eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130508hkeywordsÉmSHAKEs in CMSeSHAKEoCMS with SHAKEskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^4≤Ωgranking!˛crfcd8702irfcNumber!˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQUse of the SHAKE One-Way Hash Functions in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes GREASE (Generate Random Extensions And Sustain Extensibility), a mechanism to prevent extensibility failures in the TLS ecosystem.  It reserves a set of TLS protocol values that may be advertised to ensure peers correctly handle unknown values.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekD. Benjaminddatej÷]hfilenamegrfc8701eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130507hkeywordsÇcTLSfGREASEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^1Ïogranking!˝crfcd8701irfcNumber!˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Applying Generate Random Extensions And Sustain Extensibility (GREASE) to TLS ExtensibilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyiThis RFC marks the fiftieth anniversary for the RFC Series.  It includes both retrospective material from individuals involved at key inflection points as well as a review of the current state of affairs.  It concludes with thoughts on possibilities for the next fifty years for the Series.  This document updates the perspectives offered in RFCs 2555 and 5540.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekH. Flanaganddatej÷Lhfilenamegrfc8700eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130506hkeywordsÉgHistoryjRFC SeriesmRetrospectivekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^)Øgranking!¸crfcd8700irfcNumber!¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlesFifty Years of RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜWhen multiple congestion-controlled Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) sessions traverse the same network bottleneck, combining their controls can improve the total on-the-wire behavior in terms of delay, loss, and fairness.  This document describes such a method for flows that have the same sender, in a way that is as flexible and simple as possible while minimizing the number of changes needed to existing RTP applications.  This document also specifies how to apply the method for the Network-Assisted Dynamic Adaptation (NADA) congestion control algorithm and provides suggestions on how to apply it to other congestion control algorithms.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehS. Islam¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamekS. Gjessingddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8699eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130505hkeywordsÅctcpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^4f[granking!˚crfcd8699irfcNumber!˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Coupled Congestion Control for RTP MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document describes Network-Assisted Dynamic Adaptation (NADA), a novel congestion control scheme for interactive real-time media applications such as video conferencing.  In the proposed scheme, the sender regulates its sending rate, based on either implicit or explicit congestion signaling, in a unified approach.  The scheme can benefit from Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) markings from network nodes.  It also maintains consistent sender behavior in the absence of such markings by reacting to queuing delays and packet losses instead.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefX. Zhu¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamefR. Pan¢kaffiliationmAcousticCommsdnamejM. Ramalho¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegS. Menaddatej÷^hfilenamegrfc8698eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130504hkeywordsÇjMultimediarCongestion ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^<granking!˙crfcd8698irfcNumber!˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcNetwork-Assisted Dynamic Adaptation (NADA): A Unified Congestion Control Scheme for Real-Time MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This document introduces a generic mechanism to create a grouping of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in the context of a Path Computation Element (PCE).  This grouping can then be used to define associations between sets of LSPs or between a set of LSPs and a set of attributes (such as configuration parameters or behaviors), and it is equally applicable to the stateful PCE (active and passive modes) and the stateless PCE.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnameiE. Crabbe¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamerH. Ananthakrishnan¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnameiY. Tanakaddatej÷\hfilenamegrfc8697eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130503hkeywordsÑcPCEdPCEPkAssociationeGroupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^4zçgranking!˘crfcd8697irfcNumber!˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexåPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Establishing Relationships between Sets of Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractyRThe invention of a large-scale quantum computer would pose a serious challenge for the cryptographic algorithms that are widely deployed today.  The Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) supports key transport and key agreement algorithms that could be broken by the invention of such a quantum computer.  By storing communications that are protected with the CMS today, someone could decrypt them in the future when a large-scale quantum computer becomes available.  Once quantum-secure key management algorithms are available, the CMS will be extended to support the new algorithms if the existing syntax does not accommodate them.  This document describes a mechanism to protect today's communication from the future invention of a large-scale quantum computer by mixing the output of key transport and key agreement algorithms with a pre-shared key.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷]hfilenamegrfc8696eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130502hkeywordsÅqquantum-resistantkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]˚¿granking!¯crfcd8696irfcNumber!¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDUsing Pre-Shared Key (PSK) in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes a data model for the management of the Routing Information Protocol (RIP). Both RIP version 2 and RIPng are covered. The data model includes definitions for configuration, operational state, and Remote Procedure Calls (RPCs).

The YANG data model in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehP. Sarda¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamelV. Choudharyddatej’Çhfilenamegrfc8695eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130501hkeywordsádYANGcRIPeRIPngjdata modelhietf-riprnetwork managementgroutingkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate^PÀgranking!˜crfcd8695irfcNumber!˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<A YANG Data Model for the Routing Information Protocol (RIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThe Path Computation Element (PCE) may be used for computing services that traverse multi-area and multi-Autonomous System (multi-AS) Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic-Engineered (TE) networks.

This document examines the applicability of the PCE architecture, protocols, and protocol extensions for computing multi-area and multi-AS paths in MPLS and GMPLS networks.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehH. Zhengddatejœˆhfilenamegrfc8694eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130500hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]˙ˇgranking!ˆcrfcd8694irfcNumber!ˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkApplicability of the Path Computation Element to Inter-area and Inter-AS MPLS and GMPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¸This specification defines a protocol for an HTTP- and JSON-based Security Token Service (STS) by defining how to request and obtain security tokens from OAuth 2.0 authorization servers, including security tokens employing impersonation and delegation.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejA. Nadalin¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationfYubicodnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationdVisadnamelC. Mortimoreddatej÷Yhfilenamegrfc8693eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130499hkeywordsânJSON Web TokencJWTjDelegationmImpersonationcSTSvSecurity Token ServicehExchangeeTokeneOAuthkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^ôΩgranking!ıcrfcd8693irfcNumber!ıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOAuth 2.0 Token ExchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfDigital signatures are used to sign messages, X.509 certificates, and Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs).  This document updates the "Algorithms and Identifiers for the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Profile" (RFC 3279) and describes the conventions for using the SHAKE function family in Internet X.509 certificates and revocation lists as one-way hash functions with the RSA Probabilistic signature and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) signature algorithms.  The conventions for the associated subject public keys are also described.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegQ. Dangddatej÷[hfilenamegrfc8692eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130498hkeywordsÉnSHAKE in X.509nSHAKEs in PKIXxcertificates with SHAKE hasheskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÒoNgranking!Ùcrfcd8692irfcNumber!ÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure: Additional Algorithm Identifiers for RSASSA-PSS and ECDSA Using SHAKEsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis document provides methods and settings for using IPv6 to communicate among nodes within range of one another over a single IEEE 802.11-OCB link.  Support for these methods and settings require minimal changes to existing stacks.  This document also describes limitations associated with using these methods.  Optimizations and usage of IPv6 over more complex scenarios are not covered in this specification and are a subject for future work.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx"Moulay Ismail University of MeknesdnamejN. Benamar¢kaffiliationgEURECOMdnameiJ. H√§rri¢kaffiliationtSangmyung UniversitydnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationfYoGoKodnamehT. Ernstddatej÷Thfilenamegrfc8691eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfipwavedfullx5ipwave - IP Wireless Access in Vehicular Environmentsdnamex,IP Wireless Access in Vehicular Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130497hkeywordsÉqIPv6 over 802.11pcOCBtIPv6 over 802.11-OCBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^Ligranking!Ûcrfcd8691irfcNumber!ÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiBasic Support for IPv6 Networks Operating Outside the Context of a Basic Service Set over IEEE Std 802.11dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 8287 defines the extensions to perform LSP Ping and Traceroute for Segment Routing IGP-Prefix and IGP-Adjacency Segment Identifiers (SIDs) with the MPLS data plane. RFC 8287 proposes three Target Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) Stack sub-TLVs. While RFC 8287 defines the format and procedure to handle those sub-TLVs, it does not sufficiently clarify how the length of the Segment ID sub-TLVs should be computed to be included in the Length field of the sub-TLVs. This ambiguity has resulted in interoperability issues.

This document updates RFC 8287 by clarifying the length of each of the Segment ID sub-TLVs defined in RFC 8287.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiN. Nainar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamehF. Iqbal¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamemA. Vainshteinddatej÷Lhfilenamegrfc8690eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130496hkeywordsÅdmplskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Ò=granking!Úcrfcd8690irfcNumber!ÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Clarification of Segment ID Sub-TLV Length for RFC 8287dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/The SMTP STARTTLS option, used in negotiating transport-level encryption of SMTP connections, is not as useful from a security standpoint as it might be because of its opportunistic nature; message delivery is, by default, prioritized over security.  This document describes an SMTP service extension, REQUIRETLS, and a message header field, TLS-Required.  If the REQUIRETLS option or TLS-Required message header field is used when sending a message, it asserts a request on the part of the message sender to override the default negotiation of TLS, either by requiring that TLS be negotiated when the message is relayed or by requesting that recipient-side policy mechanisms such as MTA-STS and DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) be ignored when relaying a message for which security is unimportant.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpAltmode NetworksdnameiJ. Fentonddatej÷\hfilenamegrfc8689eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130495hkeywordsÅdSMTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ﬁ’egranking!Òcrfcd8689irfcNumber!ÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewSMTP Require TLS OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·This document defines the 608 (Rejected) Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) response code.  This response code enables calling parties to learn that an intermediary rejected their call attempt.  No one will deliver, and thus answer, the call.  As a 6xx code, the caller will be aware that future attempts to contact the same User Agent Server will likely fail.  The initial use case driving the need for the 608 response code is when the intermediary is an analytics engine.  In this case, the rejection is by a machine or other process.  This contrasts with the 607 (Unwanted) SIP response code in which a human at the target User Agent Server indicates the user did not want the call.  In some jurisdictions, this distinction is important.  This document also defines the use of the Call-Info header field in 608 responses to enable rejected callers to contact entities that blocked their calls in error.  This provides a remediation mechanism for legal callers that find their calls blocked.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuGeorgetown UniversitydnamekE.W. Burger¢kaffiliationx%Massachusetts Institute of TechnologydnamehB. Nagdaddatej÷Xhfilenamegrfc8688eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130494hkeywordsÉdSTIRgSIPCOREdIANAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Ëîøgranking!crfcd8688irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Response Code for Rejected CallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇWhen using Traffic Engineering (TE) in a dual-stack IPv4/IPv6 network, the Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) TE Label Switched Path (LSP) infrastructure may be duplicated, even if the destination IPv4 and IPv6 addresses belong to the same remote router. In order to achieve an integrated MPLS TE LSP infrastructure, OSPF routes must be computed over MPLS TE tunnels created using information propagated in another OSPF instance. This issue is solved by advertising cross-address family (X-AF) OSPF TE information.

This document describes an update to RFC 5786 that allows for the easy identification of a router's local X-AF IP addresses.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Smirnov¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Barnesddatej÷^hfilenamegrfc8687eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130493hkeywordsÖdOSPFdIPv4dIPv6bTEdMPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ﬁ≈∂granking!Ôcrfcd8687irfcNumber!ÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBOSPF Routing with Cross-Address Family Traffic Engineering TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThe goal of Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) is to provide guidance to applications that have to select one or several hosts from a set of candidates capable of providing a desired resource. ALTO is realized by a client-server protocol. Before an ALTO client can ask for guidance, it needs to discover one or more ALTO servers that can provide suitable guidance.

In some deployment scenarios, in particular if the information about the network topology is partitioned and distributed over several ALTO servers, it may be necessary to discover an ALTO server outside of the ALTO client's own network domain, in order to get appropriate guidance. This document details applicable scenarios, itemizes requirements, and specifies a procedure for ALTO cross-domain server discovery.

Technically, the procedure specified in this document takes one IP address or prefix and a U-NAPTR Service Parameter (typically, "ALTO:https") as parameters. It performs DNS lookups (for NAPTR resource records in the "in-addr.arpa." or "ip6.arpa." trees) and returns one or more URIs of information resources related to that IP address or prefix.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnameiS. Kiesel¢kaffiliationdH-DAdnamenM. Stiemerlingddatej÷]hfilenamegrfc8686eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130492hkeywordsÉx-Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO)x"ALTO cross-domain server discoveryx!ALTO third-party server discoverykobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate^<é8granking!Ócrfcd8686irfcNumber!ÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKApplication-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Cross-Domain Server DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThe Hierarchical Path Computation Element (H-PCE) architecture is defined in RFC 6805. It provides a mechanism to derive an optimum end-to-end path in a multi-domain environment by using a hierarchical relationship between domains to select the optimum sequence of domains and optimum paths across those domains.

This document defines extensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) to support H-PCE procedures.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnamekR. Casellas¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. Kingddatej÷Qhfilenamegrfc8685eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130491hkeywordsÉsTraffic EngineeringlInter-domainlMulti-domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÚÃîgranking!Ìcrfcd8685irfcNumber!ÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for the Hierarchical Path Computation Element (H-PCE) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractyãTCP/IP communication is currently restricted to a single path per connection, yet multiple paths often exist between peers. The simultaneous use of these multiple paths for a TCP/IP session would improve resource usage within the network and thus improve user experience through higher throughput and improved resilience to network failure.

Multipath TCP provides the ability to simultaneously use multiple paths between peers. This document presents a set of extensions to traditional TCP to support multipath operation. The protocol offers the same type of service to applications as TCP (i.e., a reliable bytestream), and it provides the components necessary to establish and use multiple TCP flows across potentially disjoint paths.

This document specifies v1 of Multipath TCP, obsoleting v0 as specified in RFC 6824, through clarifications and modifications primarily driven by deployment experience.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationePexipdnamegA. Ford¢kaffiliationxU. Politehnica of BucharestdnameiC. Raiciu¢kaffiliationqU. College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationxU. catholique de LouvaindnamenO. Bonaventure¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiC. Paaschddatej÷[hfilenamegrfc8684eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130490hkeywordsÖctcpjextensionsimultipathjmultihomedgsubflowkobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDate^Ç»Ögranking!Ïcrfcd8684irfcNumber!ÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>TCP Extensions for Multipath Operation with Multiple AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔThis document describes how Network Address and Protocol Translation from IPv6 Clients to IPv4 Servers (NAT64) (including 464XLAT) can be deployed in an IPv6 network -- whether it's cellular ISP, broadband ISP, or enterprise -- and the possible optimizations.  This document also discusses issues to be considered when having IPv6-only connectivity, such as: a) DNS64, b) applications or devices that use literal IPv4 addresses or non-IPv6-compliant APIs, and c) IPv4-only hosts or applications.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpThe IPv6 CompanydnameqJ. Palet Martinezddatej÷Whfilenamegrfc8683eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130489hkeywordsàdIPv6fDNSSECeNAT64eDNS64g464XLATdCLATeNAT46dPLATkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDate]›ñ"granking!Îcrfcd8683irfcNumber!ÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVAdditional Deployment Guidelines for NAT64/464XLAT in Operator and Enterprise NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document describes the TinyMT32 Pseudorandom Number Generator (PRNG), which produces 32-bit pseudorandom unsigned integers and aims at having a simple-to-use and deterministic solution.  This PRNG is a small-sized variant of the Mersenne Twister (MT) PRNG.  The main advantage of TinyMT32 over MT is the use of a small internal state, compatible with most target platforms that include embedded devices, while keeping reasonably good randomness that represents a significant improvement compared to the Park-Miller Linear Congruential PRNG.  However, neither the TinyMT nor MT PRNG is meant to be used for cryptographic applications.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtHiroshima UniversitydnamehM. Saito¢kaffiliationtHiroshima UniversitydnamelM. Matsumoto¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamekE. Baccelliddatejœhfilenamegrfc8682eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130488hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^PÙgranking!Ícrfcd8682irfcNumber!ÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-TinyMT32 Pseudorandom Number Generator (PRNG)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes two fully specified Forward Erasure Correction (FEC) Schemes for Sliding Window Random Linear Codes (RLC), one for RLC over the Galois Field (a.k.a., Finite Field) GF(2), a second one for RLC over the Galois Field GF(2^8), each time with the possibility of controlling the code density.  They can protect arbitrary media streams along the lines defined by FECFRAME extended to Sliding Window FEC Codes.  These Sliding Window FEC Codes rely on an encoding window that slides over the source symbols, generating new repair symbols whenever needed.  Compared to block FEC codes, these Sliding Window FEC Codes offer key advantages with real-time flows in terms of reduced FEC-related latency while often providing improved packet erasure recovery capabilities.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamehB. Teibiddatej÷Vhfilenamegrfc8681eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130487hkeywordsÖcRLCcFEChFECFRAMEtpacket loss recoverykreliabilitykobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDate^PÛgranking!Ècrfcd8681irfcNumber!ÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Sliding Window Random Linear Code (RLC) Forward Erasure Correction (FEC) Schemes for FECFRAMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫RFC 6363 describes a framework for using Forward Error Correction (FEC) codes to provide protection against packet loss.  The framework supports applying FEC to arbitrary packet flows over unreliable transport and is primarily intended for real-time, or streaming, media.  However, FECFRAME as per RFC 6363 is restricted to block FEC codes.  This document updates RFC 6363 to support FEC codes based on a sliding encoding window, in addition to block FEC codes, in a backward-compatible way.  During multicast/broadcast real-time content delivery, the use of sliding window codes significantly improves robustness in harsh environments, with less repair traffic and lower FEC-related added latency.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationoNetworked MediadnamehA. Begenddatej÷Vhfilenamegrfc8680eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130486hkeywordsÖcFEChFECFRAMEtpacket loss recoverycRLCxSliding Window FEC CodeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate^PÁgranking!Ëcrfcd8680irfcNumber!ËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJForward Error Correction (FEC) Framework Extension to Sliding Window CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document specifies a fast reroute framework for protecting IP/MPLS services and MPLS transport tunnels against egress node and egress link failures.  For each type of egress failure, it defines the roles of Point of Local Repair (PLR), protector, and backup egress router and the procedures of establishing a bypass tunnel from a PLR to a protector.  It describes the behaviors of these routers in handling an egress failure, including local repair on the PLR and context-based forwarding on the protector.  The framework can be used to develop egress protection mechanisms to reduce traffic loss before global repair reacts to an egress failure and control-plane protocols converge on the topology changes due to the egress failure.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegY. Shen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemM. Jeganathan¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationkRtBrick IncdnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiC. Michel¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamegH. Chenddatej÷\hfilenamegrfc8679eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130485hkeywordsÉlfast rerouteqegress protectionllocal repairkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÍŒ1granking!Ácrfcd8679irfcNumber!ÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex MPLS Egress Protection FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyConnecting an enterprise site to multiple ISPs over IPv6 using provider-assigned addresses is difficult without the use of some form of Network Address Translation (NAT). Much has been written on this topic over the last 10 to 15 years, but it still remains a problem without a clearly defined or widely implemented solution. Any multihoming solution without NAT requires hosts at the site to have addresses from each ISP and to select the egress ISP by selecting a source address for outgoing packets. It also requires routers at the site to take into account those source addresses when forwarding packets out towards the ISPs.

This document examines currently available mechanisms for providing a solution to this problem for a broad range of enterprise topologies. It covers the behavior of routers to forward traffic by taking into account source address, and it covers the behavior of hosts to select appropriate default source addresses. It also covers any possible role that routers might play in providing information to hosts to help them select appropriate source addresses. In the process of exploring potential solutions, this document also makes explicit requirements for how the solution would be expected to behave from the perspective of an enterprise site network administrator.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiC. Bowers¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. LinkovaddatejœÚhfilenamegrfc8678eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130484hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate]˚Àgranking!Êcrfcd8678irfcNumber!ÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|Enterprise Multihoming using Provider-Assigned IPv6 Addresses without Network Prefix Translation: Requirements and SolutionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty˛Adoption of cloud and fog technology allows operators to deploy a single "Service Function" (SF) to multiple "execution locations". The decision to steer traffic to a specific location may change frequently based on load, proximity, etc. Under the current Service Function Chaining (SFC) framework, steering traffic dynamically to the different execution endpoints requires a specific "rechaining", i.e., a change in the service function path reflecting the different IP endpoints to be used for the new execution points. This procedure may be complex and take time. In order to simplify rechaining and reduce the time to complete the procedure, we discuss separating the logical Service Function Path (SFP) from the specific execution endpoints. This can be done by identifying the SFs using a name rather than a routable IP endpoint (or Layer 2 address). This document describes the necessary extensions, additional functions, and protocol details in the Service Function Forwarder (SFF) to handle name-based relationships.

This document presents InterDigital's approach to name-based SFC. It does not represent IETF consensus and is presented here so that the SFC community may benefit from considering this mechanism and the possibility of its use in the edge data centers.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwInterDigital Europe LtddnamejD. Trossen¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnamenD. Purkayastha¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnameiA. Rahmanddatej÷Mhfilenamegrfc8677eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130483hkeywordsçpservice functionbSFcSFFdnSFFcSFCcSFPcNSHdFQDNb5GeNSSAIdCCNFdNSSFd3GPPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]“nìgranking!Âcrfcd8677irfcNumber!ÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexiName-Based Service Function Forwarder (nSFF) Component within a Service Function Chaining (SFC) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document defines YANG modules for the configuration and operation of IPv4-in-IPv6 softwire Border Relays and Customer Premises Equipment for the Lightweight 4over6, Mapping of Address and Port with Encapsulation (MAP-E), and Mapping of Address and Port using Translation (MAP-T) softwire mechanisms.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷Dhfilenamegrfc8676eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-130482hkeywordsãcA+Poaddress sharinghport setjPort rangewIPv4 service continuitygNETCONFhRESTCONFoProgrammabilitytDynamic provisioningjautomationdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDate]—{granking!‰crfcd8676irfcNumber!‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?YANG Modules for IPv4-in-IPv6 Address plus Port (A+P) SoftwiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document specifies the initial version of a YANG module "iana-tunnel-type", which contains a collection of IANA-maintained YANG identities used as interface types for tunnel interfaces. The module reflects the "tunnelType" registry maintained by IANA. The latest revision of this YANG module can be obtained from the IANA website.

Tunnel type values are not directly added to the Tunnel Interface Types YANG module; they must instead be added to the "tunnelType" IANA registry. Once a new tunnel type registration is made by IANA for a new tunneling scheme or even an existing one that is not already listed in the current registry (e.g., LISP, NSH), IANA will update the Tunnel Interface Types YANG module accordingly.

Some of the IETF-defined tunneling techniques are not listed in the current IANA registry. It is not the intent of this document to update the existing IANA registry with a comprehensive list of tunnel technologies. Registrants must follow the IETF registration procedure for interface types whenever a new tunnel type is needed.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehR. Asatiddatej÷Uhfilenamegrfc8675eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-130481hkeywordsÜhsoftwirenAugment tunnelqtunnel managementstunnel provisioningqtunnel activationqtunnel automationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]—xgranking!„crfcd8675irfcNumber!„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,A YANG Data Model for Tunnel Interface TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty≥This specification defines a preference for HTTP requests that expresses a desire to avoid objectionable content, according to the definition of that term by the origin server.

This specification does not define a precise semantic for "safe". Rather, the term is interpreted by the server and within the scope of each web site that chooses to act upon this information.

Support for this preference by clients and servers is optional.fadNamekBarry LeibagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷^hfilenamegrfc8674eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130480hkeywordsÉdsafejpreferencepchild-protectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Ëîºgranking!‚crfcd8674irfcNumber!‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexThe "safe" HTTP PreferencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(To accommodate byte-range requests for content that has data appended over time, this document defines semantics that allow an HTTP client and a server to perform byte-range GET and HEAD requests that start at an arbitrary byte offset within the representation and end at an indeterminate offset.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. Pratt¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamejD. Thakore¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Starkddatej÷Zhfilenamegrfc8673eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130479hkeywordsÖdhttperangedunitdlivekaggregationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate]ﬁ≈Ægranking!·crfcd8673irfcNumber!·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#HTTP Random Access and Live ContentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty˜Misissued public-key certificates can prevent TLS clients from appropriately authenticating the TLS server. Several alternatives have been proposed to detect this situation and prevent a client from establishing a TLS session with a TLS end point authenticated with an illegitimate public-key certificate. These mechanisms are either not widely deployed or limited to public web browsing.

This document proposes experimental extensions to TLS with opaque pinning tickets as a way to pin the server's identity. During an initial TLS session, the server provides an original encrypted pinning ticket. In subsequent TLS session establishment, upon receipt of the pinning ticket, the server proves its ability to decrypt the pinning ticket and thus the ownership of the pinning protection key. The client can now safely conclude that the TLS session is established with the same TLS server as the original TLS session. One of the important properties of this proposal is that no manual management actions are required.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfIntuitdnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migaultddatej÷Thfilenamegrfc8672eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130478hkeywordsÅxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ªQFgranking!‡crfcd8672irfcNumber!‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex(TLS Server Identity Pinning with TicketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP) only defines access to the Adj-RIB-In Routing Information Bases (RIBs).  This document updates BMP (RFC 7854) by adding access to the Adj-RIB-Out RIBs.  It also adds a new flag to the peer header to distinguish between Adj-RIB-In and Adj-RIB-Out.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Evens¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Bayraktar¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamejP. Lucente¢kaffiliationgTencentdnameeP. Mi¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Zhuangddatej÷]hfilenamegrfc8671eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130477hkeywordsÅkadj-rib-outkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate]¬h¸granking!ﬂcrfcd8671irfcNumber!ﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Support for Adj-RIB-Out in the BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9736∂habstractxÂThis document describes the motivation for, and benefits of, applying Segment Routing (SR) in BGP-based large-scale data centers.  It describes the design to deploy SR in those data centers for both the MPLS and IPv6 data planes.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamehG. Dawra¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehE. Aries¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnamekP. Lapukhovddatej÷9hfilenamegrfc8670eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130476hkeywordsÑbSRdMSDCbDCdSRGBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Í◊Ãgranking!ﬁcrfcd8670irfcNumber!ﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.BGP Prefix Segment in Large-Scale Data CentersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕSegment Routing (SR) leverages the source-routing paradigm. A node steers a packet through an ordered list of instructions called "segments". A segment can represent any instruction, topological or service based. The ingress node prepends an SR header to a packet containing a set of segment identifiers (SIDs). Each SID represents a topological or service-based instruction. Per-flow state is maintained only on the ingress node of the SR domain. An "SR domain" is defined as a single administrative domain for global SID assignment.

This document defines an optional, transitive BGP attribute for announcing information about BGP Prefix Segment Identifiers (BGP Prefix-SIDs) and the specification for SR-MPLS SIDs.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliation`dnamenA. Sreekantiah¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. Gredlerddatej’Rhfilenamegrfc8669eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130475hkeywordsÜbSRdMPLScBGPjPrefix-SIDkLabel-IndexdSRGBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Í◊Àgranking!›crfcd8669irfcNumber!›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Segment Routing Prefix Segment Identifier Extensions for BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThere are deployments where the Layer 3 interface on which IS-IS operates is a Layer 2 interface bundle. Existing IS-IS advertisements only support advertising link attributes of the Layer 3 interface. If entities external to IS-IS wish to control traffic flows on the individual physical links that comprise the Layer 2 interface bundle, link attribute information about the bundle members is required.

This document introduces the ability for IS-IS to advertise the link attributes of Layer 2 (L2) Bundle Members.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekA. Bashandy¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejM. Nanduri¢kaffiliationkArrcus Inc.dnamehE. AriesddatejœÖhfilenamegrfc8668eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130474hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Í◊ granking!‹crfcd8668irfcNumber!‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Advertising Layer 2 Bundle Member Link Attributes in IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}Segment Routing (SR) allows for a flexible definition of end-to-end paths within IGP topologies by encoding paths as sequences of topological sub-paths, called "segments". These segments are advertised by the link-state routing protocols (IS-IS and OSPF).

This document describes the IS-IS extensions that need to be introduced for Segment Routing operating on an MPLS data plane.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamekA. Bashandy¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraeneddatej÷Qhfilenamegrfc8667eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130473hkeywordsÜdMPLScSIDcIGPeIS-ISsLabel advertisementoSegment RoutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Í◊…granking!€crfcd8667irfcNumber!€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$IS-IS Extensions for Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_Segment Routing (SR) allows a flexible definition of end-to-end paths within IGP topologies by encoding paths as sequences of topological subpaths called "segments". These segments are advertised by the link-state routing protocols (IS-IS and OSPF).

This document describes the OSPFv3 extensions required for Segment Routing with the MPLS data plane.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Prevididdatej÷Ahfilenamegrfc8666eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130472hkeywordsÜdMPLScSIDcIGPdOSPFsLabel advertisementoSegment RoutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Í◊ƒgranking!⁄crfcd8666irfcNumber!⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%OSPFv3 Extensions for Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFSegment Routing (SR) allows a flexible definition of end-to-end paths within IGP topologies by encoding paths as sequences of topological subpaths called "segments". These segments are advertised by the link-state routing protocols (IS-IS and OSPF).

This document describes the OSPFv2 extensions required for Segment Routing.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiR. Shakir¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej’8hfilenamegrfc8665eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130471hkeywordsÜdMPLScSIDcIGPdOSPFsLabel advertisementoSegment RoutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÍŒ/granking!Ÿcrfcd8665irfcNumber!ŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#OSPF Extensions for Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌSegment Routing (SR) enables any head-end node to select any path without relying on a hop-by-hop signaling technique (e.g., LDP or RSVP-TE). It depends only on "segments" that are advertised by link-state Interior Gateway Protocols (IGPs). An SR path can be derived from a variety of mechanisms, including an IGP Shortest Path Tree (SPT), an explicit configuration, or a Path Computation Element (PCE). This document specifies extensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) that allow a stateful PCE to compute and initiate Traffic-Engineering (TE) paths, as well as a Path Computation Client (PCC) to request a path subject to certain constraints and optimization criteria in SR networks.

This document updates RFC 8408.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationsMetaswitch NetworksdnamekJ. Hardwickddatej÷;hfilenamegrfc8664eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130470hkeywordsÉbSRsTraffic-EngineeringcPCEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÍŒ-granking!ÿcrfcd8664irfcNumber!ÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstractyŸMPLS Segment Routing (SR-MPLS) is a method of source routing a packet through an MPLS data plane by imposing a stack of MPLS labels on the packet to specify the path together with any packet-specific instructions to be executed on it. SR-MPLS can be leveraged to realize a source-routing mechanism across MPLS, IPv4, and IPv6 data planes by using an MPLS label stack as a source-routing instruction set while making no changes to SR-MPLS specifications and interworking with SR-MPLS implementations.

This document describes how SR-MPLS-capable routers and IP-only routers can seamlessly coexist and interoperate through the use of SR-MPLS label stacks and IP encapsulation/tunneling such as MPLS-over-UDP as defined in RFC 7510.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkAlibaba IncdnameeX. Xu¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiS. Hassan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeZ. Liddatej÷Phfilenamegrfc8663eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130469hkeywordsÇgSR-MPLSkMPLS-in-UDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÍŒ,granking!◊crfcd8663irfcNumber!◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMPLS Segment Routing over IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄSegment Routing (SR) leverages the source-routing paradigm.  A node steers a packet through an ordered list of instructions, called segments.  Segment Routing can be applied to the Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) data plane.  Entropy labels (ELs) are used in MPLS to improve load-balancing.  This document examines and describes how ELs are to be applied to Segment Routing MPLS.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliation`dnamegS. Kini¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Shakir¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej÷,hfilenamegrfc8662eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130468hkeywordsÇxFlow-aware load balancingxECMP (equal-cost multipath)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÍŒ*granking!÷crfcd8662irfcNumber!÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFEntropy Label for Source Packet Routing in Networking (SPRING) TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvA Segment Routing (SR) node steers a packet through a controlled set of instructions, called segments, by prepending the packet with an SR header. A segment can represent any instruction, topological or service based. SR allows enforcing a flow through any topological path while maintaining per-flow state only at the ingress node to the SR domain.

The Segment Routing architecture can be directly applied to the MPLS data plane with no change in the forwarding plane. This document describes how Segment Routing MPLS operates in a network where LDP is deployed and in the case where SR-capable and non-SR-capable nodes coexist.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamekA. Bashandy¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowskiddatej÷1hfilenamegrfc8661eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130467hkeywordsÅgSR-MPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÍŒ)granking!’crfcd8661irfcNumber!’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Segment Routing MPLS Interworking with LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywSegment Routing (SR) leverages the source-routing paradigm.  A node steers a packet through a controlled set of instructions, called segments, by prepending the packet with an SR header.  In the MPLS data plane, the SR header is instantiated through a label stack.  This document specifies the forwarding behavior to allow instantiating SR over the MPLS data plane (SR-MPLS).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamekA. Bashandy¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Shakirddatej÷Phfilenamegrfc8660eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130466hkeywordsÇbSRgSR-MPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ÍŒ%granking!‘crfcd8660irfcNumber!‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Segment Routing with the MPLS Data PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘The Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) DNS Resource Record allows a DNS domain name holder to specify one or more Certification Authorities (CAs) authorized to issue certificates for that domain name. CAA Resource Records allow a public CA to implement additional controls to reduce the risk of unintended certificate mis-issue. This document defines the syntax of the CAA record and rules for processing CAA records by CAs.

This document obsoletes RFC 6844.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtVenture CryptographydnameoP. Hallam-Baker¢kaffiliationgSectigodnamelR. Stradling¢kaffiliationmLet's EncryptdnamerJ. Hoffman-Andrewsddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8659eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130465hkeywordsÜkcertificatebcacpkieissuehissuancehwildcardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]‘Ìigranking!”crfcd8659irfcNumber!”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?DNS Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) Resource RecorddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫IPv4-over-IPv6 transition mechanisms provide IPv4 connectivity services over IPv6 native networks during the IPv4/IPv6 coexistence period. DHCPv6 options have been defined to configure clients for Lightweight 4over6, Mapping of Address and Port with Encapsulation (MAP-E), Mapping of Address and Port using Translation (MAP-T) unicast softwire mechanisms, and multicast softwires. However, in many networks, configuration information is stored in an Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) server, which utilizes the Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) protocol to provide centralized management for users. When a new transition mechanism is developed, new RADIUS attributes need to be defined correspondingly.

This document defines new RADIUS attributes to carry softwire configuration parameters based on Address plus Port from a AAA server to a Broadband Network Gateway. Both unicast and multicast attributes are covered.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnameeY. Fu¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefC. Xie¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷Vhfilenamegrfc8658eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-130464hkeywordsâoIPv6 TransitioneMAP-EeMAP-TrLightweight 4over6fRADIUSoaddress sharingmauthorizationcAAAlprovisioningkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate]…˘Tgranking!“crfcd8658irfcNumber!“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJRADIUS Attributes for Softwire Mechanisms Based on Address plus Port (A+P)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThe Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) DNS record allows a domain to communicate an issuance policy to Certification Authorities (CAs) but only allows a domain to define a policy with CA-level granularity.  However, the CAA specification (RFC 8659) also provides facilities for an extension to admit a more granular, CA-specific policy.  This specification defines two such parameters: one allowing specific accounts of a CA to be identified by URIs and one allowing specific methods of domain control validation as defined by the Automatic Certificate Management Environment (ACME) protocol to be required.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiH. LandauddatejœÒhfilenamegrfc8657eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130463hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]‘Ìfgranking!—crfcd8657irfcNumber!—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexîCertification Authority Authorization (CAA) Record Extensions for Account URI and Automatic Certificate Management Environment (ACME) Method BindingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?If a host is located behind a NAT, it can be impossible for that host to communicate directly with other hosts (peers) in certain situations. In these situations, it is necessary for the host to use the services of an intermediate node that acts as a communication relay. This specification defines a protocol, called "Traversal Using Relays around NAT" (TURN), that allows the host to control the operation of the relay and to exchange packets with its peers using the relay. TURN differs from other relay control protocols in that it allows a client to communicate with multiple peers using a single relay address.

The TURN protocol was designed to be used as part of the Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) approach to NAT traversal, though it can also be used without ICE.

This document obsoletes RFCs 5766 and 6156.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationtVillanova UniversitydnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekP. Matthews¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej÷[hfilenamegrfc8656eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtramdfullx"tram - TURN Revised and ModernizeddnamexTURN Revised and Modernizeddtypebwgbidjdoc-130462hkeywordsÑcNATdTURNdSTUNcICEkobsoletedByÄepagesOopublicationDate^P ¸granking!–crfcd8656irfcNumber!–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhTraversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN): Relay Extensions to Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document provides the overall architecture for Deterministic Networking (DetNet), which provides a capability to carry specified unicast or multicast data flows for real-time applications with extremely low data loss rates and bounded latency within a network domain.  Techniques used include 1) reserving data-plane resources for individual (or aggregated) DetNet flows in some or all of the intermediate nodes along the path of the flow, 2) providing explicit routes for DetNet flows that do not immediately change with the network topology, and 3) distributing data from DetNet flow packets over time and/or space to ensure delivery of each packet's data in spite of the loss of a path.  DetNet operates at the IP layer and delivers service over lower-layer technologies such as MPLS and Time- Sensitive Networking (TSN) as defined by IEEE 802.1.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegN. Finn¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkasddatej÷Mhfilenamegrfc8655eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130461hkeywordsÑcTSNoBounded LatencysReliable NetworkingtAvailable NetworkingkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDate]±Ê◊granking!œcrfcd8655irfcNumber!œestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Deterministic Networking ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThe BGP specification (RFC 4271) mandates a maximum BGP message size of 4,096 octets.  As BGP is extended to support new Address Family Identifiers (AFIs), Subsequent AFIs (SAFIs), and other features, there is a need to extend the maximum message size beyond 4,096 octets.  This document updates the BGP specification by extending the maximum message size from 4,096 octets to 65,535 octets for all messages except for OPEN and KEEPALIVE messages.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlArrcus & IIJdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wardddatej÷Yhfilenamegrfc8654eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130460hkeywordsÉwborder gateway protocolxaddress family identifierscafikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]≤0÷granking!Œcrfcd8654irfcNumber!ŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Extended Message Support for BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyApplications differ with respect to whether they need session continuity and/or IP address reachability.  The network providing the same type of service to any mobile host and any application running on the host yields inefficiencies, as described in RFC 7333.  This document defines a new concept of enabling applications to influence the network's mobility services (session continuity and/or IP address reachability) on a per-socket basis, and suggests extensions to the networking stack's API to accommodate this concept.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhActilitydnamehA. Yegin¢kaffiliationeInteldnamehD. Moses¢kaffiliationwSungkyunkwan UniversitydnamegS. Jeonddatejœ˚hfilenamegrfc8653eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130459hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]ª∑;granking!Õcrfcd8653irfcNumber!ÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOn-Demand Mobility ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx™This document defines a YANG data model that can be used to configure and manage Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) devices.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefF. Guo¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamelM. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationsMetaswitch NetworksdnamemP. McAllister¢kaffiliationqIP Infusion IndiadnamehA. Peterddatej÷Shfilenamegrfc8652eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130458hkeywordsàdYANGdIGMPcMLDimulticastjdata modelmietf-igmp-mldrnetwork managementgroutingkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDate]¬h˜granking!Ãcrfcd8652irfcNumber!ÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjA YANG Data Model for the Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This document defines an extension to the Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) that enables a modem to use DLEP messages to pause and resume data traffic coming from its peer router.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnamehB. Cheng¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnamejD. Wiggins¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Bergerddatej÷Shfilenamegrfc8651eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130457hkeywordsÉdDLEPlFlow controlePausekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]õå|granking!Àcrfcd8651irfcNumber!ÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIDynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) Control-Plane-Based Pause ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÄThis document provides a RESTCONF binding to the dynamic subscription capability of both subscribed notifications and YANG-Push.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Voit¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameqE. Nilsen-Nygaard¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Biermanddatej÷Shfilenamegrfc8650eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130456hkeywordsÅiYANG-PushkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]—granking! crfcd8650irfcNumber! estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Dynamic Subscription to YANG Events and Datastores over RESTCONFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This document specifies the Hash Of Root Key certificate extension.  This certificate extension is carried in the self-signed certificate for a trust anchor, which is often called a Root Certification Authority (CA) certificate.  This certificate extension unambiguously identifies the next public key that will be used at some point in the future as the next Root CA certificate, eventually replacing the current one.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷Whfilenamegrfc8649eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130455hkeywordsÅltrust anchorkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate]d-ågranking!…crfcd8649irfcNumber!…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Hash Of Root Key Certificate ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÔA certain maximum amount of data can be safely encrypted when encryption is performed under a single key. This amount is called the "key lifetime". This specification describes a variety of methods for increasing the lifetime of symmetric keys. It provides two types of re-keying mechanisms based on hash functions and block ciphers that can be used with modes of operations such as CTR, GCM, CBC, CFB, and OMAC.

This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCryptoProdnamemS. Smyshlyaevddatej÷Qhfilenamegrfc8645eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130454hkeywordsÖire-keyingckeylkey lifetimeoencryption modeqmode of operationkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDate]h$granking!≈crfcd8645irfcNumber!≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex'Re-keying Mechanisms for Symmetric KeysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*Opportunistic Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (OSRTP) is an implementation of the Opportunistic Security mechanism, as defined in RFC 7435, applied to the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP).  OSRTP allows encrypted media to be used in environments where support for encryption is not known in advance and is not required.  OSRTP does not require Session Description Protocol (SDP) extensions or features and is fully backwards compatible with existing implementations using encrypted and authenticated media and implementations that do not encrypt or authenticate media packets.  OSRTP is not specific to any key management technique for Secure RTP (SRTP).  OSRTP is a transitional approach useful for migrating existing deployments of real-time communications to a fully encrypted and authenticated state.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtVillanova UniversitydnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationdAtosdnameiA. Hutton¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesske¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehT. Stachddatej÷Rhfilenamegrfc8643eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymisipbrandydfullxPsipbrandy - SIP Best-practice Recommendations Against Network Dangers to privacYdnamexDSIP Best-practice Recommendations Against Network Dangers to privacYdtypebwgbidjdoc-130453hkeywordsÖdsrtpvopportunistic securityjencryptionkbest efforteosrtpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]NÜgranking!√crfcd8643irfcNumber!√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIAn Opportunistic Approach for Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (OSRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%Well-known BGP communities are manipulated differently across various current implementations, resulting in difficulties for operators.  Network operators should deploy consistent community handling across their networks while taking the inconsistent behaviors from the various BGP implementations into consideration.  This document recommends specific actions to limit future inconsistency: namely, BGP implementors must not create further inconsistencies from this point forward.  These behavioral changes, though subtle, actually update RFC 1997.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamenJ. Borkenhagen¢kaffiliationlIIJ & ArrcusdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Bayraktarddatej÷Rhfilenamegrfc8642eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130452hkeywordsÇjOperationsxInter-Provider CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]K™Sgranking!¬crfcd8642irfcNumber!¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Policy Behavior for Well-Known BGP CommunitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document describes a mechanism that allows subscriber applications to request a continuous and customized stream of updates from a YANG datastore.  Providing such visibility into updates enables new capabilities based on the remote mirroring and monitoring of configuration and operational state.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Voitddatej÷Phfilenamegrfc8641eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130451hkeywordsÉiYANG-PushiStreamingitelemetrykobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDate]w1¢granking!¡crfcd8641irfcNumber!¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Subscription to YANG Notifications for Datastore UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx†This document provides a Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) binding to the dynamic subscription capability of both subscribed notifications and YANG-Push.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Voit¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamerA. Gonzalez Prieto¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameqE. Nilsen-Nygaard¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekA. Tripathyddatej÷Ohfilenamegrfc8640eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130450hkeywordsÅitelemetrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]w1†granking!¿crfcd8640irfcNumber!¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Dynamic Subscription to YANG Events and Datastores over NETCONFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a YANG data model and associated mechanisms enabling subscriber-specific subscriptions to a publisher's event streams.  Applying these elements allows a subscriber to request and receive a continuous, customized feed of publisher-generated information.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Voit¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamerA. Gonzalez Prieto¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameqE. Nilsen-Nygaard¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekA. Tripathyddatej÷Ohfilenamegrfc8639eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130449hkeywordsÇitelemetryiYANG-PushkobsoletedByÄepagesMopublicationDate]w1ôgranking!øcrfcd8639irfcNumber!øestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Subscription to YANG NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåDuring the transition to IPv6, there are scenarios where a backbone network internally running one IP address family (referred to as the internal IP or I-IP family) connects client networks running another IP address family (referred to as the external IP or E-IP family). In such cases, the I-IP backbone needs to offer both unicast and multicast transit services to the client E-IP networks.

This document describes a mechanism for supporting multicast across backbone networks where the I-IP and E-IP protocol families differ. The document focuses on the IPv4-over-IPv6 scenario, due to lack of real-world use cases for the IPv6-over-IPv4 scenario.fadNamel√âric Vynckedarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeM. Xu¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Wu¢kaffiliationsShenzhen UniversitydnamegS. Yang¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegC. Metzddatej÷Qhfilenamegrfc8638eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-130448hkeywordsÑiMulticastdMeshcSSMcASMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]w}granking!æcrfcd8638irfcNumber!æestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?IPv4 Multicast over an IPv6 Multicast in Softwire Mesh NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN) refers to the set of virtual network (VN) operations needed to orchestrate, control, and manage large-scale multidomain TE networks so as to facilitate network programmability, automation, efficient resource sharing, and end-to-end virtual service-aware connectivity and network function virtualization services.

The Path Computation Element (PCE) is a component, application, or network node that is capable of computing a network path or route based on a network graph and applying computational constraints. The PCE serves requests from Path Computation Clients (PCCs) that communicate with it over a local API or using the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP).

This document examines the applicability of PCE to the ACTN framework.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelliddatej÷Nhfilenamegrfc8637eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130447hkeywordsÇcPCEdACTNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]&ªwgranking!Ωcrfcd8637irfcNumber!ΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhApplicability of the Path Computation Element (PCE) to the Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document updates the Public Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT) standard (RFC 4556) to remove protocol structures tied to specific cryptographic algorithms. The PKINIT key derivation function is made negotiable, and the digest algorithms for signing the pre-authentication data and the client's X.509 certificates are made discoverable.

These changes provide preemptive protection against vulnerabilities discovered in the future in any specific cryptographic algorithm and allow incremental deployment of newer algorithms.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiApple IncdnametL. Hornquist Astrand¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnameiM. Cullen¢kaffiliationcMITdnameiG. HudsonddatejœËhfilenamegrfc8636eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130446hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]1Ngranking!ºcrfcd8636irfcNumber!ºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYPublic Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT) Algorithm AgilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydBGPsec-speaking routers are provisioned with private keys in order to sign BGPsec announcements.  The corresponding public keys are published in the Global Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), enabling verification of BGPsec messages.  This document describes two methods of generating the public-private key pairs: router-driven and operator-driven.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpIIJ Lab & ArrcusdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. PatelddatejœŸhfilenamegrfc8635eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130445hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]KOÌgranking!ªcrfcd8635irfcNumber!ªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRouter Keying for BGPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyXCertification Authorities (CAs) within the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) manage BGPsec router certificates as well as RPKI certificates.  The rollover of BGPsec router certificates must be carefully performed in order to synchronize the distribution of router public keys with BGPsec UPDATE messages verified with those router public keys.  This document describes a safe rollover process, and it discusses when and why the rollover of BGPsec router certificates is necessary.  When this rollover process is followed, the rollover will be performed without routing information being lost.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegB. Weis¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Gagliano¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. PatelddatejÃ˜hfilenamegrfc8634eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130444hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]KOÍgranking!∫crfcd8634irfcNumber!∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‡etotaletitlex"BGPsec Router Certificate RolloverdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¶The Network Time Protocol (NTP) is one of the oldest protocols on the Internet and has been widely used since its initial publication.  This document is a collection of best practices for the general operation of NTP servers and clients on the Internet.  It includes recommendations for the stable, accurate, and secure operation of NTP infrastructure.  This document is targeted at NTP version 4 as described in RFC 5905.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjOrolia USAdnameiD. Reilly¢kaffiliationwNetwork Time FoundationdnamehH. Stenn¢kaffiliationcPTBdnameiD. Siboldddatej÷Khfilenamegrfc8633eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130443hkeywordsÅcNTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]1Lgranking!πcrfcd8633irfcNumber!πestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberﬂetotaletitlex,Network Time Protocol Best Current PracticesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document defines a YANG module for alarm management.  It includes functions for alarm-list management, alarm shelving, and notifications to inform management systems.  There are also operations to manage the operator state of an alarm and administrative alarm procedures.  The module carefully maps to relevant alarm standards.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpStefan Vallin ABdnameiS. Vallin¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelM. Bjorklundddatej”.hfilenamegrfc8632eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-130442hkeywordsÇjMonitoringpFault ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDate]y&–granking!∏crfcd8632irfcNumber!∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&A YANG Data Model for Alarm ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆMany resources provided on the Web are part of sets of resources that are provided in a context that is managed by one particular service provider.  Often, these sets of resources are referred to as "Web services" or "Web APIs".  This specification defines link relations that represent relationships from Web services or APIs to resources that provide documentation, descriptions, metadata, or status information for these resources.  Documentation is primarily intended for human consumers, whereas descriptions are primarily intended for automated consumers.  Metadata provides information about a service's context.  This specification also defines a link relation to identify status resources that are used to represent information about service status.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. Wildeddatej÷Nhfilenamegrfc8631eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130441hkeywordsÖcAPImDocumentationkDescriptionhMetadatafStatuskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate]3m}granking!∑crfcd8631irfcNumber!∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Link Relation Types for Web ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document defines a Trust Anchor Locator (TAL) for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI). The TAL allows Relying Parties in the RPKI to download the current Trust Anchor (TA) Certification Authority (CA) certificate from one or more locations and verify that the key of this self-signed certificate matches the key on the TAL. Thus, Relying Parties can be configured with TA keys but can allow these TAs to change the content of their CA certificate. In particular, it allows TAs to change the set of IP Address Delegations and/or Autonomous System Identifier Delegations included in the extension(s) (RFC 3779) of their certificate.

This document obsoletes the previous definition of the TAL as provided in RFC 7730 by adding support for Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) (RFC 3986) that use HTTP over TLS (HTTPS) (RFC 7230) as the scheme.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationgW3C/MITdnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamenT. Bruijnzeelsddatejœ‚hfilenamegrfc8630eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130440hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]NÉgranking!∂crfcd8630irfcNumber!∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Trust Anchor LocatordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¶This document defines an extension to the Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) that enables the reporting and control of multi-hop forwarding by DLEP-capable modems.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnamehB. Cheng¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. BergerddatejœÂhfilenamegrfc8629eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130439hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate]7(™granking!µcrfcd8629irfcNumber!µestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDDynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP) Multi-Hop Forwarding ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe OAuth 2.0 device authorization grant is designed for Internet- connected devices that either lack a browser to perform a user-agent- based authorization or are input constrained to the extent that requiring the user to input text in order to authenticate during the authorization flow is impractical.  It enables OAuth clients on such devices (like smart TVs, media consoles, digital picture frames, and printers) to obtain user authorization to access protected resources by using a user agent on a separate device.fadNamemRoman Danyliwdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejW. Denniss¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationkARM LimiteddnamemH. Tschofenigddatej÷Ihfilenamegrfc8628eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130438hkeywordsämSecurity AreaeOAuthhSecuritymAuthorizationmSmart ObjectscIoTrInternet of ThingsxInternet of Things SecurityxOAuth for Constrained DevicesrOAuth IoT SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]WäLgranking!¥crfcd8628irfcNumber!¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$OAuth 2.0 Device Authorization GrantdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document defines new RTP payload formats for the Forward Error Correction (FEC) packets that are generated by the non-interleaved and interleaved parity codes from source media encapsulated in RTP.  These parity codes are systematic codes (Flexible FEC, or "FLEX FEC"), where a number of FEC repair packets are generated from a set of source packets from one or more source RTP streams.  These FEC repair packets are sent in a redundancy RTP stream separate from the source RTP stream(s) that carries the source packets.  RTP source packets that were lost in transmission can be reconstructed using the source and repair packets that were received.  The non-interleaved and interleaved parity codes that are defined in this specification offer a good protection against random and bursty packet losses, respectively, at a cost of complexity.  The RTP payload formats that are defined in this document address scalability issues experienced with the earlier specifications and offer several improvements.  Due to these changes, the new payload formats are not backward compatible with earlier specifications; however, endpoints that do not implement this specification can still work by simply ignoring the FEC repair packets.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiM. Zanaty¢kaffiliationlcallstats.iodnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationoNetworked MediadnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationmQualcomm Inc.dnamejG. Mandyamddatej÷Ohfilenamegrfc8627eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130437hkeywordsÇcFECxforward error correctionkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate]1Igranking!≥crfcd8627irfcNumber!≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>RTP Payload Format for Flexible Forward Error Correction (FEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡A packet-switching network may contain links with variable bandwidths (e.g., copper and radio).  The bandwidth of such links is sensitive to the external environment (e.g., climate).  Availability is typically used to describe these links when doing network planning.  This document introduces an optional Bandwidth Availability TLV in RSVP-TE signaling.  This extension can be used to set up a GMPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) in conjunction with the Ethernet SENDER_TSPEC object.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegH. Long¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnameeM. Ye¢kaffiliationcZTEdnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationtTelecom Italia S.p.AdnameoA. D'Alessandro¢kaffiliationeCienadnamegH. Shahddatej÷Mhfilenamegrfc8625eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-130436hkeywordsÑeGMPLSgRSVP-TEimicrowavewvariable bandwidth linkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]WÉ$granking!±crfcd8625irfcNumber!±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Ethernet Traffic Parameters with Availability InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe DNSSEC protocol makes use of various cryptographic algorithms in order to provide authentication of DNS data and proof of nonexistence.  To ensure interoperability between DNS resolvers and DNS authoritative servers, it is necessary to specify a set of algorithm implementation requirements and usage guidelines to ensure that there is at least one algorithm that all implementations support.  This document defines the current algorithm implementation requirements and usage guidance for DNSSEC.  This document obsoletes RFC 6944.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamegO. Suryddatejœ‡hfilenamegrfc8624eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130435hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9904epagesopublicationDate]␀#Pgranking!∞crfcd8624irfcNumber!∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCAlgorithm Implementation Requirements and Usage Guidance for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9157∂habstractysThe Path Computation Element (PCE) has been identified as an appropriate technology for the determination of the paths of point- to-multipoint (P2MP) TE Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  This document provides extensions required for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) so as to enable the usage of a stateful PCE capability in supporting P2MP TE LSPs.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehU. Palle¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnameiY. Tanaka¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeramddatejœ‚hfilenamegrfc8623eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130434hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate]G␀granking!Øcrfcd8623irfcNumber!ØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) Protocol Extensions for Usage with Point-to-Multipoint TE Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstracty˘This document specifies properties and characteristics of a Lower- Effort Per-Hop Behavior (LE PHB). The primary objective of this LE PHB is to protect Best-Effort (BE) traffic (packets forwarded with the default PHB) from LE traffic in congestion situations, i.e., when resources become scarce, BE traffic has precedence over LE traffic and may preempt it. Alternatively, packets forwarded by the LE PHB can be associated with a scavenger service class, i.e., they scavenge otherwise-unused resources only. There are numerous uses for this PHB, e.g., for background traffic of low precedence, such as bulk data transfers with low priority in time, non-time-critical backups, larger software updates, web search engines while gathering information from web servers and so on. This document recommends a standard Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) value for the LE PHB.

This specification obsoletes RFC 3662 and updates the DSCP recommended in RFCs 4594 and 8325 to use the DSCP assigned in this specification.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcKITdnamehR. Blessddatej÷Ghfilenamegrfc8622eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130433hkeywordsÉlLower EffortpPer-Hop BehaviorqScavenger ServicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]	eÏgranking!Æcrfcd8622irfcNumber!ÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDA Lower-Effort Per-Hop Behavior (LE PHB) for Differentiated ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document specifies a data model for synchronising email data with a server using the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP).  Clients can use this to efficiently search, access, organise, and send messages, and to get push notifications for fast resynchronisation when new messages are delivered or a change is made in another client.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastMaildnamejN. Jenkins¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Newmanddatej’-hfilenamegrfc8621eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130432hkeywordsÉdJMAPdJSONeemailkobsoletedByÄepageslopublicationDate]L≠lgranking!≠crfcd8621irfcNumber!≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2The JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) for MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÍThis document specifies a protocol for clients to efficiently query, fetch, and modify JSON-based data objects, with support for push notification of changes and fast resynchronisation and for out-of- band binary data upload/download.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastMaildnamejN. Jenkins¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Newmanddatej÷Hhfilenamegrfc8620eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdjmapdfullx jmap - JSON Mail Access ProtocoldnamexJSON Mail Access Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130431hkeywordsÇdJMAPdJSONkobsoletedByÄepagesZopublicationDate]0õqgranking!¨crfcd8620irfcNumber!¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9404d9670∂habstractx’RFC 5869 specifies the HMAC-based Extract-and-Expand Key Derivation Function (HKDF) algorithm.  This document assigns algorithm identifiers to the HKDF algorithm when used with three common one-way hash functions.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷Khfilenamegrfc8619eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130430hkeywordsÇdHKDFtAlgorithm IdentifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]
|Ögranking!´crfcd8619irfcNumber!´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZAlgorithm Identifiers for the HMAC-based Extract-and-Expand Key Derivation Function (HKDF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document describes a data representation for collections of DNS messages.  The format is designed for efficient storage and transmission of large packet captures of DNS traffic; it attempts to minimize the size of such packet capture files but retain the full DNS message contents along with the most useful transport metadata.  It is intended to assist with the development of DNS traffic- monitoring applications.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjSinodun ITdnamelJ. Dickinson¢kaffiliationjSinodun ITdnamehJ. Hague¢kaffiliationjSinodun ITdnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamelT. Manderson¢kaffiliationxWikimedia Foundation, Inc.dnamegJ. Bondddatej÷@hfilenamegrfc8618eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130429hkeywordsÅcDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesOopublicationDate]o8€granking!™crfcd8618irfcNumber!™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Compacted-DNS (C-DNS): A Format for DNS Packet CapturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThe Authenticated Received Chain (ARC) protocol provides an authenticated "chain of custody" for a message, allowing each entity that handles the message to see what entities handled it before and what the message's authentication assessment was at each step in the handling.

ARC allows Internet Mail Handlers to attach assertions of message authentication assessment to individual messages. As messages traverse ARC-enabled Internet Mail Handlers, additional ARC assertions can be attached to messages to form ordered sets of ARC assertions that represent the authentication assessment at each step of the message-handling paths.

ARC-enabled Internet Mail Handlers can process sets of ARC assertions to inform message disposition decisions, identify Internet Mail Handlers that might break existing authentication mechanisms, and convey original authentication assessments across trust boundaries.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamekK. Andersen¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegB. Long¢kaffiliationhValimaildnamehS. Blank¢kaffiliationcTDPdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej÷4hfilenamegrfc8617eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130428hkeywordsÜdDKIMeDMARCisignatureeemailudomian authenticationtemail authenticationkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate]%-Âgranking!©crfcd8617irfcNumber!©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/The Authenticated Received Chain (ARC) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆSender Policy Framework (SPF) (RFC 7208), DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) (RFC 6376), and Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) (RFC 7489) enable a domain owner to publish email authentication and policy information in the DNS.  In internationalized email, domain names can occur both as U-labels and A-labels.  This specification updates the SPF, DKIM, and DMARC specifications to clarify which form of internationalized domain names to use in those specifications.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levineddatej÷Khfilenamegrfc8616eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130427hkeywordsÇeemailtinternationalizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]`.granking!®crfcd8616irfcNumber!®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Email Authentication for Internationalized MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis memo defines a path prefix for "well-known locations", "/.well-known/", in selected Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) schemes.

In doing so, it obsoletes RFC 5785 and updates the URI schemes defined in RFC 7230 to reserve that space. It also updates RFC 7595 to track URI schemes that support well-known URIs in their registry.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatejœﬂhfilenamegrfc8615eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130426hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\„¢granking!ßcrfcd8615irfcNumber!ßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Well-Known Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚This document updates the meaning of the Control Flags field in the "Layer2 Info Extended Community" used for BGP Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) as defined in RFC 4761.  This document updates RFC 4761.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehR. Singh¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoS. Palislamovicddatejœ·hfilenamegrfc8614eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130425hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate]	eÁgranking!¶crfcd8614irfcNumber!¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNUpdated Processing of Control Flags for BGP Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document defines Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE), a method for application-layer protection of the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP), using CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE). OSCORE provides end-to-end protection between endpoints communicating using CoAP or CoAP-mappable HTTP. OSCORE is designed for constrained nodes and networks supporting a range of proxy operations, including translation between different transport protocols.

Although an optional functionality of CoAP, OSCORE alters CoAP options processing and IANA registration. Therefore, this document updates RFC 7252.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamekJ. Mattsson¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelF. Palombini¢kaffiliationdRISEdnamehL. Seitzddatejœ⁄hfilenamegrfc8613eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130424hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDate]%Rgranking!•crfcd8613irfcNumber!•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Object Security for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx™This document defines the requirements for the Distributed Denial-of- Service (DDoS) Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) protocols enabling coordinated response to DDoS attacks.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelA. Mortensen¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamelR. Moskowitzddatejœ‹hfilenamegrfc8612eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddotsdfullx!dots - DDoS Open Threat SignalingdnamexDDoS Open Threat Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130423hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Ìéogranking!§crfcd8612irfcNumber!§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.DDoS Open Threat Signaling (DOTS) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document defines extensions to the MPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping and Traceroute mechanisms as specified in RFC 8029. The extensions allow the MPLS LSP Ping and Traceroute mechanisms to discover and exercise specific paths of Layer 2 (L2) Equal-Cost Multipath (ECMP) over Link Aggregation Group (LAG) interfaces. Additionally, a mechanism is defined to enable the determination of the capabilities supported by a Label Switching Router (LSR).

This document updates RFC 8029.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatej÷Jhfilenamegrfc8611eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130422hkeywordsÇdMPLShLSP PingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]
|Çgranking!£crfcd8611irfcNumber!£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkLabel Switched Path (LSP) Ping and Traceroute Multipath Support for Link Aggregation Group (LAG) InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9041∂habstractyThis document proposes a notational convention to express Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) data structures (RFC 7049).  Its main goal is to provide an easy and unambiguous way to express structures for protocol messages and data formats that use CBOR or JSON.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer SITdnamekH. Birkholz¢kaffiliationsUniversitaet BremendnameiC. Vigano¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷Ihfilenamegrfc8610eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcbordfullxFcbor - Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex?Concise Binary Object Representation Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130421hkeywordsÖmbinary formatwdata interchange formattdescription languageoschema languageltree grammarkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate]ç}granking!¢crfcd8610irfcNumber!¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexêConcise Data Definition Language (CDDL): A Notational Convention to Express Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) and JSON Data StructuresdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9682¥habstracty¶Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) is a network protocol that uses a hierarchical name to forward requests and to match responses to requests. This document specifies the encoding of CCNx messages in a TLV packet format, including the TLV types used by each message element and the encoding of each value. The semantics of CCNx messages follow the encoding-independent CCNx Semantics specification.

This document is a product of the Information Centric Networking research group (ICNRG). The document received wide review among ICNRG participants and has two full implementations currently in active use, which have informed the technical maturity of the protocol specification.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjPARC, Inc.dnamehM. Mosko¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamehI. Solis¢kaffiliationxUniversity of California IrvinednamegC. Woodddatejœ’hfilenamegrfc8609eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130420hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDate]%7ïgranking!°crfcd8609irfcNumber!°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex8Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) Messages in TLV FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9510∂habstractyÚThis document specifies the algorithms, algorithm parameters, asymmetric key formats, asymmetric key sizes, and signature formats used in BGPsec (Border Gateway Protocol Security). This document updates RFC 7935 ("The Profile for Algorithms and Key Sizes for Use in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure") and obsoletes RFC 8208 ("BGPsec Algorithms, Key Formats, and Signature Formats") by adding Documentation and Experimentation Algorithm IDs, correcting the range of unassigned algorithms IDs to fill the complete range, and restructuring the document for better reading.

This document also includes example BGPsec UPDATE messages as well as the private keys used to generate the messages and the certificates necessary to validate those signatures.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamekO. Borchertddatej÷Khfilenamegrfc8608eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130419hkeywordsÖfBGPsecqBGPsec AlgorithmsqCrypto AlgorithmseECDSAlCryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]
h*granking!†crfcd8608irfcNumber!†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5BGPsec Algorithms, Key Formats, and Signature FormatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis specification adds an extension to the Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) to allow attachments associated with iCalendar data to be stored and managed on the server.

This specification documents existing code deployed by multiple vendors. It is published as an Informational specification rather than Standards Track due to its noncompliance with multiple best current practices of HTTP.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekA. Quillaud¢kaffiliationhFastMaildnamelK. Murchisonddatej’œhfilenamegrfc8607eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130418hkeywordsÑfCalDAVkcalendaringjattachmentfATTACHkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate\ı√‰granking!ücrfcd8607irfcNumber!üestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV): Managed AttachmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~The SIP Reason header field is defined to carry ISUP (ISDN User Part) cause values as well as SIP response codes.  Some services in SIP networks may need to know the ISUP location where the call was released in the PSTN (Public Switched Telephone Network) to correctly interpret the reason of release.  This document updates RFC 3326 by adding a location parameter for this purpose.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesskeddatej‘=hfilenamegrfc8606eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130417hkeywordsÉfReasondCallhLocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]∏granking!ûcrfcd8606irfcNumber!ûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNISDN User Part (ISUP) Cause Location Parameter for the SIP Reason Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document defines extensions to the vCard data format for representing and exchanging contact information used to implement the Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN) operational profile for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP).  The property and parameter defined here are used to add values to RDAP responses that are consistent with ICANN policies.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationlGoDaddy Inc.dnameiR. Carneyddatej÷Hhfilenamegrfc8605eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130416hkeywordsÇdRDAPevCardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\€mNgranking!ùcrfcd8605irfcNumber!ùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZvCard Format Extensions: ICANN Extensions for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes an application of Segment Routing to scale the network to support hundreds of thousands of network nodes, and tens of millions of physical underlay endpoints.  This use case can be applied to the interconnection of massive-scale Data Centers (DCs) and/or large aggregation networks.  Forwarding tables of midpoint and leaf nodes only require a few tens of thousands of entries.  This may be achieved by the inherently scaleable nature of Segment Routing and the design proposed in this document.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamehG. Dawra¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationkCenturyLinkdnameiD. CooperddatejœŸhfilenamegrfc8604eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130415hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]∂granking!úcrfcd8604irfcNumber!úestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex:Interconnecting Millions of Endpoints with Segment RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyùThis document specifies a base profile for X.509 v3 Certificates and X.509 v2 Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs) for use with the United States National Security Agency's Commercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite.  The profile applies to the capabilities, configuration, and operation of all components of US National Security Systems that employ such X.509 certificates.  US National Security Systems are described in NIST Special Publication 800-59.  It is also appropriate for all other US Government systems that process high-value information.  It is made publicly available for use by developers and operators of these and any other system deployments.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejM. Jenkins¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejL. ZieglarddatejœŸhfilenamegrfc8603eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130414hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‹”Ågranking!õcrfcd8603irfcNumber!õestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexmCommercial National Security Algorithm (CNSA) Suite Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx This memo updates the IANA registry rules for the Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP) protocol, by no longer requiring that postal addresses be included in contact information.

This memo updates RFC 3219.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Hardieddatejœﬁhfilenamegrfc8602eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130413hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]%Ogranking!öcrfcd8602irfcNumber!öestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Update to the Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP) IANA Registry Rules regarding Postal AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This document specifies a message header field called "Authentication-Results" for use with electronic mail messages to indicate the results of message authentication efforts. Any receiver-side software, such as mail filters or Mail User Agents (MUAs), can use this header field to relay that information in a convenient and meaningful way to users or to make sorting and filtering decisions.

This document obsoletes RFC 7601.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’Lhfilenamegrfc8601eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130412hkeywordsÜdDKIMcSPFdATPScVBRnAuthenticationjReputationkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDate\Òì6granking!ôcrfcd8601irfcNumber!ôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAMessage Header Field for Indicating Message Authentication StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty÷This document describes how to use the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) to collect and distribute security incident reports and other security-relevant information between network- connected devices, primarily for the purpose of communication among Computer Security Incident Response Teams and associated entities.  To illustrate the principles involved, this document describes such a usage for the Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF).fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Appala¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegS. Pope¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’âhfilenamegrfc8600eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130411hkeywordságpublishisubscribefpubsubdgrideiodefixmpp-gridsinformation sharingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate][granking!òcrfcd8600irfcNumber!òestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYUsing Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) for Security Information ExchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∏This document describes how a Push Notification Service (PNS) can be used to wake a suspended Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) User Agent (UA) with push notifications, and it also describes how the UA can send binding-refresh REGISTER requests and receive incoming SIP requests in an environment in which the UA may be suspended.  The document defines new SIP URI parameters to exchange PNS information between the UA and the SIP entity that will then request that push notifications be sent to the UA.  It also defines the parameters to trigger such push notification requests.  The document also defines new feature-capability indicators that can be used to indicate support of this mechanism.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationsMetaswitch NetworksdnameiM. Arnoldddatej÷Ihfilenamegrfc8599eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130410hkeywordsÉcSIPdPushlNotificationkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate\Á6Ôgranking!ócrfcd8599irfcNumber!óestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Push Notification with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document defines two Configuration Payload Attribute Types (INTERNAL_DNS_DOMAIN and INTERNAL_DNSSEC_TA) for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2).  These payloads add support for private (internal-only) DNS domains.  These domains are intended to be resolved using non-public DNS servers that are only reachable through the IPsec connection.  DNS resolution for other domains remains unchanged.  These Configuration Payloads only apply to split- tunnel configurations.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Woutersddatej÷Ghfilenamegrfc8598eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130409hkeywordsÇeIKEv2cDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Ì¡,granking!ñcrfcd8598irfcNumber!ñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPSplit DNS Configuration for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty>Software-Defined Networking (SDN) advocates for the separation of the control plane from the data plane in the network nodes and its logical centralization on one or a set of control entities. Most of the network and/or service intelligence is moved to these control entities. Typically, such an entity is seen as a compendium of interacting control functions in a vertical, tightly integrated fashion. The relocation of the control functions from a number of distributed network nodes to a logical central entity conceptually places together a number of control capabilities with different purposes. As a consequence, the existing solutions do not provide a clear separation between transport control and services that rely upon transport capabilities.

This document describes an approach called Cooperating Layered Architecture for Software-Defined Networking (CLAS), wherein the control functions associated with transport are differentiated from those related to services in such a way that they can be provided and maintained independently and can follow their own evolution path.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamemLM. Contreras¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejP. Iovannaddatej÷Dhfilenamegrfc8597eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130408hkeywordsàcSDNgControloProgrammabilitylIntelligenceiTransportgServicekFlexibilitykCooperationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‹Ó¢granking!ïcrfcd8597irfcNumber!ïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGCooperating Layered Architecture for Software-Defined Networking (CLAS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document describes how to use a Service Function Forwarder (SFF) Label (similar to a pseudowire label or VPN label) to indicate the presence of a Service Function Chaining (SFC) Network Service Header (NSH) between an MPLS label stack and the packet original packet/ frame.  This allows SFC packets using the NSH to be forwarded between SFFs over an MPLS network, and to select one of multiple SFFs in the destination MPLS node.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickxddatej÷Ihfilenamegrfc8596eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130407hkeywordsÉelabelklabel stackx service function forwarder (SFF)kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate\˙ŒDgranking!îcrfcd8596irfcNumber!îestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaMPLS Transport Encapsulation for the Service Function Chaining (SFC) Network Service Header (NSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–This document describes how Service Function Chaining (SFC) can be achieved in an MPLS network by means of a logical representation of the Network Service Header (NSH) in an MPLS label stack.  That is, the NSH is not used, but the fields of the NSH are mapped to fields in the MPLS label stack.  This approach does not deprecate or replace the NSH, but it acknowledges that there may be a need for an interim deployment of SFC functionality in brownfield networks.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drakeddatej’ hfilenamegrfc8595eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130406hkeywordsÜcSFCdMPLSxService Function ChainingcNSHvNetwork Service HeaderxMultiprotocol Label SwitchingkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate\˙Œ@granking!ìcrfcd8595irfcNumber!ìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<An MPLS-Based Forwarding Plane for Service Function ChainingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This specification defines the Sunset HTTP response header field, which indicates that a URI is likely to become unresponsive at a specified point in the future.  It also defines a sunset link relation type that allows linking to resources providing information about an upcoming resource or service sunset.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. WildeddatejœFhfilenamegrfc8594eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130405hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‹”Ägranking!ícrfcd8594irfcNumber!íestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Sunset HTTP Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document describes two reference video traffic models for evaluating RTP congestion control algorithms.  The first model statistically characterizes the behavior of a live video encoder in response to changing requests on the target video rate.  The second model is trace-driven and emulates the output of actual encoded video frame sizes from a high-resolution test sequence.  Both models are designed to strike a balance between simplicity, repeatability, and authenticity in modeling the interactions between a live video traffic source and the congestion control module.  Finally, the document describes how both approaches can be combined into a hybrid model.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefX. Zhu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegS. Mena¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnameiZ. Sarkerddatej÷Fhfilenamegrfc8593eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130404hkeywordsÇjMultimediarCongestion ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\€mDgranking!ëcrfcd8593irfcNumber!ëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Video Traffic Models for RTP Congestion Control EvaluationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx˙This document describes methods of carrying Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) using the Network Service Header (NSH).  These methods may be used, for example, to monitor latency and QoS marking to identify problems on some links or service functions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiR. Browne¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekA. Chilikin¢kaffiliationx Huawei Network.IO Innovation LabdnamejT. Mizrahiddatej÷Fhfilenamegrfc8592eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130403hkeywordsÑiTimestamplTimestampingcQoSmservice chainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‹”}granking!êcrfcd8592irfcNumber!êestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexMKey Performance Indicator (KPI) Stamping for the Network Service Header (NSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMobile messaging applications used with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) commonly use some combination of the SIP MESSAGE method and the Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP).  While these provide mechanisms for hop-by-hop security, neither natively provides end-to-end protection.  This document offers guidance on how to provide end-to-end authentication, integrity protection, and confidentiality using the Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME).  It updates and provides clarifications for RFCs 3261, 3428, and 4975.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqStandard VelocitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej÷Ehfilenamegrfc8591eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130402hkeywordsÇdMSRPdCPIMkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate\»Òœgranking!ècrfcd8591irfcNumber!èestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSIP-Based Messaging with S/MIMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension for notifying clients of operations on client-sponsored objects that were not initiated by the client through EPP.  These operations may include contractual or policy requirements including, but not limited to, regular batch processes, customer support actions, Uniform Domain-Name Dispute-Resolution Policy (UDRP) or Uniform Rapid Suspension (URS) actions, court-directed actions, and bulk updates based on customer requests.  Since the client is not directly involved or knowledgable of these operations, the extension is used along with an EPP object mapping to provide the resulting state of the postoperation object, and optionally a preoperation object, with the operation metadata of what, when, who, and why.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamehK. Feherddatejœÿhfilenamegrfc8590eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130401hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\’’´granking!écrfcd8590irfcNumber!éestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDChange Poll Extension for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document describes the 'leaptofrogans' Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme, which enables applications to launch Frogans Player on a given Frogans site.  Frogans is a medium for publishing content and services on the Internet, defined as a generic software layer on the Internet.  Frogans Player is software that enables end users to browse Frogans sites.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeOP3FTdnamehA. Tamas¢kaffiliationeOP3FTdnamejB. Phister¢kaffiliationeOP3FTdnamenJ-E. Rodriguezddatej÷Fhfilenamegrfc8589eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130400hkeywordsÑgFrogansmleaptofroganseOP3FTjURI schemekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate\‰=Ôgranking!çcrfcd8589irfcNumber!çestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe 'leaptofrogans' URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document extends the Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT), which is a token object that conveys cryptographically signed information about the participants involved in communications.  The extension is defined based on the "Signature-based Handling of Asserted information using toKENs (SHAKEN)" specification by the ATIS/SIP Forum IP-NNI Task Group.  It provides both (1) a specific set of levels of confidence in the correctness of the originating identity of a call originated in a SIP-based telephone network as well as (2) an identifier that allows the Service Provider (SP) to uniquely identify the origin of the call within its network.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamehC. Wendt¢kaffiliationiiconectivdnameiM. Barnesddatejœ›hfilenamegrfc8588eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130399hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate\€m9granking!åcrfcd8588irfcNumber!åestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxPersonal Assertion Token (PaSSporT) Extension for Signature-based Handling of Asserted information using toKENs (SHAKEN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyéIn NFS version 4.0, the fs_locations attribute informs clients about alternate locations of file systems.  An NFS version 4.0 client can use this information to handle migration and replication of server file systems.  This document describes how an NFS version 4.0 client can also use this information to discover an NFS version 4.0 server's trunking capabilities.  This document updates RFC 7530.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehC. Lever¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiD. Noveckddatej÷Dhfilenamegrfc8587eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130398hkeywordsÜgNFSv4.0imigrationkreplicationhtrunkinglfs_locationsxtransparent state migrationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\——
granking!ãcrfcd8587irfcNumber!ãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNFS Version 4.0 Trunking UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document defines the CDN-Loop request header field for HTTP.  CDN-Loop addresses an operational need that occurs when an HTTP request is intentionally forwarded between Content Delivery Networks (CDNs), but is then accidentally or maliciously re-routed back into the original CDN causing a non-terminating loop.  The new header field can be used to identify the error and terminate the loop.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamehS. Ludin¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationjCloudflarednamekN. Sullivanddatej÷Dhfilenamegrfc8586eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130397hkeywordsÅhCDN-LoopkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\¿ãIgranking!äcrfcd8586irfcNumber!äestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Loop Detection in Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document specifies the IPv4 service continuity requirements for IPv6 Customer Edge (CE) routers that are provided either by the service provider or by vendors who sell through the retail market.

Specifically, this document extends the basic requirements for IPv6 CE routers as described in RFC 7084 to allow the provisioning of IPv6 transition services for the support of IPv4-as-a-Service (IPv4aaS) by means of new transition mechanisms. The document only covers IPv4aaS, i.e., transition technologies for delivering IPv4 in IPv6-only access networks. IPv4aaS is necessary because there aren't sufficient IPv4 addresses available for every possible customer/ device. However, devices or applications in the customer Local Area Networks (LANs) may be IPv4-only or IPv6-only and still need to communicate with IPv4-only services on the Internet.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpThe IPv6 CompanydnameqJ. Palet Martinez¢kaffiliationtD-Link Systems, Inc.dnamelH. M.-H. Liu¢kaffiliationsNEC Platforms, Ltd.dnamelM. Kawashimaddatej÷Ehfilenamegrfc8585eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130396hkeywordsÉoIPv6 transitionoCE requirementsgIPv4aaSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\À≤Qgranking!âcrfcd8585irfcNumber!âestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRequirements for IPv6 Customer Edge Routers to Support IPv4-as-a-ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhAn alternative to the default Designated Forwarder (DF) selection algorithm in Ethernet VPNs (EVPNs) is defined.  The DF is the Provider Edge (PE) router responsible for sending Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, and Multicast (BUM) traffic to a multihomed Customer Edge (CE) device on a given VLAN on a particular Ethernet Segment (ES).  In addition, the ability to influence the DF election result for a VLAN based on the state of the associated Attachment Circuit (AC) is specified.  This document clarifies the DF election Finite State Machine in EVPN services.  Therefore, it updates the EVPN specification (RFC 7432).fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Mohanty¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejK. Nagaraj¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelS. SathappanddatejŒhfilenamegrfc8584eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130395hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate\¡3granking!àcrfcd8584irfcNumber!àestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFFramework for Ethernet VPN Designated Forwarder Election ExtensibilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9785d9722∂habstracty‡RFC 7068 describes requirements for Overload Control in Diameter.  This includes a requirement to allow Diameter nodes to send "load" information, even when the node is not overloaded.  The base solution defined in RFC 7683 (Diameter Overload Information Conveyance (DOIC)) describes a mechanism meeting most of the requirements but does not currently include the ability to send load information.  This document defines a mechanism for the conveying of Diameter load information.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejS. Donovan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJJ. Trottinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8583eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130394hkeywordsÇhDiameterdloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Uª‹granking!ácrfcd8583irfcNumber!áestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Diameter Load Information ConveyancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThis specification documents an extension to the Diameter Overload Indication Conveyance (DOIC) base solution, which is defined in RFC 7683.  This extension adds a new overload-control abatement algorithm.  This abatement algorithm allows for a DOIC reporting node to specify a maximum rate at which a DOIC reacting node sends Diameter requests to the DOIC reporting node.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejS. Donovan¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamegE. Noelddatej÷9hfilenamegrfc8582eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130393hkeywordsÇhDiameterhOverloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Uª‹granking!Ücrfcd8582irfcNumber!ÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDiameter Overload Rate ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This specification documents an extension to the Diameter Overload Indication Conveyance (DOIC), a base solution for Diameter overload defined in RFC 7683.  The extension defines the Peer Overload report type.  The initial use case for the peer report is the handling of occurrences of overload of a Diameter Agent.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejS. Donovanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8581eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130392hkeywordsÇhDiameterhOverloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]Uª⁄granking!Öcrfcd8581irfcNumber!ÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Diameter Agent Overload and the Peer Overload ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThe Sieve email filtering language provides a number of action commands, some of which can generate additional messages on behalf of the user. This document defines an extension to such commands to allow a copy of any generated message to be filed into a target mailbox.

This document updates RFCs 5230 and 5435 by adding a new tagged argument to the Vacation and Notify actions, respectively.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFastMaildnamelK. Murchison¢kaffiliationhFastMaildnamekB. Gondwanaddatej’hfilenamegrfc8580eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130391hkeywordsÉeSievehVacationfNotifykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\÷^Ugranking!Ñcrfcd8580irfcNumber!ÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Sieve Extension: File Carbon Copy (FCC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3The SPECIAL-USE capability of the IMAP protocol (RFC 6154) allows clients to identify special-use mailboxes, e.g., where draft or sent messages should be put.  This simplifies client configuration.  In contrast, the Sieve mail filtering language (RFC 5228) currently has no such capability.  This memo defines a Sieve extension that fills this gap: it adds a test for checking whether a special-use attribute is assigned for a particular mailbox or any mailbox, and it adds the ability to file messages into a mailbox identified solely by a special-use attribute.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoOpen Xchange OydnamehS. Boschddatej’hfilenamegrfc8579eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130390hkeywordsÉesievegmailboxkspecial-usekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\÷^Pgranking!Écrfcd8579irfcNumber!ÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Sieve Email Filtering: Delivering to Special-Use MailboxesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis document presents use cases for diverse industries that have in common a need for "deterministic flows". "Deterministic" in this context means that such flows provide guaranteed bandwidth, bounded latency, and other properties germane to the transport of time-sensitive data.  These use cases differ notably in their network topologies and specific desired behavior, providing as a group broad industry context for Deterministic Networking (DetNet).  For each use case, this document will identify the use case, identify representative solutions used today, and describe potential improvements that DetNet can enable.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeDOLBYdnamekE. Grossmanddatej÷Ahfilenamegrfc8578eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130389hkeywordsÑfDetNetcAVBcTSNcSRPkobsoletedByÄepagesaopublicationDate\’—’granking!Çcrfcd8578irfcNumber!ÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Deterministic Networking Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycAs the scale of MPLS RSVP-TE networks has grown, the number of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) supported by individual network elements has increased. Various implementation recommendations have been proposed to manage the resulting increase in the amount of control-plane state information.

However, those changes have had no effect on the number of labels that a transit Label Switching Router (LSR) has to support in the forwarding plane. That number is governed by the number of LSPs transiting or terminated at the LSR and is directly related to the total LSP state in the control plane.

This document defines a mechanism to prevent the maximum size of the label space limit on an LSR from being a constraint to control-plane scaling on that node. It introduces the notion of preinstalled 'per-TE link labels' that can be shared by MPLS RSVP-TE LSPs that traverse these TE links. This approach significantly reduces the forwarding-plane state required to support a large number of LSPs. This couples the feature benefits of the RSVP-TE control plane with the simplicity of the Segment Routing (SR) MPLS forwarding plane.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelH. Sitaraman¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliationgVerizondnameiT. Parikh¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegT. Saadddatej÷Fhfilenamegrfc8577eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130388hkeywordsÉxSegment Routed RSVP-TE tunnelsnTE link labelsuRSVP-TE shared labelskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\….igranking!Åcrfcd8577irfcNumber!ÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Signaling RSVP-TE Tunnels on a Shared MPLS Forwarding PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty◊The Internet of Things (IoT) concept refers to the usage of standard Internet protocols to allow for human-to-thing and thing-to-thing communication. The security needs for IoT systems are well recognized, and many standardization steps to provide security have been taken -- for example, the specification of the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) secured with Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS). However, security challenges still exist, not only because there are some use cases that lack a suitable solution, but also because many IoT devices and systems have been designed and deployed with very limited security capabilities. In this document, we first discuss the various stages in the lifecycle of a thing. Next, we document the security threats to a thing and the challenges that one might face to protect against these threats. Lastly, we discuss the next steps needed to facilitate the deployment of secure IoT systems. This document can be used by implementers and authors of IoT specifications as a reference for details about security considerations while documenting their specific security challenges, threat models, and mitigations.

This document is a product of the IRTF Thing-to-Thing Research Group (T2TRG).gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgPhilipsdnameqO. Garcia-Morchon¢kaffiliationgSignifydnamehS. Kumar¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehM. Sethiddatej÷Ahfilenamegrfc8576eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymet2trgdfullvt2trg - Thing-to-ThingdnamenThing-to-Thingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130387hkeywordsäcIoTrInternet of ThingscM2MrMachine-to-machinexMachine-type communicationcMTChSecuritygPrivacykTrustworthyiLifecyclekobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDate\√∫Ãgranking!Äcrfcd8576irfcNumber!ÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexBInternet of Things (IoT) Security: State of the Art and ChallengesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document defines a YANG data model for the configuration of devices and clocks using the Precision Time Protocol (PTP) as specified in IEEE Std 1588-2008.  It also defines the retrieval of the configuration information, the data sets and the running states of PTP clocks.  The YANG module in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehY. Jiang¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeJ. Xu¢kaffiliationtNational InstrumentsdnamekR. CummingsddatejŒÜhfilenamegrfc8575eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftictocdfullx8tictoc - Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdnamex/Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdtypebwgbidjdoc-130386hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\—·<granking!crfcd8575irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5YANG Data Model for the Precision Time Protocol (PTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyA web resource is routinely referenced by means of the URI with which it is directly accessed.  But cases exist where referencing a resource by means of a different URI is preferred.  This specification defines a link relation type that can be used to convey such a preference.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx%Data Archiving and Networked ServicesdnamepH. Van de Sompel¢kaffiliationwOld Dominion UniversitydnameiM. Nelson¢kaffiliationhCrossrefdnameiG. Bilder¢kaffiliationxCalifornia Digital LibrarydnamehJ. Kunze¢kaffiliationrCornell UniversitydnameiS. Warnerddatej÷Fhfilenamegrfc8574eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130385hkeywordsÇupersistent identifiercPIDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\∂§Ωgranking!~crfcd8574irfcNumber!~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexBcite-as: A Link Relation to Convey a Preferred URI for ReferencingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThe Network Time Protocol (NTP), as described in RFC 5905, states that NTP packets should be authenticated by appending NTP data to a 128-bit key and hashing the result with MD5 to obtain a 128-bit tag.  This document deprecates MD5-based authentication, which is considered too weak, and recommends the use of AES-CMAC as described in RFC 4493 as a replacement.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqBoston UniversitydnamekA. Malhotra¢kaffiliationqBoston UniversitydnamekS. Goldbergddatej÷Dhfilenamegrfc8573eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130384hkeywordsÅcNTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate]:Fgranking!}crfcd8573irfcNumber!}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Message Authentication Code for the Network Time ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9748∂habstractyYThis document presents a technique to securely provision a networking device when it is booting in a factory-default state.  Variations in the solution enable it to be used on both public and private networks.  The provisioning steps are able to update the boot image, commit an initial configuration, and execute arbitrary scripts to address auxiliary needs.  The updated device is subsequently able to establish secure connections with other systems.  For instance, a device may establish NETCONF (RFC 6241) and/or RESTCONF (RFC 8040) connections with deployment-specific network management systems.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationiT-SystemsdnamenM. Abrahamssonddatej÷Chfilenamegrfc8572eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130383hkeywordsÑizerotouchibootstrapdsztpcztpkobsoletedByÄepagesWopublicationDate\…)granking!|crfcd8572irfcNumber!|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Secure Zero Touch Provisioning (SZTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9646∂habstractx°This document defines new BGP - Link State (BGP-LS) TLVs in order to carry the IGP Traffic Engineering Metric Extensions defined in the IS-IS and OSPF protocols.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfilsddatejœœhfilenamegrfc8571eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130382hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate\å0xgranking!{crfcd8571irfcNumber!{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`BGP - Link State (BGP-LS) Advertisement of IGP Traffic Engineering Performance Metric ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUIn certain networks, such as, but not limited to, financial information networks (e.g., stock market data providers), network-performance criteria (e.g., latency) are becoming as critical to data-path selection as other metrics.

This document describes extensions to IS-IS Traffic Engineering Extensions (RFC 5305). These extensions provide a way to distribute and collect network-performance information in a scalable fashion. The information distributed using IS-IS TE Metric Extensions can then be used to make path-selection decisions based on network performance.

Note that this document only covers the mechanisms with which network-performance information is distributed. The mechanisms for measuring network performance or acting on that information, once distributed, are outside the scope of this document.

This document obsoletes RFC 7810.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Giacalone¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej÷;hfilenamegrfc8570eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130381hkeywordsÇcIGPeIS-ISkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\å0wgranking!zcrfcd8570irfcNumber!zestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0IS-IS Traffic Engineering (TE) Metric ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyóThis document describes the core concepts of the Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) architecture and presents a network protocol based on two messages: Interests and Content Objects. It specifies the set of mandatory and optional fields within those messages and describes their behavior and interpretation. This architecture and protocol specification is independent of a specific wire encoding.

The protocol also uses a control message called an Interest Return, whereby one system can return an Interest message to the previous hop due to an error condition. This indicates to the previous hop that the current system will not respond to the Interest.

This document is a product of the Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG). The document received wide review among ICNRG participants. Two full implementations are in active use and have informed the technical maturity of the protocol specification.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjPARC, Inc.dnamehM. Mosko¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamehI. Solis¢kaffiliationxUniversity of California IrvinednamegC. Woodddatej÷Chfilenamegrfc8569eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-130380hkeywordsÅxContent Centric NetworkingkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate]%7ígranking!ycrfcd8569irfcNumber!yestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex+Content-Centric Networking (CCNx) SemanticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty@This document describes open research challenges for network virtualization.  Network virtualization is following a similar path as previously taken by cloud computing.  Specifically, cloud computing popularized migration of computing functions (e.g., applications) and storage from local, dedicated, physical resources to remote virtual functions accessible through the Internet.  In a similar manner, network virtualization is encouraging migration of networking functions from dedicated physical hardware nodes to a virtualized pool of resources.  However, network virtualization can be considered to be a more complex problem than cloud computing as it not only involves virtualization of computing and storage functions but also involves abstraction of the network itself.  This document describes current research and engineering challenges in network virtualization including the guarantee of quality of service, performance improvement, support for multiple domains, network slicing, service composition, device virtualization, privacy and security, separation of control concerns, network function placement, and testing.  In addition, some proposals are made for new activities in the IETF and IRTF that could address some of these challenges.  This document is a product of the Network Function Virtualization Research Group (NFVRG).fadNamepDeborah BrungardgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationlInterDigitaldnameiA. Rahman¢kaffiliationfSIGFOXdnamejJC. Zuniga¢kaffiliationcTIDdnamemLM. Contreras¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnameiP. Aranda¢kaffiliationuKeysight TechnologiesdnamehP. Lynchddatejœ‘hfilenamegrfc8568eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfvrgdfullx'nfvrg - Network Function VirtualizationdnamexNetwork Function Virtualizationdtypebrgbidjdoc-130379hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate\¿{Kgranking!xcrfcd8568irfcNumber!xestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex*Network Virtualization Research ChallengesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty~Maintaining high Quality of Experience (QoE) increasingly requires end-to-end, holistic network management, including managed Customer Premises Equipment (CPE). Because customer management is a shared global responsibility, the Domain Name System (DNS) provides an ideal existing infrastructure for maintaining authoritative customer information that must be readily, reliably, and publicly accessible.

This document describes four new DNS resource record types for encoding customer information in the DNS. These records are intended to better facilitate high customer QoE via inter-provider cooperation and management of customer data.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCMANDdnamefE. Rye¢kaffiliationeCMANDdnamejR. Beverlyddatejœ‹hfilenamegrfc8567eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-90650hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\¢hÓgranking!wcrfcd8567irfcNumber!westateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex(Customer Management DNS Resource RecordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractycThe Hypertext Jeopardy Protocol (HTJP) inverts the request/response semantics of the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).  Using conventional HTTP, one connects to a server, asks a question, and expects a correct answer.  Using HTJP, one connects to a server, sends an answer, and expects a correct question.  This document specifies the semantics of HTJP.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemE. FokschanerddatejœRhfilenamegrfc8565eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-90649hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\¢hÎgranking!ucrfcd8565irfcNumber!uestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&Hypertext Jeopardy Protocol (HTJP/1.0)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉPoint-to-multipoint (P2MP) Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) is designed to verify multipoint connectivity.  This document specifies the support of P2MP BFD in Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL).  Similar to TRILL point-to-point BFD, BFD Control packets in TRILL P2MP BFD are transmitted using RBridge Channel messages.  This document updates RFCs 7175 and 7177.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationfVmwarednamemS. Pallagatti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekV. Govindanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8564eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130378hkeywordsÉsdata center networkfswitchimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\¬Éägranking!tcrfcd8564irfcNumber!testateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSupport of Point-to-Multipoint Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) in Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÄThis document describes active tail extensions to the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol for multipoint networks.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamemS. Pallagatti¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. MirskyddatejÕ,hfilenamegrfc8563eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130377hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\§#∂granking!scrfcd8563irfcNumber!sestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) Multipoint Active TailsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÆThis document describes extensions to the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol for its use in multipoint and multicast networks.

This document updates RFC 5880.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamemS. Pallagatti¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirskyddatej‘áhfilenamegrfc8562eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130376hkeywordsÇcBFDnMultipoint BFDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\§#≥granking!rcrfcd8562irfcNumber!restateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for Multipoint NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9780∂habstractyÖThis document defines a YANG data model for control and management of radio link interfaces and their connectivity to packet (typically Ethernet) interfaces in a microwave/millimeter wave node.  The data nodes for management of the interface protection functionality is broken out into a separate and generic YANG data model in order to make it available for other interface types as well.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamejJ. Ahlberg¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeM. Ye¢kaffiliationwNEC Laboratories EuropednameeX. Li¢kaffiliationjNokia - ITdnamelD. Spreafico¢kaffiliationnAviat NetworksdnamekM. Vaupoticddatej’1hfilenamegrfc8561eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-130375hkeywordsÇxmicrowaveRadioLinkTerminalxmicrowaveCarrierTerminationkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDate]
h'granking!qcrfcd8561irfcNumber!qestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A YANG Data Model for Microwave Radio LinkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document specifies mechanisms for backward compatibility of Ethernet VPN (EVPN) and Provider Backbone Bridge Ethernet VPN (PBB-EVPN) solutions with Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) and Provider Backbone Bridge VPLS (PBB-VPLS) solutions.  It also provides mechanisms for the seamless integration of these two technologies in the same MPLS/IP network on a per-VPN-instance basis.  Implementation of this document enables service providers to introduce EVPN/PBB-EVPN Provider Edges (PEs) in their brownfield deployments of VPLS/PBB-VPLS networks.  This document specifies the control-plane and forwarding behavior needed for the auto-discovery of the following: 1) a VPN instance, 2) multicast and unicast operation, and 3) a Media Access Control (MAC) mobility operation.  This enables seamless integration between EVPN and VPLS PEs as well as between PBB-VPLS and PBB-EVPN PEs.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamelN. Del Regno¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatej÷Chfilenamegrfc8560eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130374hkeywordsádEVPNdVPLShPBB-EVPNhPBB-VPLSx!Ethernet Virtual Private NetworksxVirtual Private LAN ServicesxProvider Backbone BridgingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‘]„granking!pcrfcd8560irfcNumber!pestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexàSeamless Integration of Ethernet VPN (EVPN) with Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) and Their Provider Backbone Bridge (PBB) EquivalentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰RFC 5176 defines Change-of-Authorization (CoA) and Disconnect Message (DM) behavior for RADIUS.  RFC 5176 also suggests that proxying these messages is possible, but it does not provide guidance as to how that is done.  This specification updates RFC 5176 to correct that omission for scenarios where networks use realm-based proxying as defined in RFC 7542.  This specification also updates RFC 5580 to allow the Operator-Name attribute in CoA-Request and Disconnect-Request packets.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKok¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Korhonenddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc8559eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130373hkeywordsÑfRADIUSwChange of AuthorizationkCoA-RequestrDisconnect-RequestkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\≠iÚgranking!ocrfcd8559irfcNumber!oestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbDynamic Authorization Proxying in the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty	This document discusses the nature of signals seen by on-path elements examining transport protocols, contrasting implicit and explicit signals.  For example, TCP's state machine uses a series of well-known messages that are exchanged in the clear.  Because these are visible to network elements on the path between the two nodes setting up the transport connection, they are often used as signals by those network elements.  In transports that do not exchange these messages in the clear, on-path network elements lack those signals.  Often, the removal of those signals is intended by those moving the messages to confidential channels.  Where the endpoints desire that network elements along the path receive these signals, this document recommends explicit signals be used.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. HardieddatejÕ˜hfilenamegrfc8558eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130372hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate\≠iÒgranking!ncrfcd8558irfcNumber!nestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexTransport Protocol Path SignalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxàThis paper documents the needs in various industries to establish multi-hop paths for characterized flows with deterministic properties.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegN. Finn¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubertddatejœ—hfilenamegrfc8557eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdetnetdfullx!detnet - Deterministic NetworkingdnamexDeterministic Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130371hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‰øgranking!mcrfcd8557irfcNumber!mestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Deterministic Networking Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThe Multicast Virtual Private Network (MVPN) specifications require the use of multicast tunnels ("P-tunnels") that traverse a service provider's backbone network.  The P-tunnels are used for carrying multicast traffic across the backbone.  A variety of P-tunnel types are supported.  Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) is a new architecture that provides optimal multicast forwarding through a "multicast domain", without requiring intermediate routers to maintain any per-flow state or to engage in an explicit tree-building protocol.  This document specifies the protocol and procedures that allow MVPN to use BIER as the method of carrying multicast traffic over a service provider's backbone network.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamelM. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekA. Dolganowddatej”êhfilenamegrfc8556eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130370hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\´º#granking!lcrfcd8556irfcNumber!lestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Multicast VPN Using Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶Public Key Infrastructure using X.509 (PKIX) certificates are used for a number of purposes, the most significant of which is the authentication of domain names.  Thus, certification authorities (CAs) in the Web PKI are trusted to verify that an applicant for a certificate legitimately represents the domain name(s) in the certificate.  As of this writing, this verification is done through a collection of ad hoc mechanisms.  This document describes a protocol that a CA and an applicant can use to automate the process of verification and certificate issuance.  The protocol also provides facilities for other certificate management functions, such as certificate revocation.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationcEFFdnamerJ. Hoffman-Andrews¢kaffiliationmLet's EncryptdnamekD. McCarney¢kaffiliationvUniversity of MichigandnameiJ. Kastenddatej÷:hfilenamegrfc8555eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdacmedfullx3acme - Automated Certificate Management Environmentdnamex,Automated Certificate Management Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130369hkeywordsÑkcertificateeHTTPScPKIeX.509kobsoletedByÄepages_opublicationDate\ÜŒÄgranking!kcrfcd8555irfcNumber!kestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Automatic Certificate Management Environment (ACME)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyõThis note describes a digital-signature system based on cryptographic hash functions, following the seminal work in this area of Lamport, Diffie, Winternitz, and Merkle, as adapted by Leighton and Micali in 1995. It specifies a one-time signature scheme and a general signature scheme. These systems provide asymmetric authentication without using large integer mathematics and can achieve a high security level. They are suitable for compact implementations, are relatively simple to implement, and are naturally resistant to side-channel attacks. Unlike many other signature systems, hash-based signatures would still be secure even if it proves feasible for an attacker to build a quantum computer.

This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) in the IRTF. This has been reviewed by many researchers, both in the research group and outside of it. The Acknowledgements section lists many of them.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Curcio¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Fluhrerddatej÷Bhfilenamegrfc8554eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130368hkeywordsÉcLMScHSShstatefulkobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDate\«sÚgranking!jcrfcd8554irfcNumber!jestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex%Leighton-Micali Hash-Based SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty‹Using an underscore for a prefix creates a space for constrained interoperation of resource records.  Original uses of an underscore character as a domain node name prefix were specified without the benefit of an IANA registry.  This produced an entirely uncoordinated set of name-creation activities, all drawing from the same namespace.  A registry for these names has now been defined by RFC 8552.  However, the existing specifications that use underscored naming need to be modified in order to be in line with the new registry.  This document specifies those changes.  The changes preserve existing software and operational practice, while adapting the specifications for those practices to the newer underscore registry model.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej÷7hfilenamegrfc8553eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130367hkeywordsÇcDNSrDomain Name SystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\í”≠granking!icrfcd8553irfcNumber!iestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberﬁetotaletitlexKDNS Attrleaf Changes: Fixing Specifications That Use Underscored Node NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty_Formally, any DNS Resource Record (RR) may occur under any domain name.  However, some services use an operational convention for defining specific interpretations of an RRset by locating the records in a DNS branch under the parent domain to which the RRset actually applies.  The top of this subordinate branch is defined by a naming convention that uses a reserved node name, which begins with the underscore character (e.g., "_name").  The underscored naming construct defines a semantic scope for DNS record types that are associated with the parent domain above the underscored branch.  This specification explores the nature of this DNS usage and defines the "Underscored and Globally Scoped DNS Node Names" registry with IANA.  The purpose of this registry is to avoid collisions resulting from the use of the same underscored name for different services.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej÷7hfilenamegrfc8552eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130366hkeywordsÇcDNSrDomain Name SystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\í”´granking!hcrfcd8552irfcNumber!hestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberﬁetotaletitlex^Scoped Interpretation of DNS Resource Records through "Underscored" Naming of Attribute LeavesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document defines Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) version 4.0.  S/MIME provides a consistent way to send and receive secure MIME data.  Digital signatures provide authentication, message integrity, and non-repudiation with proof of origin.  Encryption provides data confidentiality.  Compression can be used to reduce data size.  This document obsoletes RFC 5751.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationvBrute Squad Labs, Inc.dnamekB. Ramsdell¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷1hfilenamegrfc8551eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130365hkeywordsÅfS/MIMEkobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDate\»ÒÃgranking!gcrfcd8551irfcNumber!gestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWSecure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Version 4.0 Message SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9788∂habstractyDThis document specifies conventions for X.509 certificate usage by Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) v4.0 agents.  S/MIME provides a method to send and receive secure MIME messages, and certificates are an integral part of S/MIME agent processing.  S/MIME agents validate certificates as described in RFC 5280 ("Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Profile").  S/MIME agents must meet the certificate-processing requirements in this document as well as those in RFC 5280.  This document obsoletes RFC 5750.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationvBrute Squad Labs, Inc.dnamekB. Ramsdell¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷"hfilenamegrfc8550eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130364hkeywordsÅfS/MIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\»Ò…granking!fcrfcd8550irfcNumber!festateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSecure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Version 4.0 Certificate HandlingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√By introducing new Information Elements (IEs), this document extends the existing BGP-related IEs to enable IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) to export BGP community information, including the BGP Standard Communities defined in RFC 1997, BGP Extended Communities defined in RFC 4360, and BGP Large Communities defined in RFC 8092.  According to the network operator's BGP community planning, network traffic information can then be accumulated and analyzed at the BGP community granularity, which represents the traffic of different kinds of customers, services, or geographical regions.  Network traffic information at the BGP community granularity is useful for network traffic analysis and engineering.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeR. Gu¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dongddatej÷Bhfilenamegrfc8549eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130363hkeywordsÉicommunitycBGPeIPFIXkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\§#∞granking!ecrfcd8549irfcNumber!eestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIExport of BGP Community Information in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯This document specifies "tcpcrypt", a TCP encryption protocol designed for use in conjunction with the TCP Encryption Negotiation Option (TCP-ENO).  Tcpcrypt coexists with middleboxes by tolerating resegmentation, NATs, and other manipulations of the TCP header.  The protocol is self-contained and specifically tailored to TCP implementations, which often reside in kernels or other environments in which large external software dependencies can be undesirable.  Because the size of TCP options is limited, the protocol requires one additional one-way message latency to perform key exchange before application data can be transmitted.  However, the extra latency can be avoided between two hosts that have recently established a previous tcpcrypt connection.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiA. Bittau¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnameiD. Giffin¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamekD. Mazieres¢kaffiliationkSourcegraphdnamehQ. Slack¢kaffiliationqKestrel InstitutednamehE. Smithddatej÷@hfilenamegrfc8548eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftcpincdfullxtcpinc - TCP Increased SecuritydnamevTCP Increased Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130362hkeywordsÇctcpjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate\Âpbgranking!dcrfcd8548irfcNumber!destateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Cryptographic Protection of TCP Streams (tcpcrypt)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”Despite growing adoption of TLS, a significant fraction of TCP traffic on the Internet remains unencrypted.  The persistence of unencrypted traffic can be attributed to at least two factors.  First, some legacy protocols lack a signaling mechanism (such as a STARTTLS command) by which to convey support for encryption, thus making incremental deployment impossible.  Second, legacy applications themselves cannot always be upgraded and therefore require a way to implement encryption transparently entirely within the transport layer.  The TCP Encryption Negotiation Option (TCP-ENO) addresses both of these problems through a new TCP option kind providing out-of-band, fully backward-compatible negotiation of encryption.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiA. Bittau¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnameiD. Giffin¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamekD. Mazieres¢kaffiliationqKestrel InstitutednamehE. Smithddatej÷@hfilenamegrfc8547eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftcpincdfullxtcpinc - TCP Increased SecuritydnamevTCP Increased Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130361hkeywordsÇctcpjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Âp\granking!ccrfcd8547irfcNumber!cestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&TCP-ENO: Encryption Negotiation OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document defines the wire image, an abstraction of the information available to an on-path non-participant in a networking protocol.  This abstraction is intended to shed light on the implications that increased encryption has for network functions that use the wire image.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamemM. KuehlewindddatejœŒhfilenamegrfc8546eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130360hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate\∂óˇgranking!bcrfcd8546irfcNumber!bestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex$The Wire Image of a Network ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis memo explains the motivation and describes the reassignment of well-known ports for the One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) and the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP) for control and measurement. It also clarifies the meaning and composition of these Standards Track protocol names for the industry.

This memo updates RFCs 4656 and 5357, in terms of the UDP well-known port assignments, and it clarifies the complete OWAMP and TWAMP protocol composition for the industry.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirskyddatej÷;hfilenamegrfc8545eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130359hkeywordsÇeOWAMPeTWAMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\á/ˆgranking!acrfcd8545irfcNumber!aestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉWell-Known Port Assignments for the One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) and the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This document describes an extension to Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) object mappings that is designed to support assigning an organization to any existing object (domain, host, contact) as well as any future objects.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegL. Zhou¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegN. Kong¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Wei¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Yao¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gouldddatej‘\hfilenamegrfc8544eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130358hkeywordsÅx"epp organization mapping extensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\≠iÓgranking!`crfcd8544irfcNumber!`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEOrganization Extension for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx®This document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for provisioning and management of organization objects stored in a shared central repository.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegL. Zhou¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegN. Kong¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Yao¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliation`dnamegG. Zhouddatej”©hfilenamegrfc8543eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130357hkeywordsÅx(epp registry organization object mappingkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate\óí-granking!_crfcd8543irfcNumber!_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Organization MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a YANG data model for fabric topology in data- center networks and represents one possible view of the data-center fabric.  This document focuses on the data model only and does not endorse any kind of network design that could be based on the abovementioned model.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiY. Zhuang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefD. Shi¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeR. Gu¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamerH. Ananthakrishnanddatej÷8hfilenamegrfc8542eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-130356hkeywordsÉdYANGoFabric TopologytData-Center NetworkskobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate\ô!‹granking!^crfcd8542irfcNumber!^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=A YANG Data Model for Fabric Topology in Data-Center NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêA micro-loop is a packet-forwarding loop that may occur transiently among two or more routers in a hop-by-hop packet-forwarding paradigm.

This document analyzes the impact of using different link state IGP implementations in a single network with respect to micro-loops. The analysis is focused on the Shortest Path First (SPF) delay algorithm but also mentions the impact of SPF trigger strategies.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwOrange Business ServicednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamelM. Hornefferddatej‘ïhfilenamegrfc8541eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130355hkeywordsÇeIS-ISdOSPFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\ì*ügranking!]crfcd8541irfcNumber!]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSImpact of Shortest Path First (SPF) Trigger and Delay Strategies on IGP Micro-loopsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document is a compilation of issues found since the publication of RFC 4960 in September 2007, based on experience with implementing, testing, and using the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) along with the suggested fixes.  This document provides deltas to RFC 4960 and is organized in a time-ordered way.  The issues are listed in the order in which they were brought up.  Because some text is changed several times, the last delta in the text is the one that should be applied.  In addition to the deltas, a description of each problem and the details of the solution for each are also provided.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNetflix, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejM. Proshinddatejœ∆hfilenamegrfc8540eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130354hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9260epages^opublicationDate\l¸Egranking!\crfcd8540irfcNumber!\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCStream Control Transmission Protocol: Errata and Issues in RFC 4960dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6DHCPv4 over DHCPv6 (RFC 7341) is a mechanism for dynamically configuring IPv4 for use as an over-the-top service in an IPv6-only network. Softwires are an example of such a service. For DHCPv4 over DHCPv6 (DHCP 4o6) to function with some IPv4-over-IPv6 softwire mechanisms and deployment scenarios (e.g., RFC 7596 or RFC 7597), the operator needs to know the IPv6 address that the client will use as the source of an IPv4-in-IPv6 softwire tunnel. This address, in conjunction with the client's IPv4 address, and (in some deployments) the Port Set ID are used to create a binding table entry in the operator's softwire tunnel concentrator. This memo defines a DHCPv6 option to convey IPv6 parameters for establishing the softwire tunnel and a DHCPv4 option (to be used only with DHCP 4o6) to communicate the source tunnel IPv6 address between the DHCP 4o6 client and server. It is designed to work in conjunction with the IPv4 address allocation process.

"DHCPv6 Options for Configuration of Softwire Address and Port-Mapped Clients" (RFC 7598) describes a deterministic DHCPv6-based mechanism for provisioning softwires. This document updates RFC 7598, allowing OPTION_S46_BR (90) to be enumerated in the DHCPv6 client's Option Request Option (ORO) request and to appear directly within subsequent messages sent by the DHCPv6 server.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefL. Sunddatej÷@hfilenamegrfc8539eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130353hkeywordsàlProvisioninghSoftwirehDHCP 4o6nIPv4 over IPv6wIPv4 service continuityvIPv4 address depletioncMAPrLightweight 4over6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\å0tgranking![crfcd8539irfcNumber![estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Softwire Provisioning Using DHCPv4 over DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝The BGP Graceful Restart mechanism defined in RFC 4724 limits the usage of BGP Graceful Restart to BGP messages other than BGP NOTIFICATION messages.  This document updates RFC 4724 by defining an extension that permits the Graceful Restart procedures to be performed when the BGP speaker receives a BGP NOTIFICATION message or the Hold Time expires.  This document also defines a new subcode for BGP Cease NOTIFICATION messages; this new subcode requests a full session restart instead of a Graceful Restart.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Fernando¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegJ. Haasddatej”Çhfilenamegrfc8538eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130352hkeywordsÇcIDRcBGPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate\Éøgranking!Zcrfcd8538irfcNumber!ZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Notification Message Support for BGP Graceful RestartdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) association signaling can be used to bind two unidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs) into an associated bidirectional LSP.  When an associated bidirectional LSP is co-routed, the reverse LSP follows the same path as its forward LSP.  This document updates the fast reroute procedures defined in RFC 4090 to support both single-sided and double-sided provisioned associated bidirectional LSPs.  This document also updates the procedure for associating two reverse LSPs defined in RFC 7551 to support co-routed bidirectional LSPs.  The fast reroute procedures can ensure that, for the co-routed LSPs, traffic flows on co-routed paths in the forward and reverse directions after a failure event.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationeCienadnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamelJ. Whittakerddatej÷9hfilenamegrfc8537eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130351hkeywordsÅkRSVP-TE LSPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\gaágranking!Ycrfcd8537irfcNumber!YestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiUpdates to the Fast Reroute Procedures for Co-routed Associated Bidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹This document specifies the Time Zone Information Format (TZif) for representing and exchanging time zone information, independent of any particular service or protocol.  Two media types for this format are also defined.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Olson¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiP. Eggert¢kaffiliationhFastMaildnamelK. Murchisonddatej÷5hfilenamegrfc8536eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130350hkeywordsÉitime zoneftzdatadtzifkobsoletedByÅd9636epages"opublicationDate\c1Vgranking!Xcrfcd8536irfcNumber!XestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'The Time Zone Information Format (TZif)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThe base Multicast VPN (MVPN) specifications (RFCs 6513 and 6514) provide procedures to allow a multicast ingress node to invoke "explicit tracking" for a multicast flow or set of flows, thus learning the egress nodes for that flow or set of flows.  However, the specifications are not completely clear about how the explicit tracking procedures work in certain scenarios.  This document provides the necessary clarifications.  It also specifies a new, optimized explicit-tracking procedure.  This new procedure allows an ingress node, by sending a single message, to request explicit tracking of each of a set of flows, where the set of flows is specified using a wildcard mechanism.  This document updates RFCs 6514, 6625, 7524, 7582, and 7900.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekA. Dolganow¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Kotalwar¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehZ. Zhangddatej÷5hfilenamegrfc8534eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130349hkeywordsÅnMulticast MVPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\l¸Cgranking!Vcrfcd8534irfcNumber!VestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Explicit Tracking with Wildcard Routes in Multicast VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡This document presents a retrieval method YANG data model for connectionless Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) protocols.  It provides technology-independent RPC operations for OAM protocols that use connectionless communication.  The retrieval methods model herein presented can be extended to include technology- specific details.  There are two key benefits of this approach: First, it leads to uniformity between OAM protocols.  Second, it supports both nested OAM workflows (i.e., performing OAM functions at different or the same levels through a unified interface) as well as interactive OAM workflows (i.e., performing OAM functions at the same levels through a unified interface).fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Kumar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Wang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekS. Raghavanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8533eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlimedfullxFlime - Layer Independent OAM Management in the Multi-Layer Environmentdnamex?Layer Independent OAM Management in the Multi-Layer Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130348hkeywordsÅxCL OAM Retrieval MethodskobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate\∂Éˇgranking!Ucrfcd8533irfcNumber!UestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex†A YANG Data Model for Retrieval Methods for the Management of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Protocols That Use Connectionless CommunicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document presents a base YANG Data model for the management of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) protocols that use connectionless communications. The data model is defined using the YANG data modeling language, as specified in RFC 7950. It provides a technology-independent abstraction of key OAM constructs for OAM protocols that use connectionless communication. The base model presented here can be extended to include technology-specific details.

There are two key benefits of this approach: First, it leads to uniformity between OAM protocols. Second, it supports both nested OAM workflows (i.e., performing OAM functions at the same level or different levels through a unified interface) as well as interactive OAM workflows (i.e., performing OAM functions at the same level through a unified interface).fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Kumar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegZ. Wang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekS. Raghavanddatejœ•hfilenamegrfc8532eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlimedfullxFlime - Layer Independent OAM Management in the Multi-Layer Environmentdnamex?Layer Independent OAM Management in the Multi-Layer Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130347hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDate\∂É˝granking!Tcrfcd8532irfcNumber!TestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexêGeneric YANG Data Model for the Management of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Protocols That Use Connectionless CommunicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document presents a base YANG data model for connection-oriented Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) protocols. It provides a technology-independent abstraction of key OAM constructs for such protocols. The model presented here can be extended to include technology-specific details. This guarantees uniformity in the management of OAM protocols and provides support for nested OAM workflows (i.e., performing OAM functions at different levels through a unified interface).

The YANG data model in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Kumar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegZ. Wangddatejœ[hfilenamegrfc8531eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlimedfullxFlime - Layer Independent OAM Management in the Multi-Layer Environmentdnamex?Layer Independent OAM Management in the Multi-Layer Environmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130346hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDate\∂É˙granking!Scrfcd8531irfcNumber!SestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkGeneric YANG Data Model for Connection-Oriented Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThis document defines a logical network element (LNE) YANG module that is compliant with the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA).  This module can be used to manage the logical resource partitioning that may be present on a network device.  Examples of common industry terms for logical resource partitioning are logical systems or logical routers.  The YANG model in this document conforms with NMDA as defined in RFC 8342.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehC. Hopps¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamemD. Bogdanovic¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liuddatej’Îhfilenamegrfc8530eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130345hkeywordsÉcVRFcVSIcVPNkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDate\Ä¶°granking!Rcrfcd8530irfcNumber!RestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'YANG Model for Logical Network ElementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document defines a network instance module. This module can be used to manage the virtual resource partitioning that may be present on a network device. Examples of common industry terms for virtual resource partitioning are VPN Routing and Forwarding (VRF) instances and Virtual Switch Instances (VSIs).

The YANG data model in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) defined in RFC 8342.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnamehC. Hopps¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamemD. Bogdanovic¢kaffiliationnVolta NetworksdnamefX. Liuddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8529eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130344hkeywordsÉcVRFcVSIcVPNkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDate\Ä¶†granking!Qcrfcd8529irfcNumber!QestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%YANG Data Model for Network InstancesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx£This document defines a mechanism that adds the schema trees defined by a set of YANG modules onto a mount point defined in the schema tree in another YANG module.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnameiL. Lhotkaddatejœ»hfilenamegrfc8528eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130343hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Ä¶†granking!Pcrfcd8528irfcNumber!PestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqYANG Schema MountdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrThis document extends the RESTCONF protocol defined in RFC 8040 in order to support the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) defined in RFC 8342.

This document updates RFC 8040 by introducing new datastore resources, adding a new query parameter, and requiring the usage of the YANG library (described in RFC 8525) by RESTCONF servers implementing the NMDA.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiP. Shafer¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Wiltonddatejœƒhfilenamegrfc8527eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130342hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate\Ä¶ügranking!Ocrfcd8527irfcNumber!OestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLRESTCONF Extensions to Support the Network Management Datastore ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document extends the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) defined in RFC 6241 in order to support the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) defined in RFC 8342.

This document updates RFCs 6241 and 7950. The update to RFC 6241 adds new <get-data> and <edit-data> operations and augments existing <lock>, <unlock>, and <validate> operations. The update to RFC 7950 requires the usage of the YANG library (described in RFC 8525) by NETCONF servers implementing the NMDA.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiP. Shafer¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Wiltonddatej”hfilenamegrfc8526eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130341hkeywordsÅdNMDAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Ä¶ûgranking!Ncrfcd8526irfcNumber!NestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKNETCONF Extensions to Support the Network Management Datastore ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document describes a YANG library that provides information about the YANG modules, datastores, and datastore schemas used by a network management server.  Simple caching mechanisms are provided to allow clients to minimize retrieval of this information.  This version of the YANG library supports the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) by listing all datastores supported by a network management server and the schema that is used by each of these datastores.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationoWatsen NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Wiltonddatej÷4hfilenamegrfc8525eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130340hkeywordsÅdNMDAkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate\Ä¶úgranking!Mcrfcd8525irfcNumber!MestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlelYANG LibrarydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyhThis document introduces a command set standard to the web-based "Network Looking Glass" software. Its purpose is to provide application programmers uniform access to the Looking Glass service and to analyze a standardized response.

The interface is supposed to provide the same level of information as web-based interfaces, but in a computer-readable format.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejM. Stubbigddatej÷6hfilenamegrfc8522eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130339hkeywordsÅmLooking GlasskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Z)Agranking!Jcrfcd8522irfcNumber!JestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLooking Glass Command SetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyèThe Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) includes a method that can be used to identify the authoritative server for processing domain name, IP address, and autonomous system number queries.  The method does not describe how to identify the authoritative server for processing other RDAP query types, such as entity queries.  This limitation exists because the identifiers associated with these query types are typically unstructured.  This document updates RFC 7484 by describing an operational practice that can be used to add structure to RDAP identifiers and that makes it possible to identify the authoritative server for additional RDAP queries.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationdARINdnameiA. Newtonddatej’éhfilenamegrfc8521eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130338hkeywordsÉdRDAPfEntityiBootstrapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ÚËÆgranking!Icrfcd8521irfcNumber!IestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber›etotaletitlex7Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Object TaggingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo specifies a component-based architecture for Manufacturer Usage Descriptions (MUDs). The goal of MUD is to provide a means for end devices to signal to the network what sort of access and network functionality they require to properly function. The initial focus is on access control. Later work can delve into other aspects.

This memo specifies two YANG modules, IPv4 and IPv6 DHCP options, a Link Layer Discovery Protocol (LLDP) TLV, a URL, an X.509 certificate extension, and a means to sign and verify the descriptions.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehR. Droms¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. Romascanuddatej÷(hfilenamegrfc8520eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130337hkeywordsÉcMUDlIoT SecuritymAccess PolicykobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDate\àq√granking!Hcrfcd8520irfcNumber!HestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Manufacturer Usage Description SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This document defines a data model for Access Control Lists (ACLs).  An ACL is a user-ordered set of rules used to configure the forwarding behavior in a device.  Each rule is used to find a match on a packet and define actions that will be performed on the packet.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfVMwarednameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Agarwal¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Huang¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Blairddatej÷6hfilenamegrfc8519eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130336hkeywordsÖcACEcACLhFirewallcPBRdNMDAkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDate\àqægranking!Gcrfcd8519irfcNumber!GestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7YANG Data Model for Network Access Control Lists (ACLs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝Deployment experience gained from implementing algorithms to determine Loop-Free Alternates (LFAs) for multi-homed prefixes (MHPs) has revealed some avenues for potential improvement.  This document provides explicit inequalities that can be used to evaluate neighbors as potential alternates for MHPs.  It also provides detailed criteria for evaluating potential alternates for external prefixes advertised by OSPF ASBRs.  This document updates Section 6 of RFC 5286 by expanding some of the routing aspects.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnameiP. Sarkar¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamekU. Chunduri¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationmRtBrick, Inc.dnamejH. Gredlerddatej÷8hfilenamegrfc8518eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130335hkeywordsÉcLFArMulti-homed PrefixcIGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\á/Úgranking!Fcrfcd8518irfcNumber!FestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Selection of Loop-Free Alternates for Multi-Homed PrefixesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document summarizes an operator's perception of the benefits that may be provided by intermediary devices that execute functions beyond normal IP forwarding. Such intermediary devices are often called "middleboxes".

RFC 3234 defines a taxonomy of middleboxes and issues in the Internet. Most of those middleboxes utilize or modify application- layer data. This document primarily focuses on devices that observe and act on information carried in the transport layer, and especially information carried in TCP packets.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Dolson¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Snellman¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenetddatej÷9hfilenamegrfc8517eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130334hkeywordsâoaddress sharingcNAThfirewallpService Functionitransportpservice deliveryuInternet architecturehTCP QUICxPath Layer UDP SubstratekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Z)?granking!Ecrfcd8517irfcNumber!EestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex\An Inventory of Transport-Centric Functions Provided by Middleboxes: An Operator PerspectivedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNA Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) server can experience temporary overload because one or more clients are sending requests to the server at a higher rate than the server is capable or willing to handle.  This document defines a new CoAP response code for a server to indicate that a client should reduce the rate of requests.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranenddatej÷6hfilenamegrfc8516eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130333hkeywordsÅdCoAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\GÚAgranking!Dcrfcd8516irfcNumber!DestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJ"Too Many Requests" Response Code for the Constrained Application ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) "etsi" for Uniform Resource Names (URNs) used to identify resources published by the European Telecommunications Standards Institute (http://etsi.org).  ETSI specifies and manages resources that utilize this URN identification model.  Management activities for these and other resource types are handled by the manager of the ETSI Protocol Naming and Numbering Service (PNNS).fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfVMwarednameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationdETSIdnameqM.A. Reina Ortegaddatej÷6hfilenamegrfc8515eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130332hkeywordsÉdYANGgNETCONFhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\vqgranking!Ccrfcd8515irfcNumber!CestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex URN Namespace for ETSI DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThis document adds a new capability called "SAVEDATE" to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP).  It defines a new IMAP message attribute called "save date" that, unlike the existing "internal date" attribute, always indicates the moment at which the message was saved in its current mailbox.  The SAVEDATE capability extends the FETCH command with the means to retrieve the save date attribute and extends the SEARCH command to allow using the save date attribute in searching criteria.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoOpen Xchange OydnamehS. Boschddatej÷6hfilenamegrfc8514eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130331hkeywordsÇdimaphsavedatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\/ªDgranking!Bcrfcd8514irfcNumber!BestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - SAVEDATE ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxôThis document defines a YANG module for the Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite) Address Family Transition Router (AFTR) and Basic Bridging BroadBand (B4) elements.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenet¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumarddatej’hfilenamegrfc8513eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-130330hkeywordsáwIPv4 service continuitywIPv4 address exhaustiontService AvailabilityoAddress sharingdIPv6kReliabilitynIPv4 over IPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\>®'granking!Acrfcd8513irfcNumber!AestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/A YANG Data Model for Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑This document defines a YANG module for the Network Address Translation (NAT) function.

Network Address Translation from IPv4 to IPv4 (NAT44), Network Address and Protocol Translation from IPv6 Clients to IPv4 Servers (NAT64), customer-side translator (CLAT), Stateless IP/ICMP Translation (SIIT), Explicit Address Mappings (EAM) for SIIT, IPv6-to-IPv6 Network Prefix Translation (NPTv6), and Destination NAT are covered in this document.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenet¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemS. Vinapamula¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej÷3hfilenamegrfc8512eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130329hkeywordséoaddress sharingqaddress depletionwIPv4 service continuitygNETCONFoprogrammabilityjautomationrservice automationeNPTv6dSIITeNAT64dCLAToDestination NATsPort Restricted NATjPort RangekobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDate\>®#granking!@crfcd8512irfcNumber!@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXA YANG Module for Network Address Translation (NAT) and Network Prefix Translation (NPT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöActive Queue Management (AQM) mechanisms allow for burst tolerance while enforcing short queues to minimise the time that packets spend enqueued at a bottleneck.  This can cause noticeable performance degradation for TCP connections traversing such a bottleneck, especially if there are only a few flows or their bandwidth-delay product (BDP) is large.  The reception of a Congestion Experienced (CE) Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) mark indicates that an AQM mechanism is used at the bottleneck, and the bottleneck network queue is therefore likely to be short.  Feedback of this signal allows the TCP sender-side ECN reaction in congestion avoidance to reduce the Congestion Window (cwnd) by a smaller amount than the congestion control algorithm's reaction to inferred packet loss.  Therefore, this specification defines an experimental change to the TCP reaction specified in RFC 3168, as permitted by RFC 8311.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamejN. Khademi¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamekG. Armitage¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatejœ«hfilenamegrfc8511eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130328hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\o7granking!?crfcd8511irfcNumber!?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&TCP Alternative Backoff with ECN (ABE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOEvery OSPF interface is assigned an Interface ID that uniquely identifies the interface on the router. In some cases, it is useful to know the assigned Interface ID on the remote side of the adjacency (Remote Interface ID).

This document describes the extensions to OSPF link-local signaling (LLS) to advertise the Local Interface ID.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. Talaulikar¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnameqP. Pillay-Esnaultddatej÷5hfilenamegrfc8510eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130327hkeywordsÇcIGPdOSPFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\JMægranking!>crfcd8510irfcNumber!>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOOSPF Link-Local Signaling (LLS) Extensions for Local Interface ID AdvertisementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[The DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) were developed to provide origin authentication and integrity protection for DNS data by using digital signatures.  These digital signatures can be verified by building a chain of trust starting from a trust anchor and proceeding down to a particular node in the DNS.  This document specifies a mechanism that will allow an end user and third parties to determine the trusted key state for the root key of the resolvers that handle that user's DNS queries.  Note that this method is only applicable for determining which keys are in the trust store for the root key.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamehJ. Damas¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej÷4hfilenamegrfc8509eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130326hkeywordsâfDNSSECcKSKgRFC5011cDNShrolloverxroot-key-sentinel-is-ta-xroot-key-sentinel-not-ta-hroot keyhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\o6granking!=crfcd8509irfcNumber!=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+A Root Key Trust Anchor Sentinel for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˆThis document defines an IMAP extension that can be used to replace an existing message in a message store with a new message.  Message replacement is a common operation for clients that automatically save drafts or notes as a user composes them.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnameiS. Brandtddatejœƒhfilenamegrfc8508eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130325hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\/ªBgranking!<crfcd8508irfcNumber!<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIMAP REPLACE ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty3This document is published for the historical record. The Simple Internet Protocol was the basis for one of the candidates for the IETF's Next Generation (IPng) work that became IPv6.

The publication date of the original Internet-Draft was November 10, 1992. It is presented here substantially unchanged and is neither a complete document nor intended to be implementable.

The paragraph that follows is the Abstract from the original draft.

This document specifies a new version of IP called SIP, the Simple Internet Protocol. It also describes the changes needed to ICMP, IGMP, and transport protocols such as TCP and UDP, in order to work with SIP. A companion document [SIP-ADDR] describes the addressing and routing aspects of SIP, including issues of auto-configuration, host and subnet mobility, and multicast.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRetireddnamejS. Deering¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hindenddatej÷3hfilenamegrfc8507eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130324hkeywordsÇdIPv6dIPngkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\êÒgranking!;crfcd8507irfcNumber!;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,Simple Internet Protocol (SIP) SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document specifies a Diameter application that can be used to implement real-time credit-control for a variety of end-user services such as network access, Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) services, messaging services, and download services.  The Diameter Credit- Control application as defined in this document obsoletes RFC 4006, and it must be supported by all new Diameter Credit-Control application implementations.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfSprintdnamehL. Bertz¢kaffiliationhSandvinednameiD. Dolson¢kaffiliationhSandvinednamekY. Lifshitzddatej÷0hfilenamegrfc8506eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130323hkeywordsÇhDiameterhchargingkobsoletedByÄepagesÇopublicationDate\yc⁄granking!:crfcd8506irfcNumber!:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Diameter Credit-Control ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆThis specification updates RFC 6775 -- the Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) Neighbor Discovery specification -- to clarify the role of the protocol as a registration technique and simplify the registration operation in 6LoWPAN routers, as well as to provide enhancements to the registration capabilities and mobility detection for different network topologies, including the Routing Registrars performing routing for host routes and/or proxy Neighbor Discovery in a low-power network.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationfZededadnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamejC. Perkinsddatej÷.hfilenamegrfc8505eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130322hkeywordsÅeWi-FikobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDate[Ïé-granking!9crfcd8505irfcNumber!9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkRegistration Extensions for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) Neighbor DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÖd8928d8929d9926d9010d9685∑habstractyJThis document defines requirements for IPv6 nodes. It is expected that IPv6 will be deployed in a wide range of devices and situations. Specifying the requirements for IPv6 nodes allows IPv6 to function well and interoperate in a large number of situations and deployments.

This document obsoletes RFC 6434, and in turn RFC 4294.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdJiscdnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationgUNH-IOLdnamejT. Wintersddatej÷-hfilenamegrfc8504eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130321hkeywordsÑdIPv6xInternet Protocol Version 6qInternet ProtocolbIPkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate\QÔvgranking!8crfcd8504irfcNumber!8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‹etotaletitlevIPv6 Node RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects to configure and/or monitor Multicast communication over IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) supported by the Multiprotocol Label Switching/Border Gateway Protocol (MPLS/BGP) on a Provider Edge (PE) router.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxTohoku Institute of TechnologydnamejH. Tsunodaddatej÷1hfilenamegrfc8503eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130320hkeywordsádMVPNiPE routerhP-tunneldPMSIcMIBdSNMPgmonitorkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDate\êgranking!7crfcd8503irfcNumber!7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:BGP/MPLS Layer 3 VPN Multicast Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes two MIB modules that will be used by other MIB modules for monitoring and/or configuring Layer 2 and Layer 3 Virtual Private Networks that support multicast.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationxTohoku Institute of TechnologydnamejH. Tsunodaddatej’ñhfilenamegrfc8502eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130319hkeywordsàdMVPNcBGPdMPLShP-tunneluPMSI Tunnel attributedSNMPgmonitorjmanagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\êÌgranking!6crfcd8502irfcNumber!6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevL2L3 VPN Multicast MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBIn IPv4, Internet Service Providers (ISPs) commonly provide IN-ADDR.ARPA information for their customers by prepopulating the zone with one PTR record for every available address.  This practice does not scale in IPv6.  This document analyzes different approaches and considerations for ISPs in managing the IP6.ARPA zone.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgReteviadnameiL. Howardddatej÷2hfilenamegrfc8501eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130318hkeywordsÑdIPv6cPTRdrDNSkReverse DNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[˛µËgranking!5crfcd8501irfcNumber!5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Reverse DNS in IPv6 for Internet Service ProvidersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis document describes a mechanism to allow IS-IS routing to quickly and accurately shift traffic away from either a point-to-point or multi-access LAN interface during network maintenance or other operational events.  This is accomplished by signaling adjacent IS-IS neighbors with a higher reverse metric, i.e., the metric towards the signaling IS-IS router.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnameiS. Amante¢kaffiliationpT-Systems NordicdnamenM. Abrahamssonddatej÷:hfilenamegrfc8500eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130317hkeywordsÖcIGPeIS-ISfMetricnReverse-MetriccIIHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\x?granking!4crfcd8500irfcNumber!4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!IS-IS Routing with Reverse MetricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThe Domain Name System (DNS) is defined in literally dozens of different RFCs. The terminology used by implementers and developers of DNS protocols, and by operators of DNS systems, has sometimes changed in the decades since the DNS was first defined. This document gives current definitions for many of the terms used in the DNS in a single document.

This document obsoletes RFC 7719 and updates RFC 2308.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliation`dnamekA. Sullivan¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwaraddatej÷#hfilenamegrfc8499eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130316hkeywordsÇjvocabularyrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÅd9499epages2opublicationDate\-w;granking!3crfcd8499irfcNumber!3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleoDNS TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©The P-Served-User header field was defined based on a requirement from the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) IMS (IP Multimedia Subsystem) in order to convey the identity of the served user, his/ her registration state, and the session case that applies to that particular communication session and application invocation.  A session case is metadata that captures the status of the session of a served user regardless of whether or not the served user is registered or the session originates or terminates with the served user.  This document updates RFC 5502 by defining a new P-Served-User header field parameter, "orig-cdiv".  The parameter conveys the session case used by a proxy when handling an originating session after Call Diversion (CDIV) services have been invoked for the served user.  This document also fixes the ABNF in RFC 5502 and provides more guidance for using the P-Served-User header field in IP networks.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOrangednameiM. Mohaliddatej÷9hfilenamegrfc8498eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130315hkeywordsácSIPgRFC5502bP-d3GPPcIMSkServed-Useriorig-cdivkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\fRœgranking!2crfcd8498irfcNumber!2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÖA P-Served-User Header Field Parameter for an Originating Call Diversion (CDIV) Session Case in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑSIP networks use signaling monitoring tools to diagnose user-reported problems and to perform regression testing if network or user agent (UA) software is upgraded. As networks grow and become interconnected, including connection via transit networks, it becomes impractical to predict the path that SIP signaling will take between user agents and therefore impractical to monitor SIP signaling end to end.

This document describes an indicator for the SIP protocol that can be used to mark signaling as being of interest to logging. Such marking will typically be applied as part of network testing controlled by the network operator and is not used in normal user agent signaling. Operators of all networks on the signaling path can agree to carry such marking end to end, including the originating and terminating SIP user agents, even if a session originates and terminates in different networks.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVodafone GroupdnamehP. Dawes¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenC. Arunachalamddatej÷3hfilenamegrfc8497eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymginsipiddfullx*insipid - INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdnamex INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdtypebwgbidjdoc-130314hkeywordsÜcSIPelogmeotroubleshootingedebugidebugginggloggingkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDate[˝·◊granking!1crfcd8497irfcNumber!1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Marking SIP Messages to Be LoggeddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis text documents the current usage of P-Charge-Info, an existing Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) private header field (P-Header) used to convey billing information about the party to be charged.  This P-Header is currently used in production by several equipment vendors and carriers and has been in use since at least 2007.  This document details the registration of this header field with IANA.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamegD. York¢kaffiliationuRibbon CommunicationsdnamejT. Asverenddatej÷1hfilenamegrfc8496eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130313hkeywordsÅhp-headerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[⁄ãgranking!0crfcd8496irfcNumber!0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcP-Charge-Info: A Private Header Field (P-Header) Extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension for including an Allocation Token in "query" and "transform" commands.  The Allocation Token is used as a credential that authorizes a client to request the allocation of a specific object from the server using one of the EPP transform commands, including "create" and "transfer".fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamehK. Feherddatejœ¿hfilenamegrfc8495eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130312hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[˝·’granking!/crfcd8495irfcNumber!/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIAllocation Token Extension for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyAllied Communications Publication (ACP) 142 defines P_MUL, which is a protocol for reliable multicast suitable for bandwidth-constrained and delayed acknowledgement (Emissions Control or "EMCON") environments running over UDP. This document defines MULE (Multicast Email), an application protocol for transferring Internet Mail messages (as described in RFC 5322) over P_MUL (as defined in ACP 142). MULE enables transfer between Message Transfer Agents (MTAs). It doesn't provide a service similar to SMTP Submission (as described in RFC 6409).

This document explains how MULE can be used in conjunction with SMTP (RFC 5321), including some common SMTP extensions, to provide an alternate MTA-to-MTA transfer mechanism.

This is not an IETF specification; it describes an existing implementation. It is provided in order to facilitate interoperable implementations and third-party diagnostics.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnameiD. Wilson¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej÷2hfilenamegrfc8494eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130311hkeywordsÅeP_MULkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Ïé,granking!.crfcd8494irfcNumber!.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGMulticast Email (MULE) over Allied Communications Publication (ACP) 142dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyhThis document describes BagIt, a set of hierarchical file layout conventions for storage and transfer of arbitrary digital content.  A "bag" has just enough structure to enclose descriptive metadata "tags" and a file "payload" but does not require knowledge of the payload's internal semantics.  This BagIt format is suitable for reliable storage and transfer.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxCalifornia Digital LibrarydnamehJ. Kunze¢kaffiliationrStanford LibrariesdnamejJ. Littman¢kaffiliationsLibrary of CongressdnameiE. Madden¢kaffiliation`dnamelJ. Scancella¢kaffiliationsLibrary of CongressdnamehC. Adamsddatejœ¬hfilenamegrfc8493eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130310hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[÷ògranking!-crfcd8493irfcNumber!-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&The BagIt File Packaging Format (V1.0)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractynThis memo defines several new ciphersuites for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to support certificateless, secure authentication using only a simple, low-entropy password.  The exchange is called "TLS-PWD".  The ciphersuites are all based on an authentication and key exchange protocol, named "dragonfly", that is resistant to offline dictionary attacks.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmHP EnterprisednamejD. Harkinsddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8492eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130309hkeywordsÑx#Password Authenticated Key ExchangeqDictionary AttacknAuthenticationcTLSkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate\o‡granking!,crfcd8492irfcNumber!,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex?Secure Password Ciphersuites for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThis document defines a way for an Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) router to advertise multiple types of supported Maximum SID Depths (MSDs) at node and/or link granularity.  Such advertisements allow entities (e.g., centralized controllers) to determine whether a particular Segment ID (SID) stack can be supported in a given network.  This document only defines one type of MSD: Base MPLS Imposition.  However, it defines an encoding that can support other MSD types.  This document focuses on MSD use in a network that is Segment Routing (SR) enabled, but MSD may also be useful when SR is not enabled.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamekU. Chunduri¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsbergddatej÷3hfilenamegrfc8491eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130308hkeywordsÑfBGP-LScSIDcMSDeIS-ISkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate[Ïé+granking!+crfcd8491irfcNumber!+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Signaling Maximum SID Depth (MSD) Using IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document defines a new DNS OPCODE for DNS Stateful Operations (DSO).  DSO messages communicate operations within persistent stateful sessions using Type Length Value (TLV) syntax.  Three TLVs are defined that manage session timeouts, termination, and encryption padding, and a framework is defined for extensions to enable new stateful operations.  This document updates RFC 1035 by adding a new DNS header OPCODE that has both different message semantics and a new result code.  This document updates RFC 7766 by redefining a session, providing new guidance on connection reuse, and providing a new mechanism for handling session idle timeouts.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiR. Bellis¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamelJ. Dickinson¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationsNibbhaya ConsultingdnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekT. PusateriddatejœÃhfilenamegrfc8490eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130307hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate\å0qgranking!*crfcd8490irfcNumber!*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewDNS Stateful OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMSession Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) is a protocol that serves as a tool for other protocols in dealing with NAT traversal. It can be used by an endpoint to determine the IP address and port allocated to it by a NAT. It can also be used to check connectivity between two endpoints and as a keep-alive protocol to maintain NAT bindings. STUN works with many existing NATs and does not require any special behavior from them.

STUN is not a NAT traversal solution by itself. Rather, it is a tool to be used in the context of a NAT traversal solution.

This document obsoletes RFC 5389.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationrImpedance MismatchdnameqM. Petit-Huguenin¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationeFive9dnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationfCitrixdnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekP. Matthewsddatej÷Ghfilenamegrfc8489eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtramdfullx"tram - TURN Revised and ModernizeddnamexTURN Revised and Modernizeddtypebwgbidjdoc-130306hkeywordsÅdSIPskobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDate^P Ígranking!)crfcd8489irfcNumber!)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document describes an approach to validating the content of the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) certificate tree, as it is implemented in the RIPE NCC RPKI Validator.  This approach is independent of a particular object retrieval mechanism, which allows it to be used with repositories available over the rsync protocol, the RPKI Repository Delta Protocol (RRDP), and repositories that use a mix of both.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamelO. Muravskiy¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamenT. Bruijnzeelsddatej÷2hfilenamegrfc8488eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130305hkeywordsÉdRPKIjvalidationdRRDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‘Øgranking!(crfcd8488irfcNumber!(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbRIPE NCC's Implementation of Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Certificate Tree ValidationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document describes the IP multicast traceroute facility, named Mtrace version 2 (Mtrace2).  Unlike unicast traceroute, Mtrace2 requires special implementations on the part of routers.  This specification describes the required functionality in multicast routers, as well as how an Mtrace2 client invokes a Query and receives a Reply.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameiH. Asaeda¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamehK. Meyer¢kaffiliation`dnamefW. Leeddatej÷)hfilenamegrfc8487eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130304hkeywordsÖimulticastfmtracegmtrace2jtraceroutecPIMkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate[÷ògranking!'crfcd8487irfcNumber!'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Mtrace Version 2: Traceroute Facility for IP MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx„This document defines an extension to the Opus audio codec to encapsulate coded Ambisonics using the Ogg format.  It also contains updates to RFC 7845 to reflect necessary changes in the description of channel mapping families.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamekJ. Skoglund¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Graczykddatej÷0hfilenamegrfc8486eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecodecdfullx%codec - Internet Wideband Audio CodecdnamexInternet Wideband Audio Codecdtypebwgbidjdoc-130303hkeywordsÇmspatial audioqlossy compressionkobsoletedByÄepages
