 to production root server infrastructure.  This document aims solely to document the technical and operational experience of deploying a system that is similar to but different from the Root Server system (on which the Internet's Domain Name System is designed and built).gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxBeijing Internet InstitutednamegL. Song¢kaffiliationxBeijing Internet InstitutednamefD. Liu¢kaffiliationdTISFdnamehP. Vixie¢kaffiliationiKeio/WIDEdnamegA. Kato¢kaffiliation`dnamegS. Kerrddatej÷/hfilenamegrfc8483eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130300hkeywordsÉkRoot ServerfDNSSECdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate[ (@granking!#crfcd8483irfcNumber!#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlepYeti DNS TestbeddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThe Domain Name System (DNS) specifies a query type (QTYPE) "ANY". The operator of an authoritative DNS server might choose not to respond to such queries for reasons of local policy, motivated by security, performance, or other reasons.

The DNS specification does not include specific guidance for the behavior of DNS servers or clients in this situation. This document aims to provide such guidance.

This document updates RFCs 1034 and 1035.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgAfiliasdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationoCloudflare Inc.dnamenO. Gudmundsson¢kaffiliationoCloudflare Inc.dnamelM. Majkowski¢kaffiliationcISCdnamegE. Huntddatej’ìhfilenamegrfc8482eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130299hkeywordsÖcDNScANYfREFUSEdDDOSeABUSEkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate\7˛_granking!"crfcd8482irfcNumber!"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDProviding Minimal-Sized Responses to DNS Queries That Have QTYPE=ANYdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDeployment of BGP origin validation based on Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) is hampered by, among other things, vendor misimplementations in two critical areas: which routes are validated and whether policy is applied when not specified by configuration.  This document is meant to clarify possible misunderstandings causing those misimplementations; it thus updates RFC 6811 by clarifying that all prefixes should have their validation state set and that policy must not be applied without operator configuration.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bushddatej÷0hfilenamegrfc8481eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsidropsdfullxsidrops - SIDR OperationsdnameoSIDR Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130298hkeywordsÇhsecuritygroutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[¨;cgranking!!crfcd8481irfcNumber!!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZClarifications to BGP Origin Validation Based on Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9324∂habstracty«This document defines the "IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4e" (6TiSCH) Operation Sublayer (6top) Protocol (6P), which enables distributed scheduling in 6TiSCH networks.  6P allows neighbor nodes to add/delete Time-Slotted Channel Hopping (TSCH) cells to/on one another.  6P is part of the 6TiSCH Operation Sublayer (6top), the layer just above the IEEE Std 802.15.4 TSCH Medium Access Control layer.  6top is composed of one or more Scheduling Functions (SFs) and the 6top Protocol defined in this document.  A 6top SF decides when to add/delete cells, and it triggers 6P Transactions.  The definition of SFs is out of scope for this document; however, this document provides the requirements for an SF.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniv. of Sci. and Tech. BeijingdnamegQ. Wang¢kaffiliationxUniversitat Oberta de CatalunyadnamemX. Vilajosana¢kaffiliationnAnalog DevicesdnamekT. Watteyneddatej÷.hfilenamegrfc8480eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6tischdfullx26tisch - IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4ednamex)IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4edtypebwgbidjdoc-130297hkeywordsÉsschedule managementvdistributed schedulingx,time synchronized channel hopping schedulingkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDate[ﬂœ£granking! crfcd8480irfcNumber! estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.6TiSCH Operation Sublayer (6top) Protocol (6P)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty…This memo describes a method of storing parameters needed for private-key validation in the Private-Key Information Syntax Specification as defined in PKCS#8 format (RFC 5208). It is equally applicable to the alternative implementation of the Private-Key Information Syntax Specification as defined in RFC 5958.

The approach described in this document encodes the parameters under a private enterprise extension and does not form part of a formal standard.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnametN. Mavrogiannopoulosddatej÷/hfilenamegrfc8479eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130296hkeywordsÉlprivate keysuvalidation parametersfPKCS#8kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[¨AÎgranking!crfcd8479irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex'Storing Validation Parameters in PKCS#8dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿZstandard, or "zstd" (pronounced "zee standard"), is a data compression mechanism. This document describes the mechanism and registers a media type and content encoding to be used when transporting zstd-compressed content via Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME).

Despite use of the word "standard" as part of its name, readers are advised that this document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is being published for informational purposes only.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnameiY. Collet¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej÷,hfilenamegrfc8478eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130295hkeywordsÅkCompressionkobsoletedByÅd8878epages6opublicationDate[¥granking!crfcd8478irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Zstandard Compression and the application/zstd Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÉThis document provides a summary of the "Workshop on Internet of Things (IoT) Semantic Interoperability (IOTSI)", which took place in Santa Clara, California March 17-18, 2016.  The main goal of the workshop was to foster a discussion on the different approaches used by companies and Standards Developing Organizations (SDOs) to accomplish interoperability at the application layer.  This report summarizes the discussions and lists recommendations to the standards community.  The views and positions in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect those of the authors or the Internet Architecture Board (IAB), which organized the workshop.  Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop.  The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Jimenez¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Thalerddatej÷0hfilenamegrfc8477eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130294hkeywordsÅjdata modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ (>granking!crfcd8477irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexXReport from the Internet of Things (IoT) Semantic Interoperability (IOTSI) Workshop 2016dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a way for an Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) router to advertise multiple types of supported Maximum SID Depths (MSDs) at node and/or link granularity.  Such advertisements allow entities (e.g., centralized controllers) to determine whether a particular Segment Identifier (SID) stack can be supported in a given network.  This document only refers to the Signaling MSD as defined in RFC 8491, but it defines an encoding that can support other MSD types.  Here, the term "OSPF" means both OSPFv2 and OSPFv3.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlApstra, Inc.dnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamekU. Chunduri¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenakddatej÷4hfilenamegrfc8476eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymclsrdfullxlsr - Link State RoutingdnamerLink State Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130293hkeywordsÑfBGP-LScSIDcMSDdOSPFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\Q¨granking!crfcd8476irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Signaling Maximum SID Depth (MSD) Using OSPFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document discusses the most common scenarios of connecting an enterprise network to multiple ISPs using an address space assigned by an ISP and how the approach proposed in "Enterprise Multihoming using Provider-Assigned Addresses without Network Prefix Translation: Requirements and Solution" could be applied in those scenarios.  The problem of enterprise multihoming without address translation of any form has not been solved yet as it requires both the network to select the correct egress ISP based on the packet source address and hosts to select the correct source address based on the desired egress ISP for that traffic.  The aforementioned document proposes a solution to this problem by introducing a new routing functionality (Source Address Dependent Routing) to solve the uplink selection issue.  It also proposes using Router Advertisements to influence the host source address selection.  It focuses on solving the general problem and covering various complex use cases, and this document adopts its proposed approach to provide a solution for a limited number of common use cases.  In particular, the focus of this document is on scenarios in which an enterprise network has two Internet uplinks used either in primary/backup mode or simultaneously and hosts in that network might not yet properly support multihoming as described in RFC 8028.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkova¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamejM. Stucchiddatej÷-hfilenamegrfc8475eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130292hkeywordsÅdipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[¡DÈgranking!crfcd8475irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBUsing Conditional Router Advertisements for Enterprise MultihomingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÀThis document updates RFC 3501 (IMAP4rev1) with persistent identifiers on mailboxes and messages to allow clients to more efficiently reuse cached data when resources have changed location on the server.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFastMaildnamekB. Gondwanaddatej“Âhfilenamegrfc8474eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130291hkeywordsÇdIMAPeemailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[™K$granking!crfcd8474irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%IMAP Extension for Object IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document describes a collection of mechanisms that allow HTTP servers to cryptographically bind security tokens (such as cookies and OAuth tokens) to TLS connections.

We describe both first-party and federated scenarios. In a first- party scenario, an HTTP server is able to cryptographically bind the security tokens that it issues to a client -- and that the client subsequently returns to the server -- to the TLS connection between the client and the server. Such bound security tokens are protected from misuse, since the server can generally detect if they are replayed inappropriately, e.g., over other TLS connections.

Federated Token Bindings, on the other hand, allow servers to cryptographically bind security tokens to a TLS connection that the client has with a different server than the one issuing the token.

This document is a companion document to "The Token Binding Protocol Version 1.0" (RFC 8471).fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamehA. Popov¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamekM. Nystroem¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamejD. Balfanz¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnameiN. Harper¢kaffiliationvKings Mountain SystemsdnameiJ. Hodgesddatej÷+hfilenamegrfc8473eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgtokbinddfullwtokbind - Token BindingdnamemToken Bindingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130290hkeywordsÖfCookiecTLSeOAuthfexportfreplaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[º¯granking!crfcd8473irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewToken Binding over HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis document specifies a Transport Layer Security (TLS) extension for the negotiation of Token Binding protocol version and key parameters.  Negotiation of Token Binding in TLS 1.3 and later versions is beyond the scope of this document.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamehA. Popov¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamekM. Nystroem¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamejD. Balfanzddatej÷,hfilenamegrfc8472eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgtokbinddfullwtokbind - Token BindingdnamemToken Bindingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130289hkeywordsÑfCookiecTLSfexportfreplaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[º˜granking!crfcd8472irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOTransport Layer Security (TLS) Extension for Token Binding Protocol NegotiationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document specifies version 1.0 of the Token Binding protocol.  The Token Binding protocol allows client/server applications to create long-lived, uniquely identifiable TLS bindings spanning multiple TLS sessions and connections.  Applications are then enabled to cryptographically bind security tokens to the TLS layer, preventing token export and replay attacks.  To protect privacy, the Token Binding identifiers are only conveyed over TLS and can be reset by the user at any time.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamehA. Popov¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamekM. Nystroem¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamejD. Balfanz¢kaffiliationvKings Mountain SystemsdnameiJ. Hodgesddatej÷-hfilenamegrfc8471eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgtokbinddfullwtokbind - Token BindingdnamemToken Bindingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130288hkeywordsÖeTokenfcookiecTLSfexportfreplaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ºıgranking!crfcd8471irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&The Token Binding Protocol Version 1.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%Using TLS early data creates an exposure to the possibility of a replay attack.  This document defines mechanisms that allow clients to communicate with servers about HTTP requests that are sent in early data.  Techniques are described that use these mechanisms to mitigate the risk of replay.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationtHAProxy TechnologiesdnamejW. Tarreauddatej÷*hfilenamegrfc8470eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130287hkeywordsádHTTPcTLSfreplayeretrye0-RTTjearly datakstatus codekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[•granking!crfcd8470irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUsing Early Data in HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“The pseudowire (PW) encapsulation of Ethernet, as defined in RFC 4448, specifies that the use of the control word (CW) is optional. In the absence of the CW, an Ethernet PW packet can be misidentified as an IP packet by a label switching router (LSR). This may lead to the selection of the wrong equal-cost multipath (ECMP) path for the packet, leading in turn to the misordering of packets. This problem has become more serious due to the deployment of equipment with Ethernet Media Access Control (MAC) addresses that start with 0x4 or 0x6. The use of the Ethernet PW CW addresses this problem. This document RECOMMENDS the use of the Ethernet PW CW in all but exceptional circumstances.

This document updates RFC 4448.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationgEquinixdnamekI. Bagdonasddatej÷*hfilenamegrfc8469eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130286hkeywordsájpseudowirebPWbCWdECMPkMAC addresslout of orderhorderingkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate[Ïé%granking!crfcd8469irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Recommendation to Use the Ethernet Control WorddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThis memo updates the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) framework defined by RFC 2330 with new considerations for measurement methodology and testing.  It updates the definition of standard-formed packets to include IPv6 packets, deprecates the definition of minimal IP packet, and augments distinguishing aspects, referred to as Type-P, for test packets in RFC 2330.  This memo identifies that IPv4-IPv6 coexistence can challenge measurements within the scope of the IPPM framework.  Example use cases include, but are not limited to, IPv4-IPv6 translation, NAT, and protocol encapsulation.  IPv6 header compression and use of IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Area Networks (6LoWPAN) are considered and excluded from the standard-formed packet evaluation.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationgTU WiendnameiJ. Fabini¢kaffiliationuInside Products, Inc.dnameiN. Elkins¢kaffiliationx"Blue Cross Blue Shield of MichigandnamelM. Ackermann¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehV. Hegdeddatej÷+hfilenamegrfc8468eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130285hkeywordsÜkMeasurementkMethodologyvStandard-Formed PacketfType-PnMinimal PacketoIPv6 TransitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Ïé$granking!crfcd8468irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^IPv4, IPv6, and IPv4-IPv6 Coexistence: Updates for the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5RFC 7830 specifies the "Padding" option for Extension Mechanisms for DNS (EDNS(0)) but does not specify the actual padding length for specific applications.  This memo lists the possible options ("padding policies"), discusses the implications of each option, and provides a recommended (experimental) option.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationknic.at GmbHdnamelA. Mayrhoferddatej“Çhfilenamegrfc8467eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130284hkeywordsÅhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate[¡DÁgranking!crfcd8467irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Padding Policies for Extension Mechanisms for DNS (EDNS(0))dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document defines a YANG data model that can be used to configure a Layer 2 provider-provisioned VPN service. It is up to a management system to take this as an input and generate specific configuration models to configure the different network elements to deliver the service. How this configuration of network elements is done is out of scope for this document.

The YANG data model defined in this document includes support for point-to-point Virtual Private Wire Services (VPWSs) and multipoint Virtual Private LAN Services (VPLSs) that use Pseudowires signaled using the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) and the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) as described in RFCs 4761 and 6624.

The YANG data model defined in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture defined in RFC 8342.fadNamenIgnas Bagdonasdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefB. Wen¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekG. Fioccola¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefC. Xie¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalilddatej÷)hfilenamegrfc8466eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdl2smdfullxl2sm - L2VPN Service ModeldnamesL2VPN Service Modeldtypebwgbidjdoc-130283hkeywordsÑdL2SMmService ModelhL2VPN SMsL2VPN Service ModelkobsoletedByÄepagesûopublicationDate[¡§granking!crfcd8466irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNA YANG Data Model for Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN) Service DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document specifies how the Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace reserved for the Third Generation Partnership Project 2 (3GPP2) identities and its Namespace Specific String (NSS) for the Mobile Equipment Identity (MEID) can be used as an Instance ID.  The purpose of this Instance ID is to fulfill the requirements for defining how a specific URN needs to be constructed and used in the "+sip.instance" Contact header field parameter for outbound behavior.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Atariusddatej÷(hfilenamegrfc8465eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130282hkeywordsÉdMEIDkinstance IDcIMSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[öï/granking!crfcd8465irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Using the Mobile Equipment Identity (MEID) URN as an Instance IDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹This document defines a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Third Generation Partnership Project 2 (3GPP2) and a Namespace Specific String (NSS) for the Mobile Equipment Identity (MEID).  The structure of an MEID is 15 hexadecimal digits long and is defined in the 3GPP2 to uniquely identify each individual mobile equipment (e.g., a handset or mobile phone).  The 3GPP2 has a requirement to be able to use an MEID as a URN.  This document fulfills that requirement.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Atariusddatej÷(hfilenamegrfc8464eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130281hkeywordsÉdMEIDkinstance IDcIMSkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate[öï,granking!crfcd8464irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHA URN Namespace for Device Identity and Mobile Equipment Identity (MEID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≥This document adds a new signing algorithm, Ed25519-SHA256, to "DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Signatures" (RFC 6376).  DKIM verifiers are required to implement this algorithm.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levineddatej÷+hfilenamegrfc8463eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedcrupdfullxdcrup - DKIM Crypto UpdatednamerDKIM Crypto Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130280hkeywordsÉdDKIMged25519lcryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ôü0granking!crfcd8463irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJA New Cryptographic Signature Method for DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyßThe Internet Architecture Board (IAB) and GSM Association (GSMA) held a joint workshop on Managing Radio Networks in an Encrypted World (MaRNEW), on September 24-25, 2015. This workshop aimed to discuss solutions for bandwidth optimization on mobile networks for encrypted content, as current solutions rely on unencrypted content, which is not indicative of the security needs of today's Internet users. The workshop gathered IETF attendees, IAB members, and participants from various organizations involved in the telecommunications industry including original equipment manufacturers, content providers, and mobile network operators.

The group discussed Internet encryption trends and deployment issues identified within the IETF and the privacy needs of users that should be adhered to. Solutions designed around sharing data from the network to the endpoints and vice versa were then discussed; in addition, issues experienced when using current transport-layer protocols were also discussed. Content providers and Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) gave their own views of their experiences delivering their content with mobile network operators. Finally, technical responses to regulation were discussed to help the regulated industries relay the issues of impossible-to-implement or bad-for-privacy technologies back to regulators.

A group of suggested solutions were devised, which will be discussed in various IETF groups moving forward.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiN. Rooney¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Dawkinsddatej÷'hfilenamegrfc8462eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130279hkeywordsÅhNetworkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[¡DÂgranking!crfcd8462irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexVReport from the IAB Workshop on Managing Radio Networks in an Encrypted World (MaRNEW)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFSMTP MTA Strict Transport Security (MTA-STS) is a mechanism enabling mail service providers (SPs) to declare their ability to receive Transport Layer Security (TLS) secure SMTP connections and to specify whether sending SMTP servers should refuse to deliver to MX hosts that do not offer TLS with a trusted server certificate.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekD. Margolis¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiM. Risher¢kaffiliationjOath, Inc.dnameoB. Ramakrishnan¢kaffiliationmComcast, Inc.dnamejA. Brotman¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft, Inc.dnamehJ. Jonesddatej÷'hfilenamegrfc8461eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130278hkeywordsÇmSMTP STARTTLSmMail SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[´πågranking!crfcd8461irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,SMTP MTA Strict Transport Security (MTA-STS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥A number of protocols exist for establishing encrypted channels between SMTP Mail Transfer Agents (MTAs), including STARTTLS, DNS- Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) TLSA, and MTA Strict Transport Security (MTA-STS).  These protocols can fail due to misconfiguration or active attack, leading to undelivered messages or delivery over unencrypted or unauthenticated channels.  This document describes a reporting mechanism and format by which sending systems can share statistics and specific information about potential failures with recipient domains.  Recipient domains can then use this information to both detect potential attacks and diagnose unintentional misconfigurations.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekD. Margolis¢kaffiliationmComcast, Inc.dnamejA. Brotman¢kaffiliationjOath, Inc.dnameoB. Ramakrishnan¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft, Inc.dnamehJ. Jones¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiM. Risherddatej÷$hfilenamegrfc8460eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130277hkeywordsÇdDANEgMTA-STSkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate[´πägranking!crfcd8460irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlerSMTP TLS ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWHierarchical Service Function Chaining (hSFC) is a network architecture allowing an organization to decompose a large-scale network into multiple domains of administration.

The goals of hSFC are to make a large-scale network easier to design, simpler to control, and supportive of independent functional groups within large network operators.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhSandvinednameiD. Dolson¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehS. Homma¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej÷)hfilenamegrfc8459eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130276hkeywordsäkScalabilityrSFC-enabled domainxmultiple control domainsnSFC complexityiHierarchypservice deliveryuservice complicationspservice offeringwdifferentiated servicesslarge scale networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[°ñ…granking!crfcd8459irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Hierarchical Service Function Chaining (hSFC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõNational Bibliography Numbers (NBNs) are used by national libraries and other organizations in order to identify resources in their collections. NBNs are usually applied to resources that are not catered for by established (standard) identifier systems such as International Standard Book Number (ISBN).

A Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for NBNs was established in 2001 in RFC 3188. Since then, a number of European national libraries have implemented URN:NBN-based systems.

This document replaces RFC 3188 and defines how NBNs can be supported within the updated URN framework. A revised namespace registration (version 4) compliant to RFC 8141 is included.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxThe National Library of FinlanddnameiJ. Hakaladdatej÷*hfilenamegrfc8458eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130275hkeywordsÉuNetwork Working GroupxNational bibliography numbersvUniform resource nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[¡D‰granking!
