estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Using National Bibliography Numbers as Uniform Resource NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäRFC 6154 created an IMAP special-use LIST extension and defined an initial set of attributes.  This document defines a new attribute, "\Important", and establishes a new IANA registry for IMAP folder attributes, which include the attributes defined in RFCs 5258, 3501, and 6154.  This document also defines a new IMAP keyword, "$Important", and registers it in the registry defined in RFC 5788.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej÷&hfilenamegrfc8457eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130274hkeywordsÅoIMAP attributeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ë√™granking!	crfcd8457irfcNumber!	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@IMAP "$Important" Keyword and "\Important" Special-Use AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThis document defines methodologies for benchmarking the control-plane performance of Software-Defined Networking (SDN) Controllers.  The SDN Controller is a core component in the SDN architecture that controls the behavior of the network.  SDN Controllers have been implemented with many varying designs in order to achieve their intended network functionality.  Hence, the authors of this document have taken the approach of considering an SDN Controller to be a black box, defining the methodology in a manner that is agnostic to protocols and network services supported by controllers.  This document provides a method for measuring the performance of all controller implementations.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrVeryx TechnologiesdnamepV. Bhuvaneswaran¢kaffiliationrVeryx TechnologiesdnamehA. Basil¢kaffiliationxHewlett Packard EnterprisednamelM. Tassinari¢kaffiliationgNanoSecdnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationnVSS MonitoringdnamehS. BanksddatejÀˆhfilenamegrfc8456eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-130273hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate[ÿ‚˝granking!crfcd8456irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUBenchmarking Methodology for Software-Defined Networking (SDN) Controller PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document defines terminology for benchmarking a Software-Defined Networking (SDN) controller's control-plane performance.  It extends the terminology already defined in RFC 7426 for the purpose of benchmarking SDN Controllers.  The terms provided in this document help to benchmark an SDN Controller's performance independently of the controller's supported protocols and/or network services.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrVeryx TechnologiesdnamepV. Bhuvaneswaran¢kaffiliationrVeryx TechnologiesdnamehA. Basil¢kaffiliationxHewlett Packard EnterprisednamelM. Tassinari¢kaffiliationgNanoSecdnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationnVSS MonitoringdnamehS. BanksddatejÃhfilenamegrfc8455eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-130272hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ÿ‚˚granking!crfcd8455irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUTerminology for Benchmarking Software-Defined Networking (SDN) Controller PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx^This document provides an information model for Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejS. Belotti¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegB. Yoonddatejœ∑hfilenamegrfc8454eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130271hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ë√®granking!crfcd8454irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCInformation Model for Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€Traffic Engineered (TE) networks have a variety of mechanisms to facilitate the separation of the data plane and control plane. They also have a range of management and provisioning protocols to configure and activate network resources. These mechanisms represent key technologies for enabling flexible and dynamic networking. The term "Traffic Engineered network" refers to a network that uses any connection-oriented technology under the control of a distributed or centralized control plane to support dynamic provisioning of end-to- end connectivity.

Abstraction of network resources is a technique that can be applied to a single network domain or across multiple domains to create a single virtualized network that is under the control of a network operator or the customer of the operator that actually owns the network resources.

This document provides a framework for Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN) to support virtual network services and connectivity services.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Leeddatej÷%hfilenamegrfc8453eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130270hkeywordsÇcSDNmOrchestrationkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate[5granking!crfcd8453irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Framework for Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx„This memo specifies two authenticated encryption algorithms that are nonce misuse resistant -- that is, they do not fail catastrophically if a nonce is repeated.

This document is the product of the Crypto Forum Research Group.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Haifa and AmazondnameiS. Gueron¢kaffiliationjGoogle LLCdnamejA. Langley¢kaffiliationx$Bar Ilan University and Unbound TechdnamejY. Lindellddatej÷Ahfilenamegrfc8452eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130269hkeywordsÖxauthenticated encryptiondaeadcaescgcmcsivkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate\∑æ”granking!crfcd8452irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex<AES-GCM-SIV: Nonce Misuse-Resistant Authenticated EncryptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThis document describes monitoring features related to media streams in Web real-time communication (WebRTC).  It provides a list of RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Sender Report (SR), Receiver Report (RR), and Extended Report (XR) metrics, which may need to be supported by RTP implementations in some diverse environments.  It lists a set of identifiers for the WebRTC's statistics API.  These identifiers are a set of RTCP SR, RR, and XR metrics related to the transport of multimedia flows.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlcallstats.iodnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Dengddatej÷(hfilenamegrfc8451eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-130268hkeywordsÅxWeb real-time communicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[¢O°granking!crfcd8451irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsConsiderations for Selecting RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Metrics for the WebRTC Statistics APIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$This memo describes an RTP payload format for the High Quality (HQ) profile of Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers Standard ST 2042-1, known as VC-2. This document describes the transport of HQ Profile VC-2 in RTP packets and has applications for low-complexity, high-bandwidth streaming of both lossless and lossy compressed video.

The HQ profile of VC-2 is intended for low-latency video compression (with latency potentially on the order of lines of video) at high data rates (with compression ratios on the order of 2:1 or 4:1).fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcBBCdnameiJ. Weaverddatej÷0hfilenamegrfc8450eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130267hkeywordsÑcrtpdvc-2cVC2edirackobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[∑¯ÿgranking!crfcd8450irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5RTP Payload Format for VC-2 High Quality (HQ) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎAn extension to Transport Layer Security (TLS) is defined that allows endpoints to negotiate the maximum size of protected records that each will send the other.

This replaces the maximum fragment length extension defined in RFC 6066.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej‘˛hfilenamegrfc8449eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130266hkeywordsÑcTLSfrecordcIoTjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[n&granking!crfcd8449irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Record Size Limit Extension for TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!This document includes examples of TLS 1.3 handshakes.  Private keys and inputs are provided so that these handshakes might be reproduced.  Intermediate values, including secrets, traffic keys, and IVs, are shown so that implementations might be checked incrementally against these values.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatejœ≈hfilenamegrfc8448eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130265hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDate\<˘/granking!␀crfcd8448irfcNumber!␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Example Handshake Traces for TLS 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document describes a number of changes to TLS and DTLS IANA registries that range from adding notes to the registry all the way to changing the registration policy. These changes were mostly motivated by WG review of the TLS- and DTLS-related registries undertaken as part of the TLS 1.3 development process.

This document updates the following RFCs: 3749, 5077, 4680, 5246, 5705, 5878, 6520, and 7301.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpTableau SoftwarednamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. TurnerddatejÃGhfilenamegrfc8447eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130264hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[n&granking ˇcrfcd8447irfcNumber ˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&IANA Registry Updates for TLS and DTLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9847∂habstractyçThis document specifies version 1.3 of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol. TLS allows client/server applications to communicate over the Internet in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, and message forgery.

This document updates RFCs 5705 and 6066, and obsoletes RFCs 5077, 5246, and 6961. This document also specifies new requirements for TLS 1.2 implementations.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekE. Rescorladdatej‘ãhfilenamegrfc8446eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130263hkeywordsÖxinternational data algorithmisymmetricxtransport protocol layernauthenticationgprivacykobsoletedByÄepages†opublicationDate[n&granking ˛crfcd8446irfcNumber ˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document describes a protocol for Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal for UDP-based communication. This protocol is called Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE). ICE makes use of the Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) protocol and its extension, Traversal Using Relay NAT (TURN).

This document obsoletes RFC 5245.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘∫hfilenamegrfc8445eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcicedfullx,ice - Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdnamex&Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130262hkeywordsÅcNATkobsoletedByÄepagesdopublicationDate[Q◊granking ˝crfcd8445irfcNumber ˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgInteractive Connectivity Establishment (ICE): A Protocol for Network Address Translator (NAT) TraversaldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8863∂habstractyBit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) is an architecture that provides optimal multicast forwarding through a "BIER domain" without requiring intermediate routers to maintain multicast-related, per- flow state. BIER also does not require an explicit tree-building protocol for its operation. A multicast data packet enters a BIER domain at a Bit-Forwarding Ingress Router (BFIR) and leaves the BIER domain at one or more Bit-Forwarding Egress Routers (BFERs). The BFIR adds a BIER packet header to the packet. The BIER packet header contains a BitString in which each bit represents exactly one BFER to forward the packet to. The set of BFERs to which the multicast packet needs to be forwarded is expressed by the set of bits in the BIER packet header.

This document describes the OSPF protocol extension (from RFC 2328) that is required for BIER with MPLS encapsulation (which is defined in RFC 8296). Support for other encapsulation types and the use of multiple encapsulation types are outside the scope of this document.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehN. Kumar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekA. Dolganow¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehJ. Zhang¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrinddatejœ≤hfilenamegrfc8444eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130261hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Ïé"granking ¸crfcd8444irfcNumber ¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;OSPFv2 Extensions for Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9272∂habstractyLThis document extends the Personal Assertion Token (PASSporT) specification defined in RFC 8225 to allow the inclusion of cryptographically signed assertions of authorization for the values populated in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) 'Resource-Priority' header field, which is used for communications resource prioritization.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlVencore LabsdnamehR. Singh¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehM. Dolly¢kaffiliationlVencore LabsdnamefS. Das¢kaffiliationx&Office of Emergency Communications/DHSdnameiA. Nguyenddatej÷*hfilenamegrfc8443eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130260hkeywordsáuSIP Resource-PriorityxResource Priority Header (rph)tJSON Web Token ClaimoIdentity headervAuthentication ServiceiAssertiontVerification ServicekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate[â‰Ògranking ˚crfcd8443irfcNumber ˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQPersonal Assertion Token (PASSporT) Extension for Resource Priority AuthorizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document defines several new cipher suites for version 1.2 of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol and version 1.2 of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol.  These cipher suites are based on the Ephemeral Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman with Pre-Shared Key (ECDHE_PSK) key exchange together with the Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) algorithms AES-GCM and AES-CCM.  PSK provides light and efficient authentication, ECDHE provides forward secrecy, and AES-GCM and AES-CCM provide encryption and integrity protection.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Mattsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migaultddatejœîhfilenamegrfc8442eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130259hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ë√§granking ˙crfcd8442irfcNumber ˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIECDHE_PSK with AES-GCM and AES-CCM Cipher Suites for TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx}This document defines a mechanism for running the WebSocket Protocol (RFC 6455) over a single stream of an HTTP/2 connection.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejP. McManusddatej÷'hfilenamegrfc8441eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130258hkeywordsÅpCONNECT SETTINGSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[°ñ«granking ˘crfcd8441irfcNumber ˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Bootstrapping WebSockets with HTTP/2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines an extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) LIST command that allows the client to request the set of rights that the logged-in user has been granted on mailboxes, along with other information typically returned by the LIST command.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpFastMail Pty LtddnamelK. Murchison¢kaffiliationpFastMail Pty LtddnamekB. Gondwanaddatej÷&hfilenamegrfc8440eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130257hkeywordsÉeIMAP4dLISThMYRIGHTSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[{{granking ¯crfcd8440irfcNumber ¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCIMAP4 Extension for Returning MYRIGHTS Information in Extended LISTdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document defines the ChaCha20 stream cipher as well as the use of the Poly1305 authenticator, both as stand-alone algorithms and as a "combined mode", or Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) algorithm.

RFC 7539, the predecessor of this document, was meant to serve as a stable reference and an implementation guide. It was a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG). This document merges the errata filed against RFC 7539 and adds a little text to the Security Considerations section.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejA. Langleyddatej‘‰hfilenamegrfc8439eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130256hkeywordsÑfCHACHAhCHACHA20hPOLY1305dAEADkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDate[(´;granking ˜crfcd8439irfcNumber ˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex(ChaCha20 and Poly1305 for IETF ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document adds a new capability called "STATUS=SIZE" to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP).  It allows retrieving the total storage size of a mailbox with a single STATUS command rather than retrieving and summing the sizes of all individual messages in that mailbox.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjDovecot OydnamehS. Boschddatej÷&hfilenamegrfc8438eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130255hkeywordsÉdimapfstatusdsizekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Äjhgranking ˆcrfcd8438irfcNumber ˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIMAP Extension for STATUS=SIZEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∂This specification extends the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) to allow an administrative client to reuse the same IMAP connection on behalf of multiple IMAP user identities.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Newmanddatej÷&hfilenamegrfc8437eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeextradfullx9extra - Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddnamex1Email mailstore and eXtensions To Revise or Amenddtypebwgbidjdoc-130254hkeywordsÜdIMAPnunauthenticatedSASLeloginlauthenticatenauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Äjfgranking ıcrfcd8437irfcNumber ıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2IMAP UNAUTHENTICATE Extension for Connection ReusedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThe Differentiated Services (Diffserv) architecture specifies use of the DS field in the IPv4 and IPv6 packet headers to carry one of 64 distinct differentiated services field codepoint (DSCP) values. The Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) maintains a registry of assigned DSCP values.

This update to RFC 2474 changes the IANA registration policy for Pool 3 of the registry (i.e., DSCP values of the form xxxx01) to Standards Action, i.e., values are assigned through a Standards Track or Best Current Practice RFC. The update also removes permission for experimental and local use of the codepoints that form Pool 3 of the DSCP registry; Pool 2 Codepoints (i.e., DSCP values of the form xxxx11) remain available for these purposes.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej÷&hfilenamegrfc8436eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130253hkeywordsÇhDiffservdDSCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[|àgranking Ùcrfcd8436irfcNumber ÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxUpdate to IANA Registration Procedures for Pool 3 Values in the Differentiated Services Field Codepoints (DSCP) RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±Parallel NFS (pNFS) allows a separation between the metadata (onto a metadata server) and data (onto a storage device) for a file.  The flexible file layout type is defined in this document as an extension to pNFS that allows the use of storage devices that require only a limited degree of interaction with the metadata server and use already-existing protocols.  Client-side mirroring is also added to provide replication of files.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Halevy¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednameiT. Haynesddatej’∏hfilenamegrfc8435eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130252hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate[ÄyAgranking Ûcrfcd8435irfcNumber ÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Parallel NFS (pNFS) Flexible File LayoutdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the requirements that individual Parallel NFS (pNFS) layout types need to meet in order to work within the pNFS framework as defined in RFC 5661.  In so doing, this document aims to clearly distinguish between requirements for pNFS as a whole and those specifically directed to the pNFS file layout.  The lack of a clear separation between the two sets of requirements has been troublesome for those specifying and evaluating new layout types.  In this regard, this document updates RFC 5661.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkHammerspacednameiT. Haynesddatej’¥hfilenamegrfc8434eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130251hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Äy<granking Úcrfcd8434irfcNumber ÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Requirements for Parallel NFS (pNFS) Layout TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyTThe "alert" namespace of Uniform Resource Names (URNs) can be used in the Alert-Info header field of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) requests and responses to inform a voice over IP (VoIP) telephone (user agent) of the characteristics of the call that the user agent has originated or terminated. The user agent must resolve the URNs into a signal; that is, it must select the best available signal to present to its user to indicate the characteristics of the call.

RFC 7462 describes a non-normative algorithm for signal selection. This document describes a more efficient alternative algorithm: a user agent's designer can, based on the user agent's signals and their meanings, construct a finite state machine (FSM) to process the URNs to select a signal in a way that obeys the restrictions given in the definition of the "alert" URN namespace.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxAriadne Internet ServicesdnameiD. Worleyddatej’öhfilenamegrfc8433eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130250hkeywordsäjAlert-Infomaudio signalsncall signalingmcall transferjresolutionisignalinggsignalscSIPcURNnvisual signalskobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDate[ÑKogranking Òcrfcd8433irfcNumber ÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.A Simpler Method for Resolving Alert-Info URNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThe unification of control and management of microwave radio link interfaces is a precondition for seamless multi-layer networking and automated network provisioning and operation.

This document describes the required characteristics and use cases for control and management of radio link interface parameters using a YANG data model.

The purpose is to create a framework to identify the necessary information elements and define a YANG data model for control and management of the radio link interfaces in a microwave node. Some parts of the resulting model may be generic and could also be used by other technologies, e.g., Ethernet technology.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamejJ. Ahlberg¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeM. Ye¢kaffiliationwNEC Laboratories EuropednameeX. Li¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamemLM. Contreras¢kaffiliationx Universidad Carlos III de MadriddnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej÷&hfilenamegrfc8432eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-130249hkeywordsÑiMicrowavepmillimeter wavesjYANG Modeltinterface managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[ (9granking crfcd8432irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\A Framework for Management and Control of Microwave and Millimeter Wave Interface ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx°This document defines a YANG data model for the Routing Information Base (RIB) that aligns with the Interface to the Routing System (I2RS) RIB information model.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegA. Dass¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnamerH. Ananthakrishnan¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamegS. Kini¢kaffiliationdUberdnamejN. BahadurddatejÕ≤hfilenamegrfc8431eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-130248hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDate[´πàgranking Ôcrfcd8431irfcNumber ÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8A YANG Data Model for the Routing Information Base (RIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàRouting and routing functions in enterprise and carrier networks are typically performed by network devices (routers and switches) using a Routing Information Base (RIB).  Protocols and configurations push data into the RIB, and the RIB manager installs state into the hardware for packet forwarding.  This document specifies an information model for the RIB to enable defining a standardized data model.  The IETF's I2RS WG used this document to design the I2RS RIB data model.  This document is being published to record the higher- level information model decisions for RIBs so that other developers of RIBs may benefit from the design concepts.fadNamepMartin Vigoureuxdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdUberdnamejN. Bahadur¢kaffiliation`dnamegS. Kini¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiJ. Medvedddatej’±hfilenamegrfc8430eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-130247hkeywordsÇcRIBjinfo modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[´πÜgranking Ócrfcd8430irfcNumber ÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuRIB Information ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÁThe triple-DES (3DES) and RC4 encryption types are steadily weakening in cryptographic strength, and the deprecation process should begin for their use in Kerberos.  Accordingly, RFC 4757 has been moved to Historic status, as none of the encryption types it specifies should be used, and RFC 3961 has been updated to note the deprecation of the triple-DES encryption types.  RFC 4120 is likewise updated to remove the recommendation to implement triple-DES encryption and checksum types.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamehB. Kaduk¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehM. Shortddatej÷%hfilenamegrfc8429eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130246hkeywordsÇgGSS-APIcGSSkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate[⁄Ägranking Ìcrfcd8429irfcNumber ÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber⁄etotaletitlex/Deprecate Triple-DES (3DES) and RC4 in KerberosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis specification defines a format for representing simple sensor measurements and device parameters in Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML).  Representations are defined in JavaScript Object Notation (JSON), Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR), Extensible Markup Language (XML), and Efficient XML Interchange (EXI), which share the common SenML data model.  A simple sensor, such as a temperature sensor, could use one of these media types in protocols such as HTTP or the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) to transport the measurements of the sensor or to be configured.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationcARMdnameiZ. Shelby¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej÷$hfilenamegrfc8428eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130245hkeywordsÇcIoTjdata modelkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDate[â‰Ógranking Ïcrfcd8428irfcNumber ÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Sensor Measurement Lists (SenML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9100∂habstractySome applications use DNS messages, or parts of DNS messages, as data.  For example, a system that captures DNS queries and responses might want to be able to easily search them without having to decode the messages each time.  Another example is a system that puts together DNS queries and responses from message parts.  This document describes a general format for DNS message data in JSON.  Specific profiles of the format in this document can be described in other documents for specific applications and usage scenarios.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatejœhfilenamegrfc8427eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130244hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[VºÊgranking Îcrfcd8427irfcNumber ÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Representing DNS Messages in JSONdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOperators are looking to introduce services over Segment Routing (SR) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in networks running Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) LSPs.  In some instances, operators are also migrating existing services from RSVP-TE to SR LSPs.  For example, there might be certain services that are well suited for SR and need to coexist with RSVP-TE in the same network.  Such introduction or migration of traffic to SR might require coexistence with RSVP-TE in the same network for an extended period of time, depending on the operator's intent.  The following document provides solution options for keeping the traffic engineering database consistent across the network, accounting for the different bandwidth utilization between SR and RSVP-TE.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelH. Sitaraman¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivabalanddatejœ≤hfilenamegrfc8426eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130243hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[_û+granking Ícrfcd8426irfcNumber ÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZRecommendations for RSVP-TE and Segment Routing (SR) Label Switched Path (LSP) CoexistencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢The Prefix Information Option (PIO) in the IPv6 Neighbor Discovery Router Advertisement message defines an 8-bit flag field; this field has two flags defined, and the remaining 6 bits are reserved (Reserved1).  RFC 6275 defines a flag from this field without creating an IANA registry or updating RFC 4861.  The purpose of this document is to create an IANA registry for the PIO flags.  This document updates RFC 4861.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehO. Troanddatejœ∞hfilenamegrfc8425eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130242hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[_û(granking Ècrfcd8425irfcNumber ÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOIANA Considerations for IPv6 Neighbor Discovery Prefix Information Option FlagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document describes extensions to Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) for locally protecting the ingress node of a Point-to-Point (P2P) or Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Traffic Engineered (TE) Label Switched Path (LSP).  It extends the Fast Reroute (FRR) protection for transit nodes of an LSP to the ingress node of the LSP.  The procedures described in this document are experimental.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehR. Torviddatej÷"hfilenamegrfc8424eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130241hkeywordsÅoHead ProtectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[{Uygranking Ëcrfcd8424irfcNumber ËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYExtensions to RSVP-TE for Label Switched Path (LSP) Ingress Fast Reroute (FRR) ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis document reclassifies the RFCs related to the United States National Security Agency (NSA) Suite B cryptographic algorithms as Historic, and it discusses the reasons for doing so.  This document moves seven Informational RFCs to Historic status: RFCs 5759, 6239, 6318, 6379, 6380, 6403, and 6460.  In addition, it moves three obsolete Informational RFCs to Historic status: RFCs 4869, 5008, and 5430.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejL. Zieglarddatej‘˙hfilenamegrfc8423eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130240hkeywordsçux.509 v3 certificatesx%x.509 v2 certificate revocation listsccrliUI suitesuuser interface suitesnelliptic curvecikexcryptographic algorithm policytsecurity applicationtsuite b cryptographyccmcx%suite b x.509 public key certificatescnsakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[aB$granking Ácrfcd8423irfcNumber ÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Reclassification of Suite B Documents to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document describes key exchange algorithms based on Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol. In particular, it specifies the use of Ephemeral Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDHE) key agreement in a TLS handshake and the use of the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) and Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA) as authentication mechanisms.

This document obsoletes RFC 4492.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationcARMdnamesM. Pegourie-Gonnardddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8422eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-130239hkeywordsÇeECDSAeEdDSAkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate[i15granking Êcrfcd8422irfcNumber ÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkElliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Cipher Suites for Transport Layer Security (TLS) Versions 1.2 and EarlierdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∑habstractyThis document provides guidelines on how to make Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) conclude faster in multihomed and IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack scenarios where broken paths exist.  The provided guidelines are backward compatible with the original ICE specification (see RFC 5245).fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelP. Martinsen¢kaffiliationlMcAfee, Inc.dnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Patilddatejœghfilenamegrfc8421eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcicedfullx,ice - Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdnamex&Interactive Connectivity Establishmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130238hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate[Q÷granking Âcrfcd8421irfcNumber ÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberŸetotaletitlex_Guidelines for Multihomed and IPv4/IPv6 Dual-Stack Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxëThis document describes the use of the Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA) in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2).fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamefY. Nirddatejœ©hfilenamegrfc8420eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130237hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[{Uvgranking ‰crfcd8420irfcNumber ‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsUsing the Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA) in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]This document specifies the conventions for using the Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA) for curve25519 and curve448 in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  For each curve, EdDSA defines the PureEdDSA and HashEdDSA modes.  However, the HashEdDSA mode is not used with the CMS.  In addition, no context string is used with the CMS.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatejœõhfilenamegrfc8419eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130236hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate[i14granking „crfcd8419irfcNumber „estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmUse of Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA) Signatures in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∫This document describes the conventions for using the Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) key agreement algorithm with curve25519 and curve448 in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatejœïhfilenamegrfc8418eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130235hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[i13granking ‚crfcd8418irfcNumber ‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUse of the Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman Key Agreement Algorithm with X25519 and X448 in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis specification defines the Security Event Token (SET) data structure.  A SET describes statements of fact from the perspective of an issuer about a subject.  These statements of fact represent an event that occurred directly to or about a security subject, for example, a statement about the issuance or revocation of a token on behalf of a subject.  This specification is intended to enable representing security- and identity-related events.  A SET is a JSON Web Token (JWT), which can be optionally signed and/or encrypted.  SETs can be distributed via protocols such as HTTP.fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegP. Hunt¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejW. Denniss¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiM. Ansariddatej’√hfilenamegrfc8417eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhseceventdfullxsecevent - Security EventsdnameoSecurity Eventsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130234hkeywordsàhIdentityhSecurityeEventeTokenfClaimsdJSONnJSON Web TokencJWTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[DAgranking ·crfcd8417irfcNumber ·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSecurity Event Token (SET)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂The Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) is a global authorization infrastructure that allows the holder of Internet Number Resources (INRs) to make verifiable statements about those resources.  Network operators, e.g., Internet Service Providers (ISPs), can use the RPKI to validate BGP route origin assertions.  ISPs can also use the RPKI to validate the path of a BGP route.  However, ISPs may want to establish a local view of exceptions to the RPKI data in the form of local filters and additions.  The mechanisms described in this document provide a simple way to enable INR holders to establish a local, customized view of the RPKI, overriding global RPKI repository data as needed.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdZDNSdnameeD. Ma¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamemD. Mandelberg¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamenT. Bruijnzeelsddatej’˛hfilenamegrfc8416eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130233hkeywordsÉdRPKIrLocal Trust AnchorfBGPseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[j:xgranking ‡crfcd8416irfcNumber ‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSimplified Local Internet Number Resource Management with the RPKI (SLURM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6): an extensible mechanism for configuring nodes with network configuration parameters, IP addresses, and prefixes. Parameters can be provided statelessly, or in combination with stateful assignment of one or more IPv6 addresses and/or IPv6 prefixes. DHCPv6 can operate either in place of or in addition to stateless address autoconfiguration (SLAAC).

This document updates the text from RFC 3315 (the original DHCPv6 specification) and incorporates prefix delegation (RFC 3633), stateless DHCPv6 (RFC 3736), an option to specify an upper bound for how long a client should wait before refreshing information (RFC 4242), a mechanism for throttling DHCPv6 clients when DHCPv6 service is not available (RFC 7083), and relay agent handling of unknown messages (RFC 7283). In addition, this document clarifies the interactions between models of operation (RFC 7550). As such, this document obsoletes RFC 3315, RFC 3633, RFC 3736, RFC 4242, RFC 7083, RFC 7283, and RFC 7550.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationx!Internet Systems Consortium, Inc.dnamelT. Mrugalski¢kaffiliationx!Internet Systems Consortium, Inc.dnamelM. Siodelski¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenA. Yourtchenko¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationsNibbhaya ConsultingdnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationx:University of New Hampshire Interoperability Lab (UNH-IOL)dnamejT. Wintersddatej÷$hfilenamegrfc8415eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130232hkeywordsÉfDHCPv6dIPv6dDHCPkobsoletedByÅd9915epagesöopublicationDate[ı“rgranking ﬂcrfcd8415irfcNumber ﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÍThis specification defines a metadata format that an OAuth 2.0 client can use to obtain the information needed to interact with an OAuth 2.0 authorization server, including its endpoint locations and authorization server capabilities.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationxNomura Research Institute, Ltd.dnamekN. Sakimura¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradleyddatej‘™hfilenamegrfc8414eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130231hkeywordsãeOAuthiDiscoveryhMetadatarDiscovery MetadataxConfiguration InformationtAuthorization ServeriWebFingerxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONnJSON Web TokencJWTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[5Tógranking ﬁcrfcd8414irfcNumber ﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'OAuth 2.0 Authorization Server MetadatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4Time-Scheduled (TS) reservation of Traffic Engineering (TE) resources can be used to provide resource booking for TE Label Switched Paths so as to better guarantee services for customers and to improve the efficiency of network resource usage at any moment in time, including network usage that is planned for the future.  This document provides a framework that describes and discusses the architecture for supporting scheduled reservation of TE resources.  This document does not describe specific protocols or protocol extensions needed to realize this service.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiY. Zhuang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegH. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘Ãhfilenamegrfc8413eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130230hkeywordsâsTraffic EngineeringbTEsLabel Switched PathcLSPdMPLSxPath Computation ElementcPCExSoftware Defined NetworkingcSDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[DAgranking ›crfcd8413irfcNumber ›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Framework for Scheduled Use of ResourcesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThis document extends "PA-TNC: A Posture Attribute (PA) Protocol Compatible with Trusted Network Connect (TNC)" (RFC 5792) by providing specific attributes and message exchanges to allow endpoints to report their installed software inventory information to a NEA Server, as defined in "Network Endpoint Assessment (NEA): Overview and Requirements" (RFC 5209).fadNamenBenjamin Kadukdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamejC. Schmidt¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnameiD. Haynes¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnameiC. Coffin¢kaffiliationx.National Institute of Standards and TechnologydnamemD. Waltermire¢kaffiliationx&United States National Security AgencydnamesJ. Fitzgerald-McKayddatej÷ hfilenamegrfc8412eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsacmdfullx4sacm - Security Automation and Continuous Monitoringdnamex-Security Automation and Continuous Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-130229hkeywordsÑdSWIDfPA-TNCcNEArSoftware inventorykobsoletedByÄepageseopublicationDate[>˙ígranking ‹crfcd8412irfcNumber ‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Software Inventory Message and Attributes (SWIMA) for PA-TNCdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+When the Curdle Security Working Group was chartered, a range of object identifiers was donated by DigiCert, Inc.  for the purpose of registering the Edwards Elliptic Curve key agreement and signature algorithms.  This donated set of OIDs allowed for shorter values than would be possible using the existing S/MIME or PKIX arcs.  This document describes the donated range and the identifiers that were assigned from that range, transfers control of that range to IANA, and establishes IANA allocation policies for any future assignments within that range.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationnDigiCert, Inc.dnamejR. Andrewsddatejœ©hfilenamegrfc8411eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130228hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[i11granking €crfcd8411irfcNumber €estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIIANA Registration for the Cryptographic Algorithm Object Identifier RangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document specifies algorithm identifiers and ASN.1 encoding formats for elliptic curve constructs using the curve25519 and curve448 curves.  The signature algorithms covered are Ed25519 and Ed448.  The key agreement algorithms covered are X25519 and X448.  The encoding for public key, private key, and Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA) structures is provided.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaadddatej’•hfilenamegrfc8410eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130227hkeywordséxElliptic Curve CryptographyjCurve25519hCurve448jGoldilockseX.509dPKIXcPKIcOIDeASN.1eEdDSAgEd25519eEd448fX25519dX448kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[i1/granking ⁄crfcd8410irfcNumber ⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrAlgorithm Identifiers for Ed25519, Ed448, X25519, and X448 for Use in the Internet X.509 Public Key InfrastructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9295¥habstractyPThis document describes two SAML entity attributes: one that can be used to assign category membership semantics to an entity and another for use in claiming interoperation with or support for entities in such categories.

This document is a product of the working group process of the Research and Education FEDerations (REFEDS) group.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehI. Young¢kaffiliationeSUNETdnamelL. Johansson¢kaffiliationuShibboleth ConsortiumdnameiS. Cantorddatej’ühfilenamegrfc8409eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130226hkeywordsÅfREFEDSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Äwgranking Ÿcrfcd8409irfcNumber ŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexMThe Entity Category Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) Attribute TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰A Path Computation Element (PCE) can compute Traffic Engineering (TE) paths through a network; these paths are subject to various constraints.  Currently, TE paths are Label Switched Paths (LSPs) that are set up using the RSVP-TE signaling protocol.  However, other TE path setup methods are possible within the PCE architecture.  This document proposes an extension to the PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) to allow support for different path setup methods over a given PCEP session.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationnNuage NetworksdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationxPantheon Technologies SROdnamehR. Varga¢kaffiliationsMetaswitch NetworksdnamekJ. Hardwickddatejœhfilenamegrfc8408eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130225hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[W∏:granking ÿcrfcd8408irfcNumber ÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGConveying Path Setup Type in PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8664∂habstracty]This memo provides guidelines for authors and reviewers of specifications containing YANG modules.  Recommendations and procedures are defined, which are intended to increase interoperability and usability of Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) and RESTCONF protocol implementations that utilize YANG modules.  This document obsoletes RFC 6087.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Biermanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8407eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130224hkeywordsÉfNETMODgNETCONFhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÅd9907epages?opublicationDate[»jgranking ◊crfcd8407irfcNumber ◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMGuidelines for Authors and Reviewers of Documents Containing YANG Data ModelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8819¥habstractyﬂThis document summarizes recommended terminology for Network Coding concepts and constructs.  It provides a comprehensive set of terms in order to avoid ambiguities in future IRTF and IETF documents on Network Coding.  This document is the product of the Coding for Efficient Network Communications Research Group (NWCRG), and it is in line with the terminology used by the RFCs produced by the Reliable Multicast Transport (RMT) and FEC Framework (FECFRAME) IETF working groups.gauthorsé¢kaffiliationcNRLdnamejB. Adamson¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamehC. Adjih¢kaffiliationgIkerlandnameiJ. Bilbao¢kaffiliationkBAE SystemsdnameiV. Firoiu¢kaffiliationjTU DresdendnameiF. Fitzek¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiS. Ghanem¢kaffiliationnISAE - SupaerodnameiE. Lochin¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejA. Masucci¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamenM-J. Montpetit¢kaffiliationrAalborg UniversitydnamekM. Pedersen¢kaffiliationgIkerlandnamejG. Peralta¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationrAnsuR TechnologiesdnameiP. Saxena¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelS. Sivakumarddatej‘”hfilenamegrfc8406eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenwcrgdfullxBnwcrg - Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdnamex:Coding for efficient NetWork Communications Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-130223hkeywordsÇnNetwork CodinghTaxonomykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[2¿kgranking ÷crfcd8406irfcNumber ÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexBTaxonomy of Coding Techniques for Efficient Network CommunicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document defines a standard algorithm to temporarily postpone or "back off" link-state IGP Shortest Path First (SPF) computations. This reduces the computational load and churn on IGP nodes when multiple temporally close network events trigger multiple SPF computations.

Having one standard algorithm improves interoperability by reducing the probability and/or duration of transient forwarding loops during the IGP convergence when the IGP reacts to multiple temporally close IGP events.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationwOrange Business ServicednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationkRtBrick IncdnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliation`dnamekP. Francois¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnameiC. BowersddatejÕ9hfilenamegrfc8405eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130222hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[1“«granking ’crfcd8405irfcNumber ’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFShortest Path First (SPF) Back-Off Delay Algorithm for Link-State IGPsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiPervasive monitoring attacks on the privacy of Internet users are of serious concern to both user and operator communities.  RFC 7258 discusses the critical need to protect users' privacy when developing IETF specifications and also recognizes that making networks unmanageable to mitigate pervasive monitoring is not an acceptable outcome: an appropriate balance is needed.  This document discusses current security and network operations as well as management practices that may be impacted by the shift to increased use of encryption to help guide protocol development in support of manageable and secure networks.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej‘?hfilenamegrfc8404eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130221hkeywordsÜgNETCONFhRESTCONFjMonitoringjManagementsSecurity ManagementjOperationskobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDate[W¨ngranking ‘crfcd8404irfcNumber ‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Effects of Pervasive Encryption on OperatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'This document describes features of an MPLS path monitoring system and related use cases.  Segment-based routing enables a scalable and simple method to monitor data-plane liveliness of the complete set of paths belonging to a single domain.  The MPLS monitoring system adds features to the traditional MPLS ping and Label Switched Path (LSP) trace, in a very complementary way.  MPLS topology awareness reduces management and control-plane involvement of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) measurements while enabling new OAM features.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geib¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehN. Kumarddatej’ihfilenamegrfc8403eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130220hkeywordsÑuSegment based RoutingcOAMpLSP surveillanceoMPLS monitoringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[W¨igranking ”crfcd8403irfcNumber ”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?A Scalable and Topology-Aware MPLS Data-Plane Monitoring SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáSegment Routing (SR) leverages the source routing paradigm. A node steers a packet through an ordered list of instructions, called "segments". A segment can represent any instruction, topological or service based. A segment can have a semantic local to an SR node or global within an SR domain. SR provides a mechanism that allows a flow to be restricted to a specific topological path, while maintaining per-flow state only at the ingress node(s) to the SR domain.

SR can be directly applied to the MPLS architecture with no change to the forwarding plane. A segment is encoded as an MPLS label. An ordered list of segments is encoded as a stack of labels. The segment to process is on the top of the stack. Upon completion of a segment, the related label is popped from the stack.

SR can be applied to the IPv6 architecture, with a new type of routing header. A segment is encoded as an IPv6 address. An ordered list of segments is encoded as an ordered list of IPv6 addresses in the routing header. The active segment is indicated by the Destination Address (DA) of the packet. The next active segment is indicated by a pointer in the new routing header.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiR. Shakirddatejœ¨hfilenamegrfc8402eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130219hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate[W¨agranking “crfcd8402irfcNumber “estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSegment Routing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9256∂habstractxÑThis document defines IS-IS extensions to support multicast forwarding using the Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) architecture.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehZ. Zhangddatejœ≠hfilenamegrfc8401eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130218hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Nxgranking —crfcd8401irfcNumber —estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) Support via IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9272∂habstractx¯This document describes extensions to Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) for locally protecting the egress node(s) of a Point-to-Point (P2P) or Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Traffic Engineered (TE) Label Switched Path (LSP).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Chen¢kaffiliationeCienadnamefA. Liu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationgVerizondnameeF. Xu¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehL. Huangddatej‘+hfilenamegrfc8400eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130217hkeywordsÇcFRRlFast ReroutekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[n0granking –crfcd8400irfcNumber –estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEExtensions to RSVP-TE for Label Switched Path (LSP) Egress ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx◊The updates to RFC 5280 described in this document provide alignment with the 2008 specification for Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs) and add support for internationalized email addresses in X.509 certificates.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. HousleyddatejŒõhfilenamegrfc8399eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130216hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9549epages	opublicationDate[Ûÿgranking œcrfcd8399irfcNumber œestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Internationalization Updates to RFC 5280dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new name form for inclusion in the otherName field of an X.509 Subject Alternative Name and Issuer Alternative Name extension that allows a certificate subject to be associated with an internationalized email address.

This document updates RFC 5280.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiW. Chuangddatej‘Æhfilenamegrfc8398eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelampsdfullx8lamps - Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdnamex0Limited Additional Mechanisms for PKIX and SMIMEdtypebwgbidjdoc-130215hkeywordsÉcEAIdPKIXlemal addresskobsoletedByÅd9598epagesopublicationDate[Ûœgranking Œcrfcd8398irfcNumber ŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Internationalized Email Addresses in X.509 CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) routing can be extended to support multiple levels by building on the multilevel feature of IS-IS routing.  Depending on how nicknames are managed, there are two primary alternatives to realize TRILL multilevel: the unique nickname approach and the aggregated nickname approach as discussed in RFC 8243.  This document specifies a unique nickname approach.  This approach gives unique nicknames to all TRILL switches across the multilevel TRILL campus.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationcJITdnamegH. Zhai¢kaffiliationtChina Telcom Co. LtddnamefD. Liuddatej‘hfilenamegrfc8397eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130214hkeywordsÉjAggregatedkGlobal TreejLocal TreekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[âÌgranking Õcrfcd8397irfcNumber ÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Multilevel Using Unique NicknamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©The functions of the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) are rapidly migrating to the Internet.  This is generating new requirements for many traditional elements of the PSTN, including Telephone Numbers (TNs).  TNs no longer serve simply as telephone routing addresses: they are now identifiers that may be used by Internet-based services for a variety of purposes including session establishment, identity verification, and service enablement.  This problem statement examines how the existing tools for allocating and managing telephone numbers do not align with the use cases of the Internet environment and proposes a framework for Internet-based services relying on TNs.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmNeustar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliation`dnamejT. McGarryddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8396eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmoderndfullxTmodern - Managing, Ordering, Distributing, Exposing, & Registering telephone NumbersdnamexKManaging, Ordering, Distributing, Exposing, & Registering telephone Numbersdtypebwgbidjdoc-130213hkeywordsÉcSIPqProblem StatementwReal-Time CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[R5ˆgranking Ãcrfcd8396irfcNumber ÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉManaging, Ordering, Distributing, Exposing, and Registering Telephone Numbers (MODERN): Problem Statement, Use Cases, and FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document defines protocol extensions required to synchronize flow label states among Provider Edges (PEs) when using the BGP-based signaling procedures.  These protocol extensions are equally applicable to point-to-point Layer 2 Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs).  This document updates RFC 4761 by defining new flags in the Control Flags field of the Layer2 Info Extended Community.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehJ. Liste¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefB. Wen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. RabadanddatejÕjhfilenamegrfc8395eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130212hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate[3"ôgranking Àcrfcd8395irfcNumber ÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMExtensions to BGP-Signaled Pseudowires to Support Flow-Aware Transport LabelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉIn the Split Network Virtualization Edge (Split-NVE) architecture, the functions of the NVE are split across a server and a piece of external network equipment that is called an "External NVE". The server-resident control-plane functionality resides in control software, which may be part of hypervisor or container-management software; for simplicity, this document refers to the hypervisor as the "location" of this software.

One or more control-plane protocols between a hypervisor and its associated External NVE(s) are used by the hypervisor to distribute its virtual-machine networking state to the External NVE(s) for further handling. This document illustrates the functionality required by this type of control-plane signaling protocol and outlines the high-level requirements. Virtual-machine states as well as state transitioning are summarized to help clarify the protocol requirements.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationjArrcus IncdnamejL. Kreeger¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamehD. Blackddatej÷␀hfilenamegrfc8394eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-130211hkeywordsÇdNVO3cVDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[âÏgranking  crfcd8394irfcNumber  estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHSplit Network Virtualization Edge (Split-NVE) Control-Plane RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document describes a network that supports Service Function Chaining (SFC) using the Network Service Header (NSH) with no payload data and carrying only metadata. This is achieved by defining a new NSH "Next Protocol" type value of "None".

This document illustrates some of the functions that may be achieved or enhanced by this mechanism, but it does not provide an exhaustive list of use cases, nor is it intended to be definitive about the functions it describes. It is expected that other documents will describe specific use cases in more detail and will define the protocol mechanics for each use case.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drakeddatej’˘hfilenamegrfc8393eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130210hkeywordsÉxService Function ChainingvNetwork Service HeaderhMetadatakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÚ+ﬁgranking …crfcd8393irfcNumber …estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDOperating the Network Service Header (NSH) with Next Protocol "None"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝CBOR Web Token (CWT) is a compact means of representing claims to be transferred between two parties.  The claims in a CWT are encoded in the Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR), and CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) is used for added application-layer security protection.  A claim is a piece of information asserted about a subject and is represented as a name/value pair consisting of a claim name and a claim value.  CWT is derived from JSON Web Token (JWT) but uses CBOR rather than JSON.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliation`dnamemE. Wahlstroem¢kaffiliationjSpotify ABdnamejS. Erdtman¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”ihfilenamegrfc8392eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130209hkeywordsânJSON Web TokencJWTfClaimsx$Concise Binary Object RepresentationdCBORx"CBOR Object Signing and EncryptiondCOSEeOAuthcACEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÚ+€granking »crfcd8392irfcNumber »estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletCBOR Web Token (CWT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty’This note describes the eXtended Merkle Signature Scheme (XMSS), a hash-based digital signature system that is based on existing descriptions in scientific literature.  This note specifies Winternitz One-Time Signature Plus (WOTS+), a one-time signature scheme; XMSS, a single-tree scheme; and XMSS^MT, a multi-tree variant of XMSS.  Both XMSS and XMSS^MT use WOTS+ as a main building block.  XMSS provides cryptographic digital signatures without relying on the conjectured hardness of mathematical problems.  Instead, it is proven that it only relies on the properties of cryptographic hash functions.  XMSS provides strong security guarantees and is even secure when the collision resistance of the underlying hash function is broken.  It is suitable for compact implementations, is relatively simple to implement, and naturally resists side-channel attacks.  Unlike most other signature systems, hash-based signatures can so far withstand known attacks using quantum computers.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlTU EindhovendnamekA. Huelsing¢kaffiliationlTU DarmstadtdnamehD. Butin¢kaffiliationjgenua GmbHdnameiS. Gazdag¢kaffiliationrRadboud UniversitydnamelJ. Rijneveld¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Central FloridadnamekA. Mohaisenddatej’Ãhfilenamegrfc8391eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-130208hkeywordsèqDigital signaturelcryptographyxpost-quantum cryptographyuHash-based signaturesqMerkle signatureskMerkle treemhash functionjWinternitzx$Winternitz one-time signature schemedWOTSeW-OTSeWOTS+fW-OTS+gXMSS-MTomulti-tree XMSSkobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDate[âÁgranking «crfcd8391irfcNumber «estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex&XMSS: eXtended Merkle Signature SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉRSVP-TE provides support for the communication of exclusion information during Label Switched Path (LSP) setup. A typical LSP diversity use case is for protection, where two LSPs should follow different paths through the network in order to avoid single points of failure, thus greatly improving service availability. This document specifies an approach that can be used for network scenarios where the full path(s) is not necessarily known by use of an abstract identifier for the path. Three types of abstract identifiers are specified: client based, Path Computation Element (PCE) based, and network based. This document specifies two new diversity subobjects for the RSVP eXclude Route Object (XRO) and the Explicit Exclusion Route Subobject (EXRS).

For the protection use case, LSPs are typically created at a slow rate and exist for a long time so that it is reasonable to assume that a given (reference) path currently existing (with a well-known identifier) will continue to exist and can be used as a reference when creating the new diverse path. Re-routing of the existing (reference) LSP, before the new path is established, is not considered.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiD. Bellerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8390eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130207hkeywordsÅmLSP diversitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[OB¶granking ∆crfcd8390irfcNumber ∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*RSVP-TE Path Diversity Using Exclude RoutedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx™This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for Mapping of Address and Port with Encapsulation (MAP-E) for use with network management protocols.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnameeY. Fu¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamefB. Liu¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamegY. Chenddatej“™hfilenamegrfc8389eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-130206hkeywordsÇdIPv6cMAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate\‘™granking ≈crfcd8389irfcNumber ≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYDefinitions of Managed Objects for Mapping of Address and Port with Encapsulation (MAP-E)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document discusses the usage and applicability of BGP MPLS-based Ethernet VPN (EVPN) in a simple and fairly common deployment scenario.  The different EVPN procedures are explained in the example scenario along with the benefits and trade-offs of each option.  This document is intended to provide a simplified guide for the deployment of EVPN networks.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoS. Palislamovic¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaroddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8388eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130205hkeywordsÅdEVPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ˝£ygranking ƒcrfcd8388irfcNumber ƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Usage and Applicability of BGP MPLS-Based Ethernet VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThis memo describes challenges associated with securing resource- constrained smart object devices.  The memo describes a possible deployment model where resource-constrained devices sign message objects, discusses the availability of cryptographic libraries for resource-constrained devices, and presents some preliminary experiences with those libraries for message signing on resource- constrained devices.  Lastly, the memo discusses trade-offs involving different types of security approaches.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehM. Sethi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamegH. Backddatej’Ôhfilenamegrfc8387eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlwigdfullx+lwig - Light-Weight Implementation Guidancednamex$Light-Weight Implementation Guidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130204hkeywordsàcIoThsecurityiintegritygsigningcECCdCoAPjasymmetriclcryptographykobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateZ˚xkgranking √crfcd8387irfcNumber √estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYPractical Considerations and Implementation Experiences in Securing Smart Object NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#A number of application-layer protocols make use of IP broadcast or multicast messages for functions such as local service discovery or name resolution.  Some of these functions can only be implemented efficiently using such mechanisms.  When using broadcast or multicast messages, a passive observer in the same broadcast or multicast domain can trivially record these messages and analyze their content.  Therefore, designers of protocols that make use of broadcast or multicast messages need to take special care when designing their protocols.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx'University of Applied Sciences AugsburgdnameiR. Winter¢kaffiliationlConntac GmbHdnamehM. Faath¢kaffiliationx'University of Applied Sciences AugsburgdnamelF. Weisshaarddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8386eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130203hkeywordsÉmIP broadcastsimulticastvprivacy considerationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ˝£wgranking ¬crfcd8386irfcNumber ¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPrivacy Considerations for Protocols Relying on IP Broadcast or MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThis document specifies methods to interconnect multiple TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) sites with an intervening MPLS network using existing TRILL and VPLS (Virtual Private LAN Service) standards.  This document addresses two problems: 1) providing connection between more than two TRILL sites that are separated by an MPLS provider network and 2) providing a single logical virtualized TRILL network for different tenants that are separated by an MPLS provider network.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Umair¢kaffiliationmPALC NetworksdnamerS. Kingston Smiler¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegL. Yongddatej‘Uhfilenamegrfc8385eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130202hkeywordsÉdVPLSdVPTScTIRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[±"granking ¡crfcd8385irfcNumber ¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Transparent Transport over MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document addresses the problem of the size and freshness of the endnode learning table in edge Routing Bridges (RBridges), by allowing endnodes to volunteer for endnode learning and encapsulation/decapsulation.  Such an endnode is known as a "Smart Endnode".  Only the attached edge RBridge can distinguish a "Smart Endnode" from a "normal endnode".  The Smart Endnode uses the nickname of the attached edge RBridge, so this solution does not consume extra nicknames.  The solution also enables endnodes that are Fine-Grained Label (FGL) aware.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnameeF. Hu¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegT. Liaoddatej‘}hfilenamegrfc8384eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130201hkeywordsÇeTRILLmSmart EndnodekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Fˆàgranking ¿crfcd8384irfcNumber ¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Smart EndnodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"The TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol, by default, learns end station addresses from observing the data plane. In particular, it learns local Media Access Control (MAC) addresses and the edge switch port of attachment from the receipt of local data frames and learns remote MAC addresses and the edge switch port of attachment from the decapsulation of remotely sourced TRILL Data packets.

This document specifies a message by which a TRILL switch can explicitly request other TRILL switches to flush certain MAC reachability learned through the decapsulation of TRILL Data packets. This is a supplement to the TRILL automatic address forgetting (see Section 4.8.3 of RFC 6325) and can assist in achieving more rapid convergence in case of topology or configuration change.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefW. Hao¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Umairddatej”‡hfilenamegrfc8383eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130200hkeywordsÑkconvergencedVLANjdata labelcFGLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[â‰granking øcrfcd8383irfcNumber øestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Address Flush MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a mechanism to detect whether end-to-end data flows share a common bottleneck.  This mechanism relies on summary statistics that are calculated based on continuous measurements and used as input to a grouping algorithm that runs wherever the knowledge is needed.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxSimula Research LaboratorydnamehD. Hayes¢kaffiliationxSimula Research LaboratorydnameiS. Ferlin¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnameiK. Hiorthddatej÷!hfilenamegrfc8382eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130199hkeywordsÅcSBDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[1÷ægranking æcrfcd8382irfcNumber æestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHShared Bottleneck Detection for Coupled Congestion Control for RTP MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThe IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol is implemented by devices called TRILL switches or RBridges (Routing Bridges).  TRILL includes a general mechanism, called an RBridge Channel, for the transmission of typed messages between RBridges in the same campus and between RBridges and end stations on the same link.  This document specifies a method to send vendor-specific messages over the RBridge Channel facility.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefW. Hao¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekA. Banerjeeddatej÷!hfilenamegrfc8381eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130198hkeywordsÇcOUIcCIDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[≠agranking Ωcrfcd8381irfcNumber ΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Vendor-Specific RBridge Channel ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÀThis document describes how data center networks can benefit from non-RBridge nodes performing TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) encapsulation with assistance from a directory service.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationhDell/EMCdnamejR. Perlmanddatej÷!hfilenamegrfc8380eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130197hkeywordsÇiDirectoryhNicknamekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate[≥0granking ºcrfcd8380irfcNumber ºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUDirectory-Assisted Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) EncapsulationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìWhen a link is being prepared to be taken out of service, the traffic needs to be diverted from both ends of the link. Increasing the metric to the highest value on one side of the link is not sufficient to divert the traffic flowing in the other direction.

It is useful for the routers in an OSPFv2 or OSPFv3 routing domain to be able to advertise a link as being in a graceful-shutdown state to indicate impending maintenance activity on the link. This information can be used by the network devices to reroute the traffic effectively.

This document describes the protocol extensions to disseminate graceful-link-shutdown information in OSPFv2 and OSPFv3.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnameiP. Sarkar¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationpebay CorporationdnamejM. Nanduri¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalilddatej’ähfilenamegrfc8379eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130196hkeywordsÉdMPLScIGPdOSPFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZˆ3Ígranking ªcrfcd8379irfcNumber ªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSPF Graceful Link ShutdowndtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}When multicast sources and receivers are active at Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) sites, the core network is required to use native multicast so packets can be delivered from sources to group members.  When multicast is not available to connect the multicast sites together, a signal-free mechanism can be used to allow traffic to flow between sites.  The mechanism described in this document uses unicast replication and encapsulation over the core network for the data plane and uses the LISP mapping database system so encapsulators at the source LISP multicast site can find decapsulators at the receiver LISP multicast sites.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiV. Moreno¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacciddatej÷ hfilenamegrfc8378eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130195hkeywordsÇdLISPjdeploymentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÍ1úgranking ∫crfcd8378irfcNumber ∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Signal-Free Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis document specifies extensions to the IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol to support multi-topology routing of unicast and multi-destination traffic based on IS-IS (Intermediate System to Intermediate System) multi-topology specified in RFC 5120.  This document updates RFCs 6325 and 7177.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekA. Banerjeeddatejœ´hfilenamegrfc8377eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130194hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[`Î7granking πcrfcd8377irfcNumber πestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Multi-TopologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆLow-Power Wide Area Networks (LPWANs) are wireless technologies with characteristics such as large coverage areas, low bandwidth, possibly very small packet and application-layer data sizes, and long battery life operation.  This memo is an informational overview of the set of LPWAN technologies being considered in the IETF and of the gaps that exist between the needs of those technologies and the goal of running IP in LPWANs.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8376eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelpwandfullx.lpwan - IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdnamex&IPv6 over Low Power Wide-Area Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130193hkeywordsÅx$Low Power Wide Area Network OverviewkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate[â€granking ∏crfcd8376irfcNumber ∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Low-Power Wide Area Network (LPWAN) OverviewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document specifies the behavior that is expected from the Domain Name System with regard to DNS queries for names ending with '.home.arpa.' and designates this domain as a special-use domain name. 'home.arpa.' is designated for non-unique use in residential home networks.  The Home Networking Control Protocol (HNCP) is updated to use the 'home.arpa.' domain instead of '.home'.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Pfister¢kaffiliationsNibbhaya ConsultingdnamehT. Lemonddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8375eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130192hkeywordsÑgHomenetcTLDgRFC6761j.home.arpakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ˝£rgranking ∑crfcd8375irfcNumber ∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSpecial-Use Domain 'home.arpa.'dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyªThis document captures the design rationale of the initial draft version of what became RFC 8205 (the BGPsec protocol specification).  The designers needed to balance many competing factors, and this document lists the decisions that were made in favor of or against each design choice.  This document also presents brief summaries of the arguments that aided the decision process.  Where appropriate, this document also provides brief notes on design decisions that changed as the specification was reviewed and updated by the IETF SIDR Working Group and that resulted in RFC 8205.  These notes highlight the differences and provide pointers to details and rationale regarding those design changes.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhUSA NISTdnameiK. Sriramddatej’Ühfilenamegrfc8374eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130191hkeywordsÅxInternet Routing SecuritykobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateZ·≠granking ∂crfcd8374irfcNumber ∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex;BGPsec Design Choices and Summary of Supporting DiscussionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMUsers have various human (i.e., natural) language needs, abilities, and preferences regarding spoken, written, and signed languages. This document defines new Session Description Protocol (SDP) media- level attributes so that when establishing interactive communication sessions ("calls"), it is possible to negotiate (i.e., communicate and match) the caller's language and media needs with the capabilities of the called party. This is especially important for emergency calls, because it allows for a call to be handled by a call taker capable of communicating with the user or for a translator or relay operator to be bridged into the call during setup. However, this also applies to non-emergency calls (for example, calls to a company call center).

This document describes the need as well as a solution that uses new SDP media attributes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCore Technology ConsultingdnamejR. Gellensddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8373eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdslimdfullx/slim - Selection of Language for Internet Mediadnamex(Selection of Language for Internet Mediadtypebwgbidjdoc-130190hkeywordsÖcSDPhlanguagenhuman languagecSIPdSLIMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Ogranking µcrfcd8373irfcNumber µestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Negotiating Human Language in Real-Time CommunicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8865∂habstractxˆThis document discusses aspects to consider when developing a solution for MPLS flow identification.  The key application that needs this solution is in-band performance monitoring of MPLS flows when MPLS is used to encapsulate user data packets.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirskyddatej÷ hfilenamegrfc8372eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130189hkeywordsÉcOAMvperformance monitoringsflow identificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÍ1ògranking ¥crfcd8372irfcNumber ¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'MPLS Flow Identification ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxóThis document defines additional identifier type numbers for use with the mobile node identifier option for Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) as defined by RFC 4283.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiFutureweidnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationoVasona NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalliddatej‘Çhfilenamegrfc8371eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130188hkeywordsÉhMobilitydIPv6nAuthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[Vº„granking ≥crfcd8371irfcNumber ≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Mobile Node Identifier Types for MIPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩNetworks that utilize RSVP-TE LSPs are encountering implementations that have a limited ability to support the growth in the number of LSPs deployed.

This document defines two techniques, Refresh-Interval Independent RSVP (RI-RSVP) and Per-Peer Flow Control, that reduce the number of processing cycles required to maintain RSVP-TE LSP state in Label Switching Routers (LSRs) and hence allow implementations to support larger scale deployments.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliationjGoogle IncdnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationjGoogle IncdnameiR. Shakir¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamejD. Pacella¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegT. Saadddatej’œhfilenamegrfc8370eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130187hkeywordsÉoRSVP-TE ScalinggRI-RSVPuPer-Peer Flow ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZˆ3Êgranking ≤crfcd8370irfcNumber ≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Techniques to Improve the Scalability of RSVP-TE DeploymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty}It is clear that Unicode will eventually exhaust its supply of code points, and more will be needed.  Assuming ISO and the Unicode Consortium follow the practices of the IETF, the next Unicode code point size will be 128 bits.  This document describes how this future 128-bit Unicode can be leveraged to improve IPv6 adoption and finally bring internationalization support to IPv6.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationn128 TechnologydnameiH. Kaplanddatejœ≠hfilenamegrfc8369eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-84177hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ¿˚granking ±crfcd8369irfcNumber ±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-Internationalizing IPv6 Using 128-Bit UnicodedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM), as per BCP 161, for data networks is often subject to the problem of circular dependencies when relying on connectivity provided by the network to be managed for the OAM purposes.

Provisioning while bringing up devices and networks tends to be more difficult to automate than service provisioning later on. Changes in core network functions impacting reachability cannot be automated because of ongoing connectivity requirements for the OAM equipment itself, and widely used OAM protocols are not secure enough to be carried across the network without security concerns.

This document describes how to integrate OAM processes with an autonomic control plane in order to provide stable and secure connectivity for those OAM processes. This connectivity is not subject to the aforementioned circular dependencies.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Behringerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8368eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130186hkeywordsÉtautonomic networkingtautonomous operationoself-managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÛ·¯granking ∞crfcd8368irfcNumber ∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuUsing an Autonomic Control Plane for Stable Connectivity of Network Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyRouters and middleboxes terminate packets for various reasons.  In some cases, these packets are wrongfully terminated.  This memo describes some of the most common scenarios of wrongful termination of Internet Protocol (IP) packets and presents recommendations for mitigating them.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejJ. Yallouzddatejœ≠hfilenamegrfc8367eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-84176hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ¿˚granking Øcrfcd8367irfcNumber ØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex6Wrongful Termination of Internet Protocol (IP) PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document defines a strategy to securely assign a pledge to an owner using an artifact signed, directly or indirectly, by the pledge's manufacturer. This artifact is known as a "voucher".

This document defines an artifact format as a YANG-defined JSON document that has been signed using a Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) structure. Other YANG-derived formats are possible. The voucher artifact is normally generated by the pledge's manufacturer (i.e., the Manufacturer Authorized Signing Authority (MASA)).

This document only defines the voucher artifact, leaving it to other documents to describe specialized protocols for accessing it.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationrSandelman SoftwarednamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Pritikin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiT. Eckertddatej’Ÿhfilenamegrfc8366eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeanimadfullx:anima - Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdnamex2Autonomic Networking Integrated Model and Approachdtypebwgbidjdoc-130185hkeywordsÑgvouchertautonomic networkingtautonomous operationoself-managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÛê_granking Æcrfcd8366irfcNumber ÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A Voucher Artifact for Bootstrapping ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document specifies how Ethernet VPN (EVPN) can be used as a Network Virtualization Overlay (NVO) solution and explores the various tunnel encapsulation options over IP and their impact on the EVPN control plane and procedures.  In particular, the following encapsulation options are analyzed: Virtual Extensible LAN (VXLAN), Network Virtualization using Generic Routing Encapsulation (NVGRE), and MPLS over GRE.  This specification is also applicable to Generic Network Virtualization Encapsulation (GENEVE); however, some incremental work is required, which will be covered in a separate document.  This document also specifies new multihoming procedures for split-horizon filtering and mass withdrawal.  It also specifies EVPN route constructions for VXLAN/NVGRE encapsulations and Autonomous System Border Router (ASBR) procedures for multihoming of Network Virtualization Edge (NVE) devices.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejR. Shekhar¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickxddatej’◊hfilenamegrfc8365eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130184hkeywordsÇx+EVPN Control Plane with VxLAN Encapsulationx+EVPN Control Plane with NvGRE EncapsulationkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateZΩå∏granking ≠crfcd8365irfcNumber ≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCA Network Virtualization Overlay Solution Using Ethernet VPN (EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9746∂habstracty|Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) uses a Rendezvous Point (RP) and shared trees to forward multicast packets from new sources.  Once Last-Hop Routers (LHRs) receive packets from a new source, they may join the Shortest Path Tree (SPT) for the source for optimal forwarding.  This document defines a new mechanism that provides a way to support PIM-SM without the need for PIM registers, RPs, or shared trees.  Multicast source information is flooded throughout the multicast domain using a new generic PIM Flooding Mechanism (PFM).  This allows LHRs to learn about new sources without receiving initial data packets.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationxAegis BMD Program OfficednamegM. Brig¢kaffiliationcFMVdnamekA. Jonassonddatej’áhfilenamegrfc8364eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-130183hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZº.3granking ¨crfcd8364irfcNumber ¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6PIM Flooding Mechanism (PFM) and Source Discovery (SD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9436d8736∂habstracty¨The International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication standardization sector (ITU-T) has extended its Recommendations G.694.1 and G.872 to include a new Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) grid by defining channel spacings, a set of nominal central frequencies, and the concept of the "frequency slot". Corresponding techniques for data-plane connections are known as "flexi-grid".

Based on the characteristics of flexi-grid defined in G.694.1 and in RFCs 7698 and 7699, this document describes the Open Shortest Path First - Traffic Engineering (OSPF-TE) extensions in support of GMPLS control of networks that include devices that use the new flexible optical grid.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehH. Zheng¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnamekR. Casellas¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelliddatej’˝hfilenamegrfc8363eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-130182hkeywordsÖjflexi-gridgOSPF-TEqcentral frequencynfrequency slotochannel spacingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[QÁgranking ´crfcd8363irfcNumber ´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhGMPLS OSPF-TE Extensions in Support of Flexi-Grid Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOSPFv3 requires functional extension beyond what can readily be done with the fixed-format Link State Advertisement (LSA) as described in RFC 5340. Without LSA extension, attributes associated with OSPFv3 links and advertised IPv6 prefixes must be advertised in separate LSAs and correlated to the fixed-format LSAs. This document extends the LSA format by encoding the existing OSPFv3 LSA information in Type-Length-Value (TLV) tuples and allowing advertisement of additional information with additional TLVs. Backward-compatibility mechanisms are also described.

This document updates RFC 5340, "OSPF for IPv6", and RFC 5838, "Support of Address Families in OSPFv3", by providing TLV-based encodings for the base OSPFv3 unicast support and OSPFv3 address family support.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekD. Goethals¢kaffiliation`dnameoV. Reddy Vallem¢kaffiliation`dnamehF. BakerddatejœKhfilenamegrfc8362eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130181hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateZƒOgranking ™crfcd8362irfcNumber ™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3OSPFv3 Link State Advertisement (LSA) ExtensibilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) active-active access, a Reverse Path Forwarding (RPF) check failure issue may occur when using the pseudo-nickname mechanism specified in RFC 7781.  This document describes a solution to resolve this RPF check failure issue through centralized replication.  All ingress Routing Bridges (RBridges) send Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, and Multicast (BUM) traffic to a centralized node with unicast TRILL encapsulation.  When the centralized node receives the BUM traffic, it decapsulates the packets and forwards them to their destination RBridges using a distribution tree established per the TRILL base protocol (RFC 6325).  To avoid RPF check failure on an RBridge sitting between the ingress RBridge and the centralized replication node, some change in the RPF calculation algorithm is required.  RPF checks on each RBridge MUST be calculated as if the centralized node was the ingress RBridge, instead of being calculated using the actual ingress RBridge.  This document updates RFC 6325.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Hao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationgEquinixdnamejM. Durrani¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamehS. Gupta¢kaffiliationhMediaTecdnameeA. Quddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8361eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130180hkeywordsÑeTRILLgRBridgecCMTeLAALPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ–,ôgranking ©crfcd8361irfcNumber ©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexóTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Centralized Replication for Active-Active Broadcast, Unknown Unicast, and Multicast (BUM) TrafficdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document specifies an alternative to the certificate validation procedure specified in RFC 6487 that reduces aspects of operational fragility in the management of certificates in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), while retaining essential security features.

The procedure specified in RFC 6487 requires that Resource Certificates are rejected entirely if they are found to overclaim any resources not contained on the issuing certificate, whereas the validation process defined here allows an issuing Certification Authority (CA) to chose to communicate that such Resource Certificates should be accepted for the intersection of their resources and the issuing certificate.

It should be noted that the validation process defined here considers validation under a single trust anchor (TA) only. In particular, concerns regarding overclaims where multiple configured TAs claim overlapping resources are considered out of scope for this document.

This choice is signaled by a set of alternative Object Identifiers (OIDs) per "X.509 Extensions for IP Addresses and AS Identifiers" (RFC 3779) and "Certificate Policy (CP) for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)" (RFC 6484). It should be noted that in case these OIDs are not used for any certificate under a trust anchor, the validation procedure defined here has the same outcome as the procedure defined in RFC 6487.

Furthermore, this document provides an alternative to Route Origin Authorization (ROA) (RFC 6482) and BGPsec Router Certificate (BGPsec PKI Profiles -- publication requested) validation.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationfLACNICdnamekC. Martinez¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamenT. Bruijnzeels¢kaffiliationdARINdnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationgAFRINICdnamegD. Shawddatejœ5hfilenamegrfc8360eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130179hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ≈§~granking ®crfcd8360irfcNumber ®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAResource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Validation ReconsidereddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document discusses a Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Resource reSerVation Protocol with Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) mechanism that enables the network to assign an upstream label for a bidirectional Label Switched Path (LSP).  This is useful in scenarios where a given node does not have sufficient information to assign the correct upstream label on its own and needs to rely on the downstream node to pick an appropriate label.  This document updates RFCs 3471, 3473, and 6205 as it defines processing for a special label value in the UPSTREAM_LABEL object.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiV. Beeram¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Diosddatejœ£hfilenamegrfc8359eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130178hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateZ®S—granking ßcrfcd8359irfcNumber ßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNetwork-Assigned Upstream LabeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±RFC 5485 specifies the conventions for digital signatures on Internet-Drafts. The Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) is used to create a detached signature, which is stored in a separate companion file so that no existing utilities are impacted by the addition of the digital signature.

The RFC Editor recently published the first RFC that includes non- ASCII characters in a text file. The conventions specified in RFC 7997 were followed. We assume that non-ASCII characters will soon start appearing in Internet-Drafts as well. This document updates the handling of digital signatures on Internet-Drafts that contain non-ASCII characters in a text file.

This document updates RFC 5485.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej’Œhfilenamegrfc8358eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130177hkeywordsÉccmsxcryptographic message syntaxrdetached signaturekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateZß•granking ¶crfcd8358irfcNumber ¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Update to Digital Signatures on Internet-Draft DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document defines an extension to the DHCP protocols that allows a relay agent to use any available source port for upstream communications.  The extension also allows inclusion of a DHCP option that can be used to statelessly route responses back to the appropriate source port on downstream communications.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. ChenddatejŒöhfilenamegrfc8357eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130176hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateZüãgranking •crfcd8357irfcNumber •estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Generalized UDP Source Port for DHCP RelaydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIANA assigns values to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) parameters (messages, objects, TLVs). IANA established a top-level registry to contain all PCEP codepoints and sub-registries. This top-level registry contains sub-registries for PCEP message, object, and TLV types. The allocation policy for each of these sub-registries is IETF Review.

This document updates RFC 5440 by changing the allocation policies for these three registries to mark some of the codepoints as assigned for Experimental Use.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationtLancaster UniversitydnamegD. King¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrelddatej’vhfilenamegrfc8356eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130175hkeywordsÑcPCEdPCEPdIANAlExperimentalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZüÅgranking §crfcd8356irfcNumber §estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Experimental Codepoint Allocation for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∂This document identifies and describes the requirements for a set of use cases related to Segment Routing network resiliency on Source Packet Routing in Networking (SPRING) networks.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Shakirddatej’Ghfilenamegrfc8355eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130174hkeywordsÑoSEGMENT ROUTINGjRESILIENCYjPROTECTIONkCONVERGENCEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZº.granking £crfcd8355irfcNumber £estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMResiliency Use Cases in Source Packet Routing in Networking (SPRING) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Source Packet Routing in Networking (SPRING) architecture describes how Segment Routing can be used to steer packets through an IPv6 or MPLS network using the source routing paradigm.  This document illustrates some use cases for Segment Routing in an IPv6-only environment.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamemJ. Brzozowski¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamehJ. Leddy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Maglione¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. TownsleyddatejŒ*hfilenamegrfc8354eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspringdfullx,spring - Source Packet Routing in Networkingdnamex#Source Packet Routing in Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130173hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateZº-˙granking ¢crfcd8354irfcNumber ¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Use Cases for IPv6 Source Packet Routing in Networking (SPRING)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#The Generic Security Services Application Programming Interface (GSS-API) offers application programmers uniform access to security services atop a variety of underlying cryptographic mechanisms. This document updates the Java bindings for the GSS-API that are specified in "Generic Security Service API Version 2: Java Bindings Update" (RFC 5653). This document obsoletes RFC 5653 by adding a new output token field to the GSSException class so that when the initSecContext or acceptSecContext methods of the GSSContext class fail, it has a chance to emit an error token that can be sent to the peer for debugging or informational purpose. The stream-based GSSContext methods are also removed in this version.

The GSS-API is described at a language-independent conceptual level in "Generic Security Service Application Program Interface Version 2, Update 1" (RFC 2743). The GSS-API allows a caller application to authenticate a principal identity, to delegate rights to a peer, and to apply security services such as confidentiality and integrity on a per-message basis. Examples of security mechanisms defined for GSS-API are "The Simple Public-Key GSS-API Mechanism (SPKM)" (RFC 2025) and "The Kerberos Version 5 Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Mechanism: Version 2" (RFC 4121).fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekM. Upadhyay¢kaffiliationmActivIdentitydnamejS. Malkani¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegW. Wangddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8353eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130172hkeywordsÇdJGSSgGSS-APIkobsoletedByÄepages`opublicationDate[Z granking °crfcd8353irfcNumber °estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Generic Security Service API Version 2: Java Bindings UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document describes the challenges for energy-efficient protocol operation on constrained devices and the current practices used to overcome those challenges.  It summarizes the main link-layer techniques used for energy-efficient networking, and it highlights the impact of such techniques on the upper-layer protocols so that they can together achieve an energy-efficient behavior.  The document also provides an overview of energy-efficient mechanisms available at each layer of the IETF protocol suite specified for constrained-node networks.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcUPCdnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekM. Kovatsch¢kaffiliationx+China Academy of Telecommunication ResearchdnamegH. Tian¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefZ. Caoddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8352eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlwigdfullx+lwig - Light-Weight Implementation Guidancednamex$Light-Weight Implementation Guidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130171hkeywordsÜcIoTrRadio Duty Cyclingg6LoWPANc6LodCoAPcRPLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZƒOgranking †crfcd8352irfcNumber †estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Energy-Efficient Features of Internet of Things ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxÚThis document registers the application/pkcs8-encrypted media type for the EncryptedPrivateKeyInfo type of PKCS #8.  An instance of this media type carries a single encrypted private key, BER-encoded as a single EncryptedPrivateKeyInfo value.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmPenango, Inc.dnamejS. LeonardddatejŒ)hfilenamegrfc8351eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130170hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate[3"ìgranking ücrfcd8351irfcNumber üestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.The PKCS #8 EncryptedPrivateKeyInfo Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) is a protocol for encapsulating a station's data frames between the Wireless Transmission Point (WTP) and Access Controller (AC).  Specifically, the station's IEEE 802.11 data frames can be either locally bridged or tunneled to the AC.  When tunneled, a CAPWAP Data Channel is used for tunneling.  In many deployments, encapsulating data frames to an entity other than the AC (for example, to an Access Router (AR)) is desirable.  Furthermore, it may also be desirable to use different tunnel encapsulation modes between the WTP and the Access Router.  This document defines an extension to the CAPWAP protocol that supports this capability and refers to it as alternate tunnel encapsulation.  The alternate tunnel encapsulation allows 1) the WTP to tunnel non-management data frames to an endpoint different from the AC and 2) the WTP to tunnel using one of many known encapsulation types, such as IP-IP, IP-GRE, or CAPWAP.  The WTP may advertise support for alternate tunnel encapsulation during the discovery and join process, and the AC may select one of the supported alternate tunnel encapsulation types while configuring the WTP.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemR. Pazhyannur¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefZ. Cao¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeZ. Duddatej’Ühfilenamegrfc8350eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130169hkeywordsÑeWi-FidWLANdPMIPcGREkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ·£granking ûcrfcd8350irfcNumber ûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmAlternate Tunnel Encapsulation for Data Frames in Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies three YANG modules and one submodule. Together, they form the core routing data model that serves as a framework for configuring and managing a routing subsystem. It is expected that these modules will be augmented by additional YANG modules defining data models for control-plane protocols, route filters, and other functions. The core routing data model provides common building blocks for such extensions -- routes, Routing Information Bases (RIBs), and control-plane protocols.

The YANG modules in this document conform to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA). This document obsoletes RFC 8022.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnameiL. Lhotka¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Quddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8349eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130168hkeywordsÑmconfigurationxIPv6 Router AdvertisementsgNETCONFhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÄepagesPopublicationDateZ∞Pgranking ùcrfcd8349irfcNumber ùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7A YANG Data Model for Routing Management (NMDA Version)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxZThis document defines a YANG data model for the management of hardware on a single server.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. Romascanuddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8348eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130167hkeywordsÅjENTITY-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateZπ~granking úcrfcd8348irfcNumber úestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)A YANG Data Model for Hardware ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÉThis document describes a data model for the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP).  Both versions 2 and 3 of VRRP are covered.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeJabildnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationeJabildnamekA. Kyparlis¢kaffiliationfVMwarednameiR. Parikh¢kaffiliati