This document defines an abstract (generic, or base) YANG data model for network/service topologies and inventories.  The data model serves as a base model that is augmented with technology-specific details in other, more specific topology and inventory data models.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiJ. Medved¢kaffiliationxPantheon Technologies SROdnamehR. Varga¢kaffiliationqBracket ComputingdnamejN. Bahadur¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnamerH. Ananthakrishnan¢kaffiliationeJabildnamefX. Liuddatej’hfilenamegrfc8345eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-130164hkeywordsÅhtopologykobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateZ¨7’granking ôcrfcd8345irfcNumber ôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A YANG Data Model for Network TopologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a YANG data model for management of IP implementations. The data model includes configuration and system state.

The YANG data model in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture defined in RFC 8342.

This document obsoletes RFC 7277.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. BjorklundddatejŒ∆hfilenamegrfc8344eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130163hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateZ¨7”granking òcrfcd8344irfcNumber òestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#A YANG Data Model for IP ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜This document defines a YANG data model for the management of network interfaces. It is expected that interface-type-specific data models augment the generic interfaces data model defined in this document. The data model includes definitions for configuration and system state (status information and counters for the collection of statistics).

The YANG data model in this document conforms to the Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA) defined in RFC 8342.

This document obsoletes RFC 7223.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. BjorklundddatejŒ∑hfilenamegrfc8343eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130162hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateZ¨7—granking ócrfcd8343irfcNumber óestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A YANG Data Model for Interface ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂Datastores are a fundamental concept binding the data models written in the YANG data modeling language to network management protocols such as the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) and RESTCONF.  This document defines an architectural framework for datastores based on the experience gained with the initial simpler model, addressing requirements that were not well supported in the initial model.  This document updates RFC 7950.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiP. Shafer¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiK. Watsen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Wiltonddatej’Ühfilenamegrfc8342eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130161hkeywordsÑdYANGgNETCONFhRESTCONFrNetwork ManagementkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateZ¨7œgranking ñcrfcd8342irfcNumber ñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Network Management Datastore Architecture (NMDA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty&The standardization of network configuration interfaces for use with the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) or the RESTCONF protocol requires a structured and secure operating environment that promotes human usability and multi-vendor interoperability. There is a need for standard mechanisms to restrict NETCONF or RESTCONF protocol access for particular users to a preconfigured subset of all available NETCONF or RESTCONF protocol operations and content. This document defines such an access control model.

This document obsoletes RFC 6536.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej’hfilenamegrfc8341eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130160hkeywordsÉpNETCONF RESTCONFdYANGcXMLkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateZ¨7Ãgranking ïcrfcd8341irfcNumber ïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber[etotaletitlex*Network Configuration Access Control ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx˙This document captures the current syntax used in YANG module tree diagrams.  The purpose of this document is to provide a single location for this definition.  This syntax may be updated from time to time based on the evolution of the YANG language.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Bergerddatejœ®hfilenamegrfc8340eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130159hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ¨7«granking îcrfcd8340irfcNumber îestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber◊etotaletitlerYANG Tree DiagramsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8791∂habstractx¸Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping is a widely deployed Operation, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) mechanism in MPLS networks.  This document describes a mechanism to verify connectivity of Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Pseudowires (PWs) using LSP Ping.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegP. Jain¢kaffiliationlVMWare, Inc.dnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnameiS. Aldrinddatejœïhfilenamegrfc8339eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130158hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateZ®S»granking ìcrfcd8339irfcNumber ìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGDefinition of P2MP PW TLV for Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis document specifies a mechanism to signal Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Pseudowire (PW) trees using LDP.  Such a mechanism is suitable for any Layer 2 VPN service requiring P2MP connectivity over an IP or MPLS-enabled PSN.  A P2MP PW established via the proposed mechanism is root initiated.  This document updates RFC 7385 by reassigning the reserved value 0xFF to be the wildcard transport tunnel type.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. SivabalanddatejŒíhfilenamegrfc8338eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130157hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZü%fgranking ícrfcd8338irfcNumber íestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASignaling Root-Initiated Point-to-Multipoint Pseudowire Using LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯This document introduces a new class of Model-Based Metrics designed to assess if a complete Internet path can be expected to meet a predefined Target Transport Performance by applying a suite of IP diagnostic tests to successive subpaths. The subpath-at-a-time tests can be robustly applied to critical infrastructure, such as network interconnections or even individual devices, to accurately detect if any part of the infrastructure will prevent paths traversing it from meeting the Target Transport Performance.

Model-Based Metrics rely on mathematical models to specify a Targeted IP Diagnostic Suite, a set of IP diagnostic tests designed to assess whether common transport protocols can be expected to meet a predetermined Target Transport Performance over an Internet path.

For Bulk Transport Capacity, the IP diagnostics are built using test streams and statistical criteria for evaluating the packet transfer that mimic TCP over the complete path. The temporal structure of the test stream (e.g., bursts) mimics TCP or other transport protocols carrying bulk data over a long path. However, they are constructed to be independent of the details of the subpath under test, end systems, or applications. Likewise, the success criteria evaluates the packet transfer statistics of the subpath against criteria determined by protocol performance models applied to the Target Transport Performance of the complete path. The success criteria also does not depend on the details of the subpath, end systems, or applications.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiM. Mathis¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8337eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130156hkeywordsÖkperformancembulk capacitycBTCjdiagnosticjstatisticskobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateZ´àgranking ëcrfcd8337irfcNumber ëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Model-Based Metrics for Bulk Transport CapacitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxrThis document specifies the ORIGIN frame for HTTP/2, to indicate what origins are available on a given connection.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnameiE. Nygrenddatej’~hfilenamegrfc8336eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130155hkeywordsÇuconnection coalescingdHTTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ≤ßÄgranking êcrfcd8336irfcNumber êestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewThe ORIGIN HTTP/2 FramedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This document describes a network diagnostic tool called PROBE.  PROBE is similar to PING in that it can be used to query the status of a probed interface, but it differs from PING in that it does not require bidirectional connectivity between the probing and probed interfaces.  Instead, PROBE requires bidirectional connectivity between the probing interface and a proxy interface.  The proxy interface can reside on the same node as the probed interface, or it can reside on a node to which the probed interface is directly connected.  This document updates RFC 4884.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Thomas¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkova¢kaffiliationgVerizondnameiC. Lenart¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej’phfilenamegrfc8335eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130154hkeywordsÇdPingdICMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZèo‰granking ècrfcd8335irfcNumber èestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'PROBE: A Utility for Probing InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx“This document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension mapping for the provisioning and management of domain name registrations and applications during the launch of a domain name registry.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationnCloud RegistrydnamefW. Tan¢kaffiliationnCentralNic LtddnamehG. Brownddatej’xhfilenamegrfc8334eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130153hkeywordsácEPPgSunrisehLandrushwTrademark ClearinghousepTrademark Claimstdomain name registryllaunch phasekobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateZùö¢granking écrfcd8334irfcNumber éestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCLaunch Phase Mapping for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚This document describes a mechanism for link-state routing protocols that prevents local transient forwarding loops in case of link failure. This mechanism proposes a two-step convergence by introducing a delay between the convergence of the node adjacent to the topology change and the network-wide convergence.

Because this mechanism delays the IGP convergence, it may only be used for planned maintenance or when Fast Reroute (FRR) protects the traffic during the time between the link failure and the IGP convergence.

The mechanism is limited to the link-down event in order to keep the mechanism simple.

Simulations using real network topologies have been performed and show that local loops are a significant portion (>50%) of the total forwarding loops.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamekP. FrancoisddatejŒ=hfilenamegrfc8333eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130152hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZº-„granking çcrfcd8333irfcNumber çestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Micro-loop Prevention by Introducing a Local Convergence DelaydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÆThis memo updates RFCs 4252 and 4253 to define new public key algorithms for use of RSA keys with SHA-256 and SHA-512 for server and client authentication in SSH connections.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoBitvise LimiteddnamehD. Biderddatej’khfilenamegrfc8332eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130151hkeywordsálrsa-sha2-256lrsa-sha2-512gssh-rsaipublickeyoserver-sig-algsisignaturenauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateZ∞BMgranking åcrfcd8332irfcNumber åestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKUse of RSA Keys with SHA-256 and SHA-512 in the Secure Shell (SSH) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis memo describes a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) ancillary space (ANC) data, as defined by SMPTE ST 291-1.  SMPTE ANC data is generally used along with professional video formats to carry a range of ancillary data types, including time code, Closed Captioning, and the Active Format Description (AFD).fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcFOXdnamejT. Edwardsddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8331eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130150hkeywordsÖcSDIevideohcaptionshtimecodecANCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZçwgranking ãcrfcd8331irfcNumber ãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbRTP Payload for Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) ST 291-1 Ancillary DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎA network may contain links with variable discrete bandwidth, e.g., microwave and copper.  The bandwidth of such links may change discretely in response to a changing external environment.  The word "availability" is typically used to describe such links during network planning.  This document defines a new type of Generalized Switching Capability-Specific Information (SCSI) TLV to extend the Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) routing protocol.  The extension can be used for route computation in a network that contains links with variable discrete bandwidth.  Note that this document only covers the mechanisms by which the availability information is distributed.  The mechanisms by which availability information of a link is determined and the use of the distributed information for route computation are outside the scope of this document.  It is intended that technology-specific documents will reference this document to describe specific uses.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegH. Long¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnameeM. Ye¢kaffiliationcZTEdnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationtTelecom Italia S.p.AdnameoA. D'Alessandro¢kaffiliationeCienadnamegH. Shahddatej’hfilenamegrfc8330eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-130149hkeywordsÉimicrowavefcoppertGeneralized SCSI-TLVkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateZÑ‰<granking äcrfcd8330irfcNumber äestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiOSPF Traffic Engineering (OSPF-TE) Link Availability Extension for Links with Variable Discrete BandwidthdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|This document describes the framework for Interface to Network Security Functions (I2NSF) and defines a reference model (including major functional components) for I2NSF.  Network Security Functions (NSFs) are packet-processing engines that inspect and optionally modify packets traversing networks, either directly or in the context of sessions to which the packet is associated.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationrCurveball NetworksdnamehE. Lopez¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamelJ. Strassner¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehR. Kumarddatej’Jhfilenamegrfc8329eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymei2nsfdfullx/i2nsf - Interface to Network Security Functionsdnamex'Interface to Network Security Functionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130148hkeywordsÇosecurity policyssecurity capabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZèo„granking âcrfcd8329irfcNumber âestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Framework for Interface to Network Security FunctionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÑThe Simplified Use of Policy Abstractions (SUPA) policy-based management framework defines base YANG data models to encode policy.  These models point to device-, technology-, and service-specific YANG data models developed elsewhere.  Policy rules within an operator's environment can be used to express high-level, possibly network-wide, policies to a network management function (within a controller, an orchestrator, or a network element).  The network management function can then control the configuration and/or monitoring of network elements and services.  This document describes the SUPA basic framework, its elements, and interfaces.gauthorsà¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liu¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefC. Xie¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelJ. Strassner¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliation`dnamehM. Klyus¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Bi¢kaffiliationlChina UnicomdnamehY. Cheng¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Zhangddatej’?hfilenamegrfc8328eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130147hkeywordsàrInformation modelspYANG data modelsvEvent Condition Actionlpolicy rulesdGPIMeEPRIMrdeclarative policysintent-based policykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ≤ß~granking àcrfcd8328irfcNumber àestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexVPolicy-Based Management Framework for the Simplified Use of Policy Abstractions (SUPA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty`This document outlines an approach to mitigate the negative impact on networks resulting from maintenance activities.  It includes guidance for both IP networks and Internet Exchange Points (IXPs).  The approach is to ensure BGP-4 sessions that will be affected by maintenance are forcefully torn down before the actual maintenance activities commence.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeLONAPdnamekW. Hargrave¢kaffiliationc20CdnamekM. Griswold¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationdINEXdnamekN. Hilliardddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8327eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130146hkeywordsÑcBGPgcullingdEBGPhsessionskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateZü=granking ácrfcd8327irfcNumber áestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber÷etotaletitlexIMitigating the Negative Impact of Maintenance through BGP Session CullingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document standardizes a new well-known BGP community, GRACEFUL_SHUTDOWN, to signal the graceful shutdown of paths.  This document also describes operational procedures that use this well-known community to reduce the amount of traffic lost when BGP peering sessions are about to be shut down deliberately, e.g., for planned maintenance.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamekP. Francois¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationuStrasbourg UniversitydnamejC. Pelsser¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. FilsfilsddatejŒØhfilenamegrfc8326eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130145hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZü:granking Ücrfcd8326irfcNumber ÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGraceful BGP Session ShutdowndtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAs Internet traffic is increasingly sourced from and destined to wireless endpoints, it is crucial that Quality of Service (QoS) be aligned between wired and wireless networks; however, this is not always the case by default.  This document specifies a set of mappings from Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) to IEEE 802.11 User Priority (UP) to reconcile the marking recommendations offered by the IETF and the IEEE so as to maintain consistent QoS treatment between wired and IEEE 802.11 wireless networks.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejT. Szigeti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehJ. Henry¢kaffiliation`dnamehF. Bakerddatej’chfilenamegrfc8325eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130144hkeywordsårquality of servicecQoSkQoS classesgmappingdDSCPhDiffservoAccess CategorybACmUser PrioritybUPf802.11eWi-FikobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateZÉç^granking Öcrfcd8325irfcNumber ÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMapping Diffserv to IEEE 802.11dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8622d9956¥habstractyùThe basic design of the Domain Name System was completed almost 30 years ago.  The last half of that period has been characterized by significant changes in requirements and expectations, some of which either require changes to how the DNS is used or can be accommodated only poorly or not at all.  This document asks the question of whether it is time to either redesign and replace the DNS to match contemporary requirements and expectations (rather than continuing to try to design and implement incremental patches that are not fully satisfactory) or draw some clear lines about functionality that is not really needed or that should be performed in some other way.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej’Ehfilenamegrfc8324eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130143hkeywordsÉkdomain namemDNS functionsnDNS extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZï¸¨granking Ñcrfcd8324irfcNumber ÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlextDNS Privacy, Authorization, Special Uses, Encoding, Characters, Matching, and Root Structure: Time for Another Look?dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_The Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP), although inspired by HTTP, was designed to use UDP instead of TCP. The message layer of CoAP over UDP includes support for reliable delivery, simple congestion control, and flow control.

Some environments benefit from the availability of CoAP carried over reliable transports such as TCP or Transport Layer Security (TLS). This document outlines the changes required to use CoAP over TCP, TLS, and WebSockets transports. It also formally updates RFC 7641 for use with these transports and RFC 7959 to enable the use of larger messages over a reliable transport.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationrZebra TechnologiesdnamehS. Lemay¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnameiK. Hartke¢kaffiliationx Tampere University of TechnologydnamemB. Silverajan¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Raymorddatej’;hfilenamegrfc8323eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130142hkeywordsádCoAPx Constrained Application ProtocoldRESTcIoTrInternet of ThingsmNAT TraversalpCoAP in BrowserskobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateZÑ‰9granking Écrfcd8323irfcNumber ÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexECoAP (Constrained Application Protocol) over TCP, TLS, and WebSocketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8974∂habstractyThis document defines a resource-oriented approach for security automation information publication, discovery, and sharing.  Using this approach, producers may publish, share, and exchange representations of software descriptors, security incidents, attack indicators, software vulnerabilities, configuration checklists, and other security automation information as web-addressable resources.  Furthermore, consumers and other stakeholders may access and search this security information as needed, establishing a rapid and on-demand information exchange network for restricted internal use or public access repositories.  This specification extends the Atom Publishing Protocol and Atom Syndication Format to transport and share security automation resource representations.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgPivotaldnamehJ. Field¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamekS. Banghart¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamemD. Waltermireddatej’)hfilenamegrfc8322eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130141hkeywordsáksyndicationdatomxatom publishing protocolwatom syndication formatdrestsinformation sharingssecurity automationkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateZÑ‰7granking Çcrfcd8322irfcNumber ÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Resource-Oriented Lightweight Information Exchange (ROLIE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°This document describes a method to perform packet loss, delay, and jitter measurements on live traffic.  This method is based on an Alternate-Marking (coloring) technique.  A report is provided in order to explain an example and show the method applicability.  This technology can be applied in various situations, as detailed in this document, and could be considered Passive or Hybrid depending on the application.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekG. Fioccola¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejA. Capello¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekM. Cociglio¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamenL. Castaldelli¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehL. Zheng¢kaffiliationcZTEdnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahiddatej’hfilenamegrfc8321eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130140hkeywordsÉqAlternate MarkingnMarking MethodrColoring TechniquekobsoletedByÅd9341epages!opublicationDateZoæ2granking Åcrfcd8321irfcNumber ÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFAlternate-Marking Method for Passive and Hybrid Performance MonitoringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThis document specifies extensions to the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) to support the creation of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) for Maximally Redundant Trees (MRTs). A prime use of MRTs is for unicast and multicast IP/LDP Fast Reroute, which we will refer to as "MRT-FRR".

The sole protocol extension to LDP is simply the ability to advertise an MRT Capability. This document describes that extension and the associated behavior expected for Label Switching Routers (LSRs) and Label Edge Routers (LERs) advertising the MRT Capability.

MRT-FRR uses LDP multi-topology extensions, so three multi-topology IDs have been allocated from the MPLS MT-ID space.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameoK. Tiruveedhula¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiC. Bowers¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnandsddatej‘uhfilenamegrfc8320eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130139hkeywordsÉlfast-reroutecMRTgMRT-FRRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZèo‚granking Äcrfcd8320irfcNumber ÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3LDP Extensions to Support Maximally Redundant TreesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[The IPv6 Neighbor Discovery protocol specifies the maximum time allowed between sending unsolicited multicast Router Advertisements (RAs) from a router interface as well as the maximum router lifetime. It also allows the limits to be overridden by documents that are specific to the link layer. This document allows for overriding these values on a per-link basis.

This document specifies updates to the IPv6 Neighbor Discovery Protocol (RFC 4861) to increase the maximum time allowed between sending unsolicited multicast RAs from a router interface as well as to increase the maximum router lifetime.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfKaloomdnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationxNordic Semiconductor ASAdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationfZededadnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenA. YourtchenkoddatejÃ‚hfilenamegrfc8319eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130138hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZèoŸgranking crfcd8319irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Support for Adjustable Maximum Router Lifetimes per LinkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁThis specification formalizes an ad hoc practice used to provide advice to the IETF Nominating Committee (NomCom) about the operations of the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC).

This document updates RFC 7437.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnWonder HamsterdnamejS. Dawkinsddatej‘Thfilenamegrfc8318eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130137hkeywordsÇfnomcomdIAOCkobsoletedByÅd8713epages	opublicationDateZrc+granking ~crfcd8318irfcNumber ~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjIAB, IESG, and IAOC Selection, Confirmation, and Recall Process: IAOC Advisor for the Nominating CommitteedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThe MEF Forum (MEF) has defined a rooted-multipoint Ethernet service known as Ethernet-Tree (E-Tree).  A solution framework for supporting this service in MPLS networks is described in RFC 7387, "A Framework for Ethernet-Tree (E-Tree) Service over a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Network".  This document discusses how those functional requirements can be met with a solution based on RFC 7432, "BGP MPLS Based Ethernet VPN (EVPN)", with some extensions and a description of how such a solution can offer a more efficient implementation of these functions than that of RFC 7796, "Ethernet-Tree (E-Tree) Support in Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS)".  This document makes use of the most significant bit of the Tunnel Type field (in the P-Multicast Service Interface (PMSI) Tunnel attribute) governed by the IANA registry created by RFC 7385; hence, it updates RFC 7385 accordingly.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationcATTdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. RabadanddatejÃ hfilenamegrfc8317eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130136hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZrc$granking }crfcd8317irfcNumber }estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdEthernet-Tree (E-Tree) Support in Ethernet VPN (EVPN) and Provider Backbone Bridging EVPN (PBB-EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyùThis document describes an experimental use case that employs autonomic networking for the monitoring of Service Level Agreements (SLAs). The use case is for detecting violations of SLAs in a distributed fashion. It strives to optimize and dynamically adapt the autonomic deployment of active measurement probes in a way that maximizes the likelihood of detecting service-level violations with a given resource budget to perform active measurements. This optimization and adaptation should be done without any outside guidance or intervention.

This document is a product of the IRTF Network Management Research Group (NMRG). It is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx#University of Vale do Rio dos SinosdnamehJ. Nobre¢kaffiliationx'Federal University of Rio Grande do SuldnamelL. Granville¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamerA. Gonzalez Prietoddatej’hfilenamegrfc8316eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnmrgdfullxnmrg - Network ManagementdnamerNetwork Managementdtypebrgbidjdoc-130135hkeywordsÉtAutonomic NetworkingcSLAcP2PkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÉç\granking |crfcd8316irfcNumber |estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexcAutonomic Networking Use Case for Distributed Detection of Service Level Agreement (SLA) ViolationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxßThis document defines an extension to the Netnews Article Format that may be used to authenticate the withdrawal of existing articles.  This document updates RFC 5537.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnSTZ ElektronikdnamekM. Baeuerleddatej‘ehfilenamegrfc8315eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130134hkeywordsÉfUsenetgNetnewskCancel-LockkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÉçUgranking {crfcd8315irfcNumber {estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Cancel-Locks in Netnews ArticlesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This specification outlines current recommendations for the use of Transport Layer Security (TLS) to provide confidentiality of email traffic between a Mail User Agent (MUA) and a Mail Submission Server or Mail Access Server.  This document updates RFCs 1939, 2595, 3501, 5068, 6186, and 6409.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfu