ygranking zcrfcd8314irfcNumber zestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdCleartext Considered Obsolete: Use of Transport Layer Security (TLS) for Email Submission and AccessdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8997∑habstractyXThis document examines the use of Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) across inter-domain peering points for a specified set of deployment scenarios.  The objectives are to (1) describe the setup process for multicast-based delivery across administrative domains for these scenarios and (2) document supporting functionality to enable this process.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekP. Tarapore¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehR. Sayko¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekG. Shepherd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliationnSupportVectorsdnamekR. Krishnanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8313eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-130132hkeywordsïrmulticast securityxmulticast troubleshootingqmulticast routingsmulticast tunnelingcPIMgPIM-SSMcSSMxSource Specific MulticastcAMTcGRExAutomatic Multicast TunnelingcBGPdMBGPeM-BGPfMP-BGPhexchangenexchange pointcNNItcontent distributionovideo streamingganycastkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateZY<Ógranking ycrfcd8313irfcNumber yestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber’etotaletitlex3Use of Multicast across Inter-domain Peering PointsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywCUBIC is an extension to the current TCP standards.  It differs from the current TCP standards only in the congestion control algorithm on the sender side.  In particular, it uses a cubic function instead of a linear window increase function of the current TCP standards to improve scalability and stability under fast and long-distance networks.  CUBIC and its predecessor algorithm have been adopted as defaults by Linux and have been used for many years.  This document provides a specification of CUBIC to enable third-party implementations and to solicit community feedback through experimentation on the performance of CUBIC.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationxNorth Carolina State UniversitydnamegI. Rhee¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Nebraska-LincolndnameeL. Xu¢kaffiliationx!University of Colorado at BoulderdnameeS. Ha¢kaffiliation`dnamemA. Zimmermann¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamepR. ScheffeneggerddatejÀehfilenamegrfc8312eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130131hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9438epagesopublicationDateZ{¬fgranking xcrfcd8312irfcNumber xestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%CUBIC for Fast Long-Distance NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This memo updates RFC 3168, which specifies Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) as an alternative to packet drops for indicating network congestion to endpoints.  It relaxes restrictions in RFC 3168 that hinder experimentation towards benefits beyond just removal of loss.  This memo summarizes the anticipated areas of experimentation and updates RFC 3168 to enable experimentation in these areas.  An Experimental RFC in the IETF document stream is required to take advantage of any of these enabling updates.  In addition, this memo makes related updates to the ECN specifications for RTP in RFC 6679 and for the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) in RFCs 4341, 4342, and 5622.  This memo also records the conclusion of the ECN nonce experiment in RFC 3540 and provides the rationale for reclassification of RFC 3540 from Experimental to Historic; this reclassification enables new experimental use of the ECT(1) codepoint.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamehD. Blackddatej‘7hfilenamegrfc8311eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130130hkeywordsÅcECNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZY<Ègranking wcrfcd8311irfcNumber westateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexORelaxing Restrictions on Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) ExperimentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document discusses usage profiles, based on one or more authentication mechanisms, which can be used for DNS over Transport Layer Security (TLS) or Datagram TLS (DTLS).  These profiles can increase the privacy of DNS transactions compared to using only cleartext DNS.  This document also specifies new authentication mechanisms -- it describes several ways that a DNS client can use an authentication domain name to authenticate a (D)TLS connection to a DNS server.  Additionally, it defines (D)TLS protocol profiles for DNS clients and servers implementing DNS over (D)TLS.  This document updates RFC 7858.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationdACLUdnamejD. Gillmor¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamehT. Reddyddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8310eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130129hkeywordsÇcDNSitransportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ≤ßzgranking vcrfcd8310irfcNumber vestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Usage Profiles for DNS over TLS and DNS over DTLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThe IETF has produced many modules in the YANG modeling language. The majority of these modules are used to construct data models to model devices or monolithic functions.

A small number of YANG modules have been defined to model services (for example, the Layer 3 Virtual Private Network Service Model (L3SM) produced by the L3SM working group and documented in RFC 8049).

This document describes service models as used within the IETF and also shows where a service model might fit into a software-defined networking architecture. Note that service models do not make any assumption of how a service is actually engineered and delivered for a customer; details of how network protocols and devices are engineered to deliver a service are captured in other modules that are not exposed through the interface between the customer and the provider.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liu¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrelddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8309eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130128hkeywordsádYANGgNETCONFhRESTCONFjData ModelcSDNxSoftware Defined NetworktService OrchestratorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZY<Ëgranking ucrfcd8309irfcNumber uestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexService Models ExplaineddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx◊This memo updates RFCs 4251, 4252, 4253, and 4254 by defining a mechanism for Secure Shell (SSH) clients and servers to exchange information about supported protocol extensions confidentially after SSH key exchange.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoBitvise LimiteddnamehD. Biderddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8308eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130127hkeywordsçhext-infojext-info-sjext-info-cpSSH_MSG_EXT_INFOsSSH_MSG_NEWCOMPRESSoserver-sig-algsqdelay-compressionono-flow-controlielevationqdelay compressionsdelayed compressionlflow controlhelevatedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ∞BHgranking tcrfcd8308irfcNumber testateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Extension Negotiation in the Secure Shell (SSH) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9519∂habstractyJRFC 5785 defines a path prefix, "/.well-known/", that can be used by well-known URIs.  It was specifically defined for the "http" and "https" URI schemes.  The present memo formally updates RFC 6455, which defines the URI schemes defined for the WebSocket Protocol, to extend the use of these well-known URIs to those URI schemes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej”Uhfilenamegrfc8307eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130126hkeywordsácURIcWebhmetadatajwell-knowniWebSocketbwscwsskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZLÛgranking scrfcd8307irfcNumber sestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Well-Known URIs for the WebSocket ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAPoint-to-point Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) may be established using signaling techniques, but their paths may first need to be determined. The Path Computation Element (PCE) has been identified as an appropriate technology for the determination of the paths of point-to-multipoint (P2MP) TE LSPs.

This document describes extensions to the PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) to handle requests and responses for the computation of paths for P2MP TE LSPs.

This document obsoletes RFC 6006.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejR. Palleti¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. Kingddatejœûhfilenamegrfc8306eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130125hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateZ ÁVgranking rcrfcd8306irfcNumber restateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâExtensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for Point-to-Multipoint Traffic Engineering Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9353∂habstractyìMany communication protocols operating over the modern Internet use hostnames.  These often resolve to multiple IP addresses, each of which may have different performance and connectivity characteristics.  Since specific addresses or address families (IPv4 or IPv6) may be blocked, broken, or sub-optimal on a network, clients that attempt multiple connections in parallel have a chance of establishing a connection more quickly.  This document specifies requirements for algorithms that reduce this user-visible delay and provides an example algorithm, referred to as "Happy Eyeballs".  This document obsoletes the original algorithm description in RFC 6555.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekD. Schinazi¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Paulyddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8305eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130124hkeywordsÖdIPv6dIPv4cTCPcDNSeNAT64kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ;˜granking qcrfcd8305irfcNumber qestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Happy Eyeballs Version 2: Better Connectivity Using ConcurrencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This is an informational document that describes the transport protocol interface primitives provided by the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) and the Lightweight User Datagram Protocol (UDP-Lite) transport protocols.  It identifies the datagram services exposed to applications and how an application can configure and use the features offered by the Internet datagram transport service.  RFC 8303 documents the usage of transport features provided by IETF transport protocols, describing the way UDP, UDP-Lite, and other transport protocols expose their services to applications and how an application can configure and use the features that make up these services.  This document provides input to and context for that document, as well as offers a road map to documentation that may help users of the UDP and UDP-Lite protocols.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamehT. Jonesddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8304eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130123hkeywordsÅmUDP TransportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ{ˆ√granking pcrfcd8304irfcNumber pestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUTransport Features of the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) and Lightweight UDP (UDP-Lite)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document describes how the transport protocols Transmission Control Protocol (TCP), MultiPath TCP (MPTCP), Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP), User Datagram Protocol (UDP), and Lightweight User Datagram Protocol (UDP-Lite) expose services to applications and how an application can configure and use the features that make up these services.  It also discusses the service provided by the Low Extra Delay Background Transport (LEDBAT) congestion control mechanism.  The description results in a set of transport abstractions that can be exported in a transport services (TAPS) API.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationx'Muenster University of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamejN. Khademiddatejœ°hfilenamegrfc8303eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130122hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateZ{ˆ¡granking ocrfcd8303irfcNumber oestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGOn the Usage of Transport Features Provided by IETF Transport ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document describes mechanisms to optimize the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) and Neighbor Discovery (ND) traffic in a Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) campus.  TRILL switches maintain a cache of IP / Media Access Control (MAC) address / Data Label bindings that are learned from ARP/ND requests and responses that pass through them.  In many cases, this cache allows an edge Routing Bridge (RBridge) to avoid flooding an ARP/ND request by either responding to it directly or encapsulating it and unicasting it.  Such optimization reduces packet flooding over a TRILL campus.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Umairddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8302eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130121hkeywordsÜeproxydRARPqduplicate addresscDADdDHCPhfloodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZPUgranking ncrfcd8302irfcNumber nestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): ARP and Neighbor Discovery (ND) OptimizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4The cryptographic algorithm and key size requirements included when DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) was designed a decade ago are functionally obsolete and in need of immediate revision.  This document updates DKIM requirements to those minimally suitable for operation with currently specified algorithms.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxKitterman Technical ServicesdnamelS. Kittermanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8301eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedcrupdfullxdcrup - DKIM Crypto UpdatednamerDKIM Crypto Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130120hkeywordsÇeemailnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZbé˘granking mcrfcd8301irfcNumber mestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQCryptographic Algorithm and Key Usage Update to DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document describes a Network Service Header (NSH) imposed on packets or frames to realize Service Function Paths (SFPs).  The NSH also provides a mechanism for metadata exchange along the instantiated service paths.  The NSH is the Service Function Chaining (SFC) encapsulation required to support the SFC architecture (defined in RFC 7665).fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Quinn¢kaffiliationeInteldnamehU. Elzur¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataroddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8300eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130119hkeywordsÖxService Function ChainingvNetwork Service HeadercSFCcNSHxNetwork Service FunctionkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateZY<Êgranking lcrfcd8300irfcNumber lestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNetwork Service Header (NSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9451∂habstractyThis document defines a YANG data model that can be used for communication between customers and network operators and to deliver a Layer 3 provider-provisioned VPN service. This document is limited to BGP PE-based VPNs as described in RFCs 4026, 4110, and 4364. This model is intended to be instantiated at the management system to deliver the overall service. It is not a configuration model to be used directly on network elements. This model provides an abstracted view of the Layer 3 IP VPN service configuration components. It will be up to the management system to take this model as input and use specific configuration models to configure the different network elements to deliver the service. How the configuration of network elements is done is out of scope for this document.

This document obsoletes RFC 8049; it replaces the unimplementable module in that RFC with a new module with the same name that is not backward compatible. The changes are a series of small fixes to the YANG module and some clarifications to the text.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamekL. Tomotaki¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnamehK. Ogakiddatejœúhfilenamegrfc8299eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130118hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesºopublicationDateZbé˜granking kcrfcd8299irfcNumber kestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*YANG Data Model for L3VPN Service DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis memo describes a rate adaptation algorithm for conversational media services such as interactive video.  The solution conforms to the packet conservation principle and uses a hybrid loss-and-delay- based congestion control algorithm.  The algorithm is evaluated over both simulated Internet bottleneck scenarios as well as in a Long Term Evolution (LTE) system simulator and is shown to achieve both low latency and high video throughput in these scenarios.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelI. Johansson¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnameiZ. Sarkerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8298eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymermcatdfullx1rmcat - RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdnamex)RTP Media Congestion Avoidance Techniquesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130117hkeywordsÉpCellular NetworkrCongestion ControlcRTPkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateZ;˜granking jcrfcd8298irfcNumber jestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Self-Clocked Rate Adaptation for MultimediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxüThis memo introduces an informational HTTP status code that can be used to convey hints that help a client make preparations for processing the final response.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamefK. Okuddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8297eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130116hkeywordsÇdpushgpreloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ9Ï⁄granking icrfcd8297irfcNumber iestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(An HTTP Status Code for Indicating HintsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) is an architecture that provides optimal multicast forwarding through a "multicast domain", without requiring intermediate routers to maintain any per-flow state or to engage in an explicit tree-building protocol.  When a multicast data packet enters the domain, the ingress router determines the set of egress routers to which the packet needs to be sent.  The ingress router then encapsulates the packet in a BIER header.  The BIER header contains a bit string in which each bit represents exactly one egress router in the domain; to forward the packet to a given set of egress routers, the bits corresponding to those routers are set in the BIER header.  The details of the encapsulation depend on the type of network used to realize the multicast domain.  This document specifies a BIER encapsulation that can be used in an MPLS network or, with slight differences, in a non-MPLS network.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekA. Dolganow¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnameiI. Meilikddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8296eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130115hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZY<›granking hcrfcd8296irfcNumber hestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUEncapsulation for Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER) in MPLS and Non-MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The EST (Enrollment over Secure Transport) protocol defines the Well-Known URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) -- /.well-known/est -- along with a number of other path components that clients use for PKI (Public Key Infrastructure) services, namely certificate enrollment (e.g., /simpleenroll).  This document defines a number of other PKI services as additional path components -- specifically, firmware and trust anchors as well as symmetric, asymmetric, and encrypted keys.  This document also specifies the PAL (Package Availability List), which is an XML (Extensible Markup Language) file or JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) object that clients use to retrieve packages available and authorized for them.  This document extends the EST server path components to provide these additional services.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8295eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130114hkeywordsÖhFirmwaredTAMPoAsymmetric KeysnSymmetric KeysxProduct Availability ListkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateZr
vgranking gcrfcd8295irfcNumber gestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1EST (Enrollment over Secure Transport) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxƒThis document defines a collection of common data types using the YANG data modeling language.  These derived common types are designed to be imported by other modules defined in the routing area.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeJabildnamefX. Liu¢kaffiliationxFuturewei Technologies Inc.dnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamehC. Hopps¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Bergerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8294eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130113hkeywordsÉrNetwork ManagementgRoutingdYANGkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateZ2ÌÍgranking fcrfcd8294irfcNumber festateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Common YANG Data Types for the Routing AreadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document provides a framework for supporting multicast traffic in a network that uses Network Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3).  Both infrastructure multicast and application-specific multicast are discussed.  It describes the various mechanisms that can be used for delivering such traffic as well as the data plane and control plane considerations for each of the mechanisms.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejM. McBride¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiV. Bannai¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekR. Krishnanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8293eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-130112hkeywordsÑdNVO3eVXLANfGeneveeNVGREkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZLÒgranking ecrfcd8293irfcNumber eestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@A Framework for Multicast in Network Virtualization over Layer 3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìAn application server can use the Voluntary Application Server Identification (VAPID) method described in this document to voluntarily identify itself to a push service.  The "vapid" authentication scheme allows a client to include its identity in a signed token with requests that it makes.  The signature can be used by the push service to attribute requests that are made by the same application server to a single entity.  The identification information can allow the operator of a push service to contact the operator of the application server.  The signature can be used to restrict the use of a push message subscription to a single application server.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekP. Beverlooddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8292eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgwebpushdfullx&webpush - Web-Based Push NotificationsdnamexWeb-Based Push Notificationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130111hkeywordsÑnauthenticationjrestrictedkrestrictionisignaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÅgranking dcrfcd8292irfcNumber destateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Voluntary Application Server Identification (VAPID) for Web PushdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¿This document describes a message encryption scheme for the Web Push protocol.  This scheme provides confidentiality and integrity for messages sent from an application server to a user agent.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8291eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgwebpushdfullx&webpush - Web-Based Push NotificationsdnamexWeb-Based Push Notificationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130110hkeywordsÖcwebdpushlnotificationdhttpjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ~granking ccrfcd8291irfcNumber cestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMessage Encryption for Web PushdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis memo presents the FQ-CoDel hybrid packet scheduler and Active Queue Management (AQM) algorithm, a powerful tool for fighting bufferbloat and reducing latency.

FQ-CoDel mixes packets from multiple flows and reduces the impact of head-of-line blocking from bursty traffic. It provides isolation for low-rate traffic such as DNS, web, and videoconferencing traffic. It improves utilisation across the networking fabric, especially for bidirectional traffic, by keeping queue lengths short, and it can be implemented in a memory- and CPU-efficient fashion across a wide range of hardware.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnamevT. Hoeiland-Joergensen¢kaffiliationxIBM Linux Technology CenterdnamekP. McKenney¢kaffiliationhTeklibrednamegD. Taht¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Gettys¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejE. Dumazetddatej’…hfilenamegrfc8290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130109hkeywordsÑkbufferbloatcaqmhfq_codelhfq-codelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZPTgranking bcrfcd8290irfcNumber bestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKThe Flow Queue CoDel Packet Scheduler and Active Queue Management AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes CoDel (Controlled Delay) -- a general framework that controls bufferbloat-generated excess delay in modern networking environments.  CoDel consists of an estimator, a setpoint, and a control loop.  It requires no configuration in normal Internet deployments.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmPollere, Inc.dnamejK. Nichols¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekV. Jacobson¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekA. McGregor¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Iyengarddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130108hkeywordsÉeCoDelcAQMwActive Queue ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZPQgranking acrfcd8289irfcNumber aestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Controlled Delay Active Queue ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˘This specification defines a model for the relationships between resources on the Web ("links") and the type of those relationships ("link relation types").

It also defines the serialisation of such links in HTTP headers with the Link header field.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8288eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130107hkeywordsÅmlink relationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÔÃtgranking `crfcd8288irfcNumber `estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlekWeb LinkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøA Segment Routing (SR) architecture leverages source routing and tunneling paradigms and can be directly applied to the use of a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) data plane. A node steers a packet through a controlled set of instructions called "segments" by prepending the packet with an SR header.

The segment assignment and forwarding semantic nature of SR raises additional considerations for connectivity verification and fault isolation for a Label Switched Path (LSP) within an SR architecture. This document illustrates the problem and defines extensions to perform LSP Ping and Traceroute for Segment Routing IGP-Prefix and IGP-Adjacency Segment Identifiers (SIDs) with an MPLS data plane.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehN. Kumar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationxSouthend Technical CenterdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamegS. Kini¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8287eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130106hkeywordsÖdMPLShLSP PingfSPRINGoSegment RoutingbSRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ;ˆ˛granking _crfcd8287irfcNumber _estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexêLabel Switched Path (LSP) Ping/Traceroute for Segment Routing (SR) IGP-Prefix and IGP-Adjacency Segment Identifiers (SIDs) with MPLS Data PlanesdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8690d9214∂habstracty.Content splicing is a process that replaces the content of a main multimedia stream with other multimedia content and that delivers the substitutive multimedia content to the receivers for a period of time. The splicer is designed to handle RTP splicing and needs to know when to start and end the splicing.

This memo defines two RTP/RTCP extensions to indicate the splicing-related information to the splicer: an RTP header extension that conveys the information "in band" and an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) packet that conveys the information out of band.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. Xia¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. DengddatejœIhfilenamegrfc8286eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130105hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÒ+?granking ^crfcd8286irfcNumber ^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0RTP/RTCP Extension for RTP Splicing NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This document provides a general mechanism to use the header extension feature of RTP (the Real-time Transport Protocol).  It provides the option to use a small number of small extensions in each RTP packet, where the universe of possible extensions is large and registration is decentralized.  The actual extensions in use in a session are signaled in the setup information for that session.  This document obsoletes RFC 5285.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiD. Singer¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekH. Desineni¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. Evenddatejœãhfilenamegrfc8285eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130104hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÒ+=granking ]crfcd8285irfcNumber ]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-A General Mechanism for RTP Header ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¥The Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) identifies users by use of Jabber IDs (JIDs).  The Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) enables provision of a white pages service with a schema relating to users and support for Internet protocols.  This specification defines a schema to enable XMPP JIDs to be associated with objects in an LDAP directory so that this information can be used with white pages applications.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamehS. Killeddatejœûhfilenamegrfc8284eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130103hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ ÁUgranking \crfcd8284irfcNumber \estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexáLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Schema for Supporting the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) in White PagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThe Path Computation Element (PCE) is a core component of Software- Defined Networking (SDN) systems. It can compute optimal paths for traffic across a network and can also update the paths to reflect changes in the network or traffic demands.

PCE was developed to derive paths for MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs), which are supplied to the head end of the LSP using the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP).

SDN has a broader applicability than signaled MPLS traffic-engineered (TE) networks, and the PCE may be used to determine paths in a range of use cases including static LSPs, segment routing, Service Function Chaining (SFC), and most forms of a routed or switched network. It is, therefore, reasonable to consider PCEP as a control protocol for use in these environments to allow the PCE to be fully enabled as a central controller.

This document briefly introduces the architecture for PCE as a central controller, examines the motivations and applicability for PCEP as a control protocol in this environment, and introduces the implications for the protocol. A PCE-based central controller can simplify the processing of a distributed control plane by blending it with elements of SDN and without necessarily completely replacing it.

This document does not describe use cases in detail and does not define protocol extensions: that work is left for other documents.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegC. Zhouddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8283eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130102hkeywordsÇcPCEcSDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ<H√granking [crfcd8283irfcNumber [estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjAn Architecture for Use of PCE and the PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) in a Network with Central ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑The Path Computation Element (PCE) provides path computation functions in support of traffic engineering in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks.

MPLS and GMPLS networks may be constructed from layered service networks. It is advantageous for overall network efficiency to provide end-to-end traffic engineering across multiple network layers through a process called inter-layer traffic engineering. PCE is a candidate solution for such requirements.

The PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) is designed as a communication protocol between Path Computation Clients (PCCs) and PCEs. This document presents PCEP extensions for inter-layer traffic engineering.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpKyoto UniversitydnamefE. Oki¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takeda¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamehF. Zhangddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8282eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130101hkeywordsÑkMulti-layerlMulti-domainlInter-domainsTraffic EngineeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ<H¿granking Zcrfcd8282irfcNumber ZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Extensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for Inter-Layer MPLS and GMPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) provides mechanisms for Path Computation Elements (PCEs) to perform path computations in response to Path Computation Client (PCC) requests.

The extensions for stateful PCE provide active control of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) via PCEP, for a model where the PCC delegates control over one or more locally configured LSPs to the PCE. This document describes the creation and deletion of PCE-initiated LSPs under the stateful PCE model.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnameiE. Crabbe¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationxPantheon Technologies SROdnamehR. Vargaddatejœöhfilenamegrfc8281eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130100hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ<HΩgranking Ycrfcd8281irfcNumber YestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for PCE-Initiated LSP Setup in a Stateful PCE ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756¥habstracty⁄This document aims to propose guidelines for human rights considerations, similar to the work done on the guidelines for privacy considerations (RFC 6973). The other parts of this document explain the background of the guidelines and how they were developed.

This document is the first milestone in a longer-term research effort. It has been reviewed by the Human Rights Protocol Considerations (HRPC) Research Group and also by individuals from outside the research group.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjARTICLE 19dnamelN. ten Oever¢kaffiliationxOxford Internet InstitutednamegC. Cathddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8280eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdhrpcdfullx+hrpc - Human Rights Protocol Considerationsdnamex$Human Rights Protocol Considerationsdtypebrgbidjdoc-130099hkeywordsÜlhuman rightsdIETFiprotocolsjguidelinesnconsiderationsufreedom of expressionkobsoletedByÄepagesQopublicationDateY˘SŒgranking Xcrfcd8280irfcNumber XestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex2Research into Human Rights Protocol ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9620∂habstracty+This document specifies a new architecture for the forwarding of multicast data packets.  It provides optimal forwarding of multicast packets through a "multicast domain".  However, it does not require a protocol for explicitly building multicast distribution trees, nor does it require intermediate nodes to maintain any per-flow state.  This architecture is known as "Bit Index Explicit Replication" (BIER).  When a multicast data packet enters the domain, the ingress router determines the set of egress routers to which the packet needs to be sent.  The ingress router then encapsulates the packet in a BIER header.  The BIER header contains a bit string in which each bit represents exactly one egress router in the domain; to forward the packet to a given set of egress routers, the bits corresponding to those routers are set in the BIER header.  The procedures for forwarding a packet based on its BIER header are specified in this document.  Elimination of the per-flow state and the explicit tree-building protocols results in a considerable simplification.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekA. Dolganow¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8279eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbierdfullx'bier - Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdnamex Bit Indexed Explicit Replicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130098hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateZ=ıgranking Wcrfcd8279irfcNumber WestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Multicast Using Bit Index Explicit Replication (BIER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification defines extensions to the Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) protocol that allow a mobile access gateway (MAG) to register more than one proxy care-of address (pCoA) with the local mobility anchor (LMA) and to simultaneously establish multiple IP tunnels with the LMA.  This capability allows the MAG to utilize all the available access networks to route the mobile node's IP traffic.  This document defines the following two new mobility header options: the MAG Multipath Binding option and the MAG Identifier option.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehP. Seite¢kaffiliationhActilitydnamehA. Yegin¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelliddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8278eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130097hkeywordsÉxProxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6)kmultihominguMultiple WAN accesseskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZh(®granking Vcrfcd8278irfcNumber VestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Mobile Access Gateway (MAG) Multipath OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a set of procedures for using BGP to advertise that a specified router has bound a specified MPLS label (or a specified sequence of MPLS labels organized as a contiguous part of a label stack) to a specified address prefix.  This can be done by sending a BGP UPDATE message whose Network Layer Reachability Information field contains both the prefix and the MPLS label(s) and whose Next Hop field identifies the node at which said prefix is bound to said label(s).  This document obsoletes RFC 3107.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8277eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130096hkeywordsálasynchronoushtransferdmodecAALfsyntaxhadaptionelayerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÈá3granking Ucrfcd8277irfcNumber UestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Using BGP to Bind MPLS Labels to Address PrefixesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThis document describes an optional feature extending the NFSv4 protocol.  This feature allows extended attributes (hereinafter also referred to as xattrs) to be interrogated and manipulated using NFSv4 clients.  Xattrs are provided by a file system to associate opaque metadata, not interpreted by the file system, with files and directories.  Such support is present in many modern local file systems.  New file attributes are provided to allow clients to query the server for xattr support, with that support consisting of new operations to get and set xattrs on file system objects.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNutanixdnamegM. Naik¢kaffiliationkIBM AlmadendnamehM. Eshelddatejœïhfilenamegrfc8276eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130095hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ%≤Úgranking Tcrfcd8276irfcNumber TestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(File System Extended Attributes in NFSv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®In many environments, inheritable NFSv4 Access Control Entries (ACEs) can be rendered ineffective by the application of the per-process file mode creation mask (umask).  This can be addressed by transmitting the umask and create mode as separate pieces of data, allowing the server to make more intelligent decisions about the permissions to set on new files.  This document proposes a protocol extension to accomplish that.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnameiJ. Fields¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamenA. Gruenbacherddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8275eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130094hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ%≤granking Scrfcd8275irfcNumber SestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGAllowing Inheritable NFSv4 Access Control Entries to Override the UmaskdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$The Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) v2 (RFC7970) defines a data representation that provides a framework for sharing information about computer security incidents commonly exchanged by Computer Security Incident Response Teams (CSIRTs) .  Since the IODEF model includes a wealth of available options that can be used to describe a security incident or issue, it can be challenging for security practitioners to develop tools that leverage IODEF for incident sharing.  This document provides guidelines for IODEF implementers.  It addresses how common security indicators can be represented in IODEF and use-cases of how IODEF is being used.  This document aims to make IODEF's adoption by vendors easier and encourage faster and wider adoption of the model by CSIRTs around the world.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemP. Kampanakis¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameiM. Suzukiddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8274eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-130093hkeywordsÑtIODEF best practicesx$IODEF implementation recommendationsnIODEF examplesxIODEF practical recommendationskobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateZ=Ôgranking Rcrfcd8274irfcNumber RestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Incident Object Description Exchange Format Usage GuidancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis document outlines an approach utilizing existing IPv6 protocols to allow hosts to be assigned a unique IPv6 prefix (instead of a unique IPv6 address from a shared IPv6 prefix).  Benefits of using a unique IPv6 prefix over a unique service-provider IPv6 address include improved host isolation and enhanced subscriber management on shared network segments.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednamemJ. Brzozowski¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoG. Van de Veldeddatejœõhfilenamegrfc8273eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130092hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateZ%⁄ægranking Qcrfcd8273irfcNumber QestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUnique IPv6 Prefix per HostdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document specifies the TinyIPFIX protocol that is used for transmitting smart-metering data in constrained networks such as IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPAN, RFC 4944).  TinyIPFIX is derived from IP Flow Information Export (RFC 7011) and adopted to the needs of constrained networks.  This document specifies how the TinyIPFIX Data and Template Records are transmitted in constrained networks such as 6LoWPAN and how TinyIPFIX data can be converted into data that is not TinyIPFIX in a proxy device.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of ZurichdnamejC. Schmitt¢kaffiliationtUniversity of ZurichdnamejB. Stiller¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammellddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8272eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130091hkeywordsÉiTinyIPFIXlSmart MeterstConstrained NetworkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ
ggranking Pcrfcd8272irfcNumber PestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex2TinyIPFIX for Smart Meters in Constrained NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document updates the Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Fast Reroute (FRR) procedures defined in RFC 4090 to support Packet Switch Capable (PSC) Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  These updates allow the coordination of a bidirectional bypass tunnel assignment protecting a common facility in both forward and reverse directions of a co-routed bidirectional LSP.  In addition, these updates enable the redirection of bidirectional traffic onto bypass tunnels that ensure the co-routing of data paths in the forward and reverse directions after FRR and avoid RSVP soft-state timeout in the control plane.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejM. Taillon¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiM. Bhatiaddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8271eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130090hkeywordsÉnCo-routed LSPsxBypass assignment coordinaterRestore co-routingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY˘SÕgranking Ocrfcd8271irfcNumber OestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvUpdates to the Resource Reservation Protocol for Fast Reroute of Traffic Engineering GMPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝The Diffie-Hellman (DH) Group Exchange for the Secure Shell (SSH) transport-layer protocol specifies that servers and clients should support groups with a minimum modulus group size of 1024 bits.  Recent security research has shown that the minimum value of 1024 bits is insufficient to protect against state-sponsored actors and any organization with enough computing resources.  This RFC updates RFC 4419, which allowed for DH moduli less than 2048 bits; now, 2048 bits is the minimum acceptable group size.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjHackers.mudnamemL. Velvindron¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejM. Baushkeddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130089hkeywordsÇcSSHbDHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ'9‘granking Ncrfcd8270irfcNumber NestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVIncrease the Secure Shell Minimum Recommended Diffie-Hellman Modulus Size to 2048 BitsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document defines the use of the ARIA block cipher algorithm within the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP).  It details two modes of operation (CTR and GCM) and the SRTP key derivation functions for ARIA.  Additionally, this document defines DTLS-SRTP protection profiles and Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) parameter sets for use with ARIA.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx$National Security Research InstitutednamefW. Kim¢kaffiliationx$National Security Research InstitutednamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationx$National Security Research InstitutednamegJ. Park¢kaffiliationx$National Security Research InstitutednamegD. Kwon¢kaffiliationrKookmin UniversitydnamefD. Kimddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130088hkeywordsÑdARIAdSRTPiDTLS-SRTPeMIKEYkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY‰¸?granking Mcrfcd8269irfcNumber MestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRThe ARIA Algorithm and Its Use with the Secure Real-Time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˝This document defines added Modular Exponentiation (MODP) groups for the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol using SHA-2 hashes.  This document updates RFC 4250.  This document updates RFC 4253 by correcting an error regarding checking the Peer's DH Public Key.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejM. Baushkeddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8268eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-130087hkeywordsåjPublic KeykPrivate Keyggroup14ggroup15ggroup16ggroup17hgroupt18h2048-bith3072-bith4096-bith6144-bith8192-bitkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ1ñgranking Lcrfcd8268irfcNumber LestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgMore Modular Exponentiation (MODP) Diffie-Hellman (DH) Key Exchange (KEX) Groups for Secure Shell (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx This document specifies Upper-Layer Bindings of Network File System (NFS) protocol versions to RPC-over-RDMA version 1, thus enabling the use of Direct Data Placement.  This document obsoletes RFC 5667.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehC. Leverddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8267eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-130086hkeywordsÅmNFS-over-RDMAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÒ+=granking Kcrfcd8267irfcNumber KestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHNetwork File System (NFS) Upper-Layer Binding to RPC-over-RDMA Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document describes methods for handling Unicode strings representing memorable, human-friendly names (called "nicknames", "display names", or "petnames") for people, devices, accounts, websites, and other entities.  This document obsoletes RFC 7700.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjJabber.orgdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8266eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130085hkeywordsáhnicknamecSIPfSIMPLEdXMPPdMSRPdXCONichatroomskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY÷úgranking Jcrfcd8266irfcNumber JestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Preparation, Enforcement, and Comparison of Internationalized Strings Representing NicknamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document describes updated methods for handling Unicode strings representing usernames and passwords.  The previous approach was known as SASLprep (RFC 4013) and was based on Stringprep (RFC 3454).  The methods specified in this document provide a more sustainable approach to the handling of internationalized usernames and passwords.  This document obsoletes RFC 7613.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjJabber.orgdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8265eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130084hkeywordsáhUsernamehPasswordgUnicodetInternationalizationdi18nnAuthenticationhSASLprepkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY÷úgranking Icrfcd8265irfcNumber IestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjPreparation, Enforcement, and Comparison of Internationalized Strings Representing Usernames and PasswordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8Application protocols using Unicode code points in protocol strings need to properly handle such strings in order to enforce internationalization rules for strings placed in various protocol slots (such as addresses and identifiers) and to perform valid comparison operations (e.g., for purposes of authentication or authorization).  This document defines a framework enabling application protocols to perform the preparation, enforcement, and comparison of internationalized strings ("PRECIS") in a way that depends on the properties of Unicode code points and thus is more agile with respect to versions of Unicode.  As a result, this framework provides a more sustainable approach to the handling of internationalized strings than the previous framework, known as Stringprep (RFC 3454).  This document obsoletes RFC 7564.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjJabber.orgdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8264eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130083hkeywordsÉtinternationalizationdi18njStringprepkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateY÷úgranking Hcrfcd8264irfcNumber HestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpPRECIS Framework: Preparation, Enforcement, and Comparison of Internationalized Strings in Application ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúThe Group Domain of Interpretation (GDOI) includes the ability of a Group Controller/Key Server (GCKS) to provide a set of current Group Member (GM) devices with additional security associations (e.g., to rekey expiring security associations).  This memo adds the ability of a GCKS to request that the GM devices return an acknowledgement of receipt of its rekey message and specifies the acknowledgement method.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Weis¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Inc.dnameiU. Mangla¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegT. Karl¢kaffiliation`dnamemN. Maheshwariddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8263eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130082hkeywordsÅrmulticast securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ ÁRgranking Gcrfcd8263irfcNumber GestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKGroup Domain of Interpretation (GDOI) GROUPKEY-PUSH Acknowledgement MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„This document specifies the Content-ID header field for usage in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP). This document also updates RFC 5621, which only allows a Content-ID URL to reference a body part that is part of a multipart message-body. This update enables a Content-ID URL to reference a complete message-body and metadata provided by some additional SIP header fields.

This document updates RFC 5368 and RFC 6442 by clarifying their usage of the SIP Content-ID header field.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekI. Sedlacekddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8262eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130081hkeywordsÅcSIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÒ+5granking Fcrfcd8262irfcNumber FestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Content-ID Header Field in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) is a transport protocol originally defined to run on top of the network protocols IPv4 or IPv6.  This document specifies how SCTP can be used on top of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol.  Using the encapsulation method described in this document, SCTP is unaware of the protocols being used below DTLS; hence, explicit IP addresses cannot be used in the SCTP control chunks.  As a consequence, the SCTP associations carried over DTLS can only be single-homed.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationmNetflix, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationxWorldGate CommunicationsdnamehR. Jesup¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. LoretoddatejŒ»hfilenamegrfc8261eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130080hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateZ;granking Ecrfcd8261irfcNumber EestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFDatagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Encapsulation of SCTP PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8996d8899∂habstractyºThe Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) is a message-oriented transport protocol supporting arbitrarily large user messages. This document adds a new chunk to SCTP for carrying payload data. This allows a sender to interleave different user messages that would otherwise result in head-of-line blocking at the sender. The interleaving of user messages is required for WebRTC data channels.

Whenever an SCTP sender is allowed to send user data, it may choose from multiple outgoing SCTP streams. Multiple ways for performing this selection, called stream schedulers, are defined in this document. A stream scheduler can choose to either implement, or not implement, user message interleaving.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmNetflix, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loreto¢kaffiliationx#Metafinanz Informationssysteme GmbHdnamemR. Seggelmannddatejœòhfilenamegrfc8260eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-130079hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ;zgranking Dcrfcd8260irfcNumber DestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Stream Schedulers and User Message Interleaving for the Stream Control Transmission ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyØJavaScript Object Notation (JSON) is a lightweight, text-based, language-independent data interchange format. It was derived from the ECMAScript Programming Language Standard. JSON defines a small set of formatting rules for the portable representation of structured data.

This document removes inconsistencies with other specifications of JSON, repairs specification errors, and offers experience-based interoperability guidance.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjTextualitydnamegT. Brayddatejœêhfilenamegrfc8259eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgjsonbisdfullx+jsonbis - Javascript Object Notation Updatednamex!Javascript Object Notation Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-130078hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZ1|¿granking Ccrfcd8259irfcNumber CestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberZetotaletitlex=The JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Data Interchange FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrThis document defines a generic information structure for information carried in routing protocol Interface Switching Capability Descriptor (ISCD) Switching Capability Specific Information (SCSI) fields.  This "Generalized SCSI" can be used with routing protocols that define GMPLS ISCDs and any specific technology.  This document does not modify any existing technology-specific formats and is defined for use in conjunction with new GMPLS Switching Capability types.  The context for this document is Generalized MPLS, and the reader is expected to be familiar with the GMPLS architecture and associated protocol standards.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationoLabN ConsultingdnameiL. Bergerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8258eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130077hkeywordsÇgOSPF-TEeGMPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÊíEgranking Bcrfcd8258irfcNumber BestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexãGeneralized SCSI: A Generic Structure for Interface Switching Capability Descriptor (ISCD) Switching Capability Specific Information (SCSI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡This Informational RFC describes Data Center TCP (DCTCP): a TCP congestion control scheme for data-center traffic.  DCTCP extends the Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) processing to estimate the fraction of bytes that encounter congestion rather than simply detecting that some congestion has occurred.  DCTCP then scales the TCP congestion window based on this estimate.  This method achieves high-burst tolerance, low latency, and high throughput with shallow- buffered switches.  This memo also discusses deployment issues related to the coexistence of DCTCP and conventional TCP, discusses the lack of a negotiating mechanism between sender and receiver, and presents some possible mitigations.  This memo documents DCTCP as currently implemented by several major operating systems.  DCTCP, as described in this specification, is applicable to deployments in controlled environments like data centers, but it must not be deployed over the public Internet without additional measures.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejS. Bensley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamerP. Balasubramanian¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationnMorgan StanleydnamegG. Juddddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8257eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130076hkeywordsÑcTCPcECNeDCTCPrcongestion controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÊí?granking Acrfcd8257irfcNumber AestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Data Center TCP (DCTCP): TCP Congestion Control for Data CentersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOne of the most important Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) capabilities for transport-network operation is fault localization.  An in-service, on-demand path segment monitoring function of a transport path is indispensable, particularly when the service monitoring function is activated only between endpoints.  However, the current segment monitoring approach defined for MPLS (including the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP)) in RFC 6371 "Operations, Administration, and Maintenance Framework for MPLS-Based Transport Networks" has drawbacks.  This document provides an analysis of the existing MPLS-TP OAM mechanisms for the path segment monitoring and provides requirements to guide the development of new OAM tools to support Hitless Path Segment Monitoring (HPSM).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnameoA. D'Alessandro¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamegS. Ueno¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegK. Arai¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehY. Koikeddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8256eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130075hkeywordsàdHPSMdMPLSvMPLS Transport Profilegmpls-tpcOAMjmonitoringxHitless Path Segment MonitoringwPath Segment MonitoringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÚX†granking @crfcd8256irfcNumber @estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Requirements for Hitless MPLS Path Segment MonitoringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document defines the 'multipart/multilingual' content type, which is an addition to the Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) standard.  This content type makes it possible to send one message that contains multiple language versions of the same information.  The translations would be identified by a language tag and selected by the email client based on a user's language settings.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnMimecast, Ltd.dnamelN. Tomkinson¢kaffiliationnMimecast, Ltd.dnamemN. Borensteinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8255eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdslimdfullx/slim - Selection of Language for Internet Mediadnamex(Selection of Language for Internet Mediadtypebwgbidjdoc-130074hkeywordsàhmultiplehlanguageemultiglingualgcontentdtypeeemaildmimekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY›%Tgranking ?crfcd8255irfcNumber ?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMultiple Language Content TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThe original registration procedure for formal Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespaces required IETF Consensus.  That requirement discouraged some registrations and increased the risk for problems that could occur as a result.  The requirements have now been changed by RFC 8141, which adopts a different model, focusing on encouraging registration and publication of information for all appropriate namespaces.  This document clarifies the status of relevant older RFCs and confirms and documents advice to IANA about selected existing registrations.  This document also obsoletes RFCs 3044 and 3187 and moves them to Historic status.  These RFCs describe the ISSN and ISBN namespaces, which are now outdated because the descriptions reside in registration templates.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationxThe National Library of FinlanddnameiJ. Hakaladdatej÷hfilenamegrfc8254eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfurnbisdfullx(urnbis - Uniform Resource Names, ReviseddnamexUniform Resource Names, Reviseddtypebwgbidjdoc-130073hkeywordsÑdISBNdISSNcNBNxnational bibliography numberkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateYÍã(granking >crfcd8254irfcNumber >estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace Registration TransitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) defines the mechanisms for the communication between a Path Computation Client (PCC) and a Path Computation Element (PCE), or among PCEs. This document describes PCEPS -- the usage of Transport Layer Security (TLS) to provide a secure transport for PCEP. The additional security mechanisms are provided by the transport protocol supporting PCEP; therefore, they do not affect the flexibility and extensibility of PCEP.

This document updates RFC 5440 in regards to the PCEP initialization phase procedures.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhodyddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8253eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130072hkeywordsácPCEdPCEPePCEPShsecuritynauthenticationjencryptioncTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÍã'granking =crfcd8253irfcNumber =estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpPCEPS: Usage of TLS to Provide a Secure Transport for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty%OAuth 2.0 authorization requests from native apps should only be made through external user-agents, primarily the user's browser.  This specification details the security and usability reasons why this is the case and how native apps and authorization servers can implement this best practice.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejW. Denniss¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradleyddatejœëhfilenamegrfc8252eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-130071hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY’‡granking <crfcd8252irfcNumber <estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber‘etotaletitlexOAuth 2.0 for Native AppsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document addresses minor issues that were found in the specification of the Opus audio codec in RFC 6716.  It updates the normative decoder implementation included in Appendix A of RFC 6716.  The changes fix real and potential security-related issues, as well as minor quality-related issues.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsMozilla CorporationdnameiJM. Valin¢kaffiliationgvocTonednamefK. Vosddatejœïhfilenamegrfc8251eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecodecdfullx%codec - Internet Wideband Audio CodecdnamexInternet Wideband Audio Codecdtypebwgbidjdoc-130070hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÍã%granking ;crfcd8251irfcNumber ;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUpdates to the Opus Audio CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫To assess performance problems, this document describes optional headers embedded in each packet that provide sequence numbers and timing information as a basis for measurements.  Such measurements may be interpreted in real time or after the fact.  This document specifies the Performance and Diagnostic Metrics (PDM) Destination Options header.  The field limits, calculations, and usage in measurement of PDM are included in this document.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoInside ProductsdnameiN. Elkins¢kaffiliationxChemical Abstracts ServicednamekR. Hamilton¢kaffiliationmBCBS MichigandnamelM. Ackermannddatejœéhfilenamegrfc8250eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130069hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY–<granking :crfcd8250irfcNumber :estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@IPv6 Performance and Diagnostic Metrics (PDM) Destination OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThe base IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol has a TRILL campus-wide MTU feature, specified in RFCs 6325 and 7177, that assures that link-state changes can be successfully flooded throughout the campus while being able to take advantage of a campus-wide capability to support jumbo packets.  This document specifies recommended updates to that MTU feature to take advantage, for appropriate link-local packets, of link-local MTUs that exceed the TRILL campus MTU.  In addition, it specifies an efficient algorithm for local MTU testing.  This document updates RFCs 6325, 7177, and 7780.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Chatterjeeddatejœíhfilenamegrfc8249eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130068hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY–9granking 9crfcd8249irfcNumber 9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexETransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): MTU NegotiationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document defines the scope and set of requirements for the Security Automation and Continuous Monitoring (SACM) architecture, data model, and transfer protocols.  The requirements and scope are based on the agreed-upon use cases described in RFC 7632.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationlPulse SecurednamekL. Lorenzinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8248eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsacmdfullx4sacm - Security Automation and Continuous Monitoringdnamex-Security Automation and Continuous Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-130067hkeywordsÖrposture assessmentrposture validationrsoftware integrityunetwork authorizationssoftware compliancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYƒM4granking 8crfcd8248irfcNumber 8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASecurity Automation and Continuous Monitoring (SACM) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThe IPsec series of protocols makes use of various cryptographic algorithms in order to provide security services.  The Internet Key Exchange (IKE) protocol is used to negotiate the IPsec Security Association (IPsec SA) parameters, such as which algorithms should be used.  To ensure interoperability between different implementations, it is necessary to specify a set of algorithm implementation requirements and usage guidance to ensure that there is at least one algorithm that all implementations support.  This document updates RFC 7296 and obsoletes RFC 4307 in defining the current algorithm implementation requirements and usage guidance for IKEv2, and does minor cleaning up of the IKEv2 IANA registry.  This document does not update the algorithms used for packet encryption using IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP).fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliation`dnamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migaultddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8247eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130066hkeywordsÉeIPseccIKEuinternet key exchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYπskgranking 7crfcd8247irfcNumber 7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqAlgorithm Implementation Requirements and Usage Guidance for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9395∂habstractyThe immutable HTTP response Cache-Control extension allows servers to identify resources that will not be updated during their freshness lifetime.  This ensures that a client never needs to revalidate a cached fresh resource to be certain it has not been modified.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejP. McManusddatejœìhfilenamegrfc8246eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130065hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYªœgranking 6crfcd8246irfcNumber 6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHTTP Immutable ResponsesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞RFC 5444 specifies a generalized Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) packet/message format and describes an intended use for multiplexed MANET routing protocol messages; this use is mandated by RFC 5498 when using the MANET port or protocol number that it specifies.  This document updates RFC 5444 by providing rules and recommendations for how the multiplexer operates and how protocols can use the packet/message format.  In particular, the mandatory rules prohibit a number of uses that have been suggested in various proposals and that would have led to interoperability problems, to the impediment of protocol extension development, and/or to an inability to use optional generic parsers.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsEcole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationkBAE SystemsdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliation`dnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationoFraunhofer FKIEdnamehH. Roggeddatej÷
hfilenamegrfc8245eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130064hkeywordsÅeMANETkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÍ◊ngranking 5crfcd8245irfcNumber 5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWRules for Designing Protocols Using the Generalized Packet/Message Format from RFC 5444dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThe policy defined in RFC 6761 for IANA registrations in the "Special-Use Domain Names" registry has been shown, through experience, to present challenges that were not anticipated when RFC 6761 was written. This memo presents a list, intended to be comprehensive, of the problems that have since been identified. In addition, it reviews the history of domain names and summarizes current IETF publications and some publications from other organizations relating to Special-Use Domain Names.

This document should be considered required reading for IETF participants who wish to express an informed opinion on the topic of Special-Use Domain Names.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8244eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130063hkeywordsÉcSUNeSUTLDgRFC6761kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÈ5granking 4crfcd8244irfcNumber 4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Special-Use Domain Names Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœAlthough TRILL is based on IS-IS, which supports multilevel unicast routing, extending TRILL to multiple levels has challenges that are not addressed by the already-existing capabilities of IS-IS. One issue is with the handling of multi-destination packet distribution trees. Other issues are with TRILL switch nicknames. How are such nicknames allocated across a multilevel TRILL network? Do nicknames need to be unique across an entire multilevel TRILL network? Or can they merely be unique within each multilevel area?

This informational document enumerates and examines alternatives based on a number of factors including backward compatibility, simplicity, and scalability; it makes recommendations in some cases.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationcJITdnamegH. Zhaiddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8243eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130062hkeywordsÇsaggregaged nicknameounique nicknamekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYµ˝granking 3crfcd8243irfcNumber 3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPAlternatives for Multilevel Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõ"An Architecture for the Interface to the Routing System" (RFC 7921) abstractly describes a number of requirements for ephemeral state (in terms of capabilities and behaviors) that any protocol suite attempting to meet the needs of the Interface to the Routing System (I2RS) protocol has to provide.  This document describes, in detail, requirements for ephemeral state for those implementing the I2RS protocol.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamegJ. Haas¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Haresddatejœdhfilenamegrfc8242eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-130061hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYªÚÆgranking 2crfcd8242irfcNumber 2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCInterface to the Routing System (I2RS) Ephemeral State RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThis document presents security-related requirements for the Interface to the Routing System (I2RS) protocol, which provides a new interface to the routing system described in the I2RS architecture document (RFC 7921).  The I2RS protocol is implemented by reusing portions of existing IETF protocols and adding new features to them.  One such reuse is of the security features of a secure transport (e.g., Transport Layer Security (TLS), Secure SHell (SSH) Protocol, Datagram TLS (DTLS)) such as encryption, message integrity, mutual peer authentication, and anti-replay protection.  The new I2RS features to consider from a security perspective are as follows: a priority mechanism to handle multi-headed write transactions, an opaque secondary identifier that identifies an application using the I2RS client, and an extremely constrained read-only non-secure transport.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationiErcisssondnamejD. Migault¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. HalpernddatejœVhfilenamegrfc8241eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-130060hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYªÚ¨granking 1crfcd8241irfcNumber 1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDInterface to the Routing System (I2RS) Security-Related RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyàThis document provides a summary of the Internet of Things Software Update (IoTSU) Workshop that took place at Trinity College Dublin, Ireland on the 13th and 14th of June, 2016. The main goal of the workshop was to foster a discussion on requirements, challenges, and solutions for bringing software and firmware updates to IoT devices. This report summarizes the discussions and lists recommendations to the standards community.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Farrellddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8240eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-130059hkeywordsÑhSecuritypFirmware UpdatespSoftware UpdatesrInternet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYµ˝granking 0crfcd8240irfcNumber 0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexHReport from the Internet of Things Software Update (IoTSU) Workshop 2016dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑The purpose of this informational document is to establish test and evaluation methodology and measurement techniques for physical network equipment in the data center.  RFC 8238 is a prerequisite for this document, as it contains terminology that is considered normative.  Many of these terms and methods may be applicable beyond the scope of this document as the technologies originally applied in the data center are deployed elsewhere.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Avramov¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamegJ. Rappddatejœéhfilenamegrfc8239eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-130058hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYß9∫granking /crfcd8239irfcNumber /estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Data Center Benchmarking MethodologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThe purposes of this informational document are to establish definitions and describe measurement techniques for data center benchmarking, as well as to introduce new terminology applicable to performance evaluations of data center network equipment.  This document establishes the important concepts for benchmarking network switches and routers in the data center and is a prerequisite for the test methodology document (RFC 8239).  Many of these terms and methods may be applicable to network equipment beyond the scope of this document as the technologies originally applied in the data center are deployed elsewhere.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Avramov¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamegJ. Rappddatejœçhfilenamegrfc8238eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-130057hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYß9∏granking .crfcd8238irfcNumber .estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Data Center Benchmarking TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis document describes a method for generating an aggregated pseudowire (PW) status message transmitted for a statically configured PW on a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Path (LSP) to indicate the status of one or more PWs carried on the LSP.

The method for transmitting the PW status information is not new; however, this protocol extension allows a Service Provider (SP) to reliably monitor the individual PW status while not overwhelming the network with multiple periodic status messages. This is achieved by sending a single cumulative summary status verification message for all the PWs grouped in the same LSP.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkMonoski LLCdnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationdSETCdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemE. Bellagambaddatejœéhfilenamegrfc8237eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130056hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY˘S…granking -crfcd8237irfcNumber -estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVMPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) Pseudowire (PW) Status Refresh Reduction for Static PWsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyXThis document specifies a Password-Authenticated Key Exchange by Juggling (J-PAKE) protocol.  This protocol allows the establishment of a secure end-to-end communication channel between two remote parties over an insecure network solely based on a shared password, without requiring a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) or any trusted third party.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxNewcastle University (UK)dnamefF. Haoddatejœàhfilenamegrfc8236eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130055hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÆŸgranking ,crfcd8236irfcNumber ,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex7J-PAKE: Password-Authenticated Key Exchange by JugglingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes the Schnorr non-interactive zero-knowledge (NIZK) proof, a non-interactive variant of the three-pass Schnorr identification scheme.  The Schnorr NIZK proof allows one to prove the knowledge of a discrete logarithm without leaking any information about its value.  It can serve as a useful building block for many cryptographic protocols to ensure that participants follow the protocol specification honestly.  This document specifies the Schnorr NIZK proof in both the finite field and the elliptic curve settings.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxNewcastle University (UK)dnamefF. Haoddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8235eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130054hkeywordsÉtZero-Knowledge ProofrSchnorr NIZK proofwIdentification protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÆŸgranking +crfcd8235irfcNumber +estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,Schnorr Non-interactive Zero-Knowledge ProofdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document contains updates to MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) linear protection in Automatic Protection Switching (APS) mode defined in RFC 7271.  The updates provide rules related to the initialization of the Protection State Coordination (PSC) Control Logic (in which the state machine resides) when operating in APS mode and clarify the operation related to state transition table lookup.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Ryoo¢kaffiliationdETRIdnameiT. Cheung¢kaffiliationnHai Gaoming BVdnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegI. Busi¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefG. Wenddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8234eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130053hkeywordsÉhAPS modeninitializationxmpls-tp linear protectionkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateYù√Ógranking *crfcd8234irfcNumber *estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjUpdates to MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Linear Protection in Automatic Protection Switching (APS) ModedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn certain networks, such as, but not limited to, financial information networks (e.g., stock market data providers), network performance criteria (e.g., latency) are becoming as critical to data path selection as other metrics and constraints. These metrics are associated with the Service Level Agreement (SLA) between customers and service providers. The link bandwidth utilization (the total bandwidth of a link in actual use for the forwarding) is another important factor to consider during path computation.

IGP Traffic Engineering (TE) Metric Extensions describe mechanisms with which network performance information is distributed via OSPF and IS-IS, respectively. The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) provides mechanisms for Path Computation Elements (PCEs) to perform path computations in response to Path Computation Client (PCC) requests. This document describes the extension to PCEP to carry latency, delay variation, packet loss, and link bandwidth utilization as constraints for end-to-end path computation.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationkNano Sec CodnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiK. Kumakiddatej’ﬂhfilenamegrfc8233eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130052hkeywordsácPCEdPCEPmservice-awarefmetricbBUcLBUdLRBUkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYƒùøgranking )crfcd8233irfcNumber )estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Extensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) to Compute Service-Aware Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9756∂habstracty2A stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) has access to not only the information disseminated by the network's Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) but also the set of active paths and their reserved resources for its computation.  The additional Label Switched Path (LSP) state information allows the PCE to compute constrained paths while considering individual LSPs and their interactions.  This requires a State Synchronization mechanism between the PCE and the network, the PCE and Path Computation Clients (PCCs), and cooperating PCEs.  The basic mechanism for State Synchronization is part of the stateful PCE specification.  This document presents motivations for optimizations to the base State Synchronization procedure and specifies the required Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) extensions.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiE. Crabbe¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Medved¢kaffiliationxPantheon Technologies SROdnamehR. Varga¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhodyddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8232eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130051hkeywordsÉlStateful PCEustate synchronizationloptimizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYƒùΩgranking (crfcd8232irfcNumber (estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXOptimizations of Label Switched Path State Synchronization Procedures for a Stateful PCEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) provides mechanisms for Path Computation Elements (PCEs) to perform path computations in response to Path Computation Client (PCC) requests.

Although PCEP explicitly makes no assumptions regarding the information available to the PCE, it also makes no provisions for PCE control of timing and sequence of path computations within and across PCEP sessions. This document describes a set of extensions to PCEP to enable stateful control of MPLS-TE and GMPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs) via PCEP.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiE. Crabbe¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Medved¢kaffiliationxPantheon Technologies SROdnamehR. Vargaddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8231eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130050hkeywordsÅlStateful PCEkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateYƒùµgranking 'crfcd8231irfcNumber 'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Extensions for Stateful PCEdtypecrfciupdatedByÖd9756d8786d9353d9753d9862∂habstractyŸThe CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) specification defines cryptographic message encodings using Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR).  This specification defines algorithm encodings and representations enabling RSA algorithms to be used for COSE messages.  Encodings are specified for the use of RSA Probabilistic Signature Scheme (RSASSA-PSS) signatures, RSA Encryption Scheme - Optimal Asymmetric Encryption Padding (RSAES-OAEP) encryption, and RSA keys.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8230eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130049hkeywordsÉlCryptographyqDigital SignaturejEncryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYØ¸granking &crfcd8230irfcNumber &estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLUsing RSA Algorithms with CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThis document describes a method to transport Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE) and IPsec packets over a TCP connection for traversing network middleboxes that may block IKE negotiation over UDP.  This method, referred to as "TCP encapsulation", involves sending both IKE packets for Security Association establishment and Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) packets over a TCP connection.  This method is intended to be used as a fallback option when IKE cannot be negotiated over UDP.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehT. Pauly¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Touati¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Manthaddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8229eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130048hkeywordsÑcIKEeIKEv2eIPseccTCPkobsoletedByÅd9329epagesopublicationDateYèï˝granking %crfcd8229irfcNumber %estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*TCP Encapsulation of IKE and IPsec PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9Rules for validating identifier labels and alternate representations of those labels (variants) are known as Label Generation Rulesets (LGRs); they are used for the implementation of identifier systems such as Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs).  This document describes ways to design LGRs to support variant labels.  In designing LGRs, it is important to ensure that the label generation rules are consistent and well behaved in the presence of variants.  The design decisions can then be expressed using the XML representation of LGRs that is defined in RFC 7940.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejA. Freytagddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8228eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130047hkeywordsÉcLGRgVariantcIDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYú§™granking $crfcd8228irfcNumber $estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPGuidance on Designing Label Generation Rulesets (LGRs) Supporting Variant LabelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document describes requirements, architecture, and solutions for MPLS-TP Shared-Ring Protection (MSRP) in a ring topology for point- to-point (P2P) services.  The MSRP mechanism is described to meet the ring protection requirements as described in RFC 5654.  This document defines the Ring Protection Switching (RPS) protocol that is used to coordinate the protection behavior of the nodes on an MPLS ring.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Cheng¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeH. Li¢kaffiliationnHai Gaoming BVdnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dongddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8227eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130046hkeywordsÜswrapping protectionxshort-wrapping protectionssteering protectionoring protectionvshared ring protectiontprotection switchingkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateY¶granking #crfcd8227irfcNumber #estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAMPLS-TP Shared-Ring Protection (MSRP) Mechanism for Ring TopologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäIn order to prevent the impersonation of telephone numbers on the Internet, some kind of credential system needs to exist that cryptographically asserts authority over telephone numbers.  This document describes the use of certificates in establishing authority over telephone numbers, as a component of a broader architecture for managing telephone numbers as identities in protocols like SIP.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlNeuStar Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8226eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130045hkeywordsÇsTNAuthorizationListsJWTClaimConstraintskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÑ‰4granking "crfcd8226irfcNumber "estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Secure Telephone Identity Credentials: CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9118∂habstractyYThis document defines a method for creating and validating a token that cryptographically verifies an originating identity or, more generally, a URI or telephone number representing the originator of personal communications.  The Personal Assertion Token, PASSporT, is cryptographically signed to protect the integrity of the identity of the originator and to verify the assertion of the identity information at the destination.  The cryptographic signature is defined with the intention that it can confidently verify the originating persona even when the signature is sent to the destination party over an insecure channel.  PASSporT is particularly useful for many personal-communications applications over IP networks and other multi-hop interconnection scenarios where the originating and destination parties may not have a direct trusted relationship.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamehC. Wendt¢kaffiliationlNeuStar Inc.dnamekJ. PetersonddatejœÑhfilenamegrfc8225eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130044hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateZÑ‰,granking !crfcd8225irfcNumber !estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"PASSporT: Personal Assertion TokendtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯The baseline security mechanisms in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) are inadequate for cryptographically assuring the identity of the end users that originate SIP requests, especially in an interdomain context. This document defines a mechanism for securely identifying originators of SIP requests. It does so by defining a SIP header field for conveying a signature used for validating the identity and for conveying a reference to the credentials of the signer.

This document obsoletes RFC 4474.fadNamejAdam Roachdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. Wendtddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8224eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-130043hkeywordsÜcSIPxSecure Origin IdentificationvCommunication SecurityfRTCWeblCertificateswReal-Time CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateZÑ‰&granking  crfcd8224irfcNumber  estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJAuthenticated Identity Management in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8946∂habstractyÓRecent Targeted Label Distribution Protocol (tLDP) applications, such as remote Loop-Free Alternates (LFAs) and BGP auto-discovered pseudowires, may automatically establish a tLDP session with any Label Switching Router (LSR) in a network.  The initiating LSR has information about the targeted applications to administratively control initiation of the session.  However, the responding LSR has no such information to control acceptance of this session.  This document defines a mechanism to advertise and negotiate the Targeted Application Capability (TAC) during LDP session initialization.  As the responding LSR becomes aware of targeted applications, it may establish a limited number of tLDP sessions for certain applications.  In addition, each targeted application is mapped to LDP Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) elements to advertise only necessary LDP FEC label bindings over the session.  This document updates RFC 7473 for enabling advertisement of LDP FEC label bindings over the session.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehS. Esale¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehR. Torvi¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalil¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekU. Chunduri¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Razaddatejœçhfilenamegrfc8223eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130042hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY®ü∫granking crfcd8223irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexApplication-Aware Targeted LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥Despite its name, DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD) can use naming systems other than DNS when looking for services.  Moreover, when it uses DNS, DNS-SD uses the full capability of DNS, rather than using a subset of available octets.  This is of particular relevance where some environments use DNS labels that conform to Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA), and other environments use labels containing Unicode characters (such as containing octets corresponding to characters encoded as UTF-8).  In order for DNS-SD to be used effectively in environments where multiple different name systems and conventions for their operation are in use, it is important to attend to differences in the underlying technology and operational environment.  This memo presents an outline of the requirements for the selection of labels for conventional DNS and other resolution systems when they are expected to interoperate in this manner.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcDyndnamekA. Sullivanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8222eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednssddfullx5dnssd - Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydnamex-Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-130041hkeywordsÉcDNSdmDNSfDNS-SDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYƒM2granking crfcd8222irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjSelecting Labels for Use with Conventional DNS and Other Resolution Systems in DNS-Based Service DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document replaces RFC 7321, "Cryptographic Algorithm Implementation Requirements and Usage Guidance for Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH)".  The goal of this document is to enable ESP and AH to benefit from cryptography that is up to date while making IPsec interoperable.fadNamemEric Rescorladarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Migault¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Mattsson¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliation`dnamejT. Kivinenddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8221eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130040hkeywordsÇeIPseccIKEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÊí=granking crfcd8221irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexéCryptographic Algorithm Implementation Requirements and Usage Guidance for Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9395∂habstractyhThis document describes the procedures and recommendations for Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Provider Edges (PEs) to facilitate replication of multicast traffic to only certain ports (behind which there are interested Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) routers and/or Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) hosts) via PIM snooping and proxying.

With PIM snooping, PEs passively listen to certain PIM control messages to build control and forwarding states while transparently flooding those messages. With PIM proxying, PEs do not flood PIM Join/Prune messages but only generate their own and send them out of certain ports, based on the control states built from downstream Join/Prune messages. PIM proxying is required when PIM Join suppression is enabled on the Customer Edge (CE) devices and is useful for reducing PIM control traffic in a VPLS domain.

This document also describes PIM relay, which can be viewed as lightweight proxying, where all downstream Join/Prune messages are simply forwarded out of certain ports and are not flooded, thereby avoiding the triggering of PIM Join suppression on CE devices.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiO. Dornon¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Kotalwar¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiV. Hemige¢kaffiliationjmistnet.iodnamefR. Qiu¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehZ. Zhangddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8220eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130039hkeywordsÅimulticastkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateY∏hHgranking crfcd8220irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLProtocol Independent Multicast (PIM) over Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIBenchmarking methodologies that address the performance of network interconnect devices that are IPv4- or IPv6-capable exist, but the IPv6 transition technologies are outside of their scope.  This document provides complementary guidelines for evaluating the performance of IPv6 transition technologies.  More specifically, this document targets IPv6 transition technologies that employ encapsulation or translation mechanisms, as dual-stack nodes can be tested using the recommendations of RFCs 2544 and 5180.  The methodology also includes a metric for benchmarking load scalability.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgRCS&RDSdnamelM. Georgescu¢kaffiliationgRCS&RDSdnamejL. Pislaru¢kaffiliationxSzechenyi Istvan UniversitydnameiG. Lencseddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8219eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-130038hkeywordsêxSingle Translation TechnologiesxDouble Translation TechnologiesxEncapsulation TechnologieseNAT64eDNS64eMAP-EeMAP-TfDSLiteg464XLATc6PExDNS Resolution PerformancetOverload ScalabilityoTypical LatencyrWorst Case LatencycPDVdIPDVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYèï˙granking crfcd8219irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Benchmarking Methodology for IPv6 Transition TechnologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This document specifies a multipath extension for the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2) to discover multiple disjoint paths for Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs).  Considering the characteristics of MANETs, especially the dynamic network topology, using multiple paths can increase aggregated throughput and improve the reliability by avoiding single route failures.  The interoperability with OLSRv2 is retained.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsEcole PolytechniquednameeJ. Yi¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NantesdnamejB. Parreinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8218eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-130037hkeywordsÅeMANETkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYü”granking crfcd8218irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTMultipath Extension for the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2 (OLSRv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%RFC 3261 constrained several SIP header fields whose grammar contains the "name-addr / addr-spec" alternative to use name-addr when certain characters appear. Unfortunately, it expressed the constraints with prose copied into each header field definition, and at least one header field was missed. Further, the constraint has not been copied into documents defining extension headers whose grammar contains the alternative.

This document updates RFC 3261 to state the constraint generically and clarifies that the constraint applies to all SIP header fields where there is a choice between using name-addr or addr-spec. It also updates the RFCs that define extension SIP header fields using the alternative to clarify that the constraint applies (RFCs 3325, 3515, 3892, 4508, 5002, 5318, 5360, and 5502).fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiR. Sparksddatejœâhfilenamegrfc8217eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130036hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÇ_3granking crfcd8217irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGClarifications for When to Use the name-addr Production in SIP MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes a protocol for transferring unbounded streams of multimedia data.  It specifies the data format of the files and the actions to be taken by the server (sender) and the clients (receivers) of the streams.  It describes version 7 of this protocol.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiR. Pantos¢kaffiliationrMLB Advanced MediadnamefW. Mayddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8216eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-130035hkeywordsÉdHTMListreamingemediakobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateY®üπgranking crfcd8216irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlesHTTP Live StreamingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÄThis document reserves the IPv6 prefix 64:ff9b:1::/48 for local use within domains that enable IPv4/IPv6 translation mechanisms.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnRedpill LinprodnamekT. Andersonddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8215eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130034hkeywordsáoIPv6 transitioncIVIcMAPeNAT64dSIITgSIIT-DCjTransitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYú§®granking crfcd8215irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Local-Use IPv4/IPv6 Translation PrefixdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document describes how Ethernet VPN (EVPN) can be used to support the Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS) in MPLS/IP networks.  EVPN accomplishes the following for VPWS: provides Single-Active as well as All-Active multihoming with flow-based load-balancing, eliminates the need for Pseudowire (PW) signaling, and provides fast protection convergence upon node or link failure.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ. Rabadanddatejœähfilenamegrfc8214eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-130033hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYùÂÛgranking crfcd8214irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Virtual Private Wire Service Support in Ethernet VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv4 (DHCPv4) has no guidance for how to secure messages exchanged between servers and relay agents.  The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) states that IPsec should be used to secure messages exchanged between servers and relay agents but does not require encryption.  With recent concerns about pervasive monitoring and other attacks, it is appropriate to require securing relay-to-relay and relay-to-server communication for DHCPv6 and relay-to-server communication for DHCPv4.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefY. Palddatejœãhfilenamegrfc8213eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130032hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÑógranking crfcd8213irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Security of Messages Exchanged between Servers and Relay AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¢This document updates RFC 4271 by defining the default behavior of a BGP speaker when there is no Import or Export Policy associated with an External BGP session.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamehJ. Mauch¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejG. Hankinsddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8212eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130031hkeywordsÉfrejectcBGPdEBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY]m„granking crfcd8212irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGDefault External BGP (EBGP) Route Propagation Behavior without PoliciesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–This document analyzes actions by or against a Certification Authority (CA) or an independent repository manager in the RPKI that can adversely affect the Internet Number Resources (INRs) associated with that CA or its subordinate CAs.  The analysis is done from the perspective of an affected INR holder.  The analysis is based on examination of the data items in the RPKI repository, as controlled by a CA (or an independent repository manager) and fetched by Relying Parties (RPs).  The analysis does not purport to be comprehensive; it does represent an orderly way to analyze a number of ways that errors by or attacks against a CA or repository manager can affect the RPKI and routing decisions based on RPKI data.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationdZDNSdnameeD. Maddatej’˜hfilenamegrfc8211eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130030hkeywordsÅlBGP SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÃwÆgranking crfcd8211irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxAdverse Actions by a Certification Authority (CA) or Repository Manager in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿In order to verifiably validate the origin Autonomous Systems and Autonomous System Paths of BGP announcements, routers need a simple but reliable mechanism to receive Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RFC 6480) prefix origin data and router keys from a trusted cache. This document describes a protocol to deliver them.

This document describes version 1 of the RPKI-Router protocol. RFC 6810 describes version 0. This document updates RFC 6810.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. Austeinddatejœwhfilenamegrfc8210eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130029hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateYÃw≠granking crfcd8210irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKThe Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) to Router Protocol, Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThis document defines a standard profile for X.509 certificates used to enable validation of Autonomous System (AS) paths in the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP), as part of an extension to that protocol known as BGPsec.  BGP is the standard for inter-domain routing in the Internet; it is the "glue" that holds the Internet together.  BGPsec is being developed as one component of a solution that addresses the requirement to provide security for BGP.  The goal of BGPsec is to provide full AS path validation based on the use of strong cryptographic primitives.  The end entity (EE) certificates specified by this profile are issued to routers within an AS.  Each of these certificates is issued under a Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Certification Authority (CA) certificate.  These CA certificates and EE certificates both contain the AS Resource extension.  An EE certificate of this type asserts that the router or routers holding the corresponding private key are authorized to emit secure route advertisements on behalf of the AS(es) specified in the certificate.  This document also profiles the format of certification requests and specifies Relying Party (RP) certificate path validation procedures for these EE certificates.  This document extends the RPKI; therefore, this document updates the RPKI Resource Certificates Profile (RFC 6487).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdIPSwdnamekM. Reynolds¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kentddatejœihfilenamegrfc8209eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130028hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÃw≠granking crfcd8209irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbA Profile for BGPsec Router Certificates, Certificate Revocation Lists, and Certification RequestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document specifies the algorithms, algorithm parameters, asymmetric key formats, asymmetric key sizes, and signature formats used in BGPsec (Border Gateway Protocol Security). This document updates RFC 7935 ("The Profile for Algorithms and Key Sizes for Use in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure").

This document also includes example BGPsec UPDATE messages as well as the private keys used to generate the messages and the certificates necessary to validate those signatures.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamekO. Borchertddatejœyhfilenamegrfc8208eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130027hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8608epagesopublicationDateYÃw¨granking crfcd8208irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5BGPsec Algorithms, Key Formats, and Signature FormatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyDeployment of the BGPsec architecture and protocols has many operational considerations.  This document attempts to collect and present the most critical and universal.  Operational practices are expected to evolve as BGPsec is formalized and initially deployed.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bushddatej’Òhfilenamegrfc8207eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130026hkeywordsÑcBGPdRPKIgRoutinghSecuritykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateYÃw´granking crfcd8207irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber”etotaletitlex!BGPsec Operational ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxüThis document discusses considerations and methods for supporting and securing a common method for Autonomous System (AS) migration within the BGPsec protocol.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnameiW. George¢kaffiliationmPARSONS, Inc.dnameiS. Murphyddatej’˚hfilenamegrfc8206eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130025hkeywordsÑlas-migrationdSIDRfBGPsecgAS_PATHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÃw™granking crfcd8206irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:BGPsec Considerations for Autonomous System (AS) MigrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes BGPsec, an extension to the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) that provides security for the path of Autonomous Systems (ASes) through which a BGP UPDATE message passes.  BGPsec is implemented via an optional non-transitive BGP path attribute that carries digital signatures produced by each AS that propagates the UPDATE message.  The digital signatures provide confidence that every AS on the path of ASes listed in the UPDATE message has explicitly authorized the advertisement of the route.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNCFdnamekM. Lepinski¢kaffiliationdNISTdnameiK. Sriramddatej’Úhfilenamegrfc8205eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130024hkeywordsÑcBGPfBGPsecvBGP AS-path protectionlBGP SecuritykobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateYÃwõgranking crfcd8205irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGPsec Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8206∂habstracty!This memo describes the contributions of the Open Platform for NFV (OPNFV) project on Virtual Switch Performance (VSPERF), particularly in the areas of test setups and configuration parameters for the system under test.  This project has extended the current and completed work of the Benchmarking Methodology Working Group in the IETF and references existing literature.  The Benchmarking Methodology Working Group has traditionally conducted laboratory characterization of dedicated physical implementations of internetworking functions.  Therefore, this memo describes the additional considerations when virtual switches are implemented on general-purpose hardware.  The expanded tests and benchmarks are also influenced by the OPNFV mission to support virtualization of the "telco" infrastructure.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiM. Tahhan¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekB. O'Mahony¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatejœàhfilenamegrfc8204eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-130023hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYƒM/granking crfcd8204irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBBenchmarking Virtual Switches in the Open Platform for NFV (OPNFV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document enhances the BGP Cease NOTIFICATION message "Administrative Shutdown" and "Administrative Reset" subcodes for operators to transmit a short freeform message to describe why a BGP session was shutdown or reset.  This document updates RFC 4486.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehJ. Heitz¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejJ. Scudderddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8203eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130022hkeywordsÉcBGPeceasehshutdownkobsoletedByÅd9003epagesopublicationDateYw¸¢granking crfcd8203irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)BGP Administrative Shutdown CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document describes a mechanism that allows a single router to share one or more circuits among multiple Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) routing protocol instances.

Multiple instances allow the isolation of resources associated with each instance. Routers will form instance-specific adjacencies. Each instance can support multiple topologies. Each topology has a unique Link State Database (LSDB). Each Protocol Data Unit (PDU) will contain a new Type-Length-Value (TLV) identifying the instance and the topology (or topologies) to which the PDU belongs.

