estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletIS-IS Multi-InstancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx∑This document describes Path MTU Discovery (PMTUD) for IP version 6.  It is largely derived from RFC 1191, which describes Path MTU Discovery for IP version 4.  It obsoletes RFC 1981.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxDigital Equipment CorporationdnameiJ. McCann¢kaffiliationgRetireddnamejS. Deering¢kaffiliationxDigital Equipment CorporationdnamehJ. Mogul¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hindenddatej÷	hfilenamegrfc8201eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130020hkeywordsàhMTU-IPv6hInternethProtocoldIPv6hlink MTUhpath MTUdPMTUrPath MTU DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYi≠granking 	crfcd8201irfcNumber 	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberWetotaletitlex#Path MTU Discovery for IP version 6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxZThis document specifies version 6 of the Internet Protocol (IPv6).  It obsoletes RFC 2460.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRetireddnamejS. Deering¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hindenddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8200eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130019hkeywordsádIPv6hinternethprotocoldnextjgenerationdipngjflow labelkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateYi¨granking crfcd8200irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberVetotaletitlex1Internet Protocol, Version 6 (IPv6) SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9673∂habstracty&The YANG data modeling language is currently being considered for a wide variety of applications throughout the networking industry at large. Many standards development organizations (SDOs), open-source software projects, vendors, and users are using YANG to develop and publish YANG modules for a wide variety of applications. At the same time, there is currently no well-known terminology to categorize various types of YANG modules.

A consistent terminology would help with the categorization of YANG modules, assist in the analysis of the YANG data modeling efforts in the IETF and other organizations, and bring clarity to the YANG- related discussions between the different groups.

This document describes a set of concepts and associated terms to support consistent classification of YANG modules.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtVolta Networks, Inc.dnamemD. Bogdanovic¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiC. Mobergddatej÷
hfilenamegrfc8199eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130018hkeywordságservicegelementhstandardfvendorduserjcontrollerlorchestratorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYxCgranking crfcd8199irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYANG Module ClassificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨The DNS relies upon caching to scale; however, the cache lookup generally requires an exact match. This document specifies the use of NSEC/NSEC3 resource records to allow DNSSEC-validating resolvers to generate negative answers within a range and positive answers from wildcards. This increases performance, decreases latency, decreases resource utilization on both authoritative and recursive servers, and increases privacy. Also, it may help increase resilience to certain DoS attacks in some circumstances.

This document updates RFC 4035 by allowing validating resolvers to generate negative answers based upon NSEC/NSEC3 records and positive answers in the presence of wildcards.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwara¢kaffiliationiKeio/WIDEdnamegA. Kato¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej÷
hfilenamegrfc8198eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130017hkeywordsÑnNegative cachefNCACHEdNSECeNSEC3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYw¸†granking crfcd8198irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Aggressive Use of DNSSEC-Validated CachedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9077∂habstractyThis document defines the 607 (Unwanted) SIP response code, allowing called parties to indicate that the call or message was unwanted.  SIP entities may use this information to adjust how future calls from this calling party are handled for the called party or more broadly.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcFCCdnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej÷
hfilenamegrfc8197eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-130016hkeywordsÑcSIPhrobocallhunwantedmresponse codekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYhTgranking crfcd8197irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&A SIP Response Code for Unwanted CallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document specifies IS-IS autoconfiguration mechanisms.  The key components are IS-IS System ID self-generation, duplication detection, and duplication resolution.  These mechanisms provide limited IS-IS functions and are therefore suitable for networks where plug-and-play configuration is expected.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefB. Liu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationiT-SystemsdnamenM. Abrahamssonddatej÷	hfilenamegrfc8196eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130015hkeywordsÅwisis auto-configurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYhSgranking crfcd8196irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewIS-IS AutoconfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis document presents examples and inspiration for operator application of BGP Large Communities.  Based on operational experience with BGP Communities, this document suggests logical categories of BGP Large Communities and demonstrates an orderly manner of organizing community values within them to achieve typical goals in routing policy.  Any operator can consider using the concepts presented as the basis for their own BGP Large Communities repertoire.fadNamemWarren Kumaridarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejJ. Heasley¢kaffiliationgi3D.netdnamejM. Schmidtddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8195eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130014hkeywordsÉelargecBGPkcommunitieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYV÷Ägranking crfcd8195irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUse of BGP Large CommunitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxìThis document defines a data model for Large-Scale Measurement Platforms (LMAPs).  The data model is defined using the YANG data modeling language.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationxTechnical University of MunichdnameiV. Bajpaiddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8194eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlmapdfullx7lmap - Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancednamex0Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130013hkeywordsÇdLMAPdYANGkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateYõu=granking crfcd8194irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-A YANG Data Model for LMAP Measurement AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»This Information Model applies to the Measurement Agent within an LMAP framework.  As such, it outlines the information that is configured or preconfigured on the Measurement Agent or exists in communications with a Controller or Collector within an LMAP framework.  The purpose of such an Information Model is to provide a protocol- and device-independent view of the Measurement Agent that can be implemented via one or more Control and Report Protocols.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamelT. Burbridge¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationx Universidad Carlos III de MadriddnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. SchoenwaelderddatejœÇhfilenamegrfc8193eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlmapdfullx7lmap - Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancednamex0Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancedtypebwgbidjdoc-130012hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateYõu:granking crfcd8193irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Information Model for Large-Scale Measurement Platforms (LMAPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxáThis document sets out the problem statement for Interface to Network Security Functions (I2NSF) and outlines some companion use cases.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationgvArmourdnamehM. Zarny¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelC. Jacquenet¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehR. Kumar¢kaffiliationwSungkyunkwan UniversitydnamehJ. Jeongddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8192eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymei2nsfdfullx/i2nsf - Interface to Network Security Functionsdnamex'Interface to Network Security Functionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-130011hkeywordsÅeI2NSFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYw¸ùgranking ␀crfcd8192irfcNumber ␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPInterface to Network Security Functions (I2NSF): Problem Statement and Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqIn the basic Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) specification, a Mobile Node (MN) is assigned with a Home Network Prefix (HNP) during its initial attachment, and the MN configures its Home Address (HoA) with the HNP.  During the movement of the MN, the HNP remains unchanged to keep ongoing communications associated with the HoA.  However, the current PMIPv6 specification does not specify related operations when HNP renumbering has occurred (e.g., due to change of service provider or site topology, etc.).  In this document, a solution to support HNP renumbering is proposed, as an optional extension of the PMIPv6 specification.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefZ. Yan¢kaffiliationtSangmyung UniversitydnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefX. Leeddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8191eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130010hkeywordsÑfPMIPv6cHNPoHNP renumberinglLMA handoverkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateYÉ˙„grankingˇcrfcd8191irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Home Network Prefix Renumbering in Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty%This memo updates the IANA IPv4 and IPv6 Special-Purpose Address Registries to address issues raised by the definition of a "global" prefix. It also corrects several errors in registry entries to ensure the integrity of the IANA Special-Purpose Address Registries.

This memo updates RFC 6890.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationcPTIdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationxJohns Hopkins UniversitydnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiL. VegodaddatejœÖhfilenamegrfc8190eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-130009hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYR’:granking˛crfcd8190irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberôetotaletitlex4Updates to the Special-Purpose IP Address RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) protocol, specified in RFC 7285, defines several services that return various metrics describing the costs between network endpoints.

This document defines a new service that allows an ALTO Client to retrieve several cost metrics in a single request for an ALTO filtered cost map and endpoint cost map. In addition, it extends the constraints to further filter those maps by allowing an ALTO Client to specify a logical combination of tests on several cost metrics.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamenS. Randriamasy¢kaffiliationoNokia Bell LabsdnamehW. Roome¢kaffiliationrThales DeutschlanddnameiN. Schwanddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8189eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-130008hkeywordsãxALTO Information ResourceskNetwork MapcPIDtFiltered Network MapxEndpoint Property ServiceuEndpoint Cost ServicejMulti-CostwFiltered Multi-Cost MapvMulti-Cost Data FormatsTestable Cost Typesnor-constraintskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÔÿgranking˝crfcd8189irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Multi-Cost Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx\This memo introduces a content coding for HTTP that allows message payloads to be encrypted.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8188eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130007hkeywordsÑdhttpncontent codingpcontent encodingoencryption aeadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYMèØgranking¸crfcd8188irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Encrypted Content-Encoding for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›By default, header field values in Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) messages cannot easily carry characters outside the US-ASCII coded character set. RFC 2231 defines an encoding mechanism for use in parameters inside Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) header field values. This document specifies an encoding suitable for use in HTTP header fields that is compatible with a simplified profile of the encoding defined in RFC 2231.

This document obsoletes RFC 5987.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatejœvhfilenamegrfc8187eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-130006hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY∫·granking˚crfcd8187irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKIndicating Character Encoding and Language for HTTP Header Field ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˜This document describes an OPTIONAL feature for active performance measurement protocols that allows use of the Precision Time Protocol timestamp format defined in IEEE 1588v2, as an alternative to the Network Time Protocol that is currently used.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnameiI. Meilikddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8186eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-130005hkeywordsÑdIPPMeTWAMPiIEEE 1588ePTPv2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY9 ?granking˙crfcd8186irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZSupport of the IEEE 1588 Timestamp Format in a Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòIn some scenarios, MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) pseudowires (PWs) (RFC 5921) may be statically configured when a dynamic control plane is not available.  A fast protection mechanism for MPLS-TP PWs is needed to protect against the failure of an Attachment Circuit (AC), the failure of a Provider Edge (PE), or a failure in the Packet Switched Network (PSN).  The framework and typical scenarios of dual- homing PW local protection are described in RFC 8184.  This document proposes a dual-homing coordination mechanism for MPLS-TP PWs that is used for state exchange and switchover coordination between the dual- homing PEs for dual-homing PW local protection.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Cheng¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeH. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnameoA. D'Alessandroddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8185eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130004hkeywordsÇdmplsgmpls-tpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYQcgranking˘crfcd8185irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTDual-Homing Coordination for MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Pseudowires ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document describes a framework and several scenarios for a pseudowire (PW) dual-homing local protection mechanism that avoids unnecessary switchovers and does not depend on whether a control plane is used.  A Dual-Node Interconnection (DNI) PW is used to carry traffic between the dual-homing Provider Edge (PE) nodes when a failure occurs in one of the Attachment Circuits (AC) or PWs.  This PW dual-homing local protection mechanism is complementary to existing PW protection mechanisms.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Cheng¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeH. Li¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnameiS. Davari¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dongddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8184eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-130003hkeywordsÇdmplsgmpls-tpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYQb¸granking¯crfcd8184irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTDual-Homing Protection for MPLS and the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This note describes a simple out-of-band protocol to ease setup of the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) provisioning and publication protocols between two parties. The protocol is encoded in a small number of XML messages, which can be passed back and forth by any mutually agreeable means which provides acceptable data integrity and authentication.

This setup protocol is not part of the provisioning or publication protocol; rather, it is intended to simplify configuration of these protocols by setting up relationships and exchanging keying material used to authenticate those relationships.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. Austeinddatej’ˇhfilenamegrfc8183eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130002hkeywordsÅdRPKIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYy
>granking˜crfcd8183irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_An Out-of-Band Setup Protocol for Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Production ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“In the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), Certificate Authorities (CAs) publish certificates, including end-entity certificates, Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs), and RPKI signed objects to repositories.  Relying Parties retrieve the published information from those repositories.  This document specifies a new RPKI Repository Delta Protocol (RRDP) for this purpose.  RRDP was specifically designed for scaling.  It relies on an Update Notification File which lists the current Snapshot and Delta Files that can be retrieved using HTTPS (HTTP over Transport Layer Security (TLS)), and it enables the use of Content Distribution Networks (CDNs) or other caching infrastructures for the retrieval of these files.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamenT. Bruijnzeels¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamelO. Muravskiy¢kaffiliationhCobeniandnamehB. Weber¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. Austeinddatejœzhfilenamegrfc8182eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130001hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYy
8grankingˆcrfcd8182irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The RPKI Repository Delta Protocol (RRDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9674d9697∂habstractyThis document defines a protocol for publishing Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) objects.  Even though the RPKI will have many participants issuing certificates and creating other objects, it is operationally useful to consolidate the publication of those objects.  Even in cases where a certificate issuer runs its own publication repository, it can be useful to run the certificate engine itself on a different machine from the publication repository.  This document defines a protocol which addresses these needs.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiW3C / MITdnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationjSTEER TechdnamekA. Sonalker¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. Austeinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8181eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-130000hkeywordsÅdSIDRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYy
6grankingıcrfcd8181irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHA Publication Protocol for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty This document describes a minimal mode of operation for an IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4e (6TiSCH) network.  This minimal mode of operation specifies the baseline set of protocols that need to be supported and the recommended configurations and modes of operation sufficient to enable a 6TiSCH functional network.  6TiSCH provides IPv6 connectivity over a Time-Slotted Channel Hopping (TSCH) mesh composed of IEEE Std 802.15.4 TSCH links.  This minimal mode uses a collection of protocols with the respective configurations, including the IPv6 Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) framework, enabling interoperable IPv6 connectivity over IEEE Std 802.15.4 TSCH.  This minimal configuration provides the necessary bandwidth for network and security bootstrapping and defines the proper link between the IETF protocols that interface to IEEE Std 802.15.4 TSCH.  This minimal mode of operation should be implemented by all 6TiSCH-compliant devices.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversitat Oberta de CatalunyadnamemX. Vilajosana¢kaffiliationx!University of California BerkeleydnameiK. Pister¢kaffiliationnAnalog DevicesdnamekT. Watteyneddatejœ~hfilenamegrfc8180eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6tischdfullx26tisch - IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4ednamex)IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4edtypebwgbidjdoc-129999hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY/BngrankingÙcrfcd8180irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber“etotaletitlexHMinimal IPv6 over the TSCH Mode of IEEE 802.15.4e (6TiSCH) ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThe IETF policies about Intellectual Property Rights (IPR), such as patent rights, relative to technologies developed in the IETF are designed to ensure that IETF working groups and participants have as much information as possible about any IPR constraints on a technical proposal as early as possible in the development process.  The policies are intended to benefit the Internet community and the public at large, while respecting the legitimate rights of IPR holders.  This document sets out the IETF policies concerning IPR related to technology worked on within the IETF.  It also describes the objectives that the policies are designed to meet.  This document updates RFC 2026 and, with RFC 5378, replaces Section 10 of RFC 2026.  This document also obsoletes RFCs 3979 and 4879.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationrUniversity of UtahdnamelJ. Contrerasddatej÷␀hfilenamegrfc8179eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129998hkeywordsÇcIPRicopyrightkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY/KdgrankingÛcrfcd8179irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberOetotaletitlex/Intellectual Property Rights in IETF TechnologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸This document describes the rules relating to the extension of the NFSv4 family of protocols.  It covers the creation of minor versions, the addition of optional features to existing minor versions, and the correction of flaws in features already published as Proposed Standards.  The rules relating to the construction of minor versions and the interaction of minor version implementations that appear in this document supersede the minor versioning rules in RFC 5661 and other RFCs defining minor versions.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiD. NoveckddatejœÜhfilenamegrfc8178eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129997hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYhQgrankingÚcrfcd8178irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Rules for NFSv4 Extensions and Minor VersionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document describes the key chain YANG data model.  Key chains are commonly used for routing protocol authentication and other applications requiring symmetric keys.  A key chain is a list containing one or more elements containing a Key ID, key string, send/accept lifetimes, and the associated authentication or encryption algorithm.  By properly overlapping the send and accept lifetimes of multiple key chain elements, key strings and algorithms may be gracefully updated.  By representing them in a YANG data model, key distribution can be automated.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Qu¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehD. Yeung¢kaffiliationeJabildnamegI. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. ZhangddatejœÇhfilenamegrfc8177eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129996hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYC$«grankingÒcrfcd8177irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYANG Data Model for Key ChainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThe "amr" (Authentication Methods References) claim is defined and registered in the IANA "JSON Web Token Claims" registry, but no standard Authentication Method Reference values are currently defined.  This specification establishes a registry for Authentication Method Reference values and defines an initial set of Authentication Method Reference values.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegP. Hunt¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejA. Nadalinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8176eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129995hkeywordsÇxAuthentication Method ReferenceuAuthentication MethodkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYDpÀgrankingcrfcd8176irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Authentication Method Reference ValuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØWhen routing devices rely on modems to effect communications over wireless links, they need timely and accurate knowledge of the characteristics of the link (speed, state, etc.) in order to make routing decisions.  In mobile or other environments where these characteristics change frequently, manual configurations or the inference of state through routing or transport protocols does not allow the router to make the best decisions.  This document introduces a new protocol called the Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP), which provides a bidirectional, event-driven communication channel between the router and the modem to facilitate communication of changing link characteristics.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjVT iDirectdnamejS. Ratliff¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegS. Jury¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamenD. Satterwhite¢kaffiliationvAirbus Defence & SpacednameiR. Taylor¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Berryddatejœphfilenamegrfc8175eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129994hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDateYI grankingÔcrfcd8175irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Dynamic Link Exchange Protocol (DLEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxÿRFC 2119 specifies common key words that may be used in protocol specifications.  This document aims to reduce the ambiguity by clarifying that only UPPERCASE usage of the key words have the defined special meanings.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatejœ|hfilenamegrfc8174eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129993hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYà∂grankingÓcrfcd8174irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex9Ambiguity of Uppercase vs Lowercase in RFC 2119 Key WordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in internets based on TCP or IP. In particular, it defines objects for managing networks using the Precision Time Protocol (PTP), specified in IEEE Std. 1588-2008.

This memo specifies a MIB module in a manner that is both compliant to the Structure of Management Information version 2 (SMIv2) and semantically identical to the peer SMIv1 definitions.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoV. Shankarkumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejL. Montini¢kaffiliationuCalnex Solutions Ltd.dnamehT. Frost¢kaffiliationiMicrosemidnamegG. Dowdddatejœ}hfilenamegrfc8173eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftictocdfullx8tictoc - Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdnamex/Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdtypebwgbidjdoc-129992hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateYMè≠grankingÌcrfcd8173irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEPrecision Time Protocol Version 2 (PTPv2) Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*The Benchmarking Methodology Working Group has traditionally conducted laboratory characterization of dedicated physical implementations of internetworking functions.  This memo investigates additional considerations when network functions are virtualized and performed in general-purpose hardware.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatejœ|hfilenamegrfc8172eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-129991hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYxAgrankingÏcrfcd8172irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRConsiderations for Benchmarking Virtual Network Functions and Their InfrastructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis document describes mechanisms for providing directory service to TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) edge switches.  The directory information provided can be used in reducing multi-destination traffic, particularly ARP / Neighbor Discovery (ND) and unknown unicast flooding.  It can also be used to detect traffic with forged source addresses.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Liddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8171eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129990hkeywordsÑdPushdPulleESADIeES-ISkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateYI*…grankingÎcrfcd8171irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Edge Directory Assistance MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty>Over the many years since the introduction of the Internet Protocol, we have seen a number of transitions throughout the protocol stack, such as deploying a new protocol, or updating or replacing an existing protocol.  Many protocols and technologies were not designed to enable smooth transition to alternatives or to easily deploy extensions; thus, some transitions, such as the introduction of IPv6, have been difficult.  This document attempts to summarize some basic principles to enable future transitions, and it also summarizes what makes for a good transition plan.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8170eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129989hkeywordsÅotransition plankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYß§grankingÍcrfcd8170irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex9Planning for Protocol Adoption and Subsequent TransitionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document specifies a new Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) for Residence Time Measurement (RTM) and describes how it can be used by time synchronization protocols within an MPLS domain.

Residence time is the variable part of the propagation delay of timing and synchronization messages; knowing this delay for each message allows for a more accurate determination of the delay to be taken into account when applying the value included in a Precision Time Protocol event message.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationiZTE Corp.dnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejS. Ruffini¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamemA. Vainshteinddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8169eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129988hkeywordsÉeG-AChmResident TimedMPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY/êrgrankingÈcrfcd8169irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Residence Time Measurement in MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation allows a client to include a prefix-length hint value in the IA_PD option to indicate a preference for the size of the prefix to be delegated, but it is unclear about how the client and server should act in different situations involving the prefix-length hint.  This document provides a summary of the existing problems with the prefix-length hint and guidance on what the client and server could do in different situations.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Liu¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cuiddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8168eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129987hkeywordsàfDHCPv6x#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocoldIPv6fPrefixqPrefix DelegationmPrefix LengthdHintrAddress AllocationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateY/BmgrankingËcrfcd8168irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex DHCPv6 Prefix-Length Hint IssuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeMinor versions of Network File System (NFS) version 4 newer than minor version 0 work best when Remote Procedure Call (RPC) transports can send RPC transactions in both directions on the same connection.  This document describes how RPC transport endpoints capable of Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) convey RPCs in both directions on a single connection.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehC. Leverddatej÷␀hfilenamegrfc8167eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129986hkeywordsÇmNFS-over-RDMAmRPC-over-RDMAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYV÷grankingÁcrfcd8167irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Bidirectional Remote Procedure Call on RPC-over-RDMA TransportsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis document specifies a protocol for conveying Remote Procedure Call (RPC) messages on physical transports capable of Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA).  This protocol is referred to as the RPC-over- RDMA version 1 protocol in this document.  It requires no revision to application RPC protocols or the RPC protocol itself.  This document obsoletes RFC 5666.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehC. Lever¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejW. Simpson¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiT. Talpeyddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8166eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129985hkeywordsÅmRPC-over-RDMAkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateYV÷xgrankingÊcrfcd8166irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIRemote Direct Memory Access Transport for Remote Procedure Call Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8797∂habstracty{The IAB published RFC 7624 in response to several revelations of pervasive attacks on Internet communications.  This document considers the implications of protocol designs that associate metadata with encrypted flows.  In particular, it asserts that designs that share metadata only by explicit actions at the host are preferable to designs in which middleboxes insert metadata.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Hardieddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8165eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129984hkeywordsÑlsurveillanceeproxyhproxyingimiddleboxkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYÊxgrankingÂcrfcd8165irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Design Considerations for Metadata InsertiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÈThis document describes how "http" URIs can be accessed using Transport Layer Security (TLS) and HTTP/2 to mitigate pervasive monitoring attacks.  This mechanism not a replacement for "https" URIs; it is vulnerable to active attacks.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8164eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129983hkeywordsÇvOpportunistic SecuritydHTTPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateY·Ægranking‰crfcd8164irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Opportunistic Security for HTTP/2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMMaster-Slave/Token-Passing (MS/TP) is a medium access control method for the RS-485 physical layer and is used primarily in building automation networks.  This specification defines the frame format for transmission of IPv6 packets and the method of forming link-local and statelessly autoconfigured IPv6 addresses on MS/TP networks.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlVerizon LabsdnamegK. Lynn¢kaffiliationpJohnson ControlsdnamekJ. Martocci¢kaffiliationnDelta ControlsdnamejC. Neilson¢kaffiliationiHoneywelldnamelS. Donaldsonddatejœ{hfilenamegrfc8163eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129982hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYFΩgranking„crfcd8163irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexETransmission of IPv6 over Master-Slave/Token-Passing (MS/TP) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxŸThis document describes how to use secure DNS to associate an S/MIME user's certificate with the intended domain name, similar to the way that DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE), RFC 6698, does for TLS.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationhKirei ABdnamekJ. Schlyterddatejœ{hfilenamegrfc8162eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129981hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY/Bkgranking‚crfcd8162irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGUsing Secure DNS to Associate Certificates with Domain Names for S/MIMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∫This document provides benchmarking procedures for the Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP).  It also proposes metrics by which an NDP implementation's scaling capabilities can be measured.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamejW. Cerveny¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Thomasddatej÷␀hfilenamegrfc8161eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-129980hkeywordsÉdIPv6gScalingcNDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY-zgranking·crfcd8161irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Benchmarking the Neighbor Discovery ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx’This document specifies a new opcode in the Secure Shell terminal modes encoding.  The new opcode describes the widely used IUTF8 terminal mode bit, which indicates that terminal I/O uses UTF-8 character encoding.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationePuTTYdnameiS. Tatham¢kaffiliationgOpenSSHdnameiD. Tuckerddatej‘¸hfilenamegrfc8160eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129979hkeywordsÇlSecure ShellcSSHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX¯M⁄granking‡crfcd8160irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)IUTF8 Terminal Mode in Secure Shell (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This document describes a tunnel encapsulation for Ethernet over IPv6 with a mandatory 64-bit cookie for connecting Layer 2 (L2) Ethernet attachment circuits identified by IPv6 addresses.  The encapsulation is based on the Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3 (L2TPv3) over IP and does not use the L2TPv3 control plane.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamerM. Konstantynowicz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehG. Heron¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamemR. Schatzmayr¢kaffiliationtAlcatel-Lucent, Inc.dnamemW. Henderickxddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8159eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129978hkeywordsÇfL2TPv3jpseudowirekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYFºgrankingﬂcrfcd8159irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqKeyed IPv6 TunneldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√Network operators require NAT devices to log events like creation and deletion of translations and information about the resources that the NAT device is managing.  In many cases, the logs are essential to identify an attacker or a host that was used to launch malicious attacks and for various other purposes of accounting.  Since there is no standard way of logging this information, different NAT devices use proprietary formats; hence, it is difficult to expect consistent behavior.  This lack of standardization makes it difficult to write the Collector applications that would receive this data and process it to present useful information.  This document describes the formats for logging NAT events.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Pennoddatej’˛hfilenamegrfc8158eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129977hkeywordsÅhtemplatekobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateZ:ﬂùgrankingﬁcrfcd8158irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNIP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Elements for Logging NAT EventsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyáThere is an emerging demand for solutions that provide redundancy and load-sharing across wired and cellular links from a single Service Provider, so that a single subscriber is provided with bonded access to heterogeneous connections at the same time.

In this document, GRE (Generic Routing Encapsulation) Tunnel Bonding is specified as an enabling approach for bonded access to a wired and a wireless network in customer premises, e.g., homes. In GRE Tunnel Bonding, two GRE tunnels, one per network connection, are set up and bonded together to form a single GRE tunnel for a subscriber. Compared with each subconnection, the bonded connections promise increased access capacity and improved reliability. The solution described in this document is currently implemented by Huawei and deployed by Deutsche Telekom AG. This document will enable other developers to build interoperable implementations.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamelC. Heidemann¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnameiM. Cullenddatej÷␀hfilenamegrfc8157eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129976hkeywordsÜmHybrid AccessuBandwidth AggregationnBonding TunnelkGRE ChannelxHybrid Access Aggregation PointlHome GatewaykobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateYFªgranking›crfcd8157irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex$Huawei's GRE Tunnel Bonding ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√DHCPv6 as defined in "Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)" (RFC 3315) does not offer server redundancy.  This document defines a protocol implementation to provide DHCPv6 failover, a mechanism for running two servers with the capability for either server to take over clients' leases in case of server failure or network partition.  It meets the requirements for DHCPv6 failover detailed in "DHCPv6 Failover Requirements" (RFC 7031).fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalski¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejK. Kinnearddatej’ˇhfilenamegrfc8156eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129975hkeywordsÇfDHCPv6hFailoverkobsoletedByÄepages`opublicationDateY9 8granking‹crfcd8156irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDHCPv6 Failover ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄCurrent Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) server discovery mechanisms are relatively static and limited to explicit configuration. These are usually under the administrative control of the application or TURN service provider, and not the enterprise, ISP, or the network in which the client is located. Enterprises and ISPs wishing to provide their own TURN servers need auto-discovery mechanisms that a TURN client could use with minimal or no configuration. This document describes three such mechanisms for TURN server discovery.

This document updates RFC 5766 to relax the requirement for mutual authentication in certain cases.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Patil¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Wingddatejœshfilenamegrfc8155eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtramdfullx"tram - TURN Revised and ModernizeddnamexTURN Revised and Modernizeddtypebwgbidjdoc-129974hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX˜˛úgranking€crfcd8155irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) Server Auto DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8The Parallel Network File System (pNFS) allows a separation between the metadata (onto a metadata server) and data (onto a storage device) for a file.  The Small Computer System Interface (SCSI) layout type is defined in this document as an extension to pNFS to allow the use of SCSI-based block storage devices.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Hellwigddatej’Îhfilenamegrfc8154eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129973hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY	3√granking⁄crfcd8154irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAParallel NFS (pNFS) Small Computer System Interface (SCSI) LayoutdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyèThe RFC Editor is both the publisher and the archivist for the RFC Series.  This document applies specifically to the archivist role of the RFC Editor.  It provides guidance on when and how to preserve RFCs and describes the tools required to view or re-create RFCs as necessary.  This document also highlights gaps in the current process and suggests compromises to balance cost with best practice.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekH. Flanaganddatej’ˇhfilenamegrfc8153eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129972hkeywordsÇgarchiveiarchivingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX˜˛õgrankingŸcrfcd8153irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex6Digital Preservation Considerations for the RFC SeriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) is a data format designed for small code size and small message size.  There is a need for the ability to have basic security services defined for this data format.  This document defines the CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) protocol.  This specification describes how to create and process signatures, message authentication codes, and encryption using CBOR for serialization.  This specification additionally describes how to represent cryptographic keys using CBOR.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaadddatej’Îhfilenamegrfc8152eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcosedfullx)cose - CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex"CBOR Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129971hkeywordsÉdCoAPcECCnElliptic CurvekobsoletedByÇd9052d9053epagesyopublicationDateY]8xgrankingÿcrfcd8152irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxßThis document describes Network Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3) use cases that can be deployed in various data centers and serve different data-center applications.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegL. Yong¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamefM. Toy¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Isaac¢kaffiliationkNano Sec CodnameiV. Manralddatejœwhfilenamegrfc8151eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-129970hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY(K‚granking◊crfcd8151irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAUse Cases for Data Center Network Virtualization Overlay NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols.  In particular, it defines objects for managing Multiprotocol Label Switching - Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) linear protection.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamerS. Kingston Smiler¢kaffiliationqDell TechnologiesdnamemM. Venkatesan¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Ryooddatej’˛hfilenamegrfc8150eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129969hkeywordsÑrNetwork ManagementxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2kobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateX˜˛õgranking÷crfcd8150irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,MPLS Transport Profile Linear Protection MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèThe reoptimization of a Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Path (LSP) may be triggered based on the need to reoptimize an individual source-to-leaf (S2L) sub-LSP or a set of S2L sub-LSPs, both using the Sub-Group-based reoptimization method, or the entire P2MP-TE LSP tree using the Make-Before-Break (MBB) method.  This document discusses the application of the existing mechanisms for path reoptimization of loosely routed Point-to-Point (P2P) TE LSPs to the P2MP-TE LSPs, identifies issues in doing so, and defines procedures to address them.  When reoptimizing a large number of S2L sub-LSPs in a tree using the Sub-Group-based reoptimization method, the S2L sub-LSP descriptor list may need to be semantically fragmented.  This document defines the notion of a fragment identifier to help recipient nodes unambiguously reconstruct the fragmented S2L sub-LSP descriptor list.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegT. Saad¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationx"Defense Information Systems AgencydnamejR. Venator¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnameiY. Kamiteddatej’¯hfilenamegrfc8149eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129968hkeywordsÜrRSVP fragmentationxRSVP fragment identifierxP2MP-TE tree reoptimizationxP2MP-TE tree re-evaluationwPreferable P2MP-TE treetInter-domain P2MP-TEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX˜˛ögranking’crfcd8149irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxRSVP Extensions for Reoptimization of Loosely Routed Point-to-Multipoint Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis document describes how to use IP-based emergency services mechanisms to support the next generation of emergency calls placed by vehicles (automatically in the event of a crash or serious incident, or manually invoked by a vehicle occupant) and conveying vehicle, sensor, and location data related to the crash or incident. Such calls are often referred to as "Automatic Crash Notification" (ACN), or "Advanced Automatic Crash Notification" (AACN), even in the case of manual trigger. The "Advanced" qualifier refers to the ability to carry a richer set of data.

This document also registers a MIME media type and Emergency Call Data Type for the vehicle, sensor, and location data (often referred to as "crash data" even though there is not necessarily a crash) and an INFO package to enable carrying this and related data in SIP INFO requests. An external specification for the data format, contents, and structure is referenced in this document.

This document reuses the technical aspects of next-generation Pan- European eCall (a mandated and standardized system for emergency calls by in-vehicle systems (IVSs) within Europe and other regions). However, this document specifies use of a different set of vehicle (crash) data, specifically, the Vehicle Emergency Data Set (VEDS) rather than the eCall Minimum Set of Data (MSD). This document is an extension of the IETF eCall document, with the primary differences being that this document makes the MSD data set optional and VEDS mandatory, and it adds attribute values to the metadata/control object to permit greater functionality. This document registers a new INFO package (identical to that registered for eCall but with the addition of the VEDS MIME type). This document also describes legacy (circuit-switched) ACN systems and their migration to next-generation emergency calling, to provide background information and context.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxCore Technology ConsultingdnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationmNeustar, Inc.dnamehB. Rosen¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’˚hfilenamegrfc8148eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129967hkeywordsèiemergencydcallecallsnemergency calloemergency callsgvehiclecacndaacnxautomatic crash notificationx automatic collision notificationx%advanced automatic crash notificationx)advanced automatic collision notificationecrashqvehicle-initiatedeecallkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateY÷ˆgranking‘crfcd8148irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Next-Generation Vehicle-Initiated Emergency CallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis document describes how to use IP-based emergency services mechanisms to support the next generation of the Pan-European in-vehicle emergency call service defined under the eSafety initiative of the European Commission (generally referred to as "eCall"). eCall is a standardized and mandated system for a special form of emergency calls placed by vehicles, providing real-time communications and an integrated set of related data.

This document also registers MIME media types and an Emergency Call Data Type for the eCall vehicle data and metadata/control data, and an INFO package to enable carrying this data in SIP INFO requests.

Although this specification is designed to meet the requirements of next-generation Pan-European eCall (NG-eCall), it is specified generically such that the technology can be reused or extended to suit requirements across jurisdictions.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxCore Technology ConsultingdnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’¸hfilenamegrfc8147eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129966hkeywordséiemergencydcallecallsnemergency calloemergency callsgvehiclecacndaacnxautomatic crash notificationx automatic collision notificationx%advanced automatic crash notificationx)advanced automatic collision notificationecrashqvehicle-initiatedkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateY÷ıgranking”crfcd8147irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Next-Generation Pan-European eCalldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌEAP-pwd is an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) method that utilizes a shared password for authentication using a technique that is resistant to dictionary attacks.  It includes support for raw keys and double hashing of a password in the style of Microsoft Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol version 2 (MSCHAPv2), but it does not include support for salted passwords.  There are many existing databases of salted passwords, and it is desirable to allow their use with EAP-pwd.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmHP EnterprisednamejD. Harkinsddatej’ˇhfilenamegrfc8146eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129965hkeywordsÑx#Password-Authenticated Key ExchangedPAKEqDictionary AttackrAuthentication EAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX˙ùggranking“crfcd8146irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Adding Support for Salted Password Databases to EAP-pwddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4The DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) were developed to provide origin authentication and integrity protection for DNS data by using digital signatures.  These digital signatures can be verified by building a chain of trust starting from a trust anchor and proceeding down to a particular node in the DNS.  This document specifies two different ways for validating resolvers to signal to a server which keys are referenced in their chain of trust.  The data from such signaling allow zone administrators to monitor the progress of rollovers in a DNSSEC-signed zone.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejD. Wessels¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej’˝hfilenamegrfc8145eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129964hkeywordsÉcDNSfDNSSEClTrust AnchorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÌc-granking—crfcd8145irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDSignaling Trust Anchor Knowledge in DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstracty(This document defines how the Prefer header field (RFC 7240) can be used by a Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) client to request that certain behaviors be employed by a server while constructing a response to a request.  Furthermore, it defines the new "depth-noroot" preference.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcCMUdnamelK. Murchisonddatej’˛hfilenamegrfc8144eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129963hkeywordsÑdhttpfpreferewebavfcaldavkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX·Wgranking–crfcd8144irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSUse of the Prefer Header Field in Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides recommendations for improving the security of the Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) when using Transport Layer Security (TLS).  It modernizes the NNTP usage of TLS to be consistent with TLS best current practices.  This document updates RFC 4642.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Elieddatej’˚hfilenamegrfc8143eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129962hkeywordsÖdNNTPfUsenetgNetNewscTLShSTARTTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX˙ﬁ`grankingœcrfcd8143irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOUsing Transport Layer Security (TLS) with Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document describes the GeoJSON text sequence format and "application/geo+json-seq" media type.  This format is based on JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) text sequences and GeoJSON, and it makes arbitrarily large geographic datasets incrementally parseable without restricting the form of GeoJSON texts within a sequence.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfMapboxdnamejS. Gilliesddatej’˝hfilenamegrfc8142eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeojsondfullxgeojson - Geographic JSONdnameoGeographic JSONdtypebwgbidjdoc-129961hkeywordsÉdJSONjGeospatialxJavaScript Object NotationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY]"grankingŒcrfcd8142irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevGeoJSON Text SequencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıA Uniform Resource Name (URN) is a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) that is assigned under the "urn" URI scheme and a particular URN namespace, with the intent that the URN will be a persistent, location-independent resource identifier.  With regard to URN syntax, this document defines the canonical syntax for URNs (in a way that is consistent with URI syntax), specifies methods for determining URN-equivalence, and discusses URI conformance.  With regard to URN namespaces, this document specifies a method for defining a URN namespace and associating it with a namespace identifier, and it describes procedures for registering namespace identifiers with the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).  This document obsoletes both RFCs 2141 and 3406.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhFilamentdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej’ˇhfilenamegrfc8141eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfurnbisdfullx(urnbis - Uniform Resource Names, ReviseddnamexUniform Resource Names, Reviseddtypebwgbidjdoc-129960hkeywordsÑuUniform Resource NamecURNxUniform Resource IdentifiercURIkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateY]grankingÕcrfcd8141irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUniform Resource Names (URNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¥Ever since Gutenberg discovered and patented ASCII and the corresponding "Courier New" font with its now-famous "ten" point size, artisans and artificers have striven to represent their views of the world in print.

Similarly, starting from Darwin's discovery of the hippogriff and his subsequent registration of the creature as an International Trade Mark, men (and some women) have struggled to catalog the fabulous variety that is called "nature".

This document supplies a number of representations of all manner of things (both elemental and hypothetical) supplied by some of our best collectors of curios and delivered in a manner that may well be reused by the cunning document author.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. FarrelddatejœÄhfilenamegrfc8140eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-76839hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX‡ôgrankingÃcrfcd8140irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexÖThe Arte of ASCII: Or, An True and Accurate Representation of an Menagerie of Thynges Fabulous and Wonderful in Ye Forme of CharacterdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoTRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) supports multi-access LAN (Local Area Network) links where a single link can have multiple end stations and TRILL switches attached.  Where multiple TRILL switches are attached to a link, native traffic to and from end stations on that link is handled by a subset of those TRILL switches called "Appointed Forwarders" as originally specified in RFC 6325, with the intent that native traffic in each VLAN be handled by at most one TRILL switch.  This document clarifies and updates the Appointed Forwarder mechanism.  It updates RFCs 6325 and 7177 and obsoletes RFC 6439.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamehM. Umair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekA. Banerjee¢kaffiliationcZTEdnameeF. Huddatej÷hfilenamegrfc8139eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129959hkeywordsÜcDRBlVLAN mappingjinhibitionmport shutdownetrillx,TRansparent Interconnection of Lots of LinkskobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateY:+∑grankingÀcrfcd8139irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Appointed ForwardersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This specification introduces a new IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) dispatch type for use in 6LoWPAN route-over topologies, which initially covers the needs of Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) data packet compression (RFC 6550).  Using this dispatch type, this specification defines a method to compress the RPL Option (RFC 6553) information and Routing Header type 3 (RFC 6554), an efficient IP-in-IP technique, and is extensible for more applications.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationnUni Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationnIMT AtlantiquednamejL. Toutain¢kaffiliationcARMdnameiR. Cragieddatejœshfilenamegrfc8138eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129958hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateXÌc)granking crfcd8138irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKIPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) Routing HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9008d9914d9035∂habstractytIEEE Std 802.15.4 defines Information Elements (IEs) that can be used to extend 802.15.4 in an interoperable manner.  The IEEE 802.15 Assigned Numbers Authority (ANA) manages the registry of the Information Elements.  This document formulates a request for ANA to allocate a number from that registry for the IETF and describes how the IE is formatted to provide subtypes.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationuKinney Consulting LLCdnameiP. Kinneyddatej‘¸hfilenamegrfc8137eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129957hkeywordsÅbIEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY)“granking…crfcd8137irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.IEEE 802.15.4 Information Element for the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxñThis document proposes an additional mechanism intended to both facilitate transition from IPv4 to IPv6 and improve the latter's security and privacy.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. HindenddatejœÄhfilenamegrfc8136eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-76838hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX‡ògranking»crfcd8136irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,Additional Transition Functionality for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty	The 128-bit length of IPv6 addresses (RFC 4291) allows for new and innovative address schemes that can adapt to the challenges of today's complex network world.  It also allows for new and improved security measures and supports advanced cloud computing challenges.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnNet Insight ABdnamelM. Danielson¢kaffiliationuBesserwisser NetworksdnamejM. NilssonddatejœÄhfilenamegrfc8135eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-76837hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX‡ógranking«crfcd8135irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexComplex Addressing in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This document is a collection of implementation reports from vendors, consortiums, and researchers who have implemented one or more of the standards published from the IETF INCident Handling (INCH) and Management Incident Lightweight Exchange (MILE) working groups.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcCMUdnameiC. Inacio¢kaffiliationfUTokyodnamekD. Miyamotoddatej’¸hfilenamegrfc8134eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-129956hkeywordsÜeIODEFcRIDcSCIdINCHdMILEnImplementationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYß¢granking∆crfcd8134irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEManagement Incident Lightweight Exchange (MILE) Implementation ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÀThis document describes the Security Evaluated Standardized Password- Authenticated Key Exchange (SESPAKE) protocol.  The SESPAKE protocol provides password-authenticated key exchange for usage in systems for protection of sensitive information.  The security proofs of the protocol were made for situations involving an active adversary in the channel, including man-in-the-middle (MitM) attacks and attacks based on the impersonation of one of the subjects.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamemS. Smyshlyaev¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamekE. Alekseev¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnameiI. Oshkin¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamehV. Popovddatej’¸hfilenamegrfc8133eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129955hkeywordsÉlcryptographynsecure channelnelliptic curvekobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateX›{©granking≈crfcd8133irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexZThe Security Evaluated Standardized Password-Authenticated Key Exchange (SESPAKE) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe methods defined in RFC 7252 for the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) only allow access to a complete resource, not to parts of a resource. In case of resources with larger or complex data, or in situations where resource continuity is required, replacing or requesting the whole resource is undesirable. Several applications using CoAP need to access parts of the resources.

This specification defines the new CoAP methods, FETCH, PATCH, and iPATCH, which are used to access and update parts of a resource.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameoP. van der Stok¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationlNAVOMI, Inc.dnameiA. Sehgalddatej’˘hfilenamegrfc8132eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129954hkeywordsÅdCoAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÊœ™grankingƒcrfcd8132irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGPATCH and FETCH Methods for the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn non-packet transport networks, there are requirements where the Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) end-to-end recovery scheme needs to employ a restoration Label Switched Path (LSP) while keeping resources for the working and/or protecting LSPs reserved in the network after the failure occurs.

This document reviews how the LSP association is to be provided using Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling in the context of a GMPLS end-to-end recovery scheme when using restoration LSP where failed LSP is not torn down. In addition, this document discusses resource sharing-based setup and teardown of LSPs as well as LSP reversion procedures. No new signaling extensions are defined by this document, and it is strictly informative in nature.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehH. Zheng¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Gandhi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationlADVA OpticaldnamemP. Brzozowskiddatej’˙hfilenamegrfc8131eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129953hkeywordsÜrAssociation ObjectmLSP ReversionlLSP RecoverywGMPLS Make-Before-BreakiGMPLS 1+RkGMPLS 1+1+RkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX”Ügranking√crfcd8131irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRSVP-TE Signaling Procedure for End-to-End GMPLS Restoration and Resource SharingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the RTP payload format for the Mixed Excitation Linear Prediction Enhanced (MELPe) speech coder.  MELPe's three different speech encoding rates and sample frame sizes are supported.  Comfort noise procedures and packet loss concealment are described in detail.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxVOCAL Technologies, Ltd.dnamemV. Demjanenko¢kaffiliationxVOCAL Technologies, Ltd.dnamelD. Satterleeddatej’¸hfilenamegrfc8130eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129952hkeywordsádMELPeMELPehMELP2400hMELP1200gMELP600hSCIP-210gSCIP210kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX›{®granking¬crfcd8130irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTRTP Payload Format for the Mixed Excitation Linear Prediction Enhanced (MELPe) CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis document updates RFC 4120, as it specifies an extension in the Kerberos protocol.  It defines a new authorization data type, AD-AUTHENTICATION-INDICATOR.  The purpose of introducing this data type is to include an indicator of the strength of a client's authentication in service tickets so that application services can use it as an input into policy decisions.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlGeorgia TechdnamegA. Jain¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnameiN. Kinder¢kaffiliationmRed Hat, Inc.dnamekN. McCallumddatejœshfilenamegrfc8129eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129951hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX”granking¡crfcd8129irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Authentication Indicator in Kerberos TicketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxÄThis memo outlines the process by which the IETF makes an appointment to the ICANN Root Zone Evolution Review Committee (RZERC).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcAMSdnameiC. Morganddatejœuhfilenamegrfc8128eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129950hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX¬·Sgranking¿crfcd8128irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexNIETF Appointment Procedures for the ICANN Root Zone Evolution Review CommitteedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This specification defines a new extension, LMA-Controlled-MAG-Session-Params, to Proxy Mobile IPv6.  This option can be used by the local mobility anchor (LMA) in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain for signaling a mobile access gateway (MAG) on enforcing specific values for various configuration parameters such as heartbeat and binding refresh parameters.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Patki¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationtSangmyung UniversitydnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeQ. Fu¢kaffiliationfSprintdnamehL. Bertzddatej÷	hfilenamegrfc8127eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129949hkeywordsÇoBinding RefreshiHeartbeatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateYßÜgrankingøcrfcd8127irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVMobile Access Gateway Configuration Parameters Controlled by the Local Mobility AnchordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyñMany protocols make use of points of extensibility that use constants to identify various protocol parameters. To ensure that the values in these fields do not have conflicting uses and to promote interoperability, their allocations are often coordinated by a central record keeper. For IETF protocols, that role is filled by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).

To make assignments in a given registry prudently, guidance describing the conditions under which new values should be assigned, as well as when and how modifications to existing values can be made, is needed. This document defines a framework for the documentation of these guidelines by specification authors, in order to assure that the provided guidance for the IANA Considerations is clear and addresses the various issues that are likely in the operation of a registry.

This is the third edition of this document; it obsoletes RFC 5226.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcPTIdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiT. Nartenddatej’˝hfilenamegrfc8126eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129948hkeywordsáx#internet assigned numbers authorityfvaluesoimplementationsjcode pointqprotocol constantrprotocol parametericodepointkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateYI¬grankingæcrfcd8126irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex=Guidelines for Writing an IANA Considerations Section in RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9907¥habstractyùPassword-Authenticated Key Agreement (PAKE) schemes are interactive protocols that allow the participants to authenticate each other and derive shared cryptographic keys using a (weaker) shared password.  This document reviews different types of PAKE schemes.  Furthermore, it presents requirements and gives recommendations to designers of new schemes.  It is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxsecunet Security NetworksdnamejJ. Schmidtddatej’¸hfilenamegrfc8125eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-129947hkeywordsÑhPasswordmKey Agreementx$Password-Authenticated Key AgreementvCryptographic ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateX˜˛ògrankingΩcrfcd8125irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexDRequirements for Password-Authenticated Key Agreement (PAKE) SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˇThe WebSocket protocol enables bidirectional real-time communication between clients and servers in web-based applications.  This document specifies extensions to Session Description Protocol (SDP) for application protocols using WebSocket as a transport.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoR. Ravindranath¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiroddatej’˚hfilenamegrfc8124eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgbfcpbisdfullx+bfcpbis - Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdnamex!Binary Floor Control Protocol Bisdtypebwgbidjdoc-129946hkeywordsÇpSecure WebSocketxUniform Resource IdentifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX›{ßgrankingºcrfcd8124irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIThe Session Description Protocol (SDP) WebSocket Connection URI AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPSIP networks use signaling monitoring tools to debug customer- reported problems and for regression testing if network or client software is upgraded. As networks grow and become interconnected, including connection via transit networks, it becomes impractical to predict the path that SIP signaling will take between clients and, therefore, impractical to monitor SIP signaling end-to-end.

This document describes the requirements for adding an indicator to the SIP Protocol Data Unit (PDU) or a SIP message that marks the PDU as a candidate for logging. Such a marking will typically be applied as part of network testing controlled by the network operator and not used in regular client signaling. However, such a marking can be carried end-to-end, including the SIP terminals, even if a session originates and terminates in different networks.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVodafone GroupdnamehP. Dawes¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenC. Arunachalamddatej’˙hfilenamegrfc8123eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymginsipiddfullx*insipid - INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdnamex INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdtypebwgbidjdoc-129945hkeywordsÑelogmeotroubleshootingedebuggloggingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX”~grankingªcrfcd8123irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Requirements for Marking SIP Messages to be LoggeddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis document specifies how to establish secure connection-oriented media transport sessions over the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol using the Session Description Protocol (SDP). It defines the SDP protocol identifier, 'TCP/TLS'. It also defines the syntax and semantics for an SDP 'fingerprint' attribute that identifies the certificate that will be presented for the TLS session. This mechanism allows media transport over TLS connections to be established securely, so long as the integrity of session descriptions is assured.

This document obsoletes RFC 4572 by clarifying the usage of multiple fingerprints.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej’˘hfilenamegrfc8122eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129944hkeywordsÖcSDPcTLSkFingerprinteOfferfAnswerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÃmVgranking∫crfcd8122irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~Connection-Oriented Media Transport over the Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8844∂habstractxèThis document specifies cryptographic algorithms for use with the Mutual user authentication method for the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnamegY. Oiwa¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnamekH. Watanabe¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnameiH. Takagi¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehK. Maeda¢kaffiliationgLepidumdnamejT. Hayashi¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamegY. Iokuddatej’Íhfilenamegrfc8121eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhhttpauthdfullx5httpauth - Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdnamex*Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129943hkeywordsÇdHTTPnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÌc(grankingπcrfcd8121irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoMutual Authentication Protocol for HTTP: Cryptographic Algorithms Based on the Key Agreement Mechanism 3 (KAM3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛This document specifies an authentication scheme for the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) that is referred to as either the Mutual authentication scheme or the Mutual authentication protocol.  This scheme provides true mutual authentication between an HTTP client and an HTTP server using password-based authentication.  Unlike the Basic and Digest authentication schemes, the Mutual authentication scheme specified in this document assures the user that the server truly knows the user's encrypted password.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnamegY. Oiwa¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnamekH. Watanabe¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnameiH. Takagi¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehK. Maeda¢kaffiliationgLepidumdnamejT. Hayashi¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamegY. Iokuddatej’hfilenamegrfc8120eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhhttpauthdfullx5httpauth - Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdnamex*Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129942hkeywordsÇdHTTPnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateXÌc&granking∏crfcd8120irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Mutual Authentication Protocol for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬RFC 4458 (regarding SIP URIs for applications) defines a "cause" URI parameter, which may appear in the Request-URI of a SIP request, that is used to indicate a reason why the request arrived to the User Agent Server (UAS) receiving the message.  This document updates RFC 4458 by creating a new predefined value for the "cause" URI parameter to cover service number translation for cases of retargeting due to specific service action leading to the translation of a called service access number.  This document also provides guidance, which was missing in RFC 4458, for using the "cause" URI parameter within the History-Info header field, since this use is mandatory in some IP networks' implementations.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednameiM. Mohali¢kaffiliationxMLB@Realtime CommunicationsdnameiM. Barnesddatej’˙hfilenamegrfc8119eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129941hkeywordsÅeCausekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX…◊¨granking∑crfcd8119irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8SIP "cause" URI Parameter for Service Number TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThe Portable Document Format (PDF) is an ISO standard (ISO 32000-1:2008) defining a final-form document representation language in use for document exchange, including on the Internet, since 1993.  This document provides an overview of the PDF format and updates the media type registration of "application/pdf".  It obsoletes RFC 3778.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxAdobe Systems IncorporateddnamehM. Hardy¢kaffiliationxAdobe Systems IncorporateddnamekL. Masinter¢kaffiliationxAdobe Systems IncorporateddnamekD. Markovic¢kaffiliationoPDF AssociationdnamejD. Johnson¢kaffiliationoGlobal GraphicsdnameiM. Baileyddatej’ˇhfilenamegrfc8118eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129940hkeywordsÇxPortable Document FormatiMIME typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÃmUgranking∂crfcd8118irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe application/pdf Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypGiving a hostname to your computer and publishing it as you roam from one network to another is the Internet's equivalent of walking around with a name tag affixed to your lapel. This current practice can significantly compromise your privacy, and something should change in order to mitigate these privacy threats.

There are several possible remedies, such as fixing a variety of protocols or avoiding disclosing a hostname at all. This document describes some of the protocols that reveal hostnames today and sketches another possible remedy, which is to replace static hostnames by frequently changing randomized values.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtPrivate Octopus Inc.dnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationx'University of Applied Sciences AugsburgdnameiR. Winterddatejœhhfilenamegrfc8117eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129939hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX¬·Ngrankingµcrfcd8117irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Current Hostname Practice Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|This document analyzes common security threats to the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2) and describes their potential impacts on Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) operations.  It also analyzes which of these security vulnerabilities can be mitigated when using the mandatory-to-implement security mechanisms for OLSRv2 and how the vulnerabilities are mitigated.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliation`dnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationsEcole PolytechniquednameeJ. Yiddatej’˜hfilenamegrfc8116eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129938hkeywordsÅeMANETkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY(Kﬂgranking¥crfcd8116irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPSecurity Threats to the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2 (OLSRv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˜This document defines a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol version 6 (DHCPv6) Option for multicast IPv4 service continuity solutions, which is used to carry the IPv6 prefixes to be used to build unicast and multicast IPv4-embedded IPv6 addresses.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefJ. Qin¢kaffiliationpPhilips LightingdnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationx!The University of New South WalesdnamegX. Dengddatej’˙hfilenamegrfc8115eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129937hkeywordsíhPREFIX64cSSMcASMpPrefix DiscoveryxIPv4-Converted IPv6 AddresseswIPv4 service continuityvIPv6 Address SynthesistAny-Source MulticastxSource-Specific MulticastcPIMxIPv4-IPv6 interconnectionoIPv4  over IPv6cA+PcMAPeMAP-Eoaddress-sharingcCGNeNAT64kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXÃmTgranking≥crfcd8115irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCDHCPv6 Option for IPv4-Embedded Multicast and Unicast IPv6 PrefixesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document specifies a solution for the delivery of IPv4 multicast services to IPv4 clients over an IPv6 multicast network.  The solution relies upon a stateless IPv4-in-IPv6 encapsulation scheme and uses an IPv6 multicast distribution tree to deliver IPv4 multicast traffic.  The solution is particularly useful for the delivery of multicast service offerings to customers serviced by Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite).fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefC. Qin¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenet¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegQ. Wangddatej’˘hfilenamegrfc8114eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129936hkeywordsóiMulticastgDS-LitexIPv4-IPv6 InterconnectionhPREFIX64cSSMcASMwIPv4 service continuityxMulticast service continuityiIPv6-onlysIPv6-only multicastcPIMcMLDdIGMPcA+PcMAPeMAP-Eoaddress-sharingcCGNeNAT64nIPv4 over IPv6vIPv6 Address SynthesistAny-Source MulticastxSource-Specific MulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÃmSgranking≤crfcd8114irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRDelivery of IPv4 Multicast Services to IPv4 Clients over an IPv6 Multicast NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document specifies a Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) shared message type for defining future extensions and conducting experiments without consuming a LISP packet type codepoint for each extension.  It also defines a registry for LISP Packet Type allocations, thus updating RFC 6830.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenetddatej’hfilenamegrfc8113eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129935hkeywordsávShared Experiment CodeoLISP codepointsuExperiment IdentifiermExperiment IDxLISP Experimental RegistrynLISP ExtensionnExtending LISPkobsoletedByÅd9304epagesopublicationDateX¬
zgranking±crfcd8113irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkLocator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP): Shared Extension Message & IANA Registry for Packet Type AllocationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyA simple tool called the Locator/ID Separation Protocol Delegated Database Tree (LISP-DDT) Referral Internet Groper (RIG), also referred to in this document as "rig", can be used to query the LISP- DDT hierarchy.  This document describes how the "rig" tool works.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegA. Jain¢kaffiliationfAristadnamekI. Kouvelas¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. LewisddatejŒ‚hfilenamegrfc8112eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129934hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY(Kﬁgranking∞crfcd8112irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex`Locator/ID Separation Protocol Delegated Database Tree (LISP-DDT) Referral Internet Groper (RIG)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document describes the Locator/ID Separation Protocol Delegated Database Tree (LISP-DDT), a hierarchical distributed database that embodies the delegation of authority to provide mappings from LISP Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs) to Routing Locators (RLOCs).  It is a statically defined distribution of the EID namespace among a set of LISP-speaking servers called "DDT nodes".  Each DDT node is configured as "authoritative" for one or more EID-prefixes, along with the set of RLOCs for Map-Servers or "child" DDT nodes to which more-specific EID-prefixes are delegated.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxVAF.NET Internet ConsultingdnameiV. Fuller¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Lewis¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejV. Ermagan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegA. Jain¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Smirnovddatej’Úhfilenamegrfc8111eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129933hkeywordsàdLISPcDDTcEIDgLocatornMapping SystemjMap-ServerlMap-ReferralhReferralkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateY(K‹grankingØcrfcd8111irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexALocator/ID Separation Protocol Delegated Database Tree (LISP-DDT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÑThis memo specifies an extension to IEEE Std 802.11 to provide for opportunistic (unauthenticated) encryption to the wireless media.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmHP EnterprisednamejD. Harkins¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej’˘hfilenamegrfc8110eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129932hkeywordsÅx!opportunistic encryption wirelesskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX›{•grankingÆcrfcd8110irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Opportunistic Wireless EncryptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9672∂habstractyThis document describes the queries that a DNS resolver should emit to initialize its cache.  The result is that the resolver gets both a current NS Resource Record Set (RRset) for the root zone and the necessary address information for reaching the root servers.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhDENIC eGdnamegP. Koch¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Larson¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatejœnhfilenamegrfc8109eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129931hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9609epagesopublicationDateX…◊´granking≠crfcd8109irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Initializing a DNS Resolver with Priming QueriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo expands and clarifies the behavior of Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) endpoints that use multiple synchronization sources (SSRCs).  This occurs, for example, when an endpoint sends multiple RTP streams in a single RTP session.  This memo updates RFC 3550 with regard to handling multiple SSRCs per endpoint in RTP sessions, with a particular focus on RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) behavior.  It also updates RFC 4585 to change and clarify the calculation of the timeout of SSRCs and the inclusion of feedback messages.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatejœhfilenamegrfc8108eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129930hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXø2granking¨crfcd8108irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Sending Multiple RTP Streams in a Single RTP SessiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÿAdvertising Digital Identifiers (Ad-IDs) are used to identify advertising assets across all media platforms.  This document defines the formal Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace Identifier (NID) "adid" for Ad-IDs.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx*Advertising Digital Identification (Ad-ID)dnamegJ. Woldddatejœkhfilenamegrfc8107eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129929hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX¿Ãagranking´crfcd8107irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Advertising Digital Identifier (Ad-ID) URN Namespace DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document specifies IPv6 Router Advertisement (RA) options (called "DNS RA options") to allow IPv6 routers to advertise a list of DNS Recursive Server Addresses and a DNS Search List to IPv6 hosts.

This document, which obsoletes RFC 6106, defines a higher default value of the lifetime of the DNS RA options to reduce the likelihood of expiry of the options on links with a relatively high rate of packet loss.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwSungkyunkwan UniversitydnamehJ. Jeong¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejL. Beloeil¢kaffiliationhNTT DatadnamenS. Madanapalliddatej’¯hfilenamegrfc8106eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129928hkeywordsÖkDNS ServicejDNS OptionxRecursive DNS Server AddressoDNS Search ListxStateless AutoconfigurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÃmQgranking™crfcd8106irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7IPv6 Router Advertisement Options for DNS ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(Digital Enhanced Cordless Telecommunications (DECT) Ultra Low Energy (ULE) is a low-power air interface technology that is proposed by the DECT Forum and is defined and specified by ETSI.

The DECT air interface technology has been used worldwide in communication devices for more than 20 years. It has primarily been used to carry voice for cordless telephony but has also been deployed for data-centric services.

DECT ULE is a recent addition to the DECT interface primarily intended for low-bandwidth, low-power applications such as sensor devices, smart meters, home automation, etc. As the DECT ULE interface inherits many of the capabilities from DECT, it benefits from operation that is long-range and interference-free, worldwide- reserved frequency band, low silicon prices, and maturity. There is an added value in the ability to communicate with IPv6 over DECT ULE, such as for Internet of Things applications.

This document describes how IPv6 is transported over DECT ULE using IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) techniques.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgRTX A/SdnamekP. Mariager¢kaffiliationgRTX A/SdnamekJ. Petersen¢kaffiliationcARMdnameiZ. Shelby¢kaffiliationtBosch Sensortec GmbHdnamenM. Van de Logt¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamejD. Barthelddatej’¯hfilenamegrfc8105eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129927hkeywordsÑg6LoWPANdETSIcIoTvand Internet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY	3¡granking©crfcd8105irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlTransmission of IPv6 Packets over Digital Enhanced Cordless Telecommunications (DECT) Ultra Low Energy (ULE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis document specifies a fast mechanism for protecting pseudowires (PWs) transported by IP/MPLS tunnels against egress endpoint failures, including egress attachment circuit (AC) failure, egress provider edge (PE) failure, multi-segment PW terminating PE failure, and multi-segment PW switching PE failure.  Operating on the basis of multihomed customer edge (CE), redundant PWs, upstream label assignment, and context-specific label switching, the mechanism enables local repair to be performed by the router upstream adjacent to a failure.  The router can restore a PW in the order of tens of milliseconds, by rerouting traffic around the failure to a protector through a pre-established bypass tunnel.  Therefore, the mechanism can be used to reduce traffic loss before global repair reacts to the failure and the network converges on the topology changes due to the failure.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegY. Shen¢kaffiliationlArktan, Inc.dnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamehY. Jiangddatej’¯hfilenamegrfc8104eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129926hkeywordsÖjpseudowirebPWjprotectionllocal repairlfast reroutekobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateX…◊§granking®crfcd8104irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Pseudowire (PW) Endpoint Fast Failure ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the conventions for using ChaCha20-Poly1305 Authenticated Encryption in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  ChaCha20-Poly1305 is an authenticated encryption algorithm constructed of the ChaCha stream cipher and Poly1305 authenticator.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatejœmhfilenamegrfc8103eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129925hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXµÿ	grankingßcrfcd8103irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZUsing ChaCha20-Poly1305 Authenticated Encryption in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe loop-free alternates (LFAs) computed following the current remote-LFA specification guarantees only link protection. The resulting remote-LFA next hops (also called "PQ-nodes") may not guarantee node protection for all destinations being protected by it.

This document describes an extension to the remote-loop-free-based IP fast reroute mechanisms that specifies procedures for determining whether or not a given PQ-node provides node protection for a specific destination. The document also shows how the same procedure can be utilized for the collection of complete characteristics for alternate paths. Knowledge about the characteristics of all alternate paths is a precursor to applying the operator-defined policy for eliminating paths not fitting the constraints.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnameiP. Sarkar¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnameiC. Bowers¢kaffiliationmRtBrick, Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowskiddatej’ˆhfilenamegrfc8102eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129924hkeywordsÑcLFAjRemote-LFAcIGPoNode ProtectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX¬
ygranking¶crfcd8102irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Remote-LFA Node Protection and ManageabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÚThis document creates additional Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Resource-Priority namespaces to meet the requirements of the 3GPP-defined Mission Critical Push To Talk (MCPTT) and places these namespaces in the corresponding IANA registry.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Axellddatej’˜hfilenamegrfc8101eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129923hkeywordsÜqResource-PriorityinamespaceqResource-priorithd3GPPcIMSeMCPTTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX”}granking•crfcd8101irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄIANA Registration of New Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Resource-Priority Namespace for Mission Critical Push To Talk ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document defines a limited common set of Diffserv Per-Hop Behaviors (PHBs) and Diffserv Codepoints (DSCPs) to be applied at (inter)connections of two separately administered and operated networks, and it explains how this approach can simplify network configuration and operation.  Many network providers operate Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) using Treatment Aggregates for traffic marked with different Diffserv Per-Hop Behaviors and use MPLS for interconnection with other networks.  This document offers a simple interconnection approach that may simplify operation of Diffserv for network interconnection among providers that use MPLS and apply the Short Pipe Model.  While motivated by the requirements of MPLS network operators that use Short Pipe Model tunnels, this document is applicable to other networks, both MPLS and non-MPLS.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geib¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamehD. Blackddatej’Úhfilenamegrfc8100eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129922hkeywordsÖhDiffservoInterconnectioncPHBsTreatment AggregateoMPLS Short PipekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX¬
ygranking§crfcd8100irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Diffserv-Interconnection Classes and PracticedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document presents a Topology-Transparent Zone (TTZ) in an OSPF area.  A TTZ comprises a group of routers and a number of links connecting these routers.  Any router outside of the zone is not aware of the zone.  A TTZ hides the internal topology of the TTZ from the outside.  It does not directly advertise any internal information about the TTZ to a router outside of the TTZ.  The information about the links and routers such as a link down inside the TTZ is not advertised to any router outside of the TTZ.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Chen¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeR. Li¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationhSockratednamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefZ. Liuddatej’Úhfilenamegrfc8099eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129921hkeywordsÉcIGPdOSPFcTTZkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX∂!øgranking£crfcd8099irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSPF Topology-Transparent ZonedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÕThis memo defines a MIME content type that may be used by a Mail User Agent (MUA) or electronic mail gateway to report the disposition of a message after it has been successfully delivered to a recipient. This content type is intended to be machine processable. Additional message header fields are also defined to permit Message Disposition Notifications (MDNs) to be requested by the sender of a message. The purpose is to extend Internet Mail to support functionality often found in other messaging systems, such as X.400 and the proprietary "LAN-based" systems, and are often referred to as "read receipts," "acknowledgements," or "receipt notifications." The intention is to do this while respecting privacy concerns, which have often been expressed when such functions have been discussed in the past.

Because many messages are sent between the Internet and other messaging systems (such as X.400 or the proprietary "LAN-based" systems), the MDN protocol is designed to be useful in a multiprotocol messaging environment. To this end, the protocol described in this memo provides for the carriage of "foreign" addresses, in addition to those normally used in Internet Mail. Additional attributes may also be defined to support "tunneling" of foreign notifications through Internet Mail.

This document is an Internet Standard. It obsoletes RFC 3798 and updates RFC 2046 (message/partial media type handling) and RFC 3461 (Original-Recipient header field generation requirement).fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’Ïhfilenamegrfc8098eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129920hkeywordsÇudelivery notificationcmdnkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateX∂!ægranking¢crfcd8098irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberUetotaletitlex Message Disposition NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document defines a new BGP opaque extended community to carry the origination Autonomous System (AS) validation state inside an autonomous system.  Internal BGP (IBGP) speakers that receive this validation state can configure local policies that allow it to influence their decision process.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpSproute NetworksdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationlArrcus, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative Japan, Inc.dnamegR. Bushddatejœjhfilenamegrfc8097eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129919hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX»¬sgranking°crfcd8097irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5BGP Prefix Origin Validation State Extended CommunitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn 2005-2006, the IPv6 MIB update group published updated versions of the IP-MIB, UDP-MIB, TCP-MIB, and IP-FORWARD-MIB modules, which use the InetAddressType/InetAddress construct to handle IPv4 and IPv6 in the same table.  This document contains versions of the obsoleted IPV6-MIB, IPV6-TC, IPV6-ICMP-MIB, IPV6-TCP-MIB, and IPV6-UDP-MIB modules for the purpose of updating MIB module repositories.  This document obsoletes RFCs 2452, 2454, 2465, and 2466 (i.e., the RFCs containing these MIBs) and reclassifies them as Historic.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuArista Networks, Inc.dnameiB. Fennerddatejœ`hfilenamegrfc8096eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129918hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateXı%xgranking†crfcd8096irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*The IPv6-Specific MIB Modules Are ObsoletedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document describes, surveys, and classifies the protocol mechanisms provided by existing IETF protocols, as background for determining a common set of transport services.  It examines the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP), Multipath TCP, the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP), the User Datagram Protocol (UDP), UDP-Lite, the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP), the Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP), the Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP), File Delivery over Unidirectional Transport / Asynchronous Layered Coding (FLUTE/ALC) for Reliable Multicast, NACK- Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM), Transport Layer Security (TLS), Datagram TLS (DTLS), and the Hypertext Transport Protocol (HTTP), when HTTP is used as a pseudotransport.  This survey provides background for the definition of transport services within the TAPS working group.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamemM. Kuehlewindddatej’Ìhfilenamegrfc8095eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtapsdfullxtaps - Transport ServicesdnamerTransport Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129917hkeywordséx#Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)uMultipath TCP (MPTCP)x+Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)xUser Datagram Protocol (UDP)hUDP-Litex+Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP)x(Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP)x"Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP)xjFile Delivery over Unidirectional Transport/Asynchronous Layered Coding (FLUTE/ALC) for Reliable Multicastx'NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM)xTransport Layer Security (TLS)sDatagram TLS (DTLS)x#Hypertext Transport Protocol (HTTP)dTAPSkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateXø2grankingücrfcd8095irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOServices Provided by IETF Transport Protocols and Congestion Control MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDNS queries and responses are visible to network elements on the path between the DNS client and its server. These queries and responses can contain privacy-sensitive information, which is valuable to protect.

This document proposes the use of Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) for DNS, to protect against passive listeners and certain active attacks. As latency is critical for DNS, this proposal also discusses mechanisms to reduce DTLS round trips and reduce the DTLS handshake size. The proposed mechanism runs over port 853.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Patilddatejœjhfilenamegrfc8094eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-129916hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX∂!Ωgrankingûcrfcd8094irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1DNS over Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxmThis document requests IANA to mark BGP path attribute values 30, 31, 129, 241, 242, and 243 as "Deprecated".fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijdersddatejœihfilenamegrfc8093eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129915hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX•Ógrankingùcrfcd8093irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGDeprecation of BGP Path Attribute Values 30, 31, 129, 241, 242, and 243dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document describes the BGP Large Communities attribute, an extension to BGP-4.  This attribute provides a mechanism to signal opaque information within separate namespaces to aid in routing management.  The attribute is suitable for use with all Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs) including four-octet ASNs.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehJ. Heitz¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationfArrcusdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationgEquinixdnamekI. Bagdonas¢kaffiliationdINEXdnamekN. Hilliardddatej’Òhfilenamegrfc8092eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129914hkeywordsÑcBGPelargekcommunitiesjfour-octetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX•Óygrankingúcrfcd8092irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGP Large Communities AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyStructured syntax suffixes for media types allow other media types to build on them and make it explicit that they are built on an existing media type as their foundation.  This specification defines and registers "+json-seq" as a structured syntax suffix for JSON text sequences.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCA TechnologiesdnamehE. Wildeddatejœghfilenamegrfc8091eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129913hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX•Óygrankingõcrfcd8091irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=A Media Type Structured Syntax Suffix for JSON Text SequencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxÔThis document outlines the procedures by which the IETF makes appointments to the Community Coordination Group (CCG), which provides advice and guidance to the IETF Trust in matters related to the IANA trademarks and the IANA domain names.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatejœghfilenamegrfc8090eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129912hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX•Órgrankingöcrfcd8090irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex]Appointment Procedures for the IETF Representatives to the Community Coordination Group (CCG)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis document provides a more complete specification of the "file" Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme and replaces the very brief definition in Section 3.10 of RFC 1738.

It defines a common syntax that is intended to interoperate across the broad spectrum of existing usages. At the same time, it notes some other current practices around the use of file URIs.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcQUTdnameiM. Kerwinddatej’Ôhfilenamegrfc8089eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129911hkeywordsÇxuniform resource identifiercURLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXßœ\grankingôcrfcd8089irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuThe "file" URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9844∂habstracty!This document contains information on how best to write an RTP payload format specification.  It provides reading tips, design practices, and practical tips on how to produce an RTP payload format specification quickly and with good results.  A template is also included with instructions.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej’Qhfilenamegrfc8088eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129910hkeywordsÉcRTPnPayload formatgProcesskobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateY	3øgrankingòcrfcd8088irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"How to Write an RTP Payload FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9751∂habstractygThe goal of this document is to describe the potential benefits of applications using a transport that enables Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN).  The document outlines the principal gains in terms of increased throughput, reduced delay, and other benefits when ECN is used over a network path that includes equipment that supports Congestion Experienced (CE) marking.  It also discusses challenges for successful deployment of ECN.  It does not propose new algorithms to use ECN nor does it describe the details of implementation of ECN in endpoint devices (Internet hosts), routers, or other network devices.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzlddatej’∑hfilenamegrfc8087eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129909hkeywordsÑcecncaqmdsctpctcpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXø2grankingócrfcd8087irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<The Benefits of Using Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document specifies a method of encapsulating network protocol packets within GRE and UDP headers.  This GRE-in-UDP encapsulation allows the UDP source port field to be used as an entropy field.  This may be used for load-balancing of GRE traffic in transit networks using existing Equal-Cost Multipath (ECMP) mechanisms.  There are two applicability scenarios for GRE-in-UDP with different requirements: (1) general Internet and (2) a traffic-managed controlled environment.  The controlled environment has less restrictive requirements than the general Internet.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegL. Yong¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiE. Crabbe¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeX. Xu¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnamejT. HerbertddatejœUhfilenamegrfc8086eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129908hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXø2grankingñcrfcd8086irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGRE-in-UDP EncapsulationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty≤The User Datagram Protocol (UDP) provides a minimal message-passing transport that has no inherent congestion control mechanisms. This document provides guidelines on the use of UDP for the designers of applications, tunnels, and other protocols that use UDP. Congestion control guidelines are a primary focus, but the document also provides guidance on other topics, including message sizes, reliability, checksums, middlebox traversal, the use of Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN), Differentiated Services Code Points (DSCPs), and ports.

Because congestion control is critical to the stable operation of the Internet, applications and other protocols that choose to use UDP as an Internet transport must employ mechanisms to prevent congestion collapse and to establish some degree of fairness with concurrent traffic. They may also need to implement additional mechanisms, depending on how they use UDP.

Some guidance is also applicable to the design of other protocols (e.g., protocols layered directly on IP or via IP-based tunnels), especially when these protocols do not themselves provide congestion control.

This document obsoletes RFC 5405 and adds guidelines for multicast UDP usage.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekG. Shepherdddatej’Èhfilenamegrfc8085eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129907hkeywordsÇcUDPjguidelineskobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateXø1˚grankingïcrfcd8085irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberëetotaletitletUDP Usage GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8899∑habstractyxThis document explains what is meant by the term "network transport Circuit Breaker".  It describes the need for Circuit Breakers (CBs) for network tunnels and applications when using non-congestion- controlled traffic and explains where CBs are, and are not, needed.  It also defines requirements for building a CB and the expected outcomes of using a CB within the Internet.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej’—hfilenamegrfc8084eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129906hkeywordsárCongestion controlbCCcUDPfTunnelmEncapsulationrTransport ProtocolrCongestion ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXø1˙grankingîcrfcd8084irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber–etotaletitlex"Network Transport Circuit BreakersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/The Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is widely used in telephony, video conferencing, and telepresence applications. Such applications are often run on best-effort UDP/IP networks. If congestion control is not implemented in these applications, then network congestion can lead to uncontrolled packet loss and a resulting deterioration of the user's multimedia experience. The congestion control algorithm acts as a safety measure by stopping RTP flows from using excessive resources and protecting the network from overload. At the time of this writing, however, while there are several proprietary solutions, there is no standard algorithm for congestion control of interactive RTP flows.

This document does not propose a congestion control algorithm. It instead defines a minimal set of RTP circuit breakers: conditions under which an RTP sender needs to stop transmitting media data to protect the network from excessive congestion. It is expected that, in the absence of long-lived excessive congestion, RTP applications running on best-effort IP networks will be able to operate without triggering these circuit breakers. To avoid triggering the RTP circuit breaker, any Standards Track congestion control algorithms defined for RTP will need to operate within the envelope set by these RTP circuit breaker algorithms.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationlcallstats.iodnamehV. SinghddatejœMhfilenamegrfc8083eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129905hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXø1Úgrankingìcrfcd8083irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMultimedia Congestion Control: Circuit Breakers for Unicast RTP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document updates RFC 5104 by fixing a shortcoming in the specification language of the Codec Control Message Full Intra Request (FIR) description when using it with layered codecs.  In particular, a decoder refresh point needs to be sent by a media sender when a FIR is received on any layer of the layered bitstream, regardless of whether those layers are being sent in a single or in multiple RTP flows.  The other payload-specific feedback messages defined in RFC 5104 and RFC 4585 (which was updated by RFC 5506) have also been analyzed, and no corresponding shortcomings have been found.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkVidyo, Inc.dnameiS. Wenger¢kaffiliationkVidyo, Inc.dnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Burman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej’Òhfilenamegrfc8082eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129904hkeywordsÑmLayered CodecrFull Intra RequestcFIRuDecoder Refresh PointkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX¬
wgrankingícrfcd8082irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Using Codec Control Messages in the RTP Audio-Visual Profile with Feedback with Layered CodecsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis memo serves to register and document the "font" top-level media type, under which subtypes for representation formats for fonts may be registered.  This document also serves as a registration application for a set of intended subtypes, which are representative of some existing subtypes already in use, and currently registered under the "application" tree by their separate registrations.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationbW3dnameiC. Lilleyddatej’Ôhfilenamegrfc8081eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhjustfontdfullx$justfont - Font Top Level Media TypednamexFont Top Level Media Typedtypebwgbidjdoc-129903hkeywordsÇtInternet Media TypesdMIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX∂!ºgrankingëcrfcd8081irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe "font" Top-Level Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∆This document describes how to specify Edwards-curve Digital Security Algorithm (EdDSA) keys and signatures in DNS Security (DNSSEC).  It uses EdDSA with the choice of two curves: Ed25519 and Ed448.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnamegO. Sury¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamejR. Edmondsddatej’hfilenamegrfc8080eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcurdledfullx9curdle - CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondnamex0CURves, Deprecating and a Little more Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129902hkeywordsÑfDNSSECeEdDSAged25519eed448kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX£±õgrankingêcrfcd8080irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Edwards-Curve Digital Security Algorithm (EdDSA) for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«SIP Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs) are often designed to also be on the media path, rather than just to intercept signalling. This means that B2BUAs often implement an RTP or RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) stack as well, thus leading to separate multimedia sessions that the B2BUA correlates and bridges together. If not disciplined, this behaviour can severely impact the communication experience, especially when statistics and feedback information contained in RTCP messages get lost because of mismatches in the reported data.

This document defines the proper behaviour B2BUAs should follow when acting on both the signalling plane and media plane in order to preserve the end-to-end functionality of RTCP.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhMeetechodnamejL. Miniero¢kaffiliationgMedoozednameqS. Garcia Murillo¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejV. Pascualddatejœfhfilenamegrfc8079eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestrawdfullx7straw - Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdnamex/Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdtypebwgbidjdoc-129901hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÆ,Jgrankingècrfcd8079irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiGuidelines for End-to-End Support of the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) in Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4RFC 7344 specifies how DNS trust can be maintained across key rollovers in-band between parent and child. This document elevates RFC 7344 from Informational to Standards Track. It also adds a method for initial trust setup and removal of a secure entry point.

Changing a domain's DNSSEC status can be a complicated matter involving multiple unrelated parties. Some of these parties, such as the DNS operator, might not even be known by all the organizations involved. The inability to disable DNSSEC via in-band signaling is seen as a problem or liability that prevents some DNSSEC adoption at a large scale. This document adds a method for in-band signaling of these DNSSEC status changes.

This document describes reasonable policies to ease deployment of the initial acceptance of new secure entry points (DS records).

It is preferable that operators collaborate on the transfer or move of a domain. The best method is to perform a Key Signing Key (KSK) plus Zone Signing Key (ZSK) rollover. If that is not possible, the method using an unsigned intermediate state described in this document can be used to move the domain between two parties. This leaves the domain temporarily unsigned and vulnerable to DNS spoofing, but that is preferred over the alternative of validation failures due to a mismatched DS and DNSKEY record.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjCouldflarednamenO. Gudmundsson¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Woutersddatej’Òhfilenamegrfc8078eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129900hkeywordsÇfdnssecqtrust maintenancekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateX¬·Kgrankingécrfcd8078irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Managing DS Records from the Parent via CDS/CDNSKEYdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9615∑habstracty∂Layer 2 services (such as Frame Relay, Asynchronous Transfer Mode, and Ethernet) can be emulated over an MPLS backbone by encapsulating the Layer 2 Protocol Data Units (PDUs) and then transmitting them over pseudowires (PWs). It is also possible to use pseudowires to provide low-rate Time-Division Multiplexed and Synchronous Optical NETworking circuit emulation over an MPLS-enabled network. This document specifies a protocol for establishing and maintaining the pseudowires, using extensions to the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP). Procedures for encapsulating Layer 2 PDUs are specified in other documents.

This document is a rewrite of RFC 4447 for publication as an Internet Standard.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehG. Heronddatejœ\hfilenamegrfc8077eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129899hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateXìπTgrankingçcrfcd8077irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberTetotaletitlexLPseudowire Setup and Maintenance Using the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document defines a REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) Usage for managing shared write access to RELOAD Resources.  Shared Resources in RELOAD (ShaRe) form a basic primitive for enabling various coordination and notification schemes among distributed peers.  Access in ShaRe is controlled by a hierarchical trust delegation scheme maintained within an access list.  A new USER-CHAIN-ACL access policy allows authorized peers to write a Shared Resource without owning its corresponding certificate.  This specification also adds mechanisms to store Resources with a variable name that is useful whenever peer-independent rendezvous processes are required.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamehA. Knauf¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationkdaviko GmbHdnamegG. Hege¢kaffiliationtlink-lab & FU BerlindnamelM. Waehlischddatej’Ìhfilenamegrfc8076eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129898hkeywordsàfP2PSIPcSIPlConferencingmVoice over IPlPee