an coexist in a single SAVI device and how collisions are resolved when the same binding entry is discovered by two or more methods.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Bi¢kaffiliationxTsinghua University/BaidudnamefG. Yao¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamepE. Levy-Abegnoliddatej’hfilenamegrfc8074eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsavidfullx-savi - Source Address Validation Improvementsdnamex&Source Address Validation Improvementsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129896hkeywordsÉiSAVI-DHCPiFCFS SAVIiSEND SAVIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXß‹agrankingäcrfcd8074irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZSource Address Validation Improvement (SAVI) for Mixed Address Assignment Methods ScenariodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty3This report documents the discussions and conclusions from the Coordinating Attack Response at Internet Scale (CARIS) workshop that took place in Berlin, Germany on 18 June 2015. The purpose of this workshop was to improve mutual awareness, understanding, and coordination among the diverse participating organizations and their representatives.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliation`dnamegM. Fordddatejœghfilenamegrfc8073eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129895hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX‹%egrankingâcrfcd8073irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexFCoordinating Attack Response at Internet Scale (CARIS) Workshop ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxäThis document describes a method for applying patches to configuration datastores using data defined with the YANG data modeling language.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej’Ïhfilenamegrfc8072eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129894hkeywordsÅhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateX≠‡˛grankingàcrfcd8072irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuYANG Patch Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¥This RFC presents NETCONF Call Home and RESTCONF Call Home, which enable a NETCONF or RESTCONF server to initiate a secure connection to a NETCONF or RESTCONF client, respectively.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej’æhfilenamegrfc8071eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129893hkeywordsÅicall-homekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX≠‡¸grankingácrfcd8071irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(NETCONF Call Home and RESTCONF Call HomedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis document describes how to further extend the Public Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT) extension (defined in RFC 4556) to exchange an opaque data blob that a Key Distribution Center (KDC) can validate to ensure that the client is currently in possession of the private key during a PKINIT Authentication Service (AS) exchange.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehM. Short¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehS. Moore¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiP. Millerddatejœghfilenamegrfc8070eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129892hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateX•ÓqgrankingÜcrfcd8070irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Public Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT) Freshness ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) 'ieee' for Uniform Resource Names (URNs) used to identify resources published by the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE).  IEEE specifies and manages resources that utilize this URN identification model.  Management activities for these and other resources types are handled by the manager of the IEEE Registration Authority.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIEEEdnameiA. Thomasddatejœchfilenamegrfc8069eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129891hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXö`’grankingÖcrfcd8069irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevURN Namespace for IEEEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯Session recording is a critical requirement in many communications environments, such as call centers and financial trading organizations.  In some of these environments, all calls must be recorded for regulatory, compliance, and consumer-protection reasons.  The recording of a session is typically performed by sending a copy of a media stream to a recording device.  This document lists call flows with metadata snapshots sent from a Session Recording Client (SRC) to a Session Recording Server (SRS).fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameoR. Ravindranath¢kaffiliationnNokia NetworksdnamelP. Ravindran¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejP. Kyzivatddatej’Ôhfilenamegrfc8068eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsiprecdfullvsiprec - SIP RecordingdnamemSIP Recordingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129890hkeywordsÅfsipreqkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateXµÿgrankingÑcrfcd8068irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Recording Call FlowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyqRFC 3967 specifies a process for allowing normative references to documents at lower maturity levels ("downrefs"), which involves calling out the downref explicitly in the Last Call notice.  That requirement has proven to be unnecessarily strict, and this document updates RFC 3967, allowing the IESG more flexibility in accepting downrefs in Standards Track documents.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej’Óhfilenamegrfc8067eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129889hkeywordsÉgdownrefhmaturityilast callkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXá¸´grankingÉcrfcd8067irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberaetotaletitlex[Updating When Standards Track Documents May Refer Normatively to Documents at a Lower LeveldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúRFC 4944 defines the ESC dispatch type to allow additional dispatch octets in the 6LoWPAN header.  The value of the ESC dispatch type was updated by RFC 6282; however, its usage was not defined in either RFC 6282 or RFC 4944.  This document updates RFC 4944 and RFC 6282 by defining the ESC extension octet code points and listing registration entries for known use cases at the time of writing of this document.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekJ. Woodyattddatejœ]hfilenamegrfc8066eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129888hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXö`”grankingÇcrfcd8066irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdIPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) ESC Dispatch Code Points and GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document discusses how a number of privacy threats apply to technologies designed for IPv6 over various link-layer protocols, and it provides advice to protocol designers on how to address such threats in adaptation-layer specifications for IPv6 over such links.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatejœehfilenamegrfc8065eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129887hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateXÆagrankingÅcrfcd8065irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Privacy Considerations for IPv6 Adaptation-Layer MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis document changes the recommended default Interface Identifier (IID) generation scheme for cases where Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC) is used to generate a stable IPv6 address.  It recommends using the mechanism specified in RFC 7217 in such cases, and recommends against embedding stable link-layer addresses in IPv6 IIDs.  It formally updates RFC 2464, RFC 2467, RFC 2470, RFC 2491, RFC 2492, RFC 2497, RFC 2590, RFC 3146, RFC 3572, RFC 4291, RFC 4338, RFC 4391, RFC 5072, and RFC 5121.  This document does not change any existing recommendations concerning the use of temporary addresses as specified in RFC 4941.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Cooper¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liuddatejœehfilenamegrfc8064eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129886hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXÆ]grankingÄcrfcd8064irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Recommendation on Stable IPv6 Interface IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for a key relay object that relays DNSSEC key material between EPP clients using the poll queue defined in RFC 5730.

This key relay mapping will help facilitate changing the DNS operator of a domain while keeping the DNSSEC chain of trust intact.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdSIDNdnamelH.W. Ribbers¢kaffiliationdSIDNdnamenM.W. Groeneweg¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Gieben¢kaffiliation`dnameqA.L.J. Verschurenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc8063eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129885hkeywordsÅx Extensible Provisioning ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX£çGgrankingcrfcd8063irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Key Relay Mapping for the Extensible Provisioning ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document defines extensions to the Kerberos protocol to allow a Kerberos client to securely communicate with a Kerberos application service without revealing its identity, or without revealing more than its Kerberos realm.  It also defines extensions that allow a Kerberos client to obtain anonymous credentials without revealing its identity to the Kerberos Key Distribution Center (KDC).  This document updates RFCs 4120, 4121, and 4556.  This document obsoletes RFC 6112 and reclassifies that document as Historic.  RFC 6112 contained errors, and the protocol described in that specification is not interoperable with any known implementation.  This specification describes a protocol that interoperates with multiple implementations.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehP. Leach¢kaffiliationqHadron IndustriesdnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehS. Emeryddatejœchfilenamegrfc8062eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129884hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX∂!ªgranking~crfcd8062irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAnonymity Support for KerberosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document describes a mechanism for encrypting traffic encapsulated using the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP).  The design describes how key exchange is achieved using existing LISP control-plane mechanisms as well as how to secure the LISP data plane from third-party surveillance attacks.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Weisddatej’Êhfilenamegrfc8061eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129883hkeywordsÅdlcafkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXìπSgranking}crfcd8061irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Data-Plane ConfidentialitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¡This document defines a canonical address format encoding used in Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) control messages and in the encoding of lookup keys for the LISP Mapping Database System.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijdersddatej’Ïhfilenamegrfc8060eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129882hkeywordsÅxLocator/ID Separation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateXìπRgranking|crfcd8060irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$LISP Canonical Address Format (LCAF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9306∂habstracty’This document defines two PIM Join/Prune attributes that support the construction of multicast distribution trees where the root and receivers are located in different Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) sites.  These attributes allow the receiver site to select between unicast and multicast underlying transport and to convey the RLOC (Routing Locator) address of the receiver ETR (Egress Tunnel Router) to the control plane of the root ITR (Ingress Tunnel Router).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiJ. Arango¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationtArista Networks Inc.dnamekI. Kouvelas¢kaffiliationklispers.netdnamelD. Farinacciddatejœ`hfilenamegrfc8059eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-129881hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXÇ5Hgranking{crfcd8059irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJPIM Join Attributes for Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9798∂habstractyDThis document describes a method for signaling a one-click function for the List-Unsubscribe email header field.  The need for this arises out of the actuality that mail software sometimes fetches URLs in mail header fields, and thereby accidentally triggers unsubscriptions in the case of the List-Unsubscribe header field.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levine¢kaffiliationkoptivo GmbHdnamejT. Herkuladdatej’Ìhfilenamegrfc8058eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129880hkeywordsÇeemaillmailing listkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXÜ¸ºgrankingzcrfcd8058irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Signaling One-Click Functionality for List Email HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis document describes the Namespace Identifiers (NIDs) "bbf", "broadband-forum-org", and "dslforum-org" for Uniform Resource Names (URNs) used to identify resources published by Broadband Forum (BBF).  BBF specifies and manages resources that utilize these three URN identification models.  Management activities for these and other resource types are handled by BBF.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Stark¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelD. Sinicrope¢kaffiliationoBroadband ForumdnameiW. Luptonddatej’Ìhfilenamegrfc8057eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129879hkeywordsÉcURNoBroadband ForumcBBFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÇ5Ggrankingycrfcd8057irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespaces for Broadband ForumdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This document describes the mapping of the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) statuses with the statuses registered for use in the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP).  This document identifies gaps in the mapping, and registers RDAP statuses to fill those gaps to ensure that all of the EPP statuses specified in RFCs are supported in RDAP.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmVeriSign Inc.dnamehJ. Gouldddatejœ_hfilenamegrfc8056eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfregextdfullx*regext - Registration Protocols Extensionsdnamex!Registration Protocols Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129878hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX~ì|grankingxcrfcd8056irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbExtensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) and Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Status MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThis specification defines a new Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Via header field parameter, 'received-realm', which allows a SIP entity acting as an entry point to a transit network to indicate from which adjacent upstream network a SIP request is received by using a network realm value associated with the adjacent network.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehY. Jiangddatej’Ìhfilenamegrfc8055eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129877hkeywordsÑcSIPcViagtransiterealmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXë=Œgrankingwcrfcd8055irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Via Header Field Parameter to Indicate Received RealmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxπThis document defines an extension to the Network News Transport Protocol (NNTP) that allows a connection to be effectively and efficiently compressed between an NNTP client and server.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamelK. Murchison¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Elieddatej’Ïhfilenamegrfc8054eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129876hkeywordsÜdNNTPfUsenetgNetNewshCOMPRESSgDEFLATEkcompressionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXâQIgrankingvcrfcd8054irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) Extension for CompressiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document specifies extensions for the HTTP authentication framework for interactive clients.  Currently, fundamental features of HTTP-level authentication are insufficient for complex requirements of various Web-based applications.  This forces these applications to implement their own authentication frameworks by means such as HTML forms, which becomes one of the hurdles against introducing secure authentication mechanisms handled jointly by servers and user agents.  The extended framework fills gaps between Web application requirements and HTTP authentication provisions to solve the above problems, while maintaining compatibility with existing Web and non-Web uses of HTTP authentication.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnamegY. Oiwa¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnamekH. Watanabe¢kaffiliationkITRI / AISTdnameiH. Takagi¢kaffiliationgLepidumdnamehK. Maeda¢kaffiliationgLepidumdnamejT. Hayashi¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamegY. Iokuddatejœ_hfilenamegrfc8053eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhhttpauthdfullx5httpauth - Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdnamex*Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129875hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXâQIgrankingucrfcd8053irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6HTTP Authentication Extensions for Interactive ClientsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyπThe IEC 61850 power utility automation family of standards describes methods using Ethernet and IP for distributing control and data frames within and between substations.  The IEC 61850-90-5 and IEC 62351-9 standards specify the use of the Group Domain of Interpretation (GDOI) protocol (RFC 6407) to distribute security transforms for some IEC 61850 security protocols.  This memo defines GDOI payloads to support those security protocols.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Weis¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Seewald¢kaffiliationeSISCOdnamegH. Falkddatejœ\hfilenamegrfc8052eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129874hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY:Êägrankingtcrfcd8052irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVGroup Domain of Interpretation (GDOI) Protocol Support for IEC 62351 Security ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2A stateful Path Computation Element (PCE) maintains information about Label Switched Path (LSP) characteristics and resource usage within a network in order to provide traffic-engineering calculations for its associated Path Computation Clients (PCCs).  This document describes general considerations for a stateful PCE deployment and examines its applicability and benefits, as well as its challenges and limitations, through a number of use cases.  PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) extensions required for stateful PCE usage are covered in separate documents.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Mineiddatej’Áhfilenamegrfc8051eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129873hkeywordsÇlStateful PCEmApplicabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXpägrankingscrfcd8051irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Applicability of a Stateful Path Computation Element (PCE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx«This document extends the Multi-threaded Routing Toolkit (MRT) export format for Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) routing information by supporting the advertisement of multiple paths in BGP extensions.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnameiC. Petrie¢kaffiliationfDE-CIXdnamegT. Kingddatejœ|hfilenamegrfc8050eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129872hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateY√ógrankingrcrfcd8050irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjMulti-Threaded Routing Toolkit (MRT) Routing Information Export Format with BGP Additional Path ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a YANG data model that can be used for communication between customers and network operators and to deliver a Layer 3 provider-provisioned VPN service.  This document is limited to BGP PE-based VPNs as described in RFCs 4026, 4110, and 4364.  This model is intended to be instantiated at the management system to deliver the overall service.  It is not a configuration model to be used directly on network elements.  This model provides an abstracted view of the Layer 3 IP VPN service configuration components.  It will be up to the management system to take this model as input and use specific configuration models to configure the different network elements to deliver the service.  How the configuration of network elements is done is out of scope for this document.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxOrange Business ServicesdnamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamekL. Tomotaki¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnamehK. Ogakiddatej’Îhfilenamegrfc8049eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdl3smdfullxl3sm - L3VPN Service Model dnametL3VPN Service Model dtypebwgbidjdoc-129871hkeywordsÑdYANGdl3smel3vpnmservice modelkobsoletedByÅd8299epagesùopublicationDateXÆ,Agrankingqcrfcd8049irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*YANG Data Model for L3VPN Service DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document defines a bidirectional protocol mapping for the exchange of presence information between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP).  This document obsoletes RFC 7248.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhFilamentdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’Ëhfilenamegrfc8048eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstoxdfullrstox - SIP-TO-XMPPdnamekSIP-TO-XMPPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129870hkeywordsàx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocoldXMPPfJabberxSession Initiation ProtocolcSIPfSIMPLEhpresencelavailabilitykobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateXd*Êgrankingpcrfcd8048irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~Interworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): PresencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxìThis document defines host multihoming extensions to the Host Identity Protocol (HIP), by leveraging protocol components defined for host mobility.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegC. Vogt¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej’Áhfilenamegrfc8047eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129869hkeywordsÑchipvmultihoming extensionssmobility extensionsglocatorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX£±ögrankingocrfcd8047irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Host Multihoming with the Host Identity ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'This document defines a mobility extension to the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  Specifically, this document defines a "LOCATOR_SET" parameter for HIP messages that allows for a HIP host to notify peers about alternate addresses at which it may be reached.  This document also defines how the parameter can be used to preserve communications across a change to the IP address used by one or both peer hosts.  The same LOCATOR_SET parameter can also be used to support end-host multihoming (as specified in RFC 8047).  This document obsoletes RFC 5206.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamegC. Vogt¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej’Áhfilenamegrfc8046eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129868hkeywordsÑchipvmultihoming extensionssmobility extensionsglocatorkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateX£±ôgrankingncrfcd8046irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Host Mobility with the Host Identity ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines three new RADIUS attributes.  For devices that implement IP port ranges, these attributes are used to communicate with a RADIUS server in order to configure and report IP transport ports as well as mapping behavior for specific hosts.  This mechanism can be used in various deployment scenarios such as Carrier-Grade NAT, IPv4/IPv6 translators, Provider WLAN gateway, etc.  This document defines a mapping between some RADIUS attributes and IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Element identifiers.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Cheng¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumarddatej’Ëhfilenamegrfc8045eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129867hkeywordsêoaddress sharingraddress continuitycCGNcNATmIP assignmentoport assignmentlport controloport accountinghport setjport rangemIP/Port LimitnProvider Wi-FioPort forwardingmInternal portmExternal portlPort mappingkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateXâQHgrankingmcrfcd8045irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9RADIUS Extensions for IP Port Configuration and ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈRADIUS specifications have used data types for two decades without defining them as managed entities.  During this time, RADIUS implementations have named the data types and have used them in attribute definitions.  This document updates the specifications to better follow established practice.  We do this by naming the data types defined in RFC 6158, which have been used since at least the publication of RFC 2865.  We provide an IANA registry for the data types and update the "RADIUS Attribute Types" registry to include a Data Type field for each attribute.  Finally, we recommend that authors of RADIUS specifications use these types in preference to existing practice.  This document updates RFCs 2865, 3162, 4072, 6158, 6572, and 7268.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatejœ^hfilenamegrfc8044eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129866hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateXâQAgrankinglcrfcd8044irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletData Types in RADIUSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyRThis document presents the source-address-dependent routing (SADR) problem space from the host's perspective. Both multihomed hosts and hosts with multiple interfaces are considered. Several network architectures are presented to illustrate why source address selection and next-hop resolution are needed in view of source-address-dependent routing.

The document is scoped on identifying a set of scenarios for source-address-dependent routing from the host's perspective and analyzing a set of solutions to mitigate encountered issues. The document does not make any solution recommendations.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej’Èhfilenamegrfc8043eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129865hkeywordsÉrNeighbor DiscoveryxDuplicate Address DetectionnND Relay AgentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXpâgrankingkcrfcd8043irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexkSource-Address-Dependent Routing and Source Address Selection for IPv6 Hosts: Overview of the Problem SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document specifies an optional OSPF protocol extension to represent router metrics in a multi-access network in two parts: the metric from the router to the network and the metric from the network to the router.  For such networks, the router-to-router metric for OSPF route computation is the sum of the two parts.  This document updates RFC 2328.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindemddatej’Áhfilenamegrfc8042eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129864hkeywordsÖdOSPFiBroadcastiInterfacekSPF metricsnRadio NetworkskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXd*Âgrankingjcrfcd8042irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletOSPF Two-Part MetricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document discusses both use cases and operational experience with Multipath TCP (MPTCP) in real networks.  It lists several prominent use cases where Multipath TCP has been considered and is being used.  It also gives insight to some heuristics and decisions that have helped to realize these use cases and suggests possible improvements.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiUCLouvaindnamenO. Bonaventure¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiC. Paasch¢kaffiliationhTessaresdnamehG. Detalddatej’Êhfilenamegrfc8041eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129863hkeywordsãcTCPeMPTCPiMiddleboxrCongestion ControllPath ManageriSchedulereProxymLoad-BalancerjDatacenteruCellular/WiFi OffloadvHybrid Access NetworkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXxàgrankingicrfcd8041irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Use Cases and Operational Experience with Multipath TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxœThis document describes an HTTP-based protocol that provides a programmatic interface for accessing data defined in YANG, using the datastore concepts defined in the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF).fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej’Êhfilenamegrfc8040eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129862hkeywordsÑdYANGgNETCONFdRESTdHTTPkobsoletedByÄepagesâopublicationDateXë=Ãgrankinghcrfcd8040irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqRESTCONF ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8527∂habstractyãClock synchronization protocols are very widely used in IP-based networks.  The Network Time Protocol (NTP) has been commonly deployed for many years, and the last few years have seen an increasingly rapid deployment of the Precision Time Protocol (PTP).  As time-sensitive applications evolve, clock accuracy requirements are becoming increasingly stringent, requiring the time synchronization protocols to provide high accuracy.  This memo describes a multipath approach to PTP and NTP over IP networks, allowing the protocols to run concurrently over multiple communication paths between the master and slave clocks, without modifying these protocols.  The multipath approach can significantly contribute to clock accuracy, security, and fault tolerance.  The multipath approach that is presented in this document enables backward compatibility with nodes that do not support the multipath functionality.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhMellanoxdnamejA. Shpiner¢kaffiliationiMicrosemidnamefR. Tse¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Schelp¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahiddatej’Âhfilenamegrfc8039eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftictocdfullx8tictoc - Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdnamex/Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdtypebwgbidjdoc-129861hkeywordsÑcNTPcPTPiIEEE 1588nmultiple pathskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXIé@grankinggcrfcd8039irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMultipath Time SynchronizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a way to complement IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Data Records with Management Information Base (MIB) objects, avoiding the need to define new IPFIX Information Elements for existing MIB objects that are already fully specified.

Two IPFIX Options Templates, as well as a method for creating IPFIX Options Templates that are used to export the extra data required to fully describe Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) MIB objects in IPFIX, are specified herein.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsMojo Networks, Inc.dnamefS. B S¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamekC. McDowall¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej’≥hfilenamegrfc8038eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129860hkeywordsÉeIPFIXcMIBdSNMPkobsoletedByÄepagesUopublicationDateY	3∫grankingfcrfcd8038irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMExporting MIB Variables Using the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This document defines how to use the Diffie-Hellman algorithms "X25519" and "X448" as well as the signature algorithms "Ed25519" and "Ed448" from the IRTF CFRG elliptic curves work in JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE).fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamelI. Liusvaaraddatej’‡hfilenamegrfc8037eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129859hkeywordsÑgEd25519eEd448fX25519dX448kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXáıÛgrankingecrfcd8037irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeCFRG Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) and Signatures in JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9864∂habstractxúThis document discusses the applicability of the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) in Advanced Metering Infrastructure (AMI) networks.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationdNestdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationiItron IncdnamegD. Popaddatej’Êhfilenamegrfc8036eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129858hkeywordsÜwconstrained environmentksmart meteriutilitiesismartgridpsecure smartgridpconnected energykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXxKgrankingdcrfcd8036irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäApplicability Statement for the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) in Advanced Metering Infrastructure (AMI) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5This document updates RFC 5761 by clarifying the SDP offer/answer negotiation of RTP and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) multiplexing.  It makes it clear that an answerer can only include an "a=rtcp-mux" attribute in a Session Description Protocol (SDP) answer if the associated SDP offer contained the attribute.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej’Âhfilenamegrfc8035eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129857hkeywordsácRTPdRTCPimultiplexhrtcp-muxcSDPeofferfanswerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX<ÏÑgrankingccrfcd8035irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXSession Description Protocol (SDP) Offer/Answer Clarifications for RTP/RTCP MultiplexingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬Cable modems based on Data-Over-Cable Service Interface Specifications (DOCSIS) provide broadband Internet access to over one hundred million users worldwide.  In some cases, the cable modem connection is the bottleneck (lowest speed) link between the customer and the Internet.  As a result, the impact of buffering and bufferbloat in the cable modem can have a significant effect on user experience.  The CableLabs DOCSIS 3.1 specification introduces requirements for cable modems to support an Active Queue Management (AQM) algorithm that is intended to alleviate the impact that buffering has on latency-sensitive traffic, while preserving bulk throughput performance.  In addition, the CableLabs DOCSIS 3.0 specifications have also been amended to contain similar requirements.  This document describes the requirements on AQM that apply to DOCSIS equipment, including a description of the "DOCSIS-PIE" algorithm that is required on DOCSIS 3.1 cable modems.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamehG. White¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefR. Panddatej’…hfilenamegrfc8034eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129856hkeywordsÉglatencynaccess networkkbufferbloatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX∂!∫grankingbcrfcd8034irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex°Active Queue Management (AQM) Based on Proportional Integral Controller Enhanced (PIE) for Data-Over-Cable Service Interface Specifications (DOCSIS) Cable ModemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBufferbloat is a phenomenon in which excess buffers in the network cause high latency and latency variation. As more and more interactive applications (e.g., voice over IP, real-time video streaming, and financial transactions) run in the Internet, high latency and latency variation degrade application performance. There is a pressing need to design intelligent queue management schemes that can control latency and latency variation, and hence provide desirable quality of service to users.

This document presents a lightweight active queue management design called "PIE" (Proportional Integral controller Enhanced) that can effectively control the average queuing latency to a target value. Simulation results, theoretical analysis, and Linux testbed results have shown that PIE can ensure low latency and achieve high link utilization under various congestion situations. The design does not require per-packet timestamps, so it incurs very little overhead and is simple enough to implement in both hardware and software.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefR. Pan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Natarajan¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamehG. Whiteddatej’Âhfilenamegrfc8033eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129855hkeywordsÇwactive queue managementcAQMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX∂!∏grankingacrfcd8033irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpProportional Integral Controller Enhanced (PIE): A Lightweight Control Scheme to Address the Bufferbloat ProblemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes elliptic curve signature scheme Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA).  The algorithm is instantiated with recommended parameters for the edwards25519 and edwards448 curves.  An example implementation and test vectors are provided.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamelI. Liusvaaraddatej’Âhfilenamegrfc8032eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-129854hkeywordsÉisignatureqdigital signatureeEdDSAkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateXáıÚgranking`crfcd8032irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex1Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxéThis document describes the use of Curve25519 and Curve448 for ephemeral key exchange in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2).fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationcSJDdnamelS. Josefssonddatej’Âhfilenamegrfc8031eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129853hkeywordsÑjCurve25519hCurve448jGoldilocksnDiffie HellmankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXIé@granking_crfcd8031irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Curve25519 and Curve448 for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Key AgreementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÖThis document describes a simple protocol for the delivery of real- time events to user agents.  This scheme uses HTTP/2 server push.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamekE. Damaggio¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiB. Raymorddatej“hfilenamegrfc8030eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgwebpushdfullx&webpush - Web-Based Push NotificationsdnamexWeb-Based Push Notificationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129852hkeywordsÑdHTTPeHTTP2dPushgWebPushkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXIé?granking^crfcd8030irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Generic Event Delivery Using HTTP PushdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document describes a simple and efficient mechanism to detect data-plane failures in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs). It defines a probe message called an "MPLS echo request" and a response message called an "MPLS echo reply" for returning the result of the probe. The MPLS echo request is intended to contain sufficient information to check correct operation of the data plane and to verify the data plane against the control plane, thereby localizing faults.

This document obsoletes RFCs 4379, 6424, 6829, and 7537, and updates RFC 1122.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehN. Kumar¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatej’Ëhfilenamegrfc8029eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129851hkeywordsÇqMPLS echo requestoMPLS echo replykobsoletedByÄepagesNopublicationDateXÃmKgranking]crfcd8029irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADetecting Multiprotocol Label Switched (MPLS) Data-Plane FailuresdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9041d9570d8611∂habstractyıThis document describes expected IPv6 host behavior in a scenario that has more than one prefix, each allocated by an upstream network that is assumed to implement BCP 38 ingress filtering, when the host has multiple routers to choose from.  It also applies to other scenarios such as the usage of stateful firewalls that effectively act as address-based filters.  Host behavior in choosing a first-hop router may interact with source address selection in a given implementation.  However, the selection of the source address for a packet is done before the first-hop router for that packet is chosen.  Given that the network or host is, or appears to be, multihomed with multiple provider-allocated addresses, that the host has elected to use a source address in a given prefix, and that some but not all neighboring routers are advertising that prefix in their Router Advertisement Prefix Information Options, this document specifies to which router a host should present its transmission.  It updates RFC 4861.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. CarpenterddatejœWhfilenamegrfc8028eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129850hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX<ÏÉgranking\crfcd8028irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=First-Hop Router Selection by Hosts in a Multi-Prefix NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyXThis document describes problems that a Validating DNS resolver, stub-resolver, or application might run into within a non-compliant infrastructure.  It outlines potential detection and mitigation techniques.  The scope of the document is to create a shared approach to detect and overcome network issues that a DNSSEC software/system may face.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliationjCouldflarednamenO. Gudmundsson¢kaffiliationgParsonsdnameoS. Krishnaswamyddatej’„hfilenamegrfc8027eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129849hkeywordsÉfDNSSECpNetwork ProblemscDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX<ÏÅgranking[crfcd8027irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberœetotaletitlexDNSSEC Roadblock AvoidancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynIn IPv6-only provider networks, transporting IPv4 packets encapsulated in IPv6 is a common solution to the problem of IPv4 service continuity.  A number of differing functional approaches have been developed for this, each having their own specific characteristics.  As these approaches share a similar functional architecture and use the same data plane mechanisms, this memo specifies a DHCPv6 option, whereby a single instance of Customer Premises Equipment (CPE) can interwork with all of the standardized and proposed approaches to providing encapsulated IPv4-in-IPv6 services by providing a prioritization mechanism.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrerddatej’‰hfilenamegrfc8026eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129848hkeywordsälProvisioninghSoftwirenIPv4 over IPv6wIPv4 service continuityvIPv4 address depletioncMAPeMAP-TeMAP-EgDS-LitetLightweight 4 over 6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX,GgrankingZcrfcd8026irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhUnified IPv4-in-IPv6 Softwire Customer Premises Equipment (CPE): A DHCPv6-Based Prioritization MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡This specification updates RFC 4944 to introduce a new context switch mechanism for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) compression, expressed in terms of Pages and signaled by a new Paging Dispatch.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationcARMdnameiR. Cragieddatej’Âhfilenamegrfc8025eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129847hkeywordsÇcLNNcIOTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX?f^grankingYcrfcd8025irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLIPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) Paging DispatchdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) is being extended to the edge of operator networks including the network access nodes.  Separately, network access nodes such as Passive Optical Network (PON) Optical Line Terminations (OLTs) have evolved to support first-mile access protection, where one or more physical OLTs provide first-mile diversity to the customer edge.  Multihoming support is needed on the MPLS-enabled PON OLT to provide resiliency for provided services.  This document describes the Multi-Chassis PON (MC-PON) protection architecture in MPLS and also specifies the Inter-Chassis Communication Protocol (ICCP) extension to support it.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehY. Jiang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Luo¢kaffiliationvCharter CommunicationsdnamekE. Mallette¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegY. Shen¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehW. Chengddatej’‰hfilenamegrfc8024eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129846hkeywordsÅnPON ProtectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX?f^grankingXcrfcd8024irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAMulti-Chassis Passive Optical Network (MC-PON) Protection in MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≠This document provides context and a report on the workshop on "Root Causes and Mitigation of Name Collisions", which took place in London, United Kingdom, from March 8 to 10, 2014.  The main goal of the workshop was to foster a discussion on the causes and potential mitigations of domain name collisions.  This report provides a small amount of background and context; then, it provides a summary of the workshop's discussions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Thomas¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednameiA. Mankin¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehL. ZhangddatejœMhfilenamegrfc8023eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129845hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX$˝BgrankingWcrfcd8023irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexSReport from the Workshop and Prize on Root Causes and Mitigation of Name CollisionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document contains a specification of three YANG modules and one submodule.  Together they form the core routing data model that serves as a framework for configuring and managing a routing subsystem.  It is expected that these modules will be augmented by additional YANG modules defining data models for control-plane protocols, route filters, and other functions.  The core routing data model provides common building blocks for such extensions -- routes, Routing Information Bases (RIBs), and control-plane protocols.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnameiL. Lhotka¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindemddatej’Áhfilenamegrfc8022eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129844hkeywordsÑmconfigurationxIPv6 router advertisementsgNETCONFhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÅd8349epages@opublicationDateX#ı∏grankingVcrfcd8022irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A YANG Data Model for Routing ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document discusses the security implications of the generation of IPv6 atomic fragments and a number of interoperability issues associated with IPv6 atomic fragments.  It concludes that the aforementioned functionality is undesirable and thus documents the motivation for removing this functionality from an upcoming revision of the core IPv6 protocol specification (RFC 2460).fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liu¢kaffiliationnRedpill LinprodnamekT. Andersonddatej’‰hfilenamegrfc8021eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129843hkeywordsÉfattackcDoSqExtension HeaderskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXpàgrankingUcrfcd8021irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Generation of IPv6 Atomic Fragments Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document states clearly that when a DNS resolver receives a response with a response code of NXDOMAIN, it means that the domain name which is thus denied AND ALL THE NAMES UNDER IT do not exist.

This document clarifies RFC 1034 and modifies a portion of RFC 2308: it updates both of them.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAFNICdnamemS. Bortzmeyer¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamehS. HuqueddatejœThfilenamegrfc8020eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129842hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateX";ºgrankingTcrfcd8020irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,NXDOMAIN: There Really Is Nothing UnderneathdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis document recommends implementation and configuration best practices for Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) Responders, to allow them to resist Denial-of-Service and Distributed Denial-of-Service attacks.  Additionally, the document introduces a new mechanism called "Client Puzzles" that helps accomplish this task.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej’‰hfilenamegrfc8019eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129841hkeywordsÑfpuzzlecdosdddosgbitcoinkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateX,GgrankingScrfcd8019irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvProtecting Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Implementations from Distributed Denial-of-Service AttacksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document provides recommendations for the implementation of password-based cryptography, covering key derivation functions, encryption schemes, message authentication schemes, and ASN.1 syntax identifying the techniques.

This document represents a republication of PKCS #5 v2.1 from RSA Laboratories' Public-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) series. By publishing this RFC, change control is transferred to the IETF.

This document also obsoletes RFC 2898.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejB. Kaliski¢kaffiliationcRSAdnamehA. Ruschddatej’„hfilenamegrfc8018eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129840hkeywordsÉxpassword-based encryptionxpassword-based key derivationdsaltkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateXáıÒgrankingRcrfcd8018irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>PKCS #5: Password-Based Cryptography Specification Version 2.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9879d9579∂habstractyThis document provides recommendations for the implementation of public-key cryptography based on the RSA algorithm, covering cryptographic primitives, encryption schemes, signature schemes with appendix, and ASN.1 syntax for representing keys and for identifying the schemes.

This document represents a republication of PKCS #1 v2.2 from RSA Laboratories' Public-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) series. By publishing this RFC, change control is transferred to the IETF.

This document also obsoletes RFC 3447.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamejB. Kaliski¢kaffiliationiSubset ABdnamejJ. Jonsson¢kaffiliationcRSAdnamehA. Ruschddatej’„hfilenamegrfc8017eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129839hkeywordsâxRSA public-key cryptosystemtRSA signature schemenRSA public keyoRSA private keylPKCS #1 v1.5hRSA-OAEPgRSA-PSSx%Optimal Asymmetric Encryption PaddingxProbabilistic Signature SchemekobsoletedByÄepagesNopublicationDateX)KTgrankingQcrfcd8017irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4PKCS #1: RSA Cryptography Specifications Version 2.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3It is desirable to minimize traffic disruption caused by changing IP address during a mobility event. One mechanism to minimize disruption is to expose a shorter network path to the mobility event so that only the local network elements are aware of the changed IP address and the remote peer is unaware of the changed IP address.

This document provides such an IP address mobility solution using Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN). This is achieved by allowing a client to retain an allocation on the TURN server when the IP address of the client changes.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Patil¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelP. Martinsenddatej’ﬂhfilenamegrfc8016eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtramdfullx"tram - TURN Revised and ModernizeddnamexTURN Revised and Modernizeddtypebwgbidjdoc-129838hkeywordsÜsIP Address MobilitydVoIPcICEdSTUNcRTPfTUNNELkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX$È grankingPcrfcd8016irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Mobility with Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx⁄This document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) block that allows the reporting of burst/gap discard metrics independently of the burst/gap loss metrics for use in a range of RTP applications.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlcallstats.iodnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huangddatej’‰hfilenamegrfc8015eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-129837hkeywordsÅgXRBLOCKkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX";∫grankingOcrfcd8015irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block for Independent Reporting of Burst/Gap Discard MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThis document presents a high-level overview architecture for building data-center Network Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3) networks.  The architecture is given at a high level, showing the major components of an overall system.  An important goal is to divide the space into individual smaller components that can be implemented independently with clear inter-component interfaces and interactions.  It should be possible to build and implement individual components in isolation and have them interoperate with other independently implemented components.  That way, implementers have flexibility in implementing individual components and can optimize and innovate within their respective components without requiring changes to other components.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhDell EMCdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiJ. Hudson¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejL. Kreeger¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamekM. Lasserre¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. NartenddatejœThfilenamegrfc8014eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-129836hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateXd*‡grankingNcrfcd8014irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJAn Architecture for Data-Center Network Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis document describes how to extend the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Logical Functional Block (LFB) topology across Forwarding Elements (FEs) by defining the inter-FE LFB class.  The inter-FE LFB class provides the ability to pass data and metadata across FEs without needing any changes to the ForCES specification.  The document focuses on Ethernet transport.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamepD. Joachimpillai¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salimddatej’÷hfilenamegrfc8013eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129835hkeywordsÇfForCEShInter-FEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXÆZgrankingMcrfcd8013irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Inter-FE Logical Functional Block (LFB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty…Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Path (LSP) ping and traceroute are methods used to test Equal-Cost Multipath (ECMP) paths. Ping is known as a connectivity-verification method and traceroute is known as a fault-isolation method, as described in RFC 4379. When an LSP is signaled using the Entropy Label (EL) described in RFC 6790, the ability for LSP ping and traceroute operations to discover and exercise ECMP paths is lost for scenarios where Label Switching Routers (LSRs) apply different load-balancing techniques. One such scenario is when some LSRs apply EL-based load balancing while other LSRs apply load balancing that is not EL based (e.g., IP). Another scenario is when an EL-based LSP is stitched with another LSP that can be EL based or not EL based.

This document extends the MPLS LSP ping and traceroute multipath mechanisms in RFC 6424 to allow the ability of exercising LSPs that make use of the EL. This document updates RFC 6790.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Aldrinddatej’„hfilenamegrfc8012eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129834hkeywordsÉdMPLShLSP Pingkand EntropykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX ∑ÔgrankingLcrfcd8012irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfLabel Switched Path (LSP) and Pseudowire (PW) Ping/Trace over MPLS Networks Using Entropy Labels (ELs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty®The Internet Printing Protocol (IPP) is an application-level protocol for distributed printing using Internet tools and technologies. This document describes a simplified model consisting of abstract objects, attributes, and operations that is independent of encoding and transport. The model consists of several objects, including Printers and Jobs. Jobs optionally support multiple Documents.

IPP semantics allow End Users and Operators to query Printer capabilities; submit Print Jobs; inquire about the status of Print Jobs and Printers; and cancel, hold, and release Print Jobs. IPP semantics also allow Operators to pause and resume Jobs and Printers.

Security, internationalization, and directory issues are also addressed by the model and semantics. The IPP message encoding and transport are described in "Internet Printing Protocol/1.1: Encoding and Transport" (RFC 8010).

This document obsoletes RFCs 2911, 3381, and 3382.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehM. Sweet¢kaffiliationoHigh North Inc.dnamekI. McDonaldddatej’‚hfilenamegrfc8011eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129833hkeywordsÑcIPPgPrintercPWGuPrinter Working GroupkobsoletedByÄepages›opublicationDateX~ìzgrankingKcrfcd8011irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber\etotaletitlex3Internet Printing Protocol/1.1: Model and SemanticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThe Internet Printing Protocol (IPP) is an application-level protocol for distributed printing using Internet tools and technologies.  This document defines the rules for encoding IPP operations, attributes, and values into the Internet MIME media type called "application/ipp".  It also defines the rules for transporting a message body whose Content-Type is "application/ipp" over HTTP and/or HTTPS.  The IPP data model and operation semantics are described in "Internet Printing Protocol/1.1: Model and Semantics" (RFC 8011).fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehM. Sweet¢kaffiliationjHigh NorthdnamekI. McDonaldddatej’‹hfilenamegrfc8010eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129832hkeywordsÑcIPPgPrintercPWGuPrinter Working GroupkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateX~ìygrankingJcrfcd8010irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber\etotaletitlex6Internet Printing Protocol/1.1: Encoding and TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÊThis document specifies two encryption types and two corresponding checksum types for Kerberos 5.  The new types use AES in CTS mode (CBC mode with ciphertext stealing) for confidentiality and HMAC with a SHA-2 hash for integrity.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejM. Jenkins¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamegM. Peck¢kaffiliation`dnameiK. BurginddatejœPhfilenamegrfc8009eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129831hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX‚egrankingIcrfcd8009irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,AES Encryption with HMAC-SHA2 for Kerberos 5dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThis document captures the semantics of the "Footprint and Capabilities Advertisement" part of the Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Request Routing interface, i.e., the desired meaning of "Footprint" and "Capabilities" in the CDNI context and what the "Footprint & Capabilities Advertisement interface (FCI)" offers within CDNI.  The document also provides guidelines for the CDNI FCI protocol.  It further defines a Base Advertisement Object, the necessary registries for capabilities and footprints, and guidelines on how these registries can be extended in the future.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx)HFT Stuttgart - Univ. of Applied SciencesdnamejJ. Seedorf¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamerR. van Brandenburg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameeK. Maddatej’“hfilenamegrfc8008eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129830hkeywordsÉdCDNIpCDN InterconnectoRequest RoutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXP™êgrankingHcrfcd8008irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeContent Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Request Routing: Footprint and Capabilities SemanticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9388∂habstractyÎThis document describes the part of the Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Control interface that allows a CDN to trigger activity in an interconnected CDN that is configured to deliver content on its behalf.  The upstream CDN can use this mechanism to request that the downstream CDN pre-position metadata or content or to request that it invalidate or purge metadata or content.  The upstream CDN can monitor the status of activity that it has triggered in the downstream CDN.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Murray¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepB. Niven-Jenkinsddatej’“hfilenamegrfc8007eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129829hkeywordsÑcCDNlpre-positionjinvalidateepurgekobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateXP™êgrankingGcrfcd8007irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLContent Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Control Interface / TriggersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Metadata interface enables interconnected Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) to exchange content distribution metadata in order to enable content acquisition and delivery.  The CDNI Metadata associated with a piece of content provides a downstream CDN with sufficient information for the downstream CDN to service content requests on behalf of an upstream CDN.  This document describes both a base set of CDNI Metadata and the protocol for exchanging that metadata.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepB. Niven-Jenkins¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Murray¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. Caulfield¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameeK. Maddatej’·hfilenamegrfc8006eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129828hkeywordsîcCDNlcascaded CDNncascading CDNsscontent acquisitionrcontent delegationrrequest delegationtacquisition protocoltdelivery restrictionodelivery policyrpolicy enforcementqdelivery protocolrcontent expirationkgeo-fencingjgeofencingkgeo fencinglgeo-blockingkgeoblockinglgeo blockingifootprintmcache controlkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateXP™égrankingFcrfcd8006irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) MetadatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©This document specifies a resource record (RR) for the Domain Name System (DNS) and how to use it with the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  This RR allows a HIP node to store in the DNS its Host Identity (HI), the public component of the node public-private key pair; its Host Identity Tag (HIT), a truncated hash of its public key (PK); and the domain names of its rendezvous servers (RVSs).  This document obsoletes RFC 5205.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwLuminate Wireless, Inc.dnamekJ. Laganierddatej’ﬁhfilenamegrfc8005eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129827hkeywordsÖcHIPvHost Identity ProtocoluHost Identity PayloadcDNSrDomain Name SystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXlWgrankingEcrfcd8005irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Domain Name System (DNS) ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis document defines a rendezvous extension for the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  The rendezvous extension extends HIP and the HIP Registration Extension for initiating communication between HIP nodes via HIP rendezvous servers.  Rendezvous servers improve reachability and operation when HIP nodes are multihomed or mobile.  This document obsoletes RFC 5204.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwLuminate Wireless, Inc.dnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggertddatej’ﬁhfilenamegrfc8004eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129826hkeywordsÜcHIPvHost Identity ProtocoluHost Identity PayloadjRendezvousiHIP nodesuHIP rendezvous serverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXlRgrankingDcrfcd8004irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Rendezvous ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‘This document specifies a registration mechanism for the Host Identity Protocol (HIP) that allows hosts to register with services, such as HIP rendezvous servers or middleboxes.  This document obsoletes RFC 5203.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwLuminate Wireless, Inc.dnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggertddatej’›hfilenamegrfc8003eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129825hkeywordsÖcHIPvHost Identity ProtocoluHost Identity PayloadlRegistrationhregisterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXlRgrankingCcrfcd8003irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Registration ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Certificate (CERT) parameter is a container for digital certificates. It is used for carrying these certificates in Host Identity Protocol (HIP) control packets. This document specifies the certificate parameter and the error signaling in case of a failed verification. Additionally, this document specifies the representations of Host Identity Tags (HITs) in X.509 version 3 (v3).

The concrete use cases of certificates, including how certificates are obtained and requested and which actions are taken upon successful or failed verification, are specific to the scenario in which the certificates are used. Hence, the definition of these scenario-specific aspects is left to the documents that use the CERT parameter.

This document updates RFC 7401 and obsoletes RFC 6253.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxAlbstadt-Sigmaringen UniversitydnamegT. Heer¢kaffiliationvUniversity of HelsinkidnamekS. Varjonenddatej’·hfilenamegrfc8002eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129824hkeywordsÅxHip Certificate ExtensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXlPgrankingBcrfcd8002irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Host Identity Protocol CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˘This document provides extensions for Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE), including GMPLS, to support automatic collection of Shared Risk Link Group (SRLG) information for the TE link formed by a Label Switched Path (LSP).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationuTelefonica Global CTOdnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamekC. Margaria¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejM. Hartley¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefZ. AliddatejœPhfilenamegrfc8001eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129823hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXxÜgrankingAcrfcd8001irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRSVP-TE Extensions for Collecting Shared Risk Link Group (SRLG) InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document presents requirements for the deployment of the NFSv4 protocols for the construction of an NFSv4 file namespace in environments with multiple NFSv4 Domains.  To participate in an NFSv4 multi-domain file namespace, the server must offer a multi-domain-capable file system and support RPCSEC_GSS for user authentication.  In most instances, the server must also support identity-mapping services.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamejA. Adamson¢kaffiliationlCryptonectordnamekN. Williamsddatej’‚hfilenamegrfc8000eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129822hkeywordsÑlmulti-domainx multi-domain-capable file systemuFederated File SystemeFedFSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX?f]granking@crfcd8000irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Requirements for NFSv4 Multi-Domain Namespace DeploymentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This document describes the use of a well-known Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) community for destination-based blackholing in IP networks.  This well-known advisory transitive BGP community named "BLACKHOLE" allows an origin Autonomous System (AS) to specify that a neighboring network should discard any traffic destined towards the tagged IP prefix.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfDE-CIXdnamegT. King¢kaffiliationfDE-CIXdnamejC. Dietzel¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekJ. Snijders¢kaffiliationkSpaceNet AGdnamejG. Doering¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejG. Hankinsddatej’‚hfilenamegrfc7999eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129821hkeywordsÑjwell-knownjwell knowndRTBHxRemotely Triggered BlackholingkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateX]granking?crfcd7999irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesBLACKHOLE CommunitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxêThis document describes some aspects of the "prep tool" that is expected to be created when the new xml2rfc version 3 specification is deployed.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamemJ. Hildebrandddatej’€hfilenamegrfc7998eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129820hkeywordsÉixml2rfcv3bv3fformatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXT∑granking>crfcd7998irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex0"xml2rfc" Version 3 Preparation Tool DescriptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyIIn order to support the internationalization of protocols and a more diverse Internet community, the RFC Series must evolve to allow for the use of non-ASCII characters in RFCs. While English remains the required language of the Series, the encoding of future RFCs will be in UTF-8, allowing for a broader range of characters than typically used in the English language. This document describes the RFC Editor requirements and gives guidance regarding the use of non-ASCII characters in RFCs.

This document updates RFC 7322. Please view this document in PDF form to see the full text.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekH. Flanaganddatej’⁄hfilenamegrfc7997eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129819hkeywordsájRFC SerieseUTF-8eASCIIfformatinon-ASCIIbv3ixml2rfcv3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXT∂granking=crfcd7997irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex'The Use of Non-ASCII Characters in RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920¥habstractx∑This document specifies SVG 1.2 RFC -- an SVG profile for use in diagrams that may appear in RFCs -- and considers some of the issues concerning the creation and use of such diagrams.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxThe University of AucklanddnamekN. Brownleeddatej’Ÿhfilenamegrfc7996eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129818hkeywordsÑcRFCbv3ixml2rfcv3fformatkobsoletedByÅd9896epages5opublicationDateXT∂granking<crfcd7996irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex"SVG Drawings for RFCs: SVG 1.2 RFCdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920¥habstractx¸This document discusses options and requirements for the PDF rendering of RFCs in the RFC Series, as outlined in RFC 6949.  It also discusses the use of PDF for Internet-Drafts, and available or needed software tools for producing and working with PDF.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekL. Masinter¢kaffiliationeAdobednamehM. Hardyddatej’ÿhfilenamegrfc7995eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129817hkeywordsÑtRequests for Commentixml2rfcv3bv3fformatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXTµgranking;crfcd7995irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlesPDF Format for RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920¥habstracty∂In 2013, after a great deal of community discussion, the decision was made to shift from the plain-text, ASCII-only canonical format for RFCs to XML as the canonical format with more human-readable formats rendered from that XML.  The high-level requirements that informed this change were defined in RFC 6949, "RFC Series Format Requirements and Future Development".  Plain text remains an important format for many in the IETF community, and it will be one of the publication formats rendered from the XML.  This document outlines the rendering requirements for the plain-text RFC publication format.  These requirements do not apply to plain-text RFCs published before the format transition.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekH. Flanaganddatej’ÿhfilenamegrfc7994eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129816hkeywordsácRFCeASCIIfformatjplain-textjplain textixml2rfcv3bv3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXT¥granking:crfcd7994irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex Requirements for Plain-Text RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920¥habstractyøThe HTML format for RFCs assigns style guidance to a Cascading Style Sheet (CSS) specifically defined for the RFC Series.  The embedded, default CSS as included by the RFC Editor is expected to take into account accessibility needs and to be built along a responsive design model.  This document describes the requirements for the default CSS used by the RFC Editor.  The class names are based on the classes defined in "HTML for RFCs" (RFC 7992).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekH. Flanaganddatej’€hfilenamegrfc7993eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129815hkeywordsÑbv3ixml2rfcv3fformatdhtmlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXT¥granking9crfcd7993irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex2Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) Requirements for RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920¥habstracty;In order to meet the evolving needs of the Internet community, the canonical format for RFCs is changing from a plain-text, ASCII-only format to an XML format that will, in turn, be rendered into several publication formats.  This document defines the HTML format that will be rendered for an RFC or Internet-Draft.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamemJ. Hildebrand¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej’ÿhfilenamegrfc7992eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129814hkeywordsÖdhtmlccssbv3ixml2rfcv3fformatkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateXT≥granking8crfcd7992irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitletHTML Format for RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920¥habstractyThis document defines the "xml2rfc" version 3 vocabulary: an XML-based language used for writing RFCs and Internet-Drafts.  It is heavily derived from the version 2 vocabulary that is also under discussion.  This document obsoletes the v2 grammar described in RFC 7749.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej’ÿhfilenamegrfc7991eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129813hkeywordsÉbv3ixml2rfcv3fformatkobsoletedByÄepagesóopublicationDateXT≥granking7crfcd7991irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex"The "xml2rfc" Version 3 VocabularydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9920¥habstracty>In order to improve the readability of RFCs while supporting their archivability, the canonical format of the RFC Series will be transitioning from plain-text ASCII to XML using the xml2rfc version 3 vocabulary; different publication formats will be rendered from that base document.  With these changes comes an increase in complexity for authors, consumers, and the publisher of RFCs.  This document serves as the framework that provides the problem statement, lays out a road map of the documents that capture the specific requirements, and describes the transition plan.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekH. Flanaganddatej’ÿhfilenamegrfc7990eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129812hkeywordsÉfFormatixml2rfcv3bv3kobsoletedByÅd9720epagesopublicationDateXT≤granking6crfcd7990irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitletRFC Format FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an end-to-end session identifier for use in IP-based multimedia communication systems that enables endpoints, intermediary devices, and management systems to identify a session end-to-end, associate multiple endpoints with a given multipoint conference, track communication sessions when they are redirected, and associate one or more media flows with a given communication session. While the identifier is intended to work across multiple protocols, this document describes its usage in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).

This document also describes a backwards-compatibility mechanism for an existing session identifier implementation (RFC 7329) that is sufficiently different from the procedures defined in this document.

This document obsoletes RFC 7329.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiC. Pearce¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Giraltddatej’‡hfilenamegrfc7989eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymginsipiddfullx*insipid - INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdnamex INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdtypebwgbidjdoc-129811hkeywordsàcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolotroubleshootingjSession-IDrsession identifiergH460.27premote parameterdUUIDkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateXVgranking5crfcd7989irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOEnd-to-End Session Identification in IP-Based Multimedia Communication NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFCs 6513, 6514, and other RFCs describe procedures by which a Service Provider may offer Multicast VPN (MVPN) service to its customers.  These procedures create point-to-multipoint (P2MP) or multipoint-to-multipoint (MP2MP) trees across the Service Provider's backbone.  One type of P2MP tree that may be used is known as an "Ingress Replication (IR) tunnel".  In an IR tunnel, a parent node need not be directly connected to its child nodes.  When a parent node has to send a multicast data packet to its n child nodes, it does not use Layer 2 multicast, IP multicast, or MPLS multicast to do so.  Rather, it makes n individual copies, and then unicasts each copy, through an IP or MPLS unicast tunnel, to exactly one child node.  While the prior MVPN specifications allow the use of IR tunnels, those specifications are not always very clear or explicit about how the MVPN protocol elements and procedures are applied to IR tunnels.  This document updates RFCs 6513 and 6514 by adding additional details that are specific to the use of IR tunnels.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationpSproute NetworksdnamenK. Subramanian¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehZ. ZhangddatejœOhfilenamegrfc7988eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129810hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW˜)granking4crfcd7988irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Ingress Replication Tunnels in Multicast VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+Corruption of the Remaining Lifetime field in a Link State Protocol Data Unit (LSP) can go undetected.  In certain scenarios, this may cause or exacerbate flooding storms.  It is also a possible denial-of-service attack vector.  This document defines a backwards-compatible solution to this problem.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Wells¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. GredlerddatejœOhfilenamegrfc7987eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129809hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateXVgranking3crfcd7987irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex IS-IS Minimum Remaining LifetimedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxêThis document defines a set of new properties for iCalendar data and extends the use of some existing properties to the entire iCalendar object.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej’·hfilenamegrfc7986eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129808hkeywordsÉfalarmskcalendaringiiCalendarkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX‚dgranking2crfcd7986irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNew Properties for iCalendardtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document analyzes security threats to Simplified Multicast Forwarding (SMF), including vulnerabilities of duplicate packet detection and relay set selection mechanisms. This document is not intended to propose solutions to the threats described.

