opublicationDateW„e≠granking.crfcd7982irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcMeasurement of Round-Trip Time and Fractional Loss Using Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new optional Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) TLV named CAPABILITY, formed of multiple sub-TLVs, which allows a router to announce its capabilities within an IS-IS level or the entire routing domain.  This document obsoletes RFC 4971.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegM. ChenddatejœMhfilenamegrfc7981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129803hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateXSgranking-crfcd7981irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3IS-IS Extensions for Advertising Router InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractygComplexity is a widely used parameter in network design, yet there is no generally accepted definition of the term. Complexity metrics exist in a wide range of research papers, but most of these address only a particular aspect of a network, for example, the complexity of a graph or software. While it may be impossible to define a metric for overall network complexity, there is a desire to better understand the complexity of a network as a whole, as deployed today to provide Internet services. This document provides a framework to guide research on the topic of network complexity as well as some practical examples for trade-offs in networking.

This document summarizes the work of the IRTF's Network Complexity Research Group (NCRG) at the time of its closure. It does not present final results, but a snapshot of an ongoing activity, as a basis for future work.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. Behringer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehR. White¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej’‚hfilenamegrfc7980eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129802hkeywordsÜkComplicatedgFragileqSelf-organizationiTrade-offnTechnical DebtjDependencykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX
fˆgranking,crfcd7980irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex+A Framework for Defining Network ComplexitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThe U.S.  National Telecommunications and Information Administration (NTIA) solicited a request from the Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN) to propose how the NTIA should end its oversight of the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) functions.  After broad consultations, ICANN in turn created the IANA Stewardship Transition Coordination Group.  That group solicited proposals for the three major IANA functions: names, numbers, and protocol parameters.  This document contains the IETF response to that solicitation for protocol parameters.  It was included in an aggregate response to the NTIA alongside those for names and numbering resources that are being developed by their respective operational communities.  A reference to that response may be found in the introduction, and additional correspondence is included in the Appendix.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. HousleyddatejŒ¿hfilenamegrfc7979eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhianaplandfullx0ianaplan - Planning for the IANA/NTIA Transitiondnamex%Planning for the IANA/NTIA Transitiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129801hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateWƒŸıgranking+crfcd7979irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÖResponse to the IANA Stewardship Transition Coordination Group (ICG) Request for Proposals on the IANA Protocol Parameters RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®The IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol includes an optional mechanism (specified in RFC 7178) called RBridge Channel for the transmission of typed messages between TRILL switches in the same campus and the transmission of such messages between TRILL switches and end stations on the same link.  This document specifies extensions to the RBridge Channel protocol header to support two features as follows: (1) a standard method to tunnel payloads whose type can be indicated by Ethertype through encapsulation in RBridge Channel messages; and (2) a method to support security facilities for RBridge Channel messages.  This document updates RFC 7178.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationjIPinfusiondnamehM. Umair¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Liddatej’ﬁhfilenamegrfc7978eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129800hkeywordsÇftunnelmencapsulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÓ€ngranking*crfcd7978irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): RBridge Channel Header ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThe WebSocket protocol enables two-way real-time communication between clients and servers in situations where direct access to TCP and UDP is not available (for example, from within JavaScript in a web browser).  This document specifies a new WebSocket subprotocol as a reliable transport mechanism between Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) clients and relays to enable usage of MSRP in new scenarios.  This document normatively updates RFCs 4975 and 4976.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdXuradnamejP. Dunkley¢kaffiliationdXuradnamelG. Llewellyn¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejV. Pascual¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoR. Ravindranathddatej’ﬁhfilenamegrfc7977eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129799hkeywordsÇdMSRPiWebSocketkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW„.granking)crfcd7977irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSThe WebSocket Protocol as a Transport for the Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) has identified cases where different SIP private header extensions referred to as "P-" header fields, and defined in RFC 7315, need to be included in SIP requests and responses currently not allowed according to RFC 7315. This document updates RFC 7315, in order to allow inclusion of the affected "P-" header fields in such requests and responses.

This document also makes updates for RFC 7315 in order to fix misalignments that occurred when RFC 3455 was updated and obsoleted by RFC 7315.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejN. Biondic¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiroddatej’ﬂhfilenamegrfc7976eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129798hkeywordsÉbP-d3GPPcIMSkobsoletedByÅd9878epagesopublicationDateW‡ZFgranking(crfcd7976irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpUpdates to Private Header (P-Header) Extension Usage in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Requests and ResponsesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThe Request Routing interface comprises (1) the asynchronous advertisement of footprint and capabilities by a downstream Content Delivery Network (CDN) that allows an upstream CDN to decide whether to redirect particular user requests to that downstream CDN; and (2) the synchronous operation of an upstream CDN requesting whether a downstream CDN is prepared to accept a user request and of a downstream CDN responding with how to actually redirect the user request.  This document describes an interface for the latter part, i.e., the CDNI Request Routing Redirection interface.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepB. Niven-Jenkins¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamerR. van Brandenburgddatej’ﬂhfilenamegrfc7975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129797hkeywordsÇdHTTPcDNSkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateX␀Vﬂgranking'crfcd7975irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXRequest Routing Redirection Interface for Content Delivery Network (CDN) InterconnectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty…Recent RFCs have discussed issues with host identification in IP address-sharing systems, such as address/prefix-sharing devices and application-layer proxies.  Potential solutions for revealing a host identifier in shared address deployments have also been discussed.  This memo describes the design, deployment, and privacy considerations for one such solution in operational use on the Internet today that uses a TCP option to transmit a host identifier.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlAkamai, Inc.dnamekB. Williams¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Wingddatej’’hfilenamegrfc7974eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129796hkeywordsÖrPolicy enforcementoAddress sharingcNATkHost revealgHost-IDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXRgranking&crfcd7974irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex2An Experimental TCP Option for Host IdentificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytWhen carried over Layer 2 technologies such as Ethernet, IPv6 datagrams using Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (LoWPAN) encapsulation as defined in RFC 4944 must be identified so the receiver can correctly interpret the encoded IPv6 datagram.  The IETF officially requested the assignment of an Ethertype for that purpose and this document reports that assignment.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Duffyddatej’›hfilenamegrfc7973eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129795hkeywordsÉg6lowpanrheader compressioniethertypekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateX,⁄çgranking%crfcd7973irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhAssignment of an Ethertype for IPv6 with Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (LoWPAN) EncapsulationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEntertainment Identifier Registry (EIDR) Identifiers are used for the globally unique identification of motion picture and television content. This document defines the formal Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace Identifier (NID) for EIDR Identifiers.

This document obsoletes RFC 7302.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSandflow Consulting LLCdnamejP. Lemieuxddatej’€hfilenamegrfc7972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129794hkeywordsÉdEIDRx!Entertainment Identifier Registrygand URNkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateW“Dágranking$crfcd7972irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAEntertainment Identifier Registry (EIDR) URN Namespace DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTMany Internet applications are used to access resources such as pieces of information or server processes that are available in several equivalent replicas on different hosts.  This includes, but is not limited to, peer-to-peer file sharing applications.  The goal of Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) is to provide guidance to applications that have to select one or several hosts from a set of candidates capable of providing a desired resource.  This memo discusses deployment-related issues of ALTO.  It addresses different use cases of ALTO such as peer-to-peer file sharing and Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and presents corresponding examples.  The document also includes recommendations for network administrators and application designers planning to deploy ALTO, such as recommendations on how to generate ALTO map information.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtHochschule DarmstadtdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnameiS. Kiesel¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiM. Scharf¢kaffiliationfBENOCSdnameiH. Seidel¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. PrevididdatejœGhfilenamegrfc7971eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129793hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesMopublicationDateWˆÌ±granking#crfcd7971irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGApplication-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Deployment ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•The Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) defines a data representation for security incident reports and indicators commonly exchanged by operational security teams for mitigation and watch and warning.  This document describes an updated information model for the IODEF and provides an associated data model specified with the XML schema.  This new information and data model obsoletes RFCs 5070 and 6685.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdCERTdnamejR. Danyliwddatej’÷hfilenamegrfc7970eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-129792hkeywordsçtincident data formatoincident reportwcyber threat indicatorsxcomputer security incidentx(computer security incident response teameCSIRTdCERTusecurity data sharingx)Computer Network Defense Service ProvidereCNDSPsinformation sharingxautomated information sharingpcyber indicatorskobsoletedByÄepages¨opublicationDateX?fZgranking"crfcd7970irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The Incident Object Description Exchange Format Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'DHCP servers have evolved over the years to provide significant functionality beyond that described in the DHCP base specifications.  One aspect of this functionality is support for context-specific configuration information.  This memo describes some such features and explains their operation.fadNameoSuresh Krishnandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalskiddatej’‹hfilenamegrfc7969eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129791hkeywordsáfdhcpv4fdhcpv6xrelay-agents (relay agents)pmultiple subnetsgsubnetselinkshprefixeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXPgranking!crfcd7969irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Customizing DHCP Configuration on the Basis of Network TopologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) uses distribution trees to deliver multi-destination frames. Multiple trees can be used by an ingress Routing Bridge (RBridge) for flows, regardless of the VLAN, Fine-Grained Label (FGL), and/or multicast group of the flow. Different ingress RBridges may choose different distribution trees for TRILL Data packets in the same VLAN, FGL, and/or multicast group. To avoid unnecessary link utilization, distribution trees should be pruned based on one or more of the following: VLAN, FGL, or multicast destination address. If any VLAN, FGL, or multicast group can be sent on any tree, for typical fast-path hardware, the amount of pruning information is multiplied by the number of trees, but there is limited hardware capacity for such pruning information.

This document specifies an optional facility to restrict the TRILL Data packets sent on particular distribution trees by VLAN, FGL, and/or multicast groups, thus reducing the total amount of pruning information so that it can more easily be accommodated by fast-path hardware.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Hao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Chen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Chatterjeeddatej’⁄hfilenamegrfc7968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129790hkeywordsÉdVLANrfine-grained labelimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW∆ù‰granking crfcd7968irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Using Data Labels for Tree Selection for Multi-Destination DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty
There can be machine-to-machine (M2M) scenarios where server responses to client requests are redundant. This kind of open-loop exchange (with no response path from the server to the client) may be desired to minimize resource consumption in constrained systems while updating many resources simultaneously or performing high-frequency updates. CoAP already provides Non-confirmable (NON) messages that are not acknowledged by the recipient. However, the request/response semantics still require the server to respond with a status code indicating "the result of the attempt to understand and satisfy the request", per RFC 7252.

This specification introduces a CoAP option called 'No-Response'. Using this option, the client can explicitly express to the server its disinterest in all responses against the particular request. This option also provides granular control to enable expression of disinterest to a particular response class or a combination of response classes. The server MAY decide to suppress the response by not transmitting it back to the client according to the value of the No-Response option in the request. This option may be effective for both unicast and multicast requests. This document also discusses a few examples of applications that benefit from this option.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxTata Consultancy Services Ltd.dnamepA. Bhattacharyya¢kaffiliationxTata Consultancy Services Ltd.dnamepS. Bandyopadhyay¢kaffiliationxTata Consultancy Services Ltd.dnamefA. Pal¢kaffiliationxTata Consultancy Services Ltd.dnamegT. Boseddatej’’hfilenamegrfc7967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129789hkeywordsÅkNo-ResponsekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWƒŸıgrankingcrfcd7967irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexEConstrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Option for No Server ResponsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÇThis specification specifies requirements for providing Diameter security at the level of individual Attribute-Value Pairs (AVPs).fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationpBroadcom LimiteddnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationrInternet SolutionsdnameiK. Pillayddatej’€hfilenamegrfc7966eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129788hkeywordsÇhDiametersEnd-to-End SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÊ±grankingcrfcd7966irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoSecurity at the Attribute-Value Pair (AVP) Level for Non-neighboring Diameter Nodes: Scenarios and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√Many transport services require that user traffic, in the form of Pseudowires (PWs), be delivered via either a single co-routed bidirectional tunnel or two unidirectional tunnels that share the same routes.  This document defines an optional extension to the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) that enables the binding between PWs and the underlying Traffic Engineering (TE) tunnels.  The extension applies to both single-segment and multi-segment PWs.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefW. Cao¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Takacs¢kaffiliation`dnamefP. PanddatejœGhfilenamegrfc7965eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129787hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW∆ù„grankingcrfcd7965irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJLDP Extensions for Pseudowire Binding to Label Switched Path (LSP) TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.Routing information reduction by BGP Route Reflection or Confederation can result in persistent internal BGP route oscillations with certain routing setups and network topologies.  This document specifies two sets of additional paths that can be used to eliminate these route oscillations in a network.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnameiD. Walton¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej’›hfilenamegrfc7964eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129786hkeywordsÅuBGP churn oscillationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateWÌ¶¥grankingcrfcd7964irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Solutions for BGP Persistent Route OscillationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîRFCs 4328 and 7139 provide signaling extensions in Resource ReserVation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) to control the full set of Optical Transport Network (OTN) features.  However, these specifications do not cover the additional Optical channel Data Unit (ODU) containers defined in G.Sup43 (ODU1e, ODU3e1, and ODU3e2).  This document defines new Signal Types for these additional containers.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekA. Bonfanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Hartley¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Zhangddatej’⁄hfilenamegrfc7963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129785hkeywordsÅeGMPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW¿’#grankingcrfcd7963irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXRSVP-TE Extension for Additional Signal Types in G.709 Optical Transport Networks (OTNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty≥This document presents a taxonomy of a set of "Alternative Network Deployments" that emerged in the last decade with the aim of bringing Internet connectivity to people or providing a local communication infrastructure to serve various complementary needs and objectives. They employ architectures and topologies different from those of mainstream networks and rely on alternative governance and business models.

The document also surveys the technologies deployed in these networks, and their differing architectural characteristics, including a set of definitions and shared properties.

The classification considers models such as Community Networks, Wireless Internet Service Providers (WISPs), networks owned by individuals but leased out to network operators who use them as a low-cost medium to reach the underserved population, networks that provide connectivity by sharing wireless resources of the users, and rural utility cooperatives.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of ZaragozadnamejJ. Saldana¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamenA. Arcia-Moret¢kaffiliationfiMindsdnamehB. Braem¢kaffiliationtThe Abdus Salam ICTPdnameoE. Pietrosemoli¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednameoA. Sathiaseelan¢kaffiliationtThe Abdus Salam ICTPdnamejM. Zennaroddatej’⁄hfilenamegrfc7962eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgaiadfullx,gaia - Global Access to the Internet for Alldnamex%Global Access to the Internet for Alldtypebrgbidjdoc-129784hkeywordsáxalternative network deploymentsrcommunity networksuuser-centric networksx#Wireless Internet Service Providersrmainstream networkdgaiax%global access to the Internet for allkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateWƒŸÙgrankingcrfcd7962irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex\Alternative Network Deployments: Taxonomy, Characterization, Technologies, and ArchitecturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis document specifies a TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) IS-IS application sub-TLV that enables the reporting by a TRILL switch of sets of addresses.  Each set of addresses reports all of the addresses that designate the same interface (port) and also reports the TRILL switch by which that interface is reachable.  For example, a 48-bit MAC (Media Access Control) address, IPv4 address, and IPv6 address can be reported as all corresponding to the same interface reachable by a particular TRILL switch.  Such information could be used in some cases to synthesize responses to, or bypass the need for, the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP), the IPv6 Neighbor Discovery (ND) protocol, or the flooding of unknown MAC addresses.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Yizhouddatej’Ÿhfilenamegrfc7961eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129783hkeywordsÉlreachabilitycAFNhtemplatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWæ·grankingcrfcd7961irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Interface Addresses APPsub-TLVdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) introduces a mechanism for expressing domain-level policies and preferences for email message validation, disposition, and reporting.  However, the DMARC mechanism enables potentially disruptive interoperability issues when messages do not flow directly from the author's administrative domain to the final Recipients.  Collectively, these email flows are referred to as "indirect email flows".  This document describes these interoperability issues and presents possible methods for addressing them.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnameiF. Martin¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationmdmarcian inc.dnamejT. Draegen¢kaffiliationeYahoodnameiE. Zwicky¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamekK. Andersenddatej’€hfilenamegrfc7960eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedmarcdfullxDdmarc - Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancednamex<Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129782hkeywordsÑeDMARCdSMTPdDKIMcSPFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÌ¶¥grankingcrfcd7960irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄInteroperability Issues between Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) and Indirect Email FlowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThe Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) is a RESTful transfer protocol for constrained nodes and networks. Basic CoAP messages work well for small payloads from sensors and actuators; however, applications will need to transfer larger payloads occasionally -- for instance, for firmware updates. In contrast to HTTP, where TCP does the grunt work of segmenting and resequencing, CoAP is based on datagram transports such as UDP or Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS). These transports only offer fragmentation, which is even more problematic in constrained nodes and networks, limiting the maximum size of resource representations that can practically be transferred.

Instead of relying on IP fragmentation, this specification extends basic CoAP with a pair of "Block" options for transferring multiple blocks of information from a resource representation in multiple request-response pairs. In many important cases, the Block options enable a server to be truly stateless: the server can handle each block transfer separately, with no need for a connection setup or other server-side memory of previous block transfers. Essentially, the Block options provide a minimal way to transfer larger representations in a block-wise fashion.

A CoAP implementation that does not support these options generally is limited in the size of the representations that can be exchanged, so there is an expectation that the Block options will be widely used in CoAP implementations. Therefore, this specification updates RFC 7252.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationcARMdnameiZ. Shelbyddatej’Ÿhfilenamegrfc7959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129781hkeywordsàdCoAPx Constrained Application ProtocoldRESTrInternet of ThingscIoTlSmart ObjectqEmbedded InternetpConstrained NodekobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateW¿éNgrankingcrfcd7959irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCBlock-Wise Transfers in the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8323¥habstractykThe root zone of the Domain Name System (DNS) has been cryptographically signed using DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC).

In order to obtain secure answers from the root zone of the DNS using DNSSEC, a client must configure a suitable trust anchor. This document describes the format and publication mechanisms IANA has used to distribute the DNSSEC trust anchors.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationhKirei ABdnamekJ. Schlyter¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiG. Bailey¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej’€hfilenamegrfc7958eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129780hkeywordsÑcDNSeICANNdIANAcKSKkobsoletedByÅd9718epagesopublicationDateW∆ù‚grankingcrfcd7958irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex1DNSSEC Trust Anchor Publication for the Root ZonedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyRFC 5727 defined several processes for the former Real-time Applications and Infrastructure (RAI) area.  These processes include the evolution of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and related protocols, as well as the operation of the DISPATCH and SIPCORE working groups.  This document updates RFC 5727 to allow flexibility for the area and working group structure, while preserving the SIP change processes.  It also generalizes the DISPATCH working group processes so that they can be easily adopted by other working groups.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Cooper¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej’⁄hfilenamegrfc7957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129779hkeywordsÑhdispatchcRAIcARTjsip-changekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWæ·grankingcrfcd7957irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberCetotaletitlex8DISPATCH-Style Working Groups and the SIP Change ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe base TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol provides optimal pair-wise data frame forwarding for Layer 2 intra-subnet traffic but not for Layer 3 inter-subnet traffic. A centralized gateway solution is typically used for Layer 3 inter-subnet traffic forwarding but has the following issues:

1. Sub-optimum forwarding paths for inter-subnet traffic.

2. A centralized gateway that may need to support a very large number of gateway interfaces in a Data Center, one per tenant per Data Label used by that tenant, to provide interconnect functionality for all the Layer 2 Virtual Networks in a TRILL campus.

3. A traffic bottleneck at the gateway.

This document specifies an optional TRILL distributed gateway solution that resolves these centralized gateway issues.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefW. Hao¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationhMediaTecdnameeA. Qu¢kaffiliationlEquinix Inc.dnamejM. Durrani¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamenP. Sivamuruganddatej’÷hfilenamegrfc7956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129778hkeywordsÇftenantkdata centerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW–r`grankingcrfcd7956irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Distributed Layer 3 GatewaydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document proposes a framework for the management of the Locator/ ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Endpoint Identifier (EID) address block.  The framework described relies on hierarchical distribution of the address space, granting temporary usage of prefixes of such space to requesting organizations.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqTelecom ParisTechdnamejL. Iannone¢kaffiliationuBredbandsfylket TromsdnamelR. Jorgensen¢kaffiliationpVirtualized, LLCdnameiD. Conrad¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatejœ-hfilenamegrfc7955eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129777hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateW„%grankingcrfcd7955irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcManagement Guidelines for the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Endpoint Identifier (EID) BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document directs IANA to allocate a /32 IPv6 prefix for use with the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP).  The prefix will be used for local intra-domain routing and global endpoint identification, by sites deploying LISP as Endpoint Identifier (EID) addressing space.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqTelecom ParisTechdnamejL. Iannone¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehD. Lewis¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliation`dnameiV. Fullerddatejœ-hfilenamegrfc7954eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129776hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW„$grankingcrfcd7954irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexELocator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Endpoint Identifier (EID) BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This document specifies a new iCalendar (RFC 5545) component that allows the publication of available and unavailable time periods associated with a calendar user. This component can be used in standard iCalendar free-busy lookups, including the iCalendar Transport-independent Interoperability Protocol (iTIP; RFC 5546) free-busy requests, to generate repeating blocks of available or busy time with exceptions as needed.

This document also defines extensions to the Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) calendar access protocol (RFC 4791) and the associated scheduling protocol (RFC 6638) to specify how this new calendar component can be used when evaluating free-busy time.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationsSpherical Cow GroupdnamekM. Douglassddatej’◊hfilenamegrfc7953eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129775hkeywordsÖlavailabilitykcalendaringifree-busyiiCalendarfCalDAVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW¥ﬂßgrankingcrfcd7953irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuCalendar AvailabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‰This document defines a YANG extension that allows for defining metadata annotations in YANG modules.  The document also specifies XML and JSON encoding of annotations and other rules for annotating instances of YANG data nodes.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnameiL. Lhotkaddatej’«hfilenamegrfc7952eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129774hkeywordsÇtmetadata annotationsnYANG extensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW∆ù·grankingcrfcd7952irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Defining and Using Metadata with YANGdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÊThis document defines encoding rules for representing configuration data, state data, parameters of Remote Procedure Call (RPC) operations or actions, and notifications defined using YANG as JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) text.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnameiL. Lhotkaddatej’»hfilenamegrfc7951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129773hkeywordsÇfI-JSONhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW∆ù·grankingcrfcd7951irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'JSON Encoding of Data Modeled with YANGdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"YANG is a data modeling language used to model configuration data, state data, Remote Procedure Calls, and notifications for network management protocols.  This document describes the syntax and semantics of version 1.1 of the YANG language.  YANG version 1.1 is a maintenance release of the YANG language, addressing ambiguities and defects in the original specification.  There are a small number of backward incompatibilities from YANG version 1.  This document also specifies the YANG mappings to the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF).fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej’‘hfilenamegrfc7950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129772hkeywordsÉgNETCONFcXMLmdata modelingkobsoletedByÄepagesŸopublicationDateW∆ù‡grankingcrfcd7950irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#The YANG 1.1 Data Modeling LanguagedtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8342d8526∂habstractyuThis document defines a mechanism to use IPv4 to transport OSPFv3 packets.  Using OSPFv3 over IPv4 with the existing OSPFv3 Address Family extension can simplify transition from an OSPFv2 IPv4-only routing domain to an OSPFv3 dual-stack routing domain.  This document updates RFC 5838 to support virtual links in the IPv4 unicast address family when using OSPFv3 over IPv4.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegI. Chen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekR. Atkinsonddatej’÷hfilenamegrfc7949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129771hkeywordsÇnIPv4 transportqOSPFv3 transitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW´P»grankingcrfcd7949irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$OSPFv3 over IPv4 for IPv6 TransitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe popularity of Internet Exchange Points (IXPs) brings new challenges to interconnecting networks. While bilateral External BGP (EBGP) sessions between exchange participants were historically the most common means of exchanging reachability information over an IXP, the overhead associated with this interconnection method causes serious operational and administrative scaling problems for IXP participants.

Multilateral interconnection using Internet route servers can dramatically reduce the administrative and operational overhead associated with connecting to IXPs; in some cases, route servers are used by IXP participants as their preferred means of exchanging routing information.

This document describes operational considerations for multilateral interconnections at IXPs.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdINEXdnamekN. Hilliard¢kaffiliationjBigWave ITdnamekE. Jasinska¢kaffiliationlBloomberg LPdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationxAkamai Technologies B.V.dnameiN. Bakkerddatej’{hfilenamegrfc7948eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129770hkeywordsÅdGROWkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW–r`grankingcrfcd7948irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Internet Exchange BGP Route Server OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document outlines a specification for multilateral interconnections at Internet Exchange Points (IXPs).  Multilateral interconnection is a method of exchanging routing information among three or more External BGP (EBGP) speakers using a single intermediate broker system, referred to as a route server.  Route servers are typically used on shared access media networks, such as IXPs, to facilitate simplified interconnection among multiple Internet routers.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjBigWave ITdnamekE. Jasinska¢kaffiliationdINEXdnamekN. Hilliard¢kaffiliationlBloomberg LPdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationxAkamai Technologies B.V.dnameiN. Bakkerddatej’◊hfilenamegrfc7947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129769hkeywordsÅcIDRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW–r^grankingcrfcd7947irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Internet Exchange BGP Route ServerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{GeoJSON is a geospatial data interchange format based on JavaScript Object Notation (JSON).  It defines several types of JSON objects and the manner in which they are combined to represent data about geographic features, their properties, and their spatial extents.  GeoJSON uses a geographic coordinate reference system, World Geodetic System 1984, and units of decimal degrees.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationiHobu Inc.dnameiH. Butler¢kaffiliationgCadcorpdnamegM. Daly¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Doyle¢kaffiliationfMapboxdnamejS. Gillies¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Hagen¢kaffiliationkPlanet LabsdnameiT. Schaubddatej’◊hfilenamegrfc7946eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeojsondfullxgeojson - Geographic JSONdnameoGeographic JSONdtypebwgbidjdoc-129768hkeywordsÉdJSONjGeospatialxJavaScript Object NotationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW´≈Ugranking
