estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlerThe GeoJSON FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty?This document presents a number of considerations regarding evaluating Information-Centric Networking (ICN) and sheds some light on the impact of ICN on network security.  It also surveys the evaluation tools currently available to researchers in the ICN area and provides suggestions regarding methodology and metrics.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjTravelpingdnamenK. Pentikousis¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Ohlman¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationpIntracom TelecomdnameiS. Spirou¢kaffiliationsPolitecnico di BaridnameiG. BoggiaddatejœAhfilenamegrfc7945eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-129767hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateW‰eˆgranking	crfcd7945irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexFInformation-Centric Networking: Evaluation and Security ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµWhen making routing and resource allocation decisions, Diameter nodes currently have no generic mechanism to determine the relative priority of Diameter messages.  This document addresses this by defining a mechanism to allow Diameter endpoints to indicate the relative priority of Diameter transactions.  With this information, Diameter nodes can factor that priority into routing, resource allocation, and overload abatement decisions.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejS. Donovanddatej’÷hfilenamegrfc7944eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129766hkeywordsÇhDiameterhOverloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW£ÿgrankingcrfcd7944irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Diameter Routing Message PrioritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyZThis document describes a method for selecting IPv6 Interface Identifiers that can be employed by Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) servers when leasing non-temporary IPv6 addresses to DHCPv6 clients.  This method is a DHCPv6 server-side algorithm that does not require any updates to the existing DHCPv6 specifications.  The aforementioned method results in stable addresses within each subnet, even in the presence of multiple DHCPv6 servers or DHCPv6 server reinstallments.  It is a DHCPv6 variant of the method specified in RFC 7217 for IPv6 Stateless Address Autoconfiguration.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liuddatej’’hfilenamegrfc7943eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129765hkeywordsÜhsecuritygprivacyjresiliencyfattackhscanninghtrackingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateW‡ZEgrankingcrfcd7943irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexáA Method for Generating Semantically Opaque Interface Identifiers (IIDs) with the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÿThis document describes a simple process that allows authors of Internet-Drafts to record the status of known implementations by including an Implementation Status section. This will allow reviewers and working groups to assign due consideration to documents that have the benefit of running code, which may serve as evidence of valuable experimentation and feedback that have made the implemented protocols more mature.

This process is not mandatory. Authors of Internet-Drafts are encouraged to consider using the process for their documents, and working groups are invited to think about applying the process to all of their protocol specifications. This document obsoletes RFC 6982, advancing it to a Best Current Practice.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfIntuitdnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrelddatejœ=hfilenamegrfc7942eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129764hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÑ(Ãgrankingcrfcd7942irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÕetotaletitlexFImproving Awareness of Running Code: The Implementation Status SectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySource Description (SDES) items are normally transported in the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP).  In some cases, it can be beneficial to speed up the delivery of these items.  The main case is when a new synchronization source (SSRC) joins an RTP session and the receivers need this source's identity, relation to other sources, or its synchronization context, all of which may be fully or partially identified using SDES items.  To enable this optimization, this document specifies a new RTP header extension that can carry SDES items.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Burman¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Zanatyddatejœ0hfilenamegrfc7941eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129763hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW∆ù‡grankingcrfcd7941irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRTP Header Extension for the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Source Description ItemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9143d8843∂habstractySThis document describes a method of representing rules for validating identifier labels and alternate representations of those labels using Extensible Markup Language (XML).  These policies, known as "Label Generation Rulesets" (LGRs), are used for the implementation of Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs), for example.  The rulesets are used to implement and share that aspect of policy defining which labels and Unicode code points are permitted for registrations, which alternative code points are considered variants, and what actions may be performed on labels containing those variants.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiK. Davies¢kaffiliationkASMUS, Inc.dnamejA. Freytagddatej’”hfilenamegrfc7940eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymelagerdfullxlager - Label Generation RulesdnamevLabel Generation Rulesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129762hkeywordsÑcIDNcLGRiIDN tablemvariant tablekobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDateW´P«grankingcrfcd7940irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Representing Label Generation Rulesets Using XMLdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔThis document replaces RFC 6779; it contains revisions and extensions to the original document.  It defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects for configuring parameters of the Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) process on a router.  The extensions described in this document add objects and values to support the NHDP optimization specified in RFC 7466.  The MIB module defined in this document, denoted NHDP-MIB, also reports state, performance information, and notifications about NHDP.  This additional state and performance information is useful to troubleshoot problems and performance issues during neighbor discovery.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationnUS Army CERDECdnamegR. Cole¢kaffiliationfDelvindnamekI. Chakeres¢kaffiliationsEcole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatej’”hfilenamegrfc7939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129761hkeywordsàrNetwork ManagementxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2gRoutingrNeighbor DiscoveryeMANEThNHDP-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesHopublicationDateW∆ùﬂgrankingcrfcd7939irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEDefinition of Managed Objects for the Neighborhood Discovery ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfSome network operators build and operate data centers that support over one hundred thousand servers.  In this document, such data centers are referred to as "large-scale" to differentiate them from smaller infrastructures.  Environments of this scale have a unique set of network requirements with an emphasis on operational simplicity and network stability.  This document summarizes operational experience in designing and operating large-scale data centers using BGP as the only routing protocol.  The intent is to report on a proven and stable routing design that could be leveraged by others in the industry.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnamekP. Lapukhov¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnameiA. Premji¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Mitchellddatej’’hfilenamegrfc7938eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129760hkeywordsÉcBGPdECMPdCloskobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateW´P∆grankingcrfcd7938irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Use of BGP for Routing in Large-Scale Data CentersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis memo specifies the Logging interface between a downstream Content Distribution Network (dCDN) and an upstream CDN (uCDN) that are interconnected as per the CDN Interconnection (CDNI) framework.  First, it describes a reference model for CDNI logging.  Then, it specifies the CDNI Logging File format and the actual protocol for exchange of CDNI Logging Files.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliation`dnamekG. Bertrand¢kaffiliation`dnamejI. Oprescu¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamenR. Peterkofskyddatej’‘hfilenamegrfc7937eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129759hkeywordsÑdCDNIgLoggingcCDNoInterconnectionkobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDateWƒŸÚgrankingcrfcd7937irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEContent Distribution Network Interconnection (CDNI) Logging InterfacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxpThis document clarifies the instructions to IANA for the subprotocol registry set up for WebSockets in RFC 6455.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Hardieddatejœ@hfilenamegrfc7936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129758hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWé2›granking␀crfcd7936irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJClarifying Registry Procedures for the WebSocket Subprotocol Name RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This document specifies the algorithms, algorithms' parameters, asymmetric key formats, asymmetric key size, and signature format for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) subscribers that generate digital signatures on certificates, Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs), Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) signed objects and certification requests as well as for the relying parties (RPs) that verify these digital signatures.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelsonddatejœ<hfilenamegrfc7935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129757hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateW«Ü˘grankingˇcrfcd7935irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZThe Profile for Algorithms and Key Sizes for Use in the Resource Public Key InfrastructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8608d8208∑habstractxΩThis document recommends that networks provide general-purpose end hosts with multiple global IPv6 addresses when they attach, and it describes the benefits of and the options for doing so.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Colitti¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegV. Cerf¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekD. Schinaziddatej’”hfilenamegrfc7934eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129756hkeywordsãdIPv6dIPv4eSLAACfDHCPv6qPrefix DelegationcNATeNAT64g464XLATc/64rAddress AssignmentjAddressingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWèkÍgranking˛crfcd7934irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÃetotaletitlex)Host Address Availability RecommendationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractybThis document considers the consequences of moving the underlying network architecture from the current Internet to an Information- Centric Networking (ICN) architecture on video distribution.  As most of the traffic in future networks is expected to be video, we consider how to modify the existing video streaming mechanisms.  Several important topics related to video distribution over ICN are presented.  The wide range of scenarios covered includes the following: evolving Dynamic Adaptive Streaming over HTTP (DASH) to work over ICN and leverage the recent ISO/IEC Moving Picture Experts Group (MPEG) standard, layering encoding over ICN, introducing distinct requirements for video using Peer-to-Peer (P2P) mechanisms, adapting the Peer-to-Peer Streaming Protocol (PPSP) for ICN, creating more stringent requirements over ICN because of delay constraints added by Internet Protocol Television (IPTV), and managing digital rights in ICN.  Finally, in addition to considering how existing mechanisms would be impacted by ICN, this document lists some research issues to design ICN-specific video streaming mechanisms.gauthorså¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekC. Westphal¢kaffiliationx!Alpen-Adria University KlagenfurtdnamejS. Lederer¢kaffiliationx!Alpen-Adria University KlagenfurtdnamehD. Posch¢kaffiliationx!Alpen-Adria University KlagenfurtdnamekC. Timmerer¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehA. Azgin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefW. Liu¢kaffiliationhBitMovindnamejC. Mueller¢kaffiliationx<Instituto de Telecomunicacoes Aveirosity of Rome Tor VergatadnamehA. Detti¢kaffiliationx$Instituto de Telecomunicacoes AveirodnameiD. Corujo¢kaffiliationxCity University of Hong KongdnamegJ. Wang¢kaffiliationcMITdnamelM. Montpetit¢kaffiliationxAthlone Institute of TechnologydnameiN. Murrayddatej’”hfilenamegrfc7933eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-129755hkeywordsâcICNcCCNcNDNdDASHxadaptive video streamingxscalable video streamingdIPTVcP2PcDRMkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateW¿éMgranking˝crfcd7933irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexBAdaptive Video Streaming over Information-Centric Networking (ICN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis specification defines a lossless compressed data format that compresses data using a combination of the LZ77 algorithm and Huffman coding, with efficiency comparable to the best currently available general-purpose compression methods.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamemJ. Alakuijala¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekZ. Szabadkaddatejœ;hfilenamegrfc7932eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129754hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesÄopublicationDateWú8Çgranking¸crfcd7932irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBrotli Compressed Data FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9841∂habstractyèThe migration feature of NFSv4 allows the transfer of responsibility for a single file system from one server to another without disruption to clients.  Recent implementation experience has shown problems in the existing specification for this feature in NFSv4.0.  This document identifies the problem areas and provides revised specification text that updates the NFSv4.0 specification in RFC 7530.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcHPEdnameiD. Noveck¢kaffiliationfORACLEdnameiP. Shivam¢kaffiliationfORACLEdnamehC. Lever¢kaffiliationfORACLEdnamehB. Bakerddatejœ;hfilenamegrfc7931eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129753hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateWò¶granking˚crfcd7931irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'NFSv4.0 Migration: Specification UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–The RADIUS-over-TLS experiment described in RFC 6614 has opened RADIUS to new use cases where the 4096-octet maximum size limit of a RADIUS packet proves problematic.  This specification extends the RADIUS-over-TCP experiment (RFC 6613) to permit larger RADIUS packets.  This specification compliments other ongoing work to permit fragmentation of RADIUS authorization information.  This document registers a new RADIUS code, an action that required IESG approval.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartmanddatej’“hfilenamegrfc7930eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129752hkeywordsÅeABFABkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateW¥ﬂ¶granking˙crfcd7930irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Larger Packets for RADIUS over TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©OpenPGP is a message format for email (and file) encryption that lacks a standardized lookup mechanism to securely obtain OpenPGP public keys.  DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) is a method for publishing public keys in DNS.  This document specifies a DANE method for publishing and locating OpenPGP public keys in DNS for a specific email address using a new OPENPGPKEY DNS resource record.  Security is provided via Secure DNS, however the OPENPGPKEY record is not a replacement for verification of authenticity via the "web of trust" or manual verification.  The OPENPGPKEY record can be used to encrypt an email that would otherwise have to be sent unencrypted.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Woutersddatej’Ãhfilenamegrfc7929eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129751hkeywordsÇvopportunistic securityoencrypted emailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW§—granking˘crfcd7929irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFDNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) Bindings for OpenPGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥Unmanaged large buffers in today's networks have given rise to a slew of performance issues.  These performance issues can be addressed by some form of Active Queue Management (AQM) mechanism, optionally in combination with a packet-scheduling scheme such as fair queuing.  This document describes various criteria for performing characterizations of AQM schemes that can be used in lab testing during development, prior to deployment.fadNamepMirja K√ºhlewinddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwCNES / Telecom BretagnednamegN. Kuhn¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Natarajan¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamejN. Khademi¢kaffiliationxSimula Research Laboratory ASdnamefD. Rosddatejœ>hfilenamegrfc7928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129750hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateWú8Ågranking¯crfcd7928irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Characterization Guidelines for Active Queue Management (AQM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyjThis memo describes research challenges for Information-Centric Networking (ICN), an approach to evolve the Internet infrastructure to directly support information distribution by introducing uniquely named data as a core Internet principle. Data becomes independent from location, application, storage, and means of transportation, enabling or enhancing a number of desirable features, such as security, user mobility, multicast, and in-network caching. Mechanisms for realizing these benefits is the subject of ongoing research in the IRTF and elsewhere. This document describes current research challenges in ICN, including naming, security, routing, system scalability, mobility management, wireless networking, transport services, in-network caching, and network management.

This document is a product of the IRTF Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsà¢kaffiliationcNECdnamekD. Kutscher¢kaffiliationpOsaka UniversitydnamefS. Eum¢kaffiliationjTravelpingdnamenK. Pentikousis¢kaffiliationcUCLdnameiI. Psaras¢kaffiliationvUniversidade de AveirodnameiD. Corujo¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnameiD. Saucez¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationiFU BerlindnamelM. Waehlischddatej’‘hfilenamegrfc7927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-129749hkeywordsÅxInformation centric networkingkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateWò•granking˜crfcd7927irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex8Information-Centric Networking (ICN) Research ChallengesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyIn Traffic-Engineered (TE) systems, it is sometimes desirable to establish an end-to-end TE path with a set of constraints (such as bandwidth) across one or more networks from a source to a destination. TE information is the data relating to nodes and TE links that is used in the process of selecting a TE path. TE information is usually only available within a network. We call such a zone of visibility of TE information a domain. An example of a domain may be an IGP area or an Autonomous System.

In order to determine the potential to establish a TE path through a series of connected networks, it is necessary to have available a certain amount of TE information about each network. This need not be the full set of TE information available within each network but does need to express the potential of providing TE connectivity. This subset of TE information is called TE reachability information.

This document sets out the problem statement for the exchange of TE information between interconnected TE networks in support of end-to-end TE path establishment and describes the best current practice architecture to meet this problem statement. For reasons that are explained in this document, this work is limited to simple TE constraints and information that determine TE reachability.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehX. Zhangddatej’“hfilenamegrfc7926eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129748hkeywordsámAbstract linkmAbstract nodekAbstractionqAbstraction layerkAggregationlVirtual nodelVirtual linkkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateWò§grankingˆcrfcd7926irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberŒetotaletitlexnProblem Statement and Architecture for Information Exchange between Interconnected Traffic-Engineered NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùA common design pattern in Internet of Things (IoT) deployments is the use of a constrained device that collects data via sensors or controls actuators for use in home automation, industrial control systems, smart cities, and other IoT deployments.

This document defines a Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) 1.2 profile that offers communications security for this data exchange thereby preventing eavesdropping, tampering, and message forgery. The lack of communication security is a common vulnerability in IoT products that can easily be solved by using these well-researched and widely deployed Internet security protocols.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Fossatiddatej’µhfilenamegrfc7925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddicedfullx'dice - DTLS In Constrained Environmentsdnamex DTLS In Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129747hkeywordsÖxInternet of Things SecuritykTLS ProfilelDTLS ProfilelIoT SecuritymDTLS over SMSkobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateWé2‹grankingıcrfcd7925irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmTransport Layer Security (TLS) / Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Profiles for the Internet of ThingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTransport Layer Security (TLS) handshakes often include fairly static information, such as the server certificate and a list of trusted certification authorities (CAs). This information can be of considerable size, particularly if the server certificate is bundled with a complete certificate chain (i.e., the certificates of intermediate CAs up to the root CA).

This document defines an extension that allows a TLS client to inform a server of cached information, thereby enabling the server to omit already available information.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationl3xA SecuritydnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’«hfilenamegrfc7924eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129746hkeywordsÑvTLS Cached InformationoTLS Cached InfomTLS ExtensionpTLS OptimizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWé2⁄grankingÙcrfcd7924irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Transport Layer Security (TLS) Cached Information ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚This document provides requirements for a service that allows client applications to subscribe to updates of a YANG datastore.  Based on criteria negotiated as part of a subscription, updates will be pushed to targeted recipients.  Such a capability eliminates the need for periodic polling of YANG datastores by applications and fills a functional gap in existing YANG transports (i.e., Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) and RESTCONF).  Such a service can be summarized as a "pub/sub" service for YANG datastore updates.  Beyond a set of basic requirements for the service, various refinements are addressed.  These refinements include: periodicity of object updates, filtering out of objects underneath a requested a subtree, and delivery QoS guarantees.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Voit¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamerA. Gonzalez Prietoddatej’—hfilenamegrfc7923eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-129745hkeywordsÇgpub/sublpush updateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWuZ„grankingÛcrfcd7923irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Requirements for Subscription to YANG DatastoresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis document describes a framework for traceability in the Interface to the Routing System (I2RS) and the information model for that framework.  It specifies the motivation, requirements, and use cases, and defines an information model for recording interactions between elements implementing the I2RS protocol.  This framework provides a consistent tracing interface for components implementing the I2RS architecture to record what was done, by which component, and when.  It aims to improve the management of I2RS implementations, and can be used for troubleshooting, auditing, forensics, and accounting purposes.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiJ. Clarke¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataroddatej’—hfilenamegrfc7922eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-129744hkeywordsÇdI2RSqI2RS TraceabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWuZ‚grankingÚcrfcd7922irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTInterface to the Routing System (I2RS) Traceability: Framework and Information ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis document describes the IETF architecture for a standard, programmatic interface for state transfer in and out of the Internet routing system.  It describes the high-level architecture, the building blocks of this high-level architecture, and their interfaces, with particular focus on those to be standardized as part of the Interface to the Routing System (I2RS).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. NadeauddatejÕ‰hfilenamegrfc7921eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-129743hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateWuZ·grankingÒcrfcd7921irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7An Architecture for the Interface to the Routing SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©Traditionally, routing systems have implemented routing and signaling (e.g., MPLS) to control traffic forwarding in a network. Route computation has been controlled by relatively static policies that define link cost, route cost, or import and export routing policies. Requirements have emerged to more dynamically manage and program routing systems due to the advent of highly dynamic data-center networking, on-demand WAN services, dynamic policy-driven traffic steering and service chaining, the need for real-time security threat responsiveness via traffic control, and a paradigm of separating policy-based decision-making from the router itself. These requirements should allow controlling routing information and traffic paths and extracting network topology information, traffic statistics, and other network analytics from routing systems.

This document proposes meeting this need via an Interface to the Routing System (I2RS).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wardddatejœ7hfilenamegrfc7920eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdi2rsdfullx&i2rs - Interface to the Routing SystemdnamexInterface to the Routing Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-129742hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWuZﬂgrankingcrfcd7920irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Problem Statement for the Interface to the Routing SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/Traditional finite-field-based Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange during the Transport Layer Security (TLS) handshake suffers from a number of security, interoperability, and efficiency shortcomings. These shortcomings arise from lack of clarity about which DH group parameters TLS servers should offer and clients should accept. This document offers a solution to these shortcomings for compatible peers by using a section of the TLS "Supported Groups Registry" (renamed from "EC Named Curve Registry" by this document) to establish common finite field DH parameters with known structure and a mechanism for peers to negotiate support for these groups.

This document updates TLS versions 1.0 (RFC 2246), 1.1 (RFC 4346), and 1.2 (RFC 5246), as well as the TLS Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) extensions (RFC 4492).fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdACLUdnamejD. Gillmorddatej’ûhfilenamegrfc7919eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129741hkeywordsÜnDiffie-HellmanrDiscrete LogarithmlFinite FieldxTransport Layer SecuritycTLSkNegotiationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW´•2grankingÔcrfcd7919irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Negotiated Finite Field Diffie-Hellman Ephemeral Parameters for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This document specifies an optional behavior of Transport Layer Security (TLS) client implementations, dubbed "False Start".  It affects only protocol timing, not on-the-wire protocol data, and can be implemented unilaterally.  A TLS False Start reduces handshake latency to one round trip.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejA. Langley¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekN. Modadugu¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejB. Moellerddatejœ:hfilenamegrfc7918eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129740hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW´•1grankingÓcrfcd7918irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Transport Layer Security (TLS) False StartdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿThis document describes an extension to the IS-IS routing protocol to advertise node administrative tags.  This optional capability allows tagging and grouping of the nodes in an IS-IS domain.  The node administrative tags can be used to express and apply locally defined network policies, thereby providing a very useful operational capability.  Node administrative tags may be used by either IS-IS itself or other applications consuming information propagated via IS-IS.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnameiP. Sarkar¢kaffiliationlRtBrick Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraeneddatej’œhfilenamegrfc7917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129739hkeywordsÑcIGPeIS-ISiAdmin-TagsTraffic EngineeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÑ(ÀgrankingÌcrfcd7917irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Advertising Node Administrative Tags in IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·Loop-Free Alternates (LFAs), as defined in RFC 5286, constitute an IP Fast Reroute (IP FRR) mechanism enabling traffic protection for IP traffic (and, by extension, MPLS LDP traffic). Following early deployment experiences, this document provides operational feedback on LFAs, highlights some limitations, and proposes a set of refinements to address those limitations. It also proposes required management specifications.

This proposal is also applicable to remote-LFA solutions.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamelM. Horneffer¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnameiP. Sarkarddatej’£hfilenamegrfc7916eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129738hkeywordsÜcIGPcLFAfpolicycFRRlfast reroutepnetwork planningkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÑ(ÀgrankingÏcrfcd7916irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Operational Management of Loop-Free AlternatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxæThis document describes the Stateless IP/ICMP Translation Algorithm (SIIT), which translates between IPv4 and IPv6 packet headers (including ICMP headers).  This document obsoletes RFC 6145.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationnRedpill LinprodnamekT. Anderson¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. Gontddatej’Œhfilenamegrfc7915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129737hkeywordsâdSIIThinternethprotocolgcontrolgmessagedIPv4dIPv6x'Stateless IP/ICMP Translation AlgorithmjRFC6145biskobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateWrÊ§grankingÎcrfcd7915irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP/ICMP Translation AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document specifies the password-based key derivation function scrypt.  The function derives one or more secret keys from a secret string.  It is based on memory-hard functions, which offer added protection against attacks using custom hardware.  The document also provides an ASN.1 schema.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgTarsnapdnamekC. Percival¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefssonddatej’—hfilenamegrfc7914eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129736hkeywordsÅePBKDFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW¥ﬂ•grankingÍcrfcd7914irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1The scrypt Password-Based Key Derivation FunctiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document updates RFC 7315, by modifying the extension-access- info part of the P-Access-Network-Info header field Augmented Backus- Naur Form (ABNF), and by adding the following 'access-info' header field parameter values to the list of 'access-info' header field parameter values in the ABNF: 'operator-specific-GI' and 'utran-sai-3gpp'.  The values are defined in the ABNF but are not included in the list.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej’Õhfilenamegrfc7913eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129735hkeywordsÜiTransportdPANIdABNFuP-Access-Network-Infod3GPPcIMSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWkqgrankingÈcrfcd7913irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!P-Access-Network-Info ABNF UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÀThis document describes a procedure for when a Military Message Handling System (MMHS) message is composed by one user and is only released to the mail transfer system when one or more Authorizing Users authorize release of the message by adding the MMHS-Authorizing-Users header field.  The resulting message can be optionally signed by the sender and/or reviewer, allowing recipients to verify both the original signature (if any) and the review signatures.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’Ãhfilenamegrfc7912eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129734hkeywordsÑdMMHSfS/MIMEeMIXEReemailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWi‘ÂgrankingËcrfcd7912irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexVMessage Authorizing Email Header Field and Its Use for the Draft and Release ProceduredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document defines a BGP extension that allows the advertisement of multiple paths for the same address prefix without the new paths implicitly replacing any previous ones.  The essence of the extension is that each path is identified by a Path Identifier in addition to the address prefix.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnameiD. Walton¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej’—hfilenamegrfc7911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129733hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÑ(…grankingÁcrfcd7911irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Advertisement of Multiple Paths in BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyòThis document introduces VRRP-aware PIM, a redundancy mechanism for the Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) to interoperate with the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP).  It allows PIM to track VRRP state and to preserve multicast traffic upon failover in a redundant network with virtual routing groups enabled.  The mechanism described in this document is based on Cisco IOS software implementation.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegW. Zhouddatejœ-hfilenamegrfc7910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129732hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWc>’grankingÊcrfcd7910irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGInteroperability between the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol and PIMdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃThis document describes a method that allows parties to electronically sign Routing Policy Specification Language objects and validate such electronic signatures.  This allows relying parties to detect accidental or malicious modifications of such objects.  It also allows parties who run Internet Routing Registries or similar databases, but do not yet have authentication (based on Routing Policy System Security) of the maintainers of certain objects, to verify that the additions or modifications of such database objects are done by the legitimate holder(s) of the Internet resources mentioned in those objects.  This document updates RFCs 2622 and 4012 to add the signature attribute to supported RPSL objects.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamelR. Kisteleki¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. Habermanddatejœ9hfilenamegrfc7909eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129731hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWu¢grankingÂcrfcd7909irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwSecuring Routing Policy Specification Language (RPSL) Objects with Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA systemic vulnerability of the Border Gateway Protocol routing system, known as "route leaks", has received significant attention in recent years.  Frequent incidents that result in significant disruptions to Internet routing are labeled route leaks, but to date a common definition of the term has been lacking.  This document provides a working definition of route leaks while keeping in mind the real occurrences that have received significant attention.  Further, this document attempts to enumerate (though not exhaustively) different types of route leaks based on observed events on the Internet.  The aim is to provide a taxonomy that covers several forms of route leaks that have been observed and are of concern to the Internet user community as well as the network operator community.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgUS NISTdnameiK. Sriram¢kaffiliationgUS NISTdnamemD. Montgomery¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelE. Osterweil¢kaffiliation`dnamejB. Dicksonddatej’Œhfilenamegrfc7908eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129730hkeywordsÜcBGPfBGPSECjRoute LeaktRoute Leak DetectionuRoute Leak MitigationlBGP SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWi‘Âgranking‰crfcd7908irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Problem Definition and Classification of BGP Route LeaksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyqThis document defines key management attributes used by the National Security Agency (NSA).  The attributes can appear in asymmetric and/or symmetric key packages as well as the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) content types that subsequently envelope the key packages.  Key packages described in RFCs 5958 and 6031 are examples of where these attributes can be used.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnameiP. Timmel¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatejœ/hfilenamegrfc7906eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129729hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDateWrÊ£granking‚crfcd7906irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexBNSA's Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Key Management AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬂThis document describes the use of the ChaCha stream cipher and Poly1305 authenticator in the Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocols.

This document updates RFCs 5246 and 6347.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejA. Langley¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehW. Chang¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnametN. Mavrogiannopoulos¢kaffiliationrSecworks Sweden ABdnameoJ. Strombergson¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefssonddatej’Õhfilenamegrfc7905eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129728hkeywordsÇdAEADdDTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWkpgranking·crfcd7905irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBChaCha20-Poly1305 Cipher Suites for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document defines a SIP Usage for REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD).  The SIP Usage provides the functionality of a SIP proxy or registrar in a fully distributed system and includes a lookup service for Address of Records (AORs) stored in the overlay.  It also defines Globally Routable User Agent URIs (GRUUs) that allow the registrations to map an AOR to a specific node reachable through the overlay.  After such initial contact of a Peer, the RELOAD AppAttach method is used to establish a direct connection between nodes through which SIP messages are exchanged.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationeSkypednamekB. Lowekamp¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehS. Baset¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidtddatej’Ãhfilenamegrfc7904eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129727hkeywordsâfp2psipcp2pcsipfreloadlpeer-to-peerrsession initiationxdistributed session managementooverlay networkmSIP registrarkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateXü§granking‡crfcd7904irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8A SIP Usage for REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty∞This document registers media types for certain image formats promulgated in Microsoft Windows, namely image/wmf, image/x-wmf, image/emf, image/x-emf, and image/bmp for use with Windows Metafile, Enhanced Metafile, and Windows Bitmap formats.  Originally designed for Microsoft Windows 2.0 and 3.0, these image files are intended to be portable between applications and devices, and they may contain both vector and raster graphics.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmPenango, Inc.dnamejS. Leonardddatejœ2hfilenamegrfc7903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129726hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÓ€lgrankingﬂcrfcd7903irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexWindows Image Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThe BGP-based control procedures for Multicast Virtual Private Networks (MVPNs) make use of a BGP attribute known as the "P-Multicast Service Interface (PMSI) Tunnel" attribute.  The attribute contains a one-octet "Flags" field.  The purpose of this document is to establish an IANA registry for the assignment of the bits in this field.  Since the "Flags" field contains only eight bits, this document also defines a new BGP Extended Community, "Additional PMSI Tunnel Attribute Flags", that can be used to carry additional flags for the "P-Multicast Service Interface (PMSI) Tunnel" attribute.  This document updates RFC 6514.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehT. Morinddatejœ3hfilenamegrfc7902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129725hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWX….grankingﬁcrfcd7902irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRegistry and Extensions for P-Multicast Service Interface Tunnel Attribute FlagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document defines an EDNS0 extension that can be used by a security-aware validating resolver configured to use a forwarding resolver to send a single query, requesting a complete validation path along with the regular query answer.  The reduction in queries potentially lowers the latency and reduces the need to send multiple queries at once.  This extension mandates the use of source-IP- verified transport such as TCP or UDP with EDNS-COOKIE, so it cannot be abused in amplification attacks.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Woutersddatej’∆hfilenamegrfc7901eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129724hkeywordsÉfDNSSECeEDNS0glatencykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWi‘‰granking›crfcd7901irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCHAIN Query Requests in DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒPrevious RFCs specify the procedures necessary to allow IP multicast traffic to travel from one site to another within a BGP/MPLS IP VPN (Virtual Private Network).  However, it is sometimes desirable to allow multicast traffic whose source is in one VPN to be received by systems that are in another VPN.  This is known as a "Multicast VPN (MVPN) extranet".  This document updates RFCs 6513, 6514, and 6625 by specifying the procedures that are necessary in order to provide extranet MVPN service.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationfArktandnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationmAlibaba GroupdnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehT. Morinddatej’ hfilenamegrfc7900eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129723hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateWi‘„granking‹crfcd7900irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Extranet Multicast in BGP/IP MPLS VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8534∂habstracty’This document describes procedures to damp Multicast VPN (MVPN) routing state changes and control the effect of the churn due to the multicast dynamicity in customer sites.  The procedures described in this document are applicable to BGP-based multicast VPN and help avoid uncontrolled control-plane load increase in the core routing infrastructure.  The new procedures proposed were inspired by BGP unicast route damping principles that have been adapted to multicast.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehT. Morin¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Kebler¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegJ. Haasddatej’Õhfilenamegrfc7899eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129722hkeywordsÜidampeningimulticastcvpngdampingcbgpcpimkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWrÊ£granking€crfcd7899irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMulticast VPN State DampingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*The Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) specification and the Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) extensions to RSVP-TE allow abstract nodes and resources to be explicitly included in a path setup. Further, Exclude Route extensions to RSVP-TE allow abstract nodes and resources to be explicitly excluded in a path setup.

This document specifies new subobjects to include or exclude Autonomous Systems (ASes), which are identified by a 4-byte AS number, and Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) areas during path setup.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehU. Palle¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelV. Kondreddy¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnamekR. Casellasddatej’Ωhfilenamegrfc7898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129721hkeywordsÉgRSVP-TEfdomainjsubobjectskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWa’Dgranking⁄crfcd7898irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSDomain Subobjects for Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThe ability to compute shortest constrained Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks across multiple domains has been identified as a key requirement.  In this context, a domain is a collection of network elements within a common sphere of address management or path computational responsibility such as an Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) area or an Autonomous System (AS).  This document specifies a representation and encoding of a domain sequence, which is defined as an ordered sequence of domains traversed to reach the destination domain to be used by Path Computation Elements (PCEs) to compute inter-domain constrained shortest paths across a predetermined sequence of domains.  This document also defines new subobjects to be used to encode domain identifiers.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehU. Palle¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnamekR. Casellasddatej’Ωhfilenamegrfc7897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129720hkeywordsÑdPCEPcPCEfdomainjsubobjectskobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateWa’CgrankingŸcrfcd7897irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPDomain Subobjects for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThe Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) enables communications between a Path Computation Client (PCC) and a PCE, or between two PCEs. RFC 5440 defines the Include Route Object (IRO) to specify network elements to be traversed in the computed path. The specification does not specify if the IRO contains an ordered or unordered list of subobjects. During recent discussions, it was determined that there was a need to define a standard representation to ensure interoperability. It was also noted that there is a benefit in the handling of an attribute of the IRO's subobject, the L bit.

This document updates RFC 5440 regarding the IRO specification.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Dhodyddatej’fhfilenamegrfc7896eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129719hkeywordsÉdPCEPcPCEcIROkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWa’Cgrankingÿcrfcd7896irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextUpdate to the Include Route Object (IRO) Specification in the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a YANG library that provides information about all the YANG modules used by a network management server (e.g., a Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) server).  Simple caching mechanisms are provided to allow clients to minimize retrieval of this information.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiK. Watsenddatej’Œhfilenamegrfc7895eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129718hkeywordsÇgNETCONFhRESTCONFkobsoletedByÅd8525epagesopublicationDateWuZﬁgranking◊crfcd7895irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesYANG Module LibrarydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis document defines a set of new Certificate Signing Request attributes for use with the Enrollment over Secure Transport (EST) protocol.  These attributes provide disambiguation of the existing overloaded uses for the challengePassword attribute defined in "PKCS #9: Selected Object Classes and Attribute Types Version 2.0" (RFC 2985).  Uses include the original certificate revocation password, common authentication password uses, and EST-defined linking of transport security identity.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekM. Pritikin¢kaffiliationxRed Hound Software, Inc.dnamejC. Wallaceddatej’«hfilenamegrfc7894eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129717hkeywordsÅx Enrollment over Secure TransportkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateWX…-granking÷crfcd7894irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNAlternative Challenge Password Attributes for Enrollment over Secure TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëPseudowires (PWs) have become a common mechanism for tunneling traffic and may be found in unmanaged scenarios competing for network resources both with other PWs and with non-PW traffic, such as TCP/IP flows.  Thus, it is worthwhile specifying under what conditions such competition is acceptable, i.e., the PW traffic does not significantly harm other traffic or contribute more than it should to congestion.  We conclude that PWs transporting responsive traffic behave as desired without the need for additional mechanisms.  For inelastic PWs (such as Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) PWs), we derive a bound under which such PWs consume no more network capacity than a TCP flow.  For TDM PWs, we find that the level of congestion at which the PW can no longer deliver acceptable TDM service is never significantly greater, and is typically much lower, than this bound.  Therefore, as long as the PW is shut down when it can no longer deliver acceptable TDM service, it will never do significantly more harm than even a single TCP flow.  If the TDM service does not automatically shut down, a mechanism to block persistently unacceptable TDM pseudowires is required.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnamejY(J) Stein¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoeddatej’Àhfilenamegrfc7893eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129716hkeywordsÉjpseudowirejcongestionpTCP friendlinesskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWt_£granking’crfcd7893irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Pseudowire Congestion ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀IANA defined the "OTN Signal Type" subregistry of the "Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Parameters" registry in RFC 7139.  This document updates the "OTN Signal Type" subregistry to allow registration via Specification Required.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekA. Bonfanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Hartley¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Zhangddatejœ/hfilenamegrfc7892eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129715hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWHÙÙgranking‘crfcd7892irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAIANA Allocation Procedures for the GMPLS OTN Signal Type RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«The PIM Reverse Path Forwarding (RPF) Vector TLV defined in RFC 5496 can be included in a PIM Join Attribute such that the RPF neighbor is selected based on the unicast reachability of the RPF Vector instead of the source or Rendezvous Point associated with the multicast tree.

This document defines a new RPF Vector Attribute type such that an explicit RPF neighbor list can be encoded in the PIM Join Attribute, thus bypassing the unicast route lookup.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiJ. Asghar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoS. Krishnaswamy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Karan¢kaffiliationlDIRECTV Inc.dnamegV. Aryaddatej’Àhfilenamegrfc7891eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-129714hkeywordsÉnPath diversityeMoFRRxMaximally redundant pathskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateWa’Cgranking”crfcd7891irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Explicit Reverse Path Forwarding (RPF) VectordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThis document defines concepts and terminology for using the Session Initiation Protocol in a peer-to-peer environment where the traditional proxy-registrar and message-routing functions are replaced by a distributed mechanism.  These mechanisms may be implemented using a Distributed Hash Table or other distributed data mechanism with similar external properties.  This document includes a high-level view of the functional relationships between the network elements defined herein, a conceptual model of operations, and an outline of the related problems addressed by the P2PSIP working group, the REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) protocol, and the SIP usage document defined by the working group.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqCogent Force, LLCdnamehD. Bryan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekP. Matthews¢kaffiliationxSamsung Electronics Co., Ltd.dnamegE. Shim¢kaffiliationqSoftarmor SystemsdnameiD. Willis¢kaffiliationlHuawei (USA)dnamejS. Dawkinsddatej’ hfilenamegrfc7890eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129713hkeywordsÖtDistributed DatabasefP2PSIPcSIPkServer-lesscDHTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWk¯granking“crfcd7890irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Concepts and Terminology for Peer-to-Peer SIP (P2PSIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÈThis document defines an extension to the IMAP service whereby a server can inform the client about maximum message upload sizes, allowing the client to avoid sending APPEND commands that will fail because the messages are too large.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx"Samsung Telecommunications AmericadnamexJ. SrimushnamBoovaraghamoorthy¢kaffiliationxSamsung Electronics AmericadnamehN. Bishtddatejœ hfilenamegrfc7889eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhimapapnddfullx!imapapnd - IMAP APPEND ExtensionsdnamevIMAP APPEND Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129712hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW;öâgranking—crfcd7889irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe IMAP APPENDLIMIT ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¥The Internet Message Access Protocol (RFC 3501) contains the "literal" syntactic construct for communicating strings. When sending a literal from client to server, IMAP requires the client to wait for the server to send a command continuation request between sending the octet count and the string data. This document specifies an alternate form of literal that does not require this network round trip.

This document specifies 2 IMAP extensions: LITERAL+ and LITERAL-. LITERAL+ allows the alternate form of literals in all IMAP commands. LITERAL- is the same as LITERAL+, but it disallows the alternate form of literals unless they are 4096 bytes or less.

