D) Discriminators using the IS-IS Router CAPABILITY TLV.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatejœ+hfilenamegrfc7883eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129706hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÖêDgrankingÀcrfcd7883irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWAdvertising Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD) Discriminators in IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes various use cases for Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD) and provides requirements such that protocol mechanisms allow for simplified detection of forwarding failures.

These use cases support S-BFD, which is a simplified mechanism for using BFD with a large proportion of negotiation aspects eliminated, accelerating the establishment of a BFD session. The benefits of S-BFD include quick provisioning, as well as improved control and flexibility for network nodes initiating path monitoring.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehN. Kumarddatej’Õhfilenamegrfc7882eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129705hkeywordsÜcBFDlseamless BFDpnegotiation freerlabel verificationosegment routingbIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÖêCgranking crfcd7882irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD) Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÑThis document defines procedures for using Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD) in IPv4, IPv6, and MPLS environments.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiyaddatej’hfilenamegrfc7881eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129704hkeywordsÜcBFDlseamless BFDpnegotiation freerlabel verificationosegment routingbIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÖêBgranking…crfcd7881irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLSeamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD) for IPv4, IPv6, and MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document defines Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD), a simplified mechanism for using BFD with a large proportion of negotiation aspects eliminated, thus providing benefits such as quick provisioning, as well as improved control and flexibility for network nodes initiating path monitoring.

This document updates RFC 5880.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationnIonos NetworksdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliation`dnamemS. Pallagattiddatej’œhfilenamegrfc7880eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129703hkeywordsÖcBFDlseamless BFDpnegotiation freeosegment routingbIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÖê@granking»crfcd7880irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Seamless Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (S-BFD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs) exist on the signaling and media paths between the endpoints.  This document describes the behavior of B2BUAs when Secure Real-time Transport (SRTP) security context is set up with the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoR. Ravindranath¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejV. Pascual¢kaffiliationnNokia NetworksdnamelP. Ravindranddatej’ hfilenamegrfc7879eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestrawdfullx7straw - Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdnamex/Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdtypebwgbidjdoc-129702hkeywordsÑxSession Initiation ProtocoleB2BUAxSecure Real-time Transportx!Datagram Transport Layer SecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWN/ògranking«crfcd7879irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2DTLS-SRTP Handling in SIP Back-to-Back User AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™The Session Peering Provisioning Framework (SPPF) specifies the data model and the overall structure to provision Session Establishment Data (SED) into Session Data Registries and SIP Service Provider data stores.  To utilize this framework, one needs a substrate protocol.  Given that the Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP) is currently widely used for messaging between elements of such provisioning systems, this document specifies the usage of SOAP (via HTTPS) as the substrate protocol for SPPF.  The benefits include leveraging prevalent expertise and a higher probability that existing provisioning systems will be able to easily migrate to using an \%SPPF- based protocol.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcTNSdnamemK. Cartwright¢kaffiliationcTNSdnameiV. Bhatia¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnameiJ-F. Mule¢kaffiliationknic.at GmbHdnamelA. Mayrhoferddatej’…hfilenamegrfc7878eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdrinksdfullx7drinks - Data for Reachability of Inter/tra-NetworK SIPdnamex.Data for Reachability of Inter/tra-NetworK SIPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129701hkeywordsÖdSPPPcSIPgPeeringcSEDlProvisioningkobsoletedByÄepagesSopublicationDateW¥ﬂ•granking∆crfcd7878irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Session Peering Provisioning (SPP) Protocol over SOAPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis document specifies the data model and the overall structure for a framework to provision Session Establishment Data (SED) into Session Data Registries and SIP Service Provider (SSP) data stores.  The framework is called the "Session Peering Provisioning Framework" (SPPF).  The provisioned data is typically used by network elements for session establishment.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time 