opublicationDateWò†grankingƒcrfcd7876irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGUDP Return Path for Packet Loss and Delay Measurement for MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈTo ensure a baseline of interoperability between WebRTC endpoints, a minimum set of required codecs is specified. However, to maximize the possibility of establishing the session without the need for audio transcoding, it is also recommended to include in the offer other suitable audio codecs that are available to the browser.

This document provides some guidelines on the suitable codecs to be considered for WebRTC endpoints to address the use cases most relevant to interoperability.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOrangednameiS. Proustddatej’Àhfilenamegrfc7875eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-129698hkeywordsÜfWebRTCeaudioecodeceG.722cAMRfAMR-WBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWF:ígranking√crfcd7875irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Additional WebRTC Audio Codecs for InteroperabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxXThis document outlines the audio codec and processing requirements for WebRTC endpoints.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnameiJM. Valin¢kaffiliationkPlantronicsdnamegC. Branddatejœ1hfilenamegrfc7874eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-129697hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWF:ëgranking¬crfcd7874irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.WebRTC Audio Codec and Processing RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+DNS Cookies are a lightweight DNS transaction security mechanism that provides limited protection to DNS servers and clients against a variety of increasingly common denial-of-service and amplification/ forgery or cache poisoning attacks by off-path attackers.  DNS Cookies are tolerant of NAT, NAT-PT (Network Address Translation - Protocol Translation), and anycast and can be incrementally deployed. (Since DNS Cookies are only returned to the IP address from which they were originally received, they cannot be used to generally track Internet users.)fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejM. Andrewsddatej’…hfilenamegrfc7873eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129696hkeywordsÑqdenial of servicegforgeryocache poisoninghoff-pathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWHÙÔgranking¡crfcd7873irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Domain Name System (DNS) CookiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9018∂habstracty¿This document presents real-world data regarding the extent to which packets with IPv6 Extension Headers (EHs) are dropped in the Internet (as originally measured in August 2014 and later in June 2015, with similar results) and where in the network such dropping occurs.  The aforementioned results serve as a problem statement that is expected to trigger operational advice on the filtering of IPv6 packets carrying IPv6 EHs so that the situation improves over time.  This document also explains how the results were obtained, such that the corresponding measurements can be reproduced by other members of the community and repeated over time to observe changes in the handling of packets with IPv6 EHs.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Linkova¢kaffiliationdJiscdnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liuddatej’…hfilenamegrfc7872eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129695hkeywordsÅlpacket dropskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWi‘€granking¿crfcd7872irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUObservations on the Dropping of Packets with IPv6 Extension Headers in the Real WorlddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This document describes an Extension Mechanisms for DNS (EDNS0) option that is in active use to carry information about the network that originated a DNS query and the network for which the subsequent response can be cached.  Since it has some known operational and privacy shortcomings, a revision will be worked through the IETF for improvement.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemC. Contavalli¢kaffiliationfGooglednamepW. van der Gaast¢kaffiliationsAkamai TechnologiesdnamekD. Lawrence¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumariddatej’»hfilenamegrfc7871eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129694hkeywordsàredns-client-subnetcECSoDNS geolocationrDNS load-balancingdEDNSeEDNS0kgeolocationgprivacykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW?s◊grankingøcrfcd7871irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexClient Subnet in DNS QueriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines managed objects for Address Family Transition Routers (AFTRs) of Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite).fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnameeY. Fu¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamegY. Chenddatej’øhfilenamegrfc7870eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129693hkeywordsÅdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWa’Bgrankingæcrfcd7870irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiDual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite) Management Information Base (MIB) for Address Family Transition Routers (AFTRs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty)Virtual Network Computing (VNC) software provides remote desktop functionality.  This document describes a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme enabling the launch of VNC clients from other applications.  The scheme specifies parameters useful in securely connecting clients with remote hosts.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpDell Products LPdnameiD. Warden¢kaffiliationhUndatechdnamekI. Iordanovddatej‘Úhfilenamegrfc7869eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129692hkeywordsÉcRFBrRemote FramebufferxVirtual Network ComputingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWF:êgrankingΩcrfcd7869irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitletThe "vnc" URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyΩThis document describes the protocol design and architecture for Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol (EIGRP).  EIGRP is a routing protocol based on Distance Vector technology.  The specific algorithm used is called "DUAL", a Diffusing Update Algorithm as referenced in "Loop-Free Routing Using Diffusing Computations" (Garcia-Luna-Aceves 1993).  The algorithm and procedures were researched, developed, and simulated by SRI International.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. Savage¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameeJ. Ng¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehS. Moore¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnamehD. Slice¢kaffiliationtUniversity of ZilinadnameiP. Paluch¢kaffiliationhLinkedIndnamehR. Whiteddatejœ(hfilenamegrfc7868eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129691hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesPopublicationDateWF:êgrankingºcrfcd7868irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex:Cisco's Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol (EIGRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxßThis document defines a new RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) block that allows the reporting of loss concealment metrics for video applications of RTP.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huangddatejœ0hfilenamegrfc7867eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-129690hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWú8Ägrankingªcrfcd7867irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block for Loss Concealment Metrics for Video ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document specifies the use of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), the Session Description Protocol (SDP), and the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) for delivering real-time media and metadata from a Communication Session (CS) to a recording device.  The Session Recording Protocol specifies the use of SIP, SDP, and RTP to establish a Recording Session (RS) between the Session Recording Client (SRC), which is on the path of the CS, and a Session Recording Server (SRS) at the recording device.  This document considers only active recording, where the SRC purposefully streams media to an SRS and all participating user agents (UAs) are notified of the recording.  Passive recording, where a recording device detects media directly from the network (e.g., using port-mirroring techniques), is outside the scope of this document.  In addition, lawful intercept is outside the scope of this document.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlNICE SystemsdnamejL. Portman¢kaffiliationgGenesysdnamefH. Lum¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehC. Eckel¢kaffiliationx Illinois Institute of TechnologydnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationeUnifydnameiA. Huttonddatej’±hfilenamegrfc7866eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsiprecdfullvsiprec - SIP RecordingdnamemSIP Recordingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129689hkeywordsÅfsipreckobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateWN/ógranking∫crfcd7866irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSession Recording ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9806∂habstractyÔSession recording is a critical requirement in many communications environments, such as call centers and financial trading organizations.  In some of these environments, all calls must be recorded for regulatory, compliance, and consumer protection reasons.  The recording of a session is typically performed by sending a copy of a media stream to a recording device.  This document describes the metadata model as viewed by the Session Recording Server (SRS) and the recording metadata format.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoR. Ravindranath¢kaffiliationnNokia NetworksdnamelP. Ravindran¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejP. Kyzivatddatejœ'hfilenamegrfc7865eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsiprecdfullvsiprec - SIP RecordingdnamemSIP Recordingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129688hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateWN/ïgrankingπcrfcd7865irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Recording MetadatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysProxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) allows a mobile node to connect to the same PMIPv6 domain through different interfaces. This document describes extensions to the PMIPv6 protocol that are required to support network-based flow mobility over multiple physical interfaces.

This document updates RFC 5213. The extensions described in this document consist of the operations performed by the local mobility anchor and the mobile access gateway to manage the prefixes assigned to the different interfaces of the mobile node, as well as how the forwarding policies are handled by the network to ensure consistent flow mobility management.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej’«hfilenamegrfc7864eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129687hkeywordsÖmflow mobilitygNB-IFOMfPMIPv6cFMIcFMAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW1%©granking∏crfcd7864irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Proxy Mobile IPv6 Extensions to Support Flow MobilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxlThis document provides the External Data Representation (XDR) description for NFS version 4 minor version 2.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlPrimary DatadnameiT. Haynesddatej’¬hfilenamegrfc7863eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129686hkeywordsÇgNFSv4.2cXDRkobsoletedByÄepagesWopublicationDateX$È¬granking∑crfcd7863irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkNetwork File System (NFS) Version 4 Minor Version 2 External Data Representation Standard (XDR) DescriptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This document describes NFS version 4 minor version 2; it describes the protocol extensions made from NFS version 4 minor version 1.  Major extensions introduced in NFS version 4 minor version 2 include the following: