These specifications define the pseudorandom functions, the key agreement algorithm based on the Diffie-Hellman algorithm and a hash function, the parameters of elliptic curves, the key derivation functions, and the key export functions.gauthorsá¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamemS. Smyshlyaev¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamekE. Alekseev¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnameiI. Oshkin¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamehV. Popov¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamekS. Leontiev¢kaffiliationiFACTOR-TSdnamekV. Podobaev¢kaffiliationcTCIdnamelD. Belyavskyddatej’¡hfilenamegrfc7836eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129659hkeywordsèdHMACcPRFmkey agreementcVKOlkey exchangenkey derivationcKDFhkey treenelliptic curvekWeierstrassotwisted EdwardscTLSeIPseccIKEeIKEv2kobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateV˝¿˘grankingúcrfcd7836irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexvGuidelines on the Cryptographic Algorithms to Accompany the Usage of Standards GOST R 34.10-2012 and GOST R 34.11-2012dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxVThis document provides a threat analysis of the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnameiD. Saucez¢kaffiliationqTelecom ParisTechdnamejL. Iannone¢kaffiliationx Universite catholique de LouvaindnamenO. Bonaventureddatejœ"hfilenamegrfc7835eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129658hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW$grankingõcrfcd7835irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Threat AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThe Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) aims to improve the Internet routing scalability properties by leveraging three principles: address role separation, encapsulation, and mapping.  In this document, based on implementation work, deployment experiences, and theoretical studies, we discuss the impact that the deployment of LISP can have on both the routing infrastructure and the end user.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnameiD. Saucez¢kaffiliationqTelecom ParisTechdnamejL. Iannone¢kaffiliationx!Technical University of CataloniadnamekA. Cabellos¢kaffiliationx!Technical University of CataloniadnamehF. CorasddatejÃQhfilenamegrfc7834eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129657hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW$␀grankingöcrfcd7834irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) ImpactdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúThis document describes the use of the Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) with RADIUS in the context of the Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond web (ABFAB) architecture.  It defines two RADIUS attributes, a SAML binding, a SAML name identifier format, two SAML profiles, and two SAML confirmation methods.  The RADIUS attributes permit encapsulation of SAML Assertions and protocol messages within RADIUS, allowing SAML entities to communicate using the binding.  The two profiles describe the application of this binding for ABFAB authentication and assertion Query/Request, enabling a Relying Party to request authentication of, or assertions for, users or machines (clients).  These clients may be named using a Network Access Identifier (NAI) name identifier format.  Finally, the subject confirmation methods allow requests and queries to be issued for a previously authenticated user or machine without needing to explicitly identify them as the subject.  The use of the artifacts defined in this document is not exclusive to ABFAB.  They can be applied in any Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) scenario, such as network access control.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdJiscdnamejJ. Howlett¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnameoA. Perez-Mendezddatej’øhfilenamegrfc7833eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeabfabdfullx<abfab - Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdnamex4Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdtypebwgbidjdoc-129656hkeywordsÖeABFABcAAAcEAPfRADIUSdSAMLkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateW3»ﬁgrankingôcrfcd7833irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâA RADIUS Attribute, Binding, Profiles, Name Identifier Format, and Confirmation Methods for the Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•Federated identity is typically associated with web-based services at present, but there is growing interest in its application in non-web-based contexts.  The goal of this memo is to document a selection of the wide variety of these contexts whose user experience could be improved through the use of technologies based on the Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond web (ABFAB) architecture and specifications.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdJiscdnamehR. Smithddatej’hfilenamegrfc7832eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeabfabdfullx<abfab - Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdnamex4Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdtypebwgbidjdoc-129655hkeywordsáxFederated AuthenticationcAAAfRADIUShDiametergGSS-APIcEAPdSASLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW3»ﬁgrankingòcrfcd7832irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFApplication Bridging for Federated Access Beyond Web (ABFAB) Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúOver the last decade, a substantial amount of work has occurred in the space of federated access management. Most of this effort has focused on two use cases: network access and web-based access. However, the solutions to these use cases that have been proposed and deployed tend to have few building blocks in common.

This memo describes an architecture that makes use of extensions to the commonly used security mechanisms for both federated and non-federated access management, including the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS), the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API), the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), and the Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML). The architecture addresses the problem of federated access management to primarily non-web-based services, in a manner that will scale to large numbers of Identity Providers, Relying Parties, and federations.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdJiscdnamejJ. Howlett¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationnAugust CellarsdnameiJ. Schaadddatej’ehfilenamegrfc7831eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeabfabdfullx<abfab - Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdnamex4Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdtypebwgbidjdoc-129654hkeywordsáxFederated AuthenticationcAAAfRADIUShDiametergGSS-APIcEAPdSAMLkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateW3»‹grankingócrfcd7831irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIApplication Bridging for Federated Access Beyond Web (ABFAB) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxüThis document specifies the EDNS(0) "Padding" option, which allows DNS clients and servers to pad request and response messages by a variable number of octets.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationknic.at GmbHdnamelA. Mayrhoferddatej‘Úhfilenamegrfc7830eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-129653hkeywordsárDomain Name SystemcDNSdEDNSeEDNS0hSecurityjEncryptiongPaddingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW2f}grankingñcrfcd7830irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe EDNS(0) Padding OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) supports multihoming. However, when the failover operation specified in RFC 4960 is followed, there can be significant delay and performance degradation in the data transfer path failover. This document specifies a quick failover algorithm and introduces the SCTP Potentially Failed (SCTP-PF) destination state in SCTP Path Management.

This document also specifies a dormant state operation of SCTP that is required to be followed by an SCTP-PF implementation, but it may equally well be applied by a standard SCTP implementation, as described in RFC 4960.

Additionally, this document introduces an alternative switchback operation mode called "Primary Path Switchover" that will be beneficial in certain situations. This mode of operation applies to both a standard SCTP implementation and an SCTP-PF implementation.

The procedures defined in the document require only minimal modifications to the specification in RFC 4960. The procedures are sender-side only and do not impact the SCTP receiver.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrGE Global ResearchdnamejY. Nishida¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Natarajan¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegA. Caro¢kaffiliationvUniversity of DelawarednamegP. Amer¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejK. Nielsenddatej’ƒhfilenamegrfc7829eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129652hkeywordsÖdSCTPhFailoverimultipathkmultihomingrPotentially FailedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWøégrankingïcrfcd7829irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPSCTP-PF: A Quick Failover Algorithm for the Stream Control Transmission ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸDNS messages between clients and servers may be received over either UDP or TCP. UDP transport involves keeping less state on a busy server, but can cause truncation and retries over TCP. Additionally, UDP can be exploited for reflection attacks. Using TCP would reduce retransmits and amplification. However, clients commonly use TCP only for retries and servers typically use idle timeouts on the order of seconds.

This document defines an EDNS0 option ("edns-tcp-keepalive") that allows DNS servers to signal a variable idle timeout. This signalling encourages the use of long-lived TCP connections by allowing the state associated with TCP transport to be managed effectively with minimal impact on the DNS transaction time.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiR. Bellisddatej’≈hfilenamegrfc7828eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129651hkeywordsÖjlong-livedfdnsseccDNSfTCP/IPitransportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW∆grankingîcrfcd7828irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#The edns-tcp-keepalive EDNS0 OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx¿This document briefly describes the role of the Chair of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF), discusses its duties, and outlines the skill set a candidate for the role should ideally have.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggertddatejœ(hfilenamegrfc7827eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129650hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¸ˆpgrankingìcrfcd7827irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexThe Role of the IRTF ChairdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis memorandum defines the Real-Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP) version 2.0, which obsoletes RTSP version 1.0 defined in RFC 2326.

RTSP is an application-layer protocol for the setup and control of the delivery of data with real-time properties. RTSP provides an extensible framework to enable controlled, on-demand delivery of real-time data, such as audio and video. Sources of data can include both live data feeds and stored clips. This protocol is intended to control multiple data delivery sessions; provide a means for choosing delivery channels such as UDP, multicast UDP, and TCP; and provide a means for choosing delivery mechanisms based upon RTP (RFC 3550).fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefA. Rao¢kaffiliation`dnamekR. Lanphier¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationx(University of Applied Sciences DarmstadtdnamenM. Stiemerlingddatej’Whfilenamegrfc7826eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129649hkeywordsÑfmmusicdRTSPhRTSP/2.0xreal-time streaming protocolkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDateXb¡Çgrankingícrfcd7826irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Real-Time Streaming Protocol Version 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This document defines a solution for Network Address Translation (NAT) traversal for datagram-based media streams set up and controlled with the Real-Time Streaming Protocol version 2 (RTSP 2.0).  It uses Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) adapted to use RTSP as a signaling channel, defining the necessary RTSP extensions and procedures.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekJ. Goldberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationwNextwave Wireless, Inc.dnamegT. Zengddatej’jhfilenamegrfc7825eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129648hkeywordsåcICEnMedia DeliverycRTPdRTCPeD-ICEcAVPdAVPFdSAVPeSAVPFmsetup.ice-d-mlrtsp-ice-d-mcSDPkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateXb¡Ågrankingëcrfcd7825irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvA Network Address Translator (NAT) Traversal Mechanism for Media Controlled by the Real-Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$DHCPv6 is a protocol that is used to provide addressing and configuration information to IPv6 hosts.  This document describes the privacy issues associated with the use of DHCPv6 by Internet users.  It is intended to be an analysis of the present situation and does not propose any solutions.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalski¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiangddatejœ&hfilenamegrfc7824eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129647hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW;öÉgrankingêcrfcd7824irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Privacy Considerations for DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;In certain networks, it is critical to consider network performance criteria when selecting the path for an explicitly routed RSVP-TE Label Switched Path (LSP).  Such performance criteria can include latency, jitter, and loss or other indications such as the conformance to link performance objectives and non-RSVP TE traffic load.  This specification describes how a path computation function may use network performance data, such as is advertised via the OSPF and IS-IS TE metric extensions (defined outside the scope of this document) to perform such path selections.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Giacalone¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Prevididdatej’¥hfilenamegrfc7823eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129646hkeywordsÇsTraffic EngineeringpPath ComputationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateW1%•grankingècrfcd7823irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmPerformance-Based Path Selection for Explicitly Routed Label Switched Paths (LSPs) Using TE Metric ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊The Network Time Protocol version 4 (NTPv4) defines the optional usage of extension fields.  An extension field, as defined in RFC 5905, is an optional field that resides at the end of the NTP header and that can be used to add optional capabilities or additional information that is not conveyed in the standard NTP header.  This document updates RFC 5905 by clarifying some points regarding NTP extension fields and their usage with Message Authentication Codes (MACs).fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationwNetwork Time FoundationdnamehD. Mayerddatej’®hfilenamegrfc7822eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129645hkeywordsÇcNTPoextension fieldkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¸u^grankingécrfcd7822irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Network Time Protocol Version 4 (NTPv4) Extension FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9748∂habstractyÏThe Network Time Protocol (NTP) allows clients to synchronize to a time server using timestamped protocol messages.  To facilitate accurate timestamping, some implementations use hardware-based timestamping engines that integrate the accurate transmission time into every outgoing NTP packet during transmission.  Since these packets are transported over UDP, the UDP Checksum field is then updated to reflect this modification.  This document proposes an extension field that includes a 2-octet Checksum Complement, allowing timestamping engines to reflect the checksum modification in the last 2 octets of the packet rather than in the UDP Checksum field.  The behavior defined in this document is interoperable with existing NTP implementations.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahiddatej’∆hfilenamegrfc7821eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129644hkeywordsÑcNTPcUDPhChecksumltimestampingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¸u^grankingçcrfcd7821irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:UDP Checksum Complement in the Network Time Protocol (NTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9748∂habstracty[The One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) and the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP) are used for performance monitoring in IP networks.  Delay measurement is performed in these protocols by using timestamped test packets.  Some implementations use hardware-based timestamping engines that integrate the accurate transmission time into every outgoing OWAMP/TWAMP test packet during transmission.  Since these packets are transported over UDP, the UDP Checksum field is then updated to reflect this modification.  This document proposes to use the last 2 octets of every test packet as a Checksum Complement, allowing timestamping engines to reflect the checksum modification in the last 2 octets rather than in the UDP Checksum field.  The behavior defined in this document is completely interoperable with existing OWAMP/TWAMP implementations.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahiddatej’√hfilenamegrfc7820eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129643hkeywordsÑhChecksumcUDPdIPPMltimestampingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¸u]grankingåcrfcd7820irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzUDP Checksum Complement in the One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) and Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¬DHCP is a protocol that is used to provide addressing and configuration information to IPv4 hosts.  This document discusses the various identifiers used by DHCP and the potential privacy issues.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalskiddatej’≈hfilenamegrfc7819eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129642hkeywordsÅlDHCP PrivacykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW%GQgrankingãcrfcd7819irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPrivacy Considerations for DHCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) "mef" for Uniform Resource Names (URNs) used to identify resources published by MEF Forum (https://www.mef.net).  MEF specifies and manages resources that utilize this URN identification model.  Management activities for these and other resources types are handled by the manager of the MEF Assigned Names and Numbers (MANN) registry.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqCisco Systems IncdnameoM. Jethanandaniddatejœ"hfilenamegrfc7818eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129641hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¸u[grankingä