 Improve PrivacydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document describes a minimal initiator version of the Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocol for constrained nodes. IKEv2 is a component of IPsec used for performing mutual authentication and establishing and maintaining Security Associations (SAs). IKEv2 includes several optional features, which are not needed in minimal implementations. This document describes what is required from the minimal implementation and also describes various optimizations that can be done. The protocol described here is interoperable with a full IKEv2 implementation using shared secret authentication (IKEv2 does not require the use of certificate authentication). This minimal initiator implementation can only talk to a full IKEv2 implementation acting as the responder; thus, two minimal initiator implementations cannot talk to each other.

This document does not update or modify RFC 7296 but provides a more compact description of the minimal version of the protocol. If this document and RFC 7296 conflict, then RFC 7296 is the authoritative description.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejT. Kivinenddatej’¿hfilenamegrfc7815eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlwigdfullx+lwig - Light-Weight Implementation Guidancednamex$Light-Weight Implementation Guidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129638hkeywordsÑcIKEeIPseccIoTkConstrainedkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateVÙUÉgrankingácrfcd7815irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMinimal Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2) Initiator ImplementationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx’This document describes a BGP/MPLS IP VPN-based subnet extension solution referred to as "Virtual Subnet", which can be used for building Layer 3 network virtualization overlays within and/or between data centers.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeX. Xu¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelC. Jacquenet¢kaffiliationlBloomberg LPdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationlBloomberg LPdnamehT. Boyes¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnamefB. Feeddatej‘Óhfilenamegrfc7814eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129637hkeywordsÉxData Center InterconnectsData Center NetworkxVirtual Machine (VM) migrationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¸uYgrankingÜcrfcd7814irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAVirtual Subnet: A BGP/MPLS IP VPN-Based Subnet Extension SolutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyéIEEE 802.1Qca Path Control and Reservation (PCR) specifies explicit path control via IS-IS in Layer 2 networks in order to move beyond the shortest path capabilities provided by IEEE 802.1aq Shortest Path Bridging (SPB).  IS-IS PCR provides capabilities for the establishment and control of explicit forwarding trees in a Layer 2 network domain.  This document specifies the sub-TLVs for IS-IS PCR.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Farkas¢kaffiliationeCienadnamehN. Bragg¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelP. Unbehagen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejG. Parsons¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamepP. Ashwood-Smith¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiC. Bowersddatej’√hfilenamegrfc7813eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129636hkeywordsÇeIS-IScSPBkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateWc>”grankingÖcrfcd7813irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"IS-IS Path Control and ReservationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the architecture for IP and LDP Fast Reroute using Maximally Redundant Trees (MRT-FRR).  MRT-FRR is a technology that gives link-protection and node-protection with 100% coverage in any network topology that is still connected after the failure.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiC. Bowers¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Enyediddatej’≈hfilenamegrfc7812eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129635hkeywordsÜcMRTcFRRcLFAhrecoverygfailuregroutingkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateWc>”grankingÑcrfcd7812irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQAn Architecture for IP/LDP Fast Reroute Using Maximally Redundant Trees (MRT-FRR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis document supports the solution put forth in "An Architecture for IP/LDP Fast Reroute Using Maximally Redundant Trees (MRT-FRR)" (RFC 7812) by defining the associated MRT Lowpoint algorithm that is used in the Default MRT Profile to compute both the necessary Maximally Redundant Trees with their associated next hops and the alternates to select for MRT-FRR.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Enyedi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Csaszar¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiC. Bowers¢kaffiliationuUniversity of ArizonadnamejA. Gopalanddatej’ƒhfilenamegrfc7811eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129634hkeywordsÜcMRTcFRRcLFAhrecoverygfailuregroutingkobsoletedByÄepagesvopublicationDateWc>—grankingÉcrfcd7811irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXAn Algorithm for Computing IP/LDP Fast Reroute Using Maximally Redundant Trees (MRT-FRR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$In certain networks, such as, but not limited to, financial information networks (e.g., stock market data providers), network- performance criteria (e.g., latency) are becoming as critical to data-path selection as other metrics.

This document describes extensions to IS-IS Traffic Engineering Extensions (RFC 5305) such that network-performance information can be distributed and collected in a scalable fashion. The information distributed using IS-IS TE Metric Extensions can then be used to make path-selection decisions based on network performance.

Note that this document only covers the mechanisms with which network-performance information is distributed. The mechanisms for measuring network performance or acting on that information, once distributed, are outside the scope of this document.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Giacalone¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatejœ$hfilenamegrfc7810eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129633hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8570epagesopublicationDateW1%§grankingÇcrfcd7810irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0IS-IS Traffic Engineering (TE) Metric ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx⁄This document defines an update to the Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) calendar access protocol (RFC 4791) to allow clients and servers to exchange iCalendar data without the need to send full time zone data.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Dabooddatej’≥hfilenamegrfc7809eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftzdistdfullx,tzdist - Time Zone Data Distribution Servicednamex#Time Zone Data Distribution Servicedtypebwgbidjdoc-129632hkeywordsÑfCalDAVkcalendaringiiCalendaritime zonekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV˝¿¯grankingÅcrfcd7809irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBCalendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV): Time Zones by ReferencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document defines a time zone data distribution service that allows reliable, secure, and fast delivery of time zone data and leap-second rules to client systems such as calendaring and scheduling applications or operating systems.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsSpherical Cow GroupdnamekM. Douglass¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Dabooddatej’¶hfilenamegrfc7808eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftzdistdfullx,tzdist - Time Zone Data Distribution Servicednamex#Time Zone Data Distribution Servicedtypebwgbidjdoc-129631hkeywordsÉitime zonekcalendaringjschedulingkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateV˝¿˜grankingÄcrfcd7808irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Time Zone Data Distribution ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This document defines a "problem detail" as a way to carry machine- readable details of errors in a HTTP response to avoid the need to define new error response formats for HTTP APIs.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliation`dnamehE. Wildeddatej’¡hfilenamegrfc7807eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129630hkeywordsáfstatusdHTTPeerrorgproblemcAPIdJSONcXMLkobsoletedByÅd9457epagesopublicationDateV˝¿ıgrankingcrfcd7807irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexProblem Details for HTTP APIsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis note discusses queuing and marking/dropping algorithms.  While these algorithms may be implemented in a coupled manner, this note argues that specifications, measurements, and comparisons should decouple the different algorithms and their contributions to system behavior.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefR. PanddatejÕôhfilenamegrfc7806eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129629hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWÈ6granking~crfcd7806irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!On Queuing, Marking, and DroppingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThis document reclassifies several TCP extensions and TCP-related documents that either have been superseded, have never seen widespread use, or are no longer recommended for use to "Historic" status.  The affected documents are RFCs 675, 721, 761, 813, 816, 879, 896, 1078, and 6013.  Additionally, this document reclassifies RFCs 700, 794, 814, 817, 872, 889, 964, and 1071 to "Informational" status.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamemA. Zimmermann¢kaffiliationkMTI SystemsdnamegW. Eddy¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggertddatejœ!hfilenamegrfc7805eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129628hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW␀agranking}crfcd7805irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Moving Outdated TCP Extensions and TCP-Related Documents to Historic or Informational StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification describes a family of HTTP authentication mechanisms called the Salted Challenge Response Authentication Mechanism (SCRAM), which provides a more robust authentication mechanism than a plaintext password protected by Transport Layer Security (TLS) and avoids the deployment obstacles presented by earlier TLS-protected challenge response authentication mechanisms.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’æhfilenamegrfc7804eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhhttpauthdfullx5httpauth - Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdnamex*Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129627hkeywordsáhHTTPAUTHdHTTPdSASLeSCRAMcGS2fGSSAPIgGSS-APIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV‡tØgranking|crfcd7804irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Salted Challenge Response HTTP Authentication MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyOThe registration policy for the "Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) Capability URNs" registry, set up by RFC 6241, has turned out to be unnecessarily strict.  This document changes that registration policy to "IETF Review", allowing registrations from certain well-reviewed Experimental RFCs, in addition to Standards Track RFCs.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatejœhfilenamegrfc7803eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129626hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVŒ0granking{crfcd7803irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÀetotaletitlexIChanging the Registration Policy for the NETCONF Capability URNs RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThis document defines the Pseudo-Random Function (PRF) for the Kerberos V mechanism for the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API), based on the PRF defined for the Kerberos V cryptographic framework, for keying application protocols given an established Kerberos V GSS-API security context.

This document obsoletes RFC 4402 and reclassifies that document as Historic. RFC 4402 starts the PRF+ counter at 1; however, a number of implementations start the counter at 0. As a result, the original specification would not be interoperable with existing implementations.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehS. Emery¢kaffiliationlCryptonectordnamekN. Williamsddatejœhfilenamegrfc7802eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129625hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÿò√grankingzcrfcd7802irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|A Pseudo-Random Function (PRF) for the Kerberos V Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty^This document is intended to be a source of information about the Russian Federal standard GOST R 34.12-2015 describing the block cipher with a block length of n=128 bits and a key length of k=256 bits, which is also referred to as "Kuznyechik".  This algorithm is one of the set of Russian cryptographic standard algorithms (called GOST algorithms).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxResearch Computer Center MSUdnamekV. Dolmatovddatej’√hfilenamegrfc7801eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129624hkeywordsÇjKuznyechiklBlock CipherkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÒËVgrankingycrfcd7801irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,GOST R 34.12-2015: Block Cipher "Kuznyechik"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis specification describes how to declare in a JSON Web Token (JWT) that the presenter of the JWT possesses a particular proof-of- possession key and how the recipient can cryptographically confirm proof of possession of the key by the presenter.  Being able to prove possession of a key is also sometimes described as the presenter being a holder-of-key.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationkARM LimiteddnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’æhfilenamegrfc7800eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129623hkeywordsÑnJSON Web TokencJWTsProof-of-PossessionmHolder-of-KeykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW¶Ïgrankingxcrfcd7800irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Proof-of-Possession Key Semantics for JSON Web Tokens (JWTs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis memo provides clear definitions for Active and Passive performance assessment.  The construction of Metrics and Methods can be described as either "Active" or "Passive".  Some methods may use a subset of both Active and Passive attributes, and we refer to these as "Hybrid Methods".  This memo also describes multiple dimensions to help evaluate new methods as they emerge.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’¡hfilenamegrfc7799eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129622hkeywordsÑnIP PerformancelMeasurementsgTestingxNetwork CharacterizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWCv√grankingwcrfcd7799irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEActive and Passive Metrics and Methods (with Hybrid Types In-Between)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This memo describes an RTP payload format for the video coding standard ITU-T Recommendation H.265 and ISO/IEC International Standard 23008-2, both also known as High Efficiency Video Coding (HEVC) and developed by the Joint Collaborative Team on Video Coding (JCT-VC).  The RTP payload format allows for packetization of one or more Network Abstraction Layer (NAL) units in each RTP packet payload as well as fragmentation of a NAL unit into multiple RTP packets.  Furthermore, it supports transmission of an HEVC bitstream over a single stream as well as multiple RTP streams.  When multiple RTP streams are used, a single transport or multiple transports may be utilized.  The payload format has wide applicability in videoconferencing, Internet video streaming, and high-bitrate entertainment-quality video, among others.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejY.-K. Wang¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer HHIdnamejY. Sanchez¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer HHIdnamejT. Schierl¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiS. Wenger¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepM. M. Hannukseladdatej’ªhfilenamegrfc7798eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129621hkeywordsáeH.265q: ISO/IEC 23008-2vSingle NAL Unit PacketrAggregation PacketrFragmentation Unitx"Payload Content Information Packetx#Use of HEVC with Feedback Messages.kobsoletedByÄepagesVopublicationDateVÒËTgrankingvcrfcd7798irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:RTP Payload Format for High Efficiency Video Coding (HEVC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJSON Web Signature (JWS) represents the payload of a JWS as a base64url-encoded value and uses this value in the JWS Signature computation. While this enables arbitrary payloads to be integrity protected, some have described use cases in which the base64url encoding is unnecessary and/or an impediment to adoption, especially when the payload is large and/or detached. This specification defines a means of accommodating these use cases by defining an option to change the JWS Signing Input computation to not base64url- encode the payload. This option is intended to broaden the set of use cases for which the use of JWS is a good fit.

This specification updates RFC 7519 by stating that JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) MUST NOT use the unencoded payload option defined by this specification.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej’æhfilenamegrfc7797eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129620hkeywordsäxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONx"JSON Object Signing and EncryptiondJOSErJSON Web SignaturecJWSqDigital SignaturexMessage Authentication CodecMACqUnencoded PayloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVœîzgrankingucrfcd7797irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1JSON Web Signature (JWS) Unencoded Payload OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document specifies a generic Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) solution, which uses VLANs to indicate root or leaf traffic to support Ethernet-Tree (E-Tree) services.  A VPLS Provider Edge (PE) model is illustrated as an example for the solution.  In the solution, E-Tree VPLS PEs are interconnected by Pseudowires (PWs), which carry the VLAN indicating the E-Tree attribute.  The MAC address-based Ethernet forwarding engine and the PW work in the same way as specified in RFC 4762 and RFC 4448, respectively.  A signaling mechanism is described to support E-Tree capability and VLAN mapping negotiation.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehY. Jiang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegL. Yong¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegM. Paulddatej’Ωhfilenamegrfc7796eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129619hkeywordsÅeEtreekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÈÂGgrankingtcrfcd7796irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDEthernet-Tree (E-Tree) Support in Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This document describes Multi-Segment Pseudowire (MS-PW) protection scenarios in which pseudowire redundancy is provided on the Switching Provider Edge (S-PE) as defined in RFC 5659.  Operations of the S-PEs that provide PW redundancy are specified in this document.  Signaling of the Preferential Forwarding status as defined in RFCs 6870 and 6478 is reused.  This document does not require any change to the Terminating Provider Edges (T-PEs) of MS-PW.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Wangddatejœhfilenamegrfc7795eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129618hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateV–Á1grankingscrfcd7795irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Pseudowire Redundancy on the Switching Provider Edge (S-PE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxµThis document introduces new sub-TLVs to support advertisement of IPv4 and IPv6 prefix attribute flags and the source router ID of the router that originated a prefix advertisement.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeX. Xu¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekU. Chunduriddatej’øhfilenamegrfc7794eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129617hkeywordsÅdISISkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateVÿîFgrankingrcrfcd7794irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?IS-IS Prefix Attributes for Extended IPv4 and IPv6 ReachabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty]RFC 6598 specifies that "Reverse DNS queries for Shared Address Space addresses [100.64.0.0/10] MUST NOT be forwarded to the global DNS infrastructure."

This document formally directs IANA to add the associated zones to the "IPv4 Locally-Served DNS Zones Registry" to prevent such queries from accidentally leaking to the global DNS infrastructure.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejM. Andrewsddatejœhfilenamegrfc7793eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129616hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW5=úgrankingqcrfcd7793irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber£etotaletitlexKAdding 100.64.0.0/10 Prefixes to the IPv4 Locally-Served DNS Zones RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˆThis memo describes the extensions to the Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling protocol to support Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in a GMPLS-controlled network that includes devices using the flexible optical grid.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelliddatej’Ωhfilenamegrfc7792eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129615hkeywordsämFlexible-griduFlexible optical gridoOptical networkmOptical trailkOptical LSPeGMPLScWDMcPCEtspectrum reservationqflexible spectrumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV„]Tgrankingpcrfcd7792irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlRSVP-TE Signaling Extensions in Support of Flexi-Grid Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKThis document considers a VPN end user establishing an IPsec Security Association (SA) with a Security Gateway using the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2), where at least one of the peers has multiple interfaces or where Security Gateway is a cluster with each node having its own IP address.

The protocol described allows a peer to clone an IKEv2 SA, where an additional SA is derived from an existing one. The newly created IKE SA is set without the IKEv2 authentication exchange. This IKE SA can later be assigned to another interface or moved to another cluster node.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiErcisssondnamejD. Migault¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej’ºhfilenamegrfc7791eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129614hkeywordsÑcMIFnLoad balancinglLoad sharingfMOBIKEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÿîEgrankingocrfcd7791irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Cloning the IKE Security Association in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰The framework for the preparation, enforcement, and comparison of internationalized strings (PRECIS) defines several classes of strings for use in application protocols.  Because many protocols perform case-sensitive or case-insensitive string comparison, it is necessary to define methods for case mapping.  In addition, both the Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) and the PRECIS problem statement describe mappings for internationalized strings that are not limited to case, but include width mapping and mapping of delimiters and other special characters that can be taken into consideration.  This document provides guidelines for designers of PRECIS profiles and describes several mappings that can be applied between receiving user input and passing permitted code points to internationalized protocols.  In particular, this document describes both locale-dependent and context-depending case mappings as well as additional mappings for delimiters and special characters.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnameiY. Yoneya¢kaffiliationoKeio UniversitydnameiT. Nemotoddatejœhfilenamegrfc7790eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129613hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateV‘’’grankingncrfcd7790irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextMapping Characters for Classes of the Preparation, Enforcement, and Comparison of Internationalized Strings (PRECIS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document describes how unexpected traffic flows can emerge across an autonomous system as the result of other autonomous systems filtering or restricting the propagation of more-specific prefixes.  We provide a review of the techniques to detect the occurrence of this issue and defend against it.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsIMDEA Networks/UC3MdnamejC. Cardona¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekP. Francois¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Lucenteddatej’¥hfilenamegrfc7789eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129612hkeywordsÑtMore-specific prefixtLess-specific prefixrAutonomous systemssTraffic engineeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW¶Îgrankingmcrfcd7789irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Impact of BGP Filtering on Inter-Domain Routing PoliciesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰This document describes the Home Networking Control Protocol (HNCP), an extensible configuration protocol, and a set of requirements for home network devices.  HNCP is described as a profile of and extension to the Distributed Node Consensus Protocol (DNCP).  HNCP enables discovery of network borders, automated configuration of addresses, name resolution, service discovery, and the use of any routing protocol that supports routing based on both the source and destination address.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamekM. Stenberg¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehS. Barth¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Pfisterddatej’´hfilenamegrfc7788eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129611hkeywordsÉdIPv6gHomenetdDNCPkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateWz„grankinglcrfcd7788irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Home Networking Control ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8375∂habstractyThis document describes the Distributed Node Consensus Protocol (DNCP), a generic state synchronization protocol that uses the Trickle algorithm and hash trees.  DNCP is an abstract protocol and must be combined with a specific profile to make a complete implementable protocol.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamekM. Stenberg¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehS. Barthddatej’πhfilenamegrfc7787eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129610hkeywordsÅgHomenetkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateWz·grankingkcrfcd7787irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Distributed Node Consensus ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCongestion Exposure (ConEx) is a mechanism by which senders inform the network about expected congestion based on congestion feedback from previous packets in the same flow.  This document describes the necessary modifications to use ConEx with the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamemM. Kuehlewind¢kaffiliationlNetApp, Inc.dnamepR. Scheffeneggerddatejœhfilenamegrfc7786eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeconexdfullxconex - Congestion ExposurednamesCongestion Exposuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129609hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateW6ãgrankingjcrfcd7786irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1TCP Modifications for Congestion Exposure (ConEx)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxæThis document discusses issues induced by the change of the Dual- Stack Lite (DS-Lite) Basic Bridging BroadBand (B4) IPv6 address and sketches a set of recommendations to solve those issues.fadNameoTerry MandersongauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemS. Vinapamula¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadairddatej’ºhfilenamegrfc7785eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129608hkeywordsåwIPv4 service continuitywIPv4 address exhaustiontService AvailabilityqHigh AvailabilityoAddress sharingdIPv6kReliabilitynIPv4 over IPv6oState migrationiStabilityjDisruptiongPrivacykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateV√ÊÄgrankingicrfcd7785irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexMRecommendations for Prefix Binding in the Context of Softwire Dual-Stack LitedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx†This document specifies the MIB for the OAM (Operations, Administration, and Maintenance) objects for IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links).fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Kumar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliation`dnameoT. Senevirathneddatej’Øhfilenamegrfc7784eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129607hkeywordsÑcCFMcMEPcMIPpFault ManagementkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateV‘’’grankinghcrfcd7784irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) facilitates loop-free connectivity to non-TRILL networks via a choice of an Appointed Forwarder for a set of VLANs.  Appointed Forwarders provide VLAN-based load sharing with an active-standby model.  High-performance applications require an active-active load-sharing model.  The active-active load-sharing model can be accomplished by representing any given non-TRILL network with a single virtual RBridge (also referred to as a virtual Routing Bridge or virtual TRILL switch).  Virtual representation of the non-TRILL network with a single RBridge poses serious challenges in multi-destination RPF (Reverse Path Forwarding) check calculations.  This document specifies required enhancements to build Coordinated Multicast Trees (CMT) within the TRILL campus to solve related RPF issues.  CMT, which only requires a software upgrade, provides flexibility to RBridges in selecting a desired path of association to a given TRILL multi-destination distribution tree.  This document updates RFC 6325.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameoT. Senevirathne¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekJ. Pathangi¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiJ. Hudsonddatej’Øhfilenamegrfc7783eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129606hkeywordsÇhAffinitycRPFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVœîygrankinggcrfcd7783irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZCoordinated Multicast Trees (CMT) for Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉTRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) active-active service provides end stations with flow-level load balance and resilience against link failures at the edge of TRILL campuses, as described in RFC 7379.

This document specifies a method by which member RBridges (also referred to as Routing Bridges or TRILL switches) in an active-active edge RBridge group use their own nicknames as ingress RBridge nicknames to encapsulate frames from attached end systems. Thus, remote edge RBridges (who are not in the group) will see one host Media Access Control (MAC) address being associated with the multiple RBridges in the group. Such remote edge RBridges are required to maintain all those associations (i.e., MAC attachments) and to not flip-flop among them (as would occur prior to the implementation of this specification). The design goals of this specification are discussed herein.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationqAstute TechnologydnamegH. Zhai¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Durrani¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamehS. Guptaddatej’πhfilenamegrfc7782eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129605hkeywordsÑeLAALPgvSwitchfMC-LAGdDRNIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVœîxgrankingfcrfcd7782irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Active-Active Edge Using Multiple MAC AttachmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThe IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol provides support for flow-level multipathing for both unicast and multi-destination traffic in networks with arbitrary topology.  Active-active access at the TRILL edge is the extension of these characteristics to end stations that are multiply connected to a TRILL campus as discussed in RFC 7379.  In this document, the edge RBridge (Routing Bridge, or TRILL switch) group providing active-active access to such an end station is represented as a virtual RBridge.  Based on the concept of the virtual RBridge, along with its pseudo-nickname, this document specifies a method for TRILL active-active access by such end stations.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcJITdnamegH. Zhai¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameoT. Senevirathne¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Liddatej’±hfilenamegrfc7781eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129604hkeywordsÉovirtual RBridgekaggregationmflip-floppingkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateVœîwgrankingecrfcd7781irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Pseudo-Nickname for Active-Active AccessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®Since the publication of the TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) base protocol in 2011, active development and deployment of TRILL have revealed errata in RFC 6325 and areas that could use clarifications or updates.  RFC 7177, RFC 7357, and an intended replacement of RFC 6439 provide clarifications and updates with respect to adjacency, the TRILL ESADI (End Station Address Distribution Information) protocol, and Appointed Forwarders, respectively.  This document provides other known clarifications, corrections, and updates.  It obsoletes RFC 7180 (the previous "TRILL clarifications, corrections, and updates" RFC), and it updates RFCs 6325, 7177, and 7179.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekA. Banerjee¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamehS. Guptaddatej’πhfilenamegrfc7780eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129603hkeywordsåeTRILLgRBridgeeIS-ISlreachabilityhoverloadcMTUcDEIimulticastcRPFecolorfE-L1FSepurgekobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateVœîvgrankingdcrfcd7780irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Clarifications, Corrections, and UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8249∂habstractx}This document specifies a Directional Airtime (DAT) link metric for usage in Optimized Link State Routing version 2 (OLSRv2).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoFraunhofer FKIEdnamehH. Rogge¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamekE. Baccelliddatej’∑hfilenamegrfc7779eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129602hkeywordsäeMANETfmetricnad hoc networkgroutingkIP networksdOLSRcETTcETXiFunkfeuercDATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateWøãgrankingccrfcd7779irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoDirectional Airtime Metric Based on Packet Sequence Numbers for Optimized Link State Routing Version 2 (OLSRv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This memo describes a mobile communications use case for congestion exposure (ConEx) with a particular focus on those mobile communication networks that are architecturally similar to the 3GPP Evolved Packet System (EPS).  This memo provides a brief overview of the architecture of these networks (both access and core networks) and current QoS mechanisms and then discusses how congestion exposure concepts could be applied.  Based on this discussion, this memo suggests a set of requirements for ConEx mechanisms that particularly apply to these mobile networks.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamekD. Kutscher¢kaffiliationcNECdnamefF. Mir¢kaffiliationcNECdnameiR. Winter¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationtHewlett Packard LabsdnamehY. Zhang¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej’Ωhfilenamegrfc7778eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeconexdfullxconex - Congestion ExposurednamesCongestion Exposuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129601hkeywordsÇscongestion exposureumobile communicationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV„]Sgrankingbcrfcd7778irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Mobile Communication Congestion Exposure ScenariodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!This document describes an extension to the OSPF protocol to add an optional operational capability that allows tagging and grouping of the nodes in an OSPF domain. This allows simplification, ease of management and control over route and path selection based on configured policies. This document describes an extension to the OSPF protocol to advertise node administrative tags. The node tags can be used to express and apply locally defined network policies, which are a very useful operational capability. Node tags may be used by either OSPF itself or other applications consuming information propagated via OSPF.

This document describes the protocol extensions to disseminate node administrative tags to the OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 protocol. It provides example use cases of administrative node tags.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehS. Hegde¢kaffiliationxJive Communications, Inc.dnameiR. Shakir¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Smirnov¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraeneddatej’ºhfilenamegrfc7777eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129600hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV„]Rgrankingacrfcd7777irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Advertising Node Administrative Tags in OSPFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyßIETF Participants must not engage in harassment while at IETF meetings, virtual meetings, or social events or while participating in mailing lists. This document lays out procedures for managing and enforcing this policy.

This document updates RFC 2418 by defining new working group guidelines and procedures. This document updates RFC 7437 by allowing the Ombudsteam to form a recall petition without further signatories.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies, Inc.dnamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrelddatej’ºhfilenamegrfc7776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129599hkeywordsÉiOmbudsmanlOmbudspersonjOmbudsteamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÿò¬granking`crfcd7776irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlexIETF Anti-Harassment ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8716∂habstractyYIn existing specifications, the route preferences for IPv4/IPv6 Extended Reachability TLVs are not explicitly stated.  There are also inconsistencies in the definition of how the up/down bit applies to route preference when the prefix advertisement appears in Level 2 Link State Protocol Data Units (LSPs).  This document addresses these issues.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationwOrange Business ServicednamelS. Litkowski¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Prevididdatejœhfilenamegrfc7775eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129598hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV‘’‘granking_crfcd7775irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<IS-IS Route Preference for Extended IP and IPv6 ReachabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document defines a way to configure a parameter set for MPL (Multicast Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks) via a DHCPv6 option.  MPL has a set of parameters to control its behavior, and the parameter set is often configured as a network-wide parameter because the parameter set should be identical for each MPL Forwarder in an MPL Domain.  Using the MPL Parameter Configuration Option defined in this document, a network can easily be configured with a single set of MPL parameters.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsToshiba CorporationdnamefY. Doi¢kaffiliationkItron, Inc.dnamekM. Gillmoreddatej’∞hfilenamegrfc7774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129597hkeywordsÇcMPLfDHCPv6kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateVÈÂEgranking^crfcd7774irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcMulticast Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (MPL) Parameter Configuration Option for DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›This document defines an extension to X.509 certificates. The extension defined in this document holds data about how the certificate subject was authenticated by the Certification Authority that issued the certificate in which this extension appears.

This document also defines one data structure for inclusion in this extension. The data structure is designed to hold information when the subject is authenticated using a Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) assertion.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationl3xA SecuritydnamelS. Santessonddatejœhfilenamegrfc7773eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129596hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVﬂÖxgranking]crfcd7773irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Authentication Context Certificate ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxòFrequent Router Advertisement messages can severely impact host power consumption.  This document recommends operational practices to avoid such impact.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenA. Yourtchenko¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Colittiddatejœhfilenamegrfc7772eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129595hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVº›Qgranking\crfcd7772irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber etotaletitlex4Reducing Energy Consumption of Router AdvertisementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈIn MPLS and MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) environments, statically provisioned Single-Segment Pseudowires (SS-PWs) are protected against tunnel failure via MPLS-level and MPLS-TP-level tunnel protection.  With statically provisioned Multi-Segment Pseudowires (MS-PWs), each segment of the MS-PW is likewise protected from tunnel failures via MPLS-level and MPLS-TP-level tunnel protection.  However, static MS-PWs are not protected end-to-end against failure of one of the Switching Provider Edge Routers (S-PEs) along the path of the MS-PW.  This document describes how to achieve this protection via redundant MS-PWs by updating the existing procedures in RFC 6870.  It also contains an optional approach based on MPLS-TP Linear Protection.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationnHai Gaoming BVdnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationjSK TelecomdnamegJ. Shin¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnameoA. D'Alessandroddatej’∂hfilenamegrfc7771eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129594hkeywordsÇuend-to-end protectionqlinear protectionkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateV´È±granking[crfcd7771irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxSwitching Provider Edge (S-PE) Protection for MPLS and MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Static Multi-Segment PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$It is useful for routers in an OSPFv2 or OSPFv3 routing domain to know the capabilities of their neighbors and other routers in the routing domain.  This document proposes extensions to OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 for advertising optional router capabilities.  The Router Information (RI) Link State Advertisement (LSA) is defined for this purpose.  In OSPFv2, the RI LSA will be implemented with an Opaque LSA type ID.  In OSPFv3, the RI LSA will be implemented with a unique LSA type function code.  In both protocols, the RI LSA can be advertised at any of the defined flooding scopes (link, area, or autonomous system (AS)).  This document obsoletes RFC 4970 by providing a revised specification that includes support for advertisement of multiple instances of the RI LSA and a TLV for functional capabilities.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationfArktandnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamejS. Shafferddatej’∂hfilenamegrfc7770eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129593hkeywordsáfospfv2fospfv3xopen shortest path firstbrirrouter informationclsaxlink state advertisementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVØœÈgrankingZcrfcd7770irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Extensions to OSPF for Advertising Optional Router CapabilitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis document specifies a mechanism to signal Media Access Control (MAC) address withdrawal notification using a pseudowire (PW) Associated Channel (ACH).  Such notification is useful when statically provisioned PWs are deployed in a Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) or Hierarchical Virtual Private LAN Service (H-VPLS) environment.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationkCiena Corp.dnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamemM. Venkatesanddatej’∫hfilenamegrfc7769eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129592hkeywordsÉbPWcACHrassociated channelkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateVœîsgrankingYcrfcd7769irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDMedia Access Control (MAC) Address Withdrawal over Static PseudowiredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¥Various IPv6 transition strategies require the introduction of large- scale NATs (e.g., AFTR and NAT64) to share the limited supply of IPv4 addresses available in the network until transition is complete.  There has recently been debate over how to manage the sharing of ports between different subscribers sharing the same IPv4 address.  One factor in the discussion is the operational requirement to log the assignment of transport addresses to subscribers.  It has been argued that dynamic assignment of individual ports between subscribers requires the generation of an excessive volume of logs.  This document suggests a way to achieve dynamic port sharing while keeping log volumes low.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpPhilips LightingdnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnameeW. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. Taylor¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehJ. Huangddatejœhfilenamegrfc7768eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129591hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV´È±grankingXcrfcd7768irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex5Port Management to Reduce Logging in Large-Scale NATsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÿThis document specifies a mechanism for a host to indicate via the Port Control Protocol (PCP) which connections should be protected against network failures. These connections will then be subject to high-availability mechanisms enabled on the network side.

This approach assumes that applications and/or users have more visibility about sensitive connections than any heuristic that can be enabled on the network side to guess which connections should be check-pointed.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemS. Vinapamula¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddyddatej’∑hfilenamegrfc7767eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129590hkeywordsânserviceabilitycSDNjresiliencejrobustnesswnetwork programmabilityknetwork APIsapplication controlxservice-aware networkingjautomationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVπ{@grankingWcrfcd7767irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHApplication-Initiated Check-Pointing via the Port Control Protocol (PCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the requirement for support of TCP as a transport protocol for DNS implementations and provides guidelines towards DNS-over-TCP performance on par with that of DNS-over-UDP.  This document obsoletes RFC 5966 and therefore updates RFC 1035 and RFC 1123.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamelJ. Dickinson¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamelS. Dickinson¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiR. Bellis¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnameiA. Mankin¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamejD. Wesselsddatej’¿hfilenamegrfc7766eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129589hkeywordsÉcDNSfTCP/IPitransportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÿîDgrankingVcrfcd7766irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4DNS Transport over TCP - Implementation RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9103d8490∂habstractyhThis document describes a modified sender-side algorithm for managing the TCP and Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) retransmission timers that provides faster loss recovery when there is a small amount of outstanding data for a connection.  The modification, RTO Restart (RTOR), allows the transport to restart its retransmission timer using a smaller timeout duration, so that the effective retransmission timeout (RTO) becomes more aggressive in situations where fast retransmit cannot be used.  This enables faster loss detection and recovery for connections that are short lived or application limited.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnameiP. Hurtig¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnamelA. Brunstrom¢kaffiliationxSimula Research Laboratory ASdnamejA. Petlund¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzlddatej’ªhfilenamegrfc7765eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129588hkeywordsÉctcptretransmission timerdrtorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVº›PgrankingUcrfcd7765irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?TCP and Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) RTO RestartdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document elaborates upon the text/markdown media type for use with Markdown, a family of plain-text formatting syntaxes that optionally can be converted to formal markup languages such as HTML.  Background information, local storage strategies, and additional syntax registrations are supplied.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmPenango, Inc.dnamejS. Leonardddatej’µhfilenamegrfc7764eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129587hkeywordsÅmtext/markdownkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÒ€>grankingTcrfcd7764irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYGuidance on Markdown: Design Philosophies, Stability Strategies, and Select RegistrationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¿This document registers the text/markdown media type for use with Markdown, a family of plain-text formatting syntaxes that optionally can be converted to formal markup languages such as HTML.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmPenango, Inc.dnamejS. Leonardddatejœhfilenamegrfc7763eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129586hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÒ€<grankingScrfcd7763irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe text/markdown Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‰This document establishes an Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) registry for Content Security Policy directives and populates that registry with the directives defined in the Content Security Policy Level 2 specification.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjGoogle IncdnamegM. Westddatejœhfilenamegrfc7762eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129585hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV™…FgrankingRcrfcd7762irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFInitial Assignment for the Content Security Policy Directives RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty©This document specifies Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM). PIM-SM is a multicast routing protocol that can use the underlying unicast routing information base or a separate multicast-capable routing information base. It builds unidirectional shared trees rooted at a Rendezvous Point (RP) per group, and it optionally creates shortest-path trees per source.

This document obsoletes RFC 4601 by replacing it, addresses the errata filed against it, removes the optional (*,*,RP), PIM Multicast Border Router features and authentication using IPsec that lack sufficient deployment experience (see Appendix A), and moves the PIM specification to Internet Standard.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnameiB. Fenner¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnamekH. Holbrook¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnamekI. Kouvelas¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Parekh¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehL. ZhengddatejŒ¸hfilenamegrfc7761eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-129584hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesâopublicationDateV„]QgrankingQcrfcd7761irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberSetotaletitlexWProtocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM): Protocol Specification (Revised)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9436d8736∂habstractxíThis is the Statement of Work (SOW) for extensions to the IETF Datatracker to provide statistics about RFCs and Internet-Drafts and their authors.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatejœhfilenamegrfc7760eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129583hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV†WÁgrankingPcrfcd7760irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNStatement of Work for Extensions to the IETF Datatracker for Author StatisticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‰This specification describes the configuration of proactive MPLS-TP Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) functions for a given Label Switched Path (LSP) using a set of TLVs that are carried by the LSP Ping protocol.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliation`dnamemE. Bellagamba¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamemP. Skoldstrom¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drakeddatej’ªhfilenamegrfc7759eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129582hkeywordsÑhLSP-PINGdMPLSgMPLS-TPcOAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV≈;ÜgrankingOcrfcd7759irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexùConfiguration of Proactive Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Functions for MPLS-Based Transport Networks Using Label Switched Path (LSP) PingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document defines a capability-based extension to the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) that allows time-triggered configuration and management operations.  This extension allows NETCONF clients to invoke configuration updates according to scheduled times and allows NETCONF servers to attach timestamps to the data they send to NETCONF clients.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhTechniondnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationhTechniondnamehY. Mosesddatej’∫hfilenamegrfc7758eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129581hkeywordsÑgNETCONFrnetwork managementdtimeuclock synchronizationkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateV±_grankingNcrfcd7758irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTime Capability in NETCONFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document extends the Stateless IP/ICMP Translation Algorithm (SIIT) with an Explicit Address Mapping (EAM) algorithm and formally updates RFC 6145.  The EAM algorithm facilitates stateless IP/ICMP translation between arbitrary (non-IPv4-translatable) IPv6 endpoints and IPv4.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnRedpill LinprodnamekT. Anderson¢kaffiliationjNIC MexicodnameoA. Leiva Popperddatej’∏hfilenamegrfc7757eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129580hkeywordsäjDual StacklSingle StackdIPv4qIPv4 conservationoIPv4 exhaustioniIPv6-onlyiIPv6 onlyoIPv6 transitionxIPv6 transition technologydXLATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV≈;ÖgrankingMcrfcd7757irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumbergetotaletitlex;Explicit Address Mappings for Stateless IP/ICMP TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document describes an extension of the Stateless IP/ICMP Translation for IPv6 Internet Data Center Environments (SIIT-DC) architecture, which allows applications, protocols, or nodes that are incompatible with IPv6 and/or Network Address Translation to operate correctly with SIIT-DC. This is accomplished by introducing a new component called an SIIT-DC Edge Relay, which reverses the translations made by an SIIT-DC Border Relay. The application and/or node is thus provided with seemingly native IPv4 connectivity that provides end-to-end address transparency.

The reader is expected to be familiar with the SIIT-DC architecture described in RFC 7755.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnRedpill LinprodnamekT. Anderson¢kaffiliationxS.J.M. Steffann ConsultancydnamekS. Steffannddatej’∑hfilenamegrfc7756eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129579hkeywordsçkData CentrekData CenterjDual StacklSingle StackcIDCdIPv4qIPv4 conservationoIPv4 exhaustioniIPv6-onlyiIPv6 onlyoIPv6 transitionxIPv6 transition technologydXLATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV≈;ÑgrankingLcrfcd7756irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiStateless IP/ICMP Translation for IPv6 Internet Data Center Environments (SIIT-DC): Dual Translation ModedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚This document describes the use of the Stateless IP/ICMP Translation Algorithm (SIIT) in an IPv6 Internet Data Center (IDC). In this deployment model, traffic from legacy IPv4-only clients on the Internet is translated to IPv6 upon reaching the IDC operator's network infrastructure. From that point on, it may be treated the same as traffic from native IPv6 end users. The IPv6 endpoints may be numbered using arbitrary (non-IPv4-translatable) IPv6 addresses. This facilitates a single-stack IPv6-only network infrastructure, as well as efficient utilization of public IPv4 addresses.

The primary audience is IDC operators who are deploying IPv6, running out of available IPv4 addresses, and/or feeling that dual stack causes undesirable operational complexity.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnRedpill LinprodnamekT. Andersonddatej’∏hfilenamegrfc7755eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129578hkeywordsçkData CentrekData CenterjDual StacklSingle StackcIDCdIPv4qIPv4 conservationoIPv4 exhaustioniIPv6-onlyiIPv6 onlyoIPv6 transitionxIPv6 transition technologydXLATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV≈;ÇgrankingKcrfcd7755irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHSIIT-DC: Stateless IP/ICMP Translation for IPv6 Data Center EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty9The Internet is structured to be an open communications medium.  This openness is one of the key underpinnings of Internet innovation, but it can also allow communications that may be viewed as undesirable by certain parties.  Thus, as the Internet has grown, so have mechanisms to limit the extent and impact of abusive or objectionable communications.  Recently, there has been an increasing emphasis on "blocking" and "filtering", the active prevention of such communications.  This document examines several technical approaches to Internet blocking and filtering in terms of their alignment with the overall Internet architecture.  When it is possible to do so, the approach to blocking and filtering that is most coherent with the Internet architecture is to inform endpoints about potentially undesirable services, so that the communicants can avoid engaging in abusive or objectionable communications.  We observe that certain filtering and blocking approaches can cause unintended consequences to third parties, and we discuss the limits of efficacy of various approaches.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Cooper¢kaffiliation`dnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliation`dnamekE. Nordmarkddatej’∫hfilenamegrfc7754eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129577hkeywordsÜhFirewallfFiltervDeep Packet InspectionsDomain Name SeizurejWeb PortaliWeb ProxykobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateVÿîBgrankingJcrfcd7754irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexDTechnical Considerations for Internet Service Blocking and FilteringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"In some use cases, e.g., Lightweight 4over6, the client may require not just one port, but a port set.  This document defines an extension to the Port Control Protocol (PCP) that allows clients to manipulate a set of ports as a whole.  This is accomplished using a new MAP option: PORT_SET.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegC. Zhou¢kaffiliationpPhilips LightingdnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationsJive CommunicationsdnamelS. Perreaultddatej’∏hfilenamegrfc7753eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129576hkeywordsãwIPv4 service continuityuIPv4 address shortagecA+PfAplusPqaddress plus portcMAPjPort rangeqPort Range RoutereMAP-Epport set mappingiport bulkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV≥ÁÆgrankingIcrfcd7753irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Port Control Protocol (PCP) Extension for Port-Set AllocationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKIn a number of environments, a component external to a network is called upon to perform computations based on the network topology and current state of the connections within the network, including Traffic Engineering (TE) information. This is information typically distributed by IGP routing protocols within the network.

This document describes a mechanism by which link-state and TE information can be collected from networks and shared with external components using the BGP routing protocol. This is achieved using a new BGP Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) encoding format. The mechanism is applicable to physical and virtual IGP links. The mechanism described is subject to policy control.

Applications of this technique include Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) servers and Path Computation Elements (PCEs).fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Medved¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliation`dnamefS. Rayddatej’∂hfilenamegrfc7752eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129575hkeywordsàcBGPkNorth-BoundcAPIjLink-StatehTopologyjControllerjMulti-AreahMulti-ASkobsoletedByÅd9552epages0opublicationDateVÈÂCgrankingHcrfcd7752irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYNorth-Bound Distribution of Link-State and Traffic Engineering (TE) Information Using BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9029∂habstractydThis document specifies a Kerberos authorization data container that supersedes AD-KDC-ISSUED.  It allows for multiple Message Authentication Codes (MACs) or signatures to authenticate the contained authorization data elements.  The multiple MACs are needed to mitigate shortcomings in the existing AD-KDC-ISSUED container.  This document updates RFC 4120.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamehS. Sorce¢kaffiliationcMITdnameeT. Yuddatej’Öhfilenamegrfc7751eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129574hkeywordsÅhKerberoskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateVÿò¿grankingGcrfcd7751irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcKerberos Authorization Data Container Authenticated by Multiple Message Authentication Codes (MACs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxËThis document describes an optional extension for Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP) allowing the monitoring of the Differentiated Service Code Point and Explicit Congestion Notification fields with the TWAMP-Test protocol.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Hedin¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamenS. Baillargeonddatej’∫hfilenamegrfc7750eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129573hkeywordsÉdIPPMeTWAMPqType-P DescriptorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV±_grankingFcrfcd7750irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑDifferentiated Service Code Point and Explicit Congestion Notification Monitoring in the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document defines the "xml2rfc" version 2 vocabulary: an XML-based language used for writing RFCs and Internet-Drafts.

Version 2 represents the state of the vocabulary (as implemented by several tools and as used by the RFC Editor) around 2014.

This document obsoletes RFC 2629.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’∞hfilenamegrfc7749eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129572hkeywordsÖcXMLdIETFcRFCnInternet-DraftjVocabularykobsoletedByÅd7991epagesLopublicationDateV±_grankingEcrfcd7749irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex"The "xml2rfc" Version 2 VocabularydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty]This memo specifies two elliptic curves over prime fields that offer a high level of practical security in cryptographic applications, including Transport Layer Security (TLS).  These curves are intended to operate at the ~128-bit and ~224-bit security level, respectively, and are generated deterministically based on a list of required properties.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejA. Langley¢kaffiliationxRambus Cryptography ResearchdnamejM. Hamburg¢kaffiliationesn3rddnameiS. Turnerddatej’∂hfilenamegrfc7748eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-129571hkeywordsÉnelliptic curvelcryptographycecckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¢…UgrankingDcrfcd7748irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexElliptic Curves for SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫BGP is widely deployed and used by several service providers as the default inter-AS (Autonomous System) routing protocol.  It is of utmost importance to ensure that when a BGP peer or a downstream link of a BGP peer fails, the alternate paths are rapidly used and routes via these alternate paths are installed.  This document provides the basic BGP benchmarking methodology using existing BGP convergence terminology as defined in RFC 4098.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejR. Papneja¢kaffiliationoSkyport SystemsdnameiB. Parise¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationdIXIAdnamefD. Lee¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemI. Varlashkinddatej’âhfilenamegrfc7747eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-129570hkeywordsÅdBMWGkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateWÈ3grankingCcrfcd7747irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIBasic BGP Convergence Benchmarking Methodology for Data-Plane ConvergencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty When certain RSVP-TE optimizations are implemented, ingress Label Switching Router (LSRs) can receive RSVP RESV messages before forwarding state has been installed on all downstream nodes. According to the RSVP-TE specification, the ingress LSR can forward traffic through a Label Switched Path (LSP) as soon as it receives a RESV message. However, if the ingress LSR forwards traffic through the LSP before forwarding state has been installed on all downstream nodes, traffic can be lost.

This document describes LSP Self-ping. When an ingress LSR receives an RESV message, it can invoke LSP Self-ping procedures to ensure that forwarding state has been installed on all downstream nodes.

LSP Self-ping is a new protocol. It is not an extension of LSP Ping. Although LSP Ping and LSP Self-ping are named similarly, each is designed for a unique purpose. Each protocol listens on its own UDP port and executes its own procedures.

LSP Self-ping is an extremely lightweight mechanism. It does not consume control-plane resources on transit or egress LSRs.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamegM. Conn¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamejD. Pacella¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamekL. Tomotakiddatejœhfilenamegrfc7746eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129569hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV™…EgrankingBcrfcd7746irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Label Switched Path (LSP) Self-PingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty◊This document defines an Extensible Markup Language (XML) schema for reverse DNS management in a tightly controlled Representational State Transfer (REST) environment.  This document describes a schema that has been developed and deployed by ICANN in a "RESTful" system since 2011 and is being used by the registries responsible for reverse DNS (rDNS) delegations underneath IN-ADDR.ARPA and IP6.ARPA through an HTTPS transaction that is mediated by an X.509 certificate.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamelT. Mandersonddatejœhfilenamegrfc7745eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129568hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateV†WÁgrankingAcrfcd7745irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&XML Schemas for Reverse DNS ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*Constrained devices are nodes with limited processing power, storage space, and transmission capacities. In many cases, these devices do not provide user interfaces, and they are often intended to interact without human intervention.

This document includes a collection of representative use cases for authentication and authorization in constrained environments. These use cases aim at identifying authorization problems that arise during the life cycle of a constrained device and are intended to provide a guideline for developing a comprehensive authentication and authorization solution for this class of scenarios.

Where specific details are relevant, it is assumed that the devices use the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) as a communication protocol. However, most conclusions apply generally.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsSICS Swedish ICT ABdnamehL. Seitz¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnameiS. Gerdes¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Selander¢kaffiliationeItrondnamegM. Mani¢kaffiliationpPhilips ResearchdnamehS. Kumarddatej’∂hfilenamegrfc7744eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcacedfullxCace - Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdnamex=Authentication and Authorization for Constrained Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129567hkeywordsÑrInternet of ThingscIoTlSmart ObjecthSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV´È∞granking@crfcd7744irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJUse Cases for Authentication and Authorization in Constrained EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymIn some inter-AS (Autonomous System) and inter-area deployment scenarios for RFC 4379 ("Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping and Traceroute"), a replying Label Switching Router (LSR) may not have the available route to an initiator, and the Echo Reply message sent to the initiator would be discarded, resulting in false negatives or a complete failure of operation of the LSP Ping and Traceroute.  This document describes extensions to the LSP Ping mechanism to enable the replying LSR to have the capability to relay the Echo Response by a set of routable intermediate nodes to the initiator.  This document updates RFC 4379.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamefJ. Luo¢kaffiliation`dnamefL. Jin¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. Swallowddatejœhfilenamegrfc7743eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd