opublicationDateVÙUÅgranking>crfcd7742irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.WebRTC Video Processing and Codec RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx—This memo describes an RTP payload format for the VP8 video codec.  The payload format has wide applicability, as it supports applications from low-bitrate peer-to-peer usage to high-bitrate video conferences.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiP. Westin¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiH. Lundin¢kaffiliationgTwitterdnameiM. Glover¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekF. Galliganddatej’∞hfilenamegrfc7741eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129564hkeywordsÉcRTPbV8dWebMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÙUÄgranking=crfcd7741irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex RTP Payload Format for VP8 VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™RFC 6513 ("Multicast in MPLS/BGP IP VPNs") describes a method to support bidirectional customer multicast flows using a partial mesh of Multipoint-to-Multipoint (MP2MP) tunnels.  This document specifies how a partial mesh of MP2MP tunnels can be simulated using Ingress Replication.  This solution enables a service provider to use Ingress Replication to offer transparent bidirectional multicast service to its VPN customers.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehZ. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekA. Dolganowddatej’Æhfilenamegrfc7740eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129563hkeywordsÑdMVPNsIngress ReplicationtBidirectional C-flowhp-tunnelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¢…Tgranking<crfcd7740irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeSimulating Partial Mesh of Multipoint-to-Multipoint (MP2MP) Provider Tunnels with Ingress ReplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎIPv6 specifies the Fragment Header, which is employed for the fragmentation and reassembly mechanisms.  The Fragment Header contains an "Identification" field that, together with the IPv6 Source Address and the IPv6 Destination Address of a packet, identifies fragments that correspond to the same original datagram, such that they can be reassembled together by the receiving host.  The only requirement for setting the Identification field is that the corresponding value must be different than that employed for any other fragmented datagram sent recently with the same Source Address and Destination Address.  Some implementations use a simple global counter for setting the Identification field, thus leading to predictable Identification values.  This document analyzes the security implications of predictable Identification values, and provides implementation guidance for setting the Identification field of the Fragment Header, such that the aforementioned security implications are mitigated.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. Gontddatej’∏hfilenamegrfc7739eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129562hkeywordsÑfattackmvulnerabilityqDenial of Servicetprotocol identifierskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV±_granking;crfcd7739irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSecurity Implications of Predictable Fragment Identification ValuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxøThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace intended for persistently and uniquely naming resources published by the Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems (CCSDS).fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmViagenie/SANAdnamekM. Blanchet¢kaffiliationmViagenie/SANAdnameoA. Schiltknecht¢kaffiliationxJet Propulsion Laboratory NASAdnameiP. Shamesddatejœhfilenamegrfc7738eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129561hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV†WÂgranking:crfcd7738irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems (CCSDS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„The Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping and Traceroute use the Reply Mode field to signal the method to be used in the MPLS echo reply.  This document updates the procedures for the "Reply via Specified Path" Reply Mode.  The value of this Reply Mode is 5.  The update creates a simple way to indicate that the reverse LSP should be used as the return path.  This document also adds an optional TLV that can carry an ordered list of Reply Mode values.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegM. Chenddatej’µhfilenamegrfc7737eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129560hkeywordsÉdMPLShLSP PingjReply ModekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¢…Sgranking9crfcd7737irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGLabel Switched Path (LSP) Ping and Traceroute Reply Mode SimplificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document defines the standard media type used by the Content Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) protocol suite, including the registration procedure and recommended usage of the required payload- type parameter.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameeK. Maddatej’Øhfilenamegrfc7736eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129559hkeywordsÖdCDNIpCDN InterconnectcCDNpcontent deliveryxcontent delivery networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV{]granking8crfcd7736irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGContent Delivery Network Interconnection (CDNI) Media Type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZSeveral review teams ensure specific types of review are performed on Internet-Drafts as they progress towards becoming RFCs.  The tools used by these teams to assign and track reviews would benefit from tighter integration to the Datatracker.  This document discusses requirements for improving those tools without disrupting current work flows.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejT. Kivinenddatej’¥hfilenamegrfc7735eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129558hkeywordsÅxreview tool requirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVó˛granking7crfcd7735irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTracking Reviews of DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document describes how Ethernet Shortest Path Bridging MAC mode (SPBM) can be combined with Ethernet VPN (EVPN) to interwork with Provider Backbone Bridging Provider Edges (PBB PEs) as described in the PBB-EVPN solution (RFC 7623).  This is achieved via operational isolation of each Ethernet network attached to an EVPN core while supporting full interworking between the different variations of Ethernet networks.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehD. Allan¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationcHPEdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassiddatej’¥hfilenamegrfc7734eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129557hkeywordsÉdSPBMx)Provider Backbone Bridging Provider EdgeshPBB-EVPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¢…Sgranking6crfcd7734irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDSupport for Shortest Path Bridging MAC Mode over Ethernet VPN (EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚The purpose of this document is to provide guidance in the selection and use of protocols from the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) protocol suite to implement the features required for control in building and home environments.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmSigma DesignsdnameiA. Brandt¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamekE. Baccelli¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnameiR. Cragie¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameoP. van der Stokddatej’ßhfilenamegrfc7733eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over