MPL has two modes of operation. One mode uses the Trickle algorithm to manage control-plane and data-plane message transmissions and is applicable for deployments with few multicast sources. The other mode uses classic flooding. By providing both modes and parameterization of the Trickle algorithm, an MPL implementation can be used in a variety of multicast deployments and can trade between dissemination latency and transmission efficiency.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiNest LabsdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationlSilicon LabsdnameiR. Kelseyddatej’£hfilenamegrfc7731eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129554hkeywordsâg6lowpanh802.15.4dIPv6cLLNdROLLlmesh networkgtricklecwsnwwireless sensor networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV∆U)granking3crfcd7731irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Multicast Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (MPL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxµThis document defines a Trust Anchor Locator (TAL) for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI).  This document obsoletes RFC 6490 by adding support for multiple URIs in a TAL.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationgParsonsdnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kentddatej’≤hfilenamegrfc7730eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129553hkeywordsÇdRPKIlBGP SecuritykobsoletedByÅd8630epagesopublicationDateVï≤Ëgranking2crfcd7730irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Trust Anchor LocatordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCDeployment experience has demonstrated the value of using the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) architecture to manage resources other than packet forwarding.  In that spirit, the Forwarding Element Manager (FEM) is modeled by creating a Logical Functional Block (LFB) to represent its functionality.  We refer to this LFB as the Subsidiary Mechanism (SM) LFB.  A Control Element (CE) that controls a Forwarding Element's (FE) resources can also manage its configuration via the SM LFB.  This document introduces the SM LFB class, an LFB class that specifies the configuration parameters of an FE.  The configuration parameters include new LFB class loading and CE associations; they also provide manipulation of debug mechanisms along with a general purpose attribute definition to describe configuration information.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlZTE TX, Inc.dnamemB. Khasnabish¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salimddatej’¥hfilenamegrfc7729eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129552hkeywordsÑfForCEScLFBuSubsidiary ManagementnVirtualizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV{*ègranking1crfcd7729irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Logical Functional Block (LFB) Subsidiary ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty÷With the increased popularity of real-time multimedia applications, it is desirable to provide good control of resource usage, and users also demand more control over communication sessions.  This document describes how a receiver in a multimedia conversation can pause and resume incoming data from a sender by sending real-time feedback messages when using the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) for real- time data transport.  This document extends the Codec Control Message (CCM) RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) feedback package by explicitly allowing and describing specific use of existing CCMs and adding a group of new real-time feedback messages used to pause and resume RTP data streams.  This document updates RFC 5104.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Burman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehA. Akram¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej’Æhfilenamegrfc7728eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129551hkeywordsäcCCMdRTCPhFeedbackiBandwidthfPAUSEDgREFUSEDeTMMBReTMMBNeMixercMCUkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateVµ7granking0crfcd7728irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Stream Pause and ResumedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©The Inter-Chassis Communication Protocol (ICCP) supports an inter-chassis redundancy mechanism that is used to support high network availability.

In this document, Provider Edge (PE) devices in a Redundancy Group (RG) running ICCP are used to offer multihomed connectivity to Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) networks to improve availability of the STP networks. The ICCP TLVs and usage for the ICCP STP application are defined.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefH. Wen¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnameeJ. Huddatejœhfilenamegrfc7727eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129550hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¢…Rgranking/crfcd7727irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) Application of the Inter-Chassis Communication Protocol (ICCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxœThis document clarifies the procedures for establishing, maintaining, and removing multiple, concurrent BFD (Bidirectional Forwarding Detection) sessions for a given <MPLS LSP, FEC> as described in RFC 5884.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekV. Govindan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Rajaraman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirsky¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiS. Aldrinddatej’Æhfilenamegrfc7726eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129549hkeywordsÖgRFC5884dMPLScLSPcBFDhRFC 5884kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV´Ó«granking.crfcd7726irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXClarifying Procedures for Establishing BFD Sessions for MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxíThis document specifies a Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) status code for use when resource access is denied as a consequence of legal demands.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjTextualitydnamegT. Brayddatej’æhfilenamegrfc7725eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129548hkeywordsÅxHypertext Transfer ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV‘’—granking-crfcd7725irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-An HTTP Status Code to Report Legal ObstaclesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv4 (DHCPv4) has been extended with a Leasequery capability that allows a requestor to request information about DHCPv4 bindings (RFC 4388).  That mechanism is limited to queries for individual bindings.  In some situations, individual binding queries may not be efficient, or even possible.  In addition, continuous update of an external requestor with Leasequery data is sometimes desired.  This document expands on the DHCPv4 Leasequery protocol, and allows for active transfer of near real-time DHCPv4 binding information data via TCP.  This document updates RFC 6926, "DHCPv4 Bulk Leasequery".fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationgStaplesdnamejN. Russellddatejœ
hfilenamegrfc7724eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129547hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVtäìgranking,crfcd7724irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexActive DHCPv4 Lease QuerydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThe Port Control Protocol (PCP) anycast addresses enable PCP clients to transmit signaling messages to their closest PCP-aware on-path NAT, firewall, or other middlebox without having to learn the IP address of that middlebox via some external channel.  This document establishes one well-known IPv4 address and one well-known IPv6 address to be used as PCP anycast addresses.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnameiS. Kiesel¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehR. Pennoddatej’≥hfilenamegrfc7723eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129546hkeywordsáuPort Control Protocoloanycast addressxanycast server discoveryx&Port Control Protocol server discoverylport mappingkNAT controlpfirewall controlkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateV¢…Qgranking+crfcd7723irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Port Control Protocol (PCP) Anycast AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis specification describes an extension to the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2) to support multiple routing topologies, while retaining interoperability with OLSRv2 routers that do not implement this extension.

This specification updates RFCs 7188 and 7631 by modifying and extending TLV registries and descriptions.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkBAE SystemsdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatejœ
hfilenamegrfc7722eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129545hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÇ]granking*crfcd7722irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYMulti-Topology Extension for the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2 (OLSRv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document discusses privacy and security considerations for several IPv6 address generation mechanisms, both standardized and non-standardized.  It evaluates how different mechanisms mitigate different threats and the trade-offs that implementors, developers, and users face in choosing different addresses or address generation mechanisms.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Cooper¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatejœhfilenamegrfc7721eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129544hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV‡Ügranking)crfcd7721irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSecurity and Privacy Considerations for IPv6 Address Generation MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx‹The DNS root name service is a critical part of the Internet architecture.  The protocol and deployment requirements for the DNS root name service are defined in this document.  Operational requirements are out of scope.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdietfdfullkietf - IETFdnamedIETFgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmViagenie inc.dnamekM. Blanchet¢kaffiliationfNetnoddnamejL-J. Limanddatejœhfilenamegrfc7720eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129543hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVh∆granking(crfcd7720irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber(etotaletitlex:DNS Root Name Service Protocol and Deployment RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThe DNS is defined in literally dozens of different RFCs.  The terminology used by implementers and developers of DNS protocols, and by operators of DNS systems, has sometimes changed in the decades since the DNS was first defined.  This document gives current definitions for many of the terms used in the DNS in a single document.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationcDyndnamekA. Sullivan¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwaraddatejœhfilenamegrfc7719eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129542hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8499epagesopublicationDateVpµRgranking'crfcd7719irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleoDNS TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This memo describes the registries for OWAMP -- the One-Way Active Measurement Protocol.  The registries allow assignment of Mode bit positions and OWAMP Command numbers.  Per this memo, IANA has established the registries for new features, called the OWAMP-Modes registry and the OWAMP Control Command Number registry.  This memo updates RFC 4656.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatejœhfilenamegrfc7718eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129541hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVtäìgranking&crfcd7718irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Registries for the One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKThe One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) and Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP) security mechanisms require that both the client and server endpoints possess a shared secret.  This document describes the use of keys derived from an IKEv2 security association (SA) as the shared key in OWAMP or TWAMP.  If the shared key can be derived from the IKEv2 SA, OWAMP or TWAMP can support certificate-based key exchange; this would allow for more operational flexibility and efficiency.  The key derivation presented in this document can also facilitate automatic key management.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdEICTdnamenK. Pentikousis¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehE. Zhang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefY. Cuiddatejœ
hfilenamegrfc7717eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129540hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVtäëgranking%crfcd7717irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉIKEv2-Derived Shared Secret Key for the One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) and Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓRFCs 6513, 6514, and others describe protocols and procedures that a Service Provider (SP) may deploy in order to offer Multicast Virtual Private Network (Multicast VPN or MVPN) service to its customers.  Some of these procedures use BGP to distribute VPN-specific multicast routing information across a backbone network.  With a small number of relatively minor modifications, the same BGP procedures can also be used to distribute multicast routing information that is not specific to any VPN.  Multicast that is outside the context of a VPN is known as "Global Table Multicast", or sometimes simply as "Internet multicast".  In this document, we describe the modifications that are needed to use the BGP-MVPN procedures for Global Table Multicast.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehJ. Zhang¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamekL. Giuliano¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenK. Subramanian¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamejD. Pacelladdatej’Øhfilenamegrfc7716eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129539hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVi˛egranking$crfcd7716irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCGlobal Table Multicast with BGP Multicast VPN (BGP-MVPN) ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document describes procedures to support node protection for Point-to-Multipoint and Multipoint-to-Multipoint Label Switched Paths (P2MP and MP2MP LSPs) that have been built by the Multipoint Label Distribution Protocol (mLDP).  In order to protect a node N, the Point of Local Repair (PLR) Label Switching Router (LSR) of N must learn the Merge Point (MPT) LSR(s) of node N such that traffic can be redirected to them in case node N fails.  Redirecting the traffic around the failed node N depends on existing Point-to-Point (P2P) Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  The pre-established LSPs originate from the PLR LSR and terminate on the MPT LSRs while bypassing LSR N.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationqHuawei TechnologydnamegQ. Zhaoddatejœhfilenamegrfc7715eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129538hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVê!granking#crfcd7715irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Multipoint LDP (mLDP) Node ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx⁄This document defines how the AES-GCM Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data family of algorithms can be used to provide confidentiality and data authentication in the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP).fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamegK. IgoeddatejŒ˜hfilenamegrfc7714eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129537hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateVf'.granking"crfcd7714irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRAES-GCM Authenticated Encryption in the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document describes an abstract mechanism by which senders inform the network about the congestion recently encountered by packets in the same flow.  Today, network elements at any layer may signal congestion to the receiver by dropping packets or by Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) markings, and the receiver passes this information back to the sender in transport-layer feedback.  The mechanism described here enables the sender to also relay this congestion information back into the network in-band at the IP layer, such that the total amount of congestion from all elements on the path is revealed to all IP elements along the path, where it could, for example, be used to provide input to traffic management.  This mechanism is called Congestion Exposure, or ConEx.  The companion document, "Congestion Exposure (ConEx) Concepts and Use Cases" (RFC 6789), provides the entry point to the set of ConEx documentation.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnameiM. Mathis¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoeddatej’∞hfilenamegrfc7713eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeconexdfullxconex - Congestion ExposurednamesCongestion Exposuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129536hkeywordsàrQuality of ServicecQoSrCongestion ControliSignalinghProtocolhEncodingeAudithPolicingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÇZgranking!crfcd7713irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJCongestion Exposure (ConEx) Concepts, Abstract Mechanism, and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸This document improves the security of the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) in two ways.  First, it specifies how to establish a strong association between a domain name and an XML stream, using the concept of "prooftypes".  Second, it describes how to securely delegate a service domain name (e.g., example.com) to a target server hostname (e.g., hosting.example.net); this is especially important in multi-tenanted environments where the same target server hosts a large number of domains.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Miller¢kaffiliationd&yetdnameiP. Hanckeddatej’≠hfilenamegrfc7712eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxmppdfullx1xmpp - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129535hkeywordsÜdXMPPx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocolfJabberjfederationjdelegationhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVNaògranking crfcd7712irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWDomain Name Associations (DNA) in the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyExperience has shown that it is difficult to deploy proper PKIX certificates for Transport Layer Security (TLS) in multi-tenanted environments.  As a result, domains hosted in such environments often deploy applications using certificates that identify the hosting service, not the hosted domain.  Such deployments force end users and peer services to accept a certificate with an improper identifier, resulting in degraded security.  This document defines methods that make it easier to deploy certificates for proper server identity checking in non-HTTP application protocols.  Although these methods were developed for use in the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) as a Domain Name Association (DNA) prooftype, they might also be usable in other non-HTTP application protocols.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Miller¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’≠hfilenamegrfc7711eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxmppdfullx1xmpp - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129534hkeywordsÉx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocolfJabberjfederationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVNaïgrankingcrfcd7711irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPKIX over Secure HTTP (POSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃIn many environments offering short-term or temporary Internet access (such as coffee shops), it is common to start new connections in a captive-portal mode. This highly restricts what the customer can do until the customer has authenticated.

This document describes a DHCP option (and a Router Advertisement (RA) extension) to inform clients that they are behind some sort of captive-portal device and that they will need to authenticate to get Internet access. It is not a full solution to address all of the issues that clients may have with captive portals; it is designed to be used in larger solutions. The method of authenticating to and interacting with the captive portal is out of scope for this document.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationjCouldflarednamenO. Gudmundsson¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamekP. Ebersman¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamehS. Shengddatejœhfilenamegrfc7710eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129533hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8910epagesopublicationDateVf'.grankingcrfcd7710irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGCaptive-Portal Identification Using DHCP or Router Advertisements (RAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFEstablishment and control of Label Switch Paths (LSPs) have become mainstream tools of commercial and government network providers. One of the elements of further evolving such networks is scaling their performance in terms of LSP bandwidth and traffic loads, LSP intensity (e.g., rate of LSP creation, deletion, and modification), LSP set up delay, quality-of-service differentiation, and different levels of resilience.

The goal of this document is to present target scaling objectives and the related protocol requirements for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationxApplied Communication SciencesdnameiB. Wilson¢kaffiliationrAT&T Labs ResearchdnamehG. Clapp¢kaffiliationvVerizon CommunicationsdnameiV. Shukladdatej’≤hfilenamegrfc7709eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129532hkeywordsäx)generalized multiprotocol label switchingcOTNxoptical transport networksdWSONcTDMcWDMechurnion-demandjwavelengthkrapid setupkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateVM"¥grankingcrfcd7709irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexERequirements for Very Fast Setup of GMPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿The Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV) protocol specified in RFC 5085 provides a control channel (CC) that is associated with a pseudowire (PW).  This document specifies an additional VCCV control channel type to be used with pseudowires that do not use the PW Control Word and that are carried over an MPLS network.  This new VCCV CC type uses the Generic Associated Channel Label defined in RFC 5586 to distinguish VCCV packets from packets carrying user data.  This new VCCV CC type introduces compatibility with the method of MPLS Label Switched Path Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) identification, particularly in MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) networks (RFC 5921).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryantddatej’≤hfilenamegrfc7708eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpalsdfullx*pals - Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdnamex#Pseudowire And LDP-enabled Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129531hkeywordsÇdVCCVcGALkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateVUfgrankingcrfcd7708irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiUsing a Generic Associated Channel Label as a Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification Channel IndicatordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)IPv6 offers a much larger address space than that of its IPv4 counterpart.  An IPv6 subnet of size /64 can (in theory) accommodate approximately 1.844 * 10^19 hosts, thus resulting in a much lower host density (#hosts/#addresses) than is typical in IPv4 networks, where a site typically has 65,000 or fewer unique addresses.  As a result, it is widely assumed that it would take a tremendous effort to perform address-scanning attacks against IPv6 networks; therefore, IPv6 address-scanning attacks have been considered unfeasible.  This document formally obsoletes RFC 5157, which first discussed this assumption, by providing further analysis on how traditional address-scanning techniques apply to IPv6 networks and exploring some additional techniques that can be employed for IPv6 network reconnaissance.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationdJiscdnamehT. Chownddatejœhfilenamegrfc7707eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129530hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateV‡Ügrankingcrfcd7707irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Network Reconnaissance in IPv6 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySSome DNS recursive resolvers have longer-than-desired round-trip times to the closest DNS root server.  Some DNS recursive resolver operators want to prevent snooping of requests sent to DNS root servers by third parties.  Such resolvers can greatly decrease the round-trip time and prevent observation of requests by running a copy of the full root zone on a loopback address (such as 127.0.0.1).  This document shows how to start and maintain such a copy of the root zone that does not pose a threat to other users of the DNS, at the cost of adding some operational fragility for the operator.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamejP. Hoffmanddatejœ	hfilenamegrfc7706eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129529hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8806epagesopublicationDateVUegrankingcrfcd7706irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADecreasing Access Time to Root Servers by Running One on LoopbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document discusses some existing commonly used BGP mechanisms for Autonomous System Number (ASN) migration that are not formally part of the BGP4 protocol specification.  It is necessary to document these de facto standards to ensure that they are properly supported in future BGP protocol work.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiW. George¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiS. Amanteddatej’≤hfilenamegrfc7705eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129528hkeywordsÜlas-migrationlAS-migrationlAS_migrationlAS migrationcIDRcBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV\˛Jgrankingcrfcd7705irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUAutonomous System Migration Mechanisms and Their Effects on the BGP AS_PATH AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÅThe process of producing today's Internet technologies through a culture of open participation and diverse collaboration has proved strikingly efficient and effective, and it is distinctive among standards organizations.  During the early years of the IETF and its antecedent, participation was almost entirely composed of a small group of well-funded, American, white, male technicians, demonstrating a distinctive and challenging group dynamic, both in management and in personal interactions.  In the case of the IETF, interaction style can often contain singularly aggressive behavior, often including singularly hostile tone and content.  Groups with greater diversity make better decisions.  Obtaining meaningful diversity requires more than generic good will and statements of principle.  Many different behaviors can serve to reduce participant diversity or participation diversity.  This document discusses IETF participation in terms of the nature of diversity and practical issues that can increase or decrease it.  The document represents the authors' assessments and recommendations, following general discussions of the issues in the IETF.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationrPavonis ConsultingdnamehN. Clarkddatejœhfilenamegrfc7704eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129527hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV\˛Igrankingcrfcd7704irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex4An IETF with Much Diversity and Professional ConductdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyiThis document describes the testing result of a network utilizing a Mapping of Address and Port using Translation (MAP-T) double translation solution; it provides an overview of user applications' behavior with a shared IPv4 address.

The MAP-T software is from CERNET Center and the test environment is on the NIC.br network with real and virtualized machines.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamekE. Cordeiro¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Carnier¢kaffiliationfNIC.brdnamekA. Moreirasddatej’¨hfilenamegrfc7703eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129526hkeywordsÅhtemplatekobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateVM"≥grankingcrfcd7703irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPExperience with Testing of Mapping of Address and Port Using Translation (MAP-T)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThis document defines a bidirectional protocol mapping for the exchange of instant messages in the context of a multi-party chat session among users of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and users of the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP).  Specifically, this document defines a mapping between the SIP-based Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) and the XMPP Multi-User Chat (MUC) extension.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationkAG ProjectsdnameiS. Ibarra¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loretoddatej’|hfilenamegrfc7702eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstoxdfullrstox - SIP-TO-XMPPdnamekSIP-TO-XMPPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129525hkeywordsâiText ChatiGroupchatqInstant MessagingxSession Initiation ProtocolcSIPxMessage Sessions Relay ProtocoldMSRPx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocoldXMPPkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateVoü¨grankingcrfcd7702irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInterworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): GroupchatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThe Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) defines a mechanism for sending instant messages (IMs) within a peer-to-peer session, negotiated using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  This document defines the necessary tools for establishing multi-party chat sessions, or chat rooms, using MSRP.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Niemi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemG. Sandbakkenddatej’&hfilenamegrfc7701eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129524hkeywordsÜimessagingpmessage sessionskmulti-partydchatdMSRPfSIMPLEkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateVoü¨grankingcrfcd7701irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Multi-party Chat Using the Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx€This document describes methods for handling Unicode strings representing memorable, human-friendly names (called "nicknames", "display names", or "petnames") for people, devices, accounts, websites, and other entities.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’¨hfilenamegrfc7700eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129523hkeywordsáhnicknamecSIPfSIMPLEdXMPPdMSRPdXCONichatroomskobsoletedByÅd8266epagesopublicationDateVoü´grankingcrfcd7700irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Preparation, Enforcement, and Comparison of Internationalized Strings Representing NicknamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„GMPLS supports the description of optical switching by identifying entries in fixed lists of switchable wavelengths (called grids) through the encoding of lambda labels. Work within the ITU-T Study Group 15 has defined a finer-granularity grid, and the facility to flexibly select different widths of spectrum from the grid. This document defines a new GMPLS lambda label format to support this flexi-grid.

This document updates RFCs 3471 and 6205 by introducing a new label format.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationrNanjing UniversitydnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Zhangddatej’∞hfilenamegrfc7699eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129522hkeywordsÇeGMPLSgRSVP-TEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVNU—grankingcrfcd7699irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Generalized Labels for the Flexi-Grid in Lambda Switch Capable (LSC) Label Switching RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌTo allow efficient allocation of optical spectral bandwidth for systems that have high bit-rates, the International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) has extended its Recommendations G.694.1 and G.872 to include a new Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) grid by defining a set of nominal central frequencies, channel spacings, and the concept of the "frequency slot". In such an environment, a data-plane connection is switched based on allocated, variable-sized frequency ranges within the optical spectrum, creating what is known as a flexible grid (flexi-grid).

Given the specific characteristics of flexi-grid optical networks and their associated technology, this document defines a framework and the associated control-plane requirements for the application of the existing GMPLS architecture and control-plane protocols to the control of flexi-grid DWDM networks. The actual extensions to the GMPLS protocols will be defined in companion documents.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamesO. Gonzalez de Dios¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnamekR. Casellas¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationiStairnotednameeX. Fu¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamejI. Hussainddatej’≠hfilenamegrfc7698eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129521hkeywordsÉdDWDMjFlexi-GrideGMPLSkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateVNUœgrankingcrfcd7698irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwFramework and Requirements for GMPLS-Based Control of Flexi-Grid Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects to configure the Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) identifiers for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and the MPLS-based Transport Profile (TP).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamefP. Pan¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationjDell, Inc.dnamemM. Venkatesan¢kaffiliationfRedeemdnamejK. Sampath¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationlVMware, Inc.dnamejS. Boutrosddatej’Øhfilenamegrfc7697eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129520hkeywordsÅsMPLS-OAM-ID-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateVèˆgrankingcrfcd7697irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄMPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Identifiers Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyâMany IETF protocols use cryptographic algorithms to provide confidentiality, integrity, authentication, or digital signature.  Communicating peers must support a common set of cryptographic algorithms for these mechanisms to work properly.  This memo provides guidelines to ensure that protocols have the ability to migrate from one mandatory-to-implement algorithm suite to another over time.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatejœhfilenamegrfc7696eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129519hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVM"≤grankingcrfcd7696irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber…etotaletitlex^Guidelines for Cryptographic Algorithm Agility and Selecting Mandatory-to-Implement AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This document specifies a distributed algorithm for dividing a set of prefixes in a manner that allows for automatic assignment of sub-prefixes that are unique and non-overlapping.  Used in conjunction with a protocol that provides flooding of information among a set of participating nodes, prefix configuration within a network may be automated.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Pfister¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekB. Paterson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej’Æhfilenamegrfc7695eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129518hkeywordsÖkdistributedfprefixgaddressjassignmentghomenetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVTé<grankingcrfcd7695irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Distributed Prefix Assignment AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“In HTTP, content codings allow for payload encodings such as for compression or integrity checks. In particular, the "gzip" content coding is widely used for payload data sent in response messages.

Content codings can be used in request messages as well; however, discoverability is not on par with response messages. This document extends the HTTP "Accept-Encoding" header field for use in responses, to indicate the content codings that are supported in requests.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’¨hfilenamegrfc7694eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129517hkeywordsÇdHTTPpcontent-encodingkobsoletedByÅd9110epagesopublicationDateV9áögrankingcrfcd7694irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDHypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Client-Initiated Content-EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÖThis document describes the cryptographic hash function BLAKE2 and makes the algorithm specification and C source code conveniently available to the Internet community.  BLAKE2 comes in two main flavors: BLAKE2b is optimized for 64-bit platforms and BLAKE2s for smaller architectures.  BLAKE2 can be directly keyed, making it functionally equivalent to a Message Authentication Code (MAC).gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxQueen's University BelfastdnamemM-J. Saarinen¢kaffiliationqKudelski SecuritydnamemJ-P. Aumassonddatej’´hfilenamegrfc7693eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129516hkeywordsÇfBLAKE2rCryptographic HashkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV9áôgrankingcrfcd7693irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexCThe BLAKE2 Cryptographic Hash and Message Authentication Code (MAC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document defines a framework for creating WebSocket extensions that add compression functionality to the WebSocket Protocol.  An extension based on this framework compresses the payload data portion of WebSocket data messages on a per-message basis using parameters negotiated during the opening handshake.  This framework provides a general method for applying a compression algorithm to the contents of WebSocket messages.  Each compression algorithm has to be defined in a document defining the extension by specifying the parameter negotiation and the payload transformation algorithm in detail.  This document also specifies one specific compression extension using the DEFLATE algorithm.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejT. Yoshinoddatej’™hfilenamegrfc7692eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdhybidfullx-hybi - BiDirectional or Server-Initiated HTTPdnamex&BiDirectional or Server-Initiated HTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129515hkeywordsÇgDEFLATEdLZ77kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVf',grankingcrfcd7692irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Compression Extensions for WebSocketdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBCP 101 defines the start and end dates for the terms of IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) members; these terms have proven to be impractical.  This memo updates BCP 101 to direct the IAOC to establish more practical start and end dates for terms of IAOC members.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. BradnerddatejŒ˛hfilenamegrfc7691eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129514hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8711epagesopublicationDateV9Ñ}grankingcrfcd7691irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQUpdating the Term Dates of IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) MembersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document calls attention to the problem of delivering ICMPv6 type 2 "Packet Too Big" (PTB) messages to the intended destination (typically the server) in ECMP load-balanced or anycast network architectures.  It discusses operational mitigations that can be employed to address this class of failures.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfFastlydnameiM. Byerly¢kaffiliationhEvernotednamegM. Hite¢kaffiliationfFastlydnamejJ. Jaeggliddatej’µhfilenamegrfc7690eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129513hkeywordsÉdIPv6eICMP6qICMPv6 type 2 PTBkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateV¢zZgranking
crfcd7690irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWClose Encounters of the ICMP Type 2 Kind (Near Misses with ICMPv6 Packet Too Big (PTB))dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document provides extensions to Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) signaling for control of Wavelength Switched Optical Networks (WSONs).  Such extensions are applicable in WSONs under a number of conditions including: (a) when optional processing, such as regeneration, must be configured to occur at specific nodes along a path, (b) where equipment must be configured to accept an optical signal with specific attributes, or (c) where equipment must be configured to output an optical signal with specific attributes.  This document provides mechanisms to support distributed wavelength assignment with a choice of distributed wavelength assignment algorithms.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationdNICTdnameeS. Xu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemG. Martinelli¢kaffiliationdNICTdnamehH. HaraiddatejŒÏhfilenamegrfc7689eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129512hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVM"±granking	crfcd7689irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Signaling Extensions for Wavelength Switched Optical NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹This document provides Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) routing enhancements to support signal compatibility constraints associated with Wavelength Switched Optical Network (WSON) elements. These routing enhancements are applicable in common optical or hybrid electro-optical networks where not all the optical signals in the network are compatible with all network elements participating in the network.

This compatibility constraint model is applicable to common optical or hybrid electro-optical systems such as optical-electronic-optical (OEO) switches, regenerators, and wavelength converters, since such systems can be limited to processing only certain types of WSON signals.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernsteinddatejœhfilenamegrfc7688eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129511hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVM"Øgrankingcrfcd7688irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlGMPLS OSPF Enhancement for Signal and Network Element Compatibility for Wavelength Switched Optical NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyàThe Strengthening the Internet (STRINT) workshop assembled one hundred participants in London for two days in early 2014 to discuss how the technical community, and in particular the IETF and the W3C, should react to Pervasive Monitoring and more generally how to strengthen the Internet in the face of such attacks. The discussions covered issues of terminology, the role of user interfaces, classes of mitigation, some specific use cases, transition strategies (including opportunistic encryption), and more. The workshop ended with a few high-level recommendations, that it is believed could be implemented and could help strengthen the Internet. This is the report of that workshop.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationcW3CdnamejR. Wenning¢kaffiliationcW3CdnamefB. Bos¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchet¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’≠hfilenamegrfc7687eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129510hkeywordsÜcIABcW3CfSTREWShsecuritytpervasive monitoringfLondonkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateVoü©grankingcrfcd7687irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex<Report from the Strengthening the Internet (STRINT) WorkshopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx=This document registers the ".onion" Special-Use Domain Name.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuThe Tor Project, Inc.dnamelJ. Appelbaum¢kaffiliationhFacebookdnamejA. MuffettddatejŒˇhfilenamegrfc7686eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129509hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV*Ø8grankingcrfcd7686irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$The ".onion" Special-Use Domain NamedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx~This memo describes a Transport Layer Security (TLS) extension that can be used to pad ClientHello messages to a desired size.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjGoogle IncdnamejA. LangleyddatejŒˇhfilenamegrfc7685eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129508hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV'˛ãgrankingcrfcd7685irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A Transport Layer Security (TLS) ClientHello Padding ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊOSPFv2 requires functional extension beyond what can readily be done with the fixed-format Link State Advertisements (LSAs) as described in RFC 2328.  This document defines OSPFv2 Opaque LSAs based on Type-Length-Value (TLV) tuples that can be used to associate additional attributes with prefixes or links.  Depending on the application, these prefixes and links may or may not be advertised in the fixed-format LSAs.  The OSPFv2 Opaque LSAs are optional and fully backward compatible.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationxJive Communications, Inc.dnameiR. Shakir¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Tantsura¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Lindemddatej’®hfilenamegrfc7684eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129507hkeywordsÑhOSPF-LSAxopen shortest path firstxlink state advertisementjOpaque LSAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVUdgrankingcrfcd7684irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*OSPFv2 Prefix/Link Attribute AdvertisementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxàThis specification defines a base solution for Diameter overload control, referred to as Diameter Overload Indication Conveyance (DOIC).fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejS. Donovan¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnameiL. Morandddatej’®hfilenamegrfc7683eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129506hkeywordsÅdDOICkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateV0¯grankingcrfcd7683irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Diameter Overload Indication ConveyancedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8581∂habstractyıThe purpose of this document is to catalog issues that influenced the efficacy of Internet Routing Registries (IRRs) for inter-domain routing policy specification and application in the global routing system over the past two decades.  Additionally, it provides a discussion regarding which of these issues are still problematic in practice, and which are simply artifacts that are no longer applicable but continue to stifle inter-provider policy-based filtering adoption and IRR utility to this day.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiS. Amante¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelE. Osterweil¢kaffiliationsMerit Network, Inc.dnamehL. Blunk¢kaffiliationtSingularity NetworksdnamekD. Mitchellddatej’¨hfilenamegrfc7682eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129505hkeywordsÖvResource CertificationxInternet Routing RegistrycIRRx%Routing Policy Specification LanguagedRPSLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVi˛bgrankingcrfcd7682irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVConsiderations for Internet Routing Registries (IRRs) and Routing Policy ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyªA common format simplifies exchange of secondary school academic transcripts via electronic mail.  Existing standards are applied to prevent unauthorized alteration of transcript content and to deliver transcripts directly and securely from each student to his or her chosen recipients.  By eliminating third-party intervention and surveillance, the defined protocol better protects student privacy and independence than does current practice.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Davinddatej’®hfilenamegrfc7681eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129504hkeywordsÖuInternet Applicationseemailqschool transcriptdMIMEgOpenPGPkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateV*Œqgrankingcrfcd7681irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.Email Exchange of Secondary School TranscriptsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis memo defines a metric for one-way loss of packets across Internet paths.  It builds on notions introduced and discussed in the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) Framework document, RFC 2330; the reader is assumed to be familiar with that document.  This memo makes RFC 2680 obsolete.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiTexas A&MdnamehG. Almes¢kaffiliationdIxiadnamelS. Kalidindi¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamelM. Zekauskas¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’™hfilenamegrfc7680eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129503hkeywordsÉkPerformancekMeasurementxQuality of Service (QoS)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV´ÈÆgranking␀crfcd7680irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberRetotaletitlex7A One-Way Loss Metric for IP Performance Metrics (IPPM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis memo defines a metric for one-way delay of packets across Internet paths.  It builds on notions introduced and discussed in the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) Framework document, RFC 2330; the reader is assumed to be familiar with that document.  This memo makes RFC 2679 obsolete.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiTexas A&MdnamehG. Almes¢kaffiliationdIxiadnamelS. Kalidindi¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamelM. Zekauskas¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’°hfilenamegrfc7679eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129502hkeywordsÉkPerformancekMeasurementxQuality of Service (QoS)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV´È≠grankingˇcrfcd7679irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberQetotaletitlex8A One-Way Delay Metric for IP Performance Metrics (IPPM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty During the transition from IPv4 to IPv6, customer equipment may have to support one of the various transition methods that have been defined for carrying IPv4 packets over IPv6.  This document enumerates the information that needs to be provisioned on a customer edge router to support a list of transition techniques based on tunneling IPv4 in IPv6, with a view to defining reusable components for a reasonable transition path between these techniques.  To the extent that the provisioning is done dynamically, Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) support is needed to provide the information to the network server responsible for passing the information to the customer equipment.  This document specifies Diameter (RFC 6733) Attribute-Value Pairs (AVPs) to be used for that purpose.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegC. Zhou¢kaffiliationtPT Taylor ConsultingdnameiT. Taylor¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadairddatej’©hfilenamegrfc7678eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129501hkeywordsâgDS-LiterLightweight 4over6eMAP-EwIPv4 service continuityoIPv6 deploymenttIPv4 address sharinghDiameteriMulticastnIPv4 over IPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV)WWgranking˛crfcd7678irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjAttribute-Value Pairs for Provisioning Customer Equipment Supporting IPv4-Over-IPv6 Transitional SolutionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document registers the Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) mechanisms SCRAM-SHA-256 and SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS, provides guidance for secure implementation of the original SCRAM-SHA-1-PLUS mechanism, and updates the SCRAM registration procedures of RFC 5802.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. HansenddatejŒ˛hfilenamegrfc7677eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129500hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV7granking˝crfcd7677irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_SCRAM-SHA-256 and SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9266∂habstractyÄGeneric Routing Encapsulation (GRE) can be used to carry any network- layer payload protocol over any network-layer delivery protocol. Currently, GRE procedures are specified for IPv4, used as either the payload or delivery protocol. However, GRE procedures are not specified for IPv6.

This document specifies GRE procedures for IPv6, used as either the payload or delivery protocol.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnanddatej’´hfilenamegrfc7676eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129499hkeywordsÇcGREdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV)WVgranking¸crfcd7676irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4IPv6 Support for Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!To prevent WebRTC applications, such as browsers, from launching attacks by sending traffic to unwilling victims, periodic consent to send needs to be obtained from remote endpoints.

This document describes a consent mechanism using a new Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) usage.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejM. Perumal¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoR. Ravindranath¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej’™hfilenamegrfc7675eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-129498hkeywordsÅfWebRTCkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateV%u¢granking˚crfcd7675irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFSession Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) Usage for Consent FreshnessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxóThis document updates RFC 5575 ("Dissemination of Flow Specification Rules") to clarify the formatting of the BGP Flowspec Redirect Extended Community.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegJ. Haasddatej’™hfilenamegrfc7674eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129497hkeywordsÇcbgphflowspeckobsoletedByÅd8955epagesopublicationDateV≈’granking˙crfcd7674irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Clarification of the Flowspec Redirect Extended CommunitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) specification (RFC 6698) describes how to use TLSA resource records secured by DNSSEC (RFC 4033) to associate a server's connection endpoint with its Transport Layer Security (TLS) certificate (thus enabling administrators of domain names to specify the keys used in that domain's TLS servers).  However, application protocols that use SRV records (RFC 2782) to indirectly name the target server connection endpoints for a service domain name cannot apply the rules from RFC 6698.  Therefore, this document provides guidelines that enable such protocols to locate and use TLSA records.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamehT. Finch¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Miller¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-AndreddatejŒ‚hfilenamegrfc7673eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129496hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÎgranking˘crfcd7673irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUUsing DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) TLSA Records with SRV RecordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis memo describes a downgrade-resistant protocol for SMTP transport security between Message Transfer Agents (MTAs), based on the DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) TLSA DNS record.  Adoption of this protocol enables an incremental transition of the Internet email backbone to one using encrypted and authenticated Transport Layer Security (TLS).fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiTwo SigmadnamekV. Dukhovni¢kaffiliationgParsonsdnamekW. Hardakerddatej’Chfilenamegrfc7672eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129495hkeywordsÉdDANEdTLSAdSMTPkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateVÎgranking¯crfcd7672irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpSMTP Security via Opportunistic DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document clarifies and updates the DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) TLSA specification (RFC 6698), based on subsequent implementation experience.  It also contains guidance for implementers, operators, and protocol developers who want to use DANE records.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiTwo SigmadnamekV. Dukhovni¢kaffiliationgParsonsdnamekW. Hardakerddatej’©hfilenamegrfc7671eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129494hkeywordsÇdDANEdTLSAkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateVÎgranking˜crfcd7671irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`The DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) Protocol: Updates and Operational GuidancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThe Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2) protocol did have support for raw public keys, but it only supported RSA raw public keys.  In constrained environments, it is useful to make use of other types of public keys, such as those based on Elliptic Curve Cryptography.  This document updates RFC 7296, adding support for other types of raw public keys to IKEv2.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’Æhfilenamegrfc7670eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129493hkeywordsÅxInternet Key Exchange Version 2kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateV†W‚grankingˆcrfcd7670irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Generic Raw Public-Key Support for IKEv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx˚This document describes the way that Digital Object Identifiers (DOIs) are assigned to past and future RFCs.  The DOI is a widely used system that assigns unique identifiers to digital documents that can be queried and managed in a consistent fashion.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. LevineddatejŒ˘hfilenamegrfc7669eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129492hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV*Ø8grankingıcrfcd7669irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex,Assigning Digital Object Identifiers to RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBluetooth Smart is the brand name for the Bluetooth low energy feature in the Bluetooth specification defined by the Bluetooth Special Interest Group.  The standard Bluetooth radio has been widely implemented and available in mobile phones, notebook computers, audio headsets, and many other devices.  The low-power version of Bluetooth is a specification that enables the use of this air interface with devices such as sensors, smart meters, appliances, etc.  The low-power variant of Bluetooth has been standardized since revision 4.0 of the Bluetooth specifications, although version 4.1 or newer is required for IPv6.  This document describes how IPv6 is transported over Bluetooth low energy using IPv6 over Low-power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) techniques.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamekJ. Nieminen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejM. Isomaki¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationcARMdnameiZ. Shelby¢kaffiliationx*Universitat Politecnica de Catalunya/i2CATdnamehC. Gomezddatej’©hfilenamegrfc7668eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129491hkeywordsÑtBluetooth Low Energyg6lowpandIPv6iLow powerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV0¯grankingÙcrfcd7668irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!IPv6 over BLUETOOTH(R) Low EnergydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This document discusses point-to-point and multi-endpoint topologies used in environments based on the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP).  In particular, centralized topologies commonly employed in the video conferencing industry are mapped to the RTP terminology.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiS. Wengerddatej’§hfilenamegrfc7667eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129490hkeywordsäiReal-timekMulti-partyeMixereRelaycSFMxSelective Forwarding MiddleboxjTranslatoriMulticastcASMcSSMkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateVM"ÆgrankingÛcrfcd7667irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlenRTP TopologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, this specifies objects for managing virtual machines controlled by a hypervisor (a.k.a.  virtual machine monitor).fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnUniv. of TokyodnamegH. Asai¢kaffiliationkVMware Inc.dnamekM. MacFaden¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationxIIJ Innovation Institute Inc.dnamehK. Shima¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamegT. Tsouddatej’öhfilenamegrfc7666eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129489hkeywordsÖcMIBjHypervisoroVirtual MachinefVM-MIBxIANA-STORAGE-MEDIA-TYPE-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateV*ŒogrankingÚcrfcd7666irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKManagement Information Base for Virtual Machines Controlled by a HypervisordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨This document describes an architecture for the specification, creation, and ongoing maintenance of Service Function Chains (SFCs) in a network.  It includes architectural concepts, principles, and components used in the construction of composite services through deployment of SFCs, with a focus on those to be standardized in the IETF.  This document does not propose solutions, protocols, or extensions to existing protocols.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. PignataroddatejŒ˘hfilenamegrfc7665eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129488hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateV%u†grankingÒcrfcd7665irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Service Function Chaining (SFC) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document specifies a key exchange using discrete logarithm cryptography that is authenticated using a password or passphrase.  It is resistant to active attack, passive attack, and offline dictionary attack.  This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Harkinsddatej’©hfilenamegrfc7664eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-129487hkeywordsÖnelliptic curvedPAKEcAKEqdictionary attackwpassword authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVCÂgrankingcrfcd7664irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlevDragonfly Key ExchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThe Internet Architecture Board (IAB) through its IP Stack Evolution program, the Internet Society, and the Swiss Federal Institute of Technology (ETH) Zurich hosted the Stack Evolution in a Middlebox Internet (SEMI) workshop in Zurich on 26-27 January 2015 to explore the ability to evolve the transport layer in the presence of middlebox- and interface-related ossification of the stack.  The goal of the workshop was to produce architectural and engineering guidance on future work to break the logjam, focusing on incrementally deployable approaches with clear incentives to deployment both on the endpoints (in new transport layers and applications) as well as on middleboxes (run by network operators).  This document summarizes the contributions to the workshop and provides an overview of the discussion at the workshop, as well as the outcomes and next steps identified by the workshop.  The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamemM. Kuehlewindddatej’®hfilenamegrfc7663eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129486hkeywordsÑotransport layercTCPcUDPmencapsulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV&°4grankingÔcrfcd7663irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexNReport from the IAB Workshop on Stack Evolution in a Middlebox Internet (SEMI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis specification defines a method for a protected resource to query an OAuth 2.0 authorization server to determine the active state of an OAuth 2.0 token and to determine meta-information about this token.  OAuth 2.0 deployments can use this method to convey information about the authorization context of the token from the authorization server to the protected resource.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Richerddatej’®hfilenamegrfc7662eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129485hkeywordsáptoken validationvoauth token validationlactive tokenninactive tokenntoken metadataltoken statusrtoken status checkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV%uùgrankingÓcrfcd7662irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOAuth 2.0 Token IntrospectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document provides a mechanism to address issues that arise when TCP is used for traffic that exhibits periods where the sending rate is limited by the application rather than the congestion window. It provides an experimental update to TCP that allows a TCP sender to restart quickly following a rate-limited interval. This method is expected to benefit applications that send rate-limited traffic using TCP while also providing an appropriate response if congestion is experienced.

This document also evaluates the Experimental specification of TCP Congestion Window Validation (CWV) defined in RFC 2861 and concludes that RFC 2861 sought to address important issues but failed to deliver a widely used solution. This document therefore reclassifies the status of RFC 2861 from Experimental to Historic. This document obsoletes RFC 2861.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnameoA. Sathiaseelan¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnameiR. Secchiddatej’•hfilenamegrfc7661eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129484hkeywordsÇcCWVcTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV0˜grankingÌcrfcd7661irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Updating TCP to Support Rate-Limited TrafficdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This document defines optional Diameter attributes that can be used to help manage networks that use Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) or Diameter traffic filters. These new attributes allow for improved data traffic identification, support of ECN, and minimal Diameter filter administration.

RFC 5777 defines a Filter-Rule Attribute Value Pair (AVP) that accommodates extensions for classification, conditions, and actions. It, however, does not support traffic identification for packets using Explicit Congestion Notification as defined in RFC 3168 and does not provide specific actions when the flow(s) described by the Filter-Rule are congested.

Further, a Filter-Rule can describe multiple flows but not the exact number of flows. Flow count and other associated data (e.g., packets) are not captured by accounting applications, leaving administrators without useful information regarding the effectiveness or appropriateness of the filter definition.

The optional attributes defined in this document are forward and backwards compatible with RFC 5777.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfSprintdnamehL. Bertz¢kaffiliationfSprintdnamejS. Manning¢kaffiliation`dnamelB. HirschmanddatejŒ˙hfilenamegrfc7660eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129483hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateV≈’grankingÏcrfcd7660irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Diameter Congestion and Filter AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for devices implementing the Network Address Translator (NAT) function.  The new MIB module defined in this document, NATV2-MIB, is intended to replace module NAT-MIB (RFC 4008).  NATV2-MIB is not backwards compatible with NAT-MIB, for reasons given in the text of this document.  A companion document deprecates all objects in NAT-MIB.  NATV2-MIB can be used for the monitoring of NAT instances on a device capable of NAT function.  Compliance levels are defined for three application scenarios: basic NAT, pooled NAT, and carrier-grade NAT (CGN).fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsJive CommunicationsdnamelS. Perreault¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationtPT Taylor ConsultingdnameiT. Taylorddatej’¢hfilenamegrfc7659eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129482hkeywordsàcMIBxmanagement information baseiNATV2-MIBgNAT-MIBibasic natjpooled natqcarrier-grade natcCGNkobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDateV0ˆgrankingÎcrfcd7659irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEDefinitions of Managed Objects for Network Address Translators (NATs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„This memo deprecates MIB module NAT-MIB, a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) previously defined in RFC 4008 for devices implementing Network Address Translator (NAT) function. A companion document defines a new version, NATV2-MIB, which responds to deficiencies found in module NAT-MIB and adds new capabilities.

This document obsoletes RFC 4008. All MIB objects specified in RFC 4008 are included in this version unchanged with only the STATUS changed to deprecated.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsJive CommunicationsdnamelS. Perreault¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationtPT Taylor ConsultingdnameiT. Taylorddatej’£hfilenamegrfc7658eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129481hkeywordsÇiNATV2-MIBxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDateV0ıgrankingÍcrfcd7658irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYDeprecation of MIB Module NAT-MIB: Managed Objects for Network Address Translators (NATs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This memo describes the interaction between Differentiated Services (Diffserv) network quality-of-service (QoS) functionality and real- time network communication, including communication based on the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP).  Diffserv is based on network nodes applying different forwarding treatments to packets whose IP headers are marked with different Diffserv Codepoints (DSCPs).  WebRTC applications, as well as some conferencing applications, have begun using the Session Description Protocol (SDP) bundle negotiation mechanism to send multiple traffic streams with different QoS requirements using the same network 5-tuple.  The results of using multiple DSCPs to obtain different QoS treatments within a single network 5-tuple have transport protocol interactions, particularly with congestion control functionality (e.g., reordering).  In addition, DSCP markings may be changed or removed between the traffic source and destination.  This memo covers the implications of these Diffserv aspects for real-time network communication, including WebRTC.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Jonesddatej’|hfilenamegrfc7657eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddartdfullx/dart - DiffServ Applied to Real-time Transportsdnamex(DiffServ Applied to Real-time Transportsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129480hkeywordsÑhDiffservdDSCPcRAIcRTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVM"≠grankingÈcrfcd7657irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Differentiated Services (Diffserv) and Real-Time CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@The terminology about, and associations among, Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) sources can be complex and somewhat opaque.  This document describes a number of existing and proposed properties and relationships among RTP sources and defines common terminology for discussing protocol entities and their relationships.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationcAVAdnamehK. Gross¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Nandakumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Burmanddatej’ßhfilenamegrfc7656eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129479hkeywordsÑhTaxonomykTerminologycRTPhGroupingkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateVM"¨grankingËcrfcd7656irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA Taxonomy of Semantics and Mechanisms for Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) SourcesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis document specifies the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for ITU-T Recommendation G.711.0.  ITU-T Rec.  G.711.0 defines a lossless and stateless compression for G.711 packet payloads typically used in IP networks.  This document also defines a storage mode format for G.711.0 and a media type registration for the G.711.0 RTP payload format.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Ramalho¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiN. Harada¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejM. Perumal¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegL. Miaoddatej’¶hfilenamegrfc7655eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129478hkeywordsÖgG.711.0eG.711hG.711ZIPxLossless G.711 Compressionx G.711 Data Compression AlgorithmkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateV7!grankingÁcrfcd7655irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Payload Format for G.711.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}Modern forwarding devices attempt to minimize any control- and data-plane disruptions while performing planned software changes by implementing a technique commonly known as In-Service Software Upgrade (ISSU).  This document specifies a set of common methodologies and procedures designed to characterize the overall behavior of a Device Under Test (DUT), subject to an ISSU event.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVSS MonitoringdnamehS. Banks¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekF. Calabria¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejG. Czirjak¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. MachatddatejŒ˙hfilenamegrfc7654eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-129477hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV0ÙgrankingÊcrfcd7654irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Benchmarking Methodology for In-Service Software Upgrade (ISSU)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) has been extended with a Leasequery capability that allows a requestor to request information about DHCPv6 bindings.  That mechanism is limited to queries for DHCPv6 binding data updates prior to the time the DHCPv6 server receives the Leasequery request.  Continuous update of an external requestor with Leasequery data is sometimes desired.  This document expands on the DHCPv6 Leasequery protocol and allows for active transfer of real-time DHCPv6 binding information data via TCP.  This document also updates DHCPv6 Bulk Leasequery (RFC 5460) by adding new options.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenD. Raghuvanshi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejD. Kukretyddatej’ßhfilenamegrfc7653eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129476hkeywordsÑdDHCPdIPv6pACTIVELEASEQUERYfDHCPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÎgrankingÂcrfcd7653irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDHCPv6 Active LeasequerydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃAn IPv4 or IPv6 host can use the Port Control Protocol (PCP) to flexibly manage the IP address-mapping and port-mapping information on Network Address Translators (NATs) or firewalls to facilitate communication with remote hosts. However, the uncontrolled generation or deletion of IP address mappings on such network devices may cause security risks and should be avoided. In some cases, the client may need to prove that it is authorized to modify, create, or delete PCP mappings. This document describes an in-band authentication mechanism for PCP that can be used in those cases. The Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) is used to perform authentication between PCP devices.

This document updates RFC 6887.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnameiM. Cullen¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Zhang¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. ReddyddatejŒıhfilenamegrfc7652eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129475hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateVs/granking‰crfcd7652irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Port Control Protocol (PCP) Authentication MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¢This document defines two new configuration attributes for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2).  These attributes can be used for carrying the IPv4 address and IPv6 address of the Proxy-Call Session Control Function (P-CSCF).  When an IPsec gateway delivers these attributes to an IPsec client, the IPsec client can obtain the IPv4 and/or IPv6 address of the P-CSCF server located in the 3GPP network.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemA. Dodd-Noble¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamekF. Baboescu¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegB. Weisddatej’•hfilenamegrfc7651eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129474hkeywordsÑfP-CSCFwP-CSCF Option for IKEv2x#Proxy-Call Session Control FunctiontIMS Option for IKEv2kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateU˘⁄granking„crfcd7651irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexc3GPP IP Multimedia Subsystems (IMS) Option for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document defines a Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Usage for REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD).  The CoAP Usage provides the functionality to federate Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) in a peer-to-peer fashion.  The CoAP Usage for RELOAD allows CoAP nodes to store resources in a RELOAD peer-to-peer overlay, provides a lookup service, and enables the use of RELOAD overlay as a cache for sensor data.  This functionality is implemented in the RELOAD overlay itself, without the use of centralized servers.  The RELOAD AppAttach method is used to establish a direct connection between nodes through which CoAP messages are exchanged.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Jimenez¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GranadadnamemJ. Lopez-Vega¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Maenpaa¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej’ßhfilenamegrfc7650eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129473hkeywordsÇdCoAPfRELOADkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV%™granking‚crfcd7650irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\A Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) Usage for REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty~During IETF meetings, individual volunteers often help sessions run more smoothly by relaying information back and forth between the physical meeting room and an associated textual chatroom.  Such volunteers are commonly called "Jabber scribes".  This document summarizes experience with the Jabber scribe role and provides some suggestions for fulfilling the role at IETF meetings.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamegD. Yorkddatej’•hfilenamegrfc7649eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129472hkeywordsÇmJabber ScribemIETF MeetingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV%©granking·crfcd7649irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex'The Jabber Scribe Role at IETF MeetingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a new Port Control Protocol (PCP) functional element: the PCP proxy.  The PCP proxy relays PCP requests received from PCP clients to upstream PCP server(s).  A typical deployment usage of this function is to help establish successful PCP communications for PCP clients that cannot be configured with the address of a PCP server located more than one hop away.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsJive CommunicationsdnamelS. Perreault¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationeApplednamekS. Cheshireddatej’•hfilenamegrfc7648eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129471hkeywordsïcNAThfirewallcCGNdAFTReNAT64oport forwardingipinholinglport mappingsexternal IP addresstdiscover port numberxrunning a server behind NATkNAT controlmNAT cascadinggDS-Litesincoming connectionxcontrol outbound connectionhreferralpaddress referralkALG offloadjPCP clientjPCP serverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVPhgranking‡crfcd7648irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Port Control Protocol (PCP) Proxy FunctiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThe SIP REFER method relies on the SIP-Specific Event Notification framework.  That framework was revised by RFC 6665.  This document highlights the implications of the requirement changes in RFC 6665, and updates the definition of the REFER method described in RFC 3515 to clarify and disambiguate the impact of those changes.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejA.B. RoachddatejŒÚhfilenamegrfc7647eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-129470hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¿grankingﬂcrfcd7647irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Clarifications for the Use of REFER with RFC 6665dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜDNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) is now entering widespread deployment.  However, domain signing tools and processes are not yet as mature and reliable as those for non-DNSSEC-related domain administration tools and processes.  This document defines Negative Trust Anchors (NTAs), which can be used to mitigate DNSSEC validation failures by disabling DNSSEC validation at specified domains.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamekP. Ebersman¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationgNominetdnamelC. Griffiths¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamehR. Weberddatej’°hfilenamegrfc7646eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129469hkeywordsÜcNTAcISPxInternet Service ProvidercDNSfDNSSECvNegative Trust AnchorskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV%®grankingﬁcrfcd7646irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Definition and Use of DNSSEC Negative Trust AnchorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$This document analyzes the current state of the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol according to the requirements set forth in "Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design Guidelines" (RFC 6518) for both manual and automated key management protocols.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmEricsson Inc.dnamekU. Chunduri¢kaffiliationmEricsson Inc.dnamegA. Tian¢kaffiliationmEricsson Inc.dnameeW. LuddatejŒÓhfilenamegrfc7645eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129468hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV¿granking›crfcd7645irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQThe Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocol (KARP) IS-IS Security AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) specification is an HTTP-based protocol that makes managing identities in multi-domain scenarios easier to support via a standardized service.  Examples include, but are not limited to, enterprise-to-cloud service providers and inter-cloud scenarios.  The specification suite seeks to build upon experience with existing schemas and deployments, placing specific emphasis on simplicity of development and integration, while applying existing authentication, authorization, and privacy models.  SCIM's intent is to reduce the cost and complexity of user management operations by providing a common user schema, an extension model, and a service protocol defined by this document.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegP. Hunt¢kaffiliationiSailPointdnamejK. Grizzle¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiM. Ansari¢kaffiliationpNexus TechnologydnamemE. Wahlstroem¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamelC. Mortimoreddatej’ühfilenamegrfc7644eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdscimdfullx2scim - System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdnamex+System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129467hkeywordsÅdSCIMkobsoletedByÄepagesYopublicationDateV⁄£granking‹crfcd7644irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5System for Cross-domain Identity Management: ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9865d9967∂habstracty@The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) specifications are designed to make identity management in cloud-based applications and services easier. The specification suite builds upon experience with existing schemas and deployments, placing specific emphasis on simplicity of development and integration, while applying existing authentication, authorization, and privacy models. Its intent is to reduce the cost and complexity of user management operations by providing a common user schema and extension model as well as binding documents to provide patterns for exchanging this schema using HTTP.

This document provides a platform-neutral schema and extension model for representing users and groups and other resource types in JSON format. This schema is intended for exchange and use with cloud service providers.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegP. Hunt¢kaffiliationiSailPointdnamejK. Grizzle¢kaffiliationpNexus TechnologydnamemE. Wahlstroem¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamelC. Mortimoreddatej’ühfilenamegrfc7643eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdscimdfullx2scim - System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdnamex+System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129466hkeywordsÑhIdentitylProvisioningdUsereGroupkobsoletedByÄepageshopublicationDateV⁄£granking€crfcd7643irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8System for Cross-domain Identity Management: Core SchemadtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9865d9967∂habstractx·This document provides definitions and an overview of the System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM).  It lays out the system's concepts, models, and flows, and it includes user scenarios, use cases, and requirements.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmAlibaba GroupdnameeK. LI¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegP. Hunt¢kaffiliationmZTE (TX) Inc.dnamemB. Khasnabish¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejA. Nadalin¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamejZ. Zeltsanddatej’úhfilenamegrfc7642eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdscimdfullx2scim - System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdnamex+System for Cross-domain Identity Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129465hkeywordsÇrSIM user scenariosnSCIM use caseskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV⁄¢granking⁄crfcd7642irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^System for Cross-domain Identity Management: Definitions, Overview, Concepts, and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThe Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) is a RESTful application protocol for constrained nodes and networks.  The state of a resource on a CoAP server can change over time.  This document specifies a simple protocol extension for CoAP that enables CoAP clients to "observe" resources, i.e., to retrieve a representation of a resource and keep this representation updated by the server over a period of time.  The protocol follows a best-effort approach for sending new representations to clients and provides eventual consistency between the state observed by each client and the actual resource state at the server.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnameiK. Hartkeddatej’ühfilenamegrfc7641eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129464hkeywordsÑmSmart ObjectsrInternet of ThingscIoTdRESTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVs/grankingŸcrfcd7641irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBObserving Resources in the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8323∂habstractyáThis framework describes a practical methodology for benchmarking the traffic management capabilities of networking devices (i.e., policing, shaping, etc.).  The goals are to provide a repeatable test method that objectively compares performance of the device's traffic management capabilities and to specify the means to benchmark traffic management with representative application traffic.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdJDSUdnamenB. Constantine¢kaffiliationiDell Inc.dnamekR. KrishnanddatejŒhfilenamegrfc7640eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-129463hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateV%ßgrankingÿcrfcd7640irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTraffic Management BenchmarkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx°This specification allows HTTP CONNECT requests to indicate what protocol is intended to be used within the tunnel once established, using the ALPN header field.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeUnifydnameiA. Hutton¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Uberti¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej’§hfilenamegrfc7639eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129462hkeywordsÉlHTTP CONNECThFirewalljHTTP proxykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ|}granking◊crfcd7639irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe ALPN HTTP Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This specification defines a method for computing a hash value over a JSON Web Key (JWK).  It defines which fields in a JWK are used in the hash computation, the method of creating a canonical form for those fields, and how to convert the resulting Unicode string into a byte sequence to be hashed.  The resulting hash value can be used for identifying or selecting the key represented by the JWK that is the subject of the thumbprint.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationxNomura Research InstitutednamekN. Sakimuraddatej’ühfilenamegrfc7638eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129461hkeywordsáxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONlJSON Web KeycJWKlThumbprintOBkFingerprintfDigestkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÔ)Œgranking÷crfcd7638irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSON Web Key (JWK) ThumbprintdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes the usage of the Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) header for Network Virtualization (NVGRE) in multi-tenant data centers.  Network Virtualization decouples virtual networks and addresses from physical network infrastructure, providing isolation and concurrency between multiple virtual networks on the same physical network infrastructure.  This document also introduces a Network Virtualization framework to illustrate the use cases, but the focus is on specifying the data-plane aspect of NVGRE.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegP. Garg¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegY. WangddatejŒhfilenamegrfc7637eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129460hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV%ßgranking’crfcd7637irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexANVGRE: Network Virtualization Using Generic Routing EncapsulationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'OAuth 2.0 public clients utilizing the Authorization Code Grant are susceptible to the authorization code interception attack.  This specification describes the attack as well as a technique to mitigate against the threat through the use of Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE, pronounced "pixy").fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNomura Research InstitutednamekN. Sakimura¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejN. Agarwalddatej’°hfilenamegrfc7636eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129459hkeywordsèlsmart phonesdappsdXARAmauthorizationmcustom schemefintentqman-in-the-middlemeavesdroppingouser agent swapdspopcpopfopenidgconnectdpkceepixiekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU˘⁄granking‘crfcd7636irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Proof Key for Code Exchange by OAuth Public ClientsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document proposes the use of OAuth 2.0 to obtain and validate ephemeral tokens that can be used for Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) authentication.  The usage of ephemeral tokens ensures that access to a STUN server can be controlled even if the tokens are compromised.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Patil¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoR. Ravindranath¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiJ. Ubertiddatej’ûhfilenamegrfc7635eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtramdfullx"tram - TURN Revised and ModernizeddnamexTURN Revised and Modernizeddtypebwgbidjdoc-129458hkeywordsÖiOAuth 2.0dSTUNdTURNfWebRTCx Authentication and AuthorizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ||granking”crfcd7635irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRSession Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) Extension for Third-Party AuthorizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÃThis document describes the use of the ChaCha20 stream cipher along with the Poly1305 authenticator, combined into an AEAD algorithm for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) and for IPsec.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nirddatej’°hfilenamegrfc7634eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129457hkeywordsÜcIKEeIPsecdAEADfChaChahChaCha20eSalsakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU÷VKgranking“crfcd7634irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWChaCha20, Poly1305, and Their Use in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE) and IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe purpose of the TLS feature extension is to prevent downgrade attacks that are not otherwise prevented by the TLS protocol.  In particular, the TLS feature extension may be used to mandate support for revocation checking features in the TLS protocol such as Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) stapling.  Informing clients that an OCSP status response will always be stapled permits an immediate failure in the case that the response is not stapled.  This in turn prevents a denial-of-service attack that might otherwise be possible.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqComodo Group Inc.dnameoP. Hallam-Bakerddatej’°hfilenamegrfc7633eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129456hkeywordsÉdPKIXxTransport Layer SecurityxCryptography CertificatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVÎgranking—crfcd7633irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8X.509v3 Transport Layer Security (TLS) Feature ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis memo documents a sampling of use cases for securely aggregating configuration and operational data and evaluating that data to determine an organization's security posture.  From these operational use cases, we can derive common functional capabilities and requirements to guide development of vendor-neutral, interoperable standards for aggregating and evaluating data relevant to security posture.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamemD. Waltermire¢kaffiliationrEffective SoftwarednamemD. Harringtonddatej’§hfilenamegrfc7632eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsacmdfullx4sacm - Security Automation and Continuous Monitoringdnamex-Security Automation and Continuous Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-129455hkeywordsâssecurity automationucontinuous monitoringhendpointrposture assessmenthuse casepasset managementxconfiguration managementxvulnerability managementrcontent managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÔ+Ngranking–crfcd7632irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Endpoint Security Posture Assessment: Enterprise Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis document reorganizes the naming of already-allocated TLV (type- length-value) types and type extensions in the "Mobile Ad hoc NETwork (MANET) Parameters" registries defined by RFC 5444 to use names appropriately. It has no consequences in terms of any protocol implementation.

This document also updates the Expert Review guidelines in RFC 5444, so as to establish a policy for consistent naming of future TLV type and type extension allocations. It makes no other changes to RFC 5444.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatej’ùhfilenamegrfc7631eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129454hkeywordsÖeMANETfpacketgmessagegaddresscTLVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÔ)Õgrankingœcrfcd7631irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQTLV Naming in the Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Generalized Packet/Message FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7722∂habstractxÉThis memo specifies new HMAC-SHA-2 authentication protocols for the User-based Security Model (USM) for SNMPv3 defined in RFC 3414.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSecunet Security NetworksdnameiJ. Merkle¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamejM. Lochterddatej’¢hfilenamegrfc7630eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129453hkeywordsÜgNetworkjManagementdSNMPcUSMdHMACeSHA-2kobsoletedByÅd7860epagesopublicationDateVÎgrankingŒcrfcd7630irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUHMAC-SHA-2 Authentication Protocols in the User-based Security Model (USM) for SNMPv3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis specification defines extensions to the Mobile IP protocol for allowing a mobile node with multiple interfaces to register a care-of address for each of its network interfaces and to simultaneously establish multiple IP tunnels with its home agent.  This essentially allows the mobile node to utilize all the available network interfaces and build a higher aggregated logical pipe with its home agent for its home address traffic.  Furthermore, these extensions also allow the mobile node and the home agent to negotiate IP traffic flow policies for binding individual flows with the registered care-of addresses.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationjCEA / LISTdnamekA. Petrescuddatej’¢hfilenamegrfc7629eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129452hkeywordsÖiMultipathlFlow BindingmHybrid AccessmFlow MobilityjMIPv4-NEMOkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ||grankingÕcrfcd7629irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Flow-Binding Support for Mobile IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄OAuth enables a third-party application to obtain limited access to a protected resource, either on behalf of a resource owner by orchestrating an approval interaction or by allowing the third-party application to obtain access on its own behalf.

This document defines how an application client uses credentials obtained via OAuth over the Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) to access a protected resource at a resource server. Thereby, it enables schemes defined within the OAuth framework for non-HTTP-based application protocols.

Clients typically store the user's long-term credential. This does, however, lead to significant security vulnerabilities, for example, when such a credential leaks. A significant benefit of OAuth for usage in those clients is that the password is replaced by a shared secret with higher entropy, i.e., the token. Tokens typically provide limited access rights and can be managed and revoked separately from the user's long-term password.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehW. Mills¢kaffiliation`dnamelT. Showalter¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. TschofenigddatejŒÈhfilenamegrfc7628eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129451hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÂ06grankingÃcrfcd7628irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMA Set of Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) Mechanisms for OAuthdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThe Transport Layer Security (TLS) master secret is not cryptographically bound to important session parameters such as the server certificate.  Consequently, it is possible for an active attacker to set up two sessions, one with a client and another with a server, such that the master secrets on the two sessions are the same.  Thereafter, any mechanism that relies on the master secret for authentication, including session resumption, becomes vulnerable to a man-in-the-middle attack, where the attacker can simply forward messages back and forth between the client and server.  This specification defines a TLS extension that contextually binds the master secret to a log of the full handshake that computes it, thus preventing such attacks.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxInria Paris-RocquencourtdnamelK. Bhargavan¢kaffiliationxInria Paris-RocquencourtdnamerA. Delignat-Lavaud¢kaffiliationxINRIA Paris-RocquencourtdnamejA. Pironti¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamejA. Langley¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamefM. RayddatejŒÛhfilenamegrfc7627eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129450hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU˘⁄␀grankingÀcrfcd7627irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPTransport Layer Security (TLS) Session Hash and Extended Master Secret ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxΩThis document describes the privacy issues associated with the use of the DNS by Internet users.  It is intended to be an analysis of the present situation and does not prescribe solutions.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAFNICdnamemS. Bortzmeyerddatej’¢hfilenamegrfc7626eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdprivedfullxdprive - DNS PRIVate ExchangednametDNS PRIVate Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-129449hkeywordsÉoConfidentialityvPervasive SurveillancerDomain Name SystemkobsoletedByÅd9076epagesopublicationDateUﬁ|{granking crfcd7626irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNS Privacy ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty∂This document describes an IP/MPLS network that has an infrastructure that can be separated into two or more strata. For the implementation described in this document, the infrastructure has been separated into two strata: one for the "Hard Pipes", called the "Hard Pipe Stratum", and one for the normal IP/MPLS traffic, called the "Normal IP/MPLS Stratum".

This document introduces the concept of a Hard Pipe -- an MPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) or a pseudowire (PW) with a bandwidth that is guaranteed and can neither be exceeded nor infringed upon.

The Hard Pipe stratum does not use statistical multiplexing; for the LSPs and PWs set up within this stratum, the bandwidth is guaranteed end to end.

The document does not specify any new protocol or procedures. It does explain how the MPLS standards implementation has been deployed and operated to meet the requirements from operators that offer traditional Virtual Leased Line (VLL) services.fadNamepDeborah BrungardgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnameiJ. T. Hao¢kaffiliationsBharti Airtel, Ltd.dnamemP. Maheshwari¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehR. Huang¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegM. ChenddatejŒÔhfilenamegrfc7625eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129448hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU‰‰ˇgranking…crfcd7625irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex6Architecture of an IP/MPLS Network with Hardened PipesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÖSince the initial revelations of pervasive surveillance in 2013, several classes of attacks on Internet communications have been discovered.  In this document, we develop a threat model that describes these attacks on Internet confidentiality.  We assume an attacker that is interested in undetected, indiscriminate eavesdropping.  The threat model is based on published, verified attacks.gauthorsá¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliation`dnamekB. Schneier¢kaffiliation`dnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Hardie¢kaffiliation`dnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliation`dnamekD. Borkmannddatej’ûhfilenamegrfc7624eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129447hkeywordsÅmeavesdroppingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU÷VKgranking»crfcd7624irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex[Confidentiality in the Face of Pervasive Surveillance: A Threat Model and Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document discusses how Ethernet Provider Backbone Bridging (PBB) can be combined with Ethernet VPN (EVPN) in order to reduce the number of BGP MAC Advertisement routes by aggregating Customer/Client MAC (C-MAC) addresses via Provider Backbone MAC (B-MAC) address, provide client MAC address mobility using C-MAC aggregation, confine the scope of C-MAC learning to only active flows, offer per-site policies, and avoid C-MAC address flushing on topology changes.  The combined solution is referred to as PBB-EVPN.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehA. Isaac¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemW. HenderickxddatejŒËhfilenamegrfc7623eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129446hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV␀öÉgranking«crfcd7623irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Provider Backbone Bridging Combined with Ethernet VPN (PBB-EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¡This document defines the address format for the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP), including support for code points outside the ASCII range.  This document obsoletes RFC 6122.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’†hfilenamegrfc7622eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdxmppdfullx1xmpp - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129445hkeywordsâx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocoldXMPPfJabberiMessagingqInstant MessaginghPresencetInternationalizationdi18nfPRECISkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÔ)Ãgranking∆crfcd7622irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAExtensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): Address FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9844∂habstracty"Experience since the publication of the most recent SIP Events framework (in July 2012) has shown that there is room for interpretation around the use of Globally Routable User Agent URIs in that specification. This document clarifies the intended behavior.

This document updates RFC 6665.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejA.B. Roachddatej’§hfilenamegrfc7621eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-129444hkeywordsÅxsession initiation protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUŒáÕgranking≈crfcd7621irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoA Clarification on the Use of Globally Routable User Agent URIs (GRUUs) in the SIP Event Notification FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÉThis document describes a set of scenarios in which complications when identifying which policy to apply for a host are encountered. This problem is abstracted as "host identification". Describing these scenarios allows commonalities between scenarios to be identified, which is helpful during the solution design phase.

This document does not include any solution-specific discussions.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejB. Chatras¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationlAkamai, Inc.dnamekB. Williams¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekB. Sarikayaddatej’†hfilenamegrfc7620eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129443hkeywordsÜrIP address sharingwIPv4 service continuityohost identifierxde-multiplexing connectionsrpolicy enforcementpservice deliverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUŒáÃgrankingƒcrfcd7620irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0Scenarios with Host Identification ComplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the NULL Authentication method and the ID_NULL Identification Payload ID Type for Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2).  This allows two IKE peers to establish single-side authenticated or mutual unauthenticated IKE sessions for those use cases where a peer is unwilling or unable to authenticate or identify itself.  This ensures IKEv2 can be used for Opportunistic Security (also known as Opportunistic Encryption) to defend against Pervasive Monitoring attacks without the need to sacrifice anonymity.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslov¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Woutersddatej’ûhfilenamegrfc7619eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129442hkeywordsÜounauthenticatedvopportunistic securitytpervasive monitoringxPeer Authorization DatabasecPADxopportunistic encryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ|zgranking√crfcd7619irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVThe NULL Authentication Method in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This memo describes the dynamic allocation of shared IPv4 addresses to clients using DHCPv4. Address sharing allows a single IPv4 address to be allocated to multiple active clients simultaneously, with each client being differentiated by a unique set of transport- layer source port numbers. The necessary changes to existing DHCPv4 client and server behavior are described, and a new DHCPv4 option for provisioning clients with shared IPv4 addresses is included.

Due to the nature of IP address sharing, some limitations to its applicability are necessary. This memo describes these limitations and recommends suitable architectures and technologies where address sharing may be utilized.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. BoucadairddatejŒÈhfilenamegrfc7618eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129441hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUŒáÀgranking¬crfcd7618irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Dynamic Allocation of Shared IPv4 AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx©This document defines the "Basic" Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) authentication scheme, which transmits credentials as user-id/ password pairs, encoded using Base64.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’êhfilenamegrfc7617eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhhttpauthdfullx5httpauth - Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdnamex*Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129440hkeywordsÉdHTTPuauthentication schemexbasic authentication schemekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateVs.granking¡crfcd7617irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&The 'Basic' HTTP Authentication SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThe Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) provides a simple challenge- response authentication mechanism that may be used by a server to challenge a client request and by a client to provide authentication information.  This document defines the HTTP Digest Authentication scheme that can be used with the HTTP authentication mechanism.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamenR. Shekh-Yusef¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiD. Ahrens¢kaffiliationkNetzkonformdnameiS. Bremerddatej’õhfilenamegrfc7616eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhhttpauthdfullx5httpauth - Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdnamex*Hypertext Transfer Protocol Authenticationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129439hkeywordsÇdHTTPuauthentication schemekobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateVs-granking¿crfcd7616irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!HTTP Digest Access AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification defines the "Authentication-Info" and "Proxy- Authentication-Info" response header fields for use in Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) authentication schemes that need to return information once the client's authentication credentials have been accepted.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’òhfilenamegrfc7615eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129438hkeywordsÇdHTTPnauthenticationkobsoletedByÅd9110epagesopublicationDateVs,grankingøcrfcd7615irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMHTTP Authentication-Info and Proxy-Authentication-Info Response Header FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) REFER request, as defined by RFC 3515, triggers an implicit SIP-Specific Event Notification framework subscription.  Conflating the start of the subscription with handling the REFER request makes negotiating SUBSCRIBE extensions impossible and complicates avoiding SIP dialog sharing.  This document defines extensions to REFER that remove the implicit subscription and, if desired, replace it with an explicit one.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiR. Sparksddatej’£hfilenamegrfc7614eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-129437hkeywordsÖcSIPjSIP EventsenosubkexplicitsuboRefer-Events-AtkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ
îgrankingæcrfcd7614irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Explicit Subscriptions for the REFER MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜This document describes updated methods for handling Unicode strings representing usernames and passwords.  The previous approach was known as SASLprep (RFC 4013) and was based on stringprep (RFC 3454).  The methods specified in this document provide a more sustainable approach to the handling of internationalized usernames and passwords.  The preparation, enforcement, and comparison of internationalized strings (PRECIS) framework, RFC 7564, obsoletes RFC 3454, and this document obsoletes RFC 4013.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’ùhfilenamegrfc7613eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129436hkeywordsâhUsernamehPasswordgUnicodetInternationalizationdi18nnAuthenticationhSASLprepgstringsjstringprepkobsoletedByÅd8265epagesopublicationDateUŒáÀgrankingΩcrfcd7613irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjPreparation, Enforcement, and Comparison of Internationalized Strings Representing Usernames and PasswordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÇThis document defines a schema, object classes, and attributes, for Printers and print services, for use with directories that support the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (RFC 4510). This document is based on the Printer attributes listed in Appendix E of "Internet Printing Protocol/1.1: Model and Semantics" (RFC 2911). Additional Printer attributes are based on definitions in "Printer MIB v2" (RFC 3805), "PWG Command Set Format for IEEE 1284 Device ID v1.0" (PWG 5107.2), "IPP Job and Printer Extensions - Set 3 (JPS3)" (PWG 5100.13), and "IPP Everywhere" (PWG 5100.14).

This memo is an Independent Submission to the RFC Editor by the Internet Printing Protocol (IPP) Working Group of the IEEE-ISTO Printer Working Group (PWG), as part of their PWG "IPP Everywhere" (PWG 5100.14) project for secure mobile printing with vendor-neutral Client software.

This document obsoletes RFC 3712.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejP. Fleming¢kaffiliationjHigh NorthdnamekI. McDonaldddatejŒÂhfilenamegrfc7612eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129435hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateUì(Wgrankingºcrfcd7612irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexILightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): Schema for Printer ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUnder certain conditions, it is desirable for a Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) route reflector to be able to modify the Route Target (RT) list of a Virtual Private Network (VPN) route that the route reflector distributes, enabling the route reflector to control how a route originated within one VPN Routing and Forwarding table (VRF) is imported into other VRFs.  This technique works effectively as long as the VRF that exports the route is not on the same Provider Edge (PE) router as the VRF(s) that imports the route.  However, due to the constraints of BGP, it does not work if the two are on the same PE.  This document describes a modification to BGP allowing this technique to work when the VRFs are on the same PE and to be used in a standard manner throughout an autonomous system.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationpSproute NetworksdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Smith¢kaffiliationmMirantis Inc.dnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej’£hfilenamegrfc7611eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129434hkeywordsÜcBGPcVPNeL3VPNhExtranetjWell-knownhReservedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU‰‰˛grankingªcrfcd7611irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"BGP ACCEPT_OWN Community AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty‘This document specifies a mechanism for protecting hosts connected to a switched network against rogue DHCPv6 servers.  It is based on DHCPv6 packet filtering at the layer 2 device at which the packets are received.  A similar mechanism has been widely deployed in IPv4 networks ('DHCP snooping'); hence, it is desirable that similar functionality be provided for IPv6 networks.  This document specifies a Best Current Practice for the implementation of DHCPv6-Shield.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Liu¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameoG. Van de VeldeddatejŒÛhfilenamegrfc7610eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129433hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU÷VJgranking∫crfcd7610irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber«etotaletitlex6DHCPv6-Shield: Protecting against Rogue DHCPv6 ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractylThis document describes IBM's Shared Memory Communications over RDMA (SMC-R) protocol.  This protocol provides Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) communications to TCP endpoints in a manner that is transparent to socket applications.  It further provides for dynamic discovery of partner RDMA capabilities and dynamic setup of RDMA connections, as well as transparent high availability and load balancing when redundant RDMA network paths are available.  It maintains many of the traditional TCP/IP qualities of service such as filtering that enterprise users demand, as well as TCP socket semantics such as urgent data.fadNamerMartin StiemerlinggauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamefM. Fox¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamekC. Kassimis¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejJ. StevensddatejŒ„hfilenamegrfc7609eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129432hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesèopublicationDateUŒá grankingπcrfcd7609irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex=IBM's Shared Memory Communications over RDMA (SMC-R) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty(IPv6 prefix length, as in IPv4, is a parameter conveyed and used in IPv6 routing and forwarding processes in accordance with the Classless Inter-domain Routing (CIDR) architecture.  The length of an IPv6 prefix may be any number from zero to 128, although subnets using stateless address autoconfiguration (SLAAC) for address allocation conventionally use a /64 prefix.  Hardware and software implementations of routing and forwarding should therefore impose no rules on prefix length, but implement longest-match-first on prefixes of any valid length.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationjCEA / LISTdnamekA. Petrescu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Bakerddatej’ùhfilenamegrfc7608eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129431hkeywordsâlIPv6 RoutingdCIDRxClassless Inter-Domain RoutingxIPv6 Addressing Architecturex IPv6 Forwarding Information BasexIPv6 Routing Information BasecFIBcRIBoIPv6 DeploymentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU∞ıgranking∏crfcd7608irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber∆etotaletitlex0IPv6 Prefix Length Recommendation for ForwardingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‘This document updates RFC 4271 and proscribes the use of Autonomous System (AS) 0 in the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) OPEN, AS_PATH, AS4_PATH, AGGREGATOR, and AS4_AGGREGATOR attributes in the BGP UPDATE message.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekH. Schiller¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patelddatej’hfilenamegrfc7607eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129430hkeywordsÑcBGPdAS 0gAS_PATHgAS-PATHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU¬◊àgranking∑crfcd7607irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCodification of AS 0 ProcessingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøAccording to the base BGP specification, a BGP speaker that receives an UPDATE message containing a malformed attribute is required to reset the session over which the offending attribute was received. This behavior is undesirable because a session reset would impact not only routes with the offending attribute but also other valid routes exchanged over the session. This document partially revises the error handling for UPDATE messages and provides guidelines for the authors of documents defining new attributes. Finally, it revises the error handling procedures for a number of existing attributes.

This document updates error handling for RFCs 1997, 4271, 4360, 4456, 4760, 5543, 5701, and 6368.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationpSproute NetworksdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehK. Patelddatej’ùhfilenamegrfc7606eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129429hkeywordsÅcBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ
ígranking∂crfcd7606irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Revised Error Handling for BGP UPDATE MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyëThis document provides recommendations to designers of application and service protocols on how to use the transport protocol port number space and when to request a port assignment from IANA.  It provides designer guidance to requesters or users of port numbers on how to interact with IANA using the processes defined in RFC 6335; thus, this document complements (but does not update) that document.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touchddatej’ôhfilenamegrfc7605eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129428hkeywordsÜctcpcudpdsctpddccpgservicedianakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUƒˆ|grankingµcrfcd7605irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber•etotaletitlex8Recommendations on Using Assigned Transport Port NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis document describes several Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal techniques that were considered to be used for establishing the RTP media flows controlled by the Real-Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP).  Each technique includes a description of how it would be used, the security implications of using it, and any other deployment considerations it has.  There are also discussions on how NAT traversal techniques relate to firewalls and how each technique can be applied in different use cases.  These findings were used when selecting the NAT traversal for RTSP 2.0, which is specified in a separate document.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationpPacketVideo CorpdnamegT. Zengddatej’úhfilenamegrfc7604eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129427hkeywordsÖcRTPxReal-time Transport ProtocoliReal-timehFirewallcUDPkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateV⁄†granking¥crfcd7604irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpComparison of Different NAT Traversal Techniques for Media Controlled by the Real-Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡The objective of Energy Management (EMAN) is to provide an energy management framework for networked devices.  This document presents the applicability of the EMAN information model in a variety of scenarios with cases and target devices.  These use cases are useful for identifying requirements for the framework and MIBs.  Further, we describe the relationship of the EMAN framework to other relevant energy monitoring standards and architectures.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamelB. Schoening¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameoM. Chandramouli¢kaffiliationxLawrence Berkeley National LabdnamejB. NordmanddatejŒ#hfilenamegrfc7603eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdemandfullxeman - Energy ManagementdnameqEnergy Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129426hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ|ygranking≥crfcd7603irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Energy Management (EMAN) Applicability StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxéThis document defines the Extended Sequence Number TLV to protect Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) PDUs from replay attacks.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmEricsson Inc.dnamekU. Chunduri¢kaffiliationmEricsson Inc.dnameeW. Lu¢kaffiliationmEricsson Inc.dnamegA. Tian¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegN. ShenddatejŒ‡hfilenamegrfc7602eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129425hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU∞ıgranking≤crfcd7602irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"IS-IS Extended Sequence Number TLVdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document specifies a message header field called Authentication- Results for use with electronic mail messages to indicate the results of message authentication efforts.  Any receiver-side software, such as mail filters or Mail User Agents (MUAs), can use this header field to relay that information in a convenient and meaningful way to users or to make sorting and filtering decisions.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’îhfilenamegrfc7601eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129424hkeywordsâdDKIMjDomainKeyshSenderIDcSPFdADSPdATPScVBRnAuthenticationjReputationkobsoletedByÅd8601epages5opublicationDateU÷VIgranking±crfcd7601irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAMessage Header Field for Indicating Message Authentication StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈThis document specifies a stateless solution for service providers to progressively deploy IPv6-only network domains while still offering IPv4 service to customers.  The solution's distinctive properties are that TCP/UDP IPv4 packets are valid TCP/UDP IPv6 packets during domain traversal and that IPv4 fragmentation rules are fully preserved end to end.  Each customer can be assigned one public IPv4 address, several public IPv4 addresses, or a shared address with a restricted port set.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationiRD-IPtechdnamejR. Despres¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegG. Chen¢kaffiliationjBBIX, Inc.dnamegM. Chenddatej’îhfilenamegrfc7600eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129423hkeywordsäkCoexistencejTransitionlInterworkingiTunnelingiStatelessc4rddIPv4dIPv6gMappingqGlobal AddressingkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateU∞ıgranking∞crfcd7600irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>IPv4 Residual Deployment via IPv6 - A Stateless Solution (4rd)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÏThis document specifies the solution architecture based on "Mapping of Address and Port" stateless IPv6-IPv4 Network Address Translation (NAT64) for providing shared or non-shared IPv4 address connectivity to and across an IPv6 network.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefW. Dec¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationpSoftBank TelecomdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamekT. MurakamiddatejŒ⁄hfilenamegrfc7599eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129422hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU∫…ÍgrankingØcrfcd7599irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Mapping of Address and Port using Translation (MAP-T)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document specifies DHCPv6 options, termed Softwire46 options, for the provisioning of Softwire46 Customer Edge (CE) devices.  Softwire46 is a collective term used to refer to architectures based on the notion of IPv4 Address plus Port (A+P) for providing IPv4 connectivity across an IPv6 network.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalski¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrer¢kaffiliationsJive CommunicationsdnamelS. Perreault¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefW. Dec¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationwFreelancer TechnologiesdnamefL. Yeh¢kaffiliationx!The University of New South WalesdnamegX. Dengddatej’ìhfilenamegrfc7598eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129421hkeywordsÇcMAPfDHCPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU∫…ÈgrankingÆcrfcd7598irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDHCPv6 Options for Configuration of Softwire Address and Port-Mapped ClientsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8539∂habstractxÔThis document describes a mechanism for transporting IPv4 packets across an IPv6 network using IP encapsulation.  It also describes a generic mechanism for mapping between IPv6 addresses and IPv4 addresses as well as transport-layer ports.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefW. Dec¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationpSoftBank TelecomdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamekT. Murakami¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. TaylorddatejŒŸhfilenamegrfc7597eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129420hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateU∫…Ëgranking≠crfcd7597irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Mapping of Address and Port with Encapsulation (MAP-E)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite) (RFC 6333) describes an architecture for transporting IPv4 packets over an IPv6 network.  This document specifies an extension to DS-Lite called "Lightweight 4over6", which moves the Network Address and Port Translation (NAPT) function from the centralized DS-Lite tunnel concentrator to the tunnel client located in the Customer Premises Equipment (CPE).  This removes the requirement for a Carrier Grade NAT function in the tunnel concentrator and reduces the amount of centralized state that must be held to a per-subscriber level.  In order to delegate the NAPT function and make IPv4 address sharing possible, port-restricted IPv4 addresses are allocated to the CPEs.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrerddatej’ìhfilenamegrfc7596eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-129419hkeywordságDS-Liteoaddress sharingraddress exhaustionfapluspcA+PwIPv4 service continuityxIPv4 over IPv6 connectivitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU∫…Âgranking¨crfcd7596irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDLightweight 4over6: An Extension to the Dual-Stack Lite ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxæThis document updates the guidelines and recommendations, as well as the IANA registration processes, for the definition of Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) schemes.  It obsoletes RFC 4395.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiT. Hardieddatej’íhfilenamegrfc7595eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129418hkeywordsÖjURI schemecIRIx%Internationalized Resource IdentifierxUniform Resource IdentifierpURI registrationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÑ†Egranking´crfcd7595irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber#etotaletitlex6Guidelines and Registration Procedures for URI SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8615∂habstractybMeasuring broadband service on a large scale requires a description of the logical architecture and standardisation of the key protocols that coordinate interactions between the components.  This document presents an overall framework for large-scale measurements.  It also defines terminology for LMAP (Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performance).fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationbBTdnamelT. Burbridge¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiA. Akhterddatej’úhfilenamegrfc7594eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlmapdfullx7lmap - Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancednamex0Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129417hkeywordsájControlleriCollectorqMeasurement AgentfMetricrMeasurement MethodsMeasurement ResultshRegistrykobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateU¸/Ögranking™crfcd7594irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGA Framework for Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performance (LMAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractylThis document describes the architecture of the eduroam service for federated (wireless) network access in academia.  The combination of IEEE 802.1X, the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), and RADIUS that is used in eduroam provides a secure, scalable, and deployable service for roaming network access.  The successful deployment of eduroam over the last decade in the educational sector may serve as an example for other sectors, hence this document.  In particular, the initial architectural choices and selection of standards are described, along with the changes that were prompted by operational experience.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekK. Wierenga¢kaffiliationgRESTENAdnameiS. Winter¢kaffiliationxNicolaus Copernicus UniversitydnamemT. Wolniewiczddatej’¶hfilenamegrfc7593eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129416hkeywordsáxFederated AuthenticationcAAAfRADIUSkIEEE 802.1XgroamingcEAPgeduroamkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateV⁄ügranking©crfcd7593irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,The eduroam Architecture for Network RoamingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This specification defines methods for management of OAuth 2.0 dynamic client registrations for use cases in which the properties of a registered client may need to be changed during the lifetime of the client.  Not all authorization servers supporting dynamic client registration will support these management methods.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Richer¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationtNewcastle UniversitydnamekM. MachulakddatejŒ„hfilenamegrfc7592eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129415hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUüˇgranking®crfcd7592irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9OAuth 2.0 Dynamic Client Registration Management ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis specification defines mechanisms for dynamically registering OAuth 2.0 clients with authorization servers.  Registration requests send a set of desired client metadata values to the authorization server.  The resulting registration responses return a client identifier to use at the authorization server and the client metadata values registered for the client.  The client can then use this registration information to communicate with the authorization server using the OAuth 2.0 protocol.  This specification also defines a set of common client metadata fields and values for clients to use during registration.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Richer¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationtNewcastle UniversitydnamekM. Machulak¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamegP. Huntddatej’ähfilenamegrfc7591eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129414hkeywordsàx*OpenID Connect Dynamic Client RegistrationnOpenID Connectdoidcfopenidsuser managed accesscumatDynamic RegistrationxDynamic Client RegistrationkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateUü¸grankingßcrfcd7591irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.OAuth 2.0 Dynamic Client Registration ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxØThis document provides recommendations for the use of Transport Layer Security (TLS) in the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP).  This document updates RFC 6120.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliation`dnamekT. Alkemadeddatej’õhfilenamegrfc7590eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129413hkeywordsãx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocoldXMPPfJabbertSecure Sockets LayercSSLxTransport Layer SecuritycTLSqinstant messaginghpresencejencryptionnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateUÑºigranking¶crfcd7590irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Use of Transport Layer Security (TLS) in the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThe Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) provides mechanisms to install, manipulate, and delete the configuration of network devices.  This document describes how to use the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol with mutual X.509 authentication to secure the exchange of NETCONF messages.  This revision of RFC 5539 documents the new message framing used by NETCONF 1.1 and it obsoletes RFC 5539.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpZayed UniversitydnamehM. Badra¢kaffiliationsSNMP Research, Inc.dnameiA. Luchuk¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej’ähfilenamegrfc7589eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129412hkeywordsÇgNETCONFcTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÑºggranking•crfcd7589irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Using the NETCONF Protocol over Transport Layer Security (TLS) with Mutual X.509 AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‰This memo describes how many vendors have solved the Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) fragmentation problem.  The solution described herein is configurable.  It is widely deployed on the Internet in its default configuration.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touchddatej’úhfilenamegrfc7588eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129411hkeywordsÉcGREcMTUmFragmentationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU•¯ågranking§crfcd7588irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[A Widely Deployed Solution to the Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) Fragmentation ProblemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document defines the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for packetization of Opus-encoded speech and audio data necessary to integrate the codec in the most compatible way.  It also provides an applicability statement for the use of Opus over RTP.  Further, it describes media type registrations for the RTP payload format.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Spittka¢kaffiliationgvocTonednamefK. Vos¢kaffiliationgMozilladnameiJM. Valinddatej“”hfilenamegrfc7587eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129410hkeywordsÅkaudio codeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUì(Vgranking£crfcd7587irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6RTP Payload Format for the Opus Speech and Audio CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty5This document introduces the Scalable Address Resolution Protocol (SARP), an architecture that uses proxy gateways to scale large data center networks.  SARP is based on fast proxies that significantly reduce switches' Filtering Database (FDB) table sizes and reduce impact of ARP and Neighbor Discovery (ND) on network elements in an environment where hosts within one subnet (or VLAN) can spread over various locations.  SARP is targeted for massive data centers with a significant number of Virtual Machines (VMs) that can move across various physical locations.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiY. Nachum¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamemI. Yerushalmi¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahiddatej’òhfilenamegrfc7586eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129409hkeywordsÉcARPkdata centereproxykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUàU∂granking¢crfcd7586irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexFThe Scalable Address Resolution Protocol (SARP) for Large Data CentersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxThis document specifies a means to find authoritative RADIUS servers for a given realm.  It is used in conjunction with either RADIUS over Transport Layer Security (RADIUS/TLS) or RADIUS over Datagram Transport Layer Security (RADIUS/DTLS).fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRESTENAdnameiS. Winter¢kaffiliationiAirSpaycednamekM. McCauleyddatej’öhfilenamegrfc7585eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129408hkeywordsÖfRADIUScAAAhSecuritykReliabilitycDNSkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateV≈—granking°crfcd7585irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbDynamic Peer Discovery for RADIUS/TLS and RADIUS/DTLS Based on the Network Access Identifier (NAI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs) are often designed to be on the media path rather than just intercepting signaling. This means that B2BUAs often act on the media path leading to separate media legs that the B2BUA correlates and bridges together. When acting on the media path, B2BUAs are likely to receive Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) packets as part of Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) processing.

This document defines behavior for a B2BUA performing ICE processing. The goal of this document is to ensure that B2BUAs properly handle SIP messages that carry ICE semantics in Session Description Protocol (SDP) and STUN messages received as part of the ICE procedures for NAT and Firewall traversal of multimedia sessions.fadNamelBen Campbelldarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoR. Ravindranath¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. SalgueiroddatejŒÊhfilenamegrfc7584eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestrawdfullx7straw - Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdnamex/Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdtypebwgbidjdoc-129407hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUû˘granking†crfcd7584irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeSession Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) Message Handling for SIP Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the issues surrounding the timing of events in the rolling of a key in a DNSSEC-secured zone.  It presents timelines for the key rollover and explicitly identifies the relationships between the various parameters affecting the process.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiS. Morris¢kaffiliationfNetnoddnamehJ. Ihren¢kaffiliationgSinodundnamelJ. Dickinson¢kaffiliationcDyndnamejW. MekkingddatejŒ…hfilenamegrfc7583eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129406hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateV'˛àgrankingücrfcd7583irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)DNSSEC Key Rollover Timing ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA set of prior RFCs specify procedures for supporting multicast in BGP/MPLS IP VPNs.  These procedures allow customer multicast data to travel across a service provider's backbone network through a set of multicast tunnels.  The tunnels are advertised in certain BGP multicast auto-discovery routes, by means of a BGP attribute known as the "Provider Multicast Service Interface (PMSI) Tunnel" attribute.  Encodings have been defined that allow the PMSI Tunnel attribute to identify bidirectional (multipoint-to-multipoint) multicast distribution trees.  However, the prior RFCs do not provide all the necessary procedures for using bidirectional tunnels to support multicast VPNs.  This document updates RFCs 6513, 6514, and 6625 by specifying those procedures.  In particular, it specifies the procedures for assigning customer multicast flows (unidirectional or bidirectional) to specific bidirectional tunnels in the provider backbone, for advertising such assignments, and for determining which flows have been assigned to which tunnels.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. BoersddatejŒÂhfilenamegrfc7582eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129405hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateU•¯ãgrankingûcrfcd7582irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGMulticast Virtual Private Network (MVPN): Using Bidirectional P-TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8534d9573∂habstracty·A Wavelength Switched Optical Network (WSON) requires certain key information fields be made available to facilitate path computation and the establishment of Label Switched Paths (LSPs). The information model described in "Routing and Wavelength Assignment Information Model for Wavelength Switched Optical Networks" (RFC 7446) shows what information is required at specific points in the WSON. Part of the WSON information model contains aspects that may be of general applicability to other technologies, while other parts are specific to WSONs.

This document provides efficient, protocol-agnostic encodings for the WSON-specific information fields. It is intended that protocol- specific documents will reference this memo to describe how information is carried for specific uses. Such encodings can be used to extend GMPLS signaling and routing protocols. In addition, these encodings could be used by other mechanisms to convey this same information to a Path Computation Element (PCE).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejW. Imajuku¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. Handdatej’ïhfilenamegrfc7581eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129404hkeywordsÖpOptical NetworkssGMPLS control planeuWavelength AssignmentkOptical LSPoOptical RoutingkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateUåiágrankingùcrfcd7581irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Routing and Wavelength Assignment Information Encoding for Wavelength Switched Optical NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) can be used to control a wide variety of technologies including packet switching (e.g., MPLS), time division (e.g., Synchronous Optical Network / Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) and Optical Transport Network (OTN)), wavelength (lambdas), and spatial switching (e.g., incoming port or fiber to outgoing port or fiber).  In some of these technologies, network elements and links may impose additional routing constraints such as asymmetric switch connectivity, non- local label assignment, and label range limitations on links.  This document describes Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) routing protocol extensions to support these kinds of constraints under the control of GMPLS.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. Han¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationdCATRdnameeY. Xuddatej’íhfilenamegrfc7580eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129403hkeywordsÇdWSONoOptical RoutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUåiÜgrankingúcrfcd7580irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:OSPF-TE Extensions for General Network Element ConstraintsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhGeneralized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) can be used to control a wide variety of technologies. In some of these technologies, network elements and links may impose additional routing constraints such as asymmetric switch connectivity, non-local label assignment, and label range limitations on links.

This document provides efficient, protocol-agnostic encodings for general information elements representing connectivity and label constraints as well as label availability. It is intended that protocol-specific documents will reference this memo to describe how information is carried for specific uses.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejW. Imajuku¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. Handdatej’èhfilenamegrfc7579eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129402hkeywordsÉdWSONwOptical Network ControlxProtocol-agnostic encodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUåiÑgrankingõcrfcd7579irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIGeneral Network Element Constraint Encoding for GMPLS-Controlled NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification defines the multipart/form-data media type, which can be used by a wide variety of applications and transported by a wide variety of protocols as a way of returning a set of values as the result of a user filling out a form.  This document obsoletes RFC 2388.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekL. Masinterddatej’òhfilenamegrfc7578eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129401hkeywordsÖjmedia-typelmultipurposehinternetdmailjextensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU•¯ägrankingöcrfcd7578irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Returning Values from Forms: multipart/form-datadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¸This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines managed objects that provide information on the status of batteries in managed devices.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnameiR. Winter¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamehT. Dietzddatej’ôhfilenamegrfc7577eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdemandfullxeman - Energy ManagementdnameqEnergy Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129400hkeywordsÑqEnergy ManagementnBattery StatuskBattery MIBxManagement Information BasekobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateU∞ıgrankingôcrfcd7577irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Definition of Managed Objects for Battery MonitoringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyNThis document provides a problem statement and general gap analysis for an IP-based Autonomic Network that is mainly based on distributed network devices. The document provides background by reviewing the current status of autonomic aspects of IP networks and the extent to which current network management depends on centralization and human administrators. Finally, the document outlines the general features that are missing from current network abilities and are needed in the ideal Autonomic Network concept.

This document is a product of the IRTF's Network Management Research Group.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. BehringerddatejŒ€hfilenamegrfc7576eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnmrgdfullxnmrg - Network ManagementdnamerNetwork Managementdtypebrgbidjdoc-129399hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÉp≈grankingòcrfcd7576irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex-General Gap Analysis for Autonomic NetworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyèAutonomic systems were first described in 2001. The fundamental goal is self-management, including self-configuration, self-optimization, self-healing, and self-protection. This is achieved by an autonomic function having minimal dependencies on human administrators or centralized management systems. It usually implies distribution across network elements.

This document defines common language and outlines design goals (and what are not design goals) for autonomic functions. A high-level reference model illustrates how functional elements in an Autonomic Network interact. This document is a product of the IRTF's Network Management Research Group.gauthorsá¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. Behringer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Pritikin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Bjarnason¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamelL. Ciavagliaddatej’Éhfilenamegrfc7575eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnmrgdfullxnmrg - Network ManagementdnamerNetwork Managementdtypebrgbidjdoc-129398hkeywordsÖoself-managementiself-chopiautonomicqsecure by defaultnsimplificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÉp≈grankingócrfcd7575irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex2Autonomic Networking: Definitions and Design GoalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThe Peer-to-Peer Streaming Peer Protocol (PPSPP) is a protocol for disseminating the same content to a group of interested parties in a streaming fashion.  PPSPP supports streaming of both prerecorded (on- demand) and live audio/video content.  It is based on the peer-to- peer paradigm, where clients consuming the content are put on equal footing with the servers initially providing the content, to create a system where everyone can potentially provide upload bandwidth.  It has been designed to provide short time-till-playback for the end user and to prevent disruption of the streams by malicious peers.  PPSPP has also been designed to be flexible and extensible.  It can use different mechanisms to optimize peer uploading, prevent freeriding, and work with different peer discovery schemes (centralized trackers or Distributed Hash Tables).  It supports multiple methods for content integrity protection and chunk addressing.  Designed as a generic protocol that can run on top of various transport protocols, it currently runs on top of UDP using Low Extra Delay Background Transport (LEDBAT) for congestion control.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxVrije Universiteit AmsterdamdnameiA. Bakker¢kaffiliationxTechnische Universiteit DelftdnamekR. Petrocco¢kaffiliationxTechnische Universiteit DelftdnamenV. Grishchenkoddatej’óhfilenamegrfc7574eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdppspdfullx&ppsp - Peer to Peer Streaming ProtocoldnamexPeer to Peer Streaming Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129397hkeywordsÉovideo on demandnlive streamingxcontent integrity protectionkobsoletedByÄepagesUopublicationDateUû˘grankingñcrfcd7574irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Peer-to-Peer Streaming Peer Protocol (PPSPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document defines a bidirectional protocol mapping for the exchange of instant messages in the context of a one-to-one chat session between a user of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and a user of the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP).  Specifically for SIP text chat, this document specifies a mapping to the Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP).fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loretoddatej’éhfilenamegrfc7573eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstoxdfullrstox - SIP-TO-XMPPdnamekSIP-TO-XMPPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129396hkeywordsàiText ChatqInstant MessagingxSession Initiation ProtocolcSIPxMessage Sessions Relay ProtocoldMSRPx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocoldXMPPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUx‰ùgrankingïcrfcd7573irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexìInterworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): One-to-One Text Chat SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÀThis document defines a bidirectional protocol mapping for the exchange of single instant messages between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP).fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehA. Houri¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ. Hildebrandddatej’éhfilenamegrfc7572eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstoxdfullrstox - SIP-TO-XMPPdnamekSIP-TO-XMPPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129395hkeywordsÜdXMPPfJabbercSIPfSIMPLEbIMoInstant MessagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUx‰ögrankingîcrfcd7572irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexáInterworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): Instant MessagingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document specifies extensions to Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) to support Lock Instruct (LI) and Loopback (LB) mechanisms for Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  These mechanisms are applicable to technologies that use Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) for the control plane.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvHuawei Technologies CodnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelliddatej’ïhfilenamegrfc7571eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129394hkeywordsÅcOAMkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateUû˘grankingìcrfcd7571irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7GMPLS RSVP-TE Extensions for Lock Instruct and LoopbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?RFC 5420 extends RSVP-TE to specify or record generic attributes that apply to the whole of the path of a Label Switched Path (LSP).  This document defines an extension to the RSVP Explicit Route Object (ERO) and Record Route Object (RRO) to allow them to specify or record generic attributes that apply to a given hop.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamekC. Margaria¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemG. Martinelli¢kaffiliationjMetaswitchdnamehS. Balls¢kaffiliationjMetaswitchdnameiB. Wrightddatej’àhfilenamegrfc7570eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129393hkeywordsÇgRSVP-TEeGMPLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUû˘grankingícrfcd7570irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFLabel Switched Path (LSP) Attribute in the Explicit Route Object (ERO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥In the past, Mandatory Access Control (MAC) systems have used very rigid policies that were implemented in particular protocols and platforms. As MAC systems become more widely deployed, additional flexibility in mechanism and policy will be required. While traditional trusted systems implemented Multi-Level Security (MLS) and integrity models, modern systems have expanded to include such technologies as type enforcement. Due to the wide range of policies and mechanisms that need to be accommodated, it is unlikely that the use of a single security label format and model will be viable.

To allow multiple MAC mechanisms and label formats to co-exist in a network, this document creates a registry of label format specifications. This registry contains label format identifiers and provides for the association of each such identifier with a corresponding extensive document outlining the exact syntax and use of the particular label format.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Quigley¢kaffiliationfOraclednameeJ. Lu¢kaffiliationlPrimary DatadnameiT. Haynesddatej’ôhfilenamegrfc7569eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129392hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateU•¯âgrankingëcrfcd7569irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRegistry Specification for Mandatory Access Control (MAC) Security Label FormatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThe Secure Sockets Layer version 3.0 (SSLv3), as specified in RFC 6101, is not sufficiently secure. This document requires that SSLv3 not be used. The replacement versions, in particular, Transport Layer Security (TLS) 1.2 (RFC 5246), are considerably more secure and capable protocols.

This document updates the backward compatibility section of RFC 5246 and its predecessors to prohibit fallback to SSLv3.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamejA. Pironti¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejA. Langleyddatej’òhfilenamegrfc7568eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129391hkeywordsÑcSSLcTLShinsecureidiediediekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUåiÉgrankingêcrfcd7568irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Deprecating Secure Sockets Layer Version 3.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∑habstractyrThis memo presents recommendations to the Internet community concerning measures to improve and preserve Internet performance. It presents a strong recommendation for testing, standardization, and widespread deployment of active queue management (AQM) in network devices to improve the performance of today's Internet. It also urges a concerted effort of research, measurement, and ultimate deployment of AQM mechanisms to protect the Internet from flows that are not sufficiently responsive to congestion notification.

Based on 15 years of experience and new research, this document replaces the recommendations of RFC 2309.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. FairhurstddatejŒﬂhfilenamegrfc7567eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaqmdfullx3aqm - Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdnamex-Active Queue Management and Packet Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129390hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUû˘grankingècrfcd7567irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber≈etotaletitlex6IETF Recommendations Regarding Active Queue ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxnThis document registers an E.164 Number Mapping (ENUM) service for 'acct' URIs (Uniform Resource Identifiers).gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxEconocom-Osiatis IngenieriednamegL. Goix¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnameeK. Liddatej’ihfilenamegrfc7566eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129389hkeywordsÇtReverse Phone LookuprSocial Network WebkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUåiÇgrankingécrfcd7566irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex'Enumservice Registration for 'acct' URIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁThis document defines the 'acct' Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme as a way to identify a user's account at a service provider, irrespective of the particular protocols that can be used to interact with the account.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’8hfilenamegrfc7565eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129388hkeywordsÇxUniform Resource IdentifiercURIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU\´€grankingçcrfcd7565irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuThe 'acct' URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1Application protocols using Unicode characters in protocol strings need to properly handle such strings in order to enforce internationalization rules for strings placed in various protocol slots (such as addresses and identifiers) and to perform valid comparison operations (e.g., for purposes of authentication or authorization).  This document defines a framework enabling application protocols to perform the preparation, enforcement, and comparison of internationalized strings ("PRECIS") in a way that depends on the properties of Unicode characters and thus is agile with respect to versions of Unicode.  As a result, this framework provides a more sustainable approach to the handling of internationalized strings than the previous framework, known as Stringprep (RFC 3454).  This document obsoletes RFC 3454.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej’éhfilenamegrfc7564eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129387hkeywordsÉtinternationalizationdi18njStringprepkobsoletedByÅd8264epages(opublicationDateU\´⁄grankingåcrfcd7564irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpPRECIS Framework: Preparation, Enforcement, and Comparison of Internationalized Strings in Application ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,The Access Network Identifier (ANI) mobility option was introduced in RFC 6757, "Access Network Identifier (ANI) Option for Proxy Mobile IPv6".  This enables a Mobile Access Gateway (MAG) to convey identifiers like the network identifier, geolocation, and operator identifier.  This specification extends the Access Network Identifier mobility option with sub-options to carry the civic location and the MAG group identifier.  This specification also defines an ANI Update-Timer sub-option that determines when and how often the ANI option will be updated.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemR. Pazhyannur¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. Speicher¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameqJ. KaippallimalilddatejŒﬁhfilenamegrfc7563eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129386hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÑ†Cgrankingãcrfcd7563irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMExtensions to the Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) Access Network Identifier OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty#This document specifies the use of Digital Transmission Content Protection (DTCP) certificates as an authorization data type in the authorization extension for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol.  This is in accordance with the guidelines for authorization extensions as specified in RFC 5878.  As with other TLS extensions, this authorization data can be included in the client and server hello messages to confirm that both parties support the desired authorization data types.  If supported by both the client and the server, DTCP certificates are exchanged in the supplemental data TLS handshake message as specified in RFC 4680.  This authorization data type extension is in support of devices containing DTCP certificates issued by the Digital Transmission Licensing Administrator (DTLA).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamejD. Thakoreddatej’Ähfilenamegrfc7562eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129385hkeywordsÑxTransport Layer SecuritycTLSpSupplementalDatadDTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUû˘grankingäcrfcd7562irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexnTransport Layer Security (TLS) Authorization Using Digital Transmission Content Protection (DTCP) CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty^This document provides guidelines for achieving end-to-end Quality of Service (QoS) in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) domain where the access network is based on IEEE 802.11.  RFC 7222 describes QoS negotiation between a Mobile Access Gateway (MAG) and Local Mobility Anchor (LMA) in a PMIPv6 mobility domain.  The negotiated QoS parameters can be used for QoS policing and marking of packets to enforce QoS differentiation on the path between the MAG and LMA.  IEEE 802.11 and Wi-Fi Multimedia - Admission Control (WMM-AC) describe methods for QoS negotiation between a Wi-Fi Station (MN in PMIPv6 terminology) and an Access Point.  This document provides a mapping between the above two sets of QoS procedures and the associated QoS parameters.  This document is intended to be used as a companion document to RFC 7222 to enable implementation of end-to-end QoS.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameqJ. Kaippallimalil¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemR. Pazhyannur¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnameiP. Yeganiddatej’òhfilenamegrfc7561eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129384hkeywordsÉfPMIPv6eWi-FicQoSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÉpƒgrankingâcrfcd7561irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRMapping Quality of Service (QoS) Procedures of Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) and WLANdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) is a mechanism where network nodes can mark IP packets, instead of dropping them, to indicate congestion to the endpoints.  An ECN-capable receiver will feed this information back to the sender.  ECN is specified for TCP in such a way that it can only feed back one congestion signal per Round-Trip Time (RTT).  In contrast, ECN for other transport protocols, such as RTP/UDP and SCTP, is specified with more accurate ECN feedback.  Recent new TCP mechanisms (like Congestion Exposure (ConEx) or Data Center TCP (DCTCP)) need more accurate ECN feedback in the case where more than one marking is received in one RTT.  This document specifies requirements for an update to the TCP protocol to provide more accurate ECN feedback.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamemM. Kuehlewind¢kaffiliationlNetApp, Inc.dnamepR. Scheffenegger¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoeddatej’óhfilenamegrfc7560eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129383hkeywordsÇrcongestion controlcTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUﬁ|vgrankingàcrfcd7560irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlProblem Statement and Requirements for Increased Accuracy in Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) FeedbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñWhen an interface on a host is initialized, the host transmits Router Solicitations in order to minimize the amount of time it needs to wait until the next unsolicited multicast Router Advertisement is received.  In certain scenarios, these Router Solicitations transmitted by the host might be lost.  This document specifies a mechanism for hosts to cope with the loss of the initial Router Solicitations.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationuUnaffiliatedMicrosoftdnameiD. Anipko¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. ThalerddatejŒ“hfilenamegrfc7559eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129382hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUh‚~grankingácrfcd7559irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Packet-Loss Resiliency for Router SolicitationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTDNS-based Service Discovery (DNS-SD) over Multicast DNS (mDNS) is widely used today for discovery and resolution of services and names on a local link, but there are use cases to extend DNS-SD/mDNS to enable service discovery beyond the local link.  This document provides a problem statement and a list of requirements for scalable DNS-SD.fadNameoTerry Mandersondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlVerizon LabsdnamegK. Lynn¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamekM. Blanchet¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. MigaultddatejŒ‹hfilenamegrfc7558eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednssddfullx5dnssd - Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydnamex-Extensions for Scalable DNS Service Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-129381hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU•¯ágrankingÜcrfcd7558irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Requirements for Scalable DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD) / Multicast DNS (mDNS) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxiThis document defines the encoding of extensions to the Babel routing protocol, as specified in RFC 6126.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx!PPS / University of Paris-DiderotdnamemJ. Chroboczekddatej’óhfilenamegrfc7557eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129380hkeywordsÖeBabelgroutingiextensioncTLVgsub-TLVkobsoletedByÅd8966epagesopublicationDateUh‚~grankingÖcrfcd7557irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex2Extension Mechanism for the Babel Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document is a product of the work of the Multiple Interfaces Architecture Design team.  It outlines a solution framework for some of the issues experienced by nodes that can be attached to multiple networks simultaneously.  The framework defines the concept of a Provisioning Domain (PvD), which is a consistent set of network configuration information.  PvD-aware nodes learn PvD-specific information from the networks they are attached to and/or other sources.  PvDs are used to enable separation and configuration consistency in the presence of multiple concurrent connections.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiD. AnipkoddatejŒﬁhfilenamegrfc7556eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcmifdfullxmif - Multiple InterfacesdnamesMultiple Interfacesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129379hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUoAsgrankingÑcrfcd7556irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Multiple Provisioning Domain ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThis document defines a means of remotely initiating Multiprotocol Label Switched Protocol (MPLS) Pings on Label Switched Paths.  An MPLS Proxy Ping Request is sent to any Label Switching Router along a Label Switched Path.  The primary motivations for this facility are first to limit the number of messages and related processing when using LSP Ping in large Point-to-Multipoint LSPs, and second to enable tracing from leaf to leaf (or root).fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefV. Lim¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiS. AldrinddatejŒÃhfilenamegrfc7555eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129378hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÉp¬grankingÉcrfcd7555irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewProxy MPLS Echo RequestdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document describes the environment, problem statement, and goals for using the Time-Slotted Channel Hopping (TSCH) Medium Access Control (MAC) protocol of IEEE 802.14.4e in the context of Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLNs).  The set of goals enumerated in this document form an initial set only.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqLinear TechnologydnamekT. Watteyne¢kaffiliationxUniversity of LuxembourgdnamemM. Palattella¢kaffiliationsPolitecnico di BaridnameiL. Griecoddatej’ëhfilenamegrfc7554eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6tischdfullx26tisch - IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4ednamex)IPv6 over the TSCH mode of IEEE 802.15.4edtypebwgbidjdoc-129377hkeywordsÅf6TiSCHkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUU+agrankingÇcrfcd7554irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkUsing IEEE 802.15.4e Time-Slotted Channel Hopping (TSCH) in the Internet of Things (IoT): Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂h