ciated bidirectional LSP.  The association is achieved by defining new Association Types for use in ASSOCIATION and in Extended ASSOCIATION Objects.  One of these types enables independent provisioning of the associated bidirectional LSPs on both sides, while the other enables single-sided provisioning.  The REVERSE_LSP Object is also defined to enable a single endpoint to trigger creation of the reverse LSP and to specify parameters of the reverse LSP in the single-sided provisioning case.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegR. Jing¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. GandhiddatejŒ›hfilenamegrfc7551eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdteasdfullx5teas - Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdnamex.Traffic Engineering Architecture and Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129374hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUS¿grankingcrfcd7551irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRSVP-TE Extensions for Associated Bidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8537∂habstracty•The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) specification defined two stateful options, IA_NA and IA_TA, but did not anticipate the development of additional stateful options.  DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation added the IA_PD option, which is stateful.  Applications that use IA_NA and IA_PD together have revealed issues that need to be addressed.  This document updates RFCs 3315 and 3633 to address these issues.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelM. Siodelskiddatej’ïhfilenamegrfc7550eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129373hkeywordsêcCPEcCERbCEtCustomer Edge RouterqPrefix DelegationsIPv6 Address OptiongSessionmState MachineiAdvertisedTimekTimer T1 T2eRenewfRebindgConfirmgDeclineiProvisionkobsoletedByÅd8415epagesopublicationDateU\´⁄granking~crfcd7550irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Issues and Recommendations with Multiple Stateful DHCPv6 OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;In 3GPP networks, the signaling path between a calling user and a called user can be partitioned into segments, referred to as traffic legs. Each traffic leg may span networks belonging to different operators and will have its own characteristics that can be different from other traffic legs in the same call. A traffic leg might be associated with multiple SIP dialogs, e.g., in case a Back-to-Back User Agent (B2BUA) that modifies the SIP dialog identifier is located within the traffic leg.

This document defines a new SIP URI parameter, 'iotl' (an abbreviation for Inter-Operator Traffic Leg). The parameter can be used in a SIP URI to indicate that the entity associated with the address, or an entity responsible for the host part of the address, represents the end of a specific traffic leg (or multiple traffic legs).fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegJ. Holm¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesske¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehM. Dollyddatej’ïhfilenamegrfc7549eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129372hkeywordsâd3GPPcIMScNNIdIOTLdCSCFeRAVELcTRFhoperatorgtransitkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU[€	granking}crfcd7549irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex13GPP SIP URI Inter-Operator Traffic Leg ParameterdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document discusses use cases concerning the management of networks in which constrained devices are involved.  A problem statement, deployment options, and the requirements on the networks with constrained devices can be found in the companion document on "Management of Networks with Constrained Devices: Problem Statement and Requirements" (RFC 7547).fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnNokia NetworksdnamehM. Ersue¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnameiA. Sehgalddatej’êhfilenamegrfc7548eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129371hkeywordsÑkConstrainedjManagementcIoTcM2MkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUSÿ‘granking|crfcd7548irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Management of Networks with Constrained Devices: Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÿThis document provides a problem statement, deployment and management topology options, as well as requirements addressing the different use cases of the management of networks where constrained devices are involved.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnNokia NetworksdnamehM. Ersue¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliation`dnamejU. Herbergddatej’êhfilenamegrfc7547eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129370hkeywordsÑkConstrainedjManagementcIoTcM2MkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateUSÿ”granking{crfcd7547irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSManagement of Networks with Constrained Devices: Problem Statement and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document specifies the generic structure of the negotiation loop to establish a Generic Security Service (GSS) security context between initiator and acceptor.  The control flow of the loop is indicated for both parties, including error conditions, and indications are given for where application-specific behavior must be specified.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamehB. Kadukddatej’èhfilenamegrfc7546eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129369hkeywordsÉgGSS-APIhsecuritynauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUS¿grankingzcrfcd7546irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Structure of the Generic Security Service (GSS) Negotiation LoopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£Portions of the radio spectrum that are allocated to licensees are available for non-interfering use. This available spectrum is called "white space". Allowing secondary users access to available spectrum "unlocks" existing spectrum to maximize its utilization and to provide opportunities for innovation, resulting in greater overall spectrum utilization.

One approach to managing spectrum sharing uses databases to report spectrum availability to devices. To achieve interoperability among multiple devices and databases, a standardized protocol must be defined and implemented. This document defines such a protocol, the "Protocol to Access White-Space (PAWS) Databases".fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegV. Chen¢kaffiliationx"Applied Communication SciencesNonednamefS. Das¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationiiconectivdnameiJ. Malyar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiP. McCannddatej’vhfilenamegrfc7545eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpawsdfullx%paws - Protocol to Access WS databasednamexProtocol to Access WS databasedtypebwgbidjdoc-129368hkeywordsäpdynamic spectrumnradio spectrumqwireless spectrumhspectrumqspectrum databasenTV white spacedTVWSdTVBDrwhite space devicecWSDkobsoletedByÄepagesZopublicationDateU[€grankingycrfcd7545irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Protocol to Access White-Space (PAWS) DatabasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractytAlthough the SIP History-Info header field described in RFC 7044 is the solution adopted in IETF, the non-standard Diversion header field described, as Historic, in RFC 5806 is nevertheless already implemented and used for conveying call-diversion-related information in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) signaling.

RFC 7044 obsoletes the original RFC 4244 and redefines the History-Info header field for capturing the history information in requests.

Since the Diversion header field is used in existing network implementations for the transport of call diversion information, its interworking with the SIP History-Info standardized solution is needed. This document describes a recommended interworking guideline between the Diversion header field and the History-Info header field to handle call diversion information. This work is intended to enable the migration from non-standard implementations toward IETF specification-based implementations.

This document obsoletes RFC 6044, which describes the interworking between the Diversion header field defined in RFC 5806 and the obsoleted History-Info header field defined on RFC 4244.fadNamelBen CampbellgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOrangednameiM. Mohaliddatej’öhfilenamegrfc7544eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129367hkeywordsÇiDiversionlHistory-InfokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUƒˆ{grankingxcrfcd7544irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexãMapping and Interworking of Diversion Information between Diversion and History-Info Header Fields in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁThis document defines a new Outbound Route Filter (ORF) type, called the Covering Prefixes ORF (CP-ORF).  CP-ORF is applicable in Virtual Hub-and-Spoke VPNs.  It also is applicable in BGP/MPLS Ethernet VPN (EVPN) networks.fadNamemAlvaro Retanadarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegH. Jeng¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalil¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegL. Yongddatej’ñhfilenamegrfc7543eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129366hkeywordsÇcORFcVPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUH!,grankingwcrfcd7543irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Covering Prefixes Outbound Route Filter for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒIn order to provide inter-domain authentication services, it is necessary to have a standardized method that domains can use to identify each other's users.  This document defines the syntax for the Network Access Identifier (NAI), the user identifier submitted by the client prior to accessing resources.  This document is a revised version of RFC 4282.  It addresses issues with international character sets and makes a number of other corrections to RFC 4282.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. DeKokddatejŒŒhfilenamegrfc7542eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129365hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUC‘Ågrankingvcrfcd7542irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Network Access IdentifierdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx}This specification defines HPACK, a compression format for efficiently representing HTTP header fields, to be used in HTTP/2.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamegR. Peon¢kaffiliationiCanon CRFdnamejH. Ruellanddatej’çhfilenamegrfc7541eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129364hkeywordsÇdHTTPfHeaderkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateUU+agrankingucrfcd7541irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$HPACK: Header Compression for HTTP/2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This specification describes an optimized expression of the semantics of the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP), referred to as HTTP version 2 (HTTP/2). HTTP/2 enables a more efficient use of network resources and a reduced perception of latency by introducing header field compression and allowing multiple concurrent exchanges on the same connection. It also introduces unsolicited push of representations from servers to clients.

This specification is an alternative to, but does not obsolete, the HTTP/1.1 message syntax. HTTP's existing semantics remain unchanged.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeBitGodnameiM. Belshe¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamegR. Peon¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomsonddatej’åhfilenamegrfc7540eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129363hkeywordsÉdHTTPdSPDYcWebkobsoletedByÅd9113epages`opublicationDateUU+_grankingtcrfcd7540irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Hypertext Transfer Protocol Version 2 (HTTP/2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8740¥habstractyîThis document defines the ChaCha20 stream cipher as well as the use of the Poly1305 authenticator, both as stand-alone algorithms and as a "combined mode", or Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) algorithm.

This document does not introduce any new crypto, but is meant to serve as a stable reference and an implementation guide. It is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG).gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejA. Langleyddatej’èhfilenamegrfc7539eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-129362hkeywordsÅdAEADkobsoletedByÅd8439epages-opublicationDateUS¿grankingscrfcd7539irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex(ChaCha20 and Poly1305 for IETF ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxoThis document specifies the additional Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) status code 308 (Permanent Redirect).fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’ãhfilenamegrfc7538eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129361hkeywordsÉdHTTPhredirectkstatus codekobsoletedByÅd9110epagesopublicationDateU"¬grankingrcrfcd7538irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDThe Hypertext Transfer Protocol Status Code 308 (Permanent Redirect)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂRFCs 4379 and 6424 created name spaces for Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping. However, those RFCs did not create the corresponding IANA registries for Downstream Mapping object Flags (DS Flags), Multipath Types, Pad TLVs, and Interface and Label Stack Address Types.

There is now a need to make further code point allocations from these name spaces. This document updates RFCs 4379 and 6424 in that it creates IANA registries for that purpose.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiS. Aldrinddatej’íhfilenamegrfc7537eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129360hkeywordsÉhMPLS OAMhlsp pinghLSP-PingkobsoletedByÅd8029epagesopublicationDateUQ*Ègrankingqcrfcd7537irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(IANA Registries for LSP Ping Code PointsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMeasuring broadband performance on a large scale is important for network diagnostics by providers and users, as well as for public policy.  Understanding the various scenarios and users of measuring broadband performance is essential to development of the Large-scale Measurement of Broadband Performance (LMAP) framework, information model, and protocol.  This document details two use cases that can assist in developing that framework.  The details of the measurement metrics themselves are beyond the scope of this document.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Linsner¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationbBTdnamelT. Burbridge¢kaffiliationdNkomdnamekF. Sorensenddatej’åhfilenamegrfc7536eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlmapdfullx7lmap - Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancednamex0Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband Performancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129359hkeywordsÅdlmapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUH!*grankingpcrfcd7536irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Large-Scale Broadband Measurement Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAS112 provides a mechanism for handling reverse lookups on IP addresses that are not unique (e.g., RFC 1918 addresses). This document describes modifications to the deployment and use of AS112 infrastructure that will allow zones to be added and dropped much more easily, using DNAME resource records.

This approach makes it possible for any DNS zone administrator to sink traffic relating to parts of the global DNS namespace under their control to the AS112 infrastructure without coordination with the operators of AS112 infrastructure.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationmTwitter, Inc.dnamejB. Dickson¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelsonddatej’~hfilenamegrfc7535eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129358hkeywordsÇcDNSkroot serverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUS¿grankingocrfcd7535irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAS112 Redirection Using DNAMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMany sites connected to the Internet make use of IPv4 addresses that are not globally unique. Examples are the addresses designated in RFC 1918 for private use within individual sites.

Devices in such environments may occasionally originate Domain Name System (DNS) queries (so-called "reverse lookups") corresponding to those private-use addresses. Since the addresses concerned have only local significance, it is good practice for site administrators to ensure that such queries are answered locally. However, it is not uncommon for such queries to follow the normal delegation path in the public DNS instead of being answered within the site.

It is not possible for public DNS servers to give useful answers to such queries. In addition, due to the wide deployment of private-use addresses and the continuing growth of the Internet, the volume of such queries is large and growing. The AS112 project aims to provide a distributed sink for such queries in order to reduce the load on the corresponding authoritative servers. The AS112 project is named after the Autonomous System Number (ASN) that was assigned to it.

This document describes the steps required to install a new AS112 node and offers advice relating to such a node's operation.

This document obsoletes RFC 6304.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationeOttIXdnamelW. Sotomayorddatej’êhfilenamegrfc7534eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129357hkeywordsÑeAS112cDNSkreverse DNSganycastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUS¿grankingncrfcd7534irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAS112 Nameserver OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document describes the administration protocol for a federated file system (FedFS) that enables file access and namespace traversal across collections of independently administered fileservers.  The protocol specifies a set of interfaces by which fileservers with different administrators can form a fileserver federation that provides a namespace composed of the file systems physically hosted on and exported by the constituent fileservers.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamejJ. Lentini¢kaffiliationkIBM AlmadendnameiR. Tewari¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamehC. Leverddatej‘;hfilenamegrfc7533eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129356hkeywordsÅvFederated File SystemskobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateUQ⁄grankingmcrfcd7533irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Administration Protocol for Federated File SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°This document describes a file system federation protocol that enables file access and namespace traversal across collections of independently administered fileservers.  The protocol specifies a set of interfaces by which fileservers with different administrators can form a fileserver federation that provides a namespace composed of the file systems physically hosted on and exported by the constituent fileservers.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamejJ. Lentini¢kaffiliationkIBM AlmadendnameiR. Tewari¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamehC. Leverddatej’#hfilenamegrfc7532eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129355hkeywordsÅvFederated File SystemskobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateUQŸgrankinglcrfcd7532irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Namespace Database (NSDB) Protocol for Federated File SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The Network File System (NFS) version 4 protocol is a distributed file system protocol that owes its heritage to NFS protocol version 2 (RFC 1094) and version 3 (RFC 1813). Unlike earlier versions, the NFS version 4 protocol supports traditional file access while integrating support for file locking and the MOUNT protocol. In addition, support for strong security (and its negotiation), COMPOUND operations, client caching, and internationalization has been added. Of course, attention has been applied to making NFS version 4 operate well in an Internet environment.

RFC 7530 formally obsoletes RFC 3530. This document, together with RFC 7530, replaces RFC 3530 as the definition of the NFS version 4 protocol.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlPrimary DatadnameiT. Haynes¢kaffiliationdDelldnameiD. NoveckddatejŒ±hfilenamegrfc7531eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129354hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateUQÿgrankingkcrfcd7531irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Network File System (NFS) Version 4 External Data Representation Standard (XDR) DescriptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThe Network File System (NFS) version 4 protocol is a distributed file system protocol that builds on the heritage of NFS protocol version 2 (RFC 1094) and version 3 (RFC 1813). Unlike earlier versions, the NFS version 4 protocol supports traditional file access while integrating support for file locking and the MOUNT protocol. In addition, support for strong security (and its negotiation), COMPOUND operations, client caching, and internationalization has been added. Of course, attention has been applied to making NFS version 4 operate well in an Internet environment.

This document, together with the companion External Data Representation (XDR) description document, RFC 7531, obsoletes RFC 3530 as the definition of the NFS version 4 protocol.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlPrimary DatadnameiT. Haynes¢kaffiliationdDelldnameiD. NoveckddatejŒæhfilenamegrfc7530eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129353hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateUQ◊grankingjcrfcd7530irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Network File System (NFS) Version 4 ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7931d8587∂habstractyCThis document defines extensions to the Internet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification (iCalendar) (RFC 5545) to support use of non-Gregorian recurrence rules.  It also defines how Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) (RFC 4791) servers and clients can be extended to support these new recurrence rules.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamekG. Yakushevddatej’ñhfilenamegrfc7529eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalextdfullxcalext - Calendaring ExtensionsdnamevCalendaring Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129352hkeywordsÑkcalendaringiiCalendardiTIPfCalDAVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUC‘grankingicrfcd7529irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoNon-Gregorian Recurrence Rules in the Internet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification (iCalendar)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Hybrid Broadcast Broadband TV (HbbTV) Association for naming persistent resources defined within HbbTV specifications.  Example resources include technical documents and specifications, Extensible Markup Language (XML) Schemas, classification schemes, XML Document Type Definitions (DTDs), namespaces, style sheets, media assets, and other types of resources produced or managed by HbbTV.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqHbbTV AssociationdnamehP. Higgs¢kaffiliationqHbbTV AssociationdnamejJ. PiesingddatejŒ›hfilenamegrfc7528eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129351hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU:¡˘grankinghcrfcd7528irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Hybrid Broadcast Broadband TV (HbbTV) AssociationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhIPv6 Loopback Suppression and Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) are discussed in Appendix A of RFC 4862.  That specification mentions a hardware-assisted mechanism to detect looped back DAD messages.  If hardware cannot suppress looped back DAD messages, a software solution is required.  Several service provider communities have expressed a need for automated detection of looped back Neighbor Discovery (ND) messages used by DAD.  This document includes mitigation techniques and outlines the Enhanced DAD algorithm to automate the detection of looped back IPv6 ND messages used by DAD.  For network loopback tests, the Enhanced DAD algorithm allows IPv6 to self-heal after a loopback is placed and removed.  Further, for certain access networks, this document automates resolving a specific duplicate address conflict.  This document updates RFCs 4429, 4861, and 4862.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehR. Asati¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehH. Singh¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiW. Beebee¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationx%Lawrence Berkeley National LaboratorydnamegE. Dart¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiW. Georgeddatej’ñhfilenamegrfc7527eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129350hkeywordsÇmAutomated DADrloopback detectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU:¡˘grankinggcrfcd7527irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Enhanced Duplicate Address DetectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyLExperience with the 6to4 transition mechanism defined in RFC 3056 ("Connection of IPv6 Domains via IPv4 Clouds") has shown that the mechanism is unsuitable for widespread deployment and use in the Internet when used in its anycast mode.  Therefore, this document requests that RFC 3068 ("An Anycast Prefix for 6to4 Relay Routers") and RFC 6732 ("6to4 Provider Managed Tunnels") be made obsolete and moved to Historic status.  It recommends that future products should not support 6to4 anycast and that existing deployments should be reviewed.  This complements the guidelines in RFC 6343.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. CarpenterddatejŒ◊hfilenamegrfc7526eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129349hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateU\´ÿgrankingfcrfcd7526irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberƒetotaletitlex5Deprecating the Anycast Prefix for 6to4 Relay RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTransport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) are widely used to protect data exchanged over application protocols such as HTTP, SMTP, IMAP, POP, SIP, and XMPP.  Over the last few years, several serious attacks on TLS have emerged, including attacks on its most commonly used cipher suites and their modes of operation.  This document provides recommendations for improving the security of deployed services that use TLS and DTLS.  The recommendations are applicable to the majority of use cases.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfIntuitdnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationeNICTAdnamegR. Holz¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’çhfilenamegrfc7525eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129348hkeywordsÖxTransport Layer SecuritycTLSdDTLStSecure Sockets LayercSSLkobsoletedByÅd9325epagesopublicationDateUH!)grankingecrfcd7525irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmRecommendations for Secure Use of Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyGThis document describes procedures for building inter-area point-to-multipoint (P2MP) segmented service label switched paths (LSPs) by partitioning such LSPs into intra-area segments and using BGP as the inter-area routing and Label Distribution Protocol (LDP).  Within each IGP area, the intra-area segments are either carried over intra-area P2MP LSPs, using P2MP LSP hierarchy, or instantiated using ingress replication.  The intra-area P2MP LSPs may be signaled using P2MP RSVP-TE or P2MP multipoint LDP (mLDP).  If ingress replication is used within an IGP area, then (multipoint-to-point) LDP LSPs or (point-to-point) RSVP-TE LSPs may be used in the IGP area.  The applications/services that use such inter-area service LSPs may be BGP Multicast VPN, Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) multicast, or global table multicast over MPLS.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationfArktandnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehT. Morin¢kaffiliationfOrangednamemI. Grosclaude¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegS. SaadddatejŒ”hfilenamegrfc7524eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129347hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateUSÿ“grankingdcrfcd7524irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKInter-Area Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Segmented Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8534∂habstractx£This specification defines the use of a JSON Web Token (JWT) Bearer Token as a means for requesting an OAuth 2.0 access token as well as for client authentication.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamelC. Mortimoreddatej’Ñhfilenamegrfc7523eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129346hkeywordsÖeOAuthcJWTiAssertioneTokennSecurity TokenkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU[À&grankingccrfcd7523irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYJSON Web Token (JWT) Profile for OAuth 2.0 Client Authentication and Authorization GrantsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¿This specification defines the use of a Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) 2.0 Bearer Assertion as a means for requesting an OAuth 2.0 access token as well as for client authentication.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamelC. Mortimore¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej’Öhfilenamegrfc7522eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129345hkeywordsÉeOAuthdSAMLiAssertionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU[À&grankingbcrfcd7522irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrSecurity Assertion Markup Language (SAML) 2.0 Profile for OAuth 2.0 Client Authentication and Authorization GrantsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—This specification provides a framework for the use of assertions with OAuth 2.0 in the form of a new client authentication mechanism and a new authorization grant type. Mechanisms are specified for transporting assertions during interactions with a token endpoint; general processing rules are also specified.

The intent of this specification is to provide a common framework for OAuth 2.0 to interwork with other identity systems using assertions and to provide alternative client authentication mechanisms.

Note that this specification only defines abstract message flows and processing rules. In order to be implementable, companion specifications are necessary to provide the corresponding concrete instantiations.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationjSalesforcednamelC. Mortimore¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiY. Golandddatej’Ñhfilenamegrfc7521eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129344hkeywordsÑeOAuthdSAMLcJWTiAssertionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU[À%grankingacrfcd7521irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPAssertion Framework for OAuth 2.0 Client Authentication and Authorization GrantsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document contains a set of examples using JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE) technology to protect data.  These examples present a representative sampling of JSON Web Key (JWK) objects as well as various JSON Web Signature (JWS) and JSON Web Encryption (JWE) results given similar inputs.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Millerddatej’|hfilenamegrfc7520eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129343hkeywordsçx"JSON Object Signing and EncryptiondJOSExJavaScript Object NotationdJSONrJSON Web SignaturecJWSsJSON Web EncryptioncJWElJSON Web KeycJWKsJSON Web AlgorithmscJWAhCookbookkobsoletedByÄepagesxopublicationDateU[À$granking`crfcd7520irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNExamples of Protecting Content Using JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†JSON Web Token (JWT) is a compact, URL-safe means of representing claims to be transferred between two parties.  The claims in a JWT are encoded as a JSON object that is used as the payload of a JSON Web Signature (JWS) structure or as the plaintext of a JSON Web Encryption (JWE) structure, enabling the claims to be digitally signed or integrity protected with a Message Authentication Code (MAC) and/or encrypted.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationcNRIdnamekN. Sakimuraddatej’Ñhfilenamegrfc7519eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129342hkeywordsëiAssertioneClaimnSecurity TokenxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONnJSON Web TokencJWTx"JSON Object Signing and EncryptiondJOSErJSON Web SignaturecJWSsJSON Web EncryptioncJWElJSON Web KeycJWKsJSON Web AlgorithmscJWAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU[À$granking_crfcd7519irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletJSON Web Token (JWT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8725d7797∂habstractxThis specification registers cryptographic algorithms and identifiers to be used with the JSON Web Signature (JWS), JSON Web Encryption (JWE), and JSON Web Key (JWK) specifications.  It defines several IANA registries for these identifiers.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. JonesddatejŒªhfilenamegrfc7518eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129341hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDateU[À#granking^crfcd7518irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSON Web Algorithms (JWA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9864∂habstractyîA JSON Web Key (JWK) is a JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) data structure that represents a cryptographic key.  This specification also defines a JWK Set JSON data structure that represents a set of JWKs.  Cryptographic algorithms and identifiers for use with this specification are described in the separate JSON Web Algorithms (JWA) specification and IANA registries established by that specification.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jonesddatej’Éhfilenamegrfc7517eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129340hkeywordsåxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONx"JSON Object Signing and EncryptiondJOSErJSON Web SignaturecJWSsJSON Web EncryptioncJWElJSON Web KeycJWKsJSON Web AlgorithmscJWAkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateU[À"granking]crfcd7517irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlerJSON Web Key (JWK)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØJSON Web Encryption (JWE) represents encrypted content using JSON-based data structures.  Cryptographic algorithms and identifiers for use with this specification are described in the separate JSON Web Algorithms (JWA) specification and IANA registries defined by that specification.  Related digital signature and Message Authentication Code (MAC) capabilities are described in the separate JSON Web Signature (JWS) specification.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemJ. Hildebrandddatej’hfilenamegrfc7516eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129339hkeywordsåxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONx"JSON Object Signing and EncryptiondJOSErJSON Web SignaturecJWSsJSON Web EncryptioncJWElJSON Web KeycJWKsJSON Web AlgorithmscJWAkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateU[À!granking\crfcd7516irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSON Web Encryption (JWE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿JSON Web Signature (JWS) represents content secured with digital signatures or Message Authentication Codes (MACs) using JSON-based data structures.  Cryptographic algorithms and identifiers for use with this specification are described in the separate JSON Web Algorithms (JWA) specification and an IANA registry defined by that specification.  Related encryption capabilities are described in the separate JSON Web Encryption (JWE) specification.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationmPing IdentitydnamejJ. Bradley¢kaffiliationcNRIdnamekN. Sakimuraddatej’Ähfilenamegrfc7515eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129338hkeywordsåxJavaScript Object NotationdJSONx"JSON Object Signing and EncryptiondJOSErJSON Web SignaturecJWSsJSON Web EncryptioncJWElJSON Web KeycJWKsJSON Web AlgorithmscJWAkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateU[Àgranking[crfcd7515irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSON Web Signature (JWS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxThis document proposes a new ICMP message that a router or host may use to advise a host to reduce the rate at which it sends, in cases where the host ignores other signals provided by packet loss and Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCAIDAdnameiM. LuckieddatejŒ‡hfilenamegrfc7514eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-60751hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUAûgrankingZcrfcd7514irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.Really Explicit Congestion Notification (RECN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document specifies the procedure for creating a binding between a DHCPv4/DHCPv6-assigned IP address and a binding anchor on a Source Address Validation Improvement (SAVI) device.  The bindings set up by this procedure are used to filter packets with forged source IP addresses.  This mechanism complements BCP 38 (RFC 2827) ingress filtering, providing finer-grained source IP address validation.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnTsinghua Univ.dnameeJ. Bi¢kaffiliationnTsinghua Univ.dnameeJ. Wu¢kaffiliationnTsinghua Univ.dnamefG. Yao¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehF. Bakerddatej’çhfilenamegrfc7513eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsavidfullx-savi - Source Address Validation Improvementsdnamex&Source Address Validation Improvementsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129337hkeywordsÅiSAVI-DHCPkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateUh‚{grankingYcrfcd7513irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Source Address Validation Improvement (SAVI) Solution for DHCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This memo specifies a PKCS #11 Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) Scheme for identifying PKCS #11 objects stored in PKCS #11 tokens and also for identifying PKCS #11 tokens, slots, or libraries.  The URI scheme is based on how PKCS #11 objects, tokens, slots, and libraries are identified in "PKCS #11 v2.20: Cryptographic Token Interface Standard".fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamekJ. Pechanec¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnameiD. Moffatddatej’àhfilenamegrfc7512eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129336hkeywordsÉfPKCS11gPKCS-11gPKCS#11kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU8o÷grankingXcrfcd7512irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewThe PKCS #11 URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx’This document specifies a new routing scheme for the current version of the Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) in the spirit of "Green IT", whereby packets will be routed to get as much fresh-air time as possible.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Wilhelmddatej’óhfilenamegrfc7511eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-60750hkeywordsÅhgreen itkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUAùgrankingWcrfcd7511irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlewScenic Routing for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document specifies an IP-based encapsulation for MPLS, called MPLS-in-UDP for situations where UDP (User Datagram Protocol) encapsulation is preferred to direct use of MPLS, e.g., to enable UDP-based ECMP (Equal-Cost Multipath) or link aggregation.  The MPLS- in-UDP encapsulation technology must only be deployed within a single network (with a single network operator) or networks of an adjacent set of cooperating network operators where traffic is managed to avoid congestion, rather than over the Internet where congestion control is required.  Usage restrictions apply to MPLS-in-UDP usage for traffic that is not congestion controlled and to UDP zero checksum usage with IPv6.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeX. Xu¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehN. Sheth¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamegL. Yong¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Callon¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehD. Blackddatej’ãhfilenamegrfc7510eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129335hkeywordsádMPLScUDPfTunnelhChecksummencapsulationimultipathdECMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU"¡grankingVcrfcd7510irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEncapsulating MPLS in UDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) block that allows reporting of a post-repair loss count metric for a range of RTP applications.  In addition, another metric, repaired loss count, is also introduced in this report block for calculating the pre-repair loss count when needed, so that the RTP sender or a third-party entity is able to evaluate the effectiveness of the repair methods used by the system.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huang¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamehV. SinghddatejŒ‘hfilenamegrfc7509eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-129334hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUS¿grankingUcrfcd7509irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) for Post-Repair Loss Count MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document describes how the S/MIME protocol can be extended in order to secure message header fields defined in RFC 5322.  This technology provides security services such as data integrity, non-repudiation, and confidentiality.  This extension is referred to as 'Secure Headers'.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfDGA MIdnamekL. Cailleux¢kaffiliationdIECAdnamejC. Bonattiddatej’ãhfilenamegrfc7508eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129333hkeywordsÅnsecure headerskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU8o’grankingTcrfcd7508irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex"Securing Header Fields with S/MIMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!This document defines a Signaling Cipher Suite Value (SCSV) that prevents protocol downgrade attacks on the Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocols.  It updates RFCs 2246, 4346, 4347, 5246, and 6347.  Server update considerations are included.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejB. Moeller¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejA. LangleyddatejŒ‘hfilenamegrfc7507eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129332hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8996epagesopublicationDateU:¡˜grankingScrfcd7507irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZTLS Fallback Signaling Cipher Suite Value (SCSV) for Preventing Protocol Downgrade AttacksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyãRFC 4379 defines the MPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping/Traceroute mechanism in which the Router Alert Option (RAO) MUST be set in the IP header of the MPLS Echo Request messages and may conditionally be set in the IP header of the MPLS Echo Reply messages depending on the Reply Mode used. While a generic "Router shall examine packet" Option Value is used for the IPv4 RAO, there is no generic RAO value defined for IPv6 that can be used. This document allocates a new, generic IPv6 RAO value that can be used by MPLS Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) tools, including the MPLS Echo Request and MPLS Echo Reply messages for MPLS in IPv6 environments. Consequently, it updates RFC 4379.

The initial motivation to request an IPv6 RAO value for MPLS OAM comes from the MPLS LSP Ping/Traceroute. However, this value is applicable to all MPLS OAM and not limited to MPLS LSP Ping/ Traceroute.fadNamepDeborah Brungarddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataroddatej’éhfilenamegrfc7506eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129331hkeywordsÉdIPv6hLSP PinghMPLS OAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU4P˙grankingRcrfcd7506irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSIPv6 Router Alert Option for MPLS Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyInternet mail determines the address of a receiving server through the DNS, first by looking for an MX record and then by looking for an A/AAAA record as a fallback.  Unfortunately, this means that the A/AAAA record is taken to be mail server address even when that address does not accept mail.  The No Service MX RR, informally called "null MX", formalizes the existing mechanism by which a domain announces that it accepts no mail, without having to provide a mail server; this permits significant operational efficiencies.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levine¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnameiM. Delanyddatej’Dhfilenamegrfc7505eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129330hkeywordsÇcDNSfe-mailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUì(TgrankingQcrfcd7505irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFA "Null MX" No Service Resource Record for Domains That Accept No MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis memo defines two Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) reply codes, 521 and 556.  The 521 code was originally described in an Experimental RFC in 1995 and is in wide use, but has not previously been formally incorporated into SMTP.  The 556 code was created to support the new tests and actions specified in RFC 7505.  These codes are used to indicate that an Internet host does not accept incoming mail at all.  This specification is not applicable when the host sometimes accepts mail but may reject particular messages, or even all messages, under specific circumstances.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej’öhfilenamegrfc7504eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129329hkeywordsÑjReply codeeEmailfServergNo MailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUì(QgrankingPcrfcd7504irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSMTP 521 and 556 Reply CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&OSPFv3 is a candidate for deployments in environments where autoconfiguration is a requirement.  One such environment is the IPv6 home network where users expect to simply plug in a router and have it automatically use OSPFv3 for intra-domain routing.  This document describes the necessary mechanisms for OSPFv3 to be self-configuring.  This document updates RFC 5340 by relaxing the HelloInterval/ RouterDeadInterval checking during OSPFv3 adjacency formation and adding hysteresis to the update of self-originated Link State Advertisements (LSAs).fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRedbackdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. ArkkoddatejŒ…hfilenamegrfc7503eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129328hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU"¿grankingOcrfcd7503irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSPFv3 AutoconfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document provides a methodology for benchmarking the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) performance of devices.  Terminology related to benchmarking SIP devices is described in the companion terminology document (RFC 7501).  Using these two documents, benchmarks can be obtained and compared for different types of devices such as SIP Proxy Servers, Registrars, and Session Border Controllers.  The term "performance" in this context means the capacity of the Device Under Test (DUT) to process SIP messages.  Media streams are used only to study how they impact the signaling behavior.  The intent of the two documents is to provide a normalized set of tests that will enable an objective comparison of the capacity of SIP devices.  Test setup parameters and a methodology are necessary because SIP allows a wide range of configurations and operational conditions that can influence performance benchmark measurements.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Illinois Institute of TechnologydnameiC. Davids¢kaffiliationx"Bell Laboratories / Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationtAllot CommunicationsdnamekS. PoretskyddatejŒÕhfilenamegrfc7502eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-129327hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU,R˙grankingNcrfcd7502irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlMethodology for Benchmarking Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Devices: Basic Session Setup and RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document provides a terminology for benchmarking the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) performance of devices.  Methodology related to benchmarking SIP devices is described in the companion methodology document (RFC 7502).  Using these two documents, benchmarks can be obtained and compared for different types of devices such as SIP Proxy Servers, Registrars, and Session Border Controllers.  The term "performance" in this context means the capacity of the Device Under Test (DUT) to process SIP messages.  Media streams are used only to study how they impact the signaling behavior.  The intent of the two documents is to provide a normalized set of tests that will enable an objective comparison of the capacity of SIP devices.  Test setup parameters and a methodology are necessary because SIP allows a wide range of configurations and operational conditions that can influence performance benchmark measurements.  A standard terminology and methodology will ensure that benchmarks have consistent definitions and were obtained following the same procedures.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Illinois Institute of TechnologydnameiC. Davids¢kaffiliationx"Bell Laboratories / Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationtAllot CommunicationsdnamekS. PoretskyddatejŒÃhfilenamegrfc7501eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-129326hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU,R˙grankingMcrfcd7501irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlTerminology for Benchmarking Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Devices: Basic Session Setup and RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxmThis document provides principles for the operation of Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) registries.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamejO. KolkmanddatejŒ◊hfilenamegrfc7500eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129325hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8720epagesopublicationDateU"¿grankingLcrfcd7500irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexQPrinciples for Operation of Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThe Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) protocol is limited to a total packet size of 4096 bytes.  Provisions exist for fragmenting large amounts of authentication data across multiple packets, via Access-Challenge packets.  No similar provisions exist for fragmenting large amounts of authorization data.  This document specifies how existing RADIUS mechanisms can be leveraged to provide that functionality.  These mechanisms are largely compatible with existing implementations, and they are designed to be invisible to proxies and "fail-safe" to legacy RADIUS Clients and Servers.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnameoA. Perez-Mendez¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnamenR. Marin-Lopez¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnametF. Pereniguez-Garcia¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnameoG. Lopez-Millan¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamehD. Lopez¢kaffiliationnNetwork RADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatej’åhfilenamegrfc7499eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129324hkeywordsÖfRADIUSiattributeiextensionmfragmentationechunkkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateU"ægrankingKcrfcd7499irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Support of Fragmentation of RADIUS PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document provides an overview of the issues associated with the deployment of service functions (such as firewalls, load balancers, etc.) in large-scale environments. The term "service function chaining" is used to describe the definition and instantiation of an ordered list of instances of such service functions, and the subsequent "steering" of traffic flows through those service functions.

The set of enabled service function chains reflects operator service offerings and is designed in conjunction with application delivery and service and network policy.

This document also identifies several key areas that the Service Function Chaining (SFC) working group will investigate to guide its architectural and protocol work and associated documents.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Quinn¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. Nadeauddatej’èhfilenamegrfc7498eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsfcdfullxsfc - Service Function ChainingdnamexService Function Chainingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129323hkeywordsÉxservice function chaininghsteeringcsfckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU,R˘grankingJcrfcd7498irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Problem Statement for Service Function ChainingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This memo presents a problem statement for access rate measurement for test protocols to measure IP Performance Metrics (IPPM).  Key rate measurement test protocol aspects include the ability to control packet characteristics on the tested path, such as asymmetric rate and asymmetric packet size.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’Öhfilenamegrfc7497eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129322hkeywordsÇoInternet accessvAsymmetric Packet SizekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU"ΩgrankingIcrfcd7497irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexARate Measurement Test Protocol Problem Statement and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines two additional policies for the Partially Reliable Stream Control Transmission Protocol (PR-SCTP) extension.  These policies allow limitation of the number of retransmissions and prioritization of user messages for more efficient usage of the send buffer.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationx#Metafinanz Informationssysteme GmbHdnamemR. Seggelmann¢kaffiliationmNetflix, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. LoretoddatejŒœhfilenamegrfc7496eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129321hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU,R˘grankingHcrfcd7496irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Additional Policies for the Partially Reliable Stream Control Transmission Protocol ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*The Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) is an XML data representation framework for sharing information about computer security incidents. In IODEF, the Reference class provides references to externally specified information such as a vulnerability, Intrusion Detection System (IDS) alert, malware sample, advisory, or attack technique. In practice, these references are based on external enumeration specifications that define both the enumeration format and the specific enumeration values, but the IODEF Reference class (as specified in IODEF v1 in RFC 5070) does not indicate how to include both of these important pieces of information.

This document establishes a stand-alone data format to include both the external specification and specific enumeration identification value, and establishes an IANA registry to manage external enumeration specifications. While this document does not update IODEF v1, this enumeration reference format is used in IODEF v2 and is applicable to other formats that support this class of enumeration references.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcCISdnamelA. Montville¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. Blackddatej’ähfilenamegrfc7495eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-129320hkeywordsÖeIODEFhIncidentiReferencekEnumerationfFormatkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateU›grankingGcrfcd7495irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXEnumeration Reference Format for the Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThe Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) protocol binding for IEEE 802.11 defines two Medium Access Control (MAC) modes for IEEE 802.11 Wireless Transmission Points (WTPs): Split and Local MAC.  In the Split MAC mode, the partitioning of encryption/decryption functions is not clearly defined.  In the Split MAC mode description, IEEE 802.11 encryption is specified as located in either the Access Controller (AC) or the WTP, with no clear way for the AC to inform the WTP of where the encryption functionality should be located.  This leads to interoperability issues, especially when the AC and WTP come from different vendors.  To prevent interoperability issues, this specification defines an IEEE 802.11 MAC Profile message element in which each profile specifies an unambiguous division of encryption functionality between the WTP and AC.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegC. Shao¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemR. Pazhyannur¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegF. Bari¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationpSoftBank TelecomdnamemS. Matsushimaddatej’åhfilenamegrfc7494eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129319hkeywordsÑfCAPWAPkMAC ProfilejEncryptionkIEEE 802.11kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU4P¯grankingFcrfcd7494irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoIEEE 802.11 Medium Access Control (MAC) Profile for Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxƒI-JSON (short for "Internet JSON") is a restricted profile of JSON designed to maximize interoperability and increase confidence that software can process it successfully with predictable results.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsTextuality ServicesdnamegT. Brayddatej’åhfilenamegrfc7493eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjsondfullx!json - JavaScript Object NotationdnamexJavaScript Object Notationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129318hkeywordsÇdJSONmInternet JSONkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUÔAgrankingEcrfcd7493irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe I-JSON Message FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx⁄This document analyzes the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol according to the guidelines set forth in Section 4.2 of RFC 6518, "Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design Guidelines".fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnIonos NetworksdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Zhang¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnameoM. Jethanandaniddatej’ãhfilenamegrfc7492eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129317hkeywordsÜcBFDdKARPnreplay attacksxcryptographic authenticationhsecuritykDoS attackskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateU	⁄’grankingDcrfcd7492irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexóAnalysis of Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) Security According to the Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”Services such as content distribution, distributed databases, or inter-data center connectivity place a set of new requirements on the operation of networks. They need on-demand and application-specific reservation of network connectivity, reliability, and resources (such as bandwidth) in a variety of network applications (such as point-to-point connectivity, network virtualization, or mobile back-haul) and in a range of network technologies from packet (IP/MPLS) down to optical. An environment that operates to meet these types of requirements is said to have Application-Based Network Operations (ABNO). ABNO brings together many existing technologies and may be seen as the use of a toolbox of existing components enhanced with a few new elements.

This document describes an architecture and framework for ABNO, showing how these components fit together. It provides a cookbook of existing technologies to satisfy the architecture and meet the needs of the applications.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrelddatej’âhfilenamegrfc7491eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129316hkeywordsÑx!Software-Defined Networking (SDN)xPath Computation Element (PCE)rNetwork managementsNetwork programmingkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateUÔAgrankingCcrfcd7491irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAA PCE-Based Architecture for Application-Based Network OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‰This document describes an extension to the basic IP fast reroute mechanism, described in RFC 5286, that provides additional backup connectivity for point-to-point link failures when none can be provided by the basic mechanisms.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationwIndependent ContributordnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmVinci SystemsdnameeN. SoddatejŒŒhfilenamegrfc7490eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129315hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU8o‘grankingBcrfcd7490irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Remote Loop-Free Alternate (LFA) Fast Reroute (FRR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty@Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC) is a scalable mechanism by which a mail-originating organization can express domain-level policies and preferences for message validation, disposition, and reporting, that a mail-receiving organization can use to improve mail handling.

Originators of Internet Mail need to be able to associate reliable and authenticated domain identifiers with messages, communicate policies about messages that use those identifiers, and report about mail using those identifiers. These abilities have several benefits: Receivers can provide feedback to Domain Owners about the use of their domains; this feedback can provide valuable insight about the management of internal operations and the presence of external domain name abuse.

DMARC does not produce or encourage elevated delivery privilege of authenticated email. DMARC is a mechanism for policy distribution that enables increasingly strict handling of messages that fail authentication checks, ranging from no action, through altered delivery, up to message rejection.fadNamelPete ResnickgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawy¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnameiE. Zwickyddatej’Ühfilenamegrfc7489eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129314hkeywordsâfdomaineemailhsecurityimessagingddkimcspfnauthenticationireportingkconformancekobsoletedByÉd9989d9990d9991epagesIopublicationDateU	⁄”grankingAcrfcd7489irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGDomain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance (DMARC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8616d8553∂habstractx’This document specifies the behavior to be followed by a Port Control Protocol (PCP) client to contact its PCP server(s) when one or several PCP server IP addresses are configured.

