083epagesNopublicationDateU †granking;crfcd7483irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?JSON Responses for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document describes uniform patterns to construct HTTP URLs that may be used to retrieve registration information from registries (including both Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) and Domain Name Registries (DNRs)) using "RESTful" web access patterns.  These uniform patterns define the query syntax for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP).fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdARINdnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej’Çhfilenamegrfc7482eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfweirdsdfullx:weirds - Web Extensible Internet Registration Data Servicednamex1Web Extensible Internet Registration Data Servicedtypebwgbidjdoc-129307hkeywordsÅeWHOISkobsoletedByÅd9082epagesopublicationDateU ügranking:crfcd7482irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) Query FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractySThe Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP) provides "RESTful" web services to retrieve registration metadata from Domain Name and Regional Internet Registries.  This document describes information security services, including access control, authentication, authorization, availability, data confidentiality, and data integrity for RDAP.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegN. Kongddatej’zhfilenamegrfc7481eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfweirdsdfullx:weirds - Web Extensible Internet Registration Data Servicednamex1Web Extensible Internet Registration Data Servicedtypebwgbidjdoc-129306hkeywordsÇdRDAPhSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU ûgranking9crfcd7481irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber_etotaletitlexBSecurity Services for the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractybThis document is one of a collection that together describes the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP).  It describes how RDAP is transported using the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).  RDAP is a successor protocol to the very old WHOIS protocol.  The purpose of this document is to clarify the use of standard HTTP mechanisms for this application.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdARINdnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamekB. Ellacott¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegN. Kongddatej’yhfilenamegrfc7480eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfweirdsdfullx:weirds - Web Extensible Internet Registration Data Servicednamex1Web Extensible Internet Registration Data Servicedtypebwgbidjdoc-129305hkeywordsÇhRegistryeWHOISkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU úgranking8crfcd7480irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber_etotaletitlex:HTTP Usage in the Registration Data Access Protocol (RDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxøThe Ed25519 signature algorithm has been implemented in OpenSSH.  This document updates the IANA "SSHFP RR Types for public key algorithms" registry by adding an algorithm number for Ed25519.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. MoonesamyddatejŒÀhfilenamegrfc7479eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129304hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTˇì]granking7crfcd7479irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Using Ed25519 in SSHFP Resource RecordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes web-based real-time communication use cases. Requirements on the browser functionality are derived from the use cases.

This document was developed in an initial phase of the work with rather minor updates at later stages. It has not really served as a tool in deciding features or scope for the WG's efforts so far. It is being published to record the early conclusions of the WG. It will not be used as a set of rigid guidelines that specifications and implementations will be held to in the future.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelS. Hakansson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Erikssonddatej’áhfilenamegrfc7478eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfrtcwebdfullx0rtcweb - Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdnamex'Real-Time Communication in WEB-browsersdtypebwgbidjdoc-129303hkeywordsÑfwebrtcgbrowseriwebsocketireal-timekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUDÃgranking6crfcd7478irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Web Real-Time Communication Use Cases and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@This document specifies how a child zone in the DNS can publish a
record to indicate to a parental agent that the parental agent may
copy and process certain records from the child zone.  The existence
of the record and any change in its value can be monitored by a
parental agent and acted on depending on local policy.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmParsons, Inc.dnamekW. HardakerddatejCxhfilenamegrfc7477eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129302hkeywordsÅ`kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateUDÀgranking5crfcd7477irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Child-to-Parent Synchronization in DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9975¥habstractyÒThis document aims at establishing a common understanding about a set of scenarios that can be used as a base for the evaluation of different information-centric networking (ICN) approaches so that they can be tested and compared against each other while showcasing their own advantages. Towards this end, we review the ICN literature and document scenarios which have been considered in previous performance evaluation studies. We discuss a variety of aspects that an ICN solution can address. This includes general aspects, such as, network efficiency, reduced complexity, increased scalability and reliability, mobility support, multicast and caching performance, real-time communication efficiency, energy consumption frugality, and disruption and delay tolerance. We detail ICN-specific aspects as well, such as information security and trust, persistence, availability, provenance, and location independence.

This document is a product of the IRTF Information-Centric Networking Research Group (ICNRG).gauthorsà¢kaffiliationdEICTdnamenK. Pentikousis¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Ohlman¢kaffiliationvUniversidade de AveirodnameiD. Corujo¢kaffiliationsPolitecnico di BaridnameiG. Boggia¢kaffiliationx!Queen Mary / University of LondondnamehG. Tyson¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationeUNIRCdnamekA. Molinaro¢kaffiliationdNICTdnamefS. EumddatejŒ¬hfilenamegrfc7476eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeicnrgdfullx&icnrg - Information-Centric NetworkingdnamexInformation-Centric Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-129301hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateUD…granking4crfcd7476irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex2Information-Centric Networking: Baseline ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx±This document removes a limit on the number of Area Directors who manage an Area in the definition of "IETF Area".  This document updates RFC 2026 (BCP 9) and RFC 2418 (BCP 25).fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejS. DawkinsddatejŒ≈hfilenamegrfc7475eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129300hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTˇì]granking3crfcd7475irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber	etotaletitlex7Increasing the Number of Area Directors in an IETF AreadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·The current OSPFv2 cryptographic authentication mechanism as defined in RFCs 2328 and 5709 is vulnerable to both inter-session and intra- session replay attacks when using manual keying. Additionally, the existing cryptographic authentication mechanism does not cover the IP header. This omission can be exploited to carry out various types of attacks.

This document defines changes to the authentication sequence number mechanism that will protect OSPFv2 from both inter-session and intra- session replay attacks when using manual keys for securing OSPFv2 protocol packets. Additionally, we also describe some changes in the cryptographic hash computation that will eliminate attacks resulting from OSPFv2 not protecting the IP header.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnIonos NetworksdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamehD. Zhang¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Lindemddatej’áhfilenamegrfc7474eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129299hkeywordsÑdOSPFxcryptographic authenticationhsecuritynreplay attackskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU,R˜granking2crfcd7474irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Security Extension for OSPFv2 When Using Manual Key ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#There is no capability negotiation done for Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) applications that set up Label Switched Paths (LSPs) for IP prefixes or that signal point-to-point (P2P) Pseudowires (PWs) for Layer 2 Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs).  When an LDP session comes up, an LDP speaker may unnecessarily advertise its local state for such LDP applications even when the peer session is established for some other applications like Multipoint LDP (mLDP) or the Inter-Chassis Communication Protocol (ICCP).  This document defines a solution by which an LDP speaker announces to its peer its disinterest in such non-negotiated applications, thus disabling the unnecessary advertisement of corresponding application state, which would have otherwise been advertised over the established LDP session.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. BoutrosddatejŒ∆hfilenamegrfc7473eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129298hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTˇì\granking1crfcd7473irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCControlling State Advertisements of Non-negotiated LDP ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8223∂habstracty∫This document defines the Internet Printing Protocol (IPP) over HTTPS transport binding and the corresponding 'ipps' URI scheme, which is used to designate the access to the network location of a secure IPP print service or a network resource managed by such a service.

This document defines an alternate IPP transport binding to that defined in the original IPP URL Scheme (RFC 3510), but this document does not update or obsolete RFC 3510.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoHigh North Inc.dnamekI. McDonald¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehM. SweetddatejŒ¿hfilenamegrfc7472eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129297hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTˆeïgranking0crfcd7472irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWInternet Printing Protocol (IPP) over HTTPS Transport Binding and the 'ipps' URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynIn certain networks, such as, but not limited to, financial information networks (e.g., stock market data providers), network performance information (e.g., link propagation delay) is becoming critical to data path selection.

This document describes common extensions to RFC 3630 "Traffic Engineering (TE) Extensions to OSPF Version 2" and RFC 5329 "Traffic Engineering Extensions to OSPF Version 3" to enable network performance information to be distributed in a scalable fashion. The information distributed using OSPF TE Metric Extensions can then be used to make path selection decisions based on network performance.

Note that this document only covers the mechanisms by which network performance information is distributed. The mechanisms for measuring network performance information or using that information, once distributed, are outside the scope of this document.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamelS. Giacalone¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. PrevididdatejŒƒhfilenamegrfc7471eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129296hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTˆeîgranking/crfcd7471irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/OSPF Traffic Engineering (TE) Metric ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) is used to convey path computation requests and responses both between Path Computation Clients (PCCs) and Path Computation Elements (PCEs) and between cooperating PCEs. In PCEP, the path computation requests carry details of the constraints and objective functions that the PCC wishes the PCE to apply in its computation.

This document defines a facility to carry vendor-specific information in PCEP using a dedicated object and a new Type-Length-Value (TLV) that can be carried in any PCEP object that supports TLVs.

This document obsoletes RFC 7150. The only changes from that document are a clarification of the use of the new Type-Length-Value and the allocation of a different code point for the VENDOR-INFORMATION object.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. FarrelddatejŒªhfilenamegrfc7470eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129295hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTˆeìgranking.crfcd7470irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Conveying Vendor-Specific Constraints in the Path Computation Element Communication ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9752∂habstractyrThis document defines a new HTTP header that allows web host operators to instruct user agents to remember ("pin") the hosts' cryptographic identities over a period of time.  During that time, user agents (UAs) will require that the host presents a certificate chain including at least one Subject Public Key Info structure whose fingerprint matches one of the pinned fingerprints for that host.  By effectively reducing the number of trusted authorities who can authenticate the domain during the lifetime of the pin, pinning may reduce the incidence of man-in-the-middle attacks due to compromised Certification Authorities.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehC. Evans¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiC. Palmer¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiR. Sleeviddatej’vhfilenamegrfc7469eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfwebsecdfulluwebsec - Web SecuritydnamelWeb Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129294hkeywordsÅcpinkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU4P˜granking-crfcd7469irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Public Key Pinning Extension for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThis document describes and discusses the textual encodings of the Public-Key Infrastructure X.509 (PKIX), Public-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS), and Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  The textual encodings are well-known, are implemented by several applications and libraries, and are widely deployed.  This document articulates the de facto rules by which existing implementations operate and defines them so that future implementations can interoperate.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationmPenango, Inc.dnamejS. LeonardddatejŒ¬hfilenamegrfc7468eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129293hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU4PÙgranking,crfcd7468irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Textual Encodings of PKIX, PKCS, and CMS StructuresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This document allocates a formal Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for assignment by the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), as specified in RFC 3406.  At this time, the URN will be used primarily to uniquely identify Extensible Markup Language (XML) artefacts that provide information about NATO message text formats and service specifications as described in various NATO standards, instructions, and publications.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNATO C&I AgencydnamejA. MurdockddatejÃÌhfilenamegrfc7467eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129292hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU:v]granking+crfcd7467irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?URN Namespace for the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The link quality mechanism of the Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) enables "ignoring" some 1-hop neighbors if the measured link quality from that 1-hop neighbor is below an acceptable threshold while still retaining the corresponding link information as acquired from the HELLO message exchange. This allows immediate reinstatement of the 1-hop neighbor if the link quality later improves sufficiently.

NHDP also collects information about symmetric 2-hop neighbors. However, it specifies that if a link from a symmetric 1-hop neighbor ceases being symmetric, including while "ignored" (as described above), then corresponding symmetric 2-hop neighbors are removed. This may lead to symmetric 2-hop neighborhood information being permanently removed (until further HELLO messages are received) if the link quality of a symmetric 1-hop neighbor drops below the acceptable threshold, even if only for a moment.

This specification updates RFC 6130 "Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP)" and RFC 7181 "The Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2 (OLSRv2)" to permit, as an option, retaining, but ignoring, symmetric 2-hop information when the link quality from the corresponding 1-hop neighbor drops below the acceptable threshold. This allows immediate reinstatement of the symmetric 2-hop neighbor if the link quality later improves sufficiently, thus making the symmetric 2-hop neighborhood more "robust".fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxNLIX / Ecole PolytechniqueonednamejT. Clausenddatej’áhfilenamegrfc7466eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129291hkeywordsÑeMANETdNHDPfOLSRv2llink qualitykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateTˇì[granking*crfcd7466irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\An Optimization for the Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÒThis document requires that Transport Layer Security (TLS) clients and servers never negotiate the use of RC4 cipher suites when they establish connections.  This applies to all TLS versions.  This document updates RFCs 5246, 4346, and 2246.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamehA. Popovddatej’Ñhfilenamegrfc7465eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129290hkeywordsÇcTLSxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÂ⁄granking)crfcd7465irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexProhibiting RC4 Cipher SuitesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyCThis document describes the JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) text sequence format and associated media type "application/json-seq".  A JSON text sequence consists of any number of JSON texts, all encoded in UTF-8, each prefixed by an ASCII Record Separator (0x1E), and each ending with an ASCII Line Feed character (0x0A).fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlCryptonectordnamekN. Williamsddatej’Éhfilenamegrfc7464eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjsondfullx!json - JavaScript Object NotationdnamexJavaScript Object Notationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129289hkeywordsÖdJSONhsequencefonlineistreaminghlog filekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÓJcgranking(crfcd7464irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Text SequencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document describes the requirements and implementation of a group telephony feature commonly known as Bridged Line Appearance (BLA) or Multiple Line Appearance (MLA), or Shared Call/Line Appearance (SCA).  When implemented using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), it is referred to as shared appearances of an Address of Record (AOR) since SIP does not have the concept of lines.  This feature is commonly offered in IP Centrex services and IP Private Branch Exchange (IPBX) offerings and is likely to be implemented on SIP IP telephones and SIP feature servers used in a business environment.  This feature allows several user agents (UAs) to share a common AOR, learn about calls placed and received by other UAs in the group, and pick up or join calls within the group.  This document discusses use cases, lists requirements, and defines extensions to implement this feature.  This specification updates RFCs 3261 and 4235.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationuSylantro Systems CorpdnameoM. Soroushnejad¢kaffiliationuSylantro Systems CorpdnameqV. VenkataramananddatejŒhfilenamegrfc7463eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeblissdfullx8bliss - Basic Level of Interoperability for SIP Servicesdnamex0Basic Level of Interoperability for SIP Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129288hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesHopublicationDateU›granking'crfcd7463irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQShared Appearances of a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Address of Record (AOR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) supports the capability to provide a reference to a specific rendering to be used by the User Agent (UA) as an alerting signal (e.g., a ring tone or ringback tone) when the user is alerted. This is done using the Alert-Info header field. However, the reference (typically a URL) addresses only a specific network resource with specific rendering properties. There is currently no support for standard identifiers for describing the semantics of the alerting situation or the characteristics of the alerting signal, without being tied to a particular rendering. To overcome these limitations and support new applications, a new family of URNs for use in Alert-Info header fields (and situations with similar requirements) is defined in this specification.

This document normatively updates RFC 3261, which defines the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP). It changes the usage of the Alert-Info header field defined in RFC 3261 by additionally allowing its use in any non-100 provisional response to INVITE. This document also permits proxies to add or remove an Alert-Info header field and to add or remove Alert-Info header field values.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamehL. Liess¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiR. Jesske¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationgAriadnednameiD. Worley¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejP. KyzivatddatejŒµhfilenamegrfc7462eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesaluddfullx%salud - Sip ALerting for User DevicesdnamexSip ALerting for User Devicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129287hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateUﬁ^granking&crfcd7462irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMURNs for the Alert-Info Header Field of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx◊This document defines a subset of a Management Information Base (MIB) for energy management of devices.  The module addresses device identification, context information, and the energy relationships between devices.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejJ. Parello¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameoM. Chandramouliddatej’Åhfilenamegrfc7461eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdemandfullxeman - Energy ManagementdnameqEnergy Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129286hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basexENERGY-OBJECT-CONTEXT-MIBxIANA-ENERGY-RELATION-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateUdgranking%crfcd7461irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEnergy Object Context MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxsThis document defines a subset of the Management Information Base (MIB) for power and energy monitoring of devices.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameoM. Chandramouli¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamelB. Schoening¢kaffiliationpNEC Europe, Ltd.dnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationpNEC Europe, Ltd.dnamehT. Dietzddatej’Åhfilenamegrfc7460eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdemandfullxeman - Energy ManagementdnameqEnergy Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129285hkeywordsÑxmanagement information baseuIANAPowerStateSet-MIBqENERGY-OBJECT-MIBtPOWER-ATTRIBUTES-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDateUc˜granking$crfcd7460irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Monitoring and Control MIB for Power and EnergydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document defines key concepts of uncertainty and confidence as they pertain to location information. Methods for the manipulation of location estimates that include uncertainty information are outlined.

This document normatively updates the definition of location information representations defined in RFCs 4119 and 5491. It also deprecates related terminology defined in RFC 3693.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameoJ. WinterbottomddatejŒØhfilenamegrfc7459eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-129284hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateTÔ´Ágranking#crfcd7459irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnRepresentation of Uncertainty and Confidence in the Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWWith Wi-Fi emerging as a crucial access network for mobile service providers, it has become important to provide functions commonly available in 3G and 4G networks in Wi-Fi access networks as well. Such functions include Access Point Name (APN) Selection, multiple Packet Data Network (PDN) connections, and seamless mobility between Wi-Fi and 3G/4G networks.

The EAP Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA), and EAP-AKA', protocol is required for mobile devices to access the mobile Evolved Packet Core (EPC) via Wi-Fi networks. This document defines a few new EAP attributes to enable the above-mentioned functions in such networks. The attributes are exchanged between a client (such as a Mobile Node (MN)) and its network counterpart (such as an Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) server) in the service provider's infrastructure.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekR. Valmikam¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiR. Koodliddatej’Éhfilenamegrfc7458eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129283hkeywordsáoMobile Networksd3GPPcEAPcEPChHandoverhIdentitycAPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÔ´Êgranking"crfcd7458irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Attributes for Wi-Fi Integration with the Evolved Packet CoredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOOver the last few years, there have been several serious attacks on Transport Layer Security (TLS), including attacks on its most commonly used ciphers and modes of operation.  This document summarizes these attacks, with the goal of motivating generic and protocol-specific recommendations on the usage of TLS and Datagram TLS (DTLS).fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhPorticordnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationx Technische Universitaet MuenchendnamegR. Holz¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’Åhfilenamegrfc7457eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcutadfullxuta - Using TLS in ApplicationsdnamexUsing TLS in Applicationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129282hkeywordsáxTransport Layer SecuritycTLSlDatagram TLSdDTLStSecure Sockets LayercSSLpsecurity attackskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT”˙Ngranking!crfcd7457irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSSummarizing Known Attacks on Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram TLS (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrPerformance Monitoring (PM) is a key aspect of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM).  It allows network operators to verify the Service Level Agreement (SLA) provided to customers and to detect network anomalies.  This document specifies mechanisms for Loss Measurement and Delay Measurement in Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) networks.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoT. Senevirathne¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Kumar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatejŒßhfilenamegrfc7456eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129281hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateTˇìZgranking crfcd7456irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRLoss and Delay Measurement in Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔThis document specifies Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) fault management.  Methods in this document follow the CFM (Connectivity Fault Management) framework defined in IEEE 802.1 and reuse OAM tools where possible.  Additional messages and TLVs are defined for TRILL-specific applications or for cases where a different set of information is required other than CFM as defined in IEEE 802.1.  This document updates RFC 6325.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoT. Senevirathne¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegN. Finn¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. Kumar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Liddatej’shfilenamegrfc7455eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129280hkeywordsáeFaultjContinuitylConnectivitycOAMcCFMcMEPcCCMkobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDateTˇìYgrankingcrfcd7455irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Fault ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty⁄The Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is the protocol almost exclusively used in the Internet to exchange routing information between network domains. Due to this central nature, it is important to understand the security measures that can and should be deployed to prevent accidental or intentional routing disturbances.

This document describes measures to protect the BGP sessions itself such as Time to Live (TTL), the TCP Authentication Option (TCP-AO), and control-plane filtering. It also describes measures to better control the flow of routing information, using prefix filtering and automation of prefix filters, max-prefix filtering, Autonomous System (AS) path filtering, route flap dampening, and BGP community scrubbing.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Durand¢kaffiliationcNILdnamelI. Pepelnjak¢kaffiliationhSpaceNetdnamejG. DoeringddatejŒ∫hfilenamegrfc7454eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129279hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÂ⁄grankingcrfcd7454irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber¬etotaletitlexBGP Operations and SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes additional managed objects and textual conventions for tunnels, identifiers, and Label Switching Routers to support Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) MIB modules for transport networks.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiDell Inc.dnamemV. Mahalingam¢kaffiliationfRedeemdnamejK. Sampath¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. NadeauddatejŒΩhfilenamegrfc7453eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129278hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDateTÓJ`grankingcrfcd7453irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Traffic Engineering (TE) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyWThe term "Internet of Things" (IoT) denotes a trend where a large number of embedded devices employ communication services offered by Internet protocols. Many of these devices, often called "smart objects", are not directly operated by humans but exist as components in buildings or vehicles, or are spread out in the environment. Following the theme "Everything that can be connected will be connected", engineers and researchers designing smart object networks need to decide how to achieve this in practice.

This document offers guidance to engineers designing Internet- connected smart objects.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. McPhersonddatej’whfilenamegrfc7452eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129277hkeywordsÇmIAB StatementmSmart ObjectskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU\/grankingcrfcd7452irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex7Architectural Considerations in Smart Object NetworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThe Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) includes features to add functionality by extending the protocol.  It does not, however, describe how those extensions are managed.  This document describes a procedure for the registration and management of extensions to EPP, and it specifies a format for an IANA registry to record those extensions.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmVerisign LabsdnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej’Åhfilenamegrfc7451eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfeppextdfullx4eppext - Extensible Provisioning Protocol Extensionsdnamex+Extensible Provisioning Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129276hkeywordsÉfdomaindhostgcontactkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT–^xgrankingcrfcd7451irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Extension Registry for the Extensible Provisioning ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThis document describes Automatic Multicast Tunneling (AMT), a protocol for delivering multicast traffic from sources in a multicast-enabled network to receivers that lack multicast connectivity to the source network. The protocol uses UDP encapsulation and unicast replication to provide this functionality.

The AMT protocol is specifically designed to support rapid deployment by requiring minimal changes to existing network infrastructure.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemG. Bumgardnerddatej’hfilenamegrfc7450eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-129275hkeywordsãcAMTfIGMPv2fIGMPv3eMLDv1eMLDv2cASMcSSMkamt gatewayiamt relayumulticast replicationwmulticast encapsulationkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDateTÂ—grankingcrfcd7450irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAutomatic Multicast TunnelingdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8777d9601∂habstractyZThis memo provides application-specific requirements for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for the support of Wavelength Switched Optical Networks (WSONs).  Lightpath provisioning in WSONs requires a Routing and Wavelength Assignment (RWA) process.  From a path computation perspective, wavelength assignment is the process of determining which wavelength can be used on each hop of a path and forms an additional routing constraint to optical light path computation.  Requirements for PCEP extensions in support of optical impairments will be addressed in a separate document.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationeAcreodnamemJ. Martensson¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takeda¢kaffiliationdKDDIdnamelT. Tsuritani¢kaffiliationjTelefonicadnamesO. Gonzalez de DiosddatejŒπhfilenamegrfc7449eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129274hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT–^wgrankingcrfcd7449irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexîPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Requirements for Wavelength Switched Optical Network (WSON) Routing and Wavelength AssignmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThis document records the transfer of responsibility for the Ethernet-related MIB modules DOT3-OAM-MIB, SNMP-REPEATER-MIB, POWER-ETHERNET-MIB, DOT3-EPON-MIB, EtherLike-MIB, EFM-CU-MIB, ETHER-WIS, and MAU-MIB from the IETF to the IEEE 802.3 Working Group (WG).  This document also describes the procedures associated with the transfer in a similar way to how RFC 4663 records the transfer of the IETF Bridge MIB work to the IEEE 802.1 WG.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtPT Taylor ConsultingdnameiT. Taylor¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanuddatej’~hfilenamegrfc7448eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129273hkeywordsÇhEthernetdIEEEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT‹Ì#grankingcrfcd7448irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/MIB Transfer from the IETF to the IEEE 802.3 WGdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThe BGP Entropy Label Capability attribute is defined in RFC 6790.  Regrettably, it has a bug: although RFC 6790 mandates that routers incapable of processing Entropy Labels must remove the attribute, fulfillment of this requirement cannot be guaranteed in practice.  This specification deprecates the attribute.  A forthcoming document will propose a replacement.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. KompelladdatejŒΩhfilenamegrfc7447eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129272hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT–^vgrankingcrfcd7447irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Deprecation of BGP Entropy Label Capability AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This document provides a model of information needed by the Routing and Wavelength Assignment (RWA) process in Wavelength Switched Optical Networks (WSONs).  The purpose of the information described in this model is to facilitate constrained optical path computation in WSONs.  This model takes into account compatibility constraints between WSON signal attributes and network elements but does not include constraints due to optical impairments.  Aspects of this information that may be of use to other technologies utilizing a GMPLS control plane are discussed.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejW. Imajukuddatej’hfilenamegrfc7446eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129271hkeywordsÇdWSONcRWAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT‹Ìgrankingcrfcd7446irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Routing and Wavelength Assignment Information Model for Wavelength Switched Optical NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document identifies a set of failure cases that may be encountered by IPv6-enabled mobile customers in roaming scenarios.  The analysis reveals that the failure causes include improper configurations, incomplete functionality support in equipment, and inconsistent IPv6 deployment strategies between the home and the visited networks.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegG. Chen¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationuRogers CommunicationsdnamejD. Michaud¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadairddatej’hfilenamegrfc7445eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129270hkeywordsÉnMobile NetworkjDual StackiIPv6-onlykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTˇìXgrankingcrfcd7445irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Analysis of Failure Cases in IPv6 Roaming ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyàThis document describes a header field, SIO-Label, for use in Internet email to convey the sensitivity of the message.  This header field may carry a textual representation (a display marking) and/or a structural representation (a security label) of the sensitivity of the message.  This document also describes a header field, SIO-Label-History, for recording changes in the message's label.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekK. Zeilenga¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’yhfilenamegrfc7444eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129269hkeywordsÜeemailmheader fieldscESSnSecurity LabelrConfidential LabelsMessage SensitivitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÂ«grankingcrfcd7444irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex!Security Labels in Internet EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) labels for Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) usages, such as Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) and NAT discovery, are defined in this document to allow an application layer to negotiate STUN usages within the Transport Layer Security (TLS) connection.  ALPN protocol identifiers defined in this document apply to both TLS and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS).fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Patil¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Reddy¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationrImpedance MismatchdnameqM. Petit-HugueninddatejŒªhfilenamegrfc7443eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtramdfullx"tram - TURN Revised and ModernizeddnamexTURN Revised and Modernizeddtypebwgbidjdoc-129268hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT¡–zgrankingcrfcd7443irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjApplication-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) Labels for Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) UsagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty÷When IP multicast trees created by Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) in Any-Source Multicast (ASM) mode need to pass through an MPLS domain, it may be desirable to map such trees to Point-to-Multipoint Label Switched Paths (P2MP LSPs).  This document describes how to accomplish this in the case where such P2MP LSPs are established using Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) Extensions for P2MP and Multipoint-to-Multipoint LSPs: Multipoint LDP (mLDP).fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationfArktandnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeR. LiddatejŒ∂hfilenamegrfc7442eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129267hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT–^tgrankingcrfcd7442irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇCarrying Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) in Any-Source Multicast (ASM) Mode Trees over Multipoint LDP (mLDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLMany service providers offer "BGP/MPLS IP VPN" service to their customers.  Existing IETF standards specify the procedures and protocols that a service provider uses in order to offer this service to customers who have IP unicast and IP multicast traffic in their VPNs.  It is also desirable to be able to support customers who have MPLS multicast traffic in their VPNs.  This document specifies the procedures and protocol extensions that are needed to support customers who use the Multipoint LDP (mLDP) as the control protocol for their MPLS multicast traffic.  Existing standards do provide some support for customers who use mLDP, but only under a restrictive set of circumstances.  This document generalizes the existing support to include all cases where the customer uses mLDP, without any restrictions.  This document updates RFC 6514.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiU. JoordeddatejŒµhfilenamegrfc7441eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbessdfullxbess - BGP Enabled ServiceSdnametBGP Enabled ServiceSdtypebwgbidjdoc-129266hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateT¡–wgrankingcrfcd7441irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhEncoding Multipoint LDP (mLDP) Forwarding Equivalence Classes (FECs) in the NLRI of BGP MCAST-VPN RoutesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±The "Trivial File Transfer Protocol" (RFC 1350) is a simple, lockstep, file transfer protocol that allows a client to get or put a file onto a remote host. One of its primary uses is in the early stages of nodes booting from a Local Area Network (LAN). TFTP has been used for this application because it is very simple to implement. The employment of a lockstep scheme limits throughput when used on a LAN.

This document describes a TFTP option that allows the client and server to negotiate a window size of consecutive blocks to send as an alternative for replacing the single-block lockstep schema. The TFTP option mechanism employed is described in "TFTP Option Extension" (RFC 2347).fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeServadnamejP. MasottaddatejŒ≤hfilenamegrfc7440eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129265hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateT¡–ugrankingcrfcd7440irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevTFTP Windowsize OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis document reviews the Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) protocol suite in the context of IPv6 and identifies gaps that must be addressed in order to allow MPLS-related protocols and applications to be used with IPv6-only networks. This document is intended to focus on gaps in the standards defining the MPLS suite, and is not intended to highlight particular vendor implementations (or lack thereof) in the context of IPv6-only MPLS functionality.

In the data plane, MPLS fully supports IPv6, and MPLS labeled packets can be carried over IPv6 packets in a variety of encapsulations. However, support for IPv6 among MPLS control-plane protocols, MPLS applications, MPLS Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM), and MIB modules is mixed, with some protocols having major gaps. For most major gaps, work is in progress to upgrade the relevant protocols.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiW. George¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataroddatej’{hfilenamegrfc7439eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129264hkeywordsÜdMPLScLDPdIPv6dRSVPeL3VPNeL2VPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT≠∏Ågrankingcrfcd7439irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Gap Analysis for Operating IPv6-Only MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“There are scenarios in which an IP multicast tree traverses an MPLS domain.  In these scenarios, it can be desirable to convert the IP multicast tree "seamlessly" into an MPLS Multipoint Label Switched Path (MP-LSP) when it enters the MPLS domain, and then to convert it back to an IP multicast tree when it exits the MPLS domain.  Previous documents specify procedures that allow certain kinds of IP multicast trees (either Source-Specific Multicast trees or Bidirectional Multicast trees) to be attached to an MPLS Multipoint Label Switched Path (MP-LSP).  However, the previous documents do not specify procedures for attaching IP Any-Source Multicast trees to MP-LSPs, nor do they specify procedures for aggregating multiple IP multicast trees onto a single MP-LSP.  This document specifies the procedures to support these functions.  It does so by defining "wildcard" encodings that make it possible to specify, when setting up an MP- LSP, that a set of IP multicast trees, or a shared IP multicast tree, should be attached to that MP-LSP.  Support for non-bidirectional IP Any-Source Multicast trees is subject to certain applicability restrictions that are discussed in this document.  This document updates RFCs 6826 and 7246.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationoThomson ReutersdnamehA. Gulko¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiU. Joorde¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Tantsuraddatej’~hfilenamegrfc7438eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129263hkeywordsÇdmplsimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT∂ˆçgrankingcrfcd7438irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Multipoint LDP (mLDP) In-Band Signaling with WildcardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThe process by which the members of the IAB and IESG, and some members of the IAOC, are selected, confirmed, and recalled is specified in this document.  This document is a self-consistent, organized compilation of the process as it was known at the time of publication of RFC 3777, with various updates since that version was published.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’}hfilenamegrfc7437eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129262hkeywordsÑxInternet Architecture BoardxEngineering Steering GroupfnomcomdIAOCkobsoletedByÅd8713epages#opublicationDateT¨í±grankingcrfcd7437irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrIAB, IESG, and IAOC Selection, Confirmation, and Recall Process: Operation of the Nominating and Recall CommitteesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8318∂habstracty{A Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) is used to interconnect systems across a wide-area or metropolitan-area network, making it appear that they are on a private LAN. The systems that are interconnected may themselves be LAN switches. If, however, they are IP hosts or IP routers, certain simplifications to the operation of the VPLS are possible. We call this simplified type of VPLS an "IP-only LAN Service" (IPLS). In an IPLS, as in a VPLS, LAN interfaces are run in promiscuous mode, and frames are forwarded based on their destination Media Access Control (MAC) addresses. However, the maintenance of the MAC forwarding tables is done via signaling, rather than via the MAC address learning procedures specified in the IEEE's "Media Access Control (MAC) Bridges". This document specifies the protocol extensions and procedures for support of the IPLS service.

The original intent was to provide an alternate solution to VPLS for those Provider Edge (PE) routers that were not capable of learning MAC addresses through data plane. This became a non-issue with newer hardware. The concepts put forth by this document are still valuable and are adopted in one form or other by newer work such as Ethernet VPN in L2VPN working group and possible data center applications. At this point, no further action is planned to update this document and it is published simply as a historic record of the ideas.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkCinea Corp.dnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehG. HeronddatejŒ±hfilenamegrfc7436eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129261hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateT∂ˆågrankingcrfcd7436irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP-Only LAN Service (IPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document defines the concept "Opportunistic Security" in the context of communications protocols.  Protocol designs based on Opportunistic Security use encryption even when authentication is not available, and use authentication when possible, thereby removing barriers to the widespread use of encryption on the Internet.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiTwo SigmadnamekV. Dukhovniddatej’whfilenamegrfc7435eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129260hkeywordsÇnauthenticationjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT§¥(grankingcrfcd7435irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Opportunistic Security: Some Protection Most of the TimedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThe motivation and use cases for interworking and transporting User- to-User Information (UUI) from the ITU-T Digital Subscriber Signalling System No. 1 (DSS1) User-user information element within SIP are described in RFC 6567. As networks move to SIP, it is important that applications requiring this data can continue to function in SIP networks as well as have the ability to interwork with this ISDN service for end-to-end transparency. This document defines a usage (a new package called the ISDN UUI package) of the User-to-User header field to enable interworking with this ISDN service.

This document covers interworking with both public ISDN and private ISDN capabilities, so the potential interworking with QSIG will also be addressed.

The package is identified by the new value "isdn-uui" of the "purpose" header field parameter.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehK. Drage¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnstonddatej’{hfilenamegrfc7434eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcussdfullx'cuss - Call Control UUI Service for SIPdnamex Call Control UUI Service for SIPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129259hkeywordsÅxUUS Supplementary ServicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT¡–ugranking
crfcd7434irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Interworking ISDN Call Control User Information with SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThere is a class of applications that benefit from using SIP to exchange User-to-User Information (UUI) data during session establishment.  This information, known as call control UUI data, is a small piece of data inserted by an application initiating the session and utilized by an application accepting the session.  The syntax and semantics for the UUI data used by a specific application are defined by a UUI package.  This UUI data is opaque to SIP and its function is unrelated to any basic SIP function.  This document defines a new SIP header field, User-to-User, to transport UUI data, along with an extension mechanism.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationtHuman CommunicationsdnamekJ. Raffertyddatej’ghfilenamegrfc7433eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcussdfullx'cuss - Call Control UUI Service for SIPdnamex Call Control UUI Service for SIPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129258hkeywordsÖcUUIgPackagegContenthEncodingeMediakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT¡–tgranking	crfcd7433irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIA Mechanism for Transporting User-to-User Call Control Information in SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¡This document describes procedures for BGP MPLS-based Ethernet VPNs (EVPN).  The procedures described here meet the requirements specified in RFC 7209 -- "Requirements for Ethernet VPN (EVPN)".fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationfArktandnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationiBloombergdnamehA. Isaac¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemW. HenderickxddatejŒ≠hfilenamegrfc7432eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129257hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateTÂ∆grankingcrfcd7432irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGP MPLS-Based Ethernet VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÖd9746d9161d8584d9572d9573∂habstractyÂAs IPTV deployments grow in number and size, service providers are looking for solutions that minimize the service disruption due to faults in the IP network carrying the packets for these services.  This document describes a mechanism for minimizing packet loss in a network when node or link failures occur.  Multicast-only Fast Reroute (MoFRR) works by making simple enhancements to multicast routing protocols such as Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) and Multipoint LDP (mLDP).fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehA. Karan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. DecraeneddatejŒ@hfilenamegrfc7431eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129256hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU÷VEgrankingcrfcd7431irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMulticast-Only Fast ReroutedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxvThis document analyzes the residual threats for Multipath TCP (MPTCP) and explores possible solutions to address them.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationiUCLouvaindnameiC. Paasch¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationiUCLouvaindnamenO. Bonaventure¢kaffiliationcUPBdnameiC. Raiciuddatej’ôhfilenamegrfc7430eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129255hkeywordsÉeMPTCPhsecurityothreat analysiskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateU∞ıgrankingcrfcd7430irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIAnalysis of Residual Threats and Possible Fixes for Multipath TCP (MPTCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document analyzes deployment practices of existing IP mobility protocols in a distributed mobility management environment.  It then identifies existing limitations when compared to the requirements defined for a distributed mobility management solution.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefD. Liu¢kaffiliationlInterDigitaldnamejJC. Zuniga¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehP. Seite¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Chan¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej’yhfilenamegrfc7429eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129254hkeywordsÖcDMMxDistributed Mobility Managementfanchorlgap analysisnbest practiceskobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateT¡–sgrankingcrfcd7429irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCDistributed Mobility Management: Current Practices and Gap AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx This document describes the frame format for transmission of IPv6 packets as well as a method of forming IPv6 link-local addresses and statelessly autoconfigured IPv6 addresses on ITU-T G.9959 networks.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmSigma DesignsdnameiA. Brandt¢kaffiliationmSigma DesignsdnamehJ. BuronddatejŒ∞hfilenamegrfc7428eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129253hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT–^rgrankingcrfcd7428irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Transmission of IPv6 Packets over ITU-T G.9959 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2) protocol has limited support for the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA).  The current version only includes support for three Elliptic Curve groups, and there is a fixed hash algorithm tied to each group.  This document generalizes IKEv2 signature support to allow any signature method supported by PKIX and also adds signature hash algorithm negotiation.  This is a generic mechanism and is not limited to ECDSA; it can also be used with other signature algorithms.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationhOpus OnednameiJ. Snyderddatej’xhfilenamegrfc7427eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129252hkeywordsãeIPseccIKEeIKEv2iSignaturenAuthenticationcRSAcDSScDSAeECDSAiSASSA-PSSdPKIXkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT¨íÆgrankingcrfcd7427irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGSignature Authentication in the Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÆSoftware-Defined Networking (SDN) refers to a new approach for network programmability, that is, the capacity to initialize, control, change, and manage network behavior dynamically via open interfaces.  SDN emphasizes the role of software in running networks through the introduction of an abstraction for the data forwarding plane and, by doing so, separates it from the control plane.  This separation allows faster innovation cycles at both planes as experience has already shown.  However, there is increasing confusion as to what exactly SDN is, what the layer structure is in an SDN architecture, and how layers interface with each other.  This document, a product of the IRTF Software-Defined Networking Research Group (SDNRG), addresses these questions and provides a concise reference for the SDN research community based on relevant peer-reviewed literature, the RFC series, and relevant documents by other standards organizations.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationdEICTdnamenK. Pentikousis¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamejS. Denazis¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salim¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamenO. Koufopavlouddatej’whfilenamegrfc7426eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesdnrgdfullx#sdnrg - Software Defined NetworkingdnamexSoftware Defined Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-129251hkeywordsÜxSoftware-defined NetworkingcSDNuProgrammable NetworkslArchitectureeLayerkTerminologykobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateT≠∏~grankingcrfcd7426irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexFSoftware-Defined Networking (SDN): Layers and Architecture TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty_This memo describes how to use Adobe's Secure Real-Time Media Flow Protocol (RTMFP) to transport the video, audio, and data messages of Adobe Flash platform communications.  Aspects of this application profile include cryptographic methods and data formats, flow metadata formats, and protocol details for client-server and peer-to-peer communication.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamemM. ThornburghddatejŒÆhfilenamegrfc7425eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129250hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateTöHâgrankingcrfcd7425irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-Adobe's RTMFP Profile for Flash CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤Demands on networking infrastructure are growing exponentially due to bandwidth-hungry applications such as rich media applications and inter-data-center communications.  In this context, it is important to optimally use the bandwidth in wired networks that extensively use link aggregation groups and equal-cost multipaths as techniques for bandwidth scaling.  This document explores some of the mechanisms useful for achieving this.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvBrocade CommunicationsdnamekR. Krishnan¢kaffiliationlHuawei U.S.AdnamegL. Yong¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationmVinci SystemsdnameeN. So¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamemB. KhasnabishddatejŒ¨hfilenamegrfc7424eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129249hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT∂ˆågranking␀crfcd7424irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Mechanisms for Optimizing Link Aggregation Group (LAG) and Equal-Cost Multipath (ECMP) Component Link Utilization in NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty“The Diameter base protocol provides facilities for protocol extensibility enabling the definition of new Diameter applications or modification of existing applications.  This document is a companion document to the Diameter base protocol that further explains and clarifies the rules to extend Diameter.  Furthermore, this document provides guidelines to Diameter application designers reusing/ defining Diameter applications or creating generic Diameter extensions.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnameiL. Morand¢kaffiliationnFluke NetworksdnamejV. Fajardo¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’shfilenamegrfc7423eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129248hkeywordsÑcAAAnAuthenticationmAuthorizationjAccountingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTfï6grankingˇcrfcd7423irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber¡etotaletitlex'Diameter Applications Design GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyìIn some instances, Service Providers (SPs) have a legal logging requirement to be able to map a subscriber's inside address with the address used on the public Internet (e.g., for abuse response).  Unfortunately, many logging solutions for Carrier-Grade NATs (CGNs) require active logging of dynamic translations.  CGN port assignments are often per connection, but they could optionally use port ranges.  Research indicates that per-connection logging is not scalable in many residential broadband services.  This document suggests a way to manage CGN translations in such a way as to significantly reduce the amount of logging required while providing traceability for abuse response.  IPv6 is, of course, the preferred solution.  While deployment is in progress, SPs are forced by business imperatives to maintain support for IPv4.  This note addresses the IPv4 part of the network when a CGN solution is in use.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiC. Donley¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamemC. Grundemann¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamejV. Sarawat¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamemK. Sundaresan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejO. VautrinddatejŒ≤hfilenamegrfc7422eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129247hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTöHÜgranking˛crfcd7422irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPDeterministic Address Mapping to Reduce Logging in Carrier-Grade NAT DeploymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸThe IPv6 unicast addressing format includes a separation between the prefix used to route packets to a subnet and the interface identifier used to specify a given interface connected to that subnet.  Currently, the interface identifier is defined as 64 bits long for almost every case, leaving 64 bits for the subnet prefix.  This document describes the advantages of this fixed boundary and analyzes the issues that would be involved in treating it as a variable boundary.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationtUniv. of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationjCEA / LISTdnamekA. Petrescu¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenA. YourtchenkoddatejŒ∞hfilenamegrfc7421eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129246hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT∂ˆägranking˝crfcd7421irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Analysis of the 64-bit Boundary in IPv6 AddressingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling of the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for communications between a Path Computation Client (PCC) and a Path Computation Element (PCE), or between two PCEs.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxBrocade Communications, Inc.dnamejA. Koushik¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejE. Stephan¢kaffiliationqHuawei TechnologydnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationjMetaswitchdnamekJ. Hardwickddatej’yhfilenamegrfc7420eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129245hkeywordsÜrNetwork ManagementxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2cPCEdPCEPkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateTöHÉgranking¸crfcd7420irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Management Information Base (MIB) ModuledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) requires that messages be transmitted at regular intervals and provides a way to negotiate the interval used by BFD peers. Some BFD implementations may be restricted to only support several interval values. When such BFD implementations speak to each other, there is a possibility of two sides not being able to find a common value for the interval to run BFD sessions.

This document updates RFC 5880 by defining a small set of interval values for BFD that we call "Common Intervals" and recommends implementations to support the defined intervals. This solves the problem of finding an interval value that both BFD speakers can support while allowing a simplified implementation as seen for hardware-based BFD. It does not restrict an implementation from supporting more intervals in addition to the Common Intervals.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehN. Akiya¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoM. Binderberger¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Mirskyddatej’vhfilenamegrfc7419eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129244hkeywordsÑcBFDhhardwarehintervaletimerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT§¥&granking˚crfcd7419irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Common Interval Support in Bidirectional Forwarding DetectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty6This document provides a high-level description of things for Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) participants to consider when bringing proposals for new research groups (RGs) into the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF).  This document emphasizes differences in expectations between the two organizations.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejS. Dawkinsddatej’xhfilenamegrfc7418eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129243hkeywordsÅnResearch GroupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTãéugranking˙crfcd7418irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex$An IRTF Primer for IETF ParticipantsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxŸThis document specifies extensions to Generic Aggregate RSVP (RFC 4860) for support of the Pre-Congestion Notification (PCN) Controlled Load (CL) and Single Marking (SM) edge behaviors over a Diffserv cloud using PCN.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekA. Bhargavaddatej’thfilenamegrfc7417eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129242hkeywordsÅvgeneric aggregate rsvpkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateTêÁqgranking˘crfcd7417irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrExtensions to Generic Aggregate RSVP for IPv4 and IPv6 Reservations over Pre-Congestion Notification (PCN) DomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document presents a security threat analysis for the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPLs).  The development builds upon previous work on routing security and adapts the assessments to the issues and constraints specific to low-power and lossy networks.  A systematic approach is used in defining and evaluating the security threats.  Applicable countermeasures are application specific and are addressed in relevant applicability statements.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationx%Eaton's Cooper Power Systems BusinessdnamegT. Tsao¢kaffiliationx%Eaton's Cooper Power Systems BusinessdnamelR. Alexander¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnameiM. Dohler¢kaffiliationxUniversitat Pompeu FabradnamegV. Daza¢kaffiliationxUniversitat Pompeu FabradnameiA. Lozano¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardsonddatej’uhfilenamegrfc7416eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129241hkeywordsÉcLLNdROLLhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateT´granking¯crfcd7416irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[A Security Threat Analysis for the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPLs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe prevalent use of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) in Next Generation Networks necessitates that SIP networks provide adequate control mechanisms to maintain transaction throughput by preventing congestion collapse during traffic overloads.  A loss-based solution to remedy known vulnerabilities of the SIP 503 (Service Unavailable) overload control mechanism has already been proposed.  Using the same signaling, this document proposes a rate-based control scheme to complement the loss-based control scheme.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamegE. Noel¢kaffiliationtBT Innovate & DesigndnamekP. WilliamsddatejŒ¨hfilenamegrfc7415eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsocdfullxsoc - SIP Overload ControldnametSIP Overload Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129240hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT‘,granking˜crfcd7415irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Rate ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»This document contains a roadmap to the Request for Comments (RFC) documents relating to the Internet's Transmission Control Protocol (TCP). This roadmap provides a brief summary of the documents defining TCP and various TCP extensions that have accumulated in the RFC series. This serves as a guide and quick reference for both TCP implementers and other parties who desire information contained in the TCP-related RFCs.

This document obsoletes RFC 4614.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationbF5dnamegM. Duke¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiR. Braden¢kaffiliationkMTI SystemsdnamegW. Eddy¢kaffiliationrInterrupt SciencesdnamejE. Blanton¢kaffiliationlNetApp, Inc.dnamemA. Zimmermannddatej’lhfilenamegrfc7414eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129239hkeywordsÅkTCP RoadmapkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateT–^ngrankingˆcrfcd7414irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIA Roadmap for Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) Specification DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7805∂habstractyThis document describes an experimental TCP mechanism called TCP Fast Open (TFO).  TFO allows data to be carried in the SYN and SYN-ACK packets and consumed by the receiving end during the initial connection handshake, and saves up to one full round-trip time (RTT) compared to the standard TCP, which requires a three-way handshake (3WHS) to complete before data can be exchanged.  However, TFO deviates from the standard TCP semantics, since the data in the SYN could be replayed to an application in some rare circumstances.  Applications should not use TFO unless they can tolerate this issue, as detailed in the Applicability section.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehY. Cheng¢kaffiliationfGooglednamefJ. Chu¢kaffiliationfGooglednamepS. Radhakrishnan¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegA. JainddatejŒ®hfilenamegrfc7413eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129238hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTìt‘grankingıcrfcd7413irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlemTCP Fast OpendtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document presents the basic network objectives for the behavior of Shared Mesh Protection (SMP) that are not based on control-plane support.  This document provides an expansion of the basic requirements presented in RFC 5654 ("Requirements of an MPLS Transport Profile") and RFC 6372 ("MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Survivability Framework").  This document provides requirements for any mechanism that would be used to implement SMP for MPLS-TP data paths, in networks that delegate protection switch coordination to the data plane.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamemY. Weingarten¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamefP. Pan¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Ryoo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. MirskyddatejŒ™hfilenamegrfc7412eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129237hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTãérgrankingÙcrfcd7412irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRequirements for MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Shared Mesh ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFFast handover protocols for Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) and Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) define mobility management procedures that support unicast communication at reduced handover latency. Fast handover base operations do not affect multicast communication and, hence, do not accelerate handover management for native multicast listeners. Many multicast applications like IPTV or conferencing, though, comprise delay-sensitive, real-time traffic and will benefit from fast handover completion. This document specifies extension of the Mobile IPv6 Fast Handovers (FMIPv6) and the Fast Handovers for Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PFMIPv6) protocols to include multicast traffic management in fast handover operations. This multicast support is provided first at the control plane by management of rapid context transfer between access routers and second at the data plane by optional fast traffic forwarding that may include buffering. An FMIPv6 access router indicates support for multicast using an updated Proxy Router Advertisements message format.

This document updates RFC 5568, "Mobile IPv6 Fast Handovers".fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationtlink-lab & FU BerlindnamelM. Waehlisch¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiR. Koodli¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefD. Liuddatej’whfilenamegrfc7411eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmultimobdfullxmultimob - Multicast MobilitydnamerMulticast Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129236hkeywordsÜiMulticasthMobilitydIPv6cPIMcMLDsGroup CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT{”tgrankingÛcrfcd7411irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcMulticast Listener Extensions for Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) and Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) Fast HandoversdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document updates RFC 7001 by creating a registry for property types in the Authentication-Results header field, used in email authentication work, rather than limiting participants to using the original, small set of fixed values.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’whfilenamegrfc7410eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129235hkeywordsÇvAuthentication-ResultsjReputationkobsoletedByÅd7601epagesopublicationDateTÅcgrankingÚcrfcd7410irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEA Property Types Registry for the Authentication-Results Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)Many network devices support parallel packet processing.  This document describes how Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) can model a network device's parallelization datapath using constructs defined by the ForCES model (RFC 5812) and controlled via the ForCES protocol (RFC 5810).fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpernddatej’uhfilenamegrfc7409eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129234hkeywordsÉfForCESeModeliExtensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT{”sgrankingÒcrfcd7409irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Packet ParallelizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo extends the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) model defined in RFC 5812 and updates that RFC to allow complex data types for metadata, optional default values for data types, and optional access types for structures.  It also fixes an issue with Logical Functional Block (LFB) inheritance and introduces two new features: a new event condition called eventBecomesEqualTo and LFB properties.  The changes introduced in this memo do not alter the protocol and retain backward compatibility with older LFB models.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidisddatej’rhfilenamegrfc7408eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129233hkeywordsÉfForCESeModeliExtensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT{”qgrankingcrfcd7408irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Model ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxTThis document defines a collection of YANG definitions for configuring SNMP engines.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. SchoenwaelderddatejŒ¶hfilenamegrfc7407eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129232hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateTíQvgrankingÔcrfcd7407irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A YANG Data Model for SNMP ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This document provides a problem statement, introduces terminology, and describes an extension for the base IETF emergency services architecture to address cases where an emergency caller is not authenticated, has no identifiable service provider, or has no remaining credit with which to pay for access to the network.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationnBlackBerry LtddnameiS. McCann¢kaffiliationhMediaTekdnamehG. Bajko¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationxSiemens Corporate TechnologydnamenD. KroeselbergddatejŒ™hfilenamegrfc7406eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129231hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTìt“grankingÓcrfcd7406irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkExtensions to the Emergency Services Architecture for Dealing With Unauthenticated and Unauthorized DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∞This document extends the base definition of ABNF (Augmented Backus-Naur Form) to include a way to specify US-ASCII string literals that are matched in a case-sensitive manner.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Kyzivatddatej’thfilenamegrfc7405eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129230hkeywordsÇcBNFkABNF SyntaxkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTöHgrankingÌcrfcd7405irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Case-Sensitive String Support in ABNFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔIn an IPv6 network, it is possible to use only link-local addresses on infrastructure links between routers.  This document discusses the advantages and disadvantages of this approach to facilitate the decision process for a given network.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelM. Behringer¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiE. Vynckeddatej’thfilenamegrfc7404eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129229hkeywordsÑuIPv6 security routingjLink-LocalpRouting ProtocolhSecuritykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateTfï6grankingÏcrfcd7404irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Using Only Link-Local Addressing inside an IPv6 NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<SIP already provides the ability to perform hop-by-hop traceroute for SIP messages using the Max-Forwards header field to determine the reachability path of requests to a target.  A mechanism for media-loopback calls has also been defined separately, which enables test calls to be generated that result in media being looped back to the originator.  This document describes a means of performing hop-by-hop traceroute-style test calls using the media-loopback mechanism to test the media path when SIP sessions go through media-relaying back-to-back user agents (B2BUAs).fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiH. KaplanddatejŒ©hfilenamegrfc7403eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestrawdfullx7straw - Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdnamex/Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdtypebwgbidjdoc-129228hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT\!^grankingÎcrfcd7403irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKA Media-Based Traceroute Function for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx«This memo specifies an Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) based mechanism for transmission of user data packets, to be used with the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  This document obsoletes RFC 5202.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnameiP. Jokela¢kaffiliationnHTT ConsultingdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamehJ. Melenddatej’shfilenamegrfc7402eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129227hkeywordsÇjencryptionquser data packetskobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateU&‰ÁgrankingÍcrfcd7402irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeUsing the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) Transport Format with the Host Identity Protocol (HIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡This document specifies the details of the Host Identity Protocol (HIP). HIP allows consenting hosts to securely establish and maintain shared IP-layer state, allowing separation of the identifier and locator roles of IP addresses, thereby enabling continuity of communications across IP address changes. HIP is based on a Diffie-Hellman key exchange, using public key identifiers from a new Host Identity namespace for mutual peer authentication. The protocol is designed to be resistant to denial-of-service (DoS) and man-in-the-middle (MitM) attacks. When used together with another suitable security protocol, such as the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP), it provides integrity protection and optional encryption for upper-layer protocols, such as TCP and UDP.

This document obsoletes RFC 5201 and addresses the concerns raised by the IESG, particularly that of crypto agility. It also incorporates lessons learned from the implementations of RFC 5201.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnHTT ConsultingdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationx!Hirschmann Automation and ControldnamegT. Heer¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnameiP. Jokela¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamelT. Hendersonddatej’}hfilenamegrfc7401eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129226hkeywordsÑcHIPnIP-layer statetintegrity protectionsoptional encryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesÄopublicationDateU&‰ÊgrankingÈcrfcd7401irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Host Identity Protocol Version 2 (HIPv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8002d9374∂habstractynRFC 6282 defines header compression in 6LoWPAN packets (where "6LoWPAN" refers to "IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network").  The present document specifies a simple addition that enables the compression of generic headers and header-like payloads, without a need to define a new header compression scheme for each such new header or header-like payload.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuUniversity Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej’rhfilenamegrfc7400eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129225hkeywordsícIoTrInternet of ThingsqEmbedded InternetnSensor NetworkcWSNpConstrained nodesConstrained networkxConstrained-node networkcLLNfLoWPANopacket encodingucapability indicationd6CIOdLZ77hRFC 6282hRFC 4944padaptation layermIEEE 802.15.4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTfï4grankingËcrfcd7400irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexj6LoWPAN-GHC: Generic Header Compression for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThe Path Computation Element (PCE) architecture is set out in RFC 4655. The architecture is extended for multi-layer networking with the introduction of the Virtual Network Topology Manager (VNTM) in RFC 5623 and generalized to Hierarchical PCE (H-PCE) in RFC 6805.

These three architectural views of PCE deliberately leave some key questions unanswered, especially with respect to the interactions between architectural components. This document draws out those questions and discusses them in an architectural context with reference to other architectural components, existing protocols, and recent IETF efforts.

This document does not update the architecture documents and does not define how protocols or components must be used. It does, however, suggest how the architectural components might be combined to provide advanced PCE function.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. Kingddatej’thfilenamegrfc7399eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129224hkeywordsäcSDNxSoftware Defined NetworkingeH-PCEpHierarchical PCEdVNTMx Virtual Network Topology ManagerdABNOx#Application-Based Network OperationbTEsTraffic EngineeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTT0grankingÁcrfcd7399irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAUnanswered Questions in the Path Computation Element ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This document defines a reference path for Large-scale Measurement of Broadband Access Performance (LMAP) and measurement points for commonly used performance metrics.  Other similar measurement projects may also be able to use the extensions described here for measurement point location.  The purpose is to create an efficient way to describe the location of the measurement point(s) used to conduct a particular measurement.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationbBTdnamelT. Burbridge¢kaffiliationhSamKnowsdnamekS. Crawford¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’uhfilenamegrfc7398eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129223hkeywordsÇdLMAPsperformance metricskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÂ√grankingÊcrfcd7398irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\A Reference Path and Measurement Points for Large-Scale Measurement of Broadband PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyµThis document provides a summary of a workshop on 'Smart Object Security' that took place in Paris on March 23, 2012. The main goal of the workshop was to allow participants to share their thoughts about the ability to utilize existing and widely deployed security mechanisms for smart objects.

This report summarizes the discussions and lists the conclusions and recommendations to the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Gilger¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’Zhfilenamegrfc7397eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129222hkeywordsÑmSmart ObjectsrInternet of ThingshWorkshophSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTãéngrankingÂcrfcd7397irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.Report from the Smart Object Security WorkshopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÌThis specification defines the JSON merge patch format and processing rules.  The merge patch format is primarily intended for use with the HTTP PATCH method as a means of describing a set of modifications to a target resource's content.darea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Snellddatej’xhfilenamegrfc7396eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbididoc-57664hkeywordsÑdhttpdjsonepatchemergekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateTT0granking‰crfcd7396irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlepJSON Merge PatchdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document defines a binding for the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) over a WebSocket transport layer.  A WebSocket binding for XMPP provides higher performance than the current HTTP binding for XMPP.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamehL. Stout¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejJ. Moffitt¢kaffiliationpcstar industriesdnamejE. Cestariddatej’rhfilenamegrfc7395eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxmppdfullx1xmpp - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129221hkeywordsÇiWebSocketdXMPPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTR’"granking„crfcd7395irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNAn Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) Subprotocol for WebSocketdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆLSP-Ping is a widely deployed Operation, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) mechanism in MPLS networks.  However, in the present form, this mechanism is inadequate to verify connectivity of a segment of a Multi-Segment Pseudowire (MS-PW) and/or bidirectional co-routed Label Switched Path (LSP) from any node on the path of the MS-PW and/or bidirectional co-routed LSP.  This document defines a TLV to address this shortcoming.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Saxena¢kaffiliationnIonos NetworksdnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies, Inc.dnameiS. AldrinddatejŒ®hfilenamegrfc7394eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129220hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTfï3granking‚crfcd7394irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Definition of Time to Live TLV for LSP-Ping MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty6This document focuses on the problems encountered when using dynamic DNS in address-sharing contexts (e.g., Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite) and Network Address and Protocol Translation from IPv6 Clients to IPv4 Servers (NAT64)) during IPv6 transition.  Both issues and possible solutions are documented in this memo.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamegX. Deng¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationx2Beijing University of Posts and TelecommunicationsdnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehJ. Huang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegC. Zhouddatej’shfilenamegrfc7393eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129219hkeywordsàoaddress sharingcCGNrservice continuitytservice availabilityvuser-generated contentvaddress-sharing issuesgDS-Litex service delivery in CGN contextskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT\!]granking·crfcd7393irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex;Using the Port Control Protocol (PCP) to Update Dynamic DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèWhen set up through an explicit path, dynamic Multi-Segment Pseudowires (MS-PWs) may be required to provide a simple solution for 1:1 protection with diverse primary and backup MS-PWs for a service, or to enable controlled signaling (strict or loose) for special MS-PWs.  This document specifies the extensions and procedures required to enable dynamic MS-PWs to be established along explicit paths.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehP. Dutta¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejL. Martiniddatej’qhfilenamegrfc7392eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-129218hkeywordsÉjPseudowireeMS-PWnexplicit routekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateTÅagranking‡crfcd7392irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Explicit Path Routing for Dynamic Multi-Segment PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆExperience in implementing and deploying the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) architecture has demonstrated the need for a few small extensions both to ease programmability and to improve wire efficiency of some transactions.  The ForCES protocol is extended with a table range operation and a new extension for error handling.  This document updates the semantics in RFCs 5810 and 7121 to achieve that end goal.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salimddatej’qhfilenamegrfc7391eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129217hkeywordsÉfForCEShProtocoliExtensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTT.grankingﬂcrfcd7391irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Protocol ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThe Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) is a specialized web transfer protocol for constrained devices and constrained networks.  It is anticipated that constrained devices will often naturally operate in groups (e.g., in a building automation scenario, all lights in a given room may need to be switched on/off as a group).  This specification defines how CoAP should be used in a group communication context.  An approach for using CoAP on top of IP multicast is detailed based on existing CoAP functionality as well as new features introduced in this specification.  Also, various use cases and corresponding protocol flows are provided to illustrate important concepts.  Finally, guidance is provided for deployment in various network topologies.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnameiA. Rahman¢kaffiliationpPhilips ResearchdnamegE. Dijkddatej’rhfilenamegrfc7390eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129216hkeywordsÑimulticastlIP multicastgRESTfulxInternet of Things (IoT)kobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateTR’ grankingﬁcrfcd7390irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCGroup Communication for the Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]This document specifies a method to split the control plane (CP) and user plane (UP) for a network infrastructure based on Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6).  Existing specifications allow a mobile access gateway (MAG) to separate its control and user plane using the Alternate Care-of Address mobility option for IPv6 or Alternate IPv4 Care-of Address option for IPv4.  However, the current specification does not provide any mechanism allowing the local mobility anchor (LMA) to perform an analogous functional split.  To remedy that shortcoming, this document specifies a mobility option enabling an LMA to provide an alternate LMA address to be used for the bidirectional user-plane traffic between the MAG and LMA.  With this new option, an LMA will be able to use an IP address for its user plane that is different than the IP address used for the control plane.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoSoftbank MobilednamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemR. Pazhyannur¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationnFuturewei Inc.dnamejC. Perkinsddatej’qhfilenamegrfc7389eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129215hkeywordsÉxControl and User Plane Splitx!Control and User Plane Separationx&LMA User-Plane Address Mobility OptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTR’granking›crfcd7389irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Separation of Control and User Plane for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˝This document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines objects for managing IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs).fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnameiA. Sehgal¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegC. Zhouddatej’qhfilenamegrfc7388eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymc6lodfullx66lo - IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdnamex0IPv6 over Networks of Resource-constrained Nodesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129214hkeywordsÑrNetwork ManagementxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTR’granking‹crfcd7388irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Definition of Managed Objects for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document describes an Ethernet-Tree (E-Tree) solution framework for supporting the Metro Ethernet Forum (MEF) E-Tree service over a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) network.  The objective is to provide a simple and effective approach to emulate E-Tree services in addition to Ethernet LAN (E-LAN) services on an existing MPLS network.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamefR. Key¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegL. Yong¢kaffiliationgTelstradnameiS. Delord¢kaffiliationiOrange CHdnameiF. Jounay¢kaffiliation`dnamefL. Jinddatej’phfilenamegrfc7387eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129213hkeywordsÑcmefletherhet lanee-lantmetro ethernet forumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTIu—granking€crfcd7387irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbA Framework for Ethernet Tree (E-Tree) Service over a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÌThis specification defines the JSON merge patch format and processing rules.  The merge patch format is primarily intended for use with the HTTP PATCH method as a means of describing a set of modifications to a target resource's content.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Snellddatej’ohfilenamegrfc7386eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129212hkeywordsÑdhttpdjsonepatchemergekobsoletedByÅd7396epages	opublicationDateT<^granking⁄crfcd7386irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlepJSON Merge PatchdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉRFC 6514 created a space of Tunnel Type code points for a new BGP attribute called the "P-Multicast Service Interface Tunnel (PMSI Tunnel) attribute". However, the RFC did not create a corresponding IANA registry.

There now is need to make further code point allocations from this name space. This document serves to update RFC 6514 in that it creates an IANA registry for that purpose.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. SwallowddatejÕ∑hfilenamegrfc7385eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129211hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT8FgrankingŸcrfcd7385irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNIANA Registry for P-Multicast Service Interface (PMSI) Tunnel Type Code PointsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8317d8338∂habstracty4As time and frequency distribution protocols are becoming increasingly common and widely deployed, concern about their exposure to various security threats is increasing.  This document defines a set of security requirements for time protocols, focusing on the Precision Time Protocol (PTP) and the Network Time Protocol (NTP).  This document also discusses the security impacts of time protocol practices, the performance implications of external security practices on time protocols, and the dependencies between other security services and time synchronization.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahiddatej’phfilenamegrfc7384eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymftictocdfullx8tictoc - Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdnamex/Timing over IP Connection and Transfer of Clockdtypebwgbidjdoc-129210hkeywordsÑcptpwprecision time protocolcntpunetwork time protocolkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateT@lgrankingÿcrfcd7384irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSecurity Requirements of Time Protocols in Packet Switched NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx„This document describes a way to avoid IP fragmentation of large Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) messages.  This allows IKEv2 messages to traverse network devices that do not allow IP fragments to pass through.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjELVIS-PLUSdnamejV. Smyslovddatej’phfilenamegrfc7383eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129209hkeywordsÑpIP fragmentationcNAThfirewallnPMTU discoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT\!\granking◊crfcd7383irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFInternet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Message FragmentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxÂThis document contains a template to be used for creating a Certification Practice Statement (CPS) for an organization that is part of the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), e.g., a resource allocation registry or an ISP.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegD. Kong¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamefK. SeoddatejŒûhfilenamegrfc7382eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129208hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateU8o–granking÷crfcd7382irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber≠etotaletitlexQTemplate for a Certification Practice Statement (CPS) for the Resource PKI (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty Enterprise network administrators worldwide are in various stages of preparing for or deploying IPv6 into their networks.  The administrators face different challenges than operators of Internet access providers and have reasons for different priorities.  The overall problem for many administrators will be to offer Internet- facing services over IPv6 while continuing to support IPv4, and while introducing IPv6 access within the enterprise IT network.  The overall transition will take most networks from an IPv4-only environment to a dual-stack network environment and eventually an IPv6-only operating mode.  This document helps provide a framework for enterprise network architects or administrators who may be faced with many of these challenges as they consider their IPv6 support strategies.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmDropbox, Inc.dnameoK. Chittimaneni¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiL. Howard¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamelV. Kuarsingh¢kaffiliationoHewlett PackarddnamekY. Pouffary¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiE. Vynckeddatej’ohfilenamegrfc7381eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129207hkeywordsÅx$IPV6 migration transition enterprisekobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateTIuœgranking’crfcd7381irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Enterprise IPv6 Deployment GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜An MPEG2 Transport Stream (TS) is a standard container format used in the transmission and storage of multimedia data.  Unicast/multicast MPEG2 TS over RTP is widely deployed in IPTV systems.  This document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) block that allows the reporting of MPEG2 TS decodability statistics metrics related to transmissions of MPEG2 TS over RTP.  The metrics specified in the RTCP XR block are related to Program Specific Information (PSI) carried in MPEG TS.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegJ. Tong¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnameeC. Bi¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huangddatej’phfilenamegrfc7380eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-129206hkeywordsÅjTR 101 290kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT\![granking‘crfcd7380irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex£RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block for MPEG2 Transport Stream (TS) Program Specific Information (PSI) Decodability Statistics Metrics ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThe IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol provides support for flow-level multipathing with rapid failover for both unicast and multi-destination traffic in networks with arbitrary topology.  Active-active connection at the TRILL edge is the extension of these characteristics to end stations that are multiply connected to a TRILL campus.  This informational document discusses the high-level problems and goals when providing active-active connection at the TRILL edge.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. Hao¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiJ. Hudson¢kaffiliationcJITdnamegH. ZhaiddatejŒ£hfilenamegrfc7379eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129205hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT@lgranking”crfcd7379irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyProblem Statement and Goals for Active-Active Connection at the Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) EdgedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®The trustworthiness of location information is critically important for some location-based applications, such as emergency calling or roadside assistance.

This document describes threats to conveying location, particularly for emergency calls, and describes techniques that improve the reliability and security of location information. It also provides guidelines for assessing the trustworthiness of location information.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. AbobaddatejŒ†hfilenamegrfc7378eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129204hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTíQtgranking“crfcd7378irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletTrustworthy LocationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThe IMAP4 specification allows the searching of only the selected mailbox.  A user often wants to search multiple mailboxes, and a client that wishes to support this must issue a series of SELECT and SEARCH commands, waiting for each to complete before moving on to the next.  This extension allows a client to search multiple mailboxes with one command, limiting the delays caused by many round trips and not requiring disruption of the currently selected mailbox.  This extension also uses MAILBOX, UIDVALIDITY, and TAG fields in ESEARCH responses, allowing a client to pipeline the searches if it chooses.  This document updates RFC 4466 and obsoletes RFC 6237.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’mhfilenamegrfc7377eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5apps