e AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamekJ. Petersonddatej’nhfilenamegrfc7375eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-129201hkeywordsÜcSIPxSecure Origin IdentificationvCommunication SecurityfRTCWebfThreatwReal-Time CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTIuŒgrankingœcrfcd7375irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Secure Telephone Identity Threat ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAREsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) does not define a generic service discovery mechanism as a part of the base protocol (RFC 6940).  This document defines how the Recursive Distributed Rendezvous (ReDiR) service discovery mechanism can be applied to RELOAD overlays to provide a generic service discovery mechanism.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Maenpaa¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej’Fhfilenamegrfc7374eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129200hkeywordsÑfP2PSIPeReDiRcP2PcDHTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTIuÕgrankingŒcrfcd7374irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDService Discovery Usage for REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxŸThis document defines UTF-8 representations for IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) abstract data types (ADTs) to support interoperable usage of the IPFIX Information Elements with protocols based on textual encodings.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammellddatej’nhfilenamegrfc7373eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-129199hkeywordsÇsinformation elementgunicodekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT+GgrankingÕcrfcd7373irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPTextual Representation of IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Abstract Data TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document registers code points to allow status codes to be returned to an email client to indicate that a message is being rejected or deferred specifically because of email authentication failures.

This document updates RFC 7208, since some of the code points registered replace the ones recommended for use in that document.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. KucherawyddatejŒühfilenamegrfc7372eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129198hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÉCgrankingÃcrfcd7372irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Email Authentication Status CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document updates the IPv6 multicast addressing architecture by redefining the reserved bits as generic flag bits. The document also provides some clarifications related to the use of these flag bits.

This document updates RFCs 3956, 3306, and 4291.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiS. Venaasddatej’nhfilenamegrfc7371eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129197hkeywordsÉxIPv6 Multicast Flag Bitsx$updated unicast-prefix-based addresssupdated Embedded-RPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateTÅ≤grankingÀcrfcd7371irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Updates to the IPv6 Multicast Addressing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document recommends some editorial changes to the IANA "IS-IS TLV Codepoints" registry to more accurately document the state of the protocol.  It also sets out new guidelines for Designated Experts to apply when reviewing allocations from the registry.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsbergddatej’nhfilenamegrfc7370eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129196hkeywordsÅiCodepointkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT+Ggranking crfcd7370irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Updates to the IS-IS TLV Codepoints RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9352∂habstractyvThe work related to GMPLS Ethernet Label Switching (GELS) extended GMPLS RSVP-TE to support the establishment of Ethernet Label Switching Paths (LSPs).  IEEE Ethernet Connectivity Fault Management (CFM) specifies an adjunct Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) flow to check connectivity in Ethernet networks.  CFM can also be used with Ethernet LSPs for fault detection and triggering recovery mechanisms.  The ITU-T Y.1731 specification builds on CFM and specifies additional OAM mechanisms, including Performance Monitoring, for Ethernet networks.  This document specifies extensions of the GMPLS RSVP-TE protocol to support the setup of the associated Ethernet OAM entities of Ethernet LSPs and defines the Ethernet technology-specific TLVs based on the GMPLS OAM Configuration Framework.  This document supports, but does not modify, the IEEE and ITU-T OAM mechanisms.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Takacs¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegB. Gero¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegH. Longddatej’jhfilenamegrfc7369eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129195hkeywordsÖdGELSxEthernet Label SwitchingfPBB-TEwconnectivity monitoringqOAM configurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT@lgranking…crfcd7369irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeGMPLS RSVP-TE Extensions for Ethernet Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis text describes evolving networking technology within residential home networks with increasing numbers of devices and a trend towards increased internal routing.  The goal of this document is to define a general architecture for IPv6-based home networking, describing the associated principles, considerations, and requirements.  The text briefly highlights specific implications of the introduction of IPv6 for home networking, discusses the elements of the architecture, and suggests how standard IPv6 mechanisms and addressing can be employed in home networking.  The architecture describes the need for specific protocol extensions for certain additional functionality.  It is assumed that the IPv6 home network is not actively managed and runs as an IPv6-only or dual-stack network.  There are no recommendations in this text for the IPv4 part of the network.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationmSigma DesignsdnameiA. Brandt¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednamegJ. Weilddatej’khfilenamegrfc7368eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghomenetdfullxhomenet - Home NetworkingdnameoHome Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129194hkeywordsÅdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateTJﬁ^granking»crfcd7368irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,IPv6 Home Networking Architecture PrinciplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects for configuring aspects of the Simplified Multicast Forwarding (SMF) process for Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs).  The SMF-MIB module also reports state information, performance information, and notifications.  In addition to configuration, the additional state and performance information is useful to operators troubleshooting multicast forwarding problems.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnUS Army CERDECdnamegR. Cole¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnameiJ. Macker¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnamejB. Adamsonddatej’mhfilenamegrfc7367eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129193hkeywordsárNetwork ManagementxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2gRoutingeMANETiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateT8oÈgranking«crfcd7367irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextDefinition of Managed Objects for the Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Simplified Multicast Framework Relay Set ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_This document describes a means of negotiating the use of the encrypt-then-MAC security mechanism in place of the existing MAC-then-encrypt mechanism in Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS).  The MAC-then-encrypt mechanism has been the subject of a number of security vulnerabilities over a period of many years.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamejP. GutmannddatejŒõhfilenamegrfc7366eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129192hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÉAgranking∆crfcd7366irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Encrypt-then-MAC for Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxΩThis document provides a framework for Data Center (DC) Network Virtualization over Layer 3 (NVO3) and defines a reference model along with logical components required to design a solution.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekM. Lasserre¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehF. Balus¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehT. Morin¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejY. Rekhterddatej’khfilenamegrfc7365eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-129191hkeywordsÇdnvo3x#network virtualization over layer 3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT8Fgranking≈crfcd7365irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Framework for Data Center (DC) Network VirtualizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document describes issues associated with providing multi-tenancy in large data center networks and how these issues may be addressed using an overlay-based network virtualization approach.  A key multi-tenancy requirement is traffic isolation so that one tenant's traffic is not visible to any other tenant.  Another requirement is address space isolation so that different tenants can use the same address space within different virtual networks.  Traffic and address space isolation is achieved by assigning one or more virtual networks to each tenant, where traffic within a virtual network can only cross into another virtual network in a controlled fashion (e.g., via a configured router and/or a security gateway).  Additional functionality is required to provision virtual networks, associating a virtual machine's network interface(s) with the appropriate virtual network and maintaining that association as the virtual machine is activated, migrated, and/or deactivated.  Use of an overlay-based approach enables scalable deployment on large network infrastructures.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegL. Fang¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejL. Kreeger¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamelM. NapieraladdatejŒHhfilenamegrfc7364eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnvo3dfullx&nvo3 - Network Virtualization OverlaysdnamexNetwork Virtualization Overlaysdtypebwgbidjdoc-129190hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT8F
grankingƒcrfcd7364irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Problem Statement: Overlays for Network VirtualizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºREsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) is a peer-to-peer (P2P) signaling protocol that provides an overlay network service.  Peers in a RELOAD overlay network collectively run an overlay algorithm to organize the overlay and to store and retrieve data.  This document describes how the default topology plugin of RELOAD can be extended to support self-tuning, that is, to adapt to changing operating conditions such as churn and network size.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Maenpaa¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej’lhfilenamegrfc7363eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129189hkeywordsÉfP2PSIPcP2PeChordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT	agranking√crfcd7363irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUSelf-Tuning Distributed Hash Table (DHT) for REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document describes the behavior of signaling intermediaries in Real-Time Communication (RTC) deployments, sometimes referred to as Session Border Controllers (SBCs), when performing Hosted NAT Traversal (HNT). HNT is a set of mechanisms, such as media relaying and latching, that such intermediaries use to enable other RTC devices behind NATs to communicate with each other.

This document is non-normative and is only written to explain HNT in order to provide a reference to the Internet community and an informative description to manufacturers and users.

Latching, which is one of the HNT components, has a number of security issues covered here. Because of those, and unless all security considerations explained here are taken into account and solved, the IETF advises against use of the latching mechanism over the Internet and recommends other solutions, such as the Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) protocol.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeJitsidnamegE. Ivov¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiH. Kaplan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wingddatej’jhfilenamegrfc7362eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129188hkeywordsÇdVoIPrfirewall traversalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT`ôgranking¬crfcd7362irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexILatching: Hosted NAT Traversal (HNT) for Media in Real-Time CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁRFC 4762 describes a mechanism to remove or unlearn Media Access Control (MAC) addresses that have been dynamically learned in a Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) instance for faster convergence on topology changes.  The procedure also removes MAC addresses in the VPLS that do not require relearning due to such topology changes.  This document defines an enhancement to the MAC address withdraw procedure with an empty MAC list (RFC 4762); this enhancement enables a Provider Edge (PE) device to remove only the MAC addresses that need to be relearned.  Additional extensions to RFC 4762 MAC withdraw procedures are specified to provide an optimized MAC flushing for the Provider Backbone Bridging (PBB) VPLS specified in RFC 7041.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehP. Dutta¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehF. Balus¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnameiO. Stokes¢kaffiliationfOrangednamelG. Calvignac¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamehD. Fedykddatej’jhfilenamegrfc7361eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129187hkeywordsÉqMAC flush messagemMAC Flush TLVlMAC flushingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTUAgranking¡crfcd7361irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjLDP Extensions for Optimized MAC Address Withdrawal in a Hierarchical Virtual Private LAN Service (H-VPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.The RADIUS protocol defined in RFC 2865 has limited support for authentication and encryption of RADIUS packets.  The protocol transports data in the clear, although some parts of the packets can have obfuscated content.  Packets may be replayed verbatim by an attacker, and client-server authentication is based on fixed shared secrets.  This document specifies how the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol may be used as a fix for these problems.  It also describes how implementations of this proposal can coexist with current RADIUS systems.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatejŒïhfilenamegrfc7360eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129186hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT	Xgranking¿crfcd7360irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHDatagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) as a Transport Layer for RADIUSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9765∂habstractyïThe subtle way in which the IPv6 and IPv4 protocols coexist in typical networks, together with the lack of proper IPv6 support in popular Virtual Private Network (VPN) tunnel products, may inadvertently result in VPN tunnel traffic leakages.  That is, traffic meant to be transferred over an encrypted and integrity- protected VPN tunnel may leak out of such a tunnel and be sent in the clear on the local network towards the final destination.  This document discusses some scenarios in which such VPN tunnel traffic leakages may occur as a result of employing IPv6-unaware VPN software.  Additionally, this document offers possible mitigations for this issue.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. GontddatejŒõhfilenamegrfc7359eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-129185hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS˝3)grankingøcrfcd7359irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZLayer 3 Virtual Private Network (VPN) Tunnel Traffic Leakages in Dual-Stack Hosts/NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThe label advertising behavior of an LDP speaker for a given Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) is governed by the FEC type and not necessarily by the LDP session's negotiated label advertisement mode.  This document updates RFC 5036 to make that fact clear.  It also updates RFCs 3212, 4447, 5918, 6388, and 7140 by specifying the label advertisement mode for all currently defined LDP FEC types.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. LeymannddatejŒéhfilenamegrfc7358eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129184hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT3õgrankingæcrfcd7358irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLLabel Advertisement Discipline for LDP Forwarding Equivalence Classes (FECs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol provides least-cost pair-wise data forwarding without configuration in multi-hop networks with arbitrary topologies and link technologies. TRILL supports multipathing of both unicast and multicast traffic. Devices that implement the TRILL protocol are called TRILL switches or RBridges (Routing Bridges).

ESADI (End Station Address Distribution Information) is an optional protocol by which a TRILL switch can communicate, in a Data Label (VLAN or fine-grained label) scoped way, end station address and reachability information to TRILL switches participating in ESADI for the relevant Data Label. This document updates RFC 6325, specifically the documentation of the ESADI protocol, and is not backwards compatible.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegH. Zhai¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnameeF. Hu¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnameiO. Stokesddatej’hhfilenamegrfc7357eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129183hkeywordsÜeESADIeTRILLgRBridgepAddress LearninglReachabilitymMAC AddresseskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTiÌgrankingΩcrfcd7357irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): End Station Address Distribution Information (ESADI) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIntermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) provides efficient and reliable flooding of information to its peers; however, the current flooding scopes are limited to either area scope or domain scope. There are existing use cases where support of other flooding scopes is desirable. This document defines new Protocol Data Units (PDUs) that provide support for new flooding scopes as well as additional space for advertising information targeted for the currently supported flooding scopes. This document also defines extended Type-Length-Values (TLVs) and sub-TLVs that are encoded using 16-bit fields for Type and Length.

The protocol extensions defined in this document are not backwards compatible with existing implementations and so must be deployed with care.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegY. YangddatejŒúhfilenamegrfc7356eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129182hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTiÍgrankingºcrfcd7356irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+IS-IS Flooding Scope Link State PDUs (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHRFC 7118 specifies a WebSocket subprotocol as a reliable real-time transport mechanism between Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) entities to enable usage of SIP in web-oriented deployments.  This document updates the SIP Common Log Format (CLF), defined in RFC 6873, with a new "Transport Flag" for such SIP WebSocket transport.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationfQuobisdnamejV. Pascual¢kaffiliationfQuobisdnamehA. Roman¢kaffiliationfQuobisdnameiS. GarciaddatejŒñhfilenamegrfc7355eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129181hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateT!——grankingªcrfcd7355irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmIndicating WebSocket Protocol as a Transport in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Common Log Format (CLF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∞RFC 5328 registered the Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace "dvb" for the Digital Video Broadcasting Project.  This document updates RFC 5328 with new registrant information.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoCondition-ALPHAdnamehA. Adolf¢kaffiliationx&DVB Digital Video Broadcasting ProjectdnamejP. SiebertddatejŒñhfilenamegrfc7354eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129180hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT∆granking∫crfcd7354irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Update to the Registrant Information for the Digital Video Broadcasting Project (DVB) Uniform Resource Name (URN) NamespacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÛThis document describes requirements for a BGP security protocol design to provide cryptographic assurance that the origin Autonomous System (AS) has the right to announce the prefix and to provide assurance of the AS Path of the announcement.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamekS. Bellovin¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wardddatej’dhfilenamegrfc7353eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129179hkeywordsÖgRoutingcBGPhSecuritygAS_PATHhand RPKIkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateSıOÇgrankingπcrfcd7353irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Security Requirements for BGP Path ValidationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document defines a new test command, "duplicate", for the Sieve email filtering language.  This test adds the ability to detect duplications.  The main application for this new test is handling duplicate deliveries commonly caused by mailing list subscriptions or redirected mail addresses.  The detection is normally performed by matching the message ID to an internal list of message IDs from previously delivered messages.  For more complex applications, the "duplicate" test can also use the content of a specific header field or other parts of the message.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Boschddatej’hhfilenamegrfc7352eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129178hkeywordsÇesievetduplicate deliverieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÉ?granking∏crfcd7352irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Sieve Email Filtering: Detecting Duplicate DeliveriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÖThe XML patch document format defines an XML document structure for expressing a sequence of patch operations to be applied to an XML document.  The XML patch document format builds on the foundations defined in RFC 5261.  This specification also provides the media type registration "application/xml-patch+xml", to allow the use of XML patch documents in, for example, HTTP conversations.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamehE. Wildeddatej’jhfilenamegrfc7351eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129177hkeywordsÇjMedia TypetXML Patch OperationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÎÚ…granking∑crfcd7351irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex%A Media Type for XML Patch OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document specifies the usage of Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) as a transport protocol for Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN).  It provides guidance on when and how to use DTLS with the currently standardized STUN usages.  It also specifies modifications to the STUN and Traversal Using Relay NAT (TURN) URIs and to the TURN resolution mechanism to facilitate the resolution of STUN and TURN URIs into the IP address and port of STUN and TURN servers supporting DTLS as a transport protocol.  This document updates RFCs 5389 and 5928.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsJive CommunicationsdnameqM. Petit-Huguenin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiroddatej’hhfilenamegrfc7350eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtramdfullx"tram - TURN Revised and ModernizeddnamexTURN Revised and Modernizeddtypebwgbidjdoc-129176hkeywordsÇhSecurityjEncryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS˝3'granking∂crfcd7350irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdDatagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) as Transport for Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬This document introduces a new optional Cryptographic Authentication TLV that LDP can use to secure its Hello messages.  It secures the Hello messages against spoofing attacks and some well-known attacks against the IP header.  This document describes a mechanism to secure the LDP Hello messages using Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC) with the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) Secure Hash Standard family of algorithms.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehL. Zheng¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationnIonos NetworksdnameiM. BhatiaddatejŒEhfilenamegrfc7349eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129175hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÌÍgrankingµcrfcd7349irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&LDP Hello Cryptographic AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyêThis document describes Virtual eXtensible Local Area Network (VXLAN), which is used to address the need for overlay networks within virtualized data centers accommodating multiple tenants.  The scheme and the related protocols can be used in networks for cloud service providers and enterprise data centers.  This memo documents the deployed VXLAN protocol for the benefit of the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farrelgauthorsà¢kaffiliationiStorvisordnamemM. Mahalingam¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnamegD. Dutt¢kaffiliationfAristadnamegK. Duda¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamejP. Agarwal¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejL. Kreeger¢kaffiliationfVMwarednamejT. Sridhar¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejM. Bursell¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnameiC. WrightddatejŒòhfilenamegrfc7348eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129174hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS˝3%granking¥crfcd7348irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex|Virtual eXtensible Local Area Network (VXLAN): A Framework for Overlaying Virtualized Layer 2 Networks over Layer 3 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty~The IETF Standards Track solution for MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Linear Protection is provided in RFCs 6378, 7271, and 7324.

This document describes the pre-standard implementation of MPLS-TP Linear Protection that has been deployed by several network operators using equipment from multiple vendors. At the time of publication, these pre-standard implementations were still in operation carrying live traffic.

The specified mechanism supports 1+1 unidirectional/bidirectional protection switching and 1:1 bidirectional protection switching. It is purely supported by the MPLS-TP data plane and can work without any control plane.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Ryoo¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehH. Zhang¢kaffiliationgPhilipsdnamehF. Huang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeH. Li¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnameoA. D'AlessandroddatejŒóhfilenamegrfc7347eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129173hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateT9ﬁgranking≥crfcd7347irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLPre-standard Linear Protection Switching in MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxhThis document updates the definitions of IPv6 multicast scopes and therefore updates RFCs 4007 and 4291.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Dromsddatej’ghfilenamegrfc7346eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129172hkeywordsÅxIPv6 multicast address scopeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÍ≥©granking≤crfcd7346irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPv6 Multicast Address ScopesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document specifies how the UDP Transport Layer (UDPTL) protocol, the predominant transport protocol for T.38 fax, can be transported over the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol, how the usage of UDPTL over DTLS is indicated in the Session Description Protocol (SDP), and how UDPTL over DTLS is negotiated in a session established using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekI. Sedlacek¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelG. Salgueiroddatej’]hfilenamegrfc7345eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129171hkeywordsÜcSDPcSIPdDTLSeUDPTLcfaxitransportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSıOÅgranking±crfcd7345irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIUDP Transport Layer (UDPTL) over Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8842∂habstractyThis document describes a method to allow DNS Operators to more
easily update DNSSEC Key Signing Keys using the DNS as a
communication channel.  The technique described is aimed at
delegations in which it is currently hard to move information from
the Child to Parent.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationoOGUD ConsultingdnamenO. Gudmundsson¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. BarwoodddatejC^hfilenamegrfc7344eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129170hkeywordsÜhkey rollltrust anchorcCDSgCDNSKEYfDNSSECcDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT\◊granking∞crfcd7344irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Automating DNSSEC Delegation Trust MaintenancedtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8078d9615d9975∂habstractyRThis document specifies an updated Overlay Routable Cryptographic Hash Identifiers (ORCHID) format that obsoletes that in RFC 4843. These identifiers are intended to be used as endpoint identifiers at applications and Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) and not as identifiers for network location at the IP layer, i.e., locators. They are designed to appear as application-layer entities and at the existing IPv6 APIs, but they should not appear in actual IPv6 headers. To make them more like regular IPv6 addresses, they are expected to be routable at an overlay level. Consequently, while they are considered non-routable addresses from the IPv6-layer perspective, all existing IPv6 applications are expected to be able to use them in a manner compatible with current IPv6 addresses.

The Overlay Routable Cryptographic Hash Identifiers originally defined in RFC 4843 lacked a mechanism for cryptographic algorithm agility. The updated ORCHID format specified in this document removes this limitation by encoding, in the identifier itself, an index to the suite of cryptographic algorithms in use.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwLuminate Wireless, Inc.dnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnameiF. Dupontddatej’khfilenamegrfc7343eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129169hkeywordsÖcHIPeHIPv2fORCHIDcCGAcAPIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTÅ±grankingØcrfcd7343irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWAn IPv6 Prefix for Overlay Routable Cryptographic Hash Identifiers Version 2 (ORCHIDv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9374¥habstractxThis memo documents some operational practices that allow ARP and Neighbor Discovery (ND) to scale in data center environments.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationeYahoodnamelI. GashinskyddatejŒãhfilenamegrfc7342eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129168hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÎÚ»grankingÆcrfcd7342irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexKPractices for Scaling ARP and Neighbor Discovery (ND) in Large Data CentersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöIPv4 connectivity is still needed as networks migrate towards IPv6.  Users require IPv4 configuration even if the uplink to their service provider supports IPv6 only.  This document describes a mechanism for obtaining IPv4 configuration information dynamically in IPv6 networks by carrying DHCPv4 messages over DHCPv6 transport.  Two new DHCPv6 messages and two new DHCPv6 options are defined for this purpose.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelM. Siodelski¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiI. Farrerddatej’ihfilenamegrfc7341eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129167hkeywordsädipv6jtransitionhsoftwireimigrationftunnelhresidualdipv4fdhcpv6erelayiipv6-onlykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÌÈgranking≠crfcd7341irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'DHCPv4-over-DHCPv6 (DHCP 4o6) TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔOver the past decade, Voice over IP (VoIP) systems based on SIP have replaced many traditional telephony deployments.  Interworking VoIP systems with the traditional telephone network has reduced the overall level of calling party number and Caller ID assurances by granting attackers new and inexpensive tools to impersonate or obscure calling party numbers when orchestrating bulk commercial calling schemes, hacking voicemail boxes, or even circumventing multi-factor authentication systems trusted by banks.  Despite previous attempts to provide a secure assurance of the origin of SIP communications, we still lack effective standards for identifying the calling party in a VoIP session.  This document examines the reasons why providing identity for telephone numbers on the Internet has proven so difficult and shows how changes in the last decade may provide us with new strategies for attaching a secure identity to SIP sessions.  It also gives high-level requirements for a solution in this space.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’ehfilenamegrfc7340eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstirdfullx*stir - Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddnamex#Secure Telephone Identity Revisiteddtypebwgbidjdoc-129166hkeywordsácSIPdXMPPxSecure Origin IdentificationvCommunication SecurityfRTCWebqProblem StatementwReal-Time CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT!—–granking¨crfcd7340irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Secure Telephone Identity Problem Statement and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœOverload occurs in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) networks when SIP servers have insufficient resources to handle all the SIP messages they receive.  Even though the SIP protocol provides a limited overload control mechanism through its 503 (Service Unavailable) response code, SIP servers are still vulnerable to overload.  This document defines the behavior of SIP servers involved in overload control and also specifies a loss-based overload scheme for SIP.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxBell Labs / Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationxBell Labs / Alcatel-LucentdnamegV. Hilt¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej’ehfilenamegrfc7339eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsocdfullxsoc - SIP Overload ControldnametSIP Overload Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129165hkeywordsÇcSIPpOverload ControlkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateT!—Œgranking´crfcd7339irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Overload ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document presents a set of requirements and a framework for providing a point-to-multipoint pseudowire (PW) over MPLS Packet Switched Networks.  The requirements identified in this document are related to architecture, signaling, and maintenance aspects of point-to-multipoint PW operation.  They are proposed as guidelines for the standardization of such mechanisms.  Among other potential applications, point-to-multipoint PWs can be used to optimize the support of multicast Layer 2 services (Virtual Private LAN Service and Virtual Private Multicast Service).fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiOrange CHdnameiF. Jounay¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnameiY. Kamite¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehG. Heron¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. BocciddatejŒñhfilenamegrfc7338eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-129164hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT`ógranking™crfcd7338irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaRequirements and Framework for Point-to-Multipoint Pseudowires over MPLS Packet Switched NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyContent delivery is frequently provided by specifically architected and provisioned Content Delivery Networks (CDNs). As a result of significant growth in content delivered over IP networks, existing CDN providers are scaling up their infrastructure. Many Network Service Providers (NSPs) and Enterprise Service Providers (ESPs) are also deploying their own CDNs. To deliver contents from the Content Service Provider (CSP) to end users, the contents may traverse across multiple CDNs. This creates a need for interconnecting (previously) standalone CDNs so that they can collectively act as a single delivery platform from the CSP to the end users.

The goal of the present document is to outline the requirements for the solution and interfaces to be specified by the CDNI working group.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. LeeddatejŒuhfilenamegrfc7337eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129163hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÍ≥®granking©crfcd7337irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Content Distribution Network Interconnection (CDNI) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„This document presents a framework for Content Distribution Network Interconnection (CDNI).  The purpose of the framework is to provide an overall picture of the problem space of CDNI and to describe the relationships among the various components necessary to interconnect CDNs.  CDNI requires the specification of interfaces and mechanisms to address issues such as request routing, distribution metadata exchange, and logging information exchange across CDNs.  The intent of this document is to outline what each interface needs to accomplish and to describe how these interfaces and mechanisms fit together, while leaving their detailed specification to other documents.  This document, in combination with RFC 6707, obsoletes RFC 3466.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxAkamai Technologies, Inc.dnamekL. Peterson¢kaffiliationlVMware, Inc.dnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamerR. van Brandenburgddatej’ghfilenamegrfc7336eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129162hkeywordsádCDNIxcontent delivery networkjfederationtcdni request routinglcdni loggingmcdmi metadatalcdni controlkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateSÍ≥ßgranking®crfcd7336irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAFramework for Content Distribution Network Interconnection (CDNI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite), defined in RFC 6333, directs IANA to reserve 192.0.0.0/29 for the Basic Bridging BroadBand (B4) element.  Per this memo, IANA has generalized that reservation to include other cases where a non-routed IPv4 interface must be numbered as part of an IPv6 transition solution.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkT-Mobile USdnamehC. ByrneddatejŒühfilenamegrfc7335eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129161hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS˝3"grankingßcrfcd7335irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPv4 Service Continuity PrefixdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThe ability to compute paths for constrained point-to-multipoint (P2MP) Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) across multiple domains has been identified as a key requirement for the deployment of P2MP services in MPLS- and GMPLS-controlled networks. The Path Computation Element (PCE) has been recognized as an appropriate technology for the determination of inter-domain paths of P2MP TE LSPs.

This document describes an experiment to provide procedures and extensions to the PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) for the computation of inter-domain paths for P2MP TE LSPs.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqHuawei TechnologydnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationqHuawei TechnologydnamehD. Dhody¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnamekR. Casellasddatej’ihfilenamegrfc7334eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129160hkeywordsÅiCore-treekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÍfAgranking¶crfcd7334irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexêPCE-Based Computation Procedure to Compute Shortest Constrained Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Inter-Domain Traffic Engineering Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document defines the requirements for Distributed Mobility Management (DMM) at the network layer.  The hierarchical structure in traditional wireless networks has led primarily to centrally deployed mobility anchors.  As some wireless networks are evolving away from the hierarchical structure, it can be useful to have a distributed model for mobility management in which traffic does not need to traverse centrally deployed mobility anchors far from the optimal route.  The motivation and the problems addressed by each requirement are also described.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Chan¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefD. Liu¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehP. Seite¢kaffiliationjLandis+GyrdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationwBroadcom CommunicationsdnamekJ. Korhonenddatej’7hfilenamegrfc7333eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdmmdfullx%dmm - Distributed Mobility ManagementdnamexDistributed Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129159hkeywordsÖxDistributed Mobility ManagementxNetwork function distributionsFlat mobile networkx'Mobile network operation and managementx!Control and data plane separationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSıOÄgranking•crfcd7333irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Requirements for Distributed Mobility ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<SIP Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs) can cause unending SIP request routing loops because, as User Agent Clients, they can generate SIP requests with new Max-Forwards values.  This document discusses the difficulties associated with loop detection for B2BUAs and the requirements for them to prevent infinite loops.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiH. Kaplan¢kaffiliationfQuobisdnamejV. PascualddatejŒòhfilenamegrfc7332eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestrawdfullx7straw - Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdnamex/Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdtypebwgbidjdoc-129158hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÍ≥¶granking§crfcd7332irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaLoop Detection Mechanisms for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehN. Akiyaddatej’6hfilenamegrfc7331eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129157hkeywordsÖrNetwork ManagementxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2cBFDkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateSÌÁgranking£crfcd7331irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`This document defines two Management Information Base (MIB) modules that contain Textual Conventions to represent commonly used Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) management information.  The intent is that these TEXTUAL CONVENTIONS (TCs) will be imported and used in BFD-related MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representations.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkLucidvisiondnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehN. Akiyaddatej’6hfilenamegrfc7330eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-129156hkeywordsÖrNetwork Managementxmanagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2cBFDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÏ’granking¢crfcd7330irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Definitions of Textual Conventions (TCs) for Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀There is a need for having a globally unique session identifier for the same SIP session that can be consistently maintained across SIP Proxies, Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs), and other SIP middleboxes, for the purpose of troubleshooting. This document proposes a new SIP header to carry such a value: Session-ID.

The mechanism defined in this document has been widely deployed, and is being followed in a backward-compatible fashion for a new Standards Track document produced by the INSIPID Working Group.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiH. KaplanddatejŒôhfilenamegrfc7329eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129155hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7989epagesopublicationDateSıOÄgranking°crfcd7329irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A Session Identifier for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyµThis document presents a technique for using a Markdown syntax variant, called Pandoc, and a bit of XML (as defined in RFC 2629) as a source format for documents that are Internet-Drafts (I-Ds) or RFCs.

The goal of this technique (which is called Pandoc2rfc) is to let an author of an I-D focus on the main body of text without being distracted too much by XML tags; however, it does not alleviate the need to typeset some files in XML.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Giebenddate\Rπhfilenamegrfc7328eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129154hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateSÍf@granking†crfcd7328irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex3Writing I-Ds and RFCs Using Pandoc and a Bit of XMLdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document defines a framework for Energy Management (EMAN) for devices and device components within, or connected to, communication networks.  The framework presents a physical reference model and information model.  The information model consists of an Energy Management Domain as a set of Energy Objects.  Each Energy Object can be attributed with identity, classification, and context.  Energy Objects can be monitored and controlled with respect to power, Power State, energy, demand, Power Attributes, and battery.  Additionally, the framework models relationships and capabilities between Energy Objects.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Parello¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamelB. Schoening¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamejJ. QuittekddatejŒìhfilenamegrfc7326eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdemandfullxeman - Energy ManagementdnameqEnergy Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129153hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateT	Vgrankingûcrfcd7326irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEnergy Management FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis document provides guidelines for implementers regarding MPLS forwarding and a basis for evaluations of forwarding implementations.  Guidelines cover many aspects of MPLS forwarding.  Topics are highlighted where implementers might otherwise overlook practical requirements which are unstated or under emphasized or are optional for conformance to RFCs but are often considered mandatory by providers.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx&Outer Cape Cod Network Consulting, LLCdnamemC. Villamizar¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Amante¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliation`dnamelC. Pignataroddatej’[hfilenamegrfc7325eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129152hkeywordsÜdMPLSdECMPmlink bundlingimultipathgMPLS-TPjforwardingkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateS„“}grankingùcrfcd7325irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7MPLS Forwarding Compliance and Performance RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis document contains a number of updates to the Protection State Coordination (PSC) logic defined in RFC 6378, "MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Linear Protection".  These updates provide some rules and recommendations around the use of TLVs in PSC, address some issues raised in an ITU-T liaison statement, and clarify PSC's behavior in a case not well explained in RFC 6378.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejE. Osborneddatej’fhfilenamegrfc7324eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129151hkeywordsÑxmultiprotocol label switchinggmpls-tpcpscxprotection state coordinationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÿÖgrankingúcrfcd7324irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Updates to MPLS Transport Profile Linear ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`This document specifies a set of TCP extensions to improve performance over paths with a large bandwidth * delay product and to provide reliable operation over very high-speed paths. It defines the TCP Window Scale (WS) option and the TCP Timestamps (TS) option and their semantics. The Window Scale option is used to support larger receive windows, while the Timestamps option can be used for at least two distinct mechanisms, Protection Against Wrapped Sequences (PAWS) and Round-Trip Time Measurement (RTTM), that are also described herein.

This document obsoletes RFC 1323 and describes changes from it.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsQuantum CorporationdnameiD. Borman¢kaffiliationx!University of Southern CaliforniadnameiB. Braden¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekV. Jacobson¢kaffiliationlNetApp, Inc.dnamepR. Scheffeneggerddatej’`hfilenamegrfc7323eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129150hkeywordsájTimestampsiTimestampcRTTdRTTMlWindow ScaledPAWSkTCP optionskobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateTÅØgrankingõcrfcd7323irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#TCP Extensions for High PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty÷This document describes the fundamental and unique style conventions and editorial policies currently in use for the RFC Series.  It captures the RFC Editor's basic requirements and offers guidance regarding the style and structure of an RFC.  Additional guidance is captured on a website that reflects the experimental nature of that guidance and prepares it for future inclusion in the RFC Style Guide.  This document obsoletes RFC 2223, "Instructions to RFC Authors".gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekH. Flanagan¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnameiS. Ginozaddatej’chfilenamegrfc7322eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129149hkeywordsÑreditorial guidancefformatlstyle manualkhouse stylekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateT%«Bgrankingöcrfcd7322irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitleoRFC Style GuidedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7997∂habstractyËThis document updates the Cryptographic Algorithm Implementation Requirements for the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH). It also adds usage guidance to help in the selection of these algorithms.

ESP and AH protocols make use of various cryptographic algorithms to provide confidentiality and/or data origin authentication to protected data communications in the IP Security (IPsec) architecture. To ensure interoperability between disparate implementations, the IPsec standard specifies a set of mandatory-to- implement algorithms. This document specifies the current set of mandatory-to-implement algorithms for ESP and AH, specifies algorithms that should be implemented because they may be promoted to mandatory at some future time, and also recommends against the implementation of some obsolete algorithms. Usage guidance is also provided to help the user of ESP and AH best achieve their security goals through appropriate choices of cryptographic algorithms.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. HoffmanddatejŒëhfilenamegrfc7321eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129148hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8221epagesopublicationDateS˜Årgrankingôcrfcd7321irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexéCryptographic Algorithm Implementation Requirements and Usage Guidance for Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuSection 1.1.1 of RFC 3986 defines URI syntax as "a federated and extensible naming system wherein each scheme's specification may further restrict the syntax and semantics of identifiers using that scheme." In other words, the structure of a URI is defined by its scheme.  While it is common for schemes to further delegate their substructure to the URI's owner, publishing independent standards that mandate particular forms of URI substructure is inappropriate, because that essentially usurps ownership.  This document further describes this problematic practice and provides some acceptable alternatives for use in standards.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej’ahfilenamegrfc7320eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129147hkeywordsÅmURI structurekobsoletedByÅd8820epages	opublicationDateSøNgrankingòcrfcd7320irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexURI Design and OwnershipdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyúIEEE 802.1 has specified Connectivity Fault Management (CFM) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) facilities.  CFM messages are structured with an OpCode field and have provision for the inclusion of TLV-structured information.  IEEE 802.1 has allocated blocks of CFM OpCodes and TLV Types to the IETF.  This document specifies the IANA considerations for the assignment of values from these blocks.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej’1hfilenamegrfc7319eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129146hkeywordsácCFMcOAMlConnectivityjContinuityeFaultdIANAeTRILLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS…ÜXgrankingócrfcd7319irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberøetotaletitlexGIANA Considerations for Connectivity Fault Management (CFM) Code PointsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxπThis document updates RFC 6487 by clarifying the inclusion of policy qualifiers in the certificate policies extension of Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) resource certificates.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdARINdnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. HustonddatejŒîhfilenamegrfc7318eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129145hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS»! grankingñcrfcd7318irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKPolicy Qualifiers in Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThis document defines a YANG data model for the configuration and identification of some common system properties within a device containing a Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) server.  This document also includes data node definitions for system identification, time-of-day management, user management, DNS resolver configuration, and some protocol operations for system management.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej’ghfilenamegrfc7317eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129144hkeywordsÅgNETCONFkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateS„“}grankingïcrfcd7317irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'A YANG Data Model for System ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThis document specifies the SIP P-Private-Network-Indication P-header used by the 3GPP.  The P-Private-Network-Indication indicates that the message is part of the message traffic of a private network and identifies that private network.  A private network indication allows nodes to treat private network traffic according to a different set of rules than the set applicable to public network traffic.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxDetecon International GmbhdnamemJ. van Elburg¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehK. Drage¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiM. Ohsugi¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Schubert¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegK. AraiddatejŒíhfilenamegrfc7316eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129143hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÿÑgrankingîcrfcd7316irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) P-Private-Network-Indication Private Header (P-Header)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThis document describes a set of private header (P-header) Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) fields used by the 3GPP, along with their applicability, which is limited to particular environments.  The P-header fields are used for a variety of purposes within the networks that the partners implement, including charging and information about the networks a call traverses.  This document obsoletes RFC 3455.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesske¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehK. Drage¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. HolmbergddatejŒíhfilenamegrfc7315eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129142hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateS…ävgrankingìcrfcd7315irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZPrivate Header (P-Header) Extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for the 3GPPdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9878d7976d7913¥habstractx‰This document specifies a method for secondary DNS servers to honour the SOA EXPIRE field as if they were always transferring from the primary, even when using other secondaries to perform indirect transfers and refresh queries.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejM. Andrewsddatej’dhfilenamegrfc7314eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129141hkeywordsÜdIXFRdAXFRdzonehtransfercDNScSOAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS»!…grankingícrfcd7314irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex1Extension Mechanisms for DNS (EDNS) EXPIRE OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBIn this document, we enhance the existing BGP route refresh mechanisms to provide for the demarcation of the beginning and the ending of a route refresh.  The enhancement can be used to facilitate correction of BGP Routing Information Base (RIB) inconsistencies in a non-disruptive manner.  This document updates RFC 2918.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliation`dnametB. Venkatachalapathyddatej’hhfilenamegrfc7313eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129140hkeywordsÉwBorder Gateway Protocolgbgp ribxBGP Routing Information BasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSŒ∂[grankingëcrfcd7313irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Enhanced Route Refresh Capability for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTo obtain repeatable results in modern networks, test descriptions need an expanded stream parameter framework that also augments aspects specified as Type-P for test packets.  This memo updates the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) Framework, RFC 2330, with advanced considerations for measurement methodology and testing.  The existing framework mostly assumes deterministic connectivity, and that a single test stream will represent the characteristics of the path when it is aggregated with other flows.  Networks have evolved and test stream descriptions must evolve with them; otherwise, unexpected network features may dominate the measured performance.  This memo describes new stream parameters for both network characterization and support of application design using IPPM metrics.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxVienna University of TechnologydnameiJ. Fabini¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’fhfilenamegrfc7312eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129139hkeywordsâkMeasurementhWirelesshReactivemRepeatabilityjContinuityjActionablelConservativesSpatial CompositiontTemporal CompositionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSıO~grankingêcrfcd7312irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHAdvanced Stream and Sampling Framework for IP Performance Metrics (IPPM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»Routing protocols that have been designed to run within a single administrative domain (IGPs) generally do so by assigning a metric to each link and then choosing, as the installed path between two nodes, the path for which the total distance (sum of the metric of each link along the path) is minimized.  BGP, designed to provide routing over a large number of independent administrative domains (autonomous systems), does not make its path-selection decisions through the use of a metric.  It is generally recognized that any attempt to do so would incur significant scalability problems as well as inter-administration coordination problems.  However, there are deployments in which a single administration runs several contiguous BGP networks.  In such cases, it can be desirable, within that single administrative domain, for BGP to select paths based on a metric, just as an IGP would do.  The purpose of this document is to provide a specification for doing so.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpSproute NetworksdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekR. Fernando¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. UttaroddatejŒìhfilenamegrfc7311eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129138hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS„“|grankingècrfcd7311irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,The Accumulated IGP Metric Attribute for BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document specifies a scheme for packetizing Standard apt-X or Enhanced apt-X encoded audio data into Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) packets.  The document describes a payload format that permits transmission of multiple related audio channels in a single RTP payload and a means of establishing Standard apt-X and Enhanced apt-X connections through the Session Description Protocol (SDP).fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgAPT LtddnamejJ. Lindsay¢kaffiliationgAPT LtddnamekH. FoersterddatejŒähfilenamegrfc7310eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129137hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÿÑgrankingécrfcd7310irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?RTP Payload Format for Standard apt-X and Enhanced apt-X CodecsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœIn many existing Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) inter-domain deployments (based on RFC 4762), pseudowire (PW) connectivity offers no Provider Edge (PE) node redundancy, or offers PE node redundancy with only a single domain.  This deployment approach incurs a high risk of service interruption, since at least one domain will not offer PE node redundancy.  This document describes an inter-domain VPLS solution that provides PE node redundancy across domains.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefZ. Liu¢kaffiliation`dnamefL. Jin¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegR. Chen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefD. Cai¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salamddatej’ehfilenamegrfc7309eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129136hkeywordsÇdICCPbPWkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSƒÖgrankingçcrfcd7309irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Redundancy Mechanism for Inter-domain VPLS ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) advertises 32 administrative groups (commonly referred to as "colors" or "link colors") using the Administrative Group sub-TLV. This is defined for OSPFv2 (RFC 3630), OSPFv3 (RFC 5329) and IS-IS (RFC 5305).

This document adds a sub-TLV to the IGP TE extensions, "Extended Administrative Group". This sub-TLV provides for additional administrative groups (link colors) beyond the current limit of 32.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejE. Osborneddatej’fhfilenamegrfc7308eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129135hkeywordsÉfcolorsklink colorsqigp te extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS»!…grankingåcrfcd7308irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDExtended Administrative Groups in MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVMulti-Topology (MT) routing is supported in IP networks with the use of MT-aware IGPs. In order to provide MT routing within Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) networks, new extensions are required.

This document describes the LDP protocol extensions required to support MT routing in an MPLS environment.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationqHuawei TechnologydnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegC. Zhou¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegL. Fang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeL. Li¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. Kingddatej’chfilenamegrfc7307eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129134hkeywordsÇbMTxLabel Distribution ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSŒ∂[grankingãcrfcd7307irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!LDP Extensions for Multi-TopologydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9658∂habstractyµThis document specifies extensions to the IETF Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol (RDMAP) as specified in RFC 5040.  RDMAP provides read and write services directly to applications and enables data to be transferred directly into Upper-Layer Protocol (ULP) Buffers without intermediate data copies.  The extensions specified in this document provide the following capabilities and/or improvements: Atomic Operations and Immediate Data.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationxChelsio Communications, Inc.dnamehF. Marti¢kaffiliationxChelsio Communications, Inc.dnamemW. Noureddine¢kaffiliationxChelsio Communications, Inc.dnamelA. Eiriksson¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamehR. Sharpddatej’bhfilenamegrfc7306eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129133hkeywordsÖeiWARPeRDMAPcDDPdRDMAcDMAkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateS¨∫‰grankingäcrfcd7306irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) Protocol ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty‘This document provides an overview of a workshop held by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) on Internet Technology Adoption and Transition (ITAT). The workshop was hosted by the University of Cambridge on December 4th and 5th of 2013 in Cambridge, UK. The goal of the workshop was to facilitate adoption of Internet protocols, through examination of a variety of economic models, with particular emphasis at the waist of the hourglass (e.g., the middle of the protocol stack). This report summarizes contributions and discussions. As the topics were wide ranging, there is no single set of recommendations for IETF participants to pursue at this time. Instead, in the classic sense of early research, the workshop noted areas that deserve further exploration.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegE. LearddatejŒîhfilenamegrfc7305eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129132hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS¿ågrankingâcrfcd7305irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexRReport from the IAB Workshop on Internet Technology Adoption and Transition (ITAT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxπThis document outlines a possible, but not recommended, method to mitigate the effect of collisions in the DNS namespace by providing a means for end users to disambiguate the conflict.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. KumariddatejŒêhfilenamegrfc7304eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129131hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS∫˘}grankingàcrfcd7304irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,A Method for Mitigating Namespace CollisionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆThis specification standardizes three media types -- application/xml, application/xml-external-parsed-entity, and application/xml-dtd -- for use in exchanging network entities that are related to the Extensible Markup Language (XML) while defining text/xml and text/ xml-external-parsed-entity as aliases for the respective application/ types.  This specification also standardizes the '+xml' suffix for naming media types outside of these five types when those media types represent XML MIME entities.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of EdinburghednamekH. Thompson¢kaffiliationcW3CdnameiC. Lilleyddatej’`hfilenamegrfc7303eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129130hkeywordsÜoapplication/xmlx&application/xml-external-parsed-entitysapplication/xml-dtdhtext/xmlxtext/xml-external-parsed-entityd+xmlkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateS∫˘|grankingácrfcd7303irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleoXML Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˝Entertainment Identifier Registry (EIDR) Identifiers are used for the globally unique identification of motion picture and television content.  This document defines the formal Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace Identifier (NID) for EIDR Identifiers.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSandflow Consulting LLCdnamejP. Lemieuxddatej’1hfilenamegrfc7302eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129129hkeywordsÉdEIDRx!Entertainment Identifier RegistrycURNkobsoletedByÅd7972epagesopublicationDateS∫˘|grankingÜcrfcd7302irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAEntertainment Identifier Registry (EIDR) URN Namespace DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document describes a Transport Layer Security (TLS) extension for application-layer protocol negotiation within the TLS handshake.  For instances in which multiple application protocols are supported on the same TCP or UDP port, this extension allows the application layer to negotiate which protocol will be used within the TLS connection.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Friedl¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamehA. Popov¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamejA. Langley¢kaffiliationfOrangednamejE. Stephanddatej’bhfilenamegrfc7301eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129128hkeywordsÅdALPNkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateS¿å
grankingÖcrfcd7301irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOTransport Layer Security (TLS) Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8447∑habstractyThis document reserves two Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs) at the end of the 16-bit and 32-bit ranges, described in this document as "Last ASNs", and provides guidance to implementers and operators on their use.  This document updates Section 10 of RFC 1930.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegJ. Haas¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamekJ. Mitchellddatej’ghfilenamegrfc7300eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129127hkeywordsÇcasnilast asnskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS∫˘{grankingÑcrfcd7300irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex2Reservation of Last Autonomous System (AS) NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjWhen the Public-Key Infrastructure using X.509 (PKIX) Working Group was chartered, an object identifier arc was allocated by IANA for use by that working group.  This document describes the object identifiers that were assigned in that arc, returns control of that arc to IANA, and establishes IANA allocation policies for any future assignments within that arc.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej’dhfilenamegrfc7299eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129126hkeywordsÅx%Public-Key Infrastructure using X.509kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSŒ∂ZgrankingÉcrfcd7299irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Object Identifier Registry for the PKIX Working GroupdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9158¥habstracty'This document describes a cryptographic authentication mechanism for the Babel routing protocol.  This document updates RFC 6126.  The mechanism allocates two new TLV types for the authentication data, uses Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC), and is both optional and backward compatible.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfYandexdnamekD. Ovsienkoddatej’chfilenamegrfc7298eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129125hkeywordsÉprouting protocolnauthenticationtapplied cryptographykobsoletedByÅd8967epages7opublicationDateS¿ågrankingÇcrfcd7298irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLBabel Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC) Cryptographic AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty”This document describes the Connectivity Provisioning Profile (CPP) and proposes a CPP template to capture IP/MPLS connectivity requirements to be met within a service delivery context (e.g., Voice over IP or IP TV). The CPP defines the set of IP transfer parameters to be supported by the underlying transport network together with a reachability scope and bandwidth/capacity needs. Appropriate performance metrics, such as one-way delay or one-way delay variation, are used to characterize an IP transfer service. Both global and restricted reachability scopes can be captured in the CPP.

Such a generic CPP template is meant to (1) facilitate the automation of the service negotiation and activation procedures, thus accelerating service provisioning, (2) set (traffic) objectives of Traffic Engineering functions and service management functions, and (3) improve service and network management systems with 'decision- making' capabilities based upon negotiated/offered CPPs.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelC. Jacquenet¢kaffiliationtUniversity of SurreydnamegN. WangddatejŒéhfilenamegrfc7297eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129124hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS∂ıgrankingÅcrfcd7297irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex*IP Connectivity Provisioning Profile (CPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyLThis document describes version 2 of the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) protocol.  IKE is a component of IPsec used for performing mutual authentication and establishing and maintaining Security Associations (SAs).  This document obsoletes RFC 5996, and includes all of the errata for it.  It advances IKEv2 to be an Internet Standard.fadNameqKathleen Moriartydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Kaufman¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejT. Kivinenddatej’7hfilenamegrfc7296eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129123hkeywordsÇcIKEeIPseckobsoletedByÄepageséopublicationDateTJﬁYgrankingÄcrfcd7296irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberOetotaletitlex0Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÜd9370d8983d7427d9827d8247d7670¥habstractyôThis document provides a summary of the IAB/IRTF Workshop on 'Congestion Control for Interactive Real-Time Communication', which took place in Vancouver, Canada, on July 28, 2012. The main goal of the workshop was to foster a discussion on congestion control mechanisms for interactive real-time communication. This report summarizes the discussions and lists recommendations to the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) community.

The views and positions in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect the views and positions of the authors, the Internet Architecture Board (IAB), or the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF).gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliation`dnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliation`dnameiZ. Sarkerddatej’`hfilenamegrfc7295eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129122hkeywordsÑrCongestion ControlfRTCWEBhWorkshopwReal-Time CommunicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS∂Ùgrankingcrfcd7295irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex_Report from the IAB/IRTF Workshop on Congestion Control for Interactive Real-Time CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx†This document defines two RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) blocks that allow the reporting of concealment metrics for audio applications of RTP.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationeSTTRIdnameeC. Bi¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej’bhfilenamegrfc7294eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-129121hkeywordsÅxReal Time Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSøKgranking~crfcd7294irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Blocks for Concealment Metrics Reporting on Audio ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThis document defines an extension for the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) called "RRVS" to provide a method for senders to indicate to receivers a point in time when the ownership of the target mailbox was known to the sender. This can be used to detect changes of mailbox ownership and thus prevent mail from being delivered to the wrong party. This document also defines a header field called "Require-Recipient-Valid-Since" that can be used to tunnel the request through servers that do not support the extension.

The intended use of these facilities is on automatically generated messages, such as account statements or password change instructions, that might contain sensitive information, though it may also be useful in other applications.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkYahoo! Inc.dnamehW. Mills¢kaffiliationnFacebook, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’bhfilenamegrfc7293eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129120hkeywordsÑhSecuritygPrivacyeEmailrAccount ExpirationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS∂Ùgranking}crfcd7293irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIThe Require-Recipient-Valid-Since Header Field and SMTP Service ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇPKCS #12 v1.1 describes a transfer syntax for personal identity information, including private keys, certificates, miscellaneous secrets, and extensions. Machines, applications, browsers, Internet kiosks, and so on, that support this standard will allow a user to import, export, and exercise a single set of personal identity information. This standard supports direct transfer of personal information under several privacy and integrity modes.

This document represents a republication of PKCS #12 v1.1 from RSA Laboratories' Public Key Cryptography Standard (PKCS) series. By publishing this RFC, change control is transferred to the IETF.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamejM. Nystrom¢kaffiliationcRSAdnamelS. Parkinson¢kaffiliationcRSAdnamehA. Rusch¢kaffiliationcRSAdnamehM. Scottddatej’`hfilenamegrfc7292eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129119hkeywordsÉgPKCS#12jPKCS12v1.1kPKCS#12v1.1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSÿÇgranking|crfcd7292irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3PKCS #12: Personal Information Exchange Syntax v1.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9879d9579∂habstracty4This document specifies DHCP (IPv4 and IPv6) options to configure hosts with Port Control Protocol (PCP) server IP addresses.  The use of DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 depends on the PCP deployment scenarios.  The set of deployment scenarios to which DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 can be applied is outside the scope of this document.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wingddatej’bhfilenamegrfc7291eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129118hkeywordsÉtPCP Server discoverylPort MappingnShared AddresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSƒÖgranking{crfcd7291irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0DHCP Options for the Port Control Protocol (PCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This memo provides the supporting test plan and results to advance RFC 2680, a performance metric RFC defining one-way packet loss metrics, along the Standards Track.  Observing that the metric definitions themselves should be the primary focus rather than the implementations of metrics, this memo describes the test procedures to evaluate specific metric requirement clauses to determine if the requirement has been interpreted and implemented as intended.  Two completely independent implementations have been tested against the key specifications of RFC 2680.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamemL. Ciavattone¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geib¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationxTechnical University DarmstadtdnameiM. Wieserddatej’_hfilenamegrfc7290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-129117hkeywordsàkpacket lossxIPPM implementation comparisongperfas+enetemoIPPM comparisonlmetric¬†testdWIPMhNetProbekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSŒ∂Ygrankingzcrfcd7290irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCTest Plan and Results for Advancing RFC 2680 on the Standards TrackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@This document specifies a framework to integrate a Network Address Translation (NAT) layer into an operator's network to function as a Carrier-Grade NAT (also known as CGN or Large-Scale NAT).  The CGN infrastructure will often form a NAT444 environment as the subscriber home network will likely also maintain a subscriber-side NAT function.  Exhaustion of the IPv4 address pool is a major driver compelling some operators to implement CGN.  Although operators may wish to deploy IPv6 to strategically overcome IPv4 exhaustion, near- term needs may not be satisfied with an IPv6 deployment alone.  This document provides a practical integration model that allows the CGN platform to be integrated into the network, meeting the connectivity needs of the subscriber while being mindful of not disrupting existing services and meeting the technical challenges that CGN brings.  The model included in this document utilizes BGP/MPLS IP VPNs, which allow for virtual routing separation, helping ease the CGN's impact on the network.  This document does not intend to defend the merits of CGN.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuRogers CommunicationsdnamelV. Kuarsingh¢kaffiliationuRogers CommunicationsdnamemJ. Cianfaraniddatej’ahfilenamegrfc7289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129116hkeywordsÉfNAT444cLSNoLarge-Scale NATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS¨∫·grankingycrfcd7289irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Carrier-Grade NAT (CGN) Deployment with BGP/MPLS IP VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyIn today's Internet, the need for firewalls is generally accepted in the industry, and indeed firewalls are widely deployed in practice.  Unlike traditional firewalls that protect network links, host firewalls run in end-user systems.  Often the result is that software may be running and potentially consuming resources, but then communication is blocked by a host firewall.  It's taken for granted that this end state is either desirable or the best that can be achieved in practice, rather than (for example) an end state where the relevant software is not running or is running in a way that would not result in unwanted communication.  In this document, we explore the issues behind these assumptions and provide suggestions on improving the architecture going forward.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej’ahfilenamegrfc7288eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-129115hkeywordsÇfFilteriFilteringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS™Pgrankingxcrfcd7288irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexReflections on Host FirewallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyãMulticast communication can be enabled in Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) domains via the Local Mobility Anchors by deploying Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) proxy functions at Mobile Access Gateways, by using direct traffic distribution within an ISP's access network, or by selective route optimization schemes.  This document describes a base solution and an experimental protocol to support mobile multicast senders in PMIPv6 domains for all three scenarios.  Protocol optimizations for synchronizing PMIPv6 with PIM, as well as a peering function for MLD proxies are defined.  Mobile sources always remain agnostic of multicast mobility operations.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationxBeijing Jiaotong UniversitydnamefS. Gao¢kaffiliationxBeijing Jiaotong UniversitydnamehH. Zhang¢kaffiliationtlink-lab & FU BerlindnamelM. WaehlischddatejŒàhfilenamegrfc7287eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmultimobdfullxmultimob - Multicast MobilitydnamerMulticast Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129114hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS™Ogrankingwcrfcd7287irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEMobile Multicast Sender Support in Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) DomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚The goal of Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) is to provide guidance to applications that have to select one or several hosts from a set of candidates capable of providing a desired resource. ALTO is realized by a client-server protocol. Before an ALTO client can ask for guidance, it needs to discover one or more ALTO servers.

This document specifies a procedure for resource-consumer-initiated ALTO server discovery, which can be used if the ALTO client is embedded in the resource consumer.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnameiS. Kiesel¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationrThales DeutschlanddnameiN. Schwan¢kaffiliationxAlcatel-Lucent Bell LabsdnameiM. Scharf¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegH. SongddatejŒNhfilenamegrfc7286eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129113hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateTfï/grankingvcrfcd7286irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Server DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûApplications using the Internet already have access to some topology information of Internet Service Provider (ISP) networks. For example, views to Internet routing tables at Looking Glass servers are available and can be practically downloaded to many network application clients. What is missing is knowledge of the underlying network topologies from the point of view of ISPs. In other words, what an ISP prefers in terms of traffic optimization -- and a way to distribute it.

The Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) services defined in this document provide network information (e.g., basic network location structure and preferences of network paths) with the goal of modifying network resource consumption patterns while maintaining or improving application performance. The basic information of ALTO is based on abstract maps of a network. These maps provide a simplified view, yet enough information about a network for applications to effectively utilize them. Additional services are built on top of the maps.

This document describes a protocol implementing the ALTO services. Although the ALTO services would primarily be provided by ISPs, other entities, such as content service providers, could also provide ALTO services. Applications that could use the ALTO services are those that have a choice to which end points to connect. Examples of such applications are peer-to-peer (P2P) and content delivery networks.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehR. Alimi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnameiS. Kiesel¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehW. Roome¢kaffiliationkOpen GardendnamekS. Shalunov¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiR. Woundyddatej’]hfilenamegrfc7285eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129112hkeywordsáxALTO Information ResourceskNetwork MapcPIDtFiltered Network MaphCost MapxEndpoint Property ServiceuEndpoint Cost ServicekobsoletedByÄepages[opublicationDateT
"grankingucrfcd7285irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9274¥habstractxfThis document defines a registry for profile URIs to be used in specifications standardizing profiles.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Lanthalerddatej’_hfilenamegrfc7284eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129111hkeywordsÑgprofilehprofilescURIhregistrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS©˝-grankingtcrfcd7284irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexThe Profile URI RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡DHCPv6 is not specific about handling messages with unknown types.  This memo describes the problems associated with receiving DHCPv6 messages with unknown types, and defines how a DHCPv6 server, client, or relay agent should behave when receiving unknown DHCPv6 messages.  This document also provides advice for authors of future documents that define new messages to be sent from DHCP servers to DHCP relay agents.  This document updates RFC 3315.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefQ. Sun¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemonddatej’dhfilenamegrfc7283eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-129110hkeywordsÇfDHCPv6pUnknown MessageskobsoletedByÅd8415epagesopublicationDateS…ÜWgrankingscrfcd7283irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Handling Unknown DHCPv6 MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe IETF has had a long tradition of doing its technical work through a consensus process, taking into account the different views among IETF participants and coming to (at least rough) consensus on technical matters. In particular, the IETF is supposed not to be run by a "majority rule" philosophy. This is why we engage in rituals like "humming" instead of voting. However, more and more of our actions are now indistinguishable from voting, and quite often we are letting the majority win the day without consideration of minority concerns. This document explains some features of rough consensus, what is not rough consensus, how we have gotten away from it, how we might think about it differently, and the things we can do in order to really achieve rough consensus.

Note: This document is quite consciously being put forward as Informational. It does not propose to change any IETF processes and is therefore not a BCP. It is simply a collection of principles, hopefully around which the IETF can come to (at least rough) consensus.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies, Inc.dnamejP. Resnickddatej’chfilenamegrfc7282eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129109hkeywordsò'kaccommodateeagreeiagreementgappeaseearguehargumentiballotingkcapitulatedlcapitulationechairfchoicefchoosedcoinjcompromiseecountfdecidehdecisionhdisagreeldisagreementehandskhorse-trademhorse-tradingchumeissueejudgegjudgingdkinghmajorityfmemberhminorityfobjectiobjectionhobjectoripresidenteroughkunaddresseddvotefvotingmworking groupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS®í∑grankingrcrfcd7282irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$On Consensus and Humming in the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyRFC 7001 specifies the Authentication-Results header field for conveying results of message authentication checks.  This document defines a new authentication method to be used in the Authentication- Results header field for S/MIME-related signature checks.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’3hfilenamegrfc7281eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129108hkeywordsÇvAuthentication-ResultsfS/MIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS©˝-grankingqcrfcd7281irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexEAuthentication-Results Registration for S/MIME Signature VerificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis document updates RFC 4326 to clarify and update the allocation rules for the Unidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE) Next- Header registry.  This registry is used by ULE and Generic Stream Encapsulation (GSE) to record the code points of Extension Headers and protocols supported by these encapsulation protocols.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej’chfilenamegrfc7280eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129107hkeywordsÇcULEdIANAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS£H"grankingpcrfcd7280irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbIANA Guidance for Managing the Unidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE) Next-Header RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyäIn this document we provide a basic description of ICMP's role in the IP stack and some guidelines for future use.

This document is motivated by concerns about lack of clarity concerning when to add new Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP) types and/or codes. These concerns have highlighted a need to describe policies for when adding new features to ICMP is desirable and when it is not.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtNo Mountain SoftwarednamehM. Shore¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataroddatej’6hfilenamegrfc7279eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129106hkeywordsÉdicmpficmpv4ficmpv6kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateSà¸«grankingocrfcd7279irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberΩetotaletitlex5An Acceptable Use Policy for New ICMP Types and CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx–This document describes requirements for extending an IPv6 /64 prefix from a User Equipment Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) radio interface to a LAN link and describes two implementation examples.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlT-Mobile USAdnamehC. Byrne¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamehD. Drown¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnameiA. VizdalddatejŒâhfilenamegrfc7278eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129105hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateS¨âagrankingncrfcd7278irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnExtending an IPv6 /64 Prefix from a Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Mobile Interface to a LAN LinkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxâThis document defines a YANG data model for management of IP implementations.  The data model includes configuration data and state data.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej’ahfilenamegrfc7277eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129104hkeywordsÅfnetmodkobsoletedByÅd8344epagesopublicationDateS†œ3grankingmcrfcd7277irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#A YANG Data Model for IP ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖOperations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) is a general term that refers to a toolset for fault detection and isolation, and for performance measurement. Over the years, various OAM tools have been defined for various layers in the protocol stack.

This document summarizes some of the OAM tools defined in the IETF in the context of IP unicast, MPLS, MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP), pseudowires, and Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL). This document focuses on tools for detecting and isolating failures in networks and for performance monitoring. Control and management aspects of OAM are outside the scope of this document. Network repair functions such as Fast Reroute (FRR) and protection switching, which are often triggered by OAM protocols, are also out of the scope of this document.

The target audience of this document includes network equipment vendors, network operators, and standards development organizations. This document can be used as an index to some of the main OAM tools defined in the IETF. At the end of the document, a list of the OAM toolsets and a list of the OAM functions are presented as a summary.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationxNokia Solutions and NetworksdnamekN. Sprecher¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemE. Bellagamba¢kaffiliation`dnamemY. WeingartenddatejŒÜhfilenamegrfc7276eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129103hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateSö˘grankinglcrfcd7276irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFAn Overview of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) ToolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document specifies an Inter-Chassis Communication Protocol (ICCP) that enables Provider Edge (PE) device redundancy for Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS) and Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) applications.  The protocol runs within a set of two or more PEs, forming a Redundancy Group, for the purpose of synchronizing data among the systems.  It accommodates multi-chassis attachment circuit redundancy mechanisms as well as pseudowire redundancy mechanisms.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationpSoftbank TelecomdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiT. Nadeauddatej’^hfilenamegrfc7275eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-129102hkeywordsÅdiccpkobsoletedByÄepagesSopublicationDateS¨∫‡grankingkcrfcd7275irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnInter-Chassis Communication Protocol for Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN) Provider Edge (PE) RedundancydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBSome MPLS labels have been allocated for specific purposes. A block of labels (0-15) has been set aside to this end; these labels are commonly called "reserved labels". They will be called "special-purpose labels" in this document.

As there are only 16 of these special-purpose labels, caution is needed in the allocation of new special-purpose labels; yet, at the same time, forward progress should be allowed when one is called for.

This memo defines new procedures for the allocation and retirement of special-purpose labels, as well as a method to extend the special-purpose label space and a description of how to handle extended special-purpose labels in the data plane. Finally, this memo renames the IANA registry for special-purpose labels to "Special-Purpose MPLS Label Values" and creates a new registry called the "Extended Special-Purpose MPLS Label Values" registry.

This document updates a number of previous RFCs that use the term "reserved label". Specifically, this document updates RFCs 3032, 3038, 3209, 3811, 4182, 4928, 5331, 5586, 5921, 5960, 6391, 6478, and 6790.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. FarrelddatejŒÖhfilenamegrfc7274eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129101hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSö¯grankingjcrfcd7274irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Allocating and Retiring Special-Purpose MPLS LabelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9017∂habstracty2NTP format timestamps are used by several RTP protocols for synchronisation and statistical measurements.  This memo specifies Session Description Protocol (SDP) signalling that identifies timestamp reference clock sources and SDP signalling that identifies the media clock sources in a multimedia session.fadNamemAlissa Cooperdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhAudinatednamekA. Williams¢kaffiliationlAVA NetworksdnamehK. Gross¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamerR. van Brandenburg¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamekH. Stokkingddatej’_hfilenamegrfc7273eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129100hkeywordsÇeclockfsourcekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS≤§grankingicrfcd7273irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Clock Source SignallingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThis document defines a new RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Packet Type and an RTCP Extended Report (XR) Block Type to be used for achieving Inter-Destination Media Synchronization (IDMS). IDMS is the process of synchronizing playout across multiple media receivers. Using the RTCP XR IDMS Report Block defined in this document, media playout information from participants in a synchronization group can be collected. Based on the collected information, an RTCP IDMS Settings Packet can then be sent to distribute a common target playout point to which all the distributed receivers, sharing a media experience, can synchronize.

Typical use cases in which IDMS is useful are social TV, shared service control (i.e., applications where two or more geographically separated users are watching a media stream together), distance learning, networked video walls, networked loudspeakers, etc.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamerR. van Brandenburg¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamekH. Stokking¢kaffiliationcTNOdnameoO. van Deventer¢kaffiliationcUPVdnamejF. Boronat¢kaffiliationcUPVdnamekM. Montagud¢kaffiliationlAVA NetworksdnamehK. Grossddatej’?hfilenamegrfc7272eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129099hkeywordsÉx'Inter-Destination Media SynchronizationtRTP Control ProtocoldRTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS≤£grankinghcrfcd7272irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTInter-Destination Media Synchronization (IDMS) Using the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘This document describes alternate mechanisms to perform some of the functions of MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) linear protection defined in RFC 6378, and also defines additional mechanisms. The purpose of these alternate and additional mechanisms is to provide operator control and experience that more closely models the behavior of linear protection seen in other transport networks.

This document also introduces capabilities and modes for linear protection. A capability is an individual behavior, and a mode is a particular combination of capabilities. Two modes are defined in this document: Protection State Coordination (PSC) mode and Automatic Protection Switching (APS) mode.

This document describes the behavior of the PSC protocol including priority logic and state machine when all the capabilities associated with the APS mode are enabled.

This document updates RFC 6378 in that the capability advertisement method defined here is an addition to that document.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegJ. Ryoo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnameoA. D'Alessandro¢kaffiliationdETRIdnameiT. Cheung¢kaffiliation`dnamejE. Osborneddatej’_hfilenamegrfc7271eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129098hkeywordsàhPSC modehAPS modelcapabilitieshprioritymnon-revertivelMS-W supportjSD supportlEXER supportkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateSöıgrankinggcrfcd7271irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexæMPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Linear Protection to Match the Operational Expectations of Synchronous Digital Hierarchy, Optical Transport Network, and Ethernet Transport Network OperatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8234∂habstractyThis document describes some additional IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information Elements in the range of 1-127, which is the range compatible with field types used by NetFlow version 9 in RFC 3954, as specified in the IPFIX Information Model in RFC 7012.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenA. Yourtchenko¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej’^hfilenamegrfc7270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129097hkeywordsÅeIPFIXkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS©˝,grankingfcrfcd7270irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPCisco-Specific Information Elements Reused in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx…This document summarizes NAT64 function deployment scenarios and operational experience.  Both NAT64 Carrier-Grade NAT (NAT64-CGN) and NAT64 server Front End (NAT64-FE) are considered in this document.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegG. Chen¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefZ. Cao¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefC. Xie¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom-OrangednamehD. BinetddatejŒáhfilenamegrfc7269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129096hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSíh€grankingecrfcd7269irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'NAT64 Deployment Options and ExperiencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycRFC 3580 provides guidelines for the use of the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) within IEEE 802 local area networks (LANs).  This document defines additional attributes for use within IEEE 802 networks and clarifies the usage of the EAP-Key-Name Attribute and the Called-Station-Id Attribute.  This document updates RFCs 3580 and 4072.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejJ. Malinen¢kaffiliationoTallac NetworksdnamejP. Congdon¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationmAzuca SystemsdnamehM. JonesddatejŒáhfilenamegrfc7268eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129095hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS»!»grankingdcrfcd7268irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'RADIUS Attributes for IEEE 802 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8044∂habstractyIRFC 5254 describes the service provider requirements for extending the reach of pseudowires (PWs) across multiple Packet Switched Network domains.  A multi-segment PW is defined as a set of two or more contiguous PW segments that behave and function as a single point-to-point PW.  This document describes extensions to the PW control protocol to dynamically place the segments of the multi-segment pseudowire among a set of Provider Edge (PE) routers.  This document also updates RFC 6073 by updating the value of the Length field of the PW Switching Point PE Sub-TLV Type 0x06 to 14.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehF. Balusddatej’^hfilenamegrfc7267eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-129094hkeywordsÇbpwxpw switching point pe sub-tlvkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS†œ3grankingccrfcd7267irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Dynamic Placement of Multi-Segment PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block including two new segment types and associated Session Description Protocol (SDP) parameters that allow the reporting of mean opinion score (MOS) Metrics for use in a range of RTP applications.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Schott¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. ZornddatejŒÄhfilenamegrfc7266eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-129093hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS™Ngrankingbcrfcd7266irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Blocks for Mean Opinion Score (MOS) Metric ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrThis specification defines "jCal", a JSON format for iCalendar data.  The iCalendar data format is a text format for capturing and exchanging information normally stored within a calendaring and scheduling application, for example, tasks and events.  JSON is a lightweight, text-based, language-independent data interchange format commonly used in Internet applications.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejP. Kewisch¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationcRPIdnamekM. DouglassddatejŒÑhfilenamegrfc7265eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhjcardcaldfullx4jcardcal - JSON data formats for vCard and iCalendardnamex)JSON data formats for vCard and iCalendardtypebwgbidjdoc-129092hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSà¸∆grankingacrfcd7265irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#jCal: The JSON Format for iCalendardtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7529∂habstractyñThis document defines an optional extension to the REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) protocol to support the relay peer routing mode.  RELOAD recommends symmetric recursive routing for routing messages.  The new optional extension provides a shorter route for responses, thereby reducing overhead on intermediate peers.  This document also describes potential cases where this extension can be used.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegN. Zong¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehX. Jiang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationvCoolPad / China MobilednamehY. Zhangddatej’\hfilenamegrfc7264eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129091hkeywordsÅcP2PkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS†œ2granking`crfcd7264irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcAn Extension to the REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) Protocol to Support Relay Peer RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document defines an optional extension to the REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) protocol to support the direct response routing mode.  RELOAD recommends symmetric recursive routing for routing messages.  The new optional extension provides a shorter route for responses, thereby reducing overhead on intermediate peers.  This document also describes potential cases where this extension can be used.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegN. Zong¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehX. Jiang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationvCoolPad / China MobilednamehY. Zhangddatej’\hfilenamegrfc7263eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129090hkeywordsÅcP2PkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS†œ2granking_crfcd7263irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhAn Extension to the REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) Protocol to Support Direct Response RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo discusses the requirements for specifications that enable telepresence interoperability by describing behaviors and protocols for Controlling Multiple Streams for Telepresence (CLUE).  In addition, the problem statement and related definitions are also covered herein.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Romanow¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiS. Botzko¢kaffiliationx MLB@Realtime Communications, LLCdnameiM. BarnesddatejŒÅhfilenamegrfc7262eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-129089hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSõ/ªgranking^crfcd7262irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Requirements for Telepresence MultistreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides the offer/answer considerations for the annexa parameter of G723 and the annexb parameter of G729, G729D, and G729E when the value of the annexa or annexb parameter does not match in the Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer and answer.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Perumal¢kaffiliationcNSNdnamelP. Ravindranddatej’\hfilenamegrfc7261eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129088hkeywordsÇeofferfanswerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSà¸≈granking]crfcd7261irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Offer/Answer Considerations for G723 Annex A and G729 Annex BdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) is an integral part of transport connections; hence, it is required that OAM functions be activated/deactivated in sync with connection commissioning/ decommissioning, in order to avoid spurious alarms and ensure consistent operation.  In certain technologies, OAM entities are inherently established once the connection is set up, while other technologies require extra configuration to establish and configure OAM entities.  This document specifies extensions to Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) to support the establishment and configuration of OAM entities along with Label Switched Path signaling.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Takacs¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeJ. Heddatej’[hfilenamegrfc7260eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-129087hkeywordságMPLS-TPqTransport ProfiledGELSxEthernet Label SwitchingfPBB-TEwconnectivity monitoringqOAM configurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS≤¢granking\crfcd7260irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\GMPLS RSVP-TE Extensions for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document defines a header field that enables the author of an email or netnews message to include a Jabber ID in the message header block for the purpose of associating the author with a particular Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) address.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’^hfilenamegrfc7259eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129086hkeywordsàfJabberdXMPPx*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoleemailgnetnewstmessage header fieldbIMqinstant messagingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSu¸granking[crfcd7259irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexThe Jabber-ID Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxtPervasive monitoring is a technical attack that should be mitigated in the design of IETF protocols, where possible.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’]hfilenamegrfc7258eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129085hkeywordsÅtpervasive monitoringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSq£”grankingZcrfcd7258irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberºetotaletitlex!Pervasive Monitoring Is an AttackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects to configure and/or monitor Virtual Private LAN services.  It needs to be used in conjunction with the Pseudowire (PW) Management Information Base (PW-STD-MIB from RFC 5601).fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlLucid VisiondnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamepA. Kiran Koushik¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelR. MedirattaddatejŒ}hfilenamegrfc7257eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129084hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateSºc™grankingYcrfcd7257irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document specifies the extensions to the Access Node Control Protocol (ANCP) (RFC 6320) required for support of the multicast use cases defined in the Access Node Control Protocol framework document (RFC 5851) and one additional use case described in this document. These use cases are organized into the following ANCP capabilities:

o multicast replication initiated by the Network Access Server (NAS);

o conditional access and admission control with white and black lists;

o conditional access and admission control with grey lists;

o bandwidth delegation; and

o committed bandwidth reporting.

These capabilities may be combined according to the rules given in this specification.

This document updates RFC 6320 by assigning capability type 3 to a capability specified in this document and by changing the starting point for IANA allocation of result codes determined by IETF Consensus from 0x100 to 0x64.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekR. Maglione¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiT. TaylorddatejŒ~hfilenamegrfc7256eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdancpdfullx#ancp - Access Node Control ProtocoldnamexAccess Node Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129083hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagescopublicationDateS∂ÚgrankingXcrfcd7256irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMulticast Control Extensions for the Access Node Control Protocol (ANCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√This specification defines how the Uniform Resource Name (URN) reserved for the Global System for Mobile Communications Association (GSMA) identities and its sub-namespace for the International Mobile station Equipment Identity (IMEI) can be used as an instance-id.  Its purpose is to fulfill the requirements for defining how a specific URN needs to be constructed and used in the '+sip.instance' Contact header field parameter for outbound behavior.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjBlackberrydnamehA. Allenddatej’\hfilenamegrfc7255eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129082hkeywordsäcGSMdUMTScLTEd3GPPcIMScSIPdGRUUfMobilejidentifierkinstance IDkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateS}SêgrankingWcrfcd7255irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnUsing the International Mobile station Equipment Identity (IMEI) Uniform Resource Name (URN) as an Instance IDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis specification defines a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Global System for Mobile Communications Association (GSMA) and a Namespace Specific String (NSS) for the International Mobile station Equipment Identity (IMEI), as well as an associated parameter for the International Mobile station Equipment Identity and Software Version number (IMEISV).  The IMEI and IMEISV were introduced as part of the specification for the GSM and are also now incorporated by the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) as part of the 3GPP specification for GSM, Universal Mobile Telecommunications System (UMTS), and 3GPP Long Term Evolution (LTE) networks.  The IMEI and IMEISV are used to uniquely identify Mobile Equipment within these systems and are managed by the GSMA.  URNs from this namespace almost always contain personally identifiable information and need to be treated accordingly.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjBlackberrydnamemM. Montemurro¢kaffiliationjBlackberrydnamehA. Allen¢kaffiliationfEircomdnamekD. McDonald¢kaffiliationoGSM AssociationdnameiP. Gosdenddatej’]hfilenamegrfc7254eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129081hkeywordsácGSMdUMTScLTEd3GPPfMobilejidentifierkinstance IDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS}SégrankingVcrfcd7254irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex£A Uniform Resource Name Namespace for the Global System for Mobile Communications Association (GSMA) and the International Mobile station Equipment Identity (IMEI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx¯This document specifies OCB, a shared-key blockcipher-based encryption scheme that provides confidentiality and authenticity for plaintexts and authenticity for associated data.  This document is a product of the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG).gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSacramento StatednamejT. Krovetz¢kaffiliationhUC DavisdnamejP. Rogawayddatej’Zhfilenamegrfc7253eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcfrgdfullscfrg - Crypto ForumdnamelCrypto Forumdtypebrgbidjdoc-129080hkeywordsÉcOCBdAEADxauthenticated-encryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSá¬7grankingUcrfcd7253irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex*The OCB Authenticated-Encryption AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyπThe Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) is a specialized web transfer protocol for use with constrained nodes and constrained (e.g., low-power, lossy) networks. The nodes often have 8-bit microcontrollers with small amounts of ROM and RAM, while constrained networks such as IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs) often have high packet error rates and a typical throughput of 10s of kbit/s. The protocol is designed for machine- to-machine (M2M) applications such as smart energy and building automation.

CoAP provides a request/response interaction model between application endpoints, supports built-in discovery of services and resources, and includes key concepts of the Web such as URIs and Internet media types. CoAP is designed to easily interface with HTTP for integration with the Web while meeting specialized requirements such as multicast support, very low overhead, and simplicity for constrained environments.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcARMdnameiZ. Shelby¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnameiK. Hartke¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. BormannddatejŒChfilenamegrfc7252eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129079hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagespopublicationDateS¨∏UgrankingTcrfcd7252irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+The Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÖd9175d9876d7959d8613d8974∂habstractyThis memo describes the use of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) in the Counter and CBC-MAC Mode (CCM) of operation within Transport Layer Security (TLS) to provide confidentiality and data-origin authentication.  The AES-CCM algorithm is amenable to compact implementations, making it suitable for constrained environments, while at the same time providing a high level of security.  The cipher suites defined in this document use Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) and are advantageous in networks with limited bandwidth.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationvRuhr-University BochumdnameiD. Bailey¢kaffiliationnCerticom Corp.dnamekM. Campagna¢kaffiliationnCerticom Corp.dnamehR. DugalddatejŒÉhfilenamegrfc7251eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129078hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateS¨∏UgrankingScrfcd7251irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?AES-CCM Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Cipher Suites for TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a new certificate type and two TLS extensions for exchanging raw public keys in Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS).  The new certificate type allows raw public keys to be used for authentication.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgRed HatdnamejP. Wouters¢kaffiliationhARM Ltd.dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationxElectronic Frontier FoundationdnamejJ. Gilmore¢kaffiliationgParsonsdnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejT. Kivinenddatej’Uhfilenamegrfc7250eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-129077hkeywordsÑcTLSfDNSSECdDANEnRaw Public KeykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS¨∏SgrankingRcrfcd7250irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdUsing Raw Public Keys in Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyORFC 7020 provides information about the Internet Numbers Registry System and how it is used in the distribution of autonomous system (AS) numbers and globally unique unicast Internet Protocol (IP) address space.

This companion document identifies the IANA registries that are part of the Internet Numbers Registry System at this time.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. HousleyddatejŒshfilenamegrfc7249eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129076hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSz!grankingQcrfcd7249irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInternet Numbers RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9812∂habstractx»This document defines a bidirectional protocol mapping for the exchange of presence information between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP).fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehA. Houri¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ. Hildebrandddatej’Whfilenamegrfc7248eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstoxdfullrstox - SIP-TO-XMPPdnamekSIP-TO-XMPPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129075hkeywordsádXMPPfJabbercSIPfSIMPLEbIMqInstant MessaginghPresencekobsoletedByÅd8048epagesopublicationDateS}SågrankingPcrfcd7248irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~Interworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): PresencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7As a foundation for the definition of bidirectional protocol mappings between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP), this document specifies the architectural assumptions underlying such mappings as well as the mapping of addresses and error conditions.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationd&yetdnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehA. Houri¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ. Hildebrandddatej’Zhfilenamegrfc7247eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdstoxdfullrstox - SIP-TO-XMPPdnamekSIP-TO-XMPPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129074hkeywordsÇdXMPPcSIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS}SãgrankingOcrfcd7247irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex°Interworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): Architecture, Addresses, and Error HandlingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUAn IP Multicast Distribution Tree (MDT) may traverse both label switching (i.e., Multiprotocol Label Switching, or MPLS) and non-label switching regions of a network.  Typically, the MDT begins and ends in non-MPLS regions, but travels through an MPLS region.  In such cases, it can be useful to begin building the MDT as a pure IP MDT, then convert it to an MPLS Multipoint Label Switched Path (MP-LSP) when it enters an MPLS-enabled region, and then convert it back to a pure IP MDT when it enters a non-MPLS-enabled region.  Other documents specify the procedures for building such a hybrid MDT, using Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) in the non-MPLS region of the network, and using Multipoint Label Distribution Protocol (mLDP) in the MPLS region.  This document extends those procedures to handle the case where the link connecting the two regions is a Virtual Routing and Forwarding (VRF) table link, as defined in the "BGP IP/MPLS VPN" specification.  However, this document is primarily aimed at particular use cases where VRFs are used to support multicast applications other than multicast VPN.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Hitchen¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationoThomson ReutersdnamehA. Gulko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. TantsuraddatejŒÅhfilenamegrfc7246eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129073hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSåçgrankingNcrfcd7246irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpMultipoint Label Distribution Protocol In-Band Signaling in a Virtual Routing and Forwarding (VRF) Table ContextdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7438∂habstracty¯Session recording is a critical requirement in many communications environments such as call centers and financial trading.  In some of these environments, all calls must be recorded for regulatory, compliance, and consumer protection reasons.  Recording of a session is typically performed by sending a copy of a media stream to a recording device.  This document describes architectures for deploying session recording solutions in an environment that is based on the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeUnifydnameiA. Hutton¢kaffiliationlNICE SystemsdnamejL. Portman¢kaffiliationkIPC SystemsdnamegR. Jain¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehK. Rehorddatej’1hfilenamegrfc7245eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsiprecdfullvsiprec - SIP RecordingdnamemSIP Recordingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129072hkeywordsÅcsipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS}U°grankingMcrfcd7245irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIAn Architecture for Media Recording Using the Session Initiation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedB