s application- level protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypertext information systems.  This document defines HTTP/1.1 conditional requests, including metadata header fields for indicating state changes, request header fields for making preconditions on such state, and rules for constructing the responses to a conditional request when one or more preconditions evaluate to false.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’Xhfilenamegrfc7232eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129059hkeywordsÉxHyperText Transfer ProtocoldHTTPxHTTP conditional requestskobsoletedByÅd9110epagesopublicationDateSíh‘granking@crfcd7232irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP/1.1): Conditional RequestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThe Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) is a stateless \%application- level protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypertext information systems.  This document defines the semantics of HTTP/1.1 messages, as expressed by request methods, request header fields, response status codes, and response header fields, along with the payload of messages (metadata and body content) and mechanisms for content negotiation.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’Xhfilenamegrfc7231eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-129058hkeywordsáxHypertext Transfer ProtocoldHTTPnHTTP semanticslHTTP payloadlHTTP contentkHTTP methodpHTTP status codekobsoletedByÅd9110epageseopublicationDateSíh”granking?crfcd7231irfcN