Advanced Multipath is a formalization of multipath techniques currently in use in IP and MPLS networks and a set of extensions to existing multipath techniques.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjOCCNC, LLCdnamemC. Villamizar¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamejD. McDysan¢kaffiliationsTata CommunicationsdnamegS. Ning¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamegL. Yongddatej’Vhfilenamegrfc7226eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-129053hkeywordsádMPLSrAdvanced Multipathncomposite linkplink aggregationdECMPmlink bundlingldelay metrickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSl«granking:crfcd7226irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Requirements for Advanced Multipath in MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This document defines a new Port Control Protocol (PCP) option to learn the IPv6 prefix(es) used by a PCP-controlled NAT64 device to build IPv4-converted IPv6 addresses.  This option is needed for successful communications when IPv4 addresses are used in referrals.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. BoucadairddatejŒÄhfilenamegrfc7225eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129052hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSvÖÀgranking9crfcd7225irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEDiscovering NAT64 IPv6 Prefixes Using the Port Control Protocol (PCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxJThis document defines the initial version of the iana-if-type YANG module.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej’Mhfilenamegrfc7224eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129051hkeywordsÉdyanggnetconfliana-if-typekobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateSsËÿgranking8crfcd7224irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIANA Interface Type YANG ModuledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document defines a YANG data model for the management of network interfaces.  It is expected that interface-type-specific data models augment the generic interfaces data model defined in this document.  The data model includes configuration data and state data (status information and counters for the collection of statistics).fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej’Shfilenamegrfc7223eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129050hkeywordsÇgNETCONFoietf-interfaceskobsoletedByÅd8343epages'opublicationDateSsË◊granking7crfcd7223irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A YANG Data Model for Interface ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›This specification defines a new mobility option, the Quality-of- Service (QoS) option, for Proxy Mobile IPv6.  This option can be used by the local mobility anchor and the mobile access gateway for negotiating Quality-of-Service parameters for a mobile node's IP flows.  The negotiated QoS parameters can be used for QoS policing and marking of packets to enforce QoS differentiation on the path between the local mobility anchor and the mobile access gateway.  Furthermore, making QoS parameters available on the mobile access gateway enables mapping of these parameters to QoS rules that are specific to the access technology and allows those rules to be enforced on the access network using access-technology-specific approaches.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejM. Liebsch¢kaffiliationfOrangednamehP. Seite¢kaffiliationhKDDI LabdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationwBroadcom CommunicationsdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelliddatej’_hfilenamegrfc7222eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129049hkeywordsÜcQoSrQuality of ServicehPMIP-QoSjPMIPv6-QoShWiFi-QoSh3GPP-QoSkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateSvÖ granking6crfcd7222irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Quality-of-Service Option for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∏The productive output of an IETF working group is documents, as mandated by the working group's charter.  When a working group is ready to develop a particular document, the most common mechanism is for it to "adopt" an existing document as a starting point.  The document that a working group adopts and then develops further is based on initial input at varying levels of maturity.  An initial working group draft might be a document already in wide use, or it might be a blank sheet, wholly created by the working group, or it might represent any level of maturity in between.  This document discusses how a working group typically handles the formal documents that it targets for publication.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej’Vhfilenamegrfc7221eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129048hkeywordsádIETFgprocessmworking groupnInternet-DrafthadoptionhhandlinghcreationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS_‹Tgranking5crfcd7221irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Handling of Internet-Drafts by IETF Working GroupsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∂This document extends the Port Control Protocol (PCP) with the ability to associate a description with a PCP-instantiated mapping.  It does this by defining a new DESCRIPTION option.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. WingddatejŒÄhfilenamegrfc7220eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129047hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSs‹ggranking4crfcd7220irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Description Option for the Port Control Protocol (PCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5This memo specifies SEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) Source Address Validation Improvement (SAVI), a mechanism to provide source address validation using the SEND protocol.  The proposed mechanism complements ingress filtering techniques to provide a finer granularity on the control of IPv6 source addresses.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamerA. Garcia-Martinezddatej’Uhfilenamegrfc7219eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsavidfullx-savi - Source Address Validation Improvementsdnamex&Source Address Validation Improvementsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129046hkeywordsÑdIPv6qingress filteringppacket filteringrNeighbor DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateSl∆granking3crfcd7219irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMSEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) Source Address Validation Improvement (SAVI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyExperience has shown that people get confused when discussing the three numeric fields of the TLSA record.  This document specifies descriptive acronyms for the three numeric fields in TLSA records.  This document updates the format of the IANA registry created by RFC 6698.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmShinkuro Inc.dnamenO. Gudmundssonddatej’Khfilenamegrfc7218eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-129045hkeywordsÉfDNSSECdDANElApplicationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSVtgranking2crfcd7218irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaAdding Acronyms to Simplify Conversations about DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a method for generating IPv6 Interface Identifiers to be used with IPv6 Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC), such that an IPv6 address configured using this method is stable within each subnet, but the corresponding Interface Identifier changes when the host moves from one network to another.  This method is meant to be an alternative to generating Interface Identifiers based on hardware addresses (e.g., IEEE LAN Media Access Control (MAC) addresses), such that the benefits of stable addresses can be achieved without sacrificing the security and privacy of users.  The method specified in this document applies to all prefixes a host may be employing, including link-local, global, and unique-local prefixes (and their corresponding addresses).fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. GontddatejŒuhfilenamegrfc7217eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129044hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSae…granking1crfcd7217irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwA Method for Generating Semantically Opaque Interface Identifiers with IPv6 Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\The residential gateway is a device that has become an integral part of home networking equipment. Discovering a Location Information Server (LIS) is a necessary part of acquiring location information for location-based services. However, discovering a LIS when a residential gateway is present poses a configuration challenge, requiring a method that is able to work around the obstacle presented by the gateway.

This document describes a solution to this problem. The solution provides alternative domain names as input to the LIS discovery process based on the network addresses assigned to a Device.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationjNominet UKdnameiR. Bellisddatej’Shfilenamegrfc7216eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-129043hkeywordsÖdHELDcLISiDiscoverycNATsResidential GatewaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS_‹Rgranking0crfcd7216irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNLocation Information Server (LIS) Discovery Using IP Addresses and Reverse DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThis document is a snapshot of different Locator/Identifier Separation Protocol (LISP) deployment scenarios.  It discusses the placement of new network elements introduced by the protocol, representing the thinking of the LISP working group as of Summer 2013.  LISP deployment scenarios may have evolved since then.  This memo represents one stable point in that evolution of understanding.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehL. Jakab¢kaffiliationx!Technical University of CataloniadnametA. Cabellos-Aparicio¢kaffiliationx!Technical University of CataloniadnamehF. Coras¢kaffiliationx!Technical University of CataloniadnamerJ. Domingo-Pascual¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Lewisddatej‘»hfilenamegrfc7215eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-129042hkeywordsÇdLISPjdeploymentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSUõøgranking/crfcd7215irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWLocator/Identifier Separation Protocol (LISP) Network Element Deployment ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 5586 generalized the applicability of the pseudowire Associated Channel Header (PW-ACH) into the Generic Associated Channel G-ACh. However, registries and allocations of G-ACh parameters had been distributed throughout different, sometimes unrelated, registries. This document coalesces these into a new "Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) Parameters" registry under the "Multiprotocol Label Switching Architecture (MPLS)" heading. This document updates RFC 5586.

This document also updates RFCs 6374, 6378, 6427, and 6428.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. PignataroddatejŒshfilenamegrfc7214eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129041hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSá¬5granking.crfcd7214irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKMoving Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) IANA Registries to a New RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is the set of MPLS protocol functions applicable to the construction and operation of packet- switched transport networks.  This document presents considerations for link-layer addressing of Ethernet frames carrying MPLS-TP packets.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhBlue SundnamehD. Frost¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocciddatej’:hfilenamegrfc7213eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129040hkeywordsÅdMPLSkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateS†œ0granking-crfcd7213irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Next-Hop Ethernet AddressingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€The MPLS Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) provides an auxiliary logical data channel associated with a Label Switched Path (LSP), a pseudowire, or a section (link) over which a variety of protocols may flow.  These protocols are commonly used to provide Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) mechanisms associated with the primary data channel.  This document specifies simple procedures by which an endpoint of an LSP, pseudowire, or section may inform the other endpoints of its capabilities and configuration parameters, or other application-specific information.  This information may then be used by the receiver to validate or adjust its local configuration, and by the network operator for diagnostic purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhBlue SundnamehD. Frost¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. BocciddatejŒ>hfilenamegrfc7212eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129039hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS†œ/granking,crfcd7212irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>MPLS Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) Advertisement ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{The IETF is engaged in an effort to analyze the security of routing protocol authentication according to design guidelines discussed in RFC 6518, "Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design Guidelines".  Developing an operational and management model for routing protocol security that works with all the routing protocols will be critical to the deployability of these efforts.  This document gives recommendations to operators and implementors regarding management and operation of router authentication.  These recommendations will also assist protocol designers in understanding management issues they will face.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. Ltd.dnamehD. ZhangddatejŒnhfilenamegrfc7211eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdkarpdfullx6karp - Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdnamex/Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129038hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS†œ-granking+crfcd7211irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Operations Model for Router KeyingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document specifies the information contained in a conceptual database of long-lived cryptographic keys used by many different routing protocols for message security.  The database is designed to support both manual and automated key management.  In addition to describing the schema for the database, this document describes the operations that can be performed on the database as well as the requirements for the routing protocols that wish to use the database.  In many typical scenarios, the protocols do not directly use the long-lived key, but rather a key derivation function is used to derive a short-lived key from a long-lived key.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polk¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. Ltd.dnamehD. ZhangddatejŒphfilenamegrfc7210eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdkarpdfullx6karp - Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdnamex/Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129037hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSUõægranking*crfcd7210irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Database of Long-Lived Symmetric Cryptographic KeysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.The widespread adoption of Ethernet L2VPN services and the advent of new applications for the technology (e.g., data center interconnect) have culminated in a new set of requirements that are not readily addressable by the current Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) solution.  In particular, multihoming with all-active forwarding is not supported, and there's no existing solution to leverage Multipoint-to-Multipoint (MP2MP) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) for optimizing the delivery of multi-destination frames.  Furthermore, the provisioning of VPLS, even in the context of BGP-based auto-discovery, requires network operators to specify various network parameters on top of the access configuration.  This document specifies the requirements for an Ethernet VPN (EVPN) solution, which addresses the above issues.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationfArktandnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationiBloombergdnamehA. Isaacddatej’Yhfilenamegrfc7209eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129036hkeywordsÅnethernet l2vpnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS}Sägranking)crfcd7209irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Requirements for Ethernet VPN (EVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
Email on the Internet can be forged in a number of ways. In particular, existing protocols place no restriction on what a sending host can use as the "MAIL FROM" of a message or the domain given on the SMTP HELO/EHLO commands. This document describes version 1 of the Sender Policy Framework (SPF) protocol, whereby ADministrative Management Domains (ADMDs) can explicitly authorize the hosts that are allowed to use their domain names, and a receiving host can check such authorization.

This document obsoletes RFC 4408.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxKitterman Technical ServicesdnamelS. Kittermanddatej’Ohfilenamegrfc7208eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfspfbisdfullsspfbis - SPF UpdatednamejSPF Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-129035hkeywordsÑhspoofingcspflanti-forgerynauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateSZ€øgranking(crfcd7208irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPSender Policy Framework (SPF) for Authorizing Use of Domains in Email, Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8616d7372d8553∂habstractyThis document defines and registers with IANA a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for usage within messages standardized by the Eurosystem.  The URN namespace is managed by Deutsche Bundesbank, which is a member of the European System of Central Banks (ESCB).fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche BundesbankdnamelM. Ortseifen¢kaffiliationsDeutsche BundesbankdnamekG. Dickfeldddatej’Yhfilenamegrfc7207eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129034hkeywordsÖmURN NamespacejEurosystemgTARGET2rTARGET2-SecuritiesdESCBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSP]˚granking'crfcd7207irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for Eurosystem MessagingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document specifies the requirements for an end-to-end session identifier in IP-based multimedia communication networks.  This identifier would enable endpoints, intermediate devices, and management and monitoring systems to identify a session end-to-end across multiple SIP devices, hops, and administrative domains.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamehL. Liess¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiH. KaplanddatejŒxhfilenamegrfc7206eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymginsipiddfullx*insipid - INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdnamex INtermediary-safe SIP session IDdtypebwgbidjdoc-129033hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSs±Õgranking&crfcd7206irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcRequirements for an End-to-End Session Identification in IP-Based Multimedia Communication NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTelepresence conferencing systems seek to create an environment that gives users (or user groups) that are not co-located a feeling of co-located presence through multimedia communication that includes at least audio and video signals of high fidelity.  A number of techniques for handling audio and video streams are used to create this experience.  When these techniques are not similar, interoperability between different systems is difficult at best, and often not possible.  Conveying information about the relationships between multiple streams of media would enable senders and receivers to make choices to allow telepresence systems to interwork.  This memo describes the most typical and important use cases for sending multiple streams in a telepresence conference.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Romanow¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiS. Botzko¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamelM. Duckworth¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. EvenddatejŒ|hfilenamegrfc7205eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcluedfullx4clue - ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencednamex-ControLling mUltiple streams for tElepresencedtypebwgbidjdoc-129032hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSP]˙granking%crfcd7205irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Use Cases for Telepresence MultistreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis memo outlines high-level requirements for the integration of flexible Mandatory Access Control (MAC) functionality into the Network File System (NFS) version 4.2 (NFSv4.2).  It describes the level of protections that should be provided over protocol components and the basic structure of the proposed system.  The intent here is not to present the protocol changes but to describe the environment in which they reside.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiT. Haynesddatej’Uhfilenamegrfc7204eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-129031hkeywordsÅeNFSv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSP]˘granking$crfcd7204irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRequirements for Labeled NFSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document extends the Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) defined in RFC 5070 to exchange enriched cybersecurity information among security experts at organizations and facilitate their operations.  It provides a well-defined pattern to consistently embed structured information, such as identifier- and XML-based information.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdNICTdnamelT. Takahashi¢kaffiliationfMcAfeednamelK. Landfield¢kaffiliationeNAISTdnamenY. Kadobayashiddatej’Phfilenamegrfc7203eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-129030hkeywordsåndata structurexinformation architectureqincident responsemresponse teamqsecurity incidenttinformation exchangeqknowledge sharingrsecurity operationjautomationmvulnerabilitydCERTeCSIRTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSUõΩgranking#crfcd7203irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiAn Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) Extension for Structured Cybersecurity InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis memo discusses the problem of securing real-time multimedia sessions.  It also explains why the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) and the associated RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) do not mandate a single media security mechanism.  This is relevant for designers and reviewers of future RTP extensions to ensure that appropriate security mechanisms are mandated and that any such mechanisms are specified in a manner that conforms with the RTP architecture.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej’Ohfilenamegrfc7202eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129029hkeywordsÉdSRTPkRTP ProfilenPayload FormatkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateSLº—granking"crfcd7202irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUSecuring the RTP Framework: Why RTP Does Not Mandate a Single Media Security SolutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is used in a large number of different application domains and environments.  This heterogeneity implies that different security mechanisms are needed to provide services such as confidentiality, integrity, and source authentication of RTP and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) packets suitable for the various environments.  The range of solutions makes it difficult for RTP-based application developers to pick the most suitable mechanism.  This document provides an overview of a number of security solutions for RTP and gives guidance for developers on how to choose the appropriate security mechanism.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej’Rhfilenamegrfc7201eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-129028hkeywordsÖjSecure RTPdSRTPnkey managementireal-timeemediakobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateSLºŒgranking!crfcd7201irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Options for Securing RTP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—This specification defines a load-control event package for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  It allows SIP entities to distribute load-filtering policies to other SIP entities in the network.  The load-filtering policies contain rules to throttle calls from a specific user or based on their source or destination domain, telephone number prefix.  The mechanism helps to prevent signaling overload and complements feedback-based SIP overload control efforts.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamegC. Shen¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehA. Koikeddatej’Rhfilenamegrfc7200eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsocdfullxsoc - SIP Overload ControldnametSIP Overload Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129027hkeywordsÑcSIPpOverload ControlfServerkPerformancekobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateSaéÖgranking crfcd7200irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Load-Control Event PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–Current location configuration protocols are capable of provisioning an Internet host with a location URI that refers to the host's location.  These protocols lack a mechanism for the target host to inspect or set the privacy rules that are applied to the URIs they distribute.  This document extends the current location configuration protocols to provide hosts with a reference to the rules that are applied to a URI so that the host can view or set these rules.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameoJ. Winterbottom¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’hfilenamegrfc7199eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-129026hkeywordsÑggeoprivkgeolocationgprivacyfpolicykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSZ€ºgrankingcrfcd7199irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Location Configuration Extensions for Policy ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
Packet loss is undesirable for real-time multimedia sessions but can occur due to a variety of reasons including unplanned network outages.  In unicast transmissions, recovering from such an outage can be difficult depending on the outage duration, due to the potentially large number of missing packets.  In multicast transmissions, recovery is even more challenging as many receivers could be impacted by the outage.  For this challenge, one solution that does not incur unbounded delay is to duplicate the packets and send them in separate redundant streams, provided that the underlying network satisfies certain requirements.  This document explains how Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) streams can be duplicated without breaking RTP or RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) rules.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej’Zhfilenamegrfc7198eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129025hkeywordsÉoRTP duplicationilive/livejredundancykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSZ¨∞grankingcrfcd7198irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewDuplicating RTP StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA straightforward approach to provide protection against packet losses due to network outages with a longest duration of T time units is to duplicate the original packets and send each copy separated in time by at least T time units.  This approach is commonly referred to as "time-shifted redundancy", "temporal redundancy", or simply "delayed duplication".  This document defines an attribute to indicate the presence of temporally redundant media streams and the duplication delay in the Session Description Protocol.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameeH. Ouddatej’Lhfilenamegrfc7197eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129024hkeywordsÖlInterleavingrtemporal diversitystemporal redundancyltime shiftedsdelayed duplicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSZ¨Ægrankingcrfcd7197irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Duplication Delay Attribute in the Session Description ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5Route Flap Damping (RFD) was first proposed to reduce BGP churn in routers.  Unfortunately, RFD was found to severely penalize sites for being well connected because topological richness amplifies the number of update messages exchanged.  Many operators have turned RFD off.  Based on experimental measurement, this document recommends adjusting a few RFD algorithmic constants and limits in order to reduce the high risks with RFD.  The result is damping a non-trivial amount of long-term churn without penalizing well-behaved prefixes' normal convergence process.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamejC. Pelsser¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationpSproute NetworksdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationwLoughborough UniversitydnamejO. Maennelddatej’Thfilenamegrfc7196eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129023hkeywordsÅcrfdkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSvãRgrankingcrfcd7196irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Making Route Flap Damping UsabledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo describes use cases, requirements, and protocol extensions for using the Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer/answer model for establishing audio and video media streams over circuit-switched bearers in the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN).fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoS. Veikkolainenddatej’8hfilenamegrfc7195eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-129022hkeywordsÅdPSTNkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateSgÀÜgrankingcrfcd7195irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex§Session Description Protocol (SDP) Extension for Setting Audio and Video Media Streams over Circuit-Switched Bearers in the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxûThis document describes the commonly accepted practice of listening on TCP port 6697 for incoming Internet Relay Chat (IRC) connections encrypted via TLS/SSL.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekR. HartmannddatejŒvhfilenamegrfc7194eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-129021hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS„“ygrankingcrfcd7194irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex6Default Port for Internet Relay Chat (IRC) via TLS/SSLdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxáThis document registers the application/cms media type for use with the corresponding CMS (Cryptographic Message Syntax) content types.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej’Vhfilenamegrfc7193eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129020hkeywordsÅxCryptographic Message SyntaxkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS?Ggrankingcrfcd7193irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe application/cms Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This document describes the conventions for using several cryptographic algorithms with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) key package receipt and error content types.  Specifically, it includes conventions necessary to implement SignedData, EnvelopedData, EncryptedData, and AuthEnvelopedData.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej’Nhfilenamegrfc7192eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129019hkeywordsÉkKey PackagesKey Package ReceiptqKey Package ErrorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS?Ggrankingcrfcd7192irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Algorithms for Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Key Package Receipt and Error Content TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰This document defines the syntax for two Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) content types: one for key package receipts and another for key package errors.  The key package receipt content type is used to confirm receipt of an identified key package or collection of key packages.  The key package error content type is used to indicate an error occurred during the processing of a key package.  CMS can be used to digitally sign, digest, authenticate, or encrypt these content types.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. HousleyddatejŒmhfilenamegrfc7191eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-129018hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS?Ggrankingcrfcd7191irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNCryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Key Package Receipt and Error Content TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/Many MPLS implementations have supported multipath techniques, and many MPLS deployments have used multipath techniques, particularly in very high-bandwidth applications, such as provider IP/MPLS core networks. MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) has strongly discouraged the use of multipath techniques. Some degradation of MPLS-TP Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) performance cannot be avoided when operating over many types of multipath implementations.

Using MPLS Entropy Labels (RFC 6790), MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs) can be carried over multipath links while also providing a fully MPLS-TP-compliant server layer for MPLS-TP LSPs. This document describes the means of supporting MPLS as a server layer for MPLS-TP. The use of MPLS-TP LSPs as a server layer for MPLS LSPs is also discussed.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx!Outer Cape Cod Network ConsultingdnamemC. Villamizarddatej’Thfilenamegrfc7190eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-129017hkeywordsádMPLSncomposite linkplink aggregationdECMPmlink bundlingimultipathgMPLS-TPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS9 £grankingcrfcd7190irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Use of Multipath with MPLS and MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThis document specifies how signaling and selection processes for Pseudowire (PW) Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV) are modified to ensure backward compatibility and allow use of proactive Connectivity Verification (CV), Continuity Check (CC), and Remote Defect Indication (RDI) over MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) PWs.  This document introduces four new CV types and, to accommodate them, a new VCCV Extended CV parameter for PW Interface Parameters Sub-TLV is defined.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiG. MirskydnameiG. Mirskyddatej’Shfilenamegrfc7189eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-129016hkeywordsÇgPW VCCVqMPLS-TP CC/CV/RDIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS9 £grankingcrfcd7189irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnVirtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV) Capability Advertisement for MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This specification describes extensions to definitions of TLVs used by the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2) and the MANET Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) to increase their abilities to accommodate protocol extensions.  This document updates RFC 7181 (OLSRv2) and RFC 6130 (NHDP).fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatej’\hfilenamegrfc7188eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129015hkeywordsÑeMANETfOLSRv2dNHDPcTLVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSF„?grankingcrfcd7188irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxOptimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2 (OLSRv2) and MANET Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) Extension TLVsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7722∂habstractyThis specification updates the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2) with an optimization to improve the selection of routing multipoint relays.  The optimization retains full interoperability between implementations of OLSRv2 with and without this optimization.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. ClausenddatejŒ}hfilenamegrfc7187eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129014hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSF„=grankingcrfcd7187irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfRouting Multipoint Relay Optimization for the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2 (OLSRv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document analyzes common security threats of the Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) and describes their potential impacts on Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) routing protocols using NHDP.  This document is not intended to propose solutions to the threats described.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwLIX Ecole PolytechniquednameeJ. Yi¢kaffiliationxFujitsu Laboratories of AmericadnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationwLIX Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. ClausenddatejŒ>hfilenamegrfc7186eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129013hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSF„;grankingcrfcd7186irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Security Threats for the Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7985∂habstractyJThe Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2) includes the ability to assign metrics to links and to use those metrics to allow routing by other than minimum hop count routes.  This document provides a historic record of the rationale for, and design considerations behind, how link metrics were included in OLSRv2.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationxAlcatel-Lucent Bell LabsdnamejP. Jacquetddatej’0hfilenamegrfc7185eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129012hkeywordsâeMANETnad hoc networkiproactivedNHDPvneighborhood discoverydOLSRfOLSRv2prouting protocolgmetricskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSF„:grankingcrfcd7185irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVLink Metrics for the Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Routing Protocol OLSRv2 - RationaledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the Management Information Base (MIB) module for configuring and managing the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2).  The OLSRv2-MIB module is structured into configuration information, state information, performance information, and notifications.  This additional state and performance information is useful for troubleshooting problems and performance issues of the routing protocol.  Two levels of compliance allow this MIB module to be deployed on constrained routers.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxFujitsu Laboratories of AmericadnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationnUS Army CERDECdnamegR. Cole¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatej’7hfilenamegrfc7184eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129011hkeywordsárNetwork ManagementxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2gRoutingeMANETx/Optimized Link STate Routing Protocol version 2kobsoletedByÄepagesVopublicationDateSF„:grankingcrfcd7184irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUDefinition of Managed Objects for the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document specifies integrity and replay protection for the Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) and the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol version 2 (OLSRv2). This protection is achieved by using an HMAC-SHA-256 Integrity Check Value (ICV) TLV and a Timestamp TLV based on Portable Operating System Interface (POSIX) time.

The mechanism in this specification can also be used for other protocols that use the generalized packet/message format described in RFC 5444.

This document updates RFC 6130 and RFC 7181 by mandating the implementation of this integrity and replay protection in NHDP and OLSRv2.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxFujitsu Laboratories of AmericadnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatej’@hfilenamegrfc7183eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129010hkeywordsÖeMANETfOLSRv2hSecuritytIntegrity protectioncICVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSF„9grankingcrfcd7183irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄIntegrity Protection for the Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) and Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2 (OLSRv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂This document revises, extends, and replaces RFC 6622.  It describes general and flexible TLVs for representing cryptographic Integrity Check Values (ICVs) and timestamps, using the generalized Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) packet/message format defined in RFC 5444.  It defines two Packet TLVs, two Message TLVs, and two Address Block TLVs for affixing ICVs and timestamps to a packet, a message, and one or more addresses, respectively.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxFujitsu Laboratories of AmericadnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationx&BAE Systems Advanced Technology CentrednamekC. Dearloveddatej’Ihfilenamegrfc7182eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129009hkeywordsÖdNHDPfOLSRv2hsecurityiintegritygroutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSF„8grankingcrfcd7182irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWIntegrity Check Value and Timestamp TLV Definitions for Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÅThis specification describes version 2 of the Optimized Link State Routing Protocol (OLSRv2) for Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs).fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxAlcatel-Lucent Bell LabsdnamejP. Jacquet¢kaffiliationxFujitsu Laboratories of AmericadnamejU. Herbergddatej’-hfilenamegrfc7181eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129008hkeywordsÉeMANETnad hoc networkdNHDPkobsoletedByÄepagessopublicationDateSF„5grankingcrfcd7181irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3The Optimized Link State Routing Protocol Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÑd7183d7466d7187d7188∂habstracty|The IETF Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) protocol provides least-cost pair-wise data forwarding without configuration in multi-hop networks with arbitrary topology and link technology, safe forwarding even during periods of temporary loops, and support for multipathing of both unicast and multicast traffic. TRILL accomplishes this by using Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) link-state routing and by encapsulating traffic using a header that includes a hop count. Since publication of the TRILL base protocol in July 2011, active development of TRILL has revealed errata in RFC 6325 and some cases that could use clarifications or updates.

RFCs 6327 and 6439 provide clarifications and updates with respect to adjacency and Appointed Forwarders. This document provides other known clarifications, corrections, and updates to RFCs 6325, 6327, and 6439.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationkIonos Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnamekA. Banerjeeddatej’hfilenamegrfc7180eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129007hkeywordsàeTRILLgRBridgeeIS-ISlreachabilityhoverloadcMTUcDEIimulticastkobsoletedByÅd7780epagesopublicationDateSk!ˇgrankingcrfcd7180irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Clarifications, Corrections, and UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe IETF Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) base protocol (RFC 6325) specifies minimal hooks to safely support TRILL Header extensions.  This document specifies an initial extension providing additional flag bits and specifies some of those bits.  It updates RFC 6325.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationkIonos Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationoNexenta SystemsdnamejC. Bestlerddatej’hfilenamegrfc7179eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129006hkeywordsÉgRBridgeiextensionfoptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSk!˝grankingcrfcd7179irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Header ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7780∂habstracty;This document specifies a general channel mechanism for sending messages, such as Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) messages, between Routing Bridges (RBridges) and between RBridges and end stations in an RBridge campus through extensions to the Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) protocol.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationkIonos Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wardddatej’hfilenamegrfc7178eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129005hkeywordsÇeTRILLfnativekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSk!˚granking
crfcd7178irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): RBridge Channel SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7978∂habstracty^The IETF Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) protocol supports arbitrary link technologies between TRILL switches, including point-to-point links and multi-access Local Area Network (LAN) links that can have multiple TRILL switches and end stations attached.  TRILL uses Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) routing.  This document specifies the establishment, reporting, and termination of IS-IS adjacencies between TRILL switches, also known as RBridges (Routing Bridges).  It also concerns four other link-local aspects of TRILL: Designated RBridge (DRB) selection, MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit) testing, pseudonode creation, and BFD (Bidirectional Forwarding Detection) session bootstrapping in connection with adjacency.  State diagrams are included where appropriate.  This document obsoletes RFC 6327 and updates RFC 6325.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationjIntel LabsdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegH. Yang¢kaffiliationkIonos Corp.dnameiV. Manralddatej’Ohfilenamegrfc7177eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129004hkeywordsÜgRBridgeeTRILLiAdjacencycBFDcp2pnpoint-to-pointkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateSk!˚granking	crfcd7177irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): AdjacencydtypecrfciupdatedByÖd7780d8139d8564d8377d8249∂habstracty␀The IETF Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) protocol provides optimal pair-wise data frame forwarding without configuration in multi-hop networks with arbitrary topology and link technology; it also provides support for multipathing of both unicast and multicast traffic.  This document specifies the data formats and code points for the IS-IS extensions to support TRILL.  These data formats and code points may also be used by technologies other than TRILL.  This document obsoletes RFC 6326.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoT. Senevirathne¢kaffiliationdDelldnamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnamegD. Dutt¢kaffiliationpInsieme NetworksdnamekA. Banerjeeddatej’Thfilenamegrfc7176eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-129003hkeywordsÖhAffinityimulticastnmulti-topologylfine-graineddVLANkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateSk!˙grankingcrfcd7176irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexATransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Use of IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸThis document specifies use of the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol in Routing Bridge (RBridge) campuses based on the RBridge Channel extension to the Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) protocol.

BFD is a widely deployed Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) mechanism in IP and MPLS networks, using UDP and Associated Channel Header (ACH) encapsulation respectively. This document specifies the BFD encapsulation over TRILL.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkIonos Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationnHuawei R&D USAdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnamekA. Banerjeeddatej’hfilenamegrfc7175eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129002hkeywordsÉgRBridgedEchogone-hopkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSk!Ûgrankingcrfcd7175irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8564∂habstractyThis document specifies a reference framework for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) in Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) networks.  The focus of the document is on the fault and performance management aspects of TRILL OAM.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoT. Senevirathne¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej’Ihfilenamegrfc7174eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129001hkeywordségRBridgecCFMcBFDcMEPcMIPbMAeFaultkPerformancekMaintenancejContinuitylConnectivityeDelayjOperationsnAdministrationkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateSk!grankingcrfcd7174irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‘This document specifies how to interconnect a pair of Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) switch ports using pseudowires under existing TRILL and Pseudowire Emulation End-to-End (PWE3) standards.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegL. Yong¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationgBrocadednameiJ. Hudsonddatej’Uhfilenamegrfc7173eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-129000hkeywordsÑeTRILLkpseudowiresdMPLSgRBridgekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSk!Ôgrankingcrfcd7173irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Transport Using PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe IETF has standardized Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL), a protocol for least-cost transparent frame routing in multi-hop networks with arbitrary topologies and link technologies, using link-state routing and a hop count.  The TRILL base protocol standard supports the labeling of TRILL Data packets with up to 4K IDs.  However, there are applications that require a larger number of labels providing configurable isolation of data.  This document updates RFC 6325 by specifying optional extensions to the TRILL base protocol to safely accomplish this.  These extensions, called fine-grained labeling, are primarily intended for use in large data centers, that is, those with more than 4K users requiring configurable data isolation from each other.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehM. Zhang¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamejP. Agarwal¢kaffiliationjIntel LabsdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationpCumulus NetworksdnamegD. Duttddatej’3hfilenamegrfc7172eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128999hkeywordsÑeTRILLdVLANlFine-GrainedeLabelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSk!Ógrankingcrfcd7172irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Fine-Grained LabelingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document specifies PT-EAP, a Posture Transport (PT) protocol based on the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) and designed to be used only inside an EAP tunnel method protected by Transport Layer Security (TLS).  The document also describes the intended applicability of PT-EAP.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationtSymantec CorporationdnamekP. Sangsterddatej’,hfilenamegrfc7171eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcneadfullx!nea - Network Endpoint AssessmentdnamexNetwork Endpoint Assessmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-128998hkeywordsÅgNEA EAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSk!Ógrankingcrfcd7171irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcPT-EAP: Posture Transport (PT) Protocol for Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Tunnel MethodsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document defines the Tunnel Extensible Authentication Protocol (TEAP) version 1.  TEAP is a tunnel-based EAP method that enables secure communication between a peer and a server by using the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to establish a mutually authenticated tunnel.  Within the tunnel, TLV objects are used to convey authentication-related data between the EAP peer and the EAP server.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegH. Zhou¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationuInfineon TechnologiesdnamehS. Hannaddatej’Nhfilenamegrfc7170eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-128997hkeywordsÇcEAPfTunnelkobsoletedByÅd9930epageseopublicationDateSk!Îgrankingcrfcd7170irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Tunnel Extensible Authentication Protocol (TEAP) Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9427¥habstractyéThis document defines the NSA (No Secrecy Afforded) certificate extension appropriate for use in certain PKIX (X.509 Pubic Key Certificates) digital certificates.  Historically, clients and servers strived to maintain the privacy of their keys; however, the secrecy of their private keys cannot always be maintained.  In certain circumstances, a client or a server might feel that they will be compelled in the future to share their keys with a third party.  Some clients and servers also have been compelled to share their keys and wish to indicate to relying parties upon certificate renewal that their keys have in fact been shared with a third party.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIECA, Inc.dnameiS. TurnerddatejŒàhfilenamegrfc7169eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-55220hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS;7Ågrankingcrfcd7169irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex3The NSA (No Secrecy Afforded) Certificate ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxˆThe Hyper Text Coffee Pot Control Protocol (HTCPCP) specification does not allow for the brewing of tea, in all its variety and complexity.  This paper outlines an extension to HTCPCP to allow for pots to provide networked tea-brewing facilities.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehI. NazarddatejŒàhfilenamegrfc7168eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-55219hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS;7Ägranking␀crfcd7168irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexQThe Hyper Text Coffee Pot Control Protocol for Tea Efflux Appliances (HTCPCP-TEA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆThe Multiprotocol Label Switching Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is the common set of MPLS protocol functions defined to enable the construction and operation of packet transport networks.  The MPLS-TP supports both point-to-point and point-to-multipoint transport paths.  This document defines the elements and functions of the MPLS-TP architecture that are applicable specifically to supporting point-to-multipoint transport paths.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhBlue SundnamehD. Frost¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationoLabN ConsultingdnameiL. Bergerddatej’Shfilenamegrfc7167eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128996hkeywordsÇgmpls-tpdmplskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSL#°grankingˇcrfcd7167irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A Framework for Point-to-Multipoint MPLS in Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCurrently, OSPF for IPv6 (OSPFv3) uses IPsec as the only mechanism for authenticating protocol packets. This behavior is different from authentication mechanisms present in other routing protocols (OSPFv2, Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS), RIP, and Routing Information Protocol Next Generation (RIPng)). In some environments, it has been found that IPsec is difficult to configure and maintain and thus cannot be used. This document defines an alternative mechanism to authenticate OSPFv3 protocol packets so that OSPFv3 does not depend only upon IPsec for authentication.

The OSPFv3 Authentication Trailer was originally defined in RFC 6506. This document obsoletes RFC 6506 by providing a revised definition, including clarifications and refinements of the procedures.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationkIonos Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. LindemddatejŒjhfilenamegrfc7166eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128995hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS π¬granking˛crfcd7166irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Supporting Authentication Trailer for OSPFv3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZMany Internet applications have a need for object-based security mechanisms in addition to security mechanisms at the network layer or transport layer.  For many years, the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) has provided a binary secure object format based on ASN.1.  Over time, binary object encodings such as ASN.1 have become less common than text-based encodings, such as the JavaScript Object Notation (JSON).  This document defines a set of use cases and requirements for a secure object format encoded using JSON, drawn from a variety of application security mechanisms currently in development.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnameiR. Barnesddatej’Mhfilenamegrfc7165eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjosedfullx/jose - Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondnamex(Javascript Object Signing and Encryptiondtypebwgbidjdoc-128994hkeywordsãcJWScJWEcJWKcJWAcJWTcCMSfS/MIMEdJOSEdXMPPdALTOeOAuthkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSLºÀgranking˝crfcd7165irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHUse Cases and Requirements for JSON Object Signing and Encryption (JOSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬂThis document discusses issues that arise when RTP sessions span Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) leap seconds.  It updates RFC 3550 by describing how RTP senders and receivers should behave in the presence of leap seconds.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlAVA NetworksdnamehK. Gross¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamenR. Brandenburgddatej’Phfilenamegrfc7164eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128993hkeywordsäkLeap secondcrtpxReal-time Transport ProtocolcntpuNetwork Time ProtocolcUTCxUniversal Coordinated TimectaixInternational Atomic TimeiUnix timekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateS9ŒÇgranking¸crfcd7164irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletRTP and Leap SecondsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document updates the registration guidance provided in Section 4.2 of RFC 5031, which allows the registration of service URNs with the 'sos' service type only for emergency services "that are offered widely and in different countries".  This document updates those instructions to allow such registrations when, at the time of registration, those services are offered in only one country.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekI. Sedlacekddatej’Rhfilenamegrfc7163eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128992hkeywordsÜcsipiemergencycurngcountryd5031csoskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS-™granking˚crfcd7163irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+URN for Country-Specific Emergency ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOften, multiple IMAP (RFC 3501) clients need to coordinate changes to a common IMAP mailbox. Examples include different clients working on behalf of the same user and multiple users accessing shared mailboxes. These clients need a mechanism to efficiently synchronize state changes for messages within the mailbox.

Initially defined in RFC 4551, the Conditional Store facility provides a protected update mechanism for message state information and a mechanism for requesting only changes to the message state. This memo updates that mechanism and obsoletes RFC 4551, based on operational experience.

This document additionally updates another IMAP extension, Quick Resynchronization, which builds on the Conditional STORE extension to provide an IMAP client the ability to fully resynchronize a mailbox as part of the SELECT/EXAMINE command, without the need for additional server-side state or client round trips. Hence, this memo obsoletes RFC 5162.

Finally, this document also updates the line-length recommendation in Section 3.2.1.5 of RFC 2683.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationlSurevine LtddnamekD. Cridlandddatej’Zhfilenamegrfc7162eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgqresyncdfullx qresync - IMAP QRESYNC ExtensiondnamevIMAP QRESYNC Extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-128991hkeywordsÜdIMAPiCONDSTOREgQRESYNChVANISHEDgEXPUNGEwquick resynchronizationkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateS®ﬁgranking˙crfcd7162irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoIMAP Extensions: Quick Flag Changes Resynchronization (CONDSTORE) and Quick Mailbox Resynchronization (QRESYNC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This document specifies an experimental multicast handover optimization mechanism for Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) to accelerate the delivery of multicast traffic to mobile nodes after handovers.  The mechanism, called Subscription Information Acquisition through the LMA (SIAL), is based on speeding up the acquisition of mobile nodes' multicast context by the mobile access gateways.  To do that, extensions to the current PMIPv6 protocol are proposed.  These extensions are not only applicable to the base solution for multicast support in Proxy Mobile IPv6, but they can also be applied to other solutions developed to avoid the tunnel convergence problem.  Furthermore, these extensions are also independent of the role played by the mobile access gateway within the multicast network (acting as either multicast listener discovery proxy or multicast router).fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamemLM. Contreras¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamegI. Sotoddatej’Nhfilenamegrfc7161eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmultimobdfullxmultimob - Multicast MobilitydnamerMulticast Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128990hkeywordsÖfPMIPv6qProxy Mobile IPv6imulticasthhandoverdSIALkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateS9 ¢granking˘crfcd7161irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) Multicast Handover Optimization by the Subscription Information Acquisition through the LMA (SIAL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document clarifies the RTP specification regarding the use of different clock rates in an RTP session.  It also provides guidance on how legacy RTP implementations that use multiple clock rates can interoperate with RTP implementations that use the algorithm described in this document.  It updates RFC 3550.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrImpedance MismatchdnameqM. Petit-Huguenin¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. ZornddatejŒphfilenamegrfc7160eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128989hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSL#úgranking¯crfcd7160irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Support for Multiple Clock Rates in an RTP SessiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØJavaScript Object Notation (JSON) is a lightweight, text-based, language-independent data interchange format. It was derived from the ECMAScript Programming Language Standard. JSON defines a small set of formatting rules for the portable representation of structured data.

This document removes inconsistencies with other specifications of JSON, repairs specification errors, and offers experience-based interoperability guidance.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamegT. BrayddatejŒÇhfilenamegrfc7159eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjsondfullx!json - JavaScript Object NotationdnamexJavaScript Object Notationdtypebwgbididoc-54449hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8259epagesopublicationDateSbgranking˜crfcd7159irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=The JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Data Interchange FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØJavaScript Object Notation (JSON) is a lightweight, text-based, language-independent data interchange format. It was derived from the ECMAScript Programming Language Standard. JSON defines a small set of formatting rules for the portable representation of structured data.

This document removes inconsistencies with other specifications of JSON, repairs specification errors, and offers experience-based interoperability guidance.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamegT. BrayddatejŒnhfilenamegrfc7158eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdjsondfullx!json - JavaScript Object NotationdnamexJavaScript Object Notationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128988hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7159epagesopublicationDateSWmgrankingˆcrfcd7158irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=The JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Data Interchange FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóNetwork Address and Port Translation (NAPT) works well for conserving global addresses and addressing multihoming requirements because an IPv4 NAPT router implements three functions: source address selection, next-hop resolution, and (optionally) DNS resolution.  For IPv6 hosts, one approach could be the use of IPv6-to-IPv6 Network Prefix Translation (NPTv6).  However, NAT and NPTv6 should be avoided, if at all possible, to permit transparent end-to-end connectivity.  In this document, we analyze the use cases of multihoming.  We also describe functional requirements and possible solutions for multihoming without the use of NAT in IPv6 for hosts and small IPv6 networks that would otherwise be unable to meet minimum IPv6-allocation criteria.  We conclude that DHCPv6-based solutions are suitable to solve the multihoming issues described in this document, but NPTv6 may be required as an intermediate solution.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Miles¢kaffiliationpSoftbank TelecomdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationhNTT WestdnamejT. Okimoto¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wingddatej’	hfilenamegrfc7157eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128987hkeywordsÅeNPTv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS:
grankingıcrfcd7157irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4IPv6 Multihoming without Network Address TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäIn Proxy Mobile IPv6, packets received from a Mobile Node (MN) by the Mobile Access Gateway (MAG) to which it is attached are typically tunneled to a Local Mobility Anchor (LMA) for routing.  The term "localized routing" refers to a method by which packets are routed directly between an MN's MAG and the MAG of its Correspondent Node (CN) without involving any LMA.  In a Proxy Mobile IPv6 deployment, it may be desirable to control the establishment of localized routing sessions between two MAGs in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain by requiring that the session be authorized.  This document specifies how to accomplish this using the Diameter protocol.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamekJ. KorhonenddatejŒhfilenamegrfc7156eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128986hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSLyEgrankingÙcrfcd7156irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Diameter Support for Proxy Mobile IPv6 Localized RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document describes the Diameter protocol application used for Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting services in the Network Access Server (NAS) environment; it obsoletes RFC 4005.  When combined with the Diameter Base protocol, Transport Profile, and Extensible Authentication Protocol specifications, this application specification satisfies typical network access services requirements.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej’Jhfilenamegrfc7155eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128985hkeywordsÖcAAAnAuthenticationmAuthorizationjAccountingmRemote AccesskobsoletedByÄepagesFopublicationDateSLyDgrankingÛcrfcd7155irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Diameter Network Access Server ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractydThis document provides a set of guidelines for personal interaction in the Internet Engineering Task Force. The guidelines recognize the diversity of IETF participants, emphasize the value of mutual respect, and stress the broad applicability of our work.

This document is an updated version of the guidelines for conduct originally published in RFC 3184.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. MoonesamyddatejŒlhfilenamegrfc7154eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128984hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSWlgrankingÚcrfcd7154irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber6etotaletitlexIETF Guidelines for ConductdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document reorganizes the IANA registries for the type values and sub-type values of the BGP Extended Communities attribute and the BGP IPv6-Address-Specific Extended Communities attribute.  This is done in order to remove interdependencies among the registries, thus making it easier for IANA to determine which codepoints are available for assignment in which registries.  This document also clarifies the information that must be provided to IANA when requesting an allocation from one or more of these registries.  These changes are compatible with the existing allocations and thus do not affect protocol implementations.  The changes will, however, impact the "IANA Considerations" sections of future protocol specifications.  This document updates RFC 4360 and RFC 5701.fadNamejAlia Atlasdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejY. Rekhterddatej’Mhfilenamegrfc7153eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128983hkeywordsÅwBorder Gateway ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS#@ëgrankingÒcrfcd7153irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,IANA Registries for BGP Extended CommunitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9184∂habstracty This document provides functional requirements for the support of Metro Ethernet Forum (MEF) Ethernet Tree (E-Tree) in multipoint Layer 2 Virtual Private Network solutions (referred to as simply "L2VPN").  It is intended that potential solutions will use these requirements as guidelines.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationgTelstradnamefR. Key¢kaffiliationgTelstradnameiS. DeLord¢kaffiliationiOrange CHdnameiF. Jounay¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehL. Huang¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamefZ. Liu¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegM. Paulddatej’Phfilenamegrfc7152eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128982hkeywordsäcRMPqRooted-MultipointdVPLSxVirtual Private LAN ServiceeE-VPNx Ethernet Virtual Private NetworkdMPLSxMulti-Protocol Label SwitchingbCEpCarrier EthernetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS9 ügrankingcrfcd7152irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuRequirements for Metro Ethernet Forum (MEF) Ethernet-Tree (E-Tree) Support in Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThe File Transfer Protocol, as defined in RFC 959, does not provide a way for FTP clients and servers to differentiate between multiple DNS names that are registered for a single IP address.  This document defines a new FTP command that provides a mechanism for FTP clients and servers to identify individual virtual hosts on an FTP server.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpHethmon BrothersdnamejP. Hethmon¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamekR. McMurrayddatej’Ohfilenamegrfc7151eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128981hkeywordsÇcFTPdHOSTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS π¡grankingÔcrfcd7151irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5File Transfer Protocol HOST Command for Virtual HostsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) is used to convey path computation requests and responses both between Path Computation Clients (PCCs) and Path Computation Elements (PCEs) and between cooperating PCEs. In PCEP, the path computation requests carry details of the constraints and objective functions that the PCC wishes the PCE to apply in its computation.

This document defines a facility to carry vendor-specific information in PCEP using a dedicated object and a new Type-Length-Variable that can be carried in any existing PCEP object.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. FarrelddatejŒnhfilenamegrfc7150eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128980hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7470epagesopublicationDateS∑‡grankingÓcrfcd7150irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Conveying Vendor-Specific Constraints in the Path Computation Element Communication ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒSoftware-Defined Networking (SDN) has been one of the major buzz words of the networking industry for the past couple of years. And yet, no clear definition of what SDN actually covers has been broadly admitted so far. This document aims to clarify the SDN landscape by providing a perspective on requirements, issues, and other considerations about SDN, as seen from within a service provider environment.

It is not meant to endlessly discuss what SDN truly means but rather to suggest a functional taxonomy of the techniques that can be used under an SDN umbrella and to elaborate on the various pending issues the combined activation of such techniques inevitably raises. As such, a definition of SDN is only mentioned for the sake of clarification.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelC. Jacquenetddatej’Mhfilenamegrfc7149eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128979hkeywordsàrNetwork AutomationqPolicy ManagementxConnectivity ProvisioningxService Parameter ExposuresDynamic NegotiationxDynamic Service ProvisioningiAutonomicuProgrammable NetworkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS=KgrankingÌcrfcd7149irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUSoftware-Defined Networking: A Perspective from within a Service Provider EnvironmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis specification defines extensions to the Proxy Mobile IPv6 protocol for allowing a mobile router in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain to obtain IP prefixes for its attached mobile networks using DHCPv6 prefix delegation.  Network-based mobility management support is provided for those delegated IP prefixes just as it is provided for the mobile node's home address.  Even if the mobile router performs a handoff and changes its network point of attachment, mobility support is ensured for all the delegated IP prefixes and for all the IP nodes in the mobile network that use IP address configuration from those delegated IP prefixes.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegX. Zhou¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekC. Williams¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardosddatej’Lhfilenamegrfc7148eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128978hkeywordsÑqPrefix DelegationqProxy Mobile IPv6fPMIPv6mMobile RouterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS π¿grankingÏcrfcd7148irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Prefix Delegation Support for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols. In particular, it defines objects for managing a client using the Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) protocol (SCSI over TCP).

This document obsoletes RFC 4544.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdDelldnamehM. Bakke¢kaffiliationpHCL TechnologiesdnamemP. Venkatesenddatej’?hfilenamegrfc7147eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128977hkeywordsÅiISCSI-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDateS?GgrankingÎcrfcd7147irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWDefinitions of Managed Objects for the Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 3723 specifies IPsec requirements for block storage protocols over IP (e.g., Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI)) based on IPsec v2 (RFC 2401 and related RFCs); those requirements have subsequently been applied to remote direct data placement protocols, e.g., the Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol (RDMAP).  This document updates RFC 3723's IPsec requirements to IPsec v3 (RFC 4301 and related RFCs) and makes some changes to required algorithms based on developments in cryptography since RFC 3723 was published.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationdDelldnameiP. Koningddatej’Hhfilenamegrfc7146eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128976hkeywordsÅeIPseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS?GgrankingÍcrfcd7146irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSSecuring Block Storage Protocols over IP: RFC 3723 Requirements Update for IPsec v3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) Extensions for Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) provides the RDMA data transfer capability to iSCSI by layering iSCSI on top of an RDMA-Capable Protocol. An RDMA-Capable Protocol provides RDMA Read and Write services, which enable data to be transferred directly into SCSI I/O Buffers without intermediate data copies. This document describes the extensions to the iSCSI protocol to support RDMA services as provided by an RDMA-Capable Protocol.

This document obsoletes RFC 5046.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameeM. Ko¢kaffiliationhMellanoxdnamelA. NezhinskyddatejŒBhfilenamegrfc7145eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128975hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages[opublicationDateS?G
grankingÈcrfcd7145irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) Extensions for the Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) is a SCSI transport protocol that maps the SCSI family of protocols onto TCP/IP.  The iSCSI protocol as specified in RFC 7143 (and as previously specified by the combination of RFC 3720 and RFC 5048) is based on the SAM-2 (SCSI Architecture Model - 2) version of the SCSI family of protocols.  This document defines enhancements to the iSCSI protocol to support certain additional features of the SCSI protocol that were defined in SAM-3, SAM-4, and SAM-5.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiF. Knight¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamenM. ChadalapakaddatejŒ_hfilenamegrfc7144eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128974hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS?G	grankingËcrfcd7144irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) SCSI Features UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document describes a transport protocol for SCSI that works on top of TCP. The iSCSI protocol aims to be fully compliant with the standardized SCSI Architecture Model (SAM-2). RFC 3720 defined the original iSCSI protocol. RFC 3721 discusses iSCSI naming examples and discovery techniques. Subsequently, RFC 3980 added an additional naming format to the iSCSI protocol. RFC 4850 followed up by adding a new public extension key to iSCSI. RFC 5048 offered a number of clarifications as well as a few improvements and corrections to the original iSCSI protocol.

This document obsoletes RFCs 3720, 3980, 4850, and 5048 by consolidating them into a single document and making additional updates to the consolidated specification. This document also updates RFC 3721. The text in this document thus supersedes the text in all the noted RFCs wherever there is a difference in semantics.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamenM. Chadalapaka¢kaffiliationnInfinidat Ltd.dnameiJ. Satran¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamegK. Meth¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. Blackddatej’:hfilenamegrfc7143eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128973hkeywordsäeiSCSIdSCSIgstoragecSANmblock storagexSCSI object storage devicescOSDcSAMddiskcT10kobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateS?GgrankingÁcrfcd7143irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) Protocol (Consolidated)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÉThis memo reclassifies RFC 1142, "OSI IS-IS Intra-domain Routing Protocol", to Historic status.  This memo also obsoletes RFC 1142.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. GinsbergddatejŒnhfilenamegrfc7142eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128972hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS%$grankingÊcrfcd7142irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Reclassification of RFC 1142 to HistoricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyJThis document provides recommendations of best current practice for dropping or marking packets using any active queue management (AQM) algorithm, including Random Early Detection (RED), BLUE, Pre- Congestion Notification (PCN), and newer schemes such as CoDel (Controlled Delay) and PIE (Proportional Integral controller Enhanced).  We give three strong recommendations: (1) packet size should be taken into account when transports detect and respond to congestion indications, (2) packet size should not be taken into account when network equipment creates congestion signals (marking, dropping), and therefore (3) in the specific case of RED, the byte- mode packet drop variant that drops fewer small packets should not be used.  This memo updates RFC 2309 to deprecate deliberate preferential treatment of small packets in AQM algorithms.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnameiJ. Mannerddatej’Ehfilenamegrfc7141eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128971hkeywordsåwactive queue managementcaqmlavailabilityqdenial of servicecdosrquality of servicecqosrcongestion controlhfairnessjincentivesuarchitecture layeringhprotocolkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateSC™grankingÂcrfcd7141irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber)etotaletitlex'Byte and Packet Congestion NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document introduces a hub and spoke multipoint (HSMP) Label Switched Path (LSP), which allows traffic from root to leaf through point-to-multipoint (P2MP) LSPs and also leaf to root along the reverse path.  That means traffic entering the HSMP LSP from the application/customer at the root node travels downstream to each leaf node, exactly as if it were traveling downstream along a P2MP LSP to each leaf node.  Upstream traffic entering the HSMP LSP at any leaf node travels upstream along the tree to the root, as if it were unicast to the root.  Direct communication among the leaf nodes is not allowed.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamefL. Jin¢kaffiliationiOrange CHdnameiF. Jounay¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamejN. Leymannddatej’Khfilenamegrfc7140eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128970hkeywordsÇhP2MP LSPiMP2MP LSPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSWigranking‰crfcd7140irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?LDP Extensions for Hub and Spoke Multipoint Label Switched PathdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7358∂habstractysITU-T Recommendation G.709 [G709-2012] introduced new Optical channel Data Unit (ODU) containers (ODU0, ODU4, ODU2e, and ODUflex) and enhanced Optical Transport Network (OTN) flexibility.

This document updates the ODU-related portions of RFC 4328 to provide extensions to GMPLS signaling to control the full set of OTN features, including ODU0, ODU4, ODU2e, and ODUflex.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationdCATRdnamehG. Zhang¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamejS. Belotti¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamekK. PithewanddatejŒQhfilenamegrfc7139eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128969hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS#@çgranking„crfcd7139irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSGMPLS Signaling Extensions for Control of Evolving G.709 Optical Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7892∂habstractyThis document describes Open Shortest Path First - Traffic Engineering (OSPF-TE) routing protocol extensions to support GMPLS control of Optical Transport Networks (OTNs) specified in ITU-T Recommendation G.709 as published in 2012.  It extends mechanisms defined in RFC 4203.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamejS. Belotti¢kaffiliationtInfinera CorporationdnamefR. Rao¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drakeddatej’Ghfilenamegrfc7138eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128968hkeywordsÑdOSPFeGMPLSdG709cOTNkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateS#@ägranking‚crfcd7138irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeTraffic Engineering Extensions to OSPF for GMPLS Control of Evolving G.709 Optical Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This document describes the use of the OSPF-MANET interface in single-hop broadcast networks. It includes a mechanism to dynamically determine the presence of such a network and specific operational considerations due to its nature.

This document updates RFC 5820.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. RatliffddatejŒkhfilenamegrfc7137eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128967hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSõgranking·crfcd7137irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Use of the OSPF-MANET Interface in Single-Hop Broadcast NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨The IPv6 addressing architecture includes a unicast interface identifier that is used in the creation of many IPv6 addresses.  Interface identifiers are formed by a variety of methods.  This document clarifies that the bits in an interface identifier have no meaning and that the entire identifier should be treated as an opaque value.  In particular, RFC 4291 defines a method by which the Universal and Group bits of an IEEE link-layer address are mapped into an IPv6 unicast interface identifier.  This document clarifies that those two bits are significant only in the process of deriving interface identifiers from an IEEE link-layer address, and it updates RFC 4291 accordingly.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., LtddnamehS. JiangddatejŒghfilenamegrfc7136eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128966hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateR˘¶êgranking‡crfcd7136irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Significance of IPv6 Interface IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document creates the new Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Resource Priority header field namespace 'esnet' and registers this namespace with IANA.  The new header field namespace allows for local emergency session establishment to a public safety answering point (PSAP), between PSAPs, and between a PSAP and first responders and their organizations.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. PolkddatejŒ'hfilenamegrfc7135eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128965hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateSu'˚grankingﬂcrfcd7135irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Registering a SIP Resource Priority Header Field Namespace for Local Emergency CommunicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 4412 defines the "Resource-Priority Namespaces" and "Resource-Priority Priority-values" registries.  The management policy of these registries is "Standards Action".  This document normatively updates RFC 4412 to change the management policy of these registries to "IETF Review".fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehB. Rosenddatej’Khfilenamegrfc7134eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128964hkeywordsÇxResource-Priority Namespacesx!Resource-Priority Priority-valueskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS∑ﬂgrankingﬁcrfcd7134irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]The Management Policy of the Resource Priority Header (RPH) Registry Changed to "IETF Review"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx…This document describes Information Elements related to the data link layer.  They are used by the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol for encoding measured data link layer traffic information.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejS. Kashima¢kaffiliationhNTT EastdnamelA. Kobayashi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitkenddatej’Lhfilenamegrfc7133eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128963hkeywordsÖeIPFIXePSAMPoProvider BridgexProvider Backbone BridgeeipfixkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateS}Sâgranking›crfcd7133irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Information Elements for Data Link Layer Traffic MeasurementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document describes a threat model for the context in which External Border Gateway Protocol (EBGP) path security mechanisms will be developed. The threat model includes an analysis of the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) and focuses on the ability of an Autonomous System (AS) to verify the authenticity of the AS path info received in a BGP update. We use the term "PATHSEC" to refer to any BGP path security technology that makes use of the RPKI. PATHSEC will secure BGP, consistent with the inter-AS security focus of the RPKI.

The document characterizes classes of potential adversaries that are considered to be threats and examines classes of attacks that might be launched against PATHSEC. It does not revisit attacks against unprotected BGP, as that topic has already been addressed in the BGP-4 standard. It concludes with a brief discussion of residual vulnerabilities.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationfUNC-CHdnamefA. Chiddatej’Khfilenamegrfc7132eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128962hkeywordsÉfBGPSECdRPKIdSIDRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS]granking‹crfcd7132irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Threat Model for BGP Path SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document describes use cases and documents call flows that require the History-Info header field to capture the Request-URIs as a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Request is retargeted.  The use cases are described along with the corresponding call flow diagrams and messaging details.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationeSkypednamehF. Audet¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Schubert¢kaffiliationxDetecon International GmbhdnamemH. van Elburg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej’Khfilenamegrfc7131eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128961hkeywordsÖcSIPlHistory-InfojRFC4244bisgExampleiCall FlowkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateSÈgranking€crfcd7131irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) History-Info Header Call Flow ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a mechanism to run Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) on Link Aggregation Group (LAG) interfaces. It does so by running an independent Asynchronous mode BFD session on every LAG member link.

This mechanism allows the verification of member link continuity, either in combination with, or in absence of, Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP). It provides a shorter detection time than what LACP offers. The continuity check can also cover elements of Layer 3 (L3) bidirectional forwarding.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoM. Binderberger¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegJ. HaasddatejŒihfilenamegrfc7130eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-128960hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR¸O;granking⁄crfcd7130irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) on Link Aggregation Group (LAG) InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyNAuthenticated denial of existence allows a resolver to validate that a certain domain name does not exist. It is also used to signal that a domain name exists but does not have the specific resource record (RR) type you were asking for. When returning a negative DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) response, a name server usually includes up to two NSEC records. With NSEC version 3 (NSEC3), this amount is three.

This document provides additional background commentary and some context for the NSEC and NSEC3 mechanisms used by DNSSEC to provide authenticated denial-of-existence responses.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Gieben¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejW. Mekkingddatej’Dhfilenamegrfc7129eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128959hkeywordsÖhInternetfDNSSECsDenial of ExistencedNSECeNSEC3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR¸O;grankingŸcrfcd7129irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,Authenticated Denial of Existence in the DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚This document is an implementation report for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Router protocol as defined in RFC 6810.  The authors did not verify the accuracy of the information provided by respondents.  The respondents are experts with the implementations they reported on, and their responses are considered authoritative for the implementations for which their responses represent.  The respondents were asked to only use the "YES" answer if the feature had at least been tested in the lab.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejH. Gredler¢kaffiliationiFU BerlindnamelM. Waehlischddatej’Khfilenamegrfc7128eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128958hkeywordsÇgroutinghsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS\grankingÿcrfcd7128irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFResource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Router Implementation ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty"RFC 2026 describes the review performed by the Internet Engineering Steering Group (IESG) on IETF Proposed Standard RFCs and characterizes the maturity level of those documents.  This document updates RFC 2026 by providing a current and more accurate characterization of Proposed Standards.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationjIECA, Inc.dnameiS. Turnerddatej”lhfilenamegrfc7127eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128957hkeywordsÖhGuidanceiStandardsqStandards ProcesskAdvancementqProposed StandardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÈC®granking◊crfcd7127irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber	etotaletitlex&Characterization of Proposed StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxÓThis document provides advice on the filtering of IPv4 packets based on the IPv4 options they contain.  Additionally, it discusses the operational and interoperability implications of dropping packets based on the IP options they contain.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvUTN-FRH / SI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekR. Atkinson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. PignataroddatejŒZhfilenamegrfc7126eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-128956hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateR˘¶ègranking÷crfcd7126irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Prac