 First Mile Copper (EFMCu) Interfaces MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxœThis document discusses the security implications of native IPv6 support and IPv6 transition/coexistence technologies on "IPv4-only" networks and describes possible mitigations for the aforementioned issues.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtSI6 Networks/UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefW. LiuddatejŒbhfilenamegrfc7123eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-128953hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR˘¶égranking”crfcd7123irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Security Implications of IPv6 on IPv4 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¸This document specifies the preferred method for transporting Delay- and Disruption-Tolerant Networking (DTN) protocol data over the Internet using datagrams.  It covers convergence layers for the Bundle Protocol (RFC 5050), as well as the transportation of segments using the Licklider Transmission Protocol (LTP) (RFC 5326).  UDP and the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) are the candidate datagram protocols discussed.  UDP can only be used on a local network or in cases where the DTN node implements explicit congestion control.  DCCP addresses the congestion control problem, and its use is recommended whenever possible.  This document is a product of the Delay-Tolerant Networking Research Group (DTNRG) and represents the consensus of the DTNRG.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoOhio UniversitydnamehH. Kruse¢kaffiliationqPurdue UniversitydnamegS. Jero¢kaffiliationoOhio UniversitydnamelS. OstermannddatejŒahfilenamegrfc7122eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128952hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateS πægranking“crfcd7122irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexçDatagram Convergence Layers for the Delay- and Disruption-Tolerant Networking (DTN) Bundle Protocol and Licklider Transmission Protocol (LTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document discusses Control Element (CE) High Availability (HA) within a Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Network Element (NE).  Additionally, this document updates RFC 5810 by providing new normative text for the Cold Standby High Availability mechanism.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamehK. Ogawa¢kaffiliationxZhejiang Gongshang UniversitydnamegW. Wang¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salimddatej’Hhfilenamegrfc7121eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128951hkeywordsÇfForCESbHAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR˙úÔgranking—crfcd7121irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]High Availability within a Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Network ElementdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7391∑habstractyThis memo describes the process for early allocation of code points by IANA from registries for which "Specification Required", "RFC Required", "IETF Review", or "Standards Action" policies apply.  This process can be used to alleviate the problem where code point allocation is needed to facilitate desired or required implementation and deployment experience prior to publication of an RFC, which would normally trigger code point allocation.  The procedures in this document are intended to apply only to IETF Stream documents.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Cottonddatej’Ehfilenamegrfc7120eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128950hkeywordsÉpearly allocationfpolicyhprotocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateR„ıgranking–crfcd7120irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberdetotaletitlex4Early IANA Allocation of Standards Track Code PointsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÁThis document specifies the operation of the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol specific to IPFIX Mediators, including Template and Observation Point management, timing considerations, and other Mediator-specific concerns.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamelA. Kobayashi¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. TrammellddatejŒchfilenamegrfc7119eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128949hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateR¸O:grankingœcrfcd7119irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOOperation of the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Protocol on IPFIX MediatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-The WebSocket protocol enables two-way real-time communication between clients and servers in web-based applications.  This document specifies a WebSocket subprotocol as a reliable transport mechanism between Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) entities to enable use of SIP in web-oriented deployments.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiVersaticadnameoI. Baz Castillo¢kaffiliationiVersaticadnamerJ. Millan Villegas¢kaffiliationfQuobisdnamejV. Pascualddatej’Ghfilenamegrfc7118eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128948hkeywordsÇcSIPiWebSocketkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÈC®grankingŒcrfcd7118irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOThe WebSocket Protocol as a Transport for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËRFCs 4761 and 4762 describe a solution for Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) multicast that relies on the use of point-to-point or multipoint-to-point unicast Label Switched Paths (LSPs) for carrying multicast traffic. This solution has certain limitations for certain VPLS multicast traffic profiles. For example, it may result in highly non-optimal bandwidth utilization when a large amount of multicast traffic is to be transported.

This document describes solutions for overcoming a subset of the limitations of the existing VPLS multicast solution. It describes procedures for VPLS multicast that utilize multicast trees in the service provider (SP) network. The solution described in this document allows sharing of one such multicast tree among multiple VPLS instances. Furthermore, the solution described in this document allows a single multicast tree in the SP network to carry traffic belonging only to a specified set of one or more IP multicast streams from one or more VPLS instances.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnameiY. Kamite¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegL. Fang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliation`dnamemC. KodeboniyaddatejŒchfilenamegrfc7117eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128947hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateR˙úÓgrankingÕcrfcd7117irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Multicast in Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyyThe DTNRG Research Group has defined the experimental Licklider Transmission Protocol (LTP) and the Compressed Bundle Header Encoding (CBHE) mechanism for the InterPlanetary Network ('ipn' URI scheme).  Moreover, RFC 5050 defines values for the Bundle Protocol administrative record type.  All of these fields are subject to a registry.  For the purpose of its research work, the group has created ad hoc registries.  As the specifications are stable and have multiple interoperable implementations, the group would like to hand off the registries to IANA for official management.  This document describes the necessary IANA actions.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamehK. Scott¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. BlanchetddatejŒfhfilenamegrfc7116eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128946hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateR¸O7grankingÃcrfcd7116irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlextLicklider Transmission Protocol (LTP), Compressed Bundle Header Encoding (CBHE), and Bundle Protocol IANA RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9758∑habstractyODeployment of BGP origin validation that is based on the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) has many operational considerations.  This document attempts to collect and present those that are most critical.  It is expected to evolve as RPKI-based origin validation continues to be deployed and the dynamics are better understood.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. BushddatejŒdhfilenamegrfc7115eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128945hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR÷ÜgrankingÀcrfcd7115irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberπetotaletitlexROrigin Validation Operation Based on the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymSecure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) defined the Content-Type parameter "smime-type".  As the list of defined values for that parameter has increased, it has become clear that a registry is needed to document these values.  This document creates the registry, registers the current values, and specifies the policies for registration of new values.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. LeibaddatejŒahfilenamegrfc7114eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128944hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÈw«granking crfcd7114irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Creation of a Registry for smime-type Parameter ValuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThe IPv6 Router Advertisement Guard (RA-Guard) mechanism is commonly employed to mitigate attack vectors based on forged ICMPv6 Router Advertisement messages.  Many existing IPv6 deployments rely on RA-Guard as the first line of defense against the aforementioned attack vectors.  However, some implementations of RA-Guard have been found to be prone to circumvention by employing IPv6 Extension Headers.  This document describes the evasion techniques that affect the aforementioned implementations and formally updates RFC 6105, such that the aforementioned RA-Guard evasion vectors are eliminated.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. GontddatejŒhfilenamegrfc7113eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128943hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR˙úÎgranking…crfcd7113irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDImplementation Advice for IPv6 Router Advertisement Guard (RA-Guard)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThe IPv6 specification allows IPv6 Header Chains of an arbitrary size.  The specification also allows options that can, in turn, extend each of the headers.  In those scenarios in which the IPv6 Header Chain or options are unusually long and packets are fragmented, or scenarios in which the fragment size is very small, the First Fragment of a packet may fail to include the entire IPv6 Header Chain.  This document discusses the interoperability and security problems of such traffic, and updates RFC 2460 such that the First Fragment of a packet is required to contain the entire IPv6 Header Chain.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationnIonos NetworksdnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. BonicaddatejŒfhfilenamegrfc7112eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128942hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÈC•granking»crfcd7112irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Implications of Oversized IPv6 Header ChainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¸This memo defines URI fragment identifiers for text/csv MIME entities.  These fragment identifiers make it possible to refer to parts of a text/csv MIME entity identified by row, column, or cell.  Fragment identification can use single items or ranges.fadNamekBarry LeibagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqMapR TechnologiesdnamemM. Hausenblas¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamehE. Wilde¢kaffiliationsOpen Data InstitutednamekJ. Tennisonddatej’Ihfilenamegrfc7111eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128941hkeywordsÅdmimekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÿ´ggranking«crfcd7111irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex4URI Fragment Identifiers for the text/csv Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document defines extensions to the data-plane failure-detection protocol for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) known as "LSP ping".  These extensions allow a selection of the LSP to be used for the echo reply return path.  Enforcing a specific return path can be used to verify bidirectional connectivity and also increase LSP ping robustness.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamefW. Cao¢kaffiliationsTata CommunicationsdnamegS. Ning¢kaffiliationiOrange CHdnameiF. Jounay¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Delordddatej”ahfilenamegrfc7110eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128940hkeywordsÇlTunnel StackhReply TCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR„Ùgranking∆crfcd7110irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Return Path Specified Label Switched Path (LSP) PingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7737µhabstractyÏThere are scenarios in which the home agent needs to trigger flow binding operations towards the mobile node, such as moving a flow from one access network to another based on network resource availability.  In order for the home agent to be able to initiate interactions for flow bindings with the mobile node, this document defines new signaling messages and sub-options for Mobile IPv6.  Flow bindings initiated by a home agent are supported for mobile nodes enabled by both IPv4 and IPv6.fadNamenBrian HabermangauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhKDDI LabdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationoJEJU TechnoparkdnamefD. Kim¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefF. Xiaddatej’Dhfilenamegrfc7109eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128939hkeywordsÇeMIPv6mFlow mobilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÒ<ôgranking≈crfcd7109irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex6Flow Bindings Initiated by Home Agents for Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyWAnycast is a deployment technique commonly employed for authoritative-only servers in the Domain Name System (DNS). L-Root, one of the thirteen root servers, is deployed in this fashion.

Various techniques have been used to map deployed anycast infrastructure externally, i.e., without reference to inside knowledge about where and how such infrastructure has been deployed. Motivations for performing such measurement exercises include operational troubleshooting and infrastructure risk assessment. In the specific case of L-Root, the ability to measure and map anycast infrastructure using the techniques mentioned in this document is provided for reasons of operational transparency.

This document describes all facilities deployed at L-Root to facilitate mapping of its infrastructure and serves as documentation for L-Root as a measurable service.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamelT. MandersonddatejŒ`hfilenamegrfc7108eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128938hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR÷Ögrankingƒcrfcd7108irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexZA Summary of Various Mechanisms Deployed at L-Root for the Identification of Anycast NodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygWhen the S/MIME Mail Security Working Group was chartered, an object identifier arc was donated by RSA Data Security for use by that working group.  This document describes the object identifiers that were assigned in that donated arc, transfers control of that arc to IANA, and establishes IANA allocation policies for any future assignments within that arc.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. HousleyddatejŒehfilenamegrfc7107eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128937hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR„Ògranking√crfcd7107irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEObject Identifier Registry for the S/MIME Mail Security Working GroupdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxìThis document defines and registers a value of "grouptextchat" ("Group Text Chat") for the URI <purpose> element of SIP's Conference Event Package.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeJitsidnamegE. Ivovddatej’Ihfilenamegrfc7106eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128936hkeywordsÖcSIPxConference Event Packagelservice-urisoconference-uriskURI purposekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRﬂ`%granking¬crfcd7106irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgA Group Text Chat Purpose for Conference and Service URIs in the SIP Event Package for Conference StatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis document describes a protocol for a Device to provide location-related measurement data to a Location Information Server (LIS) within a request for location information.  Location-related measurement information provides observations concerning properties related to the position of a Device; this information could be data about network attachment or about the physical environment.  A LIS is able to use the location-related measurement data to improve the accuracy of the location estimate it provides to the Device.  A basic set of location-related measurements are defined, including common modes of network attachment as well as assisted Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) parameters.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameoJ. Winterbottomddatej’Fhfilenamegrfc7105eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128935hkeywordsÑdHELDhLocationlMeasurementslDevice-basedkobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDateR◊hﬁgranking¡crfcd7105irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWUsing Device-Provided Location-Related Measurements in Location Configuration ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPacket loss is undesirable for real-time multimedia sessions, but it can occur due to congestion or other unplanned network outages.  This is especially true for IP multicast networks, where packet loss patterns can vary greatly between receivers.  One technique that can be used to recover from packet loss without incurring unbounded delay for all the receivers is to duplicate the packets and send them in separate redundant streams.  This document defines the semantics for grouping redundant streams in the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  The semantics defined in this document are to be used with the SDP Grouping Framework.  Grouping semantics at the Synchronization Source (SSRC) level are also defined in this document for RTP streams using SSRC multiplexing.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameeH. Ouddatej’Ghfilenamegrfc7104eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128934hkeywordsÑcSDPdssrcvsynchronization sourcergrouping frameworkkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateRﬂ`!granking¿crfcd7104irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDuplication Grouping Semantics in the Session Description ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydAlthough Internet message formats have been precisely defined since the 1970s, authoring and handling software often shows only mild conformance to the specifications.  The malformed messages that result are non-standard.  Nonetheless, decades of experience have shown that using some tolerance in the handling of the malformations that result is often an acceptable approach and is better than rejecting the messages outright as nonconformant.  This document includes a collection of the best advice available regarding a variety of common malformed mail situations; it is to be used as implementation guidance.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawy¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. Shapiro¢kaffiliation`dnamehN. Freedddatej’Ghfilenamegrfc7103eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128933hkeywordsÇcMTAdSMTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR÷Ñgrankingøcrfcd7103irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Advice for Safe Handling of Malformed MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis document provides a glossary of terminology used in routing requirements and solutions for networks referred to as Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLNs).  An LLN is typically composed of many embedded devices with limited power, memory, and processing resources interconnected by a variety of links.  There is a wide scope of application areas for LLNs, including industrial monitoring, building automation (e.g., heating, ventilation, air conditioning, lighting, access control, fire), connected home, health care, environmental monitoring, urban sensor networks, energy management, assets tracking, and refrigeration.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. VasseurddatejŒWhfilenamegrfc7102eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128932hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR„Ìgrankingæcrfcd7102irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Terms Used in Routing for Low-Power and Lossy NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÓAt one time, the RFC Editor published snapshots of the "Internet Official Protocol Standards".  These documents were known as xx00 documents, the last of which was published in May 2008.  These snapshots have been replaced by a web page, so the RFC Editor will no longer be publishing these snapshots as RFCs.  As a result, the RFC Editor will classify unpublished RFC xx00 numbers through 7000 as never issued.  Starting with the RFC number 7100, xx00 numbers will be available for assignment.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcAMSdnameiS. GinozaddatejŒRhfilenamegrfc7101eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128931hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR†Î%grankingΩcrfcd7101irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexDList of Internet Official Protocol Standards: Replaced by a Web PagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx’This document updates RFC 2026 to no longer use STD 1 as a summary of "Internet Official Protocol Standards".  It obsoletes RFC 5000 and requests the IESG to move RFC 5000 (and therefore STD 1) to Historic status.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies, Inc.dnamejP. ResnickddatejŒ\hfilenamegrfc7100eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128930hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR†Î"grankingºcrfcd7100irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber	etotaletitlexIRetirement of the "Internet Official Protocol Standards" Summary DocumentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx£This document describes how the currently specified IPv6 flow label can be used to enhance layer 3/4 (L3/4) load distribution and balancing for large server farms.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationxHAProxy Technologies, Inc.dnamejW. Tarreauddatej’Chfilenamegrfc7098eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128929hkeywordsÅdECMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÿ´fgranking∫crfcd7098irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Using the IPv6 Flow Label for Load Balancing in Server FarmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«The RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) is used in conjunction with the Real- time Transport Protocol (RTP) in order to provide a variety of short- term and long-term reception statistics.  The available reporting may include aggregate information across longer periods of time as well as individual packet reporting.  This document specifies a per-packet report metric capturing individual packets discarded from the de- jitter buffer after successful reception.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnameiI. Curcioddatej’Chfilenamegrfc7097eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128928hkeywordsÉcRTPdRTCPodiscard metricskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRŒ∑grankingπcrfcd7097irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) for RLE of Discarded PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2ITU-T recommendation G.709-2012 has introduced new fixed and flexible Optical channel Data Unit (ODU) containers in Optical Transport Networks (OTNs).

This document provides an evaluation of existing Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) routing and signaling protocols against the G.709 OTNs.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamejS. Belotti¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiP. Grandi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeD. Liddatej’Bhfilenamegrfc7096eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128927hkeywordsÜgRoutingsCCAMP Working GroupdOSPFeGMPLSdG709cOTNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR◊hÿgranking∏crfcd7096irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWEvaluation of Existing GMPLS Encoding against G.709v3 Optical Transport Networks (OTNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis specification defines "jCard", a JSON format for vCard data.  The vCard data format is a text format for representing and exchanging information about individuals and other entities, for example, telephone numbers, email addresses, structured names, and delivery addresses.  JSON is a lightweight, text-based, language- independent data interchange format commonly used in Internet applications.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamejP. Kewischddatej’Bhfilenamegrfc7095eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhjcardcaldfullx4jcardcal - JSON data formats for vCard and iCalendardnamex)JSON data formats for vCard and iCalendardtypebwgbidjdoc-128926hkeywordsáejCarddJSONevCardkaddressbookhcontactsgCardDAVcPIMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR÷Ågranking∑crfcd7095irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex jCard: The JSON Format for vCarddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxåThis memo discusses architectural implications of IP anycast and provides some historical analysis of anycast use by various IETF protocols.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Oran¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelE. Osterweilddatej’Bhfilenamegrfc7094eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128925hkeywordsÇganycastlarchitecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÿ´cgranking∂crfcd7094irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex*Architectural Considerations of IP AnycastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxæThis document specifies additional example methods for generating Key Identifier values for use in the AKI (Authority Key Identifier) and SKI (Subject Key Identifier) certificate extensions.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationgTelstradnameiJ. MangerddatejŒ_hfilenamegrfc7093eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128924hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR¥µvgrankingµcrfcd7093irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8Additional Methods for Generating Key Identifiers ValuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÛIn many SIP deployments, SIP entities exist in the SIP signaling path between the originating and final terminating endpoints, which go beyond the definition of a SIP proxy, performing functions not defined in Standards Track RFCs. The only term for such devices provided in RFC 3261 is for a Back-to-Back User Agent (B2BUA), which is defined as the logical concatenation of a SIP User Agent Server (UAS) and User Agent Client (UAC).

There are numerous types of SIP B2BUAs performing different roles in different ways; for example, IP Private Branch Exchanges (IPBXs), Session Border Controllers (SBCs), and Application Servers (ASs). This document identifies several common B2BUA roles in order to provide taxonomy other documents can use and reference.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiH. Kaplan¢kaffiliationfQuobisdnamejV. Pascualddatej’Fhfilenamegrfc7092eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestrawdfullx7straw - Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdnamex/Sip Traversal Required for Applications to Workdtypebwgbidjdoc-128923hkeywordsÉcSIPeB2BUAhtaxonomykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateR¥µugranking¥crfcd7092irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHA Taxonomy of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Back-to-Back User AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty¿This document provides information about the Russian Federal standard for digital signatures (GOST R 34.10-2012), which is one of the Russian cryptographic standard algorithms (called GOST algorithms).  Recently, Russian cryptography is being used in Internet applications, and this document provides information for developers and users of GOST R 34.10-2012 regarding digital signature generation and verification.  This document updates RFC 5832.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnCryptocom Ltd.dnamekV. Dolmatov¢kaffiliationoCryptocom, Ltd.dnamelA. DegtyarevddatejŒShfilenamegrfc7091eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128922hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRØíAgranking≥crfcd7091irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.GOST R 34.10-2012: Digital Signature AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAfter an emergency call is completed (terminated either prematurely by the emergency caller or normally by the call taker), the call taker may feel the need for further communication. For example, the call may have been dropped by accident without the call taker having sufficient information about the current state of an accident victim. A call taker may trigger a callback to the emergency caller using the contact information provided with the initial emergency call. This callback could, under certain circumstances, be treated like any other call and, as a consequence, it may get blocked by authorization policies or may get forwarded to an answering machine.

The IETF emergency services architecture specification already offers a solution approach for allowing Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) callbacks to bypass authorization policies in order to reach the caller without unnecessary delays. Unfortunately, the specified mechanism only supports limited scenarios. This document discusses shortcomings of the current mechanisms and illustrates additional scenarios where better-than-normal call treatment behavior would be desirable. We describe a solution based on a new header field value for the SIP Priority header field, called "psap-callback", to mark PSAP callbacks.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwColumbia UniversityNonednamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationx"Huawei Technologies (UK) Co., Ltd.dnamehM. Patelddatej’Ehfilenamegrfc7090eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128921hkeywordsÖdPSAPhcallbackcSIPiemergencydVoIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateSP]Ùgranking≤crfcd7090irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) CallbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty*The HTTP-based Memento framework bridges the present and past Web.  It facilitates obtaining representations of prior states of a given resource by introducing datetime negotiation and TimeMaps.  Datetime negotiation is a variation on content negotiation that leverages the given resource's URI and a user agent's preferred datetime.  TimeMaps are lists that enumerate URIs of resources that encapsulate prior states of the given resource.  The framework also facilitates recognizing a resource that encapsulates a frozen prior state of another resource.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxLos Alamos National LaboratorydnamepH. Van de Sompel¢kaffiliationwOld Dominion UniversitydnameiM. Nelson¢kaffiliationxLos Alamos National LaboratorydnamelR. Sandersonddatej’Chfilenamegrfc7089eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128920hkeywordsÜdHTTPscontent negotiationtdatetime negotiationqresource versionsrarchival resourcesgMementokobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateR¥µugranking±crfcd7089irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexBHTTP Framework for Time-Based Access to Resource States -- MementodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"Music on hold" is one of the features of telephone systems that is most desired by buyers of business telephone systems.  Music on hold means that when one party to a call has the call "on hold", that party's telephone provides an audio stream (often music) to be heard by the other party.  Architectural features of SIP make it difficult to implement music on hold in a way that is fully standards-compliant.  The implementation of music on hold described in this document is fully effective, is standards-compliant, and has a number of advantages over the methods previously documented.  In particular, it is less likely to produce peculiar user interface effects and more likely to work in systems that perform authentication than the music-on-hold method described in Section 2.3 of RFC 5359.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgAriadnednameiD. Worleyddatej’Fhfilenamegrfc7088eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128919hkeywordsÅmMusic on holdkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateR˘¶çgranking∞crfcd7088irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Session Initiation Protocol Service Example -- Music on HolddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThe MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is based on a profile of the MPLS and Pseudowire (PW) procedures as specified in the MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE), PW, and Multi-Segment Pseudowire (MS-PW) architectures developed by the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF). The International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) has specified a Transport Network architecture.

This document provides a thesaurus for the interpretation of MPLS-TP terminology within the context of the ITU-T Transport Network Recommendations.

It is important to note that MPLS-TP is applicable in a wider set of contexts than just Transport Networks. The definitions presented in this document do not provide exclusive or complete interpretations of MPLS-TP concepts. This document simply allows the MPLS-TP terms to be applied within the Transport Network context.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationxNokia Solutions and NetworksdnamekN. SprecherddatejŒ]hfilenamegrfc7087eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128918hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRØí@grankingØcrfcd7087irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex≥A Thesaurus for the Interpretation of Terminology Used in MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Internet-Drafts and RFCs in the Context of the ITU-T's Transport Network RecommendationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÛThis document is the HIP-Based Overlay Networking Environment (HIP BONE) instance specification for the REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) protocol.  The document provides the details needed to build a RELOAD-based overlay that uses HIP.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Maenpaaddatej’Dhfilenamegrfc7086eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128917hkeywordsÉcHIPgoverlaycP2PkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateRÿ»>grankingÆcrfcd7086irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäHost Identity Protocol-Based Overlay Networking Environment (HIP BONE) Instance Specification for REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÇRecent statements from the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) and the Internet Corporation of Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN) Security and Stability Advisory Committee have focused on the problems that the DNS is likely to experience with top-level domains (TLDs) that contain address records (so-called "dotless domains").  In order to help researchers determine the extent of the issues with dotless domains, this document lists the current dotless TLDs and gives a script for finding them.  This document lists data about dotless TLDs but does not address the policy and technology issues other than to point to the statements of others.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levine¢kaffiliationxCybersecurity AssociationdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej’Fhfilenamegrfc7085eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128916hkeywordsÅcDNSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRüﬂ{granking≠crfcd7085irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex*Top-Level Domains That Are Already DotlessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document specifies requirements for an IPv6 Customer Edge (CE) router.  Specifically, the current version of this document focuses on the basic provisioning of an IPv6 CE router and the provisioning of IPv6 hosts attached to it.  The document also covers IP transition technologies.  Two transition technologies in RFC 5969's IPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures (6rd) and RFC 6333's Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite) are covered in the document.  The document obsoletes RFC 6204.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehH. Singh¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiW. Beebee¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiC. Donley¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Starkddatej’hfilenamegrfc7084eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128915hkeywordsÇc6rdgDS-LitekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRè•ˇgranking¨crfcd7084irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Basic Requirements for IPv6 Customer Edge RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9096d9818∂habstractxÁThis document updates RFC 3315 by redefining the default values for SOL_MAX_RT and INF_MAX_RT and defining options through which a DHCPv6 server can override the client's default value for SOL_MAX_RT and INF_MAX_RT with new values.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. DromsddatejŒQhfilenamegrfc7083eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128914hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8415epagesopublicationDateRè•˛granking´crfcd7083irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Modification to Default Values of SOL_MAX_RT and INF_MAX_RTdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThe Centralized Conferencing Manipulation Protocol (CCMP) document (RFC 6503) defines a way for a client to discover a conference control server that supports CCMP. However, it does not define a way for a client involved in a conference to determine if the conference focus supports CCMP. This information would allow a CCMP-enabled client that joins a conference using SIP to also register for the Centralized Conferencing (XCON) conference event package and take advantage of CCMP operations on the conference.

This document describes two mechanisms, depending upon the need of the User Agent (UA), to address the above limitation. The first mechanism uses the Call-Info header field, and the second mechanism defines a new value for the "purpose" header field parameter in the <service-uris> element in the SIP conferencing event package.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamenR. Shekh-Yusef¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiM. BarnesddatejŒYhfilenamegrfc7082eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128913hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateRüﬂxgranking™crfcd7082irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdIndication of Conference Focus Support for the Centralized Conferencing Manipulation Protocol (CCMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìThis document suggests some strategies for the combined use of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) both in user-oriented clients and in deployed servers.  Such strategies, which mainly consist of configuration changes and minimal software modifications to existing clients and servers, aim to provide a single, full-featured, real-time communication service by using complementary subsets of features from SIP and from XMPP.  Typically, such subsets consist of telephony capabilities from SIP and instant messaging and presence capabilities from XMPP.  This document does not define any new protocols or syntax for either SIP or XMPP and, by intent, does not attempt to standardize "best current practices".  Instead, it merely aims to provide practical guidance to those who are interested in the combined use of SIP and XMPP for real-time communication.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationijitsi.orgdnamegE. Ivov¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejE. Maroccoddatej’>hfilenamegrfc7081eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128912hkeywordsàwreal-time communicationuunified communicationevoiceevideoqinstant messagingdchathpresenceitelephonykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRñP†granking©crfcd7081irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvCUSAX: Combined Use of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe scalability of Hierarchical Virtual Private LAN Service (H-VPLS) with Ethernet access networks (RFC 4762) can be improved by incorporating Provider Backbone Bridge functionality in the VPLS access.  Provider Backbone Bridging has been standardized as IEEE 802.1ah-2008.  It aims to improve the scalability of Media Access Control (MAC) addresses and service instances in Provider Ethernet networks.  This document describes different interoperability scenarios where Provider Backbone Bridge functionality is used in H-VPLS with Ethernet or MPLS access network to attain better scalability in terms of number of customer MAC addresses and number of service instances.  The document also describes the scenarios and the mechanisms for incorporating Provider Backbone Bridge functionality within H-VPLS with existing Ethernet access and interoperability among them.  Furthermore, the document discusses the migration mechanisms and scenarios by which Provider Backbone Bridge functionality can be incorporated into H-VPLS with existing MPLS access.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehS. Salam¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationnNuage NetworksdnamehF. Balusddatej’Chfilenamegrfc7080eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128911hkeywordsÅfh-vplskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR¥µtgranking®crfcd7080irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRVirtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Interoperability with Provider Backbone BridgesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂The IETF Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) working group has defined many encapsulations of various layer 1 and layer 2 service- specific PDUs and circuit data.  In most of these encapsulations, use of the Pseudowire (PW) Control Word is required.  However, there are several encapsulations for which the Control Word is optional, and this optionality has been seen in practice to possibly introduce interoperability concerns between multiple implementations of those encapsulations.  This survey of the Pseudowire / Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV) user community was conducted to determine implementation trends and the possibility of always mandating the Control Word.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamelN. Del Regno¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehA. Malisddatej’Chfilenamegrfc7079eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128910hkeywordsÇqControl Word (CW)tControl Channel (CC)kobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateRñPügrankingßcrfcd7079irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfThe Pseudowire (PW) and Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV) Implementation Survey ResultsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrRFC 6724 defines default address selection mechanisms for IPv6 that allow nodes to select an appropriate address when faced with multiple source and/or destination addresses to choose between.  RFC 6724 allows for the future definition of methods to administratively configure the address selection policy information.  This document defines a new DHCPv6 option for such configuration, allowing a site administrator to distribute address selection policy overriding the default address selection parameters and policy table, and thus allowing the administrator to control the address selection behavior of nodes in their site.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjNTT NT LabdnamelA. Matsumoto¢kaffiliationjNTT NT LabdnamekT. Fujisaki¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. ChownddatejŒQhfilenamegrfc7078eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128909hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÕ«Ögranking¶crfcd7078irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Distributing Address Selection Policy Using DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document specifies protocol enhancements for allowing the local mobility anchor in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain to asynchronously notify the mobile access gateway about changes related to a mobility session.  These Update Notification messages are exchanged using a new Mobility Header message type specifically designed for this purpose.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejM. Liebsch¢kaffiliationdKDDIdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamekJ. Korhonenddatej’Bhfilenamegrfc7077eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128908hkeywordsÅeMIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRñPügranking•crfcd7077irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Update Notifications for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty±The Secure Shell (SSH) Public Key Subsystem protocol defines a key distribution protocol that is limited to provisioning an SSH server with a user's public keys. This document describes a new protocol that builds on the protocol defined in RFC 4819 to allow the provisioning of keys and certificates to a server using the SSH transport.

The new protocol allows the calling client to organize keys and certificates in different namespaces on a server. These namespaces can be used by the server to allow a client to configure any application running on the server (e.g., SSH, Key Management Interoperability Protocol (KMIP), Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)).

The new protocol provides a server-independent mechanism for clients to add public keys, remove public keys, add certificates, remove certificates, and list the current set of keys and certificates known by the server by namespace (e.g., list all public keys in the SSH namespace).

Rights to manage keys and certificates in a particular namespace are specific and limited to the authorized user and are defined as part of the server's implementation. The described protocol is backward compatible to version 2 defined by RFC 4819.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgP6R IncdnameiM. Joseph¢kaffiliationgP6R IncdnamehJ. Susoyddatej’Ahfilenamegrfc7076eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128907hkeywordsÉnkey managementvcertificate managementhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRè•˛granking§crfcd7076irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex'P6R's Secure Shell Public Key SubsystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡The Diameter protocol includes a capability for message redirection, controlled by an application-independent "redirect agent". In some circumstances, an operator may wish to redirect messages to an alternate domain without specifying individual hosts. This document specifies an application-specific mechanism by which a Diameter server or proxy (node) can perform such a redirection when the Straightforward-Naming Authority Pointer (S-NAPTR) is not used for dynamic peer discovery. A node performing this new function is referred to as a "Realm-based Redirect Server".

This memo updates Sections 6.13 and 6.14 of RFC 6733 with respect to the usage of the Redirect-Host-Usage and Redirect-Max-Cache-Time Attribute-Value Pairs (AVPs).fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednamefR. Hao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. Taylorddatej’Ahfilenamegrfc7075eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128906hkeywordsÇhDiametergroutingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateRè•˝granking£crfcd7075irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Realm-Based Redirection In DiameterdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysGMPLS provides control for multiple switching technologies and for hierarchical switching within a technology.  GMPLS routing and signaling use common values to indicate the type of switching technology.  These values are carried in routing protocols via the Switching Capability field, and in signaling protocols via the Switching Type field.  While the values used in these fields are the primary indicators of the technology and hierarchy level being controlled, the values are not consistently defined and used across the different technologies supported by GMPLS.  This document is intended to resolve the inconsistent definition and use of the Switching Capability and Type fields by narrowly scoping the meaning and use of the fields.  This document updates all documents that use the GMPLS Switching Capability and Types fields, in particular RFCs 3471, 4202, 4203, and 5307.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationfOrangednameiJ. MeuricddatejŒQhfilenamegrfc7074eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128905hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateRåIgranking¢crfcd7074irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDRevised Definition of the GMPLS Switching Capability and Type FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxûThis document defines a response set for describing assertions a reputation service provider can make about email identifiers, for use in generating reputons.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMimecastdnamemN. Borenstein¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’Ahfilenamegrfc7073eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfreputedfullxrepute - Reputation ServicesdnamesReputation Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128904hkeywordsájreputationfdomainhsecurityimessagingddkimcspfnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRèäìgranking°crfcd7073irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/A Reputation Response Set for Email IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxøThis document defines a mechanism to conduct queries for reputation information over the HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) using JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) as the payload meta-format.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMimecastdnamemN. Borenstein¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’Ahfilenamegrfc7072eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfreputedfullxrepute - Reputation ServicesdnamesReputation Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128903hkeywordsájreputationfdomainhsecurityimessagingddkimcspfnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateRèäígranking†crfcd7072irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA Reputation Query ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxƒThis document defines the format of reputation response data ("reputons"), the media type for packaging it, and definition of a registry for the names of reputation applications and response sets.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMimecastdnamemN. Borenstein¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’Ahfilenamegrfc7071eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfreputedfullxrepute - Reputation ServicesdnamesReputation Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128902hkeywordsájreputationfdomainhsecurityimessagingddkimcspfnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRèäígrankingücrfcd7071irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'A Media Type for Reputation InterchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes a general architecture for a reputation-based service, allowing one to request reputation-related data over the Internet, where "reputation" refers to predictions or expectations about an entity or an identifier such as a domain name.  The document roughly follows the recommendations of RFC 4101 for describing a protocol model.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMimecastdnamemN. Borenstein¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’@hfilenamegrfc7070eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfreputedfullxrepute - Reputation ServicesdnamesReputation Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128901hkeywordsáfdomainhsecurityimessagingddkimcspfnauthenticationjreputationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRèäëgrankingûcrfcd7070irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(An Architecture for Reputation ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractydContent distribution applications, such as those employing peer-to-peer (P2P) technologies, are widely used on the Internet and make up a large portion of the traffic in many networks.  Often, however, content distribution applications use network resources inefficiently.  One way to improve efficiency is to introduce storage capabilities within the network and enable cooperation between end-host and in-network content distribution mechanisms.  This is the capability provided by a DECoupled Application Data Enroute (DECADE) system, which is introduced in this document.  DECADE enables applications to take advantage of in-network storage when distributing data objects as opposed to using solely end-to-end resources.  This document presents the underlying principles and key functionalities of such a system and illustrates operation through a set of examples.gauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehR. Alimi¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnameiA. Rahman¢kaffiliationcNECdnamekD. Kutscher¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegH. Song¢kaffiliationdEICTdnamenK. Pentikousisddatej’Ahfilenamegrfc7069eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128900hkeywordsÅfdecadekobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateRñPûgrankingùcrfcd7069irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex+DECoupled Application Data Enroute (DECADE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2When a Diameter server or agent becomes overloaded, it needs to be able to gracefully reduce its load, typically by advising clients to reduce traffic for some period of time.  Otherwise, it must continue to expend resources parsing and responding to Diameter messages, possibly resulting in a progressively severe overload condition.  The existing Diameter mechanisms are not sufficient for managing overload conditions.  This document describes the limitations of the existing mechanisms.  Requirements for new overload management mechanisms are also provided.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamejE. McMurry¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekB. CampbellddatejŒQhfilenamegrfc7068eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128899hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRåGgrankingúcrfcd7068irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Diameter Overload Control RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"Edge TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) switches currently learn the mapping between MAC (Media Access Control) addresses and their egress TRILL switch by observing the data packets they ingress or egress or by the TRILL ESADI (End-Station Address Distribution Information) protocol. When an ingress TRILL switch receives a data frame for a destination address (MAC&Label) that the switch does not know, the data frame is flooded within the frame's Data Label across the TRILL campus.

This document describes the framework for using directory services to assist edge TRILL switches in reducing multi-destination frames, particularly unknown unicast frames flooding, and ARP/ND (Address Resolution Protocol / Neighbor Discovery), thus improving TRILL network scalability and security.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationeYahoodnamelI. Gashinskyddatej’@hfilenamegrfc7067eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128898hkeywordsáeTRILLmOrchestrationiDirectorydPushdPullgRBridgecARPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRìÿÛgrankingõcrfcd7067irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Directory Assistance Problem and High-Level Design ProposaldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=As the deployment of third and fourth generation cellular networks progresses, a large number of cellular hosts are being connected to the Internet.  Standardization organizations have made the Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) mandatory in their specifications.  However, the concept of IPv6 covers many aspects and numerous specifications.  In addition, the characteristics of cellular links in terms of bandwidth, cost, and delay put special requirements on how IPv6 is used.  This document considers IPv6 for cellular hosts that attach to the General Packet Radio Service (GPRS), Universal Mobile Telecommunications System (UMTS), or Evolved Packet System (EPS) networks (hereafter collectively referred to as Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) networks).  This document also lists specific IPv6 functionalities that need to be implemented in addition to what is already prescribed in the IPv6 Node Requirements document (RFC 6434).  It also discusses some issues related to the use of these components when operating in these networks.  This document obsoletes RFC 3316.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. KrishnanddatejŒPhfilenamegrfc7066eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128897hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRyú¶grankingöcrfcd7066irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCIPv6 for Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Cellular HostsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document specifies the syntax of Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) schemes for the Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) protocol.  It defines two URI schemes to provision the TURN Resolution Mechanism (RFC 5928).fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrImpedance MismatchdnameqM. Petit-Huguenin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Nandakumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. JonesddatejŒUhfilenamegrfc7065eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128896hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateRêmgrankingôcrfcd7065irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexETraversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) Uniform Resource IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxùThis document specifies the syntax and semantics of the Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme for the Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) protocol.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Nandakumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationrImpedance MismatchdnameqM. Petit-HugueninddatejŒUhfilenamegrfc7064eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128895hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateRè•¸grankingòcrfcd7064irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFURI Scheme for the Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∏This document provides supporting documentation to advance the IETF stream's Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) protocol from Proposed Standard to Internet Standard.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehL. Zheng¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Zhang¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. ParekhddatejŒThfilenamegrfc7063eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-128894hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR¢ ¶grankingócrfcd7063irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfSurvey Report on Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) Implementations and DeploymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!This document provides a framework to allow the development of protocol extensions to support Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) and Path Computation Element (PCE) control of Optical Transport Networks (OTNs) as specified in ITU-T Recommendation G.709 as published in 2012.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationdCMCCdnameeH. Li¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamejS. Belotti¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. CeccarelliddatejŒShfilenamegrfc7062eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128893hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRåDgrankingñcrfcd7062irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGFramework for GMPLS and PCE Control of G.709 Optical Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxoThis specification registers an XML-based media type for the eXtensible Access Control Markup Language (XACML).gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamejR. Sinnema¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehE. WildeddatejŒShfilenamegrfc7061eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128892hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRåAgrankingïcrfcd7061irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex@eXtensible Access Control Markup Language (XACML) XML Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) can be used to set up Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) and Multipoint-to-Multipoint (MP2MP) Label Switched Paths.  However, the specification for the Multipoint Extensions to LDP presupposes that the two endpoints of an LDP session are directly connected.  The LDP base specification allows for the case where the two endpoints of an LDP session are not directly connected; such a session is known as a "Targeted LDP" session.  This document provides the specification for using the LDP Multipoint Extensions over a Targeted LDP session.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamelM. Napierala¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. WijnandsddatejŒPhfilenamegrfc7060eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128891hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateRìÿÚgrankingîcrfcd7060irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Using LDP Multipoint Extensions on Targeted LDP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty™This document provides an overview of various ways to tunnel IPv6 packets over IPv4 networks.  It covers mechanisms in current use, touches on several mechanisms that are now only of historic interest, and discusses some newer tunnel mechanisms that are not widely used at the time of publication.  The goal of the document is helping people with an IPv6-in-IPv4 tunneling need to select the mechanisms that may apply to them.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxS.J.M. Steffann ConsultancydnamekS. Steffann¢kaffiliationxInstitute IMDEA NetworksdnamenI. van Beijnum¢kaffiliationlOpenFortressdnamekR. van ReinddatejŒRhfilenamegrfc7059eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128890hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateRñPúgrankingìcrfcd7059irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0A Comparison of IPv6-over-IPv4 Tunnel MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document provides a list of typical Media Control Channel Framework call flows.  It aims at being a simple guide to the use of the interface between Application Servers and MEDIACTRL-based Media Servers, as well as a base reference document for both implementors and protocol researchers.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnamekA. Amirante¢kaffiliationhMeetechodnamekT. Castaldi¢kaffiliationhMeetechodnamejL. Miniero¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnamekS P. Romanoddatej’@hfilenamegrfc7058eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128889hkeywordsÉiMediaCtrltMedia Server ControlxMedia Control Channel FrameworkkobsoletedByÄepages∂opublicationDateRÅçûgrankingícrfcd7058irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Media Control Channel Framework (CFW) Call Flow ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÊThis document updates the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) applicability statement from RFC 3748 to reflect recent usage of the EAP protocol in the Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond web (ABFAB) architecture.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgRESTENAdnameiS. Winter¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejJ. Saloweyddatej’@hfilenamegrfc7057eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeabfabdfullx<abfab - Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdnamex4Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdtypebwgbidjdoc-128888hkeywordsÇcEAPcAAAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR√™grankingëcrfcd7057irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexèUpdate to the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Applicability Statement for Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond Web (ABFAB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThe naming extensions to the Generic Security Service Application Programming Interface (GSS-API) provide a mechanism for applications to discover authorization and personalization information associated with GSS-API names.  The Extensible Authentication Protocol GSS-API mechanism allows an Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) peer to provide authorization attributes alongside an authentication response.  It also supplies mechanisms to process Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) messages provided in the AAA response.  This document describes how to use the Naming Extensions API to access that information.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationiJANET(UK)dnamejJ. HowlettddatejŒhfilenamegrfc7056eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeabfabdfullx<abfab - Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdnamex4Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdtypebwgbidjdoc-128887hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR√©grankingêcrfcd7056irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRName Attributes for the GSS-API Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document defines protocols, procedures, and conventions to be employed by peers implementing the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) when using the Extensible Authentication Protocol mechanism.  Through the GS2 family of mechanisms defined in RFC 5801, these protocols also define how Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) applications use the Extensible Authentication Protocol.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationiJANET(UK)dnamejJ. HowlettddatejŒhfilenamegrfc7055eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeabfabdfullx<abfab - Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdnamex4Application Bridging for Federated Access Beyond webdtypebwgbidjdoc-128886hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateR√ßgrankingècrfcd7055irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A GSS-API Mechanism for the Extensible Authentication ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄The framework for Operations, Administration and Maintenance (OAM) within the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) describes how the Maintenance Entity Group Intermediate Points (MIPs) may be situated within network nodes at incoming and outgoing interfaces.

This document elaborates on important considerations for internal MIP addressing. More precisely, it describes important restrictions for any mechanism that specifies a way of forming OAM messages so that they can be targeted at MIPs on either incoming or outgoing interfaces and forwarded correctly through the forwarding engine. Furthermore, the document includes considerations for node implementations where there is no distinction between the incoming and outgoing MIP.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationmHitachi, Ltd.dnamegH. Endo¢kaffiliationcNECdnameiR. Winter¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehY. Koike¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegM. PaulddatejŒLhfilenamegrfc7054eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128885hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÅçûgrankingécrfcd7054irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwAddressing Requirements and Design Considerations for Per-Interface Maintenance Entity Group Intermediate Points (MIPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This document updates RFC 4960 by defining a method for the sender of a DATA chunk to indicate that the corresponding Selective Acknowledgment (SACK) chunk should be sent back immediately and should not be delayed.  It is done by specifying a bit in the DATA chunk header, called the (I)mmediate bit, which can get set by either the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) implementation or the application using an SCTP stack.  Since unknown flags in chunk headers are ignored by SCTP implementations, this extension does not introduce any interoperability problems.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnamelI. Ruengeler¢kaffiliationnAdara NetworksdnamejR. StewartddatejŒOhfilenamegrfc7053eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128884hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9260epagesopublicationDateRyú¶grankingçcrfcd7053irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGSACK-IMMEDIATELY Extension for the Stream Control Transmission ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This document defines the MIB module that contains managed objects to support the monitoring devices of the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP).  These objects provide information useful for monitoring LISP devices, including determining basic LISP configuration information, LISP functional status, and operational counters and other statistics.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Schudel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegA. Jain¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiV. MorenoddatejŒNhfilenamegrfc7052eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128883hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateRs;grankingåcrfcd7052irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHosts and applications may benefit from learning if an IPv6 address is synthesized and if NAT64 and DNS64 are present in a network.  This document analyzes all proposed solutions (known at the time of writing) for communicating whether the synthesis is taking place, what address format was used, and what IPv6 prefix was used by the NAT64 and DNS64.  These solutions enable both NAT64 avoidance and local IPv6 address synthesis.  The document concludes by recommending the standardization of the approach based on heuristic discovery.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainenddatej”yhfilenamegrfc7051eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128882hkeywordsÑeNAT64eDNS64g464XLATjPref64::/nkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRyú¶grankingãcrfcd7051irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Analysis of Solution Proposals for Hosts to Learn NAT64 PrefixdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document describes a method for detecting the presence of DNS64 and for learning the IPv6 prefix used for protocol translation on an access network.  The method depends on the existence of a well-known IPv4-only fully qualified domain name "ipv4only.arpa.".  The information learned enables nodes to perform local IPv6 address synthesis and to potentially avoid NAT64 on dual-stack and multi-interface deployments.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainen¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wingddatej’2hfilenamegrfc7050eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128881hkeywordsÑeNAT64eDNS64g464XLATjPref64::/nkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRyú•grankingäcrfcd7050irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Discovery of the IPv6 Prefix Used for IPv6 Address SynthesisdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8880∂habstractyHThe Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR) is a data format whose design goals include the possibility of extremely small code size, fairly small message size, and extensibility without the need for version negotiation.  These design goals make it different from earlier binary serializations such as ASN.1 and MessagePack.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuUniversity Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej’?hfilenamegrfc7049eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128880hkeywordsÖfparsergencodermbinary formatwdata interchange formatdJSONkobsoletedByÅd8949epages6opublicationDateRhGLgrankingâcrfcd7049irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Concise Binary Object Representation (CBOR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüIPv6 Neighbor Discovery includes Neighbor Unreachability Detection.  That function is very useful when a host has an alternative neighbor -- for instance, when there are multiple default routers -- since it allows the host to switch to the alternative neighbor in a short time.  By default, this time is 3 seconds after the node starts probing.  However, if there are no alternative neighbors, this timeout behavior is far too impatient.  This document specifies relaxed rules for Neighbor Discovery retransmissions that allow an implementation to choose different timeout behavior based on whether or not there are alternative neighbors.  This document updates RFC 4861.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoArista NetworksdnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnamelI. Gashinskyddatej’Ehfilenamegrfc7048eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128879hkeywordsÉd6MANdIPv6rNeighbor DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÕ«Çgrankingàcrfcd7048irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Neighbor Unreachability Detection Is Too ImpatientdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty7Open vSwitch is an open-source software switch designed to be used as a vswitch (virtual switch) in virtualized server environments.  A vswitch forwards traffic between different virtual machines (VMs) on the same physical host and also forwards traffic between VMs and the physical network.  Open vSwitch is open to programmatic extension and control using OpenFlow and the OVSDB (Open vSwitch Database) management protocol.  This document defines the OVSDB management protocol.  The Open vSwitch project includes open-source OVSDB client and server implementations.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlVMware, Inc.dnamehB. Pfaff¢kaffiliationlVMware, Inc.dnamehB. Davieddatej’Dhfilenamegrfc7047eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128878hkeywordsÑgvswitchnvirtualizationgoverlaycOVSkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateRØí?grankingácrfcd7047irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-The Open vSwitch Database Management ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty6Group communication services exist in a large variety of flavors and technical implementations at different protocol layers.  Multicast data distribution is most efficiently performed on the lowest available layer, but a heterogeneous deployment status of multicast technologies throughout the Internet requires an adaptive service binding at runtime.  Today, it is difficult to write an application that runs everywhere and at the same time makes use of the most efficient multicast service available in the network.  Facing robustness requirements, developers are frequently forced to use a stable upper-layer protocol provided by the application itself.  This document describes a common multicast API that is suitable for transparent communication in underlay and overlay and that grants access to the different flavors of multicast.  It proposes an abstract naming scheme that uses multicast URIs, and it discusses mapping mechanisms between different namespaces and distribution technologies.  Additionally, this document describes the application of this API for building gateways that interconnect current Multicast Domains throughout the Internet.  It reports on an implementation of the programming Interface, including service middleware.  This document is a product of the Scalable Adaptive Multicast (SAM) Research Group.fadNamenBrian HabermangauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtlink-lab & FU BerlindnamelM. Waehlisch¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaasddatej’Dhfilenamegrfc7046eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesamrgdfullx2samrg - Scalable Adaptive Multicast Research Groupdnamex*Scalable Adaptive Multicast Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128877hkeywordsÑrPeer-to-Peer (P2P)radaptive multicastqmulticast  namingtmulticast addressingkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateRØí=grankingÜcrfcd7046irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex-A Common API for Transparent Hybrid MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoVarious IPv6 extension headers have been standardised since the IPv6 standard was first published.  This document updates RFC 2460 to clarify how intermediate nodes should deal with such extension headers and with any that are defined in the future.  It also specifies how extension headers should be registered by IANA, with a corresponding minor update to RFC 2780.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamehS. JiangddatejŒ\hfilenamegrfc7045eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128876hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateR¢ £grankingÖcrfcd7045irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Transmission and Processing of IPv6 Extension HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThis document defines a standard mechanism for capturing the history information associated with a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) request.  This capability enables many enhanced services by providing the information as to how and why a SIP request arrives at a specific application or user.  This document defines an optional SIP header field, History-Info, for capturing the history information in requests.  The document also defines SIP header field parameters for the History-Info and Contact header fields to tag the method by which the target of a request is determined.  In addition, this specification defines a value for the Privacy header field that directs the anonymization of values in the History-Info header field.  This document obsoletes RFC 4244.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationeSkypednamehF. Audet¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Schubert¢kaffiliationxDetecon International GmbhdnamemJ. van Elburg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej’Bhfilenamegrfc7044eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128875hkeywordsÖlhistory-infohretargetqenhanced servicesivoicemailxautomatic call distributionkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateRÒ<ígrankingÑcrfcd7044irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUAn Extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for Request History InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØ48-bit Extended Unique Identifier (EUI-48) and 64-bit Extended Unique Identifier (EUI-64) are address formats specified by the IEEE for use in various layer-2 networks, e.g., Ethernet.

This document describes two new DNS resource record types, EUI48 and EUI64, for encoding Ethernet addresses in the DNS.

This document describes potentially severe privacy implications resulting from indiscriminate publication of link-layer addresses in the DNS. EUI-48 or EUI-64 addresses SHOULD NOT be published in the public DNS. This document specifies an interoperable encoding of these address types for use in private DNS namespaces, where the privacy concerns can be constrained and mitigated.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamehJ. Ableyddatej’?hfilenamegrfc7043eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128874hkeywordsÇdIEEEhethernetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRhGKgrankingÉcrfcd7043irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Resource Records for EUI-48 and EUI-64 Addresses in the DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaSome IETF protocols make use of Ethernet frame formats and IEEE 802 parameters.  This document discusses several uses of such parameters in IETF protocols, specifies IANA considerations for assignment of points under the IANA OUI (Organizationally Unique Identifier), and provides some values for use in documentation.  This document obsoletes RFC 5342.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamehJ. Ableyddatej’>hfilenamegrfc7042eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128873hkeywordsÜhEthernetiEthertypec802cOUIcEUIdLSAPkobsoletedByÅd9542epagesopublicationDateRhGEgrankingÇcrfcd7042irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUIANA Considerations and IETF Protocol and Documentation Usage for IEEE 802 ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe IEEE 802.1 Provider Backbone Bridges (PBBs) specification defines an architecture and bridge protocols for interconnection of multiple Provider Bridged Networks (PBNs). Provider backbone bridging was defined by IEEE as a connectionless technology based on multipoint VLAN tunnels. PBB can be used to attain better scalability than Provider Bridges (PBs) in terms of the number of customer Media Access Control addresses and the number of service instances that can be supported.

The Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) provides a framework for extending Ethernet LAN services, using MPLS tunneling capabilities, through a routed MPLS backbone without running the Rapid Spanning Tree Protocol (RSTP) or the Multiple Spanning Tree Protocol (MSTP) across the backbone. As a result, VPLS has been deployed on a large scale in service provider networks.

This document discusses extensions to the VPLS Provider Edge (PE) model required to incorporate desirable PBB components while maintaining the service provider fit of the initial model.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehF. Balus¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. BitarddatejŒLhfilenamegrfc7041eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128872hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRìÿÒgrankingÅcrfcd7041irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlExtensions to the Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Provider Edge (PE) Model for Provider Backbone BridgingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document describes a mechanism called Public 4over6, which is designed to provide IPv4 Internet connectivity over an IPv6 access network using global IPv4 addresses.  Public 4over6 was developed in the IETF and is in use in some existing deployments but is not recommended for new deployments.  Future deployments of similar scenarios should use Lightweight 4over6.  Public 4over6 follows the Hub and Spoke softwire model and uses an IPv4-in-IPv6 tunnel to forward IPv4 packets over an IPv6 access network.  The bidirectionality of the IPv4 communication is achieved by explicitly allocating global non-shared IPv4 addresses to end users and by maintaining IPv4-IPv6 address binding on the border relay.  Public 4over6 aims to provide uninterrupted IPv4 services to users, like Internet Content Providers (ICPs), etc., while an operator makes the access network transition to an IPv6-only access network.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Wu¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeP. Wu¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejO. Vautrin¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Leeddatej’?hfilenamegrfc7040eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-128871hkeywordsÜmPublic 4over6nIPv4 over IPv6nAccess NetworkpDHCPv4 over IPv6kIPv6 TunneloIPv6 TransitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRÅçûgrankingÄcrfcd7040irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Public IPv4-over-IPv6 Access NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûSource Address Validation Improvement (SAVI) methods were developed to prevent nodes attached to the same IP link from spoofing each other's IP addresses, so as to complement ingress filtering with finer-grained, standardized IP source address validation.  This document is a framework document that describes and motivates the design of the SAVI methods.  Particular SAVI methods are described in other documents.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnTsinghua Univ.dnameeJ. Wu¢kaffiliationnTsinghua Univ.dnameeJ. Bi¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliation`dnamegC. Vogtddatej’	hfilenamegrfc7039eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsavidfullx-savi - Source Address Validation Improvementsdnamex&Source Address Validation Improvementsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128870hkeywordsÉmanti-spoofingeBCP38qingress filteringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRs;grankingcrfcd7039irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Source Address Validation Improvement (SAVI) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRRFC 5614 (OSPF-MDR) extends OSPF to support mobile ad hoc networks (MANETs) by specifying its operation on the new OSPF interface of type MANET.  This document describes the use of OSPF-MDR (MANET Designated Router) in a single-hop broadcast network, which is a special case of a MANET in which each router is a (one-hop) neighbor of each other router.  Unlike an OSPF broadcast interface, such an interface can have a different cost associated with each neighbor.  The document includes configuration recommendations and simplified mechanisms that can be used in single-hop broadcast networks.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqSRI InternationaldnamehR. Ogierddatej’>hfilenamegrfc7038eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128869hkeywordsÖgroutingumobile ad hoc networkwMANET designated routerhwirelessxpoint-to-multipoint interfacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRa’œgranking~crfcd7038irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Use of OSPF-MDR in Single-Hop Broadcast NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@The DHCPv6 RADIUS option provides a mechanism to exchange authorization and identification information between the DHCPv6 relay agent and DHCPv6 server.  This architecture assumes that the Network Access Server (NAS) acts as both a DHCPv6 relay agent and RADIUS client.  When receiving messages from the DHCPv6 clients, the NAS consults the RADIUS server and adds the RADIUS response when forwarding the DHCPv6 client's messages to the DHCPv6 server.  The DHCPv6 server then uses that additional information to generate an appropriate response to the DHCPv6 client's requests.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwFreelancer TechnologiesdnamefL. Yeh¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadairddatej’=hfilenamegrfc7037eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128868hkeywordsÇfDHCPv6fRADIUSkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateR`_wgranking}crfcd7037irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(RADIUS Option for the DHCPv6 Relay AgentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5When the Long-Term Archive and Notary Services (LTANS) working group was chartered, an object identifier arc was set aside for use by that working group.  This document describes the object identifiers that were assigned, and it establishes IANA allocation policies for any future assignments within that arc.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. HousleyddatejŒJhfilenamegrfc7036eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128867hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRUÇ}granking|crfcd7036irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Object Identifier Registry for the Long-Term Archive and Notary Services (LTANS) Working GroupdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis document defines an extension to the Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO) (RFC 4119) for the expression of location information that is defined relative to a reference point. The reference point may be expressed as a geodetic or civic location, and the relative offset may be one of several shapes. An alternative binary representation is described.

Optionally, a reference to a secondary document (such as a map image) can be included, along with the relationship of the map coordinate system to the reference/offset coordinate system, to allow display of the map with the reference point and the relative offset.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehB. Rosen¢kaffiliationrAruba NetworksNonednamejD. Stanley¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehG. Bajko¢kaffiliationlLookingglassdnamejA. Thomsonddatej’=hfilenamegrfc7035eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128866hkeywordsÇhRelativehlocationkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateR`_pgranking{crfcd7035irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Relative Location RepresentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=To improve the protection of web applications against clickjacking, this document describes the X-Frame-Options HTTP header field, which declares a policy, communicated from the server to the client browser, regarding whether the browser may display the transmitted content in frames that are part of other web pages.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegD. Ross¢kaffiliationnThames StanleydnamejT. Gondromddatej’>hfilenamegrfc7034eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfwebsecdfulluwebsec - Web SecuritydnamelWeb Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128865hkeywordsÉmframe-optionskHTTP headerfwebseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRWigrankingzcrfcd7034irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!HTTP Header Field X-Frame-OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This specification defines the WebFinger protocol, which can be used to discover information about people or other entities on the Internet using standard HTTP methods.  WebFinger discovers information for a URI that might not be usable as a locator otherwise, such as account or email URIs.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehJ. Smarrddatej’=hfilenamegrfc7033eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128864hkeywordsáiWebFingercJRDxJSON Resource Descriptorqservice discoveryxservice discovery protocoluinformation discoveryxinformation discovery protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRE¸Mgrankingycrfcd7033irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleiWebFingerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›Seamless MPLS design enables a single IP/MPLS network to scale over core, metro, and access parts of a large packet network infrastructure using standardized IP/MPLS protocols. One of the key goals of Seamless MPLS is to meet requirements specific to access networks including high number of devices, device position in network topology, and compute and memory constraints that limit the amount of state access devices can hold. This can be achieved with LDP Downstream-on-Demand (DoD) label advertisement. This document describes LDP DoD use cases and lists required LDP DoD procedures in the context of Seamless MPLS design.

In addition, a new optional TLV type in the LDP Label Request message is defined for fast-up convergence.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamekT. Beckhaus¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameoK. Tiruveedhula¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamerM. Konstantynowicz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. MartiniddatejŒGhfilenamegrfc7032eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128863hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateRs;grankingxcrfcd7032irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)LDP Downstream-on-Demand in Seamless MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe DHCPv6 protocol, defined in RFC 3315, allows for multiple servers to operate on a single network; however, it does not define any way the servers could share information about currently active clients and their leases.  Some sites are interested in running multiple servers in such a way as to provide increased availability in case of server failure.  In order for this to work reliably, the cooperating primary and secondary servers must maintain a consistent database of the lease information.  RFC 3315 allows for, but does not define, any redundancy or failover mechanisms.  This document outlines requirements for DHCPv6 failover, enumerates related problems, and discusses the proposed scope of work to be conducted.  This document does not define a DHCPv6 failover protocol.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalski¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejK. Kinnearddatej’9hfilenamegrfc7031eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128862hkeywordsÇfDHCPv6hFailoverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRE¸Lgrankingwcrfcd7031irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDHCPv6 Failover RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document profiles certificate enrollment for clients using Certificate Management over CMS (CMC) messages over a secure transport.  This profile, called Enrollment over Secure Transport (EST), describes a simple, yet functional, certificate management protocol targeting Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) clients that need to acquire client certificates and associated Certification Authority (CA) certificates.  It also supports client-generated public/private key pairs as well as key pairs generated by the CA.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekM. Pritikin¢kaffiliationlAKAYLA, Inc.dnamefP. Yee¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Harkinsddatej’;hfilenamegrfc7030eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-128861hkeywordsÇcpkicestkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateRhGDgrankingvcrfcd7030irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Enrollment over Secure TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9908d8951d8996∂habstractyAs the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) evolves, EAP peers rely increasingly on information received from the EAP server.  EAP extensions such as channel binding or network posture information are often carried in tunnel methods; peers are likely to rely on this information.  Cryptographic binding is a facility described in RFC 3748 that protects tunnel methods against man-in-the-middle attacks.  However, cryptographic binding focuses on protecting the server rather than the peer.  This memo explores attacks possible when the peer is not protected from man-in-the-middle attacks and recommends cryptographic binding based on an Extended Master Session Key, a new form of cryptographic binding that protects both peer and server along with other mitigations.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamelM. Wasserman¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Zhangddatej’<hfilenamegrfc7029eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-128860hkeywordsÖdMITMqman-in-the-middlesEMSK crypto bindingxExtended Master Session KeyftunnelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRUÇ}grankingucrfcd7029irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Mutual Cryptographic BindingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document proposes some experimental enhancements to the base solution to support IP multicasting in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) domain.  These enhancements include the use of a multicast tree mobility anchor as the topological anchor point for multicast traffic, as well as a direct routing option where the Mobile Access Gateway can provide access to multicast content in the local network.  The goal of these enhancements is to provide benefits such as reducing multicast traffic replication and supporting different PMIPv6 deployment scenarios.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnamejJC. Zuniga¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamemLM. Contreras¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamemCJ. Bernardos¢kaffiliationxInstituto de TelecomunicacoesdnamegS. Jeon¢kaffiliationsSoongsil UniversitydnamefY. Kimddatej’<hfilenamegrfc7028eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmultimobdfullxmultimob - Multicast MobilitydnamerMulticast Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128859hkeywordsÜhmultimobfPMIPv6dMTMAhselectorcMLDdIGMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR<®~grankingtcrfcd7028irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Multicast Mobility Routing Optimizations for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx±This document specifies the use of several Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Brainpool curves for authentication and key exchange in the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxsecunet Security NetworksdnameiJ. Merkle¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamejM. Lochterddatej’=hfilenamegrfc7027eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128858hkeywordsÇcTLSxElliptic Curve CryptographykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateRLöägrankingscrfcd7027irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUElliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Brainpool Curves for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœThe MPLS Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) is a generalization of the applicability of the pseudowire (PW) Associated Channel Header (ACH). RFC 5586 defines the concept of TLV constructs that can be carried in messages on the G-ACh by placing them in the ACH between the fixed header fields and the G-ACh message. These TLVs are called ACH TLVs

No Associated Channel Type yet defined uses an ACH TLV. Furthermore, it is believed that handling TLVs in hardware introduces significant problems to the fast path, and since G-ACh messages are intended to be processed substantially in hardware, the use of ACH TLVs is undesirable.

This document updates RFC 5586 by retiring ACH TLVs and removing the associated registry.fadNameoSpencer Dawkinsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryantddatej’<hfilenamegrfc7026eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128857hkeywordsÖcACHeG-AChjPseudowirebPWgMPLS-TPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR&fzgrankingrcrfcd7026irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWRetiring TLVs from the Associated Channel Header of the MPLS Generic Associated ChanneldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≥The initial effort of the PCE (Path Computation Element) WG focused mainly on MPLS.  As a next step, this document describes functional requirements for GMPLS applications of PCE.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdKDDIdnamehT. Otani¢kaffiliationdKDDIdnamehK. Ogaki¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Zhang¢kaffiliationpCoriant R&D GmbHdnamekC. Margariaddatej’<hfilenamegrfc7025eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128856hkeywordsÖpPath ComputationdCSPFcPCCsTraffic EngineeringbTEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR9]grankingqcrfcd7025irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Requirements for GMPLS Applications of PCEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎWith BGP/MPLS Virtual Private Networks (VPNs), providing any-to-any connectivity among sites of a given VPN would require each Provider Edge (PE) router connected to one or more of these sites to hold all the routes of that VPN. The approach described in this document allows the VPN service provider to reduce the number of PE routers that have to maintain all these routes by requiring only a subset of these routers to maintain all these routes.

Furthermore, when PE routers use ingress replication to carry the multicast traffic of VPN customers, the approach described in this document may, under certain circumstances, reduce bandwidth inefficiency associated with ingress replication and redistribute the replication load among PE routers.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegH. Jeng¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehL. Jalil¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationfArktandnamekR. AggarwalddatejŒGhfilenamegrfc7024eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128855hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRUÇ|grankingpcrfcd7024irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Virtual Hub-and-Spoke in BGP/MPLS VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document specifies the mapping of defect states between Ethernet Attachment Circuits (ACs) and associated Ethernet pseudowires (PWs) connected in accordance with the Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architecture to realize an end-to-end emulated Ethernet service.  It standardizes the behavior of Provider Edges (PEs) with respect to Ethernet PW and AC defects.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehD. Mohan¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiS. DeLord¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamehP. Niger¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamefR. QiuddatejŒFhfilenamegrfc7023eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128854hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRUÇ|grankingocrfcd7023irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPMPLS and Ethernet Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) InterworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThe RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Canonical Name (CNAME) is a persistent transport-level identifier for an RTP endpoint. While the Synchronization Source (SSRC) identifier of an RTP endpoint may change if a collision is detected or when the RTP application is restarted, its RTCP CNAME is meant to stay unchanged, so that RTP endpoints can be uniquely identified and associated with their RTP media streams.

For proper functionality, RTCP CNAMEs should be unique within the participants of an RTP session. However, the existing guidelines for choosing the RTCP CNAME provided in the RTP standard (RFC 3550) are insufficient to achieve this uniqueness. RFC 6222 was published to update those guidelines to allow endpoints to choose unique RTCP CNAMEs. Unfortunately, later investigations showed that some parts of the new algorithms were unnecessarily complicated and/or ineffective. This document addresses these concerns and replaces RFC 6222.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationdRTFMdnamekE. RescorladdatejŒEhfilenamegrfc7022eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128853hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateR&fygrankingncrfcd7022irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLGuidelines for Choosing RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Canonical Names (CNAMEs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty∆NAT444 is an IPv4 extension technology being considered by Service Providers as a means to continue offering IPv4 service to customers while transitioning to IPv6.  This technology adds an extra Carrier- Grade NAT (CGN) in the Service Provider network, often resulting in two NATs.  CableLabs, Time Warner Cable, and Rogers Communications independently tested the impacts of NAT444 on many popular Internet services using a variety of test scenarios, network topologies, and vendor equipment.  This document identifies areas where adding a second layer of NAT disrupts the communication channel for common Internet applications.  This document was updated to include the Dual-Stack Lite (DS-Lite) impacts also.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiC. Donley¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiL. Howard¢kaffiliationuRogers CommunicationsdnamelV. Kuarsingh¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamegJ. Berg¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Doshiddatej’:hfilenamegrfc7021eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128852hkeywordsàcCGNfNAT444gDS-LiteoDual-Stack LitedIPv4cNATdIPv6nLSN transitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR;çFgrankingmcrfcd7021irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexAAssessing the Impact of Carrier-Grade NAT on Network ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides information about the current Internet Numbers Registry System used in the distribution of globally unique Internet Protocol (IP) address space and autonomous system (AS) numbers.

This document also provides information about the processes for further evolution of the Internet Numbers Registry System.

This document replaces RFC 2050.

This document does not propose any changes to the current Internet Numbers Registry System. Rather, it documents the Internet Numbers Registry System as it works today.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdARINdnameiJ. Curran¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationpVirtualized, LLCdnameiD. ConradddatejŒBhfilenamegrfc7020eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128851hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateR®grankinglcrfcd7020irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$The Internet Numbers Registry SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÆWe define a REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) Usage for Application-Layer Multicast (ALM) as well as a mapping to the RELOAD experimental message type to support ALM. The ALM Usage is intended to support a variety of ALM control algorithms in an overlay-independent way. Two example algorithms are defined, based on Scribe and P2PCast.

This document is a product of the Scalable Adaptive Multicast Research Group (SAM RG).fadNamenBrian HabermangauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsAvaya Labs ResearchdnameiJ. Buford¢kaffiliationvUniversity of StirlingdnamejM. Kolbergddatej’;hfilenamegrfc7019eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesamrgdfullx2samrg - Scalable Adaptive Multicast Research Groupdnamex*Scalable Adaptive Multicast Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128850hkeywordsÅxapplication-layer multicastkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateRE¸Lgrankingkcrfcd7019irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexRApplication-Layer Multicast Extensions to REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis document describes the problem of enabling a large number of systems to communicate directly using IPsec to protect the traffic between them. It then expands on the requirements for such a solution.

Manual configuration of all possible tunnels is too cumbersome in many such cases. In other cases, the IP addresses of endpoints change, or the endpoints may be behind NAT gateways, making static configuration impossible. The Auto-Discovery VPN solution will address these requirements.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationbHPdnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehS. Hannaddatej’:hfilenamegrfc7018eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128849hkeywordsÑeIPsecgOverlaycSDNcIKEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR'¡ãgrankingjcrfcd7018irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Auto-Discovery VPN Problem Statement and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡The IETF makes heavy use of email lists to conduct its work.  This often involves accessing the archived history of those email lists.  Participants would like to have the ability to browse and search those archives using standard IMAP clients.  This memo captures the requirements for providing a service that would allow such browsing and searching, and it is intended as input to a later activity for the design and development of such a service.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiR. SparksddatejŒBhfilenamegrfc7017eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128848hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRí˛grankingicrfcd7017irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IMAP Access to IETF Email List ArchivesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis memo describes Adobe's Secure Real-Time Media Flow Protocol (RTMFP), an endpoint-to-endpoint communication protocol designed to securely transport parallel flows of real-time video, audio, and data messages, as well as bulk data, over IP networks.  RTMFP has features that make it effective for peer-to-peer (P2P) as well as client-server communications, even when Network Address Translators (NATs) are used.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamemM. Thornburghddatej’9hfilenamegrfc7016eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128847hkeywordsÅeRTMFPkobsoletedByÄepagesqopublicationDateRñPôgrankinghcrfcd7016irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Adobe's Secure Real-Time Media Flow ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides a common implementation-independent basis for the interoperable application of the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol to the handling of Aggregated Flows, which are IPFIX Flows representing packets from multiple Original Flows sharing some set of common properties.  It does this through a detailed terminology and a descriptive Intermediate Aggregation Process architecture, including a specification of methods for Original Flow counting and counter distribution across intervals.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationkConsecom AGdnameiA. Wagner¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej’!hfilenamegrfc7015eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128846hkeywordsÉmFlow meteringpFlow measurementnIPFIX mediatorkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateR6í"grankinggcrfcd7015irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDFlow Aggregation for the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£The Intermediate Flow Selection Process is the process of selecting a subset of Flows from all observed Flows.  The Intermediate Flow Selection Process may be located at an IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Exporter or Collector, or within an IPFIX Mediator.  It reduces the effort of post-processing Flow data and transferring Flow Records.  This document describes motivations for using the Intermediate Flow Selection process and presents Intermediate Flow Selection techniques.  It provides an information model for configuring Intermediate Flow Selection Process techniques and discusses what information about an Intermediate Flow Selection Process should be exported.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx!University of Napoli "Parthenope"dnamelS. D'Antonio¢kaffiliationoCAIDA/FhG FOKUSdnamehT. Zseby¢kaffiliationxTektronix Communication BerlindnamehC. Henke¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnameiL. PelusoddatejŒ:hfilenamegrfc7014eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128845hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateR6í"grankingfcrfcd7014irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFlow Selection TechniquesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyoThis document provides guidelines for how to write definitions of new Information Elements for the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol.  It provides instructions on using the proper conventions for Information Elements to be registered in the IANA IPFIX Information Element registry, and provides guidelines for expert reviewers to evaluate new registrations.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej’!hfilenamegrfc7013eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128844hkeywordsÇjIE-DOCTORSdIANAkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateR6í"grankingecrfcd7013irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber∏etotaletitlex_Guidelines for Authors and Reviewers of IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information ElementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThis document defines the data types and management policy for the information model for the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol.  This information model is maintained as the IANA "IPFIX Information Elements" registry, the initial contents of which were defined by RFC 5102.  This information model is used by the IPFIX protocol for encoding measured traffic information and information related to the traffic Observation Point, the traffic Metering Process, and the Exporting Process.  Although this model was developed for the IPFIX protocol, it is defined in an open way that allows it to be easily used in other protocols, interfaces, and applications.  This document obsoletes RFC 5102.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. TrammellddatejŒ#hfilenamegrfc7012eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128843hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR6í grankingdcrfcd7012irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Information Model for IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty1This document specifies the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol, which serves as a means for transmitting Traffic Flow information over the network.  In order to transmit Traffic Flow information from an Exporting Process to a Collecting Process, a common representation of flow data and a standard means of communicating them are required.  This document describes how the IPFIX Data and Template Records are carried over a number of transport protocols from an IPFIX Exporting Process to an IPFIX Collecting Process.  This document obsoletes RFC 5101.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. AitkenddatejŒEhfilenamegrfc7011eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128842hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesLopublicationDateR6ígrankingccrfcd7011irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberMetotaletitlexeSpecification of the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Protocol for the Exchange of Flow InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂This document briefly introduces the existing mechanisms that could be utilized for IPv6 site renumbering and tries to cover most of the explicit issues and requirements associated with IPv6 renumbering.  The content is mainly a gap analysis that provides a basis for future works to identify and develop solutions or to stimulate such development as appropriate.  The gap analysis is organized by the main steps of a renumbering process.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamefB. Liu¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiW. GeorgeddatejŒChfilenamegrfc7010eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6renumdfullx6renum - IPv6 Site RenumberingdnameuIPv6 Site Renumberingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128841hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR3∞çgrankingbcrfcd7010irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"IPv6 Site Renumbering Gap AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis document proposes an additional endpoint for OAuth authorization servers, which allows clients to notify the authorization server that a previously obtained refresh or access token is no longer needed.  This allows the authorization server to clean up security credentials.  A revocation request will invalidate the actual token and, if applicable, other tokens based on the same authorization grant.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamenT. Lodderstedt¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Dronia¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. ScurtescuddatejŒFhfilenamegrfc7009eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128840hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR}ßgrankingacrfcd7009irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOAuth 2.0 Token RevocationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyvThis document describes the KCipher-2 encryption algorithm.  KCipher-2 is a stream cipher with a 128-bit key and a 128-bit initialization vector.  Since the algorithm for KCipher-2 was published in 2007, security and efficiency have been rigorously evaluated through academic and industrial studies.  As of the publication of this document, no security vulnerabilities have been found.  KCipher-2 offers fast encryption and decryption by means of simple operations that enable efficient implementation.  KCipher-2 has been used for industrial applications, especially for mobile health monitoring and diagnostic services in Japan.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxKDDI R&D Laboratories, Inc.dnamekS. Kiyomoto¢kaffiliationxKDDI R&D Laboratories, Inc.dnamegW. Shinddatej’:hfilenamegrfc7008eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128839hkeywordsÉjencryptionmstream cipherfcipherkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateRí˛granking`crfcd7008irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex3A Description of the KCipher-2 Encryption AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe RTP Profile for Audio and Video Conferences with Minimal Control (RTP/AVP) is the basis for many other profiles, such as the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP/SAVP), the Extended RTP Profile for Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP)-Based Feedback (RTP/AVPF), and the Extended Secure RTP Profile for RTCP-Based Feedback (RTP/SAVPF).  This document updates RFC 3551, the RTP/AVP profile (and by extension, the profiles that build upon it), to reflect changes in audio codec usage since that document was originally published.fadNamenRichard Barnesdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsMozilla CorporationdnamemT. TerriberryddatejŒBhfilenamegrfc7007eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128838hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRí˝granking_crfcd7007irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑUpdate to Remove DVI4 from the Recommended Codecs for the RTP Profile for Audio and Video Conferences with Minimal Control (RTP/AVP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–The Session Description Protocol (SDP) has been extended with a capability negotiation mechanism framework that allows the endpoints to negotiate transport protocols and attributes. This framework has been extended with a media capabilities negotiation mechanism that allows endpoints to negotiate additional media-related capabilities. This negotiation is embedded into the widely used SDP offer/answer procedures.

This memo extends the SDP capability negotiation framework to allow endpoints to negotiate three additional SDP capabilities. In particular, this memo provides a mechanism to negotiate bandwidth ("b=" line), connection data ("c=" line), and session or media titles ("i=" line for each session or media).fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoS. Veikkolainen¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Gilmanddatej’9hfilenamegrfc7006eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128837hkeywordsÉptitle capabilityxconnection data capabilitytbandwidth capabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRBQgranking^crfcd7006irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPMiscellaneous Capabilities Negotiation in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¶This document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) block that allows the reporting of de-jitter buffer metrics for a range of RTP applications.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsGTE InternetworkingdnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. WuddatejŒ>hfilenamegrfc7005eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128836hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR'¡ägranking]crfcd7005irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block for De-Jitter Buffer Metric ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx…This document defines three RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) blocks that allow the reporting of loss, duplication, and discard summary statistics metrics in a range of RTP applications.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Schott¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huangddatej’-hfilenamegrfc7004eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128835hkeywordsÖgRTCP XRrSummary StatisticsnBurst/Gap LossqBurst/Gap DiscardpFrame ImpairmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR'¡Ügranking\crfcd7004irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Blocks for Summary Statistics Metrics ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≤This document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) block that allows the reporting of burst and gap discard metrics for use in a range of RTP applications.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehR. Huang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej’0hfilenamegrfc7003eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128834hkeywordsÅxReal Time Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR'¡Ögranking[crfcd7003irfcNumber[estateÅ