Zornddatej’6hfilenamegrfc6990eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128824hkeywordsÖdRTCPbXReMPEG2cPSIlDecodabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR®}grankingNcrfcd6990irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex∞RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block for MPEG-2 Transport Stream (TS) Program Specific Information (PSI) Independent Decodability Statistics Metrics ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document adds a small number of mandatory tests required for the secure operation of the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) with elliptic curve groups.  No change is required to IKE implementations that use modular exponential groups, other than a few rarely used so-called Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) groups.  This document updates the IKEv2 protocol, RFC 5996.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhPorticordnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Fluhrerddatej’5hfilenamegrfc6989eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128823hkeywordsÉxElliptic Curve cryptographypsecret key reuseorecipient testskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateQÒC3grankingMcrfcd6989irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXAdditional Diffie-Hellman Tests for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚This document defines requirements for standards specifications for Energy Management. The requirements defined in this document are concerned with monitoring functions as well as control functions. Monitoring functions include identifying energy-managed devices and their components, as well as monitoring their Power States, Power Inlets, Power Outlets, actual power, Power Attributes, received energy, provided energy, and contained batteries. Control functions include such functions as controlling power supply and Power State of energy-managed devices and their components.

This document does not specify the features that must be implemented by compliant implementations but rather lists features that must be supported by standards for Energy Management.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameoM. Chandramouli¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnameiR. Winter¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamehT. Dietz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej’5hfilenamegrfc6988eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdemandfullxeman - Energy ManagementdnameqEnergy Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128822hkeywordsÇtmonitoring functionsqcontrol functionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRJògrankingLcrfcd6988irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Requirements for Energy ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document describes a backward-compatible technique that may be used by OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) implementations to advertise a router's unavailability to forward transit traffic or to lower the preference level for the paths through such a router.

This document obsoletes RFC 3137.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiL. Nguyen¢kaffiliationoCinarra SystemsdnamehA. Zinin¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. White¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelD. McPhersonddatej’2hfilenamegrfc6987eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128821hkeywordsÅiospf stubkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateR/∫grankingKcrfcd6987irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSPF Stub Router AdvertisementdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8770¥habstractxˆThis document is intended to be a source of information about the Russian Federal standard hash function (GOST R 34.11-2012), which is one of the Russian cryptographic standard algorithms (called GOST algorithms).  This document updates RFC 5831.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoCryptocom, Ltd.dnamekV. Dolmatov¢kaffiliationoCryptocom, Ltd.dnamelA. DegtyarevddatejŒ<hfilenamegrfc6986eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128820hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateRí¸grankingJcrfcd6986irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex GOST R 34.11-2012: Hash FunctiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—Benchmarking methodologies have always relied on test conditions with constant packet sizes, with the goal of understanding what network device capability has been tested.  Tests with a constant packet size reveal device capabilities but differ significantly from the conditions encountered in operational deployment, so additional tests are sometimes conducted with a mixture of packet sizes, or "IMIX" ("Internet Mix").  The mixture of sizes a networking device will encounter is highly variable and depends on many factors.  An IMIX suited for one networking device and deployment will not be appropriate for another.  However, the mix of sizes may be known, and the tester may be asked to augment the fixed-size tests.  To address this need and the perpetual goal of specifying repeatable test conditions, this document defines a way to specify the exact repeating sequence of packet sizes from the usual set of fixed sizes and from other forms of mixed-size specification.fadNamelJoel Jaegglidarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’4hfilenamegrfc6985eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128819hkeywordsÉgTrafficgPatternlBenchmarkingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateQËk¶grankingIcrfcd6985irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJIMIX Genome: Specification of Variable Packet Sizes for Additional TestingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document captures the results of the second Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) interoperability test that took place on February 24-25, 2011, in the Internet Technology Lab (ITL) at Zhejiang Gongshang University, China.  The results of the first ForCES interoperability test were reported in RFC 6053, and this document updates RFC 6053 by providing further interoperability results.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxZhejiang Gongshang UniversitydnamegW. Wang¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamehK. Ogawa¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationvHangzhou BAUD NetworksdnamefM. Gao¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi SalimddatejŒ:hfilenamegrfc6984eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128818hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRøgrankingHcrfcd6984irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNInteroperability Report for Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document presents thoughts on the potential impact of supporting HTTP Adaptive Streaming (HAS) technologies in Content Distribution Network Interconnection (CDNI) scenarios.  The intent is to present the authors' analysis of the CDNI-HAS problem space and discuss different options put forward by the authors (and by others during informal discussions) on how to deal with HAS in the context of CDNI.  This document has been used as input information during the CDNI working group process for making a decision regarding support for HAS.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcTNOdnamerR. van Brandenburg¢kaffiliationcTNOdnameoO. van Deventer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leungddatej’4hfilenamegrfc6983eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128817hkeywordsÑevideogcachingdHTTPpcontent deliverykobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateQégrankingGcrfcd6983irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex\Models for HTTP-Adaptive-Streaming-Aware Content Distribution Network Interconnection (CDNI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a simple process that allows authors of Internet-Drafts to record the status of known implementations by including an Implementation Status section. This will allow reviewers and working groups to assign due consideration to documents that have the benefit of running code, which may serve as evidence of valuable experimentation and feedback that have made the implemented protocols more mature.

The process in this document is offered as an experiment. Authors of Internet-Drafts are encouraged to consider using the process for their documents, and working groups are invited to think about applying the process to all of their protocol specifications. The authors of this document intend to collate experiences with this experiment and to report them to the community.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhPorticordnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnameiA. FarrelddatejŒ;hfilenamegrfc6982eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128816hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7942epages	opublicationDateQÊÎfgrankingFcrfcd6982irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFImproving Awareness of Running Code: The Implementation Status SectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈThis document presents an illustrative framework for providing fast reroute in an IP or MPLS network through encapsulation and forwarding to "not-via" addresses. The general approach described here uses a single level of encapsulation and could be used to protect unicast, multicast, and LDP traffic against link, router, and shared risk group failure, regardless of network topology and metrics.

The mechanisms presented in this document are purely illustrative of the general approach and do not constitute a protocol specification. The document represents a snapshot of the work of the Routing Area Working Group at the time of publication and is published as a document of record. Further work is needed before implementation or deployment.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehM. Shandddatej’3hfilenamegrfc6981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128815hkeywordsÅgnot-viakobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateQ˙3grankingEcrfcd6981irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@A Framework for IP and MPLS Fast Reroute Using Not-Via AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document analyzes the security implications of employing IPv6 fragmentation with Neighbor Discovery (ND) messages.  It updates RFC 4861 such that use of the IPv6 Fragmentation Header is forbidden in all Neighbor Discovery messages, thus allowing for simple and effective countermeasures for Neighbor Discovery attacks.  Finally, it discusses the security implications of using IPv6 fragmentation with SEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) and formally updates RFC 3971 to provide advice regarding how the aforementioned security implications can be mitigated.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gontddatej’4hfilenamegrfc6980eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128814hkeywordsÉovulnerabilitiesgevasionjmonitoringkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateR
∞¢grankingDcrfcd6980irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHSecurity Implications of IPv6 Fragmentation with IPv6 Neighbor DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty=This document defines a deterministic digital signature generation procedure.  Such signatures are compatible with standard Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) digital signatures and can be processed with unmodified verifiers, which need not be aware of the procedure described therein.  Deterministic signatures retain the cryptographic security features associated with digital signatures but can be more easily implemented in various environments, since they do not need access to a source of high-quality randomness.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Porninddatej’4hfilenamegrfc6979eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128813hkeywordsÑcdsaeecdsaqdigital signaturemdeterministickobsoletedByÄepagesOopublicationDateRÚkgrankingCcrfcd6979irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexsDeterministic Usage of the Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyhThis document describes an extension to the TCP Authentication Option (TCP-AO) to support its use over connections that pass through Network Address Translators and/or Network Address and Port Translators (NATs/NAPTs).  This extension changes the data used to compute traffic keys, but it does not alter TCP-AO's packet processing or key generation algorithms.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touchddatej’4hfilenamegrfc6978eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128812hkeywordsÅfTCP-AOkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÈ€ñgrankingBcrfcd6978irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex7A TCP Authentication Option Extension for NAT TraversaldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document defines two new DHCPv6 messages: Reconfigure-Request and Reconfigure-Reply.  The Reconfigure-Request message is sent by a DHCPv6 relay agent to notify a DHCPv6 server about a configuration information change, so that the DHCPv6 server can send a Reconfigure message accordingly.  The Reconfigure-Reply message is used by the server to acknowledge the receipt of the Reconfigure-Request message.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekX. Pougnardddatej’3hfilenamegrfc6977eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128811hkeywordsÉsReconfigure-RequestqReconfigure-ReplysLink Address OptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÊÎfgrankingAcrfcd6977irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Triggering DHCPv6 Reconfiguration from Relay AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThis document describes an illustrative framework of a mechanism for use in conjunction with link-state routing protocols that prevents the transient loops that would otherwise occur during topology changes. It does this by correctly sequencing the forwarding information base (FIB) updates on the routers.

This mechanism can be used in the case of non-urgent (management action) link or node shutdowns and restarts or link metric changes. It can also be used in conjunction with a fast reroute mechanism that converts a sudden link or node failure into a non-urgent topology change. This is possible where a complete repair path is provided for all affected destinations.

After a non-urgent topology change, each router computes a rank that defines the time at which it can safely update its FIB. A method for accelerating this loop-free convergence process by the use of completion messages is also described.

The technology described in this document has been subject to extensive simulation using pathological convergence behavior and real network topologies and costs. However, the mechanisms described in this document are purely illustrative of the general approach and do not constitute a protocol specification. This document represents a snapshot of the work of the Routing Area Working Group at the time of publication and is published as a document of record. Further work is needed before implementation or deployment.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationxInstitute IMDEA NetworksdnamekP. Francois¢kaffiliationx Universite catholique de LouvaindnamenO. BonaventureddatejŒ9hfilenamegrfc6976eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128810hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ˜ ågranking@crfcd6976irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaFramework for Loop-Free Convergence Using the Ordered Forwarding Information Base (oFIB) ApproachdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0The DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) were developed to provide origin authentication and integrity protection for DNS data by using digital signatures.  These digital signatures can be generated using different algorithms.  This document specifies a way for validating end-system resolvers to signal to a server which digital signature and hash algorithms they support.  The extensions allow the signaling of new algorithm uptake in client code to allow zone administrators to know when it is possible to complete an algorithm rollover in a DNSSEC-signed zone.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmShinkuro Inc.dnamejS. Crocker¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. Roseddatej’2hfilenamegrfc6975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128809hkeywordsÉcDNSfDNSSECdEDNSkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateQ‘ì‡granking?crfcd6975irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSSignaling Cryptographic Algorithm Understanding in DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document presents an applicability of existing MPLS protection mechanisms, both local and end-to-end, to the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) in ring topologies.  This document does not propose any new mechanisms or protocols.  Requirements for MPLS-TP protection especially for protection in ring topologies are discussed in "Requirements of an MPLS Transport Profile" (RFC 5654) and "MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Survivability Framework" (RFC 6372).  This document discusses how most of the requirements are met by applying linear protection as defined in RFC 6378 in a ring topology.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliation`dnamemY. Weingarten¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekF. Fondelli¢kaffiliationfAltrandnamehM. Corsi¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnameeB. Wu¢kaffiliation`dnamefX. DaiddatejŒ7hfilenamegrfc6974eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128808hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ˜ ãgranking>crfcd6974irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Applicability of MPLS Transport Profile for Ring TopologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractygThis document offers guidance for developing privacy considerations for inclusion in protocol specifications.  It aims to make designers, implementers, and users of Internet protocols aware of privacy-related design choices.  It suggests that whether any individual RFC warrants a specific privacy considerations section will depend on the document's content.gauthorsá¢kaffiliationcCDTdnameiA. Cooper¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationeSkypednamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Morris¢kaffiliationcULDdnameiM. Hansen¢kaffiliationeJanetdnamehR. Smithddatej’2hfilenamegrfc6973eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128807hkeywordsÖjDisclosureiAnonymitylPseudonymityoConfidentialityhIdentitykobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateQ§=granking=crfcd6973irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex-Privacy Considerations for Internet ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖPeer-to-Peer (P2P) streaming systems becoming more and more popular on the Internet, and most of them are using proprietary protocols.  This document identifies problems associated with proprietary protocols; proposes the development of the Peer-to-Peer Streaming Protocol (PPSP), which includes the tracker and peer protocols; and discusses the scope, requirements, and use cases of PPSP.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgCoolpaddnamehY. Zhang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegN. Zongddatej’1hfilenamegrfc6972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdppspdfullx&ppsp - Peer to Peer Streaming ProtocoldnamexPeer to Peer Streaming Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128806hkeywordsÅcP2PkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÈ€ñgranking<crfcd6972irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPProblem Statement and Requirements of the Peer-to-Peer Streaming Protocol (PPSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document specifies the Depth-First Forwarding (DFF) protocol for IPv6 networks, a data-forwarding mechanism that can increase reliability of data delivery in networks with dynamic topology and/or lossy links.  The protocol operates entirely on the forwarding plane but may interact with the routing plane.  DFF forwards data packets using a mechanism similar to a "depth-first search" for the destination of a packet.  The routing plane may be informed of failures to deliver a packet or loops.  This document specifies the DFF mechanism both for IPv6 networks (as specified in RFC 2460) and for "mesh-under" Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (LoWPANs), as specified in RFC 4944.  The design of DFF assumes that the underlying link layer provides means to detect if a packet has been successfully delivered to the Next Hop or not.  It is applicable for networks with little traffic and is used for unicast transmissions only.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgFujitsudnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Texas at DallasdnamekA. Cardenas¢kaffiliationgFujitsudnamegT. Iwao¢kaffiliationiFreescalednamefM. Dow¢kaffiliationpIcesi UniversitydnamekS. Cespedesddatej’1hfilenamegrfc6971eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128805hkeywordsÜcDFFvDepth first forwardingdIPv6pForwarding planenLossy networkskReliabilitykobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateQŒ$Ÿgranking;crfcd6971irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Depth-First Forwarding (DFF) in Unreliable NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document specifies the behavior of the Universal Plug and Play (UPnP) Internet Gateway Device - Port Control Protocol Interworking Function (IGD-PCP IWF).  A UPnP IGD-PCP IWF is required to be embedded in Customer Premises (CP) routers to allow for transparent NAT control in environments where a UPnP IGD is used on the LAN side and PCP is used on the external side of the CP router.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wingddatej’2hfilenamegrfc6970eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128804hkeywordsÜdUPnPgpinholecPCPgmappingkNAT controllinterworkingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQçgranking:crfcd6970irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrUniversal Plug and Play (UPnP) Internet Gateway Device - Port Control Protocol Interworking Function (IGD-PCP IWF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document modifies the "Unassigned" number space in the IANA "OSPFv3 Instance ID Address Family Values" registry by dividing it in two halves -- one half Unassigned but managed via Standards Action, and the other Reserved for Private Use.  It updates RFC 5838.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. ChengddatejŒ8hfilenamegrfc6969eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128803hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÊÎfgranking9crfcd6969irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"OSPFv3 Instance ID Registry UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document introduces the FCAST reliable object (e.g., file) delivery application.  It is designed to operate either on top of the underlying Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) / Layered Coding Transport (LCT) reliable multicast transport protocol or the NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) transport protocol.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnamejB. AdamsonddatejŒ4hfilenamegrfc6968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128802hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateQ‹@wgranking8crfcd6968irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvFCAST: Object Delivery for the Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) and NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document is a collection of potential solutions for revealing a host identifier (denoted as HOST_ID) when a Carrier Grade NAT (CGN) or application proxies are involved in the path. This host identifier could be used by a remote server to sort packets according to the sending host. The host identifier must be unique to each host under the same shared IP address.

This document analyzes a set of potential solutions for revealing a host identifier and does not recommend a particular solution, although it does highlight the hazards of some approaches.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamehP. Levis¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Pennoddatej’0hfilenamegrfc6967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128801hkeywordsÇcNAToHost IdentifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ¿’πgranking7crfcd6967irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgAnalysis of Potential Solutions for Revealing a Host Identifier (HOST_ID) in Shared Address DeploymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the applicability of the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) with use case studies and network design considerations.  The use cases include Metro Ethernet access and aggregation transport, mobile backhaul, and packet optical transport.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegL. Fang¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationdKDDIdnamejM. Daikoku¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamefP. PanddatejŒ4hfilenamegrfc6965eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128800hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ˙ïªgranking5crfcd6965irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDMPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Applicability: Use Cases and DesigndtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyåMany end-user sites in the Internet today still have predominantly IPv4 internal infrastructures.  These sites range in size from small home/office networks to large corporate enterprise networks, but share the commonality that IPv4 provides satisfactory internal routing and addressing services for most applications.  As more and more IPv6-only services are deployed, however, end-user devices within such sites will increasingly require at least basic IPv6 functionality.  This document therefore provides operational guidance for deployment of IPv6 within predominantly IPv4 sites using the Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP).gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamejF. Templinddatej’/hfilenamegrfc6964eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128799hkeywordsâdIPv6dIPv4iIPv6/IPv4lIPv6-in-IPv4ftunneliautomaticfisatapjenterprisedsitekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ©Øgranking4crfcd6964irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexyOperational Guidance for IPv6 Deployment in IPv4 Sites Using the Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx∆This document defines a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace identifier enabling the generation of URNs that are appropriate for use in documentation and in URN-related testing and experimentation.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’0hfilenamegrfc6963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128798hkeywordsÉcURNhexamplesmdocumentationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQü∆ägranking3crfcd6963irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber∑etotaletitlex4A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document describes an experimental protocol for publicly logging the existence of Transport Layer Security (TLS) certificates as they are issued or observed, in a manner that allows anyone to audit certificate authority (CA) activity and notice the issuance of suspect certificates as well as to audit the certificate logs themselves. The intent is that eventually clients would refuse to honor certificates that do not appear in a log, effectively forcing CAs to add all issued certificates to the logs.

Logs are network services that implement the protocol operations for submissions and queries that are defined in this document.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiB. Laurie¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejA. Langley¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiE. Kasperddatej’/hfilenamegrfc6962eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128797hkeywordsÅpTLS certificateskobsoletedByÅd9162epagesopublicationDateQ∞¯2granking2crfcd6962irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCertificate TransparencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜This document defines the Transport Layer Security (TLS) Certificate Status Version 2 Extension to allow clients to specify and support several certificate status methods. (The use of the Certificate Status extension is commonly referred to as "OCSP stapling".) Also defined is a new method based on the Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) that servers can use to provide status information about not only the server's own certificate but also the status of intermediate certificates in the chain.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelY. Pettersenddatej’.hfilenamegrfc6961eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128796hkeywordsãhRFC 6066hRFC 2560hRFC 6960hRFC 5246dOCSPmOCSP staplingnmulti-staplingxcertificate status checkingvrevocation informationnstatus_requestqstatus_request_v2kobsoletedByÅd8446epages
opublicationDateQ∂îlgranking1crfcd6961irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPThe Transport Layer Security (TLS) Multiple Certificate Status Request ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThis document specifies a protocol useful in determining the current status of a digital certificate without requiring Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs).  Additional mechanisms addressing PKIX operational requirements are specified in separate documents.  This document obsoletes RFCs 2560 and 6277.  It also updates RFC 5912.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationl3xA SecuritydnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationsTraceRoute SecuritydnamehM. Myers¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Ankney¢kaffiliationoCA TechnologiesdnamejA. Malpani¢kaffiliationbA9dnamekS. Galperin¢kaffiliationtUniversity of OttawadnamehC. Adamsddatej’.hfilenamegrfc6960eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-128795hkeywordsÉdPKIXpdigital securitydocspkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateQ∞¯2granking0crfcd6960irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRX.509 Internet Public Key Infrastructure Online Certificate Status Protocol - OCSPdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9654d8954∂habstracty®The Source Address Validation Improvement (SAVI) effort aims to complement ingress filtering with finer-grained, standardized IP source address validation.  This document describes threats enabled by IP source address spoofing both in the global and finer-grained context, describes currently available solutions and challenges, and provides a starting point analysis for finer-grained (host granularity) anti-spoofing work.fadNameiTed Lemondarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. HalpernddatejŒ2hfilenamegrfc6959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsavidfullx-savi - Source Address Validation Improvementsdnamex&Source Address Validation Improvementsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128794hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ¶)Ìgranking/crfcd6959irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Source Address Validation Improvement (SAVI) Threat ScopedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxØThis document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block that allows the reporting of burst and gap loss metrics for use in a range of RTP applications.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationeSTTRIdnamehS. Zhang¢kaffiliationeSTTRIdnamegJ. Zhao¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej’/hfilenamegrfc6958eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128793hkeywordsÅxReal Time Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQüˆ<granking.crfcd6958irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block for Burst/Gap Loss Metric ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThe document describes a proxy-based mechanism allowing the use of Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) by IPv6 nodes in a point-to-multipoint architecture with a "split-horizon" forwarding scheme, primarily deployed for Digital Subscriber Line (DSL) and Fiber access architectures.  Based on the DAD signaling, the first-hop router stores in a Binding Table all known IPv6 addresses used on a point-to-multipoint domain (e.g., VLAN).  When a node performs DAD for an address already used by another node, the first-hop router defends the address rather than the device using the address.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednamehF. Costa¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednamekJ-M. Combes¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednamekX. Pougnard¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeH. Liddatej’.hfilenamegrfc6957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128792hkeywordsÑdIPv6eSLAACcDADdSAVIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ∞¯1granking-crfcd6957irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Duplicate Address Detection ProxydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document defines basic classes of Logical Function Blocks (LFBs) used in Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES).  The basic LFB classes are defined according to the ForCES Forwarding Element (FE) model and ForCES protocol specifications; they are scoped to meet requirements of typical router functions and are considered the basic LFB library for ForCES.  The library includes the descriptions of the LFBs and the XML definitions.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxZhejiang Gongshang UniversitydnamegW. Wang¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamehK. Ogawa¢kaffiliationoHangzhou DPtechdnameeC. Li¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpernddatej’,hfilenamegrfc6956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128791hkeywordsÉfForCEScLFBgLibrarykobsoletedByÄepagesoopublicationDateQŒ$ÿgranking,crfcd6956irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Logical Function Block (LFB) LibrarydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜This document describes two methods for producing an integrity check value from a Diffie-Hellman key pair and one method for producing an integrity check value from an Elliptic Curve key pair. This behavior is needed for such operations as creating the signature of a Public-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) #10 Certification Request. These algorithms are designed to provide a Proof-of-Possession of the private key and not to be a general purpose signing algorithm.

This document obsoletes RFC 2875.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationmHyTrust, Inc.dnameqH. Prafullchandraddatej’-hfilenamegrfc6955eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128790hkeywordsÉcPOPdECDHbDHkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateQ¶)Ìgranking+crfcd6955irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Diffie-Hellman Proof-of-Possession AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÆThis document specifies use of the Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Brainpool elliptic curve groups for key exchange in the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2).fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxsecunet Security NetworksdnameiJ. Merkle¢kaffiliationx:Bundesamt fuer Sicherheit in der Informationstechnik (BSI)dnamejM. Lochterddatej’/hfilenamegrfc6954eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128789hkeywordsÇcIKEnElliptic CurvekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÒC2granking*crfcd6954irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuUsing the Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Brainpool Curves for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:Portions of the radio spectrum that are assigned to a particular use but are unused or unoccupied at specific locations and times are defined as "white space".  The concept of allowing additional transmissions (which may or may not be licensed) in white space is a technique to "unlock" existing spectrum for new use.  This document includes the problem statement for the development of a protocol to access a database of white-space information followed by use cases and requirements for that protocol.  Finally, requirements associated with the protocol are presented.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejA. Mancuso¢kaffiliation`dnamekS. Probasco¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehB. PatilddatejŒ1hfilenamegrfc6953eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpawsdfullx%paws - Protocol to Access WS databasednamexProtocol to Access WS databasedtypebwgbidjdoc-128788hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQú‰>granking)crfcd6953irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKProtocol to Access White-Space (PAWS) Databases: Use Cases and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document analyzes TCP-based routing protocols, the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP), the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP), the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP), and the Multicast Source Distribution Protocol (MSDP), according to guidelines set forth in Section 4.2 of "Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols Design Guidelines", RFC 6518.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehL. Zhengddatej’.hfilenamegrfc6952eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdkarpdfullx6karp - Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdnamex/Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128787hkeywordsÑckeynauthenticationgroutingcDoSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQüˆ<granking(crfcd6952irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄAnalysis of BGP, LDP, PCEP, and MSDP Issues According to the Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design GuidedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡This document describes a simple method of encapsulating Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) packets into UDP packets and its limitations. This allows the usage of SCTP in networks with legacy NATs that do not support SCTP. It can also be used to implement SCTP on hosts without directly accessing the IP layer, for example, implementing it as part of the application without requiring special privileges.

Please note that this document only describes the functionality required within an SCTP stack to add on UDP encapsulation, providing only those mechanisms for two end-hosts to communicate with each other over UDP ports. In particular, it does not provide mechanisms to determine whether UDP encapsulation is being used by the peer, nor the mechanisms for determining which remote UDP port number can be used. These functions are out of scope for this document.

This document covers only end-hosts and not tunneling (egress or ingress) endpoints.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationnAdara NetworksdnamejR. StewartddatejŒ1hfilenamegrfc6951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128786hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ¶)Ïgranking'crfcd6951irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoUDP Encapsulation of Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Packets for End-Host to End-Host CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8899¥habstractyA number of Internet applications rely on the Domain Name System (DNS) to support their operations.  Many applications use the DNS to locate services for a domain; some, for example, transform identifiers other than domain names into formats that the DNS can process, and then fetch application data or service location data from the DNS.  Proposals incorporating sophisticated application behavior using DNS as a substrate have raised questions about the role of the DNS as an application platform.  This document explores the architectural consequences of using the DNS to implement certain application features, and it provides guidance to future application designers as to the limitations of the DNS as a substrate and the situations in which alternative designs should be considered.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationeSkypednamehB. AbobaddatejŒ+hfilenamegrfc6950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128785hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateRUÇzgranking&crfcd6950irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex?Architectural Considerations on Application Features in the DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty}This document describes the current requirements and requests for enhancements for the format of the canonical version of RFCs.  Terms are defined to help clarify exactly which stages of document production are under discussion for format changes.  The requirements described in this document will determine what changes will be made to RFC format.  This document updates RFC 2223.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqRFC Series EditordnamekH. Flanagan¢kaffiliationxIndependent Submissions EditordnamekN. BrownleeddatejŒ-hfilenamegrfc6949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128784hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQí÷bgranking%crfcd6949irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex5RFC Series Format Requirements and Future DevelopmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyDuring World IPv6 Day on June 8, 2011, several key content providers enabled their networks to offer both IPv4 and IPv6 services.  Hundreds of organizations participated in this effort, and in the months and weeks leading up to the event worked hard on preparing their networks to support this event.  The event was largely unnoticed by the general public, which is a good thing since it means that no major problems were detected.  For the Internet, however, there was a major change on a short timescale.  This memo discusses measurements that the authors made from the perspective of an end user with good IPv4 and IPv6 connectivity.  Our measurements include the number of most popular networks providing AAAA records for their service, as well as delay and connection failure statistics.fadNamenBrian HabermangauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. ArkkoddatejŒ.hfilenamegrfc6948eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128783hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÊÎegranking$crfcd6948irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex@Some Measurements on World IPv6 Day from an End-User PerspectivedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document proposes a mechanism that allows the same SDP offer to carry multiple IP addresses of different address families (e.g., IPv4 and IPv6). The proposed attribute, the "altc" attribute, solves the backward-compatibility problem that plagued Alternative Network Address Types (ANAT) due to their syntax.

The proposed solution is applicable to scenarios where connectivity checks are not required. If connectivity checks are required, Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE), as specified in RFC 5245, provides such a solution.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationkAcme PacketdnameiH. Kaplan¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiR. Gilman¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameoS. VeikkolainenddatejŒ-hfilenamegrfc6947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128782hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQí÷agranking#crfcd6947irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexNThe Session Description Protocol (SDP) Alternate Connectivity (ALTC) AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)The IPv6 specification allows packets to contain a Fragment Header without the packet being actually fragmented into multiple pieces (we refer to these packets as "atomic fragments").  Such packets are typically sent by hosts that have received an ICMPv6 "Packet Too Big" error message that advertises a Next-Hop MTU smaller than 1280 bytes, and are currently processed by some implementations as normal "fragmented traffic" (i.e., they are "reassembled" with any other queued fragments that supposedly correspond to the same original packet).  Thus, an attacker can cause hosts to employ atomic fragments by forging ICMPv6 "Packet Too Big" error messages, and then launch any fragmentation-based attacks against such traffic.  This document discusses the generation of the aforementioned atomic fragments and the corresponding security implications.  Additionally, this document formally updates RFC 2460 and RFC 5722, such that IPv6 atomic fragments are processed independently of any other fragments, thus completely eliminating the aforementioned attack vector.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. Gontddatej’,hfilenamegrfc6946eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128781hkeywordsÑmfragmentationgattacksovulnerabilitiespatomic fragmentskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateQí©5granking"crfcd6946irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Processing of IPv6 "Atomic" FragmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for monitoring the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) to Router Protocol.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnameiB. Wijnen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationfSPARTAdnamegM. BaerddatejŒ-hfilenamegrfc6945eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128780hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQçôtgranking!crfcd6945irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcDefinitions of Managed Objects for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) to Router ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThe DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) requires the use of cryptographic algorithm suites for generating digital signatures over DNS data.  There is currently an IANA registry for these algorithms, but there is no record of the recommended implementation status of each algorithm.  This document provides an applicability statement on algorithm implementation status for DNSSEC component software.  This document lists each algorithm's status based on the current reference.  In the case that an algorithm is specified without an implementation status, this document assigns one.  This document updates RFCs 2536, 2539, 3110, 4034, 4398, 5155, 5702, and 5933.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. RoseddatejŒ,hfilenamegrfc6944eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128779hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8624epagesopublicationDateQÄ©<granking crfcd6944irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUApplicability Statement: DNS Security (DNSSEC) DNSKEY Algorithm Implementation StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyIdentifiers such as hostnames, URIs, IP addresses, and email addresses are often used in security contexts to identify security principals and resources.  In such contexts, an identifier presented via some protocol is often compared using some policy to make security decisions such as whether the security principal may access the resource, what level of authentication or encryption is required, etc.  If the parties involved in a security decision use different algorithms to compare identifiers, then failure scenarios ranging from denial of service to elevation of privilege can result.  This document provides a discussion of these issues that designers should consider when defining identifiers and protocols, and when constructing architectures that use multiple protocols.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej’+hfilenamegrfc6943eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128778hkeywordsÖpCanonicalizationmNormalizationhHostnamecURIcIRIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQå≤grankingcrfcd6943irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex5Issues in Identifier Comparison for Security PurposesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&The EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP) defines extensions to the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) to support efficient re-authentication between the peer and an EAP Re-authentication (ER) server through a compatible authenticator.  This document specifies Diameter support for ERP.  It defines a new Diameter ERP application to transport ERP messages between an ER authenticator and the ER server, and a set of new Attribute-Value Pairs (AVPs) that can be used to transport the cryptographic material needed by the re-authentication server.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamelJ. Bournelle¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnameiL. Morand¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejS. Decugis¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. ZornddatejŒ,hfilenamegrfc6942eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128777hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQçôsgrankingcrfcd6942irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Diameter Support for the EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThis document provides a security framework for the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP). MPLS-TP extends MPLS technologies and introduces new Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) capabilities, a transport-oriented path protection mechanism, and strong emphasis on static provisioning supported by network management systems. This document addresses the security aspects relevant in the context of MPLS-TP specifically. It describes potential security threats as well as mitigation procedures related to MPLS-TP networks and to MPLS-TP interconnection to other MPLS and GMPLS networks. This document is built on RFC 5920 ("Security Framework for MPLS and GMPLS Networks") by providing additional security considerations that are applicable to the MPLS-TP extensions. All the security considerations from RFC 5920 are assumed to apply.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionality of a packet transport network.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegL. Fang¢kaffiliationgVelocixdnamepB. Niven-Jenkins¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelS. Mansfield¢kaffiliationlRFG SecuritydnamekR. Gravemanddatej’+hfilenamegrfc6941eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128776hkeywordsÉgthreatsjmitigationtdefensive techniqueskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÄ©<grankingcrfcd6941irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Security FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This specification defines REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD), a peer-to-peer (P2P) signaling protocol for use on the Internet.  A P2P signaling protocol provides its clients with an abstract storage and messaging service between a set of cooperating peers that form the overlay network.  RELOAD is designed to support a P2P Session Initiation Protocol (P2PSIP) network, but can be utilized by other applications with similar requirements by defining new usages that specify the Kinds of data that need to be stored for a particular application.  RELOAD defines a security model based on a certificate enrollment service that provides unique identities.  NAT traversal is a fundamental service of the protocol.  RELOAD also allows access from "client" nodes that do not need to route traffic or store data for others.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationeSkypednamekB. Lowekamp¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamehS. Baset¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej’(hfilenamegrfc6940eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfp2psipdfullx1p2psip - Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldnamex(Peer-to-Peer Session Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128775hkeywordsÖcp2pcdhtfp2psipechordlpeer to peerkobsoletedByÄepages∞opublicationDateRÿ»6grankingcrfcd6940irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6REsource LOcation And Discovery (RELOAD) Base ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx This document specifies the format and mechanism that is to be used for encoding the client link-layer address in DHCPv6 Relay-Forward messages by defining a new DHCPv6 Client Link-Layer Address option.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekG. Halwasia¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. Bhandari¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefW. DecddatejŒ+hfilenamegrfc6939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128774hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQí©3grankingcrfcd6939irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Client Link-Layer Address Option in DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∏This document requests IANA to deprecate the following BGP path attributes: DPA, ADVERTISER, and RCID_PATH / CLUSTER_ID, associated with an abandoned Internet-Draft and a Historic RFC.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej’+hfilenamegrfc6938eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128773hkeywordsÅcBGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQí©3grankingcrfcd6938irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexODeprecation of BGP Path Attributes: DPA, ADVERTISER, and RCID_PATH / CLUSTER_IDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThis document describes an experimental Proportional Rate Reduction (PRR) algorithm as an alternative to the widely deployed Fast Recovery and Rate-Halving algorithms.  These algorithms determine the amount of data sent by TCP during loss recovery.  PRR minimizes excess window adjustments, and the actual window size at the end of recovery will be as close as possible to the ssthresh, as determined by the congestion control algorithm.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiM. Mathis¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamelN. Dukkipati¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehY. Chengddatej’*hfilenamegrfc6937eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128772hkeywordsÉqTCP loss recoveryspacket conservationjself clockkobsoletedByÅd9937epagesopublicationDateQå±grankingcrfcd6937irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Proportional Rate Reduction for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠This document provides an applicability statement for the use of UDP transport checksums with IPv6.  It defines recommendations and requirements for the use of IPv6 UDP datagrams with a zero UDP checksum.  It describes the issues and design principles that need to be considered when UDP is used with IPv6 to support tunnel encapsulations, and it examines the role of the IPv6 UDP transport checksum.  The document also identifies issues and constraints for deployment on network paths that include middleboxes.  An appendix presents a summary of the trade-offs that were considered in evaluating the safety of the update to RFC 2460 that changes the use of the UDP checksum with IPv6.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. WesterlundddatejŒ*hfilenamegrfc6936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128771hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateQÄ©;grankingcrfcd6936irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMApplicability Statement for the Use of IPv6 UDP Datagrams with Zero ChecksumsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document updates the IPv6 specification (RFC 2460) to improve performance when a tunnel protocol uses UDP with IPv6 to tunnel packets.  The performance improvement is obtained by relaxing the IPv6 UDP checksum requirement for tunnel protocols whose header information is protected on the "inner" packet being carried.  Relaxing this requirement removes the overhead associated with the computation of UDP checksums on IPv6 packets that carry the tunnel protocol packets.  This specification describes how the IPv6 UDP checksum requirement can be relaxed when the encapsulated packet itself contains a checksum.  It also describes the limitations and risks of this approach and discusses the restrictions on the use of this method.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrAmericaFree.TV LLCdnamejM. Eubanks¢kaffiliationx3Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics LaboratorydnamekP. Chimento¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej’*hfilenamegrfc6935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128770hkeywordsÖfTunnelmEncapsulationiIntegrityqPacket CorruptioniMiddleboxkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÄ©:grankingcrfcd6935irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+IPv6 and UDP Checksums for Tunneled PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(The purpose of this document is to provide applicability of the Access Node Control Mechanism to broadband access based on Passive Optical Networks (PONs).  The need for an Access Node Control Mechanism between a Network Access Server (NAS) and an Access Node Complex, composed of a combination of Optical Line Termination (OLT) and Optical Network Termination (ONT) elements, is described in a multi-service reference architecture in order to perform QoS-related, service-related, and subscriber-related operations.  The Access Node Control Mechanism is also extended for interaction between components of the Access Node Complex (OLT and ONT).  The Access Node Control Mechanism will ensure that the transmission of information between the NAS and Access Node Complex (ANX) and between the OLT and ONT within an ANX does not need to go through distinct element managers but rather uses direct device-to-device communication and stays on net.  This allows for performing access-link-related operations within those network elements to meet performance objectives.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiS. Wadhwa¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegT. Haag¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeH. LiddatejŒ)hfilenamegrfc6934eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdancpdfullx#ancp - Access Node Control ProtocoldnamexAccess Node Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128769hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateQ¿’∏grankingcrfcd6934irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqApplicability of the Access Node Control Mechanism to Broadband Networks Based on Passive Optical Networks (PONs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyπThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for managing multiple logical and physical entities managed by a single Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) agent.  This document specifies version 4 of the Entity MIB.  This memo obsoletes version 3 of the Entity MIB module published as RFC 4133.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiYumaworksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationeAVAYAdnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameoM. Chandramouliddatej’(hfilenamegrfc6933eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdemandfullxeman - Energy ManagementdnameqEnergy Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128768hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basedsnmpx"simple network management protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesLopublicationDateQü…Xgrankingcrfcd6933irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevEntity MIB (Version 4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx“This memo allocates code points for four new elliptic curve domain parameter sets over finite prime fields into a registry that was established by the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) but is used by other protocols.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Harkinsddatej’*hfilenamegrfc6932eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128767hkeywordsÇnelliptic curvenDiffie-HellmankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQú‰=grankingcrfcd6932irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXBrainpool Elliptic Curves for the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) Group Description RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document expands, updates, and establishes an IANA registry for the list of URIs intended for use with XML digital signatures, encryption, canonicalization, and key management.  These URIs identify algorithms and types of information.  This document obsoletes RFC 4051.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatejŒ0hfilenamegrfc6931eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128766hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9231epages$opublicationDateQp&Çgrankingcrfcd6931irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Additional XML Security Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠The IPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures (6rd) provides both IPv4 and IPv6 connectivity services simultaneously during the IPv4/IPv6 coexistence period.  The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) 6rd option has been defined to configure the 6rd Customer Edge (CE).  However, in many networks, the configuration information may be stored in the Authentication Authorization and Accounting (AAA) servers, while user configuration is mainly acquired from a Broadband Network Gateway (BNG) through the DHCP protocol.  This document defines a Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) attribute that carries 6rd configuration information from the AAA server to BNGs.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamefD. Guo¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationiRD-IPtechdnamejR. Despres¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. MaglioneddatejŒ)hfilenamegrfc6930eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-128765hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÄ©9grankingcrfcd6930irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRADIUS Attribute for IPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures (6rd)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThe Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) protocol is nearing exhaustion of its current 8-bit Attribute Type space.  In addition, experience shows a growing need for complex grouping, along with attributes that can carry more than 253 octets of data.  This document defines changes to RADIUS that address all of the above problems.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnNetwork RADIUSdnamehA. DeKok¢kaffiliation`dnamegA. LiorddatejŒ(hfilenamegrfc6929eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128764hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDateQÄ©9grankingcrfcd6929irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGRemote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) Protocol ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€This document proposes an experiment to increase the permitted TCP initial window (IW) from between 2 and 4 segments, as specified in RFC 3390, to 10 segments with a fallback to the existing recommendation when performance issues are detected.  It discusses the motivation behind the increase, the advantages and disadvantages of the higher initial window, and presents results from several large-scale experiments showing that the higher initial window improves the overall performance of many web services without resulting in a congestion collapse.  The document closes with a discussion of usage and deployment for further experimental purposes recommended by the IETF TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensions (TCPM) working group.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamefJ. Chu¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamelN. Dukkipati¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehY. Cheng¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiM. MathisddatejŒ(hfilenamegrfc6928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128763hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ~¡Vgrankingcrfcd6928irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIncreasing TCP's Initial WindowdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyWInternationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA) provides a method to map a subset of names written in Unicode into the DNS.  Because of Unicode decisions, appearance, language and writing system conventions, and historical reasons, it often has been asserted that there is more than one way to write what competent readers and writers think of as the same host name; these different ways of writing are often called "variants". (The authors note that there are many conflicting definitions for the term "variant" in the IDNA community.) This document surveys the approaches that top-level domains have taken to the registration and provisioning of domain names that have variants.  This document is not a product of the IETF, does not propose any method to make variants work "correctly", and is not an introduction to internationalization or IDNA.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levine¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej’*hfilenamegrfc6927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128762hkeywordsÉcDNSgvariantdTLDskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQà∆grankingcrfcd6927irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex>Variants in Second-Level Names Registered in Top-Level DomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv4 (DHCPv4) Leasequery protocol allows a requestor to request information about DHCPv4 bindings.  This protocol is limited to queries for individual bindings.  In some situations, individual binding queries may not be efficient or even possible.  This document extends the DHCPv4 Leasequery protocol to allow for bulk transfer of DHCPv4 address binding data via TCP.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationxInfosys Technologies Ltd.dnamejR. Desetti¢kaffiliationxInfosys Technologies Ltd.dnamehB. Joshi¢kaffiliationxSea Street Technologies Inc.dnamejN. Russell¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks Ltd.dnamekP. Kurapati¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. VolzddatejÀÆhfilenamegrfc6926eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128761hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateQ~¡Ugrankingcrfcd6926irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevDHCPv4 Bulk LeasequerydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7724∂habstracty¨This document defines a new Relay Agent Identifier sub-option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Relay Agent Information option.  The sub-option carries a value that uniquely identifies the relay agent device within the administrative domain.  The value is normally administratively configured in the relay agent.  The sub-option allows a DHCP relay agent to include the identifier in the DHCP messages it sends.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlInfosys Ltd.dnamehB. Joshi¢kaffiliationlInfosys Ltd.dnamejR. Desetti¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stappddatej’*hfilenamegrfc6925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128760hkeywordsÇdDHCPerelaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ~¡Tgrankingcrfcd6925irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,The DHCPv4 Relay Agent Identifier Sub-OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£RFC 2648 defines the "ietf" URN namespace and a number of sub- namespaces.  RFC 3553 defines an additional sub-namespace, "params", and creates a registry to document allocations under that.  But there is no registry that lists, in one place, all sub-namespaces of "ietf".  This document creates and populates such a registry, thereby changing the mechanism defined in RFC 2648 for adding new sub- namespaces of "ietf".fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. LeibaddatejŒ&hfilenamegrfc6924eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128759hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQe¯grankingcrfcd6924irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Registration of Second-Level URN Namespaces under "ietf"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document specifies an extension to the identifiers to be used in the Transport Profile of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS-TP).  Identifiers that follow IP/MPLS conventions have already been defined.  This memo augments that set of identifiers for MPLS-TP management and Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) functions to include identifier information in a format typically used by the International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T).fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNECdnameiR. Winter¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamehM. BettsddatejŒ(hfilenamegrfc6923eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128758hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQåÆgrankingcrfcd6923irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Identifiers Following ITU-T ConventionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxjThis document registers the application/sql media type to be used for the Structured Query Language (SQL).fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoBioFortis, Inc.dnameoY. Shafranovichddatej’)hfilenamegrfc6922eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128757hkeywordsÇcSQLdMIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQe¯granking
crfcd6922irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe application/sql Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty0We are approaching the time when we will be able to communicate faster than the speed of light.  It is well known that as we approach the speed of light, time slows down.  Logically, it is reasonable to assume that as we go faster than the speed of light, time will reverse.  The major consequence of this for Internet protocols is that packets will arrive before they are sent.  This will have a major impact on the way we design Internet protocols.  This paper outlines some of the issues and suggests some directions for additional analysis of these issues.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. HindenddatejŒ/hfilenamegrfc6921eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-19634hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQZÇgranking	crfcd6921irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex?Design Considerations for Faster-Than-Light (FTL) CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis document defines a set of ways to identify a thing (a digital object in this case) using the output from a hash function.  It specifies a new URI scheme for this purpose, a way to map these to HTTP URLs, and binary and human-speakable formats for these names.  The various formats are designed to support, but not require, a strong link to the referenced object, such that the referenced object may be authenticated to the same degree as the reference to it.  The reason for this work is to standardise current uses of hash outputs in URLs and to support new information-centric applications and other uses of hash outputs in protocols.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationcNECdnamekD. Kutscher¢kaffiliationwUniversity of PaderborndnamelC. Dannewitz¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Ohlman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationqComodo Group Inc.dnameoP. Hallam-Bakerddatej‘òhfilenamegrfc6920eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128756hkeywordsÉlCryptographycURIxInformation Centric NetworkingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQubŸgrankingcrfcd6920irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNaming Things with HashesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyíRFC 2119 defines a standard set of key words for describing requirements of a specification. Many IETF documents have found that these words cannot accurately capture the nuanced requirements of their specification. This document defines additional key words that can be used to address alternative requirements scenarios. Authors who follow these guidelines should incorporate this phrase near the beginning of their document:

The key words "MUST (BUT WE KNOW YOU WON\'T)", "SHOULD CONSIDER", "REALLY SHOULD NOT", "OUGHT TO", "WOULD PROBABLY", "MAY WISH TO", "COULD", "POSSIBLE", and "MIGHT" in this document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 6919.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcBBNdnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. RescorladdatejŒ/hfilenamegrfc6919eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-19633hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQZRgrankingcrfcd6919irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex@Further Key Words for Use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement LevelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_A number of ICMPv4 message types have become obsolete in practice, but have never been formally deprecated.  This document deprecates such ICMPv4 message types, thus cleaning up the corresponding IANA registry.  Additionally, it updates RFC 792 and RFC 950, obsoletes RFC 1788, and requests the RFC Editor to change the status of RFC 1788 to Historic.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUTN-FRH / SI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataroddatej’)hfilenamegrfc6918eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128755hkeywordsÇdIANAlIPv4 OptionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQp
grankingcrfcd6918irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Formally Deprecating Some ICMPv4 Message TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThe MediaCtrl working group in the IETF has proposed an architecture for controlling media services.  The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is used as the signaling protocol that provides many inherent capabilities for message routing.  In addition to such signaling properties, a need exists for intelligent, application-level media service selection based on non-static signaling properties.  This is especially true when considered in conjunction with deployment architectures that include 1:M and M:N combinations of Application Servers and Media Servers.  This document introduces a Media Resource Broker (MRB) entity, which manages the availability of Media Servers and the media resource demands of Application Servers.  The document includes potential deployment options for an MRB and appropriate interfaces to Application Servers and Media Servers.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNS-TechnologiesdnamejC. Boulton¢kaffiliationhMeetechodnamejL. Miniero¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiG. MunsonddatejŒ%hfilenamegrfc6917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128754hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesàopublicationDateQg/grankingcrfcd6917irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMedia Resource BrokeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyˇThis document specifies the process that Certification Authorities (CAs) and Relying Parties (RPs) participating in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) will need to follow to transition to a new (and probably cryptographically stronger) algorithm set.  The process is expected to be completed over a timescale of several years.  Consequently, no emergency transition is specified.  The transition procedure defined in this document supports only a top-down migration (parent migrates before children).fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Gagliano¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationjIECA, Inc.dnameiS. Turnerddatej’'hfilenamegrfc6916eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128753hkeywordsÜx"Resource Public Key InfrastructuredRPKItAlgorithm TransitiondSIDRprouting securitylBGP securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQe¯grankingcrfcd6916irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber∂etotaletitlexMAlgorithm Agility Procedure for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKRFC 6155 specifies an extension for the HTTP-Enabled Location Delivery (HELD) protocol, allowing the use of an IP address and port number to request a Device location based on an individual packet flow.

However, certain kinds of NAT require that identifiers for both ends of the packet flow must be specified in order to unambiguously satisfy the location request.

This document specifies an XML Schema and a URN Sub-Namespace for a Flow Identity Extension for HELD to support this requirement.

This document updates RFC 6155 by deprecating the port number elements specified therein.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjNominet UKdnameiR. Bellisddatej’*hfilenamegrfc6915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128752hkeywordsÇdHELDdFlowkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateQe¯grankingcrfcd6915irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAFlow Identity Extension for HTTP-Enabled Location Delivery (HELD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°The IETF has produced many specifications related to Presence and Instant Messaging with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  Collectively, these specifications are known as SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensions (SIMPLE).  This document serves as a guide to the SIMPLE suite of specifications.  It categorizes the specifications, explains what each is for, and how they relate to each other.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej’)hfilenamegrfc6914eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128751hkeywordsÑcSIPfSIMPLEhpresencebIMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQuéqgrankingcrfcd6914irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâSIMPLE Made Simple: An Overview of the IETF Specifications for Instant Messaging and Presence Using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document defines and registers with IANA the new "fax" media feature tag for use with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  Currently, fax calls are indistinguishable from voice calls at call initiation.  Consequently, fax calls can be routed to SIP user agents that are not fax capable.  A "fax" media feature tag implemented in conjunction with caller preferences allows for more accurate fax call routing.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Hanes¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationlDigium, Inc.dnamejK. Flemingddatej’(hfilenamegrfc6913eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128750hkeywordsÅqmedia feature tagkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateQSê…grankingcrfcd6913irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJIndicating Fax over IP Capability in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyŒInternationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) makes available to DNS zone administrators a very wide range of Unicode code points.  Most operators of zones should probably not permit registration of U-labels using the entire range.  This is especially true of zones that accept registrations across organizational boundaries, such as top-level domains and, most importantly, the root.  It is unfortunately not possible to generate algorithms to determine whether permitting a code point presents a low risk.  This memo presents a set of principles that can be used to guide the decision of whether a Unicode code point may be wisely included in the repertoire of permissible code points in a U-label in a zone.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamekA. Sullivan¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejO. KolkmanddatejŒ"hfilenamegrfc6912eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128749hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQmãîgranking␀crfcd6912irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex@Principles for Unicode Code Point Inclusion in Labels in the DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂This document specifies additional IPv6 RADIUS Attributes useful in residential broadband network deployments.  The Attributes, which are used for authorization and accounting, enable assignment of a host IPv6 address and an IPv6 DNS server address via DHCPv6, assignment of an IPv6 route announced via router advertisement, assignment of a named IPv6 delegated prefix pool, and assignment of a named IPv6 pool for host DHCPv6 addressing.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefW. Dec¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehD. Miles¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelB. Lourdeletddatej’'hfilenamegrfc6911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128748hkeywordsÉcAAAfRADIUSdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ~¡Sgrankingˇcrfcd6911irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*RADIUS Attributes for IPv6 Access NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡The "completion of calls" feature defined in this specification allows the caller of a failed call to be notified when the callee becomes available to receive a call.

For the realization of a basic solution without queuing, this document references the usage of the dialog event package (RFC 4235) that is described as 'Automatic Redial' in "Session Initiation Protocol Service Examples" (RFC 5359).

For the realization of a more comprehensive solution with queuing, this document introduces an architecture for implementing these features in the Session Initiation Protocol where "completion of calls" implementations associated with the caller's and callee's endpoints cooperate to place the caller's request for completion of calls into a queue at the callee's endpoint; when a caller's request is ready to be serviced, re-attempt of the original, failed call is then made.

The architecture is designed to interoperate well with existing completion of calls solutions in other networks.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxAriadne Internet Services, Inc.dnameiD. Worley¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamemM. Huelsemann¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesske¢kaffiliationiTeleFLASHdnamemD. Alexeitsevddatej’%hfilenamegrfc6910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeblissdfullx8bliss - Basic Level of Interoperability for SIP Servicesdnamex0Basic Level of Interoperability for SIP Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128747hkeywordsèocall completionbCCcSS7rSignaling System 7xpurpose header parameterom URI parameterrm header parameterxcall-completion event packageh√Ç¬†CCBSdCCNRdCCNLvCall-Info header fieldx Presence Information Data FormatdPIDFx P-Asserted-Identity header fieldkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateQlvlgranking˛crfcd6910irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Completion of Calls for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThis specification defines a new mobility option, the IPv4 Traffic Offload Selector option, for Proxy Mobile IPv6.  This option can be used by the local mobility anchor and the mobile access gateway for negotiating IPv4 traffic offload policy for a mobility session.  Based on the negotiated IPv4 traffic offload policy, a mobile access gateway can selectively offload some of the IPv4 traffic flows in the access network instead of tunneling back to the local mobility anchor in the home network.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamegX. Zhou¢kaffiliationnRenesas MobilednamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehG. Feige¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiR. Koodl