tedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128744hkeywordsÉxPrefix origin validationpRouting securitylBGP securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQLëgranking˚crfcd6907irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrUse Cases and Interpretations of Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Objects for Issuers and Relying PartiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty÷This specification defines the 'profile' link relation type that allows resource representations to indicate that they are following one or more profiles.  A profile is defined not to alter the semantics of the resource representation itself, but to allow clients to learn about additional semantics (constraints, conventions, extensions) that are associated with the resource representation, in addition to those defined by the media type and possibly other mechanisms.fadNamekBarry LeibagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehE. Wildeddatej’hfilenamegrfc6906eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128743hkeywordsÇsapplication profilerprofile identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQJ§granking˙crfcd6906irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex The 'profile' Link Relation TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOperations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) is a general term used to identify functions and toolsets to troubleshoot and monitor networks.  This document presents OAM requirements applicable to the Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL).fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoT. Senevirathne¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamegD. Bond¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiS. Aldrin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. WatveddatejŒ#hfilenamegrfc6905eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128742hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQVgranking˘crfcd6905irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzRequirements for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) in Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]The Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) provides authentication, but not encryption, of the headers of Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) packets. However, RTP header extensions may carry sensitive information for which participants in multimedia sessions want confidentiality. This document provides a mechanism, extending the mechanisms of SRTP, to selectively encrypt RTP header extensions in SRTP.

This document updates RFC 3711, the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol specification, to require that all future SRTP encryption transforms specify how RTP header extensions are to be encrypted.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiJ. Lennoxddatej’%hfilenamegrfc6904eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128741hkeywordsÑxreal-time transport protocolcrtpqheader extensionshsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQp&Ågranking¯crfcd6904irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQEncryption of Header Extensions in the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxèThis specification defines a number of additional link relation types that can used for a range of purposes in a variety of applications types.fadNamekBarry LeibagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Snellddatej’'hfilenamegrfc6903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128740hkeywordsÉdhttpdlinkcrelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQJ§granking˜crfcd6903irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexAdditional Link Relation TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJSON Patch defines a JSON document structure for expressing a sequence of operations to apply to a JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) document; it is suitable for use with the HTTP PATCH method.  The "application/json-patch+json" media type is used to identify such patch documents.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnSalesforce.comdnamehP. Bryan¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamemM. NottinghamddatejŒ hfilenamegrfc6902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128739hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ[y·grankingˆcrfcd6902irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) PatchdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxzJSON Pointer defines a string syntax for identifying a specific value within a JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) document.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnSalesforce.comdnamehP. Bryan¢kaffiliationmSitePen (USA)dnamefK. Zyp¢kaffiliationfAkamaidnamemM. NottinghamddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6901eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128738hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ[yﬂgrankingıcrfcd6901irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) PointerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨The Link Management Protocol (LMP) is used to coordinate the properties, use, and faults of data links in networks controlled by Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS). This document defines an extension to LMP to negotiate capabilities and indicate support for LMP extensions. The defined extension is compatible with non-supporting implementations.

This document updates RFC 4204, RFC 4207, RFC 4209, and RFC 5818.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Bergerddatej’$hfilenamegrfc6898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128737hkeywordsÅcLMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQVgrankingÚcrfcd6898irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMLink Management Protocol Behavior Negotiation and Configuration ModificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôMultipath TCP (MPTCP) adds the capability of using multiple paths to a regular TCP session.  Even though it is designed to be totally backward compatible to applications, the data transport differs compared to regular TCP, and there are several additional degrees of freedom that applications may wish to exploit.  This document summarizes the impact that MPTCP may have on applications, such as changes in performance.  Furthermore, it discusses compatibility issues of MPTCP in combination with non-MPTCP-aware applications.  Finally, the document describes a basic application interface that is a simple extension of TCP's interface for MPTCP-aware applications.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxAlcatel-Lucent Bell LabsdnameiM. Scharf¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegA. FordddatejŒ"hfilenamegrfc6897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128736hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQSê»grankingÒcrfcd6897irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Multipath TCP (MPTCP) Application Interface ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty”This memo defines a generic URI and HTTP-header-friendly envelope for carrying symmetrically encrypted, authenticated, and origin-timestamped tokens. It also describes one possible usage of such tokens via a simple protocol based on HTTP cookies.

Secure Cookie Session (SCS) use cases cover a wide spectrum of applications, ranging from distribution of authorized content via HTTP (e.g., with out-of-band signed URIs) to securing browser sessions with diskless embedded devices (e.g., Small Office, Home Office (SOHO) routers) or web servers with high availability or load- balancing requirements that may want to delegate the handling of the application state to clients instead of using shared storage or forced peering.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiKoanLogicdnamejS. Barbato¢kaffiliationiKoanLogicdnamelS. Dorigotti¢kaffiliationiKoanLogicdnamejT. Fossatiddatej’hfilenamegrfc6896eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128735hkeywordsÅsHTTP Secure CookieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQBgrankingcrfcd6896irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0SCS: KoanLogic's Secure Cookie Sessions for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty]This document specifies Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) parameter assignment considerations for the allocation of Domain Name System (DNS) resource record types, CLASSes, operation codes, error codes, DNS protocol message header bits, and AFSDB resource record subtypes.  It obsoletes RFC 6195 and updates RFCs 1183, 2845, 2930, and 3597.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej”≤hfilenamegrfc6895eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128734hkeywordsÉfRRTYPEeRCODEeAFSDBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQm·÷grankingÔcrfcd6895irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber*etotaletitlex,Domain Name System (DNS) IANA ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis document describes the methodology for benchmarking MPLS Fast Reroute (FRR) protection mechanisms for link and node protection.  This document provides test methodologies and testbed setup for measuring failover times of Fast Reroute techniques while considering factors (such as underlying links) that might impact recovery times for real-time applications bound to MPLS Traffic Engineered (MPLS-TE) tunnels.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejR. Papneja¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. Vapiwala¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Karthik¢kaffiliationtAllot CommunicationsdnamekS. Poretsky¢kaffiliationtQwest CommunicationsdnamefS. Rao¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le RouxddatejÕkhfilenamegrfc6894eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128733hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateQBgrankingÓcrfcd6894irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVMethodology for Benchmarking MPLS Traffic Engineered (MPLS-TE) Fast Reroute ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Open IPTV Forum (OIPF) for naming persistent resources defined within OIPF specifications.  Example resources include technical documents and specifications, eXtensible Markup Language (XML) schemas, classification schemes, XML Document Type Definitions (DTDs), namespaces, style sheets, media assets, and other types of resources produced or managed by the OIPF.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtOpen IPTV Forum e.V.dnamehP. Higgs¢kaffiliationtOpen IPTV Forum e.V.dnamehP. SzucsddatejŒ"hfilenamegrfc6893e