This document updates the Extension Mechanisms for DNS (EDNS(0)) specification (and obsoletes RFC 2671) based on feedback from deployment experience in several implementations. It also obsoletes RFC 2673 ("Binary Labels in the Domain Name System") and adds considerations on the use of extended labels in the DNS.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuBond Internet SystemsdnamehJ. Damas¢kaffiliation`dnamehM. Graff¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamehP. Vixieddatej’&hfilenamegrfc6891eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128730hkeywordsÑnDNS extensionsrdomain name systempresource recordscoptkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQm·’grankingÎcrfcd6891irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberKetotaletitlex&Extension Mechanisms for DNS (EDNS(0))dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyZThis memo reiterates the assignment of an IPv4 address block (192.0.0.0/24) to IANA.  It also instructs IANA to restructure its IPv4 and IPv6 Special-Purpose Address Registries.  Upon restructuring, the aforementioned registries will record all special-purpose address blocks, maintaining a common set of information regarding each address block.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx;Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN)dnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationx;Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN)dnameiL. Vegoda¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationx2Johns Hopkins University (JHU) Applied Physics LabdnamekB. Habermanddatej’&hfilenamegrfc6890eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128729hkeywordsÇqInternet Protocolqspace assignmentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ~“®grankingÍcrfcd6890irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberôetotaletitlex%Special-Purpose IP Address RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8190∂habstractyØDue to specific problems, Network Address Translation - Protocol Translation (NAT-PT) was deprecated by the IETF as a mechanism to perform IPv6-IPv4 translation.  Since then, new efforts have been undertaken within IETF to standardize alternative mechanisms to perform IPv6-IPv4 translation.  This document analyzes to what extent the new stateful translation mechanisms avoid the problems that caused the IETF to deprecate NAT-PT.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiT. Saxena¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumarddatej’hfilenamegrfc6889eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128728hkeywordsáeNAT64eDNS64fNAT-PTxALG (Application Layer Gateway)mNAT traversalxIPv4-IPv6 interconnectionuIPv4-IPv6 translationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ~ˆ·grankingÈcrfcd6889irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Analysis of Stateful 64 TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx^This document defines common requirements for Carrier-Grade NATs (CGNs).  It updates RFC 4787.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamelS. Perreault¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnamekI. Yamagata¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnamekS. Miyakawa¢kaffiliationx(Japan Internet Exchange Co., Ltd. (JPIX)dnamekA. Nakagawa¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiH. Ashidaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6888eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128727hkeywordsÇcCGNcNATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ~ˆ·grankingËcrfcd6888irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex1Common Requirements for Carrier-Grade NATs (CGNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Port Control Protocol allows an IPv6 or IPv4 host to control how incoming IPv6 or IPv4 packets are translated and forwarded by a Network Address Translator (NAT) or simple firewall, and also allows a host to optimize its outgoing NAT keepalive messages.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationeApplednamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejP. Selkirkddatej’hfilenamegrfc6887eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcpdfullxpcp - Port Control ProtocoldnameuPort Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128726hkeywordsÇcNAThFirewallkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateQ~ˆ‡grankingÁcrfcd6887irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPort Control Protocol (PCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7488d7843d7652¥habstractyÀThis document describes a protocol for automating the process of creating Network Address Translation (NAT) port mappings.  Included in the protocol is a method for retrieving the external IPv4 address of a NAT gateway, thus allowing a client to make its external IPv4 address and port known to peers that may wish to communicate with it.  From 2005 onwards, this protocol was implemented in Apple products including Mac OS X, Bonjour for Windows, and AirPort wireless base stations.  In 2013, NAT Port Mapping Protocol (NAT-PMP) was superseded by the IETF Standards Track RFC "Port Control Protocol (PCP)", which builds on NAT-PMP and uses a compatible packet format, but adds a number of significant enhancements.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekM. KrochmalddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6886eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128725hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateQ~ˆ‡grankingÊcrfcd6886irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex#NAT Port Mapping Protocol (NAT-PMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóIf a protocol expects to compare two strings and is prepared only for those strings to be ASCII, then using Unicode code points in those strings requires they be prepared somehow.  Internationalizing Domain Names in Applications (here called IDNA2003) defined and used Stringprep and Nameprep.  Other protocols subsequently defined Stringprep profiles.  A new approach different from Stringprep and Nameprep is used for a revision of IDNA2003 (called IDNA2008).  Other Stringprep profiles need to be similarly updated, or a replacement of Stringprep needs to be designed.  This document outlines the issues to be faced by those designing a Stringprep replacement.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchet¢kaffiliationiDyn, Inc.dnamekA. SullivanddatejŒ hfilenamegrfc6885eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfprecisdfullx@precis - Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdnamex7Preparation and Comparison of Internationalized Stringsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128724hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateQB))grankingÂcrfcd6885irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dna