The VPNs may support a number of customer services, including RSVP and Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) traffic. This document describes how to support RSVP-TE between customer sites when a single PE supports multiple VPNs and labels are not used to identify VPNs between PEs.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiK. Kumaki¢kaffiliationx Furukawa Network Solutions Corp.dnamehT. Murai¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehD. Cheng¢kaffiliationpSoftbank TelecomdnamemS. Matsushima¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnamehP. JiangddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6882eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128721hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ942granking‚crfcd6882irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextSupport for Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) in Layer 3 Virtual Private Networks (L3VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty&The IETF and other standards organizations have efforts targeted at standardizing various aspects of placing emergency calls on IP networks.  This memo describes best current practice on how devices, networks, and services using IETF protocols should use such standards to make emergency calls.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehB. Rosen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polkddatej‘˜hfilenamegrfc6881eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128720hkeywordsàcSIPiemergencyoemergency callsnemergency callqemergency callinge9-1-1e1-1-2eecritk