ationDateQ[yﬁgranking›crfcd6877irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:464XLAT: Combination of Stateful and Stateless TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÈThis document specifies PT-TLS, a TLS-based Posture Transport (PT) protocol.  The PT-TLS protocol carries the Network Endpoint Assessment (NEA) message exchange under the protection of a Transport Layer Security (TLS) secured tunnel.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtSymantec CorporationdnamekP. Sangster¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Saloweyddatej‘∑hfilenamegrfc6876eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcneadfullx!nea - Network Endpoint AssessmentdnamexNetwork Endpoint Assessmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-128715hkeywordsÇxNetwork Endpoint AssessmentcNEAkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateQ.`egranking‹crfcd6876irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A Posture Transport Protocol over TLS (PT-TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes experiments that clarify how an approach similar to Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) was effective in reducing network traffic.  These experiments were performed in Japan by the P2P Network Experiment Council in an attempt to harmonize peer-to-peer (P2P) technology with network infrastructure.  Based on what was learned from these experiments, this document provides some suggestions that might be useful for the ALTO architecture and especially for application-independent ALTO- like server operation.fadNameqGonzalo CamarillogauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamehS. Kamei¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiT. Momose¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamehT. Inoue¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamelT. Nishitaniddatej’#hfilenamegrfc6875eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128714hkeywordsÖooverlay networkxcontent delivery networklpeer-to-peerstraffic engineeringtexperiments in JapankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ'÷Õgranking€crfcd6875irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex{The P2P Network Experiment Council's Activities and Experiments with Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) in JapandtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document describes how the zone identifier of an IPv6 scoped address, defined as <zone_id> in the IPv6 Scoped Address Architecture (RFC 4007), can be represented in a literal IPv6 address and in a Uniform Resource Identifier that includes such a literal address.  It updates the URI Generic Syntax specification (RFC 3986) accordingly.fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnameiR. HindenddatejÕÃhfilenamegrfc6874eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128713hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9844epages
opublicationDateQ'÷Õgranking⁄crfcd6874irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWRepresenting IPv6 Zone Identifiers in Address Literals and Uniform Resource IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~The SIPCLF working group has defined a Common Log Format (CLF) framework for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) servers.  This CLF mimics the successful event logging format found in well-known web servers like Apache and web proxies like Squid.  This document proposes an indexed text encoding format for the SIP CLF that retains the key advantages of a text-based format while significantly increasing processing performance over a purely text-based implementation.  This file format adheres to the SIP CLF information model and provides an effective encoding scheme for all mandatory and optional fields that appear in a SIP CLF record.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationxBell Labs / Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamekA. B. Roachddatej’$hfilenamegrfc6873eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsipclfdfullxsipclf - SIP Common Log FormatdnameuSIP Common Log Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-128712hkeywordsÅfSIPCLFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ`grankingŸcrfcd6873irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHFormat for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Common Log Format (CLF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7355∂habstractyÍWell-known web servers such as Apache and web proxies like Squid support event logging using a common log format.  The logs produced using these de facto standard formats are invaluable to system administrators for troubleshooting a server and tool writers to craft tools that mine the log files and produce reports and trends.  Furthermore, these log files can also be used to train anomaly detection systems and feed events into a security event management system.  The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) does not have a common log format, and, as a result, each server supports a distinct log format that makes it unnecessarily complex to produce tools to do trend analysis and security detection.  This document describes a framework, including requirements and analysis of existing approaches, and specifies an information model for development of a SIP common log file format that can be used uniformly by user agents, proxies, registrars, and redirect servers as well as back-to-back user agents.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx"Bell Laboratories / Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationuGeorgetown UniversitydnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationx Illinois Institute of TechnologydnameiT. Anjali¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamekH. Abdelnur¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnameiO. Festorddatej’$hfilenamegrfc6872eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsipclfdfullxsipclf - SIP Common Log FormatdnameuSIP Common Log Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-128711hkeywordsÉgloggingianalyticsqinformation modelkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateQ`grankingÿcrfcd6872irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfThe Common Log Format (CLF) for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP): Framework and Information ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂Session Description Protocol (SDP) capability negotiation provides a general framework for indicating and negotiating capabilities in SDP. The base framework defines only capabilities for negotiating transport protocols and attributes. This documents extends the framework by defining media capabilities that can be used to negotiate media types and their associated parameters.

This document updates the IANA Considerations of RFC 5939.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiR. Gilman¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6871eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128710hkeywordsÉtSession CapabilitiesuLatent ConfigurationswMedia Format CapabilitykobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateQ.`dgranking◊crfcd6871irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASession Description Protocol (SDP) Media Capabilities NegotiationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]This document describes a mechanism for signaling the active and standby status of redundant Pseudowires (PWs) between their termination points. A set of Redundant PWs is configured between Provider Edge (PE) nodes in single-segment pseudowire (SS-PW) applications or between Terminating Provider Edge (T-PE) nodes in Multi-Segment Pseudowire (MS-PW) applications.

In order for the PE/T-PE nodes to indicate the preferred PW to use for forwarding PW packets to one another, a new status bit is defined. This bit indicates a Preferential Forwarding status with a value of active or standby for each PW in a redundant set.

In addition, a second status bit is defined to allow peer PE nodes to coordinate a switchover operation of the PW.

Finally, this document updates RFC 4447 by adding details to the handling of the PW status code bits in the PW Status TLV.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehP. Muley¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekM. Aissaouiddatej’$hfilenamegrfc6870eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128709hkeywordsÜmPW redundancyiactive PWjstandby PWjprimary PWlsecondary PWmPW precedencekobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateQ%Hœgranking÷crfcd6870irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Pseudowire Preferential Forwarding Status BitdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7771∂habstractyThis document defines a value of "device" for the vCard KIND property so that the vCard format can be used to represent computing devices such as appliances, computers, or network elements (e.g., a server, router, switch, printer, sensor, or phone). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. Salgueiro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiJ. Clarke¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej’&hfilenamegrfc6869eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128708hkeywordsÅevCardkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateQ‡granking’crfcd6869irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqvCard KIND:devicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxªThis specification updates the data formats for iCalendar (RFC 5545) and vCard (RFC 6350) to allow parameter values to include certain characters forbidden by the existing specifications.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Dabooddatej’%hfilenamegrfc6868eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128707hkeywordsÇhcalendargcontactkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ`␀granking‘crfcd6868irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Parameter Value Encoding in iCalendar and vCarddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document updates the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) described in RFC 5996.  This extension allows an IKE Security Association (SA) to be created and authenticated using the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Re-authentication Protocol extension, as described in RFC 6696.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. WuddatejÕVhfilenamegrfc6867eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128706hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateQágranking”crfcd6867irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmAn Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Extension to Support EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxõThis document analyses the problems of updating the IPv6 addresses of hosts in enterprise networks that, for operational reasons, require static addresses.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. JiangddatejÕkhfilenamegrfc6866eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymf6renumdfullx6renum - IPv6 Site RenumberingdnameuIPv6 Site Renumberingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128705hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ.`dgranking“crfcd6866irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYProblem Statement for Renumbering IPv6 Hosts with Static Addresses in Enterprise NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document describes a fully-specified simple Forward Error Correction (FEC) scheme for Reed-Solomon codes over the finite field (also known as the Galois Field) GF(2^^m), with 2 <= m <= 16, that can be used to protect arbitrary media streams along the lines defined by FECFRAME.  The Reed-Solomon codes considered have attractive properties, since they offer optimal protection against packet erasures and the source symbols are part of the encoding symbols, which can greatly simplify decoding.  However, the price to pay is a limit on the maximum source block size, on the maximum number of encoding symbols, and a computational complexity higher than that of the Low-Density Parity Check (LDPC) codes, for instance.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationoINSA-Lyon/INRIAdnameiM. Cunche¢kaffiliationvISAE/Univ. of ToulousednamehJ. Lacan¢kaffiliationdCDTAdnamenA. Bouabdallah¢kaffiliationoKeio UniversitydnamelK. Matsuzonoddatej’%hfilenamegrfc6865eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128704hkeywordsÇxForward Error CorrectionlReed-SolomonkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ.`cgranking—crfcd6865irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFSimple Reed-Solomon Forward Error Correction (FEC) Scheme for FECFRAMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*The IPv4 Identification (ID) field enables fragmentation and reassembly and, as currently specified, is required to be unique within the maximum lifetime for all datagrams with a given source address/destination address/protocol tuple.  If enforced, this uniqueness requirement would limit all connections to 6.4 Mbps for typical datagram sizes.  Because individual connections commonly exceed this speed, it is clear that existing systems violate the current specification.  This document updates the specification of the IPv4 ID field in RFCs 791, 1122, and 2003 to more closely reflect current practice and to more closely match IPv6 so that the field's value is defined only when a datagram is actually fragmented.  It also discusses the impact of these changes on how datagrams are used. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. TouchddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6864eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128703hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ›granking–crfcd6864irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Updated Specification of the IPv4 ID FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document analyzes OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 according to the guidelines set forth in Section 4.2 of the "Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design Guidelines" (RFC 6518).  Key components of solutions to gaps identified in this document are already underway.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. Ltd.dnamehD. ZhangddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6863eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdkarpdfullx6karp - Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdnamex/Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128702hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ1Aêgrankingœcrfcd6863irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnAnalysis of OSPF Security According to the Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design GuidedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBDifferent routing protocols employ different mechanisms for securing protocol packets on the wire.  While most already have some method for accomplishing cryptographic message authentication, in many cases the existing methods are dated, vulnerable to attack, and employ cryptographic algorithms that have been deprecated.  The "Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols" (KARP) effort aims to overhaul and improve these mechanisms.  This document does not contain protocol specifications.  Instead, it defines the areas where protocol specification work is needed.  This document is a companion document to RFC 6518, "Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design Guidelines"; together they form the guidance and instruction KARP design teams will use to review and overhaul routing protocol transport security.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamekG. Lebovitz¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. WeisddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6862eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdkarpdfullx6karp - Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdnamex/Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128701hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ1AègrankingŒcrfcd6862irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZKeying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Overview, Threats, and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractynRFC 5988 standardized a means of indicating the relationships between resources on the Web.  This specification defines link relation types that may be used to express the relationships between a resource and an input form for constructing data submissions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameoI. DzmanashviliddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6861eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128700hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP˘grankingÕcrfcd6861irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0The "create-form" and "edit-form" Link RelationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚA transit-only network is defined as a network connecting routers only. In OSPF, transit-only networks are usually configured with routable IP addresses, which are advertised in Link State Advertisements (LSAs) but are not needed for data traffic. In addition, remote attacks can be launched against routers by sending packets to these transit-only networks. This document presents a mechanism to hide transit-only networks to speed up network convergence and reduce vulnerability to remote attacks.

In the context of this document, 'hiding' implies that the prefixes are not installed in the routing tables on OSPF routers. In some cases, IP addresses may still be visible when using OSPFv2.

This document updates RFCs 2328 and 5340. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Retana¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefA. RoyddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6860eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128699hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP˘grankingÃcrfcd6860irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Hiding Transit-Only Networks in OSPFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'RFC 3777 specifies that "sitting members" of the IAB and IESG "may not volunteer to serve on the nominating committee".  Since the time that document was written, the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) was formed; that body is not covered by RFC 3777.  There is also ambiguity in RFC 3777 about whether ex officio members and liaisons are included as "sitting members".  This document updates RFC 3777 to clarify the rules as they apply to members of the IAB, the IESG, and the IAOC.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej’#hfilenamegrfc6859eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128698hkeywordsÇfnomcomdIAOCkobsoletedByÅd7437epagesopublicationDatePı2grankingÀcrfcd6859irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQUpdate to RFC 3777 to Clarify Nominating Committee Eligibility of IETF LeadershipdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxŒThis document specifies a method for IMAP and POP servers to serve internationalized messages to conventional clients.  The specification is simple, easy to implement, and provides only rudimentary results.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenA. GulbrandsenddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6858eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128697hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ?°œgranking crfcd6858irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Simplified POP and IMAP Downgrading for Internationalized EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<The Email Address Internationalization (SMTPUTF8) extension to SMTP allows Unicode characters encoded in UTF-8 and outside the ASCII repertoire in mail header fields.  Upgraded POP and IMAP servers support internationalized messages.  If a POP or IMAP client does not support Email Address Internationalization, a POP or IMAP server cannot deliver internationalized messages to the client and cannot remove the message.  To avoid that situation, this document describes a mechanism for converting internationalized messages into the traditional message format.  As part of the conversion process, message elements that require internationalized treatment are recoded or removed, and receivers are able to recognize that they received messages containing such elements, even if they cannot process the internationalized elements.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwaraddatej’ hfilenamegrfc6857eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128696hkeywordsÑcEAIx"Email Address InternationalizationiDowngradedMAILkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ?°œgranking…crfcd6857irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFPost-Delivery Message Downgrading for Internationalized Email MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx—This specification extends the Post Office Protocol version 3 (POP3) to support international strings encoded in UTF-8 in usernames, passwords, mail addresses, message headers, and protocol-level text strings.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuQUALCOMM IncorporateddnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Newman¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Yao¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwaraddatej’!hfilenamegrfc6856eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128695hkeywordsÅqinternationalizedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ?°Œgranking»crfcd6856irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Post Office Protocol Version 3 (POP3) Support for UTF-8dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx”This specification extends the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) to support UTF-8 encoded international characters in user names, mail addresses, and message headers.  This specification replaces RFC 5738.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Newman¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegS. Shenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6855eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128694hkeywordsÇdIMAPdIDNAkobsoletedByÅd9755epagesopublicationDateQ?°Œgranking«crfcd6855irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIMAP Support for UTF-8dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThe Internet Message Format (RFC 5322) allows "group" syntax in some email header fields, such as "To:" and "CC:", but not in "From:" or "Sender:".  This document updates RFC 5322 to relax that restriction, allowing group syntax in those latter fields, as well as in "Resent-From:" and "Resent-Sender:", in certain situations.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej hfilenamegrfc6854eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128693hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateQ?°Õgranking∆crfcd6854irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbUpdate to Internet Message Format to Allow Group Syntax in the "From:" and "Sender:" Header FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxµThis document provides information for those wishing to use DHCPv6 to support their deployment of IPv6.  In particular, it discusses the provision of semi-redundant DHCPv6 services.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxComcast Cable CommunicationsdnamemJ. Brzozowski¢kaffiliationnVideotron G.P.dnamekJ. Tremblay¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednamegJ. Chen¢kaffiliationcISCdnamelT. Mrugalskiddatej’#hfilenamegrfc6853eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128692hkeywordsÉfDHCPv6jRedundancyxDeployment ConsiderationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ%HŒgranking≈crfcd6853irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber¥etotaletitlex+DHCPv6 Redundancy Deployment ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty˚On 29 August 2012, the leaders of the IEEE Standards Association, the IAB, the IETF, the Internet Society, and the W3C signed a statement affirming the importance of a jointly developed set of principles establishing a modern paradigm for global, open standards.  These principles have become known as the "OpenStand" principles.  This document contains the text of the affirmation that was signed.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdIETFdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationgIEEE-SAdnamehS. Mills¢kaffiliationcW3CdnamehJ. Jaffe¢kaffiliationcIABdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationdISOCdnamekL. St.AmourddatejÀWhfilenamegrfc6852eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128691hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQí|grankingƒcrfcd6852irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex0Affirmation of the Modern Paradigm for StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx®This document defines an IMAP extension consisting of two new commands, MOVE and UID MOVE, that are used to move messages from one mailbox to another. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamenA. Gulbrandsen¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehN. Freedddatej’"hfilenamegrfc6851eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhimapmovedfullximapmove - IMAP MOVE extensiondnamesIMAP MOVE extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-128690hkeywordsÅdIMAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQágranking√crfcd6851irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - MOVE ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols.  In particular, it defines objects for managing a Routing Bridge (RBridge), also known as a TRILL Switch, based on the IETF TRILL (Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnameoA. Rijhsinghani¢kaffiliationmH.W. EmbeddeddnamejK. ZebroseddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6850eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128689hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateQágranking¬crfcd6850irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Definitions of Managed Objects for Routing Bridges (RBridges)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸThe wide deployment of Voice over IP (VoIP), real-time text, and Video over IP services has introduced new challenges in managing and maintaining real-time voice/text/video quality, reliability, and overall performance.  In particular, media delivery is an area that needs attention.  One method of meeting these challenges is monitoring the media delivery performance by looping media back to the transmitter.  This is typically referred to as "active monitoring" of services.  Media loopback is especially popular in ensuring the quality of transport to the edge of a given VoIP, real-time text, or Video over IP service.  Today, in networks that deliver real-time media, short of running 'ping' and 'traceroute' to the edge, administrators are left without the necessary tools to actively monitor, manage, and diagnose quality issues with their service.  The extension defined herein adds new Session Description Protocol (SDP) media types and attributes that enable establishment of media sessions where the media is looped back to the transmitter.  Such media sessions will serve as monitoring and troubleshooting tools by providing the means for measurement of more advanced VoIP, real-time text, and Video over IP performance metrics.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkAcme PacketdnameiH. Kaplan¢kaffiliationdEXFOdnamejK. Hedayat¢kaffiliationgSaperixdnamehN. Venna¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationoBlinkMind, Inc.dnamekN. Strattonddatej’%hfilenamegrfc6849eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128688hkeywordsÜjmultimediaeaudioevideodRTCPjdiagnosticdvoipkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateQ'÷Ãgranking¡crfcd6849irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpAn Extension to the Session Description Protocol (SDP) and Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) for Media LoopbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÛNew fields are occasionally added to civic addresses.  A backward- compatible mechanism for adding civic address elements to the Geopriv civic address format is described.  A formal mechanism for handling unsupported extensions when translating between XML and DHCP civic address forms is defined for entities that need to perform this translation.  Initial extensions for some new elements are also defined.  The Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) protocol mechanism (defined in RFC 5222) that returns civic address element names used for validation of location information is clarified and is normatively updated to require a qualifying namespace identifier on each civic address element returned as part of the validation process. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiCommScopednameoJ. Winterbottom¢kaffiliationeSkypednamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamehB. Rosen¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiR. Georgeddatej’"hfilenamegrfc6848eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128687hkeywordsÖiExtensioneLocaleCivichLocationgGEOPRIVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePı2granking¿crfcd6848irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeSpecifying Civic Address Extensions in the Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyOFibre Channel over Ethernet (FCoE) and Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) are two emerging standards in the data center environment.  While these two protocols are seemingly unrelated, they have a very similar behavior in the forwarding plane, as both perform hop-by-hop forwarding over Ethernet, modifying the packet's Media Access Control (MAC) addresses at each hop.  This document describes an architecture for the integrated deployment of these two protocols.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnameiD. Melman¢kaffiliationgMarvelldnamejT. Mizrahi¢kaffiliationnHuawei USA R&DdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej‘˙hfilenamegrfc6847eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128686hkeywordsÑdFCoEdFCRBeTRILLgRBridgekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÔÃÍgrankingøcrfcd6847irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex\Fibre Channel over Ethernet (FCoE) over Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document specifies a RObust Header Compression (ROHC) profile for compression of TCP/IP packets. The profile, called ROHC-TCP, provides efficient and robust compression of TCP headers, including frequently used TCP options such as selective acknowledgments (SACKs) and Timestamps.

ROHC-TCP works well when used over links with significant error rates and long round-trip times. For many bandwidth-limited links where header compression is essential, such characteristics are common.

This specification obsoletes RFC 4996. It fixes a technical issue with the SACK compression and clarifies other compression methods used. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxInterDigital CommunicationsdnamelG. Pelletier¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekK. Sandlund¢kaffiliation`dnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliationrSiemens/Roke ManordnamegM. WestddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6846eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128685hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages`opublicationDateQí{grankingæcrfcd6846irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexARObust Header Compression (ROHC): A Profile for TCP/IP (ROHC-TCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document describes a mechanism to model a broadcast network as a hybrid of broadcast and point-to-multipoint networks for purposes of OSPF operation. Neighbor discovery and maintenance as well as Link State Advertisement (LSA) database synchronization are performed using the broadcast model, but the network is represented using the point-to-multipoint model in the router-LSAs of the routers connected to it. This allows an accurate representation of the cost of communication between different routers on the network, while maintaining the network efficiency of broadcast operation. This approach is relatively simple and requires minimal changes to OSPF.

This document updates both OSPFv2 (RFC 2328) and OSPFv3 (RFC 5340). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpContrail SystemsdnamehN. Sheth¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Zhangddatej’"hfilenamegrfc6845eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128684hkeywordsÑdOSPFdP2MPiBroadcastiInterfacekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatePı1grankingΩcrfcd6845irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<OSPF Hybrid Broadcast and Point-to-Multipoint Interface TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThe Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) DNS Resource Record allows a DNS domain name holder to specify one or more Certification Authorities (CAs) authorized to issue certificates for that domain.  CAA Resource Records allow a public Certification Authority to implement additional controls to reduce the risk of unintended certificate mis-issue.  This document defines the syntax of the CAA record and rules for processing CAA records by certificate issuers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrComodo Group, Inc.dnameoP. Hallam-Baker¢kaffiliationoComodo CA, Ltd.dnamelR. Stradlingddatej’!hfilenamegrfc6844eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-128683hkeywordsÉcDNSfDNSSECdPKIXkobsoletedByÅd8659epagesopublicationDateQí{grankingºcrfcd6844irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?DNS Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) Resource RecorddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx”This document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) block that allows the reporting of delay metrics for use in a range of Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationlAVA NetworksdnamehK. Gross¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej’hfilenamegrfc6843eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128682hkeywordsÇpRound Trip DelaypEnd System DelaykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatePı1grankingªcrfcd6843irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Report (XR) Block for Delay Metric ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxËThis document updates RFC 2131 "Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol" by addressing the issues arising from that document's specification that the server MUST NOT return the 'client identifier' option to the client. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmSamsung IndiadnamehN. Swamy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekG. Halwasia¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekP. JhingranddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6842eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128681hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÎ◊Úgranking∫crfcd6842irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Client Identifier Option in DHCP Server RepliesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document presents a framework to assist writers of DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) Policies and DNSSEC Practice Statements, such as domain managers and zone operators on both the top level and secondary level, who are managing and operating a DNS zone with Security Extensions implemented.

In particular, the framework provides a comprehensive list of topics that should be considered for inclusion into a DNSSEC Policy definition and Practice Statement. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhKirei ABdnamelF. Ljunggren¢kaffiliationc.SEdnamesAM. Eklund Lowinder¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamehT. Okuboddatej‘Áhfilenamegrfc6841eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128680hkeywordsÑcDNSfDNSSECbDPcDPSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÔÃÍgrankingπcrfcd6841irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A Framework for DNSSEC Policies and DNSSEC Practice StatementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis document is a collection of technical clarifications to the DNS Security (DNSSEC) document set. It is meant to serve as a resource to implementors as well as a collection of DNSSEC errata that existed at the time of writing.

This document updates the core DNSSEC documents (RFC 4033, RFC 4034, and RFC 4035) as well as the NSEC3 specification (RFC 5155). It also defines NSEC3 and SHA-2 (RFC 4509 and RFC 5702) as core parts of the DNSSEC specification.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnameiD. Blackaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6840eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128679hkeywordsÉcEAPcAAAireconnectkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ&bgranking∏crfcd6840irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAClarifications and Implementation Notes for DNS Security (DNSSEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8749∂habstractyuA content media type name sometimes includes partitioned meta- information distinguished by a structured syntax to permit noting an attribute of the media as a suffix to the name.  This document defines several structured syntax suffixes for use with media type registrations.  In particular, it defines and registers the "+json", "+ber", "+der", "+fastinfoset", "+wbxml" and "+zip" structured syntax suffixes, and provides a media type structured syntax suffix registration form for the "+xml" structured syntax suffix.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej’ hfilenamegrfc6839eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128678hkeywordsÇxstructured syntax suffixjmedia typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÿÖgranking∑crfcd6839irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Additional Media Type Structured Syntax SuffixesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7303∑habstractx√This document defines procedures for the specification and registration of media types for use in HTTP, MIME, and other Internet protocols.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehN. Freed¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. HansenddatejÕhfilenamegrfc6838eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128677hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateQÿÖgranking∂crfcd6838irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex5Media Type Specifications and Registration ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9694µhabstractyIThe Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) is a protocol to encapsulate IP packets in order to allow end sites to route to one another without injecting routes from one end of the Internet to another.  This memo presents an experimental database and a discussion of methods to transport the mapping of Endpoint IDs (EIDs) to Routing Locators (RLOCs) to routers in a reliable, scalable, and secure manner.  Our analysis concludes that transport of all EID-to- RLOC mappings scales well to at least 10^8 entries.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamenBrian HabermangauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. LearddatejÕhfilenamegrfc6837eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128676hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQA#grankingµcrfcd6837irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexINERD: A Not-so-novel Endpoint ID (EID) to Routing Locator (RLOC) DatabasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThis document describes a simple distributed index system to be used by a Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Ingress Tunnel Router (ITR) or Map-Resolver (MR) to find the Egress Tunnel Router (ETR) that holds the mapping information for a particular Endpoint Identifier (EID).  The MR can then query that ETR to obtain the actual mapping information, which consists of a list of Routing Locators (RLOCs) for the EID.  Termed the Alternative Logical Topology (ALT), the index is built as an overlay network on the public Internet using the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) and Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE).  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiV. Fuller¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. LewisddatejÕ‚hfilenamegrfc6836eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128675hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQA#granking¥crfcd6836irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFLocator/ID Separation Protocol Alternative Logical Topology (LISP+ALT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA simple tool called the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Internet Groper or 'lig' can be used to query the LISP mapping database.  This document describes how it works.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. MeyerddatejÕîhfilenamegrfc6835eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128674hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQA#granking≥crfcd6835irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8The Locator/ID Separation Protocol Internet Groper (LIG)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†This document describes the LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol) Map-Versioning mechanism, which provides in-packet information about Endpoint ID to Routing Locator (EID-to-RLOC) mappings used to encapsulate LISP data packets.  The proposed approach is based on associating a version number to EID-to-RLOC mappings and the transport of such a version number in the LISP-specific header of LISP-encapsulated packets.  LISP Map-Versioning is particularly useful to inform communicating Ingress Tunnel Routers (ITRs) and Egress Tunnel Routers (ETRs) about modifications of the mappings used to encapsulate packets.  The mechanism is transparent to implementations not supporting this feature, since in the LISP- specific header and in the Map Records, bits used for Map-Versioning can be safely ignored by ITRs and ETRs that do not support the mechanism.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqTelecom ParisTechdnamejL. Iannone¢kaffiliationvINRIA Sophia AntipolisdnameiD. Saucez¢kaffiliationx Universite catholique de LouvaindnamenO. BonaventureddatejÕÚhfilenamegrfc6834eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128673hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9302epagesopublicationDateQA#granking≤crfcd6834irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Map-VersioningdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document describes the Mapping Service for the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP), implemented by two new types of LISP- speaking devices -- the LISP Map-Resolver and LISP Map-Server -- that provides a simplified "front end" for one or more Endpoint ID to Routing Locator mapping databases.

By using this service interface and communicating with Map-Resolvers and Map-Servers, LISP Ingress Tunnel Routers and Egress Tunnel Routers are not dependent on the details of mapping database systems, which facilitates experimentation with different database designs. Since these devices implement the "edge" of the LISP infrastructure, connect directly to LISP-capable Internet end sites, and comprise the bulk of LISP-speaking devices, reducing their implementation and operational complexity should also reduce the overall cost and effort of deploying LISP. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiV. Fuller¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. FarinacciddatejÕÛhfilenamegrfc6833eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128672hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9301epagesopublicationDateQA"granking±crfcd6833irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) Map-Server InterfacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document describes techniques for allowing sites running the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) to interoperate with Internet sites that may be using either IPv4, IPv6, or both but that are not running LISP.  A fundamental property of LISP-speaking sites is that they use Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs), rather than traditional IP addresses, in the source and destination fields of all traffic they emit or receive.  While EIDs are syntactically identical to IPv4 or IPv6 addresses, normally routes to them are not carried in the global routing system, so an interoperability mechanism is needed for non- LISP-speaking sites to exchange traffic with LISP-speaking sites.  This document introduces three such mechanisms.  The first uses a new network element, the LISP Proxy Ingress Tunnel Router (Proxy-ITR), to act as an intermediate LISP Ingress Tunnel Router (ITR) for non-LISP- speaking hosts.  Second, this document adds Network Address Translation (NAT) functionality to LISP ITRs and LISP Egress Tunnel Routers (ETRs) to substitute routable IP addresses for non-routable EIDs.  Finally, this document introduces the Proxy Egress Tunnel Router (Proxy-ETR) to handle cases where a LISP ITR cannot send packets to non-LISP sites without encapsulation.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Lewis¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiV. FullerddatejÕÛhfilenamegrfc6832eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128671hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQA"granking∞crfcd6832irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMInterworking between Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) and Non-LISP SitesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how inter-domain multicast routing will function in an environment where Locator/ID Separation is deployed using the Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) architecture.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Zwiebel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. VenaasddatejÕÎhfilenamegrfc6831eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128670hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQA"grankingØcrfcd6831irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDThe Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) for Multicast EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThis document describes a network-layer-based protocol that enables separation of IP addresses into two new numbering spaces: Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs) and Routing Locators (RLOCs). No changes are required to either host protocol stacks or to the "core" of the Internet infrastructure. The Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) can be incrementally deployed, without a "flag day", and offers Traffic Engineering, multihoming, and mobility benefits to early adopters, even when there are relatively few LISP-capable sites.

Design and development of LISP was largely motivated by the problem statement produced by the October 2006 IAB Routing and Addressing Workshop. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliation`dnameiV. Fuller¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. LewisddatejÕÏhfilenamegrfc6830eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdlispdfullx%lisp - Locator/ID Separation ProtocoldnamexLocator/ID Separation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128669hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd9300d9301epagesKopublicationDateQAgrankingÆcrfcd6830irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8113∂habstractyThe Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping and traceroute mechanisms are commonly used to detect and isolate data-plane failures in all MPLS LSPs, including LSPs used for each direction of an MPLS Pseudowire (PW). However, the LSP Ping and traceroute elements used for PWs are not specified for IPv6 address usage.

This document extends the PW LSP Ping and traceroute mechanisms so they can be used with PWs that are set up and maintained using IPv6 LDP sessions. This document updates RFC 4379. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamefP. Pan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. AsatiddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6829eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128668hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8029epagesopublicationDatePÎ◊Ògranking≠crfcd6829irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhLabel Switched Path (LSP) Ping for Pseudowire Forwarding Equivalence Classes (FECs) Advertised over IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØContent splicing is a process that replaces the content of a main multimedia stream with other multimedia content and delivers the substitutive multimedia content to the receivers for a period of time. Splicing is commonly used for insertion of local advertisements by cable operators, whereby national advertisement content is replaced with a local advertisement.

This memo describes some use cases for content splicing and a set of requirements for splicing content delivered by RTP. It provides concrete guidelines for how an RTP mixer can be used to handle content splicing. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. XiaddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6828eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128667hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÎ◊Ògranking¨crfcd6828irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Content Splicing for RTP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈The ITU-T has defined an architecture and requirements for operating an Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON).

The Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) protocol suite is designed to provide a control plane for a range of network technologies. These include optical networks such as time division multiplexing (TDM) networks including the Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH), Optical Transport Networks (OTNs), and lambda switching optical networks.

The requirements for GMPLS routing to satisfy the requirements of ASON routing and an evaluation of existing GMPLS routing protocols are provided in other documents. This document defines extensions to the OSPFv2 Link State Routing Protocol to meet the requirements for routing in an ASON.

Note that this work is scoped to the requirements and evaluation expressed in RFC 4258 and RFC 4652 and the ITU-T Recommendations that were current when those documents were written. Future extensions or revisions of this work may be necessary if the ITU-T Recommendations are revised or if new requirements are introduced into a revision of RFC 4258. This document obsoletes RFC 5787 and updates RFC 5786. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvVerizon CommunicationsdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. PapadimitriouddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6827eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128666hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÔÃËgranking´crfcd6827irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJAutomatically Switched Optical Network (ASON) Routing for OSPFv2 ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíConsider an IP multicast tree, constructed by Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM), that needs to pass through an MPLS domain in which Multipoint LDP (mLDP) point-to-multipoint and/or multipoint-to-multipoint Labels Switched Paths (LSPs) can be created.  The part of the IP multicast tree that traverses the MPLS domain can be instantiated as a multipoint LSP.  When a PIM Join message is received at the border of the MPLS domain, information from that message is encoded into mLDP messages.  When the mLDP messages reach the border of the next IP domain, the encoded information is used to generate PIM messages that can be sent through the IP domain.  The result is an IP multicast tree consisting of a set of IP multicast sub-trees that are spliced together with a multipoint LSP.  This document describes procedures regarding how IP multicast trees are spliced together with multipoint LSPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamelM. NapieraladdatejŒhfilenamegrfc6826eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128665hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePı1granking™crfcd6826irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjMultipoint LDP In-Band Signaling for Point-to-Multipoint and Multipoint-to-Multipoint Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7438∂habstractyEThis memo defines the Management Information Base (MIB) objects for managing the Traffic Engineering Database (TED) information with extensions in support of the Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) with Traffic Engineering (TE) as well as Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) for use with network management protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmKDDI R&D LabsdnamekM. Miyazawa¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnamehT. Otani¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiK. Kumaki¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiT. Nadeauddatej‘3hfilenamegrfc6825eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128664hkeywordsÉgTED-MIBctedcmibkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDatePı0granking©crfcd6825irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTTraffic Engineering Database Management Information Base in Support of MPLS-TE/GMPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,TCP/IP communication is currently restricted to a single path per connection, yet multiple paths often exist between peers. The simultaneous use of these multiple paths for a TCP/IP session would improve resource usage within the network and, thus, improve user experience through higher throughput and improved resilience to network failure.

Multipath TCP provides the ability to simultaneously use multiple paths between peers. This document presents a set of extensions to traditional TCP to support multipath operation. The protocol offers the same type of service to applications as TCP (i.e., reliable bytestream), and it provides the components necessary to establish and use multiple TCP flows across potentially disjoint paths. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegA. Ford¢kaffiliationxU. Politechnica of BucharestdnameiC. Raiciu¢kaffiliationqU. College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationxU. catholique de LouvaindnamenO. BonaventureddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6824eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128663hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8684epages@opublicationDateP˘granking®crfcd6824irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>TCP Extensions for Multipath Operation with Multiple AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document describes the manner in which generic application information (i.e., information not directly related to the operation of the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol) should be advertised in IS-IS Link State Protocol Data Units (LSPs) and defines guidelines that should be used when flooding such information.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Shandddatej‘ﬁhfilenamegrfc6823eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128662hkeywordsÅx*intermediate system to intermediate systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP’n∏grankingßcrfcd6823irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Advertising Generic Information in IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document describes a mechanism that allows a single router to share one or more circuits among multiple Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) routing protocol instances.

Multiple instances allow the isolation of resources associated with each instance. Routers will form instance-specific adjacencies. Each instance can support multiple topologies. Each topology has a unique Link State Database (LSDB). Each Protocol Data Unit (PDU) will contain a new Type-Length-Value (TLV) identifying the instance and the topology (or topologies) to which the PDU belongs.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsberg¢kaffiliation`dnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wardddatej’hfilenamegrfc6822eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128661hkeywordsÅx*intermediate system to intermediate systemkobsoletedByÅd8202epagesopublicationDateP’n∏granking¶crfcd6822irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletIS-IS Multi-InstancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyßPeer-to-peer (P2P) traffic optimization techniques that aim at improving locality in the peer selection process have attracted great interest in the research community and have been the subject of much discussion. Some of this discussion has produced controversial myths, some rooted in reality while others remain unfounded. This document evaluates the most prominent myths attributed to P2P optimization techniques by referencing the most relevant study or studies that have addressed facts pertaining to the myth. Using these studies, the authors hope to either confirm or refute each specific myth.

This document is a product of the IRTF P2PRG (Peer-to-Peer Research Group).gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejE. Marocco¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamehA. Fusco¢kaffiliationxBell Labs - Alcatel-LucentdnamehI. Rimac¢kaffiliationxBell Labs - Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbaniddatej’hfilenamegrfc6821eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymep2prgdfullx#p2prg - Peer-to-Peer Research GroupdnamexPeer-to-Peer Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128660hkeywordsÖtcross-domain trafficibandwidthotransit traffictpeer-to-peer cachingrpeer-to-peer swarmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP’n∏granking•crfcd6821irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexHImproving Peer Selection in Peer-to-peer Applications: Myths vs. RealitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document examines address resolution issues related to the scaling of data centers with a very large number of hosts.  The scope of this document is relatively narrow, focusing on address resolution (the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) in IPv4 and Neighbor Discovery (ND) in IPv6) within a data center.  This document is a product of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationrMerit Network Inc.dnamehM. Karir¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefI. Fooddatej’hfilenamegrfc6820eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdarmddfullxIarmd - Address Resolution for Massive numbers of hosts in the Data centerdnamexBAddress Resolution for Massive numbers of hosts in the Data centerdtypebwgbidjdoc-128659hkeywordsÖdARMDkdata centercARPbNDrNeighbor DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQÿÑgranking§crfcd6820irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Address Resolution Problems in Large Data Center NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document gives additional security considerations for OAuth, beyond those in the OAuth 2.0 specification, based on a comprehensive threat model for the OAuth 2.0 protocol.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamenT. Lodderstedt¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejM. McGloin¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamegP. Huntddatej’hfilenamegrfc6819eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128658hkeywordsÜmauthorizationnauthenticationetokenpcounter-measuresdHTTPdRESTkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDatePÎ◊granking£crfcd6819irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2OAuth 2.0 Threat Model and Security ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9700∂habstracty␀This document updates RFC 5280, the "Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Profile".  This document changes the set of acceptable encoding methods for the explicitText field of the user notice policy qualifier and clarifies the rules for converting internationalized domain name labels to ASCII.  This document also provides some clarifications on the use of self-signed certificates, trust anchors, and some updated security considerations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfAKAYLAdnamefP. YeeddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6818eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-128657hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePı/granking¢crfcd6818irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqUpdates to the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœLow Extra Delay Background Transport (LEDBAT) is an experimental delay-based congestion control algorithm that seeks to utilize the available bandwidth on an end-to-end path while limiting the consequent increase in queueing delay on that path.  LEDBAT uses changes in one-way delay measurements to limit congestion that the flow itself induces in the network.  LEDBAT is designed for use by background bulk-transfer applications to be no more aggressive than standard TCP congestion control (as specified in RFC 5681) and to yield in the presence of competing flows, thus limiting interference with the network performance of competing flows.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpBitTorrent, Inc.dnamekS. Shalunov¢kaffiliationpBitTorrent, Inc.dnamehG. Hazel¢kaffiliationxFranklin and Marshall CollegednamejJ. Iyengar¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnamemM. Kuehlewindddatej’hfilenamegrfc6817eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfledbatdfullx-ledbat - Low Extra Delay Background Transportdnamex$Low Extra Delay Background Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128656hkeywordsÑrCongestion controlkdelay-basediscavengercP2PkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP—y∑granking°crfcd6817irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Low Extra Delay Background Transport (LEDBAT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈This document describes a fully specified simple Forward Error Correction (FEC) scheme for Low-Density Parity Check (LDPC) Staircase codes that can be used to protect media streams along the lines defined by FECFRAME.  These codes have many interesting properties: they are systematic codes, they perform close to ideal codes in many use-cases, and they also feature very high encoding and decoding throughputs.  LDPC-Staircase codes are therefore a good solution to protect a single high bitrate source flow or to protect globally several mid-rate flows within a single FECFRAME instance.  They are also a good solution whenever the processing load of a software encoder or decoder must be kept to a minimum.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationoINSA-Lyon/INRIAdnameiM. Cunche¢kaffiliationxISAE - Univ. of ToulousednamehJ. Lacanddatej’hfilenamegrfc6816eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128655hkeywordsÇxForward Error CorrectionnLDPC-StaircasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP’n∑granking†crfcd6816irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcSimple Low-Density Parity Check (LDPC) Staircase Forward Error Correction (FEC) Scheme for FECFRAMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThe Benchmarking Methodology Working Group (BMWG) has been developing key performance metrics and laboratory test methods since 1990, and continues this work at present.  The methods described in RFC 2544 are intended to generate traffic that overloads network device resources in order to assess their capacity.  Overload of shared resources would likely be harmful to user traffic performance on a production network, and there are further negative consequences identified with production application of the methods.  This memo clarifies the scope of RFC 2544 and other IETF BMWG benchmarking work for isolated test environments only, and it encourages new standards activity for measurement methods applicable outside that scope.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiK. Dubray¢kaffiliationlTurnip VideodnamejJ. McQuaid¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6815eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128654hkeywordsÇgtestingkperformancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∏m<grankingücrfcd6815irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSApplicability Statement for RFC 2544: Use on Production Networks Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySA number of IPv4 options have become obsolete in practice, but have never been formally deprecated.  This document deprecates such IPv4 options, thus cleaning up the corresponding IANA registry.  Additionally, it obsoletes RFCs 1385, 1393, 1475, and 1770, and requests that the RFC Editor change their status to Historic. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationvUTN-FRH / SI6 NetworksdnamegF. GontddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6814eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128653hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∑grankingûcrfcd6814irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Formally Deprecating Some IPv4 OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThe Network Endpoint Assessment (NEA) protocols are subject to a subtle forwarding attack that has become known as the NEA Asokan Attack.  This document describes the attack and countermeasures that may be mounted.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehS. Hannaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6813eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcneadfullx!nea - Network Endpoint AssessmentdnamexNetwork Endpoint Assessmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-128652hkeywordsåqMan-in-the-MiddledMITMhSecurityhEndpointgPosturehProtocoljForwardingcTNCgChannelgBindingmCryptographicnCountermeasurekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP…ëgrankingùcrfcd6813irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<The Network Endpoint Assessment (NEA) Asokan Attack AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty·Cisco's Service-Level Assurance Protocol (Cisco's SLA Protocol) is a Performance Measurement protocol that has been widely deployed.  The protocol is used to measure service-level parameters such as network latency, delay variation, and packet/frame loss.  This document describes the Cisco SLA Protocol Measurement-Type UDP-Measurement, to enable vendor interoperability.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley EddygauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Chiba¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Clemm¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Medley¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. Thombare¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelE. Yedavalliddatej’hfilenamegrfc6812eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128651hkeywordsÅtCisco's SLA ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP˘grankingúcrfcd6812irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&Cisco Service-Level Assurance ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%To help reduce well-known threats against BGP including prefix mis- announcing and monkey-in-the-middle attacks, one of the security requirements is the ability to validate the origination Autonomous System (AS) of BGP routes.  More specifically, one needs to validate that the AS number claiming to originate an address prefix (as derived from the AS_PATH attribute of the BGP route) is in fact authorized by the prefix holder to do so.  This document describes a simple validation mechanism to partially satisfy this requirement. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. Austeinddatej’hfilenamegrfc6811eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128650hkeywordsÇdSIDRhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateP˘grankingõcrfcd6811irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGP Prefix Origin ValidationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8893d8481∂habstractyLIn order to verifiably validate the origin Autonomous Systems of BGP announcements, routers need a simple but reliable mechanism to receive Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RFC 6480) prefix origin data from a trusted cache.  This document describes a protocol to deliver validated prefix origin data to routers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationtDragon Research LabsdnamejR. AusteinddatejÕÈhfilenamegrfc6810eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128649hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP˘grankingöcrfcd6810irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@The Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) to Router ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8210∂habstracty¬This specification defines a new SIP header field, Feature-Caps. The Feature-Caps header field conveys feature-capability indicators that are used to indicate support of features and capabilities for SIP entities that are not represented by the Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) of the Contact header field.

SIP entities that are represented by the URI of the SIP Contact header field can convey media feature tags in the Contact header field to indicate support of features and capabilities.

This specification also defines feature-capability indicators and creates a new IANA registry, "Proxy-Feature Feature-Capability Indicator Trees", for registering feature-capability indicators. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekI. Sedlacek¢kaffiliationkAcme PacketdnameiH. Kaplanddatej’hfilenamegrfc6809eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128648hkeywordsÜeproxygfeaturekfeature tagxfeature-capability indicatorlFeature-CapsjcapabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP¥x≤grankingôcrfcd6809irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcMechanism to Indicate Support of Features and Capabilities in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThis memo provides the supporting test plan and results to advance RFC 2679 on one-way delay metrics along the Standards Track, following the process in RFC 6576.  Observing that the metric definitions themselves should be the primary focus rather than the implementations of metrics, this memo describes the test procedures to evaluate specific metric requirement clauses to determine if the requirement has been interpreted and implemented as intended.  Two completely independent implementations have been tested against the key specifications of RFC 2679.  This memo also provides direct input for development of a revision of RFC 2679.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamemL. Ciavattone¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geib¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationxTechnical University DarmstadtdnameiM. Wieserddatej‘%hfilenamegrfc6808eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128647hkeywordsÉmOne-way DelayvIP Performance MetricsdIPPMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP¿VKgrankingòcrfcd6808irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOTest Plan and Results Supporting Advancement of RFC 2679 on the Standards TrackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This specification defines a method for providing multicast distribution-tree accounting data.  Simple extensions to the Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) protocol allow a rough approximation of tree-based data in a scalable fashion.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekG. Shepherd¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefY. CaiddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6807eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-128646hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP¿VKgrankingócrfcd6807irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCPopulation Count Extensions to Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This memo documents a method for a Kerberos Key Distribution Center (KDC) to respond to client requests for Kerberos tickets when the client does not have detailed configuration information on the realms of users or services.  The KDC will handle requests for principals in other realms by returning either a referral error or a cross-realm Ticket-Granting Ticket (TGT) to another realm on the referral path.  The clients will use this referral information to reach the realm of the target principal and then receive the ticket.  This memo also provides a mechanism for verifying that a request has not been tampered with in transit.  This memo updates RFC 4120. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejK. Raeburn¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhuddatej’hfilenamegrfc6806eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128645hkeywordsÉnauthenticationrsecurity protocolshidentitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∏m;grankingñcrfcd6806irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBKerberos Principal Name Canonicalization and Cross-Realm ReferralsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyComputing optimum routes for Label Switched Paths (LSPs) across multiple domains in MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) and GMPLS networks presents a problem because no single point of path computation is aware of all of the links and resources in each domain. A solution may be achieved using the Path Computation Element (PCE) architecture.

Where the sequence of domains is known a priori, various techniques can be employed to derive an optimum path. If the domains are simply connected, or if the preferred points of interconnection are also known, the Per-Domain Path Computation technique can be used. Where there are multiple connections between domains and there is no preference for the choice of points of interconnection, the Backward-Recursive PCE-based Computation (BRPC) procedure can be used to derive an optimal path.

This document examines techniques to establish the optimum path when the sequence of domains is not known in advance. The document shows how the PCE architecture can be extended to allow the optimum sequence of domains to be selected, and the optimum end-to-end path to be derived through the use of a hierarchical relationship between domains. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. FarrelddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6805eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128644hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateP≠·Ogrankingïcrfcd6805irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|The Application of the Path Computation Element Architecture to the Determination of a Sequence of Domains in MPLS and GMPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyRThis document describes the DISCOVER opcode, an experimental extension to the Domain Name System (DNS) to use multicast queries for resource discovery.  This opcode was tested in experiments run during 1995 and 1996 for the Topology Based Domain Search (TBDS) project.  This project is no longer active and there are no current plans to restart it.  TBDS was the first known use of multicast transport for DNS.  A client multicasts a DNS query using the DISCOVER opcode and processes the multiple responses that may result.  This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejB. Manningddatej»Øhfilenamegrfc6804eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128643hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateP∑grankingîcrfcd6804irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex*DISCOVER: Supporting Multicast DNS QueriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document specifies two encryption types and two corresponding checksum types for the Kerberos cryptosystem framework defined in RFC 3961.  The new types use the Camellia block cipher in CBC mode with ciphertext stealing and the CMAC algorithm for integrity protection.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwMIT Kerberos ConsortiumdnameiG. Hudsonddatej”^hfilenamegrfc6803eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128642hkeywordsÇhCamelliahKerberoskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP´=¸grankingìcrfcd6803irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Camellia Encryption for Kerberos 5dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡This memo describes an extension to the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP).  Specifically, it extends the TWAMP-Test protocol, which identifies and manages packet trains, in order to measure capacity metrics like the available path capacity, tight section capacity, and UDP delivery rate in the forward and reverse path directions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamenS. Baillargeon¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiC. Flinta¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Johnssonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6802eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdo