crfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıProxy servers play a central role as an intermediary in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) as they define and impact policies on call routing, rendezvous, and other call features.  This document specifies a framework for SIP session policies that provides a standard mechanism by which a proxy can define or influence policies on sessions, such as the codecs or media types to be used.  It defines a model, an overall architecture and new protocol mechanisms for session policies. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Alcatel-LucentdnamegV. Hilt¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. RosenbergddatejÕãhfilenamegrfc6794eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128635hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateP¡®)grankingäcrfcd6794irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBA Framework for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Session PoliciesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Autonomous System number is encoded as a two-octet entity in the base BGP specification.  This document describes extensions to BGP to carry the Autonomous System numbers as four-octet entities.  This document obsoletes RFC 4893 and updates RFC 4271. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehQ. Vohra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6793eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128634hkeywordsÇqautonomous systemwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP—y∂grankingâcrfcd6793irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>BGP Support for Four-Octet Autonomous System (AS) Number SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThis memo proposes an extensible Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) monitoring framework for extending the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) with a new RTCP Extended Reports (XR) block type to report new metrics regarding media transmission or reception quality.  In this framework, a new XR block should contain a single metric or a small number of metrics relevant to a single parameter of interest or concern, rather than containing a number of metrics that attempt to provide full coverage of all those parameters of concern to a specific application.  Applications may then "mix and match" to create a set of blocks that cover their set of concerns.  Where possible, a specific block should be designed to be reusable across more than one application, for example, for all of voice, streaming audio, and video.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegG. Hunt¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehP. Ardenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6792eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128633hkeywordsÅxReal Time Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP¥x±grankingàcrfcd6792irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Guidelines for Use of the RTP Monitoring FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^A stateless IPv4/IPv6 translator may receive ICMPv6 packets containing non-IPv4-translatable addresses as the source.  These packets should be passed across the translator as ICMP packets directed to the IPv4 destination.  This document presents recommendations for source address translation in ICMPv6 headers to handle such cases. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamepR. Vaithianathan¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6791eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128632hkeywordsÑxIP/ICMP Translation Algorithmx IPv4-translatable IPv6 addressesfICMPv6jtraceroutekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP£U"grankingácrfcd6791irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Stateless Source Address Mapping for ICMPv6 PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“Load balancing is a powerful tool for engineering traffic across a network.  This memo suggests ways of improving load balancing across MPLS networks using the concept of "entropy labels".  It defines the concept, describes why entropy labels are useful, enumerates properties of entropy labels that allow maximal benefit, and shows how they can be signaled and used for various applications.  This document updates RFCs 3031, 3107, 3209, and 5036. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationxLevel 3 Communications, LLCdnameiS. Amante¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemW. Henderickx¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamegL. Yongddatej‘Jhfilenamegrfc6790eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128631hkeywordsÑgentropydhashdecmpnload balancingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP´=˚grankingÜcrfcd6790irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,The Use of Entropy Labels in MPLS ForwardingdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7447d8012d7274∂habstractyiThis document provides the entry point to the set of documentation about the Congestion Exposure (ConEx) protocol.  It explains the motivation for including a ConEx marking at the IP layer: to expose information about congestion to network nodes.  Although such information may have a number of uses, this document focuses on how the information communicated by the ConEx marking can serve as the basis for significantly more efficient and effective traffic management than what exists on the Internet today.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiR. Woundy¢kaffiliationcCDTdnameiA. Cooperddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6789eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeconexdfullxconex - Congestion ExposurednamesCongestion Exposuredtypebwgbidjdoc-128630hkeywordsÇtCongestion SignalingrTraffic ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP¿VJgrankingÖcrfcd6789irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Congestion Exposure (ConEx) Concepts and Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’In Ethernet-based aggregation networks, several subscriber premises may be logically connected to the same interface of an Edge Router.  This document proposes a method for the Edge Router to identify the subscriber premises using the contents of the received Router Solicitation messages.  The applicability is limited to broadband network deployment scenarios in which multiple user ports are mapped to the same virtual interface on the Edge Router. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Kavanagh¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehB. Varga¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehS. Ooghe¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekE. NordmarkddatejŒ
hfilenamegrfc6788eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128629hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP´=˚grankingÑcrfcd6788irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Line-Identification OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThe Media Resource Control Protocol Version 2 (MRCPv2) allows client hosts to control media service resources such as speech synthesizers, recognizers, verifiers, and identifiers residing in servers on the network.  MRCPv2 is not a "stand-alone" protocol -- it relies on other protocols, such as the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), to coordinate MRCPv2 clients and servers and manage sessions between them, and the Session Description Protocol (SDP) to describe, discover, and exchange capabilities.  It also depends on SIP and SDP to establish the media sessions and associated parameters between the media source or sink and the media server.  Once this is done, the MRCPv2 exchange operates over the control session established above, allowing the client to control the media processing resources on the speech resource server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeVoxeodnamejD. Burnett¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemS. Shanmughamddatej’hfilenamegrfc6787eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhspeechscdfullx"speechsc - Speech Services ControldnamewSpeech Services Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128628hkeywordsãdmrcphspeechsccasrcttsospeech servicesrspeech recognitionpspeech synthesisenlsmlvspeaker authenticationtspeaker verificationvspeaker identificationkobsoletedByÄepages‡opublicationDateP¢∫grankingÉcrfcd6787irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Media Resource Control Protocol Version 2 (MRCPv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This document specifies a mechanism for delivering the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) Attribute-Value Pairs (AVPs) in encrypted form. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. Yegin¢kaffiliationnGridmerge Ltd.dnameiR. CragieddatejÕ˘hfilenamegrfc6786eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128627hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP£U"grankingÇcrfcd6786irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcEncrypting the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) Attribute-Value PairsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRSieve defines an email filtering language that can, in principle, plug into any point in the processing of an email message.  As defined in the base specification, it plugs into mail delivery.  This document defines how Sieve can plug into points in IMAP where messages are created or changed, adding the option of user-defined or installation-defined filtering (or, with Sieve extensions, features such as notifications).  Because this requires future Sieve extensions to specify their interactions with this one, this document updates the base Sieve specification, RFC 5228. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej‘Chfilenamegrfc6785eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-128626hkeywordsÇeemailifilteringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePõlgrankingÅcrfcd6785irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSupport for Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) Events in SievedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¿This document defines four new options for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6).  These options are used to carry configuration information for Kerberos. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Sakane¢kaffiliationsToshiba CorporationdnamekM. Ishiyamaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6784eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128625hkeywordsÇhsecurityfdhcpv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePö_grankingÄcrfcd6784irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKerberos Options for DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document describes considerations for mailing lists with the introduction of non-ASCII UTF-8 email addresses.  It outlines some possible scenarios for handling lists with mixtures of non-ASCII and traditional addresses but does not specify protocol changes or offer implementation or deployment advice.  This document is a product of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levine¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnamejR. Gellensddatej‘-hfilenamegrfc6783eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128624hkeywordsÉdMailtinternationalizationmmailing listskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatePö^grankingcrfcd6783irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Mailing Lists and Non-ASCII AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîOperators worldwide are in various stages of preparing for or deploying IPv6 in their networks.  These operators often face difficult challenges related to IPv6 introduction, along with those related to IPv4 run-out.  Operators will need to meet the simultaneous needs of IPv6 connectivity and continue support for IPv4 connectivity for legacy devices with a stagnant supply of IPv4 addresses.  The IPv6 transition will take most networks from an IPv4- only environment to an IPv6-dominant environment with long transition periods varying by operator.  This document helps provide a framework for wireline providers who are faced with the challenges of introducing IPv6 along with meeting the legacy needs of IPv4 connectivity, utilizing well-defined and commercially available IPv6 transition technologies.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuRogers CommunicationsdnamelV. Kuarsingh¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiL. Howardddatej’hfilenamegrfc6782eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128623hkeywordsÉjtransitionoIPv6 transitionhoperatorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePõlgranking~crfcd6782irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWireline Incremental IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a set of practices for operating the DNS with security extensions (DNSSEC). The target audience is zone administrators deploying DNSSEC.

The document discusses operational aspects of using keys and signatures in the DNS. It discusses issues of key generation, key storage, signature generation, key rollover, and related policies.

This document obsoletes RFC 4641, as it covers more operational ground and gives more up-to-date requirements with respect to key sizes and the DNSSEC operations.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejW. Mekking¢kaffiliationiSIDN LabsdnameiR. Giebenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6781eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128622hkeywordsÉfDNSSECkoperationallkey rolloverkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateP’n∂granking}crfcd6781irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'DNSSEC Operational Practices, Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†The RSVP ASSOCIATION object was defined in the context of GMPLS-controlled Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  In this context, the object is used to associate recovery LSPs with the LSP they are protecting.  This object also has broader applicability as a mechanism to associate RSVP state.  This document defines how the ASSOCIATION object can be more generally applied.  This document also defines Extended ASSOCIATION objects that, in particular, can be used in the context of the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP).  This document updates RFC 2205, RFC 3209, and RFC 3473.  It also generalizes the definition of the Association ID field defined in RFC 4872. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelA. NarayananddatejÕıhfilenamegrfc6780eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128621hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePãå;granking|crfcd6780irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"RSVP ASSOCIATION Object ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects for configuring parameters of the Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP) process on a router.  The MIB module defined in this document, denoted NHDP-MIB, also reports state, performance information, and notifications about NHDP.  This additional state and performance information is useful to troubleshoot problems and performance issues during neighbor discovery. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationnUS Army CERDECdnamegR. Cole¢kaffiliationjDRS CenGendnamekI. Chakeresddatej’hfilenamegrfc6779eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128620hkeywordsárNetwork ManagementxManagement Information basecMIBeSMIv2gRoutingrNeighbor DiscoveryeMANETkobsoletedByÅd7939epagesCopublicationDatePê—∆granking{crfcd6779irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEDefinition of Managed Objects for the Neighborhood Discovery ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty…The IETF makes heavy use of email lists to conduct its work.  Participants frequently need to search and browse the archives of these lists and have asked for improved search capabilities.  The current archive mechanism could also be made more efficient.  This memo captures the requirements for improved email list archiving and searching systems.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparksddatej’hfilenamegrfc6778eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128619hkeywordsÅdtoolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePê—∆grankingzcrfcd6778irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Requirements for Archiving IETF Email Lists and for Providing Web-Based Browsing and SearchingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯When setting up a Label Switched Path (LSP) in Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) and MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) networks, the completion of the signaling process does not necessarily mean that the cross-connection along the LSP has been programmed accordingly and in a timely manner.  Meanwhile, the completion of the signaling process may be used by LSP users or applications that control their use as an indication that the data path has become usable.  The existence of the inconsistency between the signaling messages and cross-connection programming, and the possible failure of cross- connection programming, if not properly treated, will result in data loss or even application failure.  Characterization of this performance can thus help designers to improve the way in which LSPs are used and to make applications or tools that depend on and use LSPs more robust.  This document defines a series of performance metrics to evaluate the connectivity of the data path in the signaling process. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdSJTUdnamefW. Sun¢kaffiliationdCATRdnamehG. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. Gao¢kaffiliationlUC RiversidednamefG. Xie¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejR. Papnejaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6777eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128618hkeywordsÜxProvisioning performancewPerformance measurementcUNIsBandwidth on Demandvperformance evaluationxMeasurement methodologieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePö^grankingycrfcd6777irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuLabel Switched Path (LSP) Data Path Delay Metrics in Generalized MPLS and MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines an RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Source Description (SDES) item and an RTCP Extended Report (XR) block carrying parameters that identify and describe a measurement period to which one or more other RTCP XR blocks may refer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej’hfilenamegrfc6776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxrblockdfullxExrblock - Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdnamex;Metric Blocks for use with RTCP's Extended Report Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128617hkeywordsÅtRTP Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDatePàËÙgrankingxcrfcd6776irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|Measurement Identity and Information Reporting Using a Source Description (SDES) Item and an RTCP Extended Report (XR) BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThe IETF work in IPv6 over Low-power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) defines 6LoWPANs such as IEEE 802.15.4.  This and other similar link technologies have limited or no usage of multicast signaling due to energy conservation.  In addition, the wireless network may not strictly follow the traditional concept of IP subnets and IP links.  IPv6 Neighbor Discovery was not designed for non- transitive wireless links, as its reliance on the traditional IPv6 link concept and its heavy use of multicast make it inefficient and sometimes impractical in a low-power and lossy network.  This document describes simple optimizations to IPv6 Neighbor Discovery, its addressing mechanisms, and duplicate address detection for Low- power Wireless Personal Area Networks and similar networks.  The document thus updates RFC 4944 to specify the use of the optimizations defined here. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiSensinodednameiZ. Shelby¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. BormannddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6775eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymg6lowpandfullx"6lowpan - IPv6 over Low power WPANdnamexIPv6 over Low power WPANdtypebwgbidjdoc-128616hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDatePö]grankingwcrfcd6775irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbNeighbor Discovery Optimization for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8505d9010d8929∂habstractyëThe BGP4 protocol specifies the selection and propagation of a single best path for each prefix. As defined and widely deployed today, BGP has no mechanisms to distribute alternate paths that are not considered best path between its speakers. This behavior results in a number of disadvantages for new applications and services.

The main objective of this document is to observe that by simply adding a new session between a route reflector and its client, the Nth best path can be distributed. This document also compares existing solutions and proposed ideas that enable distribution of more paths than just the best path.

This proposal does not specify any changes to the BGP protocol definition. It does not require a software upgrade of provider edge (PE) routers acting as route reflector clients. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgNTT MCLdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Fernando¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiK. KumakiddatejÃöhfilenamegrfc6774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128615hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePö]grankingvcrfcd6774irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Distribution of Diverse BGP PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©This document specifies an alternative encapsulation of the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP), referred to as DCCP-UDP.  This encapsulation allows DCCP to be carried through the current generation of Network Address Translation (NAT) middleboxes without modification of those middleboxes.  This document also updates the Session Description Protocol (SDP) information for DCCP defined in RFC 5762. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeSonusdnameiT. Phelan¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej’hfilenamegrfc6773eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128614hkeywordsÑdDCCPdNAPTcNATcUDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP£U!grankingucrfcd6773irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTDCCP-UDP: A Datagram Congestion Control Protocol UDP Encapsulation for NAT TraversaldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document defines an authorization policy language for controlling access to location information. It extends the Common Policy authorization framework to provide location-specific access control. More specifically, this document defines condition elements specific to location information in order to restrict access to data based on the current location of the Target.

Furthermore, this document defines two algorithms for reducing the granularity of returned location information. The first algorithm is defined for usage with civic location information, whereas the other one applies to geodetic location information. Both algorithms come with limitations. There are circumstances where the amount of location obfuscation provided is less than what is desired. These algorithms might not be appropriate for all application domains. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamejJ. Cuellar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Morris¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejM. Thomsonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6772eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128613hkeywordsÇtAuthorization PolicypLocation PrivacykobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDatePÔÃÊgrankingtcrfcd6772irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaGeolocation Policy: A Document Format for Expressing Privacy Preferences for Location InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\New work is typically brought to the IETF by a group of interested individuals. IETF meetings are a convenient place for such groups to hold informal get-togethers to discuss and develop their ideas. Such side meetings, which are not reflected in the IETF meeting agenda and have no official status, are often half-jokingly referred to as "bar BOF" sessions to acknowledge that some of them may eventually lead to a proposal for an official IETF BOF ("birds of a feather" session) on a given topic.

During recent IETF meetings, many such "bar BOF" get-togethers have been organized and moderated in ways that made them increasingly indistinguishable from official IETF BOFs or sometimes even IETF working group meetings.

This document argues that this recent trend is not helpful in reaching the ultimate goal of many of these get-togethers, i.e., to efficiently discuss and develop ideas for new IETF work. It encourages the organizers to consider the benefits of holding them in much less formal settings and to also consider alternative means to develop their ideas. This document also recommends that the community abandon the term "bar BOF" and instead use other terms such as "side meeting", in order to stress the unofficial nature of these get-togethers. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. CamarilloddatejŒ	hfilenamegrfc6771eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128612hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatePÅ␀=grankingscrfcd6771irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Considerations for Having a Successful "Bar BOF" Side MeetingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàContent Delivery Networks (CDNs) are commonly used for improving the End User experience of a content delivery service while keeping cost at a reasonable level.  This document focuses on use cases that correspond to identified industry needs and that are expected to be realized once open interfaces and protocols supporting the interconnection of CDNs are specified and implemented.  This document can be used to motivate the definition of the requirements to be supported by CDN Interconnection (CDNI) interfaces.  It obsoletes RFC 3570.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamekG. Bertrand¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamejE. Stephan¢kaffiliationbBTdnamelT. Burbridge¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationsAzuki Systems, Inc.dnameeK. Ma¢kaffiliationxAlcatel-Lucent (Velocix)dnameiG. Watsonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6770eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-128611hkeywordsÇcCDNdCDNIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP´=˙grankingrcrfcd6770irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Use Cases for Content Delivery Network InterconnectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈAll BGP routers in the Default-Free Zone (DFZ) are required to carry all routes in the Default-Free Routing Table (DFRT). This document describes a technique, Simple Virtual Aggregation (S-VA), that allows some BGP routers not to install all of those routes into the Forwarding Information Base (FIB).

Some routers in an Autonomous System (AS) announce an aggregate (the VA prefix) in addition to the routes they already announce. This enables other routers not to install the routes covered by the VA prefix into the FIB as long as those routes have the same next-hop as the VA prefix.

The VA prefixes that are announced within an AS are not announced to any other AS. The described functionality is of very low operational complexity, as it proposes a confined BGP speaker solution without any dependency on network-wide configuration or requirement for any form of intra-domain tunneling. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgNTT MCLdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Heitz¢kaffiliationfAristadnameeA. Lo¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehL. Zhang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeX. Xuddatej‘qhfilenamegrfc6769eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128610hkeywordsÇcBGPkaggregationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePàËÙgrankingqcrfcd6769irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Simple Virtual Aggregation (S-VA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  This document proposes an extension to the GBOND-MIB module with a set of objects for managing ATM-based multi-pair bonded xDSL interfaces, as defined in ITU-T Recommendation G.998.1.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpActelis NetworksdnamehE. Beiliddatej‘îhfilenamegrfc6768eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-128609hkeywordsårNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPxManagement Information BasegbondinglxDSL bondingkaggregationfG.BondjG.Bond/ATMgG.998.1cIMAdIMA+kobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateQ+ã˙grankingpcrfcd6768irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$ATM-Based xDSL Bonded Interfaces MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  This document defines an extension to the GBOND-MIB module with a set of objects for managing Ethernet-based multi-pair bonded Digital Subscriber Line (xDSL) interfaces, as defined in ITU-T Recommendation G.998.2.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpActelis NetworksdnamehE. Beili¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamenM. Morgensternddatej’hfilenamegrfc6767eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-128608hkeywordsârNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPxManagement Information BaselxDSL bondingpEthernet bondingkaggregationg802.3ahgG.998.2kobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateQ+ã˘grankingocrfcd6767irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Ethernet-Based xDSL Multi-Pair Bonding (G.Bond/Ethernet) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  This document proposes an extension to the GBOND-MIB module with a set of objects for managing multi-pair bonded xDSL interfaces using Time-Division Inverse Multiplexing (TDIM), as defined in ITU-T Recommendation G.998.3.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpActelis NetworksdnamehE. Beiliddatej‘ñhfilenamegrfc6766eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-128607hkeywordsárNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPxManagement Information BaselxDSL bondingkaggregationgG.998.3kobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateQ+ã˘grankingncrfcd6766irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRxDSL Multi-Pair Bonding Using Time-Division Inverse Multiplexing (G.Bond/TDIM) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  This document defines an extension to the Interfaces Group MIB with a set of common objects for managing multi-pair bonded Digital Subscriber Line (xDSL) interfaces, as defined in ITU-T Recommendations G.998.1, G.998.2, and G.998.3.  The textual conventions defining the bonding schemes are contained in a separate MIB module maintained by Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).  The MIB modules specific to each bonding technology are defined in G9981-MIB, G9982-MIB, and G9983-MIB, respectively.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpActelis NetworksdnamehE. Beili¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamenM. Morgensternddatej’hfilenamegrfc6765eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-128606hkeywordsçrNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPxManagement Information BaselxDSL bondingkaggregationeG.998gG.998.1gG.998.2gG.998.3dTDIMcIMAcEFMkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDateQ+ã˘grankingmcrfcd6765irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$xDSL Multi-Pair Bonding (G.Bond) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This specification describes how DNS SRV records, DNS TXT records, and well-known URIs can be used together or separately to locate CalDAV (Calendaring Extensions to Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV)) or CardDAV (vCard Extensions to WebDAV) services.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej‘Ωhfilenamegrfc6764eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128605hkeywordsÇcSRViiCalendarkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ%HÃgrankinglcrfcd6764irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhLocating Services for Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) and vCard Extensions to WebDAV (CardDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This document specifies how DNS resource records are named and structured to facilitate service discovery.  Given a type of service that a client is looking for, and a domain in which the client is looking for that service, this mechanism allows clients to discover a list of named instances of that desired service, using standard DNS queries.  This mechanism is referred to as DNS-based Service Discovery, or DNS-SD.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekM. KrochmalddatejÕ‹hfilenamegrfc6763eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128604hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateQ%HÃgrankingkcrfcd6763irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNS-Based Service DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstracty…As networked devices become smaller, more portable, and more ubiquitous, the ability to operate with less configured infrastructure is increasingly important. In particular, the ability to look up DNS resource record data types (including, but not limited to, host names) in the absence of a conventional managed DNS server is useful.

Multicast DNS (mDNS) provides the ability to perform DNS-like operations on the local link in the absence of any conventional Unicast DNS server. In addition, Multicast DNS designates a portion of the DNS namespace to be free for local use, without the need to pay any annual fee, and without the need to set up delegations or otherwise configure a conventional DNS server to answer for those names.

The primary benefits of Multicast DNS names are that (i) they require little or no administration or configuration to set them up, (ii) they work when no infrastructure is present, and (iii) they work during infrastructure failures.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekM. KrochmalddatejÕ›hfilenamegrfc6762eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128603hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesFopublicationDateQ%HÃgrankingjcrfcd6762irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlemMulticast DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document describes what it means to say that a Domain Name (DNS name) is reserved for special use, when reserving such a name is appropriate, and the procedure for doing so.  It establishes an IANA registry for such domain names, and seeds it with entries for some of the already established special domain names.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekM. KrochmalddatejÕ¸hfilenamegrfc6761eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128602hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ%HÃgrankingicrfcd6761irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSpecial-Use Domain NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydOne of the goals of the authors of Multicast DNS (mDNS) and DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD) was to retire AppleTalk and the AppleTalk Name Binding Protocol (NBP) and to replace them with an IP-based solution.  This document presents a brief overview of the capabilities of AppleTalk NBP and outlines the properties required of an IP-based replacement.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekM. KrochmalddatejÕzhfilenamegrfc6760eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128601hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateQ%HÀgrankinghcrfcd6760irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRequirements for a Protocol to Replace the AppleTalk Name Binding Protocol (NBP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˆThis document specifies a Cisco Systems extension to the IPFIX information model specified in RFC 5102 to export application information.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelN. Ben-DvoraddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6759eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128600hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDatePö]grankinggcrfcd6759irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUCisco Systems Export of Application Information in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This memo defines a mechanism for tunneling of SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) Message Transfer Priority values through MTAs (Message Transfer Agents) that don't support the MT-PRIORITY SMTP extension.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlbergddatej’hfilenamegrfc6758eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128599hkeywordsÇhPrioritydMMHSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÅ␀<grankingfcrfcd6758irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Tunneling of SMTP Message Transfer PrioritiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe local mobility anchor in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) domain is able to provide access-network- and access-operator-specific handling or policing of the mobile node traffic using information about the access network to which the mobile node is attached.  This specification defines a mechanism and a related mobility option for carrying the access network identifier and the access operator identification information from the mobile access gateway to the local mobility anchor over Proxy Mobile IPv6. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejM. Grayson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemR. Pazhyannurddatej’hfilenamegrfc6757eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128598hkeywordsÑcANIjANI optionx Access Network Identifier optionqPMIPv6 ANI optionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÅ␀<grankingecrfcd6757irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Access Network Identifier (ANI) Option for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7563¥habstracty√This document provides guidance to aid in the understanding of collaboration on standards development between the Telecommunication Standardization Sector of the International Telecommunication Union (ITU-T) and the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) of the Internet Society (ISOC). It is an update of and obsoletes RFC 3356. The updates reflect changes in the IETF and ITU-T since RFC 3356 was written. The bulk of this document is common text with ITU-T A Series Supplement 3 (07/2012).

Note: This was approved by TSAG on 4 July 2012 as Supplement 3 to the ITU-T A-Series of Recommendations.

This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemS. Trowbridge¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationxPearlfisher InternationaldnamejG. Fishman¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. BradnerddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6756eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128597hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePb≠}grankingdcrfcd6756irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexçInternet Engineering Task Force and International Telecommunication Union - Telecommunication Standardization Sector Collaboration GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractxÀThis document establishes an IETF URN Sub-namespace for use with OAuth-related specifications.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsPing Identity Corp.dnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’hfilenamegrfc6755eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128596hkeywordsÑeOAuthcURNmsub-namespaceuurn:ietf:params:oauthkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePnã$grankingccrfcd6755irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#An IETF URN Sub-Namespace for OAuthdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂA Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) router uses the Reverse Path Forwarding (RPF) procedure to select an upstream interface and router in order to build forwarding state.  When there are equal cost multipaths (ECMPs), existing implementations often use hash algorithms to select a path.  Such algorithms do not allow the spread of traffic among the ECMPs according to administrative metrics.  This usually leads to inefficient or ineffective use of network resources.  This document introduces the ECMP Redirect, a mechanism to improve the RPF procedure over ECMPs.  It allows ECMP selection to be based on administratively selected metrics, such as data transmission delays, path preferences, and routing metrics. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefL. Wei¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameeH. Ou¢kaffiliationlDIRECTV Inc.dnamegV. Arya¢kaffiliationlDIRECTV Inc.dnamekS. JethwaniddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6754eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-128595hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePàËÛgrankingbcrfcd6754irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCProtocol Independent Multicast Equal-Cost Multipath (ECMP) RedirectdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9436d8736∂habstracty±This document describes how to use the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) over Transport Layer Security (TLS) as a dereference protocol to resolve a reference to a Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO).  This document assumes that a Location Recipient possesses a URI that can be used in conjunction with the HTTP-Enabled Location Delivery (HELD) protocol to request the location of the Target. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiCommscopednameoJ. Winterbottom¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejM. Thomsonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6753eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128594hkeywordsÜdHELDkDereferencedlbyrdHTTPhLocationgGEOPRIVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePàËÒgrankingacrfcd6753irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKA Location Dereference Protocol Using HTTP-Enabled Location Delivery (HELD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe purpose of this document is to provide a discussion of the potential problems of using private, RFC 1918, or non-globally routable addressing within the core of a Service Provider (SP) network.  The discussion focuses on link addresses and, to a small extent, loopback addresses.  While many of the issues are well recognised within the ISP community, there appears to be no document that collectively describes the issues.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrPalo Alto NetworksdnamejA. KirkhamddatejÕúhfilenamegrfc6752eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128593hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePb≠}granking`crfcd6752irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Issues with Private IP Addressing in the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyIn customer sites having IPv4-only Customer Premises Equipment (CPE), Teredo (RFC 4380, RFC 5991, RFC 6081) provides last-resort IPv6 connectivity.  However, because it is designed to work without the involvement of Internet Service Providers, it has significant limitations (connectivity between IPv6 native addresses and Teredo addresses is uncertain; connectivity between Teredo addresses fails for some combinations of NAT types).  6a44 is a complementary solution that, being based on ISP cooperation, avoids these limitations.  At the beginning of 6a44 IPv6 addresses, it replaces the Teredo well-known prefix, present at the beginning of Teredo IPv6 addresses, with network-specific /48 prefixes assigned by local ISPs (an evolution similar to that from 6to4 to 6rd (IPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures)).  The specification is expected to be complete enough for running code to be independently written and the solution to be incrementally deployed and used.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiRD-IPtechdnamejR. Despres¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co., Ltd.dnamehS. Jiangddatej‘Ïhfilenamegrfc6751eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128592hkeywordsàkCoexistencejTransitionlInterworkingiTunnelingmEncapsulationgMappingmmap-and-encapqGlobal AddressingkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateP}^granking_crfcd6751irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexFNative IPv6 behind IPv4-to-IPv4 NAT Customer Premises Equipment (6a44)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis specification describes how to use bearer tokens in HTTP requests to access OAuth 2.0 protected resources.  Any party in possession of a bearer token (a "bearer") can use it to get access to the associated resources (without demonstrating possession of a cryptographic key).  To prevent misuse, bearer tokens need to be protected from disclosure in storage and in transport. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehD. Hardtddatej‘–hfilenamegrfc6750eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128591hkeywordsëfClientnResource OwnertAuthorization ServeroResource ServernToken EndpointvAuthorization EndpointuAuthorization RequestsAuthorization GrantrProtected ResourcelAccess TokennRefresh¬†TokenrAuthorization CodenImplicit GrantqClient IdentifierrAccess Token ScopexBearer Authorization HeaderxBearer Access¬†Token TypekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePyõgranking^crfcd6750irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token UsagedtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9700d8996∂habstracty≠The OAuth 2.0 authorization framework enables a third-party application to obtain limited access to an HTTP service, either on behalf of a resource owner by orchestrating an approval interaction between the resource owner and the HTTP service, or by allowing the third-party application to obtain access on its own behalf.  This specification replaces and obsoletes the OAuth 1.0 protocol described in RFC 5849. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehD. Hardtddatej’hfilenamegrfc6749eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeoauthdfullx"oauth - Web Authorization ProtocoldnamexWeb Authorization Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128590hkeywordsêfClientnResource OwnertAuthorization ServeroResource ServernToken EndpointvAuthorization EndpointuAuthorization RequestsAuthorization GrantrProtected ResourcelAccess TokennRefresh¬†TokenrAuthorization CodenImplicit GrantqClient IdentifierrAccess Token ScopejDelegationkobsoletedByÄepagesLopublicationDatePyógranking]crfcd6749irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%The OAuth 2.0 Authorization FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9700d8996d8252µhabstracty<This document provides an Architectural description and the Concept of Operations of some optional advanced deployment scenarios for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP), which is an evolutionary enhancement to IP.  None of the functions described here is required for the use or deployment of ILNP.  Instead, it offers descriptions of engineering and deployment options that might provide either enhanced capability or convenience in administration or management of ILNP-based systems.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoStephen FarrellgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejÕ˝hfilenamegrfc6748eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128589hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDatePû)granking\crfcd6748irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexYOptional Advanced Deployment Scenarios for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyPThis document defines an Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) extension to support the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol for IPv4 (ILNPv4).  ILNP is an experimental, evolutionary enhancement to IP.  This document is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejÕ˛hfilenamegrfc6747eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128588hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePû)granking[crfcd6747irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex_Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol for IPv4 (ILNPv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyCThis document defines two new IPv4 Options that are used only with the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol for IPv4 (ILNPv4).  ILNP is an experimental, evolutionary enhancement to IP.  This document is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejŒ␀hfilenamegrfc6746eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128587hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePû)grankingZcrfcd6746irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex?IPv4 Options for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¨This note defines an experimental ICMP message type for IPv4 used with the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP).  ILNP is an experimental, evolutionary enhancement to IP.  The ICMP message defined herein is used to dynamically update Identifier/Locator bindings for an existing ILNP session.  This is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejÕˇhfilenamegrfc6745eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128586hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePû)grankingYcrfcd6745irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexYICMP Locator Update Message for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol for IPv4 (ILNPv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyzThe Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP) is an experimental, evolutionary enhancement to IP.  ILNP has multiple instantiations.  This document describes an experimental Nonce Destination Option used only with ILNP for IPv6 (ILNPv6).  This document is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamenBrian HabermangauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejÕˇhfilenamegrfc6744eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128585hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePû)grankingXcrfcd6744irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex[IPv6 Nonce Destination Option for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol for IPv6 (ILNPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyæThis note specifies an experimental ICMPv6 message type used with the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP).  The Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP) is an experimental, evolutionary enhancement to IP.  This message is used to dynamically update Identifier/Locator bindings for an existing ILNP session.  This is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamenBrian HabermangauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejÕˇhfilenamegrfc6743eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128584hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePû)grankingWcrfcd6743irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexYICMP Locator Update Message for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol for IPv6 (ILNPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyQThis note describes additional optional resource records for use with the Domain Name System (DNS).  These optional resource records are for use with the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP).  This document is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN Bhatti¢kaffiliationgUS NISTdnamegS. RoseddatejÕ˛hfilenamegrfc6742eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128583hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePû(grankingVcrfcd6742irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexGDNS Resource Records for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyHThis document describes common (i.e., version independent) engineering details for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP), which is an experimental, evolutionary enhancement to IP.  This document is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejÕ˛hfilenamegrfc6741eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128582hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDatePû(grankingUcrfcd6741irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexEIdentifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP) Engineering ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyBThis document provides an architectural description and the concept of operations for the Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP), which is an experimental, evolutionary enhancement to IP.  This is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian FarrelgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekRJ Atkinson¢kaffiliationmU. St AndrewsdnameiSN BhattiddatejÕ˝hfilenamegrfc6740eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128581hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDatePû(grankingTcrfcd6740irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexDIdentifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP) Architectural DescriptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThe Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) protocol is an XML-based protocol for mapping service identifiers and geodetic or civic location information to service URIs and service boundaries. In particular, it can be used to determine the location-appropriate Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) for emergency services.

The <mapping> element in the LoST protocol specification encapsulates information about service boundaries and circumscribes the region within which all locations map to the same service Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) or set of URIs for a given service.

This document defines an XML protocol to exchange these mappings between two nodes. This mechanism is designed for the exchange of authoritative <mapping> elements between two entities. Exchanging cached <mapping> elements, i.e., non-authoritative elements, is possible but not envisioned. Even though the <mapping> element format is reused from the LoST specification, the mechanism in this document can be used without the LoST protocol. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej’hfilenamegrfc6739eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128580hkeywordsÅhLocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePàËgrankingScrfcd6739irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextSynchronizing Service Boundaries and <mapping> Elements Based on the Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty(The Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) is a component of the IPsec architecture and is used to perform mutual authentication as well as to establish and to maintain IPsec Security Associations (SAs) between the respective parties. IKEv2 supports several different authentication mechanisms, such as the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), certificates, and Shared Key (SK).

Diameter interworking for Mobile IPv6 between the Home Agent (HA), as a Diameter client, and the Diameter server has been specified. However, that specification focused on the usage of EAP and did not include support for SK-based authentication available with IKEv2. This document specifies the IKEv2-server-to-Diameter-server communication when the IKEv2 peer authenticates using IKEv2 with SK. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamejV. Cakulev¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamegA. Lior¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamemS. Mizikovskyddatej’hfilenamegrfc6738eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128579hkeywordsÅx(Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePä:^grankingRcrfcd6738irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfDiameter IKEv2 SK: Using Shared Keys to Support Interaction between IKEv2 Servers and Diameter ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new Diameter application and associated Command Codes.  The Capabilities Update application is intended to allow the dynamic update of certain Diameter peer capabilities while the peer-to-peer connection is in the open state. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegK. Jiao¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. ZornddatejÕ´hfilenamegrfc6737eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128578hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePä:^grankingQcrfcd6737irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,The Diameter Capabilities Update ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document describes the framework, messages, and procedures for the Diameter Network address and port translation Control Application.  This Diameter application allows per-endpoint control of Network Address Translators and Network Address and Port Translators, which are added to networks to cope with IPv4 address space depletion.  This Diameter application allows external devices to configure and manage a Network Address Translator device -- expanding the existing Diameter-based Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) and policy control capabilities with a Network Address Translator and Network Address and Port Translator control component.  These external devices can be network elements in the data plane such as a Network Access Server, or can be more centralized control plane devices such as AAA-servers.  This Diameter application establishes a context to commonly identify and manage endpoints on a gateway or server and a Network Address Translator and Network Address and Port Translator device.  This includes, for example, the control of the total number of Network Address Translator bindings allowed or the allocation of a specific Network Address Translator binding for a particular endpoint.  In addition, it allows Network Address Translator devices to provide information relevant to accounting purposes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Brockners¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekS. Bhandari¢kaffiliation`dnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationvTelcordia TechnologiesdnamejV. Fajardoddatej’hfilenamegrfc6736eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128577hkeywordsÖkNAT controleNAT44eNAT66cCGNcBNGkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDatePä:]grankingPcrfcd6736irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADiameter Network Address and Port Translation Control ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document defines Attribute-Value Pair (AVP) containers for various priority parameters for use with Diameter and the Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) framework.  The parameters themselves are defined in several different protocols that operate at either the network or application layer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlberg¢kaffiliationtPT Taylor ConsultingdnameiT. Taylorddatej‘{hfilenamegrfc6735eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128576hkeywordsÅcAVPkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatePä:]grankingOcrfcd6735irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Diameter Priority Attribute-Value PairsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySome Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) applications require the transport of cryptographic keying material.  This document specifies a set of Attribute-Value Pairs (AVPs) providing native Diameter support of cryptographic key delivery. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamejV. Cakulevddatej‘Úhfilenamegrfc6734eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128575hkeywordsÉcAAAcERPcMSKkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePä:]grankingNcrfcd6734irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Diameter Attribute-Value Pairs for Cryptographic Key TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Diameter base protocol is intended to provide an Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) framework for applications such as network access or IP mobility in both local and roaming situations.  This document specifies the message format, transport, error reporting, accounting, and security services used by all Diameter applications.  The Diameter base protocol as defined in this document obsoletes RFC 3588 and RFC 5719, and it must be supported by all new Diameter implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvTelcordia TechnologiesdnamejV. Fajardo¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6733eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128574hkeywordsÇhDiametercAAAkobsoletedByÄepagesòopublicationDatePä:\grankingMcrfcd6733irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevDiameter Base ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7075d8553µhabstractyΩ6to4 Provider Managed Tunnels (6to4-PMT) provide a framework that can help manage 6to4 tunnels operating in an anycast configuration.  The 6to4-PMT framework is intended to serve as an option for operators to help improve the experience of 6to4 operation when conditions of the network may provide sub-optimal performance or break normal 6to4 operation.  6to4-PMT supplies a stable provider prefix and forwarding environment by utilizing existing 6to4 relays with an added function of IPv6 Prefix Translation.  This operation may be particularly important in NAT444 infrastructures where a customer endpoint may be assigned a non-RFC1918 address, thus breaking the return path for anycast-based 6to4 operation.  6to4-PMT has been successfully used in a production network, implemented as open source code, and implemented by a major routing vendor.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon BonicagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuRogers CommunicationsdnamelV. Kuarsingh¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejO. Vautrinddatej’hfilenamegrfc6732eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128573hkeywordsÅh6to4-PMTkobsoletedByÅd7526epagesopublicationDatePgÛ]grankingLcrfcd6732irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex6to4 Provider Managed TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvA multi-interfaced node is connected to multiple networks, some of which might be utilizing private DNS namespaces.  A node commonly receives recursive DNS server configuration information from all connected networks.  Some of the recursive DNS servers might have information about namespaces other servers do not have.  When a multi-interfaced node needs to utilize DNS, the node has to choose which of the recursive DNS servers to use.  This document describes DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 options that can be used to configure nodes with information required to perform informed recursive DNS server selection decisions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainen¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegJ. Kato¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemonddatej”,hfilenamegrfc6731eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcmifdfullxmif - Multiple InterfacesdnamesMultiple Interfacesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128572hkeywordsÖcDNSeRDNSSiinterfacedFQDNiselectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP—yµgrankingKcrfcd6731irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBImproved Recursive DNS Server Selection for Multi-Interfaced NodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx—This document defines the requirements for a set of tools for use by the IETF Nominations Committee.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. HalpernddatejÕãhfilenamegrfc6730eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128571hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatePb≠}grankingJcrfcd6730irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Requirements for IETF Nominations Committee ToolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document registers a trace field clause for use in indicating transitions between handling queues or processing states, including enacting inter- and intra-host message transitions.  This might include message quarantining, mailing list moderation, timed delivery, queuing for further analysis, content conversion, or other similar causes, as well as optionally identifying normal handling queues. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationoCloudmark, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’hfilenamegrfc6729eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128570hkeywordsÇjQuarantinejModerationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePHO»grankingIcrfcd6729irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Indicating Email Handling States in Trace FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a data model for the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) and Packet Sampling (PSAMP) protocols.  It is for configuring and monitoring Selection Processes, Caches, Exporting Processes, and Collecting Processes of IPFIX- and PSAMP-compliant Monitoring Devices using the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF).  The data model is defined using UML (Unified Modeling Language) class diagrams and formally specified using YANG.  The configuration data is encoded in Extensible Markup Language (XML). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkTU MuenchendnamehG. Muenz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitkenddatej‘Ìhfilenamegrfc6728eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128569hkeywordsÉeIPFIXePSAMPdYANGkobsoletedByÄepagesÅopublicationDatePkËgrankingHcrfcd6728irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiConfiguration Data Model for the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) and Packet Sampling (PSAMP) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes extensions to the IPFIX-SELECTOR-MIB module.  For IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) implementations that use Packet Sampling (PSAMP) techniques, this memo defines the PSAMP- MIB module containing managed objects for providing information on applied packet selection functions and their parameters. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamehT. Dietz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamejJ. Quittekddatej’hfilenamegrfc6727eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128568hkeywordsÜePSAMPeIPFIXcMIBhSamplingiFilteringiSelectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePkËgrankingGcrfcd6727irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Definitions of Managed Objects for Packet SamplingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document defines File Delivery over Unidirectional Transport (FLUTE), a protocol for the unidirectional delivery of files over the Internet, which is particularly suited to multicast networks.  The specification builds on Asynchronous Layered Coding, the base protocol designed for massively scalable multicast distribution.  This document obsoletes RFC 3926. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehT. Paila¢kaffiliationiNokia/TUTdnamehR. Walsh¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Technologies, Inc.dnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamekR. Lehtonenddatej‘ühfilenamegrfc6726eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128567hkeywordsÅiMulticastkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDatePö\grankingFcrfcd6726irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3FLUTE - File Delivery over Unidirectional TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@The DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) require the use of cryptographic algorithm suites for generating digital signatures over DNS data.  The algorithms specified for use with DNSSEC are reflected in an IANA-maintained registry.  This document presents a set of changes for some entries of the registry. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. RoseddatejŒhfilenamegrfc6725eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128566hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP?†UgrankingEcrfcd6725irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<DNS Security (DNSSEC) DNSKEY Algorithm IANA Registry UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThis document describes two algorithms, one for source address selection and one for destination address selection. The algorithms specify default behavior for all Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) implementations. They do not override choices made by applications or upper-layer protocols, nor do they preclude the development of more advanced mechanisms for address selection. The two algorithms share a common context, including an optional mechanism for allowing administrators to provide policy that can override the default behavior. In dual-stack implementations, the destination address selection algorithm can consider both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses -- depending on the available source addresses, the algorithm might prefer IPv6 addresses over IPv4 addresses, or vice versa.

Default address selection as defined in this specification applies to all IPv6 nodes, including both hosts and routers. This document obsoletes RFC 3484. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnameiR. Draves¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamelA. Matsumoto¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chownddatej’hfilenamegrfc6724eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128565hkeywordsÑfsourcekdestinationdsortgsortingkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDatePNÁ4grankingDcrfcd6724irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Default Address Selection for Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThe control-word negotiation mechanism specified in RFC 4447 has a problem when a PE (Provider Edge) changes the preference for the use of the control word from NOT PREFERRED to PREFERRED.  This document updates RFC 4447 and RFC 6073 by adding the Label Request message to resolve this control-word negotiation issue for single-segment and multi-segment pseudowires. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamefL. Jin¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefR. Key¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiS. Delord¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Boutrosddatej’hfilenamegrfc6723eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128564hkeywordsÖlcontrol wordxcontrol word negotiationxcontrol word renegotiationx"control word negotiation mechanismx$control word renegotiation mechanismkobsoletedByÅd8077epages	opublicationDatePHO»grankingCcrfcd6723irfcNumberCes