opublicationDatePb≠}grankingAcrfcd6721irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex The Atom "deleted-entry" ElementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Generalized TTL Security Mechanism (GTSM) describes a generalized use of a packet's Time to Live (TTL) (IPv4) or Hop Limit (IPv6) to verify that the packet was sourced by a node on a connected link, thereby protecting the router\'s IP control plane from CPU utilization-based attacks. This technique improves security and is used by many protocols. This document defines the GTSM use for the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP).

This specification uses a bit reserved in RFC 5036 and therefore updates RFC 5036. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Asatiddatej’hfilenamegrfc6720eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128561hkeywordsÇdGTSMcLDPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP8};granking@crfcd6720irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWThe Generalized TTL Security Mechanism (GTSM) for the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7552∂habstractyNThe Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) constructs routes by using Objective Functions that optimize or constrain the routes it selects and uses.  This specification describes the Minimum Rank with Hysteresis Objective Function (MRHOF), an Objective Function that selects routes that minimize a metric, while using hysteresis to reduce churn in response to small metric changes.  MRHOF works with additive metrics along a route, and the metrics it uses are determined by the metrics that the RPL Destination Information Object (DIO) messages advertise. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of HoustondnamejO. Gnawali¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamehP. Levisddatej’hfilenamegrfc6719eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128560hkeywordsÑx1Routing Protocol for Low Power and Lossy NetworkscRPLxLow Power and Lossy NetworkscLLNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePNÁ4granking?crfcd6719irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3The Minimum Rank with Hysteresis Objective FunctiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document describes a framework comprised of a number of scenarios and associated requirements for pseudowire (PW) redundancy.  A set of redundant PWs is configured between provider edge (PE) nodes in single-segment PW applications or between terminating PE (T-PE) nodes in multi-segment PW applications.  In order for the PE/T-PE nodes to indicate the preferred PW to use for forwarding PW packets to one another, a new PW status is required to indicate the preferential forwarding status of active or standby for each PW in the redundant set.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehP. Muley¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekM. Aissaoui¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocciddatej’hfilenamegrfc6718eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128559hkeywordsÜfActivegstandbyjprotectionkdual-homingdvplsdvpwskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP@f™granking>crfcd6718irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuPseudowire RedundancydtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyfThis document describes a protocol, called kx509, for using Kerberos tickets to acquire X.509 certificates. These certificates may be used for many of the same purposes as X.509 certificates acquired by other means, but if a Kerberos infrastructure already exists, then the overhead of using kx509 may be much less.

While not standardized, this protocol is already in use at several large organizations, and certificates issued with this protocol are recognized by the International Grid Trust Federation. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen FarrellgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxJet Propulsion Lab - CaltechdnamegH. Hotz¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamejR. Allberyddatej‘Ohfilenamegrfc6717eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128558hkeywordsÜhKerberoseX.509ekx509cKCAkkca-servicekkca_servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP?†Tgranking=crfcd6717irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex=kx509 Kerberized Certificate Issuance Protocol in Use in 2012dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the Opus interactive speech and audio codec.  Opus is designed to handle a wide range of interactive audio applications, including Voice over IP, videoconferencing, in-game chat, and even live, distributed music performances.  It scales from low bitrate narrowband speech at 6 kbit/s to very high quality stereo music at 510 kbit/s.  Opus uses both Linear Prediction (LP) and the Modified Discrete Cosine Transform (MDCT) to achieve good compression of both speech and music. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsMozilla CorporationdnameiJM. Valin¢kaffiliationwSkype Technologies S.A.dnamefK. Vos¢kaffiliationsMozilla CorporationdnamemT. Terriberryddatej’hfilenamegrfc6716eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymecodecdfullx%codec - Internet Wideband Audio CodecdnamexInternet Wideband Audio Codecdtypebwgbidjdoc-128557hkeywordsÑevoiceemusicqlossy compressiondVOIPkobsoletedByÄepagesFopublicationDatePN