opublicationDatePNÁ2granking8crfcd6712irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure -- HTTP Transfer for the Certificate Management Protocol (CMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9480∂habstractyÉThis document establishes an IANA registry for Level of Assurance (LoA) Profiles.  The registry is intended to be used as an aid to discovering such LoA definitions in protocols that use an LoA concept, including Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) 2.0 and OpenID Connect.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhNORDUNetdnamelL. Johanssonddatej”‹hfilenamegrfc6711eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128552hkeywordsÇhIdentityiAssurancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP8}:granking7crfcd6711irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6An IANA Registry for Level of Assurance (LoA) ProfilesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines an extension to the SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) service whereby messages are given a label to indicate preferential handling, to enable mail handling nodes to take this information into account for onward processing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlbergddatej‘ihfilenamegrfc6710eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128551hkeywordsÉdSMTPhprioritydMMHSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP37Igranking6crfcd6710irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGSimple Mail Transfer Protocol Extension for Message Transfer PrioritiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyÃThis document discusses architectural issues related to the extensibility of Internet protocols, with a focus on design considerations.  It is intended to assist designers of both base protocols and extensions.  Case studies are included.  A companion document, RFC 4775 (BCP 125), discusses procedures relating to the extensibility of IETF protocols.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliation`dnamekS. CheshireddatejÃÔhfilenamegrfc6709eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128550hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDatePT,«granking5crfcd6709irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex-Design Considerations for Protocol ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„Many Internet applications are used to access resources, such as pieces of information or server processes that are available in several equivalent replicas on different hosts. This includes, but is not limited to, peer-to-peer file sharing applications. The goal of Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) is to provide guidance to applications that have to select one or several hosts from a set of candidates capable of providing a desired resource. This guidance shall be based on parameters that affect performance and efficiency of the data transmission between the hosts, e.g., the topological distance. The ultimate goal is to improve performance or Quality of Experience in the application while reducing the utilization of the underlying network infrastructure.

This document enumerates requirements for specifying, assessing, or comparing protocols and implementations. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnameiS. Kiesel¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationsComcast CorporationdnameiR. Woundy¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. YangddatejÕ˝hfilenamegrfc6708eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128549hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePgÛ]granking4crfcd6708irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) provide numerous benefits for cacheable content: reduced delivery cost, improved quality of experience for End Users, and increased robustness of delivery. For these reasons, they are frequently used for large-scale content delivery. As a result, existing CDN Providers are scaling up their infrastructure, and many Network Service Providers (NSPs) are deploying their own CDNs. It is generally desirable that a given content item can be delivered to an End User regardless of that End User's location or attachment network. This is the motivation for interconnecting standalone CDNs so they can interoperate as an open content delivery infrastructure for the end-to-end delivery of content from Content Service Providers (CSPs) to End Users. However, no standards or open specifications currently exist to facilitate such CDN Interconnection.

The goal of this document is to outline the problem area of CDN Interconnection for the IETF CDNI (CDN Interconnection) working group. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxVelocix (Alcatel-Lucent)dnamepB. Niven-Jenkins¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitarddatej’hfilenamegrfc6707eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcdnidfullx0cdni - Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondnamex)Content Delivery Networks Interconnectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-128548hkeywordsÇhDeliverycCDNkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDatePb≠|granking3crfcd6707irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEContent Distribution Network Interconnection (CDNI) Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypNodes attached to common multi-access link types (e.g., multicast- capable, shared media, non-broadcast multiple access (NBMA), etc.) can exchange packets as neighbors on the link, but they may not always be provisioned with sufficient routing information for optimal neighbor selection.  Such nodes should therefore be able to discover a trusted intermediate router on the link that provides both forwarding services to reach off-link destinations and redirection services to inform the node of an on-link neighbor that is closer to the final destination.  This redirection can provide a useful route optimization, since the triangular path from the ingress link neighbor, to the intermediate router, and finally to the egress link neighbor may be considerably longer than the direct path from ingress to egress.  However, ordinary redirection may lead to operational issues on certain link types and/or in certain deployment scenarios.  This document therefore introduces an Asymmetric Extended Route Optimization (AERO) capability that addresses the issues.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamejF. Templinddatej’hfilenamegrfc6706eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128547hkeywordsäeroutehoptimizeloptimizationhredirectkredirectionhprotocolgroutingdlinklmulti-accessdIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateP)¸ùgranking2crfcd6706irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Asymmetric Extended Route Optimization (AERO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) is a network based mobility management protocol that enables IP mobility for a host without requiring its participation in any mobility-related signaling.  PMIPv6 requires all communications to go through the local mobility anchor.  As this can be suboptimal, Localized Routing (LR) allows Mobile Nodes (MNs) attached to the same or different Mobile Access Gateways (MAGs) to route traffic by using localized forwarding or a direct tunnel between the gateways.  This document proposes initiation, utilization, and termination mechanisms for localized routing between mobile access gateways within a proxy mobile IPv6 domain.  It defines two new signaling messages, Localized Routing Initiation (LRI) and Local Routing Acknowledgment (LRA), that are used to realize this mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Koodli¢kaffiliationcNECdnamekP. Loureiro¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfNIKSUNdnamehA. Duttaddatej”∂hfilenamegrfc6705eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128546hkeywordsÅfPMIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePZƒ6granking1crfcd6705irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Localized Routing for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) FORCERENEW allows for the reconfiguration of a single host by forcing the DHCP client into a Renew state on a trigger from the DHCP server.  In the Forcerenew Nonce Authentication protocol, the server sends a nonce to the client in the initial DHCP ACK that is used for subsequent validation of a FORCERENEW message.  This document updates RFC 3203. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehD. Miles¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefW. Dec¢kaffiliationsSwisscom Schweiz AGdnamejJ. Bristow¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekR. Maglioneddatej”›hfilenamegrfc6704eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128545hkeywordsÅdDHCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP}‘granking0crfcd6704irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexForcerenew Nonce AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyConsumers of IP network performance metrics have many different uses in mind.  This memo provides "long-term" reporting considerations (e.g., hours, days, weeks, or months, as opposed to 10 seconds), based on analysis of the points of view of two key audiences.  It describes how these audience categories affect the selection of metric parameters and options when seeking information that serves their needs.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameoG. Ramachandran¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamekG. Maguluriddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6703eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128544hkeywordsÖdLosseDelayoDelay VariationhCapacitycTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP+Nqgranking/crfcd6703irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBReporting IP Network Performance Metrics: Different Points of ViewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬The disclosure process for intellectual property rights (IPR) in documents produced within the IETF stream is essential to the accurate development of community consensus.  However, this process is not always followed by IETF participants.  Regardless of the cause or motivation, noncompliance with IPR disclosure rules can delay or even derail completion of IETF specifications.  This document describes some strategies for promoting compliance with the IPR disclosure rules.  These strategies are primarily intended for use by area directors, working group chairs, and working group secretaries.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polk¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenP. Saint-AndreddatejÕ^hfilenamegrfc6702eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128543hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP)¸úgranking.crfcd6702irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMPromoting Compliance with Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) Disclosure RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8717∂habstracty≤The IETF has developed and documented policies that govern the behavior of all IETF participants with respect to Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) about which they might reasonably be aware.

The IETF takes conformance to these IPR policies very seriously. However, there has been some ambiguity as to what the appropriate sanctions are for the violation of these policies, and how and by whom those sanctions are to be applied.

This document discusses these issues and provides a suite of potential actions that can be taken within the IETF community in cases related to patents. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejP. ResnickddatejÕhhfilenamegrfc6701eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128542hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP)¸õgranking-crfcd6701irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSanctions Available for Application to Violators of IETF IPR PolicydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêEncrypted communication on the Internet often uses Transport Layer Security (TLS), which depends on third parties to certify the keys used.  This document improves on that situation by enabling the administrators of domain names to specify the keys used in that domain's TLS servers.  This requires matching improvements in TLS client software, but no change in TLS server software. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationhKirei ABdnamekJ. Schlyterddatej‘áhfilenamegrfc6698eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128541hkeywordsÑfDNSSEClcertificateskpublic keyscPKIkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateP ≈∏granking*crfcd6698irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcThe DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol: TLSAdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8749d7218d7671∂habstracty√The Handover Keying (HOKEY) Working Group seeks to minimize handover delay due to authentication when a peer moves from one point of attachment to another. Work has progressed on two different approaches to reduce handover delay: early authentication (so that authentication does not need to be performed during handover), and reuse of cryptographic material generated during an initial authentication to save time during re-authentication. A basic assumption is that the mobile host or "peer" is initially authenticated using the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), executed between the peer and an EAP server as defined in RFC 3748.

This document defines the HOKEY architecture. Specifically, it describes design objectives, the functional environment within which handover keying operates, the functions to be performed by the HOKEY architecture itself, and the assignment of those functions to architectural components. It goes on to illustrate the operation of the architecture within various deployment scenarios that are described more fully in other documents produced by the HOKEY Working Group. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiT. Taylor¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationxMotorola Solutions, Inc.dnameiK. Hoeper¢kaffiliationmINSIDE SecurednamejS. Decugisddatej’hfilenamegrfc6697eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128540hkeywordsÉxHandover Keying ArchitectureqRe-authenticationtEarly authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePB[granking)crfcd6697irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Handover Keying (HOKEY) Architecture DesigndtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) is a generic framework supporting multiple types of authentication methods.  In systems where EAP is used for authentication, it is desirable to avoid repeating the entire EAP exchange with another authenticator.  This document specifies extensions to EAP and the EAP keying hierarchy to support an EAP method-independent protocol for efficient re- authentication between the peer and an EAP re-authentication server through any authenticator.  The re-authentication server may be in the home network or in the local network to which the peer is connecting. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefZ. Cao¢kaffiliationdCATRdnameeB. He¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Shi¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej‘5hfilenamegrfc6696eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128539hkeywordsÉjEAP keyingdEMSKxinter-authenticator roamingkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDatePBZgranking(crfcd6696irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;EAP Extensions for the EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThe Forward Error Correction (FEC) Framework document (RFC 6363) defines the FEC Framework Configuration Information necessary for the FEC Framework operation. This document describes how to use signaling protocols such as the Session Announcement Protocol (SAP), the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), the Real Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP), etc. for determining and communicating the configuration information between sender(s) and receiver(s).

This document doesn't define any new signaling protocol. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. AsatiddatejÕhfilenamegrfc6695eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128538hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP}”granking'crfcd6695irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTMethods to Convey Forward Error Correction (FEC) Framework Configuration InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document describes the "about" URI scheme, which is widely used by Web browsers and some other applications to designate access to their internal resources, such as settings, application information, hidden built-in functionality, and so on.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. MoonesamyddatejÃËhfilenamegrfc6694eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128537hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP)¸ôgranking&crfcd6694irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevThe "about" URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyñThis document is a product of the Delay Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group. No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised.

This document defines PRoPHET, a Probabilistic Routing Protocol using History of Encounters and Transitivity. PRoPHET is a variant of the epidemic routing protocol for intermittently connected networks that operates by pruning the epidemic distribution tree to minimize resource usage while still attempting to achieve the \%best-case routing capabilities of epidemic routing. It is intended for use in sparse mesh networks where there is no guarantee that a fully connected path between the source and destination exists at any time, rendering traditional routing protocols unable to deliver messages between hosts. These networks are examples of networks where there is a disparity between the latency requirements of applications and the capabilities of the underlying network (networks often referred to as delay and disruption tolerant). The document presents an architectural overview followed by the protocol specification. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian FarrelgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdSICSdnamekA. Lindgren¢kaffiliationnTechnicalitiesdnamehA. Doria¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationxLulea University of TechnologydnameiS. Grasicddatej‘Shfilenamegrfc6693eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128536hkeywordsÉcDTNgRoutinggPRoPHETkobsoletedByÄepagesqopublicationDateP@f©granking%crfcd6693irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexDProbabilistic Routing Protocol for Intermittently Connected NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÙThis document defines an additional header field for use in Abuse Reporting Format (ARF) reports to permit the identification of the source port of the connection involved in an abuse incident.

This document updates RFC 6591. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamejR. Clayton¢kaffiliationoCloudmark, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej‘úhfilenamegrfc6692eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128535hkeywordsÑcARFeportsireportinghfeedbackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP
Ygranking$crfcd6692irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Source Ports in Abuse Reporting Format (ARF) ReportsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‰This memo discusses what value to use with the TCP Maximum Segment Size (MSS) option, and updates RFC 879 and RFC 2385.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsQuantum CorporationdnameiD. BormanddatejÃËhfilenamegrfc6691eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128534hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9293epages	opublicationDatePBXgranking#crfcd6691irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*TCP Options and Maximum Segment Size (MSS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This specification defines Web Linking using a link format for use by constrained web servers to describe hosted resources, their attributes, and other relationships between links.  Based on the HTTP Link Header field defined in RFC 5988, the Constrained RESTful Environments (CoRE) Link Format is carried as a payload and is assigned an Internet media type. "RESTful" refers to the Representational State Transfer (REST) architecture.  A well-known URI is defined as a default entry point for requesting the links hosted by a server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiSensinodednameiZ. Shelbyddatej‘^hfilenamegrfc6690eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcoredfullx'core - Constrained RESTful Environmentsdnamex Constrained RESTful Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128533hkeywordsÑdCoREkLink FormatwHTTP Link Header FormatrResource DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP ≈∑granking"crfcd6690irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Constrained RESTful Environments (CoRE) Link FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) ASSOCIATION object is defined in the context of GMPLS-controlled label switched paths (LSPs).  In this context, the object is used to associate recovery LSPs with the LSP they are protecting.  This document reviews how the association is to be provided in the context of GMPLS recovery.  No new procedures or mechanisms are defined by this document, and it is strictly informative in nature.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Bergerddatej‘[hfilenamegrfc6689eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128532hkeywordsÅxResource Reservation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePBXgranking!crfcd6689irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Usage of the RSVP ASSOCIATION ObjectdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXParallel NFS (pNFS) extends the Network File System version 4 (NFSv4) to enable direct client access to file data on storage devices and bypass the NFSv4 server.  This can increase both performance and parallelism, but it requires additional client functionality, some of which depends upon the type of storage used.  The pNFS specification for block storage (RFC 5663) describes how clients can identify the volumes used for pNFS, but this mechanism requires communication with the NFSv4 server.  This document updates RFC 5663 to add a mechanism that enables identification of block storage devices used by pNFS file systems without communicating with the server.  This enables clients to control access to pNFS block devices when the client initially boots, as opposed to waiting until the client can communicate with the NFSv4 server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Glasgow¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamejS. Faibishddatej’hfilenamegrfc6688eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-128531hkeywordsÜj]--------]cNFSeNFSv4dpNFScSANcGPTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂ïgranking crfcd6688irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Parallel NFS (pNFS) Block Disk ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyGThis document presents a performance evaluation of the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) for a small outdoor deployment of sensor nodes and for a large-scale smart meter network.  Detailed simulations are carried out to produce several routing performance metrics using these real-life deployment scenarios.  Please refer to the PDF version of this document, which includes several plots for the performance metrics not shown in the plain-text version.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian FarrelgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqDrexel UniversitydnamekJ. Tripathi¢kaffiliationqDrexel UniversitydnamenJ. de Oliveira¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseurddatej‘¬hfilenamegrfc6687eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128530hkeywordsÅdROLLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePnã#grankingcrfcd6687irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexUPerformance Evaluation of the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$In 2006, the IETF published a suite of protocol documents comprising the Sender Policy Framework (SPF) and Sender ID: two proposed email authentication protocols. Both of these protocols enable one to publish, via the Domain Name System, a policy declaring which mail servers were authorized to send email on behalf of the domain name being queried. There was concern that the two would conflict in some significant operational situations, interfering with message delivery.

The IESG required all of these documents (RFC 4405, RFC 4406, RFC 4407, and RFC 4408) to be published as Experimental RFCs and requested that the community observe deployment and operation of the protocols over a period of two years from the date of publication to determine a reasonable path forward.

After six years, sufficient experience and evidence have been collected that the experiments thus created can be considered concluded. This document presents those findings. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’hfilenamegrfc6686eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfspfbisdfullsspfbis - SPF UpdatednamejSPF Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-128529hkeywordsÖcSPFiSender IDnauthenticationmauthorizationeemailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP
Ygrankingcrfcd6686irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIResolution of the Sender Policy Framework (SPF) and Sender ID ExperimentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This document specifies restrictions on additions to the subset of the IANA XML Namespace and Schema registries, to require Expert Review for extensions to Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammellddatej‘dhfilenamegrfc6685eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-128528hkeywordsÇdmilejxml schemakobsoletedByÅd7970epagesopublicationDateP∂ïgrankingcrfcd6685irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeExpert Review for Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) Extensions in IANA XML RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document provides guidelines for extensions to the Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) described in RFC 5070 for exchange of incident management data, and it contains a template for Internet-Drafts describing those extensions, in order to ease the work and improve the quality of extension descriptions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammellddatej‘Öhfilenamegrfc6684eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-128527hkeywordsÇdmileqincident handlingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂îgrankingcrfcd6684irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjGuidelines and Template for Defining Extensions to the Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjAnnex E of the Digital Video Broadcasting - IPTV (DVB-IPTV) technical specification defines an optional Application-Layer Forward Error Correction (AL-FEC) protocol to protect the streaming media transported using RTP.  The DVB-IPTV AL-FEC protocol uses two layers for FEC protection.  The first (base) layer is based on the 1-D interleaved parity code.  The second (enhancement) layer is based on the Raptor code.  By offering a layered approach, the DVB-IPTV AL-FEC protocol offers good protection against both bursty and random packet losses at a cost of decent complexity.  This document describes how one can implement the DVB-IPTV AL-FEC protocol by using the 1-D interleaved parity code and Raptor code that have already been specified in separate documents.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationnNomor ResearchdnamenT. Stockhammerddatej‘áhfilenamegrfc6683eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128526hkeywordsÜcDVBcFECfAL-FECdIPTVlparity codeslRaptor codeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP+Nqgrankingcrfcd6683irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexãGuidelines for Implementing Digital Video Broadcasting - IPTV (DVB-IPTV) Application-Layer Hybrid Forward Error Correction (FEC) ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document specifies an RTP payload format for the Forward Error Correction (FEC) repair data produced by the Raptor FEC Schemes.  Raptor FEC Schemes are specified for use with the IETF FEC Framework that supports the transport of repair data over both UDP and RTP.  This document specifies the payload format that is required for the use of RTP to carry Raptor repair flows. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationnNomor ResearchdnamenT. Stockhammer¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnamegM. LubyddatejÕhfilenamegrfc6682eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128525hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP+Nqgrankingcrfcd6682irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<RTP Payload Format for Raptor Forward Error Correction (FEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document describes Fully-Specified Forward Error Correction (FEC) Schemes for the Raptor and RaptorQ codes and their application to reliable delivery of media streams in the context of the FEC Framework.  The Raptor and RaptorQ codes are systematic codes, where a number of repair symbols are generated from a set of source symbols and sent in one or more repair flows in addition to the source symbols that are sent to the receiver(s) within a source flow.  The Raptor and RaptorQ codes offer close to optimal protection against arbitrary packet losses at a low computational complexity.  Six FEC Schemes are defined: two for the protection of arbitrary packet flows, two that are optimized for small source blocks, and two for the protection of a single flow that already contains a sequence number.  Repair data may be sent over arbitrary datagram transport (e.g., UDP) or using RTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgNetflixdnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationnNomor ResearchdnamenT. Stockhammer¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnamegM. LubyddatejÕ˙hfilenamegrfc6681eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128524hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP+Nqgrankingcrfcd6681irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Raptor Forward Error Correction (FEC) Schemes for FECFRAMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Generic Security Service Application Programming Interface (GSS-API) provides a simple naming architecture that supports name-based authorization.  This document introduces new APIs that extend the GSS-API naming model to support name attribute transfer between GSS-API peers.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqCryptonector, LLCdnamekN. Williams¢kaffiliationeSUNETdnamelL. Johansson¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. JosefssonddatejÃ‚hfilenamegrfc6680eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128523hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP?†Sgrankingcrfcd6680irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVGeneric Security Service Application Programming Interface (GSS-API) Naming ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis memo specifies how Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) can be used with the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) running over UDP, using the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) as a feedback mechanism.  It defines a new RTCP Extended Report (XR) block for periodic ECN feedback, a new RTCP transport feedback message for timely reporting of congestion events, and a Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) extension used in the optional initialisation method using Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE).  Signalling and procedures for negotiation of capabilities and initialisation methods are also defined. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelI. Johansson¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationtUniversity of OxforddnamekP. O'Hanlon¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlbergddatej‘0hfilenamegrfc6679eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128522hkeywordsácECNcRTPcUDPrCongestion ControldVoIPdIPTVkPacket LosskobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateP}”grankingcrfcd6679irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) for RTP over UDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8311∂habstracty∏This memo defines the requirements for a tunnel-based Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Method.  This tunnel method will use Transport Layer Security (TLS) to establish a secure tunnel.  The tunnel will provide support for password authentication, EAP authentication, and the transport of additional data for other purposes.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxMotorola Solutions, Inc.dnameiK. Hoeper¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehS. Hanna¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegH. Zhou¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejJ. SaloweyddatejÕ}hfilenamegrfc6678eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-128521hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePÒ<grankingcrfcd6678irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexORequirements for a Tunnel-Based Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx„This document defines how to implement channel bindings for Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) methods to address the "lying Network Access Service (NAS)" problem as well as the "lying provider" problem. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationmVirginia TechdnameiT. Clancy¢kaffiliationxMotorola Solutions, Inc.dnameiK. HoeperddatejÃ÷hfilenamegrfc6677eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-128520hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂îgrankingcrfcd6677irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLChannel-Binding Support for Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) MethodsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThis document discusses which multicast addresses should be used for documentation purposes and reserves multicast addresses for such use.  Some multicast addresses are derived from AS numbers or unicast addresses.  This document also explains how these can be used for documentation purposes.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Parekh¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoG. Van de Velde¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationwIformata CommunicationsdnamejM. EubanksddatejÃ⁄hfilenamegrfc6676eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-128519hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP37Hgrankingcrfcd6676irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Multicast Addresses for DocumentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document presents a conservative loss recovery algorithm for TCP that is based on the use of the selective acknowledgment (SACK) TCP option.  The algorithm presented in this document conforms to the spirit of the current congestion control specification (RFC 5681), but allows TCP senders to recover more effectively when multiple segments are lost from a single flight of data.  This document obsoletes RFC 3517 and describes changes from it. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationqPurdue UniversitydnamejE. Blanton¢kaffiliationdICSIdnameiM. Allman¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegL. Wang¢kaffiliationvUniversity of HelsinkidnamekI. Jarvinen¢kaffiliationvUniversity of HelsinkidnamegM. Kojo¢kaffiliationlWIDE ProjectdnamejY. Nishidaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6675eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128518hkeywordsÉxtransmission control protocolnretransmissionrcongestion controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP8}:grankingcrfcd6675irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWA Conservative Loss Recovery Algorithm Based on Selective Acknowledgment (SACK) for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGateway-Initiated Dual-Stack Lite (GI-DS-Lite) is a variant of Dual- Stack Lite (DS-Lite) applicable to certain tunnel-based access architectures.  GI-DS-Lite extends existing access tunnels beyond the access gateway to an IPv4-IPv4 NAT using softwires with an embedded Context Identifier that uniquely identifies the end-system to which the tunneled packets belong.  The access gateway determines which portion of the traffic requires NAT using local policies and sends/ receives this portion to/from this softwire. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Brockners¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamekS. Speicher¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wardddatej’hfilenamegrfc6674eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-128517hkeywordsÖjGI-DS-Litex!Gateway Initiated Dual-Stack LiteoDual-Stack LiterIPv6 TransitioningnIPv6 MigrationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP
Ygrankingcrfcd6674irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Gateway-Initiated Dual-Stack Lite DeploymentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAMany user applications (and the transport protocols that make them possible) require two-way communications. To assess this capability, and to achieve test system simplicity, round-trip loss measurements are frequently conducted in practice. The Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol specified in RFC 5357 establishes a round-trip loss measurement capability for the Internet. However, there is currently no round-trip packet loss metric specified according to the RFC 2330 framework.

This memo adds round-trip loss to the set of IP Performance Metrics (IPPM). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6673eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128516hkeywordsÇbIPdIPPMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP+Nqgrankingcrfcd6673irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRound-Trip Packet Loss MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\The DNAME record provides redirection for a subtree of the domain name tree in the DNS.  That is, all names that end with a particular suffix are redirected to another part of the DNS.  This document obsoletes the original specification in RFC 2672 as well as updates the document on representing IPv6 addresses in DNS (RFC 3363). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. Rose¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamemW. WijngaardsddatejÕÃhfilenamegrfc6672eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128515hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOﬁ◊]grankingcrfcd6672irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNAME Redirection in the DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@This document assigns a Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) Type for carrying ITU-T MPLS Transport Profile Operations, Administration, and Management (MPLS-TP OAM) messages in the MPLS Generic Associated Channel.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamehM. BettsddatejÕ˚hfilenamegrfc6671eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128514hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP≠·Mgrankingcrfcd6671irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâAllocation of a Generic Associated Channel Type for ITU-T MPLS Transport Profile Operation, Maintenance, and Administration (MPLS-TP OAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is a profile of the MPLS technology for use in transport network deployments. The work on MPLS-TP has extended the MPLS technology with additional architectural elements and functions that can be used in any MPLS deployment. MPLS-TP is a set of functions and features selected from the extended MPLS toolset and applied in a consistent way to meet the needs and requirements of operators of packet transport networks.

During the process of development of the profile, additions to the MPLS toolset have been made to ensure that the tools available met the requirements. These additions were motivated by MPLS-TP, but form part of the wider MPLS toolset such that any of them could be used in any MPLS deployment.

One major set of additions provides enhanced support for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM). This enables fault management and performance monitoring to the level needed in a transport network. Many solutions and protocol extensions have been proposed to address the requirements for MPLS-TP OAM, and this document sets out the reasons for selecting a single, coherent set of solutions for standardization. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekN. Sprecher¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehKY. HongddatejÕ˘hfilenamegrfc6670eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128513hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateP®grankingcrfcd6670irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇThe Reasons for Selecting a Single Solution for MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†This document provides an overview of the Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) toolset for MPLS-based transport networks.  The toolset consists of a comprehensive set of fault management and performance monitoring capabilities (operating in the data plane) that are appropriate for transport networks as required in RFC 5860 and support the network and services at different nested levels.  This overview includes a brief recap of the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) OAM requirements and functions and the generic mechanisms created in the MPLS data plane that allow the OAM packets to run in-band and share their fate with data packets.  The protocol definitions for each of the MPLS-TP OAM tools are defined in separate documents (RFCs or Working Group documents), which are referenced by this document.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekN. Sprecher¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegL. FangddatejÕ˙hfilenamegrfc6669eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128512hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP®grankingcrfcd6669irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnAn Overview of the Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Toolset for MPLS-Based Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines algorithm names and parameters for use in some of the SHA-2 family of secure hash algorithms for data integrity verification in the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.  It also updates RFC 4253 by specifying a new RECOMMENDED data integrity algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoBitvise LimiteddnamehD. Bider¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejM. BaushkeddatejÃ&hfilenamegrfc6668eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128511hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO˜‰Ygrankingcrfcd6668irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUSHA-2 Data Integrity Verification for the Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Layer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝The "Typed Wildcard Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) Element" defines an extension to the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) that can be used when requesting, withdrawing, or releasing all label bindings for a given FEC Element type is desired.  However, a Typed Wildcard FEC Element must be individually defined for each FEC Element type.  This specification defines the Typed Wildcard FEC Elements for the Pseudowire Identifier (PWid) (0x80) and Generalized PWid (0x81) FEC Element types. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. PignataroddatejÃhfilenamegrfc6667eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128510hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂îgrankingcrfcd6667irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbLDP 'Typed Wildcard' Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) for PWid and Generalized PWid FEC ElementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥Remote triggered black hole filtering describes a method of mitigating the effects of denial-of-service attacks by selectively discarding traffic based on source or destination address.  Remote triggered black hole routing describes a method of selectively re- routing traffic into a sinkhole router (for further analysis) based on destination address.  This document updates the "IPv6 Special Purpose Address Registry" by explaining why a unique IPv6 prefix should be formally assigned by IANA for the purpose of facilitating IPv6 remote triggered black hole filtering and routing.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdINEXdnamekN. Hilliard¢kaffiliationhClaranetdnamekD. Freedmanddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6666eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128509hkeywordsÇdRTBHjblack holekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP}“granking
