estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA Discard Prefix for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This document describes an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) defined by RFC 3261. The purpose of this extension is to provide an extensible framework by which SIP nodes can request notification from remote nodes indicating that certain events have occurred.

Note that the event notification mechanisms defined herein are NOT intended to be a general-purpose infrastructure for all classes of event subscription and notification.

This document represents a backwards-compatible improvement on the original mechanism described by RFC 3265, taking into account several years of implementation experience. Accordingly, this document obsoletes RFC 3265. This document also updates RFC 4660 slightly to accommodate some small changes to the mechanism that were discussed in that document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnamejA.B. Roachddatej’hfilenamegrfc6665eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128508hkeywordsÉiSUBSCRIBEfNOTIFYestatekobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateO˚ÿ;granking	crfcd6665irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSIP-Specific Event NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7621∂habstracty!This document defines a set of Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Capability types for ASN.1 encoding for the current set of public keys defined by the PKIX working group.  This facilitates the ability for a requester to specify information on the public keys and signature algorithms to be used in responses. "Online Certificate Status Protocol Algorithm Agility" (RFC 6277) details an example of where this is used.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej‘9hfilenamegrfc6664eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-128507hkeywordsÇdOCSPcCMSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂îgrankingcrfcd6664irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.S/MIME Capabilities for Public Key DefinitionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvPre-Congestion Notification (PCN) is a means for protecting quality of service for inelastic traffic admitted to a Diffserv domain.  The overall PCN architecture is described in RFC 5559.  This memo describes the requirements for the signaling applied within the PCN-domain: (1) PCN-feedback-information is carried from the PCN-egress-node to the Decision Point; (2) the Decision Point may ask the PCN-ingress-node to measure, and report back, the rate of sent PCN-traffic between that PCN-ingress-node and PCN-egress-node.  The Decision Point may be either collocated with the PCN-ingress-node or a centralized node (in the first case, (2) is not required).  The signaling requirements pertain in particular to two edge behaviors, Controlled Load (CL) and Single Marking (SM).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of TwentednamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameiT. Taylor¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TuebingendnamehM. Menth¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. EardleyddatejÕÌhfilenamegrfc6663eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcndfullx0pcn - Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdnamex*Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128506hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂ìgrankingcrfcd6663irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMRequirements for Signaling of Pre-Congestion Information in a Diffserv DomaindtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXPre-Congestion Notification (PCN) is a means for protecting the quality of service for inelastic traffic admitted to a Diffserv domain.  The overall PCN architecture is described in RFC 5559.  This memo is one of a series describing possible boundary-node behaviors for a PCN-domain.  The behavior described here is that for a form of measurement-based load control using two PCN marking states: not- marked and excess-traffic-marked.  This behavior is known informally as the Single Marking (SM) PCN-boundary-node behavior.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Charny¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehJ. Zhang¢kaffiliationtUniversity of TwentednamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TuebingendnamehM. Menth¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. Taylorddatej’hfilenamegrfc6662eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcndfullx0pcn - Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdnamex*Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128505hkeywordsÑcPCNnsingle markingbSMredge node behaviorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂ìgrankingcrfcd6662irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfPre-Congestion Notification (PCN) Boundary-Node Behavior for the Single Marking (SM) Mode of OperationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynPre-Congestion Notification (PCN) is a means for protecting the quality of service for inelastic traffic admitted to a Diffserv domain.  The overall PCN architecture is described in RFC 5559.  This memo is one of a series describing possible boundary-node behaviors for a PCN-domain.  The behavior described here is that for a form of measurement-based load control using three PCN marking states: not- marked, threshold-marked, and excess-traffic-marked.  This behavior is known informally as the Controlled Load (CL) PCN-boundary-node behavior.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Charny¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehF. Huang¢kaffiliationtUniversity of TwentednamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TuebingendnamehM. Menth¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. Taylorddatej’hfilenamegrfc6661eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcndfullx0pcn - Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdnamex*Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128504hkeywordsÑcPCNocontrolled loadbCLvboundary node behaviorkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateP∂ìgrankingcrfcd6661irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgPre-Congestion Notification (PCN) Boundary-Node Behavior for the Controlled Load (CL) Mode of OperationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThe objective of Pre-Congestion Notification (PCN) is to protect the quality of service (QoS) of inelastic flows within a Diffserv domain. The overall rate of PCN-traffic is metered on every link in the PCN- domain, and PCN-packets are appropriately marked when certain configured rates are exceeded. Egress nodes pass information about these PCN-marks to Decision Points that then decide whether to admit or block new flow requests or to terminate some already admitted flows during serious pre-congestion.

This document specifies how PCN-marks are to be encoded into the IP header by reusing the Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) codepoints within a PCN-domain. The PCN wire protocol for non-IP protocol headers will need to be defined elsewhere. Nonetheless, this document clarifies the PCN encoding for MPLS in an informational appendix. The encoding for IP provides for up to three different PCN marking states using a single Diffserv codepoint (DSCP): not-marked (NM), threshold-marked (ThM), and excess-traffic-marked (ETM). Hence, it is called the 3-in-1 PCN encoding. This document obsoletes RFC 5696. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamelT. Moncaster¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TuebingendnamehM. Menthddatej’hfilenamegrfc6660eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcndfullx0pcn - Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdnamex*Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128503hkeywordsårQuality of ServicecQoSrCongestion ControlwCongestion NotificationjTunnellingxEncapsulation & DecapsulationwDifferentiated ServicessIntegrated ServicesjSignallinghProtocolvFlow Admission ControlpFlow TerminationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂ìgrankingcrfcd6660irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqEncoding Three Pre-Congestion Notification (PCN) States in the IP Header Using a Single Diffserv Codepoint (DSCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThe Rapid Acquisition of Multicast RTP Sessions (RAMS) solution is a method based on RTP and the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) that enables an RTP receiver to rapidly acquire and start consuming the RTP multicast data.  Upon a request from the RTP receiver, an auxiliary unicast RTP retransmission session is set up between a retransmission server and the RTP receiver, over which the reference information about the new multicast stream the RTP receiver is about to join is transmitted at an accelerated rate.  This often precedes, but may also accompany, the multicast stream itself.  When there is only one multicast stream to be acquired, the RAMS solution works in a straightforward manner.  However, when there are two or more multicast streams to be acquired from the same or different multicast RTP sessions, care should be taken to configure each RAMS session appropriately.  This document provides example scenarios and discusses how the RAMS solution could be used in such scenarios.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begenddatej‘/hfilenamegrfc6659eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128502hkeywordsÉdIPTVcFECnretransmissionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO˜‰Ygrankingcrfcd6659irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZConsiderations for Deploying the Rapid Acquisition of Multicast RTP Sessions (RAMS) MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document describes a pseudowire mechanism that is used to transport a packet service over an MPLS PSN in the case where the client Label Switching Router (LSR) and the server Provider Edge equipments are co-resident in the same equipment.  This pseudowire mechanism may be used to carry all of the required layer 2 and layer 3 protocols between the pair of client LSRs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationvVerizon CommunicationsdnamehA. MalisddatejÕ˘hfilenamegrfc6658eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128501hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂ìgrankingcrfcd6658irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Packet Pseudowire Encapsulation over an MPLS PSNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxøThis document changes RFC 2046 rules regarding default "charset" parameter values for "text/*" media types to better align with common usage by existing clients and servers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej’hfilenamegrfc6657eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128500hkeywordsÉdMIMEgcharsetdtextkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO˚ÿ:grankingcrfcd6657irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLUpdate to MIME regarding "charset" Parameter Handling in Textual Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThis memo documents a DHCPv4 option that currently exists and was previously privately defined for the operation and usage of the Cisco Systems' Subnet Allocation Option for DHCPv4.  The option is passed between the DHCPv4 Client and the DHCPv4 Server to request dynamic allocation of a subnet, give specifications of the subnet(s) allocated, and report usage statistics.  This memo documents the current usage of the option in agreement with RFC 3942, which declares that any preexisting usages of option numbers in the range 128-223 should be documented and that the working group will try to officially assign those numbers to those options.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Johnson¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. StappddatejÕ¯hfilenamegrfc6656eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128499hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP
Ygranking␀crfcd6656irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADescription of Cisco Systems' Subnet Allocation Option for DHCPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This memo describes the use of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) in the Counter with Cipher Block Chaining - Message Authentication Code (CBC-MAC) Mode (CCM) of operation within Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram TLS (DTLS) to provide confidentiality and data origin authentication.  The AES-CCM algorithm is amenable to compact implementations, making it suitable for constrained environments. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationxRSA / Security Division of EMCdnameiD. Baileyddatej’hfilenamegrfc6655eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128498hkeywordsÉnAuthenticationjEncryptionx"Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatePßgrankingˇcrfcd6655irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8AES-CCM Cipher Suites for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty⁄This document proposes an alternative IPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures (6rd) deployment model to that of RFC 5969.  The basic 6rd model allows IPv6 hosts to gain access to IPv6 networks across an IPv4 access network using 6-in-4 tunnels.  6rd requires support by a device (the 6rd customer edge, or 6rd-CE) on the customer site, which must also be assigned an IPv4 address.  The alternative model described in this document initiates the 6-in-4 tunnels from an operator-owned Gateway collocated with the operator's IPv4 network edge rather than from customer equipment, and hence is termed "Gateway-initiated 6rd" (GI 6rd).  The advantages of this approach are that it requires no modification to customer equipment and avoids assignment of IPv4 addresses to customer equipment.  The latter point means less pressure on IPv4 addresses in a high-growth environment.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegC. Zhou¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. Taylor¢kaffiliationmChina TelecomdnamegQ. Chenddatej‘Òhfilenamegrfc6654eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128497hkeywordsÅoIPv6 transitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÚükgranking˛crfcd6654irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHGateway-Initiated IPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures (GI 6rd)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyPAs interest in IPv6 deployment in cellular networks increases, several migration issues have been being raised; IPv6 prefix management is the issue addressed in this document.  Based on the idea that DHCPv6 servers can manage prefixes, we use DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation to address such prefix management issues as an access router offloading delegation of prefixes and release tasks to a DHCPv6 server.  The access router first requests a prefix for an incoming mobile node from the DHCPv6 server.  The access router may next do stateless or stateful address allocation to the mobile node, e.g., with a Router Advertisement or using DHCP.  We also describe prefix management using Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) servers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamefF. Xia¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamehT. LemonddatejÕ¥hfilenamegrfc6653eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128496hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO˜‰Ygranking˝crfcd6653irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex>DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation in Long-Term Evolution (LTE) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This memo presents extensions to the Abuse Reporting Format (ARF) and Sender Policy Framework (SPF) specifications to allow for detailed reporting of message authentication failures in an on-demand fashion.

This memo updates RFC 4408 by providing an IANA registry for SPF modifiers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAgaridnamelS. Kittermanddatej‘™hfilenamegrfc6652eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmarfdfullx'marf - Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdnamex Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-128495hkeywordsÉefraudhphishinghspoofingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÈcgranking¸crfcd6652irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Sender Policy Framework (SPF) Authentication Failure Reporting Using the Abuse Reporting FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∆This document presents extensions to the DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) specification to allow for detailed reporting of message authentication failures in an on-demand fashion. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej‘¨hfilenamegrfc6651eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmarfdfullx'marf - Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdnamex Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-128494hkeywordsÑnauthenticationefraudhphishinghspoofingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÈcgranking˚crfcd6651irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEExtensions to DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) for Failure ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚRFC 5965 defines an extensible, machine-readable format intended for mail operators to report feedback about received email to other parties.  This applicability statement describes common methods for utilizing this format for reporting both abuse and authentication failure events.  Mailbox Providers of any size, mail-sending entities, and end users can use these methods as a basis to create procedures that best suit them.  Some related optional mechanisms are also discussed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkReturn PathdnamegJ. Falk¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej”åhfilenamegrfc6650eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmarfdfullx'marf - Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdnamex Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-128493hkeywordsÇdmarfnspam reportingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÈcgranking˙crfcd6650irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkCreation and Use of Email Feedback Reports: An Applicability Statement for the Abuse Reporting Format (ARF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractycThe Kerberos 5 network authentication protocol, originally specified in RFC 1510, can use the Data Encryption Standard (DES) for encryption.  Almost 30 years after first publishing DES, the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) finally withdrew the standard in 2005, reflecting a long-established consensus that DES is insufficiently secure.  By 2008, commercial hardware costing less than USD 15,000 could break DES keys in less than a day on average.  DES is long past its sell-by date.  Accordingly, this document updates RFC 1964, RFC 4120, RFC 4121, and RFC 4757 to deprecate the use of DES, RC4-HMAC-EXP, and other weak cryptographic algorithms in Kerberos.  Because RFC 1510 (obsoleted by RFC 4120) supports only DES, this document recommends the reclassification of RFC 1510 as Historic.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnametL. Hornquist Astrand¢kaffiliationwMIT Kerberos ConsortiumdnameeT. YuddatejÃhfilenamegrfc6649eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128492hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÚükgranking˘crfcd6649irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber≥etotaletitlexPDeprecate DES, RC4-HMAC-EXP, and Other Weak Cryptographic Algorithms in KerberosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty␀Historically, designers and implementers of application protocols have often distinguished between standardized and unstandardized parameters by prefixing the names of unstandardized parameters with the string "X-" or similar constructs.  In practice, that convention causes more problems than it solves.  Therefore, this document deprecates the convention for newly defined parameters with textual (as opposed to numerical) names in application protocols.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationiRackspacednamemM. NottinghamddatejÀÂhfilenamegrfc6648eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128491hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÈcgranking¯crfcd6648irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber≤etotaletitlexKDeprecating the "X-" Prefix and Similar Constructs in Application ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document describes the art of email greylisting, the practice of providing temporarily degraded service to unknown email clients as an anti-abuse mechanism.

Greylisting is an established mechanism deemed essential to the repertoire of current anti-abuse email filtering systems. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawy¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej”ôhfilenamegrfc6647eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128490hkeywordsÉeEmailkGreylistingdSpamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOﬁ◊]granking˜crfcd6647irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Email Greylisting: An Applicability Statement for SMTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$Peer-to-peer (P2P) applications have become widely used on the Internet today and make up a large portion of the traffic in many networks.  In P2P applications, one technique for reducing the transit and uplink P2P traffic is to introduce storage capabilities within the network.  Traditional caches (e.g., P2P and Web caches) provide such storage, but they can be complex (e.g., P2P caches need to explicitly support individual P2P application protocols), and do not allow users to manage resource usage policies for content in the cache.  This document discusses the introduction of in-network storage for P2P applications and shows the need for a standard protocol for accessing this storage.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegH. Song¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegN. Zong¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yang¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehR. Alimiddatej”ˆhfilenamegrfc6646eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdecadedfullx+decade - Decoupled Application Data Enroutednamex"Decoupled Application Data Enroutedtypebwgbidjdoc-128489hkeywordsÇrIn-network storagecP2PkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO˚ÿ:grankingˆcrfcd6646irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=DECoupled Application Data Enroute (DECADE) Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document provides a methodology and framework for quantifying the performance impact of the monitoring of IP flows on a network device and the export of this information to a Collector.  It identifies the rate at which the IP flows are created, expired, and successfully exported as a new performance metric in combination with traditional throughput.  The metric is only applicable to the devices compliant with RFC 5470, "Architecture for IP Flow Information Export".  The methodology quantifies the impact of the IP flow monitoring process on the network equipment.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehJ. Novakddatej”Hhfilenamegrfc6645eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128488hkeywordsÑkPerformanceoFlow monitoringeIPFIXgNetflowkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateO˝)˚grankingıcrfcd6645irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBIP Flow Information Accounting and Export Benchmarking MethodologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document updates RFC 3315 (DHCPv6) to allow the Rebind message type to appear in the Reconfigure Message option of a Reconfigure message.  It extends the Reconfigure message to allow a DHCPv6 server to cause a DHCPv6 client to send a Rebind message.  The document also clarifies how a DHCPv6 client responds to a received Reconfigure message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmIPfonix, Inc.dnamehD. Evans¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiangddatej’hfilenamegrfc6644eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128487hkeywordsÉqinternet protocoljparametersiaddresseskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateO˝)˙grankingÙcrfcd6644irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Rebind Capability in DHCPv6 Reconfigure MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVYANG is a data modeling language used to model configuration and state data manipulated by the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF), NETCONF remote procedure calls, and NETCONF notifications.  The Structure of Management Information (SMIv2) defines fundamental data types, an object model, and the rules for writing and revising MIB modules for use with the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).  This document defines a translation of SMIv2 MIB modules into YANG modules, enabling read-only (config false) access to data objects defined in SMIv2 MIB modules via NETCONF. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej”hfilenamegrfc6643eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128486hkeywordsÉeSMIv2dYANGmdata modelingkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateO˚ÿ:grankingÛcrfcd6643irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Translation of Structure of Management Information Version 2 (SMIv2) MIB Modules to YANG ModulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'In a large RTP session using the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) feedback mechanism defined in RFC 4585, a feedback target may experience transient overload if some event causes a large number of receivers to send feedback at once.  This overload is usually avoided by ensuring that feedback reports are forwarded to all receivers, allowing them to avoid sending duplicate feedback reports.  However, there are cases where it is not recommended to forward feedback reports, and this may allow feedback implosion.  This memo discusses these cases and defines a new RTCP Third-Party Loss Report that can be used to inform receivers that the feedback target is aware of some loss event, allowing them to suppress feedback.  Associated Session Description Protocol (SDP) signaling is also defined. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefF. Xia¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegR. Evenddatej’
hfilenamegrfc6642eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128485hkeywordsÉtFeedback SuppressiondNACKnRetransmissionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO÷ÓZgrankingÚcrfcd6642irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extension for a Third-Party Loss ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe NFS version 4 (NFSv4) protocol provides a mechanism for a collection of NFS file servers to collaborate in providing an organization-wide file namespace.  The DNS SRV Resource Record (RR) allows a simple way for an organization to publish the root of its file system namespace, even to clients that might not be intimately associated with such an organization.  The DNS SRV RR can be used to join these organization-wide file namespaces together to allow construction of a global, uniform NFS file namespace. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamekC. Everhart¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamejW. Adamson¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehJ. Zhangddatej’hfilenamegrfc6641eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-128484hkeywordsÇjdomainrootwdomain root file systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÈcgrankingÒcrfcd6641irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCUsing DNS SRV to Specify a Global File Namespace with NFS Version 4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†This document attempts to provide a list of the frequently asked questions (FAQs) posed by IETF meeting attendees regarding travel logistics and local information.  It is intended to assist those who are willing to provide local information, so that if they wish to pre-populate answers to some or all of these questions either in the IETF wiki or a meeting-specific site, they have a reasonably complete list of ideas to draw from.  It is not meant as a list of required information that the host or Secretariat needs to provide; it merely serves as a guideline.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiW. Georgeddatej’hfilenamegrfc6640eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128483hkeywordsÅhMeetingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÈcgrankingcrfcd6640irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;IETF Meeting Attendees' Frequently Asked (Travel) QuestionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(A range of Management Information Base (MIB) modules has been developed to help model and manage the various aspects of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) networks. These MIB modules are defined in separate documents that focus on the specific areas of responsibility of the modules that they describe.

The MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is a profile of MPLS functionality specific to the construction of packet-switched transport networks.

This document describes the MIB-based architecture for MPLS-TP, indicates the interrelationships between different existing MIB modules that can be leveraged for MPLS-TP network management, and identifies areas where additional MIB modules are required. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationgAricentdnamemM. VenkatesanddatejÕ’hfilenamegrfc6639eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128482hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOﬁ◊\grankingÔcrfcd6639irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWMultiprotocol Label Switching Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) MIB-Based Management OverviewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document defines extensions to the Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) "calendar-access" feature to specify a standard way of performing scheduling operations with iCalendar-based calendar components.  This document defines the "calendar-auto-schedule" feature of CalDAV. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationfOraclednameoB. Desruisseauxddatej‘˛hfilenamegrfc6638eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128481hkeywordsçgcalsifyhcalschedfcalschhcalendarkcalendaringfwebcaldicaliicalendariischeduleditipdimipmtext/calendardhttpkobsoletedByÄepagesNopublicationDateOﬁ◊\grankingÓcrfcd6638irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexScheduling Extensions to CalDAVdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7953∂habstractyºThis document defines an Elliptic Curve Cryptography extension to the OpenPGP public key format and specifies three Elliptic Curves that enjoy broad support by other standards, including standards published by the US National Institute of Standards and Technology.  The document specifies the conventions for interoperability between compliant OpenPGP implementations that make use of this extension and these Elliptic Curves. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtSymantec CorporationdnameiA. JivsovddatejÕ⁄hfilenamegrfc6637eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-128480hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9580epagesopublicationDateO÷ÓZgrankingÌcrfcd6637irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) in OpenPGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘The Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) are the protocols used by hosts and multicast routers to exchange their IP multicast group memberships with each other.  This document describes ways to achieve IGMPv3 and MLDv2 protocol optimization for mobility and aims to become a guideline for the tuning of IGMPv3/MLDv2 Queries, timers, and counter values.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoKeio UniversitydnameiH. Asaeda¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefH. Liu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej‘Ôhfilenamegrfc6636eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmultimobdfullxmultimob - Multicast MobilitydnamerMulticast Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128479hkeywordsÇhMobilityfPMIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO∑JgrankingÏcrfcd6636irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexóTuning the Behavior of the Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) for Routers in Mobile and Wireless NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty|The RFC Editor model described in this document divides the responsibilities for the RFC Series into three functions: the RFC Series Editor, the RFC Production Center, and the RFC Publisher.  Internet Architecture Board (IAB) oversight via the RFC Series Oversight Committee (RSOC) is described, as is the relationship between the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) and the RSOC.  This document reflects the experience gained with "RFC Editor Model (Version 1)", documented in RFC 5620, and obsoletes that document.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej”dhfilenamegrfc6635eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128478hkeywordsÜqRFC Series EditorxIndependenet Series EditorcRSEcISEdRSOCxRFC Series Oversight CommitteekobsoletedByÅd8728epagesopublicationDateO÷ÓYgrankingÎcrfcd6635irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexRFC Editor Model (Version 2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx•This document formally deprecates the use of ICMP Source Quench messages by transport protocols, formally updating RFC 792, RFC 1122, and RFC 1812. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUTN-FRH / SI6 NetworksdnamegF. Gontddatej‘≈hfilenamegrfc6633eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128477hkeywordsárcongestion controllicmp attacksctcpltcp securitycudpddccpdsctpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≈ mgrankingÈcrfcd6633irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Deprecation of ICMP Source Quench MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document gives an overview of the IETF network management standards and summarizes existing and ongoing development of IETF Standards Track network management protocols and data models.  The document refers to other overview documents, where they exist and classifies the standards for easy orientation.  The purpose of this document is, on the one hand, to help system developers and users to select appropriate standard management protocols and data models to address relevant management needs.  On the other hand, the document can be used as an overview and guideline by other Standard Development Organizations or bodies planning to use IETF management technologies and data models.  This document does not cover Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) technologies on the data-path, e.g., OAM of tunnels, MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) OAM, and pseudowire as well as the corresponding management models.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehM. Ersue¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej‘√hfilenamegrfc6632eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128476hkeywordsÜrnetwork managementjdata modeljmonitoringmconfigurationealarmlnotificationkobsoletedByÄepagesUopublicationDateO…ø@grankingËcrfcd6632irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4An Overview of the IETF Network Management StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThe Internet Key Exchange protocol version 2 (IKEv2) does not allow secure peer authentication when using short credential strings, i.e., passwords.  Several proposals have been made to integrate password-authentication protocols into IKE.  This document provides an adaptation of Password Authenticated Connection Establishment (PACE) to the setting of IKEv2 and demonstrates the advantages of this integration.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamejD. Kuegler¢kaffiliationhPorticordnamejY. Shefferddatej‘»hfilenamegrfc6631eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128475hkeywordsÇdpacex/password authenticated connection establishmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOﬁ◊\grankingÁcrfcd6631irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiPassword Authenticated Connection Establishment with the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) is a generic framework supporting multiple types of authentication methods.

The EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP) specifies extensions to EAP and the EAP keying hierarchy to support an EAP method-independent protocol for efficient re-authentication between the peer and an EAP re-authentication server through any authenticator.

Authenticated Anticipatory Keying (AAK) is a method by which cryptographic keying material may be established upon one or more Candidate Attachment Points (CAPs) prior to handover. AAK uses the AAA infrastructure for key transport.

This document specifies the extensions necessary to enable AAK support in ERP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefZ. Cao¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej’hfilenamegrfc6630eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128474hkeywordsÑcERPcAAKcEAPtEarly-authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO…ø@grankingÊcrfcd6630irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYEAP Re-authentication Protocol Extensions for Authenticated Anticipatory Keying (ERP/AAK)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document discusses some considerations on the applicability of the level 3 multihoming Shim protocol for IPv6 (Shim6) and associated support protocols and mechanisms to provide site multihoming capabilities in IPv6.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamerA. Garcia-Martinezddatej‘Âhfilenamegrfc6629eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128473hkeywordsÖx#Cryptographically Generated AddresscCGArHash-Based AddresscHBAoFault tolerancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO÷ÓYgrankingÂcrfcd6629irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Considerations on the Application of the Level 3 Multihoming Shim Protocol for IPv6 (Shim6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes an efficient augmented password-only authentication and key exchange (AugPAKE) protocol where a user remembers a low-entropy password and its verifier is registered in the intended server.  In general, the user password is chosen from a small set of dictionary words that allows an attacker to perform exhaustive searches (i.e., off-line dictionary attacks).  The AugPAKE protocol described here is secure against passive attacks, active attacks, and off-line dictionary attacks (on the obtained messages with passive/active attacks), and also provides resistance to server compromise (in the context of augmented PAKE security).  In addition, this document describes how the AugPAKE protocol is integrated into the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2).  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAISTdnamegS. Shin¢kaffiliationdAISTdnameiK. Kobaraddatej‘’hfilenamegrfc6628eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128472hkeywordsÑdPAKEnaugmented PAKExoff-line dictionary attacksxresistance to server compromisekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO…ø@granking‰crfcd6628irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKEfficient Augmented Password-Only Authentication and Key Exchange for IKEv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThe objective of Pre-Congestion Notification (PCN) is to protect the quality of service (QoS) of inelastic flows within a Diffserv domain. On every link in the PCN-domain, the overall rate of PCN-traffic is metered, and PCN-packets are appropriately marked when certain configured rates are exceeded. Egress nodes provide decision points with information about the PCN-marks of PCN-packets that allows them to take decisions about whether to admit or block a new flow request, and to terminate some already admitted flows during serious \%pre-congestion.

The PCN working group explored a number of approaches for encoding this pre-congestion information into the IP header. This document provides details of those approaches along with an explanation of the constraints that apply to any solution. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of TwentednamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamelT. Moncaster¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TuebingendnamehM. Menth¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. BriscoeddatejÕLhfilenamegrfc6627eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcndfullx0pcn - Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdnamex*Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128471hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateP∂ígranking„crfcd6627irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Overview of Pre-Congestion Notification EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx◊The base Network Mobility for Mobile IPv4 (NEMOv4) specification defines extensions to Mobile IPv4 for mobile networks.  This specification defines a dynamic prefix allocation mechanism for NEMOv4. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamegV. Park¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamelV. Narayanan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehK. Leungddatej‘Æhfilenamegrfc6626eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-128470hkeywordsÅmmobile routerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≈ mgranking‚crfcd6626irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGDynamic Prefix Allocation for Network Mobility for Mobile IPv4 (NEMOv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇIn Multicast Virtual Private Networks (MVPNs), customer multicast flows are carried in "tunnels" through a service provider's network.  The base specifications for MVPN define BGP multicast VPN "auto-discovery routes" and specify how to use an auto-discovery route to advertise the fact that an individual customer multicast flow is being carried in a particular tunnel.  However, those specifications do not provide a way to specify, in a single such route, that multiple customer flows are being carried in a single tunnel.  Those specifications also do not provide a way to advertise that a particular tunnel is to be used by default to carry all customer flows, except in the case where that tunnel is joined by all the provider edge routers of the MVPN.  This document eliminates these restrictions by specifying the use of "wildcard" elements in the customer flow identifiers.  With wildcard elements, a single auto-discovery route can refer to multiple customer flows or even to all customer flows. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelW. Hendrickx¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefR. Qiuddatej’hfilenamegrfc6625eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128469hkeywordsÅdmvpnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO∑Jgranking·crfcd6625irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Wildcards in Multicast VPN Auto-Discovery RoutesdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7582d7900d8534∂habstractyHLayer 2 Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs) based on Frame Relay or ATM circuits have been around a long time; more recently, Ethernet VPNs, including Virtual Private LAN Service, have become popular.  Traditional L2VPNs often required a separate Service Provider infrastructure for each type and yet another for the Internet and IP VPNs.  In addition, L2VPN provisioning was cumbersome.  This document presents a new approach to the problem of offering L2VPN services where the L2VPN customer's experience is virtually identical to that offered by traditional L2VPNs, but such that a Service Provider can maintain a single network for L2VPNs, IP VPNs, and the Internet, as well as a common provisioning methodology for all services.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejB. Kothari¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelR. Cherukuriddatej‘‚hfilenamegrfc6624eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128468hkeywordsÖcBGPeL2VPNidiscoveryisignalingjpseudowirekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO∑Jgranking‡crfcd6624irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKLayer 2 Virtual Private Networks Using BGP for Auto-Discovery and SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxßThis document creates an IANA registry for the response codes for the MEDIACTRL Interactive Voice Response Control Package, as described in RFC 6231. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuGeorgetown UniversitydnameiE. BurgerddatejÕ4hfilenamegrfc6623eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128467hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≤ıgrankingﬂcrfcd6623irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFIANA Registry for MEDIACTRL Interactive Voice Response Control PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸThis document describes general and flexible TLVs for representing cryptographic Integrity Check Values (ICVs) (i.e., digital signatures or Message Authentication Codes (MACs)) as well as timestamps, using the generalized Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) packet/message format defined in RFC 5444.  It defines two Packet TLVs, two Message TLVs, and two Address Block TLVs for affixing ICVs and timestamps to a packet, a message, and an address, respectively. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxFujitsu Laboratories of AmericadnamejU. Herberg¢kaffiliationxLIX / Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6622eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128466hkeywordsÜhpacketbbdNHDPfOLSRv2hsecurityiintegritygroutingkobsoletedByÅd7182epagesopublicationDateO≤ıgrankingﬁcrfcd6622irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWIntegrity Check Value and Timestamp TLV Definitions for Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document describes a Simplified Multicast Forwarding (SMF) mechanism that provides basic Internet Protocol (IP) multicast forwarding suitable for limited wireless mesh and mobile ad hoc network (MANET) use.  It is mainly applicable in situations where efficient flooding represents an acceptable engineering design trade-off.  It defines techniques for multicast duplicate packet detection (DPD), to be applied in the forwarding process, for both IPv4 and IPv6 protocol use.  This document also specifies optional mechanisms for using reduced relay sets to achieve more efficient multicast data distribution within a mesh topology as compared to Classic Flooding.  Interactions with other protocols, such as use of information provided by concurrently running unicast routing protocols or interaction with other multicast protocols, as well as multiple deployment approaches are also described.  Distributed algorithms for selecting reduced relay sets and related discussion are provided in the appendices.  Basic issues relating to the operation of multicast MANET border routers are discussed, but ongoing work remains in this area and is beyond the scope of this document.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNRLdnameiJ. Mackerddatej‘êhfilenamegrfc6621eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128465hkeywordsçgroutinghfloodingroptimized floodingcCDSxconnected dominating setxduplicate packet detectionxhash-based packet detectioncMPRgMPR-CDSeE-CDSmedge mobilitymmobile ad hoclmesh networkkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateO∑Jgranking›crfcd6621irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSimplified Multicast ForwardingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis memo describes First-Come, First-Served Source Address Validation Improvement (FCFS SAVI), a mechanism that provides source address validation for IPv6 networks using the FCFS principle.  The proposed mechanism is intended to complement ingress filtering techniques to help detect and prevent source address spoofing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamepE. Levy-Abegnoliddatej’hfilenamegrfc6620eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsavidfullx-savi - Source Address Validation Improvementsdnamex&Source Address Validation Improvementsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128464hkeywordsÇqingress filteringeBCP38kobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateO®…»granking‹crfcd6620irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmFCFS SAVI: First-Come, First-Served Source Address Validation Improvement for Locally Assigned IPv6 AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÿThis document explains how to employ address translation in networks that serve a large number of individual customers without requiring a correspondingly large amount of private IPv4 address space. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekM. Townsleyddatej‘	hfilenamegrfc6619eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128463hkeywordsÉcNATdIPv4dIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateOﬁ◊\granking€crfcd6619irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEScalable Operation of Address Translators with Per-Interface BindingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMobile IPv6 signaling between a Mobile Node (MN) and its Home Agent (HA) is secured using IPsec.  The security association (SA) between an MN and the HA is established using Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE) version 1 or 2.  The security model specified for Mobile IPv6, which relies on IKE/IPsec, requires interaction between the Mobile IPv6 protocol component and the IKE/IPsec module of the IP stack.  This document proposes an alternate security framework for Mobile IPv6 and Dual-Stack Mobile IPv6, which relies on Transport Layer Security for establishing keying material and other bootstrapping parameters required to protect Mobile IPv6 signaling and data traffic between the MN and HA.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamenD. Kroeselbergddatej‘Ïhfilenamegrfc6618eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-128462hkeywordsÇkMobile IPv6hSecuritykobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateO≈ mgranking⁄crfcd6618irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvMobile IPv6 Security Framework Using Transport Layer Security for Communication between the Mobile Node and Home AgentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This memo describes a secure pre-shared key (PSK) authentication method for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE).  It is resistant to dictionary attack and retains security even when used with weak pre-shared keys.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Harkinsddatej‘Ÿhfilenamegrfc6617eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128461hkeywordsÇxAuthenticated Key ExchangeqDictionary AttackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO…ø?grankingŸcrfcd6617irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWSecure Pre-Shared Key (PSK) Authentication for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞OpenID has found its usage on the Internet for Web Single Sign-On.  Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) and the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) are application frameworks to generalize authentication.  This memo specifies a SASL and GSS-API mechanism for OpenID that allows the integration of existing OpenID Identity Providers with applications using SASL and GSS-API. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejH. Mauldin¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefssonddatej’hfilenamegrfc6616eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128460hkeywordsÅrweb single sign-onkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≈ mgrankingÿcrfcd6616irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexãA Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) and Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Mechanism for OpenIDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This document defines managed objects for IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX).  These objects provide information for monitoring IPFIX Exporters and IPFIX Collectors, including basic configuration information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamehT. Dietz¢kaffiliationkNTT PF LabsdnamelA. Kobayashi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationx Technische Universitaet MuenchendnamehG. Muenzddatej‘ÿhfilenamegrfc6615eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128459hkeywordsÖeIPFIXcMIBiFilteringhSamplingiSelectionkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateO÷ÓYgranking◊crfcd6615irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Definitions of Managed Objects for IP Flow Information ExportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx”This document specifies a transport profile for RADIUS using Transport Layer Security (TLS) over TCP as the transport protocol.  This enables dynamic trust relationships between RADIUS servers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgRESTENAdnameiS. Winter¢kaffiliationcOSCdnamekM. McCauley¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekK. Wierengaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6614eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128458hkeywordsÖfRADIUScAAAhSecuritykReliabilityx)Remote Authentication Dial-In User ServerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≈ mgranking÷crfcd6614irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Transport Layer Security (TLS) Encryption for RADIUSdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9765d8996∂habstracty3The Remote Authentication Dial-In User Server (RADIUS) protocol has, until now, required the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) as the underlying transport layer.  This document defines RADIUS over the Transmission Control Protocol (RADIUS/TCP), in order to address handling issues related to RADIUS over Transport Layer Security (RADIUS/TLS).  It permits TCP to be used as a transport protocol for RADIUS only when a transport layer such as TLS or IPsec provides confidentiality and security.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatej‘≈hfilenamegrfc6613eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128457hkeywordsÉx)Remote Authentication Dial-In User ServerxTransmission Control ProtocoljRADIUS/TCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≈ mgranking’crfcd6613irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleoRADIUS over TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7930d9765∂habstracty¨The use of Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) and Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) in the same network requires some care.  This document discusses scenarios where such mixed usage is appropriate and points out the need for interaction between the two mechanisms.  Solutions and recommendations to enable these scenarios are also described.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. GiarettaddatejÕuhfilenamegrfc6612eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128456hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≤ıgranking‘crfcd6612irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeInteractions between Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) and Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6): Scenarios and Related IssuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMobile IPv6 bootstrapping can be categorized into two primary scenarios: the split scenario and the integrated scenario.  In the split scenario, the mobile node's mobility service is authorized by a different service authorizer than the network access authorizer.  In the integrated scenario, the mobile node's mobility service is authorized by the same service authorizer as the network access service authorizer.  This document defines a method for home agent information discovery for the integrated scenario. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxRadio Mobile Access, Inc.dnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. YeginddatejÃ^hfilenamegrfc6611eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-128455hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≤ıgranking”crfcd6611irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) Bootstrapping for the Integrated ScenariodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=This document defines a DHCP-based scheme to enable dynamic discovery of Mobile IPv6 home network information.  New DHCP options are defined that allow a mobile node to request the home agent IP address, Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN), or home network prefix and obtain it via the DHCP response. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeKISTIdnamegH. Jang¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. Yegin¢kaffiliationxRadio Mobile Access, Inc.dnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamegJ. Choi¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamehT. LemonddatejÃâhfilenamegrfc6610eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-128454hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≤Ùgranking“crfcd6610irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDHCP Options for Home Information Discovery in Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThe Sieve Email Filtering "include" extension permits users to include one Sieve script inside another.  This can make managing large scripts or multiple sets of scripts much easier, and allows a site and its users to build up libraries of scripts.  Users are able to include their own personal scripts or site-wide scripts. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationkSerendipitydnamehA. Stoneddatej…‘hfilenamegrfc6609eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-128453hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≤Ûgranking—crfcd6609irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Sieve Email Filtering: Include ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines several subcodes for the BGP Finite State Machine (FSM) Error that could provide more information to help network operators in diagnosing BGP FSM issues and correlating network events.  This document updates RFC 4271. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dong¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamepA. SuryanarayanaddatejÕÔhfilenamegrfc6608eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128452hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO®…»granking–crfcd6608irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Subcodes for BGP Finite State Machine ErrordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis memo defines a DHCPv4 Virtual Subnet Selection (VSS) option, a DHCPv6 VSS option, and the DHCPv4 VSS and VSS-Control sub-options carried in the DHCPv4 Relay Agent Information option. These are intended for use by DHCP clients, relay agents, and proxy clients in situations where VSS information needs to be passed to the DHCP server for proper address or prefix allocation to take place.

For the DHCPv4 option and Relay Agent Information sub-options, this memo documents and extends existing usage as per RFC 3942. This memo updates RFC 3046 regarding details relating to the copying of sub- options (see Section 8).fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejR. Johnson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. StappddatejÃyhfilenamegrfc6607eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128451hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOû=qgrankingœcrfcd6607irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Virtual Subnet Selection Options for DHCPv4 and DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs) are formed by devices that are compatible with the IEEE 802.15.4 standard. However, neither the IEEE 802.15.4 standard nor the 6LoWPAN format specification defines how mesh topologies could be obtained and maintained. Thus, it should be considered how 6LoWPAN formation and multi-hop routing could be supported.

This document provides the problem statement and design space for 6LoWPAN routing. It defines the routing requirements for 6LoWPANs, considering the low-power and other particular characteristics of the devices and links. The purpose of this document is not to recommend specific solutions but to provide general, layer-agnostic guidelines about the design of 6LoWPAN routing that can lead to further analysis and protocol design. This document is intended as input to groups working on routing protocols relevant to 6LoWPANs, such as the IETF ROLL WG. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamefE. Kim¢kaffiliationxSimula Research LaboratorydnameiD. Kaspar¢kaffiliationx3Universitat Politecnica de Catalunya/Fundacio i2CATdnamehC. Gomez¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6606eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymg6lowpandfullx"6lowpan - IPv6 over Low power WPANdnamexIPv6 over Low power WPANdtypebwgbidjdoc-128450hkeywordsécWSNnSensor NetworkwWireless Sensor NetworkxWSN for Wireless Sensor Networkx L3 Mesh for Layer 3 Mesh NetworkpRouting ProtocolnSubnet Routingmieee 802.15.4cLLNiLow Powernradio 802.15.4ipowerlinejISA100.11ahRFC 4944kobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateO≈ lgrankingŒcrfcd6606irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkProblem Statement and Requirements for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Network (6LoWPAN) RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document describes how to specify Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) keys and signatures in DNS Security (DNSSEC).  It lists curves of different sizes and uses the SHA-2 family of hashes for signatures. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnameqW.C.A. Wijngaardsddatej’hfilenamegrfc6605eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128449hkeywordsäpdnskey algorithmlds hash algofcryptogDNS keypDNSKEY algorithmbDSxdigest hash cryptographygSHA-384oECDSAP256SHA256oECDSAP384SHA384kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOá‘ugrankingÕcrfcd6605irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThe Domain Name System (DNS) has long provided means, such as the CNAME (Canonical Name), whereby a DNS query can be redirected to a different name.  A DNS response header has an RCODE (Response Code) field, used for indicating errors, and response status bits.  This document clarifies, in the case of such redirected queries, how the RCODE and status bits correspond to the initial query cycle (where the CNAME or the like was detected) and subsequent or final query cycles. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej”hfilenamegrfc6604eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128448hkeywordsácDNSfDNSSECeCNAMEeDNAMEkDomain Namemresponse codencanonical namekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOâ%¬grankingÃcrfcd6604irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)xNAME RCODE and Status Bits ClarificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÿThis specification defines an optional mechanism to allow exclusion of one specific prefix from a delegated prefix set when using DHCPv6-based prefix delegation.  The new mechanism updates RFC 3633. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamehO. Troanddatej’
hfilenamegrfc6603eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128447hkeywordsÅqOPTION_PD_EXCLUDEkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateO≥U∞grankingÀcrfcd6603irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Prefix Exclude Option for DHCPv6-based Prefix DelegationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïFor extending the lifetime of a mobility session, the Proxy Mobile IPv6 specification requires the mobile access gateway to send a Proxy Binding Update message to the local mobility anchor on a per-session basis.  In the absence of signaling semantics for performing operations with group-specific scope, this results in a significant amount of signaling traffic on a periodic basis between a given mobile access gateway and a local mobility anchor.  This document defines optimizations to the binding update and revocation operations in Proxy Mobile IPv6 for performing operations with group-specific scope with the use of a group identifier. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxInstituto Nokia de TecnologiadnamekF. Abinader¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiD. Premecddatej’	hfilenamegrfc6602eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128446hkeywordsÑqProxy Mobile IPv6fPMIPv6rbulk registrationskMN group IDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≤Ûgranking crfcd6602irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Bulk Binding Update Support for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¥This document presents a generic connection admission control (GCAC) reference model and algorithm for IP-/MPLS-based networks.  Service provider (SP) IP/MPLS networks need an MPLS GCAC mechanism, as one motivational example, to reject Voice over IP (VoIP) calls when additional calls would adversely affect calls already in progress.  Without MPLS GCAC, connections on congested links will suffer degraded quality.  The MPLS GCAC algorithm can be optionally implemented in vendor equipment and deployed by service providers.  MPLS GCAC interoperates between vendor equipment and across multiple service provider domains.  The MPLS GCAC algorithm uses available standard mechanisms for MPLS-based networks, such as RSVP, Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering (DS-TE), Path Computation Element (PCE), Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS), Diffserv, and OSPF.  The MPLS GCAC algorithm does not include aspects of CAC that might be considered vendor proprietary implementations, such as detailed path selection mechanisms.  MPLS GCAC functions are implemented in a distributed manner to deliver the objective Quality of Service (QoS) for specified QoS constraints.  The objective is that the source is able to compute a source route with high likelihood that via-elements along the selected path will in fact admit the request.  In some cases (e.g., multiple Autonomous Systems (ASes)), this objective cannot always be met, but this document summarizes methods that partially meet this objective.  MPLS GCAC is applicable to any service or flow that must meet an objective QoS (delay, jitter, packet loss rate) for a specified quantity of traffic.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefG. Ash¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamejD. McDysanddatejÕÔhfilenamegrfc6601eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128445hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateOû=qgranking…crfcd6601irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXGeneric Connection Admission Control (GCAC) Algorithm Specification for IP/MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÅThis document requests the allocation of an IPv4 /10 address block to be used as Shared Address Space to accommodate the needs of Carrier- Grade NAT (CGN) devices. It is anticipated that Service Providers will use this Shared Address Space to number the interfaces that connect CGN devices to Customer Premises Equipment (CPE).

Shared Address Space is distinct from RFC 1918 private address space because it is intended for use on Service Provider networks. However, it may be used in a manner similar to RFC 1918 private address space on routing equipment that is able to do address translation across router interfaces when the addresses are identical on two different interfaces. Details are provided in the text of this document.

This document details the allocation of an additional special-use IPv4 address block and updates RFC 5735. This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednamegJ. Weil¢kaffiliationuRogers CommunicationsdnamelV. Kuarsingh¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiC. Donley¢kaffiliationmTelstra Corp.dnameoC. Liljenstolpe¢kaffiliationwFrontier CommunicationsdnamejM. Azingerddatej’
hfilenamegrfc6598eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128444hkeywordsâlshared blockcCGNcNATqCarrier Grade NATuprivate address spacepservice providersaddress translationunon-globally routablexnon-overlapping address spacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOí`*granking∆crfcd6598irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberôetotaletitlex2IANA-Reserved IPv4 Prefix for Shared Address SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the payload format for packetization of KLV (Key-Length-Value) Encoded Data, as defined by the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) in SMPTE ST 336, into the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxPAR Government Systems Corp.dnamehJ. Downs¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Arbeiterddatej’hfilenamegrfc6597eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128443hkeywordsÅcKLVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOá‘tgranking≈crfcd6597irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeRTP Payload Format for Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) ST 336 Encoded DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrRFC 5988 specifies a way to define relationships between links on the web.  This document describes a new type of such a relationship, "canonical", to designate an Internationalized Resource Identifier (IRI) as preferred over resources with duplicative content.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegM. Ohye¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. KupkeddatejÕÛhfilenamegrfc6596eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128442hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÎgrankingƒcrfcd6596irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Canonical Link RelationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThe Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) has found its usage on the Internet for Web Single Sign-On.  The Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) and the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) are application frameworks to generalize authentication.  This memo specifies a SASL mechanism and a GSS-API mechanism for SAML 2.0 that allows the integration of existing SAML Identity Providers with applications using SASL and GSS-API. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekK. Wierenga¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefssonddatej’
hfilenamegrfc6595eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128441hkeywordsÇx6Generic Security Service Application Program InterfacehSAML 2.0kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOâ%¬granking√crfcd6595irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyA Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) and GSS-API Mechanism for the Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document updates the IANA registries in RFC 4255, which defines SSHFP, a DNS Resource Record (RR) that contains a standard Secure Shell (SSH) key fingerprint used to verify SSH host keys using DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC).  This document defines additional options supporting SSH public keys applying the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) and the implementation of fingerprints computed using the SHA-256 message digest algorithm in SSHFP Resource Records. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfCZ.NICdnamegO. Suryddatej’	hfilenamegrfc6594eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128440hkeywordsÜcDNSrDomain Name SystemeSSHFPgSHA-256lSecure ShelleECDSAkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateOÎgranking¬crfcd6594irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇUse of the SHA-256 Algorithm with RSA, Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA), and Elliptic Curve DSA (ECDSA) in SSHFP Resource RecordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyWith the ubiquitous success of service discovery techniques, curious clients are faced with an increasing overload of service instances and options listed when they browse for services. A typical domain may contain web servers, remote desktop servers, printers, file servers, video content servers, automatons, Points of Presence using artificial intelligence, etc., all advertising their presence. Unsurprisingly, it is expected that some protocols and services will choose the comfort of anonymity and avoid discovery.

This memo describes a new experimental protocol for this purpose utilizing the Domain Pseudonym System (DPS), and discusses strategies for its successful implementation and deployment. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiJ. Clarke¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelG. SalgueiroddatejÕ…hfilenamegrfc6593eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-46138hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOyS∂granking¡crfcd6593irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPService Undiscovery Using Hide-and-Go-Seek for the Domain Pseudonym System (DPS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThe ever-elusive Null Packet received numerous mentions in documents in the RFC series, but it has never been explicitly defined.  This memo corrects that omission.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. PignataroddatejÕ¿hfilenamegrfc6592eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-46137hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOySµgranking¿crfcd6592irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitleoThe Null PacketdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯This memo registers an extension report type for the Abuse Reporting Format (ARF), affecting multiple registries, for use in generating receipt-time reports about messages that fail one or more email message authentication checks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejH. Fontanaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6591eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmarfdfullx'marf - Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdnamex Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-128439hkeywordsÜdauthlauth failureddkimcspfdAFRFcARFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOâ%¬grankingøcrfcd6591irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAAuthentication Failure Reporting Using the Abuse Reporting FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9991d6692∂habstractyÅEmail messages often contain information that might be considered private or sensitive, per either regulation or social norms.  When such a message becomes the subject of a report intended to be shared with other entities, the report generator may wish to redact or elide the sensitive portions of the message.  This memo suggests one method for doing so effectively. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkReturn PathdnamegJ. Falk¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’	hfilenamegrfc6590eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmarfdfullx'marf - Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdnamex Messaging Abuse Reporting Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-128438hkeywordsÑcARFdMARFmfeedback loopnspam reportingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÎgrankingæcrfcd6590irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Redaction of Potentially Sensitive Data from Mail Abuse ReportsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document describes considerations for the transition of end-user content on the Internet to IPv6.  While this is tailored to address end-user content, which is typically web-based, many aspects of this document may be more broadly applicable to the transition to IPv6 of other applications and services.  This document explores the challenges involved in the transition to IPv6, potential migration tactics, possible migration phases, and other considerations.  The audience for this document is the Internet community generally, particularly IPv6 implementers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. LivingoodddatejÕhfilenamegrfc6589eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128437hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOí`*grankingΩcrfcd6589irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Considerations for Transitioning Content to IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for ucode, an identifier system for objects and places.  ucode technology is used in many applications, and this document provides a URN namespace for ucode to enable its use in Internet-related devices and software.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxYRP Ubiquitous Networking LabdnamekC. IshikawaddatejÕÌhfilenamegrfc6588eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128436hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÎgrankingºcrfcd6588irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA URN Namespace for ucodedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`There have been many implementations and deployments of legacy syslog over TCP for many years.  That protocol has evolved without being standardized and has proven to be quite interoperable in practice.  This memo describes how TCP has been used as a transport for syslog messages.  This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamenBeno√Æt Claisedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlAdiscon GmbHdnamekR. Gerhards¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejC. Lonvickddatej’
hfilenamegrfc6587eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128435hkeywordsÇfSYSLOGtSYSLOG transport TCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOí`)grankingªcrfcd6587irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Transmission of Syslog Messages over TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document discusses our experiences from moving a small number of users to an IPv6-only network, with access to the IPv4-only parts of the Internet via a NAT64 device.  The document covers practical experiences as well as roadblocks and opportunities for this type of a network setup.  The document also makes some recommendations about where such networks are applicable and what should be taken into account in the network design.  The document also discusses further work that is needed to make IPv6-only networking applicable in all environments.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6586eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128434hkeywordsÇdIPv6eNAT64kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOí`)granking∫crfcd6586irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Experiences from an IPv6-Only NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxàThis document specifies additional HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) status codes for a variety of common situations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiRackspacednamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekR. Fieldingddatej’hfilenamegrfc6585eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128433hkeywordsÅxHypertext Transfer ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateOû=pgrankingπcrfcd6585irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAdditional HTTP Status CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document introduces four schemes that provide per-packet authentication, integrity, and anti-replay services in the context of the Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) and NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) protocols.  The first scheme is based on RSA Digital Signatures.  The second scheme relies on the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA).  The third scheme relies on a Group- keyed Message Authentication Code (MAC).  Finally, the fourth scheme merges the Digital Signature and group schemes.  These schemes have different target use cases, and they do not all provide the same service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Rocaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6584eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128432hkeywordsÇeTESLAeFLUTEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOâ%¬granking∏crfcd6584irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Simple Authentication Schemes for the Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) and NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[In IPv4, subnets are generally small, made just large enough to cover the actual number of machines on the subnet. In contrast, the default IPv6 subnet size is a /64, a number so large it covers trillions of addresses, the overwhelming number of which will be unassigned. Consequently, simplistic implementations of Neighbor Discovery (ND) can be vulnerable to deliberate or accidental denial of service (DoS), whereby they attempt to perform address resolution for large numbers of unassigned addresses. Such denial-of-service attacks can be launched intentionally (by an attacker) or result from legitimate operational tools or accident conditions. As a result of these vulnerabilities, new devices may not be able to "join" a network, it may be impossible to establish new IPv6 flows, and existing IPv6 transported flows may be interrupted.

This document describes the potential for DoS in detail and suggests possible implementation improvements as well as operational mitigation techniques that can, in some cases, be used to protect against or at least alleviate the impact of such attacks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfYahoo!dnamelI. Gashinsky¢kaffiliationeZyngadnamejJ. Jaeggli¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnameiW. KumariddatejÕÒhfilenamegrfc6583eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128431hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOrºXgranking∑crfcd6583irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Operational Neighbor Discovery ProblemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿRFC 5681 documents the following four intertwined TCP congestion control algorithms: slow start, congestion avoidance, fast retransmit, and fast recovery.  RFC 5681 explicitly allows certain modifications of these algorithms, including modifications that use the TCP Selective Acknowledgment (SACK) option (RFC 2883), and modifications that respond to "partial acknowledgments" (ACKs that cover new data, but not all the data outstanding when loss was detected) in the absence of SACK.  This document describes a specific algorithm for responding to partial acknowledgments, referred to as "NewReno".  This response to partial acknowledgments was first proposed by Janey Hoe.  This document obsoletes RFC 3782. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfBoeingdnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationdICSIdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OuludnameiA. Gurtov¢kaffiliationlWIDE ProjectdnamejY. Nishidaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6582eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128430hkeywordsÉtcongestion avoidancercongestion controlofast retransmitkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÎgranking∂crfcd6582irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The NewReno Modification to TCP's Fast Recovery AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThis document updates RFC 5043 and RFC 5044 by extending Marker Protocol Data Unit (PDU) Aligned Framing (MPA) negotiation for Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) connection establishment.  The first enhancement extends RFC 5044, enabling peer-to-peer connection establishment over MPA / Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).  The second enhancement extends both RFC 5043 and RFC 5044, by providing an option for standardized exchange of RDMA-layer connection configuration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiDell Inc.dnamekA. Kanevsky¢kaffiliationoNexenta SystemsdnamejC. Bestler¢kaffiliationeInteldnamehR. Sharp¢kaffiliationsOpen Grid ComputingdnamegS. WiseddatejÕËhfilenamegrfc6581eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128429hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÎgrankingµcrfcd6581irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDEnhanced Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) Connection EstablishmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”The original RFCs that specified the Remote Direct Data Placement (RDDP) protocol suite did not create IANA registries for RDDP error codes, operation codes, and function codes.  Extensions to the RDDP protocols now require these registries to be created.  This memo creates the RDDP registries, populates them with values defined in the original RDDP RFCs, and provides guidance to IANA for future assignment of code points within these registries. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerMartin Stiemerlingdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameeM. Ko¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. BlackddatejÕÎhfilenamegrfc6580eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128428hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateOÎgranking¥crfcd6580irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEIANA Registries for the Remote Direct Data Placement (RDDP) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∆This document specifies the 'disclosure' link relation type.  It designates a list of IPR disclosures made with respect to the material for which such a relation type is specified. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM. YevstifeyevddatejÕÎhfilenamegrfc6579eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128427hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOql␀granking≥crfcd6579irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#The 'disclosure' Link Relation TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxºThis specification defines an extension to Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) that allows efficient synchronization of the contents of a WebDAV collection. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekA. Quillaudddatej’hfilenamegrfc6578eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128426hkeywordsÇosync-collectionjsync-tokenkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOi‚≥granking≤crfcd6578irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPCollection Synchronization for Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˙This memo updates the registry of authentication method results in Authentication-Results: message header fields, correcting a discontinuity between the original registry creation and the Sender Policy Framework (SPF) specification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCloudmark, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’	hfilenamegrfc6577eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128425hkeywordsÇcSPFnAuthenticationkobsoletedByÅd7001epagesopublicationDateOql␀granking±crfcd6577irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTAuthentication-Results Registration Update for Sender Policy Framework (SPF) ResultsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty≥This document specifies tests to determine if multiple independent instantiations of a performance-metric RFC have implemented the specifications in the same way.  This is the performance-metric equivalent of interoperability, required to advance RFCs along the Standards Track.  Results from different implementations of metric RFCs will be collected under the same underlying network conditions and compared using statistical methods.  The goal is an evaluation of the metric RFC itself to determine whether its definitions are clear and unambiguous to implementors and therefore a candidate for advancement on the IETF Standards Track.  This document is an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geib¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationtCariden TechnologiesdnameiR. Fardid¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamelA. Steinmitzddatej’hfilenamegrfc6576eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128424hkeywordsÖqinter-operabilitykequivalencekmeasurementjcompliancefmetrickobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateOql␀granking∞crfcd6576irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber∞etotaletitlex:IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) Standard Advancement TestingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThe Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS), detailed in RFC 4664, provides point-to-point connections between pairs of Customer Edge (CE) devices.  It does so by binding two Attachment Circuits (each connecting a CE device with a Provider Edge (PE) device) to a pseudowire (connecting the two PEs).  In general, the Attachment Circuits must be of the same technology (e.g., both Ethernet or both ATM), and the pseudowire must carry the frames of that technology.  However, if it is known that the frames' payload consists solely of IP datagrams, it is possible to provide a point-to-point connection in which the pseudowire connects Attachment Circuits of different technologies.  This requires the PEs to perform a function known as "Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) Mediation".  ARP Mediation refers to the process of resolving Layer 2 addresses when different resolution protocols are used on either Attachment Circuit.  The methods described in this document are applicable even when the CEs run a routing protocol between them, as long as the routing protocol runs over IP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCienadnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehG. Heron¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekV. KompelladdatejÕÍhfilenamegrfc6575eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128423hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO÷ÓXgrankingØcrfcd6575irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOAddress Resolution Protocol (ARP) Mediation for IP Interworking of Layer 2 VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty:This document provides an overview of a workshop held by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) on 'Interconnecting Smart Objects with the Internet'. The workshop took place in Prague on 25 March 2011. The main goal of the workshop was to solicit feedback from the wider community on their experience with deploying IETF protocols in constrained environments. This report summarizes the discussions and lists the conclusions and recommendations to the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) community.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect IAB views and positions. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Arkkoddatej’hfilenamegrfc6574eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128422hkeywordsÇmSmart ObjectsrInternet of ThingskobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateOá‘tgrankingÆcrfcd6574irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex%Report from the Smart Object WorkshopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`RFC 5988 standardized a means of indicating the relationships between resources on the Web.  This specification defines a pair of reciprocal link relation types that may be used to express the relationship between a collection and its members.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekBarry Leibadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. AmundsenddatejÕÈhfilenamegrfc6573eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128421hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÎgranking≠crfcd6573irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&The Item and Collection Link RelationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document defines new attributes to facilitate Proxy Mobile IPv6 operations using the RADIUS infrastructure.  The protocol defined in this document uses RADIUS-based interfaces of the mobile access gateway and the local mobility anchor with the AAA server for authentication, authorization, and policy functions.  The RADIUS interactions between the mobile access gateway and the RADIUS-based AAA server take place when the mobile node (MN) attaches, authenticates, and authorizes to a Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain.  Furthermore, this document defines the RADIUS-based interface between the local mobility anchor and the AAA RADIUS server for authorizing received Proxy Binding Update messages for the mobile node's mobility session.  In addition to the interactions related to mobility session setup, this document defines the baseline for the mobile access gateway and the local mobility anchor generated accounting. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamefF. Xia¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamehD. DamicddatejÕÏhfilenamegrfc6572eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128420hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateO÷ÓXgranking¨crfcd6572irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$RADIUS Support for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8044∂habstracty‡In this document, we analyze the applicability of the Loop-Free Alternate (LFA) method of providing IP fast reroute in both the core and access parts of Service Provider networks.  We consider both the link and node failure cases, and provide guidance on the applicability of LFAs to different network topologies, with special emphasis on the access parts of the network.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationxInstitute IMDEA NetworksdnamekP. Francois¢kaffiliation`dnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Uttaro¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamelM. Hornefferddatej’hfilenamegrfc6571eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128419hkeywordsÖoIP Fast ReroutesRouting ConvergencepNetwork TopologyeIS-ISdOSPFkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateO÷ÓXgranking´crfcd6571irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexILoop-Free Alternate (LFA) Applicability in Service Provider (SP) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRA URI Template is a compact sequence of characters for describing a range of Uniform Resource Identifiers through variable expansion.  This specification defines the URI Template syntax and the process for expanding a URI Template into a URI reference, along with guidelines for the use of URI Templates on the Internet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekJ. Gregorio¢kaffiliationeAdobednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationeMITREdnameiM. Hadley¢kaffiliationiRackspacednamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliationnSalesforce.comdnamejD. Orchardddatej’hfilenamegrfc6570eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128418hkeywordsáhtemplatexUniform Resource IdentifiercURIlURI Templatex%Internationalized Resource IdentifiercIRIlIRI TemplatekobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateOrºXgranking™crfcd6570irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlelURI TemplatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document provides general guidelines for work on developing and specifying an interactive audio codec within the IETF.  These guidelines cover the development process, evaluation, requirements conformance, and intellectual property issues.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnameiJM. Valin¢kaffiliationjSPIRIT DSPdnamejS. Borilin¢kaffiliation`dnamefK. Vos¢kaffiliationsXiph.Org FoundationdnamemC. Montgomery¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamegR. Chenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6569eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecodecdfullx%codec - Internet Wideband Audio CodecdnamexInternet Wideband Audio Codecdtypebwgbidjdoc-128417hkeywordsÇkaudio codeclspeech codeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOrºWgranking©crfcd6569irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Guidelines for Development of an Audio Codec within the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document investigates potential application scenarios and use cases for low-power wireless personal area networks (LoWPANs).  This document provides dimensions of design space for LoWPAN applications.  A list of use cases and market domains that may benefit and motivate the work currently done in the 6LoWPAN Working Group is provided with the characteristics of each dimension.  A complete list of practical use cases is not the goal of this document.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamefE. Kim¢kaffiliationxSimula Research LaboratorydnameiD. Kaspar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. VasseurddatejÕ„hfilenamegrfc6568eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymg6lowpandfullx"6lowpan - IPv6 over Low power WPANdnamexIPv6 over Low power WPANdtypebwgbidjdoc-128416hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOû=pgranking®crfcd6568irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Design and Application Spaces for IPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This document introduces the transport of call control User-to-User Information (UUI) using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and develops several requirements for a new SIP mechanism.  Some SIP sessions are established by or related to a non-SIP application.  This application may have information that needs to be transported between the SIP User Agents during session establishment.  In addition to interworking with the Integrated Services Digital Network (ISDN) UUI Service, this extension will also be used for native SIP endpoints requiring application UUI.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamehL. LiessddatejÕÂhfilenamegrfc6567eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcussdfullx'cuss - Call Control UUI Service for SIPdnamex Call Control UUI Service for SIPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128415hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÎgrankingßcrfcd6567irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Problem Statement and Requirements for Transporting User-to-User Call Control Information in SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöAs an optical signal progresses along its path, it may be altered by the various physical processes in the optical fibers and devices it encounters. When such alterations result in signal degradation, these processes are usually referred to as "impairments". These physical characteristics may be important constraints to consider when using a GMPLS control plane to support path setup and maintenance in wavelength switched optical networks.

This document provides a framework for applying GMPLS protocols and the Path Computation Element (PCE) architecture to support Impairment-Aware Routing and Wavelength Assignment (IA-RWA) in wavelength switched optical networks. Specifically, this document discusses key computing constraints, scenarios, and architectural processes: routing, wavelength assignment, and impairment validation. This document does not define optical data plane aspects; impairment parameters; or measurement of, or assessment and qualification of, a route; rather, it describes the architectural and information components for protocol solutions. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemG. MartinelliddatejÕ·hfilenamegrfc6566eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128414hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOi‚≥granking¶crfcd6566irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\A Framework for the Control of Wavelength Switched Optical Networks (WSONs) with ImpairmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.Many Service Providers (SPs) offer Virtual Private Network (VPN) services to their customers using a technique in which Customer Edge (CE) routers are routing peers of Provider Edge (PE) routers.  The Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is used to distribute the customer's routes across the provider's IP backbone network, and Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) is used to tunnel customer packets across the provider's backbone.  Support currently exists for both IPv4 and IPv6 VPNs; however, only Open Shortest Path First version 2 (OSPFv2) as PE-CE protocol is specified.  This document extends those specifications to support OSPF version 3 (OSPFv3) as a PE-CE routing protocol.  The OSPFv3 PE-CE functionality is identical to that of OSPFv2 except for the differences described in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameqP. Pillay-Esnault¢kaffiliationmPollere, Inc.dnamehP. Moyer¢kaffiliationxJeff Doyle and AssociatesdnamehJ. Doyle¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamejE. Ertekin¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamekM. Lundbergddatej‘Éhfilenamegrfc6565eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128413hkeywordsÉeL3VPNhBGP/MPLScVPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO÷ÓXgranking•crfcd6565irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCOSPFv3 as a Provider Edge to Customer Edge (PE-CE) Routing ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„In IPv6, optional internet-layer information is encoded in separate headers that may be placed between the IPv6 header and the transport-layer header.  There are a small number of such extension headers currently defined.  This document describes the issues that can arise when defining new extension headers and discusses the alternate extension mechanisms in IPv6.  It also provides a common format for defining any new IPv6 extension headers, if they are needed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationeApplednamekJ. Woodyatt¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehE. Kline¢kaffiliationhSymantecdnamekJ. Hoagland¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. BhatiaddatejÕËhfilenamegrfc6564eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128412hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÉﬂ~granking§crfcd6564irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+A Uniform Format for IPv6 Extension HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document provides a summary of issues related to the use of A6 records, discusses the current status, and moves RFC 2874 to Historic status, providing clarity to implementers and operators.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationpCloudflare, Inc.dnameiD. Conrad¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. CarpenterddatejÕ„hfilenamegrfc6563eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128411hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOi‚≥granking£crfcd6563irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMoving A6 to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxƒThis memo discusses potential security issues that arise when using variable bit rate (VBR) audio with the secure RTP profile.  Guidelines to mitigate these issues are suggested. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationsMozilla CorporationdnameiJM. Valinddatej’hfilenamegrfc6562eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128410hkeywordsÅcvbrkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOi‚≥granking¢crfcd6562irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAGuidelines for the Use of Variable Bit Rate Audio with Secure RTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôThis document contains recommendations on how Internet Service Providers can use various remediation techniques to manage the effects of malicious bot infestations on computers used by their subscribers.  Internet users with infected computers are exposed to risks such as loss of personal data and increased susceptibility to online fraud.  Such computers can also become inadvertent participants in or components of an online crime network, spam network, and/or phishing network as well as be used as a part of a distributed denial-of-service attack.  Mitigating the effects of and remediating the installations of malicious bots will make it more difficult for botnets to operate and could reduce the level of online crime on the Internet in general and/or on a particular Internet Service Provider's network.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamegN. Mody¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelM. O'Reirdanddatej’hfilenamegrfc6561eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128409hkeywordsácISPxInternet Service ProvidercBotfBotnetkRemediationgmalwarelnotificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOi‚≥granking°crfcd6561irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Recommendations for the Remediation of Bots in ISP NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThe Kerberos protocol provides a framework authenticating a client using the exchange of pre-authentication data.  This document describes the use of this framework to carry out One-Time Password (OTP) authentication. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx"RSA / The Security Division of EMCdnamekG. RichardsddatejÕ⁄hfilenamegrfc6560eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128408hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateOÎgranking†crfcd6560irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*One-Time Password (OTP) Pre-AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document defines a reliable transport mechanism for the PIM protocol for transmission of Join/Prune messages.  This eliminates the need for periodic Join/Prune message transmission and processing.  The reliable transport mechanism can use either TCP or SCTP as the transport protocol.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamelM. NapieraladdatejÕﬂhfilenamegrfc6559eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-128407hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOql␀grankingücrfcd6559irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&A Reliable Transport Mechanism for PIMdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¨This document describes how the "CONVERT" IMAP extension can be used within the Sieve mail filtering language to transform messages before final delivery. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeK. Liddatej’␀hfilenamegrfc6558eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-128406hkeywordsÇeSievegCONVERTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOXΩgrankingûcrfcd6558irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Sieve Extension for Converting Messages before DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyíTime zone information serves as a basic protocol element in protocols, such as the calendaring suite and DHCP.  The Time Zone (TZ) Database specifies the indices used in various protocols, as well as their semantic meanings, for all localities throughout the world.  This database has been meticulously maintained and distributed free of charge by a group of volunteers, coordinated by a single volunteer who is now planning to retire.  This memo specifies procedures involved with maintenance of the TZ database and associated code, including how to submit proposed updates, how decisions for inclusion of those updates are made, and the selection of a designated expert by and for the time zone community.  The intent of this memo is, to the extent possible, to document existing practice and provide a means to ease succession of the database maintainers.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiP. EggertddatejÕ´hfilenamegrfc6557eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128405hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateONuµgrankingùcrfcd6557irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberØetotaletitlex1Procedures for Maintaining the Time Zone DatabasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThe amount of time it takes to establish a session using common transport APIs in dual-stack networks and networks with filtering such as proposed in BCP 38 is a barrier to IPv6 deployment.  This note describes a test that can be used to determine whether an application can reliably establish sessions quickly in a complex environment such as dual-stack (IPv4+IPv6) deployment or IPv6 deployment with multiple prefixes and upstream ingress filtering.  This test is not a test of a specific algorithm, but of the external behavior of the system as a black box.  Any algorithm that has the intended external behavior will be accepted by it.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Bakerddatej‘˙hfilenamegrfc6556eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128404hkeywordsÖptest methodologydIPv4dIPv6osession startupgmetricskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateOÎgrankingúcrfcd6556irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTesting Eyeball HappinessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªWhen a server's IPv4 path and protocol are working, but the server's IPv6 path and protocol are not working, a dual-stack client application experiences significant connection delay compared to an IPv4-only client.  This is undesirable because it causes the dual- stack client to have a worse user experience.  This document specifies requirements for algorithms that reduce this user-visible delay and provides an algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenA. YourtchenkoddatejÕ‡hfilenamegrfc6555eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128403hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8305epagesopublicationDateOÎgrankingõcrfcd6555irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Happy Eyeballs: Success with Dual-Stack HostsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªIn Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLNs), memory constraints on routers may limit them to maintaining, at most, a few routes.  In some configurations, it is necessary to use these memory-constrained routers to deliver datagrams to nodes within the LLN.  The Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) can be used in some deployments to store most, if not all, routes on one (e.g., the Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) root) or a few routers and forward the IPv6 datagram using a source routing technique to avoid large routing tables on memory-constrained routers.  This document specifies a new IPv6 Routing header type for delivering datagrams within a RPL routing domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnameiD. Culler¢kaffiliationsHewlett Packard Co.dnameiV. Manralddatej’hfilenamegrfc6554eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128402hkeywordsÇcLLNdLLNskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOql␀grankingöcrfcd6554irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiAn IPv6 Routing Header for Source Routes with the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&The Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) includes routing information in data-plane datagrams to quickly identify inconsistencies in the routing topology.  This document describes the RPL Option for use among RPL routers to include such routing information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseurddatej’hfilenamegrfc6553eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128401hkeywordsÉcLLNdLLNsgTricklekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateOql␀grankingôcrfcd6553irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwThe Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) Option for Carrying RPL Information in Data-Plane DatagramsdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9008d9914d9685∂habstracty>The Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) specification defines a generic Distance Vector protocol that is adapted to a variety of network types by the application of specific Objective Functions (OFs). An OF states the outcome of the process used by a RPL node to select and optimize routes within a RPL Instance based on the Information Objects available; an OF is not an algorithm.

This document specifies a basic Objective Function that relies only on the objects that are defined in the RPL and does not use any protocol extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubertddatej‘ˆhfilenamegrfc6552eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128400hkeywordsàxWSN for Wireless Sensor Networkx L3 Mesh for Layer 3 Mesh NetworkpRouting ProtocolnSubnet RoutingoDistance VectorrObjective FunctionxDAG for Directed Acyclic GraphcRPLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOql␀grankingòcrfcd6552irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWObjective Function Zero for the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLNs) have unique characteristics compared with traditional wired and ad hoc networks that require the specification of new routing metrics and constraints.  By contrast, with typical Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) routing metrics using hop counts or link metrics, this document specifies a set of link and node routing metrics and constraints suitable to LLNs to be used by the Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationxCorporate Technology Group - KTdnamefM. Kim¢kaffiliationmDust NetworksdnameiK. Pister¢kaffiliationjElster SASdnameiN. Dejean¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednamejD. Barthelddatej‘“hfilenamegrfc6551eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128399hkeywordsÑcRPLdROLLcLLNxConstrained based routingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOqkˇgrankingócrfcd6551irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIRouting Metrics Used for Path Calculation in Low-Power and Lossy NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLNs) are a class of network in which both the routers and their interconnect are constrained.  LLN routers typically operate with constraints on processing power, memory, and energy (battery power).  Their interconnects are characterized by high loss rates, low data rates, and instability.  LLNs are comprised of anything from a few dozen to thousands of routers.  Supported traffic flows include point-to-point (between devices inside the LLN), point-to-multipoint (from a central control point to a subset of devices inside the LLN), and multipoint-to-point (from devices inside the LLN towards a central control point).  This document specifies the IPv6 Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL), which provides a mechanism whereby multipoint-to-point traffic from devices inside the LLN towards a central control point as well as point-to-multipoint traffic from the central control point to the devices inside the LLN are supported.  Support for point-to-point traffic is also available. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsä¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Winter¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationmSigma DesignsdnameiA. Brandt¢kaffiliationuArch Rock CorporationdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationqEmber CorporationdnameiR. Kelsey¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamehP. Levis¢kaffiliationmDust NetworksdnameiK. Pister¢kaffiliationxStruik Security ConsultancydnameiR. Struik¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationtCooper Power SystemsdnamelR. Alexanderddatej‘›hfilenamegrfc6550eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128398hkeywordsáxWSN for Wireless Sensor Networkx L3 Mesh for Layer 3 Mesh NetworkpRouting ProtocolnSubnet RoutingoDistance VectorrObjective FunctionxDAG for Directed Acyclic GraphkobsoletedByÄepagesùopublicationDateOqkˇgrankingñcrfcd6550irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;RPL: IPv6 Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÖd9914d9926d9008d9010d9685∂habstracty6OSPFv3 includes a mechanism to support multiple instances of the protocol running on the same interface. OSPFv2 can utilize such a mechanism in order to support multiple routing domains on the same subnet.

This document defines the OSPFv2 Instance ID to enable separate OSPFv2 protocol instances on the same interface. Unlike OSPFv3 where the Instance ID can be used for multiple purposes, such as putting the same interface in multiple areas, the OSPFv2 Instance ID is reserved for identifying protocol instances.

This document updates RFC 2328. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Mirtorabiddatej’hfilenamegrfc6549eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128397hkeywordsÅkInstance IDkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateOi‚≥grankingïcrfcd6549irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex OSPFv2 Multi-Instance ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty£This document describes the function and responsibilities of the RFC Independent Submission Editor (ISE).  The Independent Submission stream is one of the stream producers that create draft RFCs, with the ISE as its stream approver.  The ISE is overall responsible for activities within the Independent Submission stream, working with draft editors and reviewers, and interacts with the RFC Production Center and Publisher, and the RFC Series Editor (RSE).  The ISE is appointed by the IAB, and also interacts with the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxThe University of AucklanddnamekN. Brownlee¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej’hfilenamegrfc6548eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128396hkeywordsÅxIndependent Stream EditorkobsoletedByÅd8730epagesopublicationDateOﬁ◊Wgrankingîcrfcd6548irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex#Independent Submission Editor ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis document moves "Use of /127 Prefix Length Between Routers Considered Harmful" (RFC 3627) to Historic status to reflect the updated guidance contained in "Using 127-Bit IPv6 Prefixes on Inter- Router Links" (RFC 6164).  A Standards Track document supersedes an informational document; therefore, guidance provided in RFC 6164 is to be followed when the two documents are in conflict.  This document links the two RFCs so that the IETF's updated guidance on this topic is clearer.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiW. Georgeddatej’hfilenamegrfc6547eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128395hkeywordsÑdIPv6d/127vpoint-to-point addressrinter-router linkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateONuâgrankingìcrfcd6547irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRFC 3627 to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™The Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) defines a common XML format for document exchange, and Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID) defines extensions to IODEF intended for the cooperative handling of security incidents within consortia of network operators and enterprises.  This document specifies an application-layer protocol for RID based upon the passing of RID messages over HTTP/TLS. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammellddatej’hfilenamegrfc6546eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-128394hkeywordsÑxCoordinated Incident ResponseeCSIRTx+Incident Object Description Exchange FormateIODEFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOí`)grankingícrfcd6546irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexITransport of Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID) Messages over HTTP/TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯Security incidents, such as system compromises, worms, viruses, phishing incidents, and denial of service, typically result in the loss of service, data, and resources both human and system.  Service providers and Computer Security Incident Response Teams need to be equipped and ready to assist in communicating and tracing security incidents with tools and procedures in place before the occurrence of an attack.  Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID) outlines a proactive inter-network communication method to facilitate sharing incident-handling data while integrating existing detection, tracing, source identification, and mitigation mechanisms for a complete incident-handling solution.  Combining these capabilities in a communication system provides a way to achieve higher security levels on networks.  Policy guidelines for handling incidents are recommended and can be agreed upon by a consortium using the security recommendations and considerations.  This document obsoletes RFC 6045. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamekK. Moriartyddatej’hfilenamegrfc6545eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmiledfullx,mile - Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangednamex%Managed Incident Lightweight Exchangedtypebwgbidjdoc-128393hkeywordsÑqincident responseuincident coordinationqincident handlingvincident communicationkobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDateOí`(grankingëcrfcd6545irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅInteractive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) defines a mechanism for NAT traversal for multimedia communication protocols based on the offer/answer model of session negotiation.  ICE works by providing a set of candidate transport addresses for each media stream, which are then validated with peer-to-peer connectivity checks based on Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN).  ICE provides a general framework for describing candidates but only defines UDP-based media streams.  This specification extends ICE to TCP-based media, including the ability to offer a mix of TCP and UDP-based candidates for a single stream. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationeSkypednamenB. B. Lowekamp¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnamekA. B. Roachddatej‘ˇhfilenamegrfc6544eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128392hkeywordsÑcICEcTCPcNATmNAT traversalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOi‚≤grankingêcrfcd6544irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@TCP Candidates with Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyProxy Mobile IPv6 (RFC 5213) requires that all mobile access gateways use a fixed link-local address and a fixed link-layer address on any of their access links that they share with mobile nodes.  This requirement was intended to ensure that a mobile node does not detect any change with respect to its Layer 3 attachment, even after it roams from one mobile access gateway to another.  In the absence of any reserved addresses for this use, coordination across vendors and manual configuration of these addresses on all of the mobility elements in a Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain are required.  This document attempts to simplify this operational requirement by making a reservation for special addresses that can be used for this purpose.  This document also updates RFC 5213. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. GundavelliddatejÕ…hfilenamegrfc6543eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128391hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO≤grankingècrfcd6543irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Reserved IPv6 Interface Identifier for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
Currently, channel bindings are implemented using an MD5 hash in the Kerberos Version 5 Generic Security Service Application Programming Interface (GSS-API) mechanism (RFC 4121).  This document updates RFC 4121 to allow channel bindings using algorithms negotiated based on Kerberos crypto framework as defined in RFC 3961.  In addition, because this update makes use of the last extensible field in the Kerberos client-server exchange message, extensions are defined to allow future protocol extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehS. EmeryddatejÕ·hfilenamegrfc6542eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128390hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOX≤grankingécrfcd6542irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpKerberos Version 5 Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Channel Binding Hash AgilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This experimental specification proposes a modification to DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) allowing advertisement of third-party signature authorizations that are to be interpreted as equivalent to a signature added by the administrative domain of the message's author.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCloudmark, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’hfilenamegrfc6541eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128389hkeywordsÇnAuthenticationjReputationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateONu}grankingçcrfcd6541irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Authorized Third-Party SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty␀Given the global lack of available IPv4 space, and limitations in IPv4 extension and transition technologies, this document advises that IPv6 support is no longer considered optional.  It also cautions that there are places in existing IETF documents where the term "IP" is used in a way that could be misunderstood by implementers as the term "IP" becomes a generic that can mean IPv4 + IPv6, IPv6-only, or IPv4-only, depending on context and application.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiW. George¢kaffiliationiCablelabsdnameiC. Donley¢kaffiliationsBig Switch NetworksdnameoC. Liljenstolpe¢kaffiliationqTime Warner CablednameiL. Howardddatej’hfilenamegrfc6540eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128388hkeywordsÇdIPv4krequirementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOá‘sgrankingåcrfcd6540irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber±etotaletitlex.IPv6 Support Required for All IP-Capable NodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty´Cryptographic protocols based on public-key methods have been traditionally based on certificates and Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) to support certificate management.  The emerging field of Identity-Based Encryption (IBE) protocols allows simplification of infrastructure requirements via a Private-Key Generator (PKG) while providing the same flexibility.  However, one significant limitation of IBE methods is that the PKG can end up being a de facto key escrow server, with undesirable consequences.  Another observed deficiency is a lack of mutual authentication of communicating parties.  This document specifies the Identity-Based Authenticated Key Exchange (IBAKE) protocol.  IBAKE does not suffer from the key escrow problem and in addition provides mutual authentication as well as perfect forward and backward secrecy.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamejV. Cakulev¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamekG. Sundaram¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamekI. Broustisddatej‘∫hfilenamegrfc6539eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128387hkeywordsÇcibexidentity based encryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOX®grankingãcrfcd6539irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0IBAKE: Identity-Based Authenticated Key ExchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty∆This document is a report from the IRTF Host Identity Protocol (HIP) research group documenting the collective experiences and lessons learned from studies, related experimentation, and designs completed by the research group.  The document summarizes implications of adding HIP to host protocol stacks, Internet infrastructure, and applications.  The perspective of a network operator, as well as a list of HIP experiments, are presented as well.  Portions of this report may be relevant also to other network overlay-based architectures or to attempts to deploy alternative networking architectures.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrThe Boeing CompanydnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OuludnameiA. Gurtovddatej’hfilenamegrfc6538eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128386hkeywordsÑhSecuritypID/locator spliteIPsechResearchkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateOn»,grankingäcrfcd6538irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex2The Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Experiment ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty7This document specifies a common interface for using the Host Identity Protocol (HIP) with a Distributed Hash Table (DHT) service to provide a name-to-Host-Identity-Tag lookup service and a Host- Identity-Tag-to-address lookup service.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrThe Boeing CompanydnamelJ. Ahrenholzddatej’hfilenamegrfc6537eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehiprgdfullxhiprg - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebrgbidjdoc-128385hkeywordsàcHIPvHost Identity ProtocolcDHTvDIstributed Hash TablecHITqHost Identity TagjresolutiongservicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateONurgrankingâcrfcd6537irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex7Host Identity Protocol Distributed Hash Table InterfacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThe standardization of network configuration interfaces for use with the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) requires a structured and secure operating environment that promotes human usability and multi-vendor interoperability.  There is a need for standard mechanisms to restrict NETCONF protocol access for particular users to a pre-configured subset of all available NETCONF protocol operations and content.  This document defines such an access control model. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiYumaWorksdnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej’hfilenamegrfc6536eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128384hkeywordsÉgNETCONFdYANGcXMLkobsoletedByÅd8341epages1opublicationDateOXûgrankingàcrfcd6536irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) Access Control ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈBump-in-the-Host (BIH) is a host-based IPv4 to IPv6 protocol translation mechanism that allows a class of IPv4-only applications that work through NATs to communicate with IPv6-only peers.  The host on which applications are running may be connected to IPv6-only or dual-stack access networks.  BIH hides IPv6 and makes the IPv4-only applications think they are talking with IPv4 peers by local synthesis of IPv4 addresses.  This document obsoletes RFC 2767 and RFC 3338. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehB. Huang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6535eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128383hkeywordsâcNATeNAT46cDNSeDNS46ktranslationdIPv4lapplicationsdIPv6cENRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateONufgrankingácrfcd6535irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Dual-Stack Hosts Using "Bump-in-the-Host" (BIH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThe IETF has developed a one-way packet loss metric that measures the loss rate on a Poisson and Periodic probe streams between two hosts.  However, the impact of packet loss on applications is, in general, sensitive not just to the average loss rate but also to the way in which packet losses are distributed in loss episodes (i.e., maximal sets of consecutively lost probe packets).  This document defines one-way packet loss episode metrics, specifically, the frequency and average duration of loss episodes and a probing methodology under which the loss episode metrics are to be measured. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrAT&T Labs-ResearchdnamekN. Duffield¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationrColgate UniversitydnamejJ. SommersddatejÕÊhfilenamegrfc6534eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128382hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO®…«grankingÜcrfcd6534irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Loss Episode Metrics for IP Performance Metrics (IPPM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØDelivery status notifications (DSNs) are critical to the correct operation of an email system. However, the existing Draft Standards (RFC 3461, RFC 3464, RFC 6522) are presently limited to ASCII text in the machine-readable portions of the protocol. This specification adds a new address type for international email addresses so an original recipient address with non-ASCII characters can be correctly preserved even after downgrading. This also provides updated content return media types for delivery status notifications and message disposition notifications to support use of the new address type.

This document extends RFC 3461, RFC 3464, RFC 3798, and RFC 6522. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Newman¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘˛hfilenamegrfc6533eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128381hkeywordsÅcdsnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO>ågrankingÖcrfcd6533irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Internationalized Delivery Status and Disposition NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïInternet mail was originally limited to 7-bit ASCII. MIME added support for the use of 8-bit character sets in body parts, and also defined an encoded-word construct so other character sets could be used in certain header field values. However, full internationalization of electronic mail requires additional enhancements to allow the use of Unicode, including characters outside the ASCII repertoire, in mail addresses as well as direct use of Unicode in header fields like "From:", "To:", and "Subject:", without requiring the use of complex encoded-word constructs. This document specifies an enhancement to the Internet Message Format and to MIME that allows use of Unicode in mail addresses and most header field content.

This specification updates Section 6.4 of RFC 2045 to eliminate the restriction prohibiting the use of non-identity content-transfer- encodings on subtypes of "message/". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeTWNICdnamegA. Yang¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiS. Steele¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehN. FreedddatejÕ◊hfilenamegrfc6532eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128380hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO>ã`grankingÑcrfcd6532irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInternationalized Email HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¶This document specifies an SMTP extension for transport and delivery of email messages with internationalized email addresses or header information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Yao¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefW. Maoddatej‘˛hfilenamegrfc6531eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128379hkeywordsÑdSMTPjEmail I18ntInternationalizationhSMTPUTF8kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO>ãUgrankingÉcrfcd6531irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*SMTP Extension for Internationalized EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸Full use of electronic mail throughout the world requires that (subject to other constraints) people be able to use close variations on their own names (written correctly in their own languages and scripts) as mailbox names in email addresses.  This document introduces a series of specifications that define mechanisms and protocol extensions needed to fully support internationalized email addresses.  These changes include an SMTP extension and extension of email header syntax to accommodate UTF-8 data.  The document set also includes discussion of key assumptions and issues in deploying fully internationalized email.  This document is a replacement for RFC 4952; it reflects additional issues identified since that document was published. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliation`dnameeY. Koddatej‘∏hfilenamegrfc6530eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128378hkeywordsÑdSMTPjEmail I18ntInternationalizationhSMTPUTF8kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO>ãKgrankingÇcrfcd6530irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Overview and Framework for Internationalized EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty˝This document reproduces the Host/Host Protocol developed by the ARPA Network Working Group during 1969, 1970, and 1971.  It describes a protocol used to manage communication between processes residing on independent Hosts.  It addresses issues of multiplexing multiple streams of communication (including addressing, flow control, connection establishment/disestablishment, and other signaling) over a single hardware interface.  It was the official protocol of the ARPA Network from January 1972 until the switch to TCP/IP in January 1983.  It is offered as an RFC at this late date to help complete the historical record available through the RFC series.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for the historical record.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekA. McKenzie¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. CrockerddatejÕøhfilenamegrfc6529eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128377hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateOÎgrankingÅcrfcd6529irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex'Host/Host Protocol for the ARPA NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document specifies an algorithm for the generation of TCP Initial Sequence Numbers (ISNs), such that the chances of an off-path attacker guessing the sequence numbers in use by a target connection are reduced.  This document revises (and formally obsoletes) RFC 1948, and takes the ISN generation algorithm originally proposed in that document to Standards Track, formally updating RFC 793. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvSI6 Networks / UTN-FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamekS. Bellovinddatej’hfilenamegrfc6528eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128376hkeywordsÖlTCP securitytTCP Sequence NumbersxSequence Number RandomizationkobfuscationsTCP vulnerabilitieskobsoletedByÅd9293epagesopublicationDateO)„zgrankingÄcrfcd6528irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Defending against Sequence Number AttacksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This specification defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management based on the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).  In particular, it defines objects for configuring, monitoring, and controlling routers that employ the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol Version 3 (VRRPv3) for both IPv4 and IPv6 as defined in RFC 5798.  This memo obsoletes RFC 2787. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouti