This document specifies an extension to the specifications in RFC 5101, IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX), when using the Partial Reliability extension of SCTP (PR-SCTP, Partial Reliability Stream Control Transmission Protocol).

When implemented at both the Exporting Process and Collecting Process, this method offers several advantages, such as the ability to calculate Data Record losses for PR-SCTP per Template, immediate export of Template Withdrawal Messages, immediate reuse of Template IDs within an SCTP stream, reduced likelihood of Data Record loss, and reduced demands on the Collecting Process. When implemented in only the Collecting Process or Exporting Process, then normal IPFIX behavior will be seen without all of the additional benefits. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Johnson¢kaffiliationkTU MuenchendnamehG. MuenzddatejÕUhfilenamegrfc6526eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128374hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOn»,granking~crfcd6526irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYIP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Per Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) StreamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_Many applications that use the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) want the ability to "reset" a stream.  The intention of resetting a stream is to set the numbering sequence of the stream back to 'zero' with a corresponding notification to the application layer that the reset has been performed.  Applications requiring this feature want it so that they can "reuse" streams for different purposes but still utilize the stream sequence number so that the application can track the message flows.  Thus, without this feature, a new use of an old stream would result in message numbers greater than expected, unless there is a protocol mechanism to "reset the streams back to zero".  This document also includes methods for resetting the transmission sequence numbers, adding additional streams, and resetting all stream sequence numbers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAdara NetworksdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefP. LeiddatejÕﬂhfilenamegrfc6525eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128373hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateOOûπgranking}crfcd6525irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBStream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Stream ReconfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty7The multipart/report Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) media type is a general "family" or "container" type for electronic mail reports of any kind. Although this memo defines only the use of the multipart/report media type with respect to delivery status reports, mail processing programs will benefit if a single media type is used for all kinds of reports.

This memo obsoletes "The Multipart/Report Content Type for the Reporting of Mail System Administrative Messages", RFC 3462, and marks RFC 3462 and its predecessor as "Historic". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej’␀hfilenamegrfc6522eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128372hkeywordsÇdMIMEx%Multipurpose Internet Mail ExtensionskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateO»≠grankingzcrfcd6522irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberIetotaletitlexXThe Multipart/Report Media Type for the Reporting of Mail System Administrative MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6533∂habstracty_This document describes a home agent-assisted route optimization functionality for the IPv4 Network Mobility Protocol.  The function is designed to facilitate optimal routing in cases where all nodes are connected to a single home agent; thus, the use case is route optimization within a single organization or similar entity.  The functionality enables the discovery of eligible peer nodes (based on information received from the home agent) and their network prefixes, and the establishment of a direct tunnel between such nodes.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwAalto University/ComnetdnameiA. Makela¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonenddatej’hfilenamegrfc6521eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-128371hkeywordsÉmmobile routerumobile network prefixtcorrespondent routerkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateOOûÆgrankingycrfcd6521irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCHome Agent-Assisted Route Optimization between Mobile IPv4 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document describes the Heartbeat Extension for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocols.

The Heartbeat Extension provides a new protocol for TLS/DTLS allowing the usage of keep-alive functionality without performing a renegotiation and a basis for path MTU (PMTU) discovery for DTLS. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnamemR. Seggelmann¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationuGWhiz Arts & SciencesdnamekM. Williamsddatej’␀hfilenamegrfc6520eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128370hkeywordsÅhtls/dtlskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateO1≈:grankingxcrfcd6520irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Heartbeat ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8447∂habstractyÀDual-Stack Lite is a solution to offer both IPv4 and IPv6 connectivity to customers that are addressed only with an IPv6 prefix.  Dual-Stack Lite requires pre-configuration of the Dual-Stack Lite Address Family Transition Router (AFTR) tunnel information on the Basic Bridging BroadBand (B4) element.  In many networks, the customer profile information may be stored in Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) servers, while client configurations are mainly provided through the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP).  This document specifies a new Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) attribute to carry the Dual-Stack Lite AFTR tunnel name; the RADIUS attribute is defined based on the equivalent DHCPv6 OPTION_AFTR_NAME option.  This RADIUS attribute is meant to be used between the RADIUS server and the Network Access Server (NAS); it is not intended to be used directly between the B4 element and the RADIUS server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekR. Maglione¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Durandddatej’hfilenamegrfc6519eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-128369hkeywordsÉdIPv6ftunneliattributekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO9˝*grankingwcrfcd6519irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%RADIUS Extensions for Dual-Stack LitedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·This document is one of a series concerned with defining a roadmap of protocol specification work for the use of modern cryptographic mechanisms and algorithms for message authentication in routing protocols.  In particular, it defines the framework for a key management protocol that may be used to create and manage session keys for message authentication and integrity.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekG. Lebovitz¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatiaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6518eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdkarpdfullx6karp - Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdnamex/Keying and Authentication for Routing Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128368hkeywordsòcMACdhashhsecurityhsecuringfsecuremauthorizationjprotectionfhardenihardeningninfrastructurefrouterfcryptolcryptographymcryptographicgroadmapeguideiguidelinegmessageiframeworkckeydkeysjmanagementhprotocolcKMPwkey management protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO)„ngrankingvcrfcd6518irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHKeying and Authentication for Routing Protocols (KARP) Design GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDMore that one set of mechanisms to support multicast in a layer 3 BGP/MPLS VPN has been defined. These are presented in the documents that define them as optional building blocks.

To enable interoperability between implementations, this document defines a subset of features that is considered mandatory for a multicast BGP/MPLS VPN implementation. This will help implementers and deployers understand which L3VPN multicast requirements are best satisfied by each option. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamehT. Morin¢kaffiliationbBTdnamepB. Niven-Jenkins¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnameiY. Kamite¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. Leymann¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitarddatej‘éhfilenamegrfc6517eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128367hkeywordsâdmplscvpnimulticastel3vpncbgpcpimdp2mpcldpgrsvp-tekobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateOBœ.grankingucrfcd6517irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Mandatory Features in a Layer 3 Multicast BGP/MPLS VPN SolutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThe specification for Multicast Virtual Private Networks (MVPNs) contains an option that allows the use of PIM as the control protocol between provider edge routers.  It also contains an option that allows UDP-based messages, known as Selective Provider Multicast Service Interface (S-PMSI) Join messages, to be used to bind particular customer multicast flows to particular tunnels through a service provider's network.  This document extends the MVPN specification (RFC 6513) so that these options can be used when the customer multicast flows are IPv6 flows. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekI. WijnandsddatejÕnhfilenamegrfc6516eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128366hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOBœ!grankingtcrfcd6516irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉIPv6 Multicast VPN (MVPN) Support Using PIM Control Plane and Selective Provider Multicast Service Interface (S-PMSI) Join MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*To provide Multicast VPN (MVPN) service, Provider Edge routers originate BGP Update messages that carry Multicast-VPN ("MCAST-VPN") BGP routes; they also originate unicast VPN routes that carry MVPN-specific attributes.  These routes encode addresses from the customer's address space, as well as addresses from the provider's address space.  These two address spaces are independent, and the address family (IPv4 or IPv6) of the two spaces may or may not be the same.  These routes always contain an "address family" field that specifies whether the customer addresses are IPv4 addresses or whether they are IPv6 addresses.  However, there is no field that explicitly specifies the address family of the provider addresses.  To ensure interoperability, this document specifies that provider IPv4 addresses are always encoded in these update messages as 4-octet addresses, and that the distinction between IPv4 and IPv6 is signaled solely by the length of the address field.  Specific cases are explained in detail.  This document updates RFC 6514. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej‘Ìhfilenamegrfc6515eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128365hkeywordsÉdmvpnimcast-vpnmmulticast-vpnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOBœgrankingscrfcd6515irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGIPv4 and IPv6 Infrastructure Addresses in BGP Updates for Multicast VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This document describes the BGP encodings and procedures for exchanging the information elements required by Multicast in MPLS/BGP IP VPNs, as specified in RFC 6513. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamehT. Morin¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. RekhterddatejÕhfilenamegrfc6514eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128364hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateOBœgrankingrcrfcd6514irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>BGP Encodings and Procedures for Multicast in MPLS/BGP IP VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByåd9081d7902d6515d8534d7900d7988d7441d6625d7899d7385d7582d9573∂habstracty In order for IP multicast traffic within a BGP/MPLS IP VPN (Virtual Private Network) to travel from one VPN site to another, special protocols and procedures must be implemented by the VPN Service Provider.  These protocols and procedures are specified in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. AggarwalddatejÕ(hfilenamegrfc6513eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128363hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateOBŒ¯grankingqcrfcd6513irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMulticast in MPLS/BGP IP VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7988d7900d7582∂habstracty∑The control protocol used for constructing Point-to-Multipoint and Multipoint-to-Multipoint Label Switched Paths ("MP LSPs") contains a field that identifies the address of a "root node".  Intermediate nodes are expected to be able to look up that address in their routing tables.  However, this is not possible if the route to the root node is a BGP route and the intermediate nodes are part of a BGP-free core.  This document specifies procedures that enable an MP LSP to be constructed through a BGP-free core.  In these procedures, the root node address is temporarily replaced by an address that is known to the intermediate nodes and is on the path to the true root node. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamelM. Napierala¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamejN. LeymannddatejÕ∑hfilenamegrfc6512eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128362hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOBŒÌgrankingpcrfcd6512irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Using Multipoint LDP When the Backbone Has No Route to the RootdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯There are many deployment scenarios that require an egress Label Switching Router (LSR) to receive binding of the Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Label Switched Path (LSP) to an application and a payload identifier using some "out-of-band" (OOB) mechanism.  This document defines protocol mechanisms to address this requirement.  The procedures described in this document are equally applicable for point-to-point (P2P) and point-to-multipoint (P2MP) LSPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. AggarwalddatejÕπhfilenamegrfc6511eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128361hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateOBŒ€grankingocrfcd6511irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Non-Penultimate Hop Popping Behavior and Out-of-Band Mapping for RSVP-TE Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) established using the Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) extensions may be signaled with a set of LSP-specific attributes.  These attributes may be carried in both Path and Resv messages.  This document specifies how LSP attributes are to be carried in RSVP Path and Resv messages using the Routing Backus-Naur Form and clarifies related Resv message formats.  This document updates RFC 4875 and RFC 5420. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. SwallowddatejÕ“hfilenamegrfc6510eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128360hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOBŒ–grankingncrfcd6510irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeResource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Message Formats for Label Switched Path (LSP) Attributes ObjectsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document describes the Multimedia Internet KEYing-Sakai-Kasahara Key Encryption (MIKEY-SAKKE), a method of key exchange that uses Identity-based Public Key Cryptography (IDPKC) to establish a shared secret value and certificateless signatures to provide source authentication.  MIKEY-SAKKE has a number of desirable features, including simplex transmission, scalability, low-latency call setup, and support for secure deferred delivery.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdCESGdnameiM. GrovesddatejÕ÷hfilenamegrfc6509eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128359hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO)„agrankingmcrfcd6509irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPMIKEY-SAKKE: Sakai-Kasahara Key Encryption in Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn this document, the Sakai-Kasahara Key Encryption (SAKKE) algorithm is described.  This uses Identity-Based Encryption to exchange a shared secret from a Sender to a Receiver.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdCESGdnameiM. GrovesddatejÕ◊hfilenamegrfc6508eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128358hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO)„Tgrankinglcrfcd6508irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Sakai-Kasahara Key Encryption (SAKKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMany signature schemes currently in use rely on certificates for authentication of identity.  In Identity-based cryptography, this adds unnecessary overhead and administration.  The Elliptic Curve-based Certificateless Signatures for Identity-based Encryption (ECCSI) signature scheme described in this document is certificateless.  This scheme has the additional advantages of low bandwidth and low computational requirements.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdCESGdnameiM. GrovesddatejÕ◊hfilenamegrfc6507eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128357hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO)„Egrankingkcrfcd6507irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUElliptic Curve-Based Certificateless Signatures for Identity-Based Encryption (ECCSI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycCurrently, OSPF for IPv6 (OSPFv3) uses IPsec as the only mechanism for authenticating protocol packets.  This behavior is different from authentication mechanisms present in other routing protocols (OSPFv2, Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS), RIP, and Routing Information Protocol Next Generation (RIPng)).  In some environments, it has been found that IPsec is difficult to configure and maintain and thus cannot be used.  This document defines an alternative mechanism to authenticate OSPFv3 protocol packets so that OSPFv3 does not only depend upon IPsec for authentication. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationoHewlett PackarddnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Lindemddatej‘ˇhfilenamegrfc6506eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128356hkeywordsÅpRouting securitykobsoletedByÅd7166epagesopublicationDateO9˝grankingjcrfcd6506irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Supporting Authentication Trailer for OSPFv3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document defines a Media Control Channel Framework Package for managing mixers for media conferences and connections.  The package defines request elements for managing conference mixers, managing mixers between conferences and/or connections, as well as associated responses and notifications.  The package also defines elements for auditing package capabilities and mixers [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamelS. McGlashan¢kaffiliationjRainwillowdnamelT. Melanchuk¢kaffiliationoNS-TechnologiesdnamejC. Boultonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6505eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128355hkeywordsÅpconference mixerkobsoletedByÄepagesYopublicationDateOn»+grankingicrfcd6505irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?A Mixer Control Package for the Media Control Channel FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document provides detailed call flows for the scenarios documented in the Framework for Centralized Conferencing (XCON) (RFC 5239) and in the XCON scenarios (RFC 4597).  The call flows document the use of the interface between a conference control client and a conference control server using the Centralized Conferencing Manipulation Protocol (CCMP) (RFC 6503).  The objective is to provide detailed examples for reference by both protocol researchers and developers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationhMeetechodnamejL. Miniero¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnameiR. Presta¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnamekS P. RomanoddatejÕ»hfilenamegrfc6504eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128354hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesNopublicationDateOn»+grankinghcrfcd6504irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHCentralized Conferencing Manipulation Protocol (CCMP) Call Flow ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_The Centralized Conferencing Manipulation Protocol (CCMP) allows a Centralized Conferencing (XCON) system client to create, retrieve, change, and delete objects that describe a centralized conference.  CCMP is a means to control basic and advanced conference features such as conference state and capabilities, participants, relative roles, and details.  CCMP is a stateless, XML-based, client server protocol that carries, in its request and response messages, conference information in the form of XML documents and fragments conforming to the centralized conferencing data model schema. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationoNS-TechnologiesdnamejC. Boulton¢kaffiliationtUniversity of NapolidnameiS. Romano¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej‘Ùhfilenamegrfc6503eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128353hkeywordsÑoconference userqad hoc conferencersidebar conferencetscheduled conferencekobsoletedByÄepageswopublicationDateOn»+grankinggcrfcd6503irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Centralized Conferencing Manipulation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document specifies the notification mechanism for XCON (centralized conferencing).  This mechanism reuses the SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) event package for conference state.  Additionally, the notification mechanism includes support for the XCON data model and for partial notifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliation`dnamemS. Srinivasan¢kaffiliationpGesher Erove LtddnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemJ. UrpalainenddatejÃ≈hfilenamegrfc6502eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128352hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOn»+grankingfcrfcd6502irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRConference Event Package Data Format Extension for Centralized Conferencing (XCON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypRFC 5239 defines centralized conferencing (XCON) as an association of participants with a central focus.  The state of a conference is represented by a conference object.  This document defines an XML- based conference information data model to be used for conference objects.  A conference information data model is designed to convey information about the conference and about participation in the conference.  The conference information data model defined in this document constitutes an extension of the data format specified in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) event package for conference State. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegO. Novo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationtFidelity InvestmentsdnameiD. Morgan¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemJ. UrpalainenddatejÕ…hfilenamegrfc6501eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128351hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDateOn»*grankingecrfcd6501irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEConference Information Data Model for Centralized Conferencing (XCON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document defines Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) packages that enable a Call Agent to authorize and monitor the transition of a connection to and from Voiceband Data (VBD) with or without redundancy and FEC (forward error correction).  Although the focus is on VBD, the General-Purpose Media Descriptor Parameter package can be used to authorize other modes of operation, not relevant to VBD, for a particular codec.  In addition to defining these new packages, this document describes the use of the Media Format Parameter package and Fax package with VBD, redundancy, and FEC.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehJ. Stone¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Kumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. AndreasenddatejÕ‹hfilenamegrfc6498eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128350hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateOOû£grankingbcrfcd6498irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyMedia Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) Voiceband Data (VBD) Package and General-Purpose Media Descriptor Parameter PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑This document specifies an Extension to BCP 47 that provides subtags for specifying the source language or script of transformed content, including content that has been transliterated, transcribed, or translated, or in some other way influenced by the source.  It also provides for additional information used for identification.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehM. Davis¢kaffiliationfLab126dnamekA. Phillips¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiY. Umaoka¢kaffiliationqInfinite AutomatadnamegC. Falkddatej’␀hfilenamegrfc6497eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128349hkeywordsÅflocalekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOEé¿grankingacrfcd6497irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(BCP 47 Extension T - Transformed ContentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) specifies a method for securing Neighbor Discovery (ND) signaling against specific threats.  As defined today, SEND assumes that the node sending an ND message is the owner of the address from which the message is sent and/or possesses a key that authorizes the node to act as a router, so that it is in possession of the private key or keys used to generate the digital signature on each message.  This means that the Proxy ND signaling performed by nodes that do not possess knowledge of the address owner's private key and/or knowledge of a router's key cannot be secured using SEND.  This document extends the current SEND specification in order to secure Proxy ND operation.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationxRome Tor Vergata UniversitydnameiM. Bonola¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamerA. Garcia-Martinezddatej‘⁄hfilenamegrfc6496eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymccsidfullxcsi - Cga & Send maIntenancednamevCga & Send maIntenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128348hkeywordsÜdSPNDcCGAkMobile IPv6eMIPv6qProxy Mobile IPv6fPMIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,àGgranking`crfcd6496irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Secure Proxy ND Support for SEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‘SEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) defines the Name Type field in the ICMPv6 Trust Anchor option.  This document specifies new Name Type fields based on certificate Subject Key Identifiers (SKIs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Gagliano¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationuEnterprise ArchitectsdnamehA. KukecddatejÕJhfilenamegrfc6495eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymccsidfullxcsi - Cga & Send maIntenancednamevCga & Send maIntenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128347hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,à=granking_crfcd6495irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNSubject Key Identifier (SKI) SEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) Name Type FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) utilizes X.509v3 certificates for performing router authorization.  This document specifies a certificate profile for SEND based on resource certificates along with extended key usage values required for SEND. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Gagliano¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationuEnterprise