In the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), resource certificates completely obscure names or any other information that might be useful for contacting responsible parties to deal with issues of certificate expiration, maintenance, roll-overs, compromises, etc.  This document describes the RPKI Ghostbusters Record containing human contact information that may be verified (indirectly) by a Certification Authority (CA) certificate.  The data in the record are those of a severely profiled vCard. [STANDARDS- TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bushddatej‘˙hfilenamegrfc6493eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128345hkeywordsÑdRPKItResource CertificatemHuman ContactevCardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,à)granking]crfcd6493irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAThe Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Ghostbusters RecorddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document defines a framework for certificate management interactions between an Internet Number Resource issuer ("issuer") and an Internet Number Resource recipient ("subject") through the specification of a protocol for interaction between the two parties.  The protocol supports the transmission of requests from the subject, and corresponding responses from the issuer encompassing the actions of certificate issuance, certificate revocation, and certificate status information reports.  This protocol is intended to be limited to the application of Internet Number Resource Certificate management and is not intended to be used as part of a more general certificate management framework. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamejR. Loomans¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamekB. Ellacott¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austeinddatej‘Òhfilenamegrfc6492eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128344hkeywordsÅdRPKIkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateO,àgranking\crfcd6492irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1A Protocol for Provisioning Resource CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxêThis document provides specific direction to IANA as to the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) objects it should issue. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamelT. Manderson¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiL. Vegoda¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kentddatej‘„hfilenamegrfc6491eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128343hkeywordsÜdsidrdrpkidianacas0das 0croakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,àgranking[crfcd6491irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Objects Issued by IANAdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxwThis document defines a Trust Anchor Locator (TAL) for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kentddatej‘Qhfilenamegrfc6490eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128342hkeywordsÅctalkobsoletedByÅd7730epagesopublicationDateO,áQgrankingZcrfcd6490irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) Trust Anchor LocatordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÔThis document describes how a Certification Authority (CA) in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) performs a planned rollover of its key pair.  This document also notes the implications of this key rollover procedure for relying parties (RPs).  In general, RPs are expected to maintain a local cache of the objects that have been published in the RPKI repository, and thus the way in which a CA performs key rollover impacts RPs.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kentddatej‘Ìhfilenamegrfc6489eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128341hkeywordsÅdRPKIkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateO,áEgrankingYcrfcd6489irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÆetotaletitlexZCertification Authority (CA) Key Rollover in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÒThis document defines a generic profile for signed objects used in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI).  These RPKI signed objects make use of Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) as a standard encapsulation format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamekM. Lepinski¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamefA. Chi¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kentddatej‘‰hfilenamegrfc6488eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128340hkeywordsÉcROAhmanifestlGhostBusterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,á3grankingXcrfcd6488irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHSigned Object Template for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9589∂habstracty–This document defines a standard profile for X.509 certificates for the purpose of supporting validation of assertions of "right-of-use" of Internet Number Resources (INRs).  The certificates issued under this profile are used to convey the issuer's authorization of the subject to be regarded as the current holder of a "right-of-use" of the INRs that are described in the certificate.  This document contains the normative specification of Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) syntax in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI).  This document also specifies profiles for the format of certificate requests and specifies the Relying Party RPKI certificate path validation procedure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelson¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamejR. Loomansddatej‘„hfilenamegrfc6487eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128339hkeywordsÑdrpkix"Resource Public Key InfrastructurexInternet Number ResourcescINRkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateO,á)grankingWcrfcd6487irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A Profile for X.509 PKIX Resource CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7318d8209d9829∂habstractyïThis document defines a "manifest" for use in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI).  A manifest is a signed object (file) that contains a listing of all the signed objects (files) in the repository publication point (directory) associated with an authority responsible for publishing in the repository.  For each certificate, Certificate Revocation List (CRL), or other type of signed objects issued by the authority that are published at this repository publication point, the manifest contains both the name of the file containing the object and a hash of the file content.  Manifests are intended to enable a relying party (RP) to detect certain forms of attacks against a repository.  Specifically, if an RP checks a manifest's contents against the signed objects retrieved from a repository publication point, then the RP can detect "stale" (valid) data and deletion of signed objects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamekM. LepinskiddatejÕ≤hfilenamegrfc6486eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128338hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9286epagesopublicationDateO,ágrankingVcrfcd6486irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Manifests for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document specifies the algorithms, algorithms' parameters, asymmetric key formats, asymmetric key size, and signature format for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) subscribers that generate digital signatures on certificates, Certificate Revocation Lists, and signed objects as well as for the relying parties (RPs) that verify these digital signatures. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. HustonddatejÃœhfilenamegrfc6485eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128337hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7935epagesopublicationDateO,ágrankingUcrfcd6485irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaThe Profile for Algorithms and Key Sizes for Use in the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty|This document describes the certificate policy for a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) used to support attestations about Internet Number Resource (INR) holdings.  Each organization that distributes IP addresses or Autonomous System (AS) numbers to an organization will, in parallel, issue a (public key) certificate reflecting this distribution.  These certificates will enable verification that the resources indicated in the certificate have been distributed to the holder of the associated private key and that this organization is the current, unique holder of these resources.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegD. Kong¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamefK. Seo¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamehR. Watroddatej‘‡hfilenamegrfc6484eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128336hkeywordsÇx Certification Practice StatementcCPSkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateO,á
grankingTcrfcd6484irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber≠etotaletitlexICertificate Policy (CP) for the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This document defines the semantics of a Route Origin Authorization (ROA) in terms of the context of an application of the Resource Public Key Infrastructure to validate the origination of routes advertised in the Border Gateway Protocol.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelsonddatej‘‡hfilenamegrfc6483eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128335hkeywordsÉdrpkicbgpx"Resource Public Key InfrastructurekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,ÜˇgrankingScrfcd6483irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÖValidation of Route Origination Using the Resource Certificate Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) and Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document defines a standard profile for Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs).  A ROA is a digitally signed object that provides a means of verifying that an IP address block holder has authorized an Autonomous System (AS) to originate routes to one or more prefixes within the address block. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamekM. Lepinski¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegD. Kongddatej‘‰hfilenamegrfc6482eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128334hkeywordsÇdRPKIcBGPkobsoletedByÅd9582epages	opublicationDateO,ÜÛgrankingRcrfcd6482irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0A Profile for Route Origin Authorizations (ROAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôThis document defines a profile for the structure of the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) distributed repository.  Each individual repository publication point is a directory that contains files that correspond to X.509/PKIX Resource Certificates, Certificate Revocation Lists and signed objects.  This profile defines the object (file) naming scheme, the contents of repository publication points (directories), and a suggested internal structure of a local repository cache that is intended to facilitate synchronization across a distributed collection of repository publication points and to facilitate certification path construction. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamejR. Loomans¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamemG. Michaelsonddatej‘Èhfilenamegrfc6481eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128333hkeywordsÇdrpkix"Resource Public Key InfrastructurekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,Ü‡grankingQcrfcd6481irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7A Profile for Resource Certificate Repository StructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document describes an architecture for an infrastructure to support improved security of Internet routing.  The foundation of this architecture is a Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI) that represents the allocation hierarchy of IP address space and Autonomous System (AS) numbers; and a distributed repository system for storing and disseminating the data objects that comprise the RPKI, as well as other signed objects necessary for improved routing security.  As an initial application of this architecture, the document describes how a legitimate holder of IP address space can explicitly and verifiably authorize one or more ASes to originate routes to that address space.  Such verifiable authorizations could be used, for example, to more securely construct BGP route filters.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamekM. Lepinski¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kentddatej‘Êhfilenamegrfc6480eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsidrdfullx"sidr - Secure Inter-Domain RoutingdnamexSecure Inter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128332hkeywordsÉdRPKIcBGPcROAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO,Ü”grankingPcrfcd6480irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4An Infrastructure to Support Secure Internet RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyõThis document presents an alternate method to do the anti-replay checks and updates for IP Authentication Header (AH) and Encapsulating Security Protocol (ESP).  The method defined in this document obviates the need for bit shifting and it reduces the number of times an anti-replay window is adjusted.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamehX. Zhang¢kaffiliationvFuturewei TechnologiesdnamegT. TsouddatejÕµhfilenamegrfc6479eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128331hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateOl1grankingOcrfcd6479irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0IPsec Anti-Replay Algorithm without Bit ShiftingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis document specifies a mechanism to signal Pseudowire (PW) status messages using a PW associated channel (ACh).  Such a mechanism is suitable for use where no PW dynamic control plane exits, known as static PWs, or where a Terminating Provider Edge (T-PE) needs to send a PW status message directly to a far-end T-PE.  The mechanism allows PW Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) message mapping and PW redundancy to operate on static PWs.  This document also updates RFC 5885 in the case when Bi-directional Forwarding Detection (BFD) is used to convey PW status-signaling information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehG. Heron¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. BocciddatejÕ—hfilenamegrfc6478eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128330hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO®…∆grankingNcrfcd6478irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Pseudowire Status for Static PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7274∂habstractyÈA Military Message Handling System (MMHS) processes formal messages ensuring release, distribution, security, and timely delivery across national and international strategic and tactical networks.  The MMHS Elements of Service are defined as a set of extensions to the ITU-T X.400 (1992) international standards and are specified in STANAG 4406 Edition 2 and ACP 123.  This document specifies message header fields and associated processing for RFC 5322 (Internet Message Format) to provide a comparable messaging service.  In addition, this document provides for a STANAG 4406 / Internet Email Gateway that supports message conversion.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationhSMHS LtddnamegG. LuntddatejÕ€hfilenamegrfc6477eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128329hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO»çgrankingMcrfcd6477irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Registration of Military Message Handling System (MMHS) Header Fields for Use in Internet MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛This document specifies the conventions for using Message Authentication Code (MAC) encryption with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) authenticated-enveloped-data content type.  This mirrors the use of a MAC combined with an encryption algorithm that's already employed in IPsec, Secure Socket Layer / Transport Layer Security (SSL/TLS) and Secure SHell (SSH), which is widely supported in existing crypto libraries and hardware and has been extensively analysed by the crypto community. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamejP. Gutmannddatej‘ˇhfilenamegrfc6476eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128328hkeywordsÅrauthenticated datakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOÿ∞grankingLcrfcd6476irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Using Message Authentication Code (MAC) Encryption in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThis memo defines a portion of the Proxy Mobile IPv6 Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, the Proxy Mobile IPv6 MIB can be used to monitor and control the mobile access gateway (MAG) and the local mobility anchor (LMA) functions of a Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) entity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuCyber Solutions, Inc.dnamehG. Keeni¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnamehK. Koide¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationjToyota ITCdnamekR. WakikawaddatejÕ‘hfilenamegrfc6475eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128327hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDateO®…≈grankingKcrfcd6475irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Proxy Mobile IPv6 Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡The base vCard 4.0 specification defines a large number of properties, including date of birth.  This specification adds three new properties to vCard 4.0: place of birth, place of death, and date of death. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeK. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej‘ˇhfilenamegrfc6474eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhvcarddavdfullxvcarddav - vCard and CardDAVdnameqvCard and CardDAVdtypebwgbidjdoc-128326hkeywordsÉhcontactsladdress-booktpersonal-informationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNı␀ÏgrankingJcrfcd6474irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@vCard Format Extensions: Place of Birth, Place and Date of DeathdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxõThis document defines a value of "application" for the vCard KIND property so that vCards can be used to represent software applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej‘˝hfilenamegrfc6473eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhvcarddavdfullxvcarddav - vCard and CardDAVdnameqvCard and CardDAVdtypebwgbidjdoc-128325hkeywordsÅevCardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNı␀ﬁgrankingIcrfcd6473irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevvCard KIND:applicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyßThis document recommends against the use of the AS_SET and AS_CONFED_SET types of the AS_PATH in BGPv4.  This is done to simplify the design and implementation of BGP and to make the semantics of the originator of a route more clear.  This will also simplify the design, implementation, and deployment of ongoing work in the Secure Inter-Domain Routing Working Group.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationiU.S. NISTdnameiK. Sriramddatej‘˘hfilenamegrfc6472eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128324hkeywordsÖeBGPv4hOperatordRPKIkAggregationlRoute OriginkobsoletedByÅd9774epagesopublicationDateN·QgrankingHcrfcd6472irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber¨etotaletitlex<Recommendation for Not Using AS_SET and AS_CONFED_SET in BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty–The rise of spam and other anti-social behavior on the Internet has led to the creation of shared DNS-based lists (DNSBLs) of IP addresses or domain names intended to help guide email filtering.  This memo summarizes guidelines of accepted best practice for the management of public DNSBLs by their operators as well as for the proper use of such lists by mail server administrators (DNSBL users), and it provides useful background for both parties.  It is not intended to advise on the utility or efficacy of particular DNSBLs or the DNSBL concept in general, nor to assist end users with questions about spam.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelPete ResnickgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehC. Lewis¢kaffiliationtSymantec CorporationdnamekM. Sergeantddatej‘˚hfilenamegrfc6471eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdasrgdfullxasrg - Anti-Spam Research GroupdnamexAnti-Spam Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128323hkeywordsÅlDNSBL policykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOl%grankingGcrfcd6471irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexCOverview of Best Email DNS-Based List (DNSBL) Operational PracticesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) provides mechanisms to manipulate configuration datastores.  However, client applications often need to be aware of common events, such as a change in NETCONF server capabilities, that may impact management applications.  Standard mechanisms are needed to support the monitoring of the base system events within the NETCONF server.  This document defines a YANG module that allows a NETCONF client to receive notifications for some common system events. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamejA. Biermanddatej’hfilenamegrfc6470eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - N