erencefbridgekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÎØgrankingAcrfcd6465irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`A Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) Header Extension for Mixer-to-Client Audio Level IndicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document defines a mechanism by which packets of Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) audio streams can indicate, in an RTP header extension, the audio level of the audio sample carried in the RTP packet.  In large conferences, this can reduce the load on an audio mixer or other middlebox that wants to forward only a few of the loudest audio streams, without requiring it to decode and measure every stream that is received. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationeJitsidnamegE. Ivov¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejE. Maroccoddatej‘˝hfilenamegrfc6464eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128316hkeywordsápssrc-audio-leveldssrcfspeechesoundfenergyjconferencefbridgekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateNÎØsgranking@crfcd6464irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`A Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) Header Extension for Client-to-Mixer Audio Level IndicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯This document describes a runtime local mobility anchor assignment functionality and corresponding mobility options for Proxy Mobile IPv6.  The runtime local mobility anchor assignment takes place during a Proxy Binding Update and a Proxy Binding Acknowledgement message exchange between a mobile access gateway and a local mobility anchor.  The runtime local mobility anchor assignment functionality defined in this specification can be used, for example, for load- balancing purposes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationhKDDI LabdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamefX. CuiddatejÕ—hfilenamegrfc6463eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128315hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO;ÔÀgranking?crfcd6463irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLRuntime Local Mobility Anchor (LMA) Assignment Support for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyøOn December 8-9, 2010, the IAB co-hosted an Internet privacy workshop with the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C), the Internet Society (ISOC), and MIT's Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (CSAIL). The workshop revealed some of the fundamental challenges in designing, deploying, and analyzing privacy-protective Internet protocols and systems. Although workshop participants and the community as a whole are still far from understanding how best to systematically address privacy within Internet standards development, workshop participants identified a number of potential next steps. For the IETF, these included the creation of a privacy directorate to review Internet-Drafts, further work on documenting privacy considerations for protocol developers, and a number of exploratory efforts concerning fingerprinting and anonymized routing. Potential action items for the W3C included investigating the formation of a privacy interest group and formulating guidance about fingerprinting, referrer headers, data minimization in APIs, usability, and general considerations for non-browser-based protocols.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and do not necessarily reflect the views of the IAB, W3C, ISOC, or MIT CSAIL. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. CooperddatejÕ‘hfilenamegrfc6462eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128314hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO»Çgranking>crfcd6462irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex)Report from the Internet Privacy WorkshopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠This document captures the use cases and associated requirements for interfaces that provision session establishment data into Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Service Provider components to assist with session routing.  Specifically, this document focuses on the provisioning of one such element termed the "registry".  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamepS. Channabasappaddatej’hfilenamegrfc6461eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdrinksdfullx7drinks - Data for Reachability of Inter/tra-NetworK SIPdnamex.Data for Reachability of Inter/tra-NetworK SIPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128313hkeywordsÖhregistryuregistry provisioningiregistrarqdestination groupkroute groupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO»wgranking=crfcd6461irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Data for Reachability of Inter-/Intra-NetworK SIP (DRINKS) Use Cases and Protocol RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThe United States government has published guidelines for "NSA Suite B Cryptography" that define cryptographic algorithm policy for national security applications.  This document defines a profile of Transport Layer Security (TLS) version 1.2 that is fully compliant with Suite B.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnameiM. Salter¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘˝hfilenamegrfc6460eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128312hkeywordsÅxcryptographic algorithm policykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO˝ugranking<crfcd6460irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Suite B Profile for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty©The use of cellular broadband for accessing the Internet and other data services via smartphones, tablets, and notebook/netbook computers has increased rapidly as a result of high-speed packet data networks such as HSPA, HSPA+, and now Long-Term Evolution (LTE) being deployed.  Operators that have deployed networks based on 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) network architectures are facing IPv4 address shortages at the Internet registries and are feeling pressure to migrate to IPv6.  This document describes the support for IPv6 in 3GPP network architectures.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationnRenesas MobilednamekJ. Soininen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemT. Savolainen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehG. Bajko¢kaffiliationnRenesas MobilednamelK. Iisakkiladdatej‘˘hfilenamegrfc6459eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128311hkeywordsÇjTransitioniMigrationkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateO–granking;crfcd6459irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMIPv6 in 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Evolved Packet System (EPS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThis document describes a mapping of the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) into a sockets API.  The benefits of this mapping include compatibility for TCP applications, access to new SCTP features, and a consolidated error and event notification scheme.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAdara NetworksdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationx Muenster Univ. of Appl. SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamegK. Poon¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefP. Lei¢kaffiliationbHPdnamekV. YasevichddatejÕ“hfilenamegrfc6458eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128310hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagessopublicationDateNÂ,0granking:crfcd6458irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSockets API Extensions for the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThe Path Computation Element (PCE) provides functions of path computation in support of traffic engineering in networks controlled by Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS).

MPLS and GMPLS networks may be constructed from layered client/server networks. It is advantageous for overall network efficiency to provide end-to-end traffic engineering across multiple network layers. PCE is a candidate solution for such requirements.

Generic requirements for a communication protocol between Path Computation Clients (PCCs) and PCEs are presented in RFC 4657, "Path Computation Element (PCE) Communication Protocol Generic Requirements". Generic requirements for a PCE discovery protocol are presented in RFC 4674, "Requirements for Path Computation Element (PCE) Discovery".

This document complements the generic requirements and presents detailed sets of PCC-PCE communication protocol requirements and PCE discovery protocol requirements for inter-layer traffic engineering. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takeda¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘¸hfilenamegrfc6457eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128309hkeywordsÜdPCEPkinter-layerstraffic engineeringdMPLSeGMPLScVNTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNŸkëgranking9crfcd6457irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXPCC-PCE Communication and PCE Discovery Requirements for Inter-Layer Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›This document describes the application of MPLS multi-segment pseudowires (MS-PWs) in a dual-technology environment comprising a Passive Optical Network (PON) and an MPLS Packet Switched Network (PSN).

PON technology may be used in mobile backhaul networks to support the end segments closest to the aggregation devices. In these cases, there may be a very large number of pseudowire (PW) Terminating Provider Edge (T-PE) nodes. The MPLS control plane could be used to provision these end segments, but support for the necessary protocols would complicate the management of the T-PEs and would significantly increase their expense. Alternatively, static, or management plane, configuration could be used to configure the end segments, but the very large number of such segments in a PON places a very heavy burden on the network manager.

This document describes how to set up the end segment of an end-to- end MPLS PW over a Gigabit-capable Passive Optical Network (G-PON) or 10 Gigabit-capable Passive Optical Network (XG-PON) using the G-PON and XG-PON management protocol, Optical Network Termination Management and Control Interface (OMCI). This simplifies and speeds up PW provisioning compared with manual configuration.

This document also shows how an MS-PW may be constructed from an end segment supported over a PON, and switched to one or more segments supported over an MPLS PSN. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeH. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehR. Zheng¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘˚hfilenamegrfc6456eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128308hkeywordsÜdMPLScPSNcPONeG-PONfXG-PONdOMCIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN‘2~granking8crfcd6456irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Multi-Segment Pseudowires in Passive Optical NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyéThe WebSocket Protocol enables two-way communication between a client running untrusted code in a controlled environment to a remote host that has opted-in to communications from that code.  The security model used for this is the origin-based security model commonly used by web browsers.  The protocol consists of an opening handshake followed by basic message framing, layered over TCP.  The goal of this technology is to provide a mechanism for browser-based applications that need two-way communication with servers that does not rely on opening multiple HTTP connections (e.g., using XMLHttpRequest or <iframe>s and long polling). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehI. Fette¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘¯hfilenamegrfc6455eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdhybidfullx-hybi - BiDirectional or Server-Initiated HTTPdnamex&BiDirectional or Server-Initiated HTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128307hkeywordsÉrHyBi Working GroupdHYBIiwebsocketkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateNÂ,"granking7crfcd6455irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevThe WebSocket ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8307d8441d7936∂habstractyhThis document defines the concept of an "origin", which is often used as the scope of authority or privilege by user agents.  Typically, user agents isolate content retrieved from different origins to prevent malicious web site operators from interfering with the operation of benign web sites.  In addition to outlining the principles that underlie the concept of origin, this document details how to determine the origin of a URI and how to serialize an origin into a string.  It also defines an HTTP header field, named "Origin", that indicates which origins are associated with an HTTP request. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehA. Barthddatej‘¯hfilenamegrfc6454eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfwebsecdfulluwebsec - Web SecuritydnamelWeb Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128306hkeywordsÑksame-originfpolicyhsecuritylcross-originkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÂ,granking6crfcd6454irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevThe Web Origin ConceptdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˝This document describes a URN (Uniform Resource Name) namespace that is engineered by the Open Grid Forum (OGF) for naming persistent resources.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdSARAdnamekF. Dijkstra¢kaffiliationeDANTEdnameoR. Hughes-Jonesddatej‘˜hfilenamegrfc6453eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128305hkeywordsÅjidentifierkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateNÔc˜granking5crfcd6453irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-A URN Namespace for the Open Grid Forum (OGF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis memo documents IETF consensus for Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA) derived character properties related to the three code points, existing in Unicode 5.2, that changed property values when version 6.0 was released.  The consensus is that no update is needed to RFC 5892 based on the changes made in Unicode 6.0. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelP. Faltstrom¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej‘Áhfilenamegrfc6452eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128304hkeywordsÇcDNScIDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNø_£granking4crfcd6452irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`The Unicode Code Points and Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA) - Unicode 6.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXAn important class of location-based services answers the question, "What instances of this service are closest to me?" Examples include finding restaurants, gas stations, stores, automated teller machines, wireless access points (hot spots), or parking spaces.  Currently, the Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) protocol only supports mapping locations to a single service based on service regions.  This document describes an extension that allows queries of the type "N nearest", "within distance X", and "served by".  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehA. Forte¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej‘∏hfilenamegrfc6451eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128303hkeywordsÑwlocation-based serviceshlocationcGPSqpoint of interestkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN·Dgranking3crfcd6451irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Protocol ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThe Multicast Ping Protocol specified in this document allows for checking whether an endpoint can receive multicast -- both Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) and Any-Source Multicast (ASM).  It can also be used to obtain additional multicast-related information, such as multicast tree setup time.  This protocol is based on an implementation of tools called "ssmping" and "asmping". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaasddatej‘˙hfilenamegrfc6450eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-128302hkeywordsÑcssmcasmgssmpinggasmpingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN·7granking2crfcd6450irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewMulticast Ping ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüComplaint Feedback Loops similar to those described herein have existed for more than a decade, resulting in many de facto standards and best practices. This document is an attempt to codify, and thus clarify, the ways that both providers and consumers of these feedback mechanisms intend to use the feedback, describing some already common industry practices.

This document is the result of cooperative efforts within the Messaging Anti-Abuse Working Group, a trade organization separate from the IETF. The original MAAWG document upon which this document is based was published in April, 2010. This document does not represent the consensus of the IETF; rather it is being published as an Informational RFC to make it widely available to the Internet community and simplify reference to this material from IETF work. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx"Messaging Anti-Abuse Working GroupdnamegJ. Falkddatej‘Íhfilenamegrfc6449eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128301hkeywordsÖeMAAWGcARFdMARFmfeedback loopnspam reportingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNƒîégranking1crfcd6449irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Complaint Feedback Loop Operational RecommendationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Kerberos 5 KRB-CRED message is used to transfer Kerberos credentials between applications.  When used with a secure transport, the unencrypted form of the KRB-CRED message may be desirable.  This document describes the unencrypted form of the KRB-CRED message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamehR. Yountddatej‘ıhfilenamegrfc6448eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128300hkeywordsÅjcredentialkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN√Ñngranking0crfcd6448irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3