 controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO"›‰granking.crfcd6446irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Event Notification Extension for Notification Rate ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used to support two fast reroute (FRR) methods for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS)-based traffic engineering (TE).  The two methods are the one-to-one backup method and the facility backup method. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoCA TechnologiesdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Koushik¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamehR. Cetinddatej‘ıhfilenamegrfc6445eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128297hkeywordsÖcmibcfrrxMPLS-FRR-GENERAL-STD-MIBxMPLS-FRR-ONE2ONE-STD-MIBxMPLS-FRR-FACILITY-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateN√ÑWgranking-crfcd6445irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering Management Information Base for Fast ReroutedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThe emergency services architecture developed in the IETF Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technology (ECRIT) working group describes an architecture where location information is provided by access networks to endpoints or Voice over IP (VoIP) service providers in order to determine the correct dial string and information to route the call to a Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP). To determine the PSAP Uniform Resource Identifier (URI), the usage of the Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) protocol is envisioned.

This document provides a problem statement and lists requirements for situations where the Internet Access Provider (IAP) and/or the Internet Service Provider (ISP) are only willing to disclose limited or no location information. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamehL. Liess¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Stark¢kaffiliationeSkypednamehA. Kuettddatej‘õhfilenamegrfc6444eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128296hkeywordsÑnemergency callgprivacydPSAPuLocation by ReferencekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateOÿügranking,crfcd6444irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Location Hiding: Problem Statement and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;The IETF has standardized various aspects of placing emergency calls.  This document describes how all of those component parts are used to support emergency calls from citizens and visitors to authorities.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehB. Rosen¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationrTranTech/MediaSolvdnameiA. NewtonddatejÕŒhfilenamegrfc6443eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128295hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateNÚ≠granking+crfcd6443irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Framework for Emergency Calling Using Internet MultimediadtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7852∂habstractyeThis document defines an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) to convey geographic location information from one SIP entity to another SIP entity.  The SIP extension covers end-to-end conveyance as well as location-based routing, where SIP intermediaries make routing decisions based upon the location of the Location Target. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehB. Rosen¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej‘Ùhfilenamegrfc6442eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128294hkeywordsÉcsipsgeographic locationolocation targetkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateNŸkÜgranking*crfcd6442irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Location Conveyance for the Session Initiation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8262d8787∑habstractyyIt has been common for network administrators to filter IP traffic from and BGP prefixes of unallocated IPv4 address space. Now that there are no longer any unallocated IPv4 /8s, this practise is more complicated, fragile, and expensive. Network administrators are advised to remove filters based on the registration status of the address space.

This document explains why any remaining packet and BGP prefix filters for unallocated IPv4 /8s should now be removed on border routers and documents those IPv4 unicast prefixes that should not be routed across the public Internet. This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiL. Vegodaddatej‘˚hfilenamegrfc6441eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128293hkeywordsÑfbogonsdIPv4hmartiansgfilterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN‘2ngranking)crfcd6441irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber´etotaletitlex:Time to Remove Filters for Previously Unallocated IPv4 /8sdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇIn order to derive a Domain-Specific Root Key (DSRK) from the Extended Master Session Key (EMSK) generated as a side effect of an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) method, the EAP peer must discover the name of the domain to which it is attached.

This document specifies a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol Version 6 (DHCPv6) option designed to allow a DHCPv6 server to inform clients using the EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP) EAP method of the name of the local domain for ERP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegY. Wangddatej‘‚hfilenamegrfc6440eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128292hkeywordsÑqre-authenticationhhandovercLDNiDiscoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÎØggranking(crfcd6440irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHThe EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP) Local Domain Name DHCPv6 OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?The IETF TRILL (TRansparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol provides least cost pair-wise data forwarding without configuration in multi-hop networks with arbitrary topology, safe forwarding even during periods of temporary loops, and support for multipathing of both unicast and multicast traffic. TRILL accomplishes this by using IS-IS (Intermediate System to Intermediate System) link state routing and by encapsulating traffic using a header that includes a hop count. Devices that implement TRILL are called "RBridges" (Routing Bridges).

TRILL supports multi-access LAN (Local Area Network) links that can have multiple end stations and RBridges attached. Where multiple RBridges are attached to a link, native traffic to and from end stations on that link is handled by a subset of those RBridges called "Appointed Forwarders", with the intent that native traffic in each VLAN (Virtual LAN) be handled by at most one RBridge. The purpose of this document is to improve the documentation of the Appointed Forwarder mechanism; thus, it updates RFC 6325. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjIntel LabsdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekD. Eastlake¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeY. Li¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekA. Banerjee¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnameeF. Huddatej‘¯hfilenamegrfc6439eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128291hkeywordsÇetrillx,TRansparent Interconnection of Lots of LinkskobsoletedByÅd8139epagesopublicationDateNÀ;bgranking'crfcd6439irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Routing Bridges (RBridges): Appointed ForwardersdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7180∂habstractyThe IPv6 flow label has certain restrictions on its use.  This document describes how those restrictions apply when using the flow label for load balancing by equal cost multipath routing and for link aggregation, particularly for IP-in-IPv6 tunneled traffic. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationgLevel 3dnameiS. Amanteddatej‘Ùhfilenamegrfc6438eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128290hkeywordsÇdECMPcLAGkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateN∞}ogranking&crfcd6438irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZUsing the IPv6 Flow Label for Equal Cost Multipath Routing and Link Aggregation in TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıThis document specifies the IPv6 Flow Label field and the minimum requirements for IPv6 nodes labeling flows, IPv6 nodes forwarding labeled packets, and flow state establishment methods. Even when mentioned as examples of possible uses of the flow labeling, more detailed requirements for specific use cases are out of the scope for this document.

The usage of the Flow Label field enables efficient IPv6 flow classification based only on IPv6 main header fields in fixed positions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgLevel 3dnameiS. Amante¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamelJ. RajahalmeddatejÕ«hfilenamegrfc6437eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128289hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN∞}Zgranking%crfcd6437irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPv6 Flow Label SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	Various published proposals for use of the IPv6 flow label are incompatible with its original specification in RFC 3697.  Furthermore, very little practical use is made of the flow label, partly due to some uncertainties about the correct interpretation of the specification.  This document discusses and motivates changes to the specification in order to clarify it and to introduce some additional flexibility.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgLevel 3dnameiS. Amante¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Jiangddatej‘Ûhfilenamegrfc6436eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128288hkeywordsÇdECMPcLAGkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN∞}Lgranking$crfcd6436irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Rationale for Update to the IPv6 Flow Label SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜTwo useful Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) functions in a transport network are "lock" and "loopback". The lock function enables an operator to lock a transport path such that it does not carry client traffic, but can continue to carry OAM messages and may carry test traffic. The loopback function allows an operator to set a specific node on the transport path into loopback mode such that it returns all received data.

This document specifies the lock function for MPLS networks and describes how the loopback function operates in MPLS networks.

This document updates Sections 7.1.1 and 7.1.2 of RFC 6371. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationlArktan, Inc.dnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelM. Vigoureux¢kaffiliationoZTE CorporationdnamefX. Daiddatej‘˚hfilenamegrfc6435eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128287hkeywordsÑcoamjoperationsnadministrationoand maintenancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNƒ¥5granking#crfcd6435irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;MPLS Transport Profile Lock Instruct and Loopback FunctionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document defines requirements for IPv6 nodes.  It is expected that IPv6 will be deployed in a wide range of devices and situations.  Specifying the requirements for IPv6 nodes allows IPv6 to function well and interoperate in a large number of situations and deployments.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwSRI International, Inc.dnamemE. Jankiewicz¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiT. Nartenddatej‘Úhfilenamegrfc6434eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128286hkeywordsÉxInternet Protocol Version 6qInternet ProtocolbIPkobsoletedByÅd8504epagesopublicationDateNÚ¢granking"crfcd6434irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIPv6 Node RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThe IETF intends to provide a new tool to Working Group chairs and Area Directors for the creation and updating of milestones for Working Group charters.  This document describes the requirements for the proposed new tool, and it is intended as input to a later activity for the design and development of such a tool.  This document updates RFC 6292.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej‘˚hfilenamegrfc6433eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggenareadfullx#genarea - General Area Open MeetingdnamexGeneral Area Open Meetingdtypebagbidjdoc-128285hkeywordsÇuworking group chartergcharterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN‘2Ygranking!crfcd6433irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Requirements for a Working Group Milestones TooldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*Although the use of the SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) Reason header field in responses is considered in general in RFC 3326, its use is not specified for any particular response code.  Nonetheless, existing deployments have been using Reason header fields to carry failure-related Q.850 cause codes in SIP responses to INVITE requests that have been gatewayed to Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) systems.  This document normatively describes the use of the Reason header field in carrying Q.850 cause codes in SIP responses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnameiR. Jesske¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamehL. Liessddatej‘˘hfilenamegrfc6432eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructu