ywordsÇcarfvabuse reporting formatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNºx‚grankingcrfcd6430irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Email Feedback Report Type Value: not-spamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document clarifies the Zero Window Probes (ZWPs) described in RFC 1122 ("Requirements for Internet Hosts -- Communication Layers").  In particular, it clarifies the actions that can be taken on connections that are experiencing the ZWP condition.  Rather than making a change to the standard, this document clarifies what has been until now a misinterpretation of the standard as specified in RFC 1122.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvOcarina Networks, Inc.dnamejM. Bashyam¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameoM. Jethanandani¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Ramaiahddatej‘◊hfilenamegrfc6429eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128281hkeywordsÉqzero window probewdenial of service (DoS)vsecurity vulnerabilitykobsoletedByÅd9293epagesopublicationDateN·+grankingcrfcd6429irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.TCP Sender Clarification for Persist ConditiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåContinuity Check, Proactive Connectivity Verification, and Remote Defect Indication functionalities are required for MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM).

Continuity Check monitors a Label Switched Path for any loss of continuity defect. Connectivity Verification augments Continuity Check in order to provide confirmation that the desired source is connected to the desired sink. Remote Defect Indication enables an end point to report, to its associated end point, a fault or defect condition that it detects on a pseudowire, Label Switched Path, or Section.

This document specifies specific extensions to Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) and methods for proactive Continuity Check, Continuity Verification, and Remote Defect Indication for MPLS-TP pseudowires, Label Switched Paths, and Sections using BFD as extended by this memo. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehD. Allan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamehJ. Drakeddatej‘Ôhfilenamegrfc6428eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128280hkeywordságmpls-tpcoamjOperationsnAdministrationoand Maintenancecbfdx#bidirectional forwarding dectectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNƒ¥grankingcrfcd6428irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrProactive Connectivity Verification, Continuity Check, and Remote Defect Indication for the MPLS Transport ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7214∂habstractyÿThis document specifies Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) messages to indicate service disruptive conditions for MPLS-based transport network Label Switched Paths.  The notification mechanism employs a generic method for a service disruptive condition to be communicated to a Maintenance Entity Group End Point.  This document defines an MPLS OAM channel, along with messages to communicate various types of service disruptive conditions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelA. Fulignoli¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelM. Vigoureux¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamegD. Wardddatej‘ˆhfilenamegrfc6427eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128279hkeywordsÅhmpls-oamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNƒ≥˙grankingcrfcd6427irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGMPLS Fault Management Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7214∂habstracty÷Label Switched Path Ping (LSP ping) is an existing and widely deployed Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) mechanism for Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  This document describes extensions to LSP ping so that LSP ping can be used for on-demand connectivity verification of MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) LSPs and pseudowires.  This document also clarifies procedures to be used for processing the related OAM packets.  Further, it describes procedures for using LSP ping to perform connectivity verification and route tracing functions in MPLS-TP networks.  Finally, this document updates RFC 4379 by adding a new address type and creating an IANA registry. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejN. Bahadur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliation`dnamekR. Aggarwalddatej‘¯hfilenamegrfc6426eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128278hkeywordsÉhlsp pinggmpls tpgmpls-tpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN√Ñ8grankingcrfcd6426irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:MPLS On-Demand Connectivity Verification and Route TracingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯Recent proposals have extended the scope of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) to encompass point-to-multipoint (P2MP) LSPs.

The requirement for a simple and efficient mechanism that can be used to detect data-plane failures in point-to-point (P2P) MPLS LSPs has been recognized and has led to the development of techniques for fault detection and isolation commonly referred to as "LSP ping".

The scope of this document is fault detection and isolation for P2MP MPLS LSPs. This documents does not replace any of the mechanisms of LSP ping, but clarifies their applicability to MPLS P2MP LSPs, and extends the techniques and mechanisms of LSP ping to the MPLS P2MP environment.

This document updates RFC 4379. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Saxena¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamekS. Yasukawa¢kaffiliationoCA TechnologiesdnameiT. Nadeauddatej‘˜hfilenamegrfc6425eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128277hkeywordsÅdp2mpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN√Ñ"grankingcrfcd6425irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRDetecting Data-Plane Failures in Point-to-Multipoint MPLS - Extensions to LSP PingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis document describes methods for performing LSP ping (specified in RFC 4379) traceroute over MPLS tunnels and for traceroute of stitched MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  The techniques outlined in RFC 4379 are insufficient to perform traceroute Forwarding Equivalency Class (FEC) validation and path discovery for an LSP that goes over other MPLS tunnels or for a stitched LSP.  This document deprecates the Downstream Mapping TLV (defined in RFC 4379) in favor of a new TLV that, along with other procedures outlined in this document, can be used to trace such LSPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejN. Bahadur¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallowddatej‘Ùhfilenamegrfc6424eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128276hkeywordsÉhMPLS OAMhlsp pinghLSP-PingkobsoletedByÅd8029epagesopublicationDat