This document updates RFC 3315 (DHCPv6) by making explicit the implicit requirement that relay agents not modify the content of encapsulation payloads as they are relayed back toward clients. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej‘¯hfilenamegrfc6422eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128274hkeywordsÇlDHCPv6 RelaymDHCPv6 optionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÎØXgrankingcrfcd6422irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRelay-Supplied DHCP OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxËThis memo describes the requirements for a crypto-agility solution for Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuElbrys Networks, Inc.dnameiD. NelsonddatejÕîhfilenamegrfc6421eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128273hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNø_ágrankingcrfcd6421irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSCrypto-Agility Requirements for Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÕThis document introduces a new type of PIM Join Attribute that extends PIM signaling to identify a topology that should be used when constructing a particular multicast distribution tree. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameeH. OuddatejÕÀhfilenamegrfc6420eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-128272hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN∞}grankingcrfcd6420irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,PIM Multi-Topology ID (MT-ID) Join AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybAn increasing number of hosts are operating in multiple-interface environments.  This document summarizes current practices in this area and describes in detail how some common operating systems cope with challenges that ensue from this context.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvPainless Security, LLCdnamelM. Wasserman¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamehP. Seiteddatej‘Úhfilenamegrfc6419eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcmifdfullxmif - Multiple InterfacesdnamesMultiple Interfacesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128271hkeywordsÉqcurrent practiceslmulti-homingcMIFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÀ;Cgrankingcrfcd6419irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Current Practices for Multiple-Interface HostsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document describes issues encountered by a node attached to multiple provisioning domains.  This node receives configuration information from each of its provisioning domains, where some configuration objects are global to the node and others are local to the interface.  Issues such as selecting the wrong interface to send traffic happen when conflicting node-scoped configuration objects are received and inappropriately used.  Moreover, other issues are the result of simultaneous attachment to multiple networks, such as domain selection or addressing and naming space overlaps, regardless of the provisioning mechanism.  While multiple provisioning domains are typically seen on nodes with multiple interfaces, this document also discusses situations involving single-interface nodes.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchet¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamehP. Seiteddatej‘Ûhfilenamegrfc6418eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcmifdfullxmif - Multiple InterfacesdnamesMultiple Interfacesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128270hkeywordsÑlmulti-homingcMIFcDNSdDHCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÀ;4grankingcrfcd6418irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Multiple Interfaces and Provisioning Domains Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÕThe development of new technology is driven by scientific research. The Internet, with its roots in the ARPANET and NSFNet, is no exception. Many of the fundamental, long-term improvements to the architecture, security, end-to-end protocols and management of the Internet originate in the related academic research communities. Even shorter-term, more commercially driven extensions are oftentimes derived from academic research. When interoperability is required, the IETF standardizes such new technology. Timely and relevant standardization benefits from continuous input and review from the academic research community.

For an individual researcher, it can however be quite puzzling how to begin to most effectively participate in the IETF and arguably to a much lesser degree, the IRTF. The interactions in the IETF are much different than those in academic conferences, and effective participation follows different rules. The goal of this document is to highlight such differences and provide a rough guideline that will hopefully enable researchers new to the IETF to become successful contributors more quickly. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley EddygauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardley¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationjNEC EuropednameiR. WinterddatejÕ∏hfilenamegrfc6417eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128269hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN∞}grankingcrfcd6417irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex>How to Contribute Research Results to Internet StandardizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document describes Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload formats for carrying each of MPEG-4 Audio and MPEG-4 Visual bitstreams without using MPEG-4 Systems. This document obsoletes RFC 3016. It contains a summary of changes from RFC 3016 and discusses backward compatibility to RFC 3016. It is a necessary revision of RFC 3016 in order to correct misalignments with the 3GPP Packet- switched Streaming Service (PSS) specification regarding the RTP payload format for MPEG-4 Audio.

For the purpose of directly mapping MPEG-4 Audio/Visual bitstreams onto RTP packets, this document provides specifications for the use of RTP header fields and also specifies fragmentation rules. It also provides specifications for Media Type registration and the use of the Session Description Protocol (SDP). The audio payload format described in this document has some limitations related to the signaling of audio codec parameters for the required multiplexing format. Therefore, new system designs should utilize RFC 3640, which does not have these restrictions. Nevertheless, this revision of RFC 3016 is provided to update and complete the specification and to enable interoperable implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrDolby LaboratoriesdnamejM. Schmidt¢kaffiliationsPhilips ElectronicsdnamejF. de Bont¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer IISdnameiS. Doehla¢kaffiliationsLG Electronics Inc.dnamefJ. Kimddatej‘ˆhfilenamegrfc6416eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128268hkeywordsägRFC3016cRTPfMPEG-4eAudiofVisualeVideocAACfHE AACiHE AAC v2mMPEG SurroundkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateNù∆Xgrankingcrfcd6416irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2RTP Payload Format for MPEG-4 Audio/Visual StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxúThis specification describes a method for locating host metadata as well as information about individual resources controlled by the host. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameoE. Hammer-Lahav¢kaffiliation`dnamegB. CookddatejÕühfilenamegrfc6415eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128267hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNØ{/grankingcrfcd6415irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqWeb Host MetadatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis document provides common terminology and metrics for benchmarking the performance of sub-IP layer protection mechanisms.  The performance benchmarks are measured at the IP layer; protection may be provided at the sub-IP layer.  The benchmarks and terminology can be applied in methodology documents for different sub-IP layer protection mechanisms such as Automatic Protection Switching (APS), Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP), Stateful High Availability (HA), and Multiprotocol Label Switching Fast Reroute (MPLS-FRR).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtAllot CommunicationsdnamekS. Poretsky¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejR. Papneja¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Karthik¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. VapiwaladdatejÕXhfilenamegrfc6414eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128266hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateNÀ;(grankingcrfcd6414irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Benchmarking Terminology for Protection PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document describes the methodology for benchmarking Link-State Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) Route Convergence.  The methodology is to be used for benchmarking IGP convergence time through externally observable (black-box) data-plane measurements.  The methodology can be applied to any link-state IGP, such as IS-IS and OSPF.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtAllot CommunicationsdnamekS. Poretsky¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiB. Imhoff¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. Michielsenddatej‘xhfilenamegrfc6413eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128265hkeywordsÅxInterior Gateway ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateNÀ;grankingcrfcd6413irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHBenchmarking Methodology for Link-State IGP Data-Plane Route ConvergencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document describes the terminology for benchmarking link-state Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) route convergence.  The terminology is to be used for benchmarking IGP convergence time through externally observable (black-box) data-plane measurements.  The terminology can be applied to any link-state IGP, such as IS-IS and OSPF.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtAllot CommunicationsdnamekS. Poretsky¢kaffiliationkF5 NetworksdnameiB. Imhoff¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. MichielsenddatejÕàhfilenamegrfc6412eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128264hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÀ;grankingcrfcd6412irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHTerminology for Benchmarking Link-State IGP Data-Plane Route ConvergencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThe Resource reSerVation Protocol (RSVP) allows hop-by-hop integrity protection of RSVP neighbors.  This requires messages to be cryptographically protected using a shared secret between participating nodes.  This document compares group keying for RSVP with per-neighbor or per-interface keying, and discusses the associated key provisioning methods as well as applicability and limitations of these approaches.  This document also discusses applicability of encrypting RSVP messages.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. Behringer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Weisddatej‘ıhfilenamegrfc6411eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128263hkeywordsÖsRSVP authenticationnRSVP integrityxResource reservation protocoldGDOIxGroup domain of interpretationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNØ{%grankingcrfcd6411irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Applicability of Keying Methods for RSVP SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxˇThis document updates the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) Standards Process defined in RFC 2026.  Primarily, it reduces the Standards Process from three Standards Track maturity levels to two.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationuGeorgetown UniversitydnameiE. BurgerddatejÕ hfilenamegrfc6410eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128262hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNìã“granking
crfcd6410irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber	etotaletitlex3Reducing the Standards Track to Two Maturity LevelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis memo splits message submission from message relay, allowing each service to operate according to its own rules (for security, policy, etc.), and specifies what actions are to be taken by a submission server.

Message relay is unaffected, and continues to use SMTP over port 25.

When conforming to this document, message submission uses the protocol specified here, normally over port 587.

This separation of function offers a number of benefits, including the ability to apply specific security or policy requirements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuQUALCOMM IncorporateddnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘Ùhfilenamegrfc6409eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcyamdfullvyam - Yet Another MaildnamepYet Another Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-128261hkeywordsÖdTexttInternationalizationeASCIIgUnicodeeUTF-8kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN√É˛granking	crfcd6409irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberHetotaletitlexMessage Submission for MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8314∂habstracty«The Diameter base protocol specifies mechanisms whereby a given realm may advertise Diameter nodes and the supported transport protocol.  However, these mechanisms do not reveal the Diameter applications that each node supports.  A peer outside the realm would have to perform a Diameter capability exchange with every node until it discovers one that supports the required application.  This document updates RFC 3588, "Diameter Base Protocol", and describes an improvement using an extended format for the Straightforward-Naming Authority Pointer (S-NAPTR) application service tag that allows for discovery of the supported applications without doing Diameter capability exchange beforehand. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnameiL. Morandddatej‘Ôhfilenamegrfc6408eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128260hkeywordsÇnServices FieldnPeer DiscoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNø_wgrankingcrfcd6408irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADiameter Straightforward-Naming Authority Pointer (S-NAPTR) UsagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèThis document describes the Group Domain of Interpretation (GDOI) protocol specified in RFC 3547.  The GDOI provides group key management to support secure group communications according to the architecture specified in RFC 4046.  The GDOI manages group security associations, which are used by IPsec and potentially other data security protocols.  This document replaces RFC 3547. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Weis¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Rowles¢kaffiliationcMITdnamekT. HardjonoddatejÕ¡hfilenamegrfc6407eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128259hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9838epages@opublicationDateNò‰
grankingcrfcd6407irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"The Group Domain of InterpretationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis document defines a peering architecture for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and its functional components and interfaces.  It also describes the components and the steps necessary to establish a session between two SIP Service Provider (SSP) peering domains.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamehD. Malas¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. LivingoodddatejÕâhfilenamegrfc6406eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymispeermintdfullx7speermint - Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdnamex+Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdtypebwgbidjdoc-128258hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN¥ëÅgrankingcrfcd6406irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDSession PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnect (SPEERMINT) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThis document depicts many common Voice over IP (VoIP) use cases for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) peering.  These use cases are categorized into static and on-demand, and then further sub- categorized into direct and indirect.  These use cases are not an exhaustive set, but rather the most common use cases deployed today.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoGlobal CrossingdnameiA. Uzelac¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednamefY. Leeddatej‘ûhfilenamegrfc6405eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymispeermintdfullx7speermint - Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdnamex+Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdtypebwgbidjdoc-128257hkeywordsÇdVoIPkSIP PeeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN¥ëvgrankingcrfcd6405irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Voice over IP (VoIP) SIP Peering Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=The Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnect (SPEERMINT) working group (WG) provides a peering framework that leverages the building blocks of existing IETF-defined protocols such as SIP and ENUM for the interconnection between SIP Service Providers (SSPs).  The objective of this document is to identify and enumerate SPEERMINT- specific threat vectors and to give guidance for implementers on selecting appropriate countermeasures.  Security requirements for SPEERMINT that have been derived from the threats detailed in this document can be found in RFC 6271; this document provides concrete countermeasures to meet those SPEERMINT security requirements.  In this document, the different security threats related to SPEERMINT are classified into threats to the Lookup Function (LUF), the Location Routing Function (LRF), the Signaling Function (SF), and the Media Function (MF) of a specific SIP Service Provider.  Various instances of the threats are briefly introduced inside the classification.  Finally, existing security solutions for SIP and RTP/RTCP (Real-time Transport Control Protocol) are presented to describe countermeasures currently available for such threats.  Each SSP may have connections to one or more remote SSPs through peering or transit contracts.  A potentially compromised remote SSP that attacks other SSPs is out of the scope of this document; this document focuses on attacks on an SSP from outside the trust domain such an SSP may have with other SSPs.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Seedorf¢kaffiliationcNECdnamelS. Niccolini¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationjVOIPFUTUREdnameiH. Scholzddatej‘ˆhfilenamegrfc6404eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymispeermintdfullx7speermint - Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdnamex+Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdtypebwgbidjdoc-128256hkeywordsédVoIPhSecuritygThreatsjmultimediavThreat countermeasurespSIP InterconnectlVoIP peeringpFraud preventionrNetwork protectioncSIPcRTPdRTCPmcontrol planejuser planekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN¥ëigrankingcrfcd6404irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfSession PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnect (SPEERMINT) Security Threats and Suggested CountermeasuresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›The United States government has published guidelines for "NSA Suite\0B Cryptography", which defines cryptographic algorithm policy for national security applications.  This document specifies a profile of the Certificate Management over CMS (CMC) protocol for managing Suite B X.509 public key certificates.  This profile is a refinement of RFCs 5272, 5273, and 5274.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejL. Zieglar¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliation`dnamegM. Peckddatej‘∂hfilenamegrfc6403eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128255hkeywordsÇccmcx%suite b x.509 public key certificateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNÀ:ıgrankingcrfcd6403irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Suite B Profile of Certificate Management over CMSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThis document contains a set of updates to the base syntax for CMC, a Certificate Management protocol using the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS). This document updates RFC 5272, RFC 5273, and RFC 5274.

The new items in this document are: new controls for future work in doing server side key generation, definition of a Subject Information Access value to identify CMC servers, and the registration of a port number for TCP/IP for the CMC service to run on. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej‘◊hfilenamegrfc6402eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-128254hkeywordsÅxcyrptographic message syntaxkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateNÀ:€grankingcrfcd6402irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Certificate Management over CMS (CMC) UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊSome applications require the ability to provide an elevated probability of session establishment to specific sessions in times of network congestion. When supported over the Internet Protocol suite, this may be facilitated through a network-layer admission control solution that supports prioritized access to resources (e.g., bandwidth). These resources may be explicitly set aside for prioritized sessions, or may be shared with other sessions. This document specifies extensions to the Resource reSerVation Protocol (RSVP) that can be used to support such an admission priority capability at the network layer.

Based on current security concerns, these extensions are intended for use in a single administrative domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. CarlbergddatejÕ0hfilenamegrfc6401eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128253hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateNØ{grankingcrfcd6401irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&RSVP Extensions for Admission PrioritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThe IP Router Alert Option is an IP option that alerts transit routers to more closely examine the contents of an IP packet.  The Resource reSerVation Protocol (RSVP), Pragmatic General Multicast (PGM), the Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP), Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD), Multicast Router Discovery (MRD), and General Internet Signaling Transport (GIST) are some of the protocols that make use of the IP Router Alert Option.  This document discusses security aspects and usage guidelines around the use of the current IP Router Alert Option, thereby updating RFC 2113 and RFC 2711.  Specifically, it provides recommendations against using the Router Alert in the end-to-end open Internet and identifies controlled environments where protocols depending on Router Alert can be used safely.  It also provides recommendations about protection approaches for service providers.  Finally, it provides brief guidelines for Router Alert implementation on routers.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le FaucheurddatejÕ≈hfilenamegrfc6398eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128252hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN®èXgranking˛crfcd6398irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber®etotaletitlex(IP Router Alert Considerations and UsagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˆThis document updates the Multi-threaded Routing Toolkit (MRT) export format for Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) routing information by extending it to include optional terrestrial coordinates of a BGP collector and its BGP peers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamelT. Mandersonddatej‘“hfilenamegrfc6397eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128251hkeywordsÜoGPS CoordinateswTerrestrial CoordinateskBGP SpeakerhBGP PeerlBGP LatitudemBGP LongitudekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNØ{granking˝crfcd6397irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÅMulti-Threaded Routing Toolkit (MRT) Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) Routing Information Export Format with Geo-Location ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis document describes the MRT format for routing information export.  This format was developed in concert with the Multi-threaded Routing Toolkit (MRT) from whence the format takes it name.  The format can be used to export routing protocol messages, state changes, and routing information base contents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmMerit NetworkdnamehL. Blunk¢kaffiliationmMerit NetworkdnamehM. Karir¢kaffiliationrDeepfield NetworksdnamekC. LabovitzddatejÕ¬hfilenamegrfc6396eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128250hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateNØzˇgranking¸crfcd6396irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFMulti-Threaded Routing Toolkit (MRT) Routing Information Export FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¿This document defines a new PIM Hello option to advertise an Interface Identifier that can be used by PIM protocols to uniquely identify an interface of a neighboring router. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Gulrajani¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. VenaasddatejÕπhfilenamegrfc6395eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-128249hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNìäˇgranking˚crfcd6395irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1An Interface Identifier (ID) Hello Option for PIMdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘Many current applications use the certificate-based authentication features in Transport Layer Security (TLS) to allow clients to verify that a connected server properly represents a desired domain name.  Typically, this authentication has been based on PKIX certificate chains rooted in well-known certificate authorities (CAs), but additional information can be provided via the DNS itself.  This document describes a set of use cases in which the DNS and DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) could be used to make assertions that support the TLS authentication process.  The main focus of this document is TLS server authentication, but it also covers TLS client authentication for applications where TLS clients are identified by domain names. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnameiR. Barnesddatej‘ıhfilenamegrfc6394eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddanedfullx1dane - DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdnamex*DNS-based Authentication of Named Entitiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128248hkeywordsÇcTLSdPKIXkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN®èAgranking˙crfcd6394irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPUse Cases and Requirements for DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∫This document moves RFC 4693 to Historic status.  It also obsoletes RFC 4693.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM. Yevstifeyevddatej‘Úhfilenamegrfc6393eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128247hkeywordsÇcIONhhistorickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNv*˙granking˘crfcd6393irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMoving RFC 4693 to HistoricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document surveys deployed and experimental in-network storage systems and describes their applicability for the DECADE (DECoupled Application Data Enroute) architecture.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehR. Alimi¢kaffiliationx InterDigital Communications, LLCdnameiA. Rahman¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegY. Yangddatej‘Ûhfilenamegrfc6392eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdecadedfullx+decade - Decoupled Application Data Enroutednamex"Decoupled Application Data Enroutedtypebwgbidjdoc-128246hkeywordsÉcP2PfDECADEx"DECoupled Application Data EnroutekobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateN¶A_granking¯crfcd6392irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&A Survey of In-Network Storage SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydWhere the payload of a pseudowire comprises a number of distinct flows, it can be desirable to carry those flows over the Equal Cost Multiple Paths (ECMPs) that exist in the packet switched network. Most forwarding engines are able to generate a hash of the MPLS label stack and use this mechanism to balance MPLS flows over ECMPs.

This document describes a method of identifying the flows, or flow groups, within pseudowires such that Label Switching Routers can balance flows at a finer granularity than individual pseudowires. The mechanism uses an additional label in the MPLS label stack. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamehU. Drafz¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekV. Kompella¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehJ. Regan¢kaffiliationxLevel 3 Communications, LLCdnameiS. AmanteddatejÕ√hfilenamegrfc6391eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128245hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN∞|ˆgranking˜crfcd6391irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHFlow-Aware Transport of Pseudowires over an MPLS Packet Switched NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7274∑habstracty'This document describes a framework and a process for developing Performance Metrics of protocols and applications transported over IETF-specified protocols.  These metrics can be used to characterize traffic on live networks and services.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuTelchemy IncorporateddnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. ClaiseddatejÕ¿hfilenamegrfc6390eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpmoldfullx+pmol - Performance Metrics for Other Layersdnamex$Performance Metrics for Other Layersdtypebwgbidjdoc-128244hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNåÛgrankingˆcrfcd6390irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber™etotaletitlex=Guidelines for Considering New Performance Metric DevelopmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKThis document describes procedures for distributing upstream-assigned labels for the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP).  It also describes how these procedures can be used for avoiding branch Label Switching Router (LSR) traffic replication on a LAN for LDP point-to-multipoint (P2MP) Label Switched Paths (LSPs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationfOrangednamekJL. Le RouxddatejÕåhfilenamegrfc6389eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128243hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN∏Ügrankingıcrfcd6389irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&MPLS Upstream Label Assignment for LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document describes extensions to the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) for the setup of point-to-multipoint (P2MP) and multipoint-to-multipoint (MP2MP) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in MPLS networks.  These extensions are also referred to as multipoint LDP.  Multipoint LDP constructs the P2MP or MP2MP LSPs without interacting with or relying upon any other multicast tree construction protocol.  Protocol elements and procedures for this solution are described for building such LSPs in a receiver-initiated manner.  There can be various applications for multipoint LSPs, for example IP multicast or support for multicast in BGP/MPLS Layer 3 Virtual Private Networks (L3VPNs).  Specification of how such applications can use an LDP signaled multipoint LSP is outside the scope of this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. ThomasddatejÕøhfilenamegrfc6388eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128242hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateN¥ë\grankingÙcrfcd6388irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpLabel Distribution Protocol Extensions for Point-to-Multipoint and Multipoint-to-Multipoint Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7358∂habstracty¨This document defines a method for the support of GMPLS asymmetric bandwidth bidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  The approach presented is applicable to any switching technology and builds on the original Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) model for the transport of traffic-related parameters.  This document moves the experiment documented in RFC 5467 to the standards track and obsoletes RFC 5467. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Takacs¢kaffiliationvLabN Consulting L.L.C.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednameiJ. Meuricddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6387eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128241hkeywordsÇdrsvpxresource reservation protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN}µgrankingÛcrfcd6387irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDGMPLS Asymmetric Bandwidth Bidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÔThis document describes the VP8 compressed video data format, together with a discussion of the decoding procedure for the format.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert SparksgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekJ. Bankoski¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekJ. Koleszar¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejL. Quillio¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejJ. Salonen¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamejP. Wilkins¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnameeY. XuddatejÕxhfilenamegrfc6386eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128240hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateNºx’grankingÚcrfcd6386irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex"VP8 Data Format and Decoding GuidedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThe General Area Review Team (Gen-ART) has been doing reviews of Internet-Drafts (I-Ds) since 2004.  This document discusses the experience and the lessons learned over the past 7 years of this process.  The review team initially reviewed the I-Ds before each of the IESG telechats.  Since late 2005, review team members have been assigned to review I-Ds during IETF Last Call, unless no IETF Last Call is necessary for the I-D.  The same reviewer then reviews any updates when the I-D is placed on an IESG telechat agenda.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationsResearch ConsultantdnamehA. Doria¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrand¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenterddatej‘Íhfilenamegrfc6385eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128239hkeywordsÅfgenartkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNù∆IgrankingÒcrfcd6385irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.General Area Review Team (Gen-ART) ExperiencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThe File Transfer Protocol (FTP) has a very long history, and despite the fact that today other options exist to perform file transfers, FTP is still in common use. As such, in situations where some client computers only have IPv6 connectivity while many servers are still IPv4-only and IPv6-to-IPv4 translators are used to bridge that gap, it is important that FTP is made to work through these translators to the best possible extent.

FTP has an active and a passive mode, both as original commands that are IPv4-specific and as extended, IP version agnostic commands. The only FTP mode that works without changes through an IPv6-to-IPv4 translator is extended passive. However, many existing FTP servers do not support this mode, and some clients do not ask for it. This document specifies a middlebox that may solve this mismatch. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInstitute IMDEA NetworksdnamenI. van Beijnumddatej‘Èhfilenamegrfc6384eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128238hkeywordsÉcFTPdSIITeNAT64kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNåÛﬁgrankingcrfcd6384irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCAn FTP Application Layer Gateway (ALG) for IPv6-to-IPv4 TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©The Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) has been extended to support Traffic Engineering (TE) in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks. The protocol enables signaling exchanges to establish Label Switched Paths (LSPs) that traverse nodes and link to provide end-to-end data paths. Each node is programmed with "cross-connect" information as the signaling messages are processed. The cross-connection information instructs the node how to forward data that it receives.

End points of an LSP need to know when it is safe to start sending data so that it is not misdelivered, and so that safety issues specific to optical data-plane technology are satisfied. Likewise, all label switching routers along the path of the LSP need to know when to program their data planes relative to sending and receiving control-plane messages.

This document clarifies and summarizes the RSVP-TE protocol exchanges with relation to the programming of cross-connects along an LSP for both unidirectional and bidirectional LSPs. This document does not define any new procedures or protocol extensions, and defers completely to the documents that provide normative references. The clarifications set out in this document may also be used to help interpret LSP establishment performance figures for MPLS-TE and GMPLS devices. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Shiomoto¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6383eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128237hkeywordsÖgRSVP-TEeGMPLSgMPLS-TEmcross-connectjdata planekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN}®grankingÔcrfcd6383irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaAdvice on When It Is Safe to Start Sending Data on Label Switched Paths Established Using RSVP-TEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty‰This document makes recommendations regarding the use of unique origin autonomous system numbers (ASNs) per node for globally anycasted critical infrastructure services in order to provide routing system discriminators for a given anycasted prefix.  Network management and monitoring techniques, or other operational mechanisms, may employ this new discriminator in whatever manner best accommodates their operating environment.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamekR. Donnelly¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnameiF. Scalzoddatej‘Òhfilenamegrfc6382eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128236hkeywordsécBGPdSIDRdRPKIhsecuritygroutingjoperationsdrootcTLDcDNSdDDOSgpeeringcRIRcIRRdMITMkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateN¶ATgrankingÓcrfcd6382irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber©etotaletitlexWUnique Origin Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs) per Node for Globally Anycasted ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?Several MIME type/subtype combinations exist that can contain different media formats. A receiving agent thus needs to examine the details of such media content to determine if the specific elements can be rendered given an available set of codecs. Especially when the end system has limited resources, or the connection to the end system has limited bandwidth, it is helpful to know from the Content- Type alone if the content can be rendered.

This document specifies two parameters, 'codecs' and 'profiles', that are used with various MIME types or type/subtype combinations to allow for unambiguous specification of the codecs employed by the media formats contained within, or the profile(s) of the overall container format. This document obsoletes RFC 4281; RFC 4281 defines the 'codecs' parameter, which this document retains in a backwards compatible manner with minor clarifications; the 'profiles' parameter is added by this document.

By labeling content with the specific codecs indicated to render the contained media, receiving systems can determine if the codecs are supported by the end system, and if not, can take appropriate action (such as rejecting the content, sending notification of the situation, transcoding the content to a supported type, fetching and installing the required codecs, further inspection to determine if it will be sufficient to support a subset of the indicated codecs, etc.).

Similarly, the profiles can provide an overall indication, to the receiver, of the specifications with which the content complies. This is an indication of the compatibility of the container format and its contents to some specification. The receiver may be able to work out the extent to which it can handle and render the content by examining to see which of the declared profiles it supports, and what they mean. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnQUALCOMM, Inc.dnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiD. Singer¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnameiP. Frojdhddatej‘Óhfilenamegrfc6381eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128235hkeywordsÜecodecicontainereaudioevideod3gppe3gpp2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNX™grankingÌcrfcd6381irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?The 'Codecs' and 'Profiles' Parameters for "Bucket" Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬The United States Government has published guidelines for "NSA Suite B Cryptography" dated July, 2005, which defines cryptographic algorithm policy for national security applications. This document specifies the conventions for using Suite B cryptography in IP Security (IPsec).

Since many of the Suite B algorithms are used in other environments, the majority of the conventions needed for the Suite B algorithms are already specified in other documents. This document references the source of these conventions, with some relevant detail repeated to aid developers who choose to support Suite B. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnameiK. Burgin¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamegM. Peckddatej‘Òhfilenamegrfc6380eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128234hkeywordsÉxcryptographic algorithm policytsecurity applicationtsuite b cryptographykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateNù∆<grankingÏcrfcd6380irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Suite B Profile for Internet Protocol Security (IPsec)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document proposes four cryptographic user interface suites ("UI suites") for IP Security (IPsec), similar to the two suites specified in RFC 4308.  The four new suites provide compatibility with the United States National Security Agency's Suite B specifications.  This document obsoletes RFC 4869, which presented earlier versions of these suites.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamefL. Law¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejJ. Solinasddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6379eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128233hkeywordsÑiUI suitesuuser interface suitesnelliptic curvecikekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNù∆/grankingÎcrfcd6379irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Suite B Cryptographic Suites for IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunications Union Telecommunications Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by the ITU-T.

This document addresses the functionality described in the MPLS-TP Survivability Framework document (RFC 6372) and defines a protocol that may be used to fulfill the function of the Protection State Coordination for linear protection, as described in that document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemY. Weingarten¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejE. Osborne¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekN. Sprecher¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelA. Fulignoliddatej‘Ôhfilenamegrfc6378eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128232hkeywordsÇcPSCx&Protection State Coordination ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateNØzÔgrankingÍcrfcd6378irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Linear ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7214d7271d7324∑habstractyQDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) allows an ADministrative Management Domain (ADMD) to assume some responsibility for a message.  Based on deployment experience with DKIM, this document provides guidance for the use of DKIM with scenarios that include Mailing List Managers (MLMs).  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej‘Íhfilenamegrfc6377eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-128231hkeywordsäeemaillarchitecturelverificationjanti-abusehidentityiintegritykresponsiblefauthorfsenderjoriginatorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNz6ØgrankingÈcrfcd6377irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberßetotaletitlex3DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) and Mailing ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) permits a person, role, or organization that owns the signing domain to claim some responsibility for a message by associating the domain with the message. This can be an author's organization, an operational relay, or one of their agents. DKIM separates the question of the identity of the Signer of the message from the purported author of the message. Assertion of responsibility is validated through a cryptographic signature and by querying the Signer's domain directly to retrieve the appropriate public key. Message transit from author to recipient is through relays that typically make no substantive change to the message content and thus preserve the DKIM signature.

This memo obsoletes RFC 4871 and RFC 5672. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamelM. Kucherawyddatej‘Îhfilenamegrfc6376eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-128230hkeywordséeemaillarchitectureeabuselverificationjanti-abusehidentityiintegritykresponsiblefauthorfsenderjoriginatoroemail filteringmanti-phishingnmail signaturekobsoletedByÄepagesLopublicationDateNz6ügrankingËcrfcd6376irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberLetotaletitlex,DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÑd8301d8616d8463d8553∂habstracty‹Procedures and protocol mechanisms to enable efficient and accurate measurement of packet loss, delay, and throughput in MPLS networks are defined in RFC 6374.

The MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is the set of MPLS protocol functions applicable to the construction and operation of packet- switched transport networks.

This document describes a profile of the general MPLS loss, delay, and throughput measurement techniques that suffices to meet the specific requirements of MPLS-TP.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by the ITU-T. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Frost¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. BryantddatejÕøhfilenamegrfc6375eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128229hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNv*ÏgrankingÁcrfcd6375irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMA Packet Loss and Delay Measurement Profile for MPLS-Based Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhMany service provider service level agreements (SLAs) depend on the ability to measure and monitor performance metrics for packet loss and one-way and two-way delay, as well as related metrics such as delay variation and channel throughput.  This measurement capability also provides operators with greater visibility into the performance characteristics of their networks, thereby facilitating planning, troubleshooting, and network performance evaluation.  This document specifies protocol mechanisms to enable the efficient and accurate measurement of these performance metrics in MPLS networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Frost¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. BryantddatejÕøhfilenamegrfc6374eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128228hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateNv*·grankingÊcrfcd6374irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Packet Loss and Delay Measurement for MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7214∂habstracty?The MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) supports static provisioning of transport paths via a Network Management System (NMS) and dynamic provisioning of transport paths via a control plane. This document provides the framework for MPLS-TP dynamic provisioning and covers control-plane addressing, routing, path computation, signaling, traffic engineering, and path recovery. MPLS-TP uses GMPLS as the control plane for MPLS-TP Label Switched Paths (LSPs). MPLS-TP also uses the pseudowire (PW) control plane for pseudowires. Management-plane functions are out of scope of this document.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by the ITU-T.

This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegL. Fang¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. GrayddatejÕÉhfilenamegrfc6373eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128227hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateNz6ÑgrankingÂcrfcd6373irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Control Plane FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLNetwork survivability is the ability of a network to recover traffic delivery following failure or degradation of network resources. Survivability is critical for the delivery of guaranteed network services, such as those subject to strict Service Level Agreements (SLAs) that place maximum bounds on the length of time that services may be degraded or unavailable.

The Transport Profile of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS-TP) is a packet-based transport technology based on the MPLS data plane that reuses many aspects of the MPLS management and control planes.

This document comprises a framework for the provision of survivability in an MPLS-TP network; it describes recovery elements, types, methods, and topological considerations. To enable data-plane recovery, survivability may be supported by the control plane, management plane, and by Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) functions. This document describes mechanisms for recovering MPLS-TP Label Switched Paths (LSPs). A detailed description of pseudowire recovery in MPLS-TP networks is beyond the scope of this document.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet-based transport network as defined by the ITU-T.

This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekN. Sprecher¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘≠hfilenamegrfc6372eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128226hkeywordsÉjProtectionkRestorationhRecoverykobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateNz6wgranking‰crfcd6372irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Survivability FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡The Transport Profile of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS-TP) is a packet-based transport technology based on the MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) and pseudowire (PW) data-plane architectures.

This document describes a framework to support a comprehensive set of Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) procedures that fulfill the MPLS-TP OAM requirements for fault, performance, and protection-switching management and that do not rely on the presence of a control plane.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunications Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by the ITU-T.

This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamegI. Busi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehD. AllanddatejÕàhfilenamegrfc6371eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128225hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDateNz6jgranking„crfcd6371irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWOperations, Administration, and Maintenance Framework for MPLS-Based Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6435∂habstracty≥This document specifies an initial set of identifiers to be used in the Transport Profile of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS-TP). The MPLS-TP requirements (RFC 5654) require that the elements and objects in an MPLS-TP environment are able to be configured and managed without a control plane. In such an environment, many conventions for defining identifiers are possible. This document defines identifiers for MPLS-TP management and Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) functions compatible with IP/ MPLS conventions.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by the ITU-T. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. GrayddatejÕºhfilenamegrfc6370eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128224hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNytågranking‚crfcd6370irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) protocol defines a standard communication and control mechanism through which a Control Element (CE) can control the behavior of a Forwarding Element (FE).  This document captures the experience of implementing the ForCES protocol and model.  Its aim is to help others by providing examples and possible strategies for implementing the ForCES protocol.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamenO. Koufopavlou¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamejS. DenazisddatejÕ∂hfilenamegrfc6369eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128223hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNytgranking·crfcd6369irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Implementation ExperiencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This document defines protocol extensions and procedures for BGP Provider/Customer Edge router iteration in BGP/MPLS IP VPNs.  These extensions and procedures have the objective of making the usage of the BGP/MPLS IP VPN transparent to the customer network, as far as routing information is concerned. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Marques¢kaffiliationgNTT MCLdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiK. Kumaki¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnamekT. Yamagataddatej‘Ìhfilenamegrfc6368eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128222hkeywordsÜel3vpndiBGPeloopskas-overridemattribute sethattr_setkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN}úgranking‡crfcd6368irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcInternal BGP as the Provider/Customer Edge Protocol for BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9494d7606∂habstractyThis document specifies forty-two cipher suites for the Transport Security Layer (TLS) protocol to support the Camellia encryption algorithm as a block cipher.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamehS. Kanno¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehM. Kandaddatej‘Îhfilenamegrfc6367eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128221hkeywordsÖcTLScGCMxEliptic Curve EncryptionlBlock CiphercpskkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNgÂ{grankingﬂcrfcd6367irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHAddition of the Camellia Cipher Suites to Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty7This document provides specific requirements for an Internet audio codec.  These requirements address quality, sampling rate, bit-rate, and packet-loss robustness, as well as other desirable properties.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgMozilladnamehJ. Valin¢kaffiliationwSkype Technologies S.A.dnamefK. VosddatejÕ¥hfilenamegrfc6366eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecodecdfullx%codec - Internet Wideband Audio CodecdnamexInternet Wideband Audio Codecdtypebwgbidjdoc-128220hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN^k†grankingﬁcrfcd6366irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Requirements for an Internet Audio CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty6This document provides a list of terms used in the IETF when discussing internationalization.  The purpose is to help frame discussions of internationalization in the various areas of the IETF and to help introduce the main concepts to IETF participants.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘Ìhfilenamegrfc6365eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgappsawgdfullx5appsawg - ART Area General Applications Working Groupdnamex+ART Area General Applications Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128219hkeywordsÉdi18njvocabularyetermskobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateNgÂngranking›crfcd6365irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber¶etotaletitlex4Terminology Used in Internationalization in the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬This document specifies the use of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) to describe the parameters required to signal the Forward Error Correction (FEC) Framework Configuration Information between the sender(s) and receiver(s).  This document also provides examples that show the semantics for grouping multiple source and repair flows together for the applications that simultaneously use multiple instances of the FEC Framework. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begenddatej‘øhfilenamegrfc6364eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128218hkeywordsÇqFEC configurationnFEC topologieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNò„˝granking‹crfcd6364irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSession Description Protocol Elements for the Forward Error Correction (FEC) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document describes a framework for using Forward Error Correction (FEC) codes with applications in public and private IP networks to provide protection against packet loss.  The framework supports applying FEC to arbitrary packet flows over unreliable transport and is primarily intended for real-time, or streaming, media.  This framework can be used to define Content Delivery Protocols that provide FEC for streaming media delivery or other packet flows.  Content Delivery Protocols defined using this framework can support any FEC scheme (and associated FEC codes) that is compliant with various requirements defined in this document.  Thus, Content Delivery Protocols can be defined that are not specific to a particular FEC scheme, and FEC schemes can be defined that are not specific to a particular Content Delivery Protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNetflix, Inc.dnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Rocaddatej‘Èhfilenamegrfc6363eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-128217hkeywordsÉrReliable streamingpcontent deliverykFEC schemeskobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateNò„Ôgranking€crfcd6363irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Forward Error Correction (FEC) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8680∂habstractyòThe Electronic Data Interchange - Internet Integration (EDIINT) AS1, AS2, and AS3 messages were designed specifically for the transport of EDI documents.  Since multiple interchanges could be placed within a single EDI document, there was not a need for sending multiple EDI documents in a single message.  As adoption of EDIINT grew, other uses developed aside from single EDI document transport.  Some transactions required multiple attachments to be interpreted together and stored in a single message.  This Informational RFC describes how multiple documents, including non-EDI payloads, can be attached and transmitted in a single EDIINT transport message.  The attachments are stored within the MIME multipart/related structure.  A minimal list of content-types to be supported as attachments is provided.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtDrummond Group, Inc.dnamejK. Meadorsddatej‘Óhfilenamegrfc6362eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128216hkeywordsÑfEDIINTcAS2hMultiplekAttachmentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNXjgranking⁄crfcd6362irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTMultiple Attachments for Electronic Data Interchange - Internet Integration (EDIINT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThe Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) defines a Link Control Protocol (LCP) and a method for negotiating the use of multiprotocol traffic over point-to-point links.  This document describes PPP support for the Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Protocol, allowing direct communication between Routing Bridges (RBridges) via PPP links. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkWorkingCodednamejJ. Carlson¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej‘Êhfilenamegrfc6361eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128215hkeywordsÉwpoint-to-point protocolhrbridgesorouting bridgeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNV÷›grankingŸcrfcd6361irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRPPP Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Protocol Control ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThis document concludes the For Your Information (FYI) sub-series of RFCs, established by RFC 1150 for use by the IETF User Services Area, which no longer exists.  The IESG does not intend to make any further additions to this RFC sub-series, and this document provides a record of this decision.  This document also obsoletes RFC 1150 and changes the status of RFC 1150 to Historic.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. HousleyddatejÕªhfilenamegrfc6360eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128214hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNNˆ0grankingÿcrfcd6360irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Conclusion of FYI RFC Sub-SeriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠This document captures the requirements for integrating IANA and RFC Editor state information into the Datatracker to provide the community with a unified tool to track the status of their document as it progresses from Internet-Draft (I-D) version -00 to RFC.  Extending the Datatracker to hold document data from I-D version -00 to RFC allows for increased automation between the Datatracker, IANA, and RFC Editor, thus reducing manual labor, processing errors, and potential delay.  Therefore, this document also describes the requirements to make such automation possible.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcAMSdnameiS. Ginoza¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationcAMSdnameiA. Morrisddatej‘Ëhfilenamegrfc6359eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggenareadfullx#genarea - General Area Open MeetingdnamexGeneral Area Open Meetingdtypebagbidjdoc-128213hkeywordsÇjid-trackerrbackend extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNgÂVgranking◊crfcd6359irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDatatracker Extensions to Include IANA and RFC Editor Processing InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx÷This document describes a mechanism for using additional master secret inputs with Transport Layer Security (TLS) and Datagram TLS (DTLS).  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej‘Æhfilenamegrfc6358eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128212hkeywordsÉctlsddtlsldatagram tlskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO˝egranking÷crfcd6358irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Additional Master Secret Inputs for TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOverload occurs in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) networks when SIP servers have insufficient resources to handle all SIP messages they receive.  Even though the SIP protocol provides a limited overload control mechanism through its 503 (Service Unavailable) response code, SIP servers are still vulnerable to overload.  This document discusses models and design considerations for a SIP overload control mechanism.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Alcatel-LucentdnamegV. Hilt¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamegE. Noel¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamegC. Shen¢kaffiliationnSonus NetworksdnamejA. Abdelalddatej‘Ïhfilenamegrfc6357eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsocdfullxsoc - SIP Overload ControldnametSIP Overload Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128211hkeywordsÉxSession Initiation ProtocolpOverload ControlsCongestion CollapsekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN^©ygranking’crfcd6357irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDesign Considerations for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Overload ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙Often endpoints are connected by multiple paths, but communications are usually restricted to a single path per connection. Resource usage within the network would be more efficient were it possible for these multiple paths to be used concurrently. Multipath TCP is a proposal to achieve multipath transport in TCP.

New congestion control algorithms are needed for multipath transport protocols such as Multipath TCP, as single path algorithms have a series of issues in the multipath context. One of the prominent problems is that running existing algorithms such as standard TCP independently on each path would give the multipath flow more than its fair share at a bottleneck link traversed by more than one of its subflows. Further, it is desirable that a source with multiple paths available will transfer more traffic using the least congested of the paths, achieving a property called "resource pooling" where a bundle of links effectively behaves like one shared link with bigger capacity. This would increase the overall efficiency of the network and also its robustness to failure.

This document presents a congestion control algorithm that couples the congestion control algorithms running on different subflows by linking their increase functions, and dynamically controls the overall aggressiveness of the multipath flow. The result is a practical algorithm that is fair to TCP at bottlenecks while moving traffic away from congested links. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniv. Politehnica of BucharestdnameiC. Raiciu¢kaffiliationtUniv. College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationtUniv. College LondondnamejD. Wischikddatej‘Ôhfilenamegrfc6356eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128210hkeywordsÉimultipathctcprcongestion controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNò„·granking‘crfcd6356irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Coupled Congestion Control for Multipath Transport ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document defines a new DHCPv6 Unique Identifier (DUID) type called DUID-UUID.  DUID-UUIDs are derived from the already-standardized Universally Unique IDentifier (UUID) format.  DUID-UUID makes it possible for devices to use UUIDs to identify themselves to DHC servers and vice versa.  UUIDs are globally unique and readily available on many systems, making them convenient identifiers to leverage within DHCP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejJ. Johnsonddatej‘Ëhfilenamegrfc6355eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128209hkeywordsÅxuniversally unique identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNEæ
granking”crfcd6355irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADefinition of the UUID-Based DHCPv6 Unique Identifier (DUID-UUID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document defines a simple enhancement to support RTP sessions with forward-shifted redundant encodings, i.e., redundant data sent before the corresponding primary data.  Forward-shifted redundancy can be used to conceal losses of a large number of consecutive media frames (e.g., consecutive loss of seconds or even tens of seconds of media). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamefQ. XieddatejÕµhfilenamegrfc6354eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128208hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNXgranking“crfcd6354irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Forward-Shifted RTP Redundancy Payload SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document describes a Transport Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP), that uses either the Transport Layer Security protocol or the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol. The TLS and DTLS protocols provide authentication and privacy services for SNMP applications. This document describes how the TLS Transport Model (TLSTM) implements the needed features of an SNMP Transport Subsystem to make this protection possible in an interoperable way.

This Transport Model is designed to meet the security and operational needs of network administrators. It supports the sending of SNMP messages over TLS/TCP and DTLS/UDP. The TLS mode can make use of TCP's improved support for larger packet sizes and the DTLS mode provides potentially superior operation in environments where a connectionless (e.g., UDP) transport is preferred. Both TLS and DTLS integrate well into existing public keying infrastructures.

This document also defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols. In particular, it defines objects for managing the TLS Transport Model for SNMP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnamekW. Hardakerddatej‘Áhfilenamegrfc6353eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdismsdfullx)isms - Integrated Security Model for SNMPdnamex"Integrated Security Model for SNMPdtypebwgbididoc-43478hkeywordsÖddtlsx!datagram transport layer securitystls transport modeletlstmoSNMP-TLS-TM-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateN']ﬂgranking—crfcd6353irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberNetotaletitlex`Transport Layer Security (TLS) Transport Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8996d9456∂habstractx·This document defines extensions to the Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) protocol to specify a standard way of accessing, managing, and sharing contact information based on the vCard format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Dabooddatej‘êhfilenamegrfc6352eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhvcarddavdfullxvcarddav - vCard and CardDAVdnameqvCard and CardDAVdtypebwgbidjdoc-128207hkeywordsÉgaddressladdress bookgcontactkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateN^ Ûgranking–crfcd6352irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNCardDAV: vCard Extensions to Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6764∂habstractxPThis document defines the XML schema of the vCard data format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamelS. Perreaultddatej‘Êhfilenamegrfc6351eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhvcarddavdfullxvcarddav - vCard and CardDAVdnameqvCard and CardDAVdtypebwgbidjdoc-128206hkeywordsÅevCardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN^ ‰grankingœcrfcd6351irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxCard: vCard XML RepresentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6868∂habstractysThis document defines the vCard data format for representing and exchanging a variety of information about individuals and other entities (e.g., formatted and structured name and delivery addresses, email address, multiple telephone numbers, photograph, logo, audio clips, etc.).  This document obsoletes RFCs 2425, 2426, and 4770, and updates RFC 2739. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamelS. Perreaultddatej‘Âhfilenamegrfc6350eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhvcarddavdfullxvcarddav - vCard and CardDAVdnameqvCard and CardDAVdtypebwgbidjdoc-128205hkeywordsÅevCardkobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDateN^ ÿgrankingŒcrfcd6350irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexvCard Format SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd6868d9554d9555∂habstracty}This framework describes a practical methodology for measuring end- to-end TCP Throughput in a managed IP network.  The goal is to provide a better indication in regard to user experience.  In this framework, TCP and IP parameters are specified to optimize TCP Throughput.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdJDSUdnamenB. Constantine¢kaffiliationxBell Canada (Ext. Consultant)dnameiG. Forget¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegR. Geib¢kaffiliationrSchrage ConsultingdnamejR. Schrageddatej‘Èhfilenamegrfc6349eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128204hkeywordsÇfmetrickTCP testingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNNê{grankingÕcrfcd6349irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Framework for TCP Throughput TestingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìThis document lists a set of functional requirements that served as input to the design of Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) extensions for setting up point-to-multipoint (P2MP) Label Switched Paths (LSP), in order to deliver point-to-multipoint applications over a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) infrastructure.

This work was overtaken by the protocol solution developed by the MPLS working group, but that solution did not closely follow the requirements documented here. This document is published as a historic record of the ideas and requirements that shaped the protocol work. This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationwFrance Telecom - OrangednamehT. Morinddatej‘Âhfilenamegrfc6348eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128203hkeywordsÖdMPLScLDPjmultipointdP2MPimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNgÂEgrankingÃcrfcd6348irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRRequirements for Point-to-Multipoint Extensions to the Label Distribution ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document specifies version 1.2 of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol.  The DTLS protocol provides communications privacy for datagram protocols.  The protocol allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, or message forgery.  The DTLS protocol is based on the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol and provides equivalent security guarantees.  Datagram semantics of the underlying transport are preserved by the DTLS protocol.  This document updates DTLS 1.0 to work with TLS version 1.2. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekN. Modaduguddatej‘Ïhfilenamegrfc6347eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128202hkeywordsÇddtlsmdtls protocolkobsoletedByÅd9147epages opublicationDateO˝YgrankingÀcrfcd6347irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Datagram Transport Layer Security Version 1.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÑd7905d9146d7507d8996∂habstractyGWe are facing the exhaustion of the IANA IPv4 free IP address pool. Unfortunately, IPv6 is not yet deployed widely enough to fully replace IPv4, and it is unrealistic to expect that this is going to change before the depletion of IPv4 addresses. Letting hosts seamlessly communicate in an IPv4 world without assigning a unique globally routable IPv4 address to each of them is a challenging problem.

This document proposes an IPv4 address sharing scheme, treating some of the port number bits as part of an extended IPv4 address (Address plus Port, or A+P). Instead of assigning a single IPv4 address to a single customer device, we propose to extend the address field by using bits from the port number range in the TCP/UDP header as additional endpoint identifiers, thus leaving a reduced range of ports available to applications. This means assigning the same IPv4 address to multiple clients (e.g., Customer Premises Equipment (CPE), mobile phones), each with its assigned port range. In the face of IPv4 address exhaustion, the need for addresses is stronger than the need to be able to address thousands of applications on a single host. If address translation is needed, the end-user should be in control of the translation process -- not some smart boxes in the core. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. BushddatejÕ±hfilenamegrfc6346eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128201hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateNXgranking crfcd6346irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAThe Address plus Port (A+P) Approach to the IPv4 Address ShortagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document specifies Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) Relay Element functionality, which enables PANA messaging between a PANA Client (PaC) and a PANA Authentication Agent (PAA) where the two nodes cannot reach each other by means of regular IP routing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Duffy¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationdPG&EdnameiR. Cragie¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. Yeginddatej‘Îhfilenamegrfc6345eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128200hkeywordsÇcEAPfZigBeekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNKQ»granking…crfcd6345irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLProtocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) Relay ElementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document describes requirements for, and the use of, the Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) control plane in support of the Virtual Concatenation (VCAT) layer 1 inverse multiplexing data plane mechanism and its companion Link Capacity Adjustment Scheme (LCAS).  LCAS can be used for hitless dynamic resizing of the inverse multiplex group.  These techniques apply to Optical Transport Network (OTN), Synchronous Optical Network (SONET), Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH), and Plesiochronous Digital Hierarchy (PDH) signals.  This document updates RFC 4606 by making modifications to the procedures for supporting virtual concatenation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Rabbat¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoH. van HelvoortddatejÕØhfilenamegrfc6344eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128199hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNNêigranking»crfcd6344irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexçOperating Virtual Concatenation (VCAT) and the Link Capacity Adjustment Scheme (LCAS) with Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides advice to network operators about deployment of the 6to4 technique for automatic tunneling of IPv6 over IPv4.  It is principally addressed to Internet Service Providers (ISPs), including those that do not yet support IPv6, and to Content Providers.  Some advice to implementers is also included.  The intention of the advice is to minimize both user dissatisfaction and help-desk calls.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenterddatej‘Íhfilenamegrfc6343eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128198hkeywordsÅjIPv6 relaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN@ßúgranking«crfcd6343irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Advisory Guidelines for 6to4 DeploymentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!Mobile Internet access from smartphones and other mobile devices is accelerating the exhaustion of IPv4 addresses.  IPv6 is widely seen as crucial for the continued operation and growth of the Internet, and in particular, it is critical in mobile networks.  This document discusses the issues that arise when deploying IPv6 in mobile networks.  Hence, this document can be a useful reference for service providers and network designers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. KoodliddatejÕºhfilenamegrfc6342eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128197hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN6Ãgranking∆crfcd6342irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Mobile Networks Considerations for IPv6 DeploymentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyπSession recording is a critical requirement in many business communications environments, such as call centers and financial trading floors. In some of these environments, all calls must be recorded for regulatory and compliance reasons. In others, calls may be recorded for quality control or business analytics.

Recording is typically performed by sending a copy of the session media to the recording devices. This document specifies requirements for extensions to SIP that will manage delivery of RTP media to a recording device. This is being referred to as SIP-based Media Recording. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Rehor¢kaffiliationlNICE SystemsdnamejL. Portman¢kaffiliationx!Siemens Enterprise CommunicationsdnameiA. Hutton¢kaffiliationkIPC SystemsdnamegR. JainddatejÕØhfilenamegrfc6341eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsiprecdfullvsiprec - SIP RecordingdnamemSIP Recordingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128196hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNEΩ˝granking≈crfcd6341irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAUse Cases and Requirements for SIP-Based Media Recording (SIPREC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxòThis memo defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module containing textual conventions (TCs) to represent floating-point numbers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejR. Presuhnddatej‘Íhfilenamegrfc6340eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128195hkeywordsárNetwork ManagementhIEEE 754nFloating-pointcMIBeSMIv2rTextual ConventionlFLOAT-TC-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN@5>grankingƒcrfcd6340irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDTextual Conventions for the Representation of Floating-Point NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes three abstract Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) interfaces used to encapsulate/decapsulate context tokens and compare OIDs.  This document also specifies C bindings for the abstract interfaces. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnametL. Hornquist AstrandddatejÕ≥hfilenamegrfc6339eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128194hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNV÷»granking√crfcd6339irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexãContext Token Encapsulate/Decapsulate and OID Comparison Functions for the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Schema for Academia (SCHAC).

The namespace described in this document is for naming persistent resources defined by the SCHAC participants internationally, their working groups, and other designated subordinates. The main use of this namespace will be for the creation of controlled vocabulary values for attributes in the SCHAC schema. These values will be associated with particular instances of persons or objects belonging to any of the SCHAC object classes. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUniv. MalagadnameiV. Giralt¢kaffiliationpUniv. QueenslanddnameiR. McDuffddatej‘Ëhfilenamegrfc6338eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128193hkeywordsÇfTERENAgtf-emc2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNEΩÓgranking¬crfcd6338irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYDefinition of a Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Schema for Academia (SCHAC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) utilizes the offer/answer model to establish and update multimedia sessions using the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  The description of the offer/answer model in SIP is dispersed across multiple RFCs.  This document summarizes all the current usages of the offer/answer model in SIP communication.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiSoftfrontdnamejS. Okumura¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiT. Sawada¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Kyzivatddatej‘Ëhfilenamegrfc6337eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128192hkeywordsÑfanswereoffercSDPcSIPkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateNNêUgranking¡crfcd6337irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Usage of the Offer/Answer ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,It has been identified that "Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE): A Protocol for Network Address Translator (NAT) Traversal for Offer/Answer Protocols" (RFC 5245) is missing a registry for ICE options.  This document defines this missing IANA registry and updates RFC 5245. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. PerkinsddatejÕÆhfilenamegrfc6336eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128191hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8839epagesopublicationDateN&¿granking¿crfcd6336irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFIANA Registry for Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty`This document defines the procedures that the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) uses when handling assignment and other requests related to the Service Name and Transport Protocol Port Number registry. It also discusses the rationale and principles behind these procedures and how they facilitate the long-term sustainability of the registry.

This document updates IANA's procedures by obsoleting the previous UDP and TCP port assignment procedures defined in Sections 8 and 9.1 of the IANA Allocation Guidelines, and it updates the IANA service name and port assignment procedures for UDP-Lite, the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP), and the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP). It also updates the DNS SRV specification to clarify what a service name is and how it is registered. This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationeApplednamekS. Cheshireddatej‘Ÿhfilenamegrfc6335eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128190hkeywordsádIANAitransporteportslport numbersjallocationjassignmentjprocedureskobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateN^kïgrankingøcrfcd6335irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber•etotaletitlexàInternet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) Procedures for the Management of the Service Name and Transport Protocol Port Number RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxËThis document specifies a DHCPv6 option that is meant to be used by a Dual-Stack Lite Basic Bridging BroadBand (B4) element to discover the IPv6 address of its corresponding Address Family Transition Router (AFTR). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejD. Hankins¢kaffiliationxGdansk University of TechnologydnamelT. Mrugalskiddatej‘‹hfilenamegrfc6334eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-128189hkeywordsÇhSoftwiregDS-LitekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN@5.grankingæcrfcd6334irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPDynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) Option for Dual-Stack LitedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document revisits the dual-stack model and introduces the Dual- Stack Lite technology aimed at better aligning the costs and benefits of deploying IPv6 in service provider networks.  Dual-Stack Lite enables a broadband service provider to share IPv4 addresses among customers by combining two well-known technologies: IP in IP (IPv4- in-IPv6) and Network Address Translation (NAT). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Durand¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationeApplednamekJ. Woodyatt¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamefY. Leeddatej‘Âhfilenamegrfc6333eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-128188hkeywordsÅcNATkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateN@5 grankingΩcrfcd6333irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Dual-Stack Lite Broadband Deployments Following IPv4 ExhaustiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7335∂habstractyiIn most RTP-based multicast applications, the RTP source sends inter- related data.  Due to this interdependency, randomly joining RTP receivers usually cannot start consuming the multicast data right after they join the session.  Thus, they often experience a random acquisition delay.  An RTP receiver can use one or more different approaches to achieve rapid acquisition.  Yet, due to various factors, performance of the rapid acquisition methods usually varies.  Furthermore, in some cases, the RTP receiver can do a simple multicast join (in other cases, it is compelled to do so).  For quality reporting, monitoring, and diagnostic purposes, it is important to collect detailed information from the RTP receivers about their acquisition and presentation experiences.  This document addresses this issue by defining a new report block type, called the Multicast Acquisition (MA) report block, within the framework of RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Reports (XRs) (RFC 3611).  This document also defines the necessary signaling of the new MA report block type in the Session Description Protocol (SDP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelE. Friedrichddatej‘Áhfilenamegrfc6332eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfavtextdfullx)avtext - Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdnamex Audio/Video Transport Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128187hkeywordsÜcSSMimulticastdIPTVdRAMSqrapid acquisitionsfast channel changekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN32grankingºcrfcd6332irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Multicast Acquisition Report Block Type for RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Reports (XRs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis memo describes problems with the DIGEST-MD5 Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) mechanism as specified in RFC 2831.  It marks DIGEST-MD5 as OBSOLETE in the IANA Registry of SASL mechanisms and moves RFC 2831 to Historic status.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘‡hfilenamegrfc6331eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128186hkeywordsÖdhttpxhypertext transfer protocolhsecurityfsimpleelayerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN°bgrankingªcrfcd6331irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMoving DIGEST-MD5 to HistoricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a Fully-Specified Forward Error Correction (FEC) scheme, corresponding to FEC Encoding ID 6, for the RaptorQ FEC code and its application to reliable delivery of data objects.

RaptorQ codes are a new family of codes that provide superior flexibility, support for larger source block sizes, and better coding efficiency than Raptor codes in RFC 5053. RaptorQ is also a fountain code, i.e., as many encoding symbols as needed can be generated on the fly by the encoder from the source symbols of a source block of data. The decoder is able to recover the source block from almost any set of encoding symbols of sufficient cardinality -- in most cases, a set of cardinality equal to the number of source symbols is sufficient; in rare cases, a set of cardinality slightly more than the number of source symbols is required.

The RaptorQ code described here is a systematic code, meaning that all the source symbols are among the encoding symbols that can be generated. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationdEPFLdnamenA. Shokrollahi¢kaffiliationlNetflix Inc.dnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationnNomor ResearchdnamenT. Stockhammer¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnameiL. Minderddatej‘Èhfilenamegrfc6330eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128185hkeywordsÜhFEC codemfountain codeosystematic codekAL FEC codelSub-blockingsFEC object deliverykobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDateNEΩ›granking∫crfcd6330irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;RaptorQ Forward Error Correction Scheme for Object DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸ802.1aq Shortest Path Bridging (SPB) has been standardized by the IEEE as the next step in the evolution of the various spanning tree and registration protocols.  802.1aq allows for true shortest path forwarding in a mesh Ethernet network context utilizing multiple equal cost paths.  This permits it to support much larger Layer 2 topologies, with faster convergence, and vastly improved use of the mesh topology.  Combined with this is single point provisioning for logical connectivity membership, which includes point-to-point, point-to-multipoint, and multipoint-to-multipoint variations.  This memo documents the IS-IS changes required to support this IEEE protocol and provides some context and examples. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamepP. Ashwood-Smith¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehD. Allan¢kaffiliationmCiena LimiteddnamehA. Bragg¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelP. Unbehagenddatej‘ﬁhfilenamegrfc6329eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128184hkeywordsÅcspbkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateOí`grankingπcrfcd6329irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?IS-IS Extensions Supporting IEEE 802.1aq Shortest Path BridgingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyASome protocols being developed or extended by the IETF make use of the ISO/IEC (International Organization for Standardization / International Electrotechnical Commission) Network Layer Protocol Identifier (NLPID).  This document provides NLPID IANA considerations.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej‘âhfilenamegrfc6328eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128183hkeywordsÅeNLPIDkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateN)†6granking∏crfcd6328irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber§etotaletitlex:IANA Considerations for Network Layer Protocol IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The IETF TRILL (TRansparent Interconnection of Lots of Links) protocol provides optimal pair-wise data forwarding without configuration, safe forwarding even during periods of temporary loops, and support for multipathing of both unicast and multicast traffic. TRILL accomplishes this by using IS-IS (Intermediate System to Intermediate System) link state routing and by encapsulating traffic using a header that includes a hop count. Devices that implement TRILL are called Routing Bridges (RBridges).

TRILL supports multi-access LAN (Local Area Network) links that can have multiple end stations and RBridges attached. This document describes four aspects of the TRILL LAN Hello protocol used on such links, particularly adjacency, designated RBridge selection, and MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit) and pseudonode procedures, with state machines. There is no change for IS-IS point-to-point Hellos used on links configured as point-to-point in TRILL. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationjIntel LabsdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamekA. Ghanwani¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Dutt¢kaffiliationsHewlett Packard Co.dnameiV. Manralddatej‘ﬂhfilenamegrfc6327eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128182hkeywordsÉgRBridgeeTRILLiAdjacencykobsoletedByÅd7177epagesopublicationDateN)†granking∑crfcd6327irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Routing Bridges (RBridges): AdjacencydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7180∂habstractyDThe IETF has standardized the Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) protocol, which provides transparent Layer 2 forwarding using encapsulation with a hop count and IS-IS link state routing.  This document specifies the data formats and code points for the IS-IS extensions to support TRILL. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamekD. Eastlake¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekA. Banerjee¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Dutt¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamekA. Ghanwaniddatej‘–hfilenamegrfc6326eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128181hkeywordsÑeTRILLgRBridgeeIS-ISdISISkobsoletedByÅd7176epagesopublicationDateN)†granking∂crfcd6326irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexATransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL) Use of IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·Routing Bridges (RBridges) provide optimal pair-wise forwarding without configuration, safe forwarding even during periods of temporary loops, and support for multipathing of both unicast and multicast traffic. They achieve these goals using IS-IS routing and encapsulation of traffic with a header that includes a hop count.

RBridges are compatible with previous IEEE 802.1 customer bridges as well as IPv4 and IPv6 routers and end nodes. They are as invisible to current IP routers as bridges are and, like routers, they terminate the bridge spanning tree protocol.

The design supports VLANs and the optimization of the distribution of multi-destination frames based on VLAN ID and based on IP-derived multicast groups. It also allows unicast forwarding tables at transit RBridges to be sized according to the number of RBridges (rather than the number of end nodes), which allows their forwarding tables to be substantially smaller than in conventional customer bridges. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjIntel LabsdnamejR. Perlman¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Dutt¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefS. Gai¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamekA. Ghanwaniddatej‘ïhfilenamegrfc6325eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128180hkeywordsÅeTRILLkobsoletedByÄepagescopublicationDateN)üÚgrankingµcrfcd6325irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Routing Bridges (RBridges): Base Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByéd7455d6439d7780d8361d7783d8139d7172d7177d7179d7180d8377d8249d6327d7357∂habstractyKThis document is concerned with security vulnerabilities in IPv6-in- IPv4 automatic tunnels.  These vulnerabilities allow an attacker to take advantage of inconsistencies between the IPv4 routing state and the IPv6 routing state.  The attack forms a routing loop that can be abused as a vehicle for traffic amplification to facilitate denial- of-service (DoS) attacks.  The first aim of this document is to inform on this attack and its root causes.  The second aim is to present some possible mitigation measures.  It should be noted that at the time of this writing there are no known reports of malicious attacks exploiting these vulnerabilities.  Nonetheless, these vulnerabilities can be activated by accidental misconfiguration.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNEWRSCdnamejG. Nakibly¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamejF. Templinddatej‘‰hfilenamegrfc6324eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128179hkeywordsÉmEncapsulationfISATAPc6rdkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNTÃgranking¥crfcd6324irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Routing Loop Attack Using IPv6 Automatic Tunnels: Problem Statement and Proposed MitigationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document specifies an update to the round-trip time (RTT) estimation algorithm used for TFRC (TCP-Friendly Rate Control) congestion control by the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP). It updates specifications for the CCID-3 and CCID-4 Congestion Control IDs of DCCP.

The update addresses parameter-estimation problems occurring with TFRC-based DCCP congestion control. It uses a recommendation made in the original TFRC specification to avoid the inherent problems of receiver-based RTT sampling, by utilising higher-accuracy RTT samples already available at the sender.

It is integrated into the feature set of DCCP as an end-to-end negotiable extension. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnameiG. Renker¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej‘·hfilenamegrfc6323eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128178hkeywordsÑdDCCPdTFRCfCCID-3fCCID-4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN32granking≥crfcd6323irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNSender RTT Estimate Option for the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document describes extending the IETF Datatracker to capture and display the progression of Internet-Drafts that are intended to be published as RFCs by the IAB, IRTF, or Independent Submissions Editor.  The states and annotations that are to be added to the Datatracker will be applied to Internet-Drafts as soon as any of these streams identify the Internet-Draft as a potential eventual RFC, and will continue through the lifetime of the Internet-Draft.  The goal of adding this information to the Datatracker is to give the whole Internet community more information about the status of these Internet-Drafts and the streams from which they originate.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. HoffmanddatejÕühfilenamegrfc6322eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128177hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN?xgranking≤crfcd6322irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXDatatracker States and Annotations for the IAB, IRTF, and Independent Submission StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxVThis specification defines "xCal", an XML format for iCalendar data. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationcRPIdnamekM. Douglass¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegS. Leesddatej‘Âhfilenamegrfc6321eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128176hkeywordsÅxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateN@5granking±crfcd6321irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"xCal: The XML Format for iCalendardtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7529d6868∂habstractyˆThis document describes the Access Node Control Protocol (ANCP). ANCP operates between a Network Access Server (NAS) and an Access Node (e.g., a Digital Subscriber Line Access Multiplexer (DSLAM)) in a multi-service reference architecture in order to perform operations related to Quality of Service, service, and subscribers. Use cases for ANCP are documented in RFC 5851. As well as describing the base ANCP protocol, this document specifies capabilities for Digital Subscriber Line (DSL) topology discovery, line configuration, and remote line connectivity testing. The design of ANCP allows for protocol extensions in other documents if they are needed to support other use cases and other access technologies.

ANCP is based on the General Switch Management Protocol version 3 (GSMPv3) described in RFC 3292, but with many modifications and extensions, to the point that the two protocols are not interoperable. For this reason, ANCP was assigned a separate version number to distinguish it. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiS. Wadhwa¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Moisand¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegT. Haag¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehN. Voigt¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. Taylorddatej‘‚hfilenamegrfc6320eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdancpdfullx#ancp - Access Node Control ProtocoldnamexAccess Node Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128175hkeywordsÅdancpkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDateNåÛÕgranking∞crfcd6320irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Protocol for Access Node Control Mechanism in Broadband NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7256∂habstractyjWhen a private network or internetwork grows very large, it is sometimes not possible to address all interfaces using private IPv4 address space because there are not enough addresses. This document describes the problems faced by those networks, the available options, and the issues involved in assigning a new block of private IPv4 address space.

While this informational document does not make a recommendation for action, it documents the issues surrounding the various options that have been considered. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx#Frontier Communications CorporationdnamejM. Azinger¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiL. Vegodaddatej‘Áhfilenamegrfc6319eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128174hkeywordsÅoprivate networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN&∞grankingØcrfcd6319irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHIssues Associated with Designating Additional Private IPv4 Address SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document specifies the conventions for using the United States National Security Agency's Suite B algorithms in Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) as specified in RFC 5751.  This document obsoletes RFC 5008.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejJ. SolinasddatejÕ£hfilenamegrfc6318eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128173hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN“ºgrankingÆcrfcd6318irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Suite B in Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|This document defines extensions to the current sockets API for the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  The extensions focus on the use of public-key-based identifiers discovered via DNS resolution, but also define interfaces for manual bindings between Host Identity Tags (HITs) and locators.  With the extensions, the application can also support more relaxed security models where communication can be non-HIP-based, according to local policies.  The extensions in this document are experimental and provide basic tools for further experimentation with policies.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamegM. Komu¢kaffiliationrThe Boeing CompanydnamelT. Hendersonddatej‘èhfilenamegrfc6317eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128172hkeywordsåqhost identity tagvcryptographic identitywcryptographic namespaceksockets APIeShim6ropportunistic modehresolvertHIP wildcard addressfORCHIDxsource address selectionjHIT prefixplocator handlingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN)üÂgranking≠crfcd6317irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFBasic Socket Interface Extensions for the Host Identity Protocol (HIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document specifies sockets API extensions for the multihoming shim layer. The API aims to enable interactions between applications and the multihoming shim layer for advanced locator management, and access to information about failure detection and path exploration.

This document is based on an assumption that a multihomed host is equipped with a conceptual sub-layer (hereafter called "shim sub- layer") inside the IP layer that maintains mappings between identifiers and locators. Examples of the shim are Shim6 and the Host Identity Protocol (HIP). This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamegM. Komu¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiK. Slavov¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Sugimotoddatej‘ﬂhfilenamegrfc6316eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeshim6dfullx/shim6 - Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdnamex'Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128171hkeywordsÉeShim6cHIPxidentifier/locator splitkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateN)üŸgranking¨crfcd6316irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Sockets Application Program Interface (API) for Multihoming ShimdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÒThis document registers an Enumservice for the Inter-Asterisk eXchange (IAX) protocol according to the guidelines given in RFC 6117.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkCleverSpokednamefE. Guy¢kaffiliationfnic.atdnamekK. Darilionddatej‘‚hfilenamegrfc6315eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128170hkeywordsÑdENUMeE.164dVoIPmVoice over IPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN°Wgranking´crfcd6315irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IANA Registration for Enumservice 'iax'dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁTraversal of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the sessions it establishes through Network Address Translators (NATs) is a complex problem.  Currently, there are many deployment scenarios and traversal mechanisms for media traffic.  This document provides concrete recommendations and a unified method for NAT traversal as well as documents corresponding flows.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNS-TechnologiesdnamejC. Boulton¢kaffiliationeSkypednamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationeSkypednamehF. AudetddatejÕßhfilenamegrfc6314eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128169hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateN)ü»granking™crfcd6314irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-NAT Traversal Practices for Client-Server SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis document specifies an extension to the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol specification in RFC 5101 and the IPFIX information model specified in RFC 5102 to support hierarchical structured data and lists (sequences) of Information Elements in data records.  This extension allows definition of complex data structures such as variable-length lists and specification of hierarchical containment relationships between Templates.  Finally, the semantics are provided in order to express the relationship among multiple list elements in a structured data record. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemG. Dhandapani¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehS. Yatesddatej‘·hfilenamegrfc6313eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128168hkeywordsÅwipfix information modelkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateN'] granking©crfcd6313irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Export of Structured Data in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!Mobile Internet access from smartphones and other mobile devices is accelerating the exhaustion of IPv4 addresses.  IPv6 is widely seen as crucial for the continued operation and growth of the Internet, and in particular, it is critical in mobile networks.  This document discusses the issues that arise when deploying IPv6 in mobile networks.  Hence, this document can be a useful reference for service providers and network designers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. KoodliddatejÕ´hfilenamegrfc6312eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128167hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd6342epagesopublicationDateNppgranking®crfcd6312irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Mobile Networks Considerations for IPv6 DeploymentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®The IPsec protocol suite is widely used for business-critical network traffic. In order to make IPsec deployments highly available, more scalable, and failure-resistant, they are often implemented as IPsec High Availability (HA) clusters. However, there are many issues in IPsec HA clustering, and in particular in Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) clustering. An earlier document, "IPsec Cluster Problem Statement", enumerates the issues encountered in the IKEv2/IPsec HA cluster environment. This document resolves these issues with the least possible change to the protocol.

This document defines an extension to the IKEv2 protocol to solve the main issues of "IPsec Cluster Problem Statement" in the commonly deployed hot standby cluster, and provides implementation advice for other issues. The main issues solved are the synchronization of IKEv2 Message ID counters, and of IPsec replay counters. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Singh¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. Kalyani¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationhPorticordnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehD. Zhangddatej‘·hfilenamegrfc6311eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128166hkeywordsÑwIPsec high availabilitylload sharingjclusteringifail-overkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN?igrankingßcrfcd6311irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Protocol Support for High Availability of IKEv2/IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document specifies the mapping and notification of defect states between a pseudowire (PW) and the Attachment Circuits (ACs) of the end-to-end emulated service.  It standardizes the behavior of Provider Edges (PEs) with respect to PW and AC defects.  It addresses ATM, Frame Relay, Time Division Multiplexing (TDM), and Synchronous Optical Network / Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) PW services, carried over MPLS, MPLS/IP, and Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol version 3/IP (L2TPv3/IP) Packet Switched Networks (PSNs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekM. Aissaoui¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemP. Busschbach¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Morrow¢kaffiliationoCA TechnologiesdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnamekY(J). Steinddatej‘‡hfilenamegrfc6310eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128165hkeywordsÖlinterworkingldefect stateqdefect indicationjpseudowirecOAMkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateN31Ûgranking¶crfcd6310irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQPseudowire (PW) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Message MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxªThis document clarifies and relaxes the IANA rules for Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY).  This document updates RFCs 3830, 4563, 5410, and 6043; it obsoletes RFC 4909. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Mattssonddatej‘Áhfilenamegrfc6309eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128164hkeywordsÜvshort-term key messageulong-term key messagecomacbacsbrowser and contentibroadcastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN^kÑgranking•crfcd6309irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1IANA Rules for MIKEY (Multimedia Internet KEYing)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThe lack of up-to-date documentation on IP multicast address allocation and assignment procedures has caused a great deal of confusion.  To clarify the situation, this memo describes the allocation and assignment techniques and mechanisms currently (as of this writing) in use.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savoladdatej‘ﬁhfilenamegrfc6308eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-128163hkeywordsÖjassignmentjallocationcSSMcASMdGLOPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNHÍgranking§crfcd6308irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Overview of the Internet Multicast Addressing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâA Fibre Channel pseudowire (PW) is used to carry Fibre Channel traffic over an MPLS network.  This enables service providers to take advantage of MPLS to offer "emulated" Fibre Channel services.  This document specifies the encapsulation of Fibre Channel traffic within a pseudowire.  It also specifies the common procedures for using a PW to provide a Fibre Channel service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiL. Dunbar¢kaffiliationhInfineradnamegM. Roth¢kaffiliationpOrckit-CorrigentdnamejR. SolomonddatejÕ¶hfilenamegrfc6307eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128162hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateOí`granking£crfcd6307irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOEncapsulation Methods for Transport of Fibre Channel Traffic over MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes a framework for how the current IPv4 address space can be divided into two new address categories: a core address space (Area Locators, ALOCs) that is globally unique, and an edge address space (Endpoint Locators, ELOCs) that is regionally unique. In the future, the ELOC space will only be significant in a private network or in a service provider domain. Therefore, a 32x32 bit addressing scheme and a hierarchical routing architecture are achieved. The hierarchical IPv4 framework is backwards compatible with the current IPv4 Internet.

This document also discusses a method for decoupling the location and identifier functions -- future applications can make use of the separation. The framework requires extensions to the existing Domain Name System (DNS), the existing IPv4 stack of the endpoints, middleboxes, and routers in the Internet. The framework can be implemented incrementally for endpoints, DNS, middleboxes, and routers. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekP. Frejborgddatej‘ﬂhfilenamegrfc6306eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128161hkeywordsÜrcore address spacemarea locatorsealocsredge address spaceqendpoint locatorseelocskobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateN°Hgranking¢crfcd6306irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexHierarchical IPv4 FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMany sites connected to the Internet make use of IPv4 addresses that are not globally unique. Examples are the addresses designated in RFC 1918 for private use within individual sites.

Hosts should never normally send DNS reverse-mapping queries for those addresses on the public Internet. However, such queries are frequently observed. Authoritative servers are deployed to provide authoritative answers to such queries as part of a loosely coordinated effort known as the AS112 project.

Since queries sent to AS112 servers are usually not intentional, the replies received back from those servers are typically unexpected. Unexpected inbound traffic can trigger alarms on intrusion detection systems and firewalls, and operators of such systems often mistakenly believe that they are being attacked.

This document provides background information and technical advice to those firewall operators. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationhNRC-CNRCdnamehW. MatonddatejÕ©hfilenamegrfc6305eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128160hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN?\granking°crfcd6305irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(I'm Being Attacked by PRISONER.IANA.ORG!dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLMany sites connected to the Internet make use of IPv4 addresses that are not globally unique. Examples are the addresses designated in RFC 1918 for private use within individual sites.

Devices in such environments may occasionally originate Domain Name System (DNS) queries (so-called "reverse lookups") corresponding to those private-use addresses. Since the addresses concerned have only local significance, it is good practice for site administrators to ensure that such queries are answered locally. However, it is not uncommon for such queries to follow the normal delegation path in the public DNS instead of being answered within the site.

It is not possible for public DNS servers to give useful answers to such queries. In addition, due to the wide deployment of private-use addresses and the continuing growth of the Internet, the volume of such queries is large and growing. The AS112 project aims to provide a distributed sink for such queries in order to reduce the load on the IN-ADDR.ARPA authoritative servers. The AS112 project is named after the Autonomous System Number (ASN) that was assigned to it.

This document describes the steps required to install a new AS112 node and offers advice relating to such a node's operation. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationhNRC-CNRCdnamehW. Matonddatej‘‚hfilenamegrfc6304eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128159hkeywordsÇcDNSgRFC1918kobsoletedByÅd7534epagesopublicationDateN?Lgranking†crfcd6304irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAS112 Nameserver OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÿExperience with the Domain Name System (DNS) has shown that there are a number of DNS zones that all iterative resolvers and recursive nameservers should automatically serve, unless configured otherwise.  RFC 4193 specifies that this should occur for D.F.IP6.ARPA.  This document extends the practice to cover the IN-ADDR.ARPA zones for RFC 1918 address space and other well-known zones with similar characteristics.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejM. Andrewsddatej‘‰hfilenamegrfc6303eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128158hkeywordsÖeAS112gReverselIN-ADDR.ARPAhIP6.ARPAgRFC1918kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateN?;grankingücrfcd6303irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber£etotaletitlexLocally Served DNS ZonesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyIn the wake of IPv4 exhaustion and deployment of IP address sharing techniques, this document recommends that Internet-facing servers log port number and accurate timestamps in addition to the incoming IP address.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Durand¢kaffiliationkYahoo! Inc.dnamelI. Gashinsky¢kaffiliationnFacebook, Inc.dnamefD. Lee¢kaffiliationhATT LabsdnamekS. Sheppardddatej‘ﬂhfilenamegrfc6302eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128157hkeywordsÅlport loggingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN“Øgrankingûcrfcd6302irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber¢etotaletitlex3Logging Recommendations for Internet-Facing ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8Over the last two decades, many efforts have been devoted to developing solutions for mobility support over the global Internet, resulting in a variety of proposed solutions.  We conducted a systematic survey of the previous efforts to gain an overall understanding on the solution space of mobility support.  This document reports our findings and identifies remaining issues in providing ubiquitous and efficient Internet mobility support on a global scale.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamefZ. Zhu¢kaffiliationjToyota ITCdnamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehL. ZhangddatejÕ†hfilenamegrfc6301eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128156hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateN°:grankingùcrfcd6301irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,A Survey of Mobility Support in the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThis document defines the standard algorithm that Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) senders are required to use to compute and manage their retransmission timer.  It expands on the discussion in Section 4.2.3.1 of RFC 1122 and upgrades the requirement of supporting the algorithm from a SHOULD to a MUST.  This document obsoletes RFC 2988. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpICSI/UC BerkeleydnameiV. Paxson¢kaffiliationdICSIdnameiM. Allman¢kaffiliationfGooglednamefJ. Chu¢kaffiliationdCWRUdnamejM. Sargentddatej‘„hfilenamegrfc6298eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128155hkeywordsÅcRTOkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNµogrankingöcrfcd6298irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Computing TCP's Retransmission TimerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹This document provides a survey of transport protocols that are designed to have a smaller bandwidth and/or delay impact on standard TCP than standard TCP itself when they share a bottleneck with it.  Such protocols could be used for delay-insensitive "background" traffic, as they provide what is sometimes called a "less than" (or "lower than") best-effort service.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekWesley Eddydarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationuIT / Telecom BretagnednamefD. Rosddatej‘·hfilenamegrfc6297eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfledbatdfullx-ledbat - Low Extra Delay Background Transportdnamex$Low Extra Delay Background Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128154hkeywordsÉuLess-than-Best-EffortrCongestion ControlfLEDBATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN
Wßgrankingôcrfcd6297irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6A Survey of Lower-than-Best-Effort Transport ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document describes a stateless, transport-agnostic IPv6-to-IPv6 Network Prefix Translation (NPTv6) function that provides the address-independence benefit associated with IPv4-to-IPv4 NAT (NAPT44) and provides a 1:1 relationship between addresses in the "inside" and "outside" prefixes, preserving end-to-end reachability at the network layer.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamelM. Wasserman¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. BakerddatejÕ¢hfilenamegrfc6296eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128153hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateN
Wògrankingòcrfcd6296irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IPv6-to-IPv6 Network Prefix TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5This memo describes a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for the MIDI (Musical Instrument Digital Interface) command language.  The format encodes all commands that may legally appear on a MIDI 1.0 DIN cable.  The format is suitable for interactive applications (such as network musical performance) and content-delivery applications (such as file streaming).  The format may be used over unicast and multicast UDP and TCP, and it defines tools for graceful recovery from packet loss.  Stream behavior, including the MIDI rendering method, may be customized during session setup.  The format also serves as a mode for the mpeg4-generic format, to support the MPEG 4 Audio Object Types for General MIDI, Downloadable Sounds Level 2, and Structured Audio.  This document obsoletes RFC 4695. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamejJ. Lazzaro¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamelJ. Wawrzynekddatej‘ÿhfilenamegrfc6295eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128152hkeywordsòcascqcontent streamingeDLS 2lGeneral MIDIdMIDIiMIDI filesMIDI file streamingrMIDI light controlnMIDI renderingmMIDI ringtonexMIDI streaming MIDI sequencernMIDI time codemMIDI timecodexMIDI Manufacturers AssociationwMMA mpeg4generic MPEG 4wMPEG 4 Structured AudiowMPEG 4 Synthetic CodingcMTCmmusical notesxnetwork musical performanceprecovery journallShow ControllsonificationhringtonegrtpmidicRTPhRTP MIDIoSMPTE time codenSMPTE timecodesStandard MIDI FilescXMFkobsoletedByÄepages´opublicationDateMÌ¶dgrankingócrfcd6295irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Payload Format for MIDIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty3The IPv6 protocol includes a flow label in every packet header, but this field is not used in practice.  This paper describes the flow label standard and discusses the implementation issues that it raises.  It then describes various published proposals for using the flow label and shows that most of them are inconsistent with the standard.  Methods to address this problem are briefly reviewed.  We also question whether the standard should be revised.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnameeQ. Hu¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenterddatej‘ûhfilenamegrfc6294eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128151hkeywordsÇrQuality of servicecQoSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN
Wégrankingñcrfcd6294irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex4Survey of Proposed Use Cases for the IPv6 Flow LabeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^The document gives a set of requirements for extending the IETF Datatracker to give individual IETF community members, including the IETF leadership, easy methods for tracking the progress of the Internet-Drafts and RFCs of interest to them.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. HoffmanddatejÕ†hfilenamegrfc6293eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggenareadfullx#genarea - General Area Open MeetingdnamexGeneral Area Open Meetingdtypebagbidjdoc-128150hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNµ`grankingïcrfcd6293irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRequirements for Internet-Draft Tracking by the IETF Community in the DatatrackerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰The IETF intends to provide a new tool to Area Directors for the creation, re-chartering, and closing of Working Groups.  The tool will also allow the IETF community to view the status of the chartering process.  This document describes the requirements for the proposed new tool, and it is intended as input to a later activity for the design and development of such a tool.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. HoffmanddatejÕ£hfilenamegrfc6292eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymggenareadfullx#genarea - General Area Open MeetingdnamexGeneral Area Open Meetingdtypebagbidjdoc-128149hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNµSgrankingîcrfcd6292irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Requirements for a Working Group Charter TooldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6433∑habstracty!At first glance, the acronym "OAM" seems to be well-known and well-understood. Looking at the acronym a bit more closely reveals a set of recurring problems that are revisited time and again.

This document provides a definition of the acronym "OAM" (Operations, Administration, and Maintenance) for use in all future IETF documents that refer to OAM. There are other definitions and acronyms that will be discussed while exploring the definition of the constituent parts of the "OAM" term. This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameoH. van Helvoort¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelS. MansfieldddatejÕ©hfilenamegrfc6291eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128148hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateM˚Üygrankingìcrfcd6291irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber°etotaletitlex7Guidelines for the Use of the "OAM" Acronym in the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an extension to the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) that allows for faster detection of Security Association (SA) desynchronization using a saved token.

When an IPsec tunnel between two IKEv2 peers is disconnected due to a restart of one peer, it can take as much as several minutes for the other peer to discover that the reboot has occurred, thus delaying recovery. In this text, we propose an extension to the protocol that allows for recovery immediately following the restart. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamemD. Wierbowski¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekF. Detienne¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Sethiddatej‘ﬁhfilenamegrfc6290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128147hkeywordsÅcQCDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNH’grankingícrfcd6290irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKA Quick Crash Detection Method for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) 'cablelabs' for Uniform Resource Names (URNs) used to identify resources published by Cable Television Laboratories, Inc. (CableLabs).  CableLabs specifies and manages resources that utilize this URN identification model.  Management activities for these and other resource types are handled by the manager of the CableLabs' Assigned Names and Numbers registry.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamejE. Cardona¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamepS. Channabasappa¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiJ-F. Muleddatej‘ﬂhfilenamegrfc6289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128146hkeywordsÇtnamespace identifiercnidkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN“úgrankingëcrfcd6289irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for CableLabsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) for Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace resources published by the Defence Geospatial Information Working Group (DGIWG). The DGIWG defines and manages resources that utilize this URN name model.

Management activities for these and other resource types are provided by the DGIWG Registry System (DRS). This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcOGCdnamegC. Reedddatej‘Ëhfilenamegrfc6288eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128145hkeywordsÑtNamespace IdentifiercniduDGIWG Registry SystemcdrskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNKQ∏grankingêcrfcd6288irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJURN Namespace for the Defence Geospatial Information Working Group (DGIWG)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúThis document describes an algorithm for challenge-response authentication developed by the Initiative for Open Authentication (OATH).  The specified mechanisms leverage the HMAC-based One-Time Password (HOTP) algorithm and offer one-way and mutual authentication, and electronic signature capabilities.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamejD. M'Raihi¢kaffiliationnPortwise, Inc.dnameiJ. Rydell¢kaffiliationnSymantec Corp.dnamehS. Bajaj¢kaffiliationpDiversinet Corp.dnamejS. Machani¢kaffiliationxEcole Normale SuperieurednamekD. Naccacheddatej‘€hfilenamegrfc6287eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128144hkeywordsÖdHOTPdTOTPqOne-Time PasswordnAuthenticationiSignaturekobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateN
WÖgrankingècrfcd6287irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'OCRA: OATH Challenge-Response AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂTo accommodate situations where the current requirements for the BGP Identifier are not met, this document relaxes the definition of the BGP Identifier to be a 4-octet, unsigned, non-zero integer and relaxes the "uniqueness" requirement so that only Autonomous-System-wide (AS-wide) uniqueness of the BGP Identifiers is required.  These revisions to the base BGP specification do not introduce any backward compatibility issues.  This document updates RFC 4271. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. YuanddatejÕ≠hfilenamegrfc6286eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128143hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM˚ÜBgrankingécrfcd6286irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Autonomous-System-Wide Unique BGP Identifier for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWhen an RTP receiver joins a multicast session, it may need to acquire and parse certain Reference Information before it can process any data sent in the multicast session. Depending on the join time, length of the Reference Information repetition (or appearance) interval, size of the Reference Information, and the application and transport properties, the time lag before an RTP receiver can usefully consume the multicast data, which we refer to as the Acquisition Delay, varies and can be large. This is an undesirable phenomenon for receivers that frequently switch among different multicast sessions, such as video broadcasts.

In this document, we describe a method using the existing RTP and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) machinery that reduces the acquisition delay. In this method, an auxiliary unicast RTP session carrying the Reference Information to the receiver precedes or accompanies the multicast stream. This unicast RTP flow can be transmitted at a faster than natural bitrate to further accelerate the acquisition. The motivating use case for this capability is multicast applications that carry real-time compressed audio and video. However, this method can also be used in other types of multicast applications where the acquisition delay is long enough to be a problem. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelB. Ver Steeg¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameoT. Van Caenegem¢kaffiliationtMagnum SemiconductordnamefZ. Vaxddatej‘ƒhfilenamegrfc6285eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128142hkeywordsÑcSSMimulticastdIPTVsfast channel changekobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateN
Wwgrankingçcrfcd6285irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Unicast-Based Rapid Acquisition of Multicast RTP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document presents a port mapping solution that allows RTP receivers to choose their own ports for an auxiliary unicast session in RTP applications using both unicast and multicast services.  The solution provides protection against denial-of-service or packet amplification attacks that could be used to cause one or more RTP packets to be sent to a victim client. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameoT. Van Caenegemddatej‘hfilenamegrfc6284eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128141hkeywordsÖlPort mappingpport translationcRTPimulticastcNATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN
W>grankingåcrfcd6284irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Port Mapping between Unicast and Multicast RTP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)In many scenarios, users must be able to demonstrate the (time of) existence, integrity, and validity of data including signed data for long or undetermined periods of time.  This document specifies XML syntax and processing rules for creating evidence for long-term non- repudiation of existence and integrity of data.  The Extensible Markup Language Evidence Record Syntax XMLERS provides alternative syntax and processing rules to the ASN.1 (Abstract Syntax Notation One) ERS (Evidence Record Syntax) (RFC 4998) syntax by using XML. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfSETCCEdnamepA. Jerman Blazic¢kaffiliationfSETCCEdnameiS. Saljic¢kaffiliation`dnamejT. Gondromddatej‘€hfilenamegrfc6283eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeltansdfullx-ltans - Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdnamex%Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128140hkeywordsòilong termetrustiintegrityslong term integrityqdata preservationudocument preservationjtime-stampmtime-stampingrarchive time stamprelectronic archivetelectronic archivingqtrusted archivingqlong-term archivelarchive datahevidenceoevidence recordvevidence record syntaxihash treecERScXMLdhashisignaturegrenewalialgorithmlcryptographykobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateN&£grankingãcrfcd6283irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Extensible Markup Language Evidence Record Syntax (XMLERS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThis document updates RFC 4944, "Transmission of IPv6 Packets over IEEE 802.15.4 Networks".  This document specifies an IPv6 header compression format for IPv6 packet delivery in Low Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs).  The compression format relies on shared context to allow compression of arbitrary prefixes.  How the information is maintained in that shared context is out of scope.  This document specifies compression of multicast addresses and a framework for compressing next headers.  UDP header compression is specified within this framework. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuArch Rock CorporationdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubertddatej‘›hfilenamegrfc6282eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymg6lowpandfullx"6lowpan - IPv6 over Low power WPANdnamexIPv6 over Low power WPANdtypebwgbidjdoc-128139hkeywordsÜcLLNiLow Powernradio 802.15.4ipowerlinejISA100.11ahRFC 4944kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNgÂ*grankingäcrfcd6282irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGCompression Format for IPv6 Datagrams over IEEE 802.15.4-Based NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8066∂habstractyXThis document describes the implementation of Apple Inc.'s Back to My Mac (BTMM) service.  BTMM provides network connectivity between devices so that a user can perform file sharing and screen sharing among multiple computers at home, at work, or on the road.  The implementation of BTMM addresses the issues of single sign-on authentication, secure data communication, service discovery, and end-to-end connectivity in the face of Network Address Translators (NATs) and mobility of devices.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamefZ. Zhu¢kaffiliationjToyota ITCdnamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehL. ZhangddatejÕôhfilenamegrfc6281eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128138hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN
W5grankingâcrfcd6281irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Understanding Apple's Back to My Mac (BTMM) ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty†Location-based services (such as navigation applications, emergency services, and management of equipment in the field) need geographic location information about Internet hosts, their users, and other related entities.  These applications need to securely gather and transfer location information for location services, and at the same time protect the privacy of the individuals involved.  This document describes an architecture for privacy-preserving location-based services in the Internet, focusing on authorization, security, and privacy requirements for the data formats and protocols used by these services.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnameiR. Barnes¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamekM. Lepinski¢kaffiliationx!Center for Democracy & TechnologydnameiA. Cooper¢kaffiliationx!Center for Democracy & TechnologydnameiJ. Morris¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej‘’hfilenamegrfc6280eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128137hkeywordsÇkgeolocationggeoprivkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateN+5¡grankingàcrfcd6280irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber†etotaletitlexJAn Architecture for Location and Location Privacy in Internet ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅProxy Mobile IPv6 is the IETF Standard for network-based mobility management.  In Proxy Mobile IPv6, mobile nodes are topologically anchored at a Local Mobility Anchor, which forwards all data for registered mobile nodes.  The setup and maintenance of localized routing, which allows forwarding of data packets between two mobile nodes' Mobility Access Gateways without involvement of their Local Mobility Anchor in forwarding, is not considered.  This document describes the problem space of localized routing in Proxy Mobile IPv6.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejM. Liebsch¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamehS. Jeong¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wuddatej‘⁄hfilenamegrfc6279eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetextdfullx*netext - Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdnamex!Network-Based Mobility Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128136hkeywordsÉmLocal RoutingrRoute OptimizationoTraffic OffloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateNH¬grankingácrfcd6279irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) Localized Routing Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document describes how to use the 'static-static Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman key-agreement scheme (i.e., Elliptic Curve Diffie- Hellman where both participants use static Diffie-Hellman values) with the Cryptographic Message Syntax.  In this form of key agreement, the Diffie-Hellman values of both the sender and receiver are long-term values contained in certificates.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnameiJ. Herzog¢kaffiliationvMIT Lincoln LaboratorydnameiR. Khazanddatej‘Ÿhfilenamegrfc6278eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128135hkeywordsÇgset-keyigroup-keykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÚTgrankingÜcrfcd6278irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Use of Static-Static Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman Key Agreement in Cryptographic Message SyntaxdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThe Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) requires server responses to be signed but does not specify a mechanism for selecting the signature algorithm to be used.  This may lead to avoidable interoperability failures in contexts where multiple signature algorithms are in use.  This document specifies rules for server signature algorithm selection and an extension that allows a client to advise a server that specific signature algorithms are supported. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationl3xA SecuritydnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationuDefault Deny SecuritydnameoP. Hallam-Bakerddatej‘◊hfilenamegrfc6277eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-128134hkeywordsÅdocspkobsoletedByÅd6960epagesopublicationDateMÚSÒgrankingÖcrfcd6277irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Online Certificate Status Protocol Algorithm AgilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOne aspect of network mobility support is the assignment of a prefix or prefixes to a mobile router for use on the links in the mobile network.  This document specifies how DHCPv6 prefix delegation can be used for this configuration task.  The mobile router plays the role of requesting router, while the home agent assumes the role of delegating router.  When the mobile router is outside its home network, the mobile router also assumes the role of DHCPv6 relay agent, co-located with the requesting router function. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiF. Dupont¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiW. Haddad¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamelC. Bernardosddatej‘«hfilenamegrfc6276eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-128133hkeywordsÑdIPv6mmobile routerjhome agentumobile network prefixkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN?"grankingÑcrfcd6276irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4DHCPv6 Prefix Delegation for Network Mobility (NEMO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document specifies Mobile IPv6, a protocol that allows nodes to remain reachable while moving around in the IPv6 Internet.  Each mobile node is always identified by its home address, regardless of its current point of attachment to the Internet.  While situated away from its home, a mobile node is also associated with a care-of address, which provides information about the mobile node's current location.  IPv6 packets addressed to a mobile node's home address are transparently routed to its care-of address.  The protocol enables IPv6 nodes to cache the binding of a mobile node's home address with its care-of address, and to then send any packets destined for the mobile node directly to it at this care-of address.  To support this operation, Mobile IPv6 defines a new IPv6 protocol and a new destination option.  All IPv6 nodes, whether mobile or stationary, can communicate with mobile nodes.  This document obsoletes RFC 3775. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlTellabs Inc.dnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationoRice UniversitydnamejD. Johnson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej‘€hfilenamegrfc6275eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-128132hkeywordsÖeMIPv6hmobilitydIPv6qinternet protocolenodeskobsoletedByÄepages©opublicationDateN°.grankingÉcrfcd6275irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMobility Support in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document contains a security assessment of the IETF specifications of the Internet Protocol version 4 and of a number of mechanisms and policies in use by popular IPv4 implementations.  It is based on the results of a project carried out by the UK's Centre for the Protection of National Infrastructure (CPNI).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUK CPNIdnamegF. Gontddatej‘Khfilenamegrfc6274eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-128131hkeywordsÖovulnerabilitiesqDenial of Servicejresiliencyihardeningsinformation leakagekobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDateN?øgrankingÇcrfcd6274irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Security Assessment of the Internet Protocol Version 4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document analyzes the use of hashes in Secure Neighbor Discovery (SEND), the possible threats to these hashes and the impact of recent attacks on hash functions used by SEND.  The SEND specification currently uses the SHA-1 hash algorithm and PKIX certificates and does not provide support for hash algorithm agility.  This document provides an analysis of possible threats to the hash algorithms used in SEND.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of ZagrebdnamehA. Kukec¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. JiangddatejÕ¢hfilenamegrfc6273eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymccsidfullxcsi - Cga & Send maIntenancednamevCga & Send maIntenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128130hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMˆ⁄ôgrankingÅcrfcd6273irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The Secure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) Hash Threat AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis note identifies the key infrastructure protocols of the Internet Protocol Suite for use in the Smart Grid.  The target audience is those people seeking guidance on how to construct an appropriate Internet Protocol Suite profile for the Smart Grid.  In practice, such a profile would consist of selecting what is needed for Smart Grid deployment from the picture presented here.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. MeyerddatejÕ†hfilenamegrfc6272eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128129hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateNH¥grankingÄcrfcd6272irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Internet Protocols for the Smart GriddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis memo captures protocol requirements to enable session peering of voice, presence, instant messaging, and other types of multimedia traffic.  This informational document is intended to link the various use cases described for session peering to protocol solutions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiJ-F. Muleddatej‘÷hfilenamegrfc6271eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymispeermintdfullx7speermint - Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdnamex+Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdtypebwgbidjdoc-128128hkeywordsÜnIETF speermintjguidelinesx&requirements for session interconnectsosession peeringqSIP interconnectslVoIP peeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN
W(grankingcrfcd6271irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Requirements for SIP-Based Session PeeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôThis document is the specification of the 'tn3270' Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme, which is used to designate the access to the resources available via Telnet 3270 mode (TN3270) and Telnet 3270 Enhanced mode (TN3270E).  It updates RFC 1041 and RFC 2355, which specify these protocols, and RFC 1738, which firstly mentioned this URI scheme without defining its syntax and semantics. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM. Yevstifeyevddatej‘ﬁhfilenamegrfc6270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128127hkeywordsÑcURIfTelnetkTelnet 3270fTN3270kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÏ˚†granking~crfcd6270irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewThe 'tn3270' URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe completion of IPv4 address allocations from IANA and the Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) is causing service providers around the world to question how they will continue providing IPv4 connectivity service to their subscribers when there are no longer sufficient IPv4 addresses to allocate them one per subscriber. Several possible solutions to this problem are now emerging based around the idea of shared IPv4 addressing. These solutions give rise to a number of issues, and this memo identifies those common to all such address sharing approaches. Such issues include application failures, additional service monitoring complexity, new security vulnerabilities, and so on. Solution-specific discussions are out of scope.

Deploying IPv6 is the only perennial way to ease pressure on the public IPv4 address pool without the need for address sharing mechanisms that give rise to the issues identified herein. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamegM. Ford¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiA. Durand¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamehP. Levis¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamejP. Robertsddatej‘›hfilenamegrfc6269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgintareadfullx%intarea - Internet Area Working GroupdnamexInternet Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-128126hkeywordsÑwIPv4 address exhaustionjcompletionfsharednsharing issueskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN
Wgranking}crfcd6269irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIssues with IP Address SharingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) format, and many associated formats, are expressed using ASN.1.  The current ASN.1 modules conform to the 1988 version of ASN.1.  This document updates some auxiliary ASN.1 modules to conform to the 2008 version of ASN.1; the 1988 ASN.1 modules remain the normative version.  There are no bits- on-the-wire changes to any of the formats; this is simply a change to the syntax.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoStephen Farrelldarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationjIECA, Inc.dnameiS. Turnerddatej‘‹hfilenamegrfc6268eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128125hkeywordsÉeASN.1uCertficate ExtensionsdHMACkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateN31‡granking|crfcd6268irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|Additional New ASN.1 Modules for the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) and the Public Key Infrastructure Using X.509 (PKIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThis document describes a key management protocol variant for the Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) protocol that relies on a trusted key management service.  In particular, this variant utilizes Identity-Based Authenticated Key Exchange (IBAKE) framework that allows the participating clients to perform mutual authentication and derive a session key in an asymmetric Identity-Based Encryption (IBE) framework.  This protocol, in addition to providing mutual authentication, eliminates the key escrow problem that is common in standard IBE and provides perfect forward and backward secrecy.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamejV. Cakulev¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamekG. Sundaramddatej‘‹hfilenamegrfc6267eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128124hkeywordsÉxIdentity based encryptionnauthenticationmkey agreementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN
Wgranking{crfcd6267irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}MIKEY-IBAKE: Identity-Based Authenticated Key Exchange (IBAKE) Mode of Key Distribution in Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"RFC 2616 defines the Content-Disposition response header field, but points out that it is not part of the HTTP/1.1 Standard.  This specification takes over the definition and registration of Content-Disposition, as used in HTTP, and clarifies internationalization aspects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej‘€hfilenamegrfc6266eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymghttpbisdfullnhttpbis - HTTPdnamedHTTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128123hkeywordsÉhfilenamejattachmentfinlinekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÏ˚ígrankingzcrfcd6266irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUUse of the Content-Disposition Header Field in the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›This document defines the HTTP Cookie and Set-Cookie header fields.  These header fields can be used by HTTP servers to store state (called cookies) at HTTP user agents, letting the servers maintain a stateful session over the mostly stateless HTTP protocol.  Although cookies have many historical infelicities that degrade their security and privacy, the Cookie and Set-Cookie header fields are widely used on the Internet.  This document obsoletes RFC 2965. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmU.C. BerkeleydnamehA. Barthddatej‘“hfilenamegrfc6265eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymihttpstatedfullx+httpstate - HTTP State Management MechanismdnamexHTTP State Management Mechanismdtypebwgbidjdoc-128122hkeywordsÑfCookiejSet-CookiefSecurehHttpOnlykobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateM∏®grankingycrfcd6265irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHTTP State Management MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+Global IPv6 deployment was slower than originally expected. As IPv4 address exhaustion approaches, IPv4 to IPv6 transition issues become more critical and less tractable. Host-based transition mechanisms used in dual-stack environments cannot meet all transition requirements. Most end users are not sufficiently expert to configure or maintain host-based transition mechanisms. Carrier-Grade NAT (CGN) devices with integrated transition mechanisms can reduce the operational changes required during the IPv4 to IPv6 migration or coexistence period.

This document proposes an incremental CGN approach for IPv6 transition. It can provide IPv6 access services for IPv6 hosts and IPv4 access services for IPv4 hosts while leaving much of a legacy ISP network unchanged during the initial stage of IPv4 to IPv6 migration. Unlike CGN alone, incremental CGN also supports and encourages smooth transition towards dual-stack or IPv6-only ISP networks. An integrated configurable CGN device and an adaptive home gateway (HG) device are described. Both are reusable during different transition phases, avoiding multiple upgrades. This enables IPv6 migration to be incrementally achieved according to real user requirements. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamehS. Jiang¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefD. Guo¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamelB. CarpenterddatejÕöhfilenamegrfc6264eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128121hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMˆ⁄ågrankingxcrfcd6264irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:An Incremental Carrier-Grade NAT (CGN) for IPv6 TransitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document lists the different mechanisms that enable applications using the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) and the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) to keep their RTP Network Address Translator (NAT) mappings alive.  It also makes a recommendation for a preferred mechanism.  This document is not applicable to Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) agents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednameiX. Marjou¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednamejA. Sollaudddatej‘’hfilenamegrfc6263eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgavtcoredfullx0avtcore - Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancednamex&Audio/Video Transport Core Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128120hkeywordsÉcAVTcSDPdportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMˆ⁄Ägrankingwcrfcd6263irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpApplication Mechanism for Keeping Alive the NAT Mappings Associated with RTP / RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) FlowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document specifies the payload format for packetization of SPIRIT IP-MR encoded speech signals into the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP).  The payload format supports transmission of multiple frames per packet and introduces redundancy for robustness against packet loss and bit errors. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjSPIRIT DSPdnameiS. Ikoninddatej‘€hfilenamegrfc6262eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128119hkeywordsÖdipmrgvocoderimultiratehscalablekscalabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN@5grankingvcrfcd6262irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)RTP Payload Format for IP-MR Speech CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies two transport modes for Host Identity Protocol (HIP) signaling messages that allow them to be conveyed over encrypted connections initiated with the Host Identity Protocol.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. KeranenddatejÕöhfilenamegrfc6261eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128118hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMﬁÅzgrankingucrfcd6261irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBEncrypted Signaling Transport Modes for the Host Identity ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractytThis document describes a convention by which Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN) Bundle Protocol (BP) "convergence-layer" adapters may represent endpoint identifiers in a compressed form within the primary blocks of bundles, provided those endpoint identifiers conform to the structure prescribed by this convention.

Compressed Bundle Header Encoding (CBHE) compression is a convergence-layer adaptation. It is opaque to bundle processing. Therefore, it has no impact on the interoperability of different Bundle Protocol implementations, but instead affects only the interoperability of different convergence-layer adaptation implementations.

This document is a product of the Delay-Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group. No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejRon BonicagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx>Jet Propulsion Laboratory / California Institute of TechnologydnamekS. Burleighddatej‘÷hfilenamegrfc6260eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128117hkeywordsÖcDTNxdelay-tolerant networkingbBPobundle protocolcIPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM›·Sgrankingtcrfcd6260irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex(Compressed Bundle Header Encoding (CBHE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9758µhabstracty}This document defines an extension block for use with the Delay- Tolerant Networking (DTN) Bundle Protocol.  This Previous-Hop Insertion Block (PHIB) extension block is designed to be inserted by a forwarding node to provide the endpoint identifier (EID) of an endpoint of which the forwarding node is a member so that this EID may be conveyed to the next-hop receiving node.  Knowledge of an EID of an endpoint of which a previous-hop node is a member may be required in some circumstances to support certain routing protocols (e.g., flood routing).  If this EID cannot be provided by the convergence layer or other means, the PHIB defines the mechanism whereby the EID can be provided with the bundle.  Each PHIB is always removed from the bundle by the receiving node so that its presence within the bundle is limited to exactly one hop.  This document defines the format and processing of this PHIB.  This document is a product of the Delay-Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group.  No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamehTim PolkgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamelS. Symingtonddatej‘÷hfilenamegrfc6259eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128116hkeywordsÉcDTNxDelay-Tolerant NetworkingxDistruption-Tolerant NetworkingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateM›·Hgrankingscrfcd6259irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex6Delay-Tolerant Networking Previous-Hop Insertion BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyàThis document defines an extension block that may be used with the Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN) Bundle Protocol.  This Metadata Extension Block is designed to carry additional information that DTN nodes can use to make processing decisions regarding bundles, such as deciding whether to store a bundle or determining to which nodes to forward a bundle.  The metadata that is carried in a metadata block must be formatted according to the metadata type that is identified in the block's metadata type field.  One specific metadata type, for carrying URIs as metadata, is defined in this document.  Other metadata types may be defined in separate documents.  This document is a product of the Delay Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group.  No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqPeter Saint-AndregauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamelS. Symingtonddatej‘÷hfilenamegrfc6258eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128115hkeywordsÉcDTNxDelay-Tolerant NetworkingxDistruption-Tolerant NetworkingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateM›·<grankingrcrfcd6258irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex2Delay-Tolerant Networking Metadata Extension BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyHThis document defines the bundle security protocol, which provides data integrity and confidentiality services for the Bundle Protocol. Separate capabilities are provided to protect the bundle payload and additional data that may be included within the bundle. We also describe various security considerations including some policy options.

This document is a product of the Delay-Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group. No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamelS. Symington¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnamehH. Weiss¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiP. LovellddatejÕíhfilenamegrfc6257eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128114hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateM›·2grankingqcrfcd6257irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex&Bundle Security Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractySelf-Delimiting Numeric Values (SDNVs) have recently been introduced as a field type in proposed Delay-Tolerant Networking protocols.  SDNVs encode an arbitrary-length non-negative integer or arbitrary- length bitstring with minimum overhead.  They are intended to provide protocol flexibility without sacrificing economy and to assist in future-proofing protocols under development.  This document describes formats and algorithms for SDNV encoding and decoding, along with notes on implementation and usage.  This document is a product of the Delay-Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group.  No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkMTI SystemsdnamegW. Eddy¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Daviesddatej‘◊hfilenamegrfc6256eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128113hkeywordsÇdSDNVcDTNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM›·$grankingpcrfcd6256irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex1Using Self-Delimiting Numeric Values in ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÑThe Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN) Research Group research group has defined many protocols such as the Bundle Protocol and Licklider Transmission Protocol.  The specifications of these protocols contain fields that are subject to a registry.  For the purpose of its research work, the group created ad hoc registries.  As the specifications are stable and have multiple interoperable implementations, the group would like to hand off the registries to IANA for official custody.  This document describes the actions executed by IANA.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej‘÷hfilenamegrfc6255eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-128112hkeywordsÑcDTNdSNDVeDTNRGpSpace networkingkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateM›·grankingocrfcd6255irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex9Delay-Tolerant Networking Bundle Protocol IANA RegistriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝RFC 2754 requested that each time IANA made an address assignment, it was to create appropriate inetnum and as-block objects and digitally sign them.  The purpose was to distribute the IANA-held public key in software implementations of the Distributed Routing Policy System.  In practice, this was never done on the public Internet.  This document requests that RFC 2754 be moved to Historic status.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamekM. McFaddenddatej‘’hfilenamegrfc6254eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128111hkeywordsÅcRPSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÕ¬‘grankingncrfcd6254irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Request to Move RFC 2754 to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThe Certificate (CERT) parameter is a container for digital certificates. It is used for carrying these certificates in Host Identity Protocol (HIP) control packets. This document specifies the CERT parameter and the error signaling in case of a failed verification. Additionally, this document specifies the representations of Host Identity Tags in X.509 version 3 (v3) and Simple Public Key Infrastructure (SPKI) certificates.

The concrete use of certificates, including how certificates are obtained, requested, and which actions are taken upon successful or failed verification, is specific to the scenario in which the certificates are used. Hence, the definition of these scenario- specific aspects is left to the documents that use the CERT parameter.

This document updates RFC 5201. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxCOMSYS / RWTH Aachen UniversitydnamegT. Heer¢kaffiliationx-Helsinki Institute for Information TechnologydnamekS. VarjonenddatejÕñhfilenamegrfc6253eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-128110hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8002epagesopublicationDateMﬁÅogrankingmcrfcd6253irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Host Identity Protocol CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty≥This document describes Media-independent Pre-Authentication (MPA), a new handover optimization mechanism that addresses the issues on existing mobility management protocols and mobility optimization mechanisms to support inter-domain handover. MPA is a mobile- assisted, secure handover optimization scheme that works over any link layer and with any mobility management protocol, and is most applicable to supporting optimization during inter-domain handover. MPA's pre-authentication, pre-configuration, and proactive handover techniques allow many of the handoff-related operations to take place before the mobile node has moved to the new network. We describe the details of all the associated techniques and their applicability for different scenarios involving various mobility protocols during inter-domain handover. We have implemented the MPA mechanism for various network-layer and application-layer mobility protocols, and we report a summary of experimental performance results in this document.

This document is a product of the IP Mobility Optimizations (MOBOPTS) Research Group. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfNIKSUNdnamehA. Dutta¢kaffiliationfNIKSUNdnamejV. Fajardo¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamekK. Taniuchi¢kaffiliationnColumbia Univ.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej‘◊hfilenamegrfc6252eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmoboptsdfullx2mobopts - IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdnamex(IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128109hkeywordséhMobilitylOptimizationqProactive handoffsLink-layer securityqHandover taxonomyoLayer 2 handoffoLayer 3 handoffqNetwork discoverynHandover delaykPacket lossxProactive binding updateoMulti-interfacevIP address acquisitionqTunnel managementkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateMˆ⁄tgrankinglcrfcd6252irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex`A Framework of Media-Independent Pre-Authentication (MPA) for Inter-Domain Handover OptimizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies how the Kerberos V5 protocol can be transported over the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol in order to provide additional security features.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefssonddatej‘‘hfilenamegrfc6251eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-128108hkeywordsÑhkerberosctlshstarttlsckdckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM’¿bgrankingkcrfcd6251irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIUsing Kerberos Version 5 over the Transport Layer Security (TLS) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¨This RFC attempts to document various aspects of the IP service model and how it has evolved over time.  In particular, it attempts to document the properties of the IP layer as they are seen by upper- layer protocols and applications, especially properties that were (and, at times, still are) incorrectly perceived to exist as well as properties that would cause problems if changed.  The discussion of these properties is organized around evaluating a set of claims, or misconceptions.  Finally, this document provides some guidance to protocol designers and implementers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIABdnameiD. Thalerddatej‘‘hfilenamegrfc6250eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128107hkeywordsÑqInternet ProtocoldIPv4dIPv6mservice modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM’¿Wgrankingjcrfcd6250irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexEvolution of the IP ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThis document specifies Metalink/HTTP: Mirrors and Cryptographic Hashes in HTTP header fields, a different way to get information that is usually contained in the Metalink XML-based download description format.  Metalink/HTTP describes multiple download locations (mirrors), Peer-to-Peer, cryptographic hashes, digital signatures, and other information using existing standards for HTTP header fields.  Metalink clients can use this information to make file transfers more robust and reliable.  Normative requirements for Metalink/HTTP clients and servers are described here. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Bryan¢kaffiliation`dnamehN. McNab¢kaffiliation`dnamelT. Tsujikawa¢kaffiliationkMirrorBraindnamehP. Poeml¢kaffiliation`dnamelH. Nordstromddatej‘◊hfilenamegrfc6249eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128106hkeywordsämfile transferhdownloaddlinkisignaturendata integrityxhypertext transfer protocolcftpvfile transfer protocolhmetadatagtorrentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM˚Ü8grankingicrfcd6249irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Metalink/HTTP: Mirrors and HashesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This memo reclassifies RFC 4148, "IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) Metrics Registry", as Obsolete, and withdraws the IANA IPPM Metrics Registry itself from use because it is obsolete.  The current registry structure has been found to be insufficiently detailed to uniquely identify IPPM metrics.  Despite apparent efforts to find current or even future users, no one responded to the call for interest in the RFC 4148 registry during the second half of 2010.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. MortonddatejÕáhfilenamegrfc6248eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-128105hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMß<]grankinghcrfcd6248irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexORFC 4148 and the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) Registry of Metrics Are ObsoletedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document reclassifies several TCP extensions that have never seen widespread use to Historic status.  The affected RFCs are RFC 1072, RFC 1106, RFC 1110, RFC 1145, RFC 1146, RFC 1379, RFC 1644, and RFC 1693.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. EggertddatejÕïhfilenamegrfc6247eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128104hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM“∑7grankinggcrfcd6247irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäMoving the Undeployed TCP Extensions RFC 1072, RFC 1106, RFC 1110, RFC 1145, RFC 1146, RFC 1379, RFC 1644, and RFC 1693 to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®One of the main motivations behind Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) is its ability to provide connectivity not only among customer routers and servers/hosts but also among customer IEEE bridges. VPLS is expected to deliver the same level of service that current enterprise users are accustomed to from their own enterprise bridged networks or their Ethernet Service Providers.

When customer edge (CE) devices are IEEE bridges, then there are certain issues and challenges that need to be accounted for in a VPLS network. The majority of these issues have been addressed in the IEEE 802.1ad standard for provider bridges and they can be leveraged for VPLS networks. This document extends the provider edge (PE) model described in RFC 4664 based on IEEE 802.1ad bridge module, and it illustrates a clear demarcation between the IEEE bridge module and IETF LAN emulation module. By doing so, it shows that the majority of interoperability issues with CE bridges can be delegated to the 802.1ad bridge module, thus removing the burden on the IETF LAN emulation module within a VPLS PE. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Brockners¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamehD. Mohan¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamejY. Serbestddatej‘–hfilenamegrfc6246eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128103hkeywordsÅlieee bridgeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÚTgrankingfcrfcd6246irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSVirtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Interoperability with Customer Edge (CE) BridgesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThe Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) specification contains a Key field, which MAY contain a value that is used to identify a particular GRE data stream.  This specification defines a new Mobile IP extension that is used to exchange the value to be used in the GRE Key field.  This extension further allows the Mobility Agents to set up the necessary protocol interfaces prior to receiving the mobile node traffic.  The new extension allows a Foreign Agent to request GRE tunneling without disturbing the Home Agent behavior specified for Mobile IPv4.  GRE tunneling with the Key field allows the operators to have home networks that consist of multiple Virtual Private Networks (VPNs), which may have overlapping home addresses.  When the tuple <Care of Address, Home Address, and Home Agent Address> is the same across multiple subscriber sessions, GRE tunneling will provide a means for the Foreign Agent and Home Agent to identify data streams for the individual sessions based on the GRE key.  In the absence of this key identifier, the data streams cannot be distinguished from each other -- a significant drawback when using IP-in-IP tunneling. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiP. Yegani¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamegA. Lior¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiJ. NavaliddatejÕóhfilenamegrfc6245eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-128102hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM‡G‘grankingecrfcd6245irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAGeneric Routing Encapsulation (GRE) Key Extension for Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThe Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) gives access to native capabilities of the devices within a network, defining methods for manipulating configuration databases, retrieving operational data, and invoking specific operations. YANG provides the means to define the content carried via NETCONF, both data and operations. Using both technologies, standard modules can be defined to give interoperability and commonality to devices, while still allowing devices to express their unique capabilities.

This document describes how NETCONF and YANG help build network management applications that meet the needs of network operators. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiP. Shaferddatej‘πhfilenamegrfc6244eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128101hkeywordsÅxnetwork configuration protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN“çgrankingdcrfcd6244irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=An Architecture for Network Management Using NETCONF and YANGdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThe Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) defines ways to read and edit configuration data from a NETCONF server.  In some cases, part of this data may not be set by the NETCONF client, but rather a default value known to the server is used instead.  In many situations the NETCONF client has a priori knowledge about default data, so the NETCONF server does not need to save it in a NETCONF configuration datastore or send it to the client in a retrieval operation reply.  In other situations the NETCONF client will need this data from the server.  Not all server implementations treat this default data the same way.  This document defines a capability-based extension to the NETCONF protocol that allows the NETCONF client to identify how defaults are processed by the server, and also defines new mechanisms for client control of server processing of default data. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejB. Lengyelddatej‘»hfilenamegrfc6243eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128100hkeywordsÅxnetwork configuration protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN“zgrankingccrfcd6243irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$With-defaults Capability for NETCONFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‘This document describes a method for invoking and running the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) within a Secure Shell (SSH) session as an SSH subsystem.  This document obsoletes RFC 4742. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvPainless Security, LLCdnamelM. Wassermanddatej‘⁄hfilenamegrfc6242eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128099hkeywordsÅxnetwork configuration protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN“kgrankingbcrfcd6242irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Using the NETCONF Protocol over Secure Shell (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•The Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) defined in this document provides mechanisms to install, manipulate, and delete the configuration of network devices.  It uses an Extensible Markup Language (XML)-based data encoding for the configuration data as well as the protocol messages.  The NETCONF protocol operations are realized as remote procedure calls (RPCs).  This document obsoletes RFC 4741. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegR. Enns¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklund¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamejA. Biermanddatej‘⁄hfilenamegrfc6241eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128098hkeywordsÑcXMLmConfigurationrNetwork ManagementxExtensible Markup LanguagekobsoletedByÄepagesqopublicationDateN“[grankingacrfcd6241irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7803d8526∂habstractyHThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) circuits over a Packet Switch Network (PSN). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjPMC-SierradnamehD. Zelig¢kaffiliationqResolute NetworksdnamehR. Cohen¢kaffiliationoCA TechnologiesdnameiT. Nadeauddatej‘‘hfilenamegrfc6240eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-128097hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basenPW-CEP-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateM’¿Kgranking`crfcd6240irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexySynchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Circuit Emulation over Packet (CEP) MIB Using SMIv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document describes the architecture of a Suite B compliant implementation of the Secure Shell Transport Layer Protocol and the Secure Shell Authentication Protocol.  Suite B Secure Shell makes use of the elliptic curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) key agreement, the elliptic curve digital signature algorithm (ECDSA), the Advanced Encryption Standard running in Galois/Counter Mode (AES-GCM), two members of the SHA-2 family of hashes (SHA-256 and SHA-384), and X.509 certificates.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamegK. IgoeddatejÕèhfilenamegrfc6239eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128096hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÕ¬ƒgranking_crfcd6239irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Suite B Cryptographic Suites for Secure Shell (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThis document describes an extension of the One-Time Password (OTP) algorithm, namely the HMAC-based One-Time Password (HOTP) algorithm, as defined in RFC 4226, to support the time-based moving factor. The HOTP algorithm specifies an event-based OTP algorithm, where the moving factor is an event counter. The present work bases the moving factor on a time value. A time-based variant of the OTP algorithm provides short-lived OTP values, which are desirable for enhanced security.

The proposed algorithm can be used across a wide range of network applications, from remote Virtual Private Network (VPN) access and Wi-Fi network logon to transaction-oriented Web applications. The authors believe that a common and shared algorithm will facilitate adoption of two-factor authentication on the Internet by enabling interoperability across commercial and open-source implementations. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamejD. M'Raihi¢kaffiliationpDiversinet Corp.dnamejS. Machani¢kaffiliationhSymantecdnamefM. Pei¢kaffiliationnPortwise, Inc.dnameiJ. Rydellddatej‘”hfilenamegrfc6238eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128095hkeywordsÖcOTPdOATHdHOTPxtwo factor authenticationustrong authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÕ¬∏granking^crfcd6238irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊The IMAP4 specification allows the searching of only the selected mailbox.  A user often wants to search multiple mailboxes, and a client that wishes to support this must issue a series of SELECT and SEARCH commands, waiting for each to complete before moving on to the next.  This extension allows a client to search multiple mailboxes with one command, limiting the round trips and waiting for various searches to complete, and not requiring disruption of the currently selected mailbox.  This extension also uses MAILBOX and TAG fields in ESEARCH responses, allowing a client to pipeline the searches if it chooses.  This document updates RFC 4466.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘ÿhfilenamegrfc6237eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmorgdfullxmorg - Message OrganizationdnametMessage Organizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128094hkeywordsÉeemailrmultiple mailboxesgimapextkobsoletedByÅd7377epages
opublicationDateMƒíÊgranking]crfcd6237irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#IMAP4 Multimailbox SEARCH ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙This document proposes a new generic session setup attribute to make it possible to negotiate different image attributes such as image size.  A possible use case is to make it possible for a \%low-end \%hand- held terminal to display video without the need to rescale the image, something that may consume large amounts of memory and processing power.  The document also helps to maintain an optimal bitrate for video as only the image size that is desired by the receiver is transmitted. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelI. Johansson¢kaffiliationxSamsung Electronics Co., Ltd.dnamegK. JungddatejÕãhfilenamegrfc6236eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128093hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÂrgranking\crfcd6236irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQNegotiation of Generic Image Attributes in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document describes anonymization techniques for IP flow data and the export of anonymized data using the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol.  It categorizes common anonymization schemes and defines the parameters needed to describe them.  It provides guidelines for the implementation of anonymized data export and storage over IPFIX, and describes an information model and Options- based method for anonymization metadata export within the IPFIX protocol or storage in IPFIX Files.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnameiE. Boschi¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammellddatej‘’hfilenamegrfc6235eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128092hkeywordsÜeIPFIXwflow information exportuaddress anonymizationppseudonymizationodata protectiongprivacykobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateMÕ¬™granking[crfcd6235irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP Flow Anonymization SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx-Federal Information Processing Standard, FIPSfadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiT. HansenddatejÕähfilenamegrfc6234eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128091hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMƒêygrankingZcrfcd6234irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;US Secure Hash Algorithms (SHA and SHA-based HMAC and HKDF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIANA maintains the "IS-IS TLV Codepoints" registry.  This registry documents which TLVs can appear in different types of IS-IS Protocol Data Units (PDUs), but does not document which TLVs can be found in zero Remaining Lifetime Link State PDUs (LSPs), a.k.a.  purges.  This document extends the existing registry to record the set of TLVs that are permissible in purges and updates the rules for generating and processing purges in the presence of authentication.  This document updates RFC 3563, RFC 5304, and RFC 5310. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekL. Ginsbergddatej‘—hfilenamegrfc6233eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128090hkeywordsÖx*Intermediate System to Intermediate SystemcLSPhsecuritynauthenticationdIANAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM»í\grankingYcrfcd6233irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#IS-IS Registry Extension for PurgesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜At present, an IS-IS purge does not contain any information identifying the Intermediate System (IS) that generates the purge. This makes it difficult to locate the source IS.

To address this issue, this document defines a TLV to be added to purges to record the system ID of the IS generating it. Since normal Link State Protocol Data Unit (LSP) flooding does not change LSP contents, this TLV should propagate with the purge.

This document updates RFC 5301, RFC 5304, and RFC 5310. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefF. Wei¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamefY. Qin¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednameeZ. Li¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegJ. Dongddatej‘”hfilenamegrfc6232eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128089hkeywordsÖxPurge Originator IdentificationeIIH:neLSP:yeSNP:ngPurge:ykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM»íOgrankingXcrfcd6232irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Purge Originator Identification TLV for IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8918∂habstractyæThis document defines a Media Control Channel Framework Package for Interactive Voice Response (IVR) dialog interaction on media connections and conferences.  The package defines dialog management request elements for preparing, starting, and terminating dialog interactions, as well as associated responses and notifications.  Dialog interactions are specified in a dialog language.  This package defines a lightweight IVR dialog language (supporting prompt playback, runtime controls, Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency (DTMF) collection, and media recording) and allows other dialog languages to be used.  The package also defines elements for auditing package capabilities and IVR dialogs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamelS. McGlashan¢kaffiliationjRainwillowdnamelT. Melanchuk¢kaffiliationoNS-TechnologiesdnamejC. BoultonddatejÕÑhfilenamegrfc6231eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128088hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesÜopublicationDateM‹ô]grankingWcrfcd6231irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[An Interactive Voice Response (IVR) Control Package for the Media Control Channel FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6623∂habstracty-This document describes a framework and protocol for application deployment where the application programming logic and media processing are distributed. This implies that application programming logic can seamlessly gain access to appropriate resources that are not co-located on the same physical network entity. The framework uses the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) to establish an application-level control mechanism between application servers and associated external servers such as media servers.

The motivation for the creation of this framework is to provide an interface suitable to meet the requirements of a centralized conference system, where the conference system can be distributed, as defined by the XCON working group in the IETF. It is not, however, limited to this scope. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNS-TechnologiesdnamejC. Boulton¢kaffiliationjRainwillowdnamelT. Melanchuk¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamelS. McGlashanddatejÕçhfilenamegrfc6230eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-128087hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateM‹ôSgrankingVcrfcd6230irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMedia Control Channel FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx´This document contains test vectors for the stream cipher RC4.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkSecWorks ABdnameoJ. Strombergson¢kaffiliationxSimon Josefsson Datakonsult ABdnamelS. Josefssonddatej‘”hfilenamegrfc6229eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128086hkeywordsãjarcfour128jarcfour256garcfoureARC4mmStream CipherlTest VectorsqKnown Answer TestdARC4cWEPcWPAhRFC 4345kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM“∑#grankingUcrfcd6229irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Test Vectors for the Stream Cipher RC4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThis specification defines a new Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) response code, 199 Early Dialog Terminated, that a SIP forking proxy and a User Agent Server (UAS) can use to indicate to upstream SIP entities (including the User Agent Client (UAC)) that an early dialog has been terminated, before a final response is sent towards the SIP entities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej‘”hfilenamegrfc6228eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128085hkeywordsÑc199lEarly dialoggForkingtProvisional responsekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMƒêegrankingTcrfcd6228irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Response Code for Indication of Terminated DialogdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty€It is commonly recognized that the Internet routing and addressing architecture is facing challenges in scalability, mobility, multi-homing, and inter-domain traffic engineering.  The Routing Research Group is investigating an alternate architecture to meet these challenges.  This document consists of a prioritized list of design goals for the target architecture.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameeT. Liddatej‘“hfilenamegrfc6227eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-128084hkeywordsÇurouting  architecturewaddressing architecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMƒêUgrankingScrfcd6227irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex*Design Goals for Scalable Internet RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂEach Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) router in a PIM domain that supports Any Source Multicast (ASM) maintains Group-to-RP mappings that are used to identify a Rendezvous Point (RP) for a specific multicast group. PIM-SM has defined an algorithm to choose a RP from the Group-to-RP mappings learned using various mechanisms. This algorithm does not consider the PIM mode and the mechanism through which a Group-to-RP mapping was learned.

This document defines a standard algorithm to deterministically choose between several Group-to-RP mappings for a specific group. This document first explains the requirements to extend the Group-to-RP mapping algorithm and then proposes the new algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxInfosys Technologies Ltd.dnamehB. Joshi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Kessler¢kaffiliation`dnamekD. McWalterddatej‘–hfilenamegrfc6226eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-128083hkeywordsÇpauto-RP BSR hashialgorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMƒêEgrankingRcrfcd6226irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%PIM Group-to-Rendezvous-Point MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document specifies Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol options (both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6) for the coordinate-based geographic location of the client.  The Location Configuration Information (LCI) includes Latitude, Longitude, and Altitude, with resolution or uncertainty indicators for each.  Separate parameters indicate the reference datum for each of these values.  This document obsoletes RFC 3825. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Linsner¢kaffiliationrAndrew CorporationdnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. AbobaddatejÕñhfilenamegrfc6225eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-128082hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateN31œgrankingQcrfcd6225irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcDynamic Host Configuration Protocol Options for Coordinate-Based Location Configuration InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document describes deployment options for activating multicast listener functions in Proxy Mobile IPv6 domains without modifying mobility and multicast protocol standards.  Similar to home agents in Mobile IPv6, Local Mobility Anchors of Proxy Mobile IPv6 serve as multicast subscription anchor points, while Mobile Access Gateways provide Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) proxy functions.  In this scenario, mobile nodes remain agnostic of multicast mobility operations.  Support for mobile multicast senders is outside the scope of this document.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationtlink-lab & FU BerlindnamelM. Waehlisch¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnanddatej‘Œhfilenamegrfc6224eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmultimobdfullxmultimob - Multicast MobilitydnamerMulticast Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128081hkeywordsÑiMLD proxyqmulticast routingsmobility managementttransparent handoverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM£T±grankingPcrfcd6224irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTBase Deployment for Multicast Listener Support in Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) DomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂This specification defines a new Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Via header field parameter, "keep", which allows adjacent SIP entities to explicitly negotiate usage of the Network Address Translation (NAT) keep-alive mechanisms defined in SIP Outbound, in cases where SIP Outbound is not supported, cannot be applied, or where usage of keep-alives is not implicitly negotiated as part of the SIP Outbound negotiation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmbergddatej‘#hfilenamegrfc6223eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128080hkeywordsÑcSIPdSTUNhoutboundmNAT traversalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM£T¶grankingOcrfcd6223irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Indication of Support for Keep-AlivedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThe RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Canonical Name (CNAME) is a persistent transport-level identifier for an RTP endpoint.  While the Synchronization Source (SSRC) identifier of an RTP endpoint may change if a collision is detected or when the RTP application is restarted, its RTCP CNAME is meant to stay unchanged, so that RTP endpoints can be uniquely identified and associated with their RTP media streams.  For proper functionality, RTCP CNAMEs should be unique within the participants of an RTP session.  However, the existing guidelines for choosing the RTCP CNAME provided in the RTP standard are insufficient to achieve this uniqueness.  This memo updates those guidelines to allow endpoints to choose unique RTCP CNAMEs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. WingddatejÕÇhfilenamegrfc6222eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128079hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7022epages	opublicationDateMß<PgrankingNcrfcd6222irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLGuidelines for Choosing RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Canonical Names (CNAMEs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document proposes a Lightweight DHCPv6 Relay Agent (LDRA) that is used to insert relay agent options in DHCPv6 message exchanges identifying client-facing interfaces.  The LDRA can be implemented in existing access nodes (such as Digital Subscriber Link Access Multiplexers (DSLAMs) and Ethernet switches) that do not support IPv6 control or routing functions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Miles¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehS. Ooghe¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefW. Dec¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. Kavanaghddatej‘Œhfilenamegrfc6221eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128078hkeywordsÇdipv6cdslkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM“∑grankingMcrfcd6221irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLightweight DHCPv6 Relay AgentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyMany Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) protocols make use of commonly defined values that are passed in messages or packets.  To ensure consistent interpretation of these values between independent implementations, there is a need to ensure that the values and associated semantic intent are uniquely defined.  The IETF uses registry functions to record assigned protocol parameter values and their associated semantic intentions.  For each IETF protocol parameter, it is current practice for the IETF to delegate the role of Protocol Parameter Registry Operator to a nominated entity.  This document provides a description of, and the requirements for, these delegated functions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliation`dnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliation`dnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliation`dnamexInternet Architecture BoardddatejÕõhfilenamegrfc6220eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-128077hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd8722epagesopublicationDateMß<=grankingLcrfcd6220irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexLDefining the Role and Function of IETF Protocol Parameter Registry OperatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThis document presents the China Education and Research Network (CERNET)'s IVI translation design and deployment for the IPv4/IPv6 coexistence and transition.

The IVI is a prefix-specific and stateless address mapping mechanism for "an IPv6 network to the IPv4 Internet" and "the IPv4 Internet to an IPv6 network" scenarios. In the IVI design, subsets of the ISP's IPv4 addresses are embedded in the ISP's IPv6 addresses, and the hosts using these IPv6 addresses can therefore communicate with the global IPv6 Internet directly and can communicate with the global IPv4 Internet via stateless translators. The communications can either be IPv6 initiated or IPv4 initiated. The IVI mechanism supports the end-to-end address transparency and incremental deployment. The IVI is an early design deployed in the CERNET as a reference for the IETF standard documents on IPv4/IPv6 stateless translation. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamehH. Zhang¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Wuddatej‘àhfilenamegrfc6219eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128076hkeywordsÖxStateless IPv4/IPv6 translationxIPv4/IPv6 Header TranslationxIPv4-embedded IPv6 AddressxIPv4/IPv6 Multicast Translationostateless NAT64kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM¿õ≈grankingKcrfcd6219irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑThe China Education and Research Network (CERNET) IVI Translation Design and Deployment for the IPv4/IPv6 Coexistence and TransitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty®This document defines a set of vendor-specific RADIUS Attributes designed to allow both the secure transmission of cryptographic keying material and strong authentication of any RADIUS message.  These attributes have been allocated from the Cisco vendor-specific space and have been implemented by multiple vendors.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationtAdvista TechnologiesdnamehT. Zhang¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnameiJ. Walker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Saloweyddatej‘Æhfilenamegrfc6218eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128075hkeywordsÅhSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM¨iígrankingJcrfcd6218irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexKCisco Vendor-Specific RADIUS Attributes for the Delivery of Keying MaterialdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty/Broadcasting is a technology that has been largely discarded in favor of technologies like multicast.  This document builds on RFC 919 and describes a more efficient routing mechanism for broadcast packets destined for multiple Local Area Networks (LANs) or Metropolitan Area Networks (MANs) using an alternative link layer.  It significantly reduces congestion on network equipment and does not require additional physical infrastructure investment.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. RitterddatejÕûhfilenamegrfc6217eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43482hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMï‚grankingIcrfcd6217irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex2Regional Broadcast Using an Atmospheric Link LayerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document shows example call flows demonstrating the use of Transport Layer Security (TLS), and Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  It also provides information that helps implementers build interoperable SIP software.  To help facilitate interoperability testing, it includes certificates used in the example call flows and processes to create certificates for testing.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamefK. Ono¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnamejB. HibbardddatejÕàhfilenamegrfc6216eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128074hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateMõµ!grankingHcrfcd6216irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNExample Call Flows Using Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Security MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThe framework for MPLS in transport networks (RFC 5921) provides reference models for the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) Transport Service Interfaces, which are a User-to-Network Interface (UNI), and a Network-to-Network Interface (NNI). This document updates those reference models to show detailed reference points for these interfaces, along with further clarification of the functional architecture of MPLS-TP at a UNI and NNI.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by the ITU-T. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiL. Levrau¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehD. FrostddatejÕÅhfilenamegrfc6215eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128073hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM£TõgrankingGcrfcd6215irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMPLS Transport Profile User-to-Network and Network-to-Network InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxÚThis document specifies a method for transmission of IPv6 datagrams over the same medium as specified for IPv4 datagrams in RFC 1149.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationtCheck Point SoftwarednameiR. Hindenddatej‘ﬁhfilenamegrfc6214eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43481hkeywordsÇmavian carrierjapril foolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMï∏grankingFcrfcd6214irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexAdaptation of RFC 1149 for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document describes a type-length-value (TLV) for use in the IS-IS routing protocol that allows for the proper use of the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol.  There exist certain scenarios in which IS-IS will not react appropriately to a BFD-detected forwarding plane failure without use of either this TLV or some other method. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehC. Hopps¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsbergddatej‘Õhfilenamegrfc6213eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128072hkeywordsÇqtype-length-valuex"Bidirectional Forwarding DetectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMØΩXgrankingEcrfcd6213irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuIS-IS BFD-Enabled TLVdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∞This memo updates the registry of properties in Authentication- Results: message header fields to allow relaying of the results of a Vouch By Reference query. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCloudmark, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej‘Õhfilenamegrfc6212eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128071hkeywordsÉcVBRjReputationdDKIMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM£TågrankingDcrfcd6212irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBAuthentication-Results Registration for Vouch by Reference ResultsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€The Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS), unlike X.509/PKIX certificates, is vulnerable to algorithm substitution attacks.  In an algorithm substitution attack, the attacker changes either the algorithm being used or the parameters of the algorithm in order to change the result of a signature verification process.  In X.509 certificates, the signature algorithm is protected because it is duplicated in the TBSCertificate.signature field with the proviso that the validator is to compare both fields as part of the signature validation process.  This document defines a new attribute that contains a copy of the relevant algorithm identifiers so that they are protected by the signature or authentication process. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej‘œhfilenamegrfc6211eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128070hkeywordsÉgexamplefs/mimeeSMIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMõµgrankingCcrfcd6211irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLCryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Algorithm Identifier Protection AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·New hash algorithms are being developed that may include parameters.  Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) has not currently defined any hash algorithms with parameters, but anecdotal evidence suggests that defining one could cause major problems.  This document defines just such an algorithm and describes how to use it so that experiments can be run to find out how bad including hash parameters will be.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej‘œhfilenamegrfc6210eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128069hkeywordsÉgexamplegMD5-XORmParameterizedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMõµgrankingBcrfcd6210irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Experiment: Hash Functions with Parameters in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) and S/MIMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a set of cipher suites for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to support the ARIA encryption algorithm as a block cipher.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdNSRIdnamefW. Kim¢kaffiliationdNSRIdnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationdNSRIdnamegJ. Park¢kaffiliationdNSRIdnamegD. Kwonddatej‘Œhfilenamegrfc6209eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128068hkeywordsÅoaria encryptionkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMöaˇgrankingAcrfcd6209irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDAddition of the ARIA Cipher Suites to Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document describes several Internet media types defined for the Cloud Data Management Interface (CDMI) by the Storage Networking Industry Association (SNIA). The media types are:

o application/cdmi-object

o application/cdmi-container

o application/cdmi-domain

o application/cdmi-capability

o application/cdmi-queue

This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiK. Sankar¢kaffiliationdSNIAdnamehA. Jonesddatej‘Ãhfilenamegrfc6208eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128067hkeywordsÇdsniax'Storage Networking Industry AssociationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMß<-granking@crfcd6208irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Cloud Data Management Interface (CDMI) Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document specifies media types for the following formats: MODS (Metadata Object Description Schema), MADS (Metadata Authority Description Schema), METS (Metadata Encoding and Transmission Standard), MARCXML (MARC21 XML Schema), and the SRU (Search/Retrieve via URL Response Format) protocol response XML schema.  These are all XML schemas providing representations of various forms of information including metadata and search results.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLibrary of CongressdnamelR. Denenbergddatej‘Õhfilenamegrfc6207eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128066hkeywordsädmodsx"Metadata Object Description SchemadMADSx%Metadata Authority Description SchemadMETSx+Metadata Encoding and Transmission StandardgMARCXMLqMARC21 XML SchemacSRUx'Search/Retrieve via URL Response FormatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM¨iÅgranking?crfcd6207irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇThe Media Types application/mods+xml, application/mads+xml, application/mets+xml, application/marcxml+xml, and application/sru+xmldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThe Trickle algorithm allows nodes in a lossy shared medium (e.g., low-power and lossy networks) to exchange information in a highly robust, energy efficient, simple, and scalable manner.  Dynamically adjusting transmission windows allows Trickle to spread new information on the scale of link-layer transmission times while sending only a few messages per hour when information does not change.  A simple suppression mechanism and transmission point selection allow Trickle's communication rate to scale logarithmically with density.  This document describes the Trickle algorithm and considerations in its use. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamehP. Levis¢kaffiliationwLIX Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationuArch Rock CorporationdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamejO. Gnawali¢kaffiliationxJohns Hopkins UniversitydnameeJ. Koddatej‘Àhfilenamegrfc6206eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-128065hkeywordsÑkConsistencytEventual consistencyiLow-poweriLow powerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMëûJgranking>crfcd6206irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuThe Trickle AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔTechnology in the optical domain is constantly evolving, and, as a consequence, new equipment providing lambda switching capability has been developed and is currently being deployed.

Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) is a family of protocols that can be used to operate networks built from a range of technologies including wavelength (or lambda) switching. For this purpose, GMPLS defined a wavelength label as only having significance between two neighbors. Global wavelength semantics are not considered.

In order to facilitate interoperability in a network composed of next generation lambda-switch-capable equipment, this document defines a standard lambda label format that is compliant with the Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) and Coarse Wavelength Division Multiplexing (CWDM) grids defined by the International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector. The label format defined in this document can be used in GMPLS signaling and routing protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdKDDIdnamehT. Otani¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Liddatej‘Œhfilenamegrfc6205eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128064hkeywordsÑdDWDMdCWDMpWavelength LabelcLSCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMè—granking=crfcd6205irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJGeneralized Labels for Lambda-Switch-Capable (LSC) Label Switching RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7699d8359∂habstractyVThis document specifies requirements for an IPv6 Customer Edge (CE) router.  Specifically, the current version of this document focuses on the basic provisioning of an IPv6 CE router and the provisioning of IPv6 hosts attached to it.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehH. Singh¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiW. Beebee¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiC. Donley¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Stark¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehO. Troanddatej‘≥hfilenamegrfc6204eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128063hkeywordsÅtIPv6 CE requirementskobsoletedByÅd7084epagesopublicationDateM∏®granking<crfcd6204irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Basic Requirements for IPv6 Customer Edge RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxµThis document describes an IMAP protocol extension enabling a server to perform searches with inexact matching and assigning relevancy scores for matched messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekT. Sirainenddatej‘«hfilenamegrfc6203eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmorgdfullxmorg - Message OrganizationdnametMessage Organizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128062hkeywordsÅeemailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMèøgranking;crfcd6203irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex IMAP4 Extension for Fuzzy SearchdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOn today's Internet, the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) is often used (some would say abused) to enable asynchronous, "server- initiated" communication from a server to a client as well as communication from a client to a server.  This document describes known issues and best practices related to such "bidirectional HTTP" applications, focusing on the two most common mechanisms: HTTP long polling and HTTP streaming.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loreto¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationx University of Rome "Tor Vergata"dnamejS. Salsano¢kaffiliationgWebtidednamejG. Wilkinsddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc6202eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128061hkeywordsÑxHypertext Transfer Protocolrbidirectional HTTPqHTTP long pollingnHTTP streamingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMõ¥ıgranking:crfcd6202irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Known Issues and Best Practices for the Use of Long Polling and Streaming in Bidirectional HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	An operational forwarding device may need to be restarted (automatically or manually) for a variety of reasons, an event called a "reset" in this document. Since there may be an interruption in the forwarding operation during a reset, it is useful to know how long a device takes to resume the forwarding operation.

This document specifies a methodology for characterizing reset (and reset time) during benchmarking of forwarding devices and provides clarity and consistency in reset test procedures beyond what is specified in RFC 2544. Therefore, it updates RFC 2544. This document also defines the benchmarking term "reset time" and, only in this, updates RFC 1242. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Asati¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekF. Calabria¢kaffiliationkConsulinteldnameiC. Olveraddatej‘Õhfilenamegrfc6201eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-128060hkeywordsÅxoperation redundancy failoverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMèØgranking9crfcd6201irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDevice Reset CharacterizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThe Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is heavily used in Service Provider networks for both Internet and BGP/MPLS VPN services.  For resiliency purposes, redundant routers and BGP sessions can be deployed to reduce the consequences of an Autonomous System Border Router (ASBR) or BGP session breakdown on customers' or peers' traffic.  However, simply taking down or even bringing up a BGP session for maintenance purposes may still induce connectivity losses during the BGP convergence.  This is no longer satisfactory for new applications (e.g., voice over IP, online gaming, VPN).  Therefore, a solution is required for the graceful shutdown of a (set of) BGP session(s) in order to limit the amount of traffic loss during a planned shutdown.  This document expresses requirements for such a solution.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamekP. Francois¢kaffiliationcIIJdnamejC. Pelsser¢kaffiliationxOrange Business ServicesdnamehZ. Ahmad¢kaffiliationnTelefonica I+DdnamevA.J. Elizondo Armengol¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takedaddatej‘Œhfilenamegrfc6198eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128059hkeywordsâgroutingcBGPhgracefulhshutdownqconnectivity losskmaintenanceqnetwork operationqmake-before-breakgplannedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMß<!granking6crfcd6198irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Requirements for the Graceful Shutdown of BGP SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLocation-to-Service Translation (LoST) maps service identifiers and location information to service contact URIs.  If a LoST client wants to discover available services for a particular location, it will perform a <listServicesByLocation> query to the LoST server.  However, the LoST server, in its response, does not provide context information; that is, it does not provide any additional information about the geographical region within which the returned list of services is considered valid.  Therefore, this document defines a Service List Boundary that returns a local context along with the list of services returned, in order to assist the client in not missing a change in available services when moving.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfnic.atdnamegK. Wolfddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc6197eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-128058hkeywordsÅvlistservicesbylocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMØΩIgranking5crfcd6197irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFLocation-to-Service Translation (LoST) Service List Boundary ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxjThis document registers the mailserver: URI scheme as historic in the IANA URI registry. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘»hfilenamegrfc6196eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128057hkeywordsÇjmailservercURIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMåÁgranking4crfcd6196irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Moving mailserver: URI Scheme to HistoricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis document specifies Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) parameter assignment considerations for the allocation of Domain Name System (DNS) resource record types, CLASSes, operation codes, error codes, DNS protocol message header bits, and AFSDB resource record subtypes.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej‘Õhfilenamegrfc6195eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128056hkeywordsÉfRRTYPEeRCODEeAFSDBkobsoletedByÅd6895epagesopublicationDateMëßgranking3crfcd6195irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Domain Name System (DNS) IANA ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÕThis document includes security considerations for the SHA-0 and SHA-1 message digest algorithm.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polk¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegL. Chen¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. HoffmanddatejÕÜhfilenamegrfc6194eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128055hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMëû1granking2crfcd6194irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexISecurity Considerations for the SHA-0 and SHA-1 Message-Digest AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyyThis document specifies how to establish a media session that represents a virtual private network using the Session Initiation Protocol for the purpose of on-demand media/application sharing between peers.  It extends the protocol identifier of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) so that it can negotiate use of the Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE) for media sessions in the SDP offer/answer model.  It also specifies a method to boot up IKE and generate IPsec security associations using a self-signed certificate.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert SparksgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamehM. Saito¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnameiM. Toyamaddatej‘öhfilenamegrfc6193eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128054hkeywordsÑcSIPeIPsecesetupcVPNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMõ¥Âgranking1crfcd6193irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexhMedia Description for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE) in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–This memo provides a method for protecting a router's control plane from undesired or malicious traffic. In this approach, all legitimate router control plane traffic is identified. Once legitimate traffic has been identified, a filter is deployed in the router's forwarding plane. That filter prevents traffic not specifically identified as legitimate from reaching the router's control plane, or rate-limits such traffic to an acceptable level.

Note that the filters described in this memo are applied only to traffic that is destined for the router, and not to all traffic that is passing through the router. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehD. Dugal¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegR. Dunnddatej‘…hfilenamegrfc6192eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-128053hkeywordsÉcACLxRouter Control Plane ProtectionfFilterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMì∞granking0crfcd6192irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Protecting the Router Control PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¨This document describes an algorithm for processing incoming SYN segments that allows higher connection-establishment rates between any two TCP endpoints when a TCP Timestamps option is present in the incoming SYN segment.  This document only modifies processing of SYN segments received for connections in the TIME-WAIT state; processing in all other states is unchanged.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUK CPNIdnamegF. GontddatejÕÜhfilenamegrfc6191eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128052hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateMõ¥⁄granking/crfcd6191irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberüetotaletitlex1Reducing the TIME-WAIT State Using TCP TimestampsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThis memo describes an RTP payload format for Scalable Video Coding (SVC) as defined in Annex G of ITU-T Recommendation H.264, which is technically identical to Amendment 3 of ISO/IEC International Standard 14496-10.  The RTP payload format allows for packetization of one or more Network Abstraction Layer (NAL) units in each RTP packet payload, as well as fragmentation of a NAL unit in multiple RTP packets.  Furthermore, it supports transmission of an SVC stream over a single as well as multiple RTP sessions.  The payload format defines a new media subtype name "H264-SVC", but is still backward compatible to RFC 6184 since the base layer, when encapsulated in its own RTP stream, must use the H.264 media subtype name ("H264") and the packetization method specified in RFC 6184.  The payload format has wide applicability in videoconferencing, Internet video streaming, and high-bitrate entertainment-quality video, among others. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiS. Wenger¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejY.-K. Wang¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer HHIdnamejT. Schierl¢kaffiliationeVidyodnamepA. Eleftheriadisddatej‘—hfilenamegrfc6190eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpayloaddfullx(payload - Audio/Video Transport PayloadsdnamexAudio/Video Transport Payloadsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128051hkeywordsÖcSVCcAVCiH.264/AVCuAdvanced Video CodinguScalable Video CodingkobsoletedByÄepagesdopublicationDateMƒê8granking.crfcd6190irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,RTP Payload Format for Scalable Video CodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¥This document defines ZRTP, a protocol for media path Diffie-Hellman exchange to agree on a session key and parameters for establishing unicast Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) sessions for Voice over IP (VoIP) applications.  The ZRTP protocol is media path keying because it is multiplexed on the same port as RTP and does not require support in the signaling protocol.  ZRTP does not assume a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) or require the complexity of certificates in end devices.  For the media session, ZRTP provides confidentiality, protection against man-in-the-middle (MiTM) attacks, and, in cases where the signaling protocol provides end-to-end integrity protection, authentication.  ZRTP can utilize a Session Description Protocol (SDP) attribute to provide discovery and authentication through the signaling channel.  To provide best effort SRTP, ZRTP utilizes normal RTP/AVP (Audio-Visual Profile) profiles.  ZRTP secures media sessions that include a voice media stream and can also secure media sessions that do not include voice by using an optional digital signature.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmZfone ProjectdnamemP. Zimmermann¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiJ. CallasddatejÕNhfilenamegrfc6189eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128050hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagessopublicationDateM£TÅgranking-crfcd6189irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5ZRTP: Media Path Key Agreement for Unicast Secure RTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis memo describes the use of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) with 192- and 256-bit keys within the Secure RTP (SRTP) protocol.  It details counter mode encryption for SRTP and Secure Realtime Transport Control Protocol (SRTCP) and a new SRTP Key Derivation Function (KDF) for AES-192 and AES-256. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiD. McGrewddatej‘Ãhfilenamegrfc6188eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128049hkeywordsÅdSRTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMà2Ögranking,crfcd6188irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,The Use of AES-192 and AES-256 in Secure RTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyX.509 public key certificates use a signature by a trusted certification authority to bind a given public key to a given digital identity.  This document specifies how to use X.509 version 3 public key certificates in public key algorithms in the Secure Shell protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamegK. Igoe¢kaffiliationx#Queensland University of TechnologydnamejD. StebiladdatejÕ}hfilenamegrfc6187eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128048hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMìØ¸granking+crfcd6187irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4X.509v3 Certificates for Secure Shell AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxdThis specification describes how SRV records can be used to locate email services. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej‘©hfilenamegrfc6186eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128047hkeywordsÖdimapdpop3dsmtpcdnsidiscoverykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMìØÊgranking*crfcd6186irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Use of SRV Records for Locating Email Submission/Access ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8314d8553∂habstractyaThis document describes an RTP payload format for the Reduced- Complexity Decoding Operation (RCDO) for H.264 Baseline profile bitstreams, as specified in ITU-T Recommendation H.241.  RCDO reduces the decoding cost and resource consumption of the video processing.  The RCDO RTP payload format is based on the H.264 RTP payload format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhTANDBERGdnamemT. Kristensen¢kaffiliationhTANDBERGdnamehP. Luthiddatej‘∆hfilenamegrfc6185eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128046hkeywordsáeH.264eH.241eITU-TcRTPeVideocSDPdRCDOkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM“∑granking)crfcd6185irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexORTP Payload Format for H.264 Reduced-Complexity Decoding Operation (RCDO) VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis memo describes an RTP Payload format for the ITU-T Recommendation H.264 video codec and the technically identical ISO/IEC International Standard 14496-10 video codec, excluding the Scalable Video Coding (SVC) extension and the Multiview Video Coding extension, for which the RTP payload formats are defined elsewhere. The RTP payload format allows for packetization of one or more Network Abstraction Layer Units (NALUs), produced by an H.264 video encoder, in each RTP payload. The payload format has wide applicability, as it supports applications from simple low bitrate conversational usage, to Internet video streaming with interleaved transmission, to high bitrate video-on-demand.

This memo obsoletes RFC 3984. Changes from RFC 3984 are summarized in Section 14. Issues on backward compatibility to RFC 3984 are discussed in Section 15. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamejY.-K. Wang¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationhTandbergdnamemT. Kristensen¢kaffiliationxWorldGate CommunicationsdnamehR. Jesupddatej‘∆hfilenamegrfc6184eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128045hkeywordsÉcAVCiH.264/AVCuAdvanced Video CodingkobsoletedByÄepageseopublicationDateM¿õ∫granking(crfcd6184irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"RTP Payload Format for H.264 VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5This document describes a framework for IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Mediation.  This framework extends the IPFIX reference model specified in RFC 5470 by defining the IPFIX Mediator components.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamelA. Kobayashi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationkTU MuenchendnamehG. Muenz¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamelK. IshibashiddatejÕzhfilenamegrfc6183eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-128044hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMöaıgranking'crfcd6183irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Mediation: FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?Hosts are often connected by multiple paths, but TCP restricts communications to a single path per transport connection. Resource usage within the network would be more efficient were these multiple paths able to be used concurrently. This should enhance user experience through improved resilience to network failure and higher throughput.

This document outlines architectural guidelines for the development of a Multipath Transport Protocol, with references to how these architectural components come together in the development of a Multipath TCP (MPTCP). This document lists certain high-level design decisions that provide foundations for the design of the MPTCP protocol, based upon these architectural requirements. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsRoke Manor ResearchdnamegA. Ford¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnameiC. Raiciu¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationx Universite catholique de LouvaindnamehS. Barre¢kaffiliationxFranklin and Marshall CollegednamejJ. Iyengarddatej‘œhfilenamegrfc6182eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128043hkeywordsÅxmultipath tcp architecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMà2ugranking&crfcd6182irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Architectural Guidelines for Multipath TCP DevelopmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒMultipath TCP (MPTCP for short) describes the extensions proposed for TCP so that endpoints of a given TCP connection can use multiple paths to exchange data.  Such extensions enable the exchange of segments using different source-destination address pairs, resulting in the capability of using multiple paths in a significant number of scenarios.  Some level of multihoming and mobility support can be achieved through these extensions.  However, the support for multiple IP addresses per endpoint may have implications on the security of the resulting MPTCP.  This note includes a threat analysis for MPTCP.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnuloddatej‘¬hfilenamegrfc6181eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemptcpdfullumptcp - Multipath TCPdnamemMultipath TCPdtypebwgbidjdoc-128042hkeywordsÑmMultipath TCPgthreatshsecurityeMPTCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÄ◊granking%crfcd6181irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRThreat Analysis for TCP Extensions for Multipath Operation with Multiple AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„The Internet continues to grow beyond the capabilities of IPv4.  An expansion in the address space is clearly required.  With its increase in the number of available prefixes and addresses in a subnet, and improvements in address management, IPv6 is the only real option on the table.  Yet, IPv6 deployment requires some effort, resources, and expertise.  The availability of many different deployment models is one reason why expertise is required.  This document discusses the IPv6 deployment models and migration tools, and it recommends ones that have been found to work well in operational networks in many common situations.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. BakerddatejÕwhfilenamegrfc6180eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128041hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM‡G¡granking$crfcd6180irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFGuidelines for Using IPv6 Transition Mechanisms during IPv6 DeploymentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractySince the Internet must continue to support escalating growth due to increasing demand, it is clear that current routing architectures and operational practices must be updated.  This document proposes an Internet Routing Overlay Network (IRON) that supports sustainable growth while requiring no changes to end systems and no changes to the existing routing system.  IRON further addresses other important issues including routing scaling, mobility management, multihoming, traffic engineering and NAT traversal.  While business considerations are an important determining factor for widespread adoption, they are out of scope for this document.  This document is a product of the IRTF Routing Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamejF. Templinddatej‘Œhfilenamegrfc6179eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128040hkeywordsìmEncapsulationfTunnellArchitecturekScalabilityhMobilityeMANEThSecurityiRecursionjAddressinggRoutingdIPv6iAerospacekAeronauticseSpacedIRONfRANGERcVETdSEALfISATAPkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateMz÷˙granking#crfcd6179irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex+The Internet Routing Overlay Network (IRON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies how Label Edge Routers (LERs) should behave when determining whether to MPLS encapsulate an IPv4 packet with header options.  Lack of a formal standard has resulted in different LER forwarding behaviors for IPv4 packets with header options despite being associated with a prefix-based Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC).  IPv4 option packets that belong to a prefix-based FEC, yet are forwarded into an IPv4/MPLS network without being MPLS- encapsulated, present a security risk against the MPLS infrastructure.  Further, LERs that are unable to MPLS encapsulate IPv4 packets with header options cannot operate in certain MPLS environments.  While this newly defined LER behavior is mandatory to implement, it is optional to invoke. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Smith¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJ. Mullooly¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiW. Jaeger¢kaffiliationunLayer CommunicationsdnameiT. Schollddatej‘…hfilenamegrfc6178eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-128039hkeywordsÖcFECdMPLScLERhSecuritycDoSkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMÄÕgranking"crfcd6178irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Label Edge Router Forwarding of IPv4 Option PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty∞RFC 3177 argued that in IPv6, end sites should be assigned /48 blocks in most cases. The Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) adopted that recommendation in 2002, but began reconsidering the policy in 2005. This document obsoletes the RFC 3177 recommendations on the assignment of IPv6 address space to end sites. The exact choice of how much address space to assign end sites is an issue for the operational community. The IETF's role in this case is limited to providing guidance on IPv6 architectural and operational considerations. This document reviews the architectural and operational considerations of end site assignments as well as the motivations behind the original recommendations in RFC 3177. Moreover, this document clarifies that a one-size-fits-all recommendation of /48 is not nuanced enough for the broad range of end sites and is no longer recommended as a single default.

This document obsoletes RFC 3177. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamejL. Robertsddatej‘Ãhfilenamegrfc6177eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-128038hkeywordsÉxinternet architecture boardxengineering steering grouphprotocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMèúgranking!crfcd6177irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberùetotaletitlex$IPv6 Address Assignment to End SitesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document requires that when Transport Layer Security (TLS) clients and servers establish connections, they never negotiate the use of Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) version 2.0.  This document updates the backward compatibility sections found in the Transport Layer Security (TLS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. PolkddatejÕyhfilenamegrfc6176eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-128037hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMxDÉgranking crfcd6176irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Prohibiting Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) Version 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty9This document specifies requirements for new functionality to be added to the IETF Datatracker tool to make it possible for Working Group (WG) Chairs and their Delegates to input and update the status of the Internet-Drafts (I-Ds) associated with their WGs.  After these requirements are implemented, WG Chairs will be able to use the Datatracker to provide everyone with more information about the status and progression of WG I-Ds than is currently possible.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiTrekAheaddnamenE. Juskeviciusddatej‘¬hfilenamegrfc6175eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128036hkeywordsÇrWG Document StatesjI-D StateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMz÷Îgrankingcrfcd6175irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRequirements to Extend the Datatracker for IETF Working Group Chairs and AuthorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The IETF Datatracker tool needs to be enhanced to make it possible for Working Group (WG) Chairs to provide IETF participants with more information about the status and progression of WG documents than is currently possible.

This document defines new states and status annotation tags that need to be added to the Datatracker to enable WG Chairs and their Delegates to track the status of Internet-Drafts (I-Ds) that are associated with their WGs. This document also describes the meaning of all previously implemented I-D states and substate annotation tags currently used by IETF Area Directors to indicate the status of I-Ds that have been sent to the IESG for evaluation and publication. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiTrekAheaddnamenE. Juskeviciusddatej‘∆hfilenamegrfc6174eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128035hkeywordsÇmWG I-D StateswI-D Availability StateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMz÷›grankingcrfcd6174irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Definition of IETF Working Group Document StatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines Management Information Base (MIB) objects to monitor and control the Internet Fibre Channel Protocol (iFCP) gateway instances and their associated sessions, for use with network management protocols.

This document obsoletes RFC 4369. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpHCL TechnologiesdnamemP. Venkatesenddatej‘ƒhfilenamegrfc6173eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymestormdfullxstorm - STORage MaintenancednamesSTORage Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128034hkeywordsÉxManagement Information BasecmibmIFCP-MGMT-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMz÷Œgrankingcrfcd6173irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMDefinitions of Managed Objects for the Internet Fibre Channel Protocol (iFCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWChanges to Fibre Channel have caused the specification of the Internet Fibre Channel Protocol (iFCP) address translation mode to become incorrect. Due to the absence of usage of iFCP address translation mode, it is deprecated by this document. iFCP address transparent mode remains correctly specified.

iFCP address transparent mode has been implemented and is in current use; therefore, it is not affected by this document.

