er point-to-point links, it is useful, for security and other reasons, to use 127-bit IPv6 prefixes.  Such a practice parallels the use of 31-bit prefixes in IPv4.  This document specifies the motivation for, and usages of, 127-bit IPv6 prefix lengths on inter-router point-to-point links. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationx"Juniper Networks - Keio UniversitydnamehM. Kohno¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiB. Nitzan¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationxInternet Initiative JapandnamelY. Matsuzaki¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejL. Colitti¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiT. Nartenddatej‘—hfilenamegrfc6164eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128025hkeywordsÉjaddressingmprefix lengthhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM¨isgrankingcrfcd6164irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Using 127-Bit IPv6 Prefixes on Inter-Router LinksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6547∂habstractyXThis document provides a framework for applying Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) and the Path Computation Element (PCE) architecture to the control of Wavelength Switched Optical Networks (WSONs). In particular, it examines Routing and Wavelength Assignment (RWA) of optical paths.

This document focuses on topological elements and path selection constraints that are common across different WSON environments; as such, it does not address optical impairments in any depth. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejW. Imajukuddatej‘“hfilenamegrfc6163eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-128024hkeywordsÉx*Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switchingx!Routing and Wavelength AssignmentcRWAkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateMµß,grankingcrfcd6163irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnFramework for GMPLS and Path Computation Element (PCE) Control of Wavelength Switched Optical Networks (WSONs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThis document describes conventions for using Elliptic Curve cryptographic algorithms with SignedData and EnvelopedData to protect the AsymmetricKeyPackage content type.  Specifically, it includes conventions necessary to implement Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) with EnvelopedData and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) with SignedData.  This document extends RFC 5959. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘—hfilenamegrfc6162eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128023hkeywordsÉeecdsadecdhx<EnvelopedData and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature AlgorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM£Ttgrankingcrfcd6162irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdElliptic Curve Algorithms for Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Asymmetric Key Package Content TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This document describes the conventions for using several Elliptic Curve cryptographic algorithms with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) encrypted key package content type.  Specifically, it includes conventions necessary to implement Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) with EnvelopedData and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) with SignedData.  This document extends RFC 6033. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘—hfilenamegrfc6161eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128022hkeywordsÉeecdsadecdhx<EnvelopedData and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature AlgorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM£Tcgrankingcrfcd6161irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcElliptic Curve Algorithms for Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Encrypted Key Package Content TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document describes the conventions for using several cryptographic algorithms with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) to protect the symmetric key package content type.  Specifically, it includes conventions necessary to implement SignedData, EnvelopedData, EncryptedData, and AuthEnvelopedData. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. TurnerddatejÕâhfilenamegrfc6160eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128021hkeywo