opublicationDateMtF÷grankingcrfcd6150irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevMD4 to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡This document retires MD2 and discusses the reasons for doing so.  This document moves RFC 1319 to Historic status.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegL. Chenddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc6149eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128010hkeywordsÉhsecurityjencryptionisignaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMuï…grankingcrfcd6149irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevMD2 to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈSome relay agents extract lease information from the DHCP messages exchanged between the client and DHCP server.  This lease information is used by relay agents for various purposes like antispoofing and prevention of flooding.  RFC 4388 defines a mechanism for relay agents to retrieve the lease information from the DHCP server when this information is lost.  The existing lease query mechanism is data-driven, which means that a relay agent can initiate the lease query only when it starts receiving data to and from the clients.  In certain scenarios, this model is not scalable.  This document first looks at issues in the existing mechanism and then proposes a new query type, query by Remote ID, to address these issues. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekP. Kurapati¢kaffiliationxInfosys Technologies Ltd.dnamejR. Desetti¢kaffiliationxInfosys Technologies Ltd.dnamehB. Joshiddatej—œhfilenamegrfc6148eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128009hkeywordsÅx#dynamic host configuration protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMl^£grankingcrfcd6148irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+DHCPv4 Lease Query by Relay Agent Remote IDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’DNS64 is a mechanism for synthesizing AAAA records from A records.  DNS64 is used with an IPv6/IPv4 translator to enable client-server communication between an IPv6-only client and an IPv4-only server, without requiring any changes to either the IPv6 or the IPv4 node, for the class of applications that work through NATs.  This document specifies DNS64, and provides suggestions on how it should be deployed in conjunction with IPv6/IPv4 translators. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationhShinkurodnamekA. Sullivan¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekP. Matthews¢kaffiliationnIMDEA NetworksdnamenI. van Beijnumddatej‘ƒhfilenamegrfc6147eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128008hkeywordsÅdAAAAkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateM∏ß€grankingcrfcd6147irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWDNS64: DNS Extensions for Network Address Translation from IPv6 Clients to IPv4 ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document describes stateful NAT64 translation, which allows IPv6-only clients to contact IPv4 servers using unicast UDP, TCP, or ICMP.  One or more public IPv4 addresses assigned to a NAT64 translator are shared among several IPv6-only clients.  When stateful NAT64 is used in conjunction with DNS64, no changes are usually required in the IPv6 client or the IPv4 server.fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekP. Matthews¢kaffiliationnIMDEA NetworksdnamenI. van Beijnumddatej‘µhfilenamegrfc6146eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128007hkeywordsÇeNAT64dIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateM∏ß–grankingcrfcd6146irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZStateful NAT64: Network Address and Protocol Translation from IPv6 Clients to IPv4 ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx–This document describes the Stateless IP/ICMP Translation Algorithm (SIIT), which translates between IPv4 and IPv6 packet headers (including ICMP headers).  This document obsoletes RFC 2765. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Bakerddatej‘∑hfilenamegrfc6145eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128006hkeywordsàdSIIThinternethprotocolgcontrolgmessagedIPv4dIPv6x'Stateless IP/ICMP Translation AlgorithmkobsoletedByÅd7915epages!opublicationDateM∏ß≈grankingcrfcd6145irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP/ICMP Translation AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6791d7757∂habstracty¶This note describes a framework for IPv4/IPv6 translation.  This is in the context of replacing Network Address Translation - Protocol Translation (NAT-PT), which was deprecated by RFC 4966, and to enable networks to have IPv4 and IPv6 coexist in a somewhat rational manner while transitioning to an IPv6 network.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefK. Yinddatej‘≤hfilenamegrfc6144eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-128005hkeywordsÇustateless translationtstateful translationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM∏ß∂granking␀crfcd6144irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Framework for IPv4/IPv6 TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFB ("remote framebuffer") is a simple protocol for remote access to graphical user interfaces that allows a client to view and control a window system on another computer.  Because it works at the framebuffer level, RFB is applicable to all windowing systems and applications.  This document describes the protocol used to communicate between an RFB client and RFB server.  RFB is the protocol used in VNC.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlRealVNC Ltd.dnamemT. Richardson¢kaffiliationlRealVNC Ltd.dnameiJ. Levineddatej‘∆hfilenamegrfc6143eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128004hkeywordsÑcvnccrfbrremote framebufferjremote GUIkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateMuï∂grankingˇcrfcd6143irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Remote Framebuffer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis RFC provides a framework for transporting ANSI C12.22/IEEE 1703/MC12.22 Advanced Metering Infrastructure (AMI) Application Layer Messages on an IP network.

This document is not an official submission on behalf of the ANSI C12.19 and C12.22 working groups. It was created by participants in those groups, building on knowledge of several proprietary C12.22- over-IP implementations. The content of this document is an expression of a consensus aggregation of those implementations. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsFuture DOS R&D Inc.dnamehA. Moise¢kaffiliationsFuture DOS R&D Inc.dnamejJ. Brodkinddatej‘¿hfilenamegrfc6142eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128003hkeywordsÉx Advanced Metering Infrastructurecamixapplication layer messagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMëùögranking˛crfcd6142irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5ANSI C12.22, IEEE 1703, and MC12.22 Transport Over IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThe procedures for handling SIP re-INVITEs are described in RFC 3261.  Implementation and deployment experience has uncovered a number of issues with the original documentation, and this document provides additional procedures that update the original specification to address those issues.  In particular, this document defines in which situations a UAS (User Agent Server) should generate a success response and in which situations a UAS should generate an error response to a re-INVITE.  Additionally, this document defines further details of procedures related to target-refresh requests. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamefY. Gaoddatej‘…hfilenamegrfc6141eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-128002hkeywordsÉire-INVITEloffer/answerhrollbackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMoÿRgranking˝crfcd6141irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVRe-INVITE and Target-Refresh Request Handling in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThis document defines a mechanism by which a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) server acting as a traditional Private Branch Exchange (PBX) can register with a SIP Service Provider (SSP) to receive phone calls for SIP User Agents (UAs).  In order to function properly, this mechanism requires that each of the Addresses of Record (AORs) registered in bulk map to a unique set of contacts.  This requirement is satisfied by AORs representing phone numbers regardless of the domain, since phone numbers are fully qualified and globally unique.  This document therefore focuses on this use case. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnamejA.B. Roachddatej‘œhfilenamegrfc6140eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmartinidfullx:martini - Multiple AoR reachabiliTy InformatioN Indicationdnamex0Multiple AoR reachabiliTy InformatioN Indicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-128001hkeywordsÜqBulk RegistrationuImplicit RegistrationcGINcPBXcSSPgSIP-PBXkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateMoÿCgranking¸crfcd6140irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRegistration for Multiple Phone Numbers in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¢"Routing and Addressing in Networks with Global Enterprise Recursion (RANGER)" (RFC 5720) provides an architectural framework for scalable routing and addressing.  It provides an incrementally deployable approach for scalability, provider independence, mobility, multihoming, traffic engineering, and security.  This document describes a series of use cases in order to showcase the architectural capabilities.  It further shows how the RANGER architecture restores the network-within-network principles originally intended for the sustained growth of the Internet.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamejS. Russert¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamemE. Fleischman¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamejF. Templinddatej‘phfilenamegrfc6139eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-128000hkeywordsçmEncapsulationfTunnellArchitecturekScalabilityhMobilityeMANEThSecuritydIPv6iAerospacedIRONcVETdSEALfISATAPkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateMkÈügranking˚crfcd6139irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexVRouting and Addressing in Networks with Global Enterprise Recursion (RANGER) ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&RFC 5443 describes a mechanism to achieve LDP IGP synchronization to prevent black-holing traffic (e.g., VPN) when an Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) is operational on a link but Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) is not.  If this mechanism is applied to broadcast links that have more than one LDP peer, the metric increase procedure can only be applied to the link as a whole but not to an individual peer.  When a new LDP peer comes up on a broadcast network, this can result in loss of traffic through other established peers on that network.  This document describes a mechanism to address that use-case without dropping traffic.  The mechanism does not introduce any protocol message changes.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegS. Kini¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameeW. LuddatejÕshfilenamegrfc6138eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127999hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMcRgranking˙crfcd6138irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.LDP IGP Synchronization for Broadcast NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyCHandling multiple sets of network trouble tickets (TTs) originating from different participants' inter-connected network environments poses a series of challenges for the involved institutions. A Grid is a good example of such a multi-domain project. Each of the participants follows different procedures for handling trouble in its domain, according to the local technical and linguistic profile. The TT systems of the participants collect, represent, and disseminate TT information in different formats.

As a result, management of the daily workload by a central Network Operation Centre (NOC) is a challenge on its own. Normalization of TTs to a common format at the central NOC can ease presentation, storing, and handling of the TTs. In the present document, we provide a model for automating the collection and normalization of the TT received by multiple networks forming the Grid. Each of the participants is using its home TT system within its domain for handling trouble incidents, whereas the central NOC is gathering the tickets in the normalized format for repository and handling. XML is used as the common representation language. The model was defined and used as part of the networking support activity of the EGEE (Enabling Grids for E-sciencE) project. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqPeter Saint-AndregauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCERTHdnamekD. Zisiadis¢kaffiliationeCERTHdnamekS. Kopsidas¢kaffiliationeCERTHdnameiM. Tsavli¢kaffiliationdCNRSdnamekG. Cessieuxddatej‘íhfilenamegrfc6137eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127998hkeywordsÉdGridjManagementdEGEEkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateM_(◊granking˘crfcd6137irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-The Network Trouble Ticket Data Model (NTTDM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document provides framework and requirements for Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM).  The OAM framework is intended to provide OAM layering across L2VPN services, pseudowires (PWs), and Packet Switched Network (PSN) tunnels.  This document is intended to identify OAM requirements for L2VPN services, i.e., Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS), Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS), and IP-only LAN Service (IPLS).  Furthermore, if L2VPN service OAM requirements impose specific requirements on PW OAM and/or PSN OAM, those specific PW and/or PSN OAM requirements are also identified.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Sajassi¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamehD. MohanddatejÕshfilenamegrfc6136eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127997hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateMz÷ígranking¯crfcd6136irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextLayer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN) Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Requirements and FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹This document defines an alternative connection model for Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) User Agents (UAs); this model uses the connection-oriented media (COMEDIA) mechanism in order to create the MSRP transport connection.  The model allows MSRP UAs behind Network Address Translators (NATs) to negotiate which endpoint initiates the establishment of the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) connection, in order for MSRP messages to traverse the NAT. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamegS. Blauddatej“ hfilenamegrfc6135eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127996hkeywordsÑgcomediakcomedia-tlserelaycSBCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMk‚∫granking˜crfcd6135irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMAn Alternative Connection Model for the Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬The Sieve email filtering language can be used to implement email whitelisting, blacklisting, personal distribution lists, and other sorts of list matching. Currently, this requires that all members of such lists be hard-coded in the script itself. Whenever a member of a list is added or deleted, the script needs to be updated and possibly uploaded to a mail server.

This document defines a Sieve extension for accessing externally stored lists -- lists whose members are stored externally to the script, such as using the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP), the Application Configuration Access Protocol (ACAP), vCard Extensions to WebDAV (CardDAV), or relational databases. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelPete Resnickdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. LeibaddatejÕ∞hfilenamegrfc6134eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127995hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateN?grankingˆcrfcd6134irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Sieve Extension: Externally Stored ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document describes how the Sieve email filtering language, along with some extensions, can be used to create automatic replies to incoming electronic mail messages based on the address book and presence information of the recipient.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiR. George¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. MelnikovddatejÕ}hfilenamegrfc6133eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127994hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateN?grankingıcrfcd6133irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTSieve Email Filtering: Use of Presence Information with Auto-Responder Function