gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxLIX - Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnamegJ. Deanddatej‘«hfilenamegrfc6130eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127991hkeywordsäeMANETfOLSRv2hpacketbbpRouting ProtocoldNHDPnad hoc networknbi-directionalo2-hop discoveryhWirelesscSMFkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateMõ¥ÃgrankingÚcrfcd6130irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDMobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Neighborhood Discovery Protocol (NHDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7183d7466d7188∂habstractyThis document defines the 'application/tei+xml' media type for markup languages defined in accordance with the Text Encoding and Interchange guidelines.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxTEI Consortium and INRIAdnameiL. Romary¢kaffiliationxThe Royal Library CopenhagendnamekS. Lundbergddatej‘∏hfilenamegrfc6129eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127990hkeywordsÖxText Encoding Initiativecxmlmtext encodingstext representationiMIME typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMUŒñgrankingÒcrfcd6129irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$The 'application/tei+xml' Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty÷The Session Description Protocol (SDP) has an attribute that allows RTP applications to specify an address and a port associated with the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) traffic.  In RTP-based source-specific multicast (SSM) sessions, the same attribute is used to designate the address and the RTCP port of the Feedback Target in the SDP description.  However, the RTCP port associated with the SSM session itself cannot be specified by the same attribute to avoid ambiguity, and thus, is required to be derived from the "m=" line of the media description.  Deriving the RTCP port from the "m=" line imposes an unnecessary restriction.  This document removes this restriction by introducing a new SDP attribute. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. BegenddatejÕxhfilenamegrfc6128eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127989hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMUŒâgrankingcrfcd6128irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Port for Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) SessionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8When IPv6 was designed, it was expected that the transition from IPv4 to IPv6 would occur more smoothly and expeditiously than experience has revealed. The growth of the IPv4 Internet and predicted depletion of the free pool of IPv4 address blocks on a foreseeable horizon has highlighted an urgent need to revisit IPv6 deployment models. This document provides an overview of deployment scenarios with the goal of helping to understand what types of additional tools the industry needs to assist in IPv4 and IPv6 co-existence and transition.

This document was originally created as input to the Montreal co- existence interim meeting in October 2008, which led to the rechartering of the Behave and Softwire working groups to take on new IPv4 and IPv6 co-existence work. This document is published as a historical record of the thinking at the time, but hopefully will also help readers understand the rationale behind current IETF tools for co-existence and transition. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekM. Townsleyddatej‘¿hfilenamegrfc6127eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127988hkeywordsàqaddress depletionktranslationfNAT-PTjdual-stackhSoftwirefBehavecNATfNAT444kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM›·
