Template, and IANA ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis document discusses the use of the Domain Name System (DNS) for storage of data associated with E.164 numbers, and for resolving those numbers into URIs that can be used (for example) in telephony call setup.  This document also describes how the DNS can be used to identify the services associated with an E.164 number.  This document obsoletes RFC 3761. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationsRoke Manor ResearchdnameiL. Conroy¢kaffiliationx Japan Registry Service Co., Ltd.dnamekK. Fujiwaraddatej‘πhfilenamegrfc6116eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127977hkeywordsÖcDNSeE.164eNAPTRx#dynamic delegation discovery systemie164.arpakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMz÷[granking‰crfcd6116irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmThe E.164 to Uniform Resource Identifiers (URI) Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Application (ENUM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¥It is commonly recognized that the Internet routing and addressing architecture is facing challenges in scalability, multihoming, and inter-domain traffic engineering.  This document presents, as a recommendation of future directions for the IETF, solutions that could aid the future scalability of the Internet.  To this end, this document surveys many of the proposals that were brought forward for discussion in this activity, as well as some of the subsequent analysis and the architectural recommendation of the chairs.  This document is a product of the Routing Research Group.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameeT. Liddatej…Ωhfilenamegrfc6115eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrrgdfullxrrg - Routing Research GroupdnamevRouting Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127976hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDateMdXgranking„crfcd6115irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex)Recommendation for a Routing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes the specification of the blockcipher CLEFIA.  CLEFIA is a 128-bit blockcipher, with key lengths of 128, 192, and 256 bits, which is compatible with the interface of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES).  The algorithm of CLEFIA was published in 2007, and its security has been scrutinized in the public community.  CLEFIA is one of the new-generation lightweight blockcipher algorithms designed after AES.  Among them, CLEFIA offers high performance in software and hardware as well as lightweight implementation in hardware.  CLEFIA will be of benefit to the Internet, which will be connected to more distributed and constrained devices.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim PolkgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSony CorporationdnameiM. Katagi¢kaffiliationpSony CorporationdnameiS. Moriaiddatej‘õhfilenamegrfc6114eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127975hkeywordsÉhsecurityxlightweight cryptographytencryption algorithmkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateMÄ∑granking‚crfcd6114irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexThe 128-Bit Blockcipher CLEFIAdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoKerberos is a protocol for verifying the identity of principals (e.g., a workstation user or a network server) on an open network. The Kerberos protocol provides a facility called pre-authentication. Pre-authentication mechanisms can use this facility to extend the Kerberos protocol and prove the identity of a principal.

This document describes a more formal model for this facility. The model describes what state in the Kerberos request a pre-authentication mechanism is likely to change. It also describes how multiple pre-authentication mechanisms used in the same request will interact.

This document also provides common tools needed by multiple pre-authentication mechanisms. One of these tools is a secure channel between the client and the key distribution center with a reply key strengthening mechanism; this secure channel can be used to protect the authentication exchange and thus eliminate offline dictionary attacks. With these tools, it is relatively straightforward to chain multiple authentication mechanisms, utilize a different key management system, or support a new key agreement algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. ZhuddatejÕNhfilenamegrfc6113eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-127974hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateMØΩ!granking·crfcd6113irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7A Generalized Framework for Kerberos Pre-AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document defines extensions to the Kerberos protocol to allow a Kerberos client to securely communicate with a Kerberos application service without revealing its identity, or without revealing more than its Kerberos realm.  It also defines extensions that allow a Kerberos client to obtain anonymous credentials without revealing its identity to the Kerberos Key Distribution Center (KDC).  This document updates RFCs 4120, 4121, and 4556. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehP. Leach¢kaffiliationqPainless SecuritydnamejS. Hartmanddatej‘¡hfilenamegrfc6112eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-127973hkeywordsÅnkerberos realmkobsoletedByÅd8062epagesopublicationDateMØΩgranking‡crfcd6112irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAnonymity Support for KerberosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxåThis document defines new naming constraints for well-known Kerberos principal names and well-known Kerberos realm names. [STANDARDS- TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhuddatej‘¬hfilenamegrfc6111eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-127972hkeywordsÇoprincipal nameskrealm nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMØºˇgrankingﬂcrfcd6111irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Additional Kerberos Naming ConstraintsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document specifies the mapping rules for translating YANG data models into Document Schema Definition Languages (DSDL), a coordinated set of XML schema languages standardized as ISO/IEC 19757.  The following DSDL schema languages are addressed by the mapping: Regular Language for XML Next Generation (RELAX NG), Schematron, and Schematron and Document Schema Renaming Language (DSRL).  The mapping takes one or more YANG modules and produces a set of DSDL schemas for a selected target document type -- datastore content, Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) messages, etc.  Procedures for schema-based validation of such documents are also discussed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfCESNETdnameiL. Lhotkaddatej‘¿hfilenamegrfc6110eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127971hkeywordsÖdDSDLjvalidationhRELAX NGjSchematrondDSRLkobsoletedByÄepagesdopublicationDateMUŒygrankingﬁcrfcd6110irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSMapping YANG to Document Schema Definition Languages and Validating NETCONF ContentdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7952∂habstracty|Since 1997, the Italian laws have recognized electronic delivery systems as legally usable. In 2005, after two years of technical tests, the characteristics of an official electronic delivery service, named certified electronic mail (in Italian "Posta Elettronica Certificata") were defined, giving the system legal standing.

The design of the entire system was carried out by the National Center for Informatics in the Public Administration of Italy (DigitPA), followed by efforts for the implementation and testing of the service. The DigitPA has given the Italian National Research Council (CNR), and in particular the Institute of Information Science and Technologies at the CNR (ISTI), the task of running tests on providers of the service to guarantee the correct implementation and interoperability. This document describes the certified email system adopted in Italy. It represents the system as it is at the moment of writing, following the technical regulations that were written based upon the Italian Law DPR. November 2, 2005. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgDigitPAdnamekC. Petrucci¢kaffiliationhISTI-CNRdnameiF. Gennai¢kaffiliationhISTI-CNRdnameiA. Shahin¢kaffiliation`dnamenA. Vinciarelliddatej‘ºhfilenamegrfc6109eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127970hkeywordsácPECoRegistered mailnReturn receiptvDigitally signed emailxDigitally signed notificationdMIMEeSMIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateMöaÏgranking›crfcd6109irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDLa Posta Elettronica Certificata - Italian Certified Electronic MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty The objective of this document is to describe a method of providing critical end-user notifications to web browsers, which has been deployed by Comcast, an Internet Service Provider (ISP).  Such a notification system is being used to provide near-immediate notifications to customers, such as to warn them that their traffic exhibits patterns that are indicative of malware or virus infection.  There are other proprietary systems that can perform such notifications, but those systems utilize Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) technology.  In contrast to DPI, this document describes a system that does not rely upon DPI, and is instead based in open IETF standards and open source applications.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-AndregauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamehC. Chung¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamekA. Kasyanov¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamegN. Mody¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekB. Van Lieuddatej‘ôhfilenamegrfc6108eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127969hkeywordsÑcISPxInternet Service Providerobot remediationpbot notificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMe≠§granking‹crfcd6108irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex(Comcast's Web Notification System DesigndtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymLabel Switched Paths (LSPs) set up in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) or Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks can be used to form links to carry traffic in those networks or in other (client) networks.

Protocol mechanisms already exist to facilitate the establishment of such LSPs and to bundle traffic engineering (TE) links to reduce the load on routing protocols. This document defines extensions to those mechanisms to support identifying the use to which such LSPs are to be put and to enable the TE link endpoints to be assigned addresses or unnumbered identifiers during the signaling process. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Shiomoto¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘¡hfilenamegrfc6107eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127968hkeywordsÑhTE linksmBundled linkseGMPLSx dynamically provisioned networkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMJ)Ngranking€crfcd6107irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEProcedures for Dynamically Signaled Hierarchical Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxºThis document specifies IPv6 Router Advertisement options to allow IPv6 routers to advertise a list of DNS recursive server addresses and a DNS Search List to IPv6 hosts. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlBrocade/ETRIdnamehJ. Jeong¢kaffiliationsSAMSUNG ElectronicsdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnamejL. Beloeil¢kaffiliationqiRam TechnologiesdnamenS. Madanapalliddatej‘∏hfilenamegrfc6106eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127967hkeywordsÖkDNS ServicejDNS OptionxRecursive DNS Server AddressoDNS Search ListxStateless AutoconfigurationkobsoletedByÅd8106epagesopublicationDateLÙôHgranking⁄crfcd6106irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7IPv6 Router Advertisement Options for DNS ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›Routed protocols are often susceptible to spoof attacks.  The canonical solution for IPv6 is Secure Neighbor Discovery (SEND), a solution that is non-trivial to deploy.  This document proposes a light-weight alternative and complement to SEND based on filtering in the layer-2 network fabric, using a variety of filtering criteria, including, for example, SEND status.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamepE. Levy-Abegnoli¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoG. Van de Velde¢kaffiliationjTechnodynednamelC. Popoviciu¢kaffiliationnNIIF/HungarnetdnamejJ. Mohacsiddatej‘¡hfilenamegrfc6105eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127966hkeywordsÇxSEcure Neighbor Discoveryx#Stateless Address AutoconfigurationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateMe≠ìgrankingŸcrfcd6105irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPv6 Router Advertisement GuarddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7113∂habstracty˛When deploying IPv6, whether IPv6-only or dual-stack, routers are configured to send IPv6 Router Advertisements (RAs) to convey information to nodes that enable them to autoconfigure on the network.  This information includes the implied default router address taken from the observed source address of the RA message, as well as on-link prefix information.  However, unintended misconfigurations by users or administrators, or possibly malicious attacks on the network, may lead to bogus RAs being present, which in turn can cause operational problems for hosts on the network.  In this document, we summarise the scenarios in which rogue RAs may be observed and present a list of possible solutions to the problem.  We focus on the unintended causes of rogue RAs in the text.  The goal of this text is to be Informational, and as such to present a framework around which solutions can be proposed and discussed.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Venaasddatej‘¡hfilenamegrfc6104eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127965hkeywordsÇbRAhrogue rakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMe≠Ügrankingÿcrfcd6104irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Rogue IPv6 Router Advertisement Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·This document is published as a historical record of the SSL 3.0 protocol. The original Abstract follows.

This document specifies version 3.0 of the Secure Sockets Layer (SSL 3.0) protocol, a security protocol that provides communications privacy over the Internet. The protocol allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, or message forgery. This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwNetscape communicationsdnameiA. Freier¢kaffiliationwNetscape communicationsdnamejP. Karlton¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnameiP. Kocherddatej‘Ëhfilenamegrfc6101eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127964hkeywordsÅxTransport layer securitykobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateNEΩÕgranking’crfcd6101irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3The Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) Protocol Version 3.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxŒThis document specifies protocol enhancements that allow Mobile IPv4 entities to send and receive explicit notification messages using a Mobile IPv4 message type designed for this purpose. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenBrian Habermandarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationfNetnoddnamelH. Levkowetz¢kaffiliationoVasona NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamehB. Haleyddatej‘¿hfilenamegrfc6098eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127963hkeywordsÅemipv4kobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateOâ%ºgranking“crfcd6098irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Generic Notification Message for Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLarge Proxy Mobile IPv6 deployments would benefit from a functionality where a Mobile Access Gateway could dynamically discover a Local Mobility Anchor for a Mobile Node attaching to a Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain.  The purpose of the dynamic discovery functionality is to reduce the amount of static configuration in the Mobile Access Gateway.  This document describes several possible dynamic Local Mobility Anchor discovery solutions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationoVasona NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalliddatej‘¿hfilenamegrfc6097eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127962hkeywordsÑfPMIPv6d3GPPcDNScAAAkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateMUŒmgranking—crfcd6097irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Local Mobility Anchor (LMA) Discovery for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document defines the procedure for registering chunk flags with the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) for the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  It updates RFC 4960 and also defines the IANA registry for contents for currently defined chunk types.  It does not change SCTP in any other way. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejR. StewartddatejÕhhfilenamegrfc6096eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127961hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9260epagesopublicationDateM?|pgranking–crfcd6096irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDStream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Chunk Flags RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕYANG -- the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) Data Modeling Language -- supports modeling of a tree of data elements that represent the configuration and runtime status of a particular network element managed via NETCONF.  This memo suggests enhancing YANG with supplementary modeling features and language abstractions with the aim to improve the model extensibility and reuse.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxTCS/Nokia Siemens NetworksdnamekB. Linowski¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehM. Ersue¢kaffiliationt360 Treasury SystemsdnameiS. Kuryladdatej‘…hfilenamegrfc6095eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127960hkeywordsàdYANGemodellcomplex-typemComplex TypesnTyped InstancenResource ModelkInheritanceeclasskobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDateMëùâgrankingœcrfcd6095irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Extending YANG with Language AbstractionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}The routing protocols Open Shortest Path First version 2 (OSPFv2), Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS), and Routing Information Protocol (RIP) currently define cleartext and MD5 (Message Digest 5) methods for authenticating protocol packets. Recently, effort has been made to add support for the SHA (Secure Hash Algorithm) family of hash functions for the purpose of authenticating routing protocol packets for RIP, IS-IS, and OSPF.

To encourage interoperability between disparate implementations, it is imperative that we specify the expected minimal set of algorithms, thereby ensuring that there is at least one algorithm that all implementations will have in common.

Similarly, RIP for IPv6 (RIPng) and OSPFv3 support IPsec algorithms for authenticating their protocol packets.

This document examines the current set of available algorithms, with interoperability and effective cryptographic authentication protection being the principal considerations. Cryptographic authentication of these routing protocols requires the availability of the same algorithms in disparate implementations. It is desirable that newly specified algorithms should be implemented and available in routing protocol implementations because they may be promoted to requirements at some future time. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnameiV. Manralddatej‘¡hfilenamegrfc6094eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-127959hkeywordsÅlIGP securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMJ)5grankingŒcrfcd6094irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcSummary of Cryptographic Authentication Algorithm Implementation Requirements for Routing ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document analyzes how current TCP implementations process TCP urgent indications and how the behavior of some widely deployed middleboxes affects how end systems process urgent indications.  This document updates the relevant specifications such that they accommodate current practice in processing TCP urgent indications, raises awareness about the reliability of TCP urgent indications in the Internet, and recommends against the use of urgent indications (but provides advice to applications that do). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgUTN/FRHdnamegF. Gont¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenA. Yourtchenkoddatej‘øhfilenamegrfc6093eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127958hkeywordsÅxTransmission Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÅd9293epagesopublicationDateM#rgrankingÕcrfcd6093irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1On the Implementation of the TCP Urgent MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document identifies a set of recommendations for the makers of devices and describes how to provide for "simple security" capabilities at the perimeter of local-area IPv6 networks in Internet-enabled homes and small offices.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeApplednamekJ. Woodyattddatej‘øhfilenamegrfc6092eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127957hkeywordsÉccpehfirewallffilterkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateM>(ºgrankingÃcrfcd6092irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Recommended Simple Security Capabilities in Customer Premises Equipment (CPE) for Providing Residential IPv6 Internet ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúThis memo defines Transport Layer Security (TLS) extensions and associated semantics that allow clients and servers to negotiate the use of OpenPGP certificates for a TLS session, and specifies how to transport OpenPGP certificates via TLS.  It also defines the registry for non-X.509 certificate types.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcKULdnametN. Mavrogiannopoulos¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejD. Gillmorddatej‘æhfilenamegrfc6091eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127956hkeywordsÉxCertificate type negotiationvtls handshake protocolihandshakekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMJ)$grankingÀcrfcd6091irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDUsing OpenPGP Keys for Transport Layer Security (TLS) AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This note describes the fundamental algorithms of Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) as they were defined in some seminal references from 1994 and earlier.  These descriptions may be useful for implementing the fundamental algorithms without using any of the specialized methods that were developed in following years.  Only elliptic curves defined over fields of characteristic greater than three are in scope; these curves are those used in Suite B.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamegK. Igoe¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnameiM. Salterddatej‘∆hfilenamegrfc6090eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127955hkeywordsÅcECCkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateMJ)granking crfcd6090irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Fundamental Elliptic Curve Cryptography AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document introduces extensions to Mobile IPv6 that allow nodes to bind one or more flows to a care-of address.  These extensions allow multihomed nodes to instruct home agents and other Mobile IPv6 entities to direct inbound flows to specific addresses. [STANDARDS- TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationtElevate TechnologiesdnamejH. Soliman¢kaffiliationeIT/TBdnamelN. Montavont¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Giaretta¢kaffiliationtUniversity of BremendnamenK. Kuladinithiddatej‘ªhfilenamegrfc6089eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127954hkeywordsÖsFlow IdentificationlFlow SummaryqBinding ReferencepTraffic SelectorrFlow Binding EntrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMB˛granking…crfcd6089irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFFlow Bindings in Mobile IPv6 and Network Mobility (NEMO) Basic SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxòThis document defines binary formats for IPv4 and IPv6 traffic selectors to be used in conjunction with flow bindings for Mobile IPv6. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Giarreta¢kaffiliationtElevate TechnologiesdnamejH. Soliman¢kaffiliationeIT/TBdnamelN. Montavontddatej‘ìhfilenamegrfc6088eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127953hkeywordsÇkMobile IPv6xBinary Traffic SelectorskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMB˛
granking»crfcd6088irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Traffic Selectors for Flow BindingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo provides guidelines for authors and reviewers of Standards Track specifications containing YANG data model modules.  Applicable portions may be used as a basis for reviews of other YANG data model documents.  Recommendations and procedures are defined, which are intended to increase interoperability and usability of Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) implementations that utilize YANG data model modules.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamejA. Biermanddatej‘ùhfilenamegrfc6087eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127952hkeywordsÑfNETMODgNETCONFcXMLdYANGkobsoletedByÅd8407epagesopublicationDateM>(±granking«crfcd6087irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAGuidelines for Authors and Reviewers of YANG Data Model DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis document defines a method, INFO, for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), and an Info Package mechanism.  This document obsoletes RFC 2976.  For backward compatibility, this document also specifies a "legacy" mode of usage of the INFO method that is compatible with the usage previously defined in RFC 2976, referred to as "legacy INFO Usage" in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekC. Holmberg¢kaffiliationuGeorgetown UniversitydnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationkAcme PacketdnameiH. Kaplanddatej‘¢hfilenamegrfc6086eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-127951hkeywordsÉlInfo PackagelInfo-PackageiRecv-InfokobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateM0µßgranking∆crfcd6086irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) INFO Method and Package FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTWhen transmitting an IPv6 packet with a multicast destination address, the IPv6 destination address is mapped to an Ethernet link-layer multicast address.  This document clarifies that a mapping of an IPv6 packet with a multicast destination address may in some circumstances map to an Ethernet link-layer unicast address. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekM. Townsley¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefW. DecddatejÕihfilenamegrfc6085eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127950hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMB˝˘granking≈crfcd6085irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Address Mapping of IPv6 Multicast Packets on EthernetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThe General Internet Signaling Transport (GIST) protocol currently uses TCP or Transport Layer Security (TLS) over TCP for Connection mode operation.  This document describes the usage of GIST over the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS).  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of GoettingendnameeX. Fu¢kaffiliationxUniversity of GoettingendnamekC. Dickmann¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamelJ. Crowcroftddatej‘´hfilenamegrfc6084eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127949hkeywordsÉkMultihomingiSignalingsPartial ReliabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM8vGgrankingƒcrfcd6084irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâGeneral Internet Signaling Transport (GIST) over Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) and Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThis document describes the usage of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol over the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).

DTLS over SCTP provides communications privacy for applications that use SCTP as their transport protocol and allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping and detect tampering or message forgery.

Applications using DTLS over SCTP can use almost all transport features provided by SCTP and its extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnamemR. Seggelmann¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. RescorladdatejÕehfilenamegrfc6083eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127948hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateM8v7granking√crfcd6083irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXDatagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) for Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyRRFC 2482, "Language Tagging in Unicode Plain Text", describes a mechanism for using special Unicode language tag characters to identify languages when needed without more general markup such as that provided by XML.  The Unicode Consortium has deprecated that facility and strongly recommends against its use.  RFC 2482 has been moved to Historic status to reduce the possibility that Internet implementers would consider that system an appropriate mechanism for identifying languages.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlSybase, Inc.dnamekK. Whistler¢kaffiliationsSkynav (Phil), Inc.dnamehG. Adams¢kaffiliationxAoyama Gakuin UniversitydnameiM. Duerst¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Presuhn¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘≥hfilenamegrfc6082eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127947hkeywordsÉjcharactersgstringseASCIIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL◊è¥granking¬crfcd6082irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADeprecating Unicode Language Tag Characters: RFC 2482 is HistoricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a set of extensions to the Teredo protocol.  These extensions provide additional capabilities to Teredo, including support for more types of Network Address Translations (NATs) and support for more efficient communication. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej‘πhfilenamegrfc6081eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127946hkeywordsÜdIPv6cNATitraversaljtransitionktranslationjtranslatorkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateM0µígranking¡crfcd6081irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqTeredo ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document specifies a framework to enable configuration of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) user agents (UAs) in SIP deployments.  The framework provides a means to deliver profile data that user agents need to be functional, automatically and with minimal or no User and Administrative intervention.  The framework describes how SIP user agents can discover sources, request profiles, and receive notifications related to profile modifications.  As part of this framework, a new SIP event package is defined for notification of profile changes.  The framework provides minimal data retrieval options to ensure interoperability.  The framework does not include specification of the profile data within its scope. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiSIPez LLCdnameiD. Petrie¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamepS. Channabasappaddatej‘Ωhfilenamegrfc6080eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127945hkeywordsÖcSIPmConfigurationiFrameworkjUser AgentgprofilekobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateMoÿ#granking¿crfcd6080irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGA Framework for Session Initiation Protocol User Agent Profile DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document specifies a framework to build HIP-based (Host Identity Protocol) overlay networks.  This framework uses HIP to perform connection management.  Other functions, such as data storage and retrieval or overlay maintenance, are implemented using protocols other than HIP.  These protocols are loosely referred to as "peer protocols".  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemJ. Hautakorpi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranen¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. JohnstonddatejÕUhfilenamegrfc6079eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127944hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM>(£grankingøcrfcd6079irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRHIP BONE: Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Based Overlay Networking Environment (BONE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new Host Identity Protocol (HIP) packet type called DATA.  HIP DATA packets are used to reliably convey authenticated arbitrary protocol messages over various overlay networks.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Melenddatej‘∑hfilenamegrfc6078eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127943hkeywordsÅhHIP DATAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM>(ïgrankingæcrfcd6078irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjHost Identity Protocol (HIP) Immediate Carriage and Conveyance of Upper-Layer Protocol Signaling (HICCUPS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty=This document describes some of the open problems in Internet congestion control that are known today.  This includes several new challenges that are becoming important as the network grows, as well as some issues that have been known for many years.  These challenges are generally considered to be open research topics that may require more study or application of innovative techniques before Internet-scale solutions can be confidently engineered and deployed.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars EggertgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationwUniversity of StuttgartdnameiM. Scharf¢kaffiliationhBT & UCLdnamejB. Briscoeddatej‘ühfilenamegrfc6077eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiccrgdfullx#iccrg - Internet Congestion ControldnamexInternet Congestion Controldtypebrgbidjdoc-127942hkeywordsájSignallingkPerformancejRobustnesshFairnessiStabilitylMisbehaviourlArchitecturekobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateMJ(˚grankingΩcrfcd6077irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex3Open Research Issues in Internet Congestion ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document defines a set of metrics and their usage to evaluate the performance of end-to-end Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for telephony services in both production and testing environments.  The purpose of this document is to combine a standard set of common metrics, allowing interoperable performance measurements, easing the comparison of industry implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamehD. Malas¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej‘µhfilenamegrfc6076eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpmoldfullx+pmol - Performance Metrics for Other Layersdnamex$Performance Metrics for Other Layersdtypebwgbidjdoc-127941hkeywordsàlBenchmarkingcLabdTestdTimekMeasurementgServicegSessionhProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM8v'grankingºcrfcd6076irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Basic Telephony SIP End-to-End Performance MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty!The original Application Configuration Access Protocol (ACAP) specification included a vendor registry now used in other protocols.  This document updates the description of this registry, removing the need for a direct normative reference to ACAP and removing ambiguity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekD. Cridlandddatej‘øhfilenamegrfc6075eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127940hkeywordsÇhannotatehmetadatakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÉgrankingªcrfcd6075irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwThe Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) Application Configuration Access Protocol (ACAP) Vendor Subtrees RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄Provider Provisioned Layer 2 Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs) may have different "provisioning models", i.e., models for what information needs to be configured in what entities.  Once configured, the provisioning information is distributed by a "discovery process".  When the discovery process is complete, a signaling protocol is automatically invoked to set up the mesh of pseudowires (PWs) that form the (virtual) backbone of the L2VPN.  This document specifies a number of L2VPN provisioning models, and further specifies the semantic structure of the endpoint identifiers required by each model.  It discusses the distribution of these identifiers by the discovery process, especially when discovery is based on the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP).  It then specifies how the endpoint identifiers are carried in the two signaling protocols that are used to set up PWs, the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP), and the Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol version 3 (L2TPv3). [STANDARDS- TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamejV. Radoaca¢kaffiliation`dnamefW. LuoddatejÀ˝hfilenamegrfc6074eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127939hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateM5¯¯granking∫crfcd6074irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXProvisioning, Auto-Discovery, and Signaling in Layer 2 Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document describes how to connect pseudowires (PWs) between different Packet Switched Network (PSN) domains or between two or more distinct PW control plane domains, where a control plane domain uses a common control plane protocol or instance of that protocol for a given PW.  The different PW control plane domains may belong to independent autonomous systems, or the PSN technology is heterogeneous, or a PW might need to be aggregated at a specific PSN point.  The PW packet data units are simply switched from one PW to another without changing the PW payload. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamenStewart Bryantdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamegC. Metz¢kaffiliationkLucidVisiondnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekM. Aissaouiddatej‘Ωhfilenamegrfc6073eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127938hkeywordsÑcpwscpsnwpacket switched networkwpw control plane domainkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateM5¯Ïgrankingπcrfcd6073irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletSegmented PseudowiredtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6723d7267∂habstractyThis document defines a credential service that allows Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) User Agents (UAs) to use a SIP event package to discover the certificates of other users.  This mechanism allows User Agents that want to contact a given Address-of-Record (AOR) to retrieve that AOR's certificate by subscribing to the credential service, which returns an authenticated response containing that certificate.  The credential service also allows users to store and retrieve their own certificates and private keys. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationeSkypednameiJ. Fischlddatej‘¢hfilenamegrfc6072eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127937hkeywordsÉrcredential servicecaorqaddress of recordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMQúÉgranking∏crfcd6072irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHCertificate Management Service for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOver the past few years, the number of RFCs that define and use IPsec and Internet Key Exchange (IKE) has greatly proliferated. This is complicated by the fact that these RFCs originate from numerous IETF working groups: the original IPsec WG, its various spin-offs, and other WGs that use IPsec and/or IKE to protect their protocols' traffic.

This document is a snapshot of IPsec- and IKE-related RFCs. It includes a brief description of each RFC, along with background information explaining the motivation and context of IPsec's outgrowths and extensions. It obsoletes RFC 2411, the previous "IP Security Document Roadmap."

The obsoleted IPsec roadmap (RFC 2411) briefly described the interrelationship of the various classes of base IPsec documents. The major focus of RFC 2411 was to specify the recommended contents of documents specifying additional encryption and authentication algorithms. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamejS. Frankel¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnanddatej‘±hfilenamegrfc6071eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127936hkeywordsÉqinternet protocolgprivacynauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDateMQúwgranking∑crfcd6071irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDIP Security (IPsec) and Internet Key Exchange (IKE) Document RoadmapdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThis document contains test vectors for the Public-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) #5 Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2 (PBKDF2) with the Hash-based Message Authentication Code (HMAC) Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA-1) pseudorandom function.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. JosefssonddatejÕbhfilenamegrfc6070eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127935hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM&	granking∂crfcd6070irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGPKCS #5: Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2 (PBKDF2) Test VectorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±Disruptions in end-to-end path connectivity, which last longer than one retransmission timeout, cause suboptimal TCP performance. The reason for this performance degradation is that TCP interprets segment loss induced by long connectivity disruptions as a sign of congestion, resulting in repeated retransmission timer backoffs. This, in turn, leads to a delayed detection of the re-establishment of the connection since TCP waits for the next retransmission timeout before it attempts a retransmission.

This document proposes an algorithm to make TCP more robust to long connectivity disruptions (TCP-LCD). It describes how standard ICMP messages can be exploited during timeout-based loss recovery to disambiguate true congestion loss from non-congestion loss caused by connectivity disruptions. Moreover, a reversion strategy of the retransmission timer is specified that enables a more prompt detection of whether or not the connectivity to a previously disconnected peer node has been restored. TCP-LCD is a TCP sender- only modification that effectively improves TCP performance in the case of connectivity disruptions. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvRWTH Aachen UniversitydnamemA. Zimmermann¢kaffiliationvRWTH Aachen UniversitydnamelA. Hannemannddatej‘ºhfilenamegrfc6069eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127934hkeywordsÇx(Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP)xRetranmission Timeout (RTO)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÇÒgrankingµcrfcd6069irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAMaking TCP More Robust to Long Connectivity Disruptions (TCP-LCD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=This document defines the format of Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) to identify resources that are reached using Internet mail.  It adds better internationalization and compatibility with Internationalized Resource Identifiers (IRIs; RFC 3987) to the previous syntax of 'mailto' URIs (RFC 2368). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxAoyama Gakuin UniversitydnameiM. Duerst¢kaffiliationxAdobe Systems IncorporateddnamekL. Masinter¢kaffiliationjDNA LoungednamekJ. Zawinskiddatej‘¨hfilenamegrfc6068eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127933hkeywordsÑfmailtomemail addressjURI schemecIRIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL™q€granking¥crfcd6068irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewThe 'mailto' URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document specifies an Extension to BCP 47 that provides subtags that specify language and/or locale-based behavior or refinements to language tags, according to work done by the Unicode Consortium.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehM. Davis¢kaffiliationfLab126dnamekA. Phillips¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiY. Umaokaddatej‘∂hfilenamegrfc6067eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127932hkeywordsÇflocalefbcp 47kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL˛‘Égranking≥crfcd6067irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlerBCP 47 Extension UdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document provides specifications for existing TLS extensions.  It is a companion document for RFC 5246, "The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.2".  The extensions specified are server_name, max_fragment_length, client_certificate_url, trusted_ca_keys, truncated_hmac, and status_request. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej‘µhfilenamegrfc6066eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127931hkeywordsÜkserver_namesmax_fragment_lengthvclient_certificate_urlotrusted_ca_keysntruncated_hmacnstatus_requestkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM5¯›granking≤crfcd6066irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Transport Layer Security (TLS) Extensions: Extension DefinitionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8449d9325d8446∂habstractyçThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols.  It describes the use of information provided by Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) services, such as the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS), to dynamically update user-to-group mappings in the View-based Access Control Model (VACM). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejK. Narayan¢kaffiliationuElbrys Networks, Inc.dnameiD. Nelson¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Presuhnddatej‘ºhfilenamegrfc6065eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdismsdfullx)isms - Integrated Security Model for SNMPdnamex"Integrated Security Model for SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127930hkeywordsàrNetwork ManagementhSecurityxManagement Information BasecMIBeSMIv2fRADIUScAAAdVACMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÉUgranking±crfcd6065irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexåUsing Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting Services to Dynamically Provision View-Based Access Control Model User-to-Group MappingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑The Packet-switched Streaming Service (PSS) and the Multimedia Broadcast/Multicast Service (MBMS) defined by 3GPP use the Session Description Protocol (SDP) and Real Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP) with some extensions.  This document provides information about these extensions and registers the RTSP and SDP extensions with IANA.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiP. Frojdhddatej‘Ωhfilenamegrfc6064eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127929hkeywordsÜd3GPPcPSSdMBMScSDPdRTSPdIANAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM5¯Àgranking∞crfcd6064irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuSDP and RTSP Extensions Defined for 3GPP Packet-Switched Streaming Service and Multimedia Broadcast/Multicast ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThe Dynamic Symmetric Key Provisioning Protocol (DSKPP) is a client-server protocol for initialization (and configuration) of symmetric keys to locally and remotely accessible cryptographic modules. The protocol can be run with or without private key capabilities in the cryptographic modules and with or without an established public key infrastructure.

Two variations of the protocol support multiple usage scenarios. With the four-pass variant, keys are mutually generated by the provisioning server and cryptographic module; provisioned keys are not transferred over-the-wire or over-the-air. The two-pass variant enables secure and efficient download and installation of pre-generated symmetric keys to a cryptographic module. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx"RSA / The Security Division of EMCdnamejA. Doherty¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamefM. Pei¢kaffiliationpDiversinet Corp.dnamejS. Machani¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamejM. Nystromddatej‘ªhfilenamegrfc6063eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgkeyprovdfullx(keyprov - Provisioning of Symmetric KeysdnamexProvisioning of Symmetric Keysdtypebwgbidjdoc-127928hkeywordsÖtCryptographic modulesCryptographic Tokenrkey initializationkcredentialssonline provisioningkobsoletedByÄepagesiopublicationDateMÇﬂgrankingØcrfcd6063irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Dynamic Symmetric Key Provisioning Protocol (DSKPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This specification defines an extension of Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN), a relay protocol for Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal.  This extension allows a TURN client to request TCP allocations, and defines new requests and indications for the TURN server to open and accept TCP connections with the client\'s peers.  TURN and this extension both purposefully restrict the ways in which the relayed address can be used.  In particular, it prevents users from running general-purpose servers from ports obtained from the TURN server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamelS. Perreault¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘ªhfilenamegrfc6062eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127927hkeywordsÉcNATdTURNdSTUNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÙôágrankingÆcrfcd6062irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGTraversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) Extensions for TCP AllocationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) "nena" for Uniform Resource Name (URN) resources published by the National Emergency Number Association (NENA).  NENA defines and manages resources that utilize this URN model.  Management activities for these and other resource types are provided by the NENA Registry System (NRS).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehB. RosenddatejÕFhfilenamegrfc6061eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127926hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM&¯granking≠crfcd6061irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZUniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the National Emergency Number Association (NENA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊This specification is complementary to the GMPLS Ethernet Label Switching Architecture and Framework and describes the technology-specific aspects of GMPLS control for Provider Backbone Bridge Traffic Engineering (PBB-TE).  The necessary GMPLS extensions and mechanisms are described to establish Ethernet PBB-TE point-to-point (P2P) and point-to-multipoint (P2MP) connections.  This document supports, but does not modify, the standard IEEE data plane. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationeCienadnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Takacsddatej‘ùhfilenamegrfc6060eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127925hkeywordsÅoIEEE data planekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMuï´granking¨crfcd6060irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextGeneralized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Control of Ethernet Provider Backbone Traffic Engineering (PBB-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0Detecting Network Attachment allows hosts to assess if its existing addressing or routing configuration is valid for a newly connected network.  This document provides simple procedures for Detecting Network Attachment in IPv6 hosts, and procedures for routers to support such services. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationqNetstar LogicalisdnamehG. Daleyddatej‘ªhfilenamegrfc6059eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdnadfullx"dna - Detecting Network AttachmentdnamexDetecting Network Attachmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-127924hkeywordsécDNAeDNAv6bNDwIPv6 neighbor discoveryrneighbor discoverydsendxsecure neighbor discoveryfDHCPv6xstateless autoconfigurationpchange detectionrmovement detectioneDNAv4nlink detectionhmobilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLı@ıgranking´crfcd6059irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Simple Procedures for Detecting Network Attachment in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis document specifies a mechanism that enhances Proxy Mobile IPv6 protocol signaling to support the creation of a transient binding cache entry that is used to optimize the performance of dual radio handover, as well as single radio handover.  This mechanism is applicable to the mobile node's inter-MAG (Mobility Access Gateway) handover while using a single interface or different interfaces.  The handover problem space using the Proxy Mobile IPv6 base protocol is analyzed and the use of transient binding cache entries at the local mobility anchor is described.  The specified extension to the Proxy Mobile IPv6 protocol ensures optimized forwarding of downlink as well as uplink packets between mobile nodes and the network infrastructure and avoids superfluous packet forwarding delay or even packet loss.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejM. Liebsch¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Muhanna¢kaffiliationxAlcatel-Lucent Bell LabsdnamehO. Blumeddatej‘∫hfilenamegrfc6058eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127923hkeywordsädPMIPuhandover optimizationnhandover delaydtBCEplate path switchjforwardingqmake-before-breaksdual radio handoverusingle radio handoverxtransient binding cache entrykobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateMoÿgranking™crfcd6058irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Transient Binding for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document describes the congestion management system of Comcast Cable, a large cable broadband Internet Service Provider (ISP) in the U.S.  Comcast completed deployment of this congestion management system on December 31, 2008.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamejC. Bastian¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamejT. Klieber¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamehJ. Mills¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiR. Woundyddatej‘∂hfilenamegrfc6057eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127922hkeywordsÉcISPxInternet Service ProviderrNetwork ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL˛‘mgranking©crfcd6057irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Comcast's Protocol-Agnostic Congestion Management SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÉDuring the last few years, awareness has been raised about a number of "blind" attacks that can be performed against the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) and similar protocols.  The consequences of these attacks range from throughput reduction to broken connections or data corruption.  These attacks rely on the attacker's ability to guess or know the five-tuple (Protocol, Source Address, Destination Address, Source Port, Destination Port) that identifies the transport protocol instance to be attacked.  This document describes a number of simple and efficient methods for the selection of the client port number, such that the possibility of an attacker guessing the exact value is reduced.  While this is not a replacement for cryptographic methods for protecting the transport-protocol instance, the aforementioned port selection algorithms provide improved security with very little effort and without any key management overhead.  The algorithms described in this document are local policies that may be incrementally deployed and that do not violate the specifications of any of the transport protocols that may benefit from them, such as TCP, UDP, UDP-lite, Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP), Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP), and RTP (provided that the RTP application explicitly signals the RTP and RTCP port numbers).  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeTietodnameiM. Larsen¢kaffiliationgUTN/FRHdnamegF. Gontddatej‘¥hfilenamegrfc6056eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127921hkeywordsÉctcpxtransmission control protoclmblind attackskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM5¯ægranking®crfcd6056irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberúetotaletitlex9Recommendations for Transport-Protocol Port RandomizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractywThis document explores issues with Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs) that result from the use of various encoding schemes such as UTF-8 and the ASCII-Compatible Encoding produced by the Punycode algorithm.  It focuses on the importance of agreeing on a single encoding and how complicated the state of affairs ends up being as a result of using different encodings today.fadNamejRon BonicagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationeApplednamekS. Cheshireddatej‘∞hfilenamegrfc6055eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127920hkeywordsÇgUnicodeeUTF-8kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMc9grankingßcrfcd6055irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex<IAB Thoughts on Encodings for Internationalized Domain NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCounter modes have been defined for block ciphers such as the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES).  Counter modes use a counter, which is typically assumed to be incremented by a single sender.  This memo describes the use of counter modes when applied to the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH) in multiple-sender group applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. WeisddatejÕfhfilenamegrfc6054eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127919hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateLÍµÊgranking¶crfcd6054irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuUsing Counter Modes with Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH) to Protect Group TrafficdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) defines an architectural framework and associated protocols to standardize information exchange between the control plane and the forwarding plane in a ForCES network element (ForCES NE). RFC 3654 has defined the ForCES requirements, and RFC 3746 has defined the ForCES framework.

This document is an implementation report for the ForCES Protocol, Model, and the Stream Control Transmission Protocol-based Transport Mapping Layer (SCTP TML) documents, and includes a report on interoperability testing and the current state of ForCES implementations. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of PatrasdnamemE. Haleplidis¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamehK. Ogawa¢kaffiliationxZhejiang Gongshang UniversitydnamegW. Wang¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salimddatej‘±hfilenamegrfc6053eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127918hkeywordsÉxBStream Control Transmission Protocol-based Transport Mapping LayerhSCTP TMLlforces ModelkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateL◊èîgranking•crfcd6053irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLImplementation Report for Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6984∂habstractyËThis document discusses the algorithmic translation of an IPv6 address to a corresponding IPv4 address, and vice versa, using only statically configured information.  It defines a well-known prefix for use in algorithmic translations, while allowing organizations to also use network-specific prefixes when appropriate.  Algorithmic translation is used in IPv4/IPv6 translators, as well as other types of proxies and gateways (e.g., for DNS) used in IPv4/IPv6 scenarios. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnamefC. Bao¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationx!CERNET Center/Tsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Liddatej‘µhfilenamegrfc6052eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127917hkeywordsâgaddressfprefixjtransitionktranslationcNATeNAT64fBEHAVEistatelesshstatefulkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL \Úgranking§crfcd6052irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(IPv6 Addressing of IPv4/IPv6 TranslatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This memo outlines how RTP sessions are synchronised, and discusses how rapidly such synchronisation can occur. We show that most RTP sessions can be synchronised immediately, but that the use of video switching multipoint conference units (MCUs) or large source-specific multicast (SSM) groups can greatly increase the synchronisation delay. This increase in delay can be unacceptable to some applications that use layered and/or multi-description codecs.

This memo introduces three mechanisms to reduce the synchronisation delay for such sessions. First, it updates the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) timing rules to reduce the initial synchronisation delay for SSM sessions. Second, a new feedback packet is defined for use with the extended RTP profile for RTCP-based feedback (RTP/AVPF), allowing video switching MCUs to rapidly request resynchronisation. Finally, new RTP header extensions are defined to allow rapid synchronisation of late joiners, and guarantee correct timestamp-based decoding order recovery for layered codecs in the presence of clock skew. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer HHIdnamejT. Schierlddatej‘≠hfilenamegrfc6051eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127916hkeywordsÜdrtcptrtp control protocolcmcuxmultipoint conference unitscssmxsource-specific multicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL◊è}granking£crfcd6051irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Rapid Synchronisation of RTP FlowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document describes private extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) that enable a network of trusted SIP servers to assert the service of authenticated users. The use of these extensions is only applicable inside an administrative domain with previously agreed-upon policies for generation, transport, and usage of such information. This document does NOT offer a general service identification model suitable for use between different trust domains or for use in the Internet at large.

The document also defines a URN to identify both services and User Agent (UA) applications. This URN can be used within the SIP header fields defined in this document to identify services, and also within the framework defined for caller preferences and callee capabilities to identify usage of both services and applications between end UAs. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehK. Drageddatej‘ªhfilenamegrfc6050eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127915hkeywordsÉcSIPltrust domainrservice identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÍµ◊granking¢crfcd6050irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extension for the Identification of ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo utilizes IP performance metrics that are applicable to both complete paths and sub-paths, and it defines relationships to compose a complete path metric from the sub-path metrics with some accuracy with regard to the actual metrics.  This is called "spatial composition" in RFC 2330.  The memo refers to the framework for metric composition, and provides background and motivation for combining metrics to derive others.  The descriptions of several composed metrics and statistics follow. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednamejE. Stephanddatej‘∏hfilenamegrfc6049eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127914hkeywordsÉkPerformancekMeasurementdIPPMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM#r⁄granking°crfcd6049irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSpatial Composition of MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6248∂habstractyThis document defines a set of enhancements to the Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) that allow a client to request extended information from NNTP servers regarding server status, policy, and other aspects of local configuration. These enhancements are made as new keywords to the existing LIST capability described in RFC 3977.

This memo updates and formalizes the LIST DISTRIBUTIONS and LIST SUBSCRIPTIONS commands defined in RFC 2980. It also adds the LIST COUNTS, LIST MODERATORS, and LIST MOTD commands, and specifies additional values returned by the existing LIST ACTIVE command for the status of a newsgroup. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Elieddatej‘≠hfilenamegrfc6048eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127913hkeywordsÉfUsenetgNetNewslcapabilitieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÍµ…granking†crfcd6048irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) Additions to LIST CommanddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document, "iCalendar Message-Based Interoperability Protocol (iMIP)", specifies a binding from the iCalendar Transport-independent Interoperability Protocol (iTIP) to Internet email-based transports.  Calendaring entries defined by the iCalendar Object Model (iCalendar) are wrapped using constructs from RFC 5322 and MIME (RFC 2045, RFC 2046, RFC 2047, and RFC 2049), and then transported over SMTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘ºhfilenamegrfc6047eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgcalsifydfullx=calsify - Calendaring and Scheduling Standards Simplificationdnamex3Calendaring and Scheduling Standards Simplificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127912hkeywordsÜdIMIPoelectronic mailitransportditipx9iCalendar Transport-independent Interoperability ProtocolviCalendar Object ModelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÉ@grankingücrfcd6047irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8iCalendar Message-Based Interoperability Protocol (iMIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) defines a common XML format for document exchange, and Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID) defines extensions to IODEF intended for the cooperative handling of security incidents within consortia of network operators and enterprises.  This document specifies a transport protocol for RID based upon the passing of RID messages over HTTP/TLS (Transport Layer Security).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnamekB. Trammellddatej‘∞hfilenamegrfc6046eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127911hkeywordsÜxCoordinate Incident ResponseeCSIRTdCIRTeIODEFxIncident Object ExchangerDescription FormatkobsoletedByÅd6546epagesopublicationDateL◊èggrankingûcrfcd6046irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Transport of Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID) MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNetwork security incidents, such as system compromises, worms, viruses, phishing incidents, and denial of service, typically result in the loss of service, data, and resources both human and system. Network providers and Computer Security Incident Response Teams need to be equipped and ready to assist in communicating and tracing security incidents with tools and procedures in place before the occurrence of an attack. Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID) outlines a proactive inter-network communication method to facilitate sharing incident handling data while integrating existing detection, tracing, source identification, and mitigation mechanisms for a complete incident handling solution. Combining these capabilities in a communication system provides a way to achieve higher security levels on networks. Policy guidelines for handling incidents are recommended and can be agreed upon by a consortium using the security recommendations and considerations.

RID has found use within the international research communities, but has not been widely adopted in other sectors. This publication provides the specification to those communities that have adopted it, and communities currently considering solutions for real-time inter-network defense. The specification may also accelerate development of solutions where different transports or message formats are required by leveraging the data elements and structures specified here. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamekK. Moriartyddatej‘¥hfilenamegrfc6045eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127910hkeywordsÜxCoordinated Incident ResponseeCSIRTdCIRTeIODEFxIncident Object ExchangerDescription FormatkobsoletedByÅd6545epagesKopublicationDateL◊è`grankingùcrfcd6045irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Real-time Inter-network Defense (RID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyAlthough the SIP History-Info header is the solution adopted in IETF, the non-standard Diversion header is nevertheless widely implemented and used for conveying call-diversion-related information in SIP signaling.

This document describes a recommended interworking guideline between the Diversion header and the History-Info header to handle call diversion information. In addition, an interworking policy is proposed to manage the headers' coexistence. The History-Info header is described in RFC 4244 and the non-standard Diversion header is described, as Historic, in RFC 5806.

Since the Diversion header is used in many existing network implementations for the transport of call diversion information, its interworking with the SIP History-Info standardized solution is needed. This work is intended to enable the migration from non- standard implementations and deployment toward IETF specification- based implementations and deployment. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert SparksgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednameiM. MohaliddatejÃ≈hfilenamegrfc6044eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127909hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7544epagesopublicationDateLÀ}ıgrankingúcrfcd6044irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexÖMapping and Interworking of Diversion Information between Diversion and History-Info Headers in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) specification describes a key management scheme for real-time applications.  In this document, we note that the currently defined MIKEY modes are insufficient to address deployment scenarios built around a centralized key management service.  Interest in such deployments is increasing.  Therefore, a set of new MIKEY modes that work well in such scenarios are defined.  The new modes use a trusted key management service and a ticket concept, similar to that in Kerberos.  The new modes also support features used by many existing applications, where the exact identity of the other endpoint may not be known at the start of the communication session.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekJ. Mattsson¢kaffiliationcZTEdnamegT. Tianddatej‘¥hfilenamegrfc6043eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127908hkeywordsáeMIKEYlMIKEY-TICKETcKMSdSRTPcIMSnkey managementfticketkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateMà2agrankingõcrfcd6043irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZMIKEY-TICKET: Ticket-Based Modes of Key Distribution in Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6309µhabstracty9This document specifies the use of the KeyNote trust-management system as an authorization extension in the Transport Layer Security (TLS) Handshake Protocol, according to guidelines in RFC 5878.  Extensions carried in the client and server hello messages confirm that both parties support the desired authorization data types.  Then, if supported by both the client and the server, KeyNote credentials are exchanged in the supplemental data handshake message.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim PolkgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamelA. Keromytisddatej‘¢hfilenamegrfc6042eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127907hkeywordsÑptrust managementmauthorizationnaccess controllcertificateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL \ﬂgrankingöcrfcd6042irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex:Transport Layer Security (TLS) Authorization Using KeyNotedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThe Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) protocol defines a standard framework and mechanism for the interconnection between control elements and forwarding elements in IP routers and similar devices.  In this document we describe the applicability of the ForCES model and protocol.  We provide example deployment scenarios and functionality, as well as document applications that would be inappropriate for ForCES.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiA. Crouch¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekH. Khosravi¢kaffiliationcLTUdnamehA. Doria¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegX. Wang¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamehK. Ogawaddatej‘≤hfilenamegrfc6041eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127906hkeywordsÑgRoutingmControl PlanejManagementhProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÀ}Îgrankingôcrfcd6041irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Applicability StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document redefines how the explicit congestion notification (ECN) field of the IP header should be constructed on entry to and exit from any IP-in-IP tunnel.  On encapsulation, it updates RFC 3168 to bring all IP-in-IP tunnels (v4 or v6) into line with RFC 4301 IPsec ECN processing.  On decapsulation, it updates both RFC 3168 and RFC 4301 to add new behaviours for previously unused combinations of inner and outer headers.  The new rules ensure the ECN field is correctly propagated across a tunnel whether it is used to signal one or two severity levels of congestion; whereas before, only one severity level was supported.  Tunnel endpoints can be updated in any order without affecting pre-existing uses of the ECN field, thus ensuring backward compatibility.  Nonetheless, operators wanting to support two severity levels (e.g., for pre-congestion notification -- PCN) can require compliance with this new specification.  A thorough analysis of the reasoning for these changes and the implications is included.  In the unlikely event that the new rules do not meet a specific need, RFC 4774 gives guidance on designing alternate ECN semantics, and this document extends that to include tunnelling issues. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoeddatej‘∂hfilenamegrfc6040eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127905hkeywordsâx!Congestion Control and ManagementwCongestion NotificationtInformation SecurityjTunnellingmEncapsulationmDecapsulationhProtocolcECNeIPseckobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateLÙôYgrankingòcrfcd6040irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Tunnelling of Explicit Congestion NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9601∂habstracty€Routing protocols have been extended over time to use cryptographic mechanisms to ensure that data received from a neighboring router has not been modified in transit and actually originated from an authorized neighboring router.

The cryptographic mechanisms defined to date and described in this document rely on a digest produced with a hash algorithm applied to the payload encapsulated in the routing protocol packet.

This document outlines some of the limitations of the current mechanism, problems with manual keying of these cryptographic algorithms, and possible vectors for the exploitation of these limitations. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationjNokia Inc.dnamejJ. Jaeggli¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. WhiteddatejÕ`hfilenamegrfc6039eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-127904hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¬<0grankingócrfcd6039irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKIssues with Existing Cryptographic Protection Methods for Routing ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis memo describes two closely related features for the core specification of the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP): an optional capability where the responding host returns some of the command octets or padding octets to the sender, and an optional sender packet format that ensures equal test packet sizes are used in both directions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamemL. Ciavattoneddatej‘¥hfilenamegrfc6038eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127903hkeywordsÉgTestingkPerformancefMetrickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≤V?grankingñcrfcd6038irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXTwo-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP) Reflect Octets and Symmetrical Size FeaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyuThis document describes the MVPN (Multicast in BGP/MPLS IP VPNs) solution designed and deployed by Cisco Systems.  The procedures specified in this document are largely a subset of the generalized MVPN framework recently standardized by the IETF.  However, as the deployment of the procedures specified herein predates the publication of IETF standards (in some cases by over five years), an implementation based on these procedures differs in some respects from a fully standards-compliant implementation.  These differences are pointed out in the document.  This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian FarrelgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefY. Cai¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnandsddatej‘öhfilenamegrfc6037eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127902hkeywordsÅdmvpnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¬<!grankingïcrfcd6037irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9Cisco Systems' Solution for Multicast in BGP/MPLS IP VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThis document describes practices and plans that are emerging among Internet Service Providers for the deployment of IPv6 services.  They are based on practical experience so far, as well as current plans and requirements, reported in a survey of a number of ISPs carried out in early 2010.  This document identifies a number of technology gaps, but it does not make recommendations.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehS. Jiangddatej‘≥hfilenamegrfc6036eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127901hkeywordsÅcispkobsoletedByÅd9386epagesopublicationDateL¬<grankingîcrfcd6036irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Emerging Service Provider Scenarios for IPv6 DeploymentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) event package that enables the collection and reporting of metrics that measure the quality for Voice over Internet Protocol (VoIP) sessions.  Voice call quality information derived from RTP Control Protocol Extended Reports (RTCP-XR) and call information from SIP is conveyed from a User Agent (UA) in a session, known as a reporter, to a third party, known as a collector.  A registration for the application/ vq-rtcpxr media type is also included. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuTelchemy IncorporateddnamelA. Pendleton¢kaffiliationuTelchemy IncorporateddnamehA. Clark¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamelH. Sinnreichddatej‘¥hfilenamegrfc6035eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127900hkeywordsÖcsipxVoice over Internet Protocoldvoipx%RTP Control Protocol Extended ReportsgRTCP-XRkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateLÙôwgrankingìcrfcd6035irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexESession Initiation Protocol Event Package for Voice Quality ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑This specification defines an extension to the multicast addressing architecture of the IP Version 4 protocol.  The extension presented in this document allows for unicast-prefix-based assignment of multicast addresses.  By delegating multicast addresses at the same time as unicast prefixes, network operators will be able to identify their multicast addresses without needing to run an inter-domain allocation protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej‘±hfilenamegrfc6034eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-127899hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¬<grankingícrfcd6034irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Unicast-Prefix-Based IPv4 Multicast AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'This document describes the conventions for using several cryptographic algorithms with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) encrypted key package content type.  Specifically, it includes conventions necessary to implement EnvelopedData, EncryptedData, and AuthEnvelopedData. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. TurnerddatejÕHhfilenamegrfc6033eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127898hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÇÃgrankingëcrfcd6033irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTAlgorithms for Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Encrypted Key Package Content TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6161∂habstractyThis document defines the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) encrypted key package content type, which can be used to encrypt a content that includes a key package, such as a symmetric key package or an asymmetric key package.  It is transport independent.  CMS can be used to digitally sign, digest, authenticate, or further encrypt this content type.  It is designed to be used with the CMS Content Constraints (CCC) extension, which does not constrain the EncryptedData, EnvelopedData, and AuthEnvelopedData. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘¶hfilenamegrfc6032eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127897hkeywordsÇcCCCwCMS content constraintskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÇºgrankingêcrfcd6032irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexECryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Encrypted Key Package Content TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÁThis document defines the symmetric key format content type.  It is transport independent.  The Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) can be used to digitally sign, digest, authenticate, or encrypt this content type. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. HousleyddatejÕXhfilenamegrfc6031eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgkeyprovdfullx(keyprov - Provisioning of Symmetric KeysdnamexProvisioning of Symmetric Keysdtypebwgbidjdoc-127896hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateMÇ¨grankingècrfcd6031irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexECryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Symmetric Key Package Content TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This document specifies a symmetric key format for the transport and provisioning of symmetric keys to different types of crypto modules.  For example, One-Time Password (OTP) shared secrets or symmetric cryptographic keys to strong authentication devices.  A standard key transport format enables enterprises to deploy best-of-breed solutions combining components from different vendors into the same infrastructure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmActivIdentitydnamehP. Hoyer¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamefM. Pei¢kaffiliationjDiversinetdnamejS. Machaniddatej‘∞hfilenamegrfc6030eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgkeyprovdfullx(keyprov - Provisioning of Symmetric KeysdnamexProvisioning of Symmetric Keysdtypebwgbidjdoc-127895hkeywordsémSymmetric KeylprovisioningcAESd3DESdTDEScOTPtKey transport formatwkey provisioning formatxsymmetric key protectionwsymmetric key transportmPIN transportcPINjPIN Policypkey usage policykobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateL \”grankingécrfcd6030irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Portable Symmetric Key Container (PSKC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyA significant part of the Internet traffic today is generated by peer-to-peer (P2P) applications used originally for file sharing, and more recently for real-time communications and live media streaming.  Such applications discover a route to each other through an overlay network with little knowledge of the underlying network topology.  As a result, they may choose peers based on information deduced from empirical measurements, which can lead to suboptimal choices.  This document, a product of the P2P Research Group, presents a survey of existing literature on discovering and using network topology information for Application-Layer Traffic Optimization.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-AndregauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Alcatel-LucentdnamehI. Rimac¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Alcatel-LucentdnamegV. Hilt¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Alcatel-LucentdnamehM. Tomsu¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejE. Maroccoddatej‘Øhfilenamegrfc6029eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymep2prgdfullx#p2prg - Peer-to-Peer Research GroupdnamexPeer-to-Peer Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127894hkeywordsÉlPeer-to-Peerstopology estimationxInternet coordinate systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¬;˚grankingçcrfcd6029irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexQA Survey on Research on the Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) ProblemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis document specifies two extensions to the Host Identity Protocol (HIP) to implement multi-hop routing.  The first extension allows implementing source routing in HIP.  That is, a node sending a HIP packet can define a set of nodes that the HIP packet should traverse.  The second extension allows a HIP packet to carry and record the list of nodes that forwarded it.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Keranenddatej‘Øhfilenamegrfc6028eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127893hkeywordsÉnsource routingoroute recordingooverlay networkkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateLΩ±hgrankingåcrfcd6028irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Multi-Hop Routing ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This document defines the terminology, problem statement, and requirements for implementing Internet Key Exchange (IKE) and IPsec on clusters.  It also describes gaps in existing standards and their implementation that need to be filled in order to allow peers to interoperate with clusters from different vendors.  Agreed upon terminology, problem statement, and requirements will allow IETF working groups to consider development of IPsec/IKEv2 mechanisms to simplify cluster implementations.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nirddatej‘Øhfilenamegrfc6027eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127892hkeywordsÜcIKEeIKEv2qhigh-availabilitylload-sharinghfailoverkhot-standbykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL∑:\grankingãcrfcd6027irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPsec Cluster Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document normatively updates RFC 3261, the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), to address an error in the specified handling of success (2xx class) responses to INVITE requests.  Elements following RFC 3261 exactly will misidentify retransmissions of the request as a new, unassociated request.  The correction involves modifying the INVITE transaction state machines.  The correction also changes the way responses that cannot be matched to an existing transaction are handled to address a security risk. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationeSkypednamepT. Zourzouvillysddatej‘¨hfilenamegrfc6026eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-127891hkeywordsÇmstate machinenretransmissionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL§”dgrankingäcrfcd6026irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcCorrect Transaction Handling for 2xx Responses to Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) INVITE RequestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) is widely used throughout the IETF Security Area and has been for many years.  Some specifications were written using a now deprecated version of ASN.1 and some were written using the current version of ASN.1.  Not all ASN.1 compilers support both older and current syntax.  This document is intended to provide guidance to specification authors and to implementers converting ASN.1 modules from one version of ASN.1 to another version without causing changes to the "bits on the wire".  This document does not provide a comprehensive tutorial of any version of ASN.1.  Instead, it addresses ASN.1 features that are used in IETF Security Area specifications with a focus on items that vary with the ASN.1 version.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallace¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamekC. Gardinerddatej‘∂hfilenamegrfc6025eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127890hkeywordsÑtBasic Encoding RulesxDistinguished Encoding RulesdPKIXfS/MIMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÀ}Ÿgrankingâcrfcd6025irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqASN.1 TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@A trust anchor represents an authoritative entity via a public key and associated data.  The public key is used to verify digital signatures, and the associated data is used to constrain the types of information for which the trust anchor is authoritative.  A relying party uses trust anchors to determine if a digitally signed object is valid by verifying a digital signature using the trust anchor's public key, and by enforcing the constraints expressed in the associated data for the trust anchor.  This document describes some of the problems associated with the lack of a standard trust anchor management mechanism and defines requirements for data formats and push-based protocols designed to address these problems.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamehR. Reddy¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallaceddatej‘∂hfilenamegrfc6024eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127889hkeywordsÉcPKIlcertificatesrdigital signatureskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL∆kkgrankingàcrfcd6024irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Trust Anchor Management RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyYThis document describes an extension to the Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocol that allows an IKEv2 Security Association (SA) to be created and authenticated without generating a Child SA.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for examination, experimental implementation, and evaluation.fadNamekSean TurnergauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationcNSNdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. SinghddatejÕ)hfilenamegrfc6023eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127888hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≤V2grankingácrfcd6023irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex_A Childless Initiation of the Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2) Security Association (SA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡This document defines a Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) data model to be used to monitor the NETCONF protocol.  The monitoring data model includes information about NETCONF datastores, sessions, locks, and statistics.  This data facilitates the management of a NETCONF server.  This document also defines methods for NETCONF clients to discover data models supported by a NETCONF server and defines a new NETCONF <get-schema> operation to retrieve them. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehM. Scott¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej‘´hfilenamegrfc6022eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127887hkeywordsÑcXMLgNETCONFdYANGjmonitoringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¨ÀmgrankingÜcrfcd6022irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"YANG Module for NETCONF MonitoringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx}This document introduces a collection of common data types to be used with the YANG data modeling language. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqJacobs UniversitydnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej‘®hfilenamegrfc6021eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127886hkeywordsÇdYANGgNETCONFkobsoletedByÅd6991epagesopublicationDateL¨ÀagrankingÖcrfcd6021irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevCommon YANG Data TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx’YANG is a data modeling language used to model configuration and state data manipulated by the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF), NETCONF remote procedure calls, and NETCONF notifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej‘®hfilenamegrfc6020eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetmoddfullxnetmod - Network ModelingdnamepNetwork Modelingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127885hkeywordsÉgNETCONFcXMLndata modellingkobsoletedByÄepages≠opublicationDateL¨ÀWgrankingÑcrfcd6020irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPYANG - A Data Modeling Language for the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9890µhabstractyzThis document specifies a new ASN.1 type for representing time: BinaryTime.  This document also specifies an alternate to the signing-time attribute for use with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) SignedData and AuthenticatedData content types; the binary-signing-time attribute uses BinaryTime.  CMS and the signing-time attribute are defined in RFC 5652. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘¥hfilenamegrfc6019eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43443hkeywordsÖvsigning-time attributexcryptographic message syntaxccmsjSignedDataqAuthenticatedDatakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLúÀ©grankingÉcrfcd6019irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGBinaryTime: An Alternate Format for Representing Date and Time in ASN.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∫This note discusses a feature to support building Greynets for IPv4 and IPv6.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationx"Swinburne University of TechnologydnameiW. Harrop¢kaffiliationx"Swinburne University of TechnologydnamekG. Armitageddatej‘≥hfilenamegrfc6018eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127884hkeywordsÅhdarknetskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateL§”TgrankingÇcrfcd6018irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIPv4 and IPv6 GreynetsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty3With the maturity of the Electronic Data Interchange - Internet Integration (EDIINT) standards of AS1, AS2, and AS3, applications and additional features are being built upon the basic secure transport functionality.  These features are not necessarily supported by all EDIINT applications and could cause potential problems with implementations.  The EDIINT-Features header field provides a means to resolve these problems and support new functionality.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey MelnikovgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsDrummond Group Inc.dnamejK. Meadorsddatej‘Khfilenamegrfc6017eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127883hkeywordsÅoEDIINT-FeatureskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLí£ÇgrankingÅcrfcd6017irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexQElectronic Data Interchange - Internet Integration (EDIINT) Features Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPRFC 4364 and RFC 4659 define an approach to building provider-provisioned Layer 3 VPNs (L3VPNs) for IPv4 and IPv6.  It may be desirable to use Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) to perform admission control on the links between Customer Edge (CE) routers and Provider Edge (PE) routers.  This document specifies procedures by which RSVP messages traveling from CE to CE across an L3VPN may be appropriately handled by PE routers so that admission control can be performed on PE-CE links.  Optionally, admission control across the provider's backbone may also be supported. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelA. Narayananddatej‘≠hfilenamegrfc6016eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127882hkeywordsÅel3vpnkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateL¨ÀGgrankingÄcrfcd6016irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDSupport for the Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) in Layer 3 VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄This document defines a new RTP payload format for the Forward Error Correction (FEC) that is generated by the 1-D interleaved parity code from a source media encapsulated in RTP.  The 1-D interleaved parity code is a systematic code, where a number of repair symbols are generated from a set of source symbols and sent in a repair flow separate from the source flow that carries the source symbols.  The 1-D interleaved parity code offers a good protection against bursty packet losses at a cost of reasonable complexity.  The new payload format defined in this document should only be used (with some exceptions) as a part of the Digital Video Broadcasting-IPTV (DVB- IPTV) Application-layer FEC specification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begenddatej‘Ähfilenamegrfc6015eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhfecframedfullxfecframe - FEC FrameworkdnamemFEC Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-127881hkeywordsÖcFEClinterleavingkloss repairoloss protectionjDVB AL-FECkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL∑:Igrankingcrfcd6015irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLRTP Payload Format for 1-D Interleaved Parity Forward Error Correction (FEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document specifies how DNSSEC cryptographic algorithm identifiers in the IANA registries are allocated.  It changes the requirement from "standard required" to "RFC Required".  It does not change the list of algorithms that are recommended or required for DNSSEC implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej‘§hfilenamegrfc6014eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127880hkeywordsÉfDNSSECrdigital signaturesjalgorithmskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÍµægranking~crfcd6014irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Cryptographic Algorithm Identifier Allocation for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9157µhabstractyøTCP Cookie Transactions (TCPCT) deter spoofing of connections and prevent resource exhaustion, eliminating Responder (server) state during the initial handshake.  The Initiator (client) has sole responsibility for ensuring required delays between connections.  The cookie exchange may carry data, limited to inhibit amplification and reflection denial of service attacks.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars EggertgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjDayDreamerdnamejW. SimpsonddatejÕ0hfilenamegrfc6013eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127879hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7805epages%opublicationDateM9Õgranking}crfcd6013irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexTCP Cookie Transactions (TCPCT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁThis document describes the transport of syslog messages over the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol.  It provides a secure transport for syslog messages in cases where a connectionless transport is desired.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationxEngineering Networks LtddnamehT. Petch¢kaffiliationlAdiscon GmbHdnamekR. Gerhards¢kaffiliationxHuaweisymantec TechnologiesdnamegH. Fengddatej‘≠hfilenamegrfc6012eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsyslogdfullx1syslog - Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdnamex(Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127878hkeywordsÅcTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL∑:;granking|crfcd6012irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEDatagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Transport Mapping for SyslogdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9662d8996∂habstractyThis document defines procedures for how a SIP User Agent should locate, retrieve, and maintain current configuration information from a Configuration Service.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuLinden Research, Inc.dnamekS. Lawrence¢kaffiliationx!Siemens Enterprise CommunicationsdnameiJ. Elwellddatej‘™hfilenamegrfc6011eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127877hkeywordsÉdHTTPdDHCPfDHCPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL \≈granking{crfcd6011irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) User Agent ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document specifies the syntax and semantics for the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) content constraints extension.  This extension is used to determine whether a public key is appropriate to use in the processing of a protected content.  In particular, the CMS content constraints extension is one part of the authorization decision; it is used when validating a digital signature on a CMS SignedData content or validating a message authentication code (MAC) on a CMS AuthenticatedData content or CMS AuthEnvelopedData content.  The signed or authenticated content type is identified by an ASN.1 object identifier, and this extension indicates the content types that the public key is authorized to validate.  If the authorization check is successful, the CMS content constraints extension also provides default values for absent attributes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejS. Ashmore¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallaceddatej‘§hfilenamegrfc6010eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127876hkeywordsÖmauthorizationcPKIkcertificateltrust anchordTAMPkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateLí£ngrankingzcrfcd6010irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Content Constraints ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document describes the "envelope-dsn", "redirect-dsn", "envelope-deliverby", and "redirect-deliverby" extensions to the Sieve email filtering language.  The "envelope-dsn" and "envelope- deliverby" extensions provide access to additional envelope information provided by the delivery status notification (DSN) and Deliver-By SMTP extensions, respectively.  The "redirect-dsn" and "redirect-deliverby" extensions extend Sieve's redirect action to provide control over delivery status notification and Deliver-By parameters, respectively. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamehN. Freedddatej‘©hfilenamegrfc6009eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127875hkeywordsÉdSMTPeESMTPeSievekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL™qÛgrankingycrfcd6009irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNSieve Email Filtering: Delivery Status Notifications and Deliver-By ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This memo updates the registry of properties in Authentication- Results: message header fields to allow a multiple-result report to distinguish among one or more cryptographic signatures on a message, thus associating specific results with the signatures they represent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCloudmark, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej‘©hfilenamegrfc6008eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127874hkeywordsÜdDKIMjDomainKeyshSenderIDcSPFnAuthenticationjReputationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLí£agrankingxcrfcd6008irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSAuthentication-Results Registration for Differentiating among Cryptographic ResultsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRA Path Computation Element (PCE) may be required to perform dependent path computations. Dependent path computations are requests that need to be synchronized in order to meet specific objectives. An example of a dependent request would be a PCE computing a set of services that are required to be diverse (disjointed) from each other. When a PCE computes sets of dependent path computation requests concurrently, use of the Synchronization VECtor (SVEC) list is required for association among the sets of dependent path computation requests. The SVEC object is optional and carried within the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) PCRequest (PCReq) message.

This document does not specify the PCEP SVEC object or procedure. This informational document clarifies the use of the SVEC list for synchronized path computations when computing dependent requests. The document also describes a number of usage scenarios for SVEC lists within single-domain and multi-domain environments. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiNEC Corp.dnamekI. Nishioka¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. KingddatejÕNhfilenamegrfc6007eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127873hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLòÊgrankingwcrfcd6007irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZUse of the Synchronization VECtor (SVEC) List for Synchronized Dependent Path ComputationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0Point-to-point Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) may be established using signaling techniques, but their paths may first need to be determined. The Path Computation Element (PCE) has been identified as an appropriate technology for the determination of the paths of point-to-multipoint (P2MP) TE LSPs.

This document describes extensions to the PCE communication Protocol (PCEP) to handle requests and responses for the computation of paths for P2MP TE LSPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationqHuawei TechnologydnamegQ. Zhao¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationxThales Communication FrancednamelF. Verhaeghe¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnameiT. Takeda¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnameiJ. Meuricddatej‘©hfilenamegrfc6006eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127872hkeywordsÇjEND-POINTSmfragmentationkobsoletedByÅd8306epages!opublicationDateLòÊgrankingvcrfcd6006irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâExtensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for Point-to-Multipoint Traffic Engineering Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis document describes a method for controlling two specific types of Ethernet switching via a GMPLS-based User Network Interface (UNI).  This document supports the types of switching required by the Ethernet services that have been defined in the context of the Metro Ethernet Forum (MEF) and International Telecommunication Union (ITU) G.8011.  This document is the UNI companion to "Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Support for Metro Ethernet Forum and G.8011 Ethernet Service Switching".  This document does not define or limit the underlying intra-domain or Internal NNI (I-NNI) technology used to support the UNI. [STANDARDS- TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedykddatej‘öhfilenamegrfc6005eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127871hkeywordsÖcmefcitux%International Telecommunication Unionei-nnilinternal nnikobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateL≤V grankingucrfcd6005irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaGeneralized MPLS (GMPLS) Support for Metro Ethernet Forum and G.8011 User Network Interface (UNI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a method for controlling two specific types of Ethernet switching via Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS).  This document supports the types of switching corresponding to the Ethernet services that have been defined in the context of the Metro Ethernet Forum (MEF) and International Telecommunication Union (ITU) G.8011.  Specifically, switching in support of Ethernet private line and Ethernet virtual private line services are covered.  Support for MEF- and ITU-defined parameters is also covered.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedykddatej‘öhfilenamegrfc6004eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127870hkeywordsÉx*Generalized Multi-Protocol Label SwitchingtMetro Ethernet ForumcMEFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≤Vgrankingtcrfcd6004irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Support for Metro Ethernet Forum and G.8011 Ethernet Service SwitchingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the support of Metro Ethernet Forum (MEF) Ethernet traffic parameters as described in MEF10.1 when using Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Resource ReSerVation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej‘ßhfilenamegrfc6003eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127869hkeywordsÉcmeftMetro Ethernet ForumgMEF10.1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≤Vgrankingscrfcd6003irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEthernet Traffic ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes two technology-independent extensions to Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS).  The first extension defines the new switching type Data Channel Switching Capable.  Data Channel Switching Capable interfaces are able to support switching of the whole digital channel presented on single channel interfaces.  The second extension defines a new type of generalized label and updates related objects.  The new label is called the Generalized Channel_Set Label and allows more than one data plane label to be controlled as part of a Label Switched Path (LSP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedykddatej‘éhfilenamegrfc6002eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127868hkeywordsÅx*Generalized Multi-Protocol Label SwitchingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateL≤UÛgrankingrcrfcd6002irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Data Channel Switching Capable (DCSC) and Channel Set Label ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThere are specific requirements for the support of networks comprising Label Switching Routers (LSRs) participating in different data plane switching layers controlled by a single Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) control plane instance, referred to as GMPLS Multi-Layer Networks / Multi-Region Networks (MLN/MRN).

This document defines extensions to GMPLS routing and signaling protocols so as to support the operation of GMPLS Multi-Layer / Multi-Region Networks. It covers the elements of a single GMPLS control plane instance controlling multiple Label Switched Path (LSP) regions or layers within a single Traffic Engineering (TE) domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelM. Vigoureux¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Shiomoto¢kaffiliationcATTdnamekD. Brungard¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le RouxddatejÕ*hfilenamegrfc6001eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127867hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≤UÊgrankingqcrfcd6001irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Protocol Extensions for Multi-Layer and Multi-Region Networks (MLN/MRN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIKEv2 specifies that Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) authentication must be used together with responder authentication based on public key signatures. This is necessary with old EAP methods that provide only unilateral authentication using, e.g., one- time passwords or token cards.

This document specifies how EAP methods that provide mutual authentication and key agreement can be used to provide extensible responder authentication for IKEv2 based on methods other than public key signatures. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejY. Shefferddatej‘´hfilenamegrfc5998eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127866hkeywordsÜumutual authenticationhpasswordkcredentialscAAAmkey agreementochannel bindingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLîıgrankingncrfcd5998irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1An Extension for EAP-Only Authentication in IKEv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis document describes a deployed extension to the Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) protocol, enabling clients to query the status of a RADIUS server.  This extension utilizes the Status-Server (12) Code, which was reserved for experimental use in RFC 2865.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjFreeRADIUSdnamehA. DeKokddatej‘ßhfilenamegrfc5997eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127865hkeywordsÅmstatus-serverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL|õÈgrankingmcrfcd5997irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Use of Status-Server Packets in the Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document describes version 2 of the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) protocol.  IKE is a component of IPsec used for performing mutual authentication and establishing and maintaining Security Associations (SAs).  This document replaces and updates RFC 4306, and includes all of the clarifications from RFC 4718. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Kaufman¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nir¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiP. Eronenddatej‘£hfilenamegrfc5996eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127864hkeywordsÇcIKEeIPseckobsoletedByÅd7296epagesäopublicationDateLèœSgrankinglcrfcd5996irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5998d6989∂habstractyÙThe Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Extensions for the Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) do not define the behavior for the "POST" method when applied to collections, as the base specification (HTTP) leaves implementers lots of freedom for the semantics of "POST".

This has led to a situation where many WebDAV servers do not implement POST for collections at all, although it is well suited to be used for the purpose of adding new members to a collection, where the server remains in control of the newly assigned URL. In fact, the Atom Publishing Protocol (AtomPub) uses POST exactly for that purpose. On the other hand, WebDAV-based protocols, such as the Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV), frequently require clients to pick a unique URL, although the server could easily perform that task.

This specification defines a discovery mechanism through which servers can advertise support for POST requests with the aforementioned "add collection member" semantics. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej‘•hfilenamegrfc5995eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127863hkeywordsÖdHTTPdPOSTfWebDAVkCollectionsrCollection MemberskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÜgEgrankingkcrfcd5995irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZUsing POST to Add Members to Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) CollectionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙEthernet pseudowires are widely deployed to support packet transport of Ethernet services. These services in-turn provide transport for a variety of client networks, e.g., IP and MPLS. This document uses procedures defined in the existing IETF specifications of Ethernet pseudowires carried over MPLS networks.

Many of the requirements for the services provided by the mechanisms explained in this document are also recognized by the MPLS transport profile (MPLS-TP) design effort formed jointly by the IETF and ITU-T. The solution described here does not address all of the MPLS-TP requirements, but it provides a viable form of packet transport service using tools that are already available.

This document also serves as an indication that existing MPLS techniques form an appropriate basis for the design of a fully- featured packet transport solution addressing all of the requirements of MPLS-TP. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Morrow¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelR. Cherukuri¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationbBTdnamekN. Harrison¢kaffiliationgVelocixdnamepB. Niven-Jenkinsddatej‘qhfilenamegrfc5994eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127862hkeywordsÅgmpls-tpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¨À=grankingjcrfcd5994irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Application of Ethernet Pseudowires to MPLS Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document specifies the payload format for packetization of Global System for Mobile Communications Half Rate (GSM-HR) speech codec data into the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP).  The payload format supports transmission of multiple frames per payload and packet loss robustness methods using redundancy. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx'China Mobile Communications CorporationdnamegX. Duan¢kaffiliationx'China Mobile Communications CorporationdnamegS. Wang¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamejK. Hellwig¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelI. Johanssonddatej‘"hfilenamegrfc5993eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127861hkeywordsÇlspeech codecxreal-time transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLΩ±Qgrankingicrfcd5993irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRTP Payload Format for Global System for Mobile Communications Half Rate (GSM-HR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty(This document is a guideline for registries and registrars on registering internationalized domain names (IDNs) based on (in alphabetical order) Bosnian, Bulgarian, Byelorussian, Kildin Sami, Macedonian, Montenegrin, Russian, Serbian, and Ukrainian languages in a DNS zone.  It describes appropriate characters for registration and variant considerations for characters from Greek and Latin scripts with similar appearances and/or derivations.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey MelnikovgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkRegtime LtddnamekS. Sharikov¢kaffiliationgAfiliasdnamemD. Miloshevic¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘}hfilenamegrfc5992eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127860hkeywordsäsBosnian and SerbianiBulgarianlByelorussianjBelarusianiBelarusankKildin SamijMacedoniankMontenegringRussianiUkrainiankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL™q∆grankinghcrfcd5992irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexoInternationalized Domain Names Registration and Administration Guidelines for European Languages Using CyrillicdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÂThe Teredo protocol defines a set of flags that are embedded in every Teredo IPv6 address.  This document specifies a set of security updates that modify the use of this flags field, but are backward compatible. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationhSymantecdnamekJ. Hoaglandddatej‘ßhfilenamegrfc5991eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127859hkeywordsÅsteredo ipv6 addresskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateLéX÷grankinggcrfcd5991irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewTeredo Security UpdatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬The RSA-KEM Key Transport Algorithm is a one-pass (store-and-forward) mechanism for transporting keying data to a recipient using the recipient's RSA public key. ("KEM" stands for "key encapsulation mechanism".) This document specifies the conventions for using the RSA-KEM Key Transport Algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  The ASN.1 syntax is aligned with an expected forthcoming change to American National Standard (ANS) X9.44.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrRandall ConsultingdnamejJ. Randall¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejB. Kaliski¢kaffiliationcRSAdnamekJ. Brainard¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘®hfilenamegrfc5990eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127858hkeywordsÇxkey encapsulation mechanismugeneric hybrid cipherkobsoletedByÅd9690epagesopublicationDateLõrÆgrankingfcrfcd5990irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTUse of the RSA-KEM Key Transport Algorithm in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∏The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is increasingly being used in systems that are tightly coupled with Hypertext Transport Protocol (HTTP) servers for a variety of reasons.  In many of these cases, applications can benefit from being able to discover, in near real- time, when a specific HTTP resource is created, changed, or deleted.  This document proposes a mechanism, based on the SIP Event Framework, for doing so. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnamejA.B. Roachddatej‘èhfilenamegrfc5989eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127857hkeywordsÉnLink RelationskSyndicationdAtomkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL \πgrankingecrfcd5989irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBA SIP Event Package for Subscribing to Changes to an HTTP ResourcedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxªThis document specifies relation types for Web links, and defines a registry for them.  It also defines the use of such links in HTTP headers with the Link header field. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej‘†hfilenamegrfc5988eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127856hkeywordsÖdLinkglinkingkhttp headermlink relationcwebkobsoletedByÅd8288epagesopublicationDateL \®grankingdcrfcd5988irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlekWeb LinkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßBy default, message header field parameters in Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) messages cannot carry characters outside the ISO- 8859-1 character set.  RFC 2231 defines an encoding mechanism for use in Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) headers.  This document specifies an encoding suitable for use in HTTP header fields that is compatible with a profile of the encoding defined in RFC 2231. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej‘ûhfilenamegrfc5987eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127855hkeywordsÉdHTTPvheader field parametertinternationalizationkobsoletedByÅd8187epages
opublicationDateLeiEgrankingccrfcd5987irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbCharacter Set and Language Encoding for Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Header Field ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËDiscovery of the correct Location Information Server (LIS) in the local access network is necessary for Devices that wish to acquire location information from the network.  A method is described for the discovery of a LIS in the access network serving a Device.  Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) options for IP versions 4 and 6 are defined that specify a domain name.  This domain name is then used as input to a URI-enabled NAPTR (U-NAPTR) resolution process. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrAndrew CorporationdnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationrAndrew CorporationdnameoJ. Winterbottomddatej‘§hfilenamegrfc5986eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127854hkeywordsÇgu-naptrquri-enabled naptrkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÜg6grankingbcrfcd5986irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Discovering the Local Location Information Server (LIS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty…This document defines a Layer 7 Location Configuration Protocol (L7 LCP) and describes the use of HTTP and HTTP/TLS as transports for the L7 LCP.  The L7 LCP is used for retrieving location information from a server within an access network.  It includes options for retrieving location information in two forms: by value and by reference.  The protocol is an extensible application-layer protocol that is independent of the session layer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnameiM. Barnesddatej‘shfilenamegrfc5985eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127853hkeywordsÇx'layer 7 location configuration protocolfl7 lcpkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateLÜg,grankingacrfcd5985irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%HTTP-Enabled Location Delivery (HELD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7840¥habstractx»This document proposes an experimental way of reaching infinite bandwidth in IP networks by the use of ESP-based forwarding.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekK-M. Mollerddatej‘‹hfilenamegrfc5984eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43479hkeywordsÅxextra sensory perceptionkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateMïógranking`crfcd5984irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexRIncreasing Throughput in IP Networks with ESP-Based Forwarding: ESPBasedForwardingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document describes considerations for mailing lists with the introduction of internationalized email addresses.

This document makes some specific recommendations on how mailing lists should act in various situations. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejR. GellensddatejÕVhfilenamegrfc5983eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127852hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd6783epages
opublicationDateL™qŸgranking_crfcd5983irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Mailing Lists and Internationalized Email AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØFlow-based measurement is a popular method for various network monitoring usages.  The sharing of flow-based information for monitoring applications having different requirements raises some open issues in terms of measurement system scalability, flow-based measurement flexibility, and export reliability that IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Mediation may help resolve.  This document describes some problems related to flow-based measurement that network administrators have been facing, and then it describes IPFIX Mediation applicability examples along with the problems.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkNTT PF Lab.dnamelA. Kobayashi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej‘ßhfilenamegrfc5982eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127851hkeywordsÅvflow-based measurementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL}@\granking^crfcd5982irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Mediation: Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySignaling layer protocols specified within the Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS) framework may rely on the General Internet Signaling Transport (GIST) protocol to handle authorization.  Still, the signaling layer protocol above GIST itself may require separate authorization to be performed when a node receives a request for a certain kind of service or resources.  This document presents a generic model and object formats for session authorization within the NSIS signaling layer protocols.  The goal of session authorization is to allow the exchange of information between network elements in order to authorize the use of resources for a service and to coordinate actions between the signaling and transport planes.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationcKITdnamehR. Blessddatej‘æhfilenamegrfc5981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127850hkeywordsÉwNext Steps in Signalingdgistx$General Internet Signaling TransportkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateMUŒagranking]crfcd5981irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Authorization for NSIS Signaling Layer ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—Mobility of an IP-based node affects routing paths, and as a result, can have a significant effect on the protocol operation and state management.  This document discusses the effects mobility can cause to the Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS) protocol suite, and shows how the NSIS protocols operate in different scenarios with mobility management protocols.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamehT. Sanda¢kaffiliationxUniversity of GoettingendnameeX. Fu¢kaffiliationdHUFSdnamehS. Jeong¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. TschofenigddatejÕWhfilenamegrfc5980eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127849hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateM˚Pgranking\crfcd5980irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.NSIS Protocol Operation in Mobile EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäNSIS Quality of Service (QoS) signaling enables applications to perform QoS reservation along a data flow path.  When the data flow path contains IP tunnel segments, NSIS QoS signaling has no effect within those tunnel segments.  Therefore, the resulting tunnel segments could become the weakest QoS link and invalidate the QoS efforts in the rest of the end-to-end path.  The problem with NSIS signaling within the tunnel is caused by the tunnel encapsulation that masks packets' original IP header fields.  Those original IP header fields are needed to intercept NSIS signaling messages and classify QoS data packets.  This document defines a solution to this problem by mapping end-to-end QoS session requests to corresponding QoS sessions in the tunnel, thus extending the end-to-end QoS signaling into the IP tunnel segments.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamegC. Shen¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamefS. Lee¢kaffiliationkSamsung AITdnamegJ. Bangddatej‘≠hfilenamegrfc5979eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127848hkeywordsÇhnsis qoswnext steps in signalingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateM˚Egranking[crfcd5979irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNSIS Operation over IP TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈThis document gives an overview of the Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS) framework and protocol suite created by the NSIS Working Group during the period of 2001-2010.  It also includes suggestions on how the industry can make use of the new protocols and how the community can exploit the extensibility of both the framework and existing protocols to address future signaling needs.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationcKITdnamehR. Bless¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Daviesddatej‘ùhfilenamegrfc5978eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127847hkeywordsàiSignalingdNTLPdNSLPdGISThQoS NSLPlNAT/FirewalllIP resourcesmExtensibilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¨À0grankingZcrfcd5978irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Using and Extending the NSIS Protocol FamilydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶This document describes a Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS) Quality-of-Service (QoS) Model for networks that use the Resource Management in Diffserv (RMD) concept.  RMD is a technique for adding admission control and preemption function to Differentiated Services (Diffserv) networks.  The RMD QoS Model allows devices external to the RMD network to signal reservation requests to Edge nodes in the RMD network.  The RMD Ingress Edge nodes classify the incoming flows into traffic classes and signals resource requests for the corresponding traffic class along the data path to the Egress Edge nodes for each flow.  Egress nodes reconstitute the original requests and continue forwarding them along the data path towards the final destination.  In addition, RMD defines notification functions to indicate overload situations within the domain to the Edge nodes.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehA. Bader¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekL. Westberg¢kaffiliationtUniversity of TwentednamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationvck technology conceptsdnamejC. Kappler¢kaffiliationeSonusdnameiT. Phelanddatej‘†hfilenamegrfc5977eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127846hkeywordsÇwnext steps in signalingxresource managment in diffservkobsoletedByÄepagesÄopublicationDateL¨À$grankingYcrfcd5977irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexORMD-QOSM: The NSIS Quality-of-Service Model for Resource Management in DiffservdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document describes a QoS-NSLP Quality-of-Service model (QOSM) based on ITU-T Recommendation Y.1541 Network QoS Classes and related guidance on signaling.  Y.1541 specifies 8 classes of Network Performance objectives, and the Y.1541-QOSM extensions include additional QSPEC parameters and QOSM processing guidelines.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamefG. Ash¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamehM. Dolly¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamekP. Tarapore¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiC. Dvorak¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemY. El Mghazliddatej‘éhfilenamegrfc5976eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127845hkeywordsÉhqos-nslpx!qos-nslp quality-of-service modeleqspeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¨ÀgrankingXcrfcd5976irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGY.1541-QOSM: Model for Networks Using Y.1541 Quality-of-Service ClassesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThe Quality-of-Service (QoS) NSIS signaling layer protocol (NSLP) is used to signal QoS reservations and is independent of a specific QoS model (QOSM) such as IntServ or Diffserv.  Rather, all information specific to a QOSM is encapsulated in a separate object, the QSPEC.  This document defines a template for the QSPEC including a number of QSPEC parameters.  The QSPEC parameters provide a common language to be reused in several QOSMs and thereby aim to ensure the extensibility and interoperability of QoS NSLP.  While the base protocol is QOSM-agnostic, the parameters that can be carried in the QSPEC object are possibly closely coupled to specific models.  The node initiating the NSIS signaling adds an Initiator QSPEC, which indicates the QSPEC parameters that must be interpreted by the downstream nodes less the reservation fails, thereby ensuring the intention of the NSIS initiator is preserved along the signaling path.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefG. Ash¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehA. Bader¢kaffiliationvck technology conceptsdnamejC. Kappler¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegD. OranddatejÕ#hfilenamegrfc5975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127844hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateL¨ÀgrankingWcrfcd5975irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNQSPEC Template for the Quality-of-Service NSIS Signaling Layer Protocol (NSLP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification describes the NSIS Signaling Layer Protocol (NSLP) for signaling Quality of Service (QoS) reservations in the Internet.  It is in accordance with the framework and requirements developed in NSIS.  Together with General Internet Signaling Transport (GIST), it provides functionality similar to RSVP and extends it.  The QoS NSLP is independent of the underlying QoS specification or architecture and provides support for different reservation models.  It is simplified by the elimination of support for multicast flows.  This specification explains the overall protocol approach, describes the design decisions made, and provides examples.  It specifies object, message formats, and processing rules.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Twente/EricssondnamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationdRokednamekA. McDonaldddatej‘èhfilenamegrfc5974eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127843hkeywordsÅcQoSkobsoletedByÄepagesfopublicationDateL¨ ˇgrankingVcrfcd5974irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexENSIS Signaling Layer Protocol (NSLP) for Quality-of-Service SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√This memo defines the NSIS Signaling Layer Protocol (NSLP) for Network Address Translators (NATs) and firewalls.  This NSLP allows hosts to signal on the data path for NATs and firewalls to be configured according to the needs of the application data flows.  For instance, it enables hosts behind NATs to obtain a publicly reachable address and hosts behind firewalls to receive data traffic.  The overall architecture is given by the framework and requirements defined by the Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS) working group.  The network scenarios, the protocol itself, and examples for path-coupled signaling are given in this memo.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegC. Aoun¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Daviesddatej‘ûhfilenamegrfc5973eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127842hkeywordsÑwNext Steps in SignalingdNSISvPath-coupled signalingiMiddleboxkobsoletedByÄepagesZopublicationDateL¨ ÙgrankingUcrfcd5973irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1NAT/Firewall NSIS Signaling Layer Protocol (NSLP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁThis document describes state machines for the General Internet Signaling Transport (GIST).  The states of GIST nodes for a given flow and their transitions are presented in order to illustrate how GIST may be implemented.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnameiT. Tsenov¢kaffiliationuNokia Siemens NetworkdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationpUniv. GoettingendnameeX. Fu¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegC. Aoun¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. DaviesddatejÕ?hfilenamegrfc5972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127841hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL¨ ËgrankingTcrfcd5972irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9General Internet Signaling Transport (GIST) State MachinedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThis document specifies protocol stacks for the routing and transport of per-flow signalling messages along the path taken by that flow through the network.  The design uses existing transport and security protocols under a common messaging layer, the General Internet Signalling Transport (GIST), which provides a common service for diverse signalling applications.  GIST does not handle signalling application state itself, but manages its own internal state and the configuration of the underlying transport and security protocols to enable the transfer of messages in both directions along the flow path.  The combination of GIST and the lower layer transport and security protocols provides a solution for the base protocol component of the "Next Steps in Signalling" (NSIS) framework.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationcRMRdnamejR. Hancockddatej‘hhfilenamegrfc5971eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127840hkeywordsÇdnsiswnext steps in signalingkobsoletedByÄepagesöopublicationDateL¨ ègrankingScrfcd5971irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+GIST: General Internet Signalling TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) provides a framework for passing configuration information to nodes on a network.  This document describes new options for DHCPv6 that SHOULD be used for booting a node from the network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx'IBM Germany Research & Development GmbHdnamegT. Huth¢kaffiliationx'IBM Germany Research & Development GmbHdnamekJ. Freimann¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiV. Zimmer¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej‘Øhfilenamegrfc5970eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127839hkeywordsÉdbootdIPv6fDHCPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLèœ9grankingRcrfcd5970irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDHCPv6 Options for Network BootdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document specifies an automatic tunneling mechanism tailored to advance deployment of IPv6 to end users via a service provider's IPv4 network infrastructure.  Key aspects include automatic IPv6 prefix delegation to sites, stateless operation, simple provisioning, and service, which is equivalent to native IPv6 at the sites that are served by the mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekW. Townsley¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehO. Troanddatej‘¶hfilenamegrfc5969eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127838hkeywordsÖc6rdmProvider 6to4mIPv6 softwireoIPv6 Transitiond6to4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLa~grankingQcrfcd5969irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMIPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures (6rd) -- Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThe RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) is used along with the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) to provide a control channel between media senders and receivers.  This allows constructing a feedback loop to enable application adaptation and monitoring, among other uses.  The basic reporting mechanisms offered by RTCP are generic, yet quite powerful and suffice to cover a range of uses.  This document provides guidelines on extending RTCP if those basic mechanisms prove insufficient.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej‘•hfilenamegrfc5968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127837hkeywordsÅxreal-time transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLí£UgrankingPcrfcd5968irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Guidelines for Extending the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThis document specifies a media type used to carry PKCS #10 certification requests as defined in RFC 2986.  It carries over the original specification from RFC 2311, which recently has been moved to Historic status, and properly links it to RFC 2986.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. TurnerddatejÕJhfilenamegrfc5967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127836hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLZÏÁgrankingOcrfcd5967irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!The application/pkcs10 Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÄThis document updates the requirements for the support of TCP as a transport protocol for DNS implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjNominet UKdnameiR. Bellisddatej‘Æhfilenamegrfc5966eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS Ex