mex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-127833hkeywordsÜdholegpolygongpidf-lopservice boundaryhlocationdLoSTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLvùÌgrankingLcrfcd5964irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMSpecifying Holes in Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Service BoundariesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides guidance on IPv6 deployment in Internet Exchange Points (IXPs).  It includes information regarding the switch fabric configuration, the addressing plan and general organizational tasks that need to be performed.  IXPs are mainly a Layer 2 infrastructure, and, in many cases, the best recommendations suggest that the IPv6 data, control, and management plane should not be handled differently than in IPv4.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Gaglianoddatej‘Øhfilenamegrfc5963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127832hkeywordsÑdIPv6cIXPjdeploymenthexchangekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateL|õ grankingKcrfcd5963irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2IPv6 Deployment in Internet Exchange Points (IXPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThe Geopriv Location Object introduced by the Presence Information Data Format - Location Object (PIDF-LO), RFC 4119, defines a basic XML format for carrying geographical information of a presentity.  This document defines PIDF-LO extensions to convey information about moving objects.  Elements are defined that enable expression of spatial orientation, speed, and heading of the presentity. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamehV. Singh¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationrAndrew CorporationdnamejM. Thomsonddatej‘•hfilenamegrfc5962eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127831hkeywordsÜgPIDF-LOhlocationgdynamicespeedhvelocitykorientationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLéXƒgrankingJcrfcd5962irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTDynamic Extensions to the Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬TCP has historically been considered to be protected against spoofed off-path packet injection attacks by relying on the fact that it is difficult to guess the 4-tuple (the source and destination IP addresses and the source and destination ports) in combination with the 32-bit sequence number(s).  A combination of increasing window sizes and applications using longer-term connections (e.g., H-323 or Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Ramaiah¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Dalalddatej‘£hfilenamegrfc5961eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127830hkeywordsäcRSTcSYNcFINfattacknData Injectionmvulnerabilitymblind attackscBGPespoofjmitigationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLvù˜grankingIcrfcd5961irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Improving TCP's Robustness to Blind In-Window AttacksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9293∂habstractyThe Multiprotocol Label Switching Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is the set of MPLS protocol functions applicable to the construction and operation of packet-switched transport networks. This document specifies the subset of these functions that comprises the MPLS-TP data plane: the architectural layer concerned with the encapsulation and forwarding of packets within an MPLS-TP network.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Frost¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocciddatej‘¨hfilenamegrfc5960eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127829hkeywordsÜgmpls-tpqtransport profileeitu-tidataplanecgaldgachkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLmº∫grankingHcrfcd5960irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.MPLS Transport Profile Data Plane ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7274∂habstractyZThis document describes the conventions for using several cryptographic algorithms with the EncryptedPrivateKeyInfo structure, as defined in RFC 5958.  It also includes conventions necessary to protect the AsymmetricKeyPackage content type with SignedData, EnvelopedData, EncryptedData, AuthenticatedData, and AuthEnvelopedData. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘úhfilenamegrfc5959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127828hkeywordsÇwEncryptedPrivateKeyInfotAsymmetricKeyPackagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLs'grankingGcrfcd5959irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Algorithms for Asymmetric Key Package Content TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6162∂habstracty£This document defines the syntax for private-key information and a content type for it.  Private-key information includes a private key for a specified public-key algorithm and a set of attributes.  The Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS), as defined in RFC 5652, can be used to digitally sign, digest, authenticate, or encrypt the asymmetric key format content type.  This document obsoletes RFC 5208. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘°hfilenamegrfc5958eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127827hkeywordsÖkprivate keywprivate-key informationprsa laboratoriesrprivate-key syntaxnchange controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLsgrankingFcrfcd5958irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewAsymmetric Key PackagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÆThis document describes an IMAP protocol extension enabling server- side message sorting on the commonly displayed portion of the From and To header fields. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfZimbradnamegD. Karpddatej‘öhfilenamegrfc5957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmorgdfullxmorg - Message OrganizationdnametMessage Organizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127826hkeywordsÅx Internet Message Access ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLL␀πgrankingEcrfcd5957irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Display-Based Address Sorting for the IMAP4 SORT ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This document defines the semantics for grouping the associated source and FEC-based (Forward Error Correction) repair flows in the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  The semantics defined in this document are to be used with the SDP Grouping Framework (RFC 5888).  These semantics allow the description of grouping relationships between the source and repair flows when one or more source and/or repair flows are associated in the same group, and they provide support for additive repair flows.  SSRC-level (Synchronization Source) grouping semantics are also defined in this document for Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) streams using SSRC multiplexing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begenddatej‘¶hfilenamegrfc5956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127825hkeywordsÖcFECkloss repairhgroupingcsdpkmedia lineskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLòÂ˛grankingDcrfcd5956irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOForward Error Correction Grouping Semantics in the Session Description ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÀThis document defines a new media type for TimeStampedData envelopes as described in RFC 5544.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnActalis S.p.A.dnamejA. Santoniddatej‘ßhfilenamegrfc5955eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127824hkeywordsÅxTimeStampedData envelopeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLY¸{grankingCcrfcd5955irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+The application/timestamped-data Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document corrects the Augmented Backus-Naur Form (ABNF) production rule associated with generating IPv6 literals in RFC 3261.  It also clarifies the rule for Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) comparison when the URIs contain textual representation of IP addresses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Bell Laboratories Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationiBroadSoftdnamegB. Tateddatej‘#hfilenamegrfc5954eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127823hkeywordsÜcSIPxsession initiation protocolxAugmented Backus-Naur FormxUniform Resource IdentifiermIPv6referencekIPv6addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLn∆grankingBcrfcd5954irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAEssential Correction for IPv6 ABNF and URI Comparison in RFC 3261dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThis document describes a Transport Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP), that uses either the Transport Layer Security protocol or the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol. The TLS and DTLS protocols provide authentication and privacy services for SNMP applications. This document describes how the TLS Transport Model (TLSTM) implements the needed features of a SNMP Transport Subsystem to make this protection possible in an interoperable way.

This Transport Model is designed to meet the security and operational needs of network administrators. It supports the sending of SNMP messages over TLS/TCP and DTLS/UDP. The TLS mode can make use of TCP's improved support for larger packet sizes and the DTLS mode provides potentially superior operation in environments where a connectionless (e.g., UDP) transport is preferred. Both TLS and DTLS integrate well into existing public keying infrastructures.

This document also defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols. In particular, it defines objects for managing the TLS Transport Model for SNMP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlSparta, Inc.dnamekW. Hardakerddatej‘°hfilenamegrfc5953eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdismsdfullx)isms - Integrated Security Model for SNMPdnamex"Integrated Security Model for SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127822hkeywordsÖddtlsx!datagram transport layer securitystls transport modeletlstmoSNMP-TLS-TM-MIBkobsoletedByÅd6353epagesAopublicationDateLn∆grankingAcrfcd5953irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Transport Layer Security (TLS) Transport Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyœAs IPv6 deployment increases, there will be a dramatic increase in the need to use IPv6 addresses in text.  While the IPv6 address architecture in Section 2.2 of RFC 4291 describes a flexible model for text representation of an IPv6 address, this flexibility has been causing problems for operators, system engineers, and users.  This document defines a canonical textual representation format.  It does not define a format for internal storage, such as within an application or database.  It is expected that the canonical format will be followed by humans and systems when representing IPv6 addresses as text, but all implementations must accept and be able to handle any legitimate RFC 4291 format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqNEC BIGLOBE, Ltd.dnamekS. Kawamura¢kaffiliationxNEC AccessTechnica, Ltd.dnamelM. Kawashimaddatej‘õhfilenamegrfc5952eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127821hkeywordsÉdIPv6stext representationicanonicalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLn≈˜granking@crfcd5952irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5A Recommendation for IPv6 Address Text RepresentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document specifies the requirements for the management of equipment used in networks supporting an MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP).  The requirements are defined for specification of network management aspects of protocol mechanisms and procedures that constitute the building blocks out of which the MPLS Transport Profile is constructed.  That is, these requirements indicate what management capabilities need to be available in MPLS for use in managing the MPLS-TP.  This document is intended to identify essential network management capabilities, not to specify what functions any particular MPLS implementation supports. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamefK. Lam¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelS. Mansfield¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Grayddatej‘yhfilenamegrfc5951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127820hkeywordsÇvMPLS Transport Profilegmpls-tpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLèœlgranking?crfcd5951irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexANetwork Management Requirements for MPLS-based Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document provides the network management framework for the Transport Profile for Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS-TP).

This framework relies on the management terminology from the ITU-T to describe the management architecture that could be used for an MPLS-TP management network.

The management of the MPLS-TP network could be based on multi-tiered distributed management systems. This document provides a description of the network and element management architectures that could be applied and also describes heuristics associated with fault, configuration, and performance aspects of the management system.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and PWE3 architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelS. Mansfield¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamefK. Lamddatej‘ëhfilenamegrfc5950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127819hkeywordsÇgmpls-tpxnetwork management frameworkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLèœ`granking>crfcd5950irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Network Management Framework for MPLS-based Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6; RFC 3775) provides a mobile node with IP mobility when it performs a handover from one access router to another, and fast handovers for Mobile IPv6 (FMIPv6) are specified to enhance the handover performance in terms of latency and packet loss. While MIPv6 (and FMIPv6 as well) requires the participation of the mobile node in the mobility-related signaling, Proxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6; RFC 5213) provides IP mobility to nodes that either have or do not have MIPv6 functionality without such involvement. Nevertheless, the basic performance of PMIPv6 in terms of handover latency and packet loss is considered no different from that of MIPv6.

When the fast handover is considered in such an environment, several modifications are needed to FMIPv6 to adapt to the network-based mobility management. This document specifies the usage of fast handovers for Mobile IPv6 (FMIPv6; RFC 5568) when Proxy Mobile IPv6 is used as the mobility management protocol. Necessary extensions are specified for FMIPv6 to support the scenario when the mobile node does not have IP mobility functionality and hence is not involved with either MIPv6 or FMIPv6 operations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhKDDI LabdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Koodli¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamefF. Xiaddatej‘¢hfilenamegrfc5949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127818hkeywordsÖgPFMIPv6ghandofffPMIPv6jpredictivehreactivekobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateL§”Fgranking=crfcd5949irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Fast Handovers for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂIEEE 802.16 is an air interface specification for wireless broadband access. IEEE 802.16 has specified multiple service-specific Convergence Sublayers for transmitting upper-layer protocols. The Packet CS (Packet Convergence Sublayer) is used for the transport of all packet-based protocols such as the Internet Protocol (IP) and IEEE 802.3 (Ethernet). The IP-specific part of the Packet CS enables the transport of IPv4 packets directly over the IEEE 802.16 Media Access Control (MAC) layer.

This document specifies the frame format, the Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU), and the address assignment procedures for transmitting IPv4 packets over the IP-specific part of the Packet Convergence Sublayer of IEEE 802.16. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqiRam TechnologiesdnamenS. Madanapalli¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemG. Montenegroddatej‘¶hfilenamegrfc5948eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd16ngdfullx#16ng - IP over IEEE 802.16 NetworksdnamexIP over IEEE 802.16 Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127817hkeywordsÅipacket cskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLmº™granking<crfcd5948irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLTransmission of IPv4 Packets over the IP Convergence Sublayer of IEEE 802.16dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document states requirements for a standardized SIP registration mechanism for multiple addresses of record (AORs), the mechanism being suitable for deployment by SIP service providers on a large scale in support of small to medium sized Private Branch Exchanges (PBXs).  The requirements are for a solution that can, as a minimum, support AORs based on E.164 numbers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqGonzalo Camarillodarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx!Siemens Enterprise CommunicationsdnameiJ. Elwell¢kaffiliationkAcme PacketdnameiH. Kaplanddatej‘´hfilenamegrfc5947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmartinidfullx:martini - Multiple AoR reachabiliTy InformatioN Indicationdnamex0Multiple AoR reachabiliTy InformatioN Indicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127816hkeywordsÉhTrunkingcpbxwprivate branch exchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLèœ,granking;crfcd5947irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsRequirements for Multiple Address of Record (AOR) Reachability Information in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) signaling can be used to make end-to-end resource reservations in an IP network in order to guarantee the Quality of Service (QoS) required by certain flows.  With conventional RSVP, both the data sender and receiver of a given flow take part in RSVP signaling.  Yet, there are many use cases where resource reservation is required, but the receiver, the sender, or both, is not RSVP-capable.  Where the receiver is not RSVP- capable, an RSVP router may behave as an RSVP Receiver Proxy, thereby performing RSVP signaling on behalf of the receiver.  This allows resource reservations to be established on the segment of the end-to- end path from the sender to the RSVP Receiver Proxy.  However, as discussed in the companion document "RSVP Proxy Approaches", RSVP extensions are needed to facilitate operations with an RSVP Receiver Proxy whose signaling is triggered by receipt of RSVP Path messages from the sender.  This document specifies these extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelA. Narayanan¢kaffiliationcSFRdnamejA. Guillou¢kaffiliationfAirteldnamehH. MalikddatejÕ=hfilenamegrfc5946eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127815hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateL≤U‘granking:crfcd5946irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVResource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Extensions for Path-Triggered RSVP Receiver ProxydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙The Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) can be used to make end-to- end resource reservations in an IP network in order to guarantee the quality of service required by certain flows.  RSVP assumes that both the data sender and receiver of a given flow take part in RSVP signaling.  Yet, there are use cases where resource reservation is required, but the receiver, the sender, or both, is not RSVP-capable.  This document presents RSVP proxy behaviors allowing RSVP routers to initiate or terminate RSVP signaling on behalf of a receiver or a sender that is not RSVP-capable.  This allows resource reservations to be established on a critical subset of the end-to-end path.  This document reviews conceptual approaches for deploying RSVP proxies and discusses how RSVP reservations can be synchronized with application requirements, despite the sender, receiver, or both not participating in RSVP.  This document also points out where extensions to RSVP (or to other protocols) may be needed for deployment of a given RSVP proxy approach.  However, such extensions are outside the scope of this document.  Finally, practical use cases for RSVP proxy are described.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationcTKKdnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationcSFRdnamejA. GuillouddatejÕ;hfilenamegrfc5945eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127814hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateL≤U¿granking9crfcd5945irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Proxy ApproachesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis document specifies protocol enhancements that allow transparent routing of IP datagrams to mobile nodes in the Internet.  Each mobile node is always identified by its home address, regardless of its current point of attachment to the Internet.  While situated away from its home, a mobile node is also associated with a care-of address, which provides information about its current point of attachment to the Internet.  The protocol provides for registering the care-of address with a home agent.  The home agent sends datagrams destined for the mobile node through a tunnel to the care-of address.  After arriving at the end of the tunnel, each datagram is then delivered to the mobile node. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmWiChorus Inc.dnamejC. Perkinsddatej‘úhfilenamegrfc5944eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127813hkeywordsÉmMOBILEIPSUPIPqInternet ProtocoleMIPv4kobsoletedByÄepagesdopublicationDateLı@Êgranking8crfcd5944irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%IP Mobility Support for IPv4, ReviseddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»The deployment of new IP connectivity typically results in intermittent reachability for numerous reasons that are outside the scope of this document.  In order to aid in the debugging of these persistent problems, this document proposes the creation of a new Routing Policy Specification Language attribute that allows a network to advertise an IP address that is reachable and can be used as a target for diagnostic tests (e.g., pings). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. HabermanddatejÕPhfilenamegrfc5943eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127812hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLeugranking7crfcd5943irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^A Dedicated Routing Policy Specification Language Interface Identifier for Operational TestingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎIPv6 specifies a model of a subnet that is different than the IPv4 subnet model.  The subtlety of the differences has resulted in incorrect implementations that do not interoperate.  This document spells out the most important difference: that an IPv6 address isn't automatically associated with an IPv6 on-link prefix.  This document also updates (partially due to security concerns caused by incorrect implementations) a part of the definition of "on-link" from RFC 4861. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehH. Singh¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiW. Beebee¢kaffiliationlOracle, Inc.dnamekE. NordmarkddatejÕ@hfilenamegrfc5942eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127811hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLA{granking6crfcd5942irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEIPv6 Subnet Model: The Relationship between Links and Subnet PrefixesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_This document describes a document format for exchanging transaction fraud (Thraud) information.  It extends the Incident Handling Working Group (INCH WG) Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) incident reporting document format.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamejD. M'Raihi¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnameiS. Boeyen¢kaffiliationxGrandcolas Consulting LLCdnamemM. Grandcolas¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamehS. Bajajddatej‘ûhfilenamegrfc5941eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127810hkeywordsÉfthraudx+incident object description exchange formateiodefkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLei;granking5crfcd5941irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSharing Transaction Fraud DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9The Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) allows revocation information to be conveyed as part of the SignedData, EnvelopedData, AuthenticatedData, and AuthEnvelopedData content types.  The preferred format for revocation information is the Certificate Revocation List (CRL), but an extension mechanism supports other revocation information formats.  This document defines two additional revocation information formats for Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) responses and Server-Based Certificate Validation Protocol (SCVP) requests and responses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘®hfilenamegrfc5940eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127809hkeywordsÑx"online certificate status protocoldocspx,server-based certificate validation protocoldscvpkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateLmºügranking4crfcd5940irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLAdditional Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Revocation Information ChoicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^The Session Description Protocol (SDP) was intended to describe multimedia sessions for the purposes of session announcement, session invitation, and other forms of multimedia session initiation. SDP was not intended to provide capability indication or capability negotiation; however, over the years, SDP has seen widespread adoption and as a result it has been gradually extended to provide limited support for these, notably in the form of the offer/answer model defined in RFC 3264. SDP does not define how to negotiate one or more alternative transport protocols (e.g., RTP profiles) or attributes. This makes it difficult to deploy new RTP profiles such as Secure RTP or RTP with RTCP-based feedback, negotiate use of different security keying mechanisms, etc. It also presents problems for some forms of media negotiation.

The purpose of this document is to address these shortcomings by extending SDP with capability negotiation parameters and associated offer/answer procedures to use those parameters in a backwards compatible manner.

The document defines a general SDP Capability Negotiation framework. It also specifies how to provide attributes and transport protocols as capabilities and negotiate them using the framework. Extensions for other types of capabilities (e.g., media types and media formats) may be provided in other documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej‘ùhfilenamegrfc5939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127808hkeywordsÉrmultimedia sessiontsession announcementrsession invitationkobsoletedByÄepagesMopublicationDateLèœgranking3crfcd5939irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Session Description Protocol (SDP) Capability NegotiationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6871∂habstracty»The IETF has completed its work on the core specification of TWAMP -- the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol.  This memo describes an OPTIONAL feature for TWAMP, that gives the controlling host the ability to start and stop one or more individual test sessions using Session Identifiers.  The base capability of the TWAMP protocol requires all test sessions that were previously requested and accepted to start and stop at the same time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. ChibaddatejÕ+hfilenamegrfc5938eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127807hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLb⁄µgranking2crfcd5938irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVIndividual Session Control Feature for the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This document describes how to use information associated with a trust anchor public key when validating certification paths.  This information can be used to constrain the usage of a trust anchor.  Typically, constraints are used to limit the certificate policies and names that can appear in certification paths validated using a trust anchor.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejS. Ashmore¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallaceddatej‘úhfilenamegrfc5937eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127806hkeywordsÅbTAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLmºëgranking1crfcd5937irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCUsing Trust Anchor Constraints during Certification Path ProcessingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThe standard means within the Domain Name System protocol for maintaining coherence among a zone's authoritative name servers consists of three mechanisms. Authoritative Transfer (AXFR) is one of the mechanisms and is defined in RFC 1034 and RFC 1035.

The definition of AXFR has proven insufficient in detail, thereby forcing implementations intended to be compliant to make assumptions, impeding interoperability. Yet today we have a satisfactory set of implementations that do interoperate. This document is a new definition of AXFR -- new in the sense that it records an accurate definition of an interoperable AXFR mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamehE. Lewis¢kaffiliationfTR-SysdnameiA. Hoenesddatej‘òhfilenamegrfc5936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127805hkeywordsÇvauthoritative transfernAXFR mechanismkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL(˛Fgranking0crfcd5936irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!DNS Zone Transfer Protocol (AXFR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9103∂habstractyJThis memo defines the IETF standard expression of Structure of Management Information (SMI) base datatypes in XML Schema Definition (XSD) language.  The primary objective of this memo is to enable the production of XML documents that are as faithful to the SMI as possible, using XSD as the validation mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxEllison Software ConsultingdnamejM. Ellison¢kaffiliationeMITREdnameiB. Nataleddatej‘ßhfilenamegrfc5935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127804hkeywordsÅx#structure of management informationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLiœugranking/crfcd5935irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Expressing SNMP SMI Datatypes in XML Schema Definition LanguagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThis document describes a transport independent protocol for the management of trust anchors (TAs) and community identifiers stored in a trust anchor store.  The protocol makes use of the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS), and a digital signature is used to provide integrity protection and data origin authentication.  The protocol can be used to manage trust anchor stores containing trust anchors represented as Certificate, TBSCertificate, or TrustAnchorInfo objects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejS. Ashmore¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallaceddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5934eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127803hkeywordsÇmtrust anchorsbTAkobsoletedByÄepages[opublicationDateLmºÑgranking.crfcd5934irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Trust Anchor Management Protocol (TAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÛThis document describes how to produce digital signatures and hash functions using the GOST R 34.10-2001 and GOST R 34.11-94 algorithms for DNSKEY, RRSIG, and DS resource records, for use in the Domain Name System Security Extensions (DNSSEC).fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnCryptocom Ltd.dnamekV. Dolmatov¢kaffiliationnCryptocom Ltd.dnamekA. Chuprina¢kaffiliationnCryptocom Ltd.dnamejI. Ustinovddatej‘ôhfilenamegrfc5933eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127802hkeywordsÇx&domain name system security extensionscECCkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateL7∞zgranking-crfcd5933irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPUse of GOST Signature Algorithms in DNSKEY and RRSIG Resource Records for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6944∂habstractyrThis document specifies a set of cipher suites for the Transport Security Layer (TLS) protocol to support the Camellia encryption algorithm as a block cipher.  It amends the cipher suites originally specified in RFC 4132 by introducing counterparts using the newer cryptographic hash algorithms from the SHA-2 family.  This document obsoletes RFC 4132. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamegA. Kato¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehM. Kanda¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamehS. Kannoddatej‘óhfilenamegrfc5932eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127801hkeywordsálblock cipherhsecurityhcamelliactlsccbcdsha2xcamellia encryption algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL(˛;granking,crfcd5932irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCamellia Cipher Suites for TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰This memo describes an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) method, EAP-pwd, which uses a shared password for authentication.  The password may be a low-entropy one and may be drawn from some set of possible passwords, like a dictionary, which is available to an attacker.  The underlying key exchange is resistant to active attack, passive attack, and dictionary attack.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Harkins¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej‘Öhfilenamegrfc5931eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127800hkeywordsÉx#Password Authenticated Key ExchangeqDictionary AttackrAuthentication EAPkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateLa~granking+crfcd5931irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Authentication Using Only a PassworddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8146∂habstractypThis document describes the usage of Advanced Encryption Standard Counter Mode (AES-CTR), with an explicit Initialization Vector, by the Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocol, for encrypting the IKEv2 exchanges that follow the IKE_SA_INIT exchange.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegS. Shen¢kaffiliationxHangzhou H3C Tech. Co., Ltd.dnamefY. Mao¢kaffiliationwFreescale SemiconductordnamekNSS. Murthyddatej‘¢hfilenamegrfc5930eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127799hkeywordsÇuinitialization vectorkIKE_SA_INITkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLFÜgranking*crfcd5930irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextUsing Advanced Encryption Standard Counter Mode (AES-CTR) with the Internet Key Exchange version 02 (IKEv2) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document defines three channel binding types for Transport Layer Security (TLS), tls-unique, tls-server-end-point, and tls-unique-for-telnet, in accordance with RFC 5056 (On Channel Binding).

Note that based on implementation experience, this document changes the original definition of 'tls-unique' channel binding type in the channel binding type IANA registry. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpSecure EndpointsdnameiJ. Altman¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekN. Williams¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhuddatej‘°hfilenamegrfc5929eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127798hkeywordsácTLSgchannelgbindingochannel-bindingjtls-uniquettls-server-end-pointutls-unique-for-telnetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL;Íøgranking)crfcd5929irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexChannel Bindings for TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9266∂habstractx¬This document defines a resolution mechanism to generate a list of server transport addresses that can be tried to create a Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) allocation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepDavid Harringtondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameqM. Petit-Hugueninddatej‘ühfilenamegrfc5928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127797hkeywordsÇcNATiTraversalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLY¸rgranking(crfcd5928irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) Resolution MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7350d8553∂habstractyÆThis document discusses the use of the Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP) to perform a variety of attacks against the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).  Additionally, this document describes a number of widely implemented modifications to TCP's handling of ICMP error messages that help to mitigate these issues.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUTN/FRHdnamegF. Gontddatej‘ôhfilenamegrfc5927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127796hkeywordsâmvulnerabilitymblind attackswconnection-reset attackxperformance-degrading attackxthroughput-reduction attackmsource quenchePMTUDrPath-MTU DiscoveryxICMP Destination UnreachablekobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateL3Ω[granking'crfcd5927irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexICMP Attacks against TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»The TCP Authentication Option (TCP-AO) relies on security algorithms to provide authentication between two end-points.  There are many such algorithms available, and two TCP-AO systems cannot interoperate unless they are using the same algorithms.  This document specifies the algorithms and attributes that can be used in TCP-AO's current manual keying mechanism and provides the interface for future message authentication codes (MACs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamekG. Lebovitz¢kaffiliationdRTFMdnamekE. Rescorladdatej‘óhfilenamegrfc5926eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127795hkeywordsÅxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÓógranking&crfcd5926irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCCryptographic Algorithms for the TCP Authentication Option (TCP-AO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆThis document specifies the TCP Authentication Option (TCP-AO), which obsoletes the TCP MD5 Signature option of RFC 2385 (TCP MD5).  TCP-AO specifies the use of stronger Message Authentication Codes (MACs), protects against replays even for long-lived TCP connections, and provides more details on the association of security with TCP connections than TCP MD5.  TCP-AO is compatible with either a static Master Key Tuple (MKT) configuration or an external, out-of-band MKT management mechanism; in either case, TCP-AO also protects connections when using the same MKT across repeated instances of a connection, using traffic keys derived from the MKT, and coordinates MKT changes between endpoints.  The result is intended to support current infrastructure uses of TCP MD5, such as to protect long-lived connections (as used, e.g., in BGP and LDP), and to support a larger set of MACs with minimal other system and operational changes.  TCP-AO uses a different option identifier than TCP MD5, even though TCP-AO and TCP MD5 are never permitted to be used simultaneously.  TCP-AO supports IPv6, and is fully compatible with the proposed requirements for the replacement of TCP MD5. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationsJohns Hopkins Univ.dnameiA. Mankin¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonicaddatej‘ñhfilenamegrfc5925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127794hkeywordsáxtransmission control protocolfborderggatewayhprotocolxtransmission control messagefdigestialgorithmkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateLÓãgranking%crfcd5925irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe TCP Authentication OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìThis memo documents an extended key usage (EKU) X.509 certificate extension for restricting the applicability of a certificate to use with a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) service.  As such, in addition to providing rules for SIP implementations, this memo also provides guidance to issuers of certificates for use with SIP.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuNortel Networks, Inc.dnamekS. Lawrence¢kaffiliationx Bell Laboratories Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbaniddatej‘yhfilenamegrfc5924eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127793hkeywordsÉdPKIXjSIP DomainqX.509 CertificatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL"jØgranking$crfcd5924irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQExtended Key Usage (EKU) for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) X.509 CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document enables a pair of communicating proxies to reuse a congestion-controlled connection between themselves for sending requests in the forwards and backwards direction.  Because the connection is essentially aliased for requests going in the backwards direction, reuse is predicated upon both the communicating endpoints authenticating themselves using X.509 certificates through Transport Layer Security (TLS).  For this reason, we only consider connection reuse for TLS over TCP and TLS over Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  This document also provides guidelines on connection reuse and virtual SIP servers and the interaction of connection reuse and DNS SRV lookups in SIP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Bell Laboratories Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationiBroadSoftdnamegB. Tateddatej‘thfilenamegrfc5923eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127792hkeywordsánTCP ConnectionoSCTP ConnectionnTLS ConnectiontTransport ConnectioncTLSnVirtual ServernAuthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL"j†granking#crfcd5923irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Connection Reuse in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how to construct and interpret certain information in a PKIX-compliant (Public Key Infrastructure using X.509) certificate for use in a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) over Transport Layer Security (TLS) connection.  More specifically, this document describes how to encode and extract the identity of a SIP domain in a certificate and how to use that identity for SIP domain authentication.  As such, this document is relevant both to implementors of SIP and to issuers of certificates. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Alcatel-Lucent Bell LaboratoriesdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliation`dnamekS. Lawrence¢kaffiliationx Alcatel-Lucent Bell LaboratoriesdnamejA. Jeffreyddatej‘°hfilenamegrfc5922eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127791hkeywordsÖdPKIXnAuthenticationuMutual AuthenticationeX.509cTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL"jïgranking"crfcd5922irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Domain Certificates in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document specifies an architectural framework for the application of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) to the construction of packet-switched transport networks. It describes a common set of protocol functions -- the MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) -- that supports the operational models and capabilities typical of such networks, including signaled or explicitly provisioned bidirectional connection-oriented paths, protection and restoration mechanisms, comprehensive Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) functions, and network operation in the absence of a dynamic control plane or IP forwarding support. Some of these functions are defined in existing MPLS specifications, while others require extensions to existing specifications to meet the requirements of the MPLS-TP.

This document defines the subset of the MPLS-TP applicable in general and to point-to-point transport paths. The remaining subset, applicable specifically to point-to-multipoint transport paths, is outside the scope of this document.

This document is a product of a joint Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) / International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) effort to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by the ITU-T. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Frost¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiL. Levrau¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Bergerddatej‘§hfilenamegrfc5921eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127790hkeywordsÖxmultiprotocol label switchinggmpls-tpqtransport profilecoameitu-tkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateL7∞jgranking!crfcd5921irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A Framework for MPLS in Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6215d7274∂habstracty…This document provides a security framework for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Networks.  This document addresses the security aspects that are relevant in the context of MPLS and GMPLS.  It describes the security threats, the related defensive techniques, and the mechanisms for detection and reporting.  This document emphasizes RSVP-TE and LDP security considerations, as well as inter-AS and inter-provider security considerations for building and maintaining MPLS and GMPLS networks across different domains or different Service Providers.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegL. FangddatejÕFhfilenamegrfc5920eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127789hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateL7∞^granking crfcd5920irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Security Framework for MPLS and GMPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThere are situations following Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) session establishment where it would be useful for an LDP speaker to know when its peer has advertised all of its labels.  The LDP specification provides no mechanism for an LDP speaker to notify a peer when it has completed its initial label advertisements to that peer.  This document specifies means for an LDP speaker to signal completion of its initial label advertisements following session establishment. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Asati¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Thomasddatej‘ohfilenamegrfc5919eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127788hkeywordsÉxlabel distribution protocoljEnd-of-LIBxUnrecognized NotificationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateLiœkgrankingcrfcd5919irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Signaling LDP Label Advertisement CompletiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThe Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) specification for the Wildcard Forward Equivalence Class (FEC) element has several limitations.  This document addresses those limitations by defining a Typed Wildcard FEC Element and associated procedures.  In addition, it defines a new LDP capability to address backward compatibility. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Asati¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Thomasddatej‘óhfilenamegrfc5918eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127787hkeywordsÉhWildcardxTyped Wildcard FEC ElementxTyped Wildcard FEC CapabilitykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateLiœ_grankingcrfcd5918irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRLabel Distribution Protocol (LDP) 'Typed Wildcard' Forward Equivalence Class (FEC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7358∂habstractyoThis document defines the clearance sponsor attribute.  It indicates the entity that sponsored (i.e., granted) the clearance.  This attribute is intended for use in public key certificates and attribute certificates that also include the clearance attribute.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. TurnerddatejÕhfilenamegrfc5917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127786hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLé1grankingcrfcd5917irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexClearance Sponsor AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document defines the Device Owner attribute.  It indicates the entity (e.g., company, organization, department, agency) that owns the device.  This attribute may be included in public key certificates and attribute certificates.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. TurnerddatejÕ&hfilenamegrfc5916eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127785hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLé)grankingcrfcd5916irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevDevice Owner AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document specifies the syntax and semantics for conveying Elliptic Curve (EC) private key information.  The syntax and semantics defined herein are based on similar syntax and semantics defined by the Standards for Efficient Cryptography Group (SECG).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationhCerticomdnamehD. Brownddatej‘ähfilenamegrfc5915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127784hkeywordsÉbecx*Standards for Efficient Cryptography GroupdSECGkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLégrankingcrfcd5915irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Elliptic Curve Private Key StructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a structure for representing trust anchor information.  A trust anchor is an authoritative entity represented by a public key and associated data.  The public key is used to verify digital signatures, and the associated data is used to constrain the types of information or actions for which the trust anchor is authoritative.  The structures defined in this document are intended to satisfy the format-related requirements defined in Trust Anchor Management Requirements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejS. Ashmore¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallaceddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5914eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127783hkeywordsÅwtrust anchor managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLégrankingcrfcd5914irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesTrust Anchor FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This document defines the syntax and semantics for the Clearance attribute and the Authority Clearance Constraints extension in X.509 certificates.  The Clearance attribute is used to indicate the clearance held by the subject.  The Clearance attribute may appear in the subject directory attributes extension of a public key certificate or in the attributes field of an attribute certificate.  The Authority Clearance Constraints certificate extension values in a Trust Anchor (TA), in Certification Authority (CA) public key certificates, and in an Attribute Authority (AA) public key certificate in a certification path for a given subject constrain the effective Clearance of the subject. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamekS. Chokhaniddatej‘êhfilenamegrfc5913eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127782hkeywordsÅqx.509 certificatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLé
grankingcrfcd5913irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMClearance Attribute and Authority Clearance Constraints Certificate ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„The Public Key Infrastructure using X.509 (PKIX) certificate format, and many associated formats, are expressed using ASN.1.  The current ASN.1 modules conform to the 1988 version of ASN.1.  This document updates those ASN.1 modules to conform to the 2002 version of ASN.1.  There are no bits-on-the-wire changes to any of the formats; this is simply a change to the syntax.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej‘ïhfilenamegrfc5912eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127781hkeywordsÉfS/MIMEdPKIXmASN.1 moduleskobsoletedByÄepagesuopublicationDateLç˙grankingcrfcd5912irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFNew ASN.1 Modules for the Public Key Infrastructure Using X.509 (PKIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9810d6960d9480∂habstractyÕThe Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) format, and many associated formats, are expressed using ASN.1.  The current ASN.1 modules conform to the 1988 version of ASN.1.  This document updates those ASN.1 modules to conform to the 2002 version of ASN.1.  There are no bits-on-the-wire changes to any of the formats; this is simply a change to the syntax.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej‘{hfilenamegrfc5911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127780hkeywordsÉfS/MIMEdPKIXmASN.1 moduleskobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateLçgrankingcrfcd5911irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCNew ASN.1 Modules for Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) and S/MIMEdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6268∂habstracty¥This document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension mapping for the provisioning and management of Domain Name System security (DNSSEC) extensions for domain names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, this mapping extends the EPP domain name mapping to provide additional features required for the provisioning of DNS security extensions.  This document obsoletes RFC 4310. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamehJ. Gould¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej‘ïhfilenamegrfc5910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127779hkeywordsàceppx Extensible Provisioning Protocolcxmlcdnshsecurityfdnssecqdelegation signerbdskobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateKÈ∞[grankingcrfcd5910irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcDomain Name System (DNS) Security Extensions Mapping for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"Neighbor Discovery Proxies are used to provide an address presence on a link for nodes that are no longer present on the link. They allow a node to receive packets directed at its address by allowing another device to perform Neighbor Discovery operations on its behalf.

Neighbor Discovery Proxy is used in Mobile IPv6 and related protocols to provide reachability from nodes on the home network when a Mobile Node is not at home, by allowing the Home Agent to act as proxy. It is also used as a mechanism to allow a global prefix to span multiple links, where proxies act as relays for Neighbor Discovery messages.

Neighbor Discovery Proxy currently cannot be secured using Secure Neighbor Discovery (SEND). Today, SEND assumes that a node advertising an address is the address owner and in possession of appropriate public and private keys for that node. This document describes how existing practice for proxy Neighbor Discovery relates to SEND. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuFrance Telecom OrangednamekJ-M. Combes¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationqNetstar LogicalisdnamehG. Daleyddatej‘ôhfilenamegrfc5909eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymccsidfullxcsi - Cga & Send maIntenancednamevCga & Send maIntenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127778hkeywordsÇdsendxsecure neighbor discoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL3ΩPgrankingcrfcd5909irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Securing Neighbor Discovery Proxy: Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxøThe NTP Server Option for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) provides NTPv4 (Network Time Protocol version 4) server location information to DHCPv6 hosts. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamejR. Gayraud¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelB. Lourdeletddatej‘ìhfilenamegrfc5908eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127777hkeywordsÅx,Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateLÓ{grankingcrfcd5908irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Network Time Protocol (NTP) Server Option for DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Network Time Protocol (NTP) is used in networks of all types and sizes for time synchronization of servers, workstations, and other networked equipment.  As time synchronization is more and more a mission-critical service, standardized means for monitoring and management of this subsystem of a networked host are required to allow operators of such a service to set up a monitoring system that is platform- and vendor-independent.  This document provides a standardized collection of data objects for monitoring the NTP entity of such a network participant and it is part of the NTP version 4 standardization effort. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhMeinbergdnamekH. Gerstung¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Elliott¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. HabermanddatejÕ.hfilenamegrfc5907eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127776hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÓogrankingcrfcd5907irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJDefinitions of Managed Objects for Network Time Protocol Version 4 (NTPv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis memo describes the Autokey security model for authenticating servers to clients using the Network Time Protocol (NTP) and public key cryptography. Its design is based on the premise that IPsec schemes cannot be adopted intact, since that would preclude stateless servers and severely compromise timekeeping accuracy. In addition, Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) schemes presume authenticated time values are always available to enforce certificate lifetimes; however, cryptographically verified timestamps require interaction between the timekeeping and authentication functions.

This memo includes the Autokey requirements analysis, design principles, and protocol specification. A detailed description of the protocol states, events, and transition functions is included. A prototype of the Autokey design based on this memo has been implemented, tested, and documented in the NTP version 4 (NTPv4) software distribution for the Unix, Windows, and Virtual Memory System (VMS) operating systems at http://www.ntp.org. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationkU. DelawarednamehD. Millsddatej‘ìhfilenamegrfc5906eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127775hkeywordsÉcntpentpv4wpublic key cryptographykobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateLÓ\grankingcrfcd5906irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Network Time Protocol Version 4: Autokey SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9748∂habstractyThe Network Time Protocol (NTP) is widely used to synchronize computer clocks in the Internet.  This document describes NTP version 4 (NTPv4), which is backwards compatible with NTP version 3 (NTPv3), described in RFC 1305, as well as previous versions of the protocol.  NTPv4 includes a modified protocol header to accommodate the Internet Protocol version 6 address family.  NTPv4 includes fundamental improvements in the mitigation and discipline algorithms that extend the potential accuracy to the tens of microseconds with modern workstations and fast LANs.  It includes a dynamic server discovery scheme, so that in many cases, specific server configuration is not required.  It corrects certain errors in the NTPv3 design and implementation and includes an optional extension mechanism.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkU. DelawarednamehD. Mills¢kaffiliationcISCdnameiJ. Martin¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamejJ. Burbank¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamehW. Kaschddatej‘ìhfilenamegrfc5905eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcntpdfullxntp - Network Time ProtocolsdnamevNetwork Time Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127774hkeywordsÉcNTPdSNTPoSynchronizationkobsoletedByÄepagesnopublicationDateLÓgrankingcrfcd5905irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFNetwork Time Protocol Version 4: Protocol and Algorithms SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÖd7822d8573d9748d9769d9109∂habstracty(This document defines a set of Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) Attributes that are designed to provide RADIUS support for IEEE 802.16 Privacy Key Management Version 1.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej‘ôhfilenamegrfc5904eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127773hkeywordsÑfRADIUScAAAdIEEEf802.16kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL%]˜grankingcrfcd5904irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[RADIUS Attributes for IEEE 802.16 Privacy Key Management Version 1 (PKMv1) Protocol SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis document describes three Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) groups for use in the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) and Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocols in addition to previously defined groups.  These groups are based on modular arithmetic rather than binary arithmetic.  These groups are defined to align IKE and IKEv2 with other ECC implementations and standards, particularly NIST standards.  In addition, the curves defined here can provide more efficient implementation than previously defined ECC groups.  This document obsoletes RFC 4753.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnameeD. Fu¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejJ. Solinasddatej‘ñhfilenamegrfc5903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127772hkeywordsáxElliptic Curve CryptographycECCuInternet Key Exchangenelliptic curvenDiffie-Hellmangsuite bjnist curvekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≈»grankingcrfcd5903irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCElliptic Curve Groups modulo a Prime (ECP Groups) for IKE and IKEv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyºThere has been much recent discussion on the topic of whether the IETF should develop standards for IPv6 Network Address Translators (NATs).  This document articulates the architectural issues raised by IPv6 NATs, the pros and cons of having IPv6 NATs, and provides the IAB's thoughts on the current open issues and the solution space.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejRon BonicagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Zhang¢kaffiliation`dnamekG. Lebovitzddatej‘òhfilenamegrfc5902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127771hkeywordsÜcNATdIPv6lTransparencyjEnd-to-EndgPrivacykMultihomingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLAmgrankingcrfcd5902irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex0IAB Thoughts on IPv6 Network Address TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document extends the Incident Object Description Exchange Format (IODEF) defined in RFC 5070 to support the reporting of phishing events, which is a particular type of fraud.  These extensions are flexible enough to support information gleaned from activities throughout the entire electronic fraud cycle -- from receipt of the phishing lure to the disablement of the collection site.  Both simple reporting and complete forensic reporting are possible, as is consolidating multiple incidents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxThe Cooper-Cain Group, Inc.dnamegP. Cain¢kaffiliationxThe Anti-Phishing Working GroupdnameiD. Jevansddatej‘óhfilenamegrfc5901eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127770hkeywordsÅx+Incident Object Description Exchange FormatkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateLFvgrankingcrfcd5901irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Extensions to the IODEF-Document Class for Reporting PhishingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document defines a new connectivity precondition for the Session Description Protocol (SDP) precondition framework.  A connectivity precondition can be used to delay session establishment or modification until media stream connectivity has been successfully verified.  The method of verification may vary depending on the type of transport used for the media.  For unreliable datagram transports such as UDP, verification involves probing the stream with data or control packets.  For reliable connection-oriented transports such as TCP, verification can be achieved simply by successful connection establishment or by probing the connection with data or control packets, depending on the situation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Oran¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wingddatej‘óhfilenamegrfc5898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127769hkeywordsÑcSIPmpreconditionsjconnectionlconnectivitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL3ΩFgranking
crfcd5898irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOConnectivity Preconditions for Session Description Protocol (SDP) Media StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈This document considers the problem of service identification in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  Service identification is the process of determining the user-level use case that is driving the signaling being utilized by the user agent (UA).  This document discusses the uses of service identification, and outlines several architectural principles behind the process.  It identifies perils when service identification is not done properly -- including fraud, interoperability failures, and stifling of innovation.  It then outlines a set of recommended practices for service identification.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘ñhfilenamegrfc5897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127768hkeywordsÅvservice identificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≈∫granking	crfcd5897irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRIdentification of Communications Services in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywSeveral Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) applications work in a multi-tiered architecture, where the server takes advantage of delegated user credentials to act on behalf of the user and contact additional servers.  In effect, the server acts as an agent on behalf of the user.  Examples include web applications that need to access e-mail or file servers, including CIFS (Common Internet File System) file servers.  However, delegating the user credentials to a party who is not sufficiently trusted is problematic from a security standpoint.  Kerberos provides a flag called OK-AS-DELEGATE that allows the administrator of a Kerberos realm to communicate that a particular service is trusted for delegation.  This specification adds support for this flag and similar facilities in other authentication mechanisms to GSS-API (RFC 2743). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnametL. Hornquist Astrand¢kaffiliationvPainless Security, LLCdnamejS. HartmanddatejÕ3hfilenamegrfc5896eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127767hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL(˝Ÿgrankingcrfcd5896irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API): Delegate if Approved by PolicydtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty#In the original version of the Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) protocol, any Unicode code points taken from user input were mapped into a set of Unicode code points that "made sense", and then encoded and passed to the domain name system (DNS).  The IDNA2008 protocol (described in RFCs 5890, 5891, 5892, and 5893) presumes that the input to the protocol comes from a set of "permitted" code points, which it then encodes and passes to the DNS, but does not specify what to do with the result of user input.  This document describes the actions that can be taken by an implementation between receiving user input and passing permitted code points to the new IDNA protocol.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqPeter Saint-AndregauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej‘õhfilenamegrfc5895eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127766hkeywordsÖjuser inputqcharacter mappingflocalenuser interfacegUnicodekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLèœFgrankingcrfcd5895irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexQMapping Characters for Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) 2008dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=Several years have passed since the original protocol for Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs) was completed and deployed.  During that time, a number of issues have arisen, including the need to update the system to deal with newer versions of Unicode.  Some of these issues require tuning of the existing protocols and the tables on which they depend.  This document provides an overview of a revised system and provides explanatory material for its components.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘âhfilenamegrfc5894eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgidnabisdfullxAidnabis - Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddnamex7Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddtypebwgbidjdoc-127765hkeywordsÑhIDNA2008cidneasciijcharacterskobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateLYâ]grankingcrfcd5894irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA): Background, Explanation, and RationaledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOThe use of right-to-left scripts in Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs) has presented several challenges.  This memo provides a new Bidi rule for Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA) labels, based on the encountered problems with some scripts and some shortcomings in the 2003 IDNA Bidi criterion. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrand¢kaffiliationx!Swedish Museum of Natural HistorydnamegC. Karpddatej‘êhfilenamegrfc5893eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgidnabisdfullxAidnabis - Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddnamex7Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddtypebwgbidjdoc-127764hkeywordsÖhIDNA2008cidneasciijcharactersdBidikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLYâQgrankingcrfcd5893irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRight-to-Left Scripts for Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document specifies rules for deciding whether a code point, considered in isolation or in context, is a candidate for inclusion in an Internationalized Domain Name (IDN).

It is part of the specification of Internationalizing Domain Names in Applications 2008 (IDNA2008). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelP. Faltstromddatej‘Ühfilenamegrfc5892eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgidnabisdfullxAidnabis - Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddnamex7Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddtypebwgbidjdoc-127763hkeywordsÖdIDNAcDNScIDNgUnicodehIDNA2008kobsoletedByÄepagesFopublicationDateLYâFgrankingcrfcd5892irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRThe Unicode Code Points and Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8753∂habstracty˛This document is the revised protocol definition for Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs).  The rationale for changes, the relationship to the older specification, and important terminology are provided in other documents.  This document specifies the protocol mechanism, called Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA), for registering and looking up IDNs in a way that does not require changes to the DNS itself.  IDNA is only meant for processing domain names, not free text. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘Ühfilenamegrfc5891eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgidnabisdfullxAidnabis - Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddnamex7Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddtypebwgbidjdoc-127762hkeywordsÖhIDNA2008cidneasciijcharactersqidna applicationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLYâ:grankingcrfcd5891irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA): ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document is one of a collection that, together, describe the protocol and usage context for a revision of Internationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA), superseding the earlier version.  It describes the document collection and provides definitions and other material that are common to the set. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘Ühfilenamegrfc5890eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgidnabisdfullxAidnabis - Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddnamex7Internationalized Domain Names in Applications, Reviseddtypebwgbidjdoc-127761hkeywordsÑhIDNA2008cidneasciijcharacterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLYâ/grankingcrfcd5890irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZInternationalized Domain Names for Applications (IDNA): Definitions and Document FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a model for configuring IP addresses and subnet prefixes on the interfaces of routers which connect to links with undetermined connectivity properties.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamekE. Baccelli¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Townsleyddatej‘ñhfilenamegrfc5889eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhautoconfdfullx+autoconf - Ad-Hoc Network Autoconfigurationdnamex Ad-Hoc Network Autoconfigurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127760hkeywordsànmobile networknad hoc networkeMANETtnetwork architecturetaddressing frameworkmconfigurationgroutingkIP networkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÜf¬grankingcrfcd5889irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&IP Addressing Model in Ad Hoc NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓIn this specification, we define a framework to group "m" lines in the Session Description Protocol (SDP) for different purposes.  This framework uses the "group" and "mid" SDP attributes, both of which are defined in this specification.  Additionally, we specify how to use the framework for two different purposes: for lip synchronization and for receiving a media flow consisting of several media streams on different transport addresses.  This document obsoletes RFC 3388. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej‘íhfilenamegrfc5888eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127759hkeywordsÉcSDPhgroupingcSIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≈¨granking␀crfcd5888irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The Session Description Protocol (SDP) Grouping FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9143d8843∂habstractyúThis document reviews the existing mechanisms for site renumbering for both IPv4 and IPv6, and it identifies operational issues with those mechanisms.  It also summarises current technical proposals for additional mechanisms.  Finally, there is a gap analysis identifying possible areas for future work.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqUniv. of AucklanddnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnamekR. Atkinson¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnameiH. FlinckddatejÕ3hfilenamegrfc5887eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127758hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateK˛πggrankingˇcrfcd5887irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRenumbering Still Needs WorkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåA Path Computation Element (PCE)-based architecture has been specified for the computation of Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks in the context of single or multiple domains (where a domain refers to a collection of network elements within a common sphere of address management or path computational responsibility such as Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) areas and Autonomous Systems). Path Computation Clients (PCCs) send computation requests to PCEs, and these may forward the requests to and cooperate with other PCEs forming a "path computation chain".

In PCE-based environments, it is thus critical to monitor the state of the path computation chain for troubleshooting and performance monitoring purposes: liveness of each element (PCE) involved in the PCE chain and detection of potential resource contention states and statistics in terms of path computation times are examples of such metrics of interest. This document specifies procedures and extensions to the Path Computation Element Protocol (PCEP) in order to gather such information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnamejY. IkejiriddatejÕ2hfilenamegrfc5886eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127757hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÌÔgranking˛crfcd5886irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOA Set of Monitoring Tools for Path Computation Element (PCE)-Based ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document describes Connectivity Verification (CV) Types using Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) with Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV).  VCCV provides a control channel that is associated with a pseudowire (PW), as well as the corresponding operations and management functions such as connectivity verification to be used over that control channel. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationbBTdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataroddatej‘khfilenamegrfc5885eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127756hkeywordsÖjPseudowiredVCCVcBFDhVCCV-BFDfPW OAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLaˇgranking˝crfcd5885irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for the Pseudowire Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6478d7885∂habstractyaOne desirable application of Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) is to detect a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Path (LSP) data plane failure.  LSP Ping is an existing mechanism for detecting MPLS data plane failures and for verifying the MPLS LSP data plane against the control plane.  BFD can be used for the former, but not for the latter.  However, the control plane processing required for BFD Control packets is relatively smaller than the processing required for LSP Ping messages.  A combination of LSP Ping and BFD can be used to provide faster data plane failure detection and/or make it possible to provide such detection on a greater number of LSPs.  This document describes the applicability of BFD in relation to LSP Ping for this application.  It also describes procedures for using BFD in this environment. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationbBTdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallowddatej‘#hfilenamegrfc5884eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127755hkeywordsÇxMultiprotocol Label Switchinghlsp pingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLaÚgranking¸crfcd5884irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7726∂habstractxüThis document describes the use of the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol over multihop paths, including unidirectional links. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. WardddatejÃŒhfilenamegrfc5883eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127754hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLaÂgranking˚crfcd5883irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for Multihop PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx{This document describes the generic application of the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. WardddatejÃŒhfilenamegrfc5882eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127753hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLaŸgranking˙crfcd5882irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Generic Application of Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxëThis document describes the use of the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol over IPv4 and IPv6 for single IP hops. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. WardddatejÕhfilenamegrfc5881eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127752hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLaŒgranking˘crfcd5881irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGBidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) for IPv4 and IPv6 (Single Hop)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document describes a protocol intended to detect faults in the bidirectional path between two forwarding engines, including interfaces, data link(s), and to the extent possible the forwarding engines themselves, with potentially very low latency.  It operates independently of media, data protocols, and routing protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. WardddatejÕ3hfilenamegrfc5880eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcbfddfullx(bfd - Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondnamex"Bidirectional Forwarding Detectiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127751hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateLaƒgranking¯crfcd5880irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÑd8562d9747d7419d7880∂habstracty`This document describes a set of heuristics for distinguishing IPsec ESP-NULL (Encapsulating Security Payload without encryption) packets from encrypted ESP packets.  These heuristics can be used on intermediate devices, like traffic analyzers, and deep-inspection engines, to quickly decide whether or not a given packet flow is encrypted, i.e., whether or not it can be inspected.  Use of these heuristics does not require any changes made on existing IPsec hosts that are compliant with RFC 4303.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoAuthenTec, Inc.dnamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnamekD. McDonaldddatej‘ñhfilenamegrfc5879eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127750hkeywordsÑeIPsecrWrapped ESP (WESP)odeep-inspectionqpacket inspectionkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateK˛πqgranking˜crfcd5879irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Heuristics for Detecting ESP-NULL PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This document specifies authorization extensions to the Transport Layer Security (TLS) Handshake Protocol.  Extensions are carried in the client and server hello messages to confirm that both parties support the desired authorization data types.  Then, if supported by both the client and the server, authorization information, such as attribute certificates (ACs) or Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) assertions, is exchanged in the supplemental data handshake message.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqRedPhone SecuritydnamehM. Brown¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘thfilenamegrfc5878eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127749hkeywordsÅrhandshake protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK‚Îgrankingˆcrfcd5878irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Transport Layer Security (TLS) Authorization ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8447d8996∂habstractx€This document specifies a MIME media type used to carry a single attribute certificate as defined in RFC 5755.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsVigil Security, LLCdnamejR. HousleyddatejÕ+hfilenamegrfc5877eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127748hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK‚␀grankingıcrfcd5877irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDThe application/pkix-attr-cert Media Type for Attribute CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) has a mechanism for conveying the identity of the originator of a request by means of the P-Asserted-Identity and P-Preferred-Identity header fields.  These header fields are specified for use in requests using a number of SIP methods, in particular the INVITE method.  However, RFC 3325 does not specify the insertion of the P-Asserted-Identity header field by a trusted User Agent Client (UAC), does not specify the use of P-Asserted-Identity and P-Preferred-Identity header fields with certain SIP methods such as UPDATE, REGISTER, MESSAGE, and PUBLISH, and does not specify how to handle an unexpected number of URIs or unexpected URI schemes in these header fields.  This document extends RFC 3325 to cover these situations.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx!Siemens Enterprise CommunicationsdnameiJ. Elwellddatej‘íhfilenamegrfc5876eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127747hkeywordsÇcSIPsP-Asserted-IdentitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK÷&ÂgrankingÙcrfcd5876irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEUpdates to Asserted Identity in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document describes an "xcap-diff" SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) event package for the SIP Event Notification Framework, which clients can use to receive notifications of changes to Extensible Markup Language (XML) Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP) resources.  The initial synchronization information exchange and document updates are based on the XCAP Diff format. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemJ. Urpalainen¢kaffiliationuSoftarmor Systems LLCdnameiD. Willisddatej‘khfilenamegrfc5875eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127746hkeywordsÇixcap-diffixcap diffkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÙà'grankingÛcrfcd5875irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[An Extensible Markup Language (XML) Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP) Diff Event PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This specification defines a document format that can be used to indicate that a change has occurred in a document managed by the Extensible Markup Language (XML) Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP).  This format reports which document has changed and its former and new entity tags.  It can report the differences between versions of the document, using an XML patch format.  It can report existing element and attribute content when versions of an XCAP server document change.  XCAP diff documents can be delivered to diff clients using a number of means, including a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) event package. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemJ. Urpalainenddatej‘îhfilenamegrfc5874eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127745hkeywordsÇcSIPqInstant MessagingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÙàgrankingÚcrfcd5874irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÅAn Extensible Markup Language (XML) Document Format for Indicating a Change in XML Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP) ResourcesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document defines an extension to the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) for proactively establishing a PANA Security Association between a PANA Client in one access network and a PANA Authentication Agent in another access network to which the PANA Client may move.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. YeginddatejÕ/hfilenamegrfc5873eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127744hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÌ◊ËgrankingÒcrfcd5873irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaPre-Authentication Support for the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx~This document relaxes the IANA rules for the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. YeginddatejÕ-hfilenamegrfc5872eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127743hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÈ∞Ngrankingcrfcd5872irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQIANA Rules for the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxéThis document specifies the IANA guidelines for allocating new values for the Routing Type field in the IPv6 Routing Header. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej‘òhfilenamegrfc5871eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127742hkeywordsÅrrouting type fieldkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÈØÒgrankingÔcrfcd5871irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6IANA Allocation Guidelines for the IPv6 Routing HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document specifies a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) for geographic locations using the 'geo\' scheme name.  A 'geo' URI identifies a physical location in a two- or three-dimensional coordinate reference system in a compact, simple, human-readable, and protocol-independent way.  The default coordinate reference system used is the World Geodetic System 1984 (WGS-84). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeIPComdnamelA. Mayrhofer¢kaffiliation`dnamekC. Spanringddatej‘õhfilenamegrfc5870eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127741hkeywordsÑigeographycgeocurifschemekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL^•grankingÓcrfcd5870irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBA Uniform Resource Identifier for Geographic Locations ('geo' URI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document specifies a simple Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC)-based key derivation function (HKDF), which can be used as a building block in various protocols and applications.  The key derivation function (KDF) is intended to support a wide range of applications and requirements, and is conservative in its use of cryptographic hash functions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlIBM ResearchdnamekH. Krawczyk¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. EronenddatejÕ*hfilenamegrfc5869eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127740hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÙàgrankingÌcrfcd5869irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<HMAC-based Extract-and-Expand Key Derivation Function (HKDF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document provides background information regarding large-scale Kerberos deployments in the industrial sector, with the aim of identifying issues in the current Kerberos cross-realm authentication model as defined in RFC 4120.

This document describes some examples of actual large-scale industrial systems, and lists requirements and restrictions regarding authentication operations in such environments. It also identifies a number of requirements derived from the industrial automation field. Although they are found in the field of industrial automation, these requirements are general enough and are applicable to the problem of Kerberos cross-realm operations. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwYokogawa Electric Corp.dnameiS. Sakane¢kaffiliationwYokogawa Electric Corp.dnameiK. Kamada¢kaffiliationwYokogawa Electric Corp.dnameiS. Zrelli¢kaffiliationmToshiba Corp.dnamekM. IshiyamaddatejÕ1hfilenamegrfc5868eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-127739hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK˝¿©grankingÏcrfcd5868irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Problem Statement on the Cross-Realm Operation of KerberosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThe Routing Over Low-Power and Lossy (ROLL) networks Working Group has been chartered to work on routing solutions for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (LLNs) in various markets: industrial, commercial (building), home, and urban networks.  Pursuant to this effort, this document defines the IPv6 routing requirements for building automation.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuJohnson Controls Inc.dnamekJ. Martocci¢kaffiliationwGhent University - IBCNdnameiP. De Mil¢kaffiliationrSchneider ElectricdnamegN. Riou¢kaffiliationsArts Centre VooruitdnamelW. VermeylenddatejÕ/hfilenamegrfc5867eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127738hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≈ügrankingÎcrfcd5867irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHBuilding Automation Routing Requirements in Low-Power and Lossy NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]This document describes the framework, messages, and procedures for the Diameter Quality-of-Service (QoS) application.  The Diameter QoS application allows network elements to interact with Diameter servers when allocating QoS resources in the network.  In particular, two modes of operation, namely "Pull" and "Push", are defined. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamefD. Sun¢kaffiliationmMotorola LabsdnameiP. McCann¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegT. Tsou¢kaffiliationxLulea University of TechnologydnamehA. Doria¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej‘íhfilenamegrfc5866eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127737hkeywordsÜhDiametercAAAcQoSfPolicydVoIPcSIPkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateKÌ◊’grankingÍcrfcd5866irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Diameter Quality-of-Service ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document requests one Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) from the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) for a class of real-time traffic.  This traffic class conforms to the Expedited Forwarding Per-Hop Behavior.  This traffic is also admitted by the network using a Call Admission Control (CAC) procedure involving authentication, authorization, and capacity admission.  This differs from a real-time traffic class that conforms to the Expedited Forwarding Per-Hop Behavior but is not subject to capacity admission or subject to very coarse capacity admission. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamehM. Dollyddatej‘àhfilenamegrfc5865eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127736hkeywordsÅqreal-time traffickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÈØ·grankingÈcrfcd5865irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIA Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) for Capacity-Admitted TrafficdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This document specifies how to use DNS (Domain Name Service) SRV RRs (Resource Records) to locate services for the AFS distributed file system and how the priority and weight values of the SRV RR should be interpreted in the server ranking system used by AFS.  It updates RFC 1183 to deprecate the use of the AFSDB RR to locate AFS cell database servers and provides guidance for backward compatibility. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamejR. Allberyddatej‘ëhfilenamegrfc5864eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127735hkeywordsÑrdomain name systemfsrv rrwdistributed file systemhafsdb rrkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateKŒ?wgrankingËcrfcd5864irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex DNS SRV Resource Records for AFSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstracty£DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) allows an organization to claim responsibility for transmitting a message, in a way that can be validated by a recipient.  The organization can be the author's, the originating sending site, an intermediary, or one of their agents.  A message can contain multiple signatures, from the same or different organizations involved with the message.  DKIM defines a domain-level digital signature authentication framework for email, using public key cryptography and using the domain name service as its key server technology.  This permits verification of a responsible organization, as well as the integrity of the message content.  DKIM will also provide a mechanism that permits potential email signers to publish information about their email signing practices; this will permit email receivers to make additional assessments about messages.  DKIM's authentication of email identity can assist in the global control of "spam" and "phishing".  This document provides implementation, deployment, operational, and migration considerations for DKIM.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiE. Siegel¢kaffiliationxDefault Deny Security, Inc.dnameoP. Hallam-Baker¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej‘íhfilenamegrfc5863eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-127734hkeywordsÑeEmailoElectronic MailmInternet MailtMessage VerificationkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateK˛π\grankingÁcrfcd5863irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Development, Deployment, and OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛The Path Computation Element (PCE) provides path computation functions in support of traffic engineering in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks.

Extensions to the MPLS and GMPLS signaling and routing protocols have been made in support of point-to-multipoint (P2MP) Traffic Engineered (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs). The use of PCE in MPLS networks is already established, and since P2MP TE LSP routes are sometimes complex to compute, it is likely that PCE will be used for P2MP LSPs.

Generic requirements for a communication protocol between Path Computation Clients (PCCs) and PCEs are presented in RFC 4657, "Path Computation Element (PCE) Communication Protocol Generic Requirements". This document complements the generic requirements and presents a detailed set of PCC-PCE communication protocol requirements for point-to-multipoint MPLS/GMPLS traffic engineering. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamekS. Yasukawa¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘ëhfilenamegrfc5862eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127733hkeywordsÇdmplsegmplskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLçÊgrankingÊcrfcd5862irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlPath Computation Clients (PCC) - Path Computation Element (PCE) Requirements for Point-to-Multipoint MPLS-TEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÓThis document defines two independent HTTP Cache-Control extensions that allow control over the use of stale responses by caches.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. DusseaultgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkYahoo! Inc.dnamemM. NottinghamddatejÃëhfilenamegrfc5861eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127732hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK‚∆grankingÂcrfcd5861irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex/HTTP Cache-Control Extensions for Stale ContentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÏThis document lists architectural and functional requirements for the Operations, Administration, and Maintenance of MPLS Transport Profile.  These requirements apply to pseudowires, Label Switched Paths, and Sections. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelM. Vigoureux¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationxM. C. Betts Consulting Ltd.dnamehM. Bettsddatej‘Éhfilenamegrfc5860eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127731hkeywordsÇgMPLS-TPcOAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÛ&˜granking‰crfcd5860irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Requirements for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) in MPLS Transport NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis memo documents existing usage for the "TFTP Server Address" option.  The option number currently in use is 150.  This memo documents the current usage of the option in agreement with RFC 3942, which declares that any pre-existing usages of option numbers in the range 128-223 should be documented, and the Dynamic Host Configuration working group will try to officially assign those numbers to those options.  The option is defined for DHCPv4 and works only with IPv4 addresses.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Johnsonddatej‘Éhfilenamegrfc5859eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127730hkeywordsÅdvoipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateLÌ·granking„crfcd5859irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%TFTP Server Address Option for DHCPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñIntegrating Robust Header Compression (ROHC) with IPsec (ROHCoIPsec) offers the combined benefits of IP security services and efficient bandwidth utilization.  However, in order to integrate ROHC with IPsec, extensions to the Security Policy Database (SPD) and Security Association Database (SAD) are required.  This document describes the IPsec extensions required to support ROHCoIPsec. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamejE. Ertekin¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamekC. Christou¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej‘çhfilenamegrfc5858eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127729hkeywordsÇdROHCjROHCoIPseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÌ◊∆granking‚crfcd5858irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@IPsec Extensions to Support Robust Header Compression over IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùIn order to integrate Robust Header Compression (ROHC) with IPsec, a mechanism is needed to signal ROHC channel parameters between endpoints.  Internet Key Exchange (IKE) is a mechanism that can be leveraged to exchange these parameters.  This document specifies extensions to IKEv2 that will allow ROHC and its associated channel parameters to be signaled for IPsec Security Associations (SAs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamejE. Ertekin¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamekC. Christou¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnameiR. Jasani¢kaffiliationoAuthenTec, Inc.dnamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej‘ähfilenamegrfc5857eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127728hkeywordsÇdROHCjROHCoIPseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÌ◊ßgranking·crfcd5857irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@IKEv2 Extensions to Support Robust Header Compression over IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#IP Security (IPsec) provides various security services for IP traffic.  However, the benefits of IPsec come at the cost of increased overhead.  This document outlines a framework for integrating Robust Header Compression (ROHC) over IPsec (ROHCoIPsec).  By compressing the inner headers of IP packets, ROHCoIPsec proposes to reduce the amount of overhead associated with the transmission of traffic over IPsec Security Associations (SAs).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamejE. Ertekin¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnameiR. Jasani¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamekC. Christou¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej‘ähfilenamegrfc5856eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127727hkeywordsÇdROHCjROHCoIPseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÌ◊õgranking‡crfcd5856irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIIntegration of Robust Header Compression over IPsec Security AssociationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document specifies a stable naming scheme for the nameservers that serve the zones IN-ADDR.ARPA and IP6.ARPA in the DNS.  These zones contain data that facilitate reverse mapping (address to name).  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationeICANNdnamelT. Mandersonddatej‘èhfilenamegrfc5855eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127726hkeywordsÉlIN-ADDR.ARPAhIP6.ARPAoreverse mappingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK˙ú3grankingﬂcrfcd5855irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberõetotaletitlex+Nameservers for IPv4 and IPv6 Reverse ZonesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies Metalink, an XML-based download description format.  Metalink describes download locations (mirrors), cryptographic hashes, and other information.  Clients can transparently use this information to reliably transfer files. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Bryan¢kaffiliation`dnamelT. Tsujikawa¢kaffiliation`dnamehN. McNab¢kaffiliationkMirrorBraindnamehP. Poemlddatej‘ëhfilenamegrfc5854eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127725hkeywordsãmfile transfergmirrorsndata integritydhashcxmldhttpxhypertext transfer protocolcftpvfile transfer protocolhmetadatagtorrentkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateLa∏grankingﬁcrfcd5854irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(The Metalink Download Description FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThis document describes functions implemented in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) intermediaries known as Session Border Controllers (SBCs).  The goal of this document is to describe the commonly provided functions of SBCs.  A special focus is given to those practices that are viewed to be in conflict with SIP architectural principles.  This document also explores the underlying requirements of network operators that have led to the use of these functions and practices in order to identify protocol requirements and determine whether those requirements are satisfied by existing specifications or if additional standards work is required.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemJ. Hautakorpi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationkAcme PacketdnamekR. Penfield¢kaffiliationkSkype, Inc.dnamenA. Hawrylyshen¢kaffiliationg3CLogicdnameiM. BhatiaddatejÕ(hfilenamegrfc5853eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127724hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK⁄granking›crfcd5853irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Requirements from Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Session Border Control (SBC) DeploymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRIn a transport network scenario, Data Plane connections controlled by either a Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Control Plane (Soft Permanent Connections - SPC) or a Management System (Permanent Connections - PC) may independently coexist. The ability of transforming an existing PC into an SPC and vice versa -- without actually affecting Data Plane traffic being carried over it -- is a requirement. The requirements for the conversion between permanent connections and switched connections in a GMPLS Network are defined in RFC 5493.

This memo describes an extension to GMPLS Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling that enables the transfer of connection ownership between the Management and the Control Planes. Such a transfer is referred to as a Handover. This document defines all Handover-related procedures. This includes the handling of failure conditions and subsequent reversion to original state. A basic premise of the extension is that the Handover procedures must never impact an already established Data Plane connection. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemD. Ceccarelli¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Bramanti¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationoFujitsu NetworkdnamekS. Bardalaiddatej‘ëhfilenamegrfc5852eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127723hkeywordsÇxresource reservation protocolshandover procedureskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK÷&◊granking‹crfcd5852irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄRSVP-TE Signaling Extension for LSP Handover from the Management Plane to the Control Plane in a GMPLS-Enabled Transport NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe purpose of this document is to define a framework for an Access Node Control Mechanism between a Network Access Server (NAS) and an Access Node (e.g., a Digital Subscriber Line Access Multiplexer (DSLAM)) in a multi-service reference architecture in order to perform operations related to service, quality of service, and subscribers. The Access Node Control Mechanism will ensure that the transmission of the information does not need to go through distinct element managers but rather uses a direct device-device communication. This allows for performing access-link-related operations within those network elements, while avoiding impact on the existing Operational Support Systems.

This document first identifies a number of use cases for which the Access Node Control Mechanism may be appropriate. It then presents the requirements for the Access Node Control Protocol (ANCP) that must be taken into account during protocol design. Finally, it describes requirements for the network elements that need to support ANCP and the described use cases. These requirements should be seen as guidelines rather than as absolute requirements. RFC 2119 therefore does not apply to the nodal requirements. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehS. Ooghe¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehN. Voigt¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamejM. Platnic¢kaffiliationpDeutsche TelekomdnamegT. Haag¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiS. Wadhwaddatej‘èhfilenamegrfc5851eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdancpdfullx#ancp - Access Node Control ProtocoldnamexAccess Node Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127722hkeywordsáxAccess Node Control ProtocolrTopology DiscoveryrLoop ConfigurationxRemote Connectivity TestiMulticastkAccess NodeuNetwork Access ServerkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateK‚‘granking€crfcd5851irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcFramework and Requirements for an Access Node Control Mechanism in Broadband Multi-Service NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a framework and the requirements for call control and multi-party usage of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  To enable discussion of multi-party features and applications, we define an abstract call model for describing the media relationships required by many of these.  The model and actions described here are specifically chosen to be independent of the SIP signaling and/or mixing approach chosen to actually set up the media relationships.  In addition to its dialog manipulation aspect, this framework includes requirements for communicating related information and events such as conference and session state and session history.  This framework also describes other goals that embody the spirit of SIP applications as used on the Internet such as the definition of primitives (not services), invoker and participant oriented primitives, signaling and mixing model independence, and others.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationgTekelekdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationfSIP EZdnameiD. Petrie¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnstonddatej‘ähfilenamegrfc5850eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127721hkeywordsälcall controljmultipartyhfeaturesfmixingereferd3pcclRefer methoduReplaces header fieldqJoin header fieldlconferencingkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateK˝¿∑granking⁄crfcd5850irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXA Call Control and Multi-Party Usage Framework for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆OAuth provides a method for clients to access server resources on behalf of a resource owner (such as a different client or an end-user).  It also provides a process for end-users to authorize third-party access to their server resources without sharing their credentials (typically, a username and password pair), using user-agent redirections.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameoE. Hammer-Lahavddatej‘çhfilenamegrfc5849eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127720hkeywordsÇmauthorizationjdelegationkobsoletedByÅd6749epages&opublicationDateKŒ?ûgrankingŸcrfcd5849irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevThe OAuth 1.0 ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document describes a mechanism to add origin authentication, message integrity, replay resistance, message sequencing, and detection of missing messages to the transmitted syslog messages.  This specification is intended to be used in conjunction with the work defined in RFC 5424, "The Syslog Protocol". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnameiJ. Kelsey¢kaffiliationoPGP CorporationdnameiJ. Callas¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Clemmddatej‘éhfilenamegrfc5848eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsyslogdfullx1syslog - Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdnamex(Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127719hkeywordsÇfsyslogksyslog-signkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateKÈØŒgrankingÿcrfcd5848irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevSigned Syslog MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyProxy Mobile IPv6 (PMIPv6) is a network-based mobility management protocol.  The mobility entities involved in the Proxy Mobile IPv6 protocol, the mobile access gateway (MAG) and the local mobility anchor (LMA), set up tunnels dynamically to manage mobility for a mobile node within the Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain.  This document describes a heartbeat mechanism between the MAG and the LMA to detect failures, quickly inform peers in the event of a recovery from node failures, and allow a peer to take appropriate action. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhWiChorusdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Koodli¢kaffiliationeStokednamefH. Lim¢kaffiliationeStokednamegN. Kant¢kaffiliationmQualcomm Inc.dnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationmQualcomm Inc.dnamekJ. Laganierddatej‘Uhfilenamegrfc5847eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127718hkeywordsÉqNode ReachabilityhRestartsqFailure DetectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL≈ëgranking◊crfcd5847irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Heartbeat Mechanism for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©This document defines a binding revocation mechanism to terminate a mobile node's mobility session and the associated resources.  This mechanism can be used both with base Mobile IPv6 and its extensions, such as Proxy Mobile IPv6.  The mechanism allows the mobility entity which initiates the revocation procedure to request its peer to terminate either one, multiple or all specified Binding Cache entries. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Muhanna¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiM. Khalil¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiP. Yeganiddatej‘|hfilenamegrfc5846eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127717hkeywordsãePMIP6fPMIPv6xBinding Revocation IndicationcBRIx"Binding Revocation AcknowledgementcBRAdMIP6fDSMIP6xMultiple Care-of AddressesqPMIPv6 RevocationvBulk PMIPv6 RevocationkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateL≈Ögranking÷crfcd5846irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Binding Revocation for IPv6 MobilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€This specification defines a new mobility option for allowing the mobile access gateway and the local mobility anchor to negotiate Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) encapsulation mode and exchange the downlink and uplink GRE keys that are used for marking the downlink and uplink traffic that belong to a specific mobility session.  In addition, the same mobility option can be used to negotiate the GRE encapsulation mode without exchanging the GRE keys. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Muhanna¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiM. Khalil¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leungddatej‘Thfilenamegrfc5845eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127716hkeywordsÜePMIP6fPMIPv6pDownlink GRE KeynUplink GRE KeytTLV-Header TunnelingpGRE Key ExchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL^êgranking’crfcd5845irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDGeneric Routing Encapsulation (GRE) Key Option for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis document specifies extensions to the Proxy Mobile IPv6 protocol for adding IPv4 protocol support.  The scope of IPv4 protocol support is two-fold: 1) enable IPv4 home address mobility support to the mobile node, and 2) allow the mobility entities in the Proxy Mobile IPv6 domain to exchange signaling messages over an IPv4 transport network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjToyota ITCdnamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelliddatej‘îhfilenamegrfc5844eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127715hkeywordsÖmNAT traversaljDual StackhMobilitylIPv4 SupportwIPv4 Support for PMIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateK˘ˆ»granking‘crfcd5844irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"IPv4 Support for Proxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚The IANA registry named "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Digest Algorithm Values" defines values for digest algorithms used by Instance Digests in HTTP.  Instance Digests in HTTP provide a digest, also known as a checksum or hash, of an entire representation of the current state of a resource.  This document adds new values to the registry and updates previous values.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Bryanddatej‘åhfilenamegrfc5843eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127714hkeywordsÉxHypertext Transfer ProtocoldHTTPx'Digest Algorithm Values registry updatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKŒ?lgranking”crfcd5843irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Additional Hash Algorithms for HTTP Instance DigestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis specification defines bindings, and the BIND method for creating multiple bindings to the same resource.  Creating a new binding to a resource causes at least one new URI to be mapped to that resource.  Servers are required to ensure the integrity of any bindings that they allow to be created.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehG. Clemm¢kaffiliationlIBM ResearchdnamekJ. Crawford¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschke¢kaffiliationoU.C. Santa CruzdnamelJ. Whiteheadddatej‘àhfilenamegrfc5842eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127713hkeywordsÑdHTTPfWebDAVkcollectionsihard linkkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateKŒ?ìgranking“crfcd5842irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGBinding Extensions to Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx´This document proposes a new TCP option to denote packet mood.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamefR. Hay¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnameiW. TurkalddatejÕ9hfilenamegrfc5841eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43475hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateK¥ŸÏgranking—crfcd5841irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex TCP Option to Denote Packet MooddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document describes the Wrapped Encapsulating Security Payload (WESP) protocol, which builds on the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) RFC 4303 and is designed to allow intermediate devices to (1) ascertain if data confidentiality is being employed within ESP, and if not, (2) inspect the IPsec packets for network monitoring and access control functions.  Currently, in the IPsec ESP standard, there is no deterministic way to differentiate between encrypted and unencrypted payloads by simply examining a packet.  This poses certain challenges to the intermediate devices that need to deep inspect the packet before making a decision on what should be done with that packet (Inspect and/or Allow/Drop).  The mechanism described in this document can be used to easily disambiguate integrity-only ESP from ESP-encrypted packets, without compromising on the security provided by ESP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnameiK. Grewal¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatiaddatej‘çhfilenamegrfc5840eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127712hkeywordsÅdwespkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKŒ?Ügranking–crfcd5840irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCWrapped Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) for Traffic VisibilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) events framework enables receiving asynchronous notification of various events from other SIP user agents.  This framework defines the procedures for creating, refreshing, and terminating subscriptions, as well as fetching and periodic polling of resource state.  These procedures provide no tools to avoid replaying event notifications that have already been received by a user agent.  This memo defines an extension to SIP events that allows the subscriber to condition the subscription request to whether the state has changed since the previous notification was received.  When such a condition is true, either the body of a resulting event notification or the entire notification message is suppressed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehA. Niemi¢kaffiliationqSoftarmor SystemsdnameiD. Willisddatej‘çhfilenamegrfc5839eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsipcoredfullx*sipcore - Session Initiation Protocol Corednamex Session Initiation Protocol Coredtypebwgbidjdoc-127711hkeywordsÉjSIP eventslsubnot-etagsloptimizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK‚grankingœcrfcd5839irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[An Extension to Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Events for Conditional Event NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document describes a mechanism for supporting multiple address families (AFs) in OSPFv3 using multiple instances.  It maps an AF to an OSPFv3 instance using the Instance ID field in the OSPFv3 packet header.  This approach is fairly simple and minimizes extensions to OSPFv3 for supporting multiple AFs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Mirtorabi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwalddatej‘ähfilenamegrfc5838eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127710hkeywordsÇbafkinstance idkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKŒ?™grankingŒcrfcd5838irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Support of Address Families in OSPFv3dtypecrfciupdatedByÑd6969d7949d9454d8362∂habstractyÊThis memo defines a data structure that can be appended to selected ICMP messages. The ICMP extension defined herein can be used to identify any combination of the following: the IP interface upon which a datagram arrived, the sub-IP component of an IP interface upon which a datagram arrived, the IP interface through which the datagram would have been forwarded had it been forwardable, and the IP next hop to which the datagram would have been forwarded.

Devices can use this ICMP extension to identify interfaces and their components by any combination of the following: ifIndex, IPv4 address, IPv6 address, name, and MTU. ICMP-aware devices can use these extensions to identify both numbered and unnumbered interfaces. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliation`dnamejJR. Riversddatej‘çhfilenamegrfc5837eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127709hkeywordsÅx!Internet Control Message ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKŒ?`grankingÕcrfcd5837irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Extending ICMP for Interface and Next-Hop IdentificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) early authentication may be defined as the use of EAP by a mobile device to establish authenticated keying material on a target attachment point prior to its arrival.  This document discusses the EAP early authentication problem in detail.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeQ. Wu¢kaffiliationkNetwork ZendnamegG. Zornddatej‘åhfilenamegrfc5836eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127708hkeywordsÅxeap early authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK÷&¬grankingÃcrfcd5836irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Early Authentication Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This memo describes a detailed framework for composing and aggregating metrics (both in time and in space) originally defined by the IP Performance Metrics (IPPM), RFC 2330, and developed by the IETF.  This new framework memo describes the generic composition and aggregation mechanisms.  The memo provides a basis for additional documents that implement the framework to define detailed compositions and aggregations of metrics that are useful in practice.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameqS. Van den BergheddatejÕhfilenamegrfc5835eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127707hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK√P`grankingÀcrfcd5835irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Framework for Metric CompositiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling the Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) protocol for IEEE 802.11 wireless binding.  This MIB module is presented as a basis for future work on the management of the CAPWAP protocol using the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHangzhou H3C Tech. Co., Ltd.dnamefY. Shi¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Perkins¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Elliott¢kaffiliationnFortinet, Inc.dnamehY. Zhangddatej‘êhfilenamegrfc5834eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127706hkeywordsÇcmibpCAPWAP-DOT11-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK‚ïgranking crfcd5834irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Protocol Binding MIB for IEEE 802.11dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols.  In particular, it describes the managed objects for modeling the Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Protocol.  This MIB module is presented as a basis for future work on the SNMP management of the CAPWAP protocol.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxHangzhou H3C Tech. Co., Ltd.dnamefY. Shi¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Perkins¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Elliott¢kaffiliationnFortinet, Inc.dnamehY. Zhangddatej‘êhfilenamegrfc5833eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127705hkeywordsÇcmiboCAPWAP-BASE-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDateK‚sgranking…crfcd5833irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMControl and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Protocol Base MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyπThis document is intended to be a source of information about the Russian Federal standard for digital signatures (GOST R 34.10-2001), which is one of the Russian cryptographic standard algorithms (called GOST algorithms).  Recently, Russian cryptography is being used in Internet applications, and this document has been created as information for developers and users of GOST R 34.10-2001 for digital signature generation and verification.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCryptocom, Ltd.dnamekV. Dolmatovddatej‘ghfilenamegrfc5832eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127704hkeywordsÑxrussian federal standardqdigital signaturexrussian cryptographic standardtrussian cryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKûÃÚgranking»crfcd5832irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.GOST R 34.10-2001: Digital Signature AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7091µhabstracty¸This document is intended to be a source of information about the Russian Federal standard hash function (GOST R 34.11-94), which is one of the Russian cryptographic standard algorithms (called GOST algorithms).  Recently, Russian cryptography is being used in Internet applications, and this document has been created as information for developers and users of GOST R 34.11-94 for hash computation.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCryptocom, Ltd.dnamekV. Dolmatovddatej‘ghfilenamegrfc5831eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127703hkeywordsÉxrussian federal standardxrussian cryptographic standardtrussian cryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKûÃÊgranking«crfcd5831irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex(GOST R 34.11-94: Hash Function AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6986µhabstractyXThis document is intended to be a source of information about the Russian Federal standard for electronic encryption, decryption, and message authentication algorithms (GOST 28147-89), which is one of the Russian cryptographic standard algorithms called GOST algorithms).  Recently, Russian cryptography is being used in Internet applications, and this document has been created as information for developers and users of GOST 28147-89 for encryption, decryption, and message authentication.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCryptocom, Ltd.dnamekV. Dolmatovddatej‘jhfilenamegrfc5830eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127702hkeywordsÖxrussian federal standarduelectronic encryptionjdecryptionvmessage authenticationxrussian cryptographic standardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKûÃ⁄granking∆crfcd5830irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexWGOST 28147-89: Encryption, Decryption, and Message Authentication Code (MAC) AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8891∂habstractyBThis specification defines a set of link relation types that may be used on Web resources for navigation between a resource and other resources related to version control, such as past versions and working copies.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehA. Brown¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehG. Clemm¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. ReschkeddatejÕ!hfilenamegrfc5829eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127701hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKª≤granking≈crfcd5829irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGLink Relation Types for Simple Version Navigation between Web ResourcesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_There has been significant recent work in increasing the capabilities of Ethernet switches and Ethernet forwarding models.  As a consequence, the role of Ethernet is rapidly expanding into "transport networks" that previously were the domain of other technologies such as Synchronous Optical Network (SONET) / Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH), Time-Division Multiplexing (TDM), and Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM).  This document defines an architecture and framework for a Generalized- MPLS-based control plane for Ethernet in this "transport network" capacity.  GMPLS has already been specified for similar technologies.  Some additional extensions to the GMPLS control plane are needed, and this document provides a framework for these extensions.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL. Anderssonddatej‘Ähfilenamegrfc5828eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127700hkeywordsÅrtransport networkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK©grankingƒcrfcd5828irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeGeneralized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Ethernet Label Switching Architecture and FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document proposes a new mechanism for TCP and Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) that can be used to recover lost segments when a connection's congestion window is small.  The "Early Retransmit" mechanism allows the transport to reduce, in certain special circumstances, the number of duplicate acknowledgments required to trigger a fast retransmission.  This allows the transport to use fast retransmit to recover segment losses that would otherwise require a lengthy retransmission timeout. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdICSIdnameiM. Allman¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamenK. Avrachenkov¢kaffiliationxBCAM-IKERBASQUE and LAAS-CNRSdnameiU. Ayesta¢kaffiliationoOhio UniversitydnamejJ. Blanton¢kaffiliationsKarlstad UniversitydnameiP. Hurtigddatej‘çhfilenamegrfc5827eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127699hkeywordsÇxtransmission control protocolsfast retransmissionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK÷&∂granking√crfcd5827irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHEarly Retransmit for TCP and Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document presents requirements specific to home control and automation applications for Routing Over Low power and Lossy (ROLL) networks.  In the near future, many homes will contain high numbers of wireless devices for a wide set of purposes.  Examples include actuators (relay, light dimmer, heating valve), sensors (wall switch, water leak, blood pressure), and advanced controllers (radio-frequency-based AV remote control, central server for light and heat control).  Because such devices only cover a limited radio range, routing is often required.  The aim of this document is to specify the routing requirements for networks comprising such constrained devices in a home-control and automation environment.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsSigma Designs, Inc.dnameiA. Brandt¢kaffiliationsSigma Designs, Inc.dnamehJ. Buron¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamehG. Porcuddatej‘Öhfilenamegrfc5826eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127698hkeywordsÇdrollx routing over low power and lossykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKª≤granking¬crfcd5826irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDHome Automation Routing Requirements in Low-Power and Lossy NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document describes a method for displaying downgraded messages that originally contained internationalized email addresses or internationalized header fields.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwara¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehB. Leibaddatej‘Åhfilenamegrfc5825eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127697hkeywordsÑcEAIx"Email Address InternationalizationiDowngradedMAILkobsoletedByÅd6530epagesopublicationDateK√PUgranking¡crfcd5825irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEDisplaying Downgraded Messages for Email Address InternationalizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5Today, customers expect to run triple-play services through BGP/MPLS IP-VPNs. Some service providers will deploy services that request Quality of Service (QoS) guarantees from a local Customer Edge (CE) to a remote CE across the network. As a result, the application (e.g., voice, video, bandwidth-guaranteed data pipe, etc.) requirements for an end-to-end QoS and reserving an adequate bandwidth continue to increase.

Service providers can use both an MPLS and an MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) Label Switched Path (LSP) to meet their service objectives. This document describes service-provider requirements for supporting a customer Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) and RSVP-TE over a BGP/MPLS IP-VPN. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiK. Kumaki¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnameiY. Kamiteddatej‘åhfilenamegrfc5824eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127696hkeywordsÅstriple-play servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÿ¸âgranking¿crfcd5824irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexáRequirements for Supporting Customer Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) and RSVP Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) over a BGP/MPLS IP-VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document describes extensions to OSPF to support mobile ad hoc networks (MANETs).  The extensions, called OSPF-OR (OSPF-Overlapping Relay), include mechanisms for link-local signaling (LLS), an OSPF-MANET interface, a simple technique to reduce the size of Hello packets by only transmitting incremental state changes, and a method for optimized flooding of routing updates.  OSPF-OR also provides a means to reduce unnecessary adjacencies to support larger MANETs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Chandraddatej‘âhfilenamegrfc5820eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127695hkeywordsáxopen shortest path firstemanetgospf-orvospf-overlapping relaytlink-local signalingcllsjospf-manetkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateK¢ﬂ-grankingºcrfcd5820irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Extensions to OSPF to Support Mobile Ad Hoc NetworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7137∂habstractyÎMany IMAP clients display information about total number of messages / total number of unseen messages in IMAP mailboxes.  In order to do that, they are forced to issue a LIST or LSUB command and to list all available mailboxes, followed by a STATUS command for each mailbox found.  This document provides an extension to LIST command that allows the client to request STATUS information for mailboxes together with other information typically returned by the LIST command. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekT. Sirainenddatej‘Ñhfilenamegrfc5819eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmorgdfullxmorg - Message OrganizationdnametMessage Organizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127694hkeywordsÇdlistdlsubkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKó5ëgrankingªcrfcd5819irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAIMAP4 Extension for Returning STATUS Information in Extended LISTdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document defines simple additions to the Link Management Protocol (LMP) to provide a control plane tool that can assist in the location of stranded resources by allowing adjacent Label-Switching Routers (LSRs) to confirm data channel statuses and provide triggers for notifying the management plane if any discrepancies are found.  As LMP is already used to verify data plane connectivity, it is considered to be an appropriate candidate to support this feature. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeH. Xu¢kaffiliationgFujitsudnamekS. Bardalai¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnameiJ. Meuric¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Cavigliaddatej‘âhfilenamegrfc5818eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127693hkeywordsÅcLMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK÷&™granking∫crfcd5818irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLData Channel Status Confirmation Extensions for the Link Management ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6898∂habstracty≤MPLS-TE Graceful Shutdown is a method for explicitly notifying the nodes in a Traffic Engineering (TE) enabled network that the TE capability on a link or on an entire Label Switching Router (LSR) is going to be disabled. MPLS-TE graceful shutdown mechanisms are tailored toward addressing planned outage in the network.

This document provides requirements and protocol mechanisms to reduce or eliminate traffic disruption in the event of a planned shutdown of a network resource. These operations are equally applicable to both MPLS-TE and its Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) extensions. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Zamfir¢kaffiliationrCable and WirelessdnameiJ. Newtonddatej‘åhfilenamegrfc5817eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127692hkeywordsÇgmpls-tebtekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK÷&úgrankingπcrfcd5817irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKGraceful Shutdown in MPLS and Generalized MPLS Traffic Engineering NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÚThis document updates RFC 3161.  It allows the use of ESSCertIDv2, as defined in RFC 5035, to specify the hash of a signer certificate when the hash is calculated with a function other than the Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA-1). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationl3xA SecuritydnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationfThalesdnamegN. Popeddatej‘áhfilenamegrfc5816eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127691hkeywordsÉrsigner certificateusecure hash algorithmesha-1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK∂,Ïgranking∏crfcd5816irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexESSCertIDv2 Update for RFC 3161dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÂThis document defines managed objects for IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX).  These objects provide information for monitoring IPFIX Exporters and IPFIX Collectors including the basic configuration information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamehT. Dietz¢kaffiliationkNTT PF Lab.dnamelA. Kobayashi¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationx Technische Universitaet MuenchendnamehG. Muenzddatej‘Ñhfilenamegrfc5815eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127690hkeywordsàhSelectoriCollectorhExporterhSamplingiFilteringeIPFIXiIPFIX-MIBrIPFIX-SELECTOR-MIBkobsoletedByÅd6615epages@opublicationDateKŒ?Ogranking∑crfcd5815irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Definitions of Managed Objects for IP Flow Information ExportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) is one of the most promising candidate technologies for a future data transmission network. GMPLS has been developed to control and operate different kinds of network elements, such as conventional routers, switches, Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) systems, Add-Drop Multiplexers (ADMs), photonic cross-connects (PXCs), optical cross- connects (OXCs), etc. These physically diverse devices differ drastically from one another in dynamic provisioning ability. At the same time, the need for dynamically provisioned connections is increasing because optical networks are being deployed in metro areas. As different applications have varied requirements in the provisioning performance of optical networks, it is imperative to define standardized metrics and procedures such that the performance of networks and application needs can be mapped to each other.

This document provides a series of performance metrics to evaluate the dynamic Label Switched Path (LSP) provisioning performance in GMPLS networks, specifically the dynamic LSP setup/release performance. These metrics can be used to characterize the features of GMPLS networks in LSP dynamic provisioning. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdSJTUdnamefW. Sun¢kaffiliationdCATRdnamehG. Zhangddatej‘àhfilenamegrfc5814eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127689hkeywordsÉuSignaling performancexRSVP-TE delay measurementxcontrol plane performancekobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateK¢ﬂ"granking∂crfcd5814irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Label Switched Path (LSP) Dynamic Provisioning Performance Metrics in Generalized MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines managed objects for the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Network Element (NE). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamegR. Haasddatej‘Phfilenamegrfc5813eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127688hkeywordsÑxmanagement information baseonetwork elementbnejforces-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK¢ﬁÙgrankingµcrfcd5813irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document defines the forwarding element (FE) model used in the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) protocol.  The model represents the capabilities, state, and configuration of forwarding elements within the context of the ForCES protocol, so that control elements (CEs) can control the FEs accordingly.  More specifically, the model describes the logical functions that are present in an FE, what capabilities these functions support, and how these functions are or can be interconnected.  This FE model is intended to satisfy the model requirements specified in RFC 3654. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdSelfdnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationmZnyx NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salimddatej”íhfilenamegrfc5812eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127687hkeywordsÇrforwarding elementocontrol elementkobsoletedByÄepagesÜopublicationDateK¢ﬁÊgranking¥crfcd5812irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Forwarding Element ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7408∂habstractyUThis document defines the SCTP-based TML (Transport Mapping Layer) for the ForCES (Forwarding and Control Element Separation) protocol.  It explains the rationale for choosing the SCTP (Stream Control Transmission Protocol) and also describes how this TML addresses all the requirements required by and the ForCES protocol. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqMojatatu NetworksdnamemJ. Hadi Salim¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamehK. Ogawaddatej‘åhfilenamegrfc5811eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127686hkeywordsÉfForCEScTMLx#stream conrol transmission protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK¢ﬁ‹granking≥crfcd5811irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlSCTP-Based Transport Mapping Layer (TML) for the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘This document specifies the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) protocol.  The ForCES protocol is used for communications between Control Elements (CEs) and Forwarding Elements (FEs) in a ForCES Network Element (ForCES NE).  This specification is intended to meet the ForCES protocol requirements defined in RFC 3654.  Besides the ForCES protocol, this specification also defines the requirements for the Transport Mapping Layer (TML). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationxLulea University of TechnologydnamehA. Doria¢kaffiliationdZnyxdnamemJ. Hadi Salim¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamegR. Haas¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekH. Khosravi¢kaffiliationxZhejiang Gongshang UniversitydnamegW. Wang¢kaffiliationxZhejiang Gongshang UniversitydnamegL. Dong¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Gopal¢kaffiliationdSelfdnamejJ. Halpernddatej‘Khfilenamegrfc5810eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127685hkeywordsàpcontrol elementssforwarding elementsbfebceonetwork elementbnectmlwtransport mapping layerkobsoletedByÄepages|opublicationDateK¢ﬁ–granking≤crfcd5810irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIForwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7391d7121∂habstractyXThis document defines terminology and provides requirements relating to the Location-by-Reference approach using a location Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) to handle location information within signaling and other Internet messaging.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcTCSdnamekR. MarshallddatejÕhfilenamegrfc5808eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127684hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÈØ¿granking∞crfcd5808irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Requirements for a Location-by-Reference MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚This document defines a master key used between a client of the Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) and an enforcement point, for bootstrapping lower-layer ciphering.  The master key is derived from the Master Session Key of the Extensible Authentication Protocol as a result of successful PANA authentication.  The master key guarantees cryptographic independence among enforcement points bootstrapped from PANA authentication across different address families. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. Yeginddatej‘áhfilenamegrfc5807eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127683hkeywordsÅx7protocol for carrying authentication for network accesskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKó5ÇgrankingØcrfcd5807irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDefinition of Master Key between PANA Client and Enforcement PointdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty‹This RFC, which contains the text of an Internet Draft that was submitted originally to the SIP Working Group, is being published now for the historical record and to provide a reference for later Informational RFCs. The original Abstract follows.

This document proposes an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP). This extension provides the ability for the called SIP user agent to identify from whom the call was diverted and why the call was diverted. The extension defines a general header, Diversion, which conveys the diversion information from other SIP user agents and proxies to the called user agent.

This extension allows enhanced support for various features, including Unified Messaging, Third-Party Voicemail, and Automatic Call Distribution (ACD). SIP user agents and SIP proxies that receive diversion information may use this as supplemental information for feature invocation decisions. This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemRobert SparksgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegS. Levy¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnameiM. MohaliddatejÃ”hfilenamegrfc5806eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127682hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateKûÃ»grankingÆcrfcd5806irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexDiversion Indication in SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty)Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) update operations, such as Add, Delete, and Modify operations, have atomic, consistency, isolation, durability (ACID) properties.  Each of these update operations act upon an entry.  It is often desirable to update two or more entries in a single unit of interaction, a transaction.  Transactions are necessary to support a number of applications including resource provisioning.  This document extends LDAP to support transactions.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey MelnikovgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekK. Zeilengaddatej‘2hfilenamegrfc5805eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127681hkeywordsÇdacidx'atomic consistency isolation durabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKó5vgranking≠crfcd5805irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) TransactionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°Sieve scripts allow users to filter incoming email.  Message stores are commonly sealed servers so users cannot log into them, yet users must be able to update their scripts on them.  This document describes a protocol "ManageSieve" for securely managing Sieve scripts on a remote server.  This protocol allows a user to have multiple scripts, and also alerts a user to syntactically flawed scripts. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationtBeThereBeSquare Inc.dnameiT. Martinddatej‘Dhfilenamegrfc5804eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127680hkeywordsÅkmanagesievekobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateL;Í≠granking¨crfcd5804irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A Protocol for Remotely Managing Sieve ScriptsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7817d8553∂habstractyThis memo describes how the "authPassword" Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) attribute can be used for storing secrets used by the Salted Challenge Response Authentication Message (SCRAM) mechanism in the Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) framework.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘}hfilenamegrfc5803eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127679hkeywordsÉlauthpasswordx(simple authentication and security layerdsaslkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL;Í£granking´crfcd5803irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Schema for Storing Salted Challenge Response Authentication Mechanism (SCRAM) SecretsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThe secure authentication mechanism most widely deployed and used by Internet application protocols is the transmission of clear-text passwords over a channel protected by Transport Layer Security (TLS). There are some significant security concerns with that mechanism, which could be addressed by the use of a challenge response authentication mechanism protected by TLS. Unfortunately, the challenge response mechanisms presently on the standards track all fail to meet requirements necessary for widespread deployment, and have had success only in limited use.

This specification describes a family of Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL; RFC 4422) authentication mechanisms called the Salted Challenge Response Authentication Mechanism (SCRAM), which addresses the security concerns and meets the deployability requirements. When used in combination with TLS or an equivalent security layer, a mechanism from this family could improve the status quo for application protocol authentication and provide a suitable choice for a mandatory-to-implement mechanism for future application protocol standards. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiC. Newman¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmbHdnamelA. Menon-Sen¢kaffiliationkIsode, Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekN. Williamsddatej‘zhfilenamegrfc5802eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsasldfullx/sasl - Simple Authentication and Security Layerdnamex(Simple Authentication and Security Layerdtypebwgbidjdoc-127678hkeywordsÅx(simple authentication and security layerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL;Íñgranking™crfcd5802irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSalted Challenge Response Authentication Mechanism (SCRAM) SASL and GSS-API MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9266d7677∂habstractyLThis document describes how to use a Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) mechanism in the Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) framework.  This is done by defining a new SASL mechanism family, called GS2.  This mechanism family offers a number of improvements over the previous "SASL/ GSSAPI" mechanism: it is more general, uses fewer messages for the authentication phase in some cases, and supports negotiable use of channel binding.  Only GSS-API mechanisms that support channel binding and mutual authentication are supported. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfSJD ABdnamelS. Josefsson¢kaffiliationfOraclednamekN. WilliamsddatejÕhfilenamegrfc5801eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsasldfullx/sasl - Simple Authentication and Security Layerdnamex(Simple Authentication and Security Layerdtypebwgbidjdoc-127677hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL;Íàgranking©crfcd5801irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexûUsing Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Mechanisms in Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL): The GS2 Mechanism FamilydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9266∂habstractyjThis memo defines the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) for IPv4 and IPv6.  It is version three (3) of the protocol, and it is based on VRRP (version 2) for IPv4 that is defined in RFC 3768 and in "Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol for IPv6".  VRRP specifies an election protocol that dynamically assigns responsibility for a virtual router to one of the VRRP routers on a LAN.  The VRRP router controlling the IPv4 or IPv6 address(es) associated with a virtual router is called the Master, and it forwards packets sent to these IPv4 or IPv6 addresses.  VRRP Master routers are configured with virtual IPv4 or IPv6 addresses, and VRRP Backup routers infer the address family of the virtual addresses being carried based on the transport protocol.  Within a VRRP router, the virtual routers in each of the IPv4 and IPv6 address families are a domain unto themselves and do not overlap.  The election process provides dynamic failover in the forwarding responsibility should the Master become unavailable.  For IPv4, the advantage gained from using VRRP is a higher-availability default path without requiring configuration of dynamic routing or router discovery protocols on every end-host.  For IPv6, the advantage gained from using VRRP for IPv6 is a quicker switchover to Backup routers than can be obtained with standard IPv6 Neighbor Discovery mechanisms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehS. NadasddatejÕhfilenamegrfc5798eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvrrpdfullx)vrrp - Virtual Router Redundancy Protocoldnamex"Virtual Router Redundancy Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127676hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9568epages(opublicationDateKó5egranking¶crfcd5798irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEVirtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) Version 3 for IPv4 and IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛Every version of the FTP specification has added a few new commands, with the early ones summarized in RFC 959.  RFC 2389 established a mechanism for specifying and negotiating FTP extensions.  The number of extensions, both those supported by the mechanism and some that are not, continues to increase.  An IANA registry of FTP Command and Feature names is established to reduce the likelihood of conflict of names and the consequent ambiguity.  This specification establishes that registry. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationfTR-SysdnameiA. Hoenesddatej‘Éhfilenamegrfc5797eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127675hkeywordsÇpFTP FEAT commandqFTP FEAT responsekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateKó5[granking•crfcd5797irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"FTP Command and Extension RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFRFC 4601 mandates the use of IPsec to ensure authentication of the link-local messages in the Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) routing protocol.  This document specifies mechanisms to authenticate the PIM-SM link-local messages using the IP security (IPsec) Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) or (optionally) the Authentication Header (AH).  It specifies optional mechanisms to provide confidentiality using the ESP.  Manual keying is specified as the mandatory and default group key management solution.  To deal with issues of scalability and security that exist with manual keying, optional support for an automated group key management mechanism is provided.  However, the procedures for implementing automated group key management are left to other documents.  This document updates RFC 4601. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxConcordia University/CSEdnameiW. Atwood¢kaffiliationgIRS-EMTdnamehS. Islam¢kaffiliationxConcordia University/CIISEdnamehM. Siamiddatej‘Öhfilenamegrfc5796eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-127674hkeywordsÜhsecurityfPIM-SMprouting securityqmulticast routingrlink-local messagex*Protocol Independent Multicast Sparse ModekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK¢ﬂgranking§crfcd5796irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmAuthentication and Confidentiality in Protocol Independent Multicast Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) Link-Local MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Robust Header Compression (ROHC) protocol provides an efficient, flexible, and future-proof header compression concept. It is designed to operate efficiently and robustly over various link technologies with different characteristics.

The ROHC framework, along with a set of compression profiles, was initially defined in RFC 3095. To improve and simplify the ROHC specifications, this document explicitly defines the ROHC framework and the profile for uncompressed separately. More specifically, the definition of the framework does not modify or update the definition of the framework specified by RFC 3095.

