opublicationDateK¢ﬂgrankingùcrfcd5789irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuPATCH Method for HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxŸThe aim of this document is to establish a new IANA registry for IMAP keywords and to define a procedure for keyword registration, in order to improve interoperability between different IMAP clients. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekD. Cridlandddatej‘Öhfilenamegrfc5788eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127666hkeywordsÖdIMAPeemailctagelabelgkeywordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKó°grankingúcrfcd5788irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIMAP4 Keyword RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8621∂habstracty|The ITU-T has defined an architecture and requirements for operating an Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON).

The Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) protocol suite is designed to provide a control plane for a range of network technologies including optical networks such as time division multiplexing (TDM) networks including SONET/SDH and Optical Transport Networks (OTNs), and lambda switching optical networks.

The requirements for GMPLS routing to satisfy the requirements of ASON routing, and an evaluation of existing GMPLS routing protocols are provided in other documents. This document defines extensions to the OSPFv2 Link State Routing Protocol to meet the requirements for routing in an ASON.

Note that this work is scoped to the requirements and evaluation expressed in RFC 4258 and RFC 4652 and the ITU-T Recommendations current when those documents were written. Future extensions of revisions of this work may be necessary if the ITU-T Recommendations are revised or if new requirements are introduced into a revision of RFC 4258. This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej‘mhfilenamegrfc5787eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127665hkeywordsÇeitu-tx"ospfv2 link state routing protocolkobsoletedByÅd6827epagesopublicationDateK®\ grankingõcrfcd5787irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]OSPFv2 Routing Protocols Extensions for Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON) RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßOSPF Traffic Engineering (TE) extensions are used to advertise TE Link State Advertisements (LSAs) containing information about TE-enabled links. The only addresses belonging to a router that are advertised in TE LSAs are the local addresses corresponding to TE-enabled links, and the local address corresponding to the Router ID.

In order to allow other routers in a network to compute Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineered Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) to a given router's local addresses, those addresses must also be advertised by OSPF TE.

This document describes procedures that enhance OSPF TE to advertise a router's local addresses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. KompelladdatejÕhfilenamegrfc5786eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127664hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKóëgrankingöcrfcd5786irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRAdvertising a Router's Local Addresses in OSPF Traffic Engineering (TE) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6827d8687∂habstractxïThis memo defines a path prefix for "well-known locations", "/.well-known/", in selected Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) schemes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliation`dnameoE. Hammer-Lahavddatej‘àhfilenamegrfc5785eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127663hkeywordsÅtwell-known locationskobsoletedByÅd8615epagesopublicationDateKª±˙grankingôcrfcd5785irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Defining Well-Known Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document describes a way to represent Sieve email filtering language scripts in XML. Representing Sieves in XML is intended not as an alternate storage format for Sieve but rather as a means to facilitate manipulation of scripts using XML tools.

The XML representation also defines additional elements that have no counterparts in the regular Sieve language. These elements are intended for use by graphical user interfaces and provide facilities for labeling or grouping sections of a script so they can be displayed more conveniently. These elements are represented as specially structured comments in regular Sieve format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehN. Freed¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehS. Vedamddatej‘Çhfilenamegrfc5784eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127662hkeywordsÉdSMTPeESMTPeSievekobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateKó5Ngrankingòcrfcd5784irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Sieve Email Filtering:  Sieves and Display Directives in XMLdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyWThis document is an informational snapshot taken by the IRTF\'s Internet Congestion Control Research Group (ICCRG) in October 2008.  It provides a survey of congestion control topics described by documents in the RFC series.  This does not modify or update the specifications or status of the RFC documents that are discussed.  It may be used as a reference or starting point for the future work of the research group, especially in noting gaps or open issues in the current IETF standards.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars EggertgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehM. Welzl¢kaffiliationkMTI SystemsdnamegW. EddyddatejÃ≥hfilenamegrfc5783eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeiccrgdfullx#iccrg - Internet Congestion ControldnamexInternet Congestion Controldtypebrgbidjdoc-127661hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÉ3ªgrankingócrfcd5783irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex$Congestion Control in the RFC SeriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty‡The rise of spam and other anti-social behavior on the Internet has led to the creation of shared blacklists and whitelists of IP addresses or domains.  The DNS has become the de-facto standard method of distributing these blacklists and whitelists.  This memo documents the structure and usage of DNS-based blacklists and whitelists, and the protocol used to query them.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. DusseaultgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levineddatej‘dhfilenamegrfc5782eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdasrgdfullxasrg - Anti-Spam Research GroupdnamexAnti-Spam Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127660hkeywordsÜdmailoelectronic mailcDNSdspamiblacklistiwhitelistkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK}Öjgrankingñcrfcd5782irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexDNS Blacklists and WhitelistsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∏This document specifies the rsync Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘Ñhfilenamegrfc5781eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127659hkeywordsÅhrsyncurikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKv␀0grankingïcrfcd5781irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletThe rsync URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This specification defines an experimental usage of the Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) Protocol that discovers the presence and current behavior of NATs and firewalls between the STUN client and the STUN server.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeSkypednamelD. MacDonald¢kaffiliationeSkypednamekB. Lowekampddatej‘Ähfilenamegrfc5780eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127658hkeywordsÅtNAT type diagnosticskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÛ&Îgrankingîcrfcd5780irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGNAT Behavior Discovery Using Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstracty´This specification defines Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) interactions between Proxy Mobile IPv6 entities (both Mobile Access Gateway and Local Mobility Anchor) and a AAA server within a Proxy Mobile IPv6 Domain.  These AAA interactions are primarily used to download and update mobile node specific policy profile information between Proxy Mobile IPv6 entities and a remote policy store. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationuNokia Siemens NetworkdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamelJ. Bournelle¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Muhanna¢kaffiliationkRWTH AachendnamehU. Meyerddatej‘vhfilenamegrfc5779eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127657hkeywordsÑcaaanauthenticationmauthorizationnand accountingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKàóJgrankingìcrfcd5779irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlDiameter Proxy Mobile IPv6: Mobile Access Gateway and Local Mobility Anchor Interaction with Diameter ServerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆMobile IPv6 deployments may want to bootstrap their operations dynamically based on an interaction between the home agent and the Diameter server of the Mobile Service Provider. This document specifies the interaction between a Mobile IP home agent and a Diameter server.

This document defines the home agent to the Diameter server communication when the mobile node authenticates using the Internet Key Exchange v2 protocol with the Extensible Authentication Protocol or using the Mobile IPv6 Authentication Protocol. In addition to authentication and authorization, the configuration of Mobile IPv6- specific parameters and accounting is specified in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamelJ. Bournelle¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Giaretta¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekM. NakhjiriddatejÃﬁhfilenamegrfc5778eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127656hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateK}ÖXgrankingícrfcd5778irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKDiameter Mobile IPv6: Support for Home Agent to Diameter Server InteractiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document defines a number of Diameter attribute-value pairs (AVPs) for traffic classification with actions for filtering and Quality of Service (QoS) treatment.  These AVPs can be used in existing and future Diameter applications where permitted by the Augmented Backus-Naur Form (ABNF) specification of the respective Diameter command extension policy. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationxUniversity of GoettingendnameoM. Arumaithurai¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamegA. Liorddatej‘Ñhfilenamegrfc5777eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127655hkeywordsÖhDiameternQos AttributesvTraffic classificationiFilteringkFirewallingkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateKuΩágrankingëcrfcd5777irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKTraffic Classification and Quality of Service (QoS) Attributes for DiameterdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document details the Timed Efficient Stream \%Loss-Tolerant Authentication (TESLA) packet source authentication and packet integrity verification protocol and its integration within the Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) and NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) content delivery protocols.  This document only considers the authentication/integrity verification of the packets generated by the session's sender.  The authentication and integrity verification of the packets sent by receivers, if any, is out of the scope of this document.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamemA. Francillon¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamejS. Fauriteddatej‘yhfilenamegrfc5776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127654hkeywordsÑeTESLAeFLUTEcALCdNORMkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateKª±Ógrankingêcrfcd5776irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex°Use of Timed Efficient Stream Loss-Tolerant Authentication (TESLA) in the Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) and NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) protocol, a massively scalable reliable content delivery protocol.  Asynchronous Layered Coding combines the Layered Coding Transport (LCT) building block, a multiple rate congestion control building block and the Forward Error Correction (FEC) building block to provide congestion controlled reliable asynchronous delivery of content to an unlimited number of concurrent receivers from a single sender.  This document obsoletes RFC 3450. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnamekL. Vicisanoddatej‘yhfilenamegrfc5775eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127653hkeywordsäxForward Error CorrectioncFECxLayered Coding TransportcLCTnBuilding BlockeWEBRCxreliable +object deliveryvreliable file deliveryibroadcastimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKª±‚grankingècrfcd5775irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) Protocol InstantiationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyeThis document provides a guideline for creating civic address considerations documents for individual countries, as required by RFC 4776.  Furthermore, this document also creates an IANA Registry referring to such address considerations documents and registers such address considerations for Austria.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfnic.atdnamegK. Wolf¢kaffiliationfnic.atdnamelA. MayrhoferddatejÃˇhfilenamegrfc5774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127652hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateKèHgrankingécrfcd5774irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberöetotaletitlexçConsiderations for Civic Addresses in the Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO): Guidelines and IANA Registry DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty£This document analyzes the state of the Internet domain-based routing system, concentrating on Inter-Domain Routing (IDR) and also considering the relationship between inter-domain and intra-domain routing.  The analysis is carried out with respect to RFC 1126 and other IDR requirements and design efforts looking at the routing system as it appeared to be in 2001 with editorial additions reflecting developments up to 2006.  It is the companion document to "A Set of Possible Requirements for a Future Routing Architecture" (RFC 5772), which is a discussion of requirements for the future routing architecture, addressing systems developments and future routing protocols.  This document summarizes discussions held several years ago by members of the IRTF Routing Research Group (IRTF RRG) and other interested parties.  The document is published with the support of the IRTF RRG as a record of the work completed at that time, but with the understanding that it does not necessarily represent either the latest technical understanding or the technical consensus of the research group at the date of publication.  This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss CallongauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationcLTUdnamehA. Doriaddatej‘Ihfilenamegrfc5773eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127651hkeywordságHistorydIRTFvRouting Research GroupcRRGtRouting RequirementscIDRcFDRkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateK}Ö|grankingçcrfcd5773irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex9Analysis of Inter-Domain Routing Requirements and HistorydtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractygThe requirements for routing architectures described in this document were produced by two sub-groups under the IRTF Routing Research Group (RRG) in 2001, with some editorial updates up to 2006. The two sub- groups worked independently, and the resulting requirements represent two separate views of the problem and of what is required to fix the problem. This document may usefully serve as part of the recommended reading for anyone who works on routing architecture designs for the Internet in the future.

The document is published with the support of the IRTF RRG as a record of the work completed at that time, but with the understanding that it does not necessarily represent either the latest technical understanding or the technical consensus of the research group at the date of publication. This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss CallongauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcLTUdnamehA. Doria¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamemF. Kastenholzddatej‘Ihfilenamegrfc5772eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127650hkeywordsÑvRouting Research GroupcRRGcIDRcFDRkobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDateK}Öâgrankingåcrfcd5772irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex@A Set of Possible Requirements for a Future Routing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxÎThis document provides guidance for the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) in assigning IPv4 multicast addresses.  It obsoletes RFC 3171 and RFC 3138 and updates RFC 2780.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliation`dnameiL. Vegoda¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyerddatej‘Åhfilenamegrfc5771eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-127649hkeywordsÜhinternethassignedgnumbersiauthorityhprotocoljparameterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKûÃπgrankingãcrfcd5771irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber3etotaletitlex6IANA Guidelines for IPv4 Multicast Address AssignmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document specifies extensions to the Host Identity Protocol (HIP) to facilitate Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal.  The extensions are based on the use of the Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) methodology to discover a working path between two end-hosts, and on standard techniques for encapsulating Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) packets within the User Datagram Protocol (UDP).  This document also defines elements of a procedure for NAT traversal, including the optional use of a HIP relay server.  With these extensions HIP is able to work in environments that have NATs and provides a generic NAT traversal solution to higher-layer networking applications.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdHIITdnamegM. Komu¢kaffiliationrThe Boeing CompanydnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadiclabdnamehJ. Melen¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadiclabdnamejA. Keranenddatej‘~hfilenamegrfc5770eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127648hkeywordsÇcICEiHIP relaykobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateK€ogrankingäcrfcd5770irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZBasic Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Extensions for Traversal of Network Address TranslatorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThe Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) protocol defines several STUN attributes.  The content of some of these -- FINGERPRINT, MESSAGE-INTEGRITY, and XOR-MAPPED-ADDRESS -- involve binary-logical operations (hashing, xor).  This document provides test vectors for those attributes.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameqR. Denis-Courmontddatej‘<hfilenamegrfc5769eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127647hkeywordsÑdSTUNdtestgvectorskfingerprintkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK⁄grankingâcrfcd5769irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Test Vectors for Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis specification defines a media feature tag and an option tag for use with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  The media feature tag allows a User Agent (UA) to communicate to its registrar that it supports ICE.  The option tag allows a UA to require support for ICE in order for a call to proceed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘1hfilenamegrfc5768eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127646hkeywordsÇcSIPcNATkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK⁄grankingàcrfcd5768irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlIndicating Support for Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE) in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThis document defines a guideline for a User Agent (UA) to generate an anonymous Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) message by utilizing mechanisms such as Globally Routable User Agent URIs (GRUUs) and Traversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN) without the need for a privacy service defined in RFC 3323.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekM. Munakata¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Schubert¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegT. Ohbaddatej‘`hfilenamegrfc5767eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127645hkeywordsÑcSIPcIMSgprivacyjguidelineskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK⁄˚grankingácrfcd5767irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+User-Agent-Driven Privacy Mechanism for SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖIf a host is located behind a NAT, then in certain situations it can be impossible for that host to communicate directly with other hosts (peers).  In these situations, it is necessary for the host to use the services of an intermediate node that acts as a communication relay.  This specification defines a protocol, called TURN (Traversal Using Relays around NAT), that allows the host to control the operation of the relay and to exchange packets with its peers using the relay.  TURN differs from some other relay control protocols in that it allows a client to communicate with multiple peers using a single relay address. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekP. Matthews¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘xhfilenamegrfc5766eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127644hkeywordsÑcNATdTURNdSTUNcICEkobsoletedByÅd8656epagesCopublicationDateK⁄ÌgrankingÜcrfcd5766irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhTraversal Using Relays around NAT (TURN): Relay Extensions to Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8155d8553µhabstractyHPeer-to-peer (P2P) networks have become popular for certain applications and deployments for a variety of reasons, including fault tolerance, economics, and legal issues.  It has therefore become reasonable for resource consuming and typically centralized applications like Voice over IP (VoIP) and, in general, realtime communication to adapt and exploit the benefits of P2P.  Such a migration needs to address a new set of P2P-specific security problems.  This document describes some of the known issues found in common P2P networks, analyzing the relevance of such issues and the applicability of existing solutions when using P2P architectures for realtime communication.  This document is a product of the P2P Research Group.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert SparksgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejE. Marocco¢kaffiliationx+SIP Communicator / University of StrasbourgdnamegE. Ivovddatej‘Åhfilenamegrfc5765eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymep2prgdfullx#p2prg - Peer-to-Peer Research GroupdnamexPeer-to-Peer Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127643hkeywordsÑcp2pgoverlaycrtcdvoipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÉ3ØgrankingÖcrfcd5765irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexQSecurity Issues and Solutions in Peer-to-Peer Systems for Realtime CommunicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) extension to establish keys for Secure RTP (SRTP) and Secure RTP Control Protocol (SRTCP) flows.  DTLS keying happens on the media path, independent of any out-of-band signalling channel present. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorladdatej‘Mhfilenamegrfc5764eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127642hkeywordsÇxsecure rtp control protocolesrtcpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÈØ©grankingÑcrfcd5764irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwDatagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) Extension to Establish Keys for the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9443d7983∂habstractyrThis document specifies how to use the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) to establish a Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) security context using the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol.  It describes a mechanism of transporting a fingerprint attribute in the Session Description Protocol (SDP) that identifies the key that will be presented during the DTLS handshake.  The key exchange travels along the media path as opposed to the signaling path.  The SIP Identity mechanism can be used to protect the integrity of the fingerprint attribute from modification by intermediate proxies. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkSkype, Inc.dnameiJ. Fischl¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorladdatej‘Lhfilenamegrfc5763eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127641hkeywordsÑdstipxsession initiation protocolufingerprint attributendtls handshakekobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateKÈØîgrankingÉcrfcd5763irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexáFramework for Establishing a Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) Security Context Using Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8842∂habstracty¶The Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) is a widely used transport for real-time multimedia on IP networks.  The Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) is a transport protocol that provides desirable services for real-time applications.  This memo specifies a mapping of RTP onto DCCP, along with associated signalling, such that real- time applications can make use of the services provided by DCCP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”Ìhfilenamegrfc5762eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127640hkeywordsÅxreal-time transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK⁄„grankingÇcrfcd5762irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7RTP and the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6773∂habstracty_This memo discusses issues that arise when multiplexing RTP data packets and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) packets on a single UDP port.  It updates RFC 3550 and RFC 3551 to describe when such multiplexing is and is not appropriate, and it explains how the Session Description Protocol (SDP) can be used to signal multiplexed sessions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. WesterlundddatejÃ\hfilenamegrfc5761eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127639hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK⁄ŸgrankingÅcrfcd5761irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Multiplexing RTP Data and Control Packets on a Single PortdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8035d8858∂habstractyˆThis document specifies an extension to the Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP) to use unicast feedback to a multicast sender.  The proposed extension is useful for single-source multicast sessions such as Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) communication where the traditional model of many-to-many group communication is either not available or not desired.  In addition, it can be applied to any group that might benefit from a sender-controlled summarized reporting mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpAalto UniversitydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednameoJ. Chesterfield¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekE. Schoolerddatej‘{hfilenamegrfc5760eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127638hkeywordsÇxreal-time transport protocolcssmkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateKàógrankingÄcrfcd5760irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaRTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extensions for Single-Source Multicast Sessions with Unicast FeedbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6128∂habstractyŒThis document specifies a base profile for X.509 v3 Certificates and X.509 v2 Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs) for use with the United States National Security Agency's Suite B Cryptography.  The reader is assumed to have familiarity with RFC 5280, "Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Profile".  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejJ. Solinas¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejL. Zieglarddatej‘ahfilenamegrfc5759eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127637hkeywordsÉux.509 v3 certificatesx%x.509 v2 certificate revocation listsccrlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK\¶
grankingcrfcd5759irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASuite B Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This document updates RFC 3279 to specify algorithm identifiers and ASN.1 encoding rules for the Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) digital signatures when using SHA-224, SHA-256, SHA-384, or SHA-512 as the hashing algorithm.  This specification applies to the Internet X.509 Public Key infrastructure (PKI) when digital signatures are used to sign certificates and certificate revocation lists (CRLs).  This document also identifies all four SHA2 hash algorithms for use in the Internet X.509 PKI. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegQ. Dang¢kaffiliationl3xA SecuritydnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationcRSAdnamekK. Moriarty¢kaffiliationnCerticom Corp.dnamehD. Brown¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polkddatej‘zhfilenamegrfc5758eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127636hkeywordsÉxdigital signature algorithmx*elliptic curve digital signature algorithmcpkikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK\¶granking~crfcd5758irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure: Additional Algorithms and Identifiers for DSA and ECDSAdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document discusses current mobility extensions to IP-layer multicast.  It describes problems arising from mobile group communication in general, the case of multicast listener mobility, and problems for mobile senders using Any Source Multicast and Source-Specific Multicast.  Characteristic aspects of multicast routing and deployment issues for fixed IPv6 networks are summarized.  Specific properties and interplays with the underlying network access are surveyed with respect to the relevant technologies in the wireless domain.  It outlines the principal approaches to multicast mobility, together with a comprehensive exploration of the mobile multicast problem and solution space.  This document concludes with a conceptual road map for initial steps in standardization for use by future mobile multicast protocol designers.  This document is a product of the IP Mobility Optimizations (MobOpts) Research Group.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkHAW HamburgdnamejT. Schmidt¢kaffiliationhlink-labdnamelM. Waehlisch¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej‘}hfilenamegrfc5757eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmoboptsdfullx2mobopts - IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdnamex(IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127635hkeywordsäfPMIPv6fFMIPv6fHMIPv6cSSMcASMcMLDxMobile Multicast RoutingpHybrid MulticasthWirelessjMultipointkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateKÉ3ügranking}crfcd5757irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexUMulticast Mobility in Mobile IP Version 6 (MIPv6): Problem Statement and Brief SurveydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis document updates RFC 4055.  It updates the conventions for using the RSA Encryption Scheme - Optimal Asymmetric Encryption Padding (RSAES-OAEP) key transport algorithm in the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI).  Specifically, it updates the conventions for algorithm parameters in an X.509 certificate's subjectPublicKeyInfo field. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationhCerticomdnamehD. Brown¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefK. Yiu¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polkddatej‘Ohfilenamegrfc5756eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127634hkeywordsÉursa encryption schemex%optical asymmetric encryption paddingtsubjectpublickeyinfokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK]‡„granking|crfcd5756irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Updates for RSAES-OAEP and RSASSA-PSS Algorithm ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis specification defines a profile for the use of X.509 Attribute Certificates in Internet Protocols.  Attribute certificates may be used in a wide range of applications and environments covering a broad spectrum of interoperability goals and a broader spectrum of operational and assurance requirements.  The goal of this document is to establish a common baseline for generic applications requiring broad interoperability as well as limited special purpose requirements.  The profile places emphasis on attribute certificate support for Internet electronic mail, IPsec, and WWW security applications.  This document obsoletes RFC 3281. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘ihfilenamegrfc5755eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127633hkeywordsÑoelectronic maileemaileipseclwww securitykobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateK]‡Ÿgranking{crfcd5755irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;An Internet Attribute Certificate Profile for AuthorizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰This document describes the conventions for using the Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA) message digest algorithms (SHA-224, SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512) with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  It also describes the conventions for using these algorithms with the CMS and the Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA), Rivest Shamir Adleman (RSA), and Elliptic Curve DSA (ECDSA) signature algorithms.  Further, it provides SMIMECapabilities attribute values for each algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘Ihfilenamegrfc5754eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127632hkeywordséusecure hash algorithmxmessage digest algorithmgsha-224gsha-256gsha-384gsha-512ccmscdsaxdigital signature algorithmcrsaurivest sharmi adlemaneecdsarelliptic curve dsaqsmimecapabilitieskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateK]‡Ãgrankingzcrfcd5754irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Using SHA2 Algorithms with Cryptographic Message SyntaxdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹This document describes how to use Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) public key algorithms in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  The ECC algorithms support the creation of digital signatures and the exchange of keys to encrypt or authenticate content.  The definition of the algorithm processing is based on the NIST FIPS 186-3 for digital signature, NIST SP800-56A and SEC1 for key agreement, RFC 3370 and RFC 3565 for key wrap and content encryption, NIST FIPS 180-3 for message digest, SEC1 for key derivation, and RFC 2104 and RFC 4231 for message authentication code standards.  This document obsoletes RFC 3278.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationhCerticomdnamehD. Brownddatej‘fhfilenamegrfc5753eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127631hkeywordsÉjpublic keyrdigital signaturesnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateK]‡øgrankingycrfcd5753irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYUse of Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Algorithms in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀCryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) SignedData includes the SignerInfo structure to convey per-signer information.  SignedData supports multiple signers and multiple signature algorithms per signer with multiple SignerInfo structures.  If a signer attaches more than one SignerInfo, there are concerns that an attacker could perform a downgrade attack by removing the SignerInfo(s) with the \'strong' algorithm(s).  This document defines the multiple-signatures attribute, its generation rules, and its processing rules to allow signers to convey multiple SignerInfo objects while protecting against downgrade attacks.  Additionally, this attribute may assist during periods of algorithm migration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationlSoaring HawkdnameiJ. Schaadddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5752eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127630hkeywordsÑjsigneddatajsignerinfoqdowngrade attackssalgorithm migrationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK]‡±grankingxcrfcd5752irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Multiple Signatures in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document defines Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) version 3.2.  S/MIME provides a consistent way to send and receive secure MIME data.  Digital signatures provide authentication, message integrity, and non-repudiation with proof of origin.  Encryption provides data confidentiality.  Compression can be used to reduce data size.  This document obsoletes RFC 3851. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpBrute Squad LabsdnamekB. Ramsdell¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘Xhfilenamegrfc5751eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127629hkeywordsÜfsecurelmultipurposehinternetdmailjextensionsjencryptionkobsoletedByÅd8551epages-opublicationDateK]‡ügrankingwcrfcd5751irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWSecure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Version 3.2 Message SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document specifies conventions for X.509 certificate usage by Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) v3.2 agents.  S/MIME provides a method to send and receive secure MIME messages, and certificates are an integral part of S/MIME agent processing.  S/MIME agents validate certificates as described in RFC 5280, the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and CRL Profile.  S/MIME agents must meet the certificate processing requirements in this document as well as those in RFC 5280.  This document obsoletes RFC 3850. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpBrute Squad LabsdnamekB. Ramsdell¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej‘Xhfilenamegrfc5750eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127628hkeywordsàex.509jencryptionkcertificatelmultipurposehinternetdmailjextensionsfsecurekobsoletedByÅd8550epagesopublicationDateK]‡ågrankingvcrfcd5750irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSecure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Version 3.2 Certificate HandlingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an abstract mechanism for delivering root keys from an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) server to another network server that requires the keys for offering security protected services, such as re-authentication, to an EAP peer.  The distributed root key can be either a usage-specific root key (USRK), a domain-specific root key (DSRK), or a domain-specific usage- specific root key (DSUSRK) that has been derived from an Extended Master Session Key (EMSK) hierarchy previously established between the EAP server and an EAP peer.  This document defines a template for a key distribution exchange (KDE) protocol that can distribute these different types of root keys using a AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) protocol and discusses its security requirements.  The described protocol template does not specify message formats, data encoding, or other implementation details.  It thus needs to be instantiated with a specific protocol (e.g., RADIUS or Diameter) before it can be used. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameiK. Hoeper¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekM. Nakhjiri¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohbaddatej‘Åhfilenamegrfc5749eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127627hkeywordsÜhsecuritynauthenticationhmobilitycEAPnkey managementpkey distributionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKóÖgrankingucrfcd5749irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADistribution of EAP-Based Keys for Handover and Re-AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document updates IANA registries to support the SEED block cipher algorithm for the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) and the secure Real-time Transport Control Protocol (SRTCP) in Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx Korea Internet & Security AgencydnamegS. Yoon¢kaffiliationx Korea Internet & Security AgencydnamehJ. Jeong¢kaffiliationx Korea Internet & Security AgencydnamefH. Kim¢kaffiliationx Korea Internet & Security AgencydnamehH. Jeong¢kaffiliationx Korea Internet & Security AgencydnamefY. WonddatejÕhfilenamegrfc5748eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127626hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateL|õ∑grankingtcrfcd5748irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcIANA Registry Update for Support of the SEED Cipher Algorithm in Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyáThe emerging and growing deployment of IPv6 networks will introduce cases where connectivity with IPv4 networks crossing IPv6 transit backbones is desired.  This document describes a mechanism for automatic discovery and creation of IPv4-over-IPv6 tunnels via extensions to multiprotocol BGP.  It is targeted at connecting islands of IPv4 networks across an IPv6-only backbone without the need for a manually configured overlay of tunnels.  The mechanisms described in this document have been implemented, tested, and deployed on the large research IPv6 network in China.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph DromsgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Wu¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeM. Xu¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegC. Metzddatej‘^hfilenamegrfc5747eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127625hkeywordsÖiIPv4/IPv6kcoexistencedCNGIgCERNET2mSoftwire meshkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKóugrankingscrfcd5747irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexD4over6 Transit Solution Using IP Encapsulation and MP-BGP ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySecure Socket Layer (SSL) and Transport Layer Security (TLS) renegotiation are vulnerable to an attack in which the attacker forms a TLS connection with the target server, injects content of his choice, and then splices in a new TLS connection from a client.  The server treats the client's initial TLS handshake as a renegotiation and thus believes that the initial data transmitted by the attacker is from the same entity as the subsequent client data.  This specification defines a TLS extension to cryptographically tie renegotiations to the TLS connections they are being performed over, thus preventing this attack. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationkPhoneFactordnamefM. Ray¢kaffiliationkPhoneFactordnamekS. Dispensa¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehN. Oskovddatej‘Ühfilenamegrfc5746eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127624hkeywordsÇcsslssecure socket layerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKuΩ}grankingrcrfcd5746irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexATransport Layer Security (TLS) Renegotiation Indication ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyíThis document specifies the procedures by which authors of RFC IAB stream documents grant the community "incoming" rights for copying and using the text.  It also specifies the "outgoing" rights the community grants to readers and users of those documents, and it requests that the IETF Trust manage the outgoing rights to effect this result.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkFor the IABdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej‘Éhfilenamegrfc5745eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127623hkeywordsÉoincoming rightsooutgoing rightsjietf trustkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK0"egrankingqcrfcd5745irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex4Procedures for Rights Handling in the RFC IAB StreamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyûThis document specifies the procedures by which authors of RFC Independent Submission documents grant the community "incoming" rights for copying and using the text.  It also specifies the "outgoing" rights the community grants to readers and users of those documents, and it requests that the IETF Trust manage the outgoing rights to effect this result.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiR. Braden¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Halpernddatej‘xhfilenamegrfc5744eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127622hkeywordsÉoincoming rightsooutgoing rightsjietf trustkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK*™¢grankingpcrfcd5744irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGProcedures for Rights Handling in the RFC Independent Submission StreamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyzThis memo defines the publication stream for RFCs from the Internet Research Task Force.  Most documents undergoing this process will come from IRTF Research Groups, and it is expected that they will be published as Informational or Experimental RFCs by the RFC Editor.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIRTFdnamegA. Falkddatej‘4hfilenamegrfc5743eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-127621hkeywordsÅkirtf streamkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateK3√grankingocrfcd5743irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexDDefinition of an Internet Research Task Force (IRTF) Document StreamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document describes the procedures used by the IESG for handling documents submitted for RFC publication from the Independent Submission and IRTF streams.

This document updates procedures described in RFC 2026 and RFC 3710. This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrand¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. HousleyddatejÕ	hfilenamegrfc5742eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127620hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateK3¬˙grankingncrfcd5742irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber\etotaletitlexGIESG Procedures for Handling of Independent and IRTF Stream SubmissionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty⁄RFC documents contain a number of fixed elements such as the title page header, standard boilerplates, and copyright/IPR statements.  This document describes them and introduces some updates to reflect current usage and requirements of RFC publication.  In particular, this updated structure is intended to communicate clearly the source of RFC creation and review.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkFor the IABdnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationkFor the IABdnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliation`dnamecIABddatejÃ”hfilenamegrfc5741eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127619hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7841epagesopublicationDateK3¬„grankingmcrfcd5741irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex&RFC Streams, Headers, and BoilerplatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆThis document describes the messages and procedures of the Negative- ACKnowledgment (NACK) Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) protocol.  This protocol can provide end-to-end reliable transport of bulk data objects or streams over generic IP multicast routing and forwarding services.  NORM uses a selective, negative acknowledgment mechanism for transport reliability and offers additional protocol mechanisms to allow for operation with minimal a priori coordination among senders and receivers.  A congestion control scheme is specified to allow the NORM protocol to fairly share available network bandwidth with other transport protocols such as Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).  It is capable of operating with both reciprocal multicast routing among senders and receivers and with asymmetric connectivity (possibly a unicast return path) between the senders and receivers.  The protocol offers a number of features to allow different types of applications or possibly other higher-level transport protocols to utilize its service in different ways.  The protocol leverages the use of FEC-based (forward error correction) repair and other IETF Reliable Multicast Transport (RMT) building blocks in its design.  This document obsoletes RFC 3940. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnamejB. Adamson¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnameiJ. Mackerddatej‘rhfilenamegrfc5740eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127618hkeywordsáimulticastrreliable multicastitransportwnegative-acknowledgmentxforward error correctionupacket erasure codingsgroup communicationkobsoletedByÄepages`opublicationDateK)Rgrankinglcrfcd5740irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:NACK-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) Transport ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypWhen Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) is used for remote VPN access (client to VPN gateway), the gateway assigns the client an IP address from the internal network using IKEv2 configuration payloads.  The configuration payloads specified in RFC 4306 work well for IPv4 but make it difficult to use certain features of IPv6.  This document specifies new configuration attributes for IKEv2 that allows the VPN gateway to assign IPv6 prefixes to clients, enabling all features of IPv6 to be used with the client-gateway "virtual link".  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationmQUALCOMM Inc.dnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiC. Madsonddatej‘~hfilenamegrfc5739eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127617hkeywordsÉqremote vpn accesskvpn gatewaylvirtual linkkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateK}Öègrankingkcrfcd5739irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFIPv6 Configuration in Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis specification extends the Internet Message Access Protocol version 4rev1 (IMAP4rev1) to support UTF-8 encoded international characters in user names, mail addresses, and message headers.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuQualcomm IncorporateddnamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newmanddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5738eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127616hkeywordsÇx internet message access protocoliimap4rev1kobsoletedByÅd6855epagesopublicationDateKè+grankingjcrfcd5738irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIMAP Support for UTF-8dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThree IPv4 unicast address blocks are reserved for use in examples in specifications and other documents.  This document describes the use of these blocks.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiL. Vegodaddatej‘whfilenamegrfc5737eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127615hkeywordsÇqexample addressesdIPv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPÈgrankingicrfcd5737irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.IPv4 Address Blocks Reserved for DocumentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This is a direction to IANA concerning the creation and management of the IANA IPv4 Special Purpose Address Registry.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiL. VegodaddatejÃˆhfilenamegrfc5736eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127614hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd6890epagesopublicationDateKP€grankinghcrfcd5736irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*IANA IPv4 Special Purpose Address RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This document obsoletes RFC 3330.  It describes the global and other specialized IPv4 address blocks that have been assigned by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).  It does not address IPv4 address space assigned to operators and users through the Regional Internet Registries, nor does it address IPv4 address space assigned directly by IANA prior to the creation of the Regional Internet Registries.  It also does not address allocations or assignments of IPv6 addresses or autonomous system numbers.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiM. Cotton¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiL. Vegodaddatej‘lhfilenamegrfc5735eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127613hkeywordsÇqinternet protocolqspace assignmentskobsoletedByÅd6890epages
opublicationDateKP–grankinggcrfcd5735irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSpecial Use IPv4 AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6598∑habstracty^This document describes how an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) session is mapped onto a single Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) connection.  This mapping requires use of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to protect information exchanged between an EPP client and an EPP server.  This document obsoletes RFC 4934. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”Óhfilenamegrfc5734eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127612hkeywordsÖcEPPx Extensible Provisioning ProtocolcXMLcTCPcTLSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJús§grankingfcrfcd5734irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberEetotaletitlex9Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Transport over TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∑habstractyõThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of individual or organizational social information identifiers (known as "contacts") stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in Extensible Markup Language (XML), the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to contacts.  This document obsoletes RFC 4933. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej‘Yhfilenamegrfc5733eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127611hkeywordsÖcEPPx Extensible Provisioning ProtocolcXMLgcontactjregistrantkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateJúsègrankingecrfcd5733irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberEetotaletitlex6Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Contact MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyBThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of Internet host names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to host names.  This document obsoletes RFC 4932. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej‘hhfilenamegrfc5732eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127610hkeywordsÑcEPPx Extensible Provisioning ProtocolcXMLdhostkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJúsÇgrankingdcrfcd5732irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberEetotaletitlex3Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Host MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyFThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of Internet domain names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to domain names.  This document obsoletes RFC 4931. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej‘hhfilenamegrfc5731eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127609hkeywordsÖcEPPx Extensible Provisioning ProtocolcXMLfdomainkdomain namekobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateJúsugrankingccrfcd5731irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberEetotaletitlex:Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Domain Name MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty”This document describes an application-layer client-server protocol for the provisioning and management of objects stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the protocol defines generic object management operations and an extensible framework that maps protocol operations to objects.  This document includes a protocol specification, an object mapping template, and an XML media type registration.  This document obsoletes RFC 4930. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej‘ghfilenamegrfc5730eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127608hkeywordsÅxshared framework mappingkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateJúsagrankingbcrfcd5730irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberEetotaletitlex&Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This specification defines the behavior required of Diameter agents to route requests when the User-Name Attribute Value Pair contains a Network Access Identifier formatted with multiple realms.  These multi-realm, or "Decorated", Network Access Identifiers are used in order to force the routing of request messages through a predefined list of mediating realms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnameiL. Morand¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegT. Tsouddatej‘|hfilenamegrfc5729eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127607hkeywordsÑcnaixnetwork access identifieridecoratedkmulti-realmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK2ekgrankingacrfcd5729irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVClarifications on the Routing of Diameter Requests Based on the Username and the RealmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis document describes the MIB module for the Digital Video Broadcasting Return Channel via Satellite system (DVB-RCS), as defined by the SatLabs Group.  It defines a set of MIB objects to characterize the behavior and performance of network-layer entities deploying DVB-RCS.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmSatLabs GroupdnameiS. Combes¢kaffiliationmSatLabs GroupdnamekP. Amundsen¢kaffiliationmSatLabs GroupdnamejM. Lambert¢kaffiliationmSatLabs GroupdnamejH-P. Lexowddatej‘Ähfilenamegrfc5728eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127606hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basex)digital video broadcasting return channelkDVB-RCS-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages_opublicationDateK©
granking`crfcd5728irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe SatLabs Group DVB-RCS MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractycThis memo documents a process intended to organize the future development of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and related work in the Real-time Applications and Infrastructure (RAI) Area.  As the environments in which SIP is deployed grow more numerous and diverse, modifying or extending SIP in certain ways may threaten the interoperability and security of the protocol; however, the IETF process must also cater to the realities of existing deployments and serve the needs of the implementers working with SIP.  This document therefore defines the functions of two long-lived working groups in the RAI Area that are, respectively, responsible for the maintenance of the core SIP specifications and the development of new efforts to extend and apply work in this space.  This document obsoletes RFC 3427.  This memo documents an Internet Best Current Practice.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparksddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5727eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127605hkeywordsÇcRAIgsippingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKólgranking_crfcd5727irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberCetotaletitlexoChange Process for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreadtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7957µhabstracty<Mobile IPv6 (RFC 3775) enables a mobile node to remain reachable while it roams on the Internet.  However, the location and movement of the mobile node can be revealed by the IP addresses used in signaling or data packets.  In this document, we consider the Mobile IPv6 location privacy problem described in RFC 4882, and propose efficient and secure techniques to protect location privacy of the mobile node.  This document is a product of the IP Mobility Optimizations (MobOpts) Research Group.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxInstitute for Infocomm ResearchdnamefY. Qiu¢kaffiliationfGooglednamegF. Zhao¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Koodliddatej‘uhfilenamegrfc5726eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmoboptsdfullx2mobopts - IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdnamex(IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127604hkeywordsÅgmoboptskobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateK}Ö†granking^crfcd5726irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex&Mobile IPv6 Location Privacy SolutionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThis document defines a new report block type within the framework of RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Reports (XRs).  One of the initial XR report block types is the Loss Run Length Encoding (RLE) Report Block.  This report conveys information regarding the individual Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) packet receipt and loss events experienced during the RTCP interval preceding the transmission of the report.  The new report, which is referred to as the Post-repair Loss RLE report, carries information regarding the packets that remain lost after all loss-repair methods are applied.  By comparing the RTP packet receipts/losses before and after the loss repair is completed, one can determine the effectiveness of the loss- repair methods in an aggregated fashion.  This document also defines the signaling of the Post-repair Loss RLE report in the Session Description Protocol (SDP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Begen¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefD. Hsu¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Lagueddatej‘Ähfilenamegrfc5725eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127603hkeywordsÉkLoss repairnretransmissioncFECkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateKàó;granking]crfcd5725irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Post-Repair Loss RLE Report Block Type for RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Extended Reports (XRs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo specifies the Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme "sms" for specifying one or more recipients for an SMS message.  SMS messages are two-way paging messages that can be sent from and received by a mobile phone or a suitably equipped networked device. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamehE. Wilde¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamenA. Vaha-Sipiladdatej‘~hfilenamegrfc5724eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127602hkeywordsÉcGSMcSMSjURI schemekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKFÅ˝granking\crfcd5724irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXURI Scheme for Global System for Mobile Communications (GSM) Short Message Service (SMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThe Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocol has a certain computational and communication overhead with respect to the number of round trips required and the cryptographic operations involved. In remote access situations, the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) is used for authentication, which adds several more round trips and consequently latency.

To re-establish security associations (SAs) upon a failure recovery condition is time consuming especially when an IPsec peer (such as a VPN gateway) needs to re-establish a large number of SAs with various endpoints. A high number of concurrent sessions might cause additional problems for an IPsec peer during SA re-establishment.

In order to avoid the need to re-run the key exchange protocol from scratch, it would be useful to provide an efficient way to resume an IKE/IPsec session. This document proposes an extension to IKEv2 that allows a client to re-establish an IKE SA with a gateway in a highly efficient manner, utilizing a previously established IKE SA.

A client can reconnect to a gateway from which it was disconnected. The proposed approach encodes partial IKE state into an opaque ticket, which can be stored on the client or in a centralized store, and is later made available to the IKEv2 responder for re-authentication. We use the term ticket to refer to the opaque data that is created by the IKEv2 responder. This document does not specify the format of the ticket but examples are provided. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamejY. Sheffer¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej‘~hfilenamegrfc5723eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127601hkeywordsâcIKEuInternet Key Exchangersession resumptionhfailoverqhigh availabilitytcryptographic ticketscryptographic tokensstateful resumptiontstateless resumptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKFÅÿgranking[crfcd5723irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCInternet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2) Session ResumptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0The fragmentation and reassembly algorithm specified in the base IPv6 specification allows fragments to overlap.  This document demonstrates the security issues associated with allowing overlapping fragments and updates the IPv6 specification to explicitly forbid overlapping fragments. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnanddatej‘}hfilenamegrfc5722eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127600hkeywordsÇmfragmentationuoverlapping fragmentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK2e[grankingZcrfcd5722irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Handling of Overlapping IPv6 FragmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6946∂habstracty$This specification extends the Post Office Protocol version 3 (POP3) to support un-encoded international characters in user names, passwords, mail addresses, message headers, and protocol-level textual error strings.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuQUALCOMM IncorporateddnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newmanddatej‘|hfilenamegrfc5721eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127599hkeywordsÜcPOPdUTF8dmaileemailtinternationalizationgcharsetkobsoletedByÅd6856epagesopublicationDateKàó+grankingYcrfcd5721irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevPOP3 Support for UTF-8dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty˜RANGER is an architectural framework for scalable routing and addressing in networks with global enterprise recursion.  The term "enterprise network" within this context extends to a wide variety of use cases and deployment scenarios, where an "enterprise" can be as small as a Small Office, Home Office (SOHO) network, as dynamic as a Mobile Ad Hoc Network, as complex as a multi-organizational corporation, or as large as the global Internet itself.  Such networks will require an architected solution for the coordination of routing and addressing plans with accommodations for scalability, provider-independence, mobility, multihoming, and security.  These considerations are particularly true for existing deployments, but the same principles apply even for clean-slate approaches.  The RANGER architecture addresses these requirements and provides a comprehensive framework for IPv6/IPv4 coexistence.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtBoeing Phantom WorksdnamejF. Templinddatej‘5hfilenamegrfc5720eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127598hkeywordsÅrenterprise networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÉ3ëgrankingXcrfcd5720irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLRouting and Addressing in Networks with Global Enterprise Recursion (RANGER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«The Diameter base specification, described in RFC 3588, provides a number of ways to extend Diameter, with new Diameter commands (i.e., messages used by Diameter applications) and applications as the most extensive enhancements. RFC 3588 illustrates the conditions that lead to the need to define a new Diameter application or a new command code. Depending on the scope of the Diameter extension, IETF actions are necessary. Although defining new Diameter applications does not require IETF consensus, defining new Diameter commands requires IETF consensus per RFC 3588. This has led to questionable design decisions by other Standards Development Organizations, which chose to define new applications on existing commands -- rather than asking for assignment of new command codes -- for the pure purpose of avoiding bringing their specifications to the IETF. In some cases, interoperability problems were an effect of the poor design caused by overloading existing commands.

This document aligns the extensibility rules of the Diameter application with the Diameter commands, offering ways to delegate work on Diameter to other SDOs to extend Diameter in a way that does not lead to poor design choices. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej‘xhfilenamegrfc5719eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127597hkeywordsÇtdiameter applicationqdiameter commandskobsoletedByÅd6733epagesopublicationDateKFÇgrankingWcrfcd5719irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAUpdated IANA Considerations for Diameter Command Code AllocationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThe Generic Associated Channel (G-ACh) has been defined as a generalization of the pseudowire (PW) associated control channel to enable the realization of a control/communication channel that is associated with Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs), MPLS PWs, MPLS LSP segments, and MPLS sections between adjacent MPLS-capable devices.

The MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP) is a profile of the MPLS architecture that identifies elements of the MPLS toolkit that may be combined to build a carrier-grade packet transport network based on MPLS packet switching technology.

This document describes how the G-ACh may be used to provide the infrastructure that forms part of the Management Communication Network (MCN) and a Signaling Communication Network (SCN). Collectively, the MCN and SCN may be referred to as the Data Communication Network (DCN). This document explains how MCN and SCN messages are encapsulated, carried on the G-ACh, and demultiplexed for delivery to the management or signaling/routing control plane components on an MPLS-TP node. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiD. Beller¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘|hfilenamegrfc5718eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127596hkeywordsÜgMPLS-TPcDCNcSCNcMCNeG-AchcGALkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKFÅQgrankingVcrfcd5718irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDAn In-Band Data Communication Network For the MPLS Transport ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~The Network Configuration protocol (NETCONF) defines the lock and unlock Remote Procedure Calls (RPCs), used to lock entire configuration datastores.  In some situations, a way to lock only parts of a configuration datastore is required.  This document defines a capability-based extension to the NETCONF protocol for locking portions of a configuration datastore. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejB. Lengyel¢kaffiliationnTail-f SystemsdnamelM. Bjorklundddatej‘|hfilenamegrfc5717eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127595hkeywordsÇdYANGrNetwork ManagementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK0";grankingUcrfcd5717irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Partial Lock Remote Procedure Call (RPC) for NETCONFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡This document describes and lists the functional requirements of a federated file system and defines related terms.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamejJ. Lentini¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnamekC. Everhart¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnameiD. Ellard¢kaffiliationkIBM AlmadendnameiR. Tewari¢kaffiliationkIBM AlmadendnamegM. Naikddatej‘{hfilenamegrfc5716eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127594hkeywordsÖvFederated File SystemslFederated FAeFedFSfFed-FSjFederationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPxgrankingTcrfcd5716irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Requirements for Federated File SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNA micro-loop is a packet forwarding loop that may occur transiently among two or more routers in a hop-by-hop packet forwarding paradigm.

This framework provides a summary of the causes and consequences of micro-loops and enables the reader to form a judgement on whether micro-looping is an issue that needs to be addressed in specific networks. It also provides a survey of the currently proposed mechanisms that may be used to prevent or to suppress the formation of micro-loops when an IP or MPLS network undergoes topology change due to failure, repair, or management action. When sufficiently fast convergence is not available and the topology is susceptible to micro-loops, use of one or more of these mechanisms may be desirable. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryantddatej‘zhfilenamegrfc5715eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127593hkeywordsäoIP Fast RerouteqMPLS Fast ReroutesRouting ConvergencepNetwork TopologydPLSNgnot-viapIncremental CostnPacket Markingkordered fibdofibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPƒgrankingScrfcd5715irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%A Framework for Loop-Free ConvergencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis document provides a framework for the development of IP fast- reroute mechanisms that provide protection against link or router failure by invoking locally determined repair paths.  Unlike MPLS fast-reroute, the mechanisms are applicable to a network employing conventional IP routing and forwarding.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryantddatej‘zhfilenamegrfc5714eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127592hkeywordsÖoIP Fast RerouteqMPLS Fast ReroutesRouting ConvergenceqNetwork  Topologyuloop-free-convergencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPπgrankingRcrfcd5714irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP Fast Reroute FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThe Access Node Control Protocol (ANCP) aims to communicate Quality of Service (QoS)-related, service-related, and subscriber-related configurations and operations between a Network Access Server (NAS) and an Access Node (e.g., a Digital Subscriber Line Access Multiplexer (DSLAM)). The main goal of this protocol is to allow the NAS to configure, manage, and control access equipment, including the ability for the Access Nodes to report information to the NAS.

This present document investigates security threats that all ANCP nodes could encounter. This document develops a threat model for ANCP security, with the aim of deciding which security functions are required. Based on this, security requirements regarding the Access Node Control Protocol are defined. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekH. Moustafa¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemS. De Cnodderddatej‘xhfilenamegrfc5713eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdancpdfullx#ancp - Access Node Control ProtocoldnamexAccess Node Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127591hkeywordsÉmANCP securitylANCP threatslANCP attackskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKP¨grankingQcrfcd5713irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSecurity Threats and Security Requirements for the Access Node Control Protocol (ANCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document specifies Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering Soft Preemption, a suite of protocol modifications extending the concept of preemption with the goal of reducing or eliminating traffic disruption of preempted Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs).  Initially, MPLS RSVP-TE was defined with support for only immediate TE LSP displacement upon preemption.  The utilization of a reroute request notification helps more gracefully mitigate the reroute process of preempted TE LSP.  For the brief period soft preemption is activated, reservations (though not necessarily traffic levels) are in effect under-provisioned until the TE LSP(s) can be rerouted.  For this reason, the feature is primarily, but not exclusively, interesting in MPLS-enabled IP networks with Differentiated Services and Traffic Engineering capabilities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoBritish TelecomdnamehM. Meyer¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseurddatej‘shfilenamegrfc5712eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127590hkeywordsÉxmultiprotocol label switchinggmpls-tefte lspkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPõgrankingPcrfcd5712irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(MPLS Traffic Engineering Soft PreemptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄The aim of this document is to describe a common practice with regard to the behavior of nodes that send and receive a Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Traffic Engineering (TE) Path Error messages for a preempted Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) or Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering Label Switched Path (TE LSP). (For reference to the notion of TE LSP preemption, see RFC 3209.) This document does not define any new protocol extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehI. Mineiddatej‘zhfilenamegrfc5711eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127589hkeywordsÅgrsvp-tekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPègrankingOcrfcd5711irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeNode Behavior upon Originating and Receiving Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Path Error MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document describes how Resource ReserVation Protocol (RSVP) PathErr messages may be used to trigger rerouting of Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) point-to-point Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) without first removing LSP state or resources.  Such LSP rerouting may be desirable in a number of cases, including, for example, soft-preemption and graceful shutdown.  This document describes the usage of existing Standards Track mechanisms to support LSP rerouting.  In this case, it relies on mechanisms already defined as part of RSVP-TE and simply describes a sequence of actions to be executed.  While existing protocol definitions can be used to support reroute applications, this document also defines a new reroute-specific error code to allow for the future definition of reroute-application-specific error values. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationnAlcatel LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekJP. Vasseurddatej‘uhfilenamegrfc5710eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127588hkeywordsÖxresource reservation protocoldrsvpxmultiprotocol label switchingpgeneralized mplsgrsvp-tekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPÉgrankingNcrfcd5710irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5PathErr Message Triggered MPLS and GMPLS LSP ReroutesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis document describes how the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) Secure Hash Standard family of algorithms can be used with OSPF version 2's built-in, cryptographic authentication mechanism.  This updates, but does not supercede, the cryptographic authentication mechanism specified in RFC 2328. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnameiM. Bhatia¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationsAegis Data SecuritydnamehM. Fanto¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. White¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnamekR. Atkinsonddatej‘phfilenamegrfc5709eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127587hkeywordsÑxopen shortest path firstdnistusecure  hash standardx"hashed message authentication codekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ‡ÓogrankingMcrfcd5709irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,OSPFv2 HMAC-SHA Cryptographic AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7474µhabstractyyThis memo describes X.509 key identifiers and signature encoding for version 2 of the KeyNote trust-management system (RFC 2704). X.509 certificates (RFC 5280) can be directly used in the Authorizer or Licensees field (or in both fields) in a KeyNote assertion, allowing for easy integration with protocols that already use X.509 certificates for authentication.

In addition, the document defines additional signature types that use other hash functions (beyond the MD5 and SHA1 hash functions that are defined in RFC 2792). This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim PolkgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamelA. KeromytisddatejÃ®hfilenamegrfc5708eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127586hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK\¶grankingLcrfcd5708irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHX.509 Key and Signature Encoding for the KeyNote Trust Management SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyóThe Media Server Markup Language (MSML) is used to control and invoke many different types of services on IP media servers.  The MSML control interface was initially driven by RadiSys with subsequent significant contributions from Intel, Dialogic, and others in the industry.  Clients can use it to define how multimedia sessions interact on a media server and to apply services to individuals or groups of users.  MSML can be used, for example, to control media server conferencing features such as video layout and audio mixing, create sidebar conferences or personal mixes, and set the properties of media streams.  As well, clients can use MSML to define media processing dialogs, which may be used as parts of application interactions with users or conferences.  Transformation of media streams to and from users or conferences as well as interactive voice response (IVR) dialogs are examples of such interactions, which are specified using MSML.  MSML clients may also invoke dialogs with individual users or with groups of conference participants using VoiceXMLThis document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemRobert SparksgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgRadiSysdnameiA. Saleem¢kaffiliationgRadiSysdnamefY. Xin¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekG. SharrattddatejÃ)hfilenamegrfc5707eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127585hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages∏opublicationDateKj'HgrankingKcrfcd5707irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex#Media Server Markup Language (MSML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿNew protocols or protocol extensions are best designed with due consideration of the functionality needed to operate and manage the protocols.  Retrofitting operations and management is sub-optimal.  The purpose of this document is to provide guidance to authors and reviewers of documents that define new protocols or protocol extensions regarding aspects of operations and management that should be considered.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrHuaweiSymantec USAdnamemD. Harringtonddatej‘whfilenamegrfc5706eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127584hkeywordsÇjmanagementjoperationskobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateJÛhágrankingJcrfcd5706irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Guidelines for Considering Operations and Management of New Protocols and Protocol ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıA number of protocols wish to leverage Transport Layer Security (TLS) to perform key establishment but then use some of the keying material for their own purposes.  This document describes a general mechanism for allowing that. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorladdatej‘vhfilenamegrfc5705eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127583hkeywordsÅqkey establishmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKûÃ¢grankingIcrfcd5705irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Keying Material Exporters for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8447d8446¥habstractyØThis document identifies problems that may result from the absence of formal coordination and joint development on protocols of mutual interest between standards development organizations (SDOs). Some of these problems may cause significant harm to the Internet. The document suggests that a robust procedure is required prevent this from occurring in the future. The IAB has selected a number of case studies, such as Transport MPLS (T-MPLS), as recent examples to describe the hazard to the Internet architecture that results from uncoordinated adaptation of a protocol.

This experience has resulted in a considerable improvement in the relationship between the IETF and the ITU-T. In particular, this was achieved via the establishment of the "Joint working team on MPLS-TP". In addition, the leadership of the two organizations agreed to improve inter-organizational working practices so as to avoid conflict in the future between ITU-T Recommendations and IETF RFCs.

Whilst we use ITU-T - IETF interactions in these case studies, the scope of the document extends to all SDOs that have an overlapping protocol interest with the IETF. This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkFor the IABdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationkFor the IABdnameiM. Morrow¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej‘whfilenamegrfc5704eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127582hkeywordsÖeITU-TgMPLS-TPfT-MPLSrJoint working teamcJWTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK)?grankingHcrfcd5704irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex5Uncoordinated Protocol Development Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx®This document defines extensions to the Sieve email filtering language to permit analysis and manipulation of the MIME body parts of an email message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej‘whfilenamegrfc5703eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127581hkeywordsÑeEmailoElectronic MailmInternet MailqMessage FilteringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJËrLgrankingGcrfcd5703irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYSieve Email Filtering: MIME Part Tests, Iteration, Extraction, Replacement, and EnclosuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx–This document describes how to produce RSA/SHA-256 and RSA/SHA-512 DNSKEY and RRSIG resource records for use in the Domain Name System Security Extensions (RFC 4033, RFC 4034, and RFC 4035). [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnameiJ. Jansenddatej‘vhfilenamegrfc5702eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127580hkeywordsÑfDNSSECcRSAgSHA-256gSHA-512kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateJËôKgrankingFcrfcd5702irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPUse of SHA-2 Algorithms with RSA in DNSKEY and RRSIG Resource Records for DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6944∂habstractyΩCurrent specifications of BGP Extended Communities (RFC 4360) support the IPv4 Address Specific Extended Community, but do not support an IPv6 Address Specific Extended Community.  The lack of an IPv6 Address Specific Extended Community may be a problem when an application uses the IPv4 Address Specific Extended Community, and one wants to use this application in a pure IPv6 environment.  This document defines a new BGP attribute, the IPv6 Address Specific Extended Community, that addresses this problem.  The IPv6 Address Specific Extended Community is similar to the IPv4 Address Specific Extended Community, except that it carries an IPv6 address rather than an IPv4 address. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej‘vhfilenamegrfc5701eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127579hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK)1grankingEcrfcd5701irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6IPv6 Address Specific BGP Extended Community AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7606d7153∂habstracty“Since cryptographic algorithms can become weak over the years, it is necessary to evaluate their security suitability.  When signing or verifying data, or when encrypting or decrypting data, these evaluations must be considered.  This document specifies a data structure that enables an automated analysis of the security suitability of a given cryptographic algorithm at a given point of time, which may be in the past, the present, or the future. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer SITdnamegT. Kunz¢kaffiliationtpawisda systems GmbHdnamejS. Okunick¢kaffiliationwFraunhofer GesellschaftdnamekU. Pordeschddatej‘rhfilenamegrfc5698eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeltansdfullx-ltans - Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdnamex%Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-127578hkeywordsÖqlong term archivehsecurityfpolicynhash algorithmtpublic key algorithmkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateJÛhegrankingBcrfcd5698irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNData Structure for the Security Suitability of Cryptographic Algorithms (DSSC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySome applications that associate state information with public key certificates can benefit from a way to link together a set of certificates that belong to the same end entity and that can safely be considered equivalent to one another for the purposes of referencing that application-state information.  This memo defines a certificate extension that allows applications to establish the required linkage without introducing a new application protocol data unit.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej‘rhfilenamegrfc5697eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127577hkeywordsÅhtemplatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÛhVgrankingAcrfcd5697irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOther Certificates ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe objective of the Pre-Congestion Notification (PCN) architecture is to protect the quality of service (QoS) of inelastic flows within a Diffserv domain.  It achieves this by marking packets belonging to PCN-flows when the rate of traffic exceeds certain configured thresholds on links in the domain.  These marks can then be evaluated to determine how close the domain is to being congested.  This document specifies how such marks are encoded into the IP header by redefining the Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) codepoints within such domains.  The baseline encoding described here provides only two PCN encoding states: Not-marked and PCN-marked.  Future extensions to this encoding may be needed in order to provide more than one level of marking severity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamelT. Moncaster¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationwUniversity of WuerzburgdnamehM. Menthddatej‘rhfilenamegrfc5696eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcndfullx0pcn - Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdnamex*Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127576hkeywordsÜrQuality of ServicecQoSwDifferentiated ServicesqAdmission ControliCodepointhProtocolkobsoletedByÅd6660epagesopublicationDateK)granking@crfcd5696irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Baseline Encoding and Transport of Pre-Congestion InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThis document describes a methodology specific to the benchmarking of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) forwarding devices, limited to the most common MPLS packet forwarding scenarios and delay measurements for each, considering IP flows.  It builds upon the tenets set forth in RFC 2544, RFC 1242, and other IETF Benchmarking Methodology Working Group (BMWG) efforts.  This document seeks to extend these efforts to the MPLS paradigm.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiA. Akhter¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Asati¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataroddatej‘vhfilenamegrfc5695eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-127575hkeywordsÇxmultiprotocol label switchingxmpmls forwarding deviceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÛhIgranking?crfcd5695irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5MPLS Forwarding Benchmarking Methodology for IP FlowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty7In this document, we provide a survey of P2P (Peer-to-Peer) systems.  The survey includes a definition and several taxonomies of P2P systems.  This survey also includes a description of which types of applications can be built with P2P technologies and examples of P2P applications that are currently in use on the Internet.  Finally, we discuss architectural trade-offs and provide guidelines for deciding whether or not a P2P architecture would be suitable to meet the requirements of a given application.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkFor the IABdnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej‘uhfilenamegrfc5694eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127574hkeywordsÉcP2PmdecentralizedlarchitecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK)granking>crfcd5694irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexTPeer-to-Peer (P2P) Architecture: Definition, Taxonomies, Examples, and ApplicabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçDistributed applications -- such as file sharing, real-time communication, and live and on-demand media streaming -- prevalent on the Internet use a significant amount of network resources. Such applications often transfer large amounts of data through connections established between nodes distributed across the Internet with little knowledge of the underlying network topology. Some applications are so designed that they choose a random subset of peers from a larger set with which to exchange data. Absent any topology information guiding such choices, or acting on suboptimal or local information obtained from measurements and statistics, these applications often make less than desirable choices.

This document discusses issues related to an information-sharing service that enables applications to perform better-than-random peer selection. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Seedorf¢kaffiliationlNeustar Inc.dnameiE. Burgerddatej‘uhfilenamegrfc5693eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdaltodfullx-alto - Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdnamex&Application-Layer Traffic Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127573hkeywordsÇlpeer-to-peercp2pkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ‡ÓEgranking=crfcd5693irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Application-Layer Traffic Optimization (ALTO) Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the transmission of IPv4 over Ethernet, as well as IPv6 over Ethernet, in an access network deploying the IEEE 802.16 cellular radio transmission technology.  The Ethernet on top of IEEE 802.16 is realized by bridging connections that IEEE 802.16 provides between a base station and its associated subscriber stations.  Due to the resource constraints of radio transmission systems and the limitations of the IEEE 802.16 Media Access Control (MAC) functionality for the realization of an Ethernet, the transmission of IP over Ethernet over IEEE 802.16 may considerably benefit by adding IP-specific support functions in the Ethernet over IEEE 802.16 while maintaining full compatibility with standard IP over Ethernet behavior. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamegH. Jeon¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamehS. Jeong¢kaffiliationcNSNdnameiM. Riegelddatej‘thfilenamegrfc5692eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd16ngdfullx#16ng - IP over IEEE 802.16 NetworksdnamexIP over IEEE 802.16 Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127572hkeywordsÑfBridgeeWiMAXkEthernet-CShCellularkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJËôAgranking<crfcd5692irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Transmission of IP over Ethernet over IEEE 802.16 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThis memo describes extensions for the RTP payload format defined in RFC 3640 for the transport of MPEG Surround multi-channel audio.  Additional Media Type parameters are defined to signal backwards- compatible transmission inside an MPEG-4 Audio elementary stream.  In addition, a layered transmission scheme that doesn't use the MPEG-4 systems framework is presented to transport an MPEG Surround elementary stream via RTP in parallel with an RTP stream containing the downmixed audio data. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsPhilips ElectronicsdnamejF. de Bont¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer IISdnameiS. Doehla¢kaffiliationrDolby LaboratoriesdnamejM. Schmidt¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer IISdnamepR. Sperschneiderddatej‘shfilenamegrfc5691eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127571hkeywordsÖmMPEG SurroundhRFC 3640cRTPfMPEG-4cAACkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJËr?granking;crfcd5691irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRTP Payload Format for Elementary Streams with MPEG Surround Multi-Channel AudiodtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÒThis document describes a possible congestion control mechanism for acknowledgement (ACKs) traffic in TCP.  The document specifies an end-to-end acknowledgement congestion control mechanism for TCP that uses participation from both TCP hosts: the TCP data sender and the TCP data receiver.  The TCP data sender detects lost or Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN)-marked ACK packets, and tells the TCP data receiver the ACK Ratio R to use to respond to the congestion on the reverse path from the data receiver to the data sender.  The TCP data receiver sends roughly one ACK packet for every R data packets received.  This mechanism is based on the acknowledgement congestion control in the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol's (DCCP's) Congestion Control Identifier (CCID) 2.  This acknowledgement congestion control mechanism is being specified for further evaluation by the network community.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekLars EggertgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationpTELECOM BretagnednamehA. Arcia¢kaffiliationpTELECOM BretagnednamefD. Ros¢kaffiliationxFranklin & Marshall CollegednamejJ. Iyengarddatej‘Khfilenamegrfc5690eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127570hkeywordsÅeackcckobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateKj'=granking:crfcd5690irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex0Adding Acknowledgement Congestion Control to TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÚThis specification extends the Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) MKCOL (Make Collection) method to allow collections of arbitrary resourcetype to be created and to allow properties to be set at the same time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej‘ohfilenamegrfc5689eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhvcarddavdfullxvcarddav - vCard and CardDAVdnameqvCard and CardDAVdtypebwgbidjdoc-127569hkeywordsÇfwebdavdHTTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ√Ûégranking9crfcd5689irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDExtended MKCOL for Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThe caller preferences specification for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) allows a caller to express preferences that the call be routed to a User Agent (UA) with particular capabilities.  Similarly, a specification exists to allow a UA to indicate its capabilities in a registration.  Amongst those capabilities are the type of media streams the agent supports, described as top-level MIME types.  The 'application' MIME type is used to describe a broad range of stream types, and it provides insufficient granularity as a capability.  This specification allows a UA to indicate which application subtypes the agent supports. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeSkypednamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘qhfilenamegrfc5688eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127568hkeywordsÇcSIPcIMSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKFÅÈgranking8crfcd5688irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Media Feature Tag for MIME Application SubtypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊This document provides a problem statement, lists requirements, and captures design aspects for a GEOPRIV Layer 7 (L7) Location Configuration Protocol (LCP).  This protocol aims to allow an end host to obtain location information, by value or by reference, from a Location Information Server (LIS) that is located in the access network.  The obtained location information can then be used for a variety of different protocols and purposes.  For example, it can be used as input to the Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Protocol or to convey location within the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) to other entities.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej‘shfilenamegrfc5687eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127567hkeywordsÑtLocation InformationxLocation Information ServerqLocation by ValueuLocation by ReferencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKóKgranking7crfcd5687irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSGEOPRIV Layer 7 Location Configuration Protocol: Problem Statement and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes the RTP payload format of a mU-law EMbedded Coder for Low-delay IP communication (UEMCLIP), an enhanced speech codec of ITU-T G.711.  The bitstream has a scalable structure with an embedded u-law bitstream, also known as PCMU, thus providing a handy transcoding operation between narrowband and wideband speech. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamekY. Hiwasaki¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnameiH. Ohmuroddatej‘thfilenamegrfc5686eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127566hkeywordsÖpRTP Payload typedMIMEgUEMCLIPdPCMUmSpeech CodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJËp;granking6crfcd5686irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbRTP Payload Format for mU-law EMbedded Codec for Low-delay IP Communication (UEMCLIP) Speech CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThe Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) is a protocol for setting up Virtual Private Network (VPN) tunnels from a remote location to a gateway so that the VPN client can access services in the network behind the gateway.  This document defines an IKEv2 extension that allows an overloaded VPN gateway or a VPN gateway that is being shut down for maintenance to redirect the VPN client to attach to another gateway.  The proposed mechanism can also be used in Mobile IPv6 to enable the home agent to redirect the mobile node to another home agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhWiChorusdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamejK. Wenigerddatej‘shfilenamegrfc5685eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgipsecmedfullx0ipsecme - IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex&IP Security Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127565hkeywordsÜnIKEv2 RedirecthREDIRECToREDIRECTED_FROMpanycast redirectshome agent redirectrVPN gateway directkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÛh:granking5crfcd5685irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRedirect Mechanism for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÂThis document identifies two deployment scenarios that have arisen from the unconventional network topologies formed using Network Address Translator (NAT) devices.  First, the simplicity of administering networks through the combination of NAT and DHCP has increasingly lead to the deployment of multi-level inter-connected private networks involving overlapping private IP address spaces.  Second, the proliferation of private networks in enterprises, hotels and conferences, and the wide-spread use of Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) to access an enterprise intranet from remote locations has increasingly lead to overlapping private IP address space between remote and corporate networks.  This document does not dismiss these unconventional scenarios as invalid, but recognizes them as real and offers recommendations to help ensure these deployments can function without a meltdown.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqMagnus WesterlundgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamelP. Srisuresh¢kaffiliationoYale UniversitydnamegB. Fordddatej‘8hfilenamegrfc5684eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127564hkeywordsÅxnetwork address translatorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKj'1granking4crfcd5684irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexIUnintended Consequences of NAT Deployments with Overlapping Address SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty£This document proposes to add mutual authentication, based on a human-memorizable password, to the basic, unauthenticated Diffie-Hellman key exchange. The proposed algorithm is called the Password-Authenticated Key (PAK) exchange. PAK allows two parties to authenticate themselves while performing the Diffie-Hellman exchange.

The protocol is secure against all passive and active attacks. In particular, it does not allow either type of attacker to obtain any information that would enable an offline dictionary attack on the password. PAK provides Forward Secrecy. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim PolkgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamenA. Brusilovsky¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekI. Faynberg¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamejZ. Zeltsan¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamehS. PatelddatejÀ—hfilenamegrfc5683eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127563hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateKpŒègranking3crfcd5683irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8Password-Authenticated Key (PAK) Diffie-Hellman ExchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThe purpose of this document is to move the F-RTO (Forward RTO-Recovery) functionality for TCP in RFC 4138 from Experimental to Standards Track status. The F-RTO support for Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) in RFC 4138 remains with Experimental status. See Appendix B for the differences between this document and RFC 4138.

Spurious retransmission timeouts cause suboptimal TCP performance because they often result in unnecessary retransmission of the last window of data. This document describes the F-RTO detection algorithm for detecting spurious TCP retransmission timeouts. F-RTO is a TCP sender-only algorithm that does not require any TCP options to operate. After retransmitting the first unacknowledged segment triggered by a timeout, the F-RTO algorithm of the TCP sender monitors the incoming acknowledgments to determine whether the timeout was spurious. It then decides whether to send new segments or retransmit unacknowledged segments. The algorithm effectively helps to avoid additional unnecessary retransmissions and thereby improves TCP performance in the case of a spurious timeout. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamelP. Sarolahti¢kaffiliationvUniversity of HelsinkidnamegM. Kojo¢kaffiliationjNTT DocomodnamekK. Yamamoto¢kaffiliationjNTT DocomodnamegM. Hataddatej‘>hfilenamegrfc5682eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127562hkeywordsÉdSACKxtransmission control protocolmloss recoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ√Û{granking2crfcd5682irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbForward RTO-Recovery (F-RTO): An Algorithm for Detecting Spurious Retransmission Timeouts with TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document defines TCP's four intertwined congestion control algorithms: slow start, congestion avoidance, fast retransmit, and fast recovery.  In addition, the document specifies how TCP should begin transmission after a relatively long idle period, as well as discussing various acknowledgment generation methods.  This document obsoletes 