opublicationDateKPlgranking#crfcd5667irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Network File System (NFS) Direct Data PlacementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document describes a protocol providing Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) as a new transport for Remote Procedure Call (RPC).  The RDMA transport binding conveys the benefits of efficient, bulk-data transport over high-speed networks, while providing for minimal change to RPC applications and with no required revision of the application RPC protocol, or the RPC protocol itself. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiT. Talpey¢kaffiliationeApplednamelB. Callaghanddatej‘Dhfilenamegrfc5666eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127546hkeywordsásNetwork File SystemcNFSgONC RPCdRDMAdRDDPeiWARPjInfiniBandkobsoletedByÅd8166epages"opublicationDateKP`granking"crfcd5666irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Remote Direct Memory Access Transport for Remote Procedure CalldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹This document lists IANA Considerations for Remote Procedure Call (RPC) Network Identifiers (netids) and RPC Universal Network Addresses (uaddrs).  This document updates, but does not replace, RFC 1833. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiM. Eislerddatej”hfilenamegrfc5665eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127545hkeywordsÜgrpcbindgportmapx+transport independent remote procedure callfTI-RPCttransport identifiersprotocol identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKPQgranking!crfcd5665irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeIANA Considerations for Remote Procedure Call (RPC) Network Identifiers and Universal Address FormatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.Parallel NFS (pNFS) extends Network File System version 4 (NFSv4) to allow clients to directly access file data on the storage used by the NFSv4 server.  This ability to bypass the server for data access can increase both performance and parallelism, but requires additional client functionality for data access, some of which is dependent on the class of storage used, a.k.a.  the Layout Type.  The main pNFS operations and data types in NFSv4 Minor version 1 specify a layout- type-independent layer; layout-type-specific information is conveyed using opaque data structures whose internal structure is further defined by the particular layout type specification.  This document specifies the NFSv4.1 Object-Based pNFS Layout Type as a companion to the main NFSv4 Minor version 1 specification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgPanasasdnameiB. Halevy¢kaffiliationgPanasasdnamehB. Welch¢kaffiliationgPanasasdnamejJ. Zelenkaddatej‘@hfilenamegrfc5664eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127544hkeywordsÇcOSDnstorage devicekobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateKPFgranking crfcd5664irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Object-Based Parallel NFS (pNFS) OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybParallel NFS (pNFS) extends Network File Sharing version 4 (NFSv4) to allow clients to directly access file data on the storage used by the NFSv4 server.  This ability to bypass the server for data access can increase both performance and parallelism, but requires additional client functionality for data access, some of which is dependent on the class of storage used.  The main pNFS operations document specifies storage-class-independent extensions to NFS; this document specifies the additional extensions (primarily data structures) for use of pNFS with block- and volume-based storage. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamekS. Fridella¢kaffiliationfGooglednamejJ. Glasgowddatej‘@hfilenamegrfc5663eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127543hkeywordsÇenfsv4xnetwork file sharing version 4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKP:grankingcrfcd5663irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Parallel NFS (pNFS) Block/Volume LayoutdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6688∂habstractxüThis document provides the External Data Representation Standard (XDR) description for Network File System version 4 (NFSv4) minor version 1. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoStorspeed, Inc.dnamejS. Shepler¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiM. Eisler¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiD. Noveckddatej‘@hfilenamegrfc5662eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127542hkeywordsÇcxdrenfsv4kobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDateKP/grankingcrfcd5662irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkNetwork File System (NFS) Version 4 Minor Version 1 External Data Representation Standard (XDR) DescriptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document describes the Network File System (NFS) version 4 minor version 1, including features retained from the base protocol (NFS version 4 minor version 0, which is specified in RFC 3530) and protocol extensions made subsequently.  Major extensions introduced in NFS version 4 minor version 1 include Sessions, Directory Delegations, and parallel NFS (pNFS).  NFS version 4 minor version 1 has no dependencies on NFS version 4 minor version 0, and it is considered a separate protocol.  Thus, this document neither updates nor obsoletes RFC 3530.  NFS minor version 1 is deemed superior to NFS minor version 0 with no loss of functionality, and its use is preferred over version 0.  Both NFS minor versions 0 and 1 can be used simultaneously on the same network, between the same client and server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoStorspeed, Inc.dnamejS. Shepler¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiM. Eisler¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiD. Noveckddatej‘?hfilenamegrfc5661eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127541hkeywordsésAccess Control ListcACLwCommunications SessionsvExactly Once SemanticstFile Access ProtocolpGlobal NamespacevNetwork AuthenticationsNetwork File AccesssNetwork File SystempNetwork SecuritycNFSpParallel StoragedpNFSoStorage ClusterkobsoletedByÅd8881epagesiopublicationDateKPgrankingcrfcd5661irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Network File System (NFS) Version 4 Minor Version 1 ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8434d8178∂habstractyßThis document specifies, abstractly, how to interface applications and transport protocols with IPsec so as to create "channels" by latching "connections" (packet flows) to certain IPsec Security Association (SA) parameters for the lifetime of the connections. Connection latching is layered on top of IPsec and does not modify the underlying IPsec architecture.

Connection latching can be used to protect applications against accidentally exposing live packet flows to unintended peers, whether as the result of a reconfiguration of IPsec or as the result of using weak peer identity to peer address associations. Weak association of peer ID and peer addresses is at the core of Better Than Nothing Security (BTNS); thus, connection latching can add a significant measure of protection to BTNS IPsec nodes.

Finally, the availability of IPsec channels will make it possible to use channel binding to IPsec channels. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. Williamsddatej‘lhfilenamegrfc5660eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbtnsdfullx#btns - Better-Than-Nothing SecuritydnamexBetter-Than-Nothing Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127540hkeywordsÉeIPsecsconnection latchinggchannelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJËô/grankingcrfcd5660irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#IPsec Channels: Connection LatchingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document describes an architecture for extending pseudowire emulation across multiple packet switched network (PSN) segments.  Scenarios are discussed where each segment of a given edge-to-edge emulated service spans a different provider's PSN, as are other scenarios where the emulated service originates and terminates on the same provider's PSN, but may pass through several PSN tunnel segments in that PSN.  It presents an architectural framework for such multi-segment pseudowires, defines terminology, and specifies the various protocol elements and their functions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryantddatej‘mhfilenamegrfc5659eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127539hkeywordsÇcpsnwpacket switched networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJËnõgrankingcrfcd5659irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCAn Architecture for Multi-Segment Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-EdgedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—A typical function of a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Proxy is to insert a Record-Route header into initial, dialog-creating requests in order to make subsequent, in-dialog requests pass through it.  This header contains a SIP Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) or SIPS (secure SIP) URI indicating where and how the subsequent requests should be sent to reach the proxy.  These SIP or SIPS URIs can contain IPv4 or IPv6 addresses and URI parameters that could influence the routing such as the transport parameter (for example, transport=tcp), or a compression indication like "comp=sigcomp".  When a proxy has to change some of those parameters between its incoming and outgoing interfaces (multi-homed proxies, transport protocol switching, or IPv4 to IPv6 scenarios, etc.), the question arises on what should be put in Record-Route header(s).  It is not possible to make one header have the characteristics of both interfaces at the same time.  This document aims to clarify these scenarios and fix bugs already identified on this topic; it formally recommends the use of the double Record-Route technique as an alternative to the current RFC 3261 text, which describes only a Record-Route rewriting solution. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkTech-invitednamejT. Froment¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehC. Lebel¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemB. Bonnaerensddatej‘lhfilenamegrfc5658eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127538hkeywordsÖkmulti-homedjuser agenteproxypinteroperabilityudouble record-routingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ‚:grankingcrfcd5658irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGAddressing Record-Route Issues in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyAdvancing a protocol to Draft Standard requires documentation of the interoperation and implementation of the protocol.  Historic reports have varied widely in form and level of content and there is little guidance available to new report preparers.  This document updates the existing processes and provides more detail on what is appropriate in an interoperability and implementation report.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtMessaging ArchitectsdnamelL. Dusseault¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparksddatej‘lhfilenamegrfc5657eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127537hkeywordsàgrfc2026d2026hguidanceninteroperationnimplementationgreportskadvancementndraft standardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ√ıfgrankingcrfcd5657irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber	etotaletitlexWGuidance on Interoperation and Implementation Reports for Advancement to Draft StandarddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document describes algorithms based on Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) for use within the Secure Shell (SSH) transport protocol.  In particular, it specifies Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) key agreement, Elliptic Curve Menezes-Qu-Vanstone (ECMQV) key agreement, and Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) for use in the SSH Transport Layer protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx#Queensland University of TechnologydnamejD. Stebila¢kaffiliationrQueen's UniversitydnamehJ. Greenddatej‘nhfilenamegrfc5656eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127536hkeywordsÇmKey AgreementlCryptographykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateK&—ègrankingcrfcd5656irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHElliptic Curve Algorithm Integration in the Secure Shell Transport LayerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’This document describes a file format for the storage of flow data based upon the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol.  It proposes a set of requirements for flat-file, binary flow data file formats, then specifies the IPFIX File format to meet these requirements based upon IPFIX Messages.  This IPFIX File format is designed to facilitate interoperability and reusability among a wide variety of flow storage, processing, and analysis tools. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednameiE. Boschi¢kaffiliationoFraunhofer IFAMdnamegL. Mark¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamehT. Zseby¢kaffiliationjETH ZurichdnameiA. Wagnerddatej‘mhfilenamegrfc5655eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127535hkeywordsÑiflow filelflow storagemipfix storageonetflow storagekobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateJËnägrankingcrfcd5655irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSpecification of the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) File FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document specifies the requirements of an MPLS Transport Profile (MPLS-TP). This document is a product of a joint effort of the International Telecommunications Union (ITU) and IETF to include an MPLS Transport Profile within the IETF MPLS and PWE3 architectures to support the capabilities and functionalities of a packet transport network as defined by International Telecommunications Union - Telecommunications Standardization Sector (ITU-T).

This work is based on two sources of requirements: MPLS and PWE3 architectures as defined by IETF, and packet transport networks as defined by ITU-T.

The requirements expressed in this document are for the behavior of the protocol mechanisms and procedures that constitute building blocks out of which the MPLS Transport Profile is constructed. The requirements are not implementation requirements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamepB. Niven-Jenkins¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekD. Brungard¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamehM. Betts¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekN. Sprecher¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamegS. Uenoddatej‘khfilenamegrfc5654eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127534hkeywordsÉgMPLS-TPcITUeITU-TkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJª¯Ügrankingcrfcd5654irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Requirements of an MPLS Transport ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThe Generic Security Services Application Program Interface (GSS-API) offers application programmers uniform access to security services atop a variety of underlying cryptographic mechanisms. This document updates the Java bindings for the GSS-API that are specified in "Generic Security Service API Version 2 : Java Bindings" (RFC 2853). This document obsoletes RFC 2853 by making specific and incremental clarifications and corrections to it in response to identification of transcription errors and implementation experience.

The GSS-API is described at a language-independent conceptual level in "Generic Security Service Application Program Interface Version 2, Update 1" (RFC 2743). The GSS-API allows a caller application to authenticate a principal identity, to delegate rights to a peer, and to apply security services such as confidentiality and integrity on a per-message basis. Examples of security mechanisms defined for GSS-API are "The Simple Public-Key GSS-API Mechanism" (RFC 2025) and "The Kerberos Version 5 Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Mechanism: Version 2" (RFC 4121). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekM. Upadhyay¢kaffiliationsActivIdentity Corp.dnamejS. Malkaniddatej‘bhfilenamegrfc5653eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127533hkeywordsÑfgssapixapplication program interfaceggss-apicGSIkobsoletedByÅd8353epagescopublicationDateJÅ˚¢grankingcrfcd5653irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Generic Security Service API Version 2: Java Bindings UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxµThis document describes the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  This syntax is used to digitally sign, digest, authenticate, or encrypt arbitrary message content. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘jhfilenamegrfc5652eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127532hkeywordsÇqdigital signatureomessage contentkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateJ≤¡∑grankingcrfcd5652irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberFetotaletitlex"Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8933d9629∂habstracty˝The Layered Coding Transport (LCT) Building Block provides transport level support for reliable content delivery and stream delivery protocols.  LCT is specifically designed to support protocols using IP multicast, but it also provides support to protocols that use unicast.  LCT is compatible with congestion control that provides multiple rate delivery to receivers and is also compatible with coding techniques that provide reliable delivery of content.  This document obsoletes RFC 3451. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnamekL. Vicisanoddatej‘ohfilenamegrfc5651eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127531hkeywordsÇcFECrreliable multicastkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateJ‹ˇÏgrankingcrfcd5651irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Layered Coding Transport (LCT) Building BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing parameters of the "Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Line 2 (VDSL2)" interface type, which are also applicable for managing Asymmetric Digital Subscriber Line (ADSL), ADSL2, and ADSL2+ interfaces. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpECI Telecom Ltd.dnamenM. Morgenstern¢kaffiliationmNEC AustraliadnamejS. Baillie¢kaffiliationmNEC AustraliadnamejU. Bonolloddatej‘ghfilenamegrfc5650eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-127530hkeywordsÜcmibxmanagement information basedadslx"asymmetric digital subscriber lineqVDSL2-LINE-TC-MIBnVDSL2-LINE-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages⁄opublicationDateJ√ÛVgrankingcrfcd5650irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTDefinitions of Managed Objects for Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Line 2 (VDSL2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document specifies a padding convention for use with the AES Key Wrap algorithm specified in RFC 3394.  This convention eliminates the requirement that the length of the key to be wrapped be a multiple of 64 bits, allowing a key of any practical length to be wrapped.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamejM. DworkinddatejÃÙhfilenamegrfc5649eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127529hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ≤¡®grankingcrfcd5649irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBAdvanced Encryption Standard (AES) Key Wrap with Padding AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€According to the current Mobile IPv6 specification, a mobile node may have several care-of addresses but only one, called the primary care-of address, can be registered with its home agent and the correspondent nodes.  However, for matters of cost, bandwidth, delay, etc, it is useful for the mobile node to get Internet access through multiple accesses simultaneously, in which case the mobile node would be configured with multiple active IPv6 care-of addresses.  This document proposes extensions to the Mobile IPv6 protocol to register and use multiple care-of addresses.  The extensions proposed in this document can be used by mobile routers using the NEMO (Network Mobility) Basic Support protocol as well. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjToyota ITCdnamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationhWichorusdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamehT. Ernst¢kaffiliationmINTEC NetCorednameiK. NagamiddatejÃÙhfilenamegrfc5648eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127528hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateJ∆ô)grankingcrfcd5648irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Multiple Care-of Addresses RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6089∂habstractyïSecure shell (SSH) is a secure remote-login protocol.  SSH provides for algorithms that provide authentication, key agreement, confidentiality, and data-integrity services.  The purpose of this document is to show how the AES Galois Counter Mode can be used to provide both confidentiality and data integrity to the SSH Transport Layer Protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamegK. Igoe¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnamejJ. Solinasddatej‘bhfilenamegrfc5647eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127527hkeywordsÇcsshlremote-loginkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateJúsOgrankingcrfcd5647irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEAES Galois Counter Mode for the Secure Shell Transport Layer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty’This document describes the structure, content, construction, and semantics of language tags for use in cases where it is desirable to indicate the language used in an information object.  It also describes how to register values for use in language tags and the creation of user-defined extensions for private interchange.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfLab126dnamekA. Phillips¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehM. Davisddatej‘dhfilenamegrfc5646eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdltrudfullx#ltru - Language Tag Registry UpdatednamexLanguage Tag Registry Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-127526hkeywordsÇmlanguage tagssprivate interchangekobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDateJ°íúgrankingcrfcd5646irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber/etotaletitlexTags for Identifying LanguagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÒThis memo defines the procedure used to update the IANA Language Subtag Registry, in conjunction with the publication of RFC 5646, for use in forming tags for identifying languages.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehD. Ewellddatej‘chfilenamegrfc5645eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdltrudfullx#ltru - Language Tag Registry UpdatednamexLanguage Tag Registry Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-127525hkeywordsÑmlanguage tagsplanguage taggingdltruhregistrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ°íìgrankingcrfcd5645irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Update to the Language Subtag RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€The IETF has standardized IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) for measuring end-to-end performance between two points.  This memo defines two new categories of metrics that extend the coverage to multiple measurement points.  It defines spatial metrics for measuring the performance of segments of a source to destination path, and metrics for measuring the performance between a source and many destinations in multiparty communications (e.g., a multicast tree). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamejE. Stephan¢kaffiliationtUniversity of SurreydnamehL. Liang¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej‘ohfilenamegrfc5644eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127524hkeywordsÖxMultiple point measurementtrelative performancexgroup performance statisticsper hop measurementssegment performancekobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateJ‚:grankingcrfcd5644irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4IP Performance Metrics (IPPM): Spatial and MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6248∂habstracty4This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in IPv6-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing the Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) Routing Protocol for IPv6, otherwise known as OSPF version 3 (OSPFv3). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamehD. Joyal¢kaffiliationkIP InfusiondnameiV. Manralddatej‘bhfilenamegrfc5643eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127523hkeywordsÖcmibdipv6xopen shortest path firstprouting protocoljOSPFV3-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages_opublicationDateJús?grankingcrfcd5643irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Management Information Base for OSPFv3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document defines a new OSPF Router Information (RI) TLV that allows OSPF routers to flood their hostname-to-Router-ID mapping information across an OSPF network to provide a simple and dynamic mechanism for routers running OSPF to learn about symbolic hostnames, just like for routers running IS-IS.  This mechanism is applicable to both OSPFv2 and OSPFv3. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkGoogle Inc.dnamejS. Venkata¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Harwani¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationtArbor Networks, Inc.dnamelD. McPhersonddatej‘ahfilenamegrfc5642eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127522hkeywordsÑxopen shortest path firstrrouter informationbriuospf dynamic hostnamekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJç‚ègranking
crfcd5642irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Dynamic Hostname Exchange Mechanism for OSPFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document defines additional Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3 (L2TPv3) bit values to be used within the "Circuit Status" Attribute Value Pair (AVP) to communicate finer-grained error states for Attachment Circuits (ACs) and pseudowires (PWs).  It also generalizes the Active bit and deprecates the use of the New bit in the Circuit Status AVP, updating RFC 3931, RFC 4349, RFC 4454, RFC 4591, and RFC 4719. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiN. McGill¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataroddatej‘ahfilenamegrfc5641eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127521hkeywordsásattachment circuitscacskpseudowiresbpwjactive bitgnew bitrcircuit status avpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJús,granking	crfcd5641irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLLayer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3 (L2TPv3) Extended Circuit Status ValuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPayloads transported over a Softwire mesh service (as defined by BGP Encapsulation Subsequent Address Family Identifier (SAFI) information exchange) often carry a number of identifiable, distinct flows.  It can, in some circumstances, be desirable to distribute these flows over the equal cost multiple paths (ECMPs) that exist in the packet switched network.  Currently, the payload of a packet entering the Softwire can only be interpreted by the ingress and egress routers.  Thus, the load-balancing decision of a core router is only based on the encapsulating header, presenting much less entropy than available in the payload or the encapsulated header since the Softwire encapsulation acts in a tunneling fashion.  This document describes a method for achieving comparable load-balancing efficiency in a network carrying Softwire mesh service over Layer Two Tunneling Protocol - Version 3 (L2TPv3) over IP or Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) encapsulation to what would be achieved without such encapsulation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Filsfils¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataroddatej‘ghfilenamegrfc5640eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127520hkeywordsÇx6bgp encapsulation subsequent address family identifierdsafikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJúsgrankingcrfcd5640irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Load-Balancing for Mesh SoftwiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9012µhabstracty,This memo proposes several elliptic curve domain parameters over finite prime fields for use in cryptographic applications.  The domain parameters are consistent with the relevant international standards, and can be used in X.509 certificates and certificate revocation lists (CRLs), for Internet Key Exchange (IKE), Transport Layer Security (TLS), XML signatures, and all applications or protocols based on the cryptographic message syntax (CMS).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim PolkgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcBSIdnamejM. Lochter¢kaffiliationxsecunet Security NetworksdnameiJ. MerkleddatejÃFhfilenamegrfc5639eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127519hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKègrankingcrfcd5639irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPElliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Brainpool Standard Curves and Curve GenerationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFFor Internet-centric usage, the number of SIP-required standards for presence and IM and audio/video communications can be drastically smaller than what has been published by using only the rendezvous and session-initiation capabilities of SIP. The simplification is achieved by avoiding the emulation of telephony and its model of the intelligent network. 'Simple SIP' relies on powerful computing endpoints. Simple SIP desktop applications can be combined with rich Internet applications (RIAs). Significant telephony features may also be implemented in the endpoints.

This approach for SIP reduces the number of SIP standards with which to comply -- from roughly 100 currently, and still growing, to about 11.

References for NAT traversal and for security are also provided. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeAdobednamelH. Sinnreich¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamegE. Shim¢kaffiliationrColumbia U. AlumnidnamehK. Singhddatej‘jhfilenamegrfc5638eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127518hkeywordsÉxsession initiation protocolxrich internet applicationcriakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ∫≤;grankingcrfcd5638irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Simple SIP Usage Scenario for Applications in the EndpointsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøIn commercial and enterprise deployments, Mobile IPv6 can be a service offered by a Mobility Services Provider (MSP).  In this case, all protocol operations may need to be explicitly authorized and traced, requiring the interaction between Mobile IPv6 and the AAA infrastructure.  Integrating the Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) infrastructure (e.g., Network Access Server and AAA server) also offers a solution component for Mobile IPv6 bootstrapping.  This document describes various scenarios where a AAA interface for Mobile IPv6 is required.  Additionally, it lists design goals and requirements for such an interface.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Giaretta¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekI. Guardini¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamejE. Demaria¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamelJ. Bournelle¢kaffiliationtUniversity of MurciadnamehR. Lopezddatej‘&hfilenamegrfc5637eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127517hkeywordsÉcAAAeMIPv6iMobile IPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ√Û>grankingcrfcd5637irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIAuthentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) Goals for Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a practical architecture and protocols for offering privacy for a user who requests and uses an X.509 certificate containing a pseudonym, while still retaining the ability to map such a certificate to the real user who requested it.  The architecture is compatible with IETF certificate request formats such as PKCS10 (RFC 2986) and CMC (RFC 5272).  The architecture separates the authorities involved in issuing a certificate: one for verifying ownership of a private key (Blind Issuer) and the other for validating the contents of a certificate (Anonymity Issuer).  The end entity (EE) certificates issued under this model are called Traceable Anonymous Certificates (TACs).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamegH. Park¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefY. Won¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kentddatej‘dhfilenamegrfc5636eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127516hkeywordsÜqx.509 certificatelblind issuerpanonymity issuerdtacsjend entitybeekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJì&lgrankingcrfcd5636irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTraceable Anonymous CertificatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@Remote Triggered Black Hole (RTBH) filtering is a popular and effective technique for the mitigation of denial-of-service attacks.  This document expands upon destination-based RTBH filtering by outlining a method to enable filtering by source address as well.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiW. Kumari¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelD. McPhersonddatej‘fhfilenamegrfc5635eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-127515hkeywordsÅdrtbhkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJó
Qgrankingcrfcd5635irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRemote Triggered Black Hole Filtering with Unicast Reverse Path Forwarding (uRPF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThis document specifies the use of the Quick-Start mechanism by the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP).  DCCP is a transport protocol that allows the transmission of congestion-controlled, unreliable datagrams.  DCCP is intended for applications such as streaming media, Internet telephony, and online games.  In DCCP, an application has a choice of congestion control mechanisms, each specified by a Congestion Control Identifier (CCID).  This document specifies general procedures applicable to all DCCP CCIDs and specific procedures for the use of Quick-Start with DCCP CCID 2, CCID 3, and CCID 4.  Quick-Start enables a DCCP sender to cooperate with Quick-Start routers along the end-to-end path to determine an allowed sending rate at the start of a connection and, at times, in the middle of a DCCP connection (e.g., after an idle or application- limited period).  The present specification is provided for use in controlled environments, and not as a mechanism that would be intended or appropriate for ubiquitous deployment in the global Internet.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnameoA. Sathiaseelanddatej‘fhfilenamegrfc5634eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127514hkeywordsÖdccidxcongestion control identifierfccid 2fccid 3fccid 4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJï«˛grankingcrfcd5634irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Quick-Start for the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document updates RFC 3777, Section 4, Bullet 13 to allow announcement of open positions and solicitation of volunteers to be issued before a Nominating and Recall Committee Chair has been named by the Internet Society President.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlHuawei (USA)dnamejS. Dawkinsddatej‘fhfilenamegrfc5633eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127513hkeywordsÇxInternet Architecture BoardxEngineering Steering GroupkobsoletedByÅd7437epagesopublicationDateJÉUˆgrankingcrfcd5633irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcNominating Committee Process: Earlier Announcement of Open Positions and Solicitation of VolunteersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨This document describes the experiences of Comcast, a large cable broadband Internet Service Provider (ISP) in the U.S., in a Proactive Network Provider Participation for P2P (P4P) technical trial in July 2008.  This trial used P4P iTracker technology, which is being considered by the IETF as part of the Application Layer Transport Optimization (ALTO) working group.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelC. Griffiths¢kaffiliationgComcastdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationePandodnameiL. Popkin¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiR. Woundy¢kaffiliationdYalednamegY. Yangddatej‘ehfilenamegrfc5632eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127512hkeywordsàcISPxInternet Service ProvidercP2PlPeer-to-PeercP4Px.Proactive Network Provider Partication for P2PdDCIAx*Distributed Computing Industry AssociationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ®tÈgranking␀crfcd5632irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeComcast's ISP Experiences in a Proactive Network Provider Participation for P2P (P4P) Technical TrialdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	Session mobility is the transfer of media of an ongoing communication session from one device to another.  This document describes the basic approaches and shows the signaling and media flow examples for providing this service using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  Service discovery is essential to locate targets for session transfer and is discussed using the Service Location Protocol (SLP) as an example.  This document is an informational document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamejR. Shacham¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnamelS. Thakolsri¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnamekW. Kellererddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5631eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127511hkeywordsÉxthird party call control (3pcc)htransfertvoice over ip (voip)kobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateJ‚9Ògrankingˇcrfcd5631irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Session MobilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx√This document provides clarifications and guidelines concerning the use of the SIPS URI scheme in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  It also makes normative changes to SIP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjSkype LabsdnamehF. Audetddatej‘Bhfilenamegrfc5630eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127510hkeywordsÉdSIPScSIPcTLSkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateJ‚9·granking˛crfcd5630irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGThe Use of the SIPS URI Scheme in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document describes a framework for the interaction between users and Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) based applications.  By interacting with applications, users can guide the way in which they operate.  The focus of this framework is stimulus signaling, which allows a user agent (UA) to interact with an application without knowledge of the semantics of that application.  Stimulus signaling can occur to a user interface running locally with the client, or to a remote user interface, through media streams.  Stimulus signaling encompasses a wide range of mechanisms, ranging from clicking on hyperlinks, to pressing buttons, to traditional Dual-Tone Multi- Frequency (DTMF) input.  In all cases, stimulus signaling is supported through the use of markup languages, which play a key role in this framework. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”Éhfilenamegrfc5629eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127509hkeywordsÇcsipddtmfkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateJ‚9”granking˝crfcd5629irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPA Framework for Application Interaction in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 3680 defines a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) event package for registration state. This package allows a watcher to learn about information stored by a SIP registrar, including its registered contact.

However, the registered contact is frequently unreachable and thus not useful for watchers. The Globally Routable User Agent URI (GRUU), defined in RFC 5627, is a URI that is capable of reaching a particular contact. However this URI is not included in the document format defined in RFC 3680. This specification defines an extension to the registration event package to include GRUUs assigned by the registrar. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejP. Kyzivatddatej”˘hfilenamegrfc5628eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127508hkeywordsÅlregistrationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ‚9√granking¸crfcd5628irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextRegistration Event Package Extension for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Globally Routable User Agent URIs (GRUUs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty…Several applications of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) require a user agent (UA) to construct and distribute a URI that can be used by anyone on the Internet to route a call to that specific UA instance.  A URI that routes to a specific UA instance is called a Globally Routable UA URI (GRUU).  This document describes an extension to SIP for obtaining a GRUU from a registrar and for communicating a GRUU to a peer within a dialog. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”„hfilenamegrfc5627eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127507hkeywordsÜcSIPcNAThoutbounddgruulregistrationitraversalkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateJ‚9µgranking˚crfcd5627irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfObtaining and Using Globally Routable User Agent URIs (GRUUs) in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) allows proxy servers to initiate TCP connections or to send asynchronous UDP datagrams to User Agents in order to deliver requests.  However, in a large number of real deployments, many practical considerations, such as the existence of firewalls and Network Address Translators (NATs) or the use of TLS with server-provided certificates, prevent servers from connecting to User Agents in this way.  This specification defines behaviors for User Agents, registrars, and proxy servers that allow requests to be delivered on existing connections established by the User Agent.  It also defines keep-alive behaviors needed to keep NAT bindings open and specifies the usage of multiple connections from the User Agent to its registrar. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationjSkype LabsdnamehF. AudetddatejÃÁhfilenamegrfc5626eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127506hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateJ‚9£granking˙crfcd5626irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNManaging Client-Initiated Connections in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document provides guidelines for the implementation of DNS proxies, as found in broadband gateways and other similar network devices.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjNominet UKdnameiR. Bellisddatej‘ehfilenamegrfc5625eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127505hkeywordsÇcDNSeProxykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJì&Xgranking˘crfcd5625irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberòetotaletitlex#DNS Proxy Implementation GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document defines a number of Quality of Service (QoS) parameters that can be reused for conveying QoS information within Diameter.

The defined QoS information includes data traffic parameters for describing a token bucket filter, a bandwidth parameter, and a per-hop behavior class object. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Daviesddatej‘]hfilenamegrfc5624eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127504hkeywordsÇhDiameternQoS ParameterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJç‰granking¯crfcd5624irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Quality of Service Parameters for Usage with DiameterdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡A network may comprise multiple layers. It is important to globally optimize network resource utilization, taking into account all layers rather than optimizing resource utilization at each layer independently. This allows better network efficiency to be achieved through a process that we call inter-layer traffic engineering. The Path Computation Element (PCE) can be a powerful tool to achieve inter-layer traffic engineering.

This document describes a framework for applying the PCE-based architecture to inter-layer Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) traffic engineering. It provides suggestions for the deployment of PCE in support of multi-layer networks. This document also describes network models where PCE performs inter-layer traffic engineering, and the relationship between PCE and a functional component called the Virtual Network Topology Manager (VNTM). This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx$University of Electro-CommunicationsdnamefE. Oki¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takeda¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘ehfilenamegrfc5623eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127503hkeywordsÖdMPLSeGMPLSsTraffic EngineeringsLabel Switched PathxVirtual Network TopologykobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateJ≤¡îgranking˜crfcd5623irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFFramework for PCE-Based Inter-Layer MPLS and GMPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document specifies a profile for Congestion Control Identifier 4, the small-packet variant of TCP-Friendly Rate Control (TFRC), in the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP).  CCID 4 is for experimental use, and uses TFRC-SP (RFC 4828), a variant of TFRC designed for applications that send small packets.  CCID 4 is considered experimental because TFRC-SP is itself experimental, and is not proposed for widespread deployment in the global Internet at this time.  The goal for TFRC-SP is to achieve roughly the same bandwidth in bits per second (bps) as a TCP flow using packets of up to 1500 bytes but experiencing the same level of congestion.  CCID 4 is for use for senders that send small packets and would like a TCP- friendly sending rate, possibly with Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN), while minimizing abrupt rate changes.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiE. Kohlerddatej‘fhfilenamegrfc5622eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127502hkeywordsÇfccid 4xcongestion control identifier 4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ|†Hgrankingˆcrfcd5622irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~Profile for Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) Congestion ID 4: TCP-Friendly Rate Control for Small Packets (TFRC-SP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6323d8311∂habstractx“This document specifies how message bodies are handled in SIP.  Additionally, this document specifies SIP user agent support for MIME (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) in message bodies. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej‘ehfilenamegrfc5621eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127501hkeywordsÉlMessage bodydMIMEcSIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ©|agrankingıcrfcd5621irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Message Body Handling in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8262¥habstracty?The RFC Editor performs a number of functions that may be carried out by various persons or entities.  The RFC Editor model presented in this document divides the responsibilities for the RFC Series into four functions: The RFC Series Editor, the Independent Submission Editor, the RFC Production Center, and the RFC Publisher.  It also introduces the RFC Series Advisory Group and an (optional) Independent Submission Stream Editorial Board.  The model outlined here is intended to increase flexibility and operational support options, provide for the orderly succession of the RFC Editor, and ensure the continuity of the RFC series, while maintaining RFC quality and timely processing, ensuring document accessibility, reducing costs, and increasing cost transparency.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej‘ahfilenamegrfc5620eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127500hkeywordsÇqRFC Series EditorxIndependent Stream EditorkobsoletedByÇd6548d6635epagesopublicationDateJã8RgrankingÙcrfcd5620irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexRFC Editor Model (Version 1)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document describes security guidelines for the softwire "Hubs and Spokes" and "Mesh" solutions.  Together with discussion of the softwire deployment scenarios, the vulnerability to security attacks is analyzed to provide security protection mechanisms such as authentication, integrity, and confidentiality to the softwire control and data packets. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrNICT/KDDI R&D LabsdnamekS. Yamamoto¢kaffiliationmKDDI R&D LabsdnamekC. Williams¢kaffiliationmKDDI R&D LabsdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationnBeon SolutionsdnameiF. Parentddatej‘dhfilenamegrfc5619eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127499hkeywordsÑdIPv6fTunnelhSoftwirejTransitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJè4grankingÛcrfcd5619irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Softwire Security Analysis and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis memo describes a simple extension to TWAMP (the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol).  The extension adds the option to use different security modes in the TWAMP-Control and TWAMP-Test protocols simultaneously.  The memo also describes a new IANA registry for additional features, called the TWAMP Modes registry. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationdEXFOdnamejK. Hedayatddatej‘chfilenamegrfc5618eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127498hkeywordsÉvtwamp-control protocolstwamp-test protocolktwamp modeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÉUgrankingÚcrfcd5618irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGMixed Security Mode for the Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) defines a domain-level authentication framework for email to permit verification of the source and contents of messages.  This document specifies an adjunct mechanism to aid in assessing messages that do not contain a DKIM signature for the domain used in the author's address.  It defines a record that can advertise whether a domain signs its outgoing mail as well as how other hosts can access that record. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnameiE. Allman¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Fenton¢kaffiliationkYahoo! Inc.dnameiM. Delany¢kaffiliationtTaughannock NetworksdnameiJ. Levineddatej‘chfilenamegrfc5617eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-127497hkeywordsòeemailfe-mailhrfc 5322grfc 822hrfc 5321grfc 821hrfc 4871dDKIMkdomain keysjdomainkeysdADSPcSSPlarchitecturecmtaduserhdeliverydsmtpjsubmissionhInternethmailfromimail fromfauthornreturn addressnsender signingqsigning practiceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÉUÚgrankingÒcrfcd5617irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Author Domain Signing Practices (ADSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstractyThis document describes a method for streaming multimedia attachments received by a resource- and/or network-constrained device from an IMAP server. It allows such clients, which often have limits in storage space and bandwidth, to play video and audio email content.

The document describes a profile for making use of the URLAUTH- authorized IMAP URLs (RFC 5092), the Network Announcement SIP Media Service (RFC 4240), and the Media Server Control Markup Language (RFC 5022). This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamegN. Cookddatej‘chfilenamegrfc5616eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127496hkeywordsädIMAPcSIPistreamingfstreameemailjmultimediahlemonadekattachmentsevideoeaudiokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJúsgrankingcrfcd5616irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Streaming Internet Messaging AttachmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyTThis document updates the H.248/MEGACO IANA Package registration procedures in order to better describe the Package registration process and to provide a more formal review and feedback process.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnNTEC AustraliadnameiC. Groves¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Linddatej‘ahfilenamegrfc5615eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127495hkeywordsÑgPackagejError CodetServiceChange ReasongProfilekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÅ˚êgrankingÔcrfcd5615irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberóetotaletitlex$H.248/MEGACO Registration ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document specifies an extension of OSPFv3 to support mobile ad hoc networks (MANETs).  The extension, called OSPF-MDR, is designed as a new OSPF interface type for MANETs.  OSPF-MDR is based on the selection of a subset of MANET routers, consisting of MANET Designated Routers (MDRs) and Backup MDRs.  The MDRs form a connected dominating set (CDS), and the MDRs and Backup MDRs together form a biconnected CDS for robustness.  This CDS is exploited in two ways.  First, to reduce flooding overhead, an optimized flooding procedure is used in which only (Backup) MDRs flood new link state advertisements (LSAs) back out the receiving interface; reliable flooding is ensured by retransmitting LSAs along adjacencies.  Second, adjacencies are formed only between (Backup) MDRs and a subset of their neighbors, allowing for much better scaling in dense networks.  The CDS is constructed using 2-hop neighbor information provided in a Hello protocol extension.  The Hello protocol is further optimized by allowing differential Hellos that report only changes in neighbor states.  Options are specified for originating router-LSAs that provide full or partial topology information, allowing overhead to be reduced by advertising less topology information.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqSRI InternationaldnamehR. Ogier¢kaffiliationfBoeingdnamekP. Spagnoloddatej‘Mhfilenamegrfc5614eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127494hkeywordsÜmMANET routingrlink-state routinglCDS floodinglmesh networkwMANET Designated RoutercMDRkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateJÖÔ´grankingÓcrfcd5614irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Extension of OSPF Using Connected Dominating Set (CDS) FloodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7038d9454∂habstractyÎOSPF is a link-state intra-domain routing protocol.  OSPF routers exchange information on a link using packets that follow a well-defined fixed format.  The format is not flexible enough to enable new features that need to exchange arbitrary data.  This document describes a backward-compatible technique to perform link-local signaling, i.e., exchange arbitrary data on a link.  This document replaces the experimental specification published in RFC 4813 to bring it on the Standards Track.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehA. Zinin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiL. Nguyen¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamekB. Friedman¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Yeungddatej‘`hfilenamegrfc5613eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127493hkeywordsÇxopen shortest path firsttintra-domain routingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÖÔgrankingÌcrfcd5613irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSPF Link-Local SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¡This document describes an Enterprise Number (also known as SMI Network Management Private Enterprise Code) for use in documentation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamemD. Harringtonddatej‘dhfilenamegrfc5612eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127492hkeywordsÅx.smi network management private enterprise codekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJè4grankingÏcrfcd5612irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Enterprise Number for Documentation UsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôThis document defines extensions to the Layer Two Tunneling Protocol version 3 (L2TPv3) for support of structure-agnostic and structure-aware (Circuit Emulation Service over Packet Switched Network (CESoPSN) style) Time-Division Multiplexing (TDM) pseudowires.  Support of structure-aware (Time-Division Multiplexing over IP (TDMoIP) style) pseudowires over L2TPv3 is left for further study. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamemA. Vainshtein¢kaffiliationiRebelliondnamejS. Galtzurddatej‘bhfilenamegrfc5611eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127491hkeywordsÜfl2tpv3x&layer tow tunneling protocol version 3rstructure-agnosticostructure-awaregcesopsnftdmoipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJã80grankingÎcrfcd5611irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Layer Two Tunneling Protocol version 3 - Setup of Time-Division Multiplexing (TDM) PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This document describes an extension to the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol, which is used to represent and transmit data from IP flow measurement devices for collection, storage, and analysis, to allow the encoding of IPFIX Information Model properties within an IPFIX Message stream.  This enables the export of extended type information for enterprise-specific Information Elements and the storage of such information within IPFIX Files, facilitating interoperability and reusability among a wide variety of applications and tools. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednameiE. Boschi¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationoFraunhofer IFAMdnamegL. Mark¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamehT. Zsebyddatej‘`hfilenamegrfc5610eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127490hkeywordsÑx'enterprise-specific Information ElementnIPFIX Templatektype recordutype options templatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJmsîgrankingÍcrfcd5610irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVExporting Type Information for IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Information ElementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document defines the conceptual state machines for the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA).  The state machines consist of the PANA Client (PaC) state machine and the PANA Authentication Agent (PAA) state machine.  The two state machines show how PANA can interface with the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) state machines.  The state machines and associated models are informative only.  Implementations may achieve the same results using different methods.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvTelcordia TechnologiesdnamejV. Fajardo¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationoUniv. of MurciadnamenR. Marin-Lopezddatej‘ahfilenamegrfc5609eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpanadfullx>pana - Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdnamex7Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdtypebwgbidjdoc-127489hkeywordsÉdPANAmState MachinecEAPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJã8grankingÈcrfcd5609irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUState Machines for the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis memo describes the use of a Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) authentication and authorization service with Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) secure Transport Models to authenticate users and authorize creation of secure transport sessions.  While the recommendations of this memo are generally applicable to a broad class of SNMP Transport Models, the examples focus on the Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Model. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejK. Narayan¢kaffiliationuElbrys Networks, Inc.dnameiD. Nelsonddatej‘`hfilenamegrfc5608eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdismsdfullx)isms - Integrated Security Model for SNMPdnamex"Integrated Security Model for SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127488hkeywordsÉuauthorization servicesssh transport modelxsecure shell transport modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÉT⁄grankingËcrfcd5608irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxRemote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) Usage for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) Transport ModelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document specifies Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) attributes for authorizing management access to a Network Access Server (NAS).  Both local and remote management are supported, with granular access rights and management privileges.  Specific provisions are made for remote management via Framed Management protocols and for management access over a secure transport protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuElbrys Networks, Inc.dnameiD. Nelson¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamehG. Weberddatej‘`hfilenamegrfc5607eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127487hkeywordsárNetwork ManagementqDevice Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPxNetwork Configuration ProtocolgNETCONFnAccess ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJms´grankingÁcrfcd5607irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlRemote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) Authorization for Network Access Server (NAS) ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document explores an ambiguity in the interpretation of the <retransmission-allowed> element of the Presence Information Data Format for Location Objects (PIDF-LO) in cases where PIDF-LO is conveyed by the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP). It provides recommendations for how the SIP location conveyance mechanism should adapt to this ambiguity.

Documents standardizing the SIP location conveyance mechanisms will be Standards-Track documents processed according to the usual SIP process. This document is intended primarily to provide the SIP working group with a statement of the consensus of the GEOPRIV working group on this topic. It secondarily provides tutorial information on the problem space for the general reader. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnameiT. Hardie¢kaffiliationcCDTdnameiJ. Morrisddatej‘_hfilenamegrfc5606eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127486hkeywordsÇgpidf-lox5presence information data format for location objectskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJì&BgrankingÊcrfcd5606irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDImplications of 'retransmission-allowed' for SIP Location ConveyancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling ATM Pseudowire (PW) carrying ATM cells over Packet Switched Networks (PSNs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnRADVISION Ltd.dnamekO. Nicklass¢kaffiliationbBTdnameiT. Nadeauddatej”∞hfilenamegrfc5605eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127485hkeywordsÉcmibxmanagement information basenatm pseudowirekobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateJ\˛grankingÂcrfcd5605irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Managed Objects for ATM over Packet Switched Networks (PSNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for pseudowire encapsulation for structured or unstructured Time-Division Multiplexing (TDM) (T1, E1, T3, E3) circuits over a Packet Switched Network (PSN). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnRADVISION Ltd.dnamekO. Nicklassddatej‘Ghfilenamegrfc5604eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127484hkeywordsácmibxmanagement information basexpseudowire encapsulationbt1be1bt3be3kobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateJ\˝ıgranking‰crfcd5604irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYManaged Objects for Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) over Packet Switched Networks (PSNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling of Ethernet pseudowire (PW) services. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOversidnamehD. Zelig¢kaffiliationbBTdnameiT. Nadeauddatej‘(hfilenamegrfc5603eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127483hkeywordsÇkethernet pwoPW-ENET-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ\˝‚granking„crfcd5603irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Ethernet Pseudowire (PW) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes a MIB module for PW operation over Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switching Routers (LSRs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOversidnamehD. Zelig¢kaffiliationbBTdnameiT. Nadeauddatej‘(hfilenamegrfc5602eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127482hkeywordsÇlpw operationoPW-MPLS-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ\˝¿granking‚crfcd5602irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Pseudowire (PW) over MPLS PSN Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This memo defines a Standards Track portion of the Management Information Base for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling of Pseudowire Edge-to-Edge services carried over a general Packet Switched Network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationbBTdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationfOversidnamehD. Zeligddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5601eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127481hkeywordsÉx pseudowire edge-to-edge servicesmIANA-PWE3-MIBjPW-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateJ\˝∞granking·crfcd5601irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Pseudowire (PW) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>Over its thirty-five-year history, Internet Mail has changed significantly in scale and complexity, as it has become a global infrastructure service.  These changes have been evolutionary, rather than revolutionary, reflecting a strong desire to preserve both its installed base and its usefulness.  To collaborate productively on this large and complex system, all participants need to work from a common view of it and use a common language to describe its components and the interactions among them.  But the many differences in perspective currently make it difficult to know exactly what another participant means.  To serve as the necessary common frame of reference, this document describes the enhanced Internet Mail architecture, reflecting the current service.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej‘_hfilenamegrfc5598eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127480hkeywordsò!eemailfe-mailgservicedmimelarchitecturecmtacmuacmsacmdadadmdduserjoriginatorirecipienthtransferpmessage transfergdeliverhdeliveryerelayfheadermgateway agentmgateway actorggatewayesievecdsncmdnftusslecmhsxMessage handling servicevmessage transfer agentrmessage user agentumail submission agentsmail delivery agentx administrative management domainkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateJ\˝úgrankingﬁcrfcd5598irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInternet Mail ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty]This document defines a set of requirements for NATs handling the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP).  These requirements allow DCCP applications, such as streaming applications, to operate consistently, and they are very similar to the TCP requirements for NATs, which have already been published by the IETF.  Ensuring that NATs meet this set of requirements will greatly increase the likelihood that applications using DCCP will function properly.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpVideoLAN projectdnameqR. Denis-Courmontddatej‘<hfilenamegrfc5597eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127479hkeywordsÅddccpkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateJ≤¡Ågranking›crfcd5597irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberñetotaletitlexfNetwork Address Translation (NAT) Behavioral Requirements for the Datagram Congestion Control ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document specifies an update to the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP), a connection-oriented and datagram-based transport protocol.  The update adds support for the DCCP-Listen packet.  This assists DCCP applications to communicate through middleboxes (e.g., a Network Address Port Translator or a DCCP server behind a firewall), where peering endpoints need to initiate communication in a near- simultaneous manner to establish necessary middlebox state. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej‘]hfilenamegrfc5596eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127478hkeywordsÉdDCCPmNAT traversalpMiddlebox IssueskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ≤¡cgranking‹crfcd5596irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmDatagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) Simultaneous-Open Technique to Facilitate NAT/Middlebox TraversaldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThis document describes the usage of Service Codes by the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol, RFC 4340.  It motivates the setting of a Service Code by applications.  Service Codes provide a method to identify the intended service/application to process a DCCP connection request.  This provides improved flexibility in the use and assignment of port numbers for connection multiplexing.  The use of a DCCP Service Code can also enable more explicit coordination of services with middleboxes (e.g., network address translators and firewalls).  This document updates the specification provided in RFC 4340. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej‘^hfilenamegrfc5595eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127477hkeywordsÅrDCCP-Request PortskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ≤¡Rgranking€crfcd5595irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=The Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) Service CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6335∂habstracty£This document reports the outcome of a workshop organized by the Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Area Directors of the IETF to discuss network delay and congestion issues resulting from increased Peer-to-Peer (P2P) traffic volumes.  The workshop was held on May 28, 2008 at MIT in Cambridge, MA, USA.  The goals of the workshop were twofold: to understand the technical problems that ISPs and end users are experiencing as a result of high volumes of P2P traffic, and to begin to understand how the IETF may be helpful in addressing these problems.  Gaining an understanding of where in the IETF this work might be pursued and how to extract feasible work items were highlighted as important tasks in pursuit of the latter goal.  The workshop was very well attended and produced several work items that have since been taken up by members of the IETF community.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationx!Center for Democracy & TechnologydnameiA. Cooperddatej‘]hfilenamegrfc5594eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127476hkeywordsÅdP2PIkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJspHgranking⁄crfcd5594irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPReport from the IETF Workshop on Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Infrastructure, May 28, 2008dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—The existing IMAP URL specification (RFC 5092) lists several <access> identifiers and <access> identifier prefixes that can be used to restrict access to URLAUTH-generated URLs. However, these identifiers do not provide facilities for new services such as streaming. This document proposes a set of new <access> identifiers as well as an IANA mechanism to register new <access> identifiers for future applications.

This document updates RFC 5092. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCloudmarkdnamegN. Cookddatej‘Whfilenamegrfc5593eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127475hkeywordsÇgurlauthhimap urlkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateJIR}grankingŸcrfcd5593irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIInternet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - URL Access Identifier ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis memo describes a Transport Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP), using the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.

This memo also defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets. In particular, it defines objects for monitoring and managing the Secure Shell Transport Model for SNMP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationxCobham Analytic SolutionsdnamekW. Hardakerddatej‘Yhfilenamegrfc5592eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdismsdfullx)isms - Integrated Security Model for SNMPdnamex"Integrated Security Model for SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127474hkeywordsÖrNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPlSecure ShellcSSHkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateJJ•ıgrankingÿcrfcd5592irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNSecure Shell Transport Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis memo describes a Transport Security Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).

This memo also defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for monitoring and managing the Transport Security Model for SNMP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationxCobham Analytic SolutionsdnamekW. Hardakerddatej‘Zhfilenamegrfc5591eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdismsdfullx)isms - Integrated Security Model for SNMPdnamex"Integrated Security Model for SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127473hkeywordsÖrNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPxTransport Security ModelnSecurity ModelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJJ•·granking◊crfcd5591irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberNetotaletitlexJTransport Security Model for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÖThis document defines a Transport Subsystem, extending the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) architecture defined in RFC 3411.  This document defines a subsystem to contain Transport Models that is comparable to other subsystems in the RFC 3411 architecture.  As work is being done to expand the transports to include secure transports, such as the Secure Shell (SSH) Protocol and Transport Layer Security (TLS), using a subsystem will enable consistent design and modularity of such Transport Models.  This document identifies and describes some key aspects that need to be considered for any Transport Model for SNMP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSean Turnerdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej‘Yhfilenamegrfc5590eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdismsdfullx)isms - Integrated Security Model for SNMPdnamex"Integrated Security Model for SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127472hkeywordsÑrNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPrSNMP-TRANSPORT-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateJJ•€granking÷crfcd5590irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberNetotaletitlexETransport Subsystem for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyﬁThis document describes providing Call Transfer capabilities in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  SIP extensions such as REFER and Replaces are used to provide a number of transfer services including blind transfer, consultative transfer, and attended transfer.  This work is part of the SIP multiparty call control framework.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationiSIPez LLCdnameiD. Petrieddatej‘Zhfilenamegrfc5589eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127471hkeywordsãeREFERdGRUUqAttended TransfermTarget-DialogsOut of Dialog REFERcSIPlSIP Servicesnblind transferlSIP FeatureshReplaceskReferred-BykobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateJESgranking’crfcd5589irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberïetotaletitlex9Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Call Control - TransferdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document defines a new function for the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API), which allows applications to store delegated (and other) credentials in the implicit GSS-API credential store.  This is needed for GSS-API applications to use delegated credentials as they would use other credentials. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. Williamsddatej‘]hfilenamegrfc5588eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127470hkeywordsÉgGSS-APIjcredentialngss_store_credkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJS’granking‘crfcd5588irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlGeneric Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Extension for Storing Delegated CredentialsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document introduces new application programming interfaces (APIs) to the Generic Security Services API (GSS-API) for extended mechanism attribute inquiry. These interfaces are primarily intended to reduce instances of hardcoding of mechanism identifiers in GSS applications.

These interfaces include mechanism attributes and attribute sets, a function for inquiring the attributes of a mechanism, a function for indicating mechanisms that possess given attributes, and a function for displaying mechanism attributes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. Williamsddatej‘Uhfilenamegrfc5587eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127469hkeywordsÑgGSS-APIimechanismginquiryiextensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJspFgranking”crfcd5587irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Extended Generic Security Service Mechanism Inquiry APIsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThis document generalizes the applicability of the pseudowire (PW) Associated Channel Header (ACH), enabling the realization of a control channel associated to MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs) and MPLS Sections in addition to MPLS pseudowires.  In order to identify the presence of this Associated Channel Header in the label stack, this document also assigns one of the reserved MPLS label values to the Generic Associated Channel Label (GAL), to be used as a label based exception mechanism.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelM. Vigoureux¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryantddatej‘Zhfilenamegrfc5586eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127468hkeywordságmpls-tpcoameg-achcachxassociated channel headercgalxgeneric associated labelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ.˜√granking“crfcd5586irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMPLS Generic Associated ChanneldtypecrfciupdatedByÑd7026d6423d7274d7214∂habstractyThis document provides an overview of the DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) service and describes how it can fit into a messaging service.  It also describes how DKIM relates to other IETF message signature technologies.  It is intended for those who are adopting, developing, or deploying DKIM.  DKIM allows an organization to take responsibility for transmitting a message, in a way that can be verified by a recipient.  The organization can be the author's, the originating sending site, an intermediary, or one of their agents.  A message can contain multiple signatures from the same or different organizations involved with the message.  DKIM defines a domain-level digital signature authentication framework for email, using public-key cryptography, with the domain name service as its key server technology (RFC 4871).  This permits verification of a responsible organization, as well as the integrity of the message contents.  DKIM also enables a mechanism that permits potential email signers to publish information about their email signing practices; this will permit email receivers to make additional assessments about messages.  DKIM's authentication of email identity can assist in the global control of "spam" and "phishing".  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationxDefault Deny Security, Inc.dnameoP. Hallam-Bakerddatej‘]hfilenamegrfc5585eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-127467hkeywordsÑeEmailnElectroni MailmInternet MailtMessage VerificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJS‘ˆgranking—crfcd5585irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Service OverviewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿThis document describes an RTP payload format for efficient and flexible transporting of audio data encoded with the Adaptive TRansform Audio Coding (ATRAC) family of codecs.  Recent enhancements to the ATRAC family of codecs support high-quality audio coding with multiple channels.  The RTP payload format as presented in this document also includes support for data fragmentation, elementary redundancy measures, and a variation on scalable streaming. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSony CorporationdnamekM. Hatanaka¢kaffiliationpSony CorporationdnamelJ. Matsumotoddatej‘[hfilenamegrfc5584eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127466hkeywordsècRTPeaudiomfragmentationnlayered codingkmultiplexedmmulti-sessionmmulti-channeljredundancyhscalableeATRACfATRAC3gATRAC-XwATRAC Advanced LosslesscAALpSony CorporationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJms¡granking–crfcd5584irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLRTP Payload Format for the Adaptive TRansform Acoustic Coding (ATRAC) FamilydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis memo defines semantics that allow for signaling the decoding dependency of different media descriptions with the same media type in the Session Description Protocol (SDP). This is required, for example, if media data is separated and transported in different network streams as a result of the use of a layered or multiple descriptive media coding process.

A new grouping type "DDP" -- decoding dependency -- is defined, to be used in conjunction with RFC 3388 entitled "Grouping of Media Lines in the Session Description Protocol". In addition, an attribute is specified describing the relationship of the media streams in a "DDP" group indicated by media identification attribute(s) and media format description(s). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer HHIdnamejT. Schierl¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiS. Wengerddatej‘[hfilenamegrfc5583eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127465hkeywordsÉlmedia codingcddpsdecoding dependencykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJS‘ﬁgrankingœcrfcd5583irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMSignaling Media Decoding Dependency in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document describes an architecture for a global, scalable, resilient, and administratively distributed system for mapping geographic location information to URLs, using the Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) protocol.  The architecture generalizes well-known approaches found in hierarchical lookup systems such as DNS.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej‘Zhfilenamegrfc5582eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-127464hkeywordsÑeECRITgMappingdLoSTqEmergency callingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ†F2grankingŒcrfcd5582irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Location-to-URL Mapping Architecture and FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∞This document presents the necessary information to use the Camellia symmetric block cipher in the OpenPGP protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Shawddatej‘Thfilenamegrfc5581eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127463hkeywordsÖcPGPcGPGeGnuPGjEncryptioniSymmetrickobsoletedByÅd9580epagesopublicationDateJ'	grankingÕcrfcd5581irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Camellia Cipher in OpenPGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThis document describes procedures for conveying access-network ownership and location information based on civic and geospatial location formats in Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) and Diameter.

The distribution of location information is a privacy-sensitive task. Dealing with mechanisms to preserve the user's privacy is important and is addressed in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejF. Adrangi¢kaffiliationkBridgewaterdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationkBridgewaterdnamegA. Lior¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Abobaddatej‘^hfilenamegrfc5580eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127462hkeywordsÇx*remote authentication dial-in user servicetlocation informationkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateJxπÅgrankingÃcrfcd5580irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Carrying Location Objects in RADIUS and DiameterdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8559µhabstractyThe Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP) specifies a Non-Broadcast, Multiple Access (NBMA) interface type for the transmission of IPv6 packets over IPv4 networks using automatic IPv6-in-IPv4 encapsulation.  The original specifications make no provisions for the encapsulation and transmission of IPv4 packets, however.  This document specifies a method for transmitting IPv4 packets over ISATAP interfaces.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamejF. Templinddatej‘Ihfilenamegrfc5579eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127461hkeywordsÜfISATAPfTunnelmEncapsulationnMap-and-EncapsdIPv4dIPv6kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateKàógrankingÀcrfcd5579irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexeTransmission of IPv4 Packets over Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP) InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty∂This document extends the Point-to-Point Protocol over Ethernet (PPPoE) with an optional credit-based flow control mechanism and an optional Link Quality Metric report.  These optional extensions improve the performance of PPPoE over media with variable bandwidth and limited buffering, such as mobile point-to-point radio links.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehB. Berry¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Ratliff¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekE. Paradise¢kaffiliationrHarris CorporationdnameiT. Kaiser¢kaffiliationqL3 CommunicationsdnamehM. Adamsddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc5578eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127460hkeywordsÇx%point-to-point protocol over ethernetslink quality metrickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKj'&granking crfcd5578irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexEPPP over Ethernet (PPPoE) Extensions for Credit Flow and Link MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrInternational Telecommunication Union (ITU-T) Recommendation G.722.1 is a wide-band audio codec.  This document describes the payload format for including G.722.1-generated bit streams within an RTP packet.  The document also describes the syntax and semantics of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) parameters needed to support G.722.1 audio codec. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhTandbergdnamehP. Luthi¢kaffiliationpGesher Erove LtddnamegR. Evenddatej‘\hfilenamegrfc5577eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127459hkeywordsÇx%international telecommunication unionuwide-band audio codedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJS‘Àgranking…crfcd5577irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3RTP Payload Format for ITU-T Recommendation G.722.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†The Session Description Protocol (SDP) provides mechanisms to describe attributes of multimedia sessions and of individual media streams (e.g., Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) sessions) within a multimedia session, but does not provide any mechanism to describe individual media sources within a media stream.  This document defines a mechanism to describe RTP media sources, which are identified by their synchronization source (SSRC) identifiers, in SDP, to associate attributes with these sources, and to express relationships among sources.  It also defines several source-level attributes that can be used to describe properties of media sources. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeVidyodnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationx!Helsinki University of TechnologydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationnFraunhofer HHIdnamejT. Schierlddatej‘\hfilenamegrfc5576eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127458hkeywordsàxreal-time transport protocolcrtpvsynchronization sourcedssrccfidsflow identificationcfecxforward error correctionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJJ•’granking»crfcd5576irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSource-Specific Media Attributes in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document defines a new Border Gateway Protocol Network Layer Reachability Information (BGP NLRI) encoding format that can be used to distribute traffic flow specifications. This allows the routing system to propagate information regarding more specific components of the traffic aggregate defined by an IP destination prefix.

Additionally, it defines two applications of that encoding format: one that can be used to automate inter-domain coordination of traffic filtering, such as what is required in order to mitigate (distributed) denial-of-service attacks, and a second application to provide traffic filtering in the context of a BGP/MPLS VPN service.

The information is carried via the BGP, thereby reusing protocol algorithms, operational experience, and administrative processes such as inter-provider peering agreements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Marques¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehN. Sheth¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiB. Greene¢kaffiliationkNTT AmericadnamehJ. Mauch¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelD. McPhersonddatej‘[hfilenamegrfc5575eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127457hkeywordsàcIDRtInter-domain routingcBGPdDDOSqDenial of ServicecACLhFirewallfFilterkobsoletedByÅd8955epagesopublicationDateJè3ıgranking«crfcd5575irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Dissemination of Flow Specification RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7674∂habstractyCSpeex is an open-source voice codec suitable for use in VoIP (Voice over IP) type applications.  This document describes the payload format for Speex-generated bit streams within an RTP packet.  Also included here are the necessary details for the use of Speex with the Session Description Protocol (SDP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejG. Herlein¢kaffiliationsXiph.Org FoundationdnamehJ. Valin¢kaffiliationkCreytiv.comdnamelA. Heggestad¢kaffiliationgAntisipdnamejA. Moizardddatej‘Zhfilenamegrfc5574eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127456hkeywordsÖdVoipcSDPeaudioeCELLPhXiph.OrgkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ@0granking∆crfcd5574irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&RTP Payload Format for the Speex CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP) provides a protocol framework for the development of application protocols.  This document describes a BEEP feature that enables asynchrony for individual channels.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfAndrewdnamejM. Thomsonddatej‘Whfilenamegrfc5573eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127455hkeywordsÅxasynchronous beep channelskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ0GÌgranking≈crfcd5573irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHAsynchronous Channels for the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyŒA tunnel broker with the Tunnel Setup Protocol (TSP) enables the establishment of tunnels of various inner protocols, such as IPv6 or IPv4, inside various outer protocols packets, such as IPv4, IPv6, or UDP over IPv4 for IPv4 NAT traversal.  The control protocol (TSP) is used by the tunnel client to negotiate the tunnel with the broker.  A mobile node implementing TSP can be connected to both IPv4 and IPv6 networks whether it is on IPv4 only, IPv4 behind a NAT, or on IPv6 only.  A tunnel broker may terminate the tunnels on remote tunnel servers or on itself.  This document describes the TSP within the model of the tunnel broker model.  This document defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchet¢kaffiliationnBeon SolutionsdnameiF. Parentddatej”zhfilenamegrfc5572eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127454hkeywordsÑdIPv6fTunneljTransitioncTSPkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateK}Õ…grankingƒcrfcd5572irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex7IPv6 Tunnel Broker with the Tunnel Setup Protocol (TSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document describes the framework of the Softwire "Hub and Spoke" solution with the Layer Two Tunneling Protocol version 2 (L2TPv2).  The implementation details specified in this document should be followed to achieve interoperability among different vendor implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Storer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemM. Dos Santos¢kaffiliationpTELECOM BretagnednamejB. Stevant¢kaffiliationpTELECOM BretagnednamejL. Toutain¢kaffiliationnVideotron Ltd.dnamekJ. Tremblayddatej‘Xhfilenamegrfc5571eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127453hkeywordsàhSoftwiredL2TPvSoftwire Hub and SpokelSoftwire HnSf4over6f6over4nL2TP softwirespL2TPv2 softwireskobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateJ2ñ¬granking√crfcd5571irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Softwire Hub and Spoke Deployment Framework with Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Version 2 (L2TPv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document describes an optional method for encoding explicit packet Sensitivity Labels on IPv6 packets.  It is intended for use only within Multi-Level Secure (MLS) networking environments that are both trusted and trustworthy.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejM. StJohns¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnamekR. Atkinson¢kaffiliationrUS Dept of DefensednameiG. Thomasddatej‘Vhfilenamegrfc5570eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127452hkeywordsÉrsensitivity labelscmlsrmulti-level securekobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateJ\˝ãgranking¬crfcd5570irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Common Architecture Label IPv6 Security Option (CALIPSO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyIPv6 rapid deployment on IPv4 infrastructures (6rd) builds upon mechanisms of 6to4 to enable a service provider to rapidly deploy IPv6 unicast service to IPv4 sites to which it provides customer premise equipment.  Like 6to4, it utilizes stateless IPv6 in IPv4 encapsulation in order to transit IPv4-only network infrastructure.  Unlike 6to4, a 6rd service provider uses an IPv6 prefix of its own in place of the fixed 6to4 prefix.  A service provider has used this mechanism for its own IPv6 "rapid deployment": five weeks from first exposure to 6rd principles to more than 1,500,000 residential sites being provided native IPv6, under the only condition that they activate it.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiRD-IPtechdnamejR. Despresddatej‘;hfilenamegrfc5569eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127451hkeywordsÜdIPv6dIPv4imigrationjtransitiond6to4c6rdkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateK\¶granking¡crfcd5569irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex3IPv6 Rapid Deployment on IPv4 Infrastructures (6rd)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›Mobile IPv6 enables a mobile node (MN) to maintain its connectivity to the Internet when moving from one Access Router to another, a process referred to as handover. During handover, there is a period during which the mobile node is unable to send or receive packets because of link-switching delay and IP protocol operations. This "handover latency" resulting from standard Mobile IPv6 procedures (namely, movement detection, new Care-of Address configuration, and Binding Update) is often unacceptable to real-time traffic such as Voice over IP (VoIP). Reducing the handover latency could be beneficial to non-real-time, throughput-sensitive applications as well. This document specifies a protocol to improve handover latency due to Mobile IPv6 procedures. This document does not address improving the link-switching latency.

This document updates the packet formats for the Handover Initiate (HI) and Handover Acknowledge (HAck) messages to the Mobility Header Type. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnameiR. Koodliddatej‘Vhfilenamegrfc5568eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127450hkeywordsÇempiv6phandover latencykobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateJgö granking¿crfcd5568irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMobile IPv6 Fast HandoversdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7411∂habstractyBThis document describes an architectural framework for Media Server control.  The primary focus will be to define logical entities that exist within the context of Media Server control, and define the appropriate naming conventions and interactions between them.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxRain Willow CommunicationsdnamelT. MelanchukddatejÃŸhfilenamegrfc5567eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127449hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ.˜≤grankingøcrfcd5567irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3An Architectural Framework for Media Server ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂The BGP Encapsulation Subsequent Address Family Identifier (SAFI) provides a method for the dynamic exchange of encapsulation information and for the indication of encapsulation protocol types to be used for different next hops.  Currently, support for Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE), Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TPv3), and IP in IP tunnel types are defined.  This document defines support for IPsec tunnel types. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRalph Dromsdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. White¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehE. Rosenddatej‘Rhfilenamegrfc5566eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127448hkeywordsÉwborder gateway protocoldsafix$subsequent address family identifierkobsoletedByÅd9012epagesopublicationDateJ.˜†grankingæcrfcd5566irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(BGP IPsec Tunnel Encapsulation AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=The Internet needs to be able to handle both IPv4 and IPv6 packets.  However, it is expected that some constituent networks of the Internet will be "single-protocol" networks.  One kind of single-protocol network can parse only IPv4 packets and can process only IPv4 routing information; another kind can parse only IPv6 packets and can process only IPv6 routing information.  It is nevertheless required that either kind of single-protocol network be able to provide transit service for the "other" protocol.  This is done by passing the "other kind" of routing information from one edge of the single-protocol network to the other, and by tunneling the "other kind" of data packet from one edge to the other.  The tunnels are known as "softwires".  This framework document explains how the routing information and the data packets of one protocol are passed through a single-protocol network of the other protocol.  The document is careful to specify when this can be done with existing technology and when it requires the development of new or modified technology. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Wu¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefY. Cui¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegC. Metz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. RosenddatejÃ’hfilenamegrfc5565eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127447hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ.˜égrankingΩcrfcd5565irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewSoftwire Mesh FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractytThis document constitutes technical specifications for the use of Arabic in Internet domain names and provides linguistic guidelines for Arabic domain names.  It addresses Arabic-specific linguistic issues pertaining to the use of Arabic language in domain names.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. DusseaultgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhUN-ESCWAdnamenA. El-Sherbiny¢kaffiliationhUN-ESCWAdnamehM. Farah¢kaffiliationxSyrian Telecom EstablishmentdnamekI. Oueichek¢kaffiliationnSaudiNIC, CITCdnamekA. Al-Zomanddatej‘5hfilenamegrfc5564eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127446hkeywordsÅsarabic domain nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKuΩngrankingºcrfcd5564irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLLinguistic Guidelines for the Use of the Arabic Language in Internet DomainsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty+Mobile IPv4 is a standard mobility protocol that enables an IPv4 device to move among networks while maintaining its IP address.  The mobile device has the Mobile IPv4 client function to signal its location to the routing anchor, known as the Home Agent.  However, there are many IPv4 devices without such capability due to various reasons.  This document describes Proxy Mobile IPv4 (PMIPv4), a scheme based on having the Mobile IPv4 client function in a network entity to provide mobility support for an unaltered and mobility-unaware IPv4 device.  This document also describes a particular application of PMIPv4 as specified in the WiMAX Forum and another application that is to be adopted in 3GPP2.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Dommety¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiP. Yegani¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnamelK. Chowdhuryddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5563eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127445hkeywordsÇemipv4fpmipv4kobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateKpŒògrankingªcrfcd5563irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex%WiMAX Forum / 3GPP2 Proxy Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€The proposal in this document is Experimental. While it may be deployed in the current Internet, it does not represent a consensus that this is the best possible mechanism for the use of Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) in TCP SYN/ACK packets.

This document describes an optional, experimental modification to RFC 3168 to allow TCP SYN/ACK packets to be ECN-Capable. For TCP, RFC 3168 specifies setting an ECN-Capable codepoint on data packets, but not on SYN and SYN/ACK packets. However, because of the high cost to the TCP transfer of having a SYN/ACK packet dropped, with the resulting retransmission timeout, this document describes the use of ECN for the SYN/ACK packet itself, when sent in response to a SYN packet with the two ECN flags set in the TCP header, indicating a willingness to use ECN. Setting the initial TCP SYN/ACK packet as ECN-Capable can be of great benefit to the TCP connection, avoiding the severe penalty of a retransmission timeout for a connection that has not yet started placing a load on the network. The TCP responder (the sender of the SYN/ACK packet) must reply to a report of an ECN-marked SYN/ACK packet by resending a SYN/ACK packet that is not ECN-Capable. If the resent SYN/ACK packet is acknowledged, then the TCP responder reduces its initial congestion window from two, three, or four segments to one segment, thereby reducing the subsequent load from that connection on the network. If instead the SYN/ACK packet is dropped, or for some other reason the TCP responder does not receive an acknowledgement in the specified time, the TCP responder follows TCP standards for a dropped SYN/ACK packet (setting the retransmission timer). This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwNorthwestern UniversitydnamemA. Kuzmanovic¢kaffiliationwNorthwestern UniversitydnameiA. Mondal¢kaffiliationdICSIdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameoK. Ramakrishnanddatej‘^hfilenamegrfc5562eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127444hkeywordsÅkecn-capablekobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateJEPgranking∫crfcd5562irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQAdding Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) Capability to TCP's SYN/ACK PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩA number of enhancements to the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) have been proposed.  Some have been implemented, and some are advancing toward standardization.  It is likely that additional enhancements will be proposed in the future.  This document defines a mechanism for advertising LDP enhancements at session initialization time, as well as a mechanism to enable and disable enhancements after LDP session establishment. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Thomas¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegK. Raza¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekS. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Rouxddatej‘Rhfilenamegrfc5561eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127443hkeywordsÉdMPLScLDPlCapabilitieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ^c€grankingπcrfcd5561irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlepLDP CapabilitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydWhen a packet is sent from one host to the other, one normally expects that exactly one copy of the packet that was sent arrives at the destination. It is, however, possible that a packet is either lost or that multiple copies arrive.

In earlier work, a metric for packet loss was defined. This metric quantifies the case where a packet that is sent does not arrive at its destination within a reasonable time. In this memo, a metric for another case is defined: a packet is sent, but multiple copies arrive. The document also discusses streams and methods to summarize the results of streams. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamemH. Uijterwaalddatej‘Vhfilenamegrfc5560eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127442hkeywordsÉsperformance metricsrpacket duplicationnunidirectionalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ c”granking∏crfcd5560irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#A One-Way Packet Duplication MetricdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6248∂habstractyThis document describes a general architecture for flow admission and termination based on pre-congestion information in order to protect the quality of service of established, inelastic flows within a single Diffserv domain.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejP. Eardleyddatej‘Vhfilenamegrfc5559eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcndfullx0pcn - Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdnamex*Congestion and Pre-Congestion Notificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127441hkeywordsÜrQuality of ServicecQoSrCongestion ControlwDifferentiated ServicesqAdmission ControlkTerminationkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateJ:o4granking∑crfcd5559irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Pre-Congestion Notification (PCN) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty‘Enterprise networks connect routers over various link types, and may also connect to provider networks and/or the global Internet.  Enterprise network nodes require a means to automatically provision IP addresses/prefixes and support internetworking operation in a wide variety of use cases including Small Office, Home Office (SOHO) networks, Mobile Ad hoc Networks (MANETs), multi-organizational corporate networks and the interdomain core of the global Internet itself.  This document specifies a Virtual Enterprise Traversal (VET) abstraction for autoconfiguration and operation of nodes in enterprise networks.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamekRoss CallongauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBoeing Research & TechnologydnamejF. Templinddatej”òhfilenamegrfc5558eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127440hkeywordsàjEnterpriseeMANETmEncapsulationiTunnelingqAutoconfigurationjSubnetworktProvider-IndependentsProvider-AggregatedkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateKÉ3Égranking∂crfcd5558irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex"Virtual Enterprise Traversal (VET)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.The Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) allows Path Computation Clients (PCCs) to request path computations from Path Computation Elements (PCEs), and lets the PCEs return responses. When computing or reoptimizing the routes of a set of Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) through a network, it may be advantageous to perform bulk path computations in order to avoid blocking problems and to achieve more optimal network-wide solutions. Such bulk optimization is termed Global Concurrent Optimization (GCO). A GCO is able to simultaneously consider the entire topology of the network and the complete set of existing TE LSPs, and their respective constraints, and look to optimize or reoptimize the entire network to satisfy all constraints for all TE LSPs. A GCO may also be applied to some subset of the TE LSPs in a network. The GCO application is primarily a Network Management System (NMS) solution.

This document provides application-specific requirements and the PCEP extensions in support of GCO applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegD. King¢kaffiliationx$Univeristy of Electro CommunicationsdnamefE. Okiddatej‘Uhfilenamegrfc5557eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127439hkeywordsácpccxpath communication clientcpcecgcoxglobal concurrent optimizationcnmsxnetwork management systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ_©†grankingµcrfcd5557irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexàPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) Requirements and Protocol Extensions in Support of Global Concurrent OptimizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(Current IEEE 802.1 LANs use spanning tree protocols that have a number of challenges.  These protocols need to strictly avoid loops, even temporary ones, during route propagation, because of the lack of header loop detection support.  Routing tends not to take full advantage of alternate paths, or even non-overlapping pairwise paths (in the case of spanning trees).  This document addresses these concerns and suggests applying modern network-layer routing protocols at the link layer.  This document assumes that solutions would not address issues of scalability beyond that of existing IEEE 802.1 bridged links, but that a solution would be backward compatible with 802.1, including hubs, bridges, and their existing plug-and-play capabilities.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationcSundnamejR. Perlmanddatej‘Thfilenamegrfc5556eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetrilldfullx4trill - Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdnamex,Transparent Interconnection of Lots of Linksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127438hkeywordsÇvspanning tree protocoljieee 802.1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ*œgranking¥crfcd5556irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYTransparent Interconnection of Lots of Links (TRILL): Problem and Applicability StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜The current Mobile IPv6 and Network Mobility (NEMO) specifications support IPv6 only.  This specification extends those standards to allow the registration of IPv4 addresses and prefixes, respectively, and the transport of both IPv4 and IPv6 packets over the tunnel to the home agent.  This specification also allows the mobile node to roam over both IPv6 and IPv4, including the case where Network Address Translation is present on the path between the mobile node and its home agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtElevate TechnologiesdnamejH. Solimanddatej‘Vhfilenamegrfc5555eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127437hkeywordsÉdnemoemipv6dipv4kobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateJ.˜Ägranking≥crfcd5555irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Mobile IPv6 Support for Dual Stack Hosts and RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstractyThis document clarifies and generalizes the Generic Security Service Application Programming Interface (GSS-API) "channel bindings" facility, and imposes requirements on future GSS-API mechanisms and programming language bindings of the GSS-API. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. Williamsddatej‘Vhfilenamegrfc5554eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127436hkeywordsÑcGSSgGSS-APIochannel bindingnand C-bindingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ	¸Ëgranking≤crfcd5554irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÖClarifications and Extensions to the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) for the Use of Channel BindingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThe paths taken by Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) may be computed by Path Computation Elements (PCEs). Where the TE LSP crosses multiple domains, such as Autonomous Systems (ASes), the path may be computed by multiple PCEs that cooperate, with each responsible for computing a segment of the path.

To preserve confidentiality of topology within each AS, the PCEs support a mechanism to hide the contents of a segment of a path (such as the segment of the path that traverses an AS), called the Confidential Path Segment (CPS), by encoding the contents as a Path Key Subobject (PKS) and embedding this subobject within the result of its path computation.

This document describes how to carry Path Key Subobjects in the Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Explicit Route Objects (EROs) and Record Route Objects (RROs) so as to facilitate confidentiality in the signaling of inter-domain TE LSPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekR. Bradford¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseurddatej‘Uhfilenamegrfc5553eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127435hkeywordsÜcpksrpath key subobjectcerouexplicit route objectcrrosrecord route objectkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ*õgranking±crfcd5553irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDResource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Extensions for Path Key SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a SIP interface to VoiceXML media services.  Commonly, Application Servers controlling Media Servers use this protocol for pure VoiceXML processing capabilities.  This protocol is an adjunct to the full MEDIACTRL protocol and packages mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemRobert Sparksdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehD. Burke¢kaffiliationgGenesysdnamehM. Scottddatej‘Thfilenamegrfc5552eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127434hkeywordsÖhVoiceXMLcSIPcMRFcIVRcIMSkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateJ*ãgranking∞crfcd5552irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(SIP Interface to VoiceXML Media ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBNotification and filtering mechanisms can make email more enjoyable on mobile and other constrained devices (such as those with limited screen sizes, memory, data transfer rates, etc.). Notifications make the client aware of significant events (such as the arrival of new mail) so it can react (such as by fetching interesting mail immediately). Filtering reduces the visible mail to a set of messages that meet some criteria for "interesting". This functionality is included in the goals of the Lemonade (Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environments) Working Group.

This document also discusses the use of server-to-server notifications, and how server to server notifications fit into an architecture that provides server to client notifications. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejR. Gellensddatej‘]hfilenamegrfc5551eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127433hkeywordsÇlnotificationifilteringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJåç≤grankingØcrfcd5551irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Lemonade Notifications ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝This document describes a profile (a set of required extensions, restrictions, and usage modes), dubbed Lemonade, of the IMAP, mail submission, and Sieve protocols. This profile allows clients (especially those that are constrained in memory, bandwidth, processing power, or other areas) to efficiently use IMAP and Submission to access and submit mail. This includes the ability to forward received mail without needing to download and upload the mail, to optimize submission, and to efficiently resynchronize in case of loss of connectivity with the server.

The Lemonade Profile relies upon several extensions to IMAP, Sieve, and Mail Submission protocols. The document also defines a new IMAP extension and registers several new IMAP keywords. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekD. Cridland¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegS. Maesddatej‘Nhfilenamegrfc5550eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127432hkeywordsÜdIMAPeSievedSMTPhLemonadelmobile emailwlow-bandwidth efficientkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateJåçögrankingÆcrfcd5550irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMThe Internet Email to Support Diverse Service Environments (Lemonade) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2Multiprotocol BGP (MP-BGP) specifies that the set of network-layer protocols to which the address carried in the Next Hop field may belong is determined by the Address Family Identifier (AFI) and the Subsequent Address Family Identifier (SAFI).  The current AFI/SAFI definitions for the IPv4 address family only have provisions for advertising a Next Hop address that belongs to the IPv4 protocol when advertising IPv4 Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) or VPN-IPv4 NLRI.  This document specifies the extensions necessary to allow advertising IPv4 NLRI or VPN-IPv4 NLRI with a Next Hop address that belongs to the IPv6 protocol.  This comprises an extension of the AFI/SAFI definitions to allow the address of the Next Hop for IPv4 NLRI or VPN-IPv4 NLRI to also belong to the IPv6 protocol, the encoding of the Next Hop in order to determine which of the protocols the address actually belongs to, and a new BGP Capability allowing MP-BGP Peers to dynamically discover whether they can exchange IPv4 NLRI and VPN-IPv4 NLRI with an IPv6 Next Hop. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehE. Rosenddatej‘Shfilenamegrfc5549eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127431hkeywordsÉcBGPdIPv6dIPv4kobsoletedByÅd8950epages
opublicationDateIˇ~qgranking≠crfcd5549irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMAdvertising IPv4 Network Layer Reachability Information with an IPv6 Next HopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThe application-specific routing requirements for Urban Low-Power and Lossy Networks (U-LLNs) are presented in this document.  In the near future, sensing and actuating nodes will be placed outdoors in urban environments so as to improve people's living conditions as well as to monitor compliance with increasingly strict environmental laws.  These field nodes are expected to measure and report a wide gamut of data (for example, the data required by applications that perform smart-metering or that monitor meteorological, pollution, and allergy conditions).  The majority of these nodes are expected to communicate wirelessly over a variety of links such as IEEE 802.15.4, low-power IEEE 802.11, or IEEE 802.15.1 (Bluetooth), which given the limited radio range and the large number of nodes requires the use of suitable routing protocols.  The design of such protocols will be mainly impacted by the limited resources of the nodes (memory, processing power, battery, etc.) and the particularities of the outdoor urban application scenarios.  As such, for a wireless solution for Routing Over Low-Power and Lossy (ROLL) networks to be useful, the protocol(s) ought to be energy-efficient, scalable, and autonomous.  This documents aims to specify a set of IPv6 routing requirements reflecting these and further U-LLNs' tailored characteristics.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdCTTCdnameiM. Dohler¢kaffiliationrBSAC / UC BerkeleydnamekT. Watteyne¢kaffiliationkEka SystemsdnameiT. Winter¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnamejD. Barthelddatej‘Thfilenamegrfc5548eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrolldfullx0roll - Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdnamex)Routing Over Low power and Lossy networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127430hkeywordsÉeu-llndrollxrouting over low-power and losskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ*vgranking¨crfcd5548irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Routing Requirements for Urban Low-Power and Lossy NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides a mechanism to negotiate the transfer of one or more files between two endpoints by using the Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer/answer model specified in RFC 3264.  SDP is extended to describe the attributes of the files to be transferred.  The offerer can describe either the files it wants to send or the files it would like to receive.  The answerer can either accept or reject the offer separately for each individual file.  The transfer of one or more files is initiated after a successful negotiation.  The Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) is defined as the default mechanism to actually carry the files between the endpoints.  The conventions on how to use MSRP for file transfer are also provided in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejM. Isomaki¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loreto¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Kyzivatddatej‘Thfilenamegrfc5547eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127429hkeywordsÇdmsrpxmessage session relay protocolkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateJ␀ wgranking´crfcd5547irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSA Session Description Protocol (SDP) Offer/Answer Mechanism to Enable File TransferdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This document specifies a protocol that uses the iCalendar object specification to provide scheduling interoperability between different calendaring systems. This is done without reference to a specific transport protocol so as to allow multiple methods of communication between systems. Subsequent documents will define profiles of this protocol that use specific, interoperable methods of communication between systems.

The iCalendar Transport-Independent Interoperability Protocol (iTIP) complements the iCalendar object specification by adding semantics for group scheduling methods commonly available in current calendaring systems. These scheduling methods permit two or more calendaring systems to perform transactions such as publishing, scheduling, rescheduling, responding to scheduling requests, negotiating changes, or canceling. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej‘xhfilenamegrfc5546eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgcalsifydfullx=calsify - Calendaring and Scheduling Standards Simplificationdnamex3Calendaring and Scheduling Standards Simplificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127428hkeywordsÇhcalendarjschedulingkobsoletedByÄepagesÖopublicationDateK2eAgranking™crfcd5546irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@iCalendar Transport-Independent Interoperability Protocol (iTIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6638∂habstractyThis document defines the iCalendar data format for representing and exchanging calendaring and scheduling information such as events, to-dos, journal entries, and free/busy information, independent of any particular calendar service or protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfOraclednameoB. Desruisseauxddatej‘Rhfilenamegrfc5545eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgcalsifydfullx=calsify - Calendaring and Scheduling Standards Simplificationdnamex3Calendaring and Scheduling Standards Simplificationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127427hkeywordsñgcalsifyhcalschedfcalschfcaldavhcalendarkcalendaringgmeetingeeventdtasketo-dogjournalkappointmentfagendahschedulejschedulingdicaliicalendarditipdimipmtext/calendariischeduleixCalendarkobsoletedByÄepages®opublicationDateJª¯bgranking©crfcd5545irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIInternet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification (iCalendar)dtypecrfciupdatedByàd6868d5546d7529d9253d7953d7986d9073d9074µhabstractyThis document describes an envelope that can be used to bind a file (not necessarily protected by means of cryptographic techniques) with one or more time-stamp tokens obtained for that file, where "time-stamp token" has the meaning defined in RFC 3161 or its successors. Additional types of temporal evidence are also allowed.

The proposed envelope is based on the Cryptographic Message Syntax as defined in RFC 5652. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamehTim PolkgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnActalis S.p.A.dnamejA. Santoniddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5544eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127426hkeywordsÅptime-stamp tokenkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKpKÒgranking®crfcd5544irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-Syntax for Binding Documents with Time-StampsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5955∂habstracty
This document defines a new BGP attribute, the Traffic Engineering attribute, that enables BGP to carry Traffic Engineering information.

The scope and applicability of this attribute currently excludes its use for non-VPN reachability information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamenH. Ould-Brahim¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej‘Shfilenamegrfc5543eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127425hkeywordsáfBGP-TEpBGP-TE AttributexTraffic Engineering with BGPx Inter-domain Traffic EngineeringlL1VPN BGP-TEjBGP-TE-VPNx%VPN BGP Traffic Engineering AttributekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIˇ~Ygrankingßcrfcd5543irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!BGP Traffic Engineering AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7606∂habstractyBThis memo defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module that contains textual conventions (TCs) to represent commonly used pseudowire (PW) management information.  The intent is that these TCs will be imported and used in PW-related MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationbBTdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationfOversidnamehD. Zelig¢kaffiliationiRADVISIONdnamekO. Nicklassddatej‘Qhfilenamegrfc5542eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127424hkeywordsÖjPseudowiredPWE3cMIBgPWE3-TCePW-TCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ*_granking¶crfcd5542irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADefinitions of Textual Conventions for Pseudowire (PW) ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThe computation of one or a set of Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) in MultiProtocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks is subject to a set of one or more specific optimization criteria, referred to as objective functions (e.g., minimum cost path, widest path, etc.).

In the Path Computation Element (PCE) architecture, a Path Computation Client (PCC) may want a path to be computed for one or more TE LSPs according to a specific objective function. Thus, the PCC needs to instruct the PCE to use the correct objective function. Furthermore, it is possible that not all PCEs support the same set of objective functions; therefore, it is useful for the PCC to be able to automatically discover the set of objective functions supported by each PCE.

This document defines extensions to the PCE communication Protocol (PCEP) to allow a PCE to indicate the set of objective functions it supports. Extensions are also defined so that a PCC can indicate in a path computation request the required objective function, and a PCE can report in a path computation reply the objective function that was used for path computation.

This document defines objective function code types for six objective functions previously listed in the PCE requirements work, and provides the definition of four new metric types that apply to a set of synchronized requests. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAdrian Farreldarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationqCisco System Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefY. Leeddatej‘Qhfilenamegrfc5541eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127423hkeywordsÇcpccwpath computation clientkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ.˜Ygranking•crfcd5541irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Encoding of Objective Functions in the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx{This RFC marks the 40th anniversary of the RFC document series.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamejRFC EditorddatejÃ’hfilenamegrfc5540eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43463hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI€·<granking§crfcd5540irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlep40 Years of RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8700∂habstractyThe Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) provides mechanisms to install, manipulate, and delete the configuration of network devices.  This document describes how to use the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to secure NETCONF exchanges. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuCNRS/LIMOS LaboratorydnamehM. Badraddatej‘Qhfilenamegrfc5539eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127422hkeywordsÉnAuthenticationcTLScRPCkobsoletedByÅd7589epagesopublicationDateIˇ~granking£crfcd5539irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+NETCONF over Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This memo specifies the 'news' and 'nntp' Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) schemes that were originally defined in RFC 1738.  The purpose of this document is to allow RFC 1738 to be made obsolete while keeping the information about these schemes on the Standards Track. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqPeter Saint-Andredarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationexyzzydnamelF. EllermannddatejÃdhfilenamegrfc5538eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127421hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKÿ¸zgranking¢crfcd5538irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!The 'news' and 'nntp' URI SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThis document defines the architecture of Netnews systems and specifies the correct manipulation and interpretation of Netnews articles by software that originates, distributes, stores, and displays them.  It also specifies the requirements that must be met by any protocol used to transport and serve Netnews articles. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamejR. Allbery¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Lindseyddatej‘Ohfilenamegrfc5537eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfusefordfullx'usefor - Usenet Article Standard UpdatednamexUsenet Article Standard Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-127420hkeywordsÉfuseforfUsenetgnetnewskobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateK˝granking°crfcd5537irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Netnews Architecture and ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8315∂habstractydThis document specifies the syntax of Netnews articles in the context of the Internet Message Format (RFC 5322) and Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) (RFC 2045).  This document obsoletes RFC 1036, providing an updated specification to reflect current practice and incorporating incremental changes specified in other documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamelK. Murchison¢kaffiliationxUniversity of ManchesterdnamejC. Lindsey¢kaffiliationrHealing ThresholdsdnamegD. Kohnddatej”ÿhfilenamegrfc5536eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfusefordfullx'usefor - Usenet Article Standard UpdatednamexUsenet Article Standard Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-127419hkeywordsÇfUsenetfUseforkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateK‚granking†crfcd5536irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevNetnews Article FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis memo describes a mechanism to provide a secure binding between the multiple addresses with different prefixes available to a host within a multihomed site.  This mechanism employs either Cryptographically Generated Addresses (CGAs) or a new variant of the same theme that uses the same format in the addresses.  The main idea in the new variant is that information about the multiple prefixes is included within the addresses themselves.  This is achieved by generating the interface identifiers of the addresses of a host as hashes of the available prefixes and a random number.  Then, the multiple addresses are generated by prepending the different prefixes to the generated interface identifiers.  The result is a set of addresses, called Hash-Based Addresses (HBAs), that are inherently bound to each other. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnuloddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5535eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeshim6dfullx/shim6 - Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdnamex'Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127418hkeywordsÉeShim6lmulti-homingx,cryptographically generated addresses (cgas)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ:ogrankingücrfcd5535irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHash-Based Addresses (HBA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document specifies how the level 3 multihoming Shim6 protocol (Shim6) detects failures between two communicating nodes.  It also specifies an exploration protocol for switching to another pair of interfaces and/or addresses between the same nodes if a failure occurs and an operational pair can be found. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationnIMDEA NetworksdnamenI. van Beijnumddatej‘Qhfilenamegrfc5534eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeshim6dfullx/shim6 - Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdnamex'Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127417hkeywordsÉeShim6ureachability protocoldREAPkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateJ:mıgrankingûcrfcd5534irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLFailure Detection and Locator Pair Exploration Protocol for IPv6 MultihomingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document defines the Shim6 protocol, a layer 3 shim for providing locator agility below the transport protocols, so that multihoming can be provided for IPv6 with failover and load-sharing properties, without assuming that a multihomed site will have a provider-independent IPv6 address prefix announced in the global IPv6 routing table.  The hosts in a site that has multiple provider- allocated IPv6 address prefixes will use the Shim6 protocol specified in this document to set up state with peer hosts so that the state can later be used to failover to a different locator pair, should the original one stop working. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnuloddatej‘Nhfilenamegrfc5533eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeshim6dfullx/shim6 - Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdnamex'Site Multihoming by IPv6 Intermediationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127416hkeywordsÅllocator pairkobsoletedByÄepages|opublicationDateJ:m€grankingùcrfcd5533irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Shim6: Level 3 Multihoming Shim Protocol for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸This document addresses enabling the use of Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) by the Network File System (NFS) protocols.  NFS implementations historically incur significant overhead due to data copies on end-host systems, as well as other processing overhead.  This document explores the potential benefits of RDMA to these implementations and evaluates the reasons why RDMA is especially well-suited to NFS and network file protocols in general.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Talpey¢kaffiliation`dnamekC. Juszczakddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5532eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System V