This document collects and documents a variety of machine-readable response codes, for better interoperation and error reporting. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmbHdnamenA. Gulbrandsenddatej‘Qhfilenamegrfc5530eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127413hkeywordsÅxmachine-readable response codeskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateIˇ}≤grankingöcrfcd5530irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesIMAP Response CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThis document describes the use of the Camellia block cipher algorithm in Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) mode, Counter (CTR) mode, and Counter with CBC-MAC (CCM) mode as additional, optional-to- implement Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) and Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) mechanisms to provide confidentiality, data origin authentication, and connectionless integrity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamegA. Kato¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehM. Kanda¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamehS. Kannoddatej‘Phfilenamegrfc5529eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127412hkeywordsÑeIPsechCamellialBlock CipherqMode of operationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÔ¨Êgrankingôcrfcd5529irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Modes of Operation for Camellia for Use with IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document describes the algorithms and presents test vectors for the Camellia block cipher algorithm in Counter mode (CTR) and Counter with Cipher Block Chaining MAC mode (CCM).  The purpose of this document is to make the Camellia-CTR and Camellia-CCM algorithm conveniently available to the Internet Community.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamegA. Kato¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamehM. Kanda¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamehS. Kannoddatej‘Phfilenamegrfc5528eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127411hkeywordsÉhCamellialBlock CipherqMode of operationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÔ¨≈grankingòcrfcd5528irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGCamellia Counter Mode and Camellia Counter with CBC-MAC Mode AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This memo defines an interim solution for Infrastructure ENUM in order to allow a combined User and Infrastructure ENUM implementation in e164.arpa as a national choice.  This interim solution will be deprecated after implementation of the long-term solution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIPAdnamekM. Haberler¢kaffiliationgenum.atdnamehO. Lendl¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamejR. Stastnyddatej‘Phfilenamegrfc5527eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127410hkeywordsÅie164.arpakobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateIˇ}Ügrankingócrfcd5527irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Combined User and Infrastructure ENUM in the e164.arpa TreedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document defines the use case for Infrastructure ENUM and proposes its implementation as a parallel namespace to "e164.arpa", as defined in RFC 3761, as the long-term solution to the problem of allowing carriers to provision DNS records for telephone numbers independently of those provisioned by end users (number assignees).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxComcast Cable CommunicationsdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiP. Pfautz¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamejR. Stastnyddatej‘Phfilenamegrfc5526eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127409hkeywordsÅie164.arpakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÔ¨ùgrankingñcrfcd5526irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~The E.164 to Uniform Resource Identifiers (URI) Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Application for Infrastructure ENUMdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆReliable Server Pooling (RSerPool) is a framework to provide reliable server pooling.  The RSerPool framework consists of two protocols: ASAP (Aggregate Server Access Protocol) and ENRP (Endpoint Handlespace Redundancy Protocol).  This document defines an \%SMIv2- compliant (Structure of Management Information Version 2) Management Information Base (MIB) module providing access to managed objects in an RSerPool implementation.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Duisburg-EssendnamelT. Dreibholz¢kaffiliationxDelaware State UniversitydnamehJ. Mulikddatej‘Ohfilenamegrfc5525eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127408hkeywordsáhRSerPoolxManagement Information Basedasapx aggregate server access protocoldenrpx(endpoint handlespace redundancy protocollRSERPOOL-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateIÔ¨ågrankingïcrfcd5525irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Reliable Server Pooling MIB Module DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThe URLFETCH command defined as part of URLAUTH provides a mechanism for third parties to gain access to data held within messages in a user's private store; however, this data is sent verbatim, which is not suitable for a number of applications.  This memo specifies a method for obtaining data in forms suitable for non-mail applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekD. Cridlandddatej‘Phfilenamegrfc5524eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127407hkeywordsÇdIMAPhLemonadekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateIˇ}ugrankingîcrfcd5524irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Extended URLFETCH for Binary and Converted PartsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document defines an OSPFv3-based (Open Shortest Path First version 3) Layer 1 Virtual Private Network (L1VPN) auto-discovery mechanism.  This document parallels the existing OSPF version 2 L1VPN auto-discovery mechanism.  The notable functional difference is the support of IPv6.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtLabN Consulting, LLCdnameiL. Bergerddatej‘Ohfilenamegrfc5523eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel1vpndfullx(l1vpn - Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127406hkeywordsÇxopen shortest path firstxlayer 1 virtual private networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIﬁXgrankingìcrfcd5523irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'OSPFv3-Based Layer 1 VPN Auto-DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚This document describes the requirements and desired properties of Network Mobility (NEMO) Route Optimization techniques for use in global-networked communications systems for aeronautics and space exploration.

Substantial input to these requirements was given by aeronautical communications experts outside the IETF, including members of the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) and other aeronautical communications standards bodies. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamegW. Eddy¢kaffiliationdNASAdnamejW. Ivancic¢kaffiliationfBoeingdnamehT. Davisddatej‘ohfilenamegrfc5522eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmextdfullx#mext - Mobility EXTensions for IPv6dnamexMobility EXTensions for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127405hkeywordsÖdNEMOkaeronauticsqspace explorationrroute optimizationhmobilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJÕ-ogrankingícrfcd5522irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyNetwork Mobility Route Optimization Requirements for Operational Use in Aeronautics and Space Exploration Mobile NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥The Path Computation Element (PCE) provides functions of path computation in support of traffic engineering (TE) in Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks.

When a Path Computation Client (PCC) requests a PCE for a route, it may be useful for the PCC to specify, as constraints to the path computation, abstract nodes, resources, and Shared Risk Link Groups (SRLGs) that are to be explicitly excluded from the computed route. Such constraints are termed "route exclusions".

The PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) is designed as a communication protocol between PCCs and PCEs. This document presents PCEP extensions for route exclusions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx$University of Electro-CommunicationsdnamefE. Oki¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takeda¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘-hfilenamegrfc5521eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127404hkeywordsÑdMPLSeGMPLSsTraffic EngineeringsLabel Switched PathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI˜óYgrankingëcrfcd5521irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Extensions to the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCEP) for Route ExclusionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) may be computed by Path Computation Elements (PCEs). Where the TE LSP crosses multiple domains, such as Autonomous Systems (ASes), the path may be computed by multiple PCEs that cooperate, with each responsible for computing a segment of the path. However, in some cases (e.g., when ASes are administered by separate Service Providers), it would break confidentiality rules for a PCE to supply a path segment to a PCE in another domain, thus disclosing AS-internal topology information. This issue may be circumvented by returning a loose hop and by invoking a new path computation from the domain boundary Label Switching Router (LSR) during TE LSP setup as the signaling message enters the second domain, but this technique has several issues including the problem of maintaining path diversity.

This document defines a mechanism to hide the contents of a segment of a path, called the Confidential Path Segment (CPS). The CPS may be replaced by a path-key that can be conveyed in the PCE Communication Protocol (PCEP) and signaled within in a Resource Reservation Protocol TE (RSVP-TE) explicit route object. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekR. Bradford¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘>hfilenamegrfc5520eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127403hkeywordsÉxconfidential path segmentccpsdpcepkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI˜óPgrankingêcrfcd5520irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexePreserving Topology Confidentiality in Inter-Domain Path Computation Using a Path-Key-Based MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing the Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and the Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednameoJ. Chesterfield¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamekB. Habermanddatej”ƒhfilenamegrfc5519eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-127402hkeywordsÖxmanagement information basedmgmdcmldxmulticast listener discoverylMGMD-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateI÷üÙgrankingècrfcd5519irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Multicast Group Membership Discovery MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Vouch By Reference (VBR) protocol.  VBR is a protocol for adding third-party certification to email.  It permits independent third parties to certify the owner of a domain name that is associated with received mail. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxDomain Assurance CouncildnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationxDomain Assurance CouncildnameiJ. Levine¢kaffiliationrAlt-N TechnologiesdnamekA. Hathcockddatej‘Mhfilenamegrfc5518eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127401hkeywordsÖcVBRdDKIMhSenderIDbDKjreputationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI⁄ñ
grankingécrfcd5518irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlerVouch By ReferencedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553µhabstractyThis document describes a mechanism to achieve device isolation through the application of special Layer 2 forwarding constraints. Such a mechanism allows end devices to share the same IP subnet while being Layer 2 isolated, which in turn allows network designers to employ larger subnets and so reduce the address management overhead.

Some of the numerous deployment scenarios of the aforementioned mechanism (which range from data center designs to Ethernet-to-the-home-basement networks) are mentioned in the following text to exemplify the mechanism's possible usages; however, this document is not intended to cover all such deployment scenarios nor delve into their details. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameoS. HomChaudhuri¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. FoschianoddatejÀﬂhfilenamegrfc5517eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127400hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKpŒÖgrankingçcrfcd5517irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexMCisco Systems' Private VLANs: Scalable Security in a Multi-Client EnvironmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This document registers a set of IANA Diameter Command Codes to be used in new vendor-specific Diameter applications defined for the Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Evolved Packet System (EPS).  These new Diameter applications are defined for Mobile Management Entity (MME)- and Serving GPRS (General Packet Radio Service) Support Node (SGSN)-related interfaces in the architecture for the Evolved 3GPP Packet Switched Domain, which is also known as the Evolved Packet System (EPS).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamehM. Jones¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnameiL. Morandddatej‘Mhfilenamegrfc5516eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127399hkeywordsÖd3GPPiRelease 8hDiametermcommand codescEPSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÁ∂:grankingåcrfcd5516irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrDiameter Command Code Registration for the Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Evolved Packet System (EPS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThis document describes a set of Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) Attribute Value Pair (AVP) extensions designed to carry the subscriber Access Line identification and characterization information that arrives at the Broadband Remote Access Server (BRAS) with L2TP Access Concentrator (LAC) functionality.  It also describes a mechanism to report connection speed changes, after the initial connection speeds are sent during session establishment.  The primary purpose of this document is to provide a reference for DSL equipment vendors wishing to interoperate with other vendors' products.  The L2TP AVPs defined in this document are applicable to both L2TPv2 and L2TPv3.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelV. Mammoliti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiP. Arberg¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Gibbons¢kaffiliation`dnameiP. Howardddatej‘Rhfilenamegrfc5515eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127398hkeywordsÉdL2TPvAcces Line InformationeDSLAMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ␀ Hgrankingãcrfcd5515irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) Access Line Information Attribute Value Pair (AVP) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty'There is a lack of IPv6 utilization in early 2009; this is partly linked to the fact that the number of IPv6 nodes is rather low.  This document proposes to vastly increase the number of IPv6 hosts by transforming all Social Networking platforms into IPv6 networks.  This will immediately add millions of IPv6 hosts to the existing IPv6 Internet.  This document includes sections on addressing and transport of IPv6 over a Social Network.  A working prototype has been developed.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiE. Vynckeddatej‘Shfilenamegrfc5514eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43462hkeywordsÅhfacebookkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI”ïÜgrankingäcrfcd5514irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexIPv6 over Social NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyéThree Letter Acronyms (TLAs) are commonly used to identify components of networks or protocols as designed or specified within the IETF. A common concern is that one acronym may have multiple expansions. While this may not have been an issue in the past, network convergence means that protocols that did not previously operate together are now found in close proximity. This results in contention for acronyms, and confusion in interpretation. Such confusion has the potential to degrade the performance of the Internet as misunderstandings lead to misconfiguration or other operating errors.

Given the growing use of TLAs and the relatively small number available, this document specifies a Badly Construed Proposal (BCP) for the management of a registry of TLAs within the IETF, and the procedures for the allocation of new TLAs from the registry. This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘Shfilenamegrfc5513eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43461hkeywordsÇctlalabbreviationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI”ïDgrankingâcrfcd5513irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-IANA Considerations for Three Letter AcronymsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈIn certain situations, transporting a packet from one Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) speaker to another (the BGP next hop) requires that the packet be encapsulated by the first BGP speaker and decapsulated by the second. To support these situations, there needs to be some agreement between the two BGP speakers with regard to the "encapsulation information", i.e., the format of the encapsulation header as well as the contents of various fields of the header.

The encapsulation information need not be signaled for all encapsulation types. In cases where signaling is required (such as Layer Two Tunneling Protocol - Version 3 (L2TPv3) or Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) with key), this document specifies a method by which BGP speakers can signal encapsulation information to each other. The signaling is done by sending BGP updates using the Encapsulation Subsequent Address Family Identifier (SAFI) and the IPv4 or IPv6 Address Family Identifier (AFI). In cases where no encapsulation information needs to be signaled (such as GRE without key), this document specifies a BGP extended community that can be attached to BGP UPDATE messages that carry payload prefixes in order to indicate the encapsulation protocol type to be used. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelP. Mohapatra¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej‘Nhfilenamegrfc5512eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-127397hkeywordsácBGPmEncapsulationjEncap SAFIfTunnelhSoftwiref4over6f6over4kobsoletedByÅd9012epagesopublicationDateIﬁWˆgrankingàcrfcd5512irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlThe BGP Encapsulation Subsequent Address Family Identifier (SAFI) and the BGP Tunnel Encapsulation AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµSeveral protocols have been specified in the Routing Area of the IETF using a common variant of the Backus-Naur Form (BNF) of representing message syntax. However, there is no formal definition of this version of BNF.

There is value in using the same variant of BNF for the set of protocols that are commonly used together. This reduces confusion and simplifies implementation.

Updating existing documents to use some other variant of BNF that is already formally documented would be a substantial piece of work.

This document provides a formal definition of the variant of BNF that has been used (that we call Routing BNF) and makes it available for use by new protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘Rhfilenamegrfc5511eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127396hkeywordsÅkrouting bnfkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI˜óHgrankingácrfcd5511irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpRouting Backus-Naur Form (RBNF): A Syntax Used to Form Encoding Rules in Various Routing Protocol SpecificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document describes a Fully-Specified Forward Error Correction (FEC) Scheme for the Reed-Solomon FEC codes over GF(2^^m), where m is in {2..16}, and its application to the reliable delivery of data objects on the packet erasure channel (i.e., a communication path where packets are either received without any corruption or discarded during transmission). This document also describes a Fully-Specified FEC Scheme for the special case of Reed-Solomon codes over GF(2^^8) when there is no encoding symbol group. Finally, in the context of the Under-Specified Small Block Systematic FEC Scheme (FEC Encoding ID 129), this document assigns an FEC Instance ID to the special case of Reed-Solomon codes over GF(2^^8).

Reed-Solomon codes belong to the class of Maximum Distance Separable (MDS) codes, i.e., they enable a receiver to recover the k source symbols from any set of k received symbols. The schemes described here are compatible with the implementation from Luigi Rizzo. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnISAE/LAAS-CNRSdnamehJ. Lacan¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationx Tampere University of TechnologydnamelJ. Peltotalo¢kaffiliationx Tampere University of TechnologydnamelS. Peltotaloddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5510eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127395hkeywordsÇxmaximum distance separablecMDSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI˜ó?grankingÜcrfcd5510irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Reed-Solomon Forward Error Correction (FEC) SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxìThis document registers with IANA two new DNS SRV protocol labels for resolving Instant Messaging and Presence services with SIP. [STANDARDS TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loretoddatej‘Jhfilenamegrfc5509eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127394hkeywordsÅd_sipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI÷üÁgrankingÖcrfcd5509irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexèInternet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) Registration of Instant Messaging and Presence DNS SRV RRs for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty2This document specifies the behavioral properties required of the Network Address Translator (NAT) devices in conjunction with the Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP).  The objective of this memo is to make NAT devices more predictable and compatible with diverse application protocols that traverse the devices.  Companion documents provide behavioral recommendations specific to TCP, UDP, and other protocols.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnKazeon SystemsdnamelP. Srisuresh¢kaffiliationgMPI-SWSdnamegB. Ford¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationjCornell U.dnamegS. Guhaddatej‘Hhfilenamegrfc5508eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127393hkeywordsÜxICMP Error payload translationshairpin translationjICMP QueryjICMP ErrordPingjTraceroutekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI⁄ñ	grankingÑcrfcd5508irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberîetotaletitlex$NAT Behavioral Requirements for ICMPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7857µhabstractyoThis note discusses how to extend the DNS with new data for a new application.  DNS extension discussions too often focus on reuse of the TXT Resource Record Type.  This document lists different mechanisms to extend the DNS, and concludes that the use of a new DNS Resource Record Type is the best solution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIAB¢kaffiliation`dnamelP. Faltstrom¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliation`dnamegP. Kochddatej‘Mhfilenamegrfc5507eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127392hkeywordsÇrdomain name systemtresource record typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÔ¨dgrankingÉcrfcd5507irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex%Design Choices When Expanding the DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis memo discusses benefits and issues that arise when allowing Real-time Transport Protocol (RTCP) packets to be transmitted with reduced size.  The size can be reduced if the rules on how to create compound packets outlined in RFC 3550 are removed or changed.  Based on that analysis, this memo defines certain changes to the rules to allow feedback messages to be sent as Reduced-Size RTCP packets under certain conditions when using the RTP/AVPF (Real-time Transport Protocol / Audio-Visual Profile with Feedback) profile (RFC 4585).  This document updates RFC 3550, RFC 3711, and RFC 4585. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelI. Johansson¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamemM. Westerlundddatej‘Jhfilenamegrfc5506eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127391hkeywordsÑdAVPFlnon-compoundlnon compoundhcompoundkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIﬁg/grankingÇcrfcd5506irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdSupport for Reduced-Size Real-Time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP): Opportunities and ConsequencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty␀This document describes principles of Internet host configuration.  It covers issues relating to configuration of Internet-layer parameters, as well as parameters affecting higher-layer protocols.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamekS. Cheshireddatej‘Lhfilenamegrfc5505eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127390hkeywordsÇxinternet-layer parameterxhigher-layer configurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ*EgrankingÅcrfcd5505irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex)Principles of Internet Host ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+Traditional mail systems handle only ASCII characters in SMTP envelope and mail header fields.  The Email Address Internationalization (UTF8SMTP) extension allows UTF-8 characters in SMTP envelope and mail header fields.  To avoid rejecting internationalized email messages when a server in the delivery path does not support the UTF8SMTP extension, some sort of converting mechanism is required.  This document describes a downgrading mechanism for Email Address Internationalization.  Note that this is a way to downgrade, not tunnel.  There is no associated up-conversion mechanism, although internationalized email clients might use original internationalized addresses or other data when displaying or replying to downgraded messages.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwara¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnameiY. Yoneyaddatej‘Lhfilenamegrfc5504eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127389hkeywordsÑcEAIx"Email Address InternationalizationiDowngradedMAILkobsoletedByÅd6530epagesopublicationDateI“¢∑grankingÄcrfcd5504irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Downgrading Mechanism for Email Address InternationalizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFIn order to deploy a residential telephone service at a very large scale across different domains, it is necessary for trusted elements owned by different service providers to exchange trusted information that conveys customer-specific information and expectations about the parties involved in the call.  This document describes private extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol, RFC 3261, for supporting the exchange of customer information and billing information between trusted entities in the PacketCable Distributed Call Signaling Architecture.  These extensions provide mechanisms for access network coordination to prevent theft of service, customer originated trace of harassing calls, support for operator services and emergency services, and support for various other regulatory issues.  The use of the extensions is only applicable within closed administrative domains, or among federations of administrative domains with previously agreed-upon policies where coordination of charging and other functions is required.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Andreasen¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamekB. McKibben¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekB. Marshallddatej‘Fhfilenamegrfc5503eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127388hkeywordsÜtP-DCS-TRACE-PARTY-IDjP-DCS-OSPSrP-DCS-BILLING-INFOjP-DCS-LAESnP-DCS-RedirectjP-DCS-INFOkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateI∫˛ágrankingcrfcd5503irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäPrivate Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Proxy-to-Proxy Extensions for Supporting the PacketCable Distributed Call Signaling ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the SIP P-Served-User P-header.  This header field addresses an issue that was found in the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) IMS (IP Multimedia Subsystem) between an S-CSCF (Serving Call Session Control Function) and an AS (Application Server) on the ISC (IMS Service Control) interface.  This header field conveys the identity of the served user and the session case that applies to this particular communication session and application invocation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Telecommunicatie B.V.dnamemJ. van Elburgddatej‘Ihfilenamegrfc5502eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127387hkeywordsÑcSIPfS-CSCFbAScISCkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI⁄ñgranking~crfcd5502irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkThe SIP P-Served-User Private-Header (P-Header) for the 3GPP IP Multimedia (IM) Core Network (CN) SubsystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8498d8217∂habstractyhThis document provides functional requirements for network solutions that support multicast over Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS).  It specifies requirements both from the end user and service provider standpoints.  It is intended that potential solutions will use these requirements as guidelines.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnameiY. Kamite¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegY. Wada¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamejY. Serbest¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamehT. Morin¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegL. Fangddatej‘Hhfilenamegrfc5501eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127386hkeywordsàbL2cVPNdVPLShEthernetdP2MPdIGMPcMLDcPIMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIæÁÛgranking}crfcd5501irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBRequirements for Multicast Support in Virtual Private LAN ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document enumerates several common IANA allocations for use by Mobile Ad hoc NETwork (MANET) protocols.  The following well-known numbers are required: a UDP port number, an IP protocol number, and a link-local multicast group address. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfCenGendnamekI. Chakeresddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5498eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127385hkeywordsÅomanet protocolskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∫˛rgrankingzcrfcd5498irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<IANA Allocations for Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis document describes a general and flexible TLV (type-length-value structure) for representing time-values, such as an interval or a duration, using the generalized Mobile Ad hoc NETwork (MANET) packet/ message format.  It defines two Message TLVs and two Address Block TLVs for representing validity and interval times for MANET routing protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxLIX - Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearloveddatej”îhfilenamegrfc5497eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127384hkeywordsàpRouting ProtocolcTLVgFisheyecFSRmFuzzy-SightediextensionhpacketbbgRFC5444kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∫˛ögrankingycrfcd5497irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Representing Multi-Value Time in Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a use of the Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) Join Attribute as defined in RFC 5384, which enables PIM to build multicast trees through an MPLS-enabled network, even if that network's IGP does not have a route to the source of the tree. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelIJ. Wijnands¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehA. Boers¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej‘Hhfilenamegrfc5496eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-127383hkeywordsÇcpimx-protocol independent multicast join attributekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÕL´grankingxcrfcd5496irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,The Reverse Path Forwarding (RPF) Vector TLVdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«The Hello message for the Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) has been defined to establish and maintain basic signaling node adjacencies for Label Switching Routers (LSRs) participating in a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) traffic-engineered (TE) network. The Hello message has been extended for use in Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks for state recovery of control channel or nodal faults.

The GMPLS protocol definitions for RSVP also allow a restarting node to learn which label it previously allocated for use on a Label Switched Path (LSP).

Further RSVP protocol extensions have been defined to enable a restarting node to recover full control plane state by exchanging RSVP messages with its upstream and downstream neighbors.

This document provides an informational clarification of the control plane procedures for a GMPLS network when there are multiple node failures, and describes how full control plane state can be recovered in different scenarios where the order in which the nodes restart is different.

This document does not define any new processes or procedures. All protocol mechanisms are already defined in the referenced documents. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamefJ. Gao¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamepA. Satyanarayana¢kaffiliationgFujitsudnamekS. Bardalaiddatej‘Ghfilenamegrfc5495eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127382hkeywordsÇmHello messageegmplskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∫˛ëgrankingwcrfcd5495irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkDescription of the Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic-Engineered (RSVP-TE) Graceful Restart ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the IANA guidelines for allocating new values in the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP).  This document also reserves some numbers for experimentation purposes.  The changes also affect other protocols that employ values from the ARP name spaces. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataroddatej‘Hhfilenamegrfc5494eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127381hkeywordsÉjIANA rulesxAddress Resolution ProtocolcARPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI⁄ñgrankingvcrfcd5494irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDIANA Allocation Guidelines for the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜFrom a carrier perspective, the possibility of turning a permanent connection (PC) into a soft permanent connection (SPC) and vice versa, without actually affecting data plane traffic being carried over it, is a valuable option. In other terms, such operation can be seen as a way of transferring the ownership and control of an existing and in-use data plane connection between the management plane and the control plane, leaving its data plane state untouched.

This memo sets out the requirements for such procedures within a Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) network. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Bramanti¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. Ltd.dnameeD. Li¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamejD. McDysanddatej‘Ghfilenamegrfc5493eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127380hkeywordsÑbpccspcxsoft permanent connectionrdata plane traffickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI÷ü›grankingucrfcd5493irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexïRequirements for the Conversion between Permanent Connections and Switched Connections in a Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@This document defines an Optional Parameter, called Capabilities, that is expected to facilitate the introduction of new capabilities in the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) by providing graceful capability advertisement without requiring that BGP peering be terminated.

This document obsoletes RFC 3392. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationmSonoa SystemsdnamejR. Chandraddatej‘Bhfilenamegrfc5492eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127379hkeywordsÑcbgpcidrwborder gateway protocollcapabilitieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI•‘∆grankingtcrfcd5492irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Capabilities Advertisement with BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8810∂habstracty•The Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO) specification provides a flexible and versatile means to represent location information.  There are, however, circumstances that arise when information needs to be constrained in how it is represented.  In these circumstances, the range of options that need to be implemented are reduced.  There is growing interest in being able to use location information contained in a PIDF-LO for routing applications.  To allow successful interoperability between applications, location information needs to be normative and more tightly constrained than is currently specified in RFC 4119 (PIDF-LO).  This document makes recommendations on how to constrain, represent, and interpret locations in a PIDF-LO.  It further recommends a subset of Geography Markup Language (GML) 3.1.1 that is mandatory to implement by applications involved in location-based routing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrAndrew CorporationdnameoJ. Winterbottom¢kaffiliationrAndrew CorporationdnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej‘Fhfilenamegrfc5491eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127378hkeywordsÜgPIDF-LOecivichgeodetichlocationkwell-formedhGeoShapekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI…Wgrankingscrfcd5491irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{GEOPRIV Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO) Usage Clarification, Considerations, and RecommendationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7459∂habstractxÂThis memo defines an extension to the Sieve mail filtering language (RFC 5228) for accessing mailbox and server annotations, checking for mailbox existence, and controlling mailbox creation on "fileinto" action. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘Fhfilenamegrfc5490eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127377hkeywordsÇnmail filteringhfileintokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI“¢§grankingrcrfcd5490irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjThe Sieve Mail-Filtering Language -- Extensions for Checking Mailbox Status and Accessing Mailbox MetadatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThis document extends RFC 4279, RFC 4492, and RFC 4785 and specifies a set of cipher suites that use a pre-shared key (PSK) to authenticate an Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman exchange with Ephemeral keys (ECDHE).  These cipher suites provide Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuCNRS/LIMOS LaboratorydnamehM. Badra¢kaffiliationjINEOVATIONdnameiI. Hajjehddatej‘Ghfilenamegrfc5489eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsol