tionDateI≠≈grankinglcrfcd5484irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Associating Time-Codes with RTP StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5This document captures experiences in implementing systems based on the ENUM protocol and experiences of ENUM data that have been created by others.  As such, it clarifies the ENUM and Dynamic Delegation Discovery System standards.  Its aim is to help others by reporting both what is "out there" and potential pitfalls in interpreting the set of documents that specify the ENUM protocol.  It does not revise the standards but is intended to provide technical input to future revisions of those documents.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdRMRLdnameiL. Conroy¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamekK. Fujiwaraddatej‘Ehfilenamegrfc5483eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127370hkeywordsÑcDNSeE.164eNAPTRx#dynamic delegation discovery systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI≠ƒÌgrankingkcrfcd5483irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*ENUM Implementation Issues and ExperiencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸The TCP user timeout controls how long transmitted data may remain unacknowledged before a connection is forcefully closed.  It is a local, per-connection parameter.  This document specifies a new TCP option -- the TCP User Timeout Option -- that allows one end of a TCP connection to advertise its current user timeout value.  This information provides advice to the other end of the TCP connection to adapt its user timeout accordingly.  Increasing the user timeouts on both ends of a TCP connection allows it to survive extended periods without end-to-end connectivity.  Decreasing the user timeouts allows busy servers to explicitly notify their clients that they will maintain the connection state only for a short time without connectivity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationgUTN/FRHdnamegF. Gontddatej‘Ehfilenamegrfc5482eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127369hkeywordsÅxTransmission Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIæÁ‰grankingjcrfcd5482irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewTCP User Timeout OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚Packet delay variation metrics appear in many different standards documents. The metric definition in RFC 3393 has considerable flexibility, and it allows multiple formulations of delay variation through the specification of different packet selection functions.

Although flexibility provides wide coverage and room for new ideas, it can make comparisons of independent implementations more difficult. Two different formulations of delay variation have come into wide use in the context of active measurements. This memo examines a range of circumstances for active measurements of delay variation and their uses, and recommends which of the two forms is best matched to particular conditions and tasks. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej‘Ahfilenamegrfc5481eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127368hkeywordsÑractive measurementdipdvcpdvxinter-packet delay variationkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateI≠ƒﬁgrankingicrfcd5481irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Packet Delay Variation Applicability StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis document specifies the syntax and semantics for the Subject Public Key Information field in certificates that support Elliptic Curve Cryptography.  This document updates Sections 2.3.5 and 5, and the ASN.1 module of "Algorithms and Identifiers for the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Profile", RFC 3279. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationhCerticomdnamehD. Brown¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefK. Yiu¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polkddatej‘@hfilenamegrfc5480eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnam