liationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polkddatej‘Ehfilenamegrfc5478eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127365hkeywordsÅx%us defense information systems agencykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∞t≤grankingfcrfcd5478irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWIANA Registration of New Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Resource-Priority NamespacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis memo defines an information model for the Packet SAMPling (PSAMP) protocol.  It is used by the PSAMP protocol for encoding sampled packet data and information related to the Sampling process.  As the PSAMP protocol is based on the IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) protocol, this information model is an extension to the IPFIX information model. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamehT. Dietz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationx University of Erlangen-NurembergdnamekF. Dressler¢kaffiliationxTechnical University of MunichdnamehG. Carleddatej”≥hfilenamegrfc5477eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepsampdfullwpsamp - Packet SamplingdnameoPacket Samplingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127364hkeywordsÉepsampeipfixxip flow information exportkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateI“Ægrankingecrfcd5477irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Information Model for Packet Sampling ExportsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty÷This document specifies the export of packet information from a Packet SAMPling (PSAMP) Exporting Process to a PSAMP Collecting Process.  For export of packet information, the IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) protocol is used, as both the IPFIX and PSAMP architecture match very well, and the means provided by the IPFIX protocol are sufficient.  The document specifies in detail how the IPFIX protocol is used for PSAMP export of packet information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Johnson¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamejJ. Quittekddatej‘'hfilenamegrfc5476eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepsampdfullwpsamp - Packet SamplingdnameoPacket Samplingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127363hkeywordsÑqexporting processrcollecting processeipfixxip flow information exportkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateI“Æ	grankingdcrfcd5476irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Packet Sampling (PSAMP) Protocol SpecificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThis document describes Sampling and Filtering techniques for IP packet selection.  It provides a categorization of schemes and defines what parameters are needed to describe the most common selection schemes.  Furthermore, it shows how techniques can be combined to build more elaborate packet Selectors.  The document provides the basis for the definition of information models for configuring selection techniques in Metering Processes and for reporting the technique in use to a Collector. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamehT. Zseby¢kaffiliationeDANTEdnameiM. Molina¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnamekN. Duffield¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamelS. Niccolini¢kaffiliationhEPSC-UPCdnamejF. Raspallddatej‘&hfilenamegrfc5475eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepsampdfullwpsamp - Packet SamplingdnameoPacket Samplingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127362hkeywordsÇepsamppmetering processkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateI“Ægrankingccrfcd5475irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Sampling and Filtering Techniques for IP Packet SelectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆThis document specifies a framework for the PSAMP (Packet SAMPling) protocol.  The functions of this protocol are to select packets from a stream according to a set of standardized Selectors, to form a stream of reports on the selected packets, and to export the reports to a Collector.  This framework details the components of this architecture, then describes some generic requirements, motivated by the dual aims of ubiquitous deployment and utility of the reports for applications.  Detailed requirements for selection, reporting, and exporting are described, along with configuration requirements of the PSAMP functions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnamekN. Duffield¢kaffiliationsUniversity of TexasdnamehD. Chiou¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelA. Greenberg¢kaffiliationlEPFL & NokiadnameoM. Grossglauser¢kaffiliationtPrinceton UniversitydnamejJ. Rexfordddatej”∆hfilenamegrfc5474eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymepsampdfullwpsamp - Packet SamplingdnameoPacket Samplingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127361hkeywordsÉepsamphselectoricollectorkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateI“≠¯grankingbcrfcd5474irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A Framework for Packet Selection and ReportingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document describes a bandwidth saving method for exporting Flow or packet information using the IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) protocol. As the Packet Sampling (PSAMP) protocol is based on IPFIX, these considerations are valid for PSAMP exports as well.

This method works by separating information common to several Flow Records from information specific to an individual Flow Record. Common Flow information is exported only once in a Data Record defined by an Options Template, while the rest of the specific Flow information is associated with the common information via a unique identifier. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednameiE. Boschi¢kaffiliationoFraunhofer IFAMdnamegL. Mark¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. ClaiseddatejÃKhfilenamegrfc5473eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127360hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI“≠Ógrankingacrfcd5473irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Reducing Redundancy in IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) and Packet Sampling (PSAMP) ReportsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃIn this document, we describe the applicability of the IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) protocol for a variety of applications.  We show how applications can use IPFIX, describe the relevant Information Elements (IEs) for those applications, and present opportunities and limitations of the protocol.  Furthermore, we describe relations of the IPFIX framework to other architectures and frameworks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamehT. Zseby¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednameiE. Boschi¢kaffiliationeCAIDAdnamekN. Brownlee¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej”ıhfilenamegrfc5472eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127359hkeywordsàbiesinformation elementePSAMPkmeasurementnQoS monitoringpattack detectioncAAAoipfix frameworkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI“≠‚granking`crfcd5472irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) ApplicabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document presents a list of tests for implementers of IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) compliant Exporting Processes and Collecting Processes.  This document specifies guidelines for a series of tests that can be run on the IPFIX Exporting Process and Collecting Process in order to probe the conformity and robustness of the IPFIX protocol implementations.  These tests cover all important functions, in order to gain a level of confidence in the IPFIX implementation.  Therefore, they allow the implementer to perform interoperability or plug tests with other IPFIX Exporting Processes and Collecting Processes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamejC. Schmoll¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claiseddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5471eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127358hkeywordsÇqexporting processrcollecting processkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateI“≠”granking_crfcd5471irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Guidelines for IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) TestingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines the IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) architecture for the selective monitoring of IP Flows, and for the export of measured IP Flow information from an IPFIX Device to a Collector.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnRohati SystemsdnamelG. Sadasivan¢kaffiliationx"CAIDA | The University of AucklanddnamekN. Brownlee¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittekddatej”]hfilenamegrfc5470eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127357hkeywordsÉeipfixlipfix deviceoipfix collectorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI“≠ƒgranking^crfcd5470irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Architecture for IP Flow Information ExportdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6183∂habstractyTransport Layer Security (TLS) versions 1.0 (RFC 2246) and 1.1 (RFC 4346) include cipher suites based on DES (Data Encryption Standard) and IDEA (International Data Encryption Algorithm) algorithms.  DES (when used in single-DES mode) and IDEA are no longer recommended for general use in TLS, and have been removed from TLS version 1.2 (RFC 5246).  This document specifies these cipher suites for completeness and discusses reasons why their use is no longer recommended.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronenddatej‘Bhfilenamegrfc5469eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127356hkeywordsÉkciphersuitexdata encryption standardx'international data encryption algorithmkobsoletedByÅd8996epagesopublicationDateIí7granking]crfcd5469irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=DES and IDEA Cipher Suites for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document presents a performance comparison between the per-domain path computation method and the Path Computation Element (PCE) Architecture-based Backward Recursive Path Computation (BRPC) procedure.  Metrics to capture the significant performance aspects are identified, and detailed simulations are carried out on realistic scenarios.  A performance analysis for each of the path computation methods is then undertaken.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqDrexel UniversitydnamekS. Dasgupta¢kaffiliationqDrexel UniversitydnamenJ. de Oliveira¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseurddatej‘Bhfilenamegrfc5468eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127355hkeywordsÑcpcexpath computation elementdbrpcx#backward recursive path computationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateI⁄ñgranking\crfcd5468irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCPerformance Analysis of Inter-Domain Path Computation MethodologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThis document defines a method for the support of GMPLS asymmetric bandwidth bidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  The presented approach is applicable to any switching technology and builds on the original Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) model for the transport of traffic-related parameters.  The procedures described in this document are experimental.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Takacs¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekD. Caviglia¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnameiJ. Meuricddatej”ßhfilenamegrfc5467eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127354hkeywordsÉgRSVP-TEeTSPECfADSPECkobsoletedByÅd6387epagesopublicationDateI∞t£granking[crfcd5467irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDGMPLS Asymmetric Bandwidth Bidirectional Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıThe document defines a way to persistently store named IMAP (RFC 3501) searches on the server.  Such named searches can be subsequently referenced in a SEARCH or any other command that accepts a search criterion as a parameter. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamegC. Kingddatej‘=hfilenamegrfc5466eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127353hkeywordsÅx Internet Message Access ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateIúdÊgrankingZcrfcd5466irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,IMAP4 Extension for Named Searches (Filters)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁThis document defines an IMAP extension that allows a client to request specific kinds of unsolicited notifications for specified mailboxes, such as messages being added to or deleted from such mailboxes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmbHdnamenA. Gulbrandsen¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamegC. King¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej”Phfilenamegrfc5465eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127352hkeywordsÅx Internet Message Access ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIúd”grankingYcrfcd5465irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe IMAP NOTIFY ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈThe METADATA extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol permits clients and servers to maintain "annotations" or "metadata" on IMAP servers.  It is possible to have annotations on a per-mailbox basis or on the server as a whole.  For example, this would allow comments about the purpose of a particular mailbox to be "attached" to that mailbox, or a "message of the day" containing server status information to be made available to anyone logging in to the server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej‘Bhfilenamegrfc5464eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127351hkeywordsÉx internet message access protocoljannotationhmetadatakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIê«sgrankingXcrfcd5464irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe IMAP METADATA ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈This document describes the "ihave" extension to the Sieve email filtering language.  The "ihave" extension provides a means to write scripts that can take advantage of optional Sieve features but can still run when those optional features are not available.  The extension also defines a new error control command intended to be used to report situations where no combination of available extensions satisfies the needs of the script. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehN. Freedddatej‘Dhfilenamegrfc5463eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127350hkeywordsÇdSMTPeESMTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∂≠rgrankingWcrfcd5463irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Sieve Email Filtering:  Ihave ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~The early Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) documents defined the form of the MPLS label stack entry. This includes a three-bit field called the "EXP field". The exact use of this field was not defined by these documents, except to state that it was to be "reserved for experimental use".

Although the intended use of the EXP field was as a "Class of Service" (CoS) field, it was not named a CoS field by these early documents because the use of such a CoS field was not considered to be sufficiently defined. Today a number of standards documents define its usage as a CoS field.

To avoid misunderstanding about how this field may be used, it has become increasingly necessary to rename this field. This document changes the name of the field to the "Traffic Class field" ("TC field"). In doing so, it also updates documents that define the current use of the EXP field. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Asatiddatej‘@hfilenamegrfc5462eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127349hkeywordsÑcexppclass of serviceccoshtc fieldkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateI•‘¥grankingVcrfcd5462irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Stack Entry: "EXP" Field Renamed to "Traffic Class" FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a non-standard, but widely implemented, modification to TCP's handling of ICMP soft error messages that rejects pending connection-requests when those error messages are received.  This behavior reduces the likelihood of long delays between connection-establishment attempts that may arise in a number of scenarios, including one in which dual-stack nodes that have IPv6 enabled by default are deployed in IPv4 or mixed IPv4 and IPv6 environments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUTN/FRHdnamegF. Gontddatej‘Dhfilenamegrfc5461eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127348hkeywordsÇdicmpx!Internet Control Message ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIú§\grankingUcrfcd5461irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTCP's Reaction to Soft ErrorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) has been extended with a Leasequery capability that allows a client to request information about DHCPv6 bindings.  That mechanism is limited to queries for individual bindings.  In some situations individual binding queries may not be efficient, or even possible.  This document expands on the Leasequery protocol, adding new query types and allowing for bulk transfer of DHCPv6 binding data via TCP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stappddatej‘Ahfilenamegrfc5460eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127347hkeywordsÉx"dynamic hos configuration protocoldipv6odhcpv6 bindingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI•‘ngrankingTcrfcd5460irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevDHCPv6 Bulk LeasequerydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7653∂habstractyzThis document updates the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format to be used for the International Telecommunication Union (ITU-T) Recommendation G.729.1 audio codec.  It adds Discontinuous Transmission (DTX) support to the RFC 4749 specification, in a backward-compatible way.  An updated media type registration is included for this payload format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamejA. Sollaudddatej”Khfilenamegrfc5459eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127346hkeywordsÜxreal-time transport protocolcrtpeitu-tx%international telecommunication uniongg.729.1kaudio codeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIz] grankingScrfcd5459irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKG.729.1 RTP Payload Format Update: Discontinuous Transmission (DTX) SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.The MPEG-2 standard defined by ISO 13818-1 supports a range of transmission methods for a variety of services. This document provides a threat analysis and derives the security requirements when using the Transport Stream, TS, to support an Internet network-layer using Unidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE) defined in RFC 4326. The document also provides the motivation for link-layer security for a ULE Stream. A ULE Stream may be used to send IPv4 packets, IPv6 packets, and other Protocol Data Units (PDUs) to an arbitrarily large number of Receivers supporting unicast and/or multicast transmission.

The analysis also describes applicability to the Generic Stream Encapsulation (GSE) defined by the Digital Video Broadcasting (DVB) Project. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of SurreydnamenH. Cruickshank¢kaffiliationvUniversity of BradforddnameiP. Pillai¢kaffiliationvUniversity of SalzburgdnamemM. Noisternig¢kaffiliationfLogicadnamejS. Iyengarddatej‘Chfilenamegrfc5458eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipdvbdfullsipdvb - IP over DVBdnamekIP over DVBdtypebwgbidjdoc-127345hkeywordsÜkiso 13818-1ptransport streambtsjule streamcgsexgeneric stream encapsulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI…r.grankingRcrfcd5458irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUSecurity Requirements for the Unidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty:This document establishes the IANA registries for IAX, the Inter- Asterisk eXchange protocol, an application-layer control and media protocol for creating, modifying, and terminating multimedia sessions over Internet Protocol (IP) networks.  IAX was developed by the open source community for the Asterisk PBX and is targeted primarily at Voice over Internet Protocol (VoIP) call control, but it can be used with streaming video or any other type of multimedia.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhTruPhonednamefE. Guyddatej‘1hfilenamegrfc5457eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-127344hkeywordsÑx asterisk private branch exchangecpbxdvoipxvoice over internet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKàñÏgrankingQcrfcd5457irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex>IANA Considerations for IAX: Inter-Asterisk eXchange Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyëThis document describes IAX, the Inter-Asterisk eXchange protocol, an application-layer control and media protocol for creating, modifying, and terminating multimedia sessions over Internet Protocol (IP) networks. IAX was developed by the open source community for the Asterisk Private Branch Exchange (PBX) and is targeted primarily at Voice over Internet Protocol (VoIP) call control, but it can be used with streaming video or any other type of multimedia.

IAX is an "all in one" protocol for handling multimedia in IP networks. It combines both control and media services in the same protocol. In addition, IAX uses a single UDP data stream on a static port greatly simplifying Network Address Translation (NAT) gateway traversal, eliminating the need for other protocols to work around NAT, and simplifying network and firewall management. IAX employs a compact encoding that decreases bandwidth usage and is well suited for Internet telephony service. In addition, its open nature permits new payload type additions needed to support additional services. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamevCullen Fluffy JenningsgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlDigium, Inc.dnamejM. Spencer¢kaffiliationvSaint Joseph's CollegednamejB. Capouch¢kaffiliationhTruphonednamefE. Guy¢kaffiliationtCornfed Systems, LLCdnameiF. Miller¢kaffiliation`dnamejK. Shumardddatej—\hfilenamegrfc5456eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127343hkeywordsÑx asterisk private branch exchangecpbxdvoipxvoice over internet protocolkobsoletedByÄepageseopublicationDateKàñ„grankingPcrfcd5456irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&IAX: Inter-Asterisk eXchange Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractx»This document specifies a CLASSTYPE object to support Diffserv-Aware Traffic Engineering (DS-TE) where path computation is performed with the aid of a Path Computation Element (PCE). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Parker¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejS. Boutros¢kaffiliationxKDDI R&D Laboratories, Inc.dnameiK. Kumakiddatej‘Chfilenamegrfc5455eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127342hkeywordsÑiclasstypeeds-tex"diffserv-aware traffic engineeringcpcekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateIÕLãgrankingOcrfcd5455irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXDiffserv-Aware Class-Type Object for the Path Computation Element Communication ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis specification provides IPv6 extensions to the Mobile IPv4 protocol.  The extensions allow a dual-stack node to use IPv4 and IPv6 home addresses as well as to move between IPv4 and dual stack network infrastructures. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamegV. Park¢kaffiliationtElevate TechnologiesdnamejH. Solimanddatej‘Chfilenamegrfc5454eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127341hkeywordsÇdipv6emipv4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI±µ°grankingNcrfcd5454irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevDual-Stack Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏInterface identifiers in IPv6 unicast addresses are used to identify interfaces on a link.  They are required to be unique within a subnet.  Several RFCs have specified interface identifiers or identifier ranges that have a special meaning attached to them.  An IPv6 node autoconfiguring an interface identifier in these ranges will encounter unexpected consequences.  Since there is no centralized repository for such reserved identifiers, this document aims to create one. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnanddatej‘"hfilenamegrfc5453eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127340hkeywordsÅounicast addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIí7grankingMcrfcd5453irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Reserved IPv6 Interface IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThe current Internet climate poses serious threats to the Domain Name System. In the interim period before the DNS protocol can be secured more fully, measures can already be taken to harden the DNS to make 'spoofing' a recursing nameserver many orders of magnitude harder.

Even a cryptographically secured DNS benefits from having the ability to discard bogus responses quickly, as this potentially saves large amounts of computation.

By describing certain behavior that has previously not been standardized, this document sets out how to make the DNS more resilient against accepting incorrect responses. This document updates RFC 2181. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx"Netherlabs Computer Consulting BV.dnameiA. Hubert¢kaffiliationgEquinixdnamekR. van Mookddatej‘2hfilenamegrfc5452eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127339hkeywordsÉhspoofingksource portihardeningkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÄˇ grankingLcrfcd5452irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Measures for Making DNS More Resilient against Forged AnswersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis memo defines a new header field for use with electronic mail messages to indicate the results of message authentication efforts.  Any receiver-side software, such as mail filters or Mail User Agents (MUAs), may use this message header field to relay that information in a convenient way to users or to make sorting and filtering decisions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamelM. Kucherawyddatej“¶hfilenamegrfc5451eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127338hkeywordsÇvauthentication-resultsxemail authentication resultkobsoletedByÅd7001epages+opublicationDateIÁ∂grankingKcrfcd5451irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAMessage Header Field for Indicating Message Authentication StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6577∂habstractyYThis document describes a method to inform Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) clients when RTP packets are transmitted at a time other than their 'nominal' transmission time.  It also provides a mechanism to provide improved inter-arrival jitter reports from the clients, that take into account the reported transmission times. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsApple Computer Inc.dnameiD. Singer¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekH. Desineniddatej‘5hfilenamegrfc5450eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127337hkeywordsÉsreal-time transportbIJtinter-arrival jitterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∫˛}grankingJcrfcd5450irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Transmission Time Offsets in RTP StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies an OSPFv3 interface type tailored for mobile ad hoc networks.  This interface type is derived from the broadcast interface type, and is denoted the "OSPFv3 MANET interface type".  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamekE. Baccelli¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamejP. Jacquet¢kaffiliationcCRCdnameiD. Nguyen¢kaffiliationwLIX Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausenddatej‘Dhfilenamegrfc5449eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127336hkeywordsÉxopen shortest path firstninterface typemmobile ad hockobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIê«agrankingIcrfcd5449irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9OSPF Multipoint Relay (MPR) Extension for Ad Hoc NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√This specification defines a new EAP method, EAP-AKA', which is a small revision of the EAP-AKA (Extensible Authentication Protocol Method for 3rd Generation Authentication and Key Agreement) method. The change is a new key derivation function that binds the keys derived within the method to the name of the access network. The new key derivation mechanism has been defined in the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP). This specification allows its use in EAP in an interoperable manner. In addition, EAP-AKA' employs SHA-256 instead of SHA-1.

This specification also updates RFC 4187, EAP-AKA, to prevent bidding down attacks from EAP-AKA'. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemV. Lehtovirta¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronenddatej‘;hfilenamegrfc5448eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127335hkeywordsÑcEAPcAKAdAKA'd3GPPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ ÃgrankingHcrfcd5448irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqImproved Extensible Authentication Protocol Method for 3rd Generation Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA')dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9048d9678∂habstractyàA Mobile IPv6 node requires a home agent address, a home address, and a security association with its home agent before it can start utilizing Mobile IPv6.  RFC 3775 requires that some or all of these parameters be statically configured.  Mobile IPv6 bootstrapping work aims to make this information dynamically available to the mobile node.  An important aspect of the Mobile IPv6 bootstrapping solution is to support interworking with existing Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) infrastructures.  This document describes MIPv6 bootstrapping using the Diameter Network Access Server to home AAA server interface. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationkOrange LabsdnamelJ. Bournelle¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationhWiChorusdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnamelK. Chowdhuryddatej‘Bhfilenamegrfc5447eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddimedfullx*dime - Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdnamex#Diameter Maintenance and Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127334hkeywordsÉhDiameterkMobile IPv6sIntegrated ScenariokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIúd¬grankingGcrfcd5447irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVDiameter Mobile IPv6: Support for Network Access Server to Diameter Server InteractiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆In some Mobile IPv4 deployments, identifying the mobile node or the mobility service subscriber is not enough to distinguish among the multiple services possibly provisioned to the mobile node.  The capability to specify different services in addition to the mobile node's identity can be leveraged to provide flexibility for mobility service providers to provide multiple services within a single mobility service subscription.  This document describes a Service Selection extension for Mobile IPv4 that is intended to assist home agents to make specific service selections for their mobility service subscriptions during the registration procedure.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamejU. Nilssonddatej‘Ahfilenamegrfc5446eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127333hkeywordsÉxinternet protocol version 4ohost name agentxmobility service subscriptionkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateIê«NgrankingFcrfcd5446irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Service Selection for Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document provides Forward Error Correction (FEC) Scheme specifications according to the Reliable Multicast Transport (RMT) FEC building block for the Compact No-Code FEC Scheme, the Small Block, Large Block, and Expandable FEC Scheme, the Small Block Systematic FEC Scheme, and the Compact FEC Scheme.  This document obsoletes RFC 3695 and assumes responsibility for the FEC Schemes defined in RFC 3452. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnameiM. Watsonddatej‘)hfilenamegrfc5445eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127332hkeywordsÖgcontentfstreamhdeliveryimulticastqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI≠ƒ“grankingEcrfcd5445irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Basic Forward Error Correction (FEC) SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxúThis document specifies a packet format capable of carrying multiple messages that may be used by mobile ad hoc network routing protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwLIX Ecole PolytechniquednamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationoBAE Systems ATCdnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnamegJ. Dean¢kaffiliationrINRIA RocquencourtdnamehC. Adjihddatej‘2hfilenamegrfc5444eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127331hkeywordsÉgroutingcTLVgaddresskobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateIä&CgrankingDcrfcd5444irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Generalized Mobile Ad Hoc Network (MANET) Packet/Message FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8245d7631∂habstracty|In certain networks, there is dependency on the edge-to-edge Label Switched Paths (LSPs) setup by the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP), e.g., networks that are used for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Virtual Private Network (VPN) applications.  For such applications, it is not possible to rely on Internet Protocol (IP) forwarding if the MPLS LSP is not operating appropriately.  Blackholing of labeled traffic can occur in situations where the Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) is operational on a link on which LDP is not.  While the link could still be used for IP forwarding, it is not useful for MPLS forwarding, for example, MPLS VPN applications or Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) route-free cores.  This document describes a mechanism to avoid traffic loss due to this condition without introducing any protocol changes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgGENBANDdnamegM. Jork¢kaffiliationoBritish TelecomdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegL. Fangddatej‘Ahfilenamegrfc5443eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127330hkeywordsÇxlabel distribution protocolxinterior gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI≠ƒØgrankingCcrfcd5443irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewLDP IGP SynchronizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6138∂habstracty˚This document specifies the architecture for mobile email, as described by the Open Mobile Alliance (OMA), using Internet Mail protocols.  This architecture was an important consideration for much of the work of the LEMONADE (Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environments) working group in the IETF.  This document also describes how the LEMONADE architecture meets OMA's requirements for their Mobile Email (MEM) service.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsonsddatej‘>hfilenamegrfc5442eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127329hkeywordsÇxGenhancements to internet email to supportt diverse service environmentsePhonekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∂≠bgrankingBcrfcd5442irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdLEMONADE Architecture - Supporting Open Mobile Alliance (OMA) Mobile Email (MEM) Using Internet MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThe ability to compute shortest constrained Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths (TE LSPs) in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks across multiple domains has been identified as a key requirement.  In this context, a domain is a collection of network elements within a common sphere of address management or path computational responsibility such as an IGP area or an Autonomous Systems.  This document specifies a procedure relying on the use of multiple Path Computation Elements (PCEs) to compute such inter-domain shortest constrained paths across a predetermined sequence of domains, using a backward-recursive path computation technique.  This technique preserves confidentiality across domains, which is sometimes required when domains are managed by different service providers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationjBT InfonetdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Rouxddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5441eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127328hkeywordsÇfte lspxpath computation elementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI⁄ñgrankingAcrfcd5441irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexëA Backward-Recursive PCE-Based Computation (BRPC) Procedure to Compute Shortest Constrained Inter-Domain Traffic Engineering Label Switched PathsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document specifies the Path Computation Element (PCE) Communication Protocol (PCEP) for communications between a Path Computation Client (PCC) and a PCE, or between two PCEs.  Such interactions include path computation requests and path computation replies as well as notifications of specific states related to the use of a PCE in the context of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering.  PCEP is designed to be flexible and extensible so as to easily allow for the addition of further messages and objects, should further requirements be expressed in the future. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Rouxddatej‘<hfilenamegrfc5440eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127327hkeywordsÑdMPLSeGMPLSsTraffic EngineeringsLabel Switched PathkobsoletedByÄepagesWopublicationDateI±µügranking@crfcd5440irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Path Computation Element (PCE) Communication Protocol (PCEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÖd9756d9488d7896d8253d8356∂habstractyéTraffic engineered Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS-TE) is deployed in providers' core networks. As providers plan to grow these networks, they need to understand whether existing protocols and implementations can support the network sizes that they are planning.

This document presents an analysis of some of the scaling concerns for the number of Label Switching Paths (LSPs) in MPLS-TE core networks, and examines the value of two techniques (LSP hierarchies and multipoint-to-point LSPs) for improving scaling. The intention is to motivate the development of appropriate deployment techniques and protocol extensions to enable the application of MPLS-TE in large networks.

This document only considers the question of achieving scalability for the support of point-to-point MPLS-TE LSPs. Point-to-multipoint MPLS-TE LSPs are for future study. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Yasukawa¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekO. Komolafeddatej‘=hfilenamegrfc5439eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127326hkeywordsÜxmultiprotocol label switchingrtraffic engineeredpscaling concernsclspqlabel switch pathxpoint-to-point mpls-te lspskobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateIê«>granking?crfcd5439irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6An Analysis of Scaling Issues in MPLS-TE Core NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVInstant Messaging (IM) refers to the transfer of messages between users in real-time. This document provides a mechanism whereby endpoints can request Instant Message Disposition Notifications (IMDN), including delivery, processing, and display notifications, for page-mode instant messages.

The Common Presence and Instant Messaging (CPIM) data format specified in RFC 3862 is extended with new header fields that enable endpoints to request IMDNs. A new message format is also defined to convey IMDNs.

This document also describes how SIP entities behave using this extension. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationrEricsson AustraliadnamelH. Khartabilddatej‘"hfilenamegrfc5438eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127325hkeywordsÑbimqinstant messagingdcpimx%common presence and instant messagingkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateIúdØgranking>crfcd5438irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Instant Message Disposition Notification (IMDN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx—This document describes a profile of the Sieve extension for notifications, to allow notifications to be sent over the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP), also known as Jabber. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenP. Saint-Andre¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5437eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127324hkeywordsÅfjabberkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÖïgranking=crfcd5437irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSieve Notification Mechanism: Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxìThis document describes a profile of the Sieve extension for notifications, to allow notifications to be sent by electronic mail. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson Research CenterdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationofreenet.de GmbHdnameiM. Haardtddatej‘?hfilenamegrfc5436eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127323hkeywordsÅxeletctronic mail notificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÖãgranking<crfcd5436irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Sieve Notification Mechanism: mailtodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘Users go to great lengths to be notified as quickly as possible that they have received new mail.  Most of these methods involve polling to check for new messages periodically.  A push method handled by the final delivery agent gives users quicker notifications and saves server resources.  This document does not specify the notification method, but it is expected that using existing instant messaging infrastructure such as Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP), or Global System for Mobile Communications (GSM) Short Message Service (SMS) messages will be popular.  This document describes an extension to the Sieve mail filtering language that allows users to give specific rules for how and when notifications should be sent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson Research CenterdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson Research CenterdnamelW. Segmuller¢kaffiliationqEndless CrossworddnameiT. MartinddatejÃxhfilenamegrfc5435eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127322hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÖ}granking;crfcd5435irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Sieve Email Filtering: Extension for NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8580∂habstracty,This document discusses tactics and strategy for hosting a successful IETF Birds-of-a-Feather (BOF) session, especially one oriented at the formation of an IETF Working Group.  It is based on the experiences of having participated in numerous BOFs, both successful and unsuccessful. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Nartenddatej‘=hfilenamegrfc5434eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127321hkeywordsÇhietf bofmworking groupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIáëÀgranking:crfcd5434irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGConsiderations for Having a Successful Birds-of-a-Feather (BOF) SessiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This memo defines an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) method called EAP Generalized Pre-Shared Key (EAP-GPSK).  This method is a lightweight shared-key authentication protocol supporting mutual authentication and key derivation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcLTSdnameiT. Clancy¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”¡hfilenamegrfc5433eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-127320hkeywordsÉcEAPhEAP-GPSKnpre-shared keykobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateI•‘Ygranking9crfcd5433irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQExtensible Authentication Protocol - Generalized Pre-Shared Key (EAP-GPSK) MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<The offer/answer model for the Session Description Protocol (SDP) assumes that endpoints somehow establish the Quality of Service (QoS) required for the media streams they establish.  Endpoints in closed environments typically agree out-of-band (e.g., using configuration information) regarding which QoS mechanism to use.  However, on the Internet, there is more than one QoS service available.  Consequently, there is a need for a mechanism to negotiate which QoS mechanism to use for a particular media stream.  This document defines such a mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. Dhesikan¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”‰hfilenamegrfc5432eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127319hkeywordsÇloffer/answerlmedia streamkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateI≠ƒúgranking8crfcd5432irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVQuality of Service (QoS) Mechanism Selection in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆThis document describes the need for a new pair of IANA Diameter Command Codes to be used in a vendor-specific new application, namely for the ITU-T Rec.  Q.3303.3 - Rw interface used to send a request/ response for authorizing network Quality of Service (QoS) resources and policy enforcement in a network element, as one of the recommendations of the International Telecommunication Union - Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamefD. Sunddatej‘=hfilenamegrfc5431eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127318hkeywordsáudiameter command codeeitu-thITU-T RwvPolicy-Install-RequestcpiruPolicy-Install-AnswercpiakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI¿6granking7crfcd5431irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Diameter ITU-T Rw Policy Enforcement Interface ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛The United States government has published guidelines for "NSA Suite B Cryptography", which defines cryptographic algorithm policy for national security applications.  This document defines a profile of Transport Layer Security (TLS) version 1.2 that is fully conformant with Suite B.  This document also defines a transitional profile for use with TLS version 1.0 and TLS version 1.1 which employs Suite B algorithms to the greatest extent possible.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNational Security AgencydnameiM. Salter¢kaffiliationqNetwork ResonancednamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej‘<hfilenamegrfc5430eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127317hkeywordsÅxnsa suite b cryptographykobsoletedByÅd6460epagesopublicationDateI¬ëßgranking6crfcd5430irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Suite B Profile for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠This memo updates the definition of the Sieve mail filtering language "reject" extension, originally defined in RFC 3028.

A "Joe-job" is a spam run forged to appear as though it came from an innocent party, who is then generally flooded by automated bounces, Message Disposition Notifications (MDNs), and personal messages with complaints. The original Sieve "reject" action defined in RFC 3028 required use of MDNs for rejecting messages, thus contributing to the flood of Joe-job spam to victims of Joe-jobs.

This memo updates the definition of the "reject" action to allow messages to be refused during the SMTP transaction, and defines the "ereject" action to require messages to be refused during the SMTP transaction, if possible.

The "ereject" action is intended to replace the "reject" action wherever possible. The "ereject" action is similar to "reject", but will always favor protocol-level message rejection. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkSerendipitydnamehA. Stoneddatej‘;hfilenamegrfc5429eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127316hkeywordsàesievefrefusefrejectgerejectgjoe-jobdsmtpdlmtpdspamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIæÁ—granking5crfcd5429irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Sieve Email Filtering: Reject and Extended Reject ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines a basic set of managed objects for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)-based management of events that can be generated by PacketCable- and IPCablecom-compliant Multimedia Terminal Adapter devices. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamepS. Channabasappa¢kaffiliationhtComLabsdnameoW. De Ketelaere¢kaffiliationnBroadcom Corp.dnamelE. Nechamkinddatej‘-hfilenamegrfc5428eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-127315hkeywordsÑdsnmpx"simple network management protocolxmultimedia terminal adaptersPKTC-IETF-EVENT-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateI”˙‚granking4crfcd5428irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdManagement Event Management Information Base (MIB) for PacketCable- and IPCablecom-Compliant DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractyThis MIB module defines textual conventions to represent Facility and Severity information commonly used in syslog messages.  The intent is that these textual conventions will be imported and used in MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtCyber Solutions Inc.dnamehG. Keeniddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5427eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsyslogdfullx1syslog - Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdnamex(Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127314hkeywordsÑosyslog facilityosyslog severitycMIBrtextual-conventionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI“¢égranking3crfcd5427irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Textual Conventions for Syslog ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document describes the transport for syslog messages over UDP/ IPv4 or UDP/IPv6.  The syslog protocol layered architecture provides for support of any number of transport mappings.  However, for interoperability purposes, syslog protocol implementers are required to support this transport mapping. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelA. Okmianskiddatej”˙hfilenamegrfc5426eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsyslogdfullx1syslog - Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdnamex(Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127313hkeywordsÇcudpvUser Datagram ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateI∂≠Qgranking2crfcd5426irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Transmission of Syslog Messages over UDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document describes the use of Transport Layer Security (TLS) to provide a secure connection for the transport of syslog messages.  This document describes the security threats to syslog and how TLS can be used to counter such threats.fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnamegF. Miao¢kaffiliationsHuawei TechnologiesdnameeY. Ma¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejJ. Saloweyddatej‘3hfilenamegrfc5425eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsyslogdfullx1syslog - Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdnamex(Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127312hkeywordsÇnsyslog messageosyslog securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∂≠?granking1crfcd5425irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Transport Layer Security (TLS) Transport Mapping for SyslogdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9662∂habstracty2This document describes the syslog protocol, which is used to convey event notification messages. This protocol utilizes a layered architecture, which allows the use of any number of transport protocols for transmission of syslog messages. It also provides a message format that allows vendor-specific extensions to be provided in a structured way.

This document has been written with the original design goals for traditional syslog in mind. The need for a new layered specification has arisen because standardization efforts for reliable and secure syslog extensions suffer from the lack of a Standards-Track and transport-independent RFC. Without this document, each other standard needs to define its own syslog packet format and transport mechanism, which over time will introduce subtle compatibility issues. This document tries to provide a foundation that syslog extensions can build on. This layered architecture approach also provides a solid basis that allows code to be written once for each syslog feature rather than once for each transport. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlAdiscon GmbHdnamekR. Gerhardsddatej”˙hfilenamegrfc5424eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsyslogdfullx1syslog - Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdnamex(Security Issues in Network Event Loggingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127311hkeywordsáxevent notification messagensyslog messagehberkeleyhsoftwareldistributionltransmissionhmessageskobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateI∂≠/granking0crfcd5424irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesThe Syslog ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIOne of the missing features in the existing Internet mail and messaging standards is a facility for server-to-server and server-to- client event notifications related to message store events. As the scope of Internet mail expands to support more diverse media (such as voice mail) and devices (such as cell phones) and to provide rich interactions with other services (such as web portals and legal compliance systems), the need for an interoperable notification system increases. This document attempts to enumerate the types of events that interest real-world consumers of such a system.

This document describes events and event parameters that are useful for several cases, including notification to administrative systems and end users. This is not intended as a replacement for a message access facility such as IMAP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuQUALCOMM IncorporateddnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newmanddatej‘:hfilenamegrfc5423eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127310hkeywordsÅdimapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÕLzgranking/crfcd5423irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInternet Message Store EventsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡The Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP-FAST) method enables secure communication between a peer and a server by using Transport Layer Security (TLS) to establish a mutually authenticated tunnel.  EAP- FAST also enables the provisioning credentials or other information through this protected tunnel.  This document describes the use of EAP-FAST for dynamic provisioning.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegH. ZhouddatejÃ±hfilenamegrfc5422eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127309hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateIπpMgranking.crfcd5422irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuDynamic Provisioning Using Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP-FAST)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyºThe Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP-FAST) method enables secure communication between a peer and a server by using Transport Layer Security (TLS) to establish a mutually authenticated tunnel.  Within this tunnel, a basic password exchange, based on the Generic Token Card method (EAP-GTC), may be executed to authenticate the peer.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegH. Zhouddatej‘;hfilenamegrfc5421eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127308hkeywordsÇrgeneric token cardgeap-gtckobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateIπp=granking-crfcd5421irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Basic Password Exchange within the Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP-FAST)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) may be established using the Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) extensions. This protocol includes an object (the SESSION_ATTRIBUTE object) that carries a Flags field used to indicate options and attributes of the LSP. That Flags field has eight bits, allowing for eight options to be set. Recent proposals in many documents that extend RSVP-TE have suggested uses for each of the previously unused bits.

This document defines a new object for RSVP-TE messages that allows the signaling of further attribute bits and also the carriage of arbitrary attribute parameters to make RSVP-TE easily extensible to support new requirements. Additionally, this document defines a way to record the attributes applied to the LSP on a hop-by-hop basis.

The object mechanisms defined in this document are equally applicable to Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Packet Switch Capable (PSC) LSPs and to GMPLS non-PSC LSPs.

This document replaces and obsoletes the previous version of this work, published as RFC 4420. The only change is in the encoding of the Type-Length-Variable (TLV) data structures. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekA. Ayyangarddatej‘9hfilenamegrfc5420eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127307hkeywordsÉxmultiprotocol label switchingtlabel switched pathsqSESSION_ATTRIBUTEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIä&+granking,crfcd5420irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsEncoding of Attributes for MPLS LSP Establishment Using Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6510∂habstractyOMobile IPv6 defines a set of signaling messages that enable the mobile node (MN) to authenticate and perform registration with its home agent (HA).  These authentication signaling messages between the mobile node and home agent are secured by an IPsec security association (SA) that is established between the MN and HA.  The MIP6 working group has specified a mechanism to secure the Binding Update (BU) and Binding Acknowledgement (BAck) messages using an authentication option, similar to the authentication option in Mobile IPv4, carried within the signaling messages that are exchanged between the MN and HA to establish a binding.  This document provides the justifications as to why the authentication option mechanism is needed for Mobile IPv6 deployment in certain environments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejG. Dommetyddatej‘8hfilenamegrfc5419eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127306hkeywordsÉx authentication signaling messagebmnbhakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÇG·granking+crfcd5419irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGWhy the Authentication Data Suboption is Needed for Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEarly Wireless Local Area Network (WLAN) deployments feature a "fat" Access Point (AP), which serves as a \%stand-alone interface between the wired and wireless network segments.  However, this model raises scaling, mobility, and manageability issues, and the Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) protocol is meant to address these issues.  CAPWAP effectively splits the fat AP functionality into two network elements, and the communication channel between these components may traverse potentially hostile hops.  This document analyzes the security exposure resulting from the introduction of CAPWAP and summarizes the associated security considerations for IEEE 802.11-based CAPWAP implementations and deployments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamehS. Kelly¢kaffiliationcLTSdnameiT. Clancyddatej‘0hfilenamegrfc5418eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127305hkeywordsÇdWLANhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateI∞tëgranking*crfcd5418irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgControl And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Threat Analysis for IEEE 802.11 DeploymentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points Protocol allows a Wireless Termination Point to use DHCP to discover the Access Controllers to which it is to connect.  This document describes the DHCP options to be used by the CAPWAP Protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejP. Calhounddatej‘6hfilenamegrfc5417eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127304hkeywordsàfCAPWAPf802.11dIEEElWireless LANdWiFilAccess PointqAccess ControllerxWireless Termination PointkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∞tÅgranking)crfcd5417irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYControl And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Access Controller DHCP OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypWireless LAN product architectures have evolved from single autonomous access points to systems consisting of a centralized Access Controller (AC) and Wireless Termination Points (WTPs). The general goal of centralized control architectures is to move access control, including user authentication and authorization, mobility management, and radio management from the single access point to a centralized controller.

This specification defines the Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Protocol Binding Specification for use with the IEEE 802.11 Wireless Local Area Network protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejP. Calhoun¢kaffiliationrResearch In MotiondnamemM. Montemurro¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Stanleyddatej‘7hfilenamegrfc5416eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127303hkeywordsäxOperations and ManagementeLWAPPfCAPWAPf802.11dIEEElWireless LANdWiFilAccess PointqAccess ControllerxWireless Termination PointkobsoletedByÄepagesLopublicationDateI∞trgranking(crfcd5416irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Protocol Binding for IEEE 802.11dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis specification defines the Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Protocol, meeting the objectives defined by the CAPWAP Working Group in RFC 4564.  The CAPWAP protocol is designed to be flexible, allowing it to be used for a variety of wireless technologies.  This document describes the base CAPWAP protocol, while separate binding extensions will enable its use with additional wireless technologies. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejP. Calhoun¢kaffiliationrResearch In MotiondnamemM. Montemurro¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Stanleyddatej‘:hfilenamegrfc5415eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127302hkeywordsâeLWAPPfCAPWAPf802.11dIEEElWireless LANdWiFilAccess PointqAccess ControllerxWireless Termination PointkobsoletedByÄepagesõopublicationDateI∞tbgranking'crfcd5415irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRControl And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8996d8553µhabstracty®The popularity of wireless local area networks (WLANs) has led to widespread deployments across different establishments. It has also translated into an increasing scale of the WLANs. Large-scale deployments made of large numbers of wireless termination points (WTPs) and covering substantial areas are increasingly common.

The Wireless LAN Control Protocol (WiCoP) described in this document allows for the control and provisioning of large-scale WLANs. It enables central management of these networks and realizes the objectives set forth for the Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP). This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamegS. Iino¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamekS. Govindan¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamejM. Sugiura¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamehH. Chengddatej”ühfilenamegrfc5414eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127301hkeywordsÜdwlanxwireless local area networkctwpxwireless termination pointsfcapwapx2control and provisioning of wireless access pointskobsoletedByÅd5415epages6opublicationDateKj'granking&crfcd5414irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex%Wireless LAN Control Protocol (WiCoP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyËThe Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) problem statement describes a problem that needs to be addressed before a wireless LAN (WLAN) network designer can construct a solution composed of Wireless Termination Points (WTP) and Access Controllers (AC) from multiple, different vendors. One of the primary goals is to find a solution that solves the interoperability between the two classes of devices (WTPs and ACs) that then enables an AC from one vendor to control and manage a WTP from another.

In this document, we present a protocol that forms the common technology-independent framework and the ability to negotiate and add, on top of this framework, a control protocol that contains a technology-dependent component to arrive at a complete solution. We have also presented two such control protocols -- an 802.11 Control protocol, and another, more generic image download protocol, in this document.

Even though the text in this document is written to specifically address the problem stated in RFC 3990, the solution can be applied to any problem that has a controller (equivalent to the AC) managing one or more network elements (equivalent to the WTP). This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamemP. Narasimhan¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Harkins¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamemS. Ponnuswamyddatej”¿hfilenamegrfc5413eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127300hkeywordsÅfcapwapkobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDateKÉ3xgranking%crfcd5413irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex)SLAPP: Secure Light Access Point ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyôIn recent years, there has been a shift in wireless LAN (WLAN) product architectures from autonomous access points to centralized control of lightweight access points. The general goal has been to move most of the traditional wireless functionality such as access control (user authentication and authorization), mobility, and radio management out of the access point into a centralized controller.

The IETF's CAPWAP (Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points) WG has identified that a standards-based protocol is necessary between a wireless Access Controller and Wireless Termination Points (the latter are also commonly referred to as Lightweight Access Points). This specification defines the Lightweight Access Point Protocol (LWAPP), which addresses the CAPWAP's (Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points) protocol requirements. Although the LWAPP protocol is designed to be flexible enough to be used for a variety of wireless technologies, this specific document describes the base protocol and an extension that allows it to be used with the IEEE's 802.11 wireless LAN protocol. This document defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanugauthorsá¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejP. Calhoun¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegR. Suri¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationuGWhiz Arts & SciencesdnamekM. Williams¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. O'Hara¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Kellyddatej”Íhfilenamegrfc5412eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127299hkeywordsÇelwappfcapwapkobsoletedByÄepages}opublicationDateKÉ3lgranking$crfcd5412irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex!Lightweight Access Point ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is the subject of numerous specifications that have been produced by the IETF.  It can be difficult to locate the right document, or even to determine the set of Request for Comments (RFC) about SIP.  This specification serves as a guide to the SIP RFC series.  It lists a current snapshot of the specifications under the SIP umbrella, briefly summarizes each, and groups them into categories.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘8hfilenamegrfc5411eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127298hkeywordsÉb42kdon't paniclsip overviewkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateIä%Ògranking#crfcd5411irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=A Hitchhiker's Guide to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document specifies a new Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) General Extension payload to transport the short-term key message (STKM) and long-term key message (LTKM) payloads as well as the management data LTKM reporting message and parental control message payloads defined in the Open Mobile Alliance's (OMA) Broadcast (BCAST) group's Service and Content protection specification.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekA. Jerichow¢kaffiliationpNagravision S.A.dnamehL. Pironddatej‘"hfilenamegrfc5410eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127297hkeywordsÉoMIKEY ExtensionqIANA registrationiOMA BCASTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIo√¶granking"crfcd5410irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) General Extension Payload for Open Mobile Alliance BCAST 1.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6309∂habstractykThis document describes the conventions for using the Boneh-Franklin (BF) and Boneh-Boyen (BB1) identity-based encryption algorithms in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) to encrypt content-encryption keys.  Object identifiers and the convention for encoding a recipient's identity are also defined.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpVoltage SecuritydnameiL. Martin¢kaffiliationeAxwaydnamelM. Schertlerddatej‘:hfilenamegrfc5409eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127296hkeywordsÉbbfcbbqwcontent-encryption keyskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIz\)granking!crfcd5409irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyUsing the Boneh-Franklin and Boneh-Boyen Identity-Based Encryption Algorithms with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=This document describes the security architecture required to implement identity-based encryption, a public-key encryption technology that uses a user's identity as a public key.  It also defines data structures that can be used to implement the technology.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamenG. Appenzeller¢kaffiliationpVoltage SecuritydnameiL. Martin¢kaffiliationeAxwaydnamelM. Schertlerddatej‘:hfilenamegrfc5408eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127295hkeywordsÇjpublic keyx public-key encryption technologykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIz]granking crfcd5408irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEIdentity-Based Encryption Architecture and Supporting Data StructuresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÛThis document gives example call flows of race conditions in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  Race conditions are inherently confusing and difficult to thwart; this document shows the best practices to handle them.  The elements in these call flows include SIP User Agents and SIP Proxy Servers.  Call flow diagrams and message details are given.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtNTT-east CorporationdnameiM. Hasebe¢kaffiliationtNTT-east CorporationdnamejJ. Koshiko¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnameiY. Suzuki¢kaffiliationtNTT-east CorporationdnamelT. Yoshikawa¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejP. Kyzivatddatej”>hfilenamegrfc5407eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127294hkeywordsÉosip user agentsfsip uaqsip proxy serverskobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateI@I=grankingcrfcd5407irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberìetotaletitlexNExample Call Flows of Race Conditions in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyæThe Security Considerations sections of many Internet Drafts say, in effect, "just use IPsec".  While this is sometimes correct, more often it will leave users without real, interoperable security mechanisms.  This memo offers some guidance on when IPsec Version 2 should and should not be specified.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamekS. Bellovinddatej‘,hfilenamegrfc5406eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127293hkeywordsÇqinternet securitywsecurity considerationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI•‘Hgrankingcrfcd5406irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberíetotaletitlex4Guidelines for Specifying the Use of IPsec Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThe User Datagram Protocol (UDP) provides a minimal message-passing transport that has no inherent congestion control mechanisms.  Because congestion control is critical to the stable operation of the Internet, applications and upper-layer protocols that choose to use UDP as an Internet transport must employ mechanisms to prevent congestion collapse and to establish some degree of fairness with concurrent traffic.  This document provides guidelines on the use of UDP for the designers of unicast applications and upper-layer protocols.  Congestion control guidelines are a primary focus, but the document also provides guidance on other topics, including message sizes, reliability, checksums, and middlebox traversal.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej‘4hfilenamegrfc5405eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127292hkeywordsÇvuser datagram protocolrcongestion controlkobsoletedByÅd8085epagesopublicationDateI"ˆÿgrankingcrfcd5405irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Unicast UDP Usage Guidelines for Application DesignersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the payload format for packetization of the G.719 full-band codec encoded audio signals into the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP).  The payload format supports transmission of multiple channels, multiple frames per payload, and interleaving. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelI. Johanssonddatej‘>hfilenamegrfc5404eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127291hkeywordsÇeITU-Tug.719 full-band codeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIz]grankingcrfcd5404irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Payload Format for G.719dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document describes version 2 of the RPCSEC_GSS protocol.  Version 2 is the same as version 1 (specified in RFC 2203) except that support for channel bindings has been added.  RPCSEC_GSS allows remote procedure call (RPC) protocols to access the Generic Security Services Application Programming Interface (GSS-API). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiM. Eislerddatej”hfilenamegrfc5403eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127290hkeywordsëhKerberoscONCcRPChsecuritynauthenticationiintegritycGSSgGSS-APIgprivacyoconfidentialityjencryptioncMICcNFSjcredentialhverifierimechanismgcontextkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIáë…grankingcrfcd5403irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletRPCSEC_GSS Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7861µhabstracty-This document explains the rules and procedures for utilizing compression (RFC 3274) within an Internet EDI (Electronic Data Interchange) 'AS' message, as defined in RFCs 3335, 4130, and 4823.  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNameqLisa M. DusseaultgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAxwaydnamejT. Hardingddatej”Òhfilenamegrfc5402eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127289hkeywordsÅlinternet edikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKpEºgrankingcrfcd5402irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLCompressed Data within an Internet Electronic Data Interchange (EDI) MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis document discusses the creation of reliable multicast protocols that utilize negative-acknowledgment (NACK) feedback.  The rationale for protocol design goals and assumptions are presented.  Technical challenges for NACK-based (and in some cases general) reliable multicast protocol operation are identified.  These goals and challenges are resolved into a set of functional "building blocks" that address different aspects of reliable multicast protocol operation.  It is anticipated that these building blocks will be useful in generating different instantiations of reliable multicast protocols.  This document obsoletes RFC 3941. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnamejB. Adamson¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationxNaval Research LaboratorydnameiJ. MackerddatejÃ£hfilenamegrfc5401eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127288hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateI-À+grankingcrfcd5401irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Multicast Negative-Acknowledgment (NACK) Building BlocksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèTo reduce the likelihood of conflict and confusion when relating documented examples to deployed systems, two blocks of Autonomous System numbers (ASNs) are reserved for use in examples in RFCs, books, documentation, and the like.  This document describes the reservation of two blocks of ASNs as reserved numbers for use in documentation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiG. Hustonddatej‘9hfilenamegrfc5398eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127287hkeywordsÇxautonomous system numberscasnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIAûògrankingcrfcd5398irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Autonomous System (AS) Number Reservation for Documentation UsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx™This specification defines a new WebDAV property that allows clients to quickly determine the principal corresponding to the current authenticated user. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamejW. Sanchez¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej‘7hfilenamegrfc5397eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127286hkeywordsÖdhttpfwebdavn