slugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Domain Name System (DNS) IANA ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Path Computation Element (PCE) architecture introduces the concept of policy in the context of path computation.  This document provides additional details on policy within the PCE architecture and also provides context for the support of PCE Policy.  This document introduces the use of the Policy Core Information Model (PCIM) as a framework for supporting path computation policy.  This document also provides representative scenarios for the support of PCE Policy.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlAdva OpticaldnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationoLabN ConsultingdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ashddatej‘7hfilenamegrfc5394eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127283hkeywordsÇcPCEjpce policykobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateIAûÉgrankingcrfcd5394irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Policy-Enabled Path Computation FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document normatively updates RFC 3261, the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), to address a security vulnerability identified in SIP proxy behavior. This vulnerability enables an attack against SIP networks where a small number of legitimate, even authorized, SIP requests can stimulate massive amounts of proxy-to-proxy traffic.

This document strengthens loop-detection requirements on SIP proxies when they fork requests (that is, forward a request to more than one destination). It also corrects and clarifies the description of the loop-detection algorithm such proxies are required to implement. Additionally, this document defines a Max-Breadth mechanism for limiting the number of concurrent branches pursued for any given request. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationuNortel Networks, Inc.dnamekS. Lawrence¢kaffiliationuDitech Networks, Inc.dnamenA. Hawrylyshen¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiB. Campenddatej‘7hfilenamegrfc5393eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127282hkeywordsácSIPqapplication-layerkapplicationelayerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIB◊Qgrankingcrfcd5393irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Addressing an Amplification Vulnerability in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Forking ProxiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThis document describes extensions to the OSPF version 2 and 3 protocols to support Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE) for multiple Autonomous Systems (ASes). OSPF-TE v2 and v3 extensions are defined for the flooding of TE information about inter-AS links that can be used to perform inter-AS TE path computation.

No support for flooding information from within one AS to another AS is proposed or defined in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamegM. Chen¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies Co. LtddnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegX. Duanddatej‘6hfilenamegrfc5392eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127281hkeywordsáxmultiprotocol label switchingpgeneralized mplshgmpls-tegmpls-tegisis-texopen shortest path firstgospf-tekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateInnBgrankingcrfcd5392irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]OSPF Extensions in Support of Inter-Autonomous System (AS) MPLS and GMPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document specifies a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format to be used for the ITU Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) G.711.1 audio codec.  Two media type registrations are also included. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamejA. Sollaudddatej‘5hfilenamegrfc5391eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127280hkeywordsÖxreal-time transport protocolx,itu telecommunication standardization sectorkaudio codedgpcmu-wbgpcma-wbkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI,äÊgrankingcrfcd5391irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3RTP Payload Format for ITU-T Recommendation G.711.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"Overload occurs in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) networks when proxies and user agents have insufficient resources to complete the processing of a request.  SIP provides limited support for overload handling through its 503 response code, which tells an upstream element that it is overloaded.  However, numerous problems have been identified with this mechanism.  This document summarizes the problems with the existing 503 mechanism, and provides some requirements for a solution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej‘6hfilenamegrfc5390eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127279hkeywordsÉcsipqoverload handlingl503 responsekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI?Rgrankingcrfcd5390irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJRequirements for Management of Overload in the Session Initiation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySession Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) is a protocol that serves as a tool for other protocols in dealing with Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal. It can be used by an endpoint to determine the IP address and port allocated to it by a NAT. It can also be used to check connectivity between two endpoints, and as a keep-alive protocol to maintain NAT bindings. STUN works with many existing NATs, and does not require any special behavior from them.

STUN is not a NAT traversal solution by itself. Rather, it is a tool to be used in the context of a NAT traversal solution. This is an important change from the previous version of this specification (RFC 3489), which presented STUN as a complete solution.

This document obsoletes RFC 3489. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamekP. Matthews¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wingddatej‘+hfilenamegrfc5389eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127278hkeywordsàdSIPscNATdSTUNiTraversalcICEhfirewalldTURNdVOIPkobsoletedByÅd8489epages3opublicationDateIPgrankingcrfcd5389irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7350d8553∂habstractyôThis document describes a standard way to store the configuration and the results of traceroute measurements.  This document first describes the terminology used in this document and the traceroute tool itself; afterwards, the common information model is defined, dividing the information elements into two semantically separated groups (configuration elements and results elements).  Moreover, an additional element is defined to relate configuration elements and results elements by means of a common unique identifier.  On the basis of the information model, a data model based on XML is defined to store the results of traceroute measurements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamelS. Niccolini¢kaffiliationcNECdnamemS. Tartarelli¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationcNECdnamehT. Dietz¢kaffiliationdUDeldnamehM. Swanyddatej‘6hfilenamegrfc5388eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127277hkeywordsÇxextensible markup languageuDISMAN-TRACEROUTE-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDateIAûqgrankingcrfcd5388irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Information Model and XML Data Model for Traceroute MeasurementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThe Internet network security protocol suite, IPsec, requires authentication, usually of network-layer entities, to enable access control and provide security services. This authentication can be based on mechanisms such as pre-shared symmetric keys, certificates with associated asymmetric keys, or the use of Kerberos (via Kerberized Internet Negotiation of Keys (KINK)). The need to deploy authentication information and its associated identities can be a significant obstacle to the use of IPsec.

This document explains the rationale for extending the Internet network security protocol suite to enable use of IPsec security services without authentication. These extensions are intended to protect communication, providing "better-than-nothing security" (BTNS). The extensions may be used on their own (this use is called Stand-Alone BTNS, or SAB) or may be used to provide network-layer security that can be authenticated by higher layers in the protocol stack (this use is called Channel-Bound BTNS, or CBB). The document also explains situations for which use of SAB and/or CBB extensions are applicable. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegY. Wangddatej‘2hfilenamegrfc5387eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbtnsdfullx#btns - Better-Than-Nothing SecuritydnamexBetter-Than-Nothing Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127276hkeywordsÖeipsecpstand-alone btnscsabrchannel-bound btnsccbbkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI…grankingcrfcd5387irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKProblem and Applicability Statement for Better-Than-Nothing Security (BTNS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies how to use the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) protocols, such as IKEv1 and IKEv2, to setup "unauthenticated" security associations (SAs) for use with the IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and the IPsec Authentication Header (AH).  No changes to IKEv2 bits-on-the-wire are required, but Peer Authorization Database (PAD) and Security Policy Database (SPD) extensions are specified.  Unauthenticated IPsec is herein referred to by its popular acronym, "BTNS" (Better-Than-Nothing Security). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. Williams¢kaffiliationcSSWdnamemM. Richardsonddatej‘2hfilenamegrfc5386eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbtnsdfullx#btns - Better-Than-Nothing SecuritydnamexBetter-Than-Nothing Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127275hkeywordsäxinternet protocol securityeikev1eikev2csascespbahcpadcspddbtnsuunauthenticated ipseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI∑granking
crfcd5386irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Better-Than-Nothing Security: An Unauthenticated Mode of IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÜThis document describes the properties and use of a revised Microsoft Word template (.dot) for writing Internet Drafts and RFCs. It replaces the initial template described in RFC 3285 to more fully support Word's outline modes and to be easier to use. This template can be direct-printed and direct-viewed, where either is line-for-line identical with RFC Editor-compliant ASCII output. This version obsoletes RFC 3285.

The most recent version of this template and post-processing scripts are available at http://www.isi.edu/touch/tools. This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touchddatej”√hfilenamegrfc5385eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127274hkeywordsÖlwriting I-Dslwriting RFCsiauthoringetoolstdocument preparationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKuΩ^granking	crfcd5385irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexIVersion 2.0 Microsoft Word Template for Creating Internet Drafts and RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√A "Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode" Join message sent by a given node identifies one or more multicast distribution trees that that node wishes to join.  Each tree is identified by the combination of a multicast group address and a source address (where the source address is possibly a "wild card").  Under certain conditions it can be useful, when joining a tree, to specify additional information related to the construction of the tree.  However, there has been no way to do so until now.  This document describes a modification of the Join message that allows a node to associate attributes with a particular tree.  The attributes are encoded in Type-Length-Value format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehA. Boers¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekI. Wijnands¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej‘1hfilenamegrfc5384eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-127273hkeywordsÑfpim-smxmulticast distribution treerpim join attributeiattr_typekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateIßgrankingcrfcd5384irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>The Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) Join Attribute FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7887∑habstracty2This document discusses deployment issues and describes requirements for successful deployment of mobile email that are implicit in the IETF lemonade documents.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejR. GellensddatejÃKhfilenamegrfc5383eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127272hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI␀Ùgrankingcrfcd5383irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberèetotaletitlex=Deployment Considerations for Lemonade-Compliant Mobile EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÃThis document defines a set of requirements for NATs that handle TCP that would allow many applications, such as peer-to-peer applications and online games to work consistently.  Developing NATs that meet this set of requirements will greatly increase the likelihood that these applications will function properly.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjCornell U.dnamegS. Guha¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiK. Biswas¢kaffiliationgMPI-SWSdnamegB. Ford¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Sivakumar¢kaffiliationnKazeon SystemsdnamelP. Srisureshddatej”Óhfilenamegrfc5382eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127271hkeywordsÅxnetwork address translationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH˜ªQgrankingcrfcd5382irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberéetotaletitlex#NAT Behavioral Requirements for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7857∂habstractywThis document describes how the authors developed a SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol)-based NETCONF (Network Configuration Protocol) client and server.  It describes an alternative SOAP binding for NETCONF that does not interoperate with an RFC 4743 conformant implementation making use of cookies on top of the persistent transport connections of HTTP.  When SOAP is used as a transport protocol for NETCONF, various kinds of development tools are available.  By making full use of these tools, developers can significantly reduce their workload.  The authors developed an NMS (Network Management System) and network equipment that can deal with NETCONF messages sent over SOAP.  This document aims to provide NETCONF development guidelines gained from the experience of implementing a SOAP-based NETCONF client and server.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvAlaxala Networks Corp.dnameiT. Iijima¢kaffiliationvAlaxala Networks Corp.dnamekY. Atarashi¢kaffiliationvAlaxala Networks Corp.dnameiH. Kimura¢kaffiliationvAlaxala Networks Corp.dnameiM. Kitani¢kaffiliationmHitachi, Ltd.dnamehH. Okitaddatej‘0hfilenamegrfc5381eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127270hkeywordsÑxsimple object access protocolxnetwork configuration protocolcmnsxnetwork management systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÖ–grankingcrfcd5381irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Experience of Implementing NETCONF over SOAPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡This document introduces extensions to Mobile IPv6 and IPv6 Neighbour Discovery to allow for local mobility handling.  Hierarchical mobility management for Mobile IPv6 is designed to reduce the amount of signalling between the mobile node, its correspondent nodes, and its home agent.  The Mobility Anchor Point (MAP) described in this document can also be used to improve the performance of Mobile IPv6 in terms of handover speed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtElevate TechnologiesdnamejH. Soliman¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnameoC. Castelluccia¢kaffiliationgAthonetdnamejK. ElMalki¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamejL. Bellierddatej‘,hfilenamegrfc5380eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127269hkeywordsÑkmobile ipv6wipv6 neighbor discoverycmapumobility anchor pointkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÂ7rgrankingcrfcd5380irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Hierarchical Mobile IPv6 (HMIPv6) Mobility ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¯This is an informational document that provides guidelines for using the privacy mechanism for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) that is specified in RFC 3323 and subsequently extended in RFCs 3325 and 4244.  It is intended to clarify the handling of the target SIP headers/parameters and the Session Description Protocol (SDP) parameters for each of the privacy header values (priv-values).  This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification; it is published for informational purposes.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekM. Munakata¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Schubert¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegT. Ohbaddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5379eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127268hkeywordsÑcSIPgPrivacyjpriv-valueiguidelinekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateKpŒzgrankingcrfcd5379irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex2Guidelines for Using the Privacy Mechanism for SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyyThe IETF policies about rights in Contributions to the IETF are designed to ensure that such Contributions can be made available to the IETF and Internet communities while permitting the authors to retain as many rights as possible.  This memo details the IETF policies on rights in Contributions to the IETF.  It also describes the objectives that the policies are designed to meet.  This memo obsoletes RFCs 3978 and 4748 and, with BCP 79 and RFC 5377, replaces Section 10 of RFC 2026.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationjWilmerHalednamelJ. Contrerasddatej‘/hfilenamegrfc5378eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127267hkeywordsÉxintellectual property rightsicopyrightciprkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIÚˇgrankingcrfcd5378irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberNetotaletitlex-Rights Contributors Provide to the IETF TrustdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfContributors grant intellectual property rights to the IETF.  The IETF Trust holds and manages those rights on behalf of the IETF.  The Trustees of the IETF Trust are responsible for that management.  This management includes granting the licenses to copy, implement, and otherwise use IETF Contributions, among them Internet-Drafts and RFCs.  The Trustees of the IETF Trust accepts direction from the IETF regarding the rights to be granted.  This document describes the desires of the IETF regarding outbound rights to be granted in IETF Contributions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdSelfdnamejJ. Halpernddatej‘0hfilenamegrfc5377eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127266hkeywordsÉlcontributorsrietf contributionsooutbound rightskobsoletedByÅd8721epagesopublicationDateIÚ˜grankingcrfcd5377irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRAdvice to the Trustees of the IETF Trust on Rights to Be Granted in IETF DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9Multiprotocol Label Switching Traffic Engineered (MPLS TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) may be established wholly within an Autonomous System (AS) or may cross AS boundaries.

The Path Computation Element (PCE) is a component that is capable of computing constrained paths for (G)MPLS TE LSPs. The PCE Communication Protocol (PCECP) is defined to allow communication between Path Computation Clients (PCCs) and PCEs, as well as between PCEs. The PCECP is used to request constrained paths and to supply computed paths in response. Generic requirements for the PCECP are set out in "Path Computation Element (PCE) Communication Protocol Generic Requirements", RFC 4657. This document extends those requirements to cover the use of PCECP in support of inter-AS MPLS TE. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationmKDDI R&D LabsdnameiK. Kumakiddatej‘.hfilenamegrfc5376eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127265hkeywordsècPCEePCECPlinter-AS PCErinter-provider PCEpinter-AS MPLS-TEvinter-provider MPLS-TEninter-AS PCECPtinter-provider PCECPvGMPLS path computationxMPLS-TE path computationxpath computation elementx'path computation communication protocolvpath computing elementgInterasjInteras TEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateIégranking␀crfcd5376irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUInter-AS Requirements for the Path Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCECP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√One fundamental aspect of any IP communications infrastructure is its addressing plan.  With its new address architecture and allocation policies, the introduction of IPv6 into a network means that network designers and operators need to reconsider their existing approaches to network addressing.  Lack of guidelines on handling this aspect of network design could slow down the deployment and integration of IPv6.  This document aims to provide the information and recommendations relevant to planning the addressing aspects of IPv6 deployments.  The document also provides IPv6 addressing case studies for both an enterprise and an ISP network.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoG. Van de Velde¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Popoviciu¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationx"T-Systems Enterprise Services GmbHdnamejO. Bonness¢kaffiliationx"T-Systems Enterprise Services GmbHdnamegC. Hahnddatej‘4hfilenamegrfc5375eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127264hkeywordsÇxinternet protocol version 6taddress architecturekobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateI7"0grankingˇcrfcd5375irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.IPv6 Unicast Address Assignment ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèThe Security Architecture for the Internet Protocol describes security services for traffic at the IP layer.  That architecture primarily defines services for Internet Protocol (IP) unicast packets.  This document describes how the IPsec security services are applied to IP multicast packets.  These extensions are relevant only for an IPsec implementation that supports multicast. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Weis¢kaffiliationxSecure Multicast Networks LLCdnamehG. Gross¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamekD. Ignjaticddatej‘3hfilenamegrfc5374eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127263hkeywordsÉbipeipsectip multicast packetskobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateI- Ïgranking˛crfcd5374irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKMulticast Extensions to the Security Architecture for the Internet ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document extends SIP with two header fields and associated option tags that can be used in INVITE requests to convey the requester's preference for user-interface handling related to answering of that request.  The first header, "Answer-Mode", expresses a preference as to whether the target node's user interface waits for user input before accepting the request or, instead, accepts the request without waiting on user input.  The second header, "Priv-Answer-Mode", is similar to the first, except that it requests administrative-level access and has consequent additional authentication and authorization requirements.  These behaviors have applicability to applications such as push-to-talk and to diagnostics like loop-back.  Usage of each header field in a response to indicate how the request was handled is also defined. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqSoftarmor SystemsdnameiD. Willis¢kaffiliationxResearch in Motion (RIM)dnamehA. Allenddatej‘/hfilenamegrfc5373eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127262hkeywordsäcPoCcPTTdautoiautomaticfmanualfanswerhloopbackjdiagnostickanswer-modeppriv-answer-modekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI,ä„granking˝crfcd5373irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDRequesting Answering Modes for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis memo describes extended uses for the payload header in "RTP Payload Format for JPEG 2000 Video Streams" as specified in RFC 5371, for better support of JPEG 2000 features such as scalability and main header recovery.

This memo must be accompanied with a complete implementation of "RTP Payload Format for JPEG 2000 Video Streams". That document is a complete description of the payload header and signaling, this document only describes additional processing for the payload header. There is an additional media type and Session Description Protocol (SDP) marker signaling for implementations of this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdSonydnamehA. Leung¢kaffiliationdSonydnamejS. Futemma¢kaffiliationdSonydnamejE. Itakuraddatej‘.hfilenamegrfc5372eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127261hkeywordsäxReal-time Transport Protocolxmain header compensationnpriority fieldvpriority mapping tablexpacket-number-based orderingxprogression-based orderingtlayer-based orderingxresolution-based orderingxcomponent-based orderingpSony CorporationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH˛ûgranking¸crfcd5372irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWPayload Format for JPEG 2000 Video: Extensions for Scalability and Main Header RecoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This memo describes an RTP payload format for the ISO/IEC International Standard 15444-1 | ITU-T Rec.  T.800, better known as JPEG 2000.  JPEG 2000 features are considered in the design of this payload format.  JPEG 2000 is a truly scalable compression technology allowing applications to encode once and decode many different ways.  The JPEG 2000 video stream is formed by extending from a single image to a series of JPEG 2000 images. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdSonydnamejS. Futemma¢kaffiliationdSonydnamejE. Itakura¢kaffiliationdSonydnamehA. Leungddatej‘.hfilenamegrfc5371eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127260hkeywordsÜoJPEG 2000 videocRTPxReal-time Transport Protocolkmain headerktile numberpSony CorporationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH˛ágranking˚crfcd5371irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.RTP Payload Format for JPEG 2000 Video StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how to invoke transcoding services using the conference bridge model.  This way of invocation meets the requirements for SIP regarding transcoding services invocation to support deaf, hard of hearing, and speech-impaired individuals. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”æhfilenamegrfc5370eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127259hkeywordsÑstranscoding serviceddeafohard of hearingospeech-impairedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	¸¬granking˙crfcd5370irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Conference Bridge Transcoding ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a framework for transcoding with SIP.  This framework includes how to discover the need for transcoding services in a session and how to invoke those transcoding services.  Two models for transcoding services invocation are discussed: the conference bridge model and the third-party call control model.  Both models meet the requirements for SIP regarding transcoding services invocation to support deaf, hard of hearing, and speech-impaired individuals.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”÷hfilenamegrfc5369eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127258hkeywordsÜttranscoding serviceswconference bridge modelxthird-party call control modelddeafohard of hearingospeech-impairedkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateI	¸∏granking˘crfcd5369irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDFramework for Transcoding with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This document defines extensions to the SIP REFER method so that it can be used to refer to multiple resources in a single request.  These extensions include the use of pointers to Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) lists in the Refer-To header field and the "multiple-refer" SIP option-tag. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehA. Niemi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejM. Isomaki¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationrEricsson AustraliadnamelH. Khartabilddatej‘+hfilenamegrfc5368eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127257hkeywordsÉisip referhrefer-toomultipler-referkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	¸≠granking¯crfcd5368irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHReferring to Multiple Resources in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8262∂habstracty≤This document specifies a way to create subscription to a list of resources in SIP.  This is achieved by including the list of resources in the body of a SUBSCRIBE request.  Instead of having a subscriber send a SUBSCRIBE request for each resource individually, the subscriber defines the resource list, subscribes to it, and gets notifications about changes in the resources' states using a single SUBSCRIBE dialog. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnamejA.B. Roach¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levinddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5367eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127256hkeywordsÇqsubscribe requestmresrouce listkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateI	¸£granking˜crfcd5367irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZSubscriptions to Request-Contained Resource Lists in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how to create a conference using SIP URI-list services.  In particular, it describes a mechanism that allows a User Agent Client to provide a conference server with the initial list of participants using an INVITE-contained URI list. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnstonddatej‘!hfilenamegrfc5366eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127255hkeywordsÇlsip uri listuinvite-contatined urikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	¸ógrankingˆcrfcd5366irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Conference Establishment Using Request-Contained Lists in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document specifies a mechanism that allows a SIP User Agent Client (UAC) to send a SIP MESSAGE request to a set of destinations, by using a SIP URI-list (Uniform Resource Identifier list) service.  The UAC sends a SIP MESSAGE request that includes the payload along with the URI list to the MESSAGE URI-list service, which sends a MESSAGE request including the payload to each of the URIs included in the list. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”ƒhfilenamegrfc5365eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127254hkeywordsÖquser agent clientcuacssip message requestx uniform resource identifier listpmessage uri listkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	¸ãgrankingıcrfcd5365irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLMultiple-Recipient MESSAGE Requests in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°In certain types of multimedia communications, a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) request is distributed to a group of SIP User Agents (UAs).  The sender sends a single SIP request to a server which further distributes the request to the group.  This SIP request contains a list of Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs), which identify the recipients of the SIP request.  This URI list is expressed as a resource list XML document.  This specification defines an XML extension to the XML resource list format that allows the sender of the request to qualify a recipient with a copy control level similar to the copy control level of existing email systems. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej‘-hfilenamegrfc5364eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127253hkeywordsÖcXMLdcopygcontrolhresourcedlistkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	¸}grankingÙcrfcd5364irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlExtensible Markup Language (XML) Format Extension for Representing Copy Control Attributes in Resource ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThis document describes the need for SIP URI-list services and provides requirements for their invocation.  Additionally, it defines a framework for SIP URI-list services, which includes security considerations applicable to these services. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnamejA.B. RoachddatejÃhfilenamegrfc5363eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127252hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateI	¸ygrankingÛcrfcd5363irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Framework and Security Considerations for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) URI-List ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡This document defines the SIP Pending Additions event package.  This event package is used by SIP relays to inform user agents about the consent-related status of the entries to be added to a resource list. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”"hfilenamegrfc5362eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127251hkeywordsÇoconsent-relatedmresource listkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	¸vgrankingÚcrfcd5362irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Pending Additions Event PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines an Extensible Markup Language (XML) format for a permission document used to request consent.  A permission document written in this format is used by a relay to request a specific recipient permission to perform a particular routing translation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”)hfilenamegrfc5361eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127250hkeywordsÉcxmlxextensible markup languagespremission documentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	¸grankingÒcrfcd5361irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A Document Format for Requesting ConsentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"SIP supports communications for several services, including real-time audio, video, text, instant messaging, and presence.  In its current form, it allows session invitations, instant messages, and other requests to be delivered from one party to another without requiring explicit consent of the recipient.  Without such consent, it is possible for SIP to be used for malicious purposes, including amplification and DoS (Denial of Service) attacks.  This document identifies a framework for consent-based communications in SIP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnameiD. WillisddatejÃ{hfilenamegrfc5360eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127249hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI	˚Ùgrankingcrfcd5360irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA Framework for Consent-Based Communications in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8217∑habstracty,This document gives examples of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) services.  This covers most features offered in so-called IP Centrex offerings from local exchange carriers and PBX (Private Branch Exchange) features.  Most of the services shown in this document are implemented in the SIP user agents, although some require the assistance of a SIP proxy.  Some require some extensions to SIP including the REFER, SUBSCRIBE, and NOTIFY methods and the Replaces and Join header fields.  These features are not intended to be an exhaustive set, but rather show implementations of common features likely to be implemented on SIP IP telephones in a business environment.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemC. Cunningham¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Donovan¢kaffiliationeSonusdnamejK. Summersddatej‘/hfilenamegrfc5359eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127248hkeywordsçcsipcpbxgcentrexhfeaturesdholdhtransferjforwardingiscreeningdparkfpickupfredialeclickjcall flowskobsoletedByÄepages™opublicationDateI␀ÛˇgrankingÔcrfcd5359irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberêetotaletitlex,Session Initiation Protocol Service ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyjThis document describes ways to prevent the use of default configured recursive nameservers as reflectors in Denial of Service (DoS) attacks.  It provides recommended configuration as measures to mitigate the attack.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamehJ. Damas¢kaffiliationkRegistro.brdnamehF. Nevesddatej‘.hfilenamegrfc5358eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127247hkeywordsÇqdenial of servicecdoskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH˘ÜgrankingÓcrfcd5358irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberåetotaletitlex<Preventing Use of Recursive Nameservers in Reflector AttacksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThe One-way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP), specified in RFC 4656, provides a common protocol for measuring one-way metrics between network devices.  OWAMP can be used bi-directionally to measure one-way metrics in both directions between two network elements.  However, it does not accommodate round-trip or two-way measurements.  This memo specifies a Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP), based on the OWAMP, that adds two-way or round-trip measurement capabilities.  The TWAMP measurement architecture is usually comprised of two hosts with specific roles, and this allows for some protocol simplifications, making it an attractive alternative in some circumstances. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmBrix NetworksdnamejK. Hedayat¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamemR. Krzanowski¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefK. Yum¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejJ. Babiarzddatej‘,hfilenamegrfc5357eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127246hkeywordsÇttwo-way measaurementvround-trip measurementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÍèñgrankingÌcrfcd5357irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-A Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol (TWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÜd7717d7750d5618d5938d8545d6038∂habstractyThis document describes server pool policies for Reliable Server Pooling (RSerPool) including considerations for implementing them at Endpoint Handlespace Redundancy Protocol (ENRP) servers and pool users.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Duisburg-EssendnamelT. Dreibholz¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexenddatej‘)hfilenamegrfc5356eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127245hkeywordsÉhrserpooldenrpx(endpoint handlespace redundancy protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH‚aögrankingÏcrfcd5356irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Reliable Server Pooling PoliciesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñReliable Server Pooling (RSerPool) is an architecture and set of protocols for the management and access to server pools supporting highly reliable applications and for client access mechanisms to a server pool.  This document describes security threats to the RSerPool architecture and presents requirements for security to thwart these threats.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Stillman¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehR. Gopal¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttman¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemans NetworksdnamekS. Sengodan¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. HoldregeddatejÃöhfilenamegrfc5355eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127244hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH‚aägrankingÎcrfcd5355irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmThreats Introduced by Reliable Server Pooling (RSerPool) and Requirements for Security in Response to ThreatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document details the parameters of the Aggregate Server Access Protocol (ASAP) and Endpoint Handlespace Redundancy Protocol (ENRP) defined within the Reliable Server Pooling (RSerPool) architecture.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrThe Resource GroupdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationrThe Resource GroupdnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Stillman¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexenddatej‘)hfilenamegrfc5354eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127243hkeywordsÅhrserpoolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH‚augrankingÍcrfcd5354irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfAggregate Server Access Protocol (ASAP) and Endpoint Handlespace Redundancy Protocol (ENRP) ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'The Endpoint Handlespace Redundancy Protocol (ENRP) is designed to work in conjunction with the Aggregate Server Access Protocol (ASAP) to accomplish the functionality of the Reliable Server Pooling (RSerPool) requirements and architecture.  Within the operational scope of RSerPool, ENRP defines the procedures and message formats of a distributed, fault-tolerant registry service for storing, bookkeeping, retrieving, and distributing pool operation and membership information.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrThe Resource GroupdnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationrThe Resource GroupdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Stillman¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamelA. Silvertonddatej‘)hfilenamegrfc5353eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127242hkeywordsÑhrserpooldasapx aggregate server access protocolwfault-tolerant registrykobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateH‚afgrankingÈcrfcd5353irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Endpoint Handlespace Redundancy Protocol (ENRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁAggregate Server Access Protocol (ASAP; RFC 5352), in conjunction with the Endpoint Handlespace Redundancy Protocol (ENRP; RFC 5353), provides a high-availability data transfer mechanism over IP networks. ASAP uses a handle-based addressing model that isolates a logical communication endpoint from its IP address(es), thus effectively eliminating the binding between the communication endpoint and its physical IP address(es), which normally constitutes a single point of failure.

In addition, ASAP defines each logical communication destination as a pool, providing full transparent support for server pooling and load sharing. It also allows dynamic system scalability -- members of a server pool can be added or removed at any time without interrupting the service.

ASAP is designed to take full advantage of the network level redundancy provided by the Stream Transmission Control Protocol (SCTP; RFC 4960). Each transport protocol, other than SCTP, MUST have an accompanying transport mapping document. It should be noted that ASAP messages passed between Pool Elements (PEs) and ENRP servers MUST use the SCTP transport protocol.

The high-availability server pooling is gained by combining two protocols, namely ASAP and ENRP, in which ASAP provides the user interface for Pool Handle to address translation, load sharing management, and fault management, while ENRP defines the high- availability Pool Handle translation service. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrThe Resource GroupdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationrThe Resource GroupdnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Stillman¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexenddatej‘(hfilenamegrfc5352eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127241hkeywordsÉhrserpooldenrpx(endpoint handlespace redundancy protocolkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateH‚aSgrankingËcrfcd5352irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Aggregate Server Access Protocol (ASAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThe Reliable Server Pooling effort (abbreviated "RSerPool") provides an application-independent set of services and protocols for building fault-tolerant and highly available client/server applications.  This document provides an overview of the protocols and mechanisms in the Reliable Server Pooling suite.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefP. Lei¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamefL. Ong¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Duisburg-EssendnamelT. Dreibholzddatej—ÿhfilenamegrfc5351eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127240hkeywordsÅhrserpoolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH‚a?grankingÁcrfcd5351irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0An Overview of Reliable Server Pooling ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx{This document updates the IANA allocation rules and registry of IPv4 and IPv6 Router Alert Option Values. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcTKKdnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationlSiemens/RokednamekA. McDonaldddatejÃûhfilenamegrfc5350eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127239hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH– (grankingÊcrfcd5350irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>IANA Considerations for the IPv4 and IPv6 Router Alert OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLThis document describes the use of Elliptic Curve certificates, Elliptic Curve signature schemes and Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) key agreement within the framework of PKINIT -- the Kerberos Version 5 extension that provides for the use of public key cryptography.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemK. Jaganathan¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiK. Lauterddatej”⁄hfilenamegrfc5349eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-127238hkeywordsÇdecdhxelliptic curve diffie-hellmankobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateHÿbgrankingÂcrfcd5349irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuElliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Support for Public Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document specifies TCP Friendly Rate Control (TFRC). TFRC is a congestion control mechanism for unicast flows operating in a best-effort Internet environment. It is reasonably fair when competing for bandwidth with TCP flows, but has a much lower variation of throughput over time compared with TCP, making it more suitable for applications such as streaming media where a relatively smooth sending rate is of importance.

This document obsoletes RFC 3448 and updates RFC 4342. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiJ. Padhye¢kaffiliationfDoCoModnameiJ. Widmerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5348eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127237hkeywordsÇxtcp-friendly rate controlrcongestion controlkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateH€Ã≤granking‰crfcd5348irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8TCP Friendly Rate Control (TFRC): Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThis document defines a Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) package to support fax calls.  The package allows for fax calls to be supported in two different ways.  The first one utilizes ITU-T Recommendation T.38 for fax relay under the control of the Call Agent.  The second one lets the gateway decide upon a method for fax transmission as well as handle the details of the fax call without Call Agent involvement.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasen¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamejD. Hancockddatej‘,hfilenamegrfc5347eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127236hkeywordsÑdmgcpifax callsifax relaypfax transmissionkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateI␀ÛÍgranking„crfcd5347irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Media Gateway Control Protocol Fax PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document describes experiences of operational requirements and several considerations for ENUM-based softswitches concerning call routing between two Korean Voice over IP (VoIP) carriers, gained during the ENUM pre-commercial trial hosted by the National Internet Development Agency of Korea (NIDA) in 2006.

These experiences show that an interim solution can maintain the stability of ongoing commercial softswitch system operations during the initial stage of ENUM service, where the DNS does not have sufficient data for the majority of calls. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdNIDAdnamefJ. Lim¢kaffiliationdNIDAdnamefW. Kim¢kaffiliationdNIDAdnamegC. Park¢kaffiliationdRMRLdnameiL. Conroyddatej”:hfilenamegrfc5346eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127235hkeywordsálApplicationsdENUMcDNSeE.164eNAPTRjSoftswitchkField TrialkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅ÷granking‚crfcd5346irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Operational Requirements for ENUM-Based Softswitch UsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty8The Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) is widely deployed to monitor, control, and (sometimes also) configure network elements. Even though the SNMP technology is well documented, it remains relatively unclear how SNMP is used in practice and what typical SNMP usage patterns are.

This document describes an approach to carrying out large-scale SNMP traffic measurements in order to develop a better understanding of how SNMP is used in real-world production networks. It describes the motivation, the measurement approach, and the tools and data formats needed to carry out such a study.

This document was produced within the IRTF's Network Management Research Group (NMRG), and it represents the consensus of all of the active contributors to this group. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej‘,hfilenamegrfc5345eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnmrgdfullxnmrg - Network ManagementdnamerNetwork Managementdtypebrgbidjdoc-127234hkeywordsÑplarge-scale snmpdirtfdnmrgx!network management research groupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÔø	granking·crfcd5345irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexYSimple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) Traffic Measurements and Trace Exchange FormatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document describes several use cases of peering of non-VoIP (Voice over IP) services between two or more Service Providers.  These Service Providers create a peering relationship between themselves, thus enabling their users to collaborate with users on the other Service Provider network.  The target of this document is to drive requirements for peering between domains that provide the non-VoIP based collaboration services with presence and, in particular, Instant Messaging (IM).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehA. Houri¢kaffiliationgAOL LLCdnamegE. Aoki¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemS. Parameswarddatej”hfilenamegrfc5344eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymispeermintdfullx7speermint - Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdnamex+Session PEERing for Multimedia INTerconnectdtypebwgbidjdoc-127233hkeywordsÑhnon-voipucollaboration serviceqinstant messagingbimkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateH˛ugranking‡crfcd5344irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Presence and Instant Messaging Peering Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyuThe Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) version three (SNMPv3) requires that an application know the identifier (snmpEngineID) of the remote SNMP protocol engine in order to retrieve or manipulate objects maintained on the remote SNMP entity.

This document introduces a well-known localEngineID and a discovery mechanism that can be used to learn the snmpEngineID of a remote SNMP protocol engine. The proposed mechanism is independent of the features provided by SNMP security models and may also be used by other protocol interfaces providing access to managed objects.

This document updates RFC 3411. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxJacobs University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej‘-hfilenamegrfc5343eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopsawgdfullx5opsawg - Operations and Management Area Working Groupdnamex,Operations and Management Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127232hkeywordsÉfsnmpv3lsnmpengineidmlocalengineidkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateH– &grankingﬂcrfcd5343irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberNetotaletitlexDSimple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) Context EngineID DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°Some IETF protocols make use of Ethernet frame formats and IEEE 802 parameters.  This document discusses some use of such parameters in IETF protocols and specifies IANA considerations for allocation of code points under the IANA OUI (Organizationally Unique Identifier).  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtEastlake EnterprisesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej‘*hfilenamegrfc5342eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127231hkeywordsÜhEthernetiEthertypec802cOUIcEUIdLSAPkobsoletedByÅd7042epagesopublicationDateH…jlgrankingﬁcrfcd5342irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCIANA Considerations and IETF Protocol Usage for IEEE 802 ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document creates an Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) registry for tel Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) parameters and their values.  It populates the registry with the parameters defined in the tel URI specification, along with the parameters in tel URI extensions defined for number portability and trunk groups. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationx!Bell Laboratories, Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbaniddatej“Mhfilenamegrfc5341eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-127230hkeywordsÇxuniform resource locatorgschemeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH“‡zgranking›crfcd5341irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfThe Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) tel Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) Parameter RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the modifications to OSPF to support version 6 of the Internet Protocol (IPv6). The fundamental mechanisms of OSPF (flooding, Designated Router (DR) election, area support, Short Path First (SPF) calculations, etc.) remain unchanged. However, some changes have been necessary, either due to changes in protocol semantics between IPv4 and IPv6, or simply to handle the increased address size of IPv6. These modifications will necessitate incrementing the protocol version from version 2 to version 3. OSPF for IPv6 is also referred to as OSPF version 3 (OSPFv3).

Changes between OSPF for IPv4, OSPF Version 2, and OSPF for IPv6 as described herein include the following. Addressing semantics have been removed from OSPF packets and the basic Link State Advertisements (LSAs). New LSAs have been created to carry IPv6 addresses and prefixes. OSPF now runs on a per-link basis rather than on a per-IP-subnet basis. Flooding scope for LSAs has been generalized. Authentication has been removed from the OSPF protocol and instead relies on IPv6's Authentication Header and Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP).

Even with larger IPv6 addresses, most packets in OSPF for IPv6 are almost as compact as those in OSPF for IPv4. Most fields and packet- size limitations present in OSPF for IPv4 have been relaxed. In addition, option handling has been made more flexible.

All of OSPF for IPv4's optional capabilities, including demand circuit support and Not-So-Stubby Areas (NSSAs), are also supported in OSPF for IPv6. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtAcoustra ProductionsdnameiR. Coltun¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekD. Ferguson¢kaffiliationwSycamore Networks, Inc.dnamefJ. Moy¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiA. Lindemddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5340eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127229hkeywordsÇxopen shortest path firstfospfv3kobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDateHáµ”granking‹crfcd5340irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlemOSPF for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÖd8362d6845d9454d7503d6860∂habstracty≈This document provides an evaluation of Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) protocols and mechanisms against the requirements for Multi-Layer Networks (MLNs) and Multi-Region Networks (MRNs).  In addition, this document identifies areas where additional protocol extensions or procedures are needed to satisfy these requirements, and provides guidelines for potential extensions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej‘+hfilenamegrfc5339eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127228hkeywordsÅx%general multiprotocol label switchingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÿRgranking€crfcd5339irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Evaluation of Existing GMPLS Protocols against Multi-Layer and Multi-Region Networks (MLN/MRN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document is an informative overview of how legacy applications can be made to work with the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  HIP proposes to add a cryptographic name space for network stack names.  From an application viewpoint, HIP-enabled systems support a new address family of host identifiers, but it may be a long time until such HIP-aware applications are widely deployed even if host systems are upgraded.  This informational document discusses implementation and Application Programming Interface (API) issues relating to using HIP in situations in which the system is HIP-aware but the applications are not, and is intended to aid implementors and early adopters in thinking about and locally solving systems issues regarding the incremental deployment of HIP.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrThe Boeing CompanydnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationx-Helsinki Institute for Information TechnologydnamegM. Komuddatej‘*hfilenamegrfc5338eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127227hkeywordsÖchipxcryptographic name spacesnetwork stack namescapix!application programming interfacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH“‡igranking⁄crfcd5338irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Using the Host Identity Protocol with Legacy ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔDelivery status notifications (DSNs) are critical to the correct operation of an email system. However, the existing Draft Standards (RFC 3461, RFC 3462, RFC 3464) are presently limited to US-ASCII text in the machine-readable portions of the protocol. This specification adds a new address type for international email addresses so an original recipient address with non-US-ASCII characters can be correctly preserved even after downgrading. This also provides updated content return media types for delivery status notifications and message disposition notifications to support use of the new address type.

This document experimentally extends RFC 3461, RFC 3464, and RFC 3798. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newman¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘%hfilenamegrfc5337eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127226hkeywordsÉcEAIcDSNdSMTPkobsoletedByÅd6533epagesopublicationDateH¡…“grankingŸcrfcd5337irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Internationalized Delivery Status and Disposition NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∫This document specifies an SMTP extension for transport and delivery of email messages with internationalized email addresses or header information.  Communication with systems that do not implement this specification is specified in another document.  This document updates some syntaxes and rules defined in RFC 2821 and RFC 2822, and has some material updating RFC 4952This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefJ. Yao¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefW. Maoddatej‘&hfilenamegrfc5336eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127225hkeywordsÑcEAIhUTF8SMTPdMAILhTRANSFERkobsoletedByÅd6531epagesopublicationDateH¡…œgrankingÿcrfcd5336irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4SMTP Extension for Internationalized Email AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%Full internationalization of electronic mail requires not only the capabilities to transmit non-ASCII content, to encode selected information in specific header fields, and to use non-ASCII characters in envelope addresses.  It also requires being able to express those addresses and the information based on them in mail header fields.  This document specifies an experimental variant of Internet mail that permits the use of Unicode encoded in UTF-8, rather than ASCII, as the base form for Internet email header field.  This form is permitted in transmission only if authorized by an SMTP extension, as specified in an associated specification.  This specification Updates section 6.4 of RFC 2045 to conform with the requirements.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeTWNICdnamegA. Yangddatej‘&hfilenamegrfc5335eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127224hkeywordsÇgunicodeeutf-8kobsoletedByÅd6532epagesopublicationDateH¡…Õgranking◊crfcd5335irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInternationalized Email HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx This document describes the registration of media types for the Ogg container format and conformance requirements for implementations of these types.  This document obsoletes RFC 3534. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdXiphdnamelI. Goncalves¢kaffiliationdXiphdnamekS. Pfeiffer¢kaffiliationdXiphdnamemC. Montgomeryddatej‘(hfilenamegrfc5334eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127223hkeywordsÖcOggdMIMEeVideoeAudiofCodecskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHøgranking÷crfcd5334irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleoOgg Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7845∂habstracty5This document registers Enumservices for Internet calendaring.  Specifically, this document focuses on Enumservices for scheduling with iMIP (iCalendar Message-Based Interoperability Protocol) and for accessing Internet calendaring information with CalDAV (Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationhSwisscomdnamelB. Hoeneisenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5333eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127222hkeywordsÖdENUMdiCaldiMIPei TIPfCalDAVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateJ”µ9granking’crfcd5333irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:IANA Registration of Enumservices for Internet CalendaringdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractySRFC 3032 established two data link layer codepoints for MPLS, used to distinguish whether the data link layer frame is carrying an MPLS unicast or an MPLS multicast packet. However, this usage was never deployed. This specification updates RFC 3032 by redefining the meaning of these two codepoints. Both codepoints can now be used to carry multicast packets. The second codepoint (formerly the "multicast codepoint") is now to be used only on multiaccess media, and it is to mean "the top label of the following label stack is an upstream-assigned label".

RFC 3032 does not specify the destination address to be placed in the "MAC DA" (Medium Access Layer Destination Address) field of an ethernet frame that carries an MPLS multicast packet. This document provides that specification.

This document updates RFC 3032 and RFC 4023. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Eckert¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejY. Rekhterddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5332eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127221hkeywordsÖxdata link layer codepointqmultiaccess mediawupstream-assigned labelfmac dax'medium access layer destination addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH§™rgranking‘crfcd5332irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMPLS Multicast EncapsulationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"RFC 3031 limits the MPLS architecture to downstream-assigned MPLS labels.  This document introduces the notion of upstream-assigned MPLS labels.  It describes the procedures for upstream MPLS label assignment and introduces the concept of a "Context-Specific Label Space". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej“rhfilenamegrfc5331eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127220hkeywordsÅxupstream-assigned mpls labelskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH§™`granking”crfcd5331irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?MPLS Upstream Label Assignment and Context-Specific Label SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7274∂habstractyÃSeveral Link-type sub-Type-Length-Values (sub-TLVs) have been defined for Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) and Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) in the context of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE), in order to advertise some link characteristics such as the available bandwidth, traffic engineering metric, administrative group, and so on.  By making statistical assumptions about the aggregated traffic carried onto a set of TE Label Switched Paths (LSPs) signalled with zero bandwidth (referred to as "unconstrained TE LSP" in this document), algorithms can be designed to load balance (existing or newly configured) unconstrained TE LSP across a set of equal cost paths.  This requires knowledge of the number of unconstrained TE LSPs signalled across a link.  This document specifies a new Link-type Traffic Engineering sub-TLV used to advertise the number of unconstrained TE LSPs signalled across a link. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehM. Meyer¢kaffiliationmKDDI R&D LabsdnameiK. Kumaki¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamehA. Bondaddatej‘.hfilenamegrfc5330eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127219hkeywordsÇbteclspkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH˘Égranking“crfcd5330irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexâA Link-Type sub-TLV to Convey the Number of Traffic Engineering Label Switched Paths Signalled with Zero Reserved Bandwidth across a LinkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÚThis document describes extensions to OSPFv3 to support intra-area Traffic Engineering (TE).  This document extends OSPFv2 TE to handle IPv6 networks.  A new TLV and several new sub-TLVs are defined to support IPv6 networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpIP Infusion, IncdnamekK. Ishiguro¢kaffiliationpIP Infusion, IncdnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationwData Connection LimiteddnamehA. Davey¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiA. Lindemddatej‘+hfilenamegrfc5329eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127218hkeywordsÉxopen shortest path firstfospfv3btekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH– "granking—crfcd5329irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Traffic Engineering Extensions to OSPF Version 3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Digital Video Broadcasting Project (DVB) for naming persistent resources defined within DVB standards.  Example resources include technical documents and specifications, eXtensible Markup Language (XML) Schemas, classification schemes, XML Document Type Definitions (DTDs), namespaces, style sheets, media assets, and other types of resources produced or managed by DVB.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmMicronas GmbHdnamehA. Adolf¢kaffiliationkDVB ProjectdnamekP. MacAvockddatej‘$hfilenamegrfc5328eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127217hkeywordsâbtvjtelevisionrdigital televisionfmpeg-2diptvjmultimediamcontent guidemprogram guidehmetadatakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH“‡Vgranking–crfcd5328irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Digital Video Broadcasting Project (DVB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7354d8553µhabstracty£The Licklider Transmission Protocol (LTP) is intended to serve as a reliable convergence layer over single-hop deep-space radio frequency (RF) links. LTP does Automatic Repeat reQuest (ARQ) of data transmissions by soliciting selective-acknowledgment reception reports. It is stateful and has no negotiation or handshakes. This document describes security extensions to LTP, and is part of a series of related documents describing LTP.

This document is a product of the Delay Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group. No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationlISTRAC, ISROdnamejM. Ramadas¢kaffiliationxNASA/Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamekS. Burleighddatej”Ûhfilenamegrfc5327eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-127216hkeywordsÑcltporadio frequencyxautomatic repeat requestcarqkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH– grankingœcrfcd5327irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex5Licklider Transmission Protocol - Security ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÂThis document describes the Licklider Transmission Protocol (LTP), designed to provide retransmission-based reliability over links characterized by extremely long message round-trip times (RTTs) and/or frequent interruptions in connectivity. Since communication across interplanetary space is the most prominent example of this sort of environment, LTP is principally aimed at supporting "long-haul" reliable transmission in interplanetary space, but it has applications in other environments as well.

This document is a product of the Delay Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group. No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlISTRAC, ISROdnamejM. Ramadas¢kaffiliationxNASA/Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamekS. Burleigh¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej”ˆhfilenamegrfc5326eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-127215hkeywordsÑcltppround-trip timescrttilong-haulkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateH– grankingŒcrfcd5326irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex/Licklider Transmission Protocol - SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractymThis document describes the motivation for the development of the Licklider Transmission Protocol (LTP) designed to provide retransmission-based reliability over links characterized by extremely long message round-trip times (RTTs) and/or frequent interruptions in connectivity. Since communication across interplanetary space is the most prominent example of this sort of environment, LTP is principally aimed at supporting "long-haul" reliable transmission in interplanetary space, but it has applications in other environments as well.

In an Interplanetary Internet setting deploying the Bundle protocol, LTP is intended to serve as a reliable convergence layer over single-hop deep-space radio frequency (RF) links. LTP does Automatic Repeat reQuest (ARQ) of data transmissions by soliciting selective-acknowledgment reception reports. It is stateful and has no negotiation or handshakes.

This document is a product of the Delay Tolerant Networking Research Group and has been reviewed by that group. No objections to its publication as an RFC were raised. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNASA/Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamekS. Burleigh¢kaffiliationlISTRAC, ISROdnamejM. Ramadas¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej”ﬂhfilenamegrfc5325eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-127214hkeywordsÉcltppround-trip timesilong-haulkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH– grankingÕcrfcd5325irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex,Licklider Transmission Protocol - MotivationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for information related to FC-SP, the Security Protocols defined for Fibre Channel. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Maino¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrieddatej‘'hfilenamegrfc5324eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127213hkeywordsÜxmanagement information basepT11-FC-SP-TC-MIBxT11-FC-SP-AUTHENTICATION-MIBtT11-FC-SP-ZONING-MIBtT11-FC-SP-POLICY-MIBpT11-FC-SP-SA-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesÿopublicationDateH…jSgrankingÃcrfcd5324irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0MIB for Fibre-Channel Security Protocols