opublicationDateHÊÅ»grankingΩcrfcd5309irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAPoint-to-Point Operation over LAN in Link State Routing ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis document specifies a method for exchanging IPv6 routing information using the IS-IS routing protocol.  The described method utilizes two new TLVs: a reachability TLV and an interface address TLV to distribute the necessary IPv6 information throughout a routing domain.  Using this method, one can route IPv6 along with IPv4 and OSI using a single intra-domain routing protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehC. Hoppsddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5308eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127202hkeywordsÉx*intermediate system to intermediate systemctlvcosikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅ©grankingºcrfcd5308irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewRouting IPv6 with IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7775∂habstractx†This document specifies encoding of extensions to the IS-IS routing protocol in support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej”◊hfilenamegrfc5307eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127201hkeywordsÅx*intermediate system to intermediate systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅïgrankingªcrfcd5307irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQIS-IS Extensions in Support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7074d6001d6002∂habstractymThis document describes a mechanism for a restarting router to signal to its neighbors that it is restarting, allowing them to reestablish their adjacencies without cycling through the down state, while still correctly initiating database synchronization.

This document additionally describes a mechanism for a restarting router to determine when it has achieved Link State Protocol Data Unit (LSP) database synchronization with its neighbors and a mechanism to optimize LSP database synchronization, while minimizing transient routing disruption when a router starts. This document obsoletes RFC 3847. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsbergddatej‘*hfilenamegrfc5306eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127200hkeywordsÑx*intermediate system to intermediate systemxLSP database synchronizationjLink StategRoutingkobsoletedByÅd8706epagesopublicationDateHÊÅÅgranking∫crfcd5306irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRestart Signaling for IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThis document describes extensions to the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol to support Traffic Engineering (TE).  This document extends the IS-IS protocol by specifying new information that an Intermediate System (router) can place in Link State Protocol Data Units (LSP).  This information describes additional details regarding the state of the network that are useful for traffic engineering computations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvRedback Networks, Inc.dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliation`dnamegH. Smitddatej”’hfilenamegrfc5305eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127199hkeywordsÖx*intermediate system to intermediate systembtefrouterolsp  data unitsxlink state protocol data unitskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅlgrankingπcrfcd5305irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(IS-IS Extensions for Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5307d8918∂habstracty3This document describes the authentication of Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Protocol Data Units (PDUs) using the Hashed Message Authentication Codes - Message Digest 5 (HMAC-MD5) algorithm as found in RFC 2104. IS-IS is specified in International Standards Organization (ISO) 10589, with extensions to support Internet Protocol version 4 (IPv4) described in RFC 1195. The base specification includes an authentication mechanism that allows for multiple authentication algorithms. The base specification only specifies the algorithm for cleartext passwords. This document replaces RFC 3567.

This document proposes an extension to that specification that allows the use of the HMAC-MD5 authentication algorithm to be used in conjunction with the existing authentication mechanisms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvRedback Networks, Inc.dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationvExtreme Networks, Inc.dnamekR. Atkinsonddatej‘)hfilenamegrfc5304eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127198hkeywordsàx*intermediate system to intermediate systemtIS-IS authenticationcMD5hHMAC-MD5hsecuritygroutingcisox$international standards organizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅSgranking∏crfcd5304irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"IS-IS Cryptographic AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6232d6233∂habstractyˇThe IS-IS routing protocol (Intermediate System to Intermediate System, ISO 10589) requires reliable protocols at the link layer for point-to-point links. As a result, it does not use a three-way handshake when establishing adjacencies on point-to-point media. This paper defines a backward-compatible extension to the protocol that provides for a three-way handshake. It is fully interoperable with systems that do not support the extension.

Additionally, the extension allows the robust operation of more than 256 point-to-point links on a single router.

This extension has been implemented by multiple router vendors; this paper is provided to the Internet community in order to allow interoperable implementations to be built by other vendors. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationrTenet TechnologiesdnameiR. Saluja¢kaffiliationtEastlake EnterprisesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej”∫hfilenamegrfc5303eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127197hkeywordsÅx*intermediate system to intermediate systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅ>granking∑crfcd5303irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Three-Way Handshake for IS-IS Point-to-Point AdjacenciesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes extensions to the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol to support optimal routing within a two-level domain. The IS-IS protocol is specified in ISO 10589, with extensions for supporting IPv4 (Internet Protocol) specified in RFC 1195. This document replaces RFC 2966.

This document extends the semantics presented in RFC 1195 so that a routing domain running with both level 1 and level 2 Intermediate Systems (IS) (routers) can distribute IP prefixes between level 1 and level 2, and vice versa. This distribution requires certain restrictions to ensure that persistent forwarding loops do not form. The goal of this domain-wide prefix distribution is to increase the granularity of the routing information within the domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvRedback Networks, Inc.dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliation`dnamegH. Smit¢kaffiliationgZ2 SagldnamemT. Przygiendaddatej‘%hfilenamegrfc5302eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127196hkeywordsÖx*intermediate system to intermediate systemgrouterseloopsbIPqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅ*granking∂crfcd5302irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Domain-Wide Prefix Distribution with Two-Level IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxRFC 2763 defined a simple and dynamic mechanism for routers running IS-IS to learn about symbolic hostnames. RFC 2763 defined a new TLV that allows the IS-IS routers to flood their name-to-systemID mapping information across the IS-IS network.

This document obsoletes RFC 2763. This document moves the capability provided by RFC 2763 to the Standards Track. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegN. Shenddatej‘*hfilenamegrfc5301eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127195hkeywordsÑx*intermediate system to intermediate systemgroutersctlvpname-to-systemIDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÊÅgrankingµcrfcd5301irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Dynamic Hostname Exchange Mechanism for IS-ISdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6232∂habstractyProtection and recovery are important features of service offerings in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks. Increasingly, MPLS and GMPLS networks are being extended from single domain scope to multi-domain environments.

Various schemes and processes have been developed to establish Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in multi-domain environments. These are discussed in RFC 4726: "A Framework for Inter-Domain Multiprotocol Label Switching Traffic Engineering".

This document analyzes the application of these techniques to protection and recovery in multi-domain networks. The main focus for this document is on establishing end-to-end diverse Traffic Engineering (TE) LSPs in multi-domain networks. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takeda¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamejY. Ikejiri¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseurddatej‘'hfilenamegrfc5298eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127194hkeywordsÑdmplsegmplsxmulti-domain environmentx+end-to-end diverse Traffic Engineering LSPskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH≠∏Ågranking≤crfcd5298irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Analysis of Inter-Domain Label Switched Path (LSP) RecoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This memo describes SIV (Synthetic Initialization Vector), a block cipher mode of operation.  SIV takes a key, a plaintext, and multiple variable-length octet strings that will be authenticated but not encrypted.  It produces a ciphertext having the same length as the plaintext and a synthetic initialization vector.  Depending on how it is used, SIV achieves either the goal of deterministic authenticated encryption or the goal of nonce-based, misuse-resistant authenticated encryption.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamejD. Harkinsddatej‘%hfilenamegrfc5297eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127193hkeywordsÖxauthenticated encryptionlkey wrappingnkey derivationlblock ciphervpseudo-random functionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateI␀Ûœgranking±crfcd5297irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkSynthetic Initialization Vector (SIV) Authenticated Encryption Using the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) is a generic framework supporting multiple types of authentication methods.  In systems where EAP is used for authentication, it is desirable to not repeat the entire EAP exchange with another authenticator.  This document specifies extensions to EAP and the EAP keying hierarchy to support an EAP method-independent protocol for efficient re-authentication between the peer and an EAP re-authentication server through any authenticator.  The re-authentication server may be in the home network or in the local network to which the peer is connecting. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnamelV. Narayanan¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnamejL. Dondetiddatej”Rhfilenamegrfc5296eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127192hkeywordsÇx"extensible authentication protocoltauthentication modeskobsoletedByÅd6696epages+opublicationDateHµ„granking∞crfcd5296irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7EAP Extensions for EAP Re-authentication Protocol (ERP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) defined the Extended Master Session Key (EMSK) generation, but reserved it for unspecified future uses.  This memo reserves the EMSK for the sole purpose of deriving root keys.  Root keys are master keys that can be used for multiple purposes, identified by usage definitions.  This document also specifies a mechanism for avoiding conflicts between root keys by deriving them in a manner that guarantees cryptographic separation.  Finally, this document also defines one such root key usage: Domain-Specific Root Keys are root keys made available to and used within specific key management domains. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationmQualcomm, IncdnamejL. Dondeti¢kaffiliationmQualcomm, IncdnamelV. Narayanan¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekM. Nakhjiriddatej‘#hfilenamegrfc5295eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127191hkeywordsÜjEAP keyingdEMSKdDSRKfDSUSRKxDomain-Specific Key DerivationxUsage-Specific Key DerivationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHµ‚ˆgrankingØcrfcd5295irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXSpecification for the Derivation of Root Keys from an Extended Master Session Key (EMSK)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo complements the list of multicast infrastructure security threat analysis documents by describing Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) threats specific to router interfaces connecting hosts.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationgArastradnamejJ. Lingardddatej‘%hfilenamegrfc5294eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-127190hkeywordsÅxsecurity threat analysiskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH≠∏lgrankingÆcrfcd5294irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Host Threats to Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxâThis document defines two new actions for the "Sieve" email filtering language that add and delete email header fields. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamejJ. Degener¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamekP. Guentherddatej‘$hfilenamegrfc5293eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127189hkeywordsÇjaddheadaerldeleteheaderkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateH≠∏]granking≠crfcd5293irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Sieve Email Filtering: Editheader ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document defines a new Outbound Router Filter (ORF) type for BGP, termed "Address Prefix Outbound Route Filter", that can be used to perform address-prefix-based route filtering.  This ORF-type supports prefix-length- or range-based matching, wild-card-based address prefix matching, as well as the exact address prefix matching for address families. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Sangliddatej‘$hfilenamegrfc5292eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127188hkeywordsÉcorfwborder gateway protocolx$Address Prefix Outbound Route FilterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHòÿÆgranking¨crfcd5292irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Address-Prefix-Based Outbound Route Filter for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document defines a BGP-based mechanism that allows a BGP speaker to send to its BGP peer a set of Outbound Route Filters (ORFs) that the peer would use to constrain/filter its outbound routing updates to the speaker. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej‘$hfilenamegrfc5291eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127187hkeywordsÇvborder gatway protocolcorfkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHòÿûgranking´crfcd5291irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Outbound Route Filtering Capability for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyåThis document presents some observations on "simple best-effort traffic", defined loosely for the purposes of this document as Internet traffic that is not covered by Quality of Service (QOS) mechanisms, congestion-based pricing, cost-based fairness, admissions control, or the like.  One observation is that simple best-effort traffic serves a useful role in the Internet, and is worth keeping.  While differential treatment of traffic can clearly be useful, we believe such mechanisms are useful as *adjuncts* to simple best- effort traffic, not as *replacements* of simple best-effort traffic.  A second observation is that for simple best-effort traffic, some form of rough flow-rate fairness is a useful goal for resource allocation, where "flow-rate fairness" is defined by the goal of equal flow rates for different flows over the same path.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars EggertgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdICSIdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationdICSIdnameiM. Allmanddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127186hkeywordsÅrflow-rate fairnesskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHà—dgranking™crfcd5290irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8Comments on the Usefulness of Simple Best-Effort TrafficdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’RFC 4492 describes elliptic curve cipher suites for Transport Layer Security (TLS).  However, all those cipher suites use HMAC-SHA-1 as their Message Authentication Code (MAC) algorithm.  This document describes sixteen new cipher suites for TLS that specify stronger MAC algorithms.  Eight use Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC) with SHA-256 or SHA-384, and eight use AES in Galois Counter Mode (GCM).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorladdatej‘"hfilenamegrfc5289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127185hkeywordsÇxtransport layer securitymmac algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH≠∑ˆgranking©crfcd5289irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSTLS Elliptic Curve Cipher Suites with SHA-256/384 and AES Galois Counter Mode (GCM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo describes the use of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) in Galois/Counter Mode (GCM) as a Transport Layer Security (TLS) authenticated encryption operation.  GCM provides both confidentiality and data origin authentication, can be efficiently implemented in hardware for speeds of 10 gigabits per second and above, and is also well-suited to software implementations.  This memo defines TLS cipher suites that use AES-GCM with RSA, DSA, and Diffie-Hellman-based key exchange mechanisms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekPasi Eronendarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelA. Choudhury¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiD. McGrewddatej‘!hfilenamegrfc5288eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127184hkeywordsÑxadvanced encryption standardxtransport layer securitykdata originoconfidentialitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH≠∑Ÿgranking®crfcd5288irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3AES Galois Counter Mode (GCM) Cipher Suites for TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9325∂habstractx˜This document defines extension to the Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) control protocol RFC 4447 and PWE3 IANA allocations RFC 4446 required for the setup of Time-Division Multiplexing (TDM) pseudowires in MPLS networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamemA. Vainshtein¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnamekY(J). Steinddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5287eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127183hkeywordsÑdpwe3x!pseudowire emulation edge-to-edgeftdmoipktdm optionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHö3Vgrankingßcrfcd5287irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjControl Protocol Extensions for the Setup of Time-Division Multiplexing (TDM) Pseudowires in MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThis document describes the use of loop-free alternates to provide local protection for unicast traffic in pure IP and MPLS/LDP networks in the event of a single failure, whether link, node, or shared risk link group (SRLG).  The goal of this technology is to reduce the packet loss that happens while routers converge after a topology change due to a failure.  Rapid failure repair is achieved through use of precalculated backup next-hops that are loop-free and safe to use until the distributed network convergence process completes.  This simple approach does not require any support from other routers.  The extent to which this goal can be met by this specification is dependent on the topology of the network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehA. Atlas¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehA. Zininddatej‘$hfilenamegrfc5286eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127182hkeywordsÖcFRRcLFAhrecoverygfailuregroutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH€Ã∞granking¶crfcd5286irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Basic Specification for IP Fast Reroute: Loop-Free AlternatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8518∂habstractyõThis document provides a general mechanism to use the header extension feature of RTP (the Real-Time Transport Protocol).  It provides the option to use a small number of small extensions in each RTP packet, where the universe of possible extensions is large and registration is de-centralized.  The actual extensions in use in a session are signaled in the setup information for that session. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkApple, Inc.dnameiD. Singer¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekH. Desineniddatej“hfilenamegrfc5285eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127181hkeywordsÇxreal-time transport protocolfextmapkobsoletedByÅd8285epagesopublicationDateHáµ—granking•crfcd5285irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-A General Mechanism for RTP Header ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚The Resource ReserVation Protocol (RSVP) defines an ERROR_SPEC object for communicating errors. That object has a defined format that permits the definition of 256 error codes. As RSVP has been developed and extended, the convention has been to be conservative in defining new error codes. Further, no provision for user-defined errors exists in RSVP.

This document defines a USER_ERROR_SPEC to be used in addition to the ERROR_SPEC to carry additional user information related to errors. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej‘$hfilenamegrfc5284eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127180hkeywordsÉxresource reservation protocolouser_error_specjerror_speckobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateHµ‚‡granking§crfcd5284irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUser-Defined Errors for RSVPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘To facilitate the establishment of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) that would span multiple IGP areas in a given Autonomous System (AS), this document describes a new optional Longest-Match Label Mapping Procedure for the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP).

This procedure allows the use of a label if the Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) Element matches an entry in the Routing Information Base (RIB). Matching is defined by an IP longest-match search and does not mandate an exact match. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekB. Decraene¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamehI. Mineiddatej‘"hfilenamegrfc5283eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127179hkeywordsÉxLDP label mapping proceduresmlongest-matchrprefix aggregationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÜpgranking£crfcd5283irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8LDP Extension for Inter-Area Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙An authenticated encryption algorithm combines encryption and integrity into a single operation; such algorithms may also be referred to as combined modes of an encryption cipher or as combined mode algorithms. This document describes the use of authenticated encryption algorithms with the Encrypted Payload of the Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocol.

The use of two specific authenticated encryption algorithms with the IKEv2 Encrypted Payload is also described; these two algorithms are the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) in Galois/Counter Mode (AES GCM) and AES in Counter with CBC-MAC Mode (AES CCM). Additional documents may describe the use of other authenticated encryption algorithms with the IKEv2 Encrypted Payload. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrewddatej‘'hfilenamegrfc5282eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127178hkeywordsÜqencryption cipherxcombined mode algorithmsgaes gcmx3advanced encryption standard in galois/counter modegaes ccmxaes in couner with cbc-mac modekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH≠∑»granking¢crfcd5282irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|Using Authenticated Encryption Algorithms with the Encrypted Payload of the Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•EAP-TTLS is an EAP (Extensible Authentication Protocol) method that encapsulates a TLS (Transport Layer Security) session, consisting of a handshake phase and a data phase.  During the handshake phase, the server is authenticated to the client (or client and server are mutually authenticated) using standard TLS procedures, and keying material is generated in order to create a cryptographically secure tunnel for information exchange in the subsequent data phase.  During the data phase, the client is authenticated to the server (or client and server are mutually authenticated) using an arbitrary authentication mechanism encapsulated within the secure tunnel.  The encapsulated authentication mechanism may itself be EAP, or it may be another authentication protocol such as PAP, CHAP, MS-CHAP, or MS-CHAP-V2.  Thus, EAP-TTLS allows legacy password-based authentication protocols to be used against existing authentication databases, while protecting the security of these legacy protocols against eavesdropping, man-in-the-middle, and other attacks.  The data phase may also be used for additional, arbitrary data exchange.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlUnaffiliateddnamegP. Funk¢kaffiliationgSafeNetdnameoS. Blake-Wilsonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5281eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127177hkeywordsÑcEAPcAAAnAuthenticationcTLSkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateH•Ï˝granking°crfcd5281irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrExtensible Authentication Protocol Tunneled Transport Layer Security Authenticated Protocol Version 0 (EAP-TTLSv0)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9427d8996∂habstractyﬁThis memo profiles the X.509 v3 certificate and X.509 v2 certificate revocation list (CRL) for use in the Internet.  An overview of this approach and model is provided as an introduction.  The X.509 v3 certificate format is described in detail, with additional information regarding the format and semantics of Internet name forms.  Standard certificate extensions are described and two Internet-specific extensions are defined.  A set of required certificate extensions is specified.  The X.509 v2 CRL format is described in detail along with standard and Internet-specific extensions.  An algorithm for X.509 certification path validation is described.  An ASN.1 module and examples are provided in the appendices. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnameiD. Cooper¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationgEntrustdnameiS. Boeyen¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegW. Polkddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5280eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127176hkeywordsÉhX.509 v3hX.509 v2vcertificate extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesóopublicationDateH#â{granking†crfcd5280irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByàd9925d9608d8398d8399d9549d9618d9598d6818∂habstractyÑThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) for Uniform Resource Namespace (URN) resources published by the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP).  3GPP defines and manages resources that utilize this URN name model.  Management activities for these and other resource types are provided by the 3GPP Support Team.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Monrad¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiS. Loretoddatej‘!hfilenamegrfc5279eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127175hkeywordsÉcnidtnamespace identifierd3gppkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÜp1grankingücrfcd5279irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis document registers the Enumservice named "vmsg", which is used to facilitate the real-time routing of voice, video, and unified communications to a messaging system.  This vmsg Enumservice registers three Enumservice types: "voicemsg", "videomsg", and "unifmsg".  Each type also registers the subtypes "sip", "sips", "http", and "https", as well as the subtype "tel" for the "voicemsg" type. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxComcast Cable CommunicationsdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationkAcme PacketdnamemD. Troshynskiddatej‘!hfilenamegrfc5278eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127174hkeywordsâdvmsghvoicemsghvideomsggunifmsgcsipdsipsdhttpehttpsctelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHäKègrankingûcrfcd5278irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?IANA Registration of Enumservices for Voice and Video MessagingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractyNThis document defines mechanisms that provide an asynchronous message notification delivery service for the Network Configuration protocol (NETCONF).  This is an optional capability built on top of the base NETCONF definition.  This document defines the capabilities and operations necessary to support this service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamekS. Chisholm¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejH. Trevinoddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc5277eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127173hkeywordsÜcXMLxExtensible Markup LanguagegNETCONFeEventtAsynchronous MessagelNotificationkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateHÅ)—grankingùcrfcd5277irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNETCONF Event NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThe Server-based Certificate Validation Protocol (SCVP) defines an extensible means of delegating the development and validation of certification paths to a server.  It can be used to support the development and validation of certification paths well after the expiration of the certificates in the path by specifying a time of interest in the past.  The Evidence Record Syntax (ERS) defines structures, called evidence records, to support the non-repudiation of the existence of data.  Evidence records can be used to preserve materials that comprise a certification path such that trust in the certificates can be established after the expiration of the certificates in the path and after the cryptographic algorithms used to sign the certificates in the path are no longer secure.  This document describes usage of the SCVP WantBack feature to convey evidence records, enabling SCVP responders to provide preservation evidence for certificates and certificate revocation lists (CRLs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallaceddatej‘!hfilenamegrfc5276eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeltansdfullx-ltans - Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdnamex%Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-127172hkeywordsÖcERSoEvidence RecorddSCVPx,Server-based Certificate Validation ProtocolxPKI artifact preservationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH£¡grankingúcrfcd5276irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbUsing the Server-Based Certificate Validation Protocol (SCVP) to Convey Long-Term Evidence RecordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document describes a mechanism to manage (i.e., set up, distribute, and rekey) keys used with symmetric cryptographic algorithms.  Also defined herein is a mechanism to organize users into groups to support distribution of encrypted content using symmetric cryptographic algorithms.  The mechanism uses the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) protocol and Certificate Management over CMS (CMC) protocol to manage the symmetric keys.  Any member of the group can then later use this distributed shared key to decrypt other CMS encrypted objects with the symmetric key.  This mechanism has been developed to support Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Mail List Agents (MLAs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turnerddatej—Xhfilenamegrfc5275eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127171hkeywordsÑxcryptographic message syntaxx"symmetric cryptographic algorithmsxcertificate management over cmsccmckobsoletedByÄepagesYopublicationDateHPc$grankingõcrfcd5275irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-CMS Symmetric Key Management and DistributiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document provides a set of compliance statements about the CMC (Certificate Management over CMS) enrollment protocol.  The ASN.1 structures and the transport mechanisms for the CMC enrollment protocol are covered in other documents.  This document provides the information needed to make a compliant version of CMC. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationxTraceRoute Security, Inc.dnamehM. Myersddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5274eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127170hkeywordsÇxcryptographic message syntaxwcmc enrollment protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHPc!grankingöcrfcd5274irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGCertificate Management Messages over CMS (CMC): Compliance RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6402∂habstractyThis document defines a number of transport mechanisms that are used to move CMC (Certificate Management over CMS (Cryptographic Message Syntax)) messages.  The transport mechanisms described in this document are HTTP, file, mail, and TCP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationxTraceRoute Security, Inc.dnamehM. Myersddatej‘	hfilenamegrfc5273eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127169hkeywordsÖxcryptographic message syntaxdhttpdfiledmailctcpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHPcgrankingôcrfcd5273irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Certificate Management over CMS (CMC): Transport ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6402∂habstractyThis document defines the base syntax for CMC, a Certificate Management protocol using the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS). This protocol addresses two immediate needs within the Internet Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) community:

1. The need for an interface to public key certification products and services based on CMS and PKCS #10 (Public Key Cryptography Standard), and

2. The need for a PKI enrollment protocol for encryption only keys due to algorithm or hardware design.

CMC also requires the use of the transport document and the requirements usage document along with this document for a full definition. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationxTraceRoute Security, Inc.dnamehM. Myersddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5272eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-127168hkeywordsÑxcertificate management protocolxcryptographic message syntaxcpkixpublic key infrastructurekobsoletedByÄepagesSopublicationDateHPcgrankingòcrfcd5272irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Certificate Management over CMS (CMC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6402∂habstractyJMobile IPv6 is designed to maintain its connectivity while moving from one network to another.  It is adopted in 3G CDMA networks as a way to maintain connectivity when the mobile node (MN) moves between access routers.  However, this handover procedure requires not only movement detection by the MN, but also the acquisition of a new Care-of Address and Mobile IPv6 registration with the new care-of address before the traffic can be sent or received in the target network.  During this period, packets destined for the mobile node may be lost, which may not be acceptable for a real-time application such as Voice over IP (VoIP) or video telephony.  This document specifies fast handover methods in the 3G CDMA networks in order to reduce latency and packet loss during handover.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhKDDI LabdnameiH. Yokota¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Dommetyddatej”@hfilenamegrfc5271eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127167hkeywordsÉdFMIPghandoffe3GPP2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHb‘^grankingócrfcd5271irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Mobile IPv6 Fast Handovers for 3G CDMA NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document describes how a Mobile IPv6 Fast Handover can be implemented on link layers conforming to the IEEE 802.16e suite of specifications.  The proposed scheme tries to achieve seamless handover by exploiting the link-layer handover indicators and thereby synchronizing the IEEE 802.16e handover procedures with the Mobile IPv6 fast handover procedures efficiently.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgSAMSUNGdnamegH. Jang¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamefJ. Jee¢kaffiliationcKUTdnamefY. Han¢kaffiliationsSAMSUNG ElectronicsdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamefJ. Chaddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127166hkeywordsÉkMobile IPv6uHandover optimizationrCross-layer designkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHb‘\grankingñcrfcd5270irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Mobile IPv6 Fast Handovers over IEEE 802.16e NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}Fast Mobile IPv6 requires that a Fast Binding Update is secured using a security association shared between an Access Router and a Mobile Node in order to avoid certain attacks.  In this document, a method for provisioning a shared key from the Access Router to the Mobile Node is defined to protect this signaling.  The Mobile Node generates a public/private key pair using the same public key algorithm as for SEND (RFC 3971).  The Mobile Node sends the public key to the Access Router.  The Access Router encrypts a shared handover key using the public key and sends it back to the Mobile Node.  The Mobile Node decrypts the shared handover key using the matching private key, and the handover key is then available for generating an authenticator on a Fast Binding Update.  The Mobile Node and Access Router use the Router Solicitation for Proxy Advertisement and Proxy Router Advertisement from Fast Mobile IPv6 for the key exchange.  The key exchange messages are required to have SEND security; that is, the source address is a Cryptographically Generated Address (CGA) and the messages are signed using the CGA private key of the sending node.  This allows the Access Router, prior to providing the shared handover key, to verify the authorization of the Mobile Node to claim the address so that the previous care-of CGA in the Fast Binding Update can act as the name of the key. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoDoCoMo Labs USAdnamehJ. Kempf¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnameiR. Koodliddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127165hkeywordsÅsfast binding updatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHb‘Zgrankingïcrfcd5269irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfDistributing a Symmetric Fast Mobile IPv6 (FMIPv6) Handover Key Using SEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRMobile IPv6 enables a Mobile Node (MN) to maintain its connectivity to the Internet when moving from one Access Router to another, a process referred to as handover.  During handover, there is a period during which the Mobile Node is unable to send or receive packets because of link switching delay and IP protocol operations.  This "handover latency" resulting from standard Mobile IPv6 procedures, namely movement detection, new Care-of Address configuration, and Binding Update, is often unacceptable to real-time traffic such as Voice over IP (VoIP).  Reducing the handover latency could be beneficial to non-real-time, throughput-sensitive applications as well.  This document specifies a protocol to improve handover latency due to Mobile IPv6 procedures.  This document does not address improving the link switching latency. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnameiR. Koodliddatej” hfilenamegrfc5268eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127164hkeywordsÇemipv6phandover latencykobsoletedByÅd5568epages0opublicationDateHb‘Ygrankingîcrfcd5268irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMobile IPv6 Fast HandoversdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/The IMAP4rev1 protocol has powerful search facilities as part of the core protocol, but lacks the ability to create live, updated results that can be easily handled.  This memo provides such an extension, and shows how it can be used to provide a facility similar to virtual mailboxes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekD. Cridland¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamegC. Kingddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5267eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127163hkeywordsÉiimap4rev1eesortgcontextkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÜpCgrankingìcrfcd5267irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlerContexts for IMAP4dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9394d5465∑habstractyàEnterprise users require mobility and secure connectivity when they roam and connect to the services offered in the enterprise.  Secure connectivity is required when the user connects to the enterprise from an untrusted network.  Mobility is beneficial when the user moves, either inside or outside the enterprise network, and acquires a new IP address.  This document describes a solution using Mobile IPv4 (MIPv4) and mobility extensions to IKEv2 (MOBIKE) to provide secure connectivity and mobility.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhWichorusdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. EronenddatejÃ}hfilenamegrfc5266eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127162hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHX7Ògrankingícrfcd5266irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberàetotaletitlex^Secure Connectivity and Mobility Using Mobile IPv4 and IKEv2 Mobility and Multihoming (MOBIKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document outlines a solution for the Mobile IPv4 (MIPv4) and IPsec coexistence problem for enterprise users.  The solution consists of an applicability statement for using Mobile IPv4 and IPsec for session mobility in corporate remote access scenarios, and a required mechanism for detecting the trusted internal network securely. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgCodebaydnamejS. Vaarala¢kaffiliationhBirdstepdnamekE. Klovningddatej”Dhfilenamegrfc5265eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127161hkeywordsÑimobile ipkmobile ipv4eipsecemipv4kobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateHX7Ôgrankingëcrfcd5265irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Mobile IPv4 Traversal across IPsec-Based VPN GatewaysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extension for Event State Publication describes a mechanism with which a presence user agent is able to publish presence information to a presence agent.  Using the Presence Information Data Format (PIDF), each presence publication contains full state, regardless of how much of that information has actually changed since the previous update.  As a consequence, updating a sizeable presence document with small changes bears a considerable overhead and is therefore inefficient.  Especially with low bandwidth and high latency links, this can constitute a considerable burden to the system.  This memo defines a solution that aids in reducing the impact of those constraints and increases transport efficiency by introducing a mechanism that allows for publication of partial presence information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehA. Niemi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Lonnfors¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamekE. LeppanenddatejÃéhfilenamegrfc5264eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127160hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH…j>grankingêcrfcd5264irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Publication of Partial Presence InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBy default, presence delivered using the presence event package for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is represented in the Presence Information Data Format (PIDF).  A PIDF document contains a set of elements, each representing a different aspect of the presence being reported.  When any subset of the elements change, even just a single element, a new document containing the full set of elements is delivered.  This memo defines an extension allowing delivery of only the presence data that has actually changed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Lonnfors¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepJ. Costa-Requena¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekE. Leppanen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelH. Khartabilddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5263eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127159hkeywordsÇdpidfx presence information data formatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH…j+grankingècrfcd5263irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extension for Partial Notification of Presence InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyNThe Presence Information Document Format (PIDF) specifies the baseline XML-based format for describing presence information.  One of the characteristics of the PIDF is that the document always needs to carry all presence information available for the presentity.  In some environments where low bandwidth and high latency links can exist, it is often beneficial to limit the amount of transported information over the network.  This document introduces a new MIME type that enables transporting of either only the changed parts or the full PIDF-based presence information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Lonnfors¢kaffiliationjIndividualdnamekE. Leppanen¢kaffiliationrEricsson AustraliadnamelH. Khartabil¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemJ. UrpalainenddatejÃçhfilenamegrfc5262eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127158hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH…jgrankingécrfcd5262irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFPresence Information Data Format (PIDF) Extension for Partial PresencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒExtensible Markup Language (XML) documents are widely used as containers for the exchange and storage of arbitrary data in today's systems.  In order to send changes to an XML document, an entire copy of the new version must be sent, unless there is a means of indicating only the portions that have changed.  This document describes an XML patch framework utilizing XML Path language (XPath) selectors.  These selector values and updated new data content constitute the basis of patch operations described in this document.  In addition to them, with basic &lt;add&gt;, &lt;replace&gt;, and &lt;remove&gt; directives a set of patches can then be applied to update an existing XML document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamemJ. UrpalainenddatejÃçhfilenamegrfc5261eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127157hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateH…jgrankingçcrfcd5261irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlAn Extensible Markup Language (XML) Patch Operations Framework Utilizing XML Path Language (XPath) SelectorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`This document describes the "date" and "index" extensions to the Sieve email filtering language.  The "date" extension gives Sieve the ability to test date and time values in various ways.  The "index" extension provides a means to limit header and address tests to specific instances of header fields when header fields are repeated. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehN. Freedddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5260eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127156hkeywordsÑdsmtpeesmtpddateeindexkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHvï.grankingåcrfcd5260irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Sieve Email Filtering: Date and Index ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-CONVERT defines extensions to IMAP allowing clients to request adaptation and/or transcoding of attachments.  Clients can specify the conversion details or allow servers to decide based on knowledge of client capabilities, on user or administrator preferences, or on server settings. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiP. Coatesddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5259eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127155hkeywordsÖdIMAPhLemonadegCONVERTjconversionktranscodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÿ#grankingãcrfcd5259irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Internet Message Access Protocol - CONVERT ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂIMAP4 has two commands for listing mailboxes: LIST and LSUB.  As we have added extensions, such as Mailbox Referrals, that have required specialized lists we have had to expand the number of list commands, since each extension must add its function to both LIST and LSUB, and these commands are not, as they are defined, extensible.  If we've needed the extensions to work together, we've had to add a set of commands to mix the different options, the set increasing in size with each new extension.  This document describes an extension to the base LIST command that will allow these additions to be done with mutually compatible options to the LIST command, avoiding the exponential increase in specialized list commands. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson Research CenterdnamehB. Leiba¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej”≈hfilenamegrfc5258eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgimapextdfullx4imapext - Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondnamex*Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127154hkeywordsÖeimap4dlistdlsubmextended listeemailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHM´”grankingäcrfcd5258irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDInternet Message Access Protocol version 4 - LIST Command ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThe ANNOTATE extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol permits clients and servers to maintain "meta data" for messages, or individual message parts, stored in a mailbox on the server. For example, this can be used to attach comments and other useful information to a message. It is also possible to attach annotations to specific parts of a message, so that, for example, they could be marked as seen, or important, or a comment added.

Note that this document was the product of a WG that had good consensus on how to approach the problem. Nevertheless, the WG felt it did not have enough information on implementation and deployment hurdles to meet all of the requirements of a Proposed Standard. The IETF solicits implementations and implementation reports in order to make further progress.

Implementers should be aware that this specification may change in an incompatible manner when going to Proposed Standard status. However, any incompatible changes will result in a new capability name being used to prevent problems with any deployments of the experimental extension. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationuQUALCOMM IncorporateddnamejR. Gellensddatej”Ôhfilenamegrfc5257eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgimapextdfullx4imapext - Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondnamex*Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127153hkeywordsÅdimapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHSügrankingâcrfcd5257irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Internet Message Access Protocol - ANNOTATE ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÚThis document describes the base-level server-based sorting and threading extensions to the IMAP protocol.  These extensions provide substantial performance improvements for IMAP clients that offer sorted and threaded views. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPanda ProgrammingdnamejM. Crispin¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamelK. Murchisonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5256eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgimapextdfullx4imapext - Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondnamex*Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127152hkeywordsÇxordered subject referencesoimap capabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHM´“grankingàcrfcd5256irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Internet Message Access Protocol - SORT and THREAD ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5957∂habstractyInternet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) version 4rev1 has basic support for non-ASCII characters in mailbox names and search substrings.  It also supports non-ASCII message headers and content encoded as specified by Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME).  This specification defines a collection of IMAP extensions that improve international support including language negotiation for international error text, translations for namespace prefixes, and comparator negotiation for search, sort, and thread. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newman¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmhHdnamenA. Gulbrandsen¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5255eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgimapextdfullx4imapext - Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondnamex*Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127151hkeywordsÉdimapfimapv4eimap4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHM´—grankingácrfcd5255irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Internet Message Access Protocol InternationalizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document describes the necessary requirements to allow a service provider to extend the reach of pseudowires across multiple domains.  These domains can be autonomous systems under one provider administrative control, IGP areas in one autonomous system, different autonomous systems under the administrative control of two or more service providers, or administratively established pseudowire domains.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehN. Bitar¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martiniddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc5254eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-127150hkeywordsájPseudowiredPWE3mmulti-segmenteMS-PWeSS-PWdS-PEdT-PEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH˛bgrankingÜcrfcd5254irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGRequirements for Multi-Segment Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document provides an applicability statement on the use of Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) protocols and mechanisms to support Basic Mode Layer 1 Virtual Private Networks (L1VPNs).

L1VPNs provide customer services and connectivity at Layer 1 over Layer 1 networks. The operation of L1VPNs is divided into the Basic Mode and the Enhanced Mode, where the Basic Mode of operation does not feature any exchange of routing information between the Layer 1 network and the customer domain. This document examines how GMPLS protocols can be used to satisfy the requirements of a Basic Mode L1VPN. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takedaddatej”˛hfilenamegrfc5253eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel1vpndfullx(l1vpn - Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127149hkeywordsÉegmplsx)generalized multiprotocol label switchinghl1vpn bmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHmBÉgrankingÖcrfcd5253irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNApplicability Statement for Layer 1 Virtual Private Network (L1VPN) Basic ModedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄This document defines an Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) based Layer 1 Virtual Private Network (L1VPN) auto-discovery mechanism.  This mechanism enables provider edge (PE) devices using OSPF to dynamically learn about the existence of each other, and attributes of configured customer edge (CE) links and their associations with L1VPNs.  This document builds on the L1VPN framework and requirements and provides a L1VPN basic mode auto-discovery mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwADVA Optical NetworkingdnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationtLabN Consulting, LLCdnameiL. Bergerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5252eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel1vpndfullx(l1vpn - Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127148hkeywordsÉxopen shortest path firstel1vpnxlayer 1 virtual private networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHmBqgrankingÑcrfcd5252irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%OSPF-Based Layer 1 VPN Auto-DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThis document describes the Basic Mode of Layer 1 VPNs (L1VPNs).  L1VPN Basic Mode (L1VPN BM) is a port-based VPN.  In L1VPN Basic Mode, the basic unit of service is a Label Switched Path (LSP) between a pair of customer ports within a given VPN port topology.  This document defines the operational model using either provisioning or a VPN auto-discovery mechanism, and the signaling extensions for the L1VPN BM. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Rabbat¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Bergerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5251eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel1vpndfullx(l1vpn - Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127147hkeywordsÉwvirtual private networkel1vpnhl1vpn bmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHmB0grankingÉcrfcd5251irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevLayer 1 VPN Basic ModedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·This document defines enhancements to the OSPF protocol to support a new class of link state advertisements (LSAs) called Opaque LSAs. Opaque LSAs provide a generalized mechanism to allow for the future extensibility of OSPF. Opaque LSAs consist of a standard LSA header followed by application-specific information. The information field may be used directly by OSPF or by other applications. Standard OSPF link-state database flooding mechanisms are used to distribute Opaque LSAs to all or some limited portion of the OSPF topology.

This document replaces RFC 2370 and adds to it a mechanism to enable an OSPF router to validate Autonomous System (AS)-scope Opaque LSAs originated outside of the router's OSPF area. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationdAdvadnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehA. Zinin¢kaffiliationtAcoustra ProductionsdnameiR. Coltunddatej”Æhfilenamegrfc5250eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127146hkeywordsÑhOSPF-LSAxopen shortest path firstxlink state advertisementkopaque lsaskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHmBgrankingÇcrfcd5250irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe OSPF Opaque LSA OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty◊This memo references three annotated templates for IETF documents that contain the definition of MIB modules.  It is intended to reduce the work of the editors of such documents, making these documents more uniform and easier to read and review, thus furthering the quality of such documents and expediting their publication.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxHuawei Technologies (USA)dnamemD. Harringtonddatej‘	hfilenamegrfc5249eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127145hkeywordsÖrnetwork managementxmanagement information basecmibesmiv2htemplatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHmBgrankingÅcrfcd5249irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberãetotaletitlex4Templates for Internet-Drafts Containing MIB ModulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty™The specification for enhanced mail system status codes, RFC 3463, establishes a new code model and lists a collection of status codes.  While it anticipated that more codes would be added over time, it did not provide an explicit mechanism for registering and tracking those codes.  This document specifies an IANA registry for mail system enhanced status codes, and initializes that registry with the codes so far established in published standards-track documents, as well as other codes that have become established in the industry.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5248eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127144hkeywordsÅxsimple mail transfer protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHiÇ¸grankingÄcrfcd5248irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberäetotaletitlex5A Registry for SMTP Enhanced Mail System Status CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), defined in RFC 3748, enables extensible network access authentication.  This document specifies the EAP key hierarchy and provides a framework for the transport and usage of keying material and parameters generated by EAP authentication algorithms, known as "methods".  It also provides a detailed system-level security analysis, describing the conditions under which the key management guidelines described in RFC 4962 can be satisfied. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehD. Simon¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5247eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymceapdfullx(eap - Extensible Authentication Protocoldnamex"Extensible Authentication Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127143hkeywordsÉx(extensible network access authenticationmkey hierarchygmethodskobsoletedByÄepagesOopublicationDateH•Ï◊grankingcrfcd5247irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Key Management FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8940∂habstracty=This document specifies Version 1.2 of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol.  The TLS protocol provides communications security over the Internet.  The protocol allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, or message forgery. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiT. Dierks¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorladdatej‘hfilenamegrfc5246eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127142hkeywordsÜdideaxinternational data algorithmisymmetricxtransport protocol layernauthenticationgprivacykobsoletedByÅd8446epageshopublicationDateH•ÏÍgranking~crfcd5246irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.2dtypecrfciupdatedByåd7905d7685d6176d5878d5746d7627d7465d9155d7919d7568d7507d8447∂habstractyÊThis document describes a protocol for Network Address Translator (NAT) traversal for UDP-based multimedia sessions established with the offer/answer model.  This protocol is called Interactive Connectivity Establishment (ICE).  ICE makes use of the Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) protocol and its extension, Traversal Using Relay NAT (TURN).  ICE can be used by any protocol utilizing the offer/answer model, such as the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkjdrosen.netdnamelJ. RosenbergddatejÃghfilenamegrfc5245eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127141hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd8839d8445epagesuopublicationDateK⁄–granking}crfcd5245irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇInteractive Connectivity Establishment (ICE): A Protocol for Network Address Translator (NAT) Traversal for Offer/Answer ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6336∂habstracty˘This memo updates RFC 4733 to add event codes for telephony signals used for channel-associated signalling when carried in the telephony event RTP payload.  It supersedes and adds to the original assignment of event codes for this purpose in Section 3.14 of RFC 2833.  As documented in Appendix A of RFC 4733, some of the RFC 2833 events have been deprecated because their specification was ambiguous, erroneous, or redundant.  In fact, the degree of change from Section 3.14 of RFC 2833 is such that implementations of the present document will be fully backward compatible with RFC 2833 implementations only in the case of full ABCD-bit signalling.  This document expands and improves the coverage of signalling systems compared to RFC 2833. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylorddatej”°hfilenamegrfc5244eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127140hkeywordsÇjevent codextelephony event rtp payloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHX7Ìgranking|crfcd5244irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Definition of Events for Channel-Oriented Telephony SignallingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyéThis document describes a backward-compatible optimization for the Database Exchange process in OSPFv2 and OSPFv3.  In this optimization, a router does not list a Link State Advertisement (LSA) in Database Description packets sent to a neighbor, if the same or a more recent instance of the LSA was listed in a Database Description packet already received from the neighbor.  This optimization reduces Database Description overhead by about 50% in large networks.  This optimization does not affect synchronization, since it only omits unnecessary information from Database Description packets.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqSRI InternationaldnamehR. OgierddatejÃ#hfilenamegrfc5243eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127139hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH?<µgranking{crfcd5243irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0OSPF Database Exchange Summary List OptimizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9454¥habstractyÉMany issues have been identified with the use of general-purpose character sets for internationalized domain names and similar purposes.  This memo describes a fully unified coded character set for scripts based on Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, and Chinese (CJK) characters.  It is not a complete specification of that character set.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrandddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5242eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43456hkeywordsÜcidnelatinegreekicyrilllicgchinesexinternationalized domain nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÚi†grankingzcrfcd5242irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGA Generalized Unified Character Code: Western European and CJK SectionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyˆThis document proposes a new revenue source for the IETF to support standardization activities: protocol field naming rights, i.e., the association of commercial brands with protocol fields.  This memo describes a process for assignment of rights and explores some of the issues associated with the process.  Individuals or organizations that wish to purchase naming rights for one or more protocol fields are expected to follow this process.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamegA. Falk¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5241eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43455hkeywordsÇkapril foolssfield naming rightskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÚiögrankingycrfcd5241irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexNaming Rights in IETF ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for managing the Bootstrap Router (BSR) mechanism for PIM (Protocol Independent Multicast). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInfosys Technologies Ltd.dnamehB. Joshi¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Bijlaniddatej””hfilenamegrfc5240eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-127138hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basepbootstrap routercbsrkPIM-BSR-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHEƒÉgrankingxcrfcd5240irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) Bootstrap Router MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document defines the framework for Centralized Conferencing.  The framework allows participants using various call signaling protocols, such as SIP, H.323, Jabber, Q.931 or ISDN User Part (ISUP), to exchange media in a centralized unicast conference.  The Centralized Conferencing Framework defines logical entities and naming conventions.  The framework also outlines a set of conferencing protocols, which are complementary to the call signaling protocols, for building advanced conferencing applications.  The framework binds all the defined components together for the benefit of builders of conferencing systems. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiM. Barnes¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamejC. Boulton¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levinddatej”õhfilenamegrfc5239eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127137hkeywordsÇncall signalingocall signallingkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateHM´–grankingwcrfcd5239irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A Framework for Centralized ConferencingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document specifies the use of Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) over the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP).  DTLS provides communications privacy for applications that use datagram transport protocols and allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping and detect tampering or message forgery.  DCCP is a transport protocol that provides a congestion-controlled unreliable datagram service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnSonus NetworksdnameiT. Phelanddatej”àhfilenamegrfc5238eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127136hkeywordsÇctlsrtransport protocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateH?<∑grankingvcrfcd5238irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) over the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∑habstractyòThis document revises the IANA guidelines for allocating new Protocol field values in IPv4 header.  It modifies the rules specified in RFC 2780 by removing the Expert Review option.  The change will also affect the allocation of Next Header field values in IPv6.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5237eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127135hkeywordsákipv4 headerqnext header fieldhinternethassignedgnumbersiauthoritybIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG«RÇgrankingucrfcd5237irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber%etotaletitlex1IANA Allocation Guidelines for the Protocol FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty˝This document presents two improved metrics for packet reordering, namely, Reorder Density (RD) and Reorder Buffer-occupancy Density (RBD). A threshold is used to clearly define when a packet is considered lost, to bound computational complexity at O(N), and to keep the memory requirement for evaluation independent of N, where N is the length of the packet sequence. RD is a comprehensive metric that captures the characteristics of reordering, while RBD evaluates the sequences from the point of view of recovery from reordering.

These metrics are simple to compute yet comprehensive in their characterization of packet reordering. The measures are robust and orthogonal to packet loss and duplication. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars EggertgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxColorado State UniversitydnamemA. Jayasumana¢kaffiliationuDeutsche Telekom LabsdnamejN. Piratla¢kaffiliationxColorado State UniversitydnamehT. Banka¢kaffiliationxAgilent Technologies, Inc.dnamegA. Bare¢kaffiliationxAgilent Technologies, Inc.dnamejR. Whitnerddatej”Ëhfilenamegrfc5236eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127134hkeywordsÑoreorder densitybrdx reorder buffer-occupancy densitycrbdkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHEƒygrankingtcrfcd5236irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex"Improved Packet Reordering MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThe Sieve email filtering language "spamtest", "spamtestplus", and "virustest" extensions permit users to use simple, portable commands for spam and virus tests on email messages.  Each extension provides a new test using matches against numeric "scores".  It is the responsibility of the underlying Sieve implementation to do the actual checks that result in proper input to the tests. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. Dabooddatej”¬hfilenamegrfc5235eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127133hkeywordsÑhspamtestlspamtestplusivirustestfscoreskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGé`grankingscrfcd5235irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Sieve Email Filtering: Spamtest and Virustest ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyàInternet technical specifications often need to define a formal syntax.  Over the years, a modified version of Backus-Naur Form (BNF), called Augmented BNF (ABNF), has been popular among many Internet specifications.  The current specification documents ABNF.  It balances compactness and simplicity with reasonable representational power.  The differences between standard BNF and ABNF involve naming rules, repetition, alternatives, order-independence, and value ranges.  This specification also supplies additional rule definitions and encoding for a core lexical analyzer of the type common to several Internet specifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationiTHUS plc.dnamejP. Overellddatej‘␀hfilenamegrfc5234eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127132hkeywordsÜdABNFpbackus-naur formxaugmented backus-naur formprule definitionshencodingucore lexical analyzerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG¢tægrankingrcrfcd5234irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberDetotaletitlex-Augmented BNF for Syntax Specifications: ABNFdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7405∂habstractykOn email systems that allow for 'subaddressing' or 'detailed addressing' (e.g., "ken+sieve@example.org"), it is sometimes desirable to make comparisons against these sub-parts of addresses.  This document defines an extension to the Sieve Email Filtering Language that allows users to compare against the user and detail sub-parts of an address. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamelK. Murchisonddatej”¿hfilenamegrfc5233eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127131hkeywordsÑmsubaddressingsdetailed addressinge:userg:detailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGé`lgrankingqcrfcd5233irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Sieve Email Filtering: Subaddress ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíRecent discussions have shown that it is desirable to set different IMAP (RFC 3501) flags on message delivery. This can be done, for example, by a Sieve interpreter that works as a part of a Mail Delivery Agent.

This document describes an extension to the Sieve mail filtering language for setting IMAP flags. The extension allows setting of both IMAP system flags and IMAP keywords. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej”πhfilenamegrfc5232eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127130hkeywordsÖdimapx(internet message access control protocoljimap flagsqimap system flagsmimap keywordskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGé`bgrankingpcrfcd5232irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Sieve Email Filtering: Imap4flags ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document describes the RELATIONAL extension to the Sieve mail filtering language defined in RFC 3028. This extension extends existing conditional tests in Sieve to allow relational operators. In addition to testing their content, it also allows for testing of the number of entities in header and envelope fields.

This document obsoletes RFC 3431. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson Research CenterdnamelW. Segmuller¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson Research CenterdnamehB. Leibaddatej”öhfilenamegrfc5231eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127129hkeywordsÅtrelational operatorskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateGé`Qgrankingocrfcd5231irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Sieve Email Filtering: Relational ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an extension to the Sieve email filtering language for an autoresponder similar to that of the Unix "vacation" command for replying to messages.  Various safety features are included to prevent problems such as message loops. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelT. Showalter¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehN. Freedddatej”ñhfilenamegrfc5230eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127128hkeywordsÅmautoresponderkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGé`Igrankingncrfcd5230irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Sieve Email Filtering: Vacation ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8580∂habstractyàIn advanced mail filtering rule sets, it is useful to keep state or configuration details across rules.  This document updates the Sieve filtering language (RFC 5228) with an extension to support variables.  The extension changes the interpretation of strings, adds an action to store data in variables, and supplies a new test so that the value of a string can be examined. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrUniversity of OslodnamehK. HommeddatejÀ’hfilenamegrfc5229eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127127hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGé`@grankingmcrfcd5229irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Sieve Email Filtering: Variables ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5173∂habstracty9This document describes a language for filtering email messages at time of final delivery.  It is designed to be implementable on either a mail client or mail server.  It is meant to be extensible, simple, and independent of access protocol, mail architecture, and operating system.  It is suitable for running on a mail server where users may not be allowed to execute arbitrary programs, such as on black box Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) servers, as the base language has no variables, loops, or ability to shell out to external programs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhSendmaildnamekP. Guenther¢kaffiliation`dnamelT. ShowalterddatejÃdhfilenamegrfc5228eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127126hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateGé`5grankinglcrfcd5228irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Sieve: An Email Filtering LanguagedtypecrfciupdatedByÑd5429d6785d9042d5229∂habstracty:When two hosts on the same link attempt to use the same IPv4 address at the same time (except in rare special cases where this has been arranged by prior coordination), problems ensue for one or both hosts.  This document describes (i) a simple precaution that a host can take in advance to help prevent this misconfiguration from happening, and (ii) if this misconfiguration does occur, a simple mechanism by which a host can passively detect, after the fact, that it has happened, so that the host or administrator may respond to rectify the problem. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjApple Inc.dnamekS. Cheshireddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5227eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127125hkeywordsÅxinternet protocol version 4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHmA¯grankingkcrfcd5227irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPv4 Address Conflict DetectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMany protocols make use of identifiers consisting of constants and other well-known values. Even after a protocol has been defined and deployment has begun, new values may need to be assigned (e.g., for a new option type in DHCP, or a new encryption or authentication transform for IPsec). To ensure that such quantities have consistent values and interpretations across all implementations, their assignment must be administered by a central authority. For IETF protocols, that role is provided by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).

In order for IANA to manage a given namespace prudently, it needs guidelines describing the conditions under which new values can be assigned or when modifications to existing values can be made. If IANA is expected to play a role in the management of a namespace, IANA must be given clear and concise instructions describing that role. This document discusses issues that should be considered in formulating a policy for assigning values to a namespace and provides guidelines for authors on the specific text that must be included in documents that place demands on IANA.

This document obsoletes RFC 2434. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrandddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5226eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127124hkeywordsÜx#internet assigned numbers authorityfvaluesoimplementationsjcode pointqprotocol constantrprotocol parameterkobsoletedByÅd8126epagesopublicationDateH2Ògrankingjcrfcd5226irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Guidelines for Writing an IANA Considerations Section in RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document specifies ROHC (Robust Header Compression) profiles that efficiently compress RTP/UDP/IP (Real-Time Transport Protocol, User Datagram Protocol, Internet Protocol), RTP/UDP-Lite/IP (User Datagram Protocol Lite), UDP/IP, UDP-Lite/IP, IP and ESP/IP (Encapsulating Security Payload) headers.

This specification defines a second version of the profiles found in RFC 3095, RFC 3843 and RFC 4019; it supersedes their definition, but does not obsolete them.

The ROHCv2 profiles introduce a number of simplifications to the rules and algorithms that govern the behavior of the compression endpoints. It also defines robustness mechanisms that may be used by a compressor implementation to increase the probability of decompression success when packets can be lost and/or reordered on the ROHC channel. Finally, the ROHCv2 profiles define their own specific set of header formats, using the ROHC formal notation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletier¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekK. Sandlundddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5225eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-127123hkeywordsÑdrohcd3095d3843d4019kobsoletedByÄepages|opublicationDateHº`grankingicrfcd5225irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYRObust Header Compression Version 2 (ROHCv2): Profiles for RTP, UDP, IP, ESP and UDP-LitedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document describes the need for a new IANA Diameter Command Code to be used in a vendor-specific new application for invocation of Policy Processing (Policy Evaluation, or Evaluation and Enforcement).  This application is needed as one of the implementations of the Open Mobile Alliance (OMA) Policy Evaluation, Enforcement and Management (PEEM) enabler, namely for the PEM-1 interface used to send a request/response for Policy Processing.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamejM. Brennerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5224eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127122hkeywordsÜqpolicy evaluationxor evaluation and enforcementepem-1dpeemcomatopen mobile alliancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÌ^÷grankinghcrfcd5224irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Diameter Policy Processing ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThe Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Protocol describes an XML- based protocol for mapping service identifiers and geospatial or civic location information to service contact Uniform Resource Locators (URLs). LoST servers can be located anywhere, but a placement closer to the end host, e.g., in the access network, is desirable. In disaster situations with intermittent network connectivity, such a LoST server placement provides benefits regarding the resiliency of emergency service communication.

This document describes how a LoST client can discover a LoST server using the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5223eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-127121hkeywordsÉomapping serviceqemergency servicewemergency communicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHòÿâgrankinggcrfcd5223irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoDiscovering Location-to-Service Translation (LoST) Servers Using the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document describes an XML-based protocol for mapping service identifiers and geodetic or civic location information to service contact URIs.  In particular, it can be used to determine the location-appropriate Public Safety Answering Point (PSAP) for emergency services. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnameiT. Hardie¢kaffiliationx&American Registry for Internet NumbersdnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5222eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-127120hkeywordsÇremergency servicesvemergency call routingkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDateHòÿtgrankingfcrfcd5222irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0LoST: A Location-to-Service Translation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8917d9036d6848∂habstracty	There are some problematic cases when using the default address selection mechanism that RFC 3484 defines.  This document describes additional requirements that operate with RFC 3484 to solve the problems.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamelA. Matsumoto¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekT. Fujisaki¢kaffiliationmIntec NetcorednameiR. Hiromi¢kaffiliationmINTEC SystemsdnamekK. Kanayamaddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5221eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127119hkeywordsÅxdefault address selectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH}-pgrankingecrfcd5221irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Requirements for Address Selection MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“A single physical link can have multiple prefixes assigned to it.  In that environment, end hosts might have multiple IP addresses and be required to use them selectively.  RFC 3484 defines default source and destination address selection rules and is implemented in a variety of OSs.  But, it has been too difficult to use operationally for several reasons.  In some environments where multiple prefixes are assigned on a single physical link, the host using the default address selection rules will experience some trouble in communication.  This document describes the possible problems that end hosts could encounter in an environment with multiple prefixes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamelA. Matsumoto¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekT. Fujisaki¢kaffiliationmIntec NetcorednameiR. Hiromi¢kaffiliationmINTEC SystemsdnamekK. Kanayamaddatej‘ hfilenamegrfc5220eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127118hkeywordsÅqmultiple prefixeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH}-mgrankingdcrfcd5220irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzProblem Statement for Default Address Selection in Multi-Prefix Environments: Operational Issues of RFC 3484 Default RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°This document describes an RTP (Real-Time Protocol) payload format for transporting MPEG (Moving Picture Experts Group) 1 or 2, layer III audio (commonly known as "MP3").  This format is an alternative to that described in RFC 2250, and performs better if there is packet loss.  This document obsoletes RFC 3119, correcting typographical errors in the "SDP usage" section and pseudo-code appendices. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLive Networks, Inc.dnamelR. Finlaysonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5219eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127117hkeywordsÑrreal time protocolrreal-time protocoldmpegxmoving picture experts groupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG¥Ù±grankingccrfcd5219irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5A More Loss-Tolerant RTP Payload Format for MP3 AudiodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyèThe Internet community has specified a large number of protocols to date, and these protocols have achieved varying degrees of success.  Based on case studies, this document attempts to ascertain factors that contribute to or hinder a protocol's success.  It is hoped that these observations can serve as guidance for future protocol work.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationcIABdnamehB. AbobaddatejÃéhfilenamegrfc5218eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-127116hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHÅ)Õgrankingbcrfcd5218irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex%What Makes for a Successful Protocol?dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe objective of this document is to establish a terminology framework and to suggest the operational requirements of Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) domain for interoperability of multi-domain Public Key Infrastructure, where each PKI domain is operated under a distinct policy.  This document describes the relationships between Certification Authorities (CAs), provides the definition and requirements for PKI domains, and discusses typical models of multi-domain PKI.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeSECOMdnamekM. Shimaoka¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamekN. Hastings¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamejR. Nielsenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5217eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127115hkeywordsÉcpkipmulti-domain pkijpki domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHmAÁgrankingacrfcd5217irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFMemorandum for Multi-Domain Public Key Infrastructure InteroperabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), defined in RFC 3748, provides support for multiple authentication methods. Transport Layer Security (TLS) provides for mutual authentication, integrity-protected ciphersuite negotiation, and key exchange between two endpoints. This document defines EAP-TLS, which includes support for certificate-based mutual authentication and key derivation.

This document obsoletes RFC 2716. A summary of the changes between this document and RFC 2716 is available in Appendix A. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehD. Simon¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehR. Hurstddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5216eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcemudfullwemu - EAP Method UpdatednameqEAP Method Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-127114hkeywordsÖx"extensible authentication protocolnpoint-to-pointxlink control compressionjextensiblextransport level securitykobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateG‰Fgranking`crfcd5216irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#The EAP-TLS Authentication ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9965d8996d9190∂habstractyæThis document describes an RTP payload format for transporting Vorbis encoded audio. It details the RTP encapsulation mechanism for raw Vorbis data and the delivery mechanisms for the decoder probability model (referred to as a codebook), as well as other setup information.

Also included within this memo are media type registrations and the details necessary for the use of Vorbis with the Session Description Protocol (SDP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdXiphdnamejL. Barbatoddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5215eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127113hkeywordsÇxrealtime transport protocolhcodebookkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH£∞granking_crfcd5215irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+RTP Payload Format for Vorbis Encoded AudiodtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyUThe Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP) connects dual-stack (IPv6/IPv4) nodes over IPv4 networks.  ISATAP views the IPv4 network as a link layer for IPv6 and supports an automatic tunneling abstraction similar to the Non-Broadcast Multiple Access (NBMA) model.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtBoeing Phantom WorksdnamejF. Templin¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems K.K.dnamejT. Gleeson¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiD. Thalerddatej”Èhfilenamegrfc5214eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127112hkeywordsÖdipv6dipv4xnon-broadcast multiple accessdnbmajdual-stackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‰Fgranking^crfcd5214irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙNetwork-based mobility management enables IP mobility for a host without requiring its participation in any mobility-related signaling.  The network is responsible for managing IP mobility on behalf of the host.  The mobility entities in the network are responsible for tracking the movements of the host and initiating the required mobility signaling on its behalf.  This specification describes a network-based mobility management protocol and is referred to as Proxy Mobile IPv6. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamemS. Gundavelli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationhWichorusdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehB. Patilddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5213eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127111hkeywordsÅsmobility managementkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDateH£ûgranking]crfcd5213irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqProxy Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7864d6543∂habstractyÂMost of the initial efforts to utilize Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) have been related to environments hosting devices with a single switching capability. The complexity raised by the control of such data planes is similar to that seen in classical IP/MPLS networks. By extending MPLS to support multiple switching technologies, GMPLS provides a comprehensive framework for the control of a multi-layered network of either a single switching technology or multiple switching technologies.

In GMPLS, a switching technology domain defines a region, and a network of multiple switching types is referred to in this document as a multi-region network (MRN). When referring in general to a layered network, which may consist of either single or multiple regions, this document uses the term multi-layer network (MLN). This document defines a framework for GMPLS based multi-region / multi-layer networks and lists a set of functional requirements. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Shiomoto¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelM. Vigoureux¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekD. Brungardddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5212eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127110hkeywordsÇpgeneralized mplstswitching technologykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHuK®granking\crfcd5212irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLRequirements for GMPLS-Based Multi-Region and Multi-Layer Networks (MRN/MLN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx‰This memo provides one possible plan for transitioning the Internet from a predominantly IPv4-based connectivity model to a predominantly IPv6-based connectivity model.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon BonicagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. CurranddatejÀçhfilenamegrfc5211eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127109hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHvï1granking[crfcd5211irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexAn Internet Transition PlandtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöBecause the Internet forwards packets according to the IP destination address, packet forwarding typically takes place without inspection of the source address and malicious attacks have been launched using spoofed source addresses.  In an effort to enhance the Internet with IP source address validation, a prototype implementation of the IP Source Address Validation Architecture (SAVA) was created and an evaluation was conducted on an IPv6 network.  This document reports on the prototype implementation and the test results, as well as the lessons and insights gained from experimentation.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Wu¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeJ. Bi¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnamefG. Ren¢kaffiliationsTsinghua UniversitydnameeK. Xu¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekM. Williamsddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5210eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127108hkeywordsÖxSource Address ValidationxSource Addressing SpoofingpNetwork SecuritygTestbeddIPv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHb‘SgrankingZcrfcd5210irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQA Source Address Validation Architecture (SAVA) Testbed and Deployment ExperiencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This document defines the problem statement, scope, and protocol requirements between the components of the NEA (Network Endpoint Assessment) reference model.  NEA provides owners of networks (e.g., an enterprise offering remote access) a mechanism to evaluate the posture of a system.  This may take place during the request for network access and/or subsequently at any time while connected to the network.  The learned posture information can then be applied to a variety of compliance-oriented decisions.  The posture information is frequently useful for detecting systems that are lacking or have out-of-date security protection mechanisms such as: anti-virus and host-based firewall software.  In order to provide context for the requirements, a reference model and terminology are introduced.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhSymantecdnamekP. Sangster¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekH. Khosravi¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamegM. Mani¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Narayan¢kaffiliationnNevis NetworksdnamehJ. Tardoddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5209eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcneadfullx!nea - Network Endpoint AssessmentdnamexNetwork Endpoint Assessmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-127107hkeywordsågPosturekRemediationlreassessmentiValidatoriCollectorfBrokerjcompliancegprivacyjdisclosurefreplayetrustfpolicykobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateHd¢grankingYcrfcd5209irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Network Endpoint Assessment (NEA): Overview and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶This document represents a republication of PKCS #8 v1.2 from RSA Laboratories' Public Key Cryptography Standard (PKCS) series. Change control is transferred to the IETF. The body of this document, except for the security considerations section, is taken directly from the PKCS #8 v1.2 specification.

This document describes a syntax for private-key information. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcEMCdnamejB. Kaliskiddatej”jhfilenamegrfc5208eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127106hkeywordsÉprsa laboratoriesrprivate-key syntaxnchange controlkobsoletedByÅd5958epagesopublicationDateH?<¥grankingXcrfcd5208irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexePublic-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) #8: Private-Key Information Syntax Specification Version 1.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyòThe Host Identity Protocol (HIP) changes the way in which two Internet hosts communicate.  One key advantage over other schemes is that HIP does not require modifications to the traditional network- layer functionality of the Internet, i.e., its routers.  In the current Internet, however, many devices other than routers modify the traditional network-layer behavior of the Internet.  These "middleboxes" are intermediary devices that perform functions other than the standard functions of an IP router on the datagram path between source and destination hosts.  Whereas some types of middleboxes may not interfere with HIP at all, others can affect some aspects of HIP communication, and others can render HIP communication impossible.  This document discusses the problems associated with HIP communication across network paths that include specific types of middleboxes, namely, network address translators and firewalls.  It identifies and discusses issues in the current HIP specifications that affect communication across these types of middleboxes.  This document is a product of the IRTF HIP Research Group.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggertddatej”óhfilenamegrfc5207eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehiprgdfullxhiprg - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebrgbidjdoc-127105hkeywordsàcHIPvhost identity protocoluhost identity payloadmNAT traversalsmiddlebox traversalrfirewall traversalpID locator splitqproblem statementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH	(ÁgrankingWcrfcd5207irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexONAT and Firewall Traversal Issues of Host Identity Protocol (HIP) CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document defines mobility and multihoming extensions to the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  Specifically, this document defines a general "LOCATOR" parameter for HIP messages that allows for a HIP host to notify peers about alternate addresses at which it may be reached.  This document also defines elements of procedure for mobility of a HIP host -- the process by which a host dynamically changes the primary locator that it uses to receive packets.  While the same LOCATOR parameter can also be used to support end-host multihoming, detailed procedures are left for further study.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationrThe Boeing CompanydnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamegC. Vogt¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamehJ. Arkkoddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5206eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127104hkeywordsÑchipvmultihoming extensionssmobility extentionsglocatorkobsoletedByÅd8046epages(opublicationDateH	(‹grankingVcrfcd5206irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAEnd-Host Mobility and Multihoming with the Host Identity ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document specifies a new resource record (RR) for the Domain Name System (DNS), and how to use it with the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  This RR allows a HIP node to store in the DNS its Host Identity (HI, the public component of the node public-private key pair), Host Identity Tag (HIT, a truncated hash of its public key), and the Domain Names of its rendezvous servers (RVSs).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research Nomadic LabdnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnamekJ. Laganierddatej”Ùhfilenamegrfc5205eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127103hkeywordsÖchipvhost identity protocoluhost identity payloadcdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÅd8005epagesopublicationDateH	(’grankingUcrfcd5205irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Domain Name System (DNS) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèThis document defines a rendezvous extension for the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  The rendezvous extension extends HIP and the HIP registration extension for initiating communication between HIP nodes via HIP rendezvous servers.  Rendezvous servers improve reachability and operation when HIP nodes are multi-homed or mobile.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggertddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5204eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127102hkeywordsÉxhip registration extensionihip nodesthip redezvous serverkobsoletedByÅd8004epagesopublicationDateH	(–grankingTcrfcd5204irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Rendezvous ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˘This document specifies a registration mechanism for the Host Identity Protocol (HIP) that allows hosts to register with services, such as HIP rendezvous servers or middleboxes.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationdHIITdnamejT. Koponen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggertddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5203eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127101hkeywordsÅhregisterkobsoletedByÅd8003epagesopublicationDateH	(…grankingScrfcd5203irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Host Identity Protocol (HIP) Registration ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis memo specifies an Encapsulated Security Payload (ESP) based mechanism for transmission of user data packets, to be used with the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnameiP. Jokela¢kaffiliationhICSAlabsdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamekP. Nikanderddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5202eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127100hkeywordsÅquser data packetskobsoletedByÅd7402epagesopublicationDateH	(¿grankingRcrfcd5202irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeUsing the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) Transport Format with the Host Identity Protocol (HIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis memo specifies the details of the Host Identity Protocol (HIP).  HIP allows consenting hosts to securely establish and maintain shared IP-layer state, allowing separation of the identifier and locator roles of IP addresses, thereby enabling continuity of communications across IP address changes.  HIP is based on a Sigma-compliant Diffie- Hellman key exchange, using public key identifiers from a new Host Identity namespace for mutual peer authentication.  The protocol is designed to be resistant to denial-of-service (DoS) and man-in-the- middle (MitM) attacks.  When used together with another suitable security protocol, such as the Encapsulated Security Payload (ESP), it provides integrity protection and optional encryption for upper- layer protocols, such as TCP and UDP.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhICSAlabsdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnameiP. Jokela¢kaffiliationrThe Boeing CompanydnamelT. Hendersonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5201eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127099hkeywordsÑchipnip-layer statetintegrity protectionsoptional encryptionkobsoletedByÅd7401epageshopublicationDateH	(≤grankingQcrfcd5201irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevHost Identity ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6253∂habstractyOThe Internet today is in need of a standardized form for the transmission of internationalized "text" information, paralleling the specifications for the use of ASCII that date from the early days of the ARPANET.  This document specifies that format, using UTF-8 with normalization and specific line-ending sequences. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Padlipskyddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5198eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127098hkeywordsÇqinternationalizedeutf-8kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÏÑgrankingNcrfcd5198irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Unicode Format for Network InterchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸Multimedia Internet Keying (MIKEY) is a key management protocol that can be used for \%real-time applications. In particular, it has been defined focusing on the support of the Secure \%Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP). MIKEY itself is standardized within RFC 3830 and defines four key distribution methods. Moreover, it is defined to allow extensions of the protocol. As MIKEY becomes more and more accepted, extensions to the base protocol arise, especially in terms of additional key distribution methods but also in terms of payload enhancements.

This document provides an overview about the MIKEY base document in general as well as the existing extensions for MIKEY, which have been defined or are in the process of definition. It is intended as an additional source of information for developers or architects to provide more insight in use case scenarios and motivations as well as advantages and disadvantages for the different key distribution schemes. The use cases discussed in this document are strongly related to dedicated SIP call scenarios providing challenges for key management in general, among them media before Session Description Protocol (SDP) answer, forking, and shared key conferencing. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamehS. Fries¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamekD. Ignjaticddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5197eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127097hkeywordsÑnkey managementumedia stream securityjend-to-enddSRTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHPcgrankingMcrfcd5197irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWOn the Applicability of Various Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) Modes and ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÒPresence Information Data Format (PIDF) defines a common presence data format for Common Profile for Presence (CPP) compliant presence protocols.  This memo defines a PIDF extension to represent SIP User Agent capabilities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Lonnfors¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamegK. Kissddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5196eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127096hkeywordsÉxcommon presence data formatccppxcommon profile for presencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH…iÙgrankingLcrfcd5196irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) User Agent Capability Extension to Presence Information Data Format (PIDF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThe purpose of this document is to define a BGP-based auto-discovery mechanism for Layer-1 VPNs (L1VPNs).  The auto-discovery mechanism for L1VPNs allows the provider network devices to dynamically discover the set of Provider Edges (PEs) having ports attached to Customer Edge (CE) members of the same VPN.  That information is necessary for completing the signaling phase of L1VPN connections.  One main objective of a L1VPN auto-discovery mechanism is to support the "single-end provisioning" model, where addition of a new port to a given L1VPN would involve configuration changes only on the PE that has this port and on the CE that is connected to the PE via this port. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamenH. Ould-Brahim¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5195eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel1vpndfullx(l1vpn - Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127095hkeywordsÉsimport route targetel1vpnwsingle-end provisioningkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateHI–<grankingKcrfcd5195irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)BGP-Based Auto-Discovery for Layer-1 VPNsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThis document lists the essential requirements for real-time Text-over-IP (ToIP) and defines a framework for implementation of all required functions based on the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and the Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP).  This includes interworking between Text-over-IP and existing text telephony on the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) and other networks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekA. van Wijk¢kaffiliation`dnameiG. Gybelsddatej”Åhfilenamegrfc5194eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127094hkeywordsòntext telephoneitextphoneddeafohard-of-hearingospeech-impairedpinteractive textktranscodingnspeech-to-textmuser alertingremergency servicesggatewayxanalog terminal adaptersqPSTN interworkingqtext presentationqinstant messaginglconversationsconversational textminteractivityrtotal conversationquser requirementsltext gatewayerelaymrelay servicejtext relaycTTYntext transportqtext interworkingscombination gatewaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHEƒmgrankingJcrfcd5194irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPFramework for Real-Time Text over IP Using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis document defines the general Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) framework functional elements, high-level call flow, and deployment environments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgNet.ComdnamelP. Jayaraman¢kaffiliationoUniv. of MurciadnamehR. Lopez¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepM. Parthasarathy¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. YeginddatejÃbhfilenamegrfc5193eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpanadfullx>pana - Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdnamex7Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdtypebwgbidjdoc-127093hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH*
fgrankingIcrfcd5193irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHProtocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document defines new DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 options that contain a list of IP addresses to locate one or more PANA (Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Access) Authentication Agents (PAAs).  This is one of the methods that a PANA Client (PaC) can use to locate PAAs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnameiL. Morand¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. Yegin¢kaffiliationqTech Mahindra LtddnamehS. Kumar¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamenS. Madanapalliddatej”…hfilenamegrfc5192eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127092hkeywordsÉx#dynamic host configuration protocolcpackpana clientkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH*
dgrankingHcrfcd5192irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeDHCP Options for Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) Authentication AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis document defines the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA), a network-layer transport for Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) to enable network access authentication between clients and access networks.  In EAP terms, PANA is a UDP-based EAP lower layer that runs between the EAP peer and the EAP authenticator. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekD. Forsberg¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. Yeginddatej”˛hfilenamegrfc5191eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpanadfullx>pana - Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdnamex7Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdtypebwgbidjdoc-127091hkeywordsÇceapx!exensible authentication protocolkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateH*
bgrankingGcrfcd5191irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5872∂habstractyŒThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes a set of managed objects that allow configuring middleboxes, such as firewalls and network address translators, in order to enable communication across these devices.  The definitions of managed objects in this documents follow closely the MIDCOM semantics defined in RFC 5189. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationnKazeon SystemsdnamelP. Srisureshddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5190eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmidcomdfullx midcom - Middlebox CommunicationdnamewMiddlebox Communicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127090hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basecmibfmidcomjMIDCOM-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDateGÈrÛgrankingFcrfcd5190irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Definitions of Managed Objects for Middlebox CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_This document specifies semantics for a Middlebox Communication (MIDCOM) protocol to be used by MIDCOM agents for interacting with middleboxes such as firewalls and Network Address Translators (NATs).  The semantics discussion does not include any specification of a concrete syntax or a transport protocol.  However, a concrete protocol is expected to implement the specified semantics or, more likely, a superset of it.  The MIDCOM protocol semantics is derived from the MIDCOM requirements, from the MIDCOM framework, and from working group decisions.  This document obsoletes RFC 3989. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylorddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5189eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmidcomdfullx midcom - Middlebox CommunicationdnamewMiddlebox Communicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127089hkeywordsÉcnatxnetwork address translatorhfirewallkobsoletedByÄepagesFopublicationDateGÈrÒgrankingEcrfcd5189irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Middlebox Communication (MIDCOM) Protocol SemanticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document specifies Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload formats to be used for the Enhanced Variable Rate Wideband Codec (EVRC-WB) and updates the media type registrations for EVRC-B codec.  Several media type registrations are included for EVRC-WB RTP payload formats.  In addition, a file format is specified for transport of EVRC-WB speech data in storage mode applications such as email. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekH. Desineni¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamefQ. XieddatejÃvhfilenamegrfc5188eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127088hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG≈ºÇgrankingDcrfcd5188irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyRTP Payload Format for the Enhanced Variable Rate Wideband Codec (EVRC-WB) and the Media Subtype Updates for EVRC-B CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'This document describes the OSPFv3 graceful restart.  The OSPFv3 graceful restart is identical to that of OSPFv2 except for the differences described in this document.  These differences include the format of the grace Link State Advertisements (LSAs) and other considerations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameqP. Pillay-Esnault¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiA. Lindemddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5187eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127087hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateHPcgrankingCcrfcd5187irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewOSPFv3 Graceful RestartdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe definitions of the Internet Group Management Protocol Version 3 (IGMPv3) and Multicast Listener Discovery Version 2 (MLDv2) require new behavior within the multicast routing protocols.  The additional source information contained in IGMPv3 and MLDv2 messages necessitates that multicast routing protocols manage and utilize the information.  This document describes how multicast routing protocols will interact with these source-filtering group management protocols.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxJohns Hopkins UniversitydnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationmWoven SystemsdnameiJ. Martinddatej—Ehfilenamegrfc5186eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-127086hkeywordsÇrsource informationx!source-filtering group managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH@<grankingBcrfcd5186irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexëInternet Group Management Protocol Version 3 (IGMPv3) / Multicast Listener Discovery Version 2 (MLDv2) and Multicast Routing Protocol InteractiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document describes an extension to the Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) protocol to allow a single physical link to be shared by multiple areas.  This is necessary to allow the link to be considered an intra-area link in multiple areas.  This would create an intra- area path in each of the corresponding areas sharing the same link. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmNuova SystemsdnamelS. Mirtorabi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamehA. Oswalddatej”bhfilenamegrfc5185eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-127085hkeywordsÉxopen shortest path firstjinter-areaointra-area pathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH#âxgrankingAcrfcd5185irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSPF Multi-Area AdjacencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty•This document proposes unified Layer 2 (L2) abstractions for Layer 3 (L3)-driven fast handovers.  For efficient network communication, it is vital for a protocol layer to know or utilize other layers' information, such as the form of L2 triggers.  However, each protocol layer is basically designed independently.  Since each protocol layer is also implemented independently in current operating systems, it is very hard to exchange control information between protocol layers.  This document defines nine kinds of L2 abstractions in the form of "primitives" to achieve fast handovers in the network layer as a means of solving the problem.  This mechanism is called "L3-driven fast handovers" because the network layer initiates L2 and L3 handovers by using the primitives.  This document is a product of the IP Mobility Optimizations (MobOpts) Research Group.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoKEIO UniversitydnamejF. Teraoka¢kaffiliationoKEIO UniversitydnamegK. Gogo¢kaffiliationoKEIO UniversitydnamejK. Mitsuya¢kaffiliationoKEIO UniversitydnameiR. Shibui¢kaffiliationoKEIO UniversitydnameiK. Mitaniddatej‘	hfilenamegrfc5184eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmoboptsdfullx2mobopts - IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdnamex(IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127084hkeywordsÖkl2 triggersjprimitiveswl3-driven fast handoverxip mobility optimizationsgmoboptskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH?<≤granking@crfcd5184irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexGUnified Layer 2 (L2) Abstractions for Layer 3 (L3)-Driven Fast HandoverdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@This document describes the "environment" extension to the Sieve email filtering language.  The "environment" extension gives a Sieve script access to information about the Sieve interpreter itself, where it is running, and about any transport connection currently involved in transferring the message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehN. Freedddatej”uhfilenamegrfc5183eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127083hkeywordsÅcvndkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateH?<Ægranking?crfcd5183irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Sieve Email Filtering: Environment ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6Many IMAP clients use the result of a SEARCH command as the input to perform another operation, for example, fetching the found messages, deleting them, or copying them to another mailbox.

This can be achieved using standard IMAP operations described in RFC 3501; however, this would be suboptimal. The server will send the list of found messages to the client; after that, the client will have to parse the list, reformat it, and send it back to the server. The client can't pipeline the SEARCH command with the subsequent command, and, as a result, the server might not be able to perform some optimizations.

This document proposes an IMAP extension that allows a client to tell a server to use the result of a SEARCH (or Unique Identifier (UID) SEARCH) command as an input to any subsequent command. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5182eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127082hkeywordsÑcuidqunique identifierisearchresx internet message access protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‰EÏgranking>crfcd5182irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5IMAP Extension for Referencing the Last SEARCH ResultdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’This document provides a detailed description of IPv6 deployment and integration methods and scenarios in wireless broadband access networks in coexistence with deployed IPv4 services.  In this document, we will discuss the main components of IPv6 IEEE 802.16 access networks and their differences from IPv4 IEEE 802.16 networks and how IPv6 is deployed and integrated in each of the IEEE 802.16 technologies.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnameiM-K. Shin¢kaffiliationcKUTdnamehY-H. Han¢kaffiliationbKTdnamehS-E. Kim¢kaffiliationnSiemens MobilednameiD. Premecddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5181eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127081hkeywordsÇx"Ethernet CS (Convergence Sublayer)xIPv6 CS (Convergence Sublayer)kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH$¡˝granking=crfcd5181irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,IPv6 Deployment Scenarios in 802.16 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThe benchmarking methodologies defined in RFC 2544 are IP version independent.  However, RFC 2544 does not address some of the specificities of IPv6.  This document provides additional benchmarking guidelines, which in conjunction with RFC 2544, lead to a more complete and realistic evaluation of the IPv6 performance of network interconnect devices.  IPv6 transition mechanisms are outside the scope of this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Popoviciu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Hamza¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoG. Van de Velde¢kaffiliationmFastSoft Inc.dnamekD. Dugatkinddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5180eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-127080hkeywordsÉgrfc2544dipv6wbenchmarking guidelineskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH2Ôgranking<crfcd5180irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>IPv6 Benchmarking Methodology for Network Interconnect DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxƒThis document describes the mapping of Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) domain-name-based service principal names onto Kerberos V principal names. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. Williamsddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5179eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127079hkeywordsÇqdomain-name-basedxGSS_C_NT_DOMAINBASED_SERVICEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH*
_granking;crfcd5179irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑGeneric Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Domain-Based Service Names Mapping for the Kerberos V GSS MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes domain-name-based service principal names and the corresponding name type for the Generic Security Service Application Programming Interface (GSS-API). Internationalization of the GSS-API is also covered.

Domain-based service names are similar to host-based service names, but using a domain name (not necessarily an Internet domain name) in addition to a hostname. The primary purpose of domain-based names is to provide a measure of protection to applications that utilize insecure service discovery protocols. This is achieved by providing a way to name clustered services after the "domain" which they service, thereby allowing their clients to authorize the service's servers based on authentication of their service names. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. Williams¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5178eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127078hkeywordsÉqdomain-based-namexgss-domain-based-servicesxGSS_C_NT_DOMAINBASED_SERVICEkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateH*
[granking:crfcd5178irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇGeneric Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Internationalization and Domain-Based Service Names and Name TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a protocol for supporting Mobile Networks between a Mobile Router and a Home Agent by extending the Mobile IPv4 protocol. A Mobile Router is responsible for the mobility of one or more network segments or subnets moving together. The Mobile Router hides its mobility from the nodes on the Mobile Network. The nodes on the Mobile Network may be fixed in relationship to the Mobile Router and may not have any mobility function.

Extensions to Mobile IPv4 are introduced to support Mobile Networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Dommety¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnamelV. Narayanan¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekA. Petrescuddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5177eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-127077hkeywordsâfNEMOv4oMobile NetworksoMoving NetworksmMobile RouterpLocal Fixed NodelPrefix TableuMobile Network PrefixvNested Mobile NetworkswNested Network MobilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH âgranking9crfcd5177irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Network Mobility (NEMO) Extensions for Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6626∂habstracty~This document describes a currently deployed extension to the Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) protocol, allowing dynamic changes to a user session, as implemented by network access server products.  This includes support for disconnecting users and changing authorizations applicable to a user session.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Chiba¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Dommety¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Eklund¢kaffiliationx'RSA Security - Security Division of EMCdnameiD. Mitton¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Abobaddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5176eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127076hkeywordsÅluser sessionkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateG¢tœgranking8crfcd5176irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWDynamic Authorization Extensions to Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9765d8559µhabstractyÑThe IPv6 Neighbor Discovery's Router Advertisement message contains an 8-bit field reserved for single-bit flags.  Several protocols have reserved flags in this field and others are preparing to reserve a sufficient number of flags to exhaust the field.  This document defines an option to the Router Advertisement message that expands the number of flag bits available. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hindenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5175eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbididoc-43454hkeywordsÖxneighbor discovery protocolcndpuexpanded flags optioncefox!ndp  router advertisement messagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG—◊Mgranking7crfcd5175irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&IPv6 Router Advertisement Flags OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the European Broadcasting Union (EBU) for naming persistent resources defined within EBU technical documentation and Internet resources.  Example resources include technical documents and specifications, eXtensible Markup Language (XML) Schemas, classification schemes, XML Document Type Definitions (DTDs), namespaces, style sheets, media assets, and other types of resources produced or managed by the EBU.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcEBUdnamejJ-P. Evainddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5174eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127075hkeywordsácEBUinamespacecurnibroadcasthmetadatanclassificationfschemakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH#âtgranking6crfcd5174irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the European Broadcasting Union (EBU)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¥This document defines a new command for the "Sieve" email filtering language that tests for the occurrence of one or more strings in the body of an email message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamejJ. Degener¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamekP. Guentherddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5173eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesievedfullx%sieve - Sieve Mail Filtering LanguagednamexSieve Mail Filtering Languagedtypebwgbidjdoc-127074hkeywordsÉfsearchifull texteemailkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateHgranking5crfcd5173irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Sieve Email Filtering: Body ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThe Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) provides a standard method of encapsulating network-layer protocol information over point-to-point links. PPP also defines an extensible Link Control Protocol, and proposes a family of Network Control Protocols (NCPs) for establishing and configuring different network-layer protocols.

The IPv6 Control Protocol (IPV6CP), which is an NCP for a PPP link, allows for the negotiation of desirable parameters for an IPv6 interface over PPP.

This document defines the IPv6 datagram compression option that can be negotiated by a node on the link through the IPV6CP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjTranswitchdnameiS. Varadaddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5172eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd6mandfullw6man - IPv6 MaintenancednamepIPv6 Maintenancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127073hkeywordsÖhIPv6-PPPhinternethprotocolnpoint-to-pointdipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÌ^⁄granking4crfcd5172irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexENegotiation for IPv6 Datagram Compression Using IPv6 Control ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyëThis document describes a Cisco Systems protocol that can be used to detect and disable unidirectional Ethernet fiber or copper links caused, for instance, by mis-wiring of fiber strands, interface malfunctions, media converters' faults, etc. It operates at Layer 2 in conjunction with IEEE 802.3's existing Layer 1 fault detection mechanisms.

This document explains the protocol objectives and applications, illustrates the specific premises the protocol was based upon, and describes the protocol architecture and related deployment issues to serve as a possible base for future standardization. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. Foschianoddatej”«hfilenamegrfc5171eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127072hkeywordsâhEthernethswitchescLANdIEEEc802mspanning treecSTPdFEFIoautonegotiationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH ágranking3crfcd5171irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex;Cisco Systems UniDirectional Link Detection (UDLD) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes two Fully-Specified Forward Error Correction (FEC) Schemes, Low Density Parity Check (LDPC) Staircase and LDPC Triangle, and their application to the reliable delivery of data objects on the packet erasure channel (i.e., a communication path where packets are either received without any corruption or discarded during transmission).  These systematic FEC codes belong to the well- known class of "Low Density Parity Check" codes, and are large block FEC codes in the sense of RFC 3453. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationgThomsondnamejC. Neumann¢kaffiliationrSTMicroelectronicsdnamejD. Furodetddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5170eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127071hkeywordsÇdLDPCcFECkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateHPcgranking2crfcd5170irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Low Density Parity Check (LDPC) Staircase and Triangle Forward Error Correction (FEC) SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Handover Keying (HOKEY) re-authentication problem statement.  The current Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) keying framework is not designed to support re-authentication and handovers without re-executing an EAP method.  This often causes unacceptable latency in various mobile wireless environments.  This document details the problem and defines design goals for a generic mechanism to reuse derived EAP keying material for handover.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcLTSdnameiT. Clancy¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekM. Nakhjiri¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamelV. Narayanan¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejL. Dondetiddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5169eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymehokeydfullwhokey - Handover KeyingdnameoHandover Keyingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127070hkeywordsÑehokeywhandover key managementvfast re-authenticationhmobilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÒhùgranking1crfcd5169irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Handover Key Management and Re-Authentication Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThis document defines an Extensible Markup Language (XML) Schema for video fast update in a tightly controlled environment, developed by Microsoft, Polycom, Radvision and used by multiple vendors.  This document describes a method that has been deployed in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) based systems over the last three years and is being used across real-time interactive applications from different vendors in an interoperable manner.  New implementations are discouraged from using the method described except for backward compatibility purposes.  New implementations are required to use the new Full Intra Request command in the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) channel.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levin¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationiRADVISIONdnamelP. Hagendorfddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5168eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127069hkeywordsÇxextensible markup languageqvideo fast updatekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateGÒhögranking0crfcd5168irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXML Schema for Media ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document addresses the communication between an application server and media server. The current work in IETF working groups shows these logical entities, but it does not address the physical decomposition and the protocol between the entities.

This document presents the requirements for a Media Server Control Protocol (MCP) that enables an application server to use a media server. It will address the aspects of announcements, Interactive Voice Response, and conferencing media services. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamehM. Dolly¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamegR. Evenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5167eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymimediactrldfullx mediactrl - Media Server ControldnametMedia Server Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-127068hkeywordsÑplogical entitiescmcpxinteractive voice responsexconferencing media serviceskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateGÒhògranking/crfcd5167irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Media Server Control Protocol RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document discusses the metrics to be considered in an evaluation of new or modified congestion control mechanisms for the Internet. These include metrics for the evaluation of new transport protocols, of proposed modifications to TCP, of application-level congestion control, and of Active Queue Management (AQM) mechanisms in the router. This document is the first in a series of documents aimed at improving the models that we use in the evaluation of transport protocols.

This document is a product of the Transport Modeling Research Group (TMRG), and has received detailed feedback from many members of the Research Group (RG). As the document tries to make clear, there is not necessarily a consensus within the research community (or the IETF community, the vendor community, the operations community, or any other community) about the metrics that congestion control mechanisms should be designed to optimize, in terms of trade-offs between throughput and delay, fairness between competing flows, and the like. However, we believe that there is a clear consensus that congestion control mechanisms should be evaluated in terms of trade-offs between a range of metrics, rather than in terms of optimizing for a single metric. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars EggertgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx!ICSI Center for Internet ResearchdnamehS. Floydddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5166eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtmrgdfullx(tmrg - Transport Modeling Research Groupdnamex!Transport Modeling Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-127067hkeywordsÉrtransport protocolx!transport modeling research groupdtmrgkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‰E’granking.crfcd5166irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex;Metrics for the Evaluation of Congestion Control MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace that is engineered by the Open Geospatial Consortium (OGC) for naming persistent resources published by the OGC.  The formal Namespace IDentifier (NID) is "ogc".  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxOpen Geospatial ConsortiumdnamegC. Reedddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5165eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127066hkeywordsÜhlocationjgeospatialinamespacecOGCcURNxOpen Geospatial ConsortiumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGˇÂ'granking-crfcd5165irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Open Geospatial Consortium (OGC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThere are ongoing activities in the networking community to develop solutions that aid in IP handover mechanisms between heterogeneous wired and wireless access systems including, but not limited to, IEEE 802.21.  Intelligent access selection, taking into account link-layer attributes, requires the delivery of a variety of different information types to the terminal from different sources within the network and vice-versa.  The protocol requirements for this signalling have both transport and security issues that must be considered.  The signalling must not be constrained to specific link types, so there is at least a common component to the signalling problem, which is within the scope of the IETF.  This document presents a problem statement for this core problem.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Meliaddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5164eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127065hkeywordsÇxintelligent access selectionuip handover mechanismkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‡>@granking,crfcd5164irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Mobility Services Transport: Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document describes a set of Extension Headers for the Unidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE), RFC 4326.

The Extension Header formats specified in this document define extensions appropriate to both ULE and the Generic Stream Encapsulation (GSE) for the second-generation framing structure defined by the Digital Video Broadcasting (DVB) family of specifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of SalzburgdnameqB. Collini-Nockerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5163eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipdvbdfullsipdvb - IP over DVBdnamekIP over DVBdtypebwgbidjdoc-127064hkeywordsÖxdigital video broadcastingcdvbpmpeg-2 ts-concatjpdu-concatitimestampkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateH Ögranking+crfcd5163irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoExtension Formats for Unidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE) and the Generic Stream Encapsulation (GSE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThis document defines an IMAP4 extension, which gives an IMAP client the ability to quickly resynchronize any previously opened mailbox as part of the SELECT command, without the need for server-side state or additional client round-trips.  This extension also introduces a new response that allows for a more compact representation of a list of expunged messages (and always includes the Unique Identifiers (UIDs) expunged). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekD. Cridland¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiC. Wilsonddatej‘␀hfilenamegrfc5162eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127063hkeywordsÅx Internet Message Access ProtocolkobsoletedByÅd7162epagesopublicationDateG’Agranking*crfcd5162irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4IMAP4 Extensions for Quick Mailbox ResynchronizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMost IMAP extensions are used by the client when it wants to and the server supports it.  However, a few extensions require the server to know whether a client supports that extension.  The ENABLE extension allows an IMAP client to say which extensions it supports. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmbHdnamenA. Gulbrandsen¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5161eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127062hkeywordsÅx Internet Message Access ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG’@Ígranking)crfcd5161irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe IMAP ENABLE ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty3This memo analyzes provider-to-provider Quality of Service (QoS) agreements suitable for a global QoS-enabled Internet.  It defines terminology relevant to inter-domain QoS models.  It proposes a new concept denoted by Meta-QoS-Class (MQC).  This concept could potentially drive and federate the way QoS inter-domain relationships are built between providers.  It opens up new perspectives for a QoS- enabled Internet that retains, as much as possible, the openness of the existing best-effort Internet.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus WesterlundgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamehP. Levis¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadairddatej”‚hfilenamegrfc5160eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127061hkeywordsÖcslscbgpgpeeringhdiffservqparallel internetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‡>granking(crfcd5160irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex]Considerations of Provider-to-Provider Agreements for Internet-Scale Quality of Service (QoS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document provides descriptions of Session Description Protocol (SDP) attributes used by the Open Mobile Alliance's Broadcast Service and Content Protection specification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnQUALCOMM, Inc.dnamejL. Dondeti¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamekA. Jerichowddatej”êhfilenamegrfc5159eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127060hkeywordsÉcSDPqIANA registrationiOMA BCASTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‡>(granking'crfcd5159irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Session Description Protocol (SDP) Attributes for Open Mobile Alliance (OMA) Broadcast (BCAST) Service and Content ProtectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This memo describes the service mechanism for entering a delegation of DNS servers that provide reverse lookup of 6to4 IPv6 addresses into the 6to4 reverse zone file.  The mechanism is based on a conventional DNS delegation service interface, allowing the service client to enter the details of a number of DNS servers for the delegated domain.  In the context of a 6to4 reverse delegation, the client is primarily authenticated by its source address used in the delegation request, and is authorized to use the function if its IPv6 address prefix corresponds to an address from within the requested 6to4 delegation address block.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5158eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127059hkeywordsÇcdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG–h«granking&crfcd5158irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)6to4 Reverse DNS Delegation SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThe much larger default 64-bit subnet address space of IPv6 should in principle make traditional network (port) scanning techniques used by certain network worms or scanning tools less effective.  While traditional network scanning probes (whether by individuals or automated via network worms) may become less common, administrators should be aware that attackers may use other techniques to discover IPv6 addresses on a target network, and thus they should also be aware of measures that are available to mitigate them.  This informational document discusses approaches that administrators could take when planning their site address allocation and management strategies as part of a defence-in-depth approach to network security.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chownddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5157eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127058hkeywordsÅtsubnet address spacekobsoletedByÅd7707epagesopublicationDateGÒhìgranking%crfcd5157irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&IPv6 Implications for Network ScanningdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document is a compilation of special IPv6 addresses defined in other RFCs.  It can be used as a checklist of invalid routing prefixes for developing filtering policies for routes and IP packets.  It does not discuss addresses that are assigned to operators and users through the Regional Internet Registries.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5156eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127057hkeywordsÅvinvalid routing prefixkobsoletedByÅd6890epagesopublicationDateGÙıgranking$crfcd5156irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSpecial-Use IPv6 AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThe Domain Name System Security (DNSSEC) Extensions introduced the NSEC resource record (RR) for authenticated denial of existence.  This document introduces an alternative resource record, NSEC3, which similarly provides authenticated denial of existence.  However, it also provides measures against zone enumeration and permits gradual expansion of delegation-centric zones. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgNominetdnameiB. Laurie¢kaffiliationgNominetdnameiG. Sisson¢kaffiliationgNominetdnameiR. Arends¢kaffiliationmVeriSign Inc.dnameiD. Blackaddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5155eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127056hkeywordsÑrdomain name systemdnsecoresource recordensec3kobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateG–h…granking#crfcd5155irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>DNS Security (DNSSEC) Hashed Authenticated Denial of ExistencedtypecrfciupdatedByÜd9276d6840d6944d9157d9905d9077∂habstractyæThis document specifies problems in running IP over IEEE 802.16 networks by identifying specific gaps in the IEEE 802.16 Media Access Control (MAC) for IPv4 and IPv6 support.  This document also provides an overview of IEEE 802.16 network characteristics and convergence sublayers.  Common terminology used for the base guideline while defining the solution framework is also presented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamefJ. Jee¢kaffiliationrOrdyn TechnologiesdnamenS. Madanapalli¢kaffiliationfRuncomdnameiJ. Mandinddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5154eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd16ngdfullx#16ng - IP over IEEE 802.16 NetworksdnamexIP over IEEE 802.16 Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127055hkeywordsÉeWiMAXlMobile WiMAXeWiBrokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG˝92granking"crfcd5154irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/IP over IEEE 802.16 Problem Statement and GoalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8The IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol defines how IP Flow information can be exported from routers, measurement probes, or other devices.  This document provides guidelines for the implementation and use of the IPFIX protocol.  Several sets of guidelines address Template management, transport-specific issues, implementation of Exporting and Collecting Processes, and IPFIX implementation on middleboxes (such as firewalls, network address translators, tunnel endpoints, packet classifiers, etc.).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednameiE. Boschi¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamegL. Mark¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Aitkenddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5153eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127054hkeywordsÑstemplate mangaementsexporting processestcollecting processesqipfix middleboxeskobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateH	(®granking!crfcd5153irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) Implementation GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document specifies a per-domain path computation technique for establishing inter-domain Traffic Engineering (TE) Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs). In this document, a domain refers to a collection of network elements within a common sphere of address management or path computational responsibility such as Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) areas and Autonomous Systems.

Per-domain computation applies where the full path of an inter-domain TE LSP cannot be or is not determined at the ingress node of the TE LSP, and is not signaled across domain boundaries. This is most likely to arise owing to TE visibility limitations. The signaling message indicates the destination and nodes up to the next domain boundary. It may also indicate further domain boundaries or domain identifiers. The path through each domain, possibly including the choice of exit point from the domain, must be determined within the domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekA. Ayyangar¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehR. Zhangddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5152eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127053hkeywordsÇdmplsegmplskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG«R~granking crfcd5152irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwA Per-Domain Path Computation Method for Establishing Inter-Domain Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyΩThis document describes procedures and protocol extensions for the use of Resource Reservation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling in Multiprotocol Label Switching-Traffic Engineering (MPLS-TE) packet networks and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) packet and non-packet networks to support the establishment and maintenance of Label Switched Paths that cross domain boundaries.

For the purpose of this document, a domain is considered to be any collection of network elements within a common realm of address space or path computation responsibility. Examples of such domains include Autonomous Systems, Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) routing areas, and GMPLS overlay networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekA. Ayyangar¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseurddatej”ˇhfilenamegrfc5151eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127052hkeywordsÇxmultiprotocol label switchinggmpls-tekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG«S°grankingcrfcd5151irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyInter-Domain MPLS and GMPLS Traffic Engineering -- Resource Reservation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìIn certain scenarios, there may be a need to combine several Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) such that a single end-to-end (e2e) LSP is realized and all traffic from one constituent LSP is switched onto the next LSP. We will refer to this as "LSP stitching", the key requirement being that a constituent LSP not be allocated to more than one e2e LSP. The constituent LSPs will be referred to as "LSP segments" (S-LSPs).

This document describes extensions to the existing GMPLS signaling protocol (Resource Reservation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE)) to establish e2e LSPs created from S-LSPs, and describes how the LSPs can be managed using the GMPLS signaling and routing protocols.

It may be possible to configure a GMPLS node to switch the traffic from an LSP for which it is the egress, to another LSP for which it is the ingress, without requiring any signaling or routing extensions whatsoever and such that the operation is completely transparent to other nodes. This will also result in LSP stitching in the data plane. However, this document does not cover this scenario of LSP stitching. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekA. Ayyangar¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej”˘hfilenamegrfc5150eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127051hkeywordsÜclsptlabel switched pathsge2e lspmlsp stitchingllsp segmentses-lspkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG«Rngrankingcrfcd5150irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkLabel Switched Path Stitching with Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching Traffic Engineering (GMPLS TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn some Mobile IPv6 deployments, identifying the mobile node or the mobility service subscriber is not enough to distinguish between multiple services possibly provisioned to the said mobile node and its mobility service subscription.  A capability to specify different services in addition to the mobile node identity can be leveraged to provide flexibility for mobility service providers on provisioning multiple services to one mobility service subscription.  This document describes a Service Selection Mobility Option for both conventional Mobile IPv6 and Proxy Mobile IPv6 that is intended to assist home agents to make a specific service selection for the mobility service subscription during the binding registration procedure.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamejU. Nilsson¢kaffiliationfAzairednamenV. Devarapalliddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5149eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127050hkeywordsÖemipv6x!service selection mobility optionqproxy mobile ipv6xmobilty service subscriptionxbinding registration procedurekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateG≈ºvgrankingcrfcd5149irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Service Selection for Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides recommendations for jittering (randomly modifying timing) of control traffic transmissions in Mobile Ad hoc NETwork (MANET) routing protocols to reduce the probability of transmission collisions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliation`dnamekC. Dearlove¢kaffiliation`dnamejB. Adamsonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5148eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127049hkeywordsÉxrandomly modifying timingxcontrol traffic transmissionutranmission collisionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG≈ºjgrankingcrfcd5148irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Jitter Considerations in Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis memo defines URI fragment identifiers for text/plain MIME entities.  These fragment identifiers make it possible to refer to parts of a text/plain MIME entity, either identified by character position or range, or by line position or range.  Fragment identifiers may also contain information for integrity checks to make them more robust. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamehE. Wilde¢kaffiliationxAoyama Gakuin UniversitydnameiM. Duerstddatej‘
hfilenamegrfc5147eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127048hkeywordsÇxuniform resource identifierkmime entitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG˝9+grankingcrfcd5147irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6URI Fragment Identifiers for the text/plain Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAOperation of a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) traffic engineering (TE) network as a client network to a Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) network has enhanced operational capabilities compared to those provided by a coexistent protocol model (i.e., operation of MPLS-TE over an independently managed transport layer).

The GMPLS network may be a packet or a non-packet network, and may itself be a multi-layer network supporting both packet and non-packet technologies. An MPLS-TE Label Switched Path (LSP) originates and terminates on an MPLS Label Switching Router (LSR). The GMPLS network provides transparent transport for the end-to-end MPLS-TE LSP.

This document describes a framework and Service Provider requirements for operating MPLS-TE networks over GMPLS networks. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpKDDI CorporationdnameiK. Kumakiddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5146eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127047hkeywordsÇx1multiprotocol label switching traffic engineeringxservice provider requirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‰E≈grankingcrfcd5146irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMInterworking Requirements to Support Operation of MPLS-TE over GMPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘The migration from Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE) to Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) is the process of evolving an MPLS-TE control plane to a GMPLS control plane. An appropriate migration strategy will be selected based on various factors including the service provider's network deployment plan, customer demand, and operational policy.

This document presents several migration models and strategies for migrating from MPLS-TE to GMPLS. In the course of migration, MPLS-TE and GMPLS devices, or networks, may coexist that may require interworking between MPLS-TE and GMPLS protocols. Aspects of the required interworking are discussed as it will influence the choice of a migration strategy. This framework document provides a migration toolkit to aid the operator in selection of an appropriate strategy.

This framework document also lists a set of solutions that may aid in interworking, and highlights a set of potential issues. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Shiomotoddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5145eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-127046hkeywordsÇx1multiprotocol label switching traffic engineeringmcontrol planekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‰E¨grankingcrfcd5145irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Framework for MPLS-TE to GMPLS MigrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx”This document describes a lightweight domain availability service using the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS) framework and the data model of the IRIS Domain Registry (DREG) service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx&American Registry for Internet NumbersdnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationhDENIC eGdnamegM. Sanzddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5144eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127045hkeywordsÇddregtiris domain registrykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG«Rjgrankingcrfcd5144irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeA Domain Availability Check (DCHK) Registry Type for the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty)This document describes a historical method for encapsulating Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Path signals for transport across packet-switched networks (PSNs).  The PSNs explicitly supported by this document include MPLS and IP.  Note that RFC 4842 describes the standards-track protocol for this functionality, and new implementations must use RFC 4842 rather than this document except when interoperability with older implementations is desired.  This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvVerizon CommunicationsdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationpECI Telecom Inc.dnamejJ. Brayley¢kaffiliationpECI Telecom Inc.dnamejJ. Shirron¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelS. Vogelsangddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5143eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127044hkeywordsÉcpsnwpacket switched networkgRFC4842kobsoletedByÅd4842epagesopublicationDateGøs	grankingcrfcd5143irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex}Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Circuit Emulation Service over MPLS (CEM) EncapsulationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∆This document specifies a new Mobility Header message type that can be used between a home agent and mobile node to signal to a mobile node that it should acquire a new home agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamehB. Haley¢kaffiliationoAzaire NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamegH. Deng¢kaffiliationnDoCoMo USA LabdnamehJ. KempfddatejÃkhfilenamegrfc5142eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127043hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGûÅ$grankingcrfcd5142irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Mobility Header Home Agent Switch MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This document describes a Uniform Resource Name Namespace Identification (URN NID) for the International Organization for Standardization (ISO).  This URN NID is intended for use for the identification of persistent resources published by the ISO standards body (including documents, document metadata, extracted resources such as standard schemata and standard value sets, and other resources).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISOdnamejJ. Goodwin¢kaffiliationcISOdnamegH. Apelddatej”Õhfilenamegrfc5141eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127042hkeywordsÉgurn nidx.uniform resource name namespace identificationcNSSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG‰Eôgrankingcrfcd5141irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the International Organization for Standardization (ISO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœThis document describes the Telephony Gateway Registration Protocol (TGREP) for registration of telephony prefixes supported by telephony gateways and soft switches.  The registration mechanism can also be used to export resource information.  The prefix and resource information can then be passed on to a Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP) Location Server, which in turn can propagate that routing information within and between Internet Telephony Administrative Domains (ITADs).  TGREP shares a lot of similarities with the TRIP protocol.  It has similar procedures and finite state machine for session establishment.  It also shares the same format for messages and a subset of attributes with TRIP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelM. Bangalore¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Kumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationnCitex SoftwarednameiH. Salama¢kaffiliationjMoowee IncdnameiD.N. Shahddatej‘	hfilenamegrfc5140eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-127041hkeywordsÜptelephony prefixmsoft switchesxtelephony routing over ipdtripx)internet telephony administrative domainsditadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG—◊grankingcrfcd5140irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1A Telephony Gateway REgistration Protocol (TGREP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis document defines an XML format for the representation of civic location.  This format is designed for use with Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO) documents and replaces the civic location format in RFC 4119.  The format is based on the civic address definition in PIDF-LO, but adds several new elements based on the civic types defined for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP), and adds a hierarchy to address complex road identity schemes.  The format also includes support for the xml:lang language tag and restricts the types of elements where appropriate. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfAndrewdnamejM. Thomson¢kaffiliationfAndrewdnameoJ. Winterbottomddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5139eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-127040hkeywordsÑhlocationncivic locationgpidf-lomcivic addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGøsUgrankingcrfcd5139irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Revised Civic Location Format for Presence Information Data Format Location Object (PIDF-LO)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document describes a URN (Uniform Resource Name) namespace that is engineered by the Commission for the Management and Application of Geoscience Information (CGI) for naming (i) persistent resources published by the CGI and (ii) resources published by organizations that wish them to be used in the context of services conforming to protocols and agreements issued by CGI.  The formal Namespace Identifier (NID) is "cgi".  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCSIROdnamefS. CoxddatejÃwhfilenamegrfc5138eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127039hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGør’grankingcrfcd5138irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Commission for the Management and Application of Geoscience Information (CGI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty4There are a number of circumstances in which an escape mechanism is needed in conjunction with a protocol to encode characters that cannot be represented or transmitted directly.  With ASCII coding, the traditional escape has been either the decimal or hexadecimal numeric value of the character, written in a variety of different ways.  The move to Unicode, where characters occupy two or more octets and may be coded in several different forms, has further complicated the question of escapes.  This document discusses some options now in use and discusses considerations for selecting one for use in new IETF protocols, and protocols that are now being internationalized.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5137eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127038hkeywordsÜdTexttinternationalizationeasciigunicodeeutf-8hencodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGøs"grankingcrfcd5137irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberâetotaletitlex$ASCII Escaping of Unicode CharactersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇMeasuring capacity is a task that sounds simple, but in reality can be quite complex.  In addition, the lack of a unified nomenclature on this subject makes it increasingly difficult to properly build, test, and use techniques and tools built around these constructs.  This document provides definitions for the terms 'Capacity' and 'Available Capacity' related to IP traffic traveling between a source and destination in an IP network.  By doing so, we hope to provide a common framework for the discussion and analysis of a diverse set of current and future estimation techniques.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwJHU Applied Physics LabdnamekP. Chimento¢kaffiliationxNASA Glenn Research CenterdnamehJ. Ishacddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5136eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-127037hkeywordsáibandwidthtbandwidth estimationscapacity estimationmlink capacityravailable capacityknarrow linkjtight linkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG±˙•grankingcrfcd5136irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDefining Network CapacitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document specifies requirements for a for a Network Address Translator (NAT) and a Network Address Port Translator (NAPT) that support Any Source IP Multicast or Source-Specific IP Multicast.  An IP multicast-capable NAT device that adheres to the requirements of this document can optimize the operation of IP multicast applications that are generally unaware of IP multicast NAT devices.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegD. Wing¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Eckertddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5135eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127036hkeywordsÇumulticast applicationmmulticast natkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG´ïKgrankingcrfcd5135irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberáetotaletitlexmIP Multicast Requirements for a Network Address Translator (NAT) and a Network Address Port Translator (NAPT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıThis document describes URN namespaces that will identify various objects within the EPCglobal system for identifying products within ecommerce and supply chain management applications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxRefactored Networks, LLCdnamekM. Meallingddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5134eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127035hkeywordsàuuniform resource namegAuto-IDdRFIDiEPCglobalcEPCcUPCwsupply chain managementhbar codekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateGé`grankingcrfcd5134irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgA Uniform Resource Name Namespace for the EPCglobal Electronic Product Code (EPC) and Related StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	The Integrated Services Digital Network (ISDN) Q.921-User Adaptation Layer (IUA) Protocol, described in RFC 4233, defines the message type of Terminal Endpoint Identifier (TEI) Query Request messages as 5.  However, this number is already being used by the Digital Private Network Signaling System (DPNSS)/Digital Access Signaling System 2 (DASS 2) Extensions (DUA) to the IUA Protocol described in RFC 4129.  This document updates RFC 4233 such that the message type of TEI Query Request messages is 8. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Morneaultddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5133eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127034hkeywordsÇx isdn q.921-user adaptation layerciuakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGlEHgrankingcrfcd5133irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Terminal Endpoint Identifier (TEI) Query Request Number ChangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing multicast function, independent of the specific multicast protocol(s) in use.  This document obsoletes RFC 2932. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsData Connection LtddnamekD. McWalter¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Kesslerddatej”˝hfilenamegrfc5132eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-127033hkeywordsÇxmanagament information basekIPMCAST-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateGgıgrankingcrfcd5132irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlepIP Multicast MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÏThis MIB module defines a textual convention to represent BCP 47 language tags.  The intent is that this textual convention will be imported and used in MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsData Connection LtddnamekD. McWalterddatej”Ôhfilenamegrfc5131eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127032hkeywordsÅnLANGTAG-TC-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGgÂgrankingcrfcd5131irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*A MIB Textual Convention for Language TagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document describes an extension to the IS-IS protocol to add operational capabilities that allow for ease of management and control over IP prefix distribution within an IS-IS domain.  This document enhances the IS-IS protocol by extending the information that an Intermediate System (IS) router can place in Link State Protocol (LSP) Data Units for policy use.  This extension will provide operators with a mechanism to control IP prefix distribution throughout multi-level IS-IS domains. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing Areadname