estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=A Policy Control Mechanism in IS-IS Using Administrative TagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 3270 defines how to support the Diffserv architecture in MPLS networks, including how to encode Diffserv Code Points (DSCPs) in an MPLS header.  DSCPs may be encoded in the EXP field, while other uses of that field are not precluded.  RFC 3270 makes no statement about how Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) marking might be encoded in the MPLS header.  This document defines how an operator might define some of the EXP codepoints for explicit congestion notification, without precluding other uses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationkBT ResearchdnamejB. Briscoe¢kaffiliationkBT ResearchdnamefJ. Tayddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5129eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127030hkeywordsÑhDiffservwDifferentiated ServicescQOSjECN tunnelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGöÄggranking	crfcd5129irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Explicit Congestion Marking in MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5462∂habstracty√This memo documents the various methods known to be in use by applications to establish direct communication in the presence of Network Address Translators (NATs) at the current time.  Although this memo is intended to be mainly descriptive, the Security Considerations section makes some purely advisory recommendations about how to deal with security vulnerabilities the applications could inadvertently create when using the methods described.  This memo covers NAT traversal approaches used by both TCP- and UDP-based applications.  This memo is not an endorsement of the methods described, but merely an attempt to capture them in a document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnKazeon SystemsdnamelP. Srisuresh¢kaffiliationfM.I.T.dnamegB. Ford¢kaffiliationikegel.comdnamehD. KegelddatejÃrhfilenamegrfc5128eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-127029hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateGÌ^”grankingcrfcd5128irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSState of Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Communication across Network Address Translators (NATs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/In the core of a high-capacity network, service differentiation may still be needed to support applications' utilization of the network.  Applications with similar traffic characteristics and performance requirements are mapped into Diffserv service classes based on end- to-end behavior requirements of the applications.  However, some network segments may be configured in such a way that a single forwarding treatment may satisfy the traffic characteristics and performance requirements of two or more service classes.  In these cases, it may be desirable to aggregate two or more Diffserv service classes into a single forwarding treatment.  This document provides guidelines for the aggregation of Diffserv service classes into forwarding treatments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamejJ. Babiarz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Bakerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5127eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127028hkeywordsÇsTreatment Aggregatetforwarding treatmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGør grankingcrfcd5127irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Aggregation of Diffserv Service ClassesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis document defines the format of an electronic signature that can remain valid over long periods. This includes evidence as to its validity even if the signer or verifying party later attempts to deny (i.e., repudiates) the validity of the signature.

The format can be considered as an extension to RFC 3852 and RFC 2634, where, when appropriate, additional signed and unsigned attributes have been defined.

The contents of this Informational RFC amount to a transposition of the ETSI Technical Specification (TS) 101 733 V.1.7.4 (CMS Advanced Electronic Signatures -- CAdES) and is technically equivalent to it.

The technical contents of this specification are maintained by ETSI. The ETSI TS and further updates are available free of charge at: http://www.etsi.org/WebSite/Standards/StandardsDownload.aspx This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhBull SASdnameiD. Pinkas¢kaffiliationpThales eSecuritydnamegN. Pope¢kaffiliationvSecurity and StandardsdnamegJ. Rossddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5126eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-127027hkeywordsàoverifying partyfsignerhpurchasehcontractginvoicekapplicationksmart cardsddatakobsoletedByÄepagesçopublicationDateG–h√grankingcrfcd5126irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*CMS Advanced Electronic Signatures (CAdES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxºThis document reclassifies RFC 3525, Gateway Control Protocol Version 1, to Historic Status.  This memo also obsoletes RFC 3525.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylorddatej‘
hfilenamegrfc5125eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127026hkeywordsÖfMEGACOeH.248emediaggatewaygcontrolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG´ïmgrankingcrfcd5125irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Reclassification of RFC 3525 to HistoricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=An RTP profile (SAVP) for secure real-time communications and another profile (AVPF) to provide timely feedback from the receivers to a sender are defined in RFC 3711 and RFC 4585, respectively.  This memo specifies the combination of both profiles to enable secure RTP communications with feedback. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx!Helsinki University of TechnologydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationcKTHdnamejE. Carraraddatej‘
hfilenamegrfc5124eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127025hkeywordsÇdavpfqrtp communicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGøsgrankingcrfcd5124irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfExtended Secure RTP Profile for Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP)-Based Feedback (RTP/SAVPF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÎThis document examines the implications of hop-by-hop forwarding, route aggregation, and route filtering on the concept of validation within a BGP Autonomous System (AS) Path.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. ZiningauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. White¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehB. Akyolddatej—dhfilenamegrfc5123eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127024hkeywordsÇxbgp autonomous system pathkbgp as pathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG«Hàgrankingcrfcd5123irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex,Considerations in Validating the Path in BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the use of Internationalized Resource Identifiers (IRIs) and Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) in identifying or interacting with entities that can communicate via the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcXSFdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5122eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127023hkeywordsáx*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocolx%Internationalized Resource IdentifierxUniform Resource IdentifierfJabberdxmppciricurikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGøs=grankingcrfcd5122irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexèInternationalized Resource Identifiers (IRIs) and Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) for the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòIEEE Std 802.16 is an air interface specification for fixed and mobile Broadband Wireless Access Systems.  Service-specific convergence sublayers to which upper-layer protocols interface are a part of the IEEE 802.16 MAC (Medium Access Control).  The Packet convergence sublayer (CS) is used for the transport of all packet- based protocols such as Internet Protocol (IP) and IEEE 802.3 LAN/MAN CSMA/CD Access Method (Ethernet).  IPv6 packets can be sent and received via the IP-specific part of the Packet CS.  This document specifies the addressing and operation of IPv6 over the IP-specific part of the Packet CS for hosts served by a network that utilizes the IEEE Std 802.16 air interface.  It recommends the assignment of a unique prefix (or prefixes) to each host and allows the host to use multiple identifiers within that prefix, including support for randomly generated interface identifiers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamefF. Xia¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamekB. Sarikaya¢kaffiliationkSamsung AITdnamehJH. Choi¢kaffiliationrOrdyn TechnologiesdnamenS. Madanapalliddatej‘
hfilenamegrfc5121eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymd16ngdfullx#16ng - IP over IEEE 802.16 NetworksdnamexIP over IEEE 802.16 Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-127022hkeywordsÇrNeighbor DiscoverymPer-MS PerfixkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG±˙ägrankingcrfcd5121irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPTransmission of IPv6 via the IPv6 Convergence Sublayer over IEEE 802.16 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8064∂habstractywThis document describes an optional mechanism within Intermediate System to Intermediate Systems (IS-ISs) used today by many ISPs for IGP routing within their clouds.  This document describes how to run, within a single IS-IS domain, a set of independent IP topologies that we call Multi-Topologies (MTs).  This MT extension can be used for a variety of purposes, such as an in-band management network "on top" of the original IGP topology, maintaining separate IGP routing domains for isolated multicast or IPv6 islands within the backbone, or forcing a subset of an address space to follow a different topology. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgZ2 SagldnamemT. Przygienda¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehN. Shethddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5120eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-127021hkeywordsÅeis-iskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG≠␀ùgranking␀crfcd5120irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[M-ISIS: Multi Topology (MT) Routing in Intermediate System to Intermediate Systems (IS-ISs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) for naming persistent resources that SMPTE produces or manages.  A subnamespace for Universal Labels is specifically described.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcFOXdnamejT. Edwardsddatej”˛hfilenamegrfc5119eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127020hkeywordsÇtpersistent resourcespuniversal labelskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateG¥Ù©grankingˇcrfcd5119irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx·This document provides examples of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) test messages designed to exercise and "torture" the code of an IPv6-enabled SIP implementation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Bell Laboratories-Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationxUbiquity Software CorporationdnamejC. Boulton¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnameiR. Sparksddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5118eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127019hkeywordsÉlTorture testdIPv6cSIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG≠␀ìgranking˛crfcd5118irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Torture Test Messages for Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document discusses multi-endpoint topologies used in Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP)-based environments.  In particular, centralized topologies commonly employed in the video conferencing industry are mapped to the RTP terminology.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiS. Wengerddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5117eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127018hkeywordsÇxmulti-endpoint topologiesxreal-time transport protocolkobsoletedByÅd7667epagesopublicationDateG¢t«granking˝crfcd5117irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlenRTP TopologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThis document defines algorithms for Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD), and defines a uniform interface and a registry for such algorithms.  The interface and registry can be used as an application-independent set of cryptoalgorithm suites.  This approach provides advantages in efficiency and security, and promotes the reuse of crypto implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiD. McGrewddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5116eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127017hkeywordsÑjEncryptionnAuthenticationdAEADx-authenticated encryption with associated datakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG¢t§granking¸crfcd5116irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8An Interface and Algorithms for Authenticated EncryptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document defines a new attribute for the Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP) protocol.  The attribute associates protocols/services in the PSTN offering authorized prioritization during call setup that are reachable through a TRIP gateway.  Current examples of preferential service in the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) are Government Emergency Telecommunications Service (GETS) in the U.S.  and Government Telephone Preference Scheme (GTPS) in the U.K.  The proposed attribute for TRIP is based on the NameSpace.Value tuple defined for the SIP Resource-Priority field. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlberg¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamekP. O'Hanlonddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5115eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127016hkeywordsÇlip telephonypResourcePrioritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGé`'granking˚crfcd5115irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP) Attribute for Resource PrioritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤This document describes eight Diffie-Hellman groups that can be used in conjunction with IETF protocols to provide security for Internet communications. The groups allow implementers to use the same groups with a variety of security protocols, e.g., SMIME, Secure SHell (SSH), Transport Layer Security (TLS), and Internet Key Exchange (IKE).

All of these groups comply in form and structure with relevant standards from ISO, ANSI, NIST, and the IEEE. These groups are compatible with all IETF standards that make use of Diffie-Hellman or Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman cryptography.

These groups and the associated test data are defined by NIST on their web site [EX80056A], but have not yet (as of this writing) been published in a formal NIST document. Publication of these groups and associated test data, as well as describing how to use Diffie-Hellman and Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman for key agreement in all of the protocols cited below, in one RFC, will facilitate development of interoperable implementations and support the Federal Information Processing Standard (FIPS) validation of implementations that make use of these groups. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamekM. Lepinski¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kentddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5114eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127015hkeywordsÜnelliptic curvecikectlscsshesmimeex.509kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGöÄ\granking˙crfcd5114irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Additional Diffie-Hellman Groups for Use with IETF StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃWhen multiple access networks are available, users may have difficulty in selecting which network to connect to and how to authenticate with that network.  This document defines the network discovery and selection problem, dividing it into multiple sub- problems.  Some constraints on potential solutions are outlined, and the limitations of several solutions (including existing ones) are discussed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegF. BariddatejÃnhfilenamegrfc5113eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceapdfullx(eap - Extensible Authentication Protocoldnamex"Extensible Authentication Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-127014hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateGöÄtgranking˘crfcd5113irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Network Discovery and Selection ProblemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is a text-based protocol for initiating and managing communication sessions.  The protocol is extended by the SIP-events notification framework to provide subscriptions and notifications of SIP events.  One example of such event notification mechanism is presence, which is expressed in XML documents called presence documents.  SIP can be compressed by using Signaling Compression (SigComp), which is enhanced by using the SIP/ Session Description Protocol (SDP) dictionary to achieve better compression rates.  However, the SIP/SDP dictionary is not able to increase the compression factor of (typically lengthy) presence documents.  This memo defines the presence-specific static dictionary that SigComp can use in order to compress presence documents to achieve higher efficiency.  The dictionary is compression-algorithm independent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamepM. Garcia-Martinddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5112eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127013hkeywordsÉucommunication sessionrevent notificationhpresencekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGåmgranking¯crfcd5112irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKThe Presence-Specific Static Dictionary for Signaling Compression (Sigcomp)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2This document describes an RFC 3933 experiment in the Working Group formation process, known as the Exploratory Group.  Exploratory Groups may be created as the first step toward Working Group formation, or as an intermediate step between a Birds of a Feather (BOF) session and Working Group creation.  Exploratory Groups are focused on completion of prerequisites for Working Group formation, and as a result they have a short life-time, with limited opportunities for milestone extension.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationhQUALCOMMdnamejL. Dondetiddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5111eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127012hkeywordsÉwworking group formationcbofrbirds of a featherkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÖr“granking˜crfcd5111irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Experiment in Exploratory Group Formation within the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document describes multicast routing architectures that are currently deployed on the Internet. This document briefly describes those protocols and references their specifications.

This memo also reclassifies several older RFCs to Historic. These RFCs describe multicast routing protocols that were never widely deployed or have fallen into disuse. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRon Bonicadarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savoladdatej‘hfilenamegrfc5110eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-127011hkeywordsÑhRFC 3913hRFC 2189hRFC 2201hRFC 1584kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG¢hógrankingˆcrfcd5110irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Overview of the Internet Multicast Routing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèThis document specifies a payload format for generic Forward Error Correction (FEC) for media data encapsulated in RTP.  It is based on the exclusive-or (parity) operation.  The payload format described in this document allows end systems to apply protection using various protection lengths and levels, in addition to using various protection group sizes to adapt to different media and channel characteristics.  It enables complete recovery of the protected packets or partial recovery of the critical parts of the payload depending on the packet loss situation.  This scheme is completely compatible with non-FEC-capable hosts, so the receivers in a multicast group that do not implement FEC can still work by simply ignoring the protection data.  This specification obsoletes RFC 2733 and RFC 3009.  The FEC specified in this document is not backward compatible with RFC 2733 and RFC 3009. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameeA. Liddatej”ˇhfilenamegrfc5109eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127010hkeywordsÇcfecxrealtime transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateGggrankingıcrfcd5109irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7RTP Payload Format for Generic Forward Error CorrectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThis memo defines a new suboption of the DHCP relay information option that allows the DHCP relay to specify a new value for the Server Identifier option, which is inserted by the DHCP Server.  This allows the DHCP relay to act as the actual DHCP server such that RENEW DHCPREQUESTs will come to the relay instead of going to the server directly.  This gives the relay the opportunity to include the Relay Agent option with appropriate suboptions even on DHCP RENEW messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejR. Johnson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemJ. Kumarasamy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Stappddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5107eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-127009hkeywordsÖcxmlxextensible markup langaugex#dynamic host configuration protocolqRENEW DHCPREQUESTjDHCP RENEWkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG´ïWgrankingÛcrfcd5107irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)DHCP Server Identifier Override SuboptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöThis document specifies EAP-IKEv2, an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) method that is based on the Internet Key Exchange (IKEv2) protocol.  EAP-IKEv2 provides mutual authentication and session key establishment between an EAP peer and an EAP server.  It supports authentication techniques that are based on passwords, high-entropy shared keys, and public key certificates.  EAP-IKEv2 further provides support for cryptographic ciphersuite negotiation, hash function agility, identity confidentiality (in certain modes of operation), fragmentation, and an optional "fast reconnect" mode.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamenD. Kroeselberg¢kaffiliationcNECdnamemA. Pashalidis¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamejF. Bersaniddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5106eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-127008hkeywordsÖx%cryptographic ciphersuite negotiationuhash function agilityxidentity confidentialitymfragmentationsfast reconnect modekobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateG¥Ù°grankingÚcrfcd5106irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbThe Extensible Authentication Protocol-Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (EAP-IKEv2) MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæAn ENUM domain name is tightly coupled with the underlying E.164 number.  The process of verifying whether the Registrant of an ENUM domain name is identical to the Assignee of the corresponding E.164 number is commonly called "validation".  This document describes a signed XML data format -- the Validation Token -- with which Validation Entities can convey successful completion of a validation procedure in a secure fashion. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgenum.atdnamehO. Lendlddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5105eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-127007hkeywordsÇxtelephone number mappingee.164kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGlEWgrankingÒcrfcd5105irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'ENUM Validation Token Format DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies a few extensions to the messages defined in the Audio-Visual Profile with Feedback (AVPF). They are helpful primarily in conversational multimedia scenarios where centralized multipoint functionalities are in use. However, some are also usable in smaller multicast environments and point-to-point calls.

The extensions discussed are messages related to the ITU-T Rec. H.271 Video Back Channel, Full Intra Request, Temporary Maximum Media Stream Bit Rate, and Temporal-Spatial Trade-off. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiS. Wenger¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejU. Chandra¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiB. Burmanddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5104eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127006hkeywordsárreal time protocolrreal-time protocoloitu-t rec. h271rvideo back channelrfull intra requestx'temporary maximum media stream bit ratextemporal-spatial trade-offkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateGørΩgrankingcrfcd5104irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKCodec Control Messages in the RTP Audio-Visual Profile with Feedback (AVPF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8082d7728∂habstractxﬂThis document describes an efficient method for exporting bidirectional flow (Biflow) information using the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol, representing each Biflow using a single Flow Record. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjCERT/NetSAdnamekB. Trammell¢kaffiliationnHitachi EuropednameiE. Boschiddatej”Ùhfilenamegrfc5103eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127005hkeywordsÇkflow recordfbiflowkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG¢tËgrankingÔcrfcd5103irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBBidirectional Flow Export Using IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This memo defines an information model for the IP Flow Information eXport (IPFIX) protocol.  It is used by the IPFIX protocol for encoding measured traffic information and information related to the traffic Observation Point, the traffic Metering Process, and the Exporting Process.  Although developed for the IPFIX protocol, the model is defined in an open way that easily allows using it in other protocols, interfaces, and applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Aitken¢kaffiliationfPayPaldnamehJ. Meyerddatej”Îhfilenamegrfc5102eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-127004hkeywordsÜeipfixx#ip flow information export protocolpmeasured trafficqobservation pointpmetering processqexporting processkobsoletedByÅd7012epages´opublicationDateG¢t‡grankingÓcrfcd5102irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Information Model for IP Flow Information ExportdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6313∂habstracty%This document specifies the IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol that serves for transmitting IP Traffic Flow information over the network.  In order to transmit IP Traffic Flow information from an Exporting Process to an information Collecting Process, a common representation of flow data and a standard means of communicating them is required.  This document describes how the IPFIX Data and Template Records are carried over a number of transport protocols from an IPFIX Exporting Process to an IPFIX Collecting Process. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£ga