atagram Protocol (UDP-Lite).  It defines a set of new MIB objects to characterise the behaviour and performance of transport layer endpoints deploying UDP-Lite.  UDP-Lite resembles UDP, but differs from the semantics of UDP by the addition of a single option.  This adds the capability for variable-length data checksum coverage, which can benefit a class of applications that prefer delivery of (partially) corrupted datagram payload data in preference to discarding the datagram. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnameiG. Renker¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej‘hfilenamegrfc5097eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-127001hkeywordsÑeSMIv2kUDPLITE-MIBxmanagement information basex"lightweight user datagram protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGöÄIgrankingÈcrfcd5097irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMIB for the UDP-Lite protocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≤This document defines a new experimental Mobility Header message and a Mobility option that can be used for experimental extensions to the Mobile IPv6 protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoAzaire NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalliddatej”ˇhfilenamegrfc5096eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-127000hkeywordsÑdmip6omobility headeromobility optionemipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGg~grankingËcrfcd5096irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Mobile IPv6 Experimental MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThe functionality provided by IPv6's Type 0 Routing Header can be exploited in order to achieve traffic amplification over a remote path for the purposes of generating denial-of-service traffic.  This document updates the IPv6 specification to deprecate the use of IPv6 Type 0 Routing Headers, in light of this security concern. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgAfiliasdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationwNeville-Neil ConsultingdnameoG. Neville-Neilddatej”Ühfilenamegrfc5095eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126999hkeywordsÇxipv6 type 0 routing headerutraffic amplificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG`iYgrankingÁcrfcd5095irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Deprecation of Type 0 Routing Headers in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚There is a need for vendor-specific extensions to Mobility Header messages so that Mobile IPv6 vendors are able to extend the protocol for research or deployment purposes.  This document defines a new vendor-specific mobility option. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoAzaire NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Patel¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehK. Leungddatej”Òhfilenamegrfc5094eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126998hkeywordsÉomobility headerdmip6emipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGgvgrankingÊcrfcd5094irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Mobile IPv6 Vendor Specific OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document describes an RTCP XR report block, which reports packet transport parameters.  The report block was developed by BT for pre-standards use in BT's next-generation network.  This document has been produced to describe the report block in sufficient detail to register the block type with IANA in accordance with the Specification Required policy of RFC 3611.  This specification does not standardise the new report block for use outside BT's network.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationbBTdnamegG. Huntddatej”ˇhfilenamegrfc5093eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126997hkeywordsÜwnext-generation networkgrtcp xrxreal time control protocolpextended reportsitransportgmetricskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGlE9grankingÂcrfcd5093irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQBT's eXtended Network Quality RTP Control Protocol Extended Reports (RTCP XR XNQ)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\IMAP (RFC 3501) is a rich protocol for accessing remote message stores. It provides an ideal mechanism for accessing public mailing list archives as well as private and shared message stores. This document defines a URL scheme for referencing objects on an IMAP server.

