naldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsIdentity-Based Cryptography Standard (IBCS) #1: Supersingular Curve Implementations of the BF and BB1 CryptosystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractx˚This document defines an extension to the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) protocol to enable support of Digest Authentication, for use with HTTP-style protocols like the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and HTTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoKayote NetworksdnamejB. Sterman¢kaffiliationnSecureOL, Inc.dnamemD. Sadolevsky¢kaffiliationoKayote NetworksdnamekD. Schwartz¢kaffiliationlCisco SystemdnamekD. Williams¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamegW. Beckddatej”˜hfilenamegrfc5090eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126994hkeywordsÉx*remote authentication dial-in user servicecsipdhttpkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateG¥Ùògranking‚crfcd5090irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*RADIUS Extension for Digest AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThere are various circumstances where it is highly desirable for a Path Computation Client (PCC) to be able to dynamically and automatically discover a set of Path Computation Elements (PCEs), along with information that can be used by the PCC for PCE selection.  When the PCE is a Label Switching Router (LSR) participating in the Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP), or even a server participating passively in the IGP, a simple and efficient way to announce PCEs consists of using IGP flooding.  For that purpose, this document defines extensions to the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) routing protocol for the advertisement of PCE Discovery information within an IS-IS area or within the entire IS-IS routing domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationqCisco System Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamejY. Ikejiri¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehR. Zhangddatej‘␀hfilenamegrfc5089eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126993hkeywordsÉwpath computation clientcpccx*intermediate system to intermediate systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÇŒögranking·crfcd5089irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFIS-IS Protocol Extensions for Path Computation Element (PCE) DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9353∂habstracty‹There are various circumstances where it is highly desirable for a Path Computation Client (PCC) to be able to dynamically and automatically discover a set of Path Computation Elements (PCEs), along with information that can be used by the PCC for PCE selection.  When the PCE is a Label Switching Router (LSR) participating in the Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP), or even a server participating passively in the IGP, a simple and efficient way to announce PCEs consists of using IGP flooding.  For that purpose, this document defines extensions to the Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) routing protocol for the advertisement of PCE Discovery information within an OSPF area or within the entire OSPF routing domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamekJL. Le Roux¢kaffiliationqCisco System Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamejY. Ikejiri¢kaffiliationbBTdnamehR. Zhangddatej‘␀hfilenamegrfc5088eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126992hkeywordsÉcpccwpath computation clientxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÇŒÇgranking‡crfcd5088irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEOSPF Protocol Extensions for Path Computation Element (PCE) DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9353∂habstractyITime Division Multiplexing over IP (TDMoIP) is a structure-aware method for transporting Time Division Multiplexed (TDM) signals using pseudowires (PWs).  Being structure-aware, TDMoIP is able to ensure TDM structure integrity, and thus withstand network degradations better than structure-agnostic transport.  Structure-aware methods can distinguish individual channels, enabling packet loss concealment and bandwidth conservation.  Accessibility of TDM signaling facilitates mechanisms that exploit or manipulate signaling.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnamekY(J). Stein¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnamekR. Shashoua¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnameiR. Insler¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnamehM. Anaviddatej”—hfilenamegrfc5087eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126991hkeywordsÖcTDMjpseudowiredPWE3fTDMoIPxstructure-aware TDM emulationkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateGglgrankingﬂcrfcd5087irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Time Division Multiplexing over IP (TDMoIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis document describes a method for encapsulating structured (NxDS0) Time Division Multiplexed (TDM) signals as pseudowires over packet-switching networks (PSNs).  In this regard, it complements similar work for structure-agnostic emulation of TDM bit-streams (see RFC 4553).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoAxerra NetworksdnamemA. Vainshtein¢kaffiliationoAxerra NetworksdnameiI. Sasson¢kaffiliationcKPNdnamegE. Metz¢kaffiliationuZarlink SemiconductordnamehT. Frost¢kaffiliationqOverture NetworksdnamegP. Pateddatej”«hfilenamegrfc5086eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126990hkeywordsÇenxds0cpsnkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateGgbgrankingﬁcrfcd5086irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpStructure-Aware Time Division Multiplexed (TDM) Circuit Emulation Service over Packet Switched Network (CESoPSN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyèThis document describes Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV), which provides a control channel that is associated with a pseudowire (PW), as well as the corresponding operations and management functions (such as connectivity verification) to be used over that control channel.  VCCV applies to all supported access circuit and transport types currently defined for PWs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataroddatej”¸hfilenamegrfc5085eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126989hkeywordsÅbpwkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGlE&granking›crfcd5085irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Pseudowire Virtual Circuit Connectivity Verification (VCCV): A Control Channel for PseudowiresdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5586∂habstractx⁄This document specifies the conventions for using the