an 8-bit field reserved for single-bit flags.  Several protocols have reserved flags in this field and others are preparing to reserve a sufficient number of flags to exhaust the field.  This document defines an option to the Router Advertisement message that expands the available number of flag bits available. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hindenddatej”¸hfilenamegrfc5075eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126979hkeywordsÖxneighbor discovery protocolcndpuexpanded flags optioncefox!ndp  router advertisement messagekobsoletedByÅd5175epagesopublicationDateGB=(granking”crfcd5075irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&IPv6 Router Advertisement Flags OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywDNSSEC Lookaside Validation (DLV) is a mechanism for publishing DNS Security (DNSSEC) trust anchors outside of the DNS delegation chain.  It allows validating resolvers to validate DNSSEC-signed data from zones whose ancestors either aren't signed or don't publish Delegation Signer (DS) records for their children.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiS. Weilerddatej”˙hfilenamegrfc5074eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126978hkeywordsÇldns securitymtrust anchorskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG:D¿granking“crfcd5074irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!DNSSEC Lookaside Validation (DLV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒIt is highly desired, in several cases, to take into account Traffic Engineering (TE) node capabilities during Multi Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineered Label Switched Path (TE-LSP) selection, such as, for instance, the capability to act as a branch Label Switching Router (LSR) of a Point-To-MultiPoint (P2MP) LSP.  This requires advertising these capabilities within the Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP).  For that purpose, this document specifies Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) and Intermediate System-Intermediate System (IS-IS) traffic engineering extensions for the advertisement of control plane and data plane traffic engineering node capabilities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ.P. Vasseur¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelJ.L. Le Rouxddatej”˛hfilenamegrfc5073eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126977hkeywordsÅxinterior gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG`igranking—crfcd5073irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVIGP Routing Protocol Extensions for Discovery of Traffic Engineering Node CapabilitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) provides a standard method of encapsulating network-layer protocol information over point-to-point links. PPP also defines an extensible Link Control Protocol, and proposes a family of Network Control Protocols (NCPs) for establishing and configuring different network-layer protocols.

This document defines the method for sending IPv6 packets over PPP links, the NCP for establishing and configuring the IPv6 over PPP, and the method for forming IPv6 link-local addresses on PPP links.

It also specifies the conditions for performing Duplicate Address Detection on IPv6 global unicast addresses configured for PPP links either through stateful or stateless address autoconfiguration.

