iversity of AmsterdamdnamelY. Demchenkoddatej”˛hfilenamegrfc5070eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdinchdfullx!inch - Extended Incident HandlingdnamexExtended Incident Handlingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126974hkeywordsàtincident data formatxcompuer security incidentx(computer security incident response teamecsirtdcertusecurity data sharingx)computer network defense service providerecndspkobsoletedByÅd7970epages\opublicationDateGg(grankingŒcrfcd5070irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/The Incident Object Description Exchange FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6685∂habstractyVThis document reviews the security threats associated with the marking of signalling messages to indicate that they are related to an emergency, and with the process of mapping locations to Universal Resource Identifiers (URIs) that point to Public Safety Answering Points (PSAPs). This mapping occurs as part of the process of routing emergency calls through the IP network.

Based on the identified threats, this document establishes a set of security requirements for the mapping protocol and for the handling of emergency-marked calls. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylor¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationgDetecondnamelM. Shanmugamddatej”˚hfilenamegrfc5069eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126973hkeywordsÉeecritxpublic safety answering pointsdpaspkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÇŒxgrankingÕcrfcd5069irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHSecurity Threats and Requirements for Emergency Call Marking and MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyˆEmail has become a popular distribution service for a variety of socially unacceptable, mass-effect purposes. The most obvious ones include spam and worms. This note recommends conventions for the operation of email submission and transport services between independent operators, such as enterprises and Internet Service Providers. Its goal is to improve lines of accountability for controlling abusive uses of the Internet mail service. To this end, this document offers recommendations for constructive operational policies between independent operators of email submission and transmission services.

Email authentication technologies are aimed at providing assurances and traceability between internetworked networks. In many email services, the weakest link in the chain of assurances is initial submission of a message. This document offers recommendations for constructive operational policies for this first step of email sending, the submission (or posting) of email into the transmission network. Relaying and delivery entail policies that occur subsequent to submission and are outside the scope of this document. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Hutzler¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationuQUALCOMM IncorporateddnamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnameiE. Allman¢kaffiliationx)University of Cambridge Computing ServicednamehT. Finchddatej”˙hfilenamegrfc5068eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126972hkeywordsò#dspamkemail abusehphishingeemailfe-mailgservicedmimegrfc2822hrfc 2822frfc822grfc 822grfc2821hrfc 2821frfc821grfc 821larchitecturecmtacmuacmsacmdaduserhdeliveryerelayfheadermgateway agentesievecdsncmdnftusslecmhsumail handling servicevmessage transfer agentrmessage user agentumail submission agentsmail delivery agentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG2fﬁgrankingÃcrfcd5068irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÜetotaletitlexCEmail Submission Operations: Access and Accountability RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8314∂habstractyPThis document provides requirements for "infrastructure" or "carrier" ENUM (E.164 Number Mapping), defined as the use of RFC 3761 technology to facilitate interconnection of networks for E.164 number addressed services, in particular but not restricted to VoIP (Voice over IP.) This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamegS. Lind¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiP. Pfautzddatej”˚hfilenamegrfc5067eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126971hkeywordsÇte.164 number mappinggcarrierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGB= grankingÀcrfcd5067irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Infrastructure ENUM RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·This document defines Management Information Base (MIB) modules for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  This document describes extensions to the Ethernet-like Interfaces MIB and Medium Attachment Unit (MAU) MIB modules with a set of objects for managing Ethernet in the First Mile Copper (EFMCu) interfaces 10PASS-TS and 2BASE-TL, defined in IEEE Std 802.3ah-2004 (note: IEEE Std 802.3ah-2004 has been integrated into IEEE Std 802.3- 2005).  In addition, a set of objects is defined, describing cross- connect capability of a managed device with multi-layer (stacked) interfaces, extending the stack management objects in the Interfaces Group MIB and the Inverted Stack Table MIB modules. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpActelis NetworksdnamehE. Beiliddatej”˙hfilenamegrfc5066eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126970hkeywordsårNetwork Managementx"Simple Network Management ProtocoldSNMPxManagement Information BasecMIBsTextual Conventionsh2BASE-TLi10PASS-TSg802.3ahcEFMcPAFcPMEkobsoletedByÄepagesZopublicationDateGC6granking crfcd5066irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Ethernet in the First Mile Copper (EFMCu) Interfaces MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7124∂habstracty#The Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is an inter-autonomous system routing protocol designed for Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol (TCP/IP) networks. BGP requires that all BGP speakers within a single autonomous system (AS) must be fully meshed. This represents a serious scaling problem that has been well documented in a number of proposals.

This document describes an extension to BGP that may be used to create a confederation of autonomous systems that is represented as a single autonomous system to BGP peers external to the confederation, thereby removing the "full mesh" requirement. The intention of this extension is to aid in policy administration and reduce the management complexity of maintaining a large autonomous system.

This document obsoletes RFC 3065. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrBlissfully RetireddnameiP. Traina¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudderddatej”€hfilenamegrfc5065eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126969hkeywordsÉwborder gateway protocolftcp/ipifull meshkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπÙgranking…crfcd5065irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Autonomous System Confederations for BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9774∂habstractxôThis memo defines a new email header field, Archived-At:, to provide a direct link to the archived form of an individual email message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxAoyama Gakuin UniversitydnameiM. DuerstddatejÃ]hfilenamegrfc5064eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126968hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateGlEgranking»crfcd5064irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$The Archived-At Message Header FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document describes extensions to the Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Graceful Restart mechanisms defined in RFC 3473. The extensions enable the recovery of RSVP signaling state based on the Path message last sent by the node being restarted.

Previously defined Graceful Restart mechanisms, also called recovery from nodal faults, permit recovery of signaling state from adjacent nodes when the data plane has retained the associated forwarding state across a restart. Those mechanisms do not fully support signaling state recovery on ingress nodes or recovery of all RSVP objects.

The extensions defined in this document build on the RSVP Hello extensions defined in RFC 3209, and extensions for state recovery on nodal faults defined in RFC 3473. Using these extensions, the restarting node can recover all previously transmitted Path state, including the Explicit Route Object and the downstream (outgoing) interface identifiers. The extensions can also be used to recover signaling state after the restart of an ingress node.

These extensions are not used to create or restore data plane state.

The extensions optionally support the use of Summary Refresh, defined in RFC 2961, to reduce the number of messages exchanged during the Recovery Phase when the restarting node has recovered signaling state locally for one or more Label Switched Paths (LSPs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamepA. Satyanarayana¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Rahmanddatej”¯hfilenamegrfc5063eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126967hkeywordsÉlnodal faultsjrsvp hellonstate recoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG`-granking«crfcd5063irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIExtensions to GMPLS Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Graceful RestartdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes certain security threats to SCTP.  It also describes ways to mitigate these threats, in particular by using techniques from the SCTP Specification Errata and Issues memo (RFC 4460).  These techniques are included in RFC 4960, which obsoletes RFC 2960.  It is hoped that this information will provide some useful background information for many of the newest requirements spelled out in the SCTP Specification Errata and Issues and included in RFC 4960.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. CamarilloddatejÃUhfilenamegrfc5062eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126966hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÙDgranking∆crfcd5062irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjSecurity Attacks Found Against the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) and Current CountermeasuresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖA local host may have multiple points of attachment to the Internet, giving it a degree of fault tolerance from hardware failures.  Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) (RFC 4960) was developed to take full advantage of such a multi-homed host to provide a fast failover and association survivability in the face of such hardware failures.  This document describes an extension to SCTP that will allow an SCTP stack to dynamically add an IP address to an SCTP association, dynamically delete an IP address from an SCTP association, and to request to set the primary address the peer will use when sending to an endpoint. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationx"Univ. of Applied Sciences MuensterdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationpKyoto UniversitydnamekS. Maruyama¢kaffiliationpKyoto UniversitydnameiM. KozukaddatejÃNhfilenamegrfc5061eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126965hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateFÙDgranking≈crfcd5061irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKStream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Dynamic Address ReconfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for managing the Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) protocols: PIM-SM (Sparse Mode), BIDIR-PIM (Bidirectional), and PIM-DM (Dense Mode).  This document is part of work in progress to obsolete RFC 2934, and is to be preferred where the two documents overlap.  This document does not obsolete RFC 2934. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekR. Sivaramu¢kaffiliationlArastra, IncdnamejJ. Lingard¢kaffiliationsData Connection LtddnamekD. McWalter¢kaffiliationxInfosys Technologies LtddnamehB. Joshi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Kesslerddatej”›hfilenamegrfc5060eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-126964hkeywordsácPIMfPIM-SMiBIDIR-PIMfPIM-DMqMulticast RoutingkPIM-STD-MIBxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesZopublicationDateGöÄgrankingƒcrfcd5060irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Protocol Independent Multicast MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document specifies the Bootstrap Router (BSR) mechanism for the class of multicast routing protocols in the PIM (Protocol Independent Multicast) family that use the concept of a Rendezvous Point as a means for receivers to discover the sources that send to a particular multicast group.  BSR is one way that a multicast router can learn the set of group-to-RP mappings required in order to function.  The mechanism is dynamic, largely self-configuring, and robust to router failure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejDavid Warddarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgArastradnamejN. Bhaskar¢kaffiliationfSWITCHdnamegA. Gall¢kaffiliationgArastradnamejJ. Lingard¢kaffiliationgUNINETTdnameiS. Venaasddatej”¸hfilenamegrfc5059eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-126963hkeywordsÉprendezvous pointbrppmulticast routerkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateGöÄågranking√crfcd5059irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIBootstrap Router (BSR) Mechanism for Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9436d8736µhabstracty≥While traditional IP multicast schemes (RFC 1112) are scalable for very large multicast groups, they have scalability issues with a very large number of distinct multicast groups. This document describes Xcast (Explicit Multi-unicast), a new multicast scheme with complementary scaling properties: Xcast supports a very large number of small multicast sessions. Xcast achieves this by explicitly encoding the list of destinations in the data packets, instead of using a multicast group address.

This document discusses Xcast concepts and options in several areas; it does not provide a complete technical specification. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss CallongauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiR. Boivie¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejN. Feldman¢kaffiliationnWIDE / FujitsudnamegY. Imai¢kaffiliationfESCAUXdnameiW. Livens¢kaffiliationjOneSparrowdnamegD. Oomsddatej—Chfilenamegrfc5058eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126962hkeywordsÅvexplicit multi-unicastkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateGB=granking¬crfcd5058irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex/Explicit Multicast (Xcast) Concepts and OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6Several methods in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) can create an association between endpoints known as a dialog. Some of these methods can also create a different, but related, association within an existing dialog. These multiple associations, or dialog usages, require carefully coordinated processing as they have independent life-cycles, but share common dialog state. Processing multiple dialog usages correctly is not completely understood. What is understood is difficult to implement.

This memo argues that multiple dialog usages should be avoided. It discusses alternatives to their use and clarifies essential behavior for elements that cannot currently avoid them.

This is an informative document and makes no normative statements of any kind. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnameiR. SparksddatejÃ[hfilenamegrfc5057eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126961hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG1ògranking¡crfcd5057irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Multiple Dialog Usages in the Session Initiation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—The concept of channel binding allows applications to establish that the two end-points of a secure channel at one network layer are the same as at a higher layer by binding authentication at the higher layer to the channel at the lower layer. This allows applications to delegate session protection to lower layers, which has various performance benefits.

This document discusses and formalizes the concept of channel binding to secure channels. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. WilliamsddatejÃ]hfilenamegrfc5056eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126960hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG>[ìgranking¿crfcd5056irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1On the Use of Channel Bindings to Secure ChannelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‰The Server-Based Certificate Validation Protocol (SCVP) allows a client to delegate certification path construction and certification path validation to a server.  The path construction or validation (e.g., making sure that none of the certificates in the path are revoked) is performed according to a validation policy, which contains one or more trust anchors.  It allows simplification of client implementations and use of a set of predefined validation policies. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnMicrosoft CorpdnamejT. Freeman¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationxMalpani Consulting ServicesdnamejA. Malpani¢kaffiliationdNISTdnameiD. Cooper¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegW. Polkddatej”¸hfilenamegrfc5055eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126959hkeywordsÇxcertification path constructionxcertification path validationkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateG`igrankingøcrfcd5055irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Server-Based Certificate Validation Protocol (SCVP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxœThis memo presents a technique for using the Secure Remote Password protocol as an authentication method for the Transport Layer Security protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiD. Taylor¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameeT. Wu¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnametN. Mavrogiannopoulos¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiT. Perrinddatej”˚hfilenamegrfc5054eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126958hkeywordsÇxsecure remote password protocolxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGC,grankingæcrfcd5054irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFUsing the Secure Remote Password (SRP) Protocol for TLS AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty±This document describes a Fully-Specified Forward Error Correction (FEC) scheme, corresponding to FEC Encoding ID 1, for the Raptor forward error correction code and its application to reliable delivery of data objects.

Raptor is a fountain code, i.e., as many encoding symbols as needed can be generated by the encoder on-the-fly from the source symbols of a source block of data. The decoder is able to recover the source block from any set of encoding symbols only slightly more in number than the number of source symbols.

The Raptor code described here is a systematic code, meaning that all the source symbols are among the encoding symbols that can be generated. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationdEPFLdnamenA. Shokrollahi¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationnNomor ResearchdnamenT. Stockhammerddatej”ˆhfilenamegrfc5053eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126957hkeywordsÉcfeclfec encodingkraptor codekobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateG=grankingΩcrfcd5053irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Raptor Forward Error Correction Scheme for Object DeliverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis document describes how to use Forward Error Correction (FEC) codes to efficiently provide and/or augment reliability for bulk data transfer over IP multicast.  This document defines a framework for the definition of the information that needs to be communicated in order to use an FEC code for bulk data transfer, in addition to the encoded data itself, and for definition of formats and codes for communication of that information.  Both information communicated with the encoded data itself and information that needs to be communicated 'out-of-band' are considered.  The procedures for specifying new FEC codes, defining the information communication requirements associated with those codes and registering them with the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) are also described.  The requirements on Content Delivery Protocols that wish to use FEC codes defined within this framework are also defined.  The companion document titled "The Use of Forward Error Correction (FEC) in Reliable Multicast" describes some applications of FEC codes for delivering content.  This document obsoletes RFC 3452. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamekL. Vicisanoddatej”Ïhfilenamegrfc5052eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126956hkeywordsÅrbulk data transferkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπÂgrankingºcrfcd5052irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Forward Error Correction (FEC) Building BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≤This document describes "i;unicode-casemap", a simple case-insensitive collation for Unicode strings.  It provides equality, substring, and ordering operations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamejM. Crispinddatej”˚hfilenamegrfc5051eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126955hkeywordsÇounicode stringsoi;ascii-casemapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGè≤grankingªcrfcd5051irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6i;unicode-casemap - Simple Unicode Collation AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyŒThis document describes the end-to-end protocol, block formats, and abstract service description for the exchange of messages (bundles) in Delay Tolerant Networking (DTN).

This document was produced within the IRTF's Delay Tolerant Networking Research Group (DTNRG) and represents the consensus of all of the active contributors to this group. See http://www.dtnrg.org for more information. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamehK. Scott¢kaffiliationxNASA Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamekS. Burleighddatej”˙hfilenamegrfc5050eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-126954hkeywordsÑxdelay tolerant networkingcdtnedtnrgtexchange of messageskobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateG>[ägranking∫crfcd5050irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexBundle Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—This document describes some specifics that apply when Signaling Compression (SigComp) is applied to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), such as default minimum values of SigComp parameters, compartment and state management, and a few issues on SigComp over TCP.  Any implementation of SigComp for use with SIP must conform to this document and SigComp, and in addition, support the SIP and Session Description Protocol (SDP) static dictionary. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamefZ. Liu¢kaffiliationx)EADS Defence and Security Systems LimiteddnamehR. Price¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. CamarilloddatejÃahfilenamegrfc5049eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126953hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG`h˙grankingπcrfcd5049irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQApplying Signaling Compression (SigComp) to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty—The Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) is a SCSI transport protocol and maps the SCSI architecture and command sets onto TCP/IP.  RFC 3720 defines the iSCSI protocol.  This document compiles the clarifications to the original protocol definition in RFC 3720 to serve as a companion document for the iSCSI implementers.  This document updates RFC 3720 and the text in this document supersedes the text in RFC 3720 when the two differ. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsHewlett-Packard Co.dnamenM. Chadalapakaddatej”Ôhfilenamegrfc5048eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126952hkeywordsÇdscsiniscsi protocolkobsoletedByÅd7143epages&opublicationDateG)∞granking∏crfcd5048irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) Corrections and ClarificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyùThe Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) is a SCSI transport protocol that maps the SCSI family of application protocols onto TCP/IP.  Datamover Architecture for iSCSI (DA) defines an abstract model in which the movement of data between iSCSI end nodes is logically separated from the rest of the iSCSI protocol in order to allow iSCSI to adapt to innovations available in new IP transports.  While DA defines the architectural functions required of the class of Datamover protocols, it does not define any specific Datamover protocols.  Each such Datamover protocol, defined in a separate document, provides a reliable transport for all iSCSI PDUs, but actually moves the data required for certain iSCSI PDUs without involving the remote iSCSI layer itself.  This document begins with an introduction of a few new abstractions, defines a layered architecture for iSCSI and Datamover protocols, and then models the interactions within an iSCSI end node between the iSCSI layer and the Datamover layer that happen in order to transparently perform remote data movement within an IP fabric.  It is intended that this definition will help map iSCSI to generic Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA)-capable IP fabrics in the future comprising TCP, the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP), and possibly other underlying network transport layers, such as InfiniBand.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationbHPdnamenM. Chadalapaka¢kaffiliationlBrocade Inc.dnamejJ. Hufferd¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiJ. Satran¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamegH. Shahddatej”ÿhfilenamegrfc5047eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126951hkeywordsÇwscsi transport protocolftcp/ipkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateG)©granking∑crfcd5047irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSDA: Datamover Architecture for the Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyHInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) Extensions for Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) provides the RDMA data transfer capability to iSCSI by layering iSCSI on top of an RDMA-Capable Protocol, such as the iWARP protocol suite.  An RDMA-Capable Protocol provides RDMA Read and Write services, which enable data to be transferred directly into SCSI I/O Buffers without intermediate data copies.  This document describes the extensions to the iSCSI protocol to support RDMA services as provided by an RDMA-Capable Protocol, such as the iWARP protocol suite. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameeM. Ko¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamenM. Chadalapaka¢kaffiliationmBrocade, Inc.dnamejJ. Hufferd¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamehU. Elzur¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnameiP. Thalerddatej”◊hfilenamegrfc5046eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126950hkeywordsÅrrdma data transferkobsoletedByÅd7145epagesUopublicationDateG)ügranking∂crfcd5046irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) Extensions for Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyFThis document describes the applicability of Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol (RDMAP) and the Direct Data Placement Protocol (DDP).  It compares and contrasts the different transport options over IP that DDP can use, provides guidance to ULP developers on choosing between available transports and/or how to be indifferent to the specific transport layer used, compares use of DDP with direct use of the supporting transports, and compares DDP over IP transports with non-IP transports that support RDMA functionality.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhNeteriondnamejC. Bestler¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamehL. Coeneddatej”ªhfilenamegrfc5045eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126949hkeywordsÅerdmapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG)çgrankingµcrfcd5045irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Applicability of Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol (RDMA) and Direct Data Placement (DDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstracty\Marker PDU Aligned Framing (MPA) is designed to work as an "adaptation layer" between TCP and the Direct Data Placement protocol (DDP) as described in RFC 5041.  It preserves the reliable, in-order delivery of TCP, while adding the preservation of higher-level protocol record boundaries that DDP requires.  MPA is fully compliant with applicable TCP RFCs and can be utilized with existing TCP implementations.  MPA also supports integrated implementations that combine TCP, MPA and DDP to reduce buffering requirements in the implementation and improve performance at the system level. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnameiP. Culley¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamehU. Elzur¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehR. Recio¢kaffiliationuSandburst CorporationdnameiS. Bailey¢kaffiliationiCray Inc.dnamejJ. Carrierddatej”«hfilenamegrfc5044eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126948hkeywordsÖcmpaqadaaptation layercddpxdirect data placement protocolltransmissionkobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDateG)Ögranking¥crfcd5044irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Marker PDU Aligned Framing for TCP SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7146d6581∂habstractxƒThis document specifies an adaptation layer to provide a Lower Layer Protocol (LLP) service for Direct Data Placement (DDP) using the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhNeteriondnamejC. Bestler¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Stewartddatej”Éhfilenamegrfc5043eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126947hkeywordsÇtlower layer protocolcllpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG)|granking≥crfcd5043irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRStream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Direct Data Placement (DDP) AdaptationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7146d6581∂habstractyÚThis document analyzes security issues around implementation and use of the Direct Data Placement Protocol (DDP) and Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol (RDMAP).  It first defines an architectural model for an RDMA Network Interface Card (RNIC), which can implement DDP or RDMAP and DDP.  The document reviews various attacks against the resources defined in the architectural model and the countermeasures that can be used to protect the system.  Attacks are grouped into those that can be mitigated by using secure communication channels across the network, attacks from Remote Peers, and attacks from Local Peers.  Attack categories include spoofing, tampering, information disclosure, denial of service, and elevation of privilege. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamelJ. Pinkerton¢kaffiliationdSelfdnamelE. Deleganesddatej”∫hfilenamegrfc5042eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126946hkeywordsÇxrdma network interface carddrnickobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateG)ugranking≤crfcd5042irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Direct Data Placement Protocol (DDP) / Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol (RDMAP) SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstracty-The Direct Data Placement protocol provides information to Place the incoming data directly into an upper layer protocol's receive buffer without intermediate buffers.  This removes excess CPU and memory utilization associated with transferring data through the intermediate buffers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamegH. Shah¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamelJ. Pinkerton¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehR. Recio¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnameiP. Culleyddatej”∆hfilenamegrfc5041eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126945hkeywordsÇcddpccpukobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateG)kgranking±crfcd5041irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Direct Data Placement over Reliable TransportsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyÑThis document defines a Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol (RDMAP) that operates over the Direct Data Placement Protocol (DDP protocol).  RDMAP provides read and write services directly to applications and enables data to be transferred directly into Upper Layer Protocol (ULP) Buffers without intermediate data copies.  It also enables a kernel bypass implementation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehR. Recio¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamejB. Metzler¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnameiP. Culley¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamejJ. Hilland¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Garciaddatej”∆hfilenamegrfc5040eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126944hkeywordsÉerdmapcddpxdirect data placement protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateG)egranking∞crfcd5040irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4A Remote Direct Memory Access Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyÃSpam, defined as the transmission of bulk unsolicited messages, has plagued Internet email.  Unfortunately, spam is not limited to email.  It can affect any system that enables user-to-user communications.  The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) defines a system for user-to- user multimedia communications.  Therefore, it is susceptible to spam, just as email is.  In this document, we analyze the problem of spam in SIP.  We first identify the ways in which the problem is the same and the ways in which it is different from email.  We then examine the various possible solutions that have been discussed for email and consider their applicability to SIP.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekC. JenningsddatejÃYhfilenamegrfc5039eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126943hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG~…ÛgrankingØcrfcd5039irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and SpamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS), described in RFC 3031, is a method for forwarding packets that uses short, fixed-length values carried by packets, called labels, to determine packet next hops.  A fundamental concept in MPLS is that two Label Switching Routers (LSRs) must agree on the meaning of the labels used to forward traffic between and through them.  This common understanding is achieved by using a set of procedures, called a Label Distribution Protocol (as described in RFC 3036) , by which one LSR informs another of label bindings it has made.  One such protocol, called LDP, is used by LSRs to distribute labels to support MPLS forwarding along normally routed paths.  This document reports on a survey of LDP implementations conducted in August 2002 as part of the process of advancing LDP from Proposed to Draft Standard.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnameiB. Thomas¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelL. Anderssonddatej”Âhfilenamegrfc5038eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126942hkeywordsÑxmultiprotocol label switchingdmplsclsrvlabel switched routerskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÃÎgrankingÆcrfcd5038irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCThe Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) Implementation Survey ResultsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£The purpose of this memo is to document how some of the requirements specified in RFC 1264 for advancing protocols developed by working groups within the IETF Routing Area to Draft Standard have been satisfied by LDP (Label Distribution Protocol).  Specifically, this report documents operational experience with LDP, requirement 5 of section 5.0 in RFC 1264.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. ThomasddatejÃ?hfilenamegrfc5037eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126941hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÃ·granking≠crfcd5037irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Experience with the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThe architecture for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) is described in RFC 3031.  A fundamental concept in MPLS is that two Label Switching Routers (LSRs) must agree on the meaning of the labels used to forward traffic between and through them.  This common understanding is achieved by using a set of procedures, called a label distribution protocol, by which one LSR informs another of label bindings it has made.  This document defines a set of such procedures called LDP (for Label Distribution Protocol) by which LSRs distribute labels to support MPLS forwarding along normally routed paths. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehI. Minei¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiB. Thomasddatej”Âhfilenamegrfc5036eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126940hkeywordsÉelabelldistributionhprotocolkobsoletedByÄepagesáopublicationDateGÃ÷granking¨crfcd5036irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqLDP SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÑd7358d6720d7552d6790∂habstractymIn the original Enhanced Security Services for S/MIME document (RFC 2634), a structure for cryptographically linking the certificate to be used in validation with the signature was introduced; this structure was hardwired to use SHA-1.  This document allows for the structure to have algorithm agility and defines a new attribute for this purpose. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej”hfilenamegrfc5035eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126939hkeywordsÉjvalidationisignaturekcertificatekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFœaÁgranking´crfcd5035irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHEnhanced Security Services (ESS) Update: Adding CertID Algorithm AgilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document defines a profile of the Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) for the Post Office Protocol (POP3). This extension allows a POP3 client to indicate an authentication mechanism to the server, perform an authentication protocol exchange, and optionally negotiate a security layer for subsequent protocol interactions during this session.

This document seeks to consolidate the information related to POP3 AUTH into a single document. To this end, this document obsoletes and replaces RFC 1734, and updates the information contained in Section 6.3 of RFC 2449. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamemR. Siemborski¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmbHdnamelA. Menon-Senddatej”·hfilenamegrfc5034eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126938hkeywordsÖiPOP3-AUTHdPostfOfficehProtocoleEmailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFßsÿgranking™crfcd5034irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhThe Post Office Protocol (POP3) Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) Authentication MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThe IETF's standard congestion control schemes have been widely shown to be inadequate for various environments (e.g., high-speed networks).  Recent research has yielded many alternate congestion control schemes that significantly differ from the IETF's congestion control principles.  Using these new congestion control schemes in the global Internet has possible ramifications to both the traffic using the new congestion control and to traffic using the currently standardized congestion control.  Therefore, the IETF must proceed with caution when dealing with alternate congestion control proposals.  The goal of this document is to provide guidance for considering alternate congestion control algorithms within the IETF.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkICIR / ICSIdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationkICIR / ICSIdnameiM. AllmanddatejÃQhfilenamegrfc5033eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126937hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9743epages
opublicationDateFÃ”?granking©crfcd5033irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Specifying New Congestion Control AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document describes the WITHIN extension to IMAP SEARCH.  IMAP SEARCH returns messages whose internal date is within or outside a specified interval.  The mechanism described here, OLDER and YOUNGER, differs from BEFORE and SINCE in that the client specifies an interval, rather than a date.  WITHIN is useful for persistent searches where either the device does not have the capacity to perform the search at regular intervals or the network is of limited bandwidth and thus there is a desire to reduce network traffic from sending repeated requests and redundant responses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqBEA Systems, Inc.dnameiE. Burgerddatej”Ùhfilenamegrfc5032eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126936hkeywordsÉkimap searcheoldergyoungerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF‡##granking®crfcd5032irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,WITHIN Search Extension to the IMAP ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^The content of many communication services depends on the context, such as the user's location.  We describe a 'service' URN that allows well-known context-dependent services that can be resolved in a distributed manner to be identified.  Examples include emergency services, directory assistance, and call-before-you-dig hot lines. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”˘hfilenamegrfc5031eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126935hkeywordsÇcurneecritkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÇŒmgrankingßcrfcd5031irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIA Uniform Resource Name (URN) for Emergency and Other Well-Known ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7163∂habstractyZThis document provides an applicability statement as well as a scope definition for specifying Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) extensions to support Mobile IPv4.  The goal is to allow specification of RADIUS attributes to assist the Mobile IPv4 signaling procedures.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekM. Nakhjiri¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamegA. Lior¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leungddatej”¯hfilenamegrfc5030eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126934hkeywordsÉx*remote authentication dial-in user servicecmipemipv4kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateG!/•granking¶crfcd5030irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMobile IPv4 RADIUS RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxëThis document defines a sub-TLV called "Link-attributes" carried within the TLV 22 and used to flood some link characteristics. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Prevididdatej”ıhfilenamegrfc5029eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126933hkeywordsÇolink-attributesftlv 22kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÚó¶granking•crfcd5029irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Definition of an IS-IS Link Attribute Sub-TLVdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9650∂habstractx◊This document registers a Telephone Number Mapping (ENUM) service for Instant Messaging (IM).  Specifically, this document focuses on provisioning 'im:' URIs (Uniform Resource Identifiers) in ENUM. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkPlantronicsdnamegR. Mahyddatej”¯hfilenamegrfc5028eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126932hkeywordsÉe'im:'curixuniform resource identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGì‰granking§crfcd5028irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZA Telephone Number Mapping (ENUM) Service Registration for Instant Messaging (IM) ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractyRThis document defines a new security precondition for the Session Description Protocol (SDP) precondition framework described in RFCs 3312 and 4032.  A security precondition can be used to delay session establishment or modification until media stream security for a secure media stream has been negotiated successfully. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wingddatej”˜hfilenamegrfc5027eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126931hkeywordsÜdDTLSiDTLS-SRTPcTLSeMIKEYuSecurity DescriptionsdSRTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG`$granking£crfcd5027irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKSecurity Preconditions for Session Description Protocol (SDP) Media StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA Mobile IPv6 node requires a Home Agent address, a home address, and IPsec security associations with its Home Agent before it can start utilizing Mobile IPv6 service.  RFC 3775 requires that some or all of these are statically configured.  This document defines how a Mobile IPv6 node can bootstrap this information from non-topological information and security credentials pre-configured on the Mobile Node.  The solution defined in this document solves the split scenario described in the Mobile IPv6 bootstrapping problem statement in RFC 4640.  The split scenario refers to the case where the Mobile Node's mobility service is authorized by a different service provider than basic network access.  The solution described in this document is also generically applicable to any bootstrapping case, since other scenarios are more specific realizations of the split scenario. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Giaretta¢kaffiliationoDoCoMo Labs USAdnamehJ. Kempf¢kaffiliationoAzaire NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalliddatej”¯hfilenamegrfc5026eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126930hkeywordsÇdmip6xbootstrapping problem statementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG)+granking¢crfcd5026irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Mobile IPv6 Bootstrapping in Split ScenariodtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstracty◊Authorization is a key function in presence systems.  Authorization policies, also known as authorization rules, specify what presence information can be given to which watchers, and when.  This specification defines an Extensible Markup Language (XML) document format for expressing presence authorization rules.  Such a document can be manipulated by clients using the XML Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP), although other techniques are permitted. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”˜hfilenamegrfc5025eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126929hkeywordsÑppresence systemsvauthorization policiescxmlxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGgCgranking°crfcd5025irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPresence Authorization RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyπThis memo updates the ODETTE File Transfer Protocol, an established file transfer protocol facilitating electronic data interchange of business data between trading partners, to version 2.

The protocol now supports secure and authenticated communication over the Internet using Transport Layer Security, provides file encryption, signing, and compression using Cryptographic Message Syntax, and provides signed receipts for the acknowledgement of received files.

The protocol supports both direct peer-to-peer communication and indirect communication via a Value Added Network and may be used with TCP/IP, X.25, and ISDN-based networks. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. DusseaultgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfODETTEdnameiI. FriendddatejÃhfilenamegrfc5024eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126928hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesáopublicationDateG1çgranking†crfcd5024irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex!ODETTE File Transfer Protocol 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThe Atom Publishing Protocol (AtomPub) is an application-level protocol for publishing and editing Web resources.  The protocol is based on HTTP transfer of Atom-formatted representations.  The Atom format is documented in the Atom Syndication Format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamekJ. Gregorio¢kaffiliationoNewBay SoftwarednamejB. de hOraddatej”ˆhfilenamegrfc5023eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgatompubdfullx-atompub - Atom Publishing Format and Protocoldnamex#Atom Publishing Format and Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126927hkeywordsÉgatompubmhttp transferwatom syndication formatkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateG
