tweight Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) Profile for High-Volume EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThis document specifies how a Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP) client establishes a connection to a BFCP floor control server outside the context of an offer/answer exchange.  Client and server authentication are based on Transport Layer Security (TLS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”ˆhfilenamegrfc5018eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126923hkeywordsÉtfloor control serverctlsxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateF¯;@grankingöcrfcd5018irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDConnection Establishment in the Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThis MIB module defines textual conventions to represent STD 66 Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs).  The intent is that these textual conventions will be imported and used in MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representation(s). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsData Connection LtddnamekD. McWalterddatej”Ühfilenamegrfc5017eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126922hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basejURI-TC-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÎ&:grankingôcrfcd5017irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?MIB Textual Conventions for Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) provides a cryptographic mechanism for domains to assert responsibility for the messages they handle.  A related mechanism will allow an administrator to publish various statements about their DKIM signing practices.  This document defines requirements for this mechanism, distinguishing between those that must be satisfied (MUST), and those that are highly desirable (SHOULD).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Thomasddatej”˚hfilenamegrfc5016eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-126921hkeywordsÅmcryptographickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG`grankingòcrfcd5016irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexORequirements for a DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Signing Practices ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°This document discusses Bidirectional PIM (BIDIR-PIM), a variant of PIM Sparse-Mode that builds bidirectional shared trees connecting multicast sources and receivers.  Bidirectional trees are built using a fail-safe Designated Forwarder (DF) election mechanism operating on each link of a multicast topology.  With the assistance of the DF, multicast data is natively forwarded from sources to the Rendezvous-Point (RP) and hence along the shared tree to receivers without requiring source-specific state.  The DF election takes place at RP discovery time and provides the route to the RP, thus eliminating the requirement for data-driven protocol events. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekI. Kouvelas¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekT. Speakman¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamekL. Vicisanoddatej”›hfilenamegrfc5015eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-126920hkeywordsÉcpimksparse-modelshared treeskobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateG`grankingócrfcd5015irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Bidirectional Protocol Independent Multicast (BIDIR-PIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9436d8736∂habstractygThe IPv6 default address selection document (RFC 3484) describes the rules for selecting source and destination IPv6 addresses, and indicates that applications should be able to reverse the sense of some of the address selection rules through some unspecified API.  However, no such socket API exists in the basic (RFC 3493) or advanced (RFC 3542) IPv6 socket API documents.  This document fills that gap partially by specifying new socket-level options for source address selection and flags for the getaddrinfo() API to specify address selection based on the source address preference in accordance with the socket-level options that modify the default source address selection algorithm.  The socket API described in this document will be particularly useful for IPv6 applications that want to choose between temporary and public addresses, and for Mobile IPv6 aware applications that want to use the care-of address for communication.  It also specifies socket options and flags for selecting Cryptographically Generated Address (CGA) or non-CGA source addresses.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationoAzaire NetworksdnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnamekJ. Laganierddatej”Ùhfilenamegrfc5014eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126919hkeywordsÇpgetaddrinfo()cgax#cryptographically generated addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÚóõgrankingñcrfcd5014irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,IPv6 Socket API for Source Address SelectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxµThis document defines fifteen metadata elements for resource description in a cross-disciplinary information environment.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of CaliforniadnamehJ. Kunze¢kaffiliationxDublin Core Metadata InitiativednamehT. Bakerddatej”Úhfilenamegrfc5013eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126918hkeywordsÉtresource descriptionrobject descriptorsxdigital library collectionskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateFÃ”=grankingïcrfcd5013irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$The Dublin Core Metadata Element SetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document defines terminology and enumerates requirements for the context resolution of emergency calls placed by the public using voice-over-IP (VoIP) and general Internet multimedia systems, where Internet protocols are used end to end.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationcTCSdnamekR. Marshallddatej”Ûhfilenamegrfc5012eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeecritdfullx?ecrit - Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdnamex7Emergency Context Resolution with Internet Technologiesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126917hkeywordsÇqemergency callingeecritkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateGÇŒ_grankingîcrfcd5012irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRequirements for Emergency Context Resolution with Internet TechnologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyAThis document describes a means for automated, authenticated, and authorized updating of DNSSEC "trust anchors". The method provides protection against N-1 key compromises of N keys in the trust point key set. Based on the trust established by the presence of a current anchor, other anchors may be added at the same place in the hierarchy, and, ultimately, supplant the existing anchor(s).

This mechanism will require changes to resolver management behavior (but not resolver resolution behavior), and the addition of a single flag bit to the DNSKEY record. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamejM. StJohnsddatej”ıhfilenamegrfc5011eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126916hkeywordsÇgn-1 keyfn keyskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF‡#grankingìcrfcd5011irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberJetotaletitlex8Automated Updates of DNS Security (DNSSEC) Trust AnchorsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This memo defines a new suboption of the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) relay agent information option that allows the DHCP relay to specify flags for the forwarded packet.  One flag is defined to indicate whether the DHCP relay received the packet via a unicast or broadcast packet.  This information may be used by the DHCP server to better serve clients based on whether their request was originally broadcast or unicast. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Normoyle¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Stappddatej”Ûhfilenamegrfc5010eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126915hkeywordsÇlunicast flagnbroadcast flagkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF·Ôdgrankingícrfcd5010irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol Version 4 (DHCPv4) Relay Agent Flags SuboptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes a private Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) header field (P-header) to be used by the European Telecommunications Standards Institute (ETSI) Telecommunications and Internet-converged Services and Protocols for Advanced Networks (TISPAN) for the purpose of authorizing early media flows in Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) IP Multimedia Subsystems (IMS).  This header field is useful in any SIP network that is interconnected with other SIP networks and needs to control the flow of media in the early dialog state.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehR. Ejzakddatej”Ïhfilenamegrfc5009eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126914hkeywordsåcIMScNGNkETSI TISPANfGatingkCut-throughmCall progresshChargingdPSTNlInterworkinggGatewayhRingbacklTrust domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF˘åãgrankingëcrfcd5009irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmPrivate Header (P-Header) Extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for Authorization of Early MediadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document specifies the conventions for using the United States National Security Agency's Suite B algorithms in Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) as specified in RFC 3851.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejJ. Solinasddatej”Ìhfilenamegrfc5008eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126913hkeywordsÅcnsakobsoletedByÅd6318epagesopublicationDateFÎ&,grankingêcrfcd5008irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Suite B in Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document specifies a leasequery exchange for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) that can be used to obtain lease information about DHCPv6 clients from a DHCPv6 server.  This document specifies the scope of data that can be retrieved as well as both DHCPv6 leasequery requestor and server behavior.  This document extends DHCPv6. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednamemJ. Brzozowski¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegS. Zengddatej”Ïhfilenamegrfc5007eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126912hkeywordsÉcdhcddhcpdipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÎ&%grankingècrfcd5007irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqDHCPv6 LeasequerydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx◊This document specifies a new IPv6 Router Advertisement option to allow IPv6 routers to advertise DNS recursive server addresses to IPv6 hosts.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxETRI/University of MinnesotadnamehJ. Jeong¢kaffiliationsSAMSUNG ElectronicsdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnamejL. Beloeil¢kaffiliationrOrdyn TechnologiesdnamenS. Madanapalliddatej”Úhfilenamegrfc5006eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126911hkeywordsÅpdomain namespacekobsoletedByÅd6106epagesopublicationDateF¯;grankingécrfcd5006irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6IPv6 Router Advertisement Option for DNS ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This specification defines three types of syndicated Web feeds that enable publication of entries across one or more feed documents.  This includes "paged" feeds for piecemeal access, "archived" feeds that allow reconstruction of the feed's contents, and feeds that are explicitly "complete". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottinghamddatej”ıhfilenamegrfc5005eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126910hkeywordsÇdatomcrsskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF·Ôúgrankingçcrfcd5005irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFeed Paging and ArchivingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõIn this document, we propose an extension to the BGP route selection rules that would avoid unnecessary best path transitions between external paths under certain conditions.  The proposed extension would help the overall network stability, and more importantly, would eliminate certain BGP route oscillations in which more than one external path from one BGP speaker contributes to the churn. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Sangliddatej”ÿhfilenamegrfc5004eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126909hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF·Ôêgrankingåcrfcd5004irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Avoid BGP Best Path Transitions from One External to AnotherdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^The signaling protocols used to establish point-to-point pseudowires include type-length-value (TLV) fields that identify pseudowire endpoints called attachment individual identifiers (AIIs).  This document defines AII structures in the form of new AII TLV fields that support AII aggregation for improved scalability and Virtual Private Network (VPN) auto-discovery.  It is envisioned that this would be useful in large inter-domain virtual private wire service networks where pseudowires are established between selected local and remote provider edge (PE) nodes based on customer need. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamegC. Metz¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehF. Balus¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekJ. Sugimotoddatej”Èhfilenamegrfc5003eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126908hkeywordsÇctlvjpseudowirekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF‡#grankingãcrfcd5003irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Attachment Individual Identifier (AII) Types for AggregationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis document specifies the SIP P-Profile-Key P-header.  This header field is used in the 3rd-Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) IMS (IP Multimedia Subsystem) to provide SIP registrars and SIP proxy servers with the key of the profile corresponding to the destination SIP URI of a particular SIP request.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Blancoddatej”Ûhfilenamegrfc5002eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126907hkeywordsÉd3gppcimswip multimedia subsystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFœaÛgrankingäcrfcd5002irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) P-Profile-Key Private Header (P-Header)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8217∂habstractytWith the increased use of DNS anycast, load balancing, and other mechanisms allowing more than one DNS name server to share a single IP address, it is sometimes difficult to tell which of a pool of name servers has answered a particular query.  While existing ad-hoc mechanisms allow an operator to send follow-up queries when it is necessary to debug such a configuration, the only completely reliable way to obtain the identity of the name server that responded is to have the name server include this information in the response itself.  This note defines a protocol extension to support this functionality. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austeinddatej”Úhfilenamegrfc5001eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126906hkeywordsÇqdomain name spaceinamespacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF -6grankingâcrfcd5001irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(DNS Name Server Identifier (NSID) OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyˇThis document is published by the RFC Editor to provide a summary of the current standards protocols (as of 18 February 2008). It lists those official protocol standards, Best Current Practice, and Experimental RFCs that have not been obsoleted; it is not a complete index to the RFC series. Newly published RFCs and RFCs whose status has changed are starred.

For an up-to-date list, see http://www.rfc-editor.org/rfcxx00.html, which is updated daily. This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamejRFC EditorddatejÃXhfilenamegrfc5000eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43438hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7100epagesKopublicationDateH#ã grankingàcrfcd5000irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex$Internet Official Protocol StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytIn many scenarios, users must be able prove the existence and integrity of data, including digitally signed data, in a common and reproducible way over a long and possibly undetermined period of time.  This document specifies the syntax and processing of an Evidence Record, a structure designed to support long-term non-repudiation of existence of data. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuOpen Text CorporationdnamejT. Gondrom¢kaffiliationuInterComponentWare AGdnamekR. Brandner¢kaffiliationwFraunhofer GesellschaftdnamekU. Pordeschddatej”Òhfilenamegrfc4998eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeltansdfullx-ltans - Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdnamex%Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126905hkeywordsÖqlong-term archivehsecurityitimestampoevidence recordqarchive timestampkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateF∆2IgrankingÜcrfcd4998irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEvidence Record Syntax (ERS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document defines Robust Header Compression - Formal Notation (ROHC-FN), a formal notation to specify field encodings for compressed formats when defining new profiles within the ROHC framework.  ROHC-FN offers a library of encoding methods that are often used in ROHC profiles and can thereby help to simplify future profile development work. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamejR. Finking¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletierddatej”‘hfilenamegrfc4997eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126904hkeywordsÅx+Robust Header Compression - Formal NotationkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDateFÆäÜgrankingÖcrfcd4997irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Formal Notation for RObust Header Compression (ROHC-FN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document specifies a ROHC (Robust Header Compression) profile for compression of TCP/IP packets. The profile, called ROHC-TCP, provides efficient and robust compression of TCP headers, including frequently used TCP options such as SACK (Selective Acknowledgments) and Timestamps.

ROHC-TCP works well when used over links with significant error rates and long round-trip times. For many bandwidth-limited links where header compression is essential, such characteristics are common. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletier¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekK. Sandlund¢kaffiliation`dnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliationrSiemens/Roke ManordnamegM. WestddatejÃ<hfilenamegrfc4996eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126903hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd6846epages^opublicationDateFÆä|grankingÑcrfcd4996irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexARObust Header Compression (ROHC): A Profile for TCP/IP (ROHC-TCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThe Robust Header Compression (ROHC) protocol provides an efficient, flexible, and future-proof header compression concept. It is designed to operate efficiently and robustly over various link technologies with different characteristics.

The ROHC framework, along with a set of compression profiles, was initially defined in RFC 3095. To improve and simplify the ROHC specifications, this document explicitly defines the ROHC framework and the profile for uncompressed separately. More specifically, the definition of the framework does not modify or update the definition of the framework specified by RFC 3095. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletier¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekK. SandlundddatejÃ%hfilenamegrfc4995eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126902hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5795epages(opublicationDateFÆärgrankingÉcrfcd4995irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.The RObust Header Compression (ROHC) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a Relay Agent Echo Request option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6).  The option allows a DHCPv6 relay agent to request a list of relay agent options that the server echoes back to the relay agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegS. Zeng¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednamemJ. Brzozowskiddatej”Èhfilenamegrfc4994eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126901hkeywordsÇx#dynamic host configuration protocolrrelay agent optionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÚóägrankingÇcrfcd4994irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&DHCPv6 Relay Agent Echo Request OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a lightweight UDP transfer protocol for the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS).  This transfer protocol uses a single packet for every request and response, and optionally employs compression over the contents of the packet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newtonddatej”‰hfilenamegrfc4993eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126900hkeywordsÅdiriskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπ0grankingÅcrfcd4993irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQA Lightweight UDP Transfer Protocol for the Internet Registry Information ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx”This document describes a simple TCP transfer protocol for the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS).  Data is transferred between clients and servers using chunks to achieve pipelining. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newtonddatej”‰hfilenamegrfc4992eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126899hkeywordsÉctcpxtransport control protocoldiriskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπ$grankingÄcrfcd4992irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHXML Pipelining with Chunks for the Internet Registry Information ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty9This document describes an XML Schema for use by Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS) application transfer protocols that share common characteristics.  It describes common information about the transfer protocol, such as version, supported extensions, and supported security mechanisms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newtonddatej”‰hfilenamegrfc4991eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126898hkeywordsÇdiriscxmlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπgrankingcrfcd4991irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLA Common Schema for Internet Registry Information Service Transfer ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document clarifies the use of addresses in Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) networks. The aim is to facilitate interworking of GMPLS-capable Label Switching Routers (LSRs). The document is based on experience gained in implementation, interoperability testing, and deployment.

The document describes how to interpret address and identifier fields within GMPLS protocols, and how to choose which addresses to set in those fields for specific control plane usage models. It also discusses how to handle IPv6 sources and destinations in the MPLS and GMPLS Traffic Engineering (TE) Management Information Base (MIB) modules.

This document does not define new procedures or processes. Whenever this document makes requirements statements or recommendations, these are taken from normative text in the referenced RFCs. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Shiomoto¢kaffiliationgIsocorednamejR. Papneja¢kaffiliationfGooglednameiR. Rabbatddatej”Îhfilenamegrfc4990eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126897hkeywordsÇmaddress fieldpidentifier fieldkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF‡"Ôgranking~crfcd4990irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNUse of Addresses in Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThis document adapts the Mobile IPv6 Fast Handovers to improve delay and packet loss resulting from Mobile IPv4 handover operations.  Specifically, this document addresses movement detection, IP address configuration, and location update latencies during a handover.  For reducing the IP address configuration latency, the document proposes that the new Care-of Address is always made to be the new access router's IP address.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnameiR. Koodli¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”Úhfilenamegrfc4988eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126896hkeywordsâdmip4edelaykpacket lossrmovement detectionxip address configurationvloation update latencyocare-of addressocare of addressccoakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateG)<granking|crfcd4988irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMobile IPv4 Fast HandoversdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document describes TCP SYN flooding attacks, which have been well-known to the community for several years.  Various countermeasures against these attacks, and the trade-offs of each, are described.  This document archives explanations of the attack and common defense techniques for the benefit of TCP implementers and administrators of TCP servers or networks, but does not make any standards-level recommendations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamegW. Eddyddatej”hfilenamegrfc4987eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126895hkeywordsÑmTCP SYN FloodoTCP SYN Cookiesqdenial-of-servicecDoSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF∆2Cgranking{crfcd4987irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/TCP SYN Flooding Attacks and Common MitigationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇEvery DNS security-aware resolver must have at least one Trust Anchor to use as the basis for validating responses from DNS signed zones.  For various reasons, most DNS security-aware resolvers are expected to have several Trust Anchors.  For some operations, manual monitoring and updating of Trust Anchors may be feasible, but many operations will require automated methods for updating Trust Anchors in their security-aware resolvers.  This document identifies the requirements that must be met by an automated DNS Trust Anchor rollover solution for security-aware DNS resolvers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoAfilias LimiteddnamehH. Eland¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnamehR. Mundy¢kaffiliationmShinkuro Inc.dnamejS. Crocker¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameoS. Krishnaswamyddatej”hfilenamegrfc4986eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126894hkeywordsÅodns signed zonekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF’‡∞grankingzcrfcd4986irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCRequirements Related to DNS Security (DNSSEC) Trust Anchor RolloverdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new name form for inclusion in the otherName field of an X.509 Subject Alternative Name extension that allows a certificate subject to be associated with the service name and domain name components of a DNS Service Resource Record. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santessonddatej”hfilenamegrfc4985eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126893hkeywordsÅiothernamekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateF’‡∫grankingycrfcd4985irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Subject Alternative Name for Expression of Service NamedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyºThis document reports the outcome of the Routing and Addressing Workshop that was held by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) on October 18-19, 2006, in Amsterdam, Netherlands. The primary goal of the workshop was to develop a shared understanding of the problems that the large backbone operators are facing regarding the scalability of today's Internet routing system. The key workshop findings include an analysis of the major factors that are driving routing table growth, constraints in router technology, and the limitations of today's Internet addressing architecture. It is hoped that these findings will serve as input to the IETF community and help identify next steps towards effective solutions.

Note that this document is a report on the proceedings of the workshop. The views and positions documented in this report are those of the workshop participants and not of the IAB. Furthermore, note that work on issues related to this workshop report is continuing, and this document does not intend to reflect the increased understanding of issues nor to discuss the range of potential solutions that may be the outcome of this ongoing work. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Zhang¢kaffiliation`dnamegK. Fallddatej”Ìhfilenamegrfc4984eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126892hkeywordsÉxrouting and addressing workshoptrouting table growthwaddressing architecturekobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateF¸/”grankingxcrfcd4984irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex6Report from the IAB Workshop on Routing and AddressingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for information related to the management of Fibre Channel's Registered State Change Notifications (RSCNs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejH.K. Vivek¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationmNuova SystemsdnamefS. Gaiddatej”ﬁhfilenamegrfc4983eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126891hkeywordsÇxmanagement information baseoT11-FC-RSCN-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFœaÿgrankingwcrfcd4983irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Fibre Channel Registered State Change Notification (RSCN) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This document analyzes the implications of recent attacks on commonly used hash functions on Cryptographically Generated Addresses (CGAs) and updates the CGA specification to support multiple hash algorithms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. ArkkoddatejÃEhfilenamegrfc4982eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126890hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateF§˜Jgrankingvcrfcd4982irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTSupport for Multiple Hash Algorithms in Cryptographically Generated Addresses (CGAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThe pace of research on peer-to-peer (P2P) networking in the last five years warrants a critical survey. P2P has the makings of a disruptive technology -- it can aggregate enormous storage and processing resources while minimizing entry and scaling costs.

Failures are common amongst massive numbers of distributed peers, though the impact of individual failures may be less than in conventional architectures. Thus, the key to realizing P2P's potential in applications other than casual file sharing is robustness.

P2P search methods are first couched within an overall P2P taxonomy. P2P indexes for simple key lookup are assessed, including those based on Plaxton trees, rings, tori, butterflies, de Bruijn graphs, and skip graphs. Similarly, P2P indexes for keyword lookup, information retrieval and data management are explored. Finally, early efforts to optimize range, multi-attribute, join, and aggregation queries over P2P indexes are reviewed. Insofar as they are available in the primary literature, robustness mechanisms and metrics are highlighted throughout. However, the low-level mechanisms that most affect robustness are not well isolated in the literature. Recommendations are given for future research. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of New South WalesdnameiJ. Risson¢kaffiliationxUniversity of New South WalesdnamehT. Moorsddatej”Æhfilenamegrfc4981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126889hkeywordsêtPeer-to-peer networkvDistributed hash tablerStructured overlaytUnstructured overlayqKey-based routingrConsistent hashingx#Scalable distributed data structuremDependabilityiHypercubelPlaxton treeode Bruijn graphjSkip grapheTorusqButterfly networklVector modelxLatent semantic indexingkobsoletedByÄepages[opublicationDateF·ÔTgrankingucrfcd4981irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGSurvey of Research towards Robust Peer-to-Peer Networks: Search MethodsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document is an analysis of multihoming in the context of network mobility (NEMO) in IPv6.  As there are many situations in which mobile networks may be multihomed, a taxonomy is proposed to classify the possible configurations.  The possible deployment scenarios of multihomed mobile networks are described together with the associated issues when network mobility is supported by RFC 3963 (NEMO Basic Support).  Recommendations are offered on how to address these issues.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxPanasonic Singapore LabsdnameeC. Ng¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamehT. Ernst¢kaffiliationbKTdnamegE. Paik¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnuloddatej”Íhfilenamegrfc4980eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnemodfullwnemo - Network MobilitydnamepNetwork Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126888hkeywordsÉdnemodipv6omobile networkskobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateG
‘Õgrankingtcrfcd4980irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Analysis of Multihoming in Network Mobility SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document requests IANA registration of an Enumservice for XMPP, the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol.  This Enumservice specifically allows the use of 'xmpp' Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) in the context of E.164 Number Mapping (ENUM). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgenum.atdnamelA. Mayrhoferddatej”hfilenamegrfc4979eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126887hkeywordsÇx*extensible messaging and presence protocolee.164kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπ÷grankingscrfcd4979irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(IANA Registration for Enumservice 'XMPP'dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractxpThe COMPRESS extension allows an IMAP connection to be effectively and efficiently compressed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmbHdnamenA. Gulbrandsenddatej”Ôhfilenamegrfc4978eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126886hkeywordsÅx Internet Message Access ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateF’‡ïgrankingrcrfcd4978irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe IMAP COMPRESS ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document discusses the issues associated with mobility management for dual stack mobile nodes.  Currently, two mobility management protocols are defined for IPv4 and IPv6.  Deploying both in a dual stack mobile node introduces a number of problems.  Deployment and operational issues motivate the use of a single mobility management protocol.  This document discusses such motivations.  The document also discusses requirements for the Mobile IPv4 (MIPv4) and Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) protocol so that they can support mobility management for a dual stack node.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationtElevate TechnologiesdnamejH. Solimanddatej”Òhfilenamegrfc4977eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126885hkeywordsÑxmobility management protocolemipv4emipv6imobile ipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF’‡Ügrankingqcrfcd4977irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Problem Statement: Dual Stack MobilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycTwo separate models for conveying instant messages have been defined.  Page-mode messages stand alone and are not part of a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) session, whereas session-mode messages are set up as part of a session using SIP.  The Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP) is a protocol for near real-time, peer-to-peer exchanges of binary content without intermediaries, which is designed to be signaled using a separate rendezvous protocol such as SIP.  This document introduces the notion of message relay intermediaries to MSRP and describes the extensions necessary to use them. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationkPlantronicsdnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnamekA. B. Roachddatej”Áhfilenamegrfc4976eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126884hkeywordsÑpinstante messageipage-modelsession-moderrelay intermediarykobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateF˘åÄgrankingpcrfcd4976irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Relay Extensions for the Message Sessions Relay Protocol (MSRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8553d8996d7977∂habstractyHThis document describes the Message Session Relay Protocol, a protocol for transmitting a series of related instant messages in the context of a session.  Message sessions are treated like any other media stream when set up via a rendezvous or session creation protocol such as the Session Initiation Protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationkPlantronicsdnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekC. Jenningsddatej”Áhfilenamegrfc4975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126883hkeywordsÅoinstant messagekobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDateF˘åvgrankingocrfcd4975irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The Message Session Relay Protocol (MSRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÑd8591d8873d8996d7977∂habstractyqIn certain networking topologies, it may be advantageous to maintain associations between endpoints and key transit points to support an instance of a service. Such associations are known as Calls.

A Call does not provide the actual connectivity for transmitting user traffic, but only builds a relationship by which subsequent Connections may be made. In Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) such Connections are known as Label Switched Paths (LSPs).

This document specifies how GMPLS Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling may be used and extended to support Calls. These mechanisms provide full and logical Call/Connection separation.

The mechanisms proposed in this document are applicable to any environment (including multi-area), and for any type of interface: packet, layer-2, time-division multiplexed, lambda, or fiber switching. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. FarrelddatejÃFhfilenamegrfc4974eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126882hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπÃgrankingncrfcd4974irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIGeneralized MPLS (GMPLS) RSVP-TE Signaling Extensions in Support of CallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6001µhabstractyThis document defines OSPF-xTE, an experimental traffic engineering (TE) extension to the link-state routing protocol OSPF.  OSPF-xTE defines new TE Link State Advertisements (LSAs) to disseminate TE metrics within an autonomous System (AS), which may consist of multiple areas.  When an AS consists of TE and non-TE nodes, OSPF-xTE ensures that non-TE nodes in the AS are unaffected by the TE LSAs.  OSPF-xTE generates a stand-alone TE Link State Database (TE-LSDB), distinct from the native OSPF LSDB, for computation of TE circuit paths.  OSPF-xTE is versatile and extendible to non-packet networks such as Synchronous Optical Network (SONET) / Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) and optical networks.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBill FennergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnKazeon SystemsdnamelP. Srisuresh¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiP. Josephddatej—Zhfilenamegrfc4973eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126881hkeywordsÉgospf-texlink state advertisementclsakobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateFØ›grankingmcrfcd4973irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex@OSPF-xTE: Experimental Extension to OSPF for Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
The setup of a full mesh of Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSP) among a set of Label Switch Routers (LSR) is a common deployment scenario of MPLS Traffic Engineering either for bandwidth optimization, bandwidth guarantees or fast rerouting with MPLS Fast Reroute.  Such deployment may require the configuration of a potentially large number of TE LSPs (on the order of the square of the number of LSRs).  This document specifies Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) routing extensions for Intermediate System-to-Intermediate System (IS-IS) and Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) so as to provide an automatic discovery of the set of LSRs members of a mesh in order to automate the creation of such mesh of TE LSPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamejJL. Leroux¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Yasukawa¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiP. Mabbeyddatej”·hfilenamegrfc4972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126880hkeywordsÜgmpls-teclspslabel switched pathcigpeis-isdospfkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFØ›grankinglcrfcd4972irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Routing Extensions for Discovery of Multiprotocol (MPLS) Label Switch Router (LSR) Traffic Engineering (TE) Mesh MembershipdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a new optional Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) TLV named CAPABILITY, formed of multiple sub-TLVs, which allows a router to announce its capabilities within an IS-IS level or the entire routing domain. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwalddatej”Áhfilenamegrfc4971eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126879hkeywordsÅicapabiltykobsoletedByÅd7981epages	opublicationDateFØ›grankingkcrfcd4971irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Extensions for Advertising Router InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIt is useful for routers in an OSPFv2 or OSPFv3 routing domain to know the capabilities of their neighbors and other routers in the routing domain.  This document proposes extensions to OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 for advertising optional router capabilities.  A new Router Information (RI) Link State Advertisement (LSA) is proposed for this purpose.  In OSPFv2, the RI LSA will be implemented with a new opaque LSA type ID.  In OSPFv3, the RI LSA will be implemented with a new LSA type function code.  In both protocols, the RI LSA can be advertised at any of the defined flooding scopes (link, area, or autonomous system (AS)). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationsBridgePort NetworksdnamejS. Shafferddatej”‚hfilenamegrfc4970eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126878hkeywordsáfospfv2fospfv3xopen shortest path firstbrirrouter informationclsaxlink state advertisementkobsoletedByÅd7770epagesopublicationDateFØ‹¸grankingjcrfcd4970irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Extensions to OSPF for Advertising Optional Router CapabilitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis memo registers the Enumservice "vCard" using the URI schemes "http" and "https". This Enumservice is to be used to refer from an ENUM domain name to a vCard instance describing the user of the respective E.164 number.

Information gathered from those vCards could be used before, during, or after inbound or outbound communication takes place. For example, a callee might be presented with the name and association of the caller before picking up the call. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgenum.atdnamelA. Mayrhoferddatej”Ìhfilenamegrfc4969eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126877hkeywordsÇdenumee.164kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπ␀grankingicrfcd4969irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IANA Registration for vCard EnumservicedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstracty¥This document provides different IPv6 link models that are suitable for IEEE 802.16 based networks and provides analysis of various considerations for each link model and the applicability of each link model under different deployment scenarios.  This document is the result of a design team (DT) that was formed to analyze the IPv6 link models for IEEE 802.16 based networks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOrdyn TechnologiesdnamenS. Madanapalliddatej”Óhfilenamegrfc4968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymd16ngdfullx#16ng - IP over IEEE 802.16 NetworksdnamexIP over IEEE 802.16 Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126876hkeywordsÅewimaxkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF≥Îîgrankinghcrfcd4968irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Analysis of IPv6 Link Models for 802.16 Based NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂RFC 3966 explicitly states that 'tel' URIs may not represent a dial string.  That leaves no way specify a dial string in a standardized way.  Great confusion exists with the SIP URI parameter "user=phone", and specifically, if it can represent a dial string.  This memo creates a new value for the user parameter "dialstring", so that one may specify "user=dialstring" to encode a dial string as a 'sip:' or 'sips:' URI. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehB. Rosenddatej”Íhfilenamegrfc4967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126875hkeywordsÅjdialstringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF™Vgrankinggcrfcd4967irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUDial String Parameter for the Session Initiation Protocol Uniform Resource IdentifierdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document discusses issues with the specific form of IPv6-IPv4 protocol translation mechanism implemented by the Network Address Translator - Protocol Translator (NAT-PT) defined in RFC 2766.  These issues are sufficiently serious that recommending RFC 2766 as a general purpose transition mechanism is no longer desirable, and this document recommends that the IETF should reclassify RFC 2766 from Proposed Standard to Historic status.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnEnergize UrnetdnamegC. Aoun¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Daviesddatej”‡hfilenamegrfc4966eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126874hkeywordsÇfNAT-PTev6opskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFßs˜grankingfcrfcd4966irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Reasons to Move the Network Address Translator - Protocol Translator (NAT-PT) to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÍThis document describes the collaboration and liaison relationship between the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) and the Cable Television Laboratories, Inc. (CableLabs).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiJ-F. Mule¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekW. Townsleyddatej”Óhfilenamegrfc4965eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126873hkeywordsÜxIETF CableLabs CollaborationgliaisonxCable Television LaboratoriesfDOCSISkPacketCableiOpenCablekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateFÙC€grankingecrfcd4965irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.CableLabs - IETF Standardization CollaborationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©This document describes a private Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) header (P-header) used by the Open Mobile Alliance (OMA) for Push to talk over Cellular (PoC) along with its applicability, which is limited to the OMA PoC application.  The P-Answer-State header is used for indicating the answering mode of the handset, which is particular to the PoC application.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxResearch in Motion (RIM)dnamehA. Allen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegJ. Holm¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameiT. Hallinddatej”Ïhfilenamegrfc4964eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126872hkeywordsÑhp-headercomatopen mobile alliancecpockobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateF‡"Âgrankingdcrfcd4964irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~The P-Answer-State Header Extension to the Session Initiation Protocol for the Open Mobile Alliance Push to Talk over CellulardtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyIIPv4 fragmentation is not sufficiently robust for use under some conditions in today's Internet.  At high data rates, the 16-bit IP identification field is not large enough to prevent frequent incorrectly assembled IP fragments, and the TCP and UDP checksums are insufficient to prevent the resulting corrupted datagrams from being delivered to higher protocol layers.  This note describes some easily reproduced experiments demonstrating the problem, and discusses some of the operational implications of these observations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcPSCdnamejJ. Heffner¢kaffiliationcPSCdnameiM. Mathis¢kaffiliationcPSCdnamekB. ChandlerddatejÃ?hfilenamegrfc4963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126871hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateF™d™grankingccrfcd4963irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)IPv4 Reassembly Errors at High Data RatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty«This document provides guidance to designers of Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) key management protocols.  The guidance is also useful to designers of systems and solutions that include AAA key management protocols.  Given the complexity and difficulty in designing secure, long-lasting key management algorithms and protocols by experts in the field, it is almost certainly inappropriate for IETF working groups without deep expertise in the area to be designing their own key management algorithms and protocols based on Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) protocols.  The guidelines in this document apply to documents requesting publication as IETF RFCs.  Further, these guidelines will be useful to other standards development organizations (SDOs) that specify AAA key management.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. AbobaddatejÃLhfilenamegrfc4962eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126870hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF§˜˙grankingbcrfcd4962irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÑetotaletitlexOGuidance for Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) Key ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyYThis document recommends using one UDP port pair for both communication directions of bidirectional RTP and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) sessions, commonly called "symmetric RTP" and "symmetric RTCP".  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wingddatej”Íhfilenamegrfc4961eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126869hkeywordsÇxreal time transport protocolnsymmetric rtcpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFØ‹‡grankingacrfcd4961irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÉetotaletitlex+Symmetric RTP / RTP Control Protocol (RTCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThis document obsoletes RFC 2960 and RFC 3309. It describes the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP). SCTP is designed to transport Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) signaling messages over IP networks, but is capable of broader applications.

SCTP is a reliable transport protocol operating on top of a connectionless packet network such as IP. It offers the following services to its users:

-- acknowledged error-free non-duplicated transfer of user data,

-- data fragmentation to conform to discovered path MTU size,

-- sequenced delivery of user messages within multiple streams, with an option for order-of-arrival delivery of individual user messages,

-- optional bundling of multiple user messages into a single SCTP packet, and

-- network-level fault tolerance through supporting of multi-homing at either or both ends of an association.

The design of SCTP includes appropriate congestion avoidance behavior and resistance to flooding and masquerade attacks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Stewartddatej”Úhfilenamegrfc4960eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126868hkeywordsÜdSCTPbIPhinternetitransportfpacketgnetworkkobsoletedByÅd9260epagesòopublicationDateFÙDgranking`crfcd4960irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Stream Control Transmission ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÑd6096d7053d6335d8899∂habstracty@To date, the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) has used a Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) profile which always required at least one complete round trip for an authentication, as it did not support an initial client response argument. This additional round trip at the beginning of the session is undesirable, especially when round-trip costs are high.

This document defines an extension to IMAP which allows clients and servers to avoid this round trip by allowing an initial client response argument to the IMAP AUTHENTICATE command. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelChris Newmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamemR. Siemborski¢kaffiliationvOryx Mail Systems GmbHdnamenA. Gulbrandsenddatej”Ïhfilenamegrfc4959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126867hkeywordsÇqimap authenticatex internet message access protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÙCœgranking_crfcd4959irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZIMAP Extension for Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) Initial Client ResponsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty®This document presents a framework discussing the role of various protocols and mechanisms that could be considered candidates for supporting Emergency Telecommunication Services (ETS) within a single administrative domain.  Comments about their potential usage as well as their current deployment are provided to the reader.  Specific solutions are not presented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlbergddatej”Íhfilenamegrfc4958eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfieprepdfullx(ieprep - Internet Emergency PreparednessdnamexInternet Emergency Preparednessdtypebwgbidjdoc-126866hkeywordsÉhprioritynprioritizationtpreferential servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF§˜ºgranking^crfcd4958irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlA Framework for Supporting Emergency Telecommunications Services (ETS) within a Single Administrative DomaindtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywCertain network access technologies are capable of providing various types of link-layer status information to IP.  Link-layer event notifications can help IP expeditiously detect configuration changes.  This document provides a non-exhaustive catalogue of information available from well-known access technologies.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationqGET ENST BretagnednamelN. Montavont¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamejE. Njedjou¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamemS. Veerepalli¢kaffiliationgSamsungdnamehA. YeginddatejÃDhfilenamegrfc4957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdnadfullx"dna - Detecting Network AttachmentdnamexDetecting Network Attachmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-126865hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFπ
granking]crfcd4957irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Link-Layer Event Notifications for Detecting Network AttachmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõIn the DNS security (DNSSEC) extensions, delegations to unsigned subzones are cryptographically secured.  Maintaining this cryptography is not always practical or necessary.  This document describes an experimental "Opt-In" model that allows administrators to omit this cryptography and manage the cost of adopting DNSSEC with large zones.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgNominetdnameiR. Arends¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamejM. Kosters¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiD. Blackaddatej”Ëhfilenamegrfc4956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126864hkeywordsÅpdomain namespacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF™Vgranking\crfcd4956irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNS Security (DNSSEC) Opt-IndtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬂThis document describes a methodology for deploying alternate, non-backwards-compatible, DNS Security (DNSSEC) methodologies in an experimental fashion without disrupting the deployment of standard DNSSEC. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiD. Blackaddatej”Ëhfilenamegrfc4955eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126863hkeywordsÅpdomain namespacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF™U˙granking[crfcd4955irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!DNS Security (DNSSEC) ExperimentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document defines a Simple Mail Transport Protocol (SMTP) extension whereby an SMTP client may indicate an authentication mechanism to the server, perform an authentication protocol exchange, and optionally negotiate a security layer for subsequent protocol interactions during this session. This extension includes a profile of the Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) for SMTP.

This document obsoletes RFC 2554. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlGoogle, Inc.dnamemR. Siemborski¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikovddatej”Ëhfilenamegrfc4954eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126862hkeywordsÉxsimple mail transport protocolnsecurity layerdsaslkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF§˜ßgrankingZcrfcd4954irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)SMTP Service Extension for AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5248∂habstractyJRecent analysis of potential attacks on core Internet infrastructure indicates an increased vulnerability of TCP connections to spurious resets (RSTs), sent with forged IP source addresses (spoofing).  TCP has always been susceptible to such RST spoofing attacks, which were indirectly protected by checking that the RST sequence number was inside the current receive window, as well as via the obfuscation of TCP endpoint and port numbers.  For pairs of well-known endpoints often over predictable port pairs, such as BGP or between web servers and well-known large-scale caches, increases in the path bandwidth-delay product of a connection have sufficiently increased the receive window space that off-path third parties can brute-force generate a viable RST sequence number.  The susceptibility to attack increases with the square of the bandwidth, and thus presents a significant vulnerability for recent high-speed networks.  This document addresses this vulnerability, discussing proposed solutions at the transport level and their inherent challenges, as well as existing network level solutions and the feasibility of their deployment.  This document focuses on vulnerabilities due to spoofed TCP segments, and includes a discussion of related ICMP spoofing attacks on TCP connections.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touchddatej”Ìhfilenamegrfc4953eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126861hkeywordsÇcrstxtransport control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÆäcgrankingYcrfcd4953irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Defending TCP Against Spoofing AttacksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyFull use of electronic mail throughout the world requires that people be able to use their own names, written correctly in their own languages and scripts, as mailbox names in email addresses.  This document introduces a series of specifications that define mechanisms and protocol extensions needed to fully support internationalized email addresses.  These changes include an SMTP extension and extension of email header syntax to accommodate UTF-8 data.  The document set also includes discussion of key assumptions and issues in deploying fully internationalized email.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationcICUdnameeY. Koddatej”ﬂhfilenamegrfc4952eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymceaidfullx(eai - Email Address Internationalizationdnamex"Email Address Internationalizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126860hkeywordsÅdsmtpkobsoletedByÅd6530epagesopublicationDateFßtgrankingXcrfcd4952irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Overview and Framework for Internationalized EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5336∂habstractyuLayer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) is a connection-oriented protocol that has a shared state between active endpoints.  Some of this shared state is vital for operation, but may be volatile in nature, such as packet sequence numbers used on the L2TP Control Connection.  When failure of one side of a control connection occurs, a new control connection is created and associated with the old connection by exchanging information about the old connection.  Such a mechanism is not intended as a replacement for an active fail over with some mirrored connection states, but as an aid for those parameters that are particularly difficult to have immediately available.  Protocol extensions to L2TP defined in this document are intended to facilitate state recovery, providing additional resiliency in an L2TP network, and improving a remote system's layer 2 connectivity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsRiverstone NetworksdnamegV. Jainddatej”Âhfilenamegrfc4951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126859hkeywordsÇrcontrol connectionulayer 2  connectivitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF -CgrankingWcrfcd4951irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEFail Over Extensions for Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) "failover"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıThis memo defines an extension object that can be appended to selected multi-part ICMP messages.  This extension permits Label Switching Routers to append MPLS information to ICMP messages, and has already been widely deployed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamefD. Gan¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiD. Tappan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataroddatej”Èhfilenamegrfc4950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126858hkeywordsÅx!Internet Control Message ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF≤Å€grankingVcrfcd4950irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1ICMP Extensions for Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis Glossary provides definitions, abbreviations, and explanations of terminology for information system security.  The 334 pages of entries offer recommendations to improve the comprehensibility of written material that is generated in the Internet Standards Process (RFC 2026).  The recommendations follow the principles that such writing should (a) use the same term or definition whenever the same concept is mentioned; (b) use terms in their plainest, dictionary sense; (c) use terms that are already well-established in open publications; and (d) avoid terms that either favor a particular vendor or favor a particular technology or mechanism over other, competing techniques that already exist or could be developed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Shireyddatej”®hfilenamegrfc4949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126857hkeywordsâlabbreviationgclarityjdefinitionjdictionaryhlanguagekpunctuationgsynonymkterminologygwritingkobsoletedByÄepagesmopublicationDateF -+grankingUcrfcd4949irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseisubseries£gacronymcfyifnumber$etotaletitlex%Internet Security Glossary, Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyúThis document reports the outcome of a workshop held by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) on Unwanted Internet Traffic.  The workshop was held on March 9-10, 2006 at USC/ISI in Marina del Rey, CA, USA.  The primary goal of the workshop was to foster interchange between the operator, standards, and research communities on the topic of unwanted traffic, as manifested in, for example, Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks, spam, and phishing, to gain understandings on the ultimate sources of these unwanted traffic, and to assess their impact and the effectiveness of existing solutions.  It was also a goal of the workshop to identify engineering and research topics that could be undertaken by the IAB, the IETF, the IRTF, and the network research and development community at large to develop effective countermeasures against the unwanted traffic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehL. Zhangddatej”Èhfilenamegrfc4948eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126856hkeywordsÇdspamfbotnetkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateF√áÈgrankingTcrfcd4948irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexAReport from the IAB workshop on Unwanted Traffic March 9-10, 2006dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document describes the process of binding/associating IPv4/IPv6 addresses with MPEG-2 Transport Streams (TS). This procedure is known as Address Resolution (AR) or Neighbor Discovery (ND). Such address resolution complements the higher-layer resource discovery tools that are used to advertise IP sessions.

In MPEG-2 Networks, an IP address must be associated with a Packet ID (PID) value and a specific Transmission Multiplex. This document reviews current methods appropriate to a range of technologies (such as DVB (Digital Video Broadcasting), ATSC (Advanced Television Systems Committee), DOCSIS (Data-Over-Cable Service Interface Specifications), and variants). It also describes the interaction with well-known protocols for address management including DHCP, ARP, and the ND protocol. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationx!Motorola Connected Home SolutionsdnamelM. Montpetitddatej”Êhfilenamegrfc4947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipdvbdfullsipdvb - IP over DVBdnamekIP over DVBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126855hkeywordsÖkencapsulatexmotion picture experts groupxunidirectional link protocolxUniDirectional Link Routingxaddress resolution protocolkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateFû¨¥grankingScrfcd4947irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCAddress Resolution Mechanisms for IP Datagrams over MPEG-2 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≈This memo defines an extension to the Atom Syndication Format for describing licenses associated with Atom feeds and entries.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Snellddatej”Âhfilenamegrfc4946eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126854hkeywordsÉwatom syndication formatjatom feedslatom entrieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF§˜ŸgrankingRcrfcd4946irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevAtom License ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThe Internet Key Exchange (IKE) and Public Key Infrastructure for X.509 (PKIX) certificate profile both provide frameworks that must be profiled for use in a given application.  This document provides a profile of IKE and PKIX that defines the requirements for using PKI technology in the context of IKE/IPsec.  The document complements protocol specifications such as IKEv1 and IKEv2, which assume the existence of public key certificates and related keying materials, but which do not address PKI issues explicitly.  This document addresses those issues.  The intended audience is implementers of PKI for IPsec. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwNetwork Resonance, Inc.dnameiB. Korverddatej”Êhfilenamegrfc4945eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymipki4ipsecdfullx)pki4ipsec - Profiling Use of PKI in IPSECdnamexProfiling Use of PKI in IPSECdtypebwgbidjdoc-126853hkeywordsÉuinternet key exchangex#public key infrastructure for x.509eipseckobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateFΩägrankingQcrfcd4945irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEThe Internet IP Security PKI Profile of IKEv1/ISAKMP, IKEv2, and PKIXdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis document describes the frame format for transmission of IPv6 packets and the method of forming IPv6 link-local addresses and statelessly autoconfigured addresses on IEEE 802.15.4 networks.  Additional specifications include a simple header compression scheme using shared context and provisions for packet delivery in IEEE 802.15.4 meshes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationjIntel CorpdnamenN. Kushalnagar¢kaffiliationnArch Rock CorpdnamefJ. Hui¢kaffiliationnArch Rock CorpdnameiD. Cullerddatej”Ôhfilenamegrfc4944eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymg6lowpandfullx"6lowpan - IPv6 over Low power WPANdnamexIPv6 over Low power WPANdtypebwgbidjdoc-126852hkeywordsÉxinternet protocol version 6rlink-local addressxstateless autoconfigurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF˘åôgrankingPcrfcd4944irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Transmission of IPv6 Packets over IEEE 802.15.4 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÖd8066d6775d6282d8931d8025∂habstracty«This document describes the historical and background information behind the removal of the "on-link assumption" from the conceptual host sending algorithm defined in Neighbor Discovery for IP Version 6 (IPv6).  According to the algorithm as originally described, when a host's default router list is empty, the host assumes that all destinations are on-link.  This is particularly problematic with IPv6-capable nodes that do not have off-link IPv6 connectivity (e.g., no default router).  This document describes how making this assumption causes problems and how these problems outweigh the benefits of this part of the conceptual sending algorithm.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamefS. Roy¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiA. Durand¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehJ. Paughddatej”öhfilenamegrfc4943eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126851hkeywordsÅxinternet protocol version 6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF¯;6grankingOcrfcd4943irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=IPv6 Neighbor Discovery On-Link Assumption Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe transition from a pure IPv4 network to a network where IPv4 and IPv6 coexist brings a number of extra security considerations that need to be taken into account when deploying IPv6 and operating the dual-protocol network and the associated transition mechanisms. This document attempts to give an overview of the various issues grouped into three categories:

o issues due to the IPv6 protocol itself, o issues due to transition mechanisms, and o issues due to IPv6 deployment.

This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekS. Krishnan¢kaffiliationiCSC/FunetdnameiP. Savoladdatej”‘hfilenamegrfc4942eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126850hkeywordsÉxinternet protocol version 6udual-protocol networkdipv4kobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateF¯;-grankingNcrfcd4942irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4IPv6 Transition/Co-existence Security ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqNodes use IPv6 stateless address autoconfiguration to generate addresses using a combination of locally available information and information advertised by routers.  Addresses are formed by combining network prefixes with an interface identifier.  On an interface that contains an embedded IEEE Identifier, the interface identifier is typically derived from it.  On other interface types, the interface identifier is generated through other means, for example, via random number generation.  This document describes an extension to IPv6 stateless address autoconfiguration for interfaces whose interface identifier is derived from an IEEE identifier.  Use of the extension causes nodes to generate global scope addresses from interface identifiers that change over time, even in cases where the interface contains an embedded IEEE identifier.  Changing the interface identifier (and the global scope addresses generated from it) over time makes it more difficult for eavesdroppers and other information collectors to identify when different addresses used in different transactions actually correspond to the same node. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnameiR. Draves¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamekS. Krishnanddatej”Àhfilenamegrfc4941eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126849hkeywordsÑgprivacyianonymitymunlinkabilityxcrypto-based address changingkobsoletedByÅd8981epagesopublicationDateF¯;%grankingMcrfcd4941irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBPrivacy Extensions for Stateless Address Autoconfiguration in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis memo creates a number of OSPF registries and provides guidance to IANA for assignment of code points within these registries.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationsAT&T Labs--ResearchdnameiB. Fennerddatej”‰hfilenamegrfc4940eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126848hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFû¨ggrankingLcrfcd4940irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÇetotaletitlexIANA Considerations for OSPFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThe iSNS (Internet Storage Name Service) protocol provides storage name service functionality on an IP network that is being used for iSCSI (Internet Small Computer System Interface) or iFCP (Internet Fibre Channel Protocol) storage.  This document provides a mechanism to monitor multiple iSNS Servers, including information about registered objects in an iSNS Server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationk2Wire, Inc.dnamejK. Gibbons¢kaffiliationnSnapTell, Inc.dnamekG. Ramkumar¢kaffiliationmBrocade, Inc.dnamegS. Kippddatej”Îhfilenamegrfc4939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126847hkeywordsácmibxmanagement information baseeiscsix(internet small computer system interfacedifcpxinternet fibre channel protocolhISNS-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesPopublicationDateF§¯grankingKcrfcd4939irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGDefinitions of Managed Objects for iSNS (Internet Storage Name Service)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyeThis document extends the Point-to-Point over Ethernet (PPPoE) Protocol with a credit-based flow control mechanism and Link Quality Metric report.  This optional extension should improve the performance of PPPoE over media with variable bandwidth and limited buffering, such as mobile radio links.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehB. Berry¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejH. HolgateddatejÀÚhfilenamegrfc4938eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126846hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5578epagesopublicationDateFÜÕ/grankingJcrfcd4938irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexEPPP Over Ethernet (PPPoE) Extensions for Credit Flow and Link MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxóThis document describes the IANA considerations for the PPP over Ethernet (PPPoE) protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiP. Arberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelV. MammolitiddatejÃ?hfilenamegrfc4937eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126845hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÜÕ$grankingIcrfcd4937irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1IANA Considerations for PPP over Ethernet (PPPoE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for information related to a Fibre Channel Zone Server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejH.K. Vivek¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationmNuova SystemsdnamefS. Gaiddatej”ﬁhfilenamegrfc4936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126844hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basevT11-FC-FABRIC-LOCK-MIBvT11-FC-ZONE-SERVER-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDateF≥Î¨grankingHcrfcd4936irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFibre Channel Zone Server MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for information related to the Fabric Configuration Server function of a Fibre Channel network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejH.K. Vivek¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationmNuova SystemsdnamefS. Gaiddatej”ﬁhfilenamegrfc4935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126843hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basexT11-FC-FABRIC-CONFIG-SERVER-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateF≥Î°grankingGcrfcd4935irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Fibre Channel Fabric Configuration Server MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This document describes how an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) session is mapped onto a single Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) connection.  This mapping requires use of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to protect information exchanged between an EPP client and an EPP server.  This document obsoletes RFC 3734. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”÷hfilenamegrfc4934eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126842hkeywordsÖgmappingfclientfserverctlsxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÅd5734epages
opublicationDateF^œgrankingFcrfcd4934irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Transport Over TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of individual or organizational social information identifiers (known as "contacts") stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in Extensible Markup Language (XML), the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to contacts.  This document obsoletes RFC 3733. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”◊hfilenamegrfc4933eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126841hkeywordsÇfsyntaxisemanticskobsoletedByÅd5733epages+opublicationDateF^ÿgrankingEcrfcd4933irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Contact MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of Internet host names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to host names.  This document obsoletes RFC 3732. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”Àhfilenamegrfc4932eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126840hkeywordsÇfsyntaxisemanticskobsoletedByÅd5732epagesopublicationDateF^„grankingDcrfcd4932irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Host MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of Internet domain names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to domain names.  This document obsoletes RFC 3731. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”◊hfilenamegrfc4931eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126839hkeywordsÇfsyntaxisemanticskobsoletedByÅd5731epages.opublicationDateF^ÏgrankingCcrfcd4931irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Domain Name MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document describes an application layer client-server protocol for the provisioning and management of objects stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the protocol defines generic object management operations and an extensible framework that maps protocol operations to objects.  This document includes a protocol specification, an object mapping template, and an XML media type registration.  This document obsoletes RFC 3730. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”÷hfilenamegrfc4930eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126838hkeywordsÅxshared framework mappingkobsoletedByÅd5730epagesHopublicationDateF^ˆgrankingBcrfcd4930irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty†This document provides guidelines for applying or extending the MPLS or GMPLS ((G)MPLS) protocol suites and clarifies the IETF's (G)MPLS working groups' responsibility for the (G)MPLS protocols.  This document is directed to multi-vendor fora and Standards Development Organizations (SDOs) to provide an understanding of (G)MPLS work in the IETF and documents the requisite use of IETF review procedures when considering (G)MPLS applications or protocol extensions in their work.  This document does not modify IETF processes.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. FarrelddatejÃ@hfilenamegrfc4929eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126837hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÅõ@grankingAcrfcd4929irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÅetotaletitlexmChange Process for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Protocols and ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document describes the Equal Cost Multipath (ECMP) behavior of currently deployed MPLS networks.  This document makes best practice recommendations for anyone defining an application to run over an MPLS network that wishes to avoid the reordering that can result from transmission of different packets from the same flow over multiple different equal cost paths.  These recommendations rely on inspection of the IP version number field in packets.  Despite the heuristic nature of the recommendations, they provide a relatively safe way to operate MPLS networks, even if future allocations of IP version numbers were made for some purpose.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Anderssonddatej”‡hfilenamegrfc4928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126836hkeywordsÅdECMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÉ␀granking@crfcd4928irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberÄetotaletitlex8Avoiding Equal Cost Multipath Treatment in MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9790d7274∂habstractyØFor scalability purposes, a network may comprise multiple Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) areas.  An inter-area Traffic Engineered Label Switched Path (TE-LSP) is an LSP that transits through at least two IGP areas.  In a multi-area network, topology visibility remains local to a given area, and a head-end Label Switching Router (LSR) cannot compute an inter-area shortest constrained path.  One key application of the Path Computation Element (PCE)-based architecture is the computation of inter-area TE-LSP paths.  The PCE Communication Protocol (PCECP) is used to communicate computation requests from Path Computation Clients (PCCs) to PCEs, and to return computed paths in responses.  This document lists a detailed set of PCECP-specific requirements for support of inter-area TE-LSP path computation.  It complements the generic requirements for a PCE Communication Protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamemJ.-L. Le Rouxddatej”‚hfilenamegrfc4927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126835hkeywordsÖegmplsfte-lspx&traffic engineered label switched pathcpcexpath computation elementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÅõHgranking?crfcd4927irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPath Computation Element Communication Protocol (PCECP) Specific Requirements for Inter-Area MPLS and GMPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document describes a proposed URN (Uniform Resource Name) namespace that would be managed by DANTE, representing European Research and academic networks, for naming persistent resources defined by GEANT, the Consortium of European Academic and Research Networks, its projects, activities, working groups, and other designated subordinates.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeDANTEdnamehT. Kalin¢kaffiliationeDANTEdnameiM. Molinaddatej”‚hfilenamegrfc4926eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126834hkeywordsÇuuniform resource nameedantekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateFî$+granking>crfcd4926irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA URN Namespace for GEANTdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document captures the problem statement for the Softwires Working Group, which is developing standards for the discovery, control, and encapsulation methods for connecting IPv4 networks across IPv6-only networks as well as IPv6 networks across IPv4-only networks.  The standards will encourage multiple, inter-operable vendor implementations by identifying, and extending where necessary, existing standard protocols to resolve a selected set of "IPv4/IPv6" and "IPv6/IPv4" transition problems.  This document describes the specific problems ("Hubs and Spokes" and "Mesh") that will be solved by the standards developed by the Softwires Working Group.  Some requirements (and non-requirements) are also identified to better describe the specific problem scope.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationfCERNETdnameeX. Li¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamejS. Dawkins¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Ward¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiA. DurandddatejÃBhfilenamegrfc4925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsoftwiredfulltsoftwire - SoftwiresdnameiSoftwiresdtypebwgbidjdoc-126833hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF™U›granking=crfcd4925irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSoftwire Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÔThis document provides a review of previous IAB statements on Internet transparency, as well a discussion of new transparency issues.  Far from having lessened in relevance, technical implications of intentionally or inadvertently impeding network transparency play a critical role in the Internet's ability to support innovation and global communication.  This document provides some specific illustrations of those potential impacts.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnameiE. DaviesddatejÃEhfilenamegrfc4924eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126832hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFßsÈgranking<crfcd4924irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex$Reflections on Internet TransparencydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZSome networks require communication between an interior and exterior portion of a Virtual Private Network (VPN) or through a concatenation of such networks resulting in a nested VPN, but have sensitivities about what information is communicated across the boundary, especially while providing quality of service to communications with different precedence.  This note seeks to outline the issues and the nature of the proposed solutions based on the framework for Integrated Services operation over Diffserv networks as described in RFC 2998.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationoLockheed MartindnamegP. Boseddatej”Êhfilenamegrfc4923eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126831hkeywordsÉcvpnjnested vpnsintegrated serviceskobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateF√áÛgranking;crfcd4923irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFQuality of Service (QoS) Signaling in a Nested Virtual Private NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒIn a distributed, constraint-based routing environment, the information used to compute a path may be out of date. This means that Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineered (TE) Label Switched Path (LSP) setup requests may be blocked by links or nodes without sufficient resources. Crankback is a scheme whereby setup failure information is returned from the point of failure to allow new setup attempts to be made avoiding the blocked resources. Crankback can also be applied to LSP recovery to indicate the location of the failed link or node.

This document specifies crankback signaling extensions for use in MPLS signaling using RSVP-TE as defined in "RSVP-TE: Extensions to RSVP for LSP Tunnels", RFC 3209, and GMPLS signaling as defined in "Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Functional Description", RFC 3473. These extensions mean that the LSP setup request can be retried on an alternate path that detours around blocked links or nodes. This offers significant improvements in the successful setup and recovery ratios for LSPs, especially in situations where a large number of setup requests are triggered at the same time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamepA. Satyanarayana¢kaffiliationoNEC CorporationdnamehA. Iwata¢kaffiliationoNEC CorporationdnameiN. Fujita¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefG. Ashddatej”„hfilenamegrfc4920eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126830hkeywordsÜxmultiprotocol label switchingx)generalized multiprotocol label switchingrtraffic engineeredbteclspslabel switched pathkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateFô1ggranking8crfcd4920irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Crankback Signaling Extensions for MPLS and GMPLS RSVP-TEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˙This document describes the assumptions, problem statement, and goals for transmitting IP over IEEE 802.15.4 networks.  The set of goals enumerated in this document form an initial set only.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjIntel CorpdnamenN. Kushalnagar¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationkDanfoss A/SdnamemC. Schumacherddatej”Âhfilenamegrfc4919eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymg6lowpandfullx"6lowpan - IPv6 over Low power WPANdnamexIPv6 over Low power WPANdtypebwgbidjdoc-126829hkeywordsÅmieee 802.15.4kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF√á¸granking7crfcd4919irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsIPv6 over Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPANs): Overview, Assumptions, Problem Statement, and GoalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) consists of a set of methods, headers, and content-types ancillary to HTTP/1.1 for the management of resource properties, creation and management of resource collections, URL namespace manipulation, and resource locking (collision avoidance).

RFC 2518 was published in February 1999, and this specification obsoletes RFC 2518 with minor revisions mostly due to interoperability experience. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkCommerceNetdnamelL. Dusseaultddatej”ﬁhfilenamegrfc4918eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfwebdavdfullx1webdav - WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdnamex(WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdtypebwgbidjdoc-126828hkeywordsÜfWEBDAVihypertexthtransferhprotocolcwebgcontentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÜÕFgranking6crfcd4918irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEHTTP Extensions for Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5689∂habstractyThis document specifies a new extension for use in Mobile IPv4.  This extension can be added by the Home Agent and the Foreign Agent to Registration Reply messages.  This extension carries a text string that is intended for the user of the Mobile Node. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnameiV. Sastry¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Patelddatej”‡hfilenamegrfc4917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126827hkeywordsÉjhome agentmforeign agentrregistration replykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÄ\granking5crfcd4917irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Mobile IPv4 Message String ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹This document provides a means for a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) User Agent (UA) that receives a dialog-forming request to supply its identity to the peer UA by means of a request in the reverse direction, and for that identity to be signed by an Authentication Service.  Because of retargeting of a dialog-forming request (changing the value of the Request-URI), the UA that receives it (the User Agent Server, UAS) can have a different identity from that in the To header field.  The same mechanism can be used to indicate a change of identity during a dialog, e.g., because of some action in the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) behind a gateway.  This document normatively updates RFC 3261 (SIP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx)Siemens Enterprise Communications LimiteddnameiJ. Elwellddatej”ﬂhfilenamegrfc4916eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126826hkeywordsàjuser agentbuaqapplication-layerkapplicationelayerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFy∆(granking4crfcd4916irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Connected Identity in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄This document describes an extension to Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) in order to define independent IP topologies called Multi- Topologies (MTs). The Multi-Topologies extension can be used for computing different paths for unicast traffic, multicast traffic, different classes of service based on flexible criteria, or an in- band network management topology.

An optional extension to exclude selected links from the default topology is also described. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationpForce10 NetworksdnamelS. Mirtorabi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiL. Nguyen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameqP. Pillay-Esnaultddatej”÷hfilenamegrfc4915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126825hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÜÕ8granking3crfcd4915irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Multi-Topology (MT) Routing in OSPFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFAbstract Syntax Notation X (ASN.X) is an Extensible Markup Language (XML) representation for Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) specifications.  This document specifies the ASN.X representation of encoding instructions for the XML Encoding Rules (XER).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Leggddatej”·hfilenamegrfc4914eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126824hkeywordsÅxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateFô1dgranking2crfcd4914irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkAbstract Syntax Notation X (ASN.X) Representation of Encoding Instructions for the XML Encoding Rules (XER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRAbstract Syntax Notation X (ASN.X) is an Extensible Markup Language (XML) representation for Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) specifications.  This document specifies the ASN.X representation of encoding instructions for the Generic String Encoding Rules (GSER).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Leggddatej”·hfilenamegrfc4913eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126823hkeywordsÅxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateFô1egranking1crfcd4913irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexwAbstract Syntax Notation X (ASN.X) Representation of Encoding Instructions for the Generic String Encoding Rules (GSER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìAbstract Syntax Notation X (ASN.X) is a semantically equivalent Extensible Markup Language (XML) representation for Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) specifications.  ASN.X completely avoids the numerous ambiguities inherent in the ASN.1 language; therefore, specifications written in ASN.X are much easier to parse and manage than original ASN.1 specifications.  ASN.X, together with the Robust XML Encoding Rules (RXER), constitutes a schema language for XML documents that offers, through other ASN.1 encoding rules, alternative compact binary encodings for XML instance documents.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Leggddatej”·hfilenamegrfc4912eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126822hkeywordsÖxextensible markup languageeasn.1xabstract syntax notation onexrobust xml encoding rulesdrxerkobsoletedByÄepages•opublicationDateFô1bgranking0crfcd4912irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Abstract Syntax Notation X (ASN.X)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿThis document defines encoding instructions that may be used in an Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) specification to alter how ASN.1 values are encoded by the Robust XML Encoding Rules (RXER) and Canonical Robust XML Encoding Rules (CRXER), for example, to encode a component of an ASN.1 value as an Extensible Markup Language (XML) attribute rather than as a child element.  Some of these encoding instructions also affect how an ASN.1 specification is translated into an Abstract Syntax Notation X (ASN.X) specification.  Encoding instructions that allow an ASN.1 specification to reference definitions in other XML schema languages are also defined.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Leggddatej”·hfilenamegrfc4911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126821hkeywordsàxextensible markup languageeasn.1xabstract syntax notation onexrobust xml encoding rulesdrxerx#canonical robust xml encoding rulesecrxereasn.xkobsoletedByÄepages[opublicationDateFô1fgranking/crfcd4911irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Encoding Instructions for the Robust XML Encoding Rules (RXER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document defines a set of Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) encoding rules, called the Robust XML Encoding Rules or RXER, that produce an Extensible Markup Language (XML) representation for values of any given ASN.1 data type.  Rules for producing a canonical RXER encoding are also defined.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Legg¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Pragerddatej”‚hfilenamegrfc4910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126820hkeywordsÉxextensible markup languagencanonical rxerecrxerkobsoletedByÄepagesPopublicationDateFô1fgranking.crfcd4910irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIRobust XML Encoding Rules (RXER) for Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document specifies a new Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) General Extension payload (RFC 3830) to transport the short-term key message (STKM) and long-term key message (LTKM) payloads defined in the Open Mobile Alliance's (OMA) Browser and Content (BAC) Broadcast (BCAST) group's Service and Content protection specification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnQUALCOMM, Inc.dnamejL. Dondeti¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamemD. Castleford¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamejF. Hartungddatej”Ÿhfilenamegrfc4909eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126819hkeywordsÜvshort-term key messageulong-term key messagecomacbacsbrowser and contentibroadcastkobsoletedByÇd5410d6309epagesopublicationDateFÉ␀granking-crfcd4909irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoMultimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) General Extension Payload for Open Mobile Alliance BCAST LTKM/STKM TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty§Multihoming technology improves the availability of host and network connectivity.  Since the behaviors of fixed and mobile networks differ, distinct architectures for each have been discussed and proposed.  This document proposes a common architecture for both mobile and fixed networking environments, using mobile IP (RFC 3775) and Network Mobility (NEMO; RFC 3963).  The proposed architecture requires a modification of mobile IP and NEMO so that multiple Care-of Addresses (CoAs) can be used.  In addition, multiple Home Agents (HAs) that are located in different places are required for redundancy.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmINTEC NetCorednameiK. Nagami¢kaffiliationeJAISTdnamefS. Uda¢kaffiliationlNOWARE, Inc.dnamekN. Ogashiwa¢kaffiliationsUniversity of TokyodnamehH. Esaki¢kaffiliationsUniversity of TokyodnamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamejH. Ohnishiddatej”ihfilenamegrfc4908eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126818hkeywordsÅqcare-of addresseskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateFÅõDgranking,crfcd4908irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexCMulti-homing for small scale fixed network Using Mobile IP and NEMOdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty9A link indication represents information provided by the link layer to higher layers regarding the state of the link.  This document describes the role of link indications within the Internet architecture.  While the judicious use of link indications can provide performance benefits, inappropriate use can degrade both robustness and performance.  This document summarizes current proposals, describes the architectural issues, and provides examples of appropriate and inappropriate uses of link indications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamehB. AbobaddatejÃ5hfilenamegrfc4907eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126817hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDateFr‘ögranking+crfcd4907irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex.Architectural Implications of Link IndicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document describes methods for transporting the Protocol Data Units (PDUs) of layer 2 protocols such as Frame Relay, Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) Adaption Layer 5 (AAL5), and Ethernet, and for providing a Synchronized Optical Network (SONET) circuit emulation service across an MPLS network.  This document describes the so-called "draft-martini" protocol, which has since been superseded by the Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edge Working Group specifications described in RFC 4447 and related documents.  This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationxLevel 3 Communications, LLCdnamekN. El-Aawarddatej”»hfilenamegrfc4906eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126816hkeywordsÖxmultiprotocol label switchingcpdurprotocol data unitesonetxsynchronized optical networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFy∆Agranking*crfcd4906irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Transport of Layer 2 Frames Over MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document describes methods for encapsulating the Protocol Data Units (PDUs) of layer 2 protocols such as Frame Relay, Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM), or Ethernet for transport across an MPLS network.  This document describes the so-called "draft-martini" protocol, which has since been superseded by the Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edge Working Group specifications described in RFC 4447 and related documents.  This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationxLevel 3 Communications, LLCdnamekN. El-Aawarddatej”»hfilenamegrfc4905eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126815hkeywordsÑxmultiprotocol label switchingcpdurprotocol data unitmdraft-martinikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFy∆2granking)crfcd4905irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHEncapsulation Methods for Transport of Layer 2 Frames over MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx—This document describes a standardized mechanism to convey trunk group parameters in sip and tel Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs).  An extension to the tel URI is defined for this purpose. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx"Bell Laboratories & Alcatel-LucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jenningsddatej”´hfilenamegrfc4904eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-126814hkeywordsÑgSIP TELkTrunk groupjtrunkgroupdPSTNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFp*Qgranking(crfcd4904irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRepresenting Trunk Groups in tel/sip Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThere have been several proposals around the notion that a subnet may span multiple links connected by routers.  This memo documents the issues and potential problems that have been raised with such an approach.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnameiD. ThalerddatejÃ5hfilenamegrfc4903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126813hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFÄ\granking'crfcd4903irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexMulti-Link Subnet IssuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThe Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) framework is application agnostic. Application-specific adaptations extend that framework. Previous work has focused on HTTP and work for SMTP is in progress. These protocols differ fundamentally in the way data flows, and it turns out that existing OPES requirements and IAB considerations for OPES need to be reviewed with regards to how well they fit for SMTP adaptation. This document analyzes aspects about the integrity of SMTP and mail message adaptation by OPES systems and about privacy and security issues when the OPES framework is adapted to SMTP. It also lists requirements that must be considered when creating the "SMTP adaptation with OPES" document.

The intent of this document is to capture this information before the current OPES working group shuts down. This is to provide input for subsequent working groups or individual contributors that may pick up the OPES/SMTP work at a later date. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxsimple mail transfer protocoldnamejM. StecherddatejÃ9hfilenamegrfc4902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126812hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFWR˛granking&crfcd4902irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPIntegrity, Privacy, and Security in Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) for SMTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThis specification defines how to use Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) to route Header-Compressed (HC) packets over an MPLS label switched path.  HC can significantly reduce packet-header overhead and, in combination with MPLS, can also increases bandwidth efficiency and processing scalability in terms of the maximum number of simultaneous compressed flows that use HC at each router).  Here we define how MPLS pseudowires are used to transport the HC context and control messages between the ingress and egress MPLS label switching routers.  This is defined for a specific set of existing HC mechanisms that might be used, for example, to support voice over IP.  This specification also describes extension mechanisms to allow support for future, as yet to be defined, HC protocols.  In this specification, each HC protocol operates independently over a single pseudowire instance, very much as it would over a single point-to-point link. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ash¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegJ. Hand¢kaffiliationvVerizon CommunicationsdnamehA. Malisddatej”ﬁhfilenamegrfc4901eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126811hkeywordsÇxmultiprotocol label switchingbhckobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateFr‘çgranking%crfcd4901irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Protocol Extensions for Header Compression over MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis document describes extended performance statistics for TCP.  They are designed to use TCP's ideal vantage point to diagnose performance problems in both the network and the application.  If a network-based application is performing poorly, TCP can determine if the bottleneck is in the sender, the receiver, or the network itself.  If the bottleneck is in the network, TCP can provide specific information about its nature. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Pittsburgh Supercomputing CenterdnameiM. Mathis¢kaffiliationx Pittsburgh Supercomputing CenterdnamejJ. Heffner¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoR. Raghunarayanddatej”‹hfilenamegrfc4898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126810hkeywordsÉxtransmission control protocolxmanagement information basenTCP-ESTATS-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDateF^¿granking"crfcd4898irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTCP Extended Statistics MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¿The Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) and Request for Comments (RFC) Editor have a long-standing rule that a document at a given maturity level cannot be published until all of the documents that it references as normative are at that maturity level or higher.  This rule has sometimes resulted in very long publication delays for documents and some claims that it was a major obstruction to advancing documents in maturity level.  The IETF agreed on a way to bypass this rule with RFC 3967.  This document describes a simpler procedure for downward references to Standards-Track and Best Current Practice (BCP) documents, namely "note and move on".  The procedure in RFC 3967 still applies for downward references to other classes of documents.  In both cases, annotations should be added to such References.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejS. HartmanddatejÃEhfilenamegrfc4897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126809hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFp*Ggranking!crfcd4897irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberaetotaletitlex:Handling Normative References to Standards-Track DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†This document describes common misinterpretations and some ambiguities in the Signaling Compression Protocol (SigComp), and offers guidance to developers to resolve any resultant problems.  SigComp defines a scheme for compressing messages generated by application protocols such as the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  This document updates the following RFCs: RFC 3320, RFC 3321, and RFC 3485. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamejA. Surtees¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamegM. West¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnamejA.B. Roachddatej”“hfilenamegrfc4896eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126808hkeywordsÖcsipxsession initiation protocoldudvmx&universal decompressor virtual machineialgorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFr‘Ågranking crfcd4896irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Signaling Compression (SigComp) Corrections and ClarificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document describes a new chunk type, several parameters, and procedures for the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  This new chunk type can be used to authenticate SCTP chunks by using shared keys between the sender and receiver.  The new parameters are used to establish the shared keys. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefP. Lei¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorladdatej”´hfilenamegrfc4895eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126807hkeywordsÖjchunk typekshared keysfRANDOMfCHUNKSiHMAC-ALGOkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF’‡§grankingcrfcd4895irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHAuthenticated Chunks for the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document describes how the IKEv1 (Internet Key Exchange version 1), IKEv2, and IPsec protocols use hash functions, and explains the level of vulnerability of these protocols to the reduced collision resistance of the MD5 and SHA-1 hash algorithms.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej”ﬂhfilenamegrfc4894eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126806hkeywordsÉcmd5dpkixlcertificateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF_jÂgrankingcrfcd4894irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Use of Hash Algorithms in Internet Key Exchange (IKE) and IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxœCurrently the Autonomous System (AS) number is encoded as a two-octet entity in BGP.  This document describes extensions to BGP to carry the Autonomous System number as a four-octet entity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehQ. Vohra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chenddatej”‹hfilenamegrfc4893eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126805hkeywordsÇqautonomous systemwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÅd6793epages
opublicationDateFNE§grankingcrfcd4893irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*BGP Support for Four-octet AS Number SpacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqWith the increased use of DNS anycast, load balancing, and other mechanisms allowing more than one DNS name server to share a single IP address, it is sometimes difficult to tell which of a pool of name servers has answered a particular query.  A standardized mechanism to determine the identity of a name server responding to a particular query would be useful, particularly as a diagnostic aid for administrators.  Existing ad hoc mechanisms for addressing this need have some shortcomings, not the least of which is the lack of prior analysis of exactly how such a mechanism should be designed and deployed.  This document describes the existing convention used in some widely deployed implementations of the DNS protocol, including advantages and disadvantages, and discusses some attributes of an improved mechanism.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx!Internet Systems Consortium, Inc.dnamehS. Woolf¢kaffiliationeICANNdnameiD. Conradddatej”‡hfilenamegrfc4892eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126804hkeywordsÇsdomain name serviceodns name serverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFy∆[grankingcrfcd4892irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Requirements for a Mechanism Identifying a Name Server InstancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document gives guidance on securing manually configured IPv6-in- IPv4 tunnels using IPsec in transport mode.  No additional protocol extensions are described beyond those available with the IPsec framework.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqRFG Security, LLCdnamekR. Graveman¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepM. Parthasarathy¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”€hfilenamegrfc4891eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126803hkeywordsÉqinternet protocolxinternet protocol securitykip securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFD⁄grankingcrfcd4891irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Using IPsec to Secure IPv6-in-IPv4 TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyãIn networks supporting IPv6, the Internet Control Message Protocol version 6 (ICMPv6) plays a fundamental role with a large number of functions, and a correspondingly large number of message types and options. ICMPv6 is essential to the functioning of IPv6, but there are a number of security risks associated with uncontrolled forwarding of ICMPv6 messages. Filtering strategies designed for the corresponding protocol, ICMP, in IPv4 networks are not directly applicable, because these strategies are intended to accommodate a useful auxiliary protocol that may not be required for correct functioning.

This document provides some recommendations for ICMPv6 firewall filter configuration that will allow propagation of ICMPv6 messages that are needed to maintain the functioning of the network but drop messages that are potential security risks. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationnNIIF/HUNGARNETdnamejJ. Mohacsiddatej”€hfilenamegrfc4890eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126802hkeywordsÜx+Internet Control Message Protocol version 6dipv6hsecurityffilterhfirewallficmpv6kobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateFD⁄qgrankingcrfcd4890irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Recommendations for Filtering ICMPv6 Messages in FirewallsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUWith current Network Mobility (NEMO) Basic Support, all communications to and from Mobile Network Nodes must go through the Mobile Router and Home Agent (MRHA) tunnel when the mobile network is away.  This results in increased length of packet route and increased packet delay in most cases.  To overcome these limitations, one might have to turn to Route Optimization (RO) for NEMO.  This memo documents various types of Route Optimization in NEMO and explores the benefits and tradeoffs in different aspects of NEMO Route Optimization.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxPanasonic Singapore LabsdnameeC. Ng¢kaffiliationhUC DavisdnamegF. Zhao¢kaffiliationmKDDI R&D LabsdnameiM. Watari¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubertddatej”œhfilenamegrfc4889eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnemodfullwnemo - Network MobilitydnamepNetwork Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126801hkeywordsÑdnemodmrhaxmobile router and home agentbrokobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateFû¨•grankingcrfcd4889irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Network Mobility Route Optimization Solution Space AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#With current Network Mobility (NEMO) Basic Support, all communications to and from Mobile Network Nodes must go through the bi-directional tunnel established between the Mobile Router and Home Agent when the mobile network is away.  This sub-optimal routing results in various inefficiencies associated with packet delivery, such as increased delay and bottleneck links leading to traffic congestion, which can ultimately disrupt all communications to and from the Mobile Network Nodes.  Additionally, with nesting of Mobile Networks, these inefficiencies get compounded, and stalemate conditions may occur in specific dispositions.  This document investigates such problems and provides the motivation behind Route Optimization (RO) for NEMO.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxPanasonic Singapore LabsdnameeC. Ng¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationmKDDI R&D LabsdnameiM. Watari¢kaffiliationhUC DavisdnamegF. Zhaoddatej”Œhfilenamegrfc4888eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnemodfullwnemo - Network MobilitydnamepNetwork Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126800hkeywordsÅdnemokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFû¨ögrankingcrfcd4888irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Network Mobility Route Optimization Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis paper documents some of the usage patterns and the associated issues when deploying a Home Network for Network Mobility (NEMO)- enabled Mobile Routers, conforming to the NEMO Basic Support.  The aim here is specifically to provide some examples of organization of the Home Network, as they were discussed in NEMO-related mailing lists.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubert¢kaffiliationxKeio University and WIDEdnamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationoAzaire NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalliddatej”£hfilenamegrfc4887eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnemodfullwnemo - Network MobilitydnamepNetwork Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126799hkeywordsÇdnemonmobile routerskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFû¨ãgrankingcrfcd4887irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Network Mobility Home Network ModelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬Network mobility arises when a router connecting a network to the Internet dynamically changes its point of attachment to the Internet thereby causing the reachability of the said network to be changed in relation to the fixed Internet topology.  Such a type of network is referred to as a mobile network.  With appropriate mechanisms, sessions established between nodes in the mobile network and the global Internet can be maintained after the mobile router changes its point of attachment.  This document outlines the goals expected from network mobility support and defines the requirements that must be met by the NEMO Basic Support solution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamehT. Ernstddatej”œhfilenamegrfc4886eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnemodfullwnemo - Network MobilitydnamepNetwork Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126798hkeywordsÅdnemokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFû¨}grankingcrfcd4886irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Network Mobility Support Goals and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx®This document defines a terminology for discussing network mobility (NEMO) issues and solution requirements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamehT. Ernst¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameiH-Y. Lachddatej”–hfilenamegrfc4885eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnemodfullwnemo - Network MobilitydnamepNetwork Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126797hkeywordsÅdnemokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFû¨sgrankingcrfcd4885irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Network Mobility Support TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This document redefines selected ICMP messages to support multi-part operation. A multi-part ICMP message carries all of the information that ICMP messages carried previously, as well as additional information that applications may require.

Multi-part messages are supported by an ICMP extension structure. The extension structure is situated at the end of the ICMP message. It includes an extension header followed by one or more extension objects. Each extension object contains an object header and object payload. All object headers share a common format.

This document further redefines the above mentioned ICMP messages by specifying a length attribute. All of the currently defined ICMP messages to which an extension structure can be appended include an "original datagram" field. The "original datagram" field contains the initial octets of the datagram that elicited the ICMP error message. Although the original datagram field is of variable length, the ICMP message does not include a field that specifies its length. Therefore, in order to facilitate message parsing, this document allocates eight previously reserved bits to reflect the length of the "original datagram" field.

The proposed modifications change the requirements for ICMP compliance. The impact of these changes on compliant implementations is discussed, and new requirements for future implementations are presented.

This memo updates RFC 792 and RFC 4443. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamefD. Gan¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiD. Tappan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelC. Pignataroddatej”ÿhfilenamegrfc4884eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126796hkeywordsÇx!internet control message protocolplength attributekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF6∫‘grankingcrfcd4884irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Extended ICMP to Support Multi-Part MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8335∂habstracty£The primary purpose of this document is to define terminology specific to the benchmarking of resource reservation signaling of Integrated Services (IntServ) IP routers.  These terms can be used in additional documents that define benchmarking methodologies for routers that support resource reservation or reporting formats for the benchmarking measurements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx/Budapest University of Technology and EconomicsdnamehG. Feher¢kaffiliationx/Budapest University of Technology and EconomicsdnameiK. Nemeth¢kaffiliationx!TeliaSonera International CarrierdnamegA. Korn¢kaffiliation`dnamekI. Cselenyiddatej”€hfilenamegrfc4883eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-126795hkeywordsÉgintservsintegrated servicesxbenchmarking methodologieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFßs√grankingcrfcd4883irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexABenchmarking Terminology for Resource Reservation Capable RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn this document, we discuss location privacy as applicable to Mobile IPv6.  We document the concerns arising from revealing a Home Address to an onlooker and from disclosing a Care-of Address to a correspondent.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnameiR. Koodliddatej”‹hfilenamegrfc4882eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126794hkeywordsÉqinternet protocollhome addressocare-of addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFWRıgrankingcrfcd4882irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>IP Address Location Privacy and Mobile IPv6: Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÛMobile IPv4 describes how a Mobile Node can perform IPv4-layer handoffs between subnets served by different Foreign Agents.  In certain cases, the latency involved in these handoffs can be above the threshold required for the support of delay-sensitive or real-time services.  The aim of this document is to present two methods to achieve low-latency Mobile IPv4 handoffs.  In addition, a combination of these two methods is described.  The described techniques allow greater support for real-time services on a Mobile IPv4 network by minimizing the period of time when a Mobile Node is unable to send or receive IPv4 packets due to the delay in the Mobile IPv4 Registration process.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgAthonetdnamekK. El Malkiddatej”éhfilenamegrfc4881eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126793hkeywordsÅdmip4kobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateFÜÕWgrankingcrfcd4881irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Low-Latency Handoffs in Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty.This document is maintained in order to publish all necessary information needed to develop interoperable applications based on the OpenPGP format. It is not a step-by-step cookbook for writing an application. It describes only the format and methods needed to read, check, generate, and write conforming packets crossing any network. It does not deal with storage and implementation questions. It does, however, discuss implementation issues necessary to avoid security flaws.

OpenPGP software uses a combination of strong public-key and symmetric cryptography to provide security services for electronic communications and data storage. These services include confidentiality, key management, authentication, and digital signatures. This document specifies the message formats used in OpenPGP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoPGP CorporationdnameiJ. Callas¢kaffiliationhIKS GmbHdnamenL. Donnerhacke¢kaffiliationoPGP CorporationdnameiH. Finney¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Shaw¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Thayerddatej”ˆhfilenamegrfc4880eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgopenpgpdfullx4openpgp - Open Specification for Pretty Good Privacydnamex*Open Specification for Pretty Good Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-126792hkeywordsÇwpublic-key cryptographyvsymmetric cryptographykobsoletedByÅd9580epagesZopublicationDateG+œgrankingcrfcd4880irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevOpenPGP Message FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5581∂habstractyThis document clarifies and updates a single sentence in RFC 3979.  Specifically, when third party Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) disclosures are made, the intention is that the IETF Executive Director notify the IPR holder that a third party disclosure has been filed, and to ask the IPR holder whether they have any disclosure that needs to be made, per applicable RFC 3979 rules.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Nartenddatej””hfilenamegrfc4879eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126791hkeywordsÇcipricopyrightkobsoletedByÅd8179epagesopublicationDateF’ögrankingcrfcd4879irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAClarification of the Third Party Disclosure Procedure in RFC 3979dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines objects for managing Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) capabilities on Ethernet-like interfaces conformant to the Ethernet OAM functionality defined in the Ethernet in the First Mile (EFM) clauses of the Ethernet standards.  The Ethernet OAM functionality is complementary to the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) in that it is focused on a small set of link-specific functions for directly connected Ethernet interfaces.  This document defines objects for controlling those link OAM functions and for providing results and status of the OAM functions to management entities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqHatteras NetworksdnameiM. Squireddatej”ﬂhfilenamegrfc4878eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126790hkeywordsÑcefmxethernet in the first miledsnmplDOT3-OAM-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages:opublicationDateFÜÕ`grankingcrfcd4878irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Definitions and Managed Objects for Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) Functions on Ethernet-Like InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxnThis document describes Mobile IPv6 operation with the revised IPsec architecture and IKEv2. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoAzaire NetworksdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationeCELARdnameiF. Dupontddatej””hfilenamegrfc4877eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126789hkeywordsÖmBootstrappingdMIP6tSelector GranularityoMobility HeaderrEAP AuthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF7grankingcrfcd4877irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCMobile IPv6 Operation with IKEv2 and the Revised IPsec ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty<This document consists of two primary components, a schema for agents that make use of the Lightweight Directory Access protocol (LDAP) and a proposed use case of that schema, for distributed configuration of similar directory user agents.  A set of attribute types and an object class are proposed.  In the proposed use case, directory user agents (DUAs) can use this schema to determine directory data location and access parameters for specific services they support.  In addition, in the proposed use case, attribute and object class mapping allows DUAs to reconfigure their expected (default) schema to match that of the end user's environment.  This document is intended to be a skeleton for future documents that describe configuration of specific DUA services.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationbHPdnamenB. Neal-Joslin¢kaffiliationdPADLdnameiL. Howard¢kaffiliationhInfobloxdnameiM. Ansariddatej”∆hfilenamegrfc4876eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126788hkeywordsädldapfschemagprofilemconfigurationknameservicecnsshpam_ldaphnss_ldapgrfc2307hrfc 2307kobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateFNCúgrankingcrfcd4876irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex\A Configuration Profile Schema for Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)-Based AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document describes extensions to Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) for the set up of Traffic Engineered (TE) point-to-multipoint (P2MP) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in Multi- Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) networks. The solution relies on RSVP-TE without requiring a multicast routing protocol in the Service Provider core. Protocol elements and procedures for this solution are described.

There can be various applications for P2MP TE LSPs such as IP multicast. Specification of how such applications will use a P2MP TE LSP is outside the scope of this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Yasukawaddatej”‘hfilenamegrfc4875eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126787hkeywordsÉdp2mpspoint-to-multipointstraffic engineeringkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateFD⁄egrankingcrfcd4875irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇExtensions to Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) for Point-to-Multipoint TE Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6510∂habstracty∂This document specifies ways to communicate route exclusions during path setup using Resource ReserVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE).

The RSVP-TE specification, "RSVP-TE: Extensions to RSVP for LSP Tunnels" (RFC 3209) and GMPLS extensions to RSVP-TE, "Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Resource ReserVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Extensions" (RFC 3473) allow abstract nodes and resources to be explicitly included in a path setup, but not to be explicitly excluded.

In some networks where precise explicit paths are not computed at the head end, it may be useful to specify and signal abstract nodes and resources that are to be explicitly excluded from routes. These exclusions may apply to the whole path, or to parts of a path between two abstract nodes specified in an explicit path. How Shared Risk Link Groups (SRLGs) can be excluded is also specified in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnamegCY. Lee¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamemS. De Cnodderddatej”œhfilenamegrfc4874eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126786hkeywordsÇdsrlgwshared risk link groupskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF’ïgranking
crfcd4874irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYExclude Routes - Extension to Resource ReserVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6001d8390∂habstracty'This document describes protocol specific procedures for GMPLS (Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching) RSVP-TE (Resource ReserVation Protocol - Traffic Engineering) signaling extensions to support label switched path (LSP) segment protection and restoration.  These extensions are intended to complement and be consistent with the RSVP-TE Extensions for End-to-End GMPLS Recovery (RFC 4872).  Implications and interactions with fast reroute are also addressed.  This document also updates the handling of NOTIFY_REQUEST objects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoLabN ConsultingdnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationlADVA OpticaldnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej”“hfilenamegrfc4873eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126785hkeywordsÖx&generalized multipoint label switchinggrsvp-texresource reservation protocolstraffic engineeringnNOTIFY_REQUESTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF@Ògranking	crfcd4873irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevGMPLS Segment RecoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9270∂habstractyöThis document describes protocol-specific procedures and extensions for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Resource ReSerVation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling to support end-to-end Label Switched Path (LSP) recovery that denotes protection and restoration.  A generic functional description of GMPLS recovery can be found in a companion document, RFC 4426. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeSonosdnameiJ.P. Lang¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej”“hfilenamegrfc4872eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126784hkeywordsÇxresource reservation protocolstraffic engineeringkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateF@grankingcrfcd4872irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgRSVP-TE Extensions in Support of End-to-End Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) RecoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÉd4873d9270d6780∂habstractyâDomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) defines a domain-level authentication framework for email using public-key cryptography and key server technology to permit verification of the source and contents of messages by either Mail Transfer Agents (MTAs) or Mail User Agents (MUAs).  The ultimate goal of this framework is to permit a signing domain to assert responsibility for a message, thus protecting message signer identity and the integrity of the messages they convey while retaining the functionality of Internet email as it is known today.  Protection of email identity may assist in the global control of "spam" and "phishing". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnameiE. Allman¢kaffiliationoPGP CorporationdnameiJ. Callas¢kaffiliationjYahoo! IncdnameiM. Delany¢kaffiliationjYahoo! IncdnameiM. Libbey¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Fenton¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Thomasddatej”⁄hfilenamegrfc4871eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-126783hkeywordsÜminternet mailnauthenticationdspamhphishinghspoofingqdigital signaturekobsoletedByÅd6376epagesGopublicationDateFSÖügrankingcrfcd4871irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5672∂habstracty7"DomainKeys" creates a domain-level authentication framework for email by using public key technology and the DNS to prove the provenance and contents of an email.

This document defines a framework for digitally signing email on a per-domain basis. The ultimate goal of this framework is to unequivocally prove and protect identity while retaining the semantics of Internet email as it is known today.

Proof and protection of email identity may assist in the global control of "spam" and "phishing". This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjYahoo! IncdnameiM. DelanyddatejÃ1hfilenamegrfc4870eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126782hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd4871epages)opublicationDateFSÖógrankingcrfcd4870irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVDomain-Based Email Authentication Using Public Keys Advertised in the DNS (DomainKeys)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document proposes four optional cryptographic user interface suites ("UI suites") for IPsec, similar to the two suites specified in RFC 4308.  The four new suites provide compatibility with the United States National Security Agency's Suite B specifications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamefL. Law¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejJ. Solinasddatej”Ÿhfilenamegrfc4869eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126781hkeywordsÑiui suitesuuser interface suitesnelliptic curvecikekobsoletedByÅd6379epages	opublicationDateF;∫Ògrankingcrfcd4869irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Suite B Cryptographic Suites for IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This specification describes the use of Hashed Message Authentication Mode (HMAC) in conjunction with the SHA-256, SHA-384, and SHA-512 algorithms in IPsec.  These algorithms may be used as the basis for data origin authentication and integrity verification mechanisms for the Authentication Header (AH), Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP), Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE), and IKEv2 protocols, and also as Pseudo-Random Functions (PRFs) for IKE and IKEv2.  Truncated output lengths are specified for the authentication-related variants, with the corresponding algorithms designated as HMAC-SHA-256-128, HMAC-SHA-384-192, and HMAC-SHA-512-256.  The PRF variants are not truncated, and are called PRF-HMAC-SHA-256, PRF-HMAC-SHA-384, and PRF-HMAC-SHA-512. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamehS. Kelly¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamejS. Frankelddatej”Ÿhfilenamegrfc4868eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126780hkeywordsÉxhashed authentication modesdata authenticationvintegrity verificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF;∫…grankingcrfcd4868irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Using HMAC-SHA-256, HMAC-SHA-384, and HMAC-SHA-512 with IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document specifies a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format to be used for Adaptive Multi-Rate (AMR) and Adaptive Multi-Rate Wideband (AMR-WB) encoded speech signals.  The payload format is designed to be able to interoperate with existing AMR and AMR-WB transport formats on non-IP networks.  In addition, a file format is specified for transport of AMR and AMR-WB speech data in storage mode applications such as email.  Two separate media type registrations are included, one for AMR and one for AMR-WB, specifying use of both the RTP payload format and the storage format.  This document obsoletes RFC 3267. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Sjoberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelA. Lakaniemi¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamefQ. Xieddatej”√hfilenamegrfc4867eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126779hkeywordsÇlinteroperatelapplicationskobsoletedByÄepages;opublicationDateF’égrankingcrfcd4867irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉRTP Payload Format and File Storage Format for the Adaptive Multi-Rate (AMR) and Adaptive Multi-Rate Wideband (AMR-WB) Audio CodecsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¥This document specifies an enhanced version of Mobile IPv6 route optimization, providing lower handoff delays, increased security, and reduced signaling overhead. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationxUniversitaet Karlsruhe (TH)dnamegC. Vogt¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnameiW. Haddadddatej”⁄hfilenamegrfc4866eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126778hkeywordsÖhmobilityx%cryptographically generated addressesccgaxcredit-based authorizationccbakobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateFD⁄Ugrankingcrfcd4866irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Enhanced Route Optimization for Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis memo defines an extension to the SMTP submission protocol for a client to indicate a future time for the message to be released for delivery.  This extension permits a client to use server-based storage for a message that should be held in queue until an appointed time in the future.  This is useful for clients which do not have local storage or are otherwise unable to release a message for delivery at an appointed time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehG. White¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuilddatej”»hfilenamegrfc4865eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126777hkeywordsÇxsimple mail transfer protocolvfuture-release-integerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF_jÈgrankingcrfcd4865irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<SMTP Submission Service Extension for Future Message ReleasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖAlthough there are many perceived benefits to Network Address Translation (NAT), its primary benefit of "amplifying" available address space is not needed in IPv6.  In addition to NAT's many serious disadvantages, there is a perception that other benefits exist, such as a variety of management and security attributes that could be useful for an Internet Protocol site.  IPv6 was designed with the intention of making NAT unnecessary, and this document shows how Local Network Protection (LNP) using IPv6 can provide the same or more benefits without the need for address translation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoG. Van de Velde¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegT. Hain¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationsTel Aviv UniversitydnamehE. Kleinddatej”Ÿhfilenamegrfc4864eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126776hkeywordsÑdipv6gaddressjprotectioncnatkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateFNEìgranking␀crfcd4864irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Local Network Protection for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywPseudowire signaling requires that the Pseudowire Type (PW Type) be identical in both directions.  For certain applications the configuration of the PW Type is most easily accomplished by configuring this information at just one PW endpoint.  In any form of LDP-based signaling, each PW endpoint must initiate the creation of a unidirectional LSP.  In order to allow the initiation of these two LSPs to remain independent, a means is needed for allowing the PW endpoint (lacking a priori knowledge of the PW Type) to initiate the creation of an LSP.  This document defines a Wildcard PW Type to satisfy this need. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallowddatej”⁄hfilenamegrfc4863eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126775hkeywordsÇgpw typebpwkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFD⁄Egrankingˇcrfcd4863irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWildcard Pseudowire TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrThis document specifies the steps a host takes in deciding how to autoconfigure its interfaces in IP version 6.  The autoconfiguration process includes generating a link-local address, generating global addresses via stateless address autoconfiguration, and the Duplicate Address Detection procedure to verify the uniqueness of the addresses on a link. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Thomson¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationgToshibadnameiT. Jinmeiddatej”áhfilenamegrfc4862eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126774hkeywordsÜiIPV6-AUTOdhostjlink-localxinternet protocol version 6rlink-local addressxduplicate address detectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF¯;granking˛crfcd4862irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(IPv6 Stateless Address AutoconfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9762d7527∂habstractyAThis document specifies the Neighbor Discovery protocol for IP Version 6.  IPv6 nodes on the same link use Neighbor Discovery to discover each other's presence, to determine each other's link-layer addresses, to find routers, and to maintain reachability information about the paths to active neighbors. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Narten¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationjDaydreamerdnamejW. Simpson¢kaffiliationtElevate TechnologiesdnamejH. Solimanddatej”„hfilenamegrfc4861eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126773hkeywordsÑgIPV6-NDqinternet protocoljlink-layerrlink-layer addresskobsoletedByÄepagesaopublicationDateF¯;granking˝crfcd4861irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Neighbor Discovery for IP version 6 (IPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByåd9762d8425d5942d7527d7559d6980d7048d9131d9926d8319d8028d9685∂habstracty’RFC 3175 defines aggregate Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) reservations allowing resources to be reserved in a Diffserv network for a given Per Hop Behavior (PHB), or given set of PHBs, from a given source to a given destination.  RFC 3175 also defines how end-to-end RSVP reservations can be aggregated onto such aggregate reservations when transiting through a Diffserv cloud.  There are situations where multiple such aggregate reservations are needed for the same source IP address, destination IP address, and PHB (or set of PHBs).  However, this is not supported by the aggregate reservations defined in RFC 3175.  In order to support this, the present document defines a more flexible type of aggregate RSVP reservations, referred to as generic aggregate reservation.  Multiple such generic aggregate reservations can be established for a given PHB (or set of PHBs) from a given source IP address to a given destination IP address.  The generic aggregate reservations may be used to aggregate end-to-end RSVP reservations.  This document also defines the procedures for such aggregation.  The generic aggregate reservations may also be used end-to-end directly by end-systems attached to a Diffserv network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationoLockheed MartindnamegP. Bose¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamekC. Christou¢kaffiliationsBooz Allen HamiltondnamelM. Davenportddatej”ÿhfilenamegrfc4860eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126772hkeywordsÑnsession objectssession of interestcphbpper hop behaviorkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateF;∫Ÿgranking¸crfcd4860irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCGeneric Aggregate Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) ReservationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document provides instructions to IANA for the creation of a new codepoint registry for the flags field in the Session Attribute object of the Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling messages used in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) signaling.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. FarrelddatejÃ2hfilenamegrfc4859eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126771hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF1T`granking˚crfcd4859irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇCodepoint Registry for the Flags Field in the Resource Reservation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Session Attribute ObjectdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡This document describes methodologies that have been designed to improve and facilitate IETF document flow processing.  It specifies a set of procedures under which a working group chair or secretary serves as the primary Document Shepherd for a document that has been submitted to the IESG for publication.  Before this, the Area Director responsible for the working group has traditionally filled the shepherding role.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelH. Levkowetz¢kaffiliationxCisco/University of OregondnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Mankinddatej”‹hfilenamegrfc4858eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126770hkeywordsÇtdocument shepherdingnietf documentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFBQ]granking˙crfcd4858irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Document Shepherding from Working Group Last Call to PublicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“Using Mobile IP, a mobile node registers with its home agent each time it changes care-of address.  This document describes a new kind of "regional registrations", i.e., registrations local to the visited domain.  The regional registrations are performed via a new network entity called a Gateway Foreign Agent (GFA) and introduce a layer of hierarchy in the visited domain.  Regional registrations reduce the number of signaling messages to the home network, and reduce the signaling delay when a mobile node moves from one foreign agent to another within the same visited domain.  This document is an optional extension to the Mobile IPv4 protocol.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamenE. Fogelstroem¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejA. Jonsson¢kaffiliationvNokia Siemens NetworksdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”÷hfilenamegrfc4857eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126769hkeywordsÇcGFAugateway foreign agentkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateFr‘qgranking˘crfcd4857irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Mobile IPv4 Regional RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document specifies media type registrations for the RTP payload formats defined in the RTP Profile for Audio and Video Conferences.  Some of these may also be used for transfer modes other than RTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnameiS. Casnerddatej” hfilenamegrfc4856eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126768hkeywordsÇxrealtime transport protocolx%multipurpose internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÊ_≤granking¯crfcd4856irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Media Type Registration of Payload Formats in the RTP Profile for Audio and Video ConferencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˙This document specifies the procedure to register RTP payload formats as audio, video, or other media subtype names.  This is useful in a text-based format description or control protocol to identify the type of an RTP transmission. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnameiS. Casnerddatej”Àhfilenamegrfc4855eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126767hkeywordsÇxrealtime transport protocolx%multipurpose internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÊ_∆granking˜crfcd4855irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Media Type Registration of RTP Payload FormatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8851∂habstractypThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for uniquely identifying Extensible Markup Language (XML) formats and protocols that provide extensions to the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) and are defined in specifications published by the XMPP Standards Foundation (XSF).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcXSFdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej”€hfilenamegrfc4854eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126766hkeywordsáx*Extensible Messaging and Presence ProtocoldXMPPfJabberqInstant MessaginghPresenceuUniform Resource NamecURNkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateF2úvgrankingˆcrfcd4854irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for Extensions to the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˆThis document updates the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS), which is published in RFC 3852.  This document clarifies the proper handling of the SignedData protected content type when more than one digital signature is present. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej”⁄hfilenamegrfc4853eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126765hkeywordsÖjsigneddatandigitally signlauthenticategencryptxarbitrary message contentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF%7Ygrankingıcrfcd4853irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) Multiple Signer ClarificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document analyzes the transition to IPv6 in enterprise networks focusing on IP Layer 3.  These networks are characterized as having multiple internal links and one or more router connections to one or more Providers, and as being managed by a network operations entity.  The analysis focuses on a base set of transition notational networks and requirements expanded from a previous document on enterprise scenarios.  Discussion is provided on a focused set of transition analysis required for the enterprise to transition to IPv6, assuming a Dual-IP layer (IPv4 and IPv6) network and node environment within the enterprise.  Then, a set of transition mechanisms are recommended for each notational network.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamehJ. Bound¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamekY. Pouffary¢kaffiliationeMITREdnamejS. Klynsma¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationsCommand InformationdnamehD. Greenddatej”‘hfilenamegrfc4852eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126764hkeywordsÇxinternet protocol version 6rnotational networkkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateF)LKgrankingÙcrfcd4852irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3IPv6 Enterprise Network Analysis - IP Layer 3 FocusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) based Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling (EAP-FAST) protocol.  EAP-FAST is an EAP method that enables secure communication between a peer and a server by using the Transport Layer Security (TLS) to establish a mutually authenticated tunnel.  Within the tunnel, Type-Length-Value (TLV) objects are used to convey authentication related data between the peer and the EAP server.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemN. Cam-Winget¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegH. Zhouddatej”’hfilenamegrfc4851eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126763hkeywordsÅceapkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateF;∫„grankingÛcrfcd4851irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeThe Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling Extensible Authentication Protocol Method (EAP-FAST)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9427d8996∂habstractyõThe Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) protocol, described in RFC 3720, allows for extension items to the protocol in the form of Private or Public Extension Keys.  This document describes a Public Extension Key for the purpose of enhancing iSCSI supportability.  The key accomplishes this objective by allowing iSCSI nodes to communicate architecture details during the iSCSI login sequence.  The receiving node can then use this information for enhanced logging and support.  This document updates RFC 3720 to allow iSCSI extension items to be defined by standards track RFCs and experimental RFCs in addition to informational RFCs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwNetwork Appliance, Inc.dnamenD. Wysochanskiddatej”—hfilenamegrfc4850eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126762hkeywordsÉrtransport protocolctcpxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÅd7143epages	opublicationDateF%7◊grankingÚcrfcd4850irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhDeclarative Public Extension Key for Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) Node ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFWhile RFC 2865 defines the Filter-Id attribute, it requires that the Network Access Server (NAS) be pre-populated with the desired filters.  However, in situations where the server operator does not know which filters have been pre-populated, it is useful to specify filter rules explicitly.  This document defines the NAS-Filter-Rule attribute within the Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS).  This attribute is based on the Diameter NAS-Filter-Rule Attribute Value Pair (AVP) described in RFC 4005, and the IPFilterRule syntax defined in RFC 3588. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxProCurve Networking by HPdnamejP. Congdon¢kaffiliationxProCurve Networking by HPdnamejM. Sanchez¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Abobaddatej”÷hfilenamegrfc4849eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126761hkeywordsÇx*remote authentication dial in user serviceonas-filter-rulekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateF6∫ﬂgrankingÒcrfcd4849irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRADIUS Filter Rule AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}The purpose of this document is to define a new, straightforward Dynamic Delegation Discovery Service (DDDS) application to allow mapping of domain names to URIs for particular application services and protocols.  Although defined as a new DDDS application, dubbed U-NAPTR, this is effectively an extension of the Straightforward NAPTR (S-NAPTR) DDDS Application. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiL. Daigleddatej”Ÿhfilenamegrfc4848eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126760hkeywordsÇmservice-parmsrservice parameterskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateF6∫ªgrankingcrfcd4848irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhDomain-Based Application Service Location Using URIs and the Dynamic Delegation Discovery Service (DDDS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—This document provides a framework and service level requirements for Layer 1 Virtual Private Networks (L1VPNs). This framework is intended to aid in developing and standardizing protocols and mechanisms to support interoperable L1VPNs.

The document examines motivations for L1VPNs, high level (service level) requirements, and outlines some of the architectural models that might be used to build L1VPNs. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnameiT. Takedaddatej”“hfilenamegrfc4847eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel1vpndfullx(l1vpn - Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 1 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126759hkeywordsÅeL1VPNkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateF6∫ÎgrankingÔcrfcd4847irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Framework and Requirements for Layer 1 Virtual Private NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty7There is a long-standing tradition in the Internet community, predating the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) by many years, of use of the RFC Series to publish materials that are not rooted in the IETF standards process and its review and approval mechanisms.  These documents, known as "Independent Submissions", serve a number of important functions for the Internet community, both inside and outside of the community of active IETF participants.  This document discusses the Independent Submission model and some reasons why it is important.  It then describes editorial and processing norms that can be used for Independent Submissions as the community goes forward into new relationships between the IETF community and its primary technical publisher.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. ThalerddatejÃAhfilenamegrfc4846eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126758hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFû¨RgrankingÓcrfcd4846irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex)Independent Submissions to the RFC EditordtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5744µhabstractyFrom time to time, the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) publishes documents as Requests for Comments (RFCs).  This document defines the process by which those documents are produced, reviewed, and published in the RFC Series.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationcIABdnamexInternet Architecture BoardddatejÃ@hfilenamegrfc4845eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126757hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFû¨GgrankingÌcrfcd4845irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex#Process for Publication of IAB RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyqThis document describes the framework for an RFC Series and an RFC Editor function that incorporate the principles of organized community involvement and accountability that has become necessary as the Internet technical community has grown, thereby enabling the RFC Series to continue to fulfill its mandate.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationcIABdnamexInternet Architecture Boardddatej”Êhfilenamegrfc4844eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126756hkeywordsÅstechnical publisherkobsoletedByÅd8729epagesopublicationDateFû¨:grankingÏcrfcd4844irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexThe RFC Series and RFC EditordtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5741∂habstractyfThis document introduces Overlay Routable Cryptographic Hash Identifiers (ORCHID) as a new, experimental class of IPv6-address- like identifiers. These identifiers are intended to be used as endpoint identifiers at applications and Application Programming Interfaces (API) and not as identifiers for network location at the IP layer, i.e., locators. They are designed to appear as application layer entities and at the existing IPv6 APIs, but they should not appear in actual IPv6 headers. To make them more like vanilla IPv6 addresses, they are expected to be routable at an overlay level. Consequently, while they are considered non-routable addresses from the IPv6 layer point-of-view, all existing IPv6 applications are expected to be able to use them in a manner compatible with current IPv6 addresses.

This document requests IANA to allocate a temporary prefix out of the IPv6 addressing space for Overlay Routable Cryptographic Hash Identifiers. By default, the prefix will be returned to IANA in 2014, with continued use requiring IETF consensus. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research Nomadic LabdnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnamekJ. Laganier¢kaffiliationeCELARdnameiF. DupontddatejÃ.hfilenamegrfc4843eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126755hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7343epagesopublicationDateF1T]grankingÎcrfcd4843irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKAn IPv6 Prefix for Overlay Routable Cryptographic Hash Identifiers (ORCHID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¡This document provides encapsulation formats and semantics for emulating Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) circuits and services over MPLS. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvVerizon CommunicationsdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationqOverture NetworksdnamegP. Pate¢kaffiliationqResolute NetworksdnamehR. Cohen¢kaffiliationqCorrigent SystemsdnamehD. Zeligddatej””hfilenamegrfc4842eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126754hkeywordsÅxmultiprotocol label switchingkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateFJgrankingÍcrfcd4842irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiSynchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Circuit Emulation over Packet (CEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document updates RFC 4181, "Guidelines for Authors and Reviewers of MIB Documents", to recognize the creation of the IETF Trust.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. Heardddatej”€hfilenamegrfc4841eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126753hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basexstandards-track specificationsjmib reviewkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF	|ÂgrankingÈcrfcd4841irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberoetotaletitlex+RFC 4181 Update to Recognize the IETF TrustdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx⁄This document describes architectural and operational issues that arise from link-layer protocols supporting multiple Internet Protocol encapsulation methods.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnameiD. Thalerddatej”–hfilenamegrfc4840eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126752hkeywordsÑciabslink-layer protocolpip encapsulationxinternet protocol encapsulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF[grankingËcrfcd4840irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex1Multiple Encapsulation Methods Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx©This document serves to register a media type for the Open eBook Publication Structure (OEBPS) Package Files.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxeBook Technologies, Inc.dnameiG. Conboy¢kaffiliationxeBook Technologies, Inc.dnameiJ. Rivlin¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelJ. FerraioloddatejÃ+hfilenamegrfc4839eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126751hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF-t€grankingÁcrfcd4839irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Media Type Registrations for the Open eBook Publication Structure (OEBPS) Package File (OPF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes an architecture for delay-tolerant and disruption-tolerant networks, and is an evolution of the architecture originally designed for the Interplanetary Internet, a communication system envisioned to provide Internet-like services across interplanetary distances in support of deep space exploration.  This document describes an architecture that addresses a variety of problems with internetworks having operational and performance characteristics that make conventional (Internet-like) networking approaches either unworkable or impractical.  We define a message- oriented overlay that exists above the transport (or other) layers of the networks it interconnects.  The document presents a motivation for the architecture, an architectural overview, review of state management required for its operation, and a discussion of application design issues.  This document represents the consensus of the IRTF DTN research group and has been widely reviewed by that group.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertgauthorsà¢kaffiliationx Google/Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamegV. Cerf¢kaffiliationxNASA/Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamekS. Burleigh¢kaffiliationxNASA/Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamehA. Hooke¢kaffiliationxNASA/Jet Propulsion LaboratorydnamelL. Torgerson¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamehR. Durst¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamehK. Scott¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamegK. Fall¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnamehH. Weissddatej”—hfilenamegrfc4838eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymedtnrgdfullx!dtnrg - Delay-Tolerant NetworkingdnamexDelay-Tolerant Networkingdtypebrgbidjdoc-126750hkeywordsÉsdisruption tolerantdirtfwinterplanetary internetkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateFmgrankingÊcrfcd4838irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlex&Delay-Tolerant Networking ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP based Internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing interfaces that conform to the Ethernet Passive Optical Networks (EPON) standard as defined in the IEEE Std 802.3ah-2004, which are extended capabilities to the Ethernet like interfaces. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjPMC-SIERRAdnamekL. Khermoshddatej”–hfilenamegrfc4837eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126749hkeywordsãx!Ethernet Passive Optical NetworkscpondeponkIEEE802.3ahg802.3ahdp2mpdmpcpdllidconucoltnoptical accesskobsoletedByÄepages[opublicationDateFî$&grankingÂcrfcd4837irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Managed Objects of Ethernet Passive Optical Networks (EPON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines objects for managing IEEE 802.3 Medium Attachment Units (MAUs).  This document obsoletes RFC 3636.  It amends that specification by moving MAU type OBJECT-IDENTITY definitions and relevant textual conventions into a separate Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) maintained MIB module.  In addition, management information is added to enable support for Ethernet in the First Mile (EFM) and 10GBASE-CX4 MAUs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpActelis NetworksdnamehE. Beiliddatej”Œhfilenamegrfc4836eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126748hkeywordsÉgMAU-MIBlIANA-MAU-MIBxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateF2újgranking‰crfcd4836irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDefinitions of Managed Objects for IEEE 802.3 Medium Attachment Units (MAUs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThe IPsec series of protocols makes use of various cryptographic algorithms in order to provide security services.  The Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and the Authentication Header (AH) provide two mechanisms for protecting data being sent over an IPsec Security Association (SA).  To ensure interoperability between disparate implementations, it is necessary to specify a set of mandatory-to-implement algorithms to ensure that there is at least one algorithm that all implementations will have available.  This document defines the current set of mandatory-to-implement algorithms for ESP and AH as well as specifying algorithms that should be implemented because they may be promoted to mandatory at some future time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpIP Infusion Inc.dnameiV. Manralddatej”’hfilenamegrfc4835eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126747hkeywordsçcESPeipsecnauthenticationimechanismfheaderhsecuritylarchitecturegpayloadhinternethprotocolmencapsulatingdipv4dipv6kobsoletedByÅd7321epages
opublicationDateF~granking„crfcd4835irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Cryptographic Algorithm Implementation Requirements for Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document presents a set of functional requirements for network solutions that allow the deployment of IP multicast within Layer 3 (L3) Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPNs).  It specifies requirements both from the end user and service provider standpoints.  It is intended that potential solutions specifying the support of IP multicast within such VPNs will use these requirements as guidelines.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnamehT. Morinddatej”“hfilenamegrfc4834eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126746hkeywordsÉcvpnxvirtual private networksbl3kobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateF%7kgranking‚crfcd4834irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Requirements for Multicast in Layer 3 Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔTwo common ways to communicate timezone information are POSIX 1003.1 timezone strings and timezone database names.  This memo specifies DHCP options for each of those methods.  The DHCPv4 time offset option is deprecated. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiP. Eggertddatej”…hfilenamegrfc4833eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126745hkeywordsÑktime offseteposixktz databasebtzkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateF#¡Cgranking·crfcd4833irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTimezone Options for DHCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document discusses security threats to network-based localized mobility management.  Threats may occur on two interfaces: the interface between a localized mobility anchor and a mobile access gateway, as well as the interface between a mobile access gateway and a mobile node.  Threats to the former interface impact the localized mobility management protocol itself.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversitaet Karlsruhe (TH)dnamegC. Vogt¢kaffiliationoDoCoMo USA LabsdnamehJ. Kempfddatej”Õhfilenamegrfc4832eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126744hkeywordsáxlocalized mobility anchorumobile access gatewayjcompromisemimpersonationqman in the middleqdenial of servicekIP spoofingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFågranking‡crfcd4832irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHSecurity Threats to Network-Based Localized Mobility Management (NETLMM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≠In this document, design goals for a network-based localized mobility management (NETLMM) protocol are discussed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoDoCoMo USA LabsdnamehJ. KempfddatejÃ$hfilenamegrfc4831eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126743hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFögrankingﬂcrfcd4831irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Goals for Network-Based Localized Mobility Management (NETLMM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysLocalized mobility management is a well-understood concept in the IETF, with a number of solutions already available.  This document looks at the principal shortcomings of the existing solutions, all of which involve the host in mobility management, and makes a case for network-based local mobility management.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoDoCoMo USA LabsdnamehJ. KempfddatejÃ#hfilenamegrfc4830eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnetlmmdfullx4netlmm - Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdnamex+Network-based Localized Mobility Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126742hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF¨grankingﬁcrfcd4830irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJProblem Statement for Network-Based Localized Mobility Management (NETLMM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyﬁWhen the establishment of a higher priority (Traffic Engineering Label Switched Path) TE LSP requires the preemption of a set of lower priority TE LSPs, a node has to make a local decision to select which TE LSPs will be preempted.  The preempted LSPs are then rerouted by their respective \%Head-end Label Switch Router (LSR).  This document presents a flexible policy that can be used to achieve different objectives: preempt the lowest priority LSPs; preempt the minimum number of LSPs; preempt the set of TE LSPs that provide the closest amount of bandwidth to the required bandwidth for the preempting TE LSPs (to minimize bandwidth wastage); preempt the LSPs that will have the maximum chance to get rerouted.  Simulation results are given and a comparison among several different policies, with respect to preemption cascading, number of preempted LSPs, priority, wasted bandwidth and blocking probability is also included.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss CallongauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqDrexel UniversitydnamenJ. de Oliveira¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationtVerizon LaboratoriesdnamegL. Chen¢kaffiliationwKansas State UniversitydnamejC. Scoglioddatej”Ahfilenamegrfc4829eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126741hkeywordsÉx'traffic engineering label switched pathfte lspx&multiprotocol label switching protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF-tKgranking›crfcd4829irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexJLabel Switched Path (LSP) Preemption Policies for MPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document proposes a mechanism for further experimentation, but not for widespread deployment at this time in the global Internet.

TCP-Friendly Rate Control (TFRC) is a congestion control mechanism for unicast flows operating in a best-effort Internet environment (RFC 3448). TFRC was intended for applications that use a fixed packet size, and was designed to be reasonably fair when competing for bandwidth with TCP connections using the same packet size. This document proposes TFRC-SP, a Small-Packet (SP) variant of TFRC, that is designed for applications that send small packets. The design goal for TFRC-SP is to achieve the same bandwidth in bps (bits per second) as a TCP flow using packets of up to 1500 bytes. TFRC-SP enforces a minimum interval of 10 ms between data packets to prevent a single flow from sending small packets arbitrarily frequently.

Flows using TFRC-SP compete reasonably fairly with large-packet TCP and TFRC flows in environments where large-packet flows and small-packet flows experience similar packet drop rates. However, in environments where small-packet flows experience lower packet drop rates than large-packet flows (e.g., with Drop-Tail queues in units of bytes), TFRC-SP can receive considerably more than its share of the bandwidth. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiE. Kohlerddatej”–hfilenamegrfc4828eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126740hkeywordsÅxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateF#¡1granking‹crfcd4828irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?TCP Friendly Rate Control (TFRC): The Small-Packet (SP) VariantdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡This document describes a usage of the Extensible Markup Language (XML) Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP) for manipulating the contents of Presence Information Data Format (PIDF) based presence documents.  It is intended to be used in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) based presence systems, where the Event State Compositor can use the XCAP-manipulated presence document as one of the inputs on which it builds the overall presence state for the presentity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejM. Isomaki¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekE. Leppanenddatej”Jhfilenamegrfc4827eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126739hkeywordsàdPIDFdAUIDjhard stategPUBLISHlSIP PresencefSIMPLEqpidf-manipulationvXCAP application usagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFS¶granking€crfcd4827irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzAn Extensible Markup Language (XML) Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP) Usage for Manipulating Presence Document ContentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀIn multimedia communications, presence, and instant messaging systems, there is a need to define Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) that represent services that are associated with a group of users.  One example is a resource list service.  If a user sends a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) SUBSCRIBE message to the URI representing the resource list service, the server will obtain the state of the users in the associated group, and provide it to the sender.  To facilitate definition of these services, this specification defines two Extensible Markup Language (XML) documents.  One document contains service URIs, along with their service definition and a reference to the associated group of users.  The second document contains the user lists that are referenced from the first.  This list of users can be utilized by other applications and services.  Both documents can be created and managed with the XML Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”Ghfilenamegrfc4826eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126738hkeywordsàdhttpcsipcxmldrestjbuddy listfsimplehpresenceqdata manipulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFSögranking⁄crfcd4826irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHExtensible Markup Language (XML) Formats for Representing Resource ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis specification defines the Extensible Markup Language (XML) Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP).  XCAP allows a client to read, write, and modify application configuration data stored in XML format on a server.  XCAP maps XML document sub-trees and element attributes to HTTP URIs, so that these components can be directly accessed by HTTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”œhfilenamegrfc4825eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126737hkeywordsàcsipcxmldhttpdrestjbuddy listfsimplehpresenceqdata manipulationkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateFSégrankingŸcrfcd4825irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIThe Extensible Markup Language (XML) Configuration Access Protocol (XCAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxøThis document specifies a method for encapsulating and transmitting IPv4/IPv6 packets over the Semaphore Flag Signal System (SFSS).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ. Hofmueller¢kaffiliation`dnamekA. Bachmann¢kaffiliation`dnamelIO. zmoelnigddatej”Êhfilenamegrfc4824eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43451hkeywordsÇqinternet protocolkapril foolskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateFèﬁgrankingÿcrfcd4824irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPThe Transmission of IP Datagrams over the Semaphore Flag Signaling System (SFSS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis Applicability Statement (AS) describes how to exchange structured business data securely using the File Transfer Protocol (FTP) for XML, Binary, Electronic Data Interchange (EDI - ANSI X12 or UN/EDIFACT), or other data used for business-to-business data interchange for which MIME packaging can be accomplished using standard MIME content types.  Authentication and data confidentiality are obtained by using Cryptographic Message Syntax (S/MIME) security body parts.  Authenticated acknowledgements employ multipart/signed replies to the original message.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAxwaydnamejT. Harding¢kaffiliationeAxwaydnamehR. Scottddatej”ûhfilenamegrfc4823eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfediintdfullx9ediint - Electronic Data Interchange-Internet Integrationdnamex0Electronic Data Interchange-Internet Integrationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126736hkeywordsÉwapplicability statementbastbusiness-to-businesskobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateF-t0granking◊crfcd4823irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQFTP Transport for Secure Peer-to-Peer Business Data Interchange over the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThis note describes a revision to the RIPv2 Cryptographic Authentication mechanism originally specified in RFC 2082.  This document obsoletes RFC 2082 and updates RFC 2453.  This document adds details of how the SHA family of hash algorithms can be used with RIPv2 Cryptographic Authentication, whereas the original document only specified the use of Keyed-MD5.  Also, this document clarifies a potential issue with an active attack on this mechanism and adds significant text to the Security Considerations section. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekR. Atkinson¢kaffiliation`dnamehM. Fantoddatej”Ãhfilenamegrfc4822eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126735hkeywordsÉhRIP2-MD5xRouting Information ProtocoljEncryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÊ`Hgranking÷crfcd4822irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"RIPv2 Cryptographic AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document describes a robust method for Path MTU Discovery (PMTUD) that relies on TCP or some other Packetization Layer to probe an Internet path with progressively larger packets.  This method is described as an extension to RFC 1191 and RFC 1981, which specify ICMP-based Path MTU Discovery for IP versions 4 and 6, respectively. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcPSCdnameiM. Mathis¢kaffiliationcPSCdnamejJ. Heffnerddatej”–hfilenamegrfc4821eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymepmtuddfullxpmtud - Path MTU DiscoverydnamerPath MTU Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-126734hkeywordsÇxmaximum transmission unitepmtudkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateF`ùgranking’crfcd4821irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Packetization Layer Path MTU DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8899∂habstracty@This document defines a padding chunk and a padding parameter and describes the required receiver side procedures.  The padding chunk is used to pad a Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) packet to an arbitrary size.  The padding parameter is used to pad an SCTP INIT chunk to an arbitrary size. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliation`dnamefP. LeiddatejÃ%hfilenamegrfc4820eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126733hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF`çgranking‘crfcd4820irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOPadding Chunk and Parameter for the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySecure Shell defines a user authentication mechanism that is based on public keys, but does not define any mechanism for key distribution. No common key management solution exists in current implementations. This document describes a protocol that can be used to configure public keys in an implementation-independent fashion, allowing client software to take on the burden of this configuration.

The Public Key Subsystem provides a server-independent mechanism for clients to add public keys, remove public keys, and list the current public keys known by the server. Rights to manage public keys are specific and limited to the authenticated user.

A public key may also be associated with various restrictions, including a mandatory command or subsystem. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpVanDyke SoftwarednamelJ. Galbraith¢kaffiliationpVanDyke SoftwarednamekJ. Van Dyke¢kaffiliationnSilicon CircusdnameiJ. Brightddatej”√hfilenamegrfc4819eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126732hkeywordsÇcsshdssh2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEıËbgranking”crfcd4819irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Secure Shell Public Key SubsystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9519∂habstractx‰This document defines a RADIUS (Remote Authentication Dial In User Service) attribute that carries an IPv6 prefix that is to be delegated to the user.  This attribute is usable within either RADIUS or Diameter. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehR. Dromsddatej”…hfilenamegrfc4818eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126731hkeywordsÇx*remote authentication dial in user servicehdiameterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF∂granking“crfcd4818irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&RADIUS Delegated-IPv6-Prefix AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThe Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol, Version 3 (L2TPv3) defines a protocol for tunneling a variety of payload types over IP networks.  This document defines how to carry an MPLS label stack and its payload over the L2TPv3 data encapsulation.  This enables an application that traditionally requires an MPLS-enabled core network, to utilize an L2TPv3 encapsulation over an IP network instead. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Townsley¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Wainner¢kaffiliationmSprint NexteldnamehT. Seely¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Youngddatej”Œhfilenamegrfc4817eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126730hkeywordsÉfl2tpv3x)multiprotocol label switching label stackklabel stackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF`xgranking—crfcd4817irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Encapsulation of MPLS over Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡The document describes a transparent cell transport service that makes use of the "N-to-one" cell relay mode for Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) Asynchronous Transfer-Mode (ATM) cell encapsulation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgVerizondnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamejJ. Brayley¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehT. WalshddatejÃ!hfilenamegrfc4816eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126729hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE’Ìkgranking–crfcd4816irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlPseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) Transparent Cell Transport ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûRFC 3095 defines the RObust Header Compression (ROHC) framework and profiles for IP (Internet Protocol), UDP (User Datagram Protocol), RTP (Real-Time Transport Protocol), and ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload).  Some parts of the specification are unclear or contain errors that may lead to misinterpretations that may impair interoperability between different implementations.  This document provides corrections, additions, and clarifications to RFC 3095; this document thus updates RFC 3095.  In addition, other clarifications related to RFC 3241 (ROHC over PPP), RFC 3843 (ROHC IP profile) and RFC 4109 (ROHC UDP-Lite profiles) are also provided. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliation`dnamekK. Sandlund¢kaffiliation`dnamelG. Pelletier¢kaffiliation`dnameiP. Kremerddatej”Œhfilenamegrfc4815eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126728hkeywordsábipcudpvuser datagram protocolcrtpxrealtime transport protocolcespxencapsulation security payloadkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateE’Ì^grankingœcrfcd4815irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLRObust Header Compression (ROHC): Corrections and Clarifications to RFC 3095dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNTest engineers take pains to declare all factors that affect a given measurement, including intended load, packet length, test duration, and traffic orientation.  However, current benchmarking practice overlooks two factors that have a profound impact on test results.  First, existing methodologies do not require the reporting of addresses or other test traffic contents, even though these fields can affect test results.  Second, "stuff" bits and bytes inserted in test traffic by some link-layer technologies add significant and variable overhead, which in turn affects test results.  This document describes the effects of these factors; recommends guidelines for test traffic contents; and offers formulas for determining the probability of bit- and byte-stuffing in test traffic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlNetwork TestdnameiD. Newman¢kaffiliationvSpirent CommunicationsdnameiT. Playerddatej”—hfilenamegrfc4814eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-126727hkeywordsÖdbmwglbenchmarkinggtestinglbit-stuffingmbyte-stuffingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF`jgrankingŒcrfcd4814irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDHash and Stuffing: Overlooked Factors in Network Device BenchmarkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOSPF is a link-state intra-domain routing protocol used in IP networks.  OSPF routers exchange information on a link using packets that follow a well-defined format.  The format of OSPF packets is not flexible enough to enable applications to exchange arbitrary data, which may be necessary in certain situations.  This memo describes a vendor-specific, backward-compatible technique to perform link-local signaling, i.e., exchange arbitrary data on a link.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekB. Friedman¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiL. Nguyen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Yeung¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehA. Zininddatej”Õhfilenamegrfc4813eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126726hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÅd5613epages
opublicationDateF␀ˆÈgrankingÕcrfcd4813irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSPF Link-Local SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&OSPF is a link-state intra-domain routing protocol used in IP networks. Routers find new and detect unreachable neighbors via the Hello subprotocol. Hello OSPF packets are also used to ensure two-way connectivity within time. When a router restarts its OSPF software, it may not know its neighbors. If such a router sends a Hello packet on an interface, its neighbors are going to reset the adjacency, which may not be desirable in certain conditions.

This memo describes a vendor-specific mechanism that allows OSPF routers to inform their neighbors about the restart process. Note that this mechanism requires support from neighboring routers. The mechanism described in this document was proposed before Graceful OSPF Restart, as described in RFC 3623, came into existence. It is implemented/supported by at least one major vendor and is currently deployed in the field. The purpose of this document is to capture the details of this mechanism for public use. This mechanism is not an IETF standard. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiL. Nguyen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehA. Zininddatej”Ãhfilenamegrfc4812eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126725hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF␀˜grankingÃcrfcd4812irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevOSPF Restart SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXOSPF is a link-state intra-domain routing protocol used in IP networks. Link State Database (LSDB) synchronization in OSPF is achieved via two methods -- initial LSDB synchronization when an OSPF router has just been connected to the network and asynchronous flooding that ensures continuous LSDB synchronization in the presence of topology changes after the initial procedure was completed. It may sometime be necessary for OSPF routers to resynchronize their LSDBs. The OSPF standard, however, does not allow routers to do so without actually changing the topology view of the network.

This memo describes a vendor-specific mechanism to perform such a form of out-of-band LSDB synchronization. The mechanism described in this document was proposed before Graceful OSPF Restart, as described in RFC 3623, came into existence. It is implemented/supported by at least one major vendor and is currently deployed in the field. The purpose of this document is to capture the details of this mechanism for public use. This mechanism is not an IETF standard. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiL. Nguyen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Roy¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamehA. Zininddatej”Ãhfilenamegrfc4811eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126724hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateF␀˜grankingÀcrfcd4811irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=OSPF Out-of-Band Link State Database (LSDB) ResynchronizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9454∂habstractypThere are many scenarios in which users must be able to prove the existence of data at a specific point in time and be able to demonstrate the integrity of data since that time, even when the duration from time of existence to time of demonstration spans a large period of time.  Additionally, users must be able to verify signatures on digitally signed data many years after the generation of the signature.  This document describes a class of long-term archive services to support such scenarios and the technical requirements for interacting with such services.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrCygnacom SolutionsdnamejC. Wallace¢kaffiliationwFraunhofer GesellschaftdnamekU. Pordesch¢kaffiliationuInterComponentWare AGdnamekR. Brandnerddatej”»hfilenamegrfc4810eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeltansdfullx-ltans - Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdnamex%Long-Term Archive and Notary Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126723hkeywordsÇndata integrityrdigital signatureskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateF`Xgranking crfcd4810irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Long-Term Archive Service RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`This informational document describes and identifies the requirements for transactions to handle Public Key Certificate (PKC) lifecycle transactions between Internet Protocol Security (IPsec) Virtual Private Network (VPN) Systems using Internet Key Exchange (IKE) (versions 1 and 2) and Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) Systems.  These requirements are designed to meet the needs of enterprise-scale IPsec VPN deployments.  It is intended that a standards track profile of a management protocol will be created to address many of these requirements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdIECAdnamejC. Bonatti¢kaffiliationdIECAdnameiS. Turner¢kaffiliationgJuniperdnamekG. Lebovitzddatej”Õhfilenamegrfc4809eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymipki4ipsecdfullx)pki4ipsec - Profiling Use of PKI in IPSECdnamexProfiling Use of PKI in IPSECdtypebwgbidjdoc-126722hkeywordsÅxinternet protocol securitykobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateEÊ_zgranking…crfcd4809irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Requirements for an IPsec Certificate Management ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThe TCP-MD5 option is most commonly used to secure BGP sessions between routers.  However, changing the long-term key is difficult, since the change needs to be synchronized between different organizations.  We describe single-ended strategies that will permit (mostly) unsynchronized key changes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamekS. Bellovinddatej”…hfilenamegrfc4808eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126721hkeywordsÇcbgpwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE˝àMgranking»crfcd4808irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Key Change Strategies for TCP-MD5dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document defines a Structure of Management Information Version 2 (SMIv2) Management Information Base (MIB) module for configuring the security policy database of a device implementing the IPsec protocol.  The policy-based packet filtering and the corresponding execution of actions described in this document are of a more general nature than for IPsec configuration alone, such as for configuration of a firewall.  This MIB module is designed to be extensible with other enterprise or standards-based defined packet filters and actions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlSparta, Inc.dnamegM. Baer¢kaffiliationdSelfdnamejR. Charlet¢kaffiliationlSparta, Inc.dnamekW. Hardaker¢kaffiliationsRevelstone SoftwarednamehR. Story¢kaffiliationcAROdnamegC. Wangddatej”Ãhfilenamegrfc4807eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126720hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basemIPSEC-SPD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesGopublicationDateEÙì?granking«crfcd4807irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0IPsec Security Policy Database Configuration MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêWhile the Internet Key Exchange Protocol version 2 (IKEv2) supports public key based authentication, the corresponding use of in-band Certificate Revocation Lists (CRL) is problematic due to unbounded CRL size. The size of an Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) response is however well-bounded and small. This document defines the "OCSP Content" extension to IKEv2. A CERTREQ payload with "OCSP Content" identifies zero or more trusted OCSP responders and is a request for inclusion of an OCSP response in the IKEv2 handshake. A cooperative recipient of such a request responds with a CERT payload containing the appropriate OCSP response. This content is recognizable via the same "OCSP Content" identifier.

When certificates are used with IKEv2, the communicating peers need a mechanism to determine the revocation status of the peer's certificate. OCSP is one such mechanism. This document applies when OCSP is desired and security policy prevents one of the IKEv2 peers from accessing the relevant OCSP responder directly. Firewalls are often deployed in a manner that prevents such access by IKEv2 peers outside of an enterprise network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwTraceRoute Security LLCdnamehM. Myers¢kaffiliationxSiemens Networks GmbH & Co KGdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej” hfilenamegrfc4806eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126719hkeywordsÅxinternet key exchange version 2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE„wagranking∆crfcd4806irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) Extensions to IKEv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. In particular, it describes objects used for managing DS1, J1, E1, DS2, and E2 interfaces. This document is a companion to the documents that define managed objects for the DS0, DS3/E3, and Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Interface Types.

This document obsoletes RFC 3895. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxRAD Data Communications, Ltd.dnamekO. Nicklassddatej” hfilenamegrfc4805eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126718hkeywordsÉcmibxmanagement information basegDS1-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDateEÙìVgranking≈crfcd4805irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexODefinitions of Managed Objects for the DS1, J1, E1, DS2, and E2 Interface TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸRFC 3175 specifies aggregation of Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) end-to-end reservations over aggregate RSVP reservations.  This document specifies aggregation of RSVP end-to-end reservations over MPLS Traffic Engineering (TE) tunnels or MPLS Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering (DS-TE) tunnels.  This approach is based on RFC 3175 and simply modifies the corresponding procedures for operations over MPLS TE tunnels instead of aggregate RSVP reservations.  This approach can be used to achieve admission control of a very large number of flows in a scalable manner since the devices in the core of the network are unaware of the end-to-end RSVP reservations and are only aware of the MPLS TE tunnels. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheurddatej”…hfilenamegrfc4804eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126717hkeywordsÉxmultiprotocol label switchingstraffic engineeringx'diffserv-aware mpls traffic engineeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE‹˝·grankingƒcrfcd4804irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Aggregation of Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) Reservations over MPLS TE/DS-TE TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects to configure and/or monitor a Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Label Switching Router (LSR). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej”©hfilenamegrfc4803eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126716hkeywordsÉcmibqGMPLS-LSR-STD-MIBsGMPLS-LABEL-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateEÊ_Ágranking√crfcd4803irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjGeneralized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Label Switching Router (LSR) Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS)-based traffic engineering. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej”æhfilenamegrfc4802eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126715hkeywordsÉcmibpGMPLS-TE-STD-MIBqIANA-GMPLS-TC-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateEÊ`granking¬crfcd4802irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaGeneralized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module that contains textual conventions (TCs) to represent commonly used Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) management information.  The intent is that these textual conventions will be imported and used in GMPLS-related MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej”øhfilenamegrfc4801eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126714hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basecmibpGMPLS-TC-STD-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateEÊ`/granking¡crfcd4801irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcDefinitions of Textual Conventions for Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¥This document explains how to interconnect IPv6 islands over a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS)-enabled IPv4 cloud.  This approach relies on IPv6 Provider Edge routers (6PE), which are Dual Stack in order to connect to IPv6 islands and to the MPLS core, which is only required to run IPv4 MPLS.  The 6PE routers exchange the IPv6 reachability information transparently over the core using the Multiprotocol Border Gateway Protocol (MP-BGP) over IPv4.  In doing so, the BGP Next Hop field is used to convey the IPv4 address of the 6PE router so that dynamically established IPv4-signaled MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs) can be used without explicit tunnel configuration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamelJ. De Clercq¢kaffiliationjOneSparrowdnamegD. Ooms¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejS. Prevost¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamenF. Le Faucheurddatej”Àhfilenamegrfc4798eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126713hkeywordsÅfmp-bgpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÀ“ägrankingæcrfcd4798irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMConnecting IPv6 Islands over IPv4 MPLS Using IPv6 Provider Edge Routers (6PE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyıThis document describes an implementation strategy for BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) in which the outermost MPLS label (i.e., the tunnel label) is replaced with either an IP header or an IP header with Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE).

The implementation strategy described herein enables the deployment of BGP/MPLS IP VPN technology in networks whose edge devices are MPLS and VPN aware, but whose interior devices are not. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej”«hfilenamegrfc4797eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126712hkeywordsÅwL3VPN GRE encapsulationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateE∏ªgrankingΩcrfcd4797irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUse of Provider Edge to Provider Edge (PE-PE) Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) or IP in BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡This document defines a new Session Description Protocol (SDP) media- level attribute, 'content'.  The 'content' attribute defines the content of the media stream to a more detailed level than the media description line.  The sender of an SDP session description can attach the 'content' attribute to one or more media streams.  The receiving application can then treat each media stream differently (e.g., show it on a big or small screen) based on its content. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemJ. Hautakorpi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”»hfilenamegrfc4796eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126711hkeywordsÇomedia attributegcontentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÀ“•grankingºcrfcd4796irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8The Session Description Protocol (SDP) Content AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThe goal of Link-Local Multicast Name Resolution (LLMNR) is to enable name resolution in scenarios in which conventional DNS name resolution is not possible.  LLMNR supports all current and future DNS formats, types, and classes, while operating on a separate port from DNS, and with a distinct resolver cache.  Since LLMNR only operates on the local link, it cannot be considered a substitute for DNS.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiL. EsibovddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4795eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126710hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE∏¡grankingªcrfcd4795irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Link-local Multicast Name Resolution (LLMNR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€RFC 1264 was written during what was effectively a completely different time in the life of the Internet.  It prescribed rules to protect the Internet against new routing protocols that may have various undesirable properties.  In today's Internet, there are so many other pressures against deploying unreasonable protocols that we believe that existing controls suffice, and the RFC 1264 rules just get in the way.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnameiB. FennerddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4794eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126709hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE}‘`granking∫crfcd4794irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletRFC 1264 Is ObsoletedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document describes a general Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) method suitable for use with One-Time Password (OTP) tokens, and offers particular advantages for tokens with direct electronic interfaces to their associated clients.  The method can be used to provide unilateral or mutual authentication, and key material, in protocols utilizing EAP, such as PPP, IEEE 802.1X, and Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlRSA SecuritydnamekM. Nystroemddatej”√hfilenamegrfc4793eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126708hkeywordsÇcotpx"extensible authentication protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDateEÀ“≥grankingπcrfcd4793irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7The EAP Protected One-Time Password Protocol (EAP-POTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAbstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) defines a general framework for annotating types in an ASN.1 specification with encoding instructions that alter how values of those types are encoded according to ASN.1 encoding rules.  This document defines the supporting notation for encoding instructions that apply to the Generic String Encoding Rules (GSER) and, in particular, defines an encoding instruction to provide a machine-processable representation for the declaration of a GSER ChoiceOfStrings type. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Leggddatej”»hfilenamegrfc4792eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126707hkeywordsÅeASN.1kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateE∏ granking∏crfcd4792irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBEncoding Instructions for the Generic String Encoding Rules (GSER)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document defines extensions to the Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) protocol to specify a standard way of accessing, managing, and sharing calendaring and scheduling information based on the iCalendar format.  This document defines the "calendar-access" feature of CalDAV. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeApplednamehC. Daboo¢kaffiliationfOraclednameoB. Desruisseaux¢kaffiliationkCommerceNetdnamelL. Dusseaultddatej”√hfilenamegrfc4791eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126706hkeywordsåhcalschedfcalschfcalcavhcalendarkcalendaringjschedulingfwebdavdicaliicalendarditipmtext/calendardhttpkobsoletedByÄepageskopublicationDateE˘øﬁgranking∑crfcd4791irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÜd7809d6638d8996d7953d5689d6764∂habstracty1Many Internet application protocols include string-based lookup, searching, or sorting operations.  However, the problem space for searching and sorting international strings is large, not fully explored, and is outside the area of expertise for the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).  Rather than attempt to solve such a large problem, this specification creates an abstraction framework so that application protocols can precisely identify a comparison function, and the repertoire of comparison functions can be extended in the future. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newman¢kaffiliationxAoyama Gakuin UniversitydnameiM. Duerst¢kaffiliationdOryxdnamenA. Gulbrandsenddatej”≈hfilenamegrfc4790eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126705hkeywordsÇicollationgsortingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE˘ø÷granking∂crfcd4790irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Internet Application Protocol Collation RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This document specifies how Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) messages can be transmitted directly over IEEE 802 networks.

This document obsoletes RFC 1089. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternational University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejT. JeffreeddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4789eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126704hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateEo~5grankingµcrfcd4789irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) over IEEE 802 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document updates the Enhanced Variable Rate Codec (EVRC) RTP payload formats defined in RFC 3558 with several enhancements and extensions.  In particular, it defines support for the header-free and interleaved/bundled packet formats for the EVRC-B codec, a new compact bundled format for the EVRC and EVRC-B codecs, as well as discontinuous transmission (DTX) support for EVRC and EVRC-B-encoded speech transported via RTP.  Voice over IP (VoIP) applications operating over low bandwidth dial-up and wireless networks require such enhancements for efficient use of the bandwidth. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnameiR. Kapoorddatej” hfilenamegrfc4788eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126703hkeywordsÖxenhanced variable rate codecxreal time transmission protocolfevrc-bcdtxxdiscontinuous transmissionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE•dÆgranking¥crfcd4788irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Enhancements to RTP Payload Formats for EVRC Family CodecsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5188∑habstracty>This document defines basic terminology for describing different types of Network Address Translation (NAT) behavior when handling Unicast UDP and also defines a set of requirements that would allow many applications, such as multimedia communications or online gaming, to work consistently.  Developing NATs that meet this set of requirements will greatly increase the likelihood that these applications will function properly.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehF. Audet¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jenningsddatej”ƒhfilenamegrfc4787eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfbehavedfullx5behave - Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancednamex,Behavior Engineering for Hindrance Avoidancedtypebwgbidjdoc-126702hkeywordsÉcnatcsipcudpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¡M»granking≥crfcd4787irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlexINetwork Address Translation (NAT) Behavioral Requirements for Unicast UDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7857d6888∑habstractyÅAs the Internet has grown, and as systems and networked services within enterprises have become more pervasive, many services with high availability requirements have emerged. These requirements have increased the demands on the reliability of the infrastructure on which those services rely.

Various techniques have been employed to increase the availability of services deployed on the Internet. This document presents commentary and recommendations for distribution of services using anycast. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAfilias CanadadnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationxNetnod Internet ExchangednamelK. Lindqvistddatej”≈hfilenamegrfc4786eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126701hkeywordsÉgROUTINGnLOAD-BALANCINGlLOAD-SHARINGkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEåTùgranking≤crfcd4786irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber~etotaletitlexOperation of Anycast ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document specifies authentication-only ciphersuites (with no encryption) for the Pre-Shared Key (PSK) based Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol.  These ciphersuites are useful when authentication and integrity protection is desired, but confidentiality is not needed or not permitted. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamemU. Blumenthal¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamegP. Goelddatej”»hfilenamegrfc4785eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126700hkeywordsÅlcipher suitekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¡M∫granking±crfcd4785irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYPre-Shared Key (PSK) Ciphersuites with NULL Encryption for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996µhabstractyáThe Verizon Wireless Dynamic Mobile IP Key Update procedure is a mechanism for distributing and updating Mobile IP (MIP) cryptographic keys in cdma2000(R) networks (including High Rate Packet Data, which is often referred to as 1xEV-DO). The Dynamic Mobile IP Key Update (DMU) procedure occurs between the MIP Mobile Node (MN) and RADIUS Authentication, Authorization and Accounting (AAA) Server via a cdma2000(R) Packet Data Serving Node (PDSN) that is acting as a Mobile IP Foreign Agent (FA).

cdma2000(R) is a registered trademark of the Telecommunications Industry Association (TIA). This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpRopes & Gray LLPdnamejC. Carroll¢kaffiliationmQualcomm Inc.dnamehF. Quickddatej—@hfilenamegrfc4784eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126699hkeywordsÉcmiprcryptographic keyscdmukobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateFy∆granking∞crfcd4784irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexFVerizon Wireless Dynamic Mobile IP Key Update for cdma2000(R) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an extension to Generalized MPLS (Multi-Protocol Label Switching) signaling to support communication of alarm information. GMPLS signaling already supports the control of alarm reporting, but not the communication of alarm information. This document presents both a functional description and GMPLS-RSVP specifics of such an extension. This document also proposes modification of the RSVP ERROR_SPEC object.

This document updates RFC 3473, "Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Resource ReserVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Extensions", through the addition of new, optional protocol elements. It does not change, and is fully backward compatible with, the procedures specified in RFC 3473. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdLabNdnameiL. Bergerddatej”¡hfilenamegrfc4783eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126698hkeywordsÇx)generalized multiprotocol label switchingjgmpls-rsvpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÄEîgrankingØcrfcd4783irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*GMPLS - Communication of Alarm InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document specifies an optional Quick-Start mechanism for transport protocols, in cooperation with routers, to determine an allowed sending rate at the start and, at times, in the middle of a data transfer (e.g., after an idle period). While Quick-Start is designed to be used by a range of transport protocols, in this document we only specify its use with TCP. Quick-Start is designed to allow connections to use higher sending rates when there is significant unused bandwidth along the path, and the sender and all of the routers along the path approve the Quick-Start Request.

This document describes many paths where Quick-Start Requests would not be approved. These paths include all paths containing routers, IP tunnels, MPLS paths, and the like that do not support Quick- Start. These paths also include paths with routers or middleboxes that drop packets containing IP options. Quick-Start Requests could be difficult to approve over paths that include multi-access layer- two networks. This document also describes environments where the Quick-Start process could fail with false positives, with the sender incorrectly assuming that the Quick-Start Request had been approved by all of the routers along the path. As a result of these concerns, and as a result of the difficulties and seeming absence of motivation for routers, such as core routers to deploy Quick-Start, Quick-Start is being proposed as a mechanism that could be of use in controlled environments, and not as a mechanism that would be intended or appropriate for ubiquitous deployment in the global Internet. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationdICIRdnameiM. Allman¢kaffiliationkF5 NetworksdnamegA. Jain¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamelP. SarolahtiddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4782eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126697hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDateEæïËgrankingÆcrfcd4782irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQuick-Start for TCP and IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIA mechanism for BGP that helps minimize the negative effects on routing caused by BGP restart has already been developed and is described in a separate document ("Graceful Restart Mechanism for BGP"). This document extends this mechanism to minimize the negative effects on MPLS forwarding caused by the Label Switching Router's (LSR's) control plane restart, and specifically by the restart of its BGP component when BGP is used to carry MPLS labels and the LSR is capable of preserving the MPLS forwarding state across the restart.

The mechanism described in this document is agnostic with respect to the types of the addresses carried in the BGP Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) field. As such, it works in conjunction with any of the address families that could be carried in BGP (e.g., IPv4, IPv6, etc.). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwalddatej”whfilenamegrfc4781eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126696hkeywordsÑwborder gateway protocolxmultiprotocol label switchingdnlrix*bgp network layer reachability informationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateEØøægranking≠crfcd4781irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Graceful Restart Mechanism for BGP with MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes a set of managed objects that are used to manage Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) entities, which include User Agents, and Proxy, Redirect and Registrar servers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiK. Lingle¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiJ-F. Mule¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Maeng¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Walkerddatej”¿hfilenamegrfc4780eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126695hkeywordsÜcmibrregistrar servicesnSIP-COMMON-MIBjSIP-TC-MIBvSIP-UA-MIB DEFINITIONSnSIP-SERVER-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesSopublicationDateF6∫˚granking¨crfcd4780irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEManagement Information Base for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty⁄This document provides a detailed description of IPv6 deployment and integration methods and scenarios in today\'s Service Provider (SP) Broadband (BB) networks in coexistence with deployed IPv4 services.  Cable/HFC, BB Ethernet, xDSL, and WLAN are the main BB technologies that are currently deployed, and discussed in this document.  The emerging Broadband Power Line Communications (PLC/BPL) access technology is also discussed for completeness.  In this document we will discuss main components of IPv6 BB networks, their differences from IPv4 BB networks, and how IPv6 is deployed and integrated in each of these networks using tunneling mechanisms and native IPv6.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelS. Asadullah¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Ahmed¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Popoviciu¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationkConsulinteldnamehJ. Paletddatej”«hfilenamegrfc4779eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126694hkeywordsáev6opscispdipv6jdeploymentiscenariosibroadbandhnetworkskobsoletedByÄepagesQopublicationDateEµlsgranking´crfcd4779irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:ISP IPv6 Deployment Scenarios in Broadband Access NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document is a survey of the current practices used in today's large ISP operational networks to secure layer 2 and layer 3 infrastructure devices.  The information listed here is the result of information gathered from people directly responsible for defining and implementing secure infrastructures in Internet Service Provider environments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxDouble Shot Security, Inc.dnamegM. Kaeoddatej”¥hfilenamegrfc4778eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeopsecdfullxGopsec - Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructurednamex?Operational Security Capabilities for IP Network Infrastructuredtypebwgbidjdoc-126693hkeywordsÅcispkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateE¡Mµgranking™crfcd4778irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPOperational Security Current Practices in Internet Service Provider EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyOThis document describes the interface to the Telnet server on IBM's iSeries line of midrange business computers. This interface allows Telnet clients to request a Telnet terminal or printer session using specific session attributes related to device names, encryption, language support, auto-sign-on, response codes, session association, etc.

These support functions are implemented primarily using the Telnet Environment option negotiation RFC 1572 to define new USERVAR variables that will be recognized by iSeries Telnet server. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamemT. Murphy Jr.¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehP. Rieth¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejJ. Stevensddatej”©hfilenamegrfc4777eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126692hkeywordsÖxmidrange business computerrtelnet environmentfclientfservergprinterkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateEn4rgranking©crfcd4777irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex!IBM's iSeries Telnet EnhancementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÍThis document specifies a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4 and DHCPv6) option containing the civic location of the client or the DHCP server.  The Location Configuration Information (LCI) includes information about the country, administrative units such as states, provinces, and cities, as well as street addresses, postal community names, and building information.  The option allows multiple renditions of the same address in different scripts and languages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”Õhfilenamegrfc4776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbididoc-43448hkeywordsÇclcixlocal configuration informationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE^j\granking®crfcd4776irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4 and DHCPv6) Option for Civic Addresses Configuration InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5774d6848∑habstractyWThis document discusses procedural issues related to the extensibility of IETF protocols, including when it is reasonable to extend IETF protocols with little or no review, and when extensions or variations need to be reviewed by the IETF community. Experience has shown that extension of protocols without early IETF review can carry risk. The document also recommends that major extensions to or variations of IETF protocols only take place through normal IETF processes or in coordination with the IETF.

This document is directed principally at other Standards Development Organizations (SDOs) and vendors considering requirements for extensions to IETF protocols. It does not modify formal IETF processes. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgHarvarddnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Nartenddatej”≈hfilenamegrfc4775eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126691hkeywordsÇcsdox"standards development organizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÄE'grankingßcrfcd4775irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber}etotaletitlex1Procedures for Protocol Extensions and VariationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyäThere have been a number of proposals for alternate semantics for the Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) field in the IP header RFC 3168.  This document discusses some of the issues in defining alternate semantics for the ECN field, and specifies requirements for a safe coexistence in an Internet that could include routers that do not understand the defined alternate semantics.  This document evolved as a result of discussions with the authors of one recent proposal for such alternate semantics.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. FloydddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126690hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateElø”granking¶crfcd4774irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber|etotaletitlexSSpecifying Alternate Semantics for the Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) FielddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6040∂habstractxØThis is a direction to IANA concerning the management of the IANA Special Purpose IPv6 address assignment registry.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. HustonddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4773eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126689hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd6890epagesopublicationDateEåTìgranking•crfcd4773irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Administration of the IANA Special Purpose IPv6 Address BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°The Data Encryption Standard (DES) is susceptible to brute-force attacks, which are well within the reach of a modestly financed adversary.  As a result, DES has been deprecated, and replaced by the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES).  Nonetheless, many applications continue to rely on DES for security, and designers and implementers continue to support it in new applications.  While this is not always inappropriate, it frequently is.  This note discusses DES security implications in detail, so that designers and implementers have all the information they need to make judicious decisions regarding its use.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnAruba NetworksdnamehS. KellyddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4772eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126688hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEåTôgranking§crfcd4772irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASecurity Implications of Using the Data Encryption Standard (DES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines an integrity transform for Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP; see RFC 3711), which allows the roll-over counter (ROC) to be transmitted in SRTP packets as part of the authentication tag.  The need for sending the ROC in SRTP packets arises in situations where the receiver joins an ongoing SRTP session and needs to quickly and robustly synchronize.  The mechanism also enhances SRTP operation in cases where there is a risk of losing sender-receiver synchronization. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemV. Lehtovirta¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejM. Naslund¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejK. Norrmanddatej”ƒhfilenamegrfc4771eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126687hkeywordsÅcrockobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEØøÃgranking£crfcd4771irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaIntegrity Transform Carrying Roll-Over Counter for the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThis document describes an extension to vCard to support Instant Messaging (IM) and Presence Protocol (PP) applications.  IM and PP are becoming increasingly common ways of communicating, and users want to save this contact information in their address books.  It allows a URI that is associated with IM or PP to be specified inside a vCard. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej”ƒhfilenamegrfc4770eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126686hkeywordsÇdimppx'instant messaging and presence protocolkobsoletedByÅd6350epagesopublicationDateE•c¶granking¢crfcd4770irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+vCard Extensions for Instant Messaging (IM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document registers the Enumservice type "pstn" and subtype "tel" using the URI scheme 'tel', as well as the subtype "sip" using the URI scheme 'sip' as per the IANA registration process defined in the ENUM specification, RFC 3761.  This Enumservice is used to facilitate the routing of telephone calls in those countries where number portability exists. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxComcast Cable CommunicationsdnamelJ. Livingood¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamejR. Shockeyddatej”¡hfilenamegrfc4769eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126685hkeywordsÑctelcurijuri schemecsipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEo~Hgranking°crfcd4769irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnIANA Registration for an Enumservice Containing Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) Signaling InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstracty¬The Generic Security Services API (GSS-API) provides a naming architecture that supports name-based authorization.  GSS-API authenticates two named parties to each other.  Names can be stored on access control lists (ACLs) to make authorization decisions.  Advances in security mechanisms and the way implementers wish to use GSS-API require this model to be extended for the next version of GSS-API.  As people move within an organization or change their names, the name authenticated by GSS-API may change.  Using some sort of constant identifier would make ACLs more stable.  Some mechanisms, such as public-key mechanisms, do not have a single name to be used across all environments.  Other mechanisms, such as Kerberos, may include group membership or role information as part of authentication.  This document motivates extensions to GSS-API naming and describes the extensions under discussion.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejS. Hartmanddatej”¡hfilenamegrfc4768eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126684hkeywordsÇcaclsaccess control listkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEã•granking†crfcd4768irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjDesired Enhancements to Generic Security Services Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Version 3 NamingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis memo describes the Intrusion Detection Exchange Protocol (IDXP), an application-level protocol for exchanging data between intrusion detection entities.  IDXP supports mutual-authentication, integrity, and confidentiality over a connection-oriented protocol.  The protocol provides for the exchange of IDMEF messages, unstructured text, and binary data.  The IDMEF message elements are described in RFC 4765, "The Intrusion Detection Message Exchange Format (IDMEF)", a companion document of the Intrusion Detection Exchange Format Working Group (IDWG) of the IETF.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqSecureWorks, Inc.dnamelB. Feinstein¢kaffiliationxCSC/NASA Ames Research CenterdnamekG. Matthewsddatej—Yhfilenamegrfc4767eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdidwgdfullx*idwg - Intrusion Detection Exchange Formatdnamex#Intrusion Detection Exchange Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-126683hkeywordsâiintrusionsintrusion detectiondbeephsecuritycidsqsecurity protocolosecure protocolosecure exchangeeidmefkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÙìIgrankingücrfcd4767irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0The Intrusion Detection Exchange Protocol (IDXP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(The purpose of the Intrusion Detection Exchange Format Working Group (IDWG) is to define data formats and exchange procedures for sharing information of interest to intrusion detection and response systems and to the management systems that may need to interact with them.  This document describes the high-level requirements for such a communication mechanism, including the rationale for those requirements where clarification is needed.  Scenarios are used to illustrate some requirements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternet Security Systems, Inc.dnamegM. Wood¢kaffiliationsHarvey Mudd CollegednamekM. Erlingerddatej—Ghfilenamegrfc4766eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdidwgdfullx*idwg - Intrusion Detection Exchange Formatdnamex#Intrusion Detection Exchange Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-126682hkeywordsÉeidmefdidwgx#intrusion detection exchange formatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÙìCgrankingûcrfcd4766irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Intrusion Detection Message Exchange RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThe purpose of the Intrusion Detection Message Exchange Format (IDMEF) is to define data formats and exchange procedures for sharing information of interest to intrusion detection and response systems and to the management systems that may need to interact with them.

This document describes a data model to represent information exported by intrusion detection systems and explains the rationale for using this model. An implementation of the data model in the Extensible Markup Language (XML) is presented, an XML Document Type Definition is developed, and examples are provided. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamehH. Debar¢kaffiliationhGuardiandnamehD. Curry¢kaffiliationqSecureWorks, Inc.dnamelB. Feinsteinddatej”§hfilenamegrfc4765eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdidwgdfullx*idwg - Intrusion Detection Exchange Formatdnamex#Intrusion Detection Exchange Formatdtypebwgbidjdoc-126681hkeywordsásintrusion detectionhsecurityfsecurehexchangeiintrusioncIDScXMLkobsoletedByÄepagesùopublicationDateEÙìNgrankingùcrfcd4765irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7The Intrusion Detection Message Exchange Format (IDMEF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyoThis document specifies EAP-PSK, an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) method for mutual authentication and session key derivation using a Pre-Shared Key (PSK).  EAP-PSK provides a protected communication channel when mutual authentication is successful for both parties to communicate over.  This document describes the use of this channel only for protected exchange of result indications, but future EAP-PSK extensions may use the channel for other purposes.  EAP-PSK is designed for authentication over insecure networks such as IEEE 802.11.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnamejF. Bersani¢kaffiliationxSiemens Networks GmbH & Co KGdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”Øhfilenamegrfc4764eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126680hkeywordsÅnpre-shared keykobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDateEØøπgrankingúcrfcd4764irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexVThe EAP-PSK Protocol: A Pre-Shared Key Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyêThis document specifies an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) mechanism for Shared-secret Authentication and Key Establishment (SAKE).  This RFC is published as documentation for the IANA assignment of an EAP Type for a vendor's EAP method per RFC 3748.  The specification has passed Designated Expert review for this IANA assignment.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari ArkkogauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Flarion TechnologiesdnamemM. Vanderveen¢kaffiliationxQualcomm Flarion TechnologiesdnamejH. Solimanddatej”≤hfilenamegrfc4763eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126679hkeywordsÇlIEEE 802.11inuser anonymitykobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateEn4ngrankingõcrfcd4763irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexkExtensible Authentication Protocol Method for Shared-secret Authentication and Key Establishment (EAP-SAKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document describes a Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) solution using pseudowires, a service previously implemented over other tunneling technologies and known as Transparent LAN Services (TLS). A VPLS creates an emulated LAN segment for a given set of users; i.e., it creates a Layer 2 broadcast domain that is fully capable of learning and forwarding on Ethernet MAC addresses and that is closed to a given set of users. Multiple VPLS services can be supported from a single Provider Edge (PE) node.

This document describes the control plane functions of signaling pseudowire labels using Label Distribution Protocol (LDP), extending RFC 4447. It is agnostic to discovery protocols. The data plane functions of forwarding are also described, focusing in particular on the learning of MAC addresses. The encapsulation of VPLS packets is described by RFC 4448. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekM. Lasserre¢kaffiliationnAlcatel-LucentdnamekV. Kompelladdatej”µhfilenamegrfc4762eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126678hkeywordsÇqland area networkwtransparent lan servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE≠r®grankingöcrfcd4762irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTVirtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Using Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBVirtual Private LAN Service (VPLS), also known as Transparent LAN Service and Virtual Private Switched Network service, is a useful Service Provider offering. The service offers a Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (VPN); however, in the case of VPLS, the customers in the VPN are connected by a multipoint Ethernet LAN, in contrast to the usual Layer 2 VPNs, which are point-to-point in nature.

This document describes the functions required to offer VPLS, a mechanism for signaling a VPLS, and rules for forwarding VPLS frames across a packet switched network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej”∂hfilenamegrfc4761eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126677hkeywordsÑwborder gateway protocolwtransparent lan servicex virtual private switched networkpservice providerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE≠rügrankingôcrfcd4761irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMVirtual Private LAN Service (VPLS) Using BGP for Auto-Discovery and SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8614d8395d5462∂habstracty>This document defines extensions to BGP-4 to enable it to carry routing information for multiple Network Layer protocols (e.g., IPv6, IPX, L3VPN, etc.).  The extensions are backward compatible - a router that supports the extensions can interoperate with a router that doesn't support the extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Bates¢kaffiliationmSonoa SystemsdnamejR. Chandra¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej”±hfilenamegrfc4760eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126676hkeywordsÉiMEXT-BGP4wborder gateway protocolwnetwork layer protocolskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE≠rôgrankingòcrfcd4760irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Multiprotocol Extensions for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7606∂habstractyWThis document defines a new parameter "enumdi" for the "tel" Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) to support the handling of ENUM queries in Voice over Internet Protocol (VoIP) network elements.  A VoIP network element may receive a URI containing an E.164 number, where that URI contains an "enumdi" parameter.  The presence of the "enumdi" parameter indicates that an ENUM query has already been performed on the E.164 number by a previous VoIP network element.  Equally, if a VoIP network element sends such a URI, it asserts that an ENUM query has been carried out on this number. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeOefegdnamejR. Stastny¢kaffiliationlNeustar Inc.dnamejR. Shockey¢kaffiliationsRoke Manor ResearchdnameiL. Conroyddatej”¬hfilenamegrfc4759eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-126675hkeywordsÉcDNSeE.164ptelephone numberkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE}‘\grankingócrfcd4759irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2The ENUM Dip Indicator Parameter for the "tel" URIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document constitutes Revision 1 of Cryptographic Token Key Initialization Protocol (CT-KIP) Version 1.0 from RSA Laboratories' One-Time Password Specifications (OTPS) series. The body of this document, except for the intellectual property considerations section, is taken from the CT-KIP Version 1.0 document, but comments received during the IETF review are reflected; hence, the status of a revised version. As no "bits-on-the-wire" have changed, the protocol specified herein is compatible with CT-KIP Version 1.0.

CT-KIP is a client-server protocol for initialization (and configuration) of cryptographic tokens. The protocol requires neither private-key capabilities in the cryptographic tokens, nor an established public-key infrastructure. Provisioned (or generated) secrets will only be available to the server and the cryptographic token itself. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlRSA SecuritydnamekM. Nystroemddatej”¿hfilenamegrfc4758eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126674hkeywordsÉprsa laboratoriesx one-time password specificationsdotpskobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateEo~Ngrankingñcrfcd4758irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOCryptographic Token Key Initialization Protocol (CT-KIP) Version 1.0 Revision 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThe Microsoft Windows 2000 implementation of Kerberos introduces a new encryption type based on the RC4 encryption algorithm and using an MD5 HMAC for checksum. This is offered as an alternative to using the existing DES-based encryption types.

The RC4-HMAC encryption types are used to ease upgrade of existing Windows NT environments, provide strong cryptography (128-bit key lengths), and provide exportable (meet United States government export restriction requirements) encryption. This document describes the implementation of those encryption types. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemK. Jaganathan¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiJ. Brezakddatej”¡hfilenamegrfc4757eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126673hkeywordsÅhmd5 hmackobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE}‘ggrankingïcrfcd4757irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@The RC4-HMAC Kerberos Encryption Types Used by Microsoft WindowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6649∂habstractywThis document defines the semantics that allow for grouping of Forward Error Correction (FEC) streams with the protected payload streams in Session Description Protocol (SDP).  The semantics defined in this document are to be used with "Grouping of Media Lines in the Session Description Protocol" (RFC 3388) to group together "m" lines in the same session. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjHyervisiondnameeA. Liddatej”¬hfilenamegrfc4756eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126672hkeywordsÉcfeccsdpkmedia lineskobsoletedByÅd5956epagesopublicationDateElø»grankingîcrfcd4756irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKForward Error Correction Grouping Semantics in Session Description ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxäThis document specifies transmission of IPv4/IPv6 packets and address resolution over the connected modes of InfiniBand. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejV. KashyapddatejÀhfilenamegrfc4755eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipoibdfullxipoib - IP over InfiniBanddnamerIP over InfiniBanddtypebwgbidjdoc-126671hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEãügrankingìcrfcd4755irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"IP over InfiniBand: Connected ModedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document describes how the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) may be used as the authentication method within the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) and Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocols.  ECDSA may provide benefits including computational efficiency, small signature sizes, and minimal bandwidth compared to other available digital signature methods.  This document adds ECDSA capability to IKE and IKEv2 without introducing any changes to existing IKE operation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnameeD. Fu¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejJ. Solinasddatej”ºhfilenamegrfc4754eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126670hkeywordsÅgsuite bkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEØø«grankingícrfcd4754irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYIKE and IKEv2 Authentication Using the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThis document describes new Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) groups for use in the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) and Internet Key Exchange version 2 (IKEv2) protocols in addition to previously defined groups.  Specifically, the new curve groups are based on modular arithmetic rather than binary arithmetic.  These new groups are defined to align IKE and IKEv2 with other ECC implementations and standards, particularly NIST standards.  In addition, the curves defined here can provide more efficient implementation than previously defined ECC groups.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNSAdnameeD. Fu¢kaffiliationcNSAdnamejJ. Solinasddatej”∞hfilenamegrfc4753eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126669hkeywordsÑnelliptic curvenDiffie-Hellmangsuite bjnist curvekobsoletedByÅd5903epagesopublicationDateEØø√grankingëcrfcd4753irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexECP Groups For IKE and IKEv2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThe Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) is a framework for adding authentication support to connection-based protocols. This document describes the method for using the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Kerberos V5 in the SASL.

This document replaces Section 7.2 of RFC 2222, the definition of the "GSSAPI" SASL mechanism. This document, together with RFC 4422, obsoletes RFC 2222. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeIsodednamekA. Melnikovddatej”¬hfilenamegrfc4752eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsasldfullx/sasl - Simple Authentication and Security Layerdnamex(Simple Authentication and Security Layerdtypebwgbidjdoc-126668hkeywordsÑdSASLjencryptionhprotocolhspecifickobsoletedByÄepages
