tocol (EAP) method called EAP-PAX (Password Authenticated eXchange).  This method is a lightweight shared-key authentication protocol with optional support for key provisioning, key management, identity protection, and authenticated data exchange.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcLTSdnameiT. Clancy¢kaffiliationcUMDdnamejW. Arbaughddatej”Ωhfilenamegrfc4746eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126663hkeywordsÉgEAP-PAXxpassword authenticated exchangelkey exchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEløëgrankingäcrfcd4746irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Password Authenticated ExchangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document defines a framework for authorization policies controlling access to application-specific data.  This framework combines common location- and presence-specific authorization aspects.  An XML schema specifies the language in which common policy rules are represented.  The common policy framework can be extended to other application domains. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationxSiemens Networks GmbH & Co KGdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationcCDTdnameiJ. Morris¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamejJ. Cuellar¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”øhfilenamegrfc4745eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-126662hkeywordsÉerulesjconditionskpermissionskobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateE’ÌJgrankingâcrfcd4745irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCCommon Policy: A Document Format for Expressing Privacy PreferencesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxØThis document specifies an application protocol mapping for the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) over the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliation`dnamejK. Crozierddatej”ßhfilenamegrfc4744eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126661hkeywordsÑcXMLmConfigurationrNetwork ManagementxExtensible Markup LanguagekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateEáA«grankingàcrfcd4744irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNUsing the NETCONF Protocol over the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996µhabstractyThe Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) is applicable to a wide range of devices in a variety of environments.  Web Services is one such environment and is presently characterized by the use of the Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP).  NETCONF finds many benefits in this environment: from the reuse of existing standards, to ease of software development, to integration with deployed systems.  Herein, we describe SOAP over HTTP and SOAP over Blocks Exchange Extensible Protocol (BEEP) bindings for NETCONF. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxICEsoft Technologies Inc.dnamejT. Goddardddatej”•hfilenamegrfc4743eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126660hkeywordsÜgNETCONFgXMLCONFdSOAPpdevice managmentcXMLxExtensible Markup LanguagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEáAµgrankingácrfcd4743irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Using NETCONF over the Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractx±This document describes a method for invoking and running the Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) within a Secure Shell (SSH) session as an SSH subsystem. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjThingMagicdnamelM. Wasserman¢kaffiliationxICEsoft Technologies, Inc.dnamejT. Goddardddatej”§hfilenamegrfc4742eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126659hkeywordsÅxnetwork configuration protocolkobsoletedByÅd6242epages
opublicationDateEáA°grankingÜcrfcd4742irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Using the NETCONF Configuration Protocol over Secure SHell (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThe Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF) defined in this document provides mechanisms to install, manipulate, and delete the configuration of network devices.  It uses an Extensible Markup Language (XML)-based data encoding for the configuration data as well as the protocol messages.  The NETCONF protocol operations are realized on top of a simple Remote Procedure Call (RPC) layer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegR. Ennsddatej”•hfilenamegrfc4741eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnetconfdfullxnetconf - Network ConfigurationdnameuNetwork Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126658hkeywordsÜxnetwork configuration protocolnnetwork devicecxmlxextensible markup languagecrpcuremote procedure callkobsoletedByÅd6241epages_opublicationDateEáAâgrankingÖcrfcd4741irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNETCONF Configuration ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document specifies the Diameter Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) application.  This is a Diameter application that allows a Diameter client to request authentication and authorization information.  This application is designed to be used in conjunction with SIP and provides a Diameter client co-located with a SIP server, with the ability to request the authentication of users and authorization of SIP resources usage from a Diameter server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelM. Belinchon¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameqM. Pallares-Lopez¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameuC. Canales-Valenzuela¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehK. Tammiddatej”≤hfilenamegrfc4740eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaaadfullx2aaa - Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdnamex,Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126657hkeywordsÉnauthenticationmauthorizationodiameter clientkobsoletedByÄepagesHopublicationDateEløúgrankingÑcrfcd4740irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Diameter Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThe Internet Key Exchange (IKEv2) protocol supports several mechanisms for authenticating the parties, including signatures with public-key certificates, shared secrets, and Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) methods.  Currently, each endpoint uses only one of these mechanisms to authenticate itself.  This document specifies an extension to IKEv2 that allows the use of multiple authentication exchanges, using either different mechanisms or the same mechanism.  This extension allows, for instance, performing certificate-based authentication of the client host followed by an EAP authentication of the user.  When backend authentication servers are used, they can belong to different administrative domains, such as the network access provider and the service provider.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamekJ. KorhonenddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4739eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126656hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEløÑgrankingÉcrfcd4739irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOMultiple Authentication Exchanges in the Internet Key Exchange (IKEv2) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~The Multimedia Internet Keying (MIKEY) specification describes several modes of key distribution solution that address multimedia scenarios (e.g., SIP calls and Real Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP) sessions) using pre-shared keys, public keys, and optionally a Diffie-Hellman key exchange.  In the public-key mode, the Initiator encrypts a random key with the Responder's public key and sends it to the Responder.  In many communication scenarios, the Initiator may not know the Responder's public key, or in some cases the Responder's ID (e.g., call forwarding) in advance.  We propose a new MIKEY mode that works well in such scenarios.  This mode also enhances the group key management support in MIKEY; it supports member-initiated group key download (in contrast to group manager pushing the group keys to all members).  This document updates RFC 3830 with the RSA-R mode. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamekD. Ignjatic¢kaffiliationhQUALCOMMdnamejL. Dondeti¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamehF. Audet¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamefP. Linddatej”øhfilenamegrfc4738eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126655hkeywordsÖeMIKEYdSRTPnkey managementvgroup key distributioneRSA-RkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE]xgrankingÇcrfcd4738irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYMIKEY-RSA-R: An Additional Mode of Key Distribution in Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis memo defines metrics to evaluate whether a network has maintained packet order on a packet-by-packet basis.  It provides motivations for the new metrics and discusses the measurement issues, including the context information required for all metrics.  The memo first defines a reordered singleton, and then uses it as the basis for sample metrics to quantify the extent of reordering in several useful dimensions for network characterization or receiver design.  Additional metrics quantify the frequency of reordering and the distance between separate occurrences.  We then define a metric oriented toward assessment of reordering effects on TCP.  Several examples of evaluation using the various sample metrics are included.  An appendix gives extended definitions for evaluating order with packet fragmentation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Morton¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamemL. Ciavattone¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameoG. Ramachandran¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamekS. Shalunov¢kaffiliationhVeriwavednameiJ. Perserddatej”¨hfilenamegrfc4737eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126654hkeywordsÅdippmkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateEløΩgrankingÅcrfcd4737irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPacket Reordering MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6248∂habstractyòThis document defines a mechanism for the reoptimization of loosely routed MPLS and GMPLS (Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching) Traffic Engineering (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) signaled with Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE).  This document proposes a mechanism that allows a TE LSP head-end Label Switching Router (LSR) to trigger a new path re-evaluation on every hop that has a next hop defined as a loose or abstract hop and a mid-point LSR to signal to the head-end LSR that a better path exists (compared to the current path) or that the TE LSP must be reoptimized (because of maintenance required on the TE LSP path).  The proposed mechanism applies to the cases of intra- and inter-domain (Interior Gateway Protocol area (IGP area) or Autonomous System) packet and non-packet TE LSPs following a loosely routed path.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekJP. Vasseur¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnamejY. Ikejiri¢kaffiliationjBT InfonetdnamehR. Zhangddatej”´hfilenamegrfc4736eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126653hkeywordsÇgrsvp-tekte lsp pathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEU.WgrankingÄcrfcd4736irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxReoptimization of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE) Loosely Routed Label Switched Path (LSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÿThis document is registration for the 'example' media type and 'example' subtypes within the standards tree.  The 'example/*' and '*/example' media types are defined for documentation purposes only. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylorddatej”Æhfilenamegrfc4735eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126652hkeywordsÇjmedia typegexamplekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEBe6grankingcrfcd4735irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Example Media Types for Use in DocumentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo updates RFC 4733 to add event codes for modem, fax, and text telephony signals when carried in the telephony event RTP payload.  It supersedes the assignment of event codes for this purpose in RFC 2833, and therefore obsoletes that part of RFC 2833. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylorddatej”æhfilenamegrfc4734eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126651hkeywordsÜireal-timekapplicationhprotocoldDTMFidual-tonenmultifrequencykobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateEáAtgranking~crfcd4734irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Definition of Events for Modem, Fax, and Text Telephony SignalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis memo describes how to carry dual-tone multifrequency (DTMF) signalling, other tone signals, and telephony events in RTP packets. It obsoletes RFC 2833.

This memo captures and expands upon the basic framework defined in RFC 2833, but retains only the most basic event codes. It sets up an IANA registry to which other event code assignments may be added. Companion documents add event codes to this registry relating to modem, fax, text telephony, and channel-associated signalling events. The remainder of the event codes defined in RFC 2833 are conditionally reserved in case other documents revive their use.

This document provides a number of clarifications to the original document. However, it specifically differs from RFC 2833 by removing the requirement that all compliant implementations support the DTMF events. Instead, compliant implementations taking part in out-of-band negotiations of media stream content indicate what events they support. This memo adds three new procedures to the RFC 2833 framework: subdivision of long events into segments, reporting of multiple events in a single packet, and the concept and reporting of state events. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylorddatej”Fhfilenamegrfc4733eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126650hkeywordsÜireal-timekapplicationhprotocoldDTMFidual-tonenmultifrequencykobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateEáAhgranking}crfcd4733irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCRTP Payload for DTMF Digits, Telephony Tones, and Telephony SignalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd4734d5244µhabstracty™This document provides an overview of possible avenues for denial-of-service (DoS) attack on Internet systems.  The aim is to encourage protocol designers and network engineers towards designs that are more robust.  We discuss partial solutions that reduce the effectiveness of attacks, and how some solutions might inadvertently open up alternative vulnerabilities.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationqNetwork ResonancednamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej”πhfilenamegrfc4732eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126649hkeywordsÅcdoskobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateEåTágranking|crfcd4732irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex)Internet Denial-of-Service ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty&This document extends IMAP (RFC 3501) SEARCH and UID SEARCH commands with several result options, which can control what kind of information is returned.  The following result options are defined: minimal value, maximal value, all found messages, and number of found messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiIsode LtddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationuInventure Systems LtddnamekD. Cridlandddatej”øhfilenamegrfc4731eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126648hkeywordsÇjuid searchcuidkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZ]–granking{crfcd4731irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVIMAP4 Extension to SEARCH Command for Controlling What Kind of Information Is ReturneddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9394∂habstracty¡This document describes a SIP Event Package "kpml" that enables monitoring of Dual Tone Multi-Frequency (DTMF) signals and uses Extensible Markup Language (XML) documents referred to as Key Press Markup Language (KPML).  The kpml Event Package may be used to support applications consistent with the principles defined in the document titled "A Framework for Application Interaction in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)".  The event package uses SUBSCRIBE messages and allows for XML documents that define and describe filter specifications for capturing key presses (DTMF Tones) entered at a presentation-free User Interface SIP User Agent (UA).  The event package uses NOTIFY messages and allows for XML documents to report the captured key presses (DTMF tones), consistent with the filter specifications, to an Application Server.  The scope of this package is for collecting supplemental key presses or mid-call key presses (triggers). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxCantata Technology, Inc.dnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamehM. Dollyddatej”Thfilenamegrfc4730eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126647hkeywordsÑbuajuser agentddtmfxdual tone multi-frequencykobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateEZ]∑grankingzcrfcd4730irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Event Package for Key Press Stimulus (KPML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) for Uniform Resource Name (URN) resources published by the Near Field Communication (NFC) Forum.  The NFC Forum defines and manages resources that utilize this URN identification model.  Management activities for these and other resource types are provided by the NFC Forum Technical Committee.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiNFC ForumdnamegM. Abelddatej”Ωhfilenamegrfc4729eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126646hkeywordsÅxforum technical committeekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEløugrankingycrfcd4729irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for  the Near Field Communication (NFC) ForumdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThe Dynamic Source Routing protocol (DSR) is a simple and efficient routing protocol designed specifically for use in multi-hop wireless ad hoc networks of mobile nodes.  DSR allows the network to be completely self-organizing and self-configuring, without the need for any existing network infrastructure or administration.  The protocol is composed of the two main mechanisms of "Route Discovery" and "Route Maintenance", which work together to allow nodes to discover and maintain routes to arbitrary destinations in the ad hoc network.  All aspects of the protocol operate entirely on demand, allowing the routing packet overhead of DSR to scale automatically to only what is needed to react to changes in the routes currently in use.  The protocol allows multiple routes to any destination and allows each sender to select and control the routes used in routing its packets, for example, for use in load balancing or for increased robustness.  Other advantages of the DSR protocol include easily guaranteed loop-free routing, operation in networks containing unidirectional links, use of only "soft state" in routing, and very rapid recovery when routes in the network change.  The DSR protocol is designed mainly for mobile ad hoc networks of up to about two hundred nodes and is designed to work well even with very high rates of mobility.  This document specifies the operation of the DSR protocol for routing unicast IPv4 packets.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoRice UniversitydnamejD. Johnson¢kaffiliationdUIUCdnameeY. Hu¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnamehD. Maltzddatej”±hfilenamegrfc4728eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126645hkeywordsÇoroute discoveryqroute maintenancekobsoletedByÄepageskopublicationDateEÀ“øgrankingxcrfcd4728irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMThe Dynamic Source Routing Protocol (DSR) for Mobile Ad Hoc Networks for IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„When experimenting with or extending protocols, it is often necessary to use some sort of protocol number or constant in order to actually test or experiment with the new function, even when testing in a closed environment.  This document reserves some ranges of numbers for experimentation purposes in specific protocols where the need to support experimentation has been identified, and it describes the numbers that have already been reserved by other documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnameiB. FennerddatejÃhfilenamegrfc4727eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126644hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEo~Bgrankingwcrfcd4727irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGExperimental Values In IPv4, IPv6, ICMPv4, ICMPv6, UDP, and TCP HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document provides a framework for establishing and controlling Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Traffic Engineered (TE) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in multi-domain networks.

For the purposes of this document, a domain is considered to be any collection of network elements within a common sphere of address management or path computational responsibility. Examples of such domains include Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) areas and Autonomous Systems (ASes). This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ.-P. Vasseur¢kaffiliationmNuova SystemsdnamekA. Ayyangarddatej”¡hfilenamegrfc4726eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126643hkeywordsÖdmplsgmpls-tebteclspslabel switched pathkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEdˆgrankingvcrfcd4726irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNA Framework for Inter-Domain Multiprotocol Label Switching Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©An ENUM domain name is tightly coupled with the underlying E.164 number.  The process of verifying whether or not the Registrant of an ENUM domain name is identical to the Assignee of the corresponding E.164 number is commonly called "validation".  This document describes validation requirements and a high-level architecture for an ENUM validation infrastructure.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgenum.atdnamelA. Mayrhofer¢kaffiliationfSwitchdnamelB. Hoeneisenddatej”¬hfilenamegrfc4725eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126642hkeywordsÖeE.164hRegistryiRegistrarjRegistranthAssigneekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEo~<grankingucrfcd4725irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexENUM Validation ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This document describes a mechanism for BGP that would help minimize the negative effects on routing caused by BGP restart. An End-of-RIB marker is specified and can be used to convey routing convergence information. A new BGP capability, termed "Graceful Restart Capability", is defined that would allow a BGP speaker to express its ability to preserve forwarding state during BGP restart. Finally, procedures are outlined for temporarily retaining routing information across a TCP session termination/re-establishment.

The mechanisms described in this document are applicable to all routers, both those with the ability to preserve forwarding state during BGP restart and those without (although the latter need to implement only a subset of the mechanisms described in this document). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Sangli¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Fernando¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. Scudder¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej”Ωhfilenamegrfc4724eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126641hkeywordsÉwborder gateway protocoljend-of-ribkbgp restartkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE≠r§grankingtcrfcd4724irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Graceful Restart Mechanism for BGPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8538∂habstractyËThe MIDI Manufacturers Association (MMA) and the Association of Musical Electronics Industry (AMEI) have produced the Mobile XMF standard, which was developed particularly for mobile MIDI applications.  Mobile XMF is a very compact media type providing high-quality synthetic audio content for music downloading and messaging applications that require MIME registration.  This document registers the media type audio/mobile-xmf.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejT. Kosonen¢kaffiliationcMMAdnamehT. Whiteddatej”πhfilenamegrfc4723eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126640hkeywordsÜcmmaxmidi manufacturers associationx,association of musical electronices industrydameidMIDIx$Musical Instrument Digital InterfacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEã©grankingscrfcd4723irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Registration of Media Type audio/mobile-xmfdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty«Media Server Control Markup Language (MSCML) is a markup language used in conjunction with SIP to provide advanced conferencing and interactive voice response (IVR) functions.  MSCML presents an application-level control model, as opposed to device-level control models.  One use of this protocol is for communications between a conference focus and mixer in the IETF SIP Conferencing Framework.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy JenningsgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxCantata Technology, Inc.dnamekJ. Van Dyke¢kaffiliationxCantata Technology, Inc.dnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationsPingtel CorporationdnamejA. Spitzerddatej—dhfilenamegrfc4722eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126639hkeywordsÉcsipcivrxinteractive voice responsekobsoletedByÅd5022epagesQopublicationDateEZ]grankingrcrfcd4722irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9Media Server Control Markup Language (MSCML) and ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”Mobile IP, as originally specified, defines an authentication extension (the Mobile-Foreign Authentication extension) by which a mobile node can authenticate itself to a foreign agent. Unfortunately, that extension does not provide the foreign agent any direct guarantee that the protocol is protected from replays and does not allow for the use of existing techniques (such as Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol (CHAP)) for authenticating portable computer devices.

In this specification, we define extensions for the Mobile IP Agent Advertisements and the Registration Request that allow a foreign agent to use a challenge/response mechanism to authenticate the mobile node.

Furthermore, this document updates RFC 3344 by including a new authentication extension called the Mobile-Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) Authentication extension. This new extension is provided so that a mobile node can supply credentials for authorization, using commonly available AAA infrastructure elements. This authorization-enabling extension MAY co-exist in the same Registration Request with authentication extensions defined for Mobile IP Registration by RFC 3344. This document obsoletes RFC 3012. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejP. Calhoun¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekJ. Bharatiaddatej”•hfilenamegrfc4721eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126638hkeywordsÅdchapkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¡Mƒgrankingqcrfcd4721irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Mobile IPv4 Challenge/Response Extensions (Revised)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∂This document defines a mechanism for preserving Frame Check Sequence (FCS) through Ethernet, Frame Relay, High-Level Data Link Control (HDLC), and PPP pseudowires. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgTellabsdnamehA. Malis¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehD. Allan¢kaffiliationcMCIdnamelN. Del Regnoddatej”ùhfilenamegrfc4720eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126637hkeywordsÅcfcskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateEZ^grankingpcrfcd4720irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGPseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) Frame Check Sequence RetentiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document describes the transport of Ethernet frames over the Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol, Version 3 (L2TPv3).  This includes the transport of Ethernet port-to-port frames as well as the transport of Ethernet VLAN frames.  The mechanism described in this document can be used in the creation of Pseudowires to transport Ethernet frames over an IP network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwal¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Townsley¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemM. Dos Santosddatej”Ωhfilenamegrfc4719eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126636hkeywordsÇlport-to-portdvlankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZ]±grankingocrfcd4719irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOTransport of Ethernet Frames over Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3 (L2TPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5641∂habstractydThis document clarifies many areas of the IKEv2 specification.  It does not to introduce any changes to the protocol, but rather provides descriptions that are less prone to ambiguous interpretations.  The purpose of this document is to encourage the development of interoperable implementations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej”∑hfilenamegrfc4718eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126635hkeywordsÅuinternet key exchangekobsoletedByÅd5996epages:opublicationDateEBegrankingncrfcd4718irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2IKEv2 Clarifications and Implementation GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyAn Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) Pseudowire (PW) is used to carry ATM cells over an MPLS network.  This enables service providers to offer "emulated" ATM services over existing MPLS networks.  This document specifies methods for the encapsulation of ATM cells within a pseudowire.  It also specifies the procedures for using a PW to provide an ATM service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelJ. Jayakumar¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamehM. Bocci¢kaffiliationxLevel 3 Communications, LLCdnamekN. El-Aawar¢kaffiliationpECI Telecom Inc.dnamejJ. Brayley¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Koleyniddatej”∫hfilenamegrfc4717eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126634hkeywordsÉbpwjpseudowirexmultiprotocol label switchingkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateEãÆgrankingmcrfcd4717irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZEncapsulation Methods for Transport of Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) over MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=This document formally documents an existing public key file format in use for exchanging public keys between different Secure Shell (SSH) implementations.

In addition, this document defines a standard textual representation for SSH public key fingerprints. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpVanDyke SoftwarednamelJ. Galbraith¢kaffiliationnCanola & JonesdnameiR. ThayerddatejÃ
hfilenamegrfc4716eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126633hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateEZ]¬grankinglcrfcd4716irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-The Secure Shell (SSH) Public Key File FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9519∂habstracty´Without a tel URI parameter to carry an encoding type of Integrated Services Digital Network (ISDN) subaddress, interworking between ISDN User Part (ISUP) network and a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) network is impossible in some cases.  To solve this problem, this document specifies a new optional tel URI parameter to carry the encoding type of ISDN subaddress.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekM. Munakata¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Schubert¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegT. Ohbaddatej”æhfilenamegrfc4715eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126632hkeywordsÉxuniform resource identifierdisupnisdn user partkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZ].grankingkcrfcd4715irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSThe Integrated Services Digital Network (ISDN) Subaddress Encoding Type for tel URIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThe work of the IETF is to discuss, develop, and disseminate technical specifications to support the Internet's operation.  Technical publication is the process by which that output is disseminated to the community at large.  As such, it is important to understand the requirements on the publication process.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Mankin¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehS. Hayesddatej”ªhfilenamegrfc4714eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126631hkeywordsÅxinternet engineering task forcekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE-çÄgrankingjcrfcd4714irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Requirements for IETF Technical Publication ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyùMany Chinese characters in common use have variants, which makes most of the Chinese Domain Names (CDNs) have at least two different forms.  The equivalence between Simplified Chinese (SC) and Traditional Chinese (TC) characters is very important for CDN registration.  This memo builds on the basic concepts, general guidelines, and framework of RFC 3743 to specify proposed registration and administration procedures for Chinese domain names.  The document provides the information needed for understanding and using the tables defined in the IANA table registrations for Simplified and Traditional Chinese.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefX. Lee¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamefW. Mao¢kaffiliationeTWNICdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationeTWNICdnamefN. Hsu¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej”~hfilenamegrfc4713eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126630hkeywordsÖccdnbscrsimplified chinesebtcstraditional chinesekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateE.ÈHgrankingicrfcd4713irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHRegistration and Administration Recommendations for Chinese Domain NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This memo specifies two transport mappings of the \%Real-Time Application Quality-of-Service Monitoring (RAQMON) information model defined in RFC 4710 using TCP as a native transport and the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) to carry the RAQMON information from a RAQMON Data Source (RDS) to a RAQMON Report Collector (RRC). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Siddiqui¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationkAlert LogicdnamemE. Golovinsky¢kaffiliationx#Samsung Information Systems AmericadnameiM. Rahman¢kaffiliationhBroadcomdnamefY. Kimddatej”ºhfilenamegrfc4712eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126629hkeywordsàcmibxmanagement information basedsnmpx"simple network management protocolcrdsrraqmon data sourcecqosnRAQMON-RDS-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateE8'grankinghcrfcd4712irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlTransport Mappings for Real-time Application Quality-of-Service Monitoring (RAQMON) Protocol Data Unit (PDU)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyXThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  The document proposes an extension to the Remote Monitoring MIB, RFC 2819.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for real-time application Quality of Service (QoS) monitoring. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Siddiqui¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationkAlert LogicdnamemE. Golovinskyddatej”ªhfilenamegrfc4711eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126628hkeywordsÑxmanagement information baseuremote monitoring mibcqosjRAQMON-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateE8'"grankinggcrfcd4711irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Real-time Application Quality-of-Service Monitoring (RAQMON) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThere is a need to monitor end-devices such as IP phones, pagers, Instant Messaging clients, mobile phones, and various other handheld computing devices.  This memo extends the remote network monitoring (RMON) family of specifications to allow real-time quality-of-service (QoS) monitoring of various applications that run on these devices and allows this information to be integrated with the RMON family using the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).  This memo defines the framework, architecture, relevant metrics, and transport requirements for real-time QoS monitoring of applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Siddiqui¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationkAlert LogicdnamemE. Golovinskyddatej”ªhfilenamegrfc4710eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126627hkeywordsÜqinternet protocolkend-devicescqosrquality of servicedsnmpx"simple network management protocolkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateE8'grankingfcrfcd4710irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFReal-time Application Quality-of-Service Monitoring (RAQMON) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˛In current Web browsers, there is no uniform way to specify that a user clicking on a link will be presented with an editable view of a Web Distinguished Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) server. For example, it is frequently desirable to be able to click on a link and have this link open a window that can handle drag-and-drop interaction with the resources of a WebDAV server.

This document specifies a mechanism and a document format that enables WebDAV servers to send "mounting" information to a WebDAV client. The mechanism is designed to work on any platform and with any combination of browser and WebDAV client, relying solely on the well-understood dispatch of documents through their MIME type. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej”ºhfilenamegrfc4709eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126626hkeywordsÅmdrag-and-dropkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@Agrankingecrfcd4709irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBMounting Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÓThis document standardises a new media type -- application/cellml+xml -- for use in exchanging mathematical models represented in a CellML Umbrella 1.0 compliant markup language.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxThe University of AucklanddnameiA. Millerddatej”µhfilenamegrfc4708eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126625hkeywordsàlmedia formatrmathematical modelvmathematical modellingumathematical modelingncontent MathMLpmarkup languagesnbioengineeringgbiologykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@,grankingdcrfcd4708irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqCellML Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyúThe Netnews Administration System (NAS) is a framework to simplify the administration and usage of network news (also known as Netnews) on the Internet. Data for the administration of newsgroups and hierarchies are kept in a distributed hierarchical database and are available through a client-server protocol.

The database is accessible by news servers, news administrators, and news readers. News servers can update their configuration automatically; administrators are able to get the data manually. News reader programs are able to get certain information from an NAS server, automatically or at a user's discretion, which provides detailed information about groups and hierarchies to the user.

NAS is usable in coexistence with the current, established process of control messages; an unwanted interference is impossible. Furthermore, NAS is able to reflect the somewhat chaotic structure of Usenet in a hierarchical database. NAS can be used without modification of existing news relay, news server, or news reader software; however, some tasks will be better accomplished with NAS-compliant software. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamepScott HollenbeckgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxFreie Universitaet BerlindnamegP. Grau¢kaffiliationxFreie Universitaet BerlindnameiV. Heinau¢kaffiliationxFreie Universitaet BerlindnamenH. Schlichting¢kaffiliationxFreie Universitaet BerlindnamemR. Schuettlerddatej—@hfilenamegrfc4707eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126624hkeywordsÉlnews serversrnews administratorknews readerkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateE@grankingccrfcd4707irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex#Netnews Administration System (NAS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing parameters of the "Asymmetric Digital Subscriber Line" family of interface types: ADSL, ADSL2, ADSL2+, and their variants. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpECI Telecom Ltd.dnamenM. Morgenstern¢kaffiliationpECI Telecom Ltd.dnamehM. Dodge¢kaffiliationmNEC AustraliadnamejS. Baillie¢kaffiliationmNEC AustraliadnamejU. Bonolloddatej”Øhfilenamegrfc4706eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126623hkeywordsÖcmibxmanagement information basefadsl2+qADSL2-LINE-TC-MIBnADSL2-LINE-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesßopublicationDateEn4igrankingbcrfcd4706irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexODefinitions of Managed Objects for Asymmetric Digital Subscriber Line 2 (ADSL2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document describes the encryption and key management used by GigaBeam as part of the WiFiber(tm) family of radio link products.  The security solution is documented in the hope that other wireless product development efforts will include comparable capabilities.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationhGigaBeamdnamehA. Corryddatej”∏hfilenamegrfc4705eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126622hkeywordsÉnkey managementgwifiberjradio linkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE-ç|grankingacrfcd4705irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)GigaBeam High-Speed Radio Link EncryptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document specifies a new Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) option that can be used to exchange information about a DHCPv6 client's Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) and about responsibility for updating DNS resource records (RRs) related to the client's address assignments. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volzddatej”¶hfilenamegrfc4704eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126621hkeywordsÅfdns rrkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@4granking`crfcd4704irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) Client Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) provides a mechanism for host configuration that includes dynamic assignment of IP addresses and fully qualified domain names.  To maintain accurate name-to-IP-address and IP-address-to-name mappings in the DNS, these dynamically assigned addresses and fully qualified domain names (FQDNs) require updates to the DNS.  This document identifies situations in which conflicts in the use of fully qualified domain names may arise among DHCP clients and servers, and it describes a strategy for the use of the DHCID DNS resource record (RR) in resolving those conflicts. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volzddatej”¶hfilenamegrfc4703eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126620hkeywordsÉrdynamic assignmentcdnsldhcid dns rrkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@#granking_crfcd4703irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsResolution of Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) Conflicts among Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) ClientsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This document describes a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv4 (DHCPv4) option that can be used to exchange information about a DHCPv4 client's fully qualified domain name and about responsibility for updating the DNS RR related to the client's address assignment. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej”¶hfilenamegrfc4702eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126619hkeywordsÇfdhcpv4fdns rrkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@granking^crfcd4702irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Client Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâIt is possible for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) clients to attempt to update the same DNS Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) or to update a DNS FQDN that has been added to the DNS for another purpose as they obtain DHCP leases.  Whether the DHCP server or the clients themselves perform the DNS updates, conflicts can arise.  To resolve such conflicts, RFC 4703 proposes storing client identifiers in the DNS to unambiguously associate domain names with the DHCP clients to which they refer.  This memo defines a distinct Resource Record (RR) type for this purpose for use by DHCP clients and servers: the "DHCID" RR. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationxAraneus Information Systems OydnamemA. Gustafssonddatej”¶hfilenamegrfc4701eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126618hkeywordsÇhdns fqdnxfully qualified domain namekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@	granking]crfcd4701irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiA DNS Resource Record (RR) for Encoding Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Information (DHCID RR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5494∂habstracty;This document describes an IRIS registry schema for IP address and Autonomous System Number information.  The schema extends the necessary query and result operations of IRIS to provide the functional information service needs for syntaxes and results used by Internet Protocol address registries. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnameiE. Gunduz¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamegS. Kerrddatej”ìhfilenamegrfc4698eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126617hkeywordsÉjip addressxautonomous system numberxinternet protocol addresskobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateE.È.grankingZcrfcd4698irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSIRIS: An Address Registry (areg) Type for the Internet Registry Information ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractymThis memo describes DNS iterative resolver behavior that results in a significant query volume sent to the root and top-level domain (TLD) name servers.  We offer implementation advice to iterative resolver developers to alleviate these unnecessary queries.  The recommendations made in this document are a direct byproduct of observation and analysis of abnormal query traffic patterns seen at two of the thirteen root name servers and all thirteen com/net TLD name servers.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiM. Larson¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiP. Barberddatej”Ωhfilenamegrfc4697eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126616hkeywordsÉrdomain name systemctldptop level domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEBe@grankingYcrfcd4697irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber{etotaletitlex#Observed DNS Resolution MisbehaviordtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9520∂habstractyÓThis memo offers non-normative implementation guidance for the Real-time Protocol (RTP) MIDI (Musical Instrument Digital Interface) payload format.  The memo presents its advice in the context of a network musical performance application.  In this application two musicians, located in different physical locations, interact over a network to perform as they would if located in the same room.  Underlying the performances are RTP MIDI sessions over unicast UDP.  Algorithms for sending and receiving recovery journals (the resiliency structure for the payload format) are described in detail.  Although the memo focuses on network musical performance, the presented implementation advice is relevant to other RTP MIDI applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamejJ. Lazzaro¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamelJ. Wawrzynekddatej”ïhfilenamegrfc4696eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126615hkeywordsìqcheckpoint packetrcheckpoint historymguard packetsfjitterrkeep-alive packetsdMIDItmusical telepresencexnetwork musical performancecNMPsreceiving algorithmprecovery journalx$recovery journal receiving structurex"recovery journal sending structurecRTPhRTP MIDIlqueuing MIDIqsending algorithmlsending MIDIltelepresencekobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateEU.vgrankingXcrfcd4696irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$An Implementation Guide for RTP MIDIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo describes a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for the MIDI (Musical Instrument Digital Interface) command language.  The format encodes all commands that may legally appear on a MIDI 1.0 DIN cable.  The format is suitable for interactive applications (such as network musical performance) and content-delivery applications (such as file streaming).  The format may be used over unicast and multicast UDP and TCP, and it defines tools for graceful recovery from packet loss.  Stream behavior, including the MIDI rendering method, may be customized during session setup.  The format also serves as a mode for the mpeg4-generic format, to support the MPEG 4 Audio Object Types for General MIDI, Downloadable Sounds Level 2, and Structured Audio. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamejJ. Lazzaro¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamelJ. Wawrzynekddatej”îhfilenamegrfc4695eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126614hkeywordsòcascqcontent streamingeDLS 2lGeneral MIDIdMIDIiMIDI filesMIDI file streamingrMIDI light controlnMIDI renderingmMIDI ringtonexMIDI streaming MIDI sequencernMIDI time codemMIDI timecodexMIDI Manufacturers AssociationxMMA mpeg4-generic MPEG 4wMPEG 4 Structured AudiowMPEG 4 Synthetic CodingcMTCmmusical notesxnetwork musical performanceprecovery journallShow Controllsonificationhringtonehrtp-midicRTPhRTP MIDIoSMPTE time codenSMPTE timecodesStandard MIDI FilescXMFkobsoletedByÅd6295epages©opublicationDateEU.hgrankingWcrfcd4695irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRTP Payload Format for MIDIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document defines five parameters in the "tel" Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) to carry the number portability (NP)-related information.  Those parameters can be passed to the next-hop network node after an NP database dip has been performed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnameeJ. Yuddatej”øhfilenamegrfc4694eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-126613hkeywordsÇxuniform resource identifierbnpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEBeLgrankingVcrfcd4694irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Number Portability Parameters for the "tel" URIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document describes a new document series intended for use as a repository for IETF operations documents, which should be more ephemeral than RFCs, but more referenceable than Internet-Drafts, and with more clear handling procedures than a random Web page.

It proposes to establish this series as an RFC 3933 process experiment. This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfGooglednamemH. Alvestrandddatej”ºhfilenamegrfc4693eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126612hkeywordsÅcIONkobsoletedByÅd6393epages
opublicationDateE%8îgrankingUcrfcd4693irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIETF Operational NotesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„This memo provides an analysis of the Host Density metric as it is currently used to guide registry allocations of IPv6 unicast address blocks.  This document contrasts the address efficiency as currently adopted in the allocation of IPv4 network addresses and that used by the IPv6 protocol.  Note that for large allocations there are very significant variations in the target efficiency metric between the two approaches.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej”µhfilenamegrfc4692eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126611hkeywordsÇxinternet protocol version 6xipv6 unicast address blockskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE%8âgrankingTcrfcd4692irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Considerations on the IPv6 Host Density MetricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty\Whenever the IETF decides to enter into a liaison relationship with another organization, such as a Standards Development Organization (SDO), a consortium, or an industrial forum, a liaison manager is appointed.  The procedures used by the IAB to establish and maintain liaison relationships between the IETF and other organizations are described in RFC 4052.  This document expands on the role of liaison managers and liaison representatives, giving guidelines on their mandate and the expectations, tasks, and responsibilities placed on them.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamelL. Anderssonddatej”∂hfilenamegrfc4691eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126610hkeywordsÖxinternet engineering task forcecsdox"standards development organizationjconsortiumpindustrial forumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@grankingScrfcd4691irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex@Guidelines for Acting as an IETF Liaison to Another OrganizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyúThis note describes issues raised by the deployment and use of Internationalized Domain Names.  It describes problems both at the time of registration and for use of those names in the DNS.  It recommends that IETF should update the RFCs relating to IDNs and a framework to be followed in doing so, as well as summarizing and identifying some work that is required outside the IETF.  In particular, it proposes that some changes be investigated for the Internationalizing Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) standard and its supporting tables, based on experience gained since those standards were completed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelP. Faltstrom¢kaffiliationx!Swedish Museum of Natural HistorydnamegC. Karp¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej”¥hfilenamegrfc4690eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126609hkeywordsÑcdnspdomain namespacedidnax/internationalizing domain names in applicationskobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateE·WgrankingRcrfcd4690irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexDReview and Recommendations for Internationalized Domain Names (IDNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes terminology for the benchmarking of devices that implement traffic control using packet classification based on defined criteria.  The terminology is to be applied to measurements made on the data plane to evaluate IP traffic control mechanisms.  Rules for packet classification can be based on any field in the IP header, such as the Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP), or any field in the packet payload, such as port number.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrReef Point SystemsdnamekS. Poretsky¢kaffiliationhVeriwavednameiJ. Perser¢kaffiliationiTelcordiadnamelS. Erramilli¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamejS. Khuranaddatej”∂hfilenamegrfc4689eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-126608hkeywordsÅupacket classificationkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateEGﬁdgrankingQcrfcd4689irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexETerminology for Benchmarking Network-layer Traffic Control MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯This document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for naming persistent resources defined by Aerospace and Defence Industries Association of Europe (ASD) Specification 1000D.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhInmediusdnamejS. RushingddatejÀ˙hfilenamegrfc4688eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126607hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE4+®grankingPcrfcd4688irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for Aerospace and Defence Industries Association of Europe (ASD) Specification 1000DdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEMulti-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) has been extended to encompass point-to-multipoint (P2MP) Label Switched Paths (LSPs). As with point-to-point MPLS LSPs, the requirement to detect, handle, and diagnose control and data plane defects is critical.

For operators deploying services based on P2MP MPLS LSPs, the detection and specification of how to handle those defects are important because such defects not only may affect the fundamentals of an MPLS network, but also may impact service level specification commitments for customers of their network.

This document describes requirements for data plane operations and management for P2MP MPLS LSPs. These requirements apply to all forms of P2MP MPLS LSPs, and include P2MP Traffic Engineered (TE) LSPs and multicast LSPs. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamekS. Yasukawa¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationrAria Networks Ltd.dnamegD. King¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeauddatej”æhfilenamegrfc4687eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126606hkeywordsÑxmultiprotocol label switchingdpwmpclspmp2mp mpls lspkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¥>grankingOcrfcd4687irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSOperations and Management (OAM) Requirements  for Point-to-Multipoint MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis document provides an analysis of some threats against Internet mail that are intended to be addressed by signature-based mail authentication, in particular DomainKeys Identified Mail.  It discusses the nature and location of the bad actors, what their capabilities are, and what they intend to accomplish via their attacks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Fentonddatej”∫hfilenamegrfc4686eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddkimdfullx"dkim - Domain Keys Identified MaildnamexDomain Keys Identified Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-126605hkeywordsÖeemailfattacknauthenticationisignaturecsspkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¥SgrankingNcrfcd4686irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Analysis of Threats Motivating DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxëThis memo presents a mechanism that allows feeds publishers to express threaded discussions within the Atom Syndication Format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Snellddatej”µhfilenamegrfc4685eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126604hkeywordsÑwatom syndication formatiextensionithreadingksyndicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¥}grankingMcrfcd4685irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAtom Threading ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This document defines Multi-Protocol BGP (MP-BGP) procedures that allow BGP speakers to exchange Route Target reachability information.  This information can be used to build a route distribution graph in order to limit the propagation of Virtual Private Network (VPN) Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) between different autonomous systems or distinct clusters of the same autonomous system.  This document updates RFC 4364. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejP. Marques¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegL. Fang¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Raszuk¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehK. Patel¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Guichardddatej”™hfilenamegrfc4684eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126603hkeywordsÉfmp-bgplbgp speakerslroute targetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZ]÷grankingLcrfcd4684irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexöConstrained Route Distribution for Border Gateway Protocol/MultiProtocol Label Switching (BGP/MPLS) Internet Protocol (IP) Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document defines the Subject Identification Method (SIM) for including a privacy-sensitive identifier in the subjectAltName extension of a certificate.

The SIM is an optional feature that may be used by relying parties to determine whether the subject of a particular certificate is also the person corresponding to a particular sensitive identifier. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamegJ. Park¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefH. Lee¢kaffiliationeBCQREdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polkddatej”πhfilenamegrfc4683eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126602hkeywordsÑnsubjectaltnameqprivacy-sensitivekidentifiersepepsikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE%9ygrankingKcrfcd4683irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Subject Identification Method (SIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines a basic set of managed objects for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)-based management of PacketCable- and IPCablecom-compliant Multimedia Terminal Adapter devices. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnBroadcom Corp.dnamelE. Nechamkin¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiJ-F. Muleddatej”öhfilenamegrfc4682eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-126601hkeywordsÑcmibdsnmpx"simple network management protocolqPKTC-IETF-MTA-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDateEåT£grankingJcrfcd4682irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoMultimedia Terminal Adapter (MTA) Management Information Base for PacketCable- and IPCablecom-Compliant DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractyçThis document specifies a TLS extension that enables clients to send generic user mapping hints in a supplemental data handshake message defined in RFC 4680.  One such mapping hint is defined in an informative section, the UpnDomainHint, which may be used by a server to locate a user in a directory database.  Other mapping hints may be defined in other documents in the future. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelA. Medvinsky¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegJ. Ballddatej”Æhfilenamegrfc4681eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126600hkeywordsÉxtransport layer securityqhandshake messagemupndomainhintkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE,+™grankingIcrfcd4681irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTLS User Mapping ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty¡This specification defines a TLS handshake message for exchange of supplemental application data.  TLS hello message extensions are used to determine which supplemental data types are supported by both the TLS client and the TLS server.  Then, the supplemental data handshake message is used to exchange the data.  Other documents will define the syntax of these extensions and the syntax of the associated supplemental data types. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santessonddatej”Æhfilenamegrfc4680eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126599hkeywordsÅxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateE,+ÆgrankingHcrfcd4680irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+TLS Handshake Message for Supplemental DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8447d8996µhabstracty\This document describes the set of Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service Vendor-Specific Attributes (RADIUS VSAs) defined by the DSL Forum.

These attributes are designed to transport Digital Subscriber Line (DSL) information that is not supported by the standard RADIUS attribute set. It is expected that this document will be updated if and when the DSL Forum defines additional vendor-specific attributes, since its primary purpose is to provide a reference for DSL equipment vendors wishing to interoperate with other vendors' products. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelV. Mammoliti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationvRedback Networks, Inc.dnameiP. Arberg¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamekR. Rennisonddatej”õhfilenamegrfc4679eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126598hkeywordsÑx*remote authentication dial-in user servicecvsacdslwdigital subscriber linekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE“3grankingGcrfcd4679irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex+DSL Forum Vendor-Specific RADIUS AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyäEntities responsible for distributing work across a group of systems traditionally do not know a great deal about the ability of the applications on those systems to complete the work in a satisfactory fashion.  Workload management systems traditionally know a great deal about the health of applications, but have little control over the rate in which these applications receive work.  The Server/Application State Protocol (SASP) provides a mechanism for load balancers and workload management systems to communicate better ways of distributing the existing workload to the group members.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus WesterlundgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlIBM ResearchdnameiA. Bivensddatej”|hfilenamegrfc4678eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126597hkeywordsÇdsaspx!server/application state protocolkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateE‡⁄grankingFcrfcd4678irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex$Server/Application State Protocol v1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document describes the inner workings of IETF meetings and Working Groups, discusses organizations related to the IETF, and introduces the standards process.  It is not a formal IETF process document but instead an informational overview.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationvUniversity of MichigandnameiS. Harrisddatej”≥hfilenamegrfc4677eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126596hkeywordsÅgmeetingkobsoletedByÅd6722epages2opublicationDateE—ÓgrankingEcrfcd4677irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIThe Tao of IETF - A Novice's Guide to the Internet Engineering Task ForcedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍThis document specifies a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4 and DHCPv6) option containing the civic location of the client or the DHCP server.  The Location Configuration Information (LCI) includes information about the country, administrative units such as states, provinces, and cities, as well as street addresses, postal community names, and building information.  The option allows multiple renditions of the same address in different scripts and languages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”Ñhfilenamegrfc4676eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-126595hkeywordsÇclcixlocal configuration informationkobsoletedByÅd4776epagesopublicationDateEBe$grankingDcrfcd4676irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4 and DHCPv6) Option for Civic Addresses Configuration InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document proposes additional Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) attributes for dynamic Virtual LAN assignment and prioritization, for use in provisioning of access to IEEE 802 local area networks.  These attributes are usable within either RADIUS or Diameter. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamejP. Congdon¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamejM. Sanchez¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Abobaddatej”∏hfilenamegrfc4675eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126594hkeywordsÇx*remote authentication dial-in user servicerlocal area networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE“-grankingCcrfcd4675irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6RADIUS Attributes for Virtual LAN and Priority SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·This document presents a set of requirements for a Path Computation Element (PCE) discovery mechanism that would allow a Path Computation Client (PCC) to discover dynamically and automatically a set of PCEs along with certain information relevant for PCE selection.  It is intended that solutions that specify procedures and protocols or extensions to existing protocols for such PCE discovery satisfy these requirements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelJ.L. Le Rouxddatej”∂hfilenamegrfc4674eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126593hkeywordsÇwpath computation clientcpcckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE%8tgrankingBcrfcd4674irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Requirements for Path Computation Element (PCE) DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) (RFC 2865) Dynamic Authorization Server (DAS) functions that support the dynamic authorization extensions as defined in RFC 3576.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamemS. De Cnodder¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejN. Jonnala¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Chibaddatej”∂hfilenamegrfc4673eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126592hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basex*remote authentication dial-in user servicexRADIUS-DYNAUTH-SERVER-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE“&grankingAcrfcd4673irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'RADIUS Dynamic Authorization Server MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) (RFC2865) Dynamic Authorization Client (DAC) functions that support the dynamic authorization extensions as defined in RFC 3576.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamemS. De Cnodder¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejN. Jonnala¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Chibaddatej”∑hfilenamegrfc4672eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126591hkeywordsÖx*remote authentication dial-in user servicecdacxdynamic authorization clientx%RADIUS-DYNAUTH-CLIENT-MIB DEFINITIONSxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE“granking@crfcd4672irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'RADIUS Dynamic Authorization Client MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This memo defines a set of extensions that instrument RADIUS accounting server functions. These extensions represent a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. Using these extensions, IP-based management stations can manage RADIUS accounting servers.

This memo obsoletes RFC 2621 by deprecating the MIB table containing IPv4-only address formats and defining a new table to add support for version-neutral IP address formats. The remaining MIB objects from RFC 2621 are carried forward into this document. This memo also adds UNITS and REFERENCE clauses to selected objects. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrEnterasys NetworksdnameiD. Nelsonddatej”∏hfilenamegrfc4671eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126590hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basehsecurityx!remote access dialin user serviceuRADIUS-ACC-SERVER-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÛ4ﬁgranking?crfcd4671irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%RADIUS Accounting Server MIB for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This memo defines a set of extensions that instrument RADIUS accounting client functions. These extensions represent a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. Using these extensions, IP-based management stations can manage RADIUS accounting clients.

This memo obsoletes RFC 2620 by deprecating the MIB table containing IPv4-only address formats and defining a new table to add support for version-neutral IP address formats. The remaining MIB objects from RFC 2620 are carried forward into this document. This memo also adds UNITS and REFERENCE clauses to selected objects. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrEnterasys NetworksdnameiD. Nelsonddatej”∑hfilenamegrfc4670eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126589hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basehsecurityx!remote access dialin user serviceuRADIUS-ACC-CLIENT-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÛ4«granking>crfcd4670irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%RADIUS Accounting Client MIB for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶This memo defines a set of extensions that instrument RADIUS authentication server functions. These extensions represent a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. Using these extensions, IP-based management stations can manage RADIUS authentication servers.

This memo obsoletes RFC 2619 by deprecating the MIB table containing IPv4-only address formats and defining a new table to add support for version-neutral IP address formats. The remaining MIB objects from RFC 2619 are carried forward into this document. This memo also adds UNITS and REFERENCE clauses to selected objects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrEnterasys NetworksdnameiD. Nelsonddatej”∏hfilenamegrfc4669eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126588hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basehsecurityx!remote access dialin user servicevRADIUS-AUTH-SERVER-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÛ4πgranking=crfcd4669irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)RADIUS Authentication Server MIB for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This memo defines a set of extensions that instrument RADIUS authentication client functions. These extensions represent a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. Using these extensions, IP-based management stations can manage RADIUS authentication clients.

This memo obsoletes RFC 2618 by deprecating the MIB table containing IPv4-only address formats and defining a new table to add support for version-neutral IP address formats. The remaining MIB objects from RFC 2618 are carried forward into this document. The memo also adds UNITS and REFERENCE clauses to selected objects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrEnterasys NetworksdnameiD. Nelsonddatej”∏hfilenamegrfc4668eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126587hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basehsecurityx!remote access dialin user servicevRADIUS-AUTH-CLIENT-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÛ4™granking<crfcd4668irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)RADIUS Authentication Client MIB for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	The Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) provides a standard method for setting up and managing L2TP sessions to tunnel a variety of L2 protocols.  One of the reference models supported by L2TP describes the use of an L2TP session to connect two Layer 2 circuits attached to a pair of peering L2TP Access Concentrators (LACs), which is a basic form of Layer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN).  This document defines the protocol extensions for L2TP to set up different types of L2VPNs in a unified fashion. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefW. Luoddatej”®hfilenamegrfc4667eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126586hkeywordsÖeL2VPNdL2TPfL2TPv3jpseudowireiforwarderkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE‡—granking;crfcd4667irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXLayer 2 Virtual Private Network (L2VPN) Extensions for Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This memo defines a protocol for supporting the transport of any SS7 MTP3-User signalling (e.g., ISUP and SCCP messages) over IP using the services of the Stream Control Transmission Protocol.  Also, provision is made for protocol elements that enable a seamless operation of the MTP3-User peers in the SS7 and IP domains.  This protocol would be used between a Signalling Gateway (SG) and a Media Gateway Controller (MGC) or IP-resident Database, or between two IP-based applications.  It is assumed that the SG receives SS7 signalling over a standard SS7 interface using the SS7 Message Transfer Part (MTP) to provide transport.  This document obsoletes RFC 3332. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Morneault¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepJ. Pastor-Balbasddatej”°hfilenamegrfc4666eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126585hkeywordsâcmtpdisupdsccpdsctpx#stream control tranmission protocolcmgcvmedia gateway protocolbstrsignalling gatewaykobsoletedByÄepages|opublicationDateE“granking:crfcd4666irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSignaling System 7 (SS7) Message Transfer Part 3 (MTP3) - User Adaptation Layer (M3UA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides requirements for Layer 2 Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs).  It first provides taxonomy and terminology and states generic and general service requirements.  It covers point-to-point VPNs, referred to as Virtual Private Wire Service (VPWS), as well as multipoint-to-multipoint VPNs, also known as Virtual Private LAN Service (VPLS).  Detailed requirements are expressed from both a customer as well as a service provider perspectives.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekW. Augustyn¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamejY. Serbestddatej”µhfilenamegrfc4665eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126584hkeywordsÜel2vpneppvpnxvirtual private wire servicedvpwsxvirtual private lan servicedvplskobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateE‡«granking9crfcd4665irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNService Requirements for Layer 2 Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides a framework for Layer 2 Provider Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs).  This framework is intended to aid in standardizing protocols and mechanisms to support interoperable L2VPNs.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej»úhfilenamegrfc4664eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel2vpndfullx(l2vpn - Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 2 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126583hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateE‡¿granking8crfcd4664irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Framework for Layer 2 Virtual Private Networks (L2VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document describes the plan to transition responsibility for bridging-related MIB modules from the IETF Bridge MIB Working Group to the IEEE 802.1 Working Group, which develops the bridging technology the MIB modules are designed to manage.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxEffective Software ConsultingdnamemD. Harringtonddatej”±hfilenamegrfc4663eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126582hkeywordsÅxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEtWgranking7crfcd4663irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Transferring MIB Work from IETF Bridge MIB WG to IEEE 802.1 WGdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis document presents an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)-Specific Event Notification mechanism for subscribing to a homogeneous list of resources.  Instead of sending a SUBSCRIBE for each resource individually, the subscriber can subscribe to an entire list and then receive notifications when the state of any of the resources in the list changes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnamekA. B. Roach¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. RosenbergddatejÀ√hfilenamegrfc4662eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126581hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateD˜gÓgranking6crfcd4662irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Event Notification Extension for Resource ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁThe SIP event notification framework describes the usage of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for subscriptions and notifications of changes to a state of a resource.  The document does not describe a mechanism whereby filtering of event notification information can be achieved.  Filtering is a mechanism for defining the preferred notification information to be delivered and for specifying triggers that cause that information to be delivered.  In order to enable this, a format is needed to enable the subscriber to describe the state changes of a resource that cause notifications to be sent to it and what those notifications are to contain.  This document presents a format in the form of an XML document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeTeliodnamelH. Khartabil¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekE. Leppanen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Lonnfors¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepJ. Costa-Requenaddatej”Qhfilenamegrfc4661eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126580hkeywordsÉxevent state subscriptionhpresenceofilter criteriakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¥Égranking5crfcd4661irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQAn Extensible Markup Language (XML)-Based Format for Event Notification FilteringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe SIP event notification framework describes the usage of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for subscriptions and notifications of changes to the state of a resource. The document does not describe a mechanism whereby filtering of event notification information can be achieved.

This document describes the operations a subscriber performs in order to put filtering rules associated with a subscription to event notification information in place. The handling, by the subscriber, of responses to subscriptions carrying filtering rules and the handling of notifications with filtering rules applied to them are also described. Furthermore, the document conveys how the notifier behaves when receiving such filtering rules and how a notification is constructed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeTeliodnamelH. Khartabil¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekE. Leppanen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Lonnfors¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepJ. Costa-Requenaddatej”Qhfilenamegrfc4660eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126579hkeywordsÉxevent state subscriptionhpresenceofilter criteriakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE¥Ωgranking4crfcd4660irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Functional Description of Event Notification FilteringdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6665∂habstracty≠This document describes a method by which a Service Provider may use its packet-switched backbone to provide Virtual Private Network (VPN) services for its IPv6 customers. This method reuses, and extends where necessary, the "BGP/MPLS IP VPN" method for support of IPv6. In BGP/MPLS IP VPN, "Multiprotocol BGP" is used for distributing IPv4 VPN routes over the service provider backbone, and MPLS is used to forward IPv4 VPN packets over the backbone. This document defines an IPv6 VPN address family and describes the corresponding IPv6 VPN route distribution in "Multiprotocol BGP".

This document defines support of the IPv6 VPN service over both an IPv4 and an IPv6 backbone, and for using various tunneling techniques over the core, including MPLS, IP-in-IP, Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE) and IPsec protected tunnels. The inter-working between an IPv4 site and an IPv6 site is outside the scope of this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamelJ. De Clercq¢kaffiliationjOneSparrowdnamegD. Ooms¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Carugi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenF. Le Faucheurddatej”™hfilenamegrfc4659eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126578hkeywordsÉpservice providerwborder gateway protocolxmultiprotocol label switchingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZTgranking3crfcd4659irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@BGP-MPLS IP Virtual Private Network (VPN) Extension for IPv6 VPNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚The PCE model is described in the "PCE Architecture" document and facilitates path computation requests from Path Computation Clients (PCCs) to Path Computation Elements (PCEs).  This document specifies generic requirements for a communication protocol between PCCs and PCEs, and also between PCEs where cooperation between PCEs is desirable.  Subsequent documents will specify application-specific requirements for the PCE communication protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ash¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelJ.L. Le Rouxddatej”¥hfilenamegrfc4657eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126577hkeywordsÉppce architecturecpccwpath computation clientkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZEgranking1crfcd4657irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJPath Computation Element (PCE) Communication Protocol Generic RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThe One-Way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) measures unidirectional characteristics such as one-way delay and one-way loss.  High-precision measurement of these one-way IP performance metrics became possible with wider availability of good time sources (such as GPS and CDMA).  OWAMP enables the interoperability of these measurements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamekS. Shalunov¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamemB. Teitelbaum¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamegA. Karp¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamehJ. Boote¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamelM. Zekauskasddatej”mhfilenamegrfc4656eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126576hkeywordsÑxunidirectional characteristicsgone-waycgpsdcdmakobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateE“␀granking0crfcd4656irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-A One-way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7717d7718d8545∂habstractyConstraint-based path computation is a fundamental building block for traffic engineering systems such as Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) networks. Path computation in large, multi-domain, multi-region, or multi-layer networks is complex and may require special computational components and cooperation between the different network domains.

This document specifies the architecture for a Path Computation Element (PCE)-based model to address this problem space. This document does not attempt to provide a detailed description of all the architectural components, but rather it describes a set of building blocks for the PCE architecture from which solutions may be constructed. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ.-P. Vasseur¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ashddatej”´hfilenamegrfc4655eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpcedfullxpce - Path Computation ElementdnamexPath Computation Elementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126575hkeywordsÅstraffic engineeringkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateDÏöPgranking/crfcd4655irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3A Path Computation Element (PCE)-Based ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document specifies TCP-Friendly Multicast Congestion Control (TFMCC).  TFMCC is a congestion control mechanism for multicast transmissions in a best-effort Internet environment.  It is a single-rate congestion control scheme, where the sending rate is adapted to the receiver experiencing the worst network conditions.  TFMCC is reasonably fair when competing for bandwidth with TCP flows and has a relatively low variation of throughput over time, making it suitable for applications where a relatively smooth sending rate is of importance, such as streaming media.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpDoCoMo Euro-LabsdnameiJ. Widmer¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handleyddatej”∞hfilenamegrfc4654eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126574hkeywordsÑostreaming mediaimulticasebipqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateDÏöZgranking.crfcd4654irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexITCP-Friendly Multicast Congestion Control (TFMCC): Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis document specifies Non-Congestion Robustness (NCR) for TCP.  In the absence of explicit congestion notification from the network, TCP uses loss as an indication of congestion.  One of the ways TCP detects loss is using the arrival of three duplicate acknowledgments.  However, this heuristic is not always correct, notably in the case when network paths reorder segments (for whatever reason), resulting in degraded performance.  TCP-NCR is designed to mitigate this degraded performance by increasing the number of duplicate acknowledgments required to trigger loss recovery, based on the current state of the connection, in an effort to better disambiguate true segment loss from segment reordering.  This document specifies the changes to TCP, as well as the costs and benefits of these modifications.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekLars Eggertdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtTexas A&M UniversitydnamemS. Bhandarkar¢kaffiliationtTexas A&M UniversitydnamenA. L. N. Reddy¢kaffiliationiICIR/ICSIdnameiM. Allman¢kaffiliationqPurdue UniversitydnamejE. Blantonddatej”´hfilenamegrfc4653eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126573hkeywordsÉcncrxnon-congestion robustnessxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜$ßgranking-crfcd4653irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Improving the Robustness of TCP to Non-Congestion EventsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%The Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) suite of protocols has been defined to control different switching technologies as well as different applications. These include support for requesting TDM connections including Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) and Optical Transport Networks (OTNs).

This document provides an evaluation of the IETF Routing Protocols against the routing requirements for an Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON) as defined by ITU-T. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationeCienadnamefL. Ong¢kaffiliationgTellabsdnameiJ. Sadler¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamegS. Shew¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wardddatej”¨hfilenamegrfc4652eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126572hkeywordsâegmplsx)generalized multiprotocol label switchingcotnxoptical transport networksesonetcsdhxsynchronous optical networkxsynchronous digital hierarchyeitu-tkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEGﬁigranking,crfcd4652irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoEvaluation of Existing Routing Protocols against Automatic Switched Optical Network (ASON) Routing RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractySThis document describes and evaluates strategies to enhance Mobile IPv6 Route Optimization, on the basis of existing proposals, in order to motivate and guide further research in this context.  This document is a product of the IP Mobility Optimizations (MobOpts) Research Group.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxUniversitaet Karlsruhe (TH)dnamegC. Vogt¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamehJ. Arkkoddatej”≤hfilenamegrfc4651eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmoboptsdfullx2mobopts - IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdnamex(IP Mobility Optimizations Research Groupdtypebrgbidjdoc-126571hkeywordsìkMobile IPv6rRoute OptimizationkEnhancementhMobilitygHandoffpIP Address TestsqProtected TunnelssOptimistic BehaviorxProactive IP Address Testsx Concurrent Care-of Address TestspDiverted RoutingxCredit-Based AuthorizationtHeuristic MonitoringxCrypto-Based IdentifiersqPre-Configurationx$Semi-Permanent Security AssociationsjDelegationoMobile NetworkspLocation PrivacykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE àgranking+crfcd4651irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIRTFdslugdirtfetitlexIA Taxonomy and Analysis of Enhancements to Mobile IPv6 Route OptimizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document describes a lightweight point-to-point key management protocol variant for the multimedia Internet keying (MIKEY) protocol MIKEY, as defined in RFC 3830.  In particular, this variant deploys the classic Diffie-Hellman key agreement protocol for key establishment featuring perfect forward secrecy in conjunction with a keyed hash message authentication code for achieving mutual authentication and message integrity of the key management messages exchanged.  This protocol addresses the security and performance constraints of multimedia key management in MIKEY. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Euchnerddatej”Thfilenamegrfc4650eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126570hkeywordsÜrMulticast securityeMIKEYnkey managementnDiffie-Hellmanmkey agreementdHMACkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE‡·granking*crfcd4650irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHHMAC-Authenticated Diffie-Hellman for Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This memo defines a new Relay Agent Remote-ID option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6).  This option is the DHCPv6 equivalent for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv4 (DHCPv4) Relay Agent Option's Remote-ID suboption as specified in RFC 3046. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. VolzddatejÀéhfilenamegrfc4649eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126569hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÂÏgranking)crfcd4649irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRDynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) Relay Agent Remote-ID OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This document describes the commonly used base 64, base 32, and base 16 encoding schemes.  It also discusses the use of line-feeds in encoded data, use of padding in encoded data, use of non-alphabet characters in encoded data, use of different encoding alphabets, and canonical encodings. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSJDdnamelS. Josefssonddatej”Øhfilenamegrfc4648eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126568hkeywordsÜgschemesddatajline-feedsialphabetsmbase encodingchexkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE4+•granking(crfcd4648irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data EncodingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¡This document describes a syntax, called a "language-range", for specifying items in a user's list of language preferences.  It also describes different mechanisms for comparing and matching these to language tags.  Two kinds of matching mechanisms, filtering and lookup, are defined.  Filtering produces a (potentially empty) set of language tags, whereas lookup produces a single language tag.  Possible applications include language negotiation or content selection.  This document, in combination with RFC 4646, replaces RFC 3066, which replaced RFC 1766.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkYahoo! Inc.dnamekA. Phillips¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehM. Davisddatej”∑hfilenamegrfc4647eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdltrudfullx#ltru - Language Tag Registry UpdatednamexLanguage Tag Registry Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-126567hkeywordsÅhLang-TagkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÚ}granking'crfcd4647irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber/etotaletitlexMatching of Language TagsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document describes the structure, content, construction, and semantics of language tags for use in cases where it is desirable to indicate the language used in an information object.  It also describes how to register values for use in language tags and the creation of user-defined extensions for private interchange.  This document, in combination with RFC 4647, replaces RFC 3066, which replaced RFC 1766.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkYahoo! Inc.dnamekA. Phillips¢kaffiliationfGooglednamehM. Davisddatej—6hfilenamegrfc4646eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdltrudfullx#ltru - Language Tag Registry UpdatednamexLanguage Tag Registry Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-126566hkeywordsÅhLang-TagkobsoletedByÅd5646epages;opublicationDateEÚggranking&crfcd4646irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTags for Identifying LanguagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThis memo defined the initial contents of the IANA Language Subtag Registry for use in forming tags for the identification of languages.  Since the contents of this memo only served as a starting point for the registry, its actual contents have been removed before publication to avoid confusion.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehD. Ewellddatej—6hfilenamegrfc4645eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdltrudfullx#ltru - Language Tag Registry UpdatednamexLanguage Tag Registry Updatedtypebwgbidjdoc-126565hkeywordsÅdianakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEÚWgranking%crfcd4645irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Initial Language Subtag RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis memo defines an extension to the Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) to provide asynchronous (otherwise known as "streaming") transfer of articles. This allows servers to transfer articles to other servers with much greater efficiency.

This document updates and formalizes the CHECK and TAKETHIS commands specified in RFC 2980 and deprecates the MODE STREAM command. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrCornell UniversitydnamejJ. Vinocur¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamelK. Murchisonddatej”Lhfilenamegrfc4644eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnntpextdfullxnntpext - NNTP ExtensionsdnameoNNTP Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126564hkeywordsÉecheckhtakethiskmode streamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@/granking$crfcd4644irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCNetwork News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) Extension for Streaming FeedsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÄThis document defines an extension to the Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) that allows a client to indicate an authentication mechanism to the server, to perform an authentication protocol exchange, and optionally to negotiate a security layer for subsequent protocol interactions during the remainder of an NNTP session.

This document updates and formalizes the AUTHINFO USER/PASS authentication method specified in RFC 2980 and deprecates the AUTHINFO SIMPLE and AUTHINFO GENERIC authentication methods. Additionally, this document defines a profile of the Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) for NNTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrCornell UniversitydnamejJ. Vinocur¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamelK. Murchisonddatej”~hfilenamegrfc4643eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgnntpextdfullxnntpext - NNTP ExtensionsdnameoNNTP Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126563hkeywordsÖrauthinfo user/passoauthinfo simplepauthinfo genericdsaslx(simple authentication and security layerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@Ggranking#crfcd4643irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBNetwork News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) Extension for AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines an extension to the Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) that allows an NNTP client and server to use Transport Layer Security (TLS).  The primary goal is to provide encryption for single-link confidentiality purposes, but data integrity, (optional) certificate-based peer entity authentication, and (optional) data compression are also possible. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamelK. Murchison¢kaffiliationrCornell UniversitydnamejJ. Vinocur¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newmanddatej”hfilenamegrfc4642eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgnntpextdfullxnntpext - NNTP ExtensionsdnameoNNTP Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126562hkeywordsÇjencryptionxsingle link confidentialitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@Tgranking"crfcd4642irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOUsing Transport Layer Security (TLS) with Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8143d8996∂habstractyIThis document describes a set of practices for operating the DNS with security extensions (DNSSEC). The target audience is zone administrators deploying DNSSEC.

The document discusses operational aspects of using keys and signatures in the DNS. It discusses issues of key generation, key storage, signature generation, key rollover, and related policies.

This document obsoletes RFC 2541, as it covers more operational ground and gives more up-to-date requirements with respect to key sizes and the new DNSSEC specification. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnameiR. Giebenddatej”¢hfilenamegrfc4641eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126561hkeywordsàcdnsqdomain name spacessecurity extensionsrzone administratorgDNS-SOCjcryptologypresource recordscrrskobsoletedByÅd6781epages#opublicationDateE	›Égranking!crfcd4641irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDNSSEC Operational PracticesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·A mobile node needs at least the following information: a home address, a home agent address, and a security association with home agent to register with the home agent.  The process of obtaining this information is called bootstrapping.  This document discusses issues involved with how the mobile node can be bootstrapped for Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) and various potential deployment scenarios for mobile node bootstrapping.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehA. Patel¢kaffiliationnTelecom ItaliadnamekG. Giarettaddatej”≥hfilenamegrfc4640eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126560hkeywordsÇxinternet protocol version 6kmobile nodekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZ8granking crfcd4640irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Problem Statement for bootstrapping Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. In particular, it defines a basic set of managed objects for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)-based management of Data Over Cable Service Interface Specification (DOCSIS)-compliant Cable Modems and Cable Modem Termination Systems.

This memo obsoletes RFC 2669. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednameiR. Woundy¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamehK. Marezddatej”°hfilenamegrfc4639eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-126559hkeywordsÇdsnmpx"simple network management protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateEåTçgrankingcrfcd4639irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex†Cable Device Management Information Base for Data-Over-Cable Service Interface Specification (DOCSIS) Compliant Cable Modems and Cable Modem Termination SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractyÇThe Point-to-Point Protocol over Ethernet (PPPoE), as described in RFC 2516, mandates a maximum negotiated Maximum Receive Unit (MRU) of 1492.  This document outlines a solution that relaxes this restriction and allows a maximum negotiated MRU greater than 1492 to minimize fragmentation in next-generation broadband networks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiP. Arberg¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameoD. Kourkouzelis¢kaffiliationiBellSouthdnamejM. Duckett¢kaffiliationiBellSouthdnamekT. Anschutz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejJ. MoisandddatejÀ˚hfilenamegrfc4638eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126558hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateEt6grankingcrfcd4638irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäAccommodating a Maximum Transit Unit/Maximum Receive Unit (MTU/MRU) Greater Than 1492 in the Point-to-Point Protocol over Ethernet (PPPoE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This document specifies a new extension for use by Foreign Agents operating Mobile IP for IPv4.  Currently, a foreign agent cannot supply status information without destroying the ability for a mobile node to verify authentication data supplied by the home agent.  The new extension solves this problem by making a better place for the foreign agent to provide its status information to the mobile node. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”¢hfilenamegrfc4636eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126557hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE,+§grankingcrfcd4636irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Foreign Agent Error Extension for Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUse of the Domain Name System TSIG resource record requires specification of a cryptographic message authentication code.  Currently, identifiers have been specified only for HMAC MD5 (Hashed Message Authentication Code, Message Digest 5) and GSS (Generic Security Service) TSIG algorithms.  This document standardizes identifiers and implementation requirements for additional HMAC SHA (Secure Hash Algorithm) TSIG algorithms and standardizes how to specify and handle the truncation of HMAC values in TSIG. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej”¢hfilenamegrfc4635eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126556hkeywordsÖcdnsoresource recordbrrx)cryptographic message authentication codedcmackobsoletedByÅd8945epagesopublicationDateD˜gÂgrankingcrfcd4635irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_HMAC SHA (Hashed Message Authentication Code, Secure Hash Algorithm) TSIG Algorithm IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThe United States of America has adopted a suite of Secure Hash Algorithms (SHAs), including four beyond SHA-1, as part of a Federal Information Processing Standard (FIPS), specifically SHA-224 (RFC 3874), SHA-256, SHA-384, and SHA-512. The purpose of this document is to make source code performing these hash functions conveniently available to the Internet community. The sample code supports input strings of arbitrary bit length. SHA-1's sample code from RFC 3174 has also been updated to handle input strings of arbitrary bit length. Most of the text herein was adapted by the authors from FIPS 180-2.

Code to perform SHA-based HMACs, with arbitrary bit length text, is also included. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmMotorola LabsdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiT. Hansenddatej”ñhfilenamegrfc4634eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126555hkeywordsÜdfipsx'federal information processing standardgsha-224gsha-256gsha-384gsha-512kobsoletedByÅd6234epageslopublicationDateDŒ˛%grankingcrfcd4634irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,US Secure Hash Algorithms (SHA and HMAC-SHA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–Discussion in the community has begun to question whether RFC 3683 and RFC 3934 provide the appropriate flexibility for managing Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) mailing lists.  This document is an RFC 3933 experiment designed to allow the community to experiment with a broader set of tools for mailing list management while trying to determine what the long-term guidelines should be.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejS. HartmanddatejÀ¸hfilenamegrfc4633eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126554hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÏöügrankingcrfcd4633irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Experiment in Long-Term Suspensions From Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) Mailing ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8717∑habstractynThis memo discusses the strategy for address assignment of the existing 32-bit IPv4 address space with a view toward conserving the address space and limiting the growth rate of global routing state.  This document obsoletes the original Classless Inter-domain Routing (CIDR) spec in RFC 1519, with changes made both to clarify the concepts it introduced and, after more than twelve years, to update the Internet community on the results of deploying the technology described.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiV. Fuller¢kaffiliationoTropos NetworksdnameeT. Liddatej”∞hfilenamegrfc4632eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126553hkeywordsÇiCIDR-STRAtglobal routing statekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜g›grankingcrfcd4632irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberzetotaletitlex[Classless Inter-domain Routing (CIDR): The Internet Address Assignment and Aggregation PlandtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=This document provides minor corrections to and obsoletes RFC 4327.

