and other parties who desire information contained in the TCP-related RFCs.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtBoeing Phantom WorksdnamegM. Duke¢kaffiliationx"USC Information Sciences InstitutednameiR. Braden¢kaffiliationxVerizon Federal Network SystemsdnamegW. Eddy¢kaffiliationx"Purdue University Computer SciencednamejE. BlantonddatejÀÒhfilenamegrfc4614eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126535hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7414epages!opublicationDateEt.grankingcrfcd4614irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIA Roadmap for Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) Specification DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6247∂habstractxÇThis document serves to register a media type for Downloadable Sounds.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiP. Frojdh¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekU. Lindgren¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej”®hfilenamegrfc4613eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126534hkeywordsÅcdlskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE—˜grankingcrfcd4613irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Media Type Registrations for Downloadable Sounds for Musical Instrument Digital Interface (MIDI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the MIME sub-type audio/t38.  The usage of this MIME type, which is intended for use within Session Description Protocol (SDP), is specified within ITU-T Recommendation T.38.  This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Jones¢kaffiliationsRicoh Company, LTD.dnameiH. Tamuraddatej”§hfilenamegrfc4612eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126533hkeywordsÉxitu-t recommendation t.38csdpxsession description protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDŸrÆgrankingcrfcd4612irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAReal-Time Facsimile (T.38) - audio/t38 MIME Sub-type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyhThis document describes best current practices for intra-domain and inter-domain deployment of the Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP) in conjunction with Protocol Independent Multicast Sparse Mode (PIM-SM).  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. McBride¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Meylor¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyerddatej—Dhfilenamegrfc4611eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-126532hkeywordsÇfpim-smx*protocol independent multicast sparse modekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜h0grankingcrfcd4611irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberyetotaletitlex?Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP) Deployment ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJThis specification allows Anycast-RP (Rendezvous Point) to be used inside a domain that runs Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) only.  Other multicast protocols (such as Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP), which has been used traditionally to solve this problem) are not required to support Anycast-RP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefY. Caiddatej”Rhfilenamegrfc4610eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-126531hkeywordsÖprendezvous pointbrpmmsdp registerxmulticast source discoverymregister-stopkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜h(grankingcrfcd4610irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Anycast-RP Using Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis memo describes security threats for the larger (intra-domain or inter-domain) multicast routing infrastructures.  Only Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) is analyzed, in its three main operational modes: the traditional Any-Source Multicast (ASM) model, the source-specific multicast (SSM) model, and the ASM model enhanced by the Embedded Rendezvous Point (Embedded-RP) group-to-RP mapping mechanism.  This memo also describes enhancements to the protocol operations that mitigate the identified threats.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamekR. Lehtonen¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyerddatej—9hfilenamegrfc4609eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-126530hkeywordsâpsecurity threatslintra-domainlinter-domaintany-source multicastcasmxsource-specific multicastcssmxembedded rendezvous pointkembedded-rpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE0I+grankingcrfcd4609irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhProtocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) Multicast Routing Security Issues and EnhancementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty‚IP Multicast group addresses in the 232/8 (232.0.0.0 to 232.255.255.255) range are designated as source-specific multicast destination addresses and are reserved for use by source-specific multicast applications and protocols.  This document defines operational recommendations to ensure source-specific behavior within the 232/8 range.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Rockell¢kaffiliation`dnamekG. Shepherdddatej—Dhfilenamegrfc4608eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-126529hkeywordsÇbipcssmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜h!granking␀crfcd4608irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberxetotaletitlex7Source-Specific Protocol Independent Multicast in 232/8dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIP version 4 (IPv4) addresses in the 232/8 (232.0.0.0 to 232.255.255.255) range are designated as source-specific multicast (SSM) destination addresses and are reserved for use by source-specific applications and protocols.  For IP version 6 (IPv6), the address prefix FF3x::/32 is reserved for source-specific multicast use.  This document defines an extension to the Internet network service that applies to datagrams sent to SSM addresses and defines the host and router requirements to support this extension. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmArastra, Inc.dnamekH. Holbrook¢kaffiliationoAcopia NetworksdnamegB. Cainddatej”Éhfilenamegrfc4607eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcssmdfullxssm - Source-Specific MulticastdnamexSource-Specific Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-126528hkeywordsÉdipv4cssmdipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜hgrankingˇcrfcd4607irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Source-Specific Multicast for IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThis document provides minor clarification to RFC 3946.

This document is a companion to the Generalized Multi-protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) signaling. It defines the Synchronous Optical Network (SONET)/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) technology-specific information needed when GMPLS signaling is used. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhPercevaldnameiE. Mannie¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. PapadimitriouddatejÀ˝hfilenamegrfc4606eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126527hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÏö granking˛crfcd4606irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexïGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Extensions for Synchronous Optical Network (SONET) and Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6344∂habstracty†In certain topologies, it is not necessary to run a multicast routing protocol.  It is sufficient for a device to learn and proxy group membership information and simply forward multicast packets based upon that information.  This document describes a mechanism for forwarding based solely upon Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) or Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) membership information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmAT&T ResearchdnameiB. Fenner¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameeH. He¢kaffiliationgJHU-APLdnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationxLittle River Elementary SchooldnamejH. Sandickddatej»¶hfilenamegrfc4605eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-126526hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜hgranking˝crfcd4605irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInternet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) / Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD)-Based Multicast Forwarding ("IGMP/MLD Proxying")dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Internet Group Management Protocol Version 3 (IGMPv3) and the Multicast Listener Discovery Protocol Version 2 (MLDv2) are protocols that allow a host to inform its neighboring routers of its desire to receive IPv4 and IPv6 multicast transmissions, respectively.  Source-specific multicast (SSM) is a form of multicast in which a receiver is required to specify both the network-layer address of the source and the multicast destination address in order to receive the multicast transmission.  This document defines the notion of an "SSM-aware" router and host, and clarifies and (in some cases) modifies the behavior of IGMPv3 and MLDv2 on SSM-aware routers and hosts to accommodate source-specific multicast.  This document updates the IGMPv3 and MLDv2 specifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmArastra, Inc.dnamekH. Holbrook¢kaffiliationoAcopia NetworksdnamegB. Cain¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. Habermanddatej—Jhfilenamegrfc4604eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-126525hkeywordsÅcssmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜hgranking¸crfcd4604irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexïUsing Internet Group Management Protocol Version 3 (IGMPv3) and Multicast Listener Discovery Protocol Version 2 (MLDv2) for Source-Specific MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxâThis document defines a set of values for the NAS-Port-Type RADIUS Attribute.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehG. Weber¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Foltakddatej”úhfilenamegrfc4603eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126524hkeywordsÇfradiusx*Remote Authentication Dial-In User ServicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD Æµgranking˚crfcd4603irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex1Additional Values for the NAS-Port-Type AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıThis document provides supporting documentation to advance the Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) routing protocol from IETF Experimental status to Proposed Standard.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekT. PusateriddatejÀÏhfilenamegrfc4602eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-126523hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜gˇgranking˙crfcd4602irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbProtocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) IETF Proposed Standard Requirements AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document specifies Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM). PIM-SM is a multicast routing protocol that can use the underlying unicast routing information base or a separate multicast-capable routing information base. It builds unidirectional shared trees rooted at a Rendezvous Point (RP) per group, and optionally creates shortest-path trees per source.

This document obsoletes RFC 2362, an Experimental version of PIM-SM. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnameiB. Fenner¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationgArastradnamekH. Holbrook¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekI. Kouvelasddatej”£hfilenamegrfc4601eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-126522hkeywordsÜfPIM-SMgroutinggmessagedtypeftimerseflagskobsoletedByÅd7761epagesñopublicationDateD˜g˜granking˘crfcd4601irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWProtocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM): Protocol Specification (Revised)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd5796d6226d5059∂habstractyCThis document describes a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for transporting Enhanced AC-3 (E-AC-3) encoded audio data.  E-AC-3 is a high-quality, multichannel audio coding format and is an extension of the AC-3 audio coding format, which is used in US High-Definition Television (HDTV), DVD, cable and satellite television, and other media.  E-AC-3 is an optional audio format in US and world wide digital television and high-definition DVD formats.  The RTP payload format as presented in this document includes support for data fragmentation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrDolby LaboratoriesdnamegB. Linkddatej”≥hfilenamegrfc4598eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126521hkeywordsÇrencoded audio datax multichannel audio coding formatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD ÆØgrankingˆcrfcd4598irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRReal-time Transport Protocol (RTP) Payload Format for Enhanced AC-3 (E-AC-3) AudiodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document describes multimedia conferencing scenarios.  It describes both basic and advanced conferencing scenarios involving voice, video, text, and interactive text sessions.  These scenarios will help with the definition and evaluation of the protocols being developed in the centralized conferencing XCON working group.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiN. Ismailddatej”Éhfilenamegrfc4597eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126520hkeywordsÜjmultimediaevoiceevideodtextpinteractive textdxconkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÂÙgrankingıcrfcd4597irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevConferencing ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document contains guidelines for usage of the Caller Preferences Extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  It demonstrates the benefits of caller preferences with specific example applications, provides use cases to show proper operation, provides guidance on the applicability of the registered feature tags, and describes a straightforward implementation of the preference and capability matching algorithm specified in Section 7.2 of RFC 3841.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. KyzivatddatejÀ¡hfilenamegrfc4596eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126519hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateD∆ôÍgrankingÙcrfcd4596irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZGuidelines for Usage of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Caller Preferences ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis document describes the use of IKEv2 to negotiate security protocols and transforms for Fibre Channel as part of the Fibre Channel Security Association Management Protocol.  This usage requires that IKEv2 be extended with Fibre-Channel-specific security protocols, transforms, and name types.  This document specifies these IKEv2 extensions and allocates identifiers for them.  Using new IKEv2 identifiers for Fibre Channel security protocols avoids any possible confusion between IKEv2 negotiation for IP networks and IKEv2 negotiation for Fibre Channel.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Maino¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehD. Blackddatej”Çhfilenamegrfc4595eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126518hkeywordsÅuinternet key exchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD»ÙgrankingÛcrfcd4595irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJUse of IKEv2 in the Fibre Channel Security Association Management ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document describes service classes configured with Diffserv and recommends how they can be used and how to construct them using Differentiated Services Code Points (DSCPs), traffic conditioners, Per-Hop Behaviors (PHBs), and Active Queue Management (AQM) mechanisms.  There is no intrinsic requirement that particular DSCPs, traffic conditioners, PHBs, and AQM be used for a certain service class, but as a policy and for interoperability it is useful to apply them consistently.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejJ. Babiarz¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Bakerddatej”ühfilenamegrfc4594eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126517hkeywordsáx#differentiated services code pointsttraffic conditionersqper-hop behaviorscphbddscpwactive queue managementcaqmkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateDÏö>grankingÚcrfcd4594irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Configuration Guidelines for DiffServ Service ClassesdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5865d8622∂habstracty◊Routing protocols are subject to attacks that can harm individual users or network operations as a whole.  This document provides a description and a summary of generic threats that affect routing protocols in general.  This work describes threats, including threat sources and capabilities, threat actions, and threat consequences, as well as a breakdown of routing functions that might be attacked separately.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiA. Barbir¢kaffiliationlSparta, Inc.dnameiS. Murphy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegY. Yangddatej—Ghfilenamegrfc4593eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymerpsecdfullx.rpsec - Routing Protocol Security Requirementsdnamex&Routing Protocol Security Requirementsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126516hkeywordsÑnthreat sourcesqthreat capabilitymthreat actionsthreat consequenceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE0I&grankingÒcrfcd4593irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Generic Threats to Routing ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This is an update to the wildcard definition of RFC 1034.  The interaction with wildcards and CNAME is changed, an error condition is removed, and the words defining some concepts central to wildcards are changed.  The overall goal is not to change wildcards, but to refine the definition of RFC 1034. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamehE. Lewisddatej”Yhfilenamegrfc4592eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126515hkeywordsÅecnamekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD∆ô‚grankingcrfcd4592irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/The Role of Wildcards in the Domain Name SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThe Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol, Version 3, (L2TPv3) defines a protocol for tunneling a variety of data link protocols over IP networks.  This document describes the specifics of how to tunnel Frame Relay over L2TPv3, including frame encapsulation, virtual-circuit creation and deletion, and status change notification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Townsley¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiG. Wilkie¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehS. Booth¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliation`dnamefJ. Lauddatej”~hfilenamegrfc4591eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126514hkeywordsÑsdata link protocolssframe encapsulationx%virtual-circuit creation and deletionxstatus change notificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDŒ˝ÌgrankingÔcrfcd4591irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Frame Relay over Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3 (L2TPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5641∂habstractx˚This document defines an extension to the Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) protocol to enable support of Digest Authentication, for use with HTTP-style protocols like the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and HTTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoKayote NetworksdnamejB. Sterman¢kaffiliationnSecureOL, Inc.dnamemD. Sadolevsky¢kaffiliationoKayote NetworksdnamekD. Schwartz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekD. Williams¢kaffiliationsDeutsche Telekom AGdnamegW. Beckddatej”≤hfilenamegrfc4590eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126513hkeywordsÉx*remote authentication dial-in user servicecsipdhttpkobsoletedByÅd5090epages opublicationDateD±K.grankingÓcrfcd4590irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*RADIUS Extension for Digest AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThis document creates a registry for describing the types of places a human or end system might be found.  The registry is then referenced by other protocols that need a common set of location terms as protocol constants.  Examples of location terms defined in this document include aircraft, office, and train station. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemH. TschofenigddatejÀ˝hfilenamegrfc4589eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-126512hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD©ÆgrankingÌcrfcd4589irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewLocation Types RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9RTP retransmission is an effective packet loss recovery technique for real-time applications with relaxed delay bounds.  This document describes an RTP payload format for performing retransmissions.  Retransmitted RTP packets are sent in a separate stream from the original RTP stream.  It is assumed that feedback from receivers to senders is available.  In particular, it is assumed that Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP) feedback as defined in the extended RTP profile for RTCP-based feedback (denoted RTP/AVPF) is available in this memo. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamefJ. Rey¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegD. Leon¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamekA. Miyazaki¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehV. Varsa¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamelR. Hakenbergddatej”Çhfilenamegrfc4588eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126511hkeywordsÑxreal time transport protocoldrtcpx$real-time transport control protocolhRTP/AVPFkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateD∆ôŸgrankingÏcrfcd4588irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!RTP Retransmission Payload FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∏This memo describes a scheme to packetize an H.261 video stream for transport using the Real-time Transport Protocol, RTP, with any of the underlying protocols that carry RTP.

The memo also describes the syntax and semantics of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) parameters needed to support the H.261 video codec. A media type registration is included for this payload format.

This specification obsoletes RFC 2032. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamegR. Evenddatej”ühfilenamegrfc4587eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126510hkeywordsÑiRTP-H.261xreal-time transport protocolcsdpxsession description protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÏö)grankingÎcrfcd4587irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*RTP Payload Format for H.261 Video StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the results achieved when simulating the timing rules of the Extended RTP Profile for Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP)-Based Feedback, denoted AVPF.  Unicast and multicast topologies are considered as well as several protocol and environment configurations.  The results show that the timing rules result in better performance regarding feedback delay and still preserve the well-accepted RTP rules regarding allowed bit rates for control traffic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamemC. Burmeister¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamelR. Hakenberg¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamekA. Miyazaki¢kaffiliationx!Helsinki University of TechnologydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationcOkidnamegN. Sato¢kaffiliationcOkidnamekS. Fukunagaddatej”Phfilenamegrfc4586eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126509hkeywordsÅxReal-time Transport ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDæsƒgrankingÍcrfcd4586irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Extended RTP Profile for Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP)-Based Feedback: Results of the Timing Rule SimulationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3Real-time media streams that use RTP are, to some degree, resilient against packet losses.  Receivers may use the base mechanisms of the Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP) to report packet reception statistics and thus allow a sender to adapt its transmission behavior in the mid-term.  This is the sole means for feedback and feedback-based error repair (besides a few codec-specific mechanisms).  This document defines an extension to the Audio-visual Profile (AVP) that enables receivers to provide, statistically, more immediate feedback to the senders and thus allows for short-term adaptation and efficient feedback-based repair mechanisms to be implemented.  This early feedback profile (AVPF) maintains the AVP bandwidth constraints for RTCP and preserves scalability to large groups. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx!Helsinki University of TechnologydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiS. Wenger¢kaffiliationcOkidnamegN. Sato¢kaffiliationjMatsushitadnamemC. Burmeister¢kaffiliationjMatsushitadnamefJ. Reyddatej”Qhfilenamegrfc4585eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126508hkeywordsÉlmedia streamtfeedback based errortaudio visual profilekobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateDæsægrankingÈcrfcd4585irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Extended RTP Profile for Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP)-Based Feedback (RTP/AVPF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8108d5506∂habstractyÆThis document describes data structures and API support for Mobile IPv6 as an extension to the Advanced Socket API for IPv6.

Just as the Advanced Sockets API for IPv6 gives access to various extension headers and the ICMPv6 protocol, this document specifies the same level of access for Mobile IPv6 components. It specifies a mechanism for applications to retrieve and set information for Mobility Header messages, Home Address destination options, and Routing Header Type 2 extension headers. It also specifies the common data structures and definitions that might be used by certain advanced Mobile IPv6 socket applications. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamenS. Chakrabarti¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamekE. Nordmarkddatej”•hfilenamegrfc4584eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126507hkeywordsÖsadvanced socket apixmobility header messageswhom address destinationurouting header type 2ssocket applicationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD∏3grankingËcrfcd4584irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Extension to Sockets API for Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies how to describe Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP) streams in Session Description Protocol (SDP) descriptions.  User agents using the offer/answer model to establish BFCP streams use this format in their offers and answers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”ãhfilenamegrfc4583eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126506hkeywordsÅkbfcp streamkobsoletedByÅd8856epagesopublicationDateEdÄgrankingÁcrfcd4583irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZSession Description Protocol (SDP) Format for Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP) StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFloor control is a means to manage joint or exclusive access to shared resources in a (multiparty) conferencing environment. Thereby, floor control complements other functions -- such as conference and media session setup, conference policy manipulation, and media control -- that are realized by other protocols.

This document specifies the Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP). BFCP is used between floor participants and floor control servers, and between floor chairs (i.e., moderators) and floor control servers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamevCullen Fluffy Jenningsdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationx!Helsinki University of TechnologydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamehK. Drageddatej”åhfilenamegrfc4582eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126505hkeywordsÅjconferencekobsoletedByÅd8855epagesAopublicationDateEd¸grankingÊcrfcd4582irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(The Binary Floor Control Protocol (BFCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractxùThis document defines a Type-Length-Value format for Cryptographically Generated Address (CGA) Extensions.  This document updates RFC 3972. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdUC3MdnamejM. Bagnulo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej”ßhfilenamegrfc4581eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126504hkeywordsÅctlvkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE@ÊgrankingÂcrfcd4581irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBCryptographically Generated Addresses (CGA) Extension Field FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis memo defines a new Relay Agent Subscriber-ID option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6).  The option allows a DHCPv6 relay agent to associate a stable "Subscriber-ID" with DHCPv6 client messages in a way that is independent of the client and of the underlying physical network infrastructure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. VolzddatejÀÌhfilenamegrfc4580eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126503hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD†2Ágranking‰crfcd4580irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVDynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) Relay Agent Subscriber-ID OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÚThis specification defines conferencing call control features for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  This document builds on the Conferencing Requirements and Framework documents to define how a tightly coupled SIP conference works.  The approach is explored from the perspective of different user agent (UA) types: conference-unaware, conference-aware, and focus UAs.  The use of Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) in conferencing, OPTIONS for capabilities discovery, and call control using REFER are covered in detail with example call flow diagrams.  The usage of the isfocus feature tag is defined.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levinddatej”khfilenamegrfc4579eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126502hkeywordsÑbuarconference-unawarepconference-awareefocuskobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateD˜g”granking„crfcd4579irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberwetotaletitlexMSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Call Control - Conferencing for User AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑We define Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) options being used by Preboot eXecution Environment (PXE) and Extensible Firmware Interface (EFI) clients to uniquely identify booting client machines and their pre-OS runtime environment so that the DHCP and/or PXE boot server can return the correct OS bootstrap image (or pre-boot application) name and server to the client.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamekM. Johnston¢kaffiliationgUNINETTdnameiS. Venaasddatej”¶hfilenamegrfc4578eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126501hkeywordsÇcefixextensible firmware interfacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEZ]@granking‚crfcd4578irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Options for the Intel Preboot eXecution Environment (PXE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-Many Service Providers offer Virtual Private Network (VPN) services to their customers, using a technique in which customer edge routers (CE routers) are routing peers of provider edge routers (PE routers). The Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is used to distribute the customer's routes across the provider's IP backbone network, and Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) is used to tunnel customer packets across the provider's backbone. This is known as a "BGP/MPLS IP VPN". The base specification for BGP/MPLS IP VPNs presumes that the routing protocol on the interface between a PE router and a CE router is BGP. This document extends that specification by allowing the routing protocol on the PE/CE interface to be the Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) protocol.

This document updates RFC 4364. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameqP. Pillay-Esnaultddatej”ùhfilenamegrfc4577eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126500hkeywordsÑbcexopen shortest path firstdmplsxMultiprotocol Label SwitchingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD†2…granking·crfcd4577irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[OSPF as the Provider/Customer Edge Protocol for BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This document specifies a procedure that deals with a particular issue that may arise when a Service Provider (SP) provides "BGP/MPLS IP VPN" service to a customer and the customer uses OSPFv2 to advertise its routes to the SP.  In this situation, a Customer Edge (CE) Router and a Provider Edge (PE) Router are OSPF peers, and customer routes are sent via OSPFv2 from the CE to the PE.  The customer routes are converted into BGP routes, and BGP carries them across the backbone to other PE routers.  The routes are then converted back to OSPF routes sent via OSPF to other CE routers.  As a result of this conversion, some of the information needed to prevent loops may be lost.  A procedure is needed to ensure that once a route is sent from a PE to a CE, the route will be ignored by any PE that receives it back from a CE.  This document specifies the necessary procedure, using one of the options bits in the LSA (Link State Advertisements) to indicate that an LSA has already been forwarded by a PE and should be ignored by any other PEs that see it. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiP. Psenak¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameqP. Pillay-Esnaultddatej—Vhfilenamegrfc4576eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126499hkeywordsÖpservice providerbspmprovider edgebpedOSPFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD†2¢granking‡crfcd4576irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextUsing a Link State Advertisement (LSA) Options Bit to Prevent Looping in BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‡This document defines a conference event package for tightly coupled conferences using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) events framework, along with a data format used in notifications for this package.  The conference package allows users to subscribe to a conference Uniform Resource Identifier (URI).  Notifications are sent about changes in the membership of this conference and optionally about changes in the state of additional conference components. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levinddatej”ohfilenamegrfc4575eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126498hkeywordsÉxconference event packagecurixuniform resource identifierkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateD˜g grankingﬂcrfcd4575irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Event Package for Conference StatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√This document defines a new Session Description Protocol (SDP) media-level attribute: "label".  The "label" attribute carries a pointer to a media stream in the context of an arbitrary network application that uses SDP.  The sender of the SDP document can attach the "label" attribute to a particular media stream or streams.  The application can then use the provided pointer to refer to each particular media stream in its context. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levin¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”jhfilenamegrfc4574eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126497hkeywordsÇumedia level attributelmedia streamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜g¡grankingﬁcrfcd4574irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6The Session Description Protocol (SDP) Label AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîIn conversational video applications, far-end camera control protocol is used by participants to control the remote camera.  The protocol that is commonly used is ITU H.281 over H.224.  The document registers the H224 media type.  It defines the syntax and the semantics of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) parameters needed to support far-end camera control protocol using H.224. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamegR. Even¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamekA. Lochbaumddatej”§hfilenamegrfc4573eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126496hkeywordsÑxreal time transport protocoliitu h.281eh.224vfar-end camera controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDæs∏granking›crfcd4573irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7MIME Type Registration for RTP Payload Format for H.224dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This document specifies how to establish secure connection-oriented media transport sessions over the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol using the Session Description Protocol (SDP). It defines a new SDP protocol identifier, 'TCP/TLS'. It also defines the syntax and semantics for an SDP 'fingerprint' attribute that identifies the certificate that will be presented for the TLS session. This mechanism allows media transport over TLS connections to be established securely, so long as the integrity of session descriptions is assured.

This document extends and updates RFC 4145. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnameiJ. Lennoxddatej”úhfilenamegrfc4572eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126495hkeywordsÉesetupjconnectionoreestablishmentkobsoletedByÅd8122epagesopublicationDateDæs∞granking‹crfcd4572irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~Connection-Oriented Media Transport over the Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a method for framing Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) packets onto connection-oriented transport (such as TCP).  The memo also defines how session descriptions may specify RTP streams that use the framing method. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamejJ. Lazzaroddatej”~hfilenamegrfc4571eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126494hkeywordsÉxTCP-based media transportjTCP tunnelxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateDæs´granking€crfcd4571irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuFraming Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) and RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) Packets over Connection-Oriented TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how to adapt the Session Description Protocol (SDP) to express one or more source addresses as a source filter for one or more destination "connection" addresses.  It defines the syntax and semantics for an SDP "source-filter" attribute that may reference either IPv4 or IPv6 address(es) as either an inclusive or exclusive source list for either multicast or unicast destinations.  In particular, an inclusive source-filter can be used to specify a Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) session. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmBoxnArrow.comdnamehB. Quinn¢kaffiliationsLive Networks, Inc.dnamelR. Finlaysonddatej”Ähfilenamegrfc4570eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126493hkeywordsÖqinternet protocolbipmsource-filtercssmxsource-specific multicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDæs•granking⁄crfcd4570irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Session Description Protocol (SDP) Source FiltersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis document registers with the IANA a new media feature tag associated with the 'message' media type.  This media feature tag indicates that a particular device supports 'message' as a streaming media type.  Media feature tags can be used to route calls to devices that support certain features.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. CamarilloddatejÀ¶hfilenamegrfc4569eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126492hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDæsûgrankingŸcrfcd4569irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWInternet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) Registration of the Message Media Feature TagdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document defines a Session Description Protocol (SDP) cryptographic attribute for unicast media streams.  The attribute describes a cryptographic key and other parameters that serve to configure security for a unicast media stream in either a single message or a roundtrip exchange.  The attribute can be used with a variety of SDP media transports, and this document defines how to use it for the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) unicast media streams.  The SDP crypto attribute requires the services of a data security protocol to secure the SDP message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Baugher¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wingddatej”xhfilenamegrfc4568eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126491hkeywordsÇdsrtpx#secure real-time transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateDæsógrankingÿcrfcd4568irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSession Description Protocol (SDP) Security Descriptions for Media StreamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document defines general extensions for Session Description Protocol (SDP) and Real Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP) to carry messages, as specified by a key management protocol, in order to secure the media. These extensions are presented as a framework, to be used by one or more key management protocols. As such, their use is meaningful only when complemented by an appropriate key management protocol.

General guidelines are also given on how the framework should be used together with SIP and RTSP. The usage with the Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) key management protocol is also defined. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekF. Lindholm¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejM. Naslund¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejK. Norrman¢kaffiliationxRoyal Institute of TechnologydnamejE. Carraraddatej”|hfilenamegrfc4567eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126490hkeywordsÉwkey management protocolxmultimedia internet keyingemikeykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDæsêgranking◊crfcd4567irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhKey Management Extensions for Session Description Protocol (SDP) and Real Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis memo defines the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  SDP is intended for describing multimedia sessions for the purposes of session announcement, session invitation, and other forms of multimedia session initiation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnamekV. Jacobson¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”òhfilenamegrfc4566eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126489hkeywordsácSDPembonehinternetimulticasthbackbonejmultimediaxinternet addresses syntaxkobsoletedByÅd8866epages1opublicationDateDæsàgranking÷crfcd4566irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!SDP: Session Description ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKThis document is a record of the process and findings of the Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points Working Group (CAPWAP WG) evaluation team.  The evaluation team reviewed the 4 candidate protocols as they were submitted to the working group on June 26, 2005.  his memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationnEnvysion, Inc.dnamehD. Loher¢kaffiliationxEnterasys Networks, Inc.dnameiD. Nelson¢kaffiliationwColubris Networks, Inc.dnamekO. Volinsky¢kaffiliationjHuawei USAdnamekB. SarikayaddatejÀÎhfilenamegrfc4565eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126488hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD∏-granking’crfcd4565irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Evaluation of Candidate Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document presents objectives for an interoperable protocol for the Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP).  The document aims to establish a set of focused requirements for the development and evaluation of a CAPWAP protocol.  The objectives address architecture, operation, security, and network operator requirements that are necessary to enable interoperability among Wireless Local Area Network (WLAN) devices of alternative designs.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamekS. Govindan¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamehH. Cheng¢kaffiliationfHuaweidnamegZH. Yao¢kaffiliationlChina MobilednamehWH. Zhou¢kaffiliationeInteldnamegL. Yangddatej”¢hfilenamegrfc4564eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126487hkeywordsÇdwlanxwireless local area networkkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateD∏(granking‘crfcd4564irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJObjectives for Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This memo specifies a new Type (the Key ID Information Type) for the General Extension Payload in the Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY) Protocol.  This is used in, for example, the Multimedia Broadcast/Multicast Service specified in the Third Generation Partnership Project. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcKTHdnamejE. Carrara¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemV. Lehtovirta¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejK. Norrmanddatej”°hfilenamegrfc4563eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126486hkeywordsÖhsecuritynkey managementimulticastibroadcastdMBMSkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateD•™∫granking”crfcd4563irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcThe Key ID Information Type for the General Extension Payload in Multimedia Internet KEYing (MIKEY)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6309µhabstractyqThis document describes a mechanism to ensure layer-2 separation of Local Area Network (LAN) stations accessing an IPv4 gateway over a bridged Ethernet segment.

The mechanism - called "MAC-Forced Forwarding" - implements an Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) proxy function that prohibits Ethernet Media Access Control (MAC) address resolution between hosts located within the same IPv4 subnet but at different customer premises, and in effect directs all upstream traffic to an IPv4 gateway. The IPv4 gateway provides IP-layer connectivity between these same hosts. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark TownsleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiT. Melsen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehS. Blakeddatej—\hfilenamegrfc4562eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126485hkeywordsÑhEthernetnAccess NetworkcARPdDHCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDêTŒgranking“crfcd4562irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexWMAC-Forced Forwarding: A Method for Subscriber Separation on an Ethernet Access NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚIn the context of MPLS TE Fast Reroute, the Merge Point (MP) address is required at the Point of Local Repair (PLR) in order to select a backup tunnel intersecting a fast reroutable Traffic Engineering Label Switched Path (TE LSP) on a downstream Label Switching Router (LSR).  However, existing protocol mechanisms are not sufficient to find an MP address in multi-domain routing networks where a domain is defined as an Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) area or an Autonomous System (AS).  Hence, the current MPLS Fast Reroute mechanism cannot be used in order to protect inter-domain TE LSPs from a failure of an Area Border Router (ABR) or Autonomous System Border Router (ASBR).  This document specifies the use of existing Record Route Object (RRO) IPv4 and IPv6 sub-objects (with a new flag defined) thus defining the node-id sub-object in order to solve this issue.  The MPLS Fast Reroute mechanism mentioned in this document refers to the "Facility backup" MPLS TE Fast Reroute method. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ.-P. Vasseur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelS. Sivabalanddatej”°hfilenamegrfc4561eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126484hkeywordsãx1multiprotocol label switching traffic engineeringcplrupoint of local repaircigpxinterior gateway protocolbasqautonomous systemcabrrarea border routerdasbrxautonomous system border routerkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateDë˛…granking—crfcd4561irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Definition of a Record Route Object (RRO) Node-Id Sub-ObjectdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis memo defines Management Information Bases (MIBs) for performing ping, traceroute, and lookup operations at a host. When managing a network, it is useful to be able to initiate and retrieve the results of ping or traceroute operations when they are performed at a remote host. A lookup capability is defined in order to enable resolution of either an IP address to an DNS name or a DNS name to an IP address at a remote host.

Currently, there are several enterprise-specific MIBs for performing remote ping or traceroute operations. The purpose of this memo is to define a standards-based solution to enable interoperability. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationiIBM Corp.dnamehK. Whiteddatej”£hfilenamegrfc4560eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdismandfullxdisman - Distributed ManagementdnamevDistributed Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126483hkeywordsÖcmibxmanagement information basexDISMAN-PING-MIB DEFINITIONSx!DISMAN-TRACEROUTE-MIB DEFINITIONSxDISMAN-NSLOOKUP-MIB DEFINITIONSkobsoletedByÄepagesdopublicationDateD•≠hgranking–crfcd4560irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQDefinitions of Managed Objects for Remote Ping, Traceroute, and Lookup OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty!This document describes how the Microsoft Internet Explorer (MSIE) and Internet Information Services (IIS) incorporated in Microsoft Windows 2000 use Kerberos for security enhancements of web transactions.  The Hypertext Transport Protocol (HTTP) auth-scheme of "negotiate" is defined here; when the negotiation results in the selection of Kerberos, the security services of authentication and, optionally, impersonation (the IIS server assumes the windows identity of the principal that has been authenticated) are performed.  This document explains how HTTP authentication utilizes the Simple and Protected GSS-API Negotiation mechanism.  Details of Simple And Protected Negotiate (SPNEGO) implementation are not provided in this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott HollenbeckgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemK. Jaganathan¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiJ. Brezakddatej”lhfilenamegrfc4559eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126482hkeywordsÜdmsiexmicrosoft internet explorerciisxinternet information servicesxsimple and protected negotiatennt lan managerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD•™~grankingœcrfcd4559irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGSPNEGO-based Kerberos and NTLM HTTP Authentication in Microsoft WindowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙUse of Node-ID based Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Hello messages is implied in a number of cases, e.g., when data and control planes are separated, when TE links are unnumbered.  Furthermore, when link level failure detection is performed by some means other than exchanging RSVP Hello messages, use of a Node-ID based Hello session is optimal for detecting signaling adjacency failure for Resource reSerVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE).  Nonetheless, this implied behavior is unclear, and this document formalizes use of the Node-ID based RSVP Hello session in some scenarios.  The procedure described in this document applies to both Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) and Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) capable nodes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefZ. Ali¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiR. Rahman¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejD. Prairie¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej”ûhfilenamegrfc4558eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126481hkeywordsâxMulti-Protocol Label Switchingdmplsx+Generalized  Multi-Protocol Label SwitchingegmplssTraffic Engineeringbtegrsvp-tebgrpgraceful restartkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDë˛¨grankingŒcrfcd4558irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSNode-ID Based Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Hello: A Clarification StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document defines a mechanism to enable in-band transmission of Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) responses in the Kerberos network authentication protocol.  These responses are used to verify the validity of the certificates used in Public Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT), which is the Kerberos Version 5 extension that provides for the use of public key cryptography. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemK. Jaganathan¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamekN. WilliamsddatejÀ‚hfilenamegrfc4557eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-126480hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD•™‚grankingÕcrfcd4557irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex}Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) Support for Public Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document describes protocol extensions (hereafter called PKINIT) to the Kerberos protocol specification.  These extensions provide a method for integrating public key cryptography into the initial authentication exchange, by using asymmetric-key signature and/or encryption algorithms in pre-authentication data fields. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuAerospace CorporationdnamegB. Tungddatej»ùhfilenamegrfc4556eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-126479hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateD•´¥grankingÃcrfcd4556irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGPublic Key Cryptography for Initial Authentication in Kerberos (PKINIT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8062d6112d8636∂habstracty)This document describes the MOBIKE protocol, a mobility and multihoming extension to Internet Key Exchange (IKEv2).  MOBIKE allows the IP addresses associated with IKEv2 and tunnel mode IPsec Security Associations to change.  A mobile Virtual Private Network (VPN) client could use MOBIKE to keep the connection with the VPN gateway active while moving from one address to another.  Similarly, a multihomed host could use MOBIKE to move the traffic to a different interface if, for instance, the one currently being used stops working. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronenddatej”ûhfilenamegrfc4555eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmobikedfullx'mobike - IKEv2 Mobility and MultihomingdnamexIKEv2 Mobility and Multihomingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126478hkeywordsÖuinternet key exchangeeipsecxinternet protocol securitycvpnxvirtual private networkskobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateDäôgrankingÀcrfcd4555irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0IKEv2 Mobility and Multihoming Protocol (MOBIKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeEthernet VLANs are quite commonly used in enterprise networks for the purposes of traffic segregation.  This document describes how such VLANs can be readily used to deploy IPv6 networking in an enterprise, which focuses on the scenario of early deployment prior to availability of IPv6-capable switch-router equipment.  In this method, IPv6 may be routed in parallel with the existing IPv4 in the enterprise and delivered at Layer 2 via VLAN technology.  The IPv6 connectivity to the enterprise may or may not enter the site via the same physical link.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chownddatej”¢hfilenamegrfc4554eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126477hkeywordsÅxVirtual Local Area NetworkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD†2granking crfcd4554irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Use of VLANs for IPv4-IPv6 Coexistence in Enterprise NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a pseudowire encapsulation for Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) bit-streams (T1, E1, T3, E3) that disregards any structure that may be imposed on these streams, in particular the structure imposed by the standard TDM framing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoAxerra NetworksdnamemA. Vainshtein¢kaffiliationwRAD Data CommunicationsdnameiYJ. Steinddatej”úhfilenamegrfc4553eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126476hkeywordsÑeSAToPkpseudowiresqcircuit emulationxstructure-agnostic emulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDë˛wgranking…crfcd4553irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGStructure-Agnostic Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) over Packet (SAToP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx–This document describes means and mechanisms to provide authentication/confidentiality to OSPFv3 using an IPv6 Authentication Header/Encapsulating Security Payload (AH/ESP) extension header. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoTropos NetworksdnamehM. Gupta¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehN. Melamddatej”¢hfilenamegrfc4552eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126475hkeywordsÑxopen shortest path firstuauthentication headerxencapsulating security payloadfah/espkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDêTﬂgranking»crfcd4552irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Authentication/Confidentiality for OSPFv3dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoOften, multiple IMAP (RFC 3501) clients need to coordinate changes to a common IMAP mailbox. Examples include different clients working on behalf of the same user, and multiple users accessing shared mailboxes. These clients need a mechanism to synchronize state changes for messages within the mailbox. They must be able to guarantee that only one client can change message state (e.g., message flags) at any time. An example of such an application is use of an IMAP mailbox as a message queue with multiple dequeueing clients.

The Conditional Store facility provides a protected update mechanism for message state information that can detect and resolve conflicts between multiple writing mail clients.

The Conditional Store facility also allows a client to quickly resynchronize mailbox flag changes.

This document defines an extension to IMAP (RFC 3501). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationxACI WorldWide/MessagingDirectdnamegS. Holeddatej”úhfilenamegrfc4551eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgimapextdfullx4imapext - Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondnamex*Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126474hkeywordsÅxinternet mail access protocolkobsoletedByÅd7162epagesopublicationDateDë˛Vgranking«crfcd4551irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVIMAP Extension for Conditional STORE Operation or Quick Flag Changes ResynchronizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document describes a profile (a set of required extensions, restrictions, and usage modes) of the IMAP and mail submission protocols. This profile allows clients (especially those that are constrained in memory, bandwidth, processing power, or other areas) to efficiently use IMAP and Submission to access and submit mail. This includes the ability to forward received mail without needing to download and upload the mail, to optimize submission, and to efficiently resynchronize in case of loss of connectivity with the server.

The Internet Email to Support Diverse Service Environments (Lemonade) profile relies upon extensions to IMAP and Mail Submission protocols; specifically, the URLAUTH and CATENATE IMAP protocol (RFC 3501) extensions and the BURL extension to the SUBMIT protocol (RFC 4409). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationfOraclednamegS. Maes¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej”óhfilenamegrfc4550eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126473hkeywordsÅx internet message access protocolkobsoletedByÅd5550epagesopublicationDateDëˇgranking∆crfcd4550irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIInternet Email to Support Diverse Service Environments (Lemonade) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document attempts to address some of the issues involved in building a disconnected IMAP4 client. In particular, it deals with the issues of what might be called the "driver" portion of the synchronization tool: the portion of the code responsible for issuing the correct set of IMAP4 commands to synchronize the disconnected client in the way that is most likely to make the human who uses the disconnected client happy.

This note describes different strategies that can be used by disconnected clients and shows how to use IMAP protocol in order to minimize the time of the synchronization process.

This note also lists IMAP extensions that a server should implement in order to provide better synchronization facilities to disconnected clients. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej”>hfilenamegrfc4549eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126472hkeywordsÅx internet message access protocolkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateDë˛Ïgranking≈crfcd4549irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Synchronization Operations for Disconnected IMAP4 ClientsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document is intended to accomplish two highly inter-related tasks: to establish an "initial" Internet Code Point (ICP) assignment for each of IPv4 and IPv6 address encoding in Network Service Access Point (NSAP) Addresses, and to recommend an IANA assignment policy for currently unassigned ICP values.  In the first task, this document is a partial replacement for RFC 1888 -- particularly for section 6 of RFC 1888.  In the second task, this document incorporates wording and specifications from ITU-T Recommendation X.213 and further recommends that IANA use the "IETF consensus" assignment policy in making future ICP assignments. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemJ. Rutemiller¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallowddatej”êhfilenamegrfc4548eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126471hkeywordsÅxnetwork service access pointkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateDr::grankingƒcrfcd4548irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Internet Code Point (ICP) Assignments for NSAP AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. In particular, it defines a basic set of managed objects for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) based event notification management of Data Over Cable Service Interface Specification (DOCSIS) compliant Cable Modems and Cable Modem Termination Systems. This MIB is defined as an extension to the DOCSIS Cable Device MIB.

This memo specifies a MIB module in a manner that is compliant to the Structure of Management Information Version 2 (SMIv2). The set of objects is consistent with the SNMP framework and existing SNMP standards. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamehA. Ahmad¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamelG. Nakanishiddatej”jhfilenamegrfc4547eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-126470hkeywordsÖdsnmpx"simple network management protocolcmibesmiv2x'DOCS-IETF-CABLE-DEVICE-NOTIFICATION-MIBkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateD•´granking√crfcd4547irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexßEvent Notification Management Information Base for Data over Cable Service Interface Specifications (DOCSIS)-Compliant Cable Modems and Cable Modem Termination SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractyèThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines a set of managed objects for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) based management of the Radio Frequency (RF) interfaces for systems compliant with the Data Over Cable Service Interface Specifications (DOCSIS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvATI Technologies, Inc.dnameiD. Raftus¢kaffiliationx#Cable Television Laboratories, Inc.dnamejE. Cardonaddatej”ühfilenamegrfc4546eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-126469hkeywordsâdcmtsbcmhupstreamjdownstreamdtdmaeatdmaescdmarquality of serviceuchannel utilizazationkobsoletedByÄepagesãopublicationDateD†2bgranking¬crfcd4546irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexîRadio Frequency (RF) Interface Management Information Base for Data over Cable Service Interface Specifications (DOCSIS) 2.0 Compliant RF InterfacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing user identities and the names, addresses, and credentials required manage access control, for use with various protocols.  This document was motivated by the need for the configuration of authorized user identities for the iSCSI protocol, but has been extended to be useful for other protocols that have similar requirements.  It is important to note that this MIB module provides only the set of identities to be used within access lists; it is the responsibility of other MIB modules making use of this one to tie them to their own access lists or other authorization control methods. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Bakke¢kaffiliationlQlogic Corp.dnameiJ. Muchowddatej”ûhfilenamegrfc4545eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126468hkeywordsÖcmibxmanagement information basedsnmpftcp/iplips-auth-mibkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateD|—≈granking¡crfcd4545irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIDefinitions of Managed Objects for IP Storage User Identity AuthorizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing a client using the Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) protocol (SCSI over TCP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Bakke¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamejM. Krueger¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelT. McSweeney¢kaffiliationlQlogic Corp.dnameiJ. Muchowddatej”†hfilenamegrfc4544eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126467hkeywordsÇftcp/ipdscsikobsoletedByÅd7147epagesSopublicationDateD|— granking¿crfcd4544irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSDefinitions of Managed Objects for Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯This memo describes the use of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Galois Message Authentication Code (GMAC) as a mechanism to provide data origin authentication, but not confidentiality, within the IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH).  GMAC is based on the Galois/Counter Mode (GCM) of operation, and can be efficiently implemented in hardware for speeds of 10 gigabits per second and above, and is also well-suited to software implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationlMcAfee, Inc.dnamehJ. Viegaddatej”©hfilenamegrfc4543eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126466hkeywordsÑxencapsulating security payloadcgcmsgalois/counter modeuauthentication headerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDtÓugrankingøcrfcd4543irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHThe Use of Galois Message Authentication Code (GMAC) in IPsec ESP and AHdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙RFCs 3689 and 3690 detail requirements for an Emergency Telecommunications Service (ETS), of which an Internet Emergency Preparedness Service (IEPS) would be a part. Some of these types of services require call preemption; others require call queuing or other mechanisms. IEPS requires a Call Admission Control (CAC) procedure and a Per Hop Behavior (PHB) for the data that meet the needs of this architecture. Such a CAC procedure and PHB is appropriate to any service that might use H.323 or SIP to set up real-time sessions. The key requirement is to guarantee an elevated probability of call completion to an authorized user in time of crisis.

This document primarily discusses supporting ETS in the context of the US Government and NATO, because it focuses on the Multi-Level Precedence and Preemption (MLPP) and Government Emergency Telecommunication Service (GETS) standards. The architectures described here are applicable beyond these organizations. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polkddatej”†hfilenamegrfc4542eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126465hkeywordsädiepsx'internet emergency preparedness servicevcall admission controlccaccphbpper hop behaviorx%multi-level precedence and preemptiondmlppx.government emergency telecommunication servicedgetskobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateDjMKgrankingæcrfcd4542irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpImplementing an Emergency Telecommunications Service (ETS) for Real-Time Services in the Internet Protocol SuitedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5865∂habstracty√This memo describes the recommendations for Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) snooping switches.  These are based on best current practices for IGMPv2, with further considerations for IGMPv3- and MLDv2-snooping.  Additional areas of relevance, such as link layer topology changes and Ethernet-specific encapsulation issues, are also considered.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoThrane & ThranednamenM. Christensen¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamejK. Kimball¢kaffiliationeCalixdnamekF. Solenskyddatej—Ghfilenamegrfc4541eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-126464hkeywordsÇfigmpv3emldv2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDjMDgrankingΩcrfcd4541irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuConsiderations for Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) Snooping SwitchesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty»This document describes a protocol for controlling middleboxes such as firewalls and network address translators.  It is a fully compliant implementation of the Middlebox Communications (MIDCOM) semantics described in RFC 3989.  Compared to earlier experimental versions of the SIMCO protocol, this version (3.0) uses binary message encodings in order to reduce resource requirements.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. Cadarddatej—Nhfilenamegrfc4540eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126463hkeywordsÅfmidcomkobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDateDel”grankingºcrfcd4540irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexANEC's Simple Middlebox Configuration (SIMCO) Protocol Version 3.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractysThis document serves to register a media type for the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) Material Exchange Format (MXF).  MXF, defined by SMPTE 377M, is a standard wrapper format developed for the interchange of audiovisual material, including both audiovisual essence and rich metadata.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcPBSdnamejT. EdwardsddatejÀ„hfilenamegrfc4539eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126462hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDU±grankingªcrfcd4539irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyMedia Type Registration for the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) Material Exchange Format (MXF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝This specification defines the Target-Dialog header field for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), and the corresponding option tag, tdialog.  This header field is used in requests that create SIP dialogs.  It indicates to the recipient that the sender is aware of an existing dialog with the recipient, either because the sender is on the other side of that dialog, or because it has access to the dialog identifiers.  The recipient can then authorize the request based on this awareness. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”†hfilenamegrfc4538eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126461hkeywordsÅgtdialogkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDêT¨granking∫crfcd4538irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Request Authorization through Dialog Identification in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-This document specifies an extension to the Kerberos protocol as defined in RFC 4120, in which the client can send a list of supported encryption types in decreasing preference order, and the server then selects an encryption type that is supported by both the client and the server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehP. Leach¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemK. JaganathanddatejÀﬁhfilenamegrfc4537eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-126460hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDáb,grankingπcrfcd4537irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Kerberos Cryptosystem Negotiation ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document specifies the media type for versions 1.0, 2.0, and 2.1 of the Synchronized Multimedia Integration Language (SMIL 1.0, SMIL 2.0, SMIL 2.1).  SMIL allows integration of a set of independent multimedia objects into a synchronized multimedia presentation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcW3CdnamejP. Hoschkaddatej”óhfilenamegrfc4536eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126459hkeywordsÅx,synchronized multimedia integration languagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDæs~granking∏crfcd4536irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The application/smil and application/smil+xml Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the Group Secure Association Key Management Protocol (GSAKMP).  The GSAKMP provides a security framework for creating and managing cryptographic groups on a network.  It provides mechanisms to disseminate group policy and authenticate users, rules to perform access control decisions during group establishment and recovery, capabilities to recover from the compromise of group members, delegation of group security functions, and capabilities to destroy the group.  It also generates group keys. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiH. Harney¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnamegU. Meth¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnamelA. Colegrove¢kaffiliationjIdentAwarednamehG. Grossddatej”ghfilenamegrfc4535eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126458hkeywordsÇrsecurity frameworkucryptographic networkkobsoletedByÄepagesjopublicationDateD†28granking∑crfcd4535irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8GSAKMP: Group Secure Association Key Management ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"The Group Security Policy Token is a structure used to specify the security policy and configurable parameters for a cryptographic group, such as a secure multicast group.  Because the security of a group is composed of the totality of multiple security services, mechanisms, and attributes throughout the communications infrastructure, an authenticatable representation of the features that must be supported throughout the system is needed to ensure consistent security.  This document specifies the structure of such a token. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnamelA. Colegrove¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiH. Harneyddatej”ìhfilenamegrfc4534eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126457hkeywordsÅscryptographic groupkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateD†1úgranking∂crfcd4534irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGroup Security Policy Token v1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty™This specification describes the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Content Synchronization Operation.  The operation allows a client to maintain a copy of a fragment of the Directory Information Tree (DIT).  It supports both polling for changes and listening for changes.  The operation is defined as an extension of the LDAP Search Operation.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilenga¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiJ.H. Choiddatej—Zhfilenamegrfc4533eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126456hkeywordsÇcditxdirectory information treekobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateDà«≤grankingµcrfcd4533irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexRThe Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Content Synchronization OperationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This specification provides a mechanism for Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) clients to obtain the authorization identity the server has associated with the user or application entity.  This mechanism is specified as an LDAP extended operation called the LDAP "Who am I?" operation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej—chfilenamegrfc4532eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126455hkeywordsÅvauthorization identitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà«†granking¥crfcd4532irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) "Who am I?" OperationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis specification describes a Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) extended operation to reverse (or "turn") the roles of client and server for subsequent protocol exchanges in the session, or to enable each peer to act as both client and server with respect to the other.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”Lhfilenamegrfc4531eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126454hkeywordsÇlturn requestmturn responsekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateDà«ígranking≥crfcd4531irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Turn OperationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractylThis document describes the LDAP/X.500 \'entryUUID' operational attribute and associated matching rules and syntax.  The attribute holds a server-assigned Universally Unique Identifier (UUID) for the object.  Directory clients may use this attribute to distinguish objects identified by a distinguished name or to locate an object after renaming. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”|hfilenamegrfc4530eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126453hkeywordsÇex.500xuniversally unique identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà«Ñgranking≤crfcd4530irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) entryUUID Operational AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThe Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) search operation provides mechanisms for clients to request all user application attributes, all operational attributes, and/or attributes selected by their description.  This document extends LDAP to support a mechanism that LDAP clients may use to request the return of all attributes of an object class.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. ZeilengaddatejÀÉhfilenamegrfc4529eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126452hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà«+granking±crfcd4529irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRRequesting Attributes by Object Class in the Lightweight Directory Access ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygThis document defines the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Assertion Control, which allows a client to specify that a directory operation should only be processed if an assertion applied to the target entry of the operation is true.  It can be used to construct "test and set", "test and clear", and other conditional operations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”mhfilenamegrfc4528eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126451hkeywordsÇltest and setntest and clearkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà∆ granking∞crfcd4528irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Assertion ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document specifies an extension to the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) to allow the client to read the target entry of an update operation.  The client may request to read the entry before and/or after the modifications are applied.  These reads are done as an atomic part of the update operation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. ZeilengaddatejÀ´hfilenamegrfc4527eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126450hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà«QgrankingØcrfcd4527irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Read Entry ControlsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document extends the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) to support absolute True and False filters based upon similar capabilities found in X.500 directory systems.  The document also extends the String Representation of LDAP Search Filters to support these filters. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”lhfilenamegrfc4526eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126449hkeywordsÇex.500ustring representationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà…grankingÆcrfcd4526irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Absolute True and False FiltersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis document describes an extension to the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Modify operation to support an increment capability.  This extension is useful in provisioning applications, especially when combined with the assertion control and/or the pre-read or post-read control extension.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”vhfilenamegrfc4525eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126448hkeywordsÉhpre-readipost-readqcontrol extensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà¿3granking≠crfcd4525irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Modify-Increment ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚This document provides a collection of schema elements for use with the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) from the COSINE and Internet X.500 pilot projects.

This document obsoletes RFC 1274 and updates RFCs 2247 and 2798. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”ïhfilenamegrfc4524eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126447hkeywordsÅfNamingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà¡Wgranking¨crfcd4524irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCOSINE LDAP/X.500 SchemadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document describes schema for representing X.509 certificates, X.521 security information, and related elements in directories accessible using the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP).  The LDAP definitions for these X.509 and X.521 schema elements replace those provided in RFCs 2252 and 2256. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”|hfilenamegrfc4523eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126446hkeywordsÖiLDAP3-ATDeLDAv3ex.500fsyntaxdpkixkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà¿ granking´crfcd4523irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Schema Definitions for X.509 CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywEach attribute stored in a Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) directory has a defined syntax (i.e., data type).  A syntax definition specifies how attribute values conforming to the syntax are normally represented when transferred in LDAP operations.  This representation is referred to as the LDAP\-specific encoding to distinguish it from other methods of encoding attribute values.  This document defines an attribute option, the binary option, that can be used to specify that the associated attribute values are instead encoded according to the Basic Encoding Rules (BER) used by X.500 directories. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Leggddatej”îhfilenamegrfc4522eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126445hkeywordsÇcbervldap-specific encodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà¿granking™crfcd4522irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): The Binary Encoding OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyîThe Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) is extensible.  It provides mechanisms for adding new operations, extending existing operations, and expanding user and system schemas.  This document discusses considerations for designers of LDAP extensions.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. ZeilengaddatejÀﬁhfilenamegrfc4521eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126444hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDà¿Agranking©crfcd4521irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbervetotaletitlexJConsiderations for Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty£This document provides procedures for registering extensible elements of the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP).  The document also provides guidelines to the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) describing conditions under which new values can be assigned.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. ZeilengaddatejÀ€hfilenamegrfc4520eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgldapbisdfullxldapbis - LDAP (v3) RevisiondnamerLDAP (v3) Revisiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126443hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDàø‹granking®crfcd4520irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber@etotaletitlexnInternet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) Considerations for the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document is an integral part of the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) technical specification.  It provides a technical specification of attribute types and object classes intended for use by LDAP directory clients for many directory services, such as White Pages.  These objects are widely used as a basis for the schema in many LDAP directories.  This document does not cover attributes used for the administration of directory servers, nor does it include directory objects defined for specific uses in other documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamelA. Sciberrasddatej”ìhfilenamegrfc4519eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgldapbisdfullxldapbis - LDAP (v3) RevisiondnamerLDAP (v3) Revisiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126442hkeywordsÇx%Lightweight Directory Access ProtocolfsyntaxkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateDàπBgrankingßcrfcd4519irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): Schema for User ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThe previous Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) technical specifications did not precisely define how character string matching is to be performed.  This led to a number of usability and interoperability problems.  This document defines string preparation algorithms for character-based matching rules defined for use in LDAP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. ZeilengaddatejÀÕhfilenamegrfc4518eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgldapbisdfullxldapbis - LDAP (v3) RevisiondnamerLDAP (v3) Revisiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126441hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDàπ3granking¶crfcd4518irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): Internationalized String PreparationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyREach attribute stored in a Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) directory, whose values may be transferred in the LDAP protocol, has a defined syntax that constrains the structure and format of its values.  The comparison semantics for values of a syntax are not part of the syntax definition but are instead provided through separately defined matching rules.  Matching rules specify an argument, an assertion value, which also has a defined syntax.  This document defines a base set of syntaxes and matching rules for use in defining attributes for LDAP directories. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgeB2BcomdnamegS. Leggddatej”çhfilenamegrfc4517eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgldapbisdfullxldapbis - LDAP (v3) RevisiondnamerLDAP (v3) Revisiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126440hkeywordsÑiLDAP3-ATDeLDAv3ex.500fsyntaxkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateDà∏‰granking•crfcd4517irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexILightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): Syntaxes and Matching RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrThis document describes a format for a Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Uniform Resource Locator (URL).  An LDAP URL describes an LDAP search operation that is used to retrieve information from an LDAP directory, or, in the context of an LDAP referral or reference, an LDAP URL describes a service where an LDAP operation may be progressed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsPearl Crescent, LLCdnamehM. Smith¢kaffiliationmOpsware, Inc.dnamehT. Howesddatej”Dhfilenamegrfc4516eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgldapbisdfullxldapbis - LDAP (v3) RevisiondnamerLDAP (v3) Revisiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126439hkeywordsÜhLDAP-URLgLDAPURLkLDAP searchcURLcURIfLDAPv3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDàπgranking§crfcd4516irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): Uniform Resource LocatordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) search filters are transmitted in the LDAP protocol using a binary representation that is appropriate for use on the network.  This document defines a human-readable string representation of LDAP search filters that is appropriate for use in LDAP URLs (RFC 4516) and in other applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Area