es and preferences for handling of that request.  The SIP REFER method defined in RFC 3515 provides a mechanism that allows one party to induce another to initiate a SIP request.  This document extends the REFER method to use the mechanism of RFC 3840.  By doing so, the originator of a REFER may inform the recipient as to the characteristics of the target that the induced request is expected to reach. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levin¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnstonddatej”†hfilenamegrfc4508eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126431hkeywordsÅrcaller preferenceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDwÄÜgrankingúcrfcd4508irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNConveying Feature Tags with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) REFER MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8217∂habstractyRThis document describes a mechanism that enables the Transport Layer Security (TLS) server to resume sessions and avoid keeping \%per-client session state.  The TLS server encapsulates the session state into a ticket and forwards it to the client.  The client can subsequently resume a session using the obtained ticket. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegH. Zhou¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemH. TschofenigddatejÀ‰hfilenamegrfc4507eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126430hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5077epagesopublicat