ase (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets. In particular, it defines objects for managing remote network monitoring devices.

This document obsoletes RFC 2021, updates RFC 3273, and contains a new version of the RMON2-MIB module. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemS. Waldbusserddatej”âhfilenamegrfc4502eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126425hkeywordsÉhRMON-MIBdRMONcMIBkobsoletedByÄepageséopublicationDateDr:5grankingñcrfcd4502irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Remote Network Monitoring Management Information Base Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxfThis document defines Uniform Resource Identifiers for Domain Name System resources. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSJDdnamelS. Josefssonddatej”mhfilenamegrfc4501eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126424hkeywordsÇcdnscurikobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateD[≈grankingïcrfcd4501irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Domain Name System Uniform Resource IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÁThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB), the Aggregation MIB modules, for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, the Aggregation MIB modules will be used to configure a network management agent to aggregate the values of a user-specified set of Managed Object instances and to service queries related to the aggregated Managed Object instances.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtCyber Solutions Inc.dnamehG. Keeniddatej”mhfilenamegrfc4498eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126423hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basemaggregate mibrtime aggregate mibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDtÓ|grankingícrfcd4498irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex"The Managed Object Aggregation MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document specifies interworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and QSIG within corporate telecommunication networks (also known as enterprise networks).  SIP is an Internet application-layer control (signalling) protocol for creating, modifying, and terminating sessions with one or more participants.  These sessions include, in particular, telephone calls.  QSIG is a signalling protocol for creating, modifying, and terminating circuit-switched calls (in particular, telephone calls) within Private Integrated Services Networks (PISNs).  QSIG is specified in a number of Ecma Standards and published also as ISO/IEC standards.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnameiJ. Elwell¢kaffiliationkNEC PhilipsdnamehF. Derks¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnameiP. Mourot¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamekO. Rousseauddatej”öhfilenamegrfc4497eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126422hkeywordsÉxtelecommunication networkssenterprise networksjsignallingkobsoletedByÄepagesAopublicationDateDjM(grankingëcrfcd4497irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberuetotaletitlexCInterworking between the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and QSIGdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty3The Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) framework is application agnostic.  Application-specific adaptations extend that framework.  This document describes OPES SMTP use cases and deployment scenarios in preparation for SMTP adaptation with OPES.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSecure ComputingdnamejM. Stecher¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiA. BarbirddatejÀ‘hfilenamegrfc4496eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126421hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD[Ωgrankingêcrfcd4496irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) SMTP Use CasesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document proposes an extension to the Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVPv1) to reduce the guaranteed bandwidth allocated to an existing reservation.  This mechanism can be used to affect individual reservations, aggregate reservations, or other forms of RSVP tunnels.  This specification is an extension of RFC 2205. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekS. Dhesikanddatej”ñhfilenamegrfc4495eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126420hkeywordsÅfrsvpv1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD[›grankingècrfcd4495irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeA Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP) Extension for the Reduction of Bandwidth of a Reservation FlowdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) has recently specified the Cipher-based Message Authentication Code (CMAC), which is equivalent to the One-Key CBC-MAC1 (OMAC1) algorithm submitted by Iwata and Kurosawa.  OMAC1 efficiently reduces the key size of Extended Cipher Block Chaining mode (XCBC).  This memo specifies the use of CMAC mode as an authentication mechanism of the IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and the Authentication Header (AH) protocols.  This new algorithm is named AES-CMAC-96. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamehJH. Song¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamemR. Poovendran¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamefJ. Leeddatej”ïhfilenamegrfc4494eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126419hkeywordsÜx'cipher-basd message authentication codepone-key cbc-mac1eomac1dxcbcxextended cipher block chainingxadvanced encryption standardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD•¨Mgrankingécrfcd4494irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0The AES-CMAC-96 Algorithm and Its Use with IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) has recently specified the Cipher-based Message Authentication Code (CMAC), which is equivalent to the One-Key CBC MAC1 (OMAC1) submitted by Iwata and Kurosawa.  This memo specifies an authentication algorithm based on CMAC with the 128-bit Advanced Encryption Standard (AES).  This new authentication algorithm is named AES-CMAC.  The purpose of this document is to make the AES-CMAC algorithm conveniently available to the Internet Community.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamehJH. Song¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamemR. Poovendran¢kaffiliationsSamsung ElectronicsdnamefJ. Lee¢kaffiliationqNagoya UniversitydnamehT. Iwataddatej”çhfilenamegrfc4493eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126418hkeywordsÑx(cipher-based message authentication codeeomac1pone-key cbc mac1xadvanced encryption algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD•¨ógrankingçcrfcd4493irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevThe AES-CMAC AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis document describes new key exchange algorithms based on Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol.  In particular, it specifies the use of Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) key agreement in a TLS handshake and the use of Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) as a new authentication mechanism.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgSafeNetdnameoS. Blake-Wilson¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejN. Bolyard¢kaffiliationhSun LabsdnamehV. Gupta¢kaffiliationiCorrientednamegC. Hawk¢kaffiliationoRuhr-Uni BochumdnamejB. Moellerddatej”ëhfilenamegrfc4492eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126417hkeywordsÑdecdhxelliptic curve diffie-hellmanx*elliptic curve digital signature algorithmeecdsakobsoletedByÅd8422epages#opublicationDateDjMgrankingåcrfcd4492irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRElliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Cipher Suites for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7027d7919d5246∂habstractx˛This document supplements RFC 3279.  It describes encoding formats, identifiers, and parameter formats for the algorithms GOST R 34.10-94, GOST R 34.10-2001, and GOST R 34.11-94 for use in Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamekS. Leontiev¢kaffiliationwMobile TeleSystems OJSCdnamenD. Shefanovskiddatej”ôhfilenamegrfc4491eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126416hkeywordsëdPKIXeX.509dCPPKjpublic-keyuone-way hash functionlblock cipherjencryptionjdecryptionnkey derivationiparameternmessage digestqdigital signaturef34.310f34.311i34.310-95k34.310-2004i34.311-95kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDr:/grankingãcrfcd4491irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexöUsing the GOST R 34.10-94, GOST R 34.10-2001, and GOST R 34.11-94 Algorithms with the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and CRL ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document describes the conventions for using the cryptographic algorithms GOST 28147-89, GOST R 34.10-94, GOST R 34.10-2001, and GOST R 34.11-94 with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  The CMS is used for digital signature, digest, authentication, and encryption of arbitrary message contents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamekS. Leontiev¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnameiG. Chudovddatej”óhfilenamegrfc4490eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126415hkeywordsò!eCPCMSfS/MIMEdPKIXeX.509kcertificatecCRLjrevocationjpublic-keygone-waydhasheblockfcipherjencryptionjdecryptioncMACdHMACcPRFdwrapfunwrapcUKMcKEKckeynDiffie-HellmaniagreementitransportiparameterjderivationfdigestcCBCgcounterdmodegdigitalisignaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDr:Agrankingäcrfcd4490irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉUsing the GOST 28147-89, GOST R 34.11-94, GOST R 34.10-94, and GOST R 34.10-2001 Algorithms with Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis document specifies an extension to the multicast addressing architecture of the IPv6 protocol.  The extension allows the use of Interface Identifiers (IIDs) to allocate multicast addresses.  When a link-local unicast address is configured at each interface of a node, an IID is uniquely determined.  After that, each node can generate its unique multicast addresses automatically without conflicts.  The alternative method for creating link-local multicast addresses proposed in this document is better than known methods like unicast-prefix-based IPv6 multicast addresses.  This memo updates RFC 3306. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdETRIdnameiJ-S. Park¢kaffiliationdETRIdnameiM-K. Shin¢kaffiliationdETRIdnamehH-J. Kimddatej”phfilenamegrfc4489eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126414hkeywordsÇciiduinterface identifierskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDRdÙgrankingâcrfcd4489irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<A Method for Generating Link-Scoped IPv6 Multicast AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) REFER extension as defined in RFC 3515 automatically establishes a typically short-lived event subscription used to notify the party sending a REFER request about the receiver's status in executing the transaction requested by the REFER.  These notifications are not needed in all cases.  This specification provides a way to prevent the automatic establishment of an event subscription and subsequent notifications using a new SIP extension header field that may be included in a REFER request. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. LevinddatejÀﬁhfilenamegrfc4488eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126413hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDn@Ögrankingàcrfcd4488irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSSuppression of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) REFER Method Implicit SubscriptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This document captures the issues that may arise in the deployment of IPv6 networks when they support Mobile IPv6 and firewalls. The issues are not only applicable to firewalls protecting enterprise networks, but are also applicable in 3G mobile networks such as General Packet Radio Service / Universal Mobile Telecommunications System (GPRS/UMTS) and CDMA2000 networks.

The goal of this document is to highlight the issues with firewalls and Mobile IPv6 and act as an enabler for further discussion. Issues identified here can be solved by developing appropriate solutions. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcCMUdnameeF. Le¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiS. Faccin¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehB. Patil¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”óhfilenamegrfc4487eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126412hkeywordsÇb3gomobile networkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDjM	grankingácrfcd4487irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Mobile IPv6 and Firewalls: Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document defines several subcodes for the BGP Cease NOTIFICATION message that would provide more information to aid network operators in correlating network events and diagnosing BGP peering issues. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnameiV. Gilletddatej”õhfilenamegrfc4486eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126411hkeywordsÇwborder gateway protocolibgp peerskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDRdÍgrankingÜcrfcd4486irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Subcodes for BGP Cease Notification MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9003d8203∂habstractyÈThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is a flexible yet simple tool for establishing interactive communications sessions across the Internet.  Part of this flexibility is the ease with which it can be extended.  In order to facilitate effective and interoperable extensions to SIP, some guidelines need to be followed when developing SIP extensions.  This document outlines a set of such guidelines for authors of SIP extensions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”Lhfilenamegrfc4485eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126410hkeywordsÅxinteractive communicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD|—œgrankingÖcrfcd4485irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMGuidelines for Authors of Extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document lays out a set of requirements related to trait-based authorization for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  While some authentication mechanisms are described in the base SIP specification, trait-based authorization provides information used to make policy decisions based on the attributes of a participant in a session.  This approach provides a richer framework for authorization, as well as allows greater privacy for users of an identity system.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationjCU BoulderdnameiD. Sicker¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”Ñhfilenamegrfc4484eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126409hkeywordsÅopolicy decisionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˜g∏grankingÑcrfcd4484irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPTrait-Based Authorization Requirements for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document defines an extension to the URL MIME External-Body Access-Type to satisfy the content indirection requirements for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  These extensions are aimed at allowing any MIME part in a SIP message to be referred to indirectly via a URI. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwCantata Technolgy, Inc.dnameiE. Burgerddatej”nhfilenamegrfc4483eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126408hkeywordsÉxuniversal resource locatordmimexexternal-body access-typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD|—ºgrankingÉcrfcd4483irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQA Mechanism for Content Indirection in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThe Presence Information Data Format (PIDF) defines a basic XML format for presenting presence information for a presentity.  The Contact Information for the Presence Information Data format (CIPID) is an extension that adds elements to PIDF that provide additional contact information about a presentity and its contacts, including references to address book entries and icons. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”åhfilenamegrfc4482eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126407hkeywordsÅdpidfkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD©ÆSgrankingÇcrfcd4482irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCCIPID: Contact Information for the Presence Information Data FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThe Presence Information Data Format (PIDF) defines a basic XML format for presenting presence information for a presentity.  This document extends PIDF, adding a timed status extension (<timed-status> element) that allows a presentity to declare its status for a time interval fully in the future or the past. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. SchulzrinneddatejÀÕhfilenamegrfc4481eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126406hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateD©Æ}grankingÅcrfcd4481irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäTimed Presence Extensions to the Presence Information Data Format (PIDF) to Indicate Status Information for Past and Future Time IntervalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•The Presence Information Data Format (PIDF) defines a basic format for representing presence information for a presentity. This format defines a textual note, an indication of availability (open or closed) and a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) for communication. The Rich Presence Information Data format (RPID) described here is an extension that adds optional elements to the Presence Information Data Format (PIDF). These extensions provide additional information about the presentity and its contacts. The information is designed so that much of it can be derived automatically, e.g., from calendar files or user activity.

This extension includes information about what the person is doing, a grouping identifier for a tuple, when a service or device was last used, the type of place a person is in, what media communications might remain private, the relationship of a service tuple to another presentity, the person's mood, the time zone it is located in, the type of service it offers, an icon reflecting the presentity's status, and the overall role of the presentity.

These extensions include presence information for persons, services (tuples), and devices. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationfLucentdnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejP. Kyzivat¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelJ. RosenbergddatejÀŒhfilenamegrfc4480eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126405hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateD©Æ°grankingÄcrfcd4480irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMRPID: Rich Presence Extensions to the Presence Information Data Format (PIDF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document defines the underlying presence data model used by Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensions (SIMPLE) presence agents.  The data model provides guidance on how to map various communications systems into presence documents in a consistent fashion. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”éhfilenamegrfc4479eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126404hkeywordsÉfsimplecsipxsession initiation protocolkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateD©Æ*grankingcrfcd4479irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexA Data Model for PresencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThis document extends the Internet Key Exchange (IKEv2) Protocol document [IKEv2].  With some IPsec peers, particularly in the remote access scenario, it is desirable to repeat the mutual authentication periodically.  The purpose of this is to limit the time that security associations (SAs) can be used by a third party who has gained control of the IPsec peer.  This document describes a mechanism to perform this function.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkCheck PointdnamefY. Nirddatej”öhfilenamegrfc4478eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126403hkeywordsÅhlifetimekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDOÄgranking~crfcd4478irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexARepeated Authentication in Internet Key Exchange (IKEv2) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyA node may have support for communications using IPv4 and/or IPv6 protocols.  Such a node may wish to obtain IPv4 and/or IPv6 configuration settings via the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP).  The original version of DHCP (RFC 2131) designed for IPv4 has now been complemented by a new DHCPv6 (RFC 3315) for IPv6.  This document describes issues identified with dual IP version DHCP interactions, the most important aspect of which is how to handle potential problems in clients processing configuration information received from both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 servers.  The document makes a recommendation on the general strategy on how best to handle such issues and identifies future work to be undertaken.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationx)Clausthal University of TechnologyUNINETTdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliation`dnameiC. Straufddatej”ôhfilenamegrfc4477eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126402hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD[≠granking}crfcd4477irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP): IPv4 and IPv6 Dual-Stack IssuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document describes one certificate extension that explicitly states the Attribute Certificate Policies (ACPs) that apply to a given Attribute Certificate (AC).  The goal of this document is to allow relying parties to perform an additional test when validating an AC, i.e., to assess whether a given AC carrying some attributes can be accepted on the basis of references to one or more specific ACPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhRaytheondnamejC. Francis¢kaffiliationdBulldnameiD. Pinkasddatej”òhfilenamegrfc4476eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126401hkeywordsÇcacpxattribute certificate policieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD[’granking|crfcd4476irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Attribute Certificate (AC) Policies ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx“This informational document gives examples of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) test messages designed to exercise and "torture" a SIP implementation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationoDitech NetworksdnamenA. Hawrylyshen¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej»yhfilenamegrfc4475eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126400hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateDn@√granking{crfcd4475irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Torture Test MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe existing security mechanisms in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) are inadequate for cryptographically assuring the identity of the end users that originate SIP requests, especially in an interdomain context.  This document defines a mechanism for securely identifying originators of SIP messages.  It does so by defining two new SIP header fields, Identity, for conveying a signature used for validating the identity, and Identity-Info, for conveying a reference to the certificate of the signer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jenningsddatej”èhfilenamegrfc4474eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126399hkeywordsÉhsecurityhidentitymidentity-infokobsoletedByÅd8224epages)opublicationDateD˜g•grankingzcrfcd4474irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Enhancements for Authenticated Identity Management in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis memo specifies requirements for a framework and protocols for accessing and updating Internet Media Guide (IMG) information for media-on-demand and multicast applications.  These requirements are designed to guide choice and development of IMG protocols for efficient and scalable delivery.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationlFujitsu LabsdnameiY. Nomura¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Walsh¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ-P. Luoma¢kaffiliationx!Helsinki University of TechnologydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”ëhfilenamegrfc4473eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126398hkeywordsÇnmedia-on-demanimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD~:grankingycrfcd4473irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Requirements for Internet Media Guides (IMGs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This memo presents operational considerations and issues with IPv6 Domain Name System (DNS), including a summary of special IPv6 addresses, documentation of known DNS implementation misbehavior, recommendations and considerations on how to perform DNS naming for service provisioning and for DNS resolver IPv6 support, considerations for DNS updates for both the forward and reverse trees, and miscellaneous issues.  This memo is aimed to include a summary of information about IPv6 DNS considerations for those who have experience with IPv4 DNS.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgComcastdnameiA. Durand¢kaffiliationjAutonomicadnamehJ. Ihren¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savoladdatej”êhfilenamegrfc4472eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126397hkeywordsÇrdomain name systemxinternet protocol version 6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDC–Kgrankingxcrfcd4472irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Operational Considerations and Issues with IPv6 DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document describes two methods for deriving the canonically-ordered predecessor and successor of a DNS name.  These methods may be used for dynamic NSEC resource record synthesis, enabling security-aware name servers to provide authenticated denial of existence without disclosing other owner names in a DNSSEC secured zone.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNominetdnameiG. Sisson¢kaffiliationgNominetdnameiB. Laurieddatej”°hfilenamegrfc4471eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126396hkeywordsÉpdomain namespaceldynamic nsecfdnsseckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateE“grankingwcrfcd4471irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Derivation of DNS Name Predecessor and SuccessordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis document describes how to construct DNSSEC NSEC resource records that cover a smaller range of names than called for by RFC 4034.  By generating and signing these records on demand, authoritative name servers can effectively stop the disclosure of zone contents otherwise made possible by walking the chain of NSEC records in a signed zone. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiS. Weiler¢kaffiliationmAutonomica ABdnamehJ. Ihrenddatej”îhfilenamegrfc4470eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126395hkeywordsÇldns securityrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDRd—grankingvcrfcd4470irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Minimally Covering NSEC Records and DNSSEC On-line SigningdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃThe CATENATE extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) extends the APPEND command to allow clients to create messages on the IMAP server that may contain a combination of new data along with parts of (or entire) messages already on the server.  Using this extension, the client can catenate parts of an already existing message onto a new message without having to first download the data and then upload it back to the server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuQUALCOMM IncorporateddnamejP. Resnickddatej”Öhfilenamegrfc4469eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126394hkeywordsÅfappendkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDC–Dgrankingucrfcd4469irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) CATENATE ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5550∂habstractyThe submission profile of Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) provides a standard way for an email client to submit a complete message for delivery.  This specification extends the submission profile by adding a new BURL command that can be used to fetch submission data from an Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) server.  This permits a mail client to inject content from an IMAP server into the SMTP infrastructure without downloading it to the client and uploading it back to the server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newmanddatej”Öhfilenamegrfc4468eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126393hkeywordsÜgURLAUTHdIMAPgIMAPURLxForward-without-downloadmmobile-clienthlemonadekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDwÄëgrankingtcrfcd4468irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Message Submission BURL ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5248∂habstractyóThis document describes the URLAUTH extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) (RFC 3501) and the IMAP URL Scheme (IMAPURL) (RFC 2192). This extension provides a means by which an IMAP client can use URLs carrying authorization to access limited message data on the IMAP server.

An IMAP server that supports this extension indicates this with a capability name of "URLAUTH". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamejM. Crispinddatej”àhfilenamegrfc4467eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126392hkeywordsÇhimap urlgimapurlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDwÄvgrankingscrfcd4467irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - URLAUTH ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5550d5092∂habstracty∆Over the years, many documents from IMAPEXT and LEMONADE working groups, as well as many individual documents, have added syntactic extensions to many base IMAP commands described in RFC 3501. For ease of reference, this document collects most of such ABNF changes in one place.

This document also suggests a set of standard patterns for adding options and extensions to several existing IMAP commands defined in RFC 3501. The patterns provide for compatibility between existing and future extensions.

This document updates ABNF in RFCs 2088, 2342, 3501, 3502, and 3516. It also includes part of the errata to RFC 3501. This document doesn't specify any semantic changes to the listed RFCs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. DabooddatejÀ⁄hfilenamegrfc4466eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126391hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD<;cgrankingrcrfcd4466irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Collected Extensions to IMAP4 ABNFdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7377d6237∂habstractyThis document provides a set of "torture tests" for implementers of the Signaling Compression (SigComp) protocol.  The torture tests check each of the SigComp Universal Decompressor Virtual Machine instructions in turn, focusing in particular on the boundary and error cases that are not generally encountered when running well-behaved compression algorithms.  Tests are also provided for other SigComp entities such as the dispatcher and the state handler.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamejA. Surtees¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamegM. Westddatej”ähfilenamegrfc4465eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126390hkeywordsÅx.SigComp Universal Decompressor Virtual MachinekobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDateD†1Vgrankingqcrfcd4465irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Signaling Compression (SigComp) Torture TestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis document provides an informational guide for users of the Signaling Compression (SigComp) protocol.  The aim of the document is to assist users when making SigComp implementation decisions, for example, the choice of compression algorithm and the level of robustness against lost or misordered packets.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamejA. Surtees¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamegM. WestddatejÀ hfilenamegrfc4464eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126389hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateD|—‘grankingpcrfcd4464irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Signaling Compression (SigComp) Users' GuidedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty‘This document describes a Media Resource Control Protocol (MRCP) that was developed jointly by Cisco Systems, Inc., Nuance Communications, and Speechworks, Inc. It is published as an RFC as input for further IETF development in this area.

MRCP controls media service resources like speech synthesizers, recognizers, signal generators, signal detectors, fax servers, etc., over a network. This protocol is designed to work with streaming protocols like RTSP (Real Time Streaming Protocol) or SIP (Session Initiation Protocol), which help establish control connections to external media streaming devices, and media delivery mechanisms like RTP (Real Time Protocol). This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemS. Shanmugham¢kaffiliationuNuance CommunicationsdnameiP. Monaco¢kaffiliationpSpeechworks Inc.dnamejB. Ebermanddatej»Ñhfilenamegrfc4463eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126388hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesVopublicationDateDO…grankingocrfcd4463irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexTA Media Resource Control Protocol (MRCP) Developed by Cisco, Nuance, and SpeechworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThe Secure Shell protocol (SSH) is a protocol for secure remote login and other secure network services over an insecure network.

The Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) provides security services to callers in a mechanism-independent fashion.

This memo describes methods for using the GSS-API for authentication and key exchange in SSH. It defines an SSH user authentication method that uses a specified GSS-API mechanism to authenticate a user, and a family of SSH key exchange methods that use GSS-API to authenticate a Diffie-Hellman key exchange.

This memo also defines a new host public key algorithm that can be used when no operations are needed using a host's public key, and a new user authentication method that allows an authorization name to be used in conjunction with any authentication that has already occurred as a side-effect of GSS-API-based key exchange. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcCMUdnamelJ. Hutzelman¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Salowey¢kaffiliationxVan Dyke Technologies, Inc.dnamelJ. Galbraith¢kaffiliationoU Chicago / ANLdnamehV. WelchddatejÀ∂hfilenamegrfc4462eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126387hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD[Õgrankingncrfcd4462irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑGeneric Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Authentication and Key Exchange for the Secure Shell (SSH) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8732d9142∂habstracty2This document presents a set of requirements for the establishment and maintenance of Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Traffic-Engineered (TE) Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs).

There is no intent to specify solution-specific details or application-specific requirements in this document.

The requirements presented in this document not only apply to packet-switched networks under the control of MPLS protocols, but also encompass the requirements of Layer Two Switching (L2SC), Time Division Multiplexing (TDM), lambda, and port switching networks managed by Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) protocols. Protocol solutions developed to meet the requirements set out in this document must attempt to be equally applicable to MPLS and GMPLS. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekS. Yasukawaddatej”ëhfilenamegrfc4461eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126386hkeywordsÇdp2mpxmultiprotocol label switchingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDMK}grankingmcrfcd4461irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbSignaling Requirements for Point-to-Multipoint Traffic-Engineered MPLS Label Switched Paths (LSPs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document is a compilation of issues found during six interoperability events and 5 years of experience with implementing, testing, and using SCTP along with the suggested fixes.  This document provides deltas to RFC 2960 and is organized in a time-based way.  The issues are listed in the order they were brought up.  Because some text is changed several times, the last delta in the text is the one that should be applied.  In addition to the delta, a description of the problem and the details of the solution are also provided.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamerI. Arias-Rodriguez¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamegK. Poon¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegA. Caro¢kaffiliationx"Muenster Univ. of Applied SciencesdnameiM. Tuexenddatej”ãhfilenamegrfc4460eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126385hkeywordsÜdSCTPbIPhinternetitransportfpacketgnetworkkobsoletedByÅd9260epagesmopublicationDateDQm≤grankinglcrfcd4460irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKStream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Specification Errata and IssuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%Tunneling techniques such as IP-in-IP when deployed in the middle of the network, typically between routers, have certain issues regarding how large packets can be handled: whether such packets would be fragmented and reassembled (and how), whether Path MTU Discovery would be used, or how this scenario could be operationally avoided.  This memo justifies why this is a common, non-trivial problem, and goes on to describe the different solutions and their characteristics at some length.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. SavoladdatejÀœhfilenamegrfc4459eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126384hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD6°ggrankingkcrfcd4459irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:MTU and Fragmentation Issues with In-the-Network TunnelingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is often used to initiate connections to applications such as voicemail or interactive voice recognition systems.  This specification describes a convention for forming SIP service URIs that request particular services based on redirecting targets from such applications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehF. Audet¢kaffiliationkSiemens plcdnameiJ. Elwellddatej”íhfilenamegrfc4458eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126383hkeywordsÅxuniversal resource identifierskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD6¢7grankingjcrfcd4458irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) URIs for Applications such as Voicemail and Interactive Voice Response (IVR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8119∂habstractyØThis document specifies the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) P-User-Database Private-Header (P-header).  This header field is used in the 3rd-Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) IMS (IP Multimedia Subsystem) to provide SIP registrars and SIP proxy servers with the address of the database that contains the user profile of the user that generated a particular request.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiG. Blancoddatej”îhfilenamegrfc4457eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126382hkeywordsÖd3gppx$third generation partnership projectx"3rd generation partnership projectcimswip multimedia subsystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD<<␀grankingicrfcd4457irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) P-User-Database Private-Header (P-Header)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThe Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is an inter-autonomous system routing protocol designed for TCP/IP internets. Typically, all BGP speakers within a single AS must be fully meshed so that any external routing information must be re-distributed to all other routers within that Autonomous System (AS). This represents a serious scaling problem that has been well documented with several alternatives proposed.

This document describes the use and design of a method known as "route reflection" to alleviate the need for "full mesh" Internal BGP (IBGP).

This document obsoletes RFC 2796 and RFC 1966. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Bates¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Chen¢kaffiliationmSonoa SystemsdnamejR. Chandraddatej”íhfilenamegrfc4456eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126381hkeywordsÜfBGP-RRfBordergGatewayhProtocoljautonomousfsystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDQm´grankinghcrfcd4456irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEBGP Route Reflection: An Alternative to Full Mesh Internal BGP (IBGP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7606∂habstracty1This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB), for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for Small Computer System Interface (SCSI) entities, independently of the interconnect subsystem layer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxSanrad Intelligent StoragednameqM. Hallak-Stamler¢kaffiliationvSiliquent TechnologiesdnamehM. Bakke¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekY. Lederman¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamejM. Krueger¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. McCloghrieddatej”ïhfilenamegrfc4455eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126380hkeywordsÉcmibxmanagement information basehscsi-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateD<;·grankinggcrfcd4455irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQDefinition of Managed Objects for Small Computer System Interface (SCSI) EntitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThe Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol, Version 3 (L2TPv3) defines an extensible tunneling protocol to transport layer 2 services over IP networks.  This document describes the specifics of how to use the L2TP control plane for Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) Pseudowires and provides guidelines for transporting various ATM services over an IP network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehS. Singh¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Townsley¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataroddatej”îhfilenamegrfc4454eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126379hkeywordsÅxextensible tunneling protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDjM␀grankingfcrfcd4454irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSAsynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) over Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3 (L2TPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5641∂habstractyÄThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) supports communications across many media types, including real-time audio, video, text, instant messaging, and presence.  In its current form, it allows session invitations, instant messages, and other requests to be delivered from one party to another without requiring explicit consent of the recipient.  Without such consent, it is possible for SIP to be used for malicious purposes, including spam and denial-of-service attacks.  This document identifies a set of requirements for extensions to SIP that add consent-based communications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. Willisddatej”íhfilenamegrfc4453eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126378hkeywordsÅnsip extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD6¢+grankingecrfcd4453irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVRequirements for Consent-Based Communications in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This document defines the "info" Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme for information assets with identifiers in public namespaces.  Namespaces participating in the "info" URI scheme are regulated by an "info" Registry mechanism.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdLANLdnamepH. Van de Sompel¢kaffiliationcNPGdnamejT. Hammond¢kaffiliationrManifest SolutionsdnameiE. Neylon¢kaffiliationdOCLCdnameiS. Weibelddatej”Ühfilenamegrfc4452eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126377hkeywordsÅxuniform resource identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD6°∫grankingdcrfcd4452irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRThe "info" URI Scheme for Information Assets with Identifiers in Public NamespacesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe BGP MULTI_EXIT_DISC (MED) attribute provides a mechanism for BGP speakers to convey to an adjacent AS the optimal entry point into the local AS. While BGP MEDs function correctly in many scenarios, a number of issues may arise when utilizing MEDs in dynamic or complex topologies.

This document discusses implementation and deployment considerations regarding BGP MEDs and provides information with which implementers and network operators should be familiar. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtArbor Networks, Inc.dnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationcAOLdnamegV. Gillddatej”ëhfilenamegrfc4451eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126376hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD•grankingccrfcd4451irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(BGP MULTI_EXIT_DISC (MED) ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This memo documents an experiment to review and classify Proposed Standards as not reflecting documented practice within the world today.  The results identify a set of documents that were marked as Proposed Standards that are now reclassified as Historic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemH. AlvestrandddatejÀÿhfilenamegrfc4450eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfnewtrkdfullx,newtrk - New IETF Standards Track Discussiondnamex#New IETF Standards Track Discussiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126375hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD+Úgrankingbcrfcd4450irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqGetting Rid of the Cruft: Report from an Experiment in Identifying and Reclassifying Obsolete Standards DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxæA mobile node and a correspondent node may preconfigure data useful for precomputing a Binding Management Key that can subsequently be used for authorizing Binding Updates. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejJari Arkkodarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”îhfilenamegrfc4449eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126374hkeywordsÑkmobile nodercorrespondent nodevbinding management keyobinding updateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDë˛2grankingacrfcd4449irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASecuring Mobile IPv6 Route Optimization Using a Static Shared KeydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôAn Ethernet pseudowire (PW) is used to carry Ethernet/802.3 Protocol Data Units (PDUs) over an MPLS network.  This enables service providers to offer "emulated" Ethernet services over existing MPLS networks.  This document specifies the encapsulation of Ethernet/802.3 PDUs within a pseudowire.  It also specifies the procedures for using a PW to provide a "point-to-point Ethernet" service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationxLevel 3 Communications, LLCdnamekN. El-Aawar¢kaffiliationgTellabsdnamehG. Heronddatej”ähfilenamegrfc4448eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126373hkeywordsÑbpwjpseudowirecpdusprotocol data unitskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDOægranking`crfcd4448irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBEncapsulation Methods for Transport of Ethernet over MPLS NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5462d8469∂habstracty}Layer 2 services (such as Frame Relay, Asynchronous Transfer Mode, and Ethernet) can be "emulated" over an MPLS backbone by encapsulating the Layer 2 Protocol Data Units (PDU) and transmitting them over "pseudowires".  It is also possible to use pseudowires to provide low-rate Time Division Multiplexed and a Synchronous Optical NETworking circuit emulation over an MPLS-enabled network.  This document specifies a protocol for establishing and maintaining the pseudowires, using extensions to Label Distribution Protocol (LDP).  Procedures for encapsulating Layer 2 PDUs are specified in a set of companion documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationxLevel 3 Communications, LLC.dnamekN. El-Aawar¢kaffiliationwNetwork Appliance, Inc.dnamehT. Smith¢kaffiliationgTellabsdnamehG. Heronddatej”}hfilenamegrfc4447eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126372hkeywordsÑdmplsx&multiprotocol label switching protocolcpdusprotocol data unitskobsoletedByÅd8077epages!opublicationDateDOµgranking_crfcd4447irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLPseudowire Setup and Maintenance Using the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7358d6723d6870∑habstractyèThis document allocates the fixed pseudowire identifier and other fixed protocol values for protocols that have been defined in the Pseudo Wire Edge to Edge (PWE3) working group.  Detailed IANA allocation instructions are also included in this document.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamejL. MartiniddatejÀ»hfilenamegrfc4446eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126371hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateDO™granking^crfcd4446irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbertetotaletitlex=IANA Allocations for Pseudowire Edge to Edge Emulation (PWE3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractysThis memo defines a Media Delivery Index (MDI) measurement that can be used as a diagnostic tool or a quality indicator for monitoring a network intended to deliver applications such as streaming media, MPEG video, Voice over IP, or other information sensitive to arrival time and packet loss.  It provides an indication of traffic jitter, a measure of deviation from nominal flow rates, and a data loss at-a-glance measure for a particular flow.  For instance, the MDI may be used as a reference in characterizing and comparing networks carrying UDP streaming media.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDan RomascanugauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvIneoQuest TechnologiesdnamehJ. Welch¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehJ. Clarkddatej»ûhfilenamegrfc4445eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126370hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateDOgranking]crfcd4445irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex%A Proposed Media Delivery Index (MDI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyQThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  Specifically, this document describes a MIB for the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Routing protocol when it is used to construct routing tables for IP networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAxiowave NetworksdnameiJ. Parkerddatej”ëhfilenamegrfc4444eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126369hkeywordsÉcmibprouting protocolhisis-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesgopublicationDateDFÿgranking\crfcd4444irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRManagement Information Base for Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxﬁThis document describes the format of a set of control messages used in ICMPv6 (Internet Control Message Protocol).  ICMPv6 is the Internet Control Message Protocol for Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjTranswitchdnamehA. Conta¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Deering¢kaffiliationoTropos NetworksdnamehM. GuptaddatejÀthfilenamegrfc4443eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126368hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD+granking[crfcd4443irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberYetotaletitlexcInternet Control Message Protocol (ICMPv6) for the Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4884∂habstractyRTESLA, the Timed Efficient Stream Loss-tolerant Authentication protocol, provides source authentication in multicast scenarios. TESLA is an efficient protocol with low communication and computation overhead that scales to large numbers of receivers and also tolerates packet loss. TESLA is based on loose time synchronization between the sender and the receivers. Source authentication is realized in TESLA by using Message Authentication Code (MAC) chaining. The use of TESLA within the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP) has been published, targeting multicast authentication in scenarios where SRTP is applied to protect the multimedia data. This solution assumes that TESLA parameters are made available by out-of-band mechanisms.

This document specifies payloads for the Multimedia Internet Keying (MIKEY) protocol for bootstrapping TESLA for source authentication of secure group communications using SRTP. TESLA may be bootstrapped using one of the MIKEY key management approaches, e.g., by using a digitally signed MIKEY message sent via unicast, multicast, or broadcast. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamehS. Fries¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”èhfilenamegrfc4442eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126367hkeywordsÉnauthenticationemikeyx#multimedia internet keying protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDF5grankingZcrfcd4442irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIBootstrapping Timed Efficient Stream Loss-Tolerant Authentication (TESLA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty™Since the late 1980s, IEEE 802 and IETF have cooperated in the development of Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) MIBs and Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) applications.  This document describes the policies and procedures that have developed in order to coordinate between the two organizations, as well as some of the relationship history.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamehB. Abobaddatej”âhfilenamegrfc4441eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126366hkeywordsÜdsnmpcaaax"simple network management protocolnauthenticationmauthorizationjaccountingkobsoletedByÅd7241epagesopublicationDateD+ógrankingYcrfcd4441irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexThe IEEE 802/IETF RelationshipdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyUThis document is an Internet Architecture Board (IAB) report on the role of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF), both on its own and in relationship to the IETF.  This document evolved from a discussion within the IAB as part of a process of appointing a new chair of the IRTF.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationcIABdnameiV. Paxson¢kaffiliationcIABdnamegA. Falk¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej”çhfilenamegrfc4440eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126365hkeywordsÅxinternet architecture boardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD+€grankingXcrfcd4440irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexCIAB Thoughts on the Role of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for information related to a Fibre Channel network's Fabric Address Manager. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejV. Gaonkar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationgRetireddnamefS. Gaiddatej”éhfilenamegrfc4439eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126364hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basejt11-tc-mibxt11-fc-fabric-addr-mgr-mibkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateD-«~grankingWcrfcd4439irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Fibre Channel Fabric Address Manager MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for information related to the Name Server function of a Fibre Channel network.  The Fibre Channel Name Server provides a means for Fibre Channel ports to register and discover Fibre Channel names and attributes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDan Romascanudarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSanti¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejV. Gaonkar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejH.K. Vivek¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationgRetireddnamefS. Gaiddatej”éhfilenamegrfc4438eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126363hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basevT11-fc-name-server-mibkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateD6°}grankingVcrfcd4438irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFibre-Channel Name Server MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis specification defines an extension to Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) to allow clients to author HTTP redirect reference resources whose default response is an HTTP/1.1 3xx (Redirection) status code.  A redirect reference makes it possible to access the target resourced indirectly through any URI mapped to the redirect reference resource.  This specification does not address remapping of trees of resources or regular expression based redirections.  There are no integrity guarantees associated with redirect reference resources.  Other mechanisms can also be used to achieve the same functionality as this specification.  This specification allows operators to experiment with this mechanism and develop experience on what is the best approach to the problem.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoU.C. Santa CruzdnamelJ. Whitehead¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehG. Clemm¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej”ãhfilenamegrfc4437eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfwebdavdfullx1webdav - WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdnamex(WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdtypebwgbidjdoc-126362hkeywordsÇdhttpxhyper text transfer protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDjggrankingUcrfcd4437irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNWeb Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) Redirect Reference ResourcesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThe time required to detect movement between networks and to obtain (or to continue to use) an IPv4 configuration may be significant as a fraction of the total handover latency in moving between points of attachment.  This document synthesizes, from experience in the deployment of hosts supporting ARP, DHCP, and IPv4 Link-Local addresses, a set of steps known as Detecting Network Attachment for IPv4 (DNAv4), in order to decrease the handover latency in moving between points of attachment. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejJ. Carlson¢kaffiliationnApple ComputerdnamekS. Cheshireddatej”ãhfilenamegrfc4436eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126361hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDEœgrankingTcrfcd4436irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Detecting Network Attachment in IPv4 (DNAv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThis document defines a framework for the delivery of Internet Media Guides (IMGs).  An IMG is a structured collection of multimedia session descriptions expressed using the Session Description Protocol (SDP), SDPng, or some similar session description format.  This document describes a generalized model for IMG delivery mechanisms, the use of existing protocols, and the need for additional work to create an IMG delivery infrastructure.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmFujitsu Labs.dnameiY. Nomura¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Walsh¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejJ-P. Luoma¢kaffiliationx"Columbia UniversityKeio UniversitydnameiH. Asaeda¢kaffiliation`dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”çhfilenamegrfc4435eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126360hkeywordsÉxsession description protocolcsdpesdpngkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD<;ΩgrankingScrfcd4435irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9A Framework for the Usage of Internet Media Guides (IMGs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡Some implementations of IP Security (IPsec) may want to use a pseudo-random function derived from the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES).  This document describes such an algorithm, called AES-XCBC-PRF-128. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej”{hfilenamegrfc4434eflags£mhiddenDefa