ddatej”éhfilenamegrfc4432eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126357hkeywordsÇvrivest-sharmir-adlemanupublic key encryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD¬grankingPcrfcd4432irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDRSA Key Exchange for the Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Layer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9142∂habstractxÊThis document defines a new DNS resource record, called the DNSSEC Lookaside Validation (DLV) RR, for publishing DNSSEC trust anchors outside of the DNS delegation chain.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternet Systems ConsortiumdnamejM. Andrews¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiS. Weilerddatej”mhfilenamegrfc4431eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126356hkeywordsÇcdnsqdomain name spacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDg›grankingOcrfcd4431irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The DNSSEC Lookaside Validation (DLV) DNS Resource RecorddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document describes the Kerberized Internet Negotiation of Keys (KINK) protocol.  KINK defines a low-latency, computationally inexpensive, easily managed, and cryptographically sound protocol to establish and maintain security associations using the Kerberos authentication system.  KINK reuses the Quick Mode payloads of the Internet Key Exchange (IKE), which should lead to substantial reuse of existing IKE implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationwYokogawa Electric Corp.dnameiS. Sakane¢kaffiliationwYokogawa Electric Corp.dnameiK. Kamada¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiM. Thomas¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekJ. Vilhuberddatej”âhfilenamegrfc4430eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdkinkdfullx.kink - Kerberized Internet Negotiation of Keysdnamex'Kerberized Internet Negotiation of Keysdtypebwgbidjdoc-126355hkeywordsÇcikeuinternet key exchangekobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateD+«grankingNcrfcd4430irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Kerberized Internet Negotiation of Keys (KINK)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôOptimistic Duplicate Address Detection is an interoperable modification of the existing IPv6 Neighbor Discovery (RFC 2461) and Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (RFC 2462) processes.  The intention is to minimize address configuration delays in the successful case, to reduce disruption as far as possible in the failure case, and to remain interoperable with unmodified hosts and routers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvMonash University CTIEdnamehN. Mooreddatej”èhfilenamegrfc4429eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126354hkeywordsÉxinternet protocol version 6x#stateless address autoconfigurationrneighbor discoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDC–5grankingMcrfcd4429irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Optimistic Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7527∂habstractyThis document provides an analysis grid to evaluate, compare, and contrast the Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) protocol suite capabilities with the recovery mechanisms currently proposed at the IETF CCAMP Working Group.  A detailed analysis of each of the recovery phases is provided using the terminology defined in RFC 4427.  This document focuses on transport plane survivability and recovery issues and not on control plane resilience and related aspects.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationhPercevaldnameiE. MannieddatejÀôhfilenamegrfc4428eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126353hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateD-…grankingLcrfcd4428irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAnalysis of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)-based Recovery Mechanisms (including Protection and Restoration)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document defines a common terminology for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)-based recovery mechanisms (i.e., protection and restoration).  The terminology is independent of the underlying transport technologies covered by GMPLS.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhPercevaldnameiE. Mannie¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. PapadimitriouddatejÀôhfilenamegrfc4427eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126352hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD-…grankingKcrfcd4427irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhRecovery (Protection and Restoration) Terminology for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document presents a functional description of the protocol extensions needed to support Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)-based recovery (i.e., protection and restoration).  Protocol specific formats and mechanisms will be described in companion documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Lang¢kaffiliation`dnamenB. Rajagopalan¢kaffiliation`dnamepD. PapadimitriouddatejÀïhfilenamegrfc4426eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126351hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD-…grankingJcrfcd4426irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Recovery Functional SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis memo specifies an RTP payload format for encapsulating Video Codec 1 (VC-1) compressed bit streams, as defined by the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) standard, SMPTE 421M.  SMPTE is the main standardizing body in the motion imaging industry, and the SMPTE 421M standard defines a compressed video bit stream format and decoding process for television. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejA. Klemetsddatej”ïhfilenamegrfc4425eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126350hkeywordsÑjsmpte 421mcwmvdwmv9dvc-9kobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateDgøgrankingIcrfcd4425irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+RTP Payload Format for Video Codec 1 (VC-1)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWThis document is an addendum to RFC 4348, which specifies the RTP payload format for the Variable-Rate Multimode Wideband (VMR-WB) speech codec. This document specifies some updates in RFC 4348 to enable support for the new operating mode of VMR-WB standard (i.e., VMR-WB mode 4). These updates do not affect the existing modes of VMR-WB already specified in RFC 4348.

The payload formats and their associated parameters, as well as all provisions, restrictions, use cases, features, etc., that are specified in RFC 4348 are applicable to the new operating mode with no exception. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Ahmadiddatej”ähfilenamegrfc4424eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126349hkeywordsÇlspeech codecx-variable-rate multicode wideband speech codeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˛ÇgrankingHcrfcd4424irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexyReal-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) Payload Format for the Variable-Rate Multimode Wideband (VMR-WB) Extension Audio CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This memo describes a snapshot of the reasoning behind a proposed new namespace, the Host Identity namespace, and a new protocol layer, the Host Identity Protocol (HIP), between the internetworking and transport layers.  Herein are presented the basics of the current namespaces, their strengths and weaknesses, and how a new namespace will add completeness to them.  The roles of this new namespace in the protocols are defined.  The memo describes the thinking of the authors as of Fall 2003.  The architecture may have evolved since.  This document represents one stable point in that evolution of understanding.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx.ICSAlabs, a Division of Cybertrust CorporationdnamelR. Moskowitz¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research Nomadic LabdnamekP. NikanderddatejÀ∏hfilenamegrfc4423eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymchipdfullxhip - Host Identity ProtocoldnamevHost Identity Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126348hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9063epagesopublicationDateDn@}grankingGcrfcd4423irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Host Identity Protocol (HIP) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0The Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL) is a framework for providing authentication and data security services in connection-oriented protocols via replaceable mechanisms. It provides a structured interface between protocols and mechanisms. The resulting framework allows new protocols to reuse existing mechanisms and allows old protocols to make use of new mechanisms. The framework also provides a protocol for securing subsequent protocol exchanges within a data security layer.

This document describes how a SASL mechanism is structured, describes how protocols include support for SASL, and defines the protocol for carrying a data security layer over a connection. In addition, this document defines one SASL mechanism, the EXTERNAL mechanism.

This document obsoletes RFC 2222. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmIsode LimiteddnamekA. Melnikov¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”£hfilenamegrfc4422eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsasldfullx/sasl - Simple Authentication and Security Layerdnamex(Simple Authentication and Security Layerdtypebwgbidjdoc-126347hkeywordsÉdSASLjencryptionqprotocol specifickobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateDà∑˜grankingFcrfcd4422irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Simple Authentication and Security Layer (SASL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯The RFC Payload Format for Uncompressed Video, RFC 4175, defines a scheme to packetise uncompressed, studio-quality, video streams for transport using RTP.  This memo extends the format to support additional colour sampling modes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”ähfilenamegrfc4421eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126346hkeywordsÇxrealtime transport protocollvideo streamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDg†grankingEcrfcd4421irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRTP Payload Format for Uncompressed Video: Additional Colour Sampling ModesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) may be established using the Resource Reservation Protocol Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) extensions. This protocol includes an object (the SESSION_ATTRIBUTE object) that carries a Flags field used to indicate options and attributes of the LSP. That Flags field has eight bits allowing for eight options to be set. Recent proposals in many documents that extend RSVP-TE have suggested uses for each of the previously unused bits.

This document defines a new object for RSVP-TE messages that allows the signaling of further attribute bits and also the carriage of arbitrary attribute parameters to make RSVP-TE easily extensible to support new requirements. Additionally, this document defines a way to record the attributes applied to the LSP on a hop-by-hop basis.

The object mechanisms defined in this document are equally applicable to Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Packet Switch Capable (PSC) LSPs and to GMPLS non-PSC LSPs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ.-P. Vasseur¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekA. Ayyangarddatej”Ühfilenamegrfc4420eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126345hkeywordsÅqSESSION_ATTRIBUTEkobsoletedByÅd5420epagesopublicationDateDgÄgrankingDcrfcd4420irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex©Encoding of Attributes for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Path (LSP) Establishment Using Resource ReserVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo describes a new key exchange method for the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.  It allows the SSH server to propose new groups on which to perform the Diffie-Hellman key exchange to the client.  The proposed groups need not be fixed and can change with time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Friedl¢kaffiliation`dnameiN. Provos¢kaffiliation`dnamejW. SimpsonddatejÀ¥hfilenamegrfc4419eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126344hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateD¯grankingCcrfcd4419irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQDiffie-Hellman Group Exchange for the Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Layer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8270∂habstracty_This specification describes how to generate an authentication tag using the UMAC message authentication algorithm. UMAC is designed to be very fast to compute in software on contemporary uniprocessors. Measured speeds are as low as one cycle per byte. UMAC relies on addition of 32-bit and 64-bit numbers and multiplication of 32-bit numbers, operations well-supported by contemporary machines.

To generate the authentication tag on a given message, a "universal" hash function is applied to the message and key to produce a short, fixed-length hash value, and this hash value is then xor'ed with a key-derived pseudorandom pad. UMAC enjoys a rigorous security analysis, and its only internal "cryptographic" component is a block cipher used to generate the pseudorandom pads and internal key material. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnCSU SacramentodnamejT. KrovetzddatejÀ®hfilenamegrfc4418eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126343hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDi´grankingBcrfcd4418irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9UMAC: Message Authentication Code using Universal HashingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty∂This document reports the outcome of a workshop held by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) on the future of Internet messaging.  The workshop was held on 6 and 7 October 2004 in Burlingame, CA, USA.  The goal of the workshop was to examine the current state of different messaging technologies on the Internet (including, but not limited to, electronic mail, instant messaging, and voice messaging), to look at their commonalities and differences, and to find engineering, research, and architectural topics on which future work could be done.  This report summarizes the discussions and conclusions of the workshop and of the IAB.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamejP. Resnick¢kaffiliationcJSFdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej”åhfilenamegrfc4417eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126342hkeywordsÇxinternet architecture boardrinternet messagingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDgKgrankingAcrfcd4417irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex)Report of the 2004 IAB Messaging WorkshopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'This document is a history capturing the background, motivation and thinking during the LEMONADE definition and design process.

The LEMONADE Working Group -- Internet email to support diverse service environments -- is chartered to provide enhancements to Internet mail to facilitate its use by more diverse clients. In particular, by clients on hosts not only operating in environments with high latency/bandwidth-limited unreliable links but also constrained to limited resources. The enhanced mail must be backwards compatible with existing Internet mail.

The primary motivation for this effort is -- by making Internet mail protocols richer and more adaptable to varied media and environments -- to allow mobile handheld devices tetherless access to Internet mail using only IETF mail protocols.

The requirements for these devices drive a discussion of the possible protocol enhancements needed to support multimedia messaging on limited-capability hosts in diverse service environments. A list of general principles to guide the design of the enhanced messaging protocols is documented. Finally, additional issues of providing seamless service between enhanced Internet mail and the existing separate mobile messaging infrastructure are briefly listed. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegJ. Wongddatej—Rhfilenamegrfc4416eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126341hkeywordsìdIMAPsprotocol extensionsimessaginghwirelesshhandheldxtelephone user interfacekmulti-modalhLEMONADEtextension principlesghistoryjbackgroundjmotivationhcellularlinterworkingkconstraintscTUIcWUIfclientcMMSkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateD˛qgranking@crfcd4416irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDGoals for Internet Messaging to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis document registers the Enumservice "voice" (which has a defined subtype "tel"), as per the IANA registration process defined in the ENUM specification RFC 3761.  This service indicates that the contact held in the generated Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) can be used to initiate an interactive voice (audio) call. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjSiemens AGdnamekR. Brandner¢kaffiliationxSiemens Roke Manor ResearchdnameiL. Conroy¢kaffiliationeOefegdnamejR. Stastnyddatej”ähfilenamegrfc4415eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126340hkeywordsÑxuniform resource identifiercurijvoice calljaudio callkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDh~granking?crfcd4415irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IANA Registration for Enumservice VoicedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstracty6This document describes an Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS) registry schema for registered ENUM information.  The schema extends the necessary query and result operations of IRIS to provide the functional information service needs for syntaxes and results used by ENUM registries. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. NewtonddatejÀ…hfilenamegrfc4414eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126339hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateD˛agranking>crfcd4414irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJAn ENUM Registry Type for the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis memo describes TCP/IP field behavior in the context of header compression.  Header compression is possible because most header fields do not vary randomly from packet to packet.  Many of the fields exhibit static behavior or change in a more or less predictable way.  When a header compression scheme is designed, it is of fundamental importance to understand the behavior of the fields in detail.  An example of this analysis can be seen in RFC 3095.  This memo performs a similar role for the compression of TCP/IP headers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnamegM. West¢kaffiliationxSiemens/Roke Manor ResearchdnameiS. McCannddatej”vhfilenamegrfc4413eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126338hkeywordsÇxtransmission control protocolrheader compressionkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateD}granking=crfcd4413irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuTCP/IP Field BehaviordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document defines two new Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) header fields for communicating resource priority, namely, "Resource-Priority" and "Accept-Resource-Priority".  The "Resource-Priority" header field can influence the behavior of SIP user agents (such as telephone gateways and IP telephones) and SIP proxies.  It does not directly influence the forwarding behavior of IP routers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polkddatej”Ähfilenamegrfc4412eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126337hkeywordsëbRPcRPHlpreferentialgpreemptipreemptedjpreemptionequeuecDSNdDRSNcWPScETSeQ.735dQ735hdisastereI.255eflashnflash-overridekobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateDf&granking<crfcd4412irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJCommunications Resource Priority for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7134∂habstracty]This document proposes an IANA Registration extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Reason Header to be included in a BYE Method Request as a result of a session preemption event, either at a user agent (UA), or somewhere in the network involving a reservation-based protocol such as the Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) or Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS).  This document does not attempt to address routers failing in the packet path; instead, it addresses a deliberate tear down of a flow between UAs, and informs the terminated UA(s) with an indication of what occurred. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polkddatej”Ähfilenamegrfc4411eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126336hkeywordsÖqResource-PrioritygpreemptipreemptedeQ.850mpreconditionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDhŒgranking;crfcd4411irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSExtending the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Reason Header for Preemption EventsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThe Selectively Reliable Multicast Protocol (SRMP) is a transport protocol, intended to deliver a mix of reliable and best-effort messages in an any-to-any multicast environment, where the best-effort traffic occurs in significantly greater volume than the reliable traffic and therefore can carry sequence numbers of reliable messages for loss detection.  SRMP is intended for use in a distributed simulation application environment, where only the latest value of reliable transmission for any particular data identifier requires delivery.  SRMP has two sublayers: a bundling sublayer handling message aggregation and congestion control, and a Selectively Reliable Transport (SRT) sublayer.  Selection between reliable and best-effort messages is performed by the application.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqGeorge Mason UnivdnameiM. Pullen¢kaffiliationqGeorge Mason UnivdnamegF. Zhao¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehD. Cohenddatej”thfilenamegrfc4410eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126335hkeywordsÑitransportkbest-effortcsrtxselectively reliable transportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD˛Egranking:crfcd4410irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Selectively Reliable Multicast Protocol (SRMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2This memo splits message submission from message relay, allowing each service to operate according to its own rules (for security, policy, etc.), and specifies what actions are to be taken by a submission server.

Message relay and final delivery are unaffected, and continue to use SMTP over port 25.

When conforming to this document, message submission uses the protocol specified here, normally over port 587.

This separation of function offers a number of benefits, including the ability to apply specific security or policy requirements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhQUALCOMMdnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej”chfilenamegrfc4409eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126334hkeywordsÑdsmtpxsimle mail transfer protocolbuajuser agentkobsoletedByÅd6409epagesopublicationDateDMK^granking9crfcd4409irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMessage Submission for MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyE-mail on the Internet can be forged in a number of ways.  In particular, existing protocols place no restriction on what a sending host can use as the reverse-path of a message or the domain given on the SMTP HELO/EHLO commands.  This document describes version 1 of the ender Policy Framework (SPF) protocol, whereby a domain may explicitly authorize the hosts that are allowed to use its domain name, and a receiving host may check such authorization.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegM. Wong¢kaffiliation`dnamejW. Schlittddatej”lhfilenamegrfc4408eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126333hkeywordsÇhspoofingcspfkobsoletedByÅd7208epages0opublicationDateDRf1granking8crfcd4408irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQSender Policy Framework (SPF) for Authorizing Use of Domains in E-Mail, Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6652∂habstractyBThis document defines an algorithm by which, given an e-mail message, one can extract the identity of the party that appears to have most proximately caused that message to be delivered.  This identity is called the Purported Responsible Address (PRA).This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamegJ. Lyonddatej”nhfilenamegrfc4407eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126332hkeywordsÇcpraxpurported responsible addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDRegranking7crfcd4407irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Purported Responsible Address in E-Mail MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty·Internet mail suffers from the fact that much unwanted mail is sent using spoofed addresses -- "spoofed" in this case means that the address is used without the permission of the domain owner.  This document describes a family of tests by which SMTP servers can determine whether an e-mail address in a received message was used with the permission of the owner of the domain contained in that e-mail address.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamegJ. Lyon¢kaffiliationipobox.comdnamegM. Wongddatej”nhfilenamegrfc4406eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126331hkeywordsÉxsimple mail transfer protocoldspamhspoofingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDRe
granking6crfcd4406irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Sender ID: Authenticating E-MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This memo defines an extension to the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) service that allows an SMTP client to specify the responsible submitter of an e-mail message.  The responsible submitter is the e-mail address of the entity most recently responsible for introducing a message into the transport stream.  This extension helps receiving e-mail servers efficiently determine whether the SMTP client is authorized to transmit mail on behalf of the responsible submitter's domain.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnameiE. Allman¢kaffiliationoMicrosoft Corp.dnamegH. Katzddatej”nhfilenamegrfc4405eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126330hkeywordsÉdspamhspoofinghphishingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDRdˇgranking5crfcd4405irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTSMTP Service Extension for Indicating the Responsible Submitter of an E-Mail MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing Fibre Channel Over TCP/IP (FCIP) entities, which are used to interconnect Fibre Channel (FC) fabrics with IP networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkF5 NetworksdnamelR. Natarajan¢kaffiliationxAccton Technology CorporationdnameoA. Rijhsinghaniddatej”àhfilenamegrfc4404eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126329hkeywordsÉcmibxmanagement information basemfcip-mgmt-mibkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateDhØgranking4crfcd4404irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCDefinitions of Managed Objects for Fibre Channel Over TCP/IP (FCIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This document defines the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAPv3) schema for representing Universal Description, Discovery, and Integration (UDDI) data types in an LDAP directory.  It defines the LDAP object class and attribute definitions and containment rules to model UDDI entities, defined in the UDDI version 3 information model, in an LDAPv3-compliant directory.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlNovell, Inc.dnamekB. Bergeson¢kaffiliationlNovell, Inc.dnamejK. Boogert¢kaffiliationvOracle India Pvt. Ltd.dnamepV. Nanjundaswamyddatej”Mhfilenamegrfc4403eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126328hkeywordsÅfLDAPv3kobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateD˛ígranking3crfcd4403irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Schema for Universal Description, Discovery, and Integration version 3 (UDDIv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOThis document defines the Pseudo-Random Function (PRF) for the Kerberos V mechanism for the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API), based on the PRF defined for the Kerberos V cryptographic framework, for keying application protocols given an established Kerberos V GSS-API security context. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSundnamekN. WilliamsddatejÀ hfilenamegrfc4402eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126327hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7802epagesopublicationDateCÒ<kgranking2crfcd4402irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex|A Pseudo-Random Function (PRF) for the Kerberos V Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyßThis document defines a Pseudo-Random Function (PRF) extension to the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) for keying application protocols given an established GSS-API security context.  The primary intended use of this function is to key secure session layers that do not or cannot use GSS-API per-message message integrity check (MIC) and wrap tokens for session protection. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamekN. Williamsddatej”âhfilenamegrfc4401eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126326hkeywordsÉtsecure session layerwmessage integrity checkcmickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÒ<1granking1crfcd4401irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuA Pseudo-Random Function (PRF) API Extension for the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCryptographic public keys are frequently published, and their authenticity is demonstrated by certificates.  A CERT resource record (RR) is defined so that such certificates and related certificate revocation lists can be stored in the Domain Name System (DNS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. Josefssonddatej”àhfilenamegrfc4398eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126325hkeywordsÉfSC-DNSjcryptologylauthenticitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD-»granking.crfcd4398irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Storing Certificates in the Domain Name System (DNS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6944∂habstracty)Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) has been developed by the IETF to facilitate the establishment of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in a variety of data plane technologies and across several architectural models. The ITU-T has specified an architecture for the control of Automatically Switched Optical Networks (ASON).

This document provides a lexicography for the interpretation of GMPLS terminology within the context of the ASON architecture.

It is important to note that GMPLS is applicable in a wider set of contexts than just ASON. The definitions presented in this document do not provide exclusive or complete interpretations of GMPLS concepts. This document simply allows the GMPLS terms to be applied within the ASON context. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamejI. Bryskin¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. FarrelddatejÀ◊hfilenamegrfc4397eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126324hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÒ<␀granking-crfcd4397irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex√A Lexicography for the Interpretation of Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Terminology within the Context of the ITU-T's Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies an RTP payload format for the transmission of 3GPP (3rd Generation Partnership Project) timed text.  3GPP timed text is a time-lined, decorated text media format with defined storage in a 3GP file.  Timed Text can be synchronized with audio/video contents and used in applications such as captioning, titling, and multimedia presentations.  In the following sections, the problems of streaming timed text are addressed, and a payload format for streaming 3GPP timed text over RTP is specified. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnamefJ. Rey¢kaffiliationiPanasonicdnameiY. Matsuiddatej”zhfilenamegrfc4396eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126323hkeywordsïd3GPPo3GPP timed textistreamingsreal-time streaminggtitlingndecorated textnscrolling textgkaraokephyperlinked textphighlighted textmblinking textohighlight colorjtext delayjtext stylehtext boxitext wrapktext samplessample descriptionsnmodifier boxeseUTF-8fUTF-16kobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateCˆ%´granking,crfcd4396irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRTP Payload Format for 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Timed TextdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyrThis document provides guidelines and recommendations for the definition of Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) schemes.  It also updates the process and IANA registry for URI schemes.  It obsoletes both RFC 2717 and RFC 2718.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnameiT. Hardie¢kaffiliationmAdobe SystemsdnamekL. Masinterddatej—6hfilenamegrfc4395eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126322hkeywordsÉxuniform resource identifierfsyntaxisemanticskobsoletedByÅd7595epagesopublicationDateCÒ;ëgranking+crfcd4395irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Guidelines and Registration Procedures for New URI SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Link Management Protocol (LMP) has been developed as part of the Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) protocol suite to manage Traffic Engineering (TE) resources and links.  The GMPLS control plane (routing and signaling) uses TE links for establishing Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  This memo describes the relationship of the LMP procedures to 'discovery' as defined in the International Telecommunication Union (ITU-T), and ongoing ITU-T work.  This document provides an overview of LMP in the context of the ITU-T Automatically Switched Optical Networks (ASON) and transport network terminology and relates it to the ITU-T discovery work to promote a common understanding for progressing the work of IETF and ITU-T.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamemO. Aboul-Magd¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekD. Brungard¢kaffiliationkSonos, Inc.dnamegJ. Lang¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej”Öhfilenamegrfc4394eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126321hkeywordsáegmplsdasonidiscoverycsdhcotnesonetcpdhkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÒ;/granking*crfcd4394irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A Transport Network View of the Link Management Protocol (LMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx…This document serves to register and document the standard MIME types associated with the 3GPP2 multimedia file format, which is part of the family based on the ISO Media File Format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQUALCOMMdnamelH. Garudadriddatej”:hfilenamegrfc4393eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126320hkeywordsÅx&third-generation partnership project 2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDE¸granking)crfcd4393irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2MIME Type Registrations for 3GPP2 Multimedia FilesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6381∂habstractyInfiniBand is a high-speed, channel-based interconnect between systems and devices.

This document presents an overview of the InfiniBand architecture. It further describes the requirements and guidelines for the transmission of IP over InfiniBand. Discussions in this document are applicable to both IPv4 and IPv6 unless explicitly specified. The encapsulation of IP over InfiniBand and the mechanism for IP address resolution on IB fabrics are covered in other documents. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejV. Kashyapddatej—Fhfilenamegrfc4392eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipoibdfullxipoib - IP over InfiniBanddnamerIP over InfiniBanddtypebwgbidjdoc-126319hkeywordsÉbibdipv4dipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD6°ßgranking(crfcd4392irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IP over InfiniBand (IPoIB) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document specifies a method for encapsulating and transmitting IPv4/IPv6 and Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) packets over InfiniBand (IB).  It describes the link-layer address to be used when resolving the IP addresses in IP over InfiniBand (IPoIB) subnets.  The document also describes the mapping from IP multicast addresses to InfiniBand multicast addresses.  In addition, this document defines the setup and configuration of IPoIB links. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamefJ. Chu¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejV. Kashyapddatej”=hfilenamegrfc4391eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipoibdfullxipoib - IP over InfiniBanddnamerIP over InfiniBanddtypebwgbidjdoc-126318hkeywordsÉxaddress resolution protocolcarpbibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD6°ügranking'crfcd4391irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Transmission of IP over InfiniBand (IPoIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8064∂habstracty∂IP over Infiniband (IPoIB) link-layer address is 20 octets long.  This is larger than the 16 octets reserved for the hardware address in a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol/Bootstrap Protocol (DHCP/BOOTP) message.  The above inequality imposes restrictions on the use of the DHCP message fields when used over an IPoIB network.  This document describes the use of DHCP message fields when implementing DHCP over IPoIB. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejV. Kashyapddatej”Åhfilenamegrfc4390eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipoibdfullxipoib - IP over InfiniBanddnamerIP over InfiniBanddtypebwgbidjdoc-126317hkeywordsÉibootstrapdbooteipoibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD6°ègranking&crfcd4390irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) over InfiniBanddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBridging multiple links into a single entity has several operational advantages.  A single subnet prefix is sufficient to support multiple physical links.  There is no need to allocate subnet numbers to the different networks, simplifying management.  Bridging some types of media requires network-layer support, however.  This document describes these cases and specifies the IP-layer support that enables bridging under these circumstances.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiM. Talwar¢kaffiliationqAll Play, No WorkdnamehC. Patelddatej”áhfilenamegrfc4389eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126316hkeywordsÅgndproxykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD<;4granking%crfcd4389irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Neighbor Discovery Proxies (ND Proxy)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyA Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol version 4 (DHCPv4) server is the authoritative source of IP addresses that it has provided to DHCPv4 clients.  Other processes and devices that already make use of DHCPv4 may need to access this information.  The leasequery protocol provides these processes and devices a lightweight way to access IP address information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednameiR. Woundy¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Kinnearddatej”áhfilenamegrfc4388eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126315hkeywordsÇfdhcpv4jip addresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDh\granking$crfcd4388irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) LeasequerydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6148∂habstractyÿThe protocol conventions described in this document satisfy some of the operational requirements of the Internet Public Key Infrastructure (PKI).  This document specifies the conventions for using the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP/HTTPS) as an interface mechanism to obtain certificates and certificate revocation lists (CRLs) from PKI repositories.  Additional mechanisms addressing PKIX operational requirements are specified in separate documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamejP. Gutmannddatej”whfilenamegrfc4387eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126314hkeywordsÇcpkixhypertext transfer protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÈHígranking#crfcd4387irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Operational Protocols: Certificate Store Access via HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstracty3This document defines a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) repository locator service.  The service makes use of DNS SRV records defined in accordance with RFC 2782.  The service enables certificate-using systems to locate PKI repositories.This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlEntrust Inc.dnameiS. Boeyen¢kaffiliationmVeriSign Inc.dnameoP. Hallam-Bakerddatej”àhfilenamegrfc4386eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126313hkeywordsÉcpkixpublic key infrastructuregdns srvkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„˘Êgranking"crfcd4386irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Repository Locator ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstracty|This document describes the preferred design of a Pseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) Control Word to be used over an MPLS packet switched network, and the Pseudowire Associated Channel Header.  The design of these fields is chosen so that an MPLS Label Switching Router performing MPLS payload inspection will not confuse a PWE3 payload with an IP payload. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejL. Martini¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelD. McPhersonddatej”áhfilenamegrfc4385eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126312hkeywordsÉxmultiprotocol label switchingwpacket switched networkx$pseudowire associated channel headerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCˆ%`granking!crfcd4385irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNPseudowire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) Control Word for Use over an MPLS PSNdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5586∑habstracty˙BGP communities (RFC 1997) are used by service providers for many purposes, including tagging of customer, peer, and geographically originated routes.  Such tagging is typically used to control the scope of redistribution of routes within a provider's network and to its peers and customers.  With the advent of large-scale BGP data collection (and associated research), it has become clear that the information carried in such communities is essential for a deeper understanding of the global routing system.  This memo defines standard (outbound) communities and their encodings for export to BGP route collectors.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyerddatej—:hfilenamegrfc4384eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126311hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„˘•granking crfcd4384irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberretotaletitlex#BGP Communities for Data CollectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo describes the use of the Timed Efficient Stream Loss-tolerant Authentication (RFC 4082) transform within the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP), to provide data origin authentication for multicast and broadcast data streams. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejM. Baugher¢kaffiliationxRoyal Institute of TechnologydnamejE. Carraraddatej”Ñhfilenamegrfc4383eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126310hkeywordsÇumulticast data streamubroadcast data streamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÒ;grankingcrfcd4383irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexxThe Use of Timed Efficient Stream Loss-Tolerant Authentication (TESLA) in the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects to configure and/or monitor Multiprotocol Label Switching Layer-3 Virtual Private Networks on a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switching Router (LSR) supporting this feature. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamepH. van der Lindeddatej”ehfilenamegrfc4382eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126309hkeywordsÜcmibxmanagement information basexmultiprotocol label switchingvlabel switching routerclsrrmpls-l3vpn-std-mibkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateCÌ·grankingcrfcd4382irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJMPLS/BGP Layer 3 Virtual Private Network (VPN) Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyçThis document analyses the security of the BGP/MPLS IP virtual private network (VPN) architecture that is described in RFC 4364, for the benefit of service providers and VPN users.

The analysis shows that BGP/MPLS IP VPN networks can be as secure as traditional layer-2 VPN services using Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) or Frame Relay. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. ZiningauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqCisco Systems IncdnamelM. Behringerddatej—;hfilenamegrfc4381eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126308hkeywordsÑpservice providercatmxasynchronous transfer modekframe relaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„˜Qgrankingcrfcd4381irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexGAnalysis of the Security of BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymWe propose here a service that enables nodes located behind one or more IPv4 Network Address Translations (NATs) to obtain IPv6 connectivity by tunneling packets over UDP; we call this the Teredo service.  Running the service requires the help of "Teredo servers" and "Teredo relays".  The Teredo servers are stateless, and only have to manage a small fraction of the traffic between Teredo clients; the Teredo relays act as IPv6 routers between the Teredo service and the "native" IPv6 Internet.  The relays can also provide interoperability with hosts using other transition mechanisms such as "6to4". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. HuitemaddatejÀ†hfilenamegrfc4380eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126307hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateC„˜grankingcrfcd4380irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKTeredo: Tunneling IPv6 over UDP through Network Address Translations (NATs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd6081d5991d9601∂habstractyåThis document describes a simple and efficient mechanism that can be used to detect data plane failures in Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs).  There are two parts to this document: information carried in an MPLS "echo request" and "echo reply" for the purposes of fault detection and isolation, and mechanisms for reliably sending the echo reply. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallowddatej”èhfilenamegrfc4379eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126306hkeywordsÅjdata planekobsoletedByÅd8029epages2opublicationDateC„ı˛grankingcrfcd4379irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDetecting Multi-Protocol Label Switched (MPLS) Data Plane FailuresdtypecrfciupdatedByàd7537d7506d6424d6425d5462d6426d6829d7743∂habstracty∑This document is a framework for how data plane protocols can be applied to operations and maintenance procedures for Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS).  The document is structured to outline how Operations and Management (OAM) functionality can be used to assist in fault, configuration, accounting, performance, and security management, commonly known by the acronym FCAPS.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehD. Allan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeauddatej”åhfilenamegrfc4378eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126305hkeywordsÇjdata planeefcapskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„ıwgrankingcrfcd4378irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA Framework for Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Operations and Management (OAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document specifies Operations and Management (OAM) requirements for Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS), as well as for applications of MPLS, such as pseudo-wire voice and virtual private network services.  These requirements have been gathered from network operators who have extensive experience deploying MPLS networks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Morrow¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehD. Allan¢kaffiliationmJapan TelecomdnamemS. MatsushimaddatejÀŒhfilenamegrfc4377eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126304hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„ı2grankingcrfcd4377irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Operations and Management (OAM) Requirements for Multi-Protocol Label Switched (MPLS) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇFloor control is a means to manage joint or exclusive access to shared resources in a (multiparty) conferencing environment.  Thereby, floor control complements other functions -- such as conference and media session setup, conference policy manipulation, and media control -- that are realized by other protocols.  This document defines the requirements for a floor control protocol for multiparty conferences in the context of an existing framework.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamenP. Koskelainen¢kaffiliationx!Helsinki University of TechnologydnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnameeX. Wuddatej”Öhfilenamegrfc4376eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdxcondfullxxcon - Centralized ConferencingdnamexCentralized Conferencingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126303hkeywordsÇpshared resourcesvmultiparty conferenceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÒ: grankingcrfcd4376irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Requirements for Floor Control ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`This document presents a list of requirements in support of Emergency Telecommunications Service (ETS) within a single administrative domain.  This document focuses on a specific set of administrative constraints and scope.  Solutions to these requirements are not presented in this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlbergddatej”Öhfilenamegrfc4375eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfieprepdfullx(ieprep - Internet Emergency PreparednessdnamexInternet Emergency Preparednessdtypebwgbidjdoc-126302hkeywordsÉhresourcentransit domainkstub domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÿ+Ògrankingcrfcd4375irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[Emergency Telecommunications Services (ETS) Requirements for a Single Administrative DomaindtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxıThis document describes the registration of the MIME sub-type application/xv+xml.  This sub-type is intended for use as a media descriptor for XHTML+Voice multimodal language documents.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiG. McCobbddatej”Öhfilenamegrfc4374eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126301hkeywordsÑdmimehsub-typepmedia descriptorkxhtml+voicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÿ+ìgrankingcrfcd4374irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!The application/xv+xml Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThe Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Bulk Update/Replication Protocol (LBURP) allows an LDAP client to perform a bulk update to an LDAP server. The protocol frames a sequenced set of update operations within a pair of LDAP extended operations to notify the server that the update operations in the framed set are related in such a way that the ordering of all operations can be preserved during processing even when they are sent asynchronously by the client. Update operations can be grouped within a single protocol message to maximize the efficiency of client-server communication.

The protocol is suitable for efficiently making a substantial set of updates to the entries in an LDAP server. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlNovell, Inc.dnamekR. Harrison¢kaffiliationlNovell, Inc.dnamenJ. Sermersheim¢kaffiliation`dnamegY. DongddatejÀ≈hfilenamegrfc4373eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126300hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCﬁ}Egrankingcrfcd4373irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexULightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Bulk Update/Replication Protocol (LBURP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a new Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) attribute, Chargeable-User-Identity.  This attribute can be used by a home network to identify a user for the purpose of roaming transactions that occur outside of the home network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejF. Adrangi¢kaffiliationsBridgewater SystemsdnamegA. Lior¢kaffiliationkTeliasoneradnamekJ. Korhonen¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughneyddatej”Khfilenamegrfc4372eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126299hkeywordsÑfradiusx*remote authentication dial-in user servicesroaming transactionlhome networkkobsoletedByÄepages
