ecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThe iFCP protocol (RFC 4172) provides Fibre Channel fabric functionality on an IP network in which TCP/IP switching and routing elements replace Fibre Channel components.  The iFCP protocol is used between iFCP Gateways.  This document provides a mechanism to monitor and control iFCP Gateway instances, and their associated sessions, using SNMP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrMcDATA CorporationdnamejK. Gibbons¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehC. Monia¢kaffiliationsRiverbed TechnologydnamehJ. Tseng¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamemF. Travostinoddatej”Éhfilenamegrfc4369eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126296hkeywordsÜcmibxmanagement information basedsnmpx"simple network management protocollifcp gatewaymifcp-mgmt-mibkobsoletedByÅd6173epagesopublicationDateCﬁ|Œgrankingcrfcd4369irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIDefinitions of Managed Objects for Internet Fibre Channel Protocol (iFCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This memo defines two MIB modules and corresponding MIB Object Definitions that describe how label-switching-controlled Frame-Relay and Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) interfaces can be managed given the interface stacking as defined in the MPLS-LSR-STD-MIB and MPLS-TE-STD-MIB. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehS. Hegdeddatej”Ñhfilenamegrfc4368eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126295hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basesmpls-lc-atm-std-mibrmpls-lc-fr-std-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC‡‚grankingcrfcd4368irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÜMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label-Controlled Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) and Frame-Relay Management Interface DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÎThe Domain Name System (DNS) provides an essential service on the Internet, mapping structured names to a variety of data, usually IP addresses.  These names appear in email addresses, Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs), and other application-layer identifiers that are often rendered to human users.  Because of this, there has been a strong demand to acquire names that have significance to people, through equivalence to registered trademarks, company names, types of services, and so on.  There is a danger in this trend; the humans and automata that consume and use such names will associate specific semantics with some names and thereby make assumptions about the services that are, or should be, provided by the hosts associated with the names.  Those assumptions can often be false, resulting in a variety of failure conditions.  This document discusses this problem in more detail and makes recommendations on how it can be avoided.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej”{hfilenamegrfc4367eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126294hkeywordsÅrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÈH◊grankingcrfcd4367irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex3What's in a Name: False Assumptions about DNS NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes extensions that may be used to add functionality to Transport Layer Security (TLS). It provides both generic extension mechanisms for the TLS handshake client and server hellos, and specific extensions using these generic mechanisms.

The extensions may be used by TLS clients and servers. The extensions are backwards compatible: communication is possible between TLS clients that support the extensions and TLS servers that do not support the extensions, and vice versa. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcBCIdnameoS. Blake-Wilson¢kaffiliationlRSA SecuritydnamejM. Nystrom¢kaffiliationoTransactionwarednamejD. Hopwood¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamelJ. Mikkelsen¢kaffiliationhVodafonednameiT. Wrightddatej”Çhfilenamegrfc4366eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126293hkeywordsÖitransporthprotocolelayernauthenticationgprivacykobsoletedByÇd6066d5246epagesopublicationDateDQ˜grankingcrfcd4366irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Transport Layer Security (TLS) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5746∂habstractxËThis document provides an Applicability Statement for the Virtual Private Network (VPN) solution described in RFC 4364 and other documents listed in the References section.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej»≥hfilenamegrfc4365eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126292hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateC„Ùﬁgrankingcrfcd4365irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGApplicability Statement for BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document describes a method by which a Service Provider may use an IP backbone to provide IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) for its customers.  This method uses a "peer model", in which the customers' edge routers (CE routers) send their routes to the Service Provider's edge routers (PE routers); there is no "overlay" visible to the customer's routing algorithm, and CE routers at different sites do not peer with each other.  Data packets are tunneled through the backbone, so that the core routers do not need to know the VPN routes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosen¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejY. Rekhterddatej—^hfilenamegrfc4364eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126291hkeywordsépservice providerkip backboneice routeripe routerfborderggatewayhprotocolmmultiprotocolelabeliswitchinglarchitecturegvirtualgprivatehnetworkskobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateC„Ùôgrankingcrfcd4364irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd4684d4577d5462∂habstractyêThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets. In particular, it defines two MIB modules for managing the capabilities of MAC bridges defined by the IEEE 802.1D-1998 (TM) MAC Bridges and the IEEE 802.1Q-2003 (TM) Virtual LAN (VLAN) standards for bridging between Local Area Network (LAN) segments. One MIB module defines objects for managing the 'Traffic Classes' and 'Enhanced Multicast Filtering' components of IEEE 802.1D-1998 and P802.1t-2001 (TM). The other MIB module defines objects for managing VLANs, as specified in IEEE 802.1Q-2003, P802.1u (TM), and P802.1v (TM).

Provisions are made for support of transparent bridging. Provisions are also made so that these objects apply to bridges connected by subnetworks other than LAN segments.

This memo supplements RFC 4188 and obsoletes RFC 2674. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegD. Levi¢kaffiliationrEffective SoftwarednamemD. Harringtonddatej”Çhfilenamegrfc4363eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbridgedfullsbridge - Bridge MIBdnamejBridge MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126290hkeywordsácmibxmanagement information basekmac bridgesotraffic classesxenhanced multicast filteringlp-bridge-miblq-bridge-mibkobsoletedByÄepagescopublicationDateC‡bgrankingcrfcd4363irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexpDefinitions of Managed Objects for Bridges with Traffic Classes, Multicast Filtering, and Virtual LAN ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊This document defines a ROHC (Robust Header Compression) profile for compression of IP/UDP/RTP (Internet Protocol/User Datagram Protocol/Real-Time Transport Protocol) packets, utilizing functionality provided by the lower layers to increase compression efficiency by completely eliminating the header for most packets during optimal operation.  The profile is built as an extension to the ROHC RTP profile.  It defines additional mechanisms needed in ROHC, states requirements on the assisting layer to guarantee transparency, and specifies general logic for compression and decompression related to the usage of the header-free packet format.  This document is a replacement for RFC 3242, which it obsoletes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletier¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekK. Sandlundddatej”Ähfilenamegrfc4362eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidj