estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNRObust Header Compression (ROHC): A Link-Layer Assisted Profile for IP/UDP/RTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4815∂habstractyçThis document specifies the format that is to be used for encoding Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol Version Four (DHCPv4) client identifiers, so that those identifiers will be interchangeable with identifiers used in the DHCPv6 protocol.  This document also addresses and corrects some problems in RFC 2131 and RFC 2132 with respect to the handling of DHCP client identifiers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamemB. Sommerfeldddatej”Åhfilenamegrfc4361eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126288hkeywordsÅfdhcpv6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÌ^granking	crfcd4361irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Node-specific Client Identifiers for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol Version Four (DHCPv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5494∂habstracty	This document describes the "extended community" BGP-4 attribute.  This attribute provides a mechanism for labeling information carried in BGP-4.  These labels can be used to control the distribution of this information, or for other applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Sangli¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiD. Tappan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. RekhterddatejÀ≤hfilenamegrfc4360eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126287hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„Ù?grankingcrfcd4360irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"BGP Extended Communities AttributedtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7606d7153∂habstractyThis memo describes the use of the RSA digital signature algorithm as an authentication algorithm within the revised IP Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) as described in RFC 4303 and the revised IP Authentication Header (AH) as described in RFC 4302.  The use of a digital signature algorithm, such as RSA, provides data origin authentication in applications when a secret key method (e.g., HMAC) does not provide this property.  One example is the use of ESP and AH to authenticate the sender of an IP multicast packet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Weisddatej”vhfilenamegrfc4359eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126286hkeywordsÇx!ip encapsulating security payloadqdigital signaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÿ+Kgrankingcrfcd4359irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjThe Use of RSA/SHA-1 Signatures within Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThis document describes the Namespace Identifier (NID) for Uniform Resource Namespace (URN) resources published by the Open Mobile Alliance (OMA).  OMA defines and manages resources that utilize this URN name model.  Management activities for these and other resource types are provided by the Open Mobile Naming Authority (OMNA).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtOpen Mobile AlliancednamehD. Smithddatej”Åhfilenamegrfc4358eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126285hkeywordsÑcnidtnamespace identifierdomnaxopen mobile naming authoritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC‡¿grankingcrfcd4358irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Open Mobile Alliance (OMA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the cryptographic algorithms and parameters supplementary to the original GOST specifications, GOST 28147-89, GOST R 34.10-94, GOST R 34.10-2001, and GOST R 34.11-94, for use in Internet applications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamehV. Popov¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamekI. Kurepkin¢kaffiliationjCRYPTO-PROdnamekS. Leontievddatej”xhfilenamegrfc4357eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126284hkeywordsòfcpalgsjpublic-keygone-waydhasheblockfcipheriencyptionjdecryptioncmacdhmaccprfdwrapfunwrapcukmckekckeyiparameterjderivationfdigestccbcgcounterdmodegdigitalisignaturekobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateCﬁ|igrankingcrfcd4357irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇAdditional Cryptographic Algorithms for Use with GOST 28147-89, GOST R 34.10-94, GOST R 34.10-2001, and GOST R 34.11-94 AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°The cellular telephone industry has defined a service known as the Multimedia Messaging Service (MMS). This service uses formats and protocols that are similar to, but differ in key ways from, those used in Internet mail.

One important difference between MMS and Internet Mail is that MMS uses headers that start with "X-Mms-" to carry a variety of user agent- and server-related information elements.

This document specifies how to exchange messages between these two services, including mapping information elements as used in MMS X-Mms-* headers as well as delivery and disposition reports, to and from that used in SMTP and Internet message headers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejR. Gellensddatej”fhfilenamegrfc4356eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhlemonadedfullxQlemonade - Enhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service EnvironmentsdnamexFEnhancements to Internet email to Support Diverse Service Environmentsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126283hkeywordsÇrcellular telephoneex-mmskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC≈∏ßgrankingcrfcd4356irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMapping Between the Multimedia Messaging Service (MMS) and Internet MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁThis document registers the Enumservices "email", "fax", "sms", "ems", and "mms" using the URI schemes 'tel:' and 'mailto:' as per the IANA registration process defined in the ENUM specification RFC 3761. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjSiemens AGdnamekR. Brandner¢kaffiliationxSiemens Roke Manor ResearchdnameiL. Conroy¢kaffiliationeOefegdnamejR. Stastnyddatej”dhfilenamegrfc4355eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126282hkeywordsÅrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC≈∏Bgrankingcrfcd4355irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@IANA Registration for Enumservices email, fax, mms, ems, and smsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractyThe Open Mobile Alliance (OMA) is defining the Push-to-talk over Cellular (PoC) service where SIP is the protocol used to establish half-duplex media sessions across different participants, to send instant messages, etc.  This document defines a SIP event package to support publication, subscription, and notification of additional capabilities required by the PoC service.  This SIP event package is applicable to the PoC service and may not be applicable to the general Internet.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepM. Garcia-Martinddatej”Ähfilenamegrfc4354eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126281hkeywordsÇcomatopen mobile alliancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCŒÍ grankingcrfcd4354irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexíA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Event Package and Data Format for Various Settings in Support for the Push-to-Talk over Cellular (PoC) ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) supports the initiation, modification, and termination of media sessions between user agents.  These sessions are managed by SIP dialogs, which represent a SIP relationship between a pair of user agents.  Because dialogs are between pairs of user agents, SIP's usage for two-party communications (such as a phone call), is obvious.  Communications sessions with multiple participants, generally known as conferencing, are more complicated.  This document defines a framework for how such conferencing can occur.  This framework describes the overall architecture, terminology, and protocol components needed for multi-party conferencing.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. RosenbergddatejÀ≥hfilenamegrfc4353eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126280hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÌÀgrankingcrfcd4353irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGA Framework for Conferencing with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document specifies a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for Extended Adaptive Multi-Rate Wideband (AMR-WB+) encoded audio signals.  The AMR-WB+ codec is an audio extension of the AMR-WB speech codec.  It encompasses the AMR-WB frame types and a number of new frame types designed to support high-quality music and speech.  A media type registration for AMR-WB+ is included in this specification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Sjoberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelA. Lakaniemi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiS. Wengerddatej”hfilenamegrfc4352eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126279hkeywordsÇxreal-time transport protocolmaudio signalskobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateCŒÈ›granking␀crfcd4352irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVRTP Payload Format for the Extended Adaptive Multi-Rate Wideband (AMR-WB+) Audio CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThis memo describes how to carry real-time text conversation session contents in RTP packets. Text conversation session contents are specified in ITU-T Recommendation T.140.

One payload format is described for transmitting audio and text data within a single RTP session.

This RTP payload description recommends a method to include redundant text from already transmitted packets in order to reduce the risk of text loss caused by packet loss. This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjOmnitor ABdnamelG. Hellstrom¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Jonesddatej”~hfilenamegrfc4351eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126278hkeywordsÅxitu-t recommendation t.140kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC≈∑‡grankingˇcrfcd4351irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) Payload for Text Conversation Interleaved in an Audio StreamdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace Identification (NID)convention as prescribed by the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) for identifying, naming, assigning, and managing persistent resources and XML artefacts for the New Zealand Government.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvNew Zealand GovernmentdnamekF. Hendrikx¢kaffiliationvNew Zealand GovernmentdnameiC. Wallisddatej”}hfilenamegrfc4350eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126277hkeywordsÇcnidxnamespace identificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„Û°granking˛crfcd4350irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Formal Namespace for the New Zealand GovernmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	The Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol, Version 3, (L2TPv3) defines a protocol for tunneling a variety of data link protocols over IP networks.  This document describes the specifics of how to tunnel High-Level Data Link Control (HDLC) frames over L2TPv3. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelC. Pignataro¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Townsleyddatej”|hfilenamegrfc4349eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126276hkeywordsÅjpseudowirekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCˆ$Ógranking˝crfcd4349irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^High-Level Data Link Control (HDLC) Frames over Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol, Version 3 (L2TPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5641∂habstracty›This document specifies a real-time transport protocol (RTP) payload format to be used for the Variable-Rate Multimode Wideband (VMR-WB) speech codec. The payload format is designed to be able to interoperate with existing VMR-WB transport formats on non-IP networks. A media type registration is included for VMR-WB RTP payload format.

VMR-WB is a variable-rate multimode wideband speech codec that has a number of operating modes, one of which is interoperable with AMR-WB (i.e., RFC 3267) audio codec at certain rates. Therefore, provisions have been made in this document to facilitate and simplify data packet exchange between VMR-WB and AMR-WB in the interoperable mode with no transcoding function involved. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Ahmadiddatej”hfilenamegrfc4348eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126275hkeywordsÇlspeech codecx-variable-rate multicode wideband speech codeckobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateC≈∑ägranking¸crfcd4348irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoReal-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) Payload Format for the Variable-Rate Multimode Wideband (VMR-WB) Audio CodecdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4424∂habstractyThis document specifies Version 1.0 of the Datagram Transport Layer Security (DTLS) protocol.  The DTLS protocol provides communications privacy for datagram protocols.  The protocol allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, or message forgery.  The DTLS protocol is based on the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol and provides equivalent security guarantees.  Datagram semantics of the underlying transport are preserved by the DTLS protocol.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamekN. Modaduguddatej”khfilenamegrfc4347eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126274hkeywordsÅddtlskobsoletedByÅd6347epagesopublicationDateDO¢granking˚crfcd4347irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Datagram Transport Layer SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5746d7507∂habstracty+This document specifies Version 1.1 of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol.  The TLS protocol provides communications security over the Internet.  The protocol allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, or message forgery.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnameiT. Dierks¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. RescorladdatejÀ¢hfilenamegrfc4346eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126273hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5246epagesWopublicationDateDOígranking˙crfcd4346irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.1dtypecrfciupdatedByàd6176d4680d4681d5746d7465d7919d7507d4366∂habstractxµThis document specifies methods of using the Arcfour cipher in the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol that mitigate the weakness of the cipher's key-scheduling algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Harrisddatej”zhfilenamegrfc4345eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126272hkeywordsÇnarcfour cipherxkey scheduling algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC’Ppgranking˘crfcd4345irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJImproved Arcfour Modes for the Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Layer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^Researchers have discovered that the authenticated encryption portion of the current SSH Transport Protocol is vulnerable to several attacks.

This document describes new symmetric encryption methods for the Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Protocol and gives specific recommendations on how frequently SSH implementations should rekey. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtThammasat UniversitydnamejM. Bellare¢kaffiliationtThammasat UniversitydnamehT. Kohno¢kaffiliationtThammasat UniversitydnamemC. Namprempreddatej”}hfilenamegrfc4344eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126271hkeywordsÅerekeykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC≈∑3granking¯crfcd4344irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7The Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Layer Encryption ModesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡Domain Name System (DNS) names are "case insensitive".  This document explains exactly what that means and provides a clear specification of the rules.  This clarification updates RFCs 1034, 1035, and 2181. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatejÀπhfilenamegrfc4343eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126270hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateC≈∂◊granking˜crfcd4343irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Domain Name System (DNS) Case Insensitivity ClarificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5890∂habstractyTThis document contains the profile for Congestion Control Identifier 3, TCP-Friendly Rate Control (TFRC), in the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP).  CCID 3 should be used by senders that want a TCP-friendly sending rate, possibly with Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN), while minimizing abrupt rate changes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiE. Kohler¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnameiJ. Padhyeddatej”phfilenamegrfc4342eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126269hkeywordsÑrtransport protocolcecnx explicit congestion notificationeccid3kobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateD0ºtgrankingˆcrfcd4342irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqProfile for Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) Congestion Control ID 3: TCP-Friendly Rate Control (TFRC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd6323d8311d5348∂habstracty‰This document contains the profile for Congestion Control Identifier 2 (CCID 2), TCP-like Congestion Control, in the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP).  CCID 2 should be used by senders who would like to take advantage of the available bandwidth in an environment with rapidly changing conditions, and who are able to adapt to the abrupt changes in the congestion window typical of TCP's Additive Increase Multiplicative Decrease (AIMD) congestion control. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiE. Kohlerddatej”ohfilenamegrfc4341eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126268hkeywordsÉrtransport protocoldamidx)additive increase multiplicative decreasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD-»ègrankingıcrfcd4341irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlProfile for Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) Congestion Control ID 2: TCP-like Congestion ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8311∂habstracty^The Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP) is a transport protocol that provides bidirectional unicast connections of congestion-controlled unreliable datagrams.  DCCP is suitable for applications that transfer fairly large amounts of data and that can benefit from control over the tradeoff between timeliness and reliability. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiE. Kohler¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floydddatej”phfilenamegrfc4340eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126267hkeywordsÅrtransport protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesÅopublicationDateD-»cgrankingÙcrfcd4340irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÑd6773d6335d5595d5596∂habstractyœThis document describes three approaches for IPv6 recursive DNS server address configuration.  It details the operational attributes of three solutions: RA option, DHCPv6 option, and well-known anycast addresses for recursive DNS servers.  Additionally, it suggests the deployment scenarios in four kinds of networks (ISP, enterprise, 3GPP, and unmanaged networks) considering multi-solution resolution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxETRI/University of MinnesotadnamehJ. Jeongddatej”xhfilenamegrfc4339eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126266hkeywordsÖrdomain name serverqinternet protocoluaddress configurationfdhcpv6x#dynamic host configuration protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDhãgrankingÛcrfcd4339irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<IPv6 Host Configuration of DNS Server Information ApproachesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶This document specifies the way of encapsulating IPv6, IPv4, and Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) packets over Fibre Channel. This document also specifies the method of forming IPv6 link-local addresses and statelessly autoconfigured IPv6 addresses on Fibre Channel networks, and a mechanism to perform IPv4 address resolution over Fibre Channel networks.

This document obsoletes RFC 2625 and RFC 3831. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSanti¢kaffiliationrQLogic CorporationdnamejC. Carlson¢kaffiliationfEmulexdnamehR. Nixonddatej”âhfilenamegrfc4338eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126265hkeywordsÇrlink local addressrlink-local addresskobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateCÿ*0grankingÚcrfcd4338irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Transmission of IPv6, IPv4, and Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) Packets over Fibre ChanneldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5494d8064∂habstractxÆThis document defines the standard MIME types associated with MP4 files.  It also recommends use of registered MIME types according to the type of contents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationknet&tv Inc.dnamefY. Lim¢kaffiliationnApple ComputerdnameiD. SingerddatejÀ∞hfilenamegrfc4337eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126264hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateDÏgrankingÒcrfcd4337irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!MIME Type Registration for MPEG-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6381∂habstractyãThis document describes for the historical record the motivation behind the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP), an unreliable transport protocol incorporating end-to-end congestion control.  DCCP implements a congestion-controlled, unreliable flow of datagrams for use by applications such as streaming media or on-line games.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameiE. KohlerddatejÀ∫hfilenamegrfc4336eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymddccpdfullx+dccp - Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldnamex$Datagram Congestion Control Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126263hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateD-»Wgrankingcrfcd4336irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEProblem Statement for the Datagram Congestion Control Protocol (DCCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxâThe Session Channel Break Extension provides a means to send a BREAK signal over a Secure Shell (SSH) terminal session. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpVanDyke SoftwarednamelJ. Galbraith¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamejP. RemakerddatejÀ∑hfilenamegrfc4335eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126262hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC‡˘grankingÔcrfcd4335irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6The Secure Shell (SSH) Session Channel Break ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˘This document defines two Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) extended key usage values and a public key certificate extension to carry Wireless LAN (WLAN) System Service identifiers (SSIDs).  This document obsoletes RFC 3770. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehT. Mooreddatej”yhfilenamegrfc4334eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126261hkeywordsÜceapx"extensible authentication protocollwireless landwlanxsystem service identifierdssidkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„ÛmgrankingÓcrfcd4334irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexàCertificate Extensions and Attributes Supporting Authentication in Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) and Wireless Local Area Networks (WLAN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty>This memo outlines the guidelines for selection of members of the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee, and describes the selection process used by the IAB and the IESG.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerBrian E. CarpentergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnenddatej”}hfilenamegrfc4333eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126260hkeywordsÑciaddiasax$ietf administrative support activityxietf administrative directorkobsoletedByÅd8711epages	opublicationDateCßb»grankingÌcrfcd4333irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexZThe IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC) Member Selection Guidelines and ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyJAn IP device requires basic host configuration to be able to communicate.  For example, it will typically require an IP address and the address of a DNS server.  This information is configured statically or obtained dynamically using Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) or Point-to-Point Protocol/IP Control Protocol (PPP/IPCP).  However, both DHCP and PPP/IPCP provide host configuration based on the access network.  In Mobile IPv4, the registration process boots up a Mobile Node at an access network, also known as a foreign network.  The information to configure the host needs to be based on the home network.  This document describes the Cisco vendor-specific extensions to Mobile IPv4 to provide the base host configuration in Registration Request and Reply messages.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret CullengauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Patel¢kaffiliationtFlarion TechnologiesdnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationwBirdstep Technology ASAdnamekE. Klovningddatej”Ohfilenamegrfc4332eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126259hkeywordsÜx#dynamic host configuration protocolddhcpnpoint-to-pointsip control protocolcpppdipcpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC´I€grankingÏcrfcd4332irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex1Cisco's Mobile IPv4 Host Configuration ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyWeb Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) servers are frequently deployed with quota (size) limitations.  This document discusses the properties and minor behaviors needed for clients to interoperate with quota (size) implementations on WebDAV repositories. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqNetwork ResonancednameiB. Korver¢kaffiliationdOSAFdnamelL. Dusseaultddatej”zhfilenamegrfc4331eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfwebdavdfullx1webdav - WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdnamex(WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdtypebwgbidjdoc-126258hkeywordsÅfwebdavkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateDh/grankingÎcrfcd4331irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTQuota and Size Properties for Distributed Authoring and Versioning (DAV) CollectionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty5This memorandum describes the Simple Network Time Protocol Version 4 (SNTPv4), which is a subset of the Network Time Protocol (NTP) used to synchronize computer clocks in the Internet. SNTPv4 can be used when the ultimate performance of a full NTP implementation based on RFC 1305 is neither needed nor justified. When operating with current and previous NTP and SNTP versions, SNTPv4 requires no changes to the specifications or known implementations, but rather clarifies certain design features that allow operation in a simple, stateless remote-procedure call (RPC) mode with accuracy and reliability expectations similar to the UDP/TIME protocol described in RFC 868.

This memorandum obsoletes RFC 1769, which describes SNTP Version 3 (SNTPv3), and RFC 2030, which describes SNTPv4. Its purpose is to correct certain inconsistencies in the previous documents and to clarify header formats and protocol operations for NTPv3 (IPv4) and SNTPv4 (IPv4, IPv6, and OSI), which are also used for SNTP. A further purpose is to provide guidance for home and business client implementations for routers and other consumer devices to protect the server population from abuse. A working knowledge of the NTPv3 specification, RFC 1305, is not required for an implementation of SNTP. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas NartengauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of DelawarednamehD. Millsddatej—?hfilenamegrfc4330eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126257hkeywordsÖcNTPdtimehcomputereclockosynchronizationkobsoletedByÅd5905epagesopublicationDateC‡XgrankingÍcrfcd4330irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexDSimple Network Time Protocol (SNTP) Version 4 for IPv4, IPv6 and OSIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÏThis document describes the registration of media types for the ECMAScript and JavaScript programming languages and conformance requirements for implementations of these types.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelB. Hoehrmannddatej”lhfilenamegrfc4329eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126256hkeywordsÖjJavaScriptjEMACScriptdmimefscriptgsubtypekobsoletedByÅd9239epagesopublicationDateDOägrankingÈcrfcd4329irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuScripting Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2This document is a companion to the Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) signaling documents.  It describes the technology-specific information needed to extend GMPLS signaling to control Optical Transport Networks (OTN); it also includes the so-called pre-OTN developments. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej”Ohfilenamegrfc4328eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126255hkeywordsÉcotnxoptical transport networksgpre-otnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC√KgrankingËcrfcd4328irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Extensions for G.709 Optical Transport Networks ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7139∂habstractx˘This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling the Link Management Protocol (LMP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Dubuc¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationkSonos, Inc.dnamegJ. Lang¢kaffiliationrHammerhead SystemsdnamekE. McGinnisddatej—ahfilenamegrfc4327eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126254hkeywordsÅglmp-mibkobsoletedByÅd4631epagesRopublicationDateC»SÒgrankingÁcrfcd4327irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Link Management Protocol (LMP) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThe MPEG-2 Transport Stream (TS) has been widely accepted not only for providing digital TV services, but also as a subnetwork technology for building IP networks.

This document describes a Unidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE) mechanism for the transport of IPv4 and IPv6 Datagrams and other network protocol packets directly over the ISO MPEG-2 Transport Stream as TS Private Data. ULE specifies a base encapsulation format and supports an extension format that allows it to carry additional header information to assist in network/Receiver processing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationvUniversity of SalzburgdnameqB. Collini-NockerddatejÀµhfilenamegrfc4326eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipdvbdfullsipdvb - IP over DVBdnamekIP over DVBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126253hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateC¨o$grankingÊcrfcd4326irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextUnidirectional Lightweight Encapsulation (ULE) for Transmission of IP Datagrams over an MPEG-2 Transport Stream (TS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7280∂habstractyEThis document updates RFC 3280 by defining the Authority Information Access Certificate Revocation List (CRL) extension.  RFC 3280 defines the Authority Information Access certificate extension using the same syntax.  The CRL extension provides a means of discovering and retrieving CRL issuer certificates. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej”zhfilenamegrfc4325eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126252hkeywordsÅrissuer certificatekobsoletedByÅd5280epagesopublicationDateC¢6grankingÂcrfcd4325irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Authority Information Access Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThe Calendar Access Protocol (CAP) described in this memo permits a Calendar User (CU) to utilize a Calendar User Agent (CUA) to access an iCAL-based Calendar Store (CS).  At the time of this writing, three vendors are implementing CAP, but it has already been determined that some changes are needed.  In order to get implementation experience, the participants felt that a CAP specification is needed to preserve many years of work.  Many properties in CAP which have had many years of debate, can be used by other iCalendar protocols.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoIntelliCal, LLCdnamehD. Royer¢kaffiliationfOraclednameiG. Babics¢kaffiliationdeBaydnamejS. Mansourddatej”^hfilenamegrfc4324eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126251hkeywordsámcalendar userbcuscalendar user agentccuadicalncalender storebcskobsoletedByÄepagesÉopublicationDateC©®∞granking‰crfcd4324irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCalendar Access Protocol (CAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a basic set of managed objects for SNMP-based management of extended QoS features of Cable Modems (CMs) and Cable Modem Termination Systems (CMTSs) conforming to the Data over Cable System (DOCSIS) specifications versions 1.1 and 2.0. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlMotorola BCSdnamejM. Patrick¢kaffiliationlMotorola BCSdnameiW. Murwinddatej—Qhfilenamegrfc4323eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-126250hkeywordsádsnmpx"simple network management protocolbcmkcable modemdcmtsxcable modem termination systemqdocs-ietf-qos-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesYopublicationDateCÿ*Ógranking„crfcd4323irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnData Over Cable System Interface Specification Quality of Service Management Information Base (DOCSIS-QoS MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141µhabstractyThis document describes opportunistic encryption (OE) as designed and implemented by the Linux FreeS/WAN project. OE uses the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) and IPsec protocols. The objective is to allow encryption for secure communication without any pre-arrangement specific to the pair of systems involved. DNS is used to distribute the public keys of each system involved. This is resistant to passive attacks. The use of DNS Security (DNSSEC) secures this system against active attackers as well.

As a result, the administrative overhead is reduced from the square of the number of systems to a linear dependence, and it becomes possible to make secure communication the default even when the partner is not known in advance. This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcSSWdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationfMimosadnameoD.H. Redelmeierddatej—=hfilenamegrfc4322eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-126249hkeywordsÜboeolinux frees/waneipseccdnsqdomain name spaceldns securitykobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateC¨o»granking‚crfcd4322irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Opportunistic Encryption using the Internet Key Exchange (IKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx√This document describes several problems that have been identified with the Session Initiation Protocol's (SIP) non-INVITE transaction.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnameiR. SparksddatejÀ®hfilenamegrfc4321eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126248hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateCŒÈágranking·crfcd4321irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbProblems Identified Associated with the Session Initiation Protocol's (SIP) Non-INVITE TransactiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’This document describes modifications to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) to address problems that have been identified with the SIP non-INVITE transaction.  These modifications reduce the probability of messages losing the race condition inherent in the non-INVITE transaction and reduce useless network traffic.  They also improve the robustness of SIP networks when elements stop responding.  These changes update behavior defined in RFC 3261. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnameiR. SparksddatejÀ®hfilenamegrfc4320eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126247hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCŒÈ/granking‡crfcd4320irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhActions Addressing Identified Issues with the Session Initiation Protocol's (SIP) Non-INVITE TransactiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing High Bit-Rate Digital Subscriber Line (DSL) - 2nd generation (HDSL2) and Single-Pair High-Speed Digital Subscriber Line (SHDSL) interfaces.  This document introduces extensions to several objects and textual conventions defined in HDSL2-SHDSL-Line MIB (RFC 3276).  This document obsoletes RFC 3276. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxZhone Technologies, Inc.dnamehC. Sikes¢kaffiliationxPESA Switching Systems, Inc.dnamefB. Ray¢kaffiliationkAlcatel USAdnamegR. Abbiddatej”jhfilenamegrfc4319eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126246hkeywordsÑcmibxmanagement information basethdsl2-shdsl-line-mibjinterfaceskobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDateC¢‡grankingﬂcrfcd4319irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexéDefinitions of Managed Objects for High Bit-Rate DSL - 2nd generation (HDSL2) and Single-Pair High-Speed Digital Subscriber Line (SHDSL) LinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis memo defines an SMIv2 MIB module for managing the Rapid Spanning Tree capability defined by the IEEE P802.1t and P802.1w amendments to IEEE Std 802.1D-1998 for bridging between Local Area Network (LAN) segments.  The objects in this MIB are defined to apply both to transparent bridging and to bridges connected by subnetworks other than LAN segments. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegD. Levi¢kaffiliationrEffective SoftwarednamemD. Harringtonddatej”xhfilenamegrfc4318eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbridgedfullsbridge - Bridge MIBdnamejBridge MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126245hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basex"simple network management protocolttransparent bridginghrstp-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC¨nÜgrankingﬁcrfcd4318irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDefinitions of Managed Objects for Bridges with Rapid Spanning Tree ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThis document gives examples of Session Description Protocol (SDP) offer/answer exchanges.  Examples include codec negotiation and selection, hold and resume, and addition and deletion of media streams.  The examples show multiple media types, bidirectional, unidirectional, inactive streams, and dynamic payload types.  Common Third Party Call Control (3pcc) examples are also given.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqTello CorporationdnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnameiR. SparksddatejÀ≤hfilenamegrfc4317eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126244hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC´IÜgranking›crfcd4317irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Session Description Protocol (SDP) Offer/Answer ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractySThis specification extends the Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning Protocol (WebDAV) to support datatyping.  Protocol elements are defined to let clients and servers specify the datatype, and to instruct the WebDAV method PROPFIND to return datatype information.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschkeddatej—Nhfilenamegrfc4316eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126243hkeywordsÇidatatyinghpropfindkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCùÿˇgranking‹crfcd4316irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexJDatatypes for Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) PropertiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe UIDPLUS extension of the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) provides a set of features intended to reduce the amount of time and resources used by some client operations.  The features in UIDPLUS are primarily intended for disconnected-use clients. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Crispinddatej”vhfilenamegrfc4315eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126242hkeywordsÑjIMAP4UIDPLx internet message access protocolldisconnectedioperationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC©®\granking€crfcd4315irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - UIDPLUS extensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThe Access Control List (ACL) extension (RFC 2086) of the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) permits mailbox access control lists to be retrieved and manipulated through the IMAP protocol.

This document is a revision of RFC 2086. It defines several new access control rights and clarifies which rights are required for different IMAP commands. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej”ohfilenamegrfc4314eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgimapextdfullx4imapext - Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondnamex*Internet Message Access Protocol Extensiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126241hkeywordsÑiIMAP4-ACLgControldListx internet message access protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCù–Øgranking⁄crfcd4314irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)IMAP4 Access Control List (ACL) ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document outlines the needs and requirements for a protocol to control distributed speech processing of audio streams.  By speech processing, this document specifically means automatic speech recognition (ASR), speaker recognition -- which includes both speaker identification (SI) and speaker verification (SV) -- and text-to-speech (TTS).  Other IETF protocols, such as SIP and Real Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP), address rendezvous and control for generalized media streams.  However, speech processing presents additional requirements that none of the extant IETF protocols address.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegD. Oranddatej”ghfilenamegrfc4313eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhspeechscdfullx"speechsc - Speech Services ControldnamewSpeech Services Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126240hkeywordsàqspeech processingmaudio streamsbsivspeaker identificationbsvtspeaker verificationcttsntext to speechkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCù–IgrankingŸcrfcd4313irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex£Requirements for Distributed Control of Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR), Speaker Identification/Speaker Verification (SI/SV), and Text-to-Speech (TTS) ResourcesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the use of the Camellia block cipher algorithm in Cipher Block Chaining Mode, with an explicit Initialization Vector, as a confidentiality mechanism within the context of the IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamegA. Kato¢kaffiliationx Sony Computer Entertainment Inc.dnameiS. Moriai¢kaffiliationx*Nippon Telegraph and Telephone CorporationdnamehM. Kandaddatej”chfilenamegrfc4312eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126239hkeywordsÜxcipher block chaining modeuinitialization vectorbivcespxencapsulating security payloadkip securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC´I(grankingÿcrfcd4312irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4The Camellia Cipher Algorithm and Its Use With IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThe original IPv6 conceptual sending algorithm does not do load sharing among equivalent IPv6 routers, and suggests schemes that can be problematic in practice.  This document updates the conceptual sending algorithm in RFC 2461 so that traffic to different destinations can be distributed among routers in an efficient fashion. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej”ohfilenamegrfc4311eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126238hkeywordsÇxinternet protocol version 6xconceptual sending algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCg Ägranking◊crfcd4311irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex IPv6 Host-to-Router Load SharingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension mapping for the provisioning and management of Domain Name System security extensions (DNSSEC) for domain names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, this mapping extends the EPP domain name mapping to provide additional features required for the provisioning of DNS security extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”whfilenamegrfc4310eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126237hkeywordsÇfdnssecx&domain name system security extensionskobsoletedByÅd5910epagesopublicationDateCî‚:granking÷crfcd4310irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcDomain Name System (DNS) Security Extensions Mapping for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This document describes the use of Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) in Counter with CBC-MAC (CCM) Mode, with an explicit initialization vector (IV), as an IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) mechanism to provide confidentiality, data origin authentication, and connectionless integrity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej—Vhfilenamegrfc4309eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126236hkeywordsÜlcbc-mac modeuinitialization vectorbivoconfidentialityxdata origin authenticationxconnectionless integritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC´HØgranking’crfcd4309irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaUsing Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) CCM Mode with IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThe IPsec, Internet Key Exchange (IKE), and IKEv2 protocols rely on security algorithms to provide privacy and authentication between the initiator and responder.  There are many such algorithms available, and two IPsec systems cannot interoperate unless they are using the same algorithms.  This document specifies optional suites of algorithms and attributes that can be used to simplify the administration of IPsec when used in manual keying mode, with IKEv1 or with IKEv2. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej—Ihfilenamegrfc4308eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126235hkeywordsÖcikeuinternet key exchangeeikev2ssecurity algorithmseikev1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC´Hjgranking‘crfcd4308irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCryptographic Suites for IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊The IPsec series of protocols makes use of various cryptographic algorithms in order to provide security services.  The Internet Key Exchange (IKE (RFC 2409) and IKEv2) provide a mechanism to negotiate which algorithms should be used in any given association.  However, to ensure interoperability between disparate implementations, it is necessary to specify a set of mandatory-to-implement algorithms to ensure that there is at least one algorithm that all implementations will have available.  This document defines the current set of algorithms that are mandatory to implement as part of IKEv2, as well as algorithms that should be implemented because they may be promoted to mandatory at some future time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx%Massachusetts Institute of TechnologydnamekJ. Schillerddatej—Khfilenamegrfc4307eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126234hkeywordsÉeipseccikeuinternet key exchangekobsoletedByÅd8247epagesopublicationDateC´H'granking”crfcd4307irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOCryptographic Algorithms for Use in the Internet Key Exchange Version 2 (IKEv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙This document describes version 2 of the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) protocol. IKE is a component of IPsec used for performing mutual authentication and establishing and maintaining security associations (SAs).

This version of the IKE specification combines the contents of what were previously separate documents, including Internet Security Association and Key Management Protocol (ISAKMP, RFC 2408), IKE (RFC 2409), the Internet Domain of Interpretation (DOI, RFC 2407), Network Address Translation (NAT) Traversal, Legacy authentication, and remote address acquisition.

Version 2 of IKE does not interoperate with version 1, but it has enough of the header format in common that both versions can unambiguously run over the same UDP port. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Kaufmanddatej—Jhfilenamegrfc4306eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126233hkeywordsçiISAKMPSECeipsechinternethprotocolhsecuritykassociationckeyjmanagementlcryptographynauthenticationcIKEfoakleyfisakmpkobsoletedByÅd5996epagescopublicationDateC´Gægranking“crfcd4306irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Internet Key Exchange (IKEv2) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5282∂habstractyËThe IPsec series of protocols makes use of various cryptographic algorithms in order to provide security services.  The Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and the Authentication Header (AH) provide two mechanisms for protecting data being sent over an IPsec Security Association (SA).  To ensure interoperability between disparate implementations, it is necessary to specify a set of mandatory-to-implement algorithms to ensure that there is at least one algorithm that all implementations will have available.  This document defines the current set of mandatory-to-implement algorithms for ESP and AH as well as specifying algorithms that should be implemented because they may be promoted to mandatory at some future time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej—chfilenamegrfc4305eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126232hkeywordsçcESPeipsecnauthenticationimechanismfheaderhsecuritylarchitecturegpayloadhinternethprotocolmencapsulatingdipv4dipv6kobsoletedByÅd4835epages	opublicationDateC´Gogranking—crfcd4305irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Cryptographic Algorithm Implementation Requirements for Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and Authentication Header (AH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¯The IP Security Authentication Header (AH) and Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) protocols use a sequence number to detect replay.  This document describes extensions to the Internet IP Security Domain of Interpretation (DOI) for the Internet Security Association and Key Management Protocol (ISAKMP).  These extensions support negotiation of the use of traditional 32-bit sequence numbers or extended (64-bit) sequence numbers (ESNs) for a particular AH or ESP security association. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kentddatej”<hfilenamegrfc4304eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126231hkeywordsâjipsecuritykanti-replaybahxip authentication headercespxencapsulating security payloadcikeuinternet key exchangeeikev2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC´G&granking–crfcd4304irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexñExtended Sequence Number (ESN) Addendum to IPsec Domain of Interpretation (DOI) for Internet Security Association and Key Management Protocol (ISAKMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∑This document describes an updated version of the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) protocol, which is designed to provide a mix of security services in IPv4 and IPv6.  ESP is used to provide confidentiality, data origin authentication, connectionless integrity, an anti-replay service (a form of partial sequence integrity), and limited traffic flow confidentiality.  This document obsoletes RFC 2406 (November 1998). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kentddatej”Thfilenamegrfc4303eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126230hkeywordsîcESPeipsechinternethprotocolmencapsulatinghsecuritydipv4dipv6kip securityoconfidentialitynauthenticationiintegritykanti-replaybahxip authentication headercikeuinternet key exchangeeikev2cesnxextended sequence numberkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateC´F÷grankingœcrfcd4303irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'IP Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹This document describes an updated version of the IP Authentication Header (AH), which is designed to provide authentication services in IPv4 and IPv6.  This document obsoletes RFC 2402 (November 1998). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kentddatej”<hfilenamegrfc4302eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126229hkeywordsïgIP-AUTHeipsechInternethProtocolbAHhsecuritydIPv4dIPv6kip securityoconfidentialitynauthenticationiintegritykanti-replaybahcespxencapsulating security payloadcikeuinternet key exchangeeikev2cesnxextended sequence numberkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateC´FÜgrankingŒcrfcd4302irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP Authentication HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This document describes an updated version of the "Security Architecture for IP", which is designed to provide security services for traffic at the IP layer.  This document obsoletes RFC 2401 (November 1998). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamefK. Seoddatej”^hfilenamegrfc4301eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126228hkeywordsïeipsecnauthenticationmencapsulationbIPdIPv4dIPv6hIP-layerxip authentication headerkip securityeIPsecoconfidentialityxauthentication integritykanti-replaybahcespxencapsulating security payloadcikeuinternet key exchangeeikev2cesnxextended sequence numberkobsoletedByÄepageseopublicationDateC´FAgrankingÕcrfcd4301irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Security Architecture for the Internet ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6040d7619∂habstractyóThis document describes the RTP payload format for the BroadVoice(R) narrowband and wideband speech codecs.  The narrowband codec, called BroadVoice16, or BV16, has been selected by CableLabs as a mandatory codec in PacketCable 1.5 and has a CableLabs specification.  The document also provides specifications for the use of BroadVoice with MIME and the Session Description Protocol (SDP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamejJ.-H. Chen¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamefW. Lee¢kaffiliationtBroadcom CorporationdnamejJ. Thyssenddatej”ghfilenamegrfc4298eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126227hkeywordsÜsreal time transportinarrobandhwidebandqbv16 broadvoice16csdpxsession description protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC©®granking crfcd4298irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/RTP Payload Format for BroadVoice Speech CodecsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0Overhead due to the movement of user data in the end-system network I/O processing path at high speeds is significant, and has limited the use of Internet protocols in interconnection networks, and the Internet itself -- especially where high bandwidth, low latency, and/or low overhead are required by the hosted application.

This document examines this overhead, and addresses an architectural, IP-based "copy avoidance" solution for its elimination, by enabling Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA). This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejA. Romanow¢kaffiliationbHPdnamehJ. Mogul¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiT. Talpey¢kaffiliationiSandburstdnameiS. Baileyddatej—<hfilenamegrfc4297eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126226hkeywordsÇhoverheadncopy avoidancekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCî·”granking…crfcd4297irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) over IP Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document defines an abstract architecture for Direct Data Placement (DDP) and Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) protocols to run on Internet Protocol-suite transports.  This architecture does not necessarily reflect the proper way to implement such protocols, but is, rather, a descriptive tool for defining and understanding the protocols.  DDP allows the efficient placement of data into buffers designated by Upper Layer Protocols (e.g., RDMA).  RDMA provides the semantics to enable Remote Direct Memory Access between peers in a way consistent with application requirements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiSandburstdnameiS. Bailey¢kaffiliationfNetAppdnameiT. Talpeyddatej”ahfilenamegrfc4296eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrddpdfullx#rddp - Remote Direct Data PlacementdnamexRemote Direct Data Placementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126225hkeywordsÇdrddpdwarpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCî·zgranking»crfcd4296irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlThe Architecture of Direct Data Placement (DDP) and Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) on Internet ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB), the Mobile-IPv6 MIB, for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, the Mobile-IPv6 MIB will be used to monitor and control the mobile node, home agent, and correspondent node functions of a Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) entity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtCyber Solutions Inc.dnamehG. Keeni¢kaffiliationqTohoku UniversitydnamehK. Koide¢kaffiliationrINTEC NetCore Inc.dnameiK. Nagami¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems Inc.dnamemS. Gundavelliddatej”khfilenamegrfc4295eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126224hkeywordsÇcmibemipv6kobsoletedByÄepagesmopublicationDateD<:˘granking«crfcd4295irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Mobile IPv6 Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOThis document defines requirements for IPv6 nodes.  It is expected that IPv6 will be deployed in a wide range of devices and situations.  Specifying the requirements for IPv6 nodes allows IPv6 to function well and interoperate in a large number of situations and deployments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughneyddatej—Chfilenamegrfc4294eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126223hkeywordsÅxinternet protocol version 6kobsoletedByÅd6434epagesopublicationDateD<:ègranking∆crfcd4294irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIPv6 Node RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5095∂habstractyTThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for implementations of the Internet Protocol (IP) in an IP version independent manner.  This memo obsoletes RFCs 2011, 2465, and 2466. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekS. Routhierddatej—Shfilenamegrfc4293eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126222hkeywordsçfMIB-IPbIPx"Simple Network Management ProtocolcMIBdipv6jICMPv6-MIBhinternethprotocolfip mibhipv4 mibhipv6 mibhicmp mibjicmpv6 mibkobsoletedByÄepageszopublicationDateD<:Ngranking≈crfcd4293irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Management Information Base for the Internet Protocol (IP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects related to the forwarding of Internet Protocol (IP) packets in an IP version-independent manner.  This document obsoletes RFC 2096. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxJohns Hopkins UniversitydnamekB. Habermanddatej—Vhfilenamegrfc4292eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126221hkeywordsÉiTABLE-MIBxManagement Information BaseqInternet ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateD<:grankingƒcrfcd4292irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewIP Forwarding Table MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyäThis specification defines the addressing architecture of the IP Version 6 (IPv6) protocol. The document includes the IPv6 addressing model, text representations of IPv6 addresses, definition of IPv6 unicast addresses, anycast addresses, and multicast addresses, and an IPv6 node's required addresses.

This document obsoletes RFC 3513, "IP Version 6 Addressing Architecture". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Deeringddatej”jhfilenamegrfc4291eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126220hkeywordsÖxinternet protocol version 6gunicastganycastimulticastdnodekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCˆ#granking√crfcd4291irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$IP Version 6 Addressing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÜd7136d7371d5952d6052d7346d8064µhabstracty¨This document explores the issues in the registration of internationalized domain names (IDNs).  The basic IDN definition allows a very large number of possible characters in domain names, and this richness may lead to serious user confusion about similar-looking names.  To avoid this confusion, the IDN registration process must impose rules that disallow some otherwise-valid name combinations.  This document suggests a set of mechanisms that registries might use to define and implement such rules for a broad range of languages, including adaptation of methods developed for Chinese, Japanese, and Korean domain names.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret CullengauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej—[hfilenamegrfc4290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126219hkeywordsÉtchinese domain namesujapanese domain namesskorean domain nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC¨oxgranking¬crfcd4290irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLSuggested Practices for Registration of Internationalized Domain Names (IDN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty
This document specifies IANA registration procedures for MIME external body access types and content-transfer-encodings.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehN. Freed¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej—=hfilenamegrfc4289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126218hkeywordsÜemediaetypeshexternaldbodyfaccessxcontent-transfer-encodingskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCù“.granking¡crfcd4289irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlexOMultipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) Part Four: Registration ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines procedures for the specification and registration of media types for use in MIME and other Internet protocols.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehN. Freed¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej”nhfilenamegrfc4288eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126217hkeywordsÉdmimex%multipurpose internet mail extensionskmedia typeskobsoletedByÅd6838epagesopublicationDateCùÕgranking¿crfcd4288irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Media Type Specifications and Registration ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxiThis document specifies Atom, an XML-based Web content and metadata syndication format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Nottingham¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Sayreddatej”>hfilenamegrfc4287eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgatompubdfullx-atompub - Atom Publishing Format and Protocoldnamex#Atom Publishing Format and Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126216hkeywordsÇtxml-basd web contenthmetadatakobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDateCî‚úgrankingøcrfcd4287irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Atom Syndication FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5988∂habstractyπThe concept of Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) and Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) snooping requires the ability to identify the location of multicast routers. Since snooping is not standardized, there are many mechanisms in use to identify the multicast routers. However, this can lead to interoperability issues between multicast routers and snooping switches from different vendors.

This document introduces a general mechanism that allows for the discovery of multicast routers. This new mechanism, Multicast Router Discovery (MRD), introduces a standardized means of identifying multicast routers without a dependency on particular multicast routing protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationkNetzwert AGdnameiJ. Martinddatej”fhfilenamegrfc4286eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-126215hkeywordsÖdigmpx"internet group management protocolcmldxmulticast listener discoverycmrdkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCùŒßgrankingæcrfcd4286irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMulticast Router DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuIPsec is specified as the means of securing signaling messages between the Mobile Node and Home Agent for Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6).  MIPv6 signaling messages that are secured include the Binding Updates and Acknowledgement messages used for managing the bindings between a Mobile Node and its Home Agent.  This document proposes an alternate method for securing MIPv6 signaling messages between Mobile Nodes and Home Agents.  The alternate method defined here consists of a MIPv6-specific mobility message authentication option that can be added to MIPv6 signaling messages.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Khalil¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiH. Akhtar¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnamelK. Chowdhuryddatej”fhfilenamegrfc4285eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126214hkeywordsÖkip securityeipsecdmip6kmobile nodejhome agentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC√˛grankingΩcrfcd4285irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Authentication Protocol for Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThe Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) is defined in RFC 3748. This document defines a mechanism that allows an access network to provide identity selection hints to an EAP peer -- the end of the link that responds to the authenticator. The purpose is to assist the EAP peer in selecting an appropriate Network Access Identifier (NAI). This is useful in situations where the peer does not receive a lower-layer indication of what network it is connecting to, or when there is no direct roaming relationship between the access network and the peer's home network. In the latter case, authentication is typically accomplished via a mediating network such as a roaming consortium or broker.

The mechanism defined in this document is limited in its scalability. It is intended for access networks that have a small to moderate number of direct roaming partners. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejF. Adrangi¢kaffiliationeInteldnamehV. Lortz¢kaffiliationqCingular WirelessdnamegF. Bari¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronenddatej”{hfilenamegrfc4284eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126213hkeywordsÇcnaixnetwork access identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC’P$grankingºcrfcd4284irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIIdentity Selection Hints for the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyúMobile IPv6 (MIPv6) defines a new Mobility header that is used by mobile nodes, correspondent nodes, and home agents in all messaging related to the creation and management of bindings.  Mobile IPv6 nodes need the capability to identify themselves using an identity other than the default home IP address.  Some examples of identifiers include Network Access Identifier (NAI), Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN), International Mobile Station Identifier (IMSI), and Mobile Subscriber Number (MSISDN).  This document defines a new mobility option that can be used by Mobile IPv6 entities to identify themselves in messages containing a mobility header. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leung¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Khalil¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiH. Akhtar¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnamelK. Chowdhuryddatej”{hfilenamegrfc4283eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126212hkeywordsÑomobility headerlmobile nodesscorrespondent nodeskhome agentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCè≠ögrankingªcrfcd4283irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Mobile Node Identifier Option for Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#In order to provide roaming services, it is necessary to have a standardized method for identifying users.  This document defines the syntax for the Network Access Identifier (NAI), the user identity submitted by the client during network authentication. "Roaming" may be loosely defined as the ability to use any one of multiple Internet Service Providers (ISPs), while maintaining a formal, \%customer-vendor relationship with only one.  Examples of where roaming capabilities might be required include ISP "confederations" and \%ISP-provided corporate network access support.  This document is a revised version of RFC 2486, which originally defined NAIs.  Enhancements include international character set and privacy support, as well as a number of corrections to the original RFC. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationhENDFORCEdnamejM. Beadles¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronenddatej”yhfilenamegrfc4282eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfradextdfullxradext - RADIUS EXTensionsdnameqRADIUS EXTensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126211hkeywordsÑcNAIcnaigroamingitunnelingkobsoletedByÅd7542epagesopublicationDateCùŒMgranking∫crfcd4282irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Network Access IdentifierdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhSeveral MIME type/subtype combinations exist that can contain different media formats. A receiving agent thus needs to examine the details of such media content to determine if the specific elements can be rendered given an available set of codecs. Especially when the end system has limited resources, or the connection to the end system has limited bandwidth, it would be helpful to know from the Content-Type alone if the content can be rendered.

This document adds a new parameter, "codecs", to various type/subtype combinations to allow for unambiguous specification of the codecs indicated by the media formats contained within.

By labeling content with the specific codecs indicated to render the contained media, receiving systems can determine if the codecs are supported by the end system, and if not, can take appropriate action (such as rejecting the content, sending notification of the situation, transcoding the content to a supported type, fetching and installing the required codecs, further inspection to determine if it will be sufficient to support a subset of the indicated codecs, etc.) [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationeApplednameiD. Singer¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiP. Frojdhddatej—Bhfilenamegrfc4281eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126210hkeywordsÜecodecicontainereaudioevideod3gppe3gpp2kobsoletedByÅd6381epagesopublicationDateCè≠Ogrankingπcrfcd4281irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-The Codecs Parameter for "Bucket" Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This document defines new options to discover the Broadcast and Multicast Service (BCMCS) controller in an IP network.  BCMCS is being developed for Third generation (3G) cellular telephone networks.  Users of the service interact with a controller in the network via the Mobile Node (MN) to derive information required to receive Broadcast and Multicast Service.  Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol can be used to configure the MN to access a particular controller.  This document defines the related options and option codes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpStarent NetworksdnamelK. Chowdhury¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Yegani¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiL. Madourddatej”Åhfilenamegrfc4280eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126209hkeywordsÜebcmcsb3gpthird generationrcellular telephonekmobile nodebmnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCè≠granking∏crfcd4280irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Options for Broadcast and Multicast Control ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document specifies three sets of new ciphersuites for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to support authentication based on pre-shared keys (PSKs).  These pre-shared keys are symmetric keys, shared in advance among the communicating parties.  The first set of ciphersuites uses only symmetric key operations for authentication.  The second set uses a Diffie-Hellman exchange authenticated with a pre-shared key, and the third set combines public key authentication of the server with pre-shared key authentication of the client. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemH. Tschofenigddatej”chfilenamegrfc4279eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126208hkeywordsÑcpskdpsksnsymmetric keysndiffie-hellmankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCî·granking∑crfcd4279irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Pre-Shared Key Ciphersuites for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty,The IETF Standards Process requires that all normative references for a document be at the same or higher level of standardization.  RFC 2026 section 9.1 allows the IESG to grant a variance to the standard practices of the IETF.  This document explains why the IESG is considering doing so for the revised version of the BGP-4 specification, which refers normatively to RFC 2385, "Protection of BGP Sessions via the TCP MD5 Signature Option".  RFC 2385 will remain at the Proposed Standard level.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymdietfdfullkietf - IETFdnamedIETFgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrAT&T Labs ResearchdnamekS. Bellovin¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamehA. Zininddatej”Ahfilenamegrfc4278eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering Groupdtypedietfbidjdoc-126207hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC»Sßgranking∂crfcd4278irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiStandards Maturity Variance Regarding the TCP MD5 Signature Option (RFC 2385) and the BGP-4 SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThe purpose of this memo is to document how the requirements for publication of a routing protocol as an Internet Draft Standard have been satisfied by Border Gateway Protocol version 4 (BGP-4).

This report satisfies the requirement for "the second report", as described in Section 6.0 of RFC 1264. In order to fulfill the requirement, this report augments RFC 1773 and describes additional knowledge and understanding gained in the time between when the protocol was made a Draft Standard and when it was submitted for Standard. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patelddatej”Chfilenamegrfc4277eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126206hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC»SUgrankingµcrfcd4277irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Experience with the BGP-4 ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This document reports the results of the BGP-4 implementation survey. The survey had 259 questions about implementations' support of BGP-4 as specified in RFC 4271. After a brief summary of the results, each response is listed. This document contains responses from the four implementers that completed the survey (Alcatel, Cisco, Laurel, and NextHop) and brief information from three that did not (Avici, Data Connection Ltd., and Nokia).

The editors did not use exterior means to verify the accuracy of the information submitted by the respondents. The respondents are experts with the products they reported on. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNextHopdnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiA. Retanaddatej”Ahfilenamegrfc4276eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126205hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesaopublicationDateC»Sgranking¥crfcd4276irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGP-4 Implementation ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≈This document provides a survey of implementations of BGP-4 that support RFC 1657 MIB agents according to the BGP-4 v1 MIB specification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgNextHopdnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationwHickory Hill ConsultingdnamehD. Haresddatej”Chfilenamegrfc4275eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126204hkeywordsÇwborder gateway protocolxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateC»Rºgranking≥crfcd4275irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGP-4 MIB Implementation SurveydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe purpose of this report is to document how the requirements for publication of a routing protocol as an Internet Draft Standard have been satisfied by Border Gateway Protocol version 4 (BGP-4).

This report satisfies the requirement for "the second report", as described in Section 6.0 of RFC 1264. In order to fulfill the requirement, this report augments RFC 1774 and summarizes the key features of BGP-4, as well as analyzes the protocol with respect to scaling and performance. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Patelddatej”Chfilenamegrfc4274eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126203hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC»Rzgranking≤crfcd4274irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewBGP-4 Protocol AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˝This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community In particular, it describes managed objects used for managing the Border Gateway Protocol Version 4 or lower.

The origin of this memo is from RFC 1269 "Definitions of Managed Objects for the Border Gateway Protocol (Version 3)", which was updated to support BGP-4 in RFC 1657. This memo fixes errors introduced when the MIB module was converted to use the SMIv2 language. This memo also updates references to the current SNMP framework documents.

This memo is intended to document deployed implementations of this MIB module in a historical context, to provide clarifications of some items, and to note errors where the MIB module fails to fully represent the BGP protocol. Work is currently in progress to replace this MIB module with a new one representing the current state of the BGP protocol and its extensions.

This document obsoletes RFC 1269 and RFC 1657. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtNextHop TechnologiesdnamegJ. Haas¢kaffiliationtNextHop TechnologiesdnamehS. Haresddatej”Chfilenamegrfc4273eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126202hkeywordsÑiBGP-4-MIBxmanagement information basecmibwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateC»R3granking±crfcd4273irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Definitions of Managed Objects for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçBorder Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4), along with a host of other infrastructure protocols designed before the Internet environment became perilous, was originally designed with little consideration for protection of the information it carries. There are no mechanisms internal to BGP that protect against attacks that modify, delete, forge, or replay data, any of which has the potential to disrupt overall network routing behavior.

This document discusses some of the security issues with BGP routing data dissemination. This document does not discuss security issues with forwarding of packets. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlSparta, Inc.dnameiS. Murphyddatej”Bhfilenamegrfc4272eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126201hkeywordsÑwborder gateway protocolgattackserisksninsider threatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC»QÈgranking∞crfcd4272irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%BGP Security Vulnerabilities AnalysisdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¬This document discusses the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP), which is an inter-Autonomous System routing protocol.

The primary function of a BGP speaking system is to exchange network reachability information with other BGP systems. This network reachability information includes information on the list of Autonomous Systems (ASes) that reachability information traverses. This information is sufficient for constructing a graph of AS connectivity for this reachability from which routing loops may be pruned, and, at the AS level, some policy decisions may be enforced.

BGP-4 provides a set of mechanisms for supporting Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR). These mechanisms include support for advertising a set of destinations as an IP prefix, and eliminating the concept of network "class" within BGP. BGP-4 also introduces mechanisms that allow aggregation of routes, including aggregation of AS paths.

This document obsoletes RFC 1771. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliation`dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Haresddatej”Dhfilenamegrfc4271eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126200hkeywordsÇeBGP-4groutingkobsoletedByÄepageshopublicationDateC»VgrankingØcrfcd4271irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#A Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4)dtypecrfciupdatedByåd6608d9687d9774d8212d6286d4724d7606d6793d7607d7705d9072d8654∂habstracty˝Recent announcements of better-than-expected collision attacks in popular hash algorithms have caused some people to question whether common Internet protocols need to be changed, and if so, how.  This document summarizes the use of hashes in many protocols, discusses how the collision attacks affect and do not affect the protocols, shows how to thwart known attacks on digital certificates, and discusses future directions for protocol designers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationxCounterpane Internet SecuritydnamekB. Schneierddatej”ohfilenamegrfc4270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126199hkeywordsÑqcollision attacksohash algorithmsbiptdigital certificateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCèÆËgrankingÆcrfcd4270irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Attacks on Cryptographic Hashes in Internet ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThis document describes the SEED encryption algorithm, which has been adopted by most of the security systems in the Republic of Korea. Included are a description of the encryption and the key scheduling algorithm (Section 2), the S-boxes (Appendix A), and a set of test vectors (Appendix B).

This document obsoletes RFC 4009. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehH.J. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehS.J. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnameiJ.H. Yoon¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamejD.H. Cheon¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehJ.I. Leeddatej”hhfilenamegrfc4269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126198hkeywordsÉtencryption algorithmhseed cbchseed oidkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCùŸêgranking≠crfcd4269irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe SEED Encryption AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community. In particular, it describes extensions to the Entity MIB to provide information about the state of physical entities.

In addition, this memo defines a set of Textual Conventions to represent various states of an entity. The intent is that these Textual Conventions will be imported and used in MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekS. Chisholm¢kaffiliationhSNMPinfodnamejD. Perkinsddatej”jhfilenamegrfc4268eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfentmibdfullsentmib - Entity MIBdnamejEntity MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126197hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basedsnmpsentity-state-tc-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCè¨Øgranking¨crfcd4268irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlepEntity State MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŒThis document defines the media types for the languages of the W3C Speech Interface Framework, as designed by the Voice Browser Working Group in the following specifications: the Voice Extensible Markup Language (VoiceXML), the Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML), the Speech Recognition Grammar Specification (SRGS), the Call Control XML (CCXML), and the Pronunciation Lexicon Specification (PLS).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcW3CdnamemM. Froumentinddatej—<hfilenamegrfc4267eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126196hkeywordsãmvoice browserx voice extensible markup languagehvoicexmlx speech synthesis markup languagedssmlx(speech recognition grammar specificationdsrgspcall control xmleccxmlx#pronunciation lexicon specificationcplskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateCé)®granking´crfcd4267irfcNumber´es