ed that the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is a tool for distributing reachability information in a manner that creates forwarding paths in a deterministic manner.  In this memo we will describe a class of BGP configurations for which there is more than one potential outcome, and where forwarding states other than the intended state are equally stable.  Also, the stable state where BGP converges may be selected by BGP in a non-deterministic manner.  These stable, but unintended, BGP states are termed here "BGP Wedgies".  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamejT. Griffin¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej”ihfilenamegrfc4264eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126193hkeywordsÅwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateCÉ√•granking®crfcd4264irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlekBGP WedgiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:A text media subtype for tagging content consisting of juxtaposed text and formatting directives as used by the troff series of programs and for conveying information about the intended processing steps necessary to produce formatted output is described.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. LillyddatejÀ•hfilenamegrfc4263eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126192hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCŒË⁄grankingßcrfcd4263irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Media Subtype Registration for Media Type text/troffdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document defines a certificate extension for inclusion of Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Capabilities in X.509 public key certificates, as defined by RFC 3280.  This certificate extension provides an optional method to indicate the cryptographic capabilities of an entity as a complement to the S/MIME Capabilities signed attribute in S/MIME messages according to RFC 3851. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santessonddatej”hhfilenamegrfc4262eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126191hkeywordsÅxcryptographic capabilitieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC£e”granking¶crfcd4262irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbX.509 Certificate Extension for Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) CapabilitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚This document describes how to use Transport Layer Security (TLS) to secure Common Open Policy Service (COPS) connections over the Internet.

This document also updates RFC 2748 by modifying the contents of the Client-Accept message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIntel Corp.dnameiJ. Walker¢kaffiliationkIntel Corp.dnamekA. Kulkarniddatej”fhfilenamegrfc4261eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcrapdfullx"rap - Resource Allocation ProtocoldnamexResource Allocation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126190hkeywordsÅuclient-accept messagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCî‡ºgranking•crfcd4261irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexECommon Open Policy Service (COPS) Over Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractx…This document describes how a Mobile IPv6 Fast Handover could be implemented on link layers conforming to the 802.11 suite of specifications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiP. McCannddatej”chfilenamegrfc4260eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126189hkeywordsÅjlink layerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÉ√Rgranking§crfcd4260irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Mobile IPv6 Fast Handovers for 802.11 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document describes an architecture for the transport of IP Datagrams over ISO MPEG-2 Transport Streams (TS). The MPEG-2 TS has been widely accepted not only for providing digital TV services but also as a subnetwork technology for building IP networks. Examples of systems using MPEG-2 include the Digital Video Broadcast (DVB) and Advanced Television Systems Committee (ATSC) Standards for Digital Television.

The document identifies the need for a set of Internet standards defining the interface between the MPEG-2 Transport Stream and an IP subnetwork. It suggests a new encapsulation method for IP datagrams and proposes protocols to perform IPv6/IPv4 address resolution, to associate IP packets with the properties of the Logical Channels provided by an MPEG-2 TS. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx!Motorola Connected Home SolutionsdnameoM.-J. Montpetit¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationkTIC SystemsdnamejH. Clausen¢kaffiliationvUniversity of SalzburgdnameqB. Collini-Nocker¢kaffiliationvUniversity of SalzburgdnameiH. Linderddatej”bhfilenamegrfc4259eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipdvbdfullsipdvb - IP over DVBdnamekIP over DVBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126188hkeywordsÖrdigital televisioncdvbwdigital video broadcastdatscx%advanced television systems committeekobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateCè¨Egranking£crfcd4259irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAA Framework for Transmission of IP Datagrams over MPEG-2 NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThe Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) suite of protocols has been defined to control different switching technologies as well as different applications. These include support for requesting Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) connections including Synchronous Optical Network (SONET)/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) and Optical Transport Networks (OTNs).

This document concentrates on the routing requirements placed on the GMPLS suite of protocols in order to support the capabilities and functionalities of an Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON) as defined by the ITU-T. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcATTdnamekD. Brungardddatej”Phfilenamegrfc4258eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126187hkeywordsâncontrol domainihierarchykmulti-levelkmulti-layerlinter-domainlintra-domainee-nniei-nnicunikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC{Â“granking¢crfcd4258irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÅRequirements for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Routing for the Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) is a suite of protocol extensions to MPLS to make it generally applicable, to include, for example, control of non packet-based switching, and particularly, optical switching.  One consideration is to use GMPLS protocols to upgrade the control plane of optical transport networks.  This document illustrates this process by describing those extensions to GMPLS protocols that are aimed at controlling Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) or Synchronous Optical Networking (SONET) networks.  SDH/SONET networks make good examples of this process for a variety of reasons.  This document highlights extensions to GMPLS-related routing protocols to disseminate information needed in transport path computation and network operations, together with (G)MPLS protocol extensions required for the provisioning of transport circuits.  New capabilities that an GMPLS control plane would bring to SDH/SONET networks, such as new restoration methods and multi-layer circuit establishment, are also discussed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqGrotto NetworkingdnamelG. Bernstein¢kaffiliationhPercevaldnameiE. Mannie¢kaffiliationnMetanoia, Inc.dnameiV. Sharma¢kaffiliationxMarconi Corporation, plcdnamegE. Grayddatej”`hfilenamegrfc4257eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126186hkeywordsÑdmplsqoptical switchingcsdhesonetkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDateCùÿ≤granking°crfcd4257irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex£Framework for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)-based Control of Synchronous Digital Hierarchy/Synchronous Optical Networking (SDH/SONET) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8The Secure Shell Protocol (SSH) is a protocol for secure remote login and other secure network services over an insecure network.  This document describes a general purpose authentication method for the SSH protocol, suitable for interactive authentications where the authentication data should be entered via a keyboard (or equivalent alphanumeric input device).  The major goal of this method is to allow the SSH client to support a whole class of authentication mechanism(s) without knowing the specifics of the actual authentication mechanism(s). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlsavecore.netdnameiF. Cusack¢kaffiliationxAppGate Network Security ABdnamejM. Forssenddatej”Rhfilenamegrfc4256eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126185hkeywordsÇlremote loginralphanumeric inputkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCæƒúgranking†crfcd4256irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKGeneric Message Exchange Authentication for the Secure Shell Protocol (SSH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÊThis document describes a method of verifying Secure Shell (SSH) host keys using Domain Name System Security (DNSSEC).  The document defines a new DNS resource record that contains a standard SSH key fingerprint. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgOpenSSHdnamekJ. Schlyter¢kaffiliationfSPARTAdnamejW. Griffinddatej—Xhfilenamegrfc4255eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126184hkeywordsÉrdomain name systemfdnssecxdomain name system securitykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateCæƒ%grankingücrfcd4255irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAUsing DNS to Securely Publish Secure Shell (SSH) Key FingerprintsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀Secure Shell (SSH) is a protocol for secure remote login and other secure network services over an insecure network.

This document describes the SSH Connection Protocol. It provides interactive login sessions, remote execution of commands, forwarded TCP/IP connections, and forwarded X11 connections. All of these channels are multiplexed into a single encrypted tunnel.

The SSH Connection Protocol has been designed to run on top of the SSH transport layer and user authentication protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx SSH Communications Security CorpdnameiT. Ylonen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejC. Lonvickddatej”Shfilenamegrfc4254eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126183hkeywordsÑlremote loginqinteractive loginpremote executionpencrypted tunnelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCæ√⁄grankingûcrfcd4254irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*The Secure Shell (SSH) Connection ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8308∂habstractyÂThe Secure Shell (SSH) is a protocol for secure remote login and other secure network services over an insecure network.

This document describes the SSH transport layer protocol, which typically runs on top of TCP/IP. The protocol can be used as a basis for a number of secure network services. It provides strong encryption, server authentication, and integrity protection. It may also provide compression.

Key exchange method, public key algorithm, symmetric encryption algorithm, message authentication algorithm, and hash algorithm are all negotiated.

This document also describes the Diffie-Hellman key exchange method and the minimal set of algorithms that are needed to implement the SSH transport layer protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx SSH Communications Security CorpdnameiT. Ylonen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejC. Lonvickddatej”Shfilenamegrfc4253eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126182hkeywordsÜlremote loginjencryptionuserver authenticationtintegrity protectionxdiffie-hellman key exchangendiffie hellmankobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateCæ√Ñgrankingùcrfcd4253irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/The Secure Shell (SSH) Transport Layer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByád8758d8332d8268d6668d8308d8709d9142∂habstractyThe Secure Shell Protocol (SSH) is a protocol for secure remote login and other secure network services over an insecure network.  This document describes the SSH authentication protocol framework and public key, password, and host-based client authentication methods.  Additional authentication methods are described in separate documents.  The SSH authentication protocol runs on top of the SSH transport layer protocol and provides a single authenticated tunnel for the SSH connection protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx SSH Communications Security CorpdnameiT. Ylonen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejC. Lonvickddatej”Shfilenamegrfc4252eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126181hkeywordsÑlremote loginjpublic keyhpasswordx host-based client authenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCæ√:grankingúcrfcd4252irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.The Secure Shell (SSH) Authentication ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8332d8308∂habstractyˇThe Secure Shell (SSH) Protocol is a protocol for secure remote login and other secure network services over an insecure network.  This document describes the architecture of the SSH protocol, as well as the notation and terminology used in SSH protocol documents.  It also discusses the SSH algorithm naming system that allows local extensions.  The SSH protocol consists of three major components: The Transport Layer Protocol provides server authentication, confidentiality, and integrity with perfect forward secrecy.  The User Authentication Protocol authenticates the client to the server.  The Connection Protocol multiplexes the encrypted tunnel into several logical channels.  Details of these protocols are described in separate documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx SSH Communications Security CorpdnameiT. Ylonen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejC. Lonvickddatej”Shfilenamegrfc4251eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126180hkeywordsÇlremote loginmssh algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCæ¬∫grankingõcrfcd4251irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,The Secure Shell (SSH) Protocol ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9141d8308∂habstractyThis document defines the instructions to the IANA and the initial state of the IANA assigned numbers for the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.  It is intended only for the initialization of the IANA registries referenced in the set of SSH documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx SSH Communications Security CorpdnamekS. Lehtinen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejC. Lonvickddatej”Shfilenamegrfc4250eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesecshdfulltsecsh - Secure ShelldnamelSecure Shelldtypebwgbidjdoc-126179hkeywordsÅlremote loginkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCæ¬`grankingöcrfcd4250irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0The Secure Shell (SSH) Protocol Assigned NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9142d9519d8268∂habstracty‰Implementation of generators and parsers of header fields requires certain information about those fields.  Interoperability is most likely when all such information is explicitly provided by the technical specification of the fields.  Lacking such explicit information, implementers may guess, and interoperability may suffer.  This memo identifies information useful to implementers of header field generators and parsers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Lillyddatej”ghfilenamegrfc4249eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126178hkeywordsÇvheader field generatorsheader field parserkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCŒËûgrankingôcrfcd4249irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Implementer-Friendly Specification of Message and MIME-Part Header Fields and Field ComponentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies the telnet Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme that was originally specified in RFC 1738.  The purpose of this document is to allow RFC 1738 to be made obsolete while keeping the information about the scheme on standards track. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej”ehfilenamegrfc4248eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126177hkeywordsÉxuniform resource identifiercurlxuniform resource locatorskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCfºÇgrankingòcrfcd4248irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuThe telnet URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüVoice over IP (VoIP) typically uses the encapsulation voice/RTP/UDP/IP.  When MPLS labels are added, this becomes voice/RTP/UDP/IP/MPLS-labels.  For an MPLS VPN, the packet header is typically 48 bytes, while the voice payload is often no more than 30 bytes, for example.  Header compression can significantly reduce the overhead through various compression mechanisms, such as enhanced compressed RTP (ECRTP) and robust header compression (ROHC).  We consider using MPLS to route compressed packets over an MPLS Label Switched Path (LSP) without compression/decompression cycles at each router.  This approach can increase the bandwidth efficiency as well as processing scalability of the maximum number of simultaneous flows that use header compression at each router.  In this document, we give a problem statement, goals and requirements, and an example scenario.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ash¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehB. Goode¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegJ. Hand¢kaffiliationjBT InfonetdnamehR. Zhangddatej”dhfilenamegrfc4247eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126176hkeywordsÉxmultiprotocol label switchingdvoipmvoice over ipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateClgrankingócrfcd4247irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Requirements for Header Compression over MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThe International Standard Audiovisual Number (ISAN) is a standard numbering system for the unique and international identification of audiovisual works.  This document is the definition of the formal Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace Identifier (NID) for ISAN.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcTBTdnamehM. Dolanddatej”ahfilenamegrfc4246eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126175hkeywordsÑpnumbering systemxinternational identificationkaudiovisualxuniform resource identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„Ûgrankingñcrfcd4246irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?International Standard Audiovisual Number (ISAN) URN DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty°This document examines a wide range of conferencing requirements for tightly coupled SIP conferences.  Separate documents will map the requirements to existing protocol primitives, define new protocol extensions, and introduce new protocols as needed.  Together, these documents will provide a guide for building interoperable SIP conferencing applications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levin¢kaffiliationgPolycomdnamegR. Evenddatej”chfilenamegrfc4245eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126174hkeywordsÅxsession initiation protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCé)igrankingïcrfcd4245irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<High-Level Requirements for Tightly Coupled SIP ConferencingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document defines a standard mechanism for capturing the history information associated with a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) request.  This capability enables many enhanced services by providing the information as to how and why a call arrives at a specific application or user.  This document defines a new optional SIP header, History-Info, for capturing the history information in requests. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiM. Barnesddatej”dhfilenamegrfc4244eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126173hkeywordsÖlhistory-infohretargetqenhanced servicesivoicemailxautomatic call distributionkobsoletedByÅd7044epages,opublicationDateCÉƒ‹grankingîcrfcd4244irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUAn Extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for Request History InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This memo defines a new Vendor-Specific Information suboption for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol's (DHCP) relay agent information option.  The suboption allows a DHCP relay agent to include vendor-specific information in the DHCP messages it forwards, as configured by its administrator. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Johnson¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenT. PalaniappanddatejÀ§hfilenamegrfc4243eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126172hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC£cµgrankingìcrfcd4243irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkVendor-Specific Information Suboption for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Relay Agent OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìThis document describes a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) option for specifying an upper bound for how long a client should wait before refreshing information retrieved from DHCPv6.  It is used with stateless DHCPv6 as there are no addresses or other entities with lifetimes that can tell the client when to contact the DHCPv6 server to refresh its configuration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgUNINETTdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volzddatej”dhfilenamegrfc4242eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126171hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÅd8415epagesopublicationDateCÉƒägrankingícrfcd4242irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYInformation Refresh Time Option for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyAThis memo is a digest of the user network interface specification of NTT Communications' dual stack ADSL access service, which provide a IPv6/IPv4 dual stack services to home users.  In order to simplify user setup, these services have a mechanism to configure IPv6 specific parameters automatically.  The memo focuses on two basic parameters: the prefix assigned to the user and the addresses of IPv6 DNS servers, and it specifies a way to deliver these parameters to Customer Premises Equipment (CPE) automatically.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret CullengauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamelY. Shirasaki¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamekS. Miyakawa¢kaffiliationrNTT CommunicationsdnamekT. Yamasaki¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamemA. Takenouchiddatej—[hfilenamegrfc4241eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126170hkeywordsÖxuser network specificationrntt communicationsdadslccpexcustomer preises equipmentkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateCüs·grankingëcrfcd4241irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex7A Model of IPv6/IPv4 Dual Stack Internet Access ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIn SIP-based networks, there is a need to provide basic network media services. Such services include network announcements, user interaction, and conferencing services. These services are basic building blocks, from which one can construct interesting applications. In order to have interoperability between servers offering these building blocks (also known as Media Servers) and application developers, one needs to be able to locate and invoke such services in a well defined manner.

This document describes a mechanism for providing an interoperable interface between Application Servers, which provide application services to SIP-based networks, and Media Servers, which provide the basic media processing building blocks. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxBrooktrout Technology, Inc.dnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationxBrooktrout Technology, Inc.dnamekJ. Van Dyke¢kaffiliationxBrooktrout Technology, Inc.dnamejA. Spitzerddatej”_hfilenamegrfc4240eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126169hkeywordsÑxsession initiation protocolvnetwork media servicesmmedia serverssapplication serverskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCùœﬂgrankingêcrfcd4240irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Basic Network Media Services with SIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylThis document describes the carriage of voicemail messages over Internet mail as part of a unified messaging infrastructure.

The Internet Voice Messaging (IVM) concept described in this document is not a successor format to VPIM v2 (Voice Profile for Internet Mail Version 2), but rather an alternative specification for a different application. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehS. McRae¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsonsddatej—Shfilenamegrfc4239eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-126168hkeywordsÉivoicemaildvpimxvoice profile for internet mailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCl§grankingècrfcd4239irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexInternet Voice Messaging (IVM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªVoice messaging is traditionally addressed using telephone number addressing.  This document describes two techniques for routing voice messages based on a telephone number.  The complete service uses the Voice Profile for Internet Mail (VPIM) Directory service to lookup a VPIM email address with a telephone number and confirm that the address is both valid and associated with the intended recipient.  However, this service will take time to become widely deployed in the near term.  This document also describes a basic send-and-pray service that routes and delivers messages using only the ENUM telephone number resolution service and the existing DNS mail routing facilities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuilddatej”[hfilenamegrfc4238eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-126167hkeywordsÑdvpimxtelephone number addressingxvoice profile and internet mailnvpim directorykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateCfΩCgrankingécrfcd4238irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVoice Message Routing ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractyÊThis document provides details of the Voice Profile for Internet Mail (VPIM) directory service. The service provides the email address of the recipient that is given a telephone number. It optionally provides the spoken name of the recipient and the media capabilities of the recipient.

The VPIM directory Schema provides essential additional attributes to recreate the voice mail user experience using standardized directories. This user experience provides, at the time of addressing, basic assurances that the message will be delivered as intended. This document combines two earlier documents, one from Anne Brown and one from Greg Vaudreuil, that define a voice messaging schema into a single working group submission. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuilddatej”[hfilenamegrfc4237eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-126166hkeywordsÇdvpimxvoice profile for internet mailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC^√õgrankingçcrfcd4237irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Voice Messaging Directory ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöOpen Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) framework documents several application-agnostic mechanisms such as OPES tracing, OPES bypass, and OPES callout protocol.  This document extends those generic mechanisms for Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) adaptation.  Together, application-agnostic OPES documents and this HTTP profile constitute a complete specification for HTTP adaptation with OPES. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwThe Measurement FactorydnamekA. Rousskov¢kaffiliationvCyberGuard CorporationdnamejM. Stecherddatej”`hfilenamegrfc4236eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-126165hkeywordsÑpcallout protocolcocplopes tracingkopes bypasskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCzê∏grankingåcrfcd4236irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8HTTP Adaptation with Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document defines a dialog event package for the SIP Events architecture, along with a data format used in notifications for this package.  The dialog package allows users to subscribe to another user and to receive notification of the changes in state of INVITE-initiated dialog usages in which the subscribed-to user is involved. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationlSIP Edge LLCdnamegR. Mahyddatej”_hfilenamegrfc4235eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126164hkeywordsÇjsip eventsndialog packagekobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateCé)	grankingãcrfcd4235irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRAn INVITE-Initiated Dialog Event Package for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7463d8996∂habstractyâInternet technical specifications often need to define a formal syntax.  Over the years, a modified version of Backus-Naur Form (BNF), called Augmented BNF (ABNF), has been popular among many Internet specifications.  The current specification documents ABNF.  It balances compactness and simplicity, with reasonable representational power.  The differences between standard BNF and ABNF involve naming rules, repetition, alternatives, order-independence, and value ranges.  This specification also supplies additional rule definitions and encoding for a core lexical analyzer of the type common to several Internet specifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqLisa M. Dusseaultdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationiTHUS plc.dnamejP. Overellddatej”Thfilenamegrfc4234eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126163hkeywordsádABNFpbackus-naur formxaugmented backus-naur formprule definitionshencodingucore lexical analyzeroelectronic mailkobsoletedByÅd5234epagesopublicationDateCBË÷grankingäcrfcd4234irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Augmented BNF for Syntax Specifications: ABNFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôThis document defines a protocol for backhauling of Integrated Services Digital Network (ISDN) Q.921 User messages over IP using the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP). This protocol would be used between a Signaling Gateway (SG) and Media Gateway Controller (MGC). It is assumed that the SG receives ISDN signaling over a standard ISDN interface.

