mdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwThe Measurement FactorydnamekA. Rousskovddatej”hhfilenamegrfc4228eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126158hkeywordsÇjautomationdtoolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC©ß8grankingÑcrfcd4228irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Requirements for an IETF Draft Submission ToolsetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis memo specifies a Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP) binding to the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP) core. A SOAP binding describes how SOAP messages are transmitted in the network.

The SOAP is an XML-based (eXtensible Markup Language) messaging protocol used to implement a wide variety of distributed messaging models. It defines a message format and describes a variety of message patterns, including, but not limited to, Remote Procedure Calling (RPC), asynchronous event notification, unacknowledged messages, and forwarding via SOAP intermediaries. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlClipcode.comdnamemE. O'Tuathail¢kaffiliationxDover Beach Consulting, Inc.dnamegM. Roseddatej”ehfilenamegrfc4227eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126157hkeywordsácxmlxextensible markup languagexremote procedure callingcrpcxasynchronous event notificationwunacknowledged messagesgbindingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC√?grankingÉcrfcd4227irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Using the Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP) in Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstractywThis document describes an algorithm to generate one-time password values, based on Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC). A security analysis of the algorithm is presented, and important parameters related to the secure deployment of the algorithm are discussed. The proposed algorithm can be used across a wide range of network applications ranging from remote Virtual Private Network (VPN) access, Wi-Fi network logon to transaction-oriented Web applications.

This work is a joint effort by the OATH (Open AuTHentication) membership to specify an algorithm that can be freely distributed to the technical community. The authors believe that a common and shared algorithm will facilitate adoption of two-factor authentication on the Internet by enabling interoperability across commercial and open-source implementations. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamejD. M'Raihi¢kaffiliationdUCSDdnamejM. Bellare¢kaffiliationeVascodnamelF. Hoornaert¢kaffiliationgGemplusdnamekD. Naccache¢kaffiliationgAladdindnamehO. Ranenddatej”hhfilenamegrfc4226eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126156hkeywordsÜx"hashed message authentication codeqsecurity analysisdoathsopen authenticationnauthenticationdOATHkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateC¨m˚grankingÇcrfcd4226irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/HOTP: An HMAC-Based One-Time Password AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis document is an account of the rationale behind the Mobile IPv6 (MIPv6) Route Optimization security design. The purpose of this document is to present the thinking and to preserve the reasoning behind the Mobile IPv6 security design in 2001 - 2002.

The document has two target audiences: (1) helping MIPv6 implementors to better understand the design choices in MIPv6 security procedures, and (2) allowing people dealing with mobility or multi-homing to avoid a number of potential security pitfalls in their designs. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research NomadicLabdnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnamegT. Aura¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamekE. Nordmarkddatej”uhfilenamegrfc4225eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126155hkeywordsÇemipv6cmipkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateC¢ÜgrankingÅcrfcd4225irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAMobile IP Version 6 Route Optimization Security Design BackgrounddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLRObust Header Compression (ROHC), RFC 3095, defines a framework for header compression, along with a number of compression protocols (profiles).  One operating assumption for the profiles defined in RFC 3095 is that the channel between compressor and decompressor is required to maintain packet ordering.  This document discusses aspects of using ROHC over channels that can reorder packets.  It provides guidelines on how to implement existing profiles over such channels, as well as suggestions for the design of new profiles.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletier¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekK. SandlundddatejÀ¨hfilenamegrfc4224eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126154hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC»QîgrankingÄcrfcd4224irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMRObust Header Compression (ROHC): ROHC over Channels That Can Reorder PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx–This memo reclassifies RFC 1863, A BGP/IDRP Route Server alternative to a full mesh routing, to Historic status.  This memo also obsoletes RFC 1863.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savoladdatej”`hfilenamegrfc4223eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126153hkeywordsÜhBGP-IDRPfborderggatewayhprotocollinter-domaingroutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBËtgrankingcrfcd4223irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Reclassification of RFC 1863 to HistoricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¯This document recommends methods that are intended to improve the scalability and stability of large networks using Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) Version 2 protocol.  The methods include processing OSPF Hellos and Link State Advertisement (LSA) Acknowledgments at a higher priority compared to other OSPF packets, and other congestion avoidance procedures.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamelG. Choudhuryddatej”Nhfilenamegrfc4222eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126152hkeywordsÉxopen shortest path firstclsaxlink state advertisementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCLY\granking~crfcd4222irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberpetotaletitlexQPrioritized Treatment of Specific OSPF Version 2 Packets and Congestion AvoidancedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9454∂habstractyA range of Management Information Base (MIB) modules has been developed to help model and manage the various aspects of Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) networks. These MIB modules are defined in separate documents that focus on the specific areas of responsibility of the modules that they describe.

This document describes the management architecture for MPLS and indicates the interrelationships between the different MIB modules used for MPLS network management. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationnBloomberg L.P.dnamemC. Srinivasan¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej—Ghfilenamegrfc4221eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126151hkeywordsÑcmibxmanagement information basewmanagement architecturernetwork managementkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateC~a»granking}crfcd4221irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Management OverviewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for modeling TE links as described in the Link Bundling in MPLS Traffic Engineering (TE) document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehM. Dubuc¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationkSonos, Inc.dnamegJ. Langddatej—Thfilenamegrfc4220eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126150hkeywordsÑcmibxnetwork management protocolsbteote-link-std-mibkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateC~bgranking|crfcd4220irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Traffic Engineering Link Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This document specifies a set of questions that authors should be prepared to answer as part of a solution to multihoming with IPv6.  The questions do not assume that multihoming is the only problem of interest, nor do they demand a more general solution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. Learddatej”?hfilenamegrfc4219eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmulti6dfullx!multi6 - Site Multihoming in IPv6dnamexSite Multihoming in IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126149hkeywordsÇpsecurity threatsxinternet protocol version 6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCfº<granking{crfcd4219irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAThings Multihoming in IPv6 (MULTI6) Developers Should Think AboutdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(This document lists security threats related to IPv6 multihoming. Multihoming can introduce new opportunities to redirect packets to different, unintended IP addresses.

The intent is to look at how IPv6 multihoming solutions might make the Internet less secure; we examine threats that are inherent to all IPv6 multihoming solutions rather than study any specific proposed solution. The threats in this document build upon the threats discovered and discussed as part of the Mobile IPv6 work. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliation`dnameeT. Liddatej”ahfilenamegrfc4218eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmulti6dfullx!multi6 - Site Multihoming in IPv6dnamexSite Multihoming in IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126148hkeywordsÇpsecurity threatsxinternet protocol version 6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCfª‹grankingzcrfcd4218irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Threats Relating to IPv6 Multihoming SolutionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a mechanism that can be used by FTP clients and servers to implement security and authentication using the TLS protocol defined by RFC 2246, "The TLS Protocol Version 1.0.", and the extensions to the FTP protocol defined by RFC 2228, "FTP Security Extensions". It describes the subset of the extensions that are required and the parameters to be used, discusses some of the policy issues that clients and servers will need to take, considers some of the implications of those policies, and discusses some expected behaviours of implementations to allow interoperation. This document is intended to provide TLS support for FTP in a similar way to that provided for SMTP in RFC 2487, "SMTP Service Extension for Secure SMTP over Transport Layer Security", and HTTP in RFC 2817, "Upgrading to TLS Within HTTP/1.1.".

This specification is in accordance with RFC 959, "File Transfer Protocol". It relies on RFC 2246, "The TLS Protocol Version 1.0.", and RFC 2228, "FTP Security Extensions". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIBM UK LtddnamerP. Ford-Hutchinsonddatej”ihfilenamegrfc4217eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126147hkeywordsÑhsecuritynauthenticationvfile transfer protocolxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCfΩïgrankingycrfcd4217irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuSecuring FTP with TLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty£This document discusses requirements for the support of inter-AS MPLS Traffic Engineering (MPLS TE).  Its main objective is to present a set of requirements and scenarios which would result in general guidelines for the definition, selection, and specification development for any technical solution(s) meeting these requirements and supporting the scenarios.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInfonet Services CorporationdnamehR. Zhang¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ.-P. Vasseurddatej”>hfilenamegrfc4216eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126146hkeywordsÇhinter-asgmpls-tekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC{Âzgrankingxcrfcd4216irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGMPLS Inter-Autonomous System (AS) Traffic Engineering (TE) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document analyzes the transition to IPv6 in Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) packet networks. These networks are based on General Packet Radio Service (GPRS) technology, and the radio network architecture is based on Global System for Mobile Communications (GSM) or Universal Mobile Telecommunications System (UMTS)/Wideband Code Division Multiple Access (WCDMA) technology.

The focus is on analyzing different transition scenarios and applicable transition mechanisms and finding solutions for those transition scenarios. In these scenarios, the User Equipment (UE) connects to other nodes, e.g., in the Internet, and IPv6/IPv4 transition mechanisms are needed. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Wiljakkaddatej—8hfilenamegrfc4215eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126145hkeywordsâqinternet protocoldgprsxgeneral packet radio servicex'global system for mobile communicationscgsmx*universal mobile telecommunications systemdumtsx&wideband code division multiple accessewcdmakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCPT∂grankingwcrfcd4215irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSAnalysis on IPv6 Transition in Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty°The Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP) connects IPv6 hosts/routers over IPv4 networks.  ISATAP views the IPv4 network as a link layer for IPv6 and views other nodes on the network as potential IPv6 hosts/routers.  ISATAP supports an automatic tunneling abstraction similar to the Non-Broadcast Multiple Access (NBMA) model.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid KessensgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejF. Templin¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems K.K.dnamejT. Gleeson¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiM. Talwar¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiD. Thalerddatej—;hfilenamegrfc4214eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgngtransdfullx$ngtrans - Next Generation TransitiondnamexNext Generation Transitiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126144hkeywordsÖfISATAPdipv4jlink layerdnbmaxnon-broadcast multiple accesskobsoletedByÅd5214epagesopublicationDateCLX˚grankingvcrfcd4214irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8Intra-Site Automatic Tunnel Addressing Protocol (ISATAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document specifies IPv4 compatibility mechanisms that can be implemented by IPv6 hosts and routers. Two mechanisms are specified, dual stack and configured tunneling. Dual stack implies providing complete implementations of both versions of the Internet Protocol (IPv4 and IPv6), and configured tunneling provides a means to carry IPv6 packets over unmodified IPv4 routing infrastructures.

This document obsoletes RFC 2893. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationnIntransa, Inc.dnamekR. Gilliganddatej”Qhfilenamegrfc4213eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126143hkeywordsÑjTRANS-IPV6dipv4idual sacktconfigured tunnelingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCLX∫grankingucrfcd4213irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Basic Transition Mechanisms for IPv6 Hosts and RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty˝The Public-Key Infrastructure using X.509 (PKIX) Working Group of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) has defined a number of certificate management protocols.  These protocols are primarily focused on X.509v3 public-key certificates.  However, it is sometimes desirable to manage certificates in alternative formats as well.  This document specifies how such certificates may be requested using the Certificate Request Message Format (CRMF) syntax that is used by several different protocols.  It also explains how alternative certificate formats may be incorporated into such popular protocols as PKIX Certificate Management Protocol (PKIX-CMP) and Certificate Management Messages over CMS (CMC).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtGuardeonic SolutionsdnameiM. Blinov¢kaffiliationtUniversity of OttawadnamehC. Adamsddatej—>hfilenamegrfc4212eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126142hkeywordsàgX.509v3wpublic-key certificatesdcrmfx"certificate request message formatx$pkix certificate management protocolhpkix-cmpx(certificate management messages over cmsccmckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBËgrankingtcrfcd4212irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexuAlternative Certificate Formats for the Public-Key Infrastructure Using X.509 (PKIX) Certificate Management ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This document describes the Certificate Request Message Format (CRMF) syntax and semantics.  This syntax is used to convey a request for a certificate to a Certification Authority (CA), possibly via a Registration Authority (RA), for the purposes of X.509 certificate production.  The request will typically include a public key and the associated registration information.  This document does not define a certificate request protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej”Jhfilenamegrfc4211eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126141hkeywordsåjX.509-CRMFwcertification authoritybcavregistration authoritybradpkixcpkivcertificate productiondcrmfhsecurityjencryptionmauthenticaionkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDateC<g£grankingscrfcd4211irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate Request Message Format (CRMF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9045∂habstracty`This document describes the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) Certificate Management Protocol (CMP).  Protocol messages are defined for X.509v3 certificate creation and management.  CMP provides on-line interactions between PKI components, including an exchange between a Certification Authority (CA) and a client system. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of OttawadnamehC. Adams¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationcSSHdnamehT. Kause¢kaffiliationgSafeNetdnamejT. Mononenddatej—Vhfilenamegrfc4210eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126140hkeywordsâoPKICMP securityxcryptographic authenticationdpkixcpkigX.509v3tcertificate creationvcertificate managementbcawcertification authoritykobsoletedByÅd9810epages_opublicationDateC<gFgrankingrcrfcd4210irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate Management Protocol (CMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd6712d9480d9481∂habstractyƒThe Link Management Protocol (LMP) is defined to manage traffic engineering (TE) links.  In its present form, LMP focuses on peer nodes, i.e., nodes that peer in signaling and/or routing.  This document proposes extensions to LMP to allow it to be used between a peer node and an adjacent optical line system (OLS).  These extensions are intended to satisfy the "Optical Link Interface Requirements" described in a companion document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqHatteras NetworksdnamekA. Fredette¢kaffiliationjSonos Inc.dnamegJ. Langddatej—Whfilenamegrfc4209eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126139hkeywordsÖbtestraffic engineeringjpeer nodescolsx#optical link interface requirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCfªQgrankingqcrfcd4209irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeLink Management Protocol (LMP) for Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) Optical Line SystemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6898∂habstractyQGeneralized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) defines both routing and signaling protocols for the creation of Label Switched Paths (LSPs) in various switching technologies.  These protocols can be used to support a number of deployment scenarios.  This memo addresses the application of GMPLS to the overlay model. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationfBoeingdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationmG1M Co., Ltd.dnamelH. Ishimatsu¢kaffiliationuJuniper Networks, IncdnamejY. Rekhterddatej”Ohfilenamegrfc4208eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126138hkeywordsÑclsptlabel switched pathsprouting protocolrsignaling protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCaopgrankingpcrfcd4208irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex©Generalized Multiprotocol Label Switching (GMPLS) User-Network Interface (UNI): Resource ReserVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Support for the Overlay ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx’This document details the Synchronous Optical Network (SONET)/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) technology-specific information needed when sending Link Management Protocol (LMP) test messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkSonos, Inc.dnamegJ. Lang¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriouddatej—Whfilenamegrfc4207eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126137hkeywordsàegmplsidiscoveryqlink verificationpfault managementxcontrol channel managementxlink property correlationxtraffic engineering linksptrace monitoringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCanıgrankingocrfcd4207irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÅSynchronous Optical Network (SONET)/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) Encoding for Link Management Protocol (LMP) Test MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6898∂habstractyÙTo improve scalability of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) it may be useful to aggregate Label Switched Paths (LSPs) by creating a hierarchy of such LSPs. A way to create such a hierarchy is by (a) a Label Switching Router (LSR) creating a Traffic Engineering Label Switched Path (TE LSP), (b) the LSR forming a forwarding adjacency (FA) out of that LSP (by advertising this LSP as a Traffic Engineering (TE) link into the same instance of ISIS/OSPF as the one that was used to create the LSP), (c) allowing other LSRs to use FAs for their path computation, and (d) nesting of LSPs originated by other LSRs into that LSP (by using the label stack construct).

This document describes the mechanisms to accomplish this. [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej—Yhfilenamegrfc4206eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126136hkeywordsÖclsrvlabel switching routerfte lspbfatforwarding adjacencykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCamŸgrankingncrfcd4206irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexuLabel Switched Paths (LSP) Hierarchy with Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6107d6001∂habstractx This document specifies encoding of extensions to the IS-IS routing protocol in support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej»íhfilenamegrfc4205eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126135hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5307epagesopublicationDateCamvgrankingmcrfcd4205irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Extensions in Support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£For scalability purposes, multiple data links can be combined to form a single traffic engineering (TE) link.  Furthermore, the management of TE links is not restricted to in-band messaging, but instead can be done using out-of-band techniques.  This document specifies a link management protocol (LMP) that runs between a pair of nodes and is used to manage TE links.  Specifically, LMP will be used to maintain control channel connectivity, verify the physical connectivity of the data links, correlate the link property information, suppress downstream alarms, and localize link failures for protection/restoration purposes in multiple kinds of networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkSonos, Inc.dnamegJ. Langddatej—Xhfilenamegrfc4204eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126134hkeywordsäegmplsesonetcsdhidiscoveryqlink verificationofault managmentxcontrol channel managementxlink property correlationxtraffic engineering linksptrace monitoringkobsoletedByÄepagesVopublicationDateCamgrankinglcrfcd4204irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLink Management Protocol (LMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6898∂habstractxüThis document specifies encoding of extensions to the OSPF routing protocol in support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej—Whfilenamegrfc4203eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126133hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCal»grankingkcrfcd4203irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPOSPF Extensions in Support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7074d6001d6002∂habstractyThis document specifies routing extensions in support of carrying link state information for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS).  This document enhances the routing extensions required to support MPLS Traffic Engineering (TE). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej—Whfilenamegrfc4202eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126132hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCalzgrankingjcrfcd4202irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSRouting Extensions in Support of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd7074d6001d6002∂habstractyÂFor the purpose of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) signaling, in certain cases a combination of <link identifier, label> is not sufficient to unambiguously identify the appropriate resource used by a Label Switched Path (LSP).  Such cases are handled by using the link bundling construct, which is described in this document.  This document updates the interface identification TLVs, which are defined in the GMPLS Signaling Functional Description. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationnMovaz NetworksdnameiL. Bergerddatej—ehfilenamegrfc4201eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126131hkeywordsÜxmultiprotocol label switchingx)generalized multiprotocol label switchingegmplsclspslabel switched pathxinterface identification tlvskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCal grankingicrfcd4201irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Link Bundling in MPLS Traffic Engineering (TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document describes a URN (Uniform Resource Name) namespace for identifying content resources within federated content collections.  A federated content collection often does not have a strong centralized authority but relies upon shared naming, metadata, and access conventions to provide interoperability among its members.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhZelestradnamejD. Tessmanddatej”ahfilenamegrfc4198eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126130hkeywordsÇpcontent resourcescontent collectionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCg Ägrankingfcrfcd4198irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for Federated ContentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document defines the specific requirements for edge-to-edge emulation of circuits carrying Time Division Multiplexed (TDM) digital signals of the Plesiochronous Digital Hierarchy as well as the Synchronous Optical NETwork/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy over packet-switched networks.  It is aligned to the common architecture for Pseudo Wire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3).  It makes references to the generic requirements for PWE3 where applicable and complements them by defining requirements originating from specifics of TDM circuits.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjSiemens AGdnameiM. Riegelddatej”fhfilenamegrfc4197eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-126129hkeywordsàrdigital signaturesx plesiochronous digital hierarchyesonetxsynchronous optical networkcsdhxsynchronous digital hierarchydpwe3upseudo wire emulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCfº„grankingecrfcd4197irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrRequirements for Edge-to-Edge Emulation of Time Division Multiplexed (TDM) Circuits over Packet Switching NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÒThis document describes the use of the SEED block cipher algorithm in the Cipher Block Chaining Mode, with an explicit IV, as a confidentiality mechanism within the context of the IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehH.J. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnameiJ.H. Yoon¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehS.L. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehJ.I. Leeddatej”`hfilenamegrfc4196eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126128hkeywordsÇiipsec esptencryption algorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCLXlgrankingdcrfcd4196irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0The SEED Cipher Algorithm and Its Use with IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|This document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace that is engineered by the TV-Anytime Forum for naming persistent resources published by the TV-Anytime Forum including the TV-Anytime Forum Standards, XML (Extensible Markup Language) Document Type Definitions, XML Schemas, Namespaces, and other documents.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwGITS, Waseda UniversitydnamekW. Kameyamaddatej”_hfilenamegrfc4195eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126127hkeywordsÜtdigital broadcastingbtveradioostorage systemshmetadatagschemaskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBÁ√grankingccrfcd4195irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the TV-Anytime ForumdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document specifies the S Hexdump Format (SHF), a new, XML-based open format for describing binary data in hexadecimal notation.  SHF provides the ability to describe both small and large, simple and complex hexadecimal data dumps in an open, modern, transport- and vendor-neutral format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmInformAsic ABdnameoJ. Strombergson¢kaffiliationwLunds Tekniska HogskoladnamejL. Walleij¢kaffiliationqCisco Systems IncdnamelP. Faltstromddatej”Ohfilenamegrfc4194eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126126hkeywordsçcshfsstandard hex formattsecure hash standardcshsesha-1onist fips 180-2kbinary datakdump formatkhexadecimalpintel hex formates-recxextensible markup languagecxmlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBÁSgrankingbcrfcd4194irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletThe S Hexdump FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÎThis document defines an IPv6 unicast address format that is globally unique and is intended for local communications, usually inside of a site.  These addresses are not expected to be routable on the global Internet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationgJHU-APLdnamekB. Habermanddatej”Ohfilenamegrfc4193eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126125hkeywordsÇqinternet protocolslocal communicationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBÁgrankingacrfcd4193irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Unique Local IPv6 Unicast AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a procedure that can be used to renumber a network from one prefix to another.  It uses IPv6's intrinsic ability to assign multiple addresses to a network interface to provide continuity of network service through a "make-before-break" transition, as well as addresses naming and configuration management issues.  It also uses other IPv6 features to minimize the effort and time required to complete the transition from the old prefix to the new prefix.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems GmbHdnamegE. Lear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Dromsddatej”Phfilenamegrfc4192eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126124hkeywordsÜfprefixqinternet protocolqnetwork interfaceqmake-before-breakjenterpriserconnecting routerskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC9‰Ôgranking`crfcd4192irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Procedures for Renumbering an IPv6 Network without a Flag DaydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌThis document describes an optional extension to Router Advertisement messages for communicating default router preferences and more-specific routes from routers to hosts.  This improves the ability of hosts to pick an appropriate router, especially when the host is multi-homed and the routers are on different links.  The preference values and specific routes advertised to hosts require administrative configuration; they are not automatically derived from routing tables. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiR. Draves¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej”ahfilenamegrfc4191eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126123hkeywordsÅtrouter advertisementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCzêXgranking_crfcd4191irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Default Router Preferences and More-Specific RoutesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyeThis document presents a framework for supporting authorized, emergency-related communication within the context of IP telephony.  We present a series of objectives that reflect a general view of how authorized emergency service, in line with the Emergency Telecommunications Service (ETS), should be realized within today's IP architecture and service models.  From these objectives, we present a corresponding set of protocols and capabilities, which provide a more specific set of recommendations regarding existing IETF protocols.  Finally, we present two scenarios that act as guiding models for the objectives and functions listed in this document.  These models, coupled with an example of an existing service in the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN), contribute to a constrained solution space.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcG11dnamekK. Carlberg¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamehI. Brown¢kaffiliationdUMKCdnamehC. Beardddatej”bhfilenamegrfc4190eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfieprepdfullx(ieprep - Internet Emergency PreparednessdnamexInternet Emergency Preparednessdtypebwgbidjdoc-126122hkeywordsÇwdisaster communicationspprioritized voipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCzêgranking^crfcd4190irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSFramework for Supporting Emergency Telecommunications Service (ETS) in IP TelephonydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) User Agent (UA) does not always trust all intermediaries in its request path to inspect its message bodies and/or headers contained in its message.  The UA might want to protect the message bodies and/or headers from intermediaries, except those that provide services based on its content.  This situation requires a mechanism called "end-to-middle security" to secure the information passed between the UA and intermediaries, which does not interfere with end-to-end security.  This document defines a set of requirements for a mechanism to achieve end-to-middle security.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamefK. Ono¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnamelS. Tachimotoddatej”^hfilenamegrfc4189eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126121hkeywordsÉjuser agentbuanintermediarieskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCGgranking]crfcd4189irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRequirements for End-to-Middle Security for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyiThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets. In particular, it defines objects for managing MAC bridges based on the IEEE 802.1D-1998 standard between Local Area Network (LAN) segments. Provisions are made for the support of transparent bridging. Provisions are also made so that these objects apply to bridges connected by subnetworks other than LAN segments.

The MIB module presented in this memo is a translation of the BRIDGE-MIB defined in RFC 1493 to the SMIv2 syntax.

This memo obsoletes RFC 1493. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrL-3 CommunicationsdnamejK. Norseth¢kaffiliations3Com Europe LimiteddnamegE. Bellddatej”_hfilenamegrfc4188eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfbridgedfullsbridge - Bridge MIBdnamejBridge MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126120hkeywordsÜjBRIDGE-MIBdSNMPcMIBhstandardistandardsxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateC= àgranking\crfcd4188irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Definitions of Managed Objects for BridgesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThis document specifies an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) mechanism for authentication and session key distribution that uses the Authentication and Key Agreement (AKA) mechanism. AKA is used in the 3rd generation mobile networks Universal Mobile Telecommunications System (UMTS) and CDMA2000. AKA is based on symmetric keys, and typically runs in a Subscriber Identity Module, which is a UMTS Subscriber Identity Module, USIM, or a (Removable) User Identity Module, (R)UIM, similar to a smart card.

EAP-AKA includes optional identity privacy support, optional result indications, and an optional fast re-authentication procedure. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelH. Haverinenddatej—Zhfilenamegrfc4187eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126119hkeywordsÉd3gppx*universal mobile telecommunications systemdumtskobsoletedByÄepagesOopublicationDateC≈∂égranking[crfcd4187irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgExtensible Authentication Protocol Method for 3rd Generation Authentication and Key Agreement (EAP-AKA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9048d5448∂habstracty This document specifies an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) mechanism for authentication and session key distribution using the Global System for Mobile Communications (GSM) Subscriber Identity Module (SIM).  GSM is a second generation mobile network standard.  The EAP-SIM mechanism specifies enhancements to GSM authentication and key agreement whereby multiple authentication triplets can be combined to create authentication responses and session keys of greater strength than the individual GSM triplets.  The mechanism also includes network authentication, user anonymity support, result indications, and a fast re-authentication procedure.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelH. Haverinen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Saloweyddatej—Zhfilenamegrfc4186eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126118hkeywordsÅd3gppkobsoletedByÄepages\opublicationDateC≈∂1grankingZcrfcd4186irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÅExtensible Authentication Protocol Method for Global System for Mobile Communications (GSM) Subscriber Identity Modules (EAP-SIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyŒIn the context of work on internationalizing the Domain Name System (DNS), there have been extensive discussions about "multilingual" or "internationalized" top level domain names (TLDs), especially for countries whose predominant language is not written in a Roman-based script.  This document reviews some of the motivations for such domains, several suggestions that have been made to provide needed functionality, and the constraints that the DNS imposes.  It then suggests an alternative, local translation, that may solve a superset of the problem while avoiding protocol changes, serious deployment delays, and other difficulties.  The suggestion utilizes a localization technique in applications to permit any TLD to be accessed using the vocabulary and characters of any language.  It is not restricted to language- or country-specific "multilingual" TLDs in the language(s) and script(s) of that country.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret CullengauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej—9hfilenamegrfc4185eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126117hkeywordsÑrdomain name systemlmultilingualqinternationalizedqlocal translationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCVçTgrankingYcrfcd4185irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexBNational and Local Characters for DNS Top Level Domain (TLD) NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document describes an RTP payload format for transporting audio data using the AC-3 audio compression standard.  AC-3 is a high quality, multichannel audio coding system that is used for United States HDTV, DVD, cable television, satellite television and other media.  The RTP payload format presented in this document includes support for data fragmentation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrDolby LaboratoriesdnamegB. Link¢kaffiliationrDolby LaboratoriesdnamehT. Hager¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehJ. Flaksddatej”vhfilenamegrfc4184eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126116hkeywordsÇxreal time transport protocolqaudio compressionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCNÂ"grankingXcrfcd4184irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!RTP Payload Format for AC-3 AudiodtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÖThis document suggests a method of using DNS to determine the network that contains a specified IP address, the netmask of that network, and the address(es) of first-hop routers(s) on that network.  This method supports variable-length subnet masks, delegation of subnets on non-octet boundaries, and multiple routers per subnet.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid KessensgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekE. Warnickeddatej—[hfilenamegrfc4183eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126115hkeywordsÜqdomain name spacejip addressxinternet protocol addressgnetmaskpfirst-hop routerfsubnetkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateC9‰ögrankingWcrfcd4183irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex>A Suggested Scheme for DNS Resolution of Networks and GatewaysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¥The label stack encoding for Multi-protocol Label Switching (MPLS) defines a reserved label value known as "IPv4 Explicit NULL" and a reserved label value known as "IPv6 Explicit NULL". Previously, these labels were only legal when they occurred at the bottom of the MPLS label stack. This restriction is now removed, so that these label values may legally occur anywhere in the stack.

This document updates RFC 3032. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej”Khfilenamegrfc4182eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126114hkeywordsÇxmultiprotocol label switchingripv4 explicit nullkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC'HgrankingVcrfcd4182irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Removing a Restriction on the use of MPLS Explicit NULLdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7274d5462∑habstractyXThis memo provides guidelines for authors and reviewers of IETF standards-track specifications containing MIB modules.  Applicable portions may be used as a basis for reviews of other MIB documents.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. Heardddatej”Nhfilenamegrfc4181eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgopsareadfullx3opsarea - Operations & Management Area Open Meetingdnamex)Operations & Management Area Open Meetingdtypebagbidjdoc-126113hkeywordsÉxstandards-track specificationsxmanagement information basefreviewkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateC8g5grankingUcrfcd4181irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberoetotaletitlex5Guidelines for Authors and Reviewers of MIB DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4841∂habstractx∏This RFC documents the format used for Comma-Separated Values (CSV) files and registers the associated MIME type "text/csv".  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxSolidMatrix Technologies, Inc.dnameoY. Shafranovichddatej”Phfilenamegrfc4180eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126112hkeywordsÅhtext/csvkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBÊ™grankingTcrfcd4180irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBCommon Format and MIME Type for Comma-Separated Values (CSV) FilesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7111∂habstractyAThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the National Computerization Agency (NCA) for naming persistent digital resources such as music, videos, texts, images, e-books, and other types of digital resources produced or managed by NCA.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxNational Computerization AgencydnamegS. Kangddatej—Whfilenamegrfc4179eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126111hkeywordsÉcncaxnational computerization agencyqdigital resourceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCVçgrankingScrfcd4179irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHUsing Universal Content Identifier (UCI) as Uniform Resource Names (URN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This document specifies a negotiation mechanism for the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API), which is described in RFC 2743. GSS-API peers can use this negotiation mechanism to choose from a common set of security mechanisms. If per-message integrity services are available on the established mechanism context, then the negotiation is protected against an attacker that forces the selection of a mechanism not desired by the peers.

This mechanism replaces RFC 2478 in order to fix defects in that specification and to describe how to inter-operate with implementations of that specification that are commonly deployed on the Internet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehP. Leach¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamemK. Jaganathan¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamelW. Ingersollddatej”Nhfilenamegrfc4178eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfkittendfullx9kitten - Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdnamex0Common Authentication Technology Next Generationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126110hkeywordsÜggenericgservicekapplicationhsecuritygprogramiinterfacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBÊWgrankingRcrfcd4178irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoThe Simple and Protected Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Negotiation MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2This memo provides an analysis of the architectural aspects of multi-homing support for the IPv6 protocol suite.  The purpose of this analysis is to provide a taxonomy for classification of various proposed approaches to multi-homing.  It is also an objective of this exercise to identify common aspects of this domain of study, and also to provide a framework that can allow exploration of some of the further implications of various architectural extensions that are intended to support multi-homing.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej”Phfilenamegrfc4177eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmulti6dfullx!multi6 - Site Multihoming in IPv6dnamexSite Multihoming in IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126109hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateC= .grankingQcrfcd4177irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Architectural Approaches to Multi-homing for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘This document provides a framework for the operation and management of Layer 3 Virtual Private Networks (L3VPNs).  This framework intends to produce a coherent description of the significant technical issues that are important in the design of L3VPN management solutions.  The selection of specific approaches, and making choices among information models and protocols are outside the scope of this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemMark Townsleydarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamemY. El Mghazli¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamelM. Boucadair¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamejA. GonguetddatejÀõhfilenamegrfc4176eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126108hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCGΩgrankingPcrfcd4176irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPFramework for Layer 3 Virtual Private Networks (L3VPN) Operations and ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘This memo specifies a packetization scheme for encapsulating uncompressed video into a payload format for the Real-time Transport Protocol, RTP.  It supports a range of standard- and high-definition video formats, including common television formats such as ITU BT.601, and standards from the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE), such as SMPTE 274M and SMPTE 296M.  The format is designed to be applicable and extensible to new video formats as they are developed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnameiL. Gharai¢kaffiliationuUniversity of GlasgowdnamejC. Perkinsddatej”Jhfilenamegrfc4175eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126107hkeywordsÜtpacketization schemexreal-time transport protocolxreal time transport protocolesmptex.society of motion picture television engineersmvideo formatskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC9‰GgrankingOcrfcd4175irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)RTP Payload Format for Uncompressed VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4421∂habstractywThis document describes the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) option to allow Internet Storage Name Service (iSNS) clients to discover the location of the iSNS server automatically through the use of DHCP for IPv4.  iSNS provides discovery and management capabilities for Internet SCSI (iSCSI) and Internet Fibre Channel Protocol (iFCP) storage devices in an enterprise-scale IP storage network.  iSNS provides intelligent storage management services comparable to those found in Fibre Channel networks, allowing a commodity IP network to function in a similar capacity to that of a storage area network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehC. Monia¢kaffiliationsRiverbed TechnologydnamehJ. Tseng¢kaffiliationrMcDATA CorporationdnamejK. Gibbonsddatej—<hfilenamegrfc4174eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126106hkeywordsádisnsxinternet storage name serviceeiscsiminternet scsidifcpvinternet fibre channelostorage deviceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC1≈µgrankingNcrfcd4174irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`The IPv4 Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Option for the Internet Storage Name ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyßInternet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) is a proposed transport protocol for Small Computer Systems Interface (SCSI) that operates on top of TCP.  This memo describes a standard mechanism for enabling clients to bootstrap themselves using the iSCSI protocol.  The goal of this standard is to enable iSCSI boot clients to obtain the information to open an iSCSI session with the iSCSI boot server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiP. Sarkar¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamekD. Missimer¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamenC. Sapuntzakisddatej—Thfilenamegrfc4173eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126105hkeywordsÑdscsictcpxtransport control protocolkboot serverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC1≈_grankingMcrfcd4173irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYBootstrapping Clients using the Internet Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyThis document specifies an architecture and a gateway-to-gateway protocol for the implementation of fibre channel fabric functionality over an IP network.  This functionality is provided through TCP protocols for fibre channel frame transport and the distributed fabric services specified by the fibre channel standards.  The architecture enables internetworking of fibre channel devices through gateway-accessed regions with the fault isolation properties of autonomous systems and the scalability of the IP network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehC. Monia¢kaffiliationfMcDATAdnamemR. Mullendore¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamemF. Travostino¢kaffiliationoTroika NetworksdnamehW. Jeong¢kaffiliationqAdaptec (UK) Ltd.dnamejM. Edwardsddatej—Yhfilenamegrfc4172eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126104hkeywordsÑrgateway-to-gatewaytfibre channel fabricctcpxtransport control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesoopublicationDateC1ƒÌgrankingLcrfcd4172irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?iFCP - A Protocol for Internet Fibre Channel Storage NetworkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7146d6172∂habstracty]This document specifies the Internet Storage Name Service (iSNS) protocol, used for interaction between iSNS servers and iSNS clients, which facilitates automated discovery, management, and configuration of iSCSI and Fibre Channel devices (using iFCP gateways) on a TCP/IP network.  iSNS provides intelligent storage discovery and management services comparable to those found in Fibre Channel networks, allowing a commodity IP network to function in a capacity similar to that of a storage area network.  iSNS facilitates a seamless integration of IP and Fibre Channel networks due to its ability to emulate Fibre Channel fabric services and to manage both iSCSI and Fibre Channel devices.  iSNS thereby provides value in any storage network comprised of iSCSI devices, Fibre Channel devices (using iFCP gateways), or any combination thereof. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsRiverbed TechnologydnamehJ. Tseng¢kaffiliationrMcDATA CorporationdnamejK. Gibbons¢kaffiliationfNorteldnamemF. Travostino¢kaffiliationxRincon Research CorporationdnamekC. Du Laney¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehJ. Souzaddatej—Jhfilenamegrfc4171eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-126103hkeywordsÖlisns serverslisns clientsufibre channel devicesdifcpxintelligent storage discoverykobsoletedByÄepages{opublicationDateC1ƒågrankingKcrfcd4171irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Internet Storage Name Service (iSNS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyHThis document describes a method to improve the bandwidth utilization of RTP streams over network paths that carry multiple Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) streams in parallel between two endpoints, as in voice trunking.  The method combines standard protocols that provide compression, multiplexing, and tunneling over a network path for the purpose of reducing the bandwidth used when multiple RTP streams are carried over that path.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekB. Thompson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Koren¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wingddatej”Khfilenamegrfc4170eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126102hkeywordsÅxreal-time transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC~a{grankingJcrfcd4170irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbernetotaletitlex,Tunneling Multiplexed Compressed RTP (TCRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaHTTP Digest, as specified in RFC 2617, is known to be vulnerable to man-in-the-middle attacks if the client fails to authenticate the server in TLS, or if the same passwords are used for authentication in some other context without TLS.  This is a general problem that exists not just with HTTP Digest, but also with other IETF protocols that use tunneled authentication.  This document specifies version 2 of the HTTP Digest AKA algorithm (RFC 3310).  This algorithm can be implemented in a way that it is resistant to the man-in-the-middle attack.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqTurku PolytechnicdnamekV. Torvinen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejM. Naslundddatej”Lhfilenamegrfc4169eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126101hkeywordsÑctlsxtransport layer securitywtunneled authenticationxman-in-the-middle attackskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateClIgrankingIcrfcd4169irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoHypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Digest Authentication Using Authentication and Key Agreement (AKA) Version-2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThis document specifies a mechanism for usage of SCTP (the Stream Control Transmission Protocol) as the transport mechanism between SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) entities.  SCTP is a new protocol that provides several features that may prove beneficial for transport between SIP entities that exchange a large amount of messages, including gateways and proxies.  As SIP is transport-independent, support of SCTP is a relatively straightforward process, nearly identical to support for TCP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”Mhfilenamegrfc4168eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126100hkeywordsÅstransport mechanismkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateCBÂÍgrankingHcrfcd4168irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhThe Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) as a Transport for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty?Graceful OSPF Restart, as specified in RFC 3623, provides a mechanism whereby an OSPF router can stay on the forwarding path even as its OSPF software is restarted.  This document provides an implementation report for this extension to the base OSPF protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnameiA. Lindemddatej—Fhfilenamegrfc4167eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126099hkeywordsÅxopen shortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCBÂêgrankingGcrfcd4167irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Graceful OSPF Restart Implementation ReportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôThis document describes the applicability of the several protocols developed under the signalling transport framework.  A description of the main issues regarding the use of the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) and an explanation of each adaptation layer for transport of telephony signalling information over IP infrastructure are given.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamehL. Coene¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepJ. Pastor-Balbasddatej»âhfilenamegrfc4166eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126098hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC„Ú‰grankingFcrfcd4166irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexgTelephony Signalling Transport over Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Applicability StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis document defines a protocol supporting the transport of Signaling System Number 7 (SS7) Message Transfer Part (MTP) Level 3 signaling messages over Internet Protocol (IP) using the services of the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  This protocol would be used between SS7 Signaling Points using the MTP Level 3 protocol.  The SS7 Signaling Points may also use standard SS7 links using the SS7 MTP Level 2 to provide transport of MTP Level 3 signaling messages.  The protocol operates in a manner similar to MTP Level 2 so as to provide peer-to-peer communication between SS7 endpoints. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgOpenSS7dnameiT. George¢kaffiliationgOpenSS7dnamekB. Bidulock¢kaffiliationxUniversity of North TexasdnamehR. Dantu¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnameoH. Schwarzbauer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Morneaultddatej”_hfilenamegrfc4165eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126097hkeywordsÑkss7 over ipfss7/ipgsigtrandm2uakobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateC8fÀgrankingEcrfcd4165irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcSignaling System 7 (SS7) Message Transfer Part 2 (MTP2) - User Peer-to-Peer Adaptation Layer (M2PA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document defines context replication, a complement to the context initialization procedure found in Robust Header Compression (ROHC), as specified in RFC 3095.  Profiles defining support for context replication may use the mechanism described herein to establish a new context based on another already existing context.  Context replication is introduced to reduce the overhead of the context establishment procedure.  It may be especially useful for the compression of multiple short-lived flows that may be occurring simultaneously or near-simultaneously, such as short-lived TCP flows. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletierddatej”Ihfilenamegrfc4164eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126096hkeywordsÇvcontext initializationkshort-livedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCHQgrankingDcrfcd4164irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGRObust Header Compression (ROHC): Context Replication for ROHC ProfilesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜This document contains requirements on the TCP/IP header compression scheme (profile) to be developed by the RObust Header Compression (ROHC) Working Group.  The document discusses the scope of TCP compression, performance considerations, assumptions about the surrounding environment, as well as Intellectual Property Rights concerns.  The structure of this document is inherited from RFC 3096, which defines IP/UDP/RTP requirements for ROHC.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonssonddatej”Ihfilenamegrfc4163eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126095hkeywordsÜxtransmission control protocolqinternet protocolkcompressionxperformance considerationsxintellectual property rightsciprkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateCÑ“grankingCcrfcd4163irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRObust Header Compression (ROHC): Requirements on TCP/IP Header CompressiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxæThis document proposes the addition of new cipher suites to the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to support the SEED encryption algorithm as a bulk cipher algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehH.J. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnameiJ.H. Yoon¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamehJ.I. Leeddatej”Hhfilenamegrfc4162eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126094hkeywordsÇtencryption algorithmkciphersuitekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCGÒgrankingBcrfcd4162irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Addition of SEED Cipher Suites to Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyTo allow connectivity between the general switched telephone network facsimile service (GSTN fax) and the e-mail-based Internet Fax service (i-fax), an "Internet Fax Gateway" is required. This document provides guidelines for the optional functionality of Internet Fax Gateways. In this context, an "offramp gateway" provides facsimile data transmission from i-fax to GSTN fax; vice versa, an "onramp gateway" provides data transmission from GSTN fax to i-fax. The recommendations in this document apply to the integrated service including Internet Fax terminals, computers with i-fax software on the Internet, and GSTN fax terminals on the GSTN.

This document supplements the recommendation for minimal features of an Internet Fax Gateway. In particular, it covers techniques for dropping duplicated fax messages, automatic fax re-transmission, error, return notice, and log handling, and possible authorization methods by DTMF (Dual Tone Multi-Frequency) for onramp gateways. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnameiK. Mimura¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnamekK. Yokoyama¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnamehT. Satoh¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnamekK. Watanabe¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnamejC. Kanaideddatej”Hhfilenamegrfc4161eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-126093hkeywordsÜx3general switched telephone network acsimile servicehgstn faxtinternet fax serviceei-faxoofframp gatewaynonramp gatewaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCGùgrankingAcrfcd4161irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Guidelines for Optional Services for Internet Fax GatewaysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿To allow connectivity between the General Switched Telephone Network facsimile service (GSTN fax) and the e-mail-based Internet Fax service (i-fax) an "Internet Fax Gateway" is required.  This document provides recommendations for the functionality of Internet Fax Gateways.  In this context, an "offramp gateway" provides facsimile data transmission from i-fax to GSTN fax; vice versa, an "onramp gateway" provides data transmission form GSTN fax to i-fax.  The recommendations in this document apply to the integrated service including Internet Fax terminals, computers with i-fax software on the Internet, and GSTN Fax terminals on the GSTN.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnameiK. Mimura¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnamekK. Yokoyama¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnamehT. Satoh¢kaffiliationxTOYO Communication EquipmentdnamejC. Kanaide¢kaffiliationoConsortium GARRdnamelC. Allocchioddatej”Ihfilenamegrfc4160eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-126092hkeywordsÖx4general switched telephone network facsimile servicehgstn faxtinternet fax serviceei-faxnonramp gatewaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCGUgranking@crfcd4160irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Internet Fax Gateway RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document advises of the deprecation of the use of "ip6.int" for Standards Conformant IPv6 implementations.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej”whfilenamegrfc4159eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126091hkeywordsÉdipv6cdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÑÉgranking?crfcd4159irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbermetotaletitlexDeprecation of "ip6.int"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty√This document provides guidance and recommendations to developers building X.509 public-key certification paths within their applications.  By following the guidance and recommendations defined in this document, an application developer is more likely to develop a robust X.509 certificate-enabled application that can build valid certification paths across a wide range of PKI environments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxOrion Security SolutionsdnameiM. Cooper¢kaffiliationnA&N AssociatesdnamelY. Dzambasow¢kaffiliationxGemini Security SolutionsdnamehP. Hesse¢kaffiliationuVan Dyke TechnologiesdnameiS. Joseph¢kaffiliationkBAE SystemsdnamekR. Nicholasddatej—dhfilenamegrfc4158eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-126090hkeywordsÑxcertification path discoverynpath discoveryxcertificate path buildingxcertificate path discoverykobsoletedByÄepagesQopublicationDateC8fygranking>crfcd4158irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure: Certification Path BuildingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis document specifies the prospero Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme that was originally specified in RFC 1738.  The purpose of this document is to allow RFC 1738 to be made obsolete while keeping the information about the scheme on standards track.  This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej”Ghfilenamegrfc4157eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126089hkeywordsÅxuniform resource identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÑ1granking=crfcd4157irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewThe prospero URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document specifies the wais Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme that was originally specified in RFC 1738.  The purpose of this document is to allow RFC 1738 to be made obsolete while keeping the information about the scheme on standards track.  This memo defines a Historic Document for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej”Lhfilenamegrfc4156eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126088hkeywordsÅxuniform resource identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÉ˜granking<crfcd4156irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlesThe wais URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis memo requests that the application/mbox media type be authorized for allocation by the IESG, according to the terms specified in RFC 2048.  This memo also defines a default format for the mbox database, which must be supported by all conformant implementations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegE. Hallddatej”Hhfilenamegrfc4155eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126087hkeywordsÅmmbox databasekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateC'G¥granking;crfcd4155irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe application/mbox Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty␀This document specifies the Voucher Trading System Application Programming Interface (VTS-API).  The VTS-API allows a wallet or other application to issue, transfer, and redeem vouchers in a uniform manner independent of the VTS implementation.  The VTS is a system for securely transferring vouchers; e.g., coupons, tickets, loyalty points, and gift certificates.  This process is often necessary in the course of payment and/or delivery transactions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjNTT DoCoModnameiM. Terada¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Fujimuraddatej”Ehfilenamegrfc4154eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126086hkeywordsÉfwallethtransferfredeemkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateCÜ„granking:crfcd4154irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBVoucher Trading System Application Programming Interface (VTS-API)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyºThis document specifies rules for defining voucher properties in XML syntax.  A voucher is a logical entity that represents a right to claim goods or services.  A voucher can be used to transfer a wide range of electronic values, including coupons, tickets, loyalty points, and gift certificates, which often have to be processed in the course of payment and/or delivery transactions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Fujimura¢kaffiliationjNTT DoCoModnameiM. Terada¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej”@hfilenamegrfc4153eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126085hkeywordsÇxextensible markup languagenlogical entitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÖ?granking9crfcd4153irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%XML Voucher: Generic Voucher LanguagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace (RFC 3406) for the assignment of the Common Language Equipment Identifier (CLEI) code, which is used in messages standardized by ANSI.  The URN namespace is managed by Telcordia Technologies, Inc., as the maintenance agent for ANSI T1.213.  The CLEI code is a globally unique, ten-character alphanumeric intelligent code assigned by Telcordia Technologies at the request of equipment suppliers.  The CLEI code identifies communications equipment by specifying product type and features.  There is a one-to-one relationship between a CLEI code and supplier's product ID (the manufacturer's name and the part number along with its version number).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvTelcordia TechnologiesdnameiK. Tesink¢kaffiliationvTelcordia TechnologiesdnamefR. Foxddatej”Phfilenamegrfc4152eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126084hkeywordsÇdansikansi t1.213kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCÉ©granking8crfcd4152irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Common Language Equipment Identifier (CLEI) CodedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the "tag" Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme.  Tag URIs (also known as "tags") are designed to be unique across space and time while being tractable to humans.  They are distinct from most other URIs in that they have no authoritative resolution mechanism.  A tag may be used purely as an entity identifier.  Furthermore, using tags has some advantages over the common practice of using "http" URIs as identifiers for non-HTTP-accessible resources.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxHewlett-Packard CorporationdnamekT. Kindberg¢kaffiliationxWorld Wide Web ConsortiumdnamehS. Hawkeddatej”Ihfilenamegrfc4151eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126083hkeywordsÇxuniform resource identifierqentity identifierkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC^√Egranking7crfcd4151irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletThe 'tag' URI SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for monitoring selectable performance metrics and statistics derived from the monitoring of network packets and sub-application level transactions.  The metrics can be defined through reference to existing IETF, ITU, and other standards organizations' documents.  The monitoring covers both passive and active traffic generation sources. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjHifn, Inc.dnamehR. Dietz¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamegR. Coleddatej—ahfilenamegrfc4150eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126082hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basectpmgtpm-mibkobsoletedByÄepages9opublicationDateBÌ»pgranking6crfcd4150irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Transport Performance Metrics MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects for configuring Synthetic Sources for Performance Monitoring (SSPM) algorithms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamenC. Kalbfleisch¢kaffiliationgJHU/APLdnamegR. Cole¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanuddatej—Mhfilenamegrfc4149eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126081hkeywordsÑdsspmcmibxmanagement information basehsspm mibkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDateB˜Ú:granking5crfcd4149irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYDefinition of Managed Objects for Synthetic Sources for Performance Monitoring AlgorithmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis memo defines a registry for IP Performance Metrics (IPPM). It assigns and registers an initial set of OBJECT IDENTITIES to currently defined metrics in the IETF.

This memo also defines the rules for adding IP Performance Metrics that are defined in the future and for encouraging all IP performance metrics to be registered here.

IANA has been assigned to administer this new registry. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnamejE. Stephanddatej”Ehfilenamegrfc4148eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-126080hkeywordsÉqinternet protocolqobject identitiesxiana-ippm-metrics-registry-mibkobsoletedByÅd6248epagesopublicationDateB˜Ò–granking4crfcd4148irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberletotaletitlex.IP Performance Metrics (IPPM) Metrics RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document proposes a revised format for the IANA IPv6 address registries.  Rather than providing a formal definition of the format, it is described by giving examples of the (current as of preparation of this document) contents of the registries in the proposed format.  The proposed format would bring the IANA IPv6 address registries into alignment with the current IPv6 Address Architecture specification, as well as update it to a more useful and generally accepted format.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnameiG. Hustonddatej”@hfilenamegrfc4147eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126079hkeywordsÉxinternet protocol version 6naddress formattaddress architecturekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateCÓgranking3crfcd4147irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Proposed Changes to the Format of the IANA IPv6 RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis memo documents a long-standing technique, supported by a large number of mail servers, which allows users to be notified of new mail. In addition to server support, there are a number of clients that support this, ranging from full email clients to specialized clients whose only purpose is to receive new mail notifications and alert a mail client.

In brief, the server sends the string "nm_notifyuser" CRLF to the finger port on the IP address (either configured or last used) of the user who has received new mail. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQUALCOMMdnamejR. Gellensddatej”@hfilenamegrfc4146eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126078hkeywordsÇkmail clientmnm_notifyuserkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC¥granking2crfcd4146irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSimple New Mail NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document describes how to express media transport over TCP using the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  It defines the SDP 'TCP' protocol identifier, the SDP 'setup' attribute, which describes the connection setup procedure, and the SDP 'connection' attribute, which handles connection reestablishment. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvTactical Software, LLCdnamefD. Yon¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej”Jhfilenamegrfc4145eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126077hkeywordsÉesetupjconnectionoreestablishmentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC/Igranking1crfcd4145irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCTCP-Based Media Transport in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4572µhabstractx∆This document provides simple guidelines that can make it easier for you to gain prominence and influence in most standards organizations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerBrian E. CarpentergauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej»}hfilenamegrfc4144eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126076hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateC1ƒAgranking0crfcd4144irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex?How to Gain Prominence and Influence in Standards OrganizationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈This document describes the functional specification and definition of the ENUM Naming Authority Pointer (NAPTR) record for IFax service.  IFax is "facsimile using Internet mail".  For this use, the Domain Name System (DNS) returns the email address of the referenced IFax system.  This mechanism allows email-based fax communication to use telephone numbers instead of requiring the sender to already know the recipient email address. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcPCCdnameiK. Toyoda¢kaffiliationkBrandenburgdnamejD. Crockerddatej—Phfilenamegrfc4143eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-126075hkeywordsÖenaptrxenum naming authority pointerxfacsimile using internet mailcdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC~a%granking/crfcd4143irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Facsimile Using Internet Mail (IFAX) Service of ENUMdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstracty¯Classic facsimile document exchange represents both a set of technical specifications and a class of service.  Previous work has replicated some of that service class as a profile within Internet mail.  The current specification defines "full mode" carriage of facsimile data over the Internet, building upon that previous work and adding the remaining functionality necessary for achieving reliability and capability negotiation for Internet mail, on a par with classic T.30 facsimile.  These additional features are designed to provide the highest level of interoperability with the standards-compliant email infrastructure and mail user agents, while providing a level of service that approximates what is currently enjoyed by fax users. [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkBrandenburgdnamejD. Crocker¢kaffiliationlNine by NinednamehG. Klyneddatej”<hfilenamegrfc4142eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-126074hkeywordsÉifacsimileifull modeminternet mailkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateC~`÷granking.crfcd4142irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Full-mode Fax Profile for Internet Mail (FFPIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçA message originator sometimes sends content in a form the recipient cannot process or would prefer not to process a form of lower quality than is preferred.  Such content needs to be converted to an acceptable form, with the same information or constrained information (e.g., changing from color to black and white).  In a store-and-forward environment, it may be convenient to have this conversion performed by an intermediary.  This specification integrates two ESMTP extensions and three MIME content header fields, which defines a cooperative service that permits authorized, accountable content form conversion by intermediaries. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcPCCdnameiK. Toyoda¢kaffiliationkBrandenburgdnamejD. Crockerddatej”Bhfilenamegrfc4141eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-126073hkeywordsÑeesmtpxsimple mail transfer protocolx&extended simple mail transfer protocolx%multipurpose internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC~`Égranking-crfcd4141irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/SMTP and MIME Extensions for Content ConversiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˜This document introduces extensions to Mobile IPv6 and IPv6 Neighbour Discovery to allow for local mobility handling.  Hierarchical mobility management for Mobile IPv6 is designed to reduce the amount of signalling between the Mobile Node, its Correspondent Nodes, and its Home Agent.  The Mobility Anchor Point (MAP) described in this document can also be used to improve the performance of Mobile IPv6 in terms of handover speed.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgFlariondnamejH. Soliman¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnameoC. Castelluccia¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekK. El Malki¢kaffiliationeINRIAdnamejL. Bellierddatej”Dhfilenamegrfc4140eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126072hkeywordsÖxinternet protocol version 6sneighbour discoveryrneighbor discoveryumobility anchor pointcmapkobsoletedByÅd5380epagesopublicationDateCngranking,crfcd4140irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Hierarchical Mobile IPv6 Mobility Management (HMIPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$The Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) suite of protocols has been defined to control different switching technologies and different applications. These include support for requesting Time Division Multiplexing (TDM) connections, including Synchronous Optical Network (SONET)/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) and Optical Transport Networks (OTNs).

This document concentrates on the signaling aspects of the GMPLS suite of protocols. It identifies the features to be covered by the GMPLS signaling protocol to support the capabilities of an Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON). This document provides a problem statement and additional requirements for the GMPLS signaling protocol to support the ASON functionality. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. Papadimitriou¢kaffiliationfBoeingdnamehJ. Drake¢kaffiliationcATTdnamefJ. Ash¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrel¢kaffiliationeCienadnamefL. Ongddatej”Bhfilenamegrfc4139eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-126071hkeywordsÖctdmcotnmcontrol planedcalljconnectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBË†granking+crfcd4139irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzRequirements for Generalized MPLS (GMPLS) Signaling Usage and Extensions for Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµSpurious retransmission timeouts cause suboptimal TCP performance because they often result in unnecessary retransmission of the last window of data.  This document describes the F-RTO detection algorithm for detecting spurious TCP retransmission timeouts.  F-RTO is a TCP sender-only algorithm that does not require any TCP options to operate.  After retransmitting the first unacknowledged segment triggered by a timeout, the F-RTO algorithm of the TCP sender monitors the incoming acknowledgments to determine whether the timeout was spurious.  It then decides whether to send new segments or retransmit unacknowledged segments.  The algorithm effectively helps to avoid additional unnecessary retransmissions and thereby improves TCP performance in the case of a spurious timeout.  The F-RTO algorithm can also be applied to the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamelP. Sarolahti¢kaffiliationvUniversity of HelsinkidnamegM. Kojoddatej”@hfilenamegrfc4138eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdtcpmdfullx+tcpm - TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdnamex$TCP Maintenance and Minor Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126070hkeywordsÇctcpxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB˜Òcgranking*crfcd4138irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexñForward RTO-Recovery (F-RTO): An Algorithm for Detecting Spurious Retransmission Timeouts with TCP and the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5682∂habstractyÃThis document describes a set of state machines for Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) peer, EAP stand-alone authenticator (non-pass-through), EAP backend authenticator (for use on Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) servers), and EAP full authenticator (for both local and pass-through). This set of state machines shows how EAP can be implemented to support deployment in either a peer/authenticator or peer/authenticator/AAA Server environment. The peer and stand-alone authenticator machines are illustrative of how the EAP protocol defined in RFC 3748 may be implemented. The backend and full/pass-through authenticators illustrate how EAP/AAA protocol support defined in RFC 3579 may be implemented. Where there are differences, RFC 3748 and RFC 3579 are authoritative.

The state machines are based on the EAP "Switch" model. This model includes events and actions for the interaction between the EAP Switch and EAP methods. A brief description of the EAP "Switch" model is given in the Introduction section.

The state machine and associated model are informative only. Implementations may achieve the same results using different methods. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx Meetinghouse Data CommunicationsdnamemJ. Vollbrecht¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationvUniversity of MarylanddnamejN. Petroni¢kaffiliationdTARIdnamegY. Ohbaddatej”@hfilenamegrfc4137eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymceapdfullx(eap - Extensible Authentication Protocoldnamex"Extensible Authentication Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126069hkeywordsÉxeap stand-alone authenticatorxeap backend authenticatorveap full authenticatorkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDateCˇ¯granking)crfcd4137irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRState Machines for Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Peer and AuthenticatordtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty©This document describes an extension to the OSPF protocol to reduce periodic flooding of Link State Advertisements (LSAs) in stable topologies.

Current OSPF behavior requires that all LSAs, except DoNotAge LSAs, to be refreshed every 30 minutes. This document proposes to generalize the use of DoNotAge LSAs in order to reduce protocol traffic in stable topologies. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameqP. Pillay-Esnaultddatej—Chfilenamegrfc4136eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-126068hkeywordsÑxopen shortest path firstxlink state advertisementclsahdonotagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÍ¡øgranking(crfcd4136irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8OSPF Refresh and Flooding Reduction in Stable TopologiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞When a host establishes a new link-layer connection, it may or may not have a valid IP configuration for Internet connectivity.  The host may check for link change (i.e., determine whether a link change has occurred), and then, based on the result, it can automatically decide whether its IP configuration is still valid.  During link identity detection, the host may also collect necessary information to initiate a new IP configuration if the IP subnet has changed.  In this memo, this procedure is called Detecting Network Attachment (DNA).  DNA schemes should be precise, sufficiently fast, secure, and of limited signaling.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkSamsung AITdnamehJH. Choi¢kaffiliationvCTIE Monash UniversitydnamehG. Daleyddatej”Dhfilenamegrfc4135eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdnadfullx"dna - Detecting Network AttachmentdnamexDetecting Network Attachmentdtypebwgbidjdoc-126067hkeywordsÉcdnaxdetecting attachment linkspchange detectionkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateB˚ıÄgranking'crfcd4135irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Goals of Detecting Network Attachment in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty™This document gives examples of message bodies formatted using S/MIME.  Specifically, it has examples of Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) objects and S/MIME messages (including the MIME formatting).  It includes examples of many common CMS formats.  The purpose of this document is to help increase interoperability for S/MIME and other protocols that rely on CMS.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Mail ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej»ähfilenamegrfc4134eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126066hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesàopublicationDateBÂÏgranking&crfcd4134irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexExamples of S/MIME MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for managing multiple logical and physical entities managed by a single SNMP agent.  This document specifies version 3 of the Entity MIB, which obsoletes version 2 (RFC 2737). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. McCloghrieddatej”Fhfilenamegrfc4133eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfentmibdfullsentmib - Entity MIBdnamejEntity MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126065hkeywordsÉxmanagement information basedsnmpx"simple network management protocolkobsoletedByÅd6933epages>opublicationDateCÉXgranking%crfcd4133irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevEntity MIB (Version 3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¬This document proposes the addition of new cipher suites to the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to support the Camellia encryption algorithm as a bulk cipher algorithm. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx Sony Computer Entertainment Inc.dnameiS. Moriai¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamegA. Kato¢kaffiliationx*Nippon Telegraph and Telephone CorporationdnamehM. Kandaddatej”?hfilenamegrfc4132eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126064hkeywordsÅxcamellia encryption algorithmkobsoletedByÅd5932epagesopublicationDateB·n[granking$crfcd4132irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDAddition of Camellia Cipher Suites to Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines a set of managed objects for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) based management of the Baseline Privacy Plus features of DOCSIS 1.1 and DOCSIS 2.0 (Data-over-Cable Service Interface Specification) compliant Cable Modems and Cable Modem Termination Systems. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehS. Green¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamehK. Ozawa¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnamejE. Cardona¢kaffiliation`dnamemA. Katsnelsonddatej”Ahfilenamegrfc4131eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-126063hkeywordsÑcmibdsnmpx"simple network management protocolrdocs-ietf-bpi2-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesUopublicationDateC/Ùgranking#crfcd4131irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex£Management Information Base for Data Over Cable Service Interface Specification (DOCSIS) Cable Modems and Cable Modem Termination Systems for Baseline Privacy PlusdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractyÛThis document provides an applicability statement (RFC 2026, Section 3.2) that describes how to exchange structured business data securely using the HTTP transfer protocol, instead of SMTP; the applicability statement for SMTP is found in RFC 3335.  Structured business data may be XML; Electronic Data Interchange (EDI) in either the American National Standards Committee (ANSI) X12 format or the UN Electronic Data Interchange for Administration, Commerce, and Transport (UN/EDIFACT) format; or other structured data formats.  The data is packaged using standard MIME structures.  Authentication and data confidentiality are obtained by using Cryptographic Message Syntax with S/MIME security body parts.  Authenticated acknowledgements make use of multipart/signed Message Disposition Notification (MDN) responses to the original HTTP message.  This applicability statement is informally referred to as "AS2" because it is the second applicability statement, produced after "AS1", RFC 3335. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpCyclone CommercednameiD. Moberg¢kaffiliationsDrummond Group Inc.dnamekR. Drummondddatej”?hfilenamegrfc4130eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfediintdfullx9ediint - Electronic Data Interchange-Internet Integrationdnamex0Electronic Data Interchange-Internet Integrationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126062hkeywordsÇxhyper text transfer protocolxsimple mail transfer protocolkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateBÕr¬granking"crfcd4130irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdMIME-Based Secure Peer-to-Peer Business Data Interchange Using HTTP, Applicability Statement 2 (AS2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document defines a mechanism for backhauling Digital Private Network Signaling System 1 (DPNSS 1) and Digital Access Signaling System 2 (DASS 2) messages over IP by extending the ISDN User Adaptation (IUA) Layer Protocol defined in RFC 3057.  DPNSS 1, specified in ND1301:2001/03 (formerly BTNR 188), is used to interconnect Private Branch Exchanges (PBX) in a private network.  DASS 2, specified in BTNR 190, is used to connect PBXs to the PSTN.  This document aims to become an Appendix to IUA and to be the base for a DPNSS 1/DASS 2 User Adaptation (DUA) implementation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrWipro TechnologiesdnamekR. Mukundan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Morneault¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamenN. Mangalpallyddatej—_hfilenamegrfc4129eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126061hkeywordsÑkbackhaulingtisdn user adaptationcpbxxprivate branch exchangeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateC=…Œgranking!crfcd4129irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexzDigital Private Network Signaling System (DPNSS)/Digital Access Signaling System 2 (DASS 2) Extensions to the IUA ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty8"Differentiated Services (Diffserv)-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering Requirements", RFC 3564, specifies the requirements and selection criteria for Bandwidth Constraints Models.  Two such models, the Maximum Allocation and the Russian Dolls, are described therein.  This document complements RFC 3564 by presenting the results of a performance evaluation of these two models under various operational conditions: normal load, overload, preemption fully or partially enabled, pure blocking, or complete sharing.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamefW. Laiddatej—=hfilenamegrfc4128eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126060hkeywordsãslabel switched pathclspllsp blockingnlsp preemptionxlsp priority traffic overloadtbandwidth efficiencyqbandwidth sharingtbandwidth protectionoclass isolationxmaximum allocation modelsrussian dolls modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB√?Hgranking crfcd4128irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexzBandwidth Constraints Models for Differentiated Services (Diffserv)-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering: Performance EvaluationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document provides specifications for one Bandwidth Constraints Model for Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering, which is referred to as the Russian Dolls Model.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheurddatej”>hfilenamegrfc4127eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126059hkeywordsÉeds-tesrussian dolls modelxmulti-protocol label switchingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB√>Ûgrankingcrfcd4127irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexURussian Dolls Bandwidth Constraints Model for Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document complements the Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering (DS-TE) requirements document by giving a functional specification for the Maximum Allocation with Reservation (MAR) Bandwidth Constraints Model.  Assumptions, applicability, and examples of the operation of the MAR Bandwidth Constraints Model are presented.  MAR performance is analyzed relative to the criteria for selecting a Bandwidth Constraints Model, in order to provide guidance to user implementation of the model in their networks.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ashddatej”=hfilenamegrfc4126eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126058hkeywordsâx)diffserv-enabled mpls traffic engineeringeds-tecmarubandwidth reservationtbandwidth allocationtbandwidth protectionvperformance evaluationccacmnetwork modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB√>°grankingcrfcd4126irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÅMax Allocation with Reservation Bandwidth Constraints Model for Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering & Performance ComparisonsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÙThis document provides specifications for one Bandwidth Constraints Model for Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering, which is referred to as the Maximum Allocation Model.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamefW. Laiddatej”=hfilenamegrfc4125eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126057hkeywordsÇeds-texmaximum allocation modelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB√>Agrankingcrfcd4125irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZMaximum Allocation Bandwidth Constraints Model for Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document specifies the protocol extensions for support of Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering (DS-TE).  This includes generalization of the semantics of a number of Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) extensions already defined for existing MPLS Traffic Engineering in RFC 3630, RFC 3784, and additional IGP extensions beyond those.  This also includes extensions to RSVP-TE signaling beyond those already specified in RFC 3209 for existing MPLS Traffic Engineering.  These extensions address the requirements for DS-TE spelled out in RFC 3564. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheurddatej”=hfilenamegrfc4124eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126056hkeywordsÜeDS-TEcigpxinterior gateway protocoljextensionsdrsvpxresource reservation protocolkobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDateB√=Ógrankingcrfcd4124irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJProtocol Extensions for Support of Diffserv-aware MPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes the requirements for the logical entity known as the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)-H.323 Interworking Function (SIP-H.323 IWF) that will allow the interworking between SIP and H.323.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliation`dnamehC. Agbohddatej—@hfilenamegrfc4123eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126055hkeywordsÅmSIP-H.323 IWFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÕsπgrankingcrfcd4123irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexASession Initiation Protocol (SIP)-H.323 Interworking RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This specification defines a Uniform Resource Name namespace for UUIDs (Universally Unique IDentifier), also known as GUIDs (Globally Unique IDentifier). A UUID is 128 bits long, and can guarantee uniqueness across space and time. UUIDs were originally used in the Apollo Network Computing System and later in the Open Software Foundation\'s (OSF) Distributed Computing Environment (DCE), and then in Microsoft Windows platforms.

This specification is derived from the DCE specification with the kind permission of the OSF (now known as The Open Group). Information from earlier versions of the DCE specification have been incorporated into this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehP. Leach¢kaffiliationxRefactored Networks, LLCdnamekM. Mealling¢kaffiliationxDataPower Technology, Inc.dnamegR. Salzddatej”=hfilenamegrfc4122eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126054hkeywordsÉuuniform resource namedguidxglobally unique identifierkobsoletedByÅd9562epages opublicationDateBÕsmgrankingcrfcd4122irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4A Universally Unique IDentifier (UUID) URN NamespacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This document defines protocols, procedures, and conventions to be employed by peers implementing the Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) when using the Kerberos Version 5 mechanism.

RFC 1964 is updated and incremental changes are proposed in response to recent developments such as the introduction of Kerberos cryptosystem framework. These changes support the inclusion of new cryptosystems, by defining new per-message tokens along with their encryption and checksum algorithms based on the cryptosystem profiles. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefL. Zhu¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamemK. Jaganathan¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejS. Hartmanddatej—Thfilenamegrfc4121eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-126053hkeywordsÇjGSSAPI-KERlcryptosystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB◊˚‚grankingcrfcd4121irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlThe Kerberos Version 5 Generic Security Service Application Program Interface (GSS-API) Mechanism: Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÖd6112d6542d6649d5896d8062∂habstracty‰This document provides an overview and specification of Version 5 of the Kerberos protocol, and it obsoletes RFC 1510 to clarify aspects of the protocol and its intended use that require more detailed or clearer explanation than was provided in RFC 1510.  This document is intended to provide a detailed description of the protocol, suitable for implementation, together with descriptions of the appropriate use of protocol messages and fields within those messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgUSC-ISIdnameiC. Neuman¢kaffiliationcMITdnameeT. Yu¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejS. Hartman¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejK. Raeburnddatej—]hfilenamegrfc4120eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-126052hkeywordsÉhKERBEROScCAThSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesäopublicationDateB◊˚Ögra