memDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationxNetnod Internet ExchangednamelK. Lindqvist¢kaffiliationvIndependent ResearcherdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationoLayer8 NetworksdnamehB. Black¢kaffiliationcAOLdnamegV. Gillddatej”@hfilenamegrfc4116eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmulti6dfullx!multi6 - Site Multihoming in IPv6dnamexSite Multihoming in IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-126048hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB·ngrankingcrfcd4116irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*IPv4 Multihoming Practices and LimitationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes a two-rate, three-color marker that has been in use for data services including Frame Relay services.  This marker can be used for metering per-flow traffic in the emerging IP and L2 VPN services.  The marker defined here is different from previously defined markers in the handling of the in-profile traffic.  Furthermore, this marker doesn't impose peak-rate shaping requirements on customer edge (CE) devices.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamemO. Aboul-Magd¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehS. Rabieddatej—?hfilenamegrfc4115eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126047hkeywordsÖmdata servicesqservice scenariosrmetering algorithmkcolor-blindkcolor-awarekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÕr`grankingcrfcd4115irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexcA Differentiated Service Two-Rate, Three-Color Marker with Efficient Handling of in-Profile TrafficdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension mapping for the provisioning and management of E.164 numbers that represent domain names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, this mapping extends the EPP domain name mapping to provide additional features required for the provisioning of E.164 numbers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej—bhfilenamegrfc4114eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126046hkeywordsÅxshared central repositorykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB§Ïîgrankingcrfcd4114irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCE.164 Number Mapping for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for implementations of the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) in an IP version independent manner.  This memo obsoletes RFCs 2013 and 2454. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnameiB. Fenner¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamehJ. Flickddatej—7hfilenamegrfc4113eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126045hkeywordsÜgMIB-UDPcmibxManagement Information BasegUDP-MIBqinternet protocolbipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB©Œµgrankingcrfcd4113irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Management Information Base for the User Datagram Protocol (UDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÌElectronic commerce frequently requires a substantial exchange of information in order to complete a purchase or other transaction, especially the first time the parties communicate.  A standard set of hierarchically-organized payment-related information field names in an XML syntax is defined so that this task can be more easily automated.  This is the second version of an Electronic Commerce Modeling Language (ECML) and is intended to meet the requirements of RFC 3505. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej»{hfilenamegrfc4112eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126044hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateBº´’grankingcrfcd4112irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDElectronic Commerce Modeling Language (ECML) Version 2 SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This document addresses security aspects pertaining to Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPNs).  First, it describes the security threats in the context of PPVPNs and defensive techniques to combat those threats.  It considers security issues deriving both from malicious behavior of anyone and from negligent or incorrect behavior of the providers.  It also describes how these security attacks should be detected and reported.  It then discusses possible user requirements for security of a PPVPN service.  These user requirements translate into corresponding provider requirements.  In addition, the provider may have additional requirements to make its network infrastructure secure to a level that can meet the PPVPN customer's expectations.  Finally, this document defines a template that may be used to describe and analyze the security characteristics of a specific PPVPN technology.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjAT&T Labs.dnamegL. Fangddatej»yhfilenamegrfc4111eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126043hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateB◊˙‡grankingcrfcd4111irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMSecurity Framework for Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyjThis document provides a framework for Layer 3 Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPNs).  This framework is intended to aid in the standardization of protocols and mechanisms for support of layer 3 PPVPNs.  It is the intent of this document to produce a coherent description of the significant technical issues that are important in the design of layer 3 PPVPN solutions.  Selection of specific approaches, making choices regarding engineering tradeoffs, and detailed protocol specification, are outside of the scope of this framework document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Callon¢kaffiliationoNTT CorporationdnameiM. Suzukiddatej»èhfilenamegrfc4110eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-126042hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesRopublicationDateB◊˜3grankingcrfcd4110irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNA Framework for Layer 3 Provider-Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£The required and suggested algorithms in the original Internet Key Exchange version 1 (IKEv1) specification do not reflect the current reality of the IPsec market requirements.  The original specification allows weak security and suggests algorithms that are thinly implemented.  This document updates RFC 2409, the original specification, and is intended for all IKEv1 implementations deployed today. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej—dhfilenamegrfc4109eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126041hkeywordsÜcikeeipsecfoakleynauthenticationfisakmpx internet security key managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBïŒgrankingcrfcd4109irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Algorithms for Internet Key Exchange version 1 (IKEv1)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the use of the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) to protect firmware packages, which provide object code for one or more hardware module components.  CMS is specified in RFC 3852.  A digital signature is used to protect the firmware package from undetected modification and to provide data origin authentication.  Encryption is optionally used to protect the firmware package from disclosure, and compression is optionally used to reduce the size of the protected firmware package.  A firmware package loading receipt can optionally be generated to acknowledge the successful loading of a firmware package.  Similarly, a firmware package load error report can optionally be generated to convey the failure to load a firmware package. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej”Bhfilenamegrfc4108eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126040hkeywordsÇxhardward module componentsqdigital signaturekobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateBÚï¿grankingcrfcd4108irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEUsing Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) to Protect Firmware PackagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractypThe question often arises of whether a given security system requires some form of automated key management, or whether manual keying is sufficient.  This memo provides guidelines for making such decisions.  When symmetric cryptographic mechanisms are used in a protocol, the presumption is that automated key management is generally but not always needed.  If manual keying is proposed, the burden of proving that automated key management is not required falls to the proposer.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamekS. Bellovin¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej”Hhfilenamegrfc4107eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126039hkeywordsÇxautomated key managementumanual key managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB∑∞grankingcrfcd4107irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberketotaletitlex+Guidelines for Cryptographic Key ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis memo describes the use of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) in Galois/Counter Mode (GCM) as an IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) mechanism to provide confidentiality and data origin authentication.  This method can be efficiently implemented in hardware for speeds of 10 gigabits per second and above, and is also well-suited to software implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuSecure Software, Inc.dnamehJ. Viega¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiD. McGrewddatej—:hfilenamegrfc4106eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126038hkeywordsÇcaesxadvanced encryption standardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÆgranking
