estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRThe Use of Galois/Counter Mode (GCM) in IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThis document lists a detailed set of functional requirements for the support of inter-area MPLS Traffic Engineering (inter-area MPLS TE).  It is intended that solutions that specify procedures and protocol extensions for inter-area MPLS TE satisfy these requirements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamemJ.-L. Le Roux¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemJ.-P. Vasseur¢kaffiliationfPDNETsdnamehJ. Boyleddatej—dhfilenamegrfc4105eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-126037hkeywordsÉxmultiprotocol label switchinggmpls-tegmpls tekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB©»πgranking	crfcd4105irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Requirements for Inter-Area MPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis document defines a number of changes and extensions to the Policy Core Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Schema (RFC 3703) based on the model extensions defined by the Policy Core Information Model (PCIM) Extensions (RFC 3460).  These changes and extensions consist of new LDAP object classes and attribute types.  Some of the schema items defined in this document re-implement existing concepts in accordance with their new semantics introduced by RFC 3460.  The other schema items implement new concepts, not covered by RFC 3703.  This document updates RFC 3703. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhMetaSolvdnamegM. Pana¢kaffiliationxComputer Architecture, UPCdnamehA. Reyes¢kaffiliationx!Technical University of CataloniadnamehA. Barba¢kaffiliationx!Technical University of CataloniadnamehD. Moron¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejM. Brunnerddatej—Ohfilenamegrfc4104eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126036hkeywordsÑx1policy core lightweight directory access protocoldpcimxpolicy core information modelomapping classeskobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDateBßÄUgrankingcrfcd4104irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJPolicy Core Extension Lightweight Directory Access Protocol Schema (PCELS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¸This memo obsoletes RFC 2793; it describes how to carry real-time text conversation session contents in RTP packets. Text conversation session contents are specified in ITU-T Recommendation T.140.

One payload format is described for transmitting text on a separate RTP session dedicated for the transmission of text.

This RTP payload description recommends a method to include redundant text from already transmitted packets in order to reduce the risk of text loss caused by packet loss. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjOmnitor ABdnamelG. Hellstrom¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Jonesddatej—8hfilenamegrfc4103eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126035hkeywordsÖireal-timelapplicationsevideoeaudiogpacketskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB§·ògrankingcrfcd4103irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!RTP Payload for Text ConversationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9071∂habstractx¨This document defines the text/red MIME sub-type. "Red" is short for redundant.  The actual RTP packetization for this MIME type is specified in RFC 2198. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehP. Jonesddatej”_hfilenamegrfc4102eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-126034hkeywordsÇcrtpxreal-time transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB§·Hgrankingcrfcd4102irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Registration of the text/red MIME Sub-TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6354µhabstractyThe IETF process depends on peer review.  However, IETF documents are generally written to be useful for implementors, not reviewers.  In particular, while great care is generally taken to provide a complete description of the state machines and bits on the wire, this level of detail tends to get in the way of initial understanding.  This document describes an approach for providing protocol "models" that allow reviewers to quickly grasp the essence of a system.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej”Ihfilenamegrfc4101eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126033hkeywordsÅodocument reviewkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBº´grankingcrfcd4101irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlewWriting Protocol ModelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document establishes terminology to standardize the description of benchmarking methodology for measuring eBGP convergence in the control plane of a single BGP device.  Future documents will address iBGP convergence, the initiation of forwarding based on converged control plane information and multiple interacting BGP devices.This terminology is applicable to both IPv4 and IPv6.  Illustrative examples of each version are included where relevant.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationx"Gett Communications & CCI TrainingdnamelH. Berkowitz¢kaffiliationpFolly ConsultingdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationtNexthop TechnologiesdnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliationdSAICdnameoP. Krishnaswamy¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegM. Leppddatej—`hfilenamegrfc4098eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-126032hkeywordsÉwborder gateway protocolxbenchmarking methodologydebgpkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateB§‡grankingcrfcd4098irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHTerminology for Benchmarking BGP Device Convergence in the Control PlanedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document provides an evaluation of the applicability of SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol), RSIP (Realm Specific Internet Protocol), Megaco, Diameter, and COPS (Common Open Policy Service) as the MIDCOM (Middlebox Communications) protocol.  A summary of each of the proposed protocols against the MIDCOM requirements and the MIDCOM framework is provided.  Compliancy of each of the protocols against each requirement is detailed.  A conclusion summarizes how each of the protocols fares in the evaluation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Barnesddatej—Ehfilenamegrfc4097eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmidcomdfullx midcom - Middlebox CommunicationdnamewMiddlebox Communicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126031hkeywordsàdsnmpx"simple network management protocoldrsipx realm specific internet protocolfmegacohdiameterdcopsxcommon open policy servicekobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateB∞¬Îgrankingcrfcd4097irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Middlebox Communications (MIDCOM) Protocol EvaluationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyŒThis memo discusses policies that require certain labels to be inserted in the "Subject:" header of a mail message.  Such policies are difficult to specify accurately while remaining compliant with key RFCs and are likely to be ineffective at best.  This memo discusses an alternate, \%standards-compliant approach that is significantly simpler to specify and is somewhat less likely to be ineffective.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsMemory Palace PressdnamejC. MalamudddatejÀçhfilenamegrfc4096eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126030hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBóZ˘granking␀crfcd4096irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Policy-Mandated Labels Such as "Adv:" in Email Subject Headers Considered Ineffective At BestdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This document proposes a mechanism for finding a URI associated with a solicitation class keyword, which is defined in RFC 3865, the No Soliciting SMTP Service Extension. Solicitation class keywords are simple labels consisting of a domain name that has been reversed, such as "org.example.adv". These solicitation class keywords are inserted in selected header fields or used in the ESMTP service extension, including a new \%"No-Solicit:" header, which can contain one or more solicitation class keywords inserted by the sender.

This document specifies an application based on the Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) described in RFC 3401 and related documents. An algorithm is specified to associate a solicitation class keyword with a URI which contains further information about the meaning and usage of that solicitation class keyword. For example, the registrant of the "example.org" domain could use this mechanism to create a URI which contains detailed information about the "org.example.adv" solicitation class keyword. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsMemory Palace PressdnamejC. Malamudddatej”dhfilenamegrfc4095eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126029hkeywordsácurixuniform resource identifierx$no soliciting smtp service extensionwesmtp service extensionx#dynamic delegation discovery systemddddsjno-solicitkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBóZ¨grankingˇcrfcd4095irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Attaching Meaning to Solicitation Class KeywordsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThis document reviews some of the existing Quality of Service (QoS) signaling protocols for an IP network.  The goal here is to learn from them and to avoid common misconceptions.  Further, we need to avoid mistakes during the design and implementation of any new protocol in this area.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliation`dnameeX. Fuddatej—8hfilenamegrfc4094eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126028hkeywordsçcqosrquality of servicedrsvpdnsisfyessiriboomerangedarishinsigniadbgrpesicaphmobilitykperformancehsecuritykobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDateBóZagranking˛crfcd4094irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Analysis of Existing Quality-of-Service Signaling ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysDeploying Mobile-IP v4 in networks that are connected to the Internet through a Virtual Private Network (VPN) gateway presents some problems that do not currently have well-described solutions.  This document aims to describe and illustrate these problems, and to propose some guidelines for possible solutions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejF. Adrangi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelH. Levkowetzddatej»Çhfilenamegrfc4093eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-126027hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateCìªgranking˝crfcd4093irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRProblem Statement: Mobile IPv4 Traversal of Virtual Private Network (VPN) GatewaysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how to use the Alternative Network Address Types (ANAT) semantics of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) grouping framework in SIP.  In particular, we define the sdp-anat SIP option-tag.  This SIP option-tag ensures that SDP session descriptions that use ANAT are only handled by SIP entities with ANAT support.  To justify the need for such a SIP option-tag, we describe what could possibly happen if an ANAT-unaware SIP entity tried to handle media lines grouped with ANAT. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej—9hfilenamegrfc4092eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-126026hkeywordsÇhsdp-anatjoption-tagkobsoletedByÅd5245epagesopublicationDateBßÄgranking¸crfcd4092irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexãUsage of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) Alternative Network Address Types (ANAT) Semantics in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines the Alternative Network Address Types (ANAT) semantics for the Session Description Protocol (SDP) grouping framework.  The ANAT semantics allow alternative types of network addresses to establish a particular media stream. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej»∏hfilenamegrfc4091eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-126025hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5245epagesopublicationDateBßúgranking˚crfcd4091irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlextThe Alternative Network Address Types (ANAT) Semantics for the Session Description Protocol (SDP) Grouping FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document defines RSVP-TE extensions to establish backup label-switched path (LSP) tunnels for local repair of LSP tunnels. These mechanisms enable the re-direction of traffic onto backup LSP tunnels in 10s of milliseconds, in the event of a failure.

Two methods are defined here. The one-to-one backup method creates detour LSPs for each protected LSP at each potential point of local repair. The facility backup method creates a bypass tunnel to protect a potential failure point; by taking advantage of MPLS label stacking, this bypass tunnel can protect a set of LSPs that have similar backup constraints. Both methods can be used to protect links and nodes during network failure. The described behavior and extensions to RSVP allow nodes to implement either method or both and to interoperate in a mixed network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrHammerhead SystemsdnamefP. Pan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallow¢kaffiliationmAvici SystemsdnamehA. Atlasddatej—]hfilenamegrfc4090eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126024hkeywordsÜxresource reservation protocolstraffic engineeringclspqlabel switch pathqone-to-one backupofacility backupkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateBóYÓgranking˙crfcd4090irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Fast Reroute Extensions to RSVP-TE for LSP TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÑd8796d9705d8271d8537µhabstractyÏThis document describes a joint recommendation of the Internet Architecture Board and the Internet Engineering Steering Group for administrative restructuring of the Internet Engineering Task Force.  The IETF Chair declared that the IETF had consensus to follow this recommendation on November 11, 2004.  Further work has been done to revise and refine the structures proposed.  The recommendation is being published for the record.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerBrian E. CarpentergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliation`dnamelIAB and IESGddatej—bhfilenamegrfc4089eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-126023hkeywordsÉxinternet architecture boardx#internet engineering steering groupxinternet engineering task forcekobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDateBóYÑgranking˘crfcd4089irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexAIAB and IESG Recommendation for IETF Administrative RestructuringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}The Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) and the Internet Standard Management Framework are widely used for the management of communication devices, creating a need to specify SNMP access (including access to SNMP MIB object instances) from non-SNMP management environments. For example, when out-of-band IP management is used via a separate management interface (e.g., for a device that does not support in-band IP access), a uniform way to indicate how to contact the device for management is needed. Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) fit this need well, as they allow a single text string to indicate a management access communication endpoint for a wide variety of IP-based protocols.

This document defines a URI scheme so that SNMP can be designated as the protocol used for management. The scheme also allows a URI to designate one or more MIB object instances. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoEMC CorporationdnamehD. Black¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationxInternational University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej—chfilenamegrfc4088eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126022hkeywordsÉcurixuniform resource identifiershsnmp-urikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB§‡ógranking¯crfcd4088irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZUniform Resource Identifier (URI) Scheme for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis memo defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for managing tunnels of any type over IPv4 and IPv6 networks.  Extension MIB modules may be designed for managing protocol-specific objects.  Likewise, extension MIB modules may be designed for managing security-specific objects.  This MIB module does not support tunnels over non-IP networks.  Management of such tunnels may be supported by other MIB modules.  This memo obsoletes RFC 2667. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej—7hfilenamegrfc4087eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-126021hkeywordsÉxmanagement information baseqinternet protocoljtunnel-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB§‡Sgranking˜crfcd4087irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlemIP Tunnel MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractySecurity systems are built on strong cryptographic algorithms that foil pattern analysis attempts. However, the security of these systems is dependent on generating secret quantities for passwords, cryptographic keys, and similar quantities. The use of pseudo-random processes to generate secret quantities can result in pseudo-security. A sophisticated attacker may find it easier to reproduce the environment that produced the secret quantities and to search the resulting small set of possibilities than to locate the quantities in the whole of the potential number space.

Choosing random quantities to foil a resourceful and motivated adversary is surprisingly difficult. This document points out many pitfalls in using poor entropy sources or traditional pseudo-random number generation techniques for generating such quantities. It recommends the use of truly random hardware techniques and shows that the existing hardware on many systems can be used for this purpose. It provides suggestions to ameliorate the problem when a hardware solution is not available, and it gives examples of how large such quantities need to be for some applications. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationcMITdnamekJ. Schiller¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Crockerddatej”Ehfilenamegrfc4086eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126020hkeywordsáxcryptographic algorithmsipasswordsrcryptographic keysmpseudo-randomfrandomgnumbersdseedkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDateB§‡grankingˆcrfcd4086irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberjetotaletitlex$Randomness Requirements for SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty©This document discourages the practice of embedding references to unique, globally-routable IP addresses in Internet hosts, describes some of the resulting problems, and considers selected alternatives.  This document is intended to clarify best current practices in this regard.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of WisconsindnameiD. Plonkaddatej»∏hfilenamegrfc4085eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgrowdfullx grow - Global Routing OperationsdnamexGlobal Routing Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126019hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateB©»_grankingıcrfcd4085irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberietotaletitlexAEmbedding Globally-Routable Internet Addresses Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty™As the Internet has evolved, many types of arrangements have been advertised and sold as "Internet connectivity".  Because these may differ significantly in the capabilities they offer, the range of options, and the lack of any standard terminology, the effort to distinguish between these services has caused considerable consumer confusion.  This document provides a list of terms and definitions that may be helpful to providers, consumers, and, potentially, regulators in clarifying the type and character of services being offered.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej»µhfilenamegrfc4084eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126018hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBâgrankingÙcrfcd4084irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberhetotaletitlex0Terminology for Describing Internet ConnectivitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyãThe 3rd-Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) has selected Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) as the session establishment protocol for the 3GPP IP Multimedia Core Network Subsystem (IMS).  IMS is part of Release 5 of the 3GPP specifications.  Although SIP is a protocol that fulfills most of the requirements for establishing a session in an IP network, SIP has never been evaluated against the specific 3GPP requirements for operation in a cellular network.  In this document, we express the requirements identified by 3GPP to support SIP for Release 5 of the 3GPP IMS in cellular networks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepM. Garcia-Martinddatej—;hfilenamegrfc4083eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126017hkeywordsÉx)3GPP IP multimedia core network subsystemcimsqcellular networkskobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateBíIgrankingÛcrfcd4083irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoInput 3rd-Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Release 5 Requirements on the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈This document introduces Timed Efficient Stream Loss-tolerant Authentication (TESLA). TESLA allows all receivers to check the integrity and authenticate the source of each packet in multicast or broadcast data streams. TESLA requires no trust between receivers, uses low-cost operations per packet at both sender and receiver, can tolerate any level of loss without retransmissions, and requires no per-receiver state at the sender. TESLA can protect receivers against denial of service attacks in certain circumstances. Each receiver must be loosely time-synchronized with the source in order to verify messages, but otherwise receivers do not have to send any messages. TESLA alone cannot support non-repudiation of the data source to third parties.

This informational document is intended to assist in writing standardizable and secure specifications for protocols based on TESLA in different contexts. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnameiA. Perrig¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamegD. Song¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejR. Canetti¢kaffiliationx"University of California, BerkeleydnamekJ. D. Tygar¢kaffiliationbBTdnamejB. Briscoeddatej—7hfilenamegrfc4082eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-126016hkeywordsÅldata streamskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB∫˜WgrankingÚcrfcd4082irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexsTimed Efficient Stream Loss-Tolerant Authentication (TESLA): Multicast Source Authentication Transform IntroductiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis threats document provides a detailed analysis of the security threats relevant to the Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS) protocol suite.  It calls attention to, and helps with the understanding of, various security considerations in the NSIS Requirements, Framework, and Protocol proposals.  This document does not describe vulnerabilities of specific parts of the NSIS protocol suite.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamemH. Tschofenig¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamenD. Kroeselbergddatej»ªhfilenamegrfc4081eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126015hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB©«ŒgrankingÒcrfcd4081irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Security Threats for Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%The Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS) working group is considering protocols for signaling information about a data flow along its path in the network. The NSIS suite of protocols is envisioned to support various signaling applications that need to install and/or manipulate such state in the network. Based on existing work on signaling requirements, this document proposes an architectural framework for these signaling protocols.

This document provides a model for the network entities that take part in such signaling, and for the relationship between signaling and the rest of network operation. We decompose the overall signaling protocol suite into a generic (lower) layer, with separate upper layers for each specific signaling application. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkSiemens/RMRdnamejR. Hancock¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Twente/EricssondnamenG. Karagiannis¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepS. Van den Boschddatej—chfilenamegrfc4080eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126014hkeywordsÑidata flowwarchitectural frameworkssignaling protocolsusignaling applicationkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDateB©«tgrankingcrfcd4080irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Next Steps in Signaling (NSIS): FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^GEOPRIV defines the concept of a 'using protocol' -- a protocol that carries GEOPRIV location objects.  GEOPRIV also defines various scenarios for the distribution of location objects that require the concepts of subscriptions and asynchronous notifications.  This document examines some existing IETF work on the concept of presence, shows how presence architectures map onto GEOPRIV architectures, and moreover demonstrates that tools already developed for presence could be reused to simplify the standardization and implementation of GEOPRIV.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej—`hfilenamegrfc4079eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-126013hkeywordsÅnusing protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÕs&grankingÔcrfcd4079irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHA Presence Architecture for the Distribution of GEOPRIV Location ObjectsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Uniform Resource Locator (URL) scheme "CRID:" has been devised to allow references to current or future scheduled publications of broadcast media content over television distribution platforms and the Internet.

The initial intended application is as an embedded link within scheduled programme description metadata that can be used by the home user or agent to associate a programme selection with the corresponding programme location information for subsequent automatic acquisition.

This document reproduces the \%TV-Anytime CRID definition found in the \%TV-Anytime content referencing specification, and is published as an RFC for ease of access and registration with the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA). This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxBBC Research and DevelopmentdnamekN. Earnshaw¢kaffiliationtTokyoFM BroadcastingdnamegS. Aoki¢kaffiliationkNDS LimiteddnameiA. Ashley¢kaffiliationwGITS, Waseda UniversitydnamekW. Kameyamaddatej—8hfilenamegrfc4078eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126012hkeywordsátdigital broadcastingbtveradiocurixuniform resource identifierscontent referencingostorage systemskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateBíH∑grankingÓcrfcd4078irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2The TV-Anytime Content Reference Identifier (CRID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document describes a mechanism that allows Signaling Compression (SigComp) implementations to report precise error information upon receipt of a message which cannot be decompressed.  This negative feedback can be used by the recipient to make fine-grained adjustments to the compressed message before retransmitting it, allowing for rapid and efficient recovery from error situations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpEstacado SystemsdnamejA.B. Roachddatej—bhfilenamegrfc4077eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-126011hkeywordsÇgsigcompqnegative feedbackkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBïÄgrankingÌcrfcd4077irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>A Negative Acknowledgement Mechanism for Signaling CompressiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíIPv6 hosts using Stateless Address Autoconfiguration are able to configure their IPv6 address and default router settings automatically.  However, further settings are not available.  If these hosts wish to configure their DNS, NTP, or other specific settings automatically, the stateless variant of the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) could be used.  This combination of Stateless Address Autoconfiguration and stateless DHCPv6 could be used quite commonly in IPv6 networks.  However, hosts using this combination currently have no means by which to be informed of changes in stateless DHCPv6 option settings; e.g., the addition of a new NTP server address, a change in DNS search paths, or full site renumbering.  This document is presented as a problem statement from which a solution should be proposed in a subsequent document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of SouthamptondnamehT. Chown¢kaffiliationgUNINETTdnameiS. Venaas¢kaffiliationx%Cisco Systems (India) Private LimiteddnamepA. Vijayabhaskarddatej—ahfilenamegrfc4076eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126010hkeywordsÇqinternet protocolx#stateless address autoconfigurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBíHggrankingÏcrfcd4076irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Renumbering Requirements for Stateless Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxïThis document describes a new DHCPv6 option for passing a list of Simple Network Time Protocol (SNTP) server addresses to a client. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx%Cisco Systems (India) Private LimiteddnameqV. Kalusivalingamddatej—8hfilenamegrfc4075eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126009hkeywordsÇx#dynamic host configuration protocolpserver addresseskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBíGıgrankingÎcrfcd4075irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSimple Network Time Protocol (SNTP) Configuration Option for DHCPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThere is some known misbehavior of DNS authoritative servers when they are queried for AAAA resource records.  Such behavior can block IPv4 communication that should actually be available, cause a significant delay in name resolution, or even make a denial of service attack.  This memo describes details of known cases and discusses their effects.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdJPRSdnamelY. Morishita¢kaffiliationgToshibadnameiT. Jinmeiddatej—bhfilenamegrfc4074eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-126008hkeywordsÉpresource recordsdaaaasdomain name servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBûOogrankingÍcrfcd4074irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Common Misbehavior Against DNS Queries for IPv6 AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxƒThis document describes a convention for using the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) to protect a content collection.  If desired, attributes can be associated with the content. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej—:hfilenamegrfc4073eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126007hkeywordsÅrcontent collectionkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateBÇÑ]grankingÈcrfcd4073irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHProtecting Multiple Contents with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty(The Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) provides a standard mechanism for support of various authentication methods.  This document defines the Command-Codes and AVPs necessary to carry EAP packets between a Network Access Server (NAS) and a back-end authentication server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiP. Eronen¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiT. Hiller¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegG. Zornddatej—:hfilenamegrfc4072eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcaaadfullx2aaa - Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdnamex,Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126006hkeywordsÖmcommand codescavpcnasunetwork access serverxback-end autentication serverkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateCìpgrankingËcrfcd4072irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Diameter Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) ApplicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8044d7268∂habstractyAThis document describes the structure of the IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA) as an activity housed within the Internet Society (ISOC).  It defines the roles and responsibilities of the IETF Administrative Oversight Committee (IAOC), the IETF Administrative Director (IAD), and ISOC in the fiscal and administrative support of the IETF standards process.  It also defines the membership and selection rules for the IAOC.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerBrian E. Carpenterdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnenddatej”Fhfilenamegrfc4071eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-126005hkeywordsÖdisocx'ietf administrative oversight committeedIAOCxietf administrative directorciadkobsoletedByÅd8711epagesopublicationDateBf·bgrankingÁcrfcd4071irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Structure of the IETF Administrative Support Activity (IASA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7691d4371∂habstracty€This document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing the Line Code Specific parameters of Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Line (VDSL) interfaces using Multiple Carrier Modulation (MCM) Line Coding.  It is an optional extension to the VDSL-LINE-MIB, RFC 3728, which handles line code independent objects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamehM. Dodge¢kaffiliationvPESA Switching SystemsdnamefB. Rayddatej—Rhfilenamegrfc4070eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126004hkeywordsÑxmanagement information basecmibmVDSL-LINE-MIBuVDSL-LINE-EXT-MCM-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÏ&grankingÊcrfcd4070irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexêDefinitions of Managed Object Extensions for Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Lines (VDSL) Using Multiple Carrier Modulation (MCM) Line CodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing the Line Code Specific parameters of Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Line (VDSL) interfaces using Single Carrier Modulation (SCM) Line Coding.  It is an optional extension to the VDSL-LINE-MIB, RFC 3728, which handles line code independent objects. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkECI TelecomdnamehM. Dodge¢kaffiliationvPESA Switching SystemsdnamefB. Rayddatej—Rhfilenamegrfc4069eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-126003hkeywordsÑcmibxmanagement information basemVDSL-LINE-MIBuVDSL-LINE-EXT-SCM-MIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÎ◊grankingÂcrfcd4069irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexéDefinitions of Managed Object Extensions for Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Lines (VDSL) Using Single Carrier Modulation (SCM) Line CodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty|Mobile IPv6 enables a Mobile Node to maintain its connectivity to the Internet when moving from one Access Router to another, a process referred to as handover.  During handover, there is a period during which the Mobile Node is unable to send or receive packets because of link switching delay and IP protocol operations.  This "handover latency" resulting from standard Mobile IPv6 procedures, namely movement detection, new Care of Address configuration, and Binding Update, is often unacceptable to real-time traffic such as Voice over IP.  Reducing the handover latency could be beneficial to non-real-time, throughput-sensitive applications as well.  This document specifies a protocol to improve handover latency due to Mobile IPv6 procedures.  This document does not address improving the link switching latency.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnameiR. Koodliddatej—^hfilenamegrfc4068eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgmipshopdfullxJmipshop - Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdnamex@Mobility for IP: Performance, Signaling and Handoff Optimizationdtypebwgbidjdoc-126002hkeywordsÑxinternet protocol version 6maccess routerkmobile nodebmnkobsoletedByÅd5268epages*opublicationDateBœwgranking‰crfcd4068irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFast Handovers for Mobile IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∆This document presents the Context Transfer Protocol (CXTP) that enables authorized context transfers.  Context transfers allow better support for node based mobility so that the applications running on mobile nodes can operate with minimal disruption.  Key objectives are to reduce latency and packet losses, and to avoid the re-initiation of signaling to and from the mobile node.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Nakhjiri¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Koodliddatej—Dhfilenamegrfc4067eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgseamobydfullxWseamoby - Context Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host AlertingdnamexMContext Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host Alertingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126001hkeywordsÇkmobile nodebmnkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateBœgranking„crfcd4067irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Context Transfer Protocol (CXTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyŸTo enable seamless IP-layer handover of a mobile node (MN) from one access router (AR) to another, the MN is required to discover the identities and capabilities of candidate ARs (CARs) for handover prior to the initiation of the handover.  The act of discovery of CARs has two aspects: identifying the IP addresses of the CARs and finding their capabilities.  This process is called "candidate access router discovery" (CARD).  At the time of IP-layer handover, the CAR, whose capabilities are a good match to the preferences of the MN, is chosen as the target AR for handover.  The protocol described in this document allows a mobile node to perform CARD.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Liebsch¢kaffiliation`dnamehA. Singh¢kaffiliation`dnamejH. Chaskar¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Funato¢kaffiliation`dnamegE. Shimddatej—Bhfilenamegrfc4066eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgseamobydfullxWseamoby - Context Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host AlertingdnamexMContext Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host Alertingdtypebwgbidjdoc-126000hkeywordsÑkmobile nodebmndcarsmcandidate arskobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateBœ␀†granking‚crfcd4066irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Candidate Access Router Discovery (CARD)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6The Seamoby Candidate Access Router Discovery (CARD) protocol and the Context Transfer Protocol (CXTP) are experimental protocols designed to accelerate IP handover between wireless access routers.  These protocols require IANA allocations for ICMP type and options, Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Payload Protocol Identifiers, port numbers, and registries for certain formatted message options.  This document contains instructions to IANA about which allocations are required for the Seamoby protocols.  The ICMP subtype extension format for Seamoby has been additionally designed so that it can be utilized by other experimental mobility protocols, and the SCTP port number is also available for other experimental mobility protocols.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoDoCoMo Labs USAdnamehJ. Kempfddatej—Ahfilenamegrfc4065eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgseamobydfullxWseamoby - Context Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host AlertingdnamexMContext Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host Alertingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125999hkeywordsÖx!candidate access router discoverydcardxcontext transfer protocoldCXTPdICMPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBœ␀Bgranking·crfcd4065irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLInstructions for Seamoby and Experimental Mobility Protocol IANA AllocationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMobile IPv4 message types range from 0 to 255.  This document reserves a message type for use by an individual, company, or organization for experimental purposes, to evaluate enhancements to Mobile IPv4 messages before a formal standards proposal is issued. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehA. Patel¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehK. Leungddatej—_hfilenamegrfc4064eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-125998hkeywordsÇqinternet protocolmmessage typeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBíGôgranking‡crfcd4064irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAExperimental Message, Extensions, and Error Codes for Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document discusses the applicability of various tests for measuring single router control plane convergence, specifically in regard to the Open Shortest First (OSPF) protocol.  There are two general sections in this document, the first discusses advantages and limitations of specific OSPF convergence tests, and the second discusses more general pitfalls to be considered when routing protocol convergence is tested.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlSiNett Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. White¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs (Research)dnameiA. Shaikhddatej—^hfilenamegrfc4063eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-125997hkeywordsÇhspf timexadjacency formation timekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBnÕugrankingﬂcrfcd4063irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Considerations When Using Basic OSPF Convergence BenchmarksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document explains the terminology and concepts used in OSPF benchmarking.  Although some of these terms may be defined elsewhere (and we will refer the reader to those definitions in some cases) we include discussions concerning these terms, as they relate specifically to the tasks involved in benchmarking the OSPF protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlSiNett Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. White¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs (Research)dnameiA. Shaikhddatej—^hfilenamegrfc4062eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-125996hkeywordsÇhspf timexadjacency formation timekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateBnÕ.grankingﬁcrfcd4062irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*OSPF Benchmarking Terminology and ConceptsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document provides suggestions for measuring OSPF single router control plane convergence. Its initial emphasis is on the control plane of a single OSPF router. We do not address forwarding plane performance.

NOTE: In this document, the word "convergence" relates to single router control plane convergence only. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlSiNett Corp.dnameiV. Manral¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. White¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs (Research)dnameiA. Shaikhddatej—^hfilenamegrfc4061eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-125995hkeywordsÇhspf timexadjacency formation timekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBnÃÂgranking›crfcd4061irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Benchmarking Basic OSPF Single Router Control Plane ConvergencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThis document specifies RTP payload formats for encapsulating European Telecommunications Standards Institute (ETSI) European Standard ES 202 050 DSR Advanced Front-end (AFE), ES 202 211 DSR Extended Front-end (XFE), and ES 202 212 DSR Extended Advanced Front-end (XAFE) signal processing feature streams for distributed speech recognition (DSR) systems. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameiD. Pearceddatej—_hfilenamegrfc4060eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125994hkeywordsáxreal-time transport protocolcdsrxdistributed speeech recognitioncxferextended front-enddxafexextended advanced front-endkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB|	–granking‹crfcd4060irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexØRTP Payload Formats for European Telecommunications Standards Institute (ETSI) European Standard ES 202 050, ES 202 211, and ES 202 212 Distributed Speech Recognition EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‚This document describes a certificate extension to explicitly state the warranty offered by a Certificate Authority (CA) for the certificate containing the extension.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekSam Hartmandarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationfSPYRUSdnamenD. Linsenbardt¢kaffiliationfSPYRUSdnamejS. Pontius¢kaffiliationfSPYRUSdnamekA. Sturgeonddatej—9hfilenamegrfc4059eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125993hkeywordsÉucertificate authoritybcapinsurance policykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateB|	`granking€crfcd4059irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Warranty Certificate ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty5It is expected that future IP devices will have a variety of access technologies to gain network connectivity.  Currently there are access-specific mechanisms for providing client information to the network for authentication and authorization purposes.  In addition to being limited to specific access media (e.g., 802.1X for IEEE 802 links), some of these protocols are limited to specific network topologies (e.g., PPP for point-to-point links).  The goal of this document is to identify the requirements for a link-layer agnostic protocol that allows a host and a network to authenticate each other for network access.  This protocol will run between a client's device and an agent in the network where the agent might be a client of the AAA infrastructure.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkSamsung AITdnamehA. Yegin¢kaffiliationgToshibadnamegY. Ohba¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationgFlariondnamekG. Tsirtsis¢kaffiliationhARO/NCSUdnamegC. Wangddatej—_hfilenamegrfc4058eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpanadfullx>pana - Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdnamex7Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125992hkeywordsÇtnetwork connectivityxlink layer agnostic protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBïªgranking⁄crfcd4058irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKProtocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the scenarios for IPv6 deployment within enterprise networks.  It defines a small set of basic enterprise scenarios and includes pertinent questions to allow enterprise administrators to further refine their deployment scenarios.  Enterprise deployment requirements are discussed in terms of coexistence with IPv4 nodes, networks and applications, and in terms of basic network infrastructure requirements for IPv6 deployment.  The scenarios and requirements described in this document will be the basis for further analysis to determine what coexistence techniques and mechanisms are needed for enterprise IPv6 deployment.  The results of that analysis will be published in a separate document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoHewlett PackarddnamehJ. Boundddatej—>hfilenamegrfc4057eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125991hkeywordsÅxinternet protocol version 6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB§ﬂπgrankingŸcrfcd4057irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!IPv6 Enterprise Network ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¿This document specifies the conventions for using the RSASSA-PSS (RSA Probabilistic Signature Scheme) digital signature algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej—Uhfilenamegrfc4056eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125990hkeywordsÇx"RSA probabilistic signature schemeqdigital signaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB∑^grankingÿcrfcd4056irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOUse of the RSASSA-PSS Signature Algorithm in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document supplements RFC 3279.  It describes the conventions for using the RSA Probabilistic Signature Scheme (RSASSA-PSS) signature algorithm, the RSA Encryption Scheme - Optimal Asymmetric Encryption Padding (RSAES-OAEP) key transport algorithm and additional one-way hash functions with the Public-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) #1 version 1.5 signature algorithm in the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI).  Encoding formats, algorithm identifiers, and parameter formats are specified. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejB. Kaliski¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej—_hfilenamegrfc4055eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125989hkeywordsâeASN.1jRSASSA-PSSx"RSA probabilistic signature schemessignature algorithmjRSAES-OAEPx;RSA encryption scheme optimal asymmetric encryption paddingx!public-key cryptography standardsdPKCScpkikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB∑
granking◊crfcd4055irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex¨Additional Algorithms and Identifiers for RSA Cryptography for use in the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5756∂habstractyEOptical networking poses a number challenges for Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS).  Fundamentally, optical technology is an analog rather than digital technology whereby the optical layer is lowest in the transport hierarchy and hence has an intimate relationship with the physical geography of the network.  This contribution surveys some of the aspects of optical networks that impact routing and identifies possible GMPLS responses for each: (1) Constraints arising from the design of new software controllable network elements, (2) Constraints in a single all-optical domain without wavelength conversion, (3) Complications arising in more complex networks incorporating both all-optical and opaque architectures, and (4) Impacts of diversity constraints.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnameiJ. Strand¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegA. Chiuddatej—Ghfilenamegrfc4054eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipodfulluipo - IP over OpticaldnameoIP over Opticaldtypebwgbidjdoc-125988hkeywordsàidiversitygroutingnpath selectionjimparimentcasecpmduoptical control planeegmplskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÇÑgranking÷crfcd4054irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Impairments and Other Constraints on Optical Layer RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the procedure for proper handling of incoming liaison statements from other standards development organizations (SDOs), consortia, and industry fora, and for generating liaison statements to be transmitted from IETF to other SDOs, consortia and industry fora. This procedure allows IETF to effectively collaborate with other organizations in the international standards community.

The IETF expects that liaison statements might come from a variety of organizations, and it may choose to respond to many of those. The IETF is only obligated to respond if there is an agreed liaison relationship, however. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerBrian E. CarpentergauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamemS. Trowbridge¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Bakerddatej”?hfilenamegrfc4053eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125987hkeywordsÇcsdox"standards development organizationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBnÃïgranking’crfcd4053irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbergetotaletitlex?Procedures for Handling Liaison Statements to and from the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document discusses the procedures used by the IAB to establish and maintain liaison relationships between the IETF and other Standards Development Organizations (SDOs), consortia and industry fora.  This document also discusses the appointment and responsibilities of IETF liaison managers and representatives, and the expectations of the IAB for organizations with whom liaison relationships are established.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerBrian E. CarpentergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boardddatej”?hfilenamegrfc4052eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125986hkeywordsÉxinternet architecture boardcsdox"standards development organizationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateBnÃOgranking‘crfcd4052irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberfetotaletitlex:IAB Processes for Management of IETF Liaison RelationshipsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxﬁA number of Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) intended for use with XML Digital Signatures, Encryption, and Canonicalization are defined.  These URIs identify algorithms and types of keying information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej—`hfilenamegrfc4051eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125985hkeywordsÉrdigital signaturesjencryptionpcanonicalizationkobsoletedByÅd6931epagesopublicationDateB`granking”crfcd4051irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Additional XML Security Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty|This document specifies how to use Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) with XML Signatures.  The mechanism specified provides integrity, message authentication, and/or signer authentication services for data of any type, whether located within the XML that includes the signature or included by reference.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcBCIdnameoS. Blake-Wilson¢kaffiliationkCIO AustriadnamelG. Karlinger¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamelT. Kobayashi¢kaffiliationdUNCCdnamegY. Wangddatej—=hfilenamegrfc4050eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125984hkeywordsáx*elliptic curve digital signature algorithmeecdsaxelliptic curve cryptographycecccxmlrdigital signatureshxml dsigkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB`ügranking“crfcd4050irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexOUsing the Elliptic Curve Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) for XML Digital SignaturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThis document specifies a new ASN.1 type for representing time: BinaryTime.  This document also specifies an alternate to the signing-time attribute for use with the Cryptographic Message Syntax(CMS) SignedData and AuthenticatedData content types; the binary-signing-time attribute uses BinaryTime.  CMS and the signing-time attribute are defined in RFC 3852.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej—<hfilenamegrfc4049eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125983hkeywordsÖvsigning-time attributexcryptographic message syntaxccmsjSignedDataqAuthenticatedDatakobsoletedByÅd6019epagesopublicationDateB`Ègranking—crfcd4049irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGBinaryTime: An Alternate Format for Representing Date and Time in ASN.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document recommends that RFC 1888, on Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) Network Service Access Points (NSAPs) and IPv6, be reclassified as Historic, as most of it has no further value, apart from one section, which is faulty.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelB. Carpenterddatej—`hfilenamegrfc4048eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125982hkeywordsÖhInternethProtocoldOpengSystemsoInterconnectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB`‰granking–crfcd4048irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletRFC 1888 Is ObsoletedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4548∂habstracty·This document describes the registration of the Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) sub-types to be used by the international astronomical community for the interchange of Flexible Image Transport System (FITS) files.  The encoding is defined by the published FITS standard documents.  The FITS format has been in use since 1979, and almost all data from astronomical observations are interchanged by using FITS.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtUCO/Lick ObservatorydnamehS. Allen¢kaffiliationx$National Radio Astronomy ObservatorydnamehD. Wellsddatej—`hfilenamegrfc4047eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125981hkeywordsÇx%multipurpose internet mail extensionsxastronomical observationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBf·grankingœcrfcd4047irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFMIME Sub-type Registrations for Flexible Image Transport System (FITS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThis document defines the common architecture for Multicast Security (MSEC) key management protocols to support a variety of application, transport, and network layer security protocols.  It also defines the group security association (GSA), and describes the key management protocols that help establish a GSA.  The framework and guidelines described in this document permit a modular and flexible design of group key management protocols for a variety of different settings that are specialized to applications needs.  MSEC key management protocols may be used to facilitate secure one-to-many, many-to-many, or one-to-one communication.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejM. Baugher¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejR. Canetti¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejL. Dondeti¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekF. Lindholmddatej—:hfilenamegrfc4046eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125980hkeywordsÇxgroup security associationcgsakobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDateBvn˝grankingŒcrfcd4046irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Multicast Security (MSEC) Group Key Management ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThe Layer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) provides a method for tunneling PPP packets.  This document describes an extension to L2TP, to make efficient use of L2TP tunnels within the context of deploying multicast services whose data will have to be conveyed by these tunnels.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnamejG. Bourdonddatej—Qhfilenamegrfc4045eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125979hkeywordsÇcpppwpoint-to-point protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBf‡∫grankingÕcrfcd4045irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbExtensions to Support Efficient Carrying of Multicast Traffic in Layer-2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˆThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for information related to the Fibre Channel. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrCisco Systems, IncdnamemK. McCloghrieddatej”Mhfilenamegrfc4044eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125978hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basekfc-mgmt-mibkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDateBâTgrankingÃcrfcd4044irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFibre Channel Management MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document defines a new form of name, called permanent identifier, that may be included in the subjectAltName extension of a public key certificate issued to an entity.

The permanent identifier is an optional feature that may be used by a CA to indicate that two or more certificates relate to the same entity, even if they contain different subject name (DNs) or different names in the subjectAltName extension, or if the name or the affiliation of that entity stored in the subject or another name form in the subjectAltName extension has changed.

The subject name, carried in the subject field, is only unique for each subject entity certified by the one CA as defined by the issuer name field. However, the new name form can carry a name that is unique for each subject entity certified by a CA. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdBulldnameiD. Pinkas¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Gindinddatej—Ihfilenamegrfc4043eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125977hkeywordsÇxsubjectAltName extensionbdnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBíG:grankingÀcrfcd4043irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Permanent IdentifierdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyõISO-10646 defines a large character set called the Universal Character Set (UCS), which encompasses most of the world's writing systems. The same set of codepoints is defined by Unicode, which further defines additional character properties and other implementation details. By policy of the relevant standardization committees, changes to Unicode and amendments and additions to ISO/IEC 10646 track each other, so that the character repertoires and code point assignments remain in synchronization.

The current representation formats for Unicode (UTF-7, UTF-8, UTF-16) are not storage and computation efficient on platforms that utilize the 9 bit nonet as a natural storage unit instead of the 8 bit octet.

This document describes a transformation format of Unicode that takes advantage of the nonet so that the format will be storage and computation efficient. This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqPanda ProgrammingdnamejM. Crispinddatej”ihfilenamegrfc4042eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43442hkeywordsáwuniversal character setcucsjcodeopintsgunicodeeutf-7eutf-8futf-16kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateBMÅ.granking crfcd4042irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex<UTF-9 and UTF-18 Efficient Transformation Formats of UnicodedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyKIt has often been the case that morality has not been given proper consideration in the design and specification of protocols produced within the Routing Area. This has led to a decline in the moral values within the Internet and attempts to retrofit a suitable moral code to implemented and deployed protocols has been shown to be sub-optimal.

This document specifies a requirement for all new Routing Area Internet-Drafts to include a "Morality Considerations" section, and gives guidance on what that section should contain. This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej”hhfilenamegrfc4041eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125976hkeywordsÇlmoral valuesjmoral codekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBMÄ∆granking…crfcd4041irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9Requirements for Morality Sections in Routing Area DraftsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes how to carry 64 kbit/s channel data transparently in RTP packets, using a pseudo-codec called "Clearmode". It also serves as registration for a related MIME type called "audio/clearmode".

"Clearmode" is a basic feature of VoIP Media Gateways. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjSiemens AGdnamejR. Kreuterddatej—Mhfilenamegrfc4040eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125975hkeywordsÅxrealtime transport protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBf‡1granking»crfcd4040irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3RTP Payload Format for a 64 kbit/s Transparent CalldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This document defines a new Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol version 4 (DHCPv4) option, modeled on the DHCPv6 Rapid Commit option, for obtaining IP address and configuration information using a 2-message exchange rather than the usual 4-message exchange, expediting client configuration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsSAMSUNG ElectronicsdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationsSAMSUNG ElectronicsdnamefP. Kim¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegB. Volzddatej»¥hfilenamegrfc4039eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125974hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateBJ$granking«crfcd4039irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRRapid Commit Option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol version 4 (DHCPv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬁAs IPv6 networks are deployed and the network transition is discussed, one should also consider how to enable IPv6 support in applications running on IPv6 hosts, and the best strategy to develop IP protocol support in applications.  This document specifies scenarios and aspects of application transition.  It also proposes guidelines on how to develop IP version-independent applications during the transition period.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiETRI/NISTdnameiM-K. Shin¢kaffiliationdETRIdnameiY-G. Hong¢kaffiliationcIIJdnameiJ. Hagino¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationiGSYC/URJCdnamelE. M. Castroddatej»åhfilenamegrfc4038eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125973hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateB1Ì‰granking∆crfcd4038irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Application Aspects of IPv6 TransitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThis document specifies the core of the Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) Callout Protocol (OCP).  OCP marshals application messages from other communication protocols: An OPES intermediary sends original application messages to a callout server; the callout server sends adapted application messages back to the processor.  OCP is designed with typical adaptation tasks in mind (e.g., virus and spam management, language and format translation, message anonymization, or advertisement manipulation).  As defined in this document, the OCP Core consists of application-agnostic mechanisms essential for efficient support of typical adaptations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwThe Measurement FactorydnamekA. Rousskovddatej—Khfilenamegrfc4037eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125972hkeywordsÅncallout serverkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateB1Ìãgranking≈crfcd4037irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) Callout Protocol (OCP) CoredtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines a set of managed objects for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)-based management of Data-over-Cable Service Interface Specification (DOCSIS)-compliant Cable Modem Termination Systems.  These managed objects facilitate protection of the cable network from misuse by subscribers.  The Differentiated Services MIB (RFC 3289) provides the filtering functions needed here, making use of classification items defined in this specification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiW. Sawyerddatej—ahfilenamegrfc4036eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipcdndfullx"ipcdn - IP over Cable Data NetworkdnamexIP over Cable Data Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-125971hkeywordsÉcmibdsnmpx"simple network management protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB`
√grankingƒcrfcd4036irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexíManagement Information Base for Data Over Cable Service Interface Specification (DOCSIS) Cable Modem Termination Systems for Subscriber ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstracty™This document is part of a family of documents that describe the DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC). The DNS Security Extensions are a collection of new resource records and protocol modifications that add data origin authentication and data integrity to the DNS. This document describes the DNSSEC protocol modifications. This document defines the concept of a signed zone, along with the requirements for serving and resolving by using DNSSEC. These techniques allow a security-aware resolver to authenticate both DNS resource records and authoritative DNS error indications.

This document obsoletes RFC 2535 and incorporates changes from all updates to RFC 2535. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtTelematica InstituutdnameiR. Arends¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnameiM. Larson¢kaffiliationxColorado State UniversitydnameiD. Massey¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. Roseddatej—\hfilenamegrfc4035eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125970hkeywordsÖrdomain name systemnauthenticationporigin integrityfdnssecx&domain name system security extensionskobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateBJ#¥granking√crfcd4035irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Protocol Modifications for the DNS Security ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByád8198d6840d6014d9520d4470d9824d9077∂habstractyÇThis document is part of a family of documents that describe the DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC). The DNS Security Extensions are a collection of resource records and protocol modifications that provide source authentication for the DNS. This document defines the public key (DNSKEY), delegation signer (DS), resource record digital signature (RRSIG), and authenticated denial of existence (NSEC) resource records. The purpose and format of each resource record is described in detail, and an example of each resource record is given.

This document obsoletes RFC 2535 and incorporates changes from all updates to RFC 2535. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtTelematica InstituutdnameiR. Arends¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnameiM. Larson¢kaffiliationxColorado State UniversitydnameiD. Massey¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. Roseddatej—]hfilenamegrfc4034eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125969hkeywordsÖrdomain name systemnauthenticationporigin integrityfdnssecx&domain name system security extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBJ#Vgranking¬crfcd4034irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Resource Records for the DNS Security ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByád6840d6014d6944d9905d4470d9824d9077∂habstracty¡The Domain Name System Security Extensions (DNSSEC) add data origin authentication and data integrity to the Domain Name System.  This document introduces these extensions and describes their capabilities and limitations.  This document also discusses the services that the DNS security extensions do and do not provide.  Last, this document describes the interrelationships between the documents that collectively describe DNSSEC. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtTelematica InstituutdnameiR. Arends¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnameiM. Larson¢kaffiliationxColorado State UniversitydnameiD. Massey¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. Roseddatej—Nhfilenamegrfc4033eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125968hkeywordsÖrdomain name systemnauthenticationporigin integrityfdnssecx&domain name system security extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBJ"ıgranking¡crfcd4033irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*DNS Security Introduction and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6014d6840∂habstractx˚This document updates RFC 3312, which defines the framework for preconditions in SIP.  We provide guidelines for authors of new precondition types and describe how to use SIP preconditions in situations that involve session mobility. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Kyzivatddatej—Qhfilenamegrfc4032eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125967hkeywordsÉcqosrquality of servicelpreconditionkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateB1Ï¸granking¿crfcd4032irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGUpdate to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Preconditions FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document provides requirements for Layer 3 Virtual Private Networks (L3VPNs).  It identifies requirements applicable to a number of individual approaches that a Service Provider may use to provision a Virtual Private Network (VPN) service.  This document expresses a service provider perspective, based upon past experience with IP-based service offerings and the ever-evolving needs of the customers of such services.  Toward this end, it first defines terminology and states general requirements.  Detailed requirements are expressed from a customer perspective as well as that of a service provider.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Carugi¢kaffiliationcMCIdnamejD. McDysanddatej—Dhfilenamegrfc4031eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-125966hkeywordsÉel3vpnpservice providercvpnkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateB`
grankingøcrfcd4031irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWService Requirements for Layer 3 Provider Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Relay Agent Information Option (RFC 3046) conveys information between a DHCP Relay Agent and a DHCP server.  This specification defines an authentication suboption for that option, containing a keyed hash in its payload.  The suboption supports data integrity and replay protection for relayed DHCP messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemonddatej»õhfilenamegrfc4030eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125965hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBMΩÚgrankingæcrfcd4030irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbThe Authentication Suboption for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Relay Agent OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThis document describes different scenarios for the introduction of IPv6 into an ISP's existing IPv4 network without disrupting the IPv4 service.  The scenarios for introducing IPv6 are analyzed, and the relevance of already defined transition mechanisms are evaluated.  Known challenges are also identified.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamegM. Lind¢kaffiliationuThales CommunicationsdnamejV. Ksinant¢kaffiliationsSAMSUNG ElectronicsdnamegS. Park¢kaffiliationnFrance TelecomdnameiA. Baudot¢kaffiliationiCSC/FunetdnameiP. Savoladdatej—Ehfilenamegrfc4029eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125964hkeywordsÇxinternet service providerqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB;ÉWgrankingΩcrfcd4029irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Scenarios and Analysis for Introducing IPv6 into ISP NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÂThis document defines an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  This extension allows for a periodic refresh of SIP sessions through a \%re-INVITE or UPDATE request.  The refresh allows both user agents and proxies to determine whether the SIP session is still active.  The extension defines two new header fields: \%Session-Expires, which conveys the lifetime of the session, and \%Min-SE, which conveys the minimum allowed value for the session timer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Donovan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”hhfilenamegrfc4028eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125963hkeywordsÑqre-invite requestnupdate requestosession-expiresfmin-sekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBvnIgrankingºcrfcd4028irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Session Timers in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThis document registers the media types application/dns and text/dns in accordance with RFC 2048.  The application/dns media type is used to identify data on the detached Domain Name System (DNS) format described in RFC 2540.  The text/dns media type is used to identify master files as described in RFC 1035.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. Josefssonddatej—Ihfilenamegrfc4027eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125962hkeywordsÉjmedia typeoapplication/dnshtext/dnskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBvmgrankingªcrfcd4027irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDomain Name System Media TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≠The widespread interest in provider-provisioned Virtual Private Network (VPN) solutions lead to memos proposing different and overlapping solutions. The IETF working groups (first Provider Provisioned VPNs and later Layer 2 VPNs and Layer 3 VPNs) have discussed these proposals and documented specifications. This has lead to the development of a partially new set of concepts used to describe the set of VPN services.

To a certain extent, more than one term covers the same concept, and sometimes the same term covers more than one concept. This document seeks to make the terminology in the area clearer and more intuitive. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationhAcreo ABdnameiT. Madsenddatej—Ahfilenamegrfc4026eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-125961hkeywordsÇel3vpnel2vpnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB;Égranking∫crfcd4026irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Provider Provisioned Virtual Private Network (VPN) TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This document describes a new resource record for the Domain Name System (DNS). This record may be used to store public keys for use in IP security (IPsec) systems. The record also includes provisions for indicating what system should be contacted when an IPsec tunnel is established with the entity in question.

This record replaces the functionality of the sub-type #4 of the KEY Resource Record, which has been obsoleted by RFC 3445. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcSSWdnamemM. Richardsonddatej»òhfilenamegrfc4025eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhipseckeydfullx3ipseckey - IPSEC KEYing information resource recorddnamex(IPSEC KEYing information resource recorddtypebwgbidjdoc-125960hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB1Ïñgrankingπcrfcd4025irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1A Method for Storing IPsec Keying Material in DNSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‹This document defines the expected behaviour of a client to various aspects of a Voice Profile for Internet Mail (VPIM) message or any voice and/or fax message.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsons¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Maruszakddatej—>hfilenamegrfc4024eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125959hkeywordsÖdvpimgprofileminternet mailxvoice profile for internet mailkfax messagekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateB’édgranking∏crfcd4024irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Voice Messaging Client BehaviourdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘Various applications of MPLS make use of label stacks with multiple entries.  In some cases, it is possible to replace the top label of the stack with an IP-based encapsulation, thereby enabling the application to run over networks that do not have MPLS enabled in their core routers.  This document specifies two IP-based encapsulations: MPLS-in-IP and MPLS-in-GRE (Generic Routing Encapsulation).  Each of these is applicable in some circumstances. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnMotorola, Inc.dnamejT. Worster¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehE. Rosenddatej»óhfilenamegrfc4023eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125958hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB.í?granking∑crfcd4023irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Encapsulating MPLS in IP or Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5332∂habstractyZThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for implementations of the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) in an IP version independent manner.  This memo obsoletes RFCs 2452 and 2012. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameoR. Raghunarayanddatej—Uhfilenamegrfc4022eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125957hkeywordsÑgMIB-TCPcTCPx"Simple Network Management ProtocolcMIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB7Ògranking∂crfcd4022irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGManagement Information Base for the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÜThis document defines the initial IANA registration for permanent mail and MIME message header fields, per RFC 3864. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx