S-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkEricsson ABdnamelG. Pelletierddatej—Lhfilenamegrfc4019eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125954hkeywordsÜcrtphudp-litebipxreal-time transport protocolxuser datagram protocol liteqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB`Ïgranking≥crfcd4019irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRObust Header Compression (ROHC): Profiles for User Datagram Protocol (UDP) LitedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4815∂habstracty5The iSCSI protocol provides a way for hosts to access SCSI devices over an IP network.  This document defines the use of the Service Location Protocol (SLP) by iSCSI hosts, devices, and management services, along with the SLP service type templates that describe the services they provide. [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Bakke¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejJ. Hufferd¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelK. Voruganti¢kaffiliationbHPdnamejM. Krueger¢kaffiliationgAdaptecdnameiT. Sperryddatej—Bhfilenamegrfc4018eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125953hkeywordsÇdscsicslpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBvn£granking≤crfcd4018irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexáFinding Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) Targets and Name Servers by Using Service Location Protocol version 2 (SLPv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstracty¥The IEEE 802.11i MAC Security Enhancements Amendment makes use of IEEE 802.1X, which in turn relies on the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP).  This document defines requirements for EAP methods used in IEEE 802.11 wireless LAN deployments.  The material in this document has been approved by IEEE 802.11 and is being presented as an IETF RFC for informational purposes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmAgere SystemsdnamejD. Stanley¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnameiJ. Walker¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Abobaddatej—<hfilenamegrfc4017eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125952hkeywordsÇkIEEE 802.11lwireless lankobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBD±lgranking±crfcd4017irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNExtensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Method Requirements for Wireless LANsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyãThis document discusses the threats to protocols used to carry authentication for network access.  The security requirements arising from these threats will be used as additional input to the Protocol for Carrying Authentication for Network Access (PANA) Working Group for designing the IP based network access authentication protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamepM. Parthasarathyddatej—Ahfilenamegrfc4016eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpanadfullx>pana - Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdnamex7Protocol for carrying Authentication for Network Accessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125951hkeywordsÇnauthenticationnnetwork accesskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB;ÇWgranking∞crfcd4016irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhProtocol for Carrying Authentication and Network Access (PANA) Threat Analysis and Security RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎBased on an appropriate detection algorithm, the Eifel response algorithm provides a way for a TCP sender to respond to a detected spurious timeout.  It adapts the retransmission timer to avoid further spurious timeouts and (depending on the detection algorithm) can avoid the often unnecessary go-back-N retransmits that would otherwise be sent.  In addition, the Eifel response algorithm restores the congestion control state in such a way that packet bursts are avoided. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnameiR. Ludwig¢kaffiliationdHIITdnameiA. Gurtovddatej—Jhfilenamegrfc4015eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125950hkeywordsÅxtransmision control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBàÎgrankingØcrfcd4015irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$The Eifel Response Algorithm for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyóThe RADIUS Attributes suboption enables a network element to pass identification and authorization attributes received during RADIUS authentication to a DHCP server.  When the DHCP server receives a message from a relay agent containing a RADIUS Attributes suboption, it extracts the contents of the suboption and uses that information in selecting configuration parameters for the client. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemJ. Schnizleinddatej»ïhfilenamegrfc4014eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host 