SEED is added to the set of optional symmetric encryption algorithms in CMS by providing two classes of unique object identifiers (OIDs). One OID class defines the content encryption algorithms and the other defines the key encryption algorithms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamegJ. Park¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefS. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefJ. Kim¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefJ. Leeddatej—Khfilenamegrfc4010eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125945hkeywordsÇesmimex,secure/multipurpose internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBÆRgranking™crfcd4010irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJUse of the SEED Encryption Algorithm in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]This document describes the SEED encryption algorithm, which has been adopted by most of the security systems in the Republic of Korea.  Included are a description of the cipher and the key scheduling algorithm (Section 2), the S-boxes (Appendix A), and a set of test vectors (Appendix B).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamegJ. Park¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefS. Lee¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefJ. Kim¢kaffiliationdKISAdnamefJ. Leeddatej—:hfilenamegrfc4009eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125944hkeywordsÉtencryption algorithmhseed cbchseed oidkobsoletedByÅd4269epagesopublicationDateB≠·granking©crfcd4009irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe SEED Encryption Algorithmdtype