affiliationjSiemens AGdnamekR. Brandner¢kaffiliationxSiemens Roke Manor ResearchdnameiL. Conroy¢kaffiliationeOefegdnamejR. Stastnyddatej—Lhfilenamegrfc4002eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125937hkeywordsÉkURI schemesxuniform resource identifierdenumkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateB8•granking¢crfcd4002irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0IANA Registration for Enumservice 'web' and 'ft'dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractyThis MIB module defines textual conventions to represent commonly used Internet network layer addressing information.  The intent is that these textual conventions will be imported and used in MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsSyAM Software, Inc.dnamejM. Daniele¢kaffiliationxJohns Hopkins UniversitydnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationxWind River Systems, Inc.dnamekS. Routhier¢kaffiliationxInternational University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej—Hhfilenamegrfc4001eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125936hkeywordsÉcMIBxmanagement information basex-internet network layer addressing informationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB$∏õgranking°crfcd4001irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Textual Conventions for Internet Network AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document specifies the following 16 additional OPTIONAL system administration operations for use with the Internet Printing Protocol/1.1 (IPP), plus a few associated attributes, values, and status codes, and using the IPP Printer object to manage printer fan-out and fan-in. (Printer operations: Enable-Printer and Disable-Printer, Pause-Printer-After-Current-Job, Hold-New-Jobs and Release-Held-New-Jobs, Deactivate-Printer and Activate-Printer, Restart-Printer, Shutdown-Printer and Startup-Printer.  Job operations: Reprocess-Job, Cancel-Current-Job, Suspend-Current-Job, Resume-Job, Promote-Job, Schedule-Job-After.) [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiC. Kugler¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehH. Lewis¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnamekT. Hastingsddatej—Mhfilenamegrfc3998eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcippdfullx ipp - Internet Printing ProtocoldnamexInternet Printing Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125935hkeywordsçx system administration operationsx"Enable-Printer and Disable-PrinterxPause-Printer-After-Current-Jobx'Hold-New-Jobs and Release-Held-New-Jobsx'Deactivate-Printer and Activate-PrinteroRestart-Printerx$Shutdown-Printer and Startup-PrintermReprocess-JobrCancel-Current-JobsSuspend-Current-JobjResume-JobkPromote-JobrSchedule-Job-AfterkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateB@∑øgrankingûcrfcd3998irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKInternet Printing Protocol (IPP): Job and Printer Administrative OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document is one of a set of documents that together describe all aspects of the Internet Printing Protocol (IPP).  IPP is an application-level protocol that can be used for distributed printing on the Internet.  There are multiple parts to IPP, but the primary architectural components are the Model, the Protocol, and an interface to Directory Services.  This document provides a statement of the requirements for notifications as an optional part of an IPP Service.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnamekT. Hastings¢kaffiliationxUtah Valley State CollegednamekR. K. deBry¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehH. Lewisddatej—<hfilenamegrfc3997eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcippdfullx ipp - Internet Printing ProtocoldnamexInternet Printing Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125934hkeywordsÉemodelrdirectory servicesxnotification requirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB@∑Rgrankingùcrfcd3997irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDInternet Printing Protocol (IPP): Requirements for IPP NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document describes an extension to the Internet Printing Protocol1.1: Model and Semantics (RFC 2911, RFC 2910).  This document specifies the 'ippget' Pull Delivery Method for use with the "Internet Printing Protocol (IPP): Event Notifications and Subscriptions" specification (RFC 3995).  This IPPGET Delivery Method is REQUIRED for all clients and Printers that support RFC 3995.  The Notification Recipient, acting as a client, fetches (pulls) Event Notifications by using the Get-Notifications operation defined in this document. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxGlobal Workflow SolutionsdnamejR. Herriot¢kaffiliationkXerox Corp.dnamekT. Hastings¢kaffiliationiIBM Corp.dnamehH. Lewisddatej—Rhfilenamegrfc3996eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcippdfullx ipp - Internet Printing ProtocoldnamexInternet Printing Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125933hkeywordsÉtpull delivery methodsevent notificationssevent subscriptionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB@∂Ògrankingúcrfcd3996irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVInternet Printing Protocol (IPP): The 'ippget' Delivery Method for Event NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This document describes an OPTIONAL extension to the Internet Printing Protocol/1.1: Model and Semantics (RFC 2911, RFC 2910). This extension allows a client to subscribe to printing related Events. Subscriptions are modeled as Subscription Objects. The Subscription Object specifies that when one of the specified Events occurs, the Printer delivers an asynchronous Event Notification to the specified Notification Recipient via the specified Push or Pull Delivery Method (i.e., protocol).

A client associates Subscription Objects with a particular Job by performing the Create-Job-Subscriptions operation or by submitting a Job with subscription information. A client associates Subscription Objects with the Printer by performing a Create-Printer-Subscriptions operation. Four other operations are defined for Subscription Objects: Get-Subscriptions-Attributes, Get-Subscriptions, Renew-Subscription, and Cancel-Subscription. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxGlobal Workflow SolutionsdnamejR. Herriot¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnamekT. Hastingsddatej—Shfilenamegrfc3995eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcippdfullx ipp - Internet Printing ProtocoldnamexInternet Printing Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125932hkeywordsÑhoptionalssubscription eventstsubscription objectsxasynchronous even notificationkobsoletedByÄepages_opublicationDateB@∂xgrankingõcrfcd3995irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGInternet Printing Protocol (IPP): Event Notifications and SubscriptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,In instant messaging (IM) systems, it is useful to know during an IM conversation whether the other party is composing a message; e.g., typing or recording an audio message.  This document defines a new status message content type and XML namespace that conveys information about a message being composed.  The status message can indicate the composition of a message of any type, including text, voice, or video.  The status messages are delivered to the instant messaging recipient in the same manner as the instant messages themselves. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej—Ihfilenamegrfc3994eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125931hkeywordsÑbimxstatus message content typecxmlxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB;∏grankingöcrfcd3994irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Indication of Message Composition for Instant MessagingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis memo defines a new Subscriber-ID suboption for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol's (DHCP) relay agent information option.  The suboption allows a DHCP relay agent to associate a stable "Subscriber-ID" with DHCP client messages in a way that is independent of the client and of the underlying physical network infrastructure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Johnson¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenT. Palaniappan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Stappddatej»õhfilenamegrfc3993eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125930hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB.ë∏grankingôcrfcd3993irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]Subscriber-ID Suboption for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Relay Agent OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyA Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) endpoint that has encountered an adverse failure condition (such as being involved in a transient call when a Call Agent failover occurred) could be left in a lockstep state whereby events are quarantined but not notified.  The MGCP package described in this document provides a mechanism for reporting these situations so that the new Call Agent can take the necessary fault recovery procedures.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Foster¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej—?hfilenamegrfc3992eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125929hkeywordsÅnfault recoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBáögrankingòcrfcd3992irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHMedia Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) Lockstep State Reporting MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty€The base Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) specification (RFC 3435) allows endpoints to be redirected one endpoint at a time.  This document provides extensions in the form of a new MGCP package that provides mechanisms for redirecting and resetting a group of endpoints.  It also includes the ability to more accurately redirect endpoints by allowing a list of Call Agents to be specified in a preferred order.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Foster¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej—?hfilenamegrfc3991eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125928hkeywordsÑevoicebIPhinternetdVoIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateBà3grankingócrfcd3991irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex@Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) Redirect and Reset PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≠This document describes the Configuration and Provisioning for Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) problem statement.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiAirespacednameiB. O'Hara¢kaffiliationiAirespacednamejP. Calhoun¢kaffiliationoDocomo Labs USAdnamehJ. Kempfddatej»ãhfilenamegrfc3990eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcapwapdfullx;capwap - Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdnamex2Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Pointsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125927hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB:grankingñcrfcd3990irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTConfiguration and Provisioning for Wireless Access Points (CAPWAP) Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybThis memo specifies semantics for a Middlebox Communication (MIDCOM) protocol to be used by MIDCOM agents for interacting with middleboxes such as firewalls and Network Address Translators (NATs).  The semantics discussion does not include any specification of a concrete syntax or a transport protocol.  However, a concrete protocol is expected to implement the specified semantics or, more likely, a superset of it.  The MIDCOM protocol semantics is derived from the MIDCOM requirements, from the MIDCOM framework, and from working group decisions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqMagnus Westerlunddarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationfNorteldnameiT. Taylorddatej—Ehfilenamegrfc3989eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmidcomdfullx midcom - Middlebox CommunicationdnamewMiddlebox Communicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125926hkeywordsÉcnatxnetwork address translatorhfirewallkobsoletedByÅd5189epagesFopublicationDateBàçgrankingïcrfcd3989irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Middlebox Communications (MIDCOM) Protocol SemanticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôProper functioning of RFC 1191 path Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) discovery requires that IP routers have knowledge of the MTU for each link to which they are connected.  As currently specified, the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) does not have the ability to signal the MTU for a Label Switched Path (LSP) to the ingress Label Switching Router (LSR).  In the absence of this functionality, the MTU for each LSP must be statically configured by network operators or by equivalent off-line mechanisms.  This document specifies experimental extensions to LDP in support of LSP MTU discovery.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoLayer8 NetworksdnamehB. Black¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompelladdatej—Chfilenamegrfc3988eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125925hkeywordsÜcmtucldpclspslabel switched pathvlabel switching routerclsrkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateB;mgrankingîcrfcd3988irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSMaximum Transmission Unit Signalling Extensions for the Label Distribution ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÁThis document defines a new protocol element, the Internationalized Resource Identifier (IRI), as a complement of the Uniform Resource Identifier (URI). An IRI is a sequence of characters from the Universal Character Set (Unicode/ISO 10646). A mapping from IRIs to URIs is defined, which means that IRIs can be used instead of URIs, where appropriate, to identify resources.

The approach of defining a new protocol element was chosen instead of extending or changing the definition of URIs. This was done in order to allow a clear distinction and to avoid incompatibilities with existing software. Guidelines are provided for the use and deployment of IRIs in various protocols, formats, and software components that currently deal with URIs.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcW3CdnameiM. Duerst¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamekM. Suignardddatej”Ehfilenamegrfc3987eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125924hkeywordsÉcurixuniform resource identifierwUniversal Character SetkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateAˆÙigrankingìcrfcd3987irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Internationalized Resource Identifiers (IRIs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty”A Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) is a compact sequence of characters that identifies an abstract or physical resource.  This specification defines the generic URI syntax and a process for resolving URI references that might be in relative form, along with guidelines and security considerations for the use of URIs on the Internet.  The URI syntax defines a grammar that is a superset of all valid URIs, allowing an implementation to parse the common components of a URI reference without knowing the scheme-specific requirements of every possible identifier.  This specification does not define a generative grammar for URIs; that task is performed by the individual specifications of each URI scheme. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgW3C/MITdnamenT. Berners-Lee¢kaffiliationlDay SoftwarednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationmAdobe SystemsdnamekL. Masinterddatej—Uhfilenamegrfc3986eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125923hkeywordsÜqInternet protocolbIPxuniform resource identifiercURIcwwwnworld wide webkobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateAˆÛ{grankingícrfcd3986irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberBetotaletitlex1Uniform Resource Identifier (URI): Generic SyntaxdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8820d7320∂habstractyøThis document describes an architecture for Pseudo Wire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3).  It discusses the emulation of services such as Frame Relay, ATM, Ethernet, TDM, and SONET/SDH over packet switched networks (PSNs) using IP or MPLS.  It presents the architectural framework for pseudo wires (PWs), defines terminology, and specifies the various protocol elements and their functions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiS. Bryant¢kaffiliationwOverture Networks, Inc.dnamegP. Pateddatej»íhfilenamegrfc3985eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-125922hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDateB.ë%grankingëcrfcd3985irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Pseudo Wire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5462∂habstracty3This memo describes an RTP Payload format for the ITU-T Recommendation H.264 video codec and the technically identical ISO/IEC International Standard 14496-10 video codec.  The RTP payload format allows for packetization of one or more Network Abstraction Layer Units (NALUs), produced by an H.264 video encoder, in each RTP payload.  The payload format has wide applicability, as it supports applications from simple low bit-rate conversational usage, to Internet video streaming with interleaved transmission, to high bit-rate video-on-demand. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Wenger¢kaffiliation`dnameoM.M. Hannuksela¢kaffiliation`dnamenT. Stockhammer¢kaffiliation`dnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Singerddatej—bhfilenamegrfc3984eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125921hkeywordsÇxITU-T Recommendation H.264x'ISO/IEC International Standard 14496-10kobsoletedByÅd6184epagesSopublicationDateB¥grankingêcrfcd3984irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"RTP Payload Format for H.264 VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¿This document specifies how to use the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP) as the application transport substrate for the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationhDENIC eGdnamegM. Sanzddatej…‚hfilenamegrfc3983eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125920hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA›·€grankingècrfcd3983irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjUsing the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS) over the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyFThis document describes an Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS) registry schema for registered DNS information.  The schema extends the necessary query and result operations of IRIS to provide the functional information service needs for syntaxes and results used by domain registries and registrars. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationhDENIC eGdnamegM. Sanzddatej…‰hfilenamegrfc3982eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125919hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateA›·ógrankingécrfcd3982irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYIRIS:  A Domain Registry (dreg) Type for the Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document describes an application layer client-server protocol for a framework to represent the query and result operations of the information services of Internet registries.  Specified in the Extensible Markup Language (XML), the protocol defines generic query and result operations and a mechanism for extending these operations for specific registry service needs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newton¢kaffiliationhDENIC eGdnamegM. Sanzddatej…‰hfilenamegrfc3981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125918hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDateA›‡–grankingçcrfcd3981irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDIRIS: The Internet Registry Information Service (IRIS) Core ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4992∂habstractyÂInternet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) is a SCSI transport protocol that maps the SCSI family of protocols onto TCP/IP.  This document defines an additional iSCSI node name type format to enable use of the "Network Address Authority" (NAA) worldwide naming format defined by the InterNational Committee for Information Technology Standards (INCITS) T11 - Fibre Channel (FC) protocols and used by Serial Attached SCSI (SAS).  This document updates RFC 3720. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuHewlett-Packard Corp.dnamejM. Krueger¢kaffiliationuHewlett-Packard Corp.dnamenM. Chadalapaka¢kaffiliationuHewlett-Packard Corp.dnamejR. Elliottddatej—Lhfilenamegrfc3980eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125917hkeywordsÇx)internet small computer systems interfacewscsi transport protocolkobsoletedByÅd7143epagesopublicationDateBÌgrankingåcrfcd3980irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFT11 Network Address Authority (NAA) Naming Format for iSCSI Node NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyµThe IETF policies about Intellectual Property Rights (IPR), such as patent rights, relative to technologies developed in the IETF are designed to ensure that IETF working groups and participants have as much information about any IPR constraints on a technical proposal as possible.  The policies are also intended to benefit the Internet community and the public at large, while respecting the legitimate rights of IPR holders.  This memo details the IETF policies concerning IPR related to technology worked on within the IETF.  It also describes the objectives that the policies are designed to meet.  This memo updates RFC 2026 and, with RFC 3978, replaces Section 10 of RFC 2026.  This memo also updates paragraph 4 of Section 3.2 of RFC 2028, for all purposes, including reference [2] in RFC 2418.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej”Jhfilenamegrfc3979eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbididoc-43338hkeywordsÉcIPRxIntellectual Property RightsicopyrightkobsoletedByÅd8179epagesopublicationDateB%!ngrankingãcrfcd3979irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Intellectual Property Rights in IETF TechnologydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4879∂habstractycThe IETF policies about rights in Contributions to the IETF are designed to ensure that such Contributions can be made available to the IETF and Internet communities while permitting the authors to retain as many rights as possible.  This memo details the IETF policies on rights in Contributions to the IETF.  It also describes the objectives that the policies are designed to meet.  This memo updates RFC 2026, and, with RFC 3979, replaces Section 10 of RFC 2026.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej—Ahfilenamegrfc3978eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125916hkeywordsÉxIntellectual Property RightscIPRicopyrightkobsoletedByÅd5378epagesopublicationDateB% ˙grankingäcrfcd3978irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIETF Rights in ContributionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4748∂habstracty™The Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP) has been in use in the Internet for a decade, and remains one of the most popular protocols (by volume) in use today.  This document is a replacement for RFC 977, and officially updates the protocol specification.  It clarifies some vagueness in RFC 977, includes some new base functionality, and provides a specific mechanism to add standardized extensions to NNTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhTHUS plcdnamejC. Featherddatej”khfilenamegrfc3977eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgnntpextdfullxnntpext - NNTP ExtensionsdnameoNNTP Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125915hkeywordsÇfusenetgnetnewskobsoletedByÄepages}opublicationDateE@∞grankingâcrfcd3977irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Network News Transfer Protocol (NNTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6048µhabstractyPublic Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) services such as 800-number routing (freephone), time-and-day routing, credit-card calling, and virtual private network (mapping a private network number into a public number) are realized by the Intelligent Network (IN).  This document addresses means to support existing IN services from Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) endpoints for an IP-host-to-phone call.  The call request is originated on a SIP endpoint, but the services to the call are provided by the data and procedures resident in the PSTN/IN.  To provide IN services in a transparent manner to SIP endpoints, this document describes the mechanism for interworking SIP and Intelligent Network Application Part (INAP).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. MankingauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxLucent Technologies, Inc.dnamemV. K. Gurbani¢kaffiliationlAlcatel BelldnamejF. Haerens¢kaffiliationrWipro TechnologiesdnamejV. Rastogiddatej—=hfilenamegrfc3976eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125914hkeywordsácsipsintelligent networkkcall modelsrcall model mappingrtelephony servicesx!public switched telephone networkdpstnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAÔ˚&grankingàcrfcd3976irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex:Interworking SIP and Intelligent Network (IN) ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÃThis document describes the standardization collaboration between the Open Mobile Alliance (OMA) and the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationcOMAdnamehI. Leucaddatej—Zhfilenamegrfc3975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125913hkeywordsÉuoopen mobile alliancedietfxinternet engineering task forcekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateAˇ–1grankingácrfcd3975irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex&OMA-IETF Standardization CollaborationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty(This document discusses SMTP operational experiences in IPv4/v6 dual stack environments. As IPv6-capable SMTP servers are deployed, it has become apparent that certain configurations of MX records are necessary for stable dual-stack (IPv4 and IPv6) SMTP operation. This document clarifies the existing problems in the transition period between IPv4 SMTP and IPv6 SMTP. It also defines operational requirements for stable IPv4/v6 SMTP operation.

This document does not define any new protocol. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpKyoto UniversitydnamekM. Nakamura¢kaffiliationwIIJ Research LaboratorydnameiJ. Haginoddatej—=hfilenamegrfc3974eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125912hkeywordsÖxsimple mail transfer protocoljdual stackidualstackdipv4dipv6kobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateAÔ˙∫grankingÜcrfcd3974irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex9SMTP Operational Experience in Mixed IPv4/v6 EnvironmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document specifies Protocol Independent Multicast - Dense Mode (PIM-DM).  PIM-DM is a multicast routing protocol that uses the underlying unicast routing information base to flood multicast datagrams to all multicast routers.  Prune messages are used to prevent future messages from propagating to routers without group membership information.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtNextHop TechnologiesdnamehA. Adams¢kaffiliationhITT A/CDdnamekJ. Nicholas¢kaffiliationtNextHop TechnologiesdnameiW. Siadakddatej—Fhfilenamegrfc3973eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcpimdfullx pim - Protocols for IP MulticastdnamexProtocols for IP Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-125911hkeywordsÇxmulticast routing protocolnprune messageskobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateAıã¢grankingÖcrfcd3973irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVProtocol Independent Multicast - Dense Mode (PIM-DM): Protocol Specification (Revised)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9436d8736∂habstractyThis document describes a method for binding a public signature key to an IPv6 address in the Secure Neighbor Discovery (SEND) protocol.  Cryptographically Generated Addresses (CGA) are IPv6 addresses for which the interface identifier is generated by computing a cryptographic one-way hash function from a public key and auxiliary parameters.  The binding between the public key and the address can be verified by re-computing the hash value and by comparing the hash with the interface identifier.  Messages sent from an IPv6 address can be protected by attaching the public key and auxiliary parameters and by signing the message with the corresponding private key.  The protection works without a certification authority or any security infrastructure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnamegT. Auraddatej—Shfilenamegrfc3972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsenddfullx"send - Securing Neighbor DiscoverydnamexSecuring Neighbor Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-125910hkeywordsÇxSecure Neighbor DiscoverydSENDkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB7IgrankingÑcrfcd3972irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Cryptographically Generated Addresses (CGA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd4581d4982∂habstracty¥IPv6 nodes use the Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP) to discover other nodes on the link, to determine their link-layer addresses to find routers, and to maintain reachability information about the paths to active neighbors.  If not secured, NDP is vulnerable to various attacks.  This document specifies security mechanisms for NDP.  Unlike those in the original NDP specifications, these mechanisms do not use IPsec. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationxDoCoMo Communications Labs USAdnamehJ. Kempf¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamegB. Zill¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekP. Nikanderddatej—Ohfilenamegrfc3971eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdsenddfullx"send - Securing Neighbor DiscoverydnamexSecuring Neighbor Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-125909hkeywordsÇxNeighbor Discovery ProtocolcNDPkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDateB7ÈgrankingÉcrfcd3971irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex SEcure Neighbor Discovery (SEND)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd6494d6495d6980∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for Traffic Engineered (TE) Tunnels; for example, Multi-Protocol Label Switched Paths. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompelladdatej“3hfilenamegrfc3970eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125908hkeywordsÑcMIBxManagement Information BasebTEsTraffic EngineeringkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDateA‹êMgrankingÇcrfcd3970irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexA Traffic Engineering (TE) MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∑habstractyØThis document creates an Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) registry for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and SIPS Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) parameters, and their values.  It also lists the already existing parameters to be used as initial values for that registry.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej—ﬂhfilenamegrfc3969eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125907hkeywordsÇcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA»≤grankingÅcrfcd3969irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumbercetotaletitlexåThe Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) Parameter Registry for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5727∑habstractyÆThis document creates an Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) registry for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) header field parameters and parameter values.  It also lists the already existing parameters and parameter values to be used as the initial entries for this registry.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej—·hfilenamegrfc3968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125906hkeywordsÇcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA»±grankingÄcrfcd3968irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberbetotaletitlexwThe Internet Assigned Number Authority (IANA) Header Field Parameter Registry for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyÈIETF procedures generally require that a standards track RFC may not have a normative reference to another standards track document at a lower maturity level or to a non standards track specification (other than specifications from other standards bodies).  For example, a standards track document may not have a normative reference to an informational RFC.  Exceptions to this rule are sometimes needed as the IETF uses informational RFCs to describe non-IETF standards or IETF-specific modes of use of such standards.  This document clarifies and updates the procedure used in these circumstances.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIIJdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiT. Nartenddatej…vhfilenamegrfc3967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125905hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAŸ·©grankingcrfcd3967irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberaetotaletitlex]Clarifying when Standards Track Documents may Refer Normatively to Documents at a Lower LeveldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd4897d8067∂habstractxƒThis document specifies the URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) scheme "tel".  The "tel" URI describes resources identified by telephone numbers.  This document obsoletes RFC 2806. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej—·hfilenamegrfc3966eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-125904hkeywordsÖcURIxuniform resource identifierglocatorgschemesctelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA∞€mgranking~crfcd3966irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!The tel URI for Telephone NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5341∂habstracty
This specification provides for "simple mode" carriage of facsimile data using Internet mail. Extensions to this document will follow. The current specification employs standard protocols and file formats such as TCP/IP, Internet mail protocols, Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME), and Tagged Image File Format (TIFF) for Facsimile. It can send images not only to other Internet-aware facsimile devices but also to Internet-native systems, such as PCs with common email readers which can handle MIME mail and TIFF for Facsimile data. The specification facilitates communication among existing facsimile devices, Internet mail agents, and the gateways which connect them.

This document is a revision of RFC 2305. There have been no technical changes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationlWIDE ProjectdnameiK. Toyoda¢kaffiliationlWIDE ProjectdnamegH. Ohno¢kaffiliationlWIDE ProjectdnamehJ. Murai¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Wingddatej—fhfilenamegrfc3965eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-125903hkeywordsÜhSMFAX-IMx,Simple Mode of Facsimile Using Internet Mailddatadfilefformatfe-mailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA∫? granking}crfcd3965irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A Simple Mode of Facsimile Using Internet MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThe IPv6 interim mechanism 6to4 (RFC3056) uses automatic IPv6-over-IPv4 tunneling to interconnect IPv6 networks.  The architecture includes 6to4 routers and 6to4 relay routers, which accept and decapsulate IPv4 protocol-41 ("IPv6-in-IPv4") traffic from any node in the IPv4 internet.  This characteristic enables a number of security threats, mainly Denial of Service.  It also makes it easier for nodes to spoof IPv6 addresses.  This document discusses these issues in more detail and suggests enhancements to alleviate the problems.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationqAll Play, No WorkdnamehC. Patelddatej…uhfilenamegrfc3964eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125902hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateA∞⁄ÿgranking|crfcd3964irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Security Considerations for 6to4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis document describes the Network Mobility (NEMO) Basic Support protocol that enables Mobile Networks to attach to different points in the Internet.  The protocol is an extension of Mobile IPv6 and allows session continuity for every node in the Mobile Network as the network moves.  It also allows every node in the Mobile Network to be reachable while moving around.  The Mobile Router, which connects the network to the Internet, runs the NEMO Basic Support protocol with its Home Agent.  The protocol is designed so that network mobility is transparent to the nodes inside the Mobile Network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationoKeio UniversitydnamekR. Wakikawa¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekA. Petrescu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Thubertddatej—Phfilenamegrfc3963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnemodfullwnemo - Network MobilitydnamepNetwork Mobilitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125901hkeywordsÇkmobile ipv6rsession continuitykobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateAıã%granking{crfcd3963irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Network Mobility (NEMO) Basic Support ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThe United States National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) has chosen a new Advanced Encryption Standard (AES), which is significantly faster and (it is believed) more secure than the old Data Encryption Standard (DES) algorithm.  This document is a specification for the addition of this algorithm to the Kerberos cryptosystem suite. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejK. Raeburnddatej—Ohfilenamegrfc3962eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-125900hkeywordsÅxkerberos cryptosystem suitekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB9àgrankingzcrfcd3962irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Encryption for Kerberos 5dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractySThis document describes a framework for defining encryption and checksum mechanisms for use with the Kerberos protocol, defining an abstraction layer between the Kerberos protocol and related protocols, and the actual mechanisms themselves.  The document also defines several mechanisms.  Some are taken from RFC 1510, modified in form to fit this new framework and occasionally modified in content when the old specification was incorrect.  New mechanisms are presented here as well.  This document does NOT indicate which mechanisms may be considered "required to implement". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejK. Raeburnddatej»®hfilenamegrfc3961eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfkrb-wgdfullqkrb-wg - KerberosdnamehKerberosdtypebwgbidjdoc-125899hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateB8¸grankingycrfcd3961irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Encryption and Checksum Specifications for Kerberos 5dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8429∂habstractyHThis document describes how to manage early media in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) using two models: the gateway model and the application server model.  It also describes the inputs one needs to consider in defining local policies for ringing tone generation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej…uhfilenamegrfc3960eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125898hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA»∞xgrankingxcrfcd3960irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPEarly Media and Ringing Tone Generation in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThis document defines a new disposition type (early-session) for the Content-Disposition header field in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  The treatment of "early-session" bodies is similar to the treatment of "session" bodies.  That is, they follow the offer/answer model.  Their only difference is that session descriptions whose disposition type is "early-session" are used to establish early media sessions within early dialogs, as opposed to regular sessions within regular dialogs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej—·hfilenamegrfc3959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125897hkeywordsÇcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA»∞grankingwcrfcd3959irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLThe Early Session Disposition Type for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a generalized mechanism for application service naming that allows service location without relying on rigid domain naming conventions (so-called name hacks).  The proposal defines a Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Application to map domain name, application service name, and application protocol dynamically to target server and port. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newtonddatej“3hfilenamegrfc3958eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125896hkeywordsÇdDDDSx#Dynamic Delegation Discovery SystemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA›‡ûgrankingvcrfcd3958irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkDomain-Based Application Service Location Using SRV RRs and the Dynamic Delegation Discovery Service (DDDS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstractyuAuthentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) servers, such as RADIUS and DIAMETER, are in use within the Internet today to provide authentication and authorization services for dial-up computers.  Mobile IP for IPv4 requires strong authentication between the mobile node and its home agent.  When the mobile node shares an AAA Security Association with its home AAA server, however, it is possible to use that AAA Security Association to create derived Mobility Security Associations between the mobile node and its home agent, and again between the mobile node and the foreign agent currently offering connectivity to the mobile node.  This document specifies extensions to Mobile IP registration messages that can be used to create Mobility Security Associations between the mobile node and its home agent, and/or between the mobile node and a foreign agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationiAirespacednamejP. Calhounddatej»£hfilenamegrfc3957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-125895hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB.èËgrankingucrfcd3957irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUAuthentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) Registration Keys for Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÈThis memo defines an address allocation policy in which the address of the Rendezvous Point (RP) is encoded in an IPv6 multicast group address.  For Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode (PIM-SM), this can be seen as a specification of a group-to-RP mapping mechanism.  This allows an easy deployment of scalable inter-domain multicast and simplifies the intra-domain multicast configuration as well.  This memo updates the addressing format presented in RFC 3306. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savola¢kaffiliationgJHU APLdnamekB. Habermanddatej“Ôhfilenamegrfc3956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-125894hkeywordsÉbRPpRendezvous Pointqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAè˚•grankingtcrfcd3956irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHEmbedding the Rendezvous Point (RP) Address in an IPv6 Multicast AddressdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7371∂habstracty⁄This document contains an evaluation of the five candidate protocols for an IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX) protocol, based on the requirements document produced by the IPFIX Working Group.  The protocols are characterized and grouped in broad categories, and evaluated against specific requirements.  Finally, a recommendation is made to select the NetFlow v9 protocol as the basis for the IPFIX specification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfSWITCHdnameiS. LeinenddatejÀ=hfilenamegrfc3955eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125893hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAÅ0€grankingscrfcd3955irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHEvaluation of Candidate Protocols for IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty·This document specifies the data export format for version 9 of Cisco Systems' NetFlow services, for use by implementations on the network elements and/or matching collector programs.  The version 9 export format uses templates to provide access to observations of IP packet flows in a flexible and extensible manner.  A template defines a collection of fields, with corresponding descriptions of structure and semantics.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. ClaiseddatejÀ<hfilenamegrfc3954eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125892hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateAÅ0égrankingrcrfcd3954irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex/Cisco Systems NetFlow Services Export Version 9dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx©This document registers a Telephone Number Mapping (ENUM) service for presence.  Specifically, this document focuses on provisioning pres URIs in ENUM. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej—Phfilenamegrfc3953eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125891hkeywordsÖxuniform resource identifiercuriqprovisioning presdENUMxTelephone Number MappingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAıã$grankingqcrfcd3953irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJTelephone Number Mapping (ENUM) Service Registration for Presence ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstractyrThis document describes the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) payload format for the internet Low Bit Rate Codec (iLBC) Speech developed by Global IP Sound (GIPS).  Also, within the document there are included necessary details for the use of iLBC with MIME and Session Description Protocol (SDP).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeTeliodnamehA. Duric¢kaffiliationrAalborg UniversitydnamekS. Andersenddatej—‡hfilenamegrfc3952eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125890hkeywordsÑcRTPxReal-time Transport ProtocoldiLBCxinternet Low Bit Rate CodeckobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA∞⁄fgrankingpcrfcd3952irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) Payload Format for internet Low Bit Rate Codec (iLBC) SpeechdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document specifies a speech codec suitable for robust voice communication over IP.  The codec is developed by Global IP Sound (GIPS).  It is designed for narrow band speech and results in a payload bit rate of 13.33 kbit/s for 30 ms frames and 15.20 kbit/s for 20 ms frames.  The codec enables graceful speech quality degradation in the case of lost frames, which occurs in connection with lost or delayed IP packets.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationrAalborg UniversitydnamekS. Andersen¢kaffiliationeTeliodnamehA. Duric¢kaffiliationoGlobal IP SounddnameiH. Astrom¢kaffiliationoGlobal IP SounddnamehR. Hagen¢kaffiliationoGlobal IP SounddnameiW. Kleijn¢kaffiliationoGlobal IP SounddnameiJ. Lindenddatej—ehfilenamegrfc3951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125889hkeywordsÇdiLBCxInternet Low Bit Rate CodeckobsoletedByÄepages¬opublicationDateA∞⁄jgrankingocrfcd3951irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Internet Low Bit Rate Codec (iLBC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≠This document describes the registration of the MIME sub-type image/tiff-fx.  The encodings are defined by File Format for Internet Fax and its extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekL. McIntyre¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsons¢kaffiliationuBrooktrout TechnologydnamekJ. Raffertyddatej—Qhfilenamegrfc3950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-125888hkeywordsädFFIFdTIFFcTageImageifacsimiledMIMElmultipurposehInternetdmailjextensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateB
® grankingncrfcd3950irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWTag Image File Format Fax eXtended (TIFF-FX) - image/tiff-fx MIME Sub-type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒThis document is a revised version of RFC 2301. The revisions, summarized in the list attached as Annex B, are based on discussions and suggestions for improvements that have been made since RFC 2301 was issued in March 1998, and on the results of independent implementations and interoperability testing.

This RFC 2301 revision describes the Tag Image File Format (TIFF) representation of image data specified by the International Telecommunication Union (ITU-T) Recommendations for black-and-white and color facsimile. This file format specification is commonly known as TIFF for Fax eXtended (TIFF-FX). It formally defines minimal, extended, and lossless Joint Bi-level Image experts Group (JBIG) profiles (Profiles S, F, J) for black-and-white fax and base JPEG, lossless JBIG, and Mixed Raster Content profiles (Profiles C, L, M) for color and grayscale fax. These profiles correspond to the content of the applicable ITU-T Recommendations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnamejR. Buckley¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnamejD. Venable¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekL. McIntyre¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsons¢kaffiliationjBrooktroutdnamekJ. Raffertyddatej”Fhfilenamegrfc3949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-125887hkeywordsädFFIFdTIFFcTageImageifacsimiledMIMElmultipurposehInternetdmailjextensionskobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDateB
®grankingmcrfcd3949irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFile Format for Internet FaxdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractywThis protocol specification defines methods to encapsulate and decapsulate IP Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) packets inside UDP packets for traversing Network Address Translators.  ESP encapsulation, as defined in this document, can be used in both IPv4 and IPv6 scenarios.  Whenever negotiated, encapsulation is used with Internet Key Exchange (IKE). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtF-Secure CorporationdnamekA. Huttunen¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejB. Swander¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehV. Volpe¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejL. DiBurro¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Stenbergddatej“3hfilenamegrfc3948eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125886hkeywordsÉeIPseccESPxEncapsulating Security PayloadkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA‹êgrankinglcrfcd3948irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&UDP Encapsulation of IPsec ESP PacketsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯This document describes how to detect one or more network address translation devices (NATs) between IPsec hosts, and how to negotiate the use of UDP encapsulation of IPsec packets through NAT boxes in Internet Key Exchange (IKE). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgSafeNetdnamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejB. Swander¢kaffiliationtF-Secure CorporationdnamekA. Huttunen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehV. Volpeddatej“0hfilenamegrfc3947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125885hkeywordsÇcIKEuInternet Key ExchangekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA‹ègrankingkcrfcd3947irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Negotiation of NAT-Traversal in the IKEdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document is a companion to the Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) signaling.  It defines the Synchronous Optical Network (SONET)/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) technology specific information needed when using GMPLS signaling. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiE. Mannie¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamepD. PapadimitriouddatejÀ?hfilenamegrfc3946eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-125884hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd4606epagesopublicationDateAÜoìgrankingjcrfcd3946irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexïGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Extensions for Synchronous Optical Network (SONET) and Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SDH) ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜFuture data and transmission networks will consist of elements such as routers, switches, Dense Wavelength Division Multiplexing (DWDM) systems, Add-Drop Multiplexors (ADMs), photonic cross-connects (PXCs), optical cross-connects (OXCs), etc. that will use Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) to dynamically provision resources and to provide network survivability using protection and restoration techniques.

This document describes the architecture of GMPLS. GMPLS extends MPLS to encompass time-division (e.g., SONET/SDH, PDH, G.709), wavelength (lambdas), and spatial switching (e.g., incoming port or fiber to outgoing port or fiber). The focus of GMPLS is on the control plane of these various layers since each of them can use physically diverse data or forwarding planes. The intention is to cover both the signaling and the routing part of that control plane. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiE. Mannieddatej”hfilenamegrfc3945eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-125883hkeywordsÇeGMPLSx*Generalized Multi-Protocol Label SwitchingkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDateAÜnÿgrankingicrfcd3945irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6002∂habstracty#The International Telecommunications Union Standardization Sector (ITU-T) has created the H.350 series of Recommendations that specify directory services architectures in support of multimedia conferencing protocols.  The goal of the architecture is to 'directory enable' multimedia conferencing so that these services can leverage existing identity management and enterprise directories.  A particular goal is to enable an enterprise or service provider to maintain a canonical source of users and their multimedia conferencing systems, so that multiple call servers from multiple vendors, supporting multiple protocols, can all access the same data store.  Because SIP is an IETF standard, the contents of H.350 and H.350.4 are made available via this document to the IETF community.  This document contains the entire normative text of ITU-T Recommendations H.350 and H.350.4 in sections 4 and 5, respectively.  The remaining sections are included only in this document, not in the ITU-T version.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationtU. of North CarolinadnamejT. Johnson¢kaffiliationqWaseda UniversitydnamehS. Okubo¢kaffiliationeITU-TdnameiS. Camposddatej—fhfilenamegrfc3944eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125882hkeywordsádldaprdirectory serviceseh.350eh.323eh.320eh.235csipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA∫>}grankinghcrfcd3944irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexH.350 Directory ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»The Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol (RFC 2246) includes features to negotiate selection of a lossless data compression method as part of the TLS Handshake Protocol and then to apply the algorithm associated with the selected method as part of the TLS Record Protocol.  TLS defines one standard compression method, which specifies that data exchanged via the record protocol will not be compressed.  This document describes an additional compression method associated with the Lempel-Ziv-Stac (LZS) lossless data compression algorithm for use with TLS.  This document also defines the application of the LZS algorithm to the TLS Record Protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdHifndnameiR. Friendddatej—ehfilenamegrfc3943eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125881hkeywordsÇx#lossless data compression algorithmsTLS Record ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA¢â˘grankinggcrfcd3943irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOTransport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Compression Using Lempel-Ziv-Stac (LZS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractykThis document updates RFC 2132 to reclassify Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol version 4 (DHCPv4) option codes 128 to 223 (decimal) as publicly defined options to be managed by IANA in accordance with RFC 2939.  This document directs IANA to make these option codes available for assignment as publicly defined DHCP options for future options. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegB. Volzddatej“hfilenamegrfc3942eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125880hkeywordsÉjDHCP-BOOTPiBootstrapx#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA¢â`grankingfcrfcd3942irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLReclassifying Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol version 4 (DHCPv4) OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document discusses the creation of negative-acknowledgment (NACK)-oriented reliable multicast (NORM) protocols.  The rationale for NORM goals and assumptions are presented.  Technical challenges for NACK-oriented (and in some cases general) reliable multicast protocol operation are identified.  These goals and challenges are resolved into a set of functional "building blocks" that address different aspects of NORM protocol operation.  It is anticipated that these building blocks will be useful in generating different instantiations of reliable multicast protocols.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNRLdnamejB. Adamson¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationcNRLdnameiJ. Mackerddatej“Ôhfilenamegrfc3941eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125879hkeywordsÑdNACKxNegative-acknowledgementxOriented Reliable MulticastdNORMkobsoletedByÅd5401epages$opublicationDateAûRògrankingecrfcd3941irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQNegative-Acknowledgment (NACK)-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) Building BlocksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙This document describes the messages and procedures of the Negative-acknowledgment (NACK) Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) protocol.  This protocol is designed to provide end-to-end reliable transport of bulk data objects or streams over generic IP multicast routing and forwarding services.  NORM uses a selective, negative acknowledgment mechanism for transport reliability and offers additional protocol mechanisms to allow for operation with minimal "a priori" coordination among senders and receivers.  A congestion control scheme is specified to allow the NORM protocol to fairly share available network bandwidth with other transport protocols such as Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).  It is capable of operating with both reciprocal multicast routing among senders and receivers and with asymmetric connectivity (possibly a unicast return path) between the senders and receivers.  The protocol offers a number of features to allow different types of applications or possibly other higher level transport protocols to utilize its service in different ways.  The protocol leverages the use of FEC-based repair and other IETF reliable multicast transport (RMT) building blocks in its design.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcNRLdnamejB. Adamson¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationcNRLdnameiJ. Mackerddatej—Fhfilenamegrfc3940eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125878hkeywordsÑdNACKxNegative-acknowledgementxOriented Reliable MulticastdNORMkobsoletedByÅd5740epagesPopublicationDateAûRgrankingdcrfcd3940irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJNegative-acknowledgment (NACK)-Oriented Reliable Multicast (NORM) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document describes a method for identifying the originating calling party in the headers of a stored voice mail message.  Two new header fields are defined for this purpose: Caller_ID and Called_Name.  Caller_id is used to store sufficient information for the recipient to callback, or reply to, the sender of the message.  Caller-name provides the name of the person sending the message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsons¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekJ. Maruszakddatej—·hfilenamegrfc3939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125877hkeywordsÅjVoice MailkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA»∞grankingccrfcd3939irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Calling Line Identification for Voice Mail MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\The Message-Context header defined in RFC 3458 describes the context of a message (for example: fax-message or voice-message). This specification extends the Message-Context header with one additional context value: "video-message".

A receiving user agent (UA) may use this information as a hint to optimally present the message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansenddatej”hfilenamegrfc3938eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125876hkeywordsÇjuser agentbuakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAy˙grankingbcrfcd3938irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVideo-Message Message-ContextdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœThis document describes a URN (Uniform Resource Name) namespace for identifying persistent resources published by the International Press Telecommunications Council (IPTC).  These resources include XML Data Type Definition files (DTD), XML Schema, Namespaces in XML, XSL stylesheets, other XML based document and documents of other data formats like PDF documents, Microsoft Office documents and others.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIPTCdnameiM. SteidlddatejÀ<hfilenamegrfc3937eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125875hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateAÜpãgrankingacrfcd3937irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the International Press Telecommunications Council (IPTC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractynThis memo specifies procedures for modifying the Resource reSerVation Protocol (RSVP).  This memo also lays out new assignment guidelines for number spaces for RSVP messages, object classes, class-types, and sub-objects.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationoRincon NetworksdnamegJ. Langddatej”hfilenamegrfc3936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125874hkeywordsÖdRSVPxresource reservation protocolelabelhswitchedepathskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAy¥granking`crfcd3936irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber`etotaletitlexAProcedures for Modifying the Resource reSerVation Protocol (RSVP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty®This memo gives a mission statement for the IETF, tries to define the terms used in the statement sufficiently to make the mission statement understandable and useful, argues why the IETF needs a mission statement, and tries to capture some of the debate that led to this point.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemH. Alvestrandddatej”hfilenamegrfc3935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125873hkeywordsÅqMission StatementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAyxgranking_crfcd3935irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber_etotaletitlex A Mission Statement for the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyãThis document is an update to RFC 2418 that gives WG chairs explicit responsibility for managing WG mailing lists.  In particular, it gives WG chairs the authority to temporarily suspend the mailing list posting privileges of disruptive individuals.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjThingMagicdnamelM. Wassermanddatej”hfilenamegrfc3934eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125872hkeywordsÖcBCPbWGfescapefclausejprocedureskobsoletedByÅd9945epagesopublicationDateAy0granking^crfcd3934irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBUpdates to RFC 2418 Regarding the Management of IETF Mailing ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty)The IETF has designed process changes over the last ten years in one of two ways: announcement by the IESG, sometimes based on informal agreements with limited community involvement and awareness, and formal use of the same mechanism used for protocol specification. The first mechanism has often proven to be too lightweight, the second too heavyweight.

This document specifies a middle-ground approach to the system of making changes to IETF process, one that relies heavily on a "propose and carry out an experiment, evaluate the experiment, and then establish permanent procedures based on operational experience" model rather than those previously attempted. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Dawkinsddatej—ehfilenamegrfc3933eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125871hkeywordsÅkExperimentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAåÚgranking]crfcd3933irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber]etotaletitlex$A Model for IETF Process ExperimentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyõThis document describes the IESG's procedures for handling documents submitted for RFC publication via the RFC Editor, subsequent to the changes proposed by the IESG at the Seoul IETF, March 2004.

This document updates procedures described in RFC 2026 and RFC 3710. This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymdietfdfullkietf - IETFdnamedIETFgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemH. Alvestrandddatej”Zhfilenamegrfc3932eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering Groupdtypedietfbidjdoc-125870hkeywordsÉdIESGjRFC Editorvindependent submissionkobsoletedByÅd5742epagesopublicationDateAg&îgranking\crfcd3932irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-The IESG and RFC Editor Documents: ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty3This document describes "version 3" of the Layer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TPv3).  L2TPv3 defines the base control protocol and encapsulation for tunneling multiple Layer 2 connections between two IP nodes.  Additional documents detail the specifics for each data link type being emulated. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefJ. Lau¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekM. Townsley¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiI. Goyretddatej—Phfilenamegrfc3931eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125869hkeywordsÜdL2TPxLayer Two Tunneling Protocolcpppnpoint-to-pointhprotocolgpacketskobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDateB.èLgranking[crfcd3931irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Layer Two Tunneling Protocol - Version 3 (L2TPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5641d9601µhabstractyThis document contrasts two points of view: the "document" point of view, where digital objects of interest are like pieces of paper written and viewed by people, and the "protocol" point of view where objects of interest are composite dynamic network messages.  Although each point of view has a place, adherence to a document point of view can be damaging to protocol design.  By understanding both points of view, conflicts between them may be clarified and reduced.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott HollenbeckgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatejÀ<hfilenamegrfc3930eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125868hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAyàPgrankingZcrfcd3930irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexAThe Protocol versus Document Points of View in Computer ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This document proposes an experimental set of alternative decision-making processes for use in IETF working groups.  There are a small number of cases in IETF working groups in which the group has come to consensus that a particular decision must be made but cannot agree on the decision itself.  This document describes alternative mechanisms for reaching a decision in those cases.  This is not meant to provide an exhaustive list, but to provide a known set of tools that can be used when needed.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnQualcomm, Inc.dnameiT. HardieddatejÀ;hfilenamegrfc3929eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125867hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAy~ÓgrankingYcrfcd3929irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQAlternative Decision Making Processes for Consensus-Blocked Decisions in the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8717∂habstracty>This document defines the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Client Update Protocol (LCUP).  The protocol is intended to allow an LDAP client to synchronize with the content of a directory information tree (DIT) stored by an LDAP server and to be notified about the changes to that content. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxNetscape Communications Corp.dnamelR. Megginson¢kaffiliationsPearl Crescent, LLCdnamehM. Smith¢kaffiliationeYahoodnamelO. Natkovich¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnameiJ. Parhamddatej”hfilenamegrfc3928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdldupdfullx4ldup - LDAP Duplication/Replication/Update Protocolsdnamex-LDAP Duplication/Replication/Update Protocolsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125866hkeywordsÑdLDAPx%Lightweight Directory Access ProtocoldLCUPvClient Update ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAy~RgrankingXcrfcd3928irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Client Update Protocol (LCUP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[To participate in wide-area IP networking, a host needs to be configured with IP addresses for its interfaces, either manually by the user or automatically from a source on the network such as a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) server. Unfortunately, such address configuration information may not always be available. It is therefore beneficial for a host to be able to depend on a useful subset of IP networking functions even when no address configuration is available. This document describes how a host may automatically configure an interface with an IPv4 address within the 169.254/16 prefix that is valid for communication with other devices connected to the same physical (or logical) link.

IPv4 Link-Local addresses are not suitable for communication with devices not directly connected to the same physical (or logical) link, and are only used where stable, routable addresses are not available (such as on ad hoc or isolated networks). This document does not recommend that IPv4 Link-Local addresses and routable addresses be configured simultaneously on the same interface. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnApple ComputerdnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttmanddatej—Phfilenamegrfc3927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhzeroconfdfullx(zeroconf - Zero Configuration NetworkingdnamexZero Configuration Networkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125865hkeywordsÑuDynamic Configurationjip networkjip addressj169.254/16kobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateBïgrankingWcrfcd3927irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Dynamic Configuration of IPv4 Link-Local AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThis document defines FLUTE, a protocol for the unidirectional delivery of files over the Internet, which is particularly suited to multicast networks.  The specification builds on Asynchronous Layered Coding, the base protocol designed for massively scalable multicast distribution.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehT. Paila¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnamekR. Lehtonen¢kaffiliationqINRIA Rhone-AlpesdnamegV. Roca¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Walshddatej”hfilenamegrfc3926eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125864hkeywordsÇeFLUTEx+File Delivery over Unidirectional TransportkobsoletedByÅd6726epages#opublicationDateAc/grankingVcrfcd3926irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3FLUTE - File Delivery over Unidirectional TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyqThe Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) options for Vendor Class and Vendor-Specific Information can be limiting or ambiguous when a DHCP client represents multiple vendors.  This document defines two new options, modeled on the IPv6 options for vendor class and vendor-specific information, that contain Enterprise Numbers to remove ambiguity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenJ. Littlefieldddatej”hfilenamegrfc3925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125863hkeywordsÖcdhcddhcpx#Dynamic Host Configuration Protocoleclassovendor-specifickobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateAy~*grankingUcrfcd3925irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Vendor-Identifying Vendor Options for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol version 4 (DHCPv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty9For the purposes of this document, lawful intercept is the lawfully authorized interception and monitoring of communications.  Service providers are being asked to meet legal and regulatory requirements for the interception of voice as well as data communications in IP networks in a variety of countries worldwide.  Although requirements vary from country to country, some requirements remain common even though details such as delivery formats may differ.  This document describes Cisco's Architecture for supporting lawful intercept in IP networks.  It provides a general solution that has a minimum set of common interfaces.  This document does not attempt to address any of the specific legal requirements or obligations that may exist in a particular country.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. BellovingauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Foster¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehC. SharpddatejÀ=hfilenamegrfc3924eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125862hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAÜpãgrankingTcrfcd3924irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex6Cisco Architecture for Lawful Intercept in IP NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxíThis memo defines methods of end-to-end signing and object encryption for the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxJabber Software FoundationdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej”Xhfilenamegrfc3923eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhxmpp-olddfullx5xmpp-old - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125861hkeywordsÖcxmlxextensible markup languagebimqinstant messagingfjabberkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAaÂgrankingScrfcd3923irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbEnd-to-End Signing and Object Encryption for the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≤This memo describes a mapping between the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) and the Common Presence and Instant Messaging (CPIM) specifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxJabber Software FoundationdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej”Xhfilenamegrfc3922eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhxmpp-olddfullx5xmpp-old - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125860hkeywordsÖcxmlxextensible markup languagebimqinstant messagingfjabberkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateAaÂ|grankingRcrfcd3922irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmMapping the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) to Common Presence and Instant Messaging (CPIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxThis memo describes extensions to and applications of the core features of the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP) that provide the basic instant messaging (IM) and presence functionality defined in RFC 2779. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxJabber Software FoundationdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej”Xhfilenamegrfc3921eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhxmpp-olddfullx5xmpp-old - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125859hkeywordsÖqinstant messagingbimxextensible markup languagecxmlfjabberkobsoletedByÅd6121epageskopublicationDateAaÂ␀grankingQcrfcd3921irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQExtensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): Instant Messaging and PresencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis memo defines the core features of the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP), a protocol for streaming Extensible Markup Language (XML) elements in order to exchange structured information in close to real time between any two network endpoints.  While XMPP provides a generalized, extensible framework for exchanging XML data, it is used mainly for the purpose of building instant messaging and presence applications that meet the requirements of RFC 2779. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxJabber Software FoundationdnamenP. Saint-Andreddatej”Xhfilenamegrfc3920eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymhxmpp-olddfullx5xmpp-old - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex*Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125858hkeywordsÖqinstant messagingbimxextensible markup languagecxmlfjabberkobsoletedByÅd6120epagesopublicationDateAa‰—grankingPcrfcd3920irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): CoredtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6122∂habstractyWThis memo defines additional (to those in RFC 2896) protocol identifier examples for IP version 6 and MPLS protocols. These can be used to produce valid protocolDirTable ``INDEX`` encodings, as defined by the Remote Network Monitoring MIB (Management Information Base) Version 2 [RFC2021] and the RMON Protocol Identifier Reference [RFC2895].

This document contains additional (to those in RFC 2896) protocol identifier macros for well-known protocols. A conformant implementation of the RMON-2 MIB [RFC2021] can be accomplished without the use of these protocol identifiers, and accordingly, this document does not specify any IETF standard. It is published to encourage better interoperability between RMON-2 agent implementations, by providing RMON related IPv6 and MPLS protocol information. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrFrance Telecom R&DdnamejE. Stephan¢kaffiliationkConsulinteldnamehJ. Paletddatej”Xhfilenamegrfc3919eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125857hkeywordsÅcmibkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAg%ÔgrankingOcrfcd3919irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhRemote Network Monitoring (RMON) Protocol Identifiers for IPv6 and Multi Protocol Label Switching (MPLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«The purpose of this document is to describe methodology specific to the benchmarking of multicast IP forwarding devices. It builds upon the tenets set forth in RFC 2544, RFC 2432 and other IETF Benchmarking Methodology Working Group (BMWG) efforts. This document seeks to extend these efforts to the multicast paradigm.

The BMWG produces two major classes of documents: Benchmarking Terminology documents and Benchmarking Methodology documents. The Terminology documents present the benchmarks and other related terms. The Methodology documents define the procedures required to collect the benchmarks cited in the corresponding Terminology documents. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdIxiadnamehD. Stopp¢kaffiliationvSpirent CommunicationsdnamejB. HickmanddatejÀëhfilenamegrfc3918eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-125856hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAa‰OgrankingNcrfcd3918irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Methodology for IP Multicast BenchmarkingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∆This memo defines requirements for the export of measured IP flow information out of routers, traffic measurement probes, and middleboxes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNEC Europe Ltd.dnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamehT. Zseby¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Claise¢kaffiliationtSwinburne UniversitydnameiS. Zanderddatej”]hfilenamegrfc3917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipfixdfullx"ipfix - IP Flow Information ExportdnamexIP Flow Information Exportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125855hkeywordsÑeipfixgroutersjmeasurmentkmiddleboxeskobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDateAe“égrankingMcrfcd3917irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Requirements for IP Flow Information Export (IPFIX)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_This document describes base requirements for the Pseudo-Wire Emulation Edge to Edge Working Group (PWE3 WG).  It provides guidelines for other working group documents that will define mechanisms for providing pseudo-wire emulation of Ethernet, ATM, and Frame Relay.  Requirements for pseudo-wire emulation of TDM (i.e., "synchronous bit streams at rates defined by ITU G.702") are defined in another document.  It should be noted that the PWE3 WG standardizes mechanisms that can be used to provide PWE3 services, but not the services themselves.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsRiverstone NetworksdnamegX. Xiao¢kaffiliationnArbor NetworksdnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationqOverture NetworksdnamegP. PateddatejÀçhfilenamegrfc3916eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpwe3dfullx(pwe3 - Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgednamex!Pseudowire Emulation Edge to Edgedtypebwgbidjdoc-125854hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA]∫«grankingLcrfcd3916irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Requirements for Pseudo-Wire Emulation Edge-to-Edge (PWE3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@This document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) extension mapping for the management of Domain Name System (DNS) domain names subject to "grace period" policies defined by the Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN).  Grace period policies exist to allow protocol actions to be reversed or otherwise revoked during a short period of time after the protocol action has been performed.  Specified in XML, this mapping extends the EPP domain name mapping to provide additional features required for grace period processing. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”Uhfilenamegrfc3915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125853hkeywordsÑcDNSrDomain Name SystemcEPPx Extensible Provisioning ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA]∫£grankingKcrfcd3915irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSDomain Registry Grace Period Mapping for the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIETF Internet Architecture Board (IAB) expressed nine architecture-level considerations for the Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) framework.  This document describes how OPES addresses those considerations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiA. Barbir¢kaffiliationwThe Measurement FactorydnamekA. RousskovddatejÀ=hfilenamegrfc3914eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125852hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAc.grankingJcrfcd3914irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCOpen Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) Treatment of IAB ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKThis document describes the Border Gateway Multicast Protocol (BGMP), a protocol for inter-domain multicast routing.  BGMP builds shared trees for active multicast groups, and optionally allows receiver domains to build source-specific, inter-domain, distribution branches where needed.  BGMP natively supports "source-specific multicast" (SSM).  To also support "any-source multicast" (ASM), BGMP requires that each multicast group be associated with a single root (in BGMP it is referred to as the root domain).  It requires that different ranges of the multicast address space are associated (e.g., with Unicast-Prefix-Based Multicast addressing) with different domains.  Each of these domains then becomes the root of the shared domain-trees for all groups in its range.  Multicast participants will generally receive better multicast service if the session initiator's address allocator selects addresses from its own domain's part of the space, thereby causing the root domain to be local to at least one of the session participants.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej”Uhfilenamegrfc3913eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbgmpdfullx(bgmp - Border Gateway Multicast Protocoldnamex!Border Gateway Multicast Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125851hkeywordsÉlenter-domainxsource-specific multicastcssmkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateAYÓBgrankingIcrfcd3913irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Border Gateway Multicast Protocol (BGMP): Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¡This document updates the specification of the WHOIS protocol, thereby obsoleting RFC 954.  The update is intended to remove the material from RFC 954 that does not have to do with the on-the-wire protocol, and is no longer applicable in today's Internet.  This document does not attempt to change or update the protocol per se, or document other uses of the protocol that have come into existence since the publication of RFC 954. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiL. Daigleddatej”Vhfilenamegrfc3912eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125850hkeywordsÇeWHOISgNICNAMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateATMágrankingHcrfcd3912irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWHOIS Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This document defines a new header for use with SIP multi-party applications and call control.  The Join header is used to logically join an existing SIP dialog with a new SIP dialog.  This primitive can be used to enable a variety of features, for example: "Barge-In", answering-machine-style "Message Screening" and "Call Center Monitoring".  Note that definition of these example features is non-normative. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAirespacednamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationgPingteldnameiD. Petrieddatej”hfilenamegrfc3911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125849hkeywordsÉcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkJoin headerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAy}©grankingGcrfcd3911irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) "Join" HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document describes the Services in PSTN (Public Switched Telephone Network) requesting Internet Services (SPIRITS) protocol.  The purpose of the SPIRITS protocol is to support services that originate in the cellular or wireline PSTN and necessitate interactions between the PSTN and the Internet.  On the PSTN side, the SPIRITS services are most often initiated from the Intelligent Network (IN) entities.  Internet Call Waiting and Internet Caller-ID Delivery are examples of SPIRITS services, as are location-based services on the cellular network.  The protocol defines the building blocks from which many other services can be built. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationxLucent Technologies, Inc.dnamejV. Gurbani¢kaffiliationxLucent Technologies, Inc.dnamenA. Brusilovsky¢kaffiliationxLucent Technologies, Inc.dnamekI. Faynberg¢kaffiliationoVodafone EspanadnamegJ. Gato¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Lucent TechnologiesdnameeH. Lu¢kaffiliationxLucent Technologies, Inc.dnamekM. Unmehopaddatej”Vhfilenamegrfc3910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgspiritsdfullx<spirits - Service in the PSTN/IN Requesting InTernet Servicednamex2Service in the PSTN/IN Requesting InTernet Servicedtypebwgbidjdoc-125848hkeywordséfSPIRTSdpstnx!Public Switched Telephone Networkcsiphservicesrevent notificationmeventpackagesuinternet call waitingcxmlhwirelesssintelligent networkbINodetection pointbdpkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDateAe“grankingFcrfcd3910irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDThe SPIRITS (Services in PSTN requesting Internet Services) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThis specification describes a Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) extended operation to cancel (or abandon) an outstanding operation.  Unlike the LDAP Abandon operation, but like the X.511 Directory Access Protocol (DAP) Abandon operation, this operation has a response which provides an indication of its outcome. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej”Vhfilenamegrfc3909eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125847hkeywordsÑdLDAPx%Lightweight Directory Access Protocolqabandon operationuoutstanding operationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAg%§grankingEcrfcd3909irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Cancel OperationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊This document describes how conventional hop-by-hop link-state routing protocols interact with new Traffic Engineering capabilities to create Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) shortcuts.  In particular, this document describes how Dijkstra's Shortest Path First (SPF) algorithm can be adapted so that link-state IGPs will calculate IP routes to forward traffic over tunnels that are set up by Traffic Engineering.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamegN. Shen¢kaffiliation`dnamegH. Smitddatej”Vhfilenamegrfc3906eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymertgwgdfullx"rtgwg - Routing Area Working GroupdnamexRouting Area Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125846hkeywordsÉx'hop-by-hop link-state routing protocolscSPFsshortest path firstkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAk{grankingBcrfcd3906irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSCalculating Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) Routes Over Traffic Engineering TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyîThis document describes a proposal for one form of a template for IETF patent disclosures and licensing declarations.  The optional use of this template is meant to simplify the process of such disclosures and licensing declarations and to assist disclosers in providing the necessary information to meet the obligations documented in RFC 3668.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamefV. Seeddatej”Vhfilenamegrfc3905eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125845hkeywordsÅciprkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateATLÓgrankingAcrfcd3905irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAA Template for IETF Patent Disclosures and Licensing DeclarationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˆThis document analyzes issues involved in the transition of "unmanaged networks" from IPv4 to IPv6.  Unmanaged networks typically correspond to home networks or small office networks.  A companion paper analyzes out the requirements for mechanisms needed in various transition scenarios of these networks to IPv6.  Starting from this analysis, we evaluate the suitability of mechanisms that have already been specified, proposed, or deployed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekS. Satapati¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamenR. van der Polddatej”Vhfilenamegrfc3904eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125844hkeywordsÇkhome officeqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAYÓgranking@crfcd3904irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Evaluation of IPv6 Transition Mechanisms for Unmanaged NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for publishing event state used within the SIP Events framework. The first application of this extension is for the publication of presence information.

The mechanism described in this document can be extended to support publication of any event state for which there exists an appropriate event package. It is not intended to be a general-purpose mechanism for transport of arbitrary data, as there are better-suited mechanisms for this purpose. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehA. Niemiddatej”hfilenamegrfc3903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125843hkeywordsÖcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolhpresencekinformationgpackagekobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDateAÜo™granking?crfcd3903irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extension for Event State PublicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractxæThis document defines the "application/soap+xml" media type which can be used to describe SOAP 1.2 messages serialized as XML 1.0.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkIndependentdnamehM. Baker¢kaffiliationkBEA SystemsdnamemM. NottinghamddatejÀêhfilenamegrfc3902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125842hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAYﬁ·granking>crfcd3902irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%The "application/soap+xml" media typedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty@This memo provides guidelines and Best Current Practice for operating DNS in a world where queries and responses are carried in a mixed environment of IPv4 and IPv6 networks.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemDavid Kessensdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvSUN Microsystems, Inc.dnameiA. Durand¢kaffiliationjAutonomicadnamehJ. Ihrenddatej”Whfilenamegrfc3901eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125841hkeywordsÉcDNSrdomain name systemqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateATK√granking=crfcd3901irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber[etotaletitlex)DNS IPv6 Transport Operational GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes four options for Network Information Service (NIS) related configuration information in Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6): NIS Servers, NIS+ Servers, NIS Client Domain Name, NIS+ Client Domain name. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx%Cisco Systems (India) Private LimiteddnameqV. Kalusivalingamddatej”Nhfilenamegrfc3898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125840hkeywordsáxNetwork Information ServicekNIS ServerslNIS+ ServersvNIS Client Domain NamewNIS+ Client Domain namefDHCPv6x,Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPV6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAg%granking:crfcd3898irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexqNetwork Information Service (NIS) Configuration Options for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxßThis memo documents tracing and non-blocking (bypass) requirements for Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiA. Barbirddatej”Whfilenamegrfc3897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125839hkeywordsÉgtracinglnon-blockingfbypasskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateATKägranking9crfcd3897irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIOpen Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) Entities and End Points CommunicationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing DS3 and E3 interfaces.  This document is a companion to the documents that define Managed Objects for the DS0, DS1/E1/DS2/E2 and Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Interface Types.  This document obsoletes RFC 2496. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxRAD Data Communications, Ltd.dnamekO. Nicklassddatej”Uhfilenamegrfc3896eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgatommibdfullratommib - AToM MIBdnamehAToM MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125838hkeywordsÉjDS3-E3-MIBxmanagement information basenInterface TypekobsoletedByÄepages?opublicationDateATJŸgranking8crfcd3896irfcNumber8estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Definitions of Managed Objects for the DS3/E3 Interface TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing DS1, E1, DS2 and E2 interfaces.  This document is a companion to the documents that define Managed Objects for the DS0, DS3/E3 and Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Interface Types.  This document obsoletes RFC 2495. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxRAD Data Communications, Ltd.dnamekO. Nicklassddatej”Uhfilenamegrfc3895eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgatommibdfullratommib - AToM MIBdnamehAToM MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125837hkeywordsÇcMIBxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÅd4805epagesTopublicationDateATJ granking7crfcd3895irfcNumber7estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKDefinitions of Managed Objects for the DS1, E1, DS2, and E2 Interface TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVThe Sieve scripting language allows users to control handling and disposal of their incoming e-mail. By default, an e-mail message that is processed by a Sieve script is saved in the owner's "inbox". Actions such as "fileinto" and "redirect" cancel this default behavior.

This document defines a new keyword parameter, ":copy", to be used with the Sieve "fileinto" and "redirect" actions. Adding ":copy" to an action suppresses cancellation of the default "inbox" save. It allows users to add commands to an existing script without changing the meaning of the rest of the script. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamejJ. Degenerddatej”Whfilenamegrfc3894eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125836hkeywordsÉeSievefclientfserverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAa„Ûgranking6crfcd3894irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Sieve Extension: Copying Without Side EffectsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^RFC 3261 introduces the concept of adding an S/MIME body to a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) request or response in order to provide reference integrity over its headers.  This document provides a more specific mechanism to derive integrity and authentication properties from an 'authenticated identity body', a digitally-signed SIP message, or message fragment.  A standard format for such bodies (known as Authenticated Identity Bodies, or AIBs) is given in this document.  Some considerations for the processing of AIBs by recipients of SIP messages with such bodies are also given. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej”Whfilenamegrfc3893eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125835hkeywordsácSIPxSession Initiation Protocolxauthenticated identity bodyxdigitally-signed SIP messagepmessage fragmentxAuthenticated Identity BodiescAIBkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAYÌEgranking5crfcd3893irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Authenticated Identity Body (AIB) FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˘The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) REFER method provides a mechanism where one party (the referrer) gives a second party (the referee) an arbitrary URI to reference.  If that URI is a SIP URI, the referee will send a SIP request, often an INVITE, to that URI (the refer target).  This document extends the REFER method, allowing the referrer to provide information about the REFER request to the refer target using the referee as an intermediary.  This information includes the identity of the referrer and the URI to which the referrer referred.  The mechanism utilizes S/MIME to help protect this information from a malicious intermediary.  This protection is optional, but a recipient may refuse to accept a request unless it is present. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdXtendnameiR. Sparksddatej”Whfilenamegrfc3892eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125834hkeywordsÉcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocoleREFERkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAYÌ:granking4crfcd3892irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Referred-By MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8217∂habstracty•This document defines a new header for use with Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) multi-party applications and call control.  The Replaces header is used to logically replace an existing SIP dialog with a new SIP dialog.  This primitive can be used to enable a variety of features, for example: "Attended Transfer" and "Call Pickup".  Note that the definition of these example features is non-normative. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. Biggs¢kaffiliation`dnamegR. Deanddatej”Whfilenamegrfc3891eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125833hkeywordsÑcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolxmulti-party applicationslcall controlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAYÏVgranking3crfcd3891irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) "Replaces" HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document defines a Session Description Protocol (SDP) Transport Independent Application Specific Maximum (TIAS) bandwidth modifier that does not include transport overhead; instead an additional packet rate attribute is defined. The transport independent bit-rate value together with the maximum packet rate can then be used to calculate the real bit-rate over the transport actually used.

The existing SDP bandwidth modifiers and their values include the bandwidth needed for the transport and IP layers. When using SDP with protocols like the Session Announcement Protocol (SAP), the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), and the Real-Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP), and when the involved hosts has different transport overhead, for example due to different IP versions, the interpretation of what lower layer bandwidths are included is not clear. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlundddatej”Yhfilenamegrfc3890eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125832hkeywordsÑcSDPxSession Description ProtocoldTIASx2Transport Independent Application Specific MaximumkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAKà†granking2crfcd3890irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUA Transport Independent Bandwidth Modifier for the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8Customers buying enterprise message systems often ask: Can I track the messages? Message tracking is the ability to find out the path that a particular message has taken through a messaging system and the current routing status of that message.  This document provides a model of message tracking that can be used for understanding the Internet-wide message infrastructure and to further enhance those capabilities to include message tracking, as well as requirements for proposed message tracking solutions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansenddatej”Yhfilenamegrfc3888eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmsgtrkdfullx"msgtrk - Message Tracking ProtocoldnamexMessage Tracking Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125831hkeywordsÅpMessage TrackingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA[Cìgranking0crfcd3888irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Message Tracking Model and RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«Customers buying enterprise message systems often ask: Can I track the messages? Message tracking is the ability to find out the path that a particular message has taken through a messaging system and the current routing status of that message.  This document describes the Message Tracking Query Protocol that is used in conjunction with extensions to the ESMTP protocol to provide a complete message tracking solution for the Internet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansenddatej”Zhfilenamegrfc3887eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmsgtrkdfullx"msgtrk - Message Tracking ProtocoldnamexMessage Tracking Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125830hkeywordsÉxMessage Tracking Query ProtocoldMTQPeESMTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA[Cêgranking/crfcd3887irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMessage Tracking Query ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8996d8553∂habstractyMessage Tracking is expected to be used to determine the status of undelivered e-mail upon request. Tracking is used in conjunction with Delivery Status Notifications (DSN) and Message Disposition Notifications (MDN); generally, a message tracking request will be issued only when a DSN or MDN has not been received within a reasonable timeout period.

This memo defines a MIME content-type for message tracking status in the same spirit as RFC 3464, "An Extensible Message Format for Delivery Status Notifications". It is to be issued upon a request as described in "Message Tracking Query Protocol". This memo defines only the format of the status information. An extension to SMTP to label messages for further tracking and request tracking status is defined in a separate memo. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnameiE. Allmanddatej”Zhfilenamegrfc3886eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmsgtrkdfullx"msgtrk - Message Tracking ProtocoldnamexMessage Tracking Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125829hkeywordsÑxDelivery Status NotificationscDSNx!Message Disposition NotificationscMDNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA[Cégranking.crfcd3886irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;An Extensible Message Format for Message Tracking ResponsesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx}This memo defines an extension to the SMTP service whereby a client may mark a message for future tracking. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnameiE. Allman¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansenddatej”Zhfilenamegrfc3885eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmsgtrkdfullx"msgtrk - Message Tracking ProtocoldnamexMessage Tracking Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125828hkeywordsÇdSMTPxsimple mail transfer protocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateA[Cãgranking-crfcd3885irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+SMTP Service Extension for Message TrackingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyZIPsec can secure the links of a multihop network to protect communication between trusted components, e.g., for a secure virtual network (VN), overlay, or virtual private network (VPN).  Virtual links established by IPsec tunnel mode can conflict with routing and forwarding inside VNs because IP routing depends on references to interfaces and next-hop IP addresses.  The IPsec tunnel mode specification is ambiguous on this issue, so even compliant implementations cannot be trusted to avoid conflicts.  An alternative to tunnel mode uses non-IPsec IPIP encapsulation together with IPsec transport mode, which we call IIPtran.  IPIP encapsulation occurs as a separate initial step, as the result of a forwarding lookup of the VN packet.  IPsec transport mode processes the resulting (tunneled) IP packet with an SA determined through a security association database (SAD) match on the tunnel header.  IIPtran supports dynamic routing inside the VN without changes to the current IPsec architecture.  IIPtran demonstrates how to configure any compliant IPsec implementation to avoid the aforementioned conflicts.  IIPtran is also compared to several alternative mechanisms for VN routing and their respective impact on IPsec, routing, policy enforcement, and interactions with the Internet Key Exchange (IKE).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss HousleygauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcISIdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationcNECdnameiL. Eggert¢kaffiliationcISIdnamegY. WangddatejÀñhfilenamegrfc3884eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125827hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAYﬂpgranking,crfcd3884irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex/Use of IPsec Transport Mode for Dynamic RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[OSPF is a link-state intra-domain routing protocol used in IP networks.  OSPF behavior over demand circuits (DC) is optimized in RFC 1793 to minimize the amount of overhead traffic.  A part of the OSPF demand circuit extensions is the Hello suppression mechanism.  This technique allows a demand circuit to go down when no interesting traffic is going through the link.  However, it also introduces a problem, where it becomes impossible to detect an OSPF-inactive neighbor over such a link.  This memo introduces a new mechanism called "neighbor probing" to address the above problem. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcUTAdnamefS. Rao¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamehA. Zinin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamefA. Royddatej”hfilenamegrfc3883eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125826hkeywordsÉgOSPF-DCxOpen Shortest Path FirstoDemand CircuitskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAc,8granking+crfcd3883irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Detecting Inactive Neighbors over OSPF Demand Circuits (DC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÎThis document describes an operational technique that uses BGP communities to remotely trigger black-holing of a particular destination network to block denial-of-service attacks.  Black-holing can be applied on a selection of routers rather than all BGP-speaking routers in the network.  The document also describes a sinkhole tunnel technique using BGP communities and tunnels to pull traffic into a sinkhole router for analysis.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. ZiningauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkBell CanadadnamegD. Turkddatej”[hfilenamegrfc3882eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125825hkeywordsÇcdoswborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA]Èágranking*crfcd3882irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex2Configuring BGP to Block Denial-of-Service AttacksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¬This document defines the format of data to be collected and minimum set of attributes that need to be captured for security auditing in healthcare application systems.  The format is defined as an XML schema, which is intended as a reference for healthcare standards developers and application designers.  It consolidates several previous documents on security auditing of healthcare data.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott HollenbeckgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamekG. MarshallddatejÀñhfilenamegrfc3881eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125824hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages/opublicationDateA]∫,granking)crfcd3881irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexaSecurity Audit and Access Accountability Message XML Data Definitions for Healthcare ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document defines the Call Processing Language (CPL), a language to describe and control Internet telephony services.  It is designed to be implementable on either network servers or user agents.  It is meant to be simple, extensible, easily edited by graphical clients, and independent of operating system or signalling protocol.  It is suitable for running on a server where users may not be allowed to execute arbitrary programs, as it has no variables, loops, or ability to run external programs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnameiJ. Lennox¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnameeX. Wu¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej”hfilenamegrfc3880eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-125823hkeywordsÇcCPLxCall Processing LanguagekobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDateAÜngranking(crfcd3880irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZCall Processing Language (CPL): A Language for User Control of Internet Telephony ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the issues surrounding the use of IPv6 site-local unicast addresses in their original form, and formally deprecates them.  This deprecation does not prevent their continued use until a replacement has been standardized and implemented. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelB. Carpenterddatej”:hfilenamegrfc3879eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125822hkeywordsÑqInternet Protocoldipv6larchitecturejsite-localko