The interface has been in use by the World-Wide Web (WWW) since 1993. This specification defines the 'current practice' parameters of the 'CGI/1.1' interface developed and documented at the U.S. National Centre for Supercomputing Applications. This document also defines the use of the CGI/1.1 interface on UNIX(R) and other, similar systems. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxThe Apache Software FoundationdnamekD. Robinson¢kaffiliationxThe Apache Software FoundationdnamegK. Coarddatej”hfilenamegrfc3875eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125818hkeywordsÇcwwwnworld wide webkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateA~–granking#crfcd3875irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.The Common Gateway Interface (CGI) Version 1.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÒThis document specifies a 224-bit one-way hash function, called SHA-224.  SHA-224 is based on SHA-256, but it uses a different initial value and the result is truncated to 224 bits.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej”<hfilenamegrfc3874eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125817hkeywordsÅosecure standardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAGçŸgranking"crfcd3874irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(A 224-bit One-way Hash Function: SHA-224dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThe Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) is a reliable transport protocol operating on top of a connectionless packet network such as IP. It is designed to transport public switched telephone network (PSTN) signaling messages over the connectionless packet network, but is capable of broader applications.

This memo defines the Management Information Base (MIB) module which describes the minimum set of objects needed to manage the implementation of the SCTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Pastor¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelM. Belinchonddatej”\hfilenamegrfc3873eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125816hkeywordsÑdSCTPx$Stream Control Transmission ProtocolcMIBxManagement Information BasekobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDateAKáÏgranking!crfcd3873irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMStream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes a set of managed objects that are used to manage Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP) devices. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Zinman¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Walker¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Jiangddatej”\hfilenamegrfc3872eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-125815hkeywordsÑcmibxmanagement information basedTRIPxTelephony Routing over IPkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDateAKÜgranking crfcd3872irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Management Information Base for Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a list of operational security requirements for the infrastructure of large Internet Service Provider (ISP) IP networks (routers and switches).  A framework is defined for specifying "profiles", which are collections of requirements applicable to certain network topology contexts (all, core-only, edge-only...).  The goal is to provide network operators a clear, concise way of communicating their security requirements to vendors.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuThe MITRE CorporationdnamehG. JonesddatejÀqhfilenamegrfc3871eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125814hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesQopublicationDateAGç]grankingcrfcd3871irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeOperational Security Requirements for Large Internet Service Provider (ISP) IP Network InfrastructuredtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty(This document describes a media type (application/rdf+xml) for use with the Extensible Markup Language (XML) serialization of the Resource Description Framework (RDF).  RDF is a language designed to support the Semantic Web, by facilitating resource description and data exchange on the Web.  RDF provides common structures that can be used for interoperable data exchange and follows the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) design principles of interoperability, evolution, and decentralization.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkAaronSw.comdnameiA. Swartzddatej”<hfilenamegrfc3870eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125813hkeywordsÜcxmlxextensible markup languagedmimex%multipurpose internet mail extensionscrdfxresource description frameworkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAGçWgrankingcrfcd3870irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+application/rdf+xml Media Type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document discusses IAB concerns that ongoing research is needed to further the evolution of the Internet infrastructure, and that consistent, sufficient non-commercial funding is needed to enable such research.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamekR. Atkinson¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boardddatej—ªhfilenamegrfc3869eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125812hkeywordsÉxinternet architecture boardwinternet infrastructurevnon-commercial fundingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA+÷ùgrankingcrfcd3869irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexJIAB Concerns and Recommendations Regarding Internet Research and EvolutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis document defines a protocol for the transport of any Signalling Connection Control Part-User signalling over IP using the Stream Control Transmission Protocol.  The protocol is designed to be modular and symmetric, to allow it to work in diverse architectures, such as a Signalling Gateway to IP Signalling Endpoint architecture as well as a peer-to-peer IP Signalling Endpoint architecture. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationuSignatus TechnologiesdnamemG. Sidebottom¢kaffiliationlSiemens n.v.dnamehL. Coene¢kaffiliationlSiemens n.v.dnamejG. Verwimp¢kaffiliationgTekelecdnameiJ. Keller¢kaffiliationsOpenSS7 CorporationdnamekB. Bidulockddatej”\hfilenamegrfc3868eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125811hkeywordsàcSUAx7Signaling Connection Control Part User Adaptation Layerdsctpx$stream control transmission protocolgmodularisymmetricrsignalling gatewayx signalling endpoint architecturekobsoletedByÄepagesÉopublicationDateAe—ögrankingcrfcd3868irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Signalling Connection Control Part User Adaptation Layer (SUA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThe Internet Open Trading Protocol (IOTP) provides a data exchange format for trading purposes while integrating existing pure payment protocols seamlessly. This motivates the multiple layered system architecture which consists of at least some generic IOTP application core and multiple specific payment modules.

This document addresses a common interface between the IOTP application core and the payment modules, enabling the interoperability between these kinds of modules. Furthermore, such an interface provides the foundations for a plug-in-mechanism in actual implementations of IOTP application cores.

Such interfaces exist at the Consumers', the Merchants' and the Payment Handlers' installations connecting the IOTP application core and the payment software components/legacy systems. This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgHitachidnamelY. Kawatsura¢kaffiliationrTechnoinfo ServicednameiM. Hiroya¢kaffiliationkAtos OrigindnamekH. Beykirchddatej—ehfilenamegrfc3867eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125810hkeywordsÇgmodulestdata format exchangekobsoletedByÄepagesjopublicationDateAå∑grankingcrfcd3867irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Payment Application Programmers Interface (API) for v1.0 Internet Open Trading Protocol (IOTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHIt is often desirable to be able to indicate the natural language associated with values held in a directory and to be able to query the directory for values which fulfill the user's language needs.  This document details the use of Language Tags and Ranges in the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej“Rhfilenamegrfc3866eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125809hkeywordsÉdLDAPx%lightweight directory access protocolgserverskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@ˇègrankingcrfcd3866irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLLanguage Tags and Ranges in the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstrac