series£gacronymcbcpfnumberZetotaletitlex1Registration Procedures for Message Header FieldsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9110∂habstractyThis memo specifies the Common Profile for Presence (CPP) Presence Information Data Format (PIDF) as a common presence data format for CPP-compliant Presence protocols, and also defines a new media type "application/pidf+xml" to represent the XML MIME entity for PIDF. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationgFujitsudnameiH. Sugano¢kaffiliationgFujitsudnamekS. Fujimoto¢kaffiliationlNine by NinednamehG. Klyne¢kaffiliationjVisionTechdnamejA. Bateman¢kaffiliationeInteldnamegW. Carr¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej—ªhfilenamegrfc3863eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimppdfullx.impp - Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex'Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125806hkeywordsÖx'instant messaging and presence protocolccppxcommon profile for presencetpresence data formattapplication/pidf+xmlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA"¢`grankingcrfcd3863irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Presence Information Data Format (PIDF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∑This memo defines the MIME content type 'Message/CPIM', a message format for protocols that conform to the Common Profile for Instant Messaging (CPIM) specification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlNine by NinednamehG. Klyne¢kaffiliationpIHTFP ConsultingdnameiD. Atkinsddatej—∫hfilenamegrfc3862eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimppdfullx.impp - Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex'Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125805hkeywordsÇx.instant messaging and common presence protocollmessage/cpimkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA"°˘grankingcrfcd3862irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Common Presence and Instant Messaging (CPIM): Message FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[Presence and instant messaging are defined in RFC 2778.  The Common Profiles for Presence and Instant Messaging define two Universal Resource Identifier (URI) schemes: 'im' for INSTANT INBOXes and 'pres' for PRESENTITIES.  This document provides guidance for locating the resources associated with URIs that employ these schemes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej—∫hfilenamegrfc3861eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimppdfullx.impp - Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex'Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125804hkeywordsácurigschemesxuniversal resource identifierdimppx'instant messaging and presence protocoljpresentityminstant inboxkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA"°πgrankingcrfcd3861irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Address Resolution for Instant Messaging and PresencedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractybAt the time this document was written, numerous instant messaging protocols were in use, and little interoperability between services based on these protocols has been achieved.  This specification defines common semantics and data formats for instant messaging to facilitate the creation of gateways between instant messaging services. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej—∫hfilenamegrfc3860eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimppdfullx.impp - Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex'Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125803hkeywordsÖdCPIMx$Common Profile for Instant Messagingldata formatsisemanticsqinstant messagingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA"°Egrankingcrfcd3860irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Common Profile for Instant Messaging (CPIM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàAt the time this document was written, numerous presence protocols were in use (largely as components of commercial instant messaging services), and little interoperability between services based on these protocols has been achieved.  This specification defines common semantics and data formats for presence to facilitate the creation of gateways between presence services. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej—∫hfilenamegrfc3859eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimppdfullx.impp - Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldnamex'Instant Messaging and Presence Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125802hkeywordsÖcCPPxCommon Profile for Presenceldata formatsisemanticsqinstant messagingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA"°Hgrankingcrfcd3859irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Common Profile for Presence (CPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£Watchers are defined as entities that request (i.e., subscribe to) information about a resource.  There is fairly complex state associated with these subscriptions.  The union of the state for all subscriptions to a particular resource is called the watcher information for that resource.  This state is dynamic, changing as subscribers come and go.  As a result, it is possible, and indeed useful, to subscribe to the watcher information for a particular resource.  In order to enable this, a format is needed to describe the state of watchers on a resource.  This specification describes an Extensible Markup Language (XML) document format for such state. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej—∫hfilenamegrfc3858eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125801hkeywordsÇxExtensible Markup LanguagecxmlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA4ü\grankingcrfcd3858irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHAn Extensible Markup Language (XML) Based Format for Watcher InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document defines the watcher information template-package for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) event framework.  Watcher information refers to the set of users subscribed to a particular resource within a particular event package.  Watcher information changes dynamically as users subscribe, unsubscribe, are approved, or are rejected.  A user can subscribe to this information, and therefore learn about changes to it.  This event package is a template-package because it can be applied to any event package, including itself. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej…:hfilenamegrfc3857eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125800hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA4ügrankingcrfcd3857irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVA Watcher Information Event Template-Package for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document describes the usage of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for subscriptions and notifications of presence.  Presence is defined as the willingness and ability of a user to communicate with other users on the network.  Historically, presence has been limited to "on-line" and "off-line" indicators; the notion of presence here is broader.  Subscriptions and notifications of presence are supported by defining an event package within the general SIP event notification framework.  This protocol is also compliant with the Common Presence Profile (CPP) framework. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej—∫hfilenamegrfc3856eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsimpledfullxEsimple - SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdnamex<SIP for Instant Messaging and Presence Leveraging Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125799hkeywordsÅxsubscription notificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA4ûgrankingcrfcd3856irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBA Presence Event Package for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyThis document describes protocol options for conveying objects that have been protected using the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) and Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) version 3.1 over an X.400 message transfer system. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIMCdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationdIECAdnamejC. Bonattiddatej“Ehfilenamegrfc3855eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125798hkeywordsÖccmsx$cryptographic message syntax messagedMIMEx%Multipurpose Internet Mail ExtensionsfsecurekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateADXgrankingcrfcd3855irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSTransporting Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Objects in X.400dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxºThis document describes a protocol for adding cryptographic signature and encryption services to X.400 content with Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIMCdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationdIECAdnamejC. Bonatti¢kaffiliationcFFIdnamehA. Eggenddatej“Rhfilenamegrfc3854eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125797hkeywordsÖdMIMEfsecurex%Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensionsjencryptionwcryptographic signaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateACùgrankingcrfcd3854irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQSecuring X.400 Content with Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRFC 3261 currently specifies 3DES as the mandatory-to-implement ciphersuite for implementations of S/MIME in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  This document updates the normative guidance of RFC 3261 to require the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) for S/MIME. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Petersonddatej“Rhfilenamegrfc3853eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125796hkeywordsäcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolcAESxAdvanced Encryption Standardqapplication-layerkapplicationelayerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@ˇ8grankingcrfcd3853irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_S/MIME Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Requirement for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxµThis document describes the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  This syntax is used to digitally sign, digest, authenticate, or encrypt arbitrary message content. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamehTim Polkdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej“Rhfilenamegrfc3852eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125795hkeywordsÜcCMSxCryptographic Message Syntaxndigitally signlauthenticategencryptxarbitrary message contentkobsoletedByÅd5652epages8opublicationDate@ˇºgrankingcrfcd3852irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5083d4853∂habstractyóThis document defines Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) version 3.1.  S/MIME provides a consistent way to send and receive secure MIME data.  Digital signatures provide authentication, message integrity, and non-repudiation with proof of origin.  Encryption provides data confidentiality.  Compression can be used to reduce data size.  This document obsoletes RFC 2633. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamekB. Ramsdellddatej“Rhfilenamegrfc3851eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125794hkeywordsÉfsecurex%multipurpose internet mail extensionsjencryptionkobsoletedByÅd5751epages$opublicationDate@ˇDgrankingcrfcd3851irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWSecure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Version 3.1 Message SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document specifies conventions for X.509 certificate usage by Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) agents.  S/MIME provides a method to send and receive secure MIME messages, and certificates are an integral part of S/MIME agent processing.  S/MIME agents validate certificates as described in RFC 3280, the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and CRL Profile.  S/MIME agents must meet the certificate processing requirements in this document as well as those in RFC 3280. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnSendmail, Inc.dnamekB. Ramsdellddatej“Qhfilenamegrfc3850eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125793hkeywordsÜex.509jencryptionkcertificatedmimex%multipurpose internet mail extensionsfsecurekobsoletedByÅd5750epagesopublicationDate@ˇ␀Ãgranking
crfcd3850irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSecure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) Version 3.1 Certificate HandlingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTo reduce the likelihood of conflict and confusion when relating documented examples to deployed systems, an IPv6 unicast address prefix is reserved for use in examples in RFCs, books, documentation, and the like.  Since site-local and link-local unicast addresses have special meaning in IPv6, these addresses cannot be used in many example situations.  The document describes the use of the IPv6 address prefix 2001:DB8::/32 as a reserved prefix for use in documentation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgTelstradnameiG. Huston¢kaffiliationeAPNICdnamegA. Lord¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehP. Smithddatej“Qhfilenamegrfc3849eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125792hkeywordsÉgunicastjsite-localjlink-localkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateACagranking	crfcd3849irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.IPv6 Address Prefix Reserved for DocumentationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9637∂habstractx¬This registers seven new mail transmission types (ESMTPA, ESMTPS, ESMTPSA, LMTP, LMTPA, LMTPS, LMTPSA) for use in the "with" clause of a Received header in an Internet message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiC. Newmanddatej“Qhfilenamegrfc3848eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125791hkeywordsÇdsmtpxsimple mail transfer protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA≤grankingcrfcd3848irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.ESMTP and LMTP Transmission Types RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis document describes a mechanism for a restarting router to signal to its neighbors that it is restarting, allowing them to reestablish their adjacencies without cycling through the down state, while still correctly initiating database synchronization.  This document additionally describes a mechanism for a restarting router to determine when it has achieved LSP database synchronization with its neighbors and a mechanism to optimize LSP database synchronization, while minimizing transient routing disruption when a router starts.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Shand¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekL. Ginsbergddatej“Qhfilenamegrfc3847eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125790hkeywordsÉxLSP database synchronizationxtransient routing disruptionxdatabase synchronizationkobsoletedByÅd5306epagesopublicationDate@ÌøÕgrankingcrfcd3847irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHRestart Signaling for Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyOWhen a mobile node moves between two foreign networks, it has to be re-authenticated.  If the home network has both multiple Authentication Authorization and Accounting (AAA) servers and Home Agents (HAs) in use, the Home AAA server may not have sufficient information to process the re-authentication correctly (i.e., to ensure that the same HA continues to be used).  This document defines a Mobile IP extension that carries identities for the Home AAA and HA servers in the form of Network Access Identifiers (NAIs).  The extension allows a Home Agent to pass its identity (and that of the Home AAA server) to the mobile node, which can then pass it on to the local AAA server when changing its point of attachment.  This extension may also be used in other situations requiring communication of a NAI between Mobile IP nodes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkipUnpluggeddnamelF. Johansson¢kaffiliationjBytemobilednamelT. Johanssonddatej“Ñhfilenamegrfc3846eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip4dfullxmip4 - Mobility for IPv4dnameqMobility for IPv4dtypebwgbidjdoc-125789hkeywordsÜcNAIxNetwork Access Identifiersqinternet protocolohome aaa serveriha serverkhome agentskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@„Lîgrankingcrfcd3846irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Mobile IPv4 Extension for Carrying Network Access IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¬This document redefines the wire format of the "Type Bit Map" field in the DNS NextSECure (NSEC) resource record RDATA format to cover the full resource record (RR) type space. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Schlyterddatej—πhfilenamegrfc3845eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125788hkeywordsâfdnsseclDNS Securitybrroresource recordjDNS-SECEXTcdnsnauthenticationdnsecjnextsecurekobsoletedByÉd4033d4034d4035epagesopublicationDateA"†Ägrankingcrfcd3845irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4DNS Security (DNSSEC) NextSECure (NSEC) RDATA FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyDThis Informational document records the history of discussions in the Problem WG during 2003 of how to resolve the problems described in the IETF Problem Statement.  It decomposes each of the problems described into a few areas for improvement and categorizes them as either problems affecting the routine processes used to create standards or problems affecting the fundamental structure and practices of the IETF.  Expeditious and non-disruptive solutions are proposed for the problems affecting routine processes.  The document also lists suggested ways to handle the development of solutions for the structure and practices problems proposed in IETF discussions.  Neither the working group nor the wider IETF has reached consensus on a recommendation for any of the proposals.  This document therefore has no alternative but to suggest that the search for structure and practices solutions be handed back to the control of the IESG.  While there was working group consensus on the processes for short-term and medium term improvements, there was no working group consensus on the proposals for longer-term improvements.  This document therefore includes longer-term improvement proposals only as a matter of record; they must not be regarded as recommendations from the working group.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiE. Davies¢kaffiliationxWissenschaftszentrum BerlindnamejJ. Hofmannddatej—πhfilenamegrfc3844eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgproblemdfullxproblem - Problem StatementdnameqProblem Statementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125787hkeywordsÑdietfgprocessgproblemhanalysiskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA/ ñgrankingcrfcd3844irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIETF Problem Resolution ProcessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]The original RObust Header Compression (ROHC) RFC (RFC 3095) defines a framework for header compression, along with compression protocols (profiles) for IP/UDP/RTP, IP/ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload), IP/UDP, and also a profile for uncompressed packet streams.  However, no profile was defined for compression of IP only, which has been identified as a missing piece in RFC 3095.  This document defines a ROHC compression profile for IP, similar to the IP/UDP profile defined by RFC 3095, but simplified to exclude UDP, and enhanced to compress IP header chains of arbitrary length. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Pelletierddatej“Éhfilenamegrfc3843eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125786hkeywordsÉdROHCxRObust Header Compressionucompression protocolskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@„LFgrankingcrfcd3843irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>RObust Header Compression (ROHC): A Compression Profile for IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4815∂habstractx∆This document describes a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) event package to carry message waiting status and message summaries from a messaging system to an interested User Agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegR. Mahyddatej—πhfilenamegrfc3842eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125785hkeywordsÑcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolvmessage waiting statusomessage summarykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA4ù*grankingcrfcd3842irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhA Message Summary and Message Waiting Indication Event Package for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a set of extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) which allow a caller to express preferences about request handling in servers.  These preferences include the ability to select which Uniform Resource Identifiers (URI) a request gets routed to, and to specify certain request handling directives in proxies and redirect servers.  It does so by defining three new request header fields, Accept-Contact, Reject-Contact, and Request-Disposition, which specify the caller's preferences. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Kyzivatddatej—∑hfilenamegrfc3841eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125784hkeywordsácSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolxUniform Resource IdentifierscURInAccept-ContactnReject-ContactsRequest-DispositionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA4ú•grankingcrfcd3841irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Caller Preferences for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty"This specification defines mechanisms by which a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) user agent can convey its capabilities and characteristics to other user agents and to the registrar for its domain.  This information is conveyed as parameters of the Contact header field. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejP. Kyzivatddatej—∂hfilenamegrfc3840eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125783hkeywordsÖjUser Agentbuatcontact header fieldcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDateA4ú.granking␀crfcd3840irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKIndicating User Agent Capabilities in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx»This document serves to register and document the standard MIME types associated with the 3GPP multimedia file format, which is part of the family based on the ISO Media File Format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoAlexey Melnikovdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsNokia Mobile PhonesdnamekR. Castagno¢kaffiliationtApple Computer, Inc.dnameiD. Singerddatej“Qhfilenamegrfc3839eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125782hkeywordsÉsstandard MIME typesx3GPP multimedia file formatuISO Media File FormatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@ÙKágrankingˇcrfcd3839irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVMIME Type Registrations for 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Multimedia filesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6381∂habstractxÏThis document describes policy, authorization, and enforcement requirements for the selection of the services to be applied to a given Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) flow.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiA. Barbir¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejO. Batuner¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamegA. Beck¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamegT. Chan¢kaffiliationxPurple Streak DevelopmentdnamehH. Ormanddatej—∂hfilenamegrfc3838eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125781hkeywordsÅiopes flowkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA5ígranking˛crfcd3838irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Policy, Authorization, and Enforcement Requirements of the Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThe document investigates the security threats associated with the Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) and discusses the effects of security threats on the underlying architecture.  The main goal of this document is threat discovery and analysis.  The document does not specify or recommend any solutions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiA. Barbir¢kaffiliationvIndependent consultantdnamejO. Batuner¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekB. Srinivas¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Lucent TechnologiesdnamejM. Hofmann¢kaffiliationxPurple Streak DevelopmentdnamehH. Ormanddatej—∂hfilenamegrfc3837eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125780hkeywordsÇpthreat discoveryothreat analysiskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA5Ygranking˝crfcd3837irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBSecurity Threats and Risks for Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis document specifies the requirements that the OPES (Open Pluggable Edge Services) callout protocol must satisfy in order to support the remote execution of OPES services.  The requirements are intended to help evaluate possible protocol candidates, as well as to guide the development of such protocols.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamegA. Beck¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamejM. Hofmann¢kaffiliationxPurple Streak DevelopmentdnamehH. Orman¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationxJohns Hopkins UniversitydnameiA. Terzisddatej—∂hfilenamegrfc3836eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125779hkeywordsÉpcallout protocolpremote executionmOPES serviceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA5‹granking¸crfcd3836irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFRequirements for Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) Callout ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines an architecture that enables the creation of an application service in which a data provider, a data consumer, and zero or more application entities cooperatively implement a data stream service.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiA. Barbir¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehR. Penno¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamegR. Chen¢kaffiliationxBell Labs/Lucent TechnologiesdnamejM. Hofmann¢kaffiliationxPurple Streak DevelopmentdnamehH. Ormanddatej—∂hfilenamegrfc3835eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125778hkeywordsÑsapplication servicesdata stream servicemdata consumerxdata dispatcher architecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA5£granking˚crfcd3835irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7An Architecture for Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis memo makes recommendations for software that automatically responds to incoming electronic mail messages, including "out of the office" or "vacation" response generators, mail filtering software, email-based information services, and other automatic responders.  The purpose of these recommendations is to discourage undesirable behavior which is caused or aggravated by such software, to encourage uniform behavior (where appropriate) among automatic mail responders, and to clear up some sources of confusion among implementors of automatic email responders. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TennesseednamehK. Mooreddatej—πhfilenamegrfc3834eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125777hkeywordsÅxautomatic mail responderskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA-$granking˙crfcd3834irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Recommendations for Automatic Responses to Electronic MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5436∂habstractyÅAlthough the DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) have been under development for most of the last decade, the IETF has never written down the specific set of threats against which DNSSEC is designed to protect.  Among other drawbacks, this cart-before-the-horse situation has made it difficult to determine whether DNSSEC meets its design goals, since its design goals are not well specified.  This note attempts to document some of the known threats to the DNS, and, in doing so, attempts to measure to what extent (if any) DNSSEC is a useful tool in defending against these threats.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpIHTFP ConsultingdnameiD. Atkins¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austeinddatej—πhfilenamegrfc3833eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125776hkeywordsÉodata disclosurehsecuritynauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA+’¸granking˘crfcd3833irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Threat Analysis of the Domain Name System (DNS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRemote service discovery refers to discovering desired services in given remote (i.e., non-local) DNS domains.  This document describes remote service discovery in the Service Location Protocol (SLP) via DNS SRV.  It defines the DNS SRV Resource Records for SLP Directory Agent services, discusses various issues in using SLP and DNS SRV together for remote service discovery, and gives the steps for discovering desired services in remote DNS domains.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamegW. Zhao¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttman¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelC. Bisdikian¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiW. Jeromeddatej“Qhfilenamegrfc3832eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125775hkeywordsÖcSLPxService Location ProtocolgDNS-SRVrdomain name systemoresource recordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateA	~6granking¯crfcd3832irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKRemote Service Discovery in the Service Location Protocol (SLP) via DNS SRVdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553∂habstractx€This document specifies the way of encapsulating IPv6 packets over Fibre Channel, and the method of forming IPv6 link-local addresses and statelessly autoconfigured addresses on Fibre Channel networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. DeSantiddatej“Phfilenamegrfc3831eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdimssdfullx2imss - Internet and Management Support for Storagednamex+Internet and Management Support for Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125774hkeywordsÉiaddressesjlink-localqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÅd4338epagesopublicationDateA±3granking˜crfcd3831irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Transmission of IPv6 Packets over Fibre ChanneldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis document describes a key management scheme that can be used for real-time applications (both for peer-to-peer communication and group communication).  In particular, its use to support the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol is described in detail.  Security protocols for real-time multimedia applications have started to appear.  This has brought forward the need for a key management solution to support these protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamejE. Carrara¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamekF. Lindholm¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamejM. Naslund¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamejK. Norrmanddatej—∏hfilenamegrfc3830eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125773hkeywordsÑeMIKEYxMultimedia Internet KEYingukey management schemevreal-time applicationskobsoletedByÄepagesBopublicationDateA/ %grankingˆcrfcd3830irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!MIKEY: Multimedia Internet KEYingdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6309d4738∂habstractyiThis document extends the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) bind operation with a mechanism for requesting and returning the authorization identity it establishes.  Specifically, this document defines the Authorization Identity Request and Response controls for use with the Bind operation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnAmerica OnlinednamejR. Weltman¢kaffiliationsPearl Crescent, LLCdnamehM. Smith¢kaffiliation`dnamegM. Wahlddatej“Phfilenamegrfc3829eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125772hkeywordsÅnbind operationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@Ìøpgrankingıcrfcd3829irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Authorization Identity Request and Response ControlsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyyThis document describes the Lightweight User Datagram Protocol (UDP-Lite), which is similar to the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) (RFC 768), but can also serve applications in error-prone network environments that prefer to have partially damaged payloads delivered rather than discarded.  If this feature is not used, UDP-Lite is semantically identical to UDP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationxLulea University of TechnologydnamekL-A. Larzon¢kaffiliationxThe University of ArizonadnamelM. Degermark¢kaffiliationxThe University of ArizonadnamegS. Pink¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurstddatej“Phfilenamegrfc3828eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125771hkeywordsÇhUDP-Litex"Lightweight User Datagram ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@ÙJÒgrankingÙcrfcd3828irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1The Lightweight User Datagram Protocol (UDP-Lite)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6335∂habstractx‚The snoop file format provides a way to store and exchange datalink layer packet traces.  This document describes extensions to this file format to support new media.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnameiK. Sarcarddatej jhfilenamegrfc3827eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125770hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@“&<grankingÛcrfcd3827irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAdditional Snoop Datalink TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’This document describes a symmetric encryption protocol that supplements the protocols described in the User-based Security Model (USM), which is a Security Subsystem for version 3 of the Simple Network Management Protocol for use in the SNMP Architecture.  The symmetric encryption protocol described in this document is based on the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) cipher algorithm used in Cipher FeedBack Mode (CFB), with a key size of 128 bits. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamemU. Blumenthal¢kaffiliationuAndiamo Systems, Inc.dnamehF. Maino¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. McCloghrieddatej“Éhfilenamegrfc3826eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125769hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basex"simple network management protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@“%ßgrankingÚcrfcd3826irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex]The Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Cipher Algorithm in the SNMP User-based Security ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis document specifies a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol Option for the coordinate-based geographic location of the client.  The Location Configuration Information (LCI) includes latitude, longitude, and altitude, with resolution indicators for each.  The reference datum for these values is also included. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polk¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemJ. Schnizlein¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Linsnerddatej“Phfilenamegrfc3825eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-125768hkeywordsÜddhcpx#Dynamic Host Configuration Protocolclcisgeographic locationcLCIx"Location Configuration InformationkobsoletedByÅd6225epagesopublicationDate@ÌøgrankingÒcrfcd3825irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbDynamic Host Configuration Protocol Option for Coordinate-based Location Configuration InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢There are a number of contexts in which telephone numbers are employed by Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) applications, many of which can be addressed by ENUM.  Although SIP was one of the primary applications for which ENUM was created, there is nevertheless a need to define procedures for integrating ENUM with SIP implementations.  This document illustrates how the two protocols might work in concert, and clarifies the authoring and processing of ENUM records for SIP applications.  It also provides guidelines for instances in which ENUM, for whatever reason, cannot be used to resolve a telephone number.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamefH. Liu¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnameeJ. Yu¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamekB. Campbellddatej“Éhfilenamegrfc3824eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125767hkeywordsÇqtelephone recordslapplicationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@·¯Øgrankingcrfcd3824irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Using E.164 numbers with the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty<This document registers the MIME sub-type application/sbml+xml, a media type for SBML, the Systems Biology Markup Language.  SBML is defined by The SBML Team at the California Institute of Technology and interested members of the systems biology community.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgCaltechdnameiB. Kovitzddatej“Çhfilenamegrfc3823eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125766hkeywordsÇxsub-type application/sbml+xmlxsystems biology communitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@€G˙grankingÔcrfcd3823irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>MIME Media Type for the Systems Biology Markup Language (SBML)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxåThis document defines the use of Service Location Protocol version 2 (SLPv2) by Fibre Channel over TCP/IP (FCIP) Entities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx!Computer Network Technology (CNT)dnamekD. Petersonddatej“Ohfilenamegrfc3822eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125765hkeywordsÖdFCIPxFibre Channel over TCP/IPcSLPxService Location Protocolqdynamic discoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@ˇ␀ZgrankingÓcrfcd3822irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcFinding Fibre Channel over TCP/IP (FCIP) Entities Using Service Location Protocol version 2 (SLPv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstracty¥Fibre Channel Over TCP/IP (FCIP) describes mechanisms that allow the interconnection of islands of Fibre Channel storage area networks over IP-based networks to form a unified storage area network in a single Fibre Channel fabric.  FCIP relies on IP-based network services to provide the connectivity between the storage area network islands over local area networks, metropolitan area networks, or wide area networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. Rajagopal¢kaffiliation`dnamelE. Rodriguez¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Weberddatej“Ohfilenamegrfc3821eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125764hkeywordsÖdFCIPxFibre Channel Over TCI/IPustorage area networksqIP-based networksxunified storage area networkkobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDate@˛ˇ£grankingÌcrfcd3821irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Fibre Channel Over TCP/IP (FCIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyﬁThis document forms a certificate profile for Proxy Certificates, based on X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificates as defined in RFC 3280, for use in the Internet.  The term Proxy Certificate is used to describe a certificate that is derived from, and signed by, a normal X.509 Public Key End Entity Certificate or by another Proxy Certificate for the purpose of providing restricted proxying and delegation within a PKI based authentication system. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcANLdnameiS. Tuecke¢kaffiliationdNCSAdnamehV. Welch¢kaffiliationcANLdnameiD. Engert¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekL. Pearlman¢kaffiliationdLBNLdnamekM. Thompsonddatej“Çhfilenamegrfc3820eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125763hkeywordsàeX.509cPKIxPublic Key Infrastructurenauthenticationtsecurity credentialsurestricted delegationmsingle-signontdelegation of rightskobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDate@„K—grankingÏcrfcd3820irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) Proxy Certificate ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document provides advice to the designers of digital communication equipment, link-layer protocols, and packet-switched local networks (collectively referred to as subnetworks), who wish to support the Internet protocols but may be unfamiliar with the Internet architecture and the implications of their design choices on the performance and efficiency of the Internet.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areagauthorsâ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamegP. Karn¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationnMotorola, Inc.dnamekD. Grossman¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnameiR. Ludwig¢kaffiliationfNovelldnamejJ. Mahdavi¢kaffiliationx%Sun Microsystems Laboratories, EuropednamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamehJ. Touch¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegL. Woodddatej“Nhfilenamegrfc3819eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpilcdfullx7pilc - Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdnamex0Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125762hkeywordsÉxdigital communication equipmenttlink-layer protocolsxpacket-switched local networkskobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDate@ÙI˝grankingÎcrfcd3819irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberYetotaletitlex(Advice for Internet Subnetwork DesignersdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9599∑habstractyThe charter of the Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) Extensions working group (pppext) includes the responsibility to "actively advance PPP's most useful extensions to full standard, while defending against further enhancements of questionable value." In support of that charter, the allocation of PPP protocol and other assigned numbers will no longer be "first come first served." This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqRhyolite SoftwarednamekV. Schryverddatej“Çhfilenamegrfc3818eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125761hkeywordsÇcPPPwPoint-to-Point ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@◊2¶grankingÍcrfcd3818irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberXetotaletitlex9IANA Considerations for the Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^The Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) provides a standard method for transporting multi-protocol datagrams over point-to-point links.  Layer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP), facilitates the tunneling of PPP packets across an intervening packet-switched network.  And yet a third protocol, PPP over Ethernet (PPPoE) describes how to build PPP sessions and to encapsulate PPP packets over Ethernet.  L2TP Active Discovery Relay for PPPoE describes a method to relay Active Discovery and Service Selection functionality from PPPoE over the reliable control channel within L2TP.  Two new L2TP control message types and associated PPPoE-specific Attribute Value Pairs (AVPs) for L2TP are defined.  This relay mechanism provides enhanced integration of a specific feature in the PPPoE tunneling protocol with L2TP.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmcisco SystemsdnamekW. Townsley¢kaffiliationoAOL Time WarnerdnamekR. da Silvaddatej“Çhfilenamegrfc3817eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125760hkeywordsÅnpoint-to-pointkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@◊23grankingÈcrfcd3817irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVLayer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) Active Discovery Relay for PPP over Ethernet (PPPoE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes a set of managed objects that allow monitoring of running instances of RObust Header Compression (ROHC).  The managed objects defined in this memo are grouped into three MIB modules.  The ROHC-MIB module defines managed objects shared by all ROHC profiles, the ROHC-UNCOMPRESSED-MIB module defines managed objects specific to the ROHC uncompressed profile, the ROHC-RTP-MIB module defines managed objects specific to the ROHC RTP (Real-Time Transport Protocol) profile, the ROHC UDP (User Datagram Protocol) profile, the ROHC ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload) profile, and the ROHC LLA (Link Layer Assisted) profile. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejJ. Quittek¢kaffiliationcNECdnamenM. Stiemerling¢kaffiliationwUniversity of KarlsruhednamenH. Hartensteinddatej“Çhfilenamegrfc3816eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125759hkeywordsÑdROHCxRobust Header Compressioncmibxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDate@«•⁄grankingËcrfcd3816irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCDefinitions of Managed Objects for RObust Header Compression (ROHC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for the Multiprotocol Label Switching, Label Distribution Protocol (LDP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxMarconi Communications, Inc.dnamelJ. Cucchiara¢kaffiliationkipUnpluggeddnamelH. Sjostrand¢kaffiliationxMarconi Communications, Inc.dnamejJ. Lucianiddatej“Åhfilenamegrfc3815eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125758hkeywordsÑdMPLSxMultiprotocol Label SwitchingcLDPxLabel Distribution ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesjopublicationDate@◊1˘grankingÁcrfcd3815irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexnDefinitions of Managed Objects for the Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS), Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for defining, configuring, and monitoring Forwarding Equivalence Class (FEC) to Next Hop Label Forwarding Entry (NHLFE) mappings and corresponding actions for use with Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationnBloomberg L.P.dnamemC. Srinivasan¢kaffiliationvForce10 Networks, Inc.dnamenA. Viswanathanddatej“Åhfilenamegrfc3814eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125757hkeywordsàdMPLSxMultiprotocol Label SwitchingcMIBxManagement Information BasecFECxForwarding Equivalence ClasseNHLFExNext Hop Label Forwarding EntrykobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate@◊1ÜgrankingÊcrfcd3814irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexïMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Forwarding Equivalence Class To Next Hop Label Forwarding Entry (FEC-To-NHLFE) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This memo defines an portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects to configure and/or monitor a Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switching Router (LSR). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnBloomberg L.P.dnamemC. Srinivasan¢kaffiliationvForce10 Networks, Inc.dnamenA. Viswanathan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeauddatej ghfilenamegrfc3813eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125756hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages<opublicationDate@◊1grankingÂcrfcd3813irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switching Router (LSR) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) based traffic engineering (TE). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnBloomberg L.P.dnamemC. Srinivasan¢kaffiliationvForce10 Networks, Inc.dnamenA. Viswanathan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeauddatej“Åhfilenamegrfc3812eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125755hkeywordsÜdMPLSxMultiprotocol Label SwitchingbTEsTraffic EngineeringcMIBxManagement Information BasekobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDate@◊0ágranking‰crfcd3812irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering (TE) Management Information Base (MIB)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis memo defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module which contains Textual Conventions to represent commonly used Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) management information.  The intent is that these TEXTUAL CONVENTIONS (TCs) will be imported and used in MPLS related MIB modules that would otherwise define their own representations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiT. Nadeau¢kaffiliationxMarconi Communications, Inc.dnamelJ. Cucchiaraddatej“Ähfilenamegrfc3811eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125754hkeywordsÖsTextual ConventionscTCsxMultiprotocol Label SwitchingdMPLSxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@–Ø∆granking„crfcd3811irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Definitions of Textual Conventions (TCs) for Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) ManagementdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7274∂habstractyEThis document updates RFC 2710, and it specifies Version 2 of the ulticast Listener Discovery Protocol (MLDv2).  MLD is used by an IPv6 router to discover the presence of multicast listeners on directly attached links, and to discover which multicast addresses are of interest to those neighboring nodes.  MLDv2 is designed to be interoperable with MLDv1.  MLDv2 adds the ability for a node to report interest in listening to packets with a particular multicast address only from specific source addresses or from all sources except for specific source addresses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdLIP6dnamegR. Vida¢kaffiliationdLIP6dnamehL. Costaddatej“Ähfilenamegrfc3810eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-125753hkeywordsÜxMulticast Listener Discoverycssmpsource filteringdigmppgroup managementcmldkobsoletedByÅd9777epages>opublicationDate@«•_granking‚crfcd3810irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Multicast Listener Discovery Version 2 (MLDv2) for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4604∂habstracty˚This document describes generic requirements for Provider Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPN).  The requirements are categorized into service requirements, provider requirements and engineering requirements.  These requirements are not specific to any particular type of PPVPN technology, but rather apply to all PPVPN technologies.  All PPVPN technologies are expected to meet the umbrella set of requirements described in this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelA. Nagarajanddatej“Ähfilenamegrfc3809eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymel3vpndfullx(l3vpn - Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdnamex Layer 3 Virtual Private Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-125752hkeywordsÅsservice engineeringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@«§Ògranking·crfcd3809irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNGeneric Requirements for Provider Provisioned Virtual Private Networks (PPVPN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyﬂThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  This IANA Charset MIB is now an IANA registry.  In particular, a single textual convention 'IANACharset' is defined that may be used to specify charset labels in MIB objects. 'IANACharset' was extracted from Printer MIB v2 (RFC 3805). 'IANACharset' was originally defined (and mis-named) as 'CodedCharSet' in Printer MIB v1 (RFC 1759).  A tool has been written in C, that may be used by IANA to regenerate this IANA Charset MIB, when future charsets are registered in accordance with the IANA Charset Registration Procedures (RFC 2978).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed FreedgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjHigh NorthdnamekI. McDonaldddatej“Ähfilenamegrfc3808eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125751hkeywordsÇxmanagement information basekIANACharsetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@–Øugranking‡crfcd3808irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlepIANA Charset MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇThis document defines a mechanism for the backhauling of V5.2 messages over IP using the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  This protocol may be used between a Signaling Gateway (SG) and a Media Gateway controller (MGC).  It is assumed that the SG receives V5.2 signaling over a standard V5.2 interface.  This document builds on the ISDN User Adaptation Layer Protocol (RFC 3057).  It defines all necessary extensions to the IUA Protocol needed for the V5UA protocol implementation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekE. Weilandt¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameoN. Khanchandani¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamefS. Raoddatej“hfilenamegrfc3807eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125750hkeywordsíbv5dv5.1dv5.2kbackhaulingdimapdsctpdisdnnaccess networkfc-pathic-channelcefaxenvelope function addresselapv5dpstnev5ptmcmgcrgateway controllerggatewaykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@“%Mgrankingﬂcrfcd3807irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!V5.2-User Adaptation Layer (V5UA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyLThis document defines a MIB module for the management of printer finishing device subunits.  The finishing device subunits applicable to this MIB are an integral part of the Printer System.  This MIB applies only to a Finisher Device that is connected to a Printer System.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed FreedgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHitachi Printing SolutionsdnamejR. Bergman¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehH. Lewis¢kaffiliationoHigh North Inc.dnamekI. McDonaldddatej“hfilenamegrfc3806eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhprintmibdfullvprintmib - Printer MIBdnamekPrinter MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125749hkeywordsÇhfinisherdsnmpkobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDate@–Ø%grankingﬁcrfcd3806irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitleuPrinter Finishing MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˛This document provides definitions of models and manageable objects for printing environments.  The objects included in this MIB apply to physical, as well as logical entities within a printing device.  This document obsoletes RFC 1759. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxHitachi Printing SolutionsdnamejR. Bergman¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehH. Lewis¢kaffiliationoHigh North Inc.dnamekI. McDonaldddatej“hfilenamegrfc3805eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhprintmibdfullvprintmib - Printer MIBdnamekPrinter MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125748hkeywordsÖiPrint-MIBgPrinterxManagement Information BasedsnmpjmanagementkobsoletedByÄepages´opublicationDate@–Æ”granking›crfcd3805irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlenPrinter MIB v2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document lists the various Voice Profile for Internet Mail (VPIM) email address formats that are currently in common use and defines several new address formats for special case usage.  Requirements are imposed on the formats of addresses used in VPIM submission mode. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsonsddatej“hfilenamegrfc3804eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-125747hkeywordsÉdVPIMxVoice Profile for Internet MailgformatskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@€Gúgranking‹crfcd3804irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Voice Profile for Internet Mail (VPIM) AddressingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxßThis document describes the MIME header Content-Duration that is intended for use with any time varying media content (typically audio/* or video/*). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuil¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsonsddatej“hfilenamegrfc3803eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-125746hkeywordsÖhCONT-DURpContent Durationx%multipurpose internet mail extensionsdtimeemediakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@€Ggranking€crfcd3803irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Content Duration MIME Header DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxËThis document describes the registration of the MIME sub-type audio/32KADPCM Adaptive Differential Pulse Code Modulation for toll quality audio.  This audio encoding is defined by the ITU-T in Recommendation G.726. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuil¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsonsddatej“~hfilenamegrfc3802eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-125745hkeywordsÑjMIME-ADPCMx%multipurpose internet mail extensionsx+Adaptive Differential Pulse Code ModulationeaudiokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@€Fùgranking⁄crfcd3802irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmToll Quality Voice - 32 kbit/s Adaptive Differential Pulse Code Modulation (ADPCM) MIME Sub-type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document specifies a restricted profile of the Internet multimedia messaging protocols for use between voice processing server platforms.  The profile is referred to as the Voice Profile for Internet Mail (VPIM) in this document.  These platforms have historically been special-purpose computers and often do not have the same facilities normally associated with a traditional Internet Email-capable computer.  As a result, VPIM also specifies additional functionality, as it is needed.  This profile is intended to specify the minimum common set of features to allow interworking between conforming systems.  This document obsoletes RFC 2421 and describes version 2 of the profile with greater precision.  No protocol changes were made in this revision.  A list of changes from RFC 2421 are noted in Appendix F.  Appendix A summarizes the protocol profiles of this version of VPIM. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuil¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsonsddatej“~hfilenamegrfc3801eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-125744hkeywordsÑhMIME-VP2dvpimxVoice Profile for Internet MailimessagingkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDate@€F#grankingŸcrfcd3801irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Voice Profile for Internet Mail - version 2 (VPIMv2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a MIME content-type that may be used by a mail user agent (MUA) or electronic mail gateway to report the disposition of a message after it has been successfully delivered to a recipient.  This content-type is intended to be machine-processable.  Additional message headers are also defined to permit Message Disposition Notifications (MDNs) to be requested by the sender of a message.  The purpose is to extend Internet Mail to support functionality often found in other messaging systems, such as X.400 and the proprietary "LAN-based" systems, and often referred to as "read receipts," "acknowledgements", or "receipt notifications." The intention is to do this while respecting privacy concerns, which have often been expressed when such functions have been discussed in the past.  Because many messages are sent between the Internet and other messaging systems (such as X.400 or the proprietary "LAN-based" systems), the MDN protocol is designed to be useful in a multi-protocol messaging environment.  To this end, the protocol described in this memo provides for the carriage of "foreign" addresses, in addition to those normally used in Internet Mail.  Additional attributes may also be defined to support "tunneling" of foreign notifications through Internet Mail. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqAT&T LaboratoriesdnameiT. Hansen¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuilddatej“Õhfilenamegrfc3798eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125743hkeywordsÜgEMF-MDNcMDNx!Message Disposition Notificationsjmedia-typedMIMEx%multipurpose internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÅd8098epagesopublicationDate@§êgranking÷crfcd3798irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Message Disposition NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5337d6533∂habstractyãThis document describes a method for making random selections in such a way that the unbiased nature of the choice is publicly verifiable.  As an example, the selection of the voting members of the IETF Nominations Committee (NomCom) from the pool of eligible volunteers is used.  Similar techniques would be applicable to other cases.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej“~hfilenamegrfc3797eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125742hkeywordsÇxInternet Engineering Task ForcedIETFkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@“$¬granking’crfcd3797irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCPublicly Verifiable Nominations Committee (NomCom) Random SelectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‘This document seeks to record all usage of IPv4 addresses in currently deployed IETF Operations & Management Area accepted standards.  In order to successfully transition from an all IPv4 Internet to an all IPv6 Internet, many interim steps will be taken.  One of these steps is the evolution of current protocols that have IPv4 dependencies.  It is hoped that these protocols (and their implementations) will be redesigned to be network address independent, but failing that will at least dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  To this end, all Standards (Full, Draft, and Proposed), as well as Experimental RFCs, will be surveyed and any dependencies will be documented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, II¢kaffiliationxOstfold University CollegednamelA. Bergstromddatej bhfilenamegrfc3796eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125741hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate@«≠#granking‘crfcd3796irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Survey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Operations & Management Area Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyπThis document describes IPv4 addressing dependencies in an attempt to clarify the necessary steps in re-designing and re-implementing specifications to become network address independent, or at least, to dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  This transition requires several interim steps, one of them being the evolution of current IPv4 dependent specifications to a format independent of the type of IP addressing schema used.  Hence, it is hoped that specifications will be re-designed and re-implemented to become network address independent, or at least to dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  To achieve that step, it is necessary to survey and document all IPv4 dependencies experienced by current standards (Full, Draft, and Proposed) as well as Experimental RFCs.  Hence, this document describes IPv4 addressing dependencies that deployed IETF Application Area documented Standards may experience.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdFCCNdnamehR. Sofia¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, IIddatej `hfilenamegrfc3795eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125740hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDate@«¨‚granking”crfcd3795irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexoSurvey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Application Area Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»This document seeks to document all usage of IPv4 addresses in currently deployed IETF Transport Area documented standards.  In order to successfully transition from an all IPv4 Internet to an all IPv6 Internet, many interim steps will be taken.  One of these steps is the evolution of current protocols that have IPv4 dependencies.  It is hoped that these protocols (and their implementations) will be redesigned to be network address independent, but failing that will at least dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  To this end, all Standards (Full, Draft, and Proposed) as well as Experimental RFCs will be surveyed and any dependencies will be documented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, II¢kaffiliationxOstfold University CollegednamelA. Bergstromddatej _hfilenamegrfc3794eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125739hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@«¨-granking“crfcd3794irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmSurvey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Transport Area Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≈This document seeks to document all usage of IPv4 addresses in currently deployed IETF Sub-IP Area documented standards.  In order to successfully transition from an all IPv4 Internet to an all IPv6 Internet, many interim steps will be taken.  One of these steps is the evolution of current protocols that have IPv4 dependencies.  It is hoped that these protocols (and their implementations) will be redesigned to be network address independent, but failing that will at least dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  To this end, all Standards (Full, Draft, and Proposed) as well as Experimental RFCs will be surveyed and any dependencies will be documented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, II¢kaffiliationxOstfold University CollegednamelA. Bergstromddatej ^hfilenamegrfc3793eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125738hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@«´πgranking—crfcd3793irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjSurvey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Sub-IP Area Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This document seeks to document all usage of IPv4 addresses in currently deployed IETF Security Area documented standards.  In order to successfully transition from an all IPv4 Internet to an all IPv6 Internet, many interim steps will be taken.  One of these steps is the evolution of current protocols that have IPv4 dependencies.  It is hoped that these protocols (and their implementations) will be redesigned to be network address independent, but failing that will at least dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  To this end, all Standards (Full, Draft, and Proposed) as well as Experimental RFCs will be surveyed and any dependencies will be documented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, II¢kaffiliationxOstfold University CollegednamelA. Bergstromddatej ]hfilenamegrfc3792eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125737hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@«´Bgranking–crfcd3792irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlSurvey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Security Area Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–This investigation work seeks to document all usage of IPv4 addresses in currently deployed IETF Routing Area documented standards.  In order to successfully transition from an all IPv4 Internet to an all IPv6 Internet, many interim steps will be taken.  One of these steps is the evolution of current protocols that have IPv4 dependencies.  It is hoped that these protocols (and their implementations) will be redesigned to be network address independent, but failing that will at least dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  To this end, all Standards (Full, Draft, and Proposed) as well as Experimental RFCs will be surveyed and any dependencies will be documented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkConsulinteldnameiC. Olvera¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, IIddatej \hfilenamegrfc3791eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125736hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@«´grankingœcrfcd3791irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkSurvey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Routing Area Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«This document seeks to document all usage of IPv4 addresses in currently deployed IETF Internet Area documented standards.  In order to successfully transition from an all IPv4 Internet to an all IPv6 Internet, many interim steps will be taken.  One of these steps is the evolution of current protocols that have IPv4 dependencies.  It is hoped that these protocols (and their implementations) will be redesigned to be network address independent, but failing that will at least dually support IPv4 and IPv6.  To this end, all Standards (Full, Draft, and Proposed) as well as Experimental RFCs will be surveyed and any dependencies will be documented.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkindependentdnamejC. Mickles¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, IIddatej [hfilenamegrfc3790eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125735hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDate@«™°grankingŒcrfcd3790irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexlSurvey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Internet Area Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyœThis document is a general overview and introduction to the v6ops IETF workgroup project of documenting all usage of IPv4 addresses in IETF standards track and experimental RFCs.  It is broken into seven documents conforming to the current IETF areas.  It also describes the methodology used during documentation, which types of RFCs have been documented, and provides a concatenated summary of results.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnamemP. Nesser, II¢kaffiliationxOstfold University CollegednamelA. Bergstromddatej Zhfilenamegrfc3789eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125734hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate@«™grankingÕcrfcd3789irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexrIntroduction to the Survey of IPv4 Addresses in Currently Deployed IETF Standards Track and Experimental DocumentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~This document discusses how Transport Layer Security (TLS) and IPsec can be used to secure communication for SIGTRAN protocols.  The main goal is to recommend the minimum security means that a SIGTRAN node must implement in order to attain secured communication.  The support of IPsec is mandatory for all nodes running SIGTRAN protocols.  TLS support is optional. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationx"Univ. of Applied Sciences MuensterdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationtEricsson Espana S.A.dnamepJ. Pastor-Balbasddatej“}hfilenamegrfc3788eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125733hkeywordsÉgSIGTRANsSignaling TransporthSecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@“$!grankingÃcrfcd3788irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCSecurity Considerations for Signaling Transport (SIGTRAN) ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document discusses a number of differences between the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol used to route IP traffic as described in RFC 1195 and the protocol as it is deployed today.  These differences are discussed as a service to those implementing, testing, and deploying the IS-IS Protocol to route IP traffic.  A companion document describes the differences between the protocol described in ISO 10589 and current practice.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAxiowave NetworksdnameiJ. Parkerddatej“Ãhfilenamegrfc3787eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125732hkeywordsÅorouting traffickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@∂uxgrankingÀcrfcd3787irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexfRecommendations for Interoperable IP Networks using Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document describes a mechanism that allows a system to originate more than 256 Link State PDU (LSP) fragments, a limit set by the original Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Routing protocol, as described in ISO/IEC 10589.  This mechanism can be used in IP-only, OSI-only, and dual routers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmMontilio Inc.dnamekA. Hermelin¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Previdi¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Shandddatej Ãhfilenamegrfc3786eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125731hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5311epagesopublicationDate@∂ugranking crfcd3786irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex~Extending the Number of Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Link State PDU (LSP) Fragments Beyond the 256 LimitdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractymThis document describes a common practice on how the existing metric of Interior Gateway Protocols (IGP) can be used as an alternative metric to the Traffic Engineering (TE) metric for Constraint Based Routing of MultiProtocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering tunnels.  This effectively results in the ability to perform Constraint Based Routing with optimization of one metric (e.g., link bandwidth) for some Traffic Engineering tunnels (e.g., Data Trunks) while optimizing another metric (e.g., propagation delay) for some other tunnels with different requirements (e.g., Voice Trunks).  No protocol extensions or modifications are required.  This text documents current router implementations and deployment practices.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiR. Uppili¢kaffiliationfEquantdnamekA. Vedrenne¢kaffiliationfEquantdnameiP. Merckx¢kaffiliationoGlobal CrossingdnamejT. Telkampddatej“Ãhfilenamegrfc3785eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125730hkeywordsàcIGPxInterior Gateway ProtocoldMPLSxMultiProtocol Label SwitchingbTEsTraffic Engineeringdlinkpbandwidth routerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@∑•granking…crfcd3785irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberWetotaletitlex^Use of Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) Metric as a second MPLS Traffic Engineering (TE) MetricdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÓThis document describes extensions to the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol to support Traffic Engineering (TE).  This document extends the IS-IS protocol by specifying new information that an Intermediate System (router) can place in Link State Protocol (LSP) Data Units.  This information describes additional details regarding the state of the network that are useful for traffic engineering computations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpProcket NetworksdnamegH. Smit¢kaffiliation`dnameeT. Liddatej“}hfilenamegrfc3784eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125729hkeywordsÇslink state protocolclspkobsoletedByÅd5305epagesopublicationDate@◊0granking»crfcd3784irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZIntermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Extensions for Traffic Engineering (TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4205∂habstracty\Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) is a Small Computer Systems Interface (SCSI) transport protocol designed to run on top of TCP.  The iSCSI session abstraction is equivalent to the classic SCSI "I_T nexus", which represents the logical relationship between an Initiator and a Target (I and T) required in order to communicate via the SCSI family of protocols.  The iSCSI session provides an ordered command delivery from the SCSI initiator to the SCSI target.  This document goes into the design considerations that led to the iSCSI session model as it is defined today, relates the SCSI command ordering features defined in T10 specifications to the iSCSI concepts, and finally provides guidance to system designers on how true command ordering solutions can be built based on iSCSI.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsHewlett-Packard Co.dnamenM. Chadalapaka¢kaffiliationsHewlett-Packard Co.dnamejR. Elliottddatej“Àhfilenamegrfc3783eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125728hkeywordsÇx)Internet Small Computer Systems InterfaceeiscsikobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@µ$∑granking«crfcd3783irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRSmall Computer Systems Interface (SCSI) Command Ordering Considerations with iSCSIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃThe purpose of this document is to advance NewReno TCP's Fast Retransmit and Fast Recovery algorithms in RFC 2582 from Experimental to Standards Track status.  The main change in this document relative to RFC 2582 is to specify the Careful variant of NewReno's Fast Retransmit and Fast Recovery algorithms.  The base algorithm described in RFC 2582 did not attempt to avoid unnecessary multiple Fast Retransmits that can occur after a timeout.  However, RFC 2582 also defined "Careful" and "Less Careful" variants that avoid these unnecessary Fast Retransmits, and recommended the Careful variant.  This document specifies the previously-named "Careful" variant as the basic version of NewReno TCP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdICSIdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationfBoeingdnamelT. Henderson¢kaffiliationkTeliaSoneradnameiA. Gurtovddatej—åhfilenamegrfc3782eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125727hkeywordsÇcTCPxTransmission Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÅd6582epagesopublicationDate@çó∏granking∆crfcd3782irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9The NewReno Modification to TCP's Fast Recovery AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty
SMIng (Structure of Management Information, Next Generation) (RFC3780), is a protocol-independent data definition language for management information.  This memo defines an SMIng language extension that specifies the mapping of SMIng definitions of identities, classes, and their attributes and events to dedicated definitions of nodes, scalar objects, tables and columnar objects, and notifications, for application to the SNMP management framework.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoTU BraunschweigdnamejF. Strauss¢kaffiliationxInternational University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej“Àhfilenamegrfc3781eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125726hkeywordsÅxdata definition languagekobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDate@•Z·granking≈crfcd3781irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexuNext Generation Structure of Management Information (SMIng) Mappings to the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyxThis memo defines the base SMIng (Structure of Management Information, Next Generation) language.  SMIng is a data definition language that provides a protocol-independent representation for management information.  Separate RFCs define mappings of SMIng to specific management protocols, including SNMP.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoTU BraunschweigdnamejF. Strauss¢kaffiliationxInternational University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej“…hfilenamegrfc3780eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125725hkeywordsÑeSMIngx#Structure of Management InformationoNext Generationxdata definition languagekobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate@•Zlgrankingƒcrfcd3780irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex;SMIng - Next Generation Structure of Management InformationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document defines two X.509 v3 certificate extensions.  The first binds a list of IP address blocks, or prefixes, to the subject of a certificate.  The second binds a list of autonomous system identifiers to the subject of a certificate.  These extensions may be used to convey the authorization of the subject to use the IP addresses and autonomous system identifiers contained in the extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegC. Lynn¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamegS. Kent¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamefK. Seoddatej“}hfilenamegrfc3779eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125724hkeywordsåeX.509jallocationuatrribute certificatemauthorizationx&autonomous system number authorizationkcertificatejdelegationqinternet registryxip address authorizationxpublic key infrastructurelright-to-useqsecure allocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@“#≥granking√crfcd3779irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4X.509 Extensions for IP Addresses and AS IdentifiersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyöPDF, the 'Portable Document Format', is a general document representation language that has been in use for document exchange on the Internet since 1993.  This document provides an overview of the PDF format, explains the mechanisms for digital signatures and encryption within PDF files, and updates the media type registration of 'application/pdf'.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmAdobe SystemsdnamegE. Taft¢kaffiliationmAdobe SystemsdnamejJ. Pravetz¢kaffiliationmAdobe SystemsdnameiS. Zilles¢kaffiliationmAdobe SystemsdnamekL. Masinterddatej“Àhfilenamegrfc3778eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125723hkeywordsÉxportable document formatqdocument exchangerdigital signatureskobsoletedByÅd8118epagesopublicationDate@µ$Ugranking¬crfcd3778irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe application/pdf Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractypThe process by which the members of the IAB and IESG are selected, confirmed, and recalled is specified.  This document is a self-consistent, organized compilation of the process as it was known at the time of publication.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqeList eXpress LLCdnameiJ. Galvinddatej“}hfilenamegrfc3777eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfnomcomdfullxCnomcom - Operation of the IESG/IAB Nominating and Recall Committeesdnamex:Operation of the IESG/IAB Nominating and Recall Committeesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125722hkeywordsÑcIABdIESGxInternet Architecture Boardx#Internet Engineering Steering GroupkobsoletedByÅd7437epages"opublicationDate@–Ægranking¡crfcd3777irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkIAB and IESG Selection, Confirmation, and Recall Process: Operation of the Nominating and Recall CommitteesdtypecrfciupdatedByÑd5680d5633d6859d5078∂habstractyíMobile IPv6 uses IPsec to protect signaling between the home agent and the mobile node.  Mobile IPv6 base document defines the main requirements these nodes must follow.  This document discusses these requirements in more depth, illustrates the used packet formats, describes suitable configuration procedures, and shows how implementations can process the packets in the right order. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamenV. Devarapalli¢kaffiliationqGET/ENST BretagnednameiF. Dupontddatej“}hfilenamegrfc3776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-125721hkeywordsÉeIPsechsecurityqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate@«©€granking¿crfcd3776irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQUsing IPsec to Protect Mobile IPv6 Signaling Between Mobile Nodes and Home AgentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4877∂habstractyëThis document specifies a protocol which allows nodes to remain reachable while moving around in the IPv6 Internet.  Each mobile node is always identified by its home address, regardless of its current point of attachment to the Internet.  While situated away from its home, a mobile node is also associated with a care-of address, which provides information about the mobile node's current location.  IPv6 packets addressed to a mobile node's home address are transparently routed to its care-of address.  The protocol enables IPv6 nodes to cache the binding of a mobile node's home address with its care-of address, and to then send any packets destined for the mobile node directly to it at this care-of address.  To support this operation, Mobile IPv6 defines a new IPv6 protocol and a new destination option.  All IPv6 nodes, whether mobile or stationary, can communicate with mobile nodes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoRice UniversitydnamejD. Johnson¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej“}hfilenamegrfc3775eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmip6dfullxmip6 - Mobility for IPv6dnameqMobility for IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-125720hkeywordsÉhmobilityqinternet protocolenodeskobsoletedByÅd6275epages•opublicationDate@«©^grankingøcrfcd3775irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMobility Support in IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo summarizes perceived problems in the structure, function, and processes of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).  We are attempting to identify these problems, so that they can be addressed and corrected by the IETF community.  The problems have been digested and categorized from an extensive discussion which took place on the 'problem-statement' mailing list from November 2002 to September 2003.  The problem list has been further analyzed in an attempt to determine the root causes at the heart of the perceived problems: The result will be used to guide the next stage of the process in the Problem Statement working group which is to recommend the structures and processes that will carry out the corrections.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiE. Daviesddatej“Àhfilenamegrfc3774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgproblemdfullxproblem - Problem StatementdnameqProblem Statementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125719hkeywordsÑdietfgprocessgproblemhanalysiskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@ôx-grankingæcrfcd3774irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevIETF Problem StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty€This document describes the high-level requirements for Internet Voice Mail (IVM) and establishes a baseline of desired functionality against which proposed MIME profiles for Internet Voice Messaging can be judged.  IVM is an extension of the Voice Profile for Internet Mail (VPIM) version 2 designed to support interoperability with desktop clients.  Other goals for this version of VPIM include expanded interoperability with unified messaging systems, conformance to Internet standards, and backward compatibility with voice messaging systems currently running in a VPIM enabled environment.  This document does not include goals that were met fully by VPIM version 2.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhComversednamejE. Candellddatej“|hfilenamegrfc3773eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-125718hkeywordsÑcivmxinternet voice messagingxvoice profile for internet maildvpimkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate@«§¨grankingΩcrfcd3773irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/High-Level Requirements for Internet Voice MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyíThe Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) defines a Link Control Protocol (LCP) and a method for negotiating the use of multi-protocol traffic over point-to-point links.  The PPP Vendor Extensions document adds vendor-specific general-purpose Configuration Option and Code numbers.  This document extends these features to cover vendor-specific Network, Authentication, and Control Protocols. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejJ. Carlson¢kaffiliationrL-3 CommunicationsdnamejR. Winslowddatej“Àhfilenamegrfc3772eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125717hkeywordsÑcPPPnPoint-to-Pointulink control protocolclcpkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate@∂tâgrankingºcrfcd3772irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) Vendor ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document defines and describes the IntermediateResponse message, a general mechanism for defining single-request/multiple-response operations in Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP).  The IntermediateResponse message is defined in such a way that the protocol behavior of existing LDAP operations is maintained.  This message is intended to be used in conjunction with the LDAP ExtendedRequest and ExtendedResponse to define new single-request/multiple-response operations or in conjunction with a control when extending existing LDAP operations in a way that requires them to return intermediate response information. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlNovell, Inc.dnamekR. Harrison¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej—ãhfilenamegrfc3771eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125716hkeywordsÉfLDAPv3eLDAv3ex.500kobsoletedByÇd4510d4511epagesopublicationDate@éÈõgrankingªcrfcd3771irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNThe Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Intermediate Response MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx±This document defines two EAP extended key usage values and a public key certificate extension to carry Wireless LAN (WLAN) System Service identifiers (SSIDs). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehT. Mooreddatej“ hfilenamegrfc3770eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125715hkeywordsÉdssidxsystem service identifiersceapkobsoletedByÅd4334epages	opublicationDate@µ#Ôgranking∫crfcd3770irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexàCertificate Extensions and Attributes Supporting Authentication in Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) and Wireless Local Area Networks (WLAN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxøThis document describes requirements for how IPv6 address prefixes should be delegated to an IPv6 subscriber's network (or "site").  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNTT Communications CorporationdnamekS. Miyakawa¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Dromsddatej“|hfilenamegrfc3769eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125714hkeywordsÅxinternet protocol version 6kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@◊/õgrankingπcrfcd3769irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Requirements for IPv6 Prefix DelegationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP).  VRRP specifies an election protocol that dynamically assigns responsibility for a virtual router to one of the VRRP routers on a LAN.  The VRRP router controlling the IP address(es) associated with a virtual router is called the Master, and forwards packets sent to these IP addresses.  The election process provides dynamic fail over in the forwarding responsibility should the Master become unavailable.  This allows any of the virtual router IP addresses on the LAN to be used as the default first hop router by end-hosts.  The advantage gained from using VRRP is a higher availability default path without requiring configuration of dynamic routing or router discovery protocols on every end-host. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hindenddatej—ãhfilenamegrfc3768eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdvrrpdfullx)vrrp - Virtual Router Redundancy Protocoldnamex"Virtual Router Redundancy Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125713hkeywordsâdVRRPx"Virtual Router Redundancy ProtocolclanelocaldareagnetworkbiphinternethprotocolkobsoletedByÅd5798epagesopublicationDate@}œgranking∏crfcd3768irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes a protocol whereby a user can acquire cryptographic credentials (e.g., private keys, PKCS #15 structures) from a credential server, using a workstation that has locally trusted software installed, but with no user-specific configuration.  The protocol's payloads are described in XML.  This memo also specifies a Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP) profile of the protocol.  Security requirements are met by mandating support for TLS and/or DIGEST-MD5 (through BEEP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvTrinity College DublindnamejS. Farrellddatej“zhfilenamegrfc3767eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsacreddfullx'sacred - Securely Available CredentialsdnamexSecurely Available Credentialsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125712hkeywordsÑdbeepx#blocks extensible exchange protocolcxmlxextensible mark up languagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@◊/ûgranking∑crfcd3767irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Securely Available Credentials ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∑habstractyPImplementors of systems that use public key cryptography to exchange symmetric keys need to make the public keys resistant to some predetermined level of attack.  That level of attack resistance is the strength of the system, and the symmetric keys that are exchanged must be at least as strong as the system strength requirements.  The three quantities, system strength, symmetric key strength, and public key strength, must be consistently matched for any network protocol usage.  While it is fairly easy to express the system strength requirements in terms of a symmetric key length and to choose a cipher that has a key length equal to or exceeding that requirement, it is harder to choose a public key that has a cryptographic strength meeting a symmetric key strength requirement.  This document explains how to determine the length of an asymmetric key as a function of a symmetric key strength requirement.  Some rules of thumb for estimating equivalent resistance to large-scale attacks on various algorithms are given.  The document also addresses how changing the sizes of the underlying large integers (moduli, group sizes, exponents, and so on) changes the time to use the algorithms for key exchange.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrPurple Streak Dev.dnamehH. Orman¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej—áhfilenamegrfc3766eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125711hkeywordsÉhsecuritywcryptography algorithmshintegerskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@çò9granking∂crfcd3766irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberVetotaletitlexHDetermining Strengths For Public Keys Used For Exchanging Symmetric KeysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÃThis document describes the use of a scope control Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) community.  This well-known advisory transitive community allows an origin AS to specify the extent to which a specific route should be externally propagated.  In particular this community, NOPEER, allows an origin AS to specify that a route with this attribute need not be advertised across bilateral peer connections.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgTelstradnameiG. Hustonddatej—Ghfilenamegrfc3765eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhptomainedfullxIptomaine - Prefix Taxonomy Ongoing Measurement & Inter Network Experimentdnamex>Prefix Taxonomy Ongoing Measurement & Inter Network Experimentdtypebwgbidjdoc-125710hkeywordsÇppeer connectionsjpropagatedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@|v“grankingµcrfcd3765irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexFNOPEER Community for Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) Route Scope ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxˆThis document registers an Electronic Number (ENUM) service for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), pursuant to the guidelines in RFC 3761.  Specifically, this document focuses on provisioning SIP addresses-of-record in ENUM. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej—Dhfilenamegrfc3764eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125709hkeywordsÜcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolcaorsAddresses-of-Recordqelectronic numberdENUMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@íÈÃgranking¥crfcd3764irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRenumservice registration for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Addresses-of-RecorddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstracty	With growing availability of good time sources to network nodes, it becomes increasingly possible to measure one-way IP performance metrics with high precision.  To do so in an interoperable manner, a common protocol for such measurements is required.  This document specifies requirements for a one-way active measurement protocol (OWAMP) standard.  The protocol can measure one-way delay, as well as other unidirectional characteristics, such as one-way loss.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamekS. Shalunov¢kaffiliationiInternet2dnamemB. Teitelbaumddatej—Bhfilenamegrfc3763eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125708hkeywordsÉsperformance metricsedelaynunidirectionalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@íÈFgranking≥crfcd3763irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8One-way Active Measurement Protocol (OWAMP) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-The H.323 specification defines a means for building multimedia communication services over an arbitrary Packet Based Network, including the Internet.  This document registers a Telephone Number Mapping (ENUM) service for H.323 according to specifications and guidelines in RFC 3761. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehO. Levinddatej—?hfilenamegrfc3762eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125707hkeywordsÑdENUMxTelephone Number Mappingjmultimediatpacket based networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@íË’granking≤crfcd3762irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Telephone Number Mapping (ENUM) Service Registration for H.323dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6118∂habstracty!This document discusses the use of the Domain Name System (DNS) for storage of E.164 numbers.  More specifically, how DNS can be used for identifying available services connected to one E.164 number.  It specifically obsoletes RFC 2916 to bring it in line with the Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Application specification found in the document series specified in RFC 3401.  It is very important to note that it is impossible to read and understand this document without reading the documents discussed in RFC 3401. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelP. Faltstrom¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamekM. Meallingddatej—;hfilenamegrfc3761eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125706hkeywordsáeE.164cURIxUniform Resource IdentifierdDDDSx#Dynamic Delegation Discovery SystemcDNSrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÇd6116d6117epagesopublicationDate@íË\granking±crfcd3761irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmThe E.164 to Uniform Resource Identifiers (URI) Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Application (ENUM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçAs the number, and more particularly the number of different types, of devices connecting to the Internet increases, credential mobility becomes an issue for IETF standardization.  This document responds to the requirements on protocols for secure exchange of credentials listed in RFC 3157, by presenting an abstract protocol framework.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqFuture FoundationdnamelD. Gustafson¢kaffiliationxTreasury Board of CanadadnamegM. Just¢kaffiliationlRSA SecuritydnamejM. Nystromddatej»{hfilenamegrfc3760eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsacreddfullx'sacred - Securely Available CredentialsdnamexSecurely Available Credentialsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125705hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@}ŒDgranking∞crfcd3760irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexESecurely Available Credentials (SACRED) - Credential Server FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿This document aims to clarify terms and concepts presented in RFC 3095.  RFC 3095 defines a Proposed Standard framework with profiles for RObust Header Compression (ROHC).  The standard introduces various concepts which might be difficult to understand and especially to relate correctly to the surrounding environments where header compression may be used.  This document aims at clarifying these aspects of ROHC, discussing terms such as ROHC instances, ROHC channels, ROHC feedback, and ROHC contexts, and how these terms relate to other terms, like network elements and IP interfaces, commonly used, for example, when addressing MIB issues.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonssonddatej—çhfilenamegrfc3759eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125704hkeywordsàmencapsulatinghsecuritygpayloadireal-timeitransporthprotocolduserhdatagramkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@âZ,grankingØcrfcd3759irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJRObust Header Compression (ROHC): Terminology and Channel Mapping ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis memo describes an extension to the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) that allows an SCTP endpoint to signal to its peer that it should move the cumulative ack point forward.  When both sides of an SCTP association support this extension, it can be used by an SCTP implementation to provide partially reliable data transmission service to an upper layer protocol.  This memo describes the protocol extensions, which consist of a new parameter for INIT and INIT ACK, and a new FORWARD TSN chunk type, and provides one example of a partially reliable service that can be provided to the upper layer via this mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejM. Ramalho¢kaffiliationnMotorola, Inc.dnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationx"Univ. of Applied Sciences MuensterdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationvUniversity of DelawarednameiP. Conradddatej“ hfilenamegrfc3758eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125703hkeywordsÖdSCTPx$Stream Control Transmission Protocoldinithinit ackkforward tsnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@µ#ugrankingÆcrfcd3758irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIStream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Partial Reliability ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøWith the Delegation Signer (DS) resource record (RR), the concept of a public key acting as a secure entry point (SEP) has been introduced.  During exchanges of public keys with the parent there is a need to differentiate SEP keys from other public keys in the Domain Name System KEY (DNSKEY) resource record set.  A flag bit in the DNSKEY RR is defined to indicate that DNSKEY is to be used as a SEP.  The flag bit is intended to assist in operational procedures to correctly generate DS resource records, or to indicate what DNSKEYs are intended for static configuration.  The flag bit is not to be used in the DNS verification protocol.  This document updates RFC 2535 and RFC 3755. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhRIPE NCCdnamejO. Kolkman¢kaffiliationfNIC-SEdnamekJ. Schlyter¢kaffiliationdARINdnamehE. Lewisddatej“ hfilenamegrfc3757eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125702hkeywordsÅfdnsseckobsoletedByÉd4033d4034d4035epagesopublicationDate@∂tgranking≠crfcd3757irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRDomain Name System KEY (DNSKEY) Resource Record (RR) Secure Entry Point (SEP) FlagdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThe existing IETF standards specify that IPv6 Neighbor Discovery (ND) and Address Autoconfiguration mechanisms may be protected with IPsec Authentication Header (AH).  However, the current specifications limit the security solutions to manual keying due to practical problems faced with automatic key management.  This document specifies three different trust models and discusses the threats pertinent to IPv6 Neighbor Discovery.  The purpose of this discussion is to define the requirements for Securing IPv6 Neighbor Discovery.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxEricsson Research Nomadic LabdnamekP. Nikander¢kaffiliationoDoCoMo USA LabsdnamehJ. Kempf¢kaffiliationxSun Microsystems LaboratoriesdnamekE. Nordmarkddatej“ hfilenamegrfc3756eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsenddfullx"send - Securing Neighbor DiscoverydnamexSecuring Neighbor Discoverydtypebwgbidjdoc-125701hkeywordsÇnauthenticationwsecurity key managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@¢¿ägranking¨crfcd3756irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5IPv6 Neighbor Discovery (ND) Trust Models and ThreatsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynAs the DNS Security (DNSSEC) specifications have evolved, the syntax and semantics of the DNSSEC resource records (RRs) have changed.  Many deployed nameservers understand variants of these semantics.  Dangerous interactions can occur when a resolver that understands an earlier version of these semantics queries an authoritative server that understands the new delegation signer semantics, including at least one failure scenario that will cause an unsecured zone to be unresolvable.  This document changes the type codes and mnemonics of the DNSSEC RRs (SIG, KEY, and NXT) to avoid those interactions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlSPARTA, Inc.dnameiS. Weilerddatej“ hfilenamegrfc3755eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125700hkeywordsâfdnsseclDNS Securitybrroresource recordjDNS-SECEXTcdnsnauthenticationdnsecjnextsecurekobsoletedByÉd4033d4034d4035epages	opublicationDate@∂tgranking´crfcd3755irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Legacy Resolver Compatibility for Delegation Signer (DS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd3845d3757µhabstracty{This document discusses the problems of IP Multicast use in Differentiated Services (DS) networks, expanding on the discussion in RFC 2475 ("An Architecture of Differentiated Services").  It also suggests possible solutions to these problems, describes a potential implementation model, and presents simulation results.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill FennergauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrUniv. of KarlsruhednamehR. Bless¢kaffiliationrUniv. of TuebingendnameiK. Wehrleddatej—ãhfilenamegrfc3754eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125699hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate@âYZgranking™crfcd3754irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex5IP Multicast in Differentiated Services (DS) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÙThere is a need for common definitions of terminology in the work to be done around IP mobility.  This document defines terms for mobility related terminology.  The document originated out of work done in the Seamoby Working Group but has broader applicability for terminology used in IETF-wide discourse on technology for mobility and IP networks.  Other working groups dealing with mobility may want to take advantage of this terminology.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of HelsinkidnameiJ. Manner¢kaffiliationvUniversity of HelsinkidnamegM. Kojoddatej“|hfilenamegrfc3753eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgseamobydfullxWseamoby - Context Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host AlertingdnamexMContext Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host Alertingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125698hkeywordsÉhnetworksbipqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDate@«®‰granking©crfcd3753irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexMobility Related TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¸This memo provides a discussion of use cases and deployment scenarios for Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES).  The work examines services that could be performed to requests and/or responses.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiA. Barbir¢kaffiliationxBrooktrout Technology, Inc.dnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamegR. Chen¢kaffiliationvIndividual ContributordnamejS. McHenry¢kaffiliationxPurple Streak DevelopmentdnamehH. Orman¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehR. Pennoddatej—ähfilenamegrfc3752eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdopesdfullx#opes - Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdnamexOpen Pluggable Edge Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125697hkeywordsÅxapplication data serviceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@Ävgranking®crfcd3752irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFOpen Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) Use Cases and Deployment ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyáThere have been a number of legislative initiatives in the U.S.  and elsewhere over the past few years to use the Internet to actively interfere with allegedly illegal activities of Internet users.  This memo proposes a number of requirements for a new protocol, the Omniscience Protocol, that could be used to enable such efforts.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjHarvard U.dnamejS. Bradnerddatej»ºhfilenamegrfc3751eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43439hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate@vÁgrankingßcrfcd3751irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex!Omniscience Protocol RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty:This document defines the scenarios in which IPv6 transition mechanisms are to be used in unmanaged networks.  In order to evaluate the suitability of these mechanisms, we need to define the scenarios in which these mechanisms have to be used.  One specific scope is the "unmanaged network", which typically corresponds to a home or small office network.  The scenarios are specific to a single subnet, and are defined in terms of IP connectivity supported by the gateway and the Internet Service Provider (ISP).  We first examine the generic requirements of four classes of applications: local, client, peer to peer and server.  Then, for each scenario, we infer transition requirements by analyzing the needs for smooth migration of applications from IPv4 to IPv6.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationcISCdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekS. Satapati¢kaffiliationjNLnet LabsdnamenR. van der Polddatej—ähfilenamegrfc3750eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125696hkeywordsÑmsingle subnetggatewaycispxinternet service providerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@|vWgranking¶crfcd3750irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Unmanaged Networks IPv6 Transition ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol (RFC 2246) includes features to negotiate selection of a lossless data compression method as part of the TLS Handshake Protocol and to then apply the algorithm associated with the selected method as part of the TLS Record Protocol.  TLS defines one standard compression method which specifies that data exchanged via the record protocol will not be compressed.  This document describes an additional compression method associated with a lossless data compression algorithm for use with TLS, and it describes a method for the specification of additional TLS compression methods. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej“ hfilenamegrfc3749eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125695hkeywordsÑcTLSxTransport Layer Securityxlossless data compressionrhandshake protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@•Zmgranking•crfcd3749irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Transport Layer Security Protocol Compression MethodsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8447d8996∂habstracty}This document defines the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), an authentication framework which supports multiple authentication methods.  EAP typically runs directly over data link layers such as Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) or IEEE 802, without requiring IP.  EAP provides its own support for duplicate elimination and retransmission, but is reliant on lower layer ordering guarantees.  Fragmentation is not supported within EAP itself; however, individual EAP methods may support this.  This document obsoletes RFC 2284.  A summary of the changes between this document and RFC 2284 is available in Appendix A. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationrMerit Network, IncdnamehL. Blunk¢kaffiliationxVollbrecht Consulting LLCdnamemJ. Vollbrecht¢kaffiliationcSundnamejJ. Carlson¢kaffiliationkipUnpluggeddnamelH. Levkowetzddatej“|hfilenamegrfc3748eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymceapdfullx(eap - Extensible Authentication Protocoldnamex"Extensible Authentication Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125694hkeywordsÖgPPP-EAPx"Extensible Authentication Protocolpdata link layersnpoint-to-pointhieee 802kobsoletedByÄepagesCopublicationDate@◊.Âgranking§crfcd3748irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7057d5247∂habstractyThis memo describes a MIB module that provides a conceptual layer between high-level "network-wide" policy definitions that effect configuration of the Differentiated Services (diffserv) subsystem and the instance-specific information that would include such details as the parameters for all the queues associated with each interface in a system.  This essentially provides an interface for configuring differentiated services at a conceptually higher layer than that of the Differentiated Services MIB. [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeI.NetdnamemH. Hazewinkel¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Partainddatej—åhfilenamegrfc3747eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsnmpconfdfullx-snmpconf - Configuration Management with SNMPdnamex"Configuration Management with SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125693hkeywordsÑcMIBxmanagement information basehdiffservwdifferentiated serviceskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@|vgranking£crfcd3747irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-The Differentiated Services Configuration MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¸This document defines the architectural framework for the ForCES (Forwarding and Control Element Separation) network elements, and identifies the associated entities and their interactions.  This is memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkIntel Corp.dnamegL. Yang¢kaffiliationtUniv. of North TexasdnamehR. Dantu¢kaffiliationkIntel Corp.dnamekT. Anderson¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehR. Gopalddatej—åhfilenamegrfc3746eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125692hkeywordsÅpnetwork elementskobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate@êF·granking¢crfcd3746irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxƒThis document serves to register and document the standard MIME types associated with the ISO/IEC 15444 standards, commonly known as JPEG 2000 (Joint Photographic Experts Group). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnApple ComputerdnameiD. Singer¢kaffiliationkElysium LtddnamehR. Clark¢kaffiliationjYahoo Inc.dnamefD. Leeddatej—åhfilenamegrfc3745eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125691hkeywordsÑdMIMEx%multipurpose internet mail extensionsdJPEGx joint photographic experts groupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@vË÷granking°crfcd3745irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5MIME Type Registrations for JPEG 2000 (ISO/IEC 15444)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅThis document specifies a set of methods, headers, message bodies, properties, and reports that define Access Control extensions to the WebDAV Distributed Authoring Protocol.  This protocol permits a client to read and modify access control lists that instruct a server whether to allow or deny operations upon a resource (such as HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) method invocations) by a given principal.  A lightweight representation of principals as Web resources supports integration of a wide range of user management repositories.  Search operations allow discovery and manipulation of principals using human names. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehG. Clemm¢kaffiliationjgreenbytesdnamejJ. Reschke¢kaffiliationrOracle CorporationdnameiE. Sedlar¢kaffiliationoU.C. Santa CruzdnamelJ. Whiteheadddatej“…hfilenamegrfc3744eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfwebdavdfullx1webdav - WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdnamex(WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdtypebwgbidjdoc-125690hkeywordsÉfWebDAVx(Web Distributed Authoring and VersioningnAccess ControlkobsoletedByÄepagesHopublicationDate@úIgranking†crfcd3744irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIWeb Distributed Authoring and Versioning (WebDAV) Access Control ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty£Achieving internationalized access to domain names raises many complex issues.  These are associated not only with basic protocol design, such as how names are represented on the network, compared, and converted to appropriate forms, but also with issues and options for deployment, transition, registration, and administration.  The IETF Standards for Internationalized Domain Names, known as "IDNA", focuses on access to domain names in a range of scripts that is broader in scope than the original ASCII.  The development process made it clear that use of characters with similar appearances and/or interpretations created potential for confusion, as well as difficulties in deployment and transition.  The conclusion was that, while those issues were important, they could best be addressed administratively rather than through restrictions embedded in the protocols.  This document defines a set of guidelines for applying restrictions of that type for Chinese, Japanese and Korean (CJK) scripts and the zones that use them and, perhaps, the beginning of a framework for thinking about other zones, languages, and scripts.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. AlvestrandgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeJPNICdnamejK. Konishi¢kaffiliationeTWNICdnamehK. Huang¢kaffiliationeCNNICdnamegH. Qian¢kaffiliationhPeaceNetdnameeY. Koddatej»˘hfilenamegrfc3743eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125689hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate@}Õ⁄grankingücrfcd3743irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexíJoint Engineering Team (JET) Guidelines for Internationalized Domain Names (IDN) Registration and Administration for Chinese, Japanese, and KoreandtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document describes an optional modification for TCP's slow-start for use with TCP connections with large congestion windows.  For TCP connections that are able to use congestion windows of thousands (or tens of thousands) of MSS-sized segments (for MSS the sender's MAXIMUM SEGMENT SIZE), the current slow-start procedure can result in increasing the congestion window by thousands of segments in a single round-trip time.  Such an increase can easily result in thousands of packets being dropped in one round-trip time.  This is often counter-productive for the TCP flow itself, and is also hard on the rest of the traffic sharing the congested link.  This note describes Limited Slow-Start as an optional mechanism for limiting the number of segments by which the congestion window is increased for one window of data during slow-start, in order to improve performance for TCP connections with large congestion windows.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdICSIdnamehS. Floydddatej“Æhfilenamegrfc3742eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125688hkeywordsÉcTCPxtransmission control protocoljcongestionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@jkgrankingûcrfcd3742irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Limited Slow-Start for TCP with Large Congestion WindowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyƒCanonical XML specifies a standard serialization of XML that, when applied to a subdocument, includes the subdocument's ancestor context including all of the namespace declarations and attributes in the "xml:" namespace.  However, some applications require a method which, to the extent practical, excludes ancestor context from a canonicalized subdocument.  For example, one might require a digital signature over an XML payload (subdocument) in an XML message that will not break when that subdocument is removed from its original message and/or inserted into a different context.  This requirement is satisfied by Exclusive XML Canonicalization.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrPureEdge SolutionsdnamehJ. Boyer¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationcW3CdnameiJ. Reagleddatej“≠hfilenamegrfc3741eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxmldsigdfullx xmldsig - XML Digital SignaturesdnamevXML Digital Signaturesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125687hkeywordsÇxextensible markup languageinamespacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@klgrankingùcrfcd3741irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Exclusive XML Canonicalization, Version 1.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document provides an overview and rationale of the multicast security architecture used to secure data packets of large multicast groups.  The document begins by introducing a Multicast Security Reference Framework, and proceeds to identify the security services that may be part of a secure multicast solution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamekT. Hardjono¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegB. Weisddatej“≠hfilenamegrfc3740eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125686hkeywordsÅldata packetskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@kkTgrankingúcrfcd3740irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The Multicast Group Security ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document forms a certificate profile, based on RFC 3280, for identity certificates issued to natural persons.  The profile defines specific conventions for certificates that are qualified within a defined legal framework, named Qualified Certificates.  However, the profile does not define any legal requirements for such Qualified Certificates.  The goal of this document is to define a certificate profile that supports the issuance of Qualified Certificates independent of local legal requirements.  The profile is however not limited to Qualified Certificates and further profiling may facilitate specific local needs. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationlRSA SecuritydnamejM. Nystrom¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegT. Polkddatej“≠hfilenamegrfc3739eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125685hkeywordsÉeX.509xPublic Key InfrastructurefsyntaxkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate@[zègrankingõcrfcd3739irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure: Qualified Certificates ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyhThis document specifies Wave and Equation Based Rate Control (WEBRC), which provides rate and congestion control for data delivery.  WEBRC is specifically designed to support protocols using IP multicast.  It provides multiple-rate, congestion-controlled delivery to receivers, i.e., different receivers joined to the same session may be receiving packets at different rates depending on the bandwidths of their individual connections to the sender and on competing traffic along these connections.  WEBRC requires no feedback from receivers to the sender, i.e., it is a completely receiver-driven congestion control protocol.  Thus, it is designed to scale to potentially massive numbers of receivers attached to a session from a single sender.  Furthermore, because each individual receiver adjusts to the available bandwidth between the sender and that receiver, there is the potential to deliver data to each individual receiver at the fastest possible rate for that receiver, even in a highly heterogeneous network architecture, using a single sender.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationfM.I.T.dnamehV. Goyalddatej—ãhfilenamegrfc3738eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125684hkeywordsáeWEBRCx$Wave and Equation Based Rate Controlrcongestion controlmdata deliveryimulticastbipqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate@âY%grankingöcrfcd3738irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Wave and Equation Based Rate Control (WEBRC) Building BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx›This document defines the procedures for IANA to administer and maintain the Object Identifier (OID) tree under the Remote Monitoring (rmon) root.  This memo also documents the currently assigned values. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnen¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Biermanddatej—ãhfilenamegrfc3737eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125683hkeywordsÜcMIBxManagement Information BasedIANAx#Internet Assigned Numbers AuthoritydRMONxRegistry of Remote MonitoringkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@vË“grankingôcrfcd3737irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHIANA Guidelines for the Registry of Remote Monitoring (RMON) MIB modulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGStateless Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol service for IPv6 (DHCPv6) is used by nodes to obtain configuration information, such as the addresses of DNS recursive name servers, that does not require the maintenance of any dynamic state for individual clients.  A node that uses stateless DHCP must have obtained its IPv6 addresses through some other mechanism, typically stateless address autoconfiguration.  This document explains which parts of RFC 3315 must be implemented in each of the different kinds of DHCP agents so that agent can support stateless DHCP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Dromsddatej—ähfilenamegrfc3736eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125682hkeywordsÉdDHCPx#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocoldIPv6kobsoletedByÅd8415epages	opublicationDate@vËÕgrankingòcrfcd3736irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEStateless Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Service for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) is an application layer client-server protocol for the provisioning and management of objects stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the protocol defines generic object management operations and an extensible framework that maps protocol operations to objects.  This document presents guidelines for use of EPP's extension mechanisms to define new features and object management capabilities.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej •hfilenamegrfc3735eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgprovregdfullx(provreg - Provisioning Registry ProtocoldnamexProvisioning Registry Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125681hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@d⁄˛grankingócrfcd3735irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCGuidelines for Extending the Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;This document describes how an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) session is mapped onto a single Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) connection.  This mapping requires use of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to protect information exchanged between an EPP client and an EPP server. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej“≠hfilenamegrfc3734eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgprovregdfullx(provreg - Provisioning Registry ProtocoldnamexProvisioning Registry Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125680hkeywordsÖgmappingfclientfserverctlsxtransport layer securitykobsoletedByÅd4934epages	opublicationDate@d⁄xgrankingñcrfcd3734irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Transport Over TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyxThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of individual or organizational social information identifiers (known as "contacts") stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in Extensible Markup Language (XML), the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to contacts. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej“≠hfilenamegrfc3733eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgprovregdfullx(provreg - Provisioning Registry ProtocoldnamexProvisioning Registry Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125679hkeywordsÇfsyntaxisemanticskobsoletedByÅd4933epages)opublicationDate@d⁄grankingïcrfcd3733irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Contact MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of Internet host names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to host names. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej“≠hfilenamegrfc3732eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgprovregdfullx(provreg - Provisioning Registry ProtocoldnamexProvisioning Registry Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125678hkeywordsÇfsyntaxisemanticskobsoletedByÅd4932epagesopublicationDate@dÿ@grankingîcrfcd3732irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Host MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document describes an Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) mapping for the provisioning and management of Internet domain names stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the mapping defines EPP command syntax and semantics as applied to domain names. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej“¨hfilenamegrfc3731eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgprovregdfullx(provreg - Provisioning Registry ProtocoldnamexProvisioning Registry Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125677hkeywordsÇfsyntaxisemanticskobsoletedByÅd4931epages-opublicationDate@d◊≈grankingìcrfcd3731irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP) Domain Name MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document describes an application layer client-server protocol for the provisioning and management of objects stored in a shared central repository.  Specified in XML, the protocol defines generic object management operations and an extensible framework that maps protocol operations to objects.  This document includes a protocol specification, an object mapping template, and an XML media type registration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej“¨hfilenamegrfc3730eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgprovregdfullx(provreg - Provisioning Registry ProtocoldnamexProvisioning Registry Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125676hkeywordsÅxshared framework mappingkobsoletedByÅd4930epagesEopublicationDate@d÷ògrankingícrfcd3730irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Extensible Provisioning Protocol (EPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for measuring the application performance as experienced by end-users. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamemS. Waldbusserddatej“¨hfilenamegrfc3729eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125675hkeywordsÉcMIBxManagement Information Basewapplication performancekobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDate@EÏgrankingëcrfcd3729irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Application Performance Measurement MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty	This document defines a Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Line (VDSL) interfaces. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvPESA Switching SystemsdnamefB. Ray¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamegR. Abbiddatej—>hfilenamegrfc3728eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125674hkeywordsÑxManagement Information BasecMIBdVDSLx(Very High Speed Digital Subscriber LineskobsoletedByÄepagesLopublicationDate@>ßegrankingêcrfcd3728irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRDefinitions of Managed Objects for Very High Speed Digital Subscriber Lines (VDSL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This document updates the specification of the component matching rules for Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) and X.500 directories (RFC3687) by collecting the Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1) definitions of the component matching rules into an appropriately identified ASN.1 module so that other specifications may reference the component matching rule definitions from within their own ASN.1 modules. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsAdacel TechnologiesdnamegS. Leggddatej—˝hfilenamegrfc3727eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125673hkeywordsÉdLDAPx%lightweight directory access protocoleX.500kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@>¶˙grankingècrfcd3727irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGASN.1 Module Definition for the LDAP and X.500 Component Matching RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This document defines requirements for signaling across different network environments, such as across administrative and/or technology domains.  Signaling is mainly considered for Quality of Service (Qos) such as the Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP).  However, in recent years, several other applications of signaling have been defined.  For example, signaling for label distribution in Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) or signaling to middleboxes.  To achieve wide applicability of the requirements, the starting point is a diverse set of scenarios/use cases concerning various types of networks and application interactions.  This document presents the assumptions before listing the requirements.  The requirements are grouped according to areas such as architecture and design goals, signaling flows, layering, performance, flexibility, security, and mobility.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNECdnamejM. Brunnerddatej—ähfilenamegrfc3726eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125672hkeywordsÑdrsvpxresource reservation protocolkmiddleboxesdnsiskobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate@Äuwgrankingécrfcd3726irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Requirements for Signaling ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyLThird party call control refers to the ability of one entity to create a call in which communication is actually between other parties.  Third party call control is possible using the mechanisms specified within the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  However, there are several possible approaches, each with different benefits and drawbacks.  This document discusses best current practices for the usage of SIP for third party call control.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliation`dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliation`dnamelG. Camarilloddatej—ähfilenamegrfc3725eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125671hkeywordsÑd3pccxThird Party Call ControlcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@|ujgrankingçcrfcd3725irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberUetotaletitlexcBest Current Practices for Third Party Call Control (3pcc) in the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty>The end-to-end principle is the core architectural guideline of the Internet.  In this document, we briefly examine the development of the end-to-end principle as it has been applied to the Internet architecture over the years.  We discuss current trends in the evolution of the Internet architecture in relation to the end-to-end principle, and try to draw some conclusion about the evolution of the end-to-end principle, and thus for the Internet architecture which it supports, in light of these current trends.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamehJ. Kempf¢kaffiliationcIABdnamejR. Austein¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej“¨hfilenamegrfc3724eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125670hkeywordsÅwarchitectural guidelinekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@kj‡grankingåcrfcd3724irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexnThe Rise of the Middle and the Future of End-to-End: Reflections on the Evolution of the Internet ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty,This document discusses how to secure block storage and storage discovery protocols running over IP (Internet Protocol) using IPsec and IKE (Internet Key Exchange).  Threat models and security protocols are developed for iSCSI (Internet Protocol Small Computer System Interface), iFCP (Internet Fibre Channel Storage Networking) and FCIP (Fibre Channel over TCP/IP), as well as the iSNS (Internet Storage Name Server) and SLPv2 (Service Location Protocol v2) discovery protocols.  Performance issues and resource constraints are analyzed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationrMcDATA CorporationdnamehJ. Tseng¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiJ. Walker¢kaffiliationx#Brocade Communications Systems Inc.dnameiV. Rangan¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamemF. Travostinoddatej—âhfilenamegrfc3723eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125669hkeywordsÑhinternetmthreat modelskperformancehsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesFopublicationDate@êFegrankingãcrfcd3723irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Securing Block Storage Protocols over IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7146∂habstractyËThis document describes how to prepare internationalized iSCSI names to increase the likelihood that name input and comparison work in ways that make sense for typical users throughout the world.  The Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) protocol provides a way for hosts to access SCSI devices over an IP network.  The iSCSI end-points, called initiators and targets, each have a globally-unique name that must be transcribable, as well as easily compared. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Bakkeddatej—âhfilenamegrfc3722eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125668hkeywordsÖeiSCSIx)Internet Small Computer Systems InterfaceitransportfuniquefglobalkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@êEÏgrankingäcrfcd3722irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJString Profile for Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides examples of the Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI; or SCSI over TCP) name construction and discussion of discovery of iSCSI resources (targets) by iSCSI initiators.  This document complements the iSCSI protocol document.  Flexibility is the key guiding principle behind this document.  That is, an effort has been made to satisfy the needs of both small isolated environments, as well as large environments requiring secure/scalable solutions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Bakke¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiJ. Hafner¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejJ. Hufferd¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelK. Voruganti¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamejM. Kruegerddatej—âhfilenamegrfc3721eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125667hkeywordságtargetslenvironmentsjscalibiltyftargetiinitiatordscsikdevice namekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@êEøgrankingâcrfcd3721irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFInternet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) Naming and DiscoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7143∂habstractyYThis document describes a transport protocol for Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI) that works on top of TCP.  The iSCSI protocol aims to be fully compliant with the standardized SCSI architecture model.  SCSI is a popular family of protocols that enable systems to communicate with I/O devices, especially storage devices.  SCSI protocols are request/response application protocols with a common standardized architecture model and basic command set, as well as standardized command sets for different device classes (disks, tapes, media-changers etc.).  As system interconnects move from the classical bus structure to a network structure, SCSI has to be mapped to network transport protocols.  IP networks now meet the performance requirements of fast system interconnects and as such are good candidates to "carry" SCSI. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiJ. Satran¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamegK. Meth¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenC. Sapuntzakis¢kaffiliationsHewlett-Packard Co.dnamenM. Chadalapaka¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejE. Zeidnerddatej—âhfilenamegrfc3720eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125666hkeywordsÖeiSCSIx)Internet Small Computer Systems Interfacertransport protocolctcpxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÅd7143epagesopublicationDate@êE6grankingàcrfcd3720irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Internet Small Computer Systems Interface (iSCSI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÑd7146d4850d5048d3980∂habstractyˇThis document discusses a number of differences between the Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) protocol as described in ISO 10589 and the protocol as it is deployed today.  These differences are discussed as a service to those implementing, testing, and deploying the IS-IS Protocol.  A companion document discusses differences between the protocol described in RFC 1195 and the protocol as it is deployed today for routing IP traffic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAxiowave NetworksdnameiJ. Parkerddatej“hfilenamegrfc3719eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125665hkeywordsÜgroutinghrouteingxinterior gateway protocolcigpkconformancejip traffickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@>¶ögrankingácrfcd3719irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcRecommendations for Interoperable Networks using Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyoThis memo describes various internal workings of the Unicode Consortium for the benefit of participants in the IETF.  It is intended solely for informational purposes.  Included are discussions of how the decision-making bodies of the Consortium work and their procedures, as well as information on public access to the character encoding & standardization processes.fadNametHarald T. AlvestrandgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. McGowanddatej…¨hfilenamegrfc3718eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125664hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@>¶grankingÜcrfcd3718irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexRA Summary of Unicode Consortium Procedures, Policies, Stability, and Public AccessdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¨The Internet transport infrastructure is moving towards a model of high-speed routers interconnected by optical core networks.  The architectural choices for the interaction between IP and optical network layers, specifically, the routing and signaling aspects, are maturing.  At the same time, a consensus has emerged in the industry on utilizing IP-based protocols for the optical control plane.  This document defines a framework for IP over Optical networks, considering both the IP-based control plane for optical networks as well as IP-optical network interactions (together referred to as "IP over optical networks").  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamenB. Rajagopalan¢kaffiliationvMarconi CommunicationsdnamejJ. Luciani¢kaffiliationcMCIdnamejD. Awducheddatej“¨hfilenamegrfc3717eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipodfulluipo - IP over OpticaldnameoIP over Opticaldtypebwgbidjdoc-125663hkeywordsÉxtransport infrastructuregroutersjhigh-speedkobsoletedByÄepages0opublicationDate@EgrankingÖcrfcd3717irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%IP over Optical Networks: A FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyŒIn the fall of 2003, the IETF Chair and the IAB Chair formed an IAB Advisory Committee (AdvComm), with a mandate to review the existing IETF administrative structure and relationships (RFC Editor, IETF Secretariat, IANA) and to propose changes to the IETF management process or structure to improve the overall functioning of the IETF.  The AdvComm mandate did not include the standards process itself.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. AlvestrandgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIETFdnamevIAB Advisory Committeeddatej £hfilenamegrfc3716eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125662hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate@d÷!grankingÑcrfcd3716irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex0IETF in the Large:  Administration and ExecutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥This document describes known incompatibilities between Network Address Translation (NAT) and IPsec, and describes the requirements for addressing them.  Perhaps the most common use of IPsec is in providing virtual private networking capabilities.  One very popular use of Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) is to provide telecommuter access to the corporate Intranet.  Today, NATs are widely deployed in home gateways, as well as in other locations likely to be used by telecommuters, such as hotels.  The result is that IPsec-NAT incompatibilities have become a major barrier in the deployment of IPsec in one of its principal uses.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehW. Dixonddatej“´hfilenamegrfc3715eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125661hkeywordsÉxvirtual private networksdvpnshintranetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@RHpgrankingÉcrfcd3715irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBIPsec-Network Address Translation (NAT) Compatibility RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document discusses IAB concerns about effective end-to-end congestion control for best-effort voice traffic in the Internet.  These concerns have to do with fairness, user quality, and with the dangers of congestion collapse.  The concerns are particularly relevant in light of the absence of a widespread Quality of Service (QoS) deployment in the Internet, and the likelihood that this situation will not change much in the near term.  This document is not making any recommendations about deployment paths for Voice over Internet Protocol (VoIP) in terms of QoS support, and is not claiming that best-effort service can be relied upon to give acceptable performance for VoIP.  We are merely observing that voice traffic is occasionally deployed as best-effort traffic over some links in the Internet, that we expect this occasional deployment to continue, and that we have concerns about the lack of effective end-to-end congestion control for this best-effort voice traffic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationcIABdnamehJ. Kempfddatej“´hfilenamegrfc3714eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125660hkeywordsÖjend-to-endcqosrqualify of servicedvoipqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages opublicationDate@d÷$grankingÇcrfcd3714irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexKIAB Concerns Regarding Congestion Control for Voice Traffic in the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty1This document describes the Camellia encryption algorithm.  Camellia is a block cipher with 128-bit block size and 128-, 192-, and 256-bit keys.  The algorithm description is presented together with key scheduling part and data randomizing part.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxMitsubishi Electric CorporationdnameiM. Matsui¢kaffiliationxMitsubishi Electric CorporationdnamekJ. Nakajima¢kaffiliationx Sony Computer Entertainment Inc.dnameiS. Moriaiddatej—âhfilenamegrfc3713eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125659hkeywordsÉhsecurityckeymcryptographickobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@íËgrankingÅcrfcd3713irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2A Description of the Camellia Encryption AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyœThis document defines a schema, object classes and attributes, for printers and printer services, for use with directories that support Lightweight Directory Access Protocol v3 (LDAP-TS).  This document is based on the printer attributes listed in Appendix E of Internet Printing Protocol/1.1 (IPP) (RFC 2911).  A few additional printer attributes are based on definitions in the Printer MIB (RFC 1759).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejP. Fleming¢kaffiliationjHigh NorthdnamekI. McDonaldddatej“hfilenamegrfc3712eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125658hkeywordsÇnobject classesjattributeskobsoletedByÅd7612epages!opublicationDate@>PAgrankingÄcrfcd3712irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexILightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): Schema for Printer ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document describes the Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP), a profile of the Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP), which can provide confidentiality, message authentication, and replay protection to the RTP traffic and to the control traffic for RTP, the Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejM. Baugher¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiD. McGrew¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamejM. Naslund¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamejE. Carrara¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamejK. Norrmanddatej“´hfilenamegrfc3711eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125657hkeywordsÖdSRTPx#Secure Real-time Transport ProtocolnauthenticationgtrafficmcryptographickobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDate@RHgrankingcrfcd3711irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.The Secure Real-time Transport Protocol (SRTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd6904d9335d5506∂habstracty!This memo provides a charter for the Internet Engineering Steering Group (IESG), a management function of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).  It is meant to document the charter of the IESG as it is presently understood.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdietfdfullkietf - IETFdnamedIETFgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemH. Alvestrandddatej“hfilenamegrfc3710eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering Groupdtypedietfbidjdoc-125656hkeywordsÅx#internet engineering steering groupkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@6ìgranking~crfcd3710irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleoAn IESG charterdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8717d5742d3932∂habstractxêThis document specifies a certificate extension for including logotypes in public key certificates and attribute certificates. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamelS. Santesson¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejT. Freemanddatej“hfilenamegrfc3709eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125655hkeywordsÖeX.509xPublic Key Infrastructurenauthenticationwsecurity identificationlcertificateskobsoletedByÅd9399epagesopublicationDate@54granking}crfcd3709irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure: Logotypes in X.509 CertificatesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6170∂habstracty∏TCP and Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) provide notification of duplicate segment receipt through Duplicate Selective Acknowledgement (DSACKs) and Duplicate Transmission Sequence Number (TSN) notification, respectively.  This document presents conservative methods of using this information to identify unnecessary retransmissions for various applications.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPurdue UniversitydnamejE. Blanton¢kaffiliationdICIRdnameiM. Allmanddatej“hfilenamegrfc3708eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125654hkeywordsÜeDSACKx#Duplicate Selective AcknowledgementdSCTPx$Stream Control Transmission ProtocolcTSNxTransmission Sequence NumberkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate@>NGgranking|crfcd3708irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex∏Using TCP Duplicate Selective Acknowledgement (DSACKs) and Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Duplicate Transmission Sequence Numbers (TSNs) to Detect Spurious RetransmissionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjInternet registries expose administrative and operational data via varying directory services.  This document defines functional requirements for the directory services of domain registries and the common base requirements for extending the use of these services for other types of Internet registries.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newtonddatej“hfilenamegrfc3707eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymecrispdfullx3crisp - Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldnamex+Cross Registry Information Service Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125653hkeywordsÅxdirectory services domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@#„granking{crfcd3707irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Cross Registry Internet Service Protocol (CRISP) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊This document describes the method detecting a dead Internet Key Exchange (IKE) peer that is presently in use by a number of vendors.  The method, called Dead Peer Detection (DPD) uses IPSec traffic patterns to minimize the number of IKE messages that are needed to confirm liveness.  DPD, like other keepalive mechanisms, is needed to determine when to perform IKE peer failover, and to reclaim lost resources.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehG. Huang¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamekS. Beaulieu¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamelD. Rochefortddatej“hfilenamegrfc3706eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125652hkeywordsÑwsecurity authenticationsdead peer detectioncdpdikeepalivekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@5∫grankingzcrfcd3706irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJA Traffic-Based Method of Detecting Dead Internet Key Exchange (IKE) PeersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document presents a set of High Capacity Textual Conventions for use in MIB modules which require performance history based upon 15 minute intervals.  The Textual Conventions defined in this document extend the conventions presented in RFC 3593 to 64 bit resolution using the conventions presented in RFC 2856. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvPESA Switching SystemsdnamefB. Ray¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamegR. Abbiddatej“hfilenamegrfc3705eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125651hkeywordsÉcMIBxManagement Information Basex!High Capacity Textual ConventionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@)#grankingycrfcd3705irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhHigh Capacity Textual Conventions for MIB Modules Using Performance History Based on 15 Minute IntervalsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractytBCP 38, RFC 2827, is designed to limit the impact of distributed denial of service attacks, by denying traffic with spoofed addresses access to the network, and to help ensure that traffic is traceable to its correct source network.  As a side effect of protecting the Internet against such attacks, the network implementing the solution also protects itself from this and other attacks, such as spoofed management access to networking equipment.  There are cases when this may create problems, e.g., with multihoming.  This document describes the current ingress filtering operational mechanisms, examines generic issues related to ingress filtering, and delves into the effects on multihoming in particular.  This memo updates RFC 2827.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savoladdatej“´hfilenamegrfc3704eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125650hkeywordsÖcISPxInternet Service ProviderqInternet ProtocolcDOSqDenial of ServicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@[z9grankingxcrfcd3704irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberTetotaletitlex)Ingress Filtering for Multihomed NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8704∂habstracty(This document defines a mapping of the Policy Core Information Model to a form that can be implemented in a directory that uses Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) as its access protocol.  This model defines two hierarchies of object classes: structural classes representing information for representing and controlling policy data as specified in RFC 3060, and relationship classes that indicate how instances of the structural classes are related to each other.  Classes are also added to the LDAP schema to improve the performance of a client's interactions with an LDAP server when the client is retrieving large amounts of policy-related information.  These classes exist only to optimize LDAP retrievals: there are no classes in the information model that correspond to them. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvIntelliden CorporationdnamelJ. Strassner¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehB. Moore¢kaffiliationtLemur Networks, Inc.dnamehR. Moats¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekE. Ellessonddatej“hfilenamegrfc3703eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfpolicydfullxpolicy - Policy FrameworkdnamepPolicy Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-125649hkeywordsÑdLDAPx%Lightweight Directory Access ProtocolkPolicy Coreomapping classeskobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDate@>M¿grankingwcrfcd3703irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Policy Core Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) SchemadtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4104∂habstractyAs Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) services are deployed on the Internet, there is a need for authentication, authorization, and accounting of SIP sessions.  This document sets out the basic requirements for this work.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej…®hfilenamegrfc3702eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125648hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@5bgrankingvcrfcd3702irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdAuthentication, Authorization, and Accounting Requirements for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyøThe 6bone was established in 1996 by the IETF as an IPv6 Testbed network to enable various IPv6 testing as well as to assist in the transitioning of IPv6 into the Internet.  It operates under the IPv6 address allocation 3FFE::/16 from RFC 2471.  As IPv6 is beginning its production deployment it is appropriate to plan for the phaseout of the 6bone.  This document establishes a plan for a multi-year phaseout of the 6bone and its address allocation on the assumption that the IETF is the appropriate place to determine this.  This document obsoletes RFC 2471, "IPv6 Testing Address Allocation", December, 1998.  RFC 2471 will become historic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegR. Fink¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hindenddatej“™hfilenamegrfc3701eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125647hkeywordsÖhinternethprotocolkprotocotypehsoftwarelarchitecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@HËågrankingucrfcd3701irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex06bone (IPv6 Testing Address Allocation) PhaseoutdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty0This memo contains a snapshot of the state of standardization of protocols used in the Internet as of July 22, 2004.  It lists official protocol standards and Best Current Practice RFCs; it is not a complete index to the RFC series.  The latest version of this memo is designated STD 1. [STANDARDS-TRACK]gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Ginozaddatej“Nhfilenamegrfc3700eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43436hkeywordsÅrOfficial ProtocolskobsoletedByÅd5000epages6opublicationDateA
‚ñgrankingtcrfcd3700irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex$Internet Official Protocol StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides a collection of matching rules for use with the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP).  As these matching rules are simple adaptations of matching rules specified for use with the X.500 Directory, most are already in wide use. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej“hfilenamegrfc3698eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125646hkeywordsÉdLDAPx%lightweight directory access protocolrdirectory serviceskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate@>Mgrankingrcrfcd3698irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP): Additional Matching RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4517∂habstracty˘This document specifies the IPv6 Flow Label field and the minimum requirements for IPv6 source nodes labeling flows, IPv6 nodes forwarding labeled packets, and flow state establishment methods.  Even when mentioned as examples of possible uses of the flow labeling, more detailed requirements for specific use cases are out of scope for this document.  The usage of the Flow Label field enables efficient IPv6 flow classification based only on IPv6 main header fields in fixed positions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelJ. Rajahalme¢kaffiliationjTranswitchdnamehA. Conta¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Deeringddatej“™hfilenamegrfc3697eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125645hkeywordsÑdIPv6jFlow LabelenodesgpacketskobsoletedByÅd6437epages	opublicationDate@E
grankingqcrfcd3697irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIPv6 Flow Label SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty‚Many Internet applications have been designed to deduce top-level domains (or other domain name labels) from partial information.  The introduction of new top-level domains, especially non-country-code ones, has exposed flaws in some of the methods used by these applications.  These flaws make it more difficult, or impossible, for users of the applications to access the full Internet.  This memo discusses some of the techniques that have been used and gives some guidance for minimizing their negative impact as the domain name environment evolves.  This document draws summaries of the applicable rules together in one place and supplies references to the actual standards.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejJ. Klensinddatej“hfilenamegrfc3696eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125644hkeywordsÇqtop-level domainsdtldskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@>•ªgrankingpcrfcd3696irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex?Application Techniques for Checking and Transformation of NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis document introduces some Forward Error Correction (FEC) schemes that supplement the FEC schemes described in RFC 3452.  The primary benefits of these additional FEC schemes are that they are designed for reliable bulk delivery of large objects using a more compact FEC Payload ID, and they can be used to sequentially deliver blocks of an object of indeterminate length.  Thus, they more flexibly support different delivery models with less packet header overhead.  This document also describes the Fully-Specified FEC scheme corresponding to FEC Encoding ID 0.  This Fully-Specified FEC scheme requires no FEC coding and is introduced primarily to allow simple interoperability testing between different implementations of protocol instantiations that use the FEC building block.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekL. Vicisanoddatej“hfilenamegrfc3695eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125643hkeywordsâcFECxForward Error Correctiongcontentfstreamhdeliveryimulticastqinternet protocolgcompactgschemeskobsoletedByÅd5445epagesopublicationDate@>LNgrankingocrfcd3695irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Compact Forward Error Correction (FEC) SchemesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This document provides some analysis of threats against the Geopriv protocol architecture.  It focuses on protocol threats, threats that result from the storage of data by entities in the architecture, and threats posed by the abuse of information yielded by Geopriv.  Some security properties that meet these threats are enumerated as a reference for Geopriv requirements.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Danley¢kaffiliation`dnamekD. Mulligan¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Morris¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Petersonddatej—˚hfilenamegrfc3694eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-125642hkeywordsÇpprivacy securitypdata informationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@5Ëgrankingncrfcd3694irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Threat Analysis of the Geopriv ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6280∂habstractyQLocation-based services, navigation applications, emergency services, management of equipment in the field, and other location-dependent services need geographic location information about a Target (such as a user, resource or other entity).  There is a need to securely gather and transfer location information for location services, while at the same time protect the privacy of the individuals involved.  This document focuses on the authorization, security and privacy requirements for such location-dependent services.  Specifically, it describes the requirements for the Geopriv Location Object (LO) and for the protocols that use this Location Object.  This LO is envisioned to be the primary data structure used in all Geopriv protocol exchanges to securely transfer location data.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Cuellar¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Morris¢kaffiliation`dnamekD. Mulligan¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Polkddatej“hfilenamegrfc3693eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymggeoprivdfullx%geopriv - Geographic Location/PrivacydnamexGeographic Location/Privacydtypebwgbidjdoc-125641hkeywordsÉpsecurity privacybloolocation objectkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@5ägrankingmcrfcd3693irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitletGeopriv RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd6280d7459∑habstracty÷When experimenting with or extending protocols, it is often necessary to use some sort of protocol number or constant in order to actually test or experiment with the new function, even when testing in a closed environment.  For example, to test a new DHCP option, one needs an option number to identify the new function.  This document recommends that when writing IANA Considerations sections, authors should consider assigning a small range of numbers for experimentation purposes that implementers can use when testing protocol extensions or other new features.  This document reserves some ranges of numbers for experimentation purposes in specific protocols where the need to support experimentation has been identified.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiT. Nartenddatej“3hfilenamegrfc3692eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125640hkeywordsÑdIANAx#Internet Assigned Numbers AuthorityfvaluesoimplementationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@˚µgrankinglcrfcd3692irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberRetotaletitlex<Assigning Experimental and Testing Numbers Considered UsefuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines an UNSELECT command that can be used to close the current mailbox in an Internet Message Access Protocol - version 4 (IMAP4) session without expunging it.  Certain types of IMAP clients need to release resources associated with the selected mailbox without selecting a different mailbox.  While IMAP4 provides this functionality (via a SELECT command with a nonexistent mailbox name or reselecting the same mailbox with EXAMINE command), a more clean solution is desirable. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamekA. Melnikovddatej“hfilenamegrfc3691eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125639hkeywordsÉdIMAPx Internet Message Access Protocolvmailbox session clientkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@6íægrankingkcrfcd3691irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) UNSELECT commanddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document presents a list of requirements in support of Emergency Telecommunications Service (ETS) within the context of IP telephony.  It is an extension to the general requirements presented in RFC 3689.  Solutions to these requirements are not presented in this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamekK. Carlberg¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnamekR. Atkinsonddatej…ßhfilenamegrfc3690eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfieprepdfullx(ieprep - Internet Emergency PreparednessdnamexInternet Emergency Preparednessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125638hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@#‚égrankingjcrfcd3690irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGIP Telephony Requirements for Emergency Telecommunication Service (ETS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document presents a list of general requirements in support of Emergency Telecommunications Service (ETS).  Solutions to these requirements are not presented in this document.  Additional requirements pertaining to specific applications, or types of applications, are to be specified in separate document(s).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamekK. Carlberg¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnamekR. Atkinsonddatej…ßhfilenamegrfc3689eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfieprepdfullx(ieprep - Internet Emergency PreparednessdnamexInternet Emergency Preparednessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125637hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate@#‚4grankingicrfcd3689irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBGeneral Requirements for Emergency Telecommunication Service (ETS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxÒThis document describes an IANA maintained registry for IETF standards which use Extensible Markup Language (XML) related items such as Namespaces, Document Type Declarations (DTDs), Schemas, and Resource Description Framework (RDF) Schemas.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamekM. Meallingddatej“3hfilenamegrfc3688eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125636hkeywordsÇcXMLxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@˚Ygrankinghcrfcd3688irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberQetotaletitleuThe IETF XML RegistrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty;The syntaxes of attributes in a Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) or X.500 directory range from simple data types, such as text string, integer, or boolean, to complex structured data types, such as the syntaxes of the directory schema operational attributes.  Matching rules defined for the complex syntaxes usually only provide the most immediately useful matching capability.  This document defines generic matching rules that can match any user selected component parts in an attribute value of any arbitrarily complex attribute syntax. [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsAdacel TechnologiesdnamegS. Leggddatej“hfilenamegrfc3687eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125635hkeywordsÅrsyntax data schemakobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate@)"õgrankinggcrfcd3687irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOLightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) and X.500 Component Matching RulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx«This document describes the use of Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Counter Mode, with an explicit initialization vector, as an IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) confidentiality mechanism.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej“2hfilenamegrfc3686eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125634hkeywordsácAESxAdvanced Encryption Standardx IPsec Internet Protocol SecuritycESPxEncapsulating Security Payloaduauthentication vectorlcipher blockkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@˚grankingfcrfcd3686irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeUsing Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Counter Mode With IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThe SIEVE mail filtering language "spamtest" and "virustest" extensions permit users to use simple, portable commands for spam and virus tests on email messages.  Each extension provides a new test using matches against numeric 'scores'.  It is the responsibility of the underlying SIEVE implementation to do the actual checks that result in values returned by the tests. [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCyrusoft International, Inc.dnamehC. Dabooddatej“hfilenamegrfc3685eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125633hkeywordsÇhmessagesqportable commandskobsoletedByÅd5235epages	opublicationDate@#·Œgrankingecrfcd3685irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8SIEVE Email Filtering: Spamtest and VirusTest ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^Topology Dissemination Based on Reverse-Path Forwarding (TBRPF) is a proactive, link-state routing protocol designed for mobile ad-hoc networks, which provides hop-by-hop routing along shortest paths to each destination.  Each node running TBRPF computes a source tree (providing paths to all reachable nodes) based on partial topology information stored in its topology table, using a modification of Dijkstra's algorithm.  To minimize overhead, each node reports only *part* of its source tree to neighbors.  TBRPF uses a combination of periodic and differential updates to keep all neighbors informed of the reported part of its source tree.  Each node also has the option to report additional topology information (up to the full topology), to provide improved robustness in highly mobile networks.  TBRPF performs neighbor discovery using "differential" HELLO messages which report only *changes* in the status of neighbors.  This results in HELLO messages that are much smaller than those of other link-state routing protocols such as OSPF.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqSRI InternationaldnamehR. Ogier¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejF. Templin¢kaffiliationqSRI InternationaldnamehM. Lewisddatej“hfilenamegrfc3684eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-125632hkeywordsäeTBRPFx7Topology Dissemination Based on Reverse-Path Forwardingqproactive routingoad-hoc networksrneighbor discoveryrlink-state routingumobile ad-hoc networksmobile mesh networktpacket radio networkwwireless communicationskobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDate@6íngrankingdcrfcd3684irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Topology Dissemination Based on Reverse-Path Forwarding (TBRPF)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9141∂habstractyAll self-governing bodies have ways of managing the scope of participant interaction.  The IETF uses a consensus-driven process for developing computer-communications standards in an open fashion.  An important part of this consensus-driven process is the pervasive use of mailing lists for discussion.  Notably, in a small number of cases, a participant has engaged in a "denial-of-service" attack to disrupt the consensus-driven process.  Regrettably, as these bad faith attacks become more common, the IETF needs to establish a practice that reduces or eliminates these attacks.  This memo recommends such a practice for use by the IETF.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxDover Beach Consulting, Inc.dnamegM. Roseddatej °hfilenamegrfc3683eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125631hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd9945epagesopublicationDate@RG°grankingccrfcd3683irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<A Practice for Revoking Posting Rights to IETF Mailing ListsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd9245µhabstractyQThe use of a packet's Time to Live (TTL) (IPv4) or Hop Limit (IPv6) to protect a protocol stack from CPU-utilization based attacks has been proposed in many settings (see for example, RFC 2461).  This document generalizes these techniques for use by other protocols such as BGP (RFC 1771), Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP), Bidirectional Forwarding Detection, and Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) (RFC 3036).  While the Generalized TTL Security Mechanism (GTSM) is most effective in protecting directly connected protocol peers, it can also provide a lower level of protection to multi-hop sessions.  GTSM is not directly applicable to protocols employing flooding mechanisms (e.g., multicast), and use of multi-hop GTSM should be considered on a case-by-case basis.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. ZiningauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationjconsultantdnamegV. Gill¢kaffiliationjconsultantdnamejJ. Heasley¢kaffiliationjconsultantdnamehD. Meyerddatej“hfilenamegrfc3682eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125630hkeywordsÇltime to liveppacket hop limitkobsoletedByÅd5082epagesopublicationDate@5grankingbcrfcd3682irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-The Generalized TTL Security Mechanism (GTSM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx÷This document discusses the need for delegation of the E.F.F.3.IP6.ARPA DNS zone in order to enable reverse lookups for 6bone addresses, and makes specific recommendations for the process needed to accomplish this.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIIJdnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegR. Finkddatej“2hfilenamegrfc3681eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125629hkeywordsÇcdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@í’grankingacrfcd3681irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberPetotaletitlexDelegation of E.F.F.3.IP6.ARPAdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyTThis document defines a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) event package for registrations.  Through its REGISTER method, SIP allows a user agent to create, modify, and delete registrations.  Registrations can also be altered by administrators in order to enforce policy.  As a result, these registrations represent a piece of state in the network that can change dynamically.  There are many cases where a user agent would like to be notified of changes in this state.  This event package defines a mechanism by which those user agents can request and obtain such notifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej“©hfilenamegrfc3680eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125628hkeywordsÖcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolhREGISTERrevent package namexevent package parameterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@kj'granking`crfcd3680irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCA Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Event Package for RegistrationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6140∂habstractyPrior to the publication of RFC 2489 (which was updated by RFC 2939), several option codes were assigned to proposed Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) options that were subsequently never used.  This document lists those unused option codes and directs IANA to make these option codes available for assignment to other DHCP options in the future.  The document also lists several option codes that are not currently documented in an RFC but should not be made available for reassignment to future DHCP options.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Dromsddatej“2hfilenamegrfc3679eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125627hkeywordsàgdynamicdhostmconfigurationhprotocolhinternethassignedgnumbersiauthoritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@∏Ògranking_crfcd3679irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Unused Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Option CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8910∂habstractyaThe Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMPv3) for IPv4 and the Multicast Listener Discovery (MLDv2) for IPv6 add the capability for applications to express source filters on multicast group memberships, which allows receiver applications to determine the set of senders (sources) from which to accept multicast traffic.  This capability also simplifies support of one-to-many type multicast applications.  This document specifies new socket options and functions to manage source filters for IP Multicast group memberships.  It also defines the socket structures to provide input and output arguments to these new application program interfaces (APIs).  These extensions are designed to provide access to the source filtering features, while introducing a minimum of change into the system and providing complete compatibility for existing multicast applications.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationmAT&T ResearchdnameiB. Fenner¢kaffiliationlStardust.comdnamehB. Quinnddatej“2hfilenamegrfc3678eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-125626hkeywordsÜbipqinternet protocolxapplication program interfacedapiseinputfoutputkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@íygranking^crfcd3678irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Socket Interface Extensions for Multicast Source FiltersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxThis memo outlines the process by which the IETF makes a selection of an Internet Society (ISOC) Board of Trustees appointment.fadNametHarald T. AlvestrandgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationcIABdnamexInternet Architecture Boardddatej—‡hfilenamegrfc3677eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125625hkeywordsÖdIETFxInternet Engineering Task ForcedISOCpInternet SocietyqBoard of TrusteeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?◊ª™granking]crfcd3677irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberMetotaletitlex1IETF ISOC Board of Trustee Appointment ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This specification establishes two parameters (Format and DelSP) to be used with the Text/Plain media type.  In the presence of these parameters, trailing whitespace is used to indicate flowed lines and a canonical quote indicator is used to indicate quoted lines.  This results in an encoding which appears as normal Text/Plain in older implementations, since it is in fact normal Text/Plain, yet provides for superior wrapping/flowing, and quoting.  This document supersedes the one specified in RFC 2646, "The Text/Plain Format Parameter", and adds the DelSp parameter to accommodate languages/coded character sets in which ASCII spaces are not used or appear rarely. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejR. Gellensddatej“␀hfilenamegrfc3676eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125624hkeywordsÜjmedia typedmimelmultipurposehinternetdmailiextensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@5 granking\crfcd3676irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*The Text/Plain Format and DelSp ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty Periodically there are proposals to mandate the use of a special top level name or an IP address bit to flag "adult" or "unsafe" material or the like.  This document explains why this is an ill considered idea from the legal, philosophical, and particularly, the technical points of view.fadNametHarald T. AlvestrandgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuMotorola LaboratoriesdnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej“␀hfilenamegrfc3675eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125623hkeywordsÑqtop level domainsctldlip addressesxinternet protocol filterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@!Ç…granking[crfcd3675irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.sex Considered DangerousdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) is an extensible protocol with numerous elective features.  This document introduces a general mechanism for discovery of elective features and extensions which cannot be discovered using existing mechanisms.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej—‡hfilenamegrfc3674eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125622hkeywordsÇselective extensionsjmechanismskobsoletedByÅd4512epagesopublicationDate?‚F‰grankingZcrfcd3674irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAFeature Discovery in Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) supports a mechanism for requesting the return of all user attributes but not all operational attributes.  This document describes an LDAP extension which clients may use to request the return of all operational attributes.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej—‡hfilenamegrfc3673eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125621hkeywordsÇouser mechanismsiextensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?‚FîgrankingYcrfcd3673irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTLightweight Directory Access Protocol version 3 (LDAPv3): All Operational AttributesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔIn X.500 directories, subentries are special entries used to hold information associated with a subtree or subtree refinement.  This document adapts X.500 subentries mechanisms for use with the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP).fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej—‡hfilenamegrfc3672eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125620hkeywordsÅospecial subtreekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?È˙õgrankingXcrfcd3672irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Subentries in the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractygX.500 collective attributes allow common characteristics to be shared between collections of entries.  This document summarizes the X.500 information model for collective attributes and describes use of collective attributes in LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol).  This document provides schema definitions for collective attributes for use in LDAP.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej—‡hfilenamegrfc3671eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125619hkeywordsÇex.500xinformation model schemakobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate?È˙LgrankingWcrfcd3671irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexICollective Attributes in the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe purpose of this document is to define an information model to describe the quality of service (QoS) mechanisms inherent in different network devices, including hosts.  Broadly speaking, these mechanisms describe the properties common to selecting and conditioning traffic through the forwarding path (datapath) of a network device.  This selection and conditioning of traffic in the datapath spans both major QoS architectures: Differentiated Services and Integrated Services.  This document should be used with the QoS Policy Information Model (QPIM) to model how policies can be defined to manage and configure the QoS mechanisms (i.e., the classification, marking, metering, dropping, queuing, and scheduling functionality) of devices.  Together, these two documents describe how to write QoS policy rules to configure and manage the QoS mechanisms present in the datapaths of devices.  This document, as well as QPIM, are information models.  That is, they represent information independent of a binding to a specific type of repositoryfadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehB. Moore¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiD. Durham¢kaffiliationpINTELLIDEN, Inc.dnamelJ. Strassner¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemA. Westerinen¢kaffiliationhEllacoyadnamehW. Weissddatej“2hfilenamegrfc3670eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpolicydfullxpolicy - Policy FrameworkdnamepPolicy Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-125618hkeywordsÖcQoSrquality of servicedhostonetwork devicesgtraffickobsoletedByÄepagesaopublicationDate?¸dugrankingVcrfcd3670irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGInformation Model for Describing Network Device QoS Datapath MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo lays out a conceptual framework and rules of thumb useful for working groups dealing with Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) issues.  It documents specific examples of how IPR issues have been dealt with in the IETF.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegS. Brimddatej“␀hfilenamegrfc3669eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125617hkeywordsÇcipricopyrightkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@3»QgrankingUcrfcd3669irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Guidelines for Working Groups on Intellectual Property IssuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThe IETF policies about Intellectual Property Rights (IPR), such as patent rights, relative to technologies developed in the IETF are designed to ensure that IETF working groups and participants have as much information about any IPR constraints on a technical proposal as possible.  The policies are also intended to benefit the Internet community and the public at large, while respecting the legitimate rights of IPR holders.  This memo details the IETF policies concerning IPR related to technology worked on within the IETF.  It also describes the objectives that the policies are designed to meet.  This memo updates RFC 2026 and, with RFC 3667, replaces Section 10 of RFC 2026.  This memo also updates paragraph 4 of Section 3.2 of RFC 2028, for all purposes, including reference [2] in RFC 2418.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej—ˇhfilenamegrfc3668eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125616hkeywordsÇcipricopyrightkobsoletedByÅd3979epagesopublicationDate@3»grankingTcrfcd3668irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Intellectual Property Rights in IETF TechnologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycThe IETF policies about rights in Contributions to the IETF are designed to ensure that such Contributions can be made available to the IETF and Internet communities while permitting the authors to retain as many rights as possible.  This memo details the IETF policies on rights in Contributions to the IETF.  It also describes the objectives that the policies are designed to meet.  This memo updates RFC 2026, and, with RFC 3668, replaces Section 10 of RFC 2026.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej—ˇhfilenamegrfc3667eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciprdfullx"ipr - Intellectual Property RightsdnamexIntellectual Property Rightsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125615hkeywordsÉxintellectual property rightsicopyrightciprkobsoletedByÅd3978epagesopublicationDate@3«ègrankingScrfcd3667irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIETF Rights in ContributionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyΩThis document contains best current practice examples of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) call flows showing interworking with the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN).  Elements in these call flows include SIP User Agents, SIP Proxy Servers, and PSTN Gateways.  Scenarios include SIP to PSTN, PSTN to SIP, and PSTN to PSTN via SIP.  PSTN telephony protocols are illustrated using ISDN (Integrated Services Digital Network), ISUP (ISDN User Part), and FGB (Feature Group B) circuit associated signaling.  PSTN calls are illustrated using global telephone numbers from the PSTN and private extensions served on by a PBX (Private Branch Exchange).  Call flow diagrams and message details are shown.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcMCIdnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamejS. Donovan¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamemC. Cunningham¢kaffiliationeSonusdnamejK. Summersddatej—ﬂhfilenamegrfc3666eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125614hkeywordsÉjuser proxyggatewayitelephonykobsoletedByÄepagesvopublicationDate?˘…LgrankingRcrfcd3666irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberLetotaletitlexUSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) Call FlowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty&This document gives examples of Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) call flows.  Elements in these call flows include SIP User Agents and Clients, SIP Proxy and Redirect Servers.  Scenarios include SIP Registration and SIP session establishment.  Call flow diagrams and message details are shown.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcMCIdnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamejS. Donovan¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamemC. Cunningham¢kaffiliationeSonusdnamejK. Summersddatej—ﬂhfilenamegrfc3665eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125613hkeywordsÉjuser agentlclient proxyhredirectkobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDate?˘»„grankingQcrfcd3665irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberKetotaletitlex:Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Basic Call Flow ExamplesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxŒSome implementations of IP Security (IPsec) may want to use a pseudo-random function derived from the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES).  This document describes such an algorithm, called AES-XCBC-PRF-128.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVPN ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej“2hfilenamegrfc3664eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125612hkeywordsàcIKExInternet Key Exchange ProtocoleipseckIP SecuritycAESxadvanced encryption standardcmacxmessage authentication codekobsoletedByÅd4434epagesopublicationDate@í
grankingPcrfcd3664irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKThe AES-XCBC-PRF-128 Algorithm for the Internet Key Exchange Protocol (IKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty?Domain registration data has typically been exposed to the general public via Nicname/Whois for administrative purposes.  This document describes the Referral Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Service, an experimental service using LDAP and well-known LDAP types to make domain administrative data available.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameiA. Newtonddatej—ﬂhfilenamegrfc3663eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125611hkeywordsÑdLDAPx%Lightweight Directory Access Protocolnreferral typesjwell-knownkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?‚H1grankingOcrfcd3663irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexJDomain Administrative Data in Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document proposes a differentiated services per-domain behavior (PDB) whose traffic may be "starved" (although starvation is not strictly required) in a properly functioning network.  This is in contrast to the Internet's "best-effort" or "normal Internet traffic" model, where prolonged starvation indicates network problems.  In this sense, the proposed PDB's traffic is forwarded with a "lower" priority than the normal "best-effort" Internet traffic, thus the PDB is called "Lower Effort" (LE).  Use of this PDB permits a network operator to strictly limit the effect of its traffic on "best-effort"/"normal" or all other Internet traffic.  This document gives some example uses, but does not propose constraining the PDB's use to any particular type of traffic.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrUniv. of KarlsruhednamehR. Bless¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejK. Nichols¢kaffiliationwUniv. of Tuebingen/ICSIdnameiK. Wehrleddatej—ﬂhfilenamegrfc3662eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125610hkeywordsÉotraffic networkbdsblekobsoletedByÅd8622epagesopublicationDate?‚GÎgrankingNcrfcd3662irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexDA Lower Effort Per-Domain Behavior (PDB) for Differentiated ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document provides implementation guidelines for the use of return codes in RFC 3435, Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) Version 1.0.  Return codes in RFC 3435 do not cover all possible specific situations that may ever occur in a gateway.  That is not possible and not necessary.  What is important is to ensure that the Call Agent that receives a return code behaves appropriately and consistently for the given situation.  The purpose of this document is to provide implementation guidelines to ensure that consistency.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Foster¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenC. Sivachelvanddatej—ﬂhfilenamegrfc3661eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125609hkeywordsÉjguidelinesjcall agentnimplementationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?È˚8grankingMcrfcd3661irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex7Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) Return Code UsagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÔThis document provides a basic set of Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) packages.  The generic, line, trunk, handset, RTP, DTMF (Dual Tone Multifrequency), announcement server and script packages are updates of packages from RFC 2705 with additional explanation and in some cases new versions of these packages.  In addition to these, five new packages are defined here.  These are the signal list, resource reservation, media format, supplementary services and digit map extension packages.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Foster¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej—ﬂhfilenamegrfc3660eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125608hkeywordsÜggenericdlineetrunkghandsetddtmfxdual tone multifrequencykobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate?È˙ÙgrankingLcrfcd3660irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Basic Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) PackagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document specifies new FTP commands to obtain listings of remote directories in a defined format, and to permit restarts of interrupted data transfers in STREAM mode.  It allows character sets other than US-ASCII, and also defines an optional virtual file storage structure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpHethmon SoftwarednamejP. Hethmonddatej—Xhfilenamegrfc3659eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfftpextdfullxftpext - Extensions to FTPdnameqExtensions to FTPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125607hkeywordsÑcFTPvfile transfer protocolkstream modeudata transfer storagekobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDateE˝à/grankingKcrfcd3659irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqExtensions to FTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÿThe delegation signer (DS) resource record (RR) is inserted at a zone cut (i.e., a delegation point) to indicate that the delegated zone is digitally signed and that the delegated zone recognizes the indicated key as a valid zone key for the delegated zone.  The DS RR is a modification to the DNS Security Extensions definition, motivated by operational considerations.  The intent is to use this resource record as an explicit statement about the delegation, rather than relying on inference.  This document defines the DS RR, gives examples of how it is used and describes the implications on resolvers.  This change is not backwards compatible with RFC 2535.  This document updates RFC 1035, RFC 2535, RFC 3008 and RFC 3090.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamenO. Gudmundssonddatej—ﬁhfilenamegrfc3658eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125606hkeywordsÖhzone cuthzone keyhsecuritycdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÉd4033d4034d4035epagesopublicationDate?‚GügrankingJcrfcd3658irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+Delegation Signer (DS) Resource Record (RR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3755∂habstractxèThis document specifies the conventions for using the Camellia encryption algorithm for encryption with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx Sony Computer Entertainment Inc.dnameiS. Moriai¢kaffiliationxNTT Software CorporationdnamegA. Katoddatej“1hfilenamegrfc3657eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125605hkeywordsÖcCMSxCryptographic Message Syntaxhsecuritylmail contentckeykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?¸c‘grankingIcrfcd3657irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNUse of the Camellia Encryption Algorithm in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractylAs the demand for high-performance mail delivery agents increases, it becomes apparent that single-machine solutions are inadequate to the task, both because of capacity limits and that the failure of the single machine means a loss of mail delivery for all users.  It is preferable to allow many machines to share the responsibility of mail delivery.  The Mailbox Update (MUPDATE) protocol allows a group of Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) or Post Office Protocol - Version 3 (POP3) servers to function with a unified mailbox namespace.  This document is intended to serve as a reference guide to that protocol.fadNameiNed FreedgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxCarnegie Mellon UniversitydnamemR. Siemborskiddatej—ﬁhfilenamegrfc3656eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125604hkeywordsánMailbox UpdategMUPDATEx%Distributed Mailbox Database Protocoldimapdpop3tpost office protocolx internet message access protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?ﬁT∞grankingHcrfcd3656irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexBThe Mailbox Update (MUPDATE) Distributed Mailbox Database ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyÅThis document alters the specification defined in RFC 2535.  Based on implementation experience, the Authenticated Data (AD) bit in the DNS header is not useful.  This document redefines the AD bit such that it is only set if all answers or records proving that no answers exist in the response has been cryptographically verified or otherwise meets the server's local security policy.fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamemB. Wellington¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamenO. Gudmundssonddatej“Ôhfilenamegrfc3655eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125603hkeywordsÉjDNS-SECEXTcdnsnauthenticationkobsoletedByÉd4033d4034d4035epagesopublicationDate?©KºgrankingGcrfcd3655irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Redefinition of DNS Authenticated Data (AD) bitdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document introduces the Forwarding and Control Element Separation (ForCES) architecture and defines a set of associated terminology.  This document also defines a set of architectural, modeling, and protocol requirements to logically separate the control and data forwarding planes of an IP (IPv4, IPv6, etc.) networking device.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekH. Khosravi¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekT. Andersonddatej“Ôhfilenamegrfc3654eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125602hkeywordsÉfforcesrforwarding controlwelement separation datakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?À¢‚grankingFcrfcd3654irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Requirements for Separation of IP Control and ForwardingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyôXML Signature recommends a standard means for specifying information content to be digitally signed and for representing the resulting digital signatures in XML.  Some applications require the ability to specify a subset of a given XML document as the information content to be signed.  The XML Signature specification meets this requirement with the XPath transform.  However, this transform can be difficult to implement efficiently with existing technologies.  This specification defines a new XML Signature transform to facilitate the development of efficient document subsetting implementations that interoperate under similar performance profiles.  This document is the W3C XML Signature XPath-Filter 2.0 Recommendation.  This document has been reviewed by W3C Members and other interested parties and has been endorsed by the Director as a W3C Recommendation.  It is a stable document and may be used as reference material or cited as a normative reference from another document.  W3C's role in making the Recommendation is to draw attention to the specification and to promote its widespread deployment.  This enhances the functionality and interoperability of the Web.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrPureEdge SolutionsdnamehJ. Boyer¢kaffiliationoBetrusted, Inc.dnameiM. Hughes¢kaffiliationcW3CdnameiJ. Reagleddatej—ﬁhfilenamegrfc3653eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxmldsigdfullx xmldsig - XML Digital SignaturesdnamevXML Digital Signaturesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125601hkeywordsÇxextensible markup languageqdigital signaturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?ﬁTZgrankingEcrfcd3653irfcNumberEestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXML-Signature XPath Filter 2.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÓThe Handle System is a general-purpose global name service that allows secured name resolution and administration over the public Internet.  This document describes the protocol used for client software to access the Handle System for both handle resolution and administration.  The protocol specifies the procedure for a client software to locate the responsible handle server of any given handle.  It also defines the messages exchanged between the client and server for any handle operation.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnamefS. Sun¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnameiS. Reilly¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnameiL. Lannom¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnamejJ. Petroneddatej“Ôhfilenamegrfc3652eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125600hkeywordsÅlname servicekobsoletedByÄepages5opublicationDate?∞SgrankingDcrfcd3652irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.Handle System Protocol (ver 2.1) SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyPThe Handle System is a general-purpose global name service that allows secured name resolution and administration over the public Internet.  This document provides a detailed description of the Handle System namespace, and its data, service, and operation models.  The namespace definition specifies the handle syntax and its semantic structure.  The data model defines the data structures used by the Handle System protocol and any pre-defined data types for carrying out the handle service.  The service model provides definitions of various Handle System components and explains how they work together over the network.  Finally, the Handle System operation model describes its service operation in terms of messages transmitted between client and server, and the client authentication process based on the Handle System authentication protocol.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnamefS. Sun¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnameiS. Reilly¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnameiL. Lannomddatej“Óhfilenamegrfc3651eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125599hkeywordsÅlname servicekobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate?∞grankingCcrfcd3651irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.Handle System Namespace and Service DefinitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty…This document provides an overview of the Handle System in terms of its namespace and service architecture, as well as its relationship to other Internet services such as DNS, LDAP/X.500, and URNs.  The Handle System is a general-purpose global name service that allows secured name resolution and administration over networks such as the Internet.  The Handle System manages handles, which are unique names for digital objects and other Internet resources.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnamefS. Sun¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnameiL. Lannom¢kaffiliationdCNRIdnameiB. Boeschddatej“Óhfilenamegrfc3650eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125598hkeywordsÅlname servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?£h␀grankingBcrfcd3650irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlevHandle System OverviewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThe proposals in this document are experimental.  While they may be deployed in the current Internet, they do not represent a consensus that this is the best method for high-speed congestion control.  In particular, we note that alternative experimental proposals are likely to be forthcoming, and it is not well understood how the proposals in this document will interact with such alternative proposals.  This document proposes HighSpeed TCP, a modification to TCP's congestion control mechanism for use with TCP connections with large congestion windows.  The congestion control mechanisms of the current Standard TCP constrains the congestion windows that can be achieved by TCP in realistic environments.  For example, for a Standard TCP connection with 1500-byte packets and a 100 ms round-trip time, achieving a steady-state throughput of 10 Gbps would require an average congestion window of 83,333 segments, and a packet drop rate of at most one congestion event every 5,000,000,000 packets (or equivalently, at most one congestion event every 1 2/3 hours).  This is widely acknowledged as an unrealistic constraint.  To address his limitation of TCP, this document proposes HighSpeed TCP, and solicits experimentation and feedback from the wider community.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdICSIdnamehS. Floydddatej—›hfilenamegrfc3649ef