38eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125586hkeywordsÜiETHER-MIBcMIBgNetworkjManagementdSNMPhEthernetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?{›granking6crfcd3638irfcNumber6estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKApplicability Statement for Reclassification of RFC 1643 to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyBThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing the Ethernet Wide Area Network (WAN) Interface Sublayer (WIS).  The MIB module defined in this memo is an extension of the Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Interface MIB and is implemented in conjunction with it and with the Ethernet-like Interface MIB, the 802.3 Medium Attachment Unit MIB, the Interfaces Group MIB, and the Inverted Stack Table MIB.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejC.M. Heardddatej”;hfilenamegrfc3637eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125585hkeywordsÇcMIBxManagement Information BasekobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDate?{ngranking5crfcd3637irfcNumber5estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFDefinitions of Managed Objects for the Ethernet WAN Interface SublayerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines objects for managing IEEE 802.3 Medium Attachment Units (MAUs).  This memo obsoletes RFC 2668.  This memo extends that specification by including management information useful for the management of 10 gigabit per second (Gb/s) MAUs.  This memo also obsoletes RFC 1515.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamehJ. Flickddatej”;hfilenamegrfc3636eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125584hkeywordsÑgMAU-MIBjmanagementkinformationdbasekobsoletedByÅd4836epages>opublicationDate?{granking4crfcd3636irfcNumber4estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDefinitions of Managed Objects for IEEE 802.3 Medium Attachment Units (MAUs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÑThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it defines objects for managing Ethernet-like interfaces.  This memo obsoletes RFC 2665.  It updates that specification by including management information useful for the management of 10 Gigabit per second (Gb/s) Ethernet interfaces.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamehJ. Flickddatej”;hfilenamegrfc3635eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdnamexEthernet Interfaces and Hub MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125583hkeywordsÇcMIBxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate?{kgranking3crfcd3635irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDDefinitions of Managed Objects for the Ethernet-like Interface TypesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÿThis document defines a new sub-option for the CableLabs Client Configuration (CCC) Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) option code for conveying the network addresses of Key Distribution Center (KDC) servers.fadNameoMargaret Cullendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiCableLabsdnameiK. Luehrs¢kaffiliationmComcast CablednameiR. Woundy¢kaffiliationoYAS CorporationdnamemJ. Bevilacqua¢kaffiliationoYAS CorporationdnamejN. Davoustddatej—›hfilenamegrfc3634eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125582hkeywordsÜdDHCPx#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocolcKDCwKey Distribution CentercCCCxCableLabs Client ConfigurationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?È˘ﬁgranking2crfcd3634irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexñKey Distribution Center (KDC) Server Address Sub-option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) CableLabs Client Configuration (CCC) OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤The Prefix Delegation options provide a mechanism for automated delegation of IPv6 prefixes using the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP).  This mechanism is intended for delegating a long-lived prefix from a delegating router to a requesting router, across an administrative boundary, where the delegating router does not require knowledge about the topology of the links in the network to which the prefixes will be assigned.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehO. Troan¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Dromsddatej—›hfilenamegrfc3633eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125581hkeywordsÉdDHCPx#Dynamic Host Configuration Protocolxautomated delegation routerkobsoletedByÅd8415epagesopublicationDate?‚FFgranking1crfcd3633irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLIPv6 Prefix Options for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) version 6dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7550d6603µhabstracty'This document updates version 1.1.0 of the Network Solutions Inc. (NSI) Registry Registrar Protocol (RRP) specified in RFC 2832.  The changes described in this document combined with the base specification documented in RFC 2832 specify version 2.0.0 of the VeriSign Registry Registrar Protocol.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnameqS. Veeramachaneni¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamepS. Yalamanchilliddatej“Óhfilenamegrfc3632eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125580hkeywordsácRRPfsharedlregistrationfsystemdgLTDeccTLDptop level domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?À¢granking0crfcd3632irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex8VeriSign Registry Registrar Protocol (RRP) Version 2.0.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty†Security must be built into Internet Protocols for those protocols to offer their services securely.  Many security problems can be traced to improper implementations.  However, even a proper implementation will have security problems if the fundamental protocol is itself exploitable.  Exactly how security should be implemented in a protocol will vary, because of the structure of the protocol itself.  However, there are many protocols for which standard Internet security mechanisms, already developed, may be applicable.  The precise one that is appropriate in any given situation can vary.  We review a number of different choices, explaining the properties of each.fadNamekBert WijnengauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamekS. Bellovin¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamekJ. Schiller¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamejC. Kaufmanddatej—‹hfilenamegrfc3631eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125579hkeywordsÑhprotocolohost compromisecdosqdenial of servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?‚E”granking/crfcd3631irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex$Security Mechanisms for the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxôThis document describes extensions to the OSPF protocol version 2 to support intra-area Traffic Engineering (TE), using Opaque Link State Advertisements.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpProcket NetworksdnamehD. Yeungddatej”:hfilenamegrfc3630eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125578hkeywordsÖdOSPFxopen-shortest path firstcinkestatemadvertisementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?{ˇgranking.crfcd3630irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Traffic Engineering (TE) Extensions to OSPF Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5786d4203∑habstractyGISO/IEC 10646-1 defines a large character set called the Universal Character Set (UCS) which encompasses most of the world's writing systems.  The originally proposed encodings of the UCS, however, were not compatible with many current applications and protocols, and this has led to the development of UTF-8, the object of this memo.  UTF-8 has the characteristic of preserving the full US-ASCII range, providing compatibility with file systems, parsers and other software that rely on US-ASCII values but are transparent to other values.  This memo obsoletes and replaces RFC 2279.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAlis TechnologiesdnamejF. Yergeauddatej“Ìhfilenamegrfc3629eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125577hkeywordsÖeUTF-8cUCSwUniversal Character SetnTransformationfFormatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?∞„granking-crfcd3629irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber?etotaletitlex+UTF-8, a transformation format of ISO 10646dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document defines requirements for a baseline time-stamp policy for Time-Stamping Authorities (TSAs) issuing time-stamp tokens, supported by public key certificates, with an accuracy of one second or better.  A TSA may define its own policy which enhances the policy defined in this document.  Such a policy shall incorporate or further constrain the requirements identified in this document.fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdBulldnameiD. Pinkas¢kaffiliationtSecurity & StandardsdnamegN. Pope¢kaffiliationtSecurity & StandardsdnamegJ. Rossddatej“Ìhfilenamegrfc3628eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125576hkeywordsÇftokenswpublic key certificateskobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate?©J´granking,crfcd3628irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Policy Requirements for Time-Stamping Authorities (TSAs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyêIn some cases, the operational decision may be to use IPv6 /127 prefix lengths, especially on point-to-point links between routers.  Under certain situations, this may lead to one router claiming both addresses due to subnet-router anycast being implemented.  This document discusses the issue and offers a couple of solutions to the problem; nevertheless, /127 should be avoided between two routers.fadNamejRandy BushgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiCSC/FUNETdnameiP. Savoladdatej”:hfilenamegrfc3627eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125575hkeywordsÇmaddress spaceganycastkobsoletedByÅd6547epagesopublicationDate?hÚhgranking+crfcd3627irfcNumber+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex<Use of /127 Prefix Length Between Routers Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–This document describes the Optimized Link State Routing (OLSR) protocol for mobile ad hoc networks.  The protocol is an optimization of the classical link state algorithm tailored to the requirements of a mobile wireless LAN.  The key concept used in the protocol is that of multipoint relays (MPRs).  MPRs are selected nodes which forward broadcast messages during the flooding process.  This technique substantially reduces the message overhead as compared to a classical flooding mechanism, where every node retransmits each message when it receives the first copy of the message.  In OLSR, link state information is generated only by nodes elected as MPRs.  Thus, a second optimization is achieved by minimizing the number of control messages flooded in the network.  As a third optimization, an MPR node may chose to report only links between itself and its MPR selectors.  Hence, as contrary to the classic link state algorithm, partial link state information is distributed in the network.  This information is then used for route calculation.  OLSR provides optimal routes (in terms of number of hops).  The protocol is particularly suitable for large and dense networks as the technique of MPRs works well in this context.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwProject Hipercom, INRIAdnamejT. Clausen¢kaffiliationwProject Hipercom, INRIAdnamejP. Jacquetddatej”hfilenamegrfc3626eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-125574hkeywordsÜdOLSRxOptimized Link State Routingmmobile ad hocxwireless multipoint relayscmprdmprskobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDate?ã3Ngranking*crfcd3626irfcNumber*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Optimized Link State Routing Protocol (OLSR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÛRFC 2658 specifies the streaming format for 3GPP2 13KK vocoder (High Rate Speech Service Option 17 for Wideband Spread Spectrum Communications Systems, also known as QCELP 13K vocoder) data, but does not specify a storage format.  Many implementations have been using the "QCP" file format (named for its file extension) for exchanging QCELP 13K data as well as Enhanced Variable Rate Coder (EVRC) and Selectable Mode Vocoders (SMV) data. (For example, Eudora(r), QuickTime(r), and cmda2000(r) handsets).  This document specifies the QCP file format and updates the audio/qcelp media registration to specify this format for storage, and registers the audio/evrc-qcp and audio/smv-qcp media types for EVRC and SMV (respectively) data stored in this format.fadNameqAllison J. MankingauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamejR. Gellens¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnamelH. Garudadriddatej”:hfilenamegrfc3625eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125573hkeywordsác13keqcelpeaudiojmultimediadvoipxreal time transport protocolx%multipurpose internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?{ëgranking)crfcd3625irfcNumber)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex3The QCP File Format and Media Types for Speech DatadtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÆThe base Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) includes audit commands that only allow a Call Agent to audit endpoint and/or connection state one endpoint at a time.  This document describes a new MGCP package for bulk auditing of a group of gateway endpoints.  It allows a Call Agent to determine the endpoint naming convention, the list of instantiated endpoints as well connection and endpoint state for the group of endpoints.fadNamelJon PetersongauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Foster¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekD. Auerbach¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej“Ìhfilenamegrfc3624eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125572hkeywordsÉjcall agentiendpointsrnaming conventionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?∫íçgranking(crfcd3624irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex<The Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) Bulk Audit PackagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo documents an enhancement to the OSPF routing protocol, whereby an OSPF router can stay on the forwarding path even as its OSPF software is restarted.  This is called "graceful restart" or "non-stop forwarding".  A restarting router may not be capable of adjusting its forwarding in a timely manner when the network topology changes.  In order to avoid the possible resulting routing loops, the procedure in this memo automatically reverts to a normal OSPF restart when such a topology change is detected, or when one or more of the restarting router's neighbors do not support the enhancements in this memo.  Proper network operation during a graceful restart makes assumptions upon the operating environment of the restarting router; these assumptions are also documented.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqSycamore NetworksdnamefJ. Moy¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameqP. Pillay-Esnault¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiA. Lindemddatej“Ìhfilenamegrfc3623eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125571hkeywordsÉdOSPFxopen shortest path firstsnon-stop forwardingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?µdIgranking'crfcd3623irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleuGraceful OSPF RestartdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¢This document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace that will identify various objects within the Liberty Architecture for federated network identity.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamekM. Meallingddatej—ˇhfilenamegrfc3622eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125570hkeywordsÅxfederated network identitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate@!É3granking&crfcd3622irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Liberty Alliance ProjectdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  This document proposes an extension to the Ethernet-like Interfaces MIB with a set of objects for managing Power Sourcing Equipment (PSE).fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoPowerDsine Inc.dnameiA. Berger¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanuddatej—‹hfilenamegrfc3621eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfhubmibdfullx(hubmib - Ethernet Interfac