naldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexJExtreme Networks' Ethernet Automatic Protection Switching (EAPS) Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyZThe Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP) describes a mechanism to connect multiple IP Version 4 Protocol Independent Multicast Sparse-Mode (PIM-SM) domains together.  Each PIM-SM domain uses its own independent Rendezvous Point (RP) and does not have to depend on RPs in other domains.  This document reflects existing MSDP implementations.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjconsultantdnameiB. Fenner¢kaffiliationjconsultantdnamehD. Meyerddatej”hfilenamegrfc3618eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsdpdfullx*msdp - Multicast Source Discovery Protocoldnamex#Multicast Source Discovery Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125566hkeywordsàdMSDPx#Multicast Source Discovery Protocoldipv4fpim-smuindependent multicastksparse-modebrpprendezvous pointkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?ï∑Ågranking"crfcd3618irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyGThe Trivial File Transfer Protocol (TFTP) is a very simple TRIVIAL protocol that has been in use on the Internet for quite a long time.  While this document discourages its continued use, largely due to security concerns, we do define a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) scheme, as well as discuss the protocol's applicability.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegE. LearddatejÀ9hfilenamegrfc3617eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125565hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?ï∑granking!crfcd3617irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexrUniform Resource Identifier (URI) Scheme and Applicability Statement for the Trivial File Transfer Protocol (TFTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name Namespace Identification (URN NID) for the Foundation for Intelligent Physical Agents (FIPA).  This URN NID will be used for identification of standard components published by the FIPA standards body in the area of Agent technology.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrTelecom Italia LabdnamenF. Bellifemine¢kaffiliationdEPFLdnameqI. Constantinescu¢kaffiliationcUPCdnamekS. Willmottddatej”9hfilenamegrfc3616eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125564hkeywordsÇgURN NIDx.Uniform Resource Name Namespace IdentificationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?fP)granking crfcd3616irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex]A Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for Foundation for Intelligent Physical Agents (FIPA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxâThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace that is managed by SWIFT for usage within messages standardized by SWIFT.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeSWIFTdnameiJ. Gustin¢kaffiliationeSWIFTdnameiA. Goyensddatej”9hfilenamegrfc3615eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125563hkeywordsÇqmessaging servicerinterface softwarekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?fOÊgrankingcrfcd3615irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexEA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for SWIFT Financial MessagingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty∑This document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Motion Picture Experts Group (MPEG) for naming persistent resources as part of the MPEG standards.  Example resources include technical documents and specifications, eXtensible Markup Language (XML) Schemas, classification schemes, XML Document Type Definitions (DTDs), namespaces, style sheets, media assets, and other types of resources produced or managed by MPEG.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx IBM T. J. Watson Research CenterdnamehJ. Smithddatej”9hfilenamegrfc3614eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125562hkeywordsàcisox*international organization standardizationjmultimediahmetadatacxmlnclassificationgschemesxdigital rights managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?hÚgrankingcrfcd3614irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexSA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Motion Picture Experts Group (MPEG)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Internet2 Middleware Architecture Committee for Education (MACE).  This namespace is for naming persistent resources defined by MACE, its working groups and other designated subordinates.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsUniv. of WashingtondnameiR. Morgan¢kaffiliationxUniv. of Wisconsin-MadisondnamekK. Hazeltonddatej”hfilenamegrfc3613eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125561hkeywordsÇiinternet2jmiddlewarekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?¶˝Ôgrankingcrfcd3613irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlextDefinition of a Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Middleware Architecture Committee for Education (MACE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2This document provides guidance on when it is advisable to implement some form of Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) restart mechanism and which approach might be more suitable.  The issues and extensions described in this document are equally applicable to RFC 3212, "Constraint-Based LSP Setup Using LDP".fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrOld Dog ConsultingdnameiA. Farrelddatej”9hfilenamegrfc3612eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125560hkeywordsÜdmplsofault tolerenceqhigh availabilityxmultiprotocol label switchingfcr-ldpxhigh availability restartkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?b/ıgrankingcrfcd3612irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXApplicability Statement for Restart Mechanisms for the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThis document defines the Extended Report (XR) packet type for the RTP Control Protocol (RTCP), and defines how the use of XR packets can be signaled by an application if it employs the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  XR packets are composed of report blocks, and seven block types are defined here.  The purpose of the extended reporting format is to convey information that supplements the six statistics that are contained in the report blocks used by RTCP's Sender Report (SR) and Receiver Report (RR) packets.  Some applications, such as multicast inference of network characteristics (MINC) or voice over IP (VoIP) monitoring, require other and more detailed statistics.  In addition to the block types defined here, additional block types may be defined in the future by adhering to the framework that this document provides.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgParis 6dnamekT. Friedman¢kaffiliationlIBM ResearchdnamejR. Caceres¢kaffiliationhTelchemydnamehA. Clarkddatej“Ìhfilenamegrfc3611eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125559hkeywordsâcRTPxreal time transport protocolfpacketdtypecSDPxsession description protocolfblocksbXRoExtended ReportkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDate?©IÃgrankingcrfcd3611irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/RTP Control Protocol Extended Reports (RTCP XR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx∑Counter with CBC-MAC (CCM) is a generic authenticated encryption block cipher mode.  CCM is defined for use with 128-bit block ciphers, such as the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES).fadNamerSteven M. BellovingauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdHifndnamejD. Whiting¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationiMacFergusdnamekN. Fergusonddatej”9hfilenamegrfc3610eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125558hkeywordsÑnauthenticationjencryptionhsecuritygcipherskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?px@grankingcrfcd3610irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexCounter with CBC-MAC (CCM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
This document specifies requirements for a generic route-tracing application.  It also specifies requirements for a protocol that will support that application.  Network operators will use the generic route-tracing application to verify proper operation of the IP forwarding plane.  They will also use the application to discover details regarding tunnels that support IP forwarding.  The generic route-tracing application, specified herein, supports a superset of the functionality that "traceroute" currently offers.  Like traceroute, the generic route-tracing application can discover the forwarding path between two interfaces that are contained by an IP network.  Unlike traceroute, this application can reveal details regarding tunnels that support the IP forwarding path.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcMCIdnameiR. Bonica¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationfSprintdnamehD. Meyerddatej”9hfilenamegrfc3609eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-125557hkeywordsÑjtraceroutekapplicationbIPqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate?{7grankingcrfcd3609irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Tracing Requirements for Generic TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty>This document defines a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) extension header field used in conjunction with responses to REGISTER requests to provide a mechanism by which a registrar may inform a registering user agent (UA) of a service route that the UA may use to request outbound services from the registrar's domain.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpdynamicsoft Inc.dnameiD. Willis¢kaffiliationfSwitchdnamelB. Hoeneisenddatej”hfilenamegrfc3608eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125556hkeywordsÖcSIPxSession Initiation Protocoljuser agentfdomainhregisterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?Ñ•2grankingcrfcd3608irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhSession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extension Header Field for Service Route Discovery During RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5630µhabstractx«This document revisits the so-called Chinese Lottery massively-parallel cryptanalytic attack.  It explores Internet-based analogues to the Chinese Lottery, and their potentially-serious consequences.fadNamerSteven M. BellovingauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehM. Leechddatej”9hfilenamegrfc3607eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125555hkeywordsássecurity encryptioncdesmdata standardxdistributed cryptanalysisncomputer viruslnetwork wormlcodebreakingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?b/ågrankingcrfcd3607irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexLChinese Lottery Cryptanalysis Revisited: The Internet as a Codebreaking TooldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines objects used for managing ATM-based interfaces, devices, and services, in addition to those defined in RFC 2515, the ATM-MIB, to provide additional support for the management of ATM Switched Virtual Connections (SVCs) and ATM Permanent Virtual Connections (PVCs).fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqPedestal NetworksdnameeF. Ly¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegM. Noto¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehA. Smith¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejE. Spiegel¢kaffiliationvTelcordia TechnologiesdnameiK. Tesinkddatej“Ïhfilenamegrfc3606eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgatommibdfullratommib - AToM MIBdnamehAToM MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125554hkeywordsÑcATMxasynchronous transfer modecMIBxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDate?©IPgrankingcrfcd3606irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Definitions of Supplemental Managed Objects for ATM InterfacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≤The Session Description Protocol (SDP) is used to describe the parameters of media streams used in multimedia sessions.  When a session requires multiple ports, SDP assumes that these ports have consecutive numbers.  However, when the session crosses a network address translation device that also uses port mapping, the ordering of ports can be destroyed by the translation.  To handle this, we propose an extension attribute to SDP.fadNamelJon Petersondarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Huitemaddatej”hfilenamegrfc3605eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125553hkeywordsádRTCPxReal Time Control ProtocolcSDPxSession Description Protocolcnatxnetwork access translationlport mappingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?áH«grankingcrfcd3605irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQReal Time Control Protocol (RTCP) attribute in Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx This memo provides requirements for adding optical switching support to the General Switch Management Protocol (GSMP).  It also contains clarifications and suggested changes to the GSMPv3 specification.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekH. Khosravi¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekG. Kullgren¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegS. Shew¢kaffiliationgTellabsdnameiJ. Sadler¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekA. Watanabeddatej”hfilenamegrfc3604eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgsmpdfullx)gsmp - General Switch Management Protocoldnamex"General Switch Management Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125552hkeywordsÑkcontrollersgroutersgformatsecodeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?É¬grankingcrfcd3604irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdRequirements for Adding Optical Support to the General Switch Management Protocol version 3 (GSMPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyIn order to deploy a residential telephone service at very large scale across different domains, it is necessary for trusted elements owned by different service providers to exchange trusted information that conveys customer-specific information and expectations about the parties involved in the call.  This document describes private extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) (RFC3261) for supporting the exchange of customer information and billing information between trusted entities in the PacketCable Distributed Call Signaling Architecture.  These extensions provide mechanisms for access network coordination to prevent theft of service, customer originated trace of harassing calls, support for operator services and emergency services, and support for various other regulatory issues.  The use of the extensions is only applicable within closed administrative domains, or among federations of administrative domains with previously agreed-upon policies where coordination of charging and other functions is required.fadNameqAllison J. MankingauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekW. Marshall¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelF. Andreasenddatej”hfilenamegrfc3603eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfull