This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) interfaces.  This document is a companion to the documents that define Managed Objects for the DS1/E1/DS2/E2 and DS3/E3 Interface Types.  This memo replaces RFC 2558.  Changes relative to RFC 2558 are summarized in the MIB module's REVISION clause. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvTelcordia TechnologiesdnameiK. Tesinkddatej”7hfilenamegrfc3592eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgatommibdfullratommib - AToM MIBdnamehAToM MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125542hkeywordsàcMIBxManagement Information BasedSNMPx"Simple Network Management ProtocolxSynchronous Optical NetworkxSynchronous Digital HierarchyeSONETcSDHkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDate?VyÇgrankingcrfcd3592irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex{Definitions of Managed Objects for the Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) Interface TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty&This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing Optical Interfaces associated with WavelengthDivision Multiplexing systems or characterized by the Optical Transport Network (OTN) in accordance with the OTN architecture defined in ITU-T Recommendation G.872.  The MIB module defined in this memo can be used for performance monitoring and/or configuration of such optical interface. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehH-K. Lam¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejM. Stewart¢kaffiliationcSundnamehA. Huynhddatej”7hfilenamegrfc3591eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgatommibdfullratommib - AToM MIBdnamehAToM MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125541hkeywordsêcMIBxmanagement information basedsnmpx"simple network management protocolcotnxoptical transport networkeitu-tvperformance monitoringmconfigurationddwdmxoptical transmission sessionxoptical multiplex sectionooptical channeldotukeoduktdodukkobsoletedByÄepagesÆopublicationDate?Vygrankingcrfcd3591irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Definitions of Managed Objects for the Optical Interface TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGIt has come to light that there is an issue with the selection of a suitable IPv6 source address for Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) messages when a node is performing stateless address autoconfiguration.  This document is intended to clarify the rules on selecting an IPv6 address to use for MLD messages. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpCaspian NetworksdnamekB. Habermanddatej”7hfilenamegrfc3590eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-125540hkeywordsÖhMLD-IPv6xMulticast Listener Discoveryqinternet protocolfroutergpacketskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?_Y÷grankingcrfcd3590irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLSource Address Selection for the Multicast Listener Discovery (MLD) ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyŒThis document describes the IANA's allocation of a block of Diameter Command Codes for the Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Release 5.  This document does not pass judgment on the usage of these command codes.  Further more, these command codes are for use for Release 5.  For future releases, these codes cannot be reused, but must be allocated according to the Diameter Base specification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy BushgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughneyddatej”7hfilenamegrfc3589eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125539hkeywordsÇdianajallocationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?b.‘grankingcrfcd3589irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPDiameter Command Codes for Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) Release 5dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Diameter base protocol is intended to provide an Authentication, Authorization and Accounting (AAA) framework for applications such as network access or IP mobility.  Diameter is also intended to work in both local Authentication, Authorization & Accounting and roaming situations.  This document specifies the message format, transport, error reporting, accounting and security services to be used by all Diameter applications.  The Diameter base application needs to be supported by all Diameter implementations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoAirespace, Inc.dnamejP. Calhoun¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamejE. Guttman¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkkoddatej”6hfilenamegrfc3588eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcaaadfullx2aaa - Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdnamex,Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125538hkeywordsÑcaaax'authentication authorization accountingkip mobilityvDiameter Base ProtocolkobsoletedByÅd6733epagesìopublicationDate?b.Ñgrankingcrfcd3588irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlevDiameter Base ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÉd6408d5719d5729∂habstractyPThis document obsoletes RFC 2374, "An IPv6 Aggregatable Global Unicast Address Format".  It defined an IPv6 address allocation structure that includes Top Level Aggregator (TLA) and Next Level Aggregator (NLA).  This document makes RFC 2374 and the TLA/NLA structure historic.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Deering¢kaffiliationcSundnamekE. Nordmarkddatej—∏hfilenamegrfc3587eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125537hkeywordsÑhinternethprotocollarchitecturegroutingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?:Öxgrankingcrfcd3587irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"IPv6 Global Unicast Address FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the problem space and solution requirements for developing an IP Security Policy (IPSP) configuration and management framework.  The IPSP architecture provides a scalable, decentralized framework for managing, discovering and negotiating the host and network security policies that govern access, authorization, authentication, confidentiality, data integrity, and other IP Security properties.  This document highlights such architectural components and presents their functional requirements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnamehM. Blaze¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamelA. Keromytis¢kaffiliationxSandelman Software WorksdnamemM. Richardson¢kaffiliationsXapiens CorporationdnamejL. Sanchezddatej—∏hfilenamegrfc3586eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipspdfullxipsp - IP Security PolicydnamerIP Security Policydtypebwgbidjdoc-125536hkeywordsÖdIPSPx!Internet Protocol Security Policyndata integritynauthenticationlhost networkkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate?NXºgrankingcrfcd3586irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&IP Security Policy (IPSP) RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThis document presents an object-oriented information model of IP Security (IPsec) policy designed to facilitate agreement about the content and semantics of IPsec policy, and enable derivations of task- specific representations of IPsec policy such as storage schema, distribution representations, and policy specification languages used to configure IPsec-enabled endpoints.  The information model described in this document models the configuration parameters defined by IPSec.  The information model also covers the parameters found by the Internet Key Exchange protocol (IKE).  Other key exchange protocols could easily be added to the information model by a simple extension.  Further extensions can further be added easily due to the object-oriented nature of the model.  This information model is based upon the core policy classes as defined in the Policy Core Information Model (PCIM) and in the Policy Core Information Model Extensions (PCIMe). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamehJ. Jason¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejL. Rafalow¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiE. Vynckeddatej—∏hfilenamegrfc3585eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipspdfullxipsp - IP Security PolicydnamerIP Security Policydtypebwgbidjdoc-125535hkeywordsàeIPsecxInternet Protocol Securitycikexinternet key exchange protocoldcorex Constrained RESTful EnvironmentsdpcimxPolicy Core Information ModelkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDate?NXIgrankingcrfcd3585irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,IPsec Configuration Policy Information ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty/The purpose of this document is to describe coexistence between version 3 of the Internet-standard Network Management Framework, (SNMPv3), version 2 of the Internet-standard Network Management Framework (SNMPv2), and the original Internet-standard Network Management Framework (SNMPv1).  This document also describes how to convert MIB modules from SMIv1 format to SMIv2 format.  This document obsoletes RFC 2576.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqVibrant SolutionsdnamegR. Frye¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegD. Levi¢kaffiliationxWind River Systems, Inc.dnamekS. Routhier¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnenddatej—∑hfilenamegrfc3584eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125534hkeywordsÉdSNMPx"simple network management protocoltmib information basekobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDate?J?bgranking␀crfcd3584irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberJetotaletitlexmCoexistence between Version 1, Version 2, and Version 3 of the Internet-standard Network Management FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMobile IP ensures correct routing of packets to a mobile node as the mobile node changes its point of attachment to the Internet.  However, it is also required to provide proper Quality of Service (QoS) forwarding treatment to the mobile node's packet stream at the intermediate nodes in the network, so that QoS-sensitive IP services can be supported over Mobile IP.  This document describes requirements for an IP QoS mechanism for its satisfactory operation with Mobile IP.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejH. Chaskarddatej”6hfilenamegrfc3583eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnsisdfullxnsis - Next Steps in SignalingdnamewNext Steps in Signalingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125533hkeywordsÉqinternet protocolorouting packetsdnodekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate?Vx&grankingˇcrfcd3583irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexARequirements of a Quality of Service (QoS) Solution for Mobile IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]This document outlines a set of goals for proposed new IPv6 site- multihoming architectures.  It is recognised that this set of goals is ambitious and that some goals may conflict with others.  The solution or solutions adopted may only be able to satisfy some of the goals presented here.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcISCdnamehJ. Abley¢kaffiliationoLayer8 NetworksdnamehB. Black¢kaffiliationoAOL Time WarnerdnamegV. Gillddatej—∑hfilenamegrfc3582eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmulti6dfullx!multi6 - Site Multihoming in IPv6dnamexSite Multihoming in IPv6dtypebwgbidjdoc-125532hkeywordsÇqinternet protocolfmulti6kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate?E<7granking˛crfcd3582irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Goals for IPv6 Site-Multihoming ArchitecturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) operates over UDP and TCP, among others.  When used with UDP, responses to requests are returned to the source address the request came from, and to the port written into the topmost Via header field value of the request.  This behavior is not desirable in many cases, most notably, when the client is behind a Network Address Translator (NAT).  This extension defines a new parameter for the Via header field, called "rport", that allows a client to request that the server send the response back to the source IP address and port from which the request originated. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej—∑hfilenamegrfc3581eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125531hkeywordsÑcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolxSymmetric Response Routingtreport client serverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?K„mgranking˝crfcd3581irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTAn Extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for Symmetric Response RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyrThis document provides suggestions on Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) usage by IEEE 802.1X Authenticators.  The material in this document is also included within a non-normative Appendix within the IEEE 802.1X specification, and is being presented as an IETF RFC for informational purposes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy BushgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationwHewlett Packard CompanydnamejP. Congdon¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationpTrapeze NetworksdnamehA. Smith¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegG. Zorn¢kaffiliationiEnterasysdnamehJ. Roeseddatej”6hfilenamegrfc3580eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125530hkeywordsÉcAAAnauthenticationxauthorization and accountingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?Vw•granking¸crfcd3580irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPIEEE 802.1X Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) Usage GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7268∂habstracty®This document defines Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) support for the Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), an authentication framework which supports multiple authentication mechanisms.  In the proposed scheme, the Network Access Server (NAS) forwards EAP packets to and from the RADIUS server, encapsulated within EAP-Message attributes.  This has the advantage of allowing the NAS to support any EAP authentication method, without the need for method- specific code, which resides on the RADIUS server.  While EAP was originally developed for use with PPP, it is now also in use with IEEE 802.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationiAirespacednamejP. Calhounddatej”5hfilenamegrfc3579eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125529hkeywordsÜfRADIUSjencryptioncNASgNetworkfAccessfServerkobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDate?VwHgranking˚crfcd3579irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhRADIUS (Remote Authentication Dial In User Service) Support For Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5080∂habstractyJThis document describes a way to map Integrated Services Digital Network User Part (ISUP) overlap signalling to Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  This mechanism might be implemented when using SIP in an environment where part of the call involves interworking with the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamekA. B. Roach¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationeCienadnamefL. Ongddatej—∂hfilenamegrfc3578eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125528hkeywordsàdpstnx!public switched telephone networkdISDNx#Integrated Services Digital NetworkdISUPiUser PartcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?AW•granking˙crfcd3578irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇMapping of Integrated Services Digital Network (ISDN) User Part (ISUP) Overlap Signalling to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx€The Remote Monitoring (RMON) Framework consists of a number of interrelated documents.  This memo describes these documents and how they relate to one another.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamemS. Waldbusser¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamegR. Cole¢kaffiliationkVerio, Inc.dnamenC. Kalbfleisch¢kaffiliationeAvayadnamelD. Romascanuddatej—µhfilenamegrfc3577eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125527hkeywordsÅxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?:ÑÙgranking˘crfcd3577irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBIntroduction to the Remote Monitoring (RMON) Family of MIB ModulesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty~This document describes a currently deployed extension to the Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) protocol, allowing dynamic changes to a user session, as implemented by network access server products.  This includes support for disconnecting users and changing authorizations applicable to a user session.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy BushgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehM. Chiba¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Dommety¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiM. Eklund¢kaffiliationvCircular Logic, UnLtd.dnameiD. Mitton¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehB. Abobaddatej“Mhfilenamegrfc3576eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125526hkeywordsÇcnasunetwork access serverkobsoletedByÅd5176epagesopublicationDate?(YÏgranking¯crfcd3576irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexWDynamic Authorization Extensions to Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx~This document describes the IANA considerations for the Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Abobaddatej“Mhfilenamegrfc3575eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125525hkeywordsàdIANAx#Internet Assigned Numbers AuthorityjencryptioncNASgNetworkmAccess ServerfRADIUSx*Remote Authentication Dial In User ServicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?(YÅgranking˜crfcd3575irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKIANA Considerations for RADIUS (Remote Authentication Dial In User Service)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6929∂habstractyiThis document describes different scenarios in Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) defined packet network, i.e., General Packet Radio Service (GPRS) that would need IP version 6 and IP version 4 transition.  The focus of this document is on the scenarios where the User Equipment (UE) connects to nodes in other networks, e.g., in the Internet.  GPRS network internal transition scenarios, i.e., between different GPRS elements in the network, are out of scope.  The purpose of the document is to list the scenarios for further discussion and study.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Soininenddatej—µhfilenamegrfc3574eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymev6opsdfullwv6ops - IPv6 OperationsdnameoIPv6 Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125524hkeywordsÖx#third generation parnership projectfpacketdipv6dipv4hinternetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?AW6grankingˆcrfcd3574irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Transition Scenarios for 3GPP NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThe Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) defines a mechanism for tunneling Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) sessions.  It is common for these PPP sessions to be established using modems connected over the public switched telephone network.  One of the standards governing modem operation defines procedures that enable a client modem to put the call on hold and later, re-establish the modem link with minimal delay and without having to redial.  While the modem call is on hold, the client phone line can be used to place or receive other calls.  The L2TP base protocol does not provide any means to signal these events from the L2TP Access Controller (LAC), where the modem is physically connected, to the L2TP Network Server (LNS), where the PPP session is handled.  This document describes a method to let the LNS know when a client modem connected to a LAC has placed the call on hold. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiI. Goyretddatej“Mhfilenamegrfc3573eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125523hkeywordsÖcpppnpoint-to-pointdpstnx'public switched telephone network. L2TPxLayer 2 Tunneling ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?/hgrankingıcrfcd3573irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGSignalling of Modem-On-Hold status in Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol (L2TP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyHMultiple Access Protocol over SONET/SDH (MAPOS) is a high-speed link- layer protocol that provides multiple access capability over a Synchronous Optical NETwork/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH).  This document specifies the frame format for encapsulating an IPv6 datagram in a MAPOS frame.  It also specifies the method of forming IPv6 interface identifiers, the method of detecting duplicate addresses, and the format of the Source/Target Link-layer Addresses option field used in IPv6 Neighbor Discovery messages.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas NartengauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxNTT Network Innovation LabsdnamehT. Ogura¢kaffiliationxNTT Network Innovation LabsdnamekM. Maruyama¢kaffiliationrWerk Mikro SystemsdnamejT. Yoshidaddatej“Mhfilenamegrfc3572eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125522hkeywordsÉdipv6xsynchronous optical networkxsynchronous digital hierarchykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?ÎÀgrankingÙcrfcd3572irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPInternet Protocol Version 6 over MAPOS (Multiple Access Protocol Over SONET/SDH)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8064∂habstracty This document describes a portion of the Policy Information Base (PIB) to control policy usage collection and reporting in a device.  The provisioning classes specified here allow a Policy Decision Point (PDP) to select which policy objects should collect usage information, what information should be collected and when it should be reported.  This PIB requires the presence of other PIBs (defined elsewhere) that provide the policy objects from which usage information is collected.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcMCIdnamejD. Rawlins¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekA. Kulkarni¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejK. Ho Chan¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelM. Bokaemper¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Duttddatej—µhfilenamegrfc3571eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrapdfullx"rap - Resource Allocation ProtocoldnamexResource Allocation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125521hkeywordsÅcpibkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate?1,ÙgrankingÛcrfcd3571irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Framework Policy Information Base for Usage FeedbackdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕIn describing content internetworking as a technology targeted for use in production networks, it is useful to provide examples of the sequence of events that may occur when two content networks decide to interconnect.  The scenarios presented here seek to provide some concrete examples of what content internetworking is, and also to provide a basis for evaluating content internetworking proposals.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuMedia Publisher, Inc.dnamejP. Rzewski¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefM. Day¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekD. Gillettiddatej“Mhfilenamegrfc3570eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymccdidfullx*cdi - Content Distribution Internetworkingdnamex$Content Distribution Internetworkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125520hkeywordsÅsproduction networkskobsoletedByÅd6770epagesopublicationDate?%ôTgrankingÚcrfcd3570irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex'Content Internetworking (CDI) ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆThe purpose of this document is to provide an overview of Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) and issues related to its deployment.  It discusses how the SSM service model addresses the challenges faced in inter-domain multicast deployment, changes needed to routing protocols and applications to deploy SSM and interoperability issues with current multicast service models.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationfSprintdnamepS. Bhattacharyyaddatej“Mhfilenamegrfc3569eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcssmdfullxssm - Source-Specific MulticastdnamexSource-Specific Multicastdtypebwgbidjdoc-125519hkeywordsÇtrouting applicationsxdeployment interoperabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?V√grankingÒcrfcd3569irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.An Overview of Source-Specific Multicast (SSM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractykThis document presents a summary of Request-Routing techniques that are used to direct client requests to surrogates based on various policies and a possible set of metrics.  The document covers techniques that were commonly used in the industry on or before December 2000.  In this memo, the term Request-Routing represents techniques that is commonly called content routing or content redirection.  In principle, Request-Routing techniques can be classified under: DNS Request-Routing, Transport-layer Request-Routing, and Application-layer Request-Routing.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiA. Barbir¢kaffiliationpStorigen SystemsdnamegB. Cain¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamegR. Nair¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamemO. Spatscheckddatej“Lhfilenamegrfc3568eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymccdidfullx*cdi - Content Distribution Internetworkingdnamex$Content Distribution Internetworkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125518hkeywordsÉgmetricsgroutingkredirectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?–Ògrankingcrfcd3568irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Known Content Network (CN) Request-Routing MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstracty?This document describes the authentication of Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Protocol Data Units (PDUs) using the Hashed Message Authentication Codes - Message Digest 5 (HMAC-MD5) algorithm as found in RFC 2104.  IS-IS is specified in International Standards Organization (ISO) 10589, with extensions to support Internet Protocol version 4 (IPv4) described in RFC 1195.  The base specification includes an authentication mechanism that allows for multiple authentication algorithms.  The base specification only specifies the algorithm for cleartext passwords.  This document proposes an extension to that specification that allows the use of the HMAC-MD5 authentication algorithm to be used in conjunction with the existing authentication mechanisms.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpProcket NetworksdnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationpExtreme NetworksdnamekR. Atkinsonddatej“Lhfilenamegrfc3567eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125517hkeywordsÇcisox$international standards organizationkobsoletedByÅd5304epagesopublicationDate?.'grankingÔcrfcd3567irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOIntermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Cryptographic AuthenticationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyLA Message Authentication Code (MAC) is a key-dependent one way hash function.  One popular way to construct a MAC algorithm is to use a block cipher in conjunction with the Cipher-Block-Chaining (CBC) mode of operation.  The classic CBC-MAC algorithm, while secure for messages of a pre-selected fixed length, has been shown to be insecure across messages of varying lengths such as the type found in typical IP datagrams.  This memo specifies the use of AES in CBC mode with a set of extensions to overcome this limitation.  This new algorithm is named AES-XCBC-MAC-96. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamelRuss Housleydarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamejS. Frankel¢kaffiliationeInteldnamejH. Herbertddatej”1hfilenamegrfc3566eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125516hkeywordsÑeIPsecxInternet Protocol Securitynauthenticationmhash securitykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?fOégrankingÓcrfcd3566irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4The AES-XCBC-MAC-96 Algorithm and Its Use With IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx∞This document specifies the conventions for using the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) algorithm for encryption with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaadddatej“Lhfilenamegrfc3565eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125515hkeywordsÜcAESxAdvanced Encryption StandardcCMSxCryptographic Message Syntaxhsecuritymdata encodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?–xgrankingÌcrfcd3565irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhUse of the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Encryption Algorithm in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyòThis document presents Service Provider requirements for support of Differentiated Services (Diff-Serv)-aware MPLS Traffic Engineering (DS- TE).  Its objective is to provide guidance for the definition, selection and specification of a technical solution addressing these requirements.  Specification for this solution itself is outside the scope of this document.  A problem statement is first provided.  Then, the document describes example applications scenarios identified by Service Providers where existing MPLS Traffic Engineering mechanisms fall short and Diff-Serv-aware Traffic Engineering can address the needs.  The detailed requirements that need to be addressed by the technical solution are also reviewed.  Finally, the document identifies the evaluation criteria that should be considered for selection and definition of the technical solution.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefW. Laiddatej“Lhfilenamegrfc3564eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125514hkeywordsÉxmulti-protocol label switchingxbandwidth constraints modelkoverbookingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?!_␀grankingÏcrfcd3564irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRRequirements for Support of Differentiated Services-aware MPLS Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5462∂habstractyùThis document contains the text of the agreement signed between ISOC/IETF and ISO/IEC JTC1/SC6 regarding cooperative development of the IS-IS routing protocol.  The agreement includes definitions of the related work scopes for the two organizations, request for creation and maintenance of an IS-IS registry by IANA, as well as collaboration guidelines.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamehA. Zininddatej hfilenamegrfc3563eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125513hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?V^grankingÎcrfcd3563irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexîCooperative Agreement Between the ISOC/IETF and ISO/IEC Joint Technical Committee 1/Sub Committee 6 (JTC1/SC6) on IS-IS Routing Protocol DevelopmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6233∂habstractyéThe TCP MD5 Signature Option (RFC 2385), used predominantly by BGP, has seen significant deployment in critical areas of Internet infrastructure.  The security of this option relies heavily on the quality of the keying material used to compute the MD5 signature.  This document addresses the security requirements of that keying material.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehM. Leechddatej“Lhfilenamegrfc3562eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125512hkeywordsÑcbgpwborder gateway protocolhsecurityjencryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?	–ögrankingÍcrfcd3562irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Key Management Considerations for the TCP MD5 Signature OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThe Ad hoc On-Demand Distance Vector (AODV) routing protocol is intended for use by mobile nodes in an ad hoc network.  It offers quick adaptation to dynamic link conditions, low processing and memory overhead, low network utilization, and determines unicast routes to destinations within the ad hoc network.  It uses destination sequence numbers to ensure loop freedom at all times (even in the face of anomalous delivery of routing control messages), avoiding problems (such as "counting to infinity") associated with classical distance vector protocols.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationx'University of California, Santa BarbaradnamepE. Belding-Royer¢kaffiliationxUniversity of CincinnatidnamefS. Dasddatej“Lhfilenamegrfc3561eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemanetdfullxmanet - Mobile Ad-hoc NetworksdnamevMobile Ad-hoc Networksdtypebwgbidjdoc-125511hkeywordsÑdAODVx Ad hoc On-Demand Distance Vectorgunicastnmultiple nodeskobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDate?	—grankingÈcrfcd3561irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Ad hoc On-Demand Distance Vector (AODV) RoutingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§This document describes the conventions for using the RSAES-OAEP key transport algorithm with the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  The CMS specifies the enveloped-data content type, which consists of an encrypted content and encrypted content-encryption keys for one or more recipients.  The RSAES-OAEP key transport algorithm can be used to encrypt content-encryption keys for intended recipients. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej“Khfilenamegrfc3560eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125510hkeywordsÉcCMSxCryptographic Message Syntaxssecurity encryptionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?–grankingËcrfcd3560irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSUse of the RSAES-OAEP Key Transport Algorithm in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for managing multicast address allocation. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej“{hfilenamegrfc3559eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmallocdfullx%malloc - Multicast-Address AllocationdnamexMulticast-Address Allocationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125509hkeywordsÑdmaasx$multicast address allocation serverscMIBxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages%opublicationDate>Ú	grankingÁcrfcd3559irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Multicast Address Allocation MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty”This document describes the RTP payload format for Enhanced Variable Rate Codec (EVRC) Speech and Selectable Mode Vocoder (SMV) Speech.  Two sub-formats are specified for different application scenarios.  A bundled/interleaved format is included to reduce the effect of packet loss on speech quality and amortize the overhead of the RTP header over more than one speech frame.  A non-bundled format is also supported for conversational applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnameeA. Liddatej“Ihfilenamegrfc3558eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125508hkeywordsàcRTPxreal time transport protocolgbundledkinterleaveddEVRCxEnhanced Variable Rate CodecscSMVxSelectable Mode VocoderskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?'grankingÊcrfcd3558irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^RTP Payload Format for Enhanced Variable Rate Codecs (EVRC) and Selectable Mode Vocoders (SMV)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4788∂habstractyThis document specifies an RTP payload format for encapsulating European Telecommunications Standards Institute (ETSI) European Standard (ES) 201 108 front-end signal processing feature streams for distributed speech recognition (DSR) systems. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnMotorola, Inc.dnamefQ. Xieddatej“Khfilenamegrfc3557eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125507hkeywordsÑcRTPxreal time transport protocolcDSRxdistributed speech recognitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?'
ègrankingÂcrfcd3557irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexíRTP Payload Format for European Telecommunications Standards Institute (ETSI) European Standard ES 201 108 Distributed Speech Recognition EncodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document defines an extension to the Session Description Protocol (SDP) to specify two additional modifiers for the bandwidth attribute.  These modifiers may be used to specify the bandwidth allowed for RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) packets in a Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP) session. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnameiS. Casnerddatej“Khfilenamegrfc3556eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125506hkeywordsÜcRTPxreal time transport protocolcSDPxSession Description ProtocoldRTCPtRTP Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?'	}granking‰crfcd3556irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`Session Description Protocol (SDP) Bandwidth Modifiers for RTP Control Protocol (RTCP) BandwidthdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyµThis document defines the procedure to register RTP Payload Formats as audio, video or other MIME subtype names.  This is useful in a text- based format or control protocol to identify the type of an RTP transmission.  This document also registers all the RTP payload formats defined in the RTP Profile for Audio and Video Conferences as MIME subtypes.  Some of these may also be used for transfer modes other than RTP. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffili