cronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamekM. Mealling¢kaffiliationmAdobe SystemsdnamekL. Masinter¢kaffiliationhQualcommdnameiT. Hardie¢kaffiliationlNine by NinednamehG. Klyneddatej“{hfilenamegrfc3553eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125503hkeywordsÇfsyntaxvuniform resource nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>¸‚Igranking·crfcd3553irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberIetotaletitlex<An IETF URN Sub-namespace for Registered Protocol ParametersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytAll RFCs are required to have a Security Considerations section.  Historically, such sections have been relatively weak.  This document provides guidelines to RFC authors on how to write a good Security Considerations section.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamemErik NordmarkgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationoXythos SoftwarednameiB. Korverddatej“Jhfilenamegrfc3552eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125502hkeywordsÉcRFCsRequest for CommentwSecurity ConsiderationskobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDate?(S€granking‡crfcd3552irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberHetotaletitlex:Guidelines for Writing RFC Text on Security ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9416d8996∑habstractyèThis document describes a profile called "RTP/AVP" for the use of the real-time transport protocol (RTP), version 2, and the associated control protocol, RTCP, within audio and video multiparticipant conferences with minimal control.  It provides interpretations of generic fields within the RTP specification suitable for audio and video conferences.  In particular, this document defines a set of default mappings from payload type numbers to encodings.  This document also describes how audio and video data may be carried within RTP.  It defines a set of standard encodings and their names when used within RTP.  The descriptions provide pointers to reference implementations and the detailed standards.  This document is meant as an aid for implementors of audio, video and other real-time multimedia applications.  This memorandum obsoletes RFC 1890.  It is mostly backwards-compatible except for functions removed because two interoperable implementations were not found.  The additions to RFC 1890 codify existing practice in the use of payload formats under this profile and include new payload formats defined since RFC 1890 was published. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnameiS. Casnerddatej“Jhfilenamegrfc3551eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125501hkeywordsáfRTP-AVxReal-time Transport Protocoleaudioevideojend-to-endgnetworkjconferencekobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDate?'†grankingﬂcrfcd3551irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberAetotaletitlex@RTP Profile for Audio and Video Conferences with Minimal ControldtypecrfciupdatedByÉd8860d7007d5761∑habstractyªThis memorandum describes RTP, the real-time transport protocol.  RTP provides end-to-end network transport functions suitable for applications transmitting real-time data, such as audio, video or simulation data, over multicast or unicast network services.  RTP does not address resource reservation and does not guarantee quality-of- service for real-time services.  The data transport is augmented by a control protocol (RTCP) to allow monitoring of the data delivery in a manner scalable to large multicast networks, and to provide minimal control and identification functionality.  RTP and RTCP are designed to be independent of the underlying transport and network layers.  The protocol supports the use of RTP-level translators and mixers.  Most of the text in this memorandum is identical to RFC 1889 which it obsoletes.  There are no changes in the packet formats on the wire, only changes to the rules and algorithms governing how the protocol is used.  The biggest change is an enhancement to the scalable timer algorithm for calculating when to send RTCP packets in order to minimize transmission in excess of the intended rate when many participants join a session simultaneously. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnameiS. Casner¢kaffiliationvBlue Coat Systems Inc.dnamelR. Frederick¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnamekV. Jacobsonddatej“Ihfilenamegrfc3550eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125500hkeywordsàcRTPxReal-Time Transport Protocoljend-to-endgnetworkeaudioevideodRTCPtRTP Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepageshopublicationDate?'„grankingﬁcrfcd3550irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber@etotaletitlex4RTP: A Transport Protocol for Real-Time ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByäd7164d8860d8108d6051d6222d8083d7022d5761d7160d5506∂habstractyThis document describes Linux Netlink, which is used in Linux both as an intra-kernel messaging system as well as between kernel and user space.  The focus of this document is to describe Netlink's functionality as a protocol between a Forwarding Engine Component (FEC) and a Control Plane Component (CPC), the two components that define an IP service.  As a result of this focus, this document ignores other uses of Netlink, including its use as a intra-kernel messaging system, as an inter- process communication scheme (IPC), or as a configuration tool for other non-networking or non-IP network services (such as decnet, etc.).  This document is intended as informational in the context of prior art for the ForCES IETF working group.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmZnyx NetworksdnamehJ. Salim¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekH. Khosravi¢kaffiliationdSusednamehA. Kleen¢kaffiliationjINR/SwsoftdnamelA. Kuznetsovddatej“Ihfilenamegrfc3549eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfforcesdfullx2forces - Forwarding and Control Element Separationdnamex)Forwarding and Control Element Separationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125499hkeywordsÅx"internet protocol messaging systemkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate?h/granking›crfcd3549irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Linux Netlink as an IP Services ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyHThis document describes the commonly used base 64, base 32, and base 16 encoding schemes.  It also discusses the use of line-feeds in encoded data, use of padding in encoded data, use of non-alphabet characters in encoded data, and use of different encoding alphabets.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed FreedgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. Josefssonddatej“Hhfilenamegrfc3548eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125498hkeywordsÜgschemesddatajline-feedsialphabetsmbase encodingchexkobsoletedByÅd4648epagesopublicationDate?Uagranking‹crfcd3548irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex-The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data EncodingsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∞This document presents an ISAMKP Domain of Interpretation (DOI) for group key management to support secure group communications.  The GDOI manages group security associations, which are used by IPSEC and potentially other data security protocols running at the IP or application layers.  These security associations protect one or more key-encrypting keys, traffic-encrypting keys, or data shared by group members. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejM. Baugher¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegB. Weis¢kaffiliationhVerisigndnamekT. Hardjono¢kaffiliationfSpartadnameiH. Harneyddatej“Hhfilenamegrfc3547eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmsecdfullxmsec - Multicast SecuritydnamerMulticast Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125497hkeywordsáfISAMKPx9Internet Security Association and Key Management ProtocolcDOIxDomain of Interpretationnkey managementhsecurityjencryptionkobsoletedByÅd6407epages0opublicationDate?g¬granking€crfcd3547irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"The Group Domain of InterpretationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes extensions that may be used to add functionality to Transport Layer Security (TLS).  It provides both generic extension mechanisms for the TLS handshake client and server hellos, and specific extensions using these generic mechanisms.  The extensions may be used by TLS clients and servers.  The extensions are backwards compatible - communication is possible between TLS 1.0 clients that support the extensions and TLS 1.0 servers that do not support the extensions, and vice versa. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationcBCIdnameoS. Blake-Wilson¢kaffiliationlRSA SecuritydnamejM. Nystrom¢kaffiliationvIndependent ConsultantdnamejD. Hopwood¢kaffiliationoTransactionwarednamelJ. Mikkelsen¢kaffiliationhVodafonednameiT. Wrightddatej“{hfilenamegrfc3546eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125496hkeywordsÑcTLSxtransport layer securitynauthenticationgprivacykobsoletedByÅd4366epagesopublicationDate>Úgranking⁄crfcd3546irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Transport Layer Security (TLS) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis document describes a header compression scheme for point to point links with packet loss and long delays.  It is based on Compressed Real-time Transport Protocol (CRTP), the IP/UDP/RTP header compression described in RFC 2508.  CRTP does not perform well on such links: packet loss results in context corruption and due to the long delay, many more packets are discarded before the context is repaired.  To correct the behavior of CRTP over such links, a few extensions to the protocol are specified here.  The extensions aim to reduce context corruption by changing the way the compressor updates the context at the decompressor: updates are repeated and include updates to full and differential context parameters.  With these extensions, CRTP performs well over links with packet loss, packet reordering and long delays. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Koren¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnameiS. Casner¢kaffiliationxMotorola India Electronics Ltd.dnamenJ. Geevarghese¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekB. Thompson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Ruddyddatej“Hhfilenamegrfc3545eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125495hkeywordsÖx'Compressed Real-time Transport ProtocoldCRTPcPPPnpoint to pointfheaderkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?)êÌgrankingŸcrfcd3545irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTEnhanced Compressed RTP (CRTP) for Links with High Delay, Packet Loss and ReorderingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÆThis document describes an option for negotiating the use of header compression on IP datagrams transmitted over the Point-to-Point Protocol (RFC 1661).  It defines extensions to the PPP Control Protocols for IPv4 and IPv6 (RFC 1332, RFC 2472).  Header compression may be applied to IPv4 and IPv6 datagrams in combination with TCP, UDP and RTP transport protocols as specified in RFC 2507, RFC 2508 and RFC 3545. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Koren¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnameiS. Casner¢kaffiliationwUniversitaet Bremen TZIdnamejC. Bormannddatej“Hhfilenamegrfc3544eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125494hkeywordsÖiIPCOM-PPPqinternet protocolrheader compressionnpoint-to-pointidatagramskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?)êigrankingÿcrfcd3544irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP Header Compression over PPPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyáThis document defines a Mobile IPv4 Registration Revocation mechanism whereby a mobility agent involved in providing Mobile IP services to a mobile node can notify the other mobility agent providing Mobile IP services to the same mobile node of the termination of this registration.  The mechanism is also usable by a home agent to notify a co-located mobile node of the termination of its binding as well.  Moreover, the mechanism provides for this notification to be acknowledged.  A signaling mechanism already defined by the Mobile IPv4 protocol is leveraged as a way to inform a mobile node of the revocation of its binding. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamehS. Glass¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejM. Chandraddatej—µhfilenamegrfc3543eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmobileipdfullx/mobileip - IP Routing for Wireless/Mobile Hostsdnamex$IP Routing for Wireless/Mobile Hostsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125493hkeywordsÉdIPv4qinternet protocolfmobilekobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate?:ÑÖgranking◊crfcd3543irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex&Registration Revocation in Mobile IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document provides sockets Application Program Interface (API) to support "advanced" IPv6 applications, as a supplement to a separate specification, RFC 3493.  The expected applications include Ping, Traceroute, routing daemons and the like, which typically use raw sockets to access IPv6 or ICMPv6 header fields.  This document proposes some portable interfaces for applications that use raw sockets under IPv6.  There are other features of IPv6 that some applications will need to access: interface identification (specifying the outgoing interface and determining the incoming interface), IPv6 extension headers, and path Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) information.  This document provides API access to these features too.  Additionally, some extended interfaces to libraries for the "r" commands are defined.  The extension will provide better backward compatibility to existing implementations that are not IPv6-capable.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejW. Stevens¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiM. Thomas¢kaffiliationcSundnamekE. Nordmark¢kaffiliationgToshibadnameiT. Jinmeiddatej“…hfilenamegrfc3542eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125492hkeywordsÉkapplicationgprogramiinterfacekobsoletedByÄepagesMopublicationDate>€∆Tgranking÷crfcd3542irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Advanced Sockets Application Program Interface (API) for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÏThis document describes a Uniform Resource Name (URN) namespace for the Web3D Consortium (Web3D) for naming persistent resources such as technical documents and specifications, Virtual Reality Modeling Language (VRML) and Extensible 3D (X3D) files and resources, Extensible Markup Language (XML) Document Type Definitions (DTDs), XML Schemas, namespaces, style sheets, media assets, and other resources produced or managed by Web3D.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed HardiegauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpWeb3D ConsortiumdnamehA. Walshddatej“…hfilenamegrfc3541eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125491hkeywordsáx#virtual reality monitoring languagedvrmlxextensible markup languagecx3dcxmlcdtdxdocument type definitionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>¿1granking’crfcd3541irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexHA Uniform Resource Name (URN) Namespace for the Web3D Consortium (Web3D)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty_This note describes the Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN)-nonce, an optional addition to ECN that protects against accidental or malicious concealment of marked packets from the TCP sender.  It improves the robustness of congestion control by preventing receivers from exploiting ECN to gain an unfair share of network bandwidth.  The ECN-nonce uses the two ECN-Capable Transport (ECT)codepoints in the ECN field of the IP header, and requires a flag in the TCP header.  It is computationally efficient for both routers and hosts.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnameiN. Spring¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamelD. Wetherall¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamefD. Elyddatej“{hfilenamegrfc3540eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125490hkeywordsÑcECNx explicit congestion notificationcTCPxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>Ÿ§pgranking‘crfcd3540irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCRobust Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) Signaling with NoncesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This document discusses transport issues that arise within protocols for Authentication, Authorization and Accounting (AAA).  It also provides recommendations on the use of transport by AAA protocols.  This includes usage of standards-track RFCs as well as experimental proposals. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamegJ. Woodddatej“zhfilenamegrfc3539eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcaaadfullx2aaa - Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdnamex,Authentication, Authorization and Accountingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125489hkeywordsÑcAAAnAuthenticationmAuthorizationjAccountingkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate>Ûì∂granking”crfcd3539irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDAuthentication, Authorization and Accounting (AAA) Transport ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyvThis document describes detailed Input/Output parameters for the Internet Open Trading Protocol (IOTP) Payment Application Programming Interface (API).  It also describes procedures in the Payment Bridge for the use of SET (SET Secure Electronic Transaction) as the payment protocol within Version 1.0 of the IOTP.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgHitachidnamelY. Kawatsuraddatej“zhfilenamegrfc3538eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125488hkeywordsÑgpaymenteinputfoutputiparameterkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDate>Ÿ§pgranking“crfcd3538irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexaSecure Electronic Transaction (SET) Supplement for the v1.0 Internet Open Trading Protocol (IOTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThis document defines two methods for wrapping an HMAC (Hashed Message Authentication Code) key.  The first method defined uses a Triple DES (Data Encryption Standard) key to encrypt the HMAC key.  The second method defined uses an AES (Advanced Encryption Standard) key to encrypt the HMAC key.  One place that such an algorithm is used is for the Authenticated Data type in CMS (Cryptographic Message Syntax). [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationnVigil SecuritydnamejR. Housleyddatej“…hfilenamegrfc3537eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125487hkeywordsÜdHMACx"Hashed Message Authentication CodecDESxData Encryption StandardcAESxAdvanced Encryption StandardkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate>◊Ú;granking—crfcd3537irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexñWrapping a Hashed Message Authentication Code (HMAC) key with a Triple-Data Encryption Standard (DES) Key or an Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) KeydtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty>This document provides a glossary of terms used in the IETF when discussing internationalization.  The purpose is to help frame discussions of internationalization in the various areas of the IETF and to help introduce the main concepts to IETF participants.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIMC & VPNCdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej“…hfilenamegrfc3536eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125486hkeywordsÅxinternet engineering task forcekobsoletedByÅd6365epagesopublicationDate>ƒgranking–crfcd3536irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex4Terminology Used in Internationalization in the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyUThis document provides an overview of a workshop held by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB) on Network Management.  The workshop was hosted by CNRI in Reston, VA, USA on June 4 thru June 6, 2002.  The goal of the workshop was to continue the important dialog started between network operators and protocol developers, and to guide the IETFs focus on future work regarding network management.  This report summarizes the discussions and lists the conclusions and recommendations to the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) community.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternational University BremendnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej“»hfilenamegrfc3535eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125485hkeywordsÅxInternet Architecture BoardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate> ÅRgrankingœcrfcd3535irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex4Overview of the 2002 IAB Network Management WorkshopdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyªThe Ogg Bitstream Format aims at becoming a general, freely-available standard for transporting multimedia content across computing platforms and networks.  The intention of this document is to define the MIME media type application/ogg to refer to this kind of content when transported across the Internet.  It is the intention of the Ogg Bitstream Format developers that it be usable without intellectual property concerns. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxThe Ogg Vorbis CommunitydnamejL. Walleijddatej“»hfilenamegrfc3534eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125484hkeywordsÇdmimex&multipurpose internet mail extenstionskobsoletedByÅd5334epagesopublicationDate>≈CgrankingŒcrfcd3534irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe application/ogg Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyàThis document describes the Ogg bitstream format version 0, which is a general, freely-available encapsulation format for media streams.  It is able to encapsulate any kind and number of video and audio encoding formats as well as other data streams in a single bitstream.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. MankingauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeCSIROdnamekS. Pfeifferddatej“»hfilenamegrfc3533eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125483hkeywordsåibitstreammmedia streamsevideoeaudiohxiph.orgjmultimediaxmedia interleading formatxvideo bitstream packagingxaudio bitstream packagingxfree encapsulation formatx"stream based storage of codec datapframed bitstreamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>≈dgrankingÕcrfcd3533irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex&The Ogg Encapsulation Format Version 0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåThis document identifies a set of requirements for the mechanisms used to dynamically reallocate the resources of a switching element (e.g., an ATM switch) to its partitions.  These requirements are particularly critical in the case of an operator creating a switch partition and then leasing control of that partition to a third party.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjIntel LabsdnamekT. Anderson¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejJ. Buerkleddatej“»hfilenamegrfc3532eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgsmpdfullx)gsmp - General Switch Management Protocoldnamex"General Switch Management Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125482hkeywordsÇcatmxasynchronous transfer modekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>∂î6grankingÃcrfcd3532irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Requirements for the Dynamic Partitioning of Switching ElementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‚This document proposes a method to manage the assignment of bits of an IPv6 address block or range.  When an organisation needs to make an address plan for its subnets or when an ISP needs to make an address plan for its customers, this method enables the organisation to postpone the final decision on the number of bits to partition in the address space they have.  It does it by keeping the bits around the borders of the partition to be free as long as possible.  This scheme is applicable to any bits addressing scheme using bits with partitions in the space, but its first intended use is for IPv6.  It is a generalization of RFC 1219 and can be used for IPv6 assignments.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej—âhfilenamegrfc3531eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125481hkeywordsÖladdress planjaddressingerangeespaceqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>≠û6grankingÀcrfcd3531irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNA Flexible Method for Managing the Assignment of Bits of an IPv6 Address BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyìThe Network File System (NFS) version 4 is a distributed filesystem protocol which owes heritage to NFS protocol version 2, RFC 1094, and version 3, RFC 1813.  Unlike earlier versions, the NFS version 4 protocol supports traditional file access while integrating support for file locking and the mount protocol.  In addition, support for strong security (and its negotiation), compound operations, client caching, and internationalization have been added.  Of course, attention has been applied to making NFS version 4 operate well in an Internet environment.  This document replaces RFC 3010 as the definition of the NFS version 4 protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet Transportgauthorsá¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamejS. Shepler¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamelB. Callaghan¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamekD. Robinson¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamejR. Thurlow¢kaffiliationpHummingbird Ltd.dnamehC. Beame¢kaffiliationwNetwork Appliance, Inc.dnameiM. Eisler¢kaffiliationwNetwork Appliance, Inc.dnameiD. Noveckddatej—àhfilenamegrfc3530eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymenfsv4dfullx%nfsv4 - Network File System Version 4dnamexNetwork File System Version 4dtypebwgbidjdoc-125480hkeywordsÇeNFSv4snetwork file systemkobsoletedByÅd7530epagesopublicationDate>∞X∫granking crfcd3530irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Network File System (NFS) version 4 ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty≈Markup Language-Remote Procedure Calling protocol that works over the Internet.  It defines an XML format for messages that are transfered between clients and servers using HTTP.  An XML-RPC message encodes either a procedure to be invoked by the server, along with the parameters to use in the invocation, or the result of an invocation.  Procedure parameters and results can be scalars, numbers, strings, dates, etc.; they can also be complex record and list structures.  This document specifies a how to use the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP) to transfer messages encoded in the XML-RPC format between clients and servers.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiW. Haroldddatej—àhfilenamegrfc3529eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125479hkeywordsàgXML-RPCx3Extensible Markup Language-Remote Procedure CallingfformathmessagesgclientsgserversdBEEPx#Blocks Extensible Exchange ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>∞Vgranking…crfcd3529irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexqUsing Extensible Markup Language-Remote Procedure Calling (XML-RPC) in Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8553µhabstractyTThis document describes the Mesh-enhanced Service Location Protocol (mSLP).  mSLP enhances the Service Location Protocol (SLP) with a scope-based fully-meshed peering Directory Agent (DA) architecture.  Peer DAs exchange new service registrations in shared scopes via anti- entropy and direct forwarding.  mSLP improves the reliability and consistency of SLP DA services, and simplifies Service Agent (SA) registrations in systems with multiple DAs.  mSLP is backward compatible with SLPv2 and can be deployed incrementally.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas NartengauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamegW. Zhao¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttmanddatej—àhfilenamegrfc3528eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125478hkeywordsàbDAodirectory agenteslpdamservice agentbSAeslpv2x'Mesh-enhanced Service Location ProtocoldmSLPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>±tmgranking»crfcd3528irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex.Mesh-enhanced Service Location Protocol (mSLP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document describes the link selection sub-option of the relay- agent-information option for the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4).  The giaddr specifies an IP address which determines both a subnet, and thereby a link on which a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) client resides as well as an IP address that can be used to communicate with the relay agent.  The subnet-selection option allows the functions of the giaddr to be split so that when one entity is performing as a DHCP proxy, it can specify the subnet/link from which to allocate an IP address, which is different from the IP address with which it desires to communicate with the DHCP server.  Analogous situations exist where the relay agent needs to specify the subnet/link on which a DHCP client resides, which is different from an IP address that can be used to communicate with the relay agent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejK. Kinnear¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Stapp¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejR. Johnson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemJ. Kumarasamyddatej—àhfilenamegrfc3527eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125477hkeywordsÇfDHCPv4x#dynamic host configuration protocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate>®Wxgranking«crfcd3527irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKLink Selection sub-option for the Relay Agent Information Option for DHCPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÉThis document defines new Modular Exponential (MODP) Groups for the Internet Key Exchange (IKE) protocol.  It documents the well known and used 1536 bit group 5, and also defines new 2048, 3072, 4096, 6144, and 8192 bit Diffie-Hellman groups numbered starting at 14.  The selection of the primes for theses groups follows the criteria established by Richard Schroeppel. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamesJeffrey I. Schillerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxSSH Communications SecuritydnamejT. Kivinen¢kaffiliationxSSH Communications SecuritydnamegM. Kojoddatej“«hfilenamegrfc3526eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsecdfullxipsec - IP Security ProtocoldnametIP Security Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125476hkeywordsÖdMODPxMore Modular ExponentialcIKEuInternet Key Exchangejbit groupskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate>¬fgranking∆crfcd3526irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUMore Modular Exponential (MODP) Diffie-Hellman groups for Internet Key Exchange (IKE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty8This document defines the protocol used between elements of a physically decomposed multimedia gateway, i.e., a Media Gateway and a Media Gateway Controller.  The protocol presented in this document meets the requirements for a media gateway control protocol as presented in RFC 2805.  This document replaces RFC 3015.  It is the result of continued cooperation between the IETF Megaco Working Group and ITU-T Study Group 16.  It incorporates the original text of RFC 3015, modified by corrections and clarifications discussed on the Megaco E-mail list and incorporated into the Study Group 16 Implementor's Guide for Recommendation H.248.  The present version of this document underwent ITU-T Last Call as Recommendation H.248 Amendment 1.  Because of ITU-T renumbering, it was published by the ITU-T as Recommendation H.248.1 (03/2002), Gateway Control Protocol Version 1.  Users of this specification are advised to consult the H.248 Sub-series Implementors' Guide at http://www.itu.int/itudoc/itu-t/com16/implgd for additional corrections and clarifications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkLM EricssondnameiC. Groves¢kaffiliationkLM EricssondnamekM. Pantaleo¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekT. Anderson¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiT. Taylorddatej“zhfilenamegrfc3525eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmegacodfullxmegaco - Media Gateway ControldnameuMedia Gateway Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125475hkeywordsÖfMEGACOeH.248emediaggatewaygcontrolkobsoletedByÅd5125epages’opublicationDate>‰º»granking≈crfcd3525irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Gateway Control Protocol Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document defines an extension to the Session Description Protocol (SDP) grouping framework.  It allows requesting a group of media streams to be mapped into a single resource reservation flow.  The SDP syntax needed is defined, as well as a new "semantics" attribute called Single Reservation Flow (SRF). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiA. Monradddatej—áhfilenamegrfc3524eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125474hkeywordsÑcSDPxsession description protocolcSRFwsingle reservation flowkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>≠ù grankingƒcrfcd3524irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Mapping of Media Streams to Resource Reservation FlowsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractytThis document defines the topology naming conventions that are to be used in reference to Internet Emergency Preparedness (IEPREP) phone calls.  These naming conventions should be used to focus the IEPREP Working Group during discussions and when writing requirements, gap analysis and other solutions documents.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegJ. Polkddatej—áhfilenamegrfc3523eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfieprepdfullx(ieprep - Internet Emergency PreparednessdnamexInternet Emergency Preparednessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125473hkeywordsÇqnaming convetionsephonekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ü(granking√crfcd3523irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGInternet Emergency Preparedness (IEPREP) Telephony Topology TerminologydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˚The Eifel detection algorithm allows a TCP sender to detect a posteriori whether it has entered loss recovery unnecessarily.  It requires that the TCP Timestamps option defined in RFC 1323 be enabled for a connection.  The Eifel detection algorithm makes use of the fact that the TCP Timestamps option eliminates the retransmission ambiguity in TCP.  Based on the timestamp of the first acceptable ACK that arrives during loss recovery, it decides whether loss recovery was entered unnecessarily.  The Eifel detection algorithm provides a basis for future TCP enhancements.  This includes response algorithms to back out of loss recovery by restoring a TCP sender's congestion control state.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnameiR. Ludwig¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnamehM. Meyerddatej—áhfilenamegrfc3522eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125472hkeywordsÑcTCPxtransmission control protocolmloss recoveryjtimestampskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>∞U¥granking¬crfcd3522irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%The Eifel Detection Algorithm for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty§Establishing multimedia streams must take into account requirements for end-to-end QoS, authorization of network resource usage and accurate accounting for resources used.  During session set up, policies may be enforced to ensure that the media streams being requested lie within the bounds of the service profile established for the requesting host.  Similarly, when a host requests resources to provide a certain QoS for a packet flow, policies may be enforced to ensure that the required resources lie within the bounds of the resource profile established for the requesting host.  To prevent fraud and to ensure accurate billing, this document describes various scenarios and mechanisms that provide the linkage required to verify that the resources being used to provide a requested QoS are in- line with the media streams requested (and authorized) for the session.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejL-N. Hamer¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegB. Gage¢kaffiliationmAT&T WirelessdnamehH. Shiehddatej—áhfilenamegrfc3521eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrapdfullx"rap - Resource Allocation ProtocoldnamexResource Allocation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125471hkeywordsâcqosrquality of servicegstreamsglinkagenpolicy controliadmissionmtheft servicetresource reservationetokenkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>õ7ógranking¡crfcd3521irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Framework for Session Set-up with Media AuthorizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThis document describes the representation of a session authorization policy element for supporting policy-based per-session authorization and admission control.  The goal of session authorization is to allow the exchange of information between network elements in order to authorize the use of resources for a service and to co-ordinate actions between the signaling and transport planes.  This document describes how a process on a system authorizes the reservation of resources by a host and then provides that host with a session authorization policy element which can be inserted into a resource reservation protocol (e.g., the Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) PATH message) to facilitate proper and secure reservation of those resources within the network.  We describe the encoding of session authorization information as a policy element conforming to the format of a Policy Data object (RFC 2750) and provide details relating to operations, processing rules and error scenarios. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejL-N. Hamer¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegB. Gage¢kaffiliationsInvidi TechnologiesdnamekB. Kosinski¢kaffiliationmAT&T WirelessdnamehH. Shiehddatej—Ühfilenamegrfc3520eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrapdfullx"rap - Resource Allocation ProtocoldnamexResource Allocation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125470hkeywordsÉxsession authorization policyqadmission controltresource reservationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>õ7*granking¿crfcd3520irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Session Authorization Policy ElementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty≥Mobile IP's datagram tunnelling is incompatible with Network Address Translation (NAT).  This document presents extensions to the Mobile IP protocol and a tunnelling method which permits mobile nodes using Mobile IP to operate in private address networks which are separated from the public internet by NAT devices.  The NAT traversal is based on using the Mobile IP Home Agent UDP port for encapsulated data traffic. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkipUnpluggeddnamelH. Levkowetz¢kaffiliationgNetsealdnamejS. Vaaraladdatej“«hfilenamegrfc3519eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmobileipdfullx/mobileip - IP Routing for Wireless/Mobile Hostsdnamex$IP Routing for Wireless/Mobile Hostsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125469hkeywordsêqInternet ProtocolhdatagramgtrafficiMobile IPcNATdNAPTitraversaljtunnellingitunnelingcUDPuprivate address spacejkeepaliveshport 434cMIPeMIPv4xnetwork address translationkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate> {ˇgrankingøcrfcd3519irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Mobile IP Traversal of Network Address Translation (NAT) DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThe Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) provides a standard method for transporting multi-protocol datagrams over point-to-point links.  PPP defines an extensible Link Control Protocol (LCP) and proposes a family of Network Control Protocols (NCP) for establishing and configuring different network-layer protocols.  This document defines the NCP for establishing and configuring Remote Bridging for PPP links.  This document obsoletes RFC 2878, which was based on the IEEE 802.1D- 1993 MAC Bridge.  This document extends that specification by improving support for bridge control packets. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgAnritsudnamenM. Higashiyama¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegT. Liaoddatej—Ühfilenamegrfc3518eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125468hkeywordsÖgPPP-BCPwpoint-to-point protocolxBridging Control ProtocolidatagramsgnetworkkobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate>õ6ögrankingæcrfcd3518irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) Bridging Control Protocol (BCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document presents a conservative loss recovery algorithm for TCP that is based on the use of the selective acknowledgment (SACK) TCP option.  The algorithm presented in this document conforms to the spirit of the current congestion control specification (RFC 2581), but allows TCP senders to recover more effectively when multiple segments are lost from a single flight of data. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqPurdue UniversitydnamejE. Blanton¢kaffiliationlBBN/NASA GRCdnameiM. Allman¢kaffiliationnIntel ResearchdnamegK. Fall¢kaffiliationvUniversity of KentuckydnamegL. Wangddatej—Ühfilenamegrfc3517eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125467hkeywordsÑcTCPxtransmission control protocolnretransmissionrcongestion controlkobsoletedByÅd6675epagesopublicationDate>ñ	␀grankingΩcrfcd3517irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTA Conservative Selective Acknowledgment (SACK)-based Loss Recovery Algorithm for TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis memo defines the Binary extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP4).  It provides a mechanism for IMAP4 clients and servers to exchange message body data without using a MIME content-transfer- encoding. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoOrthanc SystemsdnamelL. Nerenbergddatej—Ühfilenamegrfc3516eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125466hkeywordsÅxinternet message acess procotolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ü'ùgrankingºcrfcd3516irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIMAP4 Binary Content ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4466∂habstracty‰This document defines the REFER method.  This Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) extension requests that the recipient REFER to a resource provided in the request.  It provides a mechanism allowing the party sending the REFER to be notified of the outcome of the referenced request.  This can be used to enable many applications, including call transfer.  In addition to the REFER method, this document defines the refer event package and the Refer-To request header. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnameiR. Sparksddatej—Ühfilenamegrfc3515eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125465hkeywordsÑcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolpresource requestmcall transferkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ñhgrankingªcrfcd3515irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Refer MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8217d7647µhabstractyMFirewalls, packet filters, intrusion detection systems, and the like often have difficulty distinguishing between packets that have malicious intent and those that are merely unusual.  We define a security flag in the IPv4 header as a means of distinguishing the two cases.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. BellovingauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrAT&T Labs ResearchdnamekS. Bellovinddatej—çhfilenamegrfc3514eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43434hkeywordsÇdevilhevil bitkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>âÒ∞granking∫crfcd3514irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex$The Security Flag in the IPv4 HeaderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty@This specification defines the addressing architecture of the IP Version 6 (IPv6) protocol.  The document includes the IPv6 addressing model, text representations of IPv6 addresses, definition of IPv6 unicast addresses, anycast addresses, and multicast addresses, and an IPv6 node's required addresses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiR. Hinden¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Deeringddatej—Öhfilenamegrfc3513eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125464hkeywordsÜhinternethprotocolgunicastganycastimulticastdnodekobsoletedByÅd4291epagesopublicationDate>óT«grankingπcrfcd3513irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) Addressing ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÊThis document is written for readers interested in the Internet Standard Management Framework and its protocol, the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).  In particular, it offers guidance in the effective use of SNMP for configuration management.  This information is relevant to vendors that build network elements, management application developers, and those that acquire and deploy this technology in their networks.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxRiverstone Networks, Inc.dnamekM. MacFaden¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Partain¢kaffiliationtJDS Consulting, Inc.dnamejJ. Saperia¢kaffiliationxGold Wire Technology, Inc.dnamelW. Tackaburyddatej—Öhfilenamegrfc3512eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhsnmpconfdfullx-snmpconf - Configuration Management with SNMPdnamex"Configuration Management with SNMPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125463hkeywordsÅxinternet standard frameworkkobsoletedByÄepagesSopublicationDate>ç»∫granking∏crfcd3512irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOConfiguring Networks and Devices with Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty∂This document discusses and defines a number of tests that may be used to describe the performance characteristics of firewalls.  In addition to defining the tests, this document also describes specific formats for reporting the results of the tests.  This document is a product of the Benchmarking Methodology Working Group (BMWG) of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvSpirent CommunicationsdnamejB. Hickman¢kaffiliationlNetwork TestdnameiD. Newman¢kaffiliationvSpirent CommunicationsdnamekS. Tadjudin¢kaffiliationsGVNW Consulting IncdnameiT. Martinddatej—Öhfilenamegrfc3511eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-125462hkeywordsÑmclient servergtrafficnauthenticationkweb cachingkobsoletedByÅd9411epages"opublicationDate>õ5=granking∑crfcd3511irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Benchmarking Methodology for Firewall PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¢This memo defines the "ipp" URL (Uniform Resource Locator) scheme.  This memo updates IPP/1.1: Encoding and Transport (RFC 2910), by expanding and clarifying Section 5, "IPP URL Scheme", of RFC 2910.  An "ipp" URL is used to specify the network location of a print service that supports the IPP Protocol (RFC 2910), or of a network resource (for example, a print job) managed by such a print service. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoHigh North Inc.dnamejR. Herriot¢kaffiliationoHigh North Inc.dnamekI. McDonaldddatej—Öhfilenamegrfc3510eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcippdfullx ipp - Internet Printing ProtocoldnamexInternet Printing Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125461hkeywordsÜgIPP-E-TxInternet Printing Protocolkapplicationjmedia typecURLxUniform Resource LocatorkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ãf„granking∂crfcd3510irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Internet Printing Protocol/1.1: IPP URL SchemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyñOpen Shortest Path First (OSPF) is a link-state intra-domain routing protocol used for routing in IP networks.  Though the definition of the Area Border Router (ABR) in the OSPF specification does not require a router with multiple attached areas to have a backbone connection, it is actually necessary to provide successful routing to the inter-area and external destinations.  If this requirement is not met, all traffic destined for the areas not connected to such an ABR or out of the OSPF domain, is dropped.  This document describes alternative ABR behaviors implemented in Cisco and IBM routers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamehA. Zinin¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnameiA. Lindem¢kaffiliationpProcket NetworksdnamehD. Yeungddatej—Ñhfilenamegrfc3509eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125460hkeywordsÇgtraffichbackbonekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ëÙ§grankingµcrfcd3509irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Alternative Implementations of OSPF Area Border RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractySITU-T Recommendation H.323 version 4 introduced an H.323-specific Uniform Resource Locator (URL).  This document reproduces the H323-URL definition found in H.323, and is published as an RFC for ease of access and registration with the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. BradnergauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationiRADVISIONdnamehO. Levinddatej—Ñhfilenamegrfc3508eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125459hkeywordsÇeitu-topacket networkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ãfsgranking¥crfcd3508irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex8H.323 Uniform Resource Locator (URL) Scheme RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty}ICAP, the Internet Content Adaption Protocol, is a protocol aimed at providing simple object-based content vectoring for HTTP services.  ICAP is, in essence, a lightweight protocol for executing a "remote procedure call" on HTTP messages.  It allows ICAP clients to pass HTTP messages to ICAP servers for some sort of transformation or other processing ("adaptation").  The server executes its transformation service on messages and sends back responses to the client, usually with modified messages.  Typically, the adapted messages are either HTTP requests or HTTP responses.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed FreedgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehJ. Elson¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamehA. Cerpaddatej—Ñhfilenamegrfc3507eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125458hkeywordsÖdhttpxhyper-text markup protocolgrequesthresponsemclient serverkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDate>õ4“granking≥crfcd3507irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex+Internet Content Adaptation Protocol (ICAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXCrediting loyalty points and collecting digital coupons or gift certificates are common functions in purchasing and trading transactions.  These activities can be generalized using the concept of a "voucher", which is a digital representation of the right to claim goods or services.  This document presents a Voucher Trading System (VTS) that circulates vouchers securely and its terminology; it lists design principles and requirements for VTS and the Generic Voucher Language (GVL), with which diverse types of vouchers can be described.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamekK. Fujimura¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekD. Eastlakeddatej“©hfilenamegrfc3506eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125457hkeywordsÇxgeneric voucher languagecgvlkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>q Ígranking≤crfcd3506irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Requirements and Design for Voucher Trading System (VTS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyMThis document lists the design principles, scope, and requirements for the Electronic Commerce Modeling Language (ECML) version 2 specification.  It includes requirements as they relate to Extensible Markup Language (XML) syntax, data model, format, and payment processing.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekD. Eastlakeddatej“©hfilenamegrfc3505eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125456hkeywordsÇcxmlxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>q Ägranking±crfcd3505irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDElectronic Commerce Modeling Language (ECML): Version 2 RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractySince the publication of the RFCs specifying Version 1.0 of the Internet Open Trading Protocol (IOTP), some errors have been noted.  This informational document lists these errors and provides corrections for them.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekD. Eastlakeddatej“©hfilenamegrfc3504eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125455hkeywordsâhcommercegpaymentfsystemhmerchantcxmljextensiblefmarkuphlanguagehsecuritykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>q granking∞crfcd3504irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Internet Open Trading Protocol (IOTP) Version 1, ErratadtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyEThe Message Disposition Notification (MDN) facility defined in RFC 2298 provides a means by which a message can request that message processing by the recipient be acknowledged as well as a format to be used for such acknowledgements.  However, it doesn't describe how multiple Mail User Agents (MUAs) should handle the generation of MDNs in an Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP4) environment.  This document describes how to handle MDNs in such an environment and provides guidelines for implementers of IMAP4 that want to add MDN support to their products. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxACI Worldwide/MessagingDirectdnamekA. Melnikovddatej“®hfilenamegrfc3503eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125454hkeywordsÜcMUAomail user agenteimap4x Internet Message Access ProtocolcMDNx Message Disposition NotificationkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate>w≠ÉgrankingØcrfcd3503irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexZMessage Disposition Notification (MDN) profile for Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document describes the multiappending extension to the Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) (RFC 3501).  This extension provides substantial performance improvements for IMAP clients which upload multiple messages at a time to a mailbox on the server.  A server which supports this extension indicates this with a capability name of "MULTIAPPEND". [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamejM. Crispinddatej“®hfilenamegrfc3502eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125453hkeywordsÉfIMAPv4dimapjimapv4rev1kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>w≠+grankingÆcrfcd3502irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex?Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - MULTIAPPEND ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÇd4469d4466∂habstracty6The Internet Message Access Protocol, Version 4rev1 (IMAP4rev1) allows a client to access and manipulate electronic mail messages on a server.  IMAP4rev1 permits manipulation of mailboxes (remote message folders) in a way that is functionally equivalent to local folders.  IMAP4rev1 also provides the capability for an offline client to resynchronize with the server.  IMAP4rev1 includes operations for creating, deleting, and renaming mailboxes, checking for new messages, permanently removing messages, setting and clearing flags, RFC 2822 and RFC 2045 parsing, searching, and selective fetching of message attributes, texts, and portions thereof.  Messages in IMAP4rev1 are accessed by the use of numbers.  These numbers are either message sequence numbers or unique identifiers.  IMAP4rev1 supports a single server.  A mechanism for accessing configuration information to support multiple IMAP4rev1 servers is discussed in RFC 2244.  IMAP4rev1 does not specify a means of posting mail; this function is handled by a mail transfer protocol such as RFC 2821. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamejM. Crispinddatej“®hfilenamegrfc3501eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125452hkeywordsÑfIMAPv4x Internet Message Access ProtocolmVersion 4rev1jimapv4rev1kobsoletedByÅd9051epageslopublicationDate>w¨’granking≠crfcd3501irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0INTERNET MESSAGE ACCESS PROTOCOL - VERSION 4rev1dtypecrfciupdatedByçd5738d7817d4551d5182d8437d6186d8996d8474d8314d4466d5032d6858d4469µhabstract`darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiS. Ginozaddatej…mhfilenamegrfc3499eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43433hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDate?ﬁSgranking´crfcd3499irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex2Request for Comments Summary RFC Numbers 3400-3499dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty9This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing networks using Synchronous Optical Network (SONET) linear Automatic Protection Switching (APS) architectures. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamejJ. Kuhfeld¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamejJ. Johnson¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamekM. Thatcherddatej“®hfilenamegrfc3498eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgatommibdfullratommib - AToM MIBdnamehAToM MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125451hkeywordsâcMIBxmanagement information baseftcp/ipxtransmission control protocolqinternet protocolxSynchronous Optical NetworkeSONETxAutomatic Protection SwitchingcAPSkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate>i$¸granking™crfcd3498irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄDefinitions of Managed Objects for Synchronous Optical Network (SONET) Linear Automatic Protection Switching (APS) ArchitecturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÜThis memo specifies an RTP payload format for encapsulating uncompressed High Definition Television (HDTV) as defined by the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) standard, SMPTE 292M.  SMPTE is the main standardizing body in the motion imaging industry and the SMPTE 292M standard defines a bit-serial digital interface for local area HDTV transport. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnameiL. Gharai¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationiTektronixdnamejG. Goncher¢kaffiliationxBell Labs, Lucent CorporationdnameiA. Mankinddatej“ßhfilenamegrfc3497eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125450hkeywordsÜcRTPxreal-time transport protocoldhdtvxhigh definition televisioneSMPTEx2Society of Motion Picture and Television EngineerskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>iA8granking©crfcd3497irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\RTP Payload Format for Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE) 292M VideodtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document specifies a Resource ReSerVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling extension for support of Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) Service Class-aware Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic Engineering.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoVivace NetworksdnamekA. G. Malis¢kaffiliationoVivace NetworksdnamehT. Hsiaoddatej“ßhfilenamegrfc3496eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125449hkeywordsÑidiff-servhdiffservgrsvp-texresource reservation protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>oú0granking®crfcd3496irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexèProtocol Extension for Support of Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) Service Class-aware Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This document defines a Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) option that will be used to configure various devices deployed within CableLabs architectures.  Specifically, the document describes DHCP option content that will be used to configure one class of CableLabs client device: a PacketCable Media Terminal Adapter (MTA).  The option content defined within this document will be extended as future CableLabs client devices are developed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamehB. Beser¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehP. Duffyddatej“ßhfilenamegrfc3495eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125448hkeywordsÖdDHCPx#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocolkpacketcablecMTAvMedia Terminal AdapterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>fã}grankingßcrfcd3495irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Option for CableLabs Client ConfigurationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyuThis document recommends the retirement of version 2 of the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAPv2) and other dependent specifications, and discusses the reasons for doing so.  This document recommends RFC 1777, 1778, 1779, 1781, and 2559 (as well as documents they superseded) be moved to Historic status.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej“ßhfilenamegrfc3494eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125447hkeywordsâcDAPkinteractivefaccesseX.500dLDAPxlightweight directory protocolgSTR-REPodirectory namesrrepresenting nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>fãgranking¶crfcd3494irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexKLightweight Directory Access Protocol version 2 (LDAPv2) to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyKThe de facto standard Application Program Interface (API) for TCP/IP applications is the "sockets" interface.  Although this API was developed for Unix in the early 1980s it has also been implemented on a wide variety of non-Unix systems.  TCP/IP applications written using the sockets API have in the past enjoyed a high degree of portability and we would like the same portability with IPv6 applications.  But changes are required to the sockets API to support IPv6 and this memo describes these changes.  These include a new socket address structure to carry IPv6 addresses, new address conversion functions, and some new socket options.  These extensions are designed to provide access to the basic IPv6 features required by TCP and UDP applications, including multicasting, while introducing a minimum of change into the system and providing complete compatibility for existing IPv4 applications.  Additional extensions for advanced IPv6 features (raw sockets and access to the IPv6 extension headers) are defined in another document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnIntransa, Inc.dnamekR. Gilligan¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejS. Thomson¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnamehJ. Bound¢kaffiliationoHewlett-PackarddnameiJ. McCann¢kaffiliation`dnamejW. Stevensddatej—ˇhfilenamegrfc3493eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125446hkeywordsàhinternethprotocolcapikapplicationgprogramiinterfacectcpttransmission controlkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate>wÆgranking•crfcd3493irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex*Basic Socket Interface Extensions for IPv6dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty‹Punycode is a simple and efficient transfer encoding syntax designed for use with Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA).  It uniquely and reversibly transforms a Unicode string into an ASCII string.  ASCII characters in the Unicode string are represented literally, and non-ASCII characters are represented by ASCII characters that are allowed in host name labels (letters, digits, and hyphens).  This document defines a general algorithm called Bootstring that allows a string of basic code points to uniquely represent any string of code points drawn from a larger set.  Punycode is an instance of Bootstring that uses particular parameter values specified by this document, appropriate for IDNA. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniv. of California, BerkeleydnamekA. Costelloddatej“ßhfilenamegrfc3492eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcidndfullx#idn - Internationalized Domain NamednamexInternationalized Domain Namedtypebwgbidjdoc-125445hkeywordsÑdIDNAx.Internationalized Domain Names in Applicationsfsyntaxqstring host labelkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate>i@ëgranking§crfcd3492irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexdPunycode: A Bootstring encoding of Unicode for Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5891∂habstractyÇThis document describes how to prepare internationalized domain name (IDN) labels in order to increase the likelihood that name input and name comparison work in ways that make sense for typical users throughout the world.  This profile of the stringprep protocol is used as part of a suite of on-the-wire protocols for internationalizing the Domain Name System (DNS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjIMC & VPNCdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej“¶hfilenamegrfc3491eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidndfullx#idn - Internationalized Domain NamednamexInternationalized Domain Namedtypebwgbidjdoc-125444hkeywordsÉcIDNxInternationalized Domain NamelapplicationskobsoletedByÅd5891epagesopublicationDate>i@$granking£crfcd3491irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGNameprep: A Stringprep Profile for Internationalized Domain Names (IDN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂUntil now, there has been no standard method for domain names to use characters outside the ASCII repertoire.  This document defines internationalized domain names (IDNs) and a mechanism called Internationalizing Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) for handling them in a standard fashion.  IDNs use characters drawn from a large repertoire (Unicode), but IDNA allows the non-ASCII characters to be represented using only the ASCII characters already allowed in so-called host names today.  This backward-compatible representation is required in existing protocols like DNS, so that IDNs can be introduced with no changes to the existing infrastructure.  IDNA is only meant for processing domain names, not free text. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelP. Faltstrom¢kaffiliationjIMC & VPNCdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamekA. Costelloddatej“¶hfilenamegrfc3490eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidndfullx#idn - Internationalized Domain NamednamexInternationalized Domain Namedtypebwgbidjdoc-125443hkeywordsÑdIDNsxInternationalized Domain NameseasciijcharacterskobsoletedByÇd5890d5891epagesopublicationDate>i?ûgranking¢crfcd3490irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Internationalizing Domain Names in Applications (IDNA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyJSimple Traversal of User Datagram Protocol (UDP) Through Network Address Translators (NATs) (STUN) is a lightweight protocol that allows applications to discover the presence and types of NATs and firewalls between them and the public Internet.  It also provides the ability for applications to determine the public Internet Protocol (IP) addresses allocated to them by the NAT.  STUN works with many existing NATs, and does not require any special behavior from them.  As a result, it allows a wide variety of applications to work through existing NAT infrastructure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamemJ. Weinberger¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegR. Mahyddatej“¶hfilenamegrfc3489eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmidcomdfullx midcom - Middlebox CommunicationdnamewMiddlebox Communicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125442hkeywordsàdSTUNx*Simple Traversal of User Datagram ProtocolcUDPdNATsxNetwork Address TranslatorsklightweightlapplicationsifirewallskobsoletedByÅd5389epages/opublicationDate>zb/granking°crfcd3489irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbSTUN - Simple Traversal of User Datagram Protocol (UDP) Through Network Address Translators (NATs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¥This document describes the Router-port Group Management Protocol (RGMP).  This protocol was developed by Cisco Systems and is used between multicast routers and switches to restrict multicast packet forwarding in switches to those routers where the packets may be needed.  RGMP is designed for backbone switched networks where multiple, high speed routers are interconnected.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemAlex D. Zinindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameeI. Wu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiT. Eckertddatej—ˇhfilenamegrfc3488eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125441hkeywordsÇimulticastoswitches packetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>e:cgranking†crfcd3488irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex:Cisco Systems Router-port Group Management Protocol (RGMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document summarizes requirements for prioritizing access to circuit-switched network, end system and proxy resources for emergency preparedness communications using the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej—ˇhfilenamegrfc3487eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfieprepdfullx(ieprep - Internet Emergency PreparednessdnamexInternet Emergency Preparednessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125440hkeywordsÑx"circuit switched network resourcestend system resourcesoproxy resourcesx%emergency preparedness communicationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>e9Îgrankingücrfcd3487irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWRequirements for Resource Priority Mechanisms for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÔThis document describes a mechanism to signal that compression is desired for one or more Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) messages.  It also states when it is appropriate to send compressed SIP messages to a SIP entity. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej—˛hfilenamegrfc3486eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125439hkeywordsÇcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>;~Ägrankingûcrfcd3486irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Compressing the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5049∂habstractybThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is a text-based protocol for initiating and managing communication sessions.  The protocol can be compressed by using Signaling Compression (SigComp).  Similarly, the Session Description Protocol (SDP) is a text-based protocol intended for describing multimedia sessions for the purposes of session announcement, session invitation, and other forms of multimedia session initiation.  This memo defines the SIP/SDP-specific static dictionary that SigComp may use in order to achieve higher efficiency.  The dictionary is compression algorithm independent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationnTZI/Uni BremendnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationnTZI/Uni BremendnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationrSiemens/Roke ManordnamehR. Price¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamekA. B. Roachddatej—˛hfilenamegrfc3485eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125438hkeywordsácSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolcSDPxSession Description ProtocolgSigCompx+Static Dictionary for Signaling CompressionialgorithmkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>e;¬grankingùcrfcd3485irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÇThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) and Session Description Protocol (SDP) Static Dictionary for Signaling Compression (SigComp)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4896∂habstractyöThis document describes two algorithms, for source address selection and for destination address selection.  The algorithms specify default behavior for all Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) implementations.  They do not override choices made by applications or upper-layer protocols, nor do they preclude the development of more advanced mechanisms for address selection.  The two algorithms share a common context, including an optional mechanism for allowing administrators to provide policy that can override the default behavior.  In dual stack implementations, the destination address selection algorithm can consider both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses - depending on the available source addresses, the algorithm might prefer IPv6 addresses over IPv4 addresses, or vice-versa.  All IPv6 nodes, including both hosts and routers, must implement default address selection as defined in this specification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnameiR. Dravesddatej—˛hfilenamegrfc3484eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125437hkeywordsÉdIPv6fsourcesaddress destinationkobsoletedByÅd6724epagesopublicationDate>e9dgrankingúcrfcd3484irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Default Address Selection for Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty«Common Open Policy Services (COPS) Protocol (RFC 2748), defines the capability of reporting information to the Policy Decision Point (PDP).  The types of report information are success, failure and accounting of an installed state.  This document focuses on the COPS Report Type of Accounting and the necessary framework for the monitoring and reporting of usage feedback for an installed state.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhWorldComdnamejD. Rawlins¢kaffiliationeInteldnamekA. Kulkarni¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamelM. Bokaemper¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegK. Chanddatej“§hfilenamegrfc3483eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrapdfullx"rap - Resource Allocation ProtocoldnamexResource Allocation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125436hkeywordsÑjaccountingopolicy decisionepointcbdpkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate>É£grankingõcrfcd3483irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeFramework for Policy Usage Feedback for Common Open Policy Service with Policy Provisioning (COPS-PR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty„This document provides an overview of E.164 telephone number portability (NP) in the Global Switched Telephone Network (GSTN).  NP is a regulatory imperative seeking to liberalize local telephony service competition, by enabling end-users to retain telephone numbers while changing service providers.  NP changes the fundamental nature of a dialed E.164 number from a hierarchical physical routing address to a virtual address, thereby requiring the transparent translation of the later to the former.  In addition, there are various regulatory constraints that establish relevant parameters for NP implementation, most of which are not network technology specific.  Consequently, the implementation of NP behavior consistent with applicable regulatory constraints, as well as the need for interoperation with the existing GSTN NP implementations, are relevant topics for numerous areas of IP telephony works-in-progress with the IETF.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnameiM. Foster¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamejT. McGarry¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnameeJ. Yuddatej—˛hfilenamegrfc3482eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdenumdfullxenum - Telephone Number MappingdnamexTelephone Number Mappingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125435hkeywordsÇee.164qtelephony routingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>T$grankingöcrfcd3482irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexONumber Portability in the Global Switched Telephone Network (GSTN): An OverviewdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document describes a profile for optimizing TCP to adapt so that it handles paths including second (2.5G) and third (3G) generation wireless networks.  It describes the relevant characteristics of 2.5G and 3G networks, and specific features of example deployments of such networks.  It then recommends TCP algorithm choices for nodes known to be starting or ending on such paths, and it also discusses open issues.  The configuration options recommended in this document are commonly found in modern TCP stacks, and are widely available standards-track mechanisms that the community considers safe for use on the general Internet.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpNTT DoCoMo, Inc.dnamejH. Inamura¢kaffiliationxSun Microsystems LaboratoriesdnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationqEricsson ResearchdnameiR. Ludwig¢kaffiliationfSoneradnameiA. Gurtov¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekF. Khafizovddatej—˛hfilenamegrfc3481eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpilcdfullx7pilc - Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdnamex0Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125434hkeywordsÇepathspalgorithm stackskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>R‘grankingôcrfcd3481irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberGetotaletitlexBTCP over Second (2.5G) and Third (3G) Generation Wireless NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractycCurrent signalling used by Multi-Protocol Label Switching Traffic Engineering (MPLS TE) does not provide support for unnumbered links.  This document defines procedures and extensions to Constraint-Routing Label Distribution Protocol (CR-LDP), one of the MPLS TE signalling protocols that are needed in order to support unnumbered links. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationpNetPlane SystemsdnamekA. Kullbergddatej—˝hfilenamegrfc3480eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125433hkeywordsádmplsxmultiprotocol label switchingstraffic engineeringgmpls-tesTraffic EngineeringfCR-LDPx.Constraint-Routing Label Distribution ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>[˙grankingòcrfcd3480irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSignalling Unnumbered Links in CR-LDP (Constraint-Routing Label Distribution Protocol)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylMultiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) systems will be used in core networks where system downtime must be kept to an absolute minimum.  Many MPLS Label Switching Routers (LSRs) may, therefore, exploit Fault Tolerant (FT) hardware or software to provide high availability of the core networks.  The details of how FT is achieved for the various components of an FT LSR, including Label Distribution Protocol (LDP), the switching hardware and TCP, are implementation specific.  This document identifies issues in the LDP specification in RFC 3036, "LDP Specification", that make it difficult to implement an FT LSR using the current LDP protocols, and defines enhancements to the LDP specification to ease such FT LSR implementations.  The issues and extensions described here are equally applicable to RFC 3212, "Constraint-Based LSP Setup Using LDP" (CR-LDP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtMovaz Networks, Inc.dnameiA. Farrelddatej—˝hfilenamegrfc3479eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125432hkeywordsÖdMPLSxmultiprotocol label switchingcLDPxLabel Distribution Protocolxhigh availability restartkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDate>T¶grankingócrfcd3479irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Fault Tolerance for the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–This document describes a mechanism that helps to minimize the negative effects on MPLS traffic caused by Label Switching Router's (LSR's) control plane restart, specifically by the restart of its Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) component, on LSRs that are capable of preserving the MPLS forwarding component across the restart.  The mechanism described in this document is applicable to all LSRs, both those with the ability to preserve forwarding state during LDP restart and those without (although the latter needs to implement only a subset of the mechanism described in this document).  Supporting (a subset of) the mechanism described here by the LSRs that can not preserve their MPLS forwarding state across the restart would not reduce the negative impact on MPLS traffic caused by their control plane restart, but it would minimize the impact if their neighbor(s) are capable of preserving the forwarding state across the restart of their control plane and implement the mechanism described here.  The mechanism makes minimalistic assumptions on what has to be preserved across restart - the mechanism assumes that only the actual MPLS forwarding state has to be preserved; the mechanism does not require any of the LDP-related states to be preserved across the restart.  The procedures described in this document apply to downstream unsolicited label distribution.  Extending these procedures to downstream on demand label distribution is for further study. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamemM. Leelanivas¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhter¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamekR. Aggarwalddatej—˝hfilenamegrfc3478eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125431hkeywordsÑcLDPxLabel Distribution ProtocoldMPLSxMulti-Protocol Label SwitchingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>Tgrankingñcrfcd3478irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Graceful Restart Mechanism for Label Distribution ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÅCurrent signalling used by Multi-Protocol Label Switching Traffic Engineering (MPLS TE) does not provide support for unnumbered links.  This document defines procedures and extensions to Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) for Label Switched Path (LSP) Tunnels (RSVP-TE), one of the MPLS TE signalling protocols, that are needed in order to support unnumbered links. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamekK. Kompella¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamejY. Rekhterddatej“1hfilenamegrfc3477eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125430hkeywordsÖgMPLS-TExMulti-Protocol Label SwitchingsTraffic EngineeringdRSVPxResource ReSerVation ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate>L5Bgrankingïcrfcd3477irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Signalling Unnumbered Links in Resource ReSerVation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6107∂habstractytThe Optical Interworking Forum (OIF) has defined extensions to the Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) and the Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP) for optical User Network Interface (UNI) signaling.  These extensions consist of a set of new data objects and error codes.  This document describes these extensions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmTellium, Inc.dnamenB. Rajagopalanddatej“•hfilenamegrfc3476eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125429hkeywordsÑcoifxoptical interworking forumcunivuser network interfacekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ãf#grankingîcrfcd3476irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex”Documentation of IANA Assignments for Label Distribution Protocol (LDP), Resource ReSerVation Protocol (RSVP), and Resource ReSerVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Extensions for Optical UNI SignalingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3468∂habstractyáAutomatic Switched Optical Network (ASON) is an architecture, specified by ITU-T Study Group 15, for the introduction of a control plane for optical networks.  The ASON architecture specifies a set of reference points that defines the relationship between the ASON architectural entities.  Signaling over interfaces defined in those reference points can make use of protocols that are defined by the IETF in the context of Generalized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) work.  This document describes Constraint-Based LSP setup using LDP (CR-LDP) extensions for signaling over the interfaces defined in the ASON reference points.  The purpose of the document is to request that the IANA assigns code points necessary for the CR-LDP extensions.  The protocol specifications for the use of the CR-LDP extensions are found in ITU-T documents.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamemO. Aboul-Magdddatej“•hfilenamegrfc3475eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125428hkeywordsÇxlabel switching protocoleitu-tkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ãe—grankingìcrfcd3475irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexåDocumentation of IANA assignments for Constraint-Based LSP setup using LDP (CR-LDP) Extensions for Automatic Switched Optical Network (ASON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3468∂habstractyôThe Generalized MultiProtocol Label Switching (GMPLS) suite of protocol specifications has been defined to provide support for different technologies as well as different applications.  These include support for requesting TDM connections based on Synchronous Optical NETwork/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) as well as Optical Transport Networks (OTNs).  This document concentrates on the signaling aspects of the GMPLS suite of protocols, specifically GMPLS signaling using Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE).  It proposes additional extensions to these signaling protocols to support the capabilities of an ASON network.  This document proposes appropriate extensions towards the resolution of additional requirements identified and communicated by the ITU-T Study Group 15 in support of ITU's ASON standardization effort.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuNew York City TransitdnamefZ. Lin¢kaffiliationgTelliumdnamemD. Pendarakisddatej“•hfilenamegrfc3474eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125427hkeywordsÇesonetcsdhkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ãeÅgrankingícrfcd3474irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex‹Documentation of IANA assignments for Generalized MultiProtocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Resource Reservation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) Usage and Extensions for Automatically Switched Optical Network (ASON)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyfThis document describes extensions to Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Resource ReserVation Protocol - Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) signaling required to support Generalized MPLS.  Generalized MPLS extends the MPLS control plane to encompass time-division (e.g., Synchronous Optical Network and Synchronous Digital Hierarchy, SONET/SDH), wavelength (optical lambdas) and spatial switching (e.g., incoming port or fiber to outgoing port or fiber).  This document presents a RSVP-TE specific description of the extensions.  A generic functional description can be found in separate documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnMovaz NetworksdnameiL. Bergerddatej“1hfilenamegrfc3473eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-125426hkeywordsàeGMPLSx*Generalized Multi-Protocol Label SwitchingiSONET/SDHxSynchronous Optical NetworkxSynchronous Digital HierarchygRSVP-TExResource ReserVation ProtocolsTraffic EngineeringkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate>L4»grankingëcrfcd3473irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Resource ReserVation Protocol-Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByéd4873d4874d4974d8359d4420d4783d5420d5151d6780d4003d4201d5063d6002d6003∂habstractydThis document describes extensions to Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Constraint-based Routed Label Distribution Protocol (CR-LDP) signaling required to support Generalized MPLS.  Generalized MPLS extends the MPLS control plane to encompass time-division (e.g., Synchronous Optical Network and Synchronous Digital Hierarchy, SONET/SDH), wavelength (optical lambdas) and spatial switching (e.g., incoming port or fiber to outgoing port or fiber).  This document presents a CR-LDP specific description of the extensions.  A generic functional description can be found in separate documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamepP. Ashwood-Smith¢kaffiliationnMovaz NetworksdnameiL. Bergerddatej“1hfilenamegrfc3472eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-125425hkeywordsáeGMPLSx*Generalized Multi-Protocol Label SwitchingiSONET/SDHxSynchronous Optical NetworkxSynchronous Digital HierarchyfCR-LDPx3Constraint-based Routed Label Distribution ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>L4Egrankingêcrfcd3472irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÑGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Constraint-based Routed Label Distribution Protocol (CR-LDP) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd4201d3468∂habstractyLThis document describes extensions to Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) signaling required to support Generalized MPLS.  Generalized MPLS extends the MPLS control plane to encompass time-division (e.g., Synchronous Optical Network and Synchronous Digital Hierarchy, SONET/SDH), wavelength (optical lambdas) and spatial switching (e.g., incoming port or fiber to outgoing port or fiber).  This document presents a functional description of the extensions.  Protocol specific formats and mechanisms, and technology specific details are specified in separate documents. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnMovaz NetworksdnameiL. Bergerddatej“1hfilenamegrfc3471eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeccampdfullx,ccamp - Common Control and Measurement Planednamex$Common Control and Measurement Planedtypebwgbidjdoc-125424hkeywordsÖeGMPLSx*Generalized Multi-Protocol Label SwitchingiSONET/SDHxSynchronous Optical NetworkxSynchronous Digital HierarchykobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate>L0ìgrankingècrfcd3471irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSGeneralized Multi-Protocol Label Switching (GMPLS) Signaling Functional DescriptiondtypecrfciupdatedByâd4872d6205d8359d7074d4328d7699d4201d6002d6003∑habstractyEThe Extensible Markup Language (XML) is a framework for structuring data.  While it evolved from Standard Generalized Markup Language (SGML) -- a markup language primarily focused on structuring documents -- XML has evolved to be a widely-used mechanism for representing structured data.  There are a wide variety of Internet protocols being developed; many have need for a representation for structured data relevant to their application.  There has been much interest in the use of XML as a representation method.  This document describes basic XML concepts, analyzes various alternatives in the use of XML, and provides guidelines for the use of XML within IETF standards-track protocols.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeck¢kaffiliationxDover Beach Consulting, Inc.dnamegM. Rose¢kaffiliationxAdobe Systems IncorporateddnamekL. Masinterddatej“0hfilenamegrfc3470eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125423hkeywordsÉddataidocumentsistructurekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>1ï»grankingécrfcd3470irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberFetotaletitlexPGuidelines for the Use of Extensible Markup Language (XML) within IETF ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyÉMulti-protocol label switching (MPLS) integrates the label swapping forwarding paradigm with network layer routing.  To deliver reliable service, MPLS requires a set of procedures to provide protection of the traffic carried on different paths.  This requires that the label switching routers (LSRs) support fault detection, fault notification, and fault recovery mechanisms, and that MPLS signaling support the configuration of recovery.  With these objectives in mind, this document specifies a framework for MPLS based recovery.  Restart issues are not included in this framework.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationnMetanoia, Inc.dnameiV. Sharma¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamemF. Hellstrandddatej—˝hfilenamegrfc3469eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125422hkeywordsÅorouting traffickobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate>R”˛grankingçcrfcd3469irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBFramework for Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS)-based RecoverydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5462∂habstracty@This document documents the consensus reached by the Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Working Group within the IETF to focus its efforts on "Resource Reservation Protocol (RSVP)-TE: Extensions to RSVP for Label- Switched Paths (LSP) Tunnels" (RFC 3209) as the MPLS signalling protocol for traffic engineering applications and to undertake no new efforts relating to "Constraint-Based LSP Setup using Label Distribution Protocol (LDP)" (RFC 3212).  The recommendations of section 6 have been accepted by the IESG.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejG. Swallowddatej—¸hfilenamegrfc3468eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125421hkeywordsÉgrsvp-tecldpx0resource reservation protocol label distributionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>[¯grankingåcrfcd3468irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex[The Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) Working Group decision on MPLS signaling protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty}This document reviews the original function and purpose of the domain name system (DNS).  It contrasts that history with some of the purposes for which the DNS has recently been applied and some of the newer demands being placed upon it or suggested for it.  A framework for an alternative to placing these additional stresses on the DNS is then outlined.  This document and that framework are not a proposed solution, only a strong suggestion that the time has come to begin thinking more broadly about the problems we are encountering and possible approaches to solving them.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Klensinddatej—¸hfilenamegrfc3467eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125420hkeywordsÖghistorytinternationalizationgunicodeeasciirmultilingual nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>egrankingãcrfcd3467irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Role of the Domain Name System (DNS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyπContent (distribution) internetworking (CDI) is the technology for interconnecting content networks, sometimes previously called "content peering" or "CDN peering".  A common vocabulary helps the process of discussing such interconnection and interoperation.  This document introduces content networks and content internetworking, and defines elements for such a common vocabulary.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejTed Hardiedarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamefM. Day¢kaffiliationhStorigendnamegB. Cain¢kaffiliationoTomlinson GroupdnamelG. Tomlinson¢kaffiliationuMedia Publisher, Inc.dnamejP. Rzewskiddatej—¸hfilenamegrfc3466eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymccdidfullx*cdi - Content Distribution Internetworkingdnamex$Content Distribution Internetworkingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125419hkeywordsÅtdistribution peeringkobsoletedByÅd7336epagesopublicationDate>e:Úgrankingäcrfcd3466irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)A Model for Content Internetworking (CDI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This document proposes a small modification to the way TCP increases its congestion window.  Rather than the traditional method of increasing the congestion window by a constant amount for each arriving acknowledgment, the document suggests basing the increase on the number of previously unacknowledged bytes each ACK covers.  This change improves the performance of TCP, as well as closes a security hole TCP receivers can use to induce the sender into increasing the sending rate too rapidly.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationlBBN/NASA GRCdnameiM. Allmanddatej—¸hfilenamegrfc3465eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125418hkeywordsÖcTCPxtransmission control protocoltsecurity performancecABCxAppropriate Byte CountingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate>B°"grankingâcrfcd3465irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;TCP Congestion Control with Appropriate Byte Counting (ABC)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty“This memo defines a Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) content-type that may be used by a message transfer agent (MTA) or electronic mail gateway to report the result of an attempt to deliver a message to one or more recipients.  This content-type is intended as a machine-processable replacement for the various types of delivery status notifications currently used in Internet electronic mail.  Because many messages are sent between the Internet and other messaging systems (such as X.400 or the so-called "Local Area Network (LAN)-based" systems), the Delivery Status Notification (DSN) protocol is designed to be useful in a multi-protocol messaging environment.  To this end, the protocol described in this memo provides for the carriage of "foreign" addresses and error codes, in addition to those normally used in Internet mail.  Additional attributes may also be defined to support "tunneling" of foreign notifications through Internet mail. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TennesseednamehK. Moore¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuilddatej“0hfilenamegrfc3464eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125417hkeywordsÖcDSNxDelivery Status NotificationsdMIMEx%Multipurpose Internet Mail ExtensionslContent TypekobsoletedByÄepages(opublicationDate>0)pgrankingàcrfcd3464irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>An Extensible Message Format for Delivery Status NotificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd4865d5337d6533∂habstractymThis document defines a set of extended status codes for use within the mail system for delivery status reports, tracking, and improved diagnostics.  In combination with other information provided in the Delivery Status Notification (DSN) delivery report, these codes facilitate media and language independent rendering of message delivery status. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuilddatej“0hfilenamegrfc3463eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125416hkeywordsÑhEMS-CODEx!Enhanced Mail System Status Codesxsimple mail transfer protocoldSMTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>0)grankingácrfcd3463irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Enhanced Mail System Status CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÖd3886d5248d4468d4954d4865∂habstracty2The Multipart/Report Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) content-type is a general "family" or "container" type for electronic mail reports of any kind.  Although this memo defines only the use of the Multipart/Report content-type with respect to delivery status reports, mail processing programs will benefit if a single content-type is used to for all kinds of reports.  This document is part of a four document set describing the delivery status report service.  This collection includes the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) extensions to request delivery status reports, a MIME content for the reporting of delivery reports, an enumeration of extended status codes, and a multipart container for the delivery report, the original message, and a human-friendly summary of the failure. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelG. Vaudreuilddatej“0hfilenamegrfc3462eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125415hkeywordsÉhMIME-RPTx%Multipurpose Internet Mail ExtensionsfReportkobsoletedByÅd6522epagesopublicationDate>0'ΩgrankingÜcrfcd3462irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZThe Multipart/Report Content Type for the Reporting of Mail System Administrative MessagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5337∂habstracty␀This memo defines an extension to the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) service, which allows an SMTP client to specify (a) that Delivery Status Notifications (DSNs) should be generated under certain conditions, (b) whether such notifications should return the contents of the message, and (c) additional information, to be returned with a DSN, that allows the sender to identify both the recipient(s) for which the DSN was issued, and the transaction in which the original message was sent. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TennesseednamehK. Mooreddatej“0hfilenamegrfc3461eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125414hkeywordsÉhSMTP-DSNxSimple Mail Transfer ProtocolxDelivery Status NotificationskobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDate>0&˘grankingÖcrfcd3461irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex_Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) Service Extension for Delivery Status Notifications (DSNs)dtypecrfciupdatedByÖd8098d3885d3798d5337d6533∂habstractyÃThis document specifies a number of changes to the Policy Core Information Model (PCIM, RFC 3060).  Two types of changes are included.  First, several completely new elements are introduced, for example, classes for header filtering, that extend PCIM into areas that it did not previously cover.  Second, there are cases where elements of PCIM (for example, policy rule priorities) are deprecated, and replacement elements are defined (in this case, priorities tied to associations that refer to policy rules).  Both types of changes are done in such a way that, to the extent possible, interoperability with implementations of the original PCIM model is preserved.  This document updates RFC 3060. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamehB. Mooreddatej“0hfilenamegrfc3460eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpolicydfullxpolicy - Policy FrameworkdnamepPolicy Frameworkdtypebwgbidjdoc-125413hkeywordsÖdPCIMxPolicy Core Information Modelfcommonfschemaoobject-orientedkobsoletedByÄepages]opublicationDate>(C‰grankingÑcrfcd3460irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/Policy Core Information Model (PCIM) ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÏThis document describes the use of a mechanism for identifying body parts that a sender deems critical in a multi-part Internet mail message.  The mechanism described is a parameter to Content-Disposition, as described by RFC 3204.  By knowing what parts of a message the sender deems critical, a content gateway can intelligently handle multi-part messages when providing gateway services to systems of lesser capability.  Critical content can help a content gateway to decide what parts to forward.  It can indicate how hard a gateway should try to deliver a body part.  It can help the gateway to pick body parts that are safe to silently delete when a system of lesser capability receives a message.  In addition, critical content can help the gateway chose the notification strategy for the receiving system.  Likewise, if the sender expects the destination to do some processing on a body part, critical content allows the sender to mark body parts that the receiver must process. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrSnowShore NetworksdnameiE. Burgerddatej“/hfilenamegrfc3459eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-125412hkeywordsÑdMIMEx&Multi-purpose Internet Mail Extensionsjbody partsscontent-dispositionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>9nﬂgrankingÉcrfcd3459irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHCritical Content Multi-purpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) ParameterdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5621∂habstractyThis memo describes a new RFC 2822 message header, "Message-Context".  This header provides information about the context and presentation characteristics of a message.  A receiving user agent (UA) may use this information as a hint to optimally present the message. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationrSnowShore NetworksdnameiE. Burger¢kaffiliationhComversednamejE. Candell¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehC. Eliot¢kaffiliationlNine by NinednamehG. Klyneddatej“/hfilenamegrfc3458eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdvpimdfullx&vpim - Voice Profile for Internet MaildnamexVoice Profile for Internet Maildtypebwgbidjdoc-125411hkeywordsÇjuser agentbUAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>9ngrankingÇcrfcd3458irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Message Context for Internet MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3938∂habstractyIPsec offers much promise as a secure remote access mechanism.  However, there are a number of differing remote access scenarios, each having some shared and some unique requirements.  A thorough understanding of these requirements is necessary in order to effectively evaluate the suitability of a specific set of mechanisms for any particular remote access scenario.  This document enumerates the requirements for a number of common remote access scenarios.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiAirespacednamehS. Kelly¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamenS. Ramamoorthiddatej“/hfilenamegrfc3457eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsradfullx!ipsra - IP Security Remote AccessdnamexIP Security Remote Accessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125410hkeywordsÇeipsraxcommon remote access scenarioskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>:˝(grankingÅcrfcd3457irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Requirements for IPsec Remote Access ScenariosdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`This memo explores the requirements for host configuration in IPsec tunnel mode, and describes how the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4) may be leveraged for configuration.  In many remote access scenarios, a mechanism for making the remote host appear to be present on the local corporate network is quite useful.  This may be accomplished by assigning the host a "virtual" address from the corporate network, and then tunneling traffic via IPsec from the host's ISP-assigned address to the corporate security gateway.  In IPv4, DHCP provides for such remote host configuration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationjIntel CorpdnamehB. Patel¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehB. Aboba¢kaffiliationiAirespacednamehS. Kelly¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamehV. Guptaddatej“/hfilenamegrfc3456eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymeipsradfullx!ipsra - IP Security Remote AccessdnamexIP Security Remote Accessdtypebwgbidjdoc-125409hkeywordsÑfDHCPv4x#Dynamic Host Configuration Protocolhsecurityqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>:¸ägrankingÄcrfcd3456irfcNumberÄestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexODynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4) Configuration of IPsec Tunnel ModedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyæThis document describes a set of private Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) headers (P-headers) used by the 3rd-Generation Partnership Project (3GPP), along with their applicability, which is limited to particular environments.  The P-headers are for a variety of purposes within the networks that the partners use, including charging and information about the networks a call traverses.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepM. Garcia-Martin¢kaffiliationfLucentdnamelE. Henrikson¢kaffiliationhVodafonednamehD. Millsddatej…·hfilenamegrfc3455eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125408hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7315epages"opublicationDate>"˛␀grankingcrfcd3455irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPrivate Header (P-Header) Extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for the 3rd-Generation Partnership Project (3GPP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+This document describes a framework for preparing Unicode text strings in order to increase the likelihood that string input and string comparison work in ways that make sense for typical users throughout the world.  The stringprep protocol is useful for protocol identifier values, company and personal names, internationalized domain names, and other text strings.  This document does not specify how protocols should prepare text strings.  Protocols must create profiles of stringprep in order to fully specify the processing options. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjIMC & VPNCdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationhViageniednamekM. Blanchetddatej—‹hfilenamegrfc3454eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125407hkeywordsÇlunicode texttinternationalizationkobsoletedByÅd7564epages[opublicationDate>yÒgranking~crfcd3454irfcNumber~estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Preparation of Internationalized Strings ("stringprep")dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty´This memo describes the use of Forward Error Correction (FEC) codes to efficiently provide and/or augment reliability for one-to-many reliable data transport using IP multicast.  One of the key properties of FEC codes in this context is the ability to use the same packets containing FEC data to simultaneously repair different packet loss patterns at multiple receivers.  Different classes of FEC codes and some of their basic properties are described and terminology relevant to implementing FEC in a reliable multicast protocol is introduced.  Examples are provided of possible abstract formats for packets carrying FEC.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekL. Vicisano¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejJ. Gemmell¢kaffiliationjUniv. PisadnamehL. Rizzo¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationlCambridge U.dnamelJ. Crowcroftddatej—⁄hfilenamegrfc3453eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125406hkeywordsÉbipqinternet protocolndata transportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>∞8granking}crfcd3453irfcNumber}estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?The Use of Forward Error Correction (FEC) in Reliable MulticastdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyêThis document generally describes how to use Forward Error Correction (FEC) codes to efficiently provide and/or augment reliability for data transport.  The primary focus of this document is the application of FEC codes to one-to-many reliable data transport using IP multicast.  This document describes what information is needed to identify a specific FEC code, what information needs to be communicated out-of-band to use the FEC code, and what information is needed in data packets to identify the encoding symbols they carry.  The procedures for specifying FEC codes and registering them with the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) are also described.  This document should be read in conjunction with and uses the terminology of the companion document titled, "The Use of Forward Error Correction (FEC) in Reliable Multicast".  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekL. Vicisano¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejJ. Gemmell¢kaffiliationjUniv. PisadnamehL. Rizzo¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationtCambridge UniversitydnamelJ. Crowcroftddatej—⁄hfilenamegrfc3452eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125405hkeywordsácFECxForward Error Correctiongcontentfstreamhdeliveryimulticastqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÇd5445d5052epagesopublicationDate>Øûgranking|crfcd3452irfcNumber|estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Forward Error Correction (FEC) Building BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty˙Layered Coding Transport (LCT) provides transport level support for reliable content delivery and stream delivery protocols.  LCT is specifically designed to support protocols using IP multicast, but also provides support to protocols that use unicast.  LCT is compatible with congestion control that provides multiple rate delivery to receivers and is also compatible with coding techniques that provide reliable delivery of content.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejJ. Gemmell¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekL. Vicisano¢kaffiliationjUniv. PisadnamehL. Rizzo¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationoCambridge Univ.dnamelJ. Crowcroftddatej—ÿhfilenamegrfc3451eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125404hkeywordsácLCTxLayered Coding Transportgcontentfstreamhdeliveryimulticastqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÅd5651epagesopublicationDate>Ø(granking{crfcd3451irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Layered Coding Transport (LCT) Building BlockdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) protocol, a massively scalable reliable content delivery protocol.  Asynchronous Layered Coding combines the Layered Coding Transport (LCT) building block, a multiple rate congestion control building block and the Forward Error Correction (FEC) building block to provide congestion controlled reliable asynchronous delivery of content to an unlimited number of concurrent receivers from a single sender.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejJ. Gemmell¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekL. Vicisano¢kaffiliationjUniv. PisadnamehL. Rizzo¢kaffiliationoCambridge Univ.dnamelJ. Crowcroftddatej—‹hfilenamegrfc3450eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125403hkeywordsÖgcontenthdeliveryjcongestiongcontrolireceiverskobsoletedByÅd5775epages"opublicationDate>Æπgrankingzcrfcd3450irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8Asynchronous Layered Coding (ALC) Protocol InstantiationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyøThis document describes TCP performance problems that arise because of asymmetric effects.  These problems arise in several access networks, including bandwidth-asymmetric networks and packet radio subnetworks, for different underlying reasons.  However, the end result on TCP performance is the same in both cases: performance often degrades significantly because of imperfection and variability in the ACK feedback from the receiver to the sender.  The document details several mitigations to these effects, which have either been proposed or evaluated in the literature, or are currently deployed in networks.  These solutions use a combination of local link- layer techniques, subnetwork, and end-to-end mechanisms, consisting of: (i) techniques to manage the channel used for the upstream bottleneck link carrying the ACKs, typically using header compression or reducing the frequency of TCP ACKs, (ii) techniques to handle this reduced ACK frequency to retain the TCP sender's acknowledgment-triggered self- clocking and (iii) techniques to schedule the data and ACK packets in the reverse direction to improve performance in the presence of two-way traffic.  Each technique is described, together with known issues, and recommendations for use.  A summary of the recommendations is provided at the end of the document.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgMIT LCSdnameoH. Balakrishnan¢kaffiliationrMicrosoft ResearchdnamenV. Padmanabhan¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Aberdeen, U.K.dnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationxUniversity of Aberdeen, U.K.dnameqM. Sooriyabandaraddatej—€hfilenamegrfc3449eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpilcdfullx7pilc - Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdnamex0Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125402hkeywordsÑelinksfsenderhreceivercackkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate>tDgrankingycrfcd3449irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberEetotaletitlex6TCP Performance Implications of Network Path AsymmetrydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃThis document specifies TCP-Friendly Rate Control (TFRC).  TFRC is a congestion control mechanism for unicast flows operating in a best- effort Internet environment.  It is reasonably fair when competing for bandwidth with TCP flows, but has a much lower variation of throughput over time compared with TCP, making it more suitable for applications such as telephony or streaming media where a relatively smooth sending rate is of importance. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiJ. Padhye¢kaffiliationvUniversity of MannheimdnameiJ. Widmerddatej“.hfilenamegrfc3448eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125401hkeywordsÖdTFRCxTCP Friendly Rate Controljcongestiongunicastostreaming mediakobsoletedByÅd5348epagesopublicationDate>5í–grankingxcrfcd3448irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8TCP Friendly Rate Control (TFRC): Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty{This memo represents a republication of PKCS #1 v2.1 from RSA Laboratories' Public-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) series, and change control is retained within the PKCS process.  The body of this document is taken directly from the PKCS #1 v2.1 document, with certain corrections made during the publication process.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamesJeffrey I. Schillerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejJ. Jonsson¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejB. Kaliskiddatej—¸hfilenamegrfc3447eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125400hkeywordsÑddatafpublicckeylcryptosystemkobsoletedByÅd8017epagesHopublicationDate>B¢Rgrankingwcrfcd3447irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXPublic-Key Cryptography Standards (PKCS) #1: RSA Cryptography Specifications Version 2.1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx¯This document describes a mechanism to allow for an arbitrary number of Rendevous Points (RPs) per group in a single shared-tree Protocol Independent Multicast-Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) domain.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationeVeriodnamefD. Kim¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnameiH. Kilmer¢kaffiliationpProcket NetworksdnamelD. Farinacciddatej“.hfilenamegrfc3446eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmboneddfullxmboned - MBONE DeploymentdnamepMBONE Deploymentdtypebwgbidjdoc-125399hkeywordsÇksparse modersingle shared-treekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>8grankingvcrfcd3446irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÄAnycast Rendevous Point (RP) mechanism using Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) and Multicast Source Discovery Protocol (MSDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyﬂThis document limits the Domain Name System (DNS) KEY Resource Record (RR) to only keys used by the Domain Name System Security Extensions (DNSSEC).  The original KEY RR used sub-typing to store both DNSSEC keys and arbitrary application keys.  Storing both DNSSEC and application keys with the same record type is a mistake.  This document removes application keys from the KEY record by redefining the Protocol Octet field in the KEY RR Data.  As a result of removing application keys, all but one of the flags in the KEY record become unnecessary and are redefined.  Three existing application key sub-types are changed to reserved, but the format of the KEY record is not changed.  This document updates RFC 2535. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnameiD. Massey¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegS. Roseddatej—€hfilenamegrfc3445eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125398hkeywordsÉjDNS-SECEXTcdnsnauthenticationkobsoletedByÉd4033d4034d4035epages
opublicationDate>␀π˙grankingucrfcd3445irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Limiting the Scope of the KEY Resource Record (RR)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊There has been ongoing confusion about the differences between Information Models and Data Models for defining managed objects in network management.  This document explains the differences between these terms by analyzing how existing network management model specifications (from the IETF and other bodies such as the International Telecommunication Union (ITU) or the Distributed Management Task Force (DMTF)) fit into the universe of Information Models and Data Models.  This memo documents the main results of the 8th workshop of the Network Management Research Group (NMRG) of the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF) hosted by the University of Texas at Austin.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtUniversity of TwentednamegA. Pras¢kaffiliationxUniversity of OsnabrueckdnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej“.hfilenamegrfc3444eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125397hkeywordsÅrnetwork managementkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>(B∏grankingtcrfcd3444irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<On the Difference between Information Models and Data ModelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis document describes Time To Live (TTL) processing in hierarchical Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) networks and is motivated by the need to formalize a TTL-transparent mode of operation for an MPLS label-switched path.  It updates RFC 3032, "MPLS Label Stack Encoding".  TTL processing in both Pipe and Uniform Model hierarchical tunnels are specified with examples for both "push" and "pop" cases.  The document also complements RFC 3270, "MPLS Support of Differentiated Services" and ties together the terminology introduced in that document with TTL processing in hierarchical MPLS networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgBrocadednamejP. Agarwal¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehB. Akyolddatej“.hfilenamegrfc3443eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125396hkeywordsácTTLlTime To Livetlabel stack encodingmuniform modeljpipe modeldMPLSxMulti-Protocol Label SwitchingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate>(C^grankingscrfcd3443irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOTime To Live (TTL) Processing in Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5462∂habstracty6This document defines a new Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) option which is passed from the DHCP Server to the DHCP Client to configure a list of static routes in the client.  The network destinations in these routes are classless - each routing table entry includes a subnet mask. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationtApple Computer, Inc.dnamekS. Cheshire¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegB. Volzddatej—€hfilenamegrfc3442eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125395hkeywordsÉdDHCPx#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocoliBootstrapkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate=˙á8grankingrcrfcd3442irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexZThe Classless Static Route Option for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) version 4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXThis document describes an Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) package for the Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP).  This package includes new Local Connection Options, ATM-specific events and signals, and ATM connection parameters.  Also included is a description of codec and profile negotiation.  It extends the MGCP that is currently being deployed in a number of products.  Implementers should be aware of developments in the IETF Megaco Working Group and ITU SG16, which are currently working on a potential successor to this protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehR. Kumarddatej“.hfilenamegrfc3441eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125394hkeywordsÇjconnectionmcodec profilekobsoletedByÄepages2opublicationDate>$•grankingqcrfcd3441irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVAsynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM) Package for the Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes additional managed objects used for managing Asymmetric Digital Subscriber Line (ADSL) interfaces not covered by the ADSL Line MIB (RFC 2662). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqPedestal NetworksdnameeF. Ly¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekG. Bathrickddatej—€hfilenamegrfc3440eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125393hkeywordsÜdSNMPx"simple network management protocolcMIBxManagement Information BasedADSLx"asymmetric digital subscriber linekobsoletedByÄepages$opublicationDate>yGgrankingpcrfcd3440irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPDefinitions of Extension Managed Objects for Asymmetric Digital Subscriber LinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty—This document extends RFC 1958 by outlining some of the philosophical guidelines to which architects and designers of Internet backbone networks should adhere.  We describe the Simplicity Principle, which states that complexity is the primary mechanism that impedes efficient scaling, and discuss its implications on the architecture, design and engineering issues found in large scale Internet backbones.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyerddatej—€hfilenamegrfc3439eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125392hkeywordsÅcIABkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=˜∫˝grankingocrfcd3439irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Some Internet Architectural Guidelines and PhilosophydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document describes updates to the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) considerations for the Layer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP).  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekW. Townsleyddatej—⁄hfilenamegrfc3438eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125391hkeywordsÖdL2TPcpppnpoint-to-pointhprotocolgpacketskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>Ãègrankingncrfcd3438irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberDetotaletitlexdLayer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) Considerations UpdatedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThis document defines extensions to the Layer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) for enhanced support of link-specific Point to Point Protocol (PPP) options.  PPP endpoints typically have direct access to the common physical media connecting them and thus have detailed knowledge about the media that is in use.  When the L2TP is used, the two PPP peers are no longer directly connected over the same physical media.  Instead, L2TP inserts a virtual connection over some or all of the PPP connection by tunneling PPP frames over a packet switched network such as IP.  Under some conditions, an L2TP endpoint may need to negotiate PPP Link Control Protocol (LCP) options at a location which may not have access to all of the media information necessary for proper participation in the LCP negotiation.  This document provides a mechanism for communicating desired LCP options between L2TP endpoints in advance of PPP LCP negotiation at the far end of an L2TP tunnel, as well as a mechanism for communicating the negotiated LCP options back to where the native PPP link resides. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgzev.netdnameiW. Palter¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekW. Townsleyddatej—⁄hfilenamegrfc3437eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125390hkeywordsÑdL2TPxLayer-Two Tunneling ProtocolcLCPuLink Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate>x∑grankingmcrfcd3437irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQLayer-Two Tunneling Protocol Extensions for PPP Link Control Protocol NegotiationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty=This document describes the usage of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol, as defined in RFC 2246, over the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP), as defined in RFC 2960 and RFC 3309.  The user of TLS can take advantage of the features provided by SCTP, namely the support of multiple streams to avoid head of line blocking and the support of multi-homing to provide network level fault tolerance.  Additionally, discussions of extensions of SCTP are also supported, meaning especially the support of dynamic reconfiguration of IP- addresses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsUniversity of EssendnamelA. Jungmaier¢kaffiliationiRTFM Inc.dnamekE. Rescorla¢kaffiliationjSiemens AGdnameiM. Tuexenddatej—Ÿhfilenamegrfc3436eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125389hkeywordsÑdSCTPx$Stream Control Transmission ProtocolcTLSxTransport Layer SecuritykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate>␀∏‡grankinglcrfcd3436irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBTransport Layer Security over Stream Control Transmission ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyùThis document describes an application programming interface and a corresponding protocol (MGCP) which is used between elements of a decomposed multimedia gateway.  The decomposed multimedia gateway consists of a Call Agent, which contains the call control "intelligence", and a media gateway which contains the media functions, e.g., conversion from TDM voice to Voice over IP.  Media gateways contain endpoints on which the Call Agent can create, modify and delete connections in order to establish and control media sessions with other multimedia endpoints.  Also, the Call Agent can instruct the endpoints to detect certain events and generate signals.  The endpoints automatically communicate changes in service state to the Call Agent.  Furthermore, the Call Agent can audit endpoints as well as the connections on endpoints.  The basic and general MGCP protocol is defined in this document, however most media gateways will need to implement one or more MGCP packages, which define extensions to the protocol suitable for use with specific types of media gateways.  Such packages are defined in separate documents.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasen¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiB. Fosterddatej“.hfilenamegrfc3435eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125388hkeywordsÑevoicebIPhinternetdVoIPkobsoletedByÄepages“opublicationDate>$§∏grankingkcrfcd3435irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1Media Gateway Control Protocol (MGCP) Version 1.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3661∂habstractyÖThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for extending the alarm thresholding capabilities found in the Remote Monitoring (RMON) MIB (RFC 2819), to provide similar threshold monitoring of objects based on the Counter64 data type. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. McCloghrieddatej—Ÿhfilenamegrfc3434eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125387hkeywordsâdRMONqRemote MonitoringcMIBxManagement Information Baseicounter64esmiv2x-Structure of Management Information Version 2dSNMPx"Simple Network Management ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>Õ grankingjcrfcd3434irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Remote Monitoring MIB Extensions for High Capacity AlarmsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¶This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for extending the Entity MIB (RFC 2737) to provide generalized access to information related to physical sensors, which are often found in networking equipment (such as chassis temperature, fan RPM, power supply voltage). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationjAvaya Inc.dnamelD. Romascanu¢kaffiliationrL-3 CommunicationsdnamelK.C. Norsethddatej—Ÿhfilenamegrfc3433eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfentmibdfullsentmib - Entity MIBdnamejEntity MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125386hkeywordsÖcMIBxManagement Information Basepphysical sensorsdSNMPx"Simple Network Management ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>∏cgrankingicrfcd3433irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Entity Sensor Management Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractymThis memo describes a periodic sampling method and relevant metrics for assessing the performance of IP networks.  First, the memo motivates periodic sampling and addresses the question of its value as an alternative to the Poisson sampling described in RFC 2330.  The benefits include applicability to active and passive measurements, simulation of constant bit rate (CBR) traffic (typical of multimedia communication, or nearly CBR, as found with voice activity detection), and several instances in which analysis can be simplified.  The sampling method avoids predictability by mandating random start times and finite length tests.  Following descriptions of the sampling method and sample metric parameters, measurement methods and errors are discussed.  Finally, we give additional information on periodic measurements, including security considerations. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekV. Raisanen¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamelG. Grotefeld¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameiA. Mortonddatej“Ïhfilenamegrfc3432eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125385hkeywordsÑccbrqconstant bit rateqperiodic samplingppoisson samplingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=Óâˆgrankinghcrfcd3432irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Network performance measurement with periodic streamsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyRThis document describes the RELATIONAL extension to the Sieve mail filtering language defined in RFC 3028.  This extension extends existing conditional tests in Sieve to allow relational operators.  In addition to testing their content, it also allows for testing of the number of entities in header and envelope fields. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson Research CenterdnamelW. Segmullerddatej—ÿhfilenamegrfc3431eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125384hkeywordsÇjsieve mailrfiltering languagekobsoletedByÅd5231epagesopublicationDate=Ò"Ëgrankinggcrfcd3431irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Sieve Extension: Relational TestsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7This memo defines a transport mapping for using the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) over TCP.  The transport mapping can be used with any version of SNMP.  This document extends the transport mappings defined in STD 62, RFC 3417.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoTU BraunschweigdnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej—ÿhfilenamegrfc3430eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125383hkeywordsÑdSNMPx"Simple Network Management ProtocolcTCPxTransmission Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate>"Ngrankingfcrfcd3430irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWSimple Network Management Protocol Over Transmission Control Protocol Transport MappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractynThis document describes the assignment of one of the reserved label values defined in RFC 3032 (MPLS label stack encoding) to the 'Operation and Maintenance (OAM) Alert Label' that is used by user-plane Multiprotocol Label Switching Architecture (MPLS) OAM functions for identification of MPLS OAM packets.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcNTTdnamegH. Ohtaddatej“Ïhfilenamegrfc3429eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125382hkeywordsÅureserved lavel valueskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=‹.πgrankingecrfcd3429irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉAssignment of the 'OAM Alert Label' for Multiprotocol Label Switching Architecture (MPLS) Operation and Maintenance (OAM) FunctionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyåInstant Messaging (IM) refers to the transfer of messages between users in near real-time.  These messages are usually, but not required to be, short.  IMs are often used in a conversational mode, that is, the transfer of messages back and forth is fast enough for participants to maintain an interactive conversation.  This document proposes the MESSAGE method, an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) that allows the transfer of Instant Messages.  Since the MESSAGE request is an extension to SIP, it inherits all the request routing and security features of that protocol.  MESSAGE requests carry the content in the form of MIME body parts.  MESSAGE requests do not themselves initiate a SIP dialog; under normal usage each Instant Message stands alone, much like pager messages.  MESSAGE requests may be sent in the context of a dialog initiated by some other SIP request. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamejC. Huitema¢kaffiliationuMicrosoft CorporationdnamehD. Gurleddatej—ÿhfilenamegrfc3428eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125381hkeywordsÖcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolbIMqInstant Messagingnmessage methodkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=Ò"igrankingdcrfcd3428irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexASession Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extension for Instant MessagingdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8591∂habstracty©This memo documents a process intended to apply architectural discipline to the future development of the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  There have been concerns with regards to new SIP proposals.  Specifically, that the addition of new SIP features can be damaging towards security and/or greatly increase the complexity of the protocol.  The Transport Area directors, along with the SIP and Session Initiation Proposal Investigation (SIPPING) working group chairs, have provided suggestions for SIP modifications and extensions.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationxBell Labs, Lucent CorporationdnameiA. Mankin¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradner¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegR. Mahy¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnameiD. Willis¢kaffiliationxipDialog / Uni Bremen TZIdnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationgMarconidnamehB. Rosenddatej—ÿhfilenamegrfc3427eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125380hkeywordsÅgsippingkobsoletedByÅd5727epagesopublicationDate>∑ﬂgrankingccrfcd3427irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex8Change Process for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd3968d3969µhabstractyVThis document suggests general architectural and policy questions that the IETF community has to address when working on new standards and protocols.  We note that this document contains questions to be addressed, as opposed to guidelines or architectural principles to be followed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemErik NordmarkgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnamehS. Floydddatej“Ïhfilenamegrfc3426eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125379hkeywordsÅuinternet architecturekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=⁄‰grankingbcrfcd3426irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex/General Architectural and Policy ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty
The IQUERY method of performing inverse DNS lookups, specified in RFC 1035, has not been generally implemented and has usually been operationally disabled where it has been implemented.  Both reflect a general view in the community that the concept was unwise and that the widely-used alternate approach of using pointer (PTR) queries and reverse-mapping records is preferable.  Consequently, this document deprecates the IQUERY operation, declaring it entirely obsolete.  This document updates RFC 1035. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamemErik Nordmarkdarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamekD. Lawrenceddatej“Îhfilenamegrfc3425eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125378hkeywordsÇkdns lookupsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=‹.Igrankingacrfcd3425irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqObsoleting IQUERYdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyòAs a result of the nature of Network Address Translation (NAT) Middleboxes, communicating endpoints that are separated by one or more NATs do not know how to refer to themselves using addresses that are valid in the addressing realms of their (current and future) peers.  Various proposals have been made for "UNilateral Self-Address Fixing (UNSAF)" processes.  These are processes whereby some originating endpoint attempts to determine or fix the address (and port) by which it is known to another endpoint - e.g., to be able to use address data in the protocol exchange, or to advertise a public address from which it will receive connections.  This document outlines the reasons for which these proposals can be considered at best as short term fixes to specific problems and the specific issues to be carefully evaluated before creating an UNSAF proposal.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamemErik NordmarkgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationcIABdnamecIABddatej“Îhfilenamegrfc3424eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125377hkeywordsÇcnatkmiddleboxeskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate=⁄„¥granking`crfcd3424irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex`IAB Considerations for UNilateral Self-Address Fixing (UNSAF) Across Network Address TranslationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyØThis document defines the Common Reliable Accounting for Network Element (CRANE) protocol that enables efficient and reliable delivery of any data, mainly accounting data from Network Elements to any systems, such as mediation systems and Business Support Systems (BSS)/ Operations Support Systems (OSS).  The protocol is developed to address the critical needs for exporting high volume of accounting data from NE's with efficient use of network, storage, and processing resources.  This document specifies the architecture of the protocol and the message format, which MUST be supported by all CRANE protocol implementations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrXACCT TechnologiesdnamehK. Zhang¢kaffiliationrXACCT TechnologiesdnamehE. Elkinddatej“Îhfilenamegrfc3423eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125376hkeywordsÜddatahdeliverygmessagefformatntemplate-basedmclient/serverkobsoletedByÄepages-opublicationDate=⁄„(granking_crfcd3423irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexaXACCT's Common Reliable Accounting for Network Element (CRANE) Protocol Specification Version 1.0dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty[This memo describes a method for forwarding media access control (MAC) frames over Multiple Access Protocol over Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (MAPOS), thus providing a way to unify MAPOS network environment and MAC-based Local Area Network (LAN) environment.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpNTT LaboratoriesdnamejO. Okamoto¢kaffiliationpNTT LaboratoriesdnamekM. Maruyama¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiT. Sajimaddatej“Îhfilenamegrfc3422eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125375hkeywordsÇitunnelingoethernet frameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ﬁr≥granking^crfcd3422irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexëForwarding Media Access Control (MAC) Frames over Multiple Access Protocol over Synchronous Optical Network/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (MAPOS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines two extensions (Select and Sort) for the Service Location Protocol (SLP).  These extensions allow a User Agent (UA) to request that the Uniform Resource Locator (URL) entries in a Service Reply (SrvRply) be limited to the specified number, or be sorted according to the specified sort key list.  Using these two extensions together can facilitate discovering the best match, such as finding a service that has the maximum speed or the minimum load.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamegW. Zhao¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttman¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelC. Bisdikian¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiW. Jeromeddatej“Îhfilenamegrfc3421eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125374hkeywordsàcSLPxService Location ProtocolbUAjuser agentcURLxUniform Resource Locatormservice replygsvrrplykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=‚≤Xgranking]crfcd3421irfcNumber]estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBSelect and Sort Extensions for the Service Location Protocol (SLP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty»This document registers the message/sipfrag Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) media type.  This type is similar to message/sip, but allows certain subsets of well formed Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) messages to be represented instead of requiring a complete SIP message.  In addition to end-to-end security uses, message/sipfrag is used with the REFER method to convey information about the status of a referenced request. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnameiR. Sparksddatej“Îhfilenamegrfc3420eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125373hkeywordsÇdmimex%multipurpose internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=’õÖgranking\crfcd3420irfcNumber\estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#Internet Media Type message/sipfragdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyçThis document introduces a Management Information Base (MIB) module that defines textual conventions to represent commonly used transport-layer addressing information.  The definitions are compatible with the concept of TAddress/TDomain pairs introduced by the Structure of Management Information version 2 (SMIv2) and support the Internet transport protocols over IPv4 and IPv6. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejM. Daniele¢kaffiliationoTU BraunschweigdnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej—ÿhfilenamegrfc3419eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgopsareadfullx3opsarea - Operations & Management Area Open Meetingdnamex)Operations & Management Area Open Meetingdtypebagbidjdoc-125372hkeywordsÇcmibxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>!„granking[crfcd3419irfcNumber[estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+Textual Conventions for Transport AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document defines managed objects which describe the behavior of a Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) entity.  This document obsoletes RFC 1907, Management Information Base for Version 2 of the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMPv2). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhnddatej—◊hfilenamegrfc3418eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125371hkeywordsÉjSNMPv2-MIBx,Simple Network Management Protocol Version 2xManagement Information BasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>!ÖgrankingZcrfcd3418irfcNumberZestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlexSManagement Information Base (MIB) for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractx¶This document defines the transport of Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) messages over various protocols.  This document obsoletes RFC 1906. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhnddatej—◊hfilenamegrfc3417eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125370hkeywordsÖiTRANS-MIBiTransportdSNMPx,Simple Network Management Protocol Version 2xManagement Information BasekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate> ›grankingYcrfcd3417irfcNumberYestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlexDTransport Mappings for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5590d4789∑habstractyThis document defines version 2 of the protocol operations for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP).  It defines the syntax and elements of procedure for sending, receiving, and processing SNMP PDUs.  This document obsoletes RFC 1905. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhnddatej—◊hfilenamegrfc3416eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125369hkeywordsÖgOPS-MIBdSNMPx,Simple Network Management Protocol Version 2xManagement Information BasejoperationskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>µgrankingXcrfcd3416irfcNumberXestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlexVVersion 2 of the Protocol Operations for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty¥This document describes the View-based Access Control Model (VACM) for use in the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) architecture.  It defines the Elements of Procedure for controlling access to management information.  This document also includes a Management Information Base (MIB) for remotely managing the configuration parameters for the View- based Access Control Model.  This document obsoletes RFC 2575. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnen¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhn¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. McCloghrieddatej—◊hfilenamegrfc3415eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125368hkeywordsÖiVACM-SNMPxView-based Access Control Modelx"Simple Network Management Protocolcmibxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate>+grankingWcrfcd3415irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlexXView-based Access Control Model (VACM) for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty≥This document describes the User-based Security Model (USM) for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) version 3 for use in the SNMP architecture.  It defines the Elements of Procedure for providing SNMP message level security.  This document also includes a Management Information Base (MIB) for remotely monitoring/managing the configuration parameters for this Security Model.  This document obsoletes RFC 2574. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamemU. Blumenthal¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnenddatej—÷hfilenamegrfc3414eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125367hkeywordsájUSM-SNMPV3xUser-based Security Modelx"Simple Network Management Protocolgmessageelevelcmibxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDate>ªgrankingVcrfcd3414irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlex`User-based Security Model (USM) for version 3 of the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5590∑habstractyThis document describes five types of Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) applications which make use of an SNMP engine as described in STD 62, RFC 3411.  The types of application described are Command Generators, Command Responders, Notification Originators, Notification Receivers, and Proxy Forwarders.  This document also defines Management Information Base (MIB) modules for specifying targets of management operations, for notification filtering, and for proxy forwarding.  This document obsoletes RFC 2573. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegD. Levi¢kaffiliationxSecure Computing CorporationdnamehP. Meyer¢kaffiliationgRetireddnamejB. Stewartddatej—÷hfilenamegrfc3413eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125366hkeywordsÜhSNMP-APPx"simple network management protocollapplicationseproxyjoperationsgcommandkobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDate>EgrankingUcrfcd3413irfcNumberUestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlex6Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty·This document describes the Message Processing and Dispatching for Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) messages within the SNMP architecture.  It defines the procedures for dispatching potentially multiple versions of SNMP messages to the proper SNMP Message Processing Models, and for dispatching PDUs to SNMP applications.  This document also describes one Message Processing Model - the SNMPv3 Message Processing Model.  This document obsoletes RFC 2572. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsSNMP Research, Inc.dnamegJ. Case¢kaffiliationrEnterasys NetworksdnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhn¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnenddatej—÷hfilenamegrfc3412eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125365hkeywordsÜhMPD-SNMPx"Message Processing and Dispatchingx"Simple Network Management ProtocoljprocessingfmodelshmultiplekobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate>—grankingTcrfcd3412irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlexTMessage Processing and Dispatching for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5590∑habstractyThis document describes an architecture for describing Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) Management Frameworks.  The architecture is designed to be modular to allow the evolution of the SNMP protocol standards over time.  The major portions of the architecture are an SNMP engine containing a Message Processing Subsystem, a Security Subsystem and an Access Control Subsystem, and possibly multiple SNMP applications which provide specific functional processing of management data.  This document obsoletes RFC 2571. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrEnterasys NetworksdnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhn¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiB. Wijnenddatej—’hfilenamegrfc3411eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125364hkeywordsÉiARCH-SNMPlArchitecturex"simple network management protocolkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate> grankingScrfcd3411irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber>etotaletitlex^An Architecture for Describing Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) Management FrameworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5590d5343∂habstractyÎThe purpose of this document is to provide an overview of the third version of the Internet-Standard Management Framework, termed the SNMP version 3 Framework (SNMPv3).  This Framework is derived from and builds upon both the original Internet-Standard Management Framework (SNMPv1) and the second Internet-Standard Management Framework (SNMPv2).  The architecture is designed to be modular to allow the evolution of the Framework over time.  The document explains why using SNMPv3 instead of SNMPv1 or SNMPv2 is strongly recommended.  The document also recommends that RFCs 1157, 1441, 1901, 1909 and 1910 be retired by moving them to Historic status.  This document obsoletes RFC 2570.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsSNMP Research, Inc.dnamegJ. Case¢kaffiliationxNetwork Associates LaboratoriesdnamehR. Mundy¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejD. Partain¢kaffiliationgRetireddnamejB. Stewartddatej—’hfilenamegrfc3410eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfsnmpv3dfullwsnmpv3 - SNMP Version 3dnamenSNMP Version 3dtypebwgbidjdoc-125363hkeywordsÑdsnmpfsimplehprotocolfsnmpv3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>}grankingRcrfcd3410irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTIntroduction and Applicability Statements for Internet-Standard Management FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty›This document describes lower layer guidelines for robust header compression (ROHC) and the requirements ROHC puts on lower layers.  The purpose of this document is to support the incorporation of robust header compression algorithms, as specified in the ROHC working group, into different systems such as those specified by Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP), 3GPP Project 2 (3GPP2), European Technical Standards Institute (ETSI), etc.  This document covers only lower layer guidelines for compression of RTP/UDP/IP and UDP/IP headers as specified in [RFC3095].  Both general guidelines and guidelines specific for cellular systems are discussed in this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejK. Svanbroddatej—‘hfilenamegrfc3409eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125362hkeywordsÇdrohcjalgorithmskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=˜∫õgrankingQcrfcd3409irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Lower Layer Guidelines for Robust RTP/UDP/IP Header CompressiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyëThis document defines an additional mode of the link-layer assisted RObust Header Compression (ROHC) profile, also known as the zero-byte profile, beyond the two defined in RFC 3242.  Zero-byte header compression exists in order to prevent the single-octet ROHC header from pushing a packet voice stream into the next higher fixed packet size for the radio.  It is usable in certain widely deployed older air interfaces.  This document adds the zero-byte operation for ROHC Bidirectional Reliable mode (R-mode) to the ones specified for Unidirectional (U-mode) and Bidirectional Optimistic (O-mode) modes of header compression in RFC 3242. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamefZ. Liu¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameeK. Leddatej—‘hfilenamegrfc3408eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125361hkeywordsÑdROHCxRObust Header Compressionlsingle-octetkpacket sizekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=˜∫,grankingPcrfcd3408irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÉZero-byte Support for Bidirectional Reliable Mode (R-mode) in Extended Link-Layer Assisted RObust Header Compression (ROHC) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÒThis document defines a set of Session Description Protocol (SDP) attributes that enables SDP to provide a minimal and backwards compatible capability declaration mechanism.  Such capability declarations can be used as input to a subsequent session negotiation, which is done by means outside the scope of this document.  This provides a simple and limited solution to the general capability negotiation problem being addressed by the next generation of SDP, also known as SDPng. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelF. Andreasenddatej”hfilenamegrfc3407eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125360hkeywordsÇeSDPngxSession Description ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate=ØQOgrankingOcrfcd3407irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@Session Description Protocol (SDP) Simple Capability DeclarationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty{This document lays out general definitions of and mechanisms for establishing Uniform Resource Names (URN) "namespaces".  The URN WG has defined a syntax for URNs in RFC 2141, as well as some proposed mechanisms for their resolution and use in Internet applications in RFC 3401 and RFC 3405.  The whole rests on the concept of individual "namespaces" within the URN structure.  Apart from proof-of-concept namespaces, the use of existing identifiers in URNs has been discussed in RFC 2288.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxThinking Cat EnterprisesdnameiL. Daigle¢kaffiliationjWebWeavingdnamelD. van Gulik¢kaffiliationkIPR SystemsdnamekR. Iannella¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelP. Faltstromddatej”hfilenamegrfc3406eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125359hkeywordsÉjnamespaceslapplicationsistructurekobsoletedByÅd8141epagesopublicationDate=ØP’grankingNcrfcd3406irfcNumberNestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Uniform Resource Names (URN) Namespace Definition MechanismsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty±This document is fifth in a series that is completely specified in "Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part One: The Comprehensive DDDS" (RFC 3401).  It is very important to note that it is impossible to read and understand any document in this series without reading the others.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamekM. Meallingddatej”hfilenamegrfc3405eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125358hkeywordsÅxuniform resource identifierskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate=ØP#grankingMcrfcd3405irfcNumberMestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberAetotaletitlexTDynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part Five: URI.ARPA Assignment ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8958∂habstracty"This document describes a specification for taking Uniform Resource Identifiers (URI) and locating an authoritative server for information about that URI.  The method used to locate that authoritative server is the Dynamic Delegation Discovery System.  This document is part of a series that is specified in "Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part One: The Comprehensive DDDS" (RFC 3401).  It is very important to note that it is impossible to read and understand any document in this series without reading the others. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamekM. Meallingddatej”hfilenamegrfc3404eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125357hkeywordsÖeNAPTRcDNSrdomain name systembRRoResource RecordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ØOgrankingLcrfcd3404irfcNumberLestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part Four: The Uniform Resource Identifiers (URI)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document describes a Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Database using the Domain Name System (DNS) as a distributed database of Rules.  The Keys are domain-names and the Rules are encoded using the Naming Authority Pointer (NAPTR) Resource Record (RR).  Since this document obsoletes RFC 2915, it is the official specification for the NAPTR DNS Resource Record.  It is also part of a series that is completely specified in "Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part One: The Comprehensive DDDS" (RFC 3401).  It is very important to note that it is impossible to read and understand any document in this series without reading the others. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamekM. Meallingddatej”hfilenamegrfc3403eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125356hkeywordsÖeNAPTRcDNSrdomain name systembRRoResource RecordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ØNÑgrankingKcrfcd3403irfcNumberKestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part Three: The Domain Name System (DNS) DatabasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty^This document describes the Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) algorithm for applying dynamically retrieved string transformation rules to an application-unique string.  Well-formed transformation rules will reflect the delegation of management of information associated with the string.  This document is also part of a series that is completely specified in "Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part One: The Comprehensive DDDS" (RFC 3401).  It is very important to note that it is impossible to read and understand any document in this series without reading the others. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamekM. Meallingddatej”hfilenamegrfc3402eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125355hkeywordsÖeNAPTRcDNSrdomain name systembRRoResource RecordkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ØMÍgrankingJcrfcd3402irfcNumberJestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part Two: The AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document specifies the exact documents that make up the complete Dynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS).  DDDS is an abstract algorithm for applying dynamically retrieved string transformation rules to an application-unique string.  This document along with RFC 3402, RFC 3403 and RFC 3404 obsolete RFC 2168 and RFC 2915, as well as updates RFC 2276.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamekM. Meallingddatej”hfilenamegrfc3401eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125354hkeywordsÉeNAPTRrdomain name systembRRkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ØMagrankingIcrfcd3401irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKDynamic Delegation Discovery System (DDDS) Part One: The Comprehensive DDDSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document describes a way to perform the mapping between two
