that appear multiple times in the same message.  Multiple
instances of the same option are generated when an option exceeds
255 octets in size (the maximum size of a single option) or when
an option needs to be split apart in order to take advantage of
DHCP option overloading.  When multiple instances of the same
option appear in the options, file and/or sname fields in a DHCP
packet, the contents of these options are concatenated together
to form a single option prior to processing.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationtApple Computer, Inc.dnamekS. Cheshireddatej“Áhfilenamegrfc3396eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125351hkeywordsÖfDHCPv4x#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocoleoctetfpacketdcodekobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate=ÃM"grankingDcrfcd3396irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIEncoding Long Options in the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv4)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis memo defines extensions to the Protocol Identifier Reference
document for the identification of application verb information. It does
not obsolete any portion of the Protocol Identifier Reference document.
In particular, it describes the algorithms required to identify protocol
operations (verbs) within the protocol encapsulations managed with the
Remote Network Monitoring MIB Version 2, RFC 2021.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Bierman¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehC. Bucci¢kaffiliationjHifn, Inc.dnamehR. Dietz¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehA. Warthddatej”hfilenamegrfc3395eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125350hkeywordsÉhRMON-MIBxRemote Network Monitoringxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ò–_grankingCcrfcd3395irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFRemote Network Monitoring MIB Protocol Identifier Reference ExtensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty~The purpose of this document is to make the AES Key Wrap algorithm
conveniently available to the Internet community.  The United States
of America has adopted AES as the new encryption standard.  The AES
Key Wrap algorithm will probably be adopted by the USA for
encryption of AES keys.  The authors took most of the text in this
document from the draft AES Key Wrap posted by NIST.fadNamesJeffrey I. Schillerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationwSoaring Hawk ConsultingdnameiJ. Schaad¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejR. Housleyddatej”hfilenamegrfc3394eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125349hkeywordsÅhsecuritykobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate=ö2”grankingBcrfcd3394irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) Key Wrap AlgorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†This memo refers to a metric for variation in delay of packets across
Internet paths. The metric is based on the difference in the One-Way-
Delay of selected packets. This difference in delay is called 'IP
Packet Delay variation.'
The metric is valid for measurements between two hosts both in the
case that they have synchronized clocks and in the case that they are
not synchronized. We discuss both in this draft.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqTelecomitalia LabdnamemC. Demichelis¢kaffiliationlEricsson IPIdnamekP. Chimentoddatej“‚hfilenamegrfc3393eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125348hkeywordsÖdIPPMvIP Performance Metricsqinternet protocoldipdvxIP Packet Delay VariationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=’õgrankingAcrfcd3393irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBIP Packet Delay Variation Metric for IP Performance Metrics (IPPM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÍCurrently BGP-4 requires that when a BGP speaker receives an OPEN
message with one or more unrecognized Optional Parameters, the
speaker must terminate BGP peering. This complicates introduction of
new capabilities in BGP.
This document defines new Optional Parameter, called Capabilities,
that is expected to facilitate introduction of new capabilities in
BGP by providing graceful capability advertisement without requiring
that BGP peering be terminated.
This document obsoletes RFC2842.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamejR. Chandra¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Scudderddatej“‚hfilenamegrfc3392eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125347hkeywordsÇcBGPwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÅd5492epagesopublicationDate=“π˝granking@crfcd3392irfcNumber@estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Capabilities Advertisement with BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty The Application/Vnd.pwg-multiplexed content-type, like the
Multipart/Related content-type, provides a mechanism for representing
objects that consist of multiple components.  An Application/Vnd.pwg-
multiplexed entity contains a sequence of chunks. Each chunk contains
a MIME message or a part of a MIME message. Each MIME message
represents a component of the compound object, just as a body part of
a Multipart/Related entity represents a component. With a
Multipart/Related entity, a body part and its reference in some other
body part may be separated by many octets. With an
Application/Vnd.pwg-multiplexed entity, a message and its reference
in some other message can be made quite close by chunking the message
containing the reference. For example, if a long message contains
references to images and the producer does not know of the need for
each image until it generates the reference, then
Application/Vnd.pwg-multiplexed allows the consumer to process the
reference to the image and the image before it consumes the entire
long message.  This ability is important in printing and scanning
applications. This document defines the Application/Vnd.pwg-
multiplexed content-type. It also provides examples of its use.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Herriotddatej—‘hfilenamegrfc3391eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125346hkeywordsÇx%multipurpose internet mail extensionsjmedia typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>s«granking?crfcd3391irfcNumber?estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex5The MIME Application/Vnd.pwg-multiplexed Content-TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document specifies an optional standard for TCP to increase the
permitted initial window from one segment to roughly 4K bytes,
replacing RFC 2414.  This document discusses the advantages and
disadvantages of the higher initial window.  The document includes
discussion of experiments and simulations showing that the higher
initial window does not lead to congestion collapse. Finally, the
document provides guidance on implementation issues.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlBBN/NASA GRCdnameiM. Allman¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamelC. Partridgeddatej”hfilenamegrfc3390eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125345hkeywordsÇcTCPxtransmission control protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=√#)granking>crfcd3390irfcNumber>estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIncreasing TCP's Initial WindowdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty)This document describes an RTP [2] payload format for transporting
comfort noise (CN).  The CN payload type is primarily for use with
audio codecs that do not support comfort noise as part of the codec
itself such as ITU-T Recommendations G.711 [3], G.726 [4], G.727
[5], G.728 [6], and G.722 [7].fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamegR. Zopfddatej”hfilenamegrfc3389eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125344hkeywordsácRTPxReal-time Transport ProtocolbCNmComfort NoisefcodecseaudiojmultimediakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ö1granking=crfcd3389irfcNumber=estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAReal-time Transport Protocol (RTP) Payload for Comfort Noise (CN)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyFThis document defines two SDP attributes: 'groupe' and 'mid'. They
allow to group together several 'm' lines for two different
purposes: for lip synchronization and for receiving media from a
single flow (several media streams), encoded in different formats
during a particular session, in different ports and host interfaces.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Eriksson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnameiJ. Holler¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej—‘hfilenamegrfc3388eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125343hkeywordsãcSDPxSession Description Protocolgformatsiattributedportdhostjinterfacescfidsflow identificationslip synchronizationblskobsoletedByÅd5888epagesopublicationDate>sagranking<crfcd3388irfcNumber<estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAGrouping of Media Lines in the Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractydThe guiding principles in the design of IP network management were
simplicity and no centralized control.  The best effort service
paradigm was a result of the original management principles and the
other way around.  New methods to distinguish the service given to
one set of packets or flows relative to another are well underway.
However, as IP networks evolve the management approach of the past
may not apply to the QoS-capable network envisioned by some for the
future.  We examine some of the areas of impact that QoS is likely
to have on management and look at some questions that remain to be
addressed.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamegM. Eder¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamejH. Chaskar¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamefS. Nagddatej”hfilenamegrfc3387eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125342hkeywordsÉqinternet protocolfpacktslfuel-servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ù¡ÿgranking;crfcd3387irfcNumber;estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexnConsiderations from the Service Management Research Group (SMRG) on Quality of Service (QoS) in the IP NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document is the deliverable out of the Network Hierarchy
and Survivability Techniques Design Team established within the
Traffic Engineering Working Group.  This team collected and
documented current and near term requirements for survivability
and hierarchy in service provider environments.  For clarity,
an expanded set of definitions is included.  The team
determined that there appears to be a need to define a small
set of interoperable survivability approaches in packet and
non-packet networks.  Suggested approaches include path-based
as well as one that repairs connections in proximity to the
network fault.  They operate primarily at a single network
layer.  For hierarchy, there did not appear to be a driving
near-term need for work on 'vertical hierarchy,' defined as
communication between network layers such as TDM/optical and
MPLS.  In particular, instead of direct exchange of signaling
and routing between vertical layers, some looser form of
coordination and communication, such as the specification of
hold-off timers, is a nearer term need.  For 'horizontal
hierarchy' in data networks, there are several pressing needs.
The requirement is to be able to set up many LSPs in a service
provider network with hierarchical IGP.  This is necessary to
support layer 2 and layer 3 VPN services that require edge-to-
edge signaling across a core network.
Please send comments to te-wg@ops.ietf.orgfadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefW. Lai¢kaffiliationhWorldcomdnamejD. McDysanddatej“‹hfilenamegrfc3386eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125341hkeywordsÜgservicehprovideropacket networksjprotectionkrestorationhrecoverykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÃLògranking:crfcd3386irfcNumber:estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Network Hierarchy and Multilayer SurvivabilitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]Cyclic redundancy check (CRC) codes [Peterson] are shortened
cyclic codes used for error detection. A number of CRC codes have
been adopted in standards: ATM, IEC, IEEE, CCITT, IBM-SDLC, and
more [Baicheva].  The most important expectation from this kind
of code is a very low probability for undetected errors.  The
probability of undetected errors in such codes has been, and
still is, subject to extensive studies that have included both
analytical models and simulations. Those codes have been used
extensively in communications and magnetic recording as they
demonstrate good 'burst error' detection capabilities, but are
also good at detecting several independent bit errors.  Hardware
implementations are very simple and well known; their simplicity
has made them popular with hardware developers for many years.
However, algorithms and software for effective implementations of
CRC are now also widely available [Williams].
The probability of undetected errors depends on the polynomial
selected to generate the code, the error distribution (error
model), and the data length.
In this memo, we attempt to give some estimates for the
probability of undetected errors to facilitate the selection of
an error detection code for iSCSI.
We will also attempt to compare CRCs with other checksum forms
(Fletcher, Adler, weighted checksums), as permitted by available
data.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelD. Sheinwald¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiJ. Satran¢kaffiliationgAgilentdnameiP. Thaler¢kaffiliationgAgilentdnamejV. Cavannaddatej“Ëhfilenamegrfc3385eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125340hkeywordsÅterror detection codekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=òœ>granking9crfcd3385irfcNumber9estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexoInternet Protocol Small Computer System Interface (iSCSI) Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC)/Checksum ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty/This document discusses the fundamental requirements for replication of
