It also specifies the requirements for DPV and DPD policy management.fadNamesJeffrey I. Schillerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationdBulldnameiD. Pinkas¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejR. Housleyddatej”5hfilenamegrfc3379eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125334hkeywordsÜcdpvxDelegated Path ValidationcdpdxDelegated Path Discoveryjpublic keylcertificateskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=âêgranking3crfcd3379irfcNumber3estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLDelegated Path Validation and Delegated Path Discovery Protocol RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyzThis document describes EtherIP, an early tunneling protocol, to
provide informational and historical context for the assignment of IP
protocol 97.  EtherIP tunnels Ethernet and IEEE 802.3 media access
control frames in IP datagrams so that non-IP traffic can traverse an
IP internet.  The protocol is very lightweight, and it does not
provide protection against infinite loops.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”5hfilenamegrfc3378eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125333hkeywordsÇqinternet protocoleip 97kobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate=ã°àgranking2crfcd3378irfcNumber2estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex2EtherIP: Tunneling Ethernet Frames in IP DatagramsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx|This document specifies the set of RFCs comprising LDAPv3, and addresses
the 'IESG Note' attached to RFCs 2251 through 2256.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuSun Microsystems Inc.dnameiJ. Hodges¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnameiR. Morganddatej”5hfilenamegrfc3377eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgldapbisdfullxldapbis - LDAP (v3) RevisiondnamerLDAP (v3) Revisiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125332hkeywordsÇdldapfldapv3kobsoletedByÅd4510epagesopublicationDate=âgranking1crfcd3377irfcNumber1estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCLightweight Directory Access Protocol (v3): Technical SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis document specifies Version 3 of the Internet Group Management
Protocol, IGMPv3.  IGMP is the protocol used by IPv4 systems to report
their IP multicast group memberships to neighboring multicast routers.
Version 3 of IGMP adds support for 'source filtering', that is, the
ability for a system to report interest in receiving packets *only* from
specific source addresses, or from *all but* specific source addresses,
sent to a particular multicast address.  That information may be used by
multicast routing protocols to avoid delivering multicast packets from
specific sources to networks where there are no interested receivers.
This document obsoletes RFC 2236.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoCereva NetworksdnamegB. Cain¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejS. Deering¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekI. Kouvelas¢kaffiliationtAT&T Labs - ResearchdnameiB. Fenner¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamenA. Thyagarajanddatej”	hfilenamegrfc3376eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdidmrdfullx%idmr - Inter-Domain Multicast RoutingdnamexInter-Domain Multicast Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125331hkeywordsÜdIGMPx"Internet Group Management ProtocolimulticastgroutingbIPqInternet ProtocolkobsoletedByÅd9776epages5opublicationDate=ØFÆgranking0crfcd3376irfcNumber0estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Internet Group Management Protocol, Version 3dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4604∂habstracty©This document describes high-level functional and interface
requirements for a client-server protocol for the registration and
management of Internet domain names in shared registries. Specific
technical requirements detailed for protocol design are not presented
here.  Instead, this document focuses on the basic functions and
interfaces required of a protocol to support multiple registry and
registrar operational models.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVerisign, Inc.dnamemS. Hollenbeckddatej”4hfilenamegrfc3375eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgprovregdfullx(provreg - Provisioning Registry ProtocoldnamexProvisioning Registry Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125330hkeywordsÉcrrpmclient serverldomain nameskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÜpXgranking/crfcd3375irfcNumber/estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex0Generic Registry-Registrar Protocol RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractylIn IP access networks that support host mobility, the routing paths
between the host and the network may change frequently and rapidly.
In some cases, the host may establish certain routing-related
services on subnets that are left behind when the host moves.
Examples of such services are AAA, header compression, and QoS. In
order for the host to obtain those services on the new subnet, the
host must explicitly re-establish the service by performing the
necessary signaling flows from scratch. In some cases, this process
would considerably slow the process of establishing the mobile host
on the new subnet. An alternative is to transfer information on the
existing state associated with these services, or context, to the
new subnet, a process called 'context transfer'. This document
discusses the desirability of context transfer for facilitating
seamless IP mobility.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Kempfddatej”4hfilenamegrfc3374eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgseamobydfullxWseamoby - Context Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host AlertingdnamexMContext Transfer, Handoff Candidate Discovery, and Dormant Mode Host Alertingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125329hkeywordsÖcaaacqosx'authentication authorization accountingrquality of servicerheader compressionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÜoËgranking.crfcd3374irfcNumber.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexcProblem Description: Reasons For Performing Context Transfers Between Nodes in an IP Access NetworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty–The IS-IS routing protocol (ISO 10589) requires reliable protocols at
the link layer for point-to-point links. As a result, it does not use
a three-way handshake when establishing adjacencies on point-to-point
media. This paper defines an backward-compatible extension to the
protocol that provides for a three-way handshake. It is fully
interoperable with systems that do not support the extension.
Additionally, the extension allows the robust operation of more than
256 point-to-point links on a single router.
This extension has been implemented by multiple router vendors;  this
paper is provided as information to the Internet community in order
to allow interoperable implementations to be built by other vendors.fadNamekRoss Callondarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationrTenet TechnologiesdnameiR. Salujaddatej”4hfilenamegrfc3373eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125328hkeywordsÇelinksihandshakekobsoletedByÅd5303epages	opublicationDate=ãPZgranking-crfcd3373irfcNumber-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeThree-Way Handshake for Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Point-to-Point AdjacenciesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThe popularity of gateways that interwork between the PSTN (Public
Switched Telephone Network) and SIP networks has motivated the
publication of a set ofcommon practices that can assure consistent
behavior across implementations.  This document taxonomizes the uses
of PSTN-SIP gateways, provides uses  cases, and identifies mechanisms
necessary for interworking.  The mechanisms detail how SIP provides
for both 'encapsulation' (carriage of PSTN signaling across a SIP
network) and 'translation' (protocol mapping).fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationtQwest CommunicationsdnameiA. Vemuri¢kaffiliationgNeuStardnamekJ. Petersonddatej”4hfilenamegrfc3372eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125327hkeywordsÇdpstnxpublic switch telephone networkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÜoRgranking,crfcd3372irfcNumber,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber?etotaletitlexMSession Initiation Protocol for Telephones (SIP-T): Context and ArchitecturesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx„This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB)
