Name Resolution Protocol.  Specifically it lays out the syntactic
components and how those components are used by URI Resolution to
find the available transports for a CNRP service.  Care should be
taken with several of the URI components because, while they may look
like components found in other URI schemes, they often do not act
like them.  The 'go' scheme has more in common with the location
independent 'news' scheme than any other URI scheme.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamekM. Meallingddatej—µhfilenamegrfc3368eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcnrpdfullx&cnrp - Common Name Resolution ProtocoldnamexCommon Name Resolution Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125323hkeywordsÑcURIxuniform resource identifierdCNRPxCommon Name Resolution ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=H‹pgranking(crfcd3368irfcNumber(estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex;The 'go' URI Scheme for the Common Name Resolution ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty-People often refer to things in the real world by a common name or
phrase, e.g., a trade name, company name, or a book title. These
names are sometimes easier for people to remember  and type than
URLs.  Furthermore, because of the limited syntax of URLs, companies
and individuals are finding that the ones that might be most
reasonable for their resources are being used elsewhere and so are
unavailable. For the purposes of this document, a 'common name' is a
word or a phrase, without imposed syntactic structure, that may be
associated with a resource.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamegN. Popp¢kaffiliationnVeriSign, Inc.dnamekM. Mealling¢kaffiliationmNetword, Inc.dnamejM. Moseleyddatej—µhfilenamegrfc3367eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdcnrpdfullx&cnrp - Common Name Resolution ProtocoldnamexCommon Name Resolution Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125322hkeywordsÑdCNRPxCommon Name Resolution Protocolxunique resource locatorssclient applicationskobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate=wÎ}granking'crfcd3367irfcNumber'estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex&Common Name Resolution Protocol (CNRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty∑This document provides advice to the designers of digital
communication equipment and link-layer protocols employing link
layer Automatic Repeat reQuest (ARQ) techniques.  This document
presumes that the designers wish to support Internet protocols, but
may be unfamiliar with the architecture of the Internet and with the
implications of their design choices on the performance and
efficiency of Internet traffic carried over their links.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AberdeendnamelG. Fairhurst¢kaffiliationqCisco Systems LtddnamegL. Woodddatej—¥hfilenamegrfc3366eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpilcdfullx7pilc - Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdnamex0Performance Implications of Link Characteristicsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125321hkeywordsÇftcp/ipksubnetworkskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=wàœgranking&crfcd3366irfcNumber&estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber>etotaletitlex?Advice to link designers on link Automatic Repeat reQuest (ARQ)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractxÛIt is the consensus of the IETF that IETF standard protocols MUST
make use of appropriate strong security mechanisms. This document
describes the history and rationale for this doctrine and establishes
this doctrine as a best current practice.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx%Massachusetts Institute of TechnologydnamekJ. Schillerddatej—¥hfilenamegrfc3365eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdsaagdfullx!saag - Security Area Open MeetingdnamexSecurity Area Open Meetingdtypebagbidjdoc-125320hkeywordsÅdietfkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=H‹pgranking%crfcd3365irfcNumber%estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber=etotaletitlexSStrong Security Requirements for Internet Engineering Task Force Standard ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty7The IETF has two different proposals on the table for how to do DNS
support for IPv6, and has thus far failed to reach a clear consensus
on which approach is better.  This note attempts to examine the pros
and cons of each approach, in the hope of clarifying the debate so
that we can reach closure and move on.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsBourgeois DilettantdnamejR. Austeinddatej—¥hfilenamegrfc3364eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125319hkeywordsÉoreverse mappingcrrspresource recordskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=d˙granking$crfcd3364irfcNumber$estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTTradeoffs in Domain Name System (DNS) Support for Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÕThis document clarifies and updates the standards status of RFCs that
define direct and reverse map of IPv6 addresses in DNS. This document
moves the A6 and Bit label specifications to experimental status.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamegR. Bush¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Durand¢kaffiliation`dnamegB. Fink¢kaffiliation`dnamenO. Gudmundsson¢kaffiliation`dnamegT. Hainddatej—¥hfilenamegrfc3363eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125318hkeywordsÇoreverse mappingllabel binarykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=d-granking#crfcd3363irfcNumber#estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYRepresenting Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) Addresses in the Domain Name System (DNS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6672∂habstractxüThis document describes the registration of the MIME sub-type
image/t38.  The encoding is defined by [T.38] and is intended
for use as an SDP media descriptor.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsonsddatej—¥hfilenamegrfc3362eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125317hkeywordsÖdT.38sReal-time FacsimiledMIMEx%Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensionsiimage/t38kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=uMgranking"crfcd3362irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexAReal-time Facsimile (T.38) - image/t38 MIME Sub-type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines a DHCP-for-IPv4 option that contains a list of
domain names or IPv4 addresses that can be mapped to one or more SIP
outbound proxy servers. This is one of the many methods that a SIP
client can use to obtain the addresses of such a local SIP server.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej—¥hfilenamegrfc3361eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125316hkeywordsÖdDHCPx#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocolcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolmproxy serverskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=kˇ≈granking!crfcd3361irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexhDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP-for-IPv4) Option for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstractyThis document is being written because there are a number of
firewalls in the Internet that inappropriately reset a TCP connection
upon receiving certain TCP SYN packets, in particular, packets with
flags set in the Reserved field of the TCP header.  In this document
we argue that this practice is not conformant with TCP standards, and
is an inappropriate overloading of the semantics of the TCP reset.
We also consider the longer-term consequences of this and similar
actions as obstacles to the evolution of the Internet infrastructure.fadNamerSteven M. Bellovindarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamehS. Floydddatej—≥hfilenamegrfc3360eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125315hkeywordsÑxtransmission control protocolcrstcbitjconnectionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=Z§granking crfcd3360irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber<etotaletitlex+Inappropriate TCP Resets Considered HarmfuldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyjThis draft describes implementation codepoints within
IS-IS [ISO90 , Cal90a , Cal90b ] used today by several ISPs for
routing within their clouds.  IS-IS is an interior gateway routing
protocol developed originally by OSI and used with IP extensions as
IGP. This draft summarizes all TLV codepoints that are being used by
the protocol and its pending extensions.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeXebeodnamemT. Przygiendaddatej—≥hfilenamegrfc3359eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125314hkeywordsÖeis-iscigpcosixcomplete sequence numberlpartial datakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=d£grankingcrfcd3359irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Reserved Type, Length and Value (TLV) Codepoints in Intermediate System to Intermediate SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyIThis draft describes an optional extension to IS-IS protocol, used
today by several ISPs for routing within their clouds.  IS-IS is an
interior gateway routing protocol developed originally by OSI and
used with IP extensions as IGP. IS-IS originally does not provide
CSNP and PSNP checksums, relying on the underlying layers to verify
the integrity of information provided.  Experience with the protocol
shows that this precondition does not always hold and scenarios
can be imagined that impact protocol functionality.  This document
introduces a new optional TLV providing checksums.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationeXebeodnamemT. Przygiendaddatej—≥hfilenamegrfc3358eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125313hkeywordsÖdtypeflengthevaluexcomplete sequence numberlpartial datakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=d5grankingcrfcd3358irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGOptional Checksums in Intermediate System to Intermediate System (ISIS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty¿The Internet exhibits certain specific types of behavior (e.g.,
bursty packet loss) that can affect the performance seen by the users
as well as the operators.  The loss pattern or loss distribution is a
key parameter that determines the performance observed by the users
for certain real-time applications such as packet voice and video.
For the same loss rate, two different loss distributions could
potentially produce widely different perceptions of performance.
Using the base loss metric defined in RFC 2680, this document defines
two derived metrics 'loss distance' and 'loss period', and the
associated statistics that together capture loss patterns experienced
by packet streams on the Internet.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnameiR. Koodli¢kaffiliationhAxiowavednamelR. Ravikanthddatej—≥hfilenamegrfc3357eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdippmdfullx!ippm - IP Performance MeasurementdnamexIP Performance Measurementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125312hkeywordsÑgpacketsevoiceevideofstreamkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=\=πgrankingcrfcd3357irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex#One-way Loss Pattern Sample MetricsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyïThis document provides guidance to aid in the understanding of
collaboration on standards development between the International
Telecommunication Union -- Telecommunication Standardization Sector
(ITU-T) and the Internet Society (ISOC) / Internet Engineering Task
Force (IETF).  It is an update of and obsoletes [RFC 2436].  The
updates reflect changes in the IETF and ITU-T since RFC 2436 was
written.  The bulk of this document is common text with ITU-T
Supplement 3 to the ITU-T A-Series Recommendations.
This was approved by TUT-T TSAG on 30 November 2001 as a supplement
to the ITU-T A-Series of recommendations (will be numbered as A-
Series Supplement 3)fadNametHarald T. Alvestranddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqBell LaboratoriesdnamejG. Fishman¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej—≥hfilenamegrfc3356eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125311hkeywordsÖhinternetgsocietykengineeringdtaskeforcekobsoletedByÅd6756epagesopublicationDate=\>∞grankingcrfcd3356irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexéInternet Engineering Task Force and International Telecommunication Union - Telecommunications Standardization Sector Collaboration GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe Layer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) [RFC2661] provides a standard
method for transporting the link layer of PPP [RFC1661] between a
dial-up server and a Network Access Server, using a network
connection in lieu of a physical point to point connection.  This
document describes the use of an ATM network for the underlying
network connection. ATM User-Network Interface (UNI) Signalling
Specification Version 4.0 [SIG40] or Version 3.1 [SIG31] with ATM
Adaptation Layer 5 (AAL5) [ITU93] are supported as interfaces to the
ATM network.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamehA. Singh¢kaffiliationhParadynednameiR. Turner¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamefR. Tio¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamehS. Nanjiddatej—≥hfilenamegrfc3355eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125310hkeywordsádL2TPxLayer Two Tunneling Protocoldlinkndial-up servercATMxasynchronous transfer modepadaptation layerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=wÍÈgrankingcrfcd3355irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGLayer Two Tunnelling Protocol (L2TP) Over ATM Adaptation Layer 5 (AAL5)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyVVersion 2 of the Internet Open Trading Protocol (IOTP) will extend
the interoperable framework for Internet commerce capabilities of
Version 1 while replacing the XML messaging and digital signature
part of IOTP v1 with standards based mechanisms. Additional features
are listed and divided into those the will, may, and will not be
included.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameoD. Eastlake 3rdddatej—≤hfilenamegrfc3354eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetradedfullx&trade - Internet Open Trading ProtocoldnamexInternet Open Trading Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125309hkeywordsâgpaymentiecommercehmerchanthcustomerhdeliveryisignatureimessaginghcommercedsalekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=Z££grankingcrfcd3354irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Internet Open Trading Protocol Version 2 RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyËThis document offers a framework for IP multicast deployment in an
MPLS environment.  Issues arising when MPLS techniques are applied to
IP multicast are overviewed.  The pros and cons of existing IP
multicast routing protocols in the context of MPLS are described and
the relation to the different trigger methods and label distribution
modes are discussed.  The consequences of various layer 2 (L2)
technologies are listed.  Both point-to-point and multi-access
networks are considered.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamegD. Ooms¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamehB. Sales¢kaffiliationlColt TelecomdnameiW. Livens¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejA. Acharya¢kaffiliationgUlticomdnamekF. Griffoul¢kaffiliationiBell LabsdnameiF. Ansariddatej—≤hfilenamegrfc3353eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125308hkeywordsÉrinrternet protocolbl2xmulticast routing protocolnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=kˇIgrankingcrfcd3353irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOOverview of IP Multicast in a Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) EnvironmentdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyPThe Connection-less Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (CLDAP)
technical specification, RFC 1798, was published in 1995 as a Proposed
Standard.  This document discusses the reasons why the CLDAP technical
specification has not been furthered on the Standard Track.  This
document recommends that RFC 1798 be moved to Historic status.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej“•hfilenamegrfc3352eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125307hkeywordsÜeCLDAPlPresentationgAddresskApplicationfEntityeTitlekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>fä¨grankingcrfcd3352irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexPConnection-less Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (CLDAP) to Historic StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document aims to present a set of SIP user requirements that
support communications for deaf, hard of hearing and speech-impaired
individuals. These user requirements address the current difficulties
of deaf, hard of hearing and speech-impaired individuals in using
communications facilities, while acknowledging the multi-functional
potential of SIP-based communications.
A number of issues related to these user requirements are further
raised in this document.
Also included are some real world scenarios and some technical
requirements to show the robustness of these requirements on a
concept-level.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhMillparkdnamekN. Charlton¢kaffiliationnKoru SolutionsdnameiM. Gasson¢kaffiliationdRNIDdnameiG. Gybels¢kaffiliationdRNIDdnamejM. Spanner¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekA. van Wijkddatej—≤hfilenamegrfc3351eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125306hkeywordsÉmrelay servicestranscoding serviceitextphonekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=\>Pgrankingcrfcd3351irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexUser Requirements for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) in Support of Deaf, Hard of Hearing and Speech-impaired IndividualsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∑habstracty0As a part of their deliverables, working groups of the IETF may
develop BEEP profiles.  During the development process, it is
desirable to assign a transient identifier to each profile.  If the
profile is subsequently published as an RFC, then a permanent
identifier is subsequently assigned by the IANA.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxDover Beach Consulting, Inc.dnamegM. Roseddatej“Ghfilenamegrfc3349eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125305hkeywordsÅdbeepkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=I´ögrankingcrfcd3349irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber;etotaletitlexzA Transient Prefix for Identifying Profiles under Development by the Working Groups of the Internet Engineering Task ForcedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxπThe CHILDREN extension provides a mechanism for a client to
efficiently determine if a particular mailbox has children, without
issuing a LIST '' * or a LIST '' % for each mailbox name.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiM. Gahrns¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehR. Chengddatej“Ghfilenamegrfc3348eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125304hkeywordsÅhchildrenkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate==Ïgrankingcrfcd3348irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDThe Internet Message Action Protocol (IMAP4) Child Mailbox ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThe IP Storage Working group is chartered with developing
comprehensive technology to transport block storage data over IP
protocols.  This effort includes a protocol to transport the Small
Computer Systems Interface (SCSI) protocol over the Internet
(iSCSI).  The initial version of the iSCSI protocol will define a
mapping of SCSI transport protocol over TCP/IP so that SCSI storage
controllers (principally disk and tape arrays and libraries) can be
attached to IP networks, notably Gigabit Ethernet (GbE) and 10
Gigabit Ethernet (10 GbE).fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxHewlett-Packard CorporationdnamejM. Krueger¢kaffiliationxHewlett-Packard CorporationdnamejR. Haagensddatej“Ghfilenamegrfc3347eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcipsdfullpips - IP StoragednamejIP Storagedtypebwgbidjdoc-125303hkeywordsÜeiSCSIx Small Computer Systems InterfacecTCPxTransmission Control Protocolgstoragemfibre channelkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=?7~grankingcrfcd3347irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexjSmall Computer Systems Interface protocol over the Internet (iSCSI) Requirements and Design ConsiderationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document describes the applicability of Multiprotocol Label
Switching (MPLS) to traffic engineering in IP networks.  Special
considerations for deployment of MPLS for traffic engineering in
operational contexts are discussed and the limitations of the MPLS
approach to traffic engineering are highlighted.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationgPD NetsdnamehJ. Boyle¢kaffiliationuAOL Time Warner, Inc.dnamegV. Gill¢kaffiliationkRoutingLoopdnameiA. Hannan¢kaffiliationoGlobal CrossingdnameiD. Cooper¢kaffiliationnMovaz NetworksdnamejD. Awduche¢kaffiliationhWorldcomdnamelB. Christian¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamehW.S. Laiddatej—≤hfilenamegrfc3346eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125302hkeywordsÇxmultiprotocol label switchingbtekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=\=Ygrankingcrfcd3346irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Applicability Statement for Traffic Engineering with MPLSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÀThe Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) [1] is an inter-Autonomous System
