estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Dual Stack Hosts Using "Bump-in-the-API" (BIA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUPPP over ATM Adaptation Layer 2 defines the encapsulation that allows a PPP session to be transported over an ATM virtual circuit using the AAL2 adaptation layer. This document defines a set of class extensions to PPP over AAL2 that implement equivalent functionality to Multi Class Multi Link PPP over a single ATM virtual circuit. Instead of using Multi Link PPP as the basis for fragmentation functionality, this document uses the functionality of the Segmentation and Reassembly Service Specific Convergence Sublayer that is already required as the basic encapsulation format of PPP over AAL2.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekB. Thompson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Koren¢kaffiliationoSeaway NetworksdnameiB. Buffamddatej—‘hfilenamegrfc3337eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125294hkeywordsÜcPPPwpoint-to-point protocolcATMx-Asynchronous Transfer Mode Adaptation Layer 2daal2mencapsulationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=È¡Ägranking	crfcd3337irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexKClass Extensions for PPP over Asynchronous Transfer Mode Adaptation Layer 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÖThe Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) [1] provides a standard method for
transporting multi-protocol datagrams over point-to-point links.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekB. Thompson¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Koren¢kaffiliationoSeaway NetworksdnameiB. Buffamddatej—”hfilenamegrfc3336eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125293hkeywordsàcPPPnpoint-to-pointhprotocolcatmxAsynchronous Transfer Modedaal2hdatagramgpacketskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=˙Ügrankingcrfcd3336irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=PPP Over Asynchronous Transfer Mode Adaptation Layer 2 (AAL2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty]This document describes how to exchange structured business data
securely using SMTP transport for Electronic Data Interchange,
(EDI - either the American Standards Committee X12 or UN/EDIFACT,
Electronic Data Interchange for Administration, Commerce and
Transport), XML or other data used for business to business data
interchange. The data is packaged using standard MIME
content-types. Authentication and privacy are obtained by using
Cryptographic Message Syntax (S/MIME) or OpenPGP security body
parts. Authenticated acknowledgements make use of
multipart/signed replies to the original SMTP message.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpCyclone CommercednamejT. Harding¢kaffiliationnDrummond GroupdnamekR. Drummond¢kaffiliationmGartner GroupdnamegC. Shihddatej”4hfilenamegrfc3335eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfediintdfullx9ediint - Electronic Data Interchange-Internet Integrationdnamex0Electronic Data Interchange-Internet Integrationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125292hkeywordsÑdMIMEx%multipurpose internet mail extensionscedix0Electronic Data Interchange-Internet IntegrationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÜnŒgrankingcrfcd3335irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJMIME-based Secure Peer-to-Peer Business Data Interchange over the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document describes policy-based accounting which is an approach
to provide flexibility to accounting architectures. Accounting
policies describe the configuration of an accounting architecture in
a standardized way. They are used to instrument the accounting
architecture and can be exchanged between AAA entities in order to
share configuration information.
This document describes building blocks and message sequences for
policy-based accounting in the generic AAA architecture [RFC2903].
Examples are given for the usage of accounting policies in different
scenarios. Furthermore it is shown how accounting components can be
integrated into the AAA authorization framework [RFC2904]. This
document  does not propose a language for the description of
accounting policies. It is rather assumed that a suitable policy
language can be chosen from existing or upcoming standards.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamehT. Zseby¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnameiS. Zander¢kaffiliationpFraunhofer FOKUSdnamehC. Carleddatej”hfilenamegrfc3334eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125291hkeywordsákmeasurementhmeteringsmeter configurationlqos auditingcaaalarchitecturewinter-domain accountingkobsoletedByÄepages,opublicationDate=¨egrankingcrfcd3334irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlewPolicy-Based AccountingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyYThis Internet Draft defines a protocol for supporting the transport of
any SS7 MTP3-User signalling (e.g., ISUP and SCCP messages) over IP
using the services of the Stream Control Transmission Protocol.  Also,
provision is made for protocol elements that enable a seamless
operation of the MTP3-User peers in the SS7 and IP domains. This
protocol would be used between a Signalling Gateway (SG) and a Media
Gateway Controller (MGC) or IP-resident Database.  It is assumed that
the SG receives SS7 signalling over a standard SS7 interface using the
SS7 Message Transfer Part (MTP) to provide transport.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationuSignatus TechnologiesdnamemG. Sidebottom¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamelK. Morneault¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamepJ. Pastor-Balbasddatej”4hfilenamegrfc3332eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125290hkeywordsàdisupdsccpdsctpx#stream control tranmission protocolcmgcvmedia gateway protocolbstrsignalling gatewaykobsoletedByÅd4666epagesxopublicationDate=ã°grankingcrfcd3332irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVSignaling System 7 (SS7) Message Transfer Part 3 (MTP3) - User Adaptation Layer (M3UA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty This Internet Draft defines a protocol for backhauling of SS7 MTP2
User signaling messages over IP using the Stream Control
Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  This protocol would be used between a
Signaling Gateway (SG) and Media Gateway Controller (MGC).  It is
assumed that the SG receives SS7 signaling over a standard SS7
interface using the SS7 Message Transfer Part (MTP) to provide
transport.  The Signaling Gateway would act as a Signaling Link
Terminal.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamelK. Morneault¢kaffiliationgNetRakednamehR. Dantu¢kaffiliationuSignatus TechnologiesdnamemG. Sidebottom¢kaffiliationgOpenSS7dnamekB. Bidulock¢kaffiliationfLucentdnamehJ. Heitzddatej”3hfilenamegrfc3331eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125289hkeywordsÜdsctpx$stream control transmission protocolbsgqsignaling gatewayxmedia gateway controllercmgckobsoletedByÄepages^opublicationDate=ã†jgrankingcrfcd3331irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexOSignaling System 7 (SS7) Message Transfer Part 2 (MTP2) - User Adaptation LayerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyGThis document describes the global and other specialized IPv4 address
blocks that have been assigned by the IANA. It does not address IPv4
address space assigned to operators and users through the Regional
Internet Registries.  It also does not address allocations or
assignments of IPv6 addresses or autonomous system numbers.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdIANAdnamedIANAddatej”3hfilenamegrfc3330eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125288hkeywordsÇqinternet protocolqspace assignmentskobsoletedByÅd5735epagesopublicationDate=õvîgrankingcrfcd3330irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexSpecial-Use IPv4 AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document defines new functionality for negotiating the security
mechanisms used between a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) user
agent and its next-hop SIP entity.  This new functionality
supplements the existing methods of choosing security mechanisms
between SIP  entities.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekV. Torvinen¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehA. Niemi¢kaffiliationeNokiadnameiT. Haukkaddatej“&hfilenamegrfc3329eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125287hkeywordsÑcSIPxSession Initiation ProtocolbUAjuser agentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>hgrankingcrfcd3329irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFSecurity Mechanism Agreement for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8996∂habstractyÊThe REGISTER function is used in a SIP system primarily to associate
a temporary contact address with an address-of-record.  This contact
is generally in the form of a URI, such as Contact:
<sip:alice@pc33.atlanta.com> and is generally dynamic and associated
with the IP address or hostname of the SIP UA.  The problem is that
network topology may have one or more SIP proxies between the UA and
the registrar, such that any request travelling from the user's home
network to the registered UA must traverse these proxies.  The
REGISTER method does not give us a mechanism to discover and record
this sequence of proxies in the registrar for future use.  This
document defines an extension header field, 'Path' which provides
such a mechanismfadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpdynamicsoft Inc.dnameiD. Willis¢kaffiliationfSwitchdnamelB. Hoeneisenddatej—”hfilenamegrfc3327eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125286hkeywordsâcSIPxSession Initiation Protocold3gpphregistergcontactdpathiregistrarjuser agentbuakobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=˙Ö=grankingˇcrfcd3327irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex^Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extension Header Field for Registering Non-Adjacent ContactsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5626∂habstractyAThe REGISTER function is used in a Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) system primarily to associate a temporary contact address with an address-of-record.  This contact is generally in the form of a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI), such as Contact: <sip:alice@pc33.atlanta.com> and is generally dynamic and associated with the IP address or hostname of the SIP User Agent (UA).  The problem is that network topology may have one or more SIP proxies between the UA and the registrar, such that any request traveling from the user's home network to the registered UA must traverse these proxies.  The REGISTER method does not give us a mechanism to discover and record this sequence of proxies in the registrar for future use.  This document defines an extension header field, "Path" which provides such a mechanism. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegD. Oran¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarilloddatej—”hfilenamegrfc3326eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125285hkeywordsÑcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolx,heterogeneous error response forking problemeherfpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=Ò#ïgranking˛crfcd3326irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexAThe Reason Header Field for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd8606d9366∂habstractyThis document describes private extensions to SIP that enable a
network of trusted SIP servers to assert the identity of
authenticated users, and the application of existing privacy
mechanisms to the identity problem.  The use of these extensions is
only applicable inside an administrative domain with previously
agreed-upon policies for generation, transport and usage of such
information.  This document does NOT offer a general privacy or
identity model suitable for use between different trust domains, or
use in the Internet at large.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekC. Jennings¢kaffiliationmNeuStar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Watsonddatej“‹hfilenamegrfc3325eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125284hkeywordsÅltrust domainkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÓÑ∆granking˝crfcd3325irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexiPrivate Extensions to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) for Asserted Identity within Trusted NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5876d8217∂habstracty–There is no requirement for identities asserted by a UA in a SIP
message to be anything other than the user's desired alias.
A Network Asserted Identity is an identity initially derived by a SIP
network intermediary as a result of an authentication process.  This
draft describes short term requirements for the exchange of Network
Asserted Identities within networks of securely interconnected
trusted nodes and to User Agents securely connected to such networks.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Watsonddatej“‹hfilenamegrfc3324eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsippingdfullx3sipping - Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdnamex)Session Initiation Proposal Investigationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125283hkeywordsÑxsession initiation protocolcsipbuajuser agentkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÓÑgranking¸crfcd3324irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Short Term Requirements for Network Asserted IdentitydtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty◊This document defines new mechanisms for the Session Initiation
Protocol (SIP) in support of privacy.  Specifically, guidelines are
provided for the creation of messages that do not divulge personal
identity information.  A new 'privacy service' logical role for
intermediaries is defined to answer some privacy requirements that
user agents cannot satisfy themselves.  Finally, means are presented
by which a user can request particular functions from a privacy
service.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Petersonddatej“Íhfilenamegrfc3323eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125282hkeywordsÉcSIPxSession Initiation Protocoloprivacy servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ÓÉñgranking˚crfcd3323irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=A Privacy Mechanism for the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÃIn wireless environments and especially in cellular systems, e.g. GSM
(Global System for Mobile communications) and UMTS (Universal Mobile
Telecommunications System), there is a need to maximize the transport
efficiency for data over the radio interface. With the introduction
of SIP/SDP (Session Initiation Protocol/Session Description Protocol)
to cellular devices, compression of the signaling messages should be
considered in order to improve both service availability and quality,
mainly by reducing the user idle time, e.g. at call setup. The
purpose of this document is to outline requirements and motivations
for development of a scheme for compression and decompression of
messages from signaling protocols.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehH. Hannuddatej“%hfilenamegrfc3322eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125281hkeywordsàcsipxsession initiation protocolhwirelesshcellularcsdpxsession description protocolgSigCompuSignaling CompressionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>Ógranking˙crfcd3322irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Signaling Compression (SigComp) Requirements & AssumptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyûThis document describes how to implement certain mechanisms in
SigComp (Signaling Compression), RFC XXX, which can significantly
improve the compression efficiency compared to using simple per-
message compression.
SigComp uses a UDVM (Universal Decompressor Virtual Machine) for
decompression, and the mechanisms described in this document are
possible to implement using the UDVM instructions defined in RFC XXX.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehH. Hannu¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Christoffersson¢kaffiliationuTexas Tech UniversitydnamekS. Forsgren¢kaffiliationuTexas Tech UniversitydnamekK.-C. Leung¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamefZ. Liu¢kaffiliationrSiemens/Roke ManordnamehR. Priceddatej“-hfilenamegrfc3321eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125280hkeywordsÜcsipxsession initiation protocoldudvmx&universal decompressor virtual machinegSigCompuSignaling CompressionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>ugranking˘crfcd3321irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Signaling Compression (SigComp) - Extended OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4896∂habstracty	This document defines SigComp, a solution for compressing messages
generated by application protocols such as SIP [RFC-2543] and RTSP
[RFC-2326]. The architecture and pre-requisites of SigComp are
outlined, along with the format of the SigComp message.
Decompression functionality for SigComp is provided by a 'Universal
Decompressor Virtual Machine' optimized for the task of running
decompression algorithms. The UDVM can be configured to understand
the output of many well-known compressors such as DEFLATE [RFC-1951].fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationrSiemens/Roke ManordnamehR. Price¢kaffiliationnTZI/Uni BremendnamejC. Bormann¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamerJ. Christoffersson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehH. Hannu¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamefZ. Liu¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej“-hfilenamegrfc3320eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125279hkeywordsÜcsipxsession initiation protocoldudvmx&universal decompressor virtual machinegSigCompuSignaling CompressionkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDate>Ígranking¯crfcd3320irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSignaling Compression (SigComp)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4896∂habstractyThis document defines a DHCPv6 option that contains a list of domain
names or IPv6 addresses that can be mapped to one or more SIP
outbound proxy servers. This is one of the many methods that a SIP
client can use to obtain the addresses of such a local SIP server.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsColumbia UniversitydnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegB. Volzddatej“Hhfilenamegrfc3319eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125278hkeywordsÖfDHCPv6x#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocolcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolvoutbound proxy serverskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?(VÊgranking˜crfcd3319irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbDynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCPv6) Options for Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty\This document defines a set of PRovisioning Classes (PRCs) and textual conventions that are common to all clients that provision policy using Common Open Policy Service (COPS) protocol for Provisioning.

Structure of Policy Provisioning Information (SPPI) describes a structure for specifying policy information that can then be transmitted to a network device for the purpose of configuring policy at that device. The model underlying this structure is one of well-defined (PRCs) and instances of these classes (PRIs) residing in a virtual information store called the Policy Information Base (PIB).

One way to provision policy is by means of the (COPS) protocol with the extensions for provisioning. This protocol supports multiple clients, each of which may provision policy for a specific policy domain such as QoS, virtual private networks, or security.

As described in COPS usage for Policy Provisioning (COPS-PR), each client supports a non-overlapping and independent set of PIB modules. However, some PRovisioning Classes are common to all subject-categories (client-types) and need to be present in each.fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationx,structure of policy provisioning informationdnameiR. Sahita¢kaffiliationdsppidnamegS. Hahn¢kaffiliationdprcsdnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationtprovisioning classesdnamemK. McCloghrieddatej õhfilenamegrfc3318eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrapdfullx"rap - Resource Allocation ProtocoldnamexResource Allocation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125277hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesFopublicationDate>i$^grankingˆcrfcd3318irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Framework Policy Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÚThis document describes a Policy Information Base (PIB) for a device
implementing the Differentiated Services Architecture.  The
provisioning classes defined here provide policy control of
resources implementing the Differentiated Services Architecture.
These provisioning classes can be used with other none
Differentiated Services provisioning classes (defined in other PIBs)
to provide for a comprehensive policy controlled mapping of service
requirement to device resource capability and usage.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationeInteldnameiR. Sahita¢kaffiliationeInteldnamegS. Hahn¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrieddatej“•hfilenamegrfc3317eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125276hkeywordsÇcpibx$differentiated services architecturekobsoletedByÄepages`opublicationDate>i#grankingıcrfcd3317irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDifferentiated Services Quality of Service Policy Information BasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyˇAs the deployment of second and third generation cellular networks
progresses, a large number of cellular hosts are being connected to
the Internet. Standardization organizations are making IPv6
mandatory in their specifications. However, the concept of IPv6
covers many aspects and numerous specifications. In addition, the
characteristics of cellular links in terms of bandwidth, cost and
delay put special requirements on how IPv6 is used. This document
considers IPv6 for cellular hosts that attach to the General Packet
Radio Service (GPRS), or Universal Mobile Telecommunications System
(UMTS) networks. The document lists basic components of IPv6
functionality and discusses some issues relating to the use of these
components when operating in these networks.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekG. Kuijpers¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejH. Soliman¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Wiljakkaddatej—Ñhfilenamegrfc3316eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125275hkeywordsÇelinksibandwidthkobsoletedByÅd7066epagesopublicationDate>±søgrankingÙcrfcd3316irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexVInternet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) for Some Second and Third Generation Cellular HostsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty’The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCP) enables
DHCP servers to pass configuration parameters such as IPv6 network
addresses to IPv6 nodes.  It offers the capability of automatic
allocation of reusable network addresses and additional configuration
flexibility.  This protocol is a stateful counterpart to 'IPv6
Stateless Address Autoconfiguration' (RFC2462), and can be used
separately or concurrently with the latter to obtain configuration
parametersfadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehR. Droms¢kaffiliationoHewlett PackarddnamehJ. Bound¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamegB. Volz¢kaffiliationgNominumdnamehT. Lemon¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnameiM. Carneyddatej“Ghfilenamegrfc3315eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125274hkeywordsÖfDHCPv6x,Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6qinternet protocoljparametersiaddresseskobsoletedByÅd8415epageseopublicationDate?(PügrankingÛcrfcd3315irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6)dtypecrfciupdatedByâd6644d6221d4361d6422d7083d5494d7283d7550d7227∂habstractyiThis document contains recommendations from the Internet
Engineering Task Force (IETF) IPv6 Working Group to the Third
Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) community regarding the use
of IPv6 in the 3GPP standards. Specifically, this document
recommends that the 3GPP:
1. Specify that multiple prefixes may be assigned to each
primary PDP context,
2. Require that a given prefix must not be assigned to more
than one primary PDP context, and
3. Allow 3GPP nodes to use multiple identifiers within those
prefixes, including randomly generated identifiers.
The IPv6 Working Group supports the use of IPv6 within 3GPP and
offers these recommendations in a spirit of open cooperation
between the IPv6 Working Group and the 3GPP community.  Since the
original publication of this document as an Internet-Draft, the
3GPP has adopted the primary recommendations of this document.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjWind RiverdnamelM. Wassermanddatej”3hfilenamegrfc3314eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125273hkeywordsÅqinternet protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ãüÍgrankingÚcrfcd3314irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexQRecommendations for IPv6 in Third Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty#This document describes the need for QoS and media authorization and
defines a SIP extension that can be used to integrate QoS admission
control with call signaling and help guard against denial of service
attacks. The use of this extension is only applicable in
administrative domains, or among federations of administrative
domains with previously agreed-upon policies, where both the SIP
proxy authorizing the QoS, and the policy control of the underlying
network providing the QoS belong to that administrative domain or
federation of domains.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekW. Marshallddatej“-hfilenamegrfc3313eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125272hkeywordsÇcqosrquality of servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate>0*grankingÒcrfcd3313irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLPrivate Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) Extensions for Media AuthorizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty?This document defines a generic framework for preconditions which is
extensible through IANA registration. This document also discusses
how network quality of service can be made a precondition to
establishment of sessions initiated by the Session Initiation
Protocol (SIP). These preconditions require that the participant
reserve network resources before continuing with the session. We do
not define new quality of service reservation mechanisms; these
preconditions simply require a participant to use existing resource
reservation mechanisms before beginning the sessionfadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekW. Marshall¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”hfilenamegrfc3312eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125271hkeywordsÖcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolcqosrquality of servicelpreconditionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ØF2grankingcrfcd3312irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHIntegration of Resource Management and Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd5027d4032∂habstracty§This specification defines the new UPDATE method for the Session
Initiation Protocol (SIP). UPDATE allows a client to update
parameters of a session (such as the set of media streams and their
codecs) but has no impact on the state of a dialog. In that sense, it
is like a re-INVITE, but can be sent before the initial INVITE has
completed. This makes it very useful for updating session parameters
within early dialogs.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenbergddatej”hfilenamegrfc3311eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125270hkeywordsÑcSIPxSession Initiation Protocoljparametersmmedia streamskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=¨d™grankingÔcrfcd3311irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) UPDATE MethoddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4The Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Authentication Framework
includes two authentication schemes: Basic and Digest.  Both schemes
employ a shared secret based mechanism for access authentication.
The Authentication and Key Agreement (AKA) mechanism performs user
authentication and session key distribution in Universal Mobile
Telecommunications System (UMTS) networks.  AKA is a challenge-
response based mechanism that uses symmetric cryptography.  This memo
specifies an AKA based one-time password generation mechanism for
HTTP Digest access authentication.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamehA. Niemi¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehJ. Arkko¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamekV. Torvinenddatej”3hfilenamegrfc3310eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125269hkeywordsÉx&one-time password generation mechanismdumtsx*universal mobile telecommunications systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=ãüpgrankingÓcrfcd3310irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeHypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Digest Authentication Using Authentication and Key Agreement (AKA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÌStream Control Transmission Protocol [RFC2960] (SCTP) currently uses an
Adler-32 checksum.  For small packets Adler-32 provides weak detection
of errors.  This document changes that checksum and updates SCTP to
use a 32 bit CRC checksum.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcwitdfullx wit - Web and Internet TransportdnamexWeb and Internet TransportgauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhStanforddnamehJ. Stone¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationhSANlightdnamegD. Otisddatej”3hfilenamegrfc3309eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymetsvwgdfullx,tsvwg - Transport and Services Working Groupdnamex$Transport and Services Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125268hkeywordsÉhadler-32hchecksumoerror detectionkobsoletedByÅd4960epagesopublicationDate=òŒ¶grankingÌcrfcd3309irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Checksum ChangedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÇThe Layer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) provides a standard method
for tunneling PPP packets.  The current specification provides no
provisions for supporting Differentiated Services (diffserv) over the
L2TP control connection or subsequent data sessions.  As a result, no
standard mechanism currently exists within L2TP to provide L2TP
protocol negotiations for service discrimination.fadNameqDr. Thomas Nartendarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationtBlack Storm NetworksdnamejP. Calhoun¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamefW. Luo¢kaffiliationcTCBdnamelD. McPherson¢kaffiliationuMalibu Networks, Inc.dnameiK. Peirceddatej“Íhfilenamegrfc3308eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125267hkeywordsÜdL2TPxLayer Two Tunneling Protocolpper hop behaviorcphbhdiffservx!Differentiated Services ExtensionkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate=ÃKŸgrankingÏcrfcd3308irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexELayer Two Tunneling Protocol (L2TP) Differentiated Services ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyCThis document specifies guidelines that must be implemented by any
entity responsible for allocating IPv6 multicast addresses.  This
includes, but is not limited to, any documents or entities wishing
to assign permanent IPv6 multicast addresses, allocate dynamic IPv6
multicast addresses, and define permanent IPv6 multicast group
identifiers.  The purpose of these guidelines is to reduce the
probability of IPv6 multicast address collision, not only at the
IPv6 layer, but also at the link-layer of media that encode portions
of the IP layer address into the MAC layer address.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekB. Habermanddatej—±hfilenamegrfc3307eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmallocdfullx%malloc - Multicast-Address AllocationdnamexMulticast-Address Allocationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125266hkeywordsÉdIPv6qinternet protocolimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=uLßgrankingÎcrfcd3307irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Allocation Guidelines for IPv6 Multicast AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty•This specification defines an extension to the multicast addressing
architecture of the IP Version 6 protocol.  The extension presented
in this document allows for unicast-prefix-based allocation of
multicast addresses.  By delegating multicast addresses at the same
time as unicast prefixes, network operators will be able to identify
their multicast addresses without needing to run an inter-domain
allocation protocol.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamekB. Haberman¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thalerddatej—±hfilenamegrfc3306eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdipv6dfullx!ipv6 - IP Version 6 Working GroupdnamexIP Version 6 Working Groupdtypebwgbidjdoc-125265hkeywordsÉdIPv6qinternet protocolimulticastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=uL*grankingÍcrfcd3306irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Unicast-Prefix-based IPv6 Multicast AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd3956d4489d7371∂habstractyThis paper is the product of the W3C URI Interest Group.  It
addresses and attempts to clarify two issues pertaining to URIs, and
presents recommendations.  Section 1 addresses how URI space is
partitioned and the relationship between URIs, URLs, and URNs.
Section 2 describes how URI schemes and URN namespace ids are
registered.  Section 3 mentions additional unresolved issues not
considered by this paper and section 4 presents recommendations.
Questions concerning this document should be directed to the
uri@w3.org mailing list.fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvW3C URI Interest GroupdnamekM. Mealling¢kaffiliationvW3C URI Interest GroupdnamelR. Denenbergddatej—±hfilenamegrfc3305eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125264hkeywordsÅxinternet engineering task forcekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=oË…grankingÈcrfcd3305irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex¨Report from the Joint W3C/IETF URI Planning Interest Group: Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs), URLs, and Uniform Resource Names (URNs): Clarifications and RecommendationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This document specifies the requirements that the Middlebox Commu-
nication (midcom) protocol must satisfy in order to meet the needs
of applications wishing to influence middlebox function.  These
requirements were developed with a specific focus on network
address translation and firewall middleboxes.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationtBTexact TechnologiesdnamekR. P. Swale¢kaffiliationvMarconi CommunicationsdnamejP. A. Mart¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiP. Sijben¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegS. Brim¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehM. Shoreddatej—±hfilenamegrfc3304eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmidcomdfullx midcom - Middlebox CommunicationdnamewMiddlebox Communicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125263hkeywordsÉcnatxnetwork address protocoltfirewall middleboxeskobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate=aÑpgrankingËcrfcd3304irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Middlebox Communications (midcom) Protocol RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyAThere are a variety of intermediate devices in the Internet today
that require application intelligence for their operation.
Datagrams pertaining to real-time streaming applications such
as SIP and H.323 and peer-to-peer applications such as Napster
and NetMeeting cannot be identified by merely examining packet
headers.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationoKuokoa NetworksdnamelP. Srisuresh¢kaffiliationxFraunhofer Institute FOKUSdnameiJ. Kuthan¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationsAravox TechnologiesdnamejA. Molitor¢kaffiliationrRyerson UniversitydnameiA. Rayhanddatej—∞hfilenamegrfc3303eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmidcomdfullx midcom - Middlebox CommunicationdnamewMiddlebox Communicationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125262hkeywordsÅfmidcomkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate=aÑ␀grankingÁcrfcd3303irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Middlebox communication architecture and frameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxÂThis document describes the registration of the MIME sub-type
image/tiff.  The baseline encoding of TIFF (Tag Image File Format) is
defined by [TIFF].  This document refines an earlier sub-type
registration in RFC 1528 [TPC.INT].fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsons¢kaffiliationuBrooktrout TechnologydnamekJ. Raffertyddatej”3hfilenamegrfc3302eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-125261hkeywordsÑdTIFFuTag Image File FormatdMIMEx%Multipurpose Internet Mail ExtensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=îÊ%grankingÊcrfcd3302irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDTag Image File Format (TIFF) - image/tiff MIME Sub-type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty»L2TP [RFC2661] specifies a protocol for tunnelling PPP packets over
packet based networks and over IP networks in particular. L2TP
[RFC2661] supports remote access by ISDN and PSTN networks. This
document augments the procedures described in [RFC2661] to further
support ATM SVC or PVC based access networks. The extensions defined
within this document allow for asymmetric bi-directional call
establishment and service selection in the ATM access network.darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamejY. T'Joens¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamemP. Crivellari¢kaffiliationhBelgacomdnamehB. Salesddatej“zhfilenamegrfc3301eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgl2tpextdfullx1l2tpext - Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdnamex'Layer Two Tunneling Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125260hkeywordsÇdL2TPxLayer Two Tunneling ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=º±grankingÂcrfcd3301irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCLayer Two Tunnelling Protocol (L2TP): ATM access network extensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstract`gauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamekJ. Reynolds¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnameiR. Braden¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnameiS. Ginoza¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnamemA. De La CruzddatejÀhfilenamegrfc3300eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43421hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd3600epages1opublicationDate=‘<≤granking‰crfcd3300irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex$Internet Official Protocol StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstract`darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiS. Ginozaddatej…mhfilenamegrfc3299eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43430hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate?ﬁR¿granking„crfcd3299irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex2Request for Comments Summary RFC Numbers 3200-3299dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÀThis document describes the SPIRITS protocol requirements, based on the architecture presented in RFC 3136. (SPIRITS stands for "Service in the PSTN/IN Requesting InTernet Service".) The purpose of the protocol is to support services that originate in the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) and necessitate the interactions between the PSTN and the Internet.  Similarly, such services are called SPIRITS services. (Internet Call Waiting, Internet Caller-ID Delivery, and Internet Call Forwarding are examples of SPIRIT services, but the protocol is to define the building blocks from which many other services can be built.) On the PSTN side, the SPIRITS services are initiated from the Intelligent Network (IN) entities; the earlier IETF work on the PSTN/Internet Interworking (PINT) resulted in the protocol (RFC 2848) in support of the services initiated the other way around--from the Internet to PSTN.  To this end, this document lists general requirements for the SPIRITS protocol as well as those pertinent to IN, Wireless IN, and PINT building blocks.  The document also presents the SPIRITS WG consensus on the choice of the SPIRITS signaling protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamekI. Faynberg¢kaffiliationiVodaphonednamegJ. Gato¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameeH. Lu¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekL. Slutsmanddatej—∞hfilenamegrfc3298eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgspiritsdfullx<spirits - Service in the PSTN/IN Requesting InTernet Servicednamex2Service in the PSTN/IN Requesting InTernet Servicedtypebwgbidjdoc-125259hkeywordsÅgsupportkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=uKµgranking‚crfcd3298irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexäService in the Public Switched Telephone Network/Intelligent Network (PSTN/IN) Requesting InTernet Service (SPIRITS) Protocol RequirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxêThis memo describes a content negotiation mechanism for facsimile, voice and other messaging services that use Internet email. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationvClearswift CorporationdnamehG. Klyne¢kaffiliationkToshiba TECdnamejR. Iwazaki¢kaffiliationxBrandenburg InternetWorkingdnamejD. Crockerddatej“Fhfilenamegrfc3297eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-125258hkeywordsÉgcontentknegotiationifacsimilekobsoletedByÄepages.opublicationDate=?4√granking·crfcd3297irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex9Content Negotiation for Messaging Services based on EmaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxºThis document details schema and protocol elements for representing and managing named subordinate references in Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) Directories. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej“Fhfilenamegrfc3296eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125257hkeywordsÜdLDAPx%Lightweight Directory Access ProtocolfschemahelementskdescriptiongformatskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=<~Ògranking‡crfcd3296irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexXNamed Subordinate References in Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) DirectoriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for the use with the network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects for the General Switch Management Protocol (GSMP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott 