crosoftdnameiY. Bernet¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamehS. Blake¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamekD. Grossman¢kaffiliationpHarbour NetworksdnamehA. Smithddatej“«hfilenamegrfc3290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125251hkeywordsÅwdifferentiated serviceskobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDate=@granking⁄crfcd3290irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex1An Informal Management Model for Diffserv RoutersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty$This memo describes an SMIv2 (Structure of Management Information version 2) MIB for a device implementing the Differentiated Services Architecture.  It may be used both for monitoring and configuration of a router or switch capable of Differentiated Services functionality. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationlCisco SystemdnamehF. Baker¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegK. Chan¢kaffiliationpHarbour NetworksdnamehA. Smithddatej“∆hfilenamegrfc3289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125250hkeywordsÖcmibxManagement Information Basehdiffservx$Differentiated Services ArchitecturefrouterkobsoletedByÄepagestopublicationDate=@jgrankingŸcrfcd3289irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHManagement Information Base for the Differentiated Services ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÿThis memo specifies a Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP) binding to the Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol core (BEEP).  A SOAP binding describes how SOAP messages are transmitted in the network. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlClipcode.comdnamemE. O'Tuathail¢kaffiliationxDover Beach Consulting, Inc.dnamegM. Roseddatej“yhfilenamegrfc3288eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125249hkeywordsÉgbindingomarkup languagecxmlkobsoletedByÅd4227epagesopublicationDate=áogrankingÿcrfcd3288irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Using the Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP) in Blocks Extensible Exchange Protocol (BEEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyùThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes managed objects used for monitoring Differentiated Services (DS) Codepoint usage in packets which contain a DS field, utilizing the monitoring framework defined in the RMON-2 (Remote Network Monitoring Management Version 2) MIB. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejA. Biermanddatej“Ehfilenamegrfc3287eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125248hkeywordsÜdrmonqRemote MonitoringcMIBxmanagement information basehdiffservwDifferentiated ServiceskobsoletedByÄepagesxopublicationDate=*‡granking◊crfcd3287irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Remote Monitoring MIB Extensions for Differentiated ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document provides a high level introduction to the capabilities supported by the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  It is intended as a guide for potential users of SCTP as a general purpose transport protocol.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationqCiena CorporationdnamefL. Ong¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiJ. Yoakumddatej“∆hfilenamegrfc3286eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125247hkeywordsÑitransportelayeritelephonyisignalingkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate<ı <granking÷crfcd3286irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBAn Introduction to the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¿This document describes the steps to configure the Microsoft Word application to produce documents in Internet Draft and RFC format.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiM. Gahrns¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegT. Hainddatej ≈hfilenamegrfc3285eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125246hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd5385epagesopublicationDate<ˆ±ÿgranking’crfcd3285irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Using Microsoft Word to create Internet Drafts and RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÕThis memo describes VCDIFF, a general, efficient and portable data format suitable for encoding compressed and/or differencing data so that they can be easily transported among computers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnamegD. Korn¢kaffiliationkUC BerkeleydnamelJ. MacDonald¢kaffiliationwHewlett-Packard CompanydnamehJ. Mogul¢kaffiliationiAT&T LabsdnameeK. Voddatej“xhfilenamegrfc3284eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125245hkeywordsÖfVCDIFFitransporthportabledat&thencodingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate= Û¸granking‘crfcd3284irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;The VCDIFF Generic Differencing and Compression Data FormatdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyúThis document describes the various Internet calendaring and scheduling standards and works in progress, and the relationships between them.  Its intent is to provide a context for these documents, assist in their understanding, and potentially aid in the design of standards-based calendaring and scheduling systems.  The standards addressed are RFC 2445 (iCalendar), RFC 2446 (iTIP), and RFC 2447 (iMIP).  The work in progress addressed is "Calendar Access Protocol" (CAP).  This document also describes issues and problems that are not solved by these protocols, and that could be targets for future work.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejB. Mahoney¢kaffiliationgSteltordnameiG. Babics¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamehA. Talerddatej“xhfilenamegrfc3283eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfcalschdfullx#calsch - Calendaring and SchedulingdnamexCalendaring and Schedulingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125244hkeywordsÖrscheduling systemsccapxcalendar access protocoolditipdimipkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate= Ú„granking”crfcd3283irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGuide to Internet CalendaringdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyNThis document defines a "Content-language:" header, for use in cases where one desires to indicate the language of something that has RFC 822-like headers, like MIME body parts or Web documents, and an "Accept-Language:" header for use in cases where one wishes to indicate one's preferences with regard to language. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemH. Alvestrandddatej“∆hfilenamegrfc3282eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125243hkeywordsÉpContent-languageoAccept-LanguagefheaderkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<ˆ±Lgranking“crfcd3282irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexContent Language HeadersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractynThis specification defines a profile for the use of X.509 Attribute Certificates in Internet Protocols.  Attribute certificates may be used in a wide range of applications and environments covering a broad spectrum of interoperability goals and a broader spectrum of operational and assurance requirements.  The goal of this document is to establish a common baseline for generic applications requiring broad interoperability as well as limited special purpose requirements.  The profile places emphasis on attribute certificate support for Internet electronic mail, IPSec, and WWW security applications. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvBaltimore TechnologiesdnamejS. Farrell¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejR. Housleyddatej—Éhfilenamegrfc3281eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125242hkeywordsÖoelectronic maileemaileipseccwwwhsecuritykobsoletedByÅd5755epages(opublicationDate<‹aLgranking—crfcd3281irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex;An Internet Attribute Certificate Profile for AuthorizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÖThis memo profiles the X.509 v3 certificate and X.509 v2 Certificate Revocation List (CRL) for use in the Internet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejR. Housley¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamegW. Polk¢kaffiliationhVeriSigndnamegW. Ford¢kaffiliationiCitigroupdnamegD. Soloddatej—Éhfilenamegrfc3280eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125241hkeywordsÖeX.509cPKIxPublic Key InfrastructurecCRLxCertificate Revocation ListkobsoletedByÅd5280epagesÅopublicationDate<‹`granking–crfcd3280irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexbInternet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÇd4325d4630µhabstractyZThis document specifies algorithm identifiers and ASN.1 encoding formats for digital signatures and subject public keys used in the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI).  Digital signatures are used to sign certificates and certificate revocation list (CRLs).  Certificates include the public key of the named subject. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamejL. Bassham¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamegW. Polk¢kaffiliationdNISTdnamejR. Housleyddatej—Éhfilenamegrfc3279eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdpkixdfullx(pkix - Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dnamex!Public-Key Infrastructure (X.509)dtypebwgbidjdoc-125240hkeywordsÜeASN.1eX.509cPKIxPublic Key InfrastructurecCRLxCertificate Revocation ListkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<‹_+grankingœcrfcd3279irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexÖAlgorithms and Identifiers for the Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) ProfiledtypecrfciupdatedByÖd8692d4055d5480d4491d5758µhabstractyËThis document describes how to use Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) public-key algorithms in the Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS).  The ECC algorithms support the creation of digital signatures and the exchange of keys to encrypt or authenticate content.  The definition of the algorithm processing is based on the ANSI X9.62 standard, developed by the ANSI X9F1 working group, the IEEE 1363 standard, and the SEC 1 standard.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationmCerticom CorpdnameoS. Blake-Wilson¢kaffiliationmCerticom CorpdnamehD. Brown¢kaffiliationuCosine CommunicationsdnamejP. Lambertddatej—Éhfilenamegrfc3278eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125239hkeywordsÉjpublic keyrdigital signaturesnauthenticationkobsoletedByÅd5753epagesopublicationDate<‹^ìgrankingŒcrfcd3278irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexYUse of Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Algorithms in Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThis document describes a simple, interoperable mechanism that can be employed in Intermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) networks in order to decrease the data loss associated with deterministic blackholing of packets during transient network conditions.  The mechanism proposed here requires no IS-IS protocol changes and is completely interoperable with the existing IS-IS specification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcTCBdnamelD. McPhersonddatej—Éhfilenamegrfc3277eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdisisdfullxisis - IS-IS for IP InternetsdnamevIS-IS for IP Internetsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125238hkeywordsÅfrouterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®ÄgrankingÕcrfcd3277irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPIntermediate System to Intermediate System (IS-IS) Transient Blackhole AvoidancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyOThis document defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) module for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes objects used for managing High Bit-Rate DSL - 2nd generation (HDSL2) and Single-Pair High-Speed Digital Subscriber Line (SHDSL) interfaces. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvPESA Switching SystemsdnamefB. Ray¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamegR. Abbiddatej“∆hfilenamegrfc3276eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgadslmibdfullradslmib - ADSL MIBdnamehADSL MIBdtypebwgbidjdoc-125237hkeywordsÇcmibjinterfaceskobsoletedByÅd4319epagesBopublicationDate<Ï1xgrankingÃcrfcd3276irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexôDefinitions of Managed Objects for High Bit-Rate DSL - 2nd generation (HDSL2) and Single-Pair High-Speed Digital Subscriber Line (SHDSL) Lines ProcessingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxyThis document specifies XML (Extensible Markup Language) digital signature processing rules and syntax. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationcW3CdnameiJ. Reagle¢kaffiliationiCitigroupdnamegD. Soloddatej“§hfilenamegrfc3275eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgxmldsigdfullx xmldsig - XML Digital SignaturesdnamevXML Digital Signaturesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125236hkeywordsÇcXMLxextensible markup languagekobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDate<5␀grankingÀcrfcd3275irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex@(Extensible Markup Language) XML-Signature Syntax and ProcessingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis document defines a format for using compressed data as a Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) content type.  Compressing data before transmission provides a number of advantages, including the elimination of data redundancy which could help an attacker, speeding up processing by reducing the amount of data to be processed by later steps (such as signing or encryption), and reducing overall message size.  Although there have been proposals for adding compression at other levels (for example at the MIME or SSL level), these don't address the problem of compression of CMS content unless the compression is supplied by an external means (for example by intermixing MIME and CMS). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamesJeffrey I. Schillerdarea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamejP. Gutmannddatej“xhfilenamegrfc3274eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125235hkeywordsÉcCMSxCryptographic Message Syntaxqcontent info typekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=i<granking crfcd3274irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCCompressed Data Content Type for Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÕThis memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it defines objects for managing remote network monitoring (RMON) devices for use on high speed networks.  This document contains a MIB Module that defines these new objects and also contains definitions of some updated objects from the RMON-MIB in RFC 2819 and the RMON2-MIB in RFC 2021. [PROPOSED STANDARD]fadNamekBert Wijnendarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemS. Waldbusserddatej“Ehfilenamegrfc3273eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymgrmonmibdfullx#rmonmib - Remote Network MonitoringdnamexRemote Network Monitoringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125234hkeywordsÖdrmonxRemote Network Monitoringcmibxmanagement information baseshigh speed networkskobsoletedByÄepagesMopublicationDate=3 granking…crfcd3273irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexPRemote Network Monitoring Management Information Base for High Capacity NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4502µhabstractyThis memo describes the principles of Traffic Engineering (TE) in the Internet.  The document is intended to promote better understanding of the issues surrounding traffic engineering in IP networks, and to provide a common basis for the development of traffic engineering capabilities for the Internet.  The principles, architectures, and methodologies for performance evaluation and performance optimization of operational IP networks are discussed throughout this document.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcsubdfullqsub - Sub-IP AreadnamekSub-IP AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationnMovaz NetworksdnamejD. Awduche¢kaffiliationoCelion NetworksdnamegA. Chiu¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamejA. Elwalid¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamejI. Widjaja¢kaffiliationpRedback NetworksdnamegX. Xiaoddatej“∆hfilenamegrfc3272eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdtewgdfullx#tewg - Internet Traffic EngineeringdnamexInternet Traffic Engineeringdtypebwgbidjdoc-125233hkeywordsÉbtebiphnetworkskobsoletedByÅd9522epagesGopublicationDate<Í”ngranking»crfcd3272irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Overview and Principles of Internet Traffic EngineeringdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5462∂habstractxŒThis document expresses the Internet Society's ideology that the Internet really is for everyone.  However, it will only be such if we make it so.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering GroupgauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpInternet SocietydnamegV. Cerfddatej—Åhfilenamegrfc3271eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125232hkeywordsádisocpinternet societympolicy issuesmsocial impactoeconomic impacttinternational policyxuse and abuse of the internetkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking«crfcd3271irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexThe Internet is for EveryonedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx£This document defines a flexible solution for support of Differentiated Services (Diff-Serv) over Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) networks. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamenF. Le Faucheur¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameeL. Wu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationoPMC-Sierra Inc.dnameiS. Davari¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekP. Vaananen¢kaffiliationqAxiowave NetworksdnamekR. Krishnan¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnameiP. Cheval¢kaffiliationmSong NetworksdnamekJ. Heinanenddatej“≈hfilenamegrfc3270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125231hkeywordsàdMPLSxMulti-Protocol Label Switchingidiff-servwDifferentiated ServicesbBAsbehaviour aggregatecLSPtlabel switched pathskobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate<ÍàÁgranking∆crfcd3270irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexHMulti-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Support of Differentiated ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5462∂habstracty≠This document provides general guidelines to assist the authors of Reliable Multicast Transport (RMT) building block and protocol instantiation definitions.  The purpose of these guidelines is to ensure that any building block and protocol instantiation definitions produced contain sufficient information to fully explain their operation and use.  In addition these guidelines provide directions to specify modular and clearly defined RMT building blocks and protocol instantiations that can be refined and augmented to safely create new protocols for use in new scenarios for which any existing protocols were not designed.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamejR. Kermode¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamekL. Vicisanoddatej—Ähfilenamegrfc3269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrmtdfullx"rmt - Reliable Multicast TransportdnamexReliable Multicast Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125230hkeywordsÇkdefinitionsioperationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<€2granking≈crfcd3269irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexmAuthor Guidelines for Reliable Multicast Transport (RMT) Building Blocks and Protocol Instantiation documentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractycThis document proposes several new ciphersuites.  At present, the symmetric ciphers supported by Transport Layer Security (TLS) are RC2, RC4, International Data Encryption Algorithm (IDEA), Data Encryption Standard (DES), and triple DES.  The protocol would be enhanced by the addition of Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) ciphersuites. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationrSkygate TechnologydnamehP. Chownddatej“xhfilenamegrfc3268eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymctlsdfullxtls - Transport Layer SecuritydnamexTransport Layer Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125229hkeywordsácAESxAdvanced Encryption StandardcTLSxTransport Layer Securitydideaxinternational data algorithmisymmetrickobsoletedByÅd5246epagesopublicationDate=#[ﬂgrankingƒcrfcd3268irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRAdvanced Encryption Standard (AES) Ciphersuites for Transport Layer Security (TLS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyaThis document specifies a real-time transport protocol (RTP) payload format to be used for Adaptive Multi-Rate (AMR) and Adaptive Multi-Rate Wideband (AMR-WB) encoded speech signals.  The payload format is designed to be able to interoperate with existing AMR and AMR-WB transport formats on non-IP networks.  In addition, a file format is specified for transport of AMR and AMR-WB speech data in storage mode applications such as email.  Two separate MIME type registrations are included, one for AMR and one for AMR-WB, specifying use of both the RTP payload format and the storage format. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamejJ. Sjoberg¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamemM. Westerlund¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamelA. Lakaniemi¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamefQ. Xieddatej“xhfilenamegrfc3267eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcavtdfullxavt - Audio/Video TransportdnameuAudio/Video Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125228hkeywordsàcRTPxReal-Time Transport ProtocolsAdaptive Multi-RatecAMRxAdaptive Multi-Rate WidebandfAMR-WBlinteroperatelapplicationskobsoletedByÅd4867epages1opublicationDate=#[,granking√crfcd3267irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex¢Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) Payload Format and File Storage Format for the Adaptive Multi-Rate (AMR) and Adaptive Multi-Rate Wideband (AMR-WB) Audio CodecsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes the use of Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) addresses in conjunction with the Session Description Protocol (SDP).  Specifically, this document clarifies existing text in SDP with regards to the syntax of IPv6 addresses. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehS. Olson¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamekA. B. Roachddatej“whfilenamegrfc3266eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125227hkeywordsÅxinternet addresses syntaxkobsoletedByÅd4566epagesopublicationDate=#Zágranking¬crfcd3266irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex6Support for IPv6 in Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document describes an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP).  The purpose of this extension is to provide an extensible framework by which SIP nodes can request notification from remote nodes indicating that certain events have occurred. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamekA. B. Roachddatej“whfilenamegrfc3265eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125226hkeywordsÜcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolqapplication-layerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÅd6665epages&opublicationDate=#VÁgranking¡crfcd3265irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)-Specific Event NotificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd5367d6446d5727∂habstractyoThis document defines a mechanism by which two entities can make use of the Session Description Protocol (SDP) to arrive at a common view of a multimedia session between them.  In the model, one participant offers the other a description of the desired session from their perspective, and the other participant answers with the desired session from their perspective.  This offer/answer model is most useful in unicast sessions where information from both participants is needed for the complete view of the session.  The offer/answer model is used by protocols like the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP). [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej“whfilenamegrfc3264eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125225hkeywordsÜcSDPxSession Description Protocolqapplication-layerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=#Vogranking¿crfcd3264irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex=An Offer/Answer Model with Session Description Protocol (SDP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd9143d6157d8843∂habstractyÉThe Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) uses DNS procedures to allow a client to resolve a SIP Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) into the IP address, port, and transport protocol of the next hop to contact.  It also uses DNS to allow a server to send a response to a backup client if the primary client has failed.  This document describes those DNS procedures in detail. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej“whfilenamegrfc3263eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125224hkeywordsÜcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolqapplication-layerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=#UÏgrankingøcrfcd3263irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Session Initiation Protocol (SIP): Locating SIP ServersdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7984d8553∂habstractyThis document specifies an extension to the Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) providing reliable provisional response messages.  This extension uses the option tag 100rel and defines the Provisional Response ACKnowledgement (PRACK) method. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinneddatej“whfilenamegrfc3262eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125223hkeywordsÜcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolqapplication-layerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=#Ukgrankingæcrfcd3262irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIReliability of Provisional Responses in Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty6This document describes Session Initiation Protocol (SIP), an application-layer control (signaling) protocol for creating, modifying, and terminating sessions with one or more participants.  These sessions include Internet telephone calls, multimedia distribution, and multimedia conferences. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areagauthorsà¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationkColumbia U.dnamenH. Schulzrinne¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelG. Camarillo¢kaffiliationhWorldComdnamekA. Johnston¢kaffiliationgNeustardnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnameiR. Sparks¢kaffiliationdICIRdnamejM. Handley¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamekE. Schoolerddatej“vhfilenamegrfc3261eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125222hkeywordsÜcSIPxSession Initiation Protocolqapplication-layerjmultimediaimulticastgunicastkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=#TﬁgrankingΩcrfcd3261irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex SIP: Session Initiation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByïd6141d8760d7462d7463d5621d8898d5626d6026d5630d4320d3265d5922d5393d6878d5954d8591d3853d4916d8996d8217d6665µhabstractyŒThis memo captures Diffserv working group agreements concerning new and improved terminology, and provides minor technical clarifications.  It is intended to update RFC 2474, RFC 2475 and RFC 2597.  When RFCs 2474 and 2597 advance on the standards track, and RFC 2475 is updated, it is intended that the revisions in this memo will be incorporated, and that this memo will be obsoleted by the new RFCs.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnMotorola, Inc.dnamekD. Grossmanddatej—Çhfilenamegrfc3260eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125221hkeywordsÖgDIFFSRVkscalabilitybIPhinternethprotocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate<®Ägrankingºcrfcd3260irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex/New Terminology and Clarifications for DiffservdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty`The local Message Bus (Mbus) is a light-weight message-oriented coordination protocol for group communication between application components.  The Mbus provides automatic location of communication peers, subject based addressing, reliable message transfer and different types of communication schemes.  The protocol is layered on top of IP multicast and is specified for IPv4 and IPv6.  The IP multicast scope is limited to link-local multicast.  This document specifies the Mbus protocol, i.e., message syntax, addressing and transport mechanisms.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymcartdfullx%art - Applications and Real-Time AreadnamexApplications and Real-Time AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwTZI Universitaet BremendnamefJ. Ott¢kaffiliationx"USC Information Sciences InstitutednamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliationwTZI Universitaet BremendnamekD. Kutscherddatej—Çhfilenamegrfc3259eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfmmusicdfullx.mmusic - Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldnamex%Multiparty Multimedia Session Controldtypebwgbidjdoc-125220hkeywordsádmbusgmessagebipimulticastjaddressingitransportfsyntaxkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate<‡≤grankingªcrfcd3259irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$A Message Bus for Local CoordinationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÎThis memo describes a set of practices intended to enable an authoritative name server operator to provide access to a single named server in multiple locations.  The primary motivation for the development and deployment of these practices is to increase the distribution of Domain Name System (DNS) servers to previously under- served areas of the network topology and to reduce the latency for DNS query responses in those areas.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamejRandy Bushdarea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnameiT. Hardieddatej—Çhfilenamegrfc3258eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymednsopdfullx%dnsop - Domain Name System OperationsdnamexDomain Name System Operationsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125219hkeywordsÑcdnsgnetworkhtopologyglatencykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking∫crfcd3258irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexDDistributing Authoritative Name Servers via Shared Unicast AddressesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractysThis document describes the applicability of the Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP).  It also contrasts SCTP with the two dominant transport protocols, User Datagram Protocol (UDP) & Transmission Control Protocol (TCP), and gives some guidelines for when best to use SCTP and when not best to use SCTP.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNamepScott O. Bradnerdarea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgSiemensdnamehL. Coeneddatej—Åhfilenamegrfc3257eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsigtrandfullxsigtran - Signaling TransportdnamesSignaling Transportdtypebwgbidjdoc-125218hkeywordsàdsctpcudpctcpcrtprtransport securityitransportcnatkmultihomingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägrankingπcrfcd3257irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Stream Control Transmission Protocol Applicability StatementdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxãThis document proposes a new sub-option to the DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) Relay Agent Information Option. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoYAS CorporationdnamehD. Jones¢kaffiliationnAT&T BroadbanddnameiR. Woundyddatej—Åhfilenamegrfc3256eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcdhcdfullx dhc - Dynamic Host ConfigurationdnamexDynamic Host Configurationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125217hkeywordsÑfDOCSISx/Data-Over-Cable Service Interface SpecificationdDHCPx#Dynamic Host Configuration ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking∏crfcd3256irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexòThe DOCSIS (Data-Over-Cable Service Interface Specifications) Device Class DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) Relay Agent Information Sub-optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty%This document describes an extension to the mapping of Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) into Synchronous Optical NETwork/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) to include the use of SONET/SDH SPE/VC virtual concatenation and the use of both high order and low order payloads. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmAgere SystemsdnamehN. Jones¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiC. Murtonddatej—Åhfilenamegrfc3255eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125216hkeywordsÑcPPPwPoint-to-Point ProtocoliSONET/SDHx9Synchronous Optical NETwork/Synchronous Digital HierarchykobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking∑crfcd3255irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex∞Extending Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) over Synchronous Optical NETwork/Synchronous Digital Hierarchy (SONET/SDH) with virtual concatenation, high order and low order payloadsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty—When discussing systems for making information accessible through the Internet in standardized ways, it may be useful if the people who are discussing it have a common understanding of the terms they use.  For example, a reference to this document would give one the power to agree that the DNS (Domain Name System) is a global lookup repository with perimeter integrity and loose, converging consistency.  On the other hand, a LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol) directory server is a local, centralized repository with both lookup and search capability.  This document discusses one group of such systems which is known under the term, "directories".  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemH. Alvestrandddatej—Åhfilenamegrfc3254eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125215hkeywordsÜfdomaindnamefsystemklightweightfaccesshprotocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking∂crfcd3254irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex)Definitions for talking about directoriesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx…This document specifies a set of methods, headers, and resource types that define the WebDAV (Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning) versioning extensions to the HTTP/1.1 protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationqRational SoftwarednamehG. Clemm¢kaffiliationcIBMdnameiJ. Amsden¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamejT. Ellison¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamehC. Kaler¢kaffiliationoU.C. Santa CruzdnamelJ. Whiteheadddatej“§hfilenamegrfc3253eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdeltavdfullx4deltav - Web Versioning and Configuration Managementdnamex+Web Versioning and Configuration Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125214hkeywordsàdHTTPxhypertext transfer protocolgclientselabelmconfigurationjmanagementfWebDAVx(Web Distributed Authoring and VersioningkobsoletedByÄepagesvopublicationDate<5␀grankingµcrfcd3253irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJVersioning Extensions to WebDAV (Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx¨This document defines a reformulation of IP and two transport layer protocols (TCP and UDP) as XML applications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhMimezinednamejH. Kennedyddatej—çhfilenamegrfc3252eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43429hkeywordsÅebloatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking¥crfcd3252irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex%Binary Lexical Octet Ad-hoc TransportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractycMostly Pointless Lamp Switching (MPLampS) is an architecture for carrying electricity over IP (with an MPLS control plane).  According to our marketing department, MPLampS has the potential to dramatically lower the price, ease the distribution and usage, and improve the manageability of delivering electricity.  This document is motivated by such work as SONET/SDH over IP/MPLS (with apologies to the authors).  Readers of the previous work have been observed scratching their heads and muttering, "What next?".  This document answers that question.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmTellium, Inc.dnamenB. Rajagopalanddatej—çhfilenamegrfc3251eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125213hkeywordsÅqInternet ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate<®Ägranking≥crfcd3251irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlesElectricity over IPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx≠This document describes the registration of the MIME sub-type image/tiff-fx.  The encodings are defined by File Format for Internet Fax and its extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamepScott Hollenbeckdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnamekL. McIntyre¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamejG. Parsons¢kaffiliationuBrooktrout TechnologydnamekJ. Raffertyddatej”2hfilenamegrfc3250eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-125212hkeywordsädFFIFdTIFFcTageImageifacsimiledMIMElmultipurposehInternetdmailjextensionskobsoletedByÅd3950epagesopublicationDate=íUâgranking≤crfcd3250irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWTag Image File Format Fax eXtended (TIFF-FX) - image/tiff-fx MIME Sub-type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyThis document is intended for the implementers of software that use email to send to facsimiles using RFC 2305 and 2532.  This is an informational document and its guidelines do not supersede the referenced documents.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnameiV. Cancio¢kaffiliationxG3 Nova Technology, Inc.dnamekM. Moldovan¢kaffiliationsRicoh Company, LTD.dnameiH. Tamura¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamegD. Wingddatej”2hfilenamegrfc3249eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcfaxdfullrfax - Internet FaxdnamelInternet Faxdtypebwgbidjdoc-125211hkeywordsâcfaxdtiffgtiff-fxdifaxfe-maileemaileesmtpcdsncmdnkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate=íUgranking±crfcd3249irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex4Implementers Guide for Facsimile Using Internet MaildtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyΩFor historical interest, this document captures the EF Design Team's proposed solution, preferred by the original authors of RFC 2598 but not adopted by the working group in December 2000.  The original definition of EF was based on comparison of forwarding on an unloaded network.  This experimental Delay Bound (DB) PHB requires a bound on the delay of packets due to other traffic in the network.  At the Pittsburgh IETF meeting in August 2000, the Differentiated Services working group faced serious questions regarding RFC 2598 - the group's standards track definition of the Expedited Forwarding (EF) Per Hop Behavior (PHB).  An 'EF Design Team' volunteered to develop a re-expression of RFC 2598, bearing in mind the issues raised in the DiffServ group.  At the San Diego IETF meeting in December 2000 the DiffServ working group decided to pursue an alternative re-expression of the EF PHB.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationx"Swinburne University of TechnologydnamekG. Armitage¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnameiA. Casati¢kaffiliationwUniversity of CambridgednamelJ. Crowcroft¢kaffiliationjConsultantdnamejJ. Halpern¢kaffiliationtCorona Networks Inc.dnamehB. Kumar¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamemJ. Schnizleinddatej“§hfilenamegrfc3248eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125210hkeywordsÖpper hop behaviorcphbtexpedited forwardingbefbdbkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<5␀granking∞crfcd3248irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A Delay Bound alternative revision of RFC 2598dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty>This document was written during the process of clarification of RFC2598 "An Expedited Forwarding PHB" that led to the publication of revised specification of EF "An Expedited Forwarding PHB".  Its primary motivation is providing additional explanation to the revised EF definition and its properties.  The document also provides additional implementation examples and gives some guidance for computation of the numerical parameters of the new definition for several well known schedulers and router architectures.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areagauthorsä¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Charny¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameiJ. Bennet¢kaffiliationgTellabsdnameiK. Benson¢kaffiliationdEPFLdnameiJ. Boudec¢kaffiliationoCelion NetworksdnamegA. Chiu¢kaffiliationcTRWdnamekW. Courtney¢kaffiliationjPMC-SierradnameiS. Davari¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiV. Firoiu¢kaffiliationmAT&T ResearchdnamekC. Kalmanek¢kaffiliationrTeraOptic NetworksdnameoK. Ramakrishnanddatej“£hfilenamegrfc3247eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125209hkeywordsÖwdifferentiated servicesdfifolfair queuingedelayfjitterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<5␀grankingØcrfcd3247irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeSupplemental Information for the New Definition of the EF PHB (Expedited Forwarding Per-Hop Behavior)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty{This document defines a PHB (per-hop behavior) called Expedited Forwarding (EF).  The PHB is a basic building block in the Differentiated Services architecture.  EF is intended to provide a building block for low delay, low jitter and low loss services by ensuring that the EF aggregate is served at a certain configured rate.  This document obsoletes RFC 2598. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areagauthorsâ¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnameiA. Charny¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamemJ.C.R. Bennet¢kaffiliationgTellabsdnameiK. Benson¢kaffiliationdEPFLdnamenJ.Y. Le Boudec¢kaffiliationcTRWdnamekW. Courtney¢kaffiliationjPMC-SierradnameiS. Davari¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiV. Firoiu¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamelD. Stiliadisddatej“£hfilenamegrfc3246eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125208hkeywordsÜcPHBpper-hop behaviortexpedited forwardingwdifferentiated servicesedelayfjitterkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<5␀grankingÆcrfcd3246irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.An Expedited Forwarding PHB (Per-Hop Behavior)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty«RFC 2916 assigned responsibility for a number of administrative and operational details of Telephone Number Mapping (ENUM) to the IAB.  It also anticipated that ITU would take responsibility for determining the legitimacy and appropriateness of applicants for delegation of "country code"-level subdomains of the top-level ENUM domain.  Recently, three memos have been prepared for the ITU-T Study Group 2 (SG2) to explain the background of, and reasoning for, the relevant decisions.  The IAB has also supplied a set of procedural instructions to the RIPE NCC for implementation of their part of the model.  The content of the three memos is provided in this document for the information of the IETF community.fadNametHarald T. AlvestrandgauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcIABdnamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliation`dnamecIABddatej“£hfilenamegrfc3245eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125207hkeywordsÇcIABdARPAkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate<5␀granking≠crfcd3245irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexíThe History and Context of Telephone Number Mapping (ENUM) Operational Decisions: Informational Documents Contributed to ITU-T Study Group 2 (SG2)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyèThis memo specifies Microsoft's Windows 2000 Kerberos change password and set password protocols.  The Windows 2000 Kerberos change password protocol interoperates with the original Kerberos change password protocol.  Change password is a request reply protocol that includes a KRB_PRIV message that contains the new password for the user.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxUniversity of WashingtondnamehM. Swift¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejJ. Trostle¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiJ. Brezakddatej—¸hfilenamegrfc3244eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125206hkeywordsÉhsecuritygmessageecodeskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<ZK␀granking¨crfcd3244irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexJMicrosoft Windows 2000 Kerberos Change Password and Set Password ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyàThis document contains requirements for the 0-byte IP/UDP/RTP (Internet Protocol/User Datagram Protocol/Real-Time Transport Protocol) header compression scheme to be developed by the Robust Header Compression (ROHC) Working Group.  It also includes the basic assumptions for the typical link layers over which 0-byte compression may be implemented, and assumptions about its usage in general.fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonssonddatej—Åhfilenamegrfc3243eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125205hkeywordsâhinternethprotocolduserhdatagramureal-time applicationitransportlapplicationscLLAslink-layer assistedkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking´crfcd3243irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex`RObust Header Compression (ROHC): Requirements and Assumptions for 0-byte IP/UDP/RTP CompressiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÖThis document defines a ROHC (Robust Header Compression) profile for compression of IP/UDP/RTP (Internet Protocol/User Datagram Protocol/Real-Time Transport Protocol) packets, utilizing functionality provided by the lower layers to increase compression efficiency by completely eliminating the header for most packets during optimal operation.  The profile is built as an extension to the ROHC RTP profile.  It defines additional mechanisms needed in ROHC, states requirements on the assisting layer to guarantee transparency, and specifies general logic for compression and decompression making use of this header-free packet. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhEricssondnamelL-E. Jonsson¢kaffiliation`dnamelG. Pelletierddatej—Åhfilenamegrfc3242eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125204hkeywordsâdROHCxRObust Header CompressionbIPqinternet protocolcUDPvuser datagram protocolcRTPxreal-time transport protocolkapplicationkobsoletedByÅd4362epagesopublicationDate<®Ägranking™crfcd3242irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexNRObust Header Compression (ROHC): A Link-Layer Assisted Profile for IP/UDP/RTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx˚This document describes an option for negotiating the use of robust header compression (ROHC) on IP datagrams transmitted over the Point- to-Point Protocol (PPP).  It defines extensions to the PPP Control Protocols for IPv4 and IPv6. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameqAllison J. Mankindarea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationnTZI/Uni BremendnamejC. Bormannddatej—Ähfilenamegrfc3241eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdrohcdfullx rohc - Robust Header CompressiondnamexRobust Header Compressiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125203hkeywordsÜdROHCxRobust Header CompressioncPPPwpoint-to-point protocolhdatagramgpacketskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<⁄†ıgranking©crfcd3241irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Robust Header Compression (ROHC) over PPPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4815µhabstracty5This document describes the registration of the MIME sub-type application/dicom (Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine).  The baseline encoding is defined by the DICOM Standards Committee in "Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine".  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoDICOM CommitteednameiD. Clunie¢kaffiliation`dnamemE. Cordonnierddatej—˚hfilenamegrfc3240eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125202hkeywordsÑlmultipurposehinternetdmailjextensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<ZK␀granking®crfcd3240irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexeDigital Imaging and Communications in Medicine (DICOM) - Application/dicom MIME Sub-type RegistrationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty≠This document specifies the requirements and uses cases for some optional administrative operations for use with the Internet Printing Protocol (IPP) version 1.0 and version 1.1.  Some of these administrative operations operate on the IPP Job and Printer objects.  The remaining operations operate on a new Device object that more closely models a single output device.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnameiC. Kugler¢kaffiliationoIBM CorporationdnamehH. Lewis¢kaffiliationqXerox CorporationdnamekT. Hastingsddatej—˚hfilenamegrfc3239eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcippdfullx ipp - Internet Printing ProtocoldnamexInternet Printing Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125201hkeywordsÇfobjectfdevicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<ZK␀grankingßcrfcd3239irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexeInternet Printing Protocol (IPP): Requirements for Job, Printer, and Device Administrative OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyThis document includes comments and recommendations by the IAB on some architectural and policy issues related to the chartering of Open Pluggable Edge Services (OPES) in the IETF.  OPES are services that would be deployed at application-level intermediaries in the network, for example, at a web proxy cache between the origin server and the client.  These intermediaries would transform or filter content, with the explicit consent of either the content provider or the end user.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx!Internet Architecture Board (IAB)dnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliation`dnameiL. Daigleddatej“-hfilenamegrfc3238eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125200hkeywordsÑdOPEShinternetlarchitectureeboardkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgranking¶crfcd3238irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlexLIAB Architectural and Policy Considerations for Open Pluggable Edge ServicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxäThis document defines a basic set of requirements for reliable server pooling.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationjSiemens AGdnameiM. Tuexen¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamefQ. Xie¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejR. Stewart¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehM. Shore¢kaffiliationeCienadnamefL. Ong¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekJ. Loughney¢kaffiliationeNokiadnamekM. Stillmanddatej“,hfilenamegrfc3237eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhrserpooldfullx"rserpool - Reliable Server PoolingdnamewReliable Server Poolingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125199hkeywordsÇhrserpoolkapplicationkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate<1lÄgranking•crfcd3237irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex(Requirements for Reliable Server PoolingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document defines the 'application/xhtml+xml' MIME media type for XHTML based markup languages; it is not intended to obsolete any previous IETF documents, in particular RFC 2854 which registers 'text/html'.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationpPlanetfred, Inc.dnamehM. Baker¢kaffiliationxEricsson Mobile CommunicationsdnamehP. Starkddatej“,hfilenamegrfc3236eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125198hkeywordsÖdmimelmultipurposehinternetdmailjextensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgranking§crfcd3236irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex&The 'application/xhtml+xml' Media TypedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document discusses those things that application designers might wish to consider when designing new protocols.  While many common Internet applications will operate cleanly in the presence of Network Address Translators, others suffer from a variety of problems when crossing these devices.  Guidelines are presented herein to help ensure new protocols and applications will, to the extent possible, be compatible with NAT (Network Address Translation).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvAmaranth Networks Inc.dnamehD. Senieddatej“,hfilenamegrfc3235eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcnatdfullx!nat - Network Address TranslatorsdnamexNetwork Address Translatorsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125197hkeywordsÉdNAPTcALGhfirewallkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgranking£crfcd3235irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGNetwork Address Translator (NAT)-Friendly Application Design GuidelinesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document is intended as part of an IETF discussion about "middleboxes" - defined as any intermediary box performing functions apart from normal, standard functions of an IP router on the data path between a source host and destination host.  This document establishes a catalogue or taxonomy of middleboxes, cites previous and current IETF work concerning middleboxes, and attempts to identify some preliminary conclusions.  It does not, however, claim to be definitive.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxIBM Zurich Research LaboratorydnamelB. Carpenter¢kaffiliation`dnamegS. Brimddatej—˚hfilenamegrfc3234eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125196hkeywordsÜhinternethprotocolfrouterddatadpathdhostkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<ZK␀granking¢crfcd3234irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Middleboxes: Taxonomy and IssuesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyüThis document gives a more concrete definition of "the IETF" as it understood today.  Many RFCs refer to "the IETF".  Many important IETF documents speak of the IETF as if it were an already-defined entity.  However, no IETF document correctly defines what the IETF is.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInternet Mail ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffman¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej—˚hfilenamegrfc3233eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125195hkeywordsÑhinternetkengineeringdtaskeforcekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<ZK␀granking°crfcd3233irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber:etotaletitleqDefining the IETFdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx«This memo obsoletes RFC 1700 (STD 2) "Assigned Numbers", which contained an October 1994 snapshot of assigned Internet protocol parameters.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRFC EditordnamekJ. Reynoldsddatej“,hfilenamegrfc3232eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125194hkeywordsÜdIANAhinternethassignedgnumbersiauthorityjparameterskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgranking†crfcd3232irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex=Assigned Numbers: RFC 1700 is Replaced by an On-line DatabasedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty/This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in the Internet community.  In particular, it describes a set of managed objects that are used to schedule management operations periodically or at specified dates and times. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamegD. Levi¢kaffiliationoTU BraunschweigdnamepJ. Schoenwaelderddatej“,hfilenamegrfc3231eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfdismandfullxdisman - Distributed ManagementdnamevDistributed Managementdtypebwgbidjdoc-125193hkeywordsÇcmibxmanagement information basekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingücrfcd3231irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexCDefinitions of Managed Objects for Scheduling Management OperationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyêHTTP/1.1 defines a Content-MD5 header that allows a server to include a digest of the response body.  However, this is specifically defined to cover the body of the actual message, not the contents of the full file (which might be quite different, if the response is a Content-Range, or uses a delta encoding).  Also, the Content-MD5 is limited to one specific digest algorithm; other algorithms, such as SHA-1 (Secure Hash Standard), may be more appropriate in some circumstances.  Finally, HTTP/1.1 provides no explicit mechanism by which a client may request a digest.  This document proposes HTTP extensions that solve these problems. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationjCompaq WRLdnamehJ. Mogul¢kaffiliationgMarimbadnamekA. Van Hoffddatej“+hfilenamegrfc3230eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125192hkeywordsÇdHTTPxhypertext transfer protocolkobsoletedByÅd9530epagesopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingûcrfcd3230irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexInstance Digests in HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxtThis document describes how delta encoding can be supported as a compatible extension to HTTP/1.1. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNameiNed Freeddarea£gacronymdiesgdfullx*iesg - Internet Engineering Steering Groupdnamex#Internet Engineering Steering Groupgauthorsá¢kaffiliationjCompaq WRLdnamehJ. Mogul¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamepB. Krishnamurthy¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamejF. Douglis¢kaffiliationuUniv. of SaarbrueckendnamekA. Feldmann¢kaffiliationgMarimbadnameiY. Goland¢kaffiliationgMarimbadnamekA. van Hoff¢kaffiliationhERS/USDAdnamenD. Hellersteinddatej“+hfilenamegrfc3229eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications Areadtypedareabidjdoc-125191hkeywordsÇdHTTPxhypertext transfer protocolkobsoletedByÄepages1opublicationDate<1lÄgrankingùcrfcd3229irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlevDelta encoding in HTTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyeThis memo requests that the IANA create a registry for fields in the IGMP (Internet Group Management Protocol) protocol header, and provides guidance for the IANA to use in assigning parameters for those fields.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmAT&T ResearchdnameiB. Fennerddatej—˙hfilenamegrfc3228eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemagmadfullx,magma - Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdnamex$Multicast & Anycast Group Membershipdtypebwgbidjdoc-125190hkeywordsÉhassignedgnumbersiauthoritykobsoletedByÅd9778epagesopublicationDate<ZK␀grankingúcrfcd3228irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFIANA Considerations for IPv4 Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyÛA "security incident" as defined in the "Internet Security Glossary", RFC 2828, is a security-relevant system event in which the system's security policy is disobeyed or otherwise breached.  The purpose of this document is to provide System Administrators with guidelines on the collection and archiving of evidence relevant to such a security incident.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhIn-Q-TeldnamelD. Brezinski¢kaffiliationineart.orgdnamekT. Killaleaddatej—˙hfilenamegrfc3227eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgripdfullx-grip - G & R for Security Incident Processingdnamex&G & R for Security Incident Processingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125189hkeywordsÇhsecurityhincidentkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate<ZK␀grankingõcrfcd3227irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumber7etotaletitlex0Guidelines for Evidence Collection and ArchivingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÕThis document mandates support for EDNS0 (Extension Mechanisms for DNS) in DNS entities claiming to support either DNS Security Extensions or A6 records.  This requirement is necessary because these new features increase the size of DNS messages.  If EDNS0 is not supported fall back to TCP will happen, having a detrimental impact on query latency and DNS server load.  This document updates RFC 2535 and RFC 2874, by adding new requirements. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenO. Gudmundssonddatej—”hfilenamegrfc3226eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125188hkeywordsáfDNSSECx&domain name system security extensionsespacehsecurityjextensionseEDNS0xExtension Mechanisms for DNSExkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<é␀grankingöcrfcd3226irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexBDNSSEC and IPv6 A6 aware server/resolver message size requirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd4033d4034d4035µhabstracty≠In order to deploy DNSSEC (Domain Name System Security Extensions) operationally, DNSSEC aware servers should only perform automatic inclusion of DNSSEC RRs when there is an explicit indication that the resolver can understand those RRs.  This document proposes the use of a bit in the EDNS0 header to provide that explicit indication and describes the necessary protocol changes to implement that notification. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmNominum, Inc.dnameiD. Conradddatej—”hfilenamegrfc3225eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125187hkeywordsÇfDNSSECx&domain name system security extensionskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<é␀grankingôcrfcd3225irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Indicating Resolver Support of DNSSECdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd4033d4034d4035µhabstracty˛This document specifies how the features of the Service Location Protocol, Version 2 allow for vendor extensibility safely, with no possibility of collisions.  The specification introduces a new SLPv2 extension: The Vendor Opaque Extension.  While proprietary protocol extensions are not encouraged by IETF standards, it is important that they not hinder interoperability of compliant implementations when they are undertaken.  This document udpates RFC 2608, "The Service Location Protocol." [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttmanddatej“+hfilenamegrfc3224eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125186hkeywordsÑcSLPxService Location ProtocolfSVRLOCfopaquekobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate<1lÄgrankingòcrfcd3224irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex:Vendor Extensions for Service Location Protocol, Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyµThis document describes the terms to be used in a methodology that determines the IP packet forwarding performance of IP routers as a function of the forwarding information base installed within a router.  The forwarding performance of an IP router may be dependent upon or may be linked to the composition and size of the forwarding information base installed within a router.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtAgilent TechnologiesdnamejG. Trotterddatej—”hfilenamegrfc3222eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-125185hkeywordsÑhinternethprotocolmrouting tableibenchmarkkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<é␀grankingñcrfcd3222irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexJTerminology for Forwarding Information Base (FIB) based Router PerformancedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty£This document examines the various longer term trends visible within the characteristics of the Internet's BGP table and identifies a number of operational practices and protocol factors that contribute to these trends.  The potential impacts of these practices and protocol properties on the scaling properties of the inter-domain routing space are examined.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Architecture BoarddnameiG. Hustonddatej—”hfilenamegrfc3221eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-125184hkeywordsÑcBGPfborderggatewayhprotocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<é␀grankingïcrfcd3221irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex2Commentary on Inter-Domain Routing in the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÀThis document specifies protocol enhancements that allow transparent routing of IP datagrams to mobile nodes in the Internet.  Each mobile node is always identified by its home address, regardless of its current point of attachment to the Internet.  While situated away from its home, a mobile node is also associated with a care-of address, which provides information about its current point of attachment to the Internet.  The protocol provides for registering the care-of address with a home agent.  The home agent sends datagrams destined for the mobile node through a tunnel to the care-of address.  After arriving at the end of the tunnel, each datagram is then delivered to the mobile node. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationuNokia Research CenterdnamejC. Perkinsddatej“+hfilenamegrfc3220eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhmobileipdfullx/mobileip - IP Routing for Wireless/Mobile Hostsdnamex$IP Routing for Wireless/Mobile Hostsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125183hkeywordsÉmMOBILEIPSUPIPhInternethProtocolkobsoletedByÅd3344epagesbopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingîcrfcd3220irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexIP Mobility Support for IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¥This document presents the Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP).  TRIP is a policy driven inter-administrative domain protocol for advertising the reachability of telephony destinations between location servers, and for advertising attributes of the routes to those destinations.  TRIP's operation is independent of any signaling protocol, hence TRIP can serve as the telephony routing protocol for any signaling protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamelJ. Rosenberg¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiH. Salama¢kaffiliationqHatteras NetworksdnameiM. Squireddatej“*hfilenamegrfc3219eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymeipteldfulltiptel - IP TelephonydnamelIP Telephonydtypebwgbidjdoc-125182hkeywordsÖdTRIPxTelephony Routing over IPtinter-administrativecBGPwborder gateway protocolkobsoletedByÄepagesOopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingìcrfcd3219irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex Telephony Routing over IP (TRIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd8602µhabstractxÑThis memo describes a strategy for resisting the Million Message Attack.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjRTFM, Inc.dnamekE. Rescorladdatej“*hfilenamegrfc3218eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125181hkeywordsÇmcryptographicfsyntaxkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingícrfcd3218irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEPreventing the Million Message Attack on Cryptographic Message SyntaxdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxﬁThis document specifies the algorithm for wrapping one Triple-DES key with another Triple-DES key and the algorithm for wrapping one RC2 key with another RC2 key.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpRSA LaboratoriesdnamejR. Housleyddatej—“hfilenamegrfc3217eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125180hkeywordsÇialgorithmxdata encryption standardkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate<é␀grankingëcrfcd3217irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTriple-DES and RC2 Key WrappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty	This document describes the objectives for a new data definition language, suitable for the modeling of network management constructs, that can be directly mapped into SNMP and COPS-PR protocol operations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejC. Elliott¢kaffiliationrEnterasys NetworksdnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationqIntel CorporationdnamehJ. Jason¢kaffiliationoTU BraunschweigdnamepJ. Schoenwaelder¢kaffiliationoTU BraunschweigdnamejF. Strauss¢kaffiliationqEllacoya NetworksdnamehW. Weissddatej—“hfilenamegrfc3216eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmingdfullx;sming - Next Generation Structure of Management Informationdnamex3Next Generation Structure of Management Informationdtypebwgbidjdoc-125179hkeywordsãdSNMPfsimplegnetworkjmanagementhprotocolgCOPS-PRfcommondopenfpolicygservicelprovisioningkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate<é␀grankingêcrfcd3216irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlepSMIng ObjectivesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÖThis document provides state machine tables for ATM (Asynchronous Transfer Mode) switch LSRs.  In the current LDP specification, there is no state machine specified for processing LDP messages.  We think that defining a common state machine is very important for interoperability between different LDP and CR-LDP implementations.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnamejC. Boscher¢kaffiliationgAlcateldnameiP. Cheval¢kaffiliationeCiscodnameeL. Wu¢kaffiliationiSandburstdnamegE. Grayddatej“*hfilenamegrfc3215eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125178hkeywordsÉelabelldistributionhprotocolkobsoletedByÄepagesNopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingècrfcd3215irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitleqLDP State MachinedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document presents an approach to modify the bandwidth and possibly other parameters of an established CR-LSP (Constraint-based Routed Label Switched Paths) using CR-LDP (Constraint-based Routed Label Distribution Protocol) without service interruption. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ash¢kaffiliationpCeterus NetworksdnamefY. Lee¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamepP. Ashwood-Smith¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekB. Jamoussi¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehD. Fedyk¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekD. Skalecki¢kaffiliationlSS8 NetworksdnameeL. Liddatej“*hfilenamegrfc3214eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125177hkeywordsÖcLSPxlabel switching protocolfCR-LDPx,constraint-based routed label switched pathsldistributionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingécrfcd3214irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexLSP Modification Using CR-LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty©This document discusses the applicability of Constraint-Based LSP Setup using LDP.  It discusses possible network applications, extensions to Label Distribution Protocol (LDP) required to implement constraint-based routing, guidelines for deployment and known limitations of the protocol.  This document is a prerequisite to advancing CR-LDP on the standards track.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliationdAT&TdnamefJ. Ash¢kaffiliationmAtoga SystemsdnameiM. Girish¢kaffiliationiSandburstdnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationuNortel Networks Corp.dnamekB. Jamoussi¢kaffiliationuNortel Networks Corp.dnameiG. Wrightddatej“*hfilenamegrfc3213eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125176hkeywordsÑpconstraint-basedelabelldistributionhprotocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<1lÄgrankingçcrfcd3213irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Applicability Statement for CR-LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxøThis document specifies mechanisms and TLVs (Type/Length/Value) for support of CR-LSPs (constraint-based routed Label Switched Path) using LDP (Label Distribution Protocol). [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting Areagauthorsé¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamekB. Jamoussi¢kaffiliationiUtfors ABdnamelL. Andersson¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameiR. Callon¢kaffiliationsNetrake CorporationdnamehR. Dantu¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameeL. Wu¢kaffiliationtOTB Consulting Corp.dnameiP. Doolan¢kaffiliationgUnspec.dnamejT. Worster¢kaffiliationiIBM Corp.dnamejN. Feldman¢kaffiliationnANF ConsultingdnamekA. Fredette¢kaffiliationmAtoga SystemsdnameiM. Girish¢kaffiliationiSandburstdnamegE. Gray¢kaffiliationsSong Networks, Inc.dnamekJ. Heinanen¢kaffiliationxNewbridge Networks, Inc.dnamehT. Kilty¢kaffiliationoVivace NetworksdnamehA. Malisddatej“)hfilenamegrfc3212eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125175hkeywordsÑgCR-LSPsx+constraint-based routed Label Switched PathcLDPxLabel Distribution ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages*opublicationDate<1lÄgrankingåcrfcd3212irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex$Constraint-Based LSP Setup using LDPdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd7358d3468µhabstracty^This document provides a method of encrypting data using user-supplied passwords and, by extension, any form of variable-length keying material which is not necessarily an algorithm-specific fixed-format key.  The Cryptographic Message Syntax data format does not currently contain any provisions for password-based data encryption. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of AucklanddnamejP. Gutmannddatej—“hfilenamegrfc3211eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymesmimedfullxsmime - S/MIME Mail SecuritydnametS/MIME Mail Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-125174hkeywordsàmcryptographicgmessagefsyntaxfS/MIMEhkey wrapjderivationupasswordrecipientinfodPWRIkobsoletedByÇd3369d3370epagesopublicationDate<é␀grankingãcrfcd3211irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!Password-based Encryption for CMSdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÙThis memo discusses the applicability of "Extensions to RSVP (Resource ReSerVation Protocol) for LSP Tunnels".  It highlights the protocol's principles of operation and describes the network context for which it was designed.  Guidelines for deployment are offered and known protocol limitations are indicated.  This document is intended to accompany the submission of "Extensions to RSVP for LSP Tunnels" onto the Internet standards track.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationnMovaz NetworksdnamejD. Awduche¢kaffiliationkRoutingloopdnameiA. Hannan¢kaffiliationhPhoturisdnamegX. Xiaoddatej—“hfilenamegrfc3210eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125173hkeywordsÜhresourcekreservationhprotocolelabelhswitchedepathskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<é␀grankingäcrfcd3210irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex>Applicability Statement for Extensions to RSVP for LSP-TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty≈This document describes the use of RSVP (Resource Reservation Protocol), including all the necessary extensions, to establish label-switched paths (LSPs) in MPLS (Multi-Protocol Label Switching).  Since the flow along an LSP is completely identified by the label applied at the ingress node of the path, these paths may be treated as tunnels.  A key application of LSP tunnels is traffic engineering with MPLS as specified in RFC 2702. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÜ¢kaffiliationtMovaz Networks, Inc.dnamejD. Awduche¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnameiL. Berger¢kaffiliationvJuniper Networks, Inc.dnamefD. Gan¢kaffiliationvProcket Networks, Inc.dnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationxCosine Communications, Inc.dnamemV. Srinivasan¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamejG. Swallowddatej——hfilenamegrfc3209eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdmplsdfullx$mpls - Multiprotocol Label SwitchingdnamexMultiprotocol Label Switchingdtypebwgbidjdoc-125172hkeywordsÑdRSVPxresource reservation protocolcLSPtlabel switched pathskobsoletedByÄepages=opublicationDate<é␀grankingâcrfcd3209irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex+RSVP-TE: Extensions to RSVP for LSP TunnelsdtypecrfciupdatedByâd4874d5711d4420d5420d5151d6780d7274d3936d6790µhabstracty¢Pragmatic General Multicast (PGM) is a reliable multicast transport protocol for applications that require ordered or unordered, duplicate- free, multicast data delivery from multiple sources to multiple receivers.  PGM guarantees that a receiver in the group either receives all data packets from transmissions and repairs, or is able to detect unrecoverable data packet loss.  PGM is specifically intended as a workable solution for multicast applications with basic reliability requirements.  Its central design goal is simplicity of operation with due regard for scalability and network efficiency.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areagauthorsé¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamekT. Speakman¢kaffiliationcUCLdnamelJ. Crowcroft¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnamejJ. Gemmell¢kaffiliationpProcket NetworksdnamelD. Farinacci¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamefS. Lin¢kaffiliationnTIBCO SoftwarednamemD. Leshchiner¢kaffiliationpDigital FountaindnamegM. Luby¢kaffiliationtTalarian CorporationdnamemT. Montgomery¢kaffiliationrUniversity of PisadnamehL. Rizzo¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejA. Tweedly¢kaffiliation`dnamejN. Bhaskar¢kaffiliation`dnamemR. Edmonstone¢kaffiliation`dnameoR. Sumanasekera¢kaffiliation`dnamekL. Vicisanoddatej——hfilenamegrfc3208eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125171hkeywordsÇcPGMxpragmatic general multicastkobsoletedByÄepagesoopublicationDate<é␀grankingàcrfcd3208irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex-PGM Reliable Transport Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractynThis document describes an extension to the SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) service that allows an SMTP server and client to use TLS (Transport Layer Security) to provide private, authenticated communication over the Internet.  This gives SMTP agents the ability to protect some or all of their communications from eavesdroppers and attackers. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxInternet Mail ConsortiumdnamejP. Hoffmanddatej—˙hfilenamegrfc3207eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125170hkeywordsÖdSMTPxsimple mail transfer protocolcsslctlsxTransport Layer SecuritykobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate<ZK␀grankingácrfcd3207irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexDSMTP Service Extension for Secure SMTP over Transport Layer SecuritydtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7817µhabstractx‰This memo proposes two response codes: SYS and AUTH, which enable clients to unambiguously determine an optimal response to an authentication failure.  In addition, a new capability (AUTH-RESP-CODE) is defined. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhQUALCOMMdnamejR. Gellensddatej—˙hfilenamegrfc3206eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125169hkeywordsÇhsecuritynauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate<ZK␀grankingÜcrfcd3206irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex#The SYS and AUTH POP Response CodesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty®Recently there has been widespread interest in using Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) as a substrate for other applications-level protocols.  This document recommends technical particulars of such use, including use of default ports, URL schemes, and HTTP security mechanisms.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationwUniversity of TennesseednamehK. Mooreddatej—˙hfilenamegrfc3205eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125168hkeywordsÑihypertexthtransferhprotocolhlayeringkobsoletedByÅd9205epagesopublicationDate<ZK␀grankingÖcrfcd3205irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex!On the use of HTTP as a SubstratedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyäThis document describes MIME types for application/ISUP and application/QSIG objects for use in SIP applications, according to the rules defined in RFC 2048.  These types can be used to identify ISUP and QSIG objects within a SIP message such as INVITE or INFO, as might be implemented when using SIP in an environment where part of the call involves interworking to the PSTN. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcraidfullx4rai - Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areadnamex.Real-time Applications and Infrastructure Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationlRankom, Inc.dnamekE. Zimmerer¢kaffiliationmNeustar, Inc.dnamekJ. Peterson¢kaffiliationtQwest CommunicationsdnameiA. Vemuri¢kaffiliationnCiena NetworksdnamefL. Ong¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnamehF. Audet¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Watson¢kaffiliationoNortel NetworksdnameiM. Zonounddatej——hfilenamegrfc3204eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcsipdfullx!sip - Session Initiation ProtocoldnamexSession Initiation Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-125167hkeywordsÇdMIMEx"multipart internet mail extensionskobsoletedByÄepages
