an Electronic Commerce Modeling Language (ECML) so that this task can be more easily automated, for example by wallet software that could fill in fields.  Even for the manual data entry case, customers will be less confused by varying merchant sites if a substantial number adopt these standard fields.  In addition, some fields are defined for merchant to consumer communication.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationfBrodiadnamelT. Goldsteinddatej—Ähfilenamegrfc3106eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125071hkeywordsÉjelectronichmodelinghlanguagekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀granking"crfcd3106irfcNumber"estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex.ECML v1.1: Field Specifications for E-CommercedtypecrfciupdatedByÅd4112µhabstractx∆This document contains an SLP service type template that describes the advertisements made by RSIP servers for their services.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationsNTT DoCoMo USA LabsdnamehJ. Kempf¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamemG. Montenegroddatej”hfilenamegrfc3105eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcnatdfullx!nat - Network Address TranslatorsdnamexNetwork Address Translatorsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125070hkeywordsádRSIPx realm specific internet protocolcSLPxservice location protocolcNATjaddressinglrequirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate;∏pgranking!crfcd3105irfcNumber!estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexFinding an RSIP Server with SLPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx∏This document proposes mechanisms that enable Realm Specific IP (RSIP) to handle end-to-end IPsec (IP Security).  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationvSun Microsystems, Inc.dnamemG. Montenegro¢kaffiliationjM. BorelladnamejM. Borelladdatej”hfilenamegrfc3104eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcnatdfullx!nat - Network Address TranslatorsdnamexNetwork Address Translatorsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125069hkeywordsÖdRSIPx realm specific internet protocolcNATjaddressinglrequirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate;∏pgranking crfcd3104irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex!RSIP Support for End-to-end IPsecdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractysThis document presents a protocol with which to implement Realm Specific IP (RSIP).  The protocol defined herein allows negotiation of resources between an RSIP host and gateway, so that the host can lease some of the gateway's addressing parameters in order to establish a global network presence.  This protocol is designed to operate on the application layer and to use its own TCP or UDP port.  In particular, the protocol allows a gateway to allocate addressing and control parameters to a host such that a flow policy can be enforced at the gateway.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiCommWorksdnamejM. Borella¢kaffiliationiCommWorksdnamelD. Grabelsky¢kaffiliationtCandlestick NetworksdnameeJ. Lo¢kaffiliationgNEC USAdnamelK. Taniguchiddatej”hfilenamegrfc3103eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcnatdfullx!nat - Network Address TranslatorsdnamexNetwork Address Translatorsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125068hkeywordsÜdRSIPx Realm Specific Internet ProtocoldhostggatewaycNATlrequirementskobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDate;∏pgrankingcrfcd3103irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)Realm Specific IP: Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractydThis document examines the general framework of Realm Specific IP (RSIP).  RSIP is intended as a alternative to NAT in which the end-to- end integrity of packets is maintained.  We focus on implementation issues, deployment scenarios, and interaction with other layer-three protocols.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationiCommWorksdnamejM. Borella¢kaffiliationtCandlestick NetworksdnameeJ. Lo¢kaffiliationiCommWorksdnamelD. Grabelsky¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamemG. Montenegroddatej“Ÿhfilenamegrfc3102eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcnatdfullx!nat - Network Address TranslatorsdnamexNetwork Address Translatorsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125067hkeywordsÜdRSIPx Realm Specific Internet Protocoljend-to-endcNATjaddressinglrequirementskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate;∏pgrankingcrfcd3102irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexRealm Specific IP: FrameworkdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyoThis memo documents an optional type of Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) area that is somewhat humorously referred to as a "not-so-stubby" area (or NSSA).  NSSAs are similar to the existing OSPF stub area configuration option but have the additional capability of importing AS external routes in a limited fashion.  The OSPF NSSA Option was originally defined in RFC 1587.  The functional differences between this memo and RFC 1587 are explained in Appendix F.  All differences, while expanding capability, are backward-compatible in nature.  Implementations of this memo and of RFC 1587 will interoperate. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamekBill Fennerdarea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationtUS Geological SurveydnameiP. Murphyddatej“-hfilenamegrfc3101eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdospfdfullx#ospf - Open Shortest Path First IGPdnamexOpen Shortest Path First IGPdtypebwgbidjdoc-125066hkeywordsÖiOSPF-NSSAxOpen Shortest Path FirstrNot-So-Stubby Areaoexternal routessbackward compatiblekobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate>5íSgrankingcrfcd3101irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)The OSPF Not-So-Stubby Area (NSSA) OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstract`darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiS. Ginozaddatej ˇhfilenamegrfc3099eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43426hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate;·␀grankingcrfcd3099irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex2Request for Comments Summary RFC Numbers 3000-3099dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This memo offers useful suggestions for responsible advertising techniques that can be used via the internet in an environment where the advertiser, recipients, and the Internet Community can coexist in a productive and mutually respectful fashion.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcusvdfullxusv - User Services AreadnamerUser Services AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationwNachman Hays ConsultingdnamehT. Gavin¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationeInteldnamelS. Hambridgeddatej—hfilenamegrfc3098eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrundfullx$run - Responsible Use of the NetworkdnamexResponsible Use of the Networkdtypebwgbidjdoc-125065hkeywordsÜhinternetimarketingeusersgserviceiprovidersdispskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3098irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdiet