—hfilenamegrfc3094eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125061hkeywordsÜisignalingfgatewagcircuitgnetworkhinternethprotocolkobsoletedByÄepagesjopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3094irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+Tekelec's Transport Adapter Layer InterfacedtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstracty∏Internet Transparency via the end-to-end architecture of the Internet has allowed vast innovation of new technologies and services [1].  However, recent developments in Firewall technology have altered this model and have been shown to inhibit innovation.  We propose the Firewall Enhancement Protocol (FEP) to allow innovation, without violating the security model of a Firewall.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnameiM. Gaynor¢kaffiliationrHarvard UniversitydnamejS. Bradnerddatej»˙hfilenamegrfc3093eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43425hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3093irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex#Firewall Enhancement Protocol (FEP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyApproximately 212 RFCs so far, starting with RFC 269, contain the terms `foo', `bar', or `foobar' as metasyntactic variables without any proper explanation or definition.  This document rectifies that deficiency.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnameoD. Eastlake 3rd¢kaffiliationeXeroxdnameiC. Manros¢kaffiliationvOpen Source InitiativednamejE. Raymondddatej»˘hfilenamegrfc3092eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43424hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3092irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlerEtymology of "Foo"dtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractxøThis memo defines a protocol to provide the Pi digit generation service (PIgen) used between clients and servers on host computers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of MichigandnamejH. Kennedyddatej»«hfilenamegrfc3091eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43423hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3091irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexPi Digit Generation ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÊThe definition of a secured zone is presented, clarifying and updating sections of RFC 2535.  RFC 2535 defines a zone to be secured based on a per algorithm basis, e.g., a zone can be secured with RSA keys, and not secured with DSA keys.  This document changes this to define a zone to be secured or not secured regardless of the key algorithm used (or not used).  To further simplify the determination of a zone's status, "experimentally secure" status is deprecated. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationhNAI LabsdnamehE. Lewisddatej“£hfilenamegrfc3090eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfdnsextdfullwdnsext - DNS ExtensionsdnamenDNS Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-125060hkeywordsÖfdomaindnamefsystemcrsacdsakobsoletedByÉd4033d4034d4035epagesopublicationDate:ûÄgrankingcrfcd3090irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex3DNS Security Extension Clarification on Zone StatusdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3658µhabstractx’This document describes a SOCKS-based IPv6/IPv4 gateway mechanism that enables smooth heterogeneous communications between the IPv6 nodes and IPv4 nodes.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoNEC CorporationdnamekH. Kitamuraddatej—hfilenamegrfc3089eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgngtransdfullx$ngtrans - Next Generation TransitiondnamexNext Generation Transitiondtypebwgbidjdoc-125059hkeywordsÑhinternethprotocolkapplicationelayerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3089irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)A SOCKS-based IPv6/IPv4 Gateway MechanismdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty´The OpenLDAP Project is operating an experimental LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol) referral service known as the "OpenLDAP Root Service".  The automated system generates referrals based upon service location information published in DNS SRV RRs (Domain Name System location of services resource records).  This document describes this service.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsOpenLDAP FoundationdnamekK. Zeilengaddatej—hfilenamegrfc3088eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125058hkeywordsÑhOpenLDAPx%lightweight directory access protocolcdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3088irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex;OpenLDAP Root Service An experimental LDAP referral servicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyjThis memo describes a useful way to conceptualize the use of the standard SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) Request-URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) that the authors and many members of the SIP community think is suitable as a convention.  It does not define any new protocol with respect to RFC 2543.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnamekB. Campbell¢kaffiliationkdynamicsoftdnameiR. Sparksddatej—hfilenamegrfc3087eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-125057hkeywordsÜgsessionjinitiationhprotocolguniformhresourcejidentifierkobsoletedByÄepages'opublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3087irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex0Control of Service Context using SIP Request-URIdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyãThis document defines and discusses Per-Domain Behaviors in detail and lays out the format and required content for contributions to the Diffserv WG on PDBs and the procedure that will be applied for individual PDB specifications to advance as WG products.  This format is specified to expedite working group review of PDB submissions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymctsvdfullttsv - Transport AreadnamenTransport AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationmPacket DesigndnamejK. Nichols¢kaffiliationcIBMdnamelB. Carpenterddatej—hfilenamegrfc3086eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymhdiffservdfullx"diffserv - Differentiated ServicesdnamewDifferentiated Servicesdtypebwgbidjdoc-125056hkeywordsÉhdiffservcQoSrquality of servicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate:∆‡␀grankingcrfcd3086irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex\Definition of Differentiated Services Per Domain Behaviors and Rules for their SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxùThis document describes a URN (Uniform Resource Name) 