fect of multipath routing on a forwarder is that the forwarder potentially has several next-hops for any given destination and must use some method to choose which next-hop should be used for a given data packet.  This memo summarizes current practices, problems, and solutions.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationiMicrosoftdnameiD. Thaler¢kaffiliationtNextHop TechnologiesdnamehC. Hoppsddatej“Âhfilenamegrfc2991eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124964hkeywordsÑgroutingjforwardinggpacketsdECMPkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate9ˇÕÄgrankingØcrfcd2991irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex<Multipath Issues in Unicast and Multicast Next-Hop SelectiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractyìThis document highlights the outstanding architectural issues relating to the deployment and use of QoS mechanisms within internet networks, noting those areas where further standards work may assist with the deployment of QoS internets.  This document is the outcome of a collaborative exercise on the part of the Internet Architecture Board.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgTelstradnameiG. Hustonddatej“Âhfilenamegrfc2990eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciabdfullx!iab - Internet Architecture BoarddnamexInternet Architecture Boarddtypedietfbidjdoc-124963hkeywordsÑhinternethprotocolrquality of servicejend-to-endkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate9ˇÕÄgrankingÆcrfcd2990irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecIABdslugciabetitlex&Next Steps for the IP QoS ArchitecturedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxªThis document represents a summary of Authentication, Authorization, Accounting (AAA) protocol requir