community's expectations of Computer Security Incident Response
Teams (CSIRTs). It is not possible to define a set of requirements
that would be appropriate for all teams, but it is possible and
helpful to list and describe the general set of topics and issues
which are of concern and interest to constituent communities.

CSIRT constituents have a legitimate need and right to fully
understand the policies and procedures of 'their' Computer Security
Incident Response Team.  One way to support this understanding is to
supply detailed information which users may consider, in the form of
a formal template completed by the CSIRT.  An outline of such a
template and a filled in example are provided.darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxThe University of AucklanddnamekN. Brownlee¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttmanddatej“_hfilenamegrfc2350eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdgripdfullx-grip - G & R for Security Incident Processingdnamex&G & R for Security Incident Processingdtypebwgbidjdoc-124346hkeywordsÉeCSIRTjguidelinesduserkobsoletedByÄepages&opublicationDate5rQpgranking	.crfcd2350irfcNumber	.estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex4Expectations for Computer Security Incident ResponsedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÕThe Trivial File Transfer Protocol is a simple, lock-step, file transfer protocol which allows a client to get or put a file onto a remote host.  This document describes two TFTP options. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlBay NetworksdnameiG. Malkin¢kaffiliationxInternet Services ProjectdnameiA. Harkinddatej“∫hfilenamegrfc2349eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124345hkeywordsÜhTFTP-Optxtrivial file transfer protocolgbootingfclientfserveriextensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate5Irgranking	-crfcd2349irfcNumber	-estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex/TFTP Timeout Interval and Transfer Size OptionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty,The Trivial File Transfer Protocol is a simple, lock-step, file transfer protocol which allows a client to get or put a file onto a remote host.  This document describes a TFTP option which allows the client and server to negotiate a blocksize more applicable to the network medium. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlBay NetworksdnameiG. Malkin¢kaffiliationxInternet Services ProjectdnameiA. Harkinddatej“∫hfilenamegrfc2348eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124344hkeywordsÜhTFTP-Blkxtrivial file transfer protocolgbootingfclientfserveriextensionkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate5Irgranking	,crfcd2348irfcNumber	,estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitleuTFTP Blocksize OptiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThe Trivial File Transfer Protocol is a simple, lock-step, file transfer protocol which allows a client to get or put a file onto a remote host.  This document describes a simple extension to TFTP to allow option negotiation prior to the file transfer. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationlBay NetworksdnameiG. Malkin¢kaffiliationxInternet Services ProjectdnameiA. Harkinddatej“∫hfilenamegrfc2347eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124343hkeywordsÜhTFTP-Extxtrivial file transfer protocoliextensiongbootingfclientfserverkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate5Irgranking	+crfcd2347irfcNumber	+estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitleuTFTP Option ExtensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyCertain text formats, for example Postscript (MIME-Type: application/postscript; file extension .ps) and Portable Document Format (MIME-Type: application/pdf; file extension .pdf) specify exactly the page layout of the printed document.  The commonly used paper format is different in North America and the rest of the world.  North America uses the 'Letter' format, while the rest of the world mostly uses the ISO-standard 'A4' format.  This means that documents formatted on one continent may not be easily printable on another continent.  This memo gives advice on how to produce documents which are equally well printable with the Letter and the A4 formats.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxStockholm University and KTHdnamehJ. Palmeddatej“πhfilenamegrfc2346eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124342hkeywordsÉhportablehdocumentfformatkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate5Irgranking	*crfcd2346irfcNumber	*estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex'Making Postscript and PDF InternationaldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÓThis document proposes a company name to URL mapping service based on the oldest and least complex of Internet directory protocols, whois, in order to explore whether an extremely simple and widely-deployed protocol can succeed where more complex and powerful options have failed or been excessively delayed.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.  Discussion and suggestions for improvement are requested.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxMCI Internet ArchitecturednamejJ. Klensin¢kaffiliationsElectronic CommercednamegT. Wolf¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. Oglesbyddatej“πhfilenamegrfc2345eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124341hkeywordsÜcURLgmappinggserviceewhoiscdnsrdomain name systemkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate5Irgranking	)crfcd2345irfcNumber	)estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex'Domain 