ue management in routers, to improve the performance of today's Internet.  It also urges a concerted effort of research, measurement, and ultimate deployment of router mechanisms to protect the Internet from flows that are not sufficiently responsive to congestion notification.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral Areagauthorsè¢kaffiliationgUSC/ISIdnameiB. Braden¢kaffiliationcMITdnamehD. Clark¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamelJ. Crowcroft¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehB. Davie¢kaffiliationx%Lawrence Berkeley National LaboratorydnamejS. Deering¢kaffiliationxFiberlane CommunicationsdnameiD. Estrin¢kaffiliationpBBN TechnologiesdnamehS. Floyd¢kaffiliationuUniversity of ArizonadnamekV. Jacobson¢kaffiliationsAT&T Labs. ResearchdnamekG. Minshall¢kaffiliationjXerox PARCdnamelC. Partridge¢kaffiliationdUCLAdnamekL. Peterson¢kaffiliation`dnameoK. Ramakrishnan¢kaffiliation`dnamejS. Shenker¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ. Wroclawski¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Zhangddatej—shfilenamegrfc2309eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124310hkeywordsÉkperformancefrouterjdeploymentkobsoletedByÅd7567epagesopublicationDate5!Ù␀granking	crfcd2309irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexLRecommendations on Queue Management and Congestion Avoidance in the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd7141µhabstractynRFC1034 provided a description of how to cache negative responses.  It however had a fundamental flaw in that it did not allow a name server to hand out those cached responses to other resolvers, thereby greatly reducing the effect of the caching.  This document addresses issues raise in the light of experience and replaces RFC1034 Section 4.3.4. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfull