 Authoring and Versioningdnamex(WWW Distributed Authoring and Versioningdtypebwgbidjdoc-124294hkeywordsÖcWWWfremotegeditingglockingimechanismkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4‘+ÄgrankingÛcrfcd2291irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexWRequirements for a Distributed Authoring and Versioning Protocol for the World Wide WebdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty©Mobile IP [RFC 2002] defines media-independent procedures by which a Mobile Node can maintain existing transport and application-layer connections despite changing its point-of-attachment to the Internet and without changing its IP address.  PPP [RFC 1661] provides a standard method for transporting multi-protocol packets over point-to-point links.  As currently specified, Mobile IP Foreign Agents which support Mobile Node connections via PPP can do so only by first assigning unique addresses to those Mobile Nodes, defeating one of the primary advantages of Foreign Agents.  This documents corrects this problem by defining the Mobile-IPv4 Configuration Option to the Internet Protocol Control Protocol (IPCP) [RFC 1332].  Using this option, two peers can communicate their support for Mobile IP during the IPCP phase of PPP.  Familiarity with Mobile IP [RFC 2002], IPCP [RFC 1332], and PPP [RFC 1661] is assumed. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationhMotoroladnamejJ. Solomon¢kaffiliationlFTP SoftwarednamehS. Glassddatej—Óhfilenamegrfc2290eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-124293hkeywordsÜkMobile-IPv4gaddresscPPPwPoint-to-Point ProtocoldIPCPx"Internet Protocol Control ProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4‘+ÄgrankingÚcrfcd2290irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Mobile-IPv4 Configuration Option for PPP IPCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd2794∂habstractyThis document describes a one-time password authentication system (OTP).  The system provides authentication for system access (login) and other applications requiring authentication that is secure against passive attacks based on replaying captured reusable passwords. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationhBellcorednameiN. Haller¢kaffiliationxKaman Sciences CorporationdnamegC. Metz¢kaffiliationxNesser & Nesser ConsultingdnameiP. Nesser¢kaffiliationhBellcorednamehM. Strawddatej—Óhfilenamegrfc2289eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcotpdfullx&otp - One Time Password Authenticationdnamex One Time Password Authenticationdtypebwgbidjdoc-124292hkeywordsÖhONE-PASSx'one-time password authentication systemcOTPfreplayfattachkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4‘+ÄgrankingÒcrfcd2289irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber=etotaletitlexA One-Time Password SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty$This document discusses how three major bibliographic identifiers (the ISBN, ISSN and SICI) can be supported within the URN framework and the currently proposed syntax for URNs.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx#Coalition for Networked InformationdnamehC. Lynch¢kaffiliationoPreston & LynchdnamejC. Preston¢kaffiliationxLos Alamos National LaboratorydnameiR. Danielddatej—Óhfilenamegrfc2288eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-124291hkeywordsÉdURNsfSyntaxiframeworkkobsoletedByÄepages
