MAC-RIPEMD160 and HMAC-RIPEMD128dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractymThis document is intended to provide terminology for the benchmarking of local area network (LAN) switching devices.  It extends the terminology already defined for benchmarking network interconnect devices in RFCs 1242 and 1944 to switching devices.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxEuropean Network LaboratoriesdnamemR. Mandevilleddatej—Óhfilenamegrfc2285eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdbmwgdfullxbmwg - Benchmarking MethodologydnamexBenchmarking Methodologydtypebwgbidjdoc-124288hkeywordsàelocaldareagnetworkcMACfMediumfAccessgControlelayerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4‘+ÄgrankingÌcrfcd2285irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex2Benchmarking Terminology for LAN Switching DevicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty£The Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) provides a standard method for transporting multi-protocol datagrams over point-to-point links.  PPP also defines an extensible Link Control Protocol, which allows negotiation of an Authentication Protocol for authenticating its peer before allowing Network Layer protocols to transmit over the link.  This document defines the PPP Extensible Authentication Protocol. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationrMerit Network, IncdnamehL. Blunk¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ. Vollbrechtddatej“ìhfilenamegrfc2284eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfpppextdfullx+pppext - Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdnamex"Point-to-Point Protocol Extensionsdtypebwgbidjdoc-124287hkeywordsÉgPPP-EAPnpoint-to-pointnauthenticationkobsoletedByÅd3748epagesopublicationDate4˘ÄgrankingÏcrfcd2284irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,PPP Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd2484µhabstracty9This document defines extensions to BGP-4 to enable it to carry routing information for multiple Network Layer protocols (e.g., IPv6, IPX, etc...).  The extensions are backward compatible - a router that supports the extensions can interoperate with a router that doesn't support the extensions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamehT. Bates¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejR. Chandra¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegD. Katz¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejY. Rekhterddatej—Ìhfilenamegrfc2283eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-124286hkeywordsáiMEXT-BGP4fBorderggatewayhprotocolfroutergnetworkelayerkobsoletedByÅd2858epages	opublicationDate4‘+ÄgrankingÎcrfcd2283irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex"Multiprotocol Extensions for BGP-4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx˙The process by which the members of the IAB and IESG are selected, confirmed, and recalled is specified.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqeList eXpress LLCdnameiJ. Galvinddatej—Ìhfilenamegrfc2282eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgpoissondfullx@poisson - Process for Organization of Internet Standards ONgoingdnamex6Process for Organization of Internet Standards ONgoingdtypebwgbidjdoc-124285hkeywordsÜhInternetlArchitectureeBoardkEngineeringhSteeringeGroupkobsoletedByÅd2727epagesopublicationDate4‘+ÄgrankingÍcrfcd2282irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexkIAB and IESG Selection, Confirmation, and Recall Process: Operation of the Nominating and Recall CommitteesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyQThe memo specifies the Hot Standby Router Protocol (HSRP).  The goal of the protocol is to allow hosts to appear to use a single router and to maintain connectivity even if the actual first hop router they are using fails.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnameeT. Li¢kaffiliationpJuniper NetworksdnamegB. Cole¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameiP. Morton¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnameeD. Liddatej“ìhfilenamegrfc2281eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124284hkeywordsÅdHSRPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4˘ÄgrankingÈcrfcd2281irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex(Cisco Hot Standby Router Protocol (HSRP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis memo is the reference document for the Routing Policy Specification Language (RPSL).  RPSL allows a network operator to be able to specify routing policies at various levels in the Internet hierarchy; for example at the Autonomous System (AS) level.  At the same time, policies can be specified with sufficient detail in RPSL so that low level router configurations can be generated from them.  RPSL is extensible; new routing protocols and new protocol features can be introduced at any time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcopsdfullx$ops - Operations and Management AreadnamexOperations and Management Areagauthorsá¢kaffiliationx"USC Information Sciences InstitutednameoC. Alaettinoglu¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamehT. Bates¢kaffiliation`dnameiE. Gerich¢kaffiliation`dnamemD. Karrenberg¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Meyer¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Terpstra¢kaffiliation`dnamemC. Villamizarddatej“hfilenamegrfc2280eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcrpsdfullxrps - Routing Policy SystemdnameuRouting Policy Systemdtypebwgbidjdoc-124283hkeywordsádRPSLgnetworkhoperatorbASjautonomousfsystemhdatabasekobsoletedByÅd2622epages5opublicationDate4´M␀grankingËcrfcd2280irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex,Routing Policy Specification Language (RPSL)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractynUTF-8, the object of this memo, has the characteristic of preserving the full US-ASCII range, providing compatibility with file systems, parsers and other software that rely on US-ASCII values but are transparent to other values.  This memo updates and replaces RFC 2044, in particular addressing the question of versions of the relevant standards. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationqAlis TechnologiesdnamejF. Yergeauddatej“hfilenamegrfc2279eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124282hkeywordsÑeUTF-8cUCSnTransformationfFormatkobsoletedByÅd3629epages
opublicationDate4´M␀grankingÁcrfcd2279irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+UTF-8, a transformation format of ISO 10646dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty,MIME [RFC-2045, RFC-2046, RFC-2047, RFC-2184] and various other modern Internet protocols are capable of using many different charsets.  This in turn means that the ability to label different charsets is essential.  This registration procedure exists solely to associate a specific name or names with a given charset and to give an indication of whether or not a given charset can be used in MIME text objects.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxInnosoft International, Inc.dnamehN. Freed¢kaffiliationx"USC/Information Sciences InstitutednameiJ. Postelddatej“hfilenamegrfc2278eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124281hkeywordsáicharactercsetdmimelmultipurposehinternetdmailjextensionskobsoletedByÅd2978epages
opublicationDate4´M␀grankingÊcrfcd2278irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex$IANA Charset Registration ProceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyâThis document is the current policies being applied by the Internet Engineering Steering Group (IESG) towards the standardization efforts in the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) in order to help Internet protocols fulfill these requirements.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationgUNINETTdnamemH. Alvestrandddatej“hfilenamegrfc2277eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124280hkeywordsÅgcharsetkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate4´M␀grankingÂcrfcd2277irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex+IETF Policy on Character Sets and LanguagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyTThis document addresses the issues of the discovery of URN (Uniform Resource Name) resolver services that in turn will directly translate URNs into URLs (Uniform Resource Locators) and URCs (Uniform Resource Characteristics).  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx#MIT Laboratory for Computer SciencednamejK. Sollinsddatej“hfilenamegrfc2276eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-124279hkeywordsádURCscURNdURLsgUniformhResourcehLocatorsoCharacteristicskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4´M␀granking‰crfcd2276irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex<Architectural Principles of Uniform Resource Name ResolutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3401µhabstractyPThis document describes the View-based Access Control Model for use in the SNMP architecture [RFC2261].  It defines the Elements of Procedure for controlling access to management information.  This document also includes a MIB for remotely managing the configuration parameters for the View-based Access Control Model. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxIBM T. J. Watson ResearchdnameiB. Wijnen¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhn¢kaffiliationsCisco Systems, Inc.dnamemK. McCloghrieddatej“hfilenamegrfc2275eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43356hkeywordsÇfSNMPV3lArchitecturekobsoletedByÅd2575epages$opublicationDate4´M␀granking„crfcd2275irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexXView-based Access Control Model (VACM) for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyWThis document describes the User-based Security Model (USM) for SNMP version 3 for use in the SNMP architecture [RFC2261].  It defines the Elements of Procedure for providing SNMP message level security.  This document also includes a MIB for remotely monitoring/managing the configuration parameters for this Security Model. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxIBM T. J. Watson ResearchdnamemU. Blumenthal¢kaffiliationxIBM T. J. Watson ResearchdnameiB. Wijnenddatej“hfilenamegrfc2274eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43355hkeywordsÉlarchitecturegmessageelevelkobsoletedByÅd2574epagesLopublicationDate4´M␀granking‚crfcd2274irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex`User-based Security Model (USM) for version 3 of the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMPv3)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty§This memo describes five types of SNMP applications which make use of an SNMP engine as described in [RFC2261].  The types of application described are Command Generators, Command Responders, Notification Originators, Notification Receivers, and Proxy Forwarders.  This memo also defines MIB modules for specifying targets of management operations, for notification filtering, and for proxy forwarding. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationsSNMP Research, Inc.dnamegD. Levi¢kaffiliationxSecure Computing CorporationdnamehP. Meyer¢kaffiliationmCisco SystemsdnamejB. Stewartddatej“hfilenamegrfc2273eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43354hkeywordsâfSimplegNetworkjManagementhProtocoljoperationslnotificationifilteringeproxyjforwardingkobsoletedByÅd2573epagesFopublicationDate4´M␀granking·crfcd2273irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlesSNMPv3 ApplicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty£This document describes the Message Processing and Dispatching for SNMP messages within the SNMP architecture [RFC2271].  It defines the procedures for dispatching potentially multiple versions of SNMP messages to the proper SNMP Message Processing Models, and for dispatching PDUs to SNMP applications.  This document also describes one Message Processing Model - the SNMPv3 Message Processing Model. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsSNMP Research, Inc.dnamegJ. Case¢kaffiliationwCabletron Systems, Inc.dnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnamejR. Presuhn¢kaffiliationxIBM T. J. Watson ResearchdnameiB. Wijnenddatej“hfilenamegrfc2272eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43353hkeywordsÑfSNMPv3larchitecturehmultiplehversionskobsoletedByÅd2572epages'opublicationDate4´M␀granking‡crfcd2272irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexTMessage Processing and Dispatching for the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx—This document describes an architecture for describing SNMP Management Frameworks.  The architecture is designed to be modular to allow the evolution of the SNMP protocol standards over time. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxIBM T.J. Watson ResearchdnamemD. Harrington¢kaffiliationvCabletron Systems, IncdnamejR. Presuhn¢kaffiliationrBMC Software, Inc.dnameiB. Wijnenddatej“hfilenamegrfc2271eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43352hkeywordsâfSimplegNetworkjManagementhProtocolgMessagehsecurityfaccessgcontrolfsnmpv3kobsoletedByÅd2571epages8opublicationDate4´M␀grankingﬂcrfcd2271irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex9An Architecture for Describing SNMP Management FrameworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty≠With the increased growth of the Internet, the number of customers using BGP4 has grown significantly.  RFC1930 outlines a set of guidelines for when one needs and should use an AS.  However, the customer and service provider (ISP) are left with a problem as a result of this in that while there is no need for an allocated AS under the guidelines, certain conditions make the use of BGP4 a very pragmatic and perhaps only way to connect a customer homed to a single ISP.  This paper proposes a solution to this problem in line with recommendations set forth in RFC1930.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcISIdnamejJ. Stewart¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamehT. Bates¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamejR. Chandra¢kaffiliationeCiscodnamegE. Chenddatej“hfilenamegrfc2270eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-124278hkeywordsâjAutonomousfSystemdBGP4fBordergGatewayhProtocolcISPhInternetgServicekobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4´M␀grankingﬁcrfcd2270irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex:Using a Dedicated AS for Sites  Homed to a Single ProviderdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx⁄This document is intended to state the obvious equivalences, and explain the less obvious implications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsLucent TechnologiesdnamekG. Armitageddatej“hfilenamegrfc2269eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymciondfullxion - Internetworking Over NBMAdnamexInternetworking Over NBMAdtypebwgbidjdoc-124277hkeywordsälAsynchronoushTransferdModeiMulticastgAddressjResolutionfServerbIPhInternethProtocolkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4´M␀granking›crfcd2269irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex-Using the MARS Model in non-ATM NBMA NetworksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis memo describes a conventional (secret-key) block encryption algorithm, called RC2, which may be considered as a proposal for a DES replac