his memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationxNTT Software LaboratoriesdnamekK. Murakami¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Maruyamaddatej“^hfilenamegrfc2171eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124184hkeywordsÅkMAPOS-SONETkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate3ëgranking{crfcd2171irfcNumber{estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex9MAPOS - Multiple Access Protocol over SONET/SDH Version 1dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÅThis document specifies a method for allowing ATM-attached hosts that have direct ATM connectivity to set up end-to-end IP over ATM connections within the reachable ATM cloud, on request from applications, and for the exclusive use by the requesting applications.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx%Swiss Federal Institute of TechnologydnamenW. Almesberger¢kaffiliation`dnamelJ. Le Boudec¢kaffiliation`dnamekP. Oechslinddatej“=hfilenamegrfc2170eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124183hkeywordsÇhInternethProtocolkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate3∏™grankingzcrfcd2170irfcNumberzestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex,Application REQuested IP over ATM (AREQUIPA)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty˚The Uniform Resource Names Working Group (URN-WG) was formed to specify persistent, location-independent names for network accessible resources, as well as resolution mechanisms to retrieve the resources given such a name. At this time the URN-WG is considering one particular resolution mechanism, the NAPTR proposal [1]. That proposal specifies how a client may find a "resolver" for a URN. A resolver is a database that can provide information about the resource identified by a URN, such as the resource's location, a bibliographic description, or even the resource itself. The protocol used for the client to communicate with the resolver is not specified in the NAPTR proposal. Instead, the NAPTR resource record provides a field that indicates the "resolution protocol" and "resolution service requests" offered by the resolver.

This document specifies the "THTTP" resolution protocol - a trivial convention for encoding resolution service requests and responses as HTTP 1.0 or 1.1 requests and responses. The primary goal of THTTP is to be simple to implement so that existing HTTP servers may easily add support for URN resolution. We expect that the databases used by early resolvers will be useful when more sophisticated resolution protocols are developed later.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Danielddatej 4hfilenamegrfc2169eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-124182hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate3ëgrankingycrfcd2169irfcNumberyestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex5A Trivial Convention for using HTTP in URN ResolutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty|The requirements document for URN resolution systems defines the concept of a "resolver discovery service".  This document describes the first, experimental, RDS.  It is implemented by a new DNS Resource Record, NAPTR (Naming Authority PoinTeR), that provides rules for mapping parts of URIs to domain names.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Daniel¢kaffiliation`dnamekM. Meallingddatej 4hfilenamegrfc2168eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymcurndfullxurn - Uniform Resource NamesdnamevUniform Resource Namesdtypebwgbidjdoc-124181hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÑd3401d3402d3403d3404epagesopublicationDate3ëgrankingxcrfcd2168irfcNumberxestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexGResolution of Uniform Resource Identifiers using the Domain Name SystemdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd2915µhabstracty3This memo describes Version 1.5 of the client/server interaction of RWhois.  RWhois provides a distributed system for the discovery, retrieval, and maintenance of directory information.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamemS. Williamson¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Kosters¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Blacka¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Singh¢kaffiliation`dnamekK. Zeilstraddatej“^hfilenamegrfc2167eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124180hkeywordsÅfRWHOISkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDate3ëgrankingwcrfcd2167irfcNumberwestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex%Referral Whois (RWhois) Protocol V1.5dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty/This document specifies a set of extensions to RFC 1795 designed to improve the scalability of DLSw clarifications to RFC 1795 in the light of the implementation experience to-date.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Bryant¢kaffiliation`dnamekP. Brittainddatej 0hfilenamegrfc2166eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-124179hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate3ëgrankingvcrfcd2166irfcNumbervestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexHAPPN Implementer's Workshop Closed Pages Document DLSw v2.0 EnhancementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyØThe Service Location Protocol provides a scalable framework for the discovery and selection of network services.  Using this protocol, computers using the Internet no longer need so much static configuration of network services for network based applications.  This is especially important as computers become more portable, and users less tolerant or able to fulfill the demands of network system administration. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationm@Home NetworkdnamekJ. Veizades¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejE. Guttman¢kaffiliation`dnamejC. Perkins¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Kaplanddatej“^hfilenamegrfc2165eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymfsvrlocdfullx"svrloc - Service Location ProtocoldnamexService Location Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-124178hkeywordsÅcSLPkobsoletedByÄepagesHopublicationDate3ëgrankingucrfcd2165irfcNumberuestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexService Location ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÇd2608d2609µhabstractx≠This specification defines how to represent and maintain these mappings (MIXER Conformant Global Address Mappings of MCGAMs) in an X.500 or LDAP directory. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationjIsode Ltd.dnamehS. Killeddatej“hfilenamegrfc2164eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemixerdfullxmixer - MIME - X.400 GatewaydnametMIME - X.400 Gatewaydtypebwgbidjdoc-124177hkeywordsäklightweightidirectoryfaccesshprotocoldmimehinternetaxd.400henhancederelaykobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate4´M␀grankingtcrfcd2164irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex?Use of an X.500/LDAP directory to support MIXER address mappingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis memo is the complete technical specification to store in the Internet Domain Name System (DNS) the mapping information (MCGAM) needed by MIXER conformant e-mail gateways and other tools to map RFC822 domain names into X.400 O/R names and vice versa. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsSincrotrone TriestednamelC. Allocchioddatej“hfilenamegrfc2163eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymemixerdfullxmixer - MIME - X.400 GatewaydnametMIME - X.400 Gatewaydtypebwgbidjdoc-124176hkeywordsäiDNS-MCGAMdmimehinternethenhancedeRelaylMultipurposedmailjextensionsex.400emixerkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate4´M␀grankingscrfcd2163irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexTUsing the Internet DNS to Distribute MIXER Conformant Global Address Mapping (MCGAM)dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3597µhabstracty`The standard referred shortly into this document as "X.400" relates to the ISO/IEC 10021 - CCITT 1984, 1988 and 1992 X.400 Series Recommendations covering the Message Oriented Text Interchange Service (MOTIS).  This document covers the Inter Personal Messaging System (IPMS) only.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationsSincrotrone TriestednamelC. Allocchioddatej“hfilenamegrfc2162eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymemixerdfullxmixer - MIME - X.400 GatewaydnametMIME - X.400 Gatewaydtypebwgbidjdoc-124175hkeywordsáhMAP-MAILemixerlmultipurposehinternetdmailjextensionsdmimekobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate4´M␀grankingrcrfcd2162irfcNumberrestate