ggrankingdcrfcd2148irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameuBest Current Practicedslugcbcpfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfisubseries£gacronymcbcpfnumberetotaletitlex.Deployment of the Internet White Pages ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÊIPv6 supports datagrams larger than 65535 bytes long, often referred to as jumbograms, through use of the Jumbo Payload hop-by-hop option.  The UDP protocol has a 16-bit length field that keeps it from being able to make use of jumbograms, and though TCP does not have a length field, both the MSS option and the Urgent field are constrained by 16-bits.  This document describes some simple changes that can be made to allow TCP and UDP to make use of IPv6 jumbograms. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxBerkeley Software Design, Inc.dnameiD. Bormanddatej“∏hfilenamegrfc2147eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfipngwgdfullmipngwg - IPNGdnamedIPNGdtypebwgbidjdoc-124160hkeywordsájIPv6-JumbodUserhDatagramhProtocolhTerminalgControlhInternetkobsoletedByÅd2675epagesopublicationDate3h?pgrankingccrfcd2147irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex TCP and UDP over IPv6 JumbogramsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty»This memo provides an update and clarification to RFC 1816.  This document describes the registration policies for the top-level domain ".GOV".  The purpose of the domain is to provide naming conventions that identify US Federal government agencies in order to facilitate access to their electronic resources.  This memo provides guidance for registrations by Federal Agencies that avoids name duplication and facilitates responsiveness to the public.  It restricts registrations to coincide with the approved structure of the US government and the advice of its Chief Information Officers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamexFederal Networking Councilddatej“∑hfilenamegrfc2146eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43309hkeywordsÇcGovfFED.USkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate3h?pgrankingbcrfcd2146irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex%U.S. Government Internet Domain NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty±HTTP request and response messages include an HTTP protocol version number.  Some confusion exists concerning the proper use and interpretation of HTTP version numbers, and concerning interoperability of HTTP implementations of different protocol versions.  This document is an attempt to clarify the situation.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationxDigital Equipment CorporationdnamekJ. C. Mogul¢kaffiliationxUniversity of California IrvinednamekR. Fielding¢kaffiliationcMITdnameiJ. Gettys¢kaffiliation`dnamejH. Frystykddatej ≤hfilenamegrfc2145eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdhttpdfullx"http - HyperText Transfer ProtocoldnamexHyperText Transfer Protocoldtypebwgbidjdoc-124159hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd7230epagesopublicationDate3h?pgrankingacrfcd2145irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.Use and Interpretation of HTTP Version NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyòThere is a need in the Internet community for an unencumbered encryption algorithm with a range of key sizes that can provide security for a variety of cryptographic applications and protocols.  This document describes an existing algorithm that can be used to satisfy 