ypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx∑This document itemizes the potential values for IPv4 subnets.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationgAlantecdnamejT. Pummill¢kaffiliationcISIdnamejB. Manningddatej“¸hfilenamegrfc1860eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-123894hkeywordsÉfvaluesdIPv4gsubnetskobsoletedByÅd1878epagesopublicationDate0n<pgrankingDcrfcd1860irfcNumberDestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex%Variable Length Subnet Table For IPv4dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÿThis document is an extension to STD35, RFC1006, a standard for the Internet community.  The document does not duplicate the protocol definitions contained in RFC1006 and in International Standard ISO 8073.  It supplements that information with the description of how to implement ISO Transport Class 2 Non-use of Explicit Flow Control on top of TCP.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxDigital Equipment CorporationdnamekY. Pouffaryddatej“˚hfilenamegrfc1859eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-123893hkeywordsÉmInternationalhStandardkOrganizatiokobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate0n<pgrankingCcrfcd1859irfcNumberCestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexQISO Transport Class 2 Non-use of Explicit Flow Control over TCP RFC1006 extensiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty-IP fragmentation can be used to disguise TCP packets from IP filters used in routers and hosts.  This document describes two methods of attack as well as remedies to prevent them.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationgAlantecdnameiG. Ziemba¢kaffiliationkCybersourcednamegD. Reed¢kaffiliationmcisco SystemsdnameiP. Trainaddatej“˚hfilenamegrfc1858eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-123892hkeywordsáhinternethprotocolctcpltransmissiongcontrolgroutersehostskobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate0n<pgrankingBcrfcd1858irfcNumberBestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex1Security Considerations for IP Fragment FilteringdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd3128µhabstractyÓThis memo describes a model for operational statistics in the Internet.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.  This document defines a model and protocol for a set of tools which could be used by NSPs and Network Operation Centers (NOCs) to share data among themselves and with customers.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymgops-olddfullx'ops-old - Operational Requirements AreadnamexOperational Requirements AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx Pittsburgh Supercomputing CenterdnamejM. Lambertddatej“˚hfilenamegrfc1857eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfopstatdfullxopstat - Operational StatisticsdnamevOperational Statisticsdtypebwgbidjdoc-123891hkeywordsÉgmetricslmeasurementsopolling periodskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate0n<pgrankingAcrfcd1857irfcNumberAestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex)A Model for Common Operational StatisticsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyZNetwork administrators gather data related to the performance, utilization, usability and growth of their data network.  The amount of raw data gathered is usually quite large, typically ranging somewhere between several megabytes to several gigabytes of data each month.  Few (if any) tools exist today for the sharing of that raw data among network operators or between a network service provider (NSP) and its customers.  This document defines a model and protocol for a set of tools which could be used by NSPs and Network Operation Centers (NOCs) to share data among themselves and with customers.darea£gacronymgops-olddfullx'o