1781eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdasiddfullx4asid - Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdnamex-Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdtypebwgbidjdoc-123842hkeywordsÑgOSI-DireX.500odirectory namesrrepresenting nameskobsoletedByÅd3494epagesopublicationDate/T)Ägrankingıcrfcd1781irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex7Using the OSI Directory to Achieve User Friendly NamingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxúThis memo describes the state of standardization of protocols used in the Internet as determined by the Internet Architecture Board (IAB). [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcIABdnameiJ. Postelddatej“éhfilenamegrfc1780eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43285hkeywordsÉfstatusiprocedureeindexkobsoletedByÅd1800epages'opublicationDate/T)ÄgrankingÙcrfcd1780irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex$Internet Official Protocol StandardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty'The OSI Directory uses distinguished names as the primary keys to entries in the directory.  Distinguished Names are encoded in ASN.1.  When a distinguished name is communicated between to users not using a directory protocol (e.g., in a mail message), there is a need to have a user-oriented string representation of distinguished name.  This specification defines a string format for representing names, which is designed to give a clean representation of commonly used names, whilst being able to represent any distinguished name. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpISODE ConsortiumdnamehS. Killeddatej“éhfilenamegrfc1779eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdasiddfullx4asid - Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdnamex-Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdtypebwgbidjdoc-123841hkeywordsÑgSTR-REPeX.500odirectory namesrrepresenting nameskobsoletedByÇd2253d3494epagesopublicationDate/T)ÄgrankingÛcrfcd1779irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex.A String Representation of Distinguished NamesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty†The Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) requires that the contents of AttributeValue fields in protocol elements be octet strings.  This document defines the requirements that must be satisfied by encoding rules used to render X.500 Directory attribute syntaxes into a form suitable for use in the LDAP, then goes on to define the encoding rules for the standard set of attribute syntaxes. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationvUniversity of MichigandnamehT. Howes¢kaffiliationpISODE ConsortiumdnamehS. Kille¢kaffiliationhPSI Inc.dnamehW. Yeong¢kaffiliationiNeXor LtddnamejC. Robbinsddatej“çhfilenamegrfc1778eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdasiddfullx4asid - Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdnamex-Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdtypebwgbidjdoc-123840hkeywordsÉeX.500dLDAPxlightweight directory protocolkobsoletedByÅd3494epagesopublicationDate/T)ÄgrankingÚcrfcd1778irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex8The String Representation of Standard Attribute SyntaxesdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd2559∂habstracty•The protocol described in this document is designed to provide access to the X.500 Directory while not incurring the resource requirements of the Directory Access Protocol (DAP).This protocol is specifically targeted at simple management applications and browser applications that provide simple read/write interactive access to the X.500 Directory, and is intended to be a complement to the DAP itself. [STANDARDS-TRACK]fadNamerPatrik F√§ltstr√∂mdarea£gacronymcappdfullwapp - Applications AreadnameqApplications AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationhPSI Inc.dnamehW. Yeong¢kaffiliationvUniversity of MichigandnamehT. Howes¢kaffiliationpISODE ConsortiumdnamehS. Killeddatej“çhfilenamegrfc1777eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdasiddfullx4asid - Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdnamex-Access, Searching and Indexing of Directoriesdtypebwgbidjdoc-123839hkeywordsÑeX.500cDAPkinteractivefaccesskobsoletedByÅd3494epagesopublicationDate/T)ÄgrankingÒcrfcd1777irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlex%Lightweight Directory Access ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ¥habstractx˘Declaring that the address is the message, the IPng WG has selected a packet format which includes 1696 bytes of address space.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.gauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationoCyberCash, Inc.dnamejS. Crockerddatej—hhfilenamegrfc1776eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43294hkeywordsÅdIPngkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate/}␀grankingcrfcd1776irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlexThe Address is the MessagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyRThe Internet permits different levels of access for consumers and providers of service.  The nature of those differences is quite important in the capabilities They afford.  Hence, it is appropriate to provide terminology that distinguishes among the range, so that the Internet community can gain some clarity when distinguishing whether a user (or an organization) is "on" the Internet.  This document suggests four terms, for distinguishing the major classes of access.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationvBrandenburg ConsultingdnamejD. Crockerddatej“çhfilenamegrfc1775eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43293hkeywordsÖfaccessdfullfClienthMediatediMessagingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate/T)ÄgrankingÔcrfcd1775irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlewTo Be "On" the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty•The purpose of this report is to document how the requirements for advancing a routing protocol to Draft Standard have been satisfied by the Border Gateway Protocol version 4 (BGP-4).  This report summarizes the key features of BGP, and analyzes the protocol with respect to scaling and performance.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard of any kind.darea£gacronymcrtgdfullrrtg - Routing AreadnamelRouting AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationmcisco SystemsdnameiP. Trainaddatej“çhfilenamegrfc1774eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymcidrdfullxidr - Inter-Domain RoutingdnametInter-Domain Routingdtypebwgbidjdoc-123838hkeywordsÉfBordergGatewaygRoutingkobsoletedByÄepages
