ream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewIP Forwarding Table MIBdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty+This memo defines a portion of the Management Information Base (MIB) for use with network management protocols in TCP/IP-based internets.  In particular, it describes a representation of the SNMP parties defined in [8] as objects defined according to the Internet Standard SMI [1]. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationrHughes LAN SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationx#MIT Laboratory for Computer SciencednamehJ. Davin¢kaffiliationx!Trusted Information Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Galvinddatej“6hfilenamegrfc1353eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsnmpsecdfullwsnmpsec - SNMP SecuritydnamemSNMP Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-123551hkeywordsÑnSNMP-PARTY-MIBgnetworkjmanagementnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate*QWgrankingIcrfcd1353irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexADefinitions of Managed Objects for Administration of SNMP PartiesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty—The Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) specification [1] allows for the protection of network management operations by a variety of security protocols.  The SNMP administrative model described in [2] provides a framework for securing SNMP network management.  In the context of that framework, this memo defines protocols to support the following three security services: data integrity, data origin authentication and data confidentiality. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationxTrusted Information SystemsdnameiJ. Galvin¢kaffiliationrHughes LAN SystemsdnamemK. McCloghrie¢kaffiliationcMITdnamehJ. Davinddatej“6hfilenamegrfc1352eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsnmpsecdfullwsnmpsec - SNMP SecuritydnamemSNMP Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-123550hkeywordsÑhSNMP-SECgnetworkjmanagementnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDate*QWgrankingHcrfcd1352irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlewSNMP Security ProtocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty(This memo presents an elaboration of the SNMP administrative model set forth in [1].  This model provides a unified conceptual basis for administering SNMP protocol entities to support: authenticaiton and integrity, privacy, access control, and cooperation of protocol entities. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcsecdfullssec - Security AreadnamemSecurity AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationx#MIT Laboratory for Computer SciencednamehJ. Davin¢kaffiliationx!Trusted Information Systems, Inc.dnameiJ. Galvin¢kaffiliation`dnamemK. McCloghrieddatej“6hfilenamegrfc1351eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymgsnmpsecdfullwsnmpsec - SNMP SecuritydnamemSNMP Securitydtypebwgbidjdoc-123549hkeywordsÑjSNMP-ADMINgnetworkjmanagementnauthenticationkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate*QWgrankingGcrfcd1351irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexSNMP Administrative ModeldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyXTFTP is a very simple protocol used to transfer files.  It is from this that its name comes, Trivial File Transfer Protocol or TFTP.  Each nonterminal packet is acknowledged separately.  This document describes the protocol and its types of packets.  The document also explains the reasons behind some of the design decisions. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcMITdnamejK. Sollinsddatej“6hfilenamegrfc1350eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbidjdoc-123548hkeywordsÖdTFTPgtrivialdfilehtransfergbootingkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate*QWgrankingFcrfcd1350irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLe