4. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationx@Center for Discrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer SciencednamenD.P. Zimmermanddatej…Chfilenamegrfc1196eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42654hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1288epagesopublicationDate'Wc␀granking¨crfcd1196irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Finger User Information ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyªThis memo specifies an integrated routing protocol, based on the OSI Intra-Domain IS-IS Routing Protocol, which may be used as an interior gateway protocol (IGP) to support TCP/IP as well as OSI.  This allows a single routing protocol to be used to support pure IP environments, pure OSI environments, and dual environments.  This specification was developed by the IS-IS working group of the Internet Engineering Task Force. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxDigital Equipment CorporationdnameiR. Callonddatej—ºhfilenamegrfc1195eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43100hkeywordsÅeIS-ISkobsoletedByÄepagesUopublicationDate'Wc␀granking´crfcd1195irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex<Use of OSI IS-IS for routing in TCP/IP and dual environmentsdtypecrfciupdatedByÉd1349d5302d5304µhabstractyÿThis memo describes the Finger User Information Protocol.  This is a simple protocol which provides an interface to a remote user information program.  Based on RFC 742, a description of the original Finger protocol, this memo attempts to clarify the expected communication between the two ends of a Finger connection.  It also tries not to 