 may be obtained from the Network Information Center or from the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority.  Do not use this edition after 31-Aug-90.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex)Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency¢kaffiliation`dnamexInternet Activities Boardddatej“Ãhfilenamegrfc1140eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43057hkeywordsÑcIABhofficialhprotocolistandardskobsoletedByÅd1200epagesopublicationDate&=3grankingtcrfcd1140irfcNumbertestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexIAB official protocol standardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty`This memo defines an echo function for the connection-less network layer protocol.  Two mechanisms are introduced that may be used to implement the echo function.  The first mechanism is recommended as an interim solution for the Internet community.  The second mechanism will be progressed to the ANSI X3S3.3 working group for consideration as a work item.  When an ISO standard is adopted that provides functionality similar to that described by this memo, then this memo will become obsolete and superceded by the ISO standard.  This memo is not intended to compete with an ISO standard. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcosidfullxosi - OSI Integration AreadnametOSI Integration AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekR.A. Hagensddatej…®hfilenamegrfc1139eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymfosigendfulltosigen - OSI GeneraldnamekOSI Generaldtypebwgbidjdoc-123450hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd1574d1575epagesopublicationDate%ü␀grankingscrfcd1139irfcNumbersestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamedIETFdslugdietfetitlexEcho function for ISO 8473dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThs RFC suggests an electronic mail protocol mapping for the Internet community and UK Academic Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard.  This memo updates RFCs 822, 987, and 1026.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejS.E. Killeddatej…Bhfilenamegrfc1138eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42744hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd1327d2156epages\opublicationDate%v/Ägrankingrcrfcd1138irfcNumberrestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex3Mapping between X.400(1988) / ISO 10021 and RFC 822dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd1148µhabstractx◊This RFC suggests an electronic mail protocol mapping for the Internet community and UK Academic Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  This memo does not specify an Internet standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationxUniversity College LondondnamehS. Killeddatej…Bhfilenamegrfc1137eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42909hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate%v/Ägrankingqcrfcd1137irfcNumberqestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexAMapping between full RFC 822 and RFC 822 with restricted encodingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¥This RFC proposes a model for describing routing within the Internet.  The model is an adaptation of the "OSI Routeing Framework".  This memo does not specify an Internet standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Hares¢kaffiliation`dnamegD. Katzddatej…Bhfilenamegrfc1136eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43063hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate%v/Ägrankingpcrfcd1136irfcNumberpestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexOAdministrative Domains and Routing Domains: A model for routing in the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyŒThis memo takes a look back at the helminthiasis (infestation with, or disease caused by parasitic worms) of the Internet that was unleashed the evening of 2 November 1988.  This RFC provides information about an event that occurred in the life of the Internet.  This memo does not specify any standard.  This document provides a glimpse at the infection, its festering, and cure.  The impact of the worm on the Internet community, ethics statements, the role of the news media, crime in the computer world, and future prevention is discussed.  A documentation review presents four publications that describe in detail this particular parasitic computer program.  Reference and bibliography sections are also included.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynoldsddatej…Bhfilenamegrfc1135eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43062hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages!opublicationDate%v/Ägrankingocrfcd1135irfcNumberoestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexHelminthiasis of the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyyThis proposal is the product of the Point-to-Point Protocol Working Group of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).  Comments on this memo should be submitted to the IETF Point-to-Point Protocol Working Group chair by January 15, 1990.  Comments will be reviewed at the February 1990 IETF meeting, with the goal of advancing PPP to draft standard status. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Perkinsddatej ﬂhfilenamegrfc1134eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43061hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1171epages&opublicationDate%N¢Ägrankingncrfcd1134irfcNumbernestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexjPoint-to-Point Protocol: A proposal for multi-protocol transmission of datagrams over Point-to-Point linksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyrThis document is a case study of the implementation of routing between the NSFNET and the DDN components (the MILNET and the ARPANET).  We hope that it can be used to expand towards interconnection of other Administrative Domains.  We would welcome discussion and suggestions about the methods employed for the interconnections.  No standards are specified in this memo.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ.Y. Yu¢kaffiliation`dnamejH.W. Braunddatej ﬁhfilenamegrfc1133eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43060hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate%N¢Ägrankingmcrfcd1133irfcNumbermestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex&Routing between the NSFNET and the DDNdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty£This document specifies a standard method of encapsulating 802.2 packets on networks supporting Novell's Internet Packet Exchange Protocol (IPX).  It obsoletes earlier documents detailing the transmission of Internet packets over IPX networks.  It differs from these earlier documents in that it allows for the transmission of multiple network protocols over IPX and for the transmission of packets through IPX bridges.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameoL.J. McLaughlinddatej“—hfilenamegrfc1132eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43059hkeywordsÅfIP-IPXkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate%N¢Ägrankinglcrfcd1132irfcNumberlestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber1etotaletitlex@Standard for the transmission of 802.2 packets over IPX networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyœThis RFC is the specification of the Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) Internet routing protocol.  OSPF is in the class of Internal Gateway Protocols (IGPs) for distributing routing information between gateways of a single Autonomous System.  This routing protocol is based on the link-state approach (in contrast to the distance-vector approach).  This specification was developed by the OSPF Working Group of the Internet Engineering Task Force. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamefJ. MoyddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1131eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43058hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1247epagesopublicationDate%%µgrankingkcrfcd1131irfcNumberkestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlerOSPF specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxàThis memo describes the state of standardization of protocols used in the Internet as determined by the Internet Activities Board (IAB).darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex)Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency¢kaffiliation`dnamexInternet Activities Boardddatej“Òhfilenamegrfc1130eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43035hkeywordsÑcIABhofficialhprotocolistandardskobsoletedByÅd1140epagesopublicationDate%%µgrankingjcrfcd1130irfcNumberjestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexIAB official protocol standardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyfThis memo describes the Network Time Protocol (NTP) designed to distribute time information in a large, diverse internet system operating at speeds from mundane to lightwave.  It uses a returnable- time architecture in which a distributed subnet of time servers operating in a self-organizing, hierarchical, master-slave configuration synchronizes local clocks within the subnet and to national time standards via wire or radio.  The servers can also redistribute time information within a network via local routing algorithms and time daemons.  The architectures, algorithms and protocols which have evolved to NTP over several years of implementation and refinement are described in this paper.  The synchronization subnet which has been in regular operation in the Internet for the last several years is described along with performance data which shows that timekeeping accuracy throughout most portions of the Internet can be ordinarily maintained to within a few tens of milliseconds, even in cases of failure or disruption of clocks, time servers or networks.  This memo describes the Network Time Protocol in RFC-1119.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej“Òhfilenamegrfc1129eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43056hkeywordsÅcNTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate%%µgrankingicrfcd1129irfcNumberiestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex8Internet Time Synchronization: The Network Time ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¸This paper describes a series of experiments involving over 100,000 hosts of the Internet system and located in the U.S., Europe and the Pacific.  The experiments are designed to evaluate the availability, accuracy and reliability of international standard time distribution using the DARPA/NSF Internet and the Network Time Protocol (NTP), which is specified in RFC-1119.  NTP is designed specifically for use in a large, diverse internet system operating at speeds from mundane to lightwave.  In NTP a distributed subnet of time servers operating in a self-organizing, hierarchical, master-slave configuration exchange precision timestamps in order to synchronize subnet clocks to each other and national time standards via wire or radio.  The experiments are designed to locate Internet hosts and gateways that provide time by one of three time distribution protocols and evaluate the accuracy of their indications.  For those hosts that support NTP, the experiments determine the distribution of errors and other statistics over paths spanning major portions of the globe.  Finally, the experiments evaluate the accuracy and reliability of precision timekeeping using NTP and typical Internet paths involving DARPA, NSFNET and other agency networks.  The experiments demonstrate that timekeeping accuracy throughout most portions of the Internet can be ordinarily maintained to within a few tens of milliseconds, even in cases of failure or disruption of clocks, time servers or networks.  This memo does not specify a standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. MillsddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1128eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43055hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate%%µgrankinghcrfcd1128irfcNumberhestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexHMeasured performance of the Network Time Protocol in the Internet systemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxçThis RFC is for information only; it does not constitute a standard, draft standard, or proposed standard, and it does not define a protocol.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekR.T. BradenddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1127eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43054hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate%%µgrankinggcrfcd1127irfcNumbergestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex)Perspective on the Host Requirements RFCsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty-This document describes the functional requirements for a routing protocol to be used between autonomous systems.  This document is intended as a necessary precursor to the design of a new inter- autonomous system routing protocol and specifies requirements for the Internet applicable for use with the current DoD IP, the ISO IP, and future Internet Protocols.  It is intended that these requirements will form the basis for the future development of a new inter-autonomous systems routing architecture and protocol.  This memo does not specify a standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. LittleddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1126eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43053hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate%%µgrankingfcrfcd1126irfcNumberfestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexEGoals and functional requirements for inter-autonomous system routingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyxThe purpose of this memo is to focus discussion on particular problems in the Internet and possible methods of solution.  No proposed solutions in this document are intended as standards for the Internet.  Rather, it is hoped that a general consensus will emerge as to the appropriate solution to such problems, leading eventually to the development and adoption of standards.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Estrinddatej ﬁhfilenamegrfc1125eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43052hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate%N¢Ägrankingecrfcd1125irfcNumbereestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex;Policy requirements for inter Administrative Domain routingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty|To support the activities of the Federal Research Internet Coordinating Committee (FRICC) in creating an interconnected set of networks to serve the research community, two workshops were held to address the technical support of policy issues that arise when interconnecting such networks.  Held under the suspices of the Internet Activities Board at the request of the FRICC, and sponsored by NASA through RIACS, the workshops addressed the required and feasible technologies and architectures that could be used to satisfy the desired policies for interconnection.  The purpose of this RFC is to report the results of these workshops.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekB.M. LeinerddatejÀGhfilenamegrfc1124eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43051hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate$˛(grankingdcrfcd1124irfcNumberdestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex)Policy issues in interconnecting networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx–This RFC is an official specification for the Internet community.  It incorporates by reference, amends, corrects, and supplements the primary protocol standards documents relating to hosts. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Bradenddatej“Ûhfilenamegrfc1123eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42743hkeywordsÅmapplicabilitykobsoletedByÄepagesbopublicationDate%%µgrankingccrfcd1123irfcNumbercestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlex9Requirements for Internet Hosts - Application and SupportdtypecrfciupdatedByÜd2181d9210d5966d5321d7766d1349∂habstractx–This RFC is an official specification for the Internet community.  It incorporates by reference, amends, corrects, and supplements the primary protocol standards documents relating to hosts. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Bradenddatej“Ûhfilenamegrfc1122eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42711hkeywordsÅmapplicabilitykobsoletedByÄepagestopublicationDate%%µgrankingbcrfcd1122irfcNumberbestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlex6Requirements for Internet Hosts - Communication LayersdtypecrfciupdatedByâd4379d5884d6298d9293d6093d8029d1349d6633d6864µhabstractxõThis RFC presents a collection of poems that were presented at "Act One", a symposium held partially in celebration of the 20th anniversary of the ARPANET.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliation`dnamelL. Kleinrock¢kaffiliation`dnameiV.G. Cerf¢kaffiliation`dnamehB. BoehmddatejÀGhfilenamegrfc1121eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43050hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate$˛(grankingacrfcd1121irfcNumberaestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlesAct one - the poemsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxºThis RFC provides a history and description of the Internet Activities Board (IAB) and its subsidiary organizations.  This memo is for informational use and does not constitute a standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegV. CerfddatejÀGhfilenamegrfc1120eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43048hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1160epagesopublicationDate$˛(granking`crfcd1120irfcNumber`estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexInternet Activities BoarddtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyòThis document describes the Network Time Protocol (NTP), specifies its formal structure and summarizes information useful for its implementation.  NTP provides the mechanisms to synchronize time and coordinate time distribution in a large, diverse internet operating at rates from mundane to lightwave.  It uses a returnable-time design in which a distributed subnet of time servers operating in a self- organizing, hierarchical-master-slave configuration synchronizes local clocks within the subnet and to national time standards via wire or radio.  The servers can also redistribute reference time via local routing algorithms and time daemons. [STANDARDS-TRACK]darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej”hfilenamegrfc1119eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42891hkeywordsÖcNTPeNTPv2dtimeeclockosynchronizationkobsoletedByÅd1305epagesopublicationDate$˛(granking_crfcd1119irfcNumber_estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexBNetwork Time Protocol (version 2) specification and implementationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÌThis RFC is being distributed to members of the Internet community in order to make available some "hints" which will allow new network participants to understand how the direction of the Internet is set, how to acquire online information and how to be a good Internet neighbor.  While the information discussed may not be relevant to the research problems of the Internet, it may be interesting to a number of researchers and implementors.  No standards are defined or specified in this memo.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegE. KrolddatejÀFhfilenamegrfc1118eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43047hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate$˛(granking^crfcd1118irfcNumber^estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex!Hitchhikers guide to the Internetdtypecrfciupdated