amegrfc1097eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43032hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate$4ÅÄgrankingIcrfcd1097irfcNumberIestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamecISEdslugciseetitlex Telnet subliminal-message optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx√This RFC specifies a standard for the Internet community.  Hosts on the Internet that transmit the X display location within the Telnet protocol are expected to adopt and implement this standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejG.A. Marcyddatej“~hfilenamegrfc1096eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43031hkeywordsÅhTOPT-XDLkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate$£␀grankingHcrfcd1096irfcNumberHestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Telnet X display location optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty?This memo defines a network management architecture that uses the International Organization for Standardization's (ISO) Common Management Information Services/Common Management Information Protocol (CMIS/CMIP) in a TCP/IP environment.  This architecture provides a means by which control and monitoring information can be exchanged between a manager and a remote network element.  In particular, this memo defines the means for implementing the Draft International Standard (DIS) version of CMIS/CMIP on top of Internet transport protocols for the purpose of carrying management information defined in the Internet-standard management information base.  DIS CMIS/CMIP is suitable for deployment in TCP/IP networks while CMIS/CMIP moves toward becoming an International Standard.  Together with the relevant ISO standards and the companion RFCs that describe the initial structure of management information and management information base, these documents provide the basis for a comprehensive architecture and system for managing TCP/IP- based internets, and in particular the Internet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelU.S. Warrier¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Besawddatej»√hfilenamegrfc1095eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43030hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1189epagesCopublicationDate$4ÅÄgrankingGcrfcd1095irfcNumberGestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexFCommon Management Information Services and Protocol over TCP/IP (CMOT)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¯This RFC describes a protocol that Sun Microsystems, Inc., and others are using.  A new version of the protocol is under development, but others may benefit from the descriptions of the current protocol, and discussion of some of the design issues.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationpSun MicrosystemsdnamejB. Nowickiddatej“Ñhfilenamegrfc1094eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-43029hkeywordsÅgSUN-NFSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate$£␀grankingFcrfcd1094irfcNumberFestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex/NFS: Network File System Protocol specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¿This document describes the routing architecture for the NSFNET centered around the new NSFNET Backbone, with specific emphasis on the interface between the backbone and its attached networks.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejH.W. Braunddatej…zhfilenamegrfc1093eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Indi