amily runs over a variety of network media: IEEE 802.3 (ethernet) and 802.5 (token ring) LAN's, X.25 lines, satellite links, and serial lines.  There are standard encapsulations for IP packets defined for many of these networks, but there is no standard for serial lines.  SLIP, Serial Line IP, is a currently a de facto standard, commonly used for point-to-point serial connections running TCP/IP.  It is not an Internet standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ.L. Romkeyddatej“Shfilenamegrfc1055eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42990hkeywordsÅgIP-SLIPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"£´pgrankingcrfcd1055irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber/etotaletitlexDNonstandard for transmission of IP datagrams over serial lines: SLIPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis memo specifies the extensions required of a host implementation of the Internet Protocol (IP) to support multicasting.  IP multicasting is the transmission of an IP datagram to a "host group", a set hosts identified by a single IP destination address.  A multicast datagram is delivered to all members of its destination host group with the same "best-efforts" reliability as regular unicast IP datagrams.  It is proposed as a standard for IP multicasting in the Internet.  This specification is a major revision of RFC-988.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS.E. Deeringddatej ”hfilenamegrfc1054eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42926hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1112epagesopublicationDate"zÃgrankingcrfcd1054irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex#Host extensions for IP multicastingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx~This RFC proposes a new option to Telnet for the Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegS. Levy¢kaffiliation`dnamekT. Jacobsonddatej—ghfilenamegrfc1053eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42989hkeywordsÅhTOPT-X.3kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"SN␀grankingcrfcd1053irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitleuTelnet X.3 PAD optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty√This RFC is intended to convey to the Internet community and other interested parties the recommendations of the Internet Activities Board (IAB) for the development of network management protocols for use in the TCP/IP environment.  This memo does NOT, in and of itself, define or propose an Official Internet Protocol.  It does reflect, however, the policy of the IAB with respect to further network management development in the short and long term.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiV.G. Cerfddatej»¬hfilenamegrfc1052eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42988hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"SN␀grankingcrfcd1052irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexPIAB recommendations for the development of Internet network management standardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx»This memo specifies a standard method of encapsulating Internet Protocol (IP) and Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) datagrams on an ARCNET.  This RFC is a standard protocol for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamepP.A. Prindevilleddatej ohfilenamegrfc1051eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42987hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1201epagesopublicationDate"*oÄgrankingcrfcd1051irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexRStandard for the transmission of IP datagrams and ARP packets over ARCNET networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty This memo specifies a message protocol used in implementing Sun's Remote Procedure Call (RPC) package.  This RFC describes a standard that Sun Microsystems and others are using and is one they wish to propose for the Internet's consideration.  It is not an Internet standard at this time.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamepSun Microsystemsddatej—ghfilenamegrfc1050eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42985hkeywordsÅgSUN-RPCkobsoletedByÅd1057epagesopublicationDate"SN␀grankingcrfcd1050irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex1RPC: Remote Procedure Call Protocol specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx£This memo suggests proposed additions to the Internet Mail Protocol, RFC-822, for the Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM.A. Sirbuddatej“Ñhfilenamegrfc1049eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42984hkeywordsÅgCONTENTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"*oÄgrankingcrfcd1049irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex/Content-type header field for Internet messagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxxThis memo proposes an addition to the Bootstrap Protocol (BOOTP).  Comments and suggestions for improvements are sought.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamepP.A. Prindevilleddatej…yhfilenamegrfc1048eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42982hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÑd1533d1395d1497d1084epagesopublicationDate"4␀grankingcrfcd1048irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex#BOOTP vendor information extensionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty1An examination of a synchronization problem in the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) is presented.  This synchronization problem can cause a message to be delivered multiple times.  A method for avoiding this problem is suggested.  Nodding familiarity with the SMTP specification, RFC-821, is required.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelC. Partridgeddatej…yhfilenamegrfc1047eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42981hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"4␀grankingcrfcd1047irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexDuplicate messages and SMTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractywThis memo is intended to explore how Type-of-Service might be implemented in the Internet.  The proposal describes a method of queuing which can provide the different classes of service.  The technique also prohibits one class of service from consuming excessive resources or excluding other classes of service.  This is an "idea paper" and discussion is strongly encouraged.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegW. Prue¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej…yhfilenamegrfc1046eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42980hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"4␀grankingcrfcd1046irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex9Queuing algorithm to provide type-of-service for IP linksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty<This memo specifies the Versatile Message Transaction Protocol (VMTP) [Version 0.7 of 19-Feb-88], a transport protocol specifically designed to support the transaction model of communication, as exemplified by remote procedure call (RPC).  The full function of VMTP, including support for security, real-time, asynchronous message exchanges, streaming, multicast and idempotency, provides a rich selection to the VMTP user level.  Subsettability allows the VMTP module for particular clients and servers to be specialized and simplified to the services actually required.  Examples of such simple clients and servers include PROM network bootload programs, network boot servers, data sensors and simple controllers, to mention but a few examples.  This RFC describes a protocol proposed as a standard for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemD.R. Cheritonddatej—‚hfilenamegrfc1045eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42979hkeywordsÅdVMTPkobsoletedByÄepagesÄopublicationDate"4␀grankingcrfcd1045irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexDVMTP: Versatile Message Transaction Protocol: Protocol specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyHThis memo intends to provide a complete discussion of the protocols and techniques used to embed DoD standard Internet Protocol datagrams (and its associated higher level protocols) on Network Systems Corporation's HYPERchannel equipment.  This document is directed toward network planners and implementors who are already familiar with the TCP/IP protocol suite and the techniques used to carry TCP/IP traffic on common networks such as the DDN or the Ethernet.  No great familiarity with NSC products is assumed; an appendix is devoted to a review of NSC technologies and protocols.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekK. Hardwick¢kaffiliation`dnamelJ. Lekashmanddatej—‚hfilenamegrfc1044eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42978hkeywordsÅeIP-HCkobsoletedByÄepages+opublicationDate"4␀grankingcrfcd1044irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber-etotaletitlexJInternet Protocol on Network System's HYPERchannel: Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5494µhabstracty(This RFC suggests a proposed protocol on the TELNET Data Entry Terminal (DET) Option - DODIIS Implementation for the Internet community.  It is intended that this specification be capatible with the specification of DET Option in RFC-732.  Discussion and suggests for improvements are encouraged.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Yasuda¢kaffiliation`dnamekT. Thompsonddatej—·hfilenamegrfc1043eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42645hkeywordsÅiTOPT-DATAkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"4␀grankingcrfcd1043irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex8Telnet Data Entry Terminal option: DODIIS implementationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty%This RFC specifies a standard method of encapsulating the Internet Protocol (IP) datagrams and Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) requests and replies on IEEE 802 Networks to allow compatible and interoperable implementations.  This RFC specifies a protocol standard for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynoldsddatej—‡hfilenamegrfc1042eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42880hkeywordsÅgIP-IEEEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"4␀grankingcrfcd1042irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber+etotaletitlexDStandard for the transmission of IP datagrams over IEEE 802 networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxŒThis RFC specifies a proposed standard for the Internet community.  Hosts on the Internet that want to support 3270 data stream within the Telnet protocol, are expected to adopt and implement this standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejY. Rekhterddatej“hfilenamegrfc1041eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42977hkeywordsÅiTOPT-3270kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!€UÄgrankingcrfcd1041irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexTelnet 3270 regime optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6270µhabstracty#This RFC is the Outgrowth of a series of IAB Privacy Task Force meetings and of internal working papers distributed for those meetings.  This memo defines message encipherment and authentication procedures, as the initial phase of an effort to provide privacy enhancement services for electronic mail transfer in the Internet.  Detailed key management mechanisms to support these procedures will be defined in a subsequent RFC.  As a goal of this initial phase, it is intended that the procedures defined here be compatible with a wide range of key management approaches, including both conventional (symmetric) and public-key (asymmetric) approaches for encryption of data encrypting keys.  Use of conventional cryptography for message text encryption and/or integrity check computation is anticipated.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Linnddatej…¶hfilenamegrfc1040eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42929hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1113epagesopublicationDate!€UÄgrankingcrfcd1040irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexlPrivacy enhancement for Internet electronic mail: Part I: Message encipherment and authentication proceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC reproduces a memorandum issued on 2-JUL-87 from the Assistant Secretary of Defense for Command, Control, Communications, and Intelligence (ASDC31) to the Director of the Defense Communications Agency (DCA).  This memo is distributed for information only.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Lathamddatej…•hfilenamegrfc1039eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42876hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!€UÄgrankingcrfcd1039irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex7DoD statement on Open Systems Interconnection protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¿This memo is a pre-publication draft of the revised Internet Protocol Security Option.  This RFC reflects the version as approved by the Protocol Standards Steering group, and is provided for informational purposes only.  The final version of this document will be available from Navy publications and should not differ from this document in any major fashion.  This document will be published as a change to the MIL- STD 1777, "Internet Protocol".darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. St. Johnsddatej…•hfilenamegrfc1038eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42976hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1108epagesopublicationDate!€UÄgrankingcrfcd1038irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Draft revised IP security optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty√This document includes a specification of the NFILE file access protocol and its underlying levels of protocol, the Token List Transport Layer and Byte Stream with Mark.  The goal of this specification is to promote discussion of the ideas described here, and to encourage designers of future file protocols to take advantage of these ideas.  A secondary goal is to make the specification available to sites that might benefit from implementing NFILE.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelB. Greenberg¢kaffiliation`dnamehS. Keeneddatej—ºhfilenamegrfc1037eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42975hkeywordsÅeNFILEkobsoletedByÄepagesVopublicationDate!≤w␀grankingcrfcd1037irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexNFILE - a file access protocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyJThis RFC defines the standard format for the interchange of network News messages among USENET hosts.  It updates and replaces RFC-850, reflecting version B2.11 of the News program.  This memo is distributed as an RFC to make this information easily accessible to the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekM.R. Horton¢kaffiliation`dnamehR. Adamsddatej…Ahfilenamegrfc1036eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42778hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd5536d5537epagesopublicationDate!≤w␀grankingcrfcd1036irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+Standard for interchange of USENET messagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractx‡This RFC is the revised specification of the protocol and format used in the implementation of the Domain Name System.  It obsoletes RFC-883.  This memo documents the details of the domain name client - server communication.darea£gacronymcintdfullsint - Internet AreadnamemInternet AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISIdnamenP. Mockapetrisddatej“–hfilenamegrfc1035eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymevgmibdfullxvgmib - 100VG-AnyLAN MIBdnamep100VG-AnyLAN MIBdtypebwgbididoc-42809hkeywordsÇfDOMAINcDNSkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDate!äÍ␀grankingcrfcd1035irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlex/Domain names - implementation and specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByòd8490d1876d8767d4033d4034d4035d2845d2308d2181d3658d2065d7766d5936d4343d6604d3425d9619d5966d1101d1183d1348d1982d2535d2673d1995d1996d2136d2137d8482∂habstracty2This RFC is the revised basic definition of The Domain Name System.  It obsoletes RFC-882.  This memo describes the domain style names and their used for host address look up and electronic mail forwarding.  It discusses the clients and servers in the domain name system and the protocol used between them.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenP. Mockapetrisddatej“–hfilenamegrfc1034eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42808hkeywordsÅfDOMAINkobsoletedByÄepages7opublicationDate!äÍ␀granking
crfcd1034irfcNumber
estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlex&Domain names - concepts and facilitiesdtypecrfciupdatedByìd1876d8767d4033d4034d4035d2308d2181d8020d2065d4592d9471d5936d4343d1101d1183d1348d1982d2535d8482µhabstractxÂThis RFC provides guidelines for domain administrators in operating a domain server and maintaining their portion of the hierarchical database.  Familiarity with the domain system is assumed (see RFCs 1031, 1032, 1034, and 1035).darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Lottorddatej ‹hfilenamegrfc1033eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42971hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!äÍ␀granking	crfcd1033irfcNumber	estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex&Domain Administrators Operations GuidedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyWDomains are administrative entities that provide decentralized management of host naming and addressing.  The domain-naming system is distributed and hierarchical.  This memo describes procedures for registering a domain with the Network Information Center (NIC) of Defense Data Network (DDN), and offers guidelines on the establishment and administration of a domain in accordance with the requirements specified in RFC-920.  It is recommended that the guidelines described in this document be used by domain administrators in the establishment and control of second-level domains.  The role of the domain administrator (DA) is that of coordinator, manager, and technician.  If his domain is established at the second level or lower in the tree, the domain administrator must register by interacting with the management of the domain directly above this.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM.K. Stahlddatej ‹hfilenamegrfc1032eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42970hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!äÍ␀grankingcrfcd1032irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexDomain administrators guidedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÈThis RFC consolidates information necessary for the implementation of domain style names throughout the DDN/MILNET Internet community.  The introduction of domain style names will impact all hosts in the DDN/MILNET Internet.  This RFC is designed as an aid to implementors and administrators by providing: 1) an overview of the transition process from host tables to domains, 2) a timetable for the transition, and 3) references to documentation and software relating to the domain system.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekW.D. Lazearddatej ‹hfilenamegrfc1031eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42969hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate!äÍ␀grankingcrfcd1031irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexMILNET name domain transitiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty]This memo describes the results gathered from testing NETBLT over three networks of different bandwidths and round-trip delays.  The results are not complete, but the information gathered so far has not been promising.  The NETBLT protocol is specified in RFC-998; this document assumes an understanding of the specification as described in RFC-998.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM.L. Lambertddatej ‹hfilenamegrfc1030eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42968hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!äÍ␀grankingcrfcd1030irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex3On testing the NETBLT Protocol over divers networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyµThis memo discusses an extension to a Bridge Protocol to detect and disclose changes in heighbouring host address parameters in a Multi-Lan system of Ethernets.  The problem is one which is appearing more and more regularly as the interconnected systems grow larger on Campuses and in Commercial Institutions.  This RFC suggests a protocol enhancement for the Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegG. Parrddatej“Œhfilenamegrfc1029eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42967hkeywordsÅcarpkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate"zÃgrankingcrfcd1029irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexVMore fault tolerant approach to address resolution for a Multi-LAN system of EthernetsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÓThis memo defines a simple application-layer protocol by which management information for a gateway may be inspected or altered by remote users.  This proposal is intended only as an interim response to immediate gateway monitoring needs.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Davin¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ.D. Case¢kaffiliation`dnamehM. Fedor¢kaffiliation`dnamepM.L. Schoffstallddatej“√hfilenamegrfc1028eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42966hkeywordsÅdSGMPkobsoletedByÄepages#opublicationDate!äÍ␀grankingcrfcd1028irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex"Simple Gateway Monitoring ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx„This RFC describes the use of the Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) by subnet gateways to permit hosts on the connected subnets to communicate without being aware of the existence of subnets, using the technique of "Proxy ARP".darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamepS. Carl-Mitchell¢kaffiliation`dnameoJ.S. QuartermanddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1027eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42965hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!a˝pgrankingcrfcd1027irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex2Using ARP to implement transparent subnet gatewaysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxzThis memo suggest a proposed protocol for the Internet community, and request discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejS.E. KilleddatejÀFhfilenamegrfc1026eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42925hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd1327d2156epagesopublicationDate!:ppgrankingcrfcd1026irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex8Addendum to RFC 987: (Mapping between X.400 and RFC-822)dtypecrfciupdatedByÇd1138d1148µhabstractxÒThis memo describes some of the procedures, scoring and tests used in the TCP and IP bake offs held in the early development of these protocols.  These procedures and tests may still be of use in testing newly implemented TCP and IP modules.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. PostelddatejÀFhfilenamegrfc1025eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42964hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!:ppgrankingcrfcd1025irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlesTCP and IP bake offdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyRThis memo assigns instruction codes, defines object formats and object semantics for use with the High-Level Monitoring and Control Language, defined in RFC-1023.  A general system has been described in previous memos (RFC-1021, RFC-1022).  This system is called the High-Level Entity Management System (HEMS).  This memo is provisional and the definitions are subject to change.  Readers should confirm with the authors that they have the most recent version.  This RFC assumes a working knowledge of the ISO data encoding standard, ASN.1, and a general understanding of the IP protocol suite.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelC. Partridge¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. TrewittddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1024eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42963hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesJopublicationDate!a˝pgranking␀crfcd1024irfcNumber␀estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexHEMS variable definitionsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC specifies the High-Level Entity Management System (HEMS) Monitoring and Control Language.  This language defines the requests and replies used in HEMS.  This memo assumes knowledge of the HEMS system described in RFC-1021, and of the ISO data encoding standard, ASN.1.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. Trewitt¢kaffiliation`dnamelC. PartridgeddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1023eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42961hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1076epagesopublicationDate!a˝pgrankingˇcrfcd1023irfcNumberˇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex$HEMS monitoring and control languagedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty.This memo presents an application protocol for managing network entities such as hosts, gateways, and front end machines.  This protocol is a component of the High-level Entity Management System HEMS), described is RFC-1021.  This memo also assumes a knowledge of the ISO data encoding standard, ASN.1.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelC. Partridge¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. TrewittddatejÀhfilenamegrfc1022eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42960hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!a˝pgranking˛crfcd1022irfcNumber˛estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex,High-level Entity Management Protocol (HEMP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx±This memo provides a general overview of the High-level Entity management system (HEMS).  This system is experimental, and is currently being tested in portions of the Internet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelC. Partridge¢kaffiliation`dnamejG. Trewittddatej“Òhfilenamegrfc1021eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42959hkeywordsÅdHEMSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!a˝pgranking˝crfcd1021irfcNumber˝estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex*High-level Entity Management System (HEMS)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxuThis RFC is a list of the Assigned IP Network Numbers and EGP Autonomous System Numbers.  This RFC obsoletes RFC-997.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiS. Romano¢kaffiliation`dnamejM.K. Stahlddatej €hfilenamegrfc1020eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42938hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÉd1117d1062d1166epages3opublicationDate!äÍ␀granking¸crfcd1020irfcNumber¸estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepInternet numbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx§This memo is a report on the discussion of the representation of equations in a workshop at the ACM SIGGRAPH Conference held in Anaheim, California on 30 July 1987.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. ArnonddatejÀEhfilenamegrfc1019eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42956hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!:ppgranking˚crfcd1019irfcNumber˚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexDReport of the Workshop on Environments for Computational MathematicsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÖThis memo is a discussion of some of the ideas expressed in RFC-1016 on Source Quench.  This memo introduces the distinction of the cause of congestion in a gateway between the effects of "Funneling" and "Mismatch".  It is offered in the same spirit as RFC-1016; to stimulate discussion.  The opinions offered are personal, not corporate, opinions.  Distribution of this memo is unlimited.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemA.M. McKenzieddatej»ˇhfilenamegrfc1018eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42955hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!ëgranking˙crfcd1018irfcNumber˙estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlevSome comments on SQuIDdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¯This RFC identifies the requirements on communication networks for supporting scientific research.  It proposes some specific areas for near term work, as well as some long term goals.  This is an "idea" paper and discussion is strongly encouraged.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekB.M. Leinerddatej»ˇhfilenamegrfc1017eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42954hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate!ëgranking˘crfcd1017irfcNumber˘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexYNetwork requirements for scientific research: Internet task force on scientific computingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThe memo is intended to explore the issue of what a host could do with a source quench.  The proposal is for each source host IP module to introduce some delay between datagrams sent to the same destination host.  This is a "crazy idea paper" and discussion is essential.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamegW. Prue¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej…Îhfilenamegrfc1016eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42953hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate Ë≥pgranking¯crfcd1016irfcNumber¯estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexXSomething a Host Could Do with Source Quench: The Source Quench Introduced Delay (SQuID)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx§This RFC proposes an Interagency Research Internet as the natural outgrowth of the current Internet.  This is an "idea paper" and discussion is strongly encouraged.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekB.M. Leinerddatej…Îhfilenamegrfc1015eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42952hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate Ë≥pgranking˜crfcd1015irfcNumber˜estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex5Implementation plan for interagency research InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¬XDR is a standard for the description and encoding of data.  It is useful for transferring data between different computer architectures.  XDR fits into ISO presentation layer, and is roughly analogous in purpose to X.409, ISO Abstract Syntax Notation.  The major difference between these two is that XDR uses implicit typing, while X.409 uses explicit typing.  This RFC is distributed for information only, it does not establish a Internet standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamepSun Microsystemsddatej  hfilenamegrfc1014eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42951hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate ¡&pgrankingˆcrfcd1014irfcNumberˆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex*XDR: External Data Representation standarddtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÎThis RFC is distributed to the Internet community for information only.  It does not establish an Internet standard.  The X window system has been widely reviewed and tested.  The Internet community is encouraged to experiment with it.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenR.W. Scheiflerddatej hfilenamegrfc1013eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42950hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepageseopublicationDate ¡&pgrankingıcrfcd1013irfcNumberıestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex=X Window System Protocol, version 11: Alpha update April 1987dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxµThis RFC is a reference guide for the Internet community which provides a bibliographic summary of the Request for Comments numbers 1 through 999 issued between the years 1969-1987.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej 
hfilenamegrfc1012eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42949hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages@opublicationDate ¡&pgrankingÙcrfcd1012irfcNumberÙestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex2Bibliography of Request For Comments 1 through 999dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx·This memo is an official status report on the protocols used in the Internet community.  It identifies the documents specifying the official protocols used in the Internet.  Comments indicate any revisions or changes planned.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej “hfilenamegrfc1011eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42933hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDate òGgrankingÛcrfcd1011irfcNumberÛestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexOfficial Internet protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÇd9293d6093µhabstractyThis memo is an official status report on the numbers used in protocols in the Internet community.  It documents the currently assigned values from several series of numbers including link, socket, port, and protocol, used in network protocol implementations.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej —hfilenamegrfc1010eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42931hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1060epages,opublicationDate òGgrankingÚcrfcd1010irfcNumberÚestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepAssigned numbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty"This RFC summarizes the requirements for gateways to be used between networks supporting the Internet protocols.  This document is a formal statement of the requirements to be met by gateways used in the Internet system.  As such, it is an official specification for the Internet community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekR.T. Braden¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej ␀hfilenamegrfc1009eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42921hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1812epages6opublicationDate ¡&pgrankingÒcrfcd1009irfcNumberÒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex"Requirements for Internet gatewaysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC is being distributed to members of the Internet community in order to solicit comments on the Implementors Guide.  While this document may not be directly relevant to the research problems of the Internet, it may be of some interest to a number of researchers and implementors.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehW. McCoyddatej hfilenamegrfc1008eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42947hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesIopublicationDate ¡&pgrankingcrfcd1008irfcNumberestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex3Implementation guide for the ISO Transport ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis document supplements the Transport Service and Protocol of the International Standards Organization (ISO), IS 8072 and IS 8073, respectively, and their formal descriptions by providing conventions, option selections and parameter values.  This RFC is being distributed to members of the Internet community in order to solicit comments on the Draft Military Supplement.  While this document may not be directly relevant to the research problems of the Internet, it may be of some interest to a number of researchers and implementors.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehW. McCoyddatej hfilenamegrfc1007eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42946hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate ¡&pgrankingÔcrfcd1007irfcNumberÔestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex1Military supplement to the ISO Transport ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyøThis memo specifies a standard for the Internet community.  Hosts on the Internet that choose to implement ISO transport services on top of the TCP are expected to adopt and implement this standard.  TCP port 102 is reserved for hosts which implement this standard.  This memo specifies version 3 of the protocol and supersedes RFC-983.  Changes between the protocol is described in RFC-983 and this memo are minor, but unfortunately incompatible.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationx'Northrop Research and Technology CenterdnameiM.T. Rose¢kaffiliationx'Northrop Research and Technology CenterdnameiD.E. Cassddatej“Œhfilenamegrfc1006eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42917hkeywordsÅfTP-TCPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate òGgrankingÓcrfcd1006irfcNumberÓestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber#etotaletitlex2ISO Transport Service on top of the TCP Version: 3dtypecrfciupdatedByÅd2126µhabstractyThis RFC is a proposed specification for the encoding of Class A IP addresses for use on ARPANET-style networks such as the Milnet and Arpanet, and for enhancements to the ARPANET AHIP Host Access Protocol (AHIP; formerly known as 1822).  These enhancements increase the size of the PSN field, allow ARPANET hosts to use logical names to address each other, allow for the communication of type-of-service information from the host to the PSN and enable the PSN to provide congestion feedback to the host on a connection basis.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA. Khanna¢kaffiliation`dnamejA.G. Malisddatej —hfilenamegrfc1005eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42945hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDate òGgrankingÌcrfcd1005irfcNumberÌestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex3ARPANET AHIP-E Host Access Protocol (enhanced AHIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyHThe purpose of this RFC is to focus discussion on authentication problems in the Internet and possible methods of solution.  The proposed solutions this document are not intended as standards for the Internet at this time.  Rather, it is hoped that a general consensus will emerge as to the appropriate solution to authentication problems, leading eventually to the adoption of standards.  This document suggests mediated access-control and authentication procedures suitable for those cases when an association is to be set up between users belonging to different trust environments.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej—ghfilenamegrfc1004eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42944hkeywordsÅjCOOKIE-JARkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate p…␀grankingÏcrfcd1004irfcNumberÏestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex*Distributed-protocol authentication schemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty>This memo is intended to identify and explore issues in defining a standard for the exchange of mathematical equations.  No attempt is made at a complete definition and more questions are asked than are answered.  Questions about the user interface are only addressed to the extent that they affect interchange issues.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiA.R. Katzddatej nhfilenamegrfc1003eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42943hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate GÍÄgrankingÎcrfcd1003irfcNumberÎestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex7Issues in defining an equations representation standarddtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty0This RFC defines a proposed standard protocol to support NetBIOS services in a TCP/IP environment.  Both local network and internet operation are supported.  Various node types are defined to accommodate local and internet topologies and to allow operation with or without the use of IP broadcast.  This RFC gives the detailed specifications of the netBIOS-over-TCP packets, protocols, and defined constants and variables.  A more general overview is found in a companion RFC, "Protocol Standard For NetBIOS Service on TCP/UDP Transport: Concepts and Methods".darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamexFNetBIOS Working Group in the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency¢kaffiliation`dnamexInternet Activities Board¢kaffiliation`dnamexEnd-to-End Services Task Forceddatej“Ñhfilenamegrfc1002eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42942hkeywordsÅgNETBIOSkobsoletedByÄepagesTopublicationDate GÍÄgrankingÍcrfcd1002irfcNumberÍestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlexWProtocol standard for a NetBIOS service on a TCP/UDP transport: Detailed specificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty4This RFC defines a proposed standard protocol to support NetBIOS services in a TCP/IP environment.  Both local network and internet operation are supported.  Various node types are defined to accommodate local and internet topologies and to allow operation with or without the use of IP broadcast.  This RFC describes the NetBIOS-over-TCP protocols in a general manner, emphasizing the underlying ideas and techniques.  Detailed specifications are found in a companion RFC, "Protocol Standard For a NetBIOS Service on a TCP/UDP Transport: Detailed Specifications".darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamexFNetBIOS Working Group in the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency¢kaffiliation`dnamexInternet Activities Board¢kaffiliation`dnamexEnd-to-End Services Task Forceddatej“Ñhfilenamegrfc1001eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42941hkeywordsÅgNETBIOSkobsoletedByÄepagesDopublicationDate GÍÄgrankingÈcrfcd1001irfcNumberÈestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlexTProtocol standard for a NetBIOS service on a TCP/UDP transport: Concepts and methodsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty"This RFC Reference Guide is intended to provide a historical account by categorizing and summarizing of the Request for Comments numbers 1 through 999 issued between the years 1969-1987.  These documents have been crossed referenced to indicate which RFCs are current, obsolete, or revised.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej»˝hfilenamegrfc1000eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42940hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesïopublicationDate!ëgrankingËcrfcd1000irfcNumberËestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex$Request For Comments reference guidedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstract`darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejA. Westine¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej»¡hfilenamefrfc999eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42143hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1000epagesopublicationDate p…␀grankingÁcrfcc999irfcNumberÁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex,Requests For Comments summary notes: 900-999dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyHThis document is a description of, and a specification for, the NETBLT protocol.  It is a revision of the specification published in RFC-969.  NETBLT (NETwork BLock Transfer) is a transport level protocol intended for the rapid transfer of a large quantity of data between computers.  It provides a transfer that is reliable and flow controlled, and is designed to provide maximum throughput over a wide variety of networks.  Although NETBLT currently runs on top of the Internet Protocol (IP), it should be able to operate on top of any datagram protocol similar in function to IP.  This document is published for discussion and comment, and does not constitute a standard.  The proposal may change and certain parts of the protocol have not yet been specified; implementation of this document is therefore not advised.  Obsoletes RFC-969.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.D. Clark¢kaffiliation`dnamelM.L. Lambert¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Zhangddatej“Ñhfilenamefrfc998eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42903hkeywordsÅfNETBLTkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate GÍÄgrankingÊcrfcc998irfcNumberÊestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex%NETBLT: A bulk data transfer protocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyõThis memo is an official status report on the network numbers used in the Internet community.  As of 1-Mar-87 the Network Information Center (NIC) at SRI International has assumed responsibility for assignment of Network Numbers and Autonomous System Numbers.  This RFC documents the current assignments of these numbers at the time of this transfer of responsibility.  Obsoletes RFC-990, 960, 943, 923 and 900.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej nhfilenamefrfc997eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42932hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd1020d1117epages*opublicationDate GÍÄgrankingÂcrfcc997irfcNumberÂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepInternet numbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC specifies a standard for the ARPA Internet community.  Hosts and gateways on the DARPA Internet that choose to implement a remote statistics monitoring facility may use this protocol to send statistics data upon request to a monitoring center or debugging host.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej—‚hfilenamefrfc996eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42937hkeywordsÅgSTATSRVkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate #␀Ägranking‰crfcc996irfcNumber‰estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitleqStatistics serverdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÃThis Protocol is one of a set of International Standards produced to facilitate the interconnection of open systems.  The set of standards covers the services and protocols required to achieve such interconnection.  This Protocol is positioned with respect to other related standards by the layers defined in the Reference Model for Open Systems Interconnection (ISO 7498) and by the structure defined in the Internal Organization of the Network Layer (DIS 8648).  In particular, it is a protocol of the Network Layer.  This Protocol permits End Systems and Intermediate Systems to exchange configuration and routing information to facilitate the operation of the routing and relaying functions of the Network Layer.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex.International Organization for Standardizationddatej»¡hfilenamefrfc995eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42936hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages)opublicationDateèïÄgranking„crfcc995irfcNumber„estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex`End System to Intermediate System Routing Exchange Protocol for use in conjunction with ISO 8473dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÿThis Protocol Standard is one of a set of International Standards produced to facilitate the interconnection of open systems.  The set of standards covers the services and protocols required to achieve such interconnection.  This Protocol Standard is positioned with respect to other related standards by the layers defined in the Reference Model for Open Systems Interconnection (ISO 7498).  In particular, it is a protocol of the Network Layer.  This Protocol may be used between network-entities in end systems or in Network Layer relay systems (or both).  It provides the Connectionless-mode Network Service as defined in Addendum 1 to the Network Service Definition Covering Connectionless-mode Transmission (ISO 8348/AD1).darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex.International Organization for Standardizationddatej nhfilenamefrfc994eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42858hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages4opublicationDatef∑␀granking‚crfcc994irfcNumber‚estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexVFinal text of DIS 8473, Protocol for Providing the Connectionless-mode Network ServicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty¬This document is a discussion of the Pcmail workstation-based distributed mail system.  It is a revision of the design published in NIC RFC-984.  The revision is based on discussion and comment fromm a variety of sources, as well as further research into the design of interactive Pcmail clients and the use of client code on machines other than IBM PCs.  As this design may change, implementation of this document is not advised.  Obsoletes RFC-984.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.D. Clark¢kaffiliation`dnamelM.L. Lambertddatej…Ahfilenamefrfc993eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42919hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1056epagesopublicationDate—CÄgranking·crfcc993irfcNumber·estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex8PCMAIL: A distributed mail system for personal computersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyLThis memo describes a collection of multicast communication primitives integrated with a mechanism for handling process failure and recovery.  These primitives facilitate the implementation of fault-tolerant process groups, which can be used to provide distributed services in an environment subject to non-malicious crash failures.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekK.P. Birman¢kaffiliation`dnamekT.A. Josephddatej €hfilenamefrfc992eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42934hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate©∂Ägranking‡crfcc992irfcNumber‡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex:On communication support for fault tolerant process groupsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC identifies the documents specifying the official protocols used in the Internet.  Comments indicate any revisions or changes planned.  This memo is an official status report on the numbers used in protocols in the ARPA-Internet community.  Obsoletes RFC-961, 944 and 924.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej ⁄hfilenamefrfc991eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42893hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1011epages.opublicationDate©∂Ägrankingﬂcrfcc991irfcNumberﬂestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Official ARPA-Internet protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty=This Network Working Group Request for Comments documents the currently assigned values from several series of numbers used in network protocol implementations.  This memo is an official status report on the numbers used in protocols in the ARPA-Internet community.  See RFC-997.  Obsoletes RFC-960, 943, 923 and 900.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej Ÿhfilenamefrfc990eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42892hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1010epagesKopublicationDate©∂Ägrankingﬁcrfcc990irfcNumberﬁestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepAssigned numbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÅc997µhabstractyThis RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the Internet community and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  This RFC is the outgrowth of a series of IAB Privacy Task Force meetings and of internal working papers distributed for those meetings.  This RFC defines message encipherment and authentication procedures, as the initial phase of an effort to provide privacy enhancement services for electronic mail transfer in the Internet.  It is intended that the procedures defined here be compatible with a wide range of key management approaches, including both conventional (symmetric) and public-key (asymmetric) approaches for encryption of data encrypting keys.  Use of conventional cryptography for message text encryption and/or authentication is anticipated.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamegJ. Linnddatej…xhfilenamefrfc989eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42928hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd1040d1113epagesopublicationDate #␀Ägranking›crfcc989irfcNumber›estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexlPrivacy enhancement for Internet electronic mail: Part I: Message encipherment and authentication proceduresdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyõThis memo specifies the extensions required of a host implementation of the Internet Protocol (IP) to support internetwork multicasting.  This specification supersedes that given in RFC-966, and constitutes a proposed protocol standard for IP multicasting in the ARPA-Internet.  The reader is directed to RFC-966 for a discussion of the motivation and rationale behind the multicasting extension specified here.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS.E. Deeringddatej“4hfilenamefrfc988eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42899hkeywordsÇimulticasthInternetkobsoletedByÇd1054d1112epagesopublicationDategranking‹crfcc988irfcNumber‹estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex#Host extensions for IP multicastingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty0The X.400 series protocols have been defined by CCITT to provide an Interpersonal Messaging Service (IPMS), making use of a store and forward Message Transfer Service.  It is expected that this standard will be implemented very widely.  This document describes a set of mappings which will enable interworking between systems operating the X.400 protocols and systems using RFC-822 mail protocol or protocols derived from RFC-822.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejS.E. Killeddatej hfilenamefrfc987eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42924hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd1327d2156epagesEopublicationDateﬂÚgranking€crfcc987irfcNumber€estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex!Mapping between X.400 and RFC 822dtypecrfciupdatedByÉd1138d1148d1026µhabstracty¿This RFC suggests a method to allow the existing IP addressing, including the IP protocol field, to be used for the ISO Connectionless Network Protocol (CLNP).  This is a draft solution to one of the problems inherent in the use of "ISO-grams" in the DOD Internet.  Related issues will be discussed in subsequent RFCs.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Callon¢kaffiliation`dnamejH.W. Braunddatej hfilenamefrfc986eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42922hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1069epagesopublicationDateﬂÚgranking⁄crfcc986irfcNumber⁄estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex_Guidelines for the use of Internet-IP addresses in the ISO Connectionless-Mode Network ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyDThis RFC summarizes the requirements for gateways to be used on networks supporting the DARPA Internet protocols.  While it applies specifically to National Science Foundation research programs, the requirements are stated in a general context and are believed applicable throughout the Internet community.  The purpose of this document is to present guidance for vendors offering products that might be used or adapted for use in an Internet application.  It enumerates the protocols required and gives references to RFCs and other documents describing the current specification.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamexNational Science Foundation¢kaffiliation`dnamex Network Technical Advisory Groupddatej“Œhfilenamefrfc985eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42920hkeywordsÉlRequirementshInternethgatewayskobsoletedByÅd1009epagesopublicationDate∑pgrankingŸcrfcc985irfcNumberŸestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex*Requirements for Internet gateways - draftdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty\This document is a preliminary discussion of the design of a personal-computer-based distributed mail system.  Pcmail is a distributed mail system that provides mail service to an arbitrary number of users, each of which owns one or more personal computers (PCs).  The system is divided into two halves.  The first consists of a single entity called the "repository".  The repository is a storage center for incoming mail.  Mail for a Pcmail user can arrive externally from the Internet or internally from other repository users.  The repository also maintains a stable copy of each user's mail state.  The repository is therefore typically a computer with a large amount of disk storage.  It is published for discussion and comment, and does not constitute a standard.  As the proposal may change, implementation of this document is not advised.  See RFC-993.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.D. Clark¢kaffiliation`dnamelM.L. Lambertddatej —hfilenamefrfc984eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42918hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc993epagesopublicationDate∑pgrankingÿcrfcc984irfcNumberÿestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex8PCMAIL: A distributed mail system for personal computersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÔThis memo describes a proposed protocol standard for the ARPA Internet community.  The CCITT and the ISO have defined various session, presentation, and application recommendations which have been adopted by the international community and numerous vendors.  To the largest extent possible, it is desirable to offer these higher level services directly in the ARPA Internet, without disrupting existing facilities.  This permits users to develop expertise with ISO and CCITT applications which previously were not available in the ARPA Internet.  The intention is that hosts in the ARPA-Internet that choose to implement ISO TSAP services on top of the TCP be expected to adopt and implement this standard.  Suggestions for improvement are encouraged.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD.E. Cass¢kaffiliation`dnameiM.T. Roseddatej»¡hfilenamefrfc983eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42916hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1006epagesopublicationDateèïÄgranking◊crfcc983irfcNumber◊estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex'ISO transport arrives on top of the TCPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty=This RFC is a draft working document of the ANSI "Guidelines for the Specification of the Structure of the Domain Specific Part (DSP) of the ISO Standard NSAP Address".  It provides guidance to private address administration authorities on preferred formats and semantics for the Domain Specific Part (DSP) of an NSAP address.  This RFC specifies the way in which the DSP may be constructed so as to facilitate efficient address assignment.  This RFC is for informational purposes only and its distribution is unlimited and does not specify a standard of the ARPA-Internet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejH.W. Braunddatej»¡hfilenamefrfc982eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42915hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateèïÄgranking÷crfcc982irfcNumber÷estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlextGuidelines for the specification of the structure of the Domain Specific Part (DSP) of the ISO standard NSAP addressdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyFThis document introduces wiretap algorithms, a class of experimental, multiple routing algorithms that compute quasi-optimum routes for stations sharing a packet-radio broadcast channel.  The primary route (a minimum-distance path), and additional paths ordered by distance, which serve as alternate routes should the primary route fail, are computed.  This prototype is presented as an example of a class of routing algorithms and data-base management techniques that may find wider application in the Internet community.  Discussions and suggestions for improvements are welcomed.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej mhfilenamefrfc981eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42914hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef∑␀granking’crfcc981irfcNumber’estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex,Experimental multiple-path routing algorithmdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxıThis RFC indicates how to obtain various protocol documents used in the DARPA research community.  Included is an overview of the new 1985 DDN Protocol Handbook and available sources for obtaining related documents (such as DOD, ISO, and CCITT).darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemO.J. Jacobsen¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej mhfilenamefrfc980eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42913hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef∑␀granking‘crfcc980irfcNumber‘estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex#Protocol document order informationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyIThis memo is an updated version of BBN Report 5775, "End-to-End Functional Specification and describes important changes to the functionality of the interface between a Host and the PSN, and should be carefully reviewed by anyone involved in supporting a host on either the ARPANET or MILNET".  The new End-to-End protocol (EE) is being developed in order to correct a number of deficiencies in the old EE, to improve its performance and overall throughput, and to better equip the Packet Switch Node (PSN, also known as the IMP) to support its current and anticipated host population.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejA.G. Malisddatej lhfilenamefrfc979eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42912hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatef∑␀granking”crfcc979irfcNumber”estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex'PSN End-to-End functional specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxﬁThe purpose of the Voice File Interchange Protocol (VFIP) is to permit the interchange of various types of speech files between different systems in the ARPA-Internet community.  Suggestions for improvement are encouraged.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Gillman¢kaffiliation`dnameqW.A. Brackenridge¢kaffiliation`dnamelA. Witkowski¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej…xhfilenamefrfc978eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42911hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAÕ␀granking“crfcc978irfcNumber“estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex&Voice File Interchange Protocol (VFIP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyNNTP specifies a protocol for the distribution, inquiry, retrieval, and posting of news articles using a reliable stream-based transmission of news among the ARPA-Internet community.  NNTP is designed so that news articles are stored in a central database allowing a subscriber to select only those items he wishes to read.  Indexing, cross-referencing, and expiration of aged messages are also provided.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiB. Kantor¢kaffiliation`dnamejP. Lapsleyddatej—‚hfilenamefrfc977eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42910hkeywordsÅdNNTPkobsoletedByÅd3977epagesopublicationDateAÕ␀granking—crfcc977irfcNumber—estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexNetwork News Transfer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyhThis document defines the standard format for the transmission of mail messages between computers in the UUCP Project.  It does not however, address the format for storage of messages on one machine, nor the lower level transport mechanisms used to get the date from one machine to the next.  It represents a standard for conformance by hosts in the UUCP zone.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekM.R. Hortonddatej…xhfilenamefrfc976eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42908hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateAÕ␀granking–crfcc976irfcNumber–estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex%UUCP mail interchange format standarddtypecrfciupdatedByÅd1137µhabstractyãThis RFC proposes enhancements to the Exterior Gateway Protocol (EGP) to support a simple, multiple-level routing capability while preserving the robustness features of the current EGP model.  The enhancements generalize the concept of core system to include multiple communities of autonomous systems, called autonomous confederations.  Discussion and suggestions for improvement are requested.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej…xhfilenamefrfc975eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42907hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDateAÕ␀grankingœcrfcc975irfcNumberœestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexAutonomous confederationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractxThis RFC presents a description of how mail systems on the Internet are expected to route messages based on information from the domain system.  This involves a discussion of how mailers interpret MX RRs, which are used for message routing.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelC. Partridgeddatej“␀hfilenamefrfc974eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42693hkeywordsÅfDNS-MXkobsoletedByÅd2821epagesopublicationDateÓÄgrankingŒcrfcc974irfcNumberŒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber
etotaletitlex"Mail routing and the domain systemdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¬This RFC documents updates to Domain Name System specifications RFC-882 and RFC-883, suggests some operational guidelines, and discusses some experiences and problem areas in the present system.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenP. Mockapetrisddatej…§hfilenamefrfc973eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42810hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÇd1034d1035epages
opublicationDateÓÄgrankingÕcrfcc973irfcNumberÕestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex&Domain system changes and observationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyyThis RFC specifies a standard for the ARPA Internet community.  The Password Generator Service (PWDGEN) provides a set of six randomly generated eight-character "words" with a reasonable level of pronounceability, using a multi-level algorithm.  Hosts on the ARPA Internet that choose to implement a password generator service are expected to adopt and implement this standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekF.J. Wanchoddatej…§hfilenamefrfc972eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42906hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateÓÄgrankingÃcrfcc972irfcNumberÃestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexPassword Generator ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty_This RFC is a comparison of several data representation standards that are currently in use.  The standards discussed are the CCITT X.409 recommendation, the NBS Computer Based Message System (CBMS) standard, DARPA Multimedia Mail system, the Courier remote procedure call protocol, and the SUN Remote Procedure Call package.  No proposals in this document are intended as standards for the ARPA-Internet at this time.  Rather, it is hoped that a general consensus will emerge as to the appropriate approach to a data representation standard, leading eventually to the adoption of an ARPA-Internet standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelA.L. DeSchonddatej…£hfilenamefrfc971eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42905hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDateÓÄgrankingÀcrfcc971irfcNumberÀestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex'Survey of data representation standardsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÖThe purpose of this RFC is to focus discussion on a particular problem in the ARPA-Internet and possible methods of solution.  Most prior work on congestion in datagram systems focuses on buffer management.  In this memo the case of a packet switch with infinite storage is considered.  Such a packet switch can never run out of buffers.  It can, however, still become congested.  The meaning of congestion in an infinite-storage system is explored.  An unexpected result is found that shows a datagram network with infinite storage, first-in-first-out queuing, at least two packet switches, and a finite packet lifetime will, under overload, drop all packets.  By attacking the problem of congestion for the infinite-storage case, new solutions applicable to switches with finite storage may be found.  No proposed solutions this document are intended as standards for the ARPA-Internet at this time.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Nagleddatej…@hfilenamefrfc970eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42904hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages	opublicationDate␀granking crfcc970irfcNumber estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex(On Packet Switches With Infinite StoragedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyvThis RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  This is a preliminary discussion of the Network Block Transfer (NETBLT) protocol.  NETBLT is intended for the rapid transfer of a large quantity of data between computers.  It provides a transfer that is reliable and flow controlled, and is structured to provide maximum throughput over a wide variety of networks.  This description is published for discussion and comment, and does not constitute a standard.  As the proposal may change, implementation of this document is not advised.  See RFC-998.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.D. Clark¢kaffiliation`dnamelM.L. Lambert¢kaffiliation`dnamehL. Zhangddatej…@hfilenamefrfc969eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42902hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc998epagesopublicationDate␀granking…crfcc969irfcNumber…estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex%NETBLT: A bulk data transfer protocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxoThis memo discusses problems that arise and debugging techniques used in bringing a new network into operation.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiV.G. Cerfddatej…@hfilenamefrfc968eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42901hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate␀granking»crfcc968irfcNumber»estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexTwas the night before start-updtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÊThis RFC proposes a new set of RFCs on how the networking code is integrated with various operating systems.  It appears that this topic has not received enough exposure in the literature.  Comments and suggestions are encouraged.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM.A. Padlipskyddatej…@hfilenamefrfc967eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42900hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate␀granking«crfcc967irfcNumber«estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitletAll victims togetherdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx‰This RFC defines a model of service for Internet multicasting and proposes an extension to the Internet Protocol (IP) to support such a multicast service.  Discussion and suggestions for improvements are requested.  See RFC-988.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS.E. Deering¢kaffiliation`dnamemD.R. Cheritonddatej…@hfilenamefrfc966eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42898hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc988epagesopublicationDate␀granking∆crfcc966irfcNumber∆estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex;Host groups: A multicast extension to the Internet ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyGThis RFC describes the requirements for a graphical format on which to base a graphical on-line communication protocol, and proposes an Interactive Graphical Communication Format using the GKSM session metafile.  We hope this contribution will encourage the discussion of multimedia data exchange and the proposal of solutions.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejL. Aguilarddatej…@hfilenamefrfc965eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42897hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages3opublicationDate␀granking≈crfcc965irfcNumber≈estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex-Format for a graphical communication protocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty=The purpose of this RFC is to provide helpful information on the Military Standard Transmission Control Protocol (MIL-STD-1778) so that one can obtain a reliable implementation of this protocol standard.  This note points out three errors with this specification.  This note also proposes solutions to these problems.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.P. Sidhu¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Blumerddatej Ÿhfilenamefrfc964eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42896hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate»É␀grankingƒcrfcc964irfcNumberƒestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex[Some problems with the specification of the Military Standard Transmission Control ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty+The purpose of this RFC is to provide helpful information on the Military Standard Internet Protocol (MIL-STD-1777) so that one can obtain a reliable implementation of this protocol.  This paper points out several problems in this specification.  This note also proposes solutions to these problems.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.P. Sidhuddatej Ÿhfilenamefrfc963eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42895hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate»É␀granking√crfcc963irfcNumber√estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexOSome problems with the specification of the Military Standard Internet ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÿThis memo is in response to Bob Braden's call for a transaction oriented protocol (RFC-955), and continues the discussion of a possible transaction oriented transport protocol.  This memo does not propose a standard.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM.A. Padlipskyddatej Ÿhfilenamefrfc962eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42894hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate»É␀granking¬crfcc962irfcNumber¬estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlekTCP-4 primedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty?This memo identifies the documents specifying the official protocols used in the Internet, and comments on any revisions or changes planned.  This edition of the Official Protocols updates and obsoletes RFC-944.  This memo is an official status report on the protocols used in the ARPA-Internet community.  See RFC-991.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej…?hfilenamefrfc961eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42874hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc991epages&opublicationDate␀granking¡crfcc961irfcNumber¡estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Official ARPA-Internet protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty:This memo documents the currently assigned values from several series of numbers used in network protocol implementations.  This edition of Assigned Numbers updates and obsoletes RFC-943.  This memo is an official status report on the numbers used in protocols in the ARPA-Internet community.  See RFC-990 and 997.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej…>hfilenamefrfc960eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42873hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc990epages<opublicationDate␀granking¿crfcc960irfcNumber¿estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepAssigned numbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty*This memo is the official specification of the File Transfer Protocol (FTP) for the DARPA Internet community.  The primary intent is to clarify and correct the documentation of the FTP specification, not to change the protocol.  The following new optional commands are included in this edition of the specification: Change to Parent Directory (CDUP), Structure Mount (SMNT), Store Unique (STOU), Remove Directory (RMD), Make Directory (MKD), Print Directory (PWD), and System (SYST).  Note that this specification is compatible with the previous edition.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliation`dnamekJ. Reynoldsddatej“hfilenamefrfc959eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42682hkeywordsÅcFTPkobsoletedByÄepagesEopublicationDateüñpgrankingøcrfcc959irfcNumberøestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber	etotaletitlevFile Transfer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÜd3659d2640d2228d2773d7151d5797µhabstractyÙThis document describes the Network Time Protocol (NTP), a protocol for synchronizing a set of network clocks using a set of distributed clients and servers.  NTP is built on the User Datagram Protocol (UDP), which provides a connectionless transport mechanism.  It is evolved from the Time Protocol and the ICMP Timestamp message and is a suitable replacement for both.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej”hfilenamefrfc958eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42889hkeywordsÑcNTPdtimeeclockosynchronizationkobsoletedByÉd1059d1119d1305epagesopublicationDatex	pgrankingæcrfcc958irfcNumberæestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexNetwork Time Protocol (NTP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC discusses some experiments in clock synchronization in the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  One of the services frequently neglected in computer network design is a high-quality, time-of-day clock capable of generating accurate timestamps with small errors compared to one-way network delays.  Such a service would be useful for tracing the progress of complex transactions, synchronizing cached data bases, monitoring network performance and isolating problems.  In this memo one such clock service design will be described and its performance assessed.  This design has been incorporated as an integral part of the network routing and control protocols of the Distributed Computer Network (DCnet) architecture.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. MillsddatejÀEhfilenamefrfc957eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42888hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatex	pgrankingΩcrfcc957irfcNumberΩestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex,Experiments in network clock synchronizationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty•This RFC discussed clock synchronization algorithms for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  The recent interest within the Internet community in determining accurate time from a set of mutually suspicious network clocks has been prompted by several occasions in which errors were found in usually reliable, accurate clock servers after thunderstorms which disrupted their power supply.  To these sources of error should be added those due to malfunctioning hardware, defective software and operator mistakes, as well as random errors in the mechanism used to set and synchronize clocks.  This report suggests a stochastic model and algorithms for computing a good estimator from time-offset samples measured between clocks connected via network links.  Included in this report are descriptions of certain experiments which give an indication of the effectiveness of the algorithms.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. MillsddatejÀDhfilenamefrfc956eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42887hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatex	pgrankingºcrfcc956irfcNumberºestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+Algorithms for synchronizing network clocksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyﬁThe DoD Internet protocol suite includes two alternative transport service protocols, TCP and UDP, which provide virtual circuit and datagram service, respectively.  These two protocols represent points in the space of possible transport service attributes which are quite "far apart".  We want to examine an important class of applications, those which perform what is often called "transaction processing".  We will see that the communication needs for these applications fall into the gap "between" TCP and UDP -- neither protocol is very appropriate.  This RFC is concerned with the possible design of one or more new protocols for the ARPA-Internet, to support kinds of applications which are not well supported at present.  The RFC is intended to spur discussion in the Internet research community towards the development of new protocols and/or concepts, in order to meet these unmet application requirements.  It does not represent a standard, nor even a concrete protocol proposal.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekR.T. BradenddatejÀChfilenamefrfc955eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42886hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDatex	pgrankingªcrfcc955irfcNumberªestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexCTowards a transport service for transaction processing applicationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxóThis RFC is the official specification of the NICNAME/WHOIS protocol.  This memo describes the protocol and the service.  This is an update of RFC-812.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamenK. Harrenstien¢kaffiliation`dnamejM.K. Stahl¢kaffiliation`dnamelE.J. Feinlerddatej“hfilenamefrfc954eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42734hkeywordsÅgNICNAMEkobsoletedByÅd3912epagesopublicationDateüñpgranking∫crfcc954irfcNumber∫estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlemNICNAME/WHOISdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx™This RFC is the official specification of the Hostname Server Protocol.  This edition of the specification includes minor revisions to RFC-811 which brings it up to date.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamenK. Harrenstien¢kaffiliation`dnamejM.K. Stahl¢kaffiliation`dnamelE.J. Feinlerddatej“hfilenamefrfc953eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42732hkeywordsÅhHOSTNAMEkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateüñpgrankingπcrfcc953irfcNumberπestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitleoHostname ServerdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx≥This RFC is the official specification of the format of the Internet Host Table.  This edition of the specification includes minor revisions to RFC-810 which brings it up to date.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamenK. Harrenstien¢kaffiliation`dnamejM.K. Stahl¢kaffiliation`dnamelE.J. FeinlerddatejÀhfilenamefrfc952eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42730hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateüñpgranking∏crfcc952irfcNumber∏estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex%DoD Internet host table specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd1123µhabstractybThis RFC describes an IP/UDP bootstrap protocol (BOOTP) which allows a diskless client machine to discover its own IP address, the address of a server host, and the name of a file to be loaded into memory and executed.  The bootstrap operation can be thought of as consisting of TWO PHASES.  This RFC describes the first phase, which could be labeled `address determination and bootfile selection'.  After this address and filename information is obtained, control passes to the second phase of the bootstrap where a file transfer occurs.  The file transfer will typically use the TFTP protocol, since it is intended that both phases reside in PROM on the client.  However BOOTP could also work with other protocols such as SFTP or FTP.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejW.J. Croft¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ. Gilmoreddatej”hfilenamefrfc951eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42882hkeywordsÅeBOOTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatex	pgranking∑crfcc951irfcNumber∑estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamenDraft Standarddslugbdsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlerBootstrap ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÖd1395d1497d1542d5494d1532∂habstracty˘This memo discusses the utility of "subnets" of Internet networks, which are logically visible sub-sections of a single Internet network.  For administrative or technical reasons, many organizations have chosen to divide one Internet network into several subnets, instead of acquiring a set of Internet network numbers.  This memo specifies procedures for the use of subnets.  These procedures are for hosts (e.g., workstations).  The procedures used in and between subnet gateways are not fully described.  Important motivation and background information for a subnetting standard is provided in RFC-940.  This RFC specifies a protocol for the ARPA-Internet community.  If subnetting is implemented it is strongly recommended that these procedures be followed.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcDECdnamejJ.C. Mogul¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiJ. Postelddatej—çhfilenamefrfc950eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42710hkeywordsÅgAddresskobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateO*granking∂crfcc950irfcNumber∂estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlex&Internet Standard Subnetting ProceduredtypecrfciupdatedByÅd6918µhabstractyµThere are various contexts in which it would be desirable to have an FTP command that had the effect of the present STOR but rather than requiring the sender to specify a file name istead caused the resultant file to have a unique name relative to the current directory.  This RFC proposes an extension to the File Transfer Protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  See RFC-959.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM.A. Padlipskyddatej…Îhfilenamefrfc949eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42881hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate&Lpgrankingµcrfcc949irfcNumberµestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexFTP unique-named store commanddtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxÏThis RFC describes two methods of encapsulating Internet Protocol (IP) datagrams on an IEEE 802.3 network.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejI. Winstonddatej hfilenamefrfc948eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42879hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1042epagesopublicationDate˛øpgranking¥crfcc948irfcNumber¥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexITwo methods for the transmission of IP datagrams over IEEE 802.3 networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxöThis RFC describes the extension of a network's broadcast domain to include more than one physical network through the use of a broadcast packet repeater.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamekK. Lebowitz¢kaffiliation`dnamejD. Mankinsddatej hfilenamefrfc947eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42878hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate˛øpgranking≥crfcc947irfcNumber≥estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex.Multi-network broadcasting within the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyyMany systems provide a mechanism for finding out where a user is logged in from usually including information about telephone extension and office occupants names.  The information is useful for physically locating people and/or calling them on the phone.  In 1982 CMU designed and implemented a terminal location database and modified existing network software to handle a 64-bit number called the Terminal Location Number (or TTYLOC).  It now seems appropriate to incorporate this mechanism into the TCP-based network protocol family.  The mechanism is not viewed as a replacement for the Terminal Location Telnet Option (SEND-LOCATION) but as a shorthand mechansim for communicating terminal location information between hosts in a localized community.  This RFC proposes a new option for Telnet for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Nedvedddatej“Œhfilenamefrfc946eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42877hkeywordsÅhTOPT-TLNkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate’‡granking≤crfcc946irfcNumber≤estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex&Telnet terminal location number optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyyIn May 1983 the National Research Council (NRC) was asked jointly by DoD and NBS to study the issues and recommend a course of action.  The final report of the NRC committee was published in February 1985 (see RFC-942).  The enclosed letter is from Donald C.  Latham (ASDC3I) to DCA transmitting the NRC report and requesting specific actions relative to the recommendations of the report.  This RFC reproduces a letter from the Assistant Secretary of Defense for Command, Control, Communications, and Intelligence (ASDC3I) to the Director of the Defense Communications Agency (DCA).  This letter is distributed for information only.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej –hfilenamefrfc945eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42875hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1039epagesopublicationDate’‡granking±crfcc945irfcNumber±estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexDoD statement on the NRC reportdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÑThis RFC identifies the documents specifying the official protocols used in the Internet.  This edition of Official ARPA-Internet Protocols obsoletes RFC-924 and earlier editions.  This RFC will be updated periodically, and current information can be obtained from Joyce Reynolds.  This memo is an official status report on the protocols used in the ARPA-Internet community.  See RFC-991.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej»¿hfilenamefrfc944eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42855hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc961epages(opublicationDateÆb␀granking∞crfcc944irfcNumber∞estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Official ARPA-Internet protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty~This Network Working Group Request for Comments documents the currently assigned values from several series of numbers used in network protocol implementations.  This RFC will be updated periodically, and in any case current information can be obtained from Joyce Reynolds.  The assignment of numbers is also handled by Joyce.  If you are developing a protocol or application that will require the use of a link, socket, port, protocol, network number, etc., please contact Joyce to receive a number assignment.  This memo is an official status report on the numbers used in protocols in the ARPA-Internet community.  See RFC-990 and 997.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej»¿hfilenamefrfc943eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42854hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc960epages2opublicationDateÆb␀grankingØcrfcc943irfcNumberØestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepAssigned numbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractxˆThis RFC reproduces the National Research Council report resulting from a study of the DoD Internet Protocol (IP) and Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) in comparison with the ISO Internet Protocol (ISO-IP) and Transport Protocol level 4 (TP-4).darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamexNational Research Councilddatej…xhfilenamefrfc942eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42872hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesXopublicationDate`ôÄgrankingÆcrfcc942irfcNumberÆestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex;Transport protocols for Department of Defense data networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyjThis Addendum to the Network Service Definition Standard, ISO 8348, defines the abstract syntax and semantics of the Network Address (Network Service Access Point Address).  The Network Address defined in this Addendum is the address that appears in the primitives of the connection-mode Network Service as the calling address, called address, and responding address parameters, and in the primitives of the connectionless-mode Network Service as the source address and destination address parameters.  This document is distributed as an RFC for information only.  It does not specify a standard for the ARPA-Internet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex.International Organization for Standardizationddatej»¿hfilenamefrfc941eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42871hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages"opublicationDateÆb␀granking≠crfcc941irfcNumber≠estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexLAddendum to the network service definition covering network layer addressingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty’Several sites now contain a complex of local links connected to the Internet via a gateway.  The details of the internal connectivity are of little interest to the rest of the Internet.  One way of organizing these local complexes of links is to use the same strategy as the Internet uses to organize networks, that is, to declare each link to be an entity (like a network) and to interconnect the links with devices that perform routing functions (like gateways).  This general scheme is called subnetting, the individual links are called subnets, and the connecting devices are called subgateways (or bridges, or gateways).  This RFC discusses standardizing the protocol used in subnetted environments in the ARPA-Internet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex1Gateway Algorithms and Data Structures Task Forceddatej»øhfilenamefrfc940eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42870hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateÆb␀granking¨crfcc940irfcNumber¨estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex1Toward an Internet standard scheme for subnettingdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyfThis RFC reproduces the material from the "front pages" of the National Research Council report resulting from a study of the DOD Internet Protocol (IP) and Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) in comparison with the ISO Internet Protocol (ISO-IP) and Transport Protocol level 4 (TP-4).  The point of this RFC is to make the text of the Executive Summary widely available in a timely way.  The order of presentation has been altered, and the pagination changed.  This RFC is distributed for information only.  This RFC does not establish any policy for the DARPA research community or the DDN operational community.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamexNational Research Councilddatej…xhfilenamefrfc939eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42869hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ôÄgranking´crfcc939irfcNumber´estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexbExecutive summary of the NRC report on transport protocols for Department of Defense data networksdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyøThis RFC is being distributed to members of the DARPA research community in order to solicit their reactions to the proposals contained in it.  While the issues discussed may not be directly relevant to the research problems of the DARPA community, they may be interesting to a number of researchers and implementors.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiT. Millerddatej—‚hfilenamefrfc938eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42868hkeywordsÅdIRTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ôÄgranking™crfcc938irfcNumber™estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexMInternet Reliable Transaction Protocol functional and interface specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC suggests a simple method for workstations to dynamically access mail from a mailbox server.  This RFC specifies a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvement.  This memo is a revision of RFC-918.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÖ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Butler¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliation`dnamehD. Chase¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ. Goldberger¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynoldsddatej—‚hfilenamefrfc937eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42850hkeywordsÉdPOP2tPost Office ProtocoliVersion 2kobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ôÄgranking©crfcc937irfcNumber©estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexPost Office Protocol: Version 2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty@There have been several proposals for schemes to allow the use of a single Internet network number to refer to a collection of physical networks under common administration which are reachable from the rest of the Internet by a common route.  Such schemes allow a simplified view of an otherwise complicated topology from hosts and gateways outside of this collection.  They allow the complexity of the number and type of these networks, and routing to them, to be localized.  Additions and changes in configuration thus cause no detectable change, and no interruption of service, due to slow propagation of routing and other information outside of the local environment.  These schemes also simplify the administration of the network, as changes do not require allocation of new network numbers for each new cable installed.  This proposal discusses an alternative scheme, one that has been in use at the University of California, Berkeley since April 1984.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekM.J. Karelsddatej…whfilenamefrfc936eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42867hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate`ôÄgranking®crfcc936irfcNumber®estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex)Another Internet subnet addressing schemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyxThis RFC discusses protocols proposed recently in RFCs 914 and 916, and suggests a proposed protocol that could meet the same needs addressed in those memos.  The stated need is reliable communication between two programs over a full-duplex, point-to-point communication link, and in particular the RFCs address the need for such communication over an asynchronous link at relatively low speeds.  The suggested protocol uses the methods of existing national and international data link layer standards.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.G. Robinsonddatej…¢hfilenamefrfc935eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42866hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate7ª␀grankingßcrfcc935irfcNumberßestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexReliable link layer protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis memo concerns itself with message forwarding.  Forwarding can be thought of as encapsulating one or more messages inside another.  Although this is useful for transfer of past correspondence to new recipients, without a decapsulation process (which this memo terms "bursting"), the forwarded messages are of little use to the recipients because they can not be distributed, forwarded, replied-to, or otherwise processed as separate individual messages.  In order to burst a message it is necessary to know how the component messages were encapsulated in the draft.  At present there is no unambiguous standard for interest group digests.  This RFC proposes a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM.T. Rose¢kaffiliation`dnamenE.A. Stefferudddatej…†hfilenamefrfc934eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42865hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages
opublicationDate7ª␀granking¶crfcc934irfcNumber¶estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+Proposed standard for message encapsulationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx€This proposed option would allow a Server-Telnet to send a banner to a User-Telnet so that this banner would be displayed on the workstation screen independently of the application software running in the Server-Telnet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelS. Silvermanddatej—çhfilenamefrfc933eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42864hkeywordsÅgTOPT-OMkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate7ª␀granking•crfcc933irfcNumber•estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexOutput marking Telnet optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty This RFC proposes an alternative addressing scheme for subnets which, in most cases, requires no modification to host software whatsoever.  The drawbacks of this scheme are that the total number of subnets in any one network are limited, and that modification is required to all gateways.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.D. Clarkddatej»ˆhfilenamefrfc932eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42863hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate7ª␀granking§crfcc932irfcNumber§estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexSubnetwork addressing schemedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyhThis RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  This is the second draft of this proposal (superseding RFC-912) and incorporates a more formal description of the syntax for the request and response dialog, as well as a change to specify the type of user identification returned.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. St. Johnsddatej»hfilenamefrfc931eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42843hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1413epagesopublicationDate7ª␀granking£crfcc931irfcNumber£estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitleuAuthentication serverdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyáThis RFC specifies a standard for the ARPA Internet community.  Hosts on the ARPA Internet that exchange terminal type information within the Telnet protocol are expected to adopt and implement this standard.  This standard supersedes RFC-884.  The only change is to specify that the TERMINAL-TYPE IS sub-negotiation should be sent only in response to the TERMINAL-TYPE SEND sub-negotiation.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Solomon¢kaffiliation`dnamejE. Wimmersddatej»Ôhfilenamefrfc930eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42813hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1091epagesopublicationDate7ª␀granking¢crfcc930irfcNumber¢estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexTelnet terminal type optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÈThe Host-Front End Protocol introduced in RFC-928 is described in detail in this memo.  The first order of business is to declare that THIS IS A PROPOSAL, NOT A FINAL STANDARD, and the second order of business is to request that any readers of these documents who are able to do test implementations (a) do so and (b) coordinate their efforts with the author.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ. Lilienkamp¢kaffiliation`dnamejR. Mandell¢kaffiliation`dnamenM.A. Padlipskyddatej—ºhfilenamefrfc929eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42861hkeywordsÅdHFEPkobsoletedByÄepages8opublicationDate‹Ägranking°crfcc929irfcNumber°estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Proposed Host-Front End ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty9The broad outline of the Host-Front End Protocol introduced here and described in RFC-929 is the result of the deliberations of a number of experienced H-FP designers, who sat as a committee of the DoD Protocol Standards Technical Panel.  It is the intent of the designers that the protocol be subjected to multiple test implementations and probable iteration before being agreed upon as any sort of "standard".  Therefore, the first order of business is to declare that THIS IS A PROPOSAL, NOT A FINAL STANDARD, and the second order of business is to request that any readers of these documents who are able to do test implementations (a) do so and (b) coordinate their efforts with the author.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamenM.A. Padlipskyddatej…>hfilenamefrfc928eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42860hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate‹Ägranking†crfcc928irfcNumber†estateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex*Introduction to proposed DoD standard H-FPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyËThe following is the description of a TELNET option designed to facilitate double login avoidance.  It is intended primarily for TAC connections to target hosts on behalf of TAC users, but it can be used between any two consenting hosts.  For example, all hosts at one site (e.g., BBN) can use this option to avoid double login when TELNETing to one another.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemB.A. Andersonddatej—ºhfilenamefrfc927eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42859hkeywordsÅkTOPT-TACACSkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate‹Ägrankingücrfcc927irfcNumberüestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqProposed Standarddslugbpsfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex(TACACS user identification Telnet optiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractysThis note is the draft ISO protocol roughly similar to the DOD Internet Protocol.  This document has been prepared by retyping the text of ISO DIS 8473 of May 1984, which is currently undergoing voting within ISO as a Draft International Standard (DIS).  This document is distributred as an RFC for information only.  It does not specify a standard for the ARPA-Internet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex.International Organization for Standardizationddatej…>hfilenamefrfc926eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42857hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc994epageskopublicationDate‹Ägrankingûcrfcc926irfcNumberûestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex?Protocol for providing the connectionless mode network servicesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty…The problem of treating a set of local area networks (LANs) as one Internet network has generated some interest and concern.  It is inappropriate to give each LAN within an site a distinct Internet network number.  It is desirable to hide the details of the interconnections between the LANs within an site from people, gateways, and hosts outside the site.  The question arises on how to best do this, and even how to do it at all.  In RFC-917 Jeffery Mogul makes a case for the use of "explicit subnets" in a multi-LAN environment.  The explicit subnet scheme is a call to recursively apply the mechanisms the Internet uses to manage networks to the problem of managing LANs within one network.  In this note I urge another approach: the use of "transparent subnets" supported by a multi-LAN extension of the Address Resolution Protocol.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiJ. PostelddatejÀhfilenamefrfc925eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42856hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateæbgrankingùcrfcc925irfcNumberùestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexMulti-LAN address resolutiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC identifies the documents specifying the official protocols used in the Internet.  This edition of Official ARPA-Internet Protocols obsoletes RFC-900 and earlier editions.  This memo is an official status report on the protocols used in the ARPA-Internet community.  See RFC-991.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. PostelddatejÀhfilenamefrfc924eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42831hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc944epages#opublicationDateæbgrankingúcrfcc924irfcNumberúestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexROfficial ARPA-Internet protocols for connecting personal computers to the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyCThis RFC documents the currently assigned values from several series of numbers used in network protocol implementations.  This edition of Assigned Numbers obsoletes RFC-900 and earlier editions.  This memo is an official status report on the numbers used in protocols in the ARPA-Internet community.  See RFC-990, and 997.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. PostelddatejÀhfilenamefrfc923eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42830hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc943epages/opublicationDateæbgrankingõcrfcc923irfcNumberõestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepAssigned numbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty+We propose simple rules for broadcasting Internet datagrams on local networks that support broadcast, for addressing broadcasts, and for how gateways should handle them.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ.C. MogulddatejÀhfilenamefrfc922eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42853hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateæbgrankingöcrfcc922irfcNumberöestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlex:Broadcasting Internet datagrams in the presence of subnetsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyìThis memo is a policy statement on the implementation of the Domain Style Naming System in the Internet.  This memo is an update of RFC-881, and RFC-897.  This is an official policy statement of the IAB and the DARPA.  The intent of this memo is to detail the schedule for the implementation for the Domain Style Naming System.  The explanation of how this system works is to be found in the references.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. PostelddatejÀhfilenamefrfc921eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42827hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateæbgrankingôcrfcc921irfcNumberôestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex4Domain name system implementation schedule - reviseddtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty9This memo states the requirements on establishing a Domain, and introduces the limited set of top level domains.  This memo is a policy statement on the requirements of establishing a new domain in the ARPA-Internet and the DARPA research community.  This is an official policy statement of the IAB and the DARPA.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. ReynoldsddatejÀhfilenamefrfc920eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42852hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateæbgrankingòcrfcc920irfcNumberòestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlesDomain requirementsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty2This RFC proposes simple rules for broadcasting Internet datagrams on local networks that support broadcast, for addressing broadcasts, and for how gateways should handle them.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcDECdnamejJ.C. MogulddatejÀhfilenamefrfc919eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42851hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateæbgrankingócrfcc919irfcNumberóestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlexBroadcasting Internet DatagramsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty4This RFC suggests a simple method for workstations to dynamically access mail from a mailbox server.  The intent of the Post Office Protocol (POP) is to allow a user's workstation to access mail from a mailbox server.  It is expected that mail will be posted from the workstation to the mailbox server via the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP).  This RFC specifies a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvement.  The status of this protocol is experimental, and this protocol is dependent upon TCP.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. ReynoldsddatejÀhfilenamefrfc918eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42849hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc937epagesopublicationDateæbgrankingñcrfcc918irfcNumberñestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitletPost Office ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty<This memo discusses subnets and proposes procedures for the use of subnets, including approaches to solving the problems that arise, particularly that of routing.  A subnet of an Internet network is a logically visible sub-section of a single Internet network.  For administrative or technical reasons, many organizations have chosen to divide one Internet network into several subnets, instead of acquiring a set of Internet network numbers.  This RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ.C. MogulddatejÀhfilenamefrfc917eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42848hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateæbgrankingïcrfcc917irfcNumberïestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepInternet subnetsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC suggests a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  This paper proposes and specifies a protocol which allows two programs to reliably communicate over a communication link.  It ensures that the data entering one end of the link if received arrives at the other end intact and unaltered.  The protocol, named RATP, is designed to operate over a full duplex point-to-point connection.  It contains some features which tailor it to the RS-232 links now in common use.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiG.G. Finnddatej“hfilenamefrfc916eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42847hkeywordsÅdRATPkobsoletedByÄepages6opublicationDateæbgrankingîcrfcc916irfcNumberîestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex.Reliable Asynchronous Transfer Protocol (RATP)dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÓThis RFC proposed a new service for the ARPA-Internet community and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.  The network mail path service fills the current need of people to determine mailbox addresses for hosts that are not part of the ARPA-Internet but can be reached by one or more relay hosts that have Unix to Unix Copy (UUCP) mail, CSNET mail, MAILNET mail, BITNET mail, etc.  Anyone can use the service if they have TCP/TELENET to one of the hosts with a mail path server.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM.A. Elvy¢kaffiliation`dnameiR. Nedvedddatej…>hfilenamefrfc915eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42846hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate‹Ägrankingìcrfcc915irfcNumberìestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexNetwork mail path servicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty®This RFC focuses discussion on the particular problems in the ARPA-Internet of low speed network interconnection with personal computers, and possible methods of solution.  None of the proposed solutions in this document are intended as standards for the ARPA-Internet.  Rather, it is hoped that a general consensus will emerge as to the appropriate solution to the problems, leading eventually to the adoption of standards.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamekD.J. Farber¢kaffiliation`dnamegG. Delp¢kaffiliation`dnamejT.M. Conteddatej”hfilenamefrfc914eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42845hkeywordsÅhTHINWIREkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateñ’grankingícrfcc914irfcNumberíestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexCThinwire protocol for connecting personal computers to the InternetdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyßThis memo describes a proposed Simple File Transfer Protocol (SFTP).  It fills the need of people wanting a protocol that is more useful than TFTP but easier to implement (and less powerful) than FTP.  SFTP supports user access control, file transfers, directory listing, directory changing, file renaming and deleting.  Discussion of this proposal is encouraged, and suggestions for improvements may be sent to the author.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiM. Lottorddatej”hfilenamefrfc913eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42844hkeywordsÇdSFTPcFTPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateñ’grankingëcrfcc913irfcNumberëestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexSimple File Transfer ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyƒThis memo describes a proposed authentication protocol for verifying the identity of a user of a TCP connection.  Given a TCP port number pair, it returns a character string which identifies the owner of that connection on the server's system.  Suggested uses include automatic identification and verification of a user during an FTP session, additional verification of a TAC dial up user, and access verification for a generalized network file server.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM. St. JohnsddatejÀChfilenamefrfc912eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42842hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc931epagesopublicationDateñ’grankingêcrfcc912irfcNumberêestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlevAuthentication servicedtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx¯This memo describes an implementation of the Exterior Gateway Protocol (EGP) (in that sense it is a status report).  The memo also discusses some possible extentions and some design issues (in that sense it is an invitation for further discussion).darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnameiP. Kirtonddatej…hfilenamefrfc911eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42841hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatem˜pgrankingècrfcc911irfcNumberèestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex#EGP Gateway under Berkeley UNIX 4.2dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty≥This memo is a report on a meeting about the experimental multimedia mail system (and in a sense a status report on that experiment).  The meeting was held at Bolt Beranek and Newman on 23-24 July 1984 to discuss recent progress by groups who are building multimedia mail systems and to discuss a variety of issues related to the further development of multimedia systems.  Representatives were present from BBN, ISI, SRI and Linkabit.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamemH.C. Forsdickddatej»¸hfilenamefrfc910eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42840hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDatem˜pgrankingécrfcc910irfcNumberéestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexMultimedia mail meeting notesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty0The Loader Debugger Protocol (LDP) is an application layer protocol for loading, dumping, and debugging target machines from hosts in a network environment.  This RFC specifies a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet and DARPA research community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvemts.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiC. Welles¢kaffiliation`dnamekW. Millikenddatej“4hfilenamefrfc909eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42839hkeywordsÅcLDPkobsoletedByÄepagesáopublicationDateEgrankingçcrfcc909irfcNumberçestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexLoader Debugger ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThe Reliable Data Protocol (RDP) is designed to provide a reliable data transport service for packet-based applications.  This RFC specifies a proposed protocol for the ARPA-Internet and DARPA research community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvemts.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnameiD. Velten¢kaffiliation`dnamekR.M. Hinden¢kaffiliation`dnamefJ. Saxddatej“4hfilenamefrfc908eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42837hkeywordsÅcRDPkobsoletedByÄepages>opublicationDateEgrankingåcrfcc908irfcNumberåestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamelExperimentaldslugcexpfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlevReliable Data ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÅd1151∂habstractyFThis document specifies the Host Access Protocol (HAP).  Although HAP was originally designed as the network-access level protocol for the DARPA/DCA sponsored Wideband Packet Satellite Network, it is intended that it evolve into a standard interface SATNET and TACNET (aka MATNET) as well as the Wideband Network.  HAP is an experimental protocol, and will undergo further revision as new capabilities are added and/or different satellite networks are suported.  Implementations of HAP should be performed in coordination with satellite network development and operations personnel.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationkunspecifieddnamex$Bolt Beranek and Newman Laboratoriesddatej“4hfilenamefrfc907eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42835hkeywordsÅeIP-WBkobsoletedByÄepagesKopublicationDateEgrankingãcrfcc907irfcNumberãestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber(etotaletitlex"Host Access Protocol specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd1221µhabstractx∂It is often convenient to be able to bootstrap a computer system from a communications network.  This RFC proposes the use of the IP TFTP protocol for bootstrap loading in this case.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelR. Finlaysonddatej hfilenamefrfc906eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42834hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateãgrankingäcrfcc906irfcNumberäestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexBootstrap loading using TFTPdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty,This is the current specification of the ISO Transport Protocol.  This document is the text of ISO/TC97/SC16/N1576 as corrected by ISO/TC97/SC16/N1695.  This is the specification currently being voted on in ISO as a Draft International Standard (DIS).  This document is distributed as an RFC for your information only, it does not specify a standard for the ARPA-Internet or DARPA research community.  Our thanks to Alex McKenzie of BBN for making this online version available.  Please note the size of this document, the file contains 258,729 characters.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamecISOddatej»øhfilenamefrfc905eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42822hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepages§opublicationDateÕ.Ägrankingâcrfcc905irfcNumberâestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex0ISO Transport Protocol specification ISO DP 8073dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyRFC-904 is the specification of the Exterior Gateway Protocol (EGP).  This memo updates portions of RFC-888 and RFC-827.  This RFC specifies an official protocol of the DARPA community for use between gateways of different autonomous systems in the ARPA-Internet.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejD.L. Millsddatej—fhfilenamefrfc904eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42754hkeywordsÅcEGPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateÕ.Ägrankingàcrfcc904irfcNumberàestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex.Exterior Gateway Protocol formal specificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstractyUThis RFC suggests a method for workstations to dynamically find their protocol address (e.g., their Internet Address), when they know only their hardware address (e.g., their attached physical network address).  This RFC specifies a proposed protocol for the ARPA Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationsStanford UniversitydnamelR. Finlayson¢kaffiliation`dnamegT. Mann¢kaffiliation`dnamejJ.C. Mogul¢kaffiliation`dnamejM. Theimerddatej“Shfilenamefrfc903eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42833hkeywordsÅdRARPkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateãgrankingácrfcc903irfcNumberáestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumber&etotaletitlex%A Reverse Address Resolution ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyÊThe purpose of this memo is to explain how protocol standards are adopted for the ARPA-Internet and the DARPA research community.  There are three important aspects to be discussed: the process, the authority, and the complex relationship between the DARPA community and the DDN community.  This memo is a policy statement on how protocols become official standards for the ARPA-Internet and the DARPA research community.  This is an official policy statement of the ICCB and the DARPA.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej…Íhfilenamefrfc902eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42832hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateEgrankingÜcrfcc902irfcNumberÜestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexARPA Internet Protocol policydtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC identifies the documents specifying the official protocols used in the ARPA-Internet.  Annotations identify any revisions or changes planned.  This memo is an official status report on the protocols used in the DARPA research community.  See RFC-991.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej hfilenamefrfc901eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42804hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc924epagesopublicationDateãgrankingÖcrfcc901irfcNumberÖestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex Official ARPA-Internet protocolsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis RFC specifies parameter values use in the Internet family of protocols, such as network numbers, well known ports, protocol types, and version numbers.  This memo is an official status report on the protocol parameters used in the Internet protocol system.  See RFC-990 and 997.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynolds¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej hfilenamefrfc900eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42795hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc923epages+opublicationDateãgrankingÑcrfcc900irfcNumberÑestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamehHistoricdslugdhistfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlepAssigned NumbersdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstract`darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliationcISIdnamejA. Westineddatej Õhfilenamefrfc899eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42829hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateÙ≠pgrankingÉcrfcc899irfcNumberÉestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamemInformationaldslugcinffstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+Request For Comments summary notes: 800-899dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyThis memo is a report on the Gateway Special Interest Group Meeting that was held at ISI on 28 and 29 February 1984.  Robert Hinden of BBNCC chaired, and Jon Postel of ISI hosted the meeting.  Approximately 35 gateway designers and implementors attended.  These notes are based on the recollections of Jon Postel and Mike Muuss.  Under each topic area are Jon Postel's brief notes, and additional details from Mike Muuss.  This memo is a report on a meeting.  No conclusions, decisions, or policy statements are documented in this note.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÑ¢kaffiliationcBBNdnamekR.M. Hinden¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliationcBRLdnamehM. Muuss¢kaffiliationcISIdnamemJ.K. Reynoldsddatej»øhfilenamefrfc898eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42828hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateÕ.ÄgrankingÇcrfcc898irfcNumberÇestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex,Gateway special interest group meeting notesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyRThis memo is a policy statement on the implementation of the Domain Style Naming System in the Internet.  This memo is a partial update of RFC 881.  The intent of this memo is to detail the schedule for the implementation for the Domain Style Naming System.  The names of hosts will be changed to domain style names.  Hosts will begin to use domain style names on 14-Mar-84, and the use of old style names will be completely phased out before 2-May-84.  This applies to both the ARPA research hosts and the DDN operational hosts.  This is an official policy statement of the ICCB and the DARPA.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliationcISIdnameiJ. Postelddatej…whfilenamefrfc897eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42806hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate~ÄgrankingÅcrfcc897irfcNumberÅestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex*Domain name system implementation scheduledtypecrfciupdatedByÅc921µhabstractyThis memo discusses some aspects of congestion control in IP/TCP Internetworks.  It is intended to stimulate thought and further discussion of this topic.  While some specific suggestions are made for improved congestion control implementation, this memo does not specify any standards.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamehJ. Nagledda