is RFC specifies a standard for the ARPA Internet community.  Hosts on the ARPA Internet are expected to adopt and implement this standard.  Obsoletes RFC 651, NIC 18640.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynoldsddatej“Õhfilenamefrfc855eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42781hkeywordsÅfTELNETkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate(pgrankingWcrfcc855irfcNumberWestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlexTelnet Option SpecificationsdtypecrfciupdatedByÄ∂habstracty±This is the specification of the Telnet protocol used for remote terminal access in the ARPA Internet.  The purpose of the TELNET Protocol is to provide a fairly general, bi-directional, eight-bit byte oriented communications facility.  Its primary goal is to allow a standard method of interfacing terminal devices and terminal-oriented processes to each other.  It is envisioned that the protocol may also be used for terminal-terminal communication ("linking") and process-process communication (distributed computation).  This RFC specifies a standard for the ARPA Internet community.  Hosts on the ARPA Internet are expected to adopt and implement this standard.  Obsoletes NIC 18639.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÇ¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postel¢kaffiliation`dnamemJ.K. Reynoldsddatej“Õhfilenamefrfc854eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedıegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42681hkeywordsÅfTELNETkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate(pgrankingVcrfcc854irfcNumberVestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnameqInternet Standarddslugcstdfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyisubseries£gacronymcstdfnumberetotaletitlexTelnet Protocol SpecificationdtypecrfciupdatedByÅd5198µhabstractyÈThis RFC specifies the ARPANET Short Blocking Feature, which will allow ARPANET hosts to optionally shorten the IMP's host blocking timer.  This Feature is a replacement of the ARPANET non-blocking host interface, which was never implemented, and will be available to hosts using either the 1822 or 1822L Host Access Protocol.  This RFC is also being presented as a solicitation of comments on the Short Blocking Feature, especially from host network software implementers and maintainers.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejA.G. Malisddatej»øhfilenamefrfc852eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42780hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateÍ©ÄgrankingTcrfcc852irfcNumberTestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexARPANET short blocking featuredtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractyéThis RFC specifies the ARPANET 1822L Host Access Protocol, which is a successor to the existing 1822 Host Access Protocol.  1822L allows ARPANET hosts to use logical names as well as 1822's physical port locations to address each other.  This RFC is also being presented as a solicitation of comments on 1822L, especially from host network software implementers and maintainers.  Obsoletes RFC 802.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamejA.G. Malisddatej»Ãhfilenamefrfc851eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42721hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅc878epages/opublicationDateÍ©ÄgrankingScrfcc851irfcNumberSestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex"ARPANET 1822L Host Access ProtocoldtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty5This memo is distributed as an RFC only to make this information easily accessible to researchers in the ARPA community.  It does not specify an Internet standard.  This RFC defines the standard format for interchange of Network News articles among USENET sites.  It describes the format for articles themselves, and gives partial standards for transmission of news.  The news transmission is not entirely standardized in order to give a good deal of flexibility to the individual hosts to choose transmission hardware and software, whether to batch news and so on.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamekM.R. Hortonddatej hfilenamefrfc850eflags£mhiddenDefaultıiobsoletedıgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42777hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÅd1036epagesopublicationDate;grankingRcrfcc850irfcNumberRestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex+Standard for interchange of USENET messagesdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty|This RFC actually is a request for comments.  The issue dealt with is that of a naming registry update procedure, both as exists currently and what could exist in the future.  None of the proposed solutions are intended as standards at this time; rather it is hoped that a general consensus will emerge as the appropriate solution, leaving eventually to the adoption of standards.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelM.R. Crispinddatej œhfilenamefrfc849eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42776hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate(pgrankingQcrfcc849irfcNumberQestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex0Suggestions for improved host table distributiondtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx‰This RFC lists those hosts which provide any of these "little" TCP services: The list of hosts were taken from the NIC hostname table of 24-Feb-83.  The tests were run on February 23 and 24, and March 3 and 5 from ISI-VAXA.ARPA.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. Smallbergddatej shfilenamefrfc848eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42775hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDate¡À␀grankingPcrfcc848irfcNumberPestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlex'Who provides the "little" TCP services?dtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstracty}This is a summary of the surveys of Telnet, FTP and Mail (SMTP) servers conducted by David Smallberg in December 1982, January and February 1983 as reported in RFC 832-843, 845-846.  This memo extracts the number of hosts that accepted the connection to their server for each of Telnet, FTP, and SMTP, and compares it to the total host in the Internet (not counting TACs or ECHOS).darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÉ¢kaffiliation`dnamejA. Westine¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. Smallberg¢kaffiliation`dnameiJ. Postelddatej…whfilenamefrfc847eflags£mhiddenDefaultÙiobsoletedÙgupdatedÙegroup§gacronymdnonedfullxnone - Individual SubmissionsdnamevIndividual Submissionsdtypegindividbididoc-42774hkeywordsÄkobsoletedByÄepagesopublicationDateú·␀grankingOcrfcc847irfcNumberOestateÅiPublishedfstatus¢dnamegUnknowndslugdunknfstream¢dnamefLegacydslugflegacyetitlexSummary of Smallberg surveysdtypecrfciupdatedByÄµhabstractx‡This RFC is a survey of hosts to identify the implementation status of Telnet, FTP, and Mail on TCP.  The list of hosts was taken from the NIC hostname table of 18-Feb-83.  The tests were run on 22-Feb-83 from ISI-VAXA.ARPA.darea£gacronymcgendfullrgen - General AreadnamelGeneral AreagauthorsÅ¢kaffiliation`dnamelD. Smallb